window.NOTES={"wckKtlz2OBY":"Class Notes: Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Rabbenu Avraham ben HaRambam on the Parashah: ideas from Rabbenu Avraham's commentary on the Torah (which survives on Bereshit and Shemot), taught each week at the Monday HaMaspik LeOvdei HaShem shiur. Source: Taught at the HaMaspik shiur of the week of Hayyei Sarah 5787, HaMaspik 10.3. Topic: Why Yishak was coming back from באר לחי ראי when he met Rivkah, and why he moved there only after Avraham's death . Part One — Rabbenu Avraham on Parashat Hayyei Sarah The setting Avraham sends his servant — called עבד throughout, identified as Eliezer — to find a wife for Yishak. Eliezer finds Rivkah , negotiates with her father and brother, Betuel and Lavan , and brings her home. As they travel back, Yishak has gone out to meditate in the field — Hazal tell us, to pray Mincha — and on his way back he meets Rivkah. She is in awe of him, and falls from the camel. ויצחק בא מבוא באר לחי ראי, והוא יושב בארץ הנגב. (Bereshit 24:62) Yishak was living with his father in the Negev , somewhere in the south — but he was coming back from Beer Lahai Roi . Rabbenu Avraham's two questions: Why does the verse stress that he was coming back ? What is Beer Lahai Roi — and what was he doing there? 1. Why \"coming back\" from Beer Lahai Roi? אין ספק כי המרחק רב בין באר לחי ראי ובין המקום שאברהם ויצחק היו שוכנים בו. There was quite a distance between Beer Lahai Roi and where Avraham and Yishak lived. So why go there? אך יצחק רגיל להיפרד ממקום מושבו וללכת אל המדבר, אל הבאר הנזכרת, בדבקותו בעבודתו וחפצו לבקש התבודדות כדרך החסידים והנביאים, כמו שביארנו בספר המספיק בפרק ההתבודדות. Yishak regularly left home and went out into the wilderness, to that well, because of his attachment to his avodah and his wish for solitude — the way of the righteous and the prophets. Rabbenu Avraham points to the chapter on hitbodedut in HaMaspik itself — Chapter 12 . ( We are in Chapter 10 now; there are 13 chapters. Chapter 12 is about the importance of quiet time alone — thinking, contemplating, meditating. ) ויתעכב שם זמן קצר, ושוב היה חוזר למקום מושבו. He would stay a short while, and then return home. Why the emphasis on returning? סיפר לנו הכתוב כי הפגישה הנזכרת היתה בזמן חזרת יצחק מהבאר, לא בזמן יציאתו. ועילת דבר זה, כפי הנראה לי — כדי להודיע לבני התורה שיצחק עליו השלום יצא לעבודתו, וחזר, משהגיעה רבקה עם אליעזר. ואילו היה פוגע בהם בהליכתו, לא היה חוזר מעבודתו; אלא היתה פגישתו עמהם אחר שגמר עבודתו. The meeting happened on Yishak's way back , not on his way out — \"so that students of Torah should know\" : had he met them on his way to his avodah , he would not have turned back — not even to meet his wife for the very first time . He met them only because he had already finished. On the way out On the way back What Yishak was doing Going to pray, meditate, be mitboded Had finished his avodah Would he have stopped for Rivkah? No — he would not interrupt his avodah Yes — and he did What it teaches How deeply דבוק he was in his avodah The Rabbi: this is the awe that Yishak clearly carried — an awe Rivkah saw, and fell from the camel because of it. He was so attached to his avodah that he only met her because he was already on his way home. 2. Why did Yishak move to Beer Lahai Roi — and why only after Avraham died? The last verse in the parashah: ויהי אחרי מות אברהם, ויברך אלהים את יצחק בנו, וישב יצחק עם באר לחי ראי. (25:11) Until now he visited Beer Lahai Roi; after Avraham's death he went to live there. ישיבתו של יצחק אצל הבאר — בשביל שהבחיר עליו השלום מצא שם עבודת השם; ובחיי אברהם לא היה יכול להיפרד ממנו, אלא היה חוזר ושב מאצל אביו אל הבאר ומן הבאר אצל אביו. Why there? Because Yishak found that this was where he truly connected to Hakadosh Baruch Hu — where his time alone developed his avodat Hashem. Why only now? Because while Avraham was alive, he could not leave his father . However much spiritual benefit he gained from his solitude, \"while my father is alive, I can't leave him.\" So he travelled back and forth — though it took a great deal of his time — because being with his father mattered . וכשנסתלק אברהם עליו השלום לעולם האמת, נסע יצחק וקבע מושבו אצל הבאר שבחר בה, בשביל סגולה שהיה מוצא שם. Once Avraham passed away, Yishak moved there and settled by the well he had chosen, because of the special quality he found there . The idea to take away Before Rabbenu Avraham, we read Beer Lahai Roi as a random place name. It isn't. It is the place where Yishak developed himself spiritually — the place he would have lived all along — and he waited only out of kibbud av . The significance of a place of solitude for one's avodah. The importance of honouring and looking after one's parents — even above one's own spiritual preference.","xDLgFkTeKfU":"Class Notes: Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Rabbenu Avraham ben HaRambam on the Parashah: ideas from Rabbenu Avraham's commentary on the Torah (which survives on Bereshit and Shemot), taught each week at the Monday HaMaspik LeOvdei HaShem shiur. Source: Taught at the HaMaspik shiur of the week of Vayera 5787, HaMaspik 10.2. Topic: Four ideas: what Sarah saw in מצחק ; why Hagar feared Yishmael would die after the promise; the simple meaning of באשר הוא שם ; and בעת ההיא versus אחרי הדברים האלה , a principle for the whole Torah. Part One — Rabbenu Avraham on Parashat Vayera 1. What did Sarah see? — \"מצחק\" (Bereshit 21:9) ותרא שרה את בן הגר המצרית אשר ילדה לאברהם מצחק. Sarah sees the son of Hagar — Yishmael, the older brother — מצחק . The Torah is deliberately playing on the word: Yishak is named for laughter, and Yishmael is mesahek . But there are two kinds of שחוק . What was the problem? ראתה אותו מצחק ומנבל את פיו ומתנהג כדרך הנערים. Sarah saw him fooling around as youngsters do — and using his mouth for inappropriate speech . Playing around is understandable from the word מצחק ; where Rabbenu Avraham gets מנבל את פיו , coarse language, the Rabbi doesn't know — but for some reason he adds it. Why is that a problem? It is מפריע מדרך השלמות — it prevents a person from reaching perfection, as Shlomo HaMelekh said in Kohelet: לשחוק אמרתי מהולל... טוב כעס משחוק. When I was younger I thought it was good to laugh around; when I was older I realised that, although anger is bad, sometimes it is better than mucking around. It shows the dangers of שחוק used in the wrong way . וחששה שמא יצחק נעתק אליו ולשחוק, בהתחברו עם ישמעאל, ויבלה זמנו שעה בלא עסק בשלמות המיוחדת לו. Sarah was afraid Yishak would copy Yishmael — remember, Yishmael is older — and, instead of using his time to work on the perfection unique to him , would spend it playing around. וזה הוא הראוי שיעמוד בסגולתו... ושרה מטפלת בזה, לפי ששניהם נוכחים בבית אצלה בשעת המנוחה. Yishak's task is to work on his own perfection. There was a strong relationship between Sarah and Yishak — understandably, he is her biological child — and they spent a great deal of time together, resting and eating together. Very interesting: although both are Avraham's children, Rabbenu Avraham suggests that Sarah took personal responsibility for Yishak's spiritual development . When she saw it threatened by Yishmael's behaviour, she felt it was time to drive Yishmael out. Two new points from Rabbenu Avraham: (1) Yishmael was speaking wrongly , not just playing; (2) Sarah saw herself as responsible for guiding Yishak towards perfection. 2. Why did Hagar fear Yishmael would die? (21:16) Yishmael and Hagar have been sent away with limited food and water, and run out. Hagar distances herself: כי אמרה אל אראה במות הילד — \"I don't want to see my child die.\" The question: In last week's parashah, Lekh Lekha , the malakh told Hagar that Yishmael would grow up and become a nation — ידו בכל — prominent and influential. So what is she worried about? How will he become a nation if he dies now? A strong question. אין זה ספק מצדה בהבטחתו יתעלה שהבטיח לה, והוא יהיה פרא אדם ידו בכל ויד כל בו, אלא על דרך יראת אברהם ויעקב עליהם השלום... כשסבר שמא יגרום החטא. The easy answer would be that Hagar doubted — she lacked proper emunah. Rabbenu Avraham is not comfortable with that. He is convinced Hagar did trust the message. Rather — as the Rambam says (we learned it together in Shemonah Perakim , and it appears in many other sefarim) — שמא יגרום החטא : even when one has been promised something, such promises are often conditional . If a person misbehaves, he can lose them. So she had full faith in the promise — and at the same time feared that sin (Yishmael's, her own, or both) could stop it. A question from Mark: The obvious parallel is the Akedah. Avraham was promised Yishak would become a great nation — and then told to slaughter him. The Rabbi: Someone (he couldn't recall who) says that this was part of the test itself: you've been told clearly that you and your son will become a great nation — will you now doubt that prophecy? Avraham didn't doubt, and went ahead. Whatever one answers for Avraham and Yishak, answer the same for Hagar and Yishmael. Fair point. 3. \"באשר הוא שם\" — the simple meaning (21:17) וישמע אלהים את קול הנער, ויקרא מלאך אלהים אל הגר מן השמים... אל תיראי כי שמע אלהים אל קול הנער באשר הוא שם. The well-known interpretation (Hazal — the one we discuss on Rosh Hashanah): where he is now — although Yishmael will later do bad things, at this moment his identity is good, and he is judged as he is now. But, says Rabbenu Avraham, that is not the simple reading: פשטי דקרא, שהביט הקדוש ברוך הוא יתעלה במצבו וצערו שהוא בו, במקום שבו עזבתו אמו — תחת אחד השיחים. Geographically , not spiritually: Hashem heard the child crying where he physically was — abandoned, alone, under one of the bushes. \"I always read the verse as 'where he is now'. That's why I wanted to share it with you.\" 4. \"ויהי בעת ההיא\" vs \"אחרי הדברים האלה\" — a principle for the whole Torah After Yishmael grows up in the wilderness, the Torah says ויהי בעת ההיא — and it was at that time — and moves to the covenant between Avraham and Avimelekh. What does at that time mean? ויהי בעת ההיא — מורה על הזמן שלא הוגדר במקרא, שהוא כינוי החוזר על העת, ואותה העת לא הוגדרה; ואפשר שתהיה העת מורה על הזמן של הסיפור שקדם זכרונו, או... על הזמן של הסיפור שיבוא אחר כך. Phrase Meaning ויהי בעת ההיא — \"at that time\" Vague. It may refer to the story before it, or to the story after it. We simply don't know. אחרי הדברים האלה — \"after these things\" Defined. It always refers to the time of the story itself. וזה פירוש שאני מעתיק מן אבא מרי — this I have from my father (the Rambam). The Rabbi has not seen this anywhere in the Rambam's own writings; this may be its only source. The proof — Devarim, Parashat Eikev: ובני ישראל נסעו מבארות בני יעקן מוסרה, שם מת אהרן ויקבר שם, ויכהן אלעזר בנו תחתיו... בעת ההיא הבדיל ה' את שבט הלוי. Aharon's death is in the 40th year after leaving Egypt. Straight afterwards: בעת ההיא Hashem separated the tribe of Levi — which happened in the 2nd year (Korach / the inauguration of the Leviim). So בעת ההיא is open-ended. In Vayera, ויהי בעת ההיא could refer back to the whole narrative of Yishmael in the desert, or forward to the covenant with Avimelekh — but not necessarily to either.","Nl4yJf5AqXM":"Class Notes: Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Rabbenu Avraham ben HaRambam on the Parashah: ideas from Rabbenu Avraham's commentary on the Torah (which survives on Bereshit and Shemot), taught each week at the Monday HaMaspik LeOvdei HaShem shiur. Source: Taught at the HaMaspik shiur of Rosh Hodesh Heshvan 5787 (week of Lekh Lekha), HaMaspik 10.1. Topic: The new weekly feature, and two ideas on Bereshit: why the Torah adds טוב מאד , and why the soul is given by ויפח , breathing. A New Weekly Feature — Rabbenu Avraham on the Parashah Rabbenu Avraham wrote a commentary on the Torah , probably on all five books, but only Bereshit and Shemot survive. It is not well known: it was found only in the last hundred years or so , and the Hebrew edition of Bereshit and Shemot was brought out by Solomon David Sassoon (father-in-law of Rabbi Avraham Gabbai of Ohel David in London). The plan: through the weeks of Bereshit and Shemot (until Pesah), spend about five minutes each week , at the start or end of the Monday HaMaspik shiur, on an idea from his commentary. The Feldheim edition of HaMaspik includes selected extracts ( likkutim ) at the back, which serve as the baseline. Flicking through, the Rabbi noticed that many of the ideas tie in closely with HaMaspik, and that Rabbenu Avraham quotes not only his father but often his grandfather, Rav Maimon . Although this week is already Lekh Lekha, we begin with two short ideas on Bereshit . 1. Why \"טוב מאד\"? וירא אלהים את כל אשר עשה, והנה טוב מאד; ויהי ערב ויהי בקר יום הששי. (Bereshit 1:31) The question: אחר שאמר השם יתעלה בפרטי הבריאה \"כי טוב\", \"כי טוב\" . Each day already said ki tov . Why repeat טוב at the end, and add מאד ? His answer: מפני מציאות האדם המשיג ענייני העולם . It is human nature to try to understand the world around us. Some things we can see the benefit of immediately: the sun, the moon, the heavens and the earth; most species of animals and plants. Some are beyond us: וקצתם נשגב מן האדם אופן הכוונה במציאותם והתועלת בהם. (The Rabbi: why do we need millions upon millions of stars and galaxies? To show Hashem's glory, perhaps, but it is hard to say.) Some even seem better uncreated: ולא עוד, אלא אפשר שיעלה על הדעת במקצתם שמוטב שלא נבראו — כמציאות העקרב והנחש... והארי והדב... והתמסח : the scorpion, the snake, the lion, the bear, the crocodile . ואין ראוי לאדם שיסמוך בטוב בריאתו יתעלה על הבנתו ועל מה שיגיע אליו שכלו, אלא ידע כי לשכלו ולדעתו גבול אשר יגיע אליו. We cannot rely on our own intellect to decide what was worth creating. Our understanding has a limit. But because God is perfect, everything He creates is \"very good\", whether we understand it or not. That is what the verse says. אילו ידעתיו הייתיו : \"If I knew Him, I would be Him.\" Often attributed to Rav Saadiah; the Rabbi thinks it is actually Rav Yosef Albo, in the Ikkarim . David HaMelekh says the same: מה רבו מעשיך ה', כלם בחכמה עשית (Tehillim 104). Do I always understand them? No. But I know they are all with wisdom . פליאה דעת ממני, נשגבה לא אוכל לה (Tehillim 139). Some knowledge is too lofty for me. והראוי לאדם להסתכל במה שנסתפקו הקדמונים בתועלתו ושנעלם מהם : look at things whose benefit earlier generations doubted and which we now understand. The Rabbi's example: the appendix. As far as he knows, we still don't know what it is for; that's why it's called an appendix . But many things in the body and in nature were once a mystery and are now understood. Just as then, so now: every part of nature has a purpose. It is טוב מאד. Simple ideas, but beautiful. 2. Why \"ויפח\": He breathed ? ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים, ויהי האדם לנפש חיה. (Bereshit 2:7) (This verse opens Chapter 10 of HaMaspik.) As we learn in the Moreh: everything is homer (matter) and surah (form). Hashem made Adam עפר מן האדמה , the homer, and gave him a surah : intellect, consciousness, the mind, what we call the neshamah . Why describe the giving of the form as blowing ? כדי לקרב אל הבנתנו יושר השכל ודקות אותה צורה שכלית, שהרוחות וכדומה נראה יותר זך מן הגשמים האחרים שאנו מכירים. To convey how subtle the form is . Of all our senses, the most subtle is breath and smell . Hence the Hakhamim's play on כל הנשמה תהלל יה : read it as כל הנשימה , for every single breath we should praise Hashem. The soul and the breath go together. By contrast, the Rambam famously writes that touch is the most shameful of the senses . Smell is the sense most associated with the neshamah , so the Torah uses ויפח for implanting the surah. Summary טוב מאד: everything Hashem made is very good, even where we cannot see why. Our understanding has limits, and what earlier generations could not explain we often understand now. ויפח: breath, the subtlest sense, conveys the subtlety of the soul, the surah that makes the human being truly alive. Coming Up A short idea from Rabbenu Avraham on the parashah each week through Bereshit and Shemot, at the Monday HaMaspik shiur. Next: Parashat Vayera .","B-tMJJ3MS3g":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed), Section 1 Chapters: I:25 — שכן (to dwell); I:26 — דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם Shiur: Tuesday 10 November 2026, 8:00pm, on Zoom Next week: a revision of all the verbs of motion, the one verb we haven't learned ( סבב ), then Chapters 27 and 28. These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Recap and a Clarification Chapter 23, יצא (to go out): it can mean a physical departure, or a non-physical emergence, as in כי מציון תצא תורה . Of Hashem it means His will becoming manifest. שב means His hashgahah returning to its place: אלך ואשובה אל מקומי . Chapter 24, הלך (to go): the movement of creatures; of things like water and fire; the spreading of something non-physical, like a sound; and a person's moral conduct. Of Hashem it means the withdrawal of hashgahah , or the spreading of an effect from Him. A correction from last week Last week the Rabbi gave the impression that once hashgahah is removed, whatever happens to a person is random. Doron picked him up on it after the shiur, and he looked into it during the week. It is true that without hashgahah a person is left to chance. But that does not stop Hashem from punishing someone deliberately, in proportion to what they did. So in ויחר אף ה' בם וילך (Miriam and Aharon), וילך carries both meanings at once: Hashem withdrew His hashgahah . His anger spread, and the punishment showed itself in Miriam's tzaraat . Why Miriam and not Aharon? Perhaps she started it. Either way, the anger spread. Robert asked whether we have discussed God \"getting angry\", which sounds like an anthropomorphism. It's coming up in a later chapter. Chapter 25 — שכן, To Dwell Chapters 22 to 24 were all about hashgahah : coming, going out, going. So it fits that Rambam now turns to שכן , the root of the word שכינה . It is the last of the verbs of motion. Meaning 1: living somewhere, while moving within it ידוע שמשמעות פועל זה היא להתגורר. The plain meaning is to dwell: והוא שכן באלני ממרא , and ויהי בשכן ישראל בארץ ההיא . Rambam adds a detail: a creature that stays in a place for a long time is said to dwell there גם אם הוא נע בו בלי ספק , even though it is certainly moving around inside it. שוכן and יושב The Rabbi's own suggestion ( \"could be true, could not be true\" ): ישב means to settle in one spot and stop moving. שכן means to live in a general area while moving around in it. That may be why Hazal read וישב יעקב negatively. The verse could have said וישכן . וישב suggests he wanted to stop, almost to retire, and so: ביקש יעקב לישב בשלוה, קפץ עליו רוגזו של יוסף . The same distinction explains two more words: The word Why The Mishkan From שכן It travelled with Bnei Yisrael for 400-plus years. The Shechinah was constant, but the building moved. The two Temples, fixed in one place, are called מקדש A neighbour שכן ( שכן טוב , Avot) You live near him, but he isn't essential to you. You don't choose a neighbour the way you choose a חבר Meaning 2: something resting on something, even non-physical הדבר הושאל גם למה שאיננו בעל חיים , and even to something attached to a thing that is not a place. היום ההוא יהי חשך... תשכן עליו עננה (Iyov 3) Iyov wishes the day of his birth had stayed dark, with a cloud resting on it, so that the sun never rose and he was never born. A cloud is not a creature, and a day is not a body at all, only a stretch of time. Yet the verse says תשכן . שכן and Hashem Whenever שכן is used of Hashem, it is the second meaning. It means the constant presence of either: What it is The verses Shechinah אורו הנברא , His created light וישכן כבוד ה' על הר סיני ; ושכנתי בתוך בני ישראל Hashgahah His attention, His involvement ורצון שכני סנה כל מקום כעניינו : which one it is depends on the context. What is the \"created light\"? Rambam has quoted Onkelos on created light before (Chapter 5, the burning bush; Chapter 21), and you get the sense he isn't fond of the literal reading. The created light works on two levels: Simply: an actual, physical light. Measure the room and there would be more light in it. Deeper, and what Rambam prefers: a light created in a prophetic vision . Nothing physical; a manifestation of Hashem in the mind of the prophet. \"If you struggle, understand it as a physical light. But you should be aiming to understand it as created light in a vision.\" So at Har Sinai, every one of Bnei Yisrael had a prophetic experience in their minds, a created light. As the Gemara says, a simple person at the revelation had a greater prophetic experience than Yehezkel HaNavi. ורצון שכני סנה ומגד ארץ ומלאה ורצון שכני סנה תבואתה לראש יוסף. (Vezot HaBerachah) Moshe blesses Yosef: may the same will and hashgahah that Moshe experienced at the burning bush come to Yosef's head, all his life. Here שכן means hashgahah . The Mishkan is not holy by itself Whenever Hashem promises to dwell among us, the condition is the mitzvot. To Shlomo: הבית הזה אשר אתה בנה, אם תלך בחקתי... ושכנתי בתוך בני ישראל. \"The Mishkan isn't, in itself, a place of Shechinah. It's a place where Hashem rests His hashgahah.\" The Rabbi has said the same about the Kotel: it is holy because there we focus more on Hashem, so our prayers are better received. \"If you go to the Kotel and pray exactly as you pray anywhere else, it's nothing.\" Rav Makbili points to the Rambam in Mishneh Torah: מצות עשה ליראה מן המקדש... ולא מן המקדש אתה ירא אלא ממי שצוה על יראתו. We are not in awe of the bricks, the tapestries or the gold. We are in awe of Hashem's presence within it. לשם יחוד... לאקמא שכינתא מעפרא Many say these words every day; \"95% couldn't tell you what they mean.\" Raising the Shechinah from the dust means raising awareness: tuning in again to the created light and the hashgahah , instead of neglecting them. \"I'm not saying we should say Leshem Yihud. I'm saying that when we say these words, this is what they mean.\" Maor's question Maor asked: in the Second Temple there were corrupt kohanim, yet people brought their offerings to them. How did that work? \"That's the thing!\" That is why things didn't work as they should, why the high priests were dying every year, and why the prophets lamented: מי בקש זאת מידכם רמס חצרי. The Shechinah in the Second Temple was not the same as in the First. Maor also posted in the chat: רני ושמחי בת ציון כי הנני בא ושכנתי בתוכך נאם ה'. Chapter 26 — דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם For about fifteen chapters, from Chapter 10, Rambam has explained the verbs of motion one by one. Before turning to feelings and body parts (foot, face, back, from Chapter 28), he pauses to ask the general question: why does the Torah use these anthropomorphisms at all? Paragraph 1: the language of people דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם. The Torah speaks in words people can understand. שכל מה שיכולים כלל בני אדם להבין או לתפוס במחשבה ראשונה, הוא שיוחס להשם יתעלה. Whatever people grasp at first thought is what is attributed to Hashem. Most people, בעיון ראשון , only recognise what has a body; what has no body doesn't exist for them. So Hashem is described in physical terms to teach that He exists . Notice that Rambam says first twice: מחשבה ראשונה , עיון ראשון . At the end of the chapter he says that reading the Torah this way is reading it like נערים , like children. Why Jewish education can fall short \"There may be great Jewish education in primary and high school. The problem is that beyond that, people don't study further, and they're left with a childish conception of God.\" It may be right to teach a child that Hashem is here and Hashem is listening. The problem is staying there. Abaye and Rava (Berachot 48a): Rabbah asked the two young boys, למי מברכין? \"To Rahmana.\" ורחמנא היכא יתיב? Rava pointed to the ceiling; Abaye went outside and pointed to the sky. תרוייכו רבנן הויתו : \"you'll both be rabbis.\" Rabbah knew God isn't up there. We link height with importance, so the highest place, the heavens, is where we picture Hashem. For a child that is fine. \"But you need to grow up from that.\" Who first used this phrase this way? In the Gemara, דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם appears only in halachic contexts , never for derash. For example, הענק תעניק and השב תשיבם : one Tanna learns something from the doubled verb, the other says the Torah simply speaks as people do. Rambam applies it to non-halachic verses. He was not the first. Professor Michael Schwartz, a modern commentator on the Moreh, notes that Rabbeinu Bahya ibn Paquda , a century earlier, wrote in Sha'ar HaYihud of the Hovot HaLevavot: ועל כן אמרו רבותינו... דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם . The Kuzari does it too. Paragraph 2: only \"perfections\" are attributed to Hashem וכן כל מה שהוא שלמות אצלנו יוחס לו. Anything people see as a perfection is said of Hashem. כל מה שההמון משיג שהוא חסרון והעדר, אינו מתואר בו. Anything people see as a lack is never said of Him. Said of Hashem Never said of Hashem Moving, coming, going, standing, sitting Eating, drinking, sleeping, illness, oppression People think of these as perfections Even at first glance, people see these as deficiencies But these \"perfections\" are perfections only for us . ביחס אליו יתעלה, כל מה שאנחנו חושבים שהיא שלמות אינה אלא חסרון. Paragraph 3: moving is no better than eating Movement is a real perfection of living creatures. We need it to reach what suits us and to run from what doesn't. כי כשם שהוא זקוק לאכילה ושתיה... כך הוא זקוק לתנועה. \"I go downstairs because I'm thirsty. I go out to a meeting because I need a livelihood.\" We move because we need something, just as we eat because we need to. People are embarrassed to eat in public but not to walk. Yet ואין הבדל במציאותו יתעלה בין שיאכל וישתה לבין שיתנועע . For Hashem there is no difference; both are needs, and Hashem needs nothing. The Torah only uses movement because, at first thought, people see it as good. Paragraph 4: no movement, and no rest Aristotle showed that anything that moves has size and is divisible . Rambam will prove that Hashem has no size or body, so He has no movement. ואין לתארו גם כן נח. Nor can He be described as resting, because rest belongs only to things that move. It is like saying Hashem is alive . As Rambam writes in Yesodei HaTorah: \"God isn't alive, He is life.\" Once we remove physicality, all of these go too: ירד, עלה, הלך, נצב, עמד, סבב, ישב, שכן, יצא, בא, עבר. Rambam says it would have been unnecessary to go on at length, אלמלא מה שהורגלו דעות ההמון , and that he writes to remove the light dust of the ideas people started with in childhood . Revision Round The Rabbi asked the class which of Rambam's eleven verbs we haven't learned. סבב , to go around. That comes next week. Then a quick revision, which ran out of time: Verb Of Hashem it means Chapter ירד Resting the Shechinah; giving prophecy; punishment ( ארדה נא ואראה ) 10 עלה Prophecy ending, the Shechinah departing (Doron) 10 נצב Permanence (and more meanings, to revise) — \"Go over the notes every month or so from the beginning. You need to have the whole thing in front of you.\" Summary Chapter 25, שכן: it means to live in a place while moving within it (so the portable Mishkan, and a neighbour), or something resting on something else, even non-physical (a cloud on a day). Of Hashem it always means the second: the constant presence of His Shechinah , a created light, ideally understood as in a prophetic vision, or His hashgahah . Holy places are holy because there we turn to Hashem: ולא מן המקדש אתה ירא . Chapter 26: the Torah speaks in human language, for people's first thought, so that they know there is a God. It uses only what people see as perfections, like movement, never eating or sleeping. But for Hashem, movement is as much a deficiency as eating, and He cannot even be said to rest. All the verbs of motion must be understood figuratively. Coming Up Next week: revision of all the verbs of motion (please go over the notes), the verb סבב , then Chapters 27 and 28.","85lBhFiytKM":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam), Chapter 10, p. 210 Chapter: Perek Yud (10) — Perishut , abstinence Topic: Parashat Toldot. Why did Yitzchak ask for a meal before giving his blessing, and why are the blessings themselves so physical? Rabbeinu Avraham's answer: the meal was food for the soul , to revive Yitzchak for a prophetic blessing , and the material blessings are a means to spiritual greatness, not the goal. When: Rosh Chodesh Kislev 5787. Monday, 6:00–7:00pm on Zoom. Schedule note: Tonight the whole shiur is on the parashah, because Rabbeinu Avraham's discussion falls exactly on Toldot. Next week we go back to the section we skipped: fasting . Where We Are in Chapter 10 Perishut is an important trait. The prophets and the righteous chose simple jobs. These didn't make them rich, but left them free to serve Hashem. Perishut is in the heart. It does not mean constantly abstaining. It means thinking less about the things you do without. A wealthy person can be a true parush , because these things are not on his mind. A poor person may not be, because they are always on his mind. How to reach it (last week): Understand it intellectually. Food, drink, clothes and mansions are not who we are. We share them with animals, and they last a short time. Anyone with מוח בקדקדו knows this. Bring it down to the heart. וידעת היום is the first step. והשבות אל לבבך is the harder one. Break the habit, slowly. Assess where you are and make a plan: if you have five dishes, have four for a while. Going too fast causes physical, emotional and spiritual problems. What we are skipping (and will return to) The Rabbi doesn't like to skip, because an author orders his book for a reason. But for the parashah he gave a one-minute preview: Fasting as training for perishut, and the apparent contradiction with the Rambam in the Eight Chapters, who wrote strongly against people who cut themselves off and don't eat. Obstacles: the more people depend on you, the harder perishut becomes. The sign you have reached it: you are not moved by gaining or losing material things. The benefits of perishut, mental and spiritual. Rabbeinu Avraham then raises three questions against perishut: The question When 1 The Torah's blessings all seem physical In a couple of weeks 2 The story of Yitzchak's blessings Tonight 3 The garments of the Kohanim Later Part One — The Story (Bereishit 27) The Rabbi first read the verses, asking us to notice a few details. Yitzchak to Esav: הנה נא זקנתי לא ידעתי יום מותי. \"I have grown old; I don't know the day of my death.\" Take your gear and hunt for me. ועשה לי מטעמים כאשר אהבתי והביאה לי ואכלה בעבור תברכך נפשי בטרם אמות. \"Make me tasty food as I love, and I will eat, so that my soul may bless you before I die.\" Rivka repeats it to Yaakov: הביאה לי ציד ועשה לי מטעמים ואכלה ואברככה לפני ה' לפני מותי. She says \"and I will bless you.\" She does not say \"my soul.\" Yaakov to Yitzchak: קום נא שבה ואכלה מצידי בעבור תברכני נפשך. \"So that your soul may bless me.\" Yaakov brings wine: ויבא לו יין וישת. Yitzchak never asked for wine. The blessing: ויתן לך האלקים מטל השמים ומשמני הארץ ורב דגן ותירש. יעבדוך עמים... הוה גביר לאחיך... ארריך ארור ומברכיך ברוך. First produce, then power, then \"those who bless you are blessed.\" Yitzchak to Esav afterwards: הן גביר שמתיו לך... ודגן ותירש סמכתיו. Now he puts the mastery first and the produce second. Part Two — The Two Questions עוד אחד מן הקושיות החזקות כנגד הפרישות. One of the strong questions against perishut is the story of Yitzchak's blessing. It comes from two sides. Question 1: the meal. Yitzchak ties his blessing to tasty food, and Yaakov adds wine. איזו חיבה להנאת העולם גדולה מחיבה זו? What greater attachment to worldly pleasure could there be? Question 2: the blessing. The blessing itself is entirely physical: dew, fat of the land, grain, wine, nations bowing. כל הדברים האלו בעלי טבע גשמי הם. Part Three — The Meal Was Food for the Soul Three preliminaries 1. The soul depends on the body נשמת האדם היא הקשר בינו ובין בוראו. Our real identity is the soul, our mind and intellect. That is where the prophet receives prophecy. But as long as a person is alive, the soul is entangled with the body . ובהחלש הגוף תחלשנה מחשבותיו. When the body weakens, so do the thoughts. \"We feel this. When you're ill, it's hard to think. It's hard to develop spiritually.\" 2. Prophecy needs joy and strength אין הנבואה שורה לא מתוך עצבות ולא מתוך עצלות אלא מתוך שמחה. You cannot prophesy when you are sad, lethargic or ill. You need to be awake, full of energy and hope. So the prophets kept instruments by them, נבל ותוף וחליל וכינור . Elisha asked for a musician: ועתה קחו לי מנגן . \"This is why we have a duty to look after our bodies, to exercise and look after our health. When your body is strong, you can develop yourself spiritually much better.\" Rabbeinu Avraham notes that his father wrote this in three places: the Eight Chapters (ch. 7), the Mishneh Torah (Yesodei HaTorah), and the Moreh . We have learned the first two together. 3. Yitzchak was weak, and this was a prophetic blessing ויהי כי זקן יצחק ותכהין עיניו מראות. On the simple level, the dim eyes explain how Yaakov could take the blessing. On a deeper level, says Rabbeinu Avraham, the eyes show the state of the whole body . If his sight had weakened, so had the rest of him. Yitzchak did not want an ordinary blessing, כדרך שמברכים בני אדם איש את רעהו . He wanted to bless מתוך שפע נבואי , from prophetic inspiration. Even Bil'am could only say את אשר ישים ה' בפי ; all the more so Yitzchak. How do we know it was prophetic? When Yitzchak discovered what had happened, ויחרד יצחק חרדה גדלה עד מאד . Yet he did not retract. He said: גם ברוך יהיה . \"If your child deceived you and took the wrong thing, you'd say: I take it back. He didn't.\" Yitzchak knew the blessing came from Hashem. If it reached Yaakov, Yaakov was meant to have it. The answer Yitzchak did not ask for the meal לספק תאותו , to satisfy a craving, nor as a bribe for the blessing. He asked for it להשיב את מזגו ולשמח נפשו : to restore his strength and gladden his soul, so that he could bless prophetically. That is why he said בעבור תברכך נפשי , \"so that my soul may bless you.\" Rabbeinu Avraham adds, as his own suggestion, that Yitzchak may have felt a sudden decline. He says twice, לא ידעתי יום מותי and בטרם אמות . \"It happens with the elderly: a ninety-year-old goes into hospital and a week later he's gone.\" Part Four — Who Understood \"My Soul\"? The verses show three different people hearing the same instruction. What they heard What they understood Rivka Heard Yitzchak say תברכך נפשי Repeated only ואברככה . She did not stress the prophetic point Esav Heard it from Yitzchak directly Understood it once he heard it, and said בעבור תברכני נפשך Yaakov Never heard it. Rivka left it out Worked it out himself , and said בעבור תברכני נפשך Yaakov's insight: why would my father ask for game and tasty food now? He must be preparing for a prophetic blessing. So Yaakov said \" your soul \", and on his own initiative brought wine . ויין ישמח לבב אנוש (fitting for Rosh Chodesh). Not to drunkenness: אל תחשוב שהוא חס ושלום שתה משקה משכחת השכל. Just enough to lift the spirit. \"The difference between one shot of whiskey and five.\" About Rivka: Rabbeinu Avraham is careful. He does not mean she missed the importance of the blessing, only that she did not dwell on the exact wording. Possibly she just shortened it. \"Not every prophet understands everything all the time.\" A side point: Esav was not simply wicked כי אף שלא הגיע למעלת שלמותו של יעקב, היתה לו גם כן שלמות. Rabbeinu Avraham says plainly that Esav had a real level of perfection , though less than Yaakov's. The proofs: Yitzchak thought him worthy of the prophetic blessing, and Esav longed for the blessing and grieved when he lost it. The Rabbi recalled Rabbi Jacobs z\"l of Neve Shalom in Wembley, the scholar of Midrash, who explained this to him many times. Midrash teaches in black and white. To educate clearly, especially beginners and children, it contrasts the very righteous with the less righteous. So children come home with Yaakov HaTzaddik and Esav HaRasha . \"We need to get out of this idea that Esav was this wicked person.\" Why ask Esav to fetch it? Why not prepare the food himself, or ask Rivka? Two answers: Rabbi Avraham HeChasid (a friend of the author): to create a closeness between Yitzchak and the son he was about to bless. Rabbeinu Avraham: fresh game simply suited Yitzchak better and would revive him more. Part Five — Why the Blessings Are Physical Material blessing is the means Rabbeinu Avraham returns to his father's teaching in Hilchot Teshuvah : the Torah's material rewards are there so that a person is free to serve Hashem and grow, not as the reward itself. So read the blessing in order: Step The words What it means 1. The means מטל השמים ומשמני הארץ ורב דגן ותירש Plenty, so you are free from worry 2. The result יעבדוך עמים... הוה גביר לאחיך You grow until you stand out spiritually, and people look up to you 3. The Shechinah ארריך ארור ומברכיך ברוך The Shechinah rests on you, so whoever is with you is blessed \"You've always read these blessings wrong. Not: you'll have money, so people will bow to you because of your power. You'll have parnassah, so you'll be free to work on yourself, and people will see you're on a different level.\" Compare Ki Tavo: וראו כל עמי הארץ כי שם ה' נקרא עליך ויראו ממך. The same blessing as Bil'am's Bil'am's final blessing came with ותהי עליו רוח אלקים . מה טבו אהליך יעקב משכנתיך ישראל. אהליך יעקב — your physical homes, set so no one looked into another's ( tzniut ). משכנתיך ישראל — Bil'am is addressing Hashem : Your dwelling places among Israel, the Mishkan and the Mikdash. ושכנתי בתוכם. A few verses later Bil'am says מברכיך ברוך וארריך ארור . Because the Shechinah dwells among Israel, those who bless them are blessed. Yitzchak said the same thing, briefly. ברכת יצחק נאמרה בקיצור, ברכת בלעם בהרחבה. What about the Midrash on the order? Yitzchak puts the blessing first; Bil'am puts the curse first. A well-known derash says each showed what he really wanted. Rabbeinu Avraham says not to be troubled. Such derashot are meant for the general public . On the plain level it is the same blessing, and the order makes no difference. The blessing is that you can keep the mitzvot Yitzchak spelled out the physical goods, just as the Torah spells out the physical rewards, and hinted at the spiritual ones. את הברכה אשר תשמעו אל מצות ה' אלקיכם. (Re'eh) \"I've read this verse so many times and never noticed.\" The blessing is that you listen to Hashem's mitzvot. That is the greatest blessing. And how can you do it? When you have what you need. The proof: הן גביר שמתיו לך When Yitzchak tells Esav what he gave Yaakov, he reverses the order: הן גביר שמתיו לך... ודגן ותירש סמכתיו. The main blessing was the greatness, so he says it first. The produce is only סמכתיו , \"I supported him\": a means and a help, not the goal . Esav's blessing Esav was blessed only with this world : הנה משמני הארץ יהיה מושבך... ועל חרבך תחיה ואת אחיך תעבד. Will he always serve? Only while Yaakov's descendants keep the Torah. Onkelos: כד יעברון בנוהי על פתגמי אורייתא . When they stray from it, Esav's fear of them leaves him, though even then he does not reach Yaakov's greatness. Summary Question 1: why did Yitzchak ask for a meal? He was old and weak, his eyes were dim, and he may have felt death near. Prophecy needs a strong body and joy. The meal, and Yaakov's wine, were to revive him for a prophetic blessing : בעבור תברכך נפשי . Who understood: Yaakov worked it out alone. Esav understood once he heard it. Rivka did not pass it on. Esav had real shleimut , less than Yaakov's. Question 2: why are the blessings physical? Material plenty frees a person to grow. That growth makes him distinguished, the Shechinah rests on him, and so מברכיך ברוך , as in Bil'am's blessing. The proof is הן גביר שמתיו לך... ודגן ותירש סמכתיו : greatness is the goal, the produce only the support. \"Plenty to share on the Shabbos table this week. Listen to it again before Shabbos.\" Coming Up Next week: back to perishut. Fasting as training, the apparent contradiction with the Rambam in the Eight Chapters, the obstacles, the signs, and the benefits. Tomorrow evening: the Moreh Nebuchim shiur continues as usual.","3aGSCMFeol4":"How to Mix Food on Shabbat: The Principles of מלאכת לש Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim, Siman 321, with Siman 324:3 and the Biur Halacha When: Thursdays, 6:00–7:00pm on Zoom Next week: the remaining se'ifim of lash in Siman 321 (14–16) Tonight's Plan Last week we finished טוחן (grinding) and began לש (kneading). The Rabbi calls lash one of the most complicated and most misunderstood melachot: \"There is very little guidance on it, in English or in Hebrew, certainly for the Sephardim.\" The plan for tonight: Review last week's principles. Add the remaining principles. The psak halacha. Common examples. Read inside, if there is time. The whole of lash in the Shulchan Aruch is only four se'ifim: three in Siman 321, and one in Siman 324. Tonight we read the one in 324. Part One — The Principles 1. What is lash? Lash is binding separate particles into one unified mass , through a liquid. It applies to food, such as oil with mashed aubergine, or milk with Weetabix. It also applies to non-food, such as water with earth. 2. Is putting the ingredients together already lash? This is the big machloket of the Tannaim. Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehuda says the melacha is only the kneading . Pouring water onto flour, without kneading, is not an issur d'oraita. Rabbi (Yehuda HaNasi) says even putting the ingredients together is already lash, because it starts the mingling. Only the kneading (Rabbi Yosi) Even putting together (Rabbi) Rishonim Rambam, Rif, Rosh, and most Rishonim Yere'im, Ba'al HaTerumah, Semag, Semak Shulchan Aruch The main opinion (סתם) The second opinion (יש אומרים) Who follows it Sefardim (Chacham Ovadia) Ashkenazim (the Rema) Some Sefardi poskim are strict as well, because the Shulchan Aruch did bring the second opinion. 3. Everyone forbids the kneading Even according to Rabbi, the kneading is a second , separate issur d'oraita. So if the ingredients were put together before Shabbat, you still may not knead them on Shabbat. \"Everyone agrees that the kneading together is the prohibition. The question is whether the putting together is a prohibition or not.\" 4. When putting together is the kneading Sometimes the ingredients bind by themselves , the moment they touch. Then even Rabbi Yosi forbids putting them together. The Gemara's example: הנותן זרע פשתן במים חייב. Flaxseed in water binds on its own. Flour and water are different. They begin to mingle, but they only become dough once you knead. The Rabbi's example from last week: a little milk on Weetabix crumbs becomes one mass straight away. In his view, that is forbidden d'oraita according to everyone . 5. Three levels of mixture Level What it looks like Example Status בלילה עבה (thick) Hardly moves when tilted Dough D'oraita בלילה רכה (thin) Flows when you tilt the bowl Cake batter Derabbanan בלילה דלה (runny) Not a mixture at all Cocoa in milk Mutar Why is a thin mixture forbidden at all? Because it is עובדין דחול , a very weekday activity. 6. No unified mass, no lash Lash means the result is one unified mass. Just sticking things together is not lash. So you may make a sandwich. Bread with chocolate spread is not \"kneading the bread with the spread.\" 7. Things that never fully unify Some things become one mass when kneaded. Flour and water are like that. They are called בר גיבול . Others mix, but never fully unify, however much you knead. They are called לאו בר גיבול . The Gemara's examples are ash, coarse sand, and bran. \"Kids at the beach, playing with the water and the sand. It mingles, but it doesn't become a proper mass the way dough does.\" What does Rabbi Yosi say about these? Two Amoraim disagree (Shabbat 18): Abaye: pouring the water in is already the melacha. Kneading it achieves almost nothing anyway. Rav Yosef: you are only chayav once you knead. Mishnah Berurah Chazon Ish and Chacham Ovadia Follows Abaye Follows Rav Yosef Pouring water on is already asur d'oraita Nothing until you knead And if you do knead something that never fully unifies? The Rambam says it is only derabbanan. Most Rishonim say it is d'oraita. 8. The great heter: criss-cross strokes (שתי וערב) \"This is the major heter of lash, and we're going to use it a lot.\" Normally you mix in circles . If instead you mix in crossing strokes , one way and then the other, it is permitted. Lift the spoon out each time before changing direction. Why does it work? It is a big change from the normal way, like crushing with the back of a knife in tochen. And the result is not fully, properly mixed. This heter works for everything, and that is the practice. A note in brackets: the Gemara gives this heter for bran. The Rambam might limit it to cases that are only derabbanan. Other Rishonim apply it everywhere, and we follow them. 9. Why criss-cross isn't enough for Ashkenazim For Sefardi practice it is simple. Only the kneading is forbidden. So put the ingredients in however you like, then mix criss-cross. Even a thick mixture is fine. For Ashkenazi practice it is harder. Putting the ingredients together is already forbidden. A change in the mixing doesn't undo that. \"So you're stuck. What can Ashkenazim do? Nothing, seemingly.\" The Ashkenazi solution has two parts: Make only a thin mixture . It is only derabbanan. Because it is only derabbanan, a small change is enough: reverse the normal order of the ingredients. Example — baby cereal. Normally you put in the milk, then the cereal. An Ashkenazi puts in the cereal first, then the milk. He adds enough milk to keep it thin. Does he also need criss-cross strokes? Most poskim say no. The Mishnah Berurah says it is good to do so. So it is praiseworthy , not required. When may Ashkenazim make a thick mixture? In four cases: The ingredients were put together before Shabbat . Then just mix criss-cross. For a baby who needs that particular food. A non-Jew put the ingredients together. ( \"Quite rare — leave that.\" ) טעמו מתקלקל : made before Shabbat, it won't taste fresh . Even then, put the ingredients in reverse order, and mix criss-cross. Part Two — Inside: Siman 324:3 \"It's talking about animals, but it's the same principle.\" אין גובלין מורסן לבהמה, אבל נותנים בו מים ומעבירו בטרבד או מכה בו שתי וערב. You may not knead bran for animals by hand. But you may put the water in, and then mix it with a utensil, criss-cross . You may even do this in a huge bowl for many animals: ואפילו כור ואפילו כוריים . ויש אוסרים ליתן מים על גבי מורסן בשבת. Some forbid even putting the water in. In their view, criss-cross only helps if the water was added before Shabbat. This is the Ba'al HaTerumah. \"So you see — סתם ויש.\" The main opinion is Rabbi Yosi. The second opinion is Rabbi. The Biur Halacha Normally the halacha follows Rabbi against a single colleague. Here, though, there is an anonymous Mishnah (סתם משנה) like Rabbi Yosi, and that wins. The Biur Halacha concludes that the Shulchan Aruch's own view is to be lenient about putting the water in. The Rif, the Rosh and the Rambam stand together on this, as do the Ra'avad, the Ramban, the Ran, the Meiri and others. Most Rishonim follow Rabbi Yosi. Part Three — Practical Examples \"I wrote this document eight years ago. Some things I would change today, but fine.\" The Rabbi will share it on the group after the shiur is posted. Techina Making techina from ground sesame and water is forbidden for everyone. It binds by itself, just like flaxseed. Thinning ready-made techina with water is fine. You are loosening it, which is the opposite of lash. Ready-made techina that is too thick: you may pour water on top. Then mix it in criss-cross. This works even for Ashkenazim, because something that thick doesn't start mingling until you stir. Aubergine salad Thick result Thin result Sefardim Mix criss-cross Fine Ashkenazim Only in special cases: oil first, then aubergine, then criss-cross Oil first, then aubergine. Criss-cross is praiseworthy Most aubergine and oil salads are thin anyway. With thick mayonnaise , even Ashkenazim may put it together in any order. Just mix criss-cross. Thick mayonnaise doesn't mingle until you stir. Chilbe (fenugreek) Some Sefardim eat chilbe on Rosh Hashanah, especially the Adenim and Teimanim. The Syrians eat rubia (black-eyed beans) instead. The Rabbi's family keeps both customs , because his wife is Adeni. \"Why chilbe? Because it increases so quickly. A tiny drop makes so much.\" Making chilbe on Shabbat is forbidden for everyone. It binds and expands by itself, just like flaxseed. Salads Lettuce salads are never cut small enough to become one mass. Add oil, mayonnaise and salt as normal. Potato salad is usually in pieces, so mix normally. If it is mashed potato, or mashed avocado, mix criss-cross. Egg and onion, liver, tuna With oil or mayonnaise, these usually make a thick mixture. Sefardim: mix criss-cross. Ashkenazim: make it before Shabbat. If it genuinely won't taste the same, make it on Shabbat with the order reversed, and mix criss-cross. Does \"it won't taste fresh\" still apply, now that we have fridges? The Rabbi remembers Rabbi Falk saying no. \"But I've seen many Ashkenazim disagree. If you genuinely feel the taste isn't the same, there's plenty to rely on.\" Avocado with lemon Sefardim: a thick mixture is fine, mixed criss-cross. Ashkenazim: use plenty of lemon to keep it thin. Put the lemon in first, then the avocado. Cereal, Weetabix and porridge Cornflakes with milk: eat normally. But lots of crushed cornflakes in a little milk will clump at once. Weetabix or porridge: not in the normal way, even for Sefardim . A little milk binds it immediately. Instead, pour in a lot of milk , so it stays runny, and mix criss-cross. Thick yoghurt with banana may be mixed on Shabbat. Made the day before, it wouldn't be very tasty. Summary What lash is: binding separate particles into one mass, through a liquid. Sefardim: only the kneading is forbidden. Put the ingredients in any order, and mix criss-cross . Ashkenazim: putting together is also forbidden. Make a thin mixture, with the ingredients in reverse order . Forbidden for everyone: things that bind by themselves. Techina from sesame, chilbe, and Weetabix with a little milk. Thick mayonnaise and very thick techina don't mingle until you stir. So even Ashkenazim may put them together, then mix criss-cross. Coming Up Next week: the last three se'ifim of lash in Siman 321 (14–16) . \"They're very complex to understand, not clear at all what they mean.\" Shabbat Shalom.","uIuNDlzrK3E":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 10 (How a Person Should Conduct Himself in This World), Chapters 4 to 6: abstinence, eating and drinking, and intimacy Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 24 November 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Balanced Life Last week Saadia laid down the theme of the tenth essay. The nature of the world and of human beings is multiplicity. Some people single out one thing and pursue it to the exclusion of everything else, which is never the right way. \"The right way to conduct one's life is always with balance,\" just as Hakadosh Baruch Hu created the world with many different things. The Rabbi stressed that this is \"not just about midot .\" It applies to our character, and also to our ambitions and goals in life. He added an observation: \"That's where kana'ut , zealousness, comes from, by the way.\" Zealotry is focusing on one thing to the exclusion of others, \"and very often people who focus on one thing to the exclusion of other things, they themselves have issues with that problem, which is why they become so zealous about it.\" Saadia also set out the three faculties, desire, emotion and cognition, and taught that the wise person lives with cognition in control of the other two. And he read Shlomo HaMelech's hevel as referring to wisdom, pleasure or work taken to an extreme alone. The thirteen paths At the start of Chapter 4 Saadia listed the thirteen things he will examine: Abstinence ( perishut ) Eating and drinking Intimacy Passion Children Building up the world ( yishuv ) Long life Money Power and status Revenge, righting every wrong Work Wisdom Rest The aim tonight: Chapters 4 to 7. In the event the class reached the end of Chapter 6. Chapter 4: Abstinence ( Perishut ) The hermits Perishut means abstinence or asceticism. \"All the Rishonim talked about this. It seemed to have been a common thing in the olden days, certainly amongst other religions and even amongst Jews.\" The earliest instance the Rabbi found was the Desert Fathers , early Christian hermits and ascetics who lived mainly in Egypt from around the third century. The Rambam, Rabbeinu Bachya ibn Pakuda in Chovot HaLevavot, and others all discuss people who cut themselves off and went to live as hermits. Their argument The Rabbi warned: \"It's very depressing, this paragraph, I'm just warning you.\" ראו אנשים כי אשר ראוי שיתנהג בו האדם בעולם הזה הפרישות וההליכה על ההרים ולבכות ולספוד ולהתאבל על העולם הזה Some people hold that a person should withdraw to the mountains and weep, lament and mourn over this world. Their reasons: This world comes to an end. It turns against its inhabitants and does not endure. Whenever a person rejoices in calm and tranquility, it turns on him and his joy becomes mourning. His greatness becomes his downfall, and his success comes only with great toil. עשיר ישכב ולא יאסף, עיניו פקח ואיננו : the rich man lies down and it is gone. Every effort backfires. Try to be wise and you end up foolish; try to be pure and you are sullied; try to be healthy and illness shuts you in; try to speak well and your tongue trips you. אם אצדק פי ירשיעני . No one knows what tomorrow brings: illness, injury, bereavement, worry and loss. אל תתהלל ביום מחר כי לא תדע מה ילד יום . Whatever you lean on gives way. \"What a beautiful pasuk,\" said the Rabbi: אשר יקוט כסלו ובית עכביש מבטחו . Whatever we trust, our wealth, our status, our job, our house, is a spider's web. ישען על ביתו ולא יעמוד . The world is full of falsehood. How many mighty people has Hashem brought down, אשתוללו אבירי לב , and how many of the great has He humbled, לחלל גאון כל צבי להקל כל נכבדי ארץ . Kindness is repaid with harm. We open our eyes in the morning looking for light, and the world goes dark. כי טוב קויתי ויבא רע . And above all, our sins, the reckoning and the suffering, and the distance between us and Hashem, until it seems He is like a lion tearing at us, כשחל תצודני , and הנה יום ה' בא אכזרי ועברה וחרון אף . Their conclusion: צריך למאוס בעולם הזה, ולא יבנה בית ולא יטע, ולא יקח אשה ולא יוליד Reject this world. Don't build a house, don't plant, don't marry, don't have children. \"How many times do we hear this in this generation now? I don't want to bring children into this world.\" And don't live among people who do these things, lest they influence you. Instead go and be alone in the mountains, eat whatever plants you find, until you die in worry and mourning. \"Basically everything is fleeting in this life. Because everything is fleeting, it's not worth investing.\" Saadia: mostly true, but they erred והסתכלתי במה שאמרה האומה הזאת ומצאתי ברובם אמת, אך טעו בעזיבת הישוב ובני אדם \"I think the majority of what this group say is true.\" The Rabbi noted that Saadia said this before, in the discussion of reward and punishment in this world: most of this world is pain and difficulty. \"Some people can sympathize with that, some people less.\" Their mistake was in what they did with it: They abandoned food, clothing and shelter, which no one can live without. They abandoned marriage. If not having children were good, then everyone should do it, עד שיבטל מין המדברים , until the human race ended. With it would end wisdom, Torah, the Day of Judgment and the settled world. Hashem created the world, so that cannot be right. The Rabbi: \"That's the famous argument against the Christian doctrine of priests not getting married.\" If it is the ideal, then everyone should aspire to it, and there would be no future for humanity. They put themselves in physical danger, among wild animals, lions and snakes, heat and cold. They damaged their minds. Without proper food, warmth and company, nature deteriorates and the mind goes, until they need to come back to town to be healed, and it does not always help. They lost their humanity. Some returned and were so shocked by ordinary life that they thought people deserved to die, and some even allowed themselves to shed blood. Others became like animals: בת עמי לאכזר כיענים במדבר , like ostriches in the desert, and בערוץ נחלים לשכון, חורי עפר וכפים... בין שיחים ינהקו . When is perishut good? אבל הפרישות טובה לאדם כשיתנהג בה במקומה, והוא כי הזדמן לו המאכל והמשתה האסור והמשגל האסור והממון האסור Abstinence is good in its place: when forbidden food, forbidden drink, a forbidden relationship or forbidden money comes his way. \"You have an opportunity to steal, you have an opportunity to be deceitful... that's the time you do perishut .\" Then he should use this trait to distance himself from all of it. What exactly is Saadia's view? The Rabbi pointed out a tension. On one hand Saadia agrees that the ascetics' picture of the world is largely true. On the other, his practical conclusion is that one uses perishut only for what is forbidden. \"It doesn't seem like Saadia's of the view that it's good to fast... It doesn't seem that kind of asceticism is one which he endorses.\" Would he endorse someone fasting from permitted food? \"I don't think so. I'm not sure.\" What is clear is that abstaining from the forbidden is something everyone agrees on. Other Rishonim on Abstinence Rabbeinu Bachya: abstain in your heart In Chovot HaLevavot, Rabbeinu Bachya describes three groups who practise perishut : Group What they do Verdict 1 Want to be like angels: leave society, live in the mountains and eat vegetation Exactly Saadia's hermits. Not the Torah way 2 Live among people but reject everything physical: fast, deny all pleasures Better than the first, but also not correct 3 Separate from the world in their hearts The correct way והוא שפרשו מן העולם בלבבותם ומצפונם, וישתתפו עם אנשי העולם בנראה מגופיהם בישוב העולם... ונפשם מואסות בעולם ובהונו ונכספות לעולם הבא They withdraw inwardly but join other people outwardly in building the world, ploughing and sowing and trading. They understand what a person's situation in this world is, his sojourn here and his exposure to pain, and their souls long for the World to Come. \"Be normal. Live amongst people, have relationships with people, go out, talk, chat. I'm there, I'm with you, I'm present.\" But in your mind you know this is not the end. Rabbeinu Bachya endorses perishut , \"but only mentally, only in your mind.\" The Rambam: abstinence as medicine The class learned this in Shemonah Perakim a couple of years ago: אבל מה שעשו אותם החסידים בקצת הזמנים... הם גם כן מנהגות החולים על דרך הרפואה, בצום וקום בלילות והנחת תענוגי הגופות... ולבוש הצמר והשער ושכונת ההרים והתבודד במדברות, לא עשו דבר מזה אלא על דרך רפואה The Rambam does not deny that there were pious people who, for periods, fasted, rose at night, gave up meat and wine, and even went to the mountains. But they did it as medicine , to cure a bad trait. If someone feels he has become too preoccupied with food, he might fast by day for forty days to wean himself off it. \"That perishut is good perishut .\" For the Rambam, \"there is no independent value to perishut .\" Its only value is in training the character back to the golden mean: when you have gone too far one way, you go towards the other extreme for a time. \"Extreme is only good if you're trying to balance yourself out, but never in and of itself.\" The Kuzari: the Torah did not bring us asceticism Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi, the Chaver, says it unequivocally: תורת משה לא הביאתנו בפרישות, אך בדרך השוה תרצה, ולתת לכל כח מכחות הנפש והגוף חלקו בצדק מבלי רבות כח אחד על חברו The Torah wants the balanced path, giving every faculty of soul and body its rightful share, without one overwhelming another. One who gives too much to desire falls short in thought, and the reverse. Frequent fasting is no service for someone whose desires and strength are already weak. Look after your body. Don't treat poverty as a virtue: earn a living that still lets you study. The key line: ואין הכנעתך בימי התענית יותר קרובה אל האלוהים משמחתך בימי השבתות והמועדים Your submission on fast days is no closer to God than your joy on Shabbat and Yom Tov, when your joy is a complete service. \"Have a good time, enjoy, look after your body, look after yourself. That's the correct way.\" The Nazir The Rabbi recalled a point discussed in the past: at the end of his term the Nazir brings a sin offering. He may need to separate himself for a time, \"fine, great,\" but the offering teaches that this is not the right way to live permanently. The common thread Thinker When perishut is right Saadia When something forbidden is in front of you The Rambam As therapy , for a limited time, to bring yourself back to balance Rabbeinu Bachya In your mind : know this world is temporary, while living a normal life among people The Kuzari The Torah does not ask for it. Joy on Shabbat and Yom Tov is the greater service Someone asked what perishut means. Abstinence; it is the noun, and perishuyot is its plural. Chapter 5: Eating and Drinking The second extreme: people who think life is about good food and good drink. \"Eat and drink, for tomorrow we will die.\" Their argument ראו קצת בני אדם כי הראוי שיתנהג בו האדם שער אכילה ושתיה Food sustains body and soul, it is very pleasant, and it is what makes bodies grow and multiply. Fast one day and you weaken: your sight, hearing, thought, memory and reasoning fade. Eat, and they all return. Sometimes a person who is famished יכפור באלוהיו , denies his God, and cannot understand what he is praying. (The Rabbi: \"For Saadia, the only time he didn't concentrate in prayer seems to be when he's fasting.\") All settlement follows food. People build only where there are fields and water. Kings' taxes and soldiers' wars are about food. Hence the saying, \"this is my bread.\" The Torah promises food to the righteous: ועבדתם את ה' אלקיכם וברך את לחמך ואת מימיך , and ונתנה הארץ פריה ואכלתם לשבע . Every occasion needs food: an engagement, a wedding, a birth, a brit milah, a festival, a dedication. The Rabbi: \"Whenever you go to an event, there has to be food there.\" Kiddush on Friday night, Kiddush on Shabbat, breakfast in the morning. Friendship runs on it. \"Can I offer you a cup of tea?\" You cannot just sit and talk without something to eat or drink. Wine looks lovely, smells good and tastes sweet. It cheers the anxious, makes the miser generous and the faint-hearted brave. ויין ישמח לבב אנוש להצהיל פנים משמן . So, they say, life should be the pursuit of the best food: a better piece of meat, a better meal, a more expensive wine. The Rabbi told a true story of someone who drank a bottle of wine costing many thousands, and remarked that his urine would now be very expensive. Saadia: mostly mistaken והתבוננתי במאמרם זה ומצאתי רובו טעות, כי הם מביטים לטובותיו ולא מביטים לרעותיו They see the good and ignore the harm: The body: too much food causes indigestion, makes the body and limbs heavy and the head and eyes dull, and brings on faintness. \"You know when you've had too much at a dinner... you can't study, you can't do things.\" It changes a person's disposition. Money: the appetite drives him to chase money to buy more food. Never full: he forgets what satiety is and becomes like dogs, והכלבים עזי נפש לא ידעו שבעה . Losing the taste: like fire, which consumes whatever comes to it without feeling, ויהי העם כמאכלת אש . When you eat at the right time you enjoy it; when you eat constantly \"you lose the sensitivity to good food.\" Like death and Sheol, which never say \"enough\": אשר הרחיב כשאול נפשו והוא כמות ולא ישבע , and like the four things that are never sated in Mishlei, Sheol, the barren womb, the earth that is never full of water, and fire. Stinginess: he begrudges anyone who eats from his food, and if he gives, it is without a full heart: אכול ושתה יאמר לך ולבו בל עמך . Social disgrace: kings, great people and the wise will not sit with him. He eats fast, jumps on the best piece, is first to the meal and last to leave, with his eyes on the next course. ושמת שכין בלעך אם בעל נפש אתה . Gluttony's excesses: the Rabbi noted that among the Romans people would make themselves vomit in order to eat more. The glutton becomes a funnel, pouring in at the top and out at the bottom. כי כל שלחנות מלאו קיא צואה בלי מקום . Forgetting Hashem: כמרעיתם וישבעו, שבעו וירם לבם, על כן שכחוני . The harm of wine: it overpowers the intellect, לץ היין הומה שכר וכל שגה בו לא יחכם . It weakens the sinews and damages the liver (\"that we know today, alcohol ruins the liver\"), and brings illnesses of the blood and the bowels. It is למי אוי למי אבוי . It loosens every restraint and leads to every disgrace, and it creeps through the body slowly, אחריתו כנחש ישך . Killing, beatings, prison and punishment come through it, and all manner of fraud and trickery. Theft: one who gets used to feasting will take it wherever he can find it: כי לחמו לחם רשע ויין חמסים ישתו . When are food and drink good? הן טובים לאדם לקחת מהם כדי קיום גופו Take what you need to sustain your body. צדיק אוכל לשובע נפשו . When your intellect sees there is a place for it, send out the desire; when there is no need, hold it back. The Rabbi on eating \"This was quite radical for their times.\" In the old days, the fatter you were, the wealthier you were seen to be, and there was no concept of a diet. Two hundred years later the Rambam wrote in Hilchot Deot that most illnesses come from overeating. \"And how true is that nowadays.\" Once a person is mindful of what goes into his mouth, \"it completely changes their life.\" When should a person eat? When he is hungry, because he needs it. \"There's no such concept as eating and drinking for stress.\" Nor because \"I just desire this cake and this pudding.\" He recommended reading the Rambam's Hilchot Deot Chapter 4: \"you read that and you end up only eating and drinking what you need for a few days, until you forget it.\" Someone asked whether that means eating only when starving. \"No, not starving.\" The Rambam says to stop at around two-thirds or three-quarters of your appetite. \"Don't eat until you struggle to get up and you have to loosen your belt.\" Stop when you feel, \"I'm not hungry anymore. Of course I can eat more. There's some beautiful meat here... But I'm full up now.\" This idea, he said, is echoed by essentially all the Rishonim: eat and drink קיום נפשו , what you need to live the best life. Chapter 6: Intimacy The third extreme: people who think marital relations should be preferred over all the world's pleasures. Their argument It is the greatest pleasure there is. Everything else has a substitute; this has none. It gladdens and strengthens the soul, lightens a heavy spirit, calms anger, and relieves the black bile called melancholia. Above all, it is the cause of human existence, and the closest bond of affection between people. If it were shameful, Hashem would have kept it from His prophets. Yet Yaakov said, הבה את אשתי ואבואה אליה , without embarrassment (from that week's parasha), and Yeshayahu said, ואקרב אל הנביאה , without shame. Saadia: they are wrong וחקרתי דברים האלה ומצאתים בטלים, כי הניחו הנזק ועוצם העון They forgot the harm and the gravity of the sin: Physical harm, as the medicine of the time held: it weakens the eyes (a common belief then, which the Rambam and even the Shulchan Aruch quote; \"I don't think it's true nowadays,\" said the Rabbi), saps strength and appetite, brings illness and hastens old age. אל תתן לנשים חילך . Confusion of mind: זנות ויין ותירוש יקח לב . One whose heart is fixed on it cannot rest until he has it, and as soon as he has it he wants more, כלם מנאפים כמו תנור בערה מאפה . Spiritual stain: one who is sensitive would feel his garments are soiled, even if he washed all his days, אם התרחצתי במו שלג... אז בשחת תטבלני ותעבוני שלמותי . Lasting shame: a sin like adultery stays with a person to the end of his days. נגע וקלון ימצא וחרפתו לא תמחה . Losing all sense of being seen: he imagines everyone is blind and deaf, and does shameful things in the open. His home becomes a meeting place for the wrong people, ובית זונה יתגודדו . Measure for measure: he brings confusion of lineage, and may find it repaid in his own home, תטחן לאחר אשתי . When is this desire good? ואין התאוה הזאת טובה לאדם כי אם בזרע Saadia says its purpose is children, ואתם פרו ורבו , and it should be sent out with understanding when it is needed for that. The Rabbi thought Saadia was being brief here. Halachically marital relations are encouraged, and even obligatory, when a wife is pregnant or past childbearing age. The Ramban writes in several places that the bond has value when it creates affection and love and strengthens the relationship. \"I don't think Saadia means only to have children and nothing else. That I don't think makes sense at all. That goes against everything which we know in all the Gemara and all the Rishonim.\" His point is that this too is an extreme to avoid. Summary Zealotry is focusing on one thing to the exclusion of all else, and often comes from a person's own struggle with that very thing. Abstinence: the ascetics' picture of a painful, fleeting world is mostly true, but withdrawing, not marrying and living in the wild is wrong. If everyone did it, humanity and Torah would end. Saadia uses perishut for the forbidden; the Rambam as temporary therapy; Rabbeinu Bachya in the heart while living among people; the Kuzari says joy on Shabbat and Yom Tov is the greater service. Eating and drinking: their defenders see only the good. Overeating harms body, character, friendships and faith, and wine leads to every kind of wrong. Eat what sustains you, and stop before you are full. Intimacy: pursued as the goal of life it harms body, mind and soul. Saadia ties it to children; the Rabbi added, with the Ramban, that its value in building love is also fully recognised. Coming Up Next week, same time: Chapter 7 (passion) and the chapters that follow. The Rabbi hopes to finish the tenth essay before the winter holidays and begin a new sefer next year. Tomorrow night (Tuesday): the Rambam shiur continues.","P3tnMvatx8Y":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: The whole sefer: the Introduction and Essays 1 to 10, a review of the key takeaways Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 19 January 2026, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Why Review? The Gemara says that one who learns and does not revise is like someone who ploughs the ground and does not plant seeds. \"An essential part of learning is also chazarah , to revise and to go over.\" So before moving on, the Rabbi went through the entire sefer essay by essay, with the main points he had noted from each. What comes next Monday nights: a new sefer, HaMaspik l'Ovdei Hashem of Rabbeinu Avraham, the son of the Rambam, starting Monday 2 February . There would be no shiur the following Monday, to give the Rabbi time to prepare. Tuesday nights: once the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek (the ikkarim ) is finished, in a month or so, the class will begin the Moreh Nevuchim . \"At least we get the best of both.\" The Introduction: What Belief Is \"Many of the key points of Saadia in Emunot VeDeot actually appear in the introduction.\" Four kinds of people People go wrong in their beliefs either because they never think about belief at all or because they don't know what they are looking for. \"Saadia loves categorizing with numbers,\" and already here there are four kinds of people: Those who have reached the truth and are confident in it. Those who have reached the truth but are unsure and doubtful. Those who hold false beliefs and think they are correct. Those who keep changing their minds, jumping from one opinion to another. So he wrote the book so that people could know what the truth is and be confident in it. That is why, whenever he brought an opposing argument, he took the time to show why it is or is not correct. Belief takes work It is natural to have doubts at first. We cannot expect Hakadosh Baruch Hu to hand us all theological and philosophical knowledge \"on a plate.\" As created beings, understanding the Divine takes time and effort, like everything in life. For Saadia, emunah is something a person has analysed . \"A belief without understanding isn't a belief.\" If you haven't thought it through and dissected it rationally and cogently, \"that's not called an emunah.\" The word comes from נאמן , firm: ותקעתיו יתד במקום נאמן , a peg in a firm place, and ומצאת את לבבו נאמן לפניך . Opinion does not make reality \"Mere opinion can't transform something into reality,\" which the Rabbi called something \"lacking nowadays.\" The fool forms his opinions and then fits reality around them. The wise person forms his opinions after searching, research and analysis. The four sources of knowledge Saadia repeated this throughout the book: # Source Example 1 Sense perception What we see, hear and touch 2 Self-evident reason Two plus two equals four 3 Inference , what we would call binah , להבין דבר מתוך דבר You see smoke and infer fire 4 Reliable tradition The chain from Sinai through the Nevi'im to the Chachamim You must ask questions At the end of the introduction Saadia says it is completely wrong to think one must not question. (The Rabbi recalled the \"bizarre analogy\" Saadia gave at the time, about people who think that if you go to India you will find money there.) Not only is it allowed; there is an obligation to analyse and understand, a point later repeated by Rabbeinu Bachya in Chovot HaLevavot and others. When Chazal warn against philosophising, that refers to philosophy alone, which can lead to wrong conclusions. When one has a received tradition and wants to understand it through philosophy, one is obliged to. Eight reasons people deny the truth \"Basically all apply nowadays, some more than others\": The truth is too hard, too inconvenient , to accept. Stupidity. People are too busy with work and mundane affairs to think. People are too lazy to think. Arrogance : thinking you know everything, so you never look at other views. Social influence of disbelievers. Being presented with a shallow religion . \"That's what I call fast food Torah... lots of stories and jokes and things, and people think this is what Torah's about.\" The bad behaviour of people who ostensibly observe the Torah. Essay 1: Creation Essay 1 is about the creation of the universe only; Saadia does not get to God Himself until Essay 2. Creation from nothing. Saadia believes clearly and strongly in בריאה יש מאין . The Rabbi noted that some, famously the Ibn Ezra, held that Hashem created from existing matter, Plato's view. Saadia rejects it. Two main arguments: Design , the teleological argument, \"the most common argument in the Rishonim\": there is too much design in the universe for it to have happened by itself. Finitude : everything physical in our universe is finite, so the universe too must have a beginning. Twelve views of how the universe came about, among them: Plato: God and pre-existing matter ( hyle ) existed eternally. \"You're believing in something which you haven't seen... so why posit the hyle ? Just posit God.\" And matter cannot exist without form. The Hindus: God created from Himself. Rejected. The dualists (Mani, the Gnostics): a god of light and a god of darkness. Then neither is perfect, since each limits the other. The Atomists: tiny particles came together. But who created the atoms? Aristotle: the world is eternal. \"We don't know anything that exists which doesn't have a beginning.\" How can something come from nothing? \"If we knew how to create something from nothing, we would be God.\" The Kuzari and Rav Yosef Albo later repeat the idea that to understand God completely you would have to be God. No space or time before creation. So the question \"why did Hashem create the world then and not earlier?\" is meaningless. (Saadia held the world was about 4,000 years old in his time.) He also allowed that there may be things outside our universe, but Hashem created what we can understand; beyond that is not for us. Why create at all? His main reason, which recurs later in the book: להיטיב , to bestow good on His creatures. Essay 2: Hashem Hashem is one, not two. If there were two gods, neither would be complete or perfect. Here Saadia explains why Tanach sometimes uses the Name and sometimes Elokim . No diversity in Hashem. Saadia begins the concept of yichud that the Rambam later refines: Hashem is חכם, יכול, חי , wise, able and alive, all as one. A person can be clever and also handsome, but those are separate attributes. \"That doesn't exist in God.\" Against the Trinity, with philosophical and scriptural arguments, including refutations of the verses Christians cited. \"That was something big in his time.\" All anthropomorphism is metaphor. Saadia is \"the very first of our Chachamim that we know\" to state explicitly that every description in the Torah of God's body , actions and feelings is metaphorical: a hand, a leg, going up and coming down, anger, regret. The Torah uses metaphors deliberately, \"because otherwise we couldn't talk about God.\" Negative theology, which the Rambam develops greatly in the Moreh. When we say אל רחום וחנון , we do not mean we understand His mercy and compassion. They are infinite, and the finite cannot grasp the infinite. We mean that He acts towards us in ways we perceive as merciful, and that He is \"not not merciful.\" \"I know it's hard to think in those terms.\" The Kavod . \"The first thing I think which the Rambam would have disagreed with.\" When Moshe or Yeshayahu \"saw\" Hashem, Saadia says they saw the כבוד ה' , an image Hashem created in order to communicate with human beings. For the Rambam, prophecy is in the intellect; nothing is seen. Hashem fills the entire universe, even impure places, unlike some later Rishonim who held He is not present in impure places, which \"theologically is very difficult.\" Essay 3: Revelation Once there is a universe and a Creator, \"the natural follow on\" is that He reveals His will. Why mitzvot? Hashem created the world to bestow good, and gave the Torah because what we earn ourselves is far more enjoyable. Two kinds of mitzvot. Saadia is \"the first one, to the best of my knowledge,\" to make this distinction (the Rambam says basically the same in other words): מצוות שכליות , rational mitzvot: between people, and serving God. מצוות שמעיות , revealed mitzvot, which we could never have worked out: shatnez , kashrut and the like. Reasons even for revealed mitzvot. Strikingly, Shabbat is for rest and has social value, bringing people together. Some animals are forbidden so that we do not come to worship them. Objective morality. \"Morality is not something which is relative.\" We would know the rational mitzvot even without being told, but we still need the Torah, above all for structure : when is murder murder, theft theft, adultery adultery. Prophecy is verified by miracles. \"The second time where the Rambam clearly disagreed with Saadia.\" Not on its own (a miracle-worker who says to abandon the Torah is not accepted), but for Saadia the main proof of a prophet is a real miracle. The Egyptian magicians, whom the class had just read about in the parasha, acted בלטיהם , which Saadia understands as sleight of hand. A prophet knew he was speaking with Hashem by a pillar of Kavod above him. Yonah. A unique answer: Yonah did go the first time. He refused the second time, because he did not want Nineveh rewarded for its teshuvah. The Torah has three parts: commandments, reward and punishment, and narrative, which brings the first two alive. Mass revelation. אתם ראיתם : the Torah was given before a whole people, the only religion to claim that. The Kuzari later develops this argument. The Torah will never be abrogated. Saadia refutes seven arguments for its replacement, \"obviously the basis behind the New Testament, the basis behind the Koran.\" Incidentally: animals that are slaughtered will be rewarded, which the Rambam strongly rejected. And Israel suffers as the chosen people to increase its future reward, as the Gemara has us tell a convert, \"which needs to be understood.\" Essay 4: Free Will \"Now that's all God. Now let's talk about us, humans.\" The world was created for human beings, who are elevated above all other creatures. The Rambam and the Ibn Ezra strongly disagreed. So only humans have free will. Choosing defines an action. Free will covers acting and refraining. An action is yours only if you chose it: שוגג : switching on a light on Shabbat because you forgot it was Shabbat, or thought it was allowed. You chose the act, so it has consequences: in the time of the Mishkan, a korban; and some poskim say one should write it down today, for when the Beit HaMikdash is rebuilt. מתעסק : leaning back on the switch by accident. You did not choose it, so it is not your action. Proofs from all four sources of knowledge that we have free will. Our bad choices do not affect God. Foreknowledge. Hashem knows the result of our choices: I can choose A or B, and Hashem knows I will choose A, of my own free will. The Rambam and others give different answers. What would have happened anyway. If I give charity, the poor man would have received it some other way; if I harm someone, he would have been harmed anyway. I am rewarded or punished because it came through me. \"Many argue with this,\" as the class discussed. Pharaoh's heart. \"Because it's this week's parasha.\" The Rambam, the Ibn Ezra and the Ramban each answer, and Saadia gives an answer \"I haven't seen anyone else say\": ואני אחזק את לב פרעה means Hashem strengthened him to stay alive through the plagues so that he would see all ten, not that He took away his free will. Essay 5: Merits and Sins Everything is recorded, in a metaphorical book, and every action leaves an imprint on the soul. Tzaddik and rasha by majority. A tzaddik has more mitzvot, a rasha more aveirot, measured by importance, not number. Two categories only: above 50 per cent, reward in Olam HaBa and punishment for one's sins in this world; below, reward for one's mitzvot in this world and punishment in the next. Regret can change the status of a mitzvah or an aveirah. Why tzaddikim suffer: to pay for their sins before the next world, or to increase their reward there. This is also Saadia's explanation for the suffering of children, who bear no moral responsibility. Perfection is possible. אין צדיק בארץ אשר יעשה טוב ולא יחטא does not mean it is impossible to be perfect. The four components of teshuvah, which Saadia was the first to list and the Rishonim later develop: regret, stopping, commitment for the future and confession. And writing poetry about teshuvah helps one do teshuvah. Punished in this world, always: false oaths, perjury, murder and adultery. \"I don't know anyone else who says this.\" Rewarded in this world, always: honouring parents, compassion for animals and honesty in business, which \"seems to go against the Gemara.\" Five levels of teshuvah. The best is in the same situation, at the same age and with the same ability; the lowest is just before death. The beinoni , exactly 50-50: Hashem's compassion tips him to the side of the tzaddik. \"Until essay five, the differences between Rambam and Saadia are very small. From here... the differences become quite big.\" Essay 6: The Soul Created with the body, separated at death and reunited at the resurrection. Its nature: after going through various theories, \"some of them quite difficult,\" Saadia concludes it is a fine, ethereal creation, finer even than the spheres, the source of speech and intellect, which functions only within the body. Five names, one soul: נפש, רוח, נשמה, חיה, יחידה . Nefesh for the appetites, ruach for the emotions, neshamah for the intellect, chayah because it lives forever, and yechidah because it is unique. Why put so fine a soul in a body? Because alone it cannot function or be rewarded, so it is for its good. Reward and punishment are for body and soul together , which the Rambam disputes. The moment of death: each person sees the angel of death, \"the yellow angel of death with the blue eyes, holding a sword.\" We do not see the soul leave because it is transparent. No reincarnation. \"Reincarnation is nonsense.\" There is no gilgul neshamot . Essay 7: Resurrection With Essays 7 to 9 Saadia turns to eschatology. Two resurrections: of righteous Jews in this world in the messianic era, and of all the righteous in the World to Come, the new world that will be created. Against nature, not against logic. Even someone burned to fine particles is not completely destroyed; something always remains, from which Hashem reconstitutes him. Since it is not against reason, you may not interpret it as metaphor. When the Torah may be read non-literally: only in four cases. It contradicts the senses: Chava as \"mother of all living\" cannot include animals. It contradicts reason: אש אכלה , God is not fire. Two verses contradict each other. It contradicts tradition: the Torah says forty lashes; the tradition is thirty-nine. Many verses in Yeshayahu, Yechezkel and the Nevi'im describe resurrection. If these are metaphor, \"the whole Torah becomes a metaphor.\" Details, which Saadia believes though they are not sourced: the resurrected go straight on to the World to Come and do not die again; there is room for everyone; people recognise one another; they rise with their former blemishes, so their loved ones know them, and are then healed; and they eat, drink and marry, with free will, reward and punishment, and long lives. Essay 8: Mashiach The purpose of Mashiach is to gather Am Yisrael and bring them to Israel. It runs through the Prophets, and logically it would be unjust for Hashem not to redeem us. Two times: earlier, if we do teshuvah; and the keitz , the final deadline, at which Hashem will bring us to teshuvah, \"we'll have no option.\" A date. From three verses in Daniel Saadia worked out a date, which the class calculated at the time. \"Saadia was clearly wrong with that date,\" and the class discussed whether it is appropriate to give dates, and why so many have been wrong. Mashiach ben Yosef and Mashiach ben David: first ben Yosef, the war of Gog and Magog, and his death; then ben David. Fifteen proofs that Mashiach has not come, necessary against Christianity's claim that he already had. Essay 9: The World to Come Proofs: seven from reason, six from the Torah and four from the Chachamim. A new world, not yet created. Hashem did not create us straight into it because we must earn it ourselves. Why the Torah does not mention it explicitly: two reasons, because it is obvious, or because the Torah deals with the present world. The class looked at many other Rishonim on this. Light and fire. Reward is light and punishment is fire. People will not eat or drink but be sustained by the light. Gan Eden, Olam HaBa and Gehinom are the same place. No night or day, no seasons: continuous light. Eternal punishment, despite Hashem's compassion, so that no one can say, \"Had You given us a greater punishment, I would have kept the mitzvot.\" It gives the greatest incentive. Seven levels of reward and of punishment. Who sees whom: the wicked see the righteous and despair; righteous people close in level see one another; the wicked do not see each other. Service of Hashem, and reward and punishment, continue in the World to Come, \"the most astonishing claim.\" \"For me this was the most difficult essay, essay nine, to accept. How does he know all these things? Did he go and come back?\" Some of it is his own belief, and some is sourced in the Torah and the Chachamim. Essay 10: How to Live The most important thing: balance . Thirteen things, each with its place: abstinence, eating and drinking, intimacy, passion, wealth, children, work and building, long life, authority and power, revenge, wisdom, worship of Hashem, and rest. \"You need all these thirteen things in life, but they're mixed together, not necessarily equally.\" And balance runs through everything else too: in what we see, in music and in smell. With that Saadia concluded the sefer. The Shape of the Book The Rabbi's map of the ten essays: Essays Theme 1 to 3 God and revelation: creation, the Creator, Torah and prophecy 4 to 6 The human being: free will, mitzvot and aveirot, the soul 7 to 9 The end of days: resurrection, Mashiach, the World to Come 10 How to live in this world: balance Where Saadia and the Rambam part ways Points the Rabbi flagged along the way: Saadia The Rambam The prophets saw a created Kavod Prophecy is in the intellect Prophecy is verified by miracles Disagrees Slaughtered animals will be rewarded Strongly disagrees The world was created for man Disagrees (with the Ibn Ezra) Reward and punishment are for body and soul together Disagrees Olam HaBa is light and fire, with continued service of Hashem Disagrees Closing \"That is a brief summary of everything which we learned over the last two years... It's been a fantastic journey together. Thank you everyone for coming along on this journey, because I've learned a lot over the past two years together.\" Coming Up Next Monday: no shiur. Monday 2 February: the class begins HaMaspik l'Ovdei Hashem by Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam. The Rabbi will post on the group where to get the best hard copy. Tuesday nights: the Rambam continues, with the Moreh Nevuchim to begin once the ikkarim are finished. Chodesh tov.","d1x1Zn7Zidk":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 10 (How a Person Should Conduct Himself in This World), Chapters 18 and 19, the end of the sefer Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 12 January 2026, in person at Tiferet Hall and on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Thirteen and the Harmony Tonight the class finished the sefer, with a small celebration. Next week would be a shiur on the key takeaways of the whole book, and tonight the Rabbi also wanted to discuss options for the next sefer. Throughout the tenth essay Saadia has taught the importance of balance. He went through thirteen activities that people pursue to the exclusion of everything else: Abstinence Eating and drinking Intimacy Passion Accumulating wealth Having many children Work and developing one's career Long life Authority and power Vengeance The pursuit of intellect and wisdom Serving Hashem alone Rest In Chapter 17, last week, Saadia concluded that each of the thirteen has its place, \"even vengeance, for example, where you need to stand up and take revenge, in inverted commas, when someone's done something wrong.\" The good life balances all thirteen and takes a bit from each when it is needed. Not necessarily in equal measure: sometimes one is more appropriate than another. But a life in harmony, never taking anything to the extreme, as the Rambam later echoed. Chapter 18 shows that harmony is not only a principle for character. \"Harmony is a principle in all facets of life,\" and Saadia goes into great detail. \"We're going to need a bit of help with the English here in Chapter 18, and if anyone's musical here, help as well.\" Chapter 18: The Harmony of the Senses וראיתי לנדב בסוף המאמר הזה בזכרון מזגי המוחשים... להיותו תוספת בסדר ובבחינה למה שהקדמנו ממזג המדות At the end of the essay Saadia wants to discuss how the senses are blended, because it will help us understand what he has said about blending the traits of character. There are five senses: taste, sight, hearing, smell and touch. He will not dwell on two of them: Touch gives pleasure in only one way: what is soft. \"There's not much to discuss about that.\" Taste is also clear: it is known that a mixture gives the best flavour. Saadia's example is a dish called faludaj . The Rabbi noted that Rosenblatt's English translation does not translate the word; it seems to be a kind of sweet porridge, here made of starch, sugar and saffron. So too every mixed dish and cooked food. One ingredient on its own may taste good, but combining ingredients gives the best taste. He goes through the other three: sight, hearing and smell. Sight: colours ואומר כי המראה הנפרד מלבן או אודם או ירוק או שחור, מנהגו להחליש החוש כשיביט בו A single colour on its own, white or red or green or black, tends to weaken the sense when one stares at it, as the eye is struck by gazing at snow . Red can harm the eye, and black weakens the power of sight. The Rabbi: \"I didn't know, but there's actually a condition.\" Snow blindness is a real medical condition, photokeratitis , caused by exposure of the eyes to ultraviolet rays. \"So the point he's saying is that... staring at one thing alone without a combination isn't good for you.\" Besides the physical harm, a single colour brings little pleasure. אבל כשימזגו קצתם בקצתם : when colours are mixed, new kinds of pleasure arise and the faculties of the soul are greatly stirred. Saadia gives examples drawn from the medieval theory of the four humours: Combination of colours (as the Rabbi read them) What it arouses Red mixed with yellow or green The red bile: strength and courage Yellow mixed with black The white phlegm: lowliness and humility Black, white, yellow and red together The blood: the power of kingship and dominion Yellow mixed with yellowish-green The black bile: timidity and fear As the proportions change, different forces are aroused in the soul. \"I haven't checked each one of these whether they are scientifically correct or not... you can do that in your own time.\" The main point, he said, is one we can accept: individual colours are not as beautiful as a harmony of colours. \"If that applies to colours and taste, all the more so for our characters.\" What we see affects us A participant, AB, added a Ramban. In Parashat Vayetze, Yaakov, working as Lavan's shepherd after the fourteen years, had the flock mate while looking at the striped rods, and the offspring came out spotted. The Ramban learns from this that what a person thinks about during intimacy affects the child. \"So you see that your sight arouses different kinds of feelings within a person,\" and so too combinations of colours arouse different things in a person. Hearing: music וכן הקול הנפרד והנעימה הנפרדת... אבל המזג... The same is true of hearing. A single sound or a single rhythm, repeated, does little for the soul, \"what we call monotonous,\" and can even be bad for you. But a blend of sounds arouses its traits and powers in balance. Then comes a technical passage. \"Okay, I have no idea what this paragraph means. I'm going to read through it with the English. Anyone here understands music properly, you can help.\" There are eight rhythms. Someone in the room connected this to למנצח על השמינית , the heading of a psalm, and another recalled learning that it refers to the eight notes of a scale. \"Okay, very interesting.\" Saadia describes each rhythm in terms of beats that are moving or resting and the intervals between them, and the humour each one arouses. The Rabbi read them with the English: Rhythm Pattern (from the translation) Effect 1 Three beats: two consecutive, one quiescent With the second: the blood, and the power of kingship and dominion 2 Three beats, one quiescent and one moving (as above) 3 Two consecutive beats with no interval, then a resting beat, with a pause between rising and falling The red bile: courage and strength of heart 4 Three consecutive beats with no interval, then a gap after every three The phlegm: lowliness, submission and timidity 5 to 8 Various combinations of single and paired beats with intervals between them The black bile: traits that move a person to joy or to worry \"As I said, I have no idea what this means. Maybe someone who's an expert in music would be able to explain this paragraph.\" What struck the Rabbi was Saadia's range. \"I think it's just incredible... his expertise in so many different fields,\" whether in astrology, the science of the creation of the universe, his mathematical discussions and his treatment of atoms. \"So here he's showing us his expertise in music.\" Why kings mix the rhythms ומנהג המלכים למזוג קצתם בקצתם... ולא יצא לרבוי ברחמים ובאכזריות, ולא בגבורה ולא במורך, ולא בתוספת ובחסרון בשמחה The practice of kings is to blend the rhythms so that what they arouse fits what is needed for governing: not too much mercy and not too much cruelty, not too much courage and not too much cowardice, not too much joy and not too little. The Rabbi: different music at different times. \"When you're feeling down you play something upbeat; when you're feeling too happy... you play something a bit more low.\" Think of an orchestra, he added. \"The mixture of the violin with the piano with the flute, that causes the soul really to awaken. So you see the combination, how important it is.\" Smell: perfumes ועל זה הענין הריח הנפרד, לכל מריח ממנו כח, וכאשר ימזגו קצתם בקצתם... Each scent has its own property, and blending them gives something new. Scent Property Musk ( mor dror ) Hot and dry Camphor Cold and delicate Saffron Hot and dry Sandalwood ( ahalot ) Cold and moist Amber Moderately warm Rose water Cold and delicate When they are combined, their powers blend and become pleasing. The point of it all וכיון שההשואה בכל המוחשים יתר מעלה לאדם, כל שכן השוית מדותיו ואהבותיו שתהא מעולה לו יותר \"The final sentence, the most important sentence.\" If balance in every sense is so much better for a person, how much more so the balance of his traits and loves. Saadia takes examples from the physical world to show that spiritually and emotionally too, the best person combines different things. \"That's why it's never good to be extreme in anything.\" The Rabbi returned to kana'ut , zealotry, which \"is not necessarily something which we promote.\" Only in very particular situations is it accepted. Even Pinchas: \"once he did it, it was the right thing, but if he was to have come to ask should he have done this, he may have been told no.\" Chapter 19: The Conclusion The summary of the essay והעולה מן הכלל הזה, שאדם קונה בעולם הזה כפי תקונו, ויאכל וישתה בהיתר כפי צרכו, וישים השגחתו על החכמה והעבודה והשם הטוב The upshot: a person should acquire in this world what he needs, eat and drink what is permitted according to his need, and set his attention on wisdom, service of Hashem and a good name. From each of the thirteen he takes what is appropriate, everything in its time. וזאת תהיה הבחירה המשובחת . On eating, the Rabbi said, \"This is probably the best diet advice you could get.\" He returned to the Rambam in Hilchot Deot, which the class learned some years ago, that most medical problems come from overeating. \"People go to the gym and they want to exercise; at the end of the day... it comes down essentially to what a person brings into their body.\" Three verses from Kohelet Saadia shows that Shlomo HaMelech said the same in three verses. Verse 1. אין טוב באדם שיאכל ושתה והראה את נפשו טוב בעמלו, גם זה ראיתי אני כי מיד האלקים היא . Words What they include שיאכל ושתה Food and drink בעמלו Work, to earn money מיד האלקים What is permitted, what the Creator has graciously given, לא מה שהוא חומס וגוזל , not what is taken by force or robbery טוב Seven good things, explained below This is where perishut comes in: stop when you have eaten and drunk enough, and stop when you are tempted to make money the wrong way. Verse 2. וגם כל האדם שיאכל ושתה וראה טוב בכל עמלו, מתת אלקים היא . The same four elements: food, earning, the boundary of \"a gift of God,\" and good. Verse 3. הנה אשר ראיתי אני טוב אשר יפה לאכול ולשתות ולראות טובה בכל עמלו... כי הוא חלקו . The same four, and Saadia notes it adds אשר יפה , \"which is fitting.\" That means each trait should be used at its proper time and not at another, as the same book says, את הכל עשה יפה בעתו . \"That's how Hashem created the world, with its correct boundaries.\" Three kinds of tovah : wisdom The word טובה appears with wisdom in three verses, and each points to a different kind: Verse Saadia's explanation Proof from the end of the verse טובה חכמה עם נחלה The wisdom of nature and the creation of the world, the sciences ויתר לראי השמש : it speaks of what is under the sun טובה חכמה מגבורה Wisdom relating to the rank of kingship The parable of the small city besieged by a great king טובה חכמה מכלי קרב Wisdom relating to service of Hashem וחוטא אחד יאבד טובה הרבה : one sin destroys much good, so these are spiritual weapons Seven kinds of tov in Kohelet # The good Verse 1 A good name and reputation טוב שם משמן טוב 2 Remembering death when the time comes, and not forgetting it טוב ללכת אל בית אבל מלכת אל בית משתה 3 Anger for the honour of the Creator טוב כעס משחוק 4 Seeing the end of things טוב אחרית דבר מראשיתו 5 Patience טוב ארך רוח מגבה רוח 6 The company of the wise and righteous טוב לשמע גערת חכם 7 Believing one is not immune from sin , so as to be humble before Hashem טוב אשר תאחז בזה וגם מזה אל תנח ידך On the third, the Rabbi explained that it cannot mean real anger, \"because anger is not allowed.\" Ka'as here means standing up and being insistent about what is right. On the fourth: \"don't just look at the beginning, look at the end, look at the consequence.\" On the sixth: listen to the Chacham \"even if he's telling you something which you don't like.\" On the seventh: \"never overestimate your ability not to sin\"; we are fragile, spiritually and not just physically. Eleven things רמז אלו הי\"א דברים Shlomo HaMelech, examining the affairs of the world, hinted at eleven things: seven he called טוב , three he called טובה , and one he called יפה , each thing in its right balance. Put together with eating, drinking and earning a living within boundaries, these give a person the good life. They come to a person when he lives the balanced life. The purpose of the whole book כי כל הספר איננו מועיל כי אם לזכות הלב וכוונת תיקונו Saadia ends: the entire book is of benefit only for the purification of the heart and the intention to improve it, the refinement of a person's character and the focus to keep bettering himself throughout life. אם אתה הכינות לבך , and David's words, בכל לבי דרשתיך... בלבי צפנתי אמרתך . May our hearts be purified and humbled before Hashem, as it says, יען רך לבבך ותכנע מפני ה' . כי הנאכל והנשתה והנראה והנשמע, עושים עם כונת הלב יותר ממה שעושים בלעדיה What is eaten, drunk, seen and heard has a greater effect when the heart is focused on it. The Rabbi called it \"a bit of mindfulness here as well.\" It is another reason people overeat: they don't focus when they are eating. \"If you focus when you eat, then you eat what you need, because you take the real pleasure out of it.\" So too, focusing on avodat Hashem makes a person far more successful. Saadia's closing prayer: ואשאל מהאל יתברך ישימנו בין המאמינים ולא מן התועים, וישים בלבנו אהבתו ויראתו, כדי שנזכה לחיי העולם הזה ולחיי העולם הבא May Hashem place us among the believers and not among those who go astray, and put His love and His fear in our hearts, so that we merit the life of this world and the life of the World to Come. תם ונשלם ספר אמונות ודעות, תהלות לאל מגלה צפונות \"Praise to God who reveals the secrets. Chazak u'baruch.\" The Siyum and What Comes Next The class began the sefer on 22 January 2024 , so it took exactly two years. Saadia, the Rabbi recalled, was the very first to write Jewish theology in a structured way. Next week he would go through his notes and draw out ten to fifteen main takeaways from the ten essays, and in a couple of weeks start a new sefer. Over the last six or seven years the class has learned the Ramchal's Derech Hashem and Da'at Tevunot, and now Emunot VeDeot. The three options he was considering: Option The Rabbi's description The Moreh Nevuchim (the Rambam's Guide for the Perplexed) Will take a long time and needs consistency; \"not really something you can dip in and out of.\" Some parts are quite esoteric and hard to comprehend. \"The most challenging but equally, I believe, the most rewarding.\" HaMaspik l'Ovdei Hashem by Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam Written in Arabic as al-Kifayah , \"what is sufficient\" to serve Hashem. First translated into Hebrew only in 1965, by Rabbi Sassoon, in honour of the wedding of his son-in-law, Rabbi Gabbay of Ohel David, where the translation was given out. Feldheim later published an English translation based on it. \"I wouldn't call it a musar book... but it's more towards musar,\" and the parts he had seen were thought-provoking. He had never learned it properly, \"so this would be a nice opportunity to study together.\" The Kuzari of Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi A dialogue, like Da'at Tevunot, with \"a more mystical framework,\" quite different from the Rambam and Emunot VeDeot He invited preferences from everyone, in the room and on Zoom. Summary Harmony is a principle of all creation, not only of character. Saadia shows it in the senses. Touch and taste: softness pleases; mixed dishes taste best. Sight: a single colour tires and even harms the eye (snow blindness); blends of colours arouse different powers in the soul. Hearing: eight rhythms, each stirring a different humour. Kings blend them to avoid excess mercy or cruelty, courage or cowardice. Think of an orchestra. Smell: perfumes of different natures blend into something pleasing. Therefore the balance of one's traits is all the more valuable. Zealotry is generally not the Jewish way. Chapter 19: acquire what you need, eat and drink what is permitted as you need, and give your attention to wisdom, service of Hashem and a good name. Kohelet hints at eleven goods: seven tov , three tovah , one yafeh . The purpose of the whole book is the refinement of the heart. What we do with focus affects us more. Chazak u'baruch. Coming Up Next week: the key takeaways from the whole of Emunot VeDeot. In a couple of weeks: the new Monday sefer, chosen from the Moreh Nevuchim, HaMaspik and the Kuzari.","TbVGJLw8cvo":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 10 (How a Person Should Conduct Himself in This World), Chapters 14 to 17: wisdom, worship, rest, and the right combination, opening with a discussion of free will from the end of Chapter 13 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 5 January 2026, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. The Plan for the Last Weeks The first Monday shiur after the winter break. The plan: Tonight: Chapters 14, 15 and 16, the last three of the thirteen extremes (and, as it turned out, Chapter 17 too). Next week: Chapters 18 and 19, completing the sefer. The week after: a summary of the entire sefer, a small celebration, and a decision on the new Monday sefer. The Rabbi was considering the Moreh Nevuchim, the Kuzari, and HaMaspik l'Ovdei Hashem by Rabbeinu Avraham, the Rambam's son. The other shiurim continue as usual: the Rambam's Principles of Faith on Tuesday (up to the sixth principle) and Hilchot Shabbat on Thursday. Recap: ten extremes so far In the tenth essay Saadia is teaching how important balance is: never to take even important things alone. So far: abstinence, eating and drinking, intimacy, passion, accumulating wealth, having many children, building up the world, long life, power and authority, and revenge. Tonight: חכמה , wisdom; עבודה , worship; and מנוחה , tranquility. \"Each of these things have their places, but they're not to be taken alone.\" Part 1: Can a Person Harm Someone Hashem Has Not Decreed Should Be Harmed? Before the break the Rabbi had flagged one sentence at the end of the chapter on revenge, connected to the recent parshiot and the subject of a machloket . ומה תוחלת הנכזבה לעמוד כנגד בורא השמים והארץ, כדברינו אנחנו אנשי האמת What false hope it is to stand against the Creator of heaven and earth, according to us, the people of truth. Why take revenge, when everything is in Hashem's hands? The implication: if Hashem has decreed something for a person, no one else can change it. Saadia is explicit in Essay 4 Saadia says this even more clearly in the fourth essay, on free will. If A kills B, A has done wrong, but why is it fair to B? המות מעשה האלהים וההרג מעשה העול, ובחכמה חייבה המות, אילו לא היה העול היה מת בסיבה אחרת Death is the act of God; the killing is the act of the wrongdoer. B was meant to die anyway, and would have died some other way. A is punished because he chose to commit murder. וכן השאלה על הגנב... האבוד מעשה הבורא והגניבה מעשה האדם, וכאשר חייבה החכמה אבוד הדבר ההוא, אילו לא היה גונב היה אבד בפנים אחרים If A steals a hundred pounds from B, B was going to lose a hundred pounds anyway: an extra fine, money dropped in the street, \"whatever it may be.\" A is punished because he should not have taken it. \"I don't think one can doubt this.\" For Saadia, our free will extends only as far as what Hakadosh Baruch Hu has decreed. And it works both ways. You see a homeless man and give him a hundred pounds for a night in a hotel. According to Saadia, he would somehow have found a room anyway. You are rewarded because it came about through you. \"What that means is it quite limits our potential of our actions.\" The second approach: free will can change the outcome \"Many disagree with this... and I prefer the second approach much more, because it gives us much more free will.\" The Malbim on David and Gad. When offered a choice of punishments, David said: צר לי מאד, נפלה נא ביד ה' כי רבים רחמיו, וביד אדם אל אפלה Let me fall into the hand of Hashem, for His mercy is great, but not into the hand of man. The Malbim explains: famine is a natural punishment that comes from Hashem, but the sword of enemies depends on בחירה , their free will. So David chose the punishment that is in Hashem's direct providence, where His mercy can intervene. In human hands, \"we don't know what can happen.\" Although Hashem decrees things upon us for good or bad, other human beings have the ability to change them. The Or HaChaim on Yosef and the pit. From Parashat Vayeshev, a couple of weeks earlier. The brothers said: לכו ונהרגהו ונשלכהו באחד הברות ואמרנו חיה רעה אכלתהו, ונראה מה יהיו חלמתיו The Or HaChaim explains their reasoning. Yosef dreamt he would be a leader. If we manage to kill him, that proves his dreams were false, and a false dreamer is chayav mitah . So killing him would retroactively prove we were right to. \"A very elegant solution.\" Then Reuven says, לא נכנו נפש... השליכו אתו אל הבור הזה... ויד אל תשלחו בו . What is the difference, if he will die in the pit anyway? The simple answer is that Reuven planned to come back and save him. But the Or HaChaim says: לפי שהאדם בעל בחירה ורצון, ויכול להרוג מי שלא נתחייב מיתה, מה שאין כן חיות רעות לא יפגעו באדם אם לא יתחייב מיתה לשמים Because a person has free choice, he can kill someone who was not sentenced to death. Wild animals, however, will not harm a person unless Heaven has decreed it. That is why the verse says ויצלהו מידם , from their hands, meaning from their free will. Reuven's answer refutes their test: Yosef might be a true prophet, and they could still kill him, because free will can override the decree. So killing him would prove nothing. \"There's no words clearer than this.\" A person can be decreed on Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur to live the coming year: he will not die of famine or a car accident. \"And then a wicked person comes in the middle of the year, says I don't like you, and goes and kills you.\" The Rabbi added that the Zohar says the same, and that Rabbi Tatz has a book, Free Will and Destiny , with several chapters bringing many sources for this view. He tries to reinterpret Saadia too, \"I think it's very difficult. I think Saadia is very clear.\" Why it matters Saadia The Malbim, Or HaChaim, Zohar Can I harm someone Hashem has not decreed? No. It would have happened anyway Yes. Free will can go against the decree Can I help someone beyond his decree? It would have happened anyway; I am rewarded because it came through me Yes. If Hashem decreed poverty on him, and I help him, I have changed it What our actions mean We are the channel We are \"actually changing the world\" \"I prefer the second approach because it gives us much more autonomy... when we do good we're doing good and we're actually changing the world, because without us the world wouldn't be the same.\" Mark's question Mark noted that in the first approach you still have free will: Hashem gives you the opportunity by putting you on that road past the homeless man, and you choose whether to give or walk past. \"Correct.\" In the first approach, if you don't help, someone else will; he will somehow find money for a room. In the second, \"if I decide not to help this person, he'll stay there.\" Chapter 14: Wisdom ( Chochmah ) Their argument מתלמידי החכמים יש אומרים שאין ראוי לאדם להתעסק בעולם הזה בדבר חוץ מבקשת החכמה Some scholars say a person should do nothing in this world except pursue wisdom. As the chapter makes clear, Saadia means both Torah wisdom and the sciences. Through it one comes to understand the elements and their mixtures, nature and science, and to know the Creator, the heavens and the power of the spheres: astronomy and theology. It is sweet and pleasant to the soul: ודעת לנפשך ינעם . It heals the soul of foolishness: רפאות תהי לשרך . It nourishes like food: ושקוי לעצמותיך . It is like crystal and pearls on kings: כי לוית חן הם לראשך וענקים לגרגרתיך . One who does not understand it is as if not a human being: כי לא יבינו אל פעלת ה' ואל מעשה ידיו . Saadia: all true, but not alone ומצאתי כל מה שאמרו אמת, אבל מקום הטעות בו מה שאמרו שלא יתעסקו בדבר זולתה \"There's nothing like the pursuit of wisdom.\" The mistake is saying there is nothing else. 1. Who will feed and house you? Wisdom alone does not sustain a person. If he does not provide food, shelter and covering, what then? \"Study, and let others fork out for you?\" ואם ישליך את עצמו בצרכו על אנשים אחרים, היה נבזה ואין סומכין עליו ולא מקבלים דבריו If he throws himself on others for his needs, he is despised, no one relies on him and no one accepts his words. \"And unfortunately I think we sometimes see this.\" Of course someone who studies all day, supported by others, will gain great knowledge. \"Problem is no one's going to accept that intellect, that wisdom from you, because you are needy for them.\" As Kohelet says, וחכמת המסכן בזויה ודבריו אינם נשמעים . 2. Living on dry bread does not work either. If he says he will not rely on others and will live on the barest scraps, the refinement and subtlety of his wisdom will be lost. To focus, analyse and grasp properly, a person needs decent food and a decent home, everything in place: תפוחי זהב במשכיות כסף, דבר דבר על אפניו . The man and the Levi'im Saadia brings two proofs that Hashem provides for those devoted to wisdom: The man in the wilderness. Hashem fed Bnei Yisrael with fine food so that they could devote themselves to wisdom: ויצא העם ולקטו דבר יום ביומו למען אנסנו הילך בתורתי אם לא . The Rabbi noted that this is not the literal meaning, which is about the test of gathering only one day's portion. Saadia reads it as: I am sustaining them so that, free of distraction, they can study My Torah. The Levi'im's tithe. Levi was one of thirteen tribes, yet received a tenth of the produce of all the others, כדי שיהיה מזונם ריוח , so that their sustenance would be ample. \"When I read this I was fascinated. I've never thought about this.\" By default they were getting more than everyone else, more than an equal share, so that they would have no worries and could study and teach, which was their duty. A participant added that since the Levi'im were a small tribe, they received even more per head; the Rabbi agreed, though Saadia does not say it. 3. If everyone did it. If everyone agreed with this group, there would be no children and no marriage. And if people studied only one kind of wisdom, Torah alone or science alone, the other would be lost. Saadia says the love of worldly knowledge was planted in us to serve as a help to this work, to know קשט אמרי אמת . The Rabbi's conclusion \"Of course, pursuit of chochmah is fantastic, but it should not come at the expense of a person's basic necessities.\" There is nothing like people who dedicate themselves to Torah study all day: \"that's an incredible thing, and may we merit to be part of those kinds of people.\" But it has to come with a plan for how they will look after themselves and those who rely on them. Chapter 15: Worship ( Avodah ) Their argument ימצאו אנשים רבים שאומרים כי הטוב שיתעסק בו האדם בעולם הזה עבודת אלהיו בלבד Many say the best thing is to serve Hashem and nothing else: fast by day, rise at night to praise and thank Him (the Rabbi mentioned tikkun chatzot and Tehillim), and leave all the affairs of this world, because Hashem will provide the food and needs of one who serves Him. Serving Him is a great delight: כי נעים נאוה תהלה , and עבדו את ה' בשמחה . The reward with the Creator is great: וחמלתי עליהם כאשר יחמל איש על בנו העבד אתו . Saadia: true, but not alone כל מה שסיפרו בעבודת הבורא אמת וברור... אך מקום טעותם היה התבודדותם לבדם You cannot praise Hashem enough: גדול ה' ומהלל מאד ולגדלתו אין חקר . The mistake is isolating it and saying a person needs nothing else. 1. Without food the body cannot survive, and without marriage and children, service of Hashem itself will not continue. If one generation all did this, they would die and worship would die with them. Service is meant for fathers, sons and grandsons: למען תירא את ה' אלהיך... אתה ובנך ובן בנך כל ימי חייך . \"Judaism is about the family, it's about the generations, it's about passing it on.\" 2. The hermit cannot keep most of the Torah. Saadia recalls his distinction, from the essay on the mitzvot, between מצוות שכליות , rational commandments, and מצוות שמעיות , revealed commandments. Service of Hashem means all of them. How can a recluse keep: the mitzvot of honest weights and measures? \"But I don't do business, because I just spend the whole day saying Tehillim.\" the mitzvot of justice, לא תטה משפט לא תכיר פנים ? \"I don't go to courts. I've nothing to do with this world.\" the laws of forbidden and permitted, pure and impure, להבדיל בין הטמא ובין הטהור ? the mitzvot of the field, tithes and charity? \"But I don't have a field, because all I do is pray to God the whole day.\" If he answers that others will do business and run the courts, then הם עובדים ולא הוא : they are the ones serving, כי בהם נשלמה עבודת הבורא ולא בו . The Rabbi drew out a principle: \"A system cannot be correct... which is only right for some people but not everyone.\" Some people are more attuned to certain things. But if this is presented as the way to serve Hashem, then everyone should aspire to it, and then who will keep the rest of the Torah? 3. Trust in Hashem works through causes. והוא ששמו יתברך שם לכל דבר סיבה ומנהג קבוע שצריך שיבוקש מהפנים ההם Their trust is right, but they forget that Hashem has given everything a cause and a fixed order through which it must be sought. \"You cannot just sit back and expect the food, the money, to arrive on you.\" If they say that trust covers everything, then יבטחו עליו בשכר העבודה גם כן, שיגמל גמולו בלא עבודה : let them also trust Him to reward them without any service at all. If that is unreasonable, because service is the cause of reward, then the same applies to sustenance, which has its causes too. Sometimes Hashem does do things by sign and wonder, without a prior cause, but He does not make it a regular practice to change the nature He placed in the world. Chapter 16: Rest ( Menuchah ) Their argument אמרו אנשים כי המנוחה טובה מכל מה שיבקש האדם בעולם הזה The thirteenth group says the best thing in life is rest. \"The longer you can sleep, the longer you can stay in bed, the longer you can stay on your sunbed, that's the best life.\" It heals the soul and removes the body's weariness. The harder a person works, the more he longs for rest. \"How often do we crash on our beds at the end of the week and Shabbat.\" Kings rest more than anyone; if it were not good, they would not choose it. It relaxes the body, improves sleep and digestion and purifies the blood. The Torah praises it, ומצאו מרגוע לנפשתיכם , and commands rest on Shabbat and the festivals, so surely the whole week should be like that. Saadia: they are foolish כי המנוחה לא תשלם לאדם אלא אחר השגחה גדולה ותיקון צרכיו והכנת כל אודותיו, ואחר כך ינוח וישקוט True rest only comes after a person has worked hard and arranged all his needs. הכן בחוץ מלאכתך ועתדה בשדה לך, אחר ובנית ביתך . אבל המנוחה לבדה מבלתי דבר מעליה... ענין עצלה Rest by itself, with nothing before it, is not rest. It is עצלה , laziness. Menuchah versus atzlah The Rabbi gave an analogy. Someone can say, \"I want to rest and sit in the sauna the whole day.\" That's laziness. But when someone has worked hard, done a lot of exercise, and his body feels weak from exertion, and then he sits and relaxes, \"ah, and that's something else. Then you feel your body's being revived. That's menuchah .\" What laziness does כי האדם כשתלוהו העצלה יבוא רשו ויחסר כל טוב Poverty. \"If it's not your generation, it'll be your children, or your grandchildren.\" Like the man who buys homes for his great-grandchildren: \"there's going to come a time when that wealth runs out.\" מעט שנות מעט תנומות מעט חבק ידים לשכב, ובא כמהלך רישך ומחסרך כאיש מגן . Sadness. He cannot prepare food or provide shelter, and lives in grief, remembering the rest he craved: תאות עצל תמיתנו כי מאנו ידיו לעשות . Losing the mitzvot. He ends up abandoning prayer, fasting, Torah and mitzvot. Laziness is linked to wickedness, and its opposite is righteousness: כל היום התאוה תאוה, וצדיק יתן ולא יחשך . Illness. Heaviness, bloating, weakness, and conditions of the legs, including varicose veins, kidney trouble and חולי הפיל . The Rabbi explained that the English translation calls this \"Barbados leg,\" a swelling of the leg like an elephant's ( pil is Hebrew for elephant). The Torah itself says ששת ימים תעבד ועשית כל מלאכתך, וביום השביעי תשבת : work, then rest. אפילו מי שסיפקו אחרים אינו ראוי לבטלה Even someone who is fully provided for should not be idle: צופיה הליכות ביתה ולחם עצלות לא תאכל . The Eshet Chayil works even when she has everything. When rest is right Saadia says the soul's love of rest was given so that it would long for the tranquility the Creator has prepared for it in the World to Come: והיה מעשה הצדקה שלום ועבדת הצדקה השקט ובטח עד עולם, וישב עמי בנוה שלום . The Rabbi added the Gemara: Shabbat is one sixtieth of Olam HaBa , a taste of the rest to come. Chapter 17: The Right Combination The Rabbi listed all thirteen once more: abstinence, eating, intimacy, passion, wealth, children, building, long life, power, revenge, wisdom, worship and rest. \"Each one has many benefits.\" Even revenge has its place, in punishing those who do wrong. Each alone fails שכל מי שרוצה להתנהג באחד משלשה עשר האלו לבדו... יעוות לו חפצו ויקצר מלהגיע מה שאוהב Anyone who takes one of these alone, enjoying only it and abandoning the rest, will find his goal twisted and fall short of what he wants. As Chapter 3 said from Kohelet, anything crooked is deficient. אבל כשיתקבצו הענינים האלה כולם, הוא הנכוחה הגמורה When they are combined, that is the complete right path. Not equal shares ואין ראוי גם כן שיקח מכל אחד מהם חלק השוה בשוה, אלא יקח מכל מין מהם השיעור אשר הוא ראוי לקחת כפי מה שתחייב החכמה והתורה He should not take an equal share of each, but the amount each deserves, as wisdom and Torah require. The Rabbi: at different periods of life a person needs more of one thing, more study at one time, more focus on career, on money and the future, at another. \"Different times, different things, but you need a combination.\" Saadia's portrait of the balanced person Food, drink and intimacy: he takes what he needs to live and to have children, and enjoys what is permitted. Excess: when he wants more than is right, or to take from others, he stops himself. When he cannot stop: ישלח בה מדת הפרישות , he sends out abstinence until he loathes it. The Rabbi saw here the Rambam's idea: if you feel yourself falling into something, you must become stronger against it, and perhaps bend back and cut down even on permitted things. Money and children: he looks after what the Creator has graciously given him, according to his love for them. The land: he works it as he needs. Temptation at work: if desire pushes him to do something forbidden, he brings out abstinence. This world for the next: he lives in this world for the sake of the World to Come, recognising it is the vestibule and the preparation for Olam HaBa. Power and revenge: he does not seek them. If authority falls into his lap, he accepts it to uphold the laws of the Torah and justice among people. Rest and laziness: he keeps away from laziness entirely. Free time: whatever time remains after earning his living, he devotes to העבודה והחכמה , prayer and study, Torah and science. יהיה משובח בשני העולמות Then he will be praised in both worlds: מכל משמר נצר לבך כי ממנו תוצאות חיים . Like a compound medicine Saadia compares this to bodies compounded of the elements, and to medicine. A prescription takes three zuz of one ingredient, four gerah of another, half a zuz of another, half a gerah or an issar of another. לא יתכן שיקח מכל אחד משקל שוה : you would never give all ingredients in equal weight. כן הענין באלו המדות : so with these traits. Summary Free will and decree: Saadia holds that no one can harm or help a person beyond what Hashem decreed; the doer is judged because it came through him. The Malbim (David's choice of famine over the sword), the Or HaChaim (Reuven and the pit) and the Zohar hold that free will can override the decree. The Rabbi prefers the second: our actions really change the world. Wisdom: nothing compares to it, but it cannot replace earning a living. Relying on others makes a scholar's words unheeded; living on scraps dulls his mind. Hashem fed Israel with man and gave the Levi'im a generous tithe so they could study. Worship: true, but a recluse cannot keep the Torah's mitzvot of business, justice and the field, and cannot pass it on to children. Trust in Hashem works through causes, just as reward comes through service. Rest: menuchah follows work; without work it is atzlah , laziness, which leads to poverty, sadness, sin and illness. Rest is a taste of the World to Come. The combination: all thirteen, each in the measure wisdom and Torah require, like a compound medicine. Then a person is praised in both worlds. Coming Up Next week, 6:00 pm: in person at Tiferet Hall for those in London, and on Zoom. Chapters 18 and 19, on sight, hearing, smell and music, read together with the English, and a mini siyum. The week after: a summary of the whole sefer and a discussion of the next one: the Moreh Nevuchim, the Kuzari or HaMaspik. Suggestions welcome. The class began Saadia two years ago, after finishing Da'at Tevunot. This week: the Tuesday Rambam shiur and Thursday Hilchot Shabbat continue.","AcW5ZW6MajY":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 10 (How a Person Should Conduct Himself in This World), Chapters 10 to 13: building up the world, long life, status and power, and revenge Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 8 December 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Six Extremes So Far This was the last shiur of the term. The Rabbi said he would decide over the winter holidays which sefer the class would learn after finishing Emunot VeDeot. The tenth essay is about the best way to live. Saadia opened by explaining that the most important thing in life is balance , never being extreme in any field, and listed thirteen areas where people often go to extremes. Six have been covered: # Extreme Saadia's correct use 1 Abstinence Only to keep away from what is forbidden: food, drink or relationships 2 Food and drink For sustaining life. \"Of course you can enjoy what you eat and drink, but primary purpose is to sustain a person\" 3 Intimacy Primarily to continue the human race, though it has other purposes 4 Passion Within a marriage 5 Wealth The desire exists so that we look after our property, like Yaakov Avinu in last week's parasha, Vayishlach 6 Children Not just quantity but quality: within a framework of how we will provide for them and educate them Tonight: building up the world, long life, and status, and, if time allowed, revenge. It did. Chapter 10: Building Up the World ( Yishuv HaOlam ) Their argument ראו אנשים אחרים כי הישוב טוב מכל מה שיתעסק בו האדם The seventh group says the best thing a person can do is develop and build up his environment: Houses are essential: without them a person has no shelter from heat and cold, and nowhere to gather his friends. Fields are needed for food, without which no one can survive. Both bring a person joy, breadth of heart and serenity. ויתרון ארץ בכל הוא, מלך לשדה נעבד : a man with a worked field feels like a king. Kings and princes take pride in their buildings, מלכים ויועצי ארץ הבונים חרבות למו . The Torah promises it as a reward: ובתים מלאים כל טוב אשר לא מלאת . The Rabbi added a modern version: \"Life is about making this world a better place. You often hear this.\" That's lovely, \"but then the next generation, what's their purpose?\" If that is all, the question just passes on. Saadia: wrong, when it excludes everything else והתבוננתי בדעת אלה ומצאתים טועים, כאשר חייבו עזיבת כל דבר והעסק בשער הזה Building needs other wisdom. No one can build properly without knowledge of the land, its regions, and mathematics to work out measurements. Without broad knowledge \"the thing sought will not be reached.\" It cannot stand alone. Worry, toil and other people's money. One who throws himself into building ends up in great anxiety and strain, spending money, even other people's money, in his craving to finish what he started. The Rabbi: \"I would say councils may fall under this. They have a desire to improve their council, they end up having to tax their residents more.\" הוי בנה ביתו בלא צדק ועליותיו בלא משפט, ברעהו יעבד חנם . You may want lots of property, but not at the expense of integrity. Disappointment. Even if he completes his project, he may see something wrong in it and feel all his toil was in vain. ויצא חירם מצר לראות את הערים אשר נתן לו שלמה ולא ישרו בעיניו . Constant anguish and pressure. Jealousy. It arouses the envy of others and the anger of kings: בתי גזית בניתם ולא תשבו בם . Fields are not in your control. They grow by the will of the Creator. You need rain and Hashem's blessing. There may be drought, blight, mildew and locusts: רעב כי יהיה בארץ, דבר כי יהיה שדפון ירקון ארבה . Taxes. The demands of kings and their officials can leave him almost nothing. The Rabbi: \"In some countries you're paying loads of tax to the extent where it's actually not worth making any more money.\" ברבות הטובה רבו אוכליה . Exploiting the poor. Even if he gathers his crop, he will want to hold it back for times of scarcity and sell at extravagant prices, taking what little the poor have, לקנות בכסף דלים ואביון בעבור נעלים, ומפל בר נשביר . Why the desire exists ולא נתנה אהבת הישוב בלב האדם כי אם לתקן בו צרכו בלבד The love of building was placed in us only so that we provide for our needs: ויושב שם רעבים ויכוננו עיר מושב, ויזרעו שדות ויטעו כרמים . A place to live and food to eat, \"and that's about it.\" The Rabbi linked it to the Mishnah in Avot, מרבה נכסים מרבה דאגה : more property, more worry. \"Yes, we do have a desire to have nice homes and to have nice fields and that's good... but that's it. It shouldn't go beyond that.\" He did not want to dwell on it: \"it's pretty straightforward.\" Chapter 11: Long Life \"This is interesting because you hear this a lot nowadays: people who think life is all about living the longest life.\" Their argument ראו אחרים כי הטוב שידרוש האדם בעולם הזה השגת אריכות חייו With long life a person can achieve whatever he wants, in Torah and in worldly matters. Without it, what can he keep? The Torah constantly promises it: למען יאריכון ימיך , למען תחיה וימים רבים . Its causes, they say: good food and drink in moderation, not overexerting oneself, keeping oneself happy, and avoiding danger, spiritual or physical. The Rabbi noted that the link between happiness and longevity \"is really proven nowadays as well.\" So they exercise, look after their bodies and spend their lives pursuing a long life. \"Obviously it's a bit circular, that.\" Saadia's first argument: it does not work ואמנם כי הדברים האלה מתקנים הגוף, אבל אינם סיבה לחיים These things make for a healthy body but are not the cause of a long life. We find people who live this way and die young, and others who do the opposite and live long. Some bodies are strongly built and fail quickly; others are frail and endure. And if comfort and good food lengthened life, היו המלכים מאריכים ימים יותר מכל אדם , kings would outlive everyone. Here the Rabbi disagreed. \"I'm not sure if this would be true nowadays.\" There are exceptions, but statistically, the more a person looks after his health, the longer he lives. He asked the class which country has the highest life expectancy. Someone suggested Japan: \"Japan's very close.\" The answer, from what he had read about two years earlier: Monaco, 89 years . England was around 81 or 82. \"On average, people from wealthy countries who have had better food and better lifestyles, they live longer.\" So \"that's not his strongest argument.\" Saadia's second argument: what would you want it for? Saadia returns to the pessimistic view of this world he has expressed before in the book. The Rabbi stressed, as each time, \"this is his outlook, not necessarily agreed upon by all.\" כי אדם כל אשר יארכו ימיו ירבו יגונו ודאגותיו וצרותיו The longer one lives, the more one's sorrows, worries and troubles: צרות לבבי הרחיבו . And the more sins one accumulates, renewed every day. Then he goes through the stages of life: Stage Saadia's description Verse Childhood Foolish, knows nothing אולת קשורה בלב נער Youth Moves into recklessness and waste נער משלח מביש אמו Adulthood Enters toil and labour נפש עמל עמלה לו Old age Everything he desired is forbidden to him and he must still struggle ימי הרעה... אשר תאמר אין לי בהם חפץ In old age a person loses his radiance, moisture, sensitivity and strength, and becomes like a cloud whose rain has gone, dry and of no use: עד אשר לא תחשך השמש והאור והירח והכוכבים ושבו העבים אחר הגשם . \"A nice analogy,\" said the Rabbi. \"You've got a cloud, but there's nothing there.\" So which part of life is the best? When young you lack understanding; by the time you have it you must work hard; by the time you stop working, life is no longer worth living. \"So what do you want long life for?\" The Rabbi pushed back: \"If you take the argument to its logical conclusion, then we should commit suicide, right?... I find that very difficult.\" Why we love life והאדם הצדיק אוהב חיים בעולם הזה כי אם בעבור שמדרגה יגיע בה ויעלה ממנה לעולם הבא, לא בעבור עצמו The righteous love life in this world only as a stage from which to rise to the World to Come, not for its own sake. That, the Rabbi said, \"is agreed upon by all.\" ולמה נתנה אהבתה בלב האדם, כי אם שלא ימית את עצמו כשתבוא עליו צרה Why did Hashem place the love of life in us? So that a person would not kill himself when trouble comes, as it says, ואך את דמכם לנפשתיכם אדרש . Sadly, the Rabbi noted, even that powerful drive is sometimes not enough. The Rabbi: long days, not long years \"Not necessarily, I don't think necessarily one has to agree with such a view.\" David HaMelech says, ימי שנותינו בהם שבעים שנה ואם בגבורת שמונים שנה ורהבם עמל ואון , which we say at a funeral. That does not mean life is not worth living. Life is worth living while recognising its difficulties and challenges. His own point, which he said was not Saadia's: the Torah never says \"long life.\" It says אריכות ימים , למען יאריכון ימיך , long days . \"That's why we wish in English, when someone dies, long life. It's not precise. We don't have an expression in Hebrew for long life.\" ואברהם זקן בא בימים , as the class read a couple of weeks earlier: he came with his days. It is not the accumulation of years but the accumulation of days, which means maximising a person's potential. Someone can live many years with empty days, never having unlocked their ability. Others live short lives in years but very long lives in what they achieved: The Arizal died at 38. The Ramchal, Rabbeinu Moshe Chaim Luzzatto , died at 38. Rebbe Nachman of Breslov lived only 38 years. \"They had short days, but they had a long life in the sense that their days were filled.\" The conclusion is close to Saadia's ending: there is no intrinsic value in pursuing life for its own sake. Life is for making the most of it, preparing ourselves and maximising our potential \"so that we're able to create the best ourselves and the best identity for the world to come.\" And the circularity is an obvious fallacy: if the purpose of life is a long life, what is the purpose of the long life? Chapter 12: Status, Power and Dominion ( Serarah ) Their argument ראו אחרים כי הגדול שבמה שיתעסק בו האדם בעולם הזה הגאוה והגדולה והשררה The ninth group says the greatest thing to pursue, more than money, children or life itself, is being raised above others and holding power over them. The soul naturally inclines to greatness, and finds lowliness hard, ותראה שקשה לה השפלות לאדם אחד ולהודות לו : it is hard to submit to another person or concede to him. The Rabbi drew out the psychology. \"Why is it so hard for us to apologize to someone or to admit we're wrong? Because that means I'm lower than you.\" If I admit a mistake, you did better than me, so you are higher than me. \"I can't accept that you're higher than me, so therefore we find ways of not apologizing. That's the simple psychology.\" Power strengthens a person, giving him a sense of height, energy and expansiveness. Giving orders is pleasant. \"You can tell people, do this and don't do that. That's a lovely thing to be able to do.\" Without authority the world would not be ordered. Kings lead in war and defence; judges judge; officers correct those who do not know. The Rabbi: in every industry \"you need the CEO, the people at the top to lead things.\" The Torah gives it as a blessing to the righteous: in Toldot, a couple of weeks earlier, Yitzchak blessed Yaakov, יעבדוך עמים וישתחו לך לאמים, הוה גביר לאחיך . Saadia: necessary, but they forgot wisdom מה שזכרוהו מסדור העולם בשמירה ובמשפט, הענין הנני מודה בו The need for authority to keep society in order: Saadia agrees completely. But they have taken it too far. סידור העולם לא יקום כי אם בחכמה : the world is ordered only by wisdom, and they dropped wisdom and made power itself the goal. The Rabbi: \"Who do we put in positions of authority? We put wise people.\" You cannot pursue power for its own sake. You pursue other things, develop yourself fully, and then you may be someone able to lead. The damage of arrogance Saadia then lists what they forgot: He leaves his path. When a person grows proud and great in his own eyes, he becomes arrogant towards his relatives and friends, and sees himself as if he were יחיד בעולם , unique in the world. He despises all advice. Whatever anyone says, he rejects. לתאוה יבקש נפרד, בכל תושיה יתגלע : the one who sets himself apart rejects all counsel. \"Because I'm separate, I'm distinct, I'm unique.\" He dismisses the experience of elders, what they learned over long lives. דרך אויל ישר בעיניו . His affairs go wrong, because he takes no advice, and when they go well he credits his own strength: כי אמר בכח ידי עשיתי , of the king of Assyria. He argues with craftsmen about their craft and with scholars about their science, and tries to refute them. People think him a fool, though they won't say it to his face. ראית איש חכם בעיניו, תקוה לכסיל ממנו . The Rabbi: at least the simpleton knows he needs advice. \"A person who's arrogant and thinks they know everything, they've got no hope.\" חכם עצל בעיניו משבעה משיבי טעם . He speaks against kings and rulers, and in the end even against the wisdom of the Creator: ואמרו איכה ידע אל ויש דעה בעליון . He is hated, and so lives in fear and mistrust, and his plans are frustrated: מפר מחשבות ערומים ולא תעשינה ידיהם תושיה . Even if he reaches the top If he does attain the power and kingship he wanted, \"you wanted to be a prince, you wanted to be an MP, you wanted to be a great minister,\" then: Jealousy and hatred, even from people he never harmed: בלי עון ירוצון . As soon as they see him in power, they want to kill him: ועתה לכו ונהרגהו . The Rabbi tied this to that week's parasha, Vayeshev. Yosef had his dreams, and his brothers felt he was aloof and above them, \"and so they felt it's time to kill him.\" They threw him in a pit; \"of course, it was all part of Hashem's plan.\" Constant fear. He can never eat flour that is not sealed or drink water that has not been guarded, for fear of poison. Every day he sits under the edge of a sword, כי כפסע ביני ובין המות . No one tells him the truth. People do not tell him when he is wrong, because of his position, so he carries on. Meanwhile they speak badly of him behind his back, and in the end the Creator's judgment falls on him: ובית המלך האזינו כי לכם המשפט . Why Hashem gave us the love of status ולא נטע הבורא יתברך בנפש האדם אהבת השררה והגאוה, כי אם בעבור שתכסוף בהם לגמולו לעולם הבא \"This is very interesting.\" Hashem planted the desire to be raised above others only so that we would long for our reward in the World to Come, as it says, לא יגרע מצדיק עיניו ואת מלכים לכסא, וישיבם לנצח ויגבהו . The Rabbi brought the Gemara that in the World to Come each person is singed by the canopy of his fellow : \"I was in class with that person. How did he end up having that huge portion of the world to come, and I'm in such a lowly position?\" That needs explaining, he said, but it shows the drive. \"I want to be better than you in the sense that I want to be the best person that I can be. That's appropriate.\" Good jealousy: kin'at soferim Related to this is קנאת סופרים תרבה חכמה , the jealousy of scholars increases wisdom. The Rabbi clarified the halacha of jealousy. According to all opinions, there is no Torah prohibition on feeling jealous. What the Torah forbids is acting on it: seeing what someone else has, and scheming and planning to dispossess him and take it yourself. Feeling jealous \"is not necessarily a great character trait to have, but it's not forbidden.\" One of his teachers always said that one should be jealous of others in spiritual matters, \"in a good way, not in a way where it defeats a person.\" You see what someone else has achieved and think, \"I can do the same. What's stopping me?\" That spurs you on. \"How did he or she get that car? How did she get that house?... That's bad jealousy. That's mundane jealousy.\" A better word than jealousy, he suggested, is something that spurs you on. Chapter 13: Revenge ( Nekamah ) The tenth group: \"There are some people who insist on always being right. Life is about being right... If somebody wrongs me, oy va'avoy , I'm going to make sure they recognize their wrong. I'm going to teach him or her a lesson.\" Their argument וראו אחרים כי הטוב במה שיכוון האדם בעולם הזה שינקום מאויביו Revenge removes grief from the soul. When you are wronged, sorrow settles in your heart. When you see your enemy fall, you feel happy, your anger subsides and the bad thoughts go: \"Why did she do this to me? Why did he do that?\" People learn not to mess with you. Hashem promises it to the righteous: הן יבשו ויכלמו כל הנחרים בך... תבקשם ולא תמצאם אנשי מצתך . The Rabbi connected this to the previous chapter. \"You feel lower than them.\" By taking revenge I bring them down, \"so now we're equal again. You're not higher than me... That's why revenge is so sweet.\" Saadia: it is all vanity והתבוננתי כל מה שזכרו והנה הם הבלים (The Rabbi said he did not understand the next two lines of the Hebrew, and moved on.) A black sea. When the mind is set on the enemy, it falls into a black sea, which the Rabbi read as depression. Every day a new scheme: חשבו רעות בלב . \"You're full of toxicity. That's the word we would use nowadays.\" No mercy. His heart fills until he cannot spare anyone or accept any plea: נפש רשע אותה רע, לא יחן בעיניו רעהו . He spends everything on it, like the Medes אשר כסף לא יחשבו וזהב לא יחפצו בו . The Rabbi: \"Look at the countries like Iran, where internally they've got terrible economic issues, and yet they're still willing to finance terror.\" Any logical person would say, invest in your own economy. \"No, because revenge is more important... It distorts.\" Others pay the price. To kill his enemy he would kill a thousand friends, or himself: תמות נפשי עם פלשתים , said Shimshon. Innocent bystanders do not matter to him. He forgets Hashem: ועדת עריצים בקשו נפשי ולא שמוך לנגדם . He often fails, like the king of Moav against the king of Edom, ולא יכלו . It rebounds on him: כורה שחת בה יפל . Even if he succeeds, he has sinned, and no one can save him from it except the person he harmed: אדם עשק בדם נפש עד בור ינוס אל יתמכו בו . It is a false hope. For those who believe in fate, how can one stand against fate? (Saadia disproved fatalism in an earlier essay.) And for us, אנשי אמת , who believe everything is in Hashem's hands, it is תוחלת נכזבה , a vain hope, to stand against the Creator of heaven and earth. Isolation. People hate him, rejoice at his downfall, and no one cares for him when he is ill, אין כהה לשברך . The neighbour and the eye The Rabbi told a story he said is written in the sefarim. A man who was always jealous of his neighbour did a mitzvah, and in a dream was told he could have anything he wished, on one condition: whatever he received, his neighbour would receive double. \"You want to be a billionaire, you can be... you'll have two billion, he'll have four billion.\" He thought hard, and finally said: \"Please gouge out one of my eyes.\" Then his neighbour would lose both. \"You could ask for anything, but I prefer to lose myself and to be harmed rather than my neighbor getting more than me.\" Revenge \"can completely destroy a person's rationality.\" When is the desire for revenge right? אבל נטעה בנפש אהבת הנקמה לקחת דין הבורא מן המחזק בעוזו It was planted in us so that we would exact the Creator's justice from those who do wrong by force, so that the world is set right: לבקרים אצמית כל רשעי ארץ להכרית מעיר ה' כל פעלי און . To punish and stop wrongdoers: that is the right use. A question to come back to The Rabbi left the class with one line to think about, \"which is a big debate in the Rishonim actually.\" Saadia calls it a vain hope to stand against the Creator, \"everything's up to Hashem.\" The famous question: can a person harm another when Hashem has not decreed it? \"Hashem has decreed upon you to have a nice day today. And now I have free will to come and ruin your day... Do I have the ability to do that?\" Saadia seems to be saying no. It is tied to that week's parasha and the Or HaChaim on ונראה מה יהיו חלמתיו . He will spend the beginning of the next shiur on it. Summary Building up the world: brings worry, debt, jealousy and taxes. The desire exists so that we have a home and food. Long life: Saadia sees it as more sorrow and more sin; we love life so as to rise to the next world. The Rabbi: the Torah promises long days , not long years. Status and power: society needs authority held by wisdom. The desire exists to drive us towards the greatest portion in the World to Come, like kin'at soferim . Revenge: toxic, ruinous and a vain hope against Hashem's will. Its place is justice against wrongdoers. Coming Up No shiur for the next two weeks. The class resumes in January. Next term: first, the question of whether one person can harm another without a divine decree; then the last three extremes, wisdom alone, worship alone and rest alone, followed by Saadia's chapter on balance in life and music. \"This will take us two shiurim maximum.\" Then a new sefer. Tomorrow (Tuesday): the Rambam's Principles of Faith continue with the third principle, ein lo guf . Thursday night's shiur as usual.","Wf7ZJsccmfI":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 10 (How a Person Should Conduct Himself in This World), Chapters 7 to 9: passion, amassing money, and children Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 1 December 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The First Three Extremes Saadia is going through thirteen areas of life that people take to an extreme. Last week covered three: Abstinence. The hermits said this world is full of suffering. Saadia does not entirely disagree, but there is no reason to abstain from what Hakadosh Baruch Hu has permitted, since we have to look after our health. Perishut is for when something forbidden is in front of us. The class also surveyed other Rishonim, including the view that the Torah does not command abstinence at all, and the Nazir. Eating and drinking. All true that our strength comes from food and that good food and wine are pleasant, but the glutton forgets the side effects. Eat what you need to live. The Rambam: eat until you are almost full, never fully satiated, and most illnesses come from overeating. Intimacy. Some take it to an extreme and forget its harms. Its main purpose, says Saadia, is to continue the human race. \"I'm going to go quite fast through all of this... There isn't much to discuss on this. It's quite straightforward.\" Chapter 7: Passion ( Cheshek ) Why discuss it at all? השער הזה אם הוא גנאי לזכור, איננו יותר מגונה מדעת הכופרים This subject is not a pleasant one to mention, Saadia admits. But throughout the book he has discussed the views of heretics, זכרנום להשיב עליהם ולהסיר ספק מן הלב , in order to refute them and remove doubt from the heart. He will treat this view the same way. Their view: passion is natural, written in the stars, and from God יש אנשים שרואים שהחשק טוב מכל מה שיתנהג בו האדם Some people hold that following one's passion is the best thing a person can do. \"Passion\" here means intense longing, mainly for a person. Their claims: It refines. It purifies the spirit and the temperament until the soul becomes clear. It is \"a very subtle thing.\" It is nature. It is drawn down into the heart; \"I can't control it.\" It follows stages: הבטה , a gaze; תוחלת , a longing; קביעות , a fixation. Then other factors join in and draw a person after it. It is in the stars. When two people's constellations are aligned, in partial or full aspect, a single star governs both and creates love between them. The Rabbi noted that the class has seen before that the Rambam did not believe in astrology at all, while Saadia does believe in it to a degree. It is from Hashem. He created souls as whole entities, divided each into two, and placed each half in a different person. When one half finds the other, it is drawn to it. \"This is a concept we find in the Zohar.\" It is a duty. Some even said Hashem tests people with this passion so that they learn to love and serve Him with the same intensity. Saadia's refutation הראיתי זה בכל מה שזכרו פתאים אין עמדה There is nothing to it. He takes their claims one by one. 1. Hashem does not test with what He forbade. It cannot be that Hashem creates an irresistible attraction to something He has prohibited. ואלוה לא ישים תפלה , and כי לא אל חפץ רשע אתה . \"Hashem's not going to put someone in a situation which they can't control.\" 2. The split soul contradicts Saadia's view of the soul. In the sixth essay, Saadia taught that the soul is created together with the body at a person's formation. This was a minority view, but he held it firmly. There is no pre-existing soul to split into two halves. The Rabbi noted that this \"goes against the Zohar.\" Rabbis often speak at weddings of the idea that a couple's souls are two halves reuniting; that is a kabbalistic concept. Some base it on the Gemara in Yevamot that a man without a wife is incomplete, \"and that is the Gemara, we accept that, of course.\" It means the two complement each other. But a soul literally divided before birth is something Saadia cannot accept. 3. Astrology would make love symmetrical. If the stars caused it, then whenever Reuven loved Shimon, Shimon would love Reuven equally. ואין אנחנו מוצאים דבר כזה . \"Sometimes A has a passion for B, B doesn't have a passion for A.\" 4. The gaze is exactly what the Torah warns about. They say it starts with a look and grows into longing. That is precisely why the Creator told us to avoid it: תנה בני לבך לי ועיניך דרכי תצרנה , and ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם ואחרי עיניכם . What unfulfilled passion does to a person Leaving philosophy aside, it is also harmful. Longing that cannot be fulfilled cripples a person's faculties. The Rabbi: \"There's even an English concept for this: lovesick.\" It comes from Shir HaShirim, כי חולת אהבה אני . Saadia lists the effects: the body wastes away, a person wanders the streets sick, with wailing, sighing, grief, a pounding heart, fainting and sleeplessness, כי קרבו כתנור לבם בארבם . It affects the brain and weakens thought and memory. Sometimes he collapses suddenly at the sight of the beloved, lies in shock, and might even be thought dead. כי רבים חללים הפילה ועצומים כל הרוגיה . The Gemara in Sanhedrin The Rabbi brought a case to show that the idea of being lovesick to the point of illness existed in the time of Chazal. The Gemara discusses a man who became ill with longing for a particular woman, and the doctors said he would die unless he had her. The three cardinal sins, idolatry, murder and forbidden relations, are yehareg ve'al ya'avor , and the Chachamim said, ימות , let him die. What if she merely stood before him? Even that, said the Gemara, is forbidden: \"let him die.\" Bound to one thing ואיך יהיה האדם אסיר הוא ודעתו עד שלא ידע לעצמו אלוה ולא ענין ולא עולם הזה ולא עולם הבא בזולתו How can a person make himself a prisoner, mind and all, until he knows no God, no other concern, neither this world nor the next, except the object of his passion? His devotion goes only to it. He watches doorways and lingers on every path, שאי עיניך על שפיים , goes out late at night and rises early, hides from people lest he be caught and die of shame, ועין נואף שמרה נשף לאמר לא תשורני עין . Sometimes it even leads to bloodshed: כי נאפו ודם בידיהן . Even when he gets it תשוב מתחרטת מתועבת מואסת למי שהיתה אוהבת If he ever attains what he longed for, his soul turns to regret and revulsion towards what it loved, more than it ever loved it. וישנאה אמנון שנאה גדולה מאד : Amnon's terrible act with Tamar, and his hatred for her afterwards. \"This is an important principle just generally: all the things which we pursue, once you reach them, you actually become sick of it.\" Why? \"You could say it's from guilt, but I think there's a deeper idea, that you feel you've been tricked.\" You believed this would solve everything and bring you happiness. You experience it, \"and then, okay, fine, life moves on now.\" That dissonance makes a person angry, upset and guilty, and he ends up hating the thing. He then realises כי מכר נפשו ותורתו וכל חכמתו ושכלו , he sold his soul, his Torah and his wisdom for something he can never take back, עד יפלח חץ כבדו : once the arrow has left, it cannot return. When is passion good? ואין הענין הזה טוב כי אם באשתו של אדם שיאהבה Passion is good only between husband and wife, for the building of the world: אילת אהבים ויעלת חן, דדיה ירוך בכל עת, באהבתה תשגה תמיד . He should direct it towards his wife with understanding, as befits the pleasure of their companionship, and restrain it with strength from anything beyond that. The empty hand The Rabbi tied this to the parasha of that week, Vayishlach, with a story he attributed, he thought, to Rav Nachman of Breslov. A man came into a market town and shouted, \"In my hand is the secret of long life,\" or of riches. A few people ran to ask him, and he started running away. Others saw people running and joined them, until the whole marketplace emptied and the entire town was chasing him. At last they caught him, pinned him down and forced his hand open. Inside was nothing. \"That is generally the passions and the illusions of life, that we pursue them, we chase them... and then we get it and we finally open our hand and, okay, what was there? Wasn't much.\" Yaakov's night-time adversary, whom some identify as the angel of Esav, is asked his name and answers, למה זה תשאל לשמי , why do you ask my name? One explanation is that the yetzer hara keeps changing its form. Another: \"There is nothing there.\" A name implies a real entity, and this one is pure illusion. The trouble is that we only remember this afterwards. That is why the Gemara in Kiddushin says that if the yetzer hara meets you, משכהו לבית המדרש , pull it into the Beit Midrash. The Rabbi understands this as: use your intellect. Think it through rationally. \"How many times people get caught doing things and they realize it wasn't worth it. Their entire life ruined for a short pleasure.\" Chapter 8: Amassing Money Their argument ראו אנשים כי הטוב שיתעסק בו האדם בעולם הזה הטורח בקבוץ הממון Some say the best thing a person can do is work to gather wealth: Food, drink and marriage, the needs of the body, can only be had with money. All buying and selling, and even marriage, runs through it. \"When you got married in those days... without a price, you can't get married.\" Kings only reign through it, and armies are gathered and cities conquered for it. People dig for hidden treasure and go to the homes of the wealthy to court them only because of it. Generosity, honour and charity are only possible through it: רבים יחלו פני נדיב . The Torah's blessing in Ki Tavo, ומשלת בגוים רבים , means ruling through wealth, as Mishlei says, עשיר ברשים ימשול . \"Something which we often hear, right?\" Saadia: what is good about money He starts with what he can agree with: שהטוב ממנו הוא מה שיבוא לאדם בלא טורח ועמל Money that comes without excessive toil and labour is good. \"A person should be trying to make money,\" but not at the cost of great strain. One who chases it with anxiety, sleepless nights and endless daytime toil cannot rest. מתוקה שנת העובד אם מעט ואם הרבה יאכל, והשבע לעשיר איננו מניח לו לישון . The Rabbi: \"I know people who are extremely wealthy who can't sleep at night.\" Every couple of hours they wake to check the markets, with alarm clocks set through the night. \"That is not what Hakadosh Baruch Hu expects a person to have, to ruin their schedule, night, sleep, to be able to make some more money.\" The dangers of making it the goal It takes over your life, like the other desires, and becomes like a fire that consumes everything. שאול ואבדון לא תשבענה ועיני האדם לא תשבענה . Fights and hatred, as wolves and lions fight over prey: אריה טרף בדי גרותיו ומחנק ללבאתיו . The poor are forgotten. He takes no notice of widows and orphans, because one who loves silver is never sated with it. והנה דמעת העשוקים ואין להם מנחם . Loss of integrity. \"This is more subtle.\" Not open fighting, but losing your conscience: taking money, losing honour, betraying trust, stealing, working on the festivals and swearing falsely. אבדה האמונה ונכרתה מפיהם . Forgetting Hashem. Even if he gets the money, and the Rabbi has \"never heard of someone tell me that I've made enough money now,\" he trusts in it and forgets God. וכסף וזהב ירבה לך... ורם לבבך ושכחת את ה' אלקיך , from Parashat Ekev. The Rabbi noted the Gemara's view that in some ways a wealthy person faces a greater test than a poor one: someone who has known poverty knows he must turn to Hashem constantly, while one with money \"doesn't need to ask Hashem too much.\" Danger to life. Wealth can get a person killed, by robbers and bandits, or handed over to the authorities. \"We know stories of people who were kidnapped, people who were killed for their money.\" עשר שמור לבעליו לרעתו . Sin carried to the grave, if it was made wrongly, and no blessing for the children: נחלה מבהלת בראשונה ואחריתה לא תברך . The cycle of generations The Rabbi described \"the cycle of life.\" Poverty drives one generation to work very hard and make a lot of money. The next still has the father's values. Then it goes down, and within a couple of generations the money is lost and it starts all over again. \"Keeping money for the next generation doesn't work.\" Someone once told him he needed to make enough to buy houses for all his grandchildren. \"I said, why do you need to buy houses for your grandchildren?... Why can't your grandchildren buy their own?\" Giving it all to them takes away their ability to work hard as you did. Why Hashem gave us love of money אבל הושם האדם לאהוב הממון לשמור מה שחננו הבורא ולא יאבדנו We were given a love of possessions so that we look after what the Creator has graciously given us and do not waste it. ברכת ה' היא תעשיר ולא יוסיף עצב עמה . \"It's an interesting idea, this.\" Without it, we would have things and not take care of them. This too fits the week's parasha. ויותר יעקב לבדו : Yaakov's family crossed the river and he was left alone. Chazal, quoted by Rashi, say he had gone back for some פכים קטנים , small jars. Crossing a river at night was no simple feat, but he did it, because this is what Hakadosh Baruch Hu had blessed him with, \"I need to look after it, I mustn't waste it.\" Chapter 9: Children \"Some of these things are old fashioned, but some of them really quite speak to us in this generation. And I think the next one also speaks to us.\" Their argument ראו אנשים שישתדלו בבקשת הבנים Some people say the sole focus of life should be having more and more children: They bring joy to the soul and delight to the eye. Without them the world would not continue. They are a person's support in old age: the more children, the more people to look after you. Good children remember you kindly after your death. They are the natural objects of compassion and mercy. The great prophets all asked for them: Avraham, הן לי לא נתת זרע (Lech Lecha); Yitzchak, ויעתר יצחק לה' לנכח אשתו כי עקרה היא (a couple of weeks ago); Rachel, הבה לי בנים ואם אין מתה אנכי (the previous Shabbat's parasha). A father who teaches his child Torah and mitzvot earns reward: אב לבנים יודיע אל אמתך . Saadia: right in principle, wrong to make it everything וראיתי נכון בבנים שיחננם הבורא לעבודתו כרצונו It is right to have children whom the Creator grants, to bring them up to serve Him. Their mistake is focusing on this to the exclusion of everything else. ומה תועלת יש בהם אם לא יהיה להם מזון וכסות ומחסה, ומה טוב יש בגידולם אם לא יהיה להם חכמה ומדע What benefit are children with no food, clothing or shelter? What good is raising them without wisdom and knowledge? What is compassion worth if you cannot provide the most basic needs? The Rabbi on halacha and balance \"I think this is an important point.\" In some circles people say that birth control is forbidden in every case and a person should never use these things. \"There's no source for that.\" He set out the halacha: Mitzvah Source What it requires פרו ורבו Torah A boy and a girl, following Beit Hillel. The obligation is on the man (some say the woman too, but most likely only the man) שבת Chachamim Continuing to have children, from לא תהו בראה לשבת יצרה and בבקר זרע את זרעך ולערב אל תנח ידך . Only on the man So of course Saadia, and everyone, agrees that the Torah and Chazal want a person to have as many children as possible. But it has to be balanced against other things: can you provide for them physically, mentally and educationally? \"It's not quantity over quality. This is the key point Saadia is making, and that's the point we forget.\" It is not only about having fifteen children; it is also about each one being properly looked after. Otherwise it only brings pain to the parents, and how can parents expect anything from children to whom they could not give the basics? The burdens of children Saadia lists them bluntly: The pain of pregnancy and birth, בעצב תלדי בנים , and sometimes the mother dies in childbirth, ויהי בצאת נפשה כי מתה , Rachel at Binyamin's birth, in that week's parasha. The father's labour and danger to feed them, ואפרחיו יעלעו דם . \"Sometimes a person has too many kids, they suck the blood out of a parent.\" The trouble of raising them, nursing their illnesses and preparing their medicines. \"How many times do we wake up at night from the child crying.\" If a child dies, the grief is terrible. If they live, the fear for sons, that they will grow up properly, keeps a parent awake: משדד אב יבריח אם, בן מביש ומחפיר . And the fear for daughters, as Chazal say, keeps a father up at night. If they turn out well, there is hope; if they rebel, דור עקש ופתלתל . Why Hashem gave us love for children אבל שם האב לאהוב את הבנים... שלא יקוץ בהם Saadia's conclusion: the Creator placed love for children in parents so that they would not grow weary and disgusted with them. הנה נחלת ה' בנים שכר פרי הבטן . The Rabbi was candid: \"This I can't agree, I'm not sure. Okay, this is what he says.\" Then he developed it. Think about how much we give our children and what we get back, \"I'm not talking about the nachat and the pride, that's a different thing.\" In any other relationship, giving so much and receiving so little would be called toxic. \"You would get divorced, or... you would leave the friendship.\" So Hashem gave us love for our children to cover over the fact that they take from us. He added that there is also a biological aspect: this is our seed and our continuation. Summary Passion: claims that it is natural, astrological or a divine split of souls fail. Hashem does not test with the forbidden; the soul is created with the body; love is not always mutual; and the Torah warns about the very gaze that starts it. Unfulfilled, it makes a person sick; fulfilled, it often turns to revulsion, as with Amnon. Its place is in marriage. The empty hand: the things we chase are often illusions. Pull the yetzer hara into the Beit Midrash: think it through with your intellect. Money: good when it comes without consuming your life. Made into the goal, it brings sleeplessness, fights, neglect of the poor, loss of integrity, forgetting Hashem, danger and unblessed inheritance. The love of money exists so that we look after what Hashem gave us, like Yaakov's small jars. Children: a great good and a mitzvah, but quality and not only quantity. A parent must be able to feed, house and educate them. Hashem gave us love for them so that we keep giving. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 10 and onwards: those who live only to build up the world, those who live only for long life, and those who pursue status. The Rabbi hopes to finish the book around the end of the year. Tomorrow night (Tuesday): the Rambam's Principles of Faith continue, with the second and third principles, the unity of Hashem and His incorporeality.","H21dgn-dQnE":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 10 (How a Person Should Conduct Himself in This World), Introduction and Chapters 1 to 3, with the opening of Chapter 4 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 17 November 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Saadia's Olam HaBa in One Picture Last week the class finished the ninth essay. Before opening the tenth, the Rabbi drew together everything Saadia said about the World to Come: A physical place. Olam HaBa is a new world, inhabited by human beings made of body and soul together . Light and fire. The righteous enjoy the bliss of a light, which is what we call Olam HaBa or Gan Eden. The wicked suffer in its burning heat, which is Gehinom. No time, except when we need it to serve Hashem, and it is eternal , both the reward and the punishment. Levels. There are seven levels. Those with more and greater merits receive a more intense light; those with more and greater sins, a more intense fire. Who sees whom. Tzaddikim may see each other, but only those close to one another in level. Resha'im cannot see each other. The tzaddikim see the resha'im and think how fortunate they are; the resha'im see the tzaddikim and think how unfortunate they are. Continued service of Hashem, through prayer, and continued free choice, although Olam HaBa will only contain those whom Hashem knows will only choose correctly. \"Various of these points are very debatable. Some of them are quite difficult to accept and to understand, but we're learning Saadia Gaon.\" The Rambam, the Rabbi said, would argue with almost all of it apart from eternity: for him it is not body and soul, not light and heat, not a place at all, since nothing there is physical, and there is certainly no service of Hashem. The idea of continued service in Olam HaBa \"is certainly very peculiar to Saadia, and no one else really says that.\" Mark's question: the 51 per cent person Mark asked about someone who is 51 per cent good and 49 per cent bad. According to Saadia he is a tzaddik. He suffers for his sins in this world, receives eternal good, and sits on a very low level, but he has no Gehinom at all. \"There's only two categories\": tzaddik, from 50 upwards, and rasha, below 50. The case of exactly 50-50 was dealt with about a year ago, in the earlier essay on merits, where Hashem tips the scale towards kindness. \"But again, how many people are we going to have who are exactly 50?\" Essay 10: The Appendix to the Sefer The class began Emunot VeDeot in January 2024, and with Hashem's help will finish a little into January, two years exactly from the start. The first nine essays fall into three sections, all of them philosophical: Essays Theme The opening essays Hashem The middle essays Revelation , mitzvot and aveirot The final essays Reward and punishment and eschatology: Mashiach, Techiyat HaMeitim and Olam HaBa Saadia closes the sefer with a tenth essay, which the Rabbi called \"really an appendix\": במה הטוב לאדם לעשותו בעולמו הזה , what is the best way for a person to live his life in this world. Saadia the man \"You get to see Saadia a bit more personally here, because he talks here about life.\" The essay has nineteen short chapters, and the Rabbi planned to move through them quickly. Its theme is balance , and it shows how widely Saadia ranged. The penultimate chapter is entirely about music, and how music too must be balanced. The class has met this theme before: in Chovot HaLevavot some years ago, and last year in the Rambam's Shemonah Perakim. The Rambam is famous for the golden mean, which comes from Aristotle, \"but Saadia echoes this completely.\" Saadia was himself a balanced person: the first to write a structured philosophical book on Judaism, \"a huge groundbreaking work,\" which is why the Rabbi chose it: it lays the foundations for so much that the Rambam writes later; an expert in astronomy , as the class has seen; an expert in mathematics (recall the chapter on numbers); a grammarian, who wrote a book on dikduk and a lexicon. \"He's well-rounded, and that's the point he's trying to make in this essay.\" The plan of the essay Chapters 1 to 3: an introduction to the concept of balance. Chapters 4 to about 17: thirteen areas of life that people take to an extreme, the dangers of each extreme, and the correct path. The end: balance in life in general, including music. The Introduction: The Creator Is One, His Works Are Many The Hebrew translation is by Yehudah ibn Tibbon , who opens, Amar Yehudah ben Shaul , before Saadia's own words. הקדמת המאמר הזה שדיברו בו אנשים רבים ומעט מי שהגיע בו אל העצה המשובחת This is a subject on which many people have spoken, but few have reached the praiseworthy conclusion. Step 1: Hashem's unity requires multiplicity in creation כי בורא הכל יתברך מפני שהיה אחד בעצמו התחייב שיהיו נבראים רבים Because the Creator is One in Himself, it follows that His creations are many. The class saw this at the very start of the sefer. The Rabbi added a midrash to the same effect: Hashem created Chava as well as Adam because you cannot have one God and one human. Step 2: Nothing we call \"one\" is really one Anything we see as one is only one בדרך המספר , numerically. Examine it carefully and you find it is many. The elements. Every body contains the four qualities of medieval science, heat, cold, moisture and dryness. \"Nowadays we would substitute that for protons, neutrons, electrons.\" A tree has branches, leaves and fruit. A human has flesh, bones, sinews, veins and tendons. A chair (the Rabbi's example) is one chair only \"to the exclusion of two.\" In itself it is wood, plastic, atoms, protons and neutrons. \"This is before he knows about atoms, protons, neutrons and electrons, right? But this really is true today.\" Step 3: This is a law of creation כי הבורא אחד ומעשיו רבים The Creator is One, and His works are many. As Tehillim says, מה רבו מעשיך ה' כולם בחכמה עשית . Even the heavens are not one: they have different parts and measures, shapes, sights and signs beyond counting, as Iyov says, עושה עש כסיל וכימה וחדרי תימן . \"Okay, so what? Why is this relevant to the conduct of a human being?\" Because, as Chapter 1 will say, the same applies to a person's traits. \"Only God is one.\" It is never good to take one trait and hone in on it. Chapter 1: A Person Should Not Live by One Trait What are ahavot ? Saadia says the same is true of אהבות , literally \"loves\": אהבה לדברים ושנאה לדברים , love for things and hatred for things. The Rabbi admitted he struggled to translate it. Some render it as hanhagot , modes of conduct. It means the pull towards doing something or away from it. As Mishlei says, רבות מחשבות בלב איש ועצת ה' היא תקום : there are many things in a person's heart. Just as bodies are not made of one element, a tree is not one part and a man is not only flesh, and the heavens are not in one place: כן האדם לא יתנהג כל ימיו במידה אחת So too a person should not conduct himself all his days by a single trait. \"This is his key point.\" Instead a person should gather them together, רוב מדבר אחד ומעט מאחר , more of one and a little of another. When he balances all the aspects of his identity correctly, ישלם לו תיקון ענייניו , his affairs are put right. It is as if he had brought them to judgment, יכלכל דבריו במשפט , or weighed them on a scale and divided them into portions, פלס מעגל רגלך . Even what we think of as bad traits should not be negated completely. \"You have to utilize everything which God has given us as part of our identity.\" The Rabbi noted that this is the idea the Rambam lays out in Hilchot Deot two hundred years or more later, but Saadia is one of the first of the Chachamim to state it explicitly and elaborate on it: \"there is nothing bad and there's nothing only good.\" Why Saadia needs to say this הוא שראיתי אנשים חושבים... כי חובה שיתנהג האדם במידה אחת כל ימיו Saadia saw people who believed one should live one's whole life by a single trait: raising one love above all others, or one hatred above all others. The Rabbi's illustration: \"you get some people who are always talking about one specific kind of sin. That's not good.\" Saadia's verdict: והנה היא מתכלית הרעות כולם , this is the worst of all approaches. His arguments: Hashem gave us many feelings. If Hashem wanted us to work on only one thing, He would not have given us all these different emotions and traits. He would have made us from one element. \"Why do we have the bones, and the sinews, and the muscles?\" Hashem made us with multiplicity to show us that life is nuanced. Nothing built from one material works. A house built only from stones, or only from wood, or only from nails, is not as sound as one built from all of them together. The same is true of food, drink, clothing and everything else. Anything in excess is harmful. \"Even too much water can kill you.\" If everything around us is made of many parts, כל שכן ענייני נפשו ומידותיה , how much more so a person's own soul and character. Mark's question: generalist or specialist? Mark asked whether Saadia means only character traits, or whether he is promoting being a generalist over a specialist. The Rabbi answered by previewing the thirteen areas (listed below in Chapter 4). They are not really midot : \"Eating and drinking is not a tendency.\" They are \"thirteen different important fundamental aspects of life\" that people take to an extreme. So the essay is broader than character: it is also about balance in our ambitions, our goals and our enjoyment of this world. Another participant asked whether anyone actually desires abstinence. \"They don't desire it, they do it.\" It is an approach some people have thought is the best way to live. \"Nobody desires to live a life of abstinence.\" Chapter 2: The Damage of Extremes, and the Rule of the Intellect Extremism is not a small matter וצריך שאבאר כי חדושי הבחירה הזאת אין דעתם מעוטה, אך היא גדולה Saadia gives examples of what a single-minded path does to people: Extreme Result Those who chose to wander in the mountains (living as hermits) It drove them to madness Those who chose much eating It brought them to haemorrhoids Those who chose revenge They took revenge on themselves On the hermits: \"This was quite a common thing, because all the Rishonim talked about it.\" Saadia, the Rambam, Rabbeinu Bachya and the Kuzari all discuss Jews who went to live alone and cut themselves off from the world, a tendency the Rabbi thought originated with the Christians. They took perishut , which is an important part of life, to an extreme. On food: the person whose life is about the best pleasures, a new restaurant every week, \"this kind of meat, eaten this way.\" On revenge: \"these are normally toxic people,\" who spend their whole life trying to right every wrong done to them. \"You end up wronging yourself, you end up living the worst life.\" That is why, Saadia says, we always need wisdom in how we conduct ourselves, בהתהלכך תנחה אותך . The root principle: a person rules his traits שהאדם מושל במידותיו ושולט במה שיאהב וישנא A person is master of his traits and in control of what he loves and hates. כי לכל אחד ממה שהוא אוהב ושונא מקום ורשות : every trait, including the ones we see as bad or impure, has its place. \"Even revenge has a place sometimes.\" The Gemara says a talmid chacham who does not take revenge like a snake is not a talmid chacham. When you see the right place for a trait, ישלחנה בשיעור שישלים מעשה ההיא : use it in the amount the situation needs. When you see a place where it does not belong, ויעצרנה : hold it back. This is only possible for someone who lives with יישוב , with self-possession, \"not with impulses.\" As Mishlei says, טוב ארך אפים מגיבור ומושל ברוחו מלוכד עיר : better the slow to anger than the mighty, and one who rules his spirit than one who captures a city. \"You need to have strong self-discipline and strong self-restraint to be able to live the best life.\" The three faculties of the soul Saadia reminds us of what he taught in the essay on the soul. The soul has three faculties: Faculty What it drives כח התאוה (desire) Food, drink and intimacy; looking at beautiful sights, smelling pleasant scents, touching soft things כח הכעס (emotion, passion) Seeking dominion and status, relying on others, revenge, arrogance and brazenness כח ההכרה (cognition) ידין על כוחות האחרים : it judges the other two Whenever a person is faced with a choice, he should begin with cognition and examine it. If he sees that its end is free of harm, and all the more so if its end is praiseworthy, let him do it. If he sees significant harm on one side, let him leave it, \"because my cognition is in control of my desire.\" והמשיל הכרתו על תאוותו וכעסו, הרי הוא במוסר החכמים One whose cognition rules his desire and his anger lives by the discipline of the wise, as it says, יראת ה' מוסר חכמה . One whose desire and anger rule his cognition is not a man of discipline at all. And if he calls that his ethic, \"follow the dreams of your heart, people say,\" Saadia calls it מוסר אוילים , \"the musar of idiots.\" The Rabbi: \"Deep down we know this. We only make mistakes, we only do wrong things because we don't think this through.\" A person who loses his temper says, I'm sorry, I lost control. \"You let your ka'as ... reign over your cognition.\" Cognition knows \"you never get anything out of anger. Anger never works.\" The same with someone who eats something forbidden: \"they know it's wrong, but the desire has trumped the cognition.\" Melech and Kalem The Rabbi brought a teaching he heard from Rav Moshe Shapiro zt\"l. Why is a king, at least a king of old, looked up to? Because he is in control. מלך is an acronym for מוח, לב, כבד : brain, heart, liver. Moach: the brain decides this is right. Lev: the heart then builds up a passion and desire for it, because the brain said so. Kaved: the liver, the blood, the person himself carries it out in action. The Gemara in Berachot says that Bilam tried to curse Bnei Yisrael at the one moment when Hashem's anger is present. How long is that moment? אחד מחמשת רבוא ושמונת אלפים ושמונה מאות ושמונים ושמונה חלקים בשעה , one part in 58,888 of an hour. Tosafot asks what anyone could possibly say in so short a time, and gives two answers: Once you begin at the right time, you may continue. The Rabbi noted that some poskim learn from this that if you begin the Amidah before sof zman tefillah , it counts even if you finish after. It is disputed, and it is better to finish in time. You can say one word: כלם , \"annihilate them.\" Now look at the letters of כלם : they are מלך reversed. \"So that's the biggest curse,\" that a person lives kaved, lev, moach : he acts, then desires, then rationalises it in his brain afterwards. Most of us, when we do wrong, know we are doing wrong. \"It's not fine... we've lapsed.\" But the worst is to use our actions and desires to persuade the mind \"that this is the best way to live,\" which means living the opposite of the correct life. Chapter 3: Shlomo HaMelech's \"Hevel\" Saadia now shows that Shlomo HaMelech taught the same thing in Kohelet. ראיתי את כל המעשים שנעשו תחת השמש והנה הכל הבל ורעות רוח It cannot be that Shlomo meant the world itself is vain, because Hashem created it. \"How on earth could King Solomon say about God that He's created something which is vain?\" Rather: כי כל מעשה שיקחו בני אדם לבדו... יהיה הבל ורעות רוח Any single activity a person takes up alone , to the exclusion of all else, is hevel . This is also the meaning of מעוות לא יוכל לתקון : each thing on its own is crooked and lacking, but בהתחברותם , joined together, they are complete. The Rabbi called this \"a chiddush, by the way.\" The normal understanding is that everything is vain. Saadia says no: things taken alone are vain; in combination they are good. Three examples from Kohelet Saadia explains hevel as something worthless or misleading, citing מהבלים המה אתכם and ובגזל אל תהבלו . Then he shows that Shlomo tried three things to their limit and declared each one hevel : What Shlomo pursued alone The verse Why it fails Wisdom ונתתי את לבי לדעת חכמה יוסיף דעת יוסיף מכאוב : the more you know, the more pain you see Joy and laughter לכה נא אנסכה בשמחה וראה בטוב In the end a person feels lowered, as if he has entered the traits of animals: לשחוק אמרתי מהולל Building up the world הגדלתי מעשי, בניתי לי בתים, נטעתי לי כרמים שאניחנו לאדם שיהיה אחרי : you will die and leave it all to someone else On wisdom the Rabbi said, \"This is a point which I think some of us can identify with. Sometimes the more simple a person is, the more naive a person is, the less problems they see with the world.\" The more you study and understand, the more of the world's problems you see. It is not good to study and study \"and not be part of the world.\" On laughter he noted the word is meholal , from hollelut : \"It's crazy, people who are always looking for leisure.\" Having shown these three, Shlomo did not need to go through every other area. He was hinting that one should combine them, ושיתעסק במעט מן החכמה , a little wisdom, a little pleasure and a little building of the world, as he said, תרתי בלבי למשוך ביין את בשרי : sometimes one should enjoy wine as well. \"You need a combination of work, of leisure, and of study, all three together. And then it's not hevel .\" Opening Chapter 4: The Thirteen Paths Saadia announces his plan: to gather thirteen roots, which Rav Kafih translates as thirteen derachim , paths. For each he will explain what led people to choose it to the exclusion of all else, what they left out, and the correct place and measure for it. הפרישות : abstinence, asceticism האכילה : eating and drinking המשגל : intimacy החשק : passionate love הבנים : children (\"having a lot of DNA after you\") הישוב : building and cultivating the world החיים : long life. \"Some people, they always tell you about how healthy they are... but what are you doing with your life?\" הממון : accumulating money השררה : dominion, status and authority הנקמה : revenge, always righting the wrongs done to you העבודה : work, more and more of it החכמה : wisdom, pursuing more and more of it המנוחה : rest and tranquility Saadia then begins: V'omer harishon , the first is abstinence. That is where the class will pick up, together with other Rishonim, such as Rabbeinu Bachya's chapter on perishut . Summary Saadia's Olam HaBa: a new physical world of body and soul, light for the righteous and fire for the wicked, eternal, in seven levels, with continued service of Hashem. The Rambam disagrees with almost all of it. Essay 10 is the appendix to the sefer, on how to live in this world, and it shows Saadia the man: theologian, astronomer, mathematician and grammarian. Only God is one. Everything else, even what we call one, is many. So a person should not live by a single trait. Building a life is like building a house: stones alone or wood alone will not hold. Every trait has its place and its measure, even revenge. Cognition must rule desire and emotion. That is the discipline of the wise; the reverse is \"the musar of idiots.\" Melech, moach lev kaved , is the right order; kalem is its reverse. Kohelet's \"hevel\" refers to any single pursuit taken alone: wisdom, pleasure or building. Combined, they are good. Thirteen paths to be examined, starting next week with abstinence. Coming Up Next week, same time (6:00 to 7:00): Chapter 4 onwards, starting with perishut , with the views of other Rishonim. Tomorrow (Tuesday): the Rambam shiur continues, beginning the Thirteen Principles of Faith with Principle 1.","3TNfE69vu3A":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 9 (Olam HaBa, The World to Come), Chapters 9, 10 and 11 (the end of the essay) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 10 November 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Saadia's Picture So Far \"We are close to finishing. We may well finish tonight essay 9.\" Three chapters remained, and the Rabbi got through all three. The first part of the essay brought sources for Olam HaBa from logic, Torah and tradition. Then Saadia began his questions on its nature: Questions Saadia's answer 1 and 2: reward and punishment Intense light for the righteous, fire for the wicked 3: place A new world , newly created 4: time No time , apart from one point to come tonight, since time is a construct of this world, for relationships and rental contracts 5 and 6: eternity Reward and punishment are both perpetual 7 and 8: levels Not all equal: the intensity of the light matches one's mitzvot, and the intensity of the punishment one's wickedness, as some are merely imprisoned and some are imprisoned and tortured Tonight: Who deserves Gehinnom? Do people in Olam HaBa see each other? Are there mitzvot in Olam HaBa, and if so, what is their reward? Chapter 9, Question 9: Who Deserves Gehinnom? For Saadia, punishment in Olam HaBa is the same as Gehinnom: the intense fire, which he seems to understand very physically. Who receives it perpetually? הכופרים והמשתפים ובעלי עבירות החמורות אשר לא עשו תשובה The deniers, those who associate others with God, and those guilty of the grave sins who did not repent. Deniers and idolaters. Of them the pasuk says: ויצאו וראו בפגרי האנשים הפשעים בי (Yeshayahu 66:24) They will go out and see the corpses of the people who rebelled against Me. \"Rebelled against Me\" means either denying God or worshipping another deity besides Him. Those guilty of grave sins : sins for which the Torah prescribes karet or a death penalty of the beit din , such as murder, adultery or eating on Yom Kippur. Whoever is cut off from this world is also cut off from the righteous in the next, because he did not repent. And if he was not cut off in this world but lived out a long life and still did not repent, his punishment is harsher , because he was given more time and still did not do teshuvah. Anyone else, whose sins are lighter, can be atoned for and will not go to Gehinnom. The clear implication, \"which everyone agrees to,\" is that teshuvah atones for even the gravest sin. Gehinnom is for those who did not do teshuvah. What is karet for Saadia? \"It's the first time he's talked about karet explicitly.\" The Rabbi set out three views: The Rambam: karet has nothing to do with this life. It means having no portion in Olam HaBa : you die and have no existence after death. Tosafot , in one of the first Tosafot in Yevamot: karet is an early death , at fifty or sixty (there are different opinions). A very physical understanding. Saadia: \"a kind of combination.\" Someone who commits a karet sin is likely to die early, but not necessarily. Either way, karet in the next world means intense punishment in Olam HaBa . \"For Saadia, Olam HaBa, Gehinnom, karet, these are all synonymous terms for people who suffer in the world to come.\" What about lesser sins? If I spoke lashon hara or ate non-kosher food, why do I not go to Gehinnom? Saadia answers: these are light, and the fact that a person has only these means he guarded himself from the grave ones . He must have kept the other mitzvot: he did not deny, he believed, he did not worship idols, did not murder, did not commit adultery, did not steal, and acted with justice and righteousness. Sins that are only negative prohibitions without karet or a death penalty can be atoned. But they still need atonement. Saadia, referring back to Essay 5: whoever's deeds are mostly merits, with a few such sins against them, is repaid for those sins in this world and leaves it clean . Two boxes, again \"This is precisely the point I discussed last week. It's very black and white.\" The rasha , with the grave sins (or more sins than mitzvot): perpetual Gehinnom, its intensity depending on how many sins, \"either am I going to be just red hot, or am I going to be singed like the back of a pot.\" His mitzvot are rewarded in this world. The tzaddik , without grave sins, or who did teshuvah for them: Gan Eden, its level depending on his mitzvot, \"some will be like the sun itself, some only rays of the sun.\" His sins are atoned through suffering in this world. The beinoni , exactly fifty-fifty, is pushed over to tzaddik. The Rabbi added that, though Saadia does not mention it, it seems possible in rare cases for someone with no grave sins to have so many minor sins that they outweigh his mitzvot. \"It's very difficult to accept this kind of scheme, but that's the way Saadia believes.\" Richard's question Does teshuvah also work for karet sins? Teshuvah applies to every sin , including karet and the death penalties of the beit din. \"What do we say on Yom Kippur over and over and over again?\" על חטא שחטאנו לפניך בכרת... על חטא שחטאנו לפניך בארבע מיתות בית דין: סקילה, שרפה, הרג וחנק \"Of course you have to go through the principles of teshuvah, like we've learned in the past, and make sure you do it properly. But in principle, there's nothing which cannot be atoned with teshuvah.\" Chapter 9, Question 10: Do People See Each Other? הצדיקים והרשעים רואים קצתם את קצתם The righteous and the wicked see one another. The righteous see the wicked Again from ויצאו וראו בפגרי האנשים הפשעים בי . The Rabbi noted that others read this pasuk as referring to the time of techiyat hametim: the righteous rise and see the dead who do not rise. Saadia reads it as Olam HaBa. When the righteous see the suffering of the wicked, they will say: praised be He who saved us from these sufferings , and they will rejoice in their own lot. \"I find this difficult. It's almost a kind of 'I'm not like you, I'm much higher than you.'\" It recalls being honoured through another's disgrace, מתכבד בקלון חברו . The wicked see the righteous פחדו בציון חטאים אחזה רעדה חנפים (Yeshayahu 33:14) Sinners in Zion are afraid; trembling has seized the hypocrites. They will be astonished that the righteous pass through the burning fire and are not hurt: \"We are burnt by this fire, we are in constant pain. And you, the tzaddikim, seem to be having light from the fire, but you don't get burnt.\" And they will groan at the reward they lost. Saadia's analogy: people invited to a feast, while others are summoned only to watch them eat, and sigh as they watch. הנה עבדי יאכלו ואתם תרעבו (Yeshayahu 65:13) Behold, My servants will eat, and you will be hungry. The source: Vayikra Rabbah Saadia does not cite it, but his wording clearly comes from a Midrash in Vayikra Rabbah on the last pasuk of Tehillim 12, which the Rabbi said we recite on Shemini Atzeret: סביב רשעים יתהלכון (Tehillim 12:9) The wicked walk all around. The first opinion: around the wicked the righteous walk . When the righteous leave Gan Eden and see the wicked judged in Gehinnom, נפשם שמחה עליהם , and they praise HaKadosh Baruch Hu for the sufferings He brought on them in this world. The Rabbi suggested this does not mean they are happy that the wicked suffer, but that they are happy with their own lot: what they suffered in this world was worth it for the next. Rabbi Chanina says the reverse: around the righteous the wicked walk . When the wicked come up from Gehinnom and see the righteous sitting at peace in Gan Eden, their souls are pained. \"Why did I not get this reward?\" So Saadia has a source. There are hints in the Gemara as well: כל אחד ואחד נכוה מחופתו של חבירו , each person in Olam HaBa is \"burnt\" by the canopy of the one greater than him. But for the Rambam , the Rabbi said, such a concept is not possible: there is no physicality, so no seeing each other. The Rambam would say not every Midrash is to be taken literally; some must be interpreted differently, and not every opinion is accepted. The Tuesday shiur the next night, on what the Rabbi called \"one of the most important paragraphs\" in the Rambam on the afterlife, would show why. Do the righteous see each other? Saadia continues: among the righteous, those whose levels are close will meet , and those whose levels are far apart will not. Someone on the top rung will not see someone on the bottom rung of Gan Eden. \"Where did he get this from? I don't know.\" And the wicked? Saadia offers a guess, ועלה בדעתי : even those close in level do not meet , perhaps because they are so occupied with their own pain that they cannot pay attention to anyone else. \"You live in your own suffering.\" Chapter 10: Is There Avodat Hashem in Olam HaBa? \"Now we come to something even more astonishing.\" Ask the average person what Olam HaBa is, and they will say it is where you reap the consequences of your actions. Are there further mitzvot there? \"I imagine 99.9% of people will tell you, of course not,\" because היום לעשותם ומחר לקבל שכרם : today to do them, tomorrow to receive their reward. \"I've never seen it before, and I don't know anyone else who says this.\" Saadia: yes Is there service of Hashem in Olam HaBa? \"I say yes.\" Logic. It is not possible that a rational being would be left without command and service. If service were unnecessary in the next world, Hashem would have made it unnecessary in this one too. Since He made us serve Him here, we must serve Him there. What the service is. There remains a duty to believe in His divinity , and not to attribute to Him what angers Him or describe Him with unworthy qualities. \"This is the way of complete reason, and it is impossible otherwise.\" Scripture. There is another, regular service. Hashem will designate a place to which people come from where they live (remember, Olam HaBa for Saadia is a physical world) at set intervals, like Shabbat and Rosh Chodesh for us now . They will serve there as commanded and return home without further service: והיה מדי חדש בחדשו ומדי שבת בשבתו יבוא כל בשר להשתחות לפני אמר ה' (Yeshayahu 66:23) From new moon to new moon and from Shabbat to Shabbat, all flesh will come to bow before Me, says Hashem. And when they have finished, ויצאו וראו בפגרי האנשים הפשעים בי : they go out and see the wicked. Most other Rishonim read this pasuk as referring to the days of Mashiach and techiyat hametim, when the Beit HaMikdash is rebuilt and sacrifices offered. Saadia reads it as Olam HaBa. And since he said earlier there is no time in Olam HaBa, how will people know when to serve? \"I don't know. Obviously they'll be told.\" That is why he says it will be like Shabbat and Rosh Chodesh. This is the \"one point\" of time he had mentioned in Chapter 6. The Gemara. The righteous do not stand idle in the next world any more than in this one: תלמידי חכמים אין להם מנוחה לא בעולם הזה ולא בעולם הבא, שנאמר ילכו מחיל אל חיל יראה אל אלהים בציון (Berachot 64a) Torah scholars have no rest, neither in this world nor in the next, as it says: they go from strength to strength, appearing before God in Zion. The wicked do not serve If the righteous serve, might the wicked also serve and so rectify themselves? No. The wicked are not commanded to serve, because of their suffering : part of their punishment is that they have no more chance. And service would take them out of their perpetual punishment, which would be harsher than the law allows, since we have said the punishment is perpetual. \"Mitzvot Are Void in the Future\" \"Saadia is a complete outlier here.\" The pasuk can easily be refuted, since it is likely about the time of techiyat hametim and the Beit HaMikdash. And there are Gemarot that say the exact opposite. Shabbat 151b. Even for David HaMelech , who died on Shabbat, one may not desecrate Shabbat once he has died. Why? כיון שמת אדם בטל מן המצוות : once a person dies, he is free of the mitzvot. That is why we break Shabbat to save a life: as the Gemara in Yoma says, חלל עליו שבת אחת כדי שישמור שבתות הרבה (Yoma 85b) Desecrate one Shabbat for him so that he may keep many Shabbatot. We save a life so that the person can do more mitzvot. A dead person cannot, so we do not break Shabbat for him. And Rabbi Yochanan on במתים חפשי (Tehillim 88:6), \"free among the dead\": \"Dead people have a holiday?\" No: once a person dies he becomes free of the mitzvot . Niddah 61b. A garment with sha'atnez may not be sold to a non-Jew or made into a saddle for a donkey, but it may be made into shrouds for the dead . Rav Yosef says: זאת אומרת מצוות בטלות לעתיד לבא , this teaches that mitzvot are void in the future. \"I know people nowadays wouldn't like to do that. But this is halachah. Halachah is you can get buried with sha'atnez.\" The Rabbi's resolution \"The only answer I can think of\": look again at Saadia's language. He speaks of service and duty : to believe in God, not to speak badly of Him, and the periodic bowing and offerings. \"I think you have to say we have no other choice but to say that even according to Saadia there are no mitzvot in the world to come,\" no halachot, no sha'atnez, no Shabbat, no building a sukkah or eating matzah. Those belong to this world, as the Gemarot say. There are no mitzvot, but there is avodah , because, Saadia says, serving Hashem is something one must do for eternity. \"This is my own interpretation. I don't know if this is correct or not, but I found it so difficult to square this with the most basic Gemarot, which everyone agrees to.\" If a dead person had mitzvot, why could I not break Shabbat for him? \"But even that, nobody agrees to him. We're taught from a young age, and rightly so, that the time to do mitzvot, the time to serve Hashem, is in this world. The next world is consequences.\" For the Rambam it does not begin: Olam HaBa is נהנים מזיו השכינה . How others read \"no rest in the next world\" The Rabbi's own understanding has always been that the Gemara means continuous elevation . The Ramchal says explicitly that one rises higher and higher in Olam HaBa, according to one's deeds. The Ramban would say something similar: it takes time to shed one's physical identity, so one keeps growing. The Ben Ish Chai: the mitzvot cease at death, \"for a person becomes free of the mitzvot after death and his deeds are complete; but from the study of Torah he is not free, for even after death the soul engages in Torah above.\" That is far from Saadia. It is not service with choice, but continuous development of the intellect, \"and the Rambam would agree with that.\" The Maharsha asks why the Gemara says talmidei chachamim and not tzaddikim. Because it is about the intellect : there is no rest and no end to intellectual contemplation, in this world or the next. The intellect is always active, and that activity is itself its rest. In Olam HaBa the righteous sit with crowns on their heads ונהנים מזיו השכינה בעיון השכלי , enjoying the radiance of the Shechinah through intellectual contemplation. \"I think this could be written by the Rambam 100%.\" Chapter 10, the Last Two Questions: Reward and Free Will If people serve in Olam HaBa, what is their reward if they serve, and what happens if they do not? \"Meaning, you've got complete free will whether you want to serve Hashem in Olam HaBa. I'm sitting there in Gan Eden and I decide I don't want to serve Hashem anymore.\" Saadia says he answered this already: the Creator gives these commands only to those He knows will choose to serve and not to rebel . And when they choose to serve, He adds to their good. Since these two questions go back to earlier answers, his list counts as \"ten\" questions. So, \"believe it or not,\" there is free will to serve in Olam HaBa, but Hashem will only place in Gan Eden those He knows will serve, and they will be rewarded further for it. The Rabbi compared it to Saadia's answer, studied about two years earlier, to the question of human free will and Hashem's foreknowledge: we have free will, but Hashem knows what we will choose. \"And what happens if I worked really hard in this world, and Hashem knows I'm not going to serve in the next world? What happens to me then? I just find this so perplexing.\" This is not about the days of Mashiach: the Rambam allows free will in Messianic times and perhaps at techiyat hametim. Saadia is speaking of Olam HaBa itself. Flora's question Why should service in this world imply service in the next? The Rabbi agreed one need not accept it. In this world we face \"not a test, a kind of challenge. We finished our challenge and now we reap the consequences of the choices which we made.\" Flora added that the righteous sit with crowns on their heads and bask in the glory of Hashem; at most there is Torah and continuing to progress higher in spirituality. \"That's precisely what the Maharsha said, and the Ben Ish Chai to some extent.\" The more you understand HaKadosh Baruch Hu, the more you bask in the radiance of the Shechinah. \"But not that I've got free will to choose or not to choose to serve Hashem. That's a completely different concept. I don't know any other Rishon or Acharon who says what he says.\" Chapter 11: Why We Are Not Told the Details Saadia closes the essay by asking why we have only vague concepts of light and heat, and no detail of the exact reward for each mitzvah and the exact punishment for each sin. It was not defined in this world, for several reasons: We have not seen even the root. We know the general concept of reward and punishment only by deduction, not experience. How could we grasp its finer branches? It would be too much. The words of the Torah would become burdensome. We would pick and choose. If we knew the reward of each mitzvah, we would choose the ones with the bigger reward and neglect the rest. As the Mishnah says: הוי זהיר במצוה קלה כבחמורה, שאין אתה יודע מתן שכרן של מצות (Avot 2:1) Be as careful with a light mitzvah as with a weighty one, for you do not know the reward of the mitzvot. We focus on what is near. Our hearts are most occupied with what is coming next, the salvation . Before Olam HaBa, for Saadia, come Mashiach and techiyat hametim in this world, so the Prophets spent far more time on those than on Olam HaBa. Saadia's hope אני מקוה שיפרשו לנו בימות מלך המשיח גמול כל עבודה ועונש כל עבירה I hope that in the days of the King Mashiach they will explain to us the reward of every service and the punishment of every sin. At that time hearts will turn to seek wisdom and be refined to receive understanding, because they will be close to the time of Olam HaBa. That is why prophecy then will include everyone: והיה אחרי כן אשפוך את רוחי על כל בשר (Yoel 3:1) After this I will pour out My spirit on all flesh. They will prophesy not only about past and future but about reward and punishment, and Hashem will give signs to confirm it: ונתתי מופתים בשמים ובארץ (Yoel 3:3). These things will come before techiyat hametim: השמש יהפך לחשך והירח לדם לפני בוא יום ה' הגדול והנורא (Yoel 3:4). The final words of the essay Whoever cares for his soul will be among the wise, and whoever guides others will be among those who bring the many to righteousness: והמשכלים יזהרו כזהר הרקיע ומצדיקי הרבים ככוכבים לעולם ועד (Daniel 12:3) The wise will shine like the radiance of the firmament, and those who bring the many to righteousness like the stars, forever and ever. So every wise person should be vigilant and teach people the right way: teach your children, teach others, and spread the word of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Looking Ahead That completes Essay 9. \"As you can see, Saadia has a very physical understanding of Olam HaBa.\" The Rabbi planned to open the next shiur with a reflection on the whole essay, after the Tuesday shiur on the Rambam's conception of Olam HaBa. Then Essay 10 , which follows the end of Saadia's philosophical book. It is \"more mussar, more of an ethical work,\" with short chapters, not all of it relevant, so the class would go through it quickly, finish the book, and begin something new in January or February.","LNrrWdkS714":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 9 (Olam HaBa, The World to Come), the second half of Chapter 7 and Chapter 8 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 3 November 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Questions So Far The Rabbi opened by saying he was not feeling well, but did not want to cancel, since there would be no other shiurim that week. In Essay 9 Saadia has brought proofs for Olam HaBa from logic, Torah and tradition, and has begun answering his questions about its nature: Question Saadia's answer 1 and 2: reward and punishment The light and the heat of a sun 3: place A new place, newly created 4: time No time as we know it; day and night, months and years are only for the needs of this world 5 and 6: eternity Both reward and punishment are eternal Why eternal? Last week: if the reward were limited, a person could complain, \"Had You given me a greater incentive, I would have served You.\" So Hashem gives the greatest incentive, eternal reward, and no excuse remains. The same with punishment: had it been only a thousand or five thousand years, a person could say a real, eternal threat would have kept him from sin. And if someone suggests the threat is only a threat, Saadia replies that Hashem has said it, and what He says He keeps. Chapter 7 (continued): Proofs That It Is Forever Saadia now brings verses showing that Olam HaBa is eternal: אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם (Daniel 12:2) Some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt. Since for Saadia techiyat hametim leads into Olam HaBa, this shows clearly that both the reward and the punishment are eternal. נעמות בימינך נצח (Tehillim 16:11): pleasures at Your right hand forever. עד נצח לא יראו אור (Tehillim 49:20): they will never see light, for eternity. ושנותיך לא יתמו. בני עבדיך ישכונו וזרעם לפניך יכון (Tehillim 102:28-29): Your years will not end; the children of Your servants will dwell, and their descendants will be established before You. As Hashem endures forever, so will they before Him. If the future is endless, why not the past? Someone might argue: if created beings can exist with Hashem without end at the end of time, why could they not have existed without beginning at the start of time? There is a big difference, says Saadia. It is impossible for a created thing to have always existed alongside its Creator, because every maker must precede what he makes . Since Hashem made us, He was before us, so we cannot be eternal in the past. But once He has made us, just as we see that He sustains us every day, He can sustain us tomorrow, and having promised to do so, He can sustain us day after day forever. Reason has no difficulty with that. So: a beginning there must be, but an end there need not be. Then are we equal to God? A last point: if we exist forever, are we not equal to the Creator? That is plainly false, says Saadia. How can a being of body and spirit, which needs time and place, which receives pleasure, which is commanded and warned, and which depends on being kept in existence, be compared to the One who is exalted above all these? Even in eternity we remain His creations, with limits, while He has none. Is Eternal Punishment the Accepted View? \"You'll say that's probably the correct approach, the view of the Chachamim. And that's generally what we're brought up with, that Olam HaBa is eternal. The thing is, it's not entirely clear in the Gemara that that's the case.\" The Gemara in Rosh Hashanah פושעי ישראל בגופן ופושעי אומות העולם בגופן יורדין לגיהנם ונידונין בה שנים עשר חדש. לאחר שנים עשר חדש גופן כלה ונשמתן נשרפת ורוח מפזרתן תחת כפות רגלי הצדיקים... אבל המינין והמסורות והאפיקורסין... יורדין לגיהנם ונידונין בה לדורי דורות (Rosh Hashanah 17a) Sinners of Israel and of the nations with their bodies go down to Gehinnom and are judged there for twelve months. After twelve months their body is consumed, their soul is burnt, and the wind scatters them under the soles of the feet of the righteous... But the heretics, the informers and the apikorsim... go down to Gehinnom and are judged there for all generations. So most sinners, \"probably lots of us,\" go to Gehinnom for at most twelve months . Only those with the gravest sins, the deniers of the Torah, are judged for generations. How does Saadia square this? For him there is either eternal Gan Eden or eternal Gehinnom. Into which category fall those punished for twelve months who then receive eternal bliss? Perhaps one would have to say that for Saadia most people never go to Gehinnom at all, and only the real heretics receive eternal Gehinnom. But Saadia never mentions the twelve months anywhere in his writings. Tosafot: even the worst are not forever Another Gemara says: כל היורדין לגיהנם עולין, חוץ משלשה שיורדין ואין עולין All who go down to Gehinnom come up, except for three who go down and do not come up. That seems to say at least three categories receive eternal punishment. But Tosafot says: אין לפרש דאין עולים לעולם , it cannot mean they never come up. Rather, everyone else comes up immediately , and the fire of Gehinnom barely touches them; the three do not come up immediately, but they do come up. Even those who have no share in Olam HaBa are not judged for more than twelve months: אלא לאחר י\"ב חודש לא חיין ולא נידונין, ושרויין בלא טובה ובלא רעה After twelve months they neither live nor are judged, and remain without good and without bad. \"You could say it's similar to the Rambam in this, but let's not go there now.\" The point is that Tosafot reads \"they do not come up\" as לאו דווקא , not literally. In Tosafot's mind, at least as one approach, it was not settled that Gehinnom is eternal. And Tosafot obviously does not accept Saadia's reasoning that a limited threat would leave people an excuse. The Mishnah in Eduyot says plainly: משפט רשעים בגיהנם שנים עשר חדש The judgment of the wicked in Gehinnom is twelve months. You could say this means ordinary sinners, and the great heretics last longer. \"What bothers me though, is Saadia makes no reference to these mentions at all in the Gemara of twelve months.\" Why this is only a problem for Saadia The difficulty arises only because Saadia equates Gan Eden and Gehinnom with Olam HaBa . The Rambam, the Ramban and \"the vast majority of Rishonim\" do not. For them, all these Gemarot describe a kind of purging , limited to twelve months, after which one goes on to Olam HaBa, which is something new. But for Saadia, whenever the Mishnah and Talmud speak of Gehinnom they are speaking of Olam HaBa, and they say the wicked receive twelve months. \"There is an end, it is finite, it's not eternal. So, I think it's open for interpretation.\" Other dissenting voices Rabbi Yitzchak Aboab da Fonseca , about 400 years ago. The Rabbi could not find his book either in print or online, but saw it quoted: he argued, against a lot of opposition, that there is no eternal damnation in Judaism . The Rabbi shared an article about him in the chat. \"He's not so well known, but there was such an opinion.\" Rabbi Chasdai Crescas , in the Or HaShem . Crescas was the teacher of Rabbi Yosef Albo of the Ikarim, both in Spain. The Rabbi read a passage in which Crescas speaks of the final loss of the soul after the suffering of Gehinnom as the loss of its preparedness, \"as if it remains naked, without preparation,\" which Chazal called \"ashes,\" the remains of what is burnt. And when the loss is not of the final degree, \"it is not far-fetched that there should be a temporary purging , as His wisdom decrees.\" (The Rabbi mentioned that the class might study the Or HaShem after finishing Emunot VeDeot.) The Rambam \"Just to repeat what we've learned in the past about the Rambam, my understanding of the Rambam, and it's pretty clear this is his approach\": the greatest punishment is karet . There is no hellfire. All the references in Shas to the soul being burnt are metaphors for being cut off and having no portion in Olam HaBa. The Rambam does have a temporary Gehinnom for purging. But there is no eternal damnation, unless one reads the Rambam (which is possible) as saying that the person who is cut off remains aware of it and suffers eternal shame at what he lost. \"It's not certain either way. But there isn't a vengeful constant punishing. That's not in the Rambam's books.\" Summary on eternity View Reward Punishment Saadia Eternal Eternal, for every rasha The Gemara (simple reading) Eternal Twelve months of purging for most; perhaps eternal for a few Tosafot (one suggestion) Eternal At most twelve months, then neither good nor bad Aboab, Crescas Not eternal / possibly temporary Rambam Eternal Eternal loss , being cut off: \"an eternal missed opportunity,\" not torment \"All of these things are very open, with different views. Saadia is not on the fence here.\" Chapter 8, Question 7: Is Everyone's Reward the Same? One mitzvah, one sin Saadia says something very interesting: כמו שלא יכלה גמול אלף זכיות, כן לא יכלה גמול זכות אחת. וכמו שאלף חובה לא יכלה עונשה, כן חובה אחת לא יכלה עונשה Just as the reward of a thousand merits never ends, so the reward of one merit never ends. And just as the punishment of a thousand sins never ends, so the punishment of one sin never ends. So what is the difference between one and a thousand? Both are eternal, but each person's lot is commensurate with his deeds . Whoever does one merit, or ten, or a hundred, or a thousand, will be rewarded according to what he did, only it is eternal. Levels of a good life Saadia's analogy is life in this world: Some people's good fortune is simply tranquillity . Others have tranquillity and also eat and drink the best. Others have all that and fine clothes . Others have all that and high rank and position . So too in Olam HaBa, each with a verse: ישכן לבטח : some will simply dwell in safety, relaxed. בצל כנפיך יחסיון (Tehillim 36:8): others shelter in the shadow of Hashem's wings, \"a bit more.\" ירוין מדשן ביתך (Tehillim 36:9): others are sated from the abundance of His house. והלבש אתך מחלצות (Zecharyah 3:4): others are clothed in fine garments. \"He's taking this so literally.\" ונתתי לך מהלכים בין העמדים האלה (Zecharyah 3:7): the highest walk among the angels who stand, שרפים עמדים ממעל לו (Yeshayahu 6:2). The person of a thousand mitzvot is with the angels. The person of one mitzvah has eternity too, \"but it's eternal tranquillity. That's it.\" Levels of punishment And the reverse. In this world, some are punished by being held in custody ; others are also dragged with ropes ; others are bound ; others are bound in chains and fetters ; and others, besides all that, are beaten with painful blows. So too in Olam HaBa: ואספו אספה אסיר על בור וסגרו על מסגר (Yeshayahu 24:22): gathered as prisoners in a pit and locked up. עוונותיו ילכדנו את הרשע ובחבלי חטאתו יתמך (Mishlei 5:22): his iniquities will trap the wicked, and he will be held by the cords of his sin. Others in shackles . וסער מתגורר על ראש רשעים יחול (Yirmiyahu 23:19): a raging storm will burst on the head of the wicked. \"This does seem very reductive, I have to say.\" What everyone agrees on The concept of levels in Olam HaBa is agreed by all: the Rambam says it clearly, the Ramban says it clearly. The Rabbi recalled his analogy of seats in a stadium. \"He puts it in very material terms, which is what I find difficult. But the concept is agreed upon by all\": the level you reach corresponds to the mitzvot you have done. Saadia extends this to levels of punishment too. The Rabbi added that the Rambam , in his commentary to the end of Makkot, says something similar about a single mitzvah: if you do one mitzvah properly , with no self-interest at all, \"which is very difficult,\" you are guaranteed a place in Olam HaBa, because that act has made an impact on your character. The Problem: What If I Have Both? \"Who can tell me the obvious question on this?\" If one mitzvah earns eternal bliss and one sin earns eternal Gehinnom, what about someone with a thousand mitzvot and one sin? Most of us have great middot and also flaws; we do many good things and some bad, and most of us will end with some sin for which we have not done teshuvah. How can I have both eternal Gan Eden and eternal Gehinnom? The Rambam would answer simply. Every sin has an impact on you, but at the end of your life we look at all your actions together : your good deeds, your moral and intellectual perfection, your negative traits. We assess who you are as an entire person, and that determines your level for eternity. The Ramban would not have the problem either, because for him there is a waiting room: twelve months of Gehinnom for purging. Saadia has no waiting room. Olam HaBa is Gan Eden and Gehinnom for eternity, and each mitzvah and each aveirah is eternal. \"Can't you subtract the sins from the mitzvot?\" A participant suggested deducting one from the other. \"That's a nice attempt, but it doesn't work like that.\" The pasuk in Ekev says Hashem אשר לא ישא פנים ולא יקח שחד (Devarim 10:17), does not show favour and takes no bribe. How can one bribe God? The Midrash says: the bribe of mitzvot . As the Ramban explains on this pasuk, you cannot weigh a mitzvah against an aveirah. Even after a whole life of mitzvot, one sin without teshuvah must face its consequence, whether in this world, in Gehinnom or elsewhere. Everyone agrees there is no cancelling-out game. That is why, when children come home before Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur with pictures of scales, the Rabbi always takes time to explain: \"For little kids, fine, it's a weighing up, but there's no concept of weighing up.\" A participant offered the image of two different bank accounts . \"Exactly.\" Saadia's answer: two boxes Saadia already answered this in the essay on merits and demerits (Essay 5). He takes a very simple view: everyone is either a tzaddik or a rasha , judged by the majority of their deeds. 51% good, 49% bad: you are a tzaddik, a ben Olam HaBa, at a low level (tranquillity, no \"metaphorical beautiful clothes\"). Your 49% of sins are punished in this world , through its sufferings. 51% bad, 49% good: you are a rasha with eternal damnation, though not as severe as someone at 99%. Your 49% of mitzvot are rewarded in this world . Exactly 50-50: the beinoni. Hashem is מטה כלפי חסד , inclines toward kindness, and gives Olam HaBa. \"Already at that time, I found that concept very difficult, and I still do.\" It is \"just two boxes. It's very extreme, it's very simplistic.\" Within each box there are levels, and there is no mixture. For the Rambam, by contrast, we look at your whole identity, and only HaKadosh Baruch Hu can make that calculation: כי לא מחשבותי מחשבותיכם . If a mitzvah's reward is eternal, how can a rasha be paid for it in this world, and how can a tzaddik's sin be punished here? \"It's a famous question, there are answers to it.\" The class had discussed it in the past. A question about gilgul A participant suggested that perhaps someone is punished by coming back to earth for a hard life. The Rabbi answered that reincarnation is not the traditional view. Saadia did not believe in it, nor did the Rambam, and there is no mention of it anywhere in Shas or Midrash. \"But having said that, there are opinions which do believe in it.\" It appears in the Zohar , and is held from the Ramban onwards; the Ramban explains the whole of Iyov through gilgulim. But even after the Ramban many disagreed: Rabbi Yosef Albo threw it out, as did Menasheh ben Israel . Only from the time of the Arizal , about 500 years ago, did it become doctrine. \"Until then it was still a debate.\" Seven Levels of Light, Seven Levels of Fire Saadia now elaborates on the levels. Reason requires that the righteous have degrees, each above the other, and Scripture shows seven of them, each described with a different verse about light. \"Remember, this is all based on Saadia's view of Olam HaBa, the reward being light.\" Level The righteous receive Verse 1 Light like the rays of the sun וזרחה לכם יראי שמי שמש צדקה (Malachi 3:20) 2 Delight in its rays ומרפא בכנפיה (Malachi 3:20) 3 Light that stays, as if planted אור זרע לצדיק (Tehillim 97:11) 4 Ever-increasing light אור צדיקים ישמח (Mishlei 13:9) 5 Light like the firmament והמשכלים יזהרו כזהר הרקיע (Daniel 12:3) 6 Light like the stars ומצדיקי הרבים ככוכבים לעולם ועד (Daniel 12:3) 7 Light like the sun itself ואהביו כצאת השמש בגבורתו (Shoftim 5:31) He supports the idea of degrees from Moshe and those below him. Moshe's face shone; Yehoshua's less, as Hashem told Moshe ונתתה מהודך עליו (Bamidbar 27:20), \"some of your majesty,\" not all of it; and the seventy elders less still: ויאצל מן הרוח אשר עליו ויתן על שבעים איש הזקנים (Bamidbar 11:25). The purpose of giving Yehoshua some of Moshe's majesty was למען ישמעו כל עדת בני ישראל , so that the whole community, including the elders, would listen to him. So there were levels: Moshe, Yehoshua, the elders. As in this world, so in the next. And the wicked likewise have seven levels of fire : Level The wicked receive Verse 1 Fire that reddens the face פני להבים פניהם (Yeshayahu 13:8) 2 Faces blackened like a burnt pot כל פנים קבצו פארור (Yoel 2:6) 3 Roasting and cooking הנה היום בא בער כתנור (Malachi 3:19) 4 Fire that consumes תאכלהו אש לא נפח (Iyov 20:26) 5 Fire consuming wood מדרתה אש ועצים (Yeshayahu 30:33) 6 Fire consuming earth and stones ותאכל ארץ ויבלה (Devarim 32:22) 7 Fire in the depths of the earth כי אש היא עד אבדון תאכל (Iyov 31:12) Just as the plagues of Egypt struck everyone, but each according to his measure, as it says יפלס נתיב לאפו (Tehillim 78:50), He weighs out a path for His anger. And פלס ומאזני משפט לה' (Mishlei 16:11): the balance and scales of justice are Hashem's. The number seven The Rabbi noted a Midrash in the name of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai about seven kinds of faces of the righteous: like the sun, the moon, the firmament, the stars, lightning, roses and the Menorah. \"The concept of seven levels is a very old concept.\" That is also why at the end of Ne'ilah we say ה' הוא האלהים seven times, as if the Shechinah is ascending back through the seven levels. \"But you see how literally Saadia is taking all of this.\" For the Rambam there are certainly levels, though not specifically seven. \"Every human being is going to be in a different level. Maybe there are seven categories, but certainly each person will be taken into account in his entire framework, who he is.\" Summary and Looking Ahead Eternity: Saadia holds reward and punishment are both eternal. Many Rishonim agree, but there are dissenting views: the Gemara's twelve months, Tosafot, Aboab, Crescas. The Rambam's eternal punishment is eternal loss, not torment. Levels: for Saadia there are really only two categories , tzaddik or rasha, but within each there are seven sub-levels , of light or of fire. Next week: Do we see each other in Olam HaBa? Do the righteous see the wicked and the wicked the righteous, and does each group see its own? Is there service of Hashem in Olam HaBa? Is there free will? \"Shocking things we're going to learn.\" There would be no shiur that Tuesday or Thursday; the full schedule would resume the following week.","-xSaGP-a1ts":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 6 (The Soul), Chapter 8, the end of the essay Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 31 March 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Seven Chapters on the Soul The final shiur before Pesach. Through the summer, unless otherwise announced, the shiur runs 6 to 7. Tonight finishes the sixth essay. Chapter 1: the theories of what the soul is, and Saadia's refutations. Chapter 2: the study of the soul is a very subtle discipline. Chapter 3: Saadia's view. The soul is created , simultaneously with the body , a very fine ethereal substance finer than the spheres, which only works together with the body . It has several names, and its primary dwelling is the heart. Chapter 4: why Hashem puts a lofty soul in a lowly body. The body is not necessarily low, and this is the only way the soul can work toward eternal bliss. Chapter 5: whatever happens to the soul happens to the body in the world to come, and vice versa. Olam HaBa is not for the body alone, the soul alone or the bones alone. Chapter 6: מספר ימים , a set number of days, which Hashem increases or decreases according to a person's obedience. Chapter 7: at death a person sees the angel, yellow with blue eyes and an outstretched sword. We cannot see the soul leave because it is transparent. It then waits until Olam HaBa in a kind of waiting room, and its place there corresponds, on a limited level, to the bliss it will have. Two main parts, then: what the soul is and is not, and what happens to it in and before Olam HaBa. What reward and punishment actually are is a whole essay of its own, Essay 9. Ahead: Essay 7 on resurrection, Essay 8 on Mashiach, Essay 9 on reward and punishment, and Essay 10 on general musar and ethics. Chapter 8 adds \"a whole new topic\": gilgul , reincarnation. Part 1: Does the Soul Decompose? Before reincarnation, Saadia adds one paragraph that, the Rabbi said, really belongs at the end of Chapter 7. Having said that the soul waits after death to be rejoined with the body, he now rejects views that hold otherwise. View 1: the soul decomposes with the body. This follows from the theories in Chapter 1 that the soul is something physical, a fire or a wind, or an accident of the body (the five \"accident\" theories). If the soul is only an accident of the body's creation, then when the body decomposes, so does the soul. If it is physical, it too decomposes with the body. View 2: the soul returns to its source. Those who held the soul is a spiritual matter that came from God (or from God and something else, or from two deities) believe that after death it returns to where it came from. Saadia has already shown these theories are mistaken, so we are left with his: the body dies, the soul waits, and the two are combined again for bliss in Olam HaBa at the resurrection. \"A bit of Hashem\"? The Rabbi drew out a point: Saadia here clearly rejects the idea that the soul is something from Hashem Himself. \"You ask little children who go to schools what's the neshamah, they'll tell you it's a bit of Hashem. That's literally how they translate it in some schools.\" According to Saadia, and according to mainstream opinion, that is a totally wrong understanding. The Rabbi said the concept of everyone having a spark of Hashem within him comes from Chabad , but \"we have to be very careful with using such terminology. Our souls are not a part of Hashem.\" The idea that Hashem enters into something physical within our essence \"is very Christian... it doesn't work like that.\" Saadia says those who think the soul comes from God and returns to Him are wrong. Rather, the soul is a new creation, simultaneous with the body; while the body decomposes, the soul waits under the wings of the Shechinah, and is rejoined with the resurrected body for eternal bliss. Part 2: Reincarnation Defining terms Saadia does not use the word gilgul , which is a later term. He calls it העתקה , \"copying over,\" transmigration. Reincarnation must not be confused with resurrection. Resurrection is a fundamental belief of the Chachamim, of the Rambam and of all Jews; how it will happen is \"a conversation for another time,\" but that there will be a resurrection is accepted. Olam HaBa and Mashiach likewise. \"These are all things which are non-negotiable.\" Reincarnation is something completely different: a person dies, and while the soul is waiting, \"perhaps it's bored or for whatever reason,\" it comes back down in a new human body, or in an animal; and part of the theory was that an animal's soul could also pass into a human. Saadia's text \"I found some people who call themselves Jews who believe in transmigration. They say the soul of Reuven can later go into Shimon, and many of them also say that the spirit of a human can go into an animal and the soul of an animal into a human, and more of this nonsense and madness. I thought about what caused them to believe this madness, and I found four primary mistakes.\" Why \"people who call themselves Jews\"? The explanation given is that Saadia means the Karaites , one of his main targets throughout the book, who held this belief. He clearly speaks of them very negatively. Some of his four arguments, the Rabbi warned, are debatable: \"not all of them are so smooth.\" Mistake 1: It assumes the soul comes from God Those who believe that a soul leaves one body and enters another are, says Saadia, following those who believe the soul comes from God, from something spiritual. But Saadia holds firmly that the soul is created simultaneously with the body, \"so that in itself has already stopped the whole concept of gilgul neshamot, because there is no concept of taking a soul and putting it into a body.\" Whether the body is \"created\" at conception or at birth, which was discussed in Chapter 3, the soul is created with it. And since we have already rejected the idea that the soul comes from God Himself, reincarnation falls with it. The Rabbi's verdict: \"That's not so convincing.\" It is less an argument against reincarnation than a reminder that it rests on a view already rejected. Mistake 2: Humans resemble animals People see similarities between humans and animals, \"long before evolution.\" One person is humble like a sheep, another bad like a donkey, one always eating like a dog, another fast like a bird. So, they say, he must have come from a bird, from a dog. The Rabbi noted that in contemporary Jewish belief in gilgul we hear very little about animal souls coming into humans. We hear of humans coming back as humans, or, \"if you're really bad,\" as animals. In Saadia's time, the two-way traffic was a big belief. Saadia: \"May God have mercy upon them for their stupidity.\" If the soul of an animal comes into a human, it now functions as a human soul. So the body must have the power to transform its nature, to upgrade it. And when a human soul enters an animal, the body must downgrade it to fit an animal. Yet they also say the soul gives the person animal characteristics while he is still in human form. \"So what's in charge?\" They both deny that the soul has its own intrinsic nature and contradict themselves. The Rabbi's analogy from recent experience: try transferring from one kind of phone to another, from a Samsung to an iPhone. \"There's so many things you can't do... because it's two different things.\" Transferring between two iPhones is one thing; between two different systems, much of it does not work. So too between animal and human souls. Again debatable: \"especially if we're only looking at reincarnation of humans into humans, then this argument falls away.\" Mistake 3: Why do babies suffer? \"It's a very common claim you hear nowadays.\" God is omnibenevolent; He does no injustice. So why are babies born with disabilities and terrible diseases, rachmana litzlan ? It must be that they are suffering for sins committed in a previous body. Saadia gives two answers. Answer 1: reward in the future. Saadia already said in Essay 5, when he discussed why the righteous suffer, that suffering can serve to increase a person's reward. The child suffering now will be compensated in the future. The Rabbi noted that this is Saadia's opinion rather than an argument; the Rambam rejects that kind of suffering, so for the Rambam, who clearly did not believe in reincarnation either, another explanation of children's suffering is needed. Answer 2: commanded for the future. \"I'm not entirely clear on this. I'll do my best to explain the way I understood it.\" Ask them about the soul's original state, before it was placed in a human. Was it under commandments then? If not, there can be no punishment, because there was no commandment. If it was, then at that point it had done nothing wrong or right, so it was given commandments for the future . If they accept that a soul can be charged with responsibilities looking toward the future, they should also accept that it can suffer now for reward in the future. Either way, a baby's suffering is no proof of a previous life. Q: What about the sins of the fathers visited on three or four generations? You could read the Torah that way, but not according to the Chachamim's interpretation, which everyone accepts: children suffer for parents' deeds only when they continue those deeds. A baby has not yet done anything. And if you say Hashem is punishing the child for what he will do later, that runs into Yishmael , of whom it says באשר הוא שם : we are judged for what we are now, not for what we will do. The Rabbi acknowledged the exception of the rebellious son, \"the 13-year-old who gets killed,\" which \"is a whole discussion.\" The general principle remains that a person is punished for what he is doing now. Mistake 4: Five verses They bring \"dubious sources from the verses.\" Saadia refutes five. 1. \"Those who are not here with us today.\" Moshe, on the last day of his life, makes the covenant את אשר ישנו פה עמנו היום... ואת אשר איננו פה עמנו היום . They said this means those here now and those who will later come in other bodies. \"Clearly a nonsensical proof.\" If it were the same souls, they would already be \"with me today.\" Rather, it means future generations, who will receive these words through tradition and must keep the covenant. The Rabbi mentioned the famous Midrash that all the souls of Am Yisrael were present at the covenant, the souls in bodies and the souls waiting to enter bodies. Saadia cannot accept that, because he holds there is \"no warehouse of souls waiting to be put into bodies.\" In any case, none of the Rishonim who did believe in gilgul brought a proof from this verse. 2. \"Who did not walk.\" אשרי האיש אשר לא הלך בעצת רשעים : why past tense? Because, they say, he is praised for not having sinned in his previous life. Saadia: the verse is praising a constant, present way of life. Proof: the next verse says ובתורתו יהגה יומם ולילה , literally \"he will study.\" If you take the tenses literally, he is rewarded for Torah he has not yet learned. The Rabbi added (Saadia implies it but does not say it) that Hebrew often uses past or future for something present and constant. \"Lifestyle is a lifestyle in which you don't walk in the path of the wicked, and you study Torah.\" 3. \"It changes like clay.\" תתהפך כחומר חותם ויתיצבו כמו לבוש (Iyov). \"The next proof is actually a proof which was brought by some of the Rishonim.\" Some understood that the soul changes like pliable clay from one form to another, and that, said the Rabbi, is how the Ramban understood it. Saadia calls this naive. The preceding verse mentions the earth, and it is the earth that changes: it will bring forth all kinds of strange and frightening things to punish the wicked, who will be attached to it \"like a garment attached to you,\" unable to shake off their punishment. The Ibn Ezra explains the verse the same way. 4. \"He restores my soul.\" נפשי ישובב (Tehillim 23). They thought it meant restoring the soul into another body. \"Doesn't mean restore me into a new body, it means give me some tranquility... give me energy back again.\" Saadia brings a series of verses, and the Rabbi said \"he's 100% right here\": Shimshon , thirsty, drank, and ותשב רוחו ויחי : he had not died; his energy returned. The Egyptian whom David fed when he was starving: ותשב רוחו אליו . ונפש אדניו ישיב : a faithful messenger restores his master's soul. תורת ה' תמימה משיבת נפש : Torah restores the soul, giving you energy for life. Saadia adds: \"I'm more exalted than discussing these stupid examples. I shouldn't even be talking to you about this, only because I'm afraid people believe these things.\" 5. \"From the four winds.\" מארבע רוחות באי הרוח ופחי בהרוגים האלה ויחיו (Yechezkel 37). They read ruach as the soul, blown into dead bodies so that they live again. But \"four directions\" is about the winds: Hashem calls the wind from all four directions to give life to the bodies. It is a verse about resurrection , not reincarnation. Saadia's summary Soul decomposition is rejected; the soul waits. Four mistakes behind reincarnation: equating oneself with those who think the soul comes from God; animal and human resemblances, though one soul's essence cannot change into another; children's suffering, which is for future reward; and five verses, each easily refuted, \"and indeed they are easily refuted.\" Part 3: The Rabbi's Survey of Gilgul In the last twenty minutes, the Rabbi surveyed the history of the idea. Outside Judaism The first account we have is from the 5th century BCE. Pythagoras and other Greek philosophers believed in reincarnation; Pythagoras said it came from the Egyptians, and that a human soul passes through every kind of animal in a process of about 3,000 years. Plato also believed it, and this may be what Saadia means about animal characteristics: Plato said adulterers come back as donkeys, the dishonest as foxes, and good people as bees and ants. In Jewish sources \"Imagine there isn't the Zohar, okay?\" Then there is no mention of gilgul anywhere in Tanach, in the Talmud, or in all of rabbinic literature. \"To the best of my knowledge, the very first person to bring this concept down is Rav Saadia Gaon, and he only brings it to ridicule it and to disprove it.\" The Rambam makes absolutely no mention of it anywhere in his writings, for or against. \"He just didn't bother wasting his time with it.\" We have testimony that the Rambam's son wrote that his father did not believe in it. Rishonim who did believe in it include the Ramban in the 13th century, who explains the whole book of Iyov , and Iyov's suffering, through gilgul. Kabbalists in Spain liked to connect it to yibbum : when the brother marries the widow and has a child, the soul of the first husband comes back in that child. Hence Er and Onan : Onan \"knew that the child won't be his,\" that it would carry his brother's soul, and so he wasted his seed rather than give Tamar a child. Yosef Albo and Chasdai Crescas , the Spanish philosophers, reject the whole concept. It truly flourishes with the Arizal , who told people they were the gilgul of this or that figure. His student Rabbi Chaim Vital wrote Sha'ar HaGilgulim , full of such identifications: \"I saw this woman, and I could see she was a gilgul of that person.\" \"Then this became accepted and mainstream practice, like many things which the Arizal encouraged became normal things.\" The Rabbi said he could list ten practices off the top of his head that most Jews assume are required by halachah, which in fact come from the Arizal. Gilgul is \"a classical example.\" People assume it is a fundamental belief, and rabbis preach that if you are bad you will come back as an animal, or even a stone. The Zohar \"The Zohar is the problem here.\" If the Zohar was written by Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai in the first or second century CE, then there is a rabbinic source for gilgul. But it appears nowhere else, and the Zohar only came to public light around 1290, the claim being that it had been hidden for 1,200 years. The question of its authorship \"is a shiur beyond the scope of this evening... It may leave you uncomfortable.\" So do Jews believe in reincarnation? \"Depends who you ask.\" According to Tanach and Talmud, \"a flat no.\" According to the kabbalistic sources, the Arizal and the Zohar, \"a certain yes.\" Is it a fundamental belief? \"I think we can all agree it's not.\" Can those who believe it call one who does not a heretic? \"Absolutely not... It's not principles of faith. It's a belief some have, some don't have.\" Two extremes Belz and the Rashash. Belzer Chassidim do not learn the commentary on the Talmud of the Rashash (Rabbi Shmuel Strashun of Lithuania), \"a beautiful interpretation... really, really nice and clear.\" Why? The Gemara in Bava Metzia explains ברוך אתה בבאך וברוך אתה בצאתך : blessed is your leaving the world without sin, just as you came into the world without sin. The Rashash wrote that this is a difficulty for those who believe in gilgul, since a reincarnated soul would come into the world with sin. Because of that, a Belzer Rebbe forbade his Chassidim to study him as a suspected heretic. \"That doesn't make sense, in my understanding... You're going to have to not learn the Rambam either. You have to not learn Saadia Gaon.\" Breslov. The Rabbi recalled a conversation with a renowned talmid chacham of the Mir who is also a Breslover Chassid. He told him: in Breslov we say, when you go up and are offered the chance to come back as a gilgul, say no, because you may end up doing worse the second time. The questions it raises The Rabbi has a whole shiur on how gilgul is supposed to work, for those who believe it: how many times can you come back, and why? How do you know what you came back for? Can you make things worse? And at the resurrection, \"your soul is one, your body's two, so which body did you come back into?\" \"Loads of really bizarre interpretations.\" But for Saadia, \"this conversation is futile because there's nothing to talk about.\" Summary The soul does not decompose with the body, and it does not \"return\" to God as a part of Him. It is a new creation that waits, then rejoins the body. The Rabbi warned against teaching children that the soul is \"a bit of Hashem.\" Reincarnation (העתקה) is not resurrection. Resurrection is a fundamental belief; reincarnation is not. Saadia's four mistakes : it presumes the soul comes from God; animal-human resemblance cannot change a soul's nature; children's suffering is for future reward, not past sin; and five verses misread (Moshe's covenant, Tehillim 1, Iyov's clay, \"He restores my soul,\" the four winds). History: Pythagoras and Plato; nothing in Tanach or Talmud; first mentioned by Saadia only to refute it; ignored by the Rambam; accepted by the Ramban; rejected by Albo and Crescas; made mainstream by the Arizal and Sha'ar HaGilgulim; the Zohar is the crux. Not a principle of faith. Neither side may call the other heretics. Coming Up This completes Saadia's introduction and the first six essays . Remaining: Essay 7 on resurrection, Essay 8 on redemption, Essay 9 on the world to come, and Essay 10, \"some nice ethics.\" The shiur resumes a week after Pesach (the Monday after Pesach is Isru Chag) with Essay 7 . In the meantime: \"feel free to review, go over the six essays... so that we'll be clear on Saadia's philosophy before we continue.\"","TD_50pP01bM":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 9 (Olam HaBa, The World to Come), Chapter 5 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 20 October 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Where We Are in the Book The first shiur after the long break of the Yamim Tovim, with the weekly schedule back: Monday Emunot VeDeot, Tuesday Rambam, Thursday Hilchot Shabbat. The Rabbi began with an overview of the whole sefer: Essays Theme 1 to 3 HaKadosh Baruch Hu : His attributes and how we relate to Him 4 to 6 Revelation and our duties : mitzvot and aveirot, tzaddikim and resha'im, prophecy, Torah, kinds of mitzvot 7 to 9 The end of days Rav Saadia's picture of the end of days has several points other Rishonim disagree with: Essays 7 and 8: In this world, on this planet, Mashiach will come, and that era will last until the end of the 6,000 years. During it there will be a techiyat hametim for specific people in Am Yisrael. Essay 9: All of this leads to Olam HaBa , when everyone who deserves it, Jewish or non-Jewish , will be resurrected into a new world . In Elul the class did Chapters 1 to 4 : proofs for Olam HaBa from logic, from the Torah (with the question of why the Torah does not mention it explicitly) and from rabbinic tradition. But Saadia had not yet said what Olam HaBa is . From Chapter 5 to the end of the essay (Chapter 11) he goes through fourteen questions on its nature. \"He's the first one to actually discuss them,\" the Rabbi said, \"and I'll tell you now straight up that some of his views are really quite radical.\" Some were not accepted at all. Two points before starting The Rambam disagrees with almost every point. \"I was going through it: almost every single point that Saadia says, the Rambam disagrees with.\" The Rabbi promised to \"iron out the differences\" point by point. Saadia takes a very literal view of the pesukim about Olam HaBa, something the Rambam would later say was wrong. And the crucial background: for Saadia, Olam HaBa is a new world . It is a \"world to come\" in the plain sense. It does not exist yet and will be created in the future. Mashiach and one stage of techiyat hametim happen here, in the world we know. Then there will be a final resurrection of all who deserve Olam HaBa, in a new world, where people will enjoy \"the bliss or the torment of the world to come.\" Chapter 5: Saadia's List of Questions Saadia opens by recalling what he has already established. The reward of soul and body cannot be separated , because they are one agent . The soul and body are reunited to begin the new world. Since the body took part in the work and the effort, it must be rewarded equally. The Rabbi noted that the majority of Rishonim and Acharonim follow Saadia that Olam HaBa is body and soul together. The Rambam does not: for him Olam HaBa is soul alone . Now Saadia lists what still needs explaining: and 2. The nature of the reward and the nature of the punishment . The place : what environment will there be? Like this world or different? Time : will there be time at all, and if so, for what purpose? Perpetuity : what makes reward and punishment everlasting? Saadia holds that they are, and will explain why. Equality : does everyone receive equal reward and equal punishment, or are there differences? Who deserves everlasting reward and everlasting punishment. Meeting : will those being rewarded meet each other? Will those being punished? Will the two groups meet? Service : is there avodat Hashem, Torah and mitzvot, in Olam HaBa? Choice : could people choose wrongly and sin there? \"We're not talking about Messianic times. We're talking about the world to come.\" Reward for that service : if there is service in Olam HaBa, where is its reward? Saadia calls these \"ten matters,\" though, as the Rabbi said, \"it's not really ten,\" and in all there are fourteen points across Chapters 5 to 11. Some questions are straightforward; some are \"really interesting.\" Is there service in Olam HaBa? \"Surely you would say no, we serve in this world, and then that's it. But Saadia has a question about it.\" There are statements in Chazal here and there, but nothing entirely clear, and there are different approaches. Tonight: points 1 and 2. The Nature of Reward and Punishment: One Substance Saadia says he mentioned this briefly in Chapter 4 when he quoted Chazal, but must now explain more: שהגמול והעונש שני ענינים דקים יבראם אלהינו בעת השלום Reward and punishment are two subtle things that our God will create at the time of repayment. They are not here yet; they will be created at the end of time. Each person will receive what is fitting for him. And both come from one substance : דומה לכח האש שורפת ומאירה Like the power of fire, which burns and gives light. This substance gives light to the righteous and not to the wicked, and burns the wicked and not the righteous. \"Somehow the tzaddikim only get the light and not the heat of the fire, and somehow the resha'im only get the fire and not the light.\" Whether this is meant literally, the Rabbi said, we will see. The sun in Malachi כי הנה היום בא בער כתנור... וזרחה לכם יראי שמי שמש צדקה ומרפא בכנפיה (Malachi 3:19-20) For behold, the day is coming, burning like an oven... and for you who fear My name, a sun of righteousness will shine, with healing in its wings. For Saadia, the \"day\" that burns and the \"sun of righteousness\" are one thing . The Tanach sometimes uses יום to mean the sun. In Shoftim, והיום רד מאד , the day had gone far down, and הנה נא רפה היום לערב , the day has waned toward evening: in both, \"day\" means the sun, which was setting. So Malachi describes the future as a sun that gives both burning heat and bright light. The difference from our sun With the sun we know, heat and light are mixed together and inseparable . More light means more heat; less heat, less light. But the substance Hashem will create will gather its light to the righteous and its heat to the wicked . How? Saadia offers two possibilities: A special property Hashem gives the substance itself. It is the same light and heat, but Hashem shields the righteous from the heat and hides the light from the wicked. Saadia says the second is more likely , because we have already seen it happen. In the plague of darkness: ולכל בני ישראל היה אור במושבתם (Shemot 10:23) But all the Children of Israel had light in their dwellings. The Israelites lived among the Egyptians, as we see from ושאלה אשה משכנתה ומגרת ביתה , each woman would ask her neighbour (Shemot 3:22). Yet the Egyptians had darkness and the Israelites had light. Hashem can do it, and so in the future He will give only light to the tzaddikim and only heat and fire to the resha'im. Why the Tanach calls reward light and punishment fire On this basis Saadia explains why Scripture calls reward light and punishment fire : כי עמך מקור חיים באורך נראה אור (Tehillim 36:10): with You is the source of life; in Your light we see light. אור זרע לצדיק (Tehillim 97:11): light is sown for the righteous. אור צדיקים ישמח (Mishlei 13:9): the light of the righteous rejoices. להשיב נפשו מני שחת לאור באור החיים (Iyov 33:30): to bring his soul back from the pit, to be lit with the light of life. And for the wicked: והיה החסן לנערת ופעלו לניצוץ ובערו שניהם (Yeshayahu 1:31): the strong will be like chaff and his work a spark, and both will burn. כי ערוך מאתמול תפתה (Yeshayahu 30:33): Tofteh has long been prepared. The Rabbi explained that Tofet was a place near Yerushalayim where idolaters burned their children as sacrifices; it returns at the end of the chapter. ואש אכלה אהלי שחד (Iyov 15:34): fire will consume the tents of bribery. תאכלהו אש לא נפח (Iyov 20:26): a fire not blown will consume him. רוחכם אש תאכלכם (Yeshayahu 33:11): your spirit is a fire that will consume you. ימטר על רשעים פחים אש וגפרית (Tehillim 11:6): He will rain coals, fire and brimstone on the wicked. The Gemara behind it Saadia does not cite it, but the Rabbi pointed out that this is an explicit Gemara (Avodah Zarah): אמר רבי שמעון בן לקיש: אין גיהנם לעתיד לבא, אלא הקדוש ברוך הוא מוציא חמה מנרתיקה ומקדיר, רשעים נידונין בה וצדיקים מתרפאין בה Reish Lakish said: there is no Gehinnom in the future. Rather, HaKadosh Baruch Hu takes the sun out of its sheath; the wicked are judged by it and the righteous are healed by it. The wicked, from כי הנה היום בא בער כתנור ; the righteous, from וזרחה לכם יראי שמי שמש צדקה ומרפא בכנפיה . And not only are they healed, they delight in it . The Gemara speaks of Gehinnom . Saadia, as we will see at the end of the chapter, holds that Gehinnom and Olam HaBa are the same thing : Gehinnom is the name for the punishment of the World to Come. So he reads this Gemara as describing Olam HaBa itself, and he takes it \"extremely literally.\" The question of eternal punishment In a later chapter Saadia will hold that both the light and the fire are perpetual , and he will ask himself the obvious question. Eternal reward for a lifetime of hard work makes sense. \"But why would Hashem give perpetual punishment for those who sinned for 120 years? Even the worst person, you sin for 100 years, 120 years.\" The class will see his answer when they get there. The key point for now: for Saadia, it seems, reward and punishment in Olam HaBa are physical . We have bodies and souls. The greatest reward is an intense light, \"an intense sustenance of light\"; for the wicked it is an intense fire, \"and it goes on and on and on.\" The Rabbi's Reflections Photosynthesis and Yom Kippur \"The closest analogy I can think of for this, it actually works quite nicely\": photosynthesis . Plants are subtle and live and grow from sunlight; the more sunshine and warmth, the more they grow. Olam HaBa will be a very subtle place where body and soul come together. Or think of Yom Kippur . We do not eat or drink, the closest we come all year to being beyond the physical. That is why on Yom Kippur we say ברוך שם כבוד מלכותו לעולם ועד aloud. The Ramchal writes that it belongs to the angels, because it means that Hashem's sovereignty and unity are seen , manifest. All year we say שמע ישראל and believe Hashem is One, but we do not see His sovereignty spread out, so we say Baruch Shem quietly. On Yom Kippur we wean ourselves off the physical. Scientifically we are sustained by the food we ate the day before, \"but on this level, we're being sustained through the light of Yom Kippur.\" Just as plants are sustained by light, so too the tzaddikim, who will not only be sustained but נהנים מזיו , will take pleasure in the radiance. \"I find this deeply troubling\" But what kind of pleasure is light? Sitting in the sun is lovely. \"That's what we have holidays for.\" Imagine the most tropical island you can, where the weather is always perfect, not too hot, not too cold, sunshine all the time. Some people live like that all year round, moving between continents and climates. \"But is that the climax? Is that it?\" Saadia, the Rabbi observed, speaks of אור של השמש , the light of a sun, not אור של השם , the light of Hashem. In describing the essence of reward and punishment he does not mention basking in the Shechinah, an idea that the Rambam, the Ramban, the Ramchal \"and everyone\" all echo. \"I find this deeply troubling.\" The Rambam's view The Rambam certainly disagrees: There is no body in Olam HaBa, so sunshine means nothing to the soul. More deeply, the reward is far more elevated: basking in the presence of HaKadosh Baruch Hu , the light of Hashem. \"You could explain all of Saadia metaphorically and say when he's referring to the light of the sun, he means the light of Hashem. I don't think he means that. He doesn't say that.\" Nor is that the thrust of the rest of the chapter. Questions from Mark \"Light in whose presence?\" Mark asked whether this is just pure light or pure fire, since Hashem is not mentioned. \"That's what it seems to be.\" Can one person have both? Most people are not perfect, Mark argued. They could have light for the good they did and fire for the wrong, like the hot embarrassment one feels after doing something wrong. The Rabbi called it \"a very, very important and good point.\" Most of us are beinonim. Do we spend some years in fire and then move to light? Saadia deals with this a couple of chapters later, when he discusses perpetuity and mitzvot and aveirot. What happens to someone who dies today? If Olam HaBa does not exist yet, where is the soul now? According to Saadia, there is no account of it. Souls are suspended, waiting, \"because he says very clearly, these things will only be created at the time of the end.\" The Rambam's view of Gan Eden and Gehinnom would be the subject of the Tuesday shiur the next night. Living on Light: Moshe Rabbeinu The next paragraph shows again how physical Saadia's picture is. If Olam HaBa is body and soul, and the only reward is light, and the Gemara quoted in Chapter 4 says there is no eating or drinking there, how will bodies live? The answer, says Saadia, is Moshe Rabbeinu , whom Hashem kept alive three times for forty days without food or drink: ואשב בהר ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה לחם לא אכלתי ומים לא שתיתי (Devarim 9:9) I remained on the mountain forty days and forty nights; I ate no bread and drank no water. How did he live? By the light Hashem placed on his face : ומשה לא ידע כי קרן עור פניו (Shemot 34:29) Moshe did not know that the skin of his face was radiant. That, says Saadia, is the sign by which we understand how the righteous will live in Olam HaBa: by light, without food. This was the wonder Hashem promised Moshe: נגד כל עמך אעשה נפלאת אשר לא נבראו בכל הארץ ובכל הגוים (Shemot 34:10). The same verse, opposite readings Saadia then reads the famous verse: עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך יעשה למחכה לו (Yeshayahu 64:3) No eye has seen, O God, except You, what He will do for those who wait for Him. For Saadia, what no eye has seen is a body living with no food . Moshe saw it, but no public eye has. The Rabbi pointed out that the Rambam uses precisely this verse to prove the opposite : that Olam HaBa cannot be anything physical. Sun and fire we have experienced, maybe not at the same intensity, but we have. If no eye has seen it, it cannot be sunshine or fire. \"Completely divergent, completely different views.\" Burning Without Being Consumed Another physical question. If the wicked have bodies and their punishment is fire, fire eventually consumes what it burns. How can anyone be punished perpetually in fire? We have no precedent from experience, says Saadia, and because we have none, Scripture states it explicitly: כי תולעתם לא תמות ואשם לא תכבה (Yeshayahu 66:24) Their worm will not die and their fire will not be extinguished. So their souls must be preserved in their bodies in a subtle way, so that they feel the painful heat without being destroyed. And the same must be true of the righteous: their constant intense light must not harm them. \"You think about it, you sit at the beach for too long, you get damaged.\" Saadia adds an analogy: when people want to punish someone for a long time, they give him food and drink so that he survives the punishment. \"It's ironic. I'm trying to punish you, but I'm giving you food and giving you medicine. Why? Because I want to punish you.\" But they do so within nature. The Creator acts above nature and preserves the person without food. \"Unbelievable,\" said the Rabbi. \"Scary, no? If this is what reward and punishment is, it's pretty scary.\" He added a verse Saadia could have brought: the burning bush . וירא והנה הסנה בער באש והסנה איננו אכל (Shemot 3:2) He looked, and behold, the bush was burning with fire, and the bush was not consumed. The same idea: burning, but never consumed. Why \"Gan Eden\" and \"Gehinnom\" Finally, Saadia explains the names. Gan Eden. The reward is called Gan Eden because there is nothing in this world more honoured than the garden where Hashem placed Adam , which the class had just read about that Shabbat in Bereishit. It is the closest image of bliss we have, so whenever Scripture wants to describe something wonderful it uses it: כגן עדן הארץ לפניו (Yoel 2:3). Gehinnom. The punishment is called Gehinnom because Scripture calls it Tofteh , a place near the Beit HaMikdash: לכן הנה ימים באים נאם ה' ולא יאמר עוד התפת וגיא בן הנם כי אם גיא ההרגה וקברו בתפת מאין מקום (Yirmiyahu 7:32) Therefore days are coming, says Hashem, when it will no longer be called Tofet or the Valley of Ben Hinnom, but the Valley of Slaughter. Tofet was where they offered human sacrifices and burned them. It stood in גיא בן הנם , the valley of a man named Hinnom. Because Tofet's fire was in that valley, \"Gei Ben Hinnom\" became the name for burning, shortened to Gehinnom . It could equally have been called Tofet. Just as Gan Eden is the image for anything good, Tofet is the image for anything terrible: והיו בתי ירושלם ובתי מלכי יהודה כמקום התפת הטמאים (Yirmiyahu 19:13). Gan Eden and Gehinnom are Olam HaBa \"The remarkable thing,\" the Rabbi said, is that for Saadia Gan Eden and Gehinnom are synonymous with Olam HaBa . That answers Mark's question: this is why Saadia gives no description of what happens between a person's death today and Olam HaBa, at the end of the 6,000 years, which Saadia takes literally. The Rambam and other Rishonim see it differently. The Ramban agrees with Saadia that Olam HaBa is body and soul, but not that it is Gan Eden and Gehinnom. What \"basically everyone holds\" is that Gan Eden and Gehinnom are temporary places where people receive reward or punishment according to their deeds until Olam HaBa. The Rambam and Ramban disagree about what Olam HaBa is, but agree that it is not Gan Eden and Gehinnom. Summary of Chapter 5 Question Saadia Rambam What are reward and punishment? One created substance: light for the righteous, fire for the wicked Closeness to Hashem or distance from Him Why does Scripture say light and fire? Because that is literally what they are Light is closeness; fire is the embarrassment of being far from Hashem How are bodies sustained? By light, like Moshe on the mountain There is no body and no sustenance How can fire burn forever? Hashem preserves the body above nature No question: no body Gan Eden and Gehinnom The same as Olam HaBa Preliminary stages before Olam HaBa Saadia will go further later in the essay, describing levels in Olam HaBa and seven levels of Gehinnom, with different kinds of fire. Discussion: Was Saadia Speaking Literally? The Rabbi asked why Saadia, of all people, would hold such a literal view. Perhaps he kept to basic terms so as not to push people away, since the idea of fire and light was so ingrained. But Saadia was willing \"so many times\" to go against the masses. Remember how strongly he fought for the incorporeality of God , castigating those who believed otherwise, at a time (the 900s) when many held a corporeal view. Today we take incorporeality for granted because of the Rambam. In Essay 7, too, Saadia set out when one may interpret the Torah metaphorically: when the literal reading goes against reason. \"So if he really did believe that the sun and the light are just closeness to Hashem or being far and embarrassed from Hashem, you would have expected Saadia to say something like that.\" Instead he doubles down later with seven levels of Gehinnom. A participant added that this makes it even more problematic: the book is written for someone coming to learn, and this would have been exactly the place to explain what is metaphorical, as the Moreh Nevuchim does a couple of centuries later. The Rabbi noted that Saadia also took techiyat hametim (Essay 7, which began as a separate pamphlet he wrote because of the confusion around it) and Mashiach (Essay 8, with the nations arriving in different carriages and modes of transport) quite literally. The final, tenth essay, encouraging good behaviour, was a separate work added to the book. \"So there's elements of him which seem sometimes to take things very metaphorically, certainly at the beginning of the book, in the first two essays.\" But here \"he seems to be taking everything\" literally, and the coming chapters describe quite a physical environment. \"I don't have a clear answer. I think it's quite difficult to say that this is all metaphorical.\" מאוד מאוד קשה , it's very, very difficult. \"But it's part of the sefer, we learn it. We don't always understand all of the Chachamim, and the Chachamim are not monolithic. But that's the view of Saadia. I ascribe to the school of the Rambam.\" Next week: Chapter 6, the place and the time of Olam HaBa .","wdFVpdnBQtQ":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 9 (Olam HaBa, The World to Come), Chapters 3 and 4 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 29 September 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Case for Olam HaBa So Far Rav Saadia's conception of עולם הבא is a newly created world in which body and soul together enjoy bliss for eternity. The first half of Essay 9 establishes that there is such a world at all: Chapter 1: six proofs from logic . Chapter 2: proofs from the Torah , followed by the question: if Olam HaBa is so logical, and can be proven from the Torah, why does the Torah not mention it explicitly? Whenever the Torah speaks about reward, it seems to be about this world. Saadia gave two answers, and over the last two shiurim the class went through nine answers in total, seven from the Rishonim and two from later thinkers. Tonight, moving quickly, the Rabbi covered two chapters: Chapter 3: not proofs, but seven categories , seven different ways in which the Torah and Tanach refer to Olam HaBa. Chapter 4: a few of the very many statements of Chazal about Olam HaBa. From Chapter 5 to the end of the essay, Saadia goes into \"the meat\" of what Olam HaBa is: what happens there, where and when it is, whether there is time, whether there are still mitzvot. That would begin after Sukkot. The shiur was given in the Aseret Yemei Teshuvah, two days before Yom Kippur, and started at 5:30 because of the early Minchah time. Several of the Rabbi's comments tied the chapter to the prayers of the season. Chapter 3: Seven Categories Saadia opens: having brought the proofs from the Torah in the previous chapter, he will now add seven categories . Each has a source in the Torah and is then followed up in Nevi'im and Ketuvim. Category 1: Life and death The Torah calls Olam HaBa חיים , life, and calls the lot of those who do not merit it מות , death. אשר יעשה אתם האדם וחי בהם (Vayikra 18:5) Which a person shall do and live by them. הנפש החטאת היא תמות (Yechezkel 18:4) The soul that sins, it shall die. Saadia reads \"and live by them\" as life in the World to Come. His reasoning: in this world, the tzaddik and the rasha seem to be equal . So when the Tanach promises life for doing the mitzvot, it cannot mean life in this world. It must mean the life of Olam HaBa. \"He takes two questions and balances them off each other.\" He brings many verses: כי מצאי מצא חיים... כל משנאי אהבו מות (Mishlei 8:35-36): whoever finds me finds life; all who hate me love death. ארח חיים למעלה למשכיל למען סור משאול מטה (Mishlei 15:24): the path of life goes upward for the wise, to turn away from the grave below. דרכי שאול ביתה (Mishlei 7:27): her house is the way to the grave. לולא האמנתי לראות בטוב ה' בארץ חיים (Tehillim 27:13), \"which we say now, this month.\" The \"land of life\" is clearly Olam HaBa. כי לא תעזב נפשי לשאול לא תתן חסידך לראות שחת. תודיעני ארח חיים (Tehillim 16:10-11): You will not abandon my soul to the grave... You will make known to me the path of life. Why we say Psalm 16 at a funeral The Rabbi pointed out that Tehillim 16, מכתם לדוד , is recited at a levayah because of this very pasuk. It is such a strong proof: David HaMelech says, don't leave me in the grave, show me the path of life. \"What do you mean? You're dead, you're in the grave. That's the point.\" Even after I die and lie in the grave, show me the real life, the life of Olam HaBa. At a funeral we are saying that the person has died physically, but please God he will have the path of life for the future. Why \"life\" has no adjective in the Amidah A digression for the season. In the Aseret Yemei Teshuvah we add four lines to the Amidah: Berachah Addition Wording 1 זכרנו לחיים life, unqualified 2 מי כמוך... זוכר יצוריו לחיים ברחמים life, unqualified 3rd from end וכתוב לחיים טובים כל בני בריתך good life Last בספר חיים ברכה ושלום life of blessing and peace Two questions: Why is it just \"life\" in the first two, while at the end we must add adjectives? How can we ask for anything in the first three berachot, which are meant only for praise and recognition, not requests? \"There's much more to this, but in short\": חיים without an adjective means the real life , the life of Olam HaBa. That needs no qualification. If you have to put an adjective on life, it is not Olam HaBa. The judgment of Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur is certainly about the coming year. But, the Rabbi said, there is also a judgment of what kind of life we will live in the coming year: will it be a life of Olam HaBa? Tosafot already writes that the judgment of Rosh Hashanah concerns חיי עולם הבא . Why would you need a judgment every year on your Olam HaBa, after 120? Because it means the life of Olam HaBa in this world . People can have parnassah, health, children, even happiness, \"great lives. But they're not living a life of spiritual yearning, a life where they're able to grow, to fulfil their potential, to become the best version of themselves. That's חיים. That's זכרנו לחיים.\" And that can be asked for in the opening berachot, because it is part of recognising Hashem: a life lived with Hashem. \"Living the year where I am present, I am aware, I am cognisant of HaKadosh Baruch Hu's presence in my life.\" Only at the end come the physical things: good life, health, blessing, peace, money. A question: death or karet? A participant asked: for the Rambam, the opposite of Olam HaBa is karet , being cut off, and most people will reach Olam HaBa at some level of closeness to Hashem. Saadia seems to speak of plain death. The Rabbi called it \"a very excellent point.\" From these verses alone one could read it either way, but she was right: Saadia does not see punishment the way the Rambam does . For the Rambam, the Rabbi said, punishment is always \"in inverted commas.\" There is no punishment, only consequences, and the greatest is karet. For Saadia, punishments are punishments in the literal sense , and he will describe them in quite graphic terms in a later chapter. Saadia himself will then ask why a merciful God would need to do this. The Rambam never has that question, because for him it is about consequences, not punishment. Category 2: Good stored away The Tanach tells us that good is stored away for the deeds of the righteous, and bad held back for the wicked . זכר צדיק לברכה ושם רשעים ירקב (Mishlei 10:7). The simple meaning is what we say when a righteous person dies: the memory of the righteous is a blessing, and the name of the wicked should rot. Saadia reads it as storing: the righteous will be remembered by Hashem for blessing, i.e. blessing is kept for them in the future, and rot is kept for the wicked. זכרה לי אלהי לטובה (Nechemiah 5:19): remember me, my God, for good. זכרה אלהי לטוביה ולסנבלט כמעשיו אלה (Nechemiah 6:14): Tobiah and Sanballat were the officials who tried to stop Nechemiah rebuilding. Remember their deeds. וצדקה תהיה לנו (Devarim 6:25): it will be stored up as good for us. והלך לפניך צדקך כבוד ה' יאספך (Yeshayahu 58:8): your righteousness will walk before you. Another verse said at funerals. Tzedakah is not charity On וצדקה תהיה לנו the Rabbi recalled a point Rabbi Sacks always made: the Torah's word for justice and for charity is the same. \"Charity is the wrong translation of צדקה.\" In English, charity means going above and beyond, something voluntary that marks an extra kind person. The Torah does not see it like that. \"צדקה, it's צדק, you have to give it.\" If you make 100 pounds, Hashem says 90 are yours, \"10 pounds, it's צדק, it's just. It's not charity, it's an obligation.\" Category 3: The books The Tanach speaks of Hashem having books in which the deeds of the righteous and the wicked are written. Saadia has already given a whole essay on the incorporeality of God, so obviously there are no literal books, but the concept is that everything is recorded. \"Of course that's what these whole ten days are about.\" The righteous were signed already on Rosh Hashanah; most of us are beinonim waiting until Yom Kippur. Those are the books of the year. But there are also books for eternity . מחני נא מספרך אשר כתבת (Shemot 32:32) Erase me, please, from Your book which You have written. Ask most people what Moshe meant after the golden calf, and they will say: erase me from the Torah. They will tell you the famous idea that Tetzaveh , the parashah before Ki Tisa, is the only parashah from Moshe's birth onward without his name. That is the midrashic reading, and the Gemara does explain it as the Torah. But the simple meaning, according to the Rishonim, is different. The Ramban says the book of life , and Saadia says something similar, the book of the righteous . If You do not accept my prayer, I do not want to be counted among Your tzaddikim. Erase me from the books where You write who will have Olam HaBa. More verses: ימחו מספר חיים (Tehillim 69:29): let them be erased from the book of life. ויכתב ספר זכרון לפניו ליראי ה' ולחשבי שמו (Malachi 3:16): a book of remembrance was written before Him, for those who fear God and value His name. הנה כתובה לפני (Yeshayahu 65:6): behold, it is written before Me. Category 4: A tribunal Hashem has a מעמד , a setting or tribunal, at which He repays every deed, good and bad. הלוא אם תיטיב שאת ואם לא תיטיב לפתח חטאת רבץ (Bereishit 4:7) If you improve, you will be uplifted; and if you do not improve, sin crouches at the door. The Targum Onkelos , followed by the sages, reads this as: if you do not improve, your sin is waiting for you at the entrance of the tribunal , the day of judgment. את הצדיק ואת הרשע ישפט האלהים כי עת לכל חפץ ועל כל המעשה שם (Kohelet 3:17): God will judge the righteous and the wicked, for there is a time for everything, and for every deed there . \"What does 'there' mean?\" There is a beit din to come. שם פחדו פחד (Tehillim 14:5): there they were very afraid. שם נפלו פעלי און (Tehillim 36:13): there the evildoers fell. And the verse about crying out there and not being answered. On that last idea the Rabbi said: \"You can cry now as much as you want. Hashem listens to our prayers now.\" But there, you see what you have done and the consequences of everything in your life. \"You're going to cry there, but there's no answer anymore. That's it. This is the time.\" Category 5: Hashem judges justly The Tanach stresses that Hashem judges with justice . If so, Saadia argues, there must be an Olam HaBa, because \"this world, whether we like it or not, at least to our eyes, doesn't always seem to be just.\" The real justice must come in the next. הצור תמים פעלו כי כל דרכיו משפט אל אמונה ואין עול צדיק וישר הוא (Devarim 32:4) The Rock, His work is perfect, for all His ways are just; a faithful God without iniquity, righteous and upright is He. This was from that week's parashah, Ha'azinu, read that morning. It too is said at a funeral: someone has died, it seems unfair, and we declare that His ways are just. There will be a place where justice is done. More verses: וצדיק באמונתו יחיה (Chavakuk 2:4); צדיק כתמר יפרח (Tehillim 92:13); והוא ישפט תבל בצדק (Tehillim 9:9); כי נכח עיני ה' דרכי איש (Mishlei 5:21); כי את כל מעשה האלהים יבא במשפט (Kohelet 12:14). A gematria for Rosh Hashanah The Rabbi shared a gematria he heard from Jeremy Benjamin a few years earlier and checked himself: למשפטיך עמדו היום כי הכל עבדיך (Tehillim 119:91) For Your judgments they stand today, for all are Your servants. The simple meaning is the future judgment. But the gematria of the verse equals ראש השנה , the day \"they stood today\" for judgment. \"Work it out afterwards.\" He added that he is \"not a huge fan of gematriot.\" As Pirkei Avot says, they are פרפראות לחכמה , the gravy for wisdom. \"When you have an idea, then you can add a gematria to add gravy to the meat. But you can't make an idea from a gematria. But here, it's so perfect.\" Category 6: A day of retribution Close to Category 4, but here the Tanach speaks of a set day for repayment. קרוב יום ה' הגדול קרוב ומהר מאד... יום עברה היום ההוא... והצרתי לאדם והלכו כעורים (Tzefaniah 1:14-17) The great day of Hashem is near, near and hastening... a day of wrath is that day... I will bring distress on people and they will walk like the blind. התקוששו וקושו... בטרם לדת חק (Tzefaniah 2:1-2) Gather yourselves together... before the decree is born. Category 7: \"Good\" Finally, the Tanach calls the reward of Olam HaBa טוב , good, and withholds it from the wicked. למען ייטב להם ולבניהם לעולם (Devarim 5:26): that it may be good for them and their children forever . מה רב טובך אשר צפנת ליראיך (Tehillim 31:20): how great is Your goodness that You have stored away for those who fear You. כי גם יודע אני אשר יהיה טוב ליראי האלהים אשר ייראו מלפניו. וטוב לא יהיה לרשע (Kohelet 8:12-13): I know it will be good for those who fear God... and it will not be good for the wicked. Saadia's conclusion: let reason decide the reading Someone might try to interpret all these verses differently and say they are not about Olam HaBa. Saadia replies: that is not so, because reason requires reward in another world , as Chapter 1 showed. And when an interpretation agrees with what reason testifies to be true, that is the interpretation you hold to. Summary of Chapter 3 # How the Tanach describes Olam HaBa 1 Life , with its opposite death 2 Good stored away for the righteous, bad for the wicked 3 Books in which all deeds are written 4 A tribunal where everything is judged 5 Hashem judges justly , which can only be fully true in Olam HaBa 6 A day of retribution 7 It is called good , and its opposite not good So Chapter 1 gave proofs from logic, and Chapters 2 and 3 from Scripture: Chapter 2 proofs, Chapter 3 the categories of how Scripture refers to Olam HaBa. Chapter 4: The Testimony of Tradition Saadia now turns to הקבלה , the tradition of Chazal. The material is \"too broad to write it all,\" so he gives the headlines: five proofs . Proof 1: The corridor and the palace Our sages received from the prophets that this world, compared to Olam HaBa, is like a corridor before a royal palace . A person must prepare himself as is fitting before entering the king's palace. העולם הזה דומה לפרוזדור בפני העולם הבא, התקן עצמך בפרוזדור כדי שתכנס לטרקלין (Avot 4:16) This world is like a corridor before the World to Come. Prepare yourself in the corridor so that you may enter the banquet hall. When you enter a wedding or a palace, you touch yourself up in the entrance so you look your best. If you did not prepare there, \"you're going to walk in, you're going to look terrible.\" So this world is the vestibule, and Olam HaBa is the real thing. Proof 2: One hour here, one hour there The sages also said that one hour of teshuvah in this world is worth more than all the life of Olam HaBa, in which there is no teshuvah. Whatever is written on the soul in this world cannot be erased once a person has died. And one hour of rest in Olam HaBa is better than all the life of this world. יפה שעה אחת בתשובה ומעשים טובים בעולם הזה מכל חיי העולם הבא, ויפה שעה אחת של קורת רוח בעולם הבא מכל חיי העולם הזה (Avot 4:17) Better one hour of teshuvah and good deeds in this world than all the life of the World to Come; and better one hour of tranquillity in the World to Come than all the life of this world. \"A lot of ink has been spilt on this Mishnah.\" The Rabbi noted that it is said by Rabbi Yaakov , the same Rabbi Yaakov who says in Kiddushin that שכר מצוה בהאי עלמא ליכא , there is no reward for mitzvot in this world. The obvious question: the first half says one hour here is better than all of Olam HaBa, and the second half says one hour there is better than all of this world. \"So which one is it? Make your mind up.\" The answer is that they speak about two different things. This world is for deeds; Olam HaBa is for reward and satisfaction. Deeds: one moment of good deeds here is worth more than all of Olam HaBa, because there you can no longer do good deeds. \"This is the end.\" So we must make the most of our deeds now. Reward: one moment of satisfaction in the next world is greater than everything in this one. \"Of course, we can't live with that perspective always, everything's about the next world. We are at the end of the day a this-world religion. We do care about this world.\" But we always need the bigger picture of eternity in front of us. Proof 3: No eating or drinking The sages taught that the life of Olam HaBa is like light , with nothing of this world in it, only the reward of the righteous in the glory of the Creator: העולם הבא אין בו לא אכילה ולא שתיה ולא פריה ורביה ולא משא ומתן... אלא צדיקים יושבין ועטרותיהם בראשיהם ונהנין מזיו השכינה (Berachot 17a) In the World to Come there is no eating, no drinking, no procreation, no business... only the righteous sitting with their crowns on their heads, enjoying the radiance of the Shechinah. Saadia promises to explain in the coming chapters what this pleasure is. \"Saadia is quite brave in trying to explain what Olam HaBa is,\" the Rabbi said. The Rambam, as the class had learned, backed off: I can only tell you it is unfathomable. Saadia tries quite hard to explain it. Proof 4: Those who have no share The sages held this belief so strongly that they taught that whoever denies Olam HaBa, or the giving of the Torah, or does not accept the tradition, has no share in Olam HaBa for all his good deeds. ואלו שאין להם חלק לעולם הבא: האומר אין תחיית המתים, ואין תורה מן השמים, ואפיקורס (Sanhedrin 10:1) These have no share in the World to Come: one who says there is no resurrection of the dead, one who says the Torah is not from Heaven, and the apikores. The apikores , the Rabbi explained, is one who disgraces the Chachamim and does not accept the rabbinic tradition. The Rabbi pointed out that this proof works for Saadia but not for the Rambam . For Saadia, Olam HaBa is body and soul together, so resurrection is the beginning of Olam HaBa, and denying techiyat hametim is denying Olam HaBa. For the Rambam, techiyat hametim belongs to this world, and Olam HaBa is the soul alone. The Rambam would of course agree that one must believe in Olam HaBa, but this particular proof depends on Saadia's view. The same Mishnah lists those who caused the many to sin: שלשה מלכים וארבעה הדיוטות אין להם חלק לעולם הבא (Sanhedrin 10:2) The three kings are Yerovam, Achav and Menasheh ; the four commoners are Bilam, Doeg, Achitofel and Gechazi . All of them led others astray. Either way, the sages clearly believed in Olam HaBa. Proof 5: The Targum Finally, the Targum , which the Rabbi dated to around the first or second century, \"so it's 2,000 years old,\" speaks of Olam HaBa. Two examples come from Vezot HaBerachah , the parashah coming up: On ויתא ראשי עם (Devarim 33:21), about Moshe, \"he came at the head of the people,\" Onkelos translates ועל ונפק בריש עמא , he will go out and come in at the head of the people. Moshe will be our leader even in Olam HaBa . On יחי ראובן ואל ימת (Devarim 33:6), \"may Reuven live and not die,\" Onkelos translates: יחי ראובן בחיי עלמא ומותא תנינא לא ימות , may Reuven live in eternal life and not die the second death . And in the Targum of the Prophets there is more than anyone could count. Summary of Chapter 4 This world is a corridor before Olam HaBa. One hour of satisfaction in Olam HaBa outweighs all of this world (and one hour of good deeds here outweighs all of the next). Olam HaBa has no eating or drinking , only the radiance of the Shechinah. The Mishnah lists those who have no share in Olam HaBa. The Targumim speak of Olam HaBa in many places. The End of Part One Saadia concludes: having explained the World to Come from reason (Chapter 1), Scripture (Chapters 2 and 3) and tradition (Chapter 4), we can say that we all agree on it, clearly and explicitly. That completes the first half of the essay. From Chapter 5 to the end, Saadia turns to the nature of Olam HaBa and asks many questions: What is the nature of reward and punishment? Where will it be? Will there be time? Will we be able to see each other? Will there still be service of Hashem? Will we be able to choose? He goes through them one by one over the coming chapters. The next Monday shiur would be in three weeks, after Sukkot and Shemini Atzeret, with no Tuesday shiur the next night because of Yom Kippur. The Rabbi reminded everyone that while Minchah is early the shiur starts earlier, returning to the usual time when the clocks change.","scAishruvDs":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 9 (Olam HaBa, The World to Come), Chapter 2 (continued), with the survey of the Rishonim in the Abarbanel Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 15 September 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are in Essay 9 Essay 9 is about עולם הבא , the World to Come. For Rav Saadia, Olam HaBa comes after Mashiach, after תחיית המתים, after the rebuilding of the Beit HaMikdash. After all of that, a new world is created. The details of that world (body and soul, what will be there, when it will be, time and so on) come in Chapters 5 to 11 , the end of the essay. For now Saadia is still establishing the concept itself : where do we get the idea that there is an afterlife at all? Chapter 1: six logical arguments that there must be an afterlife. Chapter 2: six proofs from the Torah . Yitzchak went up to the Akeidah even though, if there is no afterlife, what would he gain? Moshe Rabbeinu, Eliyahu and Elisha could perform miracles for others but could not do them for themselves. Then Saadia threw in the question that fills this whole shiur: Lovely, you've proved to me from logic, and you've proved to me from the Torah, that there is a concept of Olam HaBa. So why is there no explicit mention of it in the Torah? Saadia, the Rabbi stressed, was the first person to ask this question . He gives two answers: It can be known by reason. The afterlife can be deduced by pure logic, and the Torah does not need to spell out what reason already tells us. Reward and punishment in this world , however, is something the Torah does need to tell us. The Torah deals with the now. It speaks about the present and the foreseeable future. What comes after will come after. \"I don't know how convinced you are with these answers,\" the Rabbi said, but those are Saadia's. Since Saadia was the first to ask but \"certainly not the last,\" every century of Rishonim returned to the question. The Abarbanel , writing at the end of the 15th century, right at the end of the period of the Rishonim, collected seven answers from them. There are more, and the Rabbi added two later ones at the end. Last week covered the first two. Answer 1 (Recap): The Rambam, \"The Wrong Question\" The Rambam's answer, which the Rabbi had also discussed in his Tuesday Rambam shiur the week before, is that the question is a non-starter . The question assumes the Torah mentions reward in this world but not in the next. According to the Rambam, the Torah does not mention reward and punishment at all, neither in this world nor in the next. It does not want reward to be the motive for observing the Torah and the mitzvot. We are meant to do it לשמה . What about Ki Tavo, which had just been read that Shabbat, with its blessings and curses? The Rabbi noted how excited people get about it. They read the curses quietly and nobody wants to be called up. At a Shabbat Chatan he had just attended \"there was a whole tumult\" over who would be called up for which aliyah. For the Rambam, none of it is reward and punishment. The blessings are supports and the curses are impediments : When you do the will of Hashem, doing the truth because it is the truth, Hashem blesses you so that you can carry out that intention and pursue your goals. You will be blessed in your field and your house and you will not be ill, because \"if you have illness, how can you study? How can you devote yourself to developing your character, developing your intellect, developing your identity?\" If you want to go down the path of not treating the mitzvot as important, then Hashem will not even give you the means to care about them. בדרך שאדם רוצה לילך בה מוליכין אותו (Makkot 10b) In the way a person wants to go, he is led. \"You want to go on the path of developing yourself? I'll help you. I'll bless you, and you'll be able to focus. You don't want to go on that path? I'll put impediments in your path as well.\" Answer 2 (Recap): The Ibn Ezra, \"Too Deep for the Masses\" The Torah speaks about reward in this world, and only hints at the next, because Olam HaBa is too deep and too complex to explain to the masses. The Torah speaks about what is practical and tangible, what people can relate to. This is close to an idea the Rambam uses often (familiar to those in the Tuesday shiur): the Torah and Chazal sometimes say things for the masses , who need something concrete. But the Ibn Ezra differs from the Rambam on the main point. For the Ibn Ezra, reward and punishment in this world is real . It is simply not the main thing, and the afterlife is hinted at because the majority of people need that. Answer 3: The Ramban, \"Reason Already Tells You\" The Abarbanel brings the third answer in the name of the Ramban , and it is \"basically identical to the first answer of Saadia.\" The survival of the soul after death, and the spiritual reward and punishment that go with it, are demanded by human nature and by reason . You do not need religion to tell you that your actions have consequences. Philosophically, it has to be so. The Torah is not a book written to tell you about what follows naturally. What it promises are things above nature , which come from השגחה עליונה, Hashem's direct providence. The Abarbanel's example is the pasuk in Behar about Shemittah: וצויתי את ברכתי לכם בשנה הששית ועשת את התבואה לשלש השנים (Vayikra 25:21) I will command My blessing for you in the sixth year, and it will produce enough for three years. \"These are things which are beyond comprehension, beyond nature, that you're going to have produce of one year to last for three years.\" Similarly, the punishments for eating forbidden foods are consequences in this world, and these too are beyond the natural order. As the Ramban put it, the promises of the covenant are all דברים נסיים , miracles. Plant a field and normally you get one year of produce; supernaturally you get three. That is what the Torah needs to mention: the places where Hashem changes the course of this world. The reward of the soul in the next world follows from the nature of the soul and אינו בדרך פלא , it is not a wonder. It is obvious, so the Torah did not need to write it. The Abarbanel says the Ramban considered this the correct answer. Albo's objection Some commentators had a problem with this. Rabbi Yosef Albo , in the Sefer HaIkarim , says: I don't understand. The Torah mentions plenty of things that reason already knows. His example is בחירה , free will. Free will is a logical imperative, \"because if there's no free will, our whole Torah falls apart.\" The Rabbi said he always makes this point on Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur: if you don't believe you can change, there is no point to them. And yet the Torah emphasises it: ובחרת בחיים . So the Torah does mention things that can be deduced, and to say it left out Olam HaBa because it is obvious is, says Albo, a weak answer. The Rabbi added two more points: It is clearly not obvious. \"Nowadays not everyone believes in Olam HaBa.\" Even if it were obvious, why would the Torah then write nothing about it and focus only on this world? \"For me personally, I much prefer the first two answers so far, of the Rambam or of the Ibn Ezra. This I find difficult.\" But it is the answer of the Ramban and the first answer of Saadia, \"and therefore it has weight.\" Answer 4: The Kuzari, \"First Prove the Medicine Works\" The fourth answer comes from the Kuzari . It is not entirely clear who wrote the Kuzari, the Rabbi noted, but we assume it was Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi in the 12th century. The book is a dialogue between the king of the Khazars and the Rabbi, and the king asks this question. The Kuzari's parable is a doctor . For you to listen to a doctor, he first has to prove that he is effective. He gives you a small medicine, shows you it works, and only then gives you the real treatment you need. So too with Hashem. If the covenant in Bechukotai and Ki Tavo had been all about spiritual promises, Bnei Yisrael would not have believed them , because the principle of providence had not yet been proven to them. We were only just entering the covenant. \"I haven't seen any credible evidence of the consequences of my actions.\" So Hashem first promised them things they could perceive with their senses : that He runs the world and gives good to the good and bad to the bad, here and now. Then they would believe that everything is in His hand, כחומר ביד היוצר , like clay in the hand of the potter. The punishments and rewards that can be felt in this world become the clearest evidence of השגחה פרטית. The Rabbi's analogy: an investment \"Let me give you a much simpler analogy.\" Imagine trying to convince someone to invest with you. If you say, \"Invest with me and in fifty years you'll be a billionaire,\" he will say: maybe, maybe not, you haven't shown me anything. But if you say, \"In one month I'll make you 500 pounds,\" fine, let's do a deal. If that works, next you can talk about 1,000 and 10,000 pounds, and then about billions in fifty years' time. Hashem is making a deal between us and Him. At the start, the objectives have to be ones \"where you can meet them and I can see them now, in the next month or two.\" Why there were more miracles then This also answers a question people often ask: why were there so many more miracles in the days of the Torah? The Red Sea, the well, water from a rock. \"When was the last time anyone here in the last 1,000, 2,000 years saw a rock giving water?\" One approach is that then there was גילוי פנים and now there is הסתר פנים. But the simpler answer is the Kuzari's: that was the beginning . We had just become a nation, with centuries and millennia ahead of us. At the start of a covenant, Hashem puts things straightforwardly and openly. Connection to Saadia's second answer The Rabbi suggested this is really Saadia's second answer , that the Torah deals with the now. You can read them separately, but they tie together. Think of the people's position: \"We're entering Canaan, we've become a nation with these new laws. Starting fresh. We're not coming off a tradition of thousands of years.\" What they need to know is: will we have water? Will there be famine? The covenant can be verified through the now, not through an afterlife thousands of years away. Answer 5: The Abarbanel, \"Weaning Us Off Avodah Zarah\" The fifth answer is quite similar to the fourth. The Abarbanel attributes it to Saadia Gaon, but as the Rabbi pointed out, it is not in Saadia (\"maybe he had a different version\"), so the Rabbi called it the answer of the Abarbanel himself . The Rambam's principle The Rambam does not give this answer, since for him the question never begins. But it uses a principle the Rambam uses throughout the Moreh Nevuchim: many mitzvot are designed to wean Bnei Yisrael off avodah zarah . The Torah was given into a world of polytheism and paganism. Meat and milk. The Rishonim give many reasons. The Rambam's is very simple: it was a pagan practice. Peot and shaving with a razor. This is what the idolatrous priests did. The Rabbi said that well over a hundred mitzvot are connected by the Rambam to avodah zarah. Most famously, and the Rambam was \"heavily criticised for this,\" he held that the korbanot of Vayikra (olah, chatat, asham, minchah and all their details) are only בדיעבד . Ideally we go direct to Hashem; what is the point of bringing an animal and killing it? But people living in a culture of avodah zarah psychologically needed a physical form of worship. Without korbanot they would go and sacrifice to idols on private altars. So the Torah says: sacrifice, but only to the One God, in the Beit HaMikdash. Will there be korbanot in the future? \"That's a big discussion.\" The Rambam did codify the laws of korbanot for the future, but that is for another time. Applied to reward Idolatry offered quick, easy rewards for small investments . Pray to this idol and you'll get a son; to that one and you'll get rain; to another and you'll get money and health. The pagan priests promised physical results and held special services at special times to bring rain or increase produce, since they believed all of it was governed by the stars and the celestial bodies. When Hashem wanted to bring us back to pure faith, He gave us His Torah and forbade their forms of worship. He then had to tell us: don't think that by giving up those services you will lose the physical goods the idols promised you. The Abarbanel quotes the words of the women in Yirmiyahu: ומן אז חדלנו לקטר למלכת השמים... חסרנו כל (Yirmiyahu 44:18) Since we stopped offering incense to the Queen of Heaven, we have lacked everything. \"We don't want that to be.\" It is the opposite: when we distance ourselves from avodah zarah we come close to Hashem and have more success. But people were used to hearing that serving the idol brings reward and neglecting it brings punishment, so Hashem gave them an equivalent. \"It's like the korbanot.\" Really, the main thing is reward in the next world, and that is hinted at in the Torah. But while Hashem was making us into a people and taking us off avodah zarah, the people's worry was: if I stop serving, I'll lose my rain, my health, my livelihood. Hashem promises: no. Serve Me, and you will have them. Answer 6: The Kuzari Again, \"Body and Soul Together\" The sixth answer is also from the Kuzari. \"To be honest, I don't fully understand this answer,\" the Rabbi said, but he explained it to the best of his understanding. Of course the real reward is spiritual: the soul's delight in true intellect and in cleaving to Hashem. That is mentioned in the covenant, though briefly, in Bechukotai: ונתתי משכני בתוככם... והתהלכתי בתוככם והייתי לכם לאלהים (Vayikra 26:11-12) I will place My dwelling among you... I will walk among you and be your God. The Kuzari's point is that this closeness to Hashem comes together with the body as well. \"The body will follow the soul, and the soul follows the body.\" Already in this world a person can get a taste of the reward of the next. The Gemara's blessing עולמך תראה בחייך , \"may you see your world in your lifetime,\" means experiencing a little of the afterlife already here. The Gemara also says that Shabbat is one sixtieth of Olam HaBa. But for the body to enjoy that spiritual reward together with the soul, the body has to be healthy and prosperous. That is why the Torah promises physical blessings. \"There is a mention of the spiritual, but the main one is the physical, because the physical needs to be there to enjoy the spiritual, not just in the next world, but already in this world.\" The Rabbi added, \"I'm not convinced,\" and moved on. Answer 7: The Ikarim, \"A Covenant With a Nation\" The seventh and final answer in the Abarbanel comes from Rabbi Yosef Albo , \"the Spanish philosopher from the 1400s,\" in the Sefer HaIkarim. The Rabbi called it \"another very good answer.\" The covenant of Bechukotai and Ki Tavo is made with the community, the tzibbur, the nation as a whole . Its rewards and punishments are national , not individual. Rain, produce, victory over enemies: these cannot be given to one person and withheld from his neighbour. \"If there's rain, there's rain for you and me, we live next to each other. If we're going to beat our enemies, it's for you and me together.\" The covenant was with all of Am Yisrael, so it speaks about what belongs to the people as a whole. אמנם ייעודי הנפש אינם אלא לכל איש ואיש מישראל בפרטות But the promises of the soul are only for each individual in Israel, one by one. Spiritual reward in the afterlife is individual, \"tailor-made for each person,\" as the series has seen many times. A national covenant can only be expressed in physical terms. \"I think that's a very convincing answer.\" Two Later Answers The Ramchal: it cannot be described Rabbi Moshe Chaim Luzzatto gives an answer similar to the Ibn Ezra's but different, and the Rabbi thought the Rambam would probably agree with it. The Torah does not elaborate on Olam HaBa because it is not something that can be talked about . עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך (Yeshayahu 64:3) No eye has seen it, O God, except You. \"How can I give you a list of blessings and curses of what Olam HaBa is going to be, when the whole concept is something unfathomable for the human mind?\" A human being in a physical body cannot grasp it, as discussed at length in the previous Tuesday's Rambam shiur. So the Torah references it but does not expand on it. Rabbi Sacks: do not neglect this world The final answer is from Rabbi Sacks . The Rabbi was not sure whether it was his own, \"but I think there's beauty to it,\" and called it \"a very important outlook on life.\" Look through history at how the Jewish people have always tried to progress the world . Look at Israel today: its advances in science, technology and medicine, including a new cancer treatment from Israeli scientists announced a couple of weeks before. \"We are leading the world in many fields despite the tiny percentage of our population.\" That is because, although we believe that Olam HaBa is the ultimate, it cannot come at the expense of living the best life in this world . When we say ויכלו השמים והארץ וכל צבאם we become partners in מעשה בראשית. Hashem created this beautiful world, and we have a duty to take part in its creation and development with Him. If a religion places all its emphasis on the afterlife, people forget about this world. \"Let's be frank here\": the jihadi philosophy of \"I'll commit suicide, I'll be a suicide bomber, because I have an afterlife\" stems from a negation of this world . Jews and Muslims both believe in an afterlife, with very different understandings of what it is. But Judaism holds that this life is also important, \"perhaps as important.\" Had the Torah focused too much on the afterlife, we would neglect our role and purpose here. So Hashem told us: focus on this world, make the most of it, לתקן עולם במלכות שדי , bring Hashem into the physical world in which we live. Summary: Nine Answers # Source Why the Torah promises this world and only hints at the next 1 Rambam Wrong question. The Torah mentions no reward at all, only supports and impediments 2 Ibn Ezra Olam HaBa is too deep for the masses, who need something tangible 3 Ramban (= Saadia's first answer) Reward in the next world follows from reason and nature; only the supernatural rewards of this world need stating 4 Kuzari (close to Saadia's second answer) A new covenant needs promises that can be verified now 5 Abarbanel To wean the people off avodah zarah, which promised immediate physical rewards 6 Kuzari The body must flourish to share with the soul in the spiritual reward, even in this world 7 Ikarim (Rabbi Yosef Albo) The covenant is national, and national reward can only be physical; spiritual reward is individual 8 Ramchal Olam HaBa cannot be described: עין לא ראתה 9 Rabbi Sacks Too much focus on the next world would make us neglect this one Saadia gave two of the nine. \"Each one of you will have different preferences.\" The Rabbi's own: The Rambam's is the best , \"because the Rambam isn't giving an answer. The Rambam is saying there's no question at all. You misunderstood the whole concept of what the Torah is saying.\" There is also a lot of truth to the Ibn Ezra , that it is too deep, together with the Ramchal , that it cannot be described. And the other answers add the focus on this world, the national covenant and the present. Looking Ahead The Rabbi stopped early rather than start Chapter 3, which is \"one big topic\" that he wanted to do in one go. There would be no shiur the following Monday because of Rosh Hashanah ; the series would resume two weeks later, during the Aseret Yemei Teshuvah. Chapter 3: further proofs from the Torah, with many pesukim that each need investigating. Chapter 4: sources for Olam HaBa from Chazal and the tradition. Chapters 5 to 11: \"the meat\" of what Olam HaBa is. Saadia asks about thirteen questions on its nature: Will we be able to see each other there? What is the nature of the reward and of the punishment? Will there be time? Will there be mitzvot and aveirot? \"He's the first one to ask any of these questions.\"","EMoSRUCO7S8":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 9 (Olam HaBa, The World to Come), Chapter 2 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 8 September 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Where Essay 9 Begins Last week the series resumed with an overview of the first eight essays and an introduction to Essay 9, which is all about עולם הבא , the World to Come. In Rav Saadia's scheme, Olam HaBa comes after Mashiach, after techiyat hametim, after the Beit HaMikdash and that whole period. Then a new world begins, in which all the promises are fulfilled. Essays 7 and 8 dealt with techiyat hametim and Mashiach; now the focus is \"the real afterlife, the real world to come, the eternal one.\" Chapter 1 gave proofs from reason that there must be an afterlife, \"some quite compelling, some different\": Justice. How can HaKadosh Baruch Hu create a world with no justice? We see the wicked prospering and the righteous suffering. What of children who suffer? This world is full of suffering. A God who is entirely good would not create only this. \"Quite a pessimistic view of the world.\" Restlessness. People Saadia had met are never really at peace, because they know there is something else. The plan for tonight Chapter 2 moves to the second source: the pesukim . Where in Tanach do we find the concept of Olam HaBa? In the middle of this, Saadia digresses with a question: why is Olam HaBa only hinted at in the Torah, never mentioned explicitly? That question, with the answers of the Rishonim, would take up most of the shiur. Saadia promises thirteen proofs from Scripture: six in this chapter and seven in the next. Chapter 3 would wait for another week. Six Proofs From Tanach Proof 1: Those who gave up their lives Yitzchak. The Midrashim say that at first Yitzchak did not know what was going to happen at the Akeidah. But at some point he recognised he was to be sacrificed, and he gave himself up. Only at the last moment did the angel intervene. Saadia asks: if consequences and reward exist only in this world, what was Yitzchak expecting? \"You die, that's the end.\" And Hashem, who is just, would not have commanded it. Chananyah, Mishael and Azaryah threw themselves into the fiery furnace rather than worship the idol (Daniel 3). \"I'm throwing myself into the fire, I'm going to burn, I'm going to die. What am I going to get afterwards?\" They must have believed something awaited them. Daniel , caught praying in defiance of the king's decree, was thrown to the lions (Daniel 6). What was he expecting? All of these show that the righteous and the prophets agreed that the reward is in the next world . One could bring many stories from the Gemara, but Saadia finds his examples in Tanach itself. Why not Avraham? A participant asked why Saadia does not mention Avraham being thrown into the fire. Because, the Rabbi answered, that is not explicit in Tanach. The fire of Ur Kasdim, and Haran trying to copy Avraham, are lovely, but they are Midrashim. Saadia's examples are explicit stories in the Nevi'im. \"Saadia is the master, really the master of Tanach. Everything he manages to find a source from Tanach for.\" And, the Rabbi added, \"I'm sure what has stood out for all of us over the last two years is he loves numbers. Everything is always numbered. Six this, seven that, ten this.\" Richard's challenge: obedience alone Richard asked: could Yitzchak not simply have accepted that this was what was required of him? The Rabbi first answered from Saadia's side. Yitzchak was an adult. \"If my father was trying to kill me and I didn't believe in anything after my death, I would fight back.\" Avraham, by contrast, proves nothing: he could think that by obeying Hashem he would have a wonderful future in this world. But Yitzchak was going to die. Then he developed Richard's point. In the Tuesday Rambam shiur the week before, the class had focused on the highest level of service : you do the truth because it is the truth , you serve Hashem because that is the right thing and nothing else. Serving Hashem for reward is fine, a good service, \"it's not the perfect, not the ultimate. The ultimate is you do the will of Hashem because it is the will of Hashem.\" \"If you take that approach, I think Rav Saadia's proof completely falls away.\" Yitzchak could say: there is no future and no world to come, but Hashem told me to do it, it is the right thing, full stop. \"If you take the Rambam's view of reward and punishment, there's no proof here. The Rambam would never suggest a proof like this.\" Masada The argument has been used in history. Josephus records that Elazar , who led the defenders of Masada, told them exactly this in his speech before the end: the main reward is in the World to Come, so why should we be afraid of death? Saadia's Question: Why No Explicit Mention? Before the next proof, Saadia digresses. Look through the Torah and all you find is reward and punishment in this world . \"No further do you have to look than this week's parashah, Parashat Ki Tavo.\" On Shabbat, in shishi, it would be read quietly: \"two and a half pages of terrible things happen if you don't keep My mitzvot and half a page of wonderful things happen if you do.\" The Torah, Hashem and Moshe all seem to speak about this world. Why? Of course there are hints, says Saadia, but it is not explicit, for two reasons. The Rabbi read them first and promised to examine them later. Answer 1: Reason already knows it Things that reason requires the Torah does not dwell on at length, and the afterlife can be deduced by reason, as Chapter 1 showed. But things that are less logical, like the punishments of this world, the Torah must spell out. Saadia's example is the first commandment to Adam: מכל עץ הגן אכל תאכל. ומעץ הדעת טוב ורע לא תאכל ממנו (Bereishit 2:16-17) From every tree of the garden you may eat, but from the tree of knowledge of good and bad you shall not eat. Why did Hashem not command Adam not to murder, not to commit adultery, not to steal? Because reason teaches all these. But the tree of knowledge cannot be deduced, so it had to be commanded. So too reward in the World to Come: the Torah did not mention it because reason teaches it. \"I think some of us are probably thinking it's a pretty weak answer.\" The Rabbi raised two points: Saadia contradicts himself. In Essay 3 , on Torah and revelation, Saadia insisted that even rational mitzvot must be revealed. Don't murder: what happens if you do, and what is the framework? Don't commit adultery: what counts as adultery? Don't steal: what counts as belonging to someone? He went into great detail to show these must be taught. \"And yet here he's saying the logical things don't need to be taught.\" The Gemara behind it. Saadia clearly has in mind the Gemara in Sanhedrin , which derives the seven Noahide laws from the words of the command to Adam, ויצו ה' אלהים על האדם : \"and He commanded\" refers to courts; \"Hashem\" to cursing God; \"Elokim\" to idolatry; \"on the man\" to murder; and so on. So the laws are hinted at in the pasuk, but only the tree of knowledge is stated explicitly, because only it is not logical. That is exactly Saadia's point about Olam HaBa. Answer 2: The Torah speaks to the present The Torah and prophecy generally focus on what is needed now , and say less about what lies far ahead. When the Torah was given, \"we're becoming a nation, we're entering the land.\" The people needed to know what would happen there: do what I ask and you will have rain, produce and peace; if not, war. So the Torah elaborated on that and only hinted at the World to Come. \"That's something in the future, so worry about it when it comes.\" Back to the Proofs Proof 2: Moshe Rabbeinu \"One of the greatest proofs.\" Over and over the Torah promises rain in the land, produce, peace and all the beauty of living in Eretz Canaan. Moshe never received any of it ; he never entered the land. If the only reward is what is promised explicitly, Moshe, the greatest of all, should surely have received it. So the real reward must be in the World to Come. Proof 3: Eliyahu and Elisha Eliyahu blessed the widow of Tzarfat, telling her to fill her vessels, and she had flour and oil (Melachim I 17), yet he did not have such things for himself. Elisha was dead and buried, and when a dead man was thrown into his grave and touched his bones, the man came back to life (Melachim II 13:20-21). Elisha was dead and yet could give life to another. If everything were about this world, how could someone give another what he does not have himself? \"If I don't have money, how can I give money to someone else?\" But if Eliyahu and Elisha have a great reward in the World to Come, then they can give in this world what they cannot receive in it. Proof 4: Sedom The Torah tells us that Sedom did terrible things and was destroyed. That would mean every city that commits the same atrocities should be destroyed, \"take for example cities nowadays committing atrocities.\" It does not happen. So their retribution must come in the next world. You could ask in brackets: then why was Sedom punished in this world? \"You don't know Hashem's calculations. Sometimes this world, sometimes the other world. That's acceptable.\" Proof 5: Exile When Bnei Yisrael sinned, served other gods or behaved badly, Hashem exiled us. Other nations also serve idols and do terrible things, and they are not exiled. If everything were in this world, they should receive the same. So it must come in the World to Come. \"Again, you can refute this proof and say Bnei Yisrael are different, more was expected of us, but fine.\" Proof 6: Children Hashem commands terrible things, such as the killing of children. The class had read in Parashat Matot a couple of weeks earlier of the children of Midian killed by Bnei Yisrael; and there were the children who died in the Flood. \"We see nowadays lots of children suffering.\" How can that be fair? There must be compensation in the World to Come . The class had seen this argument in an earlier essay. The Rambam took issue with it and \"didn't like it at all,\" but Saadia holds that children who suffer or are killed while innocent receive recompense for it. Summary of the six proofs # Proof The point 1 Yitzchak, Chananyah, Mishael, Azaryah, Daniel Why give up your life if this world is all there is? (The Rambam would not accept this proof) 2 Moshe Rabbeinu The greatest of all never received the Torah's promises 3 Eliyahu and Elisha They gave others what they did not have themselves 4 Sedom Other wicked cities are not destroyed in this world 5 Exile Other idolatrous nations are not exiled 6 Children Innocent suffering must be compensated Chapter 1 gave six proofs from logic; Chapter 2 six proofs from characters and events in Tanach. Mark: Moshe's forty days Mark suggested a hint Saadia did not use: Moshe's forty days on the mountain without food. In Olam HaBa there is no physicality; we sit with Hashem and learn. Isn't that a taste of the World to Come? The Torah does say explicitly that Moshe ate no bread and drank no water. The Ramchal , for example, uses it as a model of what Olam HaBa is about: sitting with Hashem with no physicality. And Saadia himself will link it to Olam HaBa in a coming chapter. But the Torah does not say it was bliss or the ultimate reward. \"Remember, Moshe wasn't going up for reward.\" He went up to receive the Torah, and the second and third times it would have been better had he not needed to. \"You can connect the concept to that, but to prove there is such a concept from it I think is a bit difficult.\" Mark agreed it was not a proof. The Rishonim on Saadia's Question \"Saadia is the first one to ask this question. We've seen this over and over again: Saadia was the trailblazer in philosophical questions which were discussed by the Rishonim later on.\" Many Rishonim took up this question, and the Abarbanel , Don Yitzchak Abarbanel of 15th-century Spain, gathered their answers together. The Rabbi planned to go through the Abarbanel over this shiur and the next. The passage comes at the end of Bechukotai , the first set of blessings and curses, \"but it's actually perfect timing for this week,\" since Ki Tavo has the second set. It is a very long passage, and the Rabbi read selected parts. The Abarbanel writes that everyone asks why the rewards in the Torah are all physical, with no mention of anything after death. He has already brought many proofs from the Prophets for reward and punishment after death, but the question remains. He has gathered seven answers , naming the author of each. The class's suggestions Before reading, the Rabbi asked: based on the Tuesday Rambam shiurim, what would the Rambam answer? Flora suggested that if we knew for certain about the reward of the World to Come, we might do the mitzvot only to get the reward, and not with emunah and sincerity. \"That was excellent what you said,\" the Rabbi said. \"Half you answered.\" Another participant suggested that perhaps there is no reward in the World to Come at all, and we are just told so to make us behave. \"No, I'm sorry, I can't accept that.\" Belief in Olam HaBa is explicit in our Chachamim, mentioned throughout, and supported by the proofs from logic and Torah the class had seen. \"The question isn't whether we believe in it or not.\" We believe in it; the question is only why the Torah does not mention it explicitly. The Rabbi also wished Maria mazal tov on her son's wedding that week, and dedicated the shiur to the success of the couple. Answer 1: The Rambam, \"There Is No Reward in the Torah\" The Rambam says, as the class had learned about a year earlier: you have misunderstood. The good and bad things the Torah promises are not reward and punishment at all. The reward is ultimate bliss and connection with the Almighty, as a person develops their consciousness and spiritual identity by working on themselves and becoming the best version of themselves in this world. The blessings and curses are means: If you go on the path of wanting to serve HaKadosh Baruch Hu, Hashem says: \"I'll give you your money, I'll give you your produce on time, so you'll be able to focus and to pray and to study. I will take away your enemies; you won't have to fight.\" If you do not want that, Hashem puts impediments in your way, such as wars, illnesses and famine, and you are distanced even further from your perfection. The Rabbi gave a current example. \"Look at now. Two years we've been in Gaza.\" A good friend of his told him his beit midrash is almost empty, because most of the religious reservists are away fighting. \"We don't want that. That's a curse. We want to be able to sit in the beit midrash and to study, and we can't.\" That is what the Rambam means: if we were all doing the right thing, Hashem would give us the conditions to develop and focus. \"It's not reward and punishment. You missed the point.\" So why is reward mentioned neither in this world nor in the next? Because the Torah does not want it to be the focus . Do the truth because it is the truth; do it out of love because it is the right thing. \"We don't keep the mitzvot because of reward and because of punishment.\" This, the Abarbanel says, is the opinion of the Rambam in Sanhedrin and the Mishneh Torah. The Abarbanel has questions on it, which the Rabbi did not go into; \"the Abarbanel wasn't a fan of this view.\" But it is the first answer: you asked the wrong question. \"Clearly it's not Saadia's answer.\" Answer 2: The Ibn Ezra, \"Too Deep for Most People\" \"A bit of a patronising answer, but also makes a lot of sense,\" and it ties in with the previous week's Rambam shiur. Spiritual reward is a deep matter, hard to grasp . What is Olam HaBa? Eternal bliss, yes, connection with Hashem, devekut, as in the Ramchal and the Rambam. But for most people these are hard to understand, \"and even for us, we're still very limited in what that means.\" The Rabbi's analogy: you could spend all day trying to explain to someone blind from birth the difference between red, purple, green and yellow. \"Where am I going to start? I've never seen colour before.\" So too, \"stuck in our body in this physical world, how much can I tell you about what Olam HaBa is? We know very, very basic things.\" The Ibn Ezra continues: the Torah was not given to the wise alone , but to people of every age and both genders. Because most people's understanding is weak, the Torah gives them things that are tangible. The wise recognise the real thing from the hints; for them it is \"just an opening to the real thing, which is Olam HaBa.\" Albo's question Rabbi Yosef Albo asks: the Torah does ask us to move beyond physical thinking, even when it is hard. We are all physical, and yet we are commanded to negate physicality from Hashem . So why not tell us the World to Come is not physical either? The answer: one is negative and one is positive. \"I can command you: negate physicality from God. What exactly is God, I can't tell you, but I can tell you He is not physical. I can define God through negatives.\" To describe Olam HaBa positively , to say what it is, is impossible. \"It's something too spiritual.\" Summary and Looking Ahead Chapter 2: six proofs from figures and events in Tanach that there is an Olam HaBa. Saadia's question , asked for the first time: why is Olam HaBa not mentioned explicitly? His answers: reason already teaches it, and the Torah speaks to the needs of the present. The Rambam: there is no reward or punishment in the Torah at all, only supports and impediments; the Torah does not want reward to be the focus. The Ibn Ezra: Olam HaBa is too deep to explain to most people, so the Torah gives tangible promises and only hints at it for the wise. The third answer, the Ramban's , would come next week, with a closer look at Saadia's answers and the views of the Kuzari , Rabbi Yosef Albo , the Ramchal , the Maharal and contemporaries including Rabbi Sacks .","-frjXD4DFFo":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 9 (Reward and Punishment in the World to Come), Chapter 1 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 1 September 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Back After the Summer \"I hope you've had a refreshing August and a good break.\" The series began in January 2024, so it is now close to two years in. The plan is to finish the book by the end of this calendar year and begin a new sefer in January. This is the final philosophical essay ; Essay 10 will be \"something different.\" Where We Are: The Whole Book So Far Essay Subject 1 Creation : the world was created; it is not pre-existent or eternal. Saadia begins with the world before discussing God 2 What we mean when we speak of God : His unity, and the descriptions of Him in the Torah 3 Revelation : the Creator reveals His will and gives commandments; the kinds of mitzvot; the truth of the Torah 4 Free will : who says we can make those choices? Free will and God's knowledge 5 Mitzvot and sins : how a person is classified by a majority of merits or sins, and why those with more mitzvot do not always seem to have the best lives 6 The soul : its nature, its elements, its creation 7 Techiyat HaMetim 8 The redemption and Mashiach 9 Olam HaBa The book has three parts: God ; God's relationship with humans (mitzvot, revelation, the soul); and the end of times (resurrection, Mashiach and, finally, Olam HaBa). The key point leading into this essay: everything in Essays 7 and 8, resurrection and both redemptions, takes place in this world. Saadia held there are two redemptions, one through Mashiach for Am Yisrael, and one for all humanity. Olam HaBa is something completely different. What Essay 9 will cover Eleven chapters, among them: Chapter 1 (tonight): what Olam HaBa is, and proofs from reason that it must exist. Chapter 2: proofs from the verses , and the major question: if there are hints and sources for Olam HaBa, why is it never mentioned explicitly in the Torah , and why does the Torah's reward always seem to be physical, in this world, as in the Shema we say every day? There are seven approaches among the Rishonim, which the Abarbanel \"beautifully brings\" together. That will take a whole shiur. Chapters 3 and 4: rabbinic tradition about Olam HaBa. Later: Is there time in Olam HaBa? Is there service , even mitzvot , in Olam HaBa? \"Now you may be thinking to yourself, of course there isn't. You'll be surprised.\" And Saadia even dares to describe the nature of the reward , with seven levels of pleasure , something the Rambam refuses to discuss, and on which the Ramchal gives only a very limited understanding. Two Views of Olam HaBa Before the text, the Rabbi set out Saadia's view, \"which is the view of the majority of the Rishonim.\" Saadia (with the Ramban, the Ramchal and many Rishonim) Olam HaBa is a new world. It does not exist now. It is called \"the world to come\" because it will be created in the future . All the wonderful things discussed over the past months, the redemption, resurrection, Mashiach, Eliyahu, the Beit HaMikdash, \"they're all happening in olam hazeh .\" Saadia takes literally the Gemara that this world lasts 6,000 years . \"We're about to begin 5786. So even if Mashiach comes today, we've got maximum 214 years of enjoying all of these things.\" \"We're running out of time. That's the reality.\" It is enjoyed by body and soul together. The body took part in the mitzvot, in building a better identity in this world, so it is only right and just that it share in the reward. As Essay 7 explained, those resurrected in the Messianic era are not resurrected again; they are transported over into the new world. The Rambam Olam HaBa already exists. It is called \"the world to come\" not because it has not yet been created, but because it comes after this world for each person. \"It has been created already, but we haven't reached that yet.\" There is no body in it. For the Rambam, a bodily Olam HaBa is a contradiction in terms: its whole essence is something the body cannot enjoy. Reward in three places Saadia also said, in Essay 5, that there is some reward and punishment in this world (though some Gemaras say there is none, or only a little), and, in Essays 7 and 8, that one's share in the redemption and resurrection will be commensurate with one's level , so there is consequence in the Messianic era too. But the major, most perpetual reward is Olam HaBa , and that is the subject now. Question: who merits it? Richard asked whether Olam HaBa is reserved for the most righteous, or available to all except those who have done something bad. Saadia never discusses numbers, \"but I don't need Saadia, because the Mishnah tells you\": כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא , except very specific categories. The presumption is that the vast majority of Jews will be there. The Ramchal writes explicitly in Derech Hashem and Da'at Tevunot that only מעט מזער , a tiny few, will have no portion. People know the line from Shabbat morning, where it is quoted with ועמך כלם צדיקים and then cut off, so they think every Jew has a portion. \"But you haven't seen the next part, which says: apart from.\" Still, those excluded are the minority. Gemul , Not \"Reward\" The essay is titled בגמול ועונש , usually translated \"reward and punishment.\" The Rabbi does not like the word \"reward,\" because it suggests you do something and get paid: \"A child does something, you pay the child five pounds.\" What is the root of גמול ? A participant answered: to bestow. And that, said the Rabbi, comes from גמל , a camel. We say in the Amidah גומל חסדים טובים . \"Is there chasadim ra'im ? There is bad kindness\": kindness with no limit, which keeps the other person dependent. The camel is \"the quintessential animal which is able to live off itself for months. It drinks, and then that's it.\" The independent animal. True kindness, chasadim tovim , lets the other person build their own identity and live their own life. So gemul is not \"I did X, therefore I get Y.\" \"This is just who I am. This is the consequence of the kind of person I've created for myself.\" Onesh , by contrast, is punishment, and Saadia will ask a striking question about it later: why is it not enough for Hashem to give Olam HaBa to those who merit it? Why must He punish those who do not? Chapter 1: The Day of Distinction הודיענו אלהינו יתברך ויתעלה כי כבר קבע זמן לגמול הצדיקים, ובו יבדיל בין הצדיקים ובין הכופרים Hashem has fixed a time to reward the righteous, when He will distinguish between them and the deniers: והיו לי אמר ה׳ צבאות ליום אשר אני עשה סגלה... ושבתם וראיתם בין צדיק לרשע, בין עבד אלהים לאשר לא עבדו (Malachi 3:17-18) Saadia understands that day as Olam HaBa . The prophets confirmed it with signs and wonders, and he will show that it is necessary from reason, from Scripture and from tradition . Where is Gan Eden? The Rabbi noted that Saadia never uses the term גן עדן anywhere in the book. He speaks of resurrection, redemption and Olam HaBa, but not Gan Eden. Where does the concept come from? Is it one concept or two? The Rambam, following the Gemara, seems to treat gan and eden as two things. That discussion belongs to the new Tuesday series on the Rambam's introduction to Perek Chelek, starting the next evening. The human at the centre כי השמים והארץ ומה שביניהם לא נבראו כולם כי אם בעבור האדם Saadia recalls Essays 4 and 6: everything was created for the human being , which is why he is placed in the middle with the worlds surrounding him, like the important part of an egg in its centre. \"Obviously we know nowadays that isn't correct, at least in terms of the astronomy.\" The Ibn Ezra and the Rambam disagreed with him, \"and they were proven right, at least astronomically.\" That is why the human was given a superior soul, with intellect and wisdom, and was commanded with mitzvot and prohibitions, as a preparation for eternal life . That life will begin when Hashem has finished creating all the people He wishes to create. Then He will settle us in another world and reward us there. Seven Proofs from Reason 1. This world is too mixed to be the goal שכל טובה שיש בעולם יש עמה רעה, ועם כל הצלחה עמל, ועם כל הנאה צער, ועם כל שמחה יגון Hashem, in His wisdom, justice and goodness to His creatures, would not make this world the final reward of the soul. In this world: every good comes with some bad; every success comes with toil; every pleasure comes with pain; every joy comes with sorrow. At best it is equal, and really the world tilts more towards difficulty and sadness than happiness. So it would be absurd for a wise Creator to make the soul's ultimate benefit its days of toil and fear. There must be another place , with complete life and true happiness. \"This is a very pessimistic view of the world,\" the Rabbi said, but the idea was echoed by many later. The Ramchal opens Mesillat Yesharim with it, and develops it in Derech Hashem: every good in this world is coupled with something negative, so this world cannot be the end product. If we die at 120 and that is the end for eternity, \"God hasn't done a good job.\" \"You know, just today, 800 people died in an earthquake.\" Everyone works hard and has troubles. \"There must be something more.\" The Rambam disagrees with the premise The Rambam does not necessarily reject this as a proof of Olam HaBa. What he rejects is the claim that the world has more evil than good . In the Moreh (3:12) he writes that people, even the wise, often think so, and he singles out al-Razi , who wrote a well-known book which, \"amongst other mad and foolish things,\" argued that human existence is a punishment. (The Rabbi observed that if you really believed that, taken to its logical conclusion, \"what's the purpose of living?\") The Rambam's answer: The error is judging the universe by one person. An ignorant person thinks the universe exists for him, so when something goes wrong he concludes the whole universe is evil. Look at the whole, the spheres, the stars, the elements, minerals, plants, all species, and humanity is an infinitesimal part of it. Most of the evils we suffer we bring on ourselves , by our own free will, and then blame God, who is far from them. The man who weakens his sight by excess and goes blind; the man who falls ill from bad food. \"Now you may say, there are natural tragedies which have got nothing to do with the human. That's a question, that's discussed.\" But that is the Rambam's view. 2. No soul is ever at rest כי אני מוצא כל נפשות בני אדם אינן נחות בעולם הזה ולא בוטחות, אפילו מגיע לגדולת המלכות ולרחבת הממון Saadia speaks from experience: the people he has met are never truly settled or secure, even those who reach kingship and great wealth . No one says, \"I've reached utopia.\" This is because the soul knows there is a more honoured place and longs for it. If not for that, it would be calm and tranquil. The Rabbi asked the class for their own views: \"Very much what he said so far has been quite subjective.\" 3. We condemn what we desire Human nature desires things that the human intellect condemns: promiscuity, theft, arrogance, revenge and the like. When a person controls himself and holds back, it costs him: he is pained, upset, frustrated. Why bear all that? Only because he expects good at the end. The Rabbi drew this out: almost everyone, even many who do these things, recognises that they are objectively wrong. \"Where does this wrong feeling come from? Only, says Saadia, because I know there's Olam HaBa.\" The Rambam's ideal is different He recalled the Shemonah Perakim , the Rambam's introduction to Avot, studied over a year earlier. The Rambam distinguishes: Rational mitzvot , which we would know anyway (theft, honesty, honouring parents): here the ideal is to reach the point where you no longer desire to do wrong at all . At the start you may need yirat Shamayim , \"Hashem said I can't,\" but ultimately the temptation itself should go. Revealed mitzvot , which we would not know without Hashem telling us, such as kashrut: here it is fine, even commendable, to say, \"I wish I could eat non-kosher, but Hashem said I can't.\" Saadia seems to assume a lifelong battle with temptation. If the Rambam's ideal is reached, \"this whole proof would fall apart,\" unless Saadia is speaking of most people for most of their lives rather than the ultimate state. 4. We praise what costs us The converse: the intellect approves of justice and uprightness , of doing good and of warning others away from bad, even though people get angry when you correct them, and it can bring insults, even blows. \"If someone genuinely takes someone aside and cares for them and tells them to improve their behaviour, sometimes it can rebound.\" Why would anyone do it, and why do we know deep down that it is right, unless there is a great reward to come ? 5. Wrongs between people go unrighted We see people wrong one another, and then both die, with no justice. Since Hashem is a true judge, there must be another place where the one who suffered is compensated and the one who caused pain pays for the pleasure he took. This is about personal interactions between people, not yet the general question of the righteous and the wicked. 6. The righteous suffer and the wicked prosper כי אנחנו רואים כופרים בנעימים בעולם הזה ומאמינים בצער בו, ואי אפשר שאין להם עולם אחר \"This is one of the greatest questions against religion.\" Saadia turns it into a proof : if the righteous suffer and the wicked thrive here, there must be another world where each receives his due. 7. One death for one murder or ten Under capital punishment, a person who killed once before witnesses is put to death once. A person who killed ten times, or committed adultery twenty times, and was only caught the last time, is also put to death once. \"Where's the justice in that?\" There must be another place where it is played out properly. Then Why Not Start in Olam HaBa? ואם יאמר אומר: ולמה לא היה טוב שיברא הבורא את האדם הזה בעולם האחר מתחלה, ויניח לו כל הצער? If reason proves Olam HaBa, why not take us straight there and spare us the troubles of this world? Saadia answers in a single line: as explained in Essay 3. The Rabbi asked who remembered the answer. A participant gave it: a person needs to earn it himself. Earned reward is greater. That, said the Rabbi, brings us back to the word גמול : the camel, independence. \"You've earned it, you've chosen it, it's yours, as opposed to just being thrust upon you and given to you without doing anything.\" Summary Structure: Essays 1-2 on God, 3-6 on God and humanity, 7-9 on the end of times. Resurrection and redemption happen in this world ; Olam HaBa is something else. Saadia (with most Rishonim, the Ramban, the Ramchal): Olam HaBa is a new world , created after 6,000 years, for body and soul . The Rambam: it exists now , after death, for the soul alone . Who merits it: kol Yisrael , apart from specific exclusions; per the Ramchal, only a tiny few miss out. גמול is not payment but the consequence of who you have made yourself, like the self-sufficient camel . Seven proofs from reason: this world is too mixed to be the goal; no soul is ever at rest; we condemn what we desire; we praise what costs us; wrongs between people go unrighted; the righteous suffer and the wicked prosper; one death for one crime or for ten. The Rambam rejects the premise that evil outweighs good in the world (Moreh 3:12), and his ideal of overcoming desire for moral wrongs (Shemonah Perakim) would weaken the third proof. Why not begin in Olam HaBa? Because it must be earned (Essay 3). Coming Up Next week, at the usual time of 6 o'clock: Chapter 2 , the proofs from the verses, and why the Torah does not mention Olam HaBa explicitly. The Rambam series on Perek Chelek begins the following evening.","hZA9n0I8VLg":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 8 (The Final Redemption), Chapters 8 and 9 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 21 July 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. The Last Shiur of the Term The final class of the summer term, completing Essay 8. After the break, from early September in Elul, comes Essay 9 on Olam HaBa , which should take a couple of months, and then Essay 10 , which is part of the book but \"a whole different kind of approach. It's less philosophical, it's more just musar, ethical encouragement,\" after Sukkot. The aim is to finish the whole sefer by the end of 2025. Recap: Essay 8 So Far Chapters 1 to 4: the purpose of the redemption, to save us from exile, bring us together into Eretz Yisrael and be Hashem's chosen people; and two times for it: the fixed קץ , or any time we merit it by improving our ways. Saadia worked out what he believed was the ketz , but \"he wasn't precise with that.\" He predicted 963, \"so we're already a thousand years over his prediction.\" Chapters 5 and 6: the redemption comes through a person, Mashiach . It will certainly be Mashiach ben David , perhaps preceded by Mashiach ben Yosef if we have to wait for the ketz . Ben Yosef gathers some of the people, has early success, and is killed; many give up hope; there is the war of Gog uMagog ; then ben David. If we return of our own accord, none of that is needed. Saadia gathered the prophecies into a timeline. \"Whether it's going to happen exactly as he predicts, we'll wait to see. As the Rambam said, when it happens we'll see exactly how it happens.\" But almost everyone agrees there will be a redemption, through a person called Mashiach. Chapters 7 to 9: refutations of those who say the redemption is not coming, not because the prophecies are false, but for two reasons: The prophecies were conditional , and we sinned and lost them. Chapter 7 gave five refutations : unlike Moshe's promises in the Torah, the consolations in Yeshayahu, Yirmiyahu and Yechezkel carry no conditions. They were already fulfilled in the Second Temple period. That is tonight's Chapter 8 . Chapter 9: the Christian claim that Mashiach already came, in Jesus. Chapter 8: Fifteen Proofs וכיון שחקרתי יסוד בנינם, אשיבה עליהם אחר כן בחמשה עשר שער: חמשה מהם מן הכתוב, וחמשה מן ההגדה, וחמשה ממה שישיגהו החוש Saadia answers the claim that the prophecies were fulfilled in the Second Temple with fifteen proofs : five from the verses themselves, compared with what Scripture records of the Second Temple; five from tradition and history : things we have never been told happened; five from what we can see with our own senses. \"This is very timely,\" the Rabbi noted: some of these prophecies are in the Shiva d'Nechemta , the seven haftarot of consolation after Tishah b'Av. \"And Saadia takes a very literal approach to many of these things.\" Five from the Verses 1. Every Jew will be gathered in וקבצתים מן הארצות, והביאותים אל אדמתם (Yechezkel 34:13) Saadia reads the prophecy as all of Israel, with no one left in exile. But in the Second Temple period only 42,360 returned: כל הקהל כאחד ארבע רבוא אלפים שלש מאות וששים (Nechemiah 7:66). \"Okay, that's not decisive,\" the Rabbi said, because \"I will gather them\" might mean most of them. But it is a fact that not everyone came back: there are communities that trace themselves to the First Temple exile, who stayed on in Iraq, Persia and Iran. For Saadia, the final redemption means everyone. \"No one's going to be forgotten about.\" 2. From the islands Yeshayahu says we will be gathered from the islands of the sea . The first exile was only to Bavel and Persia, \"Iraq and Iran,\" never to islands, so the return from it cannot be this prophecy. \"Whereas now, of course, at least in England, we're living on an island, and many Jews across the world are living on islands.\" 3. The nations will build the walls ובנו בני נכר חמתיך (Yeshayahu 60:10) In the Second Temple period, far from helping, the nations stopped us building , for a pause of about eighteen or nineteen years, and the walls were built under threat: באחת ידו עשה במלאכה, ואחת מחזקת השלח (Nechemiah 4:11) One hand on the bricks, the other on a weapon. Again, \"not a decisive proof,\" since Koresh did help, and the prophecy might mean that some nations would lend their authority to the building. 4. The gates always open ופתחו שעריך תמיד, יומם ולילה לא יסגרו (Yeshayahu 60:11) Yerushalayim will be so secure that its gates never close. But in the Second Temple period they closed the gates before sunset and opened them only late in the day: לא יפתחו שערי ירושלם עד חם השמש (Nechemiah 7:3). So that prophecy must be about the third Temple. 5. All nations will serve us כי הגוי והממלכה אשר לא יעבדוך יאבדו (Yeshayahu 60:12) In the Second Temple period we ourselves were under foreign rule: הנה אנחנו היום עבדים, והארץ אשר נתתה לאבתינו (Nechemiah 9:36). \"Although many great things happened in the time of the Second Temple, these five things didn't happen.\" If they have not happened, they must still happen. Five from Tradition and History \"We have never been told these things happened.\" Saadia again reads them literally. 6. Seven years of fuel from Gog's weapons ויצאו ישבי ערי ישראל ובערו והשיקו בנשק ומגן וצנה, בקשת ובחצים ובמקל יד וברמח, ובערו בהם אש שבע שנים (Yechezkel 39:9) After the war of Gog, the leftover weapons will serve as fuel for seven years. We are never told this happened. \"Are we going to use the weapons for fuel for seven years? We'll see, I suppose. Perhaps we've moved on, we don't need these things anymore.\" 7. The rivers split The river of Egypt will be dried up in one place and the Perat , the Euphrates, split into seven streams: והחרים ה׳ את לשון ים מצרים... והכהו לשבעה נחלים (Yeshayahu 11:15). It never happened. 8. The Mount of Olives split ונבקע הר הזיתים מחציו מזרחה וימה גיא גדולה מאד (Zechariah 14:4) The Mount of Olives will split into a northern half and a southern half with a great valley between. We do not know that this ever happened. 9. The Temple built to Yechezkel's plan Yechezkel gives the form of the House from beginning to end. The Second Temple was not built according to those instructions , so his prophecy must be about the third. 10. The river from the Temple והנה מים יצאים מתחת מפתן הבית (Yechezkel 47:1) A great river will flow out from the Mikdash, with fruit trees on both banks whose fruit never fails, for food and for healing : ועל הנחל יעלה על שפתו מזה ומזה כל עץ מאכל... והיה פריו למאכל ועלהו לתרופה (Yechezkel 47:12) \"It's very easy to explain away many of these prophecies as being not literal, but metaphoric. But Saadia takes them all very literally.\" Five We Can See for Ourselves \"These are very strong proofs.\" 11. All humanity will know God is One ביום ההוא יהיה ה׳ אחד ושמו אחד (Zechariah 14:9) \"We say this every day in Aleinu.\" Yet we still see people who deny God and do not recognise His unity. 12. No more taxes to other nations נשבע ה׳ בימינו ובזרוע עזו, אם אתן את דגנך עוד מאכל לאיביך (Yeshayahu 62:8) Hashem swore that we would never again give our produce to others. Yet Jews everywhere still pay taxes to the countries they live in. 13. No more war וכתתו חרבותם לאתים... לא ישא גוי אל גוי חרב (Yeshayahu 2:4) \"This is a famous prophecy.\" Yet \"we have nuclear weapons now. Every nation is trying to develop the best weapons.\" 14. Wolf and lamb וגר זאב עם כבש (Yeshayahu 11:6) The wolf will graze with the lamb, the lion will eat straw like cattle, and a nursing child will play by a snake's hole. But the lion still devours, the wolf still devours and the snake still bites. This is the one prophecy Saadia allows might be read metaphorically : perhaps it only means that bad people and good people will no longer harm each other. Even so, it has not happened. There is no less violence, robbery and oppression of the weak today than before. \"Either way you look at this prophecy, it hasn't happened, so it must happen in the future.\" 15. Sedom restored ושבתי את שביתהן, את שבות סדם ובנותיה... תשבן לקדמתן (Yechezkel 16:53, 55) Sedom will return to its original state. What was that? Before the destruction, its waters were sweet: וישא לוט את עיניו וירא את כל ככר הירדן כי כלה משקה... כגן ה׳ כארץ מצרים (Bereishit 13:10) That is why Lot chose it when he parted from Avraham. Today, \"when you're driving down from Yerushalayim past the Yam HaMelach,\" it is all dry and salty. Saadia's conclusion ואלה הענינים כולם מורים ראיה גמורה על שהנחמות אלו לא היו עדיין All fifteen together are a complete proof that these consolations have not yet come . The prophets promised them, so they must still come, in the final redemption. Anyone who says the redemption will not come because it already happened must explain these fifteen promises. \"It cannot be Mashiach has come already.\" The Rambam, the Rabbi added, did not go into all of this. \"The Rambam just said, wait and see.\" Saadia takes the words of the prophets very literally and awaits them. A question: was Saadia also answering the Christians? A participant suggested that Saadia, living among people who strongly believed their Mashiach had already come, was helping Jews to deal with that. \"That's precisely correct.\" Chapter 7 began with \"people who call themselves Jews,\" most likely the Karaites. But the very first sentence of Chapter 9 says these same fifteen proofs answer the Christians too. Chapter 9: Against the Christian Claim וכל אשר השיבונו על אלה, היא תשובת הנוצרים None of the fifteen prophecies came true in the time of Jesus either, so he cannot have been Mashiach. A puzzle about the dates Saadia reports that the Christians placed Jesus 138 years before the destruction of the Second Temple (some texts have a slightly different number). With the destruction in 70 CE, that would put him around 68 BCE, which does not match the usual dating. One suggestion is that in Saadia's time some held he was born then and that the count of the era was from his crucifixion. \"It does seem there was an opinion held at the time\" of a date sixty or seventy years earlier than we understand today. The Rabbi said he had not researched what the Christian view was a thousand years ago. The proof from the seventy weeks The Christians also bring a specific proof from Daniel 9 , the \"seventy weeks.\" Everyone, including us, agrees these are weeks of years : 70 times 7, 490 years . At the end, the verse says: ואחרי השבעים ששים ושנים יכרת משיח ואין לו (Daniel 9:26) \"Mashiach will be cut off.\" They read this as Jesus being crucified: so Mashiach came, was killed, and it is all over. Saadia's reading of the 490 years: from the exile to the building of the Second Temple and through its existence. Of them, 434 were the time of the House, which stood 420 years, since there was an interruption early in the building ( ושבעים ששים ושנים תשוב ונבנתה רחוב וחרוץ ובצוק העתים ), as recorded at the start of Ezra. The last \"week\" is a time of war, trouble and broken covenant. The 490 years combine good and bad , as someone might say, \"I spent fifty days at a wedding, in sickness and in drunkenness\": a single total covering different kinds of time. \"Cut off\" is not Jesus: four answers \"Mashiach\" does not mean a specific person. \"People think Mashiach is a specific kind of person. His name is Mr Mashiach.\" It simply means anointed , from the root משח , as in הכהן המשיח in Shemot, Vayikra and Tzav. The verse means every anointed Kohen will be cut off : with the destruction, the line of Kohanim Gedolim came to an end. The word yikaret is used only for a just death. כל אכל חלב... ונכרתה (Vayikra 7:25): karet is the punishment for one who deserves it. But according to the Christians, Jesus was killed unjustly . So the verse would not use that word of him. It is tied to the destruction of the Temple. The cutting off happens together with the destruction. Even by today's dates, Jesus died around 30 or 33 CE, decades before 70. The time span does not work. From Daniel's vision to Jesus's death was only about 245 years, not 490. So, says Saadia, they invented a longer Persian period, claiming seventeen Persian kings over hundreds of years. He answers from Daniel that there were only four . \"Honestly, I struggled to understand it completely.\" Beyond this proof Saadia closes by noting that, aside from these answers, Christianity holds that the Torah has been abrogated , against our belief that it never changes (Essay 3), and holds the Trinity , against the unity of God (Essay 2), and other things he will not bring in this book. Summary Chapter 7 (last week): the consolations are unconditional . Chapter 8: the consolations were not fulfilled in the Second Temple period, by fifteen proofs: From the verses: all Jews gathered in; from the islands; nations building the walls; gates always open; nations serving us. From history: seven years of fuel from Gog's weapons; the rivers split; the Mount of Olives split; the Temple to Yechezkel's plan; the river and healing trees from the Temple. From what we see: all humanity knowing God is One; no taxes to other nations; no more war; wolf and lamb (literal or metaphorical, neither has happened); Sedom restored. Chapter 9: the same proofs answer the Christians. \"יכרת משיח\" means every anointed Kohen, not one man; karet means a just death; it belongs with the destruction of the Temple; and the 490 years do not fit. Essay 8 as a whole: the purpose, timing and miracles of the redemption, and the refutation of those who say it already happened. Coming Up A break until Monday 1 September , when Essay 9, on Olam HaBa , begins: in Saadia's framework, \"a whole new world,\" whereas resurrection and redemption happen in this world. Essay 9 in September and October, Essay 10 in November and December. \"This whole book we began in January a year and a half ago.\" Over the holidays, the Rabbi suggested going back over what has been learned: the unity of God, prophecy, the soul, resurrection, and now Mashiach.","k_OWETdL09I":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 8 (The Final Redemption), the second half of Chapter 6 and Chapter 7 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 14 July 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Story So Far \"We're coming towards the end of Saadia's essay on Mashiach,\" perhaps the penultimate shiur on it. Chapters 1 and 2: the purpose of the redemption is to gather us in the land and make us God's chosen people there, with hope and encouragement that it will come. Chapters 3 and 4: following the Gemara, the redemption comes either at the fixed time, the קץ , or earlier through teshuvah . Saadia calculated what he believed was the ketz , and the class discussed the whole idea of calculating the end. Chapter 5 and half of Chapter 6: the word Mashiach finally appears. If we do teshuvah, the process is less painful and there is one Mashiach, Mashiach ben David . If we wait for the ketz without deserving it, we will be almost compelled to do teshuvah by our circumstances, through the process of Mashiach ben Yosef , who gathers followers and is killed by a Christian emperor. The Gemara already speaks of Mashiach ben Yosef and of his death; Saadia filled in the description from the verses. Either way, Mashiach ben David comes. The nations become subservient and carry the Jews home by every means of transport. There is a war with Gog uMagog , and we are victorious. The main point last week: it is a process , perhaps months, perhaps years. At its end, everyone comes back to Eretz Yisrael from the four corners of the globe. \"This is very apt for the Three Weeks. It's a beautiful time to be studying this, as we prepare for Tishah b'Av and for what we're missing out on.\" Chapter 6 (continued): Five Stages After the Ingathering 1. The ingathering of the believers וכאשר יתקבצו החיים מישראל המאמינים כאשר זכרתים... The Rabbi pointed out a striking word: the believers . \"There seems to be still, even at this point, a choice to make.\" A Jew living somewhere in the world who sees what is happening and decides, \"I'm not up for this,\" will not go. \"You can't just sit back and say, I'll wait for Mashiach to take me on eagle's wings and end up in Israel. We need to make a choice that this is the time now to go to Israel.\" Saadia says it will be the vast majority. 2. The resurrection תהיה אז תחיית המתים, כאשר בארתי במאמר הקודם לזה Once the process is nearly complete and most of Am Yisrael is in Israel, the resurrection happens, as explained in Essay 7. Mashiach ben Yosef will be at their head , among the very first to rise, \"for he is a righteous man and a ruler,\" and the Creator will reward him with good. We who are alive will be reunited with the relatives who died before us. 3. The Beit HaMikdash אז יחדש הבורא בנין מקדשו, כמו שנאמר כי בנה ה׳ ציון נראה בכבודו \"We say this verse many times on Tishah b'Av.\" It will be built with all the measurements and chambers described by Yechezkel , in the prophecy that begins בעשרים וחמש שנה לגלותנו . Will it be built by God or by man? There is a dispute among the Rishonim. Rashi holds it will be entirely God-built; other Rishonim say it will be built by people. Saadia does not express a view. \"You could say maybe it implies more that it's going to be Hashem, but I don't think there's a proof either way.\" It will be made of precious stones: ושמתי כדכד שמשתיך, ושעריך לאבני אקדח (Yeshayahu 54:12) And the whole land will be settled, with no ruined place left: והיה השרב לאגם, וצמאון למבועי מים (Yeshayahu 35:7) People ask how the whole Jewish people will fit. Israel holds about ten million people in total today; worldwide there are perhaps another six million Jews. \"It's not difficult if you think about the south, how much space there is.\" It is empty now because it is desert and hard to live in. \"When we all gather there and we need the space,\" it will be irrigated and there will be room for everyone. 4. The light of the Shechinah אז יאיר אור השכינה וזורח בבית המקדש, עד שיהיו המאורות נגדו כהים The Shechinah will shine in the Mikdash so brightly that all other lights are dim beside it. As Saadia explained in Essay 2, that created light is brighter than any other. קומי אורי כי בא אורך (Yeshayahu 60:1). It will be so palpable that someone who does not know the way to the Mikdash will simply follow the light, \"because it stretches from heaven to earth.\" \"You won't need signposts telling you this is the way to the Kotel.\" The verse is והלכו גוים לאורך (Yeshayahu 60:3): the nations will walk by your light. \"I don't know if he means it literally.\" The Rabbi noted that the printed text has a whole paragraph missing at this point, with the verse for the next stage displaced into it. 5. Prophecy In the missing paragraph, Saadia says prophecy will rest on the people , even the young. It will be so widespread that the nations will ask an ordinary Jew what happened yesterday and what will happen tomorrow, and if he can answer, they will know he is from Israel: ונודע בגוים זרעם, וצאצאיהם בתוך העמים (Yeshayahu 61:9) Saadia and the Rambam on prophecy The Rabbi recalled the debate from the essay on prophecy, studied over a year earlier: Saadia The Rambam What is prophecy? A gift from God , granted to one who is fitting for it A human achievement : refine your intellect and character \"until you've tuned into the radio signal\" Why in the days of Mashiach? People will simply be gifted with it Seeing the Shechinah and all that is happening will drive people to work on themselves until they reach it Everyone agrees that prophecy fluctuates: \"You may tune in one day, you may tune out the next.\" And everyone agrees that a portion of Am Yisrael will have prophecy . \"There's no distinction made between men and women.\" There were women prophets, and if a person refines himself, man or woman alike will receive it. What the Rambam would say On all these details, the Rambam would say: leave the exact chronology and mechanics until it happens. \"Let it work out by itself.\" But in general he agrees with the ideas: there will be a Beit HaMikdash, there will be Eliyahu HaNavi, there will be a Mashiach, there will be a resurrection. Has the Process Begun? \"Arguably, I think many would argue nowadays this process has begun in some sort or fashion.\" It may take another year, or fifty, or a hundred. But \"the fact that we now have some state of sovereignty over the land of Israel is a clear sign that the messianic process has begun in some shape or manner.\" Are we fit for Mashiach ben David now? \"Nobody can answer that.\" Yet some prophecies are already being fulfilled: \"Israel's leading the way in being able to bring out water from the most desolate and dry places.\" \"And be'ezrat Hashem , maybe speedily in our days, in the next few weeks, and then we can cancel Tishah b'Av.\" Shabbetai Tzvi and the two Mashiachs The Rabbi gave a historical example of the two-Mashiach idea \"playing out in actual life.\" In the 1660s, the vast majority of Jews believed Shabbetai Tzvi was Mashiach. It is documented that many in Europe sold their houses and set off for Israel. The Sultan summoned him and offered him death or conversion, and in 1666 he converted to Islam. Some of his defenders then argued that this was the death of Mashiach ben Yosef : \"killing\" did not mean literal death but a spiritual death, conversion, and Mashiach ben David would follow in a few years. \"Clearly that didn't happen. Retrospectively, we know for a fact he was a false Mashiach.\" But it shows people reinterpreting events to fit the two-Mashiach scheme. It Will Last Until the End of the World ויעמדו על הענין הזה כל ימי העולם, לא ישתנה ענינם Once all five stages have happened, this state will continue for the rest of the world's existence: ישראל נושע בה׳ תשועת עולמים (Yeshayahu 45:17) But Saadia is troubled by the word \"everlasting.\" As many Rishonim held, he believes this world ends at 6,000 years , and then comes Olam HaBa, a newly created world (the subject of Essay 9, after the summer). So why \"everlasting salvation\"? Saadia explains that the verse means it will have no break : no further war, no further exile, for as long as this world lasts. \"Which for us now, according to Saadia, is about 214 years.\" For the Rambam , by contrast, everything Saadia said is true, but the world does not end. We die and go to Olam HaBa, which is spiritual and exists now. No new world is created. Choosing Good in the Days of Mashiach העם יבחרו בעבודה ולא ימרו In that period the people will choose to serve and not rebel: ומל ה׳ אלהיך את לבבך , \"Hashem will circumcise your heart\" to love and fear Him (Devarim 30:6), \"one of the most beautiful verses in the Torah.\" ונתתי לכם לב חדש (Yechezkel 36:26), \"the famous verse which the Christians distorted\": a new heart, meaning the will to do what is right. Why will they choose good? Because: they see the light of the Shechinah and have prophecy; they are well provided for ; no one oppresses them ; they lack nothing and succeed in all they do; there will be no plague, no affliction, no illness , and all mourning and sorrow will be gone. It will be a world of joy and gladness, as if the heavens and the land were renewed: כי הנני בורא שמים חדשים וארץ חדשה... כי הנני בורא את ירושלם גילה ועמה משוש (Yeshayahu 65:17-18) \"A new world in a metaphoric sense.\" How glorious, says Saadia, a world that is all joy, all service of God, all reward. And Tehillim: אשר בנינו כנטעים... מזוינו מלאים... אלופינו מסבלים, אין פרץ ואין יוצאת (Tehillim 144:12-14) \"No breaching, no more war.\" That is the end of Chapter 6. But where is free will? The Rabbi noted that Rav Crescas famously raised the problem. Saadia himself said in Essay 7 that there will still be free will and reward in the days of resurrection and redemption. But if everyone will be inclined to choose good, where is the free will, and how can there be reward for something you were inclined to do? The answer: there will still be free will, since reward requires it. But the choice will not be between good and bad , since no one will want to do bad. It will be between good and better. \"We all want to do good. But how much are we going to push ourselves?\" How motivated will I be to become even better than I already am? \"That's still up for grabs.\" And on that basis there will still be reward in the next world, whether it is Saadia's new world or the Rambam's spiritual one. \"This is why we talk all the time about Mashiach bimheirah beyamenu . We want it now more than ever. We want to see an end to the suffering in the world, an end to the illnesses, an end to the sadness, an end to wars.\" It may take time, but everyone, including the Rambam, agrees we will enjoy it until the end of 6,000. \"What will happen after 6,000? Wait and see. We'll see if the Rambam's right or Saadia was right.\" Questions The seventh millennium A participant asked about the seventh millennium. In short (fuller treatment in Essay 9): for the Rambam , the 6,000/7,000 scheme is one Midrashic opinion, not to be followed. For Saadia, the Ramban and many others , the world ends at 6,000 and the next world is physical, body and soul. The Gemara speaks of a thousand years in which the world lies desolate. The Rabbi suggested that is not literal, but describes a period in which souls grow used to being out of their physical bodies and ready for the new body they will receive in the new world. \"We don't go straight from the end of 6,000 into the new world.\" Who is \"ben David\"? \"We've got no idea.\" Ben David means a descendant of David. How will we know? Interestingly, Saadia never gives criteria for identifying Mashiach anywhere in this essay. The Rambam does , in Hilchot Melachim Chapter 11, and this is where he spends most of his detail: If a king arises from the House of David who studies Torah and keeps the mitzvot, compels all Israel to walk in the way of the Torah and repair its breaches, and fights the wars of Hashem, he is presumed to be Mashiach. If he succeeds, builds the Mikdash in its place and gathers the dispersed of Israel , he is certainly Mashiach. (Hilchot Melachim 11:4) The Rambam also says one should not assume Mashiach must perform miracles and wonders. \"According to the Rambam, Mashiach is a much more proactive thing\": leading the wars, bringing people to Torah. \"We'll see when that happens. We'll see who it is.\" \"There Is No Mashiach for Israel\" Before moving on, the Rabbi brought a famous statement in the Gemara (Sanhedrin), by Rav Hillel (\"not Beit Hillel, a different Hillel\"): אין משיח לישראל , \"there is no Mashiach for Israel.\" Rav Yosef Albo (Sefer HaIkkarim 4:42, 1400s) understood that this sage held all the messianic prophecies were already fulfilled in the days of Chizkiyahu. Other Rabbis contradicted him. Some commentators say the prophecies were fulfilled at the start of the Second Temple, when Koresh ordered it built. Albo therefore concludes that, unlike the Rambam , he does not count belief in Mashiach as a principle of faith. \"It's a true belief, which we have to believe.\" But one who denies it is not a heretic. Rashi explains that Hillel meant there would be no specific person : a redemption, but without an individual anointed one. \"Remember, we've been learning at the beginning of Saadia the idea of there being a redemption.\" Either way, this opinion is rejected. Chapter 7: Is the Redemption Conditional? The last three chapters of the essay are polemical: Chapters 7 and 8: against Jews , the Karaites, \"who believe Mashiach had already come,\" so that none of this will happen. Chapter 9: against the Christian belief that Mashiach has come in Jesus. כי יש אנשים שנקראים יהודים, חושבים כי אלה הייעודים כולם ואלה הנחמות היו כולם בבית שני, ועברו ולא נשאר להם דבר There are people \"who call themselves Jews\" who make two arguments: All the promises were fulfilled in the Second Temple period. That is Chapter 8. The promises were conditional. Verses such as לא יבוא עוד שמשך וירחך לא יאסף , \"your sun will no longer set,\" were promised on condition that we not worship idols. It is like Moshe's למען ירבו ימיכם וימי בניכם על האדמה , which depends on והיה אם שמע תשמעו . Because we sinned, we lost our sovereignty, some promises were fulfilled in the Second Temple and the rest never will be. \"Mashiach's not going to come.\" Saadia examined the claim of a condition carefully and found it false for five reasons : 1. The Torah states its conditions; the consolations have none Moshe's promises are explicitly conditional: כי אם שמר תשמרון את כל המצוה הזאת... והוריש ה׳ את כל הגוים האלה (Devarim 11:22-23) כי אם שמע תשמע בקלו... ואיבתי את איביך (Shemot 23:22) והיה עקב תשמעון (Devarim 7:12) \"Throughout the Torah we always see: when you do this, this will happen.\" But the messianic consolations studied in the previous chapters contain no condition . They are \"straightforward and outright promises.\" 2. The Torah also states the reverse Moshe did not only say \"if you listen, you will receive.\" He also gave the opposite: והיה אם שכח תשכח את ה׳ אלהיך (Devarim 8:19), and in the Torah reading of Tishah b'Av, כי תוליד בנים... העידתי בכם היום (Devarim 4:25-26), that we would be scattered. This is a תנאי כפול , a double condition. The consolations have no condition, let alone its reverse. 3. The promise is sworn like the covenant with Noach כי מי נח זאת לי, אשר נשבעתי מעבר מי נח עוד על הארץ, כן נשבעתי מקצף עליך (Yeshayahu 54:9) After the Flood, people still sinned, but Hashem had sworn never to bring another flood; He punishes them in other ways. So too our redemption is sworn like the waters of Noach. \"True, we sinned. Hashem will punish us with other things, but He's not going to punish us by taking away the messianic redemption.\" 4. The promise includes that we will not sin As Chapter 6 showed, part of the prophecy is that the people will hold fast to service and not rebel , and God knows all that will be. So even if there were a condition, it would do no harm, since in those days we will not sin, \"all the more so when there is no condition.\" 5. The Torah itself swears to it Some of these promises are sworn in the Torah itself, in Ha'azinu: כי אשא אל שמים ידי, ואמרתי חי אנכי לעלם... אשכיר חצי מדם (Devarim 32:40-42) \"I'm promising it's going to happen.\" All these points refute the claim of a condition. Summary After the ingathering of the believers (going is still a choice), there will be resurrection (Mashiach ben Yosef first), the Beit HaMikdash of precious stones, a fully settled and irrigated land, the light of the Shechinah guiding the way, and prophecy throughout the people. Saadia vs the Rambam on prophecy: a gift from God, or a human achievement. Both agree it will return, for men and women alike. Shabbetai Tzvi's followers reinterpreted his conversion as the \"death\" of Mashiach ben Yosef. \"Everlasting salvation\" means no break until this world ends at 6,000 years, for Saadia; the Rambam holds the world does not end. Choosing good: with the Shechinah, prosperity, peace and no illness, people will choose to serve. The free choice that remains is between good and better , so reward remains (Rav Crescas). The Rambam's criteria for Mashiach (Hilchot Melachim 11:4): presumed if he leads in Torah and fights Hashem's wars; certain if he builds the Mikdash and gathers the exiles. \"אין משיח לישראל\": Albo, the prophecies were fulfilled in Chizkiyahu's day, so belief in Mashiach is not a principle; Rashi, a redemption without a specific person. The opinion is rejected. Chapter 7: the redemption is not conditional : no condition is stated, no reverse is stated, it is sworn like Noach's covenant, it includes our not sinning, and the Torah swears to it. Coming Up Next week, the last Monday shiur before the break: Chapter 8 , Saadia's fifteen proofs that the prophecies were not fulfilled in the Second Temple period, \"some beautiful prophecies,\" and Chapter 9 , against the Christian claim. Then a break until around Rosh Chodesh Elul, at the end of August, to begin Essay 9 on Olam HaBa , followed by Essay 10 on behaviour and conduct.","db_z4Ocek9c":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 8 (The Final Redemption), Chapter 5 and the first part of Chapter 6 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 7 July 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Halfway Through the Essay \"We are halfway through the eighth essay, on ge'ulah .\" Until now the word Mashiach has not appeared once. Tonight it does. Chapters 1 and 2: the purpose of the redemption is to gather us, bring us to the Land of Israel and be there for Hashem as an am segulah . Saadia brought proofs from the prophets and from reason, and gave encouragement that the redemption will come. At the end of Chapter 2 he set out two possible times : the קץ , the decreed end, whenever it is, or earlier, if we bring it through teshuvah and good deeds . Chapters 3 and 4: Saadia's attempt, from the verses in Daniel, to work out the date of the ketz , which came to the 960s CE. It passed, and he died some twenty years before it. The Ramban, the Abarbanel and others also tried, though the Rambam says not to, because the Gemara forbids it. Now, in Chapters 5 and 6, Saadia describes what will happen , at the ketz or earlier, and introduces the one who spearheads the redemption: the Mashiach . The Rabbi noted that some hold that the person of Mashiach, an anointed leader, is not itself the critical belief; what is critical is the redemption . And Saadia introduces a concept he develops at length: there are two Mashiachs. Chapter 5: If the End Comes and We Have Not Returned כיון שפירשתי קץ גאולתנו... אומר עתה, כיון שידענו שאם לא תושלם תשובתנו נעמוד עד שנבוא לקץ, ואם ישלם הקץ קודם שנשוב, לא יתכן שתהיה הישועה ואנחנו חוטאים Saadia asks an important question. We were exiled for our sins. The whole point of the redemption is that we return. If the ketz arrives and we still have not done teshuvah, how can Hashem redeem a nation that is not worthy? It would be in vain. His answer, from the tradition of the Hachamim: אבל קדמונינו ז\"ל קבלו שתקרנה אותנו צרות רבות ורעות, שנבחר בעבורן התשובה ונהיה ראויים להגאל When the time is ripe, many troubles will befall Am Yisrael, and these will push us to choose teshuvah and become fit for redemption. This is the Gemara in Sanhedrin seen last week: if Israel do teshuvah they are redeemed, and if not, Hashem sets up a king whose decrees are as harsh as Haman's , and they do teshuvah and are redeemed. So the ketz cannot simply arrive by itself. Some time before it, things will happen that force us to return. \"And I would argue that we're living somewhat through such a time, in which people are generally returning and coming back to HaKadosh Baruch Hu, especially in Israel. And perhaps this is the beginning of coming towards the ketz .\" Mashiach ben David and Mashiach ben Yosef We are used to thinking of the redemption as led by Mashiach ben David . The Gemara and the Rambam require that he descend from David, from the verse in Parashat Vayechi: לא יסור שבט מיהודה ומחקק מבין רגליו . But there is also someone who comes first and prepares the way: Mashiach ben Yosef . Where does this come from? The Gemara. It speaks of Mashiach ben Yosef, and of a great mourning described in a verse in Zechariah, which the Gemara explains as mourning for Mashiach ben Yosef, who is killed . The prophets , which Saadia will quote. The Midrashim , especially one called Midrash Vayosha , written a couple of hundred years before Saadia. From these three sources Saadia builds \"a whole dramatic elaboration of what's going to happen.\" The Rambam never mentions Mashiach ben Yosef. Whenever he writes about Mashiach he speaks of the redemption and of Mashiach ben David, but never ben Yosef. Perhaps he took that Gemara as aggadah not meant to be taken as literally happening, or for other reasons. Saadia's narrative ואמרו שסבת זה עמידת איש מבני יוסף בגליל A man of the tribe of Yosef arises in the Galil , in the north. People of our nation gather to him. He goes up to Yerushalayim , which at that time is under the rule of Edom , that is, Christian control. (The Rabbi noted a mistranslation from the Arabic in the text here.) He stays there for some time. Then a king named Armilus comes against them, fights, captures the city , kills, takes captives and afflicts. The man from the tribe of Yosef is among those killed. Great troubles follow. The hardest is the loss of favour with all the kingdoms, until Jews are driven out into the deserts , hungry and thirsty. Because of these troubles, many abandon their faith . Those who remain are refined in the deserts. Then Eliyahu appears to them, and the redemption comes, through Mashiach ben David. \"By the way, this is all if we don't do teshuvah,\" as Saadia will say. If we do teshuvah, \"then great, then it just happens.\" Who is Armilus? Mashiach ben Yosef being killed is in the Gemara. Armilus is not in any verse. And there is something peculiar about it. Saadia was writing in the early tenth century (he died in 942), and Jerusalem was then under the Caliphate, not under Christian rule . Yet, on the strength of the tradition and the Midrashim, he still believed there would be a Christian king who would fight us. The Rabbi showed the entry in the Jewish Encyclopedia: Saadia is the earliest trustworthy authority to speak of Armilus. He records an ancient tradition: if the Jews do not prove worthy, God will force them to repent through persecutions; a man from the tribe of Yosef will arise and wrest Jerusalem from the Edomites; Armilus will conquer the holy city and kill its inhabitants, including Mashiach ben Yosef; and then Mashiach will come. Armilus is the last powerful anti-Jewish king, the Gog of the prophets under another name. In Midrash Vayosha he is described as bald, with one large eye and one small, deaf in the right ear and maimed in the right arm. \"It all sounds quite peculiar.\" In Kabbalistic literature the idea is very pronounced, though Saadia was not a Kabbalist. The Rabbi recalled that the Arizal says that somewhere in the daily Amidah, when we pray for Mashiach, we should have in mind that Mashiach ben Yosef not be killed by Armilus, though he could not remember exactly where. \"It all sounds for us quite difficult, because our enemies at the moment are not Christian. In the grand scheme of things, the enemies of Am Yisrael are more Islamic than Christian.\" The Rambam would say these are Midrashim and ideas, not literal predictions. Saadia takes them very seriously. Saadia's Verses for Each Step Saadia says that after hearing these traditions, he looked into Scripture and found a source for each one. The prophecies are scattered in different places; the order is the tradition of the Hachamim, and he has arranged the verses accordingly. The event The verse 1 Edom holds the Mikdash at the time of redemption ועלו מושעים בהר ציון לשפט את הר עשו (Ovadiah 1:21). Esav is Christian, not Islamic, rule 2 A descendant of Rachel fights them אם לא יסחבום צעירי הצאן (Yirmiyahu 49:20). The youngest of the flock are Rachel's sons, Yosef and Binyamin; and rachel itself means a ewe 3 Only a few gather to him ולקחתי אתכם אחד מעיר ושנים ממשפחה (Yirmiyahu 3:14) 4 The enemy captures and kills them ואספתי את כל הגוים אל ירושלם למלחמה ונלכדה העיר ונשסו הבתים (Zechariah 14:2) 5 Their king is killed and mourned והביטו אלי את אשר דקרו, וספדו עליו כמספד על היחיד (Zechariah 12:10). The Gemara says this is the mourning for Mashiach ben Yosef 6 Great distress for the nation והיתה עת צרה אשר לא נהיתה מהיות גוי עד העת ההיא (Daniel 12:1) 7 Hatred of the nations drives them into the deserts והבאתי אתכם אל מדבר העמים ונשפטתי אתכם שם (Yechezkel 20:35) 8 Hunger and thirst , as their fathers suffered כאשר נשפטתי את אבותיכם במדבר (Yechezkel 20:36) 9 They are tested and refined in their faith והעברתי אתכם תחת השבט, והבאתי אתכם במסרת הברית (Yechezkel 20:37) 10 The weak in faith lose heart וברותי מכם המרדים והפושעים בי (Yechezkel 20:38) 11 Eliyahu comes to those who remain הנה אנכי שלח לכם את אליה הנביא... והשיב לב אבות על בנים (Malachi 3:23-24), the last verses of the Prophets On step 10: the faint-hearted will say, \"Is this what we hoped for? We had a Mashiach, and he was killed. Is this the redemption?\" הנה עניני הדברים האלה מפורשים במקרא, והוסיפו לנו קדמונינו שסדורם זה אחר זה Saadia ends the chapter by praising God for telling us all this in advance, so that it will not strike us suddenly and we will not lose hope when it happens. We will know it is a test. And when it comes to pass: מכנף הארץ זמרת שמענו צבי לצדיק , \"from the ends of the earth we have heard songs: glory to the righteous.\" What to take from Chapter 5 This is why the Rambam said not to spend too long on these details. \"When it happens, it will happen, and you'll know how it's going to happen.\" Will there really be a Christian king, if it happens in the next few years? \"Maybe in a hundred years' time it will be different. Who knows?\" But the key point, which is certainly true from the Gemara, is this: if we do teshuvah and take responsibility, we can dictate how Mashiach comes and do it without trouble. If not, we wait for the ketz and are subject to this process, forced into teshuvah through enemies . \"That's what we're seeing, I think, nowadays to an extent. People are coming back to HaKadosh Baruch Hu because they're seeing that the whole country of Israel is a miracle, surrounded by all our enemies, and yet somehow we still continue.\" Chapter 6: Mashiach ben David Saadia now turns to the final Mashiach. Again he reads the verses very literally, \"and some of them, we're going to see, are almost impossible to happen.\" אם לא נשוב בתשובה ויעמוד איש מבני יוסף, או אם נשוב בתשובה ולא יהיה... יראה לנו משיח בן דוד פתאום This, the Rabbi said, makes it clear: the whole episode of Mashiach ben Yosef only happens if we do not do teshuvah. If we take responsibility, we do not need that terrible time. Either way, Mashiach ben David will appear . Ben Yosef is not certain; ben David is. What does \"Mashiach\" mean? \"I should have explained this at the beginning.\" Mashiach means anointed , from limshoach , to smear with oil. The word appears often in Vayikra: והכהן המשיח תחתיו מבניו יעשה אתה , the anointed Kohen Gadol, who was anointed with the שמן המשחה . In Parashat Shoftim there is the כהן משוח מלחמה , who addressed the people before battle and sent home the fearful and the newly betrothed. \"It means nothing more than that.\" In the redemption, Mashiach is the one appointed and selected to spearhead and lead it. (Saadia returns to the word in Chapter 9, on Christianity.) If ben Yosef comes first: the forerunner ואם יהיה משיח בן יוסף קודם לו, יהיה כשליח לו, מתקן האומה ומסקל הדרך He will be like an emissary who prepares the nation and clears the road: הנני שלח מלאכי ופנה דרך לפני (Malachi 3:1). And he will be like fire refining the great sinners and like launderers' soap for the lighter sinners: כי הוא כאש מצרף וכברית מכבסים (Malachi 3:2). Serious sinners go through the fire of teshuvah; lesser ones just need cleaning. \"See, he's taking the verses very literally.\" If not: ben David comes suddenly ופתאם יבוא אל היכלו האדון אשר אתם מבקשים (Malachi 3:1) And if Jerusalem is then in the hands of Armilus, ben David will kill him : ונתתי את נקמתי באדום ביד עמי ישראל (Yechezkel 25:14). That is why Saadia insists the enemy must be a Christian king, Edom. Comforting the broken Whether or not ben Yosef came, ben David will visit the people and heal their fear, their despair and their broken spirit: רוח אדני אלהים עלי, יען משח ה׳ אתי לבשר ענוים, שלחני לחבש לנשברי לב, לקרא לשבוים דרור... לקרא שנת רצון לה׳ (Yeshayahu 61:1-2) \"To those who are kept hostage, to free them.\" פאר תחת אפר. The next verse: לשום לאבלי ציון, לתת להם פאר תחת אפר (61:3), splendour in place of ashes. The prophet is playing on the letters: אפר , ash, mourning and destruction, becomes פאר , beauty, like tiferet . The Rabbi linked this to the halachah that a mourner does not wear tefillin, which are called pe'er , on the day of the death before the funeral, and to the custom of not wearing tefillin on the morning of Tishah b'Av: \"we don't have pe'er , we only have efer .\" In the afternoon we have hope, so we put on tefillin again. They will settle the land and dwell in it: ובנו חרבות עולם (61:4). A process, not a moment The Rabbi stressed that at this point it is not yet the full redemption. There is no Beit HaMikdash yet. \"People think, ah, Mashiach comes and then everything happens, we fly to Israel. No, it's a process, a long process.\" The War of Gog ואז ישמעו אנשי גוג את שם בן דוד... ויושבים לבטח בלי מבצר ובלי חומה Things are going well. Ben David has come and Jerusalem is being rebuilt. Then Gog (Saadia does not identify him) hears of it: the people are prospering and living securely, without walls or fortresses. \"Sounds familiar, right? The State of Israel, we're developing.\" He decides to seize them: אעלה על ארץ פרזות, אבוא השקטים ישבי לבטח, כלם ישבים באין חומה, ובריח ודלתים אין להם (Yechezkel 38:11) Many nations join him, and he passes through many lands to reach us: ובאת ממקומך מירכתי צפון, אתה ועמים רבים אתך (38:15). It is not one front but many, and the enemy is not on our border; he comes from far away. Two kinds of people join Gog רשעים גדולים מתמכרים למות , \"great wicked men, addicted to death.\" \"Such a nice term, because it's so true. We could say about many of our enemies today, some of our enemies are just addicted to death.\" These are the ones Mashiach ben David will kill: וקבצתי את כל הגוים והורדתים אל עמק יהושפט (Yoel 4:2). The Rabbi read through Yoel 4 in English: proclaim war among the nations, beat ploughshares into swords, swing the sickle for the harvest is ripe, \"multitudes, multitudes in the valley of decision.\" People who come to join Am Yisrael and see the wonders. Of them it says: כי אז אהפך אל עמים שפה ברורה לקרא כלם בשם ה׳ (Tzefaniah 3:9). So even under Mashiach ben David there is a war. The difference from ben Yosef's war: there, we lose and are persecuted; here, we win . The Nations Come Up for Sukkot ויגזור עליהם בן דוד לבוא בכל שנה לחוג את חג הסכות והיה כל הנותר מכל הגוים הבאים על ירושלם, ועלו מדי שנה בשנה להשתחות למלך ה׳ צבאות ולחג את חג הסכות (Zechariah 14:16) A nation that does not come will have no rain (14:17). And if Egypt says, \"We don't need rain, the Nile waters our land,\" Hashem will decree that they lose the Nile too (14:18). The Rabbi drew out what this shows: life continues as usual. The nations and countries still exist. Some came to fight and were judged in the valley of decision. Others live alongside us, some working for us, as the prophets say, and come up every year. \"The people are still living in other countries, and life is continuing.\" The Nations Bring the Jews Home At this stage, many of Bnei Yisrael are still scattered. There has been no full ingathering yet. The nations will then realise that the best way to draw close to ben David is to bring the Jews to him as a gift : ולקחום עמים והביאום אל מקומם (Yeshayahu 14:2). Each nation according to its means, \"according to its GDP\": Who How Verse Rich nations On horses and mules, in covered wagons and carriages, with honour והביאו את כל אחיכם מכל הגוים מנחה לה׳, בסוסים וברכב ובצבים ובפרדים ובכרכרות (Yeshayahu 66:20) Poor nations On their shoulders, and their children in their arms והביאו בניך בחצן (Yeshayahu 49:22) Across the sea In ships כי לי איים יקוו ואניות תרשיש (Yeshayahu 60:9) From Kush In papyrus boats as far as Egypt, because in the high, mountainous places ordinary ships would break, but the reed boats bend and pass safely הוי ארץ צלצל כנפים אשר מעבר לנהרי כוש, השלח בים צירים ובכלי גמא על פני מים (Yeshayahu 18:1-2); מעבר לנהרי כוש עתרי בת פוצי יובלון מנחתי (Tzefaniah 3:10) Where there are no nations to bring them An angel of Hashem carries them, as if on clouds, or like a bird, or on the winds מי אלה כעב תעופינה (Yeshayahu 60:8); והחיות רצוא ושוב (Yechezkel 1:14); אמר לצפון תני ולתימן אל תכלאי, הביאי בני מרחוק (Yeshayahu 43:6) \"Incredible, right?\" How would you translate it today? \"I suppose some nations will have beautiful Qatari planes with all the luxuries. That's going to be the rich ones. And the other ones are flying economy, Wizz Air, to Israel.\" These are verses, so we have to deal with them, but Saadia reads them \"very, very literally, which is why the Rambam didn't like all of this. When it happens, we'll see how it happens.\" The rest of Chapter 6, on resurrection, the Beit HaMikdash and prophecy, is for next week. Question: What Difference Does My Teshuvah Make? A participant asked what difference it makes to God whether she does teshuvah, and whether Mashiach will not come without it. The Rabbi said the first question was a whole topic in itself, for another time, perhaps in Elul. On the second: the redemption is definitely coming. If we do not do teshuvah now and take responsibility, it slows the process down. Summary If the ketz comes before teshuvah , troubles will force us to return, \"a king as harsh as Haman\" (Sanhedrin). Two Mashiachs. Saadia, from the Gemara, the prophets and Midrash Vayosha, describes Mashiach ben Yosef , who rises in the Galil, takes Jerusalem from Edom, and is killed by Armilus . The Rambam never mentions ben Yosef. Armilus is not in any verse. Saadia is the earliest reliable source for him, and expected a Christian enemy even though Jerusalem was then under Muslim rule. Ben Yosef only comes if we do not do teshuvah. Ben David comes either way. Mashiach means anointed , like the Kohen Gadol anointed with oil. Ben David heals the broken, pe'er tachat efer , rebuilds, and then defeats Gog and the nations in the valley of Yehoshafat. This war we win. The nations come up every year for Sukkot, and bring the scattered Jews home, each in its own way. Life goes on as usual. It is a long process , not a single moment. \"You could say the messianic process has somewhat begun already,\" with the ingathering and the Negev blooming. \"No one knows. I'm not saying it's happening, I'm just saying it's possible, and we should be aware of that.\" Coming Up Next week: the rest of Chapter 6, on resurrection, the Beit HaMikdash and prophecy in the Messianic era, and Chapter 7, \"a whole new concept.\"","furzHhfjzTo":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 8 (The Final Redemption), Chapters 3 and 4 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 30 June 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Tonight's Topic: Calculating the End \"We have a very interesting shiur tonight, and that is calculating when Mashiach is going to come.\" To the best of the Rabbi's knowledge, Rav Saadia is the very first of our Hachamim to give a specific date. There are verses in Daniel that point towards a date, but he is the first to turn them into a precise number, and many followed him. \"Quick spoiler\": all of the predicted dates came and went. And it is fascinating that the Rishonim who gave dates all chose dates in the near future, and most of them died before their date arrived, \"so they never saw it erupting in their faces that it didn't happen as they predicted.\" Recap: Chapters 1 and 2 The word Mashiach has not yet appeared. It only comes in Chapter 5. So far Saadia has spoken of ge'ulah , redemption. Four purposes of the redemption: to save us from our troubles, to gather us together, to bring us to Israel, and to make us God's treasured nation. Four proofs: the prophets said it, Hashem Himself promised it, it would be unjust for our suffering to have no end, and the prophets compare the final redemption to leaving Egypt. Encouragement: Hashem will not forsake us, and if He can measure the waters and the dust, He can redeem us. Two times for the redemption , from the Gemara in Sanhedrin: the קץ , the fixed end time, or earlier, brought about by teshuvah . What is the ketz ? That is Chapter 3. Chapter 3: The Three Angels in Daniel שהוא יתברך הראה לנו בנבואות דניאל שלשה מלאכים, האחד מהם עומד ממעל לחדקל, ושנים האחרים עומדים על שתי השפתות, ושואלים את העומד למעלה מתי תהיה הגאולה Saadia's source is the last chapter of Daniel, Chapter 12. (The first verses of that chapter, about those who sleep in the dust awakening, were studied in the previous essay on resurrection.) One angel stands above the river, which Saadia identifies from Chapter 10 as the Chiddekel , the Tigris, and two angels stand on either bank. They ask the angel above: when will the redemption be? The verses The Rabbi went through the verses inside before returning to Saadia. Verse 5: Daniel sees two more angels, one on each bank. Verse 6: one asks the man clothed in linen, above the waters: עד מתי קץ הפלאות , \"how long until the end of these wonders?\" Verse 7: the angel above raises his right and left hands to heaven and swears כי למועד מועדים וחצי , \"for a time, times and a half,\" and when the shattering of the holy people's power is complete, all this will end. \"Does that make any sense to any of us? No, obviously.\" Daniel himself did not understand: Verse 8: ואני שמעתי ולא אבין, ואמרה אדני מה אחרית אלה , \"I heard and did not understand. My master, what is the end of these things?\" Verse 9: לך דניאל כי סתמים וחתמים הדברים עד עת קץ , \"Go, Daniel, for the words are closed and sealed until the time of the end.\" Verse 10: many will be purified and refined, the wicked will not understand, but the wise will understand . Verse 11: from the time the daily offering is removed and the \"abomination of desolation\" is set up: 1,290 days . Verse 12: אשרי המחכה ויגיע לימים אלף שלש מאות שלשים וחמשה , \"happy is the one who waits and reaches 1,335 days.\" When is Daniel speaking? Daniel lives in the Babylonian exile, around the start of the Second Temple. The Second Temple was destroyed in 70 CE and stood 420 years, which takes us back to around 350 BCE. \"There are different specific dates. Some say this, some say that.\" The point is that Daniel is speaking sometime in the fourth century BCE . Saadia reads the passage The angel swore, even though nobody asked him to swear. The two other angels heard \"a time, times and a half\" and it was enough for them: they understood it. Daniel did not, and asked. Before giving the answer, the angel first explained why he had concealed it : סתמתי את הדבר כדי שלא יעמדו עליו הפתאים והכסילים The foolish do not long for what the wise long for: the reward of Olam HaBa and eternal life. They only want worldly power and honour. But the wise will persist and understand. Only then did the angel give the number: 1,335 . Days means years Saadia says the \"days\" are years . \"This is nothing novel Saadia's telling us here.\" He brings proofs: A house in a walled city (Parashat Behar). Normally ancestral land returns at the Yovel. A house in a walled city can be redeemed only within a year, after which it belongs to the buyer permanently. The Torah first says ימים תהיה גאלתו , \"days shall be its redemption,\" and the next verse explains: ואם לא יגאל עד מלאת לו שנה תמימה . \"Days\" means a year. Prophecies about kingdoms sometimes say years outright: seventy years for Tzor, and מקץ ארבעים שנה אקבץ את מצרים , \"at the end of forty years I will gather Egypt.\" Sometimes the same verse uses both. Hashem tells Yechezkel: ואני נתתי לך את שני עונם למספר ימים שלש מאות ותשעים יום , \"I have given you the years of their iniquity as a number of days, 390 days.\" (The Rabbi noted that the printed text of Saadia here has a mistake: it must read years , \"otherwise it defeats the whole purpose.\") The verse equates years with days. So the angel's answer is 1,335 years . \"Everyone really accepts that.\" How does \"times and a half\" make 1,335? ואנחנו צריכים להשתדל ולחקור על פירוש מועד מועדים וחצי, עד שיהיו כמו אלף שלש מאות שלשים וחמש שנה Daniel understood the link and asked no more. We have to work it out. Saadia is the first to try. Drop the first word. למועד just means \"at the time of,\" as in למועד חדש האביב . It introduces the time; it is not part of the count. \"Times\" means two periods of Jewish sovereignty. Standing in Daniel's day, at the end of the First Temple exile and before the Second Temple, we had been sovereign twice: Period Years Sovereign in the land with the Mishkan, before the First Temple was built ( תפ\"ח ) 480 The First Temple ( ת\"י ) 410 Two \"times\" 890 \"And a half\" ( תמ\"ה ) 445 Total 1,335 והכל אלף שלש מאות שלשים וחמש בדיוק, בלי תוספת ומחסור : exactly 1,335, \"without addition or subtraction.\" \"This is remarkable. Incredible.\" The 1,290 Verse 11 gives 1,290. Is it 1,335 or 1,290? Saadia says the 1,290 is counted from an event at the beginning of the Second Temple , the \"removal of the daily offering,\" which took place 45 years after Daniel's prophecy. 45 plus 1,290 is 1,335: the same end point. The 2,300 There is a third number, in Daniel Chapter 8: עד ערב בקר אלפים ושלש מאות ונצדק קדש , \"until 2,300 evenings and mornings, and the holy place will be vindicated.\" \"You see, he's really troubling himself to work it out.\" Saadia says it must be halved , because \"evenings and mornings\" counts half-days: 1,150 . Then he says it must be counted from a point 185 years after Daniel's words, which again arrives at the same end. So Saadia has three numbers, 1,335, 1,290 and 1,150 , each counted from a different starting point, all arriving at one date. \"Obviously he's troubled by that,\" which is why he devotes the next chapter to showing that Tanach does this elsewhere. Chapter 4: Different Numbers, One End ובעבור הדמות הספק בשלשה זמנים בקץ הזה... Saadia says Hashem deliberately gave us different numbers for the earlier exiles too, so that we would not think a doubt over the ketz means it is untrue. Just as those were reconciled, so can this be. The Rabbi asked the class where else the Torah gives different numbers for the same thing. \"The number of years in Egypt?\" \"Excellent, well done.\" Egypt: 400, 430 and 210 Number Where Saadia's reading 400 The Brit Bein HaBetarim: כי גר יהיה זרעך בארץ לא להם ועבדום וענו אתם ארבע מאות שנה Counted from the birth of Yitzchak , \"your seed,\" when Avraham was 100 430 Parashat Bo: the dwelling of Bnei Yisrael was 430 years Includes 30 years of Avraham's own wandering before Yitzchak was born 210 The actual time in Egypt The lifespans of Kehat, Amram and Moshe make 400 years in Egypt impossible How does Saadia reach 30 years of wandering? The Torah says explicitly that Avram was 75 when he left Charan: ואברם בן חמש שנים ושבעים שנה בצאתו מחרן . He was 100 when Yitzchak was born, so 25 years from Charan. We are not told how long the journey from Ur Kasdim to Charan took, so Saadia assumes 5 years. That makes 30, and 400 plus 30 is 430. The obvious questions remain: how can Avraham's wanderings be called the dwelling of Bnei Yisrael , and how can Ur Kasdim and Charan be called Egypt ? \"These are your questions, not to go into now.\" The Midrashim deal with them, and the Kabbalah too, where gilgulim are involved, \"obviously Saadia doesn't take that approach.\" Either way, one exile, three numbers, three different starting points. Babylon: 52 and 70 52 years: כי לפי מלאת לבבל שבעים שנה אפקד אתכם , \"when seventy years are complete for Babylon, I will remember you.\" That is seventy years of Babylonian rule . But the exile only began in the nineteenth year of Nevuchadnetzar ( ובחדש החמישי בעשור לחדש, היא שנת תשע עשרה שנה למלך נבוכדנאצר ), so seventy years of Babylon means 52 years of exile , ending with Koresh. 70 years: למלאות לחרבות ירושלם שבעים שנה , seventy years for the ruins of Yerushalayim . The answer: after 52 years Koresh gave permission to build, and they began. Then the work stopped for about 18 years, as Ezra says: באדין בטלת עבידת בית אלהא די בירושלם... עד שנת תרתין למלכות דריוש , until the second year of Daryavesh, which completes the seventy. At 52 the rebuilding began; at 70 came the full redemption. Saadia's conclusion וכאשר לא הזיק בקץ הראשון שלשה זמנים, ולא הזיק בקץ השני שני זמנים, אף כן לא יזיק בקץ השלישי שיש לו שלשה זמנים Three numbers did not undermine the first redemption, and two numbers did not undermine the second, so three numbers do not undermine the third: 1,150, 1,290 and 1,335 . God has graced His people with the wisdom to explain them all. What Date Does Saadia Reach? Saadia's answer is 1,335 years from Daniel's vision. The commentators place that episode in 371 BCE , \"give or take ten, twenty, thirty years.\" That takes us to 964 CE . Saadia died in 942 CE . \"So Saadia is dying and he's thinking to himself, another 22 years and Mashiach is going to come. Note the closeness here.\" Mashiach did not come in 964. The other Rishonim's dates Who Predicted Died Rav Saadia Gaon 964 942 Ramban 1358 1270 Rabbeinu Bachya of Saragossa 1403 1340 Abarbanel , of the Spanish expulsion 1510 1508 Malbim (Meir Leibush ben Yechiel Michel), \"a standard text when you want to study Tanach\" 1928 1879 \"They've all come, they've all gone, and nothing's happened.\" The Gemara: Do Not Calculate אמר רבי שמואל בר נחמני אמר רבי יונתן: תיפח עצמן של מחשבי קצין, שהיו אומרים כיון שהגיע את הקץ ולא בא, שוב אינו בא. אלא חכה לו, שנאמר אם יתמהמה חכה לו (Sanhedrin 97b) \"May the bones of those who calculate the end be cursed.\" Why? Because if you are wrong, people wait for the day, the day comes and nothing happens, and they conclude he is never coming . Rather, wait for him. The Rabbi imagined a younger contemporary of Saadia. \"If he believed it was 964, he must have talked about this in his Shabbat sermons. He must have said, Mashiach is coming in 22 years' time, do teshuvah now. I have no proof for it, but it's plausible.\" Then 964 comes and nothing happens. \"What is this all about?\" The Rambam codifies it At the end of Hilchot Melachim, Chapter 12, at the very end of the laws of Mashiach: ועל כל פנים, אין סדור הויית הדברים האלו ולא דקדוקיהן עיקר בדת. ולעולם לא יתעסק אדם בדברי ההגדות, ולא יאריך במדרשות האמורים בענינים אלו וכיוצא בהן, ולא ישימם עיקר, שאין מביאין לא לידי יראה ולא לידי אהבה. וכן לא יחשב הקצין. אמרו חכמים: תפח רוחן של מחשבי הקצים. אלא יחכה ויאמין בכלל הדבר The order of these events is not important. Do not spend your time on these Midrashim and do not make them central, because they bring neither fear nor love of Hashem. And do not calculate the end . Wait, and believe in the matter in general. This matters for next week too, because in Chapter 5 Saadia gives a detailed account of what will happen: two Mashiachs, one who dies, a war. \"Something the Rambam doesn't like either.\" Ibn Ezra The Ibn Ezra agrees with the Rambam. On לך דניאל כי סתמים וחתמים הדברים , he says: don't ask any more . The matter is permanently sealed. \"I'm going to give you a bunch of numbers to confuse you, but it's permanently sealed.\" And indeed the Ibn Ezra made no calculations, because he held it was wrong. How Could the Others Calculate? So we have Saadia, the Ramban and others on one side, and the Rambam and Ibn Ezra, strongly supported by the Gemara, on the other. Saadia himself does not address the question. Others do: The Ramban: the Gemara's curse was for the time of the Gemara, the fourth and fifth centuries, when the end was still far off. Now that it is imminent, calculating is permitted. \"Clearly, it wasn't right. He was wrong. That's a fact.\" The Abarbanel: the curse is on those who calculate through astrology and similar means. Calculating from the verses Hashem gave us in Daniel is what those verses are for. \"That's a logical answer.\" But his date did not come either. The Rabbi's answer: ripe times \"Perhaps the simplest answer to all of this\" is that the Gemara forbids calculating in a definite way . Saying \"Mashiach will come by this date\" is forbidden. But it is fine to say this is a ripe time for Mashiach to come: 964, 1358, 1928. \"It could well be that all of these Rishonim are right, by the way. They all have different proofs, different calculations, really complicated stuff.\" All those dates may have been ripe for the ketz , \"but we didn't utilise that ripeness. We didn't use that time.\" And today \"How many times have I heard, as a young child, throughout my years, rabbis get up and say Mashiach's coming?\" As a bachur in yeshivah, he heard that Rav Kanievsky had said Mashiach was coming that year, because it was a Shemittah year or something. \"I used to think to myself, this is it, this is the year.\" In the last couple of weeks, with Iran, people sent him clips of rabbis and asked whether this was Gog uMagog. \"The answer always is: I don't know, no one knows. It's possible, and it's a ripe time to come. That should be our perspective, that should be our answer.\" Question: What About the 6,000 Years? A participant asked about the Gemara that the world exists for 6,000 years. Doesn't that mean Mashiach must come by the year 6000? \"No. The Gemara doesn't say that.\" The 6,000 years is the grand scheme of things, the end of the world , not a date for the redemption. \"By the way, this is all assuming we take this Gemara literally. The Rambam doesn't.\" If the world ends at 6000 and we believe in the Messianic redemption, Mashiach would have to come before then, but when? \"A year before, a hundred years before, two hundred years before, who knows?\" (\"One second before,\" a participant added.) \"Precisely. Who knows?\" Summary Saadia is the first to give a date. In Daniel 12, the angel's answer \"a time, times and a half\" is explained as 1,335 years , since \"days\" in prophecy can mean years (the walled-city house, Yechezkel's 390). \"Times and a half\": two periods of sovereignty, 480 years with the Mishkan and 410 with the First Temple, make 890; half is 445; together, exactly 1,335 . 1,290 and 1,150 (half of 2,300 evenings and mornings) are counted from later starting points and reach the same end. Chapter 4: the Torah gives Egypt 400, 430 and 210 years, and Babylon 52 and 70, each from a different perspective. So too the final redemption. The dates: Saadia's comes to about 964 CE , 22 years after his death; the Ramban's 1358, Rabbeinu Bachya's 1403, the Abarbanel's 1510, the Malbim's 1928. All passed. The Gemara curses those who calculate the end ; the Rambam codifies it and the Ibn Ezra agrees. The Ramban (it is now imminent) and the Abarbanel (only astrology is forbidden) justify their calculations. The Rabbi's view: these were ripe times , not deadlines. No one knows when Mashiach will come. Coming Up Next week, at the normal time: Chapters 5 and 6 , where Saadia finally introduces Mashiach : what will happen, the wars, and the two types of Mashiach.","wvvocW1WReo":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 8 (The Final Redemption), Chapters 1 and 2 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 23 June 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Where We Are in the Book The first shiur on the eighth essay. The Rabbi began by placing it in the structure of the whole book, which falls into three sections: Section Essays Theme 1 1 and 2 God Himself : creation from nothing, and Hashem's unity and how we describe Him 2 3 to 6 The human being and his relationship with God : Torah, mitzvot and prophecy (3), free will and obedience (4), mitzvot, sins and teshuvah (5), and the soul, our identity (6) 3 7 to 9 The end of days , both in this world and in the next Essay 7, finished last time, dealt with תחיית המתים . Essay 9 will deal with עולם הבא , the new world, and reward and punishment there. Between them, still in this world, comes Essay 8: the redemption and Mashiach . What is redemption? What is Mashiach? Is there a fixed time for him to come? Can we hasten his arrival? Has he already come? What is his essence? These are the questions Saadia takes up over nine chapters. Essay 10, on general musar and conduct, \"is not a philosophical work. That's just a conclusion to his book about the best way we as Jews and as humans should behave.\" The plan is to finish Essay 8 by Tishah b'Av. Tonight: Chapters 1 and 2 , the purpose of the redemption and the traditional proofs that it will come. Chapters 3 to 5 will deal with when it will come. The essay is headed מאמר השמיני: גאולה אחרונה , the final redemption, meaning the final redemption in this world . A word about the timing The Rabbi said how appropriate it felt to be preparing this essay \"with everything happening in Israel and Iran.\" But he added a clear caution: \"I don't like people who come along and say this is Mashiach times, and Mashiach's about to come, and this is the war of Gog uMagog. I think it's hubris to be able to think we know when Mashiach is coming.\" What we can say, and what this chapter shows, is that the Land of Israel is an essential part of Mashiach . When we are sovereign and gathered into the land, \"this is certainly the beginning of the Messianic times.\" How long that goes on, decades or longer, nobody knows. Chapter 1: The Four Elements of the Redemption הודיענו אלהינו על ידי נביאיו שיגאלנו קהל בני ישראל מן העוני אשר אנחנו בו, ויקבץ תפוצותינו ממזרח וממערב מצפון ומים, ויביאנו אל עיר קדשו וישכיננו בו, ונהיה סגולתו ונחלתו Hashem has told us through all His prophets that the redemption will bring four things: Relief from our suffering. \"He will redeem us from the affliction we are in.\" Since the destruction of the Second Temple in 70 CE we have been through almost 2,000 years of pogroms, holocausts and every kind of terrible situation. The first purpose of the redemption is to save us from that. The ingathering. He will gather us from east and west, north and south. Jews have ended up in every part of the world: \"that's part of our nature.\" To the Land. He will bring us to His holy city, Yerushalayim and Eretz Yisrael, and settle us there. We will be His segulah and His inheritance. What is a segulah ? The phrase עם סגולה comes from Parashat Yitro, just before the revelation at Sinai: והייתם לי סגולה מכל העמים . It is often translated \"chosen people,\" and its meaning is debated among the Rishonim. Saadia seems to hold that it is a peculiar and unique quality of Am Yisrael that will be expressed in Eretz Yisrael . We are an am segulah now, but it is not yet manifest. So even if every Jew made aliyah, and the country thrived economically and militarily, that would fulfil points two and three. But \"we still have the pressures and we're not sovereign and we still have the enemies around us. That's not number four yet.\" The fourth point is when it is clear to the world around us that we are God's people , \"and that's not clear at the moment, unfortunately.\" The sources There are many, but Saadia brings Zechariah: הנני מושיע את עמי מארץ מזרח ומארץ מבוא השמש, והבאתי אתם ושכנו בתוך ירושלים (Zechariah 8:7-8) \"I will save My people\" (relief), \"from the land of the east and the land of the setting sun\" (ingathering), \"and I will bring them, and they will dwell in Yerushalayim\" (the land). The prophets, he says, expanded on this until they filled many books. And it goes back to Moshe. \"I don't want you to think this is just something from the later prophets.\" The very first prophet already promised it: ושב ה׳ אלהיך את שבותך ורחמך, ושב וקבצך מכל העמים אשר הפיצך ה׳ אלהיך שמה. אם יהיה נדחך בקצה השמים משם יקבצך ה׳ אלהיך ומשם יקחך. והביאך ה׳ אלהיך אל הארץ (Devarim 30:3-5) So redemption is not only that we will live good lives and be saved from the attacks we suffer. \"It's more than that, it's the brit , it's the covenant with the land.\" Our sovereignty and military strength in the land are an essential part of it. \"Again, I'm not saying we are in the messianic times... No one has the ability, I strongly believe, to say when.\" Not Philosophy but Tradition והסתכלתי בענין הזה בדרך עיון... Saadia notes that this subject, unlike the earlier essays, cannot be argued philosophically . When he wrote about God, creation from nothing and free will, he brought rational arguments, and that was the whole thrust of those essays. Here he does not, because Mashiach is a matter of tradition . There is one exception, which he will reach later: his philosophical arguments against those who believe Mashiach has already come . Still, from a traditional perspective the redemption is necessary, for four reasons . 1. The prophets' signs will be fulfilled Moshe made many signs and promised the redemption. So did Yeshayahu, Yirmiyahu and the other prophets. The God who sent them and gave them those signs will certainly fulfil their words: מקים דבר עבדו ועצת מלאכיו ישלים (Yeshayahu 44:26) 2. God is just As established in Essay 2, Hashem is perfect and does no injustice. \"And look, this is Rav Saadia writing this a thousand years ago. This is before the Holocaust in Germany, before the pogroms in Ukraine, before the blood libels in Syria and in Iraq.\" Even then, he says, we had suffered a very long time. Without doubt, part of our suffering is punishment and part of it is a test . But either way, each has a limit . A punishment or a test cannot go on forever. When it ends, we must be healed and rewarded: כי נרצה עונה, כי לקחה מיד ה׳ כפלים בכל חטאתיה (Yeshayahu 40:2) Saadia is speaking here about our national history. Justice means that the time must come when Hashem says, \"That's it. Now it's time to reward you.\" 3. God is faithful to His own promises Leaving aside what He promised through the prophets, Hashem Himself has promised, and He keeps His word: יבש חציר נבל ציץ, ודבר אלהינו יקום לעולם (Yeshayahu 40:8) Everything physical withers and fades, but the word of Hashem is everlasting. 4. Compare it with Egypt The first promise Hashem ever made us was about Egypt, told to Avraham in a single line: וגם את הגוי אשר יעבדו דן אנכי, ואחרי כן יצאו ברכש גדול (Bereishit 15:14) That was all. And look what we saw: the plagues, the splitting of the sea, the manna and the quail, the revelation at Sinai, the sun standing still for Yehoshua. All the more so for the final redemption, about which we have been promised so much more, and which will be at least double the first, to match all the affliction we have been through: תחת בשתכם משנה, וכלמה ירנו חלקם, לכן בארצם משנה יירשו (Yeshayahu 61:7) The prophet even calls all our suffering, in the grand scheme of things, a small moment : \"I know we've been through so much, two millennia of suffering nationally,\" but the redemption will be far greater: ברגע קטן עזבתיך, וברחמים גדלים אקבצך (Yeshayahu 54:7) והיטבך והרבך מאבתיך (Devarim 30:5) This, the Rabbi explained, is why the prophets so often compare the end of days to Egypt. We were stuck there as a nation, and Hashem took us out. \"Yes, there were certain failures, with the spies, with Korach and so on. But look at the whole picture\": the revelation, the land, the conquest, hundreds of years of sovereignty. The end will be greater still: כימי צאתך מארץ מצרים אראנו נפלאות (Michah 7:15) That is why we are always told to remember Egypt: it is the beginning of our redemption, and a reminder that it will happen again. Waiting Without Despair ועל כן אנחנו סובלים ומיחלים... לא נקוץ ולא נכסוף, אך נוסיף חזק ואמץ Therefore, says Saadia, we wait patiently. We do not grow frustrated or angry, but add strength and courage, as it says: חזקו ויאמץ לבבכם כל המיחלים לה׳ . \"And again, this is him writing a thousand years ago.\" A thousand years on we still do not have the full redemption. \"Certainly the State of Israel is a partial redemption, because we have our land back,\" though we are not yet fully sovereign. Why do we look like fools? Other nations look at us and wonder: what is this nation waiting for? When someone comes to convert, the Gemara tells us to ask him why he would want to join a nation that is oppressed, belittled and scorned, \"and still we say this till today.\" The Rabbi called it \"a historical fact, not a tradition or belief\": no other nation has been through so much suffering and oppression. The onlookers must think we are fools for holding on to our faith. Saadia says they think so because they have not been through what we have been through, and have not seen what we have seen. He gives two analogies. The seed. Someone who has never seen wheat grow watches a man throw seed into a furrow in the ground and thinks he is a fool. He does not realise that he himself is the fool, until the crop comes back thirty-fold. הזרעים בדמעה ברנה יקצרו (Tehillim 126:5) \"Beautiful verse. Those who sow with tears will reap with song.\" We have planted with all the tears our people have shed through the generations, \"and especially recently,\" and we will harvest with joy. The child. Someone who has never seen children brought up watches a parent bear all the toil of raising a son and asks: what can he possibly hope to gain from this? \"Just think about how much effort, those who are parents, we put in for our children, from when they're babies all the way through.\" Then the child grows, becomes wise, rises to some great position, and the onlooker realises how wrong he was. That is why the redemption is compared to the birth of a son: בטרם יבוא חבל לה והמליטה זכר . You go through the pain, and at the end there is a child who brings you happiness. Tears and harvest today The Rabbi applied this, carefully, to the present. \"God forbid to put the 7th of October into any kind of context, because of the suffering of the families and the people who died and were injured, and still now the hostages who are still there, the fifty hostages. May HaKadosh Baruch Hu bring them out soon.\" But almost two years on, we can look back at that terrible day, when we asked what good could possibly come from it, and see something emerging: the defeat of Israel's enemies, Hamas, Hezbollah, Syria, and now Iran. \"We planted in tears, and now, be'ezrat Hashem , we'll see the full sovereignty of Am Yisrael, may it be in our days.\" Chapter 1 in short The purpose of the redemption: relief from oppression, the ingathering, the ingathering to Eretz Yisrael, and there being manifest as God's unique nation. The proofs are from tradition, not philosophy: the prophets' words, God's justice, God's own promise, and the comparison with Egypt. Encouragement: do not despair. Like seed in the ground and a child being raised, what looks like waste brings joy at the end. Chapter 2, Part 1: Of Course He Can Chapter 2 continues the encouragement, and then turns to the question of when . The Rabbi noted that Saadia has not yet used the word Mashiach at all. He calls the essay Ge'ulah , redemption. The person who will usher it in, and the fact that Saadia literally holds there will be two Mashiachs , comes later. Saadia now brings four images: One for whom the heavens are measured by a span : how could it be hard for Him to send us the redemption? One for whom all the waters are like the spread of a palm : how could it be hard for Him to gather our scattered ones from among them? One for whom all the dust of the earth is a measured thing : how could it not be easy for Him to gather its exiles? One for whom the mountains are weighed on a scale: how could it not be near for Him to rebuild our holy mountain? Why these four? Because they come straight from the haftarah of Shabbat Nachamu, read after Tishah b'Av: מי מדד בשעלו מים, ושמים בזרת תכן, וכל בשלש עפר הארץ, ושקל בפלס הרים וגבעות במאזנים (Yeshayahu 40:12) No human can measure the waters, the heavens, the dust and the mountains, \"even nowadays.\" If Hashem can, He can certainly bring the redemption. Saadia continues with two more: For Him all the nations are like drops from a bucket : how would He not humble them before us? הן גוים כמר מדלי וכשחק מאזנים נחשבו (Yeshayahu 40:15). For Him shaking the wicked off the earth is like gathering up a sheet and shaking it out: לאחז בכנפות הארץ וינערו רשעים ממנה (Iyov 38:13). \"I could have just said,\" Saadia writes, \"that one who created all this from nothing\" can certainly redeem us, since creation is far greater than redemption. But he chose to point to the simple images in the verses themselves. He has not forgotten us It is forbidden to think that Hashem does not know our situation, or that He will not act for us, or that He has no mercy, or that He cannot save us and does not hear our prayers. The Rabbi put it in the language of omnipotence, omnibenevolence and omniscience: all three are behind the promise. למה תאמר יעקב ותדבר ישראל נסתרה דרכי מה׳ (Yeshayahu 40:27) הן לא קצרה יד ה׳ מהושיע (Yeshayahu 59:1) כי אל רחום ה׳ אלהיך, לא ירפך ולא ישחיתך (Devarim 4:31) \"Sometimes we feel we pray and we don't get answered. Never think they're not answered.\" And unlike what other religions have claimed about Am Yisrael, we have never been forsaken. The Rabbi spoke personally here. \"Especially over the past week, how HaKadosh Baruch Hu never forsakes us. I think we're living through miracles, honestly.\" People say that if Hashem did miracles today, as in the old days, they would believe. \"If you can't see how a tiny, tiny nation, so small, surrounded by enemies, has been able to destroy one after the other all of them, you'll never see the hand of Hashem.\" Chapter 2, Part 2: Two Possible Times for the Redemption Now Saadia begins a theme that runs over the next few chapters. אבל הדבר שאנחנו מאמינים בו, הוא שם לשעבודנו שני זמנים: אחד מהם זמן התשובה, והשני זמן הקץ, ואיזה מהם שיקדים תבוא הגאולה Our exile has two possible ends : The time of teshuvah. The redemption can come at any time, based on our conduct. There are many Midrashim to this effect, such as the famous one that if all Israel kept Shabbat, the redemption would come. The time of the ketz , the fixed end time, whatever it is. Saadia will discuss when that is in the coming chapters. Whichever comes first, the redemption comes. If our teshuvah is complete, the ketz does not matter. It will be as Parashat Nitzavim says: when all these things come upon you, the blessing and the curse, ושבת עד ה׳ אלהיך ושמעת בקלו , you return to Hashem and listen to His voice, ושב ה׳ אלהיך את שבותך ורחמך . If our teshuvah falls short, we remain until the ketz . But what about those who deserve it now? How is it fair to the people who have done full teshuvah? Saadia answers that, as with every collective calamity (famine, sword, plague), some are being punished and some are being tested , and those who are tested will be rewarded later. In the Flood there were young children who died. They will be rewarded. In Egypt , Saadia has no doubt there were many righteous people who suffered under the bondage until the redemption came. So nobody can say to us, \"If there were righteous people among you, you would have been redeemed.\" Saadia points to Moshe, Aharon and Miriam, who were certainly righteous. (\"Well, not Moshe, but Aharon and Miriam did at least\" live through the bondage.) They went through the exile in Egypt, and the redemption did not come for them until its time. Redemption is national, not individual. Many of us may deserve it, but it comes to the nation as a whole. The source: Sanhedrin רבי אליעזר אומר: אם ישראל עושין תשובה נגאלין, ואם לאו אין נגאלין. אמר לו רבי יהושע: אם אין עושין תשובה אין נגאלין? אלא הקדוש ברוך הוא מעמיד להן מלך שגזרותיו קשות כהמן, וישראל עושין תשובה ומחזירן למוטב (Sanhedrin 97b) Rabbi Eliezer says redemption depends on teshuvah. Rabbi Yehoshua asks: if they never do teshuvah, will it never come? Rather, Hashem will raise up a king with decrees as harsh as Haman's, and Israel will do teshuvah. The Gemara goes on to reconcile two phrases in one verse, בעתה (\"in its time\") and אחישנה (\"I will hasten it\"): זכו, אחישנה. לא זכו, בעתה. If they merit it, I will hasten it. If not, it will come in its time. Teshuvah will happen either way The Rabbi drew out a point Saadia makes later: teshuvah is going to have to happen. If we do it of our own free will, the redemption will come now, nationally. If not, then in the prelude to the ketz we will be forced into teshuvah by suffering. That is where the wars of Gog uMagog come in, which Saadia will discuss. \"I don't know if you've seen, there have been clips of secular Israeli presenters saying Tehillim on TV and recognising the hand of Hashem. This is what we mean when we say forced into teshuvah. We're being forced into teshuvah now, and be'ezrat Hashem , may our nation recognise that, all of us across the world, as soon as possible, and then redemption will come of its own accord.\" Summary Structure of the book: Essays 1-2 on God, 3-6 on the human being and God, 7-9 on the end of days. Essay 8 is the redemption in this world. Four elements of the redemption: relief from suffering, ingathering, return to Eretz Yisrael, and being manifest there as Hashem's segulah . Promised by all the prophets, beginning with Moshe. Four traditional reasons it must come: the prophets' words, God's justice (punishment and test must end), God's own promise, and the precedent of Egypt, which the final redemption will at least double. Waiting with courage: like seed sown in tears and a child raised with toil, what looks foolish to onlookers ends in joy. He can and He will: the One who measures the waters, heavens, earth and mountains can redeem us. He has not forgotten or forsaken us. Two times for the redemption: through teshuvah, at any time, or at the fixed ketz . Redemption is national; and before the ketz we will be brought to teshuvah one way or another. Coming Up Next week, at the usual time of 6 o'clock: Chapters 3 and 4 , on the ketz , what it is and what will happen then, before Saadia turns to the details of Mashiach himself. The Rambam shiur the following evening was moved to 8:30 to 9:30.","EWYQILcLSyg":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 7 (The Resurrection of the Dead), the end of Chapter 8 and Chapter 9, with a summary of the whole essay Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 9 June 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. The Plan for Tonight The last class on the seventh essay, on תחיית המתים . The Rabbi set out three tasks: Finish Chapters 8 and 9 : the last four of Saadia's ten questions about resurrection, and his closing chapter. Give a synopsis of Saadia's whole approach to the end of days. Compare it with the other approaches among the Rishonim on how the three great promises fit together: משיח , תחיית המתים and עולם הבא . \"Of course, there are more small details such as Eliyahu HaNavi, the Beit HaMikdash, but I'm not going to go into that.\" The focus is on how those three come together. Recap: The First Six Questions In Chapter 8 Saadia works through ten questions people ask about resurrection. Last week covered the first six: There are two resurrections. One happens in this world and ushers in the Messianic era. It is reserved for the Jewish people, \"for those who have been through the galut , those who have suffered in exile.\" The second comes with עולם הבא , when everyone else who merits it, including the righteous of the nations, is resurrected for eternity. Those resurrected in this world do not die again. After a period of a few hundred years they are transported straight into the new world of Olam HaBa. \"There's no death again.\" There will be enough space. Saadia \"did all the maths\" and concluded there is plenty of room. We will recognise our loved ones and relatives. People rise with their physical blemishes so that they can be recognised. Once everyone has recognised one another, they are miraculously healed. Life goes on as normal after the first resurrection: people eat, drink, marry and have children. Question Seven: How Do We Pass into a World Without Eating? איך יחיה את כל עולם הבא שאין בו אכילה ולא בעילה וכל הנאות שנהגו בהם בעולם הזה Everyone agrees on what the Gemara says: העולם הבא אין בו לא אכילה ולא שתיה , in Olam HaBa there is no eating and no drinking. But according to Saadia, the first resurrection happens in this world. Someone dies today, Mashiach comes tomorrow, and he is resurrected. Now he eats, drinks and marries. Then he is to be transported, with his body, into a world with no eating, no drinking and no procreation. How does a body that has grown used to all this suddenly do without it? Saadia answers: just like Moshe Rabbeinu. כמו שנהג משה רבנו עליו השלום... ועמד בלעדיהם ארבעים יום בהר סיני שלשה פעמים בחייו Moshe was a human being. He ate, he drank, he was married and had children. Yet three times in his life, for forty days each time (120 days in all), he went without these things. The day before he went up the mountain he was eating and drinking, and then on the mountain the Torah says plainly: לחם לא אכלתי ומים לא שתיתי , \"I did not eat bread and I did not drink water.\" So it is possible to have a body and be free of its needs. In the same way, those living in the Messianic world will be carried over into Olam HaBa. \"We will refine this more when we see the Ramchal's approach, but remember this point.\" Question Eight: Can the Resurrected Still Sin? This, said the Rabbi, \"is really interesting.\" כיון שהמחויים בימי הישועה הם מונחים לבחירתם בעבודה Saadia takes it as a certainty that there will be free choice during the time of resurrection . That raises a danger. A righteous person dies yesterday and is resurrected today. This world goes on, and so does free will. What if he now makes the wrong choices and loses the real reward, the גמול of Olam HaBa? Resurrection could turn out to be bad for him. Saadia's answer: God knows in advance לא הבטיח הצדיקים שבעולם הבא גמולם מתמיד אלא בידיעתו שהם בוחרים בעבודה ולא במרי. כן אמר אחר שיודע מה שיהיה, לא הבטיח תחיית המתים צדיקי ישראל עד שידע כי בימות המשיח יבחרו בעבודה ולא במרי God knows the future. (Saadia's way of reconciling free will with God's foreknowledge was covered in Essay 4.) He only resurrects, and only gives Olam HaBa to, those He knows will make the right choices in the time of resurrection and in Olam HaBa itself. Free will in Olam HaBa itself? \"I went over this a few times trying to make sure I understood this correctly, because I'm so used to the other understanding.\" The striking point is that Saadia answers by comparing the resurrected to those already in Olam HaBa . In other words, Saadia holds that even in Olam HaBa there is still an element of free will. \"This may sound shocking to you.\" The usual narrative, the way the Rambam puts it \"black and white,\" the way the Ramchal portrays it, and the way we are generally taught, is that this world is the world of work and of choice, and choice comes to an end in Olam HaBa. \"If I can say, it makes the most sense.\" But Saadia says otherwise. Where might he get this from? The Rabbi could not find a direct source, but pointed to something along these lines: The verse we say before Minchah: ילכו מחיל אל חיל יראה אל אלהים בציון , \"they go from strength to strength.\" The Gemara on the last page of Berachot: the righteous have no rest, not in this world and not in the World to Come . There are different understandings of that Gemara, but Saadia probably read it to mean that work does not end in Olam HaBa . There is continuous elevation, because there are always choices to make. \"For me, I find this very, very difficult to understand. What is the purpose of having continuous bechirah ? Surely the purpose of free will is to be able to make choices for a limited period of time, but then there comes a time when choice comes to an end, and now we reap the consequences of our choices.\" What everyone agrees on: free will in the days of Mashiach The Rabbi stressed that almost everyone after Saadia disagrees with him about Olam HaBa: there is a time for bechirah in this world, and then it ends. But the first half of what Saadia says is agreed: in the days of Mashiach there will still be free choice. Here he brought Rav Dessler , who has an essay on this. \"Perhaps we're just anachronising it from Rav Dessler to Saadia,\" but it may be what Saadia meant. In the Messianic era, free will will no longer be a choice between good and bad, but between good and better. There will be no temptation to do wrong, because ומלאה הארץ דעה את ה׳ , the land will be full of the knowledge of Hashem. But how I do the good, to what extent, with what intention: all of that is still free choice. The Rabbi compared this to what the sefarim write about Adam HaRishon before the sin . How could he sin without a yetzer hara ? One idea is that the choice to do bad existed, but it was external to him. By sinning he brought it inside, which made it a much greater difficulty. So in Messianic times there will be bechirah , \"no longer between bad and good, but between good and excellent,\" and those choices will have consequences for one's portion in Olam HaBa. Once we reach Olam HaBa itself, the general consensus is that choice is over. Saadia held otherwise, \"which is fascinating.\" Question Nine: Is There Reward for Service in the Days of Mashiach? אם יש להם על עבודה היא בימות המשיח גמול Yes, \"100%.\" Just as the righteous are rewarded for their service in this world, so they will be in the days of Mashiach. It is not possible to have service without reward. And whatever good is done in the Messianic era will be added to one's earlier merits and rewarded in Olam HaBa. Free will continues in the days of Mashiach, and so does reward for how we use it. Question Ten: What About Those Who Are Alive When It Happens? A very specific question. Mashiach comes, the resurrection begins, and everyone of the last few thousand years is resurrected. \"May we see it in our days.\" It works for those who were dead. But what about those alive at the time , and those born during the Messianic era ? Remember, in those days people eat, drink and have children. Do they have to die and come back to life? Saadia says there is no tradition on this . Nothing explicit comes from the Hachamim, so there are three approaches: Approach 1 They will never die , because there is no more death; they simply carry on living 2 They will die and come back to life , to be equal with those who died and rose 3 שיחיו שנים רבות וימותו וילכו לעולם הבא : they will live many years, then die, and go on to Olam HaBa Saadia prefers the third, \"which I like\" too. Unlike the resurrected, who never die again, those who were alive will die, and they will not get a second resurrection in this world. They will be resurrected only once, for Olam HaBa. The reason: resurrection is the reward of exile Saadia explains why: the promise of resurrection in the redemption was made only to those who went through the exile. Those who suffered \"the death of exile\" are owed a resurrection in this world. If you never went through that, there is no need for one. You live through the days of Mashiach, and when your time comes you die, and you are resurrected for the final time in Olam HaBa. Very long lives Saadia stresses that their lives will be very long, perhaps four or five hundred years, so that dying at a hundred will be like dying young: כי הנער בן מאה שנה ימות והחוטא בן מאה שנה יקולל (Yeshayahu 65:20) The Rabbi drew out the second half. Normally, if a hundred-year-old does something foolish, we overlook it because he is an old man. In the days of Mashiach, a hundred-year-old who sins will be told off, because at a hundred he is still a youth. Life spans will be like those of the early generations: לא יבנו ואחר ישב לא יטעו ואחר יאכל כי כימי העץ ימי עמי ומעשה ידיהם יבלו בחירי (Yeshayahu 65:22) That is the end of Chapter 8. Chapter 9: Six Reasons to Hold On to This Belief The final chapter is very short. Saadia explains why he spent so long on resurrection and wrote it all down: he wanted it to be a teaching for Israel, and he saw great benefit in it. He gives six reasons: It is one of the Creator's greatest signs. Seeing it will increase our trust in Hashem's ability. All the prophets will be gathered together , with the righteous kings and the great Hachamim. \"What would we give to see one prophet now? If I could give you an hour with Moshe Rabbeinu, an hour with Yeshayahu, an hour with Rashi, an hour with the Rambam... we would go crazy about that. You're going to see them all together.\" We will be reunited with our relatives : the son with his father, a brother with his brother, a lover with his beloved, and the student with his Rabbi. \"Those are the four loves.\" \"I'll see my students again, my parents again, my children again, my siblings again. It's beautiful.\" Those who come back will tell us what happened to them in the time they were dead. \"It's going to be fascinating to know.\" All the generations of Bnei Yisrael will come together, in great splendour, and each generation will speak of its own. It strengthens our belief in Olam HaBa. Once we have seen this promise fulfilled, we can trust that all the later promises will be fulfilled too: ביום ההוא... הנה אלהינו זה קוינו לו ויושיענו , \"on that day it will be said: this is our God, we hoped for Him and He saved us.\" Saadia ends with a personal prayer: that he will merit to be among those who see it, either alive in his own lifetime or, at the very least, by being resurrected to see it. And he blesses God, who is faithful to keep His promises. Saadia's Full Picture of the End of Days With the essay finished, the Rabbi summarised Saadia's eschatology \"in a few sentences.\" Saadia expected it soon. In a few places in the essay you can see that he felt it could come today or tomorrow. When he calculated how much space would be needed, he only counted the dead up to his own generation. \"This is a thousand years ago. We're waiting.\" The Rabbi added his own inference: Saadia does not say this, but he probably held, with the Gemara, that this world lasts 6,000 years. We are now in 5785, so even if Mashiach came today, the period of the first resurrection would be at most about 215 years. The sequence: Mashiach comes , and with him the first resurrection, for the righteous of Bnei Yisrael. The resurrected, and those of us alive at the time, eat, drink, marry, have children and make choices . There is bechirah , and life continues as usual. For the resurrected it is a very long life, and for the living a long one too. Those alive when Mashiach came eventually die, and are resurrected later into Olam HaBa. Those who were resurrected live on until the end of this world and are transported directly into Olam HaBa, with no second death. Then the real Olam HaBa begins : a new world , which does not yet exist. The righteous of the nations of all the generations are resurrected into it, and everyone lives together for eternity, body and soul . For Saadia, then, resurrection is in a sense synonymous with Olam HaBa . The second resurrection begins the new world. The Messianic era is a temporary stage, with choice, reward and consequences, and it is a prelude: \"slowly warming us up\" for the real thing. The Rambam: Three Separate Things \"The Rambam's approach is extremely different.\" For the Rambam, Mashiach is one thing, resurrection is another, and Olam HaBa is another. They cannot be equated. Olam HaBa is the pinnacle , what everything is for. It is a state of pure consciousness, the soul and nothing else. No body, no physicality, no eating, no drinking. It is not a new world somewhere in the future. \"It's when we die, that's Olam HaBa.\" If you die today, you reach Olam HaBa today. Resurrection is a bodily resurrection, but in this world and for a limited time. In the Messianic era, those who died will be brought back. They will eat, drink and have children, and then die again and return to Olam HaBa. The Rambam wrote, in his letter on resurrection, that people drive him mad with questions about what will happen when Mashiach comes: will it be like this, will it be like that. \"Maybe he was referring to Saadia.\" He would not get involved. Everyone wants to know whether money will grow on trees in the days of Mashiach, or, as the Gemara says, clothes growing on trees. \"That's not the important thing.\" He wished people would ask about Olam HaBa, which is what really matters. The Abarbanel's question The Abarbanel already raises the major difficulty with the Rambam. If the pinnacle is the pure spiritual consciousness of Olam HaBa, why bring someone out of it to spend another hundred or two hundred years in this world? For Saadia it makes sense: the Messianic era is a warm-up for the real thing. For the Rambam it is a step backwards. \"That's very difficult. I don't have an answer to that. I'm not sure if there is an answer to that question.\" The Ramban: Resurrection Leads into Olam HaBa The Ramban challenged the Rambam with a number of proofs. The Rabbi gave one. The Mishnah says כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא , except for certain people, the first being one who denies the resurrection of the dead . Why should denying one belief cost a person his Olam HaBa? The Gemara answers: הוא כפר בתחיית המתים, לפיכך לא יהיה לו חלק בתחיית המתים, שכל מדותיו של הקדוש ברוך הוא מדה כנגד מדה (Sanhedrin 90a) Measure for measure: you denied resurrection, so you will not have a portion in it, and so you are excluded from Olam HaBa. The Ramban concludes that resurrection and Olam HaBa are bound up together : resurrection ushers in the world to come. Gan Eden: the waiting room The Ramban takes on Saadia's approach and refines it, and the Kabbalists and the Ramchal (Rav Moshe Chaim Luzzatto) follow him. If Olam HaBa is a world that does not yet exist, where is someone who dies today? He is in גן עדן , also called עולם הנשמות , the world of souls. It is a temporary place, a taste ( me'ein ) of what is to come, but only for the soul , which waits for resurrection. Why is that not the real thing? Because, as the Ramban writes, and many echoed after him, the body did the work in this world, so the body must receive the reward as well. The real Olam HaBa has to be body and soul together, in the new world that will be created. The proof from an invitation The Gemara often says of a person who did teshuvah that a heavenly voice declared him מזומן לחיי העולם הבא , \"invited to the life of the World to Come.\" According to the Rambam, you are not invited: when you die, you are there. According to the Ramban this phrase is a proof. \"You got an invitation for a wedding in a few months' time. You don't have it yet. It hasn't come yet.\" The Ramchal and Rabbeinu Bachya The Ramchal goes further still. He accepts the whole sequence of Saadia and the Ramban: you die, you are in the world of souls, you wait for Mashiach, then there is resurrection, then Olam HaBa begins, body and soul together. But, coming back to Moshe on the mountain, there will be phases in Olam HaBa : continuous growth and elevation from level to level. That is what ילכו מחיל אל חיל means. Rabbeinu Bachya takes the same approach as the Ramban and Saadia. The Rabbi read his presentation. The Rambam's opinion is that the resurrected will eat, drink, have children and live long lives, and then die and begin Olam HaBa, since there can be no bodily functions in the next world. Rabbeinu Bachya responds: לא כן דעת חכמי התלמוד והקבלה That is not the view of the Sages of the Talmud and the Kabbalah. In their view there is no death after resurrection. And it must be body and soul together: just as they both strove to keep the mitzvot and toiled in them, so they will be rewarded together, for אין הקדוש ברוך הוא מקפח שכר כל בריה , Hashem does not withhold the reward of any creature. Many Rishonim followed this approach , including the Rashba and the Ritva . Others followed the Rambam. The Abarbanel brings both opinions and says he prefers the Rambam's. The Dispute Is Smaller Than It Looks The Rabbi ended with one point: \"the machloket we're making is much bigger than it really is.\" Even Saadia and the Ramban, who believe in a new world with a body in it, must accept the Gemara that in Olam HaBa there is no eating and no drinking . So what kind of body is it? The Ramban and Rabbeinu Bachya explain that the body is there, because it must receive its reward, but it is secondary to the soul . Just like Moshe on the mountain: he was not divested of his body, \"it's not like he died,\" but the body had no more desire to eat or drink. It was totally subdued to the soul. The Ramchal develops this into phases. At first the body is there and you sense its pull, but it is subdued, \"like a guest in someone's house\": maybe he wants to do something wrong, but he can't, he's a guest. Then it rises higher, to where the temptation is gone altogether, and higher still. So according to the Ramban there is a body, according to Saadia there is a body, according to the Ramchal there is a body, but it is a refined body (גוף זך) . \"You can't compare it to our body now.\" No eating, no drinking, no excretion. \"A body which just follows the soul.\" The Rambam says pure soul. There remain major differences between the Rambam and Saadia: whether Olam HaBa is a new world or what happens when you die, and whether there is death after resurrection. But on what we will be like in Olam HaBa, body and soul or soul alone, \"the difference between those two opinions is actually very minute,\" because even for those who say body and soul, it will not be the body we know. Summary Question 7: the resurrected will pass into a world with no eating or drinking just as Moshe lived on the mountain without them, three times for forty days. Question 8: there will be free will in the days of Mashiach , and God only resurrects those He knows will choose well. Saadia even holds there is free will in Olam HaBa , which almost everyone after him rejects. Rav Dessler: in Messianic times the choice is between good and better. Question 9: there is reward for service in the days of Mashiach. Question 10: those alive when Mashiach comes will live very long lives, die, and be resurrected only for Olam HaBa, because the first resurrection is the reward of those who suffered exile . Chapter 9: six reasons the belief matters, from seeing Hashem's power to meeting the prophets, our families and all the generations, to strengthening our faith in Olam HaBa. Saadia: Mashiach and the first resurrection are a prelude in this world; Olam HaBa is a new world of body and soul . Followed by the Ramban, Rabbeinu Bachya, the Ramchal, the Rashba and the Ritva. The Rambam: Olam HaBa is the soul alone , reached at death; resurrection is temporary and in this world. The Abarbanel prefers it, but asks why anyone would be brought back. The gap is smaller than it seems: the body of Olam HaBa, for those who hold there is one, is a refined body wholly subdued to the soul. Coming Up That concludes the seventh essay. Three essays remain: Essay 8 on Mashiach , Essay 9 on Olam HaBa (reward and punishment), and Essay 10 on musar , general conduct and behaviour. There is no shiur next week. Essay 8 begins in two weeks' time, on Monday at the usual time of 6 o'clock, with the aim of finishing it by the end of the summer.","_1C9UxuFeec":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 7 (The Resurrection of the Dead), Chapter 7 and Chapter 8 (Questions 1 to 6) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 26 May 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Saadia's Thesis So Far Two chapters of the essay remain, plus a very short ninth chapter that is really a conclusion. Next week is Shavuot, with no shiur. After that, a shiur on the different views of techiyat hametim in the Rishonim, \"three main different ways to understand\" it, since \"the permutations of how and when and the details of techiyat hametim are quite different.\" So far Saadia has answered the challenges from nature and from logic , setting out when we may read Tanach non-literally; answered the verses that seem to deny resurrection; and brought the explicit verses that affirm it: Yechezkel, Yeshayahu, Daniel, and Ha'azinu in the Torah itself. The Rabbi looked ahead to Essay 9. There Saadia is the first to ask why Olam HaBa is not explicitly mentioned in Tanach: there are many hints in the Gemara, but nothing explicit. \"But techiyat hametim is black and white in the Torah.\" Saadia's thesis, then, is two resurrections : During the days of Mashiach, in this world , \"whilst we're still eating and drinking and marrying... and living the life of this world.\" Reserved for Am Yisrael. When the world to come begins : a second resurrection for all the righteous of the world. There are sources in the Gemara, the Rabbi noted, for two such stages. Chapter 7: The Chachamim The fourth avenue: is there anything in rabbinic tradition against resurrection? Saadia says no: דבריהם ז\"ל מלאים מזכרה וסיפורה , their words are full of it. (A textual note: the printed text reads \"the tradition of the prophets and the tradition of the Chachamim,\" but the Rabbi said those words are not in the old prints. The prophets belong to the third avenue, Scripture; this avenue is rabbinic tradition.) Saadia mentions only \"a few\" as a souvenir, because there are so many. The Gemara records kings and queens asking the Chachamim about it, such as Cleopatra , who asked whether the dead will rise clothed or unclothed, and our own people asked too, and the Chachamim answered each. Saadia brings four statements. 1. Seven shepherds and eight princes Saadia opens with הנני שלח לכם את אליה הנביא , I am sending you Eliyahu, which he connects to Mashiach, and the verse in Michah : והקמנו עליו שבעה רעים ושמנה נסיכי אדם , we will raise up against him seven shepherds and eight princes of men. Who are they? The Gemara in Sukkah answers, in Saadia's text: Seven shepherds: David in the middle; Shet and Metushelach on his right; Avraham, Yitzchak, Yaakov and Moshe on his left. Eight princes: Yishai, Shaul, Shmuel, Amos, Tzefaniah, Chizkiyahu, Eliyahu and Mashiach . Saadia concludes: הנה אמרו בפירוש שהמתים יעמדו בימי הישועה , they said explicitly that the dead will rise in the days of the salvation. And since Mashiach is one of the eight princes, Mashiach and resurrection \"go hand in hand.\" That is his point: not just that the Chachamim speak of resurrection, but that it comes with the redemption . \"We'll see our shepherds, we'll see Avraham, Yitzchak, Yaakov, and we'll see our princes, we'll see the prophets.\" Textual variants. \"Just in case you're wondering\": the Ben Ish Chai already asks why Shet and Metushelach are on the right and the Avot on the left. And our Gemara's text differs: Adam, Shet and Metushelach on the right, and Avraham, Yaakov and Moshe on the left. Yitzchak is missing, and Rashi brings a Midrash that Yitzchak went to save his children from Gehinnom. The texts vary on whether the list includes Yitzchak or Adam, but the main point stands. 2. The seven years of Gog מי שימות בשבע שני גוג לא יעמוד בתחיית המתים : whoever dies in the seven years of Gog will not rise. The war of Gog and Magog is \"a whole other discussion,\" but it is certainly a prelude to the days of Mashiach: seven years of chaos and upheaval. \"We're not entirely sure who this Gog is. Whenever there's some attack on Israel, everyone rushes to identify Gog as the enemy,\" sometimes Iran. The Gemara's analogy: before a wedding there is a reception, \"a drinks party.\" If you are invited to the drinks party, you will be invited to the wedding; if not, not. So Rabbi Yonah said: whoever dies in the seven years of Gog will not merit to see Mashiach. וסימנך דאכיל פרפרא אכיל סעודתא : whoever eats the appetiser eats the feast. 3. Whoever denies resurrection מי שכפר בתחיית המתים לא יחיה לימות המשיח , even if his deeds are good. This is measure for measure : if you do not believe in something, how can it be given to you? Saadia quotes the Gemara in Sanhedrin , which brings a proof from the officer of Yehoram ben Achav . In the famine, Elisha announced that tomorrow there would be plenty of food. The king's aide, on whose arm the king leaned, scoffed: even if Hashem opened windows in the heavens, could this be? Elisha answered: הנך ראה בעיניך ומשם לא תאכל , you will see it with your own eyes, but you will not eat of it. And so it was. Because he denied Elisha's words, he saw it and did not enjoy it. So too, whoever denies resurrection will not take part in it, שכל מדותיו של הקדוש ברוך הוא מדה כנגד מדה . Hence the Mishnah: כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא , except one who denies techiyat hametim . \"If you deny it, you can't be part of it. Nothing I can do about that.\" The Rabbi applied the same logic to a question people ask: will a good, moral person who does not believe in Hashem have Olam HaBa? \"The answer is no... Yes, he's a great person, he's a moral person and everything. But reality is you don't believe in the world to come, so how can you have a portion in the world to come? ... It's not part of your identity.\" 4. Rising in their shrouds המתים יעמדו בתכריכיהם : the dead will rise in their shrouds, and the tzaddikim in their own clothes . If you wonder how, it is no harder for Hashem to restore their clothes than their bodies. When this spread among the masses, people began to go to great lengths over their burial clothes: \"I want to get the best clothes, so that when I'm resurrected I'll stand up in my fine tuxedo.\" It became a great evil: those who could not afford what their dead had demanded would not bury them, and the burden fell on the community. Until Rabban Gamliel decreed that he himself be buried in two simple linen garments, and everyone followed. To this day, men and women, wealthy or poor, are buried in the same white linen shrouds. Questions on Chapter 7 When was this? Over 1,900 years ago: Rabban Gamliel lived in the 1st century. As head of the Sanhedrin he said, I will be buried in simple white shrouds, and \"I don't know any Jewish community which doesn't bury in white shrouds.\" Will people long dead rise in shrouds that have rotted away? \"If Hashem can bring back the body, says Saadia, He can bring back your shrouds as well. Once you get out in your shrouds, then you'll run home and get your best clothes again.\" With flesh, or like a ghost in a shroud? With flesh, the Rabbi imagined, \"because they're going to be normal human beings for a period of time... who eat, who drink, who marry, have children,\" and for that you need flesh. Flora said she had always thought Rabban Gamliel's decree was simply about expensive coffins and clothes and making everyone equal. \"No, the Gemara says this\": people would not bury their loved ones because of wills demanding the finest clothes. What Saadia sees in these sources All four show rabbinic support for resurrection, \"but more importantly for Saadia,\" the first shows it comes together with Mashiach . His sequence: seven years of upheaval, Mashiach, resurrection for Am Yisrael ; life continues to an extent, with marriage, children, the service of Hashem and free will; and then the move to Olam HaBa. Chapter 8: Ten Questions Having established all this, Saadia turns to the consequences of the belief: ten questions people ask, answered from Scripture, logic and tradition. \"Some are very interesting.\" Tonight covered six. Question 1: Who will be resurrected? כל צדיק ובעל תשובה : every righteous person, and anyone who lived wickedly but repented. Only those who die wicked without teshuvah will not rise, \"and that makes sense logically,\" since Scripture promises many times that Hashem accepts those who return. The Rabbi recalled the famous Gemara on the levels of atonement, touched on earlier in this series. For a positive commandment neglected, teshuvah alone; for a negative prohibition, teshuvah and Yom Kippur; for a prohibition carrying the death penalty, Yom Kippur and some suffering too; and for chillul Hashem , death. Saadia then brings a teaching that one might have thought even death would not atone, and the verse הנה אני פתח את קברותיכם teaches that even one who did the worst sins, if he repented and died, will be resurrected. וזה הנה חייבת תחיית המתים לכל השב : resurrection is assured for every penitent. ואני אומר כמעט הם מבני עמנו שמתים בבלי תשובה : very few of our people die without teshuvah. The Ramchal writes the same \"black and white\" in Derech Hashem: very few will not have Olam HaBa. And Saadia stresses again that this resurrection is for the Jewish people, for those who suffered in exile. Must you be buried in Israel? Saadia never says that resurrection is only for those in Eretz Yisrael. Why do so many people who lived and died abroad want to be buried there? Because of the Gemara in Ketubot , where Rabbi Elazar says that those who die outside the Land will not be resurrected. But the Gemara answers that those buried outside will roll through underground tunnels, מחילות , to Israel. \"Whether that's literal or not, I don't know. I can't imagine it is.\" The Rabbi added that taking bodies to Israel raises a halachic complication: a dead body is tamei , so what gives you the right to bring impurity into the Land? That is why many tzaddikim who lived with their communities in the diaspora asked to be buried there. \"That's a whole discussion.\" Saadia's own view, as Question 3 will show, is that the resurrection takes place in the whole world. Question 2: Will they die again? כי לא ימותו : no. אבל מעתיקים אותם מימות המשיח אל נעימות עולם הבא : they are transferred from the days of Mashiach to the delight of Olam HaBa. As the Chachamim said, the dead whom Hashem revives do not return to their dust. A digression: what kind of body? For Saadia, the ultimate world to come, after Gog, after Eliyahu, after Mashiach and the resurrection, is body and soul together , because the body took part in the service of this world and must benefit. Yet the Gemara says, undisputed, העולם הבא אין בו לא אכילה ולא שתיה and no marital relations. So what is the body for? It will be an elevated body , like Moshe Rabbeinu's . Moshe ate, drank, had a wife and children; yet three times he spent forty days on high learning Torah, without eating or drinking. He still had his body, but it was elevated. Our bodies now are essentially animal bodies, \"we are mammals,\" with impulses we learn to control. In Olam HaBa there will be no impulses. So in Saadia's sequence, you rise in this world with your normal desires and enjoy this life, \"however long it is.\" Then you do not die again: at the end of this world you are transferred, \"how? I've got no idea,\" into the new world, living for eternity in \"a Moshe Rabbeinu kind of body.\" Q: What's the use of a body that has nothing to do? \"That's a very good question, which is why the Rambam denies\" any body in Olam HaBa: for him it is consciousness alone, for exactly that reason. Saadia, and many after him, felt it would be unfair for the body to toil for 120 years alongside the soul and receive no reward. On Yom Kippur your body wants to eat and your mind says no; \"that body worked, it suffered, so how are you going to compensate and remunerate that body?\" Q: Maybe it's an AI body, a robot? \"Who knows? Could be.\" Flora objected that a robot is not truly alive. \"No, actually it makes total sense.\" The Ramchal , as those who learned Da'at Tevunot with him years ago will remember, says something very similar. Now we have urges for good from the soul and for bad from the body, in constant battle; even the greatest tzaddik who never sinned still has the animal urge within him. In Olam HaBa there are levels: first the urge is held in check, as Moshe's bodily needs were suspended for those forty days; and then a higher level where the body is entirely subjugated to the soul. \"The body just tags along... It just follows whatever the soul wants... which is what we would nowadays call a robot.\" Houses and furniture? In the first resurrection, in this world, certainly: \"You'll continue living in your houses.\" In the world to come, \"no idea.\" What will we do there? \"In short, you bask in the divine presence.\" Where is that world? Saadia follows the Gemara of 6,000 years, then a thousand years of desolation, then the new world; the Rambam does not take that Gemara as final. \"Who knows? No one can tell you the answer.\" And that new world will include all the pious of the nations, who do not take part in the first resurrection. Question 3: Will the land hold them all? The Rabbi noted that the Rambam mentions some of these questions, how will it look, will I rise in my clothes, with my blemishes, and his answer is: don't spend your time on them, you'll see when it happens. \"And the Rambam has a very strong point... How does it help to know now?\" But Saadia felt it right to answer them. Saadia's calculation, for the resurrection in this world, which is for the Jewish people: About 2,200 years since the nation was formed, in Saadia's day. That is about 32 generations . Each generation about 1.2 million : the Torah counts 600,000 men leaving Egypt, so double it for men and women. 1.2 million times 32 is 38.4 million people. Even giving each person more than 200 amot , about 100 metres, \"for his plants, for his animal, for his car,\" they would occupy less than 1/150th of the earth. He works through parsah , mil and amah : \"To be honest, I didn't fully understand it, it's quite complex.\" The conclusion: \"don't bother worrying about that. There will be plenty space for everyone.\" The Rabbi drew two inferences. This calculation is a proof that the first resurrection is only for Israel , since he counts only Bnei Yisrael. And since he measures against the whole earth , Saadia clearly holds that it happens throughout the world, not only in Eretz Yisrael. What about skyscrapers? \"You're right actually... He's very clever. With skyscrapers there'll be much more space.\" Maybe we'll all fit in Israel: \"That's why Tel Aviv has so many skyscrapers.\" Question 4: Will they recognise their families? \"This is a question people love asking when they lose a loved one.\" The Rambam would say: we'll find out. Saadia says yes . His proof is the Gemara of the shepherds and princes: to see them and appreciate that they are our shepherds, Moshe and David and Yishai, we will have to know who they are. And Yechezkel describes the land divided tribe by tribe, so we will know our tribe and our people. Just as we will know them, we will surely know our loved ones, living together in the same tribes. \"Provided, of course, your family gets resurrected.\" It is possible that a husband will be resurrected and his wife not, or vice versa, \"however unpleasant that may seem and sound.\" Question 5: Will the blind and the maimed rise with their defects? Yes, at first: שיחיה תחילה במומו כדי שיכירו בני אדם שהוא הוא , he will first rise with his defect so that people recognise him. ואחר כן ירפאהו הבורא : then the Creator will heal him completely. The Gemara in Sanhedrin says the same: עומדים במומן ומתרפאין . Hence Ha'azinu: אני אמית ואחיה מחצתי ואני ארפא : first I bring to life, and then I heal the wounds. \"If a person lived this world without legs and then he gets up walking around, people won't recognise him.\" So first you meet your family and they know you, and then, miraculously, your legs and your eyesight return. Saadia brings Yeshayahu 35: אז תפקחנה עיני עורים ואזני חרשים תפתחנה. אז ידלג כאיל פסח ותרן לשון אלם : the eyes of the blind will open, the ears of the deaf, the lame will leap like a deer, and the tongue of the mute will sing. What age will we be? Saadia does not discuss it. The Rabbi would assume a person's prime, perhaps the twenties and thirties mentioned in Pirkei Avot, read the week before. \"I don't know what age a person will come back.\" A participant preferred coming back aged ten, with life ahead. \"We'll see what happens... if we'll live to see it.\" Then you'd be too young for a husband or wife, another said, which leads to the next question. Question 6: Will they eat, drink and marry? ודאי כן : certainly. Like the son of the woman of Tzarfat , revived by Eliyahu , and the son of the Shunamit , revived by Elisha : אכלו ושתו וחיו עד זמנם , they ate and drank and lived out their time, and likely married. Saadia understands that those boys truly died and were revived; the Rabbi recalled that one of the Rishonim reads them as merely unconscious. So too us: when we are resurrected we will eat, drink, marry and have children. Summary Chapter 7: four rabbinic statements 1. Seven shepherds and eight princes (Sukkah), including Mashiach: so the dead rise with the redemption . 2. Whoever dies in the seven years of Gog will not rise: whoever eats the appetiser eats the feast. 3. Whoever denies resurrection will not share in it, measure for measure, like the officer who saw the plenty but did not eat. 4. The dead rise in their shrouds , and the tzaddikim in their clothes, which led to Rabban Gamliel's simple linen shrouds, still the universal custom. Chapter 8: the first six of ten questions 1. Who rises? Every tzaddik and every penitent; very few of Israel will be left out. Burial in Israel is not required for Saadia. 2. Will they die again? No. They pass straight into Olam HaBa in an elevated, \"Moshe Rabbeinu\" body, which the Ramchal describes as one that simply follows the soul. The Rambam, for this very reason, has no body there at all. 3. Is there room? 38.4 million people would fill less than 1/150th of the earth. 4. Will we know our families? Yes, as we will know the shepherds and princes. 5. Defects? We rise with them so we can be recognised, then are healed: \"I strike and I heal.\" 6. Eating, drinking and marriage? Certainly, like the children revived by Eliyahu and Elisha. Coming Up No shiur next week because of Shavuot. Next time: the remaining questions, how we move to Olam HaBa , whether there will still be free choice , and more, finishing the essay. Then a shiur surveying the views of the Rishonim, including the Rambam's approach, on techiyat hametim .","4iMxyHzuGeg":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 7 (The Resurrection of the Dead), Chapters 4, 5 and 6 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 19 May 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Where the Essay Stands Three \"fairly short chapters and quite quick to go through\" tonight. Two resurrections: one at the end, ushering in Olam HaBa; and one just for Israel, in this world . Four avenues of challenge : Nature (Chapter 1): it is not a natural impossibility; Hashem can do it. Logic (Chapter 2): it is not impossible for lack of material. Nor may we explain the resurrection verses as metaphor, because there are only four times the Torah may be read non-literally: when it contradicts the senses, contradicts logic (Hashem is not literally אש אוכלה , a consuming fire), contradicts another verse, or contradicts tradition (the Torah says forty lashes; the tradition, halachah leMoshe miSinai , says 39, so the forty is rounded off). Scripture (Chapter 3): Saadia explained each verse that seemed to say we go to dust and do not return. Tradition : next week, Chapter 7. In Chapters 4, 5 and 6, Saadia brings verses that positively show techiyat hametim . In the other edition of the essay, the Rabbi noted, Saadia says why: there were many Jews who believed in resurrection at the end of days for Olam HaBa, but not necessarily in a resurrection in this world . So his emphasis in Chapter 4 is on showing that it happens in this world. Chapter 4: Three Prophets ואחר כן מצאתי כתוב מפורש מה שייעד לו בוראנו : now I bring explicit verses in which our Creator promised it. Three: from Yechezkel, Yeshayahu and Daniel. 1. Yechezkel 37: \"I will open your graves\" The people complained, יבשו עצמותינו ואבדה תקותנו נגזרנו לנו : our bones are dried up, our hope is lost, we are doomed. Hashem tells Yechezkel to prophesy: כה אמר ה' אלקים הנה אני פתח את קברותיכם והעליתי אתכם מקברותיכם עמי והבאתי אתכם אל אדמת ישראל. וידעתם כי אני ה' בפתחי את קברותיכם ובהעלותי אתכם מקברותיכם עמי. ונתתי רוחי בכם וחייתם והנחתי אתכם על אדמתכם... The Rabbi drew out three points: A: there is a techiyat hametim . B: עמי , \"My people\": there is a resurrection reserved for Bnei Yisrael. C: על אדמת ישראל : it happens in this world, \"when we have a country called Israel.\" The resurrection that ushers in the new world is for all the pious, Jews and non-Jews, and in a new world \"you don't have Israel there.\" Saadia's explanation, phrase by phrase: \"Our bones are dried up.\" The prophecy opens this way because Hashem knows people will think: how can bones live after drying, how can the moisture return, how can the pure soul be joined again to the body it left? \"People lose their loved ones and you think to yourself, how is this person who's in the grave and is drying up in the grave, how will that come back alive again?\" \"I will bring you up from your graves, My people\" : a promise for Bnei Yisrael alone. \"I will bring you to the land of Israel\" : so it takes place in this world, not only at the entrance to Olam HaBa. \"And you shall know that I am Hashem when I open your graves\" , which seems repetitive. Saadia: שכל אחד מהם כשיחיה יתברר לו כי אשר הוא חי הוא אשר מת , each one, when he lives again, will know that he is the same person who died. The Rishonim asked whether a resurrected person will know he lived before, and Saadia's view is yes : \"I'm the same person, I lived 100 years ago, a thousand years ago... these were my relatives, this was my wife, this was my husband... you're going to know your family connections.\" \"I will settle you on your land\" : these long days of tranquillity will be on the earth of this world. 2. Yeshayahu 26: \"Your dead shall live\" \"This is one verse which actually we say at stone settings.\" יחיו מתיך נבלתי יקומון הקיצו ורננו שכני עפר כי טל אורת טלך וארץ רפאים תפיל. Your dead shall live, my corpses shall rise; awake and sing, you who dwell in the dust, for your dew is a dew of lights, and the earth shall cast down the shades. Saadia reads the last phrase as Hashem keeping in the ground those who did not merit resurrection. Saadia shows that it matches Yechezkel point for point: \"Your dead shall live\" parallels \"I will open your graves\": it is for the Jewish people. \"My corpses shall rise\" parallels \"I will bring you up from your graves.\" \"It's all matching, it's symmetrical.\" \"Awake\" parallels \"you shall know.\" When you wake from sleep, you know you were dreaming, you remember the dream, and you know you are the same person who slept. So too, \"you're going to wake up and you're going to realise, ah, I'm alive. I who lived 1,125 years ago... I am that person.\" \"A dew\" answers \"our bones are dried up\": you will be moist again. \"Of lights\" , in the plural, refers to the soul's faculties, which Saadia set out in the essay on the soul. All of them will come alive again. \"This is an amazing thing. We don't know yet what techiyat hametim in Olam HaBa looks like. But techiyat hametim in this world, which is reserved for Am Yisrael... will be one which the Jewish people will wake up and they will know their lives.\" 3. Daniel 12: \"Many of those who sleep in the dust\" ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם. Many of those who sleep in the dust shall awake, some to everlasting life and some to everlasting shame. Again יקיצו , \"shall awake,\" with the same meaning: you will know your previous life. The 47 verses. Then Saadia says \"something interesting.\" How do we know this verse is about resurrection in this world ? Because it comes at the end of 47 verses about the exiles, from the start of Daniel 11: Verses Kingdom 1 Persia , its three kings. Daniel lived at the end of the Persian Empire, so one verse sufficed 13 Greece 20 Rome 10 The Arabs , the Ishmaelites. Saadia lived in the 10th century, as the Arabs spread and conquered 3 The salvation , at the start of Daniel 12: \"At that time Michael will arise,\" and \"many of those who sleep in the dust shall awake... and shine like the brightness of the firmament\" The Rabbi totted it up with the class: 1, 14, 34, 44, then 45, 46 and 47. And for Saadia, the exile under the Arabs is the last exile . Why only three verses for the future? Because Yeshayahu and Yechezkel already explained it. The point: just as the exiles of Persia, Greece, Rome and the Arabs were all in this world, so too the final three verses, about the end of the exiles and the salvation, must be in this world . Questions on Chapter 4 Q (Richard): Does the presumption of literalism apply to Tanach generally, or only to the Torah? To all of Tanach. Where a prophecy is clear and comprehensible on its face, it is treated as a prophecy, \"not a meditation.\" Is that generally accepted? \"Yes, everyone accepts that... Saadia is the starting point and everyone really builds on him,\" some narrowing it, some widening it. Midrash is the opposite. The Maharitz Chayes , about 200 years ago, said that with Midrash the presumption is that it is not literal, unless it makes sense. When the Midrash says Moshe was ten amot tall, or that Batya stretched out her arm and it grew long, \"there are messages behind it.\" Batya's arm means she put in huge effort: \"if you really want to do something in life, you can do it.\" The Rabbi's pet hate: \"One of the most annoying things, seeing adults reading in synagogue The Little Midrash Says .\" It is lovely for children, who cannot yet grasp the deeper meanings, \"but then they have to come to a point where I understand... it's just a metaphor which has something much deeper inside it.\" Chapter 5: If You Allegorise Resurrection \"It seems to be this was something which was bugging him, because he dedicates a whole other chapter\" to those who want to make resurrection entirely metaphorical. Leave aside Chapter 2's principles; let us hear their actual arguments. Their reading Perhaps the resurrection verses mean the revival of the kingdom : Jewish sovereignty coming back to life after it \"died.\" Raising someone from lowliness is like raising him from the dust: מקימי מעפר דל מאשפת ירים אביון להושיבי עם נדיבים (from Hallel): Hashem raises the poor from the dust. The poor man is not literally sitting in dust. The verses compare someone in trouble to the dead: נשכחתי כמת מלב , I am forgotten like a dead man. So the \"dead\" would be Am Yisrael in its suffering, and \"resurrection\" would be uplifting them and giving them back sovereignty and power. Saadia's answer: be consistent אם יתחייב בזה הפרשן : if the interpreter does this, with no reason forcing him, he will have to do the same with three things: the mitzvot , the ancient narratives , and the miracles , עד שלא ישאר מהם דבר כפשוטו , until nothing is left in its plain sense. \"Fine, you want to do that? Do that. But if you're going to do that, then be consistent.\" 1. Mitzvot \"Do not eat chametz\" (Parashat Bo). There is no reason to reinterpret it: it contradicts neither the senses, nor logic, nor tradition, nor another verse. But if I like metaphors, it could mean do not commit adultery , because Hoshea compares adulterers to leavened dough: כלם מנאפים כמו תנור... מלוש בצק עד חמצתו . They stick to one another like sticky dough. So Pesach would carry an extra prohibition of zenut . \"Do not kindle fire on Shabbat\" (Parashat Vayakhel) could mean do not wage war on Shabbat , because war is made with fire, and the Torah calls war with Sichon fire: כי אש יצאה מחשבון להבה מקרית סיחן . \"Do not take the mother with the young\" (Parashat Ki Tetze) could mean: when you conquer your enemies, do not kill old and young together , because Hoshea says of Shalman's destruction of Beit Arbel, אם על בנים רטשה , mother was dashed with children, meaning young and old alike. \"You see, you can explain the whole Torah away.\" When one of the four criteria forces you, do it; when none does, don't. The Rabbi added that the Rashba quotes people living in the time of the Rishonim who explained tefillin non-literally: there is no mitzvah to tie anything on, it is about connecting with your heart. \"I know this sounds very modern and very 21st century... But then the whole Torah becomes ridiculous.\" 2. Narratives Saadia starts at the beginning. \"In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth\" could mean He created a nation from among the nations . When Israel is in exile it is \" תהו ובהו ,\" void; and when Hashem restores them, He says כי הנני בורא שמים חדשים וארץ חדשה , I create new heavens and a new earth, meaning new times. \"Let the earth sprout grass\" could mean healthy bodies , since Yeshayahu blesses ועצמותיכם כדשא תפרחנה , your bones shall flourish like grass. \"Fruit trees bearing fruit\" could mean a successful king , from Yechezkel's parable of the cedar planted on the mountain of Israel that ונשא ענף ועשה פרי , bears branches and fruit. \"Let there be lights\" could mean Torah, Prophets and Writings , since נר מצוה ותורה אור and נר לרגלי דברך ואור לנתיבתי . \"There's nothing left literal... very creative. You can go through many, many more examples like this where everything becomes non-literal, even the whole creation narrative.\" 3. Miracles The splitting of the Sea. ויבאו בני ישראל בתוך הים ביבשה והמים להם חמה מימינם ומשמאלם . Perhaps the \"water\" on their right and left was Pharaoh's army , through which they passed safely, and there was no splitting at all, because an army is compared to water: Yeshayahu calls the king of Ashur, Sancheriv, מי הנהר העצומים והרבים , the mighty and many waters of the river. The sun standing still for Yehoshua: וידם השמש וירח עמד . Perhaps it means the Israelite kingdom was firmly established in the land, since the verses compare the end of a kingdom to the sun setting ( ובאה השמש על הנביאים וקדר עליהם היום ; באה שמשה בעוד יומם ), and its rise to the sun shining. The Rabbi noted that one of the Rishonim, the Ralbag , does interpret the verse in a similar way. \"But Saadia is saying a good point, which is all miracles we can really throw out of the window.\" The conclusion Whoever reads the resurrection passages as parables must, to be consistent, read the creation story, all the miracles and all the commandments as parables too. יצא מכלל תורת ישראל : if he does that, he has left the Torah of Israel. And if he will not, because he clearly recognises that is wrong, \"then why are you doing that with techiyat hametim? You need to be consistent, full stop.\" Question: weren't the mitzvot already established? Richard objected: the mitzvot Saadia uses as examples already had established, transmitted meanings; \"don't eat chametz\" was always understood as leaven. A new prophecy, at the moment it was given, would not have had an accepted interpretation yet. The Rabbi understood the question, but answered that Saadia is looking at it \"from the perspective of now,\" a thousand years ago and today, looking back at both the Torah and the prophecies. \"Why should we interpret the prophecies of Yechezkel, Yeshayahu and Daniel any different to the verses which say don't eat chametz?\" Richard suggested what would matter is how the verses came to be interpreted: if there were early suggestions that they were metaphorical, that would be relevant. \"You would have a very valid point if we have evidence that there were competing interpretations from the beginning.\" Do we? \"Not that I know of.\" Other questions Can I know if I've been resurrected? A participant asked how one could tell. The Rabbi explained that she was thinking of reincarnation , which Saadia rejects entirely (the class spent a whole chapter on it before Pesach). Resurrection has not happened yet; no one has been resurrected. Will it happen? \"Every decent human being will\" be resurrected. Must Mashiach come before the year 6000? \"It is written. It is written in the Talmud.\" But the Rambam and others do not take that opinion as authoritative. \"I simply don't know. Maybe it will be, maybe it won't be... I don't think anyone can tell you. There are too many opinions.\" Chapter 6: The Song of Ha'azinu \"I'm going to fly through this.\" Saadia found resurrection in the Shirah of Ha'azinu , a testimony for Israel throughout the ages. Its arc: Hashem's kindness : הלא הוא אביך קנך הוא עשך ויכננך . The people's rebellion when life goes well: וישמן ישרון ויבעט . Consequences : אסתירה פני מהם , I will hide My face. Mercy : Hashem will punish those who punished us, לולי כעס אויב אגור , and have mercy on us through our difficulties. Redemption. And then the verse: ראו עתה כי אני אני הוא ואין אלהים עמדי אני אמית ואחיה מחצתי ואני ארפא ואין מידי מציל. Four heretics, four answers Saadia says the verse answers four kinds of heretics : Heretic Answer in the verse 1. There is no God אני אני הוא : it is I 2. There is a God, but with a partner (two powers, three powers) ואין אלהים עמדי : there is no god with Me 3. Denies techiyat hametim אני אמית ואחיה : I put to death and I bring to life 4. Denies Hashem's providence and punishment ואין מידי מציל : none can save from My hand Why \"I put to death and bring to life\" means resurrection. Hashem knew some would say it means He makes one generation die and brings another new generation to life, that is, new babies. So the verse adds מחצתי ואני ארפא , I strike and I heal. Just as the one healed is the same one who was struck, so the one brought to life is the same one who was put to death. \"And so there we see from that is the concept of techiyat hametim in the Torah itself.\" The rest of the Shirah goes on to the final salvation. Summary Chapter 4, three prophets. Yechezkel 37 : \"My people,\" \"to the land of Israel,\" so a resurrection for Israel in this world; and \"you shall know,\" so the resurrected will remember who they were and recognise their families. Yeshayahu 26 , said at stone settings, matches it point by point: \"awake\" as from a dream, \"dew\" against the dried bones, \"lights\" for the soul's faculties. Daniel 12 comes after 47 verses on Persia, Greece, Rome and the Arabs, all in this world, so its resurrection is too. Tanach is literal by default; Midrash is not (Maharitz Chayes). Hence Batya's arm, and the Rabbi's plea to grow out of The Little Midrash Says . Chapter 5, consistency. If resurrection means national revival, then chametz means adultery, Shabbat fire means war, the mother bird means sparing old and young, creation means Israel's restoration, light means Torah, the Sea's walls were Pharaoh's army and the sun's standing was the kingdom's founding. Whoever does that has left the Torah of Israel; whoever won't must read resurrection literally too. Chapter 6, Ha'azinu. \"I put to death and I bring to life\" alongside \"I strike and I heal\" means the same person who died is revived. The verse answers four heretics. Coming Up Next Monday at 6 to 7: Chapter 7 , the fourth avenue, the Chachamim , followed by Chapters 8 and 9 , the general questions people ask about resurrection, such as \"will I see my loved ones?\" After finishing the essay there will be a whole shiur on the different views of the Rishonim, the Rambam and others, on techiyat hametim and how it relates to Mashiach and Olam HaBa.","-Rwyj6G1dqk":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 7 (The Resurrection of the Dead), Chapters 2 and 3 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 12 May 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: Two Resurrections and Four Avenues Essays 7, 8 and 9 are all about the end of times: techiyat hametim , Mashiach, and Olam HaBa (what reward and punishment are). Tonight continues the first part of Saadia's eschatology. Two resurrections. One ushers in the world to come, for anyone, Jew or non-Jew, who has a chelek le'olam haba ; that is discussed in Essay 9. The other is be'et hage'ulah , at the time of redemption: in this world, unique to the tzaddikim of Israel. This essay focuses more on that one. Four avenues of challenge , whichever resurrection we mean: is it impossible by nature , by logic , by Scripture , or by the tradition of the Chachamim? Chapter 1, the challenge from nature \"We've never seen it.\" Do you doubt all the miracles of the Torah? Just as Hashem did those, He can do this. \"Not enough matter.\" Even Hashem cannot do the impossible, like making 10 smaller than 5. But Saadia showed that Hashem created each human from distinct matter, so each can be resurrected individually. (Whether his figures for the quantity of matter are scientifically true today \"is not the point.\") \"Matter that passes through many forms.\" A man is eaten by a lion, the lion is fed to a fish, a man eats the fish, and that man is burned to ash. Saadia's point: only Hashem can create יש מאין , something from nothing; humans can only make יש מיש . In the same way, humans can break something down into something else, however fine, but no one can destroy something into nothing . After the Holocaust; cremation; tattoos This question was asked after the Holocaust about those who were gassed and burned, Hashem yikom damam : how will they be resurrected? The answer given was the same: the matter has been broken down very finely, \"but it's there... Humans cannot destroy into nothing.\" Cremation. We instinctively recoil from it, and \"obviously it's forbidden.\" But pinpointing exactly what the prohibition is \"is a whole shiur in its own right.\" One argument, put forward by the Munkatcher Rebbe about a hundred years ago, was that cremating yourself denies techiyat hametim . \"Based on everything which we've learned in Saadia and other Rishonim, that argument does not stand.\" It is forbidden for other reasons, but not because it prevents resurrection. Tattoos. \"If you have a tattoo on your body, you won't be resurrected.\" The Rabbi suggested some Jewish mother made it up to stop her son getting one. A tattoo that violates the Torah's definition is a negative prohibition punishable by lashes, like any other, and has nothing to do with techiyat hametim . Chapter 2: The Challenge from Logic \"A really important\" chapter: when do we have the right to interpret the Torah metaphorically? Three logical challenges ואחר כן חקרתי במוצא השלישי שהוא מחשבה בשכל. Saadia now turns to the avenue of reason, and finds three possible logical objections: It is impossible , and therefore illogical. Already answered in Chapter 1: there is enough matter. The One who can do it never promised it ; that is, the resurrection verses should be reinterpreted. This is the heart of the chapter. The consequences are illogical , which would make the principle itself illogical. Saadia will deal with the ramifications of resurrection in Chapter 8. On the second: there are many verses that promise resurrection. Even if one could find other ways to interpret them, \"after analysis I don't believe there's any reason\" to do so. כי כל אשר בספרי הנביאים הוא כאשר נראה משמעו : everything in the books of the prophets means what its words plainly indicate. The presumption of literalism So when you study Tanach, your starting, prima facie presumption is that it is literal . Contrast Midrash , where, as the Rambam writes, the presumption is that it is not literal. Nowadays \"it's very popular\" to allegorise parts of the Torah. The Rabbi said he did not like to repeat nonsense, but there are even some rabbis who say that Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov were not real historical figures but symbols, that Avraham and Sarah are חומר וצורה , matter and form. And Saadia himself has already allegorised: throughout Essay 2, all the references to Hashem's hands, legs and eyes are metaphor. \"Eyes\" means Hashem watches over; \"I stretch My hand over Egypt\" means He punishes. \"Great, lovely. But then where are the limits to that?\" Only four cases Only in four cases may the Torah be read non-literally: 1. When it contradicts the senses. Adam called his wife חוה because היא היתה אם כל חי , the mother of all living. Is she the mother of all life? There are many animals not born of Chavah. So it must mean the mother of all human life. 2. When it contradicts reason. כי ה' אלקיך אש אכלה : Hashem is a consuming fire. It goes against all logic to say the Omnipresent is fire. So the verse is missing a letter of comparison, the kaf : He is like a consuming fire. His punishment, if you anger Him, spreads quickly as fire devours, as it says elsewhere, כי אש קדחה באפי . 3. When two verses contradict. Malachi says of tzedakah, ובחנוני נא בזאת , test Me with this. But the Torah says לא תנסו את ה' אלקיכם , do not test Hashem. So there must be two kinds of testing: - Testing whether Hashem is able to do something: forbidden. In the desert, Israel asked היוכל אל לערך שלחן במדבר , can God set a table in the wilderness? - Testing whether I am worthy , having acknowledged that He can: permitted. Gideon said, if You will save Israel through me, אנסה נא רק הפעם בגזה , let me test just once with the fleece, asking for dew on the fleece and then on the ground. Gideon was apprehensive about going into battle. He knew Hashem could do it; the question was whether He would do it for him, whether he was on a sufficient spiritual level. If so, he would feel confident to lead. \"And we have the right to understand that because of the contradiction.\" 4. When it contradicts the tradition. The Torah says ארבעים יכנו לא יסיף , forty lashes, but we know from the whole tractate of Makkot that it is 39 . The Torah is rounding off. Where else does the Torah round off? \"We read this every year, I don't think people realise this.\" After the sin of the spies, who toured the land for forty days, Hashem decreed יום לשנה , a year for each day, forty years in the desert. But the spies were sent more than a year after the Exodus, so from the decree it was only 39 years. והשנה הראשונה כבר עברה בלא עונש : the first year had already passed without that punishment. So \"forty\" is rounded. When one of these four applies, we look for another usage of the words until the verse fits. But without one of them, we may not take the resurrection verses out of their plain sense . We should leave them as they are, for the One who promised them is able and knows. Further examples Reason: the Rambam reinterpreted all the Torah's appearances of angels as happening in dreams or prophetic visions, because angels are non-corporeal and cannot literally come down to humans. Similarly Bilam's donkey : the whole story in Parashat Balak, the three beatings and the donkey answering back, happened in a vision, in Bilam's mind. \"Because the Rambam must feel that this is something which is illogical for donkeys to speak. Yes, Hashem does miracles, but... this is too much.\" Tradition: לא תבשל גדי בחלב אמו , if we stopped there, would only forbid a kid in its own mother's milk. But the oral tradition says the Torah forbids any kosher meat with any milk. So \"kid in its mother's milk\" is an example, not a limit. Saadia's \"reductio\" in Chapter 5 The Rabbi previewed Chapter 5, where Saadia presses the point: if you allegorise resurrection, I will allegorise the whole Torah. \"Do not eat chametz on Pesach\" could mean \"do not commit adultery,\" and \"do not light a fire on Shabbat\" could mean \"do not go to war on Shabbat,\" and he brings verses to support each. \"If you're going to start doing this, there's no end... There'll be no mitzvot left in the Torah.\" Who decides what contradicts the senses or reason? \"Lovely. But the problem is these four things, some of them are very specified.\" We know what our tradition says; we can see a contradiction between verses. \"But then observation of our senses or logic, who defines what our senses are?\" The age of the universe. Most of the scientific world says the universe is billions of years old. A simple reading of the Torah seems to say about 6,000. Do we have the right to reinterpret? The Rabbi asked the class which category it would fall under. Not tradition, and not a contradiction between verses. Not logic, he argued: \"it's not illogical to say the world is 6,000 years old. It's only illogical to say that when you have the data.\" And data, the tree rings and the bones, is observed with the senses. So it would be the senses. Then the counter-argument: who says to rely on the scientific data? \"You can question everything... You can see something with your eyes and you can say, am I really seeing it?\" Some will say, \"I haven't seen the world to be billions of years old, therefore... I don't have the right to reinterpret.\" \"I don't have an answer to that. I'm just raising the question.\" Logic is just as open. The Rambam says it is illogical for donkeys to speak; others say if Hashem split the sea He can make a donkey talk. אין לדבר סוף. Even Hashem's corporeality: \"I'm not talking about amei ha'aretz ... I'm talking about great chachamim in Germany\" who believed Hashem had a body, because the Torah says so, and who did not think it went against logic. \"Who defines what logic is? ... This is why there is so much argument about these things.\" Avraham and Sarah as symbols: an old debate This is not a modern argument. The Rashba already mentioned such people around him, and blamed the Rambam's books. There were two famous bans on the Rambam's books, the second in the time of the Rashba, and Abba Mari , who pushed for it, argued that people were using the Rambam to allegorise everything. The classic example, 800 years ago: Avraham and Sarah were not real people but symbols of chomer and tzurah . By Saadia's four categories, may a rabbi say so in shul? A participant said no, because it is written in the Torah and the Torah is true. But does it fit one of the four? Only, the Rabbi said, by a \"quite weak\" argument that it contradicts the senses because we lack historical evidence. The Rambam's Refinement: Moreh Nevuchim 2:25 The Rambam \"doesn't argue with Saadia, but he refines it, I would say.\" He discusses the creation of the world. Aristotle , whom the Rambam highly revered, believed in God but held that the universe, like God, is eternal, with no act of creation. Can we reinterpret בראשית ברא אלקים to fit him? The Rambam, as the Rabbi translated: the reason I rejected the eternity of the world is not that the Torah says otherwise. The verses that indicate creation are no more numerous than the verses about Hashem having a body , and we reinterpret those. In fact, it would have been easier to reinterpret the first chapters of Bereishit than all the verses throughout Tanach about God's body. So why not? Two reasons: Proof. That Hashem has no body has been proven , rationally: it is impossible for the God we believe in to have a body, so we are forced to reinterpret. The eternity of the world has not been proven; Aristotle had arguments, but no conclusive proof. \"I only reinterpret Torah when I have a clear proof.\" Foundations. Believing Hashem has no body does not destroy any foundation of the Torah or contradict any prophet; the verse about His \"hand\" is no real contradiction. But Aristotle's eternal, unchanging world היא הורסת את התורה מעיקרה , destroys the Torah from its root, since it denies all miracles and all our hopes for the future. So for the Rambam, two conditions: there must be a need (a proof forcing reinterpretation), and even then you may not reinterpret if it would destroy a fundamental of the faith. Even so, \"it's still open for interpretation\": the incorporeality of God is proven \"in the Rambam's mind.\" Applying it Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov as symbols? \"A resounding no.\" You are not forced to it, since there is no evidence against them, \"some people say there's evidence. Okay, but doesn't force you.\" And it would destroy the whole Torah. Har Sinai, where Hashem revealed Himself to 600,000 men? Lack of evidence in neighbouring civilisations \"isn't a proof of evidence against,\" so it is not forced, and reinterpreting it would destroy the Torah. Bilam's donkey? Reinterpreting does not destroy the Torah, since a talking donkey is not a principle. Must you? According to the Rambam, yes. Others say Hashem can do that. The overarching principles agreed by all: you may not reinterpret where it would undermine our fundamentals (the Rambam), and, from Saadia, only where the text conflicts with the senses, with reason, with another verse, or with tradition. Chapter 3: The Challenge from Scripture Now the third avenue: are there verses that seem to deny resurrection? Saadia brings two groups. Group 1: \"He does not return\" ויזכר כי בשר המה רוח הולך ולא ישוב (Tehillim 78): He remembers that they are flesh, a passing wind that does not return. אנוש כחציר ימיו כציץ השדה כן יציץ. כי רוח עברה בו ואיננו (Tehillim 103): man's days are like grass; the wind passes over him and he is gone. כלה ענן וילך... לא ישוב עוד לביתו (Iyov): like a cloud that passes, he will not return to his home. ואיש שכב ולא יקום (Iyov): a man lies down and does not rise. The Rabbi asked the class for the one simple answer to all of them. Saadia: these verses underscore how mortal a human is . Once in the grave, you cannot choose to get up of your own accord . None of them says that Hashem will not resurrect. When people say them, as David does in Tehillim, they are pleas for mercy : look how mortal we are, have mercy on us. When Hashem says them, they describe His compassion for creatures who have no power of their own. And the more Scripture stresses that man cannot rise by himself, the more it magnifies Hashem's greatness , since only He can raise him, just as at Har Sinai: has anyone ever heard the voice of God and lived? Group 2: Kohelet 9 \"Before we see Saadia inside,\" the Rabbi read the verses in Kohelet 9: כי מי אשר יבחר אל כל החיים יש בטחון כי לכלב חי הוא טוב מן האריה המת. כי החיים יודעים שימתו והמתים אינם יודעים מאומה ואין עוד להם שכר כי נשכח זכרם. גם אהבתם גם שנאתם גם קנאתם כבר אבדה וחלק אין להם עוד לעולם בכל אשר נעשה תחת השמש. Whoever is still among the living has hope: a live dog is better than a dead lion. The living know they will die; the dead know nothing, they have no more reward, their memory is forgotten. Their love, their hatred, their jealousy are already lost, and they have no more portion in anything done under the sun. \"Pretty clear these verses seem to be saying that once you die, you die.\" Saadia admits: זאת הספיקה חזקה , this is a strong difficulty. His answer comes from the verse before : לב בני אדם מלא רע והוללות בלבבם בחייהם ואחריו אל המתים , the hearts of men are full of evil and madness while they live, and after that, to the dead. So, says Saadia, these are not Shlomo's own words . They are Shlomo reporting the words of the fools whose hearts are full of madness: what's the point of life, once you die there's nothing. The parallel: Pharaoh's מי ה' אשר אשמע בקלו , \"who is Hashem that I should listen to Him?\" Taken alone, the verse seems to say there is no God. But it is the Torah reporting what Pharaoh said. So too here. And since Shlomo calls these views madness, whoever holds them is unworthy of a portion in the world to come, as it says, לא יגרך רע , evil shall not dwell with You. המקום יצילנו מכל צרה ומכל אולת : may Hashem save us from all trouble and all folly. Other commentators. The Ibn Ezra says exactly like Saadia: these are the words of the wicked. The Metzudat David reads them as Shlomo's own words, with a different message: while you are alive, יש בטחון , you still have hope, because you can still do teshuvah. That is why a live dog is better than a dead lion. The living know they will die, and so can still repent; once dead, you no longer have the ability to change. \"Use your life well.\" Summary Nothing can be destroyed into nothing , so even those burned in the Holocaust will rise. Cremation is forbidden, but not because it prevents resurrection; and tattoos have nothing to do with it. Three logical challenges : impossibility (answered in Chapter 1), reinterpretation (this chapter), and absurd consequences (Chapter 8). Tanach is presumed literal (Midrash the opposite). It may be read otherwise only when it contradicts the senses ( em kol chai ), reason ( esh ochlah ), another verse (testing Hashem: Gideon's fleece), or tradition (forty lashes as 39, like 39 years \"rounded\" to 40). But who defines the senses and reason? The age of the universe, Bilam's donkey and God's corporeality show how open those two categories are. The Rambam's refinement (Moreh 2:25): reinterpret only when forced by proof, and never where it would destroy a fundamental. Hence God's body is reinterpreted, but creation is not; nor Avraham and Sarah, nor Har Sinai. Verses that seem to deny resurrection only stress that man cannot rise by himself. Kohelet 9 reports the words of fools (Saadia, Ibn Ezra), or urges teshuvah while alive (Metzudat David). Coming Up Chapter 4: the verses throughout Tanach that show techiyat hametim will happen. Chapter 5: Saadia's demonstration that if those verses are allegory, the whole Torah can be allegorised.","2xCmJd1myp4":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 7 (The Resurrection of the Dead), Introduction and Chapter 1 (the longer version) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 28 April 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Book So Far The first shiur after Pesach, on Rosh Chodesh Iyar, opening the spring term. The class began the sefer in January 2024. Essay Subject 1 Creation 2 Hashem: how to understand our definition of God 3 Revelation: Torah and prophecy 4 Merits and demerits, mitzvot and aveirot, including free will 5 Teshuvah and the categories of people 6 The nefesh : the soul's essence, and what happens when we die From here to the end of the book: Essay 7 on techiyat hametim , resurrection; Essay 8 on ge'ulah , the Messianic redemption; Essay 9 on sechar va'onesh , not why the righteous suffer (that was Essays 4 and 5) but what reward is and what Olam HaBa is; and Essay 10 , which is musar , \"not a philosophical work, it's a practical work\" on doing the will of Hashem. Introductory Point 1: Two Kinds of Resurrection The very order of the essays, resurrection, then redemption, then Olam HaBa, shows that Saadia understands techiyat hametim differently from the Rambam. The Rambam For the Rambam, Olam HaBa begins when we die: each person enters it according to his deeds. Techiyat hametim is a separate stage: there will be a time when people are resurrected and then die again and go on to Olam HaBa, because for the Rambam Olam HaBa is for the soul alone. Saadia For Saadia, as he will develop in Essay 9, Olam HaBa is body and soul . So everyone who deserves Olam HaBa will need to have the body resurrected. And there are two resurrections : The main resurrection, which ushers in Olam HaBa. It is for all of humanity: everyone who deserves it, Jews and non-Jews alike, including the chassidei umot ha'olam . It comes at the end of days, after this world ends and a new world begins. A resurrection when Mashiach comes , uniquely for the tzaddikim of Israel . Those considered righteous over \"the last 5,700 years\" will be resurrected to enjoy the Messianic era. That era too will come to an end, and they will rise again with everyone else for Olam HaBa, \"but they also get this special treat.\" The Rabbi gave the terms used for the two: techiyat hametim be'et hage'ulah , at the time of the redemption, the Messianic one; and be'et hayeshu'ah , at the time of the salvation, which he identified with the final one for all who deserve it. Why the Messianic resurrection? Saadia describes it later in the essay as a kind of justice . The righteous of Israel suffered so much in this world that, aside from their reward in Olam HaBa, they should also see some benefit in this world, the one they lived in, before the new world comes. Because resurrection is such a big subject, he will also discuss the philosophical arguments against it and for it. \"In this essay he's talking about resurrection, resurrection both places, but the focus is on resurrection at Messianic time.\" Q (Mark): If the righteous are resurrected as a physical benefit for their suffering, what is the real purpose? Can they do more mitzvot and increase their portion? The same question applies to anyone alive when Mashiach comes, be'ezrat Hashem today. In short, which Essay 8 will touch on: there is still bechirah , free will, and active choices to be made. A person's final judgment, the identity he will have for Olam HaBa, will also depend on how he lives in the Messianic era. That applies to the resurrected tzaddikim too: \"this extra benefit, this extra choice.\" Introductory Point 2: Two Versions of the Essay This essay, uniquely, exists in two versions. Saadia wrote an essay on resurrection as part of Emunot VeDeot, and then, because the subject mattered so much to people and gave hope amid the suffering of exile, he made it into a standalone book . So he goes over things already taught \"as if you've never learned it before,\" where normally he would say \"as we learned earlier.\" Which version belongs in Emunot VeDeot is an academic debate, but \"there's no debate that they were both written by Saadia.\" The class will follow the longer one : it contains most of what is in the shorter one, and the Rabbi will supplement whatever is missing; Ibn Tibbon , who translated the whole book into Hebrew, translated the longer one; in the English Book of Beliefs and Opinions (the red book), the main text has the shorter essay, and the longer one appears in the Appendix to Treatise 7 after Essay 10, on page 409. \"You'll notice he's much more verbose in this essay,\" since it was intended for everyone, including people who had not learned his book. (The screen share failed again, and the Rabbi posted a Sefaria link for the class to follow: \"You'll be pleased to learn I actually got a new computer, baruch Hashem .\") Saadia's Introduction The title בתחיית המתים, והנכבד שבו מעדי בני ישראל אשר יעד הבורא בהם את ישועה : on the resurrection of the dead, one of the most honoured promises to the Jewish people, which Hashem promised them for the time of the salvation. \"We never had any essay title like that.\" The shorter edition just says techiyat hametim . Again you see the intended audience: \"This essay is for every Tom, Dick and Harry, every Jewish person, to give them consolation and hope during the suffering and the exile which we go through.\" What the masses believe Ibn Tibbon writes in the name of the author: blessed be the God of Israel, המאמין דברו בצדיקו ומקיים אמרתו , who keeps His promises. The masses of Am Yisrael believe that Hashem will resurrect our dead at the time of the redemption. They interpret every passage in Tanach about resurrection literally , and support it with tradition. That literalism is important, because some were saying there is no resurrection and that all the verses are figurative. Saadia says you cannot do that, and he will develop two lines of defence: Chapter 2: there are only four instances in which the Torah may be interpreted metaphorically. Chapter 4 onward: he will go through the allegorical interpretations people gave to the resurrection verses and show why even those are wrong. \"So clearly this was something which was happening at this time.\" A minority of the nation believe in resurrection only at the entrance to Olam HaBa, not at the time of the redemption. Saadia calls that incorrect, \"I don't think he calls them heretical,\" and says they rely on weak proofs. He will prove there are two. Why he wrote it It is the duty of every righteous person to explain things properly and to refute what is wrong: שפתי צדיק ידעון רצון ופי רשעים תהפכות . Saadia says he took great care with \"this book\" (he calls it a book, because it was meant to stand alone), so that it would be as clear as possible, and he wrote it for the nation, להיות להם משען וסעד , to be a support in their service of God and in bearing the difficulties of exile. He followed the way Hashem commands for hard times, in Yeshayahu 35 : חזקו ידים רפות וברכים כשלות אמצו. אמרו לנמהרי לב חזקו אל תיראו הנה אלהיכם . Strengthen the weak hands and the failing knees; say to the faint-hearted, be strong, do not fear, here is your God. Four sources of challenge Saadia found that all possible challenges to resurrection come from four sources, ארבעה אין להם חמישי , \"four, with no fifth.\" These are the four sources of knowledge from his introduction to the whole book, which the class learned back in January a year and a half ago. The Rabbi asked the class to recall them: The senses. Anything we perceive: \"I feel that this seat is under me... I can see the sun, therefore I know there is a sun.\" Reason , common logic: one plus one is two. \"That's not sensual. I can't touch that.\" Inference : deriving new knowledge from what we already know. Tradition : Scripture and the prophets. He introduces them \"as if we've never seen them before,\" because this book was written for those who had not learned the main work. In this essay he names them הטבע והשכל והכתוב והקבלה : nature, reason, Scripture and tradition. He will refute every challenge from each, and then bring three major proofs for resurrection. Chapter 1: The Challenge from Nature He begins with nature \"because that's inherent. That's the first thing around us.\" Question 1: We have never seen it In nature, animals are born, grow, live and die; we never see them come alive again. Perhaps nature rules out resurrection. Saadia's answer: this challenge only works for those who believe the world is eternal , that nature is nature and always has been and always will be. But we monotheists believe that the Creator changes the ways of nature whenever He wishes. All agree that He sent His prophets with signs and wonders. \"And even more than that\": we believe in creation from nothing , which Saadia proved at great length in Essay 1. Philosophically that is very difficult: how can anyone, even God, create something from nothing? \"But still, we believe that.\" And if you believe that, resurrection is easier. So whoever denies resurrection because \"nature doesn't do that\" must also deny: Moshe's staff turning into a snake ; the water turning to blood ; the splitting of the Sea ; the sun standing still for Yehoshua; and all the other miracles in the books, until he denies creation from nothing and ends up denying God. ותחיית המתים יותר קל : resurrection is easier and closer to imagine than creation from nothing. He adds an actual resurrection in Tanach: the son of the Shunamit , whom Hashem revived through Elisha , the story in the Haftarah of Vayera, where the child cries ראשי ראשי , \"my head, my head,\" and dies. The Rabbi noted it is not the strongest proof, because not everyone understands it as a real death; some say the child fainted into unconsciousness and the prophet brought him out of his trance. \"Question one and answer one: Hashem can do it. It's like all the other miracles in Tanach.\" Background: God cannot do the impossible The Rabbi reminded the class of what Saadia established back in the essay on God, accepted by almost every Jewish philosopher: Hashem cannot do the impossible. Not because His omnipotence is limited, but because absurdities are not things at all. God cannot make yesterday come back. He cannot make 10 smaller than 5. He cannot make a square into a triangle, \"because that's no longer a square, it's a triangle.\" Can God create a rock He cannot lift? No. \"That's not a limitation. That just means He can't do it because it's not possible.\" Question 2: There isn't enough matter So perhaps resurrection is one of those impossibilities. The premise: matter is finite and recycled. When a person dies, his body decomposes into the elements, and from those same elements another person is later formed, and then a third and a fourth. If person two was made from person one's matter, how can both be alive at the resurrection? \"Hashem can't do that, because Hashem can't do the impossible.\" The Rabbi stressed that this is not about reincarnation, which Saadia called nonsense before Pesach, nor about the soul, which was all Essay 6. It is purely about physical matter. A participant (Flora) asked him to go through it again, which he did: if a man died a hundred years after creation, his body decomposed into the atmosphere and the earth, and five hundred years later another was formed from that same material, the resurrection could not bring back both. Saadia's text: when the body has decomposed and each part has returned to its element, heat, cold, moisture and dryness, and those elements combine into a second body, and the second dies and becomes a third, and the third a fourth, how will each of them be resurrected? The answer. If a second body could only be formed from the matter of the first, the challenge would be right. But the elements are far, far greater in quantity than all the bodies formed from them . \"And how much more? Listen to this.\" The wise know that the air between the earth and the beginning of the first heaven is equal to the whole earth, including its mountains, seas, plants, animals and everything in it, 1,089 times over , because it is 33 times 33. \"I'm not going to pretend I understand and know about this.\" What the Rabbi had read is that Saadia takes this from the Islamic mathematicians and astronomers of the 8th and 9th centuries , who measured a degree of the earth's meridian. Whatever the science, the point is that Hashem has plenty of matter. When He creates person two and person three, He does not take person one's matter. Person one's matter stays, dispersed in the earth, the air and the moisture, but it does not enter person two. \"Because if it did enter person two, you couldn't do resurrection.\" The shorter edition's example. A man with only one cup who breaks it can do nothing until he remoulds it. But a man with many cups can put the broken one aside and remould it later, whenever he chooses. So too Hashem. An aside on gilgul. For those who do believe in reincarnation, a major question is what happens at the resurrection, \"because it's one soul, one identity, and it's got two bodies.\" That, the Rabbi noted, is a different question from Saadia's, which is about the same matter being used twice. \"Okay, let's leave that.\" Question 3: Bodies that pass through many forms In closing, the Rabbi summarised a third question from the chapter: what of a person whose body passes through many different forms? Saadia's answer: no creature has the ability to destroy something completely . The matter is always there, and so it will come back and be resurrected. Summary Saadia's map: resurrection (7), redemption (8), Olam HaBa and reward (9), ethics (10). Two resurrections , unlike the Rambam: one at the Messianic redemption for the tzaddikim of Israel, as justice for their suffering in this world; and one ushering in Olam HaBa for all who deserve it, Jews and non-Jews. Free will continues in the Messianic era. Two versions of the essay; the class follows the longer, translated by Ibn Tibbon, written as a book of hope for the masses in exile. The masses believe and read literally ; a minority accept only the final resurrection. Saadia will show both exist. Four possible challenges , from the four sources of knowledge: nature, reason, Scripture and tradition. Nature: if God made the world from nothing and did the miracles of Tanach, He can raise the dead. \"Resurrection is easier.\" Not enough matter? God cannot do the impossible, but this is not impossible: the elements are 1,089 times the earth, and Hashem never needs to reuse one person's matter for another, like a man with many cups. Nothing is ever destroyed completely , so every body can be restored. Coming Up Next week, at the normal time of 6 to 7: Chapter 2 , the challenges from reason , and the crucial question of when the Torah may be interpreted non-literally , against those who made resurrection a metaphor. \"And hopefully by then I'll be able to share the screen with you all.\" Chodesh tov.","C7_DBh4lPAI":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 6 (The Soul), Chapters 6 and 7 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 24 March 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Soul So Far The penultimate shiur before the Pesach break. Next week, at the summer time of 6 to 7, comes Chapter 8, on gilgul , which closes the essay; Essay 7 begins after Pesach. A point not yet made: Jew and non-Jew Before the recap, the Rabbi pointed out something he thought worth noting. Nowhere in this essay does Saadia differentiate between the soul of a Jew and the soul of a non-Jew. He touches on the subject briefly in a later essay, but for all intents and purposes, everything learned here about the neshamah applies to all human beings. \"And the same is in the Rambam's philosophy as well. The neshamah of a Jew and a non-Jew are not intrinsically different.\" Others, especially in the kabbalistic literature, make the idea of an essential difference very pronounced. \"But in Saadia, he makes no difference of this, and we assume this applies to all human beings.\" Chapters 1 to 5 Chapters 1 to 3: Saadia disproved various theories of the soul and gave his own. It is \"a very subtle discipline.\" The soul is created , simultaneously with the body , an ethereal essence with the power of speech, finer and purer even than the spheres, and called by several names. Chapter 4: Why would God put this elevated essence into a lowly body? Because God does nothing wrong, and this is the soul's nature: it only functions together with the body. \"You can't ask me that God should create a neshamah which functions out of the body, because that's no longer a neshamah... That's an angelic sphere.\" Only with the body can it attain the eternal bliss of Olam HaBa. The soul's pain is either the result of its own choices or a kind of compensation for the future. Chapter 5: Body and soul are one entity . Whenever the Torah speaks of reward, punishment or consequence, it means both together, not the body alone, the soul alone, or (as some held) the bones alone. This is the view of the vast majority of the Chachamim: Olam HaBa is for body and soul together. The Rambam , as the class learned in Hilchot Teshuvah , famously held that Olam HaBa is for the soul alone. The Rabbi has said in other shiurim that the difference between these views may not be so great, \"they actually could be saying the same thing in different words,\" but he left that for the essay on techiyat hametim . Tonight: Chapter 6 , on how long body and soul stay together, and Chapter 7 , on what happens to the soul at death. \"Two relatively straightforward chapters. Chapter 7 is quite interesting.\" Chapter 6: The Length of a Life \"There's one point to this chapter; it's a short chapter.\" A set lifespan, with additions and deductions The Torah speaks of a person having a set number of days. So Saadia posits that every human being is created with a certain lifespan , whether 50, 60, 70, 80 or 90 years. Throughout life a person can increase that lifespan through positive action or decrease it through negative action. During that time, body and soul are united as one. Saadia's verses for the basic lifespan: One of the blessings in Parashat Mishpatim : את מספר ימיך אמלא , \"I will fill the number of your days,\" allowing you to live the full length set out for you. To Moshe: הן קרבו ימיך למות , your days are drawing to an end. Then he adds that Hashem at times adds or subtracts (the Rabbi noted a word missing from the printed text, מוסיף or פוחת ). Foreknowledge and choice, again God knows how long you will live, including all the additions and deductions, but this \"does not take away our choice.\" This is exactly Saadia's answer in Essay 4. There he was the first to raise the question of how God's foreknowledge can be reconciled with our free will, and he answered that God knows but does not cause. \"He knows what's going to be the end of our entire lifespan based on the choices we will make in our lifetime, but He's not causing that.\" How the Rabbi understands it There is a basic vitality, a basic span of time, that every body is given. Some are born with a weak constitution and a natural span of 60, others with a strong one and a span of 90. The default \"at least in those days\" was 70. Hashem may then add, say, another 30 years so that a person lives to 100, or take 30 away so that he lives to 40. \"Each person with their basic lifespan, with increases or decreases, and that's how it works.\" Proofs for additions and deductions Mishlei : יראת ה' תוסיף ימים , the fear of Hashem adds days. Chizkiyahu prayed, and Hashem said, I will add fifteen years to your life. \"So there was a basic lifespan of Chizkiyahu, and then God adds him 15 years.\" The many rewards in the Torah of \"I will lengthen your days\": you have a set number, and they will be lengthened. The plague. When the people complained, a plague broke out and many died, and it was halted with the incense. If everyone simply died at his set time, \"then a plague would make no sense,\" and stopping it would have achieved nothing. The plague must have been a deduction from people's natural lifespans. Shmuel I 26 : David says of Shaul, כי אם ה' יגפנו או יומו יבוא ומת , either Hashem will strike him, or his day will come and he will die. The verse itself distinguishes between a person's natural day and a punishment that befalls him. The disclaimer Saadia ends with a warning: don't misquote me as saying that every righteous person lives long and every wicked person lives short. That is up to how God plays it out. If a righteous person did not live long, his reward must be in the world to come; if a wicked person lived long, his consequence must come in the next world. \"There is a time for everything.\" The Rabbi added that the Ibn Ezra says exactly the same: \"your days\" in the Torah are the set days every human is created with, according to his nature, constitution and vitality, which Hashem then increases or decreases. In answer to a question he mentioned the midrash about King David and Adam , which illustrates that a lifespan can be shaped by factors beyond a person's natural constitution. Don't make the calculations Q: Someone does a great deal of good and dies suddenly, not old. How are we meant to think about it? Perhaps without the good he would have died earlier, or with more good he would have lived longer? The Rabbi said Saadia essentially asks this himself. After everything he has said about long and short lives, don't start calculating: he lived these years because he did that, she lived those years because she did this. Yeshayahu says in the name of Hashem, כי לא מחשבותי מחשבותיכם , My thoughts are not your thoughts. \"I've mentioned this point many times in the past... it's impossible to judge anyone in this world, in terms of what their reward will be or punishment will be in the next one.\" We can judge according to the laws Hashem gave us for this world: the Torah tells a court what to do with a murderer or with someone who eats forbidden food. But a person's full identity for the next world depends not just on his choices but on the struggle he had to make them . Two people can do exactly the same act, and in God's eyes one has far greater weight than the other, because for one it was easy and for the other it was hard. \"This is the most basic answer to צדיק ורע לו רשע וטוב לו ... How do you define what a tzaddik or a rasha is? ... You never know and I don't know, no one will know. And anyone who says that they do know, doesn't know.\" So we can accept the chapter as theory, but in practice we should never start applying it to individual lives. Asked how we even know there is a world to come, he said there are many proofs, which will come in Essays 7 and 9; Saadia is \"the first one who goes through this systematically.\" Chapter 7: What Happens at Death The essay so far: what the soul is not (Chapters 1 and 2), what it is (3), why God joins it to a body (4), body and soul as one entity in the world to come (5), and the lifespan (6). Now: how does the soul leave the body when we die, and what happens to it? When the soul departs, we can no longer speak, move or communicate; that is the sign that it has left. \"Here he relies completely on tradition. He's got no philosophical or rational proofs for this.\" And, the Rabbi noted, \"Saadia takes a statement in the Gemara very literally, which is interesting.\" Point 1: The angel and the fear What do the Chachamim tell us? When a person's lifespan has come to an end, an angel is sent to separate body and soul. It appears to the dying person as: a figure holding an outstretched sword , pointed at him; of a yellowish substance, like fire ; full of eyes ; and those eyes are bluish . (The Hebrew translation is not quite right here; in the Arabic the colour is blue.) The angel does not attack. But the sight is so terrifying that the person dies of fear. Saadia is quoting the Chachamim; the Gemara in Avodah Zarah says something very close to this. Saadia's verses. Where do the Chachamim know this from? Saadia finds it in Scripture: The sword: in Divrei HaYamim, David lifted his eyes and saw the angel with its sword drawn, and after he prayed the sword was returned to its sheath. Fire: Yechezkel describes the angels as כגחלי אש בוערות , like burning coals of fire. Eyes: Yechezkel also says they were full of eyes all around. Blue eyes: this one is from reason. If the eyes were the same yellowish fiery colour as the body, there would be no clear demarcation between them. You only see eyes when they contrast, and blue stands out against yellow and orange. He adds two more proofs that one can die of fear: At Har Sinai , the people came to Moshe and said, למה נמות כי תאכלנו האש הגדולה הזאת , why should we die from this great fire? The Midrash says they actually died and were revived; the simple reading is that they almost died of fright. \"So the more so when he's got a sword about to pierce you.\" King David saw the angel. It did not come to attack him, but he was terrified of it, and as a result he lived with a constant coldness for the rest of his life. Why was King David always cold? \"Very interesting,\" said the Rabbi, \"because the traditional way... I always learned\" was different. The verse says that in his old age David was covered with garments and could not get warm. The well-known explanation: when David cut the corner of Shaul's garment, he disrespected clothing, and so clothing \"disrespected him\" and never warmed him. Saadia's reason is different, and the Rabbi went looking for the source and found it: a Midrash, brought also by the Radak , that David was startled by the angel's appearance and suffered from that chronic coldness until the day he died. The Radak brings both explanations, along with the simple one: David was only 70, but worn out by all his wars, and as a person ages his natural moisture lessens and he feels cold. \"I don't know why one understanding is more known than the other.\" Literal or metaphorical? \"Saadia takes this literally, which others don't.\" Some commentators explain that \"full of eyes\" means you cannot hide from this angel: wherever you creep, it sees you. The fire, too, could be read metaphorically. \"No, Saadia takes it literally. He believes and he understands that a person dies because of fear and of this image, and every time a human dies, they see this thing just before they die.\" The Rabbi said he himself would have read it more as a metaphor. \"I suppose we'll find out one day on our deathbeds.\" Q: Doesn't the Gemara say some souls leave like a hair drawn out of milk? Yes: for some people death is as gentle as drawing a hair from milk, and for the wicked it is like pulling thorns out of wool. The Rabbi reconciled this with Saadia: everyone dies of fear, but the shock needed varies. For the righteous, whose body and soul are in harmony, \"just a little disalignment is enough to separate them.\" For the wicked, who lived with body and soul out of harmony, \"you need a full electric shock in order to separate you.\" Near-death experiences. One participant mentioned the tunnel and the light. \"No one's ever been there and come back, so anyone can say lots of things. I only believe what the Chachamim tell us.\" Perhaps that is the beginning of Olam HaBa; \"I've got no idea.\" Another found the angel frightening and hard to believe, since an angel is meant to be beautiful. The Rabbi answered that there are destructive angels too, not only cherubs. The Chachamim say it is true \"in some kind of sense, whether literally or metaphorically.\" And the Shechinah at the bedside? The Gemara, brought by Rashi in Parashat Vayechi , says the Shechinah is at the head of a sick person's bed, which is why Yaakov bowed toward the head of the bed. Perhaps the Shechinah is this angel. In the Rabbi's understanding, and that of the Ramchal and the Rambam, an angel is not a creature with a name; it is an emanation from God. So at a deathbed there is a greater manifestation of godliness, \"for the good or for the bad.\" What is the Shechinah? \"A relatively more tangible manifestation of godliness in a certain place,\" as at the Kotel. \"I just find it remarkable that, essentially, Saadia is positing, the Gemara is positing, every person dies out of fear... Even if you died from illness, you died from fear at the end of the day.\" Point 2: Why can't we see the soul leave? Because, as Chapter 3 taught, the soul is so fine and transparent, like the heavenly spheres, which cannot be seen because of their refinement. The Rabbi's analogy: take ten glass candle holders, put each one inside the other, and put a candle inside. Because of the light and the transparency of the glass, it looks like one thing; you cannot see the ten glasses. So too the soul leaves, but it is too fine to be seen. Point 3: The waiting room What happens to the soul once it leaves the body? It is kept guarded until the end of days , when body and soul reunite. This will be developed at length in Essay 7. For Saadia, Olam HaBa is a world that will be created in the future , to be enjoyed by body and soul together. Everyone has a portion unless cut off, and each will be resurrected, reconnected, and enjoy it eternally according to his own level. \"But what happens until then?\" Someone who died today, or 2,000 years ago, must wait. (A participant called it \"the waiting room in Gan Eden,\" and the Rabbi agreed: \"Very good.\") Your condition in the waiting room matches your level in the world to come. A great portion of Olam HaBa means a higher level in the waiting room; a small portion, a lower one. The souls of the righteous are with God; \"the people who aren't so lucky are hovering around... not such a pleasant experience.\" This is where the concepts of Gan Eden and Gehinnom come from. They are kinds of waiting rooms, where soul is separate from body. The real bliss or the real consequence is Olam HaBa; \"this is not the real thing, it's a taste of what's going to be.\" So even a very righteous person who dies today is not fully enjoying Olam HaBa until he is reconnected with his body. The soul and the decomposing body Saadia adds a point that \"slightly explains the concept of Kaddish, twelve months.\" The hardest time for the soul is right after death. All souls suffer something until the body decomposes , because the soul sees what is happening to the body. \"Remember it's one entity.\" The body is being consumed by worms and maggots. Saadia's analogy: just as a person would be upset seeing the house he lived in for many years burned down or overrun with thorns and thistles, \"I lived here, I have memories here,\" so the soul is upset seeing the body consumed. How much pain depends on the person's level. Saadia says the worms are to a dead body like a needle to a living person. The Rabbi expanded: some people squirm and panic at the very thought of an injection; others say \"boom, put it in my vein, all is good.\" Same with the grave. The source is the verse in Iyov, ונפשו עליו תאבל , his soul mourns over him. \"This is what we mean when we talk about חיבוט הקבר ,\" the suffering of the grave. Some understand it as a literal beating; Saadia understands the pain as the soul watching the body decompose. Everyone has some moments of it, which is how one can explain the benefit of Kaddish in the first months, when the body is at the height of decomposition. A participant found all this very frightening. \"Well, it's reality. Whether you like it or not, we are eaten up by maggots and worms.\" Asked whether the soul waits for one year, he answered: no, until the world to come is created, which could be hundreds of years; the first stage of distress lasts until the body decomposes. Point 4: How long the wait lasts The separation lasts until Hashem has created all the human beings He intends to create . Then He will bring the soul from heaven and the body from the ground and reunite them, and techiyat hametim will begin. והרוח תשוב אל האלקים : the spirit returns to Hashem, and then returns to be reunited. \"The entire entity of the human will come together.\" What Olam HaBa will be like will come in Essay 9. The map ahead: Essay 7 is techiyat hametim , Essay 8 is ge'ulah , Essay 9 is Olam HaBa and reward and punishment. Summary Chapter 6 1. Every person is created with a set lifespan based on his constitution, which Hashem may lengthen or shorten according to his conduct (Chizkiyahu's fifteen years, the plague, \"either Hashem strikes him or his day comes\"). 2. God's knowledge of the final total does not take away our free will, as in Essay 4. 3. Don't calculate. Long life does not prove righteousness, nor short life wickedness. \"My thoughts are not your thoughts.\" No one can judge another's standing for the next world, because it depends on the struggle behind each choice. Chapter 7 1. We die of fear at the sight of a fiery, yellow angel full of blue eyes with a drawn sword. Saadia takes the Chachamim literally and finds each detail in Scripture. Hence too, he says, King David's lifelong cold. 2. We cannot see the soul leave because it is too fine, like ten nested glasses around a flame. 3. The soul waits in Gan Eden or Gehinnom, a waiting room matched to its future level, and suffers while it watches the body decompose: Saadia's reading of chibbut hakever , and a reason for Kaddish. 4. The wait ends when Hashem has created all the souls He intends; then body and soul are reunited for the resurrection. Coming Up Next week, at 6 to 7, the last shiur before Pesach: Chapter 8, gilgul . \"That will be a really fascinating shiur, I'm telling you that. We're going to survey all the different views with regards to gilgul.\"","_BkjUIX6EgQ":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 5 (Merits and Demerits), Chapter 6 and the first part of Chapter 7 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 10 February 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on a transcript of the recording. Recap: The Fifth Essay So Far The Rabbi opened by reviewing the three main things the essay has taught up to now. Mitzvot and aveirot leave an impression. Our zechuyot and chovot make an impress on the soul and on our identity, and הקדוש ברוך הוא remembers it all . Ten categories of people. He ran through them once more: # Name Who he is 1 צדיק A majority of mitzvot 2 רשע A majority of aveirot 3 עובד The obedient person, committed to one particular mitzvah 4 ממרה The disobedient person, who wilfully neglects one mitzvah he has no interest in keeping 5 שלם The complete person, who does everything 6 מקצר Negligent in the mitzvot aseh , the positive commandments 7 חוטא Transgresses negative prohibitions, but not the most severe ones 8 מזיד The rebellious person, who violates prohibitions carrying the death penalty or karet 9 כופר The denier: one who serves avodah zarah , denies God's existence, or has doubts. All three fall under \"denier\" 10 שב The penitent Last week covered the tenth category: the four aspects of teshuvah , which Saadia was the very first to enumerate, and which all the Rishonim after him took up, with the same idea and the same verses. Saadia also gave advice on how to keep one's resolve (writing and meditating on poems about the futility of human life) and listed things that help teshuvah: tefillah, tzedakah, and teaching others the ways of Hashem . Theodicy. Earlier chapters gave Saadia's explanation of why the righteous suffer and the wicked seem to prosper. \"Now we have three remaining chapters which are easier chapters to get through. There aren't great fundamental things there. They're more lists of things.\" The plan: a chapter and a half tonight, and the remaining chapter and a half next week. Chapter 6: A Chapter of Lists \"Remember we've said how Saadia loves lists, very often exhaustive lists.\" Chapter 6 is a string of side points that grow out of the discussion of teshuvah in Chapter 5. By the end of the shiur the Rabbi counted seven of them. Point 1: Seven things that prevent tefillah In Chapter 5 Saadia said that tefillah helps teshuvah. So he digresses to explain when tefillah does not work. Since prayer is an essential part of teshuvah, the message is: make sure you do not fall into any of these seven categories, \"so that your tefillah will not be worthless and your teshuvah will be effective.\" Of the seven, the recording preserves the Rabbi's discussion of the first two. 1. When the decree has been sealed. Sometimes Hashem passes an edict that cannot be changed. Moshe prayed to enter the Land, and the Chachamim say he prayed 515 times, the numerical value of ואתחנן . The answer was, in effect, \"that's it, there's nothing more to talk about.\" Sometimes, through no fault of your own, the decree has been passed, \"and there's nothing you can do even with the greatest of tefillah, and we have to understand that.\" 2. Prayer without concentration. Saadia quotes Tehillim 78: ויפתוהו בפיהם ובלשונם יכזבו לו ולבם לא נכון עמו . They tried to seduce Hashem with their lips, but their heart was not with Him, and so the prayer was not accepted. The Rabbi added a caveat from the Gemara in Ta'anit 8a . The Gemara says a person's prayer is only accepted if he prays with full attention and sincerity, and quotes the same verse. But the very next verse says והוא רחום יכפר עון , the merciful One still forgives, which seems to say that even without intent you are forgiven. The Gemara answers: in a congregation, even without full intent, prayer is still somewhat effective. When Saadia says prayer without kavanah is worth nothing, that applies to an individual praying alone. \"So sitting and reading lots of things and not understanding what you're reading doesn't have much value here.\" Point 2: Three things that block teshuvah Saadia now returns to teshuvah itself. The Rabbi reminded those who came to the Tuesday night shiurim on Rambam Hilchot Teshuvah : in Chapter 4 the Rambam lists 24 things that impede teshuvah, quoting the Rif , some more severe than others. Saadia is again the first to list such impediments, and he gives only three . \"This was then developed and ended up by the Rambam's time to 24.\" All sins can be forgiven aside from these three: 1. Being a bad influence on others. Either philosophically, teaching people the wrong ideas, or practically, giving wrong psakim . This cannot be rectified, because the effect is already out of your hands: \"how can you remedy that when you've told many people the wrong thing?\" Saadia brings the verse משגה ישרים בדרך רע , one who leads the upright astray onto an evil path. The same verse ends that the innocent \"will still inherit goodness\": those who followed in sincerity, thinking they were doing right, are not blamed. The Rambam says the same of one who sins and causes the public to sin, as with the idols set up in public places. \"The greater the influence, the more a person needs to be careful that he doesn't lead them down the wrong path.\" 2. Defaming someone. This too cannot be rectified. The Rabbi brought the halachah, cited by the Rema in the Shulchan Aruch , in the laws of asking forgiveness. Normally, if someone asks you sincerely and genuinely, you have to forgive. But there is one sin you do not have to forgive: public defamation. Even if he asks three times, brings different people each time and is sincere, there is no obligation. \"You stole money from me, give the money back, we can rectify that. You hurt me, you hit me, you can rectify that. But if you defame me, how are we going to do that? How are we going to get those words back?\" 3. Having forbidden property in your possession. You can do teshuvah, but while the stolen item is still with you, it does not work. Saadia brings the verse חבל ישיב רשע גזלה ישלם : the wicked man should return what he has taken, and then he will walk in the ways of life. If the victim has died, return it to his estate; if that is impossible, make it hefker . The Rambam adds another possibility: donate it to public funds, so that it benefits the community rather than whoever happens to pick it up. \"Don't do this in the first instance. This is when you've got no alternative.\" Questions on restitution Q: If I stole something and used it, can I rectify it by returning it and paying for the benefit? Most certainly. In fact, halachically, simply returning it is enough. If your use did not devalue it, you do not even have to pay for the use; it was wrong, but there is no financial liability. If you did devalue it, you return it and pay for the loss in value. Q: What if I changed the object into something new? Then שינוי קונה : the change acquires it, and it now belongs to you. But you must pay the value of the original and ask for mechilah . Sometimes two changes are needed, a change in the thing itself and a change in its name, before it becomes yours according to everyone. The principle is like the idea in English law of putting the person back in his original position. There are two elements: the material restitution, and the mechilah . \"When you have the two together, then you can ask me\" for forgiveness, not before. Point 3: Four sins that bring consequences even after teshuvah \"This is something really interesting, which you probably never heard before. This is the first time I've ever seen it when I saw it inside... To the best of my knowledge, it's his own idea, and it's not really replicated in later authorities.\" Saadia says there are certain sins for which teshuvah works, yet you will still have to suffer something in this world. He reads the Torah's phrase לא ינקה , \"he will not be absolved,\" to mean exactly this: teshuvah rectifies your place in the world to come, but some consequence will come in this world. The four: Swearing in vain. כי לא ינקה ה' את אשר ישא את שמו לשוא. (The Rabbi paused over the text: the older editions have the word את , matching the verse, which reassured him that nothing was missing.) Murder. The verse speaks of blood that \"will not be cleansed.\" Adultery. \"He will not be absolved.\" Perjury. A false witness, one who lies in court, \"will not be absolved.\" The Rabbi first named the first item as perjury and then corrected himself: the first is swearing in vain and the fourth is perjury. Is there a source? The Talmud Yerushalmi at the beginning of Pe'ah says something slightly similar. Just as the Mishnah lists things whose fruits you enjoy in this world while the principal remains for the next, the Yerushalmi lists sins that are punished in this world with something remaining for the next. Two of the four in that list match Saadia's, adultery and murder; for the other two Saadia has perjury and swearing in vain. But the Rabbi did not think it really proves Saadia's point. The Yerushalmi says that if you don't do teshuvah , you will suffer in both worlds. \"It doesn't say that if I do full teshuvah that I'm going to suffer in this world, right? So that's why I find this a very interesting paragraph.\" Point 4: Sins against another person If you have hurt someone, abused them or struck them, teshuvah alone does not work. It depends on the forgiveness of the person you hurt. Saadia brings Yosef's brothers, who said אנא שא נא , \"please forgive.\" You must ask three times . If he has died before you could ask, Saadia says you must ask in the presence of ten people, three times . The Rabbi found this very interesting, because the Gemara and the Poskim do not say it in this form. The halachah says to take ten people and ask forgiveness; it does not say three times. Why ten? The Rishonim explain it from how one asks a living person: first you ask him privately, once. If he says no, you bring three people and ask again; if no, another three; if no, another three. \"Think about it: you got him and three, three, three is nine, is ten.\" Saadia, however, requires both: ten people and three times. Later it became customary to go to the grave, \"but really in the presence of ten people suffices.\" Point 5: Three mitzvot rewarded in this world Even a person who ends up denying God and committing the worst sins will receive some reward in this world if he has done any of these, because the Torah promises long life for them: Honouring parents. Sending away the mother bird. Saadia reads this broadly, \"as being a general concept of welfare, of animal welfare, not a specific thing of the bird and the chicken.\" Honesty in business : אבן שלמה וצדק , a whole and honest weight, with the promise of long days. A fourth case: when good has been decreed for someone, as with Yehu , who was promised that four generations of his descendants would sit on the throne, even though he and his children sinned. The Gemara's problem. Classically, only two mitzvot are mentioned with long life, kibbud av va'em and shiluach haken , although Saadia is \"entirely right\" that the Torah also says it of honest weights. The famous Gemara tells of a man whose father told him to climb a ladder and send away the mother bird. He was doing both mitzvot at once, and he fell and died. Because of this, the Gemara concludes that there is no real reward in this world: \"long life\" means life in the world that is eternally long. The Rambam understands it the same way. Saadia, as so often, stays committed to the verse. The main meaning may be what the Chachamim say, long life in the world to come, \"but nonetheless, there still is something here... and therefore it must be that in this world you will receive something.\" Q: Doesn't charity come into it? The verse says צדקה תציל ממות , charity saves from death. That means it saves you from a particular instance of death. \"Doesn't mean it prolongs your life... It's not the same as you'll have long days.\" It is a minor point; the big point is that for these three, there has to be some manifestation of reward in this world. Point 6: Three sins the Torah calls \"a sin in you\" Saadia adds, in parentheses: although there are four sins whose consequences must be felt in this world, do not think there are more. For three sins the Torah says והיה בך חטא , \"it will be a sin in you\": delaying vows , refusing to lend to a poor person , and not paying employees on time . Even so, full teshuvah works for them, and you will not suffer any consequence. Point 7: Five levels of teshuvah Saadia gives five levels, in descending order. (The Rambam has three.) The same capability. You repent in exactly the same circumstances in which you sinned, and you hold back. Less capability. You repent when the circumstances are not the same. When threatened. When threatened and the punishment has begun. As you die. Even teshuvah at the time of death is still considered teshuvah. This, says Saadia, is why it is customary at a deathbed to tell the dying person to say ותהא מיתתי כפרה על כל עונותי , let my death be an atonement for all my sins. The Rabbi noted that this custom, found in the Siddurim, is a later idea, though the Gemara does say that death atones. He added a practical word. We do this as close to death as possible, so that the person is conscious of it before he dies, but \"it doesn't have to be the last second.\" He has seen people go every hour to make sure it is said just as the person dies. \"No point in that. Once the person can no longer sin, is in palliative care, then that's it. You do it once and finished.\" Summary of Chapter 6 # Point 1 Seven things that prevent tefillah being accepted 2 Three things that block teshuvah: misleading others, defamation, holding stolen property 3 Four sins with a consequence in this world even after full teshuvah: swearing in vain, murder, adultery, perjury 4 Sins against others depend on the victim's forgiveness; if he has died, ask three times before ten people 5 Three mitzvot guaranteed some reward in this world: honouring parents, animal welfare, honesty in business 6 Three sins called \"a sin in you\" that full teshuvah still clears: delaying vows, not lending to the poor, late wages 7 Five levels of teshuvah, down to teshuvah at death \"Again, you see Saadia loves the list. All of them have got lists.\" Chapter 7, Part 1: The Eleventh Category, the Beinoni Chapter 7 has two parts. The first introduces an eleventh category; the second gives eleven contrasts in the weight of mitzvot, to be done next week. The eleventh category is the בינוני , the \"equal\" person: one whose sins and merits are equal in number. Saadia then writes six words about him that can be read in two ways, and \"there's a huge difference\" between them. Two readings of one line Reading 1: \"If he is found to receive the mercy of God, he will be counted with the righteous.\" The emphasis is on Hashem's compassion. Reading 2: \"If such a person can be found, he will receive compassion and be counted with the tzaddikim.\" The emphasis is on how rare he is. Why it matters: the Rambam's beinoni When the class learned the Rambam, they saw that the Rambam rejects the idea of someone who is exactly fifty-fifty, because the statistical chance of finding such a person, taking into account everything about him, is almost nil: \"maybe one person in seven and eight billion people.\" Yet the Gemara has a great deal to say about the beinoni and how Hashem's kindness tips him toward the tzaddikim. So for the Rambam, the beinoni is not a fifty-fifty tally of mitzvot. \"It's about your identity, what kind of person you are, and this is why he writes the majority of us are beinonim.\" We live our lives in contradiction, with some good values and some bad values, sometimes more of one, sometimes more of the other. That is very common, which is why the Gemara needs to tell us so much about the beinoni. If Saadia agrees, then he must be read the first way: Hashem has mercy and counts this person among the tzaddikim. He would not write \"if he can be found\" about something so common. But some read Saadia the second way, as understanding the beinoni statistically: an exact fifty-fifty, and if you can find such a person, he will receive mercy. \"This line really is a crucial line to understand Saadia's philosophy with the beinoni... That's open for discussion.\" The Rabbi's own leaning: \"I much more understand the idea of it being a more holistic identity approach.\" Either way, Saadia's point is the same. Whether you are fifty-fifty in your identity or in your actions, Hashem's kindness counts you among the tzaddikim. Mercy is not an attribute Saadia adds a reminder of what the class learned in the early essays of the book. When we speak of Hashem's mercy, we do not mean that mercy is an attribute of God. \"Not possible... there's no attributes to Him.\" Rather, it is described in relation to us, the creatures: it is one of the ways in which Hashem appears to us as compassionate. And that compassion is that He places us among the tzaddikim even though we are fifty-fifty. Three places where Hashem's mercy appears He accepts our teshuvah , our return to Him. He listens to our prayers in deep pain, in the most difficult times of despair. He places the beinoni in the category of the righteous. This is what is meant by מטה כלפי חסד : He tilts toward kindness. \"At the end of the day, there are only two categories: tzaddik and rasha. There's no third category.\" The beinoni does not remain a category of his own. Hashem's kindness is that if you are fifty-fifty, He puts you with the righteous. Summary Tefillah has limits. It cannot overturn a sealed decree, as Moshe's 515 prayers show, and prayer without kavanah is of little worth for an individual, though a congregation's prayer still helps (Ta'anit 8a). Three things block teshuvah : leading others astray, public defamation, and keeping stolen property. Saadia lists three; by the Rambam's time the list had grown to 24. Restitution plus mechilah is the formula for wrongs against others. Saadia's own chiddush : four sins (swearing in vain, murder, adultery, perjury) carry a consequence in this world even after full teshuvah. The Yerushalmi in Pe'ah is similar but speaks only of one who has not repented. Wrongs against others need the victim's forgiveness; after his death, Saadia requires asking three times before ten people. Three mitzvot carry reward in this world. Saadia insists on the plain sense of \"long days,\" against the Gemara's and the Rambam's reading of eternal life. Five levels of teshuvah , down to \"let my death be an atonement.\" The beinoni is counted with the tzaddikim. Whether he is a rare statistical fifty-fifty or, as the Rambam and the Rabbi prefer, the common person of mixed identity, Hashem tilts toward kindness. Coming Up Next week the class will finish the rest of Chapter 7, the eleven contrasts in the weight of deeds , and Chapter 8, \"eleven really random... separate things,\" closing the essay with points about thoughts and other matters. In two weeks' time: Essay 6, on the soul , \"a very interesting and deep essay.\"","HwmdMPBGQ3I":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 6 (The Soul), Chapters 4 and 5 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 17 March 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: What the Soul Is The first shiur after Purim. The plan: Chapters 4 and 5 tonight; Chapters 6 and 7 next week, at 6:45 (\"the final week before the clocks change\"); and Chapter 8, the last of the essay, the week after, before a break for Pesach. Chapter 1: what the nefesh is not : not an accidental by-product of the body, not air, not fire, not two kinds of air, and not the blood, as Anan thought. Chapter 2: why Saadia went through the other opinions: understanding the soul is complex and subtle. Hence מי יודע , \"who knows\" the subtle difference between lofty and lowly souls. Chapter 3: Saadia's seven points: The soul is created , not eternal. It is created simultaneously with the body . There is no warehouse of souls, \"which immediately, by the way, will negate the whole concept of gilgulim ,\" to come in Chapter 8. It is lofty and ethereal , finer than the spheres, since it has intellect and (for Saadia, unlike the Rambam) the spheres do not. It is independently wise , not dependent on the body. It only works together with the body . There is one soul with different faculties , hence its names: nefesh for desire, ruach for emotion, neshamah for intellect, chayah because it lives eternally, yechidah because it is unique. The Rambam says the same, with five faculties. It dwells in the heart . \"These two chapters, we're going to do quickly. They're pretty straightforward.\" Chapter 4: Why Would God Put a Lofty Soul in a Lowly Body? Having shown that the soul is lofty, ethereal and unique, finer even than the spheres, Saadia takes up the question people ask: why would HaKadosh Baruch Hu join it to something as lowly as the human body? People conclude that God is doing the soul a wrong. Step 1: God does no wrong A general principle. From Essays 1 and 2, it is impossible to believe God would do wrong to any of His creatures. He does not need them; He gives them their very being. And God is entirely good: one of Saadia's main answers to why God created the universe was to bestow goodness . Why would He create the soul in order to harm it? A specific argument. People do harm for one of three reasons , \"and there's no fourth reason.\" When anyone has ever done you harm, it came from one of these: Why people do harm Why it cannot apply to God 1 Fear : he is afraid you will hurt him, so he attacks first God fears no one 2 Desire : you have something he wants, money or belongings God desires nothing 3 Ignorance : he misunderstands the nature of something God knows everything Saadia finds all three in one verse: אשר לא נשא פני שרים ולא נכר שוע לפני דל כי מעשה ידיו כלם (Iyov 34:19) \"He does not favour princes\" : people favour the powerful because they fear them; He fears no one. \"He does not prefer the rich over the poor\" : people favour the wealthy because they want their wealth; He wants nothing. \"For they are all the work of His hands\" : having made them all, He knows them all and all their actions. Step 2: The soul cannot function alone Fine, God is not harming the soul. But why join them? Because that is the nature of the soul: it cannot function alone. It must be joined to something in order to be active, and only by being active can it reach eternal bliss . When a person does good, the soul gains light; when he sins, it is darkened. אור זרע לצדיק , light is sown for the righteous: the soul is illuminated, while one who goes astray is shadowed, darkened, lost. \"When we say we ... we're talking about the guf and the nefesh together.\" For Saadia, as these chapters show \"black and white,\" body and soul act as one. The nefesh directs and the body is its vehicle, but a human's good deed is the deed of both together. Refining silver and gold Hashem examines whether the soul does good or bad. Saadia compares this to refining silver and gold in fire : the precious metal remains and the dross is burnt away. \"The Chachamim did the same, by the way. They always liked this analogy.\" וצרפתים כצרף את הכסף , \"I will refine them as silver is refined.\" בחנני כזהב אצא , \"test me, and I shall come out like gold.\" But those that keep their dross are כסף נמאס , \"rejected silver,\" and will not have great bliss in the future. Even a soul with impurities can still purify itself. That is teshuvah, possible as long as one is alive. \"Once your soul leaves your body at death, then there's no more hope. But whilst the soul is still active together with the body, there is hope.\" Step 3: Then why not leave the soul alone? Wouldn't it be better if God simply created the soul and left it alone, free of the body? No: alone, it would not reach bliss , because it only reaches eternal bliss by working with the body and choosing good . \"Just like fire can only work together with something combustible.\" Without something to burn, there is no fire. And without each other, neither is functional . When we die, the body without the soul is nothing; the soul without the body cannot act. Then there would be no point to creating them, and no point to the universe, because, as Saadia argued at the start of the free will essay and as the start of Bereishit shows, the universe was created for the human being . Step 4: Then why not give the soul a different nature? Why not create a soul that can attain goodness without being joined to a body? Saadia answers with a principle he has used many times: this is like asking why God did not make a human being with the powers of the angels . \"That's no longer a human. You're asking that a man won't be a man and a soul won't be a soul.\" A soul that worked alone would be something else, a sphere, an angel. \"Beautiful words.\" It is like saying, \"I like fire, but I want fire to go down; I want water to go up,\" or wanting fire to cool and snow to warm. You are asking for them not to be fire and water. Those who ask this \"are completely perverting intellect,\" Saadia says. True wisdom is for things to be according to their essence. The Rabbi singled this out: \"This is a line which nowadays people would do good to remember. Look at this key line here.\" Wisdom is not defined by how a person wants it to be. \"Many of the problems of the world which we live in nowadays stem from a lack of understanding of this one line in Saadia. People want biology, they want nature, they want science to be according to the way they want it to be. No. Facts are facts. Wisdom is wisdom.\" Or as Yeshayahu puts it, היאמר חמר ליצרו מה תעשה , \"shall the clay say to its Maker, what are you doing?\" A participant summed it up: the channel of bliss for the soul is action , mitzvot, and action needs a body. \"That's precisely what it is. You summarised what he said.\" Step 5: Why then does the soul suffer? If God wants only good for the soul, why does it sin and suffer? It chooses to: אשר עשה האלהים את האדם ישר והמה בקשו חשבנות רבים , \"God made man upright, but they sought many schemes.\" Step 6: Is the body really impure? \"This is fascinating.\" Even granting all that, isn't the soul still mixed with something impure , the body? Saadia rejects the premise. \"Very different to how we are often taught,\" especially in Kabbalistic literature, that the body is inherently bad and the soul inherently good. The human body is pure at its core. Impurity ( tumah ) is not something rational , so one cannot extend it by reasoning to places the Torah did not. The Torah makes only certain emissions of the body impure, and only once they are emitted. While they are within the person, they do not make him impure. Those who say otherwise \"are making up their own Torah, and want us to accept it. We're not going to accept that.\" So part of the question, why put the lofty soul in a lowly body, falls away: who said the body is lowly? \"True, the body is not like the soul. Accept that. But don't tell me the body is this kind of impure, terrible thing.\" The German pietists. The Rabbi contrasted this with the medieval Chassidei Ashkenaz , whose literature, he said, \"you'll be shocked to read\": a self-flagellating negation of the body, rolling in snow to deaden the body's feeling, letting bees sting them. \"It's literally in Sefer HaRokeach and Sefer Chassidim ... because the body is bad and we have to negate the body. There's none of that in Saadia.\" Why is a dead body impure, then? The greatest impurity is a corpse. On Saadia's principle, that is because there has been an emission: the departure of the soul . Tumah is essentially a vacuum ; when a person dies, the body is emptied of its soul. The Or HaChaim , Rabbi Chaim ben Attar, some 250 years ago, uses this to explain why only Jewish bodies convey impurity: in his very Kabbalistic understanding, the soul of a Jew differs from that of a non-Jew, so its departure leaves a greater vacuum. \"I don't think Saadia would agree. Certainly the Rambam wouldn't agree.\" Halachically, by the strict halachah of the Shulchan Aruch and certainly of the Sephardi authorities, a Kohen may touch non-Jewish bodies and walk in non-Jewish cemeteries . The Rabbi mentioned the episode \"a year or so ago\" when Chassidim in Stamford Hill petitioned for a special route avoiding the Science Museum in South Kensington, which connects to the tube station and holds many human bones. \"Misplaced, in my humble opinion. If you want to be machmir , that's great, but it's a chumrah . The halachah is that a non-Jewish body does not make a Kohen impure.\" Two reasons a soul suffers Saadia closes the chapter: Its own sins. Like a person who goes out in the cold and then asks why he is ill: it is his own fault. ערום ראה רעה ונסתר ופתיים עברו ונענשו , \"the prudent see trouble and hide; the simple go on and suffer for it.\" The soul has been given the choice. To do it good in the future , as discussed in previous essays: to test it and raise it up. אשרי הגבר אשר תיסרנו יה , \"happy is the man whom You chasten.\" Summary of Chapter 4 Question Answer Is God harming the soul by putting it in a body? No. God does no harm; people harm out of fear, desire or ignorance, none of which applies to Him Then why join them? The soul cannot function alone ; only by acting and choosing good through the body can it gain light and eternal bliss Why not leave it alone? Alone it is non-functional, the body is non-functional, and the universe loses its purpose Why not give it a different nature? Then it would not be a soul. Asking for that is like asking fire to be cold. Wisdom is for things to be according to their essence Why does it suffer? Its own choices, or suffering for its future good Isn't the body impure? No. Only emissions are impure; death is the soul's departure Chapter 5: Body and Soul Are One Unit \"Chapter 5 is quite short.\" Saadia emphasises that body and soul are one unit , and everything that happens to them in the future happens to them together. \"We're going to dwell on this in great detail in the next essay.\" For Saadia, as for the Ramban , Olam HaBa is guf and nefesh together : inseparable in this world and the next. וייצר ה' אלהים את האדם עפר מן האדמה ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים : one human being. They are separated for a time at death, which Chapters 6 and 7 will discuss, but they are created together and will receive their bliss together. Three mistaken views Reward and punishment are for... Their proof 1 The soul alone נפש כי תחטא , \"a soul that sins\"; הנפש החטאת היא תמות , \"the soul that sins shall die\" 2 The body alone יבוא כל בשר להשתחות לפני , \"all flesh shall come to bow before Me\" 3 The bones alone ותהי עונתם על עצמותם , \"their iniquities are upon their bones\" (Yechezkel); כל עצמותי תאמרנה , \"all my bones shall say\" The third is the view of Binyamin al-Nahawandi , a Persian Karaite who lived about a hundred years before Saadia. Chapter 1 met the first Karaite, Anan; the Rabbi named Anan, Binyamin and Shaul as the three famous early Karaites. Saadia: all three are wrong. Reward and punishment are for soul and body together . And all three make the same mistake: they do not understand how Hebrew is used in Tanach. \"The soul.\" The Torah also says נפש אשר תגע , \"a soul that touches,\" and נפש אשר תאכל , \"a soul that eats.\" A soul cannot touch or eat. So nefesh in such verses means the whole person , body and soul. \"The flesh.\" The Torah also says ויברך כל בשר שם קדשו , \"all flesh shall bless His holy name.\" Flesh cannot bless without a soul; speech and blessing need the soul. So basar too means the whole person. \"The bones.\" Binyamin, says Saadia, \"is not stupid.\" In anatomy it is true that the skeleton is the main structure of the body and the flesh, sinews and nerves are built around it. But Binyamin knew one cannot carry one discipline into another, Torah into anatomy. His real source was the verses, and \"all my bones shall say\" shows that bones cannot speak; it means my whole essence . Shaul's burial Binyamin went so far as to say that the people burnt Shaul's body . The story: defeated, Shaul fell on his sword after his armour-bearer refused to kill him. The Philistines cut off his head, stripped him and displayed his body. The men of Yavesh Gilad risked their lives, came by night, and took the bodies of Shaul and his sons: ויבאו יבשה וישרפו אתם שם. ויקחו את עצמתיהם ויקברו They came to Yavesh and burnt them there. And they took their bones and buried them. Binyamin read it literally: they burnt the bodies and kept only the bones, \"because the bones are the main thing; who cares about the body?\" Saadia rejects this absolutely. וישרפו אתם means they burnt for them : out of respect, they burnt the king's remaining belongings so others would not use them. Compare ויבך אתו אביו , \"his father wept him ,\" which means wept for him. And \"bones\" means the person: הניחו את עצמתי אצל עצמתיו , \"lay my bones beside his bones\"; Moshe took the bones of Yosef , meaning Yosef himself. The Radak , the Rabbi pointed out, brings both readings as acceptable: that they burnt the beds, belongings and embalming materials out of honour for the king; or, without naming Binyamin, that they burnt the flesh because it had begun to decay and be infested, and out of respect did not bury it in that state. Saadia rejects the second outright. The five organs of speech Saadia's principle of Hebrew usage: when something comes from several parts, Scripture will name one of them and mean the whole. Speech needs five organs, and the Tanach uses each by itself: Organ Verse פה , mouth פי יספר צדקתך , \"my mouth will tell of Your righteousness\" לשון , tongue ולשוני תהגה צדקך , \"my tongue will speak of Your righteousness\" שפה , lip שפתי ישבחונך , \"my lips will praise You\" חך , palate אל חכך שופר , \"to your palate, the shofar\" גרון , throat קרא בגרון , \"cry out with the throat\" \"Don't tell me that when he calls with his throat he only used his throat.\" The Rabbi noted the same five sources of speech appear in Hebrew grammar and in the Kabbalah, with different letters coming from different organs; but every letter still needs the whole apparatus. So too a human being is soul, flesh and bones together. Scripture sometimes says soul, sometimes flesh, sometimes bones, sometimes skin, and means the whole person . Even בביתה לא ישכנו רגליה , \"her feet do not stay in her house\": it is not about her feet. From reason, Scripture and tradition Saadia has shown from reason and from Scripture that body and soul are one entity. He ends with tradition , the famous Gemara : The soul will say in judgment, it's not my fault, it's the body's; the body will say, it's the soul's. A king had an orchard and set two guards over it, one lame and one blind . The lame man could not reach the fruit; the blind man could not see it. So the blind man carried the lame man on his shoulders, and together they took the fruit. Each pleaded innocence before the king. But they did it together, and they are both equally liable . So too body and soul: one unit, judged together. Summary God does no harm : people harm from fear, desire or ignorance, none of which applies to Him (Iyov 34:19). The soul cannot function alone. Through the body it acts, chooses good, gains light and is refined like silver and gold; while alive, it can always purify itself through teshuvah. A soul that needs no body would not be a soul , any more than cold fire. \" True wisdom is for things to be according to their essence ,\" a line the Rabbi urged for our times. The body is not inherently impure : impurity attaches only to emissions, and death is the soul's departure. Contrast the Chassidei Ashkenaz' negation of the body and the Or HaChaim's view of tumah, with its halachic implications for Kohanim. Body and soul are one unit , judged and rewarded together. The views that Olam HaBa is for the soul, the body or (with Binyamin al-Nahawandi ) the bones alone all misread Hebrew, which names one part to mean the whole, as with the five organs of speech, and as in Saadia's reading of Shaul's burial. The lame and the blind : tradition confirms what reason and Scripture teach. Coming Up Next week, at 6:45: Chapters 6 and 7 , what happens when the soul leaves the body. The week after: Chapter 8, reincarnation , \"which is a lot to talk about.\" Then a break for Pesach.","v0QDJydDYE4":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 6 (The Soul), Chapters 2 and 3 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 10 March 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Six Views That Saadia Rejects The sixth essay, on the nefesh , follows creation from nothing, HaKadosh Baruch Hu, revelation, free will, and merits and demerits. Of its eight chapters, the first five deal with the essence of the soul, Chapters 6 and 7 with what happens after death, and Chapter 8 with reincarnation. (During March, while sunset is later, the shiur would start later than usual, returning to 6 o'clock once the clocks change.) Last week, in Chapter 1, Saadia went through six views of the soul and tried to disprove each: The soul is an accident ( mikreh ), a by-product of the body, not an intended creation, in five versions: a self-moving number of knowledge, the completion of the body, the four natures, the coordination of the senses, or a by-product of the blood. It is air . It is fire . It has two parts (rational and animal). It is two kinds of air . It is the blood (Anan, the Karaite leader). The seventh view, Saadia's own, comes in Chapter 3. First, a short digression in Chapter 2. Chapter 2: \"Who Knows?\" Why did Saadia go into the six wrong views? Because, as he said of creation from nothing in Essay 1 and the essence of God in Essay 2, we are dealing with something very subtle and deep . The soul has the same status. That is why there are so many conflicting views. So too, Shlomo HaMelech praises one who can understand the rational, speaking soul of man: מי יודע רוח בני האדם העלה היא למעלה, ורוח הבהמה הירדת היא למטה לארץ (Kohelet 3:21) Who knows the spirit of man that goes upward, and the spirit of the animal that goes down to the earth? Not a doubt, but an affirmation Some read the verse as a question: who knows whether the human spirit goes up and the animal's goes down? Nobody knows. Saadia completely negates that reading. \"Who knows\" does not express doubt; it establishes that there is a difference, and praises whoever can grasp it. It is like saying, \"Who knows how wise Reuven is? Who knows how hard-working Shimon is?\" You are not casting doubt; you are affirming that Reuven is wise and Shimon hard-working. The wonder is that two souls in two bodies look identical , yet are very different. A few verses earlier Kohelet says, ומותר האדם מן הבהמה אין , \"man has no advantage over the beast\" (which we say in Shacharit): we live, die, procreate and eat alike, and הכל הולך אל מקום אחד , all go to one place, to dust. So who can discern the difference in their souls? Do not think Shlomo is asking whether there is a difference. \"No one wise would say something like this. This goes against logic. Even an ignorant person wouldn't believe there's no difference.\" We see that humans are more honoured than animals; we make animals work for us and ride on them. The bodies are alike because they are made of the same nature. The question is who can understand the difference , like two stones, one ordinary and one a ruby. Both are stones; one is worth millions and one a few pennies. You need to be an expert in jewellery to know why. The difference applies not only between human and animal souls, but between human souls too : some are lofty and some lowly. Saadia offers a second reading: it is not a question at all but a statement. Whoever knows, knows : one who understands will see that one soul is lofty and another lowly. \"That's the end of Chapter 2. We don't have to spend too much time on it.\" Chapter 3: Saadia's Seven Points on the Soul Point 1: The soul is created Based on Essay 1, nothing is eternal except God . Saadia is refuting Plato , who held that the soul is co-eternal with God. No matter, no substance, no soul existed before creation. The proof: ויצר רוח אדם בקרבו , \"He forms the spirit of man within him.\" \"Relatively straightforward.\" Point 2: Each soul is created with its own body When does God create the soul? Simultaneously with the body , whether at birth or, \"more likely,\" with the forming of the fetus: \"it makes no difference really.\" Yotzer ruach adam bekirbo : He forms it in him, together with him. \"Now why is this important? I'll tell you why.\" Kabbalistic literature, and in truth some Midrashim, are full of the idea that Adam HaRishon contained all the souls to be created. The Rabbi recalled the Ramchal (from Da'at Tevunot , learnt by some of the class): all souls were united in Adam; when he sinned, failing the test of recognising Hashem's unity, there was a splitting up, and all the different souls we have today emerged. The Ramban clearly holds that all souls were created at the beginning (\"I'm not sure what the Rambam holds\"), and there are Gemarot to the effect that there is a storehouse of souls from which each soul is placed into a body when it is born. Saadia rejects all of this. Each soul is individual, created together with its body. His only source is the same verse, and it can be read either way: \"He creates the spirit in man\" could mean He puts in a soul that already existed. \"Neither interpretation is muchrach .\" But Saadia is very clear. A question: didn't Saadia say last week that when the storehouse of souls is used up, the world to come begins? The Rabbi: \"I understand why you think he said that. When I learnt it, I also had that question.\" He reread the opening of Chapter 1: Hashem separates body and soul until He has completed the number of souls He decides to create ; then this world ends, a new world is created, and body and soul are reunited. There is a set number of humans Hashem wants to create, but no storehouse . \"You're thinking of that because the Gemara says something along those lines. Saadia is rejecting that.\" A digression: Chassidic dynasties The Rabbi digressed \"really quickly\" to show where the storehouse idea leads. In the Chassidic world, the son inherits his father's leadership. On what basis? Chassidut is about 300 years old, and the dynasty was not there at the very beginning; it developed in a later generation, perhaps 250 years ago. The explanation offered was that a holy Rebbe, through his elevated thoughts at the time of conception, can draw down a holy soul from the storehouse. So even if the son does not seem as holy or learned, his soul must be holy. \"I've seen with my own eyes Chassidic rabbis leading dynasties, not the big ones, who are ignorant, who are not talmidei chachamim . They don't know halachah.\" Why? Because his father must have drawn down a very holy spark. \"None of this is acceptable in Saadia's mind.\" Point 3: The soul is a substance purer than the heavenly spheres The soul's essence is a pure substance, like the purity of the spheres . It receives light , as the spheres receive light from the stars, but its essence is purer and more refined than the spheres, and therefore it is endowed with intellect and speech . It is a substance , not an accident; an intentional creation in every creature. It is like the spheres : a sphere has no light of its own; its brightness depends on how much light the stars shine on it. So the soul's brightness depends on a person's intellect, virtues and good deeds , which illuminate it, or his wicked deeds, which darken it. This is what Saadia taught at the start of Essay 5: every action leaves an impression on the soul. Everyone agrees with this, and the Ramchal in Derech Hashem elaborates on it at length. The illumination or darkness is not always felt, because the soul is concealed within the body; when it is freed, it shows how much light or darkness it has gathered in its lifetime. It is finer than the spheres , because for Saadia the spheres have no intellect, reason or speech, and the soul does. \"I can't tell you exactly what it is.\" The word the Rabbi was looking for: ethereal . The soul is an ethereal substance like that of the spheres, \"but even more ethereal.\" He found this view attributed to John Philoponus , a Greek-Egyptian philosopher in the Aristotelian tradition, some centuries before Saadia: the soul as a kind of light of the heavenly spheres. \"Whether Saadia got it from him or not, I don't know.\" The Rambam differs on one point: in the Mishneh Torah (Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah, the Rabbi thought chapter 3) he holds that the spheres have some kind of intellect , though not speech. \"I don't fully understand it myself. Maybe one day we'll go into it.\" Saadia's two proofs Saadia recalls the four sources of knowledge from his introduction: sense perception, reason, inference and tradition . He draws on two of them. Reason. When the soul is joined to the body, a person is full of wonders: life, reason, speech. When the soul departs, the body is barren of all of it. So these all stem from the soul. If the soul were an ordinary earthly substance, it could not have such wonders; and since the spheres lack intellect and speech, the soul must be more refined than the spheres . Scripture. Pure souls shine when they do good, as the spheres shine with the light of the stars: והמשכלים יזהרו כזהר הרקיע , \"the wise shall shine like the brightness of the firmament\" (Daniel). Wicked souls are like a sphere with no light on it: ושמים לא זכו בעיניו, אף כי נתעב ונאלח איש שתה כמים עולה , \"the heavens are not pure in His eyes; how much less one who is abominable, who drinks iniquity like water\" (Iyov). Scripture would not compare the soul to bright or dim spheres unless they were similar in substance. \"Who knows\" again Saadia returns once more to the verse from Chapter 2, citing והרוח תשוב אל האלהים אשר נתנה , \"and the spirit returns to God who gave it,\" from the end of Kohelet. \"I don't know why he says this now. I couldn't understand this.\" The Rabbi's suggestion: having just spoken of bright and dim souls, Saadia reinforces that there are different kinds of souls, and that \"who knows\" affirms the difference rather than questioning it. Point 4: The soul is intelligent in itself The soul has intelligence of its own nature , not from the body, which has no such function. Saadia's proof is \"something very interesting... apparently this was known even in Saadia's time\": a blind person sees in his dreams . We dream of what our senses took in during the day. But a blind person has never seen anything, and yet sees things in his dreams. (The Rabbi thought this is supported scientifically today, in what is called haptic perception.) He did not get this from his eyes, so he must get it from the soul. This also answers the view from last week that the soul is the coordination of the senses . \"You're doing it the wrong way around.\" It is not the coordination of the senses that produces the soul; the soul gives the coordination of the senses . \"It is driving the senses; the senses are not driving the soul.\" ( Philippe offered a modern analogy: the relation between a mobile phone and the cloud.) Point 5: The soul acts only through an instrument \"Straightforward.\" The soul's functions only manifest through an instrument, an organ: the body . The collaboration of body and soul lets the soul's potential become actual. Point 6: Three faculties, and the names of the soul Once body and soul are joined, the soul has three faculties: Faculty Hebrew Meaning Discernment כח ההכרה Reason, logic Anger כח הכעס Not literally anger: strong emotions Desire כח התאוה The appetitive faculty This is Plato's division of the soul: reason; spirit, which houses anger and the emotions; and appetite, which houses the desire for physical pleasures. That is why the soul has different names. Not because there are different souls or parts, but because of its different faculties: Name Faculty Verse נפש Desire כי תאוה נפשך , \"when your nefesh desires\" רוח Emotion כל רוחו יוציא כסיל , \"a fool vents all his ruach \" נשמה Intellect ונשמת שדי תבינם , \"the neshamah of the Almighty gives them understanding\" Saadia adds two more names : חיה , because the soul lives on for as long as Hashem wills, eternally; יחידה , because it is unique : there is nothing like it. The Rabbi asked where the soul is called this in a passage connected to Purim. After giving Flora ten seconds, he answered: Tehillim 22, the psalm of Purim, הצילה מחרב נפשי, מיד כלב יחידתי , \"save my soul from the sword, my yechidah from the dog.\" One soul, not three: Saadia and the Rambam against the philosophers and the Kabbalists Plato did not only divide the soul into three faculties; he placed them in three different parts of the body (the Rabbi thought brain, heart and liver). Saadia rejects that: there is one soul with three faculties . \"It's interesting that the Rambam says exactly the same.\" In the first of his Shemonah Perakim , learnt about a year and a half earlier, the Rambam goes to length to say the philosophers erred in thinking the soul has different parts. There is one soul with faculties . The Rambam counts five : nutritive, sensory, imaginative, appetitive and rational. The rational and appetitive match Saadia's; the emotions are probably included in another. But on the main point they agree: one soul, many faculties, hence many names . The Kabbalah speaks instead of five souls , or five levels, in ascending order: נפש, רוח, נשמה, חיה, יחידה . Everyone has a nefesh ; even animals have one, though a different kind. Above it a person has ruach and neshamah . The Kabbalists say only Moshe Rabbeinu was in touch with the fifth, the yechidah ; the fourth too is beyond ordinary reach. The extra soul of Shabbat is a true concept, \"but the question is how you understand it.\" In the Kabbalah one receives the additional nefesh , ruach and neshamah at stages: on Friday night, and again on Shabbat morning, the nefesh at Nishmat , the ruach in the Shacharit Amidah, and the neshamah at the Keter Kedushah of Musaf, at the word איה . \"That's why the old Iraqi custom was to elongate Ayeh , because they were accepting the neshamah of Shabbat.\" Not the fourth and fifth, because no one can reach them, even on Shabbat. \"If you want to do this, do it in the sense that you are getting in touch with the extra elements.\" For Saadia, the Rambam and the medieval thinkers, there is one soul whose faculties are more pronounced on Shabbat : our desire grows, which is why there is more food and certain physical pleasures are meant to be enjoyed; our intellect is engaged in Torah. Point 7: The seat of the soul is the heart This ethereal substance with three faculties dwells in the heart . Movement and perception come through the arteries and the chambers of the heart. We know great nerves come from the brain, but for Saadia they are physical channels that need the heart to function. That is why Scripture pairs the heart and the soul: בכל לבבך ובכל נפשך . \"Fascinating chapter, no?\" Summary Chapter 2: understanding the soul is as subtle as creation from nothing and the existence of God, hence the many views. מי יודע in Kohelet does not doubt the difference between souls; it affirms it and admires whoever can discern it (or: \"whoever knows, knows\"). Chapter 3, Saadia's seven points: The soul is created , not eternal (against Plato). Each soul is created individually, together with its body : no storehouse of souls, no splitting of Adam's soul. It is a substance , ethereal like the heavenly spheres but purer , receiving light from a person's intellect and deeds. Proved from reason and from Scripture (Daniel, Iyov). It is intelligent in itself , as shown by the blind man who sees in dreams; the soul drives the senses. It acts only through the body , its instrument. It has three faculties , desire, emotion and intellect, which give the names nefesh, ruach, neshamah ; it is also called chayah (eternal) and yechidah (unique). Five names for one soul , as the Rambam also holds, against the philosophers' parts and the Kabbalists' levels. Its seat is the heart .","7WbJMolA_qg":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 6 (The Soul, Death and What Follows), Chapter 1 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 3 March 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. The Sixth Essay: A Bridge Between the Two Halves of the Book The sixth essay is \"really a bridge between the two sections of the book.\" It has eight chapters: Chapters Subject 1 to 5 The essence and nature of the soul 6 and 7 What happens to the soul as we die and after death 8 Reincarnation : \"probably a bit of a surprise to some of you, what Saadia is going to say about gilgul\" Where the essay sits Essay Subject 1 The universe and its creation (\"the beginning, last year at this time\") 2 God: His essence, His attributes, anthropomorphism 3 Revelation: Torah and prophecy 4 Free will 5 Merits and demerits 6 The soul 7 Techiyat hametim , resurrection 8 Mashiach 9 The world to come 10 Mussar: advice on how to be a good person The first five essays established that there is a creation, that there is a God, that He revealed Himself, that we have free will, and therefore that we must choose wisely. Essays 7 to 9 are about the result of those choices: resurrection, Mashiach and Olam HaBa. \"But who's going to enjoy that? The nefesh . What's the nefesh ?\" The soul is one of the three great philosophical questions, with creation and God, and \"really that should have come before,\" but Saadia places it here as the segue into what happens to the soul afterwards. \"I have to be honest with you. Specifically the first chapter is very difficult, it's very philosophical. I'll do my best to explain as much as I was able to understand.\" As with creation in Essay 1, where he went through thirteen theories, Saadia first lists the theories of what the soul is and tries to disprove them, and only in Chapter 2 gives his own. Saadia's Opening Statement Saadia writes that Hashem has informed us that the soul of a person is created in his heart upon the completion of his body , as the verse says, ויצר רוח אדם בקרבו , \"He forms the spirit of man within him,\" the ruach that Saadia identifies with the soul. When? \"I don't know if that means in the womb or when the child is born.\" The Rabbi thought it probably means when the being is formed in the mother's womb, and grows together with it. Body and soul are joined for the duration of a person's life . When that time ends, they separate . They remain separate until the number of souls God intends to create is complete . Then He reunites them for the world to come . \"We have the prophets who tell us about this. Now we're going to try to understand this rationally and philosophically, like we do every essay.\" Two things to notice straight away Olam HaBa is body and soul together. Saadia will say so explicitly later, but it is implied here. The Rambam, as the class learnt at length in Hilchot Teshuvah, holds that Olam HaBa is the soul alone . Olam HaBa is a world still to come. For Saadia, and for many who followed him, including the Ramchal in Derech Hashem , Olam HaBa is a future world that does not yet exist. When a person dies now, the soul waits in what is commonly called Gan Eden , \"like a kind of waiting room.\" For the Rambam, Gan Eden and Olam HaBa are synonymous: you die, and you are in Olam HaBa. It is called \"the world to come\" not because it does not exist yet, but because it comes after this life. The Rambam has a specific halachah to negate the other view, \"because this was a very common belief,\" and he knew Saadia, whom he quotes. The class will see more in Essays 7 and 9. A question from Mark: new souls Mark asked when the soul enters. The Rabbi would imagine at conception, though Saadia is not bound by the various Midrashim on the soul in the womb. Mark then noted that if Hashem continues creating souls until the full number is reached, every soul is new, which implicitly negates gilgul . \"You're right, a hundred percent.\" But Saadia will negate reincarnation in far stronger terms: Chapter 8 is devoted to refuting it, and \"he completely ridicules\" it. Eleven Theories, Seven Discussed \"The first thing we have to do is try to understand the essence of the soul.\" Many philosophers have debated it. Saadia says he will not mention most of the many views. He brings seven , in addition to four he already dealt with in Essay 1, making eleven in all. The four from Essay 1 Theory of creation Implication for the soul 1 God created the world from pre-existing eternal matter (Plato's view) The soul is made from that matter 2 God created the world from His own essence The soul is from God's essence 3 God created from Himself combined with another essence 4 Two deities created the world between them Since Saadia has already refuted these, and holds that the soul is a new creation God has made, he does not repeat the refutations. Everyone agrees that we have a nefesh : \"whatever life force gives us, allows us to live, as opposed to when we're dead and we just have a dead body.\" The question is what it is. Theory 1: The Soul Is an Accident The first theory is that the soul is not a substance at all but an accident . Term Meaning עצם ( etzem ) A substance , a separate entity created together with the body מקרה ( mikreh ) An accident : a consequence or property of the body, like the ability to stand or sit. You have the body, and as a result it has a soul Those who held this did so because no one has ever seen the soul, only its functions. It is too fine to be sensed, \"just like you can't feel the concept of sitting or standing.\" Within this theory Saadia lists five types . The Rabbi researched who held each (\"I'm no expert in this stuff\"): The soul is... Associated with 1a A self-moving number Xenocrates , a student of Plato and a mathematician who saw everything as mathematics: \"number\" for the capacity to know, \"self-moving\" because through the soul we live and move 1b The completion and perfection of the body Aristotle 1c The balanced combination of the four natures (blood, phlegm, black bile, yellow bile) Hippocrates , the physician; the Rambam often quotes the four natures 1d The coordination of the senses 1e Something resulting from the blood Not that the soul is the blood (that is Anan's view, below) Aristotle's axe The Rabbi read Aristotle's view from a summary on screen. The soul is the structure and functioning of the body. There is a hierarchy: plants with a vegetative soul, animals above them with appetite, humans above animals with reason. The axe: its body is wood and metal; its \"soul\" is its capacity to chop . If it loses that, it is no longer an axe, just wood and metal. The eye: if the eye were an animal, sight would be its soul. When it no longer sees, it is an eye in name only. So too a dead animal is an animal in name only: same body, no soul. For Aristotle body and soul are not two separate things. \"The completion of the creation of the human, when it's all working and it's all together, is what we call its soul... It's not something separate.\" What the five share: the soul is not a separate entity but a result of the body. A participant observed that the middle three are the most alike, each a coordination or completion; the first is a different kind of description, \"and also the fifth.\" Saadia's two refutations Something accidental cannot be the source of such wisdom and glory. The human being has great wisdom, through which he can change the world. The Rabbi was \"almost certain\" Saadia refers back to the opening of Essay 4, where he spent chapters showing that the human is the pinnacle of creation (which the Rambam and Ibn Ezra disputed). Such a being cannot be a mere by-product; the soul must be a separate substance . An accident cannot itself bear accidents. Many things happen to the soul: love, hatred, will, anger; a soul is happy or sad, wise or foolish, pure or bad. These are all accidents of the soul , things that happen to it. If the soul were itself only an accident, these could not be attributed to it. Since they are, the soul must be a substance . \"If you don't fully understand this, it's fine. I also don't fully understand all of it. The main thing is the concepts.\" Doron's question: accident and intention Doron asked whether calling the soul an \"accident\" means Hashem made it unintentionally. The Rabbi: no. On this theory Hashem did intend it, but He made the body as the main thing, and the soul is a resulting by-product. Saadia's objection is that for so glorious a being, the soul must be \"much more central and integral and substantial than just being a mikreh , a side point.\" Doron pressed that Hashem created the whole human with intention, soul included. The Rabbi agreed that everything God does is intentional; the issue is direct versus indirect. A participant added the verse: Hashem formed a body from earth, and blew into it . Before that \"it's not human yet... it was earth.\" The Rabbi: \"Exactly. It's direct, intentional, it's a substance.\" But he reminded the class that Saadia is arguing here from reason, not bringing verses. Theories 2 and 3: The Soul Is Air, or Fire Some philosophers held that the soul is air (the Rabbi found this attributed to Diogenes ); others, apparently Democritus , that it is fire . Saadia: if the soul were air, its nature would be warm and moist ; if fire, warm and dry . We do not find these to be the nature of the soul. The class struggled with what this means. \"These are very old concepts. Even the Rambam talks about moisture.\" Theory 4: Two Souls, Rational and Animal The soul has two parts : the rational, intellectual part, which is eternal and dwells in the heart ; the animal part, which is spread throughout the body and is not eternal . The Rabbi found it attributed to al-Baqillani , an early Muslim theologian around Saadia's time. \"This is starting to sound closer to what we're used to hearing,\" and a participant immediately made the connection: the Kabbalah , with its five parts of the soul , the nefesh as the animal part and higher parts above it. \"Precisely, you're very good, well done.\" Saadia rejects any division of the soul: If the rational and the animal souls were separate, they could not come together. One is eternal and one is not; they are entirely different. If the rational part sits in the heart and the animal part is diffused through the senses, the intellect could not perceive anything the senses feel . And if you answer that the intellect coordinates the animal soul's sensations, two distinct things cannot be coordinated in that way. \"I'm sticking with my problem.\" \"This is really important, because this means Saadia rejects outright the concept of their being parts to a soul.\" For Saadia the soul has many names, which are synonyms : one soul with five names. The Kabbalists saw the different names as different aspects or levels of the soul. The Rabbi reminded Mark of a question he had asked when the class learnt the Ramchal : the Ramchal says the soul comes from Hashem, and just as Hashem is one, the soul is one. How does that fit with his speaking elsewhere of different elements? For Saadia there is no tension: the soul comes from Hashem and, like Him, is one essence . A participant suggested al-Baqillani may have drawn his idea from the heart's two great chambers , one for the intellect and one for the animal life through which the blood flows. \"Maybe, maybe.\" Theory 5: Two Kinds of Air The soul is a substance made of two airs together: the air within the body and the air we breathe in from outside. Its holders reasoned that to live you must breathe, and the nefesh is the life force, so breath must be part of it. Saadia: no. The purpose of respiration is only to cool the natural heat of the heart , as one blows excess smoke away from a fire. A participant pointed out that this is not true. The Rabbi agreed: \"he didn't have the science.\" But the general point stands: for Saadia breathing is a purely physical process of living, and so has nothing to do with the creation of the soul. Theory 6: Anan, the Soul Is the Blood The sixth theory is that of Anan ben David . Flora identified him: the founder of the Karaite schism . (Karaism arose in the eighth and ninth centuries; it is distinct from the earlier Sadducees.) Anan held that the soul is the blood . It is the blood running through us that gives life, speech and thought. A participant remarked that it is \"not completely wrong.\" \"Correct, correct.\" And as a Karaite he took the Torah literally. His proof is from Devarim: כי הדם הוא הנפש , \"for the blood is the soul,\" the reason we may not eat blood. Saadia answers: Anan forgot the earlier verse in Acharei Mot, כי נפש הבשר בדם היא , \"the soul of the flesh is in the blood.\" The blood is the seat of the soul , its dwelling place. When the soul is happy, the blood is plentiful and a person feels good; when he is worried, the blood drains. There is a very close association, but the soul sits in the blood. (The Rabbi corrected the Sefaria page on screen, which had attached the wrong verse to each side.) When the Torah says \"the blood is the soul,\" that is a way of speaking: Mishlei calls wisdom לב , the heart: חסר לב , \"lacking heart,\" means lacking wisdom. Heart and wisdom are not the same thing; the heart is the place of wisdom . שפה אחת at the Tower of Bavel: \"I never realised this, but it's beautiful.\" Safah literally means a lip . Language is called \"lip\" because we speak with our lips. So too, the blood is called the soul because the soul is in the blood , not because they are identical. Summary Essay 6 bridges the book: the first half established creation, God, revelation, free will and merit; the second turns to resurrection, Mashiach and the world to come. First Saadia asks what the soul is . The soul is created with the body , joined to it for life, separated at death, and reunited when the full number of souls is complete. For Saadia Olam HaBa is body and soul, in a world still to come; the Rambam disagrees on both counts. Continual creation of new souls already implies there is no gilgul . Theory 1, the soul as an accident (Xenocrates' self-moving number, Aristotle's perfection of the body, Hippocrates' four natures, the coordination of the senses, a by-product of blood): rejected because so glorious a being cannot rest on a by-product, and because an accident cannot bear accidents such as love, wisdom and sorrow. Theories 2 and 3, air or fire: rejected; the soul does not have their natures. Theory 4, two souls, rational and animal: rejected. The soul is one ; its names are synonyms, against the Kabbalistic parts of the soul. Theory 5, two airs: rejected; breathing is a physical process. Theory 6, Anan the Karaite, the soul is the blood: rejected; the soul dwells in the blood , as the heart is the seat of wisdom and the lip the organ of language. So far, then: the soul is a separate substance created by God . It is not air, not fire, not a combination of airs, and it does sit in the blood. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 2, Saadia's own definition of what the soul is.","Fue3Dhm_qU8":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 5 (Merits and Demerits), the second half of Chapter 7 and Chapter 8, completing the essay Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 24 February 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: The Fifth Essay So Far Tonight the class finishes the fifth essay, on mitzvot and aveirot, teshuvah and the status of a person. Next week, at 6 o'clock, it begins Essay 6, on the soul : \"a difficult essay, very abstract... some concepts are quite obscure.\" There are ten essays, \"so we are well over halfway, because the first two are the big ones.\" What the essay has covered: Mitzvot and aveirot leave an impression on the soul. Ten categories of people: # Category 1 Tzaddik Majority mitzvot 2 Rasha Majority aveirot 3 Oved Committed to one mitzvah 4 Mamre Disobedient to one specific mitzvah 5 Shalem Complete; keeps all the mitzvot 6 Mekatzer Negligent with positive commandments 7 Choteh Offends against negative commandments 8 Mezid Rebellious with negative commandments that carry the death penalty 9 Kofer Serves avodah zarah , believes in nothing, or has significant doubts 10 Shav The penitent Theodicy: why the righteous suffer and why the wicked prosper. Teshuvah: Saadia was the first to list its ingredients: stopping the sin now, regret, acceptance for the future and confession. Writing poetry about not sinning helps; so do prayer and charity. Chapter 6 , a number of side points: seven things that prevent prayer from being accepted ; things that block teshuvah , such as still having the stolen item in your hand, or causing the public to sin; four sins for which a consequence must come in this world even after full teshuvah , \"Saadia's chiddush ... something the later authorities didn't really take on\"; and five levels of teshuvah (the Rambam lists three): repenting in exactly the same circumstances in which you sinned, and holding back; repenting when the circumstances are not the same; repenting when threatened; repenting once punishment has begun; repenting before death. That is why, Saadia writes, it is customary before death to say, let my death be an atonement for all my sins . The first half of Chapter 7: the beinoni , the eleventh kind of person. Two readings of Saadia: literally fifty-fifty, which would be very rare, or more generally someone whose identity is fifty-fifty. Either way, Hashem מטה כלפי חסד , tilts toward kindness, and counts him among the tzaddikim. Chapter 7, Part 2: The Same Deed Weighs Differently Saadia now lists contrasts: the same mitzvah is greater when one kind of person does it, and the same sin is worse when another kind of person does it. \"It's the same sin two people do, but when one kind of person does something, it's worse.\" Saadia does not say why he lists them here, but the Rabbi suggested it follows from something the Rambam also writes: mitzvot and aveirot are not only about the action but about its context , and no one can assess the value of each mitzvah and aveirah except HaKadosh Baruch Hu , as Yeshayahu says, \"My thoughts are not your thoughts.\" There are countless variables; these are some of the main ones to take into account in \"the great weighing.\" 1. The righteous person Service from a righteous person is considered greater , and a sin from a righteous person is worse : \"even the prophet and even the priest\" are condemned for their hypocrisy. Probably because an important, righteous person sets an example . The corollary: when a well-known, righteous person sins, it is \"a terrible way to cause other people to sin.\" 2. The holy place Serving Hashem in a holy place is greater: \"there, in My house, they will serve Me.\" A sin there is worse: \"even in My house I found their wickedness.\" This is why the Torah says in various places that a sin committed in Eretz Yisrael is worse than one committed outside: \"the land will spit you out.\" \"It's a great responsibility to live in Israel.\" And within Israel, Yerushalayim; within Yerushalayim, the Beit HaMikdash. The greater the sanctity, the greater the service and the greater the responsibility. 3. Youth and old age Dedicating oneself to Hashem when young is greater than when old: ומבחוריכם לנזרים , \"I raised up your young men as Nazirites.\" A sin of promiscuity by an elderly person is worse: in Hoshea's rebuke of Ephraim, even the old have joined in. \"It's one thing, young people, full of desire, full of passion, are sinning. It's another thing when you're an old person committing these sins.\" 4. Wealth and honesty Integrity from a poor person is more valued: טוב רש הולך בתמו , \"better a poor man who walks in his integrity.\" Dishonesty from a wealthy person is worse. Saadia is obviously referring to Natan's parable to David: the man with many sheep who took the poor man's one lamb. David had many wives, and Uriyah had one; David took Batsheva and caused her to be widowed so that he could marry her. \"So the wealthier you are, whether financially wealthy or wealthy in the sense that you've got plenty of something, the more you have to be careful about your honesty.\" And a poor person who is scrupulous in business is on a higher level than an ordinary honest person. 5. Enemies and friends Helping someone you dislike is greater; harming a friend is worse: \"he stretched out his hands against those at peace with him.\" There is an explicit Gemara on this, though \"Saadia prefers to quote things from the pesukim rather than from the Chachamim,\" a reflection of his time, a couple of centuries after the close of the Talmud, before it carried the binding authority it had for the later Rishonim. In the previous week's parashah , the Torah speaks of returning the straying animal of your enemy . The Chachamim say: if two people need help and you can give precedence only to one, \"you're driving your car and you see two people stranded,\" one a good friend and one someone you do not like at all, help the one you like less . It takes greater effort, and \"that's building the relationship.\" This is not about an enemy coming to kill you. There the Gemara warns against showing mercy: \"you have to use your common sense with these things.\" 6. Humility and arrogance Humility from a great person is greater: והאיש משה ענו מאד . Arrogance from a lowly person is worse. The Rabbi noted that two lines were missing from the text on screen and supplied Saadia's proof: כרם זלת לבני אדם (Tehillim 12:9), the last verse of the psalm said on Shemini Atzeret. The Radak reads it: when the wicked are elevated, people come to cheapen the mitzvot, because they see the wicked raised up. Saadia plays on the words: when the lowly ( zulut , the cheap and base) raise themselves up ( rum ), it is terrible. \"It's one thing to be arrogant when you've got a right to be arrogant. But it's worse to be arrogant when you have no right.\" 7. Victims who are poor or who benefit others Stealing from or cheating a poor person is worse than cheating an ordinary person: גזלת העני בבתיכם , \"the spoil of the poor is in your houses.\" So too, harming someone who benefits the public , a righteous person with influence on others, is worse than harming an ordinary person. 8. Many acts versus one Many acts of deceit are worse than one great deception. \"A person who steals once a thousand pounds is not as bad as a person who steals twice five hundred pounds,\" though the total is the same. The Rabbi asked where we learnt a similar idea, \"perhaps the opposite.\" The Rambam on Pirkei Avot : better to give one coin to a thousand people than a thousand coins to one, because every act of giving instils itself in our character . So too every act of theft and deceit instils itself in our character. 9. Holy days Sinning on a special day, such as a fast day , is worse: הן ביום צמכם תמצאו חפץ , \"on the day of your fast you pursue your business.\" \"This is probably the closest you have\" to explaining a puzzling practice. Many people are more careful about Yom Kippur than about Shabbat, \"which really, if you think about it, doesn't make any sense at all.\" Driving on Shabbat carries sekilah , a death penalty; on Yom Kippur it carries karet , severe but less so. \"And yet you have this practice of people that they will drive to synagogue every Shabbat, but on Yom Kippur they'll take the long walk.\" The only explanation is this idea: on the designated day of forgiveness and atonement, sinning is like throwing it in His face. The Pele Yoetz. The Rabbi recalled seeing, many years ago, that the Pele Yoetz writes that a sin on Pesach is worse than at any other time of year. Chametz is puffed up, the essence of sin, which makes something look much bigger than it is; afterwards you see it was not worth it. Matzah is the mitzvah. This ties back to the Gemara in Sukkah : at the end of days the righteous and the wicked will see the yetzer hara . The righteous will weep: how did we conquer such a mountain? The wicked will weep: how did we fail to conquer a hair? Which is it? \"Depends from which perspective.\" Before you sin, it looks like a mountain. Once you have done it, \"it's nothing, it's not worth it, it's a hair's breadth.\" 10. Charity from the poor Charity from a poor person is greater: טוב מעט ביראת ה' , \"better a little with the fear of Hashem.\" Of course a wealthy person's charity is never diminished; \"this is one variable.\" 11. Abstinence from the indulged The more indulged a person is, the greater his abstinence. A fast is great, but a fast from an indulgent person is greater. In Yoel (the haftarah): תקעו שופר בציון, קדשו צום... יצא חתן מחדרו וכלה מחפתה , \"blow the shofar, sanctify a fast... let the groom come out of his chamber and the bride from her canopy.\" Even a bride and groom at the peak of their joy should fast, and their fast is worth more than an ordinary person's. In the same way, we are commanded to give the first fruits , to redeem the firstborn son , to bring the firstborn animals to the altar, and to pray early in the morning , because these are the most valuable to us. \"You always give your best,\" as the Rambam also says. \"My most energy, I can go for a jog, do exercise... use that time for HaKadosh Baruch Hu. That is great.\" Summary of the contrasts # Greater merit Greater sin 1 Service by the righteous Sin by the righteous 2 Service in a holy place Sin in a holy place 3 Dedication in youth Sin in old age 4 Integrity of the poor Dishonesty of the rich 5 Helping an enemy Harming a friend 6 Humility of the great Arrogance of the lowly 7 Robbing the poor; harming a public benefactor 8 Many acts of deceit rather than one 9 Sinning on a holy day 10 Charity from the poor 11 Abstinence by the indulged \"These are all defaults, and of course there'll be many other points to bear in mind,\" all going into \"the great assessment HaKadosh Baruch Hu will do at the end of days.\" Questions from the class Donald asked about contrast 4: Saadia's verse ends מעקש דרכים והוא עשיר , \"than one who is perverse in his ways, though he be rich.\" Shouldn't a rich person's integrity count for more, since he has more opportunities to deceive? The Rabbi thought Saadia quotes the verse only for its first half. Another participant argued that integrity and dishonesty go hand in hand: the bigger the business, the greater the scope for deceit, so a rich man's fraud is worse and his honesty correspondingly significant. They agreed it is \"basically the same thing\" split in two. What counts as rich? In this context, financially wealthy: relative to others, someone with more than he needs to live, even to fund a comfortable lifestyle. It is a spectrum, and the more one has, the greater the sin of deception. (Generally, the Rabbi added, a \"wealthy\" person is one who is content. \"I see a lot of people with money and they feel they're poor and they want more money. Unfortunately that's an illness.\") Chapter 8: Closing Reflections \"Chapter 8 is a bit of, I don't want to call it a random chapter. It's just different points all put together.\" About ten or eleven final reflections. 1. Thoughts Good thoughts are rewarded , even if the good is never carried out: יעזב רשע דרכו ואיש און מחשבתיו . The verse stresses thoughts ; a person's hopes and intentions to do good are valuable. Bad thoughts that are not acted on are not counted as deeds , though they are תועבת ה' מחשבות רע , \"an abomination to Hashem.\" Except thoughts of heresy , \"because heresy is intrinsically something you do in your mind\": למען תפש את בית ישראל בלבם , \"to catch the house of Israel in their hearts.\" Saadia does not quote it, but there is an explicit Gemara ( Kiddushin 39b-40a ). A father tells his son to climb up and send away the mother bird, two mitzvot whose reward in the Torah is long life, and the son falls and dies on the way. Perhaps he had a bad thought on the way up? No: מחשבה רעה אין הקב\"ה מצרפה למעשה , Hashem does not join a bad thought to an action, except for avodah zarah . (A participant supplied the other half: מחשבה טובה הקב\"ה מצרפה למעשה , a good thought Hashem does join to the deed.) The Rabbi drew out two things: The Gemara speaks of avodah zarah . Saadia extends it to all heresy , since it is all done in the mind. Saadia says there is some guilt for bad thoughts. Someone who hates a person and fantasises about killing him, even schemes how to do it, but never has the nerve, is not punished as a murderer. Yet there is guilt in the thought itself. \"It's a chiddush .\" The general understanding of the Gemara is that, though it is not good to cultivate such thoughts, there is no culpability. And on Saadia's view the Gemara's answer is puzzling: if a bad thought carries some guilt, why could it not explain the son's death? Saadia must hold that the guilt is \"nowhere near as bad as an action,\" too little to cause such a punishment. 2. Allegorising the Torah Allegorising a verse is legitimate, and rewarded, for four reasons only: to reconcile it with what our senses perceive; to reconcile it with reason ; because otherwise it would contradict another verse ; because otherwise it would contradict our tradition . אם תבקשנה ככסף וכמטמונים תחפשנה , \"if you seek it like silver and search for it like hidden treasure,\" you will find the right interpretation. Allegorising for any other reason is a sin: the Mishnah's המגלה פנים בתורה שלא כהלכה . Saadia will treat this fully in Essay 7 . The Rabbi recalled a shiur a few years ago comparing Saadia and the Rambam: the Rambam allows allegory more broadly. \"There are people who call themselves Orthodox rabbis who have allegorised the Avot, Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov... that's wrong.\" Doron asked whether the Rambam allegorises Gan Eden and the snake. The Rabbi did not remember either way and would check, but noted that a talking snake conflicts with our senses, so even Saadia might accept it. The Rambam certainly reads Bilam's donkey non-literally: \"he understands there's no such thing as the donkey of Bilam speaking.\" Where is the line? \"It's very hard,\" since one can always claim something conflicts with reason; the Rabbi's understanding is that for the Rambam one may not allegorise against a fundamental of faith, so one could not allegorise away the splitting of the Yam Suf. 3. Allegorising the mitzvot Someone who allegorises mitzvot and leads people to doubt whether to perform them behaves like the false prophets , \"who follow their own spirit.\" \"You can allegorise verses in the Torah. You can't allegorise mitzvot.\" \"This is interesting, and I'll tell you why.\" This was very widespread, \"believe it or not,\" in Andalusia and beyond in the eleventh to thirteenth centuries. The Rambam had minor controversies in his lifetime, but the two major ones came after his death in 1204: in the 1230s , involving Rabbeinu Yonah , when the Rambam's books were burnt (Rabbeinu Yonah later admitted it was wrong); in the 1290s , with the Rashba and Abba Mari . In both, opponents claimed that because of the Rambam people were allegorising mitzvot. The letters show that some scholars said tefillin were not literal boxes to wear but contents to contemplate, \"wear the boxes in your mind,\" and the same for many other mitzvot. 4. New ideas about God and creation Those who invent new ideas about God are doing something terrible. Some explained נעשה אדם בצלמנו , \"let us make man in our image,\" as Hashem creating man together with an angel, to make it greater; but נשאו לשוא עריך , they exalt You in vain, because it detracts from the unity of God. \"Be careful when you're explaining things about HaKadosh Baruch Hu and about creation, that you are honest and stick to the tradition.\" 5. The judge who punishes A judge who imposes punishment to protect the Torah , as the Chachamim allow in order to make a fence, is rewarded. A judge who does it for his own benefit , for a sense of superiority, does terrible harm to his own soul. Why does Saadia include this? Some say he is referring to David ben Zakkai , the Exilarch, with whom he clashed many times, as the class learnt in its very first shiur on Saadia over a year ago. Saadia lost his position over a financial case that the Exilarch insisted be decided one way, because Saadia stood up for the truth. 6 to 10. No excuse Saadia lists situations that are no excuse : 6. The ignorant Those who go and ask the Chacham are praised. But if you never bothered to ask, \"you can't say, I didn't know,\" because you had the chance to learn. \"If you tried to learn and made mistakes, that's fine.\" 7. The poor and prayer If you could not pray because you were working every moment to put bread on the table, you have an excuse. \"But if you had a spare five minutes and you didn't use those five minutes for prayer,\" that will be held against you. Poverty excuses only what poverty requires 8. The sick Terrible illness or tragedy is no excuse for cursing Hashem and saying He is unfair: ולא זעקו אלי בלבם כי ייליליו על משכבותם , \"they did not cry out to Me with their heart, but wail upon their beds\" 9. The drunk Drunkenness is no excuse; you bear full liability, \"something reflected nowadays in civil law as well.\" Saadia quotes the Gemara in Eruvin , though not the full story: at the drunkenness of Lot , a far extreme, transactions are void and some liabilities do not apply 10. The oppressed Jews oppressed by non-Jews have no excuse to abandon Torah and mitzvot. \"As Jews, most of us have done this most of the generations,\" though there have always been some who went over to the other side 11. Teshuvah once more Repeating a sin makes it harder: ככלב שב על קאו , \"like a dog returning to its vomit.\" Saying \"I will sin and then do teshuvah\" does not work. This is not a contradiction of Chapter 5, where relapse after a genuine teshuvah did not undo it; here he means someone who repents while intending to sin again. Yom Kippur alone does not work. \"You may have thought Yom Kippur works for everyone. No. Yom Kippur only works together with teshuvah.\" Causing others to sin makes teshuvah very hard. But one who causes others to do good will not easily be punished: because he guarded My ways, I will guard him. \"I could bring many examples, I'd never end. These are the main principles.\" So Saadia concludes the fifth essay. Summary Chapter 7: the same deed weighs differently depending on who does it, where , when , to whom and how often : eleven contrasts, all part of a weighing only Hashem can make. Thoughts: good intentions are rewarded; bad ones are not counted as deeds, but for Saadia carry some guilt; heresy, in the mind, is judged as a deed. Allegory: permitted only to reconcile Scripture with the senses, reason, other verses or tradition. Never allegorise mitzvot , a live issue in the Rambam controversies. Stick to tradition in speaking of God and creation; a judge must punish for the Torah's sake, not his own (perhaps aimed at David ben Zakkai). No excuse for ignorance one could have remedied, prayer one had time for, cursing in illness, drunkenness, or abandoning Judaism under oppression. Teshuvah: repeated sin is harder to undo; Yom Kippur needs teshuvah; causing others to sin, or to do good, has lasting weight. Coming Up Next week, be'ezrat Hashem: Essay 6, the nefesh , the soul.","mNqw90M8ByE":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 5 (Merits and Demerits), the end of Chapter 4 (categories 6 to 9) and Chapter 5 (the penitent) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 3 February 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Merits, Demerits and Ten Categories The fifth essay is on zechuyot vechovot , merits and demerits, mitzvot and aveirot. The first four chapters established: A person's deeds divide into merits and demerits, and every action leaves an impression on one's character, positive or negative. HaKadosh Baruch Hu records everything, and everything has its consequence, in this world, the next, or both. Saadia's theodicy: why the righteous sometimes suffer, and (last week) six reasons why the wicked seem to prosper and live long. Ten categories of people. \"Saadia always likes numbers.\" Five have been seen: # Category Definition 1 Tzaddik More merits than demerits; judged by the majority 2 Rasha More demerits than merits 3 Oved Singles out one mitzvah to which he is committed come what may. \"We should all really try to do that in our lives.\" The Rambam and Rabbi Yosef Albo in the Ikkarim agree that one such mitzvah earns a portion in the world to come 4 Mamre Singles out one mitzvah he refuses, ideologically, ever to perform: a mumar ledavar echad 5 Shalem Keeps all the mitzvot. A level above the tzaddik. Saadia holds it is possible, and reinterprets the verses that seem to say otherwise Tonight: categories six to nine, which close Chapter 4, and the tenth, the penitent, to which Saadia devotes Chapter 5. Chapter 4: Categories 6 to 9, in Ascending Severity \"Pay attention to six, seven, eight and nine. They are in ascending order in terms of severity.\" 6. The Mekatzer: the negligent A person who keeps everything, but is lax with positive commandments . Not ideologically or wilfully; he is just not particularly interested in performing them. They are the kinds of mitzvot from which women are exempt, and which a man may have no interest in doing. 7. The Choteh: the offender A person who violates negative commandments, but not the severe ones . How do we know what is severe? By the punishment. These are prohibitions without death or karet : eating non-kosher food, wearing shaatnez , superstitious practices, deceit and the like. More severe than neglecting positive commandments, but without the gravest consequences. 8. The Mezid: the rebellious offender A person who intentionally violates the severe negative commandments , those carrying the death penalty or karet : the forbidden relationships, desecrating Shabbat, eating on Yom Kippur, eating chametz on Pesach. Note that this is a different kind of label from tzaddik and rasha. A person may have more merits than demerits and still carry the label mezid , because he has violated one of the Torah's severe prohibitions. 9. The Kofer: the denier A person who leaves the root , who denies God. There are three ways: The denier who... 9a serves something else : idols, the sun, the moon, a human being \"Even if you believe in God, but you've served other forms, you are now a kofer\" 9b serves nothing at all He is simply not interested. \"This is probably one of the most common ones nowadays\" 9c has significant doubts about his faith People call him a believer, part of the community; he may even pray. \"But deep down he's not a believer... He puts on a show.\" As the verse says, ויפתוהו בפיהם , they beguiled Him with their mouths, but the heart was not there Anyone in this ninth category, Saadia says, is called one who desecrates the name of Heaven . Teshuvah fully atones for all nine categories, except for four sins which, even with complete teshuvah, still require some consequence in this world. Saadia says he will explain them in Chapter 6. Saadia and the Gemara in Yoma: the source of the Rambam's four levels \"This is very interesting, because the Rambam almost mimics this later on, in Hilchot Teshuvah chapter 1,\" learnt on Tuesday nights some time ago. The Gemara in Yoma already distinguishes severe prohibitions, those carrying death or karet , from lesser ones. Saadia further divides the lesser offences into neglect of positive commandments and violation of negative ones. \"The Gemara doesn't do that. Saadia is doing it of his own accord, but the principle is there.\" And when he adds, of the kofer , that he is מחלל שם שמים , \"I'm certain he's referring here to the Gemara in Yoma, which the Rambam quotes.\" Saadia The Rambam, Hilchot Teshuvah ch. 1 What atones 6. Mekatzer (positive commandments) Neglect of a positive commandment Teshuvah alone , immediately. \"I haven't been putting on tefillin... I decide I'm going to start. That's it.\" No need for Yom Kippur 7. Choteh (lesser negative commandments) Negative commandments without death or karet Teshuvah and Yom Kippur : \"what Yom Kippur is most effective for\" 8. Mezid (death or karet ) Prohibitions carrying karet or death Teshuvah, Yom Kippur and suffering in this world 9. Kofer Chillul Hashem Teshuvah, Yom Kippur, suffering, and death : full atonement comes only with death The Rambam does not define chillul Hashem in Hilchot Teshuvah, and defines it differently elsewhere. Saadia defines it as the denier , in any of his three forms. That is why he \"threw in this sentence\" about desecrating the name of Heaven. Chapter 5: The Tenth Category, the Penitent העשירי הוא השב , the tenth is the penitent, who performs the conditions of teshuvah. \"As I've said many times as we've been learning this book, the reason I love this book, and why it's such an important one, is because so many things which we are accustomed to believing, this is just how things have always been, this is what Judaism has always said, actually originate in this book.\" Every year we hear about the process of teshuvah and its parts. But nowhere in the Talmud is there a set list of the ingredients of teshuvah. The Gemara speaks of teshuvah generally and mentions elements here and there. Saadia is the very first to put the ingredients together , \"and literally everyone copied him after this\": Rabbeinu Bachya in Chovot HaLevavot , the Rambam , Rabbeinu Yonah in Shaarei Teshuvah , and the Meiri in his Chibbur HaTeshuvah . \"Each of these individual ingredients is scattered across the Tanach... but putting it together and explaining that this is the process of teshuvah, Saadia was the very first one to do this.\" The four ingredients # Ingredient Time 1 עזיבת החטא : leaving the sin, stopping what you are doing The present 2 חרטה : regret and remorse The past 3 בקשת כפרה : a request for atonement, what we call vidui , confession 4 קבלה : firm resolve not to repeat the sin The future All four are needed. All four in one passage: Hoshea 14 As usual, Saadia prefers to go straight to Scripture rather than quote the Chachamim. He finds all four in the opening verses of the Haftarah of Shabbat Shuvah , \"and for Saadia this is the perfect haftarah\": שובה ישראל עד ה' אלהיך כי כשלת בעונך. קחו עמכם דברים ושובו אל ה', אמרו אליו כל תשא עון וקח טוב ונשלמה פרים שפתינו. אשור לא יושיענו, על סוס לא נרכב, ולא נאמר עוד אלהינו למעשה ידינו. שובה ישראל , \"return, Israel\": stop now what you are doing. Leaving the sin in the present. כי כשלת בעונך , \"for you have stumbled in your iniquity\": you recognise it as a stumbling , a failure, an obstacle in the path of your character. That is regret. קחו עמכם דברים , \"take words with you\": confession, the request for atonement. ולא נאמר עוד אלהינו למעשה ידינו , \"we will no longer call the work of our hands our god\": the resolve not to lapse back into sin. Two digressions on difficult words Because he is explaining the verses, Saadia \"takes the liberty\" of explaining two puzzling phrases. כל תשא עון וקח טוב. All the Rishonim struggle with this. The Ibn Ezra gives perhaps the easiest reading by swapping the words: \"forgive all sin, and accept our good.\" Saadia will not swap them. Instead he explains כל not as \"all\" but as \"corresponding to,\" \"in accordance with\" : in return for Your bearing our iniquity, accept from us the prayer declaring that You are good. He shows the usage from: Daniel 2:8 , in Aramaic: כל קבל די חזיתון , \"because you see that the matter has escaped me,\" you are playing for time. Everyone agrees kol kevel there does not mean \"all.\" Kohelet 5:15 , in Hebrew: כל עמת שבא כן ילך , \"just as he came, so shall he go.\" כל הבאיש על עם לא יועילו למו is the same usage. \"If you didn't get the exact word here, it's fine, don't worry about it. But I just found it really interesting. For me it was actually something new.\" ונשלמה פרים שפתינו , literally \"and we will pay bulls, our lips.\" Either add a letter, כפרים , \" like bulls,\" or supply a few missing words: may what we offer with our lips, the confession and the request for atonement, be accepted as if we had offered sacrifices . Same meaning either way. Why Ashur, the horse and idols? Why does the prophet mention these three in particular? Because in Hoshea's time these were the sins the people were embroiled in , as the start of the book says, כי המה עלו אשור פרא בודד לו , they went up to Ashur, a wild ass alone. \"If the most prevalent sins were murder and adultery, he would have said that.\" Different Verses, the Same Four Ingredients After Saadia, all the Rishonim followed his four ingredients. So did Rabbeinu Nissim (not the Ran, the commentator on the Talmud) in the long vidui recited on Yom Kippur in almost every community, some in Shacharit, some at Minchah, some at night, \"a very long and really emotional confession,\" built on the same four elements. They differ only on which verse proves which point: Ingredient Saadia Others Leaving the sin שובה ישראל The Rambam and Rabbeinu Bachya: יעזב רשע דרכו , \"let the wicked forsake his way,\" which is more explicit Regret כי כשלת בעונך Rabbeinu Bachya: מי יודע ישוב ונחם (Yoel). The Rambam: כי אחרי שובי נחמתי (Yirmiyahu) Confession קחו עמכם דברים The Rambam: ומודה ועוזב ירחם , \"he who confesses and forsakes will find mercy,\" a verse said in Selichot Resolve ולא נאמר עוד אלהינו למעשה ידינו All the Rishonim use Saadia's verse The Rambam's \"little spin\" The Rambam, Hilchot Teshuvah 2:2, defines teshuvah: the sinner leaves his sin, removes it from his thoughts and resolves never to do it again, as it says, יעזב רשע דרכו ; he regrets the past, as it says, כי אחרי שובי נחמתי ; ויעיד עליו יודע תעלומות , and the One who knows all secrets will testify that he will never return to this sin, as it says, ולא נאמר עוד אלהינו למעשה ידינו . \"I just realised now\": the Rambam uses the verse in two ways . \"Our god\" is the idol, the work of our hands; but Eloheinu is also the true God, who will testify that we will no longer make the work of our hands our gods. The Rambam's major change: three, then confession \"Secondly, the major, major point.\" For Saadia, teshuvah is one big thing : all four ingredients lumped together. The Rambam uses the same four, but differently. Teshuvah itself is three : azivah , charatah and kabbalah , the present, the past and the future. Only after these does the fourth, vidui , come in. \"You can't really do vidui before you do the other three parts.\" (The class went into this in great detail when it learnt Hilchot Teshuvah.) The Missing Ingredient, and Why We Have Selichot Saadia then makes an observation about his own community: The majority of our nation do teshuvah with every ingredient bar one. They confess a great deal, they regret a great deal, they stop. But I sense that they find the fourth difficult. When they come on Yom Kippur and pray and fast, they stop, they regret and they ask for atonement, but they are not actually committed to not doing it again. How can people be inspired to firm resolve? Saadia's answer is \"fascinating\": compose poems about the futility of this world , about how a person should separate himself from its vanities; poems about human frailty, how we will die and decompose in the grave, and all the suffering we will go through, until a person becomes repulsed by this world and by sin, and will not want to go back to it. That, Saadia says, is why the earlier rabbis wrote all kinds of poems for these days. \"This is where the concept of poems and Selichot comes from.\" The Rabbi drew out the history: There is no concept of Selichot in the Talmud . They were composed after the Talmud, which is why reciting them is a minhag . There is no halachah to recite Selichot at any time, even on Erev Yom Kippur. (\"I know we Sephardim do Selichot from forty days before, from Elul, but this is all later customs.\") The first Selichah whose author we know for certain is by Rabbi Shlomo HaBavli , in the tenth century. There are very likely earlier ones whose authors and dates we do not know. And already in Saadia we see the idea of poems for Yom Kippur. \"This is why I say every year: the piyutim and the Selichot nowadays, unless you are actually following with the English translation of what these things mean, they're really pointless. Because there's no intrinsic value in saying the piyutim and the Selichot, aside from what Saadia is telling us, which is to arouse us to teshuvah and to resolve not to re-offend.\" Singing them \"with your loudest voice\" without knowing what they mean misses their purpose, and in truth they are poems that are sometimes hard to understand, \"and very often only the poet himself understood what he was writing.\" The main part of Selichot is the Thirteen Attributes and the confessions; the piyutim are there to arouse us. Three Further Aids Saadia adds three things that help teshuvah. \"They're not the main ingredients, but they are like the sauce. They're like the gravy, if you like.\" An increase in prayer. An increase in justice ( tzedek ). Teaching others to become better: אלמדה פשעים דרכיך וחטאים אליך ישובו , \"I will teach transgressors Your ways, and sinners will return to You.\" On the second, some texts read tzedakah , charity, which would fit the Rambam and other sources. But Saadia's proof is בחסד ואמת יכפר עון , \"through kindness and truth iniquity is atoned.\" He reads chesed as prayer, asking for God's mercy; and if the other word meant charity, \"I'm not sure why charity is called truth.\" So the Rabbi thinks it is tzedek : a commitment to justice and straightness, though it could include charity. The Rambam lists prayer and charity, not teaching others, and adds changing one's name and exile . As the class explained when learning Hilchot Teshuvah, these mean creating a new identity : \"change your name, change who you are, change your perceptions, change your values. Exile from your place, start again fresh.\" Relapse Does Not Undo Teshuvah The last point of the chapter. \"These are things we hear year in, year out, but Saadia is the first one, to my knowledge, to formulate this\": If a person does sincere teshuvah and it is accepted, and afterwards he relapses, his previous teshuvah is not affected . All his earlier sins remain forgiven. Only the new sins count, from a clean slate. Even if this happens many times. This is not the person the Mishnah describes, who says \"I will sin and repent, I will sin and repent\"; that does not work. It is the person who genuinely regrets, resolves and confesses, and a month later falls back. \"This happens. We're human beings, we're frail.\" The dry cleaner People ask on Yom Kippur: is it worth it, if I end up the same? First, that should not be the case every year; if it is, something is wrong. But leaving that aside, the Rabbi's analogy: you take your suit or dress to the dry cleaner even though you know it will get dirty again at the next wedding. A little dirt is much easier to clean than dirt that has built up. Accumulated sin becomes \"much more lodged into your character,\" and needs a far greater effort to change. \"For three transgressions... but for four\" על שלשה פשעי... ועל ארבעה לא אשיבנו (Amos). Do not misread this to mean that after repenting three times, a fourth lapse cannot be repented. Saadia: it refers only to punishment . If a person sins and repents after the first, second and third summons, Hashem does not bring what was decreed. On the fourth, teshuvah still works and wipes the slate, but if a decree has already been set, the punishment must still come . \"Quite a novel point.\" The Rambam, in Hilchot Teshuvah chapter 3, interprets the verse differently, \"more Talmudically, to be honest,\" as the class discussed at the time. Summary Categories 6 to 9 , in rising severity: the mekatzer (lax in positive commandments), the choteh (lesser negative commandments), the mezid (death or karet ), and the kofer , who serves another, serves nothing, or harbours serious doubt behind a show of belief. These map onto the Gemara in Yoma and the Rambam's four levels of atonement ; Saadia identifies chillul Hashem with the kofer . Saadia is the first to define the ingredients of teshuvah : leaving the sin, regret, confession and resolve, all found in Hoshea 14 . Every later Rishon follows him; all quote his verse for resolve. Along the way: כל as \"corresponding to,\" and פרים שפתינו as \" like bulls.\" The Rambam puts the three inner steps first and confession after; Saadia treats teshuvah as one whole. Resolve is the weak link , and Selichot and piyutim exist to strengthen it, which is why they must be understood. Three aids: prayer, justice (or charity) and teaching others. The Rambam adds a new name and exile: a new identity. Relapse does not undo earlier teshuvah ; the \"fourth transgression\" concerns a decree already set. Coming Up Chapter 6: more on prayer and teshuvah, and the four sins that teshuvah alone does not fully atone. Chapter 7: an eleventh category, the beinoni , whose merits and demerits are equal. Chapter 8: some thoughts \"about thoughts.\" Next week, be'ezrat Hashem, Chapters 6 and 7, perhaps 8 as well.","W1ZsBaoZXag":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 5 (Merits and Demerits), the second half of Chapter 3 and Chapter 4 (categories 3 to 5) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 27 January 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Two and a Half Chapters of Theodicy Saadia likes to look at the majority of a person's actions . With a majority of mitzvot, a person is termed one of zechuyot , merits; with a majority of sins, of chovot , demerits. Hashem remembers everything, and every action leaves its impression on the soul. In Chapter 2 Saadia introduced the ten categories of people, of which the class has seen two, the tzaddik and the rasha , and began his theodicy. The tzaddik's portion in the world to come is commensurate with his deeds, and the consequences of his minority of sins come in this world; the rasha is rewarded here for his minority of good deeds and receives the main consequences in the next. The class also discussed regret for sins and for mitzvot. Chapter 3 so far gave the reasons the righteous suffer : payment for the minority of sins; a test for those who can bear it; and, as with young children, to increase reward , which the Rambam does not accept. Tonight: why the wicked prosper , and then Chapter 4, the next categories of people. Chapter 3: Six Reasons the Wicked Prosper (The text on screen said seven; \"that's a mistake, it's for six reasons.\") 1. Payment for the minority As explained in Chapter 2, the rasha receives in this world the reward for his minority of good deeds, so that his retribution in the next world is complete. 2. Hashem knows he will repent Hashem lengthens his time in this world so that he can do teshuvah. The example is Menashe . Saadia does not quote it, but this is exactly the Gemara in Sanhedrin: whoever says Menashe has no portion in the world to come weakens the hands of penitents. Menashe began to reign at twelve and reigned fifty-five years, and he did evil \"like Achav.\" Achav reigned twenty-two years, so Menashe reigned wickedly for twenty-two years, with the idols and everything we know about him, and repented for the remaining thirty-three, \"even though the teshuvah wasn't complete.\" 3. A righteous descendant will come from him You yourself may not deserve to live, but your descendants are worthy. Achaz was a wicked king who passed his son through the fire to Molech, yet Hashem lengthened his days so that Chizkiyahu would come from him. Amon reigned two years and worshipped idols publicly, but his son was the great king Yoshiyahu . \"You never know why a wicked person still has everything going well for him. Perhaps it's for someone. It could be a son, a grandson, it could be even a few generations down. Who knows?\" 4. He is Hashem's rod A wicked person or nation may be kept alive and prosperous to act as the rod of Hashem's retribution . Of Ashur, בגוי חנף אשלחנו ועל עם עברתי אצונו , \"I send him against an ungodly nation, and against the people of My wrath I command him\": Ashur, the rod of My anger, punishes Israel. \"Which is difficult.\" 5. A righteous person asked for him Sometimes no good will come from you, you are no emissary, and you will not repent, but a righteous person benefits from you and asks for you to be spared. When Lot fled Sedom he asked that the city of Tzoar , where he was staying, not be destroyed, and Hashem agreed, though the city did not deserve it in its own right. (Remember that \"righteous,\" in Saadia's definition, means fifty-one over forty-nine, \"if we're looking at it in purely mathematical terms. Obviously it's much more than that.\") 6. To intensify his punishment Hashem may keep a wicked person alive in order to intensify his final punishment. Pharaoh was preserved through the ten plagues for the dramatic finale: וינער ה' את מצרים בתוך הים , \"Hashem shook off Egypt into the sea.\" Yirmiyahu's question מדוע דרך רשעים צלחה , \"why does the way of the wicked prosper?\" Some read it as Yirmiyahu's general question. Saadia: no, he knew the reasons; he wanted to know which of the six was the main one . \"Fascinatingly,\" he was told it was the last: to give them a more intensified punishment. The Rabbi could explain this only two ways: either like Pharaoh, a more severe end, or by giving them goodness in this world so that their punishment in the next world is complete and they do not enjoy the reward of their good deeds there. One Sin and the Fly in the Ointment Having explained the theodicy, Saadia adds one thing. Kohelet says זבובי מות יבאיש יביע שמן רוקח , dead flies spoil the perfumer's precious oil, and וחוטא אחד יאבד טובה הרבה , \"one sinner destroys much good.\" How can one sin have such a terrible effect? Saadia: it is a matter of naming . A person with 200 deeds, 100 merits and 100 demerits, is equally balanced, a beinoni (to be discussed later). One more action then changes the definition of what kind of person he is . Saadia does not quote it, but he clearly refers to the Gemara in Kiddushin : a person should always see himself as half guilty and half meritorious; one good deed makes him a tzaddik, one bad deed makes him a rasha. One sin does not change his actual deeds. He still has his 100 good deeds. But how we describe him changes, and \"in that sense it can have terrible consequences.\" The Ibn Ezra brings others who explain it differently: one sin of a person in a position of power and influence can do terrible damage because of its effect on the people he influences. A Discussion: Is the Line Between Tzaddik and Rasha Fair? \"Something I really struggle with.\" The Rabbi put it crudely, acknowledging that no human could make these calculations with all the variables involved: Merits Demerits Category Consequence Person A 50.1% 49.9% Tzaddik A portion in the world to come; suffering here, perhaps twelve months of Gehinnom, but limited Person B 49.9% 50.1% Rasha His good deeds repaid in this world; punishment in the next, \"whatever punishment is,\" seemingly for eternity \"The difference between these two people is minuscule... How is that fair? I personally find that very difficult. I don't have an answer.\" Voices from the class \"Who's counting?\" The Rabbi: for Hashem it is no calculation; \"it's an automatic spreadsheet, if you like.\" (\"He's got AI.\" \"He's got more than AI, but that's not for now.\") The Rambam in Hilchot Teshuvah: a participant recalled that the Rambam has a person see the whole world as balanced, so that each action can tip it. The Rabbi: when the class learnt that, they talked about identity . \"If we take that approach then I'm happy, it makes a lot of sense, because we look at the totality of who you are,\" not 50.1 against 49.9. \"I just feel that's the only way we can interpret Saadia.\" Robert asked whether all mitzvot and sins carry equal weight. \"Absolutely not.\" He added that a tzaddik is judged by a hair's breadth, so the majority is beyond our conception. \"A hundred percent. It's absolutely impossible for even the greatest human, the greatest computer, the greatest AI.\" Lifespan: a participant asked whether it matters how long a person lives. Everything is taken into account, \"millions, perhaps billions of variables.\" For the Rabbi this is one of the best and simplest answers to tzaddik vera lo : \"you've got no idea how HaKadosh Baruch Hu judges... you have no idea who's a tzaddik and who's a rasha.\" Some cases everyone agrees on: \"I think we can all agree that Hitler was a rasha.\" Is it judged only at the end of life? A participant pointed out that someone near the middle could flip from day to day. \"That's a very good point.\" The ultimate judgment is at the end of life, the great Day of Judgment, but a person is also judged every year and, as the Gemara says, every day . That is how Saadia explained last week why someone who improves his ways may suddenly suffer: he tipped from 49 to 51 and now his 49 must be paid here. And it can fluctuate back. A tzaddik who is nearly half rasha: a participant noted that we usually imagine a tzaddik as complete, yet Saadia's tzaddik could be doing a great deal wrong. \"Most definitely.\" Saadia has another category for that, the shalem . \"Tzaddik didn't mean someone who's perfect. Tzaddik means good people, generally good people,\" and the Rabbi believes this is how the Talmud uses the term too. Doron: in the natural world, minuscule percentages are everything. A bridge a fraction off falls down; owning one extra percent of a company gives controlling interest. If a line must be drawn somewhere, the last act tips the totality before it. \"That's an excellent point.\" The Rabbi said his difficulty might be \"more emotional than intellectual... we're talking about eternity here.\" Like karet , there must be a cut-off point where very similar people end up in very different places. Rabbi David Gottlieb: a participant recalled an essay of his, twenty or thirty years ago, giving eight answers to why the righteous suffer, among them that Hashem judges the tzaddik on a much higher scale (\"he might get eight out of ten\"), so his percentage goes down, while \"little Johnny\" who reached four when he should have reached two is credited; and common destiny , that we are all tied together. The Rabbi agreed: someone can be a real tzaddik inwardly and outwardly, yet the effort he puts in may not match the effort of someone we perceive as a rasha. All of this is taken into account. Chapter 4: The Oved, One Who Serves Saadia now returns to the ten categories. The oved is a person who commits himself to a particular mitzvah and never goes against it for the rest of his life. He may not do many other mitzvot. But this one: \"I'm never going to miss tefillah at the right time... never going to do something financially improper... never going to have a forbidden relationship.\" One mitzvah, taken on fully, genuinely, wholeheartedly and sincerely for life. In that sense even Esav could be called an oved , for his incredible kibbud av . A person who has never fully committed to any single mitzvah cannot be called an oved . Saadia quotes the Mishnah, כל העושה מצוה אחת מטיבין לו , \"whoever does one mitzvah, good is done for him,\" and says the Talmud Yerushalmi explains it as one mitzvah a person chooses and keeps forever. The Bavli interprets the same Mishnah as referring to someone exactly balanced, fifty-fifty. The Rabbi thought Saadia accepts both: he used the Bavli's idea earlier in speaking of the majority, and now uses the Yerushalmi's for the oved . The Sefer HaIkkarim: one mitzvah or all of them? \"I want to stop for a moment and focus on the book Sefer HaIkkarim.\" Rabbi Yosef Albo (Spain, fourteenth to fifteenth century) wrote on the principles of faith, and he quotes the Rambam on this very point. The Rabbi read it in English: The question: does the Torah bring a person to perfection only if he keeps all of it, or also if he keeps part? Horn of the dilemma Problem All the mitzvot are needed Then no one could reach perfection: כי אדם אין צדיק בארץ אשר יעשה טוב ולא יחטא . The Torah, given as an act of grace to bring Israel eternal life, would prevent them from reaching it, \"and all Israel would go down to the pit.\" One mitzvah is enough Then why burden us with so many? If one simple drug cures a disease, it is idle to compound many. \"It's vain to accomplish with many acts what can be realised with one.\" Albo's answer: many things in nature are not strictly necessary but amplify. One eye would be enough to see, but two let us see at all angles and in depth. So Hashem multiplied the ways to perfection, not all of them necessary, but making it easier to reach. This is the meaning of the Mishnah רצה הקב\"ה לזכות את ישראל לפיכך הרבה להם תורה ומצות : Hashem wanted to give Israel merit, so He gave them many mitzvot, more opportunities. And so, Albo writes, the Rambam says in his Commentary on the Mishnah that it is one of the principles of faith that if a person does one of the 613 mitzvot properly and correctly, without mixing in any other intention or self-interest, he merits a portion in the world to come . \"Like you say, if you want to do well in business, diversify.\" The more mitzvot, the more chances. Albo adds that of course the more one does, the greater one's portion. But one mitzvah, done properly, sincerely, wholeheartedly, for no reason but the service of HaKadosh Baruch Hu, makes a person an oved with a portion in the world to come. \"That's something we can all think about. What is it that we can turn back to at the end of our lives, on our deathbeds, and say: this is something I was completely committed to for my entire life, and I never veered from it.\" What counts as \"for its own sake\"? A participant asked whether enjoying the mitzvah counts as an ulterior motive. The Rabbi: \"The Rambam clearly says doing a mitzvah for reward does not work.\" It means doing it because I love God and want to be close to Him. \"When you love someone, you really, really love them... I just want to do everything you've told me to do because I want to make you pleased and happy.\" That is lishmah . \"Of course such a level is very difficult,\" but the more one tries, the likelier it becomes, like the golfer who said: \"the harder I practise, the luckier I get.\" Hopefully one action in our lives will be done for no other reason than that it is Hashem's mitzvah. A participant told a story of the Vilna Gaon explaining to his study partner that he had gone over everything a hundred times. Flora connected it to the Gemara on ושבתם וראיתם... בין עבד אלהים לאשר לא עבדו : the difference between one who served and one who did not is between someone who revised his learning a hundred times and someone who revised it a hundred and one times. The Rabbi's suggestion: a hundred times you may be doing for yourself, to remember it; the hundred and first is because this is the Torah of Hashem and you love it. \"It's not the difference of the one.\" Category 4: The Mamre, the Disobedient The mamre is someone with one mitzvah he rejects . Some mitzvot he works hard at, believes in and finds sensible; one he does not care about. Saadia cites the saying that every person has his own law , according to the variety of human beings. The Rabbi gave \"a much later saying\": everyone thinks they are in the centre. Anyone more religious is to the right; anyone less religious is not religious enough. We all have things we are more and less particular about. That is fine. The problem is rejecting something: \"I'm not doing that.\" The Hebrew term is מומר לדבר אחד , someone rebellious regarding one thing. The text on screen had a mistake here, the Rabbi noted. The halachic consequence: conversion This has consequences in halachah. The Rabbi brought a classic teshuvah of the Achiezer , Rabbi Chaim Ozer Grodzinski, from over a hundred years ago. A convert must accept the entire Torah; one who rejects a single thing is not converted. Yet many women who convert today will not be covering their hair, which is a mitzvah, whether from the Torah or the Chachamim. How can batei din convert them? The Achiezer's answer is exactly this distinction, between קבלת מצות , accepting the mitzvot, and עשיית מצות , doing them. \"How many of us have so many things we want to do in life, we aspire to do, but we haven't done yet? Whether it's going to the gym, eating more healthily, spending more time with our spouses.\" There are many mitzvot we should be doing and do not. \"It's a problem, but fine. One day, hopefully, you'll do it.\" Such a convert can be accepted. The problem is someone who says: this mitzvah I reject; it is sexist, it is patriarchal. Rejection out of conviction is the mamre : \"even if it's one mitzvah, that's a problem.\" Category 5: The Shalem, the Complete The shalem is the completely righteous person who does everything correctly . (This answers the earlier point: the ordinary tzaddik is the majority; the shalem is the one we nowadays call a tzaddik.) Some people think no human can keep every mitzvah. Saadia: it is possible . Otherwise Hashem would not have commanded the mitzvot. And the atonements in the Torah? They are written for the potential : if you sin, here is the way back. That does not mean you must sin. And the verse כי אדם אין צדיק בארץ אשר יעשה טוב ולא יחטא , \"there is no righteous man on earth who does good and does not sin\"? Saadia reads it as: there is no one, even a tzaddik, who does good without the possibility of sinning . It does not say he sins; it says he could. \"Now this is a remarkable interpretation, certainly not the simple interpretation.\" The Ibn Ezra and others read it as it sounds: humans sin. And it contradicts an explicit Gemara in Sanhedrin: Rabbi Eliezer fell ill and his students came to visit. They wept; Rabbi Akiva laughed. Why are you crying? How can we not cry, when the great Rabbi Eliezer is in pain? Rabbi Akiva: that is why I laugh. When I saw everything going well for my teacher, I thought perhaps he was receiving his reward in this world. Now that I see him suffering, I am glad: his portion in the world to come is secure. Rabbi Eliezer asked: have I neglected anything of the Torah? Rabbi Akiva answered: you yourself taught us, \"there is no righteous man on earth who does good and does not sin.\" So the Gemara understands the verse simply: people do sin. (The Rabbi noted in passing that Rabbi Akiva is the symbol of hope in many Gemarot where everyone else cries and he laughs, a subject of a past shiur.) There is also the Gemara that four people died only through the serpent's counsel , without sin of their own, which would seem to support Saadia. \"In any case, it's quite remarkable. Saadia believes it's possible for a person to be absolutely perfect and never to do something wrong.\" Summary Six reasons the wicked prosper: payment for their minority of good deeds; Hashem knows they will repent (Menashe); a righteous descendant will come from them (Achaz, Amon); they serve as Hashem's rod (Ashur); a righteous person asked for them (Tzoar); and to intensify their punishment (Pharaoh). Yirmiyahu was told the main reason is the last. \"One sinner destroys much good\" is about naming : one act can change a balanced person's category, not erase his deeds. The Ibn Ezra brings the influence of the powerful. The fairness of a razor-thin line troubled the Rabbi. The class suggested identity, unequal weights, countless variables, daily judgment, and that tipping points are part of reality. \"You have no idea who's a tzaddik and who's a rasha.\" The oved : one who commits to one mitzvah for life (the Yerushalmi). Sefer HaIkkarim and the Rambam : one mitzvah done perfectly lishmah earns the world to come; the many mitzvot are many opportunities. The mamre : one who rejects even one mitzvah. The Achiezer distinguishes accepting mitzvot from doing them, with consequences for conversion. The shalem : one who keeps everything. Saadia holds it is possible , reading Kohelet as speaking of the capacity to sin, against the simple reading and the Gemara of Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Akiva. Coming Up Next week, at the usual time of 6 o'clock: the rest of Chapter 4, categories six to nine (the mekatzer , negligent; the choteh , offender; the mezid , rebellious offender; and the kofer , unbeliever), and then the penitent and Saadia's discussion of teshuvah .","pJJzaBVcE_8":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 5 (Merits and Demerits), the end of Chapter 2 and the first part of Chapter 3, with the Rambam's Moreh Nevuchim 3:22-23 on Iyov Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 20 January 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: The Majority and the Minority The book so far: Essay 1 on creation, Essay 2 on God, Essay 3 on Torah and revelation, Essay 4 on free will. Essay 5 is about how we serve God and the consequences of our deeds. From Chapter 1: Hashem remembers everything, and every action leaves an impression on the soul, so that two people can do outwardly identical things, one permitted and one forbidden, with starkly different effects on their souls. Everything is paid in full in the Olam HaBa that Hashem will create (unlike the Rambam's view, that Olam HaBa already coexists with this world). From Chapter 2: there are ten categories of people (\"not ten levels, ten categories\"). A tzaddik has more merits than demerits; a rasha has more demerits than merits. The majority is repaid in the world to come; the minority in this world. So the tzaddik's sins are dealt with here, and the rasha's mitzvot are rewarded here. Last week the class asked why the rasha's mitzvot should be repaid in this finite world. The Rabbi's answer: his are not olam haba mitzvot. They are done with ulterior motives and not fully. That is why the Rambam writes that one mitzvah done fully, genuinely and sincerely earns a portion in the world to come : such a deed can only be repaid there. \"I'm not entirely sure if Saadia would agree with that,\" but Saadia's weighing of the majority \"can be perhaps compatible with the Rambam's identity approach\" learnt on Tuesday nights. Tonight: the rest of Chapter 2 and Chapter 3, Saadia's theodicy, \"and then we will see the Rambam's understanding based on the book of Iyov.\" What about the flourishing tzaddik? A participant asked: if the tzaddik's sins are paid here, how can we ever see a tzaddik flourishing, with everything going well? The Rabbi offered two possibilities: either such a person is entirely perfect, a shalem , so that everything goes well in both worlds; or, better, everyone has some suffering, \"we don't know the exact suffering a person has,\" and his inconveniences here and there are the payment for his few bad deeds, while overall he flourishes. A Word of Caution Before Theodicy \"There's one thing I want to say before we do this, which Saadia doesn't say. The Rambam, to the best of my knowledge, doesn't say this, but later authorities say this, and I think there's so much truth to it.\" The Rabbi said Rav Dessler states it explicitly, though it surely precedes him: We are learning general theodicy. We must be very careful never to apply it to particular people. \"Whatever we learn in Saadia tonight, or in the Rambam in the Moreh, we can't now start viewing people through the lens of Saadia and the Moreh and say, he's going through this because of that.\" The Mishnah already tells us we have no idea who is a tzaddik and who is a rasha. Some people seem good and are not; some seem not to be decent people and have things we are not aware of. \"I've given many, many shiurim on the whole concept of theodicy and all the different approaches. And yet when people ask me, why does this happen to me or to him or to her, I don't use these approaches... No one can answer such a question. Really only HaKadosh Baruch Hu can.\" A participant connected this to Rabbi Yannai in Pirkei Avot: אין בידינו לא משלות הרשעים ואף לא מיסורי הצדיקים , \"it is not in our hands to explain the tranquillity of the wicked or the suffering of the righteous.\" The participant had always translated it as the seeming suffering of the righteous. The Rabbi checked the text and said the word \"seemingly\" is not there, though some commentators surely say so, and the idea is true. Another participant added that each person has a potential known only to Hashem: someone very good may reach eight out of ten when he should have reached ten. \"Which is why we cannot judge anyone, for the good or for the bad. And the only way we can answer is to look into ourselves, not what's happening to them.\" Chapter 2 Continued: Regretting Good Deeds Saadia: everything said so far about the majority applies when a person has no regret . But: If he did many mitzvot and regretted them, he loses them through his regret. ובשוב צדיק מצדקתו ועשה עול... כל צדקתו אשר עשה לא תזכרנה , \"when a righteous man turns from his righteousness... none of his righteousness will be remembered.\" The Gemara explains this as one who regrets his good deeds. If he did many sins, regretted them and fulfilled the conditions of repentance, he is rid of them : ובשוב רשע מרשעתו... כל פשעיו אשר עשה לא יזכרו לו . \"Isn't this a bit shocking? You do a good deed, and you regret it, you've lost that deed. I find that very difficult. The Gemara does say that.\" An asymmetry in the text The Rabbi pointed out something in Saadia's wording. Later in this essay (Chapters 6 or 7), Saadia sets out the process of teshuvah: regret, a commitment for the future and the rest. Here: What Saadia requires to lose it Mitzvot Regret alone. \"Full stop. The regret itself makes you lose them.\" Sins Regret and fulfilling the process of teshuvah Take a simple case. A person gives charity. A week later he loses a lot of money and says, \"I wish I had never given that money.\" He has not resolved never to give again; he just regrets it. Is that enough to lose the mitzvah? If so, it would seem that mitzvot are easier to lose than sins, \"which would be theologically very difficult,\" since we have always been taught that Hashem's kindness and reward are greater than His punishment. Or perhaps Saadia only spells out the process for sins because we are more familiar with teshuvah, and the same process would be needed to lose a mitzvah. \"I'm not entirely clear what Saadia means here, but I think this is a very important point to bring out.\" Suggestions from the class Mark: regretting a mitzvah, as in the charity example, is really questioning Hashem: why did I lose the money? It involves a lack of faith , which makes it more severe and explains why no formula is needed. The Rabbi: \"Interesting. So you're saying every regret of a mitzvah has an element of lack of faith... I have to think about that.\" Later in the text Saadia indeed calls the regret denial ( kefirah ): \"It's interesting, Mark, he uses that word.\" Doron , connecting to Tuesday nights: a mitzvah builds something positive in your identity; \"it's not some sort of candy counter.\" Regretting it disconnects you from what you built. A sin needs a process for the future, because merely regretting it doesn't stop anything. Another participant: a mitzvah has an immediate merit and a continuing merit, credited to you as long as you stand behind it. Regret withdraws you from the good intention. The Rabbi: \"All three of your things are agreeing with each other.\" He heard the identity idea but was again \"reluctant to impose the Rambam's ideas on Saadia,\" though he said he had found some indications over the week that Saadia may accept it. The burglary and Shacharit \"I'm not giving an answer to this. Just appreciate the following scenario.\" I got up early, said Shema and prayed in shul. Then I found out that while I was in shul my house was burgled. \"I should never have gone to synagogue.\" I regret it, so I have lost the mitzvah. It is now 8:30 and there is still time for Shacharit. Am I now obliged to pray again? And if I then regret my regret? In teshuvah, as the Rambam's Hilchot Teshuvah puts it very strongly, the sin is uprooted : it is gone from a person's identity, so much so that one may not remind him of it. Is the lost mitzvah the same? A participant: there was a forfeiture . The action happened; what is lost is the merit, the identity building. There is no new obligation. The Rabbi agreed, and then wondered aloud whether teshuvah works the same way. \"You can't rewrite history. The deed has been done. Even when you do teshuvah, the deed is there... Anything associated to that is completely wiped out.\" That is why part of teshuvah is repairing what one can in this world: a thief must return what he stole. \"It doesn't mean you didn't steal.\" The practical lesson \"What's key here is for us to appreciate how important it is to take pride and enjoyment in the good which we do in life, and not, chas veshalom, to regret it.\" But if the charity turned out to be dishonest, that is a legitimate regret. \"Hashem didn't command me to give to dishonest institutions. I regret that I was duped, and that's fine... be more vigilant in the future.\" When fortunes reverse Saadia continues: A person whose merits are the majority, with his reward prepared in the world to come, begins to regret his good deeds, to deny . His merits are knocked off, and Hashem pays some of them in this world. People see that as soon as he became a heretic, the good life began, \"and this can actually cause people to sin.\" But the truth is that the good he receives now is the reward that would have been his in the next world. Conversely, a person with a majority of demerits begins to mend his ways, and just then everything goes badly for him. People are astonished. But it is not because he started doing good. His past sins must now be dealt with, and Hashem decides to do it in this world. \"When you think about this, you will get rid of any doubts,\" Saadia says, \"and strengthen your heart for service.\" The Rabbi summed it up: \"Mending a person's ways can actually sometimes lead to misfortune in this world, and vice versa. A person leaving the path of HaKadosh Baruch Hu... can actually lead to many benefits and enjoyment of the pleasures of this world.\" Each deed separately Finally Saadia addresses a question: how can one regret undo many mitzvot? He answers that it is incorrect to think so: each deed must be regretted separately . Likewise, one act of repentance does not wipe out all one's sins; each must be repented. \"It's not just you change one way and everything changes.\" Chapter 3: Why the Righteous Suffer Saadia gives \"two explanations, two slash three. It's two and a half.\" 1. Payment for the minority As in Chapter 2: the majority of a tzaddik's deeds are rewarded in the next world, and his minority of sins is dealt with in this one. 2. A test the person can bear Hashem brings misfortune on a person when He knows the person can endure it , and then rewards him for enduring: ה' צדיק יבחן , \"Hashem tests the righteous.\" He does not generally give such tests to people who will not withstand them. The question is: will your faith be impaired by the suffering, or will you remain strong, steadfast and loyal? The Rabbi noted that Saadia generally portrays Iyov as a good person who accepted what Hashem gave him; the Rambam, as the class would see, disagrees. The Midrash of the flax. When good quality flax is pressed and pounded, its quality improves. Bad flax, when struck, simply breaks. So Hashem gives this test only to the righteous. Does Hashem tell you why? Saadia makes a further distinction: Suffering for sins: when a person asks why, it is customary for Hashem to tell him, as in על מה עשה ה' ככה , \"why has Hashem done this?\", which is followed by the answer \"because they forsook the covenant.\" Knowing the reason encourages teshuvah. Suffering as a test: generally Hashem does not tell him. Saadia's example is Moshe , who asked Hashem why this was happening; \"I'm critical of Moshe's question here,\" Saadia says. On Saadia's reading Moshe's suffering was a test to increase his reward, and he was not told the reason. That is fitting: \"the whole purpose of this test is, are you going to stand strong in your faith. If I tell you that I'm testing you, then there's no point in it.\" \"Has anyone ever tried it and it's worked? I'm sure many of us, when going through difficult times in life, have asked HaKadosh Baruch Hu why, and we don't always get the answer.\" The Rabbi's own suggestion, \"purely, I have no proof of this,\" based on the Gemara in Berachot, which links the nature of the suffering to the sin being rectified: perhaps Saadia means that the explanation is latent in the form the suffering takes. \"As I said, I don't fully understand this, because I haven't always experienced this.\" 2½. To increase reward \"Furthermore,\" Saadia says, even a person who is complete may sometimes suffer, not as a test, but purely to increase his reward in the next world . His proof: infants . Babies suffer great illnesses and pain, and sometimes die. It cannot be for sins, and it cannot be a test, \"because you can't test a one year old.\" It must be to increase their reward. The suffering of the good is for their good, like a parent who pushes a child roughly out of the path of a car, or gives him bitter medicine for his health. The Rabbi believes this is how Saadia understands יסורין של אהבה , sufferings of love. The Gemara says that if a person sees suffering come upon him he should examine his deeds; if he finds nothing, they are sufferings of love. Saadia says the same elsewhere: in Essays 6 and 8 he uses it to explain why the children in the Flood died, and why the children of Midian were killed (in Parashat Matot-Masei), though they had done nothing wrong. They are compensated in the next world. \"Theologically I don't understand this. I don't know how this works. I'll be honest with you, I much prefer the Rambam's approach, that sufferings come for a reason.\" How does suffering cleanse sin? The Rabbi added something he said he should have explained earlier: what is the mechanism by which suffering wipes away sin? The Gemara in Berachot says: נאמר ברית במלח ונאמר ברית ביסורין . \"Covenant\" is said of salt, and \"covenant\" is said of suffering. Just as salt sweetens meat, suffering cleanses a person of his sins. The Maharsha explains it beautifully. Salt draws the blood out of the meat, and that is what gives it its taste; an expensive piece of meat without salt and seasoning is not enjoyed. A person sins with his body, and the driving force of the body is the blood, כי הדם הוא הנפש . Suffering, as it were, draws the blood out: it weakens the physical self and quells the passions that cause sin. \"When a person is in bed, or has a serious illness, they're weaker. Then they start thinking about the serious questions of life,\" because the physical identity is weakened and the spiritual identity can come forward. \"So that I can understand. But how can it be that just because I suffer in this world, somehow I get some kind of reward in the next world?\" That does not fit the Rambam's whole understanding of Olam HaBa, which the Tuesday class is learning. Why not just give the reward? Saadia asks it himself: if the suffering of an infant is only to increase his reward, why not simply give the extra reward without the suffering? He answers with what he said in the first essays about the purpose of creation: what one has worked for is a greater reward . Having suffered, one has earned it. \"I find this all very, very difficult.\" The second part of the chapter, Saadia's six explanations of why the wicked prosper (the Rabbi corrected a misprint of \"seven\"), was left for next week. Reincarnation: Not Saadia's Answer Some people, Saadia will say in Essay 6, adopted belief in gilgul , reincarnation, to explain the suffering of children: they are paying for a previous life. Saadia calls this absolute nonsense and takes it apart very forcefully. The Rambam did not believe in reincarnation either. The Zohar believes in it very strongly, and those who follow it can explain children's suffering that way. The Rabbi said he would discuss it properly in Essay 6, but made the point now: \"there is no classical or traditional source which believes in gilgul... There's no mention in Tanach, in the whole of Chazal.\" It became widespread and accepted because of the Zohar, \"which is a whole different question as regards the authenticity and the dating of the Zohar, which is not for now.\" The Belz chassid and the Rashash To show how heated the issue is, the Rabbi told a story from his yeshiva days in Gateshead. He was friendly with a Belzer chassid, and mentioned a question asked by the Rashash , Rabbi Shmuel Strashun of Lithuania, whose concise and precise notes are printed at the back of the Talmud. \"We don't learn the Rashash,\" came the answer. Why not? The Gemara in Bava Metzia explains the blessing ברוך אתה בבאך וברוך אתה בצאתך : just as you come into the world clean and without sin, so may you leave it. On this the Rashash writes a few words: this is a difficulty for the doctrine of reincarnation , since according to the Zohar a person enters the world with all kinds of sins to rectify. Because of that comment, the chassid explained, a Belzer Rebbe some time ago ruled that the Rashash did not have the right hashkafah, and to this day, as the Rabbi has been told and others have confirmed, Belzer chassidim do not study his commentary on the Talmud. Other Rishonim on the Suffering of the Righteous Chovot HaLevavot (Rabbeinu Bachya) says exactly what Saadia says, with the same three explanations: the minority of sins, a test, and increased reward. The Kuzari gives the same three and adds a fourth: a righteous person can suffer for the sins of his ancestors . The Rambam: Moreh Nevuchim 3:22-23 on Iyov \"There's a really lot to get through, I'm going to go really quickly.\" The Rabbi translated the key points: The book of Iyov is a fascinating book that teaches much about hashgachah , providence. Did it happen? One of the Chachamim says Iyov never existed and was only a parable . Others say he did, but they disagree on when: in the time of Avraham, of Moshe, of David, or of the Babylonian exile. That very disagreement supports the view that it never happened. Either way, it deals with the question \"which everyone asks, any intellectual human\": why do the righteous suffer? \"There was a man in the land of Utz\": Utz is from עצה , counsel. The whole book is advice on how to deal with theodicy. Iyov is never called wise. He is called tam , upright, a good person with good deeds, but not wise, \"because if he was a wise person he would not have had these questions.\" So for the Rambam, Iyov did not understand things properly. The Rambam identifies the views of Iyov and his friends with the views on providence: Speaker View The Rabbi's gloss Iyov From God's perspective the righteous and the wicked fare the same; He does not attend to human deeds The view of Aristotle (or Epicurus). For the Rambam, Iyov really denied hashgachah, which is why the Chachamim criticised him Eliphaz Everything is for sins you have done The Rambam: this is the view of our Torah Bildad Compensation ( pitzui ): if you have no sin, it is to increase your reward Saadia's third explanation. The Rambam uses a legal word, \"compensation,\" and does not accept it Tzofar You can never answer the question, because you cannot understand the will of God, any more than why the sky is blue. Accept it and move on The view of the Arabic theologians Elihu A fifth approach \"A bit more complicated,\" left aside The Rambam concludes very clearly that the correct approach, that of our Torah, is Eliphaz's: there is no suffering and no death without sin . He rejects the Gemara that four people died without sin. He rejects the idea that Hashem makes people suffer to increase their reward. So for the Rambam, yissurin shel ahava are simply sufferings that wipe away sins you do not know about. This ties in with something learnt \"a few months ago\": Saadia held that animals slaughtered or made to suffer will be rewarded in the next world, and the Rambam rejected it outright. \"It's not just the concept of hashgachah, it's much deeper than that. Can you reward someone just for suffering they go through? Saadia says yes... For the Rambam it doesn't work like that. Suffering is for sin, and that's it.\" How the Rambam explains the suffering of babies the class did not discuss here; the Rabbi noted only that he cannot use Saadia's answer. Summary Regret undoes deeds. Regretting mitzvot loses them; regretting sins with full teshuvah removes them. Saadia's wording seems to require less to lose a mitzvah than a sin, which is \"theologically very difficult.\" Take pride in your good deeds. Fortunes can reverse: a righteous person who turns to denial may prosper, and a sinner who mends his ways may suffer, as past accounts are settled in this world. Each deed is regretted or repented separately. Saadia's reasons for the suffering of the righteous: payment for the minority of sins; a test of faith given only to those who can bear it (the flax), whose reason is generally not revealed; and suffering to increase reward , proved from infants, which is his reading of yissurin shel ahava . The Gemara and Maharsha: suffering cleanses like salt, by weakening the physical drives behind sin. Chovot HaLevavot agrees with Saadia; the Kuzari adds ancestral sin. Saadia and the Rambam both reject gilgul as an explanation, and it has no source in Tanach or Chazal. The Rambam (Moreh 3:22-23): Iyov is a parable; the friends represent the philosophical views; the Torah's view is Eliphaz's, that all suffering is for sin . Never apply theodicy to individuals. Only Hashem knows why a particular person suffers. Coming Up Next week: the second half of Chapter 3, Saadia's six explanations of why the wicked prosper , \"much easier, much simpler,\" and then Chapter 4.","dHYIDcQJkbA":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 5 (Merits and Demerits), Chapter 1 and the first part of Chapter 2 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 13 January 2025, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Halfway Through the Book The first shiur after the winter break. The Rabbi noted that the series had begun almost exactly a year earlier, and that the class was now \"about halfway\" through Rav Saadia Gaon's book, even though it was only beginning the fifth of ten essays: \"the big ones, the major ones, are the first three, four. Other ones are relatively smaller.\" The plan, be'ezrat Hashem, is to complete Emunot VeDeot during 2025. A recap of the year Subject Introduction Saadia's purpose in writing; what emunah is; the ways a person reaches wisdom; and the reasons people deny HaKadosh Baruch Hu Essay 1 Proof that there is a Creator (not yet \"what we call HaKadosh Baruch Hu,\" only that the universe has a creator), against thirteen views of how the universe came to be Essay 2 Hashem as we conceive Him: His unity , the anthropomorphisms of the Torah, and a couple of chapters disproving the Trinity Essay 3 Revelation : the Torah; the categories of mitzvot (the rational ones and the revealed ones, which we need Hashem to give us); reasons for the mitzvot; and prophecy : what a prophet is, the case of Yonah, and how a prophet proves he is true Essay 4 (Sukkot to the end of 2024) Free will . The universe was created for human beings, who are the pinnacle of creation and have been given free will, proved from the senses, logic, the Torah and tradition Believing in the right God On Essay 2 the Rabbi added a point he has made before. The Rambam rules that a person who believes Hashem has a body is a heretic, even if that is how he was brought up and he knows no better. The Raavad objects: it was not his fault, and many people believe Hashem has some kind of body. The Rambam's answer is that such a person simply does not believe in Hashem . \"It's all well and good saying you believe in Hashem, but if you have the wrong conception of what Hashem is, then you can't say you believe in Hashem.\" The logic of Essay 5 Essay 1: there is a creation. Essay 2: there is a God, correctly conceived. Essay 3: Hashem has revealed His will. Essay 4: you can choose . Essay 5: the results of those choices . The essay is called זכיות וחובות , Merits and Demerits . In it Saadia will: list ten categories of people (tzaddik, rasha, oved, mamre, kofer and so on) and explain each; discuss teshuvah , both repentance for bad deeds and regret for good ones; discuss theodicy , why people who seem to be good suffer in this world. The Rabbi said that Rabbi Yehudah HaChasid , some centuries after Saadia, gave this essay special significance and called it the essay on repentance . \"But it's really the same thing.\" And on theodicy: there are statements in Chazal, \"but as someone who actually asks the question and discusses it\" systematically, Saadia is the first. One curiosity: every essay so far opened with a short introduction before Chapter 1. From this essay to the end of the book, Saadia does not do this again. \"Is it because he decides to change his method, or because he felt these don't need it? I'm not entirely clear, but just interesting to point that out.\" Chapter 1, Part 1: We Are Judged by the Majority Saadia opens: Hashem has informed us that when a person's acts of service are many, they are called merits ( zechuyot ) , and when his transgressions are many, they are together called demerits ( chovot ) . Of course every mitzvah is a mitzvah and every aveirah is an aveirah. The Rabbi explained Saadia's point as one of classification : a person with many mitzvot is classified as a zakai , a good person; a person with more sins than mitzvot is a chayav . The few mitzvot of such a person are not called his \"merits.\" \"He very much views the person as the sum of the majority of his or her actions .\" Saadia will develop this throughout the essay, and the class will need to discuss how it fits with what they learnt in the Rambam's Hilchot Teshuvah : \"I don't know if it goes so finely or not.\" Chapter 1, Part 2: Hashem Sees Everything Hashem remembers and watches over everything a person does: גדל העצה ורב העליליה אשר עיניך פקחות על כל דרכי בני אדם , \"great in counsel and mighty in deed, whose eyes are open to all the ways of mankind.\" All our merits and demerits are known to Him and guarded by Him. Chapter 1, Part 3: Every Act Leaves an Impression on the Soul Every action a person does leaves an impression on his soul. The majority of one's actions make the soul either pure and pristine or murky and dirty . That is why the Torah says of sin ונשא עונו , \"he shall bear his iniquity\": you carry it, because it changes who you are. \"But I don't see it\" You might say: I do good and I don't see any effect; I do bad and I don't see any effect. Saadia answers that though we cannot see it, it is clear to Hashem, and Scripture and tradition both attest to it. Then he works the point through an observation: there are many professions in which the uninitiated cannot tell the expert from the amateur, or good work from bad. Only someone who understands the discipline can. Discipline What the layman cannot tell apart Money changing (\"he likes the analogy of money changing; he's given it a number of times\") A good coin from a bad coin Medicine The pulse of the veins, contraction from dilation, correct treatment from incorrect Physiognomy ( kiyafa ) A person's nature from the lines of his face and hands. \"I didn't know there was a word for this discipline.\" Aristotle believed in it, and so, it seems, did Saadia Pearls and jewellery \"For most of us jewellery looks the same,\" but an expert can tell Saadia's rule: the more refined a discipline, the more people will be mistaken about it , and the more people will claim to know it who do not. The soul is the most refined thing of all. Our physical senses cannot sense the impressions made on it, but they are revealed to its Creator, who made it. (Whether Saadia thought the nefesh is something physical or the whole identity of a person will be discussed in Essay 6 , which is all about the soul.) Hashem compares the soul to the heavens and the stars, and to a candle , נר ה' נשמת אדם : just as a candle reveals every nook and cranny, as the class would be learning on Thursday night about bedikat chametz , the soul illuminates every aspect of a person. Metaphorically, it is even powerful enough to melt gold. Kosher and non-kosher, permitted and forbidden \"Think about it. This is quite interesting.\" A person eats two foods, one kosher and one not; both give him the same sustenance, \"the same protein, the same everything.\" A man is intimate with two women, one permitted to him and one forbidden; the action and the pleasure seem identical. To us they are the same. Only the Examiner knows the impact of each on the soul. When there are more merits, the soul shines and the person \"will see with light\"; the more sins, the cloudier it becomes. The Ramchal , some 800 years later, says exactly the same in Derech Hashem : everything a person does affects his soul, and the reason we cannot see it is that we live in a physical body. It is all there in potential , waiting to shine when it is no longer held back and hidden in the body. Chapter 1, Part 4: It Is All Written Down Hashem records everything for good and for bad: of the righteous, ויכתב ספר זכרון לפניו , \"a book of remembrance was written before Him\"; of the wicked, הנה כתובה לפני לא אחשה כי אם שלמתי , \"it is written before Me; I will not keep silent, but will repay.\" Saadia reflects on why the Torah speaks of writing. Hashem gave us speech and letters. We created alphabets to express in writing what we think, and by writing things down we keep them for posterity. Hashem obviously needs no book, but He uses the image of books and accounts because that is how humans operate and understand. Chapter 1, Part 5: The World of Action and the World of Reward The end of the chapter begins Saadia's discussion of theodicy, which continues in Chapter 2. While we live in this world of action (not the Kabbalistic olam ha'asiyah ; he simply means the world where we act), Hashem remembers everything, and He has prepared the consequences in the world of reward . He will create that world when the full number of human beings He has decided to create is complete . How this differs from the Rambam The Rabbi asked what stands out compared with what the class learnt on Tuesday nights with the Rambam. A participant answered: doesn't the Rambam say Olam HaBa is here? \"Excellent.\" Saadia , and in truth the simple reading of the Gemara: Olam HaBa is a world that will come into being after this world is completed , when all the humans who need to be created are finished. Saadia will develop this in Essay 9 . The Rambam : Olam HaBa, \"the world to come,\" means the world that comes for us after we finish this world. It already exists. \"Every action which you do is Olam HaBa or not, and it's here waiting for us.\" The traditional sense, the Gemara's 6,000 years, the Rambam does not necessarily accept. But there is also a Gemara that the end of days is only ushered in when all the souls that need to be created are completed . When is that? \"No idea. I don't think anyone can give the answer to that.\" In that world everything will be accounted for: כי את כל מעשה האלהים יבא במשפט . Two puzzling lines Saadia adds that Hashem does not leave people in this world without some reward for their good deeds and some punishment for their bad ones, \"as an indicator\" of their status for the time when the next world is created. The Rabbi asked the class how to read this, and offered two readings: The reward and punishment of the next world are given in miniature here, as a sign of what awaits. The main reward is in the next world, but the minority of a person's deeds are paid here. (This matches what Saadia says in Chapter 2.) \"It's not clear. Let's keep both interpretations in mind.\" Saadia supports the idea that there is reward in this world from the blessings and curses of the Torah, and from הלא הוא כמס עמדי חתום באוצרתי , \"it is stored up with Me, sealed in My storehouses.\" Chapter 2: The Ten Categories of People From Scripture and tradition, Saadia lays out ten levels of people with respect to merits and demerits: # Hebrew English 1 צדיק the righteous 2 רשע the wicked 3 עובד the obedient (the Rabbi corrected a misprint in the text on screen) 4 ממרה the disobedient 5 שלם the blameless 6 מקצר the deficient, the negligent 7 חוטא the offender 8 מזיד the wilful offender 9 שב the penitent 10 כופר the denier The ten are not five pairs . The tzaddik and rasha, the oved and mamre, the shalem and mekatzer correspond; but the choteh and mezid are both negatives, and the shav and kofer are not opposites. \"It would be wrong to think of these as pairs.\" There is also an eleventh : the בינוני , the person exactly in the middle. Saadia devotes a whole chapter to him, but his point is that the beinoni , being exactly half and half, is pushed over by Hashem to the side of the righteous. Saadia explains categories 1 and 2, then returns to theodicy, and only in Chapter 4 resumes the rest of the list. The tzaddik and the rasha: like hot and cold A tzaddik is someone whose merits are the majority; a rasha is someone whose demerits are the majority. This is how we speak of nature: something is hot when there is more heat than cold in it; a body is healthy when there is more health in it than illness. So the prophets call kings righteous or wicked by the majority of their deeds: Yehoshafat is called righteous, though he was rebuked: הלרשע לעזר ולשנאי ה' תאהב ובזאת עליך קצף , \"should you help the wicked and love those who hate Hashem? For this there is wrath upon you.\" Chizkiyahu is called righteous, though לא כגמל עליו השיב יחזקיהו כי גבה לבו : despite all the good he was given, he grew arrogant. Yehu (son of Yehoshafat son of Nimshi) is called wicked, though he stamped out the worship of Baal. He killed Izevel and trampled her body, killed the royal princes and Yehoram. He did something good, but the majority of his deeds were bad. Tzidkiyahu is called wicked, though he did not let his officials kill Yirmiyahu. He constantly ignored Yirmiyahu's warnings of destruction, entered the alliance with the neighbouring countries that brought Nevuchadnezzar against Jerusalem, and imprisoned the prophet, but he kept him alive. Payment for the minority in this world Hashem, in His wisdom, decided to repay the minority of a person's deeds in this world , and keep the majority for the next. Why? Because in the next world there is no changing position . Whatever level a person enters it at is the level he stays at permanently: אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם , \"some to everlasting life, and some to everlasting abhorrence.\" The Rabbi called this \"very important.\" The Kabbalists, the Ramchal , speak of a person rising from level to level in the next world, and there is a hint to that in the Gemara: ילכו מחיל אל חיל . Saadia rejects it. \"Whatever position you enter when you die is what you will be for eternity, for the good and for the bad.\" So the tzaddik's sins are dealt with here, and the rasha's mitzvot are paid here: ומשלם לשנאיו אל פניו להאבידו , \"He repays those who hate Him to their face, to destroy them.\" The Rabbi's Question: Is This Fair to the Rasha? \"Let's stop there for a moment. Doesn't that trouble you?\" The reward and punishment of Olam HaBa are eternal and incomparable to anything in this world. \"Let's be really, really crude here\": Mitzvot Sins Next world This world Tzaddik 60 40 Eternal reward for the 60 Punished for the 40 Rasha 40 60 Eternal punishment for the 60 Rewarded for the 40 For the tzaddik this is a good deal: \"it's better for me to take it here.\" But why should the rasha's forty mitzvot be paid off in this world, when even 120 years of a perfect life, \"all the pleasures of this world,\" are incomparable to the reward of Olam HaBa? Suggestions from the class One participant cited the Mishnah in Pirkei Avot that one hour of bliss in the world to come is better than all the life of this world . The Rabbi: that explains the tzaddik, \"Fantastic. But how would you explain the opposite?\" Another suggested that perhaps the reward here is of a lower order. \"It's still incomparable.\" Another suggested that if reward and punishment were too obvious, free will would be compromised. \"I hear it, but it's still not fair to the rasha. You can't give me the opportunity to have free will at the expense of the rasha.\" Theodicy: Moshe, Aharon and Aviyah Saadia's examples: Moshe and Aharon sinned once, at the waters. \"Whatever the sin was,\" and the Rabbi noted there are at least two dozen opinions about what it was, they were punished in this world and could not enter Eretz Yisrael. Aviyah, son of Yerovam , from a wicked house, did one good thing: he removed the barriers his father had set up to stop people going to the Temple. He was rewarded in this world: יען נמצא בו דבר טוב , \"because some good thing was found in him,\" he alone of his family would be buried properly. So, Saadia concludes, it is possible for a righteous person, with many sins though fewer than his mitzvot, to be afflicted and oppressed his whole life, \"as if he doesn't keep anything,\" and for a sinner with some mitzvot to have a wonderful life, so that people say, \"look at this person, he seems to be such a righteous person.\" A black and white picture Mark asked: doesn't that assume the righteous person gets absolutely no benefit in this world for any good he does, \"not even a taste of the world to come\"? \"That's what he's saying. That's a good point.\" \"The more I think about it, the more I think actually Saadia has a bit of a black and white approach.\" If life is going wonderfully, either you are a terrible person being paid here, or you are completely perfect. If life is terrible, either you are a tzaddik having your sins dealt with here, or you are thoroughly wicked and will suffer in both worlds. \"It's difficult, because it's not really how the world runs, let's be honest about it. Although who are we to be able to judge to understand. But that's his view.\" The Rabbi's Answer: It Depends on Who You Are \"I don't like it when people force interpretations into words of Rishonim, and therefore I'm hesitant to do this.\" But the Rabbi suggested that Saadia's \"majority\" is not arithmetic. It is about identity , an idea that fits the Rambam, and which Rav Dessler also argues. When Saadia says the majority of a person's actions are good, he means: that is who he is . At his core he is good; he wants to do good and does good most of the time. He slips. A rasha is a person who is bad at his core. Here and there he does something good. So a rasha's mitzvah is, \"in inverted commas, an olam hazeh mitzvah\": done for some benefit, with ulterior motives, because his values are this world's. \"You are an olam hazeh person. And so it's fitting that you have consequences for your actions in olam hazeh .\" The tzaddik's world is the next world. \"Yes, here and there I slip, but it's not really who I am. This world is not who I am.\" So his slips can be dealt with here. This also explains the Rambam on the Mishnah that Hashem gave Israel many mitzvot: since there are so many, a person has the opportunity to do at least one properly, \"fully, genuinely, completely, no ulterior motives,\" and by it he earns a portion in the world to come. A mitzvah that is genuine and sincere cannot be paid off in this world . So Saadia must be speaking of a person who does more bad than good, whose good is not sincere or complete. \"This is what you value. You care about this world. We'll pay you off here. You'll have a great life here.\" \"Think about it. We'll pick up on it next week.\" Summary The fifth essay , Merits and Demerits , is about the results of our choices : the ten categories of people, teshuvah, and theodicy. A person is classified by the majority of his deeds. Hashem sees and records everything ; \"writing\" is an image for our sake. Every act leaves an impression on the soul , invisible to us like the expert's distinctions in money changing, medicine or jewels, but clear to the soul's Creator. The Ramchal says the same. Olam HaBa for Saadia is a world to be created after this one is complete; for the Rambam it already exists. Ten categories of people, not five pairs, plus the beinoni , who is counted with the righteous. The majority is repaid in the next world, the minority in this one , because one's level in the next world is fixed forever (against the Kabbalistic idea of ascent). The Rabbi's reading: \"majority\" means identity . The rasha's mitzvot belong to this world because he does; a sincere, complete mitzvah could never be repaid here. Coming Up Next week: finishing Chapter 2, where Saadia discusses regret for mitzvot and for aveirot and continues the theodicy, and Chapter 3, which is devoted to why the righteous suffer in this world.","8yy9wnKwAbI":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 4 (Free Will), Chapter 6, completing the essay Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 16 December 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: The Fourth Essay So Far This was the last shiur of the autumn term, held at a different time from usual, and it completed the fourth essay. (The Rabbi's Thursday night shiurim on the laws of Chanukah in the Shulchan Aruch would be finished the following Monday at the usual time, so that the community could come to Chanukah having learnt them. Essay 5 would begin in January.) The Rabbi reviewed the essay: Chapters 1 and 2: for Saadia, the earth is the pinnacle of creation and humans are the pinnacle of the earth, so the entire universe was created for humanity. The Ibn Ezra and the Rambam disagreed. Chapter 3: Saadia's very strict understanding of bechirah . Choice comes before the act, not simultaneously with it. It includes abstaining from an action. And it covers acting inadvertently , as distinct from acting with no knowledge at all, which is not an action from the Torah's point of view. Chapter 4: proofs that we have free will from the senses , from reason , from Scripture and from tradition (the Gemara), and the famous question of how Hashem's full foreknowledge fits with our free will. Chapter 5: seven questions on free will: why command the righteous; why send prophets to the wicked; who is responsible when someone carries out what was decreed; David and Avshalom ; Sancheriv and Nevuchadnezzar ; and why humans sometimes have to lie. The plan for Chapter 6 In the final chapter Saadia gives eight categories of verses in Tanach that seem to contradict bechirah . Both at the beginning and at the end he tells us: I am giving you the keys ( petachim ). There are many such verses; whenever you meet one that seems to show Hashem taking away free will, apply one of these keys. His opening principle: most of these apparent contradictions come from not using language broadly . \"If you're not willing to be liberal with the use of language, you're going to be very limited in your understanding of language.\" You have to be willing to understand things in a broader sense than the exact words. Category 1: A Warning, Not a Prevention There is a big difference between Hashem warning you not to do something and Hashem preventing you. When Avimelech took Sarah (read in shul a few weeks earlier), Hashem told him in a dream at night: ואחשך גם אנכי אותך מחטוא לי , \"I also withheld you from sinning against Me.\" People think Hashem intervened and stopped Avimelech from exercising his free will. Saadia: that is the wrong way to understand the story. It means I withheld you by warning you : I told you not to do this, and that you would be punished if you did. The same use of \"cannot\" appears in the Torah: לא יוכל בעלה הראשון אשר שלחה לשוב לקחתה : a man may remarry his divorcee (indeed there is a mitzvah to do so), but if she married someone else in between, \"her first husband cannot take her back.\" If he wanted to, he could. \"Cannot\" means he has been commanded not to. לא תוכל לזבוח את הפסח : \"you cannot slaughter the Pesach\" outside Yerushalayim. \"Of course I'm able to do so if I want to.\" So when Hashem told Avimelech \"I withheld you,\" it means I warned you, and you decided not to, \"which was the right thing. But it was completely your choice.\" As the Rabbi said last time, Rashi , the Radak and the vast majority of the Rishonim read the story simply: Hashem struck Avimelech with an illness at night that physically prevented him. Those Rishonim must say that there are extreme instances when Hashem intervenes to prevent a person being violated, even though normally there is free will. \"But as we have seen over and over through this essay, Saadia takes a very sharp line... Free will is complete. You can do whatever you want.\" So Saadia prefers to read the words broadly. Category 2: Hashem Takes Away Physical Benefits The second category: Hashem takes away something physical from a person, and people mistake it for Hashem taking away his free will. The verse השמן לב העם הזה ואזניו הכבד ועיניו השע (Yeshayahu 6:10) The heh can be read two ways: as an instruction : \"Make the heart of this people fat, make their ears heavy, and shut their eyes,\" lest they repent; or as a description of the past : \"The heart of this people has grown fat, their ears heavy.\" Three readings How the verse reads Is free will a problem? Rashi A description of the past. The people hardened their own hearts and closed their ears, because they knew that if they listened they would have to do teshuvah, and they did not want to No. It is a historical fact about their own choice The Rambam Hashem says He will harden their hearts. Not that He makes them sin, but that He takes away the gift of teshuvah from people who have sunk so far into darkness that they are no longer fit for it Hashem does sometimes withhold teshuvah, as with Pharaoh Saadia Hashem will bring upon them physical, mundane troubles , loss of money, war, \"whatever it may be,\" that preoccupy and confuse them , so that they cannot pay attention to mending their ways, \"confused like a blind man at noon\" No. Teshuvah becomes harder, but \"if you really focus and push yourself, you can\" Rashi's reading, the Rabbi illustrated: \"Sometimes a person knows that if he goes to a certain shiur, a certain Shabbaton, he's going to do teshuvah. But he doesn't want to. So he says, okay, I'm not going to go. He hardens his heart.\" The Rambam, the Rabbi noted, quotes this verse as one of the key sources for a principle the class has discussed many times, on Mondays and on Tuesdays, and which they saw when they learnt Shemonah Perakim : a person or a nation can descend so far that Hashem takes away the ability to do teshuvah , so that they receive the consequences of their actions. Saadia does not accept that. \"A person has strict free will, complete free will. So it can't be that the pasuk is saying, I'm going to take away your ability to do teshuvah.\" What it means is: I am going to confuse them and take away their tranquillity of mind. \"Of course, if you choose and you persist and you overcome these challenges, of course you can do teshuvah.\" Category 3: Strengthening the Heart (Pharaoh and Sichon) \"The third one is really quite astonishing, and he is unique. I think he's the only one who offers this interpretation.\" The famous question ויחזק ה' את לב פרעה : Hashem strengthened Pharaoh's heart. Saadia himself concedes that the verse says \"the heart of Pharaoh.\" It seems Hashem strengthened his resolve and prevented him from doing the right thing. How can that be? Saadia's answer It does not mean that Hashem hardened his resolve not to release the people. \"Nothing to do with that, because a person has full free will.\" It means that Hashem gave Pharaoh the life and the physical strength to withstand the plagues . There was hail, there was pestilence; he could have died in any of them. Hashem kept him alive, because He wanted the whole drama to continue to the Red Sea, as the verse says, to show Pharaoh His power: בעבור הראתך את כחי , \"for this I have kept you standing, to show you My strength.\" The same applies to Sichon : כי הקשה ה' אלהיך את רוחו ואמץ את לבבו . What plague threatened Sichon? Saadia: when he heard that Bnei Yisrael were coming he might have had a heart attack , lost his physical capacity, and been unable to make a conscious choice. Hashem strengthened his heart so that he would not die of fear. Then, with free choice, he chose not to let the Jewish people pass through his land. Saadia brings verses showing that people sometimes die of fear, as when hearts \"melted.\" Richard asked: why then does the verse speak of strengthening his heart ? \"Good question.\" Saadia himself addresses it. Lev here must be understood broadly, as the nefesh , the life force: when something terrible happens, Hashem gives you the ability to get through it. \"If we're honest, it's not what the pasuk says really... Be broad, be liberal with the words.\" This is why, the Rabbi said, Saadia's approach is rarely mentioned when people discuss Pharaoh's free will. \"Difficult, I know. It is very difficult.\" The other approaches The Rabbi set out the classical alternatives: The Rambam (consistent with the principle above): Pharaoh chose wrongly by his own free will in the first five plagues. In the last five, Hashem took away his ability to do teshuvah, so that he would suffer the consequences of his choices. The Ramban on Shemot chapter 7 asks the question everyone asks: if Hashem hardened his heart, what was his sin? He gives two explanations, both true : Pharaoh's wickedness, the great evils he had done to Israel without cause, meant that justice required the ways of repentance be withheld from him (the Midrash's approach, like the Rambam). In the later plagues, Pharaoh would have released them only because he could not bear the plagues , not to do the will of his Creator. So Hashem strengthened his heart. The Rabbi explained the Ramban's second answer: the hand of God was so apparent that releasing the people at plague six would not have been a free choice. \"It's like Hashem putting a gun to your head.\" Even Pharaoh's servants mocked him for holding out. So Hashem strengthened his heart \" in order to give him a level playing field of free will .\" It sounds paradoxical, but \"that makes perfect sense\": before, it was not fifty-fifty; Hashem strengthens his heart to make it fifty-fifty. What \"Hashem strengthened Pharaoh's heart\" means The Rambam Hashem withheld teshuvah after Pharaoh's own wicked choices The Ramban Both: repentance withheld, and Hashem restored a level playing field so his choice would be free Saadia Hashem kept him physically alive and strong through the plagues; his choices remained entirely his own Saadia cannot accept either explanation, because both limit free will to some extent. \"We're seeing how strong Saadia is.\" Category 4: Declaring a Status Some verses seem to say Hashem does something to a person, when He is really declaring what the person is . Saadia's analogy is the judge. We say \"the judge vindicated Reuven and condemned Shimon\" (as the Torah says of judges, והצדיקו את הצדיק והרשיעו את הרשע ). We do not mean he caused Reuven to do good and Shimon to do bad. He declared their status . A judge who \"falsifies\" a document has not forged it; he has ruled it a forgery. A money changer who \"made this coin good and that coin bad\" has not minted them; he has assessed them. With this in mind you understand: אם ללצים הוא יליץ ולענוים יתן חן : \"He scorns the scorners and gives grace to the humble.\" Not that Hashem makes scoffers scoff and makes the humble gracious. He declares the mockers to be leitzim and the humble to be anavim . \"He's just stating who you are.\" אני ה' פתיתי את הנביא ההוא : \"I, Hashem, have enticed that prophet.\" Not that Hashem seduced him into false prophecy. He declared him a false prophet, one who had chosen to be one. אכן השא השאת לעם הזה ולירושלם לאמר שלום יהיה לכם : \"You have surely deceived this people, saying you will have peace.\" Not that Hashem made the false prophets deceive them. When destruction came, Hashem made it clear that they were false prophets; He passed judgment on them. למה תתענו ה' מדרכיך : \"Why, Hashem, do You make us stray from Your ways?\" It means: do not give us the status of strayers. Have mercy, forgive us, \"and let us be good people in Your eyes again.\" Category 5: Asking Hashem to Turn Our Hearts Verses like \"bring me back to You\" and \"turn my heart to Your commandments\" sound as though we are asking Hashem to force us to be good. \"How can you ask Hashem to make you be good? You have to choose to be good.\" Saadia: these are requests for atonement . If You forgive me and give me some credit, You will encourage me to do the right thing and do teshuvah. If You do not forgive me, I will give up hope and sin. Hashem turns my heart indirectly : \"You're going to turn my heart to You by forgiving me.\" The turning is still my choice. Category 6: How We Were Created \"It's very straightforward this, and everyone agrees.\" Some verses describe how Hashem made us, and people mistake them for His ongoing control. ומה' מענה לשון (Mishlei 16:1), \"from Hashem is the answer of the tongue.\" People think Hashem puts the words in my mouth. No: Hashem gave me a tongue, teeth, a mouth and a heart to think with. \"Now I have to use that tongue and that mouth and that heart to make my own choices.\" The same applies to how we use our eyes and our hearts. Category 7: \"The Heart of a King\" as an Exaggeration \"Seven is, again, very difficult to accept.\" פלגי מים לב מלך ביד ה' על כל אשר יחפץ יטנו (Mishlei 21:1) Like channels of water is the heart of a king in the hand of Hashem; He directs it wherever He wishes. The simple reading, given by the Ibn Ezra and the majority: just as one channels water, Hashem directs the heart of a king wherever He wants. Kings receive special direction from Hashem. Saadia reads it the other way round: just as a person can channel water wherever he likes, so the king can channel his own heart and decisions wherever he wants . Why mention a king? It is an exaggeration to bring out how strong free will is: even the king, with all his power, chooses freely, and certainly the rest of us do. He gives the same interpretation in his commentary on Mishlei. \"The problem is, what does he do with the words, in the hand of Hashem ? I don't know the answer to that. I don't understand.\" The Ibn Ezra does not accept Saadia's reading. On the simple reading, the answer to the free will question has to be what the class discussed a couple of weeks earlier: in great matters affecting huge numbers of people, as kings do, Hashem sometimes intervenes \"for the greater sake of humanity.\" Saadia cannot accept even that, \"so he turns it on its head.\" Category 8: Hashem Acts, and the Person Then Chooses When Hashem does something new and the person then chooses to act, the verse may attribute the action to Hashem. There are three aspects. 8a: Salvation or strength Hashem saves someone or strengthens him, and he then does something good or bad. The verse says Hashem did it, because He did it indirectly , by keeping the person alive and able. ויער אלהי ישראל את רוח פול מלך אשור : Hashem \"stirred the spirit\" of Pul, king of Ashur, who exiled the two and a half tribes. It does not mean Pul had no free choice. Hashem gave him strength and kept him alive, and Pul decided to exile them. העיר ה' את רוח כורש : Hashem \"stirred the spirit\" of Koresh, king of Persia, to build the Temple. Hashem gave him the financial ability, the encouragement and the strength; Koresh chose to build it. 8b: Clarity of mind Prayers such as \"show me Your way,\" \"guide me in Your laws,\" \"turn my eyes from seeing falsehood,\" and \"the hand of God was on Yehudah to give them one heart\" sound like requests to be compelled. \"What kind of prayer is that?\" They mean: Hashem, give me clarity of mind , serenity, tranquillity and understanding, a state of mindfulness in which I am able to serve You and keep Your mitzvot. I still choose the path. 8c: A miracle that inspires Hashem does a miracle; people are awakened by it and choose to do right. It looks as if Hashem compelled them. He did not; they were encouraged. Eliyahu on Har HaKarmel, at the contest with the prophets of Baal (Melachim I 18:37): ענני ה' ענני וידעו העם הזה כי אתה ה' האלהים ואתה הסבת את לבם אחרנית On the usual reading, \"You have turned their hearts back,\" Hashem is turning their hearts, which sounds like compulsion. \"Again a very unique interpretation\": Saadia adds the letter heh before achoranit , reading it as an adjective: their backward hearts . Their hearts are now turned away from You; by answering my sacrifice in place of the Baal sacrifice, You will turn their backward hearts. The miracle encourages them, they choose, and it is described as if Hashem turned them. \"Saadia is willing to add in a letter here to take away the problem of free will.\" Similarly ואת רוחי אתן בקרבכם ועשיתי את אשר בחקי תלכו , \"I will put My spirit within you and cause you to walk in My statutes\": not that Hashem will force us, but that by showing us wonders He will make us want to choose the right thing. A Ninth Key: The Order of the Verses Saadia ends with \"one more thing\": sometimes the order of the verses misleads us, and a verse must be read as continuing an earlier one. Tehillim 34 , said every Shabbat: עיני ה' אל צדיקים ואזניו אל שועתם. פני ה' בעשי רע להכרית מארץ זכרם. צעקו וה' שמע ומכל צרותם הצילם. Having said that Hashem's face is set against the wicked to cut them off, the next verse says \"they cried out and Hashem heard and saved them from all their troubles.\" The \"they\" refers back two verses, to the righteous. Many Rishonim read it this way, and so does Saadia. The case that matters for free will is David's psalm after Batsheva (Tehillim 51): לך לבדך חטאתי והרע בעיניך עשיתי, למען תצדק בדברך תזכה בשפטך Read straight on, it sounds as if David sinned in order that Hashem's decree would be proved right, as though David were compelled. No: \"so that You will be justified in Your words\" goes back to the earlier request, הרבה כבסני מעוני , \"wash me thoroughly from my iniquity,\" whiten me like snow. Forgive me, so that You will be proved right in Your word that whoever does teshuvah is received back. \"So this is really a ninth category,\" the Rabbi said, \"he calls them eight, but really it's nine keys.\" Saadia concludes: with these categories I have removed all your questions. We have no claim that Hashem forces us to do good or bad. \"Are we wiser than the Almighty? Certainly not.\" Summary: Saadia's Keys to Verses That Seem to Deny Free Will # Key Example 1 A warning , described as a prevention \"I withheld you from sinning\" (Avimelech) 2 Hashem takes away physical benefits; repentance is harder but still possible \"Make the heart of this people fat\" 3 Hashem gives physical strength to survive so a person can go on choosing \"Hashem strengthened Pharaoh's heart\"; Sichon 4 Hashem declares a status \"He scorns the scorners\"; \"I enticed that prophet\" 5 We ask for forgiveness , which encourages us to return \"Turn my heart to Your commandments\" 6 A description of how we were created \"From Hashem is the answer of the tongue\" 7 An exaggeration : even the king chooses freely \"The heart of a king is like channels of water\" 8a Hashem gives salvation or strength , then the person acts Pul; Koresh 8b Hashem gives clarity of mind \"Show me Your way\" 8c Hashem does a miracle that inspires a choice Eliyahu: \"their backward hearts\" 9 Read the verse as continuing an earlier verse Tehillim 34; Tehillim 51 \"What comes out from this, as I hope you've recognised and appreciate now, is how strict Saadia is with free will. He's willing to take pesukim and really reinterpret them from the standard way, at least what we call the standard way, to uphold his really firm understanding of complete free will.\" The Rabbi admitted he had rushed the last part: \"We could have spent much longer on this.\" Where the Book Goes Next With six chapters, the fourth essay is complete. The Rabbi placed it in the book's journey: Essay 1: the universe and its creation. Essay 2: Hashem and His unity. Essay 3: revelation, prophecy, the Torah and the mitzvot in the broad sense. Essay 4: free will. Essay 5 (from January): the merits and demerits , going into the details of the mitzvot and aveirot of the Torah.","8Emvykjz7to":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 4 (Free Will), Chapter 5, questions 3 to 7, and the opening of Chapter 6 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 2 December 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are in the Fourth Essay The fourth essay of Emunot VeDeot is Rav Saadia Gaon's treatment of human free will. The Rabbi reviewed the ground covered so far: Chapters 1 and 2: Saadia builds his picture of the world with man at the centre , and tries to prove that humans are the purpose of the creation of the universe. As the class saw at the time, that argument \"wasn't so straightforward.\" Chapter 3: Saadia's own view of free will. A human has complete and absolute free will . Free will comes before the action , not at the same moment as it, and it also covers abstaining from something. Chapter 4: Saadia's four kinds of proof that humans have free will: from the senses , from logic , from Scripture , and from the Talmud and tradition . That chapter also took up the age-old question of how human free will fits with Hashem's full knowledge. Chapter 5 (begun last week): seven questions about free will. Two were done last week: Why command the righteous, if Hashem knows they will do the right thing anyway? Because humans need to know what the right thing is. Why send prophets and messengers to the wicked, if Hashem knows they will sin? Saadia gave six or seven answers, but the main one is that they will have no excuse to say they did not know. \"We'll see Saadia really holds with this strongly,\" the Rabbi said of absolute free will. \"We'll see this in a few examples this evening.\" Questions 3 and 4: The Decreed Death and the Decreed Loss Saadia's text The third question: suppose it has been decreed that a person is going to die, whether as a punishment or as some kind of trial. Someone else then decides, with his own free will, to kill him. To whom do we attribute the action? Saadia answers: The fact that the victim died was the decree of God. The fact that he was killed was the work of the killer, and he will be held accountable for it. \"He would have died naturally had it not been for the perpetrator.\" The fourth question is the same case with money. If it was decreed that a person will lose money, and someone steals it from him, then the loss was the decree of God, but the fact that it happened through theft was the thief's action, and he will be held accountable. Had it not been stolen, it would have been lost in some other way, perhaps through a fine. Saadia supports this with the answer that Shemaya and his brother gave to the king of Edom who was about to execute them: we have been decreed to die whether you kill us or not, perhaps by a natural death, but you will still be held accountable for deciding to kill us. The Rabbi's question to the class Before explaining the two ways of reading this, the Rabbi put a question to the class: \"If it's been decided and decreed upon a person on Rosh Hashanah, you will be poor... I see this person, he's a friend of mine, I have huge compassion for him, and I decide I'm going to completely look after him. Is it possible for me, a third party, to come in and change the decree of HaKadosh Baruch Hu?\" He made clear that this is not about teshuvah. Of course תשובה ותפילה וצדקה מעבירין את רוע הגזרה : repentance, prayer and charity remove the evil of the decree. The question is about someone who has not changed at all: \"I'm the same person I was six months ago when this decree was passed on me. Can someone else now come and change that?\" One participant suggested that the decree itself might be open-ended. It is decreed that a person will be poor, but part of the decree may be that the poverty can be alleviated by others, just as some illnesses are decreed with the possibility of healing and some without. The Rabbi called this \"a middle view between the two views\" and said it could be \"the beginning of trying to bridge\" the two readings. Flora felt that for Saadia this would not work, since whatever happens, pleasant or harsh, was the decree. She found the deterministic picture difficult, and raised Chizkiyahu , who was told he would die and was given another fifteen years. Reading 1: everything was going to happen anyway The first and simpler reading of Saadia is that everything that happens to a person has already been determined , and all the merit or punishment we receive is for being the one who brings it about . On this reading, if I bring a poor man off the street and look after him, it was always decreed on Rosh Hashanah that by a certain date he would end up in a home with plenty of food. I am rewarded for choosing to be the one who did it. If I kill someone at 6:11 on the second of December, it was already decreed that he would die at 6:11 on the second of December. I am punished because I was the conduit, and because he could have died peacefully at home rather than by a knife. The consequence: \"our free will doesn't really have the effect to change anything in the world,\" because everything is going to happen for the good or for the bad. \"Really we are just robots doing things. Yes, we're choosing to do them, that's true. But really our choices are quite not really meaningful, because they would have happened anyway.\" Rabbeinu Bachya, Chovot HaLevavot , says something similar, and even more strongly. He teaches that no one can hurt a person without Hashem's agreement. If you depend on many masters, you must keep pleasing all of them. If there is only one Master who can do good or bad to you, you will trust only in Him. Once a person recognises that nothing can happen to him without Hashem's will , he stops worrying and stops hoping that people will be good to him or fearing they will be bad to him. Reading 2: the Or HaChaim and the brothers of Yosef The Rabbi then brought what he called \"a fascinating\" reading from the Or HaChaim , Rabbi Chaim ben Attar of Morocco (late 1600s to 1700s), on the story of Yosef and his brothers, which was about to be read in shul in a couple of weeks. The brothers say: let us kill him, ונראה מה יהיו חלמתיו , \"and we will see what becomes of his dreams.\" Reuven says, let us not take his life; throw him into the pit instead. The simple reading is that Reuven simply did not want them to do it with their own hands. The Or HaChaim reads it as a theological debate about free will : The brothers' reasoning. Everything happens by the will of Hashem. If Yosef's dreams are true, we must respect him. If they are false, he is a false dreamer, a kind of false prophet, and deserves death. So here is a simple litmus test: try to kill him. If we succeed, his dreams were never true, because it could only happen if Hashem willed it. If we fail, his dreams are true. Reuven's answer. You have misunderstood. Even if it has been decreed that a person will live, a human being, through his free will, has the ability to go against it. Yosef might be meant to live and his dreams might be true, and you could still kill him. Your killing him would prove nothing. Reuven's alternative test. Put him in the pit. The snakes and scorpions there do not mean certain death; the Rishonim write that he could have survived. A human has free will and can kill someone decreed to live, but an animal, a snake or a scorpion will only attack a person if it has been decreed upon him. So if he survives the pit, his dreams are true; if the creatures kill him, it was decreed. That is the meaning of ויצלהו מידם , \"he saved him from their hands.\" Reuven saved the brothers from their mistaken view of free will. \"And Reuven wins the day.\" The Rabbi added that the Zohar on that parasha says essentially the same, though the Or HaChaim does not cite it: a human can do anything. If someone is decreed to be poor, I have the ability as a human to improve the world and take him off the streets, \"and I'll be going against Hashem as well, and that's fine. Hashem has given us free will to be able to use it for the good and for the bad.\" What is at stake Reading 1 (simple reading of Saadia; Chovot HaLevavot) Reading 2 (Or HaChaim; Zohar) Can my choice change what happens to another person? No. It would have happened anyway Yes. A human can override what was decreed What am I rewarded or punished for? Being the conduit, and the harsh or kind way it happened The outcome itself How meaningful are our choices? Limited \"Far more meaningful\" What must I worry about? Only my standing with Hashem Also other people's free will The second reading has a cost: \"We can't just hope and pray that Hashem looks after us. We also have to hope and pray that we're not affected by other people's free will.\" When we pray on Rosh Hashanah for life, we pray to live naturally, but we still have to take precautions against other people. Even so, Rabbi Tatz argues that there is a concept of extra shielding: someone with real merit can sometimes be protected from these things. Rabbi Tatz's reading of Saadia Rabbi Akiva Tatz , \"well known in London,\" has a book called Freedom, Will and Destiny , which the Rabbi \"highly recommended,\" especially its chapter in the middle on whether one person can affect another. Rabbi Tatz goes through all the views and notes that Saadia is the first to seem to say that we only do what was going to happen anyway. But he wants to read Saadia like the Or HaChaim : Saadia is only addressing the very specific case in which a death was already decreed, and asking how the action is attributed. The Rabbi was not convinced. A decree of death is for a specific date and time. If Saadia held like the Or HaChaim, his question would only arise in the \"extremely remote\" case where I happen to decide to kill someone at exactly the moment he was decreed to die naturally. \"Why would Saadia be asking such a remote and theoretical question?\" So the Rabbi believes the simple reading is correct: Saadia holds that when a person is killed, it was decreed that he die at that time, and the killer is punished for his not dying naturally. \"I personally prefer the second view, because it means life becomes much more meaningful. But I think we have to admit the simple reading of Saadia makes our choices in life quite meaningless, in that we are just doing what would have happened in the world anyway... more accented, for the good and for the bad.\" A question from the class If I was only the instrument of what God wanted, why should I be punished? The Rabbi: according to Saadia, you chose to be the instrument. Had you chosen good, the person would have died in his bed, or slipped and died. He died through your killing him, and you are held accountable for that. When the participant pressed that the decree might have been \"to be killed,\" the Rabbi answered that even then it could have been by anyone, even by a bear. The fact that this particular human chose to do it is what he is responsible for. \"Where was man?\" The Rabbi closed this section with Rabbi Lord Jonathan Sacks , zichrono livracha. When asked where God was during the Holocaust, Rabbi Sacks would answer: where was man during the Holocaust? That answer, the Rabbi said, \"is only intelligible according to the second reading.\" It may well be that God decreed for all those people to live, and humans chose evil. It is a fundamental principle, as we learned in the Rambam, that humans have free will and Hashem does not intervene with it. On the simple reading of Saadia, \"and definitely according to Rabbeinu Bachya,\" Rabbi Sacks' answer has no ground to stand on. On the second reading it is a beautiful answer: the Jews were meant to live, and the Nazis went against the decree of HaKadosh Baruch Hu and are culpable for it. Question 5: David, Batsheva and Avshalom After David's sin with Batsheva (\"we don't need to go into the details of what exactly the sin was; there's a whole discussion on that\"), the prophet Natan told him: הנני מקים עליך רעה מביתך , \"I will raise up evil against you from your own house.\" Someone from his household would take his wives: \"you did it in secret, and I will do this before all Israel.\" This was Avshalom , who publicly took David's ten concubines. So the question: if this was decreed as David's punishment, what happened to Avshalom's free will? Saadia's answer Hashem, through Natan, was telling David what Avshalom was going to choose . Avshalom had complete free will to do it or not; Hashem simply knew he would. This follows Saadia's answer in chapter 4 to the question of free will and divine knowledge: Hashem knows the end that the human will choose. But then, if Avshalom was going to do it anyway, how is this a punishment for David? Saadia adds: the punishment was to pain David's heart with the knowledge. Living with the knowledge that your own child is going to behave this way, until it happens, \"is a terrible punishment.\" The Rabbi pointed out that, again, on Saadia's view it would have happened anyway , regardless of what David did with Batsheva. The Radak's in-between view The Radak , Rabbi David Kimchi, on this verse, asks the same question and answers differently. Hashem knew Avshalom was a lustful person who was going to sin with some woman in any case. Had David not sinned, Hashem would have made sure it was not with David's wives, which is far worse. Because of David's sin, Avshalom's choice was directed to David's household. The decree was that something bad would come from his house; Avshalom chose to sin; the decree determined with whom . \"It's a kind of in-between.\" The Radak quotes Saadia at the end and does not seem to disagree with him; it is another interpretation. Question 6: \"Ashur, the Rod of My Anger\" The prophets describe the wicked kings who devastated Israel as the instruments of God : הוי אשור שבט אפי , \"Woe, Ashur, the rod of My anger,\" and of the king of Babylon, ונתתי את חרבי בידו , \"I will put My sword in his hand.\" It sounds as if Hashem is telling them to do these terrible things. Saadia's answer has two parts: Hashem gives them the ability , by not holding them back. Hashem gives them the courage. \"I'm not telling you to do it, but I'm giving you the possibility and the potential to be able to do it. And in that sense, if you go and do it, you are My staff.\" It is entirely their choice, and Hashem knows they will do it. That is why the same prophet says that Hashem will punish the arrogance of the king of Ashur. In that sense Sancheriv and Nevuchadnezzar are Hashem's staff and stick. Question 7: Does Hashem Ever Make the Righteous Lie? If everything that happens is Hashem's decree, and righteous people sometimes need to lie for good reasons, as we were reading in the recent parashiyot about Avraham and Yitzchak, then it seems Hashem is causing the righteous to lie. Saadia's answer You never really have to lie. \"There's always a way out.\" People who lie, and then say \"I had no choice,\" are blaming God. Use language that can be understood more than one way. Even when you have licence to say something untrue, for example when your life or your wife is in danger, you can speak metaphorically. If the other person takes you literally and misunderstands, \"that's his problem, not yours.\" Avraham and Sarah. Avraham said אחותי היא , \"she is my sister.\" He meant my relative: she was his niece, the daughter of his brother Haran. The Torah uses \"brother\" in this wider sense elsewhere: Avraham says to Lot, his nephew, כי אנשים אחים אנחנו , \"for we are brothers.\" The people of the place took it literally and wanted to sin with her, and the sin was theirs. Saadia adds that it is common courtesy to ask a visitor about his work and his needs, not to ask how a woman travelling with him is related to him; Avraham sensed that something was wrong in that place. Yaakov and Yitzchak. The Rabbi added an example \"which springs to mind\" from the previous week's parasha. Yaakov said אנכי עשו בכרך . Rashi reads it as two statements: \"I am\" the one bringing you food, and \"Esav is your firstborn.\" What looks like a lie is not. Saadia ends the chapter by saying he has given seven questions and seven answers. When you meet similar questions, \"you have to use your common sense.\" He cannot address every single problem. The Rabbi's difficulty, and a simpler answer \"I don't fully understand this.\" The Talmud says in many places, in many contexts, that a person is allowed to say something untrue: for humility, for modesty, to avoid causing harm to others, for the sake of peace, and when life is in danger. \"To say that really a person never has to lie, I think is quite difficult.\" The Rabbi preferred a much simpler answer, the one given by Rav Dessler , which he said he was used to: truth and falsehood are not about factual accuracy, but about what is right . In the Holocaust, a non-Jew is hiding a Jew in his house and a Nazi asks whether there is a Jew inside. He answers no. Saadia would have him craft an ambiguous sentence, but \"you'll be caught out.\" The simple point is that here you have licence to lie; \"it's the mitzvah to lie here, actually. That's actually emet.\" Emet and sheker ask whether this is the right thing, whether it is nachon . In that sense there is no question at all, because saying the right thing is saying the truth, and it is no sin. Summary of Chapter 5 Question Saadia's answer 1. Why command the righteous? Humans need to know what is right 2. Why send prophets to the wicked? So they have no excuse 3 and 4. A decreed death or loss carried out by someone else The outcome was God's decree; the harsh way it happened is the perpetrator's, and he is accountable 5. Avshalom and David's wives Hashem foretold what Avshalom would freely choose; David's punishment was knowing in advance 6. Ashur and Babylon as God's rod and sword Hashem gave them the ability and courage, but the choice was theirs 7. Does Hashem make the righteous lie? One can always avoid an outright lie, using language open to interpretation Chapter 6: Verses That Seem to Deny Free Will The class had been extended to finish the essay, since there would be no shiur the following week and probably not the week after. The Rabbi began the sixth and final chapter. Saadia: \"I will complete my essay by bringing the places in Tanach where there are doubts about free will.\" Almost all of them, he says, come from not recognising that language is fluid . The Rabbi recalled the earlier discussion of anthropomorphisms in the Torah (Essay 2), where Saadia was the first, at least the first recorded, to be unequivocal that Hashem has no body and that all the bodily descriptions are metaphors. There he wrote a small essay on how language is used: if we always take language strictly, our ability to express ourselves is very limited, so language must be allowed to stretch. The same applies here. Saadia gives eight categories of verses, with at least one example of each, and any other verse can be put under one of them. Category 1: a warning is not a compulsion Some verses are Hashem warning us, not forcing us . The example is from Parashat Vayera. Avraham and Sarah come to Gerar, Avimelech takes Sarah, and Hashem comes to him at night. Avimelech protests that he did not know she was a married woman. Hashem says: ואחשך גם אנכי אותך מחטוא לי , \"I also withheld you from sinning against Me.\" Saadia: people think Hashem physically stopped him. Wrong. It means I commanded you not to . Compare the Torah's language elsewhere: A man who divorced his wife, after she married someone else: לא יוכל בעלה הראשון... לשוב לקחתה , \"her first husband cannot take her back.\" Of course he physically can. It means he is commanded not to. לא תוכל לזבוח את הפסח outside the chosen place: \"you cannot sacrifice the Pesach.\" Of course I can take my lamb and slaughter it outside Jerusalem. It means I have been commanded not to. So \"I withheld you\" means I warned you and commanded you not to sin with a married woman. The Rabbi offered \"special bonus marks\" for another example Saadia does not bring, and gave one from Sefer Devarim: a man with two wives, one more loved than the other, whose firstborn is from the less loved wife. לא יוכל לבכר את בן האהובה , \"he cannot give the firstborn's double portion to the son of the beloved.\" Of course he can. He is \"not able\" in relation to Hashem's will, because he has been commanded not to. A very original reading \"This is, I have to tell you, a very original reading. What he says about the language is true, but his reading of the story of Avimelech and Sarah is very original.\" The Rishonim did not read it this way: Commentator \"I withheld you from sinning\" means Rashi Hashem took away his physical strength Radak Hashem took away his desire to sin Saadia Hashem commanded him; Avimelech could have sinned and chose not to The simple reading, the Rabbi said, is that Hashem does sometimes intervene and suspend free will in unique circumstances: \"how is Hashem going to allow Avraham's wife Sarah to be defiled?\" Hashem momentarily paused Avimelech's free will. \"Saadia doesn't like that, because for Saadia free will is comprehensive.\" Theoretically, Avimelech could have done it; he did not, because he recognised how terrible the sin was. \"Saadia has a few very novel approaches to the pesukim in Tanach, which we're very surprised when we see them.\" Coming Up Rather than rush through the rest of the chapter, the Rabbi asked the class and decided \"it's better we do things properly.\" The remaining seven categories of Chapter 6, with more of Saadia's novel readings of Tanach on free will, will be done in two or three weeks, after which the class begins Essay 5. There will be no shiur next week; the group will be told about the week after. \"Chodesh tov, everyone.\"","7qGVr_G69mg":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 4 (Obedience and Disobedience, Compulsion and Justice), the end of Chapter 4 (with the other Rishonim on foreknowledge) and Chapter 5, questions 1 to 4 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 25 November 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Essay 4 So Far Chapters 1-2: man is the pinnacle of creation , and for Saadia the whole universe was created for him. Some Rishonim disagreed, but everyone agrees that bechirah , free choice, was given to the human. Chapter 3: free will covers abstaining as well as acting; it comes before the action, not simultaneously or, \"even more absurdly,\" after it; and only a conscious act counts. Leaning back and switching on a light is not even shogeg ; there is no sacrifice for it, because it is not called an action. \"That underscores Saadia's principle of bechirah .\" Chapter 4: proofs of free will from sense, logic, Scripture and tradition (\"choose life\"; \"everything is in the hands of Heaven except the fear of Heaven\"). Then two questions: why Hashem allows evil (it is not bad for Him ; it is our arena of choice), and the famous question, which Saadia is the first on record to ask : how can Hashem know everything, and we still be free? Tonight the Rabbi first completed that topic with the other Rishonim, then began Chapter 5. Part 1: Approach 1, Saadia: He Knows the Choice Both are true. We have complete free will, and Hashem knows the future. What He knows is what we will choose : after all our deliberations, He knew from the beginning where we would end up. He watches from the side. Our freedom is intact and so is His perfect knowledge. The difficulties As discussed last week: We become actors. \"When we're really confronted with a choice, going forwards and backwards, should I, shouldn't I, we know deep down at the end of the day someone already knows what I'm going to choose. That's quite defeating.\" Why live it out? The purpose of this world is to go through the battle of choice and incorporate goodness within us . If Hashem knows every choice, He could simulate our lives. \"Okay, we can get around that somewhat\": we have to actually do and experience choosing the right thing, not just have it simulated. \"Fine, that's somewhat understandable. But it's hard to accept that this is the only way to reconcile the contradiction.\" Part 2: Approach 2, Rabbeinu Bachya and the Rambam: Both True, Beyond Us Rabbeinu Bachya: accept both Chovot HaLevavot was one of the next to discuss it, \"and he really gave up.\" We don't know exactly how it works, but we know God is just and trustworthy , and He told us about reward and punishment. The best approach is to accept that we have full choice and will be rewarded or punished, and that Hashem knows all. Our understanding is limited. If there were an explanation humans could understand, God would have revealed it to us. \"Accept it. Move on.\" \"Rabbeinu Bachya was a philosopher. But he recognises that sometimes it's better to have a good question unresolved than a weak answer to the question.\" The Rambam: the answer lies in Hashem's knowledge \"Maybe Rabbeinu Bachya meant what the Rambam was going to write. The Rambam says something very similar, but much deeper, and people who didn't understand him thought he was saying something very simple.\" (Those at the Tuesday night shiur on Hilchot Teshuvah had seen it.) Both are true: humans must have free will, and Hashem must know the future, or His knowledge would be imperfect. The question assumes we understand Hashem's knowledge. But we cannot understand His essence, as we saw in Saadia's Essay 1, and His attributes are part of His essence . So we cannot understand His yedi'ah and chochmah either. We ask from a human perspective. Humans live in past, present and future , so we see a contradiction. Hashem does not live in time. If we could understand His knowledge, we would see how the two are reconciled. With our human minds, in time, we cannot. The Mishnah says it explicitly: הכל צפוי והרשות נתונה Everything is foreseen, and permission is given. (Rabbi Akiva, Avot 3:15) The commentators say this is exactly the Rambam's point: both are true, and how they fit is beyond the human mind. The Ra'avad misread him The Ra'avad , the Rambam's critic, thought the Rambam was simply raising the question and saying he couldn't answer it, and objected: if you can't answer a question, don't raise it . His own answer is very like Saadia's, and at the end he admits he does not really like it. \"But the truth is the Ra'avad didn't understand the Rambam. He had a lot of depth there.\" Part 3: Approaches 3 and 4: Limiting One Principle The first two approaches keep both principles and reconcile them. The next two limit one of them . Approach 3, the Ralbag: Hashem withdraws His knowledge The Ralbag , Rabbi Levi ben Gershon (Gersonides): philosopher, mathematician, astronomer, commentator on the Torah, \"an incredible mind. If you think the Rambam was a rationalist, the Ralbag was much more of a rationalist.\" And yet he \"comes up with the following phenomenal approach\": To allow humans free will, Hashem limited His knowledge of our future choices . He knows our actions as we do them, and everything is taken into account, but as it were, He withdrew Himself ( tzimtzum ) from knowing in advance what we will choose. That is not an imperfection, because He chose it. The parable of the wealthy father. I am the wealthiest man and could support everyone in the world. I decide, so that my son will learn to make his own way, to leave him in poverty to work his way up. That does not diminish my wealth. At any moment I could say, \"Son, come back to the palace.\" I have willingly chosen not to. So too, it would be an imperfection if Hashem could not know; it is not one if He chooses not to. An echo of the Arizal , about 300 years later. The Arizal asked how anything, even humans or evil, can exist if Hashem's essence permeates everything, and answered with tzimtzum : Hashem withdrew Himself. \"That's a much broader thing. The Ralbag says it on one very specific matter: Hashem's knowledge of our future actions.\" Approach 4, Chasdai Crescas: He knows us completely Chasdai Crescas , 14th century, from Zaragoza, author of Or Hashem , The Light of Hashem . He limits the other principle. Hashem is perfect, so He knows our choices. In what sense are we free? \"Perhaps the most difficult approach to accept and understand.\" Hashem understands every human being so completely that He can predict exactly what he or she will choose. \"Flora, this is what you touched on last week\": the mother who sends her child to the shop with a pound and just knows he'll come back with sweets. She has no magical powers. She knows how he grew up, his challenges, his choices. Does the child have free will? Of course. At any moment he could do what his mother said. But looking from the side, she knows what he'll choose. So too Hashem, with total knowledge, knows rather than guesses. \"If we could input every single aspect of a human being's identity, where you were born, what kind of parents you had, how many siblings, which synagogue, which school, which culture, we'd be able to predict how that person would respond to any situation. There is talk about this nowadays. Already there are things that can tell you all your political views from ten answers. Same idea, much deeper.\" The problem: this is very deterministic . If our choices are the product of millions of factors, upbringing, family, community, social identity, then we are programmed . \"It might answer the contradiction, but it leaves you with another question: in what sense do we really have free will?\" The four approaches Approach Free will Foreknowledge 1 Saadia Complete Hashem knows the outcome of our deliberation ; knowing is not causing 2 Rabbeinu Bachya and the Rambam Complete Complete. Reconciled from Hashem's perspective , beyond time, not from ours. \"Everything is foreseen and permission is given\" 3 The Ralbag Complete Hashem voluntarily withdraws His knowledge of our future choices 4 Chasdai Crescas Real but conditioned Hashem knows us so completely He knows what we will choose. \"He spoke about AI.\" \"Those are basically the four main approaches in the Rishonim. The later discussion really goes around these four.\" From the class: AI and 100% Chaim said this is exactly AI today. The Rabbi: we are not yet at the stage where inputting your age, parents, siblings, community and education predicts you; it would be more likely to be right, \"but still not 100%.\" Chaim: only at 100% do you truly have no choice, and 100% is only Hashem. The Rabbi stressed how nuanced humans are: two people with the same school, synagogue and community, one of whom had a single different experience , may respond quite differently. \"One tiny permutation can change the whole equation.\" The Rabbi's own view: \"If you ask me my best understanding, it's certainly the Rambam's approach . That for me makes the most sense, and I think it's very deep.\" Part 4: Chapter 5, Question 1: Why Command the Righteous? Chapters 5 and 6 remain. Chapter 5 asks seven questions on free will; Chapter 6 takes verses that seem to contradict it. \"Again Saadia takes a very, very strict approach: we have full free will, and he tries not to limit it at all.\" The question: if Hashem knows the righteous person will do the right thing (He told Avraham to bind his son, and he did), what is the point of commanding him? \"I don't fully understand what the question is,\" the Rabbi admitted, but Saadia gives four answers: To know what is right. The righteous person needs to know what Hashem wants. To reward him properly. The Gemara says greater is one who is commanded and does than one who is not commanded and does . Symmetry. If Hashem rewarded what He never commanded because He knew it would be done, He would also have to punish for what He never forbade. That is not right, so He discloses both the good and the bad. Encouragement. Hearing that Hashem commands it encourages us. I warned him, and so he will live. Rav Yosef the blind The Rabbi told the Gemara's story. Rav Yosef was blind , and there was a debate whether a blind person is obligated in mitzvot. At first he said: whoever tells me the halacha is that a blind person is exempt , I'll make a party. I keep all the mitzvot anyway; if I'm exempt and still do them, think how much reward I'll get! Then he changed his mind: I'll make a party for whoever tells me a blind person is obligated . When you are commanded, there are greater challenges, a greater pull not to do it, so the reward is greater . Part 5: Question 2: Why Warn the Wicked? If Hashem knows the wicked will not listen, why send prophets to call them to teshuvah? Saadia gives seven answers (the Rabbi noted a missing line in the printed text and supplied it from his own edition): # Answer 1 No excuse. Otherwise the wicked could say, \"Had You sent me prophets, had You told me off, I wouldn't have done it.\" So Hashem, \"as it were, covers Himself\" 2 Punishment is for what happens, not what would happen. Without a warning, Hashem would be punishing them for what He knows they would have done had He warned them. Reward and punishment are not based on potential 3 Completeness. Just as Hashem gave proofs of His existence (Essay 1), and some still deny Him, there should be wide-ranging statements of His will, for believers and deniers alike 4 A command has its own value. If I tell you to do something bad and you don't, I am still wrong and foolish for saying it. So a command to do good is still wise, even if it is ignored 5 Otherwise a command's worth depends on obedience. If a good command ignored is \"pointless,\" then a bad command obeyed would be \"worthwhile.\" That is ridiculous 6 Equality. Hashem gave the wicked and the righteous the same reason and ability, so He gives both the same commands 7 A lesson for others. \"Perhaps the best answer.\" Even if the command is worthless to the wicked man, everyone else sees that he was commanded, did not listen, and was punished. \"People still talk about the Flood, about Sedom.\" It teaches the righteous and future generations Answer 2 and last week's problem \"This is an important point.\" It comes back to the Rabbi's problem from last week: if Hashem knows what we will choose, why live through the world at all? Here Saadia gives his answer: Hashem does not reward and punish based on potential or simulation , on \"if I created this kind of human he would choose A, B and C through billions of choices.\" He rewards and punishes what actually happens . \"We have to go through it.\" Part 6: Questions 3 and 4: Decreed Death, Human Murder \"A very interesting question.\" Questions three and four are really one. Question 3. Suppose Hashem has decreed a person must die this year, as punishment or as a trial. A wicked person comes and kills him, as Izevel killed the prophets . To whom do we attribute the act? Izevel could argue she was merely fulfilling the word of God. Question 4. Hashem decrees that a person will lose £1,000 this year, and a thief burgles £1,000 from him. Is the theft Hashem's act? Saadia's answer: the outcome is Hashem's decree; the way it happens is the human's act . The person was to die, but he could have died in many ways , naturally in his bed, not waking in the morning. That he died this way is the murderer's doing. \"You're going to lose £1,000, yes. But you could have paid lots of parking fines this year , been caught in lots of bus lanes. Someone decides to steal from you; that's his problem. Hashem has many ways.\" Shmaya and Achiya Saadia brings the two brothers Shmaya and Achiya before the king of Edom, that is, Rome, told in the Gemara ( Ta'anit 18b ). The king said: do a miracle; let your God save you, as He saved Chananyah, Mishael and Azaryah from the fire. They replied: They were perfectly righteous and fit for a miracle. We are not , and we are deserving of death. If you do not kill us, Hashem has many bears and many lions to punish us with. We were handed to you only so that you would be punished for our blood. \"Yes, we deserve to die. But why does it have to be you ?\" From the class Flora recalled a Gemara: even those like Nevuchadnezzar or Pharaoh , who carried out what was decreed, were punished because they overstepped , acting with needless cruelty. The Rabbi agreed: that is the answer of the Ra'avad and the Ramban , seen on Tuesday night with the Rambam's question of why Egypt was punished when Avraham had been told his descendants would be enslaved for 400 years. They went overboard. Part 7: The Rabbi's Problem: The £5,000 Gift \"I'll tell you the problem I have with this.\" Hashem decrees that a poor man will receive £5,000 more this year than last. I see he has been struggling, and out of the goodness of my heart I give him £5,000 of charity. By Saadia's logic, he would have got his £5,000 with me or without me , just as the victim would have died with or without the murderer. If he'd won the lottery or found it on the floor, the outcome is the same. So human action becomes a bit meaningless . \"I could easily say at the back of my mind: if I step back now and don't give him £1,000, someone else will.\" And all my actions are just fulfilling decrees of God , \"which again I find difficult. It comes back to Saadia's answer on foreknowledge: an actor idea, that we're just acting out what Hashem has already decreed.\" Participants pushed back: You are the one who listened first. Hashem's will is that he gets the money; how it happens is up to people, and you were the first to fulfil it. Action moulds the man. It doesn't matter that someone else would have done it; the giving shapes you . \"It's not about brownie points.\" The more mitzvot you do, the more accustomed you become to doing good, and that is why there are poor people: so we have the chance to give . The Rabbi agreed it is good, \"of course it's good, I'm not telling you not to do it,\" but held to his point: \"I'm essentially acting out what would have happened anyway. Yes, I did it and I get the credit, fantastic. But it would have happened anyway.\" There are other approaches, which he promised for next week. Summary Four approaches to foreknowledge and free will: Saadia (He knows the outcome of our choice); Rabbeinu Bachya and the Rambam (both true, reconciled only from Hashem's timeless perspective, \"everything is foreseen and permission is given\"; the Ra'avad misread it); the Ralbag (Hashem voluntarily withdraws His foreknowledge, like the father who leaves his son to make his own way); Chasdai Crescas (He knows us so completely He knows our choices, which is very deterministic). The Rabbi's own preference is the Rambam . Why command the righteous? To know what is right, for greater reward (\"greater is one who is commanded\"; Rav Yosef the blind), for symmetry, and for encouragement. Why warn the wicked? Seven answers, chief among them: no excuse , punishment only for what actually happens (not for a simulation), and a lesson for others . Decreed outcomes, human means: the death or loss is Hashem's decree; how it happens is the human's act (Shmaya and Achiya: \"many bears, many lions\"). Those who carry out a decree with excess cruelty are punished for that too. The Rabbi's difficulty: if outcomes are fixed, does my charity matter? The class answered that action moulds the man . Coming Up Next week: the rest of Chapter 5, with three more questions, including Sancheriv , and more on this theme, with a different approach to the Rabbi's question. Then Chapter 6: verses that seem to contradict free will, \"very interesting to see the different approaches in the Rishonim.\"","wNFJqDZ9mwQ":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 4 (Obedience and Disobedience, Compulsion and Justice), Chapters 3-4 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 18 November 2024, in person and on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: From Creation to Choice We have established that there is a Creator (Essay 1), that this Creator is who we call Hashem (Essay 2), and that He revealed His will , the Torah (Essay 3). \"Now we talk about our choices. The first time we're talking about humans.\" Last week, in the introduction and Chapters 1-2 of Essay 4, Saadia argued that the world was created for humans and that humans are the most elevated creatures. Everyone agrees humans are the most elevated creatures on earth ; whether the whole world was created for us was debated, \"but it's not so relevant to the rest of this essay.\" All Saadia needed was that humans are the pinnacle, so to them Hashem gave the Torah and the commandments, and they have free will . Chapters 3 to 6 are about free will itself, \"all fascinating questions.\" Saadia's absolute free will Among the Rishonim there are different views of how much free will a human has. Saadia is one of the most extreme : he holds complete and absolute free will , stricter even than the Rambam. \"For Saadia everything really is up to human beings.\" This may be a reaction to Islamic philosophy . As Saadia told us in his introduction, he lived in a milieu with strong Islamic influence, and one dominant school there held determinism : God is in control and determines everything, and humans do not really have free will. \"Saadia completely rejects that and goes to the other extreme.\" Part 1: Chapter 3, Four Points on Free Will Point 1: Hashem gives us the ability Since Hashem gave us the Torah and revealed His will, it is fitting and just that He give us the ability and the strength to do what He commanded and refrain from what He forbade. This is clear rationally and scripturally : Hashem never commands what a person cannot do. Rationally: God would never give someone a task he has no ability to perform or refrain from. Scripturally: עמי מה עשיתי לך ומה הלאתיך? ענה בי , \"My people, what have I done to you, how have I wearied you? Answer Me\" (Michah 6:3). Have I ever given you something you could not fulfil? \"This was a famous idea developed by many after him. Rav Dessler has a famous piece on how God never tests a person beyond what he can do. But this is the origin of the concept.\" Point 2: The choice comes before the action Free will must precede the action: a person must be fully able to choose to do or to refrain. Saadia rejects two alternatives: The view Saadia's verdict Choice is simultaneous with the action: God determines the act, and our \"choice\" is our consent at the moment Then choice and act are mutually interdependent , so it is still determined . Absurd Choice comes after the action, letting us somehow withdraw from it Absurd. \"The action's done. How can my will change that?\" \"This is only intelligible\" against the background of compromise schools within Islamic thought . Unwilling to say that everything is determined, they tried to bring choice in alongside determinism. Saadia rejects both, as did essentially all the Rishonim, \"although we will see one of the Rishonim, Chasdai Crescas in Or Hashem , took a similar kind of approach\" to the question of foreknowledge. The main thing, which all Jewish thinkers hold: our free will comes before the action. Point 3: Not acting is also a choice Another compromise held that we choose our actions, but our abstentions are determined by Hashem . Saadia says no: choosing not to do something is also part of our free will. Human passivity is not like Hashem's. Before Hashem created the world He was, \"if we can use such terms,\" passive, and we do not say His passivity was a kind of activity. (We saw in Essay 1 that there is no \"opposite\" with Hashem.) But with humans, not doing usually means doing the opposite. If you don't favour something, you are normally against it. \"It's rare to actually find a middle ground.\" Saadia's verses: keep My mitzvot... and do not do the abominations , and they did no wrong; they walked in His ways (Tehillim 119:3). Doing the mitzvot means not doing the abominations, and not doing wrong means walking in His way: opposites. \"Generally speaking, humans are black and white. I like that person, I don't like that person. I'm in a good mood, a bad mood. If you think about it deeply enough, normally human activities have an opposite.\" The deeper point , as the Rabbi understood it: Hashem is passive towards our choices. If every passivity were really a hidden activity, then Hashem's passivity towards our actions would be a kind of activity in them, and we would not have full free will . So Saadia distinguishes: our passivity is a subtle kind of activity, so free will covers it; Hashem's passivity is complete, so it does not interfere at all. Leo asked whether being passive is itself a decision. \"That's the point. It's will.\" And Hashem not yet creating was not a will, since there was no conflict. Asked whether Hashem's passivity might still be \"creative\" in some way: \"That's a precise point. It's not. If Hashem's passivity were active, then every time He passively allows us to choose, we still wouldn't have free will.\" Point 4: What you did not choose is not your action \"An interesting point which has halachic consequences.\" An action is yours only if you chose it. Someone may object: the Torah punishes mistakes . Saadia: no, in those cases you did choose the act ; you just lacked some knowledge. The accidental killer was intentionally chopping wood . He was not careful, but the chopping was his conscious act. That is why he must flee to the city of refuge. Breaking Shabbat by mistake: you were intentionally cutting wood; you just forgot it was Shabbat. The Rabbi laid out the halachic categories, taking Shabbat: Category Example Consequence Mezid (deliberate) You know it is Shabbat, know it is forbidden, and do it The death penalty Shogeg (inadvertent) You forget it is Shabbat : you wake up thinking it is Sunday and water and plough your garden. Or you know it is Shabbat but don't know watering is forbidden . Either way, the act itself is fully conscious A korban chatat in Temple times Mit'asek (unaware of the act) You lean back against a wall and switch on a light you didn't see. Or you carry outside an eruv without knowing something is in your pocket Nothing , not even a korban. \"It's not your action. I never chose to do that\" \"Nowadays you should write down every time you do a shogeg , so when the Beit HaMikdash is rebuilt you bring a korban chatat for it.\" And: \"Most of the time when people do something on Shabbat, it's mit'asek , by the way.\" A participant asked the difference between not realising it is Shabbat and not realising you are carrying. \"Huge difference.\" When you forget it is Shabbat, the watering is a fully conscious decision; you are not punished, but you bring a korban. When you carry unknowingly, you never decided to carry . \"That's precisely the point of this chapter. It fits in beautifully.\" Sleepwalking: if you were sleepwalking while cutting wood and the wood killed someone, you would not go to the city of refuge. A participant objected that anyone could then claim to have been sleepwalking. \"Of course. We're talking theoretically.\" In practice it depends on whether the person is believed. Chapter 3 in four points: Hashem gives us the ability; the choice comes before the action; abstaining is a choice too; and only a chosen act is your act. Part 2: Chapter 4, Four Proofs That We Are Free Hashem gives us absolute free will and compels us to nothing, good or bad . Saadia's proofs come from four sources: 1. Sense: we feel it Can I choose now to speak? Yes. Can I choose to stop? Yes. Nothing and no one tells us what to do. \"I think we can all agree that we do feel, deep down, that we can choose what to do in life.\" 2. Logic: five arguments # Argument 1 One act cannot have two agents. In Essay 1, among the thirteen theories of creation (almost a year ago), Saadia rejected dualism , the Gnostic two gods, partly because one thing cannot come from two agents. If God compelled us, every human act would have two agents, God and the human 2 Why command at all? If it is all determined, commandments are pointless 3 Why reward or punish someone compelled to do good or bad? 4 No difference between good and bad people. If I hire one man to build my house and another to demolish my other house for renovation, I pay both equally : each did what I asked 5 The wicked would have a perfect defence. In the World to Come, anyone could say, \"I had no ability to go against Your will,\" and he would be right The Rabbi called the first \"probably the weakest.\" A participant pointed out that dualism is about two gods creating, which is quite different from a human acting under compulsion. \"Correct. Not great. You're 100% right. The other ones are much better.\" Arguments 3 to 5 are similar, \"and obviously a strong proof.\" 3. Scripture \"Choose life.\" To the sinners: this came from your own hand . It is not My fault. The false prophets : Hashem says, I am not responsible for them; they chose to be false. \"Over and over again you see in Scripture God saying: it's not Me, it's you who chose this.\" 4. Tradition \"A side point: I don't know if you've noticed, Saadia rarely quotes Chazal .\" The Rabbi could find only two places before this in the book. The central role of Chazal \"became much stronger in the time of the Rishonim; the Geonim didn't have that relationship yet with the Talmud.\" הכל בידי שמים חוץ מיראת שמים Everything is in the hands of Heaven except the fear of Heaven. (Berachot 33b) Whether you become ill is in the hands of Heaven (\"although if you go out without your coat, you're responsible\"). The fear of Heaven is up to you, as the verse says: what does Hashem your God ask of you, but to fear Him? Three literary devices Even though all this is clear, the Torah drives it home in three ways : A question: החפץ אחפץ מות רשע? \"Do I desire the death of the wicked?\" (Yechezkel 18:23) An assertion: I do not desire it; I want him to return and live. An oath: חי אני... אם אחפץ במות הרשע , \"As I live... I do not desire the death of the wicked\" (Yechezkel 33:11). The first half of Essay 4: humans are the pinnacle (Chapters 1-2); free will is complete, comes before the act, covers abstaining, and only chosen acts count (Chapter 3); and it is proven by sense, logic, Scripture and tradition (Chapter 4). \"Now we have lots of questions to ask on free will.\" The rest of Chapter 4 and Chapters 5-6 are questions and answers. Part 3: Why Does Hashem Allow Evil? Saadia asks three questions here, \"in truth only two, because two and three are basically the same.\" Question 1. If everything were determined, evil would simply be God's will. But now that humans are completely free and do evil, God tolerates it . If He is all-powerful and all-good, why doesn't He stop it? Saadia: humans push away things they dislike, because those things bother them. If something bothers you and you can stop it, it would be strange not to. But Hashem is not affected by anything . As we learned in Essay 2 on anthropomorphism, when Hashem says \"I hate sin\" , He does not hate anything; it makes no difference to Him. He tells us not to sin because it is bad for us . Wronging others harms our own souls. האתי הם מכעיסים נאם ה׳? הלוא אתם Is it Me they anger, says Hashem? Is it not themselves? (Yirmiyahu 7:19) So evil looks bad from our perspective, but it is not bad for Hashem. He told us not to do it, for our good, and He tolerates it because it makes no difference to Him. Part 4: Foreknowledge and Free Will \"Now I want you just to appreciate: Saadia is the very first Jewish thinker recorded who asked this question ,\" which would occupy the Rishonim for the next three to four hundred years. The dilemma If we choose freely, Hashem does not know what we will choose, and His knowledge is limited . That makes Him imperfect, which is unacceptable. If Hashem does know what we will choose, since He knows everything, then it is not possible for us to do otherwise, and we have no free will . Saadia: \"simpler than the question before\" \"For Saadia it's unbelievable. Look what he says.\" The answer is one line: There is no proof that Hashem's knowing something causes it. Whoever thinks so is either mistaken or making it up. His proof: if whatever Hashem knew were caused by His knowing it, everything would have existed from the very beginning , since He knew everything from the beginning. There would be no such thing as anything coming into being. Rather, He knows things as they are : what will be made, He knows will be made, and what a person will choose, He knows the person will choose . Question 3: can I keep quiet at seven o'clock? If Hashem knows I will speak at 7 o'clock this evening, do I have the ability to keep quiet at 7 o'clock? Saadia: if I were going to keep quiet, then from the beginning we would have said that Hashem knew I would keep quiet. \"This is the key line.\" Hashem knows the end result : what the person will finally decide after all his thoughts, his deliberation, his delaying, his going forwards and backwards . The alarm clock Your alarm goes off at 6 o'clock. \"I'm going to be late for work. Give it one more minute. Should I get up? Okay, one more minute.\" At 6.05 it rings again. At 6.06 you get up. Hashem knows you will get up at 6.06 and three seconds. He hasn't caused it. You chose. He just knows that after all your deliberating, that is what you will choose. The Kuzari agrees The Kuzari (Essay 5, Chapter 20) takes the same approach: knowledge of something is not its cause , just as knowing something that has already happened does not cause it. \"I know everything you've done in your life. I haven't caused it.\" So too knowledge of the future. Rav Yosef Albo also took this approach. Part 5: The Class Wrestles with It Johnny and the sweets Flora offered the analogy she uses with A-level students: a mother gives Johnny a pound to buy sugar and milk, knowing in her heart that he will sneak in some sweets as well. \"That's a different theory. That's Chasdai Crescas , a very deterministic view.\" The mother knows because she knows his nature , so he could not have chosen otherwise. Saadia says something different: Hashem knows the choice . Johnny will reach the shop, think \"what if my mum catches me, what if she doesn't,\" and he has the choice, and Hashem knows that after all his deliberation he will choose the sweets. \"Not that I know his nature and that he likes sweets. I know he's going to buy sweets because I know that's what he's going to choose .\" Eve recalled an image she had heard, perhaps from Rabbi Sacks: a shepherd on a hill watching which sheep go through; he sees it but does not cause it. Richard summed up: we only know the past and draw conclusions about the future, but Hashem has knowledge of our conclusion , whatever thought process we go through. \"Correct. Precisely. He doesn't have to work it out.\" \"Am I just following the script?\" Doron objected: I accept that knowing my past doesn't mean you caused it. But if you know my one future path, I'm simply an actor following the script . It would be different if Hashem knew all the branching possibilities (if you choose X it leads to Y), rather than the single path I will take. The Rabbi polled the room. Philippe : it makes sense. Maria : \"not my favourite, but I can wrap my head around it.\" Eve : \"I suppose it does.\" Asked whether Hashem follows non-Jews too: \"A really big question.\" On the Rambam's approach, whether Hashem is involved depends on whether the person wants a relationship with Him , Jew or non-Jew. \"But that's a whole different discussion.\" The Rabbi's own difficulty \"The problem with this approach is exactly what you're saying.\" Next time you face a big choice, at the back of your mind: it's been decided . A participant asked whether it also brings back the problem of reward and punishment. And the Rabbi added a problem of his own: \"If Hashem knows exactly what I will choose for the next 120 years from the moment I'm born, what's the point of living? He could create me, you, Richard, Doron, Leo, run us through His 'program', see that this one gets 60%, that one 70% or 80%, and give us our reward. Finished. You don't need a world. It's one of the biggest problems I've had with this approach for many, many years. \" Saadia does slightly address this in the next chapter: you have to live it , to go through the process of choosing. A participant suggested the purpose is to experience it. \"Okay, just give us that experience.\" We know why Hashem created the world, that we acquire goodness for ourselves by choosing. But He could give us the experience of the choices we would have made. \"Why create me?\" The discussion continued after the recording stopped. Summary Saadia's free will is absolute , perhaps a reaction to Islamic determinism. Chapter 3: Hashem never commands the impossible; the choice precedes the act (not simultaneous, not after); abstaining is a choice too , while Hashem's passivity is complete and does not interfere; and only a chosen act is yours , as the halachic categories mezid , shogeg and mit'asek show. Chapter 4, proofs: sense, five logical arguments (the strongest: why command, why reward, the builder and demolisher paid alike, the wicked man's perfect defence), Scripture, and Chazal: \"everything is in the hands of Heaven except the fear of Heaven.\" Hashem says it by question, assertion and oath. Why evil? Sin does not affect Hashem; He forbids it for our sake. \"Is it Me they anger?\" Foreknowledge: Saadia is the first to ask it. Knowing is not causing. Hashem knows the conclusion of our deliberation (the alarm at 6.06), as the Kuzari also says. The class pressed hard, and the Rabbi shared his own difficulty: if He knows, why live it out? Coming Up Next week: the other approaches of the Rishonim to foreknowledge: the Rambam, the Ralbag, Chasdai Crescas , with side points from the Ra'avad and Rabbeinu Bachya in Chovot HaLevavot .","uI7ETZGheZA":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 4 (Obedience and Disobedience, Compulsion and Justice), Introduction and Chapters 1-2 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 11 November 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Three Essays Done Essay Theme 1 Creation : the whole concept of creation 2 Who created : Hashem, His attributes and how we describe Him 3 Revelation : the Torah, its parts, the prophets who revealed it, and (last week) twelve questions on the Torah With these three fundamentals in place (creation, what God is, and how He reveals Himself in the world), Saadia turns to free will . Essay 4 in outline A relatively short essay: an introduction and six chapters . Saadia is the very first to discuss free will systematically. The Talmud has fleeting references to our ability to choose good, but the philosophical problems with free will begin with him. The famous question, in Chapter 4 : Hashem is perfect and knows everything, including the future. If He knows the future, how do we have free will? Many Rishonim discussed it, and we will see their approaches. \"It's worked out very nicely\" : tomorrow night's Tuesday shiur on the Rambam's Hilchot Teshuvah reaches Chapter 5 , which is all about free will. \"So maybe a bit of repetition tonight and tomorrow, but that's just how it's fallen out.\" The order of Saadia's argument Before he can raise the problems, Saadia must establish that humans have free will . That is not a given, \"especially in today's society,\" where many believe everything is predetermined: we are biologically programmed beings acting according to instinct, upbringing and those around us. Before even that, he must establish that only humans have free will, and so the Torah is directed only to humans . To do that, he makes the case that humans are the pinnacle of creation and the centre of the universe . Tonight: the introduction and Chapters 1 and 2, Saadia's proofs that the world was created for humans, \"and the Rishonim who opposed him and disagreed with him.\" Part 1: The Title of the Essay Saadia names the essay avodah u-meri, tzedek ve-hekhre'ach : obedience and disobedience, justice and compulsion . As the commentators on Saadia point out, the first two words are not precise . Obedience and disobedience, merits and demerits , are really the subject of Essay 5 . This essay is about: Compulsion: do we have free will? Justice: Hashem can reward, punish and compensate us only justly if we have free will . Without full free will, the whole concept of reward and punishment falls away. The Rabbi's explanation: Saadia's goal is obedience and disobedience, the mitzvot and the aveirot , in Essay 5. But first he must establish that we can choose between good and bad. Part 2: The Introduction: The Middle Is the Most Important Of all the many creatures, Saadia says, we should have no doubt for whom the universe was created . Natural science will show us. Whatever matters most is in the middle When you build something, the most important part goes in the middle. Saadia brings examples from nature: Example Why it shows the centre matters The seed in a fruit or plant It is in the middle because the whole plant grows from it. Sometimes the seed itself is what we eat, like an almond , encased on the outside The yolk of an egg It is in the middle because the chick grows from it The heart It is in the middle of the body, the centre of you The pupil The part you see through is in the middle of the eye \"As you can see, if you haven't worked this out yet, he's clearly going towards a geocentric universe ,\" the common belief until Galileo in the 1600s changed how we see the universe. From the cosmos to man The earth is in the middle of the universe , and the whole cosmos revolves around it. So the earth is the most important part. On the earth, water and dust are inanimate , and animals do not speak . What remains is the human . So humans are the pinnacle and centre of the universe . Saadia then brings verses: אנכי עשיתי ארץ ואדם עליה בראתי , \"I made the earth and created man upon it\" (Yeshayahu 45:12). The Rabbi did not see the proof: \"Yes, perhaps the human is the centre of the earth, but it doesn't necessarily mean the human is the centre of the universe.\" The order of Bereishit , with man created last, and the Midrash of a king who builds a palace, furnishes it, and only then brings in its resident . First Hashem builds a beautiful home and furnishes it, then puts you in it. The egg: yolk or white? There is a discussion in the Gemara over whether the chick grows from the white or the yolk, with halachic ramifications. Saadia holds it is the yolk ; the Ibn Ezra says the white , and brings a proof from the Gemara. Participants answered that it is the yolk, since a blood spot sometimes appears there, and Donald later confirmed that apparently it is indeed the yolk that matters most. Part 3: The Ibn Ezra and the Rambam Disagree The Ibn Ezra On the first verse of the Torah, the Ibn Ezra writes: ואל תשים לבך לדברי הגאון שאמר שהאדם נכבד מהמלאכים Pay no attention to the words of the Gaon, who said that man is more honoured than the angels. In another book he went through Saadia's proofs and said they are all incorrect : The most elevated humans are the prophets , and yet Yehoshua prostrated himself and showed great deference before an angel. The heart is important, \"I agree, but the head is as important, if not more.\" And the head is not in the middle. The seed: that works for an almond, where the edible seed is in the middle. But in grapes and other fruit, the seed in the middle is inedible , and the important part is the fruit around it . Saadia's premise is gone \"Nowadays we would say that Saadia is clearly incorrect. We may not be comfortable saying that, but that's the reality.\" Even if we accept his premise that the middle is the most important, which the Ibn Ezra disputed, the earth is not the middle . Everything does not revolve around the earth; we are a speck in a huge universe, and if anything we revolve around the sun. The Rambam and the Ibn Ezra could not raise this, living in a pre-heliocentric world. \"It could well be that humans are the pinnacle of creation, but not because of Saadia's proofs.\" The Rambam: Moreh Nevuchim 3:13 The Rambam did not believe humans were the pinnacle of creation. You may think everything in the universe exists for man, and some verses suggest it, such as לא תהו בראה, לשבת יצרה , \"He did not create it for emptiness; He formed it to be inhabited\" (Yeshayahu 45:18). But analyse it. \"We have a duty always to analyse our thoughts and opinions.\" Could Hashem have created the human without all the cosmos around us? Of course He could. We don't need all the galaxies. So what are they for? And even if you said the universe is for man , the question remains: what is the purpose of man serving God ? \"This is a famous paragraph, by the way.\" God gains nothing from our service. If you say it is for us, to perfect ourselves, why is our perfection important? And so on. The conclusion: you will go back endlessly, and must stop somewhere: so Hashem desired, so His wisdom decreed . The Rabbi linked it to Ne'ilah , where we say that Hashem set man apart from the beginning, and who can say to Him, what are You doing? \"It's just Hashem wanted to do so.\" Was the universe created for man? Saadia Yes. Man is the centre and pinnacle, and everything is for him The Ibn Ezra No. Saadia's proofs are all incorrect, and the angels are higher The Rambam No. Hashem did not need the cosmos for man; ultimately, that was His will The rest of the essay still stands. \"Regardless of whether you believe like Saadia or like the Rambam, the free will discussions we're going to have are still true and still correct.\" From the class: the Milky Way and beyond A participant asked whether God created the Milky Way too. The Rabbi: everyone agrees He created everything. The Milky Way is our galaxy, and there are millions or billions of others, each with its systems and stars. \"It's very funny, because at the end of Chapter 1 Saadia says: don't think I believe the universe was created for humans out of human arrogance ; it's because of all the objective proofs. I think if Saadia were around today and knew its vastness, it would be much harder to argue that all of this is just for humans . There must be something else going on. Why? I've got no idea. This is the will of Hashem.\" Asked whether there might be more gods in the Milky Way: \" Absolutely not. God forbid, there is one God.\" But whether there are other populations or creatures in another galaxy, \"that could well be, we don't know. In fact some of our Rabbis did believe there were. A talk for another time.\" To a joke about Elon Musks on other galaxies sending satellites: \"Maybe one day. We'll see.\" Part 4: Chapter 1, Why Man Was Given Choice With the introduction aside, Saadia says the prophets told us humans are the most important : Tehillim 8 : \"You have made him little less than the angels ... You have given him dominion over the works of Your hands,\" the fish of the sea and the beasts. \"The whole Psalm talks about how amazing humans are.\" Hashem gave us the ability to work the land and control over the earth. Because we are the pinnacle, He gave us the choice , and commanded us to choose good: ראה נתתי לפניך היום את החיים ואת הטוב ואת המות ואת הרע... ובחרת בחיים See, I have set before you today life and good, death and evil... and you shall choose life. (Devarim 30:15, 19) And we accept it because the Torah was proven through the prophets and their miracles. The Rabbi noted that the Ibn Ezra would agree that humans have a certain authority over the animals, as the Torah says. The question is only whether everything was created for man . In what way are we better? Wisdom Through our wisdom : We remember the past and think about the future . We have made animals work for us and produce for us. We draw and irrigate water , and even build water wheels that draw it by themselves, without our labour. We build beautiful homes , make beautiful clothes , and cook , blending flavours to please the palate. We form armies, encampments and kingdoms . We study astronomy : how far each planet is from the next. \"If you think there's another creature with all of this, show me . You're not going to find it.\" So man is the root and foundation of the world , and to him are directed the commandments, the prohibitions, and the consequences for good and bad. \"Not my arrogance.\" Saadia insists: when I say man is more important than all else, it is not a mistake, and not my arrogance or brazenness . It is clear and correct. Hashem gave us this advantage so that we would be the ones to whom the mitzvot are given, for the root of all is the fear of Hashem . Introduction and Chapter 1 compared The claim Do the Ibn Ezra and the Rambam agree? Introduction The earth, and so man, is most important because it is in the centre No. The centre is not necessarily the most important, and we are not in the centre Chapter 1 Man has an advantage over other creatures: wisdom , and therefore receives the commandments It could well be that they agree In the middle of all this Saadia slips in one key sentence: we have the ability to choose , and so we must choose in life. From the class: speech A participant added that we are the only ones who can talk . The Rabbi: animals do express themselves, with barks and meows (\"maybe dogs think that we bark\"), and dolphins communicate very well. But we have speech , and many languages. \"That's another thing you could say is a proof of how the human is higher than everything else.\" Part 5: Chapter 2, Eight Questions on Man's Greatness \"I'm going to do this really quickly, because the real essay on free will starts in Chapter 3.\" Having established man as the pinnacle, Saadia asks eight questions on it. 1. Why is man so small? All of this, for us tiny beings? Our body is small, but our mind and consciousness are greater than heaven and earth. Physically we can only run, or get on a plane. But our minds can think about what is happening in Australia , beyond this planet, and even about God . \"Physically small, but spiritually very great and very vast.\" 2. Why don't we live longer? The Rabbi put it to the class. One answer was that we once did live long; another, that the Torah says \"on the day you eat of it you shall die.\" Saadia's answer: Hashem gave us a short life in this world, the life of work , and in the future we will have the continuous life (he will discuss resurrection in Essay 9). This world is the life of achievement , so it must be limited, and the reward comes in the World to Come. \"Why does Saadia not give the answer the Torah gives?\" A participant said: who says it's a different answer? \"Exactly. That's it.\" Before Adam sinned he lived in Eden, in what we would call the World to Come . After the sin he had to go through death, and receive the World to Come in a different state. Saadia is explaining in words people will understand what the Torah means. \"That's the beauty of Saadia. He can't just give an answer because the Torah says so. It's amazing he's writing this 1,100 years ago. He could have said the Torah says, but that's not good enough for the people he was addressing.\" 3. Why is our body so feeble? We are made of blood and phlegm, full of excesses. Why not more refined? Man is already the most refined of all the creatures. Anything more refined would be an angel . Asking for a more refined human is like asking for heaven made of dust or earth made of fire: you are asking for there to be no heaven, no earth, no human . \"You're just saying nonsense.\" A participant pointed to Tehillim 8, \"a little less than the angels.\" \"Very good, that's precisely correct,\" and Saadia quoted that Psalm in Chapter 1. The Rabbi compared it to a remark of the Rambam's: why is the sky blue? Because it is blue. If it were green, you'd ask why it is green. \"This is the human. If you want a different kind of human, it's not the human anymore.\" 4. Why illnesses? Flu, colds, the big ones. Through our pains we are humbled and aroused to return to Hashem as we lie in bed. \"And there's a lot of truth to that, by the way.\" \"When we're fit, healthy, wealthy, everything's perfect, what do I need God for? You know the story of the guy who comes to synagogue on Yom Kippur for the final half an hour and says: God, I had a fantastic year, please just stay out of it for the coming year.\" \" There's no atheist in the foxhole. When you're in bed, in hospital, when things have gone terribly wrong, suddenly you open up your tefillin and pray.\" If a person keeps the connection with Hashem when everything is going well, Hashem doesn't need to bring these things . He brings them to people who need reminding, \"and many of us need these reminders.\" 5. Why are we exposed to heat, cold and dangerous animals? Snakes, lions that can eat you, elephants that can throw you around. So that we know what Hashem's punishments could be. If there were nothing to fear, a person told \"I'll punish you\" would say, \"What can You do to me?\" Malachi: a day is coming burning like an oven . We need to know scorching heat to grasp it. Ha'azinu: when we do wrong and the nations turn against us, their punishments will be like the venom of serpents . \"I need to know what the venom of a serpent is.\" 6. Why so many cravings and desires? So that we learn to choose correctly how and when to use them: Desire Its proper use Its misuse Money To live properly Stealing and cheating Food Looking after the body Eating the wrong things Relations Bringing the next generation, as Hashem instructed Passion for women forbidden to him \"The rasha is happy about all his desires.\" Hashem creates the cravings so we can choose between the good uses and the bad. 7. Why punishment, and not just no reward? \"This is interesting. I think there's a bit of depth there.\" The Rabbi recalled the Ramchal from Derech Hashem: Hashem created the world to bestow goodness , and we must be the authors of our own identity by choosing good. The question everyone asks: fine, those who choose good are rewarded with Olam HaBa. Those who don't simply lose the opportunity . Why the fire of Gehinnom ? If it purifies, fine; but endless suffering, why? Rabbeinu Yonah writes, and the Rabbi took this as Saadia's point too: if there were nothing to fear, there would be no incentive. If doing good brings reward and not doing it costs nothing, \"life's good anyway, maybe I'll do it.\" When you know there will be consequences, \"I have to do it.\" 8. How can the Torah execute a human being? (Saadia counts seven questions; this eighth continues the seventh.) If man is so important, how can some sins carry the death penalty ? Saadia: when a limb is poisoned , it is right to amputate it to save the body. So too with the human race. One who commits such a sin has corrupted himself and may corrupt the species, so he is removed so that the rest can continue to live . Saadia ends: any other question you have, think about it and you will find an answer. Everything Hashem does is correct. # Question Saadia's answer 1 Why is man so small? The body is small; the mind is vast 2 Why so short a life? This is the life of work; the life of reward is long and constant 3 Why so feeble a body? It is the best body; any better would be an angel 4 Why illness? To awaken us to teshuvah 5 Why dangers in nature? So we can grasp what punishment means 6 Why cravings? To learn to use them rightly 7 Why punishment, not just loss? Fear gives the incentive to choose rightly 8 Why execution? Amputating a poisoned limb to save the body Summary Essay 4 is about free will , and Saadia is the first to treat it systematically. The famous question of foreknowledge comes in Chapter 4. The title names obedience and disobedience, but those are really Essay 5's subject. This essay is about compulsion and justice : reward and punishment are just only if we choose freely. The introduction argues that the centre is always most important (seed, yolk, heart, pupil) and the earth is the centre , so man is the purpose of creation. The Ibn Ezra rejects every proof; the Rambam (Moreh 3:13) says the cosmos was not needed for man and in the end it was Hashem's will . Today, with the earth a speck in a vast universe, Saadia's premise is plainly wrong, but the essay's discussion of free will stands either way. Chapter 1: man's advantage is wisdom , so he receives the commandments and the power to choose : \"choose life.\" \"Not my arrogance,\" says Saadia. Chapter 2: eight questions on man's greatness, from his small body to the death penalty, each answered. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 3 and some of Chapter 4: the dynamics of free will, and then the contradiction between free will and foreknowledge . Tomorrow night: the Tuesday shiur on the Rambam's Hilchot Teshuvah, Chapter 5, on free will.","MALJW3MSBko":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 3 (Commandment and Prohibition), Chapter 10, questions 4 to 12 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 4 November 2024, on Zoom (the first shiur after the Sukkot break, the week of Parashat Lech Lecha) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where Essay 3 Sits The Rabbi welcomed everyone back from the break to the winter schedule : 6 to 7 pm, hopefully mostly in person as well as on Zoom. Before starting Essay 4, the last chapter of Essay 3 had to be finished. Essay Theme 1 The Creator : the concept of a Creator at all 2 The Jewish Creator : Hashem's unity, anthropomorphism in the Torah, the ways we describe Hashem 3 Revelation : rational and revelatory mitzvot; prophecy, how a prophet is verified (Saadia's approach and the Rambam's), and false prophets; and, in Chapters 7 to 9, abrogation : can the Torah be changed, superseded or swapped? Chapter 10. Since the essay is about revelation, and Tanach is the pinnacle of revelation, Saadia answers twelve questions people ask about whether the Torah is rational. They come from the 200 objections of Chivi al-Balkhi . Before the break we saw the first three: why the Torah is not clear and needs interpretation, and numbers that seem to contradict . Tonight, questions four to twelve, \"some of them very interesting in terms of his understanding of the mitzvah vis-à-vis the other Rishonim.\" Question 4: Sacrifices Someone may be troubled by the sacrifices : that animals are slaughtered, and that we offer up their blood and fat . How is this fair to the animal? \"Something which people have been asking for many years, but he was the first person to articulate this question. It's fascinating,\" eleven hundred years ago. Slaughter in place of death Hashem decreed that every creature dies . Humans have a fixed lifespan . An animal's lifespan is until it is slaughtered . Shechitah stands in place of death. Is slaughter more painful than dying naturally? Only Hashem knows. \"I don't know if that's true or not.\" If it is true , and if it can be proven scientifically , then the animals will be compensated for that pain. Nothing in the Torah indicates compensation for animals, but if it were proven, Saadia would have to concede it. Why the blood and the fat? The soul is sustained by the blood. When we see the blood sprinkled and the fat burnt, we say to ourselves that we will sin no more, so that our own blood will not be spilt and our fat burnt as we are seeing before us. The Rabbi noted that this second idea is recorded by many Rishonim, notably the Ramban on the Torah: seeing it arouses a feeling of guilt , \"really this should be happening to me,\" and so it becomes a way of teshuvah . The animal takes our place, and that brings us back to Hashem. The Rambam against compensation for animals \"What's fascinating about this is that Saadia seems to be indicating a system of compensation for animals , which we don't find anywhere in Tanach or in our literature.\" The Rambam did not like it at all, and in the Moreh Nevuchim he referred to Saadia without naming him: Our Torah deals only with human situations. Compensation for animals has never been heard in our faith from ancient times, and none of our Rabbis spoke of it. One of the later Geonim heard it from the Arab philosophers, the Mu'tazilites , thought it correct, and adopted it. In Saadia's defence: he never says categorically that animals are compensated, only that if slaughter were proven more painful, he would have to accept some compensation. \"But the Rambam is right.\" And in the Rambam's philosophy it is completely alien: there is no individual providence ( hashgachah peratit ) over animals , only general providence ( hashgachah kelalit ) over the species. The Rabbi noted that the Ramchal in Derech Hashem, which the class had learned, says the same. \"So this would make absolute nonsense for the Rambam.\" Question 5: Why the Shechinah Among Humans and Not Angels? The question is not why Hashem rests His Shechinah among humans; that is fine, He wants to give us the ability to build His presence. The question is why among humans and not among the angels . \"Simple answer. Who told you the angels don't have the Shechinah among them? \" It is quite possible Hashem rests it among them many times more. Saadia brings the verse: אל נערץ בסוד קדשים רבה, ונורא על כל סביביו God is revered in the great council of the holy ones, and awesome over all who surround Him. (Tehillim 89:8) \"All who surround Him\" are the angels. So it is not a question. Question 6: Does Hashem Need a Mishkan? Someone is astonished by building a Mishkan for God : people playing music for Him, baking for Him, offering fragrant incense, flour, oil, wine and bikkurim . Does Hashem need all this? \"We've had this many times, but the revolutionary thing is that Saadia is the first one saying something of this kind.\" All of these are ways of service, not things God needs. Reason tells you Hashem needs nothing; rather, everything needs Him. We serve Him with the best humans have , \"at least in those days\": meat, wine, salt, flour, frankincense, oil and sweet fruit. Bring a little, and Hashem repays far more. Honour Hashem from your wealth, and your storehouses will be filled with plenty (Mishlei 3). Offer to God thanksgiving, pay your vows, and call on Me in the day of trouble; I will deliver you (Tehillim 50). We honour Him in the Mishkan, the place called the Shechinah's dwelling , with gold, silver, precious stones, scarlet and fine linen, and Hashem compensates us by revealing Himself there: ונעדתי שמה , \"I will meet with you there.\" There He hears our prayers. By creating the Mishkan, we create a space where we can connect with the divine and develop the relationship. \"Not, chas veshalom, that He needs it.\" Question 7: Brit Milah \"Which is actually this week's parashah . And his answer is interesting.\" How can a person created by Hashem be born incomplete , so that only by cutting off the foreskin does he become complete? Surely cutting something off makes you less perfect, not more. Saadia: removing the extra Something complete is something with nothing extra. Hashem created the foreskin as an addition to the body . We are born incomplete because we have too much , and removing it makes us complete. The Rambam: weakening the limb The Rambam ( Moreh Nevuchim 3:49 ) rejects this, again without naming Saadia: \"Any person who thinks has a simple question\": how can natural things be imperfect, and become perfect only when we intervene? Surely nature is perfect and intervention makes it imperfect. It was also clear to the Rambam that the foreskin has a benefit , being a protection. So removing it cannot be perfecting an imperfection. The real purpose: to complete a deficiency not in the body but in our character . Milah weakens the limb , not so much that one cannot have children, but enough to subdue excessive passion and desire . It is symbolic of restraining oneself . His proof , as a doctor: circumcision weakens the organ and lessens enjoyment, \"there's no doubt to me.\" And he brings the Hachamim: a woman who has had relations with an uncircumcised man finds it hard to separate from him. Why Avraham first? Because Avraham was the epitome of modesty . As we read this week, coming to Egypt he tells Sarah, הנה נא ידעתי כי אשה יפת מראה את , \" Behold, now I know that you are beautiful.\" The Hachamim read it: only now, approaching a dangerous place, did he really look at her. A Midrash for Saadia: the five foreskins \"In Saadia's defence, there is a Midrash which says exactly what Saadia says\": Midrash Tanchuma on this week's parashah. Hashem tells Avraham, התהלך לפני והיה תמים , \"walk before Me and be complete.\" Avraham is astonished: if I circumcise myself I will be deficient ! Hashem answers: you think you are complete now? You are missing five things. There are five foreskins : Foreskin Verse The literal one The ear \"their ear is uncircumcised; they cannot listen\" The heart \"circumcise the foreskin of your heart\" (Parashat Ekev) The lips \"uncircumcised of lips\" The flesh And the gematria: אברם (Avram) is 243 , but a human has 248 limbs . With the milah the five \"foreskins\" are removed, the letter ה is added, and אברהם is 248 . \"Now you've got a proper ear, a proper tongue. Now you will become perfect.\" Not all Midrashim are equal \"This is important to recognise.\" Midrash Tanchuma is not a Midrash from the time of the Talmud. Midrash Rabbah (Bereishit Rabbah, Shemot Rabbah, Vayikra Rabbah) comes from the Tanna'im and Amora'im. Tanchuma, like Midrash Shocher Tov , is much later: the Rabbi looked it up during the shiur and gave somewhere between the 8th and 10th centuries . So the Rambam was not bound by it. \"It's a beautiful Midrash, it fits in beautifully, but it doesn't bother the Rambam.\" \"Do not be fooled by hearing the word Midrash . When you hear the word Midrash, you always have to understand where the Midrash is from .\" From the class Chaim asked whether, with the metaphorical foreskins, removing them makes you stronger . The Rabbi: according to Saadia and the Tanchuma, yes , all of them make you better, more wholesome and more complete. That is exactly what the Rambam rejects for the physical milah: you bleed, you lose a protection. A participant suggested hygiene . \"No one says that, and the Rishonim don't say that, but of course it's a benefit.\" Whether it was the reason for the mitzvah is debatable, but it could be added as a modern reason. Question 8: The Red Heifer The parah adumah purifies the impure , yet those who prepare and administer it, who must be pure to do so, become impure . \"This whole essay is about the rationality of the Torah.\" The question is not how ashes purify. It is how one thing can have opposite effects . Saadia: it is not outside our experience. Fire melts lead, yet solidifies milk. Water makes most things moist, yet apparently makes the sycamore tree drier. \"If anyone can elaborate or explain this, I'll be glad to hear.\" Food is good when hungry and bad when full. Medicine is good when ill and bad when well. The Rabbi was not convinced. In all these examples, the substance is the same and the state of the person differs . That is why food helps the hungry and harms the full. But if the ashes have the power to make the impure pure, why should they make the pure impure? \"I don't fully appreciate this answer.\" The Ibn Ezra quotes Saadia with the example of honey , bad for some and good for others, \"and sorry, I don't really think it's correct.\" Question 9: Azazel \"Now we come to something which we dealt with recently, and this is fascinating. Really fascinating. \" On Yom Kippur we read Acharei Mot: two goats, one offered in the Temple and one sent to Azazel . People think Azazel is some kind of demon . Are we offering sacrifices to demons, to other gods? Saadia: Azazel is a place No. Azazel is the name of a place, a mountain , like other names in Tanach ending in el . Sending the goat la'azazel simply tells you where it was sent. \"What's the obvious question? They all have el in them, but Azazel is different.\" The Ibn Ezra raises the linguistic problems. One goat is offered in the Temple, atoning for the Kohanim who serve there . The other atones for the people outside the Temple , ordinary Israel, so it is sent to a nearby place called Azazel. Why the lots? People thought the lots were cast because the goats went to two different deities . No: both are sacrifices to the same God . The lots simply make clear which is for whom. \"This to me is the strangest thing, casting lots. It sounds very demonic.\" And of Saadia's answer: \"Not a very convincing answer.\" The Gemara and Rashi: a fierce cliff The Gemara (Yoma) reads עזאזל as עז וקשה , strong and hard. Rashi on the Torah: הר עז וקשה, צוק גבוה , a formidable mountain, a high cliff, sheer, not a slope. The Rishonim did not take it as the simple meaning, since the word har , mountain, is missing. The Ibn Ezra's hint and the Ramban's revelation The Ibn Ezra brings Saadia's view and the difficulties with it, then gives no answer of his own, only a hint: \"I will reveal to you a little of the secret by a hint: when you are thirty-three, you will understand it.\" \"Ibn Ezra does this sometimes, a few places in the Torah, when he wants to tell you something too controversial to say outright.\" (The Rabbi added that he should one day do a series on the Ibn Ezra's hints, \"it's unbelievable the kinds of things he was referring to,\" and what scholars have debated about each.) The Ramban quotes him: ורבי אברהם נאמן רוח מכסה דבר, ואני הרכיל מגלה סודו Rabbi Avraham, being of faithful spirit, conceals the matter; and I, the talebearer , will reveal his secret. The Ramban brings Midrashim: we give Samael , the forces of evil, a bribe , so that he will not interfere with the offering to Hashem in the Temple. Samael sees it and says: this nation is like the angels. As the angels are barefoot, they are barefoot; as the angels do not eat or drink, they do not eat or drink on Yom Kippur. The thirty-three. The Rabbi counted it out on the screen: count 33 verses from the first mention of Azazel in Acharei Mot. The 33rd verse, already past the Yom Kippur service, forbids offering sacrifices outside the Temple: ולא יזבחו עוד את זבחיהם לשעירם They shall no longer offer their sacrifices to the goat-demons . (Vayikra 17:7) That is the Ibn Ezra's hint: once you are past 33 verses, you will understand what Azazel is. For the Ibn Ezra and the Ramban, one offering goes to Hashem, and so that the se'irim , the forces of evil, do not hijack it , Azazel is given \"his taste.\" Azazel is... Saadia A place near the Temple; both goats are for Hashem; the lots only assign them The Gemara and Rashi A hard, sheer cliff The Ibn Ezra (hinted) and the Ramban A portion given to the forces of evil , a \"bribe\" to Samael \"Saadia is very monotheistic. Let me rephrase that, not that the others aren't.\" But Saadia, and the Rambam, who \"would never, ever accept an interpretation like this,\" cannot accept offering anything to another force, so for Saadia it must be a place. \"How to understand the Ibn Ezra and the Ramban is really difficult, to be honest. But that's perhaps a discussion for another time.\" Philippe asked where this place was. The Rabbi explained that for Saadia it is a cliff just outside, very near the Temple, from which the goat was thrown down into the wilderness, and it was called Azazel. Question 10: The Eglah Arufah When a murdered person is found outside a town and no one knows who killed him , the elders take a calf, break its neck by a stream, and that ground may never be worked again. The Torah says no one knows who struck him . So why do the elders, who did no sin , bring this offering? The purpose of the eglah arufah Saadia Atonement for the elders. One can be punished for passivity as well as action. Had there been more security guards and officers, they would have known who committed this murder. They lose the calf and the use of the field The Rambam \"Much simpler, and classic Rambam.\" It is a strange public act : measuring to the nearest town, the elders, the calf, the barren valley. People will talk about it , and whoever knows the murderer, or something about him, will come forward. As soon as the killer is found, the procedure stops. \"There's no atonement here\" The Abarbanel Atonement for the people, and more. A calf that has never borne young or been worked, whose potential is cut off, should shock us into remembering that a human being with his whole life ahead of him was killed. Why are we not shaken? It drives us to find the killer and to improve society so it never happens again Question 11: Why Is the People of God So Downtrodden? The Jewish people, the nation of God, seem the most downtrodden. \"Look at us nowadays: how much international pressure on Israel, how much Jews across the world are being persecuted.\" This, the Rabbi said, was a common Christian argument : you are always persecuted by us, so how can you be God's people? Saadia's answer: If Hashem made us always sovereign and in power , the other religions would say we only serve Hashem because life is good for us , just as the Satan said of Iyov : he has everything, that is why he serves You. They would also excuse themselves: we do not serve God because we lack the Jews' luck. Instead He lowered us and exiled us, and we did not give up our faith , and so in the long run we are deserving of Hashem's justice and mercy. לא נסוג אחור לבנו ותט אשורינו מני ארחך Our heart has not turned back, nor have our steps strayed from Your path. (Tehillim 44:19) Question 12: Why No Olam HaBa in the Torah? \"A huge question.\" Why does the Torah mention only reward in this world and never the World to Come? \"Great question. Two-line answer. What's going to be his answer?\" Saadia: I have written a whole essay on this, Essay 9 , and I will explain everything necessary there. End of Essay 3. Summary: The Twelve Questions of Chapter 10 # Question Answer 1 The Torah is unclear Intellect and tradition complete it (before the break) 2-3 Contradictory numbers Reconciled (before the break) 4 Sacrifices, blood and fat They awaken us to teshuvah; if shechitah is more painful, animals may be compensated (the Rambam rejects this) 5 Shechinah among humans, not angels Who says not? Perhaps far more among the angels 6 Does Hashem need a Mishkan? No; it is for us, to build the relationship 7 Milah Saadia and the Tanchuma: removing the extra makes us complete. The Rambam: to weaken desire, a lesson in restraint 8 The red heifer One thing can have opposite effects (the Rabbi was unconvinced) 9 Azazel Saadia: a place. The Gemara and Rashi: a sheer cliff. The Ibn Ezra and the Ramban: a portion for the forces of evil 10 The eglah arufah Saadia: atonement for the elders' negligence. The Rambam: publicity to catch the killer. The Abarbanel: shock us into fixing society 11 Israel's suffering So no one can say we serve Hashem only because life is good 12 No Olam HaBa in the Torah See Essay 9 Coming Up: Essay 4 Next week, same time: Essay 4, on free will . A relatively short essay of six chapters. Saadia is the first to ask the famous question of free will and Hashem's foreknowledge , which comes in Chapters 3 and 4. He also discusses the status of man in the universe , and there will be a big debate among the Rishonim over whether man is indeed the centre of the universe.","9livl44uUG4":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 3 (Commandment and Prohibition), the end of Chapter 8, Chapter 9 and the opening of Chapter 10 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 14 October 2024, on Zoom (the last shiur before Sukkot) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are in Essay 3 So far in the essay Saadia has explained: Two categories of mitzvot , the rational and the revelatory. The prophets : why we need them, and how they are verified, both to others and to themselves. The three-part structure of Tanach : command, consequence and narrative. Chapter 7: can the Torah be abrogated? Saadia's firm opinion is no . He brings sources he believes prove it, \"but as we saw last time, it's not so straightforward\": Rav Yosef Albo in Sefer HaIkkarim perhaps dissented, and other sources suggest things may be different in the time of Mashiach. He also refuted seven arguments for bitul (abrogation). Chapter 8 began: what if a prophet tries to take us away from the Torah with miracles ? Miracles normally verify a prophet, but even a miracle does not trump our intellect and logic . \"Even miracles have their limits.\" Tonight: the rest of Chapter 8 (three verses), Chapter 9 (ten questions and two further points), and a start on Chapter 10. Part 1: Chapter 8, Three Verses Having dealt with logic, Saadia turns to three verses commonly brought in his time to prove the Torah was meant to change. \"The third one is the most famous, which he spends the least time on.\" 1. \"From Sinai, from Seir, from Paran\" \"Timely,\" the Rabbi said: it comes from Vezot HaBerachah . ה׳ מסיני בא וזרח משעיר למו הופיע מהר פארן Hashem came from Sinai, shone forth to them from Seir, appeared from Mount Paran. (Devarim 33:2) Saadia does not spell out the claim, but the Rabbi explained it: three revelations. Sinai is the Torah. Seir was read as Jesus and Christianity. Paran was read as Muhammad . Saadia says no: these are three names for the same place . A mountain is called by different names by the different towns around it , just as a sea is called by different names in different countries. Mount Sinai is next to a place called Sinai, and Seir and Paran are right next to it . Sinai and Paran adjoin: the Israelites travelled from the wilderness of Sinai and the cloud rested in the wilderness of Paran. Seir and Paran adjoin: in Bereishit, the Chori in their hills of Seir, as far as El Paran. Seir is Sinai: in the Song of Devorah, \"Hashem, when You went out from Seir ...\" and the next verse says \" this is Sinai .\" So the verse describes one revelation, Matan Torah, from three angles , called Sinai by us and Seir and Paran by other nations. The remaining difficulty: Chavakuk. אלוה מתימן יבוא וקדוש מהר פארן God will come from Teiman, the Holy One from Mount Paran. (Chavakuk 3:3) Chavakuk lived before Muhammad, so if Paran means Islam the future tense fits very well. But for Saadia, Paran is Sinai, already past. Why \" will come\"? Saadia's answer is about Hebrew usage. A constant action can be described in the past or the future. In Tehillim, the sins in the desert are told in the past (\"they rebelled at the Sea of Reeds,\" \"they craved\") and then, a few verses on, in the future (\"the earth will open and swallow\"). If someone does good to you constantly, you might say \"he will do good to me.\" The Rabbi understood it so: the revelation at Sinai continues to reverberate through the generations , so the prophet speaks of it in the future. The Rabbi added that the Ibn Ezra on this verse in Vezot HaBerachah quotes Saadia and says exactly the same. 2. \"A messenger sent among the nations\" וציר בגוים שלח, קומו ונקומה עליה למלחמה A messenger has been sent among the nations: rise up, let us rise against her in battle. (Ovadiah 1:1) \"Quite technical.\" The Christians read the messenger as Jesus , sent to wage war on paganism. Saadia says the messenger is a prophet , and the verse refers to the war in the days of King Yehoshafat , when the surrounding nations attacked. The king prayed, and Hashem sent the prophet Yachaziel , who told them to stand firm and watch Hashem's salvation. \"Rise up for war\" meant rise up in prayer : Yehoshafat appointed people to sing and praise Hashem, and they were saved from the enemy. \"A very strong argument, and it's clear that's the intention of the pasuk.\" 3. \"A new covenant\" הנה ימים באים נאם ה׳ וכרתי את בית ישראל ואת בית יהודה ברית חדשה, לא כברית אשר כרתי את אבותם... אשר המה הפרו את בריתי Behold, days are coming, says Hashem, when I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel and the house of Yehudah, not like the covenant I made with their fathers... which they broke. (Yirmiyahu 31) \"How ridiculous,\" says Saadia. You bring me the verse, but you don't bother to look at the next one: \" I will put My Torah within them ,\" this same Torah, and they will no longer teach each other bad things. And then: as surely as there is a sun, a moon and stars, these laws will never cease . So what is new about the covenant? Only that this time it will not be broken. The earlier covenants were broken; Israel did not keep its side. The new covenant is new in the sense of refreshed : in the times of Mashiach Israel will keep its side of the pact as Hashem keeps His. Part 2: Chapter 9, Ten Questions \"I want to try to finish both chapters tonight, so we can finish the essay and then take a break until after Sukkot.\" Chapter 7 gave the logical arguments against abrogation; Chapter 8, the verses. Chapter 9 takes questions people ask that seem to show Hashem changing His commands , and if the Torah changed in the past, perhaps it will change again, perhaps there is a New Testament or a Quran. Saadia says there are many such questions. He will answer ten , and \"look at the technique I'm answering\": with it, you can answer all the others. 1. Adam's children married their sisters \"Really interesting. I had actually never thought of this until I read this.\" Adam and Chava had children. Whom did those children marry , to give Adam grandchildren? They must have married their sisters. Later Hashem forbade it. Abrogation? Saadia: marrying your sister was always forbidden , even before the Torah. The proof is Avraham : going down to Avimelech, or to Pharaoh, he asks Sarah to say \"you are my sister\" so that they will not kill him. That only works if everyone knew people did not marry their sisters . Otherwise he should still fear for his life, since she might be his sister and his wife. So how could Adam's children? Because they had no choice. There were no other humans. Once there were, the dispensation no longer applied: Someone ill eats on Yom Kippur . If he feels better halfway through the day, he cannot keep eating because he already broke his fast; the reason has gone, so he fasts again. Someone in the desert may eat non-kosher food when there is nothing else. The moment he has kosher food and is no longer starving, the excuse is gone. \"What I don't understand is who said this prohibition, and where. Who told humanity at the beginning you can't marry your sister? That's Saadia's understanding.\" And: \"We read the story every year and I never paid attention to it.\" 2. Kayin was not executed Kayin killed Hevel and was punished with wandering , yet later the Torah makes murder a capital crime . Saadia: there was no change. The death penalty applies only with witnesses and a court to try him. Kayin had neither, so Hashem gave him a different punishment. \"Surprising and interesting,\" the Rabbi said: according to Saadia, long before the Torah, the law was already that a murderer is put to death given witnesses and a court . The Rabbi raised a difficulty from the Gemara in Ketubot : although the four capital punishments of the court have ceased, the judgment of the four deaths has not ceased . Hashem brings about the death of one who is liable, even without witnesses. So why did Hashem not tell Kayin his punishment was death? A participant suggested that perhaps Kayin did die early, since we do not know how he died. The Rabbi agreed we don't know, but \"then why does Hashem not just say that to him?\" What Hashem told him was נע ונד תהיה בארץ , you will be a wanderer in the land: a different punishment. 3. Who may offer sacrifices? \"This also I wasn't fully clear on.\" Before Vayikra, apparently anyone could offer sacrifices; afterwards, only Aharon and his descendants . Saadia: it was never anyone at all. Before Aharon too it had to be an appointed person , someone with that role. \"Who appointed this person? Where do we find it in the Torah? I can't understand this.\" Mark suggested from the class that the firstborn held that position, and it was transferred because of what they did. It was pointed out that the firstborn's role was transferred to the Levi'im , and the Torah counts the exchange. The Rabbi agreed it was a fair point: before, the appointed person may have been the firstborn , someone of importance; afterwards it is the Kohen. It has always been someone appointed. 4. Sacrifices on Shabbat The Torah forbids work on Shabbat, yet sacrifices were offered on Shabbat. Saadia turns it on its head: it proves the opposite. Sacrifices were offered before Shabbat was given , so Hashem did not abrogate them and let them continue. The same with brit milah on the eighth day, commanded long before the Torah, and still done on Shabbat. The Torah prohibited things that were never commanded before; what was already commanded continues. 5. The Akeidah Hashem told Avraham to offer Yitzchak, then told him not to harm him. Saadia: \"a completely incorrect question. Even people who believe in abrogation can't use it.\" Abrogation means a law is given, fulfilled , then replaced; here nothing was fulfilled . Rather, Hashem revealed at the end what He meant from the start: \"bring him up.\" That's it. Once Avraham brought him up and prepared the knife and the wood, he had done what he was told. \"That was always the intention of Hashem.\" 6. Bilam: \"don't go\" and then \"go\" Rashi asks this on the Torah. Hashem told Bilam not to go with Balak's messengers, then a few verses later told him to go. Answer Rashi Hashem advised him not to go, but seeing how much he wanted to, said: if you really want to, go. As with the spies : \"my advice is not to go; if you want to, you can.\" Not a change Saadia Two different groups. \"Don't go\" meant not with this group . Balak then sent more honourable messengers, and Hashem said go with these , so that it would be reported that Israel was saved even from someone as important as Balak, who sent his whole cabinet to fetch Bilam, and still could not defeat them 7. Chizkiyahu Hashem told Chizkiyahu he would die, and then gave him fifteen more years . Saadia: that is how Hashem works. He warns and decrees, and if you do teshuvah , the decree is revoked. The Rabbi linked it to the Rambam's rule from a few weeks earlier: a prophet's failed prediction of bad is not yet proof he is false, because the people may have repented, as with Yonah , read on Yom Kippur the week before, whose warning to Nineveh was annulled when they did teshuvah. 8. The Levi'im replace the firstborn \"Mark, this touches what you said.\" Saadia: a king promotes his subjects , and if they do not do their job well, they are demoted. Hashem gave Adam paradise and then expelled him; He brought us to the Land and then exiled us. So too the firstborn had the privilege and lost it. 9. Yericho on Shabbat \"This is very interesting.\" The Israelites circled Yericho for seven days, and people assumed the seventh day, when the walls fell, was Shabbat : war on Shabbat. Saadia says that is a wrong assumption . One of the seven days was Shabbat, and on it they circled the city and blew the shofar (biblically there is nothing wrong with blowing shofar, and apparently carrying the Ark was allowed). But the destruction on the seventh day happened on a weekday . Nowhere does it say the seventh day was Shabbat. \"The problem is, either he didn't have this Yerushalmi in front of him...\" The Yerushalmi says explicitly that a commanded war may be fought even on Shabbat, because Yericho was conquered on Shabbat . That is what all the Rishonim say and how the halacha is decided: milchemet chovah , an obligatory war, may be waged on Shabbat. Saadia either did not know it, or was bothered by the question of abrogation, and he answered differently. 10. The direction of prayer First we prayed towards the Mishkan , then towards the Temple . Hashem changed His mind? No. We were always commanded to pray towards the Ark . The Ark was in different places, so we faced different directions, but the point never changed . # Question Saadia's answer 1 Adam's children married their sisters No choice; always forbidden 2 Kayin not executed No witnesses or court 3 Sacrifices: anyone, then only Aharon Always an appointed person 4 Sacrifices on Shabbat Commanded before Shabbat, so continued (like milah) 5 The Akeidah \"Bring him up\", and he did 6 Bilam Not this group; the next one 7 Chizkiyahu's fifteen years Teshuvah revokes a decree 8 Levi'im for the firstborn Promotion and demotion 9 Yericho on Shabbat The conquest was on a weekday 10 Direction of prayer Always towards the Ark Part 3: What Does Olam Mean? In Chapter 7 Saadia used olam to prove eternity: חקת עולם , an everlasting statute. People object that olam can mean three things: fifty years ; the lifespan of the generation addressed ; or everlasting . Saadia replies that Yirmiyahu , about 850 years after Moshe, still commanded keeping Shabbat. That is long after fifty years and long after Moshe's generation, so olam there means everlasting . \"I have no clue what he's talking about here. I tried to discover what he meant, but I don't understand.\" The question was about the Hebrew servant , whose ear is pierced and who serves le'olam , which the Hachamim read as until the Yovel. Shabbat proves Shabbat; how does that answer the servant? The Rabbi's best explanation: although olam alone can mean fifty years, the phrase chukat olam , an everlasting law , is used of Shabbat, and there it plainly means forever. So every chukat olam means forever . \"That's the only way I have to explain it.\" \"Perhaps you misunderstood the tradition\" How can you be sure the tradition meant eternity? This, the Rabbi reminded the class, is the skeptic of Essay 1, one of the thirteen views Saadia argued against. Saadia's reply: is there anything in the world certain to you? If he says no, he is simply a skeptic, and \"I don't talk to such people.\" If he says yes, then we answer that we know with certainty the law was given, and the tradition, the kabbalah with which Saadia opened Chapter 7, is that it is for eternity. Part 4: The Brahmins and Adam The last part of the chapter concerns the Brahmins , a Hindu sect from around 500 BCE, long before Saadia. People argued from them: Adam permitted wearing shatnez , ploughing with an ox and a donkey together, and (in the Rabbi's edition) meat and milk . If laws never change, why did the Torah forbid them? \"I don't understand these people,\" says Saadia. The Brahmins don't even say this. They say Adam allowed these things, and we agree: before the Torah, they were optional. Forbidding what was optional is not abrogation. And if a Brahmin now claimed that Adam commanded them to do these things, \"that's nonsense.\" That was never their tradition: why did you not say so yesterday? Saadia's closing inspiration Saadia ends the chapter with a challenge. I have worked so hard, gone through all the verses, all the logic and all the questions, to prove to you that the Torah will not be abrogated . Will you not now put in the effort to keep it ? Will all my effort be in vain? Chapters 7-9 in brief: Chapter 7, the tradition, some verses and logical reasons. Chapter 8, the verses from Tanach that seem to prove abrogation. Chapter 9, the ten questions, chukat olam , and the Brahmins. Saadia's conclusion: there is no proof that the Torah can be abrogated. Part 5: Chapter 10, Twelve Questions from Chivi al-Balkhi Chapter 10 is not about abrogation . Since the essay is about the Torah and its commandments, Saadia goes through twelve general questions that trouble people so much they say they cannot believe in the Torah. \"Some of them are quite strong ones.\" Who was Chivi al-Balkhi? These twelve are part of 200 questions raised by Chivi al-Balkhi , a biblical critic . It is not clear whether he was a Jew or a Christian. He offered 200 objections to the divine origin of the Bible, and Saadia was very upset that people were learning his books. Saadia wrote a book of responses, as we learned in his Introduction, but it is lost. Here he chose twelve, presumably the strongest , \"and he's saying, if I don't deal with them, people will be confused and may come to heresy.\" \"He's very confident, as we see over and over, that his explanations are the right ones.\" Saadia first summarises what he has done: abrogation; why prophets live like other humans, so as to show the prophet is a normal person; and in the earlier essays the Creator , against anthropomorphism , and the attributes we use of Hashem. \"If I didn't do this, people would become heretics.\" Before Essay 4, twelve more things must be cleared up. Question 1: the Torah is unclear What is the point of a book in which so many mitzvot have no explanation ? Why adhere to an incomplete, unclear book? Saadia: the Five Books are not the only source of our law. There are two other springs: Spring When The spring of intellect Before the Torah The Torah itself The spring of tradition After the Torah was given Whatever is unclear in the Torah, intellect and tradition together complete it, in quality and in quantity . Question 2: contradictions in the numbers Saadia, the Rabbi said, is one of the first to discuss this kind of biblical criticism; critics would later claim two different editions were put together. In Shmuel , Yoav's census counts 800,000 men of war in Israel. In Divrei HaYamim it counts 1,100,000 . A discrepancy of 300,000 . (Mark helped with the arithmetic in the shiur: elef alafim u'me'ah elef is a million and a hundred thousand .) Saadia's answer, \"straight out from Chazal\": 800,000 is the standing army . 300,000 are reservists . A verse elsewhere says 24,000 served each month : 24,000 x 12 = 288,000 . The remaining 12,000 came from the princes , the nesi'im , and their families. 288,000 + 12,000 = 300,000. \"Actually fits in beautifully.\" The Rabbi connected this to the rule we say every morning in Shacharit among Rabbi Yishmael's thirteen principles , and found in the 32 principles of Rabbi Eliezer son of Rabbi Yose HaGelili : when two verses contradict each other, a third verse comes and reconciles them . The proof brought for it is these very verses , with the verse of the 24,000 as the third. \"The third one is a really strong one, and actually I spent quite some time looking into this. But that we will have to leave for next time.\" Summary Three verses (Chapter 8): Sinai, Seir and Paran are one mountain with three names (Chavakuk's future tense describes a revelation that keeps reverberating); the messenger among the nations is the prophet Yachaziel in Yehoshafat's day; the new covenant is the same Torah, written within them, that this time will not be broken. Ten questions (Chapter 9): each apparent change is no change: dispensation (Adam's children), missing witnesses (Kayin), an appointed person (sacrifices), prior commands continuing (sacrifices and milah on Shabbat), a command fulfilled (\"bring him up\"), different messengers (Bilam), teshuvah (Chizkiyahu), promotion and demotion (the Levi'im), a weekday conquest (Yericho, against the Yerushalmi), and one constant direction (the Ark). Chukat olam means forever, as with Shabbat 850 years after Moshe; the skeptic who doubts the tradition doubts everything. The Brahmins only say Adam permitted these things, which is no abrogation. Saadia's appeal: after all my effort to prove it, put in the effort to keep it . Chapter 10 begins Chivi al-Balkhi's questions: the Torah is completed by intellect and tradition , and the census numbers reconcile through reservists serving monthly . Coming Up A break for Sukkot. The shiur resumes after Sukkot, most likely on the Monday after Shabbat Bereishit , to finish Essay 3 with the rest of Chapter 10. Then Essay 4 , on our worship of Hashem: Essay 3 was the more philosophical side of the mitzvot; Essay 4 is more about how to serve Hashem . The Rabbi wished everyone Chag Sameach .","2ZMqnHs8_m4":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 3 (Commandment and Prohibition), Chapter 7 and the opening of Chapter 8 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 7 October 2024, on Zoom (an early shiur, on the anniversary of 7 October and the week of Yom Kippur) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Essay 3 So Far Chapters Theme 1-3 Rational and revelatory mitzvot , with reasons for some of each. Even the rational ones needed the Torah, to give a framework : adultery is wrong, but what constitutes a married woman? 4 Prophecy is verified through miracles and wonders , and prophets are regular, ordinary people who had occasional prophecy, which is not how the Rambam and others understood it 5 The prophet knows he is prophesying by a physical manifestation : a pillar, a cloud. The Egyptian magicians managed three wonders, but the Torah tells us it was only achizat einayim , sleight of hand, in limited spaces. And Yonah : \"When we read Yonah this Shabbat, on Yom Kippur, bear in mind\" that he did what he was told and fled only before he would be sent a second time 6 Tanach has a threefold structure: command, consequence and narrative . The classic example, again from Yom Kippur, is Acharei Mot: do not enter the Holy of Holies (the command), so that you do not die (the consequence), as Aharon's sons died (the narrative). And the tradition : we live by what others tell us, and the Torah was received by so many people with such incredible claims that it must be true Chapters 7 to 10: one topic The essay has covered the categories of mitzvot, prophecy and how prophets are verified, and the structure of the prophets' writings. The last topic, spread over four chapters, is the abrogation of prophecy . We received the Torah of Moshe, but perhaps it will be changed, \"as many have claimed throughout the generations\": the Christians , who claimed Jesus brought a new prophecy, and the Muslims , who claimed it of Muhammad. The claim is that the Torah changes and adapts to the times. \"We're going to go through them fast.\" Part 1: Chapter 7, Tradition and Verses A reliable tradition Saadia opens: the nation has a complete and reliable tradition that the mitzvot of the Torah will never be changed , and they said they heard this explicitly, to rule out any opposing belief. By tradition he means Chazal : we do have such statements in the Hachamim. The proofs from the verses Saadia then looked for proofs of this tradition and found several: \"For your generations.\" Many mitzvot are commanded לדורותיכם , which he understands to mean in perpetuity. \"A heritage.\" תורה צוה לנו משה מורשה קהלת יעקב Moshe commanded us the Torah, a heritage for the congregation of Yaakov. (Devarim 33:4) The Rabbi explained the word. It does not say yerushah , an inheritance, which is received passively. It says morashah , a bequest: something you receive with a duty to pass it on , as the Gemara draws out. The Torah was inherited and will be bequeathed, which implies the same Torah continuing from generation to generation. \"Our nation is only a nation through its Torah.\" This is the famous line, quoted many times after Saadia: כי אומתנו אינה אומה כי אם בתורותיה Our nation is only a nation through its Torahs. And Hashem promises in Yirmiyahu that Israel will exist as long as heaven and earth : just as I have set the sun, the moon and the stars, if these laws cease, only then will the seed of Israel cease to be a nation. So: Israel lasts as long as heaven and earth; Israel is only a nation through its Torah; therefore the Torah lasts as long as heaven and earth. The last words of the prophets. זכרו תורת משה עבדי... הנה אנכי שלח לכם את אליה הנביא Remember the Torah of Moshe My servant... behold, I am sending you Eliyahu the prophet. (Malachi 3:22-23) The final verses of the Nevi'im: remember and perform the Torah of Moshe until the end of days , when Eliyahu comes. The Rabbi added that in the original Saadia also brings חקת עולם , \"an eternal statute,\" a phrase the Torah uses often. \"They missed it out here,\" in the edition on the screen. The context of the Yirmiyahu verse \"You have to see the context.\" Saadia is clearly referring to the famous Christian proof text , which he addresses explicitly a couple of chapters later. Just before the verses he quotes: הנה ימים באים נאם ה׳ וכרתי את בית ישראל ואת בית יהודה ברית חדשה Behold, days are coming, says Hashem, when I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel and the house of Yehudah. (Yirmiyahu 31) This \"new covenant\" was read as the New Testament . Saadia's answer later is to read on: Not like the covenant I made when I took them out of Egypt, which they broke . Rather, I will write it on their hearts : the same Torah, now engraved within them. And then: as long as these laws of sun, moon and stars endure, so will Israel. So it cannot be that the Torah will be nullified. \"Only a nation through its Torah\": a surprising line The Rabbi noted how this sentence has been used. Anti-Zionists quote it often: you cannot have a nation or a state without religion , because we are a nation not through our land but through our laws. \"Which I find interesting, and honestly surprising and difficult.\" The land came first. Long before the covenant of Torah at Sinai (Parashat Yitro), Hashem made the covenant of the land with Avraham (Parashat Lech Lecha). From Bereishit onwards, again and again: this land will be yours, as a covenant forever. A possible defence: that covenant was with Avraham, not the nation. The first covenant with the nation was at Har Sinai, and it was for the Torah. \"True. But that covenant included, seemingly, the one He made with Avraham.\" The only support the Rabbi could think of is the halacha of conversion . A convert's essential requirement is kabbalat mitzvot , accepting the mitzvot. There is no acceptance of a land. If someone does not truly accept the laws in his heart, even if he says he does, perhaps it is not a conversion. In that sense our nationhood is defined by the Torah. \"We did have a national identity before we had a religious identity. People forget that.\" Part 2: Who Disagreed? Rav Yosef Albo The Rambam's ninth principle is that the Torah will never change. The Rabbi recalled a series on the Thirteen Principles about five or six years ago that discussed it at length. Two issues: some disagreed , and messianic times . Albo: \"forever\" can be limited Rav Yosef Albo , the Spanish philosopher of about 600 years ago, in Sefer HaIkkarim, Ma'amar 3, chapters 13 to 23 , argues with Saadia and the Rambam. He agrees the Torah is eternal in practice , but not that it is impossible for it to change. עולם can mean a limited time. The Hebrew servant whose ear is pierced will \"serve forever,\" and the Gemara interprets that as until the Jubilee year : forever means the \"eternity\" of the Yovel. He brings other proofs too, and refutes the verses that seem to make eternity an imperative. From chapter 16, the Rabbi read: I see no evidence or necessity that the immutability of the law should be a fundamental principle , and nothing to prevent Hashem from adding or diminishing as His wisdom decrees. Albo's example: meat after Kayin and Hevel \"As it were, Hashem changed the Torah.\" Adam was only permitted fruit and vegetation . Meat was permitted only later. Albo's explanation, which the Rabbi called \"a very interesting approach\": Humans need an outlet , a way to spill blood. With no permission to kill animals, there was no distinction: you could kill neither. Hevel brought an animal from his flock and it was accepted. Kayin thought he too would bring a sacrifice of a living creature. When Kayin killed Hevel, it was in his eyes a sacrifice. He equated animal and human. So Hashem drew a clear line: you may kill animals and eat meat; you may not kill humans. That is why, in the same passage that permits meat, the Torah also forbids murder. It is like a doctor who prescribes one medicine for a time and then, once you are a little better, prescribes another. Albo's two conditions How would we know Hashem had prescribed new medicine? A revelation of the same calibre : like the first, before 600,000 men between twenty and sixty, about two million people. Through a prophet greater than Moshe , \"which is almost impossible,\" since we are told Moshe is the greatest of all prophets. He is not suggesting the Torah was changed by Jesus or Muhammad, \"of course not.\" His point is only theoretical. Could the Torah change? Saadia and the Rambam Impossible. The Torah will never change Rav Yosef Albo In theory , given a revelation and a prophet at least equal to Sinai and Moshe Part 3: Messianic Times \"Now the major issue.\" Saadia and the Rambam are both forceful that the Torah will exist as long as heaven and earth. But there are statements in the Hachamim that indicate otherwise . The Yerushalmi: in the future the Prophets and the Writings will be annulled (but not the Five Books). What that means is debated: the mitzvot in them? Their sanctity? Midrash Tehillim 146 , a more explicit source, brought by Albo against Saadia and the Rambam. Midrash Tehillim is of unclear authorship and not from the time of Chazal, perhaps around 700 CE, \"but it is an accepted Midrash, and an authoritative one.\" Every morning in Pesukei deZimra we say ה׳ מתיר אסורים , Hashem releases the imprisoned . The Midrash reads it as \"permits the forbidden\" : in messianic times non-kosher animals will be permitted , and even the prohibition of niddah will be lifted. \"Which is quite shocking.\" \"You could argue this is a Midrash with a deeper interpretation; it doesn't mean these things literally.\" But Albo brought it as a source for his view. Conclusion of Part 1: In effect, everyone agrees the Torah does not change and we do not accept religions that come after it. In theory, Albo allows it; Saadia and the Rambam do not. And there are sources suggesting that in messianic times things may change, whether literally or metaphorically. \"We're talking about tiny nuances in very limited things here, nothing of great significance. It's a more theoretical question.\" From the class: Purim A participant asked about the dictum that when Mashiach comes all the festivals will be annulled except Purim . The Rabbi said it is in that same Yerushalmi . This is why Shabbetai Tzvi , \"obviously a false Mashiach,\" ate on Tisha b'Av and engaged in terrible promiscuity, and the Sabbateans quoted exactly this Midrash of matir asurim . Their excuse was partly Kabbalistic, that to reveal the Messiah one must descend to the depths of impurity to release the sparks buried even there, and partly this idea that in the future prohibitions would become permitted. \"With that mindset, anything can become permissible.\" Why Saadia and the Rambam kept it simple \"I think it's worthwhile dwelling on this for a moment.\" Saadia wrote, as we learned at the start of the year, in response to the people around him : the confusion of the Jewish people and the religions surrounding them. They were arguing about the whole Torah being abrogated. The Rambam's Thirteen Principles , by the consensus of many, were a response to the Christian creeds . We had many beliefs but no creed, so he composed one. When you are trying to make things simple and convince people, you keep things general. The Rambam recognised the dangers of Christianity and Islam . He knew these Midrashim and the Yerushalmi, but \"it's such a small point, it's not worthwhile confusing people with it.\" Saadia may have had the same mindset. Albo wanted to address the issue up front, and so he did. Part 4: Abrogation Is the Wrong Word Back in Saadia, the second part of Chapter 7 is philosophical: the concept of abrogation is not possible . Any commandment must be one of four kinds: If the law was given... Then... 1. For eternity It is for eternity. It cannot be abrogated 2. For a fixed time , say 100 years During the 100 years it cannot change. After that it expires , which is not abrogation 3. For a fixed place , say Egypt or Israel In that place it holds forever; elsewhere it never applied. Not abrogation 4. For as long as a condition holds , say as long as the Nile flows When the Nile stops, it lapses. Not abrogation A fifth possibility: the law was given for a time that we cannot know, namely until the next revelation . Saadia answers that this too is not abrogation, because Hashem knows when that time will be. From His perspective He gave it for, say, 2,000 years. \"Nothing too sharp or convincing. He's just trying to say the word abrogation is not something you need to consider in relation to Torah.\" Part 5: Seven Arguments for Abrogation The rest of the chapter lists seven philosophical arguments people made for abrogation (the arguments from verses come in the next chapter, \"and those we will do inside, because it's important to see the Pesukim and how different religions interpreted them\"). \"Some of them, I have to be honest with you, I don't understand. They're so difficult to accept.\" The Rabbi went through them orally. \"As you can see, these arguments are very, very weak. That's why they're interesting.\" # The argument Saadia's answer 1 Birth and death. People are given life and it is taken away; so too the Torah Hashem gave life with death in view , so that we reach the true life of Olam HaBa . He did not give the Torah in order to abrogate it. If every law were given in order to be abrogated, there would never be a law 2 The dead. When alive they kept the Torah; once dead they don't. So the Torah can lapse The dead cannot keep mitzvot; the living can. You can't compare them 3 Work and rest. People work one day and rest the next; perhaps the Torah has periods of \"rest\" Humans need rest. Nothing says the Torah cannot be kept at all times; it was given for eternity 4 Rich and poor. Fortunes vary; perhaps the Torah applies at some times and not others Fortunes are reward or consequence . The Torah cannot be a reward: the first law was not a reward for anything 5 Dates change colour , red then green, and nature changes. \"Go with the times\" \"This is bizarre, but this is obviously what people used to argue.\" Change is the nature of nature. The Torah need not change 6 Shabbat. Before the Torah you could work on the seventh day; then it was forbidden. Perhaps it will change again Work was optional , never a duty. The Torah abrogated nothing by forbidding it. Only if the future demanded that you must work on Shabbat would it be comparable 7 Avraham and Moshe. There was a Torah of Avraham, then the Torah of Moshe; perhaps a third will come \"The one which has some substance to it.\" The Torah of Moshe never abrogated the Torah of Avraham. It added to it \"We're living a thousand years after Saadia. Clearly the arguments were different in those days.\" Saadia concludes that all of these are flimsy, weak arguments . Part 6: Chapter 8, The Prophet Who Performs Miracles Saadia now faces a hard question from his own position. In Chapters 4 and 5 he said prophecy is verified by miracles . So what if a prophet comes with real miracles , a cloud, a pillar, not sleight of hand, and tells you to change the Torah? \"Don't forget, this is obviously a response to the alleged miracles of Jesus, all the tales that were told.\" The Rabbi pointed out that the question can be asked on the Rambam's approach too. Mark answered from the class: you cannot add and you cannot subtract, so if someone changes something, he may have performed the miracle but he is not a real prophet. The Rabbi agreed that the Torah says such a prophet is to be put to death. Logic trumps miracles \"This is going to show you how at the forefront of everything about Saadia was his logic and his reason.\" Prophets are verified by miracles, yet if a prophet uses miracles to tell you to commit adultery, \"my logic trumps miracles.\" Saadia's answer: we never believed Moshe because of miracles alone. First the prophet tells us how to behave . We hear it, and it makes sense . Then we ask him for a miracle, and when he performs it, we believe him. If what he says is nonsense from the start, we do not ask for a miracle at all , \"because you don't ask a miracle for the impossible.\" The analogy of the court Claim before the Dayan Response Reuven says Shimon owes me a thousand pounds Plausible. \"Give me evidence.\" If he brings witnesses or a contract, Shimon pays Reuven says Shimon owes me a river Impossible, nobody owns rivers. The Dayan does not ask for proof . \"Bye.\" English law has the same idea: a vexatious claim is not accepted in the first place, and the court never asks for evidence. So with prophecy: If he says Hashem commands us to fast on a certain day , we ask for a miracle, and if we see it, we believe and fast. If he says Hashem commanded him to commit adultery, to steal , that Hashem will bring another flood on the whole world, or that He created heaven and earth in one year when the Torah says six days, we will not ask for a miracle . It is against logic or accepted tradition . \"Miracles are great. Miracles are verification. Miracles, however, will never make us do something which is against the actual words of the Torah, because that goes against all logic. God will not send miracles to go against logic. \" Summary Chapters 7-10 deal with one question: can the Torah be abrogated? , against the Christian and Muslim claims of a new revelation. Tradition and verses: ledoroteichem , morashah (a bequest to pass on), \"our nation is only a nation through its Torah\" with Yirmiyahu's promise, and \"Remember the Torah of Moshe\" in Malachi. The \"new covenant\" of Yirmiyahu is the same Torah written on the heart . \"Only a nation through its Torah\" is quoted against Zionism, but the covenant of the land with Avraham came before Sinai. Its best support is kabbalat mitzvot in conversion. Rav Yosef Albo holds abrogation is possible in theory ( olam as a limited time; meat after Kayin and Hevel; the doctor's new prescription), given a Sinai-scale revelation and a prophet greater than Moshe. Sources such as the Yerushalmi and Midrash Tehillim on matir asurim hint at change in messianic times, which the Sabbateans abused. Saadia and the Rambam kept it simple for their audience. Abrogation is the wrong word: a law is eternal, time-bound, place-bound or conditional, and in none of these is it abrogated. Seven weak arguments refuted, the strongest being Avraham to Moshe: Moshe added, he did not abrogate. Chapter 8: a prophet who works miracles to overturn the Torah is not even asked for proof, like a claim to own a river. Logic trumps miracles.","lbzBtIkP6rI":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 3 (Commandment and Prohibition), Chapters 5-6 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 30 September 2024, on Zoom (the last shiur before Rosh Hashanah) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are in Essay 3 The third essay is about the mitzvot . So far: Chapter What Saadia told us Introduction Hashem created the world to bestow goodness ; the mitzvot and staying away from the aveirot are the means to the best life 1-2 Two categories: the rational mitzvot ( sichliyot ), self-understood, and the revelatory mitzvot ( shim'iyot ), which Hashem has to tell us. He gave reasons for some of both 3 Even the rational mitzvot came through prophets, mainly because we need an exact framework . We know adultery is wrong, but what makes a woman married? The Nevi'im and the Torah gave the rational mitzvot their structure 4 A prophet is verified through miracles , \"which was surprising, but that was his approach.\" The Rambam strongly disagreed: the sign is foretelling the future and having it come true. Saadia also stressed that a prophet is a regular human being , unlike the Kuzari , for whom he is almost a kind of angel Tonight: Chapter 5 , in three parts (how the prophet himself knows, the Egyptian magicians, and Yonah, \"topical as well,\" just before the Yamim Noraim), and Chapter 6 , in two parts (the structure of the Torah, and why we can trust the tradition). Part 1: How Does the Prophet Himself Know? Last week was about how we, the public , know a prophet is genuine. But how does the prophet know that what he is experiencing is a real prophecy? The Rabbi admitted he was surprised by the question, since Saadia already told us in Essay 2 about the kavod nivra , the created glory the prophet sees. Perhaps Saadia is asking something sharper: how does the prophet know this isn't a hallucination , or that it doesn't come from the wrong side ? Saadia: a visible sign When the prophecy begins, the prophet sees a clear sign , and it departs when the prophecy ends: A pillar of cloud , or A pillar of fire , or An extraordinary, shining light , not ordinary light. When he sees it he knows for certain that he is now prophesying. Sometimes the public sees it too , which is then a further verification, though that does not always happen. The proof from Ki Tisa When Moshe went to the Tent, the people would look out at a clear sky. As the prophecy began, a pillar of cloud descended , stood at the entrance of the Tent, and Hashem spoke with Moshe. The cloud stayed for as long as the prophecy lasted. והיה כבא משה האהלה ירד עמוד הענן ועמד פתח האהל ודבר עם משה When Moshe entered the Tent, the pillar of cloud would descend and stand at the entrance of the Tent, and He would speak with Moshe. (Shemot 33:9) The Rabbi noted it is peculiar that Saadia quotes the verse before this one and not this main verse. When Moshe came back to tell them the prophecy, the people could say: this is all true, we saw the clear sky, we saw the cloud come down on you, and it stayed exactly as long as you were prophesying. Not only Moshe Moshe was a different kind of prophet, and with the other prophets we do not find a clear sign. But Tehillim shows it applied to all of them: משה ואהרן בכהניו ושמואל בקראי שמו... בעמוד ענן ידבר אליהם Moshe and Aharon among His priests, and Shmuel among those who call His name... in a pillar of cloud He would speak to them. (Tehillim 99:6-7) The Gemara famously equates Shmuel with Moshe and Aharon : Shmuel is the quintessential prophet after Moshe, \"Moshe in his generation, Shmuel in his generation.\" Since we find the cloud with both, in two different generations, all the prophets must have had some such sign. The Ramchal: the prophet feels it in his body The Rabbi set against this the approach of the Ramchal in Derech Hashem , which some of the class had learned with him a few years earlier. \"In a sense you could say that this is what the kavod nivra is really about.\" Prophecy is not essentially a message. We find thousands of prophets in our history, and we have messages from only a handful: Yechezkel, Yirmiyahu, Yehoshua and the other main prophets whose books we have. The essence of prophecy is the deep connection with Hashem while we are still in our bodies . It is rewarding in itself: understanding, developing one's intellect, knowing Hashem. So the body feels it. The whole body trembles. All the prophets apart from Moshe would crumble and fall to the floor when prophecy began, as we see with Eli and the start of Shmuel's prophecy. Fear would envelop them and they would know they were experiencing the divine. How the prophet knows Saadia An external sign : a cloud, fire or light The Ramchal An internal experience : a clear feeling in the body Part 2: Could the Egyptian Magicians Do Miracles? If miracles verify a prophet, Saadia faces an obvious question. The Torah tells us the Egyptian sorcerers also turned water into blood and brought frogs out of the Nile. \"How can I say that miracles are a proof of prophecy, if the Egyptians can do the same?\" Ten against three Moshe performed ten wonders: the staff turned into a snake , and the first nine plagues , in which Moshe was involved, sometimes with Aharon, but leading them. The tenth plague does not count, since Moshe did nothing for it; it simply happened at midnight. The magicians matched only three: the staff into a snake, the blood and the frogs. (The Rabbi corrected himself in the shiur: the Torah is clear that they could not do the lice.) \"Belateihem\": in secret Each time, the Torah says the magicians did the same בלטיהם ( belateihem ). Saadia says this word always means secretly, deceptively . He brings it from the root לט, covering or wrapping: הנה היא לוטה בשמלה אחרי האפוד : the sword of Golyat, \"wrapped in a cloth behind the ephod.\" הלוט הלוט על כל העמים : \"the covering that covers all the nations.\" וילט פניו באדרתו : he wrapped his face in his cloak . Speaking to David בלט , in secret. So the Torah is not comparing them to Moshe but contrasting them. \"Reuven said things which make sense, whereas Shimon said corrupt things.\" Moshe did it as Hashem commanded ; the Egyptians did it in their secret, deceptive way . \"They were playing with your eyes.\" How they did it Saadia says he does not need to demonstrate it at length, but: The blood: they did it in small places , perhaps putting in some potion beforehand to turn the water red. The frogs: again in small areas , perhaps putting something in the water to make the frogs come out. Moshe , by contrast, changed all the water and brought frogs out of every part of the Nile , a vast stretch said to run from the south of Egypt to Alexandria. That cannot be done by deception. \"That's clearly the work of Hashem.\" The staff into a snake Saadia does not explain, \"but you have to say, like people do magic tricks nowadays: a quick flash of the hands, a quick exchange, and I've changed my staff into a snake.\" Ibn Ezra and Rashi on the word Not all the Rishonim read the word this way. Commentator Root of the word Meaning Saadia לט , covering Secretly , deceptively Ibn Ezra להט , as in להט החרב המתהפכת , the flaming sword at the gate of Eden Shining , a quick flash like a spark. The same idea as Saadia, sleight of eye ( achizat einayim ), from a different root Rashi (and Onkelos ) Similar to lahat hacherev , but not the same word בלחשיהם , with their incantations . The sword at Eden seemed to turn by itself, as if worked by an incantation \"It's not an essential point, but if you understand what I'm saying, I think it's a very interesting one.\" Rashi clearly knew of a link between this word and the turning sword, but did not understand the link as the Ibn Ezra did, \"which is probably the correct one.\" The consequence: for Rashi the magicians really did something, so Rashi would still have Saadia's question . For Saadia and the Ibn Ezra, the question is answered. They never really did it. Part 3: Did Yonah Really Run Away? \"Topical. And Saadia comes up with a very original answer.\" The question: prophecy is given to reliable, verified people. So how can Tanach tell of a prophet who did not carry out his prophecy ? At the centre of Yom Kippur we read the story of Yonah , sent to Nineveh, who runs away to Tarshish. Why would Hashem choose someone who would not do what he was told? Saadia: he did go Nowhere in the four chapters of the book of Yonah does it say that Yonah did not do what he was told. It does not say that he went, but Saadia insists we must assume he did, because Hashem would never choose someone who would refuse . He brings cases where Hashem tells Moshe to speak to the people and the Torah never says Moshe went and spoke. We simply assume he did. So too with Yonah. Then why did he flee? Because of a change and addition in Hashem's message. The sequence, as the Rabbi laid it out: The first mission was only a warning to Nineveh. Yonah went, delivered it, and did his job. Yonah feared he would be sent a second time , now with a clear reprimand and its consequences . He knew that this time the people would do teshuvah , the decree would be annulled, and they would think he was a liar . So before he could be sent again , he fled to a place where there is no prophecy . Hashem brought him back to the place of prophecy, so that he would prophesy the second time. He told them the consequences, they did teshuvah, and the decree was annulled. This explains the end of the book, where Yonah says: I knew that You are merciful , and that is why I fled. \"Fascinating, says Saadia. The story of Yonah, you've misunderstood it all these years.\" He was not given a prophecy and then escaped. He did exactly what he was told , and ran only before the second mission. \"I wonder if anyone's ever read the story like this.\" The Kuzari and the Ibn Ezra The Kuzari teaches that prophecy is only in the Land of Israel , or about it, and that Yonah fled the place of prophecy and Hashem returned him. Perhaps the Kuzari means the same as Saadia, that he fled to avoid being prophesied to a second time. \"Maybe the Kuzari means that, maybe not.\" The Ibn Ezra , in his introduction to the book of Yonah, brings Saadia's explanation and rejects it , giving a different interpretation. Part 4: Chapter 6, The Structure of the Torah The Torah is not only commands and prohibitions. It is full of stories . What are they for? Three components Saadia says the Torah contains: Commands and prohibitions. Consequences , \"the fruits of your actions\": what happens if you do and if you don't. Historical narratives : accounts of people who did good and were rewarded and who did bad and were punished, through which we improve our service and our character . This, he says, is how all wise books of all nations are written, different as they are. A proper framework needs all three. The parable of the doctor A man has a fever, and the doctor sees he has too much blood. What the doctor says Saadia's verdict \"Don't eat meat or drink wine.\" Good, but incomplete \"...so that your fever doesn't worsen and kill you.\" Much better : with the consequence, he is more likely to listen. Still not complete \"...just as So-and-so had this fever, ate meat and drank wine, and died.\" Complete : command, consequence and example The same parable in Rashi The Rabbi pointed out that this is almost identical to a Midrash , quoted by Rashi on the verse read on Yom Kippur: Hashem spoke to Moshe after the death of Aharon's two sons , and told him to tell Aharon not to enter the Holy of Holies at any time. Rashi (Vayikra 16:1): a parable of a sick man visited by a doctor who tells him, don't eat cold food and don't lie in a damp place . Another doctor comes and says, don't eat cold food and don't lie in a damp place, so that you don't die the way So-and-so died . The second one got through to him. \"Whether Saadia knew of this or not, it's interesting. The exact example is different but it's the same idea.\" The verse itself has all three parts: the command (do not enter), the consequence (lest you die), and the example (the two sons of Aharon who entered and died). A note for Essay 4 \"We have to bear this in mind.\" If the narratives show good deeds rewarded and bad deeds punished, that seems to imply reward and punishment in this world . The Rabbi flagged it to return to when Saadia discusses reward and punishment in the next essay. Part 5: How Do We Know the Torah Is True? \"A very famous part of Emunot VeDeot, which is commonly quoted. Let's see it in its full context.\" Saadia touched on it in his Introduction; here he goes into it. Life depends on reports Hashem knew we would need the Torah handed down so that later generations could know it is true as the first generation did. So He gave us the ability to receive from others, and a place in our heart to believe what we hear . Without relying on reports: Commerce: someone tells you that if you do this in business you will do well. \"How do I know? I never saw it.\" Danger: someone tells you not to go down that path at night, or that there are many accidents on this road. Instructions: you would only do what you were told while the person stood in front of you. Saadia takes it to an extreme: you would even come to doubt your own memory . Inheritance and family: you would not know that this belonged to your father or grandfather. \"How do we know this is my mother? Because you're told as a child, this is your mother, this is your father.\" Without reports we would have no hope and no fear . Believing only what you can sense is the approach of the skeptics from Essay 1. The Rabbi added that we know nowadays that data and information are always manipulated to some degree, \"but we do rely on reports in the broad sense.\" The verse: seeing and hearing are equal כי עברו איי כתיים וראו, וקדר שלחו והתבוננו מאד Cross to the isles of the Kittim and see ; send to Kedar and pay close attention . (Yirmiyahu 2:10) Saadia sees here that the Torah equates seeing with hearing a report . A verified report is almost like sensing something yourself. But why add התבוננו מאד , \"pay very close attention\"? Two ways a report goes wrong An error of reasoning. The person wants to tell the truth but has got it wrong. Wilful deception. He is lying to you. Both happen with one person . But: Many people together will not all make the same mistake. If many people conspire to fabricate a story, it can never stay hidden . Wherever the report travels, the story of how it came about travels with it . The Torah was received by vast numbers , so it is safe from both. The Introduction filled this out The Rabbi reminded the class that Saadia was more detailed in Chapter 6 of the Introduction , and added a point he \"fails to mention here, which I don't really understand, because this is crucial\": \"You are My witnesses.\" The Torah tells the people you saw it : the ten plagues, the splitting of the sea, the revelation at Sinai. You cannot tell people they saw something they did not see. The manna is the greatest of the miracles , because a continuous miracle is greater than a one-off . How could a couple of million people live forty years in the desert without food? \"If people knew how to do this, the philosophers would have provided for their students,\" and in those days the philosophers and academics were poor. If parents told their children \"we ate manna for forty years\" and it never happened, the children would answer: this is your field, we saw you eating its fruit. \"I can be the most charismatic person, and perhaps I can make up a whole story about something that happened in a faraway land and convince you. But it's going to be very, very hard to tell you that yesterday you did X when you never did X, and convince you that you did it.\" \"Almost identical to what the Kuzari would say a couple of centuries later.\" From the class Chaim objected: the claim does not hold water. We see leaders in the world today who cheat, and whole peoples are deceived and follow them. The Rabbi answered with the saying, \" You can fool some of the people all the time, you can fool all the people some of the time, but you can't fool all the people all the time. \" Leaders lie about what is happening in some faraway land, and many people believe them. But to stand before thousands and tell them that they themselves saw smoke from heaven and ate manna for forty years, and then have them pass it on to their children, is not possible however charismatic you are. Chaim pressed further: perhaps someone wrote the story much later and told people, \"these are your ancestors.\" The Rabbi said he would go over this point properly the following week. Summary How the prophet knows: for Saadia, an external sign , a cloud, fire or light, as with Moshe in the Tent and Shmuel (\"in a pillar of cloud He would speak to them\"). For the Ramchal , an inner experience: the body trembles and falls. The Egyptian magicians did not really perform miracles. בלטיהם means secretly, deceptively , in small places and by sleight of hand. The Ibn Ezra agrees on the idea; Rashi reads \"with their incantations.\" Yonah did go. He delivered the warning, then fled before a second mission with consequences, fearing Nineveh's teshuvah would make him look a liar. The Ibn Ezra rejects this reading. The structure of the Torah: commands, consequences and stories, like the doctor's prescription, a parable that appears in Rashi on Acharei Mot. It hints at reward and punishment in this world, a point for Essay 4. The truth of the Torah: life runs on reports; a report fails through error or deceit; a tradition held by a whole people, about what they themselves saw , can be neither. Coming Up Next week, Monday 7 October: the anniversary of 7 October, so the shiur will be at the earliest possible time, 5.15 , so everyone can go on to their commemorative services. After that, back to after Mincha and Arvit. Chapter 7: abrogation, and how we know the Torah cannot be superseded. Chaim's question will be taken up first. The Rabbi wished everyone a Shanah Tovah .","YPdOIlRQ9eg":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 3 (Commandment and Prohibition), Chapters 3-4 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 23 September 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are in Essay 3 The third essay of Emunot VeDeot is about the mitzvot . So far Rav Saadia has told us: The introduction to the essay: Hashem created the world to bestow goodness , and the mitzvot, together with keeping away from the aveirot , are the means by which we pursue the good life . Chapter 1: there are two categories of mitzvot . He only names them at the start of this week's chapter: Category Hebrew What it means Examples Rational mitzvot מצוות שכליות ( mitzvot sichliyot ) Mitzvot we could have worked out ourselves, without Torah and without prophets Murder, adultery, theft, honesty Revelatory mitzvot מצוות שמעיות ( mitzvot shim'iyot ) Mitzvot we only know because Hashem told us Shabbat, the festivals, the Kohen, kashrut, the incest prohibitions, niddah Chapter 2: he gave reasons for both. Even the revelatory mitzvot have a bit of reason to them when you go into them, although the fact that Hashem commanded them is sufficient in itself to keep them. He will go through more of the revelatory mitzvot later in the essay. Tonight is two chapters: Chapter 3 , on why we need prophets at all, and Chapter 4 , on how a prophet is verified and what a prophet is. Part 1: Chapter 3, The Question If half the mitzvot are rational, why do we need Hashem to command them? \"Why do you need Hashem to tell us don't murder? Why do you need the Ten Commandments, half of which are pure logical sense?\" The revelatory mitzvot, fine: we could never have worked those out. But why an instruction for things that are clear in and of themselves? Two answers Saadia has already given Answer Where Greater reward. When Hashem commands something and we do it, we are not just doing what makes sense; we are listening to our Creator , and the reward is greater End of Chapter 1 of this essay It takes humanity time. We would eventually have worked out these objective norms, but it takes people a long time, and until then it would not have been fair to leave them without guidance The Introduction to the whole book The Rabbi noted that the second answer is historically a bit difficult : by the time the Torah was given there were already legal codes containing the basic rational laws. \"But in any case, that's what he says in his introduction.\" Now Saadia gives a completely different explanation , and the whole chapter is devoted to it. \"I heard people saying\" Saadia opens: I have heard people say that there is no need for prophets . Our intellect is enough to work out what is good and what is bad for us. Saadia adds that if they were right, Hashem would not have sent messengers, because He does not do what is unnecessary . Who were these people? The Rabbi explained that Saadia is referring to a sect he names later in the book: the Brahmins , who believed in natural religion . Of course there is a God and of course there is law, but the law is natural , and Hashem left it to us to work out what makes the most sense. The Rabbi linked it to later ideas: Natural justice , an idea slightly echoed by Aristotle. Deism , centuries later: there is a Creator, we must do what is right, but it is up to us to decide what that is and how to behave. \"But then I thought about it deeply\" Saadia rejects them. On reflection, he says, there is a great need for prophets, and not only for the revelatory mitzvot . We need them for the rational mitzvot too , because without the prophets we would never know how to keep them. The rational mitzvot give us the principle; they do not give us the framework . Part 2: Four Examples of a Missing Framework 1. Gratitude and prayer It is completely logical that we should thank Hashem . If He created us, as the first two essays established, we owe Him gratitude. But there is no framework : not what to say, not when to say it, not in what form. So the prophets came and gave us: Set times: morning, afternoon and evening. A text: the Men of the Great Assembly, some of whom were themselves prophets, composed the prayers. A structure: begin with praise , petition in the middle, and end with thanksgiving . A direction: the Arabic word Saadia uses here is the qibla , the term for the direction the Arabs pray towards. He means the correct direction in which to pray to Hashem. A halachic aside: is prayer from the Torah? The Rabbi pointed to the famous debate among the Rishonim: View Prayer is... The Ramban (Nachmanides) An institution of the Hachamim . There is no instruction in the Torah to pray The Rambam (and seemingly the Shulchan Aruch) Biblical. The concept comes from the Torah; the Men of the Great Assembly and the Hachamim gave it its exact structure \"Saadia clearly here is understanding like the Rambam.\" If prayer were purely rabbinic, it would make no sense for him to say the prophets , from Moshe through to the Men of the Great Assembly, gave us its framework. For Saadia, tefillah is de'oraita . 2. Adultery and marriage It is completely logical that adultery is wrong . But what makes a woman married , so that relations with her are adultery? Is it saying something to your fiancée? Giving money? Her father's agreement? Two witnesses? The whole city watching? Some kind of mark placed on her, which may have been the fashion at the time? \"We know it makes logical sense that adultery is forbidden, but what makes a married woman? We don't know.\" The prophets told us: Saadia lists testimony, a document and mohar . The Rabbi explained it through the halacha: Method of kiddushin Saadia's word Money (the ring under the chuppah is the equivalent of money) Mohar . Usually translated \"dowry\", and probably translated that way in the English editions, but the Rabbi thinks here it simply means money A document (not the ketubah, a separate document) Shtar Relations with the intention of creating a marriage Not mentioned All of them require two witnesses Edut Why does Saadia leave out the third method? The Rabbi gave two possibilities: It is improper. The Gemara says it works, but it is an incorrect way to marry, and Rav would administer lashes to those who did it. So Saadia did not want to include it. It was already the practice. More likely, based on the famous Rambam: קודם מתן תורה (before the giving of the Torah), a man would simply live with a woman and she would be his wife. That was already the established practice, so it was not something the prophets needed to reveal. A second halachic point. The Rambam held that kiddushei kesef (marriage with money) is mi-divrei soferim , so that it is not strictly biblical even though the other methods are. Saadia clearly does not hold that. Here he disagrees with the Rambam: he sees money as part of the revelation from the prophets , derived from the Torah, and so de'oraita. \"This is an intricacy. If you get it, fine; if not, don't worry about it.\" 3. Theft and ownership It makes perfect sense that theft is wrong . But when is something someone's property ? Is it mine if I worked for it? Inherited it? Found it in the desert? Paid money? Took hold of it? Simply declared it mine? \"Many different possibilities. We don't know.\" The prophets told us the correct way. In halacha there are indeed different modes of acquisition: sometimes money, sometimes lifting the item, different transactions for different things. Saadia sees the foundations of how something is acquired as already given in the Torah, with the Hachamim elaborating on it. 4. Punishment It makes sense that when someone does wrong there must be consequences . But what are they? Do you tell the person off? Curse him? Hurt him? Lashes, and if so how many ? A rebuke, and for how long , a day or a week? Do you put him to death? Is everyone's punishment the same? The Torah gives clear instructions for each mitzvah, sometimes similar, sometimes not, and sometimes the consequence is simply payment. The Rabbi added that all the Rishonim agree that the punishments are given in the Torah. The point: we would never agree If it were left to us to agree among ourselves, we would all disagree . \"Saadia says something here which we know nowadays as well, for sure: humans can never agree on anything.\" Summary of Chapter 3: Even the rational mitzvot need prophets, because reason gives us the principle but not the framework. The Rabbi put it as the difference between natural justice and legal justice . Natural justice we can work out ourselves. Legal justice, the specific definitions of how things are done, is what we need the Torah and the prophets for. Part 3: Chapter 4, How Do We Verify a Prophet? \"Here this is very interesting. Saadia is going to take an approach which the Rambam thoroughly rejects.\" Much of what we have learned in Emunot VeDeot is echoed by the Rambam later on, but not this. Having established that we need prophets, how do we know whether someone is really speaking the word of Hashem? The Rabbi put the question to the class. Flora answered that the Torah says if what the prophet foretells does not come to pass, he is a false prophet. She thought of the time of Yeshayahu, with great nations looming on the horizon: the people were frightened, would not listen, and it went against them in the end. \"Very good. The Rambam writes exactly that. And we'll see Saadia does not take that approach at all.\" Saadia: miracles are the sign Saadia's argument: Humans know their limits. We cannot change nature or change the essence of a thing. We are powerless to do this. Only the Creator can. Hashem took different kinds of matter, sometimes things whose natural state is not to stick together, and combined them into something new, in which you no longer see their separate qualities. That is the work of the Creator. So when Hashem chooses a prophet, He gives him a sign. Either control over nature (fire does not burn, water stops flowing, the heavenly luminaries stop moving) or changing the essence of something (the inanimate into the living, the living into the inanimate, water into blood, blood into water). Whoever sees such a sign must accept that this is a holy person and believe what he says, because Hashem would not have given him the sign unless he were verified by Him. Saadia's proof text is the Torah itself: all the signs Moshe performed in the book of Shemot . Those who believed him were the righteous, and those who did not were wrong. The obvious problem \"What's the most basic problem with this?\" We find false prophets in Tanach who could do miracles. Saadia himself answers this, but only a few chapters later (Chapter 7 or 8). \"I don't understand why he left it until then and not now,\" since his wording here is slightly misleading: it sounds as if any miracle makes you a prophet. Later he qualifies it: A miracle verifies a prophet only if he does not tell you to go against the Torah . If he does, the miracle is not acceptable, because no miracle can prove what is rationally impossible , and it is impossible for the Torah to be overturned. \"Which is quite amazing when you think about it. Saadia is saying that although miracles are so high, our reason is greater than that .\" So the definition of a prophet is really a combination: miracles, together with not commanding anything against the Torah. The prophet himself knows differently This whole discussion is about how we, the onlookers , know he is a prophet. The prophet himself knows because, as we learned in Essay 2 , he sees the kavod nivra , the created glory, the image of the glory of Hashem that Saadia understood the prophet would see. Part 4: The Rambam Disagrees The Rambam does not mention Saadia, \"but it could well be he's directing his words to him.\" In Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah , after discussing the qualities of a prophet, how one prepares for prophecy, how Moshe's prophecy differed from all others, and how prophecy can be either a message for others or simply for the prophet's own growth, he opens Chapter 8 : משה רבינו לא האמינו בו ישראל מפני האותות שעשה, שהמאמין על פי האותות יש בלבו דופי, שאפשר שיעשה האות בלט וכשוף Israel did not believe in Moshe Rabbeinu because of the signs he performed, for whoever believes because of signs has a doubt in his heart, since a sign can be performed by sleight of hand and sorcery. You can do magic. \"Witchcraft is real. You can play on the eyes.\" That is exactly why the Torah forbids it, and it is why miracles are an insufficient basis. Then what were Moshe's miracles for? Purpose of the miracles Saadia To verify that Moshe is a prophet (his proof from Shemot) The Rambam To solve problems. To escape the Egyptians, the sea split. We needed food, so the manna came down. They were thirsty, so he struck the rock. When Korach rebelled, the earth swallowed them. \"The miracles are not trying to prove that he's a prophet.\" That answers Saadia's proof from Shemot. The Rabbi acknowledged that there is a well-known question on the Rambam, since verses do strongly imply that the signs were given so that the people would believe. \"There are ways around it. The Rambam understood it differently. It's not for now.\" How the Rambam verifies Moshe: Sinai ובמה האמינו בו? במעמד הר סיני, שעינינו ראו ולא זר, ואזנינו שמעו ולא אחר With what did they believe in him? At the stand at Mount Sinai, where our own eyes saw, and not a stranger's, and our own ears heard, and not another's. The 600,000 men, about two million people with the elderly, those under twenty, the wives and children, themselves heard Moshe receiving the Torah, and that has been passed down to us. This is the famous argument of mass revelation that people ascribe to the Kuzari . \"But actually there are hints of it in the Rambam here, and we saw already in Saadia's own Introduction . Saadia put forward the famous argument of the Kuzari quite strongly there.\" This is also why a prophet who performs miracles and tells you to go against the Torah is not listened to, and is in fact put to death: miracles are not a verification. And the prophets after Moshe: telling the future They did not have Sinai. For them, the Rambam says, no miracle is needed . The test is exactly what Flora said: let him tell us what will happen , and we wait and see. If even a tiny detail fails, he is a false prophet. If everything comes true, he is to be believed. The Rambam (Chapter 10) adds an important qualification: If a prophet foretells something bad and it does not happen, that is not yet a reason to distrust him . The bad thing may have been decreed, and Hashem relented because the people did teshuvah. If he foretells something good and it does not happen, he is certainly a false prophet . Hashem never retracts a promise of good. The Kotzker Rebbe \"One of the first big arguments between Saadia and the Rambam: how important miracles are.\" The Rabbi brought the Kotzker Rebbe , from what was \"most certainly the most logical and rational sect of Hasidim,\" the source of many pithy sayings. In Arvit we say that Hashem performed signs and wonders in the land of the children of Cham . The Kotzker used to say, based on the Rambam: many Rebbes are called great because they did this miracle or that miracle. Miracles are for the children of Cham. Miracles do not prove anything. \"For me personally, nowadays especially, when people are very good at being manipulative and doing things which you may think is a miracle, but either it's witchcraft or black magic or it's simply naivety, being able to pull the wool over your eyes, I think the Rambam makes a lot of sense.\" Part 5: Hashem Does Not Change Nature for No Reason The second point of Chapter 4 is short. \"I'll be honest, I don't fully understand the second part, but we'll make our way through it.\" Saadia warns against a misunderstanding. Hashem changes nature only to verify prophecy , and only with advance warning . He does not change things for no reason, because if we thought the world could change at any moment, there would be no reality : You come home after a long trip and you don't know if this is your wife and children anymore . A witness says \"I saw him doing it,\" but perhaps his nature changed and it was a different person. We need to believe that existence stays as it is unless Hashem decides otherwise and gives notice. The Rabbi found the \"advance notice\" condition hard, since the prophets sometimes performed wonders without telling the people beforehand. Perhaps Saadia means that Hashem gives advance notice to the prophet . Either way, the point stands: things do not change without reason. Part 6: A Prophet Is a Normal Human Being The third point, and here the disagreement is not with the Rambam but with the Kuzari . Saadia goes to great lengths: Do not elevate the prophet. The only thing that distinguishes a prophet from regular people is that at certain times he can perform miracles . Everything else is the same. Why? If prophets were a different kind of being, people would say the miracles are no sign from Hashem: \"this is just a different category of person, a different species.\" So in His wisdom Hashem makes prophets: Fall ill and die , rather than live unnaturally long lives. Eat, drink and marry. Without promises of health or wealth , unprotected from theft, from being hurt, from being cursed (many prophets were), and even from being killed (prophets were killed). Perform miracles only at specific times , not all the time, or again people would think they are a different kind of person. No prophecy through angels. For the same reason Hashem never sends angels as prophets. We do not know what angels can or cannot do, so if an angel performed a miracle we would not know it came from Hashem. Only a human who does what no human can do is a clear sign. \"Aven ve'avel\" Saadia ends the chapter with people he has heard saying: How can a prophet die? How can a prophet be hungry? How can a prophet marry? How can people attack a prophet? How can a prophet not know everything? \"Even nowadays people say he has to know everything. It doesn't work like that.\" Saadia's verdict on all of it: און ועול , aven ve'avel , iniquity and injustice. \"A beautiful pun.\" Rather, Hashem created the prophets as they are, with this one advantage, with great wisdom . The Kuzari: a different kind of human Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi saw it the other way. The Rabbi read the English of the Kuzari: if we find a man who walks through fire unhurt , who goes without food for a long time without starving, on whose face shines a light the eye cannot bear , who is never ill and does not age, who dies when his time comes like a man lying down on his couch, and who knows the hidden past and future , is that not a degree beyond the ordinary human? The King answers that this is indeed a divine, angelic degree . The Kuzari says these are the characteristics of the true prophets. The prophet is... Saadia A regular human being , just like us, who at certain times can do miracles The Kuzari A different kind of human , almost a divine intermediary The Rambam (the Rabbi's view) Somewhere in between, closer to Saadia. Not some divine being, but the Rambam never downplays the prophet: his qualities are innumerable, his intellect must be vast and his character almost perfect, as in Shemonah Perakim \"Saadia's almost downplaying the prophet.\" Summary Chapter 3: Why do we need prophets for the rational mitzvot? Besides greater reward and not making humanity wait, Saadia's main answer is that reason gives the principle, not the framework . Prayer, marriage, ownership and punishment all need definitions, and left to ourselves we would never agree . Natural justice versus legal justice. Halachic asides: Saadia holds tefillah is de'oraita (like the Rambam, against the Ramban) and that kiddushei kesef is biblical (against the Rambam). Chapter 4, verification: for Saadia, a prophet is verified by miracles , provided he does not command against the Torah, because reason outranks miracles . The Rambam rejects miracles altogether: Moshe is verified by Sinai , later prophets by foretelling the future , and a promise of good must always come true. The Kotzker: \"miracles are for the children of Cham.\" Hashem does not change nature for no reason , or there would be no stable reality. A prophet is a normal human being , who gets ill, eats, marries and dies, so that his miracles are recognised as Hashem's. The Kuzari disagrees; the Rambam sits in between, closer to Saadia. Coming Up Next week: still an early start, 5.30 , for the last time because of the time of Mincha. After that the shiur moves later, to around 6.30 , after Mincha.","Imw1-UKUuME":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 3, Chapter 2 (second half: reasons for the revealed mitzvot), with the opening question of Chapter 3 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 16 September 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap Essay 1 was about creation, Essay 2 about the unity of God, and Essay 3 is about mitzvot and revelation . The Introduction: HaKadosh Baruch Hu created the world to bestow goodness , \"a theme which the Ramchal will develop some 700 years later.\" He gave us mitzvot and aveirot so that we would work for that good, because \"when you work for something in life, you benefit more and you enjoy it more.\" Chapter 1: two categories of mitzvot. The rational mitzvot fall into three groups: serving and thanking Hashem (tefillah, the Temple service), reverence for Hashem (not cursing, not taking His name in vain), and social conduct. The second category is mitzvot logic does not dictate, given, in Rav Saadia's words from Yeshayahu, so that Hashem might \"make the Torah great and glorious\" and increase our reward. Like the Midrash: does HaKadosh Baruch Hu care whether you slaughter through half or most of the signs? He gave them to give us more opportunities to serve Him, like a worker paid for work whose purpose he may not understand. And the rational mitzvot are implanted within us : morality is objective. Chapter 2, part 1: the reasons for four rational mitzvot. Murder defeats Hashem's will to bestow good. Adultery means not knowing who your father is, so you cannot show gratitude, and inheritance breaks down. Theft discourages cultivating your talents and the world. Dishonesty contradicts our senses and causes cognitive dissonance. And \"good\" cannot mean \"good for me,\" since the same act would be good for me and bad for you, like honey laced with poison. \"Pleasure is not the sole criterion of what is good.\" Tonight: the second half of Chapter 2, the revealed mitzvot. Chapter 2, Part 2: Five Categories of Revealed Mitzvot Rav Saadia turns to mitzvot that are not immediately logical, \"perhaps neutral from the perspective of reason,\" where Hashem chose to make some things sacred, some forbidden, some permitted and the rest neutral. He discusses five: # Category What is distinguished 1 Sacred days Shabbat and festivals, set apart from ordinary days 2 Sacred people Kohanim and prophets, set apart from everyone else 3 Food Some foods forbidden, some permitted 4 Forbidden relations Some people forbidden, some permitted, some obligatory 5 Times of separation Times when relations with one's wife are forbidden Although the main purpose, as Chapter 1 explained, is to increase our service of Hashem and our reward, \"I still find some partial rationale for them.\" And yet the wisdom of God is above us . Ta'am: a taste The Rabbi noted that the Sefer HaChinuch says exactly the same in its introduction: I will give you reasons for all the mitzvot, but never forget that Hashem's wisdom is above all of this. \"It's a beautiful idea. A taste in Hebrew is ta'am , and a reason is also ta'am .\" Even when you think you understand a mitzvah, you only have a taste of it, never the whole picture. 1. Shabbat and the Festivals Rav Saadia gives several reasons: Rest. Simply, humans need rest. Developing the intellect. Free from work, there is time to study. Spiritual growth. We pray more on Shabbat. Social value. People meet one another and talk, and it is a time for public announcements. \"Saadia is seeing spiritual, moral and social value in Shabbat and the festivals.\" What about the Torah's reasons? The Rabbi pointed out that the Torah itself does give rest as a reason: למען ינוח עבדך ואמתך כמוך , \"so that your servant and your maidservant may rest like you\" (Devarim 5:14). \"People forget. The Torah does say, black and white, lema'an yanuach .\" And that is Rav Saadia's first reason. He repeated an idea he has taught before: \"We are human beings, not human doings. During the six days we are human doings. Shabbat allows us to be human beings.\" That is why on Shabbat you are not meant to prepare for the future or work on your goals and aspirations. \"It's about being in the present.\" The Torah also gives creation in six days and the memory of Egypt as reasons, and it is interesting that Rav Saadia does not mention them. Perhaps he includes them in \"developing the intellect\": studying the creation of the world, that there is a God, the Exodus. Chaim was struck by the social reason: in an agricultural society, every farmer was alone in his own field and rarely met the others. Shabbat brought the community together. \"Exactly. It's so important,\" said the Rabbi. \"Very pragmatic.\" Torah or rest? The Rabbi brought the Yerushalmi , cited in the halachah by the Rema (Orach Chaim 290). Two Amoraim disagree: one says Shabbat was given for Torah study , the other for rest . The Gemara reconciles them: for working people, who do not study during the week, Shabbat is for Torah. For the scholars who labour in Torah all week, Shabbat is for rest. He recalled a gloss there, he thought by Rav Yaakov Emden , saying that on Shabbat one should not study in great depth but revise, read Navi and Chumash. So Shabbat is a combination of a bit of Torah and a bit of rest, and that is where Rav Saadia gets his two ideas of developing wisdom and resting. 2. Kohanim and Prophets Why are certain people singled out for reverence? So that we can learn from them . Rav Saadia is drawing directly on Malachi: כי שפתי כהן ישמרו דעת ותורה יבקשו מפיהו For the lips of the Kohen guard knowledge, and they seek Torah from his mouth. (Malachi 2:7) \"People forget this. People think the Kohen was serving the whole time in the Beit HaMikdash. It's absolutely wrong.\" Each Kohen served only about two weeks a year in the rotation, and not full-time even then. The rest of the time his role was to learn and teach Torah . If everyone has to work, nobody is free to teach, so you must designate people. Rav Saadia lists their roles: to teach, to pray on the people's behalf, to inspire them to a pious path and develop their character, and to set people back on the right path. \"Both spiritual aspects and social aspects. He's your teacher, he's your guide, he's your mentor.\" A participant added that kohanim and prophets are representatives of Hashem, like a king's councillors whom one respects. Mark suggested it teaches the importance of delegation : the Kohen is, as it were, Hashem's manifestation in this world, which is why the firstborn, which belongs to Hashem, is given to the Kohen. \"Interesting,\" said the Rabbi. Another participant said the Kohen intercedes for the people. The Rabbi brought the Maharal : the word כהן has the numerical value 75 . Seven represents the natural world; eight represents what is above it. The Kohen, at 75, stands between the people, the \"seventy\", and Hashem, the \"eighty\". He added that the Kuzari sees the Kohen as a distinct level within Israel. Nowadays, he noted, the Kohen does not have that role, and the closest equivalent is the Chachamim. 3. Kashrut \"You've got many different reasons given in the Rishonim. This reason you've probably never heard before. The most original reason I've ever seen.\" Why may we eat some animals and not others? So that people never make the mistake, as happened in pagan times, of associating animals with gods . If some animals are permitted and others impure, then no animal can be an object of worship. How can you worship something you are allowed to eat? That was the whole point in Egypt: if the lamb is a deity, how could you slaughter it? And the forbidden animals are not forbidden because they are holy. The Torah calls them tamei , impure. So both sides of the law strip animals of any divine status. A participant who has been vegetarian most of her life did not follow. Another asked whether the permitted animals might still be taken for gods, or the forbidden ones considered holy, like a \"lion king\". The Rabbi explained: \"If you can eat it, it can hardly be something you can worship. And if you can't eat it, it's not because it's holy. It's because it's tamei .\" So the laws of kashrut are about keeping us away from avodah zarah . Other Rishonim give other reasons: the Rambam in the Moreh Nevuchim writes, in a famous and difficult passage, that some of the laws are for health . Elsewhere it is explained that predatory animals would instil their character in the one who eats them. The first to give reasons for mitzvot The Rabbi stressed what a landmark this is: \"Saadia Gaon is the very first person to give reasons for mitzvot. This in itself is huge. We're seeing the most original reasons.\" Later came the Rambam, the Sefer HaChinuch, the Ramban, the Abarbanel and many commentators. But you never find the Gemara or the Geonim doing anything like this. He is the first, right at the end of the Gaonic period. A participant suggested people surely discussed reasons before, perhaps without daring to write them down. \"The first one to document it, the first which we have,\" the Rabbi agreed. 4. Forbidden Relations: Incest Adultery was already explained among the rational mitzvot. But why are a man and his sister, or a father and his daughter, forbidden? Rav Saadia gives two reasons: Constant proximity. You spend so much time with your siblings and parents that, if they were permitted, there would be relations all the time, an increase in promiscuity, \"and too much isn't good.\" Marriageability. If relatives could marry, some people would never be sought by anyone outside. Others would reason: this girl's own brother passes her over for her sister, so neither will I. People would pursue only physical beauty, \"and relationships are much more than external, hedonistic pleasure and physical beauty.\" The Rabbi admitted a difficulty with the first reason: your wife is also available to you all the time, so what is the difference? And the laws of niddah would apply to a sister as well. \"So I don't fully understand this reason.\" Other reasons The Rambam develops the first reason too: the Torah repeats the many forms of forbidden relations so that we keep away from constant intimacy. He adds that it is unfitting to have relations with someone you must honour and revere . How can you honour your mother, or a daughter respect her father, after such intimacy? Marie had made exactly this point: it would ruin the relationship. \"Very good. That's exactly what the Rambam says.\" Another reason given: marrying out brings in wealth and connections, rather than trapping them within the family. A participant noted that Freud also discussed the incest taboo, and another recalled the Roman emperors who married their sisters, whose children then had higher status and caused rivalry with half-siblings. \"Too much kindness\" The Rabbi has said before that the Torah calls one of these relations חסד הוא (Vayikra 20:17). Onkelos translates it as disgrace, but the simple meaning is \"overflowing kindness.\" Love between brother and sister, father and daughter, is a great love, \"but even love has to have a boundary.\" There is chesed that is bad: constantly helping someone without giving him independence. A participant added that even between husband and wife the period of separation guards against familiarity breeding contempt. \"Abomination\" Doron asked: the Torah calls these relations תועבות , abominations, which the Canaanites did and for which the land became defiled. The Rambam's reasons explain why they would be abominable. Rav Saadia's pragmatic reasons do not. The Rabbi agreed that Rav Saadia's reasons are pragmatic, and suggested that perhaps he reads \"abomination\" as going on the last items in the list. 5. Times of Separation Why may a man not be with his wife at certain times? Humility and restraint. The Rabbi understood it as recognising the limits of one's flesh: you cannot indulge it all the time. Appreciation of the spiritual. Rav Saadia seems to hold that after relations a person was impure and could not enter the Temple, or, in his view, pray, until purified. The period of separation therefore gives time to focus on these things and appreciate them, and instils fear of heaven. (Nowadays, the Rabbi noted, one may pray.) Other Rishonim give other reasons. The Sefer HaChinuch and others relate it to the Torah's term דוה , as if it were a kind of illness that could be caught, \"which scientifically is not correct nowadays.\" Another reason given: the purpose of relations is ultimately procreation , which is not possible at that time. Rav Saadia mentions that he will discuss other mitzvot later, such as the red heifer , the korbanot , the eglah arufah and brit milah . Here he only wants to give a taste. He concludes that in all the revealed mitzvot you will find branches of reason, many reasons. But ultimately Hashem's wisdom is higher than anything humans can attain: כי גבהו שמים מארץ כן גבהו דרכי מדרכיכם As the heavens are higher than the earth, so are My ways higher than your ways. (Yeshayahu 55:9) Sichliyot and Shim'iyot; Mishpatim and Chukim At the opening of Chapter 3, Rav Saadia names the categories for the first time: Rav Saadia Meaning The Rambam's terms שכליות ( sichliyot ) Mitzvot that reason can dictate Mishpatim שמעיות ( shim'iyot ) Mitzvot we know because we heard them, by revelation Chukim There is no mitzvah without a reason The Rabbi called this crucial. Ask most people, even children, what a chok is, and they will say: a mitzvah with no reason, and the classic example is the parah adumah . \"No. The Rambam rejects that,\" and he argues strongly with the Ramban about it. For the Rambam, a mishpat is a mitzvah whose reason is immediately apparent. A chok is one whose reason takes longer to understand . There is no such thing as a mitzvah with no reason. People who say God gives mitzvot without reasons are embarrassing HaKadosh Baruch Hu. \"You don't know the reason for it. That doesn't mean it's got no reason.\" A participant asked about Shlomo HaMelech , who said of the red heifer, אמרתי אחכמה והיא רחוקה ממני , \"I said I would become wise, but it was far from me.\" Every year we read the reasons and still do not understand. The Rabbi answered that Shlomo said he did not grasp it fully, not that it had no reason. \"If he didn't think you need to understand it, he wouldn't have said it.\" It shows that you must try to understand the mitzvot, even if you do not always succeed. Another participant noted that for Rav Saadia, the main point of the revealed mitzvot is still obedience and service, and their partial benefits are secondary. \"Correct. Good point.\" Which category is higher? Rav Saadia does not say which category is more important. Is a rational mitzvah higher, because it is basic morality? Or a revealed one, because Hashem had to reveal it? The Rabbi noted that we are told not to weigh up the positive mitzvot against each other, as the Rambam taught, whereas the negative ones can be weighed by their punishments. On this question there is a dispute, and he asked the class to guess who says what: View The Kuzari The revealed mitzvot are higher. החקים השכליים הם הקדמות והצעות לתורה האלהית : the rational laws are only a preface and introduction to the divine Torah, like derech eretz kadmah laTorah Rabbeinu Bachya The rational mitzvot are more important, precisely because they are rational Is there an accepted reason? A participant asked which reason is the accepted one today. \"Now we appreciate what the Shulchan Aruch did,\" the Rabbi said. With halachah there were the Rambam, the Rosh and many others reaching different conclusions, so the Shulchan Aruch gave a ruling. In practice there is a psak . With understanding and reasons there is none. \"It's your decision, what makes the most sense to you. You can have your own reason.\" The ta'amim are only as far as the human mind can recognise. The participant felt some things, like the incest taboo, are simply embedded in the human mind. The Rabbi suggested Rav Saadia would say there were times when people did these things, and that the Bible's influence over thousands of years may have made them repugnant to us. Another participant pointed out that the Code of Hammurabi , older than the Torah, already forbids it. \"True,\" said the Rabbi, though if Hammurabi had to legislate against it, perhaps people were doing it. Chapter 3: Why Do We Need Revelation for What Reason Can Tell Us? Chapter 3 asks: if the rational mitzvot can be worked out by reason, why do we need prophets and revelation for them? Rav Saadia has in mind the Brahmins , a Hindu sect who held a kind of natural religion , like modern deism : you believe in God, but you know what is good and bad by yourself. \"I don't need someone to tell me. Just do what your heart feels is right.\" He accepts that we need revelation for the shim'iyot . The question is about the sichliyot . The Rabbi noted that Rav Saadia already gave one answer in the Introduction to the book: people could work these things out themselves, but it would take time , and in the meantime they would stumble. Revelation gave us an immediate \"yes\" and \"no.\" At this point the Zoom connection froze repeatedly, and the shiur ended there. Summary The revealed mitzvot have a partial rationale, but Hashem's wisdom is above us. A ta'am is only a taste. Shabbat and festivals: rest, study, prayer and community. \"Human beings, not human doings.\" For the worker, Torah; for the scholar, rest. Kohanim and prophets: set apart to teach , pray, guide and inspire. The Kohen served only about two weeks a year. Kashrut: so that no animal can be taken for a god, the most original reason the Rabbi had seen. Rav Saadia is the first to give reasons for mitzvot . Incest: to prevent constant promiscuity, and so that everyone is marriageable. The Rambam adds the honour owed to parents. Even chesed needs boundaries. Niddah: humility, and time to appreciate the spiritual. Sichliyot and shim'iyot , the Rambam's mishpatim and chukim. For the Rambam, no mitzvah lacks a reason; a chok is one whose reason is harder to grasp. Which is higher? The Kuzari favours the revealed, Rabbeinu Bachya the rational. Chapter 3: why reveal what reason can find? Partly because reason takes time, and revelation spares us stumbling on the way. Coming Up The rest of Chapter 3 : Rav Saadia's full answer to why revelation is needed even for the rational mitzvot.","yNXQ8-t7_ZY":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 3 (Command and Prohibition): the Introduction, Chapter 1 (the categories of mitzvot) and the opening of Chapter 2 (the rational mitzvot) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 9 September 2024, on Zoom (the first shiur after the summer break) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: The Book So Far With a few new people in the class, the Rabbi gave an overview. Rav Saadia wrote Emunot VeDeot about 1,100 years ago in Iraq . In his introduction he explains why: he saw people going through life without understanding its purpose, without thinking, and surrounded by conflicting faiths and viewpoints. So he wrote the very first book of collected Jewish theology , about 200 years before the Rambam. The Torah, Talmud and Midrash are full of philosophy and theology, but no one before him had set it out in a structured way. \"Many of the things the Rambam writes, actually Saadia preceded him.\" Section Content Introduction Why he wrote the book; the sources of knowledge Essay 1 The existence of a Creator , as the Rambam later does, before speaking of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. He works through the opposing views, from Plato and Aristotle onward, \"not afraid to quote them,\" and tries to disprove each Essay 2 Who this Creator is: His unity , three chapters against the Trinity , and most of all the anthropomorphisms in the Torah Essay 3 (tonight) Revelation, mitzvot and the reasons for mitzvot The first two essays were very philosophical, because proving a Creator is one of the hardest things to prove or disprove. As Rav Saadia himself conceded, normally we perceive things with our senses and our logic, and here we cannot. Essay 3 has a brief introduction and ten chapters . What Essay 3 will do Rav Saadia is the first to distinguish between mitzvot sichliyot , rational mitzvot that make sense, and mitzvot that are not intuitively rational, though he tries to make even those \"as appetising to the intellect\" as he can. The Rambam later develops this. The Rabbi reminded the class of the Rambam's Shemoneh Perakim , which they had learned: Rational mitzvot: we should develop within ourselves a desire to do them, because they are right. Mitzvot with no intuitive reason, such as eating non-kosher food: here, as the Chachamim say in the Midrash, it is fine, even praiseworthy, to want to do otherwise. You may walk past a non-kosher shop, smell the aromas, and say, \"I wish I could enjoy this, but HaKadosh Baruch Hu doesn't allow me.\" And he previewed how down-to-earth Rav Saadia's reasons can be. Why did Hashem give us the festivals ? Because human beings need a break, and because they are a good time for people to gather and for public announcements to be made. The Introduction: Why Mitzvot at All? אמר המחבר: The author says: before this essay on command and prohibition, I must first introduce... We have established in the first two essays that HaKadosh Baruch Hu is eternal and created the world alone. Now we must understand that He created the world only for goodness . At the end of Essay 1, Rav Saadia gave three answers to why a Creator would create, and one was that He is benevolent and wishes to bestow goodness. Scripture says so throughout: טוב ה׳ לכל ורחמיו על כל מעשיו , \"Hashem is good to all, and His mercy is on all His works\" (Tehillim 145:9). Two levels of goodness Existence itself. The very fact that Hashem allowed humans to exist is goodness, as Yeshayahu says, כל הנקרא בשמי ולכבודי בראתיו , \"I created him for My glory.\" Thriving. Not only to exist and survive, but to succeed and prosper in that existence. That comes through the commandments and prohibitions : תודיעני ארח חיים שבע שמחות , \"Show me the path of life, fullness of joy\" (Tehillim 16:11). \"Wouldn't life be easier without them?\" Then comes the obvious question, \"as many of you may be thinking\": if the mitzvot are meant to make our life good, wouldn't it be better without them? You can't eat this, you can't do that. Surely Hashem could give us the goodness without all these restrictions and burdens. Rav Saadia answers: The good you earn is greater than the good you are given. Reward for work is far greater than something handed to you as a favour. The Rabbi's example: a person who works hard, succeeds in business and buys his own home, versus one whose parents gifted him the house. \"That's lovely and that's great, but it's not the same as: I have worked for this and earned it myself. You can't even compare the two.\" Hashem wanted us to benefit doubly and properly. As Yeshayahu says, הנה ה׳ אלהים בחזק יבוא... הנה שכרו אתו ופעלתו לפניו , \"Hashem comes with strength... His reward is with Him and His recompense before Him\" (Yeshayahu 40:10). On one hand He wants to give us goodness; on the other, we must earn it. \"Anyone who has studied Derech Hashem with me: basically the whole first section of Derech Hashem of Rabbi Moshe Chaim Luzzatto is summarised in that one paragraph of Saadia Gaon.\" \"We are the authors of our identity.\" If someone writes your story for you, that's lovely, but it's not the same as writing your own story and creating your own identity. Bread of shame The Ramchal adds another angle that Rav Saadia does not mention: bread of shame . When someone is constantly giving to you, there comes a point where it is simply shameful. The Rabbi explained: human relationships need equilibrium . When someone does you a favour, you either repay it, or, if you cannot, you give thanks or a present. Someone invites you for Shabbat; you invite them back. \"You can't be in a proper relationship where it's always one way.\" The root of the idea is already here in Rav Saadia: everything worthwhile in life is something we put effort into. A participant wrote in the chat that in a relationship that cannot be reciprocated, the receiver may avoid the giver, out of shame and inadequacy. \"Precisely, thank you,\" said the Rabbi. Psychology describes how giving too much one way can even lead the receiver to turn on the giver, \"because you don't know how to relate to this imbalanced relationship.\" It is an extreme case, but it happens. Chapter 1: The Categories of Mitzvot Rav Saadia opens: we have a body of laws through which to serve HaKadosh Baruch Hu, and we are obligated to keep them, as the Torah says in the next week's parasha (Ki Tavo): היום הזה ה׳ אלהיך מצוך לעשות את החקים האלה... ושמרת ועשית אותם בכל לבבך , \"This day Hashem your God commands you to do these laws... keep them and do them with all your heart\" (Devarim 26:16). These instructions came with signs and wonders : the public revelation at Sinai, which proved their authority, and in response we immediately committed to keeping them. But that alone is not sufficient. It remains our duty to understand them, so that we do not do them without understanding. As always with Rav Saadia: first the verse, then the rational explanation. Four premises of reason # What reason dictates 1 Repay goodness. When someone does you good, either do good back, if they need it, or, if they do not need it or you cannot, give thanks 2 Do not abuse your benefactor. Logic does not allow you to curse and abuse someone who has been good to you 3 Do not harm others. Within human relationships, we should not cause pain and harm 4 Work should be rewarded. If I ask you to work for me, you have given of yourself, and I compensate you The first great category: the rational mitzvot The first three premises produce the rational mitzvot, in three groups: Group Premise Examples Rav Saadia gives Thanks to Hashem We cannot repay Hashem, so we give appreciation and thanksgiving Prayer, submission, honour and respect in the synagogue, standing in the proper places, revering His name and His Temple Not abusing Hashem Not acting against Him, \"although of course it makes no difference to God. It's not the way relationships are\" Not believing in another god (\"if you have a relationship with one person, you don't bring a third person into it\"), not taking His name in vain or swearing falsely, not blaspheming Not harming one another ולא תונו איש את עמיתו , \"do not wrong one another\" (Vayikra 25:17) Telling the truth, pursuing justice, not killing or paining others, not committing adultery or stealing, and wanting for others what you want for yourself \"Everything written in the Torah on these, full stop.\" Morality is objective Here the Rabbi drew out a famous philosophical question: is morality subjective? For Rav Saadia, absolutely not. Hashem implanted in our hearts the recognition of what is good and what is bad. Whatever He commanded, He planted in us an approval of it, and whatever He forbade, a recognition that it is bad, as Mishlei says of wisdom: כי אמת יהגה חכי, ותועבת שפתי רשע For my palate speaks truth, and wickedness is an abomination to my lips. (Mishlei 8:7) \"I taste it, it's within my palate. I sense it, I feel it.\" A participant asked about caring for the world we were given, which is a mitzvah early in the Torah, לעבדה ולשמרה , \"to work it and to guard it.\" The Rabbi said it could fall under the third group, since harming the environment leaves it worse for other people, or under the first, since it would be ingratitude for what we have been given. \"Something which makes total sense.\" The second great category: mitzvot reason does not dictate The fourth premise produces the other category: things that are not intrinsically good or bad , but which Hashem made good or bad by commanding them. The Rabbi cited the famous Midrash about the laws of shechitah , which require cutting the majority of the windpipe and gullet: does HaKadosh Baruch Hu care whether one cuts a half or a majority? \"True, it makes no difference,\" the Rabbi said. But Hashem wanted to give us more mitzvot, in the mundane parts of life too, to increase our success . Rav Saadia quotes: ה׳ חפץ למען צדקו יגדיל תורה ויאדיר Hashem desired, for the sake of His righteousness, to make the Torah great and glorious. (Yeshayahu 42:21) Had He not commanded kashrut or the details of shechitah, it would have made no difference. But since He commanded them, doing them is good and refraining is bad. This is like the fourth premise: \"If I give you a task, and you didn't fulfil it, that's a problem. I could have not given you any task. I gave you a task, now you need to fulfil it.\" The Rabbi stressed that this does not mean \"illogical\", only that there is no logic dictating it . And Rav Saadia adds: even in this category, with a bit of analysis, we can find some partial benefit , some small logical reason, just as in the first category we found a great deal. Summary of Chapter 1 The purpose of the mitzvot and aveirot is to bestow goodness , and earned goodness is the greater good. Rational mitzvot , implanted within us: (1) honouring Hashem, (2) not abusing Hashem, (3) relationships between people. Commanded mitzvot : those reason does not dictate, which Hashem added to increase our \"workload\" and our reward. So, according to Rav Saadia, every mitzvah in the Torah falls into one of these categories. A participant asked where Rav Saadia discusses the actual benefit of the mitzvot. The Rabbi said that comes in the essay on reward and punishment, together with the question of why the righteous suffer. Here he is only discussing the mitzvot themselves and their purpose. Do the rational mitzvot apply to everyone? Chaim asked: God created all people, not only Jews. If these mitzvot are dictated by logic, shouldn't everyone be bound by them? Yet all the examples are from our Torah. \"An excellent question,\" said the Rabbi. Rav Saadia discusses it later in the book. In short, the Seven Noachide Laws capture the essence of this. Interpersonal mitzvot fall under the law of establishing courts and justice , creating a fair society. Non-Jews are also commanded not to blaspheme. In the greater framework, the general principles apply to all humanity. Israel has more detail and more mitzvot, \"and this is exactly his point: for Israel, He made the Torah great and glorious.\" Chapter 2: The Rational Mitzvot Explained Rav Saadia begins with the easy part, the mitzvot logic dictates, and gives \"very basic and simple reasons.\" He picks four major ones: 1. Murder If killing were allowed, everyone would end up killing each other. That defeats the very purpose for which Hashem created the world: to bestow goodness, so that people could enjoy it. Causing death or injury takes that away. 2. Adultery So that humans do not become like animals . The Rabbi explained the reason, which he said many Rishonim, the Rambam included, repeat: if a woman could be with more than one man, no one would know the identity of a child's father. (This, he noted, is why the Torah allows a man several wives but not a woman several husbands: paternity remains known.) Rav Saadia gives the consequences: A child would not know whom to repay for the good of being born and raised, which ties back to the first premise. A father could not bequeath an inheritance. People could not know their relatives , and so could not show the special kindness we naturally feel for family: brothers, cousins, uncles and aunts. The Rabbi pointed out that Rav Saadia distinguishes between adultery , which falls in the rational category, and incest , which he places in the other category, to be discussed later. So not all forbidden relations are, for him, dictated by logic. 3. Theft If theft were allowed, people would rely on stealing for their livelihood and would not cultivate the world . The Rabbi expanded: \"The way anyone makes money in this world is that you have something to offer which benefits other human beings. Every single job is: I have something to give to someone, and I'm paid for that.\" If people made their living by stealing, there would be no incentive to make the world better. \"This is a basic and simple argument against communism.\" And everyone would hide whatever they had, and things would disappear. 4. Truth Truth means describing something as it is. A lie describes it as other than it is. When you are told one thing and then perceive the opposite, it creates conflict within you, what the Rabbi called cognitive dissonance . \"Truth and falsehood is also sensual.\" So it is logical that Hashem commands us to keep away from falsehood. \"I'll just do what's good for me\" Some people say nothing is intrinsically wrong. The guiding principle should simply be: I do what brings me pleasure and benefit, and I avoid what harms or worries me. Rav Saadia says that if you think this, you are a fool, and he will discuss it more in Essay 4. Briefly: What is \"good for you\" is equally logical for everyone else. If I hurt my enemy, that brings me satisfaction, so by this logic it is good. But it is bad for him. If I take whatever money or whatever woman I want, it brings me pleasure, but it causes pain to the one I took from. The same act would then be wisdom and folly at once , and nothing can be both good and bad at the same time. Society cannot exist like that. His illustration: honey with poison in it . Your principle says eat what is pleasant, so eat the honey, and avoid what harms, so do not eat the poison. Your logic would tell you to eat it and not eat it at the same time, \"and that cannot be.\" Summary Essay 3 is about revelation, mitzvot and their reasons. Rav Saadia is the first to distinguish rational mitzvot from those reason does not dictate. Hashem created the world to bestow goodness: existence first, then thriving through the mitzvot. Earned good is greater than gifted good , the seed of the Ramchal's Derech Hashem and his idea of \"bread of shame.\" \"We are the authors of our identity.\" Four premises of reason: repay goodness, do not abuse a benefactor, do not harm others, and reward work. The rational mitzvot: thanks to Hashem, not abusing Hashem, and not harming people. Morality is objective , implanted within us. The commanded mitzvot: not intrinsically good or bad, given to \"make the Torah great and glorious,\" and even these have some discoverable benefit. Chapter 2: murder destroys the purpose of creation; adultery confuses lineage, gratitude, inheritance and family love; theft removes the incentive to cultivate the world; lies create inner dissonance. Hedonism fails: what is good for me is bad for another, and nothing can be both, like honey laced with poison. Coming Up Next Monday: the rest of Chapter 2, the mitzvot that are less logical, and on to Chapter 3. A participant asked about killing in war . Rav Saadia does not discuss war here, and the Rabbi offered to discuss it after the shiur.","WNshCH2P_og":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 2, Chapter 12 (the last two categories: action, and being acted upon), with the opening of Chapter 13 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 15 July 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap We are going through the ten anthropomorphisms in the Torah. Last week, in Chapter 11, we covered feelings, relation, place, time, possession and position. Chapter 12 deals with the final two: Part Category 1 Action ( po'el ): Hashem doing things 2 Being acted upon ( nif'al ): Hashem as the recipient of action 9. Action: Hashem Does Not \"Do\" Even though the Torah speaks of Hashem doing this or that, we cannot take it literally. Rav Saadia gives two reasons: A physical agent must act in order to affect something else. This is an Aristotelian idea: nothing physical can affect another physical thing unless it does something itself. If you want to move something by blowing on it, you have to blow. \"But the way of Hashem is, He just wishes, and things happen.\" When we say Hashem did something, it means He willed it, and it came to be . A physical agent needs material, time and space to act. That is inappropriate to say of Hashem. Doing and stopping The Torah describes both Hashem acting and Hashem ceasing to act, and both mean the same kind of thing: Action Its opposite Meaning Creation : Hashem \"made\" וישבת ביום השביעי , \"and He rested on the seventh day\" (Bereishit 2:2) He created through will and desire . \"He rested\" does not mean He rested from work. It means His will to create stopped : \"no more creating\" Prophecy : \"Hashem spoke\" to Moshe, to Aharon החשיתי מעולם... אתאפק , \"I have long kept silent, I have restrained Myself\" (Yeshayahu 42:14) \"Hashem spoke\" means He created speech that reached the prophet's ears. God has no voice and no sound waves. \"I kept silent\" means He stopped that created communication and was waiting, patient The Mutakallimun Why does Rav Saadia stress the opposite of speech? Because the Arab philosophers, the Mutakallimun , held the same idea of created speech but did not accept that it could stop. The Rabbi explained their name: kalam is Arabic for \"word\", from the same idea as medaber , to speak. They held that creation is constantly sustained by Hashem's constant speech , so to say that Hashem stops speaking was unacceptable to them. We too say that Hashem constantly renews creation, המחדש בטובו בכל יום תמיד מעשה בראשית , but not through ongoing speech. The Torah shows us both the act and its opposite: He created and He rested, He spoke and He stopped speaking. Creating versus sustaining A participant asked: if Hashem stopped creating, what of ratzo vashov , the constant \"running and returning\" that keeps the world going? The Rabbi offered a parable he has given before: Close your eyes and create in your mind the most beautiful universe you could wish to live in. Spend half an hour focusing on it, meditating on it. Then someone calls your name, and that universe is gone from your mind. The half-hour of creating it, that was vaya'as , the doing. Now, if you simply keep holding it in your mind, that is mechadesh betuvo , the constant renewal. So the maintenance of the world came from an initial act, and then vayishbot , He stopped actively creating. Of course He sustains it, but sustaining is different from creating. \"You might not speak, but you're constantly thinking about it.\" The participant added: \"The moment you stop, it's cut off.\" \"Precisely. Very good.\" Remembering, but never forgetting When Hashem rescues people from difficulty, the Torah calls it remembering : ויזכר אלהים את נח , \"God remembered Noach\" (Bereishit 8:1), and ויזכר אלהים את רחל , \"God remembered Rachel\" (Bereishit 30:22). It means He now intervenes to help. The Rabbi pointed out something interesting. Unlike \"made and rested\" or \"spoke and kept silent,\" we never find the opposite , that Hashem forgot . Theoretically one could say \"Hashem forgot,\" meaning He no longer helps. What we do find is that He did not remember : ולא זכר הדום רגליו , He did not remember His footstool, the Beit HaMikdash, and allowed it to be destroyed (Eicha 2:1). The attributes are all in reference to us Finally: when Hashem acts with mercy, we call Him merciful and compassionate . When He exacts punishment, He is אל נקמות , the God of vengeance. שומר הברית והחסד , \"He keeps the covenant and kindness,\" for His people. All these terms refer only to creatures , to us. \"This is the first person to say this. This is a fundamental principle which we're going to develop. He is the first one to explicitly come up with this concept: that all attributes of Hashem are only in reference to His creatures.\" Two Kinds of Names: Essence and Action From this the Rabbi developed the distinction that grows out of Rav Saadia's principle: Names of action ( shemot hape'ulot ) Names of essence ( shemot ha'etzem ) Examples Compassionate, gracious, kind, slow to anger, vengeful, zealous One, eternal ( Kadmon ), wise When Only after creation: they need a world to act upon True whether or not there is a world How understood Positively, but only as perceived by us . \"When we say on Yom Kippur over and over again Rachum veChanun, Erech Apayim , there's no such thing as Hashem slow to anger. It's just how it's perceived to us\" Only negatively . And all of them are one; they are not parts of Hashem As he said in Chapters 8 and 9, all these names are from our perspective, and Hashem remains one entity. \"This is critical to understand.\" The Rambam: negative theology The Rabbi read from the Moreh Nevuchim, Part 1, Chapter 59 . The Rambam has rejected all positive attributes. We can only know what Hashem is not : \"Hashem is one\" means He is not two. \"Hashem is eternal\" means He has no beginning or end. A person may then ask: if everything is negative, and once I have proven the negative that is it, what advantage did Moshe have over anyone else? The Rambam answers: there is indeed a huge difference. Just as each additional positive attribute specifies an object more and brings you nearer to it, so each additional negation brings you nearer to understanding HaKadosh Baruch Hu. The more you negate, the more you understand. So, the Rabbi explained, even Rav Saadia's names of essence are not ways to understand Hashem positively. They describe what He is not. Rabbeinu Bachya and Rav Yosef Albo Rabbeinu Bachya (in Chovot HaLevavot) follows Rav Saadia. He divides Hashem's names into essential and active . Essential names are those that applied before there were any creations. Active names, such as \"compassionate\", come after creation: whenever there are two, it is a name of action. And even the essential names are all one, with no diversity. The key lines: whenever you speak of attributes, understand them as negating the opposite . \"Negating the attributes of Hashem gives us a better understanding of Hashem than affirming His attributes,\" and the more we negate, the deeper our understanding of His unity. Rabbeinu Bachya says he will not elaborate, since Rav Saadia already did. \"It all started here,\" the Rabbi said. \"This is the source of this concept,\" found in all the Sephardi medieval philosophers. Rav Yosef Albo makes a sweeping statement: כל בעלי העיון הפילוסופי חושבים שאי אפשר לייחס לשם יתברך שום תואר, לא עצמי ולא מקרי מצד עצמו... אלא שוללי, ושכל התארים השוללים צודקים עליו All philosophers hold that one cannot ascribe to Hashem any attribute, essential or accidental, in Himself... only negative ones, and all negative attributes are true of Him. But with regard to His actions , we can speak positively, because those relate to us as humans. \"Is everyone with me?\" The Rabbi checked that the class understood, and when someone said \"more or less,\" he replied that \"more or less\" was not good enough. \"This is really the basis of medieval Jewish philosophy.\" He repeated it: Names of action , such as compassionate, kind, slow to anger, vengeful, describe how Hashem's conduct is perceived by us . Names of essence , such as one, eternal, wise, existed before humans. Hashem is infinite, and \"you cannot understand what one means in infinity, or eternal , or wise , because that wisdom is infinite wisdom.\" All you can properly grasp is the negation: Hashem is not not-wise . Or, in the image he has used before: the Or Ein Sof and the sunglasses we wear to perceive the light. The names of action describe the light as we see it through our glasses. The Ramchal builds on this \"By the way, I'll quickly throw in, the whole philosophy of the Ramchal is based on this.\" In Derech Hashem and Da'at Tevunot , the whole of creation depends on Yichud Hashem . Why? Because unity is the only thing the human mind can fully understand about Hashem. We cannot understand His wisdom or compassion in His essence. We can only arrive at negation, and the one negation we can fully grasp is that there is no other force but Hashem . \"Hashem Echad means there are not two. That's possible for us to gain a full understanding of.\" The Rabbi recalled that the Ramchal never quotes anyone except Chazal, with one exception: the Rambam, and he quotes exactly this passage on negative theology, on which he builds his whole philosophy of Yichud Hashem. A participant asked why the infinite needs any \"definition\" at all, even negative. \"For us, correct,\" said the Rabbi. The distinction is only for our purposes: names of action we can understand in some positive sense, for the creatures. Names of essence we will never understand positively. 10. Being Acted Upon: Can Hashem Be Seen? The tenth category is Hashem as a recipient . Nobody really thinks Hashem can receive anything: money, a bribe, a gift. We all accept that, since He is not physical. But people do think Hashem can be the \"recipient\" of sight , that you could see Him. Rav Saadia makes clear that this is impossible. Things are seen through physical media, through the air and the eye. And he adds: We cannot even properly think about Hashem. How then would you see Him, if you can barely conceive of Him? The elephant in the room Then comes the big problem: Moshe on Sinai after the golden calf. הראני נא את כבדך Show me, please, Your glory. (Shemot 33:18) לא תוכל לראת את פני, כי לא יראני האדם וחי You cannot see My face, for no man can see Me and live. (33:20) ושכתי כפי עליך עד עברי. והסרתי את כפי וראית את אחרי, ופני לא יראו I will cover you with My palm until I pass. Then I will remove My palm, and you will see My back, but My face will not be seen. (33:22-23) \"What on earth is going on here?\" (The Rabbi noted that the verse speaks of Hashem's palm , not a tallit. That image comes from the Gemara, which is a separate matter.) We have just established that one cannot see Hashem. How will Rav Saadia answer? Rav Saadia: the front and back of the created light He answers from what we learned in Chapter 10 about the prophets' visions. Hashem creates an image, called the Kavod Hashem . Moshe was asking: show me the Kavod , this amazing image You create. Hashem answers: I will show you. But know that the other prophets only see a kind of reflection of this image. You cannot see it in full from its beginning, because the beginning is too intense. Let it recede and dilute a little, and then you can see it. That is \"seeing My back.\" I will cover you, block your eyes, so that you do not see the beginning of this powerful image, and then you will see its back. Rav Saadia explains further: Hashem creates a special light and shows it to the prophets. When they see it, they say \"I saw the glory of God,\" and sometimes simply \"I saw Hashem,\" leaving words out ( derech chisaron ). His proof: about the elders at Sinai it says ויראו את אלהי ישראל , \"they saw the God of Israel\" (Shemot 24:10), and a few verses later ומראה כבוד ה׳ , \"the appearance of the glory of Hashem\" (24:17). And Targum Onkelos and Targum Yonatan already translate the first as וחזו ית יקר אלהא דישראל , \"they saw the glory of the God of Israel.\" Even the prophets cannot look at this light directly; they see diluted reflections. Moshe felt this was his opportunity to see the light itself. Hashem said: its beginning is too much. I will cover you with My \"palm,\" which is the cloud of glory , a blindfold if you like, until the intense beginning passes. Then I will remove it and you will see the back : the less intense part of the vision. But seeing God Himself is impossible. The Rabbi noted that Ibn Ezra says something similar in his commentary on Iyov, explaining the covering as a kind of cloud that envelops the person, and that Rabbeinu Bachya also took on Rav Saadia's concept of the created glory. A participant connected this to the mirrors : the other prophets see through many mirrors, while Moshe sees the actual image, but even that, not at its most intense. \"That fits very nicely with panai and achorai ,\" said another: the intensity at the beginning and the receding at the end. The Rambam The Rambam in the Moreh says that all these visions are deep intellectual understanding , not physical sight. But, the Rabbi read, the Rambam then adds that if someone finds it hard to understand what it means to \"see\" God intellectually, and wants to read it more literally, he may take it as seeing with the eyes, as long as what he sees is a created light and not God. \"He doesn't say Saadia, but he's referring to that. That's not what I believe it means, but that's acceptable. I won't criticise you for understanding that.\" Three interpretations What is \"front\" and \"back\"? Rav Saadia The front and back of the created image . Moshe saw the less intense part of the Kavod, in his mind but as a real vision The Rambam Depth of understanding . \"Front\" is the deepest understanding of Hashem, beyond any human. \"Back\" is the most a human can reach, which Moshe attained. There is no image at all Some Ba'alei HaTosafot Read it literally: a front and back of Hashem passing by. (Rejected) Richard had made the point last week that Rav Saadia's \"seeing\" is still in the mind. \"You're right,\" said the Rabbi. It is a seeing in the prophet's consciousness, a spiritual creation, a special vision. \"Even the greatest prophet is not seeing anything physically which you can touch. But in your mind you have this very physical manifestation of this creation.\" Marie asked what front and back mean at all. A participant suggested each person has a different limit to his mind, and Moshe was the least limited. \"Everything you've said so far is correct,\" said the Rabbi. The question is only whether Hashem was giving Moshe a deep understanding (the Rambam) or showing him a vision and its less intense part (Rav Saadia). Several participants felt Rav Saadia's reading fits the text better. The Rabbi agreed: \"Saadia's interpretation is much clearer and much more understandable in the text, whereas the Rambam's is very floppy: front and back are just ways to describe deeper or less deep understanding. Saadia is beautiful. The front of the image, the very intense part, I can't show you. But I can show you the back of the image, the Kavod Hashem. It's a much better interpretation.\" And it explains panim el panim , \"face to face,\" with Moshe: a more direct, clearer, more intense vision. Panim always refers to the more direct. Chaim 's takeaway: the moment you give any option of seeing Hashem, you reduce Him. \"Exactly. That's the key takeaway.\" Once more, the Kabbalah The Rabbi again saw a parallel with the Kabbalah. All the Sefirot go back to the Or Ein Sof , but the Kabbalists stress that the Or Ein Sof is not Hashem Himself. It is an emanation. Each of the ten Sefirot contains ten Sefirot, so perhaps Moshe reached the top Sefirah but not its own highest point, its \"back\" rather than its keter . Richard asked whether this was forcing the Kabbalah. The Rabbi linked it to the Or HaGanuz , the hidden light: had we stayed in Gan Eden we would understand this light far more, and Olam HaBa means understanding HaKadosh Baruch Hu. \"But even in Olam HaBa, you're never going to understand Hashem Himself. Closer, but of course, it's impossible. That's our definition of God.\" Chapter 13: Final Questions With fifteen minutes left, the Rabbi began the last chapter of the essay, with a word of encouragement for anyone who had found Essay 2 too difficult. \"When we start in September it gets much easier. It's still a philosophical book, but it'll be more practical. Please don't become disillusioned.\" Chapter 13 has a few final questions and then \"a beautiful essay, really beautiful, on the love of Hashem,\" which he believes the Rambam drew on. Question 1: How can we know something non-physical? If Hashem has nothing physical, how can we perceive Him at all? Rav Saadia's answer: we all understand and accept that lying is bad and truth is good . Have you ever touched that? We understand that something cannot be present and not present at the same time, and other impossibilities. We have never seen these, yet we know them to be true. In the same way, though we cannot see Hashem, we can understand Him as far as possible. A participant objected that \"lying is bad\" might be a social construct, learned from experience because lying leads to bad outcomes, and the same might be said of logical impossibilities. \"It's a fair point,\" said the Rabbi. \"I don't think we're able to prove that either way.\" Question 2: How can Hashem be everywhere? \"This is going to be a machloket between the Chassidim and the Ashkenazim as well, the Gra and the Ba'al HaTanya.\" How can we say there is nowhere without Hashem, when there is a world here? The Rabbi noted that the song \"Hashem is here, Hashem is there, Hashem is truly everywhere\" represents only one opinion in Jewish theology, the Lubavitch one, not that of the Gra. Rav Saadia answers: Hashem existed before there was any space, as discussed in Chapter 11. If creating physical things caused any division or gap in Him, He would not have created them. His presence after creation is exactly as it was before, without any effect, as the verse says, הלא את השמים ואת הארץ אני מלא , \"Do I not fill heaven and earth?\" (Yirmiyahu 23:24). \"We can't get our heads around it, because we think: my body is here, this earth is here, so where does Hashem go in all of that? But from Hashem's perspective, it's all the same.\" His examples: some walls do not stop sound . Glass does not stop light. Sunlight is not affected by all the dirt in the world. Physical things can exist without affecting what passes through them. \"I don't really think they are marvels nowadays, science has shown us how these things work,\" but that is how Rav Saadia illustrates it. Shir HaYichud The Rabbi asked whether anyone knew Shir HaYichud . Its author is uncertain, but some believe it was Rav Saadia. In Europe some would say it at the end of every Shacharit, a song for each day of the week, and the Mishnah Berurah quotes it. Few communities say it daily now, but many Ashkenazi communities still say it on the night of Yom Kippur. He read from the song for Tuesday, and asked the class whether it sounded familiar: all the walls cannot divide You; the dirt does not soil You; You are in everything . These are the very examples Rav Saadia gave: sound through walls, light through glass, the sun untouched by dirt. \"So some believed that this was written by Rav Saadia. The whole thing is about Hashem being everywhere.\" Summary Action: Hashem does not act physically. \"He made\" means He willed. \"He rested\" means His will to create stopped. \"He spoke\" means He created speech. \"I kept silent\" means He stopped it. Against the Mutakallimun, created speech can cease. Creating and sustaining differ, like imagining a universe and then simply holding it in mind. Hashem \"remembers,\" but the Torah never says He forgets , only that He \"did not remember.\" All attributes of action (merciful, gracious, vengeful) refer only to creatures. Rav Saadia is the first to say so explicitly. Names of essence (one, eternal, wise) can only be understood negatively and are all one: the Rambam's negative theology, Rabbeinu Bachya, Rav Yosef Albo, and the foundation of the Ramchal's Yichud Hashem. Being acted upon: Hashem receives nothing, including sight. We can barely think of Him, let alone see Him. \"You will see My back\": for Rav Saadia, Moshe saw the less intense part of the created Kavod , after Hashem shielded him from its intense beginning with a cloud. For the Rambam, front and back are degrees of understanding. The Rabbi found Rav Saadia's reading clearer and better. Chapter 13: we know non-physical truths like honesty and logical impossibility, so we can know Hashem. And creation does not divide Hashem's presence, like sound through walls and light through glass, as the Shir HaYichud puts it. Coming Up Next Monday, the last shiur before the summer break: A fascinating teshuvah of the Maharshal about those who did not want to say Shir HaYichud. How this relates to the dispute between the Vilna Gaon and the Ba'al HaTanya on Hashem's presence in the world. Rav Saadia's essay on the love of Hashem . A theological question Rav Saadia is the first to raise: can Hashem do the impossible?","8uwy4gyeQWw":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 2, Chapter 11 (categories 3 to 8: feelings, relation, place, time, possession and position) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 8 July 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: The Ten Anthropomorphisms The Rabbi planned to continue until the end of Essay 2, this week, next week and perhaps the week after, then break for August and resume in September. Since Chapter 8, Rav Saadia has been working through the ways the Tanach describes HaKadosh Baruch Hu in physical terms. His general principle: we must always understand them metaphorically , because a literal reading goes against our intellect and against our tradition. He showed that Targum Onkelos and Targum Yonatan ben Uziel read such verses metaphorically, and that the Torah uses organs of nature too, as in קפאו תהמת בלב ים , \"the depths congealed in the heart of the sea,\" where nobody thinks the sea has a heart. Then came the ten categories: # Category Covered 1 Substance Impossible, since Hashem created all substance, and the verses show He is above every level of existence 2 Quantity No length, height, size, division or multiplication. One commentator says Rav Saadia dwelt on this here because of the book Shi'ur Komah , which speaks of Hashem's \"measurements\" Prophecy The Kavod Nivra , the created glory that Hashem reveals to the prophets The created glory, once more When the verse says וירא אליו ה׳ באלני ממרא , \"Hashem appeared to him [Avraham] in the plains of Mamre,\" it is speaking bederech chisaron , with a word missing. It means the Kavod of Hashem was manifested with the three angels. \"Chas veshalom, you can never see Hashem, because there's nothing there to see.\" The visions of Yechezkel and the chariot are of an image Hashem creates. As Richard said last week, it is all happening in the prophet's mind, but even in his mind he is not seeing God Himself. He is seeing the image God created there. What then was special about Moshe? Rav Saadia does not use the idea of the aspaklaria , the mirrors. For him, Moshe's uniqueness, the principle that there is no prophet like Moshe, was that Hashem spoke to him directly , in his mind. With all other prophets it was the image, the intermediary angels, that spoke. And in all prophecy the prophet knows for certain that he is in prophecy. \"There's no such thing as doubtful prophecy. If you're a prophet, you know 100% this is the Kavod Hashem.\" 3. Happenings and Feelings The Rabbi noted that the word mikrim is usually translated \"accidents,\" which is not quite right. It means happenings , and here mainly feelings . Hashem can have no happenings or changing circumstances, because He created all circumstances and happenings. Verse Meaning כי ה׳ אהב משפט , \"Hashem loves justice\"; Hashem loves righteousness; כי באלה חפצתי , \"for in these I delight\" (Yirmiyahu 9:23) Whatever Hashem commanded us to do is called \"loved\" by Him. He commands it strongly שש הנה שנא ה׳ , \"six things Hashem hates\" (Mishlei 6:16), and \"all these I hate\" Whatever He commanded us not to do is called \"hated\" רוצה ה׳ את יראיו , \"Hashem desires those who fear Him\"; רצית ה׳ ארצך , \"You favoured Your land\" (Tehillim 85:2) When Hashem wants to reward , the Torah says He is pleased. \"A perfect God cannot want\" וחרה אף ה׳ בכם ועצר את השמים , \"Hashem's anger will flare and He will close the heavens\" (from the Shema) Anger means punishment . The verse itself shows it: the \"anger\" is the closing of the heavens Real anger, will and hatred apply only to someone who wants and who is afraid. \"How can Hashem want something if He's created all? How can He be afraid of something if He's created all?\" So: if I want it, that is called \"I love.\" If you do it and I am pleased with you, \"I love you.\" If I do not want it, \"I hate it.\" If you do it after I told you I hate it, \"I am angry with you.\" \"Simple as that.\" 4. Relation The fourth category is relation , in the sense of yichus , a relational standing between two things: הללו עבדי ה׳ , \"Praise, servants of Hashem,\" as if Hashem has servants. Hashem as King , which requires subjects. משרתיו אש לוהט , \"His ministers are a flaming fire\" (Tehillim 104:4), the angels as His ministers. \"Those who love Hashem\" and \"those who hate Hashem.\" This is impossible, says Rav Saadia. Hashem was there when none of these things existed. Before He created anything, there was nothing to be King of, and yet He was God. He cannot now suddenly be in a relationship with what He created. So why call Him King? To convey grandeur . For us, the greatest thing is a king, one who can do what he wants. But not a king in the sense that a king needs subjects and servants, \"because He was that before.\" And \"lovers of Hashem\" simply means those who do His will . Mark asked about היום הרת עולם... אם כבנים אם כעבדים , \"if as children, if as servants,\" from Rosh Hashanah. That, said the Rabbi, is for us, to express our relationship with Hashem. But a father is incomplete without a son, and a king without subjects. You cannot describe Hashem like that, because it takes away from His completeness. \"We don't do anything for God\" Chaim drew the conclusion: God does not need any relationship with us. Everything we do, we do for ourselves. \"God doesn't need anything from you. He was there and He will be there, with you or without you.\" The Rabbi agreed: \"That's precisely what we're saying. It's very important. It's the core idea of Judaism.\" Even leshem shamayim , for the sake of heaven, does not mean we affect or influence God in any way. Chaim added that if you asked a thousand people, most would say they do mitzvot \"for God.\" \"That's why we're learning,\" said the Rabbi. Flora asked: doesn't mitzvah come from the root meaning to join ? So there must be some relationship, joining ourselves to Hashem. The beam of light The Rabbi answered with an analogy he loves: Remember the opening of the old Mr Bean films? There's a beam of light, and he falls into it. That's Hashem, if you like. Mitzvah, deveikut , attaching ourselves, means bringing yourself into that beam . The light is there regardless of what you do, whether you choose to come or not, whether you exist or not, whether you do a mitzvah or an aveirah. That beam will always be there. That's God. There's no relation. If you want, you can bring yourself there. The word mitzvah is connected to joining and cleaving. \"You're bringing yourself nearer to that spotlight, and you're coming closer to God. But God's God, and God is there whether you like it or not, and whatever you do.\" 5. Place Then there are the verses that seem to give Hashem a place : ושכנתי בתוכם , \"I will dwell among them,\" the Beit HaMikdash, and \"Hashem is in heaven.\" Rav Saadia rejects this for three reasons: He created place. How can He need it? He existed before there was any place. If He did not need a place then, how can He need one now? Place belongs to physical things. A place gives stability, a surrounding, a context. God needs no framework to ground Him. What we were taught as children \"Ask any child,\" said the Rabbi, and this is the problem: \"We're taught things as children and we're never taught how to develop from that.\" The Rambam already says that we teach children in stages: learn Torah and you get sweets; when you are a little older, learn Torah and you get money; and so on, until you learn for its own sake. \"We develop our children and teenagers very much in their halachic skills, their reasoning skills, their analytical skills. But in their theological skills we remain with what we were taught as kids.\" You were taught that Hashem is in heaven, so if you want to daven, look up at the sky. \"There's no such thing as Hashem in the sky. Hashem is everywhere.\" Why \"in heaven\"? So why does the Torah speak of Hashem in heaven, if He is no more there than here? The Rabbi explained: who do you look up to more, physically? A tall person more than a short one. Generally, taller people seem to carry more gravitas. \"We value things which are high. It's just something psychological in our brains.\" So to tell us how important Hashem is, the Torah speaks of Him being in heaven. A participant suggested another angle: when these verses were written, the heavens were unreachable . That was the story of the Tower of Bavel: they thought they could build up to reach the unreachable. \"Heaven\" conveys separation: we are here, one kind of existence, and there is another beyond reach. \"Nice. Very nice,\" said the Rabbi. \"He doesn't say that, but that's interesting.\" The same applies to אשא עיני אל ההרים , \"I lift my eyes to the mountains\" (Tehillim 121:1): Hashem is not in the mountains. \"We forget it, but you have to realise this was groundbreaking for the time, 1,100 years ago.\" The Rabbi read from Rav Moshe Taku , the Tosafist from last week, who quotes Rav Saadia's Sefer HaEmunot on exactly this point, that when the prophets say Hashem is in heaven above it is only a way of glorifying Him, and responds with verses of his own, accusing Rav Saadia of arguing with the Torah. \"So they understood, when it says Hashem is in heaven, Hashem is in heaven. It's fascinating, but that's how people believed.\" Rav Saadia's two explanations Saying Hashem is in a place is a way to magnify that place . God is not there any more than anywhere else. \"I will dwell among you\" refers not to Hashem Himself but to the Shechinah , the created glory, just as with the prophets. The Kotel: What Does Shechinah Mean? This led to the longest discussion of the evening. What do we mean when we say it is more effective to pray at the Kotel? Flora said the Shechinah was there; you feel His presence, the kedushah of the place. The Rabbi pressed her: \"What does Shechinah mean?\" To dwell. \"Are you telling me God dwells in the Temple more than He dwells elsewhere?\" \"Flora, let's get something very clear. The way you feel about something doesn't mean that it's true. That could purely be because you've been taught something. What you feel isn't a proof. I want you to explain to me rationally what it means that the Shechinah is there.\" Marie suggested it is purely psychological. But the Torah does say there is a dwelling there. So, the Rabbi explained, there are two possibilities following Rav Saadia: Explanation Meaning The created glory Something really is there, but it is a creation of Hashem, not Hashem Himself. Just as He created an image for the prophets, this godliness rested in the Beit HaMikdash and was more perceptible there An important place Nothing is there that is not everywhere. \"I dwell there\" tells you this is an important place, as heaven is \"important\" Mark offered a third idea: the place is holy because mitzvot were done there day in, day out, every moment, like \"the lovers of Hashem\" being those who constantly do His will. \"Which is very nice,\" said the Rabbi, adding it to the list. And the Rabbi was firm on the practical side: \"Chas veshalom to think that Hashem is there more than anywhere else. If we go to the Kotel and tell our kids, 'Hashem listens here better,' that's terrible. It's completely the wrong message to children.\" Notes in the wall Marie asked about the notes people place in the Kotel. The Rabbi said there is no source for it; it is a very new, modern practice. At best it helps you collect your thoughts. \"But trust me, if you just pray the words, it's exactly the same.\" The problem is that people think Hashem picks up the note and listens to it more if it is in the wall, so they push it very high or very deep. \"That's ridiculous. That's kefirah . That's all the wrong things put together.\" God cannot be in a stone. Kissing the stones is fine, because it is the holiest place you can reach, whether because of the mitzvot done there or because the created Kavod of Hashem was there. And crying is fine, \"because you want the Beit HaMikdash to be restored.\" Flora said she does not put notes in the wall and can pray anywhere, \"but I just feel there is something there.\" The Rabbi agreed: \"It's a holy place, Flora, 100%. The Torah tells us the Shechinah was there.\" The question is whether a person feels it because of the real kedushah , \"which I imagine is the case with you,\" or because he has been told it is holy and makes himself feel it. 6. Time Hashem has no time, for three reasons: He created time , as we learned in Essay 1. He existed before there was time. If so, He cannot become subject to it once it exists. Time measures physical things. What is not physical is above time. Rav Saadia brings three verses that seem to put Hashem in time, \"some more difficult than others\": Verse Problem Rav Saadia Ibn Ezra ומעולם עד עולם אתה אל , \"from everlasting to everlasting You are God\" (Tehillim 90:2) Seems to place Him in time Through all eternity Your actions have been to help us. \"Pretty straightforward\" לפני לא נוצר אל ואחרי לא יהיה , \"before Me no god was formed, and after Me there will be none\" (Yeshayahu 43:10) What is \"before God\"? A past, present and future? \"We say it, we don't think about it\" Not before Me , but before My actions : before and after I sent the prophet, I am the same God. The passage is about His prophet Before I was revealed to the people, and after גם מיום אני הוא , \"also from the day, I am He\" (Yeshayahu 43:13) From that day He is God, and not before? From the day of the Revelation at Sinai, I am the One commanding you From when there was a day at all, the first day of creation: I have always been there For \"after Me\" meaning \"after My actions,\" Rav Saadia brings Natan the prophet's words to Batsheva, ואני אבוא אחריך ומלאתי את דבריך , \"and I will come in after you and confirm your words\" (Melachim I 1:14): after your action, not following your person. On the third verse, the Rabbi commented: \"Quite a lot of additions to the verse, according to Rav Saadia. Not the smoothest. Ibn Ezra has a much simpler explanation.\" But both are trying to escape the same problem: implying a time limitation on God, which is unacceptable. Does interacting with us put Hashem in time? Two participants pressed the point. The Torah describes Hashem acting causally and in sequence: He speaks with Moshe after something happens, He \"is comforted,\" He changes His mind. Doesn't that put Him in time? The Rabbi's answer: \"If He's going to interact with humans, He has to play ball with human time.\" That is not a limitation on Him. \"From us there has been a change. This was ten minutes ago and this is now, because we live in time. From Hashem's perspective there is no 'was'. It's all happening in one go. I know it's hard for us to understand what that means, but we have to accept it.\" He compared it to the Rambam on free will, which they had learned in Pirkei Avot and the Shemoneh Perakim. Moshe was on the mountain for forty days, \"but that's all from Moshe's side, for the people downstairs. In God's perspective there's no forty days.\" (\"Is God comforted, does He change His mind\" is a separate question about emotion, already covered.) 7. Possession Hashem owns everything. So what does it mean when the Torah calls us His: עם סגולה , His treasured people, His possession, חבל נחלתו , the lot of His inheritance, כי חלק ה׳ עמו , \"Hashem's portion is His people\"? Are we His more than a non-Jew is? No. It is to show importance . Among humans, your treasure chest is where you keep the best things. \"You are My treasure\" means \"you are very important to Me.\" It works the other way too. The pious speak of Hashem as their portion: ה׳ מנת חלקי , \"Hashem is my portion\" (Tehillim 16:5). And \"the God of Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov\" or \"the God of the Hebrews\" does not exclude anyone else. He is the God of all, but this expresses importance, treasure and love for His pious ones. 8. Position: Standing and Sitting God is not physical, so He has no position: no sitting, no standing, no going up or down. Only physical things sit or stand. He existed before these things existed. A position is a relation between two things: you sit on a chair, you stand on a floor. It would introduce diversity and change , and we learned at length earlier in the essay that there is no change in Hashem. Verse Meaning וישב ה׳ מלך לעולם , \"Hashem sat as King forever\" (Tehillim 29:10) He is permanent and enduring , like someone who sits in his place forever קומה ה׳ , \"Arise, Hashem,\" and smite Your enemies Act quickly . Deal with the enemies quickly ויתיצב עמו שם , Hashem \"stood with him there,\" at the Thirteen Attributes (Shemot 34:5) \"This is more difficult.\" It is stated so plainly that Rav Saadia says it refers to the Shechinah , the created image, which stood there וילך ה׳ כאשר כלה לדבר אל אברהם , \"Hashem went, when He finished speaking to Avraham\" (Bereishit 18:33) Again the created light \"So it's interesting,\" the Rabbi noted. \"Sometimes Saadia is happy to say it's a metaphor. But other times he says, actually, it's literal, but it's not God. It's the Shechinah which He has created.\" Summary Feelings: Hashem's \"love\" means His command to do, \"hate\" His command not to do, \"desire\" His reward and \"anger\" His punishment. Only one who wants and fears has real feelings. Relation: Hashem was God before there was anything to be King of. \"King,\" \"servants\" and \"Father\" are for us, to express importance. We do nothing for Hashem ; mitzvot bring us into the beam of light that is always there. Place: He created place and existed before it. \"In heaven\" conveys importance, or the unreachable. \"I will dwell among them\" means either an important place or the created Shechinah . Never teach children that Hashem listens better at the Kotel. Time: He created time and preceded it. Verses about \"before Me\" and \"from that day\" refer to His actions or to the Revelation (Rav Saadia), or more simply to His revelation and eternity (Ibn Ezra). He enters our time only because we live in it. Possession: He owns everything. \"Treasured people\" expresses love and importance. Position: He neither sits nor stands. \"Sat as King\" means permanence and \"Arise\" means speed. Where the verse is too plain (\"He stood with him there\"), it is the created Shechinah. Coming Up Next Monday: Chapter 12, categories 9 and 10, actions of Hashem and Hashem as the recipient of action, including the episode in Ki Tisa of \"you will see My back, but My face shall not be seen.\" Then Chapter 13, a few final questions, and a review of the whole essay the following Monday.","v7UlhkC-hfc":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 2, the end of Chapter 10 (organs used of nature; the prophetic visions and the created glory) and the opening of Chapter 11 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 1 July 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are in Essay 2 The essay began with the unity of Hashem . Not only that there are not two, but more importantly that there is no diversity and no change in HaKadosh Baruch Hu. All His \"character traits\", as we call them, are included in His oneness. Then came a long discussion of the Trinity : its philosophical problems, the verses brought for it, and the different Christian beliefs about it. Last week we did Chapters 8, 9 and most of 10, where Rav Saadia began on the anthropomorphic descriptions of Hashem in the Tanach. His principle: whatever the Torah says that gives Hashem something physical, and whenever we speak of Hashem in human terms, it is metaphor. His proofs: Hashem cannot have any substance, since He created all substance . Verses show Hashem is above every level of physical existence. As discussed in Essay 1, if Hashem had made the world out of Himself, why would one part of Him choose to become this and another that? He then set out ten categories in which we speak of Hashem. The first was substance (a hand, a leg) and the second quantity (length, depth, width, being divisible or added to). All of these would limit Him and take away from His completeness and infinite essence. And our tradition agrees: Onkelos , a thousand years before Rav Saadia, translated \"the hand of Hashem\" in the plagues as \"a plague from Hashem.\" Rav Saadia is the first to say this explicitly, the Rabbi stressed. Those before him did not say it in so many words, but the way they interpreted the verses shows they did not take them literally. The Organs of Nature To close the point, Rav Saadia shows how often the Tanach gives organs to inanimate things , and how we read these as metaphor without even noticing. \"Just like you immediately interpret these things metaphorically, so too when it comes to HaKadosh Baruch Hu.\" He counts twelve. Organ Verse What it really means Head וראש עפרות תבל , \"the head of the dust of the world\" (Mishlei 8:26) The beginning Eye וכסה את עין הארץ , the locusts \"will cover the eye of the land\" (Shemot 10:5) The whole surface. Rashi explains it as covering the sun. A participant suggested \"as far as the eye can see.\" \"That's another interpretation of the Rishonim. Very good.\" Ear והאזיני ארץ , \"give ear, O earth\" (Yeshayahu 1:2) Bear witness Face על פני הארץ , the quails covered \"the face of the land\" (Bamidbar 11:31, in Beha'alotecha, read a couple of weeks earlier) Everywhere you looked Mouth ותפתח הארץ את פיה , \"the earth opened its mouth\" and swallowed Korach (Bamidbar 16:32, that week's parasha) It opened up Wing מכנף הארץ , \"from the wing of the earth\" (Yeshayahu 24:16) From the edges, as wings are at the edge of the body Hand על יד היאר , \"on the hand of the river\" (Shemot 2:5) Alongside, since your hands are at your sides Lip על שפת היאר , Moshe placed \"on the lip of the Nile\" (Shemot 2:3) On the bank, as the lips are at the edge of the face Heart בלב ים , \"in the heart of the sea,\" as we say in the Shirah (Shemot 15:8) The depths, the midst Navel על טבור הארץ , \"on the navel of the land\" (Yechezkel 38:12) The centre Belly מבטן שאול שועתי , \"from the belly of the grave I cried\" (Yonah 2:3) From the depths of despair Womb בגיחו מרחם יצא , the sea \"burst forth from the womb\" (Iyov 38:8) It forced its way out, like a baby from its mother Thigh מירכתי ארץ , \"from the thighs of the earth\" The far corners of the earth Just as our senses force us to read these twelve metaphorically, because nature plainly has no such organs, so our intellect tells us the God we believe in cannot have them either. And if you insist on following the literal text, you will have to say the earth and the water have these twelve organs, \"and that's impossible.\" So too every similar expression about Hashem: the prophet's plea הטה אלהי אזנך ושמע, פקח עיניך , \"incline Your ear, open Your eyes\"; Hashem saw, Hashem heard, Hashem spoke, Hashem thought. Each has its interpretation. \"And Hashem smelled\" Rav Saadia singles out one verse as especially harsh: after the Flood, וירח ה׳ את ריח הניחח , \"Hashem smelled the pleasing aroma\" of Noach's offering (Bereishit 8:21). \"It's hard for us to accept.\" It means Hashem accepted the offering, and then made the covenant. Rav Saadia's proof is מריח מים יפרח , a tree will sprout \"from the scent of water\" (Iyov 14:9). Plants do not grow from the smell of water. Logic tells us it means from receiving water. In the same way, \"Hashem smelled\" means \"Hashem accepted.\" The Real Problem: The Prophetic Visions Now Rav Saadia comes to the final problem in this section. Fine, we interpret \"He heard\" metaphorically. But the prophets describe visions in which Hashem appears, as it were, in very anthropomorphic images: The form of a man, seated on a throne. The throne like a sapphire stone , and above it the likeness of a person (Yechezkel). ראיתי את ה׳ יושב על כסאו וכל צבא השמים עמד עליו מימינו ומשמאלו , \"I saw Hashem sitting on His throne, and all the host of heaven standing by Him, on His right and His left\" (Melachim I 22:19). How do we interpret this? What we would answer, and what the Rambam answers The Rabbi asked the class. Based on everything so far, we would say: Hashem is incorporeal, so the throne and the angels on the right and left are the nearest the prophet could come to describing the indescribable. The prophet never literally saw a throne. These are metaphors. \"That's what the Rambam says.\" Rav Saadia: the visions were real, and they were created \"Saadia does not say that. Saadia feels that this is too explicit to be interpreted metaphorically. He understands this to be literal.\" But how can it be literal if Hashem has no body? Here Rav Saadia says something fascinating: Yes, the prophets really saw these images. But they were created . The throne, the ministering angels on the right and the left: the Almighty created them, to show the prophet that He is there. This is something unique that only the prophets saw, with great grandeur. This is called the Glory of God , the Kavod . Daniel describes the same: ועתיק יומין יתב , \"and the Ancient of Days sat\" on a throne (Daniel 7:9). It is a recurring image, and it is called Kavod Hashem . This, says Rav Saadia, is what the Sages meant by the word Shechinah : a created image, physical and anthropomorphic, displayed to show the prophet that he is in the presence of something immense. Sometimes there is no human figure, and the manifestations differ, but all of them are Kavod Hashem . The proof: Hashem does not speak to the prophets Rav Saadia's proof is striking. Yechezkel says Hashem told him: בן אדם עמד על רגליך ואדבר אתך , \"Son of man, stand on your feet and I will speak with you\" (Yechezkel 2:1). Rav Saadia says that cannot be Hashem Himself speaking, because the Torah says there will never be a prophet like Moshe , with whom Hashem spoke. So in all other prophecies it was never Hashem Himself speaking to the prophet. It was a created image , of an angel or a throne, which Hashem creates in order to relate and speak to His prophets. Sometimes the text names the angel, and that is a created being. And where it says simply \"Hashem spoke\" or \"Hashem appeared\" to anyone other than Moshe, Rav Saadia says a word is missing , as Hebrew sometimes omits a word. It means the Glory of Hashem, the angel of Hashem, something of Hashem, not Hashem Himself. Why this is so surprising The Rabbi pointed out that the verse does not actually say Hashem never speaks to other prophets. It says: ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה אשר ידעו ה׳ פנים אל פנים There never again arose a prophet in Israel like Moshe, whom Hashem knew face to face. (Devarim 34:10) The usual understanding is that panim el panim means Hashem spoke to Moshe directly and clearly , with little barrier, compared with the other prophets. The class had learned this in Derech Hashem and Da'at Tevunot years earlier: the Sages speak of the aspaklaria , the \"mirror\". You can see something through a whole chain of reflections, mirror after mirror. Different prophets saw through different numbers of mirrors. Moshe had only one barrier; the others had many. \"Rav Saadia doesn't understand that. Rav Saadia understands that Hashem Himself never spoke to the prophets. Really quite remarkable.\" Two things we learn about Rav Saadia There is a limit to metaphor. Rav Saadia is willing to read things metaphorically, but \"he doesn't go full on.\" Organs, hands, legs, standing and sitting can all be interpreted. But when the prophet says \"I saw\" , that is literal, and he has no licence to make it a metaphor. So the prophets did see these things, but what they saw was an image Hashem created, not Hashem. Prophets never meet Hashem Himself, only His created glory. Rav Saadia The Rambam Did the prophets see a throne and angels? Yes, literally No , it is all metaphor What was it? A created image: the Kavod Nivra (created glory), with the Dibbur Nivra (created speech) relayed through it They saw the middot of Hashem: mercy on the right, judgment on the left Does this give Hashem a body? No, because the images are creations No, because nothing physical was seen \"I think this is quite a chiddush . When I saw this, it took me time to process it, because I wasn't used to this concept. Not the way people are used to understanding it.\" A Parallel in the Kabbalah The Rabbi added a point \"to make us aware of, don't worry if we don't fully grasp it.\" In the Kabbalah too, even when we speak of Hashem, we are never talking about Hashem Himself . The highest level beyond the ten Sefirot is the Or Ein Sof , the Infinite Light. Even that is not Hashem Himself, who is inconceivable and cannot be grasped in any sense. It is an emanation , the highest and most direct one. \"That strikes me as remarkable, that Rav Saadia and the Kabbalists are both saying the same thing in very different terms. They don't know of each other, but they are essentially saying the same thing.\" The class discusses Moshe A participant summarised: Moshe communicated directly with the Almighty, while the other prophets only received messages, as in a dream. Another noted that from that contact Moshe's face shone so that he had to wear a veil, and suggested it changed him physically in ways we lack the tools to understand. The Rabbi agreed that \"words are not the tool to describe something which is beyond words.\" But he insisted there is still a large gap between the Kabbalists and Rav Saadia. According to the Kabbalists and the Ramchal, no human being, not even Moshe Rabbeinu, could communicate directly with HaKadosh Baruch Hu. There is always an aspaklaria . Moshe heard Hashem with one barrier; the other prophets with many more. According to Rav Saadia, panim el panim means Moshe did communicate directly with the divine, while the others saw only something created. \"Which is something the brain cannot comprehend.\" Richard asked whether Rav Saadia really means something was created, since in the Ramchal prophecy was about visions and awareness of emanations, not created things. Is it more than a dream image? The Rabbi's understanding: not a physical throne actually standing there, but a created appearance , which Hashem makes so that the prophet can communicate. And the appearance is very corporeal: the prophet in Daniel truly saw someone seated on a throne. For the Rambam and the Ramchal, by contrast, the prophet saw no such thing. As the class learned in the Ramchal, the essence of prophecy is that the prophet knows he is communicating with the divine, and understanding is imparted to him through images. Why Is This Discussion Under \"Quantity\"? Rav Saadia is working through ten categories: substance, quantity, then \"happenings\" (Hashem loving or hating), relation (Hashem as King), place, and so on. Why did he put the whole long discussion of organs and prophetic images under the second category, quantity, rather than the first, substance? One of the commentaries on Rav Saadia suggests an interesting answer. There was an early mystical text containing \"extremely crude descriptions of HaKadosh Baruch Hu\": the length of His legs, the size of His hands, and so on. There is historical evidence it was known in the Gaonic period. So Rav Saadia put the discussion under quantity , to dispel any belief that such a book should be read literally. (In this class the Rabbi named the book as Sefer HaBahir , which some Rishonim attributed to Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah , the first-century Tanna who also composed the prayer Ana BeKoach . In his recap the following week he referred to it as Shi'ur Komah , the text that describes Hashem's \"measurements\".) \"Be extremely careful learning it,\" the Rabbi warned. \"We cannot accept any of this. If you want to accept it, the only way is metaphorically.\" A participant mentioned the later teaching of Rabbi Chaim Vital linking each part of the body to something above, so that repairing a part of yourself repairs something in heaven. The Rabbi said that is something else entirely, and much later, in the 16th century. Ana BeKoach A participant asked about Ana BeKoach, which we say every day in the korbanot of Shacharit and which seems to go to \"a different level.\" The Rabbi said it depends on one's orientation, and it can simply be said as a prayer, but it is meant to be very mystical. The Ben Ish Chai writes, and the Rabbi had seen older Iraqi and Indian congregants do this, that everyone stands for it because of its great significance. He remembered Charlie Morris standing for it, closing his eyes and going quiet at the end. Not Everyone Agreed: Rav Moshe Taku The Rabbi wanted the class to appreciate something. \"I don't think any of us seriously believe in a God who has any kind of corporeal description.\" But this was not accepted by everyone. Rav Moshe Taku , one of the Ba'alei HaTosafot , in France around the 12th century and quoted in halachic works, wrote that Hashem has physical attributes. In fact, \"believe it or not, Rav Moshe Taku accuses Rav Saadia of heresy .\" He brings: בצלמו : tzelem means something physical. The Midrash that at the Sea they saw Hashem like a young warrior , and at Sinai like an old man , a grandfather full of mercy. The Gemara that in the World to Come Hashem will make a circle of the righteous , standing in the middle, and each tzaddik will point to Him. He read all of these literally. The Rambam: the opposite extreme The Rambam made incorporeality one of the Thirteen Principles of Faith . The Rabbi noted that it is the only one of the thirteen with no explicit source in the Torah or the Talmud. Even Mashiach is clearly in the Gemara. Nowhere does it say Hashem has no body, and yet the Rambam included it and wrote that whoever denies any of the principles is a heretic. \"Which means, according to the Rambam, Rav Moshe Taku was a heretic.\" The Ra'avad , the Rambam's harshest critic, commented that there were people greater than the Rambam who held that Hashem has a corporeal aspect. He did not say he held it himself, but he sympathised. The Rabbi also mentioned an academic essay trying to show that even Rashi believed in a corporeal God. \"That's not so clear,\" but especially around France it may have been a more accepted belief. So, the Rabbi said, this explains why Rav Saadia goes to such lengths, and why the Rambam was so harsh. He doubted that any rabbi or sefer of the last 500 years has put forward such a view. \"I cannot accept that. I think we have to say the Rambam and Rav Saadia are right. I cannot believe in a God who has any kind of physical essence. Rationally Rav Saadia brings good proofs. As for the verses, I don't think they are conclusive either way. But as Rav Saadia told us, whenever something goes against our logic and rational analysis, we have to interpret it metaphorically.\" Chapter 11: Happenings. Does Hashem Love and Hate? The third category is accidents , meaning happenings. Hashem has no happenings and no feelings, because He created all happenings and feelings. Where Scripture says Hashem likes, loves, hates or detests something, it means this: Anything Hashem commanded is called \"loved\" by Him. Not that He actually loves it, but that it is one of His commandments, telling us that we too should pursue it. So כי ה׳ אהב משפט , \"Hashem loves justice\" (Tehillim 37:28), means it is what He desires and commands. Whose words are these? This sparked a sharp debate. One participant argued that even \"wanting\" or \"choosing\" limits Hashem, and that human language reduces God. He added that it was David HaMelech, a human, who wrote \"Hashem loves justice.\" David dreamt at night and wrote in the morning. Only Moshe saw God, so these are David's words, not God's. The Rabbi pushed back. Tehillim is divinely inspired, the words of Scripture, and he offered an analogy: Think of boys reading the parasha. The words are exactly the same, the same Torah, the same te'amim . Yet you can hear which teacher taught each one, from the way he sings and pronounces the words. Every person brings a tiny twist of his own. The Sages say something similar about Devarim . The first four books are Hashem's words dictated. The fifth is Hashem's words \"with Moshe's twist.\" The Rabbi acknowledged that Devarim is clearly a different kind of text, with far more expressive language. The Sages were bothered by this themselves, and answered that it is Moshe describing what he was told. The prophets likewise all bring the same core messages, which the Rabbi summarised as: stay away from idols, keep Shabbat, and look after the widow and the orphan . Each says it in his own way. So when David HaMelech wrote Tehillim, he had visions and wrote them with his own twist, \"but those words are still visions from HaKadosh Baruch Hu. That's our belief.\" The participant replied that good Torah reading is about understanding the words and knowing where to put the emphasis, not about the tune. \"If you don't like the analogy, leave the analogy,\" said the Rabbi. The point stands: the prophets convey Hashem's message with their own human personality, while the Torah is word for word. And when David says \"Hashem loves justice,\" it means this is what His commandments are. Rav Saadia develops this in the rest of the chapter. Summary Twelve organs of nature (the head of the dust, the eye of the land, the mouth of the earth, the lip of the Nile, the heart of the sea...) are read as metaphor without a second thought. Reason requires the same for Hashem. \"Hashem smelled\" means Hashem accepted, as a tree sprouts \"from the scent of water.\" The prophetic visions of thrones and angels are, for Rav Saadia, literal but created : the Kavod , the Shechinah, the created glory and created speech. Hashem never spoke directly to any prophet but Moshe. Where the text says \"Hashem spoke,\" a word is missing: the Glory of Hashem. There is a limit to metaphor. Organs can be interpreted, but \"I saw\" cannot. The Rambam, by contrast, reads the visions entirely as metaphor. The Kabbalah similarly holds that even the Or Ein Sof is not Hashem Himself, though for the Kabbalists not even Moshe had direct contact. Why under \"quantity\"? Possibly to answer an early mystical text that measured Hashem's limbs. Not everyone agreed: Rav Moshe Taku accused Rav Saadia of heresy. The Rambam made incorporeality a principle of faith. The Ra'avad said greater people than the Rambam held otherwise. The Rabbi sides firmly with Rav Saadia and the Rambam. Chapter 11: Hashem has no feelings. \"Hashem loves justice\" means justice is His command. Coming Up Next week: the rest of Chapter 11 , the other categories in which we speak of Hashem, then a couple more chapters on this theme.","8jrawLeRyBA":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 2, Chapter 8 (subtlety and strength; the principle of metaphor), Chapter 9 (the ten categories; substance and quantity) and the opening of Chapter 10 (the ten bodily descriptions) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 24 June 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: The Story of Essay 2 So Far Chapter Content 1 The five things the prophets tell us about Hashem: one, living, omnipotent, omniscient, incomparable 2 Proofs that there can be only one deity, against the dualists 3 Verses that use both Hashem and Elohim, or otherwise seem to imply plurality 4 Hashem as living, omnipotent and omniscient, all in one, with no diversity in Him. It is wrong to think of Him as having distinct qualities 5 Philosophical arguments against the Trinity 6 Verses that seem to support the Trinity, and Rav Saadia's answers 7 The four categories of Christian belief about the Trinity in his day Following up on last week, the Rabbi mentioned that someone had pointed out to him afterwards that the doctrine of the Immaculate Conception was only formally accepted by the Catholic Church in 1854 . It shows that over a thousand years ago, in Rav Saadia's time, there was still a wide diversity of opinion about what exactly the Trinity meant. That completes the discussion of Yichud Hashem. For the rest of Essay 2, Rav Saadia turns to anthropomorphism : verses that seem to give HaKadosh Baruch Hu some physical essence. \"He is the first rabbi we know of to address this head-on, directly.\" Chapter 8, Part 1: Can the Most Subtle Be the Strongest? Before he gets to anthropomorphism, Rav Saadia asks one more question. We have established that Hashem is the most subtle of all , with no diversity. But how can the most subtle also be the strongest ? Isn't that an oxymoron? Look at reality, he says. Reality tells you that the more subtle something is, the more powerful it is : Subtler Coarser Who is stronger? The soul The body The soul directs the body Wisdom The soul Wisdom is even more abstract, and it directs the soul in how to direct the body Water Earth Earth is crass and material, water flows and is lighter, yet water seeps in and uproots the earth Wind Water Wind is intangible, yet it moves the water Fire Wind Fire is the strongest of all (he goes into some of the old science of fire) This is why we find wisdom specifically attributed to HaKadosh Baruch Hu: ה׳ בחכמה יסד ארץ Hashem founded the earth with wisdom. (Mishlei 3:19) So Hashem's being the most subtle of all does not take away from His strength. On the contrary, the more subtle, the stronger. Chapter 8, Part 2: The Foundational Principle Now Rav Saadia turns to the question of physical descriptions, which will occupy several chapters. \"Everyone after him really accepted this,\" the Rabbi said, then corrected himself: \"I'm not being precise there, because there were Rishonim, believe it or not. I know this is shocking. One of the Ba'alei HaTosafot writes explicitly that HaKadosh Baruch Hu has physical attributes. That's a very, very difficult concept to understand, but we have to reject that.\" Rav Saadia will give convincing proofs, from Scripture, from logic and from tradition, that we cannot attribute any physical qualities to Hashem. Substance and happening He begins: we hear believers speaking about Hashem as if He listens to our prayers, hears us, sees everything we do. Let me first set out a principle: Any hint of substance (an ear, an eye, a nose) or of happening (\"Hashem came down to see Sodom\", \"Hashem stretched out His hand\") is inappropriate to say of the Creator. Not a little inappropriate. It is false. Why? We established in Essay 1 that the Creator created all physical things. There is no substance and no event in this universe that He did not create. It is ludicrous to describe Him with one of the things He Himself created. Three points It is rationally impossible to attribute any substance or happening to Hashem. All verses in Scripture that seem to imply physical attributes must be given an explanation zulat hagashmah , other than the physical one, \"until it sits well with our logic.\" When we speak with each other, when rabbis and believers say \"Hashem is listening\" and so on, we are only borrowing the terms. There is absolutely no comparison to human properties. Once these three points are clear, says Rav Saadia, do not be troubled by what you read. Everything that follows, in these chapters and in all of Scripture, is built on this foundational principle. \"You build upwards from the bottom. You don't build from the top downwards.\" Whenever you find something in the Tanach that gives you doubts, follow this principle. Why does the Torah speak this way at all? The Rabbi raised a question Rav Saadia will address explicitly later: why use these expressions? Why give the impression that Hashem has an eye and an ear? Why not speak plainly? A participant answered that it helps people understand, by giving examples from human life. \"Very good,\" said the Rabbi, and added more: \"If we weren't able to use these expressions, we would have no words to talk about God.\" He mentioned a group of Muslim thinkers who held that one could not speak about God in any of these terms. They were mocked and lost any ability to teach, \"because they can never talk about Hashem.\" If you only allow purely spiritual terms, you have nothing to say except that He exists. Even the words \"the way Hashem relates to us\" imply some association. \"So you end up with nothing.\" A participant compared it to the Japanese koan: we know the sound of two hands clapping, but what is the sound of one hand? It is very hard to convey such things without human terms. \"Nice analogy,\" said the Rabbi. Chapter 9: The Ten Categories Rav Saadia now lists ten things that the Tanach seems to attribute to Hashem, and will go through each: # Category Example 1 Substance That Hashem is made of something 2 Quantity A certain height, length or width 3 Quality 4 Relation Friend of one, enemy of another: \"I loved Yaakov, and Esav I hated\" 5 Place Verses about Hashem sitting on a throne 6 Time As if Hashem were bound by time 7 Possession Verses about Hashem owning something 8 Position Hashem having a certain status relative to others 9 Action Hashem acting 10 Being acted upon Hashem as the recipient of action He spends most of his time on the first three. \"And again, this is foundational, because no one has done this before him. We're so used to it, but never forget that he's doing this for the first time.\" And of course he chose ten, \"because he loves the number ten.\" 1. Substance People debate what Hashem is \"made of\": Some say He is like a human, only a huge and very powerful one. Some say He is fire , from the verse כי ה׳ אלהיך אש אכלה הוא , \"Hashem your God is a consuming fire\" (Devarim 4:24), \"which we read on Tisha b'Av\". They picture a huge ball of fire. Some say He is air. Some say He is space . (The Rabbi noted that the Hebrew edition's word here, kesef , silver, is a mistranslation from the Arabic. It should be space : they believe He is one great space.) Rav Saadia's answer: since He is the Creator of humans, of fire, of air, of space and of all existence, as established in Essay 1, logic tells you none of these can be part of His essence. The five levels of existence Rav Saadia shows the same from Scripture. There are five levels of existence (leaving humans aside), and for each the Tanach tells us not to compare Hashem to it. The Rabbi noted that Rabbi Yehuda HaLevi does something similar in the Kuzari about a century later. Level Verse Inanimate : the most precious are gold and silver למי תדמיוני ותשוו... הזלים זהב מכיס וכסף בקנה ישקלו : \"To whom will you liken Me and make Me equal... those who pour gold from the purse and weigh silver on the scale\" (Yeshayahu 46:5-6) Vegetation : the most precious is the cedar , the tallest and most beautiful tree, to which the righteous are compared Yeshayahu's mockery of the idol carved from a tree: neither the image nor the cedar is comparable to Him Animals The Torah's warning not to make an image of any animal, bird, creeping thing or fish (Devarim 4) Stars \"To whom will you liken Me? Lift up your eyes on high and see who created these\" (Yeshayahu 40:25-26) Angels כי מי בשחק יערך לה׳ : \"Who in the heavens can be compared to Hashem?\" (Tehillim 89:7) On the double expression in Yeshayahu (\"to whom will you liken Me, and make Me equal\"), Rav Saadia explains it works both ways: nothing is comparable to Hashem, and Hashem is not comparable to anything. These verses gather together all five levels of existence and push us away from comparing Hashem to any of them. \"This should be your foundation\" whenever you meet a problematic verse. \"In the Image of God\" Now Rav Saadia faces one of the most difficult verses in the Torah: ויברא אלהים את האדם בצלמו, בצלם אלהים ברא אותו God created man in His image, in the image of God He created him. (Bereishit 1:27) The Rabbi asked the class how they had always understood it. Flora suggested it means that just as Hashem is full of mercy and kindness, we should follow His ways, though she noted this is difficult since we have been told His middot are not really describable. She added Tehillim 8: ותחסרהו מעט מאלהים , \"You have made him a little less than the divine.\" The Rabbi gave the classical approach : tzelem means we have, as it were, the same power as God. Not identical, but the same kind of ability. Just as HaKadosh Baruch Hu creates and can destroy, we can create and destroy, physically and spiritually. When we do the right thing we create spiritual worlds. When we do the wrong thing we destroy them. Rav Saadia's reading Rav Saadia takes tzelem to mean a physical form. \"And to be honest, he's right,\" the Rabbi said, \"because tzelem normally has the connotation of something physical.\" In modern Hebrew, letzalem means to take a photograph. Rav Saadia: This is simply an expression of honour and distinction for the human being. All lands belong to Hashem, yet He says \"My land\": עיני ה׳ אלהיך בה , \"the eyes of Hashem your God are always on it\" (Devarim 11:12). Does He own this land more? No, it signifies special importance. All mountains belong to Hashem, yet He says \"My mountain.\" All forms of all creatures, fish, animals and birds, are His, and He distinguished one of them by calling it \"My form.\" So according to Rav Saadia, the form is physical, but \"My form\" means \" this is My favourite form of all the forms I have created ,\" not \"I have a form too.\" A lively debate Chaim found it weak: \"It's very general, and it's not convincing, to me at least.\" He suggested that something in our form and construction lets us act almost on the level of God, giving the human power to go beyond himself. The Rabbi said that is the approach of the Nefesh HaChaim , from about 200 years ago, and is quite acceptable, but it means tzelem is entirely spiritual. Rav Saadia wanted to keep the word's physical meaning. The Rabbi offered an illustration of Rav Saadia's point: \"If I have a class of students and I really like one of them, I'll say 'this is my child'.\" Chaim objected that the Torah is extremely precise, and if it meant \"this is Mine,\" it would have said so. The Rabbi replied that every interpretation here involves some assumption. Another participant noted that idols are also called tzelamim because they are formed things, and wondered how one can be made \"in the form\" of One who has no form. A participant offered her own simple approach: Hashem has no form at all. \"Tzelem\" means we should do as Hashem tells us in the Torah, which He wrote before creating the world. \"I don't know if it's too simple.\" The Rabbi said it was an absolutely fine explanation, and that she and Chaim were really saying the same thing: that we are godly. \"But just remember, you take away the simple meaning of tzelem .\" Rav Saadia, who is very precise about verses, would not do that, so he keeps tzelem physical and reads the possessive as love and distinction. \"A consuming fire\" What about אש אכלה ? Does it mean Hashem is fire? No: He is like a consuming fire to those who deny Him. Scripture often drops the letter כ , \"like\": Verse Meaning ויוצא אתכם מכור הברזל ממצרים : \"He took you out of the iron furnace, out of Egypt\" (Devarim 4:20) Egypt was not literally an iron crucible. It means as if from a crucible David to Shaul: אחרי כלב מת אחרי פרעש אחד , \"after a dead dog, after a single flea\" (Shmuel I 24:14) Meaning like a dead dog, like a flea The warriors whose faces were the faces of lions (Divrei HaYamim I 12:9) Their faces were like lions' faces A participant expanded on the crucible: you take many pieces of iron, melt them and forge something new. That is why the Exodus took so long. Hashem had to break the people down and purify them. \"That's precisely the point he's making,\" said the Rabbi. He took them out as if from a smelting furnace, but the kaf is missing. 2. Quantity Quantity has two aspects, both impossible for Hashem: Dimensions : length, width, depth. Addition and division : adding a part to Him or taking one away. Rav Saadia proves this in three ways: Proof Content Logic The same rationale as before: dimensions, addition and subtraction are all things Hashem created. He cannot be subject to what He created Scripture The warning at Sinai: you saw no image of any kind, so make no form of this or that, and many more verses Tradition The Targumim The evidence of the Targum The Rabbi explained the background. The two main Targumim are Targum Onkelos , by the convert Onkelos, around the first century, and Targum Yonatan ben Uziel . The Gemara speaks of the Targum's importance, and rules that each week one should read the parasha twice with one translation, the Targum, which was the Aramaic vernacular of the time. Rav Saadia's point: whenever these Targumim meet a verse that seems to give Hashem physicality, they change it . Here, the Rabbi suggested, Rav Saadia is \"clearly taking a knock at the Karaites.\" Our Sages, who are trusted in their interpretation of the Torah, the students of the prophets, consistently steered away from physical readings, from their knowledge of the prophets and from their own reasoning. Verse Targum הנה יד ה׳ הויה במקנך : \"the hand of Hashem will be on your cattle\" (Shemot 9:3, the plague of pestilence) \"a plague from before Hashem\": the hand becomes a plague ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת הספיר : \"under His feet was like a sapphire brick\" (Shemot 24:10) \"under His throne of glory\" פי ה׳ : \"the mouth of Hashem\" \"the word (instruction) of Hashem\" כי בכיתם באזני ה׳ : \"you wept in the ears of Hashem\" (Bamidbar 11:18, from the previous week's parasha) \" before Hashem\" A participant objected that Hashem has no chair either. \"I understand,\" said the Rabbi. \"The chair also must be understood metaphorically. But the point is they're bothered by the feet, and they're taking it to something that is easier to read metaphorically.\" Chapter 10: Ten Bodily Descriptions Having shown from logic, Scripture and tradition that Hashem has no physical form, Rav Saadia goes through ten bodily terms the Tanach uses of Hashem: # Organ Verse 1 Head וכובע ישועה בראשו , a helmet of salvation on His head (Yeshayahu 59:17) 2 Eye עיני ה׳ אלהיך בה , the eyes of Hashem are always on the land (Devarim 11:12) 3 Ear כי בכיתם באזני ה׳ , you wept in the ears of Hashem 4 Mouth כי פי ה׳ דבר , for the mouth of Hashem has spoken 5 Lip ומוצא שפתי לא אשנה , I will not alter what has gone out of My lips (Tehillim 89:35) 6 Face יאר ה׳ פניו אליך , Hashem make His face shine on you 7 Hand יד ה׳ , the hand of Hashem 8 Heart ויאמר ה׳ אל לבו , Hashem said to His heart, after the Flood, that He would not destroy again (Bereishit 8:21) 9 Innards על כן המו מעי לו , therefore My innards yearn for him, for Ephraim (Yirmiyahu 31:19) 10 Foot נשתחוה להדום רגליו , we will bow at His footstool (Tehillim 132:7) Are these the only ten? Of course not, said the Rabbi. There is the nose : the verse about the anger of Hashem \"smoking\" ( יעשן אף ה׳ ). \"If you think of those cartoons of the angry man with smoke coming out of his nose, that's what the verse means literally.\" There is the outstretched arm , and many more. \"As I've shown you, Saadia loves the number ten. So he just picks ten. That's the only explanation I have.\" The richness of language All of these, says Rav Saadia, must be explained another way. Language borrows terms. \"That's the beauty, the richness of language.\" Verse Literally השמים מספרים כבוד אל (Tehillim 19:2) The heavens speak וים אמר אין עמדי (Iyov 28:14) The sea speaks אבדון ומות אמרו (Iyov 28:22) Death and destruction speak The stone that \"heard\" and will be a witness (Yehoshua 24:27) Stones hear The mountains that burst into song Mountains sing The hills that gird themselves with joy (Tehillim 65:13) Hills get dressed \"And many others which it would take me a long time to count.\" Obviously none of these are meant literally. The heavens \"speak\" in the sense that looking at them proclaims the glory of God. In the same way the Torah uses language when it speaks about HaKadosh Baruch Hu. And if you ask why the Torah does not just use plain words and spare us all these chapters, the answer is as before: with only plain language, we could not speak of Hashem hearing, seeing or being compassionate. \"All we would have is the existence of Hashem, nothing else to talk about.\" What each term means Rav Saadia then explains the ten: Term Meaning Proof from human usage Head Importance, eminence כבודי ומרים ראשי , \"my glory, and the One who lifts my head\" (Tehillim 3:4): you lift your head to someone important Eye Attention, hashgachah (providence) Yosef to the brothers: ואשימה עיני עליו , \"that I may set my eye on him\" (Bereishit 44:21). He did not want literally to put his eye on Binyamin Face Anger or favour Chanah after Eli spoke to her: ופניה לא היו לה עוד , \"her face was no longer [sad]\" (Shmuel I 1:18). The face expresses happiness or sadness Ear Listening and accepting Yehuda to Yosef: ידבר נא עבדך דבר באזני אדני , \"let your servant speak a word in my lord's ears\" (Bereishit 44:18). He did not go up and speak into his ear. It means \"please listen\" Mouth, lips Command, instruction \"By the mouth of Aharon\" means his instructions. שפתי צדיק ירעו רבים , \"the lips of the righteous shepherd many\" (Mishlei 10:21): his teachings, not his lips, shepherd Hand Ability and capacity Heart Wisdom Young people who \"lack a heart\" lack understanding Innards Compassion \"My innards yearn for Ephraim\": it is deep within Me Foot Dominion \"My enemies are under my feet\" Next: The Prophetic Visions The Rabbi previewed the next question. On Shavuot, a congregant came to him at the end of the first day and asked for his sermon to be on the Haftarah , the vision of the Merkavah , the chariot, in Yechezkel. \"The get-out answer for me was that the Mishnah says you're not meant to study the chariot of Yechezkel in public. The simple answer is, it's too hard for me to teach something like that.\" But the question stands. You have told me that \"the eye of Hashem\" means providence, \"the ears of Hashem\" means He listens, \"His heart\" means wisdom. Fine. But how will you explain the vivid visions of the prophets , who see chariots, lions and wings? That is for next week. Summary Chapter 8: the most subtle is the strongest. Soul over body, wisdom over soul, water over earth, wind over water. So Hashem's subtlety does not lessen His power: \"with wisdom He founded the earth.\" The foundational principle: no substance and no \"happening\" may be attributed to the Creator, who created all of them. Every such verse must be explained non-physically, and our everyday language about Hashem only borrows terms. Why the Torah speaks this way: without such language we could say nothing about Hashem but that He exists. The ten categories: substance, quantity, quality, relation, place, time, possession, position, action, being acted upon. Substance: not human, fire, air or space, since He created them all. Scripture rules out comparison with all five levels of existence, from gold to angels. Tzelem Elokim: for Rav Saadia, a physical form that Hashem singled out with love (\"My form,\" like \"My land\" and \"My mountain\"). The classical and Nefesh HaChaim readings make tzelem spiritual instead. \"A consuming fire\" means like fire. Scripture often drops the kaf . Quantity: no dimensions, no addition or division, proven by logic, by verse and by the Targumim , which consistently turn the physical into the non-physical. The ten organs (head, eye, ear, mouth, lip, face, hand, heart, innards, foot) are borrowed language, as when the heavens \"speak\" and the hills \"dress in joy.\" \"I just love it. It's beautiful to see the way Rav Saadia puts this, the first one putting this forward, how he explains it and how detailed he goes into this, and how important it clearly is to him and to all those that followed him.\" Coming Up Next Monday: the prophetic visions, then the remaining seven categories. The Rabbi hoped to finish Essay 2 within a week or two and move on to Essay 3, \"more practical things, and more chiddush .\" The Tuesday evening shiur that week was moved to 8.15 to 9.15.","bo3scXCgi_k":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 2 (the Unity of the Creator), Chapters 1 to 3 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 10 June 2024, on Zoom (the night before Erev Shavuot) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are We have studied the eight chapters of the Introduction in detail, then spent several weeks on Essay 1 , all about the creation of the universe. We are now in Essay 2 , on Yichud Hashem , the unity of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Last week's introduction to the essay made one main point: the more subtle an argument, the more theoretical and abstract it becomes. Rav Saadia warned us not to expect something concrete at the end of an investigation into the unity of Hashem. \"This is definitely a sound logical argument nowadays as well. The deeper you go into something, the more abstract it becomes.\" The plan for tonight was three short chapters: Chapter Subject 1 The five things the prophets tell us about Hashem, and three rational proofs that He is one 2 Refuting the dualists , who say there are two gods 3 Verses that seem to imply more than one deity, such as Hashem Elohim Chapter 1: Five Things the Prophets Tell Us Rav Saadia begins: HaKadosh Baruch Hu has told us through the prophets that He is: # Attribute Verses brought 1 One שמע ישראל ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד (Devarim 6:4), and ראו עתה כי אני אני הוא ואין אלהים עמדי , \"See now that I, I am He, and there is no god with Me\" 2 Living : He is involved in the universe \"Who of all flesh has heard the voice of the living God speaking?\" There is revelation and interaction. And Yirmiyahu: Hashem is the living God 3 Omnipotent ידעת כי כל תוכל ולא יבצר ממך מזמה , \"I know that You can do all, and nothing is withheld from You\" (Iyov 42:2), and Divrei HaYamim: everything is Yours, Hashem 4 Omniscient A verse in Iyov: we cannot even fathom the depths of God's wisdom 5 Incomparable Tehillim: there is none like Him \"We have all the miracles in the Torah to verify this,\" but Rav Saadia does exactly what he promised in the Introduction: first he brings the verses, and then he shows that philosophical analysis reaches the same conclusion. The thirteen chapters of Essay 2 will discuss each of these five. The first three chapters deal only with the first: unity . A word on \"attributes\" The Rabbi was careful about the word: \"I'm careful to use the word attributes , because like we will discuss, there is no way to give attributes. Part of God's simplicity is that there is no division. There are no parts to Him.\" When we speak about the thirteen attributes of mercy , or these five attributes in Rav Saadia, we do not mean HaKadosh Baruch Hu has different parts. As the Rambam teaches, the attributes describe how He relates to us as humans and how He is revealed to us, not what He actually is. \"We have to be always careful and have that in mind.\" The two roots of error Rav Saadia says that those who deny the unity of God fall into one of two mistakes: They compare God to His creatures, to everything else that exists, and do not see that He is entirely different. They seize on every word. The Rabbi loved Rav Saadia's phrase here: such a person \"catches us with every word\" the Torah uses to describe God, reading each one physically and literally, not as metaphor. Rav Saadia will discuss later in the book when the Torah may be read metaphorically. The Rabbi mentioned he had given a shiur on this about a year earlier, based on Rav Saadia and the Rambam's approach in the Moreh Nevuchim. Three Proofs That Hashem Is One Proof 1: Number belongs to matter In Essay 1, one of the arguments for a Creator was that there must be something distinct and different from everything else in existence. An essential feature of physical things is that they have number and quantity . So if we say there is more than one deity, we have given the Creator quantity, and He enters the ordinary laws of physical beings. This principle comes from Aristotle , and the Rambam also discusses it in the Moreh Nevuchim : anything that is many, anything with quantity, is matter. The essence of something that is not matter is that it has no quantity. \"Now, I don't think it's compelling, this,\" the Rabbi said, \"but this was an old argument.\" Proof 2: There is no need for a second All the proofs in Essay 1 established a Creator. Once we have established one, why posit a second? Nothing in those proofs calls for it. Proof 3: There is no proof of a second The third is related but goes further. Logically, I have only brought proof of one Creator. How can I then leap to a second? (Someone might say there is a reason to posit a second. That is what Chapter 2 deals with.) \"Three arguments, not extremely strong\" The Rabbi's assessment: \"Three arguments, not extremely strong, but rational, logical arguments.\" He added that today people struggle to believe in one God. The struggle is no longer two gods against one. But in Rav Saadia's day there were people who believed exactly that, the dualist Manichaean system from Iran that the class met in Essay 1, so he now turns to refute them. (He also noted that the Sefaria English translation stops here, \"so maybe someone can take the initiative of writing the translation. All of Sefaria is only people writing translations, so everyone needs to contribute.\") Chapter 2: Against the Dualists Rav Saadia says that any argument he uses to disprove dualism is also a proof for monotheism. He touched on this in Essay 1, but now he adds more. Argument 1: Why stop at two? I ask the dualists: if you want more than one deity, why only two? Why not three, or four? Why not say everything in existence has its own god? Once you posit two, where is the limit? They answer: the many different circumstances of life can all be put into two categories . Everything is either beneficial or harmful, useful or destructive. So there are two deities: a god of light, of benefit and of doing good to humanity, and a god of darkness, destruction and evil. Rabbi Sacks on why dualism appeals The Rabbi brought a point he attributed to Rabbi Sacks's book Morality : people like dualism because it gives an easy way to understand the world. If you like things binary and black and white, with no grey, it fits perfectly: the god of the good and the god of the bad. Monotheism is harder to live with. We believe in One, yet we see darkness, problems and suffering, \"and that's sometimes too difficult for people to accept. So what's easier is, let's make two gods.\" Rav Saadia's reply: there are many other ways to divide the world Even if you divide things into beneficial and harmful, why stop there? Rav Saadia lists other natural divisions, any of which could just as well give you a pantheon: Category How many The senses (each beneficial or harmful) 5, so 5 × 2 = 10 Natural colours 7 Tastes 9 Elements 4 Categories of substance 10 Kinds of motion 6 Kinds of quantity 7 Tenses: past, present, future 3 On the nine tastes, the Rabbi read Rav Saadia's grouping: sweet and fatty, which are moist and hot; bitter, salty and sharp, which are hot and dry; astringent, sour and pungent, which are dry and cold; and the tasteless, like water. \"I don't know the details of what these nine are, but apparently they're nine.\" \"Remember I told you right at the beginning, he loves numbers. He loves categorising, quantifying.\" (In those days, he noted, they had no periodic table and spoke of four elements.) The dualists, says Rav Saadia, simply picked one number out of all the possible numbers. The Rabbi's difficulty with this argument The Rabbi was candid: \"I don't really find this argument very strong at all, actually. I'm positing two because I see good and bad in the world. There's no need to go more than what I have to. Once you're going up, you go up as much as you need to in your philosophy.\" A dualist who cannot accept good and bad from one deity goes to two, and two suffices. \"I find the whole thrust of his argument quite difficult, unless anyone's got another way of understanding it.\" A participant suggested Rav Saadia means subcategories of gods: within \"bad\" you could have war, pestilence and so on, each with its own deity. And Mark added that what one person finds useful another finds useless, so you would have to keep subdividing. \"In a sense people pick their deities.\" The Rabbi agreed: \"The pagans had all kinds of gods. Once you go into two, you can end up anywhere. I suppose that's what he's saying.\" Argument 2: How would two gods work together? The second argument is more philosophical, and the Rabbi called it strong. Suppose there are two deities and something is to be created: Option Problem They can only create together Then each one is incomplete. That is not the God we have described Each forces the other (light forces darkness to create good, and vice versa) Then neither is free or complete Each does what it chooses Then there would be constant conflict. The god of death says kill and the god of life says live, so things would be living and dying at the same moment, and everything would be a permanent mixture of light and darkness \"How are you going to understand this combination of the two deities? It's illogical, it doesn't make sense, and I think we can agree that's a strong argument.\" Rav Saadia anticipates further replies: perhaps one hides from the other when it gives life. Then it is not all-knowing. Perhaps there is a link between them. Then it is really one big God, or there must be a third thing between them. \"Take it to heart\" Having disproved more than one deity, we can accept what the Tanach tells us: אתה הראת לדעת כי ה׳ הוא האלהים אין עוד מלבדו You have been shown to know that Hashem is God; there is none besides Him. (Devarim 4:35) וידעת היום והשבת אל לבבך And you shall know this day and take it to your heart. (Devarim 4:39) The Rabbi dwelt on this. Yichud Hashem is not something you know once and are done with. It is so easy to see the darkness and suffering in the world and say there must be other powers. And when we do not recognise that everything that happens to us is from HaKadosh Baruch Hu, that too is a denial of Yichud Hashem. \"It's something which we need to constantly work on, which is why morning and evening we remind ourselves God is one. Not just one God and not two deities, but there's nothing else: no other forces, no other powers. You begin your day, everything from Hashem. You come home in the evening, everything from Hashem. You put your tefillin on, it's all about Yichud Hashem. That's what life is all about.\" He added that the Ramchal held that the whole of creation is about the revelation of Hashem's oneness . Summary of Chapter 2 Why stop at two? You could divide the world into any number of categories. How would two gods combine? They would be in constant conflict, things would live and die at once, and the world would be a far bigger mess than it is. In Essay 1 Rav Saadia showed that two gods could not have created the world. Now he shows that two gods existing at all makes no sense. Chapter 3: Hashem and Elohim What Rav Saadia does not say Before the chapter, the Rabbi asked the class: why does the Torah sometimes use the four-letter name and sometimes Elohim ? Everyone knows the answer: Elohim is midat hadin , the attribute of justice, and Hashem is midat harachamim , the attribute of mercy. \"That's a beautiful answer. But Saadia doesn't go on to that. Saadia doesn't say that at all in this chapter, which is fascinating.\" Rav Saadia had no Kabbalah in the form we know it today: no Zohar, no Arizal. Things we grow up hearing as children, he does not mention, whether because he did not know them or did not accept them. Flora objected that he must have known something, because the dualists were pointing to these very names. The Rabbi agreed that this is the question Rav Saadia is addressing. The documentary hypothesis Because Flora raised it, the Rabbi mentioned it briefly, though \"I don't like to create doubts in people's minds.\" The documentary hypothesis arose in Germany in the last couple of hundred years: the claim that the Torah is an amalgamation of four texts . It has since been debunked, he said, though other hypotheses have replaced it. One of its strongest arguments is that Bereishit chapter 1 uses only Elohim , while chapter 2 introduces the four-letter name ( ה׳ אלהים ). Supposedly these are two texts about two gods, later stitched together. Rabbi Soloveitchik in The Lonely Man of Faith \"really rips it apart\" and explains the beauty of why there are two accounts. \"It's a worthwhile read. It's a very hard read,\" because his English is so high-level. Rav Saadia is responding to a version of this question a thousand years earlier. He is not dealing with two authors, but with the claim that the two names mean two gods. Question 1: Why do the names change? Rav Saadia answers simply. If the Tanach used Hashem in some places and Elohim in others with no further information, you would have an argument for two deities. But Scripture explicitly tells us they are the same : כי כה אמר ה׳ בורא השמים הוא האלהים For thus says Hashem, Creator of the heavens: He is God. (Yeshayahu 45:18) דעו כי ה׳ הוא אלהים Know that Hashem is God. (Tehillim 100:3) And when all the people cried out at the altar, ה׳ הוא האלהים , \"Hashem is God,\" as we cry out in Selichot and on Yom Kippur. Once this is established, it should not bother us which name is used where. Gideon and Yerubaal Rav Saadia's example is two consecutive verses in Shoftim (8:29-30): וילך ירבעל בן יואש וישב בביתו. ולגדעון היו שבעים בנים Yerubaal son of Yoash went and dwelt in his house. And Gideon had seventy sons. Are there two people, one called Gideon and one called Yerubaal? No. The context shows they are the same person, the judge Gideon son of Yoash. Once we know that, it does not bother us when the text switches names. In the same way, once the Tanach has told us that Hashem is Elohim, the change of names is no problem. Rav Saadia does not explain why the Torah uses one name or the other, the Rabbi told Flora. \"It could well be that he accepted that\" ( midat hadin and so on), \"it could well be that he's just not going into that now.\" His point is only that once we know it is the same God, it makes no difference. Question 2: \"Hashem and God\" The harder case is where a verse joins two names with a vav , \"and\", which seems to imply two: אלהי ואדני לריבי My God and my Lord, for my cause. (Tehillim 35:23) וירעם מן שמים ה׳ ועליון יתן קולו Hashem thundered from heaven, and the Most High gave forth His voice. (Shmuel II 22:14) Who is Hashem and who is the Most High? Here the same speaker seems to name two gods. The answer, \"really not difficult\": the vav here means \"as well\" , adding a second reason or description. Who thunders from heaven? The One who is both Hashem and the Most High, because He is supreme and because He is Hashem. Rav Saadia proves it from: שמעו זאת כל העמים האזינו כל ישבי חלד, גם בני אדם גם בני איש Hear this, all you peoples; listen, all inhabitants of the world, both sons of adam and sons of ish . (Tehillim 49:2-3) Are \"nations\" and \"inhabitants of the world\" two categories of people, or adam and ish two kinds of human? Clearly not. It is one audience, described several ways for emphasis. Likewise יעקב עבדי וישרון בחרתי בו , \"Yaakov My servant, and Yeshurun whom I have chosen\" (Yeshayahu 44:2): Yaakov and Yeshurun are the same person. A key principle: when to read the Torah as metaphor Here Rav Saadia states, almost in passing, a principle the Rabbi said is important to remember: Wherever we, the believers in One God, find any text about God's actions or His creation that contradicts sound logic and proper analysis , we must say that the language is not to be taken literally. \"Nowadays we accept this. We know that many things are metaphorical. But in Saadia's time this was quite radical,\" because many believed the Torah must be read literally. He is willing to say: wherever something about God contradicts sound logic, do not be afraid to read it metaphorically. The Rabbi connected this to the Rambam in the Moreh Nevuchim , from an earlier shiur on Aristotle. The Rambam famously wrote that if Aristotle could prove the universe is eternal, he would reinterpret בראשית ברא אלהים . He even wrote that this would be easier for him than reinterpreting all the verses that seem to give Hashem a body: Hashem's hand, Hashem's arm. \"So Saadia is already preceding him by 200 years.\" Rav Saadia says he will not explain here how to interpret metaphorically, since he covered that in his introduction to the Torah. But later in this book he will discuss when a person has licence to do so. Question 3: \"Hashem sent me, and His spirit\" The third question, \"relatively the biggest,\" is: ועתה ה׳ אלהים שלחני ורוחו And now Hashem God has sent me, and His spirit. (Yeshayahu 48:16) This, the Rabbi noted, was a famous verse used by Christians to prove the Trinity. Read simply, there is God and there is His spirit, two distinct things: \"at least one and a half.\" Rav Saadia answers that for this to make sense it must mean ברוחו , \" with His spirit.\" He brings parallels: דרשו ה׳ ועזו , \"Seek Hashem and His strength\" (Tehillim 105:4), \"which we say every day,\" means seek Hashem in His might, not Hashem plus His might. ועליך יזרח ה׳ וכבודו עליך יראה , \"Upon you Hashem will shine, and His glory will appear upon you\" (Yeshayahu 60:2), does not mean two things. Hashem is revealed with His glory. \"You have to be willing to metaphorise it when necessary.\" Summary Chapter 1: the prophets tell us Hashem is one, living, omnipotent, omniscient and incomparable . Essay 2 will cover all five. The \"attributes\" describe how Hashem relates to us, not parts of Him. People deny unity either by comparing God to creatures or by reading every word literally . Three proofs of unity: number belongs to matter; there is no need for a second Creator; there is no proof of one. \"Not extremely strong, but rational.\" Chapter 2, against dualism: why stop at two, when the world divides into five senses, seven colours, nine tastes? (The Rabbi found this weak.) And how would two gods cooperate? They would be in constant conflict. (The Rabbi found this strong.) \"Take it to heart\": Yichud Hashem must be worked on daily, recognising that everything comes from Hashem. Chapter 3: Hashem and Elohim are the same, as Scripture says explicitly, just as Gideon is Yerubaal . The vav in \"Hashem and the Most High\" means \"as well\". \"Hashem sent me and His spirit\" means with His spirit. The principle: anything about God that contradicts sound reason must be read as metaphor, two centuries before the Rambam said the same. Much of this is basic and accepted today, the Rabbi said, \"but it's nice to see them in their original form, how these arguments were first articulated.\" Rav Saadia was writing against all the sects around him, and he goes to painstaking lengths even on things we now take for granted. Essay 3 will bring more chiddush . Coming Up Next Monday: Chapters 4 and 5 of Essay 2: the other attributes of Hashem, that He is living and omnipotent, philosophically and from the verses. Shavuot: a participant mentioned the custom of staying awake on the night of 4 Sivan, citing the Zohar on Parashat Balak. The Rabbi closed with the idea, first written by the Shelah HaKadosh about 400 years ago, that Shavuot is a Yom HaDin for our Torah . Just as Rosh Hashanah is about our vision and aspirations for the coming year, not only what we have done, so on Shavuot we are judged on our commitment to Torah, and over the next 48 hours we position ourselves and set our ambitions with regard to HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Chag Sameach.","D9r8RAPFUhw":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 2 (the Unity of the Creator), the Introduction, and the opening of Chapter 1 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 3 June 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are in the Book The Rabbi opened by placing the new essay in the structure of the whole sefer: Stage What Rav Saadia has done The Introduction (eight chapters) Laid down the basic foundations needed to understand the book: the sources of knowledge, and how we approach doubt and truth Essay 1 Established that there is a Creator : that the world has a beginning, that something external brought it into being, and that it was created from nothing . He then went through twelve other theories of how the world came to be and tried to refute each one Essay 2 (tonight) Now that we know there is a Creator, who and what is this Creator? How can we describe Him and talk about His attributes? So far, the Rabbi stressed, we have said nothing at all about what the Creator is. All we have shown is that there is one. The Rambam follows the same order The Rambam does exactly the same at the start of the Mishneh Torah. In Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah he does not begin with \"God\" or \"Hashem\". He begins with the Matzui Rishon , the First Existent, and only a few halachot later does he say that this primal being is the One we call HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Rav Saadia also first spent the whole of Essay 1 proving the existence of a first cause before saying anything about who that cause is. Why this essay matters Much of what Rav Saadia says in Essay 2 will sound familiar, the Rabbi said, but he is the first to lay it out in black and white : Anthropomorphism. He is the first to say plainly: do not take the Torah literally when it describes Hashem in physical terms, and he explains when we may and may not read it literally. We usually credit this to the Rambam, who fought hard against a physical understanding of Hashem, but Rav Saadia preceded him by about 200 years . The divine attributes. If Hashem is one, how do we understand descriptions such as Rachum and Chanun , merciful and gracious, or the Thirteen Attributes ? He does not mention it explicitly, but the same question applies to the ten Sefirot of the Kabbalah. These are things we have to reconcile, and Rav Saadia is the very first to discuss them. \"This is why I love this book so much. So many of the things he says are well known, but we forget who was the first one to actually lay down these principles, upon which the Rambam later worked, and many others after him.\" Essay 2 has an introduction and thirteen chapters , all short and to the point. Tonight was the introduction. The Point of the Introduction Before speaking about Hashem at all, Rav Saadia makes a point about how knowledge works : When we study science and work from data, the correct path is to move from the concrete to the abstract . All science begins with physical data in front of us. We extrapolate and build from it, and therefore the more you understand, the more abstract it gets . People make the mistake of thinking that the more they know, the more concrete and tangible their knowledge should become, as if it should be \"in my hands\". That is not how knowledge works. Each step you learn from the one before is less concrete than the last, because you started from what you could see, and from it you are drawing out things you cannot see. He will then explain why this principle must be understood before we discuss HaKadosh Baruch Hu and His attributes. The Rabbi noted that the English translation in the edition the class had been using stops here, so from this point he translated the Hebrew himself. The Principles of Knowledge אמר המחבר: אקדים במאמר הזה לאמר, כי תחלות המדעים גסות ותכליתם דקות The author says: I will open this essay by stating that the beginnings of the sciences are coarse and their ends are fine. The Rabbi announced that Rav Saadia lays out six principles, and listed them: The beginning of all knowledge is coarse and concrete ( gasot , literally \"large\" or \"thick\"), and its end is fine and subtle. Knowledge eventually reaches a final point. There is a limit to how much the human mind can reach. Our knowledge is finite . Understanding goes up in levels. Each level you go up is finer, more refined and more abstract than the one before. The final point you reach will always be the most abstract. When you reach it, that is what you want. And the warning that comes with the last point: do not make the mistake of wanting the end to be concrete. \"I've worked so long on this, I want to be able to touch it.\" If you try to do that, you are trying to go back to where you started, because you began with physical data. That perverts the correct way of studying science . Making the final knowledge concrete means bringing your science back down to the beginning. Rav Saadia then says he will explain where he gets each principle from, and afterwards why he needs them here. Principle 1: All science starts from the senses The Rabbi stressed that at this stage we are talking about pure science , with no theology and no esoteric meaning. \"I think this is still true today, that the scientific process necessitates starting with physical data.\" Anything discernible to the senses is something we can all know equally. I do not know it more than you do. In fact we have no advantage over animals here. They see and hear just as we do. What they lack is the intellect to move from the first level to the second. \"Anything which is equal between humans and animals, that's the most thick, the most concrete, the most crass.\" A human begins to think about the thing he senses. Why is it sometimes black and sometimes white, sometimes hot and sometimes cold? Then he thinks about quantity , then dimensions (length, width, depth), then the framework in which it sits and how it interacts with other things, then time : how long it has existed and how long it will last. Each step goes deeper and becomes more abstract. Just as the source was the most physical, the end product is the finest. What is a theory? \"You never call something which is sensible a theory. That would be an oxymoron. If I can touch it, it's not a theory.\" A theory is something I have reached in my mind and have proof for, but cannot show you to touch. The higher you go, the more theoretical and abstract your science becomes. A debate in the class A participant objected that in science you can eventually prove a theory physically, but here everything stays theoretical: \"You never see God. There is no way you take a telescope and see God.\" The Rabbi answered that \"we haven't said a word about God yet,\" and that once a theory is proven with factual data it is no longer properly a theory. How much proof turns a theory into fact is a fair question to come back to. Principle 2: Knowledge is finite Knowledge has a final point. Humans may never actually reach it, because no one spends long enough going deep enough, but in principle human knowledge is finite. Rav Saadia gives three reasons : # Reason 1 Man is finite. We are not limitless, and our intellect is one of our faculties, so it is finite too. This ties back to Essay 1, where he showed that the created universe itself is finite 2 We can only grasp something and call ourselves knowledgeable because it has an end. Knowledge with no end point could never be understood 3 The main reason. The source of all further knowledge is the senses and physical data, and physical data is finite . What you extrapolate cannot be greater than its source, so knowledge that starts from the finite cannot become infinite \"It may be very, very, very far, but there is a finite point where knowledge stops.\" Is the universe finite? This sparked a long discussion. One participant argued that there is no proof the universe is finite. The Rabbi disagreed: there is strong scientific evidence. \"Until the 1960s people would have said what you've said, but now almost the entire scientific community accepts that the universe is expanding,\" as seen in the redshift . Something expanding has a limit at any given moment. Others argued that what we can observe grows as our instruments improve, so we do not know where it ends. The Rabbi's reply was that \"not knowing\" is not the same as \"infinite\". We can only go by what we see, and what we know is finite. Richard pointed out how much science has advanced, with DNA and AI. The Rabbi agreed, but said that even these are grounded in physical things. DNA is an amazing discovery, but it begins with physical cells. \"We learn out incredible things from it, but it starts with something physical, and there is an end to how far you can go.\" Principle 3: You climb in knowledge, not in ignorance Knowledge has a root from which it grows, level upon level. Ignorance is not a thing in itself. It is simply the absence of knowledge. The Rabbi linked this to Essay 1, where Rav Saadia held (though this is disputed, a machloket ) that darkness is not a creation but simply the absence of light. In the same way, there is no creation called foolishness. Being ignorant just means not having enough knowledge. You progress in knowledge, but there are no levels of ignorance to progress through. Rav Saadia adds that if ignorance were itself a foundation that could breed, there would be constant conflict between levels of ignorance and levels of wisdom. \"I don't fully understand what he means there,\" the Rabbi admitted, \"but I think we can accept the idea that there is progression in knowledge, but there is no progression in ignorance.\" A participant compared it to binary : zero multiplied by a million is still zero. Put a one in front of it and you already have something. The Rabbi agreed: the moment you move out of the zero, you can build. Principles 4 and 5: The end is always the finest. The example of snow You look out of your window at snow falling. It looks like stones coming down: that is the crass level. Look carefully and you see it is just hardened water . Think further and you realise this water came from vapour that rose up. So the thing that looked like a stone is really vapour. Then you ask what made the vapour rise, and you keep going deeper. The final thing is always the finest and most abstract. If you try to get to the end and want it to be physical and concrete, \"you're corrupting how science works.\" The principles summarised All knowledge starts with the concrete and physical and moves to the abstract. The knowledge we can extrapolate, one thing from the next, is finite. We progress up in knowledge, but there is no progression in ignorance. The higher you go, the more abstract it becomes. The final point of knowledge is the most abstract, and you must not expect it to be concrete. Why Rav Saadia Brings All This Rav Saadia now explains why a discussion about science belongs in a book on beliefs. In Essay 1 he established that there is a Creator, yet people resist accepting it: The objection What they say \"I didn't see Him\" They want a Creator they can see and touch \"Too abstract\" The idea is too abstract to accept \"There's something beyond\" There must be something above this Creator: a god above the god above the god \"He's physical\" They accept a Creator but picture Him as a physical being. Others are not foolish enough to say so outright, but give Him some quality, quantity, place or time, confining Him within physical dimensions The principles are there to remove these mistaken thoughts. The central point, which the Rabbi said runs through the whole essay and which the Rambam uses constantly: The very fact that Hashem is so hard to comprehend shows that you have reached the end of the path . His abstractness does not take away from His truth. You might think that because God is so abstract He is less scientific, and therefore \"less God\". On the contrary: the deeper you go, the more abstract it becomes. People say, \"Science is what I can see. If I can't see it, it's not science.\" Rav Saadia's answer: \"That's precisely the point.\" Anyone who says God is too abstract to believe in has stepped out of scientific discourse, because if you want to reach something concrete at the end of your scientific progress, you have missed the point. \"Deep, deep, who can find it?\" Rav Saadia brings the verse עמק עמק מי ימצאנו , \"deep, deep, who can find it?\" (Kohelet 7:24). He recalls the opinions he refuted in Essay 1, that the source of all things is something very fine, like dust, or a hair, or the indivisible atom. The Creator is above even all of these. That is the final point of knowledge. The Rabbi called the next lines beautiful. Hashem is: deeper than all that is deep, stronger than all that is strong ( chazak mikol chazak ), more hidden than all that is hidden ( ne'lam mikol ne'lam ), higher than all that is high ( gavoah mikol gavoah ), \"until really we have no words for it.\" This, the Rabbi said, is almost exactly what the Rambam says two centuries later: we can only speak about God in the negative sense, not the positive, because He is so abstract. And Rav Saadia's message is that the abstractness should not tell you He is unreal. \"The deeper something is, the more abstract it becomes.\" He also brings the verses in Iyov (11:7-9): החקר אלוה תמצא... ארוכה מארץ מדה ורחבה מני ים Can you find out the depth of God? ... Its measure is longer than the earth and wider than the sea. \"There's no end to it, full stop. So beautiful.\" \"Can we talk about God?\" The Rabbi mentioned a talk he had given a few months earlier elsewhere with exactly that title, Can we talk about God? \"And the answer basically was no. We can't really talk about God. We can only talk about God in a negative sense,\" just as with the deepest scientific theories there is a limit to how far you can go. He reminded the class how carefully the book is structured: Essay 1, there is a Creator. Essay 2, this Creator is the one God. Essay 3, what this one God wants from us. \"Saadia is very organised, very numerical, very concentrated in how he puts forward his arguments.\" The Refutations The Rabbi then turned the question to the class: based on tonight's principles, how will Rav Saadia refute those who think God is physical? Flora answered: Hashem cannot be physical, because the deeper we go into something we thought was concrete, the less we can grasp it. \"Precisely,\" said the Rabbi. \"It's too abstract. That's exactly his point.\" 1. Against \"God is physical\" We began with physical data and went deeper and deeper. And what do you come out with at the end? Something physical? Then you have gone back to the ABCs. We are not looking for a Creator who is basically the same as one of us. We are looking for the Creator of the entire physical universe. Anything physical you can think of, the Creator created it. God is external, abstract, outside of everything physical. 2. Against \"there is a god above God\" Again the Rabbi asked the class, and the answer came: knowledge is finite. Once we have reached the Creator who is beyond, why posit more and more? Rav Saadia anticipates a reply: perhaps knowledge is finite, but we simply have not reached its end yet, and there is a creator above the Creator still to be discovered. His answer: all data begins with the physical. Once we have left the physical and posited something abstract and external to the universe, there is nothing that would lead us on to the next step. Once we reach a Creator outside the universe, we have reached the end. A participant added that our grasp of Hashem is limited by our own capacity. If our ability grew, we would understand more of Him. The Rabbi agreed: \"We are limited.\" It is our ability to understand that is finite. 3. Against the \"clever\" version: the square root of 10,000 Then there are those who do not say outright that God has a body, but say God rests, God moves, or God gets angry. After all, the Torah says וחרה אף ה׳ בכם , \"and Hashem's anger will flare against you.\" Rav Saadia says that in truth they are saying God is physical in substance, just not in so many words. The Rabbi loved the example: It is like someone who says: \"I'm not claiming a hundred pounds from Reuven. I'm claiming the square root of ten thousand pounds.\" He has not said the words, but it is exactly the same claim. Saying God occupies space or exists in time is the same as saying He is physical. Why he went on at length Rav Saadia says that although he normally likes to be brief and get to the point, he has spent time on this because these principles are foundational for the whole book and especially this essay, and understanding them now will prevent trouble later. Summary of the refutations Claim Refutation God is too abstract to be real That is how science works. The deeper the knowledge, the more abstract God is physical You have gone deep only to end up back at the beginning There is a creator beyond the Creator Knowledge is finite. Once we have left the physical and reached something external, there is nothing beyond God is not a body, but has space, time or emotion The square root of 10,000 pounds: the same claim in disguise Mitzvot: Physical Acts and an Abstract God Chaim asked: if God is so abstract, how are our physical mitzvot connected to Him? Perhaps Rav Saadia started from the physical in order to show the road in between. The Rabbi said Rav Saadia will discuss later why we have mitzvot and aveirot at all. \"We are physical people, we do physical activities, and how is that connected to something abstract?\" His basic answer: everything we do, we do for ourselves. \"The mitzvot are for us, never for HaKadosh Baruch Hu, and all the attributes of Hashem are all for us, not for Him.\" A participant added that there is a difference between mitzvot we understand, which change our character, and those that are totally beyond us. She said that, to her, mitzvot put us in harmony with the world , and through that harmony we connect to God. Another participant asked whether the word mitzvah means \"to join\". The Rabbi said it was a beautiful point made by Rav Dessler : the simple meaning is \"commandment\", but the root is related to the Aramaic צוותא ( tzavta ), togetherness and joining. \"Precisely. Very good.\" Why Is the Concept of God So Difficult? Rav Saadia closes the introduction with one more question: why does the concept of God need so much discussion? Two reasons: The more precious something is, the more analysis it needs. As is well known, it is easier to find glass than pearls. (A participant noted that pearls became precious because they were hard to find. \"It depends where you're looking,\" the Rabbi conceded, \"but the idea is there.\") Too many people have said too many things about it, whether because they did not understand, were not clever enough, did not pay attention, deliberately chose not to understand, or out of plain laziness. As he said at the beginning of the book, this is why the truth needs to be revealed. \"Now I've got rid of those who have these questions. Now let me talk about what we can say about God.\" Chapter 1: Five Things We Can Say About Hashem With a few minutes left, the Rabbi began Chapter 1, to be gone over again next time. He noted that the Rambam in the Mishneh Torah and the Ramchal in Derech Hashem also open by laying out what we can say about Hashem, and Rav Saadia does the same. What the prophets have told us is that He is: # Attribute Verses the Rabbi read 1 One \"See now that I, I am He, and there is no god with Me\"; \"Hashem alone leads him\" (Devarim 32). \"You could quote tens of other verses\" 2 Living \"Who of all flesh has heard the voice of the living God ?\" (Devarim 5); \"Hashem is the true God, He is the living God \" (Yirmiyahu 10:10). A living God means an involved God, not one who belongs only to the past 3 Omnipotent Nothing stops Him. He can do anything 4 Omniscient \"There is no end to His understanding\" 5 Incomparable \"There is none like You,\" as we say in Shacharit As Rav Saadia promised in the Introduction, we accept these from the prophets first, and then analyse them with the intellect . He brings verses for each, and will then explain them philosophically. Does God have a companion? A participant asked whether God has a wife or a friend. Based on tonight, the Rabbi said, no : that would make God physical, and HaKadosh Baruch Hu is beyond anything like that. He added that the question already assumes God is male. \"HaKadosh Baruch Hu isn't he or she. He's not male, he's not female.\" Humans need companions; that is a human concept. (He mentioned there is a Midrash along those lines and suggested discussing it offline.) Summary Essay 1 proved there is a Creator. Essay 2 asks who and what this Creator is. Rav Saadia is the first to set out plainly the rejection of anthropomorphism and the problem of the divine attributes, 200 years before the Rambam. Knowledge moves from the concrete to the abstract. It begins with the senses, which we share with animals, and each level is finer than the last. Knowledge is finite, because man is finite, because only what has an end can be grasped, and because its physical source is finite. There is progress in knowledge but not in ignorance, which is only an absence, like darkness. The end of knowledge is the most abstract. Wanting it to be concrete perverts science, as with the snow that turns out to be vapour. So God's abstractness is a sign of truth, not of unreality. He is deeper than all deep, more hidden than all hidden. The Rambam would later say we can only describe Him negatively. The refutations: too abstract (that is how knowledge works), physical (back to the ABCs), a god above God (knowledge is finite and ends once we leave the physical), and \"space and time but not a body\" (the square root of 10,000 pounds). Mitzvot and attributes are for us, not for Hashem. Chapter 1: the prophets tell us Hashem is one, living, omnipotent, omniscient and incomparable. We accept it from them, then analyse it with reason. Coming Up The Rabbi was away the following night, and some of the weekday classes paused around Shavuot. The Monday shiur would resume the next week with with Essay 2, Chapter 1 : the five attributes in detail, and then the attributes of Hashem and why He has no body.","6dFEiy9_xvo":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 1 (Creation), Chapter 1, Part 1: the scriptural sources and the first three of Rav Saadia's four proofs Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 8 April 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Where We Are The class had finished the whole Introduction (a summary is on the WhatsApp group) and begun the introduction to Essay 1 of 10 , on how creation came about . The essay has four chapters: Chapter Content 1 Four proofs for the creation of the universe (tonight: the first three) 2 Develops this further 3-4 Twelve other opinions , examined one by one, and related questions Why this is hard to prove In his introduction to the essay Rav Saadia admitted the difficulty. Of the three sources of knowledge from the book's Introduction: Sense perception: nobody was around to see creation. Self-evident axioms: creation is not one. Deduction: it is very hard to deduce something you have no sense perception of. His answer: don't worry , because every rival theory has the same problem and must also rely on deduction. \"Actually, you should know, my proofs are going to be stronger, so trust what I'm going to say.\" \"Very courageous of Saadia, to put forward his view with his proofs, then list all the other opinions, twelve of them, disprove them, and say: you know, mine are the best. Well, let's go through it and see if he's right.\" Part 1: Why the Rabbi Will Move Quickly Through Essay 1 \"In essence, this whole essay we could skip if we wanted to, because contemporary science has now proven that the world did have a beginning .\" Rav Saadia believes in creation ex nihilo , creation from nothingness: there was a \"time\" when nothing existed except the first existence, God, called in medieval literature the First Existent ( HaMatzui HaRishon ) , the First Cause. How long ago is not the question here: 5,700 years or billions of years \"makes no difference.\" The claim is only that there was a beginning . The three classical positions View Claim Who held it Aristotle God and the universe are both eternal ; they always existed and always will Aristotle Plato God created the world, but from eternal matter ( hyle , היולי) that always coexisted with Him Plato; among the Rishonim, Rabbi Avraham ibn Ezra Creation from nothing There was nothing but God, and He created everything Rav Saadia and the majority of the Rishonim The Rambam's struggle with Aristotle The Rambam loved Aristotle, \"one of his main philosophical teachers,\" and was troubled that Aristotle held the world to be eternal. In the Moreh Nevuchim : The Rambam brings seven proofs for creation, three of which are among Rav Saadia's four . His famous conclusion: Aristotle's arguments are not conclusive, so I have no need to accept them. If Aristotle had proven the eternity of the world, I would have reinterpreted the Torah to fit it. And that, he says, would have been easier than reinterpreting all the verses that seem to give God a body. On Plato , the Rambam wrote that he himself prefers creation from nothing , but that those who accept Plato's view have something to rely on : it can be made compatible with the Torah. The ibn Ezra read בראשית ברא אלהים that way: God created from eternal \"floating matter,\" and that matter is not itself called a creation. Rav Saadia rejects them all. \"Saadia is going to say that it is our belief that creation is from nothingness.\" Science and Yehi Or Today the debate is settled: the universe most definitely had a beginning. Redshift : the universe is continuously expanding, which means there was something at the beginning, however small, from which it all began. This fits the Big Bang , \"which is completely compatible with Torah.\" The Ramban at the beginning of Bereshit seems to indicate something like it. Chazal say everything was created on day one and only laid out over the six days. ויאמר אלהים יהי אור : the famous question of how there could be light before the sun on day four. One basic answer: Yehi Or was the light of the Big Bang , the initial spark from which everything shot out. \"So I'm going to go slightly fast through essay one, because we know the truth, both from science and Torah.\" But it is still worth learning \"how the medieval Hachamim used their logic and their philosophy.\" Part 2: The Thesis and the Verses Hashem has told us that everything is created, and that He created it from nothing , as it says בראשית ברא אלהים את השמים ואת הארץ . The word bara \"A famous argument brought by the Rishonim, and Saadia may have been the first to put it forward\": The word ברא is used only of God. Nowhere in Tanach is bara used of a human being. For people we find יצר ( yatzar , formed) and בנה ( banah , built) . Word Meaning יצירה ( yetzirah ) Forming from what already exists. A צייר ( tzayar ) , an artist, takes what is in front of him and forms it בריאה ( beri'ah ) A more intense kind of creation: creation from nothing The Rabbi connected this to the four worlds of Kabbalah : Asiyah (action, this world), then Yetzirah , then Beri'ah higher still. Beri'ah is higher than Yetzirah \"because yetzirah is forming from existence; beri'ah is creating from nothing.\" Rav Saadia also brings Yeshayahu: אנכי ה' עשה כל נטה שמים לבדי (Yeshayahu 44:24) I am Hashem who makes all things, who alone stretched out the heavens. Then philosophy Since the Torah has told us this and it is verified by signs and wonders, we accept it . Then Rav Saadia asks whether it can be proven rationally . \"This is the structure we said he would follow in each essay: put forward the theory, prove it from the verses, and then try to do it philosophically.\" If philosophy can prove it, why do we need the Torah? He answered this in the Introduction: some people cannot do it philosophically , so the Torah gives the facts. Our duty , once we have accepted what the Torah says, is to analyse it and see whether we can prove it philosophically too. \"There are many proofs. I'm only going to give you four.\" Part 3: Proof One: The Finite Universe Heaven and earth are finite in size. A finite body can only contain a finite force. And since the force within them is finite, they must have a beginning and an end. The argument as the Rabbi laid it out: The earth is in the middle and the heavens revolve around it. (Rav Saadia's geocentric astronomy.) So the whole system is finite . Everything has a force within it that sustains it; if the body is finite, its force must be finite. A finite force eventually stops, and what has an end must have had a beginning. Rav Saadia tests his own proof \"When this proof was told to me, I didn't speedily accept it until I had verified it. \" He raises three objections against himself: Objection Rav Saadia's reply Perhaps the earth is infinite in length and breadth Then the sun could not go around it every day and return to where it began, nor could the moon and stars Perhaps the earth is finite but the heavens infinite We see the heavens rotating around us. Something that goes round has a circumference, so it is finite Perhaps what rotates is finite, but beyond it there is more, static and endless Why assume something we have no perception of? We see nothing beyond what rotates around us Is the proof still any good? The obvious problem: it is based on a geocentric universe , which we now know is wrong. But the Rabbi argued that a version survives in a heliocentric universe: \"Just do it the other way round. How could the earth go around the sun if it were infinite?\" A rotation means a circumference, and a circumference means it is finite. A participant, Mark, agreed: whichever way you look at it, a rotation means an end. (When asked, the Rabbi clarified that this proof is about space ; time is the fourth proof.) He also cautioned against reading Rav Saadia anachronistically : \"Now we know there's much more beyond what we see than the millions of galaxies. But in those days all you had is the earth and the things around it. Why should you assume there's more behind it?\" For them, the only universe was the observable universe . \"Look, don't forget, we're talking about someone in the 900s. The science is going to be flawed, obviously. And he would be the first one, I'm sure, like the Rambam would be, to say: accept the science of the day. But the arguments can still somewhat stand.\" Many worlds? Then Rav Saadia raises something remarkable: perhaps there are infinitely many earths and heavens , each with its own observable rotation, going on forever in space and time. \"It's interesting that he thought of this.\" His rejection is scientific , not theological: By nature earth cannot be above fire , and earth will not sit above air or water, because fire and air are lighter, and earth and water heavier. If there were another earth somewhere else, it would fall through the air and fire and land on our earth ; another mass of water would come down through the atmosphere onto ours. \"They didn't know about gravity,\" someone noted. Exactly, said the Rabbi: \"It's very interesting. He was willing to accept there may be other universes, but felt it would be scientifically incorrect. Which means that nowadays, when this scientific reason is no longer true, then perhaps.\" He added that a later Spanish Jewish philosopher from Zaragoza concluded that parallel universes are possible , \"why on earth should we be so arrogant as to assume that all we have is our universe?\" The verses ולמקצה השמים ועד קצה השמים (Devarim 4:32) From one end of the heavens to the other end of the heavens. Moshe is exhorting the people: has any nation, from one end of the earth to the other, witnessed anything like Har Sinai? \"Clearly that's just exhorting the people,\" but Rav Saadia reads in it that heaven and earth have an end . \"It's an interesting proof. I don't know how convincing it is.\" וזרח השמש ובא השמש (Kohelet 1:5) The sun rises and the sun sets, and returns to its place. Rav Saadia takes this as testimony that the sun circles the earth daily . Galileo and \"sunrise\" The Rabbi noted that this verse was the source of the Church's opposition to Copernicus and Galileo : the verse says the sun rises and sets, so the sun moves. Does that make our Torah wrong? \"Let me ask you a question. Now that we have Copernicus and Galileo, why do we still talk about sunrise and sunset? There's no such thing! The answer is: that's what we see.\" The verse speaks the same way, in the language of what we see. \"Saadia took this further and actually believed from it that the sun rotates around the earth. He was wrong. That was the science at the time.\" But the crux of the argument, he said, can still be accepted in some version. Part 4: Proof Two: Composition and Design \"This is essentially what we call nowadays in philosophy the argument from design , or the teleological argument,\" though Rav Saadia does not express it in those words. The second proof: from the joining of parts and the putting together of things. I look at the whole world and see that everything is beautifully put together , and it is clear to me that there must be a Creator . Again he tests it step by step: Perhaps composition is only in living bodies , mammals and plants, but not in larger things? No: look at the earth : soil, stone and sand , all combined together. Perhaps not in the heavens? \"Then I go up to heaven in my mind\" and see sphere within sphere , all within each other, some stars brighter and some dimmer, forming one magnificent cosmos. (The astronomy of the time pictured the planets nested like onion shells , each inside the other. \"Obviously we know that's not true nowadays.\") \"When I saw all of this composition, I accepted the second proof.\" The verses: ידיך עשוני ויכוננוני (Tehillim 119:73): Your hands made me and established me. The way my body is put together shows an artisan. יוצר הארץ ועשה הוא כוננה (Yeshayahu 45:18): He formed the earth and made it, He established it. כי אראה שמיך מעשה אצבעתיך ירח וכוכבים אשר כוננתה (Tehillim 8:4): when I see Your heavens, the work of Your fingers, the moon and the stars which You established. What it is meant to defeat: the claim that the world is eternal. \"If it went on and on and on, fine. But how did this amazing composition come together?\" The quill and the dog This became \"the most famous and strongest proof of the medieval Rishonim\" after Rav Saadia. The Rambam used it, and it was developed by a philosopher from Zaragoza in Spain with an example used by many after him: You leave a quill, ink and a blank sheet of parchment . You come back a day later and find a beautiful piece of writing . Either someone wrote it, or a dog knocked the ink over and out came beautiful script. \"That is possible, 0.001 percent, but it's highly unlikely. The design shows. \" Rav Shach and the eye surgeon. Rav Shach in Israel had a major eye operation. Afterwards the surgeon showed him everything they had done and how intricate the eye is. Rav Shach turned to him and said: \"How can you not believe, after doing what you did? How can you not believe there's a Creator, that someone put all of this together?\" Julian and the second law of thermodynamics A participant, Julian, connected the proof to modern physics: the second law of thermodynamics (entropy) says order in the world is always decreasing. \"If I show you a film of an ice cream melting and then show it backwards, you know which way is forwards, because an ice cream doesn't freeze itself from drips back into a cone.\" Creating more order requires work, energy put in. \"We have this massive system of order and detail which can't happen by itself.\" The Rabbi's caveats: Rav Saadia assumes God . He is not proving God here (in his time everyone accepted a Creator; the dispute was about the right way to serve Him). He is proving creation , a beginning. The design argument does not disprove Plato : \"You could still say, yes, Hashem designed it, but there was still eternal matter.\" \"That, I think, is a very strong proof.\" Part 5: Proof Three: Accidents ( Mikrim ) \"This is Kalam philosophy now,\" the method of the Arabic theologians (the Ash'arites among them). Not \"accidents\" in the modern sense In the translations the term is \"accidents\" (Hebrew מקרים, mikrim ). \"Be careful you don't get confused. It's not accident in the sense of what we call an accident today.\" It means happenings : things that happen to a body. Every happening is a new thing. Nothing physical stays static. Even a table: plastic may take hundreds or thousands of years, \"but it eventually decays.\" Things grow, are completed, and break down; things happen to them from within and from without. The argument Every physical body has accidents happening to it constantly, internally or externally. Every accident is a new, created thing. A body is never free of its accidents. Therefore the body itself is also created. \"Since the happenings to these bodies are all new things, the thing itself must also be new , must also have had a creation.\" The Rabbi admitted: \"I'll be honest, I did find this difficult to get my head around. I initially thought the argument was that since everything decays, it must have had a beginning. He's not saying that. \" The point is that whatever is inseparable from created things is itself created. Julian called it a complete argument, \"very complex, and it takes a while to get your head around it.\" A participant suggested a seed : it grows and dies. The Rabbi pushed back that one could still ask: \"Maybe the seed was there for eternity, and things just happen to it all the time: it gets watered and grows and decays, and grows and decays.\" That is exactly what the proof must exclude. Testing it on the earth and the heavens Objection Reply Perhaps the earth itself has no accidents , only the plants on it? The earth is inherently connected with what grows and changes on it, so it follows the same laws: it too is a happening Perhaps the heavens have no accidents? They have them constantly: perpetual motion that never rests; some move fast, some slow ; some give light to others and some receive light (like the moon); different colours So the heavens, too, are created. The verse: אנכי עשיתי ארץ ואדם עליה בראתי, אני ידי נטו שמים (Yeshayahu 45:12) I made the earth and created man upon it; My hands stretched out the heavens. A striking reversal: Aristotle held that the perpetual rotation of the heavenly bodies proves they are eternal . Rav Saadia reads the very same motion the other way: because things are always happening to the heavens, they are happenings themselves, and had a beginning . Questions from the Class \"When was this written, and how did they know what was going on in space? They didn't have telescopes.\" (Maria) About a thousand years ago. \"They had a whole science at the time,\" including the 28 stations of the moon. Rav Saadia was not a scientist writing in the Torah; he was \"the big rabbi, the first,\" writing on the basis of the science of his day. \"Of course the science is going to be flawed. That's not the point. We're looking at the way he's thinking , and some of the arguments are still applicable.\" Why God created all the stars: \"a question for another time.\" \"Don't diamonds last forever?\" \"That's why the song goes like that.\" But all physical matter decays ; some faster, some slower. \"It may take thousands and thousands of years, but everything physical comes to an end.\" Someone added: even uranium. Summary Essay 1 argues for creation from nothing . Since science now confirms the universe had a beginning (redshift, the Big Bang, perhaps hinted at by the Ramban on Yehi Or ), the Rabbi will move through it relatively quickly. Three positions: Aristotle (eternal universe), Plato (creation from eternal matter, accepted as legitimate by the Rambam and held by the ibn Ezra ), and creation from nothing (Rav Saadia and most Rishonim). The Rambam : had Aristotle proven eternity, he would have reinterpreted the Torah. Scripture first: bara is used only of God and means creation from nothing, as distinct from yatzar . Proof 1, finitude: heaven and earth are finite, so their force is finite, so they began. Built on geocentric astronomy, but the Rabbi thinks a version survives. Rav Saadia even considers many worlds , rejecting them on the physics of his day. Proof 2, design: everything is composed and put together, so there is a Maker (the quill and the dog , Rav Shach , and entropy ). It does not, on its own, refute Plato. Proof 3, accidents: whatever is never free of created happenings is itself created, applied to the earth and even to the heavens, against Aristotle. Coming Up Next week (6 o'clock): the fourth proof, from time .","5go8GEk3GC8":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: The title and structure of the book; Rav Saadia's Introduction, the opening section (his reasons for writing) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 29 January 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap Last week was a biographical sketch of Rav Saadia Gaon : his life, his time period and the difficulties he went through, and the main targets of the book : Christianity, Islam and the Karaites . Tonight: a few more words of introduction for the first half, and then into the text itself. Part 1: The Title The book is called ספר האמונות והדעות, Sefer HaEmunot VeHaDeot . Rav Saadia wrote it in Arabic and called it Kitab al-Amanat wa-l-I'tiqadat . Emunah and de'ah \"It's important to understand this always. Emunah is often the beginning. It's a faith. Emunah is something which you believe in, but you don't necessarily know it.\" \"It would be wrong to say I believe 2 plus 2 is 4. You know 2 plus 2 is 4.\" Hebrew draws the same distinction, between אמונה ( emunah ), belief , and ידיעה ( yedi'ah ), knowledge . I believe in techiyat hametim , the resurrection of the dead. Is there empirical evidence for it? No. Has anyone witnessed it? No. \"So it's something you believe in. At least at this stage, it's an emunah .\" For many people, belief in Hashem is the same: they believe it, but they cannot rationally prove it. The purpose of the whole book: to take our emunot and turn them into de'ot . \"Things which we only believe in, which we're told to believe in, actually become things which we know are true.\" Part 2: Rav Saadia and the Rambam: Two Kinds of Perplexity About 200 years later the Rambam wrote the famous Moreh Nevuchim, the Guide for the Perplexed . The Rabbi read from Alexander Altmann's introduction to his English translation of Rav Saadia: \"There is a marked difference between the perplexity from which Maimonides sought to rescue his people and that which Saadia encountered. In the case of Maimonides the problem was summed up in the question: Aristotle or the Torah?... In the case of Saadia the situation was different. The bewilderment which characterized Saadia's own age is due not so much to the conflict between one particular creed or philosophy and the traditional faith as to the impact of so many rival creeds and philosophies upon the minds of his contemporaries.\" The Rambam Rav Saadia Who he is talking to A specific person: the philosopher who knows Tanach and Aristotle and cannot square them The masses The problem Aristotle or Torah? Too many competing faiths His method Harmonise the two; sometimes he cannot, and says Aristotle is wrong Clear away the confusion Reach Narrow \"Far broader and greater than that of the Rambam\" The confusion of Rav Saadia's age: Islam had begun about 300 years earlier, and many sects had arisen arguing over the correct interpretation of Muhammad. There was Eastern Christianity in Iraq at the time. And among the Jews themselves there were Karaite Jews and rabbinic Jews . \"The simple, average Jew sees around him this kind of Muslim, that kind of Muslim, this kind of Christian,\" and even two kinds of Jews. \"If that was in those generations, all the more so nowadays. There are so many different people. Multiculturalism. We're faced with so many different kinds of people.\" He read another line quoted in Altmann's introduction to capture the mood of the age: Muslims, Christians, Jews and Magians are all walking in error and darkness, and there are only two kinds of people left in the world: One group that is intelligent but lacking in faith, and the other which has faith but is lacking in intelligence. \"How true is that today?\" Not faith against reason: faith against pseudo -reason \"The real conflict as Saadia sees it is not one between faith and reason, but between faith and pseudo-reason . Saadia insists, and his book is intended as a proof for this thesis, that intellectual speculation, if properly and patiently carried out to its final conclusion, confirms the doctrines of the Jewish faith . The trouble is, he asserts, that so few people care to complete their research... They pretend to follow their reason, but in fact they only follow their Nature: the irrational factors which impede the progress of true speculation, such as indolence, prejudice, resentment.\" The Rabbi stressed: \"This is really important. It's not a conflict between faith and reason.\" The debate is not rationalists on one side, faithful Jews on the other . It is between faith and people who claim to use their reason but are not using it correctly . Rav Saadia will use real reason to show that the emunot of the Torah \"are not just emunot , they are de'ot . They are well-established principles.\" Part 3: The Structure of the Book An introduction and ten essays ( ma'amarim ) , each with its own chapters: Essay Subject 1 Creation 2 The unity of God ( Yichud Hashem ) 3 Mitzvot and revelation (Har Sinai) 4 Free will 5 Good and bad (merits and demerits) 6 The human soul 7 Techiyat hametim, resurrection 8 Mashiach and the redemption ( Geulah ) 9 Reward and punishment (\"all the famous questions\") 10 Behaviour : the golden mean, the Rambam's middle path A warning about the Hebrew Rav Saadia's Hebrew (in translation) is flowery and poetic. \"It's not like the Rambam. The Rambam always uses the most simple, clear Hebrew, which, if you understand Hebrew, is very, very easy to read.\" So the class will translate as it goes. He brings proofs from every discipline: mathematics, astronomy, medicine, even music . He loves quoting pesukim : he was a linguist of biblical Hebrew, the author of the first dictionary, and the teacher of Dunash. The Introduction The Introduction has eight chapters . The first is \"just an introduction to the introduction,\" praising Hashem. Then the purpose: \"He's going to use the ten essays to ask you to use your reason. But I need to show you that you know how to use your reason, because people say they're using their reason but they're not using it correctly. So that's what he's going to do in the introduction\": show why people make philosophical mistakes. Part 4: The Opening Praise The class followed the Hebrew on Sefaria , with the Rabbi sharing his screen and highlighting. פתח המחבר ואמר... The author opened and said: Blessed is Hashem, to whom the clear and certain truth is fitting... As the Rabbi translated it: Hashem verifies for thinking beings (humans) the existence of their own souls clearly. \"Don't think God has created us and made us foolish, so we can't understand anything. No, He gives us the ability to understand things.\" Our senses too perceive what logic understands. Hashem has given us: the ability to understand things correctly , to remove mistakes and remove doubts from ourselves, to use proofs and verify the evidence . \"Just beginning and saying Baruch Hashem : He's given us the ability to think and to understand things, and that's what we're going to try to use.\" Part 5: What Rav Saadia Promises After the brief praise, Rav Saadia says he will begin by explaining why people have doubts when they seek the truth, and how to remove those doubts , until they reach their goal. Sometimes false beliefs come to dominate correct ones in people's minds, until they think the falsehood is what their own logic tells them. He will try to remove them, as David HaMelech asked: גל עיני ואביטה נפלאות מתורתך (Tehillim 119:18) Open my eyes, that I may behold wonders from Your Torah. \"Near, not far\" My wish is that this introduction, together with the whole book, should be קרוב ולא רחוק, near and not far . From easy disciplines, not hard ones. From the roots of proofs, not their subdivisions. \"Similar to how the Ramchal began, remember? I'm going to give you the principles, not proofs from sub-things.\" So that the burden is lighter and the learning straighter, as Shlomo HaMelech said of wisdom that is near and clear: אז תבין צדק ומשפט ומישרים כל מעגל טוב (Mishlei 2:9), then you will understand justice and judgment and every good path. Part 6: Why People Get It Wrong Two reasons, even with physical things The foundations of our judgments rest on what we sense : we see, smell and touch, and our minds judge on that basis. Even about physical, sensible things , people err for one of two reasons: Lack of knowledge. The seeker does not have the ability or does not know how to judge. Laziness. He has the ability, but \"he's light on himself\" and abandons careful, penetrating analysis. Rav Saadia's example: looking for Reuven. Reason What it looks like He doesn't know Reuven Reuven may be standing next to him and he won't recognise him, or he thinks someone else is Reuven He knows Reuven, but seeks him lazily Half-heartedly, not wholeheartedly. \"Doesn't know that to find Reuven you have to make phone calls, Google him, ask this person, ask that person,\" or knows all that and can't be bothered The same two reasons in matters of the intellect He does not know how to prove things. He thinks a proof is not a proof, and something that is not a proof is one. \"He uses the wrong means to find the truth because he doesn't know what it looks like.\" He knows how, but is hasty: he jumps to a conclusion before completing his analysis . \"This is what we call in English prejudice . You've already made a judgment without fully thinking about it.\" And when both come together , a person who is poor at analysis and impatient, the goal is very far, \"perhaps even give up hope.\" Worse still, add a third : he does not even know what he is looking for (he does not know what Reuven looks like). Then he will reach the truth only by chance. Worst of all : he finds the truth but does not realise he has it . \"People come to conclusions and they're saying the right thing, but they don't realise they're saying the right thing.\" Rav Saadia's analogies: weights, deposits and coins The creditor who cannot use scales. Someone is owed money but does not understand weights and measures. Even if his debtor pays in full, he will not know he has been paid in full. If he accepts less, he thinks he has been clever and tricked the debtor, when really he has lost out. \"Beautiful the way he uses it. Why did he give the example of weights? Because weight is a physical thing, but also weighing up reason, weighing up arguments . Exactly the same.\" The Rabbi applied it: the advice for any big decision is to write two columns, pros and cons. But you may not know what is a pro and what is a con. And even if you list ten of each, how much weight does each one carry? \"You end up with the wrong decision. But I used my reason, I thought it all through, I wrote it all out! But you used reason wrong, if you don't know the basics of how to use reason.\" The banker and the currencies. A person who receives deposits and is no expert in sorting money will take the wrong currency. Or he is an expert, \"but you're just lazy that day. You didn't drink your coffee and you're not paying attention to the money put in front of you.\" Shlomo HaMelech compares wisdom and Torah to money: כסף נבחר לשון צדיק (Mishlei 10:20), the tongue of the righteous is choice silver , the correct, precise currency. Those with little skill in sorting are likened to the wicked, who muddle the truth. Only those who take on the burden of investigation ( chakirah ) are called wise, and only then are their doubts removed. The word recalls the Mishnah in Sanhedrin, where witnesses undergo chakirot : real investigation to see if they are lying. And David HaMelech's prayer: ואל תצל מפי דבר אמת עד מאד (Tehillim 119:43) Do not take from my mouth a word of truth, very true. \"A beautiful expression. Not just true, but very true . And the only way you can get that, says Rav Saadia, is (a) you understand how to use your intellect, and (b) you are ready to put in the effort.\" Part 7: Four Kinds of People \"I see,\" says Rav Saadia, people who have belief and knowledge in different ways. \"And I told you last week: Saadia loves categorising things.\" # Who Rav Saadia's verse 1 Reached the truth, know it, and are happy with it נמצאו דבריך ואכלם ויהי דברך לי לששון ולשמחת לבבי (Yirmeyahu 15:16): Your words were found and I ate them; they became joy and gladness to my heart 2 Reached the truth but doubt it : it is not verified for them; parts seem strange or unclear כמו זר נחשבו (Hoshea 8:12): it is regarded as something strange 3 Hold a false view and are sure it is right אל יאמן בשו נתעה כי שוא תהיה תמורתו (Iyov 15:31): let him not trust in vanity, for vanity will be his return 4 Always changing : adopts one view, finds a problem, switches to a second, then a third, all his life עמל הכסילים תיגענו אשר לא ידע ללכת אל עיר (Kohelet 10:15) Category 2 , the Rabbi said, describes many believing Jews: \"who know the truth but don't fully understand it, or have doubts. They haven't verified it.\" Category 4 is like a man trying to reach a city who does not know the way. He sets off in one direction, gets lost, comes back; tries a second road, a third, a fourth. \"The toil of the fool wears him out, for he does not know how to go to the city. Fantastic example. He was never taught how to read a map. He's got the map in front of him, and ten different paths.\" \"We haven't talked about religion yet, by the way. There's nothing religious here. We can use this for your political views, whatever it may be.\" A fifth category for today \"Nowadays we would add category five : the people who don't bother thinking . Saadia didn't have that. You just take whatever propaganda is put in front of you, whether advertisements or social media.\" At least Rav Saadia's four categories are all thinking: even the person who reaches the wrong conclusion \"is trying. He's got the wrong method, so he's come to the wrong conclusion.\" That, in the Rabbi's opinion, is better than not thinking at all. \"And unfortunately, that is an illness which we have nowadays. People do not even bother to think.\" Part 8: Why He Wrote the Book \"When I considered these categories, my heart ached for the human species , and my soul was stirred, at the very least, for our people Israel .\" \"I can't save humanity, but at the very least let me try to save Bnei Yisrael.\" In his time he saw: many who profess faith , but whose faith is not refined and whose knowledge is not clear ; counter-arguments growing all around them (exactly the bewilderment of competing faiths described above); people sunk in doubt and drowned in the sea of confusion , with no swimmer to take their hands and lift them out of the depths. Since Hashem has given me some knowledge with which I can support them, I feel it is an obligation upon me , as the prophet said: ה' אלהים נתן לי לשון למודים (Yeshayahu 50:4), Hashem has given me a learned tongue. Humility Rav Saadia confesses that his wisdom is not complete and his knowledge is lacking: \"I'm not the wisest of my generation.\" He brings a verse here whose relevance the Rabbi said he could not work out. \"In any case, what he's saying is: I don't know everything.\" Yet he does not give up hope that Hashem will help him reach his goal: וידעתי אלהי כי אתה בחן לבב ומישרים תרצה (Divrei HaYamim I 29:17) I know, my God, that You test the heart and desire uprightness. Part 9: The Oath: \"Correct My Book\" \"Let's end with a dramatic paragraph.\" Rav Saadia swears by God, the Creator of all , that any wise person who studies this book and finds a mistake should correct it , or a word that is unclear should straighten it . And let him not say to himself, it's not my book, I didn't write it, it's not for me to edit someone else's work . Why? כי לחכמים חמלה על החכמה For the wise have compassion for wisdom. \"What a beautiful sentence.\" Wise people care about wisdom. There is a camaraderie among them, a closeness like that of relatives, as Shlomo HaMelech says: אמר לחכמה אחתי את (Mishlei 7:4), say to wisdom, you are my sister . \"If there's a book out there which is wrong, we care. Edit it. Send me the mistakes.\" Fools, by contrast, are compassionate towards their foolishness and will not let go of it. \"You start to get a sense of who Saadia is. Very similar to the Rambam in the sense he's intolerant of stupid people . I'll be tolerant of you if you don't understand things or don't yet know the way of understanding things, and I'll try to help you. But I'm not tolerant of you if you're just foolish and lazy.\" So he sends a message to all his fellow wise readers: if you see a problem in this book, tell me. \"I'm not perfect, my knowledge isn't complete.\" Summary The title: emunot (beliefs) and de'ot (knowledge). The goal: to turn belief into knowledge. Rav Saadia and the Rambam: the Rambam answered the philosopher torn between Aristotle and Torah ; Rav Saadia answers the masses bewildered by many rival faiths , a situation very like our own multicultural world. Faith versus pseudo -reason: reason, carried out properly and patiently, confirms the Torah. The problem is people who claim to reason but are lazy, prejudiced or resentful. Structure: an introduction (eight chapters) and ten essays , from creation and God's unity to the soul, resurrection, redemption, reward and punishment and the middle path. Why people miss the truth: lack of method or laziness and prejudice , and worst, not knowing what you are looking for ( Reuven , the scales , the coins ). Four kinds of people: sure and right; right but doubting; sure and wrong; forever switching. The Rabbi's fifth: those who don't bother to think. Why he wrote: \"my heart ached\"; people were drowning in doubt and no one was swimming out to save them. The oath: correct my mistakes, כי לחכמים חמלה על החכמה . Coming Up Next week (back to the usual 6:30 to 7:30): finish Chapter 2 of the Introduction and at least Chapter 3, moving faster now that the background is done. Those who want to can read ahead in the English: \"The beginning bits are quite easy. There's not much to debate.\"","rXI91TQN7g4":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Opening shiur: a biography of Rav Saadia Gaon and an overview of the book Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 22 January 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Where We Are: The Third Book The Rabbi opened a new series. About four years earlier the group had begun a journey through the classic books of Jewish thought: # Book Author 1 Derech Hashem (The Way of God) The Ramchal 2 Da'at Tevunot The Ramchal 3 Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) Rav Saadia Gaon The group had \"unanimously decided\" that the third book would be Rav Saadia's. The usual English title is The Book of Beliefs and Opinions , and the Rabbi promised to come back to whether that is a precise translation (he did, the following week). Written in Arabic \"One important point to put straight at the beginning\": Emunot VeDeot was written in Arabic. It was later translated into Hebrew, and only in the twentieth century into English (an abridged translation in 1946 , and the full one a couple of years later). Rav Saadia wrote almost all his commentaries and books in Arabic, and so did many of the Geonim, because \"in order for their works to be accessible they have to be in the vernacular,\" and the vernacular of the time was Arabic. The plan for the evening: The life of Rav Saadia Gaon , with the controversies he lived through. An overview of the book : who it is written for, whom it argues against, and its key ideas. How to get hold of the text. Part 1: The Life of Rav Saadia Gaon Egypt and Tiberias Born in Egypt in 892 , at the end of the ninth century, \"over 1,100 years ago.\" Rav Saadia traces his family back to Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa of the Talmud, and through him to Yehudah . \"At least in Saadia's eyes, he has a lineage all the way back to Yehudah.\" Recognised from a young age as a very bright student, he was sent to Eretz Yisrael to study, to Tiberias . Tiberias at that time was \"the hotbed\" of the grammarians and the masoretes: The text of the Torah we have today goes back to Ben Asher , the great grammarian of Tiberias, whose text is essentially that of the Aram Tzova (the Aleppo Codex) . Many argue, at least in the academic approach, that it was around this time and in Tiberias that the system of ta'amim (the cantillation marks) was developed. This is why, the Rabbi explained, Rav Saadia was so concentrated on language and the precision of words . He returned to Egypt at 15 , and at about 20 published his first book: a dictionary of the Hebrew language . Dunash and Dror Yikra The dictionary heavily influenced his student Dunash ben Labrat . \"Who's heard of Dunash? No one's heard of Dunash?\" Someone on Zoom got it: Dror Yikra . If you look at the first letters of the stanzas of that beautiful Shabbat song, they spell דונש, Dunash . Dunash became a famous grammarian, and many of our grammar rules come from him. \"You can see how important language was to Saadia.\" Rav Saadia also spent some time in Aleppo in his twenties, but was mostly in Egypt, and later ended up in Bavel (Iraq) : \"a happy time, but a tragic time as well.\" \"It's not an exaggeration to say that Saadia in different ways actually saved Judaism.\" The calendar crisis of 922 Background. Originally the months and festivals were fixed by the sighting of the moon, with the Hachamim of Eretz Yisrael in charge of sanctifying the new month. Then Hillel (Hillel II, not the Hillel of Beit Hillel) , around 359 CE , fixed the calendar . From then on the authority of Eretz Yisrael over the months and festivals was effectively over, and the fixed calendar spread across the Jewish world, unchallenged for centuries. The challenge. In the early tenth century one of the Geonim of Eretz Yisrael, Rabbi Aharon ben Meir , argued that the Hachamim of Eretz Yisrael still had the main jurisdiction over the calendar, and that the right to decide the months and festivals should return to them, not to the Geonim of Bavel. The result. The argument grew so fierce that in 922 : Pesach began on Eretz Yisrael Sunday Bavel and the rest of the world Tuesday \"So whilst in Israel they were eating matzah on Seder night and not having any chametz, a few miles away in Iraq they were having chametz.\" And at the end of the festival, the Jews of Eretz Yisrael were eating chametz while the rest of the world was still keeping Pesach. \"If this would continue, this would destroy Judaism. Because we're all based upon our festivals and our calendar, and we have to be on the same page.\" Rav Saadia, aged about 30 , wrote many letters and responsa on the issue, especially to the Hachamim of Eretz Yisrael. He convinced Rabbi Aharon ben Meir's own colleagues that they were wrong; they left him and sided with Rav Saadia. It caused a conflict between the two men, \"but he managed to get through that.\" It happened for one year and never again. \"Just imagine we had a situation nowadays where Jews in one part of the world are celebrating at a different time. That would be crazy.\" The fight against the Karaites His second great battle was against the Karaites ( Kara'im ) . He wrote strongly and polemically against them and made many enemies. Where they came from (eighth century). In Bavel the Reish Galuta (the Exilarch) held full political power over the Jewish community and represented it before the Caliph. When the Reish Galuta died, his eldest son Anan ben David , a great scholar, expected to succeed him. The Hachamim distrusted him, \"probably for good reason, as we're going to see,\" and persuaded the Caliph to appoint his younger brother instead. Anan was furious \"and breaks away, as you do when you're upset about something in your shul: you break away and create your own shul. Nothing new there.\" He rejected rabbinic Judaism: \"these rabbis, they make up the Torah.\" The name Kara'im comes from מקרא, mikra , the verse: only what is written, nothing else. The idea went back to the Sadducees , who had fizzled out; Anan reignited it. The sect had real influence for centuries and still existed in the Rambam's time. What literalism looks like. In that week's parashah, Beshalach, Moshe tells the people to gather a double portion of manna on Friday, and says: אל יצא איש ממקמו ביום השביעי (Shemot 16:29) No man shall leave his place on the seventh day. Read with the Oral Torah, this means do not go out of your domain to gather manna. Read literally, \"stay in your chair the whole Shabbat\" , and the really observant Karaites never left their chairs on Shabbat. Likewise, since the Torah says לא תבערו אש (do not kindle fire in your dwellings), they sat in pitch darkness on Friday night and had no hot food , even from a fire lit before Shabbat. Why we eat chamin on Shabbat morning. This, the Rabbi said, is where the custom comes from. The Rema in the Shulchan Aruch writes that someone who deliberately refuses hot food on Shabbat morning should be checked, because he may be a heretic . The custom of keeping food hot overnight developed to counter the Karaites : there is nothing wrong with enjoying a fire lit before Shabbat. The dispute with the Exilarch His \"greatest conflict and saddest time\" was with the Reish Galuta, David ben Zakkai . 928 (aged 36): David ben Zakkai brought Rav Saadia from Egypt and appointed him Rosh Yeshivah (Gaon) of Sura . Bavel had the two great yeshivot familiar from the Talmud, Sura and Pumbedita , which continued all the way through the Geonic period. Why: the Reish Galuta felt that the Gaon of Pumbedita, Kohen Tzedek , was not subservient enough. He wanted a heavyweight to re-establish the authority of the Exilarch. The Rabbi stressed that the Exilarch was a political position, not a rabbinic one . \"The best equivalent I can give for nowadays is probably the head of the Board of Deputies,\" except that the Board represents a small segment of the community, while the Reish Galuta was over everyone. \"He wasn't a talmid chacham . He's a political position. So he wants a rabbi to support him.\" At first it worked. Rav Saadia \"comes in with big shoulders\" and the Gaon of Pumbedita was diminished. But the Exilarch \"soon finds out that Saadia is a principled person,\" and within two years a huge fight broke out: A dispute arose over the will of a wealthy member of the community . To execute it his way, the Exilarch needed the signatures of both Geonim. The weakened Gaon of Pumbedita quickly signed. Rav Saadia refused. He thought the interpretation of the will was completely wrong. The Exilarch's son publicly humiliated Rav Saadia in the synagogue and beat at least one of his children . Rav Saadia, who had only religious power, put the Exilarch in cherem . The dispute went to the Caliph , who sided with the Exilarch. Rav Saadia lost his position in 930. Seven years out of office, and forgiveness For seven years he had no position. \"This is a time where he writes a lot,\" and it is believed that Emunot VeDeot was written during these years . In 937 the community, \"as communities change, presidents change,\" reappointed him Gaon of Sura , which he remained until his death in 942 . \"Unbelievably, in 940 David ben Zakkai passes away, and Saadia looks after his son, the one who had humiliated him.\" He did not bear a grudge; he had forgiven what happened. Summary of his life Age / year Event 892 Born in Egypt; traces his line to Yehudah Youth Studies Hebrew language in Tiberias ; returns to Egypt at 15 About 20 Publishes his Hebrew dictionary 20s Time in Aleppo ; mostly in Egypt 30 (922) Saves the unity of the calendar in the Ben Meir dispute 36 (928) Appointed Gaon of Sura 930 Deposed after the dispute with David ben Zakkai 930-937 Seven years without a post; writes Emunot VeDeot 937-942 Gaon of Sura again until his death, aged about 50 \"A very short life,\" but he goes down in history as one of the great Geonim of the Babylonian period . Part 2: The Book Philosophy was not a luxury Today we think of philosophy as a luxury, something some people study at university. In the Middle Ages, the Rabbi said, it was the opposite: philosophy was a fundamental part of refining your intellect. If you did not know philosophy, you had not really refined your mind. Throughout the Sefaradi Rishonim, philosophy was integral to understanding God, understanding Torah, \"and understanding just being a Jew.\" Rav Saadia will argue strongly that reason, logic and intellect are fundamental to understanding Torah and understanding God. The father of Jewish philosophy Emunot VeDeot is the very first book to establish Jewish philosophy in a structured manner. Nobody before Rav Saadia had done this. Many ideas we take for granted, often credited to the Rambam, originated with Rav Saadia . \"I wouldn't say it's the most important book of Jewish philosophy. I don't think it is. But it's a fundamental one, because it lays the roots for all future philosophy.\" The Rambam quotes Rav Saadia many times, in the original Arabic. He sometimes disagreed with him, but highly respected him. Two audiences Rav Saadia writes in his introduction (which the class would study in the coming weeks) that he is addressing two groups, \"and unfortunately both groups still exist very much today\": Group Description 1. The pious without understanding Jews who consider themselves religious. \"They know very well which hechsher to look at when buying a product,\" but ask them a basic question about their beliefs and they cannot answer. \"That's tragic,\" he says. You cannot be a pious Jew without understanding Hashem and the philosophy of Judaism. 2. The non-observant He wants to show them they can also have faith . So this is already an early attempt \"to bridge the gap between those who are observant and those who are less observant.\" The Rabbi added: \"Many of us, myself included, fall into the first category. We do lots of things we don't necessarily understand.\" Three targets He asked the class which three groups the book aims at. The answer: Christianity, Islam and the Karaites. 1. Christianity. Rav Saadia is extremely polemical, especially about the Trinity : a grave violation of monotheism and of the principle that God has no body , which he is probably the first to articulate clearly. \"How can you say God was killed?\" His position, \"which may not be very politically correct nowadays,\" is that everything positive in Christianity comes from Judaism, and the rest is nonsense . The Rabbi pointed out that this has halachic ramifications , and that where you live shapes how freely you can speak: Masechet Avodah Zarah forbids doing business with idol worshippers three days before their festivals , since the profit leads them to thank their idols. Rabbeinu Tam in twelfth-century France was asked how Jews in France and England could trade before Christmas: \"We should have to close down our shops in December!\" He \"squeezes his way out\" and rules that Christianity is shittuf : imperfect monotheism. Forbidden for a Jew to believe, but not pagan. Rav Saadia rejects that: for him it is avodah zarah, full stop. And he can say so because he does not have to be circumspect: he is not living in Christian Europe. 2. Islam. Here he is more careful. Muslims are perfect monotheists , a position the Rambam repeats two hundred years later. Rav Saadia does not speak about Muhammad in the disparaging terms the Rambam uses (\"I'm telling you what the Rambam writes, I'm not saying that\"). He rationally disproves some of their claims, but his main objection, to Islam and Christianity alike, is exclusivity : \"How can you say God is merciful when, according to your belief, the majority of human beings in the world are not going to have a place in heaven?\" The Rabbi worked it through: even granting that all of the world's roughly 3.2 billion Christians fully believe, they are still not the majority of 8 billion people, so on that belief most of humanity is damned. The same is true of the roughly 2.8 billion Muslims. Ask Rav Saadia: how can a merciful God do that? The Jewish alternative , which will fit Rav Saadia's definition of the chosen people: Hashem created a chosen people with a duty to reflect morality across the world , but every human being, provided they are not pagans and live moral lives, has a portion in the World to Come. 3. The Karaites , as described above. Some of these battles are less relevant to us now, \"because the situations have changed,\" but \"it's the way he articulates the arguments which we can learn from.\" \"He loves categories\" \"Something I have to tell you about Saadia. He loves categories. He loves numbers. He loves boxing things up into three this, four that, ten this.\" He will do it all through the book: numbered arguments for, numbered arguments against. Part 3: Key Ideas of the Book 1. Revelation is not the highest level Here Rav Saadia differs greatly from Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi , author of the Kuzari. Rav Saadia writes that there are four ways to understand God and Judaism. As the Rabbi presented them: # Way Explanation 1 Intellect How Avraham understood God: look around the world and you will understand God and Torah 2 Logic / deduction Once you have knowledge, you derive one thing from another and piece things together 3 Tradition ( mesorah ) What people tell you, handed down 4 Revelation Har Sinai On tradition: Rav Saadia says that humans are unique in being able to accumulate knowledge and pass it on. A monkey or a dolphin can be trained, but it cannot teach what it learned to its young. (The Rabbi asked someone to research whether that is still believed today: \"that was at least the belief of science at the time.\") After all, how does anyone learn to speak? From what people tell them. So why do we need Har Sinai at all? Rav Saadia's answer, very simply: most people do not like to use their intellect ; even those who do will not patiently use their logic; and many will not patiently listen to the views of others before them. Revelation is \"a catch-all\" for those who cannot develop their understanding themselves. \"Which means, essentially, revelation is the lowest level, or it's not high up. Whereas for Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi, revelation is everything. Revelation underpins everything. Not for Saadia.\" 2. No anthropomorphism To the best of the Rabbi's knowledge, Rav Saadia is the first to say that all the anthropomorphic images in the Torah must be taken non-literally : the hand of Hashem, Hashem being happy or sad, going down or going up. Why does the Torah talk that way? Because the Torah speaks in the language humans can understand. \"You can't explain to a three-year-old or a five-year-old what we talk about in our shiurim, about God being the primal force. You have to talk about God sitting there on a chair in heaven, judging us, with a long beard.\" The problem is that most people stay with that picture all their lives, and that, says Rav Saadia, is absolutely wrong. But he does not go as far as the Rambam : Believing in an anthropomorphic God is... Rav Saadia Wrong , but it does not make you a heretic The Rambam (Hilchot Teshuvah, as learned together in the series on the Principles) Heresy ; such a person has no portion in the World to Come The Ra'avad's gloss on that Rambam וכמה גדולים וטובים ממנו הלכו בזו המחשבה : many greater than him held this view The Ra'avad's comment shows that this was a widely held belief at the time, even among Hachamim . Today \"you won't find an educated and intellectual Jew who will believe in an anthropomorphic God,\" but in those days it was not simple, and Rav Saadia goes to great lengths to reject it. 3. Creation from nothing Rav Saadia respects Aristotle , \"but he doesn't think he's the all.\" The Rambam had a much closer relationship with Aristotle. This is one of the key places where Rav Saadia argues strongly with Aristotle and insists that the world was created from nothing . \"These are basic things to us, but he is the one who articulates them for the very first time in clear philosophical language.\" 4. Every Jew has mitzvot Rav Saadia argues that there is a continuum of Jewish observance : there is not a Jew in the world who does not have mitzvot . Even a completely non-religious Jew is always doing mitzvot he may not know about. The class would see later in the book why this matters philosophically to him. \"People like to say these kinds of things nowadays, but he was already saying this a thousand years ago.\" Part 4: Getting the Text In English the Rabbi found only two translations online: one abridged , and one full, from 1948, by Rosenblatt (the Yale translation, The Book of Beliefs and Opinions ). Interestingly, there were many translations in other languages, Spanish and German among them. The shiurim will read the Hebrew text , translating and discussing it, \"because you always have to understand things in the original language. It's not the original language, but it's closer at least.\" No edition has Hebrew and English side by side, so he suggested getting both: the Hebrew to follow the class, the English to prepare beforehand or review afterwards. He would post a recommended Hebrew edition on the group, and offered a group order for those attending in person. Checking live, he found that Sefaria has the full Hebrew text (with partial English), and promised to post the link. \"I personally find it difficult to learn from computers,\" but it works. Summary Rav Saadia Gaon (892-942): born in Egypt, trained in Tiberias as a linguist, author of the first Hebrew dictionary and teacher of Dunash ben Labrat ( Dror Yikra ). He saved the unity of the Jewish calendar in 922, when Eretz Yisrael and Bavel kept Pesach on different days. He fought the Karaites , whose literalism lies behind our chamin on Shabbat morning. Gaon of Sura , deposed after refusing to sign the Exilarch's ruling, he wrote Emunot VeDeot in the years out of office , and later forgave and cared for the son who had humiliated him. The book is the first structured work of Jewish philosophy, written in Arabic, for the pious who do not understand and the unobservant who could have faith , against Christianity, Islam and the Karaites . Key ideas: revelation is a \"catch-all,\" not the pinnacle (unlike the Kuzari); no anthropomorphism (but not heresy, unlike the Rambam); creation from nothing against Aristotle; every Jew has mitzvot . Coming Up Next week (exceptionally at 6:15 to 7:15): a few words on the title , then into the text itself, beginning with Rav Saadia's introduction .","IyvxzEwZWrs":"Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Even HaEzer, Hilchot Gittin Siman: 135, Seifim 1-5 (the whole siman) Shiur: A Dayanut shiur by Dayan Daniel Kada, Wednesday 29 June 2022 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction: The Siman of Reading the Get The whole of Siman 135 comes from one daf of Gemara, Gittin 19b . Seifim 1, 2 and 3 form one sugya; then come Seif 4 and Seif 5. Seifim 1-3 are \"the most practical part of the siman. It's very, very relevant, and sometimes dayanim can forget to do it when you've had a long get\": the law of reading the get before and after it is given . Part 1: The Sugya in Gittin The Gemara says that the two edim who witness the get must read it ( kore'in et ha-get ). The Gemara then asks from a Beraita: A man gave his wife a get; she received it and threw it into the sea or the fire; and then the husband said, \"It wasn't a get.\" He has no power to forbid her. Once he gave it before edim and she received it, he can no longer claim it was not a get. The Gemara asks: if the get must always be read before the two edim, how could the husband even claim it was not a get? The answer: of course they read it. The case is where, after the edim read it, the husband took it back and had it for a few moments. Perhaps he moved things around in his pockets and could have swapped it for another document. One might think that because of those moments, perhaps he swapped it. The Beraita teaches that we are not concerned: since he gave it before the two edim, she is divorced. What if it was not read at all? The Rabbi asked the class: from this Gemara, what is the status of a get that was not read at all , before or after? The Tosafot make a simple diyuk (inference). If reading the get were not essential ( me'akev ), why does the Gemara need the answer that he took it back and maybe swapped it? It could simply have answered: in that case they did not read it. So if the get was not read, it is invalid , or more precisely, she is a safek megureshet (doubtfully divorced). A question from Yevamot Tosafot then raise a contradiction from a Gemara in Yevamot (which will come up again in Siman 152). After writing a get, they would prepare a shovar , a receipt, like the get certificate given today. The get goes to the wife (through the husband), and the receipt to the husband. In that case, the sofer mixed them up, so that the husband ended up handing his wife the receipt instead of the get . Obviously no get was given, and the Gemara says tetze (she must leave a second husband). The Gemara asks: it was not her fault, both wanted the get given! It answers that it is her fault, because she should have read it in advance . Tosafot's question: there they did not read the get at all, and still the Gemara explains tetze by the swap of the receipt for the get. If not reading is itself essential, why mention the swap? Tosafot's two answers Not reading the get Yevamot First answer A middle ground: lechatchila lo tinasei, ve-im nis'et lo tetze (lechatchila she should not remarry; if she did, she need not leave) Tetze because she was handed a receipt, not a get. That is why our Gemara could not simply answer \"they did not read it,\" since that would not explain the Beraita's ruling that she is fully divorced Second answer Me'akev : she is a safek megureshet , so tetze They did read it. But afterwards the husband had moments alone with it, as in our Gemara, and she ended up with only the receipt. It is her fault for not checking again A student asked whether \"it's her fault\" in Yevamot implies a rabbinic penalty ( knas ), rather than a true doubt min haTorah . The Rabbi explained that we do not know what happened there: we only know she ended up with the receipt. Perhaps she never received the get at all, so she is not divorced; and even if she did, it is a penalty, since she should have checked. In our case, at least according to Tosafot's second answer, an unread get is essential: it is at least a safek get, so tetze . The Rambam and the Ran The Rambam and the Ran take the Gemara here very simply: the get must be read, and if it was not, you don't know it's a get , so she is a safek megureshet . The Rambam does not address the Gemara in Yevamot; perhaps he learned like Tosafot's second answer. Either way, the Rambam clearly holds an unread get leaves her safek megureshet . \"So therefore it's very clear that it's critical to read the get, the edim to read the get.\" Part 2: Who Reads, and When (Seif 1) Who reads The Gemara clearly says the edim read the get. The Rema records that it is the minhag for the dayanim together with the edim to read it, and that is what happens nowadays. But the essential point is that the edim read it. In Perth, a student said, everyone reads it together, before and after. Read only after the giving What if they did not read it before he gave it, and only read it afterwards? \"This can happen. It's happened with us actually,\" in a rushed get: they gave the get and then remembered that it needed reading. Tosafot say it is obvious that she is divorced, and all the Rishonim , the Rosh and the Rambam among them, agree: she is divorced, with no issue at all. The Mordechai , in the name of the Maharam , says she is not divorced min haTorah . Why? Because it is lacking in the testimony of the edim. When they witnessed the giving, they did not know there was a get. It was a document; it could be anything. So reading it afterwards does not help. The Shulchan Aruch rules like all the Rishonim: she is divorced and may remarry lechatchila. A student found the Maharam's logic strange: surely the edim assume it is a get. The Rabbi pressed: how do you know? Perhaps they were just summoned to watch \"what I'm giving this woman.\" They are edei mesira , so they must know what they are witnessing. There must at least be some statement or context of a get (this is discussed in the next siman, 136). The student's point: the Maharam seems to require that until they have actually seen it, they do not know it, even if they think or assume so. \"They have to know for certain.\" But the Shulchan Aruch does not rule like the Maharam. Read again afterwards Tosafot write that the Ri used to read the get again after the giving , just to make sure. The Rambam says there is no need, even lechatchila: the reading beforehand is enough. The Shulchan Aruch rules like Tosafot: it is good to read it after the giving as well . The Rema adds (with the text corrected by the Pitchei Teshuva) that even if they did not read it word for word afterwards, but looked at it and made sure it was the same get, that also suffices. \"No matter what, we do read it word for word, before and after.\" But if there is no time, glancing through it to check the names, the places and at least the essential text is enough. The Rema then lists the edim reading it together and signing in each other's presence: \"all the questions we ask.\" The Rabbi noted that from the Rema it seems the chakirot (the formal questions) come after the giving; in his Beth Din they are done before the giving, and afterwards the get is just read again. Seif 1 in brief: read it before; it is good to read it afterwards too; if it was only read afterwards, it is fine. Part 3: When the Husband Had It Alone (Seif 2) What if the get was read as it should be, and then the husband had time alone with it, put it in his pocket, and then gave it? The Gemara shows that it should have been read again , since he had it alone and could have done something with it. But if it was not, and he gave it, she is divorced , and he cannot claim he swapped it. The Rishonim add: if the husband has the get in his hand , for example he gave it and later comes along with it and says \"although we read it, I swapped it in between,\" then since he holds the get, he has raglayim la-davar (support for his claim) and is believed. But only as long as she has not remarried . Otherwise there would be \"a simple way out\" for an unhappy remarried woman: she gives her get back to the first husband, he says \"look, I've got the get in my hand, I swapped it,\" and her second marriage is undone. Once she has remarried, he is not believed. The Pitchei Teshuva brings another case where he would not be believed even before she remarried: if he is a Kohen . He could use this claim to say there was never a get and return to live with her, since otherwise as a Kohen he could not take back a divorcee. Why the get is torn The Beit Shmuel says this is why the get is torn ( keri'a ) afterwards. Since it has been marked, it is clear that this was the document used to divorce. No one can later claim that a different document was given and that the original is in his hand. The class confirmed the order: they read it, and then tear it. Part 4: Not Read, and No Longer in Her Hands (Seif 3) הרי שלא קרא הגט קודם נתינה, ונאבד או זרקתו If the get is still before us, one can always read it afterwards, as in Seif 1. Here she has thrown it away, so it can no longer be checked. Which of Tosafot's answers does the Shulchan Aruch follow? Clearly the second : she is a safek megureshet , and the get must be done again. The Rema adds that because she is safek megureshet , afilu nis'et tetze : even if she remarried, she must leave. The Rema then brings yesh cholkin , which is Tosafot's first answer : lechatchila lo tinasei, ve-im nis'et lo tetze . Rabbeinu Yerucham: even if she still holds it The Rema adds a further point from Rabbeinu Yerucham : even if the get is still in her hand , and she has not thrown it away, she should lechatchila not remarry with it. Yet we learned in Seif 1 that the get can be read afterwards and it is fine. The difference is timing. Reading afterwards works when it is done immediately , in the same context. Rabbeinu Yerucham speaks of where she comes back with the get a day or a week later and says, \"we didn't read the get, but this is the get.\" That is too late, a kind of extended penalty. What is the time limit? A student pointed out that we allow the husband to take the get out of sight for a moment. The Rabbi: \"It's very hazy.\" He would say: the same sitting, the same context . Once everyone has gone out and come back, it is really too late. The Rambam The Rambam says she is only safek megureshet where the get has been lost or thrown away . If she still has it in her hand, then even without the edim having read it, there are the edei mesira and the signed edim on it, and the fact that she holds it is enough for her to remarry lechatchila. That is how the Beit Yosef reads the Rambam. A student suggested that where the get was not read and she cannot remarry with it, the husband could simply be given it back and give it again, without writing a new get. The Rabbi recalled a sugya suggesting that a flaw in the setting of the giving can taint the get itself, though he could not place it, and thought one might at least need a new pair of edim. Practical lessons Always read the get before giving it. At least the edim must read it. The minhag is that the dayanim, or at least the Rosh Beth Din, read it too. It is good to read it after as well. At the very least, if it was not read before, read it afterwards. Make sure the husband has no opportunity to be alone with the get. \"This is important, by the way.\" If the dayanim suddenly have to step out before the giving and the get is left on the table, they should not do that; and if they must, the get has to be read again before it is given . Part 5: A Blank Paper (Seif 4) The Gemara brings Shmuel : if he gives her a niyar chalak (a blank paper) and says the correct words, she is divorced. How can that be, if there is no writing? The Gemara answers that Shmuel means where the get is checked afterwards with mei milin (a liquid that brings out hidden writing): if writing appears, it works; if nothing appears, it is nothing. And even when writing appears, Shmuel's \"divorced\" means only safek megureshet , since perhaps the writing was not there at the time of giving. What does niyar chalak mean? The Rabbi asked the class. A student said: completely blank. And what is Shmuel's doubt? Maybe there is ink on it that only appears later. The Rabbi pressed: if it is known for certain that the get was written in invisible ink, given while nothing could be seen, and only later brought out, is it a get? The Gemara seems to say: who cares if it comes out now; at the time of giving there was no visible writing. Opinion Meaning of niyar chalak Shmuel's doubt Tosafot, and so Rashi implies Not truly blank. The edim did not have a chance to examine it properly and were not sure whether there was writing Whether there was writing at the time. If it were known to have been blank, or known to have only invisible writing, bringing it out later would not help Ritva Truly blank to the eye, with absorbed writing ( ksav balua ) Is invisible writing considered writing? If it comes out afterwards, it was there all along: is that enough? The student found Tosafot's reading hard to fit into the words of the Gemara, which seem to reconstruct the case only through the test. The Rabbi agreed \"it's still a bit difficult,\" though the end of Tosafot can be read that way, and Rashi also learns like this. The Rabbi then asked: what about the reading? If the writing came out afterwards, why not just read it afterwards, which we said is fine? That would be difficult for the Maharam, who requires reading beforehand. Without any test What if a niyar chalak , whatever it means, was given and no test was done afterwards ? Bepashtut one would say there is no doubt at all. Tosafot: the test was only needed in the Gemara's initial assumption that she is definitely divorced. Now that it is concluded to be a doubt, the doubt exists even without the test . It is not better than a case where the get was not read before or after, but it is not worse. The Rashba rejects this: if so, the Gemara should have said so. Shmuel's doubt exists only with the test . Without a test, even Shmuel agrees there is nothing, and she is not divorced. The plain reading of the Gemara is more like the Rashba. The Shulchan Aruch נתן לה נייר חלק ואמר לה הרי זה גיטך, אם לא בדקו הנייר במי מילין לראות אם יש בו שום דבר כתוב, או אם בדקו ויצא הכתב, הרי זו ספק מגורשת The Rabbi pointed out that the text must read o im (\"or if\"); some editions print only \" im \", which does not make sense. The Tur and the old editions have \" o im \". So the Shulchan Aruch includes both cases : either no test was done, or a test was done and the writing appeared. In both, she is a safek megureshet . Only if they tested it and nothing appeared is she not divorced. That is like Tosafot, not the Rashba . What does niyar chalak mean for the Shulchan Aruch? Some follow the Ritva : even if it is known for certain there was no visible writing before, there is still a doubt, because the Gemara's doubt is whether absorbed writing counts. The Beit Shmuel rules that if it is known for certain there was no writing, even if writing came out afterwards, it is not a get. The Gemara's doubt is only where it was unclear before the giving whether there was writing on it. Part 6: Thrown Into Her Courtyard (Seif 5) זרק לה הגט לחצרה או לבין החביות, ובקשו ולא מצאו אלא מזוזה... This is also on the same daf. He threw a get into her courtyard (her domain, so that is sorted out), and when they go to look they find only one document, a mezuza (a small parchment scroll). Only one document there: no concern. We assume the one document found is the one he threw, and it is a mezuza. What are the chances that he threw a get and a mouse took it away, leaving only a mezuza? Very unlikely. She is not divorced, and there is no concern. Several documents there (for example between the barrels): there were already documents there before, so perhaps he did throw a get and it was taken away. Where does reading come in? The Shulchan Aruch: if they did not read the get beforehand, then with only one document found there is no concern, and with several documents she is a safek megureshet . If they did read it, then whether one mezuza or several were found, she is a safek megureshet . Why is reading relevant? A student suggested the doubt is whether it landed in her courtyard. The Rabbi: no, nothing in this sugya is about the courtyard. The doubt is that perhaps he switched it , and what he threw was the mezuza. Because they read it, we know a get existed, so the question becomes whether he threw the get or switched it for the mezuza that was found. Had it not been read at all and only one mezuza found, we would say there was nothing. Three positions: Opinion Read beforehand, mezuza(s) found Rashba Read in advance and thrown: whether one mezuza or several, certainly divorced Shulchan Aruch (Tur, Rambam) Even having read it, and even with two or three documents there, still safek megureshet : \" afilu nimtze'a mezuza achat \" Bach (brought in the Beit Shmuel) A middle view: with one mezuza, safek ; with several documents already there, she is certainly divorced , since it is highly unlikely that what he threw was a mezuza when there were documents there already \"Very good. That is the end of Siman 135.\" Summary of the Halachot The edim must read the get before it is given (Gittin 19b). If it was not read at all and can no longer be checked, she is a safek megureshet and must leave even a second husband (Tosafot's second answer, Shulchan Aruch, Rema). Some hold lechatchila she should not remarry, but if she did she need not leave. The dayanim read together with the edim (Rema), but it is the edim's reading that matters. Read only after the giving: she is divorced (all the Rishonim, Shulchan Aruch), against the Maharam's view that the edim's testimony was incomplete. It is good to read the get again after the giving (the Ri, Shulchan Aruch); checking it is the same get suffices (Rema). Reading afterwards must be in the same sitting. If the husband had the get alone after it was read, it should be read again. If not, she is divorced; he is only believed to claim a swap while holding the get and before she remarries, and a Kohen not even then. The get is torn afterwards so no one can claim another document was used (Beit Shmuel). Never leave the get unattended before the giving; if it was, read it again. A blank paper (Seif 4): if not tested, or tested and writing appeared, she is safek megureshet (Tosafot, Shulchan Aruch, against the Rashba). If tested and nothing appeared, she is not divorced. If known for certain there was no writing, it is not a get (Beit Shmuel). A get thrown into her courtyard (Seif 5): if it was not read and only a mezuza is found, there is no concern; if read, safek megureshet even with one mezuza (Shulchan Aruch). The Rashba holds she is certainly divorced; the Bach, certainly divorced where several documents were there.","ppt8RniEVBk":"Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Even HaEzer, Hilchot Gittin Siman: 143, Seifim 1-5 Shiur: A Dayanut shiur by Dayan Daniel Kada, a Wednesday in May 2023 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction: A \"Geshmak\" Siman \"Siman 143 is a really geshmak siman.\" It deals with a get given on condition ( get al tenai ). In most of it the Shulchan Aruch follows the Rambam , and the Rambam has \"a very interesting shita which everyone disagreed with him on.\" Since the Shulchan Aruch does seem to rule like him, the Rabbi began with the Rambam, and only then turned to the Shulchan Aruch. Part 1: The Rambam's Laws of Conditions The four requirements In Hilchot Ishut, chapter 6 , the Rambam sets out that whenever kiddushin or gerushin are made conditional, the condition must meet four requirements, the mishpetei ha-tenaim . If any one is missing, there is no condition. A doubled condition ( tenai kaful ): stated both ways. \"If you give me 100 zuz, you are divorced; if you don't, you are not divorced.\" The positive before the negative ( hen kodem la-lav ). The condition before the act ( tenai kodem la-ma'aseh ). It also cannot concern the past (\"if you slept yesterday, you are divorced\"). Something possible to fulfil. As the Gemara says, not \"on condition that you go up to the sky.\" \"Well, maybe nowadays that would work, because nowadays you can go to the moon,\" but in those days it was impossible. What happens if a condition is invalid? The whole act is not void. Rather, it is as if the condition was never made , and the kiddushin or gerushin take effect without it. \"Im\" and \"me-achshav\" The Rambam continues, later in the same chapter: if a man said, \"If I give you 200 zuz, you are betrothed,\" gave her the ring in Nisan , and paid the money only in Elul , the kiddushin take effect only from Elul . If someone else betrothed her in between, she is married to him. So far everyone agrees there are two kinds of conditions: Formula When it takes effect Im (\"if you do this, you are divorced\") Only later , when the condition is fulfilled. A regular condition, needing all four requirements Me-achshav (\"from now\") or al menat (\"on condition that\") Retroactively ( le-mafre'a ) from the time of giving, once the condition is fulfilled. The Gemara says al menat is like saying me-achshav . Anything in between does not undo it Part 2: An Unfulfilled Condition (Seif 1) The Shulchan Aruch begins with a point also found in the laws of kiddushin: if a condition is not fulfilled , it is as if nothing happened at all. It is not even re'ach ha-get (a \"whiff\" of a get). One might have argued that since there was some divorce here, it gives her the scent of a divorcee, so she could not return to him. No: if her husband later dies, she may even marry a Kohen . The Maharit: who made the approach? The Ba'er Heitev brings an interesting Maharit . The couple could not agree. He said: \"Give me 200, and I'll divorce you.\" She said: \"I won't give you 200. If you want to divorce, we divorce, but I'm not accepting the get if I have to pay.\" A stalemate. Later he gave her the get without mentioning the condition . Does she have to pay, and does the get work? The Maharit: if the husband approached her later and said \"I want to give you the get now,\" the get is without the condition, and she does not have to pay. If she approached him and said \"let's get divorced now,\" the condition still stands, and she has to pay. The source, as the Pitchei Teshuva explains, is the law of buying and selling . If buyer and seller could not agree on a price (the seller wanted 200, the buyer offered 100), and later one of them comes back and the sale goes through without mentioning a price: if the buyer came back saying \"I'm ready to buy,\" he has accepted the seller's price of 200; if the seller came back saying \"I'm ready to sell,\" he has accepted 100. So too with gerushin: whoever propositions afterwards has accepted the other's terms. The Pitchei Teshuva rejects this. This is not buying and selling; it is ma'aseh gerushin , and what counts is what was said at the time of the divorce . \"We may even have been to the Beth Din and we talked and we discussed,\" but even if it is the next morning, if at the giving no condition was mentioned, the Maharit would make her pay on the basis of yesterday's discussion. He brings a proof from the Radbaz : as long as the condition was not stated at the time of the gerushin , it is not a condition. She does not pay, whoever made the approach, and the get is kasher. When the Beth Din forgets the condition \"Very interestingly, you should know this, because you are sadrei gittin \" (those who arrange gittin): the Pitchei Teshuva brings the exact opposite in another case. The husband comes to the Beth Din in the morning and says: \"I'm ready to give the get, on condition that she pays me £200.\" The Rosh Beth Din says, \"Fine.\" Then the woman comes in, they write and give the get, and everyone forgets to write or mention the condition. Does she have to pay? Based on what we just learned, there was no condition at the time of giving. But here the Pitchei Teshuva says she does have to pay . In strong language, the husband says: \"I only agreed, I only came to the Beth Din, on the basis that I would be getting £200 from this. At the end of the day I'm getting nothing, so I didn't agree to this.\" Expressing his intention to the Beth Din, who arrange the get, makes it as if it were there at the time of the act. \"It's as if I gave the get with the condition. If the mesader ha-get forgot to say it, it's his problem, not mine.\" The Rabbi noted that the mechanism is interesting. One cannot hold the woman liable, since she was never told; one cannot hold the Beth Din liable, since it is only indirect ( grama ); and the husband cannot lose out, since a get must be given willingly. So his expressed intention carries through to the giving, even though the Beth Din did not say anything, and the woman does not need to know, just as a get can be given against her will. The same sitting? It obviously has to be in the same context . A student from Perth raised a real difficulty: a couple might come to the Beth Din for a discussion, or the husband might come alone, and the get is only done at the next sitting, which could be six months later . The Rabbi's answer: if six months earlier he expressed his intention, he still remembers it and is relying on the Beth Din , entering the get \"with that frame of mind,\" then it would seem to be the same. But if everyone forgot, including him , then it is a kasher get with no condition. When the Beth Din says something different If the Beth Din said something else, his silence counts as agreement. In fact, when a get is given, it is said that this is a get without anything attached to it . If he kept quiet, he has accepted that. Likewise if he wanted 200 and the Beth Din told him he can give the get on condition of 100, he must either object and not go ahead, or, if he keeps quiet and proceeds, he has agreed to 100. Part 3: The Problem in the Rambam (Hilchot Gerushin 8) So far, from the laws of kiddushin, it seems there are two levels: im , which works later, and me-achshav , which works now, retroactively. For me-achshav and al menat , the Rambam holds the laws of conditions do not apply : it need not be doubled or stated before the act, \"because it's working somehow differently.\" (Not all Rishonim agree.) Now to Hilchot Gerushin, chapter 8, halacha 1 , the main source of the Shulchan Aruch. The Rambam says that a get given with im only works later, not when it is given. So if he gave the get in Nisan and said, \"If you pay me 200 zuz in Elul, you are divorced,\" she is not divorced until Elul, even though she holds the get. Therefore: he can change the condition , add money or take it off; if he dies , or the get is destroyed , before the condition is fulfilled, it does not work. And then the striking line: lo tinasei ad she-yitkayem ha-tenai; ve-im nis'et lo tetze : she should not remarry until the condition is fulfilled, but if she did, she need not leave. \"Does that make sense?\" If the divorce only happens later, how can she stay married to someone else in the meantime? \"It's a huge, everyone asks this question\": the Maggid Mishneh and the Mishneh LaMelech among others. Many Rishonim simply leave it as a difficulty. Four differences between \"im\" and \"me-achshav\" Im Me-achshav / al menat 1. Can he change the condition? Yes No: it already works retroactively 2. If the husband dies first Not divorced; she needs chalitza Divorced retroactively while he was alive; no yibum needed 3. Must the get still be with her at fulfilment? Yes: the divorce happens then, and (as in Siman 135-136) she must have the get at the moment it takes effect No: it does not matter if she no longer holds it 4. Remarrying before fulfilment (Rambam) Lechatchila not; if she did, she need not leave Lechatchila she may The first three are straightforward. The fourth is the problem, and on both of its halves many Rishonim disagree. Part 4: The Rambam's Three Levels (Hilchot Gerushin 9) The Rambam's own approach emerges in chapter 9 , which discusses \" harei zeh gittech le-achar zman ,\" a divorce that simply takes effect at a later time, with no condition attached. \"I'm divorcing you, and this will happen later on. I don't say 'if this happens' or 'if you give me this'.\" The Rambam compares it to, and distinguishes it from, a conditional get. A later halacha there clarifies the difference, and is the key to it all. The Rabbi noted that a great deal has been written on how to understand this (the Avi Ezri among others). \"Don't ask me.\" But the plain reading of the Rambam shows three levels : Level What it is Laws of conditions? How it works 1. Me-achshav (and al menat ) Divorce now , pending fulfilment No. The Rambam never calls this gerushin al tenai . It must only be possible to fulfil, otherwise it simply cannot take effect Takes effect now. She may remarry lechatchila 2. Im The middle level Yes, all four requirements The divorce has begun but not ended 3. Le-achar zman Divorce at a later time No, it is not a condition at all Nothing happens now. If he dies, or the get is not in her possession when it takes effect, there is no divorce The middle level is the Rambam's chiddush . With im , although the complete divorce happens six months later, the gerushin have already somewhat begun . Because it is not complete, she should not remarry. But because the process has begun, \"it's like there's been a partial\" divorce. That is enough to allow her to stay with a second husband if she married, and for the children not to be mamzerim . So for the Rambam, the laws of conditions ( hilchot tenaim ) operate only in the category of im . Me-achshav is not hilchot tenaim : it works now. Le-achar zman is not hilchot tenaim : it simply works later. Im is a special case where the act works partially but not completely. \"That's a unique shita which the Rishonim didn't accept. They all disagreed with him. They said there's no middle ground.\" A student remarked that this contradicts everything we intuitively know about conditions. The Rabbi: \"Because we're used to learning it in yeshiva, not with the Shulchan Aruch.\" Does the Rambam apply this elsewhere? A student asked whether this applies beyond gittin, for example to the transfer of ownership. The Rabbi suggested how it might: \"This is yours if you go to London.\" Before you go, may you use the item? The Rav would tell you not to, since you have not gone yet. But if you did use it, perhaps it is fine, because the transfer has already somewhat begun though not completed. \"It's not how we ever learned in yeshiva.\" Part 5: The Shulchan Aruch (Seif 2) The Shulchan Aruch quotes the Rambam verbatim : a get on condition with im only takes effect later; even though she holds the get, he can change the conditions; if the get was lost or burnt before the condition was fulfilled, she is not divorced; and lo tinasei . The difference between im and al menat is pure Rambam, \"no major chiddushim there.\" But regarding a get me-achshav , he does not follow the Rambam. He rules like the Ramban and the Ran : even with al menat , she should not remarry , because the condition may not end up being fulfilled. If she did remarry, they separate until the condition is fulfilled . The exception: a condition that is a negative act ( shev ve-al ta'aseh ) and within his control , for example \"on condition that you never go to Australia in your life.\" No one can force him to go, so she may remarry. The Rambam makes no such distinctions. \"Ve-im nis'et tetze\": Beit Shmuel and Taz The big question is the words ve-im nis'et tetze in the Shulchan Aruch's section on im . The Beit Shmuel reads them as continuing the previous line: if the get was burnt or lost and she remarried, she must leave, because there is no longer a get. The Rambam does not address a case where she married while the get was intact. So the Shulchan Aruch follows the Rambam : with im , if she remarried, she may stay. The Taz reads it as a new statement : with im , if she remarried (even with the get intact), she must leave . This requires \"putting a full stop\" in the text, a slightly more strained reading. The Taz's proof: the Shulchan Aruch rules that with me-achshav , if she remarried, she must separate. If he followed the Rambam that with im she may stay, then im would be more lenient than me-achshav , which makes no sense, since me-achshav is the stronger divorce. So the Shulchan Aruch must be following all the Rishonim against the Rambam. Halacha lema'aseh , as the poskim rule: with an im condition, if she remarried, she must separate from him and they must not have children until the condition is fulfilled. If they did have a child, the Rabbi suggested the child would be a safek mamzer : kasher according to the Rambam, a mamzer according to the Rishonim. Summary of the three levels \"Maybe you want to just write this down\": Me-achshav / al menat Im Le-achar zman Takes effect Retroactively from the giving When fulfilled At the stated time Mishpetei ha-tenaim Rambam: no. Rosh: yes. Shulchan Aruch (in Hilchot Kiddushin): stam like the Rambam, yesh like the Rosh Everyone agrees: yes Everyone agrees: no; it is not a condition at all Get destroyed or husband dies before fulfilment Fine: it works retroactively The get must still exist The get must exist and be with her Remarrying before fulfilment: Rambam Lechatchila permitted Lechatchila not; if she did, she stays Not permitted; if she did, she must leave and the child is a mamzer Remarrying before fulfilment: Shulchan Aruch Not lechatchila (Ramban, Ran); if she did, separate, unless a negative condition in his control Beit Shmuel: like the Rambam. Taz and most poskim: she must leave Same as the Rambam \"It's just very useful to have the three levels in your head.\" The Shulchan Aruch ends Seif 2 by noting that the date of the get functions like a condition, which will be discussed in Siman 144. The Rema brings one Rishon who holds that al menat is not like saying me-achshav , and is concerned for that stringency; this too belongs to Siman 144. Part 6: Paying a Dead Man (Seif 3) על מנת שתתני לי מאתים זוז מכאן ועד שלושים יום, ומת בתוך שלושים, קודם שנתנה לו, אינה יכולה לקיים התנאי She cannot fulfil the condition by paying his heirs. Did we not learn that al menat can be fulfilled after his death? The answer: he said \"give it to me .\" This is a machloket in the Gemara between Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel , who allows payment to the heirs, and the Tanna Kamma , who holds that \"to me\" means to him alone. We rule like the Tanna Kamma. By contrast, if the condition were \"on condition that you travel to Perth,\" she could fulfil it after his death; but \"give me money\" means give it to him . Limbo: no yibum and no freedom Now the next part of the seif. He said \"on condition that you give me 200 zuz\" with no time limit , and died. Clearly she cannot fulfil it, so the get has not taken effect. But the Shulchan Aruch says not only that she needs yibum or chalitza: she cannot do yibum, only chalitza . Why? \"This is the shita of the Rambam, again this middle shita.\" The Shulchan Aruch is deliberately lengthy in the first part of the seif: With a thirty-day limit: day 1, day 2, day 30 come and go, and she has not given it. The condition is void , so there is no get at all. She does yibum or chalitza as normal. With no time limit: we do not say the condition is void. She is in limbo : she has not fulfilled it, but it has not been cancelled either. So the get has not taken effect, and she cannot marry anyone else. But she also cannot do yibum , because the condition has not been voided, so \"there's this mini get hanging in the air.\" With a possible get in place, the yavam may be her husband's brother whom she is forbidden to marry. The only option is chalitza . Other Rishonim do not see this middle ground: once he died she can never fulfil the condition, so it should be the same as the thirty-day case. A student asked: why isn't this like an impossible condition, which is void? The Rabbi: at the time it was made, it was entirely possible. \"Go to the sky\" is impossible from the start. \"Give me 200 pounds\" was very possible; he just died the next day, and with no time frame, the condition goes on and is never voided. Part 7: Giving, Forgiving and Substitutes (Seifim 4-5) Payment against his will She gave the 200 as he asked, but he would not accept it: he was running away from her. So she threw it into his courtyard, or put it through his letterbox. Is that a valid giving? The Rambam holds that min haTorah giving against his will is a valid giving. Other Rishonim say it is a safek , since the Gemara leaves it unresolved, so she must leave ( tetze ) if she remarried. \"As we've learned many times,\" the difference between pasul and safek is big: pasul means don't remarry, but if she did, she may stay; safek means they cannot live together, since a child would be a safek mamzer . \"It's forgiven\" He said \"give me 200,\" and then within the thirty days said \" I forgive it \" ( machul lach ). The Gemara says she is not divorced . Part of his condition was that she should endure the pain ( tza'ar ) of paying. Even though he forgave the money, that aspect was never experienced, so the condition was not fulfilled. But if he said \" the get works without it, don't worry about it ,\" that works, even without giving the get again: he has changed the condition and effectively cancelled it. The Shulchan Aruch writes this as yesh omrim , but the Beit Shmuel says it should not be yesh omrim : there is no machloket, everyone agrees. The Pitchei Teshuva brings a machloket about someone else paying . If her father paid from his own account, does it work? Is the condition about her pain, so it must be her money (meaning her father would have to give it to her, and she give it to the husband)? Or does the husband ultimately just want the money, so as long as he receives it, it is sufficient? Paying the value instead (Seif 5) If she gives the value of what was stipulated instead of the thing itself, it does not work until she gives the actual item or he says the get works without it. Here too the Beit Shmuel says it is not really a yesh omrim . The Rema brings yesh omrim that if he agrees to accept the value, it works, since that shows he wanted its financial worth. \"Very good. It was lovely. I enjoyed learning again.\" The next shiur was set for Wednesday the 17th. Summary of the Halachot A conditional get or kiddushin needs the four mishpetei ha-tenaim : doubled, positive first, condition before the act, and possible. Otherwise the condition is void and the act stands. An unfulfilled condition leaves nothing at all, not even re'ach ha-get : she may later marry a Kohen. A condition not stated at the giving is not a condition, whoever made the approach (Pitchei Teshuva, Radbaz, against the Maharit). But if the husband told the Beth Din his condition and they forgot to state it, it still binds. If he stays silent when the Beth Din states otherwise, he has agreed. Im takes effect only when fulfilled: he can change it, his death or the loss of the get cancels it. Me-achshav / *al menat * works retroactively. The Rambam's three levels: me-achshav (divorced now), im (begun but not completed, so if she remarried she may stay), le-achar zman (nothing until then). The Rishonim reject the middle level. Remarrying before fulfilment: with me-achshav , not lechatchila, and if she did, separate, except for a negative condition in his control (Ramban, Ran, Shulchan Aruch). With im , the Taz and most poskim say she must leave; the Beit Shmuel reads the Shulchan Aruch like the Rambam. \"Give me\" means him, not his heirs (Tanna Kamma). With no time limit and he died, she is in limbo: no yibum, only chalitza (Rambam, Shulchan Aruch). Payment against his will is valid min haTorah for the Rambam; others treat it as a safek . \"It's forgiven\" does not fulfil the condition; \"the get works without it\" cancels the condition (Beit Shmuel: no machloket). Paying the value works only if he accepts it (Rema).","HbAlC5-yWoE":"Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Even HaEzer, Hilchot Gittin Siman: 130, Seifim 9-18 Shiur: A Dayanut shiur by Dayan Daniel Kada, Wednesday 9 March 2022 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Where We Are One of the students had just finished two solid days of Beth Din sittings, with four gittin in one go. Where he serves, the Beth Din only convenes a few times a year, so the sofer writes several gittin in a day. The aim was to finish Siman 130, the siman of the chatima (the signing of the edim), to keep pace with the other shiur. The previous shiur covered Seifim 1-8: Seif 1 on the general concept of chatima and why we have it, and Seifim 2-8 on peculiar cases of signatures. Seifim 9 and 10 go together, and deal with divorcing several women with one document . Part 1: One Get for Five Women (Seifim 9-10) The Mishna and the machloket The Mishna says that if one writes one get for five women: if it is a klal (a unified document), they are all divorced; if it is a tofes (a series of separate texts), only the last one is divorced. What do klal and tofes mean? Opinion What makes it a klal Rabbi Yochanan It depends on the zman (the date). One date written once unites the signatures into one, so they refer to all five, together with \" u-ploni \" joining each one Resh Lakish Even one date is not enough. Only if they write \"we, so-and-so and so-and-so, have divorced our wives\" at the beginning, and then specify at the end who is divorcing whom (X for Y, A for B), otherwise the get is lacking ve-chatav lah (written specifically for her) The concern behind this, as earlier in the siman (the case of a greeting written beneath the get), is always: what are the signatures going on? If there is one date, everything is united and the signatures cover all five. If there are five dates, the signatures only cover the last one. This is even where all five dates are the same day; with different dates it would anyway be pasul because of mukdam (pre-dating). The halacha is like Rabbi Yochanan against Resh Lakish, and so the Shulchan Aruch rules: one date suffices to make it one get. \"Tofes\" in a new sense \"Don't get confused.\" Until now in the siman, tofes was contrasted with toref : the toref is the essential part (names, date), and the tofes the standard text. Here tofes means something else: does each woman need her own separate body of text? The Rambam , in his plain reading, holds that one body of text suffices. If they are all divorcing in the same place on one date, apart from the names everything can be shared. The Tur holds that five tofsim are needed: one date to unite them, but for each woman the names, the places and the essential text of the get. The words harei at muteret le-chol adam (you are permitted to any man) are part of the toref, so they must be written for each. The Shulchan Aruch quotes the Rambam's language, but in the Beit Yosef he explains the Rambam like the Tur. The Beit Shmuel rules that this is the Shulchan Aruch's intention: five individual texts for five women. Edei mesira, and who may remarry Although according to the Rambam the edei chatima suffice, bediavad everyone agrees edei mesira are needed too . This one document must be given in front of edei mesira to each of the five women. What if the edei mesira are not before us, and the women want to remarry? Who is divorced Tur Each woman in turn: the first brings the document to the Beth Din, it is approved, and it is passed on to the next Rambam, Shulchan Aruch, Beit Shmuel, Taz Only the one who holds the document: ha-megilla yotzet mitachat yadah . It cannot be passed on, since we know it was in her possession Does Rabbi Yochanan accept Resh Lakish's method? If one did it Resh Lakish's way (\"we, so-and-so and so-and-so, have divorced...\"), does that work for Rabbi Yochanan too? Most hold, of course, that Rabbi Yochanan agrees, as long as at the end it is specified who is divorcing whom. The Rashba disagrees: for Rabbi Yochanan, writing it that way is too much unification , so it lacks ve-chatav lah . The Shulchan Aruch follows the Rambam, Rif and Ran : it is kasher. Resh Lakish argues with Rabbi Yochanan, but Rabbi Yochanan agrees to Resh Lakish's method. Different dates Where there are different dates, or even, says the Beit Shmuel, where the words \" u-ve-yom ploni \" (and on such a day) are written for each, the signatures go only on the last one, and only the last is certainly divorced. But the others, if they come to the Beth Din holding the get, are safek megureshet . They are not simply invalid; it is just that, bepashtut , the signatures go on the last one. The Shulchan Aruch inside Bichlal means unified with one date, and with the tofes written for each: like Rabbi Yochanan, and like the Tur. It must be given with edei mesira. Without edei mesira before us, only the one holding the document is divorced, like the Rambam and not like the Tur. If each has its own date, then with edei mesira all are fine. Without them, if the one holding the get is the last, she is divorced; if it is one of the first ones, it is a doubt. Writing \" uve-yom ploni \" for each also breaks the unity. If they wrote it in the plural (Resh Lakish's way), it is kasher: the Shulchan Aruch rules like the Rishonim against the Rashba. Summary of Seifim 9-10 Case Edei mesira present Without edei mesira One date, u-ploni , each with its text All five divorced The woman holding the get is certainly divorced; the other four are safek Five dates Only the last certainly divorced If the last holds the get, she is certainly divorced; if another holds it, all are safek Written in the plural (Resh Lakish's way) without specifying at the end who divorces whom, the get is not merely a doubt: it is batel. Part 2: Peirush Shemotehem : Writing Out the Names (Seif 11) The Mishna: originally the edim signed, and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel instituted that they should write out their names ( mefarshin shemotehem ). According to Rabbi Meir ( edei chatima karti , the signatures effect the divorce), signatures are anyway needed min haTorah , so the takana means: don't just sign a mark, as one signs for a bank. Write the names out properly, \"black and white.\" According to Rabbi Elazar ( edei mesira karti , the delivery effects it), signatures are not really needed. The Gemara says the takana was that they should sign at all, in case the edei mesira die. We rule like Rabbi Elazar. So does the takana of writing out the names apply? The Rashba holds that according to Rabbi Elazar, a signature alone suffices , with no need to write out the names. The Rambam rules like Rabbi Elazar and still requires peirush shemotehem . Bepashtut : once the takana was explained as writing out the names for Rabbi Meir, the same applies for Rabbi Elazar, even if it is not essential. The Shulchan Aruch follows the Rambam. What does peirush shemotehem mean? Opinion Meaning Rashi, Rashba Writing your name, as opposed to a scribbled signature Tur Even before the takana they wrote their first name. Peirush shemotehem means your name and your father's name The Shulchan Aruch rules like the Tur, which is why edim sign their name and their father's name. \"Despite the fact the whole concept of chatima is in itself an addition you don't need... we take on the most.\" The Beit Shmuel makes an important point. In Siman 133 we will learn that if there are only edei mesira and the get has not yet been given, it should not be given. But if there were signatures, only without fulfilling the takana of peirush shemotehem , the get may be given once it has been written. \"So you see how this whole concept of peirush shemotehem is a kind of chumra.\" \"Yosef ben Yaakov, ed\" The Mishna, and the Shulchan Aruch, rule that any of these suffices: - your name and your father's name without the word ed ; - your name with the word ed ; - your father's name with the word ed . But just \" Yosef \", without ed , is not enough. The Rivash explains: a name alone does not show that it is testimony. Signing with your father's name shows that you are signing as a witness, so you do not need ed . With one name, you need the word ed . Why pasul, if it is only a chumra? (Seif 12) The Shulchan Aruch says that \"Yosef\" alone is pasul , because the names were not written out. The Rabbi asked the class: if the whole thing is a chumra, why should it invalidate the get? The answer: all of this is without edei mesira . If edei mesira come before us, it is all fine; nothing here is intrinsically flawed ( mezuyaf mitocho ). This is speaking where we rely on the signatures alone, either because there were no edei mesira or because they are not before us. A key principle for all of Siman 130 and 133: when a get has proper signatures, we have a chazaka that it was given before kasher edei mesira, and we do not need to ask. When the signatures are questionable, the edei mesira must be brought before us. So \"pasul\" here means we cannot rely on this get unless edei mesira come. Even holding that edei mesira are everything, the signatures matter, because they give us the chazaka of the edei mesira. It is the same as last week, where after all the cases of pasul signatures the Shulchan Aruch said: if there are edei mesira, it is kasher. Part 3: The Rema on Signing A mistake in the father's name The Rema: since \"Yosef ed \" suffices, if the witness erred in his father's name , it is kasher, though only bish'at hadechak . What is an \"error\"? The Beit Shmuel : a slip in writing. But if your father is Yaakov and you wrote \"ben Yitzchak\", \"that cannot be.\" That is not a mistake; it is a different person. The Rabbi agreed with the Pitchei Teshuva and the Beit Meir : this will not suffice, because it gives the impression of a different person. Other details No de-mitkarei or kinuim for edim, even lechatchila. Just the main name. No honorifics : not Rav or Chacham , just \" Ploni ben Ploni, ed \", as in the get itself. Anyone with a question about his name should not sign , for example a witness whose father is a meshumad, or any case where there is doubt how to write his name. \"Stay out of it.\" Eliyahu and Eliya The Darkei Moshe says that someone named Eliyahu should not sign, because it is unclear whether it is written with or without the final vav . The Rabbi: \"I don't think we do this.\" Taken literally, a person named Eliyahu could never sign, and the same would be true of a Matisyahu for Ashkenazim. A student said that in five or six years they had never had an issue with it. The Rabbi mentioned that in Israel there are professional edim : with ten gittin a day, every day, being a witness is their nine-to-five job. In London they pay a witness something like £25. \"So if someone wants to apply for the job and his name was Eliyahu, would they turn him down?\" A signature in Rashi script The Rabbi read with the class a Pitchei Teshuva he especially likes. It brings a case where the signatures were in Rashi script rather than ktav Ashurit , and the question of whether they could be read. The Panim Me'irot discusses a name where a child read the reish as a dalet and the tzadi as a yud-nun , misreading \"Peretz.\" The principle: even if an ordinary child misreads it, if a clever child can read it correctly, it is fine . The Ashkenazi children could not read it, but a clever Sephardi child could, and they relied on that. \"I found this fascinating\": how, in the middle of Europe two hundred years ago, they managed to find a clever Sephardi child. A student noted that Rashi script has other problems for a get, for example its final mem resembles a samech . The Rabbi agreed, while pointing out that the case they read concerned the signatures, a different question from the text of the get. An aside on reading Hebrew correctly The Rabbi shared a recent frustration from a parents' evening: in conversation one may say \"Dovid\" or \"Yosef,\" but when reading Torah or Tehillim one must read correctly, with the stress in the right place. Children reading from a Tehillim with no trop and no meteg have no way of knowing where the stress falls, as in mizmor le-Asaf . Why do Ashkenazim struggle with this? European languages are mostly mil'el (stressed on the penultimate syllable), while Sephardim, coming from Arabic, naturally stress mil'ra : \"Asaf,\" \"Yosef,\" \"David\" come out right without effort. Part 4: Signing in Front of Each Other (Seif 13) עדי הגט אין חותמין אלא זה בפני זה, ואם חתמו שלא זה בפני זה פסול The edim of the get sign only in each other's presence; if they did not, it is pasul. The source is the Gemara in Gittin. The reason is a gezeira : if a husband told ten people to sign, and one did not, the get is batel, because he insisted that all ten sign (as learned in Siman 120). If all ten must sign together, they will not give the get when only nine have signed. If two were allowed to sign separately, they might give the get after two signed without the other eight. So there is a comprehensive gezeira that even two edim must always sign in each other's presence . The Darkei Moshe spells out that we do not say that since they signed together, verifying one signature ( kiyum ) automatically verifies the other. Both signatures must be verified. A third witness added afterwards The husband instructed two people to sign; they signed in each other's presence. Then he asked a third person to sign as well, after the other two had gone. Is it kasher? Opinion Ruling Rashi Pasul: all three did not sign in each other's presence Rabbeinu Tam Kasher: once two have signed, as the husband originally instructed, the get is finished The Ran explains the root of the machloket beautifully, and it will come up in the laws of shlichut : can a husband nullify a get once it is complete? For Rashi, the husband's wish for a third signature nullifies the existing get, so all three must sign together. For Rabbeinu Tam, once it has been signed it can no longer be nullified, so his wish for a third signature does not matter. The Shulchan Aruch does not mention this, though the Beit Yosef brings the machloket. The plain silence of the Shulchan Aruch, not distinguishing, suggests pasul, like Rashi. The Rema's wording is not great: it could imply all three were there from the start, which is obviously pasul. The Beit Shmuel takes the trouble to explain that it means the first two signed and then the third was asked to sign. Part 5: Languages (Seifim 14-16) Can a get be in two languages? Although not in the Shulchan Aruch, this is a good introduction. The Rambam says a get written half in Hebrew and half in another language is pasul , apparently based on a Tosefta. The Ra'avad says it is kasher . The Tosefta that invalidates it speaks where each witness signs in a language matching a different part. If the get is in two languages and the edim sign in one language, the signatures unify the get. The Shulchan Aruch rules like the Rambam: pasul. Signatures in different languages The Mishna clearly says that edim may sign in different languages: one in Hebrew and one in another language (Seif 16) is kasher. What is the difference? The Beit Yosef explains that each signature is an entity in itself , while a get is one get. So one signature in one language and the other in another is absolutely fine. Likewise a get written in one language and signed in another (Hebrew and Greek, or the reverse) is kasher, as the Mishna says, and so rules the Shulchan Aruch: גט שנכתב בכתב מאיזה לשון ועדים חתומים בכתב אחר, כשר Must the witnesses understand it? The Yerushalmi says this is only where, when the get is read before the edim, they understand what is being read . \"Which makes sense. You can't sign on something which you don't at least understand.\" But the Rambam and the Shulchan Aruch write: ve-hu she-yihyu ha-edim makirim leshon ha-ktav ve-ha-ktiva , that the edim recognise both the language and the script. If it stopped at \"the language,\" it would parallel the Yerushalmi exactly. The Rabbi gave his own example: \"I can't read Arabic. I understand Arabic fluently.\" If a get were written in Arabic and read out to him, he would understand and could sign. So what does \" ve-ha-ktiva \" add? Must one be able to read the alphabet? The Beit Shmuel goes back and forth, and concludes, which is rare, that knowing the script is required only when the witness reads it himself . If it is read to him, it suffices that he understands what is read. Practical advice for small communities In a smaller community, where the same pool of witnesses is not always available, a regular congregant who cannot write Hebrew could sign \" Daniel, son of Chaim, witness \" in English. That would be better than what the Rabbi once saw at a wedding: the Rabbi holding his pen over the hand of a witness who could not write properly, guiding him. \"What's the point of that? That means he's signing. It's not your signature anymore.\" This will be discussed later. Part 6: One Invalid Witness (Seif 17) גט שחתמו בו עדים פסולים, אפילו אחד פסול ואחד כשר, הרי זה פסול, אף על פי שיש עדי מסירה, שהרי הוא מזויף מתוכו \"A very important klal .\" Although we rule like Rabbi Elazar that edei mesira effect the divorce, a get signed by an invalid witness, even one invalid and one valid, is pasul even with edei mesira , because it is mezuyaf mitocho , forged from within. The flaw is in the document itself. What kind of invalidity is this? The Geonim (quoted by the Rambam) hold the get is batel . The Rambam holds it is pasul derabbanan . Why should a rabbinic flaw negate edei mesira, which work min haTorah ? The Rabbi recalled an explanation of the Geonim he had heard in a shiur: with only edei mesira , the get is a shtar kinyan , a document of acquisition; the edei mesira effect the divorce. Once one adds signatures, it becomes a shtar ra'aya , a document of evidence, and a document of evidence that is mezuyaf mitocho is invalid. \"Even though you could have got away without anything, now that you put signatures in, you made it worse.\" The Shulchan Aruch writes harei zeh pasul , like the Rambam. Part 7: The Sofer as a Witness (Seif 18) יש ליזהר שלא לחתום הסופר לעד, מפני שיש פוסלים One should be careful that the sofer does not sign as a witness, since some invalidate it. This is a chumra, and to understand it we must go back to the great machloket in Siman 120 about omer imru (whether a husband can tell people to tell others to write and sign). If omer imru is invalid , the husband always speaks to the sofer and edim directly, and there is no reason a sofer cannot sign. If omer imru is valid , then a sofer signing is a problem. The husband tells two agents to tell the sofer to write and the edim to sign. The agents may feel it is a little degrading for the sofer only to write, as if the husband does not trust him, so they tell the sofer to write and sign and ask only one other witness. They have deviated from the husband's instruction, and the get is batel. Opinion Omer imru Sofer as witness Rif, Rambam ( pashtut ) Invalid Kasher Tosafot Valid Pasul The Ri Even if omer imru is invalid, pasul, taking the chumrot of both sides. Yet he himself rules that if she remarried it is kasher, and if not yet remarried, another get should be written This explains the Shulchan Aruch's wording: he does not say \"pasul\", but \" yesh lizaher \", one should be careful. Since he rules like the Rambam in Siman 120 that omer imru is invalid, by that logic a sofer signing should be kasher, and the chumra comes from taking on both sides. A student suggested that from the wording, even if the get has not yet been given, there is no need to write another, since \" yesh lizaher \" is even weaker than a lechatchila-bediavad distinction. \"That's a good point.\" What is clear: if she remarried with it and had a child, the child is absolutely fine, because we hold omer imru is invalid. Don't confuse this with the sofer serving as one of the three dayanim in the Beth Din. \"That's a different she'ela , much easier,\" and it can be done. Two edim and the sofer, three signatures? The Rosh and the Ran hold that one should not have the sofer sign together with two edim, because of the gezeira . When one of the three dies A final practical point. The husband says to three people: \"Write and sign.\" As learned in Siman 120, this means one writes and two sign. Then one of the three dies, and the husband cannot be reached to instruct again. Is there a way out? A student suggested the sofer sign. The Rabbi: no, the husband instructed one to write and two to sign, so that would be going against his instruction. The Beit Shmuel agrees: signing would be a problem. Rather, don't sign at all . Use edei mesira alone, which suffice. The Beit Meir , brought in the Pitchei Teshuva, asks: why is that against the husband's instruction? It is true that when a husband tells ten people to sign, all ten must sign, because he deliberately went beyond what is needed. But when he takes three people and says \"write and sign,\" all he means is \"sort me out.\" Had he known that bediavad the get could work with edei mesira, he would not have minded. The Pitchei Teshuva brings a case: a man going to war told three people to write and sign. He went missing in the war, and before they could act, one of the three died. Despite the Beit Meir, who was lenient against the Beit Shmuel, the authorities in that case did not want to rely on it , and she was left an aguna . The class broke for Purim, with the next shiur set for Wednesday 23 March. Summary of the Halachot One get for several women (Seifim 9-10): one date unites it (Rabbi Yochanan), each woman needs her own text (Tur, Beit Shmuel), and it must be given to each before edei mesira. Without them, only the woman holding it is certainly divorced. Resh Lakish's plural formula is kasher (against the Rashba). With separate dates, only the last is certainly divorced; the others are safek . Edim write out their names (Seif 11): name and father's name (Tur, Shulchan Aruch), even though we rule like Rabbi Elazar. A signature without this takana may still be given (Beit Shmuel). \"Yosef ben Yaakov\", \"Yosef ed\" or \"ben Yaakov ed\" suffice; \"Yosef\" alone is pasul (Seif 12), meaning we cannot rely on it without edei mesira. Proper signatures give a chazaka of edei mesira ; questionable ones require the edei mesira themselves. A slip in the father's name is kasher bish'at hadechak (Rema); writing a different name is not (Beit Shmuel, Beit Meir). No kinuim, no titles, and anyone with a doubtful name should not sign. Edim sign in each other's presence (Seif 13), and both signatures must be verified. A third witness added later: pasul for Rashi, kasher for Rabbeinu Tam. Languages: a get in two languages is pasul (Rambam, Shulchan Aruch); signatures in different languages, or a get signed in a different language from its text, are kasher. Edim must understand the get when it is read to them (Beit Shmuel). One invalid witness makes the get pasul even with edei mesira: mezuyaf mitocho (Seif 17). The sofer should not sign as a witness (Seif 18), a chumra stemming from omer imru . He may sit as one of the dayanim. If one of three appointed people dies , the Beit Shmuel says use edei mesira without signatures; the Beit Meir is lenient, but in practice others did not rely on it.","RBqL9DTwl9I":"Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Even HaEzer, Hilchot Gittin Siman: 129, Seif 16 to the end of the siman Shiur: A Dayanut shiur by Dayan Daniel Kada, Wednesday 2 February 2022 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Where We Are The aim was to finish Siman 129 in this shiur. The previous shiur covered Seifim 10-15: Seif 10: changing the father's or mother's name. You don't have to write them, but if you do, make sure not to change them. Seif 11: no names at all. Bepashtut the get is batel and the child a mamzer. Seifim 12-13: two names, but now in a third place. Seif 14: two names used at different times, written together. Seif 15: the first of four scenarios, a shortened or childish form of a name. Seif 15 leads straight into the next two seifim, so the Rabbi reviewed it. Seifim 15-17 together give four scenarios of a person with two related names. \"As I told you at the beginning of the siman, only the first three, four, five seifim are dina de-Gemara . The rest is conjecture of many of the Rishonim, and therefore it's talui al haminhag .\" Part 1: The Four Scenarios of Related Names Scenario 1: a shortened or childish form (Seif 15) The Shulchan Aruch's examples are forms like Yitzchak and Itzik, Aharon and a short form, Peretz and a short form. It is sufficient to write just the first name, Yitzchak or Aharon, because the short form comes out of the main name. If you want to write both you may, and today we probably would. Scenario 2: a non-Hebrew name that interprets the Hebrew one (Seif 16) The Shulchan Aruch: Yehuda is called Leib (lion), because of gur aryeh Yehuda . Since the second name comes out of the first, write \"Yehuda de-mitkarei Leib\"*, \"and so is the custom in these countries.\" But if the second name is totally unrelated, for example Yehuda and Paul, write ha-mechuneh***. This is a fundamental machloket: Basis Rule Shulchan Aruch Maharik Related name: de-mitkarei . Unrelated: ha-mechuneh Rema Mordechai It depends whether the name is kodesh (Hebrew) or la'az (vernacular). Two Hebrew names are on an equal footing: Yehuda de-mitkarei Yisrael . A la'az name is on a different footing: Yehuda ha-mechuneh Leib When in doubt, de-mitkarei . The Beit Shmuel says that if there is any doubt whether a name is kodesh or la'az , then even for Ashkenazim one writes de-mitkarei , because de-mitkarei is always more encompassing than ha-mechuneh . The Rabbi asked for an example of a name doubtful between Hebrew and vernacular. A student suggested David or Jacob. The Rabbi thought that according to the principles of this siman, an English David is not a new name at all: it is not a different kind of name, so it falls under last week's scenario. But he could hear the argument for classic biblical names whose English form differs a little, like Zedekiah or Zechariah. The Beit Shmuel on \"ha-mechuneh\" The surname Lieberman is actually the Yiddish form of Eliezer. Take a man whose Hebrew name is Eliezer Ze'ev , called to the Torah as Eliezer Ze'ev, but commonly called Lieberman Wolf . The Beit Shmuel (on Seif 14) \"incredibly\" says to write Eliezer ha-mechuneh Lieberman, Ze'ev ha-mechuneh Wolf . He holds that ha-mechuneh refers only to the last word before it. So for a man called to the Torah as Moshe Yehuda but known as Moshe Leib, he writes \"Moshe Yehuda ha-mechuneh Leib\", where ha-mechuneh goes only on Yehuda, since Moshe is just Moshe. All the Acharonim disagree: ha-mechuneh refers to the whole name . One writes Eliezer Ze'ev ha-mechuneh Lieberman Wolf . The Tiv Gittin goes so far as to say that a get written the Beit Shmuel's way is batel . Lema'aseh: definitely do like the Tiv Gittin, but perhaps one can rely on the Beit Shmuel if it was already written his way. If the vernacular name is part of the actual given name, for example he was named Yehuda Leib at birth and called to the Torah that way, then there is no ha-mechuneh at all. That is simply his name. Scenario 3: the family name (Seif 17, second part) Here there is another machloket between the Shulchan Aruch and the Rema, reflecting a machloket of Rishonim. Ruling Shulchan Aruch (Beit Yosef, following the Maharik , Rav Yosef Colon) Write the family name, but since it is not a personal name, write it with ha-mechuneh Rema (Darkei Moshe, following what he wrote in the Seder Gittin ) Never write a family name Why not? Among those who do write it, there is a debate over the formula: ha-mechuneh , or another term used specifically for family names. Since there is doubt, don't write any family name at all. Today, the minhag in all gittin across the world is not to write family names , \"despite the fact, if we're realistic, that's probably the best way to identify someone.\" Instead of all the de-mitkarei of David, Dovid and so on, the family name would be clearest. What we rely on is the principle from the Torat Gittin learned at the beginning of Siman 128, which \"will come up in a few places\": today there is a ma'aseh beit din (the Beth Din's record), which records the person's full name, address and postcode. That serves as the identification behind the get, and it is sufficient. The Pitchei Teshuva adds that even if a man is always called by his surname and even signs with it (his example is a man called Roizen, rarely called by his first name, who signed \"Roizen\"), one still does not write the family name. Scenario 4: an exact translation (Seif 17) אם השם השני בלשון לעז והכל אחד, כגון חיים ויוואנט, כותבין חיים דמתקרי ויוואנט Vivant (from which the English Vivian comes) means life: an exact translation of Chaim . This is different from Yehuda and Leib, where Leib is not literally Yehuda but the idea of Yehuda as a lion. Here the English is an exact translation, and still the Shulchan Aruch writes de-mitkarei . The Rema does not argue here, but relies on what he wrote in the previous seif: for a la'az name, always ha-mechuneh , even when it is an exact translation. The four scenarios together # Case Shulchan Aruch Rema 1 Short or childish form (Yitzchak, Itzik) Not needed; may be written Same 2 Vernacular name connected to the Hebrew (Yehuda, Leib) De-mitkarei ; unrelated name, ha-mechuneh Hebrew pair, de-mitkarei ; Hebrew and la'az, ha-mechuneh 3 Family name Written, with ha-mechuneh Never written (and this is the minhag today) 4 Exact translation (Chaim, Vivant) De-mitkarei Ha-mechuneh Part 2: Hebrew Name or Common Name? (The Rema in Seif 16) Back to the Rema in the middle of Seif 16: מי שיש לו שם עברי שעולה בו לקרות בתורה ויש עוד לו שם בלשון לעז, עושין שם העברי עיקר והשני כותבין דמתקרי. והוא הדין באשה עושין שם העברי עיקר To set the framework: when someone has two completely different names, say Chaim and Paul, that was covered at the beginning of the siman. These seifim are about similar names: Yehuda and Leon, Chaim and Vivian. Here the question is which comes first, a Hebrew name or a vernacular one. The Rema , quoting the Seder Gittin : the Hebrew name is always the ikar , even if it is less commonly used. For some reason, the Hebrew name has precedence. The Beit Yosef , in his own view: who cares whether it is Hebrew or vernacular? Write the name that is used more regularly. The Rabbi: \"I think it's very mistaber \" (very reasonable). The Beit Yosef's example is Simcha and Alegría (Spanish for happiness). If most people call her Alegría and she is rarely called Simcha: Text Shulchan Aruch (Sephardi gittin) Alegría de-mitkariya Simcha : the more common name first, and de-mitkarei since the names are related Rema Simcha ha-mechuneh Alegría : Hebrew first, and ha-mechuneh because the second is a la'az name \"There would be a double machloket there.\" Three limits on the Rema The Torat Gittin , brought in the Pitchei Teshuva, limits the Rema's rule: Only when the la'az name is an interpretation of the Hebrew name. If it has no connection and is the more common name, even the Rema agrees to write the common la'az name first. If her Hebrew name is Simcha but everyone calls her Catherine, write Catherine first. This explains a teshuva of the Rema about a woman named Esther whom everyone called Peslin , where he wrote Peslin first and then Esther. Since the names are unrelated, the common name comes first; where they are related, the Hebrew name is the ikar. \"I see some logic to that.\" (For the Shulchan Aruch, unrelated names take ha-mechuneh ; for the Rema, since they are on different levels, presumably also ha-mechuneh .) If he is called to the Torah and signs with the la'az name , then even the Rema agrees the la'az name comes first. The Rema's rule is only where the Hebrew name is the one used for aliya la-Torah . If the la'az name was added because of illness ( shem choli ), even the Rema writes it first, since, as we will see, the name of illness generally takes precedence. That is hard to imagine today, since we add Refael or Chaim, but evidently it was once common to add a vernacular name. A student described a Sephardi case: a man whose Hebrew name was Asher , called to the Torah as Mas'ud (Arabic, roughly \"prosperous,\" a translation of Asher), whose ketuba said Asher. According to what we learned, even the Rema would write Mas'ud first, since he is called to the Torah by it. De-itkarei and de-mitkarei The Rema then distinguishes between two Hebrew names both commonly used and where one is less common, writing de-itkarei in one case and de-mitkarei in the other. \"I can't explain this.\" The Get Pashut says the text must be reversed: mitkarei is present tense, \"is called now.\" \"Everyone throws this out, and no one does this today. We always write de-mitkarei .\" Names that are not written A shameful nickname ( kinui shel genai ) is not written in the get. A meshumad who did teshuva: his name from his Christian period is ignored, and vechol shum need not even be written for it. (This was touched on in Seifim 5-6.) A kinui never used on its own. The example given is \"Shimon Boich\", where he was called Shimon or Shimon Boich but never just Boich. Then just write Shimon. But the Pitchei Teshuva qualifies: if the kinui has its own standing as a name , write it. If he is Yehuda Leib and sometimes called just Leib, then Leib is an accepted name, and one writes Yehuda Leib de-mitkarei Leib . Boich, which is not a real name, is not written. Part 3: A Name Changed Because of Illness (Seif 18) \"This is the last hot seif of the siman.\" מי שנשתנה שמו מחמת חולי, אף על פי שקורין אותו תמיד בשם ראשון, מכל מקום שם השינוי עיקר. הגה: אפילו הוא לעז ושם ראשון עברי, וכותבין אותו קודם ואחר כך דמתקרי פלוני שם ראשון The Rabbi took \"the most radical example.\" A man was Chaim his whole life. He became ill and they gave him a new name, say Vital . Now the get says Vital de-mitkarei Chaim , even though people still use Chaim far more and only rarely Vital. Almost all the Rishonim say the new name is always written first, and so rules the Shulchan Aruch, against an opinion quoted in the Darkei Moshe to write the more prevalent name. The reasoning: the name was given at such a tumultuous point in his life that it is the name he is known by, the name people will remember. The Rema adds , and this is the halacha: even if the new name is la'az and the original Hebrew, the new name is written first. The Noda BiYehuda: here even the Rema agrees to write de-mitkarei , not ha-mechuneh . Normally for the Rema, Hebrew and la'az on different levels take ha-mechuneh . But it would be absurd to write \" ha-mechuneh Chaim\", \"nicknamed Chaim\", for the name everyone calls him every day of his life. \"As you can see, all of this is just opinions, and it's very difficult. That's why so much machloket and so many different opinions.\" The Rabbi's story About three or four years earlier, the Rabbi was called to the bedside of a man who had been in hospital for about a month. It was a Friday morning, and the doctors had given him two or three hours. Together with another senior Rav and his son, three Rabbanim in all, they decided on the spot to do a change of name : his name was Moshe , and they added Chai . They waited about two hours, it was getting close to Shabbat, and they left. After Shabbat he was still alive, and three or four weeks later he was released. Now he comes to shul every day. When he was first called up to the Torah after coming home, he was called by his old name. The Rabbi, one of the only people who knew that Chai had been added, realised what had happened. He let it go that time, then made an announcement that he must be called Moshe Chai . People still call him Moshe in conversation, but in shul, for an aliya, a mi sheberach or a prayer for healing, he is Moshe Chai. Why tell the story? Because in his get one would write Moshe Chai de-mitkarei Moshe : the name of healing comes first, even though it is used less often. A new name that was never used What if the new name was given at the bedside, only one Rav knows about it, and it was never used again? Opinion Ruling Rabbeinu Peretz Two gittin : one with the original name (Chaim), one with the new name (Vital) Another opinion (brought in the Beit Yosef) No need to write the new name at all. If it was given once and never used, ignore it The Beit Yosef, oseh shalom : perhaps there is no machloket. If the new name is not used, write only the old name, Chaim. Rabbeinu Peretz requires two gittin where the husband divorces while still ill , so we don't yet know how his name will develop. For example, a childless couple, where the wife fears yibum if he dies, adds a name and decides to do a get. We cannot write \"Vital de-mitkarei Chaim\", because maybe Vital will never be used again, so two gittin are written, one with each name. But if he divorces three years after his operation and no one has used the name since, everyone agrees: write just Chaim. How does the Shulchan Aruch rule? He writes a stam and a yesh , so he is not following the Beit Yosef's reconciliation. Pashtut , like a stam and yesh : min hadin the get with the old name suffices; to be machmir, write two gittin like Rabbeinu Peretz. That is how the Beit Shmuel reads the Shulchan Aruch. But the Beit Shmuel himself rules like the Beit Yosef's reconciliation : if the new name is completely forgotten, do not write it at all; only if the get is given right after the new name was added, while its future is unknown, must two gittin be given misafek . The Rabbi pointed out that this cuts both ways: Case Shulchan Aruch ( stam ) Beit Shmuel Name changed and get given the same day The old name suffices, since the new name is not yet established (a leniency) Two gittin New name completely forgotten A second get is only a chumra No point in a second get Called to the Torah by a different name Even when a name of illness comes first, this is only where he is not called to the Torah and does not sign by another name. If he is given an extra name for illness but is called up and signs differently, that name comes first and then the name of illness. The Rema: ve-im oleh le-sefer Torah bishnehem, kotvin ha-sheni ikar ve-ha-rishon de-mitkarei . Following the Torat Gittin, which most Acharonim and the Pitchei Teshuva follow: he was born Yitzchak and his name was changed to Chaim , and he is called up as both. Then the new name is the ikar and the old name de-mitkarei . A crucial point: changing versus adding \"I have to say something really, really important, which I should have said at the beginning of the seif.\" The Rabbi said he only appreciated this after going through it several times: \"This whole seif is talking about where you changed his name. Today we don't do that. Today we add .\" In the seif, the name has been nishtaneh : he is no longer called Yitzchak; his name is now Chaim, even if his friends still call him Yitzchak. That is why the new name comes first even though the old one is still commonly used. When a name is added , as is done today, \"that's a whole different sugya \": you simply add the name, as in the story of Moshe Chai. A student mentioned a case from his yeshiva years of a boy whose name was actually changed , because it was not a proper name (his father had named him after a moshav), and it was replaced with Avraham. That is a classic case of this seif. Asked whether such a name falls under kinui shel genai , the Rabbi said he had always seen genai in the context of avoda zara and other religions, not just an unusual or unattractive name. Further details Edim never write de-mitkarei . Witnesses sign with their main name, full stop. Several changes. If his original name was Tzvi and friends called him Hirsh, then he was renamed Aryeh and friends began calling him Leib, write each name with its kinui, newest first. \"Obviously this guy is not getting much better and they keep on changing his name.\" A change not caused by illness. For example, a woman called Esther Malka who over a long time became simply Malka. The Maharik says this seif only applies to a change because of illness, which is end-of-life and well known. The Rema holds it applies even without illness. The Get Pashut compromises: it need not be illness, but it must be a substantial reason , such as escaping debts or persecution. If people just started calling you another name, this seif does not apply. Part 4: Two Gittin Out of Doubt (Seif 19) יש מי שאומר שכשנותנין שני גיטין מספק, לא יתנם בבת אחת אלא בזה אחר זה Why not give both together? The Rishonim and Acharonim give a number of reasons: Source Reason The Rabbi's comment Mahari Weil Perhaps the husband's intention is on the invalid get. He compares it to mekadesh bemilveh (betrothing with a loan), where she is not betrothed because his mind is on the loan Difficult: in that case one is not giving two documents, it is a single act of giving Darkei Moshe A concern of breira (retroactive clarification) The Acharonim, including Rav Baruch Frankel, ask: breira is where something is clarified only afterwards. Here, whichever get has the right name works. What breira is there? A third reason The Torah says sefer , one document. Two gittin given together are not one sefer Bach A concern of chatzitza (one get interposing between her and the other) \"As you can see, all of them are interesting explanations.\" This is only the view of the Mahari Weil; the Terumat HaDeshen disagrees, but the Shulchan Aruch follows the Mahari Weil: better to give them one after the other. The Rema: do the full get ceremony with the first get, then the second. Explain to him why two gittin are being given. If he gave them one after the other, it is absolutely fine. The Noda BiYehuda's practice The Noda BiYehuda , quoted in several places, says that when he gives two gittin because of these concerns, he does not tell the husband in advance . He calls him in, they write the get, he gives it to his wife, and only then does he tell him, \"You're not finished yet, you have to do another get.\" That way none of the concerns ( breira , his intention, chatzitza ) can apply. But that only works with the husband present. With a shaliach it cannot be done, because the husband must instruct the agent about everything at once. So, he says, he does not like doing two gittin through a shaliach, and whenever a shaliach is used, everything must be clarified before both gittin. Part 5: The Remaining Seifim A ger (as discussed in the context of Seif 9): one may not write \" ben Avraham \" alone, which would be wrong. Write ben Avraham Avinu if one wants to allude to his being a ger. Gershom and Gershon are two completely different names: one is associated with gerushin and the other with gerut . If it is unknown which is his name, give two gittin. This applies only when the man has no fixed name. All the doubtful spellings in these seifim are for when we do not know what his name is. For example, Eliya (without the -hu ) appears in five places in Tanach, so it is acceptable. And the Rema's rule applies to the whole seif: if a man has signed his whole life in a particular form and is called to the Torah that way, write it that way. The doubt is only for a man known as \"Sam\" whose Hebrew form we have to work out. Matityahu and Matisyahu \"If you look in any Sephardi siddur, you will find in Al HaNissim of Chanukah: bimei Matityah ben Yochanan Kohen Gadol .\" In Ashkenazi siddurim: bimei Matisyahu . What is this based on? \"It's based on the seif here, mamash .\" The Shulchan Aruch rules Matityah : that is the common name. The Darkei Moshe and the Beit Shmuel say that Ashkenazim call him Matisyahu , and so write it that way. The Beit Shmuel concludes to give two gittin. Sephardim write Matityah . \"That's why in your siddurim you say Matisyahu, whereas we say Matityah.\" \"The rest is pshutim . Straightforward.\" Next time, be'ezrat Hashem, Siman 130. Summary of the Halachot Related names, four scenarios: a short form need not be written. A vernacular name connected to the Hebrew takes de-mitkarei (Shulchan Aruch, Maharik), or for the Rema, ha-mechuneh whenever it is la'az. In doubt whether a name is Hebrew or la'az, write de-mitkarei . Ha-mechuneh refers to the whole name (Acharonim, Tiv Gittin), not just the last word (Beit Shmuel). Family names are not written today (Rema), even if he is called and signs by his surname. The Beth Din's records serve as identification. An exact translation (Chaim, Vivant): de-mitkarei for the Shulchan Aruch, ha-mechuneh for the Rema. Hebrew or common name first? The Shulchan Aruch writes the more common name first; the Rema puts the Hebrew name first, except where the la'az name is unrelated, is used for aliya and signing, or was added for illness. Always de-mitkarei , never de-itkarei (Get Pashut). Shameful names, and kinuim never used alone, are not written. A name changed for illness is written first, even if la'az and used less, and with de-mitkarei (Noda BiYehuda). If it was never used, write the old name; if the get is given while the new name's future is unknown, two gittin (Beit Shmuel, Beit Yosef). The seif speaks of changing a name, not adding one , as is done today. Edim sign without de-mitkarei . Two gittin out of doubt are given one after the other, each with a full ceremony (Mahari Weil, Shulchan Aruch, Rema). The Noda BiYehuda did not tell the husband in advance, but with a shaliach everything must be set out beforehand. Spelling doubts need two gittin only when the man has no fixed name. Matityah for Sephardim, Matisyahu for Ashkenazim.","rwDiH6cDpzo":"Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Even HaEzer, Hilchot Gittin Siman: 129, Seifim 10-16 Shiur: A Dayanut shiur by Dayan Daniel Kada, Wednesday 19 January 2022 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Where We Are The previous shiur covered the meshumad and the ger (Seifim 5-6), titles such as Kohen and Levi (Seif 7), and what to write when there is no father's name, or the father is not Jewish (Seif 9). We saw that although min hadin the father's name does not have to be written, if it is written that is fine. This shiur opens with Seif 10: what if the father's name was written, but wrongly? The Rabbi opened with a quiz question for the class: in Tanach, what is the name of Yoav's father? Everyone answered: Yoav ben Tzeruya, so his father is Tzeruya. \"Okay, we'll come back to that later.\" Part 1: The Wrong Father's Name (Seif 10) יוסף בן שמעון ששינה וכתב יוסף בן שמואל, פסול Yosef ben Shimon who changed and wrote \"Yosef ben Shmuel\": the get is pasul. Why is it pasul if the father's name is not required? The source is the Rosh and the Terumat HaDeshen . The Rosh says it is pasul because people will think it is someone else . But did we not learn in Siman 128 that the mekom leidah (place of birth) does not have to be written, and if it was written wrongly it is still kasher? The answer: the father's name is different. It is more like the mekom dirah (place of residence). We learned in Siman 128 that changing the place of residence makes the get pasul. The father's name is just as fundamental to a person's identity. So although one does not have to write it, writing it wrongly invalidates the get. Rashi: if she remarried, she does not leave The Beit Yosef quotes the Orchot Chaim , who brings Rashi : im nis'et lo tetze , if she has already remarried on this get, she does not have to leave her second husband. Opinion Ruling Rosh, Terumat HaDeshen, Tur Pasul. Tetze : if she remarried, she must leave Rashi (via the Orchot Chaim) If she has remarried, she does not leave Shulchan Aruch Brings the Rosh as the stam (unattributed main ruling) and Rashi as yesh omrim The Rema's qualification The Rema, quoting the Orchot Chaim, limits Rashi: his leniency applies only where the man had been known ( muchzak ) by the wrong name for at least thirty days . If everyone knows him as Yosef ben Shimon, and for the last ten days he has called himself Yosef ben Shmuel, and the get says ben Shmuel, then even Rashi agrees it is pasul. The Rabbi noted that if one looks in the Beit Yosef, this does not seem to be Rashi's own explanation. Rashi says the institution of writing vechol shum was made because of people who would deliberately write the wrong name of their father, so that all names would be covered. The implication is that for Rashi, under all circumstances, a change in the father's name is kasher bediavad. But that is how the Rema understands Rashi, and that is the psak: even according to the yesh omrim , only if he was known by that name for at least thirty days. What is the halacha lema'aseh? A student suggested that the Rema rules this way. The Rabbi disagreed: \"The Rema is just qualifying the yesh . How do you see he's paskening that?\" The general rule is that where the Shulchan Aruch brings a stam and a yesh , the halacha is like the stam . The Get Pashut says that based on the Shulchan Aruch alone, the halacha is tetze . But he also quotes a teshuva of the Beit Yosef in Avkat Rochel , where he is more hesitant and suggests perhaps one can rely on Rashi. So the Get Pashut concludes : we instruct the husband according to the stam, that they should separate. But if the husband wants to stay with her, we cannot force him not to. A student pointed out that there are different levels of bediavad with a get: if it has been written but not given, you rewrite it; if it has been given, do you give it again; and the most extreme, she has already remarried. Perhaps the Shulchan Aruch only relies on the lenient view in the most extreme case. The Rabbi answered that this is not a compromise between two views: the first view says pasul in all circumstances, and Rashi says kasher once she has remarried. The language would have looked different if the Shulchan Aruch intended a compromise. Bottom line: if this happens, we advise the husband to separate, but if he wants to stay with her, we cannot really object. Part 2: Three Limitations on Seif 10 There are situations where one can be lenient: Where there is a doubt whether it is a mistake at all. The Get Pashut says we certainly do not protest in such a case. We only protest when it is clearly a mistake. The wife's father's name. The Get Pashut makes a very interesting point. The rule of tetze is about a change in the husband's father's name. If the wife's father's name was changed, perhaps bediavad one can be lenient. Why? Because once women marry, they are known by their husband's name. This was always so among the Spanish and Portuguese and older Sephardi communities, and still is: in the S&P community a mi sheberach or a memorial for a married woman says \"Sarah de Avraham\", Sarah the wife of Avraham, not her father's name. So people do not know her as Sarah bat Shimon; they know her as Sarah, wife of Avraham. It is more comparable to the mekom leidah than to the mekom dirah . A student objected: if the name written is a complete mistake, unrelated to the husband, it is still wrong. \"That's a fair point. It's still wrong.\" The Rabbi agreed it is \"obviously not an answer\" on its own, but something to combine with other factors ( litzaref ) when necessary. Only a real change. The Pitchei Teshuva brings that this applies only to a shinui mamash . If the father's name is Yitzchak Shmuel and one wrote Yitzchak Shimon, that is a real change. If one wrote only \"Yitzchak\" or only \"Shmuel\", that is just missing information, not a change, and it is fine. The Pitchei Teshuva also brings a long piece about one Acharon who was brave enough to be lenient against all the Rishonim and Acharonim, and everyone came out strongly against him. One cannot even combine his view as a snif (a supporting consideration). Part 3: Can a Son Rename His Father? Even according to Rashi, what case is meant? A man everyone knows as Yosef ben Shimon starts calling himself ben Shmuel? Or a man who arrives in a new town where no one knows him, calls himself Yosef ben Shmuel for six months, and the get is written that way, and later it emerges that in his home town he was Yosef ben Shimon? \"Essentially, the question here is: do you as a son have the ability to give yourself a new identity of your father's name? You can change your own name, but can you change your father's name?\" This is not the same as a man with two names in two places (Seif 4), because everything until now was about your own name , which you have power over. Here the father has one name in his town, and the son, perhaps because his father is a known rasha or he is embarrassed of him, calls himself by a different father's name. Opinion Ruling Terumat HaDeshen Absolutely not. \"Your father is your father is your father, full stop.\" If your father has two names, fine, but he has one, and you cannot give him a new one. It remains a change Radbaz Part of calling yourself a name is how you identify with your father. You can call yourself \"Yosef ben Shmuel\" as a second name for all intents and purposes Pitchei Teshuva Rules like the Radbaz . The whole seif applies only where everyone in this town knows the father's real name. If no one knows the old name, writing the name he has been using is fine A student mentioned someone whose middle name is \"Ben-Zion\", which is common. The Rabbi agreed that happens, but here we mean a case where the Beth Din asked \"what is your father's name?\" and he gave this name. A wrong title If the father's name was correct but a wrong title was added, for example \"Levi\" or \"Kohen\" when he is not, the Beit Meir is lenient: it is like the mekom leidah and should not be a problem. Part 4: Yoav ben Tzeruya Now the answer to the opening quiz. The Rabbi said that when he learned this Pitchei Teshuva a few years earlier, he was shocked. The Pitchei Teshuva writes in the name of the Get Pashut that if one wrote the mother's name in the get instead of the father's, it is kasher , because everyone knows it is a woman's name. This is not the case from the previous shiur, where there is no Jewish father. Here there is a Jewish father with a name, and for whatever reason the mother's name was written. But, says the Get Pashut, what about a name like Simcha , used for both men and women? If the mother's name is Simcha and he writes \"ben Simcha\", people will think it is a different man, since his father is Shimon. Then there may be a problem. The example the Get Pashut gives is Yoav ben Tzeruya . \"My whole life, Yoav ben Tzeruya, I always thought Tzeruya was his father's name. And it's not. Tzeruya is his mother's name.\" Ask anyone, the Rabbi said, and they will tell you Tzeruya is the father. A student looked it up: Divrei HaYamim lists Tzeruya as David's sister . A meshumad father The Rema discusses a man whose father is a meshumad, who is called to the Torah by his grandfather's name. People still know his father is a meshumad. The Rabbi's practical advice: where the father is a meshumad, it is better to leave his name out, rather than \"start getting into complicated scenarios\" of which name to write. Seif 10 in brief: if the father's name was wrongly written, tetze , unless the husband does not want to separate, and only where it is clearly a mistake and everyone knows his father's real name. Seif 11 was covered earlier: almost all Rishonim hold that a get without names is invalid, except the Mordechai's view. Pasul in the Shulchan Aruch most likely means batel , which is why the children are mamzerim min haTorah , against the Beit Shmuel's suggestion that it is only derabbanan . Part 5: A Third Place (Seifim 12-13) מי שיש לו שם אחד בגליל ושם אחד ביהודה, והלך למקום אחר ושם קורין אותו בשם אחר One who has one name in the Galil and one name in Yehuda, and went to another place where he is called by a different name. Until now we discussed a man with one name in one place and another name in another place, divorcing in one of them. Now he has two names, but he is in a third place , say France, where no one knows him by either of those names. Lechatchila: write the French name and the names he has in Yehuda and the Galil. Bediavad: if only the French name, the name where he is now, was written, it is fine. The order of names The Rabbi asked the class: what if one wrote \"Yehuda-name de-mitkarei Galil-name de-mitkarei French-name\", with the French name last? A student suggested a problem, since the tafel may not come before the ikar. The Rabbi corrected: we learned that one may not write tafel vechol shum , but we learned clearly from the Rivash in Seif 2 that tafel de-mitkarei ikar is fine. So it should be the same here, and the order does not matter. The Get Pashut suggests that may only be true when he is in one of those two places. Here, both names come first and he is not known by either in the third place, so perhaps it is pasul. The Mishneh LaMelech completely disagrees, and the Pitchei Teshuva rules like him: there is no difference between this and Seif 2. Lechatchila write the French name first; bediavad, if written last with de-mitkarei , it is fine. With vechol shum : Seif 13 What if one wrote the Yehuda or Galil name and then vechol shum , to include the French name? A student suggested batel . \"That's not what we learned in Seif 2... In Seif 4 we learned pasul.\" But here: כתב שם שיש לו במקום אחר, וכתב וכל שום שיש לו בשביל מקום כתיבה ונתינה, בטל If he wrote the name he has in another place and wrote \"vechol shum\" for the place of writing and giving, it is void. The student explained it well: in the earlier case there was an ikar and a tafel. Here, the name written is not a name at all in this place . \"Excellent, very good, well done.\" The Shulchan Aruch quotes the Ran , who says einah megureshet . The Maggid Mishneh says otherwise. The Beit Yosef does not want to make a machloket between the Ran and the Maggid Mishneh, so he explains the word pasul as batel , and rules it is batel. Why is this worse than Seif 4, which is only pasul derabbanan ? Because in Seif 4 at least the name he has in the place of writing was written, even as the tafel. Here no one in this place knows the name written at all, so the get is batel. Summary of Seifim 12-13: in a third place with a new name, always write that name first. If only that name was written, it is fine; lechatchila add the others. If the other names were written followed by vechol shum , the get is batel. Part 6: Real Doubts About a Name, and Changing Towns What if there is a real doubt about how to write a name? At the end of the siman we will see cases of such doubt that two gittin are given. For example, Ashkenazim give two gittin for the name Matisyahu , one spelled Matityahu and one Matisyahu . Sephardim always write Matityahu . A student described a recent case with a Sephardi first name, Salim, which became a big argument among Rabbanim over whether to spell it with an aleph . The Rabbi's view: \"We Sephardim don't add in alephs unless it's protracted.\" Ashkenazim add alephs and ayins everywhere. One argument from the other side was that for Ashkenazi readers, an aleph would make it sound like \"Solem.\" Moving to a new town to change one's name Suppose such a doubt cannot be resolved. The husband, fed up with the Rabbanim in Yerushalayim arguing over two or three gittin, moves to Bnei Brak, calls himself Shmuel , and two weeks later asks the Beth Din there for a get in the name Shmuel. Can one go to a new place where no one knows the previous name, take a new name, and divorce with it? Opinion Ruling Penei Yehoshua Hevel havalim . It is not about the place where you give the get. You will come back to your original town, they will see the get and say \"this is a ridiculous get, it's not who you are\" Tiv Gittin (brought in the Pitchei Teshuva) More doubtful; perhaps one goes after the place where the get is given Acharonim If he lives there thirty days and establishes his name, even the Penei Yehoshua should agree. A name can be established in thirty days The reverse case. The get was given, and then they realise the wife's name was written wrongly. Can she move to a new place, live there thirty days under the name in the get, and be divorced with it? Obviously not, because at the time the get was given, it was not her name. The Get Pashut suggests that perhaps, after the thirty days, the husband could make a kinyan to take the get back from her and then give it to her again, since it was written bishmah (for her). His only hesitation is that it was originally written bipsul , so the invalidity remains attached to it. He is not happy with it. Part 7: Two Names in the Same Place (Seif 14) מי שיש לו שני שמות... ראובן ושמעון... יכתוב ראובן דמתקרי שמעון. ואם כתב ראובן שמעון ביחד, יש מי שאומר שפסול One who is called Reuven by some and Shimon by others writes \"Reuven de-mitkarei Shimon.\" If he wrote \"Reuven Shimon\" together, some say it is pasul. Rema: but if he is sometimes called by both names together, or signs that way, it is kasher. A student gave a real example: an uncle named Yehuda David . His English name is David, almost everyone calls him David, some family call him Yehuda, and he is called to the Torah as Yehuda David. That is the Rema's case: writing \"Yehuda David\" would definitely be fine. But if some call him David, some call him Yehuda, and no one calls him Yehuda David, not even at the Torah, and the get says \"Yehuda David\", the Shulchan Aruch rules yesh mi she-omer shepasul . The background: Terumat HaDeshen and Maharik Opinion Ruling Reason Terumat HaDeshen Kasher At the end of the day he is known by both names Maharik Pasul No one knows him by the combined name. He is only known as Yehuda, or as David The Beit Yosef, oseh shalom bimromav . He suggests there is no machloket. The Terumat HaDeshen speaks of someone called by both names in the same place : in Perth, some family and friends call him Yehuda and others call him David, so \"Yehuda David\" is recognisable. The Maharik speaks of someone called Yehuda in Perth and David in Canberra , where each place is unlikely to know the other name, so \"Yehuda David\" is pasul. How does the Shulchan Aruch rule? The Shulchan Aruch does not distinguish. A student argued that the phrase yesh mi she-omer itself shows there is a machloket. The Rabbi disagreed: \"That's not correct. It's a well-known klal : whenever the Shulchan Aruch quotes a halacha which he finds in only one Rishon, not a din from the Gemara, not a consensus, he writes yesh mi she-omer .\" So yesh mi she-omer alone does not prove a machloket. Two readings: The Get Pashut: the Shulchan Aruch has retracted from the Beit Yosef. If he held there was no machloket, he would surely have specified that the pasul ruling is for two places. Since he does not distinguish, there is a machloket (Terumat HaDeshen: kasher in all cases; Maharik: pasul in all cases), and the Shulchan Aruch rules like the Maharik, at least lechatchila, even in the same place. The Beit Shmuel reads the Beit Yosef's reconciliation into the Shulchan Aruch: pasul only where he is known by two names in two places; in the same place, kasher. The Rabbi found this a stretch: \"You would expect the Shulchan Aruch to say something like that.\" \"If anything, the Get Pashut makes much more sense.\" The nafka mina : the Yehuda-David case, in one town, with friends calling him Yehuda and family David, never called by both together, and the get says \"Yehuda David\": pasul according to the Get Pashut, kasher according to the Beit Shmuel. Jewish and non-Jewish names The Rema continues: if Jews call him by one name and non-Jews by another, write \" Ploni de-mitkarei Ploni \". But if the non-Jewish name is simply how non-Jews pronounce the Jewish name , write just the Jewish name. The Rabbi's illustration: Chassidim may say \"Lunden\" for London; one need not write \"Lunden de-mitkarei London\", because one comes from the other. \"Bear this in mind, by the way, because now we're going to get to loads and loads of little pratim ... it's going to get very, very detailed.\" Part 8: De-mitkarei or Ha-mechuneh ? The Rema says elsewhere that whenever a non-Jewish name is written, one writes ha-mechuneh (who is nicknamed), not de-mitkarei . Sephardim do not use the word ha-mechuneh at all. The Perth students said that the Ashkenazi sofer they work with mostly writes de-mitkarei now. A sample get they had sent the Rabbi read \"Gideon de-mitkarei Justin\", though Gideon and Justin are not related at all. Yet here the Rema writes de-mitkarei for a non-Jewish name. The Acharonim \"bend over backwards\" to explain it. \"There's no rhyme or rhythm with all of this. It's very difficult. But we'll see it as we go along.\" Part 9: Shortened Names (Seif 15) What if the second name is a nickname, \"more than just a nickname, a childish way of saying your name\"? The Shulchan Aruch gives Yitzchak and its short form, and Aharon and its short form. The Beit Shmuel adds examples such as Binyamin and a short form, Avigail and Avigi, Tzipora and Pura, Shmuel and Muel. There is no need to write the shortened name. One can assume it is the same name. If someone else in town shares the name, it is worthwhile writing it. And if you want to write it, there is no problem. If the nickname has its own standing as a name, it must be written. A student's example: Daniel and Dan . Dan is a name in its own right, so one must write \"Daniel de-mitkarei Dan.\" The class debated whether Ari is a name in its own right or just short for Ariel; in English it certainly is. The Rabbi noted that in his own case, being called Danny occasionally would allow just Daniel, but if Danny is really his main name, both should be written. Part 10: Yehuda, Leib and Leon (Seif 16) \"As I told you at the beginning of the siman, none of these things are found in the Gemara; these are all the Rishonim's conjectures,\" which is why it is hard to put the seifim together. The Shulchan Aruch discusses Leon , which means lion ( aryeh ), and Yehuda is called by that name because of gur aryeh Yehuda (Bereshit 49:9). So one writes \" Yehuda de-mitkarei Leib .\" This was a very common family of names: Leon is common among the Spanish and Portuguese and in English, Ashkenazim use Leib, and Middle Eastern Jews use Aslan (also found in Arabic; many Iraqi Jews are called Aslan). The Shulchan Aruch and the Rema When to write de-mitkarei When to write ha-mechuneh Shulchan Aruch The second name derives from the first (Leib from Yehuda) The second name has nothing to do with the first. Jewish or non-Jewish does not matter Rema The second name is Jewish The second name is non-Jewish But this seems to contradict the Rema at the end of Seif 14 , where he wrote de-mitkarei for a non-Jewish name. Some say that there it is lav davka , and he really means ha-mechuneh . The Beit Shmuel answers: in Seif 14, even the \"Jewish\" name is a non-Jewish name : Jews call him Leib and non-Jews call him Leon. Both are on the same level, so one writes \"Leib de-mitkarei Leon.\" Here, the Jewish name is a real Jewish name, Yehuda, so one writes \" Yehuda ha-mechuneh Leib .\" In the Rema's thinking, ha-mechuneh marks a different language or culture. So, a student asked, Yitzchak and Isaac? According to the Rema, ha-mechuneh : it is just the English version. Next time: the rest of Seif 16 with Seifim 17 and 18, \"and the rest is literally plain sailing all the way till the end.\" Summary of the Halachot A wrong father's name (Seif 10) makes the get pasul, since the father's name is like the mekom dirah (Rosh). Rashi allows her to stay if remarried; the Rema limits this to where he was known by that name for thirty days. The halacha follows the stam : advise them to separate, but if the husband wants to stay, we cannot force him (Get Pashut). Leniencies: a doubtful mistake; perhaps an error in the wife's father's name (women being known by their husband's name); and an omission rather than a real change. A son cannot rename his father in a place where the father's real name is known (Terumat HaDeshen), but where no one knows it, the name he uses is fine (Radbaz, Pitchei Teshuva). The mother's name instead of the father's is kasher, unless it is a name used by men too (Get Pashut). Yoav ben Tzeruya is named after his mother. In a third place (Seif 12), write the local name first; bediavad that name alone is fine, and with de-mitkarei the order does not matter (Mishneh LaMelech, Pitchei Teshuva). Other names plus vechol shum for the local name (Seif 13) is batel , since the written name is not known there at all. A new town for a new name works only after thirty days of being known by it. A wrongly named get cannot be fixed by the wife adopting the name. Two names used separately (Seif 14): write de-mitkarei . Writing them together is pasul according to the Get Pashut even in one town; the Beit Shmuel limits this to two towns. A short form of a name need not be written, unless it is a name in its own right (Daniel and Dan). Yehuda and Leib (Seif 16): the Shulchan Aruch writes de-mitkarei for a derived name; the Rema distinguishes Jewish ( de-mitkarei ) from non-Jewish ( ha-mechuneh ) names.","12PExSRIqcw":"Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Even HaEzer, Hilchot Gittin Siman: 128, Seifim 4-7 (the river and the other waters of the city), with the opening of Siman 129 (Seifim 11 and 1) Shiur: A Dayanut shiur by Dayan Daniel Kada, Wednesday 17 November 2021 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Where We Are Siman 128 deals with the place ( makom ) written in the get: the town where the get is written and signed, the place of the husband and the wife, and the custom of identifying the town by the river it stands on. The previous shiur covered the place of writing and signing, and the places of the husband and wife. This shiur picks up with the river and the other waters of the town, and then, with the time that was left, opens Siman 129, the siman of the names. Part 1: Writing the River Is Only a Minhag (Seif 4) The formula in the get is that the town is \"דיתבא על נהר פלוני\" , the city that sits on such-and-such a river. The first and most important point is that this is not required by the Gemara . In the Gemara, Nehardea had rivers, and yet the get only wrote \"Nehardea\", without the river. All the Rishonim therefore agree that writing the river is not required min hadin . It became customary to do so. It is a minhag , and it is not recorded in the Rambam at all. The Rosh , the Ran and others bring the custom of writing the river. So if the river was left out entirely, the get is kasher , as the Rambam implies. The Seder HaGet goes further: even lechatchila , if the get has already been written without the river, it may be given. \"It's important to know: if you forgot the river,\" you can still give that get. The Get Pashut's two exceptions The Pitchei Teshuva brings the Get Pashut , who says there are two cases where one might, min hadin , have to write the river: When there is another city with the same name. The classic example is London : there is London in England and London in Ontario, Canada. The river then distinguishes them. When every get written in that place has always included the river. If every get in London says \"London, which sits on the river Thames,\" and suddenly a get appears that says \"London\" without the Thames, people may think it is a different London. Where writing the river has become firmly established, it has \"almost become part of the name of the city.\" The Rabbi observed that both of these apply to London , so there is room to say that in London one does, min hadin , need to write the river. Even so, the Get Pashut still says one can be lenient : if a get was given in such a place without the river, bimkom igun (where she would otherwise be left unable to remarry), she may remarry with that get. Two smaller points The river is written once. The Pitchei Teshuva brings from the Get Pashut that one does not repeat the river every time the town is mentioned. It is written at the beginning, and every later mention of the place relies on that first reference. Two rivers: \"נהר פלוני ונהר פלוני\". The Shulchan Aruch writes nahar ploni uploni , which implies writing \"the river Thames and X\" with the word \"river\" only once. All the Acharonim say one should write the word nahar before each river: nahar ploni ve-nahar ploni . Otherwise it implies one river with two names, rather than two separate rivers. One should also keep the same order consistently, and avoid changes in the seder . Part 2: Changing the Name of the River We have learned that leaving the river out is fine. What if the sofer wrote the wrong name of the river ? The Beit Yosef brings two opinions: Opinion Ruling Reasoning Maharil Kasher Since the river did not need to be written at all, an error in it does not matter Maharik Yesh poslin (some invalidate) Even though it did not need to be written, once it was written and changed, it becomes a change in the get An apparent contradiction in the Rema The Rema writes clearly that if the river was not written at all, or was changed , the get is kasher , like the Maharil. But a line later he says that if one wrote the name of a river that is outside the techum of the city, yesh poslin . \"But you just said a line ago that if you changed it, it's kasher.\" How do we reconcile this? The Beit Shmuel's answer. Simply changing the river's name is fine, as the Rema rules. Writing a river outside the techum is different. That river no longer belongs to the town at all, so it is as if one has written a different city . A mistaken name of the right river is just a mistake. A correct name of a river that has nothing to do with the town \"is even worse, because then it's implying it's a different city.\" The Rabbi's example: writing \"London, which sits on the river Bern\" gives the impression that this is not the London of England. The Get Pashut's answer (brought in the Pitchei Teshuva) is much simpler. \"Kasher\" and \"yesh poslin\" in the Rema are the same two opinions the Rema brought in Seif 2 about changing the name of the place. There the Rema rules that bimkom dechak one can be lenient. The same applies here: even a real change , even one that makes it look like a different town, is kasher bediavad and bimkom dechak , because the river did not need to be written in the first place. So: - Beit Shmuel: distinguishes between a mistake in the river's name (kasher) and naming a river outside the town (which implies a different town, and is a problem). - Get Pashut: there is a real machloket, mirroring Seif 2, and the final ruling there, kasher bimkom dechak , applies here as well. Part 3: Which Rivers Count? Outside the Techum The Rabbi mentioned that when he was preparing for his own exam on this section, he went through old Rabbanut questions, and this was one of the questions on a Rav Ir exam: if the river is outside the techum, or outside the city, do you write it? View Ruling First opinion As long as the city draws from the river (water, washing clothes and so on), you write it Rashba Only if it is within the techum . Outside the techum you do not write it. If you did write it, the get is kasher bediavad only if the river is visible from the city The Rema rules like the Rashba. In practice: Outside the techum: do not write it. If it was written, it is kasher bediavad only if it can be seen from the city. Within the techum but outside the ibbur (the built-up area) of the town: write it only if the town is supplied from it ( mistapek mimenu ): water, bathing ( rechitza ), laundry ( kibbus ). If it is within the techum but not really used, do not write it. Within the town itself, where people actually live: write it. The condition of use only applies to the middle band, in the techum but outside the ibbur. \"Adding things on makes things more confusing\" The Rabbi added a personal observation. This is another example of how halacha evolves, and how \"sometimes when you add things on, they make things more confusing.\" As he had said last time about kiddushin, \"it's better if you don't say anything.\" Those who began writing the river surely did so for the right reasons. But as time goes on we arrive at people making mistakes in rivers, or not knowing which river to write. Part 4: Practice in Perth and Elsewhere One of the students on the call, from the Beth Din in Perth, had sent the Rabbi the text used there. The Perth get writes the city as sitting on the sea-coast ( kef yama ) and on the Swan River , with the sea first and then the river. Since the sofer there does all of them in the same order, it makes no difference which comes first. No place for the wife appeared on that get. The Rabbi pointed out that this is because it was a get shlichut (sent by an agent), as discussed the previous week. City limits. The real difficulty in a place like Perth is where the city ends: Fremantle, or further south. \"How far until you're considered out of Perth?\" The Canning River. A student asked whether the Canning River should be written too. Checking on a map, they found that it is an offshoot of the Swan. That settles it: the Rema rules that when two rivers come together, you write only the main one . The Perth community, about 130 years old, has never written it. Yerushalayim. A student mentioned that for Yerushalayim, mei hashiloach (the waters of the Shiloach) is written. This comes from the Get Pashut and is not in the Shulchan Aruch or Rema. A student noted from another shiur that with smaller lakes and seas that are no longer noticeable features of the town, there is a tendency to leave them out. The Rabbi explained why this was once so important: \"in the olden days, you need it.\" A town without a river was hard to live in, so the river was central to the town's identity. Part 5: The Sea, Springs, Wells and Pools The Rabbi distinguished between kinds of water: A bor is like a cistern, collected rainwater. A be'er or ma'ayan is a spring, where water flows. The rulings: The sea ( kef yama ) is significant, so it is written alongside the river. Springs and wells ( be'erot , ma'ayanot ) : the Rema says you do write them, and the Seder HaGet says this is the minhag. But where there is a river , don't bother writing the springs and fountains: just write the river. They are offshoots, not significant enough to sit beside a river. If one did write a spring where it should not have been written, the Pitchei Teshuva brings the Get Pashut that one should not give the get with it lechatchila, but if it was given it is kasher. Cisterns and pools ( borot , agamim ) are not written at all. They change too often. Wells are dug and then filled in again, as in Parashat Toldot, read two weeks before, with all the re-digging of Yitzchak's wells. The rule for doubts When two rivers meet and one loses its name, or whenever there is any doubt whether something is an offshoot or part of the same river, the principle is: It is always better to have a doubt from leaving something out than a doubt whether you made a mistake. \"Whenever you've got a doubt, leave it out. Just write the main one.\" Part 6: Rivers That Dry Up or Freeze The Pitchei Teshuva brings the Mishpetei Shmuel : if a river sometimes dries up , go after the majority. If it has water most of the time, it has the status of a river and you write it. If most of the time it is dry, you don't. The Get Pashut qualifies this: going after the majority only applies when you write the get at a time when there is water in the river. If the river is dry for two months a year and the get is written during those two months, you do not write it. Frozen rivers Rav Moshe Feinstein discusses rivers that do not dry up but freeze in winter , like the Hudson River in New York, which can freeze over for months. A frozen river loses the name nahar only when people actually walk across it and use it as a road. If, even when frozen, people still cross by bridges and do not go over the ice itself, it keeps the name nahar and is written. He adds that even though today no one draws water or washes clothes in the Hudson, it is still written, because \"it's still a kind of ID.\" The Rabbi pointed out how the river's role has changed: from the main resource of the town, where people got their water and their needs, to a kind of identifying mark. A student asked a sharp question: if the river is now only an ID, why should it matter whether it is dry or frozen? It is still called the Hudson River. \"That's a good question.\" The Rabbi's response was that if people were walking over it for three or four months a year, it is not clear it would still carry the name \"river\" in that period. He left it to check whether Rav Moshe addresses the point. Part 7: A Minhag That Went Too Far The Rabbi ended Siman 128 with what he called \"a huge chumra\" recorded in the Darkei Moshe : a minhag that a get should only be written in a place with two rivers . The Noda BiYehuda takes this on and says you do not need two rivers: a nahar and a ma'ayan satisfy the minhag. But everyone rejects it. The Beit Meir says he saw many gittin written in cities with only one river, and if one always had to travel to a city with two rivers, it would be ridiculous. We do not hold of this at all . It is interesting, he said, that the Darkei Moshe recorded such a minhag at all, \"which shows you to what extent these things can end up going.\" Summary of Siman 128 as the Rabbi gave it The siman covered the place of signing by the edim, the husband and the wife; then the river; changes in the river; and the other waters that supply the city. In short: write the sea, write wells and springs (unless there is a river), but not cisterns and pools , because they are too transient. Part 8: Opening Siman 129, the Siman of Names Asked about Siman 129, the Rabbi warned the class about what makes it hard. \"The problem with 129 is that there's so little Gemara on it. And when you don't have a lot of Gemara, you end up with lots of speculations from Rishonim.\" There are essentially two or three Gemarot for a siman of some thirty seifim. Towards the end it is all small seifim with very specific scenarios. The siman is so intertwined that it needs to be learned once through quickly and then gone over again . There are endless permutations and variables: What is the main name and what is the secondary ( tafel ) name? He is called up to the Torah by one name, his friends call him another, he signs a third. His name was changed because of illness. He has an English or secular name. So the key is to get the klalim (principles), mainly in Seifim 1 and 2, and not get bogged down in the details. Part 9: A Get With No Names (Seif 11) Before Seif 1, the Rabbi jumped ahead to Seif 11, \"because it's a good introduction to the siman\" and shows how central the names are. The Shulchan Aruch rules that a get without the names of the husband and wife is \"פסול והבנים ממזרים\" , invalid, and children from a subsequent marriage are mamzerim. The wording is strange: pasul usually means invalid derabbanan , while mamzerim implies the get is void min haTorah . Most likely, the Rabbi said, \"pasul\" here is not precise and means batel , void. The source The Gemara in Gittin tells of a man who handed his wife a Sefer Torah in front of edei mesirah (witnesses to the delivery) and said, \"Here is your get,\" on the basis that everything is written in the Torah. The Gemara rejects this. All the Rishonim learn from here that the get must contain his name and her name ( shemo u-shemah ), or it is not a get. Why are the names essential? Rishon Reason Ramban The Torah calls the get a sefer keritut , which implies a sipur devarim , a narrative. \"If you have a story without the name of Harry Potter, it's not going to be a story of Harry Potter.\" Without the names of the man and the woman there is no story, so there is no sefer Ohr Zarua Without the names, the get cannot be lishmah (written for this man and this woman). Even if the sofer had them in mind, if he did not write them it is not lishmah The Ramban also brings a view that the invalidity is only derabbanan , and rejects it. Rabbeinu Yoel: a get without names is kasher The Mordechai brings Rabbeinu Yoel , who holds that a get without names is kasher . How? Because, as we rule, edei mesirah karti (the get takes effect through the witnesses to its delivery), and those two edim see the man and see the woman. That identifies them. \"As you clearly see, everyone disagreed.\" The Beit Shmuel suggests that the Shulchan Aruch wrote pasul because he was concerned for the view that it is only derabbanan, and that banim mamzerim means mamzerim derabbanan. The Rabbi called this \"a huge dochak \" (strained reading). The Gra completely rejects Rabbeinu Yoel: the get is void, and all his proofs are \"nothing.\" The Pitchei Teshuva brings the Get Pashut , who says that since Rabbeinu Yoel exists, she is a safek megureshet (doubtfully divorced). The Gra is not interested, but the Pitchei Teshuva seems to leave it there. The point for the whole siman: according to all the Rishonim, the names are essential, because without them you lose either the sipur devarim or the lishmah . Part 10: \"וכל שום שיש לו\" (Seif 1) \"This is really the core of the siman.\" The Shulchan Aruch writes that one writes the man's name and the woman's name, and the Gemara (Gittin 34b) says that Rabban Gamliel instituted writing \"ploni ve-chol shum she-yesh lo\" , \"so-and-so, and any name he has.\" What does that mean? Shita Meaning of vechol shum she-yesh lo Bahag, Rambam Literally write the words. Write the main name and then \" vechol shum she-yesh lo \", which covers every other name Rabbeinu Tam It does not mean writing those words. It means spelling out each name explicitly . His proof: later in the same Gemara, when a get is sent between two places where he has a different name in each, the names must be written out Ra'avad, and others A middle view: if the get is being sent from one place to another, there is more chance of confusion, so write the names explicitly. In the same place, vechol shum suffices The Rabbi's own example: his formal name is Daniel . His passport says Daniel, he is called up to the Torah as Daniel, but most people know him as Danny . According to the Bahag and Rambam one writes \"Daniel vechol shum she-yesh lo \", which includes Danny and anything else. According to Rabbeinu Tam one writes \"Daniel de-mitkarei Danny\" (Daniel who is called Danny). The Beit Yosef and the Shulchan Aruch After bringing all of this, the Beit Yosef says it is good to write both : the de-mitkarei and vechol shum . \"What's there to lose? You've got the best of both worlds.\" Yet in Seif 1 the Shulchan Aruch writes only vechol shum , and never mentions de-mitkarei , while throughout the rest of the siman he does write de-mitkarei . The Rabbi said he never found a real answer. His suggestion: in Seif 1, at the head of the siman, the Shulchan Aruch states the essential law , like the Rambam and Bahag, that vechol shum alone suffices. Elsewhere he reflects the advice of the Beit Yosef to write the de-mitkarei as well. The Rema and the minhag today The Rema follows Rabbeinu Tam outright: there is no need to write vechol shum ; if he has two names, write \" ploni de-mitkarei ploni \", \"and so is the custom.\" Community Practice Sephardim everywhere Both: de-mitkarei and vechol shum Ashkenazim in Israel Both, because of the Beit Yosef. Gittin in Israel are uniform in this Ashkenazim in chutz la'aretz Only de-mitkarei , without vechol shum . The Perth students confirmed this is what they do Is one of them enough bediavad? A student suggested that strictly speaking, either one alone should be good for everyone. The Rabbi pointed to the Taz : The other Rishonim would agree that if one wrote de-mitkarei without vechol shum , it is fine. But Rabbeinu Tam is the more insistent one . If one wrote only vechol shum , then according to the Rema, if another get can easily be written, one should write another rather than rely on it. Therefore, says the Taz, if a get is written in one place (which follows the Shulchan Aruch) and sent to another (which follows the Rema), always do like the Rema and write the de-mitkarei . In practice the question rarely arises today, because even Sephardim always write the de-mitkarei . But if it were missing, vechol shum alone would be fine for Sephardim. A short later seif in the siman, which gives the standard text written in the get, says the same thing again: the Shulchan Aruch writes just vechol shum , and the Rema repeats the same machloket. Part 11: Ikar and Tafel, and Must Both Be Written? The next major idea is the main name ( ikar ) and the secondary name ( tafel ). Everyone agrees that the ikar comes first. \"Like in Hilchot Berachot, we all agree you make the beracha on the ikar. The question is, what's the ikar and what's the tafel?\" Who defines the ikar? Back to Daniel and Danny. A student said the point is to identify the person to most people, so it is the name he is most commonly known by. But what does \"commonly known\" mean? Many would argue that how you are called up to the Torah is how you are publicly known, since the shul is the most public place. So too the passport and how one signs. Others would say it is the name people actually use when they speak about you, which for the Rabbi is Danny. A student recalled a get done in Perth for a woman who attended a Reform synagogue where she was called up to the Torah by a particular name. The Dayan included that name because people knew her by it. \"Very good, I like that.\" The Rabbi asked the class to keep this question in mind throughout: whenever the siman uses ikar and tafel , who actually defines what the ikar is? Do you have to write the tafel at all? Shita Ruling Tosafot (Gittin 34b) Lechatchila write both. Bediavad one name is fine if the divorce is in the same town . If the get is sent to another place, min hadin both names are needed, and if only one was written, a new get must be written Rashba Even one name suffices , ikar or tafel, either is acceptable Rambam Does not distinguish between one place and two. Always write both names The Rashba's proof is from the Mishna on hanicha (where the get is placed) and how a person is divorced. The Gemara says write \" Miriam de-mitkarya Sarah \" and not the reverse. Is that not a proof that both names are needed? The Rashba answers: no, it only says that if you write both, don't put them the wrong way round, with the tafel first. \"I'm telling you, it's really important to get these shitot clear at the beginning, because they'll resurface all the time.\" The ikar of the halacha is like the majority of Rishonim: lechatchila always write both names , either through vechol shum or through de-mitkarei . Only the Rashba would allow writing a single name without anything else. The Rabbi had to leave early. He said this is \"a really important seif,\" and that next session should focus on reviewing it, since everything that follows ties in with it. Summary of the Halachot Writing the river is only a minhag. Without it the get is kasher, and per the Seder HaGet may even be given lechatchila. The Get Pashut suggests it may be needed where another city has the same name, or where every previous get included it (both true of London), but even then one is lenient bimkom igun . Write the river once , and with two rivers write nahar ploni ve-nahar ploni . A wrong river name is kasher (Maharil, Rema). Writing a river outside the techum is a problem: for the Beit Shmuel because it implies a different city; for the Get Pashut it is the same machloket as Seif 2, kasher bimkom dechak . Outside the techum , don't write the river (Rashba, Rema); bediavad kasher only if visible from the city. Within the techum but outside the town, write it only if the town uses it. Where rivers merge, write only the main one. Write the sea; write springs and wells unless there is a river; never write cisterns and pools. When in doubt, leave it out. A river that dries up is written if it usually has water and has water when the get is written. A frozen river keeps its name unless people walk across it (Rav Moshe Feinstein). The Darkei Moshe's \"two rivers\" minhag is not followed. A get with no names is invalid and children of a later marriage are mamzerim (Ramban: no sipur ; Ohr Zarua: no lishmah ). Rabbeinu Yoel's lone view leads the Get Pashut to treat her as a safek megureshet . \"Vechol shum she-yesh lo\": Rambam and Bahag write the words; Rabbeinu Tam spells out each name. Sephardim and Israeli gittin write both de-mitkarei and vechol shum ; Ashkenazim in chutz la'aretz write only de-mitkarei . Between communities, follow the Rema (Taz). Ikar and tafel: lechatchila always write both names, ikar first. Keep asking who defines the ikar.","82JqIgutxow":"Series: Prayer Series (Tiferet Eyal, 2021-22) Shiur: Part 6 of the six-part Prayer Series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom for Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 18 January 2022 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap and Plan The final shiur of the series. So far: the history and theology of prayer; the Siddur from the beginning; the Shema with creation, revelation and redemption; the Amidah and the ways of repeating it; and last week, the Torah reading: \"how we talk to God through prayer, and God talks to us in Torah reading,\" its origins with Moshe and Ezra, Monday and Thursday, the annual and triennial cycles, aliyot, Kohen and Levi, and women being called up. Tonight: a few remaining points on the Torah and Haftarah , then Musaf and sacrifices in messianic times , ending with Aleinu LeShabeach , \"a very enigmatic tefillah\" that we say three times a day. Part 1: The Mashlim Kaddish Is Not for Mourners The Rabbi wanted to correct a widespread idea. Many people think that if they have a yahrzeit ( nahala ) they must be called up for the seventh aliyah, the mashlim , and say the Kaddish after it, \"and if not, then trouble will break loose.\" Mashlim simply means \"completing\": it completes the seven aliyot. The Kaddish after it is there to make a break between the Torah reading and the Haftarah , the reading of the Prophets. The idea that this Kaddish elevates the soul of the deceased developed only about a hundred years ago. \"It's got nothing to do with that in the slightest.\" Nowhere in our liturgy is a half Kaddish designated for mourners. Mourners' Kaddish is always Kaddish Yehe Shelama or Kaddish Al Yisrael. So the idea that a mourner must be called seventh is \"nonsensical, to be honest.\" If someone with a yahrzeit is called up and says it, lovely, but there is no obligation. The reverse is also untrue: some people called up seventh, whose parents are alive, refuse to say the Kaddish for fear it will cause their parents to die. \"That's also nonsense.\" Anyone may say it. Part 2: Children Reading the Torah The Shulchan Aruch rules clearly that a child may be called up and read his own portion , as we saw last week alongside the halacha about women. \"This is absolutely black and white.\" The child must be of the age of chinuch , about six or seven. In Yemenite communities across the world this is common practice. A child reading more than his own aliyah , for example the whole parashah, should be only in extenuating circumstances, when no one else can read, or for educational purposes. Part 3: The Haftarah What the word means הפטרה comes from the root פ־ט־ר , to dismiss or depart. We now depart from the Torah and begin something of lesser importance, the Prophets. Why was it instituted? The Mishnah and Gemara in the fourth chapter of Megillah (which Daf Yomi learners had just studied) discuss the Haftarah at length, but nowhere give a reason for adding a reading from the Prophets after the parashah. The Rishonim offer three explanations: Source Reason Abudarham (fourteenth-century Spain) Antiochus , of Hanukkah infamy, forbade public reading of the Torah, but apparently not the Prophets. So we read passages from the Prophets on the theme of the parashah, and kept the custom even after the Torah reading resumed Shibbolei HaLeket (Rabbi Tzidkiyah ben Avraham, thirteenth-century Rome) Originally people stayed after prayers each day to study Torah and Prophets. When they no longer had the time, this was cut down to a few fixed verses from the Prophets, Uva LeTzion , after the Amidah. To compensate for the lost weekday study, the Prophets are read on Shabbat Another explanation in the Rishonim To counter those, such as the Samaritans , who held that the Torah is only the five books. Reading the Prophets after the Torah shows they are an essential part of our Torah The Rabbi found the first \"very difficult to accept\": why would the Greeks object to the Torah but not the Prophets? And the Hanukkah story is early second century BCE. Still, it is one theory. The Rabbi's own suggestion \"On a personal level, I would suggest a very simple idea.\" The Prophets are rich in musar , rebuke and chastisement. The Torah is Hashem speaking through Moshe; a prophet's words are Hashem's message, but \"with their own touch.\" Yeshayahu, Yirmiyahu and Yechezkel each have their own style and tone, and reading them can arouse a person. Shabbat, after the Torah reading, was a good time to hear some musar. \"Basically this was the Rabbi's sermon in those days, before the concept of the Rabbi's sermon came along... To be honest, if you read the Nevi'im, that's sometimes more powerful than any sermon, if you read it properly.\" Part 4: Mi Sheberach and the Prayer for the Government Mother's name or father's name? People often ask whether to pray for someone with the father's or the mother's name. \"It's very simple.\" The Gemara in Shabbat (66b) , which the Rabbi showed on screen, records Amoraim praying with the formula פלוני בר פלונית , \"so-and-so son of Mrs so-and-so.\" When praying for someone, use the mother's name. Why? Flora: רחמים , mercy, shares a root with רחם , the womb. \"Lovely, that's a very nice idea.\" The Rishonim , on a simpler, technical level: you are more certain who a person's mother is than his father. The mother is seen giving birth; the father is normally, \"but not always,\" her husband. It is the more direct biological link. The Ben Ish Chai , in his responsa Torah Lishmah : men have more sins than women. Women go through the difficulties of childbirth, which is their tikkun , and men, with a greater obligation to study Torah, end up wasting more time from Torah. So women find greater favour in Hashem's eyes, and when asking for mercy it is better to attribute a person to his mother. The exception is an aliyah to the Torah , where we use the father's name, since aliyot today are for men. Hacham Ovadia Yosef has a responsum: it makes no real difference; the mother's name is better, but those with an established custom to use the father's name (for example in memorial prayers) may keep it. Some in the Indian community use the mother's name for the first thirty days and the father's afterwards, or vice versa. \"If you have an established minhag, stick to the minhag. But otherwise, by default, you always use the mother's name.\" The prayer for the Royal Family Many communities, including the Spanish and Portuguese, say a prayer for the Royal Family. It rests on the Prophets and the Mishnah: Yirmiyahu tells the exiles: settle down, seek the peace of the city where I have exiled you, and pray for it , for in its peace you will have peace. Pirkei Avot: pray for the welfare of the government , for without it people would not behave well. One could argue that, since the monarchy has little real authority today, it would make more sense to pray for the Prime Minister, \"which he probably needs lots of our prayers at the moment,\" and the government. But the prayer was instituted when the monarch was fully sovereign, and we keep it. \"I think it's a valuable prayer,\" going all the way back to Yirmiyahu. Part 5: Musaf An additional prayer for an additional offering Musaf dates from the time of Ezra , about 2,500 years ago. On Shabbat, festivals and Rosh Chodesh an extra offering, the musaf (\"addition\"), was brought beyond the daily morning and afternoon sacrifices. As explained in the first shiur, Shacharit and Mincha correspond to the daily morning and afternoon offerings , brought 365 days a year. Arvit has no sacrificial counterpart , which is why it is of lower importance than Shacharit and Mincha. So although Arvit is more frequent, Musaf ranks above Arvit , because it is more directly linked to the Temple offerings. Korban: coming close קרבן comes from קרוב , to draw near. A sacrifice was not just slaughtering an animal but surrendering something of ourselves to the One greater than us. As with prayer (Part 1), it is not asking Hashem to do our will; it is pledging ourselves to do His. \"The actual sacrificial ritual in itself is worthless.\" That is why the Prophets castigate people who brought sacrifices without their hearts: Amos, chapter 5: \"I hate, I reject your festivals... your burnt offerings... your peace offerings I will not look at. Take away the noise of your songs... But let justice roll down like water, and righteousness like a mighty stream .\" Yeshayahu, chapter 1 (read on the Shabbat before Tisha B'Av): \"Who asked you to trample My courts?... Bring no more vain offerings... Your new moons and your festivals My soul hates... Wash yourselves, make yourselves clean , remove your evil deeds... uphold the orphan, plead for the widow.\" \"The truth is, prayer today is the same idea. We pray to Hashem, and then we go out and commit wrongdoings to our fellow friends, and we think if we pray to HaKadosh Baruch Hu we'll be fine... When something becomes too ritualistic, then we lose the focus.\" Part 6: Will Animal Sacrifices Return? The core of Musaf is the prayer for the Temple to be rebuilt and the offerings restored . We describe what was offered on Shabbat or Rosh Chodesh and ask that it be renewed. So when the Third Temple is built, speedily in our days, will we go back to animal sacrifices? The mainstream view The majority of Orthodox Jews will say yes: we await the rebuilding of the Temple and the restoration of animal sacrifices. What about animal welfare? The typical answer: if we kill animals to eat meat for physical pleasure, surely we may for sacrificial purposes. We care for animals, but they are secondary to human beings, and if a sacrifice furthers human worship of the Divine it is justified. \"And I must say, that's the mainstream opinion.\" The Rambam: a concession But there are other voices, most prominently the Rambam in the Moreh Nevuchim (3:32) , which the Rabbi quoted at length because it is a general principle the Rambam returns to repeatedly. \"Many precepts in our law are the result of a similar course... It is impossible to go suddenly from one extreme to the other. \" (As we learned last year in Hilchot Deot: you cannot change your character overnight.) It is human nature that a person cannot suddenly abandon everything he is used to. In those days, the universal mode of worship was sacrificing animals in temples, bowing to images and burning incense to idols. \"It was in accordance with the wisdom and plan of God, as displayed in the whole creation, that He did not command us to give up and discontinue all these manners of service ,\" because that would go against human nature. It would have been like a prophet today telling us in Hashem's name not to pray, not to fast, not to seek His help in times of trouble. Imagine a verified prophet telling us: stop praying, don't fast on Yom Kippur. We would say it's impossible; we're too used to it. So too, Hashem saw it would be impossible to tell Bnei Yisrael simply to stop sacrificing. Instead: \"To blot out idolatry, I'm telling you, you can't serve the idols. But continue bringing sacrifices, as many as you want, but bring them just to Me.\" It is \"a kind of concession.\" The Ramban famously took the Rambam to task: you are effectively saying the whole of Sefer Vayikra is a concession! For the Ramban, sacrifices are Hashem's will and will always exist. But using the Rambam one could argue that by the time of the Third Temple, having been \"weaned\" off sacrifices for two thousand years, we will no longer need them and will serve Hashem without them. The difficulty is that we mention the sacrifices in our prayers every Shabbat. Rav Kook: a minchah offering Rav Kook , the first Ashkenazi Chief Rabbi of then-Palestine (died 1935), pointed to the verse in Malachi, chapter 3 , about the messianic era: וערבה לה׳ מנחת יהודה וירושלים , \"then the offering of Yehudah and Yerushalayim will be pleasing to Hashem.\" On a simple level minchah means a gift or offering in general. Rav Kook took it literally: in Parashat Vayikra a minchah is specifically a flour offering . In messianic times, all the sacrifices will be replaced by menachot , flour offerings, without killing animals. So wherever our prayers mention sacrifices, that is how it was historically; in the time of Mashiach they will be flour offerings. This must be understood in the context of Rav Kook's philosophy (the Rabbi has written about this). He held that vegetarianism was the human ideal, aspirationally , but not for now: if we don't take our pleasure from animals, we will end up harming human beings. \"It's well known, by the way, the various prominent Nazis were vegetarians.\" So the references to animal sacrifice in the prayer book are either flour offerings or belong to the very beginning of the messianic era, before the vegetarian ideal can be realised. Others say similar things: Rabbi Chaim Hirschensohn , a nineteenth-century posek, in Malki BaKodesh , writes that the system will undergo significant changes, with a different concept of sacrifices in messianic times. Wait and see \"We can't deny that that is the mainstream opinion. But there are other voices, such as the Rambam, such as Rav Kook, such as others, who understand that it's not as simple as you think it is. There will be a temple, there will be a form of sacrifices, but it won't be the animal kind of sacrifices.\" The most important thing is not the sacrifices themselves but the kavanat halev that comes with them. \"I think we'll just wait and see.\" We pray for the Beit HaMikdash, and to restore in some way the concept of offerings: ways of coming close to Hashem by sacrificing our money and material possessions to Him. Question: Shacharit, Mincha and Arvit are linked to the Avot. Is there one for Musaf? \"No, definitely not.\" The Gemara in Berachot speaks of the Avot only in connection with Shacharit, Mincha and Arvit. \"Although it would be nice, Musaf and Yosef, I'm sure you can find some kind of connection there.\" Part 7: Aleinu LeShabeach \"I think it's one of the most misunderstood parts of our liturgy.\" In almost every siddur, Aleinu and its second paragraph, על כן נקוה לך , close Shacharit, Mincha and Arvit. Spanish and Portuguese siddurim do not include Al Ken Nekaveh . Originally only on Rosh Hashanah People think the Malchuyot section of Musaf of Rosh Hashanah borrowed Aleinu from the daily prayers. \"Nothing could be further from the truth.\" Aleinu started only in Musaf of Rosh Hashanah , said once or twice a year. The first formal siddur, Rav Amram Gaon's (ninth century), has no Aleinu at the end of weekday or Shabbat services. How it reached the daily prayers The first mention of Aleinu at the end of Shacharit is in the Machzor Vitry by Rabbeinu Simcha , a student of Rashi. He says to say it silently , without giving a reason. Read the English: it speaks sharply of blotting out idolatry and that there is only one God, with some anti-Christian references. Living in France surrounded by Christians, it makes sense to say it quietly, \"because it's not the kindest of prayers to other religions.\" Why add it? It has been suggested that in 1171 , in Blois in France, Jewish men and women were burnt at the stake for refusing to accept Christianity, and the legend has it they died with Aleinu LeShabeach on their lips . Christian Europe added it to the end of Shacharit in their memory. Only later, around the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, was it added to Mincha and Arvit . The reason: the Gemara in Berachot says that after the Amidah you should not just run off, but take a moment before departing from Hashem. The Rambam codifies sitting for a moment to contemplate before going to work (he does not mention Aleinu at all). Aleinu became a way to bring you back down to this world after your encounter with the Divine. \"The problem is today, that's not the case. Aleinu LeShabeach is what you say whilst you're folding up your tallit, or whilst you're walking on your way out. And it's just a shame, because Aleinu LeShabeach is such a beautiful prayer.\" Because we say it so often, it even loses its impact on Rosh Hashanah. Who wrote it? Flora had learned that it was Yehoshua , and mentioned the custom of spitting at a certain line, which she had seen in Gibraltar. The Rishonim give three opinions: Author Source The Rabbi's comment Yehoshua , when uprooting the idolatrous Canaanites on entering the land: שלא שמנו כמשפחות האדמה First suggested by Rabbi Yehudah HaChasid (Germany, twelfth century) Difficult: the prayer calls Hashem הקדוש ברוך הוא , a Talmudic phrase found nowhere in Tanach Rav (third century), as part of the Rosh Hashanah prayers he composed Abudarham \"Makes a lot of sense.\" It is post-Christianity: the \"families of the earth\" who worship הבל וריק , vanity and emptiness. And לראות מהרה בתפארת עזך , \"to see speedily the splendour of Your strength,\" is to the Rabbi clearly a reference to the Mikdash, destroyed a century or two earlier The Men of the Great Assembly , about 2,500 years ago, with the general prayers Another opinion Why the Spanish and Portuguese say only half Saying Aleinu after the prayers is late and not an obligation . The Arizal insisted especially on the second paragraph . Since the Spanish and Portuguese reject anything kabbalistically inclined, they say only the first. People ask how one can say only half of Aleinu, but historically saying both paragraphs was not common practice until around the sixteenth century or later. \"So it makes sense, the minhag to only say the first paragraph.\" The Takeaway From the Whole Series \"The main takeaway which I would like everyone to take away from this whole series, from all the history which we've seen, is how fluid our liturgy and tefillah is.\" Today the liturgy feels set in stone, and any suggested change meets harsh opposition. But over three thousand years it has evolved dramatically. Tefillah is the Amidah: five to ten minutes, three times a day. \"That's the core of tefillah. Everything else is all a nice way to envelop the tefillah.\" As we said in the first shiur, HaKadosh Baruch Hu wants the heart. It doesn't matter how much or how little you say; what matters is the kavanah. \"If a person honestly and genuinely believes that by saying less he will have more kavanah, then I have no question at all that's the right thing to do. Better to say less with kavanah, which is the Amidah, rather than pray the entire Siddur, which has been added on each generation, and just race through it.\" Question: is the minimum just the Amidah? No. The core of tefillah is the Amidah and the Shema with its blessings , at Shacharit and at Arvit too. What the Rabbi meant is Pesukei DeZimrah and Korbanot: \"If you have a choice to pray for fifteen minutes with kavanah, choose Amidah and Birkot Kriat Shema rather than praying in fifteen minutes from Pesukei DeZimrah, from Korbanot, and mumbling all from beginning to the end.\" Asked whether this differs for men and women: \"No difference at all.\" Pesukei DeZimrah is a warm-up; as the Rambam writes, you say some zemirot , and you choose which. Summary The mashlim Kaddish separates Torah from Haftarah. It is not for mourners or yahrzeits, and people with living parents may say it. Children of chinuch age (six or seven) may be called up and read their own aliyah (Shulchan Aruch); reading more only when needed or for education. Haftarah (from patar , to depart): Antiochus (Abudarham, difficult), compensation for lost weekday study (Shibbolei HaLeket), or against those who denied the Prophets. The Rabbi: it was the sermon before sermons. Mi Sheberach: use the mother's name (Gemara Shabbat 66b), except for an aliyah or an established custom (Hacham Ovadia; Ben Ish Chai). The prayer for the government goes back to Yirmiyahu and Pirkei Avot. Musaf corresponds to the additional offering and so outranks Arvit. Korban means coming close; the Prophets (Amos 5, Yeshayahu 1) rejected sacrifice without the heart. Sacrifices in the Third Temple: the mainstream view expects animal sacrifices; the Rambam (Moreh 3:32) saw them as a concession to human nature (opposed by the Ramban ); Rav Kook (Malachi 3: minchah ) expected flour offerings; also Malki BaKodesh . \"We'll wait and see.\" No Avot for Musaf. Aleinu began in Rosh Hashanah Musaf; reached Shacharit in Christian France (Machzor Vitry, said silently; Blois 1171) and Mincha/Arvit by the fourteenth or fifteenth century. Authorship: Yehoshua, Rav (most plausible), or the Men of the Great Assembly. The Spanish and Portuguese omit Al Ken Nekaveh , which the Arizal promoted. The series in one line: the liturgy is fluid; the core is the Amidah and the Shema with its blessings; better less with kavanah than everything in a rush. Coming Up After a week's break, a new series from Tuesday 1 February : dikduk (Hebrew grammar), and Makamim , Middle Eastern hazanut, together with the world-class Hazan David Raymond .","SIRfLvEtCWs":"Series: Prayer Series (Tiferet Eyal, 2021-22) Shiur: Part 5 of the six-part Prayer Series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom for Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 11 January 2022 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap and Plan Before the break the series covered the history and theology of prayer (Part 1), the Siddur from Modeh Ani to Yishtabach (Part 2), the Shema and its blessings (Part 3), and the Amidah and the customs of its repetition (Part 4). Recordings are available on request. Tonight: the Torah within our tefillah , its origins, the halachot and minhagim of the reading, with a focus on women and children being called up, and a closing thought for Tu BiShvat . Part 1: Reading the Torah in Public The Torah is central to our faith because it is our constitution . It is the covenant between Hashem and Israel and records the duties and obligations of that covenant. Where does the Torah itself speak of reading the whole Torah publicly? Hakhel (as Flora answered). In Parashat Vayelech , before he dies, Moshe instructs that every seven years the Torah be read before the whole people, men, women and children . Hence the name: to gather everyone. Ezra. In the fifth century BCE, when Ezra led the exiles back from Bavel to Yerushalayim, he organised a public reading of the Torah on Rosh Hashanah. The people had completely forgotten the Torah in Bavel. They had married out; they didn't know what Sukkot was. Ezra read the Torah to them to bring them back to practice. So public reading goes back as far as Moshe, and runs through our history. Part 2: Shabbat, Monday and Thursday An institution of Moshe The Rambam (Hilchot Tefillah, chapter 12) writes: משה רבנו תקן להם לישראל that they should read the Torah publicly on Shabbat, Monday and Thursday mornings, so that they should not go three days without hearing Torah . And Ezra instituted reading at Mincha on Shabbat as well. The source is the Gemara in Bava Kama , which derives it from a verse in that week's parashah (Beshalach). After the splitting of the Red Sea, Israel went three days in the wilderness and found no water . The Gemara explains: they went three days without Torah, and so they began to complain. Moshe therefore instituted that they should never go three days without Torah. Read on Monday, Thursday and Shabbat, and three days never pass. Some poskim hold that reading the Torah is a biblical mitzvah. The Rabbi did not think the Rambam held that; he saw it as an institution from the time of Moshe, but not necessarily a biblical obligation. The Gemara then asks: if Moshe instituted it, how can the Mishnah attribute the reading to Ezra, a thousand years later? It answers that Ezra elaborated and \"added some flesh\" to Moshe's original institution. Why Monday and Thursday? If the point is not to go three days without Torah, why not Shabbat, Tuesday, Thursday? Or Shabbat, Sunday, Wednesday? Rashi explains, based on the Midrash: after the sin of the Golden Calf, it was on a Thursday that Hashem told Moshe to ascend Sinai to receive the second tablets, and forty days later, on a Monday , he came down. Those were days of atonement and forgiveness , so they are auspicious, and the Hachamim chose them. Shabbat Mincha: Ezra's addition The Gemara gives Ezra's reason for the Shabbat afternoon reading in two words: משום יושבי קרנות , \"because of those who sit in the corners.\" What does that mean? Interpretation Who are the yoshvei keranot ? Why read on Shabbat afternoon? Rashi People busy with work all week who don't come to shul on Monday and Thursday To compensate them with an extra reading. So all of us, even if we heard the Torah on Monday and Thursday, listen again on Shabbat afternoon because of those who don't come during the week The Meiri (on Bava Kama) People who, on Shabbat afternoon, with no work, just eat, drink and idle the afternoon away To add some Torah to the Shabbat afternoon, so people use their time wisely The commentators ask on Rashi: if the point is not to go three days without Torah, how does a Shabbat afternoon reading help someone who still goes all week without? The Rabbi found the Meiri \"makes much more sense.\" Remember that in those days the reading was not \"just a procession: we take it out, we read it, we put it back away.\" There was a targum and an explanation. \"It was like a shiur almost.\" Part 3: From Reading to a Yearly Cycle Moshe and Ezra instituted that the Torah be read, but set no quota or specific portion. About 2,000 years ago, at the beginning of the period of the Mishnah, two customs developed: Place Custom Bavel (the main centre) Complete the Torah every year Eretz Yisrael Complete the Torah every three years . This was certainly the common minhag there until at least the sixth or seventh century, and there is evidence that in Egypt it was still done as late as the twelfth century , about 900 years ago By the thirteenth or fourteenth century, every community had adopted the annual cycle. Parashah or sidra? The weekly divisions, Bereshit, Noach, Lech Lecha, are relatively new. In the Torah itself a parashah is really a new paragraph in the text. The Ashkenazi term sidra , the organised order for completing the Torah, is \"a bit more precise,\" but Sephardim call it the parashah. Simchat Torah: \"the most misunderstood festival\" \"There is no such festival called Simchat Torah, just so you should know... There is no festival anywhere in Torah, Mishnah, Gemara called Simchat Torah.\" What we call Simchat Torah is really the second day of Shemini Atzeret , just as every festival in the diaspora has a second day. Once communities everywhere were completing the Torah at this time of year, the second day of Shemini Atzeret, \"which is anyway quite boring,\" with no sukkah or matzah of its own, became a celebration of completing the Torah. The Hatan Torah and Hatan Bereshit are fourteenth- and fifteenth-century developments. A thousand years ago there was no Simchat Torah; it is an innovation of the time of the Rishonim. Out of interest: some Reform communities use a three-year cycle \"loosely based\" on the old Yerushalayim practice, but differently. To keep Simchat Torah annually, they read the first third of every parashah in year one, the second third in year two, and the final third in year three. The old custom simply read the Torah through in order over three years. An obligation on the community, not the individual The poskim explain that Kriat HaTorah is a חובת הציבור , an obligation on the congregation, not on each individual. Some consequences: The coming Pesach (2022): the eighth day of Pesach falls on Shabbat. In Israel there is no eighth day, so they will already read the next parashah. Someone flying to Israel in the week after Pesach will miss a parashah. It is advisable to read it yourself, but there is no obligation to hear it. Someone who must leave in the middle of the reading has no obligation to hear the part he missed. Question: if an entire community went to Israel and returned, would it need to catch up? It depends on how you define a community, which is hard today. In a village with one shul, if the whole community went away, the Rabbi would agree. But if one London synagogue goes, \"the community is still London.\" He mentioned the discussion after the first lockdown, when the whole of England missed many parashiyot: some said one must restart from where reading stopped and catch up; others said just start from where you are up to. Part 4: Aliyot How many The Mishnah in Megillah sets the number of aliyot: Day Aliyot Weekdays (Monday, Thursday) 3 Rosh Chodesh 4 Yom Tov 5 Yom Kippur 6 Shabbat 7 On Yom Tov, Yom Kippur and Shabbat you may not do fewer, but you may add ( מוסיפין עליהן ), which is why we have extra call-ups then. On weekdays and Rosh Chodesh the Hachamim did not allow additions, because it would burden the community; people are busy. Kohen and Levi The first two aliyot go to the Kohen and Levi . The Gemara derives this from the word וקדשתו , \"you shall sanctify him\": there is a positive commandment to honour a Kohen in various ways, one of which is the first aliyah. And since Moshe gave the Torah to the Kohanim and the Levi'im together, the Levi follows the Kohen. Can a Kohen forgo the first aliyah? Generally a Kohen may waive his privileges, for example to be served first at a meal or to lead Birkat HaMazon. But for the Torah reading the Gemara says he may not , because people might murmur that he isn't a real Kohen, perhaps a chalal . He may, however, leave the room of his own accord. \"What's incorrect, by the way, is to pressure a Kohen to leave. That's absolutely wrong.\" A Talmid Hacham before an ignorant Kohen? There is a major dispute among the Rishonim whether the order Kohen, Levi, Yisrael applies when a Talmid Hacham is present. The Rambam held strongly that it does not. The Kohen goes first to honour him and to prevent rivalry, but when a Hacham is present, he takes precedence. He wrote very sharply against the custom of giving an ignorant Kohen precedence over a Yisrael who is a Talmid Hacham. The Shulchan Aruch writes that the minhag has developed to call the Kohen first even if he is ignorant. The very fact that he calls it a minhag shows, \"beneath the lines,\" that there were those who opposed it. \"But in any case, that's become the minhag today.\" The Spanish and Portuguese on Simchat Torah call up the Hatan Bereshit first and the Hatan Torah second , even if they are Yisraelim and Kohanim are present. \"When I first saw this minhag a few years ago, I was quite shocked.\" On investigation it seems to rest on this idea: the Hatanim are meant to be the Hachamim of the day, with a special connection to the Torah. Another reason given is that we assume the Kohen forgoes his right. \"It's very difficult,\" but that is its basis. When there is no Kohen A Levi may be called first in place of a Kohen. Rashi brings two opinions; the Shulchan Aruch codifies that a Levi has no right to it but may be called up. Never call a Levi after a Yisrael who replaced the Kohen, as it implies the Yisrael was a Kohen. Never call two Kohanim or two Levi'im in a row , as people will say one of them is not genuine. (A Kohen or Levi may be called for a later aliyah such as the seventh, but never, for example, a Levi for the third.) Eve: when there is no Levi, why is the same Kohen called again, and why does he say the bracha again? Because there is no other option. A different Kohen would imply the first wasn't real; a Yisrael would imply the Kohen was really a Levi. So the same Kohen is called a second time. Part 5: Women and Aliyot The Gemara and the Shulchan Aruch \"Believe it or not,\" there is an explicit Gemara in Megillah : תנו רבנן: הכל עולין למנין שבעה, ואפילו קטן ואפילו אשה. אבל אמרו חכמים: אשה לא תקרא בתורה מפני כבוד הציבור. Everyone counts towards the seven aliyot, even a child and even a woman. But the Hachamim said a woman should not read in the Torah because of kevod hatzibur , the honour of the congregation. Some people, even some who consider themselves Orthodox, argue that women can be called up. The key question is what kevod hatzibur means. What is kevod hatzibur ? Participants suggested it shames the men, implying they can't read, and someone raised yuhara , pretentiousness, which \"is such a concept,\" but the Rabbi preferred the first answer. 1. Humiliation of the congregation: the Ritva and Rabbeinu Avraham Min HaHar. Most Rishonim do not explain the term. The Ritva (Rabbi Yom Tov of Seville, thirteenth century) and Rabbeinu Avraham Min HaHar of Provence both explain that it is humiliating for the congregation, because it implies the men cannot read and depend on a woman to read for them. This must be understood in historical context. In the time of the Mishnah and Gemara there was no ba'al koreh : whoever was called up read his own portion. Calling up a woman to read implied that no man in the community was proficient. Even if the men were capable, \"it doesn't look good. It looks like the men are ignorant.\" 2. Immodesty: the Levush. The Levush (sixteenth century, one of the early Acharonim) says it is pritzut , immodest, for a woman to be called up in the presence of men. But the Rishonim do not say this, and given that the earlier authorities explain kevod hatzibur as humiliation, \"I think it's quite a stretch for this to be the correct interpretation.\" 3. Kol isha ? \"Absolutely incorrect.\" Some have argued recently that the concern is kol be'isha ervah , the prohibition on men hearing a woman sing. That is clearly wrong. First, if that were the reason, the Gemara would have said so. Second, as Hacham Ovadia Yosef and many others rule, reading the Torah is not singing . It is melodious, a chant of the te'amim , but it is not song. Hacham Ovadia notes that strictly speaking ( me'ikar hadin ) a woman may read the Megillah for men , since men and women have the same obligation to hear it. We don't do this today for other reasons, but no one ever suggested it was a problem of kol isha . \"But today no one reads his own aliyah\" So kevod hatzibur is about the embarrassment of the men relying on a woman. Some argue today, \"and I believe incorrectly,\" that since people called up no longer read for themselves but rely on the Rabbi or hazan, the concern no longer applies. Why does the Shulchan Aruch record it at all? The Shulchan Aruch quotes the Gemara almost verbatim: everyone counts towards seven, even a woman and a child, but the Hachamim said not to because of kevod hatzibur . The Talmud gives history; the Shulchan Aruch is a halachic code. If we don't call women up, why tell us that in principle they can be? Two fascinating answers: Hacham Ben Zion Abba Shaul suggests, and ends with tzarich iyun , that it may apply to a family minyan . Imagine a grandmother's 80th birthday, with a Sefer Torah brought to her home for a Shabbat morning service with children and grandchildren. There is no congregation and no implication that \"the men are so illiterate here,\" so perhaps it would be permissible. He doesn't say to go and do it. Hacham Ovadia Yosef points to the halacha that once someone has been called up by name, he must go up , or people will start rumours that he isn't really Jewish. So if a warden wanting to cause trouble called up a woman by her full name, she would then be obliged to take the aliyah. \"It would be wrong for the person to call her up. But now that she's been called up, it would then be the right thing for her to have the aliyah. I think that's fascinating.\" Today The reality is that Orthodox communities don't do this, even though one could argue kevod hatzibur no longer applies: Minhag. Many minhagim remain even when the original reason no longer applies, like the Ashkenazi custom of kitniyot , which arose from medieval concerns about grains being mixed. Once something is ingrained in our identity and the structure of our service, it has a quasi-halachic status. Meta-halachically , women's aliyot have become a political issue, sometimes championed by people who may not always have pure motives. Rav Moshe Feinstein on a woman wearing a tallit The Rabbi again quoted this responsum: may a woman wear a tallit? It is a positive time-bound mitzvah, from which women are exempt, but so are sukkah, shofar and lulav, which women do perform. Rav Moshe Feinstein answers: yes , if she does it because she genuinely feels it brings her closer to Hashem. But if she does it because she feels lower than men, \"men got the mitzvah, I didn't get the mitzvah,\" that is effectively saying God was wrong, and then she may not. \"We believe that men and women have different roles... on the same level. Same level doesn't necessarily mean that they have to have the identical roles.\" Flora was not sure, as a woman, that she would accept that answer. The Rabbi acknowledged it is difficult, and restated his two points: this is unheard of in our tradition, and its associations today are sometimes the problem. A test of sincerity: would she wear the tallit at home, alone, praying Shacharit? \"If she does that, then fine, then obviously it's genuine.\" Children's aliyot and the Haftarah were left for the following week. Part 6: A Thought for Tu BiShvat Tu BiShvat, falling that Sunday night and Monday, is \"a purely halachic day\" that has taken on meaning over the last few hundred years. It is about fruit, not vegetables , and the Torah distinguishes between them. Before the sin, Adam is told he may eat from all the fruit of the trees . Nothing about vegetation. After the sin, his curse: thorns and thistles , and you will eat the herb of the field . The Gemara in Pesachim says that when Adam heard this his eyes streamed with tears: \"I am going to end up eating the same food as my donkey.\" Why is that a curse? The Maharal of Prague explains that אדם is named after the אדמה , the earth. A patch of bare earth looks lifeless; dig as much as you like and you find only soil. But drop in a seed, nurture it, and flowers and even huge trees grow out of that seemingly lifeless clod. Earth has tremendous hidden potential. By contrast בהמה , an animal, is בה מה , \"what is in it is what it is\": what you see is what you get. A human being nurtured in intellect and character can grow and release the fruits within him. The same difference separates fruit from vegetables: Vegetable Fruit tree Cut and consumed, and nothing is left What you see is a tiny fraction: take the fruit and the whole tree remains No further produce Even the fruit holds a seed that can produce more Man's proper state is the pri , the fruit, with a vast reserve of potential waiting to be actualised. When Adam sinned he fell to the level of an animal, and his diet fell to vegetation. Even in English, a person alive but with no mental capacity, bar minan , is called \"a vegetable.\" \"It would just sound wrong to call such a person a fruit,\" because fruit implies potential and growth. A tree grows tall, צדיק כתמר יפרח , like a date palm; vegetation stays low on the ground. That is our conflict as human beings: whether we look down to the grass and our base instincts, or look up to the trees, where we aspire and want to shine. That is why Tu BiShvat has come to speak to human beings too: where we want to end up is as a fruit tree, not just vegetation. Summary Public Torah reading goes back to Hakhel (Moshe) and Ezra . Monday, Thursday and Shabbat readings are an institution of Moshe (Rambam, Hilchot Tefillah 12; Bava Kama: \"three days without water\"), on Monday and Thursday because those were days of forgiveness after the Golden Calf. Shabbat Mincha is Ezra's , for the yoshvei keranot : those who miss the weekday readings (Rashi) or those idling on Shabbat afternoon (Meiri, more convincing). Annual cycle in Bavel, triennial in Eretz Yisrael (still in Egypt in the twelfth century); universal annual cycle by the thirteenth or fourteenth century. Simchat Torah is really the second day of Shemini Atzeret. Kriat HaTorah is a communal obligation ; an individual who misses a portion need not make it up. Aliyot: 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, adding only on Yom Tov and Shabbat. A Kohen cannot forgo the first aliyah but may leave; never pressure him. The Rambam put a Talmid Hacham before an ignorant Kohen; the Shulchan Aruch records the opposite minhag . With no Kohen, a Levi may go first; never a Levi after a Yisrael, never two Kohanim or Levi'im in a row; with no Levi, the same Kohen is called again. Women's aliyot: permitted in principle (Gemara, Shulchan Aruch) but not done because of kevod hatzibur , the embarrassment of implying the men can't read (Ritva, Rabbeinu Avraham Min HaHar), not immodesty (Levush) and certainly not kol isha (Hacham Ovadia). Hacham Ben Zion's family-minyan suggestion and Hacham Ovadia's \"called by name\" case explain why the Shulchan Aruch records it. Today it is not done, as minhag and because of its political associations; Rav Moshe Feinstein's test of sincerity. Tu BiShvat: be a fruit tree, not a vegetable (Maharal). Coming Up Next week: children's aliyot and the Haftarah, then the completion of tefillah: Musaf and the concept of the sacrifices. After the series: a new series on Hebrew grammar . \"You may think this is simple and basic. It's absolutely not.\" Grammar is so basic that no one talks about it, because it is assumed you know it, but you are not meant to study Torah without knowing how to read properly.","6EtiTekwAHg":"Series: Prayer Series (Tiferet Eyal, 2021-22) Shiur: Part 4 of the six-part Prayer Series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom for Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 21 December 2021 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap and Plan The series so far: the history and theology of prayer (Part 1); the Siddur from Birkot HaShachar to Nishmat (Part 2); and the Shema and its blessings, creation, revelation and redemption (Part 3). After the winter break: the reading of the Torah in the service, including children and women reading. Tonight: the silent Amidah , briefly, and then the main focus, the repetition of the Amidah . The Spanish and Portuguese have a unique custom of reciting an abridged repetition, half aloud and half silent, and other communities rely on it in pressing circumstances. The Rabbi wanted to trace where this comes from, how the customs evolved, and when one may rely on them. Part 1: The Four Names of the Amidah Rabbinic literature has four names for the Amidah: Name Meaning Why תפלה The prayer The Rambam always calls it simply Tefillah , because it is prayer: the centrepiece of every service עמידה Standing The only part of the prayer where standing is required (standing at Baruch She'amar or Vayevarech David is only customary) שמונה עשרה Eighteen The weekday Amidah's eighteen blessings תפלת לחש The silent prayer The only part that must be said silently \"We forget and we lose the centrality of the Amidah, because we have throughout the generations added so much onto the tefillah before and after.\" A thousand years ago Shacharit, Mincha and Arvit were simple: the Amidah, plus the blessings of the Shema morning and evening. \"That's it.\" Part 2: Amidah: Standing Before the Shechinah Why stand only here? Because everything until now is a warm-up. This is when we stand face to face with the Shechinah. That is the beauty of the Amidah: we pray together as a tzibur for the warm-up and the parts after, but here we try to forget everyone around us and imagine we are one-to-one with Hashem. No interruptions The Mishnah in Berachot says: even if a snake is wrapped around your ankle, you must not interrupt. The Gemara qualifies this: a snake that is not poisonous. (Today, of course, we would tell people to interrupt, \"because we never know.\") The point is hyperbolic: nothing may interrupt the Amidah. Not phones, and not gesturing to other people. Contrast the Shema : if someone greets you with \"Shabbat shalom\" in the middle of the first paragraph and it would be disrespectful to ignore him, you may respond or nod. In the Amidah, \"you cannot do anything.\" Kavanah is indispensable The Amidah is the only part of the prayer where kavanah is me'akev , where lack of concentration invalidates it. The Gemara, the Rambam, and even the Shulchan Aruch rule that if you prayed the Amidah without kavanah, you must repeat it. \"I repeat that. You have to repeat the prayer.\" Why have we never heard of anyone doing this? The Maharam of Rothenburg , teacher of the Rosh in the thirteenth century, said that although this is the halachic requirement, he no longer advises it, because \"probably the second time you're not going to have concentration either.\" This became accepted practice. But for the Shema, as long as you said the words you fulfilled the mitzvah; for the Amidah, strictly, you did not. Kavanah over standing If the choice is between praying sitting with kavanah or standing with less kavanah , the halacha is clear: concentration is more important. So almost all contemporary poskim rule that on a plane you should not make a minyan in the back of the plane, with people walking around and disturbing you. Better to pray quietly in your seat. What kavanah means There are two levels: The minimum, codified in halacha (the Rambam): recognising that you are standing before the Shechinah , even if you don't understand every word. \"For a few moments, I am now in conversation with the Divine. That is called kavanah.\" The better level: understanding what you are saying. This is why the verse אדני שפתי תפתח was added before the Amidah, which really begins with Baruch Atah . It gives a person a few moments to focus and set out on the journey of tefillah. Part 3: Shemoneh Esreh: Eighteen, and the Nineteenth The structure is three blessings, then thirteen (or fourteen), then three : eighteen, or nineteen. The eighteen were composed by the Men of the Great Assembly , about 2,400 years ago. The nineteenth blessing ולמלשינים אל תהי תקוה was added in the second century , in response to the minim , the heretics: both the Sadducees , constantly fighting the rabbis, and the Christians , who believed Jesus was the Mashiach. The Gemara says that Rabban Gamliel decided to institute the blessing. He stood in the Bet HaMidrash and asked who could formulate its text, and Shmuel HaKatan (not Shmuel the prophet) got up and composed it. Couldn't Rabban Gamliel compose a blessing himself? Rav Kook answers with Pirkei Avot, where each of the Hachamim has his motto. Hillel used to say to greet people pleasantly. Shmuel HaKatan's motto was the verse בנפל אויבך אל תשמח , \"When your enemy falls, do not rejoice.\" Rabban Gamliel needed someone who could compose this blessing without any agenda, without personal hate . He himself felt disappointment and strife with these people. Read the English of this blessing: uproot them, break them, shatter them. \"It's very difficult to say these words in the modern era.\" Only someone who would never rejoice at his enemy's downfall could write it. Why eighteen? The Gemara in Berachot gives three reasons: # Eighteen corresponds to... 1 The 18 vertebrae of the spine, because we bow during the Amidah. The halacha is to bend until you feel the vertebrae stand out: \"you can't just nod your head\" 2 The 18 mentions of Hashem's name in the Shema , since we go straight from the Shema to the Amidah 3 The 18 mentions of Hashem's name in Mizmor LeDavid, Havu LaShem (the psalm we say on Friday night), which also alludes to the Avot, around whom the Amidah centres Part 4: Tefillat Lachash: Learning From Hannah The silent form of prayer is learned from Hannah . Barren for many years, she went to Shiloh, bitter and weeping, and prayed: only her lips moved, and her voice was not heard . Eli, thinking her drunk, confronted her: he had never seen anyone pray with moving lips and no sound. Hannah answered that she was not drunk, but a woman of sorrowful spirit. Hannah taught the Kohen Gadol, and all generations, that when you are close, you don't need to shout . \"Prayer ultimately is an intimate conversation, and no one else is meant to hear.\" We don't do the whole service this way, because we are a community, but the climax is quiet and intimate. But silent does not mean thinking. \"That's another misconception.\" The words must actually be said , to concretise one's thoughts. In yeshiva they were always encouraged to speak out what they learned with a study partner, because \"even if you think you understand in your head, when you actually speak it out you concretise things.\" So too in the Amidah: we articulate the words, but quietly enough that others don't hear. Part 5: Custom and Law: Feet and Steps Everything binding in our religion must derive from the Torah or the Gemara . As the Rambam writes clearly, the Talmud, sealed in the fifth century, was the final vote of the Sanhedrin, our legislative body, and no one can argue with it. \"Which is why I always make a point in all of my shiurim to always trace the origins of certain things which we do. Is it biblical? Is it rabbinic? Is it customary?\" Which is more important: standing with the feet together, or taking three steps back before the Amidah? Rick answered correctly that the three steps are a custom. (Jane noted that the idea is approaching a king, which is indeed the idea, but the question is the source.) Practice Source Feet together The Gemara : the angels have \"a straight leg,\" and we put our feet together to resemble them. Codified by the Rambam. Three steps Not in the Shulchan Aruch at all. Recorded only in the Rama , for Ashkenazim, a late addition from the Ashkenazi Rishonim \"If you ever want to know what's the din and what's extra, just look at the Rambam.\" On a plane you don't need to take three steps back. Forward, not back The original custom was only to take three steps forward , approaching the Shechinah. If the whole synagogue stepped forward, the front row would hit the wall, so people began stepping back first and then forward. Some poskim were unhappy with this back-and-forth: \"It looks like it's not respectful... you're playing a game here.\" The correct practice, which not many know: take three steps back earlier , at תהלות לאל עליון , and then, when beginning the Amidah, step forward. Then at Adonai sefatai tiftach you have stepped forward and are in your place. The three steps back at the end are likewise a minhag, and the steps forward afterwards just return you to your place. Coming forward and going back express intimacy, coming closer to the Shechinah . Part 6: Praise, Request, Thanksgiving Every prayer has the same structure: שבח , praise: the first three blessings (the God of our forefathers, who revives the dead, the holy God). בקשה , request: the middle blessings. הודאה , thanksgiving: the last three, from רצה . \"This is just basic derech eretz.\" When you write an email asking someone for something, you don't just ask; you begin by acknowledging them, perhaps thanking them for something they did, and end with thanks. So too: I address You because You are the source of all; then I ask; then I thank. On Shabbat the rabbis removed the middle requests so as not to burden us, leaving a short Amidah, since Shabbat is not a time to stress about our weekday needs. \"But obviously the Jewish people didn't want to accept that,\" and added plenty before and after. Part 7: The Repetition of the Amidah The Rabbi had spent considerable time researching this. \"It's fascinating to see the different customs and how this all evolved.\" The original purpose The Mishnah in Rosh Hashanah records a debate: The Sages: even though the shaliach tzibur prays, everyone must still pray for themselves. Rabban Gamliel: the shaliach tzibur can fulfil the obligation for everyone. The Gemara explains that the halacha is a hybrid. All year round, if you know how to pray, you must pray yourself ; the hazan's repetition is for those who cannot. On Rosh Hashanah , and on Yom Kippur of the Yovel year (which once every fifty years had very long special brachot), even those who know how to pray may rely on the hazan because the brachot are unfamiliar. This is the halacha in the Rambam and the Shulchan Aruch. Context: a world without siddurim The Gemara must be read in context. When the Hachamim instituted the repetition there were no siddurim . The first siddur we have is that of Rav Amram Gaon , around the eighth or ninth century, in Babylonia. People knew the Amidah because their parents taught them, and not everyone could. So: those who know pray; those who don't listen to the hazan. But as siddurim spread and people became proficient readers, in the early medieval period people began to ask: \"Why are we doing things twice?\" That might have been enough to abolish the repetition. The complication: Kedushah At the same time another minhag was developing. The Yerushalmi , quoted by Tosafot in a couple of places, records that originally Kedushah was said only at Shacharit on Shabbat and Yom Tov . In the time of the Geonim it spread to every prayer with a repetition . And saying Kedushah in unison with ten people is a Torah mitzvah , to sanctify Hashem's name. Abolish the repetition and you lose the Kedushah. Two conflicting pressures: on the one hand, people can read and no longer need a repetition; on the other, without a repetition there is no Kedushah. \"So different methods came up through the generations to try to address these issues.\" Method 1: Rav Hai Gaon: three blessings and stop The earliest approach the Rabbi knew of is Rav Hai Gaon (tenth century), quoted by the Beit Yosef . When it is getting late and the sun is setting: everyone prays the silent Amidah, then the hazan repeats only the first three blessings with Kedushah , through האל הקדוש , and stops. Remarkably, the Shulchan Aruch records this as acceptable when time is short. \"It's quite striking: we can take an Amidah instituted to be eighteen blessings, and just do three blessings to do Kedushah.\" But it never caught on. Rav Hai Gaon describes it as the custom in the Babylonian yeshivot, when a late shiur ran into Mincha at sunset. Since then it is scarcely recorded. \"I've never seen this done... It just feels you're stopping mid-air.\" Method 2: The Rambam: one Amidah, aloud The Rambam's approach: no silent Amidah at all . The hazan says the whole Amidah aloud, with Kedushah. Those who know how to pray say it quietly along with him; those who don't, listen. There is just one prayer, no repetition. The Rabbi showed two of the Rambam's responsa ( Teshuvot HaRambam 256 and 258 ), offering to send them afterwards. \"The problems which we have in each generation are the same problems.\" The Rambam describes his reasons: During the repetition people chat with one another and go out , and the hazan's blessings go unanswered. Even Torah scholars use the time to talk, and apparently to clear their throats and noses. The uninitiated person sees this and concludes the repetition is a waste of time, when he is the one who needs it most because he can't read. The Rambam calls it a chillul Hashem , applying the verse עת לעשות לה׳ הפרו תורתך : \"It's become a joke.\" So the Rambam made his takkanah : on Shabbat and Yom Tov, at all the prayers , because so many people come, and at Mincha when time is short , the hazan says one Amidah aloud. His son, Rabbeinu Avraham , writes that because of his father's takkanah this became the practice throughout Egypt . \"And unfortunately, nothing has really changed since the Rambam's time. Exact same thing happens today.\" The Radbaz , Rabbeinu David ben Zimra (1479-1573, as Jane looked up), chief rabbi of Egypt and author of over two thousand responsa, writes that everyone in Egypt still did this because of the Rambam, more than 350 years later. He was unhappy and tried to restore the original takkanah, pointing out that in Aleppo , Damascus , and among Jews who came from Aden , they did a repetition. Whether he succeeded, the Rabbi did not know. He had read that some Yemenite communities, who accepted the Rambam in almost everything, still keep one loud Amidah today, though he had never seen it. Method 3: The Spanish and Portuguese: aloud, silent, aloud The method many know: The hazan says the first three blessings aloud , with Kedushah together. The middle blessings are silent , for everyone, including the hazan. The hazan says the last three blessings , from Retzeh , aloud. The Radbaz saw this in some places and suggested it grew out of the Rambam's method: a hazan who leads every prayer aloud never gets a chance for lachash , to pour out his own words silently to Hashem. The silent middle gives him that space. Advantages: besides Kedushah, you get Modim DeRabbanan (under the Rambam's method the hazan says Modim aloud while everyone says it with him, so there is never the congregation's own Modim), and the hazan can include Birkat Kohanim . Hacham Shem Tov Gaguine , in the Keter Shem Tov , the Spanish and Portuguese authority about a hundred years ago, favoured it for these reasons, and writes that he saw it only in London and Amsterdam . The problem: \"I'll be honest, I'm not a big fan of this custom.\" After the silent middle, the hazan comes back in while some people are ahead and others behind. People are disturbed and don't know where he is up to. \"I can't tell you how many times people have asked me, if I'm in the middle of Amidah, do I need to respond?\" The Rabbi suspects (without proof) that originally the hazan said the middle part very slowly , giving everyone time to finish before resuming. When he leads without a repetition and there is time, he always draws out the middle blessings for exactly this reason. The halacha: respond to the hazan's last blessings only if you have finished your Amidah . If you are still in the middle, you may not respond to anything, not even amen. When was this done? There are two reasons for a shortened repetition: lack of time , or people making a mockery of the repetition when crowds come on Shabbat and Yom Tov. The original Spanish and Portuguese practice was to do this at Musaf on Shabbat and Yom Tov (after a long sermon and long parashah, people anxious to leave and chatting) and at weekday Mincha (people at work and busy). Doing it at Shacharit is, to the Rabbi's understanding, only from the last twenty or thirty years , because often there are only ten people. \"I humbly suggest on Sundays or bank holidays, when we have the time and we have ample minyan, not just ten exactly, there's no reason why we shouldn't do repetition.\" Method 4: The Ashkenazi custom When Ashkenazim are short of time, only the hazan begins the Amidah aloud. Everyone listens and answers Baruch Hu uvaruch Shemo , amen and Kedushah. When he reaches HaEl HaKadosh , the whole community begins the silent Amidah together. The drawback: no Modim DeRabbanan and no Birkat Kohanim. \"Every method has its advantages. Every method has its disadvantages.\" They all evolved from the same tension: the repetition no longer seems needed, yet we have Kedushah, Modim DeRabbanan and Birkat Kohanim. The kabbalistic view According to the kabbalists , all this is futile. The repetition has nothing to do with people who can't read; it is about moving \"from one world to the next,\" and so on. For them one must always repeat the Amidah , however busy one is. The shiur did not take that approach, but it explains why some people always insist on a repetition. The takeaway: don't make a mockery of the repetition. No talking, no chatting, no walking in and out. Listen, pay attention, and answer amen and Baruch Hu uvaruch Shemo . Questions at the End Adam: isn't petition the most self-regarding part, as if there were \"a technology\" for getting God to answer? Isn't the main thing awareness of standing before God? Petition isn't pressing God's buttons, the Rabbi said (as in the first shiur). Its purpose is to recognise that Hashem is the source of our wisdom, wealth and health, and that recognition brings intimacy. \"Petition isn't a means in itself. It's a means to an end of the intimacy with the Divine.\" Can you ask for something very specific? \"Of course. On the contrary, we must ask for everything,\" like the son who should have asked his father rather than his uncle. Asking purely out of devotion, with no hope of reward, is the highest level, but \"almost impossible.\" The Hachamim instituted petition because it is close to our hearts: \"people listen to things when it's relevant to their lives... The Rambam probably got there, but I don't think anyone else gets there.\" Rick (from the choir): why do we answer Baruch Hu uvaruch Shemo sometimes and not at others? If you are relying on the hazan to fulfil your obligation, you never answer Baruch Hu uvaruch Shemo . And if you haven't finished your own Amidah you can't answer anything, not even amen (\"If there's a snake around your ankle, you can't say amen, can you?\"). In the Spanish and Portuguese Musaf, the silent middle gives you time to finish, which is why the hazan should go slowly, so that from Modim you can respond. Gabriel: on Rosh Hashanah we need to hear 101 tekiot. How, without a repetition? Exactly. That's why Rosh Hashanah is different: every community in the world does a full repetition on Rosh Hashanah, without fail. Summary Four names: Tefillah (the Rambam's name: it is prayer), Amidah, Shemoneh Esreh, Tefillat Lachash. Amidah: face to face with the Shechinah. No interruptions (the snake). Kavanah is indispensable : strictly one repeats without it, but since the Maharam of Rothenburg we don't. Kavanah outranks standing (pray seated on a plane). Minimum kavanah: knowing you stand before the Shechinah. Eighteen blessings from the Men of the Great Assembly; the nineteenth by Shmuel HaKatan , chosen (Rav Kook) because he never rejoiced at an enemy's fall. Eighteen = vertebrae, names in Shema, names in Havu LaShem . Silent, from Hannah , but the words must be spoken. Feet together is Gemara and Rambam; three steps is a late Ashkenazi custom (Rama). Originally only steps forward; step back at Tehillot LeEl Elyon . Structure: praise, request, thanksgiving. Repetition: instituted for those who couldn't pray, in a world without siddurim; preserved because of Kedushah . Four methods: Rav Hai Gaon (three blessings and stop, in the Shulchan Aruch but never adopted); the Rambam (one loud Amidah, against the mockery of the repetition; all Egypt for centuries until the Radbaz; reportedly still some Yemenites); Spanish and Portuguese (first and last three aloud, middle silent; gives Modim DeRabbanan and Birkat Kohanim; hazan must go slowly; don't respond mid-Amidah); Ashkenazi (hazan aloud to HaEl HaKadosh, then all begin). Kabbalists always repeat. Don't make a mockery of the repetition. Coming Up After the winter break, Tuesday 11 January : the reading of the Torah , its origins as a takkanah, and children and women reading from the Torah .","2r_6e3cjB0Q":"Series: Prayer Series (Tiferet Eyal, 2021-22) Shiur: Part 3 of the six-part Prayer Series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom for Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 14 December 2021 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap In the first two shiurim the Rabbi covered the theology of prayer (why pray if Hashem knows our needs), its history and origins, and then the Siddur from Modeh Ani to Yishtabach, including Baruch She'amar, Nishmat, Pesukei DeZimrah and the Korbanot. Tonight's focus is the next part of the service, after the Kaddish: the Shema and the brachot before and after it. Next week: the Amidah and the customs of its repetition. Part 1: Barechu, the Call to Communal Prayer The core of tefillah As explained last week, the essential parts of prayer are three: The Amidah The Shema with its blessings before and after Birkot HaShachar In the times of the Talmud and the early Geonim, until about 1,300 or 1,400 years ago, that was the entire tefillah. Pesukei DeZimrah was added later. So Barechu marks the moment we begin \"prayer proper.\" The most communal part of the service Pesukei DeZimrah can be said alone; there is no concept of minyan for it. But the Shema and its blessings are a communal, led service : Until the time of the Rosh (Rabbeinu Asher, around the thirteenth century), the common practice was for the hazan alone to say the blessings of the Shema while everyone listened and answered amen. The Rosh saw that these are long blessings and people were not concentrating, so he had everyone read together with the hazan. This is why the Sephardi custom of the hazan saying every word aloud is the correct one historically: for the Shema and its blessings, the hazan was saying it on everyone's behalf, to be motzi them. The Kedushah in the middle of the first blessing was, according to many, instituted only to be said in a congregation. \"The Amidah is the time when you're alone, by yourself with God. And the Shema and the blessings, the time you're together in the community.\" So Barechu is the hazan's call: \"I am now going to be leading us in this blessing, in this recognition of HaKadosh Baruch Hu as the source of all. Join me. At the very least, listen.\" Barechu goes back to the Mishnah. Why there is no Barechu at Mincha Barechu is said only at Shacharit and Arvit, the two services with the blessings of the Shema. Mincha is just an Amidah, a silent prayer. We pray it with a minyan, but there is no congregational prayer together, so there is no Barechu. The same logic explains Barechu at the Torah reading . Originally, the person called up would read his own portion on behalf of the whole congregation. So he called out Barechu: pay attention, I am doing this on your behalf. Barechu at the end of the service Daniel asked about the Barechu at the end of the service, by Aleinu. This, the Rabbi explained, is a much later addition, brought in the Rishonim, for people who came late and missed Barechu. Since it is such an important call, it was added at the end so latecomers at least hear it on the way out. The same applies to Barechu at the end of Arvit. Shabbat and Yom Tov: the Rivash argued that if it is a compensation, it only makes sense on weekdays. On Shabbat and Yom Tov no one comes later than the Torah reading, where they will have heard Barechu. The Shulchan Aruch rules in two places, in Hilchot Tefillah and in Hilchot Shabbat, that on Shabbat and Yom Tov we do not say Barechu at the end. This is the minhag of the Spanish and Portuguese, and of the Ashkenazim too. Oriental Sephardim do say it again, based on the Arizal , for kabbalistic reasons. Standing and bowing for Barechu There is no need to stand or bow for Barechu. This is clear in the poskim. Among Ashkenazim standing became the minhag. Among Sephardim it is not sourced. The Spanish and Portuguese do stand: \"I've searched and searched for some kind of source for this. I haven't found any.\" If you are in such a congregation, of course you stand out of respect for the minhag. Most Sephardi communities stay seated. If you were already standing for Kaddish, you remain standing as usual. Hacham Ben Zion Abba Shaul goes further: one should not bow, because we bow only where the Hachamim instituted bowing, and they never did so at Barechu. Part 2: The Structure of the Blessings A bracha is something that begins or ends with Baruch Atah . Around the Shema there are two blessings before and one after : Blessing Opens Theme 1 יוצר אור ובורא חשך Creation 2 אהבת עולם Revelation Shema The unity of Hashem 3 אמת ויציב Redemption Creation, revelation, redemption: \"essentially the foundations of Judaism.\" The formation of the universe in Bereshit, the giving of the Torah in Shemot, and redemption, from Egypt in the past and the Mashiach in the future. The same pattern holds morning and evening. All in the present tense Notice that the blessings are in the present tense . \"This drama of creation, revelation and redemption is an ongoing drama.\" It is not that Hashem created the world, revealed Himself and then left. \"God creates, and God also reveals, on a constant and regular basis.\" The Hachamim say the words of the Torah should be new and fresh in our eyes every day, not something received 3,000 years ago at Sinai. And redemption speaks of the past, our daily redemption, and the future. These blessings were formulated by the Men of the Great Assembly , about 2,300 years ago. Since originally the hazan said them on everyone's behalf, someone who finds it difficult to pray can still be yotzeh by listening to the hazan saying each word. Part 3: The First Blessing: Creation \"Who creates everything\" יוצר אור ובורא חשך, עושה שלום ובורא את הכל : Hashem forms light, creates darkness, makes peace and creates everything. The Gemara already notices that this paraphrases a verse in Yeshayahu but changes it. The verse reads עושה שלום ובורא רע , \"makes peace and creates evil .\" Why did the Hachamim change it? The whole point of the Shema is Yichud Hashem : there is only one primal source and force for everything that exists. So you might expect the blessing to keep \"creates evil.\" But to soften it, the Hachamim used a euphemism, בורא את הכל , \"creates everything.\" The Gemara then asks: in that case, why not soften \"darkness\" too, and say נוגה , dimness? Because we must make clear that Hashem controls both day and night, and there aren't two forces . \"The Hachamim have taken a pasuk in Yeshayahu and basically sweetened it up... Instead of specifically mentioning evil right at the beginning of the day, it's more general, He creates everything. We still nonetheless say darkness, because we have to make it very clear that Hashem is the source of everything.\" (To Jane: we certainly need to know that we can choose good or bad, but we don't need to begin the day by reminding ourselves that Hashem created evil.) Against dualism This opening, which is also the opening of the Arvit blessing, is directed against dualism: the temptation to see suffering and evil as a force independent of, and opposed to, Hashem. In Christianity there is God, all good, and Satan doing evil, a contest between them. \"No. This verse in Yeshayahu, and also this part of the prayer, affirms our belief that all forces emanate from the one HaKadosh Baruch Hu.\" The Rabbi quoted a prolific Jewish author of the last generation who said the greatest obstacle to faith is suffering in this world. Many of the questions people ask boil down to this: why is there so much evil? Why do bad things happen to me when I'm a good person? \"There's no satisfactory answer which ticks all the boxes.\" That is exactly why we remind ourselves morning and evening that there is one force behind good and bad. Why the best time is the transition This, said the Rabbi, is why the ideal ( mitzvah min hamuvchar ) is to pray at netz , sunrise. The Gemara speaks very highly of it. The Rambam holds that saying Shema after sunrise is no longer the first-instance ( lechatchila ) fulfilment, only bedi'avad . We hold it is still first-rate within the first three hours, though it is better before sunrise. For Arvit , the Shulchan Aruch says to pray as early as possible in the evening. Why? Because these are the moments when we see the forces changing : night fading and day arriving, day fading and night arriving. \"Don't see this as two separate forces. Use this time of transition as a way to recognise HaKadosh Baruch Hu is involved behind it all.\" It is hard to live like that. We see powerful people, we see sin, and we take them as separate parts of reality, forgetting that there is a Creator in control and direction of the world. Shabbat additions On Shabbat the first blessing has two extra paragraphs, because there is more time, with the same emphasis on creation. It also has אין כערכך , the four-part declaration that there is none like Hashem: in this world, the next world, the resurrection and the days of Mashiach. Hashem is behind it all. The spiritual creation and the Kedushah Creation includes the spiritual realms, \"all the drama happening in the upper hemispheres.\" So the second part of the first blessing describes the angels , who worship Hashem and are sent on missions. We repeat what they say: קדוש קדוש קדוש ה׳ צבאות comes from Yeshayahu (the haftarah of Parashat Yitro). ברוך כבוד ה׳ ממקומו comes from Yechezkel . Both prophets had mystical visions of Hashem with His heavenly hosts. Can an individual say this Kedushah alone? The Shulchan Aruch brings two opinions: Opinion Reasoning The Rosh: may be said alone Unlike the Kedushah of the Amidah ( Nakdishach ), which is itself a prayer sanctifying Hashem, here we are only retelling what the angels say , sipur devarim The Rambam: only with a minyan It is a real Kedushah, a davar shebikdushah . \"Just like no one would ever dream to do Nakdishach without a minyan\" The Shulchan Aruch rules that we must be concerned for the Rambam's view. So when praying alone, say it not as a prayer but as if reading verses from the prophets , which is always permitted. One blessing, two parts The paragraph that follows Kedushah is often treated as something new, but it is not. Kedushah creates a dramatic \"full stop,\" but the text simply continues describing the angels' pleasant songs and praises. So: First part: the physical creation, sun, stars and luminaries. Second part: the spiritual creation, the angels, through Kedushah and what follows. Conclusion: ברוך אתה ה׳ יוצר המאורות . Part 4: The Second Blessing: Revelation We move from the cosmic beauty of the universe and Hashem's broad relationship with it to Hashem's revelation and intimacy with humanity, especially the Jewish people. You taught our ancestors to fear and appreciate You; do the same for us. Teach us Your Torah, give us the ability to understand and contemplate it, enlighten our eyes in Your Torah. \"The first blessing is very non-personal. God is amazing, but it's nothing to do with us. The second blessing is intimate, it's personal. God, You taught our ancestors, now teach us, give us a connection with You, choose us as well.\" It ends הבוחר בעמו ישראל באהבה , \"who chooses His people Israel with love,\" the perfect lead-in to the Shema. In the Spanish and Portuguese tradition this line is sung very ornately. \"Whoever composed the music for the Spanish and Portuguese was a person of a great soul... Every place where there's a special part of the music is something very lofty.\" This is the climax. The Rabbi recalled that in yeshiva people spent a long time on this blessing, since it is our connection with Hashem and with the Torah. Gathering the tzitzit There is a custom, mentioned later (not by the Rambam), to gather the four tzitziyot here, because we mention bringing us in peace from the four corners of the earth . Really one should take the tzitzit only at the third paragraph of the Shema, which speaks of them. The problem: people search for their tzitzit and mumble the words. \"Again, this is the theme which we've seen already in this series. When lots of things are added on, you take away from the main thing.\" The point of this blessing is connection and relationship, and the tzitzit should not distract from it. Ahavat Olam or Ahavah Rabbah Sephardim say אהבת עולם אהבתנו ; Ashkenazim say אהבה רבה אהבתנו . Most differences in tefillah are just differences of custom, but this is one of the only ones that goes back to a debate in the Gemara itself . Text Meaning Followed by אהבת עולם Everlasting love, \"very affectionate and emotional\" The Rambam and the Rif , the Sephardi Rishonim, and our minhag אהבה רבה Great love Tosafot and the Ashkenazi Rishonim The question is how intense and deep Hashem's love is: everlasting, or \"just\" great. Part 5: The Shema Itself 245 words and 248 The blessings so far are rabbinic, from the Men of the Great Assembly. The Shema itself is a mitzvah from the Torah. It contains 245 words . The Zohar (not the halachic sources or Rishonim) says: add three words to make 248 , the number of positive mitzvot and of our limbs, so that we accept the yoke of Heaven with all 248 limbs. The Shulchan Aruch follows the Zohar here , and the hazan repeats ה׳ אלהיכם אמת on behalf of the congregation. \"Hashem Elokeichem at the end of the Shema isn't part of the Shema. Get this clear.\" Ashkenazim: when praying alone, they say אל מלך נאמן before the Shema to make up the three words. The first to record this is the Agur , around the fourteenth century. Another reason given: the Gemara in Shabbat says אמן is an abbreviation of El Melech Ne'eman . Sephardim do not say it, because it would be an interruption ( hefsek ). There is also a discussion about answering amen at this point. Sephardim hold one does not answer amen; Ashkenazim are more equivocal, which is why they recommend finishing together with the hazan. What Shema means שמע is more than hearing. In Hebrew, atah shome'a? means \"do you understand?\" The Rabbi cited the previous week's parashah: when the brothers blamed each other for selling Yosef, they did not know that Yosef understood ( כי שמע יוסף ), because he had positioned an interpreter. Of course he heard them; he was pretending not to understand. So Shema means to listen, understand, reflect and appreciate the unity of Hashem. Why we cover our eyes The custom goes back to the Gemara : Rabbi was in the middle of teaching when the time for Shema came, and he covered his eyes and said it. But why? The Rambam: simply to concentrate . He had students in front of him. The Ramchal: we don't need reminding twice a day that there is only one God. What we need reminding of is that everything that happens in this world emanates from Hashem , and that is hard. Covering our eyes says: \"HaKadosh Baruch Hu, I can't see Your unity. If I look at this world I see discrepancy, light and darkness, good and bad. I can't see the Yichud Hashem, and that's why I'm covering my eyes. However, I believe in it.\" Yaakov and Yosef The Rabbi told \"a beautiful\" idea from the previous week's parashah. After 22 years, Yaakov and Yosef meet. Yosef falls on Yaakov's neck, but the Hachamim say Yaakov did not fall on Yosef's, because Yaakov was saying Kriat Shema . Why only Yaakov? If it was the time for Shema, Yosef should have said it too. The answer: Yosef had experienced the Yichud Hashem. He had been sold, sent down to Egypt, and saw how it all led to him providing for the whole world. For him it wasn't belief; he had lived Shema Yisrael for 22 years. Yaakov had lived only with belief : \"Where has my son Yosef gone? I believe everything comes from Hashem. I can't see it.\" Now, reunited with his son, he finally saw it. That was the moment to say Shema Yisrael, Hashem Echad . Baruch Shem silently ברוך שם כבוד מלכותו לעולם ועד is said quietly. According to the Ramchal, this was the secret Yaakov revealed to his sons before he died: the revelation of Hashem's glory and kingship in the time of Mashiach. We believe it but don't yet see it , so we say it quietly. On Yom Kippur , when we are separated from our physical desires and are \"all good,\" without the balance of good and bad, we say it aloud . Saying the Shema aloud together Rabbi David Abudarham , the Spanish commentator on the prayer book (fourteenth or fifteenth century), records the minhag that the whole community says the first verse aloud together , as if all testifying for one another. In the Torah the ע of Shema and the ד of Echad are written large, spelling עד , a witness. The Rabbi had only seen this minhag kept among the Spanish and Portuguese , where everyone joins the hazan aloud with a special tune. As Jane noted, this is also why we say Shema before death: to recognise that everything comes from Hashem. The paragraphs of the Shema The first two paragraphs are the Torah mitzvah of Shema: the acceptance of the yoke of Heaven , kabbalat ol malchut shamayim , and the acceptance of the yoke of the mitzvot . The third paragraph, tzitzit , was added because there is a mitzvah to remember the Exodus every day . Many verses could do that, but this one has a double advantage: it remembers Egypt, and tzitzit are a reminder of the mitzvot we have just accepted. Part 6: The Third Blessing: Redemption The final blessing begins ויציב ונכון וקים וישר ונאמן : fifteen adjectives , all meaning essentially \"true.\" Why fifteen? Source Reason Abudarham (Flora's suggestion) The 15 steps in the Temple and the 15 Shir HaMa'alot psalms sung on them The Zohar , quoted by the Shulchan Aruch Each word has the vav (6): 15 × 6 = 90 . Hashem's four-letter name is 26, plus its 4 letters, is 30 ; three times for the three paragraphs of the Shema = 90 \"It's quite rare for the Shulchan Aruch to incorporate not just the Zohar, but the reasons of the Zohar.\" The word אמת appears six times. The Abudarham explains: for the three paragraphs of the Shema and the three blessings around it. Everything we have said is true. The blessing speaks of the redemption from Egypt and at the Red Sea, and ends גאל ישראל : He redeemed us, and He will redeem us. Summary The core of tefillah is the Amidah, the Shema with its blessings, and Birkot HaShachar. Barechu (from the Mishnah) opens the communal part. Until the Rosh, the hazan said these blessings for everyone, which is why the Sephardi hazan reading every word aloud is historically correct. No Barechu at Mincha , which has no Shema. Barechu at the end is a late compensation for latecomers; per the Rivash and Shulchan Aruch , not said on Shabbat and Yom Tov (Spanish and Portuguese, Ashkenazim), though Oriental Sephardim say it following the Arizal. No need to stand ; Hacham Ben Zion Abba Shaul says not to bow. Creation, revelation, redemption , all in the present tense . Creation: \"creates everything\" softens Yeshayahu's \"creates evil,\" but \"darkness\" stays to reject dualism . Pray at the transitions of sunrise and nightfall . Kedushah alone: the Rosh permits, the Rambam requires a minyan; the Shulchan Aruch says to say it as reading verses. Revelation: personal and intimate, climaxing in \"who chooses His people Israel with love.\" Don't let gathering tzitzit distract. Ahavat Olam (Sephardim, Rambam, Rif) vs Ahavah Rabbah (Ashkenazim, Tosafot), a debate from the Gemara. Shema: 245 words; Hashem Elokeichem Emet makes 248 (Zohar, Shulchan Aruch). Ashkenazim say El Melech Ne'eman alone; Sephardim consider it a hefsek . Shema means understand. We cover our eyes to concentrate (Rambam) or because we cannot yet see Hashem's unity (Ramchal), like Yaakov saying Shema on meeting Yosef. Baruch Shem is quiet, except on Yom Kippur. Redemption: fifteen words of truth (Abudarham: the Temple steps; Zohar and Shulchan Aruch: 90, three times Hashem's name) and six times emet . Coming Up Next week , the final shiur of 2021: the depth of the Amidah , and the customs of its repetition .","1mHRYEfIR_Q":"Series: Prayer Series (Tiferet Eyal, 2021-22) Shiur: Part 2 of the six-part Prayer Series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom for Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 7 December 2021 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap and Plan Last time the Rabbi covered the origins of prayer as a whole, how prayer works if Hashem already knows our needs, women's obligation in prayer, and a general overview of what tefillah is. Tonight's task was large: to go from the very beginning of the morning prayers , the brachot on waking, all the way to Nishmat on Shabbat . The aim was to understand the sources of each part, the reasons we say them, and their relative importance . \"You think certain things are so ingrained, but actually you start to uncover their origins, and you start to recognise how some things are more important than others.\" Part 1: Modeh Ani, a 350-Year-Old Newcomer Open almost any prayer book and the first thing you find is מודה אני . When was it composed? Two thousand years ago? Three thousand? The first record we have of Modeh Ani is from the middle of the seventeenth century , about 350 years ago. In the old Spanish and Portuguese siddurim you will not find it at all. Hacham Gaguine , in the Keter Shem Tov , where he records the Spanish and Portuguese customs, writes that he looked through all the old siddurim and did not find it. The reason it was instituted: Modeh Ani has no mention of Hashem's name , so it may be said as soon as you wake up, before washing your hands. \"It's a lovely prayer, don't get me wrong,\" but it shows how something everyone now takes for granted can be quite late. Part 2: The Talmudic Morning Blessings Netilat Yadayim: why do we wash? Washing hands with a bracha in the morning is a Talmudic institution . But the Rishonim argue about the reason: View Reason for washing The Rambam During the night your hands touched parts of your body, and it is not appropriate to come before Hashem in prayer like that. \"It's got nothing to do with evil spirits.\" The Rashba , quoting the Zohar and kabbalistic sources A רוח רעה , an impure spirit, rests on the hands during sleep The Shulchan Aruch rules that we should be concerned about the ruach ra'ah . This is why people are careful not to walk out of the house before washing. Hacham Ovadia Yosef held that the whole house counts as one domain . As long as you don't leave the house, you may walk to the sink to wash. Some people keep a basin by their bed and won't take a step before washing: \"that's a lovely thing, but halachically it's not required.\" Asher Yatzar and Elokai Neshama אשר יצר is a Talmudic blessing, said whenever one goes to the toilet, typically in the morning. אלהי נשמה שנתת בי has its source in the Talmud, Berachot 60 . We thank Hashem for creating our soul and returning it to us. The Rabbi pointed out that Modeh Ani is a synopsis of Elokai Neshama . Both thank Hashem for returning the soul. But Elokai Neshama contains Hashem's name and cannot be said before washing. \"Whoever instituted Modeh Ani 350 years ago wanted you to begin straight away with the concept of Elokai Neshama as soon as you wake up.\" Birkot HaShachar Then come the morning blessings: פוקח עורים (opens the eyes of the blind), מתיר אסורים (releases the bound), זוקף כפופים (raises the bowed), מלביש ערומים (clothes the naked). We thank Hashem for our most basic gifts: to see, move, walk, stand straight, and have strength for the day. All of these are in the Talmud. (The blessing about being made a woman \"is a discussion in its own right,\" for another time.) Hanoten Laya'ef Koach: one blessing less One blessing, הנותן ליעף כח , \"who gives strength to the weary,\" is in every siddur in the world except one. It is not in the Talmud and not in the Spanish and Portuguese siddur . The Rabbi put Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim 46 on the screen. The code of Jewish law for Sephardim says \"black and white\" that we do not say this bracha, because it has no Talmudic source. So how did it spread? As Adrian guessed, through the Arizal , who held it was a correct blessing to make. It then spread across all the Middle Eastern Sephardim and is in every Middle Eastern siddur. The Spanish and Portuguese, however, still do not say it, \"because the Shulchan Aruch says not to do it, and the Spanish and Portuguese never follow Kabbalah.\" They say one blessing fewer each morning. She'asa Li Kol Tzorki on Yom Kippur and Tisha B'Av Jillian asked about communities that omit שעשה לי כל צרכי on a fast day. It is a halachic debate, the Rabbi said, and it concerns only Yom Kippur and Tisha B'Av , not other fasts. The blessing thanks Hashem for our basic need to walk and go out, which needs shoes. Since shoes are forbidden on those days, some omit it. Hacham Ovadia held that one does say it. The blessing itself is Talmudic either way. Birkot HaTorah The blessings over the Torah are clearly in the Talmud, in Berachot. The Rabbi cited the famous Gemara in Nedarim : the Beit HaMikdash was destroyed because לא ברכו בתורה תחילה , on the simple meaning, they did not say the blessings over the Torah. Learning Torah is not just an academic, intellectual pursuit. It is recognising that Hashem gave us a way to connect with and understand \"the mind of the Creator of the universe.\" The Gemara's text asks והערב נא , \"make it sweet for us.\" The Rabbi compared it to the saying: if you get a job you enjoy, you'll never have to work a day in your life. We ask that Torah be sweet in our mouths, so that it never feels like work. The order of importance After the Amidah and the Shema, the next most important part of Shacharit is Birkot HaShachar and Birkot HaTorah. These first two or three pages are Talmudically obligated. Everything after them, even Baruch She'amar, is not from the Talmud. \"Unfortunately, you see sometimes people come late and they just join in the middle. But it's not correct.\" If you come late, begin with these. And there is no point saying the whole prayer book while rushing the brachot of the morning. Part 3: What Does It Mean to Bless Hashem? Every blessing begins ברוך אתה ה׳ אלהינו מלך העולם . But how can we bless Hashem? He doesn't need our blessing. As always, the Rabbi said, look to the root. ב־ר־ך has two meanings in Tanach: ברך , berech , a knee : bending the knee, prostrating, acknowledging. ברכה , berechah , a pool or spring of water. That's why a swimming pool in modern Hebrew is a brecha : a collection of water. So when we say Baruch Atah Hashem we are not blessing Hashem. We are acknowledging that He is the source , the spring from which everything flows. Every time we benefit from something in this world, we acknowledge Him as the source of it all. The Maharal: just beyond one The Maharal of Prague noted that the letters of ברך are each \"just above one\": Letter Value ב 2 ר 200 כ 20 The idea of a bracha is that by recognising Hashem as the source of everything, we bring down more shefa , more energy and divine flow. Once you recognise the oneness of Hashem, the One, that brings down further abundance, \"just beyond the one.\" Part 4: Korbanot After Birkot HaShachar come the Korbanot, opening with the Akedah . The Rabbi asked: if you had only thirty seconds to say one paragraph between the Akedah and the Kaddish after Rabbi Yishmael, which would it be? Someone suggested the Ketoret : \"That I would say is second most important.\" The answer is פרשת התמיד , the daily offering: צו את בני ישראל ואמרת אלהם את קרבני לחמי לאשי . Why? The whole of Tefillat HaShachar corresponds to the morning Tamid . That is the reason we pray Shacharit at all. The Rabbi showed the Shulchan Aruch : after Birkot HaShachar and Birkot HaTorah, the only thing in this section he writes you must say is the Korban HaTamid . The rest is \"good to say.\" Saying the Tamid goes back to the Geonim , about 1,300 or 1,400 years, already in the siddur of Rav Amram Gaon . The Akedah and other passages are much later. The Tur in the fourteenth century records them as custom, which is why the Shulchan Aruch only says it is good to say them. Kabbalah places great importance on saying Shema Yisrael here too; according to the kabbalists one should say the Shema four times a day (twice in Shacharit, once in Arvit, once before sleep). That too is much later. Of course the verses of the Akedah are from the Torah, but saying them daily is a far later recommendation than the Tamid. Eizehu Mekoman and Rabbi Yishmael The Gemara in Kiddushin says a person should learn every day a bit of Chumash, a bit of Mishnah and a bit of Gemara . So we say: Requirement Fulfilled by Chumash The Korban HaTamid Mishnah איזהו מקומן , the chapter on the places of the sacrifices Gemara (Talmudic-style literature) רבי ישמעאל אומר , the thirteen principles of Torah interpretation Why choose Eizehu Mekoman from all the Mishnayot? On a simple level, because prayer replaces the sacrifices. But many chapters deal with sacrifices. \"Let me tell you something remarkable about Eizehu Mekoman. The chapter of Eizehu Mekoman, believe this or not, is the only chapter in the entire Mishnah which hasn't got an argument between the Rabbis.\" There is no \"Rabbi Meir says this, Rabbi Yehudah says that.\" No names at all, only halachot. As Hacham Gaguine brings in the Keter Shem Tov , we want to begin the day without machloket . Kaddish After the Korbanot we say Kaddish Al Yisrael . The principle: after rabbinic statements , Kaddish Al Yisrael; after verses , Kaddish Yehe Shelama. Part 5: Pesukei DeZimrah Everyone else calls this section Pesukei DeZimrah. Uniquely, among the Spanish and Portuguese the whole service up to this point is called Zemirot . But where does the practice of saying Baruch She'amar, Yishtabach and all the Hallelukahs in between come from? Not an obligation in the Talmud Two Gemarot indicate that it was not required daily practice: Shabbat 118b: Rabbi Yossi says, יהא חלקי מגומרי הלל בכל יום , \"May my portion be with those who complete Hallel every day.\" This doesn't mean the Hallel of Hanukkah, as is clear from context. It means reciting praise of Hashem. In the time of the Tannaim it was considered a fortunate thing to say these psalms daily, but not a requirement. Berachot 4b: whoever says Ashrei three times a day is guaranteed a portion in the World to Come . \"You don't give these guarantees to things which are obligatory.\" On Ashrei the Rabbi added: \"Nothing in Judaism works by recitals.\" Ashrei runs from aleph to tav. Saying it three times a day means living the whole day with the recognition, from A to Z, that Hashem is the source of everything. Someone who lives intimately with Hashem like that of course has a portion in the World to Come. In the time of the Geonim these Zemirot were the praiseworthy thing to say. By the Rishonim , about a thousand years ago, they had basically become obligatory. (Asked about Hodu , the Rabbi said where to place it is a later debate; Ashkenazim say it after Baruch She'amar, and it is really part of Pesukei DeZimrah.) Why praise comes first Pesukei DeZimrah opens with a bracha, Baruch She'amar , and closes with one, Yishtabach , with praises in between. As we learned last time, every prayer should begin with shevach , praise, before asking. The Amidah itself opens with three blessings of praise before the first request, for wisdom. Modelled on that, a whole section of praise precedes the prayer. Who wrote Baruch She'amar? The prevalent view among the later Rishonim: composed by one of the Geonim of the seventh or eighth century. The first text we have of it is from the ninth century . Another view: it goes back to the Men of the Great Assembly , about 2,300 years ago, written when a paper with the text fell from heaven. Robert asked how the Geonim could compose a bracha. \"An excellent question... something I need to think about.\" If it is from the Men of the Great Assembly there is no question. If it is Geonic, it is harder, but not strange: the Ashkenazi Rishonim composed many brachot for Yom Tov, as in the yotzrot . The long text and the 87 words In the original manuscripts Baruch She'amar is a very long text , and to the Rabbi's knowledge the only community that preserves it today is the Spanish and Portuguese . All other siddurim, Ashkenazi and Middle Eastern Sephardi, have a much shorter version of 87 words , because the Arizal held that kabbalistically it must contain 87 words, so it was cut down. The Rabbi pointed to the Middle Eastern siddurim, which on Shabbat print a passage in brackets before Baruch She'amar about Hashem giving Shabbat to Israel. That passage comes from the long Spanish and Portuguese text. Because it was so beautiful, someone placed it before the blessing, so that at least it would still be said. \"It just shows how much change we've had in the last 500 years based on the Arizal, and how the Spanish and Portuguese resisted all of these changes and kept to the original text, like we've seen with Birkot HaShachar, like we've seen now with Baruch She'amar.\" Holding the tzitzit During Baruch She'amar we hold the tzitzit together. The Rabbi was not sure of the origin of the minhag, but it is recorded as old in the Keter Shem Tov , and Hacham Gaguine explains it: Each corner has 8 strings and 5 knots = 13 . Two corners held together: 26 , the numerical value of Hashem's name. The word Baruch appears 13 times in Baruch She'amar, but Hashem's name only once. Holding the tzitzit, it is as if we say Hashem's name with them. What comes first if you are short of time? Within Pesukei DeZimrah there is Ashrei, which is very important, and the Shirah. (\"I'm not going to go through each of the Hallelukahs. That would be a whole series of shiurim.\") But if the choice is between Birkot HaShachar and Baruch She'amar/Yishtabach, there is no question: Birkot HaShachar , because they are from the Talmud. Pesukei DeZimrah or the Amidah with the minyan? If you come late, do you join the tzibur for the Amidah, or start from Baruch She'amar and miss the minyan? A thousand years ago, when Pesukei DeZimrah was just becoming established, the Amidah and Shema with the minyan clearly trumped it. But the later you go in the halachic responsa, the more ingrained Pesukei DeZimrah became, and the more the position was articulated that it is better to pray alone than to miss Pesukei DeZimrah. \"Of course we should say it every day. I'm not in any way coming to say that shouldn't be the case. What I'm saying is that you need to always recognise the source and origin of things, so you know their relevance, their importance relative to one another.\" Pesukei DeZimrah after the Amidah? Irene asked about saying the skipped Pesukei DeZimrah afterwards. \"I'm sorry to say, it's a bit ridiculous.\" Its whole purpose is to begin with praise: \"It's like you're about to play a football game. You go for stretches before. Pesukei DeZimrah are the stretching before the prayer. It's like you play the football game and then you go for your stretches afterwards.\" It is always good to stretch, and reading psalms is wonderful, but once you have prayed the Amidah, Pesukei DeZimrah is over. Part 6: Shabbat and Nishmat The paradox of the Shabbat prayers On a weekday the Amidah has 19 blessings ; on Shabbat only 7 . The Talmud says that on Shabbat the rabbis did not want to burden us ( tircha ) with a long prayer. Shabbat is not a time to start worrying about parnasah, health and wisdom. The paradox is that the Shabbat service is the longest of the week. But look at what was removed and what was added. The Hachamim removed requests , which put us under stress: \"I need this, God, give me that.\" What was added is praise, connection, intimacy with Hashem : the extra mizmorim of Shabbat, and among them Nishmat , which concludes Pesukei DeZimrah. The meaning of Nishmat Nishmat is \"a beautiful, beautiful prayer, one of the most beautiful parts of our service,\" and very emotional. It is a statement of the gap between mortal human beings and the Divine : \"we're simply not on the same playing field.\" We cannot grasp the extent of Hashem's kindness, the multitude of miracles and wonders He does for us at every moment. The Rabbi urged everyone to read it in English and understand what they are saying. Who wrote Nishmat? It is mentioned in the Gemara , so it is certainly very old. But its authorship is \"absolutely remarkable.\" 1. Yitzchak and Rivka. The Rabbi showed the text on screen: the first letters of four stanzas spell יצחק , and there is also רבקה . Some suggest an otherwise unknown author named Yitzchak, with Rivka, composed it with this acrostic. This is a common position in the Rishonim. 2. Rabbeinu Tam: Shimon Kefa, Saint Peter. Rabbeinu Tam , Rashi's grandson in the twelfth century, \"the greatest Ashkenazi Rishon,\" held that the author was Shimon Kefa , known as Saint Peter , based on the acrostic שמעון found in Ashkenazi siddurim. The Rabbi told the background. Peter lived in the first century and was one of Jesus' first disciples. The early Christians faced a question: did converts to Christianity also need to convert to Judaism, and keep how much? Some said full conversion; Paul said no circumcision and no dietary laws; Peter held they must keep the seven Noahide laws. A legend recorded in the eleventh or twelfth century (whether it is true \"you have to ask historians\") says Peter was a secret observant Jew , sent by the Hachamim to infiltrate the early church. The rabbis feared early Christianity resembled Judaism too much and would draw Jews towards it. His mission was to rise to influence and give Christianity its own identity: according to the legend, he moved the day of rest from Saturday to Sunday and instituted different festivals. To keep his secret connection with Judaism he composed Nishmat, and the Hachamim gave it a prominent place in recognition of his work. 3. The objection. The Machzor Vitry , also twelfth century, rejects this sharply: \"There are those who say concerning Peter that he established this prayer. God forbid, no such thing should occur in Israel. Anyone who says this, when the Temple is built, will have to bring a sin offering.\" There is also an opinion attributing it to Shimon ben Shetach . \"It's just fascinating\" that a Rishon as great as Rabbeinu Tam suggested Nishmat was written by one of the early Christian saints. \"Either way it's definitely a holy prayer, whichever opinion you hold... It's said in special times, and we should always make sure to try to say it with as much kavanah as possible.\" Summary Modeh Ani is a mid-seventeenth-century addition, absent from the old Spanish and Portuguese siddurim. It is a synopsis of Elokai Neshama (Berachot 60) without Hashem's name, so it can be said before washing. Netilat Yadayim: the Rambam (hygiene and dignity) vs the Rashba/Zohar ( ruach ra'ah ). The Shulchan Aruch takes the ruach ra'ah into account; Hacham Ovadia allows walking to the sink within the house. Birkot HaShachar are Talmudic, except Hanoten Laya'ef Koach , which the Shulchan Aruch (OC 46) says not to say. It spread through the Arizal; the Spanish and Portuguese still omit it. Order of importance: Amidah and Shema, then Birkot HaShachar and Birkot HaTorah. Latecomers should not skip them. Bracha means acknowledging Hashem as the source (knee, pool); the Maharal's \"just beyond one\" brings down shefa . Korbanot: the Tamid is the essential paragraph (Shulchan Aruch); Eizehu Mekoman is the one Mishnah chapter with no dispute (Keter Shem Tov); Chumash, Mishnah and Gemara daily (Kiddushin). Pesukei DeZimrah was not obligatory in the Talmud (Shabbat 118b; Berachot 4b), was praiseworthy for the Geonim and effectively obligatory by the Rishonim. Praise comes before requests, like stretching before a game. Baruch She'amar: Geonic (first text ninth century) or from the Men of the Great Assembly. The long original text survives only among the Spanish and Portuguese; others cut it to 87 words following the Arizal. Shabbat removes requests and adds praise. Nishmat expresses the gap between man and Hashem. Its author: Yitzchak and Rivka, Shimon ben Shetach, or, according to Rabbeinu Tam, Shimon Kefa (Saint Peter), which the Machzor Vitry vehemently rejects. Coming Up Next week: the Shema and the blessings before and after it. Same time, same Zoom link.","WZDbAc8G9b4":"Series: Prayer Series (Tiferet Eyal, 2021-22) Shiur: Part 1 of the six-part Prayer Series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom for Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 23 November 2021 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction: Back to Basics The Rabbi opened a new kind of series, one that \"goes down to the root of what we do on a daily basis.\" We often discuss important topics, he said, but forget the basics precisely because we are so used to doing them. Tefillah is something we do a lot, yet we don't always recognise its value, its importance and its role in our lives. There are people who have prayed for thirty, forty, fifty or sixty years, day in and day out, \"as halacha demands, correctly so,\" who still could not explain what the purpose of tefillah is, why they pray, or which parts of the prayer are the more important ones. Of course there are other advantages to coming to the Bet HaKnesset: we socialise, and maybe have a nice kiddush. \"But the reality is that we're coming to pray.\" So we need to understand three things: what we are doing, why we are doing it, and how it should be done. The plan for the series The series examines tefillah historically, and also tries to understand what is actually happening at each part of the prayer: Shiur Topic 1 Origins of prayer, women and prayer, how prayer works, and how prayer squares with Hashem's knowledge of what we need 2 Pesukei DeZimrah and Nishmat: their origins and their importance relative to the rest of tefillah 3 The Shema and its blessings: revelation and redemption, and how they connect 4 The Amidah, the root of prayer, and the customs surrounding its repetition 5 Tefillah and Torah: why we read the Torah in the middle of prayer, and children and women reading from the Torah 6 Musaf and the completion of prayer There would be no shiur the following week, which was Hanukkah. Part 1: Where Does Prayer Come From? Prayer throughout the Torah The concept of people praying runs through the whole Torah, and the Rabbi pointed to the parashiyot read in recent weeks: Avraham prays that the cities of Sedom should be saved. Yitzchak is praying in the field in the afternoon when Rivka arrives. Yaakov , at the beginning of Vayishlach, prays: הצילני נא , \"Please save me\" from my brother. Moshe prays for his sister Miriam when she is struck with tzara'at. Hannah prays for a child. So tefillah was clearly central in the lives of our patriarchs and matriarchs. Yet seemingly nowhere does Hashem tell us explicitly in the Torah that there is a mitzvah to pray. The Rambam and the Ramban This is a famous and central argument between the Rambam (Maimonides) and the Ramban (Nachmanides): is there a Torah obligation to pray? The Rambam points to the Gemara at the beginning of Masechet Ta'anit, which quotes the verse we say every day in the Shema, to love Hashem ולעבדו בכל לבבכם , \"and to serve Him with all your heart\": איזו היא עבודה שבלב? הוי אומר זו תפלה What is the service of the heart? You must say, this is prayer. We can serve Hashem with our hands, with our lips, with our minds. What service comes from the heart? Tefillah. So for the Rambam, when the Torah commands us to serve Hashem with our hearts, that is a biblical obligation to pray, and he counts it among the 613 mitzvot. The Rabbi drew out the implication. People often equate prayer with saying words and gathering together, but for the Gemara and the Rambam, prayer is an activity of the heart : \"with the heart and in the heart.\" You cannot just recite words without understanding them. He quoted the prophet Yeshayahu, where Hashem complains that \"with their lips they honoured Me, but their heart was far from Me.\" Hashem is not interested in that. \"The essence of prayer isn't what you say, but how you say it, and the engagement of the heart in the words of the prayer.\" The Ramban argued, pointing to a different Gemara in Berachot. Rabbi Elazar says: if you are in doubt whether you read the Shema, you must read it again; if you are in doubt whether you prayed, you do not pray again, and that is the halacha. You go to sleep at night and can't remember whether you said Shema: you say it again. But a doubt about whether you prayed? You don't repeat it. Why the difference? Because of a basic principle: Doubt about... Rule A mitzvah from the Torah ( de'oraita ) Be stringent and repeat it A mitzvah from the Hachamim ( derabanan ) No need to repeat it So Shema, which is biblical, must be repeated when in doubt, while tefillah, which is not repeated, must be rabbinic. How does the Ramban deal with the Gemara of \"service of the heart\"? He concedes that there is a Torah obligation to pray in a time of trouble : a war, an attack, any kind of tragedy or distress. That is undisputed, even by the Ramban. But the prayers we say on a regular basis are, for him, entirely rabbinic. The Rabbi found this striking. Ask a Jewish person what is central to Judaism and they will say Shabbat, kashrut, and probably prayer. The High Holy Days are all about prayer. And yet, according to the Ramban, regular prayer is only rabbinic. The Rambam: from the heart to a fixed text The Rambam adds an important qualification. Even though prayer is a Torah obligation, the Torah never tells us how much to pray. All it says is \"service of the heart.\" So for the Rambam the biblical obligation is once a day , at no specific time . Morning, afternoon and evening prayers were later rabbinic institutions. The Rabbi read from the Rambam (Hilchot Tefillah, chapter 1): It is a positive Torah commandment to pray every day, as it says, \"You shall serve Hashem your God.\" The number of prayers is not prescribed in the Torah, nor is there a specific formula, nor fixed times... When we were exiled among the nations, the speech of each person became a mixture of many tongues, and no one could express himself coherently in any one language... When Ezra and his court saw this, they established eighteen blessings in sequence. So, said the Rabbi, a set text for prayer is really a concession . In essence, we should be praying in our own tongue and our own words. The problem is that we are not always able to express ourselves properly, so Ezra fixed the eighteen blessings to be said daily. And, the Rabbi explained, once there was a fixed text, Ezra also fixed the times: Shacharit and Mincha, twice a day. \"Can you see where we're going? We're going from an obligation of the heart, which is completely free-spirited. It's up to you what you want to say, how you want to say it, when you want to say it... And then it ends up with an obligation two times a day with a set text.\" That is how we arrived at today's rigid framework, with exact times for when Shacharit and Mincha may start and end. For the Ramban, by contrast, there never was a biblical obligation. The rabbis established prayer to build our relationship with Hashem, and when they did, they set it for twice a day. For the Rambam, the obligation in its raw form is biblical, while its structure is rabbinic. Why it is called a Siddur This, said the Rabbi, is why our prayer book is called a Siddur , from the word seder , order. It gives order and structure to our prayer. \"Really, you don't need a Siddur. You can open your heart and pray wherever you want to pray.\" But it gives us a structure. \"It's important that we never forget: the root of prayer is the heart, and the root of prayer is something which really should be coming spontaneously.\" Part 2: Women and Prayer The Rambam's view has a major consequence. There is a principle that women are exempt from positive, time-bound mitzvot , which is why they are exempt from blowing shofar or shaking the lulav. But according to the Rambam, the biblical mitzvah of prayer can be done at any time of the day. It is therefore not time-bound , and on a biblical level there is no distinction at all between men and women. This is how the Rambam rules, and the Shulchan Aruch follows him: men and women have an equal obligation to pray. Women do not need to pray with a minyan, but those are later details. \"People often think prayer is about men,\" because men make the minyan, lead the service and run it. \"But the reality is that women and men have equal obligation to pray.\" The Rabbi called this \"very central to our belief, that women and men have the equal roles of reaching out to HaKadosh Baruch Hu.\" It is not that the men pray while the women are at home. The practical halacha The obligation is at least once a day, an Amidah. A woman can choose Shacharit, Mincha or Arvit. The consensus of the poskim is that women are not obligated to pray twice a day. Some opinions hold that once the rabbis instituted two prayers, they included women too, so if a woman can pray twice a day, that is lovely . But the main view is once a day. In extenuating circumstances , for example looking after children, lack of time, or being unable to pray in Hebrew or English, one can rely on the recital of Birkot HaShachar , from הנותן לשכוי בינה until the end of Birkot HaTorah . Some hold that this fulfils the bare requirement of tefillah. In short: in the first instance a woman should pray the Amidah at least once a day. If she cannot, the minimum is Birkot HaShachar. Part 3: The Four Kinds of Prayer Before tackling how prayer works, the Rabbi distinguished between the different things we do when we pray. Every part of the prayer, he said, falls into one of four categories : # Type What we do Daily example 1 Adoration / praise Exalt and sanctify Hashem, asking for nothing קדוש קדוש קדוש ; \"who forms light and creates darkness,\" who rolls away the night and brings the day 2 Thanksgiving ( hoda'ah ) Thank Hashem for what He has done מודים אנחנו לך ; the morning blessings, \"who opens the eyes of the blind\" 3 Penitence Speak about our own failures and ask forgiveness סלח לנו ; Tachanun after the Amidah 4 Petition ( bakasha ) Ask for what we need The middle blessings of the weekday Amidah He placed Hallel , hallel vehoda'ah , more in the category of thanksgiving. Why the congregation says its own Modim In the repetition of the Amidah we usually just listen and answer amen. There are two places where the congregation comes in: Kedushah ( Kadosh, Kadosh, Kadosh ), which is a new element of the prayer, and Modim . When the hazan reaches Modim, we say our own Modim, even though we already said it in our silent Amidah. It is the only part of the Amidah that the congregation says twice. Why? The hazan is our representative, praying on our behalf. \"Well, one thing you can't do on someone else's behalf is thank.\" You cannot answer amen and say, \"I agree with that.\" Thanksgiving must come from you, so we rely on no messenger for it. Petition is the smallest part The fourth kind, petition, is what we usually associate with prayer: \"I need to pray now\" means \"I need to ask God for something.\" But on Shabbat there is almost no petition at all, and even in a weekday Shacharit only about ten percent is actually asking. Pesukei DeZimrah are songs of praise; the Shema and its blessings ask for nothing; only the middle blessings of the Amidah are requests. The Rabbi's challenge: next time you pray, try to find any part of the prayer that doesn't fit one of these four categories. From the class Is Adon Olam a prayer? \"Not really. Adon Olam is a song.\" It is not an intrinsic part of tefillah and doesn't really fit any of the four categories, except perhaps praise. Jane mentioned being encouraged to add her own personal prayers, especially in difficult times. The Rabbi agreed that prayer is about your own heart, but pushed back gently on equating it with asking: \"Why can't prayer be where you turn to God in the middle of the day and say, God, thank you so much for everything You've done for me?\" Free prayer can be asking, thanking, or admitting, \"I was wrong in what I did earlier.\" A real prayer, as we will see in the Amidah, contains all four elements. Part 4: The Problem With Asking The first three types of prayer make sense, said the Rabbi, and the Rishonim were comfortable with them: Praise: not because Hashem needs our praises, but to remind ourselves that there is a force behind the universe and that He is in charge. Thanksgiving: we must never be ungrateful. Acknowledging Hashem for the good in our lives makes us more appreciative and grateful people, also towards other human beings. Penitence: we are human and make mistakes. We need a way to express remorse and say, \"I want to change, and I've decided to change.\" The one type the medieval Jewish and Islamic philosophers grappled with is petition . We believe in a God who is all good, just and benevolent, who wants the best for us. Then whatever situation we are in must be for the best. \"A person falls ill, and suddenly everyone says, let's pray for him. I don't understand. Do we believe God is just? So that means that person fell ill correctly.\" So you take out your Tehillim and start praying. For what? Hashem made this person ill or poor, for whatever reason, and that is what is best for him. \"Surely we are not changing Hashem's mind when we pray.\" And we certainly are not saying, \"Hashem thinks this is best, but I think not, so let me change His mind.\" He knows what is best for us and wants what is best for us. So why ask? Part 5: Rav Yosef Albo's Answer: The Doctor There are different answers, but the best, said the Rabbi, is that of Rav Yosef Albo , the fifteenth-century Spanish philosopher, in Sefer HaIkarim (the Book of Principles), section 4, chapter 18 , \"a worthwhile chapter to see.\" The parable A patient goes to the doctor, who tells him he is in a terrible state. After years of neglecting his health, the only way to keep him alive is to amputate both legs . The patient accepts that the doctor knows best, but asks: \"You've reached this decision based on how I've eaten and lived for years. Give me one month. Let me change my diet, my routine, my exercise.\" After a month: \"You look much better. We can get away with amputating one leg .\" He becomes even stricter. Next month: no amputation , just an operation. Next month: no operation , just tablets. He doesn't want the side effects of tablets, so he keeps going. Six months after the first visit: no amputation, no operation, no tablets . \"You can go home, you're a healthy, free man.\" Did the doctor want the best for his patient? Yes. Did he know what was best? Yes. Did he change his mind six times? Yes. Was he ever mistaken? No. \"The doctor was changing the diagnosis every month, but was never wrong.\" The lesson \"Prayer of petition is transformative. The purpose of prayer of petition is not to get God to change His mind. Rather, it's for the person to change oneself.\" Hashem looks at a person and decides that, in his current state, his diagnosis for the coming year is illness. When that person prays, the prayer transforms him. The decree was made on person X . \"You're now person Y. You've changed your essence, you've changed your identity, therefore you get now a different diagnosis.\" The text of the Ikarim The Rabbi read the passage in translation: The influences from above come down upon the recipient when he is in a certain degree and state of preparation to receive them. If a person does not prepare himself, he withholds the good from himself. For example, if it has been determined from on high that a person's crop should prosper in a given year, and he neglects to plough or sow his land, then God may bring the most abundant rain, but his crops will not prosper, since he did not plough or sow. Every good decreed is conditional on a certain degree of right conduct, and every evil decreed is conditional on a certain degree of wickedness or predisposition to it. Albo compares it to a king who decrees that all the uncircumcised in a country should be killed, or should receive gold: anyone who has himself circumcised is no longer included. And to the objection that the divine will cannot be changed by prayer, he answers that the divine will from the outset was that the decree should stand if the person stays as he is, and change if he changes. Part 6: How Does Prayer Transform Us? The Rabbi gave two ways. 1. Self-scrutiny: the meaning of lehitpalel The verb להתפלל is reflexive, from the root פ־ל־ל . A hit- verb in Hebrew is something you do to yourself: להתרחץ , to wash oneself; להתלבש , to dress oneself (one of the participants, Eva, supplied examples); להתרחק , to distance oneself. The only place in Tanach where the verb is not reflexive is in Tehillim, ויעמד פינחס ויפלל , \"Pinchas stood up and prayed.\" פלילים are judges. So lehitpalel literally means to judge yourself, to examine yourself . When we ask for something, prayer becomes an opportunity for introspection: Praying for wisdom , the first request of the Amidah: am I doing everything within my capability to attain wisdom? Praying for health : am I doing everything I can to live healthily? Praying for parnasah : am I being too lazy? Praying שמע קולנו , \"hear our voice, have mercy on us\": am I being merciful and kind to other people? 2. Relationship When you pray for wisdom, you acknowledge that Hashem is the source of wisdom. Imagine a son who needs money and food and goes to ask his uncle. When the father finds out, he is hurt: \"Why didn't you come and ask me?\" We often get upset when people we are close to don't ask us for favours. Asking is a sign of relationship. The Rabbi cited \"the famous idea\" of the snake . After the sin, the snake was given what seems like the best livelihood: it eats the dust and never has to work again. But it never once needs to ask, so it has no relationship with Hashem. Human beings forge that relationship by asking, and in doing so transform ourselves. Part 7: Praying for Someone Else All of this works well when a person prays for himself. But the Rabbi is often asked, \"Can you pray for this person?\" How does that work? First: \"Nothing beats prayer for yourself. However great the rabbi is, nothing beats prayer for yourself, because ultimately it's down to you.\" But when you ask someone to pray for you, the one praying is transformed, raising himself through introspection and relationship. Because we are all intertwined, and that change came about through your request , you have an indirect contribution to his transformation. That can bring about a different decree for the person in need. A caution: \"I don't want you to go away from tonight and start thinking about every prayer and trying to measure how much you've changed.\" This is a broad, philosophical understanding of prayer, not something to calculate each time we pray. Questions at the End Did the Jews lead the way in prayer? Every religion has some form of it. Yes, said the Rabbi. Prayer goes back to Avraham, one of the first human beings, at the very beginning, so other religions with the concept of prayer must have taken it from us. But they emphasise different aspects. In Christianity, for example, prayer is very much about the penitential element, recognising one's mistakes. \"Prayer is a broad term. There's four different aspects to it.\" What about Shema for women? Women are not obligated in Shema, because it is a time-bound mitzvah, said in the morning and evening. It is a good thing to say it and remember Hashem, \"but really, if you had a choice between Shema and Amidah, 100% Amidah would be better.\" (The questioner had first thought the minimum was \"the blessings\"; the Rabbi clarified that the minimum is the Amidah, with Birkot HaShachar as the substitute when one cannot.) Summary Origins: prayer appears throughout the Torah, from Avraham to Hannah. For the Rambam , prayer is a Torah mitzvah , avodah shebalev , the service of the heart (Ta'anit). For the Ramban , regular prayer is rabbinic , with a Torah obligation only in times of trouble (from the Gemara in Berachot on a doubt whether one prayed). The halacha follows the Rambam. From the heart to the Siddur: the Torah obligation is once a day, at any time, in any words. Ezra fixed eighteen blessings because exile made it hard for people to express themselves, and fixed times followed. A set text is a concession; the root of prayer is the heart. Women: since the biblical mitzvah is not time-bound, women are obligated to pray just like men (Rambam, Shulchan Aruch): an Amidah at least once a day, or Birkot HaShachar in extenuating circumstances. Four kinds of prayer: praise, thanksgiving, penitence and petition. Petition is only about ten percent of Shacharit. We say our own Modim because no one can thank on your behalf. Why ask? Rav Yosef Albo (Ikarim 4:18): like the doctor whose diagnosis changes as the patient changes, prayer does not change Hashem's mind; it changes us , and the decree applies to who we are. How prayer transforms: lehitpalel means to judge oneself (self-scrutiny), and asking builds a relationship with Hashem (unlike the snake). Praying for others works indirectly, but nothing beats praying for yourself. \"Prayer is from the heart. Prayer is a biblical obligation on a daily basis. Prayer is about self-change. And prayer is at its best when you do it for yourself.\" Coming Up Next week: no shiur, as it is Hanukkah. Happy Hanukkah. In two weeks: the history of Pesukei DeZimrah , the songs of praise, and Nishmat .","BTfTaLQ1Qn4":"Series: Kashrut, a six-part course at Tiferet Eyal, Hendon Shiur: Part 6 of the six-part Kashrut course, given by Dayan Daniel Kada at Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 9 July 2019 (the penultimate session) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction \"Welcome back to our penultimate shiur. We've got lots to cover today.\" The plan was five topics: meat, milk, cheese and butter, produce from Israel, and eating out in a vegan restaurant . The following week would be a practical bug-checking session, with Rabbi Tobin joining to give a live demonstration. In the event, the class ran over time and the vegan restaurant topic was not reached. Part 1: Meat Terefah: \"torn\" We know which animals are kosher and that they need shechita. Where does the word taref come from? From the Torah (Shemot 22:30): וּבָשָׂר בַּשָּׂדֶה טְרֵפָה לֹא תֹאכֵלוּ Flesh torn in the field you shall not eat. Terefah means torn : an animal mauled by another animal. Shechita must be done on a living animal; once it is dead (a nevelah ), shechita is useless. The Hachamim explain, also from the Torah, that an animal that will not live another 12 months counts as terefah . The same principle appears elsewhere: if false witnesses testify that a man broke Shabbat and deserves death, and are caught lying, they are normally executed themselves. But if their victim was a person who would anyway die within 12 months, they are not, because \"you tried to kill someone who is already dead.\" Checking the lungs The Hachamim identified many defects (the Rabbi spoke of 72 ) which mean an animal will probably die within the year. But we do not need to check for them after shechita. We rely on chazakah , presumption: the animal stood before us looking healthy. Only if you see something, such as a broken leg, must you check. There is one exception , because it is relatively common: the lungs . A puncture in the lung makes the animal terefah , so the lungs must be physically checked after every shechita . A puncture is often covered by a sirchah , an adhesion or scab. Can the inspector gently peel it off , feel that the lung underneath is smooth, then inflate the lung and see whether air escapes? If it holds, there was no puncture. Which part of the lung? First, a disagreement between the Shulchan Aruch and the Rama about where on the lung a sirchah invalidates the animal. Surprisingly, here Sephardim are more lenient: the Shulchan Aruch's area is smaller than the Rama's. In practice, however, all kashrut authorities follow the Rama's wider area . \"Even the most Sephardi kashrut authority\" will not supply meat with an adhesion in the area that the Shulchan Aruch permits but the Rama forbids. May one peel off the adhesion? The Shulchan Aruch (Yoreh De'ah 39:10) writes very strongly against it. The Rabbi read it out \"because people aren't aware of the severity of this issue\": wherever adhesions of the lung are forbidden, there is no difference between small and thick, and one may not rely on those who peel it away by hand and find the lung intact. Anyone who relies on this is feeding people non-kosher food , since the puncture may simply have healed over. The Rama writes that there are those who permit rubbing and peeling adhesions, and that the custom is to be lenient . Three levels of meat \"People don't realise. They think there are only two levels, Ashkenazim and Sephardim. There are actually three.\" Level Meaning Kosher (no adjective) Possibly from an animal whose adhesions were peeled off and the lung inflated, following the Rama's leniency Glatt (Yiddish, \"smooth\") No real adhesions. The Rama allows up to one or two ririn , tiny mucus-like protrusions that can be peeled away. Better, but still relies on this Halak Beit Yosef (Hebrew chalak , \"smooth\") The entire lung completely smooth , with nothing at all protruding, following the Shulchan Aruch, who rejects peeling even \"the smallest thing\" Glatt and halak both mean \"smooth,\" but they refer to different standards. Halak Beit Yosef is the higher one, and \"we Sephardim have a duty to follow this. I'm not sparing my words here. This is a duty to follow. There's no reason why we can't.\" The Rabbi mentioned two London butchers he uses that sell Halak Beit Yosef meat, Menachem's and Kosher Deli , adding that there are surely others. Ashkenazim who do not buy it there are not opposed to Halak Beit Yosef; some simply want a different hechsher. In America there are Chassidic hechsherim that are Halak Beit Yosef. Tip: if you cannot get Halak Beit Yosef, lamb is the best choice. Young animals are the least likely to be terefah , unlike an older cow or ox. \"Glatt chicken\" Glatt only applies to meat , to the lungs of cattle. \"Glatt chicken\" is a misnomer. \"Glatt bean sprouts\" or \"glatt lettuce\" is borrowing the word to mean a higher level of supervision. \"What they really should be advertising is halak lettuce, because that's the highest level.\" Chickens do not need to be glatt. Invited to an Ashkenazi home or a function You are invited to an Ashkenazi family, or to a Sephardi family that does not keep this, or to a wedding or function (even under the strictest Ashkenazi hechsher). The meat is not Halak Beit Yosef. May you eat it? Chacham Ovadia Yosef has a teshuvah on this. The question was first raised about 400 years ago by the Devar Shmuel (Rabbi Shmuel Abuhav). The answer is that it is permitted because of a sfek sfeika , a double doubt: Ordinary kosher meat does not necessarily come from an animal that had adhesions peeled off. Many of the animals will have been completely smooth. You do not know which this is. Even if it was, perhaps the halachah follows the Rama , who permits it. So, while not in the first instance, when invited to someone's home or a wedding, you may eat the meat . Some will not, but there is what to rely on, as long as it is not regular. A participant asked about restaurants . The Rabbi distinguished being invited from actively going out to buy non-Halak Beit Yosef meat, and was not sure the leniency extends that far. Again, it depends how often one does it. Three places where eating together overrides the stringency The Rabbi noted that in the course they had now met three places where Chacham Ovadia allows one community to eat at another's table, even though the food is forbidden to it, so long as it is not a regular practice: Bishul Akum : Sephardim may eat by Ashkenazim, even though Sephardim normally require a Jew to put the pot on. Kitniyot on Pesach : Ashkenazim may eat by Sephardim, even from pots in which rice was cooked. Meat : Sephardim may eat non-Halak Beit Yosef meat by Ashkenazim. Where do all these different rulings come from? Not from the Shulchan Aruch or Rama inventing them, but from different interpretations of the Gemara: the Shulchan Aruch follows the Rif and the Rambam, the Rama follows the European Rishonim such as Tosafot. Part 2: Milk Kosher animal, kosher milk Whatever comes from a kosher animal is kosher; whatever comes from a non-kosher animal is not. Cow, goat and sheep milk is kosher. Camel, pig, horse and even whale milk is not. The Rabbi had looked into pig's milk: pigs do produce milk, rich in fat (around 8.5% compared with about 3.9% for cows) and nutritious. But it is rarely found, because sows have about fourteen teats and do not like being milked. Camel milk is somewhat more common. Chalav Akum and Chalav Yisrael Since milk from different animals cannot be told apart once milked, the Hachamim, already in the Gemara, decreed that a Jew must see the milking , from the milking until the bottling. Term Meaning Chalav Akum Milked by a non-Jew with no Jew present: forbidden rabbinically , like Bishul Akum Chalav Stam Unsupervised milk from a place with reliable government regulation (Rav Moshe Feinstein): see below Chalav Yisrael Milked by a Jew, or by a non-Jew with a Jew present from milking to bottling A farm with no non-kosher animals Several centuries ago there was a debate: what if a farm or area has no non-kosher animals at all ? The Pri Chadash (Jerusalem) held that such milk is permitted. The Chatam Sofer and most poskim disagreed: the decree is comprehensive, and a Jew must be present regardless. Rav Moshe Feinstein: anan sahadi On that basis, supermarket milk from Tesco or Sainsbury's would be forbidden. Along came Rav Moshe Feinstein : today, with government legislation , a company caught mixing in non-kosher milk would face huge fines and would go bust. Why does that make it permitted? Not simply because the company fears for itself, but because of the halachic principle of anan sahadi , \"we are witnesses\": something so clear it is as if we testified to it. The Rabbi's example from marriage: one way to effect kiddushin is through marital relations. The witnesses do not see the act itself; they see the couple secluded in a locked room for long enough, and that suffices, because it is as if we witnessed the act. So too here: with strict regulation and heavy penalties, it is as if a Jew witnessed the milking . This is chalav stam . Some say Rav Moshe did not really mean it. \"He definitely did mean it.\" The Rabbi read Rav Moshe's words: there is a clear reason to be lenient , and indeed it is permitted; but a ba'al nefesh , someone especially spiritually sensitive, should be stringent. The Chazon Ish (Rav Avraham Yeshayahu Karelitz of Bnei Brak) held the same, from a different angle. He liked the Pri Chadash's reasoning about a place without non-kosher animals, and added that if non-kosher milk is more expensive , there is no reason to add it. He too said a ba'al nefesh should be stringent. Rabbi Yosef Mashash of Morocco wrote that in Morocco everyone was lenient. Chacham Ovadia: stringent where it is easy Chacham Ovadia Yosef and his son are stringent. If Chalav Yisrael is readily available, one should get it. If it is difficult, one may rely on Rav Moshe. Living in Golders Green or Hendon , where kosher milk is easy to get, there is no reason to be lenient. \"Out of the area\" does not mean Bristol. It means somewhere without a kosher shop open all hours: if you wake up and there is no milk, Chacham Ovadia would be lenient. Powdered milk and chocolate Chacham Ovadia rules that powdered milk is fine , for two reasons: It can apparently only be made from kosher animals' milk (pig's milk, a participant noted, does not curdle). The decree was on milk itself . Powdered milk may have changed its identity, and the decree was not extended to it. So chocolates made with milk powder are permitted according to him, as well as according to Rav Moshe. Many disagree, which is why many are stringent. Don't call it non-kosher \"Personally, I'll tell you, I'm particular to have Chalav Yisrael.\" But once in a hotel, with no Chalav Yisrael available, he used the hotel's milk (in clean glass or plastic, with cold milk; not from a mug that might have been used for who knows what). The Rabbi stressed the importance of understanding the difference between a chumrah and the basic halachah. He told a true story of a schoolchild who was told that eating a Mars bar is like eating non-kosher food . When he later saw people eating Mars bars, he went and ate actual non-kosher food. A Mars bar, according to Chacham Ovadia, contains powdered milk, not real milk. \"We don't make up our own gedarim .\" His practical guidance: Milk itself : if you live in the area, buy Chalav Yisrael . Chocolates such as KitKat, Twix and Mars: those who refrain should continue, \"and that's great.\" But those who do not have plenty to rely on. Yoghurt has the same status as milk. Within the EU , and in America , one may rely on chalav stam because of strict regulation. Outside these areas (he wondered about places like Mexico, Argentina or China), one would have to check. Video surveillance instead of a person present: a big discussion in the last 20 to 30 years. Rav Elyashiv and Rav Wosner both permitted it; different agencies have different standards. \"I'm not coming up in the shiur thinking those who keep Chalav Yisrael can go and have [chalav stam]. That's not my intent, absolutely not.\" The aim was to explain how everything works and why some are stringent and some are lenient. (A participant asked about milk from a cow that is itself terefah , for example from infection. Milk from an animal that will die within 12 months is indeed not kosher. But as with meat, we rely on chazakah and need not check unless we know otherwise.) Part 3: Cheese and Butter Why non-Jewish cheese is forbidden The Mishnah says cheese of non-Jews is forbidden, and the Gemara gives seven reasons . Most no longer apply, for example non-kosher milk in the holes of the cheese, the surface smeared with lard, or non-kosher vinegar. The main reason, Shmuel's , codified in the Shulchan Aruch: cheese is made by adding an enzyme, rennet , taken from the stomach lining of an animal, which may be non-kosher . A drop that is never nullified The rennet is a tiny amount, far less than one in sixty. Normally that would be nullified. But rennet is a davar hama'amid , it is what forms the cheese, and such a thing is never nullified, not in sixty and not in a thousand. The Rabbi compared it to a whole insect : a beriyah , a complete creature, is never nullified. If a whole insect falls into a huge pot of food and is lost, the whole pot must be thrown out, even if it is a million times bigger. If you can see it, simply take it out. And you may not chop it up deliberately to nullify it. A Jew may not cause nullification on purpose; a non-Jew producing food for himself may. Microbial rennet does not help In many places in Europe, cheese is still made with animal rennet, so it definitely needs a hechsher. In the USA, and apparently in England, much cheese is made with microbial rennet or even thistle . Surely that solves the problem? No. This was already discussed in the times of the Gemara, and the Rambam and Shulchan Aruch rule that even if you know for certain the cheese was set with herbs or another non-animal enzyme, it is still forbidden . The Hachamim made a comprehensive decree on all non-Jewish cheese. What makes cheese \"Jewish\"? Rama: supervision by a Jew is enough. Shulchan Aruch (and this is how the kashrut authorities operate): supervision is not enough. Unlike milk, a Jew, the mashgiach, must personally put the rennet in . He must be part of the making of the cheese. Soft and cream cheese Rav Moshe Feinstein held that the decree applies only to hard cheese . Soft cheese and cream cheese can set from their own acidity without rennet, so they were never included, and many institutions follow this. Provided the ingredients are kosher , such cheese does not need a hechsher. The OU on some Philadelphia cream cheese means only that the ingredients are kosher; no Jew is involved in making it. At the time, kashrut authorities in England were looking into certifying cream cheeses on the same basis. \"Soft\" means properly soft. A cheese like Cheddar is still hard cheese for this purpose. Butter The Shulchan Aruch discusses whether the Hachamim extended the cheese decree to butter , and rules that where the custom is to be lenient, one may be lenient . The custom is that they did not extend it. But take care: non-kosher fats , even lard , are sometimes added to butter. It must be pure butter , with nothing added. So in a hotel, butter with pure ingredients, for example the small individual portions, is acceptable. Part 4: Produce from Israel Produce grown in Israel requires terumot and ma'asrot . If you own a farm or vineyard in Israel, or buy without a hechsher there, you must separate them; a good hechsher takes care of it. But do you need to take terumot and ma'asrot from an Israeli avocado bought at Tesco in England ? Everyone agrees that produce which left Israel before it became obligated needs nothing. If you picked an apple and took it straight to England, it is exempt. Produce becomes obligated at the stage of miru'ach , when the harvested heap is piled and smoothed over. Commercial produce has almost certainly reached that stage during production. The question is whether an obligation that already took effect falls away once the produce leaves the Land . Chacham Ovadia brings many proofs and many poskim who rule that it does. The Rabbi read his words: even though they were processed and smoothed over in Israel, they are exempt from terumot and ma'asrot. Shemittah is different. The holiness of seventh-year produce is intrinsic and does not leave with it. In a Shemittah year one must be careful not to buy Shemittah produce. The Rabbi began to suggest that, faced with peaches from Spain and peaches from Israel , one might buy the Spanish ones to avoid the dispute, but then reconsidered: perhaps one wants to support Israeli produce . \"You have to make a decision with that.\" Ashkenazim do take terumot and ma'asrot from Israeli produce abroad; the Federation, for example, has a whole system for it. Summary Terefah means \"torn\"; an animal that will die within 12 months is terefah . We rely on chazakah for most defects but must check the lungs after every shechita. Three levels of meat : plain kosher (adhesions peeled), glatt (up to two tiny ririn ), and Halak Beit Yosef (completely smooth). Sephardim should buy Halak Beit Yosef ; lamb is the safest alternative. When invited to an Ashkenazi home or a wedding, a Sephardi may eat ordinary kosher meat because of a double doubt (Chacham Ovadia, following the Devar Shmuel), but not as a regular practice. Milk: Chalav Akum is rabbinically forbidden. Rav Moshe Feinstein permits chalav stam where regulation is strict ( anan sahadi ), but a ba'al nefesh is stringent. Chacham Ovadia: buy Chalav Yisrael where it is easily available; powdered milk and milk chocolate are permitted. Cheese: forbidden because of rennet, a davar hama'amid , even when made with non-animal rennet. For Sephardim, a Jew must add the rennet himself. Soft and cream cheese are exempt (Rav Moshe Feinstein) if the ingredients are kosher. Butter from non-Jews is permitted if it is pure , with no added fats. Israeli produce bought abroad is exempt from terumot and ma'asrot (Chacham Ovadia), but Shemittah produce is not. Next week, the final session: a practical bug-checking demonstration with Rabbi Tobin.","uugHqfMR0-8":"Series: Kashrut, a six-part course at Tiferet Eyal, Hendon Shiur: Part 5 of the six-part Kashrut course, given by Dayan Daniel Kada at Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 25 June 2019 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction After the philosophy of kashrut, vegetarianism, meat and milk, and Tevilat Kelim, the Rabbi turned to \"a slightly contentious topic\": hechsherim , what a kashrut certificate actually does and when it is needed, followed by: Bishul Akum : food cooked by a non-Jew, typically in liquid. Pat Akum : food baked by a non-Jew, dry. Unlike last week's Tevilat Kelim, which is a Torah mitzvah, everything tonight is a rabbinic enactment . Part 1: Why Did the Hachamim Forbid It? The Rambam writes: There are some foods that the Hachamim prohibited, even though there is no biblical basis for the prohibition... in order to prevent Jews from mingling with non-Jews socially, so they will not come to intermarry with them. Imagine meat that is perfectly kosher: a kosher animal, correct shechita, blood removed, kosher pots, all in your own kitchen. Or something naturally kosher, like potatoes or wheat. If a non-Jew cooks or bakes it for you, there may still be a problem. A participant objected that the intermarriage concern hardly applies to a 75-year-old happily married with children. The Rabbi answered that if everyone thought that way, nobody would keep anything: \"Everybody can have an exception.\" A rabbinic decree is made comprehensively. A second reason Other Rishonim give another reason: a non-Jew may not appreciate how careful one must be , and could feed you something non-kosher. Some non-Jews have worked with Jewish families for years and know exactly what is needed. Others might have just finished a pork sandwich and not washed their hands. The difference: a non-religious Jew Can a Jew who is not observant cook for you? By the Rambam's reason (intermarriage), there is no problem. There is no prohibition on marrying a non-observant Jew. By the second reason (lack of sensitivity), the same concern could apply. Chacham Ovadia Yosef has a long teshuvah on this. His conclusion: one may rely on a non-religious Jew to light the fire or cook, though ideally, to satisfy all opinions, a religious Jew should do it. That is why many kashrut agencies employ religious staff for this. But at home, if a relative who is not religious cooks for you, there is plenty to rely on. Part 2: What Does a Hechsher Actually Do? \"Many people are brought up thinking that everything is non-kosher until you see a kosher stamp. The truth is the absolute opposite. Almost everything is kosher. \" Apart from meat, poultry and fish , where the Torah gives signs for which species are kosher and requires shechita, nearly everything is kosher in principle. The main exceptions are wine, cheese, milk and eggs (some of which would be covered in a later shiur). The problem is processing Modern food is ultra-processed, full of flavourings, oils and ingredients of unknown origin, often extracted from forbidden sources: Some sweets used to be coloured or flavoured with cochineal , made from a beetle. Flavourings are a particular problem: even a tiny amount that gives taste is never nullified ( batel ) in sixty. \"Vegetable oil\" can include oils derived from fish , possibly non-kosher fish. Named oils such as olive oil or sunflower oil are fine. When you can read the ingredients In a country where you can trust the ingredient lists (England and America definitely; France and Germany too; Spain he was unsure about), if you go through every ingredient and know for certain each one is kosher, there is no problem eating it without any hechsher . That is exactly what the KLBD food guide does: A product with the KLBD logo means they have a contract with the manufacturer, can go in, and must be notified of any change . That is a higher level. The majority of products listed have no logo but are still listed as kosher. That simply means they have checked the ingredients . They have no contact with the manufacturer. What about the same production line being used for pork? First, at big companies, with modern health and safety and cleanliness, it is highly unlikely. Second, even if it happened, it is not there to give flavour, so it would be nullified in sixty . But if you know it is happening, you cannot rely on nullification. Juices and smoothies You can buy orange juice at Asda or Tesco with no hechsher, because the ingredients are clear. But watch smoothies : many now use grape juice as a cheaper sweetener instead of sugar, and grape products are a kashrut issue. A participant mentioned that a particular brand's smoothies had been flagged as not acceptable even without grapes. The Rabbi had not heard this and said he would look into it; perhaps the authorities had found a specific problem on that production line. The vegan restaurant Previewing a later shiur, the Rabbi mentioned that someone involved in kashrut supervision had discovered that a vegan restaurant was using breadcrumbs that had been immersed in pig fat . That is for flavour, so it is not nullified. Vegan does not mean kosher. Even when all the ingredients are kosher , there remains the question of Bishul Akum and Pat Akum . Part 3: Bishul Akum: Two Conditions If any food cooked by a non-Jew were forbidden, one could never eat anything cooked by a non-Jew. So the Hachamim limited the decree to food that meets both conditions: It cannot be eaten raw ( eino ne'echal kemot shehu chai ). It is fit to be served at a royal table ( oleh al shulchan melachim ). Condition 1: not eaten raw If the food could have been eaten raw, the non-Jew has not done you a great favour by cooking it, so it will not bring people together. Vegetable soup of water, carrots and tomatoes: permitted, since they can be eaten raw. Tea and coffee : permitted. The main ingredient is water , which you could drink without him. Eggs : forbidden. Chazanim sometimes eat raw eggs for their voice, as noted in a number of places in halachah, but that is not the normal way. Fish today. Twenty years ago, salmon cooked by a non-Jew was forbidden. Today, with sushi and sashimi , raw fish has become the norm, so in England fish is basically no longer subject to Bishul Akum . These halachot depend on time and place. Asked about raw meat dishes, the Rabbi said that is still a specific, uncommon form of eating; if it became widespread, \"things will change, 100%.\" These are among the hardest halachot to define, because they depend on the norms of a place. Condition 2: a royal table The Chazon Ish and Rabbi Shimon Schwab. In the 1950s Rabbi Schwab visited the Chazon Ish, who was upset that many people in Europe were eating canned sardines , cooked by non-Jews. Rabbi Schwab answered that sardines would never be served at a royal banquet. The Chazon Ish replied that, from what he had heard, the kings of England eat sardines for breakfast . \"I don't know where he got that from.\" For him, if royalty eat it at all, it is \"fit for kings.\" The accepted view. Rabbi Schwab's response, which is how Chacham Ovadia rules and what virtually all kashrut agencies follow: the Gemara says shulchan melachim , the table of kings in the plural, meaning banquets , formal dinners where distinguished people enjoy each other's company. You will never get canned sardines there. The practical test is: would it be served at a special dinner, such as a wedding? Food that wealthy or aristocratic people eat privately does not count. So chickpeas, corn snacks, cornflakes, popcorn are permitted, provided the ingredients are kosher. Canned food Canned food is not served at royal banquets. But it depends how it was made. If the food was cooked before canning , it was already cooked by a non-Jew before it reached the can. The Rabbi looked into how John West tuna is made: it is cooked for two to four hours first, then canned and steamed. That would normally be a problem, but since tuna is eaten raw today, it is not a Bishul Akum issue. Nevertheless, he advised buying tuna with a hechsher , for a different reason: there are eleven species called tuna. Most canned tuna is skipjack , which is kosher, but one cannot be 100% certain. Canned fruit, canned corn : permitted. Canned potatoes : probably fine, if they are cooked in the can, but check. Crisps: the variety or the food? A big discussion among the Acharonim: what if a food is \"royal\" in general, but this form of it is not? Crisps are made of potatoes, and potatoes (baked, roasted, deliciously cooked) are served at royal tables. Some poskim say every form of the food is therefore included. The final halachah , again followed by the agencies, is that we look at each specific food : A non-Jew may not bake or cook you potatoes . But crisps are not served at royal banquets, so crisps such as Walkers are permitted if the ingredients are kosher. Some are stringent. Quick reference Permitted if cooked by a non-Jew (ingredients kosher) Must be cooked by a Jew Tea, coffee, beer (water is the main ingredient) Meat , chicken Vegetable soup; carrots, green peas, cabbage, mushrooms Potatoes Fish today (eaten raw) Eggs Popcorn, crisps, cornflakes, snacks, chocolate Rice (not eaten raw) Canned corn, canned fruit Beetroot , now often served raw in restaurants On beer : Tosafot in Avodah Zarah records that people regularly drank beer with non-Jews. On cooked beetroot : factory-cooked plain beetroot (for example from Asda) is fine. Beetroot cooked in the back of a greengrocer's is not, because who knows what else was cooked in those pots. Part 4: The Au Pair at Home \"Unfortunately I think lots of people have stumbled with this.\" Many families have non-Jewish cleaners, au pairs or housekeepers, and ask them to cook chicken, meat or rice for the children. That is Bishul Akum. What can you do? It is not enough to switch the fire on in the morning and let her cook all day. Shulchan Aruch (Sephardim): at the very least, you light the fire and put the pot on . That is a significant enough contribution for it to count as bishul Yisrael . If she put the pot on, you stirring it and taking part in the cooking can also help. Foods that are not subject to Bishul Akum (those eaten raw, or not \"royal\") she may cook entirely by herself. Restaurants: a problem for Sephardim Ashkenazim (Rama): it is enough for a Jew to light the fire . Many commercial kitchens now have pilot lights that are lit once every few months and stay on permanently, which is cheaper than switching off. Sephardim: a Jew must put each pot on the fire , which is difficult to arrange in a restaurant. So even in restaurants in Golders Green, Hendon or anywhere in England with the best hechsher, there is a problem for Sephardim. Chacham Ovadia was asked and wrote that if necessary one may be lenient , combining: The doubt whether the halachah follows the Shulchan Aruch or the Rama on putting the pot on. An opinion among the Rishonim (not followed on its own) that cooking done commercially , as a business rather than personally for you, is not Bishul Akum at all. So in practice people do eat out, though some will not because of this. One simple step: if you order an egg , go in and put it on yourself, and it is fine even according to the Shulchan Aruch. Kashering after Bishul Akum If a non-Jew cooked meat in your pot when he should not have, the pot needs kashering . Interestingly, even an earthenware pot, which normally cannot be kashered and must be discarded, may be kashered by hagalah here, because Bishul Akum is only rabbinic. Part 5: Pat Akum: Bread Baked by a Non-Jew Bread is a staple , so there are many more leniencies. Three categories: Term Meaning Status Pat Yisrael Baked by a Jew Permitted Pat Ba'al HaBayit Baked by a non-Jew for his own family , then offered to you Always forbidden , even if his oven is brand new (unless life is in danger) Pat Palter Baked by a non-Jewish commercial bakery Depends: see below Why Sephardim are often stricter with non-Jewish food Across Bishul Akum, Pat Akum and yayin nesech , the Shulchan Aruch is often stricter than the Rama . One suggestion: the Rambam, the source of most of the Shulchan Aruch's rulings, lived among Muslims with whom there was real social interaction. Tosafot and the Ashkenazi authorities in France and England lived among Christians who persecuted them and had no social contact with them. In Spain, \"until 1492, things were pretty good, relatively,\" so the concern about intermarriage was much greater. The Sephardi rule for bakery bread For Sephardim, commercial non-Jewish bread should not be eaten unless there is no Pat Yisrael within 18 minutes' walk . Living in Golders Green, with Grodzinski's and other bakeries nearby, one should not buy bread such as Kingsmill . What do the hechsherim on Kingsmill, Warburtons and Hovis mean? Only that the ingredients and production are fine. They are not certifying the baking itself; it is still Pat Akum. (There was talk at the time of making one of them Pat Yisrael.) So on holiday , with no Pat Yisrael nearby, you may buy Kingsmill from Sainsbury's or Tesco. In France, he had seen very religious people eating baguettes , since it is known nothing is added, though one must be careful about additives. No kosher equivalent, or tastier If a specific product has no Pat Yisrael equivalent, you may buy it (after checking the ingredients): Crumpets : there are no kosher ones, so it is fine. Ryvita and Jacob's crackers had no hechsher 10 to 20 years ago, and people bought them because there was no equivalent. If a particular brand is tastier and you prefer it, you need not buy an inferior substitute. Price counts too. A participant pointed out that if non-Jewish bread is far cheaper, the same logic applies. The Rabbi agreed: \"You're right, 100%.\" The Shulchan Aruch's requirement to walk 18 minutes applies only when the two are equal in taste and in price . \"Of course, according to the Kabbalah, you should never have Pat Akum. I'm telling you the halachot according to the peshat .\" What counts as pat ? Anything from the five grains (wheat, barley and so on) that is baked: Pies, crackers, pretzels, flatbreads, melba toast, pitta, bagels. Cereals and biscuits : Pat Akum, with no real equivalent. That is why we buy Weetabix at Tesco: the ingredients are fine, there is no hechsher, and there is no equivalent. If someone started making an equal Weetabix with a hechsher at the same price, we would have to stop. Pancakes and blintzes depend on how they are made: deep-fried in oil falls under Bishul ; cooked with just a little oil falls under Pat . Dried pasta : a participant noted that it is not cooked before you buy it, and the Rabbi agreed there is no problem at all. Biscuits or cakes with chocolate : check whether they contain milk . The key contrast Pat Akum (bread) Bishul Akum (cooked food) Leniency when no equivalent Yes : if there is no equal Pat Yisrael in taste and price nearby No : if it meets both conditions, it is forbidden regardless Summary A hechsher is not what makes food kosher. Most food is kosher; where the ingredients can be trusted and fully understood, no hechsher is needed. Beware flavourings, \"vegetable oil,\" grape juice in smoothies, and non-kosher fats. Bishul Akum is a rabbinic decree against intermarriage (Rambam) and against careless cooking. A non-religious Jew may cook for you (Chacham Ovadia). It applies only to food that cannot be eaten raw and is fit for a royal banquet : meat, chicken, potatoes, eggs, rice. Not to tea, coffee, beer, snacks, crisps, popcorn, cornflakes, raw-edible vegetables, or (today) fish. At home, a Jew must at least light the fire and put the pot on (Shulchan Aruch). Sephardim eating in restaurants rely on Chacham Ovadia's combined leniency. Pat Ba'al HaBayit is always forbidden. Pat Palter , for Sephardim, only when no equal Pat Yisrael (in taste and price) is within 18 minutes' walk. Cereals, biscuits, crackers and specific breads with no equivalent may be bought once the ingredients are checked. Coming up: cheese, milk and wine , and then eating out in vegan restaurants .","eXkiUGGc1tk":"Series: Kashrut, a six-part course at Tiferet Eyal, Hendon Shiur: Part 4 of the six-part Kashrut course, given by Dayan Daniel Kada at Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 18 June 2019 (the first shiur after Shavuot) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction After the philosophy of kashrut, vegetarianism, and meat and milk before Shavuot, the course moved on to Tevilat Kelim , immersing utensils in a mikveh. \"It's relatively small, but there's a lot of details to it, and sometimes some misconceptions.\" The Rabbi covered the basics first and then some practical modern questions. Part 1: The Source: The Spoils of Midian Tevilat Kelim is one of the 613 mitzvot. Its source is at the end of Sefer Bamidbar, when the soldiers returned from the war with Midian carrying spoils, including utensils they did not want to throw away. Elazar HaKohen told them (Bamidbar 31:22-23): As far as the gold, silver, copper, iron, tin and lead are concerned, כָּל דָּבָר אֲשֶׁר יָבֹא בָאֵשׁ תַּעֲבִירוּ בָאֵשׁ וְטָהֵר אַךְ בְּמֵי נִדָּה יִתְחַטָּא whatever was used over fire must be passed through fire and it will be clean, but it must also be purified with the water of sprinkling ; and whatever was not used over fire shall be passed through water. Two separate things: hagalah and tevilah The verse describes two different processes , and people often confuse them: Hag'alat Kelim (kashering) Tevilat Kelim (immersion) What it deals with Non-kosher food the utensil has absorbed The utensil passing from non-Jewish to Jewish ownership When Whenever a utensil has absorbed something forbidden, new or old, from anyone Whenever a food utensil is acquired from a non-Jew, even brand new How Fire or boiling water, depending on how it absorbed Immersion in a mikveh Don't make the mistake of thinking that a used utensil bought from a non-Jew can simply be dipped in the mikveh and used. If it has absorbed non-kosher food, it needs kashering too. And the reverse: kashering a second-hand utensil (as caterers sometimes do with large equipment) is not enough ; because it came from a non-Jew, it also needs tevilah. Most of us buy things new, so kashering does not usually arise, and the main issue is tevilah. The Gemara The Gemara (Avodah Zarah) codifies this: Rav Nachman said in the name of Rabbah bar Avuha: new utensils bought from a non-Jew, which he never used, must be immersed in a mikveh , and brings the verse from Midian as the source. This teaches that the two are not linked . There is an opinion that tevilah is only needed when kashering is needed; that is not the halachah. Even with no kashering at all, there must be tevilah. Part 2: Why Immerse? Usually you find many reasons for a mitzvah in the Gemara and Rishonim. Here, despite a great deal of searching, the Rabbi found only two , and the first is not really a reason. The Rosh (Rabbeinu Asher): it is a gezeirat hakatuv , a decree of Scripture that the finite mind cannot fully grasp. The Yerushalmi , quoted by the Ritva : water is the source of life and has the power to transform. People once went to the mikveh to become pure; women still do. Most powerfully, water can change a person's entire identity: conversion . A convert who immerses changes his or her whole status, to the point that halachically the biological ties to his or her parents are severed. Eating, as we saw in the first shiurim, is full of holiness. Just as a person who converts goes into the water, so a utensil taken from a non-Jew and about to be used for food, something we ingest, goes into the mikveh to be elevated for holy use. That is also why only food utensils need it. Kasher first, then immerse When a utensil needs both, the order is always kashering first, then tevilah , as with the spoils of Midian. If it was done the other way round, the tevilah must be repeated . Kashering is the physical refinement, expelling what was absorbed. Only after that can we deal with the spiritual , elevating it through the mikveh. How you kasher depends on how it became non-kosher: kebol'o kach polto , the way it absorbed is the way it is expelled. Sometimes boiling water is enough; sometimes it needs fire. The severity of a Torah mitzvah One may not use a utensil before it has been immersed. There is even an opinion (not followed in halachah) that one should not keep unimmersed utensils in the house at all, in case one comes to use them. If an immersed utensil gets mixed up with similar unimmersed ones, we would normally follow the majority. But since this is a Torah obligation and easy to remedy, immerse them again without a berachah . This only applies to utensils whose tevilah is required by the Torah. Part 3: Which Materials? Material Status Berachah? Metals : aluminium, tin, iron, gold, silver, copper, lead, anything that can be smelted Torah obligation (listed in the verse) Yes Glass Rabbinic : the Hachamim decreed it because, like metal, it can be melted down and reformed Yes Pyrex Same as glass (Chacham Ovadia Yosef): it is a type of glass Yes Plastic, nylon No tevilah according to most poskim No Wood, earthenware, ceramics, porcelain, bone, ivory (uncoated) No tevilah No Wood or earthenware coated inside with metal Needs tevilah Yes Coated only on the outside , where food does not touch No tevilah No Coated with glass Tevilah No Rabbinic enactments generally carry a berachah, just like washing hands or lighting Chanukah candles, so glass is immersed with a berachah. Glass (and Pyrex) does not absorb, so it never needs kashering . But it still needs tevilah . Plastic The poskim of the last hundred years have discussed plastic at length. Logically it is like glass: it can be melted and reformed. But glass itself is a rabbinic enactment , and we do not add to the enactments of the Hachamim. Plastic did not exist when they made the decree, so it is not included. Chacham Ovadia rules this clearly. Those who wish to be stringent may immerse plastic, but without a berachah , since one cannot bless on something one is not obligated to do. Wood \"Sometimes you go to the mikveh and you see people toveling wood. You're just thinking to yourself where they got this from.\" Wood definitely does not need tevilah. Coated items and glazed china Coated wood or earthenware is only rabbinic, so whether a glass coating counts is a doubt, hence tevilah without a berachah. A member of the class asked about ordinary glazed china . The Rabbi said he would need to research exactly what modern glazes are made of; another participant noted that glazes are largely silica, the main ingredient of glass. Part 4: Which Utensils? Not everything that holds food needs tevilah. The basic test A utensil needs tevilah if: Food or drink is eaten or drunk directly from it (plates, cups and so on); or Food is prepared in it: made edible from inedible, or further prepared even if already edible. Storage only Spice jars and similar containers used only to store food, without eating from them or preparing food in them, do not need tevilah . If a container is brought to the table with the meal (for example a salt cellar ), some say it is part of the table settings and needs tevilah, so immerse it without a berachah . Many say even this is unnecessary. A container that never leaves the kitchen definitely needs nothing. So when buying something, ask: will I eat from it? Drink from it? Prepare food in it? If none of these, will it be on the table? If not, it does not need the mikveh. Primary use Always go by the primary use : A penknife bought to cut fruit (unlikely) would need tevilah with a berachah. One bought for general use, even if you occasionally cut fruit with it, does not. A kitchen knife you use for building work does not need tevilah. You can change a utensil's designation. Direct contact with food Only utensils that touch the food directly : Can opener, bottle opener : no tevilah. Tripod under a pot: no tevilah. Jar for tea bags : no tevilah, even if it comes to the table, since the tea is always wrapped. A jar for loose tea granules that comes to the table would be different. In Israel, the holders for milk bags : no tevilah (they are plastic anyway, and the milk is always inside its bag). Nutcracker : it only opens the shell, but it is so bound up with the act of eating (unlike a can opener, which you put aside before eating) that it should be immersed without a berachah . Inedible food A utensil used only for food that is not yet edible does not need tevilah. The dough hook of a Kenwood mixer does not need it. The Kenwood itself generally does, since you can make ice cream in it, which is edible. Part 5: From a Non-Jew Only Rav Moshe Feinstein and Chacham Ovadia Yosef both conclude that tevilah is only required for something bought from a non-Jew . Who counts: the owner or the workers? What if a factory is owned by a Jew and staffed by non-Jews, or the reverse? We follow the owner. \"Made in China\" or anywhere with a non-Jewish majority: needs tevilah. Made in Israel : even if the workers may be non-Jews, the majority of factory owners are Jewish. If you buy it from a Jewish seller, it does not need tevilah at all . Be aware that much of what is sold in Israel is imported; being bought in Israel does not make it Israeli-made. Borrowing from a non-Jew: no tevilah. Don't sell your utensils with the chametz People sometimes sell their utensils together with their chametz before Pesach. A proper sale document sells only the chametz within the utensils (and, for those who are stringent, the absorbed chametz), not the utensils themselves. If one did sell the utensils, Chacham Ovadia's conclusion is that they do not need to be immersed again, since that was not the intention. But in the first instance, do not sell the utensils . The coffee jar: Rav Moshe Feinstein's ruling You finish a jar of olives or a Nescafé jar bought at Tesco and want to keep the nice glass jar for years. Glass, from a non-Jew: surely it needs tevilah? Rav Moshe Feinstein: no. Such jars are made for one-time use , and disposables are not kelim and need no tevilah. The company is not selling you a container; it is selling you coffee or olives. When you later decide to use the jar permanently, ownership is not passing from a non-Jew to a Jew. The jar is already yours, and only its use has changed. So it does not need tevilah , even if you intended to reuse it when you bought it. But some products, such as certain chocolate spreads, come in decorative glasses designed to be reused, where part of what you pay for is the glass. If you want to reuse such a glass, it is better to immerse it, probably without a berachah . Part 6: Electrical Appliances A new urn, blender or toaster: what do you do? The Rabbi first asked the class what they did, and heard a range of answers. The least desirable option: nothing A minority opinion holds that electrical appliances need no tevilah. Because they only work when plugged into the wall, they are considered attached to the ground ( mechubar lakarka ), and the ground does not become impure or need tevilah. Chacham Ovadia and many others rejected this : the appliance is its own utensil, and calling it part of the ground because it is plugged in is a large stretch. Three better options, in order of preference Immerse it and leave it to dry for a day or two (or three) before use. The Rabbi has done this many times without problems, though people have told him theirs broke. \"I'm not taking responsibility for it.\" Used within an hour it would be a danger to life; after drying for days there is no danger to life, only to your money. And if it does break and you return it, you have to tell the shop you put it in water. Dismantle and reassemble it , if you know how. Like melting metal down and recasting it, the utensil is then made in your possession , not transferred from a non-Jew. (A participant asked whether simply removing and replacing the fuse would count. The Rabbi said that fixing something is not making it, so it is questionable, though better than nothing.) Give it as an outright gift to a non-Jewish neighbour and ask to borrow it back indefinitely. Something borrowed from a non-Jew does not need tevilah. Toasters Toasters are less of a problem. Rav Moshe Feinstein writes that a toaster does not need tevilah, since it only warms food that is already edible rather than preparing it. Chacham Ovadia says to immerse a toaster without a berachah . Built-in ovens and microwaves A built-in oven that is too heavy to lift is more like part of the house, part of the ground, though the Rabbi wanted to think about it further. A microwave does not really touch the food, which is always placed in a dish, so some say it does not need tevilah, even the glass plate inside. Again he wanted to give this more thought. Part 7: Eating in the Home of a Non-Religious Jew You are invited to a home that does not keep kashrut. Leave aside the food itself: if it is cold , the dishes and cutlery are completely clean , and the food is simple fruit or vegetables, there is no transfer of taste and no kashrut problem, even if the host eats non-kosher. But there is a tevilah problem . A non-Jew's utensils do not need tevilah, but a Jew's do, and his have not been immersed. Your host may have gone to great lengths to buy food with the best hechsher, and only as you sit down do you realise the dishes are unimmersed. Main halachah: try not to get into this situation, since eating from such dishes is a problem. If it would genuinely offend the host, one may rely on the opinions that permit it. The main reason: when you borrow a utensil you have full use of it, so you would have to immerse it. A guest at a meal is only given the right to use the dishes for half an hour, which is not borrowing. (Most poskim do treat it as borrowing, hence the first instance.) A non-kosher hotel breakfast Using clean plates and cutlery for plain, cold fruit is fine in principle. In practice the Rabbi would not advise it unless you know what you are doing, because sharp foods (lemon, strong vegetables, even radishes ) can draw out non-kosher absorptions even when cold. Plain tomato, cucumber or simple fruit is fine. Bread on a clean cold plate is fine; butter or anything fatty on a non-kosher plate is not. Taste only passes through heat, fat or sharpness . Part 8: What About a Convert? A question the Rabbi was asked a few months earlier. A convert has been keeping kosher for two or three years before conversion. Once they immerse and become Jewish, do their utensils need tevilah? No. In the verse, it was the utensils that passed from non-Jewish to Jewish hands. Here the utensils stay with the same owner; it is the person who changes. The Avnei Nezer (the Sochatchover Rebbe) even suggests that when the convert goes into the mikveh, his possessions are elevated together with him, the same idea of transformed identity with which the shiur began. Someone who wants to be stringent may immerse them, without a berachah . (In answer to a question: a non-religious Jew's utensils certainly do need tevilah, otherwise the dilemma in the previous section would not arise. If you acquire something from a Jew who you assume never immersed it, it needs tevilah.) Summary Tevilat Kelim is a Torah mitzvah, learnt from the spoils of Midian. It is separate from kashering ; when both are needed, kasher first, then immerse . Reasons: a decree of Scripture (Rosh), or, like conversion , elevating the utensil to holy use (Yerushalmi). Metal: Torah, with a berachah. Glass and Pyrex: rabbinic, with a berachah. Plastic: no tevilah. Wood, earthenware, porcelain: none unless coated inside with metal. Only utensils used to eat, drink or prepare edible food , in direct contact with it, judged by primary use . Table items such as salt cellars and nutcrackers: without a berachah. Only items bought from a non-Jew (follow the factory owner). Israeli goods from a Jewish seller and borrowed items need nothing. Disposable jars reused later need no tevilah (Rav Moshe Feinstein). Electrical appliances: immerse and dry, dismantle and reassemble, or gift and borrow back. Toaster: no tevilah per Rav Moshe, without a berachah per Chacham Ovadia. At a non-religious host's home, avoid unimmersed dishes if possible; if it would offend, there are opinions to rely on. A convert need not immerse his or her existing utensils. Next week: Bishul Akum and Pat Akum : cooking and baking by a non-Jew, and what to know about the bread on supermarket shelves. Three more shiurim remain in the series.","C_w-o-L1lXM":"Series: Kashrut, a six-part course at Tiferet Eyal, Hendon Shiur: Part 3 of the six-part Kashrut course, given by Dayan Daniel Kada at Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 28 May 2019 (a week and a half before Shavuot) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction: Why Now? After two shiurim on the ideas behind kashrut (its reasons, then vegetarianism), this shiur moved on to the practical halachot. The timing was deliberate: Shavuot was a week and a half away. On Shavuot there is a mitzvah to eat meat, as on every Chag, and also a minhag to eat dairy, so questions about meat and milk come up constantly. Recap: four reasons for the prohibition Meat on its own is permitted and dairy on its own is permitted. Why forbid them together? From the first shiur: Source Reason Sefer HaChinuch Two disparate forces that must not be combined, like sha'atnez (wool and linen) and kilayim (grafting fruits) Ramban Cruelty : cooking a kid in its own mother's milk. Every animal can be seen as a mother and every animal as someone's young, so it extends to all meat and milk Rashbam (Rashi's grandson) Gluttony : mixing meat with the very substance that gave the animal life and nourishment Rambam (1) Unhealthy ; and (2), his preferred reason though he is not certain of it, it was a pagan practice , and the Torah weans us away from idolatry towards Yichud Hashem , as with tattoos, rounding the corners of the head and shaving with a razor Part 1: Three Verses, Three Prohibitions The Torah repeats the same five words three times , in Parashat Mishpatim , Ki Tisa and Re'eh : לֹא תְבַשֵּׁל גְּדִי בַּחֲלֵב אִמּוֹ Do not cook a kid in its mother's milk. From this the Hachamim learn three separate Torah prohibitions : Eating meat and milk cooked together. Cooking meat and milk together, even with no intention of eating it. Benefiting from meat and milk cooked together, even by someone else: you may not buy or sell it, and you may not even feed it to your animals. The prohibition on benefit and on cooking is just as much from the Torah as the prohibition on eating, \"which we would never consider doing.\" Why it matters what is Torah and what is rabbinic In principle: the Rambam writes that someone who teaches publicly that a rabbinic prohibition is from the Torah transgresses the negative commandment of not adding to the Torah ( bal tosif ). The Hachamim had the right to add their enactments only because they declared them rabbinic. This was the mistake in the story of Adam and Chavah: they added that one may not even touch the tree, when Hashem had only forbidden eating from it. In practice, for meat and milk: the rabbinic extensions only forbid eating , not cooking or benefit. The Torah prohibition applies only to domesticated animals ( behemah , like the gedi ). Therefore: Wild animals such as deer , even if kosher, and poultry with milk are only rabbinically forbidden. One may cook them with dairy and benefit from them, for example by selling them. Strictly speaking one may even cook chicken with milk in the same pot , though for mar'it ayin (appearances) it is better not to. The problem with using your own pot is only that the walls absorb the taste. The act of cooking itself is not forbidden. Part 2: Cooking Cooking meat and milk together is forbidden by the Torah regardless of whether anyone will eat it. What counts as cooking? Boiling, frying, barbecuing, roasting : all forms of cooking with fire are forbidden by the Torah . Smoking or salting meat and milk together: after much discussion, the conclusion is that this is forbidden only rabbinically . Microwaves: Rav Moshe Feinstein and Chacham Ovadia Yosef When microwaves appeared, there was a major debate, with Rav Moshe Feinstein and Chacham Ovadia Yosef in the two main camps. To understand it, the Rabbi recalled a point from his Hilchot Shabbat series: one may cook in the sun on Shabbat , for example by putting an egg out in the sun. The Rishonim give two reasons: It is not the normal way of cooking. Cooking by definition means by fire ; heat without fire is not cooking. Rav Moshe Feinstein Chacham Ovadia Yosef Follows reason 1: it depends on what is normal 2: cooking means fire Microwave It is the normal way of cooking today It uses radiation, not fire Meat and milk in a microwave Torah prohibition : no benefit, cannot even be fed to a dog At most rabbinic Chacham Ovadia discussed the hot springs of Teveria : the Mishnah in Chullin treats cooking meat and milk in them as rabbinically forbidden. He argued that a microwave is not even comparable, since the springs at least transmit heat. His conclusion: a microwave is at worst rabbinic , so if meat and milk were cooked together in one, one may still benefit from the food or give it to others. (Asked about using one microwave for meat and milk at different times, the Rabbi said it would need kashering, something he would return to.) Washing meaty and milky tablecloths together May you put a meaty tablecloth and a cheesy tablecloth into the washing machine together? The poskim say there is no problem at all , even if heavily stained, for several reasons: It is not the way of cooking . There is almost certainly sixty times as much water as any residue. The cleaning agents make it all inedible. The au pair's pot The Rabbi previewed the next shiur on a non-Jewish au pair cooking for you. Here is the reverse case: your au pair asks you to switch on the stove for her. You must check what is in the pot . If she has meat and milk together in it, lighting the fire makes you the one cooking meat and milk, which is a Torah prohibition. (Chicken, deer or other wild animals with dairy would not be a problem, as above.) A Jewish chef in a non-kosher kitchen To show the gravity of this prohibition: the halachah is that if someone needs the income and cannot find other work, a Jew may work as a chef cooking non-kosher food, even pork , for non-Jews. But he may never cook meat and milk together , because that act is itself forbidden by the Torah. \"There's absolutely no way around that.\" Part 3: Benefit Since meat and milk cooked together are forbidden in benefit: You may not feed it even to pets . Even feeding a stray dog is discussed, and the conclusion is better not to, since the good feeling of having fed it may itself be a benefit. You may not even burn it to get rid of it, because that is further cooking. Even the ashes are forbidden in benefit. All this applies only where a Torah prohibition was transgressed. Part 4: Eating The Torah uses the word tevashel (cook) for all three prohibitions. From this the Hachamim derive that the Torah only forbids eating meat and milk that were cooked together . A piece of cold cooked meat and a piece of cheese, eaten together in one mouthful, are not forbidden by the Torah. The Hachamim forbade them. Twice and three times removed The Hachamim then went further: after eating meat, one may not eat dairy until the next meal . Notice how far this is from the Torah: Case Distance from the Torah prohibition Cold meat and cheese in one mouthful Rabbinic: not cooked together Dairy some hours after meat Twice removed : not cooked together, and not even in the same mouth Dairy some hours after chicken Three times removed : poultry with milk is itself only rabbinic \"It's remarkable how people are so particular about this.\" Even people who are not observant of Shabbat often keep this. Why wait? Rambam versus Rashi Rambam Rashi Reason Meat gets stuck in the teeth and takes hours to dissolve The taste lingers in the mouth and stomach Chewed meat but did not swallow Must wait No need to wait Swallowed meat without chewing No need to wait Must wait False teeth \"Just change your teeth\": milky teeth and meaty teeth Must wait Halachah: we follow both opinions. One must wait whether one chewed without swallowing or swallowed without chewing. How long? There are really only two opinions The Gemara says to wait from meal to meal . What does that mean? The Rambam: the time normally between one meal and the next, which is about six hours ( kemo shesh sha'ot ). Tosafot: nothing to do with time. Meat and dairy may not be eaten in the same meal. Once you finish, say Birkat HaMazon and rinse your mouth, you may start a dairy meal immediately. Rule Shulchan Aruch (Maran) , for Sephardim Follows the Rambam: six hours Rama , for Ashkenazim Follows Tosafot: a new meal is enough, but the custom is to wait one hour Where did three hours come from? \"There is no such opinion as three hours.\" The custom is not very old and hard to trace. One suggestion: some opinions (not ours) understand six hours as seasonal hours ( sha'ot zemaniyot ), the daylight divided into twelve. In summer six such hours can be about nine; in winter, much shorter, closer to three. Since three hours worked in winter, people reasoned it should work in summer too. Ashkenazim have a solid basis for one hour , though most will tell you they wait three or six, since later authorities pushed for the Rambam's view. If one's family tradition is longer, that is something else. Sephardim should follow the Shulchan Aruch: six hours . Many from India, Iraq and elsewhere have a custom of three hours, and that is fine, but the main halachah for us is six. Leniencies within the six hours \"About six hours.\" Chacham Ovadia notes the Rambam's word kemo , \"about.\" Best to wait the full six, but if there is a need (you are in someone's house and are offered tea with milk), from five and a half hours it counts as six. Doubt. If you are unsure whether six hours have passed but clearly a long time has, you may be lenient, because a doubt in a rabbinic law is lenient. Counting from when? From when you finished eating the meat , not from Birkat HaMazon. You said a berachah on dairy too soon You made a coffee with milk, said the berachah, and then remembered you are still meaty. Should you drink? Yes, take a sip. Saying God's name in vain is a Torah matter; waiting after meat is twice or three times removed from the Torah. The same applies to someone who made a berachah on meat during the Nine Days (only a minhag), and even on a fast day : take a sip and stop. But this only applies to food that is intrinsically permitted and is only forbidden because of the timing. If the food itself is forbidden, even only rabbinically (for example, food cooked by a non-Jew, the topic of a later shiur), you do not eat it. Children Children generally need only wait one hour , since the longer waiting periods are so far removed from the Torah. We should still educate them in self-control. For a luxury such as an ice cream, better to teach them to wait longer; for a necessity such as a cup of milk before bed, one hour is fine. From about a year before bar or bat mitzvah ( girls 11, boys 12 ), start educating them towards six hours if possible. Children under three may be given dairy right after meat: just wipe their mouth and face in between. The ill, nursing and pregnant women, and the elderly Because \"meal to meal\" can be interpreted as one hour, in cases of need one may wait one hour : Someone who is unwell , not necessarily bedridden: a headache, feeling uncomfortable, and a dairy drink will help. A nursing woman who needs milk. A pregnant woman with a craving. Elderly people , who are generally considered in this category. In such cases, eat something pareve in between and rinse the mouth . Tasting, false teeth and braces Tasting without chewing or swallowing (for example tasting the chamin on Erev Shabbat) and spitting it out does not make you meaty. Rinse your mouth and continue with dairy. False teeth and braces : discussed extensively by the poskim. Taste is only transferred by heat, which does not happen in the mouth, so one set is fine . \"You don't have to go and buy another one tonight.\" Almond milk In answer to a question, apparently about plant-based \"milk\" such as almond milk with meat: it is not dairy, but the Hachamim forbade it because it looks like dairy. If you cook such a dish, put almonds next to it to show what it is. Part 5: The Challah in the Meaty Oven: Notein Ta'am bar Notein Ta'am \"The next point is going to do you a big favour in your life.\" You bake challot in your clean meaty oven for Shabbat. They are sitting in your freezer. Can you now have them as a cheese sandwich ? This is נ\"ט בר נ\"ט ( notein ta'am bar notein ta'am ), a flavour twice removed: An hour ago a steak was cooked in the oven. The walls absorbed its flavour: flavour one . That is why you may not cook something dairy in it. You then bake something pareve in it. Whatever flavour passes from the walls into the bread is flavour two . Flavour two may be eaten with dairy . Ruling Ashkenazim Only after the fact ( bedi'avad ) Chacham Ovadia Yosef Even in the first instance ( lechatchilah ) you may bake challah in the meaty oven intending to eat it with cheese Ben Ish Chai Stringent, but the main halachah is like Chacham Ovadia And the reverse: pareve food baked in a milky oven may be eaten with meat. But the oven must be clean. If there is actual meat residue, the bread takes flavour one directly from the meat and may not be eaten with dairy. \"Clean\" means no visible residue , not two hours on self-clean; \"that's for Pesach.\" Bread that took its taste from actual meat has the taste of the meat itself ( ta'am ke'ikar ), and after eating it one would have to wait as after meat. Part 6: Dairy on Shavuot The custom of eating dairy on Shavuot is not mentioned before the 13th century . Three reasons: New laws at Sinai. When Bnei Yisrael received the Torah, all their pots became non-kosher, since until then they had been cooking without these rules, and they had no correctly slaughtered meat. So they ate dairy. Why not kasher the pots? Matan Torah was on Shabbat : they left Egypt on a Thursday, and seven weeks later was Shabbat. Torah is compared to milk : \"honey and milk under your tongue.\" Some also eat honey on Shavuot. The Shtei HaLechem (the Rama). On Shavuot the Temple offering was two loaves of bread from the new wheat (on Pesach it had been barley). How does dairy recall two loaves? Because of the halachah that bread on the table at a meaty meal may not then be used at a dairy meal , and vice versa. If you eat a dairy meal with one loaf and then a meat meal, you need a second loaf , and so you have two loaves. The same halachah applies on Shabbat: a challah left on the table during a meat meal cannot be taken the next day for a dairy picnic unless it was clearly set aside and wrapped, because meat has very likely touched it. A Chassidic opinion Some Chassidic works, including the Noam Elimelech , write that on Shavuot one may eat dairy one hour after meat , even for those who wait six hours all year, to accommodate the custom. The Rabbi mentioned it as an interesting opinion to be aware of, but \"we don't follow this type of stuff.\" Summary The Torah says lo tevashel gedi bachalev imo three times : three Torah prohibitions, on eating , cooking and benefit . Only domesticated animals with milk are forbidden by the Torah. Poultry and wild animals are rabbinic, forbidden only to eat, not to cook or sell. Microwave cooking: Torah-level according to Rav Moshe Feinstein, at most rabbinic according to Chacham Ovadia. Never cook meat and milk together, not even for a non-Jew or as part of a job. Check the au pair's pot before lighting the fire. Waiting after meat: six hours for Sephardim (from five and a half in case of need), one hour according to the Rama, with three hours a later custom. Count from when you finished eating. Children, the ill, nursing and pregnant women, and the elderly may wait one hour if needed. Bread baked in a clean meaty oven may be eaten with cheese ( notein ta'am bar notein ta'am ). Three reasons for dairy on Shavuot, including the Rama's Shtei HaLechem . As you can see, there is a lot more to talk about. People study this for a year in kollel. But this is the basics. Next shiur, after Shavuot (Tuesday 18 June): Tevilat Kelim , immersing utensils bought from a non-Jew.","MocjEw7YgoQ":"Series: Kashrut, a six-part course at Tiferet Eyal, Hendon Shiur: Part 2 of the six-part Kashrut course, given by Dayan Daniel Kada at Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 21 May 2019 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction: Does the Torah Have an Opinion on Veganism? Before moving on to the practical halachot of kashrut in the coming weeks (including, later, eating out in vegetarian and vegan restaurants), the Rabbi devoted this shiur to a philosophical question. Can veganism be reconciled with the Torah? More than that: does the Torah have an opinion about it? Is it for it, against it, or neutral? What is veganism? A vegetarian abstains from meat, chicken and fish. A vegan goes further and abstains from dairy and other animal products. Veganism is the philosophy behind it. Some people are vegan simply for their health. Veganism as a principle is defined by the Vegan Society as: A way of living which seeks to exclude, as far as is possible and practicable, all forms of exploitation of, and cruelty to, animals for food, clothing or any other purpose. The question of the evening is the Torah's view of that philosophy, not of any individual's diet. The obvious starting point Asked what the Torah's default position would be, the class answered that eating meat is clearly acceptable. The Rabbi agreed this is the obvious starting point: Sefer Vayikra , which we were reading at the time, is full of korbanot , sacrifices. The role of the Kohen was to slaughter animals. There is a mitzvah to eat meat on the Chagim . The Shulchan Aruch says one should enjoy Shabbat with meat, fish and delicacies . Pesach Sheni , two days earlier. The Rabbi took a moment to correct a common misconception: Pesach Sheni is not a \"second Pesach.\" Someone who did not eat matzah on Pesach does not make it up a month later. It is a second chance only to bring the Korban Pesach , the Pesach lamb, for those who could not bring it at the time. The whole festival of Pesach was historically oriented around that lamb. So killing animals seems to be part and parcel of the Torah. \"However... things aren't as simple as you think they may be.\" Part 1: Covering the Blood: A Loaded Metaphor The core verse of the evening is from Parashat Acharei Mot (Vayikra 17:13), read two weeks before: אֲשֶׁר יָצוּד צֵיד חַיָּה אוֹ עוֹף אֲשֶׁר יֵאָכֵל וְשָׁפַךְ אֶת דָּמוֹ וְכִסָּהוּ בֶּעָפָר One who hunts and traps a beast or bird that may be eaten shall pour out its blood and cover it with earth. This is the source of the mitzvah of kisui hadam , covering the blood after slaughtering a wild animal or bird. It is one of the 613 mitzvot, and one makes a berachah on it: asher kideshanu bemitzvotav vetzivanu al kisui hadam . Read on its own, the verse sounds innocent. But in English, what do we mean when we say someone is \"covering up the blood\"? Murder. \"And cover his blood\" There is only one other place in the Torah with this phrase: Yosef and his brothers . When the brothers plan to kill Yosef, Yehudah says (Bereshit 37:26): מַה בֶּצַע כִּי נַהֲרֹג אֶת אָחִינוּ וְכִסִּינוּ אֶת דָּמוֹ What gain is there if we kill our brother and cover up his blood? That is clearly talking about murder. The prophet Yeshayahu (26:21) uses the same language for Hashem's judgement: וְגִלְּתָה הָאָרֶץ אֶת דָּמֶיהָ וְלֹא תְכַסֶּה עוֹד עַל הֲרוּגֶיהָ The land shall reveal its blood and shall no longer cover its slain. \"And he shall pour out its blood\" Now look at the other phrase in the verse: veshafach et damo , he spills its blood. Yechezkel (22:4) uses it of murder: בְּדָמֵךְ אֲשֶׁר שָׁפַכְתְּ אָשַׁמְתְּ By the blood that you have shed, you have become guilty. So the Torah, when it describes shechita, uses two loaded metaphors : shefichat damim , spilling blood, and kisui be'afar , covering in earth. Both appear elsewhere in the context of murder. It is as if the Torah is saying: yes, you may slaughter, but there is something like a crime going on here . Kill the animal if you wish, but cover up the blood. \"Cover over your sin.\" This is a mixed message, and the rest of the shiur tries to understand it. Part 2: From Adam to Noach: A Change in the Law \"We're all used to eating meat and chicken... What we need to recognise is it wasn't always the case, and it could be it won't be the case in the future. So all those who are praying very hard for Mashiach, you have to know what comes with it.\" Adam: only plants Before creating Adam, Hashem says (Bereshit 1:29): הִנֵּה נָתַתִּי לָכֶם אֶת כָּל עֵשֶׂב זֹרֵעַ זֶרַע... וְאֶת כָּל הָעֵץ אֲשֶׁר בּוֹ פְרִי עֵץ זֹרֵעַ זָרַע לָכֶם יִהְיֶה לְאָכְלָה I have given you every seed-bearing plant... and every tree that has seed-bearing fruit; they shall be yours for food. There is no mention of meat, birds or even fish. The Midrash comments: Hashem did not create His creatures in order that they should be killed. Noach: meat is permitted After the flood, Noach brings sacrifices, and Hashem says (Bereshit 9:3): כָּל רֶמֶשׂ אֲשֶׁר הוּא חַי לָכֶם יִהְיֶה לְאָכְלָה כְּיֶרֶק עֵשֶׂב נָתַתִּי לָכֶם אֶת כֹּל Every moving thing that lives shall be food for you; as the green plants, I have given you everything. We read Bereshit and Noach a week apart and forget the time in between. The flood came 1,656 years after creation. For 1,656 years, humanity ate only plants. (Someone in the class pointed out that people lived seven, eight, nine hundred years in that era. The Rabbi acknowledged the point: obviously human bodies could live on such a diet.) Reward or concession? The Ramban reads the permission as a reward for Noach's sacrifice: humanity had progressed. Rav Yosef Albo (15th-century Spanish philosopher) reads it as a concession . Hashem's original intention was that humans live on plants alone. After the flood, He \"as it were changed His mind.\" Kayin and Hevel: the first human sacrifice Why the change? Albo points back to Kayin and Hevel . Hevel brought an animal offering and it was accepted; Kayin brought a vegetable offering and it was not. At that time there was no clear line between killing animals and killing humans: all living creatures were off limits. Kayin saw his brother kill an animal for Hashem and be accepted, and concluded that in the context of sacrifice, one may kill any living creature . According to Albo, Kayin's killing of Hevel was not plain murder. It was the first human sacrifice. \"It sounds crazy because we're not used to such a concept, but it's a very interesting theory, and if you think about it, it makes sense.\" Add to this the violence, the chamas , of Noach's generation. Hashem saw that people needed an outlet and a clear distinction between animals and humans . So He told Noach: now you may kill animals, but not human beings . Reading the verses together Look at the next two verses: אַךְ בָּשָׂר בְּנַפְשׁוֹ דָמוֹ לֹא תֹאכֵלוּ (9:4) But flesh with its life, its blood, you shall not eat. The Rabbi gave two readings: do not eat the blood (hence salting and removing blood), and ever min hachai , do not eat a limb before the animal is fully dead. Dead flesh you may have; the blood, which gave the creature life, you may not. שֹׁפֵךְ דַּם הָאָדָם בָּאָדָם דָּמוֹ יִשָּׁפֵךְ כִּי בְּצֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים עָשָׂה אֶת הָאָדָם (9:6) Whoever sheds the blood of man, by man shall his blood be shed, for in the image of God He made man. So three steps: (1) you may eat animals; (2) not their blood; (3) and do not kill human beings, because of the tzelem Elokim . Two ways to read this: Humans and animals are essentially similar, except that humans bear the image of God. Humans are fundamentally different from animals. The Rabbi's view is that both are true. We are part animal and part soul. Our bodies have animal drives, and in that sense we are like animals. But we also have the neshamah , the image of God, and there is a constant struggle between the two. Back to covering the blood Now return to Acharei Mot. The very next verse after covering the blood (Vayikra 17:14) says: כִּי נֶפֶשׁ כָּל בָּשָׂר דָּמוֹ בְנַפְשׁוֹ הוּא For the life of all flesh is its blood. This is the same language Hashem used to Noach. The Rabbi's reading: Hashem made a concession to Noach, but do not let it become just an act of killing . You are spilling blood, so cover it. Be a little embarrassed by what you have done. And we make a berachah, because this is the situation we find ourselves in after the Ark, and it is permitted. So there is a sharp distinction between eating salad and vegetables and eating meat. Meat is allowed, but perhaps it is not the ideal . \"If a person has the same choice what to eat, then according to what we've learned so far, it would be preferred for him to go to the vegetables.\" Part 3: Is Vegetarianism Part of the Final Tikkun ? How old is the vegetarian movement? The class guessed 40 or 50 years. The Rabbi offered his own \"humble idea.\" At the beginning of Bereshit there are three punishments : Punishment Then Today Adam : by the sweat of your brow Hard physical labour Since the Industrial Revolution, very few men work by the sweat of their brow Chavah : pain in childbirth Women often died in childbirth Far less pain and danger: epidurals, modern medicine Migdal Bavel : the confusion of languages Humanity scattered Google Translate: you can understand a document in a language you do not know The world is constantly moving towards a final tikkun , a final rectification. The Messianic era is not something that arrives from one day to the next; it is a process, and change must be incremental. All these punishments are slowly being undone. Perhaps the growth of vegetarianism and veganism is part of the same process , a return to the pre-Noach ideal. Ten or twenty years ago it was far less common; who knows where it will be in another twenty or thirty. (He added in passing that the move towards the equality of men and women can be traced back to Adam and Chavah before the sin in the same way.) Part 4: Rav Kook and Rav Soloveitchik Rav Kook Rav Avraham Yitzchak HaKohen Kook , Chief Rabbi of Palestine before the State of Israel, was \"an unbelievable, great, really an amazing thinker, well ahead of his times.\" He wrote an essay on vegetarianism and argued that ideally and ideologically we should all be vegetarians . He himself abstained, eating only a little meat on Yom Tov. But he did not promote it in practice , for two reasons: Wrong priorities. In his time there were far bigger things in the picture. Focusing on something \"as minute as this\" would not have been right. Blurring the line between humans and animals. That is exactly what happened with Kayin. The korbanot will be flour offerings What, then, of all the sacrifices? Rav Kook said the korbanot too were a concession . He cited a verse we say every day after the ketoret in Shacharit and Minchah (Malachi 3:4): וְעָרְבָה לַה' מִנְחַת יְהוּדָה וִירוּשָׁלָםִ כִּימֵי עוֹלָם וּכְשָׁנִים קַדְמֹנִיּוֹת Then the offering of Yehudah and Yerushalayim shall be pleasing to Hashem, as in the days of old. A minchah is a flour offering . Vayikra has animal offerings, bird offerings and menachot , and there is a whole tractate called Menachot. From this verse, Rav Kook argued, in the future, with Mashiach and the rebuilt Beit HaMikdash, there will only be flour offerings , not animal sacrifices. The Rabbi noted that he does not explain what would happen to the Korban Pesach, and that this view is not unanimous; others hold that animal sacrifices will return in full. But it shows how far Rav Kook took his ideology. He even explained sha'atnez through it: not mixing wool (from the animal kingdom) with linen (from the plant kingdom). Rav Soloveitchik Rav Yosef Dov (Joseph B.) Soloveitchik , grandson of Rav Chaim of Brisk, was a towering talmid chacham who was also learned in science and philosophy. Others after him described his approach as Modern Orthodox. According to the Rabbi, he went further than Rav Kook: he was a full vegetarian and held that it should actually be practised, not just held as an ideal. His answer on the korbanot: out of gratitude for life, a person should want to give everything back to Hashem. That is how he explains the Akeidah . Avraham was so overwhelmed with gratitude and love that he wanted to offer life back to God. Hashem taught him that we do not do this : human sacrifice is forbidden. So Avraham brought a ram instead, to express his love through it. Animal sacrifice is therefore a necessary evil . Really we should be giving ourselves to God; we cannot, so the best we can do is offer animals. But this belongs only within the context of avodah , the service in the Temple, as a way to build a relationship with Hashem. Outside of that, it does not apply. So for Rav Soloveitchik there is no contradiction between being pro-vegetarian and having animal sacrifices in the Beit HaMikdash. Part 5: Must We Eat Meat on Yom Tov? The Rabbi posed a test case. A prospective convert must accept all 613 mitzvot. What if they say, in full disclosure, \"I'm vegetarian. I won't eat meat on the Chag.\" Can they be converted? The answer depends on whether there is an obligation to eat meat on Yom Tov. The mitzvah to eat meat comes from simchat Yom Tov , rejoicing on the festival: In Temple times , when animals were offered, there was an obligation to eat meat on the festivals. Today , the main fulfilment of simchat Yom Tov is with wine , not meat. Eating meat is a mitzvah according to some opinions, but not an obligation . So a vegetarian convert poses no problem. Practical point for Shavuot That year Shavuot came straight after Shabbat, giving three days of Shabbat and Yom Tov. People ask whether they need meat at every meal. The answer: have meat at one meal , since there is a mitzvah according to some opinions, but there is no obligation to do so at every meal. This also resolves the tension between the mitzvah of meat and the custom of eating dairy on Shavuot. Conclusion The Rabbi ended with a disclaimer: \"I've given this whole shiur as if I'm a vegetarian myself. I'm not. I aspire to be this one day.\" He had presented one side. There are others who hold that full animal sacrifices will return, and some who even think vegetarianism is wrong, since it seems to imply the Torah's sacrifices were wrong. He had tried to show that they need not contradict each other. What the shiur showed: When the Torah describes slaughter, it uses loaded metaphors of murder: spilling blood and covering it up. The Torah is not saying you are doing something wrong. It is saying: remember, you are killing a soul, not cutting down a tree. Before Noach, humans were not allowed to eat meat . Afterwards they were, as a concession. Rav Kook and Rav Soloveitchik , great Hachamim of their generations, were pro-vegetarian and saw no contradiction with the korbanot or with meat on Yom Tov. Why we have become numb to it In the old days you saw the shechita, or at least your mother was involved in kashering the meat and removing the blood. Today you buy a packet, put it in the pot and onto your plate, and forget the whole process it has been through, the blood spilled and the pain the animal went through. Of course we're allowed to eat it, and it's fine to eat it. We can enjoy ourselves. But ultimately there seems to be some aspiration that one day we'll be aiming towards a more refined state. Several people who stopped eating meat told the Rabbi they feel much better and less heavy, \"because at the end of the day you're ingesting other life into you.\" Vegetarianism, he concluded, is compatible with Torah, and perhaps even the Torah's will: that the world will eventually be refined enough that we will not need to kill animals to satisfy our desires. Next week: the halachot of meat and milk , beyond the basics: what happens if something gets mixed in, the one-in-sixty rule, children, how long to wait, and more.","jcMRNf1Xt2I":"Series: Kashrut, a six-part course at Tiferet Eyal, Hendon Shiur: Part 1 of the six-part Kashrut course, given by Dayan Daniel Kada at Tiferet Eyal, Tuesday 14 May 2019 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Part 1: What Makes Food Kosher? The Nine Main Things The recording begins as the Rabbi is finishing his list of what a hechsher actually has to check. He gave six main things and then three more : # Requirement 1 The food itself : a kosher species of animal, bird or fish 2 Shechita : correct slaughter 3 Purging the meat of forbidden fats and blood 4 Not a nevelah or terefah : not an animal that would die within the next 12 months 5 No forbidden mixtures , above all meat and milk 6 Bug infestation in fruit and vegetables 7 Grape products 8 Utensils : food cooked in non-kosher utensils cannot be eaten 9 Food cooked by a non-Jew : most cooked foods are prohibited There are many other points, he added, and threw out a few words to show it: chadash , terumot and ma'asrot . All of these are part of what a hechsher covers. But once you have \"ticked all those nine boxes,\" you can say the food is kosher and permitted to eat. Several of these items (meat and milk, utensils, cooking by non-Jews, bugs) would each get their own shiur later in the course. Three categories for tonight Out of all of this, the Rabbi chose three main categories of non-kosher food to explore that evening: The animals and birds themselves. What is wrong with rabbit? What is wrong with pork? Meat and milk mixed together. Terefah : an animal that has not been slaughtered correctly, or that is going to die within 12 months. Part 2: May We Give Reasons for Mitzvot? Before giving any reasons, the Rabbi said, we have to establish that searching for reasons is legitimate, and that it must be done with care. The Rambam: every mitzvah has a reason In the Moreh Nevuchim (Guide for the Perplexed), Part 3, Chapter 31 , the Rambam argues against people who think the Torah is so lofty that one should not look for reasons at all, and should treat the whole Torah as unfathomable decrees. That, says the Rambam, is simply wrong. Every one of the 613 mitzvot, including those we call chukim , has a reason , even if we do not always know it. The Rabbi read the passage: Every one of the 613 commandments serves to inculcate some truth, to remove some erroneous opinion, to establish proper relations in society, to diminish evil, to train in good manners or to warn against bad habits. All this depends on three things: opinions, morals and social conduct. It is our duty, the Rambam holds, to probe and understand the reasons, which is why he devotes a whole section of the Guide to them. \"We all keep kashrut at different levels, but we all keep kashrut. But why?\" The Sefer HaChinuch: a book for a son on Shabbat afternoons Following in the Rambam's path is the Sefer HaChinuch , the \"Book of Education,\" written in the 13th century in Barcelona . The author is anonymous and saw the Rambam as his teacher. In his introduction he explains that he wrote it to occupy his son on long summer Shabbat afternoons: rather than waste the time, the boy could go through the mitzvot of each week's parashah and learn their rationale. It runs from the first mitzvah in the Torah, having children, to the last, writing a Sefer Torah. A ta'am is a taste The Rabbi stressed the limits of the exercise. The Rambam calls these reasons ta'amim . A ta'am is a reason, but the literal meaning is a taste . \"We want to make them palatable, in the literal sense.\" We are giving the mitzvot a flavour. We cannot imagine we will reach the full depth and comprehensive rationale of each mitzvah; that is beyond our grasp. A relatively new enterprise Giving reasons for mitzvot is relatively new: The Torah very rarely gives reasons for its mitzvot. The Gemara is mostly legal, the ins and outs, not philosophical reasons. It became common in medieval times , with the Rambam, Rav Saadia Gaon, the Chinuch and others. In places like Spain, where Jews and non-Jews mixed and Christians and Muslims mocked Jewish practice, there was a need to defend the religion and make it understandable. (The Rabbi noted that the Rambam's Thirteen Principles were likewise a response to the Muslim creed of his time, a subject for another day.) The Torah assumes many things and expects us to understand them. It was the Rambam and those after him who articulated them one by one. The only two food laws the Torah explains In the Torah, no reason is given for kashrut: not for meat and milk, not for why a camel is not kosher. We are given signs , not reasons. The Gemara also gives none. There are just two exceptions: The gid hanasheh (sciatic nerve) : we do not eat it in memory of Yaakov's wrestling with the angel, who injured him there. Blood : the Torah says blood is the life of the creature, so eating blood is like eating the creature's life itself. Part 3: The Rambam's Approach: Kashrut Is Healthy Eating The Rabbi asked the class what they thought the Rambam gives as the reason for kashrut. The answer is startling: health . In Moreh Nevuchim 3:48 , the Rambam writes: I maintain that food forbidden by the Torah is unwholesome. There is nothing among the forbidden kinds of food whose injurious character is doubted except pork and fat. But also in these cases the doubt is not justified. On pork he adds that its habits and food are dirty and loathsome, and that if we were allowed to eat it, \"the streets and the houses would be more dirty than any cesspool, as may be seen in the country of the Franks\" (referring to the Christians). Forbidden fats, he says, fill a person up and interrupt digestion. Meat boiled in milk is \"undoubtedly gross food\" and makes one overfull. The Chinuch follows the same line and develops it: the body is the vessel for the soul. If you do not look after the body, the soul will not have permanence in this world. The same applies to a terefah : an animal that will die within 12 months is diseased, and eating it is physically unhealthy. The difficulties The Rabbi admitted he finds this \"very, very difficult,\" and so did many of the Rambam's successors: Is it true? If you collected the statistics, he doubted you would find that Jews are healthier and live longer than non-Jews. Is that all the Torah is? To relegate the laws of the Torah to a diet is hard to accept. Rav Yosef Albo's question: if the Torah is simply giving health guidelines, why command only the Jews? \" Verachamav al kol ma'asav \", Hashem's mercy is on all His creatures. Why not tell the non-Jews as well? So he turned to the other Rishonim, category by category. Part 4: Category One: Forbidden Animals Why does the Torah forbid an animal that lacks split hooves or does not chew the cud? The Ramban: spiritual health The Ramban (Nachmanides) takes issue with the Rambam. It has nothing to do with physical health, he says, because the Torah introduces these laws with וְאַנְשֵׁי קֹדֶשׁ תִּהְיוּן לִי , \"you shall be holy people to Me.\" It is spiritual health . The forbidden animals are predatory , and all the forbidden birds are birds of prey . Eating them instils a cruel nature . \"You are what you eat.\" How can food affect the soul? The Rabbi answered with an analogy: people take tablets and substances that change their mood and psyche. In the same way, according to the Ramban, certain foods can change a person's spiritual nature, essence and identity. To the best of his knowledge, these are the only two reasons given for the forbidden species themselves: the Rambam (physical health) and the Ramban (spiritual health). Part 5: Category Two: Meat and Milk Here there are more opinions: Source Reason Sefer HaChinuch Like sha'atnez (wool and linen) and kilayim (grafting fruits): two disparate forces in the world that must not be combined Ramban Cruelty : cooking a kid in the milk of its own mother is cruel Rashbam (Rashi's grandson) Gluttony : mixing dead meat with the milk that gives life and nourishment to the young is a gluttonous act Rambam Unhealthy, but more likely a ban on an idolatrous practice On the Chinuch's comparison to kilayim, the Rabbi mentioned that grafted citrus fruits are the reason some do not say Shehecheyanu on them: grafting is something that should not be happening. On the Ramban: the Torah's words are \"do not cook a kid in its mother's milk.\" The prohibition extends to all meat and milk because, on a wider level, every animal can be seen as a mother and every animal was once someone's young. The Rambam: an idolatrous rite The Rambam writes: Meat boiled in milk is undoubtedly gross food... but I think that most probably it is also prohibited because it is somehow connected with idolatry, forming perhaps part of the service or being used at some festival of the heathen. This I consider the best reason for the prohibition; but as far as I have seen the books on their rites, nothing is mentioned of this custom. The Rambam studied the idolaters' books and did not find it, but still thought it the best reason. Mrs Finn's birthday lamb A colleague from Wembley once pointed the Rabbi to a book called Palestine Peasantry by Elizabeth Anne Finn , who lived in 19th-century Palestine and kept a diary. For her birthday she asked an Arab fellah (peasant) to roast her a lamb. He replied that he would do it, but by their tradition it could only be cooked together with milk, with leben . She asked whether this was not against the law of Moses. Yes, he said, and they did other things too, like cooking the young in its mother's milk and kilayim , practices that stood in direct contrast to the Torah. Weaning us off idolatry The Rambam explains many mitzvot this way: not using a razor on the beard, not rounding the corners of the head, not making tattoos. These were the practices of the idolaters when the Torah was given. One can object that pagans today do not eat meat with milk. But the whole idea of the Torah is Yichud Hashem , the unity of God, and shattering the idols , not only physical idols but theological ones. Many mitzvot, says the Rambam, serve to take us away from idolatrous practice, and meat and milk may be one of them. Part 6: Category Three: Terefah Source Reason Rambam Health: the animal is diseased Ramban Terefah literally means torn , killed by another animal. It has been subjected to cruelty, and eating it breeds cruelty in us Rav Shimshon Raphael Hirsch (Germany, 19th century) The Torah says a torn animal is for the scavengers . Eating it brings us down to the food of animals. Refraining reminds us of our higher calling Interim summary The word kasher and the nine main things it entails. Giving reasons for mitzvot is important, but must be done with caution. Forbidden animals: physical health (Rambam) or spiritual health (Ramban). Meat and milk: disparate forces (Chinuch), cruelty (Ramban), gluttony (Rashbam), unhealthy or idolatrous (Rambam). Terefah: unhealthy, spiritually harmful from cruelty, or the food of scavengers. Part 7: A Holistic Approach: Four Kinds of Mitzvot In the last quarter of an hour the Rabbi offered a more holistic approach, based on an idea he had read some years ago in a book by Dennis Prager . There are four purposes of mitzvot: Reflexive mitzvot , which develop the person. Tefillah comes from lehitpallel , to judge oneself. Bikkurim , the first fruits, teach appreciation and gratitude. Tzitzit remind us of our duties and identity. Ethical mitzvot : murder, theft, perjury, and at a \"lower\" level charity, caring for the ill, the poor and the weak, and lashon hara. Being good does not happen by itself; the Legislator has to define what good is. \"Hitler thought he was doing something very good, ridding the world of vermin.\" Without a definition of good, a person can end up with a warped understanding of it. Laws of holiness. We are body and soul, in constant battle. The two main things we share with the animal kingdom are eating and procreation , and the Torah sanctifies both. We make a berachah before eating and Birkat HaMazon after, we wash our hands. There are forbidden relationships, and times when even a couple abstain. We also sanctify time : Shabbat and the Chagim. National mitzvot , which preserve our identity as a people: matzah , maror , Tisha b'Av , Purim , Chanukah . Kashrut, the Rabbi argued, contains all four. 1 and 2: Limiting meat, reflexively and ethically Keeping kosher limits which animals we may eat. The Rabbi previewed the next week's shiur: Rav Yosef Albo (15th century) writes that ideally we should not eat meat at all, and Rav Kook speaks of this too. Killing animals for our own benefit is cruel, but because of human nature and history, Hashem \"as it were changed His mind\" and permitted meat. The Torah describes eating meat as a craving . The Torah recognises it and says: you may, but in a certain way. Fewer species : kashrut limits how much and what kind of meat we eat. Ever min hachai : not eating a limb from a living animal. Shechita with a sharp knife, not a blunt one, so that there is minimum cruelty. Why the pig? Green and red lights Why a pig is forbidden and a lamb permitted may be like asking why green means go and red means stop. You can offer psychological explanations, but when driving we simply know: green is go, red is stop. The Rabbi brought a fascinating midrash, quoted by the Ohr HaChaim on Vayikra 11 : the Hebrew for pig is חזיר ( chazir ), from the root לחזור , to return. In the times of Mashiach, the pig will \"return\" and become permitted. \"Now, we're not yet in the times of Mashiach, just in case.\" What it shows is that, at least according to this midrash, there is nothing intrinsically wrong with the pig. The point is the limitation . Removing the blood: a built-in revulsion All the blood must be removed. If you want to eat meat, think about the blood that was spilled for it. The Rabbi reflected: Human beings get together to eat and enjoy each other's company. On what? Something they've killed together. We don't like to think about it whilst we're eating, but if you think about it, it's something crazy. Human nature needs to be recognised. Meat is allowed, but with limits: on how many animals, on how they are slaughtered so there is no pain, and on the blood, so that there is a revulsion at its shedding. That develops us reflexively and protects animal welfare ethically. 3: Holiness Kashrut gives self-discipline . We do not eat whatever we want, however we want, like animals. The Rabbi cited the Midrash (Tanchuma): the mitzvot were given to refine people , to refine our base instincts and bring holiness into our eating. 4: National identity When you travel, you ask: is there a community here? Is there kosher food? Kashrut brings you into contact with other Jews. The Rabbi told a story from his yeshiva days. A group of four or five \"irresponsible yeshiva boys\" went to Harrogate in Yorkshire for Shabbat with very little food. By the long summer Shabbat afternoon they had run out entirely. After Shabbat, what do you do when you are stuck without kosher food? \"Find the nearest Chabad.\" They went to Chabad in Leeds and stayed until the early hours of the morning, eating, learning Torah and having a good time together. The fact that they had to eat certain things forced them to connect with the Jewish community around them. Summary Every mitzvah has a reason (Rambam), but what we can offer are ta'amim , tastes, not the full depth. The Torah itself explains only two food laws: the gid hanasheh and blood . Forbidden animals: physical health (Rambam) or spiritual health, avoiding the traits of predators (Ramban). Meat and milk: disparate forces (Chinuch), cruelty (Ramban), gluttony (Rashbam), an idolatrous rite (Rambam's preferred reason). Terefah: disease (Rambam), cruelty (Ramban), the food of scavengers (Rav S. R. Hirsch). Holistically , kashrut offers four things: self-development , ethical treatment of animals , holiness and self-discipline , and national identity . Not everything adds up, and not everything comes together. But as we've said, it's a ta'am . It's a taste, it's a flavour. The Rabbi's closing thought: next time we sit down to a piece of meat, we should think about the whole process it has been through, and what that process should remind us of: our responsibilities to each other and to the world. Next week (Tuesday 21 May, 7.45pm): Is veganism compatible with Judaism? The pros and cons of vegetarianism in the Torah.","kTR61qWuHHs":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam); Rabbeinu Avraham's commentary on the Torah for the weekly parashah Chapter: Perek Yud (10) — Perishut , abstinence (Part 3: how to attain it) Topic: Rabbeinu Avraham on Parashat Chayei Sarah — why Yitzchak was \"coming back\" from באר לחי ראי , and why he moved there only after Avraham's death ; the intellectual path to perishut (sensual pleasure, clothes, homes and power are not who we are; Shlomo's verdict הכל הבל ); the real obstacle — habit ; the two stages (training for beginners, second nature for the prophets); Yom Kippur , the Rechavim and the Chovot HaLevavot as proofs; and the first practical guidance : assess yourself honestly, then change slowly — a ramp, not steps . Schedule note: Throughout the winter the HaMaspik shiur is Monday, 6:00–7:00pm , b'ezrat Hashem. Next week the Rabbi will skip ahead within Chapter 10 to Rabbeinu Avraham's long discussion of Yitzchak's blessings — it falls exactly on Parashat Toldot — and will take the whole shiur. There will be no separate parashah feature next week. We return to the section on fasting the week after. The Weekly Feature — Rabbeinu Avraham on the Parashah Each week we look at something small from Rabbeinu Avraham's commentary on the Torah , which survives on Bereishit and Shemot . Last week (Vayera) we saw three or four ideas — Yitzchak and Yishmael, the difference between the phrases of timing, and more. This week, Parashat Chayei Sarah , two ideas — \"which is really one idea\" — about the same place: באר לחי ראי . Part One — Rabbeinu Avraham on Parashat Chayei Sarah The setting Avraham sends his servant — called עבד throughout, identified as Eliezer — to find a wife for Yitzchak. Eliezer finds Rivka , negotiates with her father and brother, Betuel and Lavan , and brings her home. As they travel back, Yitzchak has gone out to meditate in the field — Chazal tell us, to pray Mincha — and on his way back he meets Rivka. She is in awe of him, and falls from the camel. ויצחק בא מבוא באר לחי ראי, והוא יושב בארץ הנגב. (Bereishit 24:62) Yitzchak was living with his father in the Negev , somewhere in the south — but he was coming back from Be'er Lachai Roi . Rabbeinu Avraham's two questions: Why does the verse stress that he was coming back ? What is Be'er Lachai Roi — and what was he doing there? 1. Why \"coming back\" from Be'er Lachai Roi? אין ספק כי המרחק רב בין באר לחי ראי ובין המקום שאברהם ויצחק היו שוכנים בו. There was quite a distance between Be'er Lachai Roi and where Avraham and Yitzchak lived. So why go there? אך יצחק רגיל להיפרד ממקום מושבו וללכת אל המדבר, אל הבאר הנזכרת, בדבקותו בעבודתו וחפצו לבקש התבודדות כדרך החסידים והנביאים, כמו שביארנו בספר המספיק בפרק ההתבודדות. Yitzchak regularly left home and went out into the wilderness, to that well, because of his attachment to his avodah and his wish for solitude — the way of the righteous and the prophets. Rabbeinu Avraham points to the chapter on hitbodedut in HaMaspik itself — Chapter 12 . ( We are in Chapter 10 now; there are 13 chapters. Chapter 12 is about the importance of quiet time alone — thinking, contemplating, meditating. ) ויתעכב שם זמן קצר, ושוב היה חוזר למקום מושבו. He would stay a short while, and then return home. Why the emphasis on returning? סיפר לנו הכתוב כי הפגישה הנזכרת היתה בזמן חזרת יצחק מהבאר, לא בזמן יציאתו. ועילת דבר זה, כפי הנראה לי — כדי להודיע לבני התורה שיצחק עליו השלום יצא לעבודתו, וחזר, משהגיעה רבקה עם אליעזר. ואילו היה פוגע בהם בהליכתו, לא היה חוזר מעבודתו; אלא היתה פגישתו עמהם אחר שגמר עבודתו. The meeting happened on Yitzchak's way back , not on his way out — \"so that students of Torah should know\" : had he met them on his way to his avodah , he would not have turned back — not even to meet his wife for the very first time . He met them only because he had already finished. On the way out On the way back What Yitzchak was doing Going to pray, meditate, be mitboded Had finished his avodah Would he have stopped for Rivka? No — he would not interrupt his avodah Yes — and he did What it teaches How deeply דבוק he was in his avodah The Rabbi: this is the awe that Yitzchak clearly carried — an awe Rivka saw, and fell from the camel because of it. He was so attached to his avodah that he only met her because he was already on his way home. 2. Why did Yitzchak move to Be'er Lachai Roi — and why only after Avraham died? The last verse in the parashah: ויהי אחרי מות אברהם, ויברך אלהים את יצחק בנו, וישב יצחק עם באר לחי ראי. (25:11) Until now he visited Be'er Lachai Roi; after Avraham's death he went to live there. ישיבתו של יצחק אצל הבאר — בשביל שהבחיר עליו השלום מצא שם עבודת השם; ובחיי אברהם לא היה יכול להיפרד ממנו, אלא היה חוזר ושב מאצל אביו אל הבאר ומן הבאר אצל אביו. Why there? Because Yitzchak found that this was where he truly connected to Hakadosh Baruch Hu — where his time alone developed his avodat Hashem. Why only now? Because while Avraham was alive, he could not leave his father . However much spiritual benefit he gained from his solitude, \"while my father is alive, I can't leave him.\" So he travelled back and forth — though it took a great deal of his time — because being with his father mattered . וכשנסתלק אברהם עליו השלום לעולם האמת, נסע יצחק וקבע מושבו אצל הבאר שבחר בה, בשביל סגולה שהיה מוצא שם. Once Avraham passed away, Yitzchak moved there and settled by the well he had chosen, because of the special quality he found there . The idea to take away Before Rabbeinu Avraham, we read Be'er Lachai Roi as a random place name. It isn't. It is the place where Yitzchak developed himself spiritually — the place he would have lived all along — and he waited only out of kibbud av . The significance of a place of solitude for one's avodah. The importance of honouring and looking after one's parents — even above one's own spiritual preference. Part Two — A Change of Plan for Next Week \"I don't like to skip.\" But Rabbeinu Avraham has a beautiful commentary on Parashat Toldot inside this very chapter on perishut — all about the blessings of Yitzchak, Esav and Yaakov . Learning in order, we would reach it in three weeks — after Toldot has passed. So: tonight we continue where we left off. Next week we skip ahead within Chapter 10 to the section on the blessings, with no separate parashah feature — \"it'll be really appropriate, and it's a beautiful explanation of the whole concept of the blessings; it will take the whole shiur, b'ezrat Hashem.\" Then we come back to the section on fasting . Part Three — Recap: Where We Are in Chapter 10 The human being is חומר (matter) and צורה (form/intellect) ; a person's identity needs both. Perishut is a vital trait. The prophets and the righteous chose simple work — farmers, shepherds — that freed them to focus on Hakadosh Baruch Hu; and the Torah shows its importance (consecrating the captured Canaanite cities, and more). The definition — the vital point: perishut is not living simply while constantly thinking about what you are giving up. True perishut sprouts from the heart. A true parush abstains and doesn't need to think about what he is missing. So a wealthy person can be a parush — it doesn't affect him; and a poor person who lives very simply may not be — he is thinking about it all the time. A person is where his heart is, where his mind is. Now: how do we attain it? Rabbeinu Avraham works in two stages — first intellectually , then practical advice . Part Four — The Intellectual Path to Perishut The battle is in the mind ובאשר לדרך השגתה של הפרישות האמיתית, כלומר זו שבלב — הרי מאבק לעמוד כנגד הדחף הטבעי, שכן אהבת העולם נטועה בלב האדם. Attaining real perishut — the kind in the heart — is a battle against our natural drive , because love of this world is planted in the human heart . It is part of our chomer . ואמר שלמה המלך: גם את העולם נתן בלבם. (Kohelet 3:11) (More on this in Chapter 11.) ברם עיקר המאבק — שיחליט האדם בדעתו החלטה נקייה מנגיעה אישית ומנטיית סמיות, כי תענוגות העולם הזה אינם תכלית היותו אדם. The main battle is an intellectual decision — clear, free of self-interest and bias — that the pleasures of this world are not the purpose of being human . They don't define who I am; they are auxiliary to my real identity. The Rabbi: before any practical step — fasting, simple clothing and so on — there is no point in any of it if you haven't first made the right intellectual choice. (1) Sensual pleasure — shared with animals שאפילו התענוגות היותר גדולים בנוגע לחושים, שמלכי הארץ משתמשים בכוח שליטתם — הם אכילה, שתייה ומשגל — הרי הם שותפים בהם עם הבהמות: כלב, חמור, חזיר ושאר בעלי חיים. Even the greatest sensual pleasures , the kind kings can command with all their power — the best food, drink and relations — we share with the animal kingdom . So that can't be your identity. Perhaps you enjoy more sensual pleasure than other people. \"Okay, lovely — but it hasn't changed who you are.\" The lion, king of the animals, may enjoy more than other animals — maybe, maybe not — but you are still sharing a trait with animals. Intellectually, this cannot be the purpose. We learned this in the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah , where the Rambam makes the same argument — and Rabbeinu Avraham takes it directly from his father. (2) The more physical, the more embarrassed ועוד צריך להתבונן, כי תענוגות העולם הזה, ככל שהם גסים ומגושמים ורחוקים מן האנושות — גם מגונים בדעת. \"A very interesting point.\" The more sensual something is, the more embarrassed we are by it. \"Have you ever thought about that?\" ולכן יצניע האדם את עצמו בשעת המשגל, ולא בשעת אכילה... ויש שיצניעו בשעת אכילה ושתיית מים. Act How private we are about it Marital relations Almost everyone conceals it Eating Most don't mind eating in public — but more sensitive people prefer to eat privately Drinking Most don't mind — the even more sensitive won't even drink in front of others Smelling The least physical — \"I don't know anyone who's embarrassed to smell something in front of other people\" (More on this in Chapter 13.) The conclusion: it cannot be that this is the reason Hashem made us. We share it with animals, and we are naturally embarrassed by it. (3) Fine clothes — only appearance ואשר לבגדים נאים — כיוון שמיוחדים הם לאדם על פני בעלי חיים שאין בהם דעה, יתפאר האדם בהם, כמו שנאמר: הדרת אדם לבושו. Animals don't wear clothes; we do — so a person likes to think his clothes exalt him. Chazal say \"a person's clothes are his glory\" — they can tell you a lot about him. ואף על פי כן, באמת תמצא כי נוי הבגדים אינו אלא דמיון... בו יוכל אדם להטעות זולתו לחשוב שיש בו גדולה. But beautiful clothing is only an image — a way to impress , not quite deceive, others into thinking we have some greatness, power or wealth. הלא רוב בני אדם מוציאים זמנם לריק בסידור המצנפת, ובשיעור עטיית הבגד, ובצחצוח הנעליים, וסוברים שמעשיהם מורים על נוי — ואינם יודעים שזו שפלות וגאווה ושיגעון. Most people waste their time perfecting the hat, making sure the suit or dress is just right, polishing their shoes — and think this shows they are someone special. \"And that's all there is; there's nothing more to it.\" The proof: if a person were alone, where no one could see him — would he bother to put on his finest clothes? \"If you're alone, you take your clothes off. So that can't be who you are.\" (4) Mansions — for other people's eyes ואותם המסתופפים בבתי פאר — אינם בוחרים בהם על פי רוב אלא כדי שיתגדלו בעיני הבריות, ולא כדי שימצאו שם מקום נאה. Most people who live in huge, luxurious houses do so to gain approval from others — not because it is truly nicer. שאם לא כן — הרי מראה גן, או כברת דשא, או שדה רענן, משובבים את הנפש מאוד יותר. והלא אם יכלאו אדם לחודש תמים בתוך ארמון המלך, שתקרתו זהב ורצפתו כסף — תצטער נפשו על פי רוב, וישתוקק אל מראה שדה עשב. \"He says something so true.\" A patch of green grass refreshes the soul far more. Lock a person for a whole month inside the king's palace — gold ceiling, silver floor — and he will be longing to get out and see some grass, some green . A balance — \"most of the time\": the Gemara in Berachot says אשה נאה, דירה נאה וכלים נאים מרחיבים דעתו של אדם — a pleasant home broadens a person's mind , and the Rambam speaks of this too. \"It's true — it is nice to live in a clean, nice house.\" If you have it, it can help you think clearly. But most people who chase these things aren't doing it for that — they want the biggest, the most expensive . (5) Power and authority — the most subtle of all וכן אהבת השררה והשלטון — אף שהיא דבר שבנפש, הרי היא אהבת דבר שאינו חלק מתכונת האדם ועצמותו, אלא אדרבה, דבר הטורד מן השלמות. Power is not physical — the most subtle thing so far. But it still isn't who you are — and worse, it distracts from perfection . כמו שאמר שלמה: שמוני נוטרה את הכרמים, כרמי שלי לא נטרתי. (Shir HaShirim 1:6) It pulls you away from yourself. \"I was made guardian of everyone else's vineyards — but my own vineyard I didn't keep.\" You are so busy dealing with the people under you that you never deal with yourself. It depends on others. If a person were alone, he would have no authority at all. You need other people under you to have it — so clearly it is not part of who you are . What people pursue Why it can't be the purpose Food, drink, relations We share them with animals — and we are embarrassed by them Fine clothes Only an image to impress others — alone, you wouldn't bother Mansions Mostly for others' approval — a field of grass refreshes more Power and authority Distracts from yourself — and needs others to exist at all The conclusion — and Shlomo HaMelech's experiment הוי אומר: מי שמתעסק בכל אלו — בתענוגות החושים, ובדמיונות שבהם, ובשררה — הרי הוא מתעסק בדברים שמחוץ לעצמותו, ואינם שלמות. ועליהם נאמר: משמרים הבלי שוא, חסדם יעזובו. (Yonah 2:9) Whoever focuses on these is developing things outside himself, which are not perfection — \"you're wasting your time.\" וידוע אתה כי שלמה המלך השיג מן החכמה והבינה מה שלא השיג איש זולתו; ועל פי עדותו התענג בכל מיני תענוגות וקנה רכוש... ורצה להבין מה התועלת בכל אלה כפי חכמתו ובינתו — ומצא שאין בהם חפץ. Shlomo HaMelech had everything — and, with his unmatched wisdom, examined what it was all worth: תרתי בלבי למשוך ביין את בשרי... הגדלתי מעשי, בניתי לי בתים... וכל אשר שאלו עיני לא אצלתי מהם... ופניתי אני בכל מעשי שעשו ידי ובעמל שעמלתי לעשות — והנה הכל הבל ורעות רוח, ואין יתרון תחת השמש. (Kohelet 2) I tried wine; I built myself houses; whatever my eyes asked for, I didn't deny them — and then I looked at it all: it is all vanity, and there is no lasting gain. Full stop. Part Five — The Real Obstacle: Habit \"Intellectually, I think this is clear. I don't think any of us should struggle with this.\" We know our pleasures, homes, clothes — even our power — are not who we are. So what's the problem? אלא שההישג הזה על ידי ההגות השכלית — סותרים אותו הרגלי המעשים היומיומיים. The daily rush of life — and our habits — make us forget these intellectual truths. Anyone with a brain knows what we've said is true. \"But if you tell me, now start living according to it — that's too hard. I know it's true, but I don't want to.\" Rabbeinu Avraham's examples: it's all about what you're used to Example The point The man with a chariot — מי שרגיל לרכוב על סוסי מרכבות מתבייש ללכת בשוק כשהוא הולך רגלי Used to riding, he is embarrassed to walk — he'd rather eat dry bread at home than be seen without his horse. (Today: always driven by your driver, and embarrassed to go without.) The merchant — הסוחר... הולך ונושא סחורתו בשוק ואינו מתבייש A peddler who always walks, carrying his goods, isn't embarrassed at all . The married man and the single man A married man finds it hard to be away from his wife even one week ; one who is unmarried, or travels abroad for business, can manage far longer. As the Gemara says: אינו דומה מי שיש לו פת בסלו למי שאין לו פת בסלו . Fasting several days — הצם הרבה ימים רצופים, יתענה ברעב ביום ראשון יותר מביום שני, וביום שני יותר מביום שלישי, עד שיעשה לו הצום טבע שני The first day is the hardest ; each day gets easier, until fasting becomes second nature . The lesson: the more used to something we are, the harder it is to give up — and the reverse is also true. Stage one — summary Recognise the intellectual truth, by contemplating the reality that what most people seek — the best food, drink, women, clothes, homes and power — is not intrinsic to a person's identity : we share it with animals, and we are naturally embarrassed by it. We know it; habit stops us living it. Part Six — The Two Stages: Training and Second Nature והוא הדין בכל דברי העולם הזה ובמראיתן. ולכן שקדו החסידים והנביאים על התשת הרגלי העולם, נוסף על התמדת פרישת הלב. So the righteous and the prophets worked to break their worldly habits — in addition to the perishut of the heart. A question raised at the start of the chapter The Rabbi: this whole chapter seems to contradict the Rambam in the Eight Chapters ( Shemonah Perakim , which we learned a couple of years ago), where he says it is wrong to forsake the pleasures of this world — and here Rabbeinu Avraham seems to say the opposite. We'll deal with that in depth, probably next time — but this is the opening. Stage 1 — the beginners: external training לבשו צמר והסתפקו במאכלים גסים, וקשרו עצמם במעט שבתענית; וקצתם פרשו מנשים, ונעתקו ממגוריהם, והתגוררו במערות הרים וגבעות רחוקים מן הישוב. To break the habit, some wore simple wool , ate coarse food , took on some fasting ; some separated from their wives for a period (as Moshe Rabbeinu did), or left home to live in caves , far from settlement. We have evidence there were Jews like this — Rabbeinu Bachya writes about it. וכל זה למי שהתחיל בהרגלים האלה, כמו תלמידי הנביאים בתחילת הכשרתם. But note this line: all of this is for beginners — like the students of the prophets at the start of their training. This is not the real thing. The real thing is in the heart. Stage 2 — second nature ומי שהתבסס בחסידות, וכל שכן הנביאים, התחזקו בעבודה כזאת שנעשתה להם הפרישות טבע שני, ואין לבם הולך אחר מה שאיננו בגדר ההכרח שנצרך לחייו... כגון אם יאכל פת חרבה או מרקחת מתוקה, או ילבש משי או פשתן גס או צמר גס או כותנה; וכן הדבר בארמונות וסוכות. Once fully developed — and certainly the prophets — perishut becomes second nature . It makes no difference to them: Food: \"Serve me the best meat or dry bread — I need food to live and to serve God. Whatever you give me, I don't care.\" Clothes: \"The nicest silk or a simple thick garment — all I need is to be warm and covered.\" Home: \"Give me the mansion or a tent in the desert — makes no difference to me.\" כי הפרישות שבלבם נשתרשה בהם, עד כדי כך ששוב אין להם צורך בהרחקה חיצונית כדי להישמר מן העבירות. It is ingrained in the heart — they no longer need external distancing . \"This is what I told you last week, the week before: it's all about the heart, all about the intellect. Perishut is not about actions; it's about recognising.\" והמה החסידים אשר הזכירם הכתוב, כגון אליהו ואלישע ונביאים אחרים ההולכים בעקבותיהם... אבל מי שקטן מהם — מצטרך להתרחקות. The beginner The developed (Eliyahu, Elisha, the prophets) Knows the truth intellectually? Yes Yes Lives it? Habit gets in the way It is second nature Needs fasting, simple clothes, distance? Yes — as training No Food / clothes / home \"Meat is too much of a pleasure — I'd better fast\" \"Makes no difference to me\" The key point: the training is necessary for the beginner — not because it's the real thing , but to get out of the habit of enjoying these things. You mustn't think it is the תכלית. Even great people had to train Gechazi , the student of Elisha — הלא ראית איך שהביא העולם הזה את גיחזי לחשוב באותה שעה בכסף ובגדים — fell and ran after money, because it was not yet second nature to him; he was still training . Abba Chilkiya, Chanan HaNechba and their colleagues were constantly working to engrain it. David: זכור ה' לדוד את כל ענותו — remember all the training I went through. Daniel: לחם חמודות לא אכלתי, ובשר ויין לא בא אל פי, וסוך לא סכתי — the times he restrained himself. Yeshayahu: כאשר הלך עבדי ישעיהו ערום ויחף — walked barefoot and unclothed. The prophets all practised some abstinence at some point — but that was not the end goal. The end goal is that it becomes second nature. A parallel from the chapter on bitachon The Rabbi recalled the summer, when we learned Chapter 8, Bitachon : three levels of bitachon, the highest being full confidence that what you ask for will be given. Then Rabbeinu Avraham described a fourth level — \"I'm not even sure you can call this bitachon\" — where you have so absorbed the intellectual truth that whatever happens is the best from Hakadosh Baruch Hu that you no longer need to trust that anything will or won't happen. \"It's paradoxical — almost a kind of apathy. Not apathy in a negative sense, but: I don't care, because whatever Hakadosh Baruch Hu gives me is the best.\" Perishut is the same: I don't care whether you give me the best clothes or the worst, the best home or the worst, the best food or the worst — it's so ingrained in me that I don't get excited, or not excited, about these things. Of course this is an extremely high level. But it can be applied: how excited do we get by physical, sensual things? Are we more excited when we learn and understand something — or when we get a new kind of meat to eat? These are questions we need to ask. Part Seven — The Proofs That Perishut Is Valued ושמא תאמר: עניינם של פסוקים אלה אינם מעידים אלא היות הפרישות עיקרה לבקש רחמים לעת צרה? Perhaps you'll say: the prophets fasted and went barefoot not because perishut is valuable , but only to beseech Hashem in a time of trouble . על כך אשיבך: כי דווקא כאן מוציאים אנו ראיה צחה ועדות שלימה על עוצם שבח הפרישות ומעלתו היתרה, ורצון הבורא יתעלה בו. That is exactly the proof. If abstinence is the means people use to come close to Hashem and seek His mercy — that shows how valued it is . (1) Yom Kippur ולכן חיברה התורה את העינוי עם הכפרה: ובחודש השביעי בעשור לחודש תענו את נפשותיכם... כי ביום הזה יכפר עליכם. (Vayikra 16:29–30) The Torah ties atonement to affliction. On the day of atonement — afflict yourselves by not eating and drinking — for on this day He will atone for you. \"What better proof do you want?\" Abstinence is a means of coming close to Hashem — and the ultimate atonement depends on it. In the heart — but you may need to get there through physical training. (2) The will of Yonadav ben Rechav ממה שהובא בצוואת יונדב בן רכב לבניו — שידורו באהלים ולא ישתו יין — ושילם ה' שכרם על שקיימו מצות אביהם עליהם, כמו שמספר ירמיהו. (Yirmiyahu 35) Yonadav ben Rechav — who helped King Yehu — commanded his descendants in his will never to drink wine and always to live in tents . They kept it, and Hashem rewarded them , as Yirmiyahu relates. (3) The Chovot HaLevavot — even the false prophets knew it וכמה ברור מה שכתב רבינו בחיי בחובות הלבבות, שהפרישות היא מנהג החסידים. (Chovot HaLevavot, Sha'ar 6) How correct is Rabbeinu Bachya: perishut is the practice of the righteous . His proof from Zechariah : ולא ילבשו אדרת שער למען כחש. (Zechariah 13:4) Zechariah prophesies against the false prophets : a time will come when they will no longer wear the hairy cloak to deceive . It was the practice of false prophets to present themselves as holy — \"holier-than-thou\" — by dressing simply. Which shows that living simply was recognised as a sign of spiritual level — so perishut is an important trait. End of the section — summary The intellectual way to perishut is to contemplate the falseness of physical things . At first, a person needs external acts — fasting, simplicity — to break the habit of pursuing worldly pleasure. But that is only the beginning. The real level is that it makes no difference to him or her whether the food is good or not, because abstaining has become second nature — the perishut of the chassidim and the prophets. Part Eight — Practical Guidance Begins וכאן הנני פותח לך דרך המאבק הזה על ידי הדרכות הלכה למעשה, הנהגה כללית. \"Intellectually I've explained how to attain perishut. Now practically.\" General guidelines — \"there are lots of examples, and I'll try to tell you as much as I can.\" Principle 1 — habit cannot be changed in one go עליך לדעת כי ההרגל, ביחוד אם נתאחד עם הטבע, אי אפשר לשנותו בבת אחת — אלא אם קרוב העניין למעשה ניסים; ואל תנסה. Habit — especially habit joined to our nature — cannot be changed all at once , unless by something close to a miracle — and don't rely on miracles . \"This is the most simple thing — but it's worth repeating. This is the beauty of Rabbeinu Avraham: he tells us the most basic things, in a beautiful way.\" Not from one day to the next, not one month to the next, not even one year to the next — it is the work of a lifetime, and must be done extremely slowly. Principle 2 — first, an honest assessment ולכן חייב אתה קודם כל לבדוק באיזו מידה שוגה לבך באהבת העולם הזה, ולבחון את הרגליך הקודמים שתמכו בעניין הזה. \"Oh, this is so beautiful.\" Before you take on anything practical, you need to do something intellectual: (a) How strongly is my heart attached to the pleasures of this world? (b) How habitual have I been in them? \"That's not something you do in five minutes.\" It's something to think about over a period of time. The Rabbi's scale: say 10 is complete love for these things and 0 is no interest at all. If you're at 8 or 9 and you start fasting — you'll go crazy. Rabbeinu Avraham will say later that you will be emotionally damaged . So first, assess where you stand. ואז אם נתעוררת שתוכל להתחיל בדבר זה, בדרך שנהגו הנביאים — תתחיל לשנות את הרגליך מעט מעט, מדרגה אחר מדרגה; ולא תחפוז, כי כל דבר בחפזון ישוב ריקם. Then, if you feel ready — change your habits little by little . Don't rush : whatever is done in haste comes back empty-handed — it falls back in your face . A ramp, not steps — ולא תעלה במעלות ולא תעלה במעלות על מזבחי. (Shemot 20:23, end of Parashat Yitro) The Rabbi cited this famous verse — and believes the Vilna Gaon explains it this way. You may not build steps up to the altar; it must be a ramp . Steps mean jumping from level to level. \"You don't jump in avodat Hashem. You go very gradually — 1.1, 1.2, 1.3 — slowly but surely, up the ramp.\" The worked example: five dishes והנה לך דוגמה: אם היית מן החובבים את עניין האכילה, ורגיל אתה ללפת את הפת בכמה מאכלי תאווה בסעודה אחת, כדרכם של המפונקים, ואז גמרת אומר לעבור אל דרך החסידים... שמסתפקים בפת חרבה וכיוצא בזה — You've done your self-assessment and realise: \"I like my food.\" At every meal you have your main dish with all kinds of sides — not a one-off celebration, but generally — and now you decide to live like the chassidim, on dry bread. הרי הדרך שבה תגיע למטרתך — איננה על ידי שינוי פתאומי... כי דרך זו לא תצלח; וגם אם תצלח, תביא אחריה מבוכה חיצונית ופנימית. A sudden change will not work. And listen to this: even if it does work, it will bring external and internal confusion. (Later he says it damages you emotionally and spiritually.) \"We all know people who go cold turkey — give something up from one day to the next. It doesn't work. And even if it does, it causes issues.\" והדרכה הנכונה היא: שאם היית רגיל לאכול בסעודה אחת כמה מיני מאכלים, תפחית מעט מספרם — למשל אם היו חמישה, תעמיד אותם על ארבעה, ותשהה ימים או חודשים או שנים, עד שתמצא שהסתגלת לזה... Step What you do Until when 1. Number of dishes Five → four → three → two → one Each stage until you no longer miss the previous one — days, months or years 2. Quality From tasty to necessary : cooked meat with vegetables → plainer food; delicacies like dates and sweet things → vegetables and cheese Until it becomes part of your nature 3. Quantity Less of the simple food Until you eat only what the body needs The weights analogy: \"You don't jump to 20 kilos. You go 5, 10, 15 — you've done 10 for two or three weeks, now 11, now 12.\" Same here, in reverse: five dishes to four, and stay at four until it is part of you; then three, and so on — until you have forgotten you ever ate five , as if you had always lived this way. (Of course — taking health and everything else into account.) וזהו שאמר אליהו עליו השלום: עשי לי משם עוגה קטנה בראשונה. (Melachim I 17:13) Eliyahu asked the widow only for a small cake — that's all I need. What we can take from this \"This example is extremely far from me, at least — I even feel uncomfortable talking about eating only simple foods. We all have our pleasures, our guilty pleasures, foods we know aren't good for our health.\" But there is a point here we do need to work on: How attached are we to the sensual pleasures of this world? Try to decrease that slightly — each on our own level . Establish honestly where you are first , and only then make a plan. Take it slowly. Go too fast and you'll have physical, emotional and spiritual problems. Summary Rabbeinu Avraham on Chayei Sarah: Yitzchak used to travel the distance from the Negev to Be'er Lachai Roi for solitude and avodah (the hitbodedut of Chapter 12). The Torah stresses he was coming back when he met Rivka — on his way out , he would not have stopped even for her. He moved there only after Avraham died , because while his father lived he would not leave him — kibbud av above his own spiritual preference. The intellectual path: the battle is first a clear decision that this world's pleasures are not the purpose of being human . Sensual pleasure we share with animals, and the more physical it is, the more embarrassed we are. Clothes are an image for others; mansions are mostly for others' approval — a field of grass refreshes more; power pulls us from ourselves and depends on others. Shlomo HaMelech tried everything: הכל הבל . The obstacle is habit — the chariot-rider and the peddler, the married man and the traveller, the faster whose first day is hardest. So beginners need external training (simple clothes, fasting, solitude) — but it is not the goal . For Eliyahu, Elisha and the prophets , perishut became second nature : food, clothes and home make no difference , like the fourth level of bitachon . Proofs that perishut is valued: Yom Kippur ties atonement to affliction; the Rechavim were rewarded; and even false prophets wore the hairy cloak. Practically: habit can't be changed in one go . First assess how attached you are; then change little by little — a ramp, not steps ( ולא תעלה במעלות ): five dishes to four, then plainer food, then less — until you no longer remember wanting more. Cold turkey fails — and even when it works, it does damage. Coming Up Next week, Monday 6:00pm: a skip ahead within Chapter 10 to Rabbeinu Avraham's long explanation of Yitzchak's blessings to Yaakov and Esav — timed for Parashat Toldot , taking the whole shiur. The question: if everything in this chapter is true, why does Yitzchak bless with physical things — and why does he ask for food before blessing? The week after: back to the section on fasting , and the apparent contradiction with the Rambam's Eight Chapters . Later: Chapter 11 , and Chapter 12 on hitbodedut . B'Ezrat Hashem","UIDUt-BBuSM":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), Part 3 of the introduction Shiur: Part 3 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 20 May 2025 These notes are based on a machine transcript of the recording (no Sofer transcript exists for this class). Tonight's Question \"You know the saying: two Jews, three opinions. How did this come about? Is it correct practice? What can't be argued about, and what can? \" Recap Part 1 The written Torah came with an oral explanation , taught Hashem → Moshe → Aharon → his sons → elders → people, everyone four times , with a final 37-day review 2 A prophet has no intellectual authority over any Sage: 1,000 prophets vs 1,001 Sages, follow the Sages . A prophet claiming the law is X because I'm a prophet is put to death. His one power: a temporary , non-idolatrous suspension of a mitzvah (Eliyahu's sacrifice at Carmel; break Shabbat this Shabbat ) That was a digression, to show that the Oral Law is about transmission by Sages , not prophetic authority. (Prophets were part of the chain, as we'll see tonight, but held no superiority in it.) Now: how do disputes arise? Part 1: What Each Generation Handed On When Yehoshua died, he passed on three bodies of Torah: Body Dispute? 1 Everything Moshe received from Hashem Never 2 What was derived since Moshe through the 13 principles , agreed by all No 3 What was derived and disputed , then decided by majority (say 10 against 9), passed on with the ruling Disputed, then decided Remember: Moshe received the principles and some details. Other details were left for each generation's Sages to derive. \" Each generation more and more is derived , because that's how Torah works. There's always more to learn, always more to extrapolate.\" The chain Yehoshua → the elders → the prophets , prophet to prophet → the Men of the Great Assembly , 120 of the greatest Sages of their time, who also derived new laws. The last of them, Shimon HaTzaddik , also Kohen Gadol , about 2,100 years ago, counts as the first of the Sages . Prophecy ends; the dispute with the Sadducees begins; the era of the Chachamim starts. Then, generation after generation, to… Part 2: Rabbeinu HaKadosh Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi , called Rabbeinu HaKadosh , \"our holy Rabbi\": Wisdom The highest level Torah and greatness together The Gemara: from Moshe to Rebbi we never saw Torah and prominence and wealth combined in one person Nasi A prince, and extremely wealthy Piety and humility He distanced himself from physical pleasure. Dying, he raised his ten fingers and said: Hashem is my witness I never enjoyed the world even with my little finger Language Impeccable, unlike anyone's. His students learned the meaning of words from his maidservant \"To do a major project, it's not enough to be wise and have great language. You also need prominence and authority. His wealth helped him do it.\" (At least, that's the Rambam's implication.) He gathered everything received from Moshe until his day: the accepted and the disputed. \"Isn't that incredible?\" The Rambam traces his chain: Rebbi ← Shimon his father ← Gamliel ← Shimon ← Gamliel ← Shimon ← Hillel ← Shemaya and Avtalyon ← Yehudah ben Tabbai and Shimon ben Shetach ← Yehoshua ben Perachyah and Nittai HaArbeli ← Yosei ben Yoezer and Yosei ben Yochanan ← Antigonos ← Shimon HaTzaddik ← Ezra ← Baruch ben Neriyah ← Yirmeyahu . Here it stops. In the Introduction to the Mishneh Torah he continues: Yirmeyahu ← Tzefanyah ← Chabakuk ← Nachum ← Yoel ← Michah ← Yeshayahu ← Amos ← Hoshea ← Zecharyah ← Yehoyada ← Elisha ← Eliyahu ← Achiyah ← David ← Shmuel ← Eli ← Pinchas ← Yehoshua ← Moshe ← Hashem . Rav Kook , opening schools as guest speaker, would line up about a hundred students , each one a generation: you're Moshe, you're Yehoshua, you're Pinchas… and this is our generation. \"It's absolutely incredible that we can literally trace back, generation to generation .\" Part 3: What No One Ever Disputed Whatever came from Moshe has no dispute. Verse Received meaning Disputed? עין תחת עין Monetary compensation \"We never heard any rabbi say it means a literal eye\" פרי עץ הדר Etrog Never ענף עץ עבות Hadas (myrtle) Never וקצתה את כפה (the woman who grabs) Money Never בת כהן who sins is burnt Only if married or betrothed (the plain verse doesn't say so) Never Then why does the Gemara search for sources? Bava Kamma (start of chapter 8) spends two full pages on eight or nine derivations that an eye for an eye means money. Sukkah gives about four sources that pri etz hadar means etrog. Why, if it's all from Moshe? To hang the law on the verse. \"From an educational point of view, the best way to remember something is to connect it to a verse we know by heart.\" Hadar = הדר , \"that dwells \" on its tree from year to year , as the etrog does. Or הדר על פני מים , \"that grows by water .\" \"Many fruits live from year to year. They know 100% it's etrog. They're arguing only about which verse to hang it on . I say this mnemonic, you say that. \" Part 4: Then What Is Halachah leMoshe miSinai ? The question: Tefillin must be black. No source in the Torah. (It isn't rabbinic either: wear non-black tefillin every day and you've never fulfilled the mitzvah . Rabbinic law like muktzeh , of course, has no verse.) The Sages call it הלכה למשה מסיני . But by the Rambam's account, everything received is from Moshe at Sinai. So why label only some? The answer: Over time the Sages found some anchor in the verse for most received laws ( an eye for an eye = money). For a few they couldn't : not even a hint . For black tefillin, the only authority is Moshe . Those are called halachah leMoshe miSinai . The Rambam lists them , \"before there was an encyclopedia, before you could Google\": about 25 across all of Shas. \" I think it's all of them, but perhaps I forgot one. \" Examples: Tefillin are black. Every Torah \"eating\" means a kezayit . Sukkah walls: halachically you may \" bend \" a wall, or extend it up or down . Mikveh: the rules of interpositions ( chatzitzot ). Part 5: The Five Categories The whole Torah divides into five parts. # Category Example Dispute? 1 Explanations received from Moshe , with an anchor in the verse Eye for eye = money; etrog Never. Once received generation to generation, no argument 2 Halachah leMoshe miSinai : received, no anchor Black tefillin; kezayit Never , and forbidden to dispute 3 Derived through the 13 principles Yes , and decided by majority 4 Gezerot : fences the Sages made to protect the Torah Muktzeh ; poultry and milk Yes , and decided by majority 5 Takkanot : beneficial enactments Studying each festival's laws 30 days before it ; the enactments of Usha ; Ezra's ten enactments ; prozbul ; immersion for a ba'al keri Yes On category 3: \"If it's received, I must accept it. If you derived it, I can argue back .\" That's why the Gemara always asks what are they arguing about? What's the reason? \"I want to understand your rationale, because then I have the right to disagree.\" On category 4: Rabbi Yosei HaGelili 's town ate poultry with milk : he didn't feel the fence was needed. \" You feel it needs a fence; I'm the rabbi in my city, I feel it doesn't. \" Some fences were later lifted : there was a time gentile oil was forbidden, then abolished. Gezerot vs takkanot: a gezerah is stronger, a decree or fence ( don't, or X may happen ). A takkanah is good practice instituted for our benefit, which became law once accepted . Part 6: Why Disputes Multiplied The Gemara (Sotah 47b): משרבו תלמידי שמאי והלל שלא שמשו כל צרכן, רבו מחלוקות בישראל ונעשית תורה כשתי תורות. When the students of Shammai and Hillel multiplied who had not served their teachers enough , disputes multiplied, and the Torah became like two Torahs. The wrong reading Many people read this as applying to categories 1 and 2 : students didn't listen properly, so what was transmitted got garbled , \" Chinese whispers .\" Moshe said black tefillin; the students half-heard; \"black, grey, something…\" The Rambam: people who say this don't understand. \"One of the worst statements I've ever heard.\" It kills the whole transmission . Categories 1 and 2 are intact and factual , even when students didn't fully learn from their teachers. The right reading: method, not memory Disputes belong only to category 3. Why did they increase? Two people with full intellectual ability, the same rigorous training from their teachers, given the same text to derive from: 99 times out of 100, they'll reach the same answer. There's human diversity, so some minor disagreements, which existed even between Beit Hillel and Beit Shammai and before, but the core derivations come out the same. When that skill isn't properly honed , the derivations diverge more and more . So there was far less dispute before Hillel and Shammai and their students (about 1,900 years ago), and the further we go, the more . \"Contrary to what we're told to believe: yes, difference of opinion can be good. But we'll agree to disagree, I've got my opinion : an opinion reached without proper intellectual rigour means nothing to me. \" A correction about today \"The amount of dispute in contemporary halachah increases each generation…\" The Rabbi retracted that wording: Today's Sages, even the greatest, aren't doing category 3 at all. Category 3 ended with the Gemara . Nor categories 1 and 2 (\"they weren't with Moshe\"), nor 4 and 5 (no authority for new gezerot and takkanot ). No rabbi can stand up and derive a new law from the verse. All they can do is apply the Talmud's derivations to new situations. Electricity on Shabbat or Yom Tov: \"I can't go to the Torah and derive what it says about electricity. I don't have the authority. All I can do is look in the Talmud and extrapolate from its extrapolations.\" Part 7: Then Why Does the Mishnah Record Minority Views? If the law follows the majority, why did Rebbi , whom the Rambam praises so highly, write the Sages say X, Rabbi Meir says Y ? Why not just the final law? \"We touched on this in the letter to Rav Pinchas HaDayan .\" Pinchas asked why the Mishneh Torah gives no dissenting views, when Rebbi did . The answer: it took time for the halachah to crystallise. Poultry and milk. Most Sages made the fence; Rabbi Yosei HaGelili didn't. Rebbi writes it's forbidden . A grandson of someone from Rabbi Yosei HaGelili's town walks around eating chicken with milk, following his town's custom ( minhag was much stronger then ). Is he doing wrong? No : he's under Rabbi Yosei HaGelili's authority . So the Mishnah tells you: there is such an opinion; we don't follow it; but if you meet such a person, he has a tradition and can't be criticised. ** Today , no community traces back to Rabbi Yosei HaGelili: \"someone who says I eat chicken and milk because I follow Rabbi Yosei HaGelili : you throw him out. \" Already in the Rambam's time no one could claim that chain, \" so there's no point in my Mishneh Torah writing opposing views.\" Summary Each generation passed on three bodies: what came from Moshe ; what was derived and agreed ; what was derived, disputed and decided by majority . The chain: Moshe → Yehoshua → elders → prophets → Great Assembly → Shimon HaTzaddik (first of the Sages) → … → Rabbeinu HaKadosh , uniquely wise, wealthy, pious and eloquent, who gathered it all. Rav Kook's line of a hundred students. Received laws are never disputed (eye for eye = money; etrog; hadas). The Gemara's many \"sources\" just anchor them to verses, for memory. Halachah leMoshe miSinai = received laws with no anchor at all (black tefillin, kezayit): about 25 , all listed by the Rambam. Five categories: received with anchor; received without; derived ; gezerot ; takkanot . Only 3–5 can be disputed, decided by majority. Disputes multiplied not from \"Chinese whispers\" (\"one of the worst statements\") but because the skill of derivation weakened. Same method → same answer, 99 times in 100. No one derives new law today ; we apply the Talmud's derivations (electricity). The Mishnah records minority views because communities still followed them; that's long over , so the Mishneh Torah doesn't. Coming Up Next week: more on dispute, and the structure of the Mishnah .","ghUElbEEiXk":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim, Siman 321 (סימן שכ\"א) Seifim covered: 9–13 and 19 (completing tochen, with the Rema), then the introduction to lash (Seifim 14–16 come next week). Topic: The last heterim of מלאכת טוחן : cooked meat and fish, grating cheese, why a grater is muktzeh but an egg slicer is not , cutting דק דק , the Rashba's heter of grinding סמוך לסעודה (and whether Sefardim rely on it), and finishing what was already mostly crushed. A practical summary sheet of tochen. Then מלאכת לש : the machloket Rabbi and Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehuda (is putting the water in already kneading?), the Weetabix case, and the three levels of mixture: בלילה עבה, בלילה רכה , and no mixture at all. Schedule note: Thursday, 6:00–7:00pm on Zoom. \"We will be continuing in Hilchot Shabbat until at least we finish this chapter, for the time being.\" Where We Are Siman 321 has three big topics: pickling , tochen (crushing) and lash (kneading). Last week: Pickling (עיבוד באוכלים): a rabbinic prohibition, either because it resembles tanning (מעבד) , as Rashi explains, or because it resembles cooking (בישול) , as the Rambam explains. We went through when salting is permitted and when it is not. Tochen: grinding, crushing, mashing, and even cutting very small , all forbidden. We learned the first three heterim (below). Tonight: finish tochen, then begin \"a major and really complicated, but we will make it clear,\" מלאכת לש . Part One — Completing מלאכת טוחן Recap: the first three heterim # Heter What it means Example 1 שינוי גדול (Seif 7) Crushing with the back of a knife or the underside of a cup : such an extreme deviation that the melacha becomes permitted Mashing avocado with the back of a fork; crushing pepper with a knife handle 2 אין טוחן אחר טוחן Something already ground and reconstituted may be ground again Crumbling bread back into \"flour\"; crushing crackers by hand 3 Already edible + not גידולי קרקע (Seif 9) Two מחלוקות ראשונים combined Cooked meat and chicken may be cut very fine Two conditions on heter 1: It may not be done into or onto a place set aside for grinding : a regular chopping board is fine , a mortar is not . The Rabbi promised a summary sheet of common tochen questions, \"which I wrote some time ago\" (below). Seif 9 — Cooked meat, raw meat מותר לחתוך בשר מבושל או צלי דק דק בסכין. You may cut cooked or roasted meat very finely with a knife, in the regular way : not with a shinui, and it was never ground before. Why? Two machlokot combine: Machloket One side The other side Halacha Does tochen apply to food that is already edible? Most Rishonim: yes, even edible food The Ba'al HaTerumah (and possibly the Rambam, \"though that's debatable\" ): tochen only applies to inedible food, which the crushing makes edible We don't follow the lenient view: the Shulchan Aruch applies tochen even to vegetables Are animals גידולי קרקע? ( אין טוחן אלא בגידולי קרקע ) They don't grow from the ground; they are born from animals They get all their nutrition from the ground Debatable Together, Maran permits cutting what is already edible and comes from an animal : cooked meat and cooked chicken. The sushi example: a big piece of raw salmon isn't edible as it is, but cut into tiny pieces with something else, it is. That is the kind of \"making edible\" the Ba'al HaTerumah means. אבל אסור לחתוך דק דק בשר חי לפני העופות, הואיל ואינם יכולים לאכלו בלא חיתוך, כמשוי ליה אוכל. Raw meat (or raw fish in a big piece) may not be cut very fine, e.g. for birds, \"or for anyone for that matter,\" because you are making it edible . But even with cooked meat, no specialised instrument. You may crush cooked meat, fish or chicken, but not with a grinder : grinders are muktzeh ; every heter we have learned works only without a specialised instrument . Seif 10 — Grating cheese אסור לגרור הגבינה בשבת במורג חרוץ בעל פיפיות שקורין ראליו. Work it through: (a) Cheese is edible before grating . (b) Cheese doesn't come from something that grows from the ground. So cheese may be mashed, crushed and grated completely on Shabbat . You don't even need the combination of Seif 9. However: you may not use an instrument designated for grating . That is the prohibition of this seif. The same applies to eggs: boiled eggs may be mashed (cooked, and from an animal rather than directly from the ground), but not with a grater or a masher . That would be an איסור דרבנן : \"Chachamim forbade all kinds of special instruments, and thus all of these instruments become muktzeh on Shabbos.\" Slicers are different Utensil What it does during the week Shabbat Grater, mincer, garlic crusher, onion chopper, mouli Grates or crushes: tochen Forbidden (and muktzeh) Egg slicer, cheese slicer Only slices , never grinds Permitted \"Even during the week all it's doing is slicing. It's not crushing, it's not grating, it's not doing מלאכת טוחן.\" Robert: isn't cheese from the ground too? Robert: The milk came from the cow, and the cow ate from the ground, and the milk made the cheese. Why isn't cheese גידולי קרקע? The Rabbi: \"No, because it's quite far, and it's not the milk itself. It's a whole procedure.\" Cheese has its own new status . Meat or an egg, directly from the animal , are still potentially גידולי קרקע. Cheese clearly isn't. Seif 11 — Water for vegetables (a side point) מותר להשקות התלוש כדי שלא יכמוש. Not our topic: it relates to kotzer , which we touched on long ago. Background: Chachamim forbade putting things into water on Shabbat because of tircha (bother). Drawing water used to be a long procedure. That is why the Mishnah in Sukkah says don't change the water for the lulav on Yom Tov. But for food purposes it is permitted: you may put vegetables in water so they don't wither . Seif 12 — Cutting very fine המחתך הירק דק דק, חייב משום טוחן. A fourth form of tochen beyond grinding, crushing and mashing: cutting into very small pieces. How small? שיעור דקותן לא נודע לנו : \"we don't know the measure of its fineness.\" (The Rabbi thinks it's the Sefer Mitzvot Gedolot who says this.) Already the early Rishonim didn't know. The Rema: ויש אומרים דאסור לחתוך גרוגרות וחרובים לפני הזקנים… אבל מותר לפרר הלחם לפני התרנגולים, כיון דכבר נטחן… דאין טוחן אחר טוחן. You may not cut figs, dates or carobs very small \"in front of the elderly\" . The Magen Avraham says it's davka (only for the elderly), but everyone disagrees : it's אורחא דמילתא , just the usual reason you'd cut so small. But bread may be crumbled, for chickens or anyone, because of אין טוחן אחר טוחן . Heter 4 — The Rashba: grinding סמוך לסעודה The Rema: וכל זה לא מיירי אלא לחתוך ולהניח, אבל אם לאכלו מיד הכל שרי, מידי דהוי אבורר לאכול מיד דשרי. (תשובת הרשב\"א) The source: In borer (learned in the summer) the Gemara itself permits selecting for immediate consumption , סמוך לסעודה . \"To the best of my knowledge, the Rashba is the first one\" to say the same applies to tochen . It is not in the Gemara . What it permits: Even with none of the first three heterim (no shinui, never ground before, never cooked), you may mash a banana in the regular way if you're about to feed it to your baby now . The Biur Halacha explains why it can even be done with a knife , unlike borer, where immediate selection is only permitted without a kli: דכמו בבורר דרך אכילה הוא… והכא נמי… אפילו כשחותך בסכין לאכול לאלתר דרך אכילה היא, ומשום שאינה מיוחדת לטחינה. Cutting with a knife to eat right away is the way of eating , and a knife isn't designated for grinding. The Beit Yosef brings the Rashba but adds: if you can cut slightly bigger pieces, it's good , because it's the Rashba's chiddush , not the Gemara's. Can we rely on it? Posek Position Reasoning Ben Ish Chai ( \"as far as I remember\" ) Don't rely on it at all The Shulchan Aruch didn't codify it , though the Beit Yosef brought it, so perhaps Maran didn't hold of it in the end Chacham Ovadia Mutar me'ikar hadin The Shulchan Aruch doesn't codify everything in the Beit Yosef (mainly what is in the Gemara). The Beit Yosef never said he disagrees , only \"if possible, cut bigger\" . And the Rema brings it as halacha The practical upshot (Chacham Ovadia): Any crushing, mashing or tochen is permitted סמוך לסעודה : for the meal you are about to eat, \"certainly in the next 5–10 minutes\" (as in Hilchot Borer). If you can cut bigger pieces, תבוא עליו ברכה. But it's fine me'ikar hadin even if you don't. \"Not everyone agrees to this, but there's plenty to rely on.\" \"Major heter.\" You wake up on Shabbat and want to mash a banana for the baby : permitted. You can't find a knife to mash the avocado with the back of it, and you want to mix it with oil and eat it now with your crackers or challah: permitted. Preparing in the kitchen the meal you're about to eat in a few minutes : permitted. Seif 19 — Finishing what was mostly crushed (heter 5) שום, בוסר ומלילות שריסקן מערב שבת, ומחוסרין דיכה, אסור לגמור דיכתן בשבת. ואם אינן מחוסרין אלא שחיקה… מותר. Three Hebrew stages of crushing (remember them as רד\"ש): Stage Hebrew Meaning On Shabbat, if this is what remains 1 ריסוק Beginning to break it down — 2 דיכה More thoroughly ground Forbidden to finish 3 שחיקה Completely ground Permitted to finish The example: before Shabbat you broke down baked potatoes , but they aren't fully mashed. On Shabbat you may finish making mashed potato , since it was mainly done already. לפיכך מותר לגמור שחיקת הריפות בעץ פרור בקדרה בשבת , when they were put on the fire before Shabbat. \"I don't know how practical it is. One or two cases.\" The Rema — ממרח doesn't apply to food There is a melacha of ממרח (spreading, smoothing), but it doesn't apply to foods : you may spread avocado on bread (mash it in a permitted way, then spread); chocolate spread on bread is fine. The Rabbi's Tochen Summary Sheet \"A document which I composed some time ago, about eight, nine years ago… I just came across it this week, b'siyata dishmaya. It had been buried somewhere for a good few years.\" (He offered to put it on the group.) Definition In the Mishkan, after borer the seeds were ground into a fine powder , sieved, made into a paste, baked, and made into a dye . Tochen = grinding, or cutting into minute pieces ( דק דק , measure unclear, but it must be very small). It applies to non-edibles too: the Rambam and the Gemara talk about crushing a clod of earth . \"We'll talk in a moment about mud on your jacket.\" Implements: a mincer, chopper, fork or knife is de'oraita when crushing completely. By hand: it is rabbinic unless that is the usual manner. Crushing a clod of soil by hand is the usual way, so de'oraita . Food isn't usually crushed by hand, so mashing a banana by hand to leave for later is a shinui : not de'oraita , but still forbidden. Forms of tochen Form Examples Grinding / crushing (Seifim 7–8) Grain into flour, coffee beans into powder, pepper , dry hadassim leaves ( \"you can do this by mistake… the hadassim go dry very quickly\" ) Mashing (Seif 12) Avocado, banana, dates, figs Shredding / grating Onions and vegetables Specialised utensils can never be used , even on a product you may grind: a mincer, grater, onion chopper, garlic crusher or mouli. This is because of עובדין דחול . The five permitted methods # Heter Rule Practical 1 שינוי גדול Handle of a knife, underside of a cup. Even long before the meal , even completely Not into a mortar. A chopping board is fine , since it's also used for regular cutting. Avocado and banana with the back of a knife, then spread 2 אין טוחן אחר טוחן (Seifim 8, 12) Already ground before Sugar cubes (refined sugar), bread, biscuits, cake, matzah, medication tablets ( \"constituted from already-ground stuff\" ), snuff that hardened (the Ben Ish Chai). No specialised utensil 3 Not from the ground + already edible Combination of poskim Cooked meat ; fish need not even be cooked ( \"I should have made this clearer\" : fish are not גידולי קרקע); cheese, eggs, liver , even long before the meal, in the usual manner 4 סמוך לסעודה (the Rashba) טוחן דרך אכילה Debatable, so cut slightly larger if you can: תבוא עליו ברכה (Chacham Ovadia) 5 Mostly crushed already (Seif 19) Needs ריסוק and דיכה done, only שחיקה lacking Finish mashing potatoes or cooked carrots Practical examples Mashing banana or avocado (for babies or anyone): permitted with the back of a knife . Spreading is fine: no ממרח with food . A very small salad (tomato, cucumber, pepper): peeling must be close to the meal because of borer , preferably with a knife, not a peeler . (Chacham Ovadia allows a peeler, since the peel is גוף אחד , one body with the vegetable, but a knife is better.) You may take the stem off tomatoes . Don't cut too small unless it's right before the meal . A tiny \"falafel\" salad: cut very close to the meal , preferably a bit larger than usual. Salting cucumbers (from last week): salt together with oil or lemon , or oil first , to avoid the pickling issue. Egg and onion: shell the eggs close to the meal (borer). No tochen with egg , and an egg slicer may be used. Chopping the onion small must be right before the meal . Mud Case Ruling Why Dry mud on your body (e.g. running in shorts) May be removed , even though it's a פסיק רישא that it crumbles Only derabbanan ; you have no interest in grinding it; כבוד הבריות Mud on your clothes You may not remove it You may ask a non-Jew to take off the layer of mud שבות דשבות במקום כבוד הבריות A stain you want washed out Generally not , even through a non-Jew That involves water : \"a whole different question… de'oraita\" Two rabbinic prohibitions connected to tochen Gezeirah Source Today Things that make a noise Tosafot: you may not put wheat in a millstone before Shabbat, because the noise makes people think you started it on Shabbat. From here, the Ashkenazi prohibition on leaving anything set before Shabbat that makes a loud noise Washing machines: \"I don't know washing machines which make noise nowadays.\" Even Ashkenazim would likely be allowed; for Sefardim it's straight out mutar . Rav Moshe Feinstein: an alarm clock is permitted, since its whole nature is to be set beforehand, so the gezeirah never covered it שחיקת סממנים Medicines used to be made by grinding plants , so Chachamim restricted taking medicine on Shabbat A whole chapter, Siman 328 , \"in a couple of months' time\" Seif 13 — Honey from the hive (before lash) אסור לרדות דבש מן הכוורת… מפני שדומה לתולש. You may not take honey from the honeycomb , because it resembles detaching (kotzer) . Kotzer normally means detaching something from its source of growth . Honey doesn't grow in the comb, it was made there, but Chachamim forbade it rabbinically . Honey already out of the comb , or floating on top: permitted . Part Two — מלאכת לש (Kneading): An Introduction \"One of the most misunderstood melachot\" \"I think it's one of the most misunderstood melachot, if not the most misunderstood.\" In the many contemporary books on the melachot, in Hebrew and English, \"I have not come across a sefer which properly goes through לש in a really structured manner.\" The Orchot Shabbat was very good, \"probably the best,\" but it's for Ashkenazim . For Sefardim , \"I don't know a sefer which goes through לש properly.\" Where it's found: only a few seifim: Siman 321, Seifim 14–16 ; Siman 324 ; and Siman 340 . \"We will learn those two because we have to learn the whole לש together.\" The plan: at least next week, maybe another shiur after that. \"When you understand the principles, it all makes total sense, and it's very easy to apply.\" A preview of the Rabbi's practical list (to come at the end): techina ; aubergine salad with oil or mayonnaise; chilbe (חילבה) ; coleslaw ; potato salad with mayonnaise; egg and onion with oil or mayonnaise; avocado with lemon; banana salads ; thick yoghurt with banana ; cornflakes in thick yoghurt ( \"not Weetabix, Weetabix is something separate\" ); porridge, pudding, jelly . What is lash? The tenth melacha. In making the dye: plant, harvest, thresh, select (borer), grind ( tochen ), and now knead the powder with liquid, then cook it. Definition: gathering together, through a liquid, things that were not gathered before , transforming separate particles into a paste-like, unified mass . The liquid can be anything that binds: water, fruit juice, milk, oil . Lash applies to non-edibles too. The fundamental machloket: is putting the water in already kneading? Making challah has two stages : מגבל / נתינה: putting the ingredients together (water onto flour); גיבול: kneading it with your hands into dough. Kneading is clearly lash. Is stage one lash too? Shabbat 18a: מאן תנא נתינת מים לדיו זו היא שרייתן? אמר רב יוסף: רבי היא, דתניא: אחד נותן את הקמח ואחד נותן את המים, האחרון חייב, דברי רבי. רבי יוסי בר יהודה אומר: אינו חייב עד שיגבל. Rabbi (Yehuda HaNasi) Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehuda Putting the liquid in Already lash. Whoever adds the second ingredient, the one that begins the mingling, is chayav Not chayav Chayav when? At the mingling; and kneading is a second toldah of lash Only when you knead (עד שיגבל) Mixed before Shabbat? You still can't knead it together on Shabbat Mixing alone was never the issue Why the halacha doesn't simply follow Rabbi Normally הלכה כרבי מחבירו : Rabbi, the author of the Mishnah, usually prevails. But the Rambam, the Rif and most Rishonim rule like Rabbi Yosi , because of a סתם משנה : נותנין מים למורסן, אבל לא גובלין . You may add water to bran , but not knead it. The anonymous Mishnah is the default position, and הלכה כסתם משנה trumps הלכה כרבי מחבירו . The Rambam: הלש כגרוגרת חייב… המגבל את עפרה זו היא לישה . The melacha is the kneading. Other Rishonim , such as the Semag , the Ba'al HaTerumah and Rabbi Baruch of Mainz : halacha follows Rabbi , despite the stam Mishnah. How the Shulchan Aruch rules Ruling Shulchan Aruch (here in 321 and in 324:3 ) סתם like the Rambam and Rif : lash is the kneading . Then a יש אומרים : the Ba'al HaTerumah and Semag Rema We are machmir like the Ba'al HaTerumah and Semag. Ashkenazim: even the mingling is a problem Ben Ish Chai, Kaf HaChaim, Or LeTzion (Rav Ben Zion Abba Shaul) Since Maran brought the second opinion in both places , one should try to be machmir and treat the mingling as a problem Chacham Ovadia (following the Chida ) A סתם ויש means the halacha is like the סתם . The יש אומרים is brought משום כבודו , so you know there's another opinion: \"you don't need to follow it.\" The mingling is not the problem; the kneading is On the \"rules\" of reading the Shulchan Aruch: \"The honest truth is, I believe all of these rules are not really rules. For every rule… you have rules which go against it.\" The Shulchan Aruch's purpose was to summarise the Beit Yosef : \"this is the main opinion, but you should know there is a second opinion.\" The shiur proceeds like Chacham Ovadia, the Rambam and the Rif: the melacha is the kneading. Crucial caveat: when mixing is the kneading \"Extremely important caveat. This is something people don't know. I've seen people do this on Shabbos, and I think it's a melacha d'oraita.\" Some mixtures bind by themselves the moment you combine them. Then even Rabbi Yosi agrees you're chayav with the mixing itself. \"Not begins: happens!\" Weetabix with a little milk ( \"Again, I haven't seen anyone who discusses this, but לעניות דעתי I think this is pashut\" ): With lots of milk , \"that's less of an issue. Let's talk about that later.\" But some like their Weetabix \"very stodgy\" and pour in a bit of milk . The milk is completely absorbed and binds the crumbly Weetabix into one unified mass . The pouring itself is the lash. The source, Siman 340: הנותן זרע פשתן או שומשמין במים חייב משום לש, מפני שמתערבים ונתלים זה בזה. Sesame or flax seeds put into water mix and cling together by themselves , so putting them in is chayav even according to Rabbi Yosi . The three levels of mixture The result determines the severity: Level Hebrew What it looks like Examples Status (kneading, following Rabbi Yosi) 1 בלילה עבה (thick mixture) Barely moves ; if it moves, extremely laboriously Dough ; toothpaste mixed with something Chayav d'oraita 2 בלילה רכה (thin mixture) Unified and stays together, but moves when you tilt it , poured easily. Not runny Cake batter Rabbinic 3 No בלילה Runny , not a mixture at all Hot chocolate : cocoa powder into water Mutar \"Those are the three levels of לש.\" (The precise definitions come \"only much later on\" in the Shulchan Aruch.) Summary Tochen: five heterim A שינוי גדול : the back of a knife or the underside of a cup, onto a chopping board, never a mortar. אין טוחן אחר טוחן : bread, crackers, sugar cubes, tablets, snuff. Already edible and not from the ground : cooked meat and chicken ; fish even raw ; cheese, eggs and liver outright. סמוך לסעודה (the Rashba, via the Rema): Chacham Ovadia permits it me'ikar hadin, preferably cutting a little bigger; the Ben Ish Chai doesn't rely on it. Mostly crushed before Shabbat (only שחיקה lacking): finish it. Tochen: always No specialised utensils : graters, mincers, crushers are muktzeh. Slicers are fine. Raw meat may not be cut very fine. ממרח doesn't apply to food. Mud may be brushed off the body, and off clothes through a non-Jew. Lash Gathering separate particles into one mass through a liquid. Rabbi vs Rabbi Yosi bar Rabbi Yehuda: Maran and Chacham Ovadia: only the kneading is lash. Ashkenazim (the Rema) and some Sefardi poskim: even the mingling . But if the mixing itself binds ( Weetabix with a little milk , sesame in water), the pouring is lash according to all. בלילה עבה = de'oraita; בלילה רכה = derabbanan; no mixture = mutar. Coming Up Next week: a quick review of these principles, \"lay out a couple more things\" ; then Siman 321, Seifim 14–16 in the Shulchan Aruch; then \"flesh this out in practical examples and see how we can actually do לש on Shabbos.\" At least one more shiur after that on lash, with Siman 324 and Siman 340 , and the Rabbi's practical list: techina, aubergine salad, coleslaw, potato salad, egg and onion; avocado, yoghurt and cereal, Weetabix, porridge, pudding, jelly. Shabbat Shalom.","hn0Sb5FEDWQ":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Mishneh Torah (1177) Shiur: The final class of the summer term, following the series on the Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 22 July 2025 (during the Three Weeks) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. The Four Great Introductions Whenever he can, the Rambam uses his writing as a springboard for principles of faith and character . Four places stand out: Introduction Focus Status To Avot ( Shemonah Perakim ) Character and behaviour Done last year, in depth To the Mishnah Tradition and the Oral Law (with digressions on prophecy and the purpose of life) This term To Perek Helek (Sanhedrin ch. 10) Theology and philosophy : mistaken beliefs, the 13 Principles , much more September–December To the Mishneh Torah Short and self-contained Tonight Written in 1177 , when the Rambam was about 40 . Some of it overlaps the Introduction to the Mishnah, \"which is why it's so appropriate to do it now\"; some is new. Part 1: An Opening Verse, and a Nod to His Critics אז לא אבוש בהביטי אל כל מצותיך (Tehillim 119) Then I shall not be ashamed, when I gaze at all Your commandments. Why open his halachic magnum opus (the Moreh is his philosophical one) with this verse? \"Perhaps he recognised this book wouldn't go down well with everyone . And indeed it did not.\" Some of the Rambam's books were burned , because Rabbeinu Yonah of Gerona thought some views (in the Moreh) heretical. \" Later he recognised he was wrong. \" The Mishneh Torah itself was criticised : by Rav Pinchas HaDayan (whose letter we learned this winter), and by the Ra'avad : how can you write a book without sources? \" And yet the Ra'avad spent an enormous amount of time writing a gloss on the entire Mishneh Torah. So he clearly thought it significant.\" \"There's no halachic work like it, in brevity, clarity and system .\" So the verse is \" like a nod to those who will criticise him : I'm not embarrassed. I'm confident I'm doing the right thing. Here I am. \" Part 2: The Written and the Oral Torah כל המצוות שנתנו לו למשה בסיני, בפירושן נתנו. Every mitzvah given at Sinai came with its explanation . ואתנה לך את לחת האבן והתורה והמצוה (Ki Tisa): התורה = the written Torah; המצוה = its explanation , the Oral Torah . We're commanded to keep the Torah according to the mitzvah . Moshe wrote the whole Torah in his own hand before he died , gave a scroll to each tribe , and put a thirteenth in the Ark as testimony: לקח את ספר התורה הזה ושמתם אתו מצד ארון ברית ה׳ (Vayelech). The explanation he did not write. He taught it to the elders, Yehoshua and all Israel : את כל הדבר אשר אנכי מצוה אתכם אתו תשמרו לעשות . Hence: Oral Torah. (Recall from the Introduction to the Mishnah: the four-times teaching , Eruvin 54b.) Moshe taught it in his beit din to the seventy elders ; Elazar, Pinchas and Yehoshua received it directly; to Yehoshua, his student, he handed it on and charged him with it. Part 3: Forty Generations, from Moshe to Rav Ashi \" I don't know anyone who did this before the Rambam : the whole transmission, all the way down.\" Received from Yehoshua Moshe The elders Yehoshua Eli The elders and Pinchas Shmuel Eli and his beit din David Shmuel Achiyah HaShiloni A Levi who left Egypt and heard Moshe as a child (Bava Batra): the Levites didn't die after the spies, and he lived very long. Received from David Eliyahu → Elisha → Yehoyada HaKohen → Zecharyah → Hoshea → Amos → Yeshayahu → Michah → Yoel → Nachum → Chabakuk → Tzefanyah → Yirmeyahu Each from the one before, and his beit din Baruch ben Neriyah Yirmeyahu. He witnessed the destruction, went with the exiles to Bavel , and carried on teaching there Ezra and his beit din Baruch The Men of the Great Assembly Ezra's beit din = אנשי כנסת הגדולה : Chaggai, Zecharyah, Malachi, Daniel, Chananyah, Mishael, Azaryah, Nechemyah ben Chachalyah, Mordechai, Zerubavel , and many others, 120 elders in all. The last and youngest: Shimon HaTzaddik , Kohen Gadol after Ezra, who received the whole Oral Torah from them. The pairs ( Zugot ) From Antigonos of Socho (about 100 BCE): two leaders per generation, the Nasi (head of the academy) named first, then the Av Beit Din . \"This lasts about 150 years.\" Pair Yosei ben Yoezer and Yosei ben Yochanan The first zug Yehoshua ben Perachyah and Nittai HaArbeli Yehudah ben Tabbai and Shimon ben Shetach Shemaya and Avtalyon Gerei tzedek , righteous converts Hillel and Shammai Then Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai and Rabban Shimon, Hillel's son . \"Did you notice? The first with a title. Everyone until now, all the prophets too, had none: they were above titles . Now it's רבן : not yet Rabbi , still higher.\" Rabban Yochanan's five students Rabbi Eliezer HaGadol, Rabbi Yehoshua, Rabbi Yosei HaKohen, Rabbi Shimon ben Netanel, Rabbi Elazar ben Arach. Rabbi Akiva ben Yosef received from Rabbi Eliezer HaGadol. (His father was a convert.) How to spell Akiva? \"The Rambam writes עקיבה, with a heh . There's a big discussion in Hilchot Gittin (and for the ketubah ): heh or aleph ? Sephardim generally write it with a heh ; Ashkenazim with an aleph . \" Rabbi Yishmael and Rabbi Meir, son of a righteous convert , received from Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Meir's colleagues: Rabbi Yehudah, Rabbi Yosei, Rabbi Shimon, Rabbi Nechemyah, Rabbi Elazar ben Shammua, Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar, Shimon ben Azzai, Rabbi Chanina ben Teradyon . Rabbi Akiva's colleagues: Rabbi Tarfon, Rabbi Shimon ben Elazar, Rabbi Yochanan ben Nuri . Hillel's line: Hillel → Shimon → Rabban Gamliel the Elder → Rabban Shimon → Rabban Gamliel → Rabban Shimon → Rabbi Yehudah, \"Rabbeinu HaKadosh\" , who also received from Rabbi Elazar ben Shammua . Part 4: The Digression on Rabbeinu HaKadosh Instead of continuing to the Amoraim, the Rambam stops to talk about Rebbi . Why? \" He loved Rabbeinu HaKadosh. And notice he calls his own book Mishneh Torah . I think he saw similarities between himself and Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi .\" (The Rabbi's view: \"I've got no source for this.\") מימות משה רבנו ועד רבנו הקדוש לא חברו חבור שמלמדין אותו ברבים בתורה שבעל פה. From Moshe to Rebbi, no one composed a book of the Oral Torah to teach in public. But in every generation, the head of the court or the prophet would write for himself a record of the traditions he heard from his teachers , and teach them orally in public. Writing down the Oral Torah \" Notice : the Rambam seems to say there's no problem writing down the Oral Law. We know Rebbi had to make a decision, despite the prohibition, to write it. But the prohibition is on writing it en masse, to teach from . Private notes to remember and transmit properly were always fine.\" Each leader wrote according to his ability: what he'd heard, and the new laws each generation derived by the 13 principles and the Great Court agreed. Then Rebbi gathered it all , every tradition, law and explanation from Moshe on, and composed the Mishnah , taught it publicly , and it spread to all Israel . Why? לפי שראה שהתלמידים מתמעטים והולכים, והצרות מתחדשות ובאות, ומלכות הרשעה פושטת בעולם ומתגברת, וישראל מתגלגלין והולכין לקצוות. He saw students dwindling , new troubles coming , the wicked empire spreading and growing , and Israel wandering to the ends of the earth . So he made one book in everyone's hands , to be learned quickly and not forgotten , and spent the rest of his life teaching it with his court. \" I believe the Rambam sees himself (not spiritually, but in identity) as a kind of Rebbi. Rebbi gathered the Oral Torah into the Mishnah around 250 ; the Rambam does the same a thousand years later, in 1177 . That's why he calls it Mishneh Torah . \" Part 5: From Rebbi to Rav Ashi From Rebbi His sons Shimon and Gamliel , Rabbi Afes , Rabbi Chanina bar Chama , Rabbi Chiya , Rav , Rabbi Yannai , Bar Kappara , Shmuel , Rabbi Yochanan , Rabbi Hoshaya : eleven greats Rabbi Yochanan Young in Rebbi's day; later studied under Rabbi Yannai Rav Also from Rabbi Yannai. Composed the Sifra and Sifrei Shmuel From Rabbi Chanina bar Chama Rabbi Chiya The Tosefta Rabbi Hoshaya and Bar Kappara Baraitot Rabbi Yochanan The Yerushalmi , in Eretz Yisrael, about 300 years after the destruction (c. 370) From Rav and Shmuel (Bavel) Rav Huna, Rav Yehudah, Rav Nachman, Rav Kahana From Rabbi Yochanan (Israel) Rabbah bar bar Chanah, Rabbi Ami, Rabbi Asi, Rav Dimi, Rav Avin From Rav Huna and Rav Yehudah Rabbah and Rav Yosef From Rabbah and Rav Yosef Abaye and Rava … Rav Ashi and Ravina Mar bar Rav Ashi His father Rav Ashi, and Ravina נמצא מרב אשי עד משה רבנו ארבעים איש. From Rav Ashi back to Moshe: forty men. Over about 2,000 years : from Sinai (2448, 3,338 years ago ) to Rav Ashi, about 500 CE . The Rambam lists them backwards, ending …Yehoshua, Moshe Rabbeinu, from the mouth of Hashem, God of Israel. \" Incredible. \" Where did he get it all? \"From Tanach , from Avot chapter 1, and from what's scattered through the Mishnah and Gemara about who learned from whom. Remember his ten appendices last week. There are small disagreements, but it's basically accepted. \" \"And thousands and tens of thousands heard from them.\" These forty were the heads of their generations (heads of academies, Exilarchs, Sanhedrin), but thousands of others learned with them. Why list them? Everything in the Mishneh Torah goes back to Moshe. It all comes from the written Torah or the Oral Torah (Mishnah and Gemara), and anything else he labels ( a later authority said , a custom ). \"The problem with books today is they put everything together and you don't know the origin . No kitniyot on Pesach : there are many examples.\" The answer to his critics. Why no sources? \" Because it all comes back to Moshe. I don't need to cite them.\" Part 6: What the Talmuds Contain Rav Ashi composed the Bavli in Shinar (Babylonia) , about a century after Rabbi Yochanan's Yerushalmi (today dated about 495 CE ). Both explain the Mishnah, and with the Tosefta, Sifra and Sifrei they clarify forbidden and permitted, pure and impure, liable and exempt, valid and invalid , plus: גזרות , fences ושמרתם את משמרתי : עשו משמרת למשמרתי (e.g. poultry and milk) תקנות , enactments Good practice, binding but not a fence (e.g. prozbul ). \" The Rambam is very particular about the difference between a gezerah and a takkanah \" מנהגות , customs Each court's safeguards: binding too, לא תסור מן הדבר אשר יגידו לך ימין ושמאל Derived laws By the 13 principles, ruled by the Great Court of the generation Part 7: Why the Talmud Binds Us, and Nothing After It Does נמצא רבינא ורב אשי וחבריהם סוף גדולי חכמי ישראל מעתיקי תורה שבעל פה : the last great transmitters of the Oral Torah , whose gezerot , takkanot and customs spread through all Israel, wherever they lived . After Rav Ashi, Israel scattered further , to distant islands (\"places like England\"); roads became unsafe; Torah study dwindled ; no more yeshivot of thousands, just \"a few survivors whom Hashem calls,\" in each city and land. Any beit din after the Talmud , in any land, that decreed, enacted or set a custom: its acts did not spread to all Israel , because of distance, bad roads, and because the Great Sanhedrin of 71 had ceased years before the Talmud was compiled. The Talmud After the Talmud Authority \"All the Sages of Israel, or most of them,\" who received person from person, back to Moshe Local courts A gezerah or custom Binding on every Jew, everywhere; we compel every city Binds only its city. One city can't be forced to adopt another's practice; a court needn't adopt another's decree A later scholar with better proofs Irrelevant : \"you can have the best proof; the Talmud is the Sanhedrin , you must accept it\" Follow whoever's reasoning is stronger , earlier or later: the 700 scholar can override the 600 one A modern example: \"A beit din in New Square declared it forbidden to touch an iPhone . That's a gezerah . Does it apply to me? No. לא פשטו מעשיו בכל ישראל. \" The Sanhedrin in exile: after the destruction it moved through ten places in the Land, until about 425 CE . But since everything in the Talmud was accepted by all Israel , its authority holds. Why we still mourn on Tisha B'Av \"We have a sovereign state. Why mourn?\" \"We don't have the Temple. But greater than that, we don't have a Sanhedrin. We don't have a supreme court. Our Torah, since 500 CE, has been fossilised. The beauty of the Sanhedrin was that it could interpret the Torah as each generation needed , with authority. Our hands are tied. \" Second-day Yom Tov. \"People ask why we keep it: we know the calendar exactly. You're right. It doesn't make sense, and I'd be the first to say we don't need it. The problem is the Gemara says we must, and the Gemara is the Sanhedrin. Until you give me a new Sanhedrin, we keep it. If Mashiach comes before Tisha B'Av and we have a Sanhedrin, it may decide we don't need it.\" Part 8: The Geonim, and the Problem Everyone after the Talmud = the Geonim : in Eretz Yisrael, Shinar (Babylonia), Spain and France . They explained the Talmud because: - its way is very deep , and - it's in Aramaic mixed with other languages : clear to Babylonians when composed, but understood by no one elsewhere , nor in Babylonia by the Geonim's time. What they produced: - Responsa , collected into books - Commentaries on single laws, difficult chapters, whole tractates - Halachot pesukot : decided law, for those who can't reach the Talmud's depths \" Holy work , from the Talmud's close until now \": 1,108 years after the destruction , i.e. 1177 . No chain for this period. \"Notice: he doesn't trace the Sages from 500 to his own time . He could have. It makes no difference : there's no longer a binding transmission. Everyone interprets on his own.\" \"In our time, troubles have multiplied\" ובזמן הזה תכפו צרות יתרות… ואבדה חכמת חכמינו ובינת נבונינו נסתתרה. Even the Geonim's explanations, which they thought plain, have become hard , and few understand them properly. \"Needless to say the Talmud itself\" : Bavli, Yerushalmi, Sifra, Sifrei, Tosefta, which require a broad mind, a wise soul and a long time . Part 9: The Mishneh Torah ומפני זה נערתי חצני אני משה בן מימון הספרדי, ונשענתי על הצור ברוך הוא, ובינותי בכל אלו הספרים. So I, Moshe son of Maimon the Sefaradi, girded my loins, relied on the Rock, and studied all these books , and decided to compose from them, on forbidden and permitted, impure and pure, and all the Torah's laws : כולן בלשון ברורה ודרך קצרה Clear language, concise בלי קושיא ובלי פירוק No questions and answers ולא זה אומר בכה וזה אומר בכה No \"this one says, that one says\" אלא דברים ברורים קרובים נכונים Only clear, accessible, correct rulings , as they emerge from everything from Rebbi until now \" What an incredible statement to make. \" \"No other book\" כללו של דבר, כדי שלא יהא אדם צריך לחבור אחר בעולם בדין מדיני ישראל, אלא יהא חבור זה מקבץ לתורה שבעל פה כולה, עם התקנות והמנהגות והגזרות שנעשו מימות משה רבנו ועד חבור התלמוד, וכמו שפירשו לנו הגאונים. In short: so that no one needs any other book in the world for any law of Israel. This one gathers the whole Oral Torah: every takkanah, custom and decree from Moshe to the Talmud, as the Geonim explained them. לפיכך קראתי שם חבור זה \"משנה תורה\", לפי שאדם קורא בתורה שבכתב תחלה, ואחר כך קורא בזה, ויודע ממנו תורה שבעל פה כולה, ואינו צריך לקרות ספר אחר ביניהם. Therefore I called it Mishneh Torah , the \"second Torah\": read the written Torah first, then this, and you know the whole Oral Torah, with no need for any other book. \" He was criticised for this line. \" What he did not mean As he wrote in the letter to Rav Pinchas HaDayan : he never said not to study Mishnah and Gemara. It's a summary. To go in depth , study Mishnah, Gemara, Geonim, Tosefta, baraitot. \" And it's true. If you know Tanach and the Mishneh Torah inside out, you can't really go wrong : you know the basics of everything. And it doesn't take as long as the whole of Shas. That's why he called it the second Torah. Remarkable.\" The structure Divided by topic ( halachot ), each into chapters , each into numbered halachot , \"so they can be ordered in one's memory.\" Some sections cover one mitzvah with many details; others, many mitzvot on one subject. Named by topic , not by number of mitzvot. 613 mitzvot: 248 positive , like the limbs of the body , and 365 negative , like the days of the solar year . Summary Opening verse, אז לא אבוש : a confident nod to critics (Rabbeinu Yonah, Rav Pinchas, the Ra'avad, who glossed it anyway). Written Torah = התורה; Oral = המצוה. Moshe wrote 13 scrolls; the explanation he taught . Forty generations from Moshe to Rav Ashi , all named: prophets, the Great Assembly (120, last Shimon HaTzaddik ), the Zugot , the first titles (Rabban), Rabbi Akiva ( עקיבה with a heh ), Rebbi , the Amoraim. It shows everything in the Mishneh Torah goes back to Sinai : hence no need for sources. Rebbi wrote the Mishnah because students dwindled and exile spread. Private notes were always allowed. The Rambam saw himself as a Rebbi a thousand years on. The Talmud binds all Israel : accepted by all, the last link of the chain, the final Sanhedrin . Nothing after it does , beyond its locality ( New Square's iPhone ban ); later scholars are judged on their reasoning . Without a Sanhedrin our hands are tied : why Tisha B'Av still matters, and why we keep second-day Yom Tov . The Geonim explained, but by 1177 even they were hard. So: Mishneh Torah , clear, concise, no disputes, \"no other book needed,\" as a summary , not a replacement for study. 613 = 248 + 365. Coming Up The summer break , then from September : the Introduction to Perek Helek , \"a very philosophical work,\" and the principles of faith .","gRQTax69fS4":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), conclusion and the ten appendices ( perakim ) Shiur: Part 9, the final class of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 15 July 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Nine sessions: the Oral Torah given to Moshe and taught four times; each generation deriving new laws; prophecy vs the Sages' authority ; laws added each generation; Rebbi gathering them into the Mishnah ; its structure in detail; the Talmud's four purposes , including derashot , intentionally cryptic; and \"the four cubits of halachah\" : everything on earth is for the human, whose purpose is perfecting intellect and character , above all knowing Hashem and His unity . Last week: why so many people, when so few strive for perfection? To make the Sage's work possible, and to give him a society. \"The four cubits\" = walking with God in that pursuit. Tonight: the last part of the introduction, then ten appendices that show the Rambam's encyclopedic knowledge . Part 1: The Two Talmuds וכאשר רב אשי חיבר התלמוד : one of the greatest of his generation, clearly with heavenly help. The Bavli: 35 tractates The Mishnah has 61 tractates , but the Babylonian Talmud covers only 35 : Order Bavli? Zera'im Only Berachot Mo'ed All but Shekalim Nashim All seven Nezikin Not Eduyot or Avot Kodashim Not Middot or Kinnim Taharot Only Niddah Rav Ashi died; the Bavli closed around 500 CE , about 1,500 years ago. The Yerushalmi Meanwhile, the Sages of the Land of Israel compiled the Yerushalmi , associated with Rabbi Yochanan , earlier, around 400 . \"It's called the Jerusalem Talmud, but it isn't specifically Jerusalem: it's the Talmud of Eretz Yisrael . In English academia it used to be called the Palestinian Talmud , from before 1948.\" It covers the first five orders ; in Taharot , only Niddah . So nothing in Taharot except Niddah has any Gemara, Bavli or Yerushalmi. \"Understood only with great difficulty,\" with help from the Tosefta and baraitot. That's why Jewish learning has always focused on the Bavli : \"Even people who finish the Bavli don't necessarily go on to the Yerushalmi. Learn the Bavli more. The Yerushalmi is of course Torah, and we need to study it one day.\" Part 2: After the Talmud: No Adding, No Subtracting הרי כל מי שעמד אחריו, להבין דבריהם ולא להוסיף ולא לגרוע. Everyone after Ravina and Rav Ashi can only understand and interpret, never add or subtract. Why? The Talmud is the final Sanhedrin , so it can't be argued with. Everyone later is explaining . The Geonim וחיברו הגאונים פירושים רבים, ולא עלה ביד אחד מהם להשלים פירוש כל התלמוד. The Geonim wrote many commentaries, but to the Rambam's knowledge none completed the whole Talmud : some died young, some were caught up in disputes . They also wrote halachic summaries , in Arabic and Hebrew: Work Author Halachot Gedolot Rabbi Shimon Kayyara (8th century) Halachot Ketuot / Halachot Pesukot Rav Yehudai Gaon (8th century) Halachot of Rav Acha of Shabcha The She'iltot Seder Tanna'im The Rif supersedes them all וההלכות אשר עשה רבנו הגדול רבי יצחק… הספיקו במקום אלו כולם. Rabbi Yitzchak Alfasi , the Rif (North Africa, 1000s), abridged the whole Talmud and extracted every practical halachah : \"no one before him managed that.\" ואין לי תפיסה עליו בהן… לא יגיעו לעשרה בשום פנים. I disagree with him in fewer than ten places. \" Outstanding. Between the Rishonim there are thousands of disputes across the Talmud. The Rambam went through the Rif and says perhaps ten places are wrong.\" Part 3: The Rambam's Own Teachers וקבצתי מה שבא לידי מפירושי אבי ז״ל וזולתו, מרבינו יוסף הלוי. I gathered what I received from my father , and above all from Rabbi Yosef HaLevi . \" The Rambam rarely writes like this about anyone. He was very critical. When he gives compliments, take them very seriously. \" כי לב זה האיש בתלמוד מבהיל… עד שכמעט אומר עליו: לפניו לא היה מלך כמוהו בדרכו. This man's understanding of Talmud is astounding. One could almost say of him: \"before him there was no king like him in his way.\" Rabbi Yosef HaLevi ibn Migash , the Ri Migash , appointed rosh yeshivah by the Rif . A chain of tradition The Rif Rabbi Yitzchak Alfasi ↓ his student The Ri Migash , Rabbi Yosef HaLevi ↓ his student Rabbi Maimon , the Rambam's father, an av beit din , \"not just a rabbi\" ↓ his son Rabbeinu Moshe ben Maimon The commentary we lost The Rambam says he also wrote commentaries on the Talmud : on Mo'ed, Nashim and Nezikin , plus four tractates elsewhere. \" Unfortunately we don't have it. We have no direct commentary of the Rambam on the Talmud. It would have shed so much light on his Mishneh Torah , which all the other commentators had to do in his stead.\" Part 4: Why a Commentary on the Mishnah? The Mishnah is fully explained only by the Gemara , and can't always be understood from itself . \"Today we have the Rambam, Bartenura , many commentaries, recently Kehati , and English ones. In those days it was very hard. \" # Benefit 1 Understanding. אם תשאל גדול שבגאונים פירוש הלכה מן המשנה , he'll say give me time to look it up in the Gemara . \" The greatest of his time had to go and look it up! \" No one can remember all the Talmud, and one mishnah may be explained over four or five pages , or across two tractates . Here it's gathered in one place 2 Rulings : who the halachah follows 3 An introduction for beginners : learning the whole Mishnah teaches you to think the Talmudic way 4 Review for experts : a way to revise the Talmud \"Not for stones\" כי אין חיבורנו להבין האבנים, אלא להבין מי שיבין. Our work is not to make stones understand, but to help one who understands. \"Basically: I'm not writing word by word for people who aren't clever. Self-explanatory mishnayot get no comment. \" His rules of ruling Case Rule Beit Shammai vs Beit Hillel Halachah follows Beit Hillel : he'll note only the few exceptions סתם משנה (anonymous mishnah) Halachah follows it ; exceptions noted with reasons Every other dispute He'll always say who the halachah follows, even individual vs majority \"May Hashem guide us to the truth.\" A trailblazer: no one wrote another full Mishnah commentary until Rabbi Ovadiah of Bartenura , Italy, 15th century , just as no one before or since has done a Mishneh Torah. Part 5: The Ten Appendices \"Now you'll be exposed to the Rambam's encyclopedic knowledge . Remember: this is before ChatGPT, before the internet. \" He says they're \"not essential\" , but they help anyone who wants to be complete in Mishnah. 1. The Sages who taught halachah: 91 The class's guesses: \"20\"; \"lower than 20.\" (Philip laughed throughout: \"Please, can we stick to the topic? Thank you.\") The Mishnah names Sages only from Shimon HaTzaddik onward (before him: prophets, back to Moshe). From Shimon HaTzaddik to Rebbi, 91 Sages taught at least one halachah in their own name. \" He's 100% right. It's all been verified since. No one has argued. \" Familiar and unfamiliar names: Rabbi Hyrkanos, Shemaya and Avtalyon, Yehoshua ben Perachyah, Yehoshua ben Beteira, Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah, Rabbi Tzadok, Yehudah ben Tabbai, Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel … and some mentioned only once : Pappias , Rabbi Meyasha , Rabbi Yosei ben HaChotef Efrati . 2. Sages named for ethics or stories: 37 Not halachah: an ethical teaching (often only in Avot ) or a story : - Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi , only in Avot - Yehudah ben Teima : הוי עז כנמר , \" we say it every morning in Shacharit ,\" yet he appears once in the whole Mishnah - Ben Azzai, Ben Zoma, Rabbi Chananyah ben Akashya, Ben Bag Bag, Ben Hei Hei, Antigonos - Elyehoeinai ben HaKof , a Kohen Gadol mentioned once, in Parah ; Chanamel HaMitzri 91 + 37 = 128. Mark's AI: \"between 120 and 130.\" \" It can't give you an exact number. The Rambam gives 91. Unbelievable.\" Plus two not counted, making 130 Why left out Elisha ben Avuyah Quoted in Avot ( teaching a child is like ink on new paper; an old man, on erased paper ), but called אחר , \"the other\": he \"entered the orchard\"** and became a heretic Menachem Mentioned only to say Shammai replaced him as av beit din . Teaches nothing, not even through a story 3. Lineages Lineage Sages From King David , via Hillel (← Shefatyah ← Avital ← David) Hillel and his descendants: Shimon , Rabban Gamliel the Elder , Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel , Rabban Gamliel , Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi , and his son Rabban Gamliel : seven Converts or descendants of converts Shemaya and Avtalyon (converts); Rabbi Akiva , son of a convert (\" less well known ; perhaps why his learning was neglected early\"); Rabbi Meir , descended, per the Gemara, from the Caesar Nero , who fled and married a Jewish woman Kohanim Shimon HaTzaddik , Rabbi Chanina Segan HaKohanim, Yishmael ben Phiabi (Kohen Gadol), Yosei ben Yoezer , Rabbi Yosei HaKohen, Rabbi Tarfon , Elyehoeinai ben HaKof, Chanamel HaMitzri , and more \"The rest, as far as we know, are Yisraelim ; we aren't told.\" 4. Generations Groupings of the leading Sages by generation: before the destruction , e.g. Shimon HaTzaddik and Dosa ben Hyrkanos; Antigonos; the Zugot (Yosei ben Yoezer and Yosei ben Yochanan, Yehoshua ben Perachyah and Nittai HaArbeli… Hillel and Shammai): \"these seven pairs were in the Second Temple and didn't see the destruction\" . Then those who saw it, and onward. 5. Teachers and students Who learned from whom (e.g. Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai's students). \" Rabbi X says in the name of Y \" means X was Y's student. 6. Who is \"Rabbi Eliezer\"? (the essential one) \" Everyone must learn this before studying Mishnah. People study for years and never ask which Rabbi Eliezer.\" The Mishnah says… It means… Rabbi Eliezer Rabbi Eliezer ben Hyrkanos , student of Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai Rabbi Yehoshua Rabbi Yehoshua ben Chananyah Rabbi Yehudah Rabbi Yehudah bar Ila'i Rabbi Elazar Rabbi Elazar ben Shammua Rabbi Shimon Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai Ben Azzai, Ben Zoma, Ben Nannas Shimon ben Azzai, Shimon ben Zoma, Shimon ben Nannas Ben Bag Bag Rabbi Yochanan אחרים אומרים (\"others say\") Rabbi Meir יש אומרים (\"some say\") Rabbi Natan חכמים Sometimes many, sometimes one Sage whose view was accepted by all באמת אמרו Halachah leMoshe miSinai סתם משנה Accepted, undisputed , usually transmitted from Rabbi Meir ( סתם משנה רבי מאיר ), though not always the halachah Why \"others\" and \"some say\"? (Horayot 13b.) Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel , the Nasi, sat between Rabbi Meir (the chacham ) and Rabbi Natan ( av beit din ), with equal honour to all three. Wanting more respect for the Nasi's office, he changed the protocol so that only he received the full honour. Insulted, Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Natan spent the day asking him questions he couldn't answer . The consequence: their teachings would be cited without their names. \" Others say \" = Rabbi Meir; \" some say \" = Rabbi Natan. 7. Titles Level Title Examples Highest No title : too great for any title to fit Hillel, Shammai, Shemaya and Avtalyon , Shimon HaTzaddik, Antigonos, Yosei ben Yoezer, Yehoshua ben Perachyah, Nittai HaArbeli, Choni HaMe'agel , Yehudah ben Tabbai, Shimon ben Shetach, Akavya ben Mahalalel, Bava ben Buta … Next רבן Rabban Gamliel, Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai Third רבי (and אבא , as in Abba Shaul ) Rabbi Meir, Rabbi Yehudah, even Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi 8. Names from trades and places Trades: Nachum HaLavlar (the scribe), Rabbi Shimon HaShezori (the weaver, as given in class), Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar (the sandal-maker) Places: Ish Chadid, Ish Ono, Ish Bartota, Ish Tzereidah, Ish Yerushalayim Some named after their father , some after a more famous brother 9. Who argues with whom \" Fascinating. \" Most disputes 1 Rabbi Meir, Rabbi Yehudah, Rabbi Shimon, Rabbi Yosei (Rabbi Elazar sometimes) 2 Rabbi Akiva, Rabbi Eliezer, Rabbi Yehoshua 3 Rabbi Akiva, Rabbi Yishmael, Rabbi Tarfon, Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah 4 Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel The rest Very rarely 10. Sages with a single halachah: 37 Some taught a great deal (Rabbi Meir, Rabbi Yehudah); some sparingly. Rabbi Eliezer ben Yaakov taught little, so the Gemara calls his Mishnah קב ונקי , \"a small measure, but clean,\" and we often follow him . Of the 91, 37 taught exactly one halachah (\"37 again, but a different 37\"), each listed: Nachum HaLavlar and Rabbi Meyasha in Pe'ah ; Chananyah ben Chachinai and Rabbi Yosei ben HaChotef Efrati in Kilayim ; Elazar Chisma and Rabbi Yosei ben Meshullam in Terumot ; Rabbi Chutzpit in Shevi'it ; Nachum HaMadi ; Rabbi Ila'i and Rabbi Dostai in Eruvin ; Rabbi Shimon ben Bechri in Shekalim ; and the rest. The Whole Series in Brief The Oral Law and its intact transmission Prophets vs Sages : prophecy gives no interpretive authority The nature of dispute : never over what came from Sinai; not \"Chinese whispers\" Rabbinic authority in derivation The structure of the Mishnah and the Talmud Derashot and aggadah : deep wisdom, deliberately wrapped The purpose of humanity : wise and good ; \"the four cubits of halachah\" Why the Commentary , and the ten appendices Coming Up The four great introductions: Avot ( Shemonah Perakim : character; done a year ago); the Mishnah (structure of Mishnah and Talmud; finished tonight ); the Mishneh Torah (short, one or two sessions : next week and perhaps the week after, then the summer break); and Perek Helek , Chapter 10 of Sanhedrin, \"completely philosophical and theological, very deep and important\": from September .","GClVsQhLnlo":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), section ז: why Hashem created so many who never pursue wisdom Shiur: Part 8 (penultimate) of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 8 July 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Over seven classes: the Oral Torah; Sages vs prophets; the purpose, structure and order of the Mishnah ; the Talmud's four purposes, the fourth being derashot , which led the Rambam to the puzzling statement: אין לו להקב״ה בעולמו אלא ארבע אמות של הלכה בלבד. What can it mean? There was no halachah in the days of Shem and Ever's yeshivah , or Avraham. So the Rambam digressed into the purpose of Earth (not the whole universe): man-made things have obvious purposes; natural things serve the human , broadly speaking, and the wiser you are, the more of their purposes you grasp. The human's purpose: to fully develop intellect (all disciplines, culminating in knowing Hashem ) and character . A person isn't complete without both . The Question ונשארה כאן שאלה אחת. You said Divine wisdom makes nothing in vain; the most honoured creature on earth is man; man's purpose is the intellect. Then why did Hashem create all the people who never develop it? ואנחנו נמצא שרוב בני האדם ערומים מן החכמה, מבקשי תאוות : most people are naked of wisdom , chasing pleasures. The wise, restrained person is a rarity, a marvel: לא ימצא אלא אחד בדור מן הדורות , one in many generations. Clarifying the question It isn't a question about people. Each person has בחירה , free will, which the Rambam calls an עמוד גדול , a great pillar of the Torah. That few choose this path is the person's problem. It's a question about Hashem. If He made the Earth for the perfected human , and that person comes along once in generations, \" statistically, it doesn't seem to make sense. \" Answer 1: The Others Make the Sage's Life Possible מציאות כל אותם בני אדם לשתי סיבות. האחת, לשמש לאיש ההוא. Their existence has two reasons. First, to serve that one person. The Rambam is \"very pragmatic\" : שאילו היו בני אדם כולם מבקשי החכמה ומתפלספים, היה העולם חרב והיה נכרת המין האנושי בזמן מועט. If everyone were a philosopher, the world would stop, and humanity wouldn't last long. Why? Humans are deeply needy : To have… You'd need to learn… Bread Ploughing, harvesting, threshing, grinding, cooking, baking, and making the tools Clothes Spinning and weaving Shelter Building, and all its tools ולא יספיקו ימי מתושלח ללמוד אלו המלאכות. Not even Metushelach's 969 years would be enough to learn them all. And when would you study? So Hashem created everyone else to do that work : תתיישב הארץ ותמצא החכמה : the land is settled, and wisdom is found. \"If not for the madmen…\" A saying of the Rambam's time: לולא המשוגעים היתה הארץ נחרבת : if not for the madmen, the world would be destroyed. A man, perhaps not even strong , crosses the seas in winter and deserts in the summer heat , exposed to wild animals and creatures , for perhaps a dinar . Then he hands his hard-won money to builders to lay deep foundations and raise a wall to stand for centuries , knowing full well he hasn't enough life left to wear out a wall of reeds . \"What greater folly could there be?\" \"Still true today, with different challenges: leaving your family, planes , exposure to illness, Zoom meetings , back and forth. To make money to build a mansion that will last a few hundred years, which you'll never enjoy for a few hundred years. In the grand scheme, it's ridiculous. But we need it. We need people to develop the land.\" On a \"learn-only\" society \"It always bothered me: in some circles, every boy must go to yeshivah and kollel and learn his whole life , unless there are extenuating circumstances. And then their chachamim , when ill, go to doctors; when they need legal advice, go to lawyers. If everyone did what the rosh yeshivah said and studied all their lives, where would the doctors, lawyers and builders come from? You'd use the secular Jew, the one who only learns in the evening.\" \" Long-term immersion in Torah and wisdom is amazing , literally the pinnacle of creation. But that's for a limited few. We need ordinary people to develop the world. \" What am ha'aretz really means ועל כן קוראים החכמים לאנשים אשר אין להם חכמה \"עם הארץ\", כלומר שהם עומדים לישוב הארץ. \"How beautiful.\" Am ha'aretz isn't simply \"an ignoramus\"; it means \"people of the land\" , those whose role is to settle and develop the earth : building, clothing, food. \" It's not a derogatory term. They're a necessary part of creation.\" When should everyone study? In the days of Mashiach , when everything will be provided. \"From where we stand today it starts to make sense: robots are already taking humans' place in so many things.\" Dov and Richard: is it all or nothing? Isn't there a spectrum? Thousands are learned and fine people, not one-in-a-generation, but surely not just \"general people\"? \" 100%. The Rambam talks in extremities . It's not a zero-sum game , as if you're either perfect or an am ha'aretz . There are millions of levels: 10%, 50%, 70%. From the person's side , each develops by his free choice, and dedicating yourself to study is wonderful. From Hashem's side , if the near-perfect person appears once in generations, why create everyone else? Because some must be devoted to other things, to provide. \" When the fool is served by the wise ושמא יאמר אומר : sometimes a foolish man , rich through inheritance or luck, lives grandly, and wise men work for him . Isn't that backwards? \"You don't see the whole picture.\" In his wealth and power he commands a great palace or a vast vineyard , \"like kings do.\" One day a chasid will rest in that palace's shade and take delight in it, setting his mind right. One day he'll find in that vineyard a remedy that saves him from a snakebite. כך הן הנהגות השם יתברך ויתרומם בחכמתו : עצות מרחוק אמונה אומן . Rav Kook in England was so taken with Rembrandt's paintings that he said (in words to the effect) the painter must have had a spark of prophecy . Ben Zoma on the Temple Mount בן זומא ראה אוכלוסין על גב מעלה בהר הבית, אמר: ברוך… שברא כל אלו לשמשני (Berachot 58a) Seeing the throngs of pilgrims: Blessed is He who created all these to serve me. \"You hear that and think: arrogant, narcissistic. \" The Rambam: he was unique in his generation , and his meaning is gratitude : thank Hashem for a diverse humanity, people building homes, making clothes, growing food, so that I can devote myself to wisdom and character. What \"serving the Sage\" doesn't mean \"To be abundantly clear: the Rambam strictly forbids a chacham using Torah as a spade to dig with . He'd have hated the idea of simply supporting him.\" It means the world develops the things the Sage can then acquire and use properly . The legitimate links the Gemara allows: doing business with the Sage, or marrying one's daughter to him. The Rambam on Bava Kamma A law \"anti-Semites like to quote\" : if a Jew's ox gores a gentile's, in a place with no set laws , there's no liability. The Rambam's own Commentary there: If their law favours them, we judge by their law. But where a society lacks שלמות המדות האנושיות , the laws and norms between person and person that sustain human society , its members אינו בכלל האדם על האמת : are not adam in the true sense. \"This needs a discourse of its own.\" The Rabbi's reading: consistent with tonight's theme, a full adam is one working on intellect and character. A society living without the basic values (the seven Noahide laws, courts and justice) has left the category of adam . Answer 2: Even the Sage Needs Company אנשי החכמה הם מעטים מאוד. People of real wisdom are very few , and that's simply how reality is. Don't ask why. The Rambam uses this logic often: it's like asking why there are nine spheres or four elements (fire, air, water, earth, as they believed then). In the Moreh : why is the sky blue? \"If it were green, you'd ask why green.\" Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: ראיתי בני עלייה והן מועטין, אם שנים הן, אני ובני הן : I've seen the people of ascent, and they are few; if there are two, they are my son and I. משום כך הומצאו ההמון להיות לחברה לאנשי המעלה. The masses were created to be company for the people of excellence. ואולי תחשוב שזאת התועלת מעוטה? אדרבה, חשובה היא. You may think this a small benefit. On the contrary, it matters. \"Perhaps even more than the first.\" His proofs: - Mishpatim: לא אגרשנו מפניך בשנה אחת, פן תהיה הארץ שממה . Hashem wouldn't drive out the nations of Canaan all at once, lest the land become desolate . - Kohelet: סוף דבר הכל נשמע, את האלהים ירא ואת מצותיו שמור, כי זה כל האדם . The Midrash: the whole world was created only to be company for this one. A rabbi's memoir. \"A famous American rabbi's community said at his retirement: how will we cope without you? He answered: more than you need me, I need you. A rabbi without a community isn't a rabbi. He can't give, can't influence, can't fulfil his potential.\" Answer Kind 1. To provide Pragmatic and physical 2. To be company Emotional and social : a life without people is desolate and lonely Robert: wouldn't a Sage rather have a few more Sages as friends? \"Of course. But that's not reality : like the blue sky. A person will take any company over loneliness. Trust me, a rav would rather preach to an ignorant community that at least listens than have no one on Shabbat morning . More than a rabbi gives his community, the community gives the rabbi. \" \"The Four Cubits of Halachah,\" Solved הלכה comes from הליכה , walking : walking with Hashem, developing . ילכו מחיל אל חיל ; צדיקים אין להם מנוחה : the righteous have no rest, always walking , always growing. אין לו להקב״ה בעולמו אלא ארבע אמות של הלכה : all Hashem has in His world are those who walk , who keep developing their intellect and character. That is the purpose of creation. \" I can't tell you how many people have asked me: why are you still studying? You've studied so many years, take a break! That's not how we think. We never stop studying until the day we die. Every day: what more can I learn, revise, study? There's no end. \" וכבר התבאר… כי תכלית כל מה שבעולם ההויה וההפסד איש שלם, כולל החכמה והמעשה. The purpose of everything in this world of coming-to-be and passing-away is the complete person, who unites wisdom and deed. וכבר יצאנו מענין זה, אשר נכנסנו בו מרוב אהבה : I've strayed from the topic, out of great love. But these are fine matters of faith, not to be taken lightly. \"Now he comes back to the Talmud.\" Questions \"Isn't a great Sage's soul a gift from God, not something he develops?\" \" No, absolutely not. That's completely antithetical to the Rambam's philosophy. You're thinking of something kabbalistic , Chassidic stories of a holy neshamah coming down. A person must choose this path. We're all born with deficiencies , and we grow.\" So anyone can become like Moshe Rabbeinu? \"The Rambam writes it black and white , in the Mishneh Torah (Hilchot Teshuvah, ch. 5): every person can become like Moshe Rabbeinu. \" Richard: so is everyone else's purpose to support the perfect person, or to become perfect, and are they failing? \"A fantastic question ; it shows real depth. It depends on whose perspective. - From the person's: your purpose is to aim for perfection. I wouldn't say failed , too harsh; you haven't maximised your potential . None of us has ; we all know our failings. - From Hashem's: He knows only one in a generation will reach it, and creates everyone anyway , because of what they provide for that person.\" Summary The question is on Hashem, not man: man has free will; but why create a world for the perfected person who comes once in generations? Answer 1: to provide. If all were philosophers, the world would collapse; not even Metushelach could master every craft. \"If not for the madmen…\" Am ha'aretz = \"people of the land,\" who settle it; not a slur. Not everyone should learn all day: who'd be the doctors? Everyone studies in Mashiach's days . Even the fool served by the wise ultimately serves the wise: the palace a chasid will rest in , the vineyard's cure . Ben Zoma's blessing is gratitude, not arrogance. Answer 2: company. The wise are few ( \"my son and I\" ), as fixed as the four elements; the masses exist to be their society : פן תהיה הארץ שממה ; כי זה כל האדם . \"More than you need me, I need you.\" \"The four cubits of halachah\" = those who walk , who never stop growing. That is all Hashem has in His world. Greatness is chosen, not gifted ; anyone can be like Moshe . We all fall short of our potential, yet everyone has a purpose . Coming Up Next week (the final class), or the week after: more principles of Talmud; the Rambam's teachers, the Rif and Rabbi Yosef ibn Migash ; why his Commentary on the Mishnah is so foundational; and then something \" remarkable, before ChatGPT, before encyclopedias \": ten short chapters listing the Sages of the Mishnah, their relationships, and how often each appears.","TTeWf1UX1iQ":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), section ו: the purpose of creation and of the human being Shiur: Part 7 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 1 July 2025 (week of Parashat Chukat) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap We're nearing the end of the introduction: the transmission of the Oral Torah; prophets and Sages; how law developed; the purpose and structure of the Mishnah ; then the Talmud and Ravina and Rav Ashi's four purposes: explain, rule, record developments, and derashot . Last week: aggadah looks simple, sometimes bizarre, but holds great depth . The Sages wrote cryptically (1) to invite diverse interpretation , (2) to keep deep truths from undeveloped minds , and (3) because it was written for everyone, children included . If a statement seems absurd, blame yourself , pray for siyata dishmaya , and remember the man who knows every science but astronomy. Never mock the Sages. The cliffhanger: אין לו להקב״ה בעולמו אלא ארבע אמות של הלכה בלבד . It seems to say only the law matters , against everything the Rambam teaches. Before explaining it, he goes back to fundamentals: the purpose of creation and of the human being. Part 1: Everything Has a Purpose דע כי החכמים… כבר בארו שכל דבר נמצא יש לו בהכרח תכלית אשר בשבילה היתה מציאותו. Everything that exists necessarily has a purpose for which it exists. Things people make Nothing is manufactured for no reason: Tool Purpose Saw (made by the smith) To saw Axe To cut Needle To join cloth Things Hashem made Trees, grasses, minerals, springs, stones, animals: what are they for? Some Easy to see Some Need much investigation Some Hidden and subtle , knowable only through prophecy Who can say why some ants have wings and others don't? Why some creatures have many legs, like the centipede, and others few? What is this worm, this ant, for? The wiser you are, the more purposes you know : ככל שיהיה יותר מלומד וחזק השקידה וזך השכל, ידע תכלית יותר דברים . Shlomo HaMelech knew the purpose of every species, as far as a person can: וידבר על העצים מן הארז אשר בלבנון ועד האזוב… ועל הבהמה ועל העוף ועל הרמש ועל הדגים , \"a sign that he had divine power.\" ויבאו מכל העמים לשמע את חכמת שלמה. The general rule: everything below the moon is for the human כל הנמצאים מתחת גלגל הירח, אמנם הם נמצאו בשביל האדם בלבד. Note the limit: \"under the moon,\" meaning planet Earth . Purpose of the whole universe Rav Saadia Gaon ( Emunot veDeot , essay 4; \"those on Monday nights\") Everything, planets and stars included , is for the human The Rambam Rejects that. Only what's on Earth is for the human Examples: - Animals: some for food (cattle); some for work : donkeys carry loads, horses cover distances, \"the cars of those days.\" - Plants: some for food (fruit), some for medicine . - Where we see no use: הרי זה לחולשת דעתנו : it's our ignorance . From the elephant to the smallest worm, each has a purpose for us. His proof: in every generation the uses of plants and animals are discovered that earlier generations didn't know. No single person can grasp all of them; knowledge builds generation on generation . And poisons? Even deadly herbs have uses: applied to the body, apparently medicinal. A snake is venomous, yet useful (things are made from it). Part 2: Then What Is the Human For? If everything is for the human, we must ask: why does the human exist, and what is his purpose? Animals and plants have one or two functions: the palm gives dates; the spider's web teaches weaving; the swallow \"can help build.\" A human does many different things. So the philosophers examined every human action, and concluded there is one final purpose ; everything else (eating, drinking, pleasure) is to keep us alive so we can reach it: והוא ציור המושכלות וידיעת האמיתות כמות שהן. Grasping intellectual truths, and knowing reality as it is. Why not eating, building or ruling? It can't be to eat, drink, procreate, build a wall or be a king , for (1) these are accidents that add nothing to his essence , and (2) animals share every one of them . Animals have kings too: \"the lion king , hierarchies in every species.\" Wisdom is what changes a person's very being , moving him from a lowly state to an elevated one, from potential to actuality : כי האדם קודם שישכיל אמנם הוא כבהמה. Before a person develops his intellect, he is like an animal. \"Think of a baby: eats, drinks, excretes. That's it.\" אין האדם נבדל משאר בעלי החיים אלא בהגיון : we're rational beings. And the highest knowledge והנכבד שבמושכלות: ציור אחדות השם יתברך ויתרומם, ומה שכרוך בזה מן האלהיות. לפי ששאר החכמות אינן אלא להתלמד בהן כדי להגיע לחכמה האלהית. The most important thing to understand is the unity of Hashem , and all of theology. All other disciplines (mathematics, astronomy, which the Rambam loved, the sciences) exist to bring us to the knowledge of God. The pyramid Level Top Understanding Hashem Middle Developing the intellect : every discipline, every course of study Base Functions shared with animals : eating, drinking, procreating, entertainment, which keep us alive for the above Beneath us Everything on Earth, which exists for the human Class comment: \"So we're the kings of the world.\" \"Correct, but for what? To use the world around us to develop the intellect, and through it, our understanding of the Divine and of reality .\" Part 3: You Can't Grow Without Saying No בציור המושכלות יחויב להרחיק ההפרזה בהנאות הגשמיות. Developing the intellect requires distancing oneself from excess in physical pleasure. If a person chases desires , gives priority to sensation and enslaves his intellect to his appetites , he is like an animal ; nothing divine can be seen in him, and he becomes mere matter that perishes, שוחה בים ההיולי : \"swimming in the sea of matter.\" \" Beautiful phrase. \" This week's parashah, Chukat זאת התורה אדם כי ימות באהל : in context, the law of one who dies in a tent . The Sages (Berachot 63b): the Torah endures only in one who \"kills himself\" in the tent of Torah. What it doesn't mean: cutting yourself off from the world. \"100%\" : the Rambam never believed in denying the basic things of life, and \" hated \" fasting and self-affliction. What it does mean: anything worth achieving requires some self-denial. \"Leave Torah aside. You want to become a professor, you're doing your A-levels , your degree, your exams: I can't watch that film tonight; I can't go out with my friends; I need to study. Wise people recognise it's worth it. A lot of people don't, which is why they don't study and end up undeveloped. \" \" My stomach is rumbling , but I'm in the middle of a shiur, trying to understand something: I'll eat in an hour. I'd love to go to the cinema with friends tonight, but I want to learn a bit. It's a negation. That's the reality of life in every discipline, and certainly in Torah.\" Part 4: The Purpose of Earth Is a Wise and Good Person הנה יתבאר מכל ההקדמות כי תכלית עולמנו ומה שבו אמנם הוא איש חכם וטוב. The purpose of our world (again: our world , not the universe, against Rav Saadia) is a wise and good person. When one person achieves both wisdom and action: Meaning חכמה , wisdom ציור האמיתות כמות שהן : understanding reality as it truly is, as far as humanly possible מעשה , action יושר המזג , balance in natural things; הרחקת ההתמכרות , no addictions : taking from the physical only what the body needs ; and צדק המדות כולן , refined character Wisdom must include action: כל שחכמתו מרובה ממעשיו, אין חכמתו מתקיימת . The Chida and his snuff. Rav Chaim Yosef David Azulai (1700s) wrote this in his diary. Snuff and tobacco were new and popular, and he enjoyed it a lot . Staying in someone's home, he got up at night to get some , and realised he'd woken his host . \" At that moment he realised: I'm too addicted. From then on, never again.\" \" That's a real chacham . That's what the Rambam means by 'addiction': anything that forces my choices , so I'm not fully in control.\" Not just Jewish wisdom This isn't known only from the prophets, but from the ancient sages of the nations. \"The greatest of philosophers,\" Aristotle (in the Ethics ): כוונת האל בנו שנהיה נבונים וטובים : God's intention for us is that we be wise and good. Both are needed Verdict Wise but chasing pleasure Not truly wise : true wisdom takes from physical pleasure only what sustains the body. Yirmeyahu : איכה תאמרו חכמים אנחנו ותורת ה׳ אתנו? Restrained and good-charactered but unlearned Also incomplete : he acts rightly, but not from conviction , not from wisdom. (Still, the Rambam is clear: better good character without learning than learning without good character) Both The purpose of the world So אין בור ירא חטא ולא עם הארץ חסיד (Avot 2): an ignoramus can't be truly God-fearing or pious. That's why the Torah always says ולמדתם… ושמרתם לעשתם : learning before doing . Wisdom leads to good action; good action alone doesn't lead to wisdom. תלמוד מביא לידי מעשה. \"Good character\" here isn't just nice middot : \" obviously you can't be a chacham with bad middot.\" It means balance : if your intellect were fully developed, it would have come down into your actions and temperament . A small addiction means the intellect hasn't reached the heart . Part 5: \"But Why Does It Matter?\" Murray asked: why is it necessary to be intellectual? Does it mean \"not an idiot,\" or someone who reads books all the time? \" Reading books, that's what we're talking about. The ultimate book is the Torah, but all disciplines, all sciences, all knowledge matter, and together they bring you to knowing Hashem.\" Why that matters? The Rambam takes it as self-evident. The alternative is to entertain yourself, live 70, 80, 100 years, die, \"and swim in the sea of materialism.\" \"But you die anyway, intellectual or not.\" \" The intellect stays with you forever. Your intellect is your identity. How many steaks someone ate goes to the grave with him. But a developed intellect and character reach the core of the person , what we call the neshamah , and that's eternal .\" Does it come back at the resurrection? \"No need. Whatever Mashiach, resurrection and Olam HaBa are, every one of our Sages agrees some part of us is eternal .\" Eternity, as taught to the Rabbi as a child: \"Fill Wembley Stadium with tiny sunflower seeds . Every day a bird takes one . Millions, perhaps billions of years. That's eternity. \" A further thought: \"Why doesn't the Rambam say that here, when he does elsewhere? I think he's saying that even forgetting the next world , to live as a human being in this world , developing intellect and character is what distinguishes us from animals. That's what makes us human. \" Part 6: Then Why Create Everyone Else? ונשארה כאן שאלה אחת. You said Divine wisdom makes nothing in vain; everything on earth is for the human; the human is for the intellect. Then why did Hashem create all the people who never develop it? רוב בני אדם ערומים מן החכמה, מבקשי תאוות : most people are naked of wisdom , chasing desires; the wise, restrained person is so rare , a marvel, perhaps one in a generation . \"How much more so today.\" \"Most of us are running to make a living.\" \"That's absolutely fair, and that's actually part of his answer.\" The Rambam's two answers (more next week): 1 The others make the wise one's life possible. If he had to build his own house and weave his own clothes , he'd have no time to develop himself 2 Even the wise need society. Alone, \" they'd be bored .\" Other, different people give their lives colour Summary Everything has a purpose : obvious, discoverable, or knowable only by prophecy. Shlomo knew them all. Unknown uses reflect our ignorance ; each generation discovers more. Everything on Earth (not the whole universe, against Rav Saadia) is for the human. The human's one purpose: ציור המושכלות , grasping truth, crowned by knowing Hashem's unity . Eating, building, ruling: animals do those too . Before developing his intellect, a person is like an animal. It requires self-denial : not asceticism (the Rambam hated it) but priorities , as in exams: אדם כי ימות באהל . Otherwise you're \" swimming in the sea of matter .\" The purpose of the world is a person both wise and good , with no addictions ( the Chida's snuff ). Aristotle agrees: \"wise and good.\" Learning comes first , because it leads to action. The intellect is eternal : steaks aren't. And even in this world, it's what makes us human . Why so many others? They sustain the wise and give them society . More next week. Coming Up Next week: the two answers in depth, and then, finally, \"the four cubits of halachah.\"","LbnCGFgo0LQ":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), on the derashot and aggadah of the Sages Shiur: Part 6 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 24 June 2025 These notes are based on a machine transcript of the recording (no Sofer transcript exists for this class). Recap The Oral tradition came from Moshe; each generation's Sages derived new laws (a prophet has no extra authority); Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi gathered everything into the Mishnah, whose orders and tractates the Rambam explained one by one. Then, about 250 years later , Ravina and Rav Ashi , the last Sages, composed the Talmud , with four purposes : # 1 Explanation Interpreting the Mishnah, with the disputes and each side's proofs 2 Ruling In the Mishnah's disputes and over its interpretation 3 Additions Laws derived from the Mishnah, and gezerot and takkanot up to the Gemara 4 Derashot Tonight The first three are halachah . The fourth is aggadah : the Sages take a verse and play on it , and sometimes the result looks far-fetched . Why did they do it? An Example: Kinah, Dimonah, Adadah A verse lists towns in Yehudah: קינה ודימונה ועדעדה . A Sage was asked what it means: it's a list of cities, isn't it? \" Really? Is that the purpose of the verse? \" (Gittin 7a): קינה : whoever has a grievance ( kinah ) against his fellow ודימונה : and keeps silent ( domem ) ועדעדה : He who dwells forever ( adei ad ) will take up his cause. And another list: צקלג ומדמנה וסנסנה : whoever has cause to cry out and argue ( tza'ak ) and keeps silent ( domem ), He who dwells in the thornbush ( sneh , the burning bush) will act for him. \"The Talmud is scattered with these. You read them and think: what on earth are they saying? Sometimes it seems really far-fetched, really obscure. People think: what are these rabbis on about? It's ridiculous. \" Don't Skip the Aggadah \" In many yeshivot I was in , when we reached a section of aggadah in the tractate, the rabbi would say: skip it, go on to the next bit. Maybe we were too young to appreciate it.\" The Rambam: don't think it lacks value. In it are the most beautiful and deep wonders of wisdom , everything the philosophers of every generation sought to understand. Read simply, it looks ridiculous; delve into it, and you uncover the deepest wisdom, which the Sages did not want simply to give out. \"The deeper something is, the more it needs to be wrapped and shelled.\" The more precious something is, the more you cover and protect it. The Rambam was \" the first to really put this out, and everyone followed him. \" The best place to see it: Bava Batra 73–75 , \" you'll literally fall off your chair .\" Three Reasons the Sages Hid Their Wisdom 1. It invites more interpretation The more obscure something is, the more \"fruit\" it bears: students have more room to think. \" Halachah is halachah : little scope to develop your own ideas. Aggadah and derashot are where the person who is creative and artistic in their intellect can bring out the beauty of the mind, with completely different interpretations. That's the beauty of aggadah. \" 2. To keep secrets from those who can't receive them To blind the eyes of fools , who would never grasp these truths if presented bare. That's why it's forbidden to teach the secrets of Torah to the unprepared : the wise always kept their secrets to themselves. Rav Yosef and the Sages of Pumbedita (Chagigah 13a) The Rambam doesn't name him; the Gemara does: Rav Yosef (end of the 3rd century). Two great bodies of secret wisdom , as the Talmud identifies them. \"Ask someone today where our secrets are, they'll say the Zohar . The Zohar wasn't around then.\" מעשה בראשית , the work of Creation מעשה מרכבה , the Chariot Text The opening chapters of Bereishit Yechezkel's vision (the Shavuot haftarah) Content (Moreh Nevuchim) Everything Hashem made: the sciences , geography, mathematics, astronomy; today, physics, chemistry Hashem Himself : philosophy and theology Rav Yosef Weak in it Expert The deal: you teach me Bereishit, I'll teach you Merkavah. They taught him. Then he refused. Isn't that fraud? \"God forbid\" that Rav Yosef, with all his wisdom, did it out of jealousy , a trait found \"only in fools.\" Learning with them, he realised they couldn't grasp Ma'aseh Merkavah , so he couldn't teach it. The Gemara's source: דבש וחלב תחת לשונך (Shir HaShirim): the sweetest things, honey and milk, must sometimes stay under the tongue , kept quietly. The children's midrash book \"There are lovely books, The Midrash Says , The Little Midrash Says , with cartoons. Children read them. Fantastic. The danger is adults reading them and thinking that's what Torah is .\" Batya and the baby Moshe: ותשלח את אמתה ותקחה . In context, אמה is a maidservant : she sent her maid. But the Midrash notices the maids were called נערות a moment before, and reads אמה as forearm (as in the measure, an amah ): her arm grew miraculously long and reached the baby. \"Of course her arm didn't grow.\" The Sages' message is fairly plain to anyone who thinks: there's nothing you can't do if you truly want to. Normally she couldn't have reached him, but her compassion , the crying baby, gave her extra strength. \"You've heard of mothers who see their baby in peril and suddenly do something extraordinary to save it.\" \"That's a simple midrash; there are far deeper ones.\" What we should do Work hard and sharpen the intellect, and pray : גל עיני ואביטה נפלאות מתורתך (Tehillim 119): open my eyes, that I may see wonders from Your Torah. If you grasp some secrets, keep them to yourself , or share them privately with someone on your level or higher . \"Simple people won't understand, and they'll mock you .\" Public Kabbalah classes. \"Recently, in Israel, rabbis give Kabbalah shiurim for the public . People who never opened a Talmud come in the evening, get excited: wow, I know the secrets of Hashem! This is exactly what I'm saying: don't. Even a tiny drop, keep it to yourself. \" פרדס : פשט (plain), רמז (hints, numerical values), דרש (what we're learning now), סוד . \"It's called sod because it's meant to stay a sod . We teach derashot publicly. The secrets, never. \" 3. Most people can only receive stories Teaching the masses , \"including women and children,\" the Rambam says, and the undeveloped, you can only give stories, riddles and parables . So the Sages spoke of deep things בדרך רמז , by hint: the deep take the depth; the simple keep the simple layer. Blame Yourself, Not the Sages If a rabbinic statement seems bizarre and far from logic, don't attribute the failure to the Sages' words. Attribute it to your own intellect. Intellects differ, just as temperaments do; you can't compare a fully developed intellect with one that exists only in potential. The Rambam's parable: the size of the sun Take a man expert in medicine, mathematics, music and the sciences , but ignorant of astronomy . Tell him the sun is 166⅜ times the size of the earth . He'll say it's ridiculous : how can someone on earth, unable to travel around it, barely able to look at it, calculate its size to three-eighths? He'd be sure. But let him learn astronomy and read the Almagest (\"I googled this\": Claudius Ptolemy , 2nd-century Greek mathematician and astronomer, on the motions of the stars and planets) and he'll be as certain of 166⅜ as that the sun exists. (Today's figure: the sun's diameter is about 109 times the earth's. \"Close, but still.\") All the more so someone who never developed his intellect at all, whose only intellectual life went \" from his mother's understanding to his wife's .\" He'd mock the Sages outright. (The Rabbi noted the Rambam \"was not the biggest fan of women\"; in that culture women were unlearned.) So: never say this makes no sense, it's wrong. Study more wisdom, more disciplines , until you understand. The Rambam holds that the sciences are an essential part of the wisdom of Torah ; in the Moreh he writes that a prophet must master them too. The Sages blamed themselves The Talmudic Sages had developed intellects , a whole community of scholars, and rejected worldly luxury . Yet when they couldn't understand their predecessors, they said: לבם של ראשונים כפתחו של אולם, ושלנו כפתחו של מחט סדקית : the hearts of earlier generations were like the great hall's doorway; ours are like the eye of a needle. How much more so us. The Rambam names four obstacles in his own day: 1 חולשת השכל : the intellect has weakened 2 חוזק התאוה : desires are stronger 3 Laziness in pursuing wisdom 4 Pursuit of the world's luxuries \"How on earth can you say it's the Sages' fault for not explaining, and not your own?\" \"Boiling excrement\" The Gemara (Gittin 57a): כל המלעיג על דברי חכמים נידון בצואה רותחת : whoever mocks the Sages' words is judged in boiling excrement . The Rambam: a metaphor . The punishment is eternal foolishness . Mock the Sages instead of delving into them, and you stay forever in that filth of ignorance , never developing yourself. So the Sages commanded us to study Torah by night, and by day whenever we don't need to work. A Cliffhanger: \"Only the Four Cubits of Halachah\" אין לו להקב״ה בעולמו אלא ארבע אמות של הלכה בלבד. The Holy One has nothing in His world but the four cubits of halachah. This seems to contradict everything the Rambam just said: the goal is the secrets hidden in aggadah, yet this says God cares only about halachah . And at face value it can't be true: in the days of Shem, Ever and Avraham there was no fixed halachah . Does God care nothing for all other wisdom? \" I don't know if you've noticed what he's doing here. He proves his point with a statement that superficially goes against it , then shows that its depth means the opposite : a living example of an aggadah whose surface meaning is wrong.\" But first he must ask: what is the purpose of humanity, and of the whole universe? Summary The Talmud's fourth element is derashot and aggadah , which often look far-fetched (Kinah, Dimonah, Adadah). Don't skip them , as many yeshivot do. They hold the deepest wisdom , wrapped up because it's precious. Three reasons for the wrapping: (1) it invites creative interpretation ; (2) secrets must be kept from those who can't receive them ( Rav Yosef and Pumbedita; honey and milk under the tongue ; no public Kabbalah; sod stays *sod ); (3) the masses can only receive stories: Batya's arm *. If it seems absurd, blame yourself , as the sun's size seems absurd to a wise man who knows no astronomy. Study more disciplines; pray : גל עיני . The Talmudic Sages said our hearts are a needle's eye . In the Rambam's time: weaker minds, stronger desires, laziness, luxury. Mocking the Sages = \" boiling excrement \" = eternal foolishness . Next: \"the four cubits of halachah\" , and the purpose of humanity. Coming Up Next Tuesday, back at 8–9: the purpose of the universe and of human beings , and how it unlocks \"the four cubits of halachah.\" (Thursday's halachah shiur may move because of a wedding; watch the group.)","ZKN8V8M5ebM":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), the structure of the Mishnah, concluded Shiur: Part 5 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 10 June 2025 (after Shavuot) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap The Oral Torah: Moshe received it, taught it at least four times , and reviewed it before he died. Each generation's Sages (not prophets: prophecy gives no interpretive authority) derived new laws, sometimes differently. Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi , in the third century CE , about 1,700 years ago, gathered it all into the Mishnah , in five categories : from Moshe with a scriptural source; from Moshe without ( halachah leMoshe miSinai ); derived (disputable, more so over time); fences (poultry and milk, by majority); takkanot (e.g. prozbul , so people can lend). The structure (two weeks ago; \"to the best of my knowledge the Rambam is the only one who gives a reason for every tractate's place\"): Zera'im with Berachot first; Mo'ed , \"a meeting ,\" for the festivals are meeting times with Hashem, and Shabbat too ( מועדי ); Nashim from Yevamot to Kiddushin; Nezikin, where Bava Kamma first fixes the hazards in society, Bava Metzia covers relationships the Torah addresses (guardians, disputed articles), Bava Batra those it largely doesn't (land). Then Sanhedrin, Makkot… up to Avot. Part 1: Why Avot Is Here We've just finished six weeks of Pirkei Avot, \"but its messages are timeless .\" This is exactly the kind of opening where the Rambam loves to digress into ethics . Why put ethics in an order about torts and damages? Reason 1: so we accept the Sanhedrin להודיעך אמיתת הקבלה, שהיא אמת, שקבלוה רבים מרבים. To show that the tradition is true , received by many from many. So Avot opens with the chain: משה קבל תורה מסיני ומסרה ליהושע, ויהושע לזקנים, וזקנים לנביאים, ונביאים מסרוה לאנשי כנסת הגדולה , then the Sages by name, generation after generation. \"Look in Tanach and you'll see the link.\" The Great Assembly included Daniel and Ezra ; Shimon HaTzaddik was its last member. Why here? The previous tractates are all the Sanhedrin's : it administers Bava Kamma, Metzia and Batra, lashes (Makkot), oaths (Shevuot), testimony (Eduyot), capital cases for idolatry . Where does it get that authority? Avot answers: an unbroken chain back to Moshe . שהוא בדורו כמו הם בדורותיהם. Each generation's Sanhedrin is like the Sages of Moshe's time. יפתח בדורו כשמואל בדורו : the leaders of each generation are, for that generation, like Moshe and Shmuel in theirs. \"Otherwise, where would you stop?\" ובזה יש מוסר לבני אדם, שלא יאמרו: משפט פלוני נקבל, ובתקנת פלוני נאמין? אין הדבר כן, כי המשפט אינו לפלוני השופט, אלא לה׳ אשר צונו בו. Judgment doesn't belong to so-and-so the judge. It belongs to Hashem , who commanded it. \"You may think: I'd have accepted Moshe's Sanhedrin, Yehoshua's, maybe Shmuel's. But our rabbis are the same as us. No. They have an unbroken tradition.\" Which authority, and when? (The Rabbi gave a whole series on rabbinic authority today a few years ago.) For the Rambam , this binding authority ended with the Talmud , about 250 years after the Mishnah, around 500 CE . It belongs to the Sanhedrin . A thought experiment. \"If we had a Sanhedrin today of the 71 gedolei hador (whether that's possible is another discussion) and it enacted something, it would bind us, and ignoring it would violate לא תסור . One I've always thought of: if you're keeping two days of Yom Tov in Israel, you should have to wear a tie. Everyone around you is working; it's so easy to jump on a bus, take out your phone. Make a takkanah!\" But if one Sage, or three, or seventy, or a letter signed by a hundred rabbis, says something: \"It's important to listen and see if there's something we need to take on. But if other rabbis tell you otherwise and you don't, that's fine. That's not lo tasur . \" \"That's the Rambam. Others disagreed.\" Today's batei din have very limited halachic power: no fines and penalties ( dinei kenasot ); they can arbitrate , do conversions , divorces , marriages . Much of what the Sanhedrin did requires semichah , a direct chain of ordination. Reason 2: so judges behave לומר בזה המסכתא מוסרי כל חכם מחכמי האומה. To record the ethics of every Sage of the nation, so we learn from them. That's for everyone, but judges need it most : עמי הארץ, אם לא יהיו בעלי מוסר, לא יזיקו לציבור, אלא יזיקו לעצמן בלבד. והשופט, אם לא יהיה בעל מוסר וחינוך, יאבד עצמו ויאבד בני אדם כולם. An ordinary person without good character harms only himself . A judge without it destroys himself and everyone. \"How can you administer capital punishment or lashes, or judge between people, when you're not living up to the highest standards? Pirkei Avot gives the highest standards.\" What a judge must have at his fingertips (all from Avot) The balance הוו מתונים בדין Be deliberate. Don't rush; you can be tricked or miss things …but no עינוי הדין Don't delay a clear case. \"You have the answer. Give it. Cut to the chase. \" Witnesses Be precise with them, and don't phrase questions so they sense the answer you want and lie to supply it Litigants Don't teach them what to say. (\"The halachic status of lawyers : a talk for another time\") Dignity No light-headedness with ordinary people, lest he be demeaned …but not aloof Don't hide from people. The Sanhedrin are עיני העדה , the eyes of the community: \" not the leader in the ivory tower. \" No luxury Don't pursue leisure and pleasure, lest desire pull him from judging (Avot ch. 6) Don't seek cases אל תהי חושך עצמך מן הדין : someone always eager to be dayan invites suspicion of getting something on the side Compromise Always try for pesharah . Never ruling, always compromising: \" that's the best .\" Only if that fails, be firm and to the point Patience Hear people out, even when long-winded, until they're clearly off-topic The Sephardi way. \"In the Ashkenazi world the rosh yeshivah is the highest level, far above a community rabbi. For us, all our greatest Sages were always community rabbis , because the greatest psak and wisdom come from living with the people and hearing them . But you can't be one of them either: you have to keep that authority.\" \"Like an expert doctor\" The Rambam, a physician, \"always likes to compare things to medicine\": Doctor Judge If food and drink can heal, no medicine . (\"Nowadays: any problem, take this pill . That's not a good doctor. A good doctor first tries changing your diet\") Compromise If not, mild remedies close to food (sweet potions) A ruling given with mercy and compassion If that fails, the strongest medicines ( scammony and other Arabic-named drugs; the class suggested \"antibiotics\") Be firm and sharp \"I once saw someone I really respect, the kindest person, the best middot , behaving so sharp, so strong, so difficult , because the case had dragged on and nobody would compromise . However kind you are, if compromise doesn't work, you're strong .\" Last: no pursuit of wealth and display : מלך במשפט יעמיד ארץ . The Gemara: if the dayan is like a king, who needs nothing from anyone , let him judge; if he needs from others, he shouldn't . So Avot comes after Sanhedrin : it holds the ethics every judge needs. And Horayot last הוריות : mistaken rulings of the Sanhedrin or beit din. מי שיש לו טבע בשר ודם אי אפשר שלא ישגה : no human can avoid all error. Last , because hopefully we won't need it. Nezikin: eight tractates. Part 2: Order 5, Kodashim (11 tractates) # Tractate Why here 1 Zevachim Animal sacrifices: Vayikra ch. 1 2 Menachot Flour offerings: Vayikra ch. 2 3 Chullin Ordinary meat for your own table. Re'eh: after \"bring your offerings to the place He chooses\" comes רק בכל אות נפשך תזבח ואכלת בשר . Shechitah, which parts may be eaten 4 Bechorot Firstborns , human ( pidyon haben ) and animal. Next in that passage: לא תוכל לאכול בשעריך… ובכרת בקרך וצאנך 5 Arachin Valuations : consecrating a person's value (\"I pledge my wife's value, my son's\") at the Torah's fixed rates by sex and age. Not intrinsic holiness: financial 6 Temurah Transferring holiness from one animal to another 7 Keritot Why karet, in Sacrifices? Because whatever carries karet when deliberate carries a chatat (sin offering) when inadvertent (e.g. Shabbat) 8 Me'ilah The lower level : what deliberately carries lashes carries an asham me'ilah inadvertently 9 Tamid No laws : a description of the daily offering, so near the end 10 Middot No laws : the measurements of the Temple , so that \"one day, when it's rebuilt, we'll have the exact details\" 11 Kinnim Very complicated: bird offerings that got mixed up . It's rare , so last The shape: 1–4 concern intrinsically holy bodies; 5–6 financial holiness; 7–8 the sin and guilt offerings; 9–11 description and rare cases. Pronunciation: \"Many tractates' names are completely mispronounced in the yeshivah world. It's not Arochin ; it's ערכין, Arachin . There's no tractate called Kreisus ; it's כריתות, Keritot .\" Keeping a list. On inadvertent karet sins, one posek wrote that since we can't bring a chatat today, you should write each one down , so that when the Temple is rebuilt you'll bring a chatat for each. \"This was written. Literally.\" Part 3: Order 6, Taharot (12 tractates) # Tractate Why here 1 Kelim First define what a kli (utensil) is : only a utensil or a person can become impure 2 Ohalot The dead body , the most severe impurity 3 Nega'im Tzara'at , next most severe, and the only other impurity transmitted by a tent 4 Parah The red heifer : purifying from the dead body 5 Taharot The lesser impurities (e.g. animal carcasses), which give the whole order its name 6 Mikva'ot Purifying from them in the mikveh 7 Niddah Severe, but applies to one gender only , so after the general ones 8 Machshirin What makes food susceptible to impurity: contact with liquid (Shemini) 9 Zavim A man with a persistent emission: one gender 10 Tevul Yom Also one gender 11 Yadayim Impurity of the hands only: a single body part 12 Uktzin Fruit stems : does a stem share its fruit's impurity? Rare and with no verse , so last The leper and the dead What separates humans from animals is the power of speech , our consciousness expressed in words. One who destroys his faculty of speech (tzara'at comes from lashon hara ) is, as a human, dead , though alive, breathing, eating and drinking. So the metzora sits alone outside the camp seven days , tears his clothes , lets his hair grow : he sits shivah for himself , mourning the identity he lost until he regains it. The totals 61 tractates, 523 chapters. \"Only the Rambam could do something like this, before you could put it into AI or Google .\" Why? \" Before we go into the detail, I need to give you a framework of the whole Mishnah. And how beautiful it is.\" Part 4: From Mishnah to Talmud The Tosefta and other baraitot Rebbi wrote as concisely and precisely as possible , for memory and transmission. But he wrote for people like himself , and some found it hard . Work By Purpose Tosefta Rabbi Chiya , Rebbi's student (early 4th century), and similarly Rabbi Oshaya Explain the Mishnah and add what can be derived from it \"only with hard work\" Sifra and Sifrei Rav (late 3rd / early 4th century) Also explanatory, adding material But none of these baraitot come close to the Mishnah in the precision of its words and its brevity . ראוה בנות ויאשרוה, מלכות ופילגשים ויהללוה : when compared, all praise the Mishnah . \"The Mishnah is the head .\" Ravina and Rav Ashi Each generation tried to understand the Mishnah better, disagreed on its explanation, and added : עד שהגיע הזמן לרבינא ורב אשי, והם סוף חכמי התלמוד. Until the time of Ravina and Rav Ashi, the last Sages of the Talmud. By then ( around 500 CE , about 250 years after the Mishnah) there was so much explanation and disagreement that Rav Ashi decided to do for everyone after Rebbi what Rebbi had done for everyone from Moshe to himself : וקיבץ כל מאמרי האומרים, והתבוננות המתבוננים, והשמועות השומעים… וחיבר התלמוד. He gathered all the sayings, reflections and teachings, and composed the Talmud . The four contents of the Talmud \" Whenever you learn Talmud, it has to be one of these four things. Pay attention to this.\" # Purpose What it covers 1 Explanation ( perush ) Explaining the Mishnah; the disputes over how to explain it; each side's proofs for why its reading is better. \"Often pages of the Gemara\" 2 Ruling ( psak ) Deciding (a) disputes in the Mishnah; (b) disputes about how to explain it; (c) disputes over what to derive from it, just as the Sages derived from the Torah 3 New law Everything derived from the Mishnah, and the gezerot and *takkanot added from 250 to 500 CE *, that weren't in Rebbi's day 4 Derashot דרשות מתאימות לענין כל פרק ופרק : aggadah , \"flowery\" teachings that take a verse, turn it on its head , and draw out an idea or moral that isn't its plain meaning at all Summary Avot sits in Nezikin for two reasons: (1) its unbroken chain from Sinai shows why we must accept each generation's Sanhedrin : judgment belongs to Hashem , not \"so-and-so\" (for the Rambam, that binding authority ended with the Talmud ); (2) judges above all need its ethics: deliberate but prompt, dignified but approachable, unworldly, compromise first , \" like an expert doctor .\" Horayot (judicial error) comes last. Kodashim: animal and flour offerings → ordinary meat → firstborns (the order of Re'eh ) → valuations and exchanges → Keritot (karet ↔ chatat ) → Me'ilah → descriptive Tamid and Middot → rare Kinnim. It's Arachin and Keritot . Taharot: define a utensil → the dead (most severe) → tzara'at , \" sitting shivah for oneself \" → purification → lesser impurities → mikveh → one-gender impurities → hands → stems. 61 tractates, 523 chapters. Tosefta, Sifra, Sifrei explain and add, but the Mishnah is the head . Rav Ashi and Ravina compiled the Talmud , whose four contents are explanation, ruling, new law and derashot . Coming Up Next: the Sages' derashot and aggadah . \"I'll show you some of the most bizarre ones; you'll fall off your chair .\" Why did the Sages teach in such strange ways? The Rambam gives \" a beautiful, very deep idea \" and develops it at length.","A4Nk7Ni0qwE":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), the structure of the Mishnah Shiur: Part 4 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 27 May 2025 (a few days before Shavuot) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Before Shavuot: Why We Read Ruth There are many reasons for the old custom, Sephardi and Ashkenazi alike, of reading Megillat Ruth on Shavuot. One fits this series exactly. On Shavuot we think of Sinai and the written Torah, and there's a danger of thinking that's all Torah is. No book shows the authority of the Sages better than Ruth. לא יבא עמוני ומואבי בקהל ה׳… עד עולם. An Ammonite or Moabite may not marry into Israel, even after conversion . (Today we can't identify anyone, so all converts may marry; in theory a traceable male Moabite couldn't.) So David was taunted by Do'eg , a Sage of his time, that his lineage was flawed: his great-grandmother Ruth was a Moabite. Only the Sages' oral tradition saves him: מואבי ולא מואבית , a male Moabite, not a female , though the Torah's wording would normally include both. Without the Oral Torah, Ruth wouldn't be someone we respect, and David couldn't be king. So as we celebrate receiving the written Torah, we read Ruth to remember that the Oral Torah is an essential part of it. Recap: Parts 1–3 Everything taught orally to Moshe was taught so that every Jew heard it at least four times ; Moshe reviewed it all before he died. Each generation derived new laws and passed them on with what came from Moshe. Differences arose, but mostly there was consensus . A prophet has no authority in interpretation : 1,000 prophets, 1,001 Sages, the Sages win . לא בשמים היא : everyone has an equal right to derive, provided the method is right , and we follow the majority. His one power: a temporary suspension of a law, never involving idolatry. Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi , \" Rebbi ,\" the teacher, third century, gathered it all into the Mishnah , in five categories : # Category Example Dispute? 1 From Moshe, with a scriptural support the Sages later found Eye for eye = money ; pri etz hadar = etrog Never 2 From Moshe, no scriptural support Black tefillin Never 3 Derived by each generation's Sages; probably most of the Mishnah. Rebbi records minority views so you know a community following one has a source Yes , more as generations passed 4 Rabbinic fences ( gezerot ) Meat and milk extended to poultry Yes 5 Takkanot : advice that became halachah Ezra : wash clothes on Thursday , not Friday Yes The Rambam is always particular in the Mishneh Torah to mark the difference: a fence is אסרו / הרחיקו , you may not go against it; a takkanah isn't a fence protecting a Torah law, \"just good practice , how to become better people.\" Of course we follow it. Tonight: A Tour of the Whole Mishnah \"I don't think anyone did it before him, and I don't think anyone has since. Anyone who's studied Mishnah will appreciate how beautifully he puts it. \" People learn Mishnah and Gemara their whole lives and never ask: - Why does Seder Nashim put divorce ( Gittin ) before marriage ( Kiddushin )? - Why is Avot , ethics, in Nezikin , damages ? - Why does Moed go Shabbat → Pesach → Yom Kippur → Sukkot → Rosh Hashanah ? \"For the Rambam, before you can learn Mishnah and Gemara you need a framework. He gives you the framework.\" The Six Orders # Order Covers 1 זרעים , Seeds Mitzvot of the produce of the land : mixtures, shemittah , orlah , terumot , ma'asrot , other gifts 2 מועד , Times Shabbat and the festivals : what's obligatory, forbidden, permitted 3 נשים , Women Relationships : yibbum , chalitzah , ketubah , kiddushin , gittin 4 נזיקין , Damages Money between people: commerce, partnerships, inheritance, torts 5 קדשים , Holy things Sacrifices 6 טהרות , Purities Purity and impurity Moed is better translated \"times\" : מועד is an appointment . Shabbat and the festivals are appointments with Hashem . Rebbi called each division a סדר , an \"order\" or structure. Why this order Reason Seeds first We eat from the land , and without food we can't serve Hashem Seeds → Times Mishpatim : שש שנים תזרע את ארצך comes before שלש רגלים תחג לי בשנה Women → Damages Mishpatim again: וכי ימכר איש את בתו לאמה before וכי יגח שור Holy things The sacrifices are in Vayikra (Vayikra, Tzav) Purities last Purity follows sacrifices in Vayikra itself : Tazria, Metzora, Acharei Mot Inside each order: orders → tractates ( מסכתא ) → chapters ( פרק ) → individual laws , which Rebbi called הלכה . \"We call each one a mishnah ; the Yerushalmi calls it a halachah .\" Order 1: Zera'im (11 tractates) Why open with Berachot? \" The best doctor , wanting to keep a healthy person healthy, starts with diet . You can take the best medications and spend hours in the gym, but without a good diet it's worthless.\" So Rebbi, \"Hashem being with him,\" began with blessings : you can't eat without first making a blessing . Berachot also covers blessings on mitzvot , to make the subject complete, and Keriat Shema , the only non-blessing in it, because you can't discuss the blessings of the Shema without the Shema itself . That's why it opens מאימתי קורין את שמע . # Tractate Why here 1 Berachot Food first; and before eating, a blessing 2 Pe'ah The only gift given while the crop is still standing : leave the corner unharvested. All others are post-harvest 3 Demai With Pe'ah, the only two that go to the poor (doubtfully-tithed produce may be fed to them) 4 Kilayim Follows Pe'ah in the Torah : Kedoshim's פאת שדך then שדך לא תזרע כלאים 5 Shevi'it Kedoshim's orlah would be next, but shemittah comes whether you like it or not , while orlah only when you plant a fruit tree. More frequent first. And shemittah has its own parashah (Behar); orlah a few verses 6 Terumot After harvest, the first gift : to the kohen , about 2% 7 Ma'asrot (first tithe) Next, to the Levi : 10% 8 Ma'aser Sheni The second tithe, eaten in Jerusalem 9 Challah Now I grind and make dough 10 Orlah Grain is done; now fruit . (Orlah still applies outside Israel : no fruit for the first three years) 11 Bikkurim First fruits brought to the Temple Order 2: Mo'ed (12 tractates) # Tractate Why here 1 Shabbat Most important, and every seven days 2 Eruvin Carrying and the eruv belong with Shabbat 3 Pesachim The first mitzvah given as a nation ( Bo ), and first in Emor's festival list 4 Shekalim Ki Tisa , near Bo 5 Yoma (Yom Kippur) Acharei Mot , next in the Torah 6 Sukkah The remaining Torah festivals (Emor). Before Beitzah, because Sukkot has many more mitzvot 7 Beitzah Shavuot has no tractate; its equivalent is the general laws of Yom Tov (the class's daily-halachah series draws mostly on Beitzah) 8 Rosh Hashanah The last Torah festival remaining 9 Ta'anit Now the prophets : the fasts, instituted by Yeshayahu -era prophets ( צום הרביעי, החמישי, השביעי, העשירי ) 10 Megillah Also prophetic, but later : Mordechai 11 Mo'ed Katan Chol HaMo'ed, mourning: on the \"small festivals\" fasting and eulogies are forbidden 12 Chagigah The festival offering is for men only ; everything else applies to men and women , so it goes last Order 3: Nashim (7 tractates) Why open with Yevamot , a rare case? Yibbum : if a man dies childless , his brother must marry the widow or release her through chalitzah . The Torah creates a bond ( zikah ) that can't just be left; otherwise she could never remarry. \"Does this still happen?\" \" 100%. It happens in Israel. It's never happened in our beit din yet; it's one of the things I'd want to do one day.\" Recently married soldiers who fell : their brothers must do yibbum or chalitzah. What if there's no brother? \"Then she's free.\" The class tried: it's earlier in the Torah? No: yibbum is in Ki Teitzei , near the end. The answer: it's the only compulsory one. \"Marriage is a mitzvah, but at the end of the day you choose . Yibbum: once it happens, it must be done. \" פתח בדבר המחויב : begin with the obligatory. # Tractate (Rambam's order) Why 1 Yevamot Compulsory 2 Ketubot Marriage proper (not betrothal), like yibbum 3 Nedarim A husband may annul his wife's vows 4 Nazir A kind of vow 5 Gittin When marriage breaks down: divorce 6 Sotah Another kind of divorce : the wayward wife 7 Kiddushin (Why last? below) \"Oh wow, I've just realised something.\" In our printed Shas the order is Nedarim, Nazir, Sotah, Gittin, Kiddushin , but the Rambam's has Gittin before Sotah . \"I have to research this.\" Why does Kiddushin , betrothal, come last? Ketubot first, fine: like Yevamot it's marriage , not betrothal. But why after divorce? \"The only answer I have\": it follows the verse. Ki Teitzei: כי יקח איש אשה… וכתב לה ספר כריתת… ויצאה מביתו והלכה והיתה לאיש אחר . Kiddushin is learned from והיתה לאיש אחר , which comes after the get, and the Sages compare הויה ליציאה , marrying to leaving. A later commentator's reason (not the Rambam's), which the Rabbi \"finds funny\": מקדים רפואה למכה , Hashem sends the cure before the illness . \"Marriage can be beautiful, but it can also be a disaster. People wouldn't marry if they knew they couldn't get out. So put the divorce first: don't worry, you can get divorced. \" (Kiddushin = betrothal / engagement, the first stage; Ketubot = marriage itself.) Order 4: Nezikin, the First Half # Tractate About Why here 1 Bava Kamma Damages : what you or your property do (your ox , fire, pit , your hands; your car) \"A judge shouldn't judge until he has removed the things causing damage.\" Deal with the hazards first 2 Bava Metzia Deposits , claims, renting; movables Mishpatim: after the ox come the four kinds of guardian 3 Bava Batra Land , partnerships, the wording of contracts Mostly rabbinic logic , little scriptural source, so last of the three 4 Sanhedrin The judges They judge all the above 5 Makkot Lashes Administered by the judges 6 Shevuot Oaths Also administered by the judges 7 Eduyot Testimonies 8 Avodah Zarah Idolatry A judge must know exactly how each idol is worshipped : death applies only to worship in its own manner 9 Avot Next time 10 Horayot Mistaken rulings of the Sanhedrin and dayanim Is Makkot its own tractate? The Rambam says yes. One opinion in Tosafot : it's a continuation of Sanhedrin . That matters for a siyum , in the Nine Days to eat meat, or for firstborns on Erev Pesach : on that view, finishing Makkot isn't finishing a tractate. Summary Ruth on Shavuot: מואבי ולא מואבית . Without the Oral Torah, no David. Five categories in the Mishnah: received with/without support ( never disputed ); derived, fences, takkanot ( disputable ). Six orders: Seeds (food comes first) → Times → Women → Damages (all following Mishpatim ) → Holy things → Purities (Vayikra). Then tractate → chapter → halachah . Zera'im opens with Berachot (\"the best doctor starts with diet\"), then follows the gifts in the order they arise , from Pe'ah standing in the field to Challah in the dough, then fruit. Mo'ed: Shabbat; Pesach (first mitzvah); Torah order (Shekalim, Yom Kippur); remaining festivals; prophetic fasts, then Megillah ; Chagigah last, as it's men only . Nashim: Yevamot first, the one compulsory case ; Kiddushin last because the verse puts the get first (or: the cure before the illness ). Nezikin: remove the hazards first (Bava Kamma); then the judges and their tools; Avodah Zarah so judges know idolatry precisely. Coming Up After Shavuot (no shiur next week): why Avot sits in Nezikin , where the Rambam digresses at length on character, for everyone and for dayanim; Orders 5 and 6; and then the Sages' aggadah and derush , \"the Rambam has his unique interpretation.\"","XlB_J3dfrf4":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Rav Shilat's edition), Part 2 of the introduction Shiur: Part 2 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 13 May 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap The Rambam scatters principles of faith throughout his halachic works wherever he can. His four great introductions: the Mishnah (this series), Avot (done), Perek Helek , the Mishneh Torah . \"If you understand this introduction, your whole perspective on the Oral Law, the Talmud and the Mishnah changes. \" Part 1: every mitzvah came with its explanation , taught so that everyone heard it four times , over forty years , with a final 37-day review . The tradition is intact . So why so many disputes? Talmudic disputes are a different thing from later ones (Rishonim, Acharonim, or today's question of electricity on Shabbat ). The question here is: why can't you read a page of Mishnah or Gemara without he says this, he says that ? Why do we say Eizehu Mekoman every morning? Before Baruch She'amar we learn a little Torah (the Korbanot), a little Gemara ( Rabbi Yishmael ), and a little Mishnah. Of all the chapters on sacrifices, why this one? Because it's the only chapter in Shas with no dispute. No Rabbi Meir, no Rabbi Shimon; just straight halachah. Which shows how full of disputes everything else is. The Rambam's answer (Part 1): nothing received from Sinai is disputed. Disputes arise only where something wasn't given to Moshe and had to be derived by analysis and comparison ( iyun vehekesh ). Sukkah Two walls and a bit is enough Transmitted : no dispute Covered with sechach that can't contract impurity Transmitted : no dispute Minimum size Not transmitted → Beit Hillel vs Beit Shammai Whenever you see a dispute anywhere in Shas, it's something that was not handed down from Moshe. The introduction has eight parts . Part 1 is done; tonight, Part 2 , which \"I want to finish tonight.\" Part 2: A Prophet Has No Advantage in Interpreting Torah ודע כי הנבואה אינה מועילה בעיון פירושה של תורה ובהוצאת ענפים בשלש עשרה מידות. What Yehoshua and Pinchas could do, Ravina and Rav Ashi can do. Yehoshua and Shmuel are exactly equal to Ravina and Rav Ashi , the close of the Gemara 1,500 years ago, in how they derive law. Two prophets vs three Sages: follow the three , the majority. \"The whole premise of Torah is extrapolation , and that's done through intellect . Not prophecy, not something spiritual or secret. Using the wisdom Hashem gave us to bring out what we believe is the truth of Torah: that's what Torah is. \" \"These and these are the words of the living God\" The Maharal of Prague on אלו ואלו דברי אלהים חיים . People take it to mean \"everyone's right.\" But some questions have one objective answer . How many roofs did the Mishkan have? Tannaim disagree: three or four. \"If I went back in time and looked, there was only one answer.\" Say it was three. The one who said four is still divrei Elokim chayim , because his method of deriving it is a true method of Torah study , even if the answer is objectively wrong. The proof from Birkat HaTorah: we follow it with יברכך so the blessing falls immediately on Torah. But you could say the blessing and then learn opinions the halachah rejects , or ones objectively wrong, and you've still fulfilled the mitzvah , \"because the method of extrapolation was the true one. That's what Torah is. \" \"So what is special about a prophet?\" חי נפשי : it is one of the great principles on which our faith rests . But first the Rambam must explain the kinds of prophecy and how a prophet is verified , because many people err here, \"even special people,\" meaning Rav Saadia Gaon , who held that a prophet is verified by miracles . The Rambam: wrong . (From here the class switched to Rav Shilat's text , \"basically the most precise one.\") The Four Kinds of Prophet Claims to speak Message Status 1A In the name of an idol (\"the sun / a star / Baal told me…\") Serve it False: chenek (strangulation) 1B In Hashem's name Serve an idol False: chenek 2A In Hashem's name Serve Hashem, but adds, subtracts or reinterprets a mitzvah, attributing it to prophecy False: chenek 2B In Hashem's name Keep the Torah as given, plus specific instructions (go to war, don't) True, if verified 1A and 1B: idolatry והנביא ההוא או חלם החלום ההוא יומת (Re'eh). We ask him for no sign. Even if he performs miracles we've never heard of , he is put to death: כי מנסה ה׳ אלהיכם אתכם , Hashem is testing you. עדות השכל המכחיש טענתו חזקה מעדות העין הרואה מופתו The testimony of reason that refutes his claim is stronger than the testimony of the eye that saw his miracle , since it is plainly proven that only One may be served . \"This is why, when people tell you this rabbi did that, I saw him do this : miracles are lovely, sometimes needed, Eliyahu and Elisha did them. But they're no proof of who you are. \" The Kotzker Rebbe on the Maariv line אותות ומופתים באדמת בני חם : \" miracles and wonders \" belong \"in the land of the children of Ham\" . Plenty of people do miracles. It's irrelevant. 2A: changing the Torah \"because Hashem told me\" Examples: - Orlah: \"Hashem told me that from now on, wait four years, not three,\" or two . - Against tradition: \" An eye for an eye has always meant money; Hashem has now told me it means literally an eye.\" ויחס זה לנבואה , the key words: he attributes it to prophecy. He is a false prophet, put to death by chenek , because he ascribed to Hashem what He didn't say. Why? Moshe already told us: לא בשמים היא , it is not in heaven; and בפיך ובלבבך לעשותו : by your mouth (the verses we know) and your heart (our intellect, what we derive). Nothing else. The crucial distinction. A Sage sees people being careless with orlah, waiting only two and a half years, and says add a year as a safeguard . That's fine. That's what a Sage does , not a prophet: עשו סייג לתורה , ושמרתם את משמרתי . Avraham, the Gemara says, kept even the rabbinic laws. \"Just make clear it's not from prophecy .\" Poultry and milk: a prophet who says Hashem has now extended meat-and-milk to poultry is put to death . A rabbi who says Hashem forbids poultry and milk isn't claiming prophecy, but he still violates a prohibition (as the Rambam writes in the Mishneh Torah) by ascribing to Hashem what He didn't say. Once the Torah was given, it's ours to derive. \"Hashem gives prophets messages like should you go to war or not . He doesn't give messages on how to interpret Torah. \" 2B: the true prophet He calls people to keep the Torah, promising reward and warning of punishment, like Yeshayahu, Yirmeyahu, Yechezkel , and gives specific instructions ( fight this city on this day ; don't go to war today ). Once verified , ignoring him is מיתה בידי שמים , death at the hands of Heaven (Shoftim). But if he makes exactly these claims and fails verification , he's put to death , even though he only told people to do good. \" Being a prophet is a dodgy business. More likely than not you'll be put to death.\" How a Prophet Is Verified וכי תאמר בלבבך איכה נדע את הדבר אשר לא דברו ה׳… אשר ידבר הנביא בשם ה׳ ולא יהיה (Shoftim) If he predicts in Hashem's name and it doesn't happen, he's not a prophet. Note: the verse says only that a failed prediction disqualifies him, not that a fulfilled one qualifies him. So one or two hits leave him in doubt , until he is right every time , again and again (no number given). As with Shmuel : וידע כל ישראל מדן ועד באר שבע כי נאמן שמואל לנביא לה׳ . Mark: isn't there a chazakah of three? No. \"We don't find that. Three seems not to be enough.\" Why that's possible: people asked prophets everything In those days people went to the prophet for everything: Shaul went to Shmuel about lost donkeys . Why? Because the Torah forbids astrologers, diviners and fortune-tellers : כי הגוים האלה אשר אתה יורש אותם אל מעננים ואל קסמים ישמעו, ואתה לא כן נתן לך ה׳ אלהיך. נביא מקרבך מאחיך כמני יקים לך ה׳ אלהיך, אליו תשמעון. Prophet vs fortune-teller Fortune-teller / astrologer Prophet Predictions Sometimes right , \"necessarily\" Right in everything , small and great, always Why some are \"better\" ויתרון זה על זה במיעוט שקרי אחד משקרי זולתו : one is a better liar than the other One miss Expected; they don't claim otherwise He's false , and put to death: לא יפל מדבר ה׳ ארצה Traders: \"This is what good traders do. They know how the market's going to go, and that's how they become millionaires and billionaires. The really good ones are almost always right, but they're sometimes wrong. \" Bad news may be cancelled; good news may not Prophesies If it doesn't happen Disaster (famine, war) Not false : Hashem accepts teshuvah and relents ( ונחם על הרעה ). Yonah : forty days and Nineveh is overturned ; they repented, and it wasn't Specific good (no war this year, rain and plenty) False prophet , to be executed: בזדון דברו הנביא, לא תגור ממנו , don't fear him for his past predictions כל דבר שיצא מפי הקב״ה לטובה, אפילו על תנאי, אינו חוזר. Yirmeyahu vs the false prophets: he prophesied disaster ; they promised all would be well . \"I'm safe,\" the Rambam has him say: if bad news doesn't come, Hashem relented. But if your good news fails, you're false. Mark: so to be verified, you must be consistently right on positive predictions, since negatives can be \"explained away\"? Correct , or on negative ones that do happen. Unfulfilled bad predictions neither verify him nor diminish him. Then why was Yaakov afraid of Esav? Hashem had promised him: ואנכי עמך ושמרתיך בכל אשר תלך . The Sages: he feared his sins had cost him the promise. The Rambam: that can happen to an individual , for a personal promise. But a public prophecy, once spoken in Hashem's name, must come true. The Prophet's One Real Advantage Once verified, if he commands us to suspend any mitzvah, positive or negative, we must obey. Whoever disobeys is punished. Doesn't this contradict 2A? The class answered: temporarily , and it must lapse afterwards . בכל אם יאמר לך נביא עבור על דברי תורה שמע לו, חוץ מעבודה זרה. Three conditions: 1 Temporary ( hora'at sha'ah ), never permanent 2 Never idolatry , not even \"just for this hour\" 3 He makes clear it's a one-off. Asked are you overturning Moshe? , he says no, only now Examples Eliyahu on Mount Carmel offered a sacrifice outside the Temple , forbidden on pain of karet ( השמר לך פן תעלה עלתיך בכל מקום אשר תראה ). Asked by a beit din may we do this in future? , he'd say no, it's karet; I'm doing this once , to refute the prophets of Baal. Elisha in the war with Moav told them to cut down fruit trees , despite לא תשחית את עצה . Asked is this mitzvah annulled? : if he said forever , death; only now : obey him. The Rambam's striking example: Shabbat A verified prophet (say Eliyahu or Shmuel) stands in the square on Shabbat morning : everyone, men and women, light fires, take up arms, and go to war. Not a life-threatening emergency; just Hashem told me . We must get up at once, without delay, with alacrity and diligence , transgressing Shabbat, lighting fires, waging war, and expect reward from Hashem for doing so. אליו תשמעון. \"I'm an old man.\" One who considers himself pious, an elder \"with his shtreimel and bekishe on Shabbat,\" who has never broken Shabbat in his life , says: I don't dispute the prophet, but I won't go. Plenty of others can. He has transgressed Hashem's word and is liable to death at the hands of Heaven. The One who commanded Shabbat commanded us to obey the prophet : והיה האיש אשר לא ישמע אל דברי אשר ידבר בשמי, אנכי אדרש מעמו . But if, while lighting fires and readying weapons, someone ties a knot or does the washing , anything the prophet didn't command, he is liable to stoning . The prophet's instruction covers only what he said. And the next week, the same prophet says: Hashem told me the Shabbat boundary (techum) is now one amah less, 1,999 amot , attributing it to prophecy, not reinterpreting. He's a false prophet. We put him to death , \"that same prophet we all broke Shabbat for last week.\" ובזה לבדו נבדל הנביא משאר בני אדם בתורה In this alone does a prophet differ from everyone else regarding Torah. Even the greatest Sage or chief rabbi who says I feel Hashem wants us to break Shabbat today; I had a dream : forbidden , unless he's a verified prophet. In Interpretation: Just Another Sage But in explaining the laws and their fine points, he is like the other Sages. If he gives an interpretation and the Sages another, and he says Hashem told me mine is right : we don't listen to him. Even a thousand prophets, all like Eliyahu and Elisha, against a thousand and one Sages: אחרי רבים להטות. The halachah follows the 1,001 Sages, not the 1,000 great prophets. The Gemara: אילו אמרה לי יהושע בן נון בפומיה, לא צייתנא ליה : even if Yehoshua bin Nun said it himself, I wouldn't listen. There is no adding to the Torah by way of prophecy. A prophet who claims my interpretation is right because I'm a prophet is another kind of false prophet, put to death : שאין תורה אחרת תבוא מאת ה׳ . For understanding, we're never sent to prophets. The verse doesn't say go to the prophet , but ובאת אל הכהנים הלוים ואל השופט אשר יהיה בימים ההם : go to the Sage of your day. Summary Disputes arise only where nothing was handed down (e.g. the sukkah's minimum size). Eizehu Mekoman is the one dispute-free chapter. Prophecy gives no edge in interpretation. Yehoshua = Rav Ashi; follow the majority of Sages. אלו ואלו : a rejected view is still Torah, because its method is true . Four kinds of prophet: idol-prophets (1A, 1B), and the prophet who changes the Torah in Hashem's name (2A), are put to death , even with miracles. Reason outweighs the eye. A Sage may add a safeguard ; a prophet may not attribute a change to prophecy. Verification: every prediction must come true, small and great. Fortune-tellers are \"better liars\"; one miss makes a false prophet. Bad news may be cancelled by teshuvah; good news never. The prophet's one advantage: a verified prophet may order a temporary , non-idolatrous , explicitly one-off suspension of a mitzvah (Eliyahu at Carmel, Elisha's trees, war on Shabbat ), and we must obey. But one amah off the techum \"from Hashem,\" and he's false. In interpreting Torah he's just another Sage: 1,001 Sages outvote 1,000 prophets. Coming Up Next week: how the oral transmission continued, and how it became the written Mishnah and Talmud .","bwoDjkPI6hg":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to the Commentary on the Mishnah (Hebrew translation of Rav Yehudah al-Harizi), opening section Shiur: Part 1 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 6 May 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Where This Series Fits Over the past four or five years the class has studied many of the Rambam's works: Studied Hilchot Talmud Torah Laws of Torah study Hilchot Deot Character traits Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah Foundations of the Torah The 13 Principles of Faith Shemonah Perakim The introduction to Avot, winter 2024 Hilchot Teshuvah Recently The Letters The past few months Now: the Rambam's introductions to parts of the Talmud, \"certainly until Rosh Hashanah, likely past that, because there's a lot to get through.\" The Rambam's three great works Work Kind Mishneh Torah Halachic: the 14-book codification of the whole Torah Commentary on the Mishnah Halachic Moreh Nevuchim Philosophical : his magnum opus in that field \"More precious to me than everything I teach\" In the Letter on Resurrection the Rambam describes great scholars, steeped in halachah from youth, who don't know the basic philosophical principles of the Torah. One even doubted whether Hashem has a body. So whenever his halachic writing gives an opening, he digresses into faith . At the end of his commentary on Berachot he explains: ואין זה מקום להביא שם הדבר הזה, לולי אשר כוונתי כשיבוא זכר דבר בעניין האמונה לבאר בו מעט, כי יקר בעיני ללמד עיקר מעיקרי הדת ואמונה יותר מכל אשר אלמדהו. This isn't the place for it, but my purpose, whenever something touches on faith, is to explain a little, because teaching one principle of faith is more precious to me than anything else I teach. The same habit shows in the Mishneh Torah, which often ends a section with an idea to think about. The four great \"digressions\" Where What it covers 1 Introduction to Avot ( Shemonah Perakim ) Character and basic philosophy: already studied 2 Introduction to Chapter 10 of Sanhedrin ( Perek Helek ) כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא : what earns and what forfeits Olam HaBa, and the 13 Principles 3 Introduction to the Mishneh Torah Mainly the transmission of Torah 4 Introduction to the Mishnah The whole concept of Rabbinic law : this series The plan: the Introduction to the Mishnah over May, June and early July ; then the short Introduction to the Mishneh Torah , which links neatly; then, after Sukkot, through the winter, Perek Helek and the principles of faith. Why this matters so much The Introduction to the Mishnah answers the questions we usually take for granted: - How do we know there is an Oral Torah? - Why should we trust what the Sages said? - What is the status of the Talmud ? - Why do the Sages argue so much , all the way through the Mishnah and Talmud? (Intellectual, halachic disputes, \"not the political kind\") - What are aggadah and midrash ? - What are a Mishnah, baraita, Tosefta, Talmud ? \"I promise you, if we study this properly, you'll come out with such a clear understanding of the Oral Law, of rabbinic arguments, of midrash. If I had ten things my son must do before he goes to yeshivah, this would be one of them. You can't go to yeshivah and not know this. Unfortunately I only learned it much later in life, and so many questions I had in yeshivah would have been answered.\" About the text The Commentary on the Mishnah was the Rambam's first Jewish work, written between about 20 and 23 , and corrected throughout his life (we see this in the manuscripts). \"Remarkable, when you think what he writes here, at such a young age.\" Written in Arabic , so the masses could understand it; translated into Hebrew by different people for different parts. This introduction was translated by Rav Yehudah al-Harizi , the text used in class. (\"Not the best text. Next week we'll use a different one.\") Part 1: Every Mitzvah Came With Its Explanation דע כי כל מצוה שנתן הקדוש ברוך הוא למשה רבינו עליו השלום, נתנה לו בפירושה. Know that every mitzvah Hashem gave Moshe was given with its explanation : He would tell him the mitzvah, and then its explanation and meaning. How Moshe taught each mitzvah (from the Gemara, Eruvin 54b) Step Who comes in Where they sit 1 Aharon alone; Moshe teaches him the mitzvah and its explanation Aharon sits at Moshe's right 2 Elazar and Itamar , Aharon's sons (Nadav and Avihu have died) One at Moshe's left , one at Aharon's right 3 The 70 elders 4 The whole people , \"anyone who sought Hashem\" Then the teaching passes down the line: Teacher Teaches Moshe (leaves after teaching four times) Aharon → sons → elders → people Aharon (leaves after teaching once) Repeats it to all Elazar and Itamar Repeat it to the elders and the people The elders Repeat it to the people The result: everyone hears it four times. From Moshe From Aharon From his sons From the elders Total Aharon 4 4 Elazar & Itamar 3 1 4 The elders 2 1 1 4 The people 1 1 1 1 4 Then the people teach one another , write the mitzvah itself in scrolls , and go around until everyone can read it, and learn its explanation , which is the tradition ( kabbalah ). Why the Rambam spells this out He is building a picture of a yeshivah : the rosh yeshivah gives a shiur, the students analyse it, discuss it, go back and ask. Teaching passes from one to the next, and people discuss and pass it on . \"Four times\": where have we seen that? In the Letter on Martyrdom , mocking the rabbi who answered without thinking: anything said in public should be examined at least four times , and anything written, \"theoretically a thousand.\" For the Rambam, you haven't properly learned something until you've learned it four times . Written scroll by scroll The Gemara debates whether the Torah was written as one book at the end of forty years or מגילה מגילה , scroll by scroll. The Rambam clearly holds scroll by scroll : each mitzvah was written when given, discussed, and then the next came. \"The whole Torah was given at Sinai\" : it's \"not precise\" to imagine Moshe received all 613 mitzvot and their explanation on Shavuot. On Shavuot he received the Ten Commandments ; over the next forty days, Mishpatim ; then he taught, went back for more. Pesach Sheni , the daughters of Tzelofchad , the laws in Bamidbar came later. \"A process of forty years.\" (The Rabbi has a separate pre-Shavuot shiur on what \"given at Sinai\" means, with three or four explanations.) Part 2: The Example of Sukkah The Baraita (Sifra, Behar): מה ענין שמיטה אצל הר סיני? והלא כל התורה כולה נאמרה מסיני! אלא מה שמיטה נאמרה כללותיה ופרטותיה ודקדוקיה מסיני, אף כולן נאמרו כללותיהן ודקדוקיהן מסיני. Why mention Sinai with Shemittah, when the whole Torah was said at Sinai? To teach that just as Shemittah's principles, details and fine points came from Sinai, so did all the mitzvot. (\"By the end of this introduction you'll know exactly what a Mishnah, baraita, Tosefta and Talmud are.\") What the verse says, and what Moshe was told בסכת תשבו שבעת ימים : \"In sukkot you shall dwell seven days.\" Four words. What is a sukkah? What does \"dwell\" mean: sit, live, sleep, build my bathroom there? What does \"seven days\" mean? Minimum size, maximum, height, materials? Told to Moshe orally It applies to men, not women The sick and travellers are exempt The covering ( sechach ) must be something that grows from the ground Not wool , not silk Not utensils , even if from plants: pillows, cushions, garments Eating, drinking and sleeping there is the whole obligation Minimum 7 × 7 tefachim At least 10 tefachim high And so with all 613 mitzvot: the mitzvot in writing, their explanation by mouth. You can't read the Torah without it וקשרתם לאות על ידך והיו לטטפת בין עיניך : tie what , where on the hand (\"the hand's a big place\"), what are totafot ? לא תעשה כל מלאכה : \"Your definition of work is different from mine. Some people will tell you going to the gym is melachah . \" Moshe was told there are 39 forbidden labours , and taught it. Part 3: The Last Thirty-Seven Days In the fortieth year, on Rosh Chodesh Shevat , Moshe gathered the people: \"My time to die has come. Whoever heard a halachah and forgot it, let him come and I'll repeat it. Whoever has a question, let him come and ask.\" הואיל משה באר את התורה הזאת . The Sifrei: כל מי ששכח הלכה אחת יבוא וישנה, וכל מי שצריך פירוש יבוא ויתפרש. From Rosh Chodesh Shevat to 7 Adar , 37 days , Moshe did nothing but review and clarify. Then, at midday on 7 Adar ( בעצם היום הזה ), he went up the mountain. His death was \"for us\": we lost him. משה רבנו לא מת אלא עלה ומשמש במרום : Moshe didn't die; he ascended and serves above. \"These matters are very long, and not needed here.\" Why stress all this? The Rambam is painting an intact transmission : forty years of mitzvot, each taught four times , discussed and written, then a final chance to review everything. Part 4: Where Do Disputes Come From? After Moshe died, Yehoshua and his generation studied what he had left. כל מה שקבל משה... לא תהיה עליו מחלוקת. Whatever was received from Moshe: there is no dispute about it. Whatever they had not heard from the Prophet , the branches of those roots, they derived by reasoning , through the thirteen principles given at Sinai . Some of these rulings were agreed ; on some a dispute arose : this one says so, that one says so . When a dispute arose, they followed the majority : אחרי רבים להטות . The question If the Torah was handed down with such care, taught four times and reviewed at the end, why is there so much argument? The Rambam's answer, \"so crucial, and different from how others understood it\" Don't make the terrible mistake of thinking disputes arose from \"Chinese whispers\" : that as time passed, people garbled what was transmitted. \"He goes very strongly against this.\" (More in a couple of weeks.) Roots : given by Hashem to Moshe Branches : derived later Transmission Moshe → Yehoshua → elders → prophets… ( Avot 1:1 ) Worked out by the Sages, and long before the Talmud, even in the prophets' time Dispute? Never Possible Sukkah Eat, drink and sleep in it; the sick are exempt. \"No one argues about that\" (sleeping in the cold is \"a medieval argument,\" much later) E.g. how high a sukkah may be, if that wasn't given Four species פרי עץ הדר = etrog . \"No argument\" How the branches are derived: the thirteen principles of Rabbi Yishmael ( בשלש עשרה מדות התורה נדרשת ), said every morning in Shacharit, \"because the whole Oral Law is based on this .\" (Strictly there are 32 ; these are the main 13.) Different principles can lead to different conclusions , and that's where dispute comes from. \"We'll see why it intensified later.\" Organic growth. \"Branches grow organically from the roots. An extrapolation can't contradict the roots. \" Recent movements that \"took the roots and reinterpreted them\": \"it doesn't work like that. The roots, given from Hashem to Moshe, cannot be changed .\" Coming later: if everything goes back to Sinai, what is the special category הלכה למשה מסיני ? \"We'll see.\" Question from the class If Israel hadn't sinned with the Golden Calf and the spies, they'd have entered the Land after a few months. Wouldn't Moshe then have received everything at Sinai? \"No. Moshe would simply have continued : he'd have entered the Land, and teaching would have gone on for years more. It's 100% clear Moshe didn't get the whole Torah on Shavuot. Pesach Sheni, Tzelofchad, Rosh Chodesh : just look at the Torah. It was an ongoing process. \" Part 5: A Prophet Has No Edge in Interpretation The Rambam now takes issue with Rav Saadia Gaon (\"those on Monday nights know his Emunot veDeot \"). ודע שהנבואה אינה מועילה בפירוש התורה ובהוצאת ענפי המצות בשלש עשרה מדות. Prophecy gives no advantage in interpreting the Torah or deriving the branches through the thirteen principles. What Yehoshua and Pinchas could do by study and reasoning, Ravina and Rav Ashi can do. \"Ravina and Rav Ashi, a thousand or more years later, far below Yehoshua and Pinchas in prophecy: in extrapolation they're equal. Don't tell me I'm a prophet, so my interpretation is better .\" So what is a prophet's advantage? \" חי נפשי , by my life, so much depends on this principle.\" But first he must explain the kinds of prophecy. Miracles don't prove a prophet Most people get this wrong, even some important ones , imagining that prophecy is established only by a wondrous sign : like Moshe's, like Eliyahu reviving the widow's son , like Elisha's miracles. That's Rav Saadia Gaon ( Emunot veDeot , essay 3): if he can't do miracles, he's not a prophet. The Rambam: \"No, no, no, no. It's not true.\" Eliyahu and Elisha didn't perform miracles to prove their prophecy, which was already established . They did them because the situation needed it (to revive a child), and Hashem fulfils the wishes of the righteous. Prophecy is proven by something else , which depends on dividing prophecy into two kinds: next week . Summary The series: the Rambam's introductions, starting with the Introduction to the Mishnah : the Oral Law, its transmission, the Sages' authority, why they argue, aggadah and midrash. \"One of ten things before yeshivah.\" The Rambam digresses into faith wherever he can: \"more precious to me than anything else I teach.\" His four great digressions: Avot, Perek Helek, Mishneh Torah, Mishnah . Every mitzvah came with its explanation. Moshe taught each one so that everyone heard it four times ; they discussed it, wrote it scroll by scroll , and passed it on. Sukkah shows how little the verse says alone. The Torah came over forty years , not in one day at Sinai, and in the last 37 days Moshe reviewed everything. What came from Sinai is never disputed. Disputes arise only in the branches , derived through the 13 principles , and were settled by majority . Not \"Chinese whispers.\" Branches must grow from the roots, never against them. Prophecy gives no edge in interpretation : Ravina and Rav Ashi = Yehoshua and Pinchas . Miracles don't prove a prophet , against Rav Saadia Gaon . Coming Up Next week: the two kinds of prophecy , how a prophet is verified, and what a prophet's real advantage is , \" חי נפשי , so much depends on it.\"","w0lmqAt7qjE":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: the concluding paragraph on the Thirteen Principles, with Hilchot Issurei Bi'ah, Hilchot Teshuvah, Hilchot Mamrim, the Shulchan Aruch, the Chazon Ish and Rav Kook Shiur: Part 19 (the final shiur) of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 10 March 2026 (after Purim) These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Where We Are We began after Sukkot : the Rambam on Olam HaBa and the afterlife, then, \"since this Mishnah discusses Olam HaBa,\" the principles needed for a portion in it . Principles Hashem 1 exists, 2 one, 3 no body, 4 first, before all, 5 serve nothing else Revelation 6 prophecy, 7 Moshe the greatest prophet, 8 Torah from heaven, 9 it won't change Consequences 10 Hashem knows our deeds and thoughts, 11 reward and punishment, 12 Mashiach, 13 Techiyat HaMetim (simply understood: physical ) Tonight: the Rambam's final paragraph. \" An incredible statement. \" Part 1: The Text וכאשר יאמין האדם אלה היסודות כולם, ונתבררה אמונתו בהם, הוא נכנס בכלל ישראל, ומצוה לאהבו ולרחם עליו ולנהוג עמו בכל מה שצוה השם יתברך איש לאחיו מן האהבה והאחוה. ואפילו עשה מה שיכול מן העבירות מחמת התאוה ותגבורת הטבע הגרוע, הוא נענש כפי חטאיו, ויש לו חלק לעולם הבא, והוא מפושעי ישראל. A believer in all thirteen Status Within Klal Yisrael , once his faith is clear to him, \" not just we learnt it \" Our duty Love him, have compassion (help him, charity when he needs it ), and every interpersonal mitzvah of brotherhood Even if He commits every sin he can, from desire and the overpowering of his lower nature: \" eats non-kosher, even eats on Yom Kippur ; he wants to eat and finds it hard\" Then Punished for his sins , but he has a portion in Olam HaBa : one of the sinners of Israel \" Hate the sin, love the sinner. He'll have to reconcile what he's done , but if he's going through a hard time , we have a mitzvah from the Torah to help him .\" וכשיפקפק אדם ביסוד מאלו היסודות, הרי יצא מן הכלל, וכפר בעיקר, ונקרא מין ואפיקורוס, וקוצץ בנטיעות, ומצוה לשנאתו ולאבדו, ועליו נאמר: הלא משנאיך ה׳ אשנא. One who rejects any one principle Status Has left the community ; denied a fundamental ; called min and apikoros ; \"cuts the plantings\" Our duty \"A mitzvah to hate him and destroy him.\" \" That's what the Rambam says. I'm not going to dilute his words. \" Verse הלא משנאיך ה׳ אשנא : an enemy of Hashem is one who denies a principle The closing lines והארכתי בדברים הרבה ויצאתי מענין חבורי, אבל עשיתי זאת לפי שראיתי בו תועלת באמונה, לפי שאספתי בו דברים מועילים מפוזרים מספרים גדולים. ולכן דע אותם והצלח בהם, וחזור עליהם פעמים רבות, והתבונן בהם התבוננות יפה. ואם ישיאך לבך ותחשוב שהבנת ענינם מפעם אחת או מעשרה, ה׳ יודע שהשיאך על שקר. ולכן אל תמהר בקריאתו, כי אני לא חברתיו כפי מה שנזדמן לי, אלא אחר עיון גדול והתבוננות, ואחר שראיתי דעות ברורות ואמיתיות… וידעתי מה שראוי להאמין ממנו, והבאתי ראיה בטענות ובראיות על כל ענין וענין. ה׳ יתברך ינחני בדרך הטובה. ואחזור לפרק. \" I went on at length, beyond my purpose , because it benefits faith : I gathered useful things scattered across great books .\" \" Know them, go over them many times, reflect deeply. If your heart tells you you've understood after once, or ten times, Hashem knows it's deceived you. Don't rush. \" \" That's why we went through each principle properly, in detail. \" \" I wrote this only after great study and reflection , weighing true views against false, with proof for each .\" \" May Hashem guide me on the good path. And I return to the chapter \": back to the Mishnah. Part 2: Not Knowing vs Rejecting The convert (Hilchot Issurei Bi'ah 14:2) When a prospective convert accepts that it's hard and still wants it: מודיעין אותו עיקרי הדת, שהוא ייחוד השם ואיסור עבודה זרה, ומאריכין בדבר זה , plus some mitzvot . That's it. \" He needn't know about Mashiach or resurrection , only Principles 1, 2 and 5 , at length. Presumably he'll learn the rest once he's Jewish. But didn't the Rambam just say that without the thirteen you're not part of Klal Yisrael ? \" The answer: the Rambam's word is ונתקלקל / שיפקפק : active rejection . Status \"I don't know there's a Mashiach, a resurrection, reward and punishment; I wasn't taught\" Part of Am Yisrael. \"Of course you should know it, but you haven't rejected it .\" Taught, and rejects it : there's no Mashiach, no resurrection Kofer The exception: Principle 3 Ignorance is rejection. \"Even if you were taught God has a body and sits on a throne in heaven , you're a heretic,\" and the Rambam is sharp on this in the Moreh . It's the opposite of the truth. Point one: we're talking about active rejection. Heresy vs karet sins Hilchot Teshuvah 3 lists 24 with no portion in Olam HaBa: about half are the principles; the rest are other sins, e.g. machti'ei harabim , causing the public to sin ( Yerovam ben Nevat set up idols publicly; or deliberately teaching people the wrong thing ). Karet sinner (e.g. machti harabim ) Apikoros Olam HaBa (without teshuvah) None None Am Yisrael Still part of it Not part of it Our duty Still love him Distance : \"a mitzvah to hate and destroy\" Minyan Counts Doesn't count : \"I'm not talking about someone who just doesn't know what Mashiach is, but one who says in shul I don't believe the Torah is from heaven \" Shechitah Kosher Treif , even if he knows all the laws and does it precisely \" That is the gravity of this paragraph. \" \" Unfortunately we know people who don't accept all thirteen. How do we relate to them? Do we take the Rambam's words, distance, hate, push away ? Or is there another understanding, one the Rambam would agree with? \" Six points. Part 3: Six Considerations 1. Who is an apikoros ? That an apikoros has no portion in Olam HaBa is a Mishnah. Accepted. But the Gemara (Sanhedrin 99b) defines him differently: Opinion Apikoros = One מבזה תלמיד חכם : disparages a Torah scholar Other מבזה חברו בפני תלמיד חכם : disparages his fellow before a scholar The Rambam (Hilchot Teshuvah 3:8) instead defines him as one who denies prophecy, or Moshe's . The Beit Yosef , Kesef Mishneh and Vilna Gaon ask: ומכל מקום קשה לי, דאמרינן מאן אפיקורוס… צריך טעם למה השמיטה רבנו וכתב במקומה מה שכתב. The Me'iri (a difficult answer): disparage the Sages and you'll come to deny everything : they're the guardians of the Torah, of prophecy, of revelation . \" My point: already in the Gemara there's a discussion of what an apikoros is.\" And not everyone counts thirteen: Albo's Ikkarim has three ; Crescas ( Or Hashem ) and the Radbaz disagree too. Principle 3 again: the Ra'avad: והלא גדולים וטובים ממנו : greater and better men (some Ba'alei Tosafot ) believed in God's corporeality . \" Are they all kofrim ? Maybe the Rambam would say so. But you first have to define an apikoros , and it's not always clear. \" But some principles everyone accepts : belief in God , one God, not two . On that, the Gemara says to kill him. So what then? 2. The Chazon Ish: \"only when Providence was revealed\" Shulchan Aruch, Yoreh De'ah 158:2: האפיקורסים, והם הכופרים בתורה ובנבואה מישראל, מצוה להרגן; אם יש בידו להרגן בסייף בפרהסיא, הורגו; ואם לאו, היה מסבב בסיבות עד שיסבב הריגתו. כיצד? ראה אחד מהם שנפל לבאר והסולם בבאר, קודם ומסלקו ואומר: הריני טרוד להוריד בני מן הגג ואחזירנו לך. If you can, openly ; if not ( the law of the land ), indirectly : he's fallen in a well with a ladder there; take the ladder : \"I'm busy getting my son down from the roof, I'll bring it back.\" \" Unbelievable. From the Gemara , in the Rambam , and the Shulchan Aruch, only 500 years ago .\" The Chazon Ish (R. Avraham Yeshayahu Karelitz; late 1800s to mid-1950s; begun ~1911, written before the Holocaust), Yoreh De'ah 2:16 : ונראה דאין דין מורידין אלא בזמן שהשגחתו יתברך גלויה, כמו בזמן שהיו נסים מצויים ומשמש בת קול, וצדיקי הדור תחת השגחה פרטית הנראית לכל עין… ואז היה ביעור רשעים גדרו של עולם, שהכל ידעו כי הדחת הדור מביא פורענות לעולם… אבל בזמן ההעלם, שנכרתה האמונה מדלת העם, אין במעשה הורדה גדר הפרצה אלא הוספה בפרצה, שיהיה בעיניהם כמעשה השחתה ואלמות ח״ו. וכיון שכל עצמנו לתקן, אין הדין נוהג בשעה שאין בו תיקון. ועלינו להחזירם בעבותות אהבה ולהעמידם בקרן אורה במה שידינו מגעת. When Providence is revealed (miracles, bat kol , visible hashgachah pratit ) In a time of concealment Removing the wicked fixes the world : everyone sees leading the generation astray brings calamity Even simple people struggle with faith; acting would widen the breach : people would see it as destruction and violence : \" look how religious people behave \" The law applies The law exists to repair, so where it doesn't repair, it doesn't apply \"We must bring them back with cords of love, and raise them up in a ray of light, as far as we can.\" The Rabbi's comments: 1. \" The Chazon Ish isn't some liberal rabbi : revered in the Charedi world, the posek hador for Ashkenazim of the last generation. And he wrote it before the Holocaust: al achat kamah vekamah after it. \" 2. \" Very creative. Was there revealed providence in the 1500s , when the Shulchan Aruch codified it? The 1100s , for the Rambam? Nothing drastically changed between the 1500s and the 1900s. The Rambam records non-practical laws, but the Shulchan Aruch only records practical halachah , nothing from Temple times. And yet the Chazon Ish says it no longer applies. What's changed? I'll leave you to work that out. \" 3. \" He could have stopped at it doesn't apply . He doesn't: we must bring them back with cords of love . \" A parallel: suicide. The Gemara: no mourning , not buried in a Jewish cemetery : \"quite severe.\" Today everyone who takes their own life is buried in kever Yisrael . \" I've had the very unfortunate experience of a levayah for a teenager who took his own life. \" The poskim of the last 50–100 years: such a person is essentially mentally ill . \" Lovely, and 100% great that our poskim say this, but it begs the question: when does the original halachah apply? Maybe things have changed. \" 3. Tinok shenishba (Hilchot Mamrim 3:3) במה דברים אמורים? באיש שכפר בתורה שבעל פה במחשבתו ובדברים שנראו לו, והלך אחר דעתו הקלה ואחר שרירות לבו, וכופר בתורה שבעל פה תחילה, כצדוק ובייתוס וכל התועים אחריהם. אבל בני התועים האלה ובני בניהם, שהדיחו אותם אבותם ונולדו במינות וגדלו אותן עליו, הרי הן כתינוק שנשבה לבין הגויים וגדלוהו הגויים על דתם, שהוא אנוס… ואף על פי ששמע אחר כך שהיה יהודי וראה היהודים ודתם, הרי הוא כאנוס, שהרי גדלוהו על טעותם. כך אלו שאמרנו, האוחזים בדרכי אבותם הקראים שטעו. לפיכך ראוי להחזירן בתשובה ולמשוך אותם בדברי שלום עד שיחזרו לאיתן התורה. Ones , duress: under duress even adultery or murder , though wrong, isn't punished by death . \" We learnt this in the Rambam's letters. \" The Rambam extends ones dramatically: born and raised among heretics (his case: the Karaites ) = compelled , like a child captured and raised by gentiles ( tinok shenishba , a concept in the Gemara; Jewish children taken by Christians and raised Christian). \" For his time, quite groundbreaking. So everyone raised in non-religious homes, Reform or Conservative homes , and I'd go further: even the rabbis of those movements, if that's how they were raised : the Rambam would perhaps say they're tinok shenishba . Not only no mitzvah to hate them: a mitzvah to love them and bring them back. \" 4. Intellectual ones (the Rabbi's own suggestion) \" My own idea. I don't know how true it is, I have no proof, but I believe it. \" The Rambam limits it to one who went after דעתו הקלה ושרירות לבו : his shallow opinion and the caprice of his heart . So perhaps there's an intellectual ones : someone who thought it through seriously and was intellectually compelled isn't that person. 5. Emotional ones : Rav Kook ( Iggerot HaRe'iyah I, letter 138, 5678/1918) Background (the testimony laws): a public Shabbat desecrator can't testify . Yet one suspected of arayot (adultery) can , because יצרו תקפו: his inclination overpowered him . \" It's terrible, but deep down he's a good person , so we don't suspect him of lying in court .\" A father wrote to Rav Kook: his son had left religion. - \" I understand why you're sad. But if you think, as many do, that the right thing is to abandon those who left Torah and faith through the storm of the times ( על ידי זרם הזמן הסוער ), הנני אומר בפה מלא שלא זו הדרך שה׳ חפץ בה : I say outright: that is not the way Hashem wants. \" - As one suspected of arayot is anus because his inclination overpowered him, so the current of the age is like a wicked maidservant , given great power , enticing our young people with all her charms to follow her. הם אנוסים גמורים, וחלילה לנו לדון אונס כרצון : they are under complete duress; God forbid we judge duress as free will. - The inner Jewish soul is still in their hearts ; don't compare them to the wicked . When times change they'll return. - His advice: bring him near , explain as you can, and above all let him keep a love for the nation , Jewish at least culturally. \" Radical too. Our youth, the jaded , are enticed by the hedonistic culture we live in . Emotional ones : bring them back through love , and intellectual discourse .\" 6. No proper rebuke Some Rishonim: one who hasn't been rebuked , told clearly and unequivocally , is still an anus . Arachin 16b: Rabbi Tarfon: תמהני אם יש בדור הזה מי שמקבל תוכחה : I'd be surprised if anyone in this generation can accept rebuke . Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah: תמהני אם יש בדור הזה שיודע להוכיח : I'd be surprised if anyone knows how to give it . \" If you're only liable once properly rebuked, and almost no one knows how to rebuke… something to think about. \" Conclusion # Consideration 1 Not everyone accepts the Rambam's definition of apikoros (the Gemara; Albo, Crescas, Radbaz; the Ra'avad on corporeality) 2 The Chazon Ish: the law applied only when Providence was revealed ; today, cords of love 3 The Rambam's own tinok shenishba : raised in a home with the wrong beliefs 4 Intellectual ones 5 Rav Kook's emotional ones : the storm of the age 6 No one properly rebuked , and no one who knows how to rebuke \" Yes, we must accept the Rambam's principles, teach them and go over them, as he told us: not once, not ten times, all the time. And one who rejects them, we must distance. But I'd say even the Rambam, today, on the basis of one of these six, would say: even clear and absolute kofrim , we still have a duty to try to bring them back, to love them, to help them, to be there for them, and to teach them the real, authentic Torah. \" Summary Believe all thirteen and you're within Klal Yisrael , owed love and help , even if you sin greatly from desire: a sinner of Israel, with a portion in Olam HaBa . Reject one and you've left the community : min , *apikoros , \"a mitzvah to hate and destroy,\" no minyan, treif shechitah: graver than karet sins *. \"Go over them many times; don't think once or ten times is enough.\" Only active rejection counts : converts learn only one God and no idolatry ; except corporeality , where ignorance itself is heresy . Six reasons for love today: definition , the Chazon Ish , tinok shenishba , intellectual ones , Rav Kook's emotional ones , no proper rebuke . Coming Up The end of the series. \" Baruch Hashem, this past year we've learnt the Rambam's Introduction to the Mishnah, his Introduction to Perek Chelek, and his Introduction to Mishneh Torah. \" No shiur next Tuesday ; in two weeks, the week before Pesach, we begin the Moreh Nevuchim, the Guide for the Perplexed .","nFMIR2_7txM":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 13 ( Techiyat HaMetim ), with the Rambam's Ma'amar Techiyat HaMetim , Mishneh Torah, the Ra'avad, and the other Rishonim Shiur: Part 18 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 24 February 2026 (the week before Purim) These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. The shiur was dedicated לעילוי נשמת יצחק דניאל בן משה זאב. \"All the Torah we learn, and all the Torah disseminated through the technology being used: may it be for the elevation of his soul. מן השמים תנוחמו.\" Schedule Next week is Purim: no shiur. In two weeks: one final shiur on the Rambam's concluding paragraph : what follows if one denies any of the principles, and how that applies today. Then the Moreh. Part 1: The Text: Two Words היסוד השלשה עשר: תחיית המתים. וכבר בארנוה. The thirteenth principle: the resurrection of the dead. We have already explained it. \" Full stop. That's the end. \" \"Already explained\" : earlier in the Introduction (we learnt it around November , before the winter break), where, discussing Olam HaBa , the Rambam also covers Mashiach, Gan Eden, Gehinnom and Techiyat HaMetim : ותחיית המתים יסוד מיסודות תורת משה רבנו עליו השלום, ואין דת ולא דבקות בדת היהודית למי שלא יאמין זה. אבל היא לצדיקים… Resurrection is a foundation of Moshe's Torah : no part in the Jewish religion for one who doesn't believe it. But it's for the righteous. ודע כי האדם יש לו למות בהכרח, ויפרד וישוב למה שהורכב ממנו. Know that a person must die, separating and returning to what he was composed of. \" Perhaps the most illuminating sentence. We're made from the dust . The righteous will rise again in their bodies, and then die again , and then go to Olam HaBa , which for the Rambam is purely spiritual .\" Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal Text Ani Ma'amin שתהיה תחיית המתים בעת שיעלה רצון מאת הבורא, יתברך שמו ויתעלה זכרו לעד ולנצח נצחים Yigdal מתים יחיה אל ברוב חסדו, ברוך עדי עד שם תהלתו \" Basically the same; nothing new. \" Part 2: The Sources In Chazal and the liturgy, everywhere : the second berachah of the Amidah , three times a day. In the Torah, nothing explicit , though many hints; in Nevi'im and Ketuvim, explicit . Source Verse Reading Ha'azinu (Devarim 32:39) ראו עתה כי אני אני הוא ואין אלהים עמדי, אני אמית ואחיה, מחצתי ואני ארפא \"I strike and I heal\" is the same person , so \"I kill and I bring to life\" is too. \" The order seems wrong: it should be I give life and I kill . \" ( Rav Saadia brings this) Yeshayahu 26:19 יחיו מתיך נבלתי יקומון, הקיצו ורננו שכני עפר Your dead will live; dwellers in the dust, awake and sing Daniel 12:2 ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו, אלה לחיי עולם Many who sleep in the dust will awake , some to everlasting life Not reincarnation Resurrection = the dead come alive again . Not gilgul , a soul returning in a new body : \" clearly the Rambam didn't believe in it ; it doesn't fit anywhere in his framework and he never discusses it . Nor did Rav Saadia. There's no traditional source for it. \" A different topic. Part 3: Why So Brief? \" The question that bothered many since medieval times. \" Work What the Rambam says about resurrection Commentary on the Mishnah (this) Two words : \"already explained\" Mishneh Torah Focuses on Olam HaBa and the immortal soul . One mention , in Hilchot Teshuvah 3: no portion for הכופרים בתחיית המתים ובביאת הגואל Moreh Nevuchim Nowhere discussed philosophically ; one incidental reference in another context In his lifetime the Rambam was accused of not believing in physical resurrection. The Ra'avad The Rambam (Hilchot Teshuvah 8:2): העולם הבא אין בו גוף וגויה… אין בו לא אכילה ולא שתיה… ולא תשמיש המטה, אלא צדיקים יושבין ועטרותיהן בראשיהן ונהנין מזיו השכינה. No body, no eating or drinking: the righteous sit with crowns on their heads, enjoying the radiance of the Shechinah. The Ra'avad (R. Avraham ben David, \" his harshest critic \"): אמר אברהם: דברי האיש הזה בעיני קרובים למי שאומר אין תחיית המתים לגופות אלא לנשמות בלבד. \"This man's words seem to me close to one who says resurrection is for souls only, not bodies.\" בחיי ראשי, לא היה זה דעת חכמים : Chazal say the righteous rise in their clothes ; they rise with their blemishes (a broken leg, a broken hip) and are then healed . וכל אלה מוכיחים כי בגויותיהם עומדים בחיים . \" That's essentially the question: did the Rambam believe in a physical resurrection? What is the thirteenth principle? \" \" If we didn't have his letter, people would have a point. \" Part 4: Ma'amar Techiyat HaMetim \"Last year we did a series on the Rambam's letters, martyrdom and others. The major one we didn't do was the letter on resurrection.\" Why he wrote it The Mishneh Torah had spread to many lands. A certain chacham (\" from Syria, I think \") cited it to deny physical resurrection : only the soul is immortal . At first he ignored it : \" people always misunderstand whatever you write .\" Then in 1189 a letter from Yemen : many were denying physical resurrection because he'd written so little. So he wrote the essay. \"However clear you are, people twist your words\" שמע ישראל ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד. Ask any Jew: God is one. The Christians of his day: three names, Hashem, Elokeinu, Hashem : Father, Son and Holy Ghost ; echad : the Trinity is one . \" A verse so clear, so explicit, so unambiguous , and they made the unity of God into the Trinity . What do you want me to do if people misunderstand me ?\" His defence \" It's not true. I wrote it in my Commentary: it's one of the Thirteen Principles. I was brief \" ( he concedes it ). There will be a physical resurrection. Why so terse? A. Brevity is my style. B. There's little to say : it can't be defended by philosophical investigation ; it's a matter of belief . C. It's not the main thing. The main thing is Olam HaBa : נהנין מזיו השכינה . \" I don't want people bogged down in resurrection and thinking that's all. \" How it fits If Olam HaBa is purely spiritual, what is a physical resurrection for? The dead rise, live a while, die again, and then begin Olam HaBa: \"the real McCoy, the final destination.\" The Rambam's picture, assembled Stage 1 The righteous die and begin their Olam HaBa 2 Around the time of Mashiach (before or after), here on earth , the dead come alive physically 3 They live some time (\"we don't know how long\") 4 They die again and return to the dust 5 Olam HaBa : pure spiritual bliss , nothing physical \" I don't know of any other Rishon who takes this approach. I think he's unique .\" In academia , some argue the letter wasn't his real view but written for the masses . \" It's not for me to put a view on that. He wrote it, and we take his words at face value. \" Part 5: Hints of Another Reading 1. Professor Marc Shapiro: the order in Hilchot Teshuvah 3 Halachah Principles 3:7 1–5 3:8 6–9 , and 10 3:6 הכופרים בתחיית המתים ובביאת הגואל : 13 then 12 — 11 missing \" Two questions: where's 11 ? (Rav Shilat: it's a consequence of 10 .) And why 13 before 12? Everywhere else Mashiach comes first .\" Shapiro's suggestion: in Mishneh Torah \" Techiyat HaMetim \" alludes to a spiritual resurrection: the immortality of the soul , i.e. Principle 11 (Olam HaBa) . That answers both : 11 is there , and the order is 11, 12 . \" Interesting. The problem: he does have Principle 13. \" 2. The cemetery blessing (the Keter Shem Tov ) Rav Gaguin , Chacham of the Spanish and Portuguese in the early 1900s, recorded their customs in seven volumes . Text Gemara (Berachot 58b) ברוך… אשר יצר אתכם בדין, וזן אתכם בדין, וכלכל אתכם בדין, והמית אתכם בדין, ויודע מספר כולכם, והוא עתיד להחיותכם ולקיימכם… ברוך אתה ה׳ מחיה המתים The Rambam (Hilchot Berachot 10:11) … והמית אתכם בדין, ועתיד להקימכם בדין לחיי העולם הבא … \" Only the Rambam adds lechayei Olam HaBa . There are many texts in the Geonim and Rishonim ( I'm looking into this now: I was asked about the text in a certain cemetery ), and no one else brings in Olam HaBa .\" But in his letter, resurrection has nothing to do with Olam HaBa : you come out of it, live, die, and go back. Here he seems to equate them. The Keter Shem Tov ends: והמשכיל יבין. \" Code words. When a chacham wants to convey something he's uncomfortable saying outright ( Ibn Ezra uses it a lot; vehamaskil yidom : if you're wise, keep quiet). His book isn't theological; he rarely writes it. I think he was hinting at this. \" 3. Chief Rabbi Hertz The Hertz Siddur (used in most United Synagogues ), p. 255 : \" Maimonides makes the doctrine of the resurrection of the dead identical with that of the immortality of the soul, and explains the Talmudic sayings to the contrary as figurative language. \" \" Did Hertz not know of the letter? Of course he knew. \" \"Why am I telling you this?\" \" Perhaps I should keep quiet: vehamaskil yavin . I'll tell you why. Let me be very clear: the overwhelming understanding of the Rambam is that the thirteenth principle is belief in a physical resurrection. \" The difficulties The ruling Philosophically , how resurrection fits his eschatology is very difficult He writes very clearly that he believes , so al panav , our duty is to believe in a physical resurrection. \" Sometimes we accept what we can't understand. \" His brevity everywhere, and the hints \" Someone who says I find physical resurrection difficult; I understand the thirteenth principle as Olam HaBa, spiritual identity: I wouldn't call him a kofer. Deny any of the principles and you are a kofer , with many sanctions (our final shiur). But this, I personally wouldn't , given everything we've seen.\" Part 6: The Other Views The Rambam The Ramban (with R. Meir HaLevi / the Ramah ; articulated by the Ramchal and the Kabbalah ) Rav Saadia Gaon (and other Rishonim) Order Resurrection in this world , then death , then Olam HaBa Mashiach (12) → death → the world ends → resurrection ushers in Olam HaBa Two resurrections Olam HaBa is… Souls only Body and soul together The body Returns to dust Our own bodies , \" you'll see your flesh ,\" but refined and elevated After Live forever 1st , at Mashiach's time, for the righteous of Israel ; 2nd , after the world ends, for all with a portion in Olam HaBa, including non-Jews Strength \" Philosophically very cogent ,\" though a physical Olam HaBa is a strong question for the Rambam (We learnt it in Emunot veDe'ot ) Albo: \"When they come alive, we'll know\" Sefer HaIkkarim IV:35: הדבר הזה חלקו בו גדולי האחרונים. - The Rambam \"and many sages with him\" : the dead, once revived, will use all their physical senses as is normal, then die and return to dust. - R. Meir HaLevi, the Ramah and the Ramban : they'll live their natural span , and then their bodies will be refined like Eliyahu and Chanoch , body and soul , enjoying the radiance of the Shechinah , sustained forever without food or drink . \"Strange, but possible.\" Which is right? התשובה לכל זה וכיוצא בו היא כמו שהשיב רבי יהושע בן חנניה לאנשי אלכסנדריא: כשיחיו נחכם להם. When they come alive, we'll know. \" So much ambiguity: when, how, where it falls in the framework. Don't worry about it. You don't need to know. \" The takeaway: \" There is a thirteenth principle: belief in a physical resurrection of the dead. When, why, how: different views. Someone who believes only in a spiritual resurrection I wouldn't call a kofer , but one can't go against the Rambam's plain words in his letter . Prima facie, it's physical. \" Part 7: Questions Robert: Does the Rambam hold people keep being born after Techiyat HaMetim , and so miss it, since it's a once-in-history event? \" I'm almost sure the Rambam doesn't discuss it anywhere. Rav Saadia does: they'll marry, have children, live long. But if Mashiach came now, you and I wouldn't experience it either : we're alive. It's for those who died. I don't see an issue. \" Will they wake up to strangers , a hundred or two hundred years on? *As a baby or an old person? * \"Rav Saadia happens to say you'll meet your family , but that's not the point . Not as a baby: some kind of adult. When it happens, we'll worry about it. \" Is it like Mashiach, believe but don't calculate? \" You're right. \" Even Olam HaBa , the Rambam says, is like describing colours to someone blind from birth : \" how do you explain red and green? \" But he spends ages on Olam HaBa because it's the ultimate . Mashiach and Techiyat HaMetim are principles of faith, but compared to Olam HaBa, far lower. \" The Rambam says it about Mashiach, Albo about resurrection, and I think the Rambam would say the same : it can't be defended philosophically; it's a belief. \" Part 8: From What Body? Rav Saadia is unequivocal : the actual decomposed parts of the body are reconstituted . Cremation \" I once gave a whole shiur on cremation. God forbid: it's absolutely forbidden. The exact source is a big discussion: six or seven views .\" One of the most interesting: the Minchat Elazar ( the Munkatcher Rebbe ): cremating yourself denies resurrection : how will you rise from ashes? \" My simple thought when I read that: so all the people burnt to ashes in Auschwitz won't be resurrected? \" Rav Saadia himself : nothing is completely destroyed ; from the smallest atoms left, Hashem can reconstitute the body . Body at resurrection Rav Saadia The original matter , reconstituted Chasdai Crescas ( Or Hashem ) and others Not necessarily : Hashem can fashion a body identical to the one you lived in Summary Principle 13: Techiyat HaMetim , \"already explained\" : a foundation of the Torah, for the righteous , and \"a person must die and return to dust\" . Sources: אני אמית ואחיה (Ha'azinu); Yeshayahu , Daniel . Not gilgul . The Rambam is terse everywhere , and the Ra'avad read him as souls only . Ma'amar Techiyat HaMetim (after a 1189 letter from Yemen ): physical resurrection, yes ; brief because of style , because it's belief, not philosophy , and because Olam HaBa is the main thing . Rise, live, die again, then Olam HaBa : \" unique among the Rishonim .\" Hints otherwise: Shapiro (13 before 12, 11 missing); the cemetery blessing's lechayei Olam HaBa and the Keter Shem Tov's והמשכיל יבין ; Rabbi Hertz . The ruling: al panav we believe in a physical resurrection ; one who understands it spiritually \"I wouldn't call a kofer \" . The Ramban: resurrection ushers in an embodied Olam HaBa . Rav Saadia: two resurrections . Albo: כשיחיו נחכם להם. Same matter or new? Rav Saadia vs Crescas ; cremation is forbidden, but Hashem can raise even the ashes of Auschwitz . Coming Up After Purim (in two weeks): the final shiur of the series : the Rambam's concluding paragraph , what follows from denying any of the Thirteen Principles , and how it applies today . Then the Moreh Nevuchim . \" Wishing everyone a beautiful, happy and enjoyable Purim. \"","JN_uI33QIgw":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 12 (the days of Mashiach), with Hilchot Melachim 11–12 Shiur: Part 17 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 10 February 2026 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Where We Are \" Coming to the end of the Rambam's introductions: the final one, to Perek Chelek , whose second part is the Thirteen Ikkarim .\" Category Principles Hashem 1–5 Revelation : prophecy, Moshe, the Torah not changing 6–9 Consequences and eschatology : Hashem's knowledge (10), consequences of our actions (11, last week), Mashiach (12), Techiyat HaMetim (13) 10–13 \" We've already seen some discussion of Mashiach in this hakdamah , so tonight: the points we haven't covered yet .\" Part 1: The Text היסוד השנים עשר: ימות המשיח. והוא להאמין ולאמת שיבוא, ולא יחשוב שיתאחר, ״אם יתמהמה חכה לו״. ולא ישים לו זמן, ולא יעשה לו סברות במקראות להוציא זמן ביאתו. אמרו חכמים: ״תפח רוחן של מחשבי קצין״. ושיאמין שיהיה לו יתרון ומעלה וכבוד על כל המלכים שהיו מעולם, כפי שנבאו עליו כל הנביאים, ממשה רבנו עליו השלום עד מלאכי עליו השלום. ומי שיסתפק בו או נתמעטה אצלו מעלתו, כפר בתורה, שהעידה בו בפירוש בפרשת בלעם ובפרשת אתם נצבים. ומכלל יסוד זה, שאין מלך לישראל אלא מבית דוד ומזרע שלמה בלבד, וכל החולק על המשפחה הזאת כפר בשם יתברך ובדברי נביאיו. Five elements # Element Source 1 Mashiach will come להאמין ולאמת שיבוא 2 Await him, even if he delays אם יתמהמה חכה לו ( Chavakuk 2:3 ) 3 Don't calculate the time , nor build arguments from verses תפח רוחן של מחשבי קצין ( Sanhedrin 97b ): \" may the bones of those who calculate the end wither \" 4 His greatness above all kings who ever were Prophesied from Moshe to Malachi . Doubting him, or diminishing his greatness, is denying the Torah 5 From the House of David, the seed of Shlomo alone לא יסור שבט מיהודה . Disputing the family = denying Hashem and His prophets \" Primarily, Mashiach is a king. It's important to recognise that.\" Part 2: Where Is Mashiach in the Torah? (Hilchot Melachim 11:1) המלך המשיח עתיד לעמוד ולהחזיר מלכות דוד ליושנה לממשלה הראשונה, ובונה המקדש, ומקבץ נדחי ישראל, וחוזרין כל המשפטים בימיו כשהיו מקודם: מקריבין קרבנות, ועושין שמטין ויובלות… וכל מי שאינו מאמין בו, או מי שאינו מחכה לביאתו, לא בשאר נביאים בלבד הוא כופר, אלא בתורה ובמשה רבנו. The Messianic king will… Restore David's kingdom to its former glory Build the Temple Gather Israel from the four corners of the world Restore the laws : sacrifices , Shemittah and Yovel \"Doesn't believe in him, or doesn't await his coming .\" \" Waiting seems to be an essential part. \" In the Prophets it's everywhere : \"he doesn't need a source for that.\" As throughout the principles, the Rambam wants a source in the Torah itself : Parashah Verse Reading Nitzavim ושב ה׳ אלהיך את שבותך ורחמך, ושב וקבצך מכל העמים… אם יהיה נדחך בקצה השמים, משם יקבצך… והביאך ה׳ אלהיך The ingathering : \"a prelude to all the prophecies\" Balak Bilaam's final prophecy : two meshichim Below The two Messiahs: משיח הראשון שהוא דוד, שהושיע את ישראל מיד צריהם; ומשיח האחרון שעומד מבניו, שמושיע את ישראל באחרונה. \" David was in a sense the first Melech HaMashiach : not the final one, but the first .\" Verse (Bamidbar 24:17) The Rambam אראנו ולא עתה David אשורנו ולא קרוב The final Mashiach דרך כוכב מיעקב David וקם שבט מישראל The Melech HaMashiach (After three attempts to curse turned to blessing, Balak says \"thank you, time to go\"; Bilaam: let me tell you a bit about them , their future grandeur.) Part 3: The Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal Text Covers Ani Ma'amin אני מאמין באמונה שלמה בביאת המשיח, ואף על פי שיתמהמה, עם כל זה אחכה לו בכל יום שיבוא Coming and waiting . \"Every day\" is an addition : \" these two words may be problematic \" Yigdal ישלח לקץ ימין משיחנו, לפדות מחכי קץ ישועתו Coming and waiting Ketz yamin , with a final nun ! \" I've always thought it was yamim with a mem. Looking now, it's yamin, a play on yamim : days. I wonder if anyone notices when they sing it. \" Both stress not only the coming but the waiting. \"In distinction to other religions in which the Mashiach has already come\": \" Rabbi Sacks used to talk about this a lot. It's a source of hope : we haven't yet experienced the Golden Age ; we're awaiting the redemption of mankind . There's comfort in that. \" Part 4: What \"Mashiach\" Means Root מ־ש־ח: to anoint, to smear. Both the Kohen and the king were anointed with oil: the Kohen Gadol is HaKohen HaMashiach . \" At the coronation after Queen Elizabeth passed away , an essential part of the ceremony was the oil from Jerusalem .\" Mashiach is primarily a king , so he's \"the anointed\" ; but HaMashiach , the anointed one: the finale, the highest of all the kings . \" That's where the Rambam gets his greatness above all kings. \" Part 5: Waiting ( Tzippita LiYeshuah ) How we wait in practice: the Amidah Berachah Words Teka BeShofar תקע בשופר גדול לחרותנו, ושא נס לקבץ גלויותינו… ברוך אתה ה׳ מקבץ נדחי עמו ישראל : the ingathering , \" an essential task of Mashiach\" Et Tzemach David את צמח דוד עבדך מהרה תצמיח, וקרנו תרום בישועתך, כי לישועתך קוינו כל היום ( Hashivah Shofteinu , VeLiYerushalayim ) Restoring the judges, the Sanhedrin, Yerushalayim : \" not specifically Mashiach \" \"We pray for wisdom, parnassah , health : people focus there, and towards the later berachot we lose focus . But two (or three) are about Mashiach. Praying them three times a day , or as women do, that's how we fulfil awaiting Mashiach .\" The source Shabbat 31a: among the questions asked of a person after 120 : צפית לישועה? Did you await the salvation? Is waiting really part of the principle? The Rambam says so here and in Mishneh Torah ( או מי שאינו מחכה לביאתו ). The Brisker Rav wrote a lot on this: the principle is to believe and await. \" The difficulty: to call someone who believes but doesn't actively await a denier of a principle : quite severe, quite harsh. So others reinterpret it. It doesn't seem like that to me. Perhaps because it's so distant , believing intellectually isn't enough; you have to live with it , and that's waiting .\" \"Every day\"? The Ani Ma'amin's bechol yom isn't in the Rambam. \" The Gemara says there are times Mashiach can't come : Shabbat and Yom Tov , and so on.\" \" I don't know if the Rambam meant every single day. The point is it's in your mind, regularly .\" The Chafetz Chaim (R. Yisrael Meir HaKohen): \" it's told he had a suit and a suitcase packed for Mashiach. Legend or not, it's only 100 years ago, so it's very likely true. \" Part 6: Don't Calculate Why does the Rambam put this right after waiting? \" Calculate a date and you've said he's coming then , in however many years. That pushes it off and stops you thinking he can come at any moment. \" The Rambam never calculated : forbidden, and part of this principle . Ibn Ezra also forbids it. Yet: Calculated a date Roughly Rav Saadia Gaon 20–30 years after his own death The Ramban The 1200s (\"I think\") Abarbanel The 1500s The Malbim The 1900s \" Many great Chachamim calculated dates. And the reality is they were all wrong. \" Defending them: \" The most basic answer : these weren't definitive dates but yearnings and hopes : a ripe time for Mashiach.\" The Rambam's reply: \" Even predicting a ripe time 10 or 20 years off takes away from the yearning. Don't do this. It's forbidden. Stay away. \" Will: The Talmud often mentions 6,000 years. Would the Rambam say that's the wrong approach? \" Excellent question. Two points: 1. No one in the Talmud makes a calculation. The calculations are by later authorities . The 6,000 years says the world will exist 6,000 years , not that Mashiach comes by then (though that would follow). 2. The Rambam says explicitly it's not to be taken literally : one opinion we don't follow , or read differently. It could be longer, even eternal. No 6,000-year limit .\" Part 7: A Process, Not a Moment \" Not going into it tonight , but the Rambam 100% believes it's a gradual process , and so did Rav Saadia : not from one moment to the next .\" Where are we going? \" Towards Eretz Yisrael : full sovereignty , the Beit HaMikdash rebuilt, the Sanhedrin sitting again, as a sovereign nation lived in Temple times, like the empires of David and Shlomo , when all the nations around came .\" Rav Kook and many others saw the rebirth of the State of Israel as the beginning of the process . \" Historically it's never happened that a nation is exiled twice and returns : Babylon (Iraq) , back; Rome , back a third time. And the miracles of the State today, I think, are pretty clearly supernatural. Of course there's much more to do: many faults and problems, and we're clearly not sovereign: all the wars we've been at. But it's the beginning .\" Part 8: A Person or an Era? For the Rambam, Mashiach is an actual person : \"he will come\" , \"greatest of all kings\" . Not just a concept or an era. But some thought otherwise. Midrash Tanchuma: אמר הקב״ה: בעולם הזה הייתם נושעים על ידי בני אדם : in Egypt Moshe ; under Sisera Devorah and Barak ; against Midian… the Judges ; ועל ידי שהיו בשר ודם, הייתם חוזרים ומשתעבדים : human saviours, so it didn't last . לעתיד לבוא: אני בעצמי גואל אתכם, ושוב אין אתם משתעבדים , as it says ישראל נושע בה׳ תשועת עולמים . Rav Menachem Kasher ( Torah Shelemah , the last 150 years): - Hoshea, Yeshayahu, Michah, Yirmiyahu and Zechariah speak of a specific human Mashiach . - Nachum, Tzefaniah, Chavakuk, Malachi, Yoel and Daniel don't. So perhaps not necessarily a person . \" I think that's very, very difficult. The Rambam would certainly disagree. \" Part 9: Rabbi Hillel's \"No Mashiach\" Sanhedrin 99a: רבי הלל אומר: אין להם משיח לישראל, שכבר אכלוהו בימי חזקיה. There's no Mashiach for Israel; they already \"consumed\" him in Chizkiyahu's day. אמר רב יוסף: שרי ליה מריה לרבי הלל! May his Master forgive Rabbi Hillel! Chizkiyahu was in the First Temple , yet Zechariah , in the Second , prophesied Mashiach still to come. Rashi: אין להם משיח לישראל, אלא הקב״ה ימלוך בעצמו ויגאלם לבדו : Rabbi Hillel didn't deny a Messianic era , only a personal individual Messiah ; the prophecies of a person were fulfilled in Chizkiyahu . \" Already in Rashi : collective redemption rather than an individual leading the way .\" Rav Yosef Albo's challenge ( Sefer HaIkkarim , the opening) \" A book of principles that begins by saying principles are dangerous. \" The Rambam counts Mashiach as a principle , and whoever denies a principle is a heretic with no portion in Olam HaBa . וזה דבר מתמיה מאד : Rabbi Hillel, אחד מחכמי ישראל הנזכרים בתלמוד , said ein Mashiach , so by the Rambam he'd be a kofer . Then why does the Gemara give him that honoured title? \" When the Gemara cites someone to refute him, it's in derogatory terms. Not here. \" Albo rejects the reinterpretations ( \"may he be forgiven\" means he was wrong ) and concludes: בלי ספק רבי הלל לא היה מאמין בביאת המשיח כלל, ואף על פי כן לא היה נקרא כופר , because ביאת המשיח אינה עיקר לתורת משה : the Torah can exist without it . Mashiach is… Denier is… The Rambam A principle of faith A heretic , no portion in Olam HaBa Albo A true belief every Jew must hold , but not foundational Wrong, not a kofer , like one who holds creation from pre-existing matter (Principle 4) \" That's one of Albo's big proofs against the Thirteen Principles. \" (Others: Rashi's era reading; or Mashiach without miracles.) \"I'm relying on Rabbi Hillel\": the Chatam Sofer The Chatam Sofer (R. Moshe Sofer, ~250 years ago) was asked exactly this. Today you would be a kofer . \" Like halachah: two views in the Gemara, sometimes on huge matters; once the psak is given, it must be followed. A chacham who sticks to his view against the Sanhedrin's ruling: terrible things can happen to him.\" \"The same in theology. Everyone now holds there will be a Messianic era. You can't say I eat chicken and dairy because Rabbi Yosei HaGelili did.\" And Rabbi Hillel himself? \"Then there was no psak : it was his view, and he probably changed it once he saw the consensus.\" \" Today everyone would agree a person must believe in Mashiach. Deny it: per the Rambam, a heretic; per Albo, wrong but not a heretic , like other beliefs, e.g. Gan Eden , which the Rambam says clearly is not one of the principles .\" Part 10: The Seed of Shlomo? Rav Kafich , \" authoritative on the Rambam's thought in many ways \": not to be taken literally . - He doesn't know the Rambam's source , and some Gemarot suggest otherwise . - Throughout Mishneh Torah , whenever the Rambam discusses Mashiach, he says only the House of David, never Shlomo . - His suggestion: the Rambam added Shlomo to counter the Christian genealogy of Jesus through Natan son of David . \" I wouldn't say hyperbole , but not an essential part .\" Part 11: How Do We Recognise Mashiach? (Hilchot Melachim 11:3–4) \"Don't think the Mashiach must perform miracles, do new things, or revive the dead.\" Proof: Rabbi Akiva , a great chacham of the Mishnah, was Bar Kozba's arms-bearer , and he and many sages of his generation thought him Mashiach until he was killed . They never asked him for a miracle. Stage Criteria Status 1 From the House of David (note: David , not Shlomo); studies Torah and keeps the mitzvot like David , Written and Oral ; leads Israel in the Torah's ways ; fights the wars of Hashem בחזקת שהוא משיח : presumed Mashiach 2 אם עשה והצליח, ובנה מקדש במקומו, וקבץ נדחי ישראל משיח בודאי : certainly Mashiach Failed Didn't succeed, or was killed \" We were wrong. \" Like other good kings who died : not necessarily a false Mashiach , just a wrong assumption Jesus and \"the Yishmaeli who followed\" The Rambam: people thought Yeshu HaNotzri was Mashiach, and he was killed. Why would Hashem allow that? \" Machshevot haBorei : we don't fully understand Hashem's ways.\" \" Notice: whenever the Rambam refers to Jesus he names him; Muhammad he never names \": וכל הדברים האלו של ישו הנוצרי ושל זה הישמעאלי שעמד אחריו . \" He had no respect for Muhammad. \" אינן אלא לישר דרך למלך המשיח : only to pave the way for the King Mashiach . Christianity dominates the world, and so the idea of Mashiach has spread everywhere ; \"when the true Mashiach comes, they'll realise they were wrong .\" \"The wars of Hashem\": physical or spiritual? \" Famously, many believed the Lubavitcher Rebbe was Mashiach. The son of Rav Mordechai Eliyahu , Rav Yosef Eliyahu, told me his father used to say the Lubavitcher Rebbe maybe was Mashiach in his time. But he's died. And he went to no wars that I know of. So Chabad say the wars of Hashem are spiritual wars .\" \" I don't think that's what the Rambam means : it's in the same paragraph as Bar Kozba , who fought physical wars , the revolt against Rome , successful for a time. The simple reading is physical wars. Front line or in the background? I don't know. He leads a people who win wars and take back sovereignty of Eretz Yisrael.\" \" Nowhere in Mishneh Torah or this principle does the Rambam say Mashiach needs prophetic powers or to be a miracle worker .\" Will he be a rabbi? \" He studies, knows and teaches Torah and keeps the mitzvot : if that's not a rabbi, what is? But he needn't have the title. Many people do all of that and aren't rabbis , and they'd be fully able to be Mashiach .\" \"A rabbi and a warrior.\" Part 12: What Happens Then? (Hilchot Melachim 12) Life continues as usual , with two differences: 1. Sovereignty \" No more UN resolutions, no more wars : completely sovereign\" 2. Freedom from the burdens of making a living \" Not that money grows on trees , nor clothes , as some passages indicate; nor the wolf literally dwelling with the sheep : metaphors for peace and tranquillity \" Hilchot Melachim 12:4: לא נתאוו החכמים והנביאים ימות המשיח… אלא כדי שיהיו פנויין בתורה וחכמתה, ולא יהיה להם נוגש ומבטל, כדי שיזכו לחיי העולם הבא. We long for Mashiach only to be free for Torah and wisdom , with nothing to hinder us , so as to merit Olam HaBa . \" This is key. As we explained last week, Olam HaBa is the direct consequence of one's moral and intellectual development . In Messianic times we'll have more time for it . A person today who's financially stable and free to sit and learn is already, in a sense, living in the times of Mashiach. \" \" Free will remains. Life continues as usual. But Israel is sovereign , and we're far freer to develop our intellect, pursue wisdom and keep the mitzvot .\" Summary Principle 12, five elements: Mashiach will come ; await him even if he delays; don't calculate ; he'll be greater than all kings ; from David (and Shlomo). Torah sources: Nitzavim (the ingathering) and Balak ( two Messiahs: David and the last ). Mashiach = the anointed king , the anointed one. Waiting is part of the principle ( צפית לישועה ; the Brisker Rav ): we do it in the Amidah . \"Every day\" is the Ani Ma'amin's addition. Calculating pushes him away. Rav Saadia, the Ramban, Abarbanel and the Malbim did it, as \"ripe times\" , and were all wrong . The 6,000 years isn't a deadline, and the Rambam doesn't take it literally . A gradual process : Rav Kook and the State of Israel as its beginning. A person, not just an era (vs Rashi on Rabbi Hillel , Rav Kasher ). Rabbi Hillel's denial : Albo says not a kofer , because Mashiach isn't foundational ; the Chatam Sofer : after the psak , today you would be . Seed of Shlomo : Rav Kafich , anti-Christian emphasis , absent from Mishneh Torah. Recognising him: Torah, mitzvot, leadership, the wars of Hashem ( physical , the Rabbi argues) → presumed ; Temple and ingathering → certain . No miracles required ( Rabbi Akiva and Bar Kozba ). Jesus and Muhammad \"paved the way.\" Messianic times : sovereignty and freedom to learn , for the sake of Olam HaBa . Coming Up Next week: Principle 13, Techiyat HaMetim , \" the final, or penultimate, shiur on this ; I'll try to make it the final.\" Then, in a few weeks, the Moreh Nevuchim .","RnjS8UwtZwE":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 11 (reward and punishment) Shiur: Part 16 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 3 February 2026 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Where We Are Principles 1–5 Hashem Himself 6–7 Prophecy , and Moshe's unique prophecy 8–9 The Torah is from heaven, and will never change 10 Hashem knows our deeds and our thoughts , and what His knowledge means 11 Tonight. \" I've deliberately titled the shiur the consequences of our actions . You'll see why.\" 12–13 Mashiach and Techiyat HaMetim : \"things we've already discussed in this series,\" probably together, next week Part 1: The Text היסוד האחד עשר: כי הוא יתברך נותן שכר למי שעושה מצות התורה, ויעניש למי שעובר על אזהרותיה. וכי השכר הגדול העולם הבא, והעונש החזק הכרת. וכבר אמרנו בזה הענין מה שיספיק. Hashem rewards those who keep the Torah's commandments and punishes those who transgress its prohibitions. The greatest reward is Olam HaBa; the severest punishment is karet . (We've already said enough on this.) והמקרא המורה על היסוד הזה מה שנאמר: ״ועתה אם תשא חטאתם, ואם אין מחני נא מספרך אשר כתבת״. והשיב לו השם יתברך: ״מי אשר חטא לי אמחנו מספרי״. ראיה שיודע העובד והחוטא, לתת שכר לזה ועונש לזה. After the Golden Calf , Moshe: If You will bear their sin; if not, blot me out of Your book. Hashem: Whoever has sinned against Me, him I will blot out of My book. Proof that Hashem knows the server and the sinner, to reward the one and punish the other . Why this verse? \" There are so many verses : Adam was told not to eat, ate, and was punished; Noach's generation ; Sodom ; Avimelech . Far more explicit. Why go to this specific dialogue ? It's a great proof, but there are so many before it. I believe it's the root of this principle. \" (Answer in Part 4.) The Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal Text Covers Ani Ma'amin שהבורא יתברך שמו גומל טוב לשומרי מצותיו ומעניש לעוברי מצותיו Part one only Yigdal גומל לאיש חסד כמפעלו, נותן לרשע רע כרשעתו Part one only Both state the main part : there are consequences . Neither includes Olam HaBa and karet , \" which the Rambam includes for a reason \" (Part 6). Part 2: The Missing Principle in Hilchot Teshuvah Hilchot Teshuvah 3 (learnt together a couple of years ago) lists who has no portion in Olam HaBa : Rambam Principle 3:7, the five minim : no God; two gods ; a body ; not the First ; serving avodah zarah 1–5 3:8, deniers of prophecy / of Moshe's 6, 7 The Torah is not from heaven (even one verse), or Hashem has exchanged the mitzvot 8, 9 Hashem doesn't know human deeds 10 Denying Techiyat HaMetim and Mashiach 12, 13 Reward and punishment Missing The commentators ask: where's Principle 11? \" The explanation, I think, is simple: it's a follow-on from Principle 10 . Hashem's knowledge of our actions is only relevant if there are consequences. So He knows: so what? Eleven is what 10 means. \" Part 3: Everything Is Just: The Moreh and the Me'iri Moreh III:17: ומכלל יסודי תורת משה רבנו שלא יתכן מלפניו יתעלה עול בשום אופן, ושכל האסון הבא על בני אדם או הטובה המגעת להם, יחיד או ציבור, הכל כפי הדין ומשפט צדק שאין בו עול כלל. אפילו נדקר אדם מקוץ בידו והוציאו מיד, הרי זה עונש עליו… אלא שאיננו יודעים כיצד הוא הדין. No injustice from Hashem in any way. Every misfortune or good, to an individual or a community , is just : even a thorn in the hand pulled out at once is a consequence. We just don't know how the judgement works. The Me'iri (Shabbat 55): \"Not a direct student of the Rambam, about a hundred years later, but he shared his outlook and often followed him.\" מכרע הדת להאמין שכל מה שיקרה לאדם מטוב ועד רע, הכל מושגח מאת השם יתברך בפרטות : everything that happens to a person, good to bad, is under particular providence. Don't be put off by righteous people suffering and the wicked prospering : אין הקב״ה מקפח שכר כל בריה . Chazal: אין מיתה בלא חטא ואין יסורין בלא עון : no death without sin, no suffering without iniquity . The Rambam emphatically brings this: the correct understanding. But what about… Statement Seems to say The Me'iri Bava Batra 17a / Shabbat 55b: four people died only through the serpent's counsel (Adam's sin) Death without sin Derech haflagah : exaggeration . They sinned very little Kohelet 7:20: אין אדם צדיק בארץ אשר יעשה טוב ולא יחטא Everyone sins Not literal either: no one avoids even a small sin (Rav Saadia, learnt on Monday nights, was also bothered by Kohelet: he read it as no one lacks the ability to sin. \"But the simple meaning is no one doesn't sin.\") The Me'iri's key line: ואף על פי שנשאר דעת האומר כן בתיובתא, אין עיקרי אמונות תלויים בראיות של פשטי מקראות ואגדות. Principles of faith don't hang on the plain sense of individual verses and aggadot. \" Beautiful. Don't build philosophy and theology on individual statements of Chazal. \" The general rule stands: everything is a consequence of one's own deeds. Part 4: \"Reward and Punishment\" Is the Wrong Picture \" I've been using the phrase reward and punishment , but the truth is it's really a Christian idea. We don't really have that concept , and I'll prove it.\" \" Many religious people think Olam HaBa and Gehinnom are places where you get rewarded and punished. Some have a very crude understanding. And truth be told, some Chachamim believed that : on Monday nights we just finished Emunot veDe'ot : Rav Saadia sees Gehinnom as a kind of fire and Olam HaBa as a kind of light . We struggled with it. I reject it, only because I have the Rambam, who explained it far more deeply. \" For the Rambam there's no principle called \"reward and punishment\". Principle 11 is: Hashem responds to our choices. Principle 10: Hashem knows what we do, and we have choice . Principle 11: there are repercussions to what we do with that choice. Middah keneged middah Usually translated \"measure for measure\" or \"tit for tat\" : \" you do X, Hashem gives you Y; you do good, He gives you gold coins .\" The deeper idea:** \"In this world a person creates his or her own identity.\" That's the key point to remember from tonight. This world The next world The world of process : choices, doing , forming identity No longer achieving, but existing on the identity you made \" A kind of simulation of our real identity, which we can change while we can choose\" Our real identity, fully realised and felt Shabbat: one-sixtieth of Olam HaBa \" So many explanations, but I think it's simpler. ששת ימים תעבד ועשית כל מלאכתך… וביום השביעי תשבת. The six days are human doing ; Shabbat is human being . We stop achieving and just exist in the moment. \" \" That's why the halachah forbids preparing on Shabbat for after Shabbat : start preparing and you're thinking about next week , not living in the moment .\" \" Exactly what Olam HaBa is: the world of existing , on the essence you made of yourself here.\" Olam HaBa is a shared space between Hashem and us : true existence , who we really are . The Ramchal, opening of Mesillat Yesharim : שהאדם לא נברא אלא להתענג על ה׳ ולהנות מזיו שכינתו, שזהו התענוג האמיתי והעידון הגדול מכל העידונים שיכולים להמצא. ומקום העידון הזה באמת הוא העולם הבא… אך הדרך כדי להגיע אל מחוז חפצנו זה, הוא זה העולם. Man was created to delight in Hashem and bask in the radiance of His presence , the greatest bliss. Its place is Olam HaBa; the way there is this world. \" Simple as that. \" \" Our reward isn't a bag of gold coins for our trouble, and our punishment isn't a smack for being bad. It's simply what I've chosen for myself. \" Sechar and onesh = the repercussions of a person's choices . Back to the verse: nothing is transferable \" That's why the Rambam chose this verse. Moshe offers: take their sin, or blot me out*. Hashem: Whoever has sinned against Me, him I will blot out . It doesn't work like that. Reward and punishment can't be transferred, because it's who you are *.\" \"With Adam, Noach, Sodom you might picture a system : they sinned, so God punished . The Rambam says: don't think like that. \" \" In some circles you'll find: do a mitzvah on my behalf , sell me the value of your mitzvah , say Kaddish for my father and I'll pay you . Each has its own context , but generally the Rambam would frown on all of these. That's not how Torah works. \" Part 5: Tzaddik , Rasha and the Fair Question Start point: כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא : \" that's how we're created to be .\" How much of that shared existence we have is commensurate with who we become . \" The only thing Hashem won't do is make the choices for us. \" Who is a rasha ? \"People picture an evil person with fangs . It doesn't have to be like that. \" Rasha Tzaddik Lives for The moment : no memory, no past , no identity beyond the whims of now The long game Has No purpose, direction, future, goals Thinks of the future and makes the right choice for it Model Esav Yaakov Why Chazal make Esav and Yaakov the stereotypes, when \"in the Torah Yaakov has questionable moments and Esav good ones\": the lentil stew. Esav comes home hungry and tired*: feed me . Yaakov: sell me the birthright . Esav: *הנה אנכי הולך למות ולמה זה לי בכרה . \" The whim of the moment. Yaakov works hard cooking and plays the long game , always .\" Karet The greatest punishment, karet : you don't share this existence with God. אין לו חלק לעולם הבא . The Rambam (Hilchot Teshuvah 8:5): הנקמה שאין נקמה גדולה ממנה, שתכרת הנפש ולא תזכה לאותן החיים. Sechar mitzvah mitzvah (Avot 4:2) שכר מצוה מצוה, ושכר עבירה עבירה. \"Many commentators try to explain it. The Rambam explains it : the reward of a mitzvah is the mitzvah itself : it creates the person I am . The result of a sin is the sin itself : you've tarnished yourself . There's no you went against My word, so I'll hurt you . \" Sinners who aren't resha'im \" Many people commit many sins , even terrible things, but deep down they care : they know, believe, want to do good , but they get stuck in muck . That's not a rasha . They have a vision of Olam HaBa and a place in it ; they keep getting stuck.\" \"Is it fair?\" The kind atheist \" I've been asked this so many times. He's so kind, so moral, so good, so clever, but an atheist. No portion in the world to come? **\" Through the lens of… Answer Reward and punishment \" Doesn't make sense. Why such a big punishment for a great person?\" Identity \" Makes total sense. How can you share a space with something you don't believe in? You lived your life with an identity that denied any existence beyond you , so you don't live with that existence when you die .\" (\"This, by the way, is what Yom Kippur is all about\": we've discussed it in the past.) \"Then why does Hashem get angry?\" וחרה אף ה׳ בכם (in the Shema): His nostrils burn . If consequences are natural , why speak like this? דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם. \" We're meat brains. \" \" A good parent's anger isn't revenge , nor punishing the child for the wrong . Correct anger says: I have a vision for who you are; I'm trying to mould you; you're not living up to it, and I want you to see I'm upset. \" So too Hashem: I want you to share existence with Me, and you're not. Part 6: Not a Candy Machine Avot 1:3, Antigonos: אל תהיו כעבדים המשמשין את הרב על מנת לקבל פרס. Why peras , a prize , and not sachar ? Peras , a prize \"Rewarding\" At work The salary and the bonuses \" My job has been rewarding \": the fruits , something beyond the financial In avodat Hashem \"I put in X, press a button, coins come out\" Who I become \" People who serve for a prize treat Hashem as a candy machine, a gold-coin machine. That's where all these segulot come from : say this verse twice on that day, then repeat it, and you'll get money .\" The Tuesday of Parashat Beshalach \" Last week you were probably bombarded : say Parashat HaMan twice today, there's a segulah for the Tuesday of Beshalach .\" \" There is a 'segulah', in inverted commas, to say Parashat HaMan every day: it's recorded in the Shulchan Aruch. But the idea isn't mystical magic. It reminds you that parnassah comes from Hashem. Go to work with that in mind, live like that, and of course Hashem gives you hashgachah pratit, because, as we learnt last week, the more you live with Hashem, the more providence you have.\" \"But only at this hour, on the ninth hour of the ninth day of the eleventh : it doesn't work like that. Certainly not in the Rambam's world. \" Why the Rambam includes Olam HaBa and karet in the principle \" It's not enough to believe in reward and punishment if you think of it in an immature, crude way. That's wrong. It means an identity that exists with Hashem , or one that doesn't . That's what sechar va'onesh mean. \" Part 7: Is There Reward in This World? On the Rambam's account, the reward can only be in Olam HaBa. Chazal say clearly: there's no reward for mitzvot in this world ( שכר מצוה בהאי עלמא ליכא ). But we say every morning: אלו דברים שאדם אוכל פירותיהם בעולם הזה והקרן קיימת לו לעולם הבא . The Rambam: those aren't reward; they're perot , fruits. Help from Hashem to live the best life here : encouragement, guidance, support , so that you're not preoccupied with money or health and can focus on building your identity . \" Other Rishonim disagree. But on the Rambam's view of Olam HaBa, reward in this world is philosophically impossible . You can't pay me off with extra money or extra years. You may get them, to support you in creating the best identity.\" Not a checklist \" So easy to fall into the trap : I put on tefillin, learn my Torah, go to my shiur, pray, say my Tehillim: tick, and I get my reward. Of course we keep doing all of it , but with the idea that the more I do, the more I develop who I am .\" The Rambam's two axes, everywhere: character ( middot , moral perfection) and intellect (Torah and all study). Why doesn't the Torah mention Olam HaBa? With Rav Saadia we saw several reasons; Ibn Ezra : people can't grasp it ; there are many hints , nothing explicit. For the Rambam, the question doesn't begin. \"If Olam HaBa were a prize : why not mention the prize? Everyone asks. \" But the Torah is the guide to living well , and Olam HaBa is the natural outcome of the identity it builds . \" When you do the mitzvot, that is Olam HaBa. You're making yourself the best person: the greatest reward, full stop. It fits so beautifully. \" Summary Principle 11: Hashem rewards and punishes ; the greatest reward is Olam HaBa, the severest punishment karet . Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal give only the first part. It's missing from Hilchot Teshuvah 3's list because it's the consequence of Principle 10 . Everything that happens is just (Moreh III:17; the Me'iri ): אין מיתה בלא חטא . Contrary aggadot are exaggeration ; principles of faith don't rest on individual statements . \"Reward and punishment\" is a crude, Christian-sounding frame (and Rav Saadia's fire and light ). The Rambam: middah keneged middah means you create your own identity. This world is process, the next is existence : Shabbat, one-sixtieth of Olam HaBa ; the Ramchal . The verse of the Golden Calf : you can't transfer merits or sins . The rasha lives for the moment (Esav); the tzaddik plays the long game (Yaakov).* Karet = *no shared existence . The kind atheist makes sense once you see it as identity . Antigonos's peras : not a candy machine ; segulot such as Parashat HaMan work by reminding us, not by magic. This world gives only perot , support for the real goal. The Torah needn't mention Olam HaBa : keeping it is Olam HaBa . Coming Up Next week: Principles 12 and 13, Mashiach and Techiyat HaMetim , \" probably together .\" Then, \"in a couple of weeks,\" Moreh Nevuchim on Tuesday nights. Next Monday: the sefer of the Rambam's son, HaMaspik LeOvdei Hashem .","Q9N17Ck0P14":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 10 (Hashem knows and attends to human deeds), with Moreh Nevuchim III:17, 18 and 20 Shiur: Part 15 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 27 January 2026 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Where We Are \" We're coming close to the end. \" Principles 1–5 : Hashem Himself. 6–9 : Hashem and humanity: prophecy , Moshe's uniqueness , the Torah , and last week the Torah never changing (and whether Nevi'im change in Messianic times). Where does Principle 10 belong? Principles Theme Category 3 11, 12, 13 Reward and punishment (11), Mashiach (12), Techiyat HaMetim (13) Principle 10 God's knowledge Could go either way: a continuation of 6–9 (the divine–human relationship), or a prelude to 11 , since Hashem must know our actions for reward and punishment to exist Part 1: The Text היסוד העשירי: כי הוא יתברך יודע מעשיהם של בני אדם ואינו מעלים עינו מהם, לא כדעת מי שאמר ״עזב ה׳ את הארץ״. אלא כמו שנאמר: ״גדל העצה ורב העליליה אשר עיניך פקחות על כל דרכי בני אדם״, ונאמר: ״וירא ה׳ כי רבה רעת האדם בארץ״, ונאמר: ״זעקת סדם ועמרה כי רבה״. וזה מורה על היסוד העשירי הזה. Hashem knows the deeds of human beings and does not turn His eyes from them. Not like those who say \"Hashem has forsaken the earth\" : created it and left it to run alone . Two parts 10A יודע מעשיהם He knows our actions 10B ואינו מעלים עינו מהם He doesn't ignore them : He doesn't merely know while paying no attention ; He acts correspondingly Part 2: Actions Only, or Thoughts Too? \" One academic picked up that the Rambam says only יודע מעשיהם : deeds .\" Formulation Thoughts? Rambam יודע מעשיהם של בני אדם Not stated Ani Ma'amin שהבורא יתברך שמו יודע כל מעשה בני אדם וכל מחשבותם, שנאמר: היצר יחד לבם, המבין אל כל מעשיהם Adds \"and all their thoughts\" Yigdal צופה ויודע סתרינו, מביט לסוף דבר בקדמתו \"Our hidden depths\" : seemingly thoughts too \"A hundred percent the Rambam meant actions and thoughts.\" Rav Shilat: when the Rambam writes וגו׳ , he often means see the end of the verse . And וירא ה׳ כי רבה רעת האדם בארץ continues: וכל יצר מחשבת לבו רק רע כל היום : all the thoughts of his heart . \" I think it's not correct to say he limits it to actions. That's how the Ani Ma'amin understood him too.\" Question: How can thoughts count, if I never acted? \"If Hashem doesn't ignore my thoughts, but I never turned them into actions, how is that fair? What about free will?\" \" A fair question, and very relevant. \" # Point 1. The passing thought A thought that pops into your head carries no responsibility, good or bad : \" that's just the brain working of its own accord. \" The Ba'al HaTanya stresses: don't feel bad about it. The question is what you do next : dwell on and develop it , or move on ? 2. Developed bad thoughts Mostly not punishable by the strict letter. Fantasising about murder : \" Is it healthy? No. Will he be punished? Likely not. \" 3. The exceptions Developed thoughts of avodah zarah and of zenut (promiscuity), and only if dwelt on 4. Good thoughts Rewarded even if you couldn't carry them out Part 3: Three Verses, Three Levels Why these three verses? Hashem attends to the individual , the city/nation and humanity as a whole . Hilchot Teshuvah 3:1–2 (which we learned a couple of years ago): כל אחד ואחד מבני האדם יש לו זכיות ועונות. מי שזכיותיו יתירות על עונותיו צדיק, ומי שעונותיו יתירות על זכיותיו רשע, מחצה למחצה בינוני. וכן המדינה: אם היו זכיות כל יושביה מרובות על עונותיהן הרי זו צדקת, ואם היו עונותיהם מרובין הרי זו רשעה. וכן כל העולם כולו. Level Rambam's verse Hilchot Teshuvah The individual אשר עיניך פקחות על כל דרכי בני אדם (Yirmiyahu 32:19): every single person's ways צדיק / רשע / בינוני The city זעקת סדם ועמרה כי רבה מדינה צדקת / רשעה Humanity וירא ה׳ כי רבה רעת האדם בארץ (before the Flood) וכן כל העולם כולו Part 4: Hashgachah : Why It Matters \" Hashem definitely didn't create the world and leave it : He's involved in our day-to-day lives . That's hashgachah : משגיח מן החלנות , peering in , attentive to what we do.\" \" Psychologically, one of our deepest needs is to be noticed. Small children need lots of attention, and parents give it . We still need it as adults ; it's just covered up. The more we value someone, the more we yearn for their attention. So the most important Being in the universe paying attention to our actions should be a source of great chizuk .\" \" Then it suddenly matters whether I wait three hours or six between meat and milk. Many people say who cares, what's the difference? When we know Hashem is paying attention , every action, choice and decision is important. \" Part 5: Five Views of Providence (Moreh III:17) \" Ancient views, heard for a long time. \" # View Providence over… Everything else Problem 1 Epicurus (hence apikoros ) Nothing : even the planets and constellations by chance ; no organiser All mikreh \" Even Aristotle called this unacceptable.\" Some Jews held it : כחשו בה׳ ויאמרו לא הוא 2 Aristotle The heavenly bodies : Hashem ensures they run by the laws of nature ( hashgachah klalit ). \" Even Aristotle accepts an order to the universe \" Random : a leaf falling = a ship of a thousand people sinking ; an ox's dung killing ants = a building collapsing on its occupants ; a spider and a fly = a lion and a righteous man . \" It's nature. \" Ignores the individual human 3 The Ash'ariyya (Islamic school) Everything : the wind blows in exactly the proportion Hashem wants ; every leaf falls by ancient decree, at this time, at this angle — Fatalism : no free will . \" How can you command mitzvot if we can't choose? Our actions become pointless. \" Both philosophically and theologically \"very difficult\" 4 (A related Islamic school) Everything , like view 3 — Same, but humans have free will 5 \"The view of our Torah\" ( the Rambam's ) Everything relating to humans Random , as Aristotle held The Rambam's view in his words יסוד תורת משה רבנו… שהאדם בעל יכולת גמורה : man has complete free will , and all creatures move by their own will , as Hashem wished. \" No doubt about it.\" Side point: reward for animals \" was never heard in our nation \"; one of the later Geonim , having heard it from the Mu'tazila , thought it correct. \" He means Rav Saadia Gaon : in Emunot veDe'ot he held animals receive some reward.\" אני מאמין שההשגחה האלהית בעולם השפל הזה, כלומר מתחת לגלגל הירח, היא רק בפרטי מין האדם בלבד… ואילו שאר בעלי החיים, כל שכן הצמחים וזולתם, דעתי בהם כדעת אריסטו. Below the sphere of the moon, providence is over individual humans alone. Animals, and certainly plants, as Aristotle said . אינני מאמין כלל שהעלה נפל בהשגחה עליו, ולא שהעכביש טרף את הזבוב בגזירה ורצון אלהי פרטי כעת… כל אלו לדעתי במקרה גמור. A leaf doesn't fall by providence. A spider doesn't catch a fly in its web by a particular divine decree. Someone spits in the street and kills a creature: not hashgachah , \" it was in the wrong place .\" Pure chance. His proofs Tehillim: ה׳ מה אדם ותדעהו, בן אנוש ותחשבהו ; מה אנוש כי תזכרנו, ובן אדם כי תפקדנו : why do You attend to man ? \" Many verses show Hashem cares about humans specifically. None about particular parts of nature.\" Chavakuk 1:14: ותעשה אדם כדגי הים, כרמש לא משל בו. The prophet, lamenting the destruction: You've made us like the fish of the sea . The Rambam: as if we humans were abandoned like the fish and the creatures of the earth . \" The complaint implies that normally animals have no hashgachah . You're treating us like them. \" Part 6: Klalit and Pratit The difficulty: many verses say Hashem cares for animals : - פותח את ידך ומשביע לכל חי רצון ; - נותן לבהמה לחמה, לבני ערב אשר יקראו (said every morning); - הכפירים שאגים לטרף ולבקש מאל אכלם ; - The Gemara: יושב הקב״ה וזן מקרני ראמים עד ביצי כנים : from the huge re'em to lice eggs . The Rambam: no contradiction. These are providence over the species , not the individual. Animals: hashgachah klalit . Humans: hashgachah pratit . The taxi rank \" I own a taxi rank: 100 black cabs , all bought the same year, same condition, same mileage . I need to downsize to 80 . Do I agonise over which 20? This one I took there, that one I took there? No: whichever 20 first. I care about my taxis as a whole, not the individual cab.* That's klalit . Pratit is *an affinity to each individual cab .\" \" Lions: say the world needs a million lions and there are a few too many. Which ones die? Hashem doesn't get involved. He sustains the species : gives them what they need to exist .\" Why only humans? The Rambam: \" Like asking why Hashem created the earth , or why He gave intellect to humans and not animals . I can't answer. That's what Hashem did, and detailed, individual providence is for humans .\" \"Everything happens for a reason\"? \" This view is quite widespread in Jewish circles , and to be fair, it's not without source . Apparently the Ba'al Shem Tov held it (\" I haven't seen it inside \"), and some Chassidic masters : a leaf falls at its moment for a reason , like the Islamic school . The Rambam clearly didn't believe that. The Ramchal, lifshuto , didn't either. That's the view we take. \" The exception: what belongs to a human \" If you're a farmer, there's hashgachah on your individual sheep : if one dies, it affects you . Own a cat, and there's hashgachah on the cat. \" \" Lucky are the cats and dogs belonging to humans : depends on the owner! Lucky are those belonging to good humans. Why this cat ended up in a human home and not that one? That's randomness. \" Question: If a species goes extinct, is that providence on the species or on each one? \" On the species. For whatever reason, it's no longer needed . He doesn't sustain the species, so the individuals fall away one by one : the attention is to the species, klalit .\" Part 7: Not All Humans Equally (Moreh III:18) Why do humans have providence? Intellect. So, logically: the more developed a person, the greater the providence. ההשגחה האלהית אינה על כל פרטי מין האדם בשוה, אלא יש שוני בהשגחה עליהם כשוני שלמותם האנושית. Providence is not equal over all humans ; it varies as their human perfection varies , in character and intellect . Hence great hashgachah over the righteous : I will be with you, I will shield you ; רגלי חסידיו ישמר : He guards the feet of the devout . \" Even within the human race, not everyone has hashgachah. Someone who completely neglects God may be left to randomness. Amalek* said mikreh , everything is chance, *and they'll be left to chance. You don't want to be left to the whims of nature: come under Hashem's hashgachah . \" Part 8: What Does \"God Knows\" Mean? (Moreh III:20) The philosophers' objections Objection Argument New knowledge Hashem is perfect and lacks nothing, so He can't acquire anything new . Knowing a human act when it happens is new knowledge; knowing the transient means His knowledge changes , an imperfection. He can only know the constant and unchanging. Plurality Humans are many : knowing them involves plurality , but Hashem's unity demands oneness The Rambam's response: הסבה לכל הספקות שבאו… שהם הניחו יחס בין ידיעתנו לידיעתו יתעלה : the source of every doubt is that they assumed a likeness between our knowledge and His. \" From the human perspective you're a hundred percent right : if God thinks as humans do, you have major philosophical problems . But His knowledge is part of His essence. Not more clever, but qualitatively different. You can't compare it, so you can't ask these questions. \" In ch. 20: Hashem can't have new knowledge (an imperfection), nor many different thoughts . Yet in one knowledge He knows many things , and everything that will happen . The same key unlocks free will and foreknowledge If Hashem knows the past, present and future, He knows what I'll choose. So do I have free will? Thinker Answer Rav Saadia Gaon ( Emunot veDe'ot ; \"also the Islamic view\") Foreknowledge isn't determinative. \" I'll choose what to do tonight after this shiur , a full 50–50 choice , and Hashem knows what I'll freely choose \" Rabbeinu Bachya Beyond human understanding : rely on faith that they're reconciled Ralbag (R. Levi ben Gershon) and Avraham ibn Daud Full free will ; Hashem willingly curtailed His knowledge of future human acts, not an imperfection, because He chose it (\"what that means is a different question\") Chasdai Crescas Determinist, not fatalist: choices follow from genes, upbringing, parents, school, community . \" Put all of it in a database and you could predict what a person will do.\" Only Hashem knows the whole mixture. \" Severely limiting free choice; a difficult view \" The Rambam \" The best answer, in my view. \" We live under time : past, present, future, so the question is unanswerable for us . God's knowledge isn't ours. Summary Principle 10: Hashem (A) knows and (B) attends to human deeds and acts correspondingly : not \"Hashem has forsaken the earth.\" Thoughts too: the Rambam's וגו׳ points to וכל יצר מחשבת לבו . Passing thoughts carry no blame ; only developed thoughts of avodah zarah and zenut are punished; good intentions are rewarded . Three verses, three levels: individual , city , humanity (Hilchot Teshuvah 3). Hashgachah answers our deep need to be noticed , and makes every choice matter . Five views: Epicurus (none), Aristotle (heavens only), Ash'ariyya (everything, fatalism), a free-will variant , and the Torah's: providence over humans, chance for the rest . Animals: klalit (the species; the taxi rank ). Humans: pratit , plus what humans own . \" Everything happens for a reason \" for leaves is not the Rambam's or the Ramchal's view . Providence grows with perfection : the righteous are guarded ; the neglectful, like Amalek , are left to mikreh . God's knowledge is qualitatively different (Moreh III:20). That answers both the philosophers and free will vs foreknowledge . Coming Up Next week: Principle 11, reward and punishment . \" About three more shiurim on the principles, 11, 12 and 13 ,\" then Moreh Nevuchim on Tuesday nights . Next Monday: the new sefer of Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam .","Uwa2BUQ9AgQ":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: the close of Principle 8 ( Torah min HaShamayim ) and Principle 9 (the Torah will never be abrogated) Shiur: Part 14 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 20 January 2026 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Announcements Monday nights, from 2 February: the HaMaspik LeOvdei Hashem ( The Sufficient Guide to Serving Hashem ) of the Rambam's son , \"a fascinating book.\" Tuesday nights, after the Thirteen Principles: the Moreh Nevuchim , the Rambam's Guide for the Perplexed . Recap: Where We Are Principles Theme 1–5 What Hashem is and isn't 6–7 Hashem's relationship with humanity through prophecy , to unique individuals, and Moshe's unique prophecy 8 Revelation en masse , to all of Am Yisrael: the Torah we have is the Torah given to Moshe 3,300 years ago 9 Tonight: the fourth and final principle of revelation Part 1: Principle 8 Summed Up # Point The Rabbi 1 Our Torah is Moshe's Torah The script may have changed; keri u-ketiv and chaserot u-melot inconsistencies are acknowledged by Chazal . \" But the actual text is the same text. \" 2 Entirely min HaShamayim : dictated by Hashem to Moshe ; no autonomous authorship, not Moshe's, not anyone's after Biblical criticism , the old academic theory of four documents compiled together , \" now really shelved by most ,\" is complete kefirah . (The Gemara entertains Yehoshua for the last eight verses ; Ibn Ezra and some Rishonim felt one or two words perhaps weren't. \"But generally speaking, it's the same Torah.\") 3 All equally holy Some parts contain more principles : you wouldn't learn the Torah's principles from the kings of Edom rather than the Ten Commandments or Shema . \" That's fine to say. \" Not that one is holier 4 The Oral Torah is part of it Below \"The beauty of our Torah\": through exile to the four corners of the earth , Sephardim, Ashkenazim, Chassidim, Teimanim, Italians, Spanish and Portuguese , all with different customs, rites and rituals, the text of the Torah is identical . The Ten Commandments in the Temple \" Already in the Beit HaMikdash they said the Ten Commandments every day , and it was cancelled , because people began ascribing more holiness to them than the rest of the Torah.\" Fundamentals that contain everything else: yes. Holier: no. The Oral Torah: the class-notes analogy \"You go to a class and take notes . You understand them because you heard the class ; others won't . The Torah is Moshe's notes from Hashem , complemented by the oral tradition .\" Verse What only the Oral Torah tells you והיו לטטפת בין עיניך What are totafot ? בסכת תשבו שבעת ימים What's a sukkah? Its dimensions? How many walls? ( Halachah leMoshe miSinai ) Accepting the full divinity and importance of the written Torah while denying the Oral Torah is still a denial of Principle 8. That was the Karaites' error ( Kara'im , from Mikra , the written text): \" considered kefirah .\" Part 2: Why We Believe: The Kuzari's Proof (Rav Saadia Gaon gives it in brief; Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi develops it.) The King of the Khazars asks: why should I believe your religion over the others? אנכי ה׳ אלהיך אשר הוצאתיך מארץ מצרים Why \"who took you out of Egypt\" and not \"who created heaven and earth\"? Surely creation is greater! Because no one was there at creation to testify. But the Exodus, you saw . The argument Audience Can a claim of shared revelation be faked? 10, 20, 100 people \" A highly charismatic leader might pull the wool over their eyes : you forgot, but I took you out of Egypt . There are cults that do all kinds of things\" 600,000 men aged 20–60 , so with children, elderly and women about two million (\"whether exact or not doesn't matter\") No. \" The only religion that claimed, and has claimed ever since, a revelation en masse \" Shemot 19:4: אתם ראיתם אשר עשיתי למצרים, ואשא אתכם על כנפי נשרים ואבא אתכם אלי. Rashi: אתם ראיתם — לא מסורת היא בידכם, לא בדברים אני משגר אתכם, לא בעדים אני מעמיד לכם, אלא אתם ראיתם. Not a tradition handed to you, not words I send you, not witnesses I bring: you saw . \" Other religions have always claimed God appeared to the leader , their prophet , individuals . To make this claim to a whole people, it has to be true .\" And it's a book to your detriment: \"Until you receive it, you could strike a match on the seventh day . Now you'll be stoned for it. Who would accept that when they didn't hear it themselves? \" \"Perhaps someone later said your ancestors saw it?\" The unbroken chain. In the Introduction to Mishneh Torah (which we learned together) the Rambam lists the generations: \" only a hundred-plus generations , and we know who each one was , historically. When could it have been added? Who would have accepted it? \" The Ramban: אין אדם מנחיל שקר לבניו : no one bequeaths a lie to his children , something demonstrably false . Part 3: Principle 9: The Text היסוד התשיעי: הביטול. והוא שזאת תורת משה לא תבטל, ולא תבא תורה מאת ה׳ זולתה, ולא יתוסף בה ולא יגרע ממנה, לא בכתב ולא בפירוש, שנאמר: לא תסף עליו ולא תגרע ממנו. וכבר בארנו מה שצריך לבאר ביסוד הזה בהקדמת החבור הזה. This Torah of Moshe will never be nullified. No other Torah will come from Hashem. Nothing added, nothing removed , neither Written nor Oral : לא תסף עליו ולא תגרע ממנו (Va'etchanan, Re'eh). \" We've already explained this \": earlier in the Introduction, a prophet who comes to add is put to death ; \"the Rambam went into great detail.\" The censored text \"Read the square brackets, not the round ones . Start with HaBitul: abrogation.\" Because of Christendom this was considered offensive, so it was changed (the round brackets) to a version saying the Torah is from Hashem, \"but that's just Principle 8.\" \"We know from the original manuscripts that the correct text is the second one. It was censored: the whole basis of Christianity is that the Torah has been abrogated*: the Brit Chadashah , *Yirmiyahu's 'new covenant' , the basis of the New Testament .\" Two distinct parts Part A Part B Claim The Torah is eternal : never superseded by another Torah or faith The laws within the Torah never change Rejects Christianity and Islam , which didn't deny revelation to Israel but claimed a new revelation, through Jesus or Muhammad , overtaking it Adding to or subtracting from individual laws Note \" Arguably the primary part \" \" They don't necessarily follow from each other. I could accept no new faith and still allow change within Judaism. The Rambam holds both : since Hashem doesn't let the whole Torah change, every individual law is immutable \" Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal: Part A only Text Ani Ma'amin שזאת התורה לא תהא מוחלפת, ולא תהא תורה אחרת מאת הבורא יתברך שמו Yigdal לא יחליף האל ולא ימיר דתו, לעולמים לזולתו Neither mentions that each law is immutable. Part 4: Part A: No Other Torah \" This is why we find the term 'Old Testament' offensive. It implies there's an old one and a new one , which isn't true.\" Yirmiyahu 31: \"the new covenant\" הנה ימים באים נאם ה׳ וכרתי את בית ישראל ואת בית יהודה ברית חדשה… כי זאת הברית אשר אכרת את בית ישראל אחרי הימים ההם נאם ה׳: נתתי את תורתי בקרבם ועל לבם אכתבנה, והייתי להם לאלהים והם יהיו לי לעם. \"The most famous so-called proof for the New Testament, and so easily refuted.\" (Rav Saadia already dealt with it in Emunot veDe'ot : \" from the beginning, a contentious point .\") # The verses say So 1 With Beit Yisrael and Beit Yehudah Not for the whole world 2 נתתי את תורתי בקרבם The same Torah : My Torah, put within them 3 In that time war will cease Not 2,000 years ago : wars continued then and till today 4 כה אמר ה׳ נתן שמש לאור יומם, חקת ירח וכוכבים לאור לילה… אם ימשו החקים האלה מלפני… גם זרע ישראל ישבתו מהיות גוי לפני כל הימים As the sun, moon and stars endure, so Israel endures as a nation : the Torah won't change So what is the Brit Chadashah ? All the commentators: a covenant renewed so it's never broken again , in the times of Mashiach : a new loyal bond between Hashem and Am Yisrael. And logically: Christians and Muslims accept that God gave the Torah before 600,000 men . \" If He wanted to change it, why reveal Himself one-on-one to Jesus, one-on-one to Muhammad? Surely He'd call everyone together again , as He did at the beginning.\" Spot the difference The Rabbi read two texts aloud: \"Bonus if you can tell me where it's from. The prize? An extra five minutes of shiur at the end.\" Text 1 Text 2 God has spoken to mankind in many languages: through Judaism to Jews, Christianity to Christians, Islam to Muslims . God is the God of all humanity, but no single faith is or should be the faith of all humanity . God communicates in human language, but there are dimensions of the divine that must forever elude us . As Jews we believe God has made a covenant with a singular people , but that does not exclude the possibility of other peoples, cultures and faiths finding their own relationship with God within the shared frame of the Noahide laws . God is the God of all humanity, but between Babel and the end of days no single faith is the faith of all humanity. Robert: \"The first acknowledges that Christians and Muslims have some sort of prophecy; the second doesn't.\" \" Excellent. That is the key difference. \" Both by Rabbi Jonathan Sacks , The Dignity of Difference , 2002 . Text 1 : the first edition, August 2002 . \" A huge uproar, understandably \": it suggests God spoke through Christianity and Islam , and that they are equal to Judaism . Rabbi Rakow zichrono livrachah , Rav of Gateshead , called the book heresy and a deviation from authentic tradition , and demanded he repudiate it and withdraw it . Rabbi Sacks backed down ; the revised edition, end of 2002 , has Text 2 . The issue: Text 1 implies revelation to other faiths : against this principle . \"He was clearly trying to be diplomatic .\" Text 2 is fine. \" Dimensions that elude us \" = I can't explain why God appeared only to the Jews. Other faiths finding their own relationship with God : \" The Rambam basically said that : Hashem allowed Christianity and Islam to bring people closer to God . They believe in a God; that's great. But you cannot acknowledge that God revealed Himself to them. \" Part 5: Part B: The Laws Don't Change 1. Values and attitude \" This is why we're so opposed to Reform and Conservative : changing the Torah's laws to fit the society we live in , the liberal ideas of each century , goes against 9B .\" But: interpretation and attitude can change between generations, not the law itself . \" God forbid the Torah itself ever changes. What can change is our attitude to people who don't observe all of it .\" Question: Eliyahu on Har HaCarmel brought a sacrifice outside the Temple. Did the law change, or was it a hora'at sha'ah ? \" That's what the Rambam meant by 'we've already explained this.' \" A verified prophet says… Response \"This once\" , e.g. sacrifice outside , or break this Shabbat and go somewhere We must listen. A zaken who refuses, \" how can we break Shabbat? \", is put to death as a zaken mamre : rebelling against the prophet \"Shabbat no longer applies, forever\" We don't listen, and he is put to death Anything involving avodah zarah Never , not even once \"All learnt in the Gemara, accepted by all.\" Follow-up: Fire came from heaven: so Hashem accepted it? \" Clearly. Of course. \" But do we learn the rule independently, or from this story, \"marking our own homework\"? \" Once you're verified as a navi , anything you say from Hashem is accepted. Where exactly it stems from, I can't remember. We'll put it for another time. \" 2. The Sanhedrin's power to reinterpret \" Controversial, but sourced. Not everyone accepts it, but we have Chachamim who say it: the Supreme Court can reinterpret the Torah.\" Example:* ayin tachat ayin . Read without the Oral Torah: *your eye for my eye . Bava Kamma spends two full pages, about eight arguments , showing it means money : the value of the eye , however assessed. 3. Why can't a law change? Rav Yosef Albo ( Sefer HaIkkarim ) Proposed reason Albo's verdict Hashem doesn't change , so neither should His Torah Rejected Torah is emet , and truth is eternal Rejected Why rejected: laws do change within the Torah itself. Adam was given כל ירק עשב… לאכלה : no meat . After the Flood , meat was permitted. \" Hashem doesn't change, but the patient does. A doctor changes the prescription when the patient's diet and lifestyle change.\" Albo's answer: the Torah was given directly, en masse , so how could we know a change came from Hashem? Only two ways: Route Possible? A prophet greater than the original one No : Moshe is the greatest Evidence as convincing as, or more than, the original Theoretically yes \"So for Albo, if three million people again claimed a mass revelation telling them don't keep Shabbat , seemingly the law would change . Why shouldn't Hashem add or diminish as His wisdom decrees? I don't think the Rambam would agree. \" 4. Rabbinic additions: bal tosif How could the rabbis add so many mitzvot? The Torah forbids meat and milk ; Chazal added poultry . The Rambam (Hilchot Mamrim 2:9): \"A court may decree a prohibition on something permitted, binding for generations . What then is do not add, do not detract ? They may not add to the Torah's words or detract from them as if it were scriptural law. The Torah forbids cooking and eating meat and milk; fowl in milk is permitted by Torah law. If the court says fowl in milk is permitted, but **we forbid it as a decree : that's allowed . It's a safeguard , not an addition.\" Says Status \" The Torah permits chicken and milk; we forbid it as a fence \" Permitted : a gezeirah \" The Torah forbids chicken and milk \" Violates bal tosif \" That's why throughout the Gemara: d'Oraita or d'Rabbanan ? Always know the source ; don't be loose in what you say. \" 5. What will change: Nevi'im and Ketuvim Hilchot Megillah 2:18: כל ספרי הנביאים וכל הכתובים עתידין ליבטל לימות המשיח, חוץ ממגילת אסתר, הרי היא קיימת כחמשה חומשי תורה וכהלכות של תורה שבעל פה שאינן בטלין לעולם. All of Nevi'im and Ketuvim will be nullified in Messianic times, except Megillat Esther , which endures like the Five Books and the Oral Torah . The Ra'avad disagrees (\" you've misunderstood it \"). But the Rambam codifies it. This principle covers only the Chumash and the Oral Torah. Question: You've often said the other fasts are divrei kabbalah , not quite d'Rabbanan . So Ta'anit Esther stays, and the others disappear? \" Correct. We won't do them any more. That's exactly what the Rambam is saying. \" 6. Midrashim on change in the future Midrash Tehillim 146 ( \"not clear who wrote it; apparently 8th–9th century; after the Gemara, authoritative to some extent\" ), on ה׳ מתיר אסורים : not only releasing the bound (the morning berachah ; prisoners and hostages) but permitting the forbidden : יש אומרים: כל הבהמה שנטמאת בעולם הזה, מטהר אותה הקב״ה לעתיד לבוא : non-kosher animals become kosher . אין איסור גדול מן הנדה… ולעתיד לבוא הוא מתירה : \"Hashem will permit niddah .\" \" Why? I've got no idea. Really quite shocking .\" Vayikra Rabbah , \" much more authoritative ,\" on the sacrifices in the future . \" No shortage of rabbinic sources implying some change in Messianic times .\" The Rambam doesn't imply that. He says Nevi'im and Ketuvim end then, but the Chumash doesn't : \" the thrust everywhere in the Rambam: in Messianic times, law continues, Torah continues. Kosher animals won't change. \" How he'd deal with these Midrashim, reject or read metaphorically : \"I don't know exactly.\" Shabbetai Tzvi : \" this is why the false Mashiach was so successful. He used these texts : how can you do this? — Mashiach is here, so the laws can change . If it's Messianic times, anything can change. \" Question: \"So in Messianic times we can eat pork?\" \" There's an opinion that says so, yes. \" \"So why not now?\" \" We're not in Messianic times. \" Summary Principle 8: same Torah , wholly divine (biblical criticism is kefirah ), equally holy , with the Oral Torah ( the Karaites denied it). The Kuzari's proof: אנכי… אשר הוצאתיך : a revelation to two million people , אתם ראיתם (Rashi), an unbroken chain of 100+ generations , אין אדם מנחיל שקר לבניו . Principle 9 (the uncensored text, HaBitul ): (A) no other Torah , rejecting Christianity and Islam ; (B) no individual law changes . Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal cover only A . Yirmiyahu's Brit Chadashah is refuted from its own verses; Rabbi Sacks's Dignity of Difference revision shows the line: other faiths may relate to God; they did not receive revelation . Part B: attitudes and interpretation shift, laws don't ( Reform/Conservative ); a prophet may suspend, never abolish ( Eliyahu ); the Sanhedrin reinterprets ( ayin tachat ayin ); Albo grounds immutability in the evidence of mass revelation ; rabbinic fences must be labelled d'Rabbanan . Nevi'im and Ketuvim (except Esther ) end in Messianic times ( the Rambam vs the Ra'avad ); Midrashim of future change are at odds with the Rambam , and fuelled Shabbetai Tzvi . Coming Up Next week: Principle 10: Hashem knows the deeds of human beings , the start of the final category, reward and punishment .","uye93O77XFI":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 7 (the uniqueness of Moshe's prophecy) and the opening of Principle 8 ( Torah min HaShamayim ) Shiur: Part 13 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 13 January 2026 (on a patchy connection: \"if I cut out, let me know\") These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Recap The Thirteen Principles fall into three categories. 1–5: Hashem Himself. 6–9: revelation , the relationship between Hashem and humans. Last week, Principle 6 : there is such a thing as prophecy : Hashem communicates with intellectually and morally developed human beings . \" The principle isn't that the words of the prophets are true. It's that Hashem relates to humans .\" Tonight: Principle 7 quickly, then Principle 8, Torah min HaShamayim , \"a very important principle, with lots to discuss.\" Seven is the longest principle; eight the second longest. Part 1: Principle 7: The Text היסוד השביעי: נבואת משה רבנו עליו השלום. והוא שנאמין כי הוא אביהם של כל הנביאים אשר היו לפניו ואשר קמו אחריו, כולם הם תחתיו במעלה. Moshe surpasses the prophets before him (Avraham, Yitzchak…) and all who came after. \" This is interesting. As a principle of faith , the Rambam asserts Moshe is greater than all who will come after. So Mashiach, whoever and whenever he will be, won't be on Moshe's level. Not everyone agreed , as we'll see.\" והוא היה הנבחר מכל מין האדם, אשר השיג מידיעתו יתברך יותר מכל מה שהשיג או ישיג שום אדם שנמצא או שימצא. He understood God as much as possible : more than anyone has or ever will . Phrase Meaning הגיע התעלותו מן האנושיות עד המעלה המלאכותית, ונכלל במעלת המלאכים He rose to an angelic level , within the category of angels לא נשאר מסך שלא קרעו No barrier he didn't break through ולא מנעו מונע גופני Nothing physical held him back from connecting to Hashem ולא נתערב לו שום חסרון בין רב למעט No deficiency , large or small ונתבטלו ממנו הכוחות הדמיוניות והחושיות His physical senses nullified : hence 40 days and nights without food or drink, three times ונבדל כח המתעורר והמשתוקק His appetitive drive removed : he controlled it ונשאר שכל בלבד \" Pure intellect. He had a human body, but he was essentially an intellect walking within a human body .\" שהיה מדבר עם השם יתברך בלא אמצעיות מן המלאכים He spoke with Hashem directly, with no angelic intermediary (The Rambam's note that this needs long explanation, \"I'll do it elsewhere,\" we read last week; he did it in Moreh, Section Two.) Question: Is that prophecy, or just closeness? \"Other prophets foretold the future; Moshe didn't really. He was close to Hashem. Is that nevuah ?\" \" Remember the key thing from last week. The essence of prophecy is not (A) telling other people information , nor (B) knowing the future . Often it includes those. The essence is a connection with Hashem through which you understand things about godliness , the philosophy and theology of Hashem, more than you ever did before .\" Part 2: The Four Differences ואחזור לכונת זה היסוד השביעי, ואומר שנבואת משה רבנו עליו השלום נבדלת מנבואת כל הנביאים בארבעה דברים. \"Spoke\": a borrowed term \" When we say Hashem spoke : remember Principle 3. Hashem has no mouth ; there are no sound waves , since nothing physical about Him emits them. 'God spoke to Moshe' means a vision, a recognition, an understanding of God. \" The source: Bamidbar 12 ויאמר שמעו נא דברי: אם יהיה נביאכם ה׳ במראה אליו אתודע, בחלום אדבר בו. לא כן עבדי משה, בכל ביתי נאמן הוא. פה אל פה אדבר בו, ומראה ולא בחידת, ותמנת ה׳ יביט. ומדוע לא יראתם לדבר בעבדי במשה? Hashem to Aharon and Miriam : other prophets, in a vision or a dream . Not so My servant Moshe : mouth to mouth , openly, not in riddles ; he beholds the image of Hashem . Why weren't you afraid to speak against him? # Other prophets Moshe Rambam's proof 1. Intermediary Through an intermediary , so the prophecy comes bechidot , as a riddle , and must be worked on Directly , no riddles פה אל פה אדבר בו 2. Awake or asleep Only asleep : בחלום הלילה , בחזיון לילה ; by day only אחר שתפול תרדמה על האדם , when all sensation is gone and only the mind works ( מחזה ומראה : במראות אלהים ) By day, awake , standing between the two keruvim : ונועדתי לך שם ומראה ולא בחידות , ונועדתי לך שם 3. Fear Lose their strength , fear and trepidation , lose all sense of where they are As a friend speaks to a friend פה אל פה 4. Initiative Wait until Hashem wishes to give prophecy Whenever Moshe wants (once he's prepared himself ) Hashem speaks to him On the intermediary: \" The more intermediaries between your vision and God, the greater the effort to understand the message. The Kabbalah and the Ramchal speak of the aspaklaria , a lens : the prophets see through dull, unpolished lenses and must decipher . Moshe: nothing in between. \" (\"This is why Miriam and Aharon spoke against Moshe… let's not go into that now.\") Questions Tehillim 99:6: משה ואהרן בכהניו ושמואל בקראי שמו, קראים אל ה׳ והוא יענם. Some commentators say Shmuel is being compared to Moshe and Aharon. \"The Gemara learns שמואל בדורו כמשה בדורו . It's making a point: don't think you only listen to Moshe and Aharon and Shmuel, a later and lower generation , less. Give him the same kavod , listen to him equally. It doesn't mean Shmuel is greater than Moshe. Of course not. \" Bilaam: In Balak, did Bilaam lose his strength and fall? \" Where did you get that he didn't? He stands on the hills and has his prophecies. He must have fallen to the floor.\" Part 3: Why So Much Emphasis? The Rambam is elaborate on both 7 and 8 : Moshe's prophecy is unique , and the Torah today is exactly as in Moshe's day . \" I imagine there's an element of rebutting the Muslims and the Christians , who claimed the Torah changed . No prophet can arise like Moshe , so it's not logical to substitute the Torah of Moshe with the New Testament or the Quran . So-called prophets who called themselves prophets can't replace it.\" The verse, and how the Rambam reads it ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה אשר ידעו ה׳ פנים אל פנים (Devarim 34:10) Word The Rambam's reading קם The past : none arose עוד The future : none will \" That's not definitely the meaning of the verse , but it's certainly how the Rambam reads it.\" Before Moshe After Moshe The Rambam None like him None ever, including Mashiach Rav Yosef Albo None like him Mashiach may be greater The Arizal and Chabad, again The Arizal: Moshe was the greatest prophet , but in Kabbalistic matters Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai was greater . The first Lubavitcher Rebbe (the Ba'al HaTanya) develops this. \" The Rambam would clearly say that's complete heresy. I don't really understand what it means : surely part of nevuah is understanding whatever Kabbalah teaches , since it's teaching about God. I'm just telling you what's written. \" Part 4: Principle 7 in the Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal Text Assessment Ani Ma'amin שנבואת משה רבנו עליו השלום היתה אמיתית, ושהוא היה אב לנביאים, לקודמים לפניו ולבאים אחריו \"His prophecy was true\" isn't the principle. \"Father of the prophets\" is the main point . But it misses the qualitative difference Yigdal לא קם בישראל כמשה עוד, נביא ומביט את תמונתו Emphasises one of the four differences : a direct vision , no intermediary \"They're not always precise.\" Part 5: Principle 8: Torah min HaShamayim \" I wouldn't say controversial, but there's a lot to dissect. As we read, count how many points he makes.\" היסוד השמיני: היות התורה מן השמים. והוא שנאמין כי כל התורה הזאת הנתונה על ידי משה רבנו עליו השלום, היא כולה מפי הגבורה. כלומר שהגיעה אליו כולה מאת ה׳, בענין שנקרא על דרך השאלה ״דבור״, ואין ידוע איך הגיעה אליו אלא הוא, משה עליו השלום, שהגיעה לו. The whole Torah given through Moshe is entirely from Hashem. \"Speech\" is a borrowed term . No one knows how it reached him except Moshe himself . וכי הוא היה כסופר שקורין לפניו והוא כותב כולה, מאורעותיה וסיפוריה ומצוותיה. ולפיכך נקרא מחוקק. Moshe was like a scribe being dictated to : events, stories, commandments. Hence he's called מחוקק , \" like a copyist .\" No verse is \"less\" Torah ואין הפרש בין ״ובני חם כוש ומצרים ופוט וכנען״, ״ושם אשתו מהיטבאל בת מטרד בת מי זהב״, ״ותמנע היתה פילגש״, ובין ״אנכי ה׳ אלהיך״ ו״שמע ישראל״. כי הכל מפי הגבורה, והכל תורת ה׳ תמימה טהורה קדושה אמת. \"Seemingly superfluous\" verses The heart of the Torah The sons of Cham Anochi Hashem Elokecha Mehetavel bat Matred bat Mei Zahav , wife of one of the kings of Edom (Vayishlach) Shema Yisrael Timna, concubine of Eliphaz son of Esav \"No difference: every word is from the Almighty.\" (The Rabbi: \"God forbid I say they are superfluous; they seem so.\") Standing for the Ten Commandments The Rambam's famous teshuvah: he opposed standing for the Aseret HaDibrot : it implies one part of the Torah is more important , \" and that's not true .\" \" Ashkenazim do, and the Spanish and Portuguese communities do. When we stand in our community, I say every time : we're not standing because it's more important, but because within it are principles from which we learn the rest of the Torah : a way to remember the whole Torah . Anyone who says it's more important is going against this principle. \" The worst heretic וזה שאומר שכמו אלה הפסוקים והסיפורים משה ספרם מדעתו, הנה הוא אצל חכמינו ונביאינו כופר ומגלה פנים יותר מכל הכופרים, לפי שחשב שיש בתורה לב וקליפה, ושאלה דברי הימים והסיפורים אין תועלת בהם ושהם מאת משה רבנו עליו השלום. Whoever says Moshe wrote such verses on his own is, for our Sages and prophets, a heretic, more brazen than all heretics : he treats the Torah as having a core and a shell , with useless stories Moshe added . וזה ענין ״אין תורה מן השמים״, אמרו: הוא האומר שכל התורה מפי הגבורה חוץ מפסוק זה, שלא אמרו הקב״ה אלא משה מפי עצמו אמרו. וזהו ״כי דבר ה׳ בזה״. Denying Torah min HaShamayim isn't only saying the Torah wasn't given; it's saying \"all of it, except this one verse Moshe added.\" That is \"he has disgraced the word of Hashem.\" (On the text: \"ה׳ יתברך וינזר ממאמר הכופרים — this is a different text; it should read something else. He means: may Hashem be exalted above the words of the heretics.\") אלא כל דבור ודבור מן התורה יש בהם חכמות ופלאים למי שהבינם, ולא הושג תכלית חכמתה, ״ארוכה מארץ מדה ורחבה מני ים״. ואין לאיש אלא להלוך בעקבות דוד משיח אלהי יעקב… Every word holds wisdom and wonders for one who understands; its depth is \"longer than the earth, wider than the sea.\" Follow King David (in Tehillim) in valuing every verse. The Oral Torah וכן פירושה המקובל גם כן מפי הגבורה. וזה שאנו עושים היום תבנית הסוכה והלולב והשופר והציצית והתפילין וזולתם, הוא בעצמו התבנית שאמר השם למשה ואמר לנו, והוא מוסר השליחות נאמן בשליחותו. The received explanation is also from Hashem. The form of sukkah, lulav, shofar, tzitzit, tefillin we make today is the very form Hashem told Moshe at Sinai 3,330+ years ago . והמאמר המורה על היסוד הזה הוא מה שנאמר: ויאמר משה בזאת תדעון כי ה׳ שלחני לעשות את כל המעשים האלה כי לא מלבי. Moshe to Korach's followers : \"not from my own heart.\" \" I'm just a copyist, passing the message on. \" Part 6: The Four Points of Principle 8 A note on the text: \"The real text says כי כל התורה הזאת המצויה בידינו: all the Torah in our hands. This text is a bit corrupted; in future I'll use a different one.\" # Point In the Rabbi's words 1 The Torah we have is Moshe's Torah \"The Torah we read Monday, Thursday and Shabbat is the one given to Moshe 3,000 years ago \" 2 Wholly divine \" Not even one word can be attributed to Moshe's own authorship\" 3 All parts equally holy \" No distinction between parts of the Torah\" 4 The Oral Torah is from Sinai \" How to make tefillin, how to make a sukkah \": passed down generation to generation In the Ani Ma'amin and Yigdal Text Covers Ani Ma'amin שכל התורה המצויה עתה בידינו היא הנתונה למשה רבנו עליו השלום Point 1 only (the correct Rambam text); misses 2, 3 and 4 , \"unless kol haTorah includes the Oral Torah\" Yigdal תורת אמת נתן לעמו אל, על יד נביאו נאמן ביתו Point 2 (divine origin, through faithful Moshe) \" They're abbreviations; they're not the main thing. \" Robert's question \"I'm not saying I believe this, but: could one hold the Rambam's Torah was unchanged since Moshe, but scribal errors have crept in since? I believe there's one letter difference with the Teimani Sefer Torah.\" \" Logically: when would more mistakes creep in, before the Rambam or after? \" Before. \" True. So it's unlikely. But keep the question in mind ; you'll see why.\" Part 7: Point 1: \"The Same Torah\" In academic terms: the Masoretic text is the true text , as against the Samaritan text and the Dead Sea Scrolls , \" and there are clear differences .\" \" I used to have a congregant , I won't say which shul, who would follow my Torah reading from his Samaritan copy , and after Shabbat point out the differences .\" Masoretic Samaritan Bereishit 2:2 ויכל אלהים ביום השביעי ביום הששי Rashi asks: surely He finished on the sixth day ? The Midrash: on the seventh Hashem created rest : \"a deep idea.\" The Samaritan text just says the sixth . \" Important differences. \" Only the words Not what this principle requires: - Te'amim , the cantillation: \" a machloket among our Sages when they came about; some say as late as the 8th or 9th century \"; - Parashah petuchah / *setumah , the open and closed sections; - Tagin , the crowns on the letters; - The script ( ketav ): \"they may have had a different script. *\" \" We're concerned with the exact words. \" But even that is difficult 1. Chaser and maleh (Kiddushin 30a) The Gemara counts the middle letter / word / verse of the Torah. A rabbi asks which half a letter belongs to. \"Bring a Sefer Torah and count!\" אינהו בקיאי בחסירות ויתירות, אנן לא בקיאינן : they (earlier generations) were expert in defective and full spellings; we are not. (Said in the 300s–400s.) Word Chaser (defective) Maleh (full) Yaakov יעקב יעקוב , e.g. the end of Bechukotai Asher hotzeiticha ( Aseret HaDibrot ) without yud with yud Halachah: a Sefer Torah with chaser written as *maleh (or vice versa) is not pasul . \" *I'm not talking about extra words or letters. Let me be very clear. \" Pitchei Teshuvah cites a posek who argued that since we aren't certain what's chaser and maleh , perhaps one shouldn't make a berachah on the Torah reading . \" An extreme view; we don't hold that. But it shows that already in the Gemara there were differences .\" 2. Tannaim with different texts Verse Our Torah Rabbi Meir's Torah וירא אלהים את כל אשר עשה והנה טוב מאד מאד (very) מות ( death ): טוב מות ויעש… להם כתנות עור עור with ayin (leather) אור with aleph ( light ) \" Of course we didn't take those on. And it's incredible : Torahs across the world, Sephardim, Ashkenazim, Teimanim , are almost identical , a very few minor differences, nothing substantially different in meaning .\" 3. Keri and ketiv Written one way, read another . Usually minor, but some major: in Ki Tavo's curses , ישגלנה written, ישכבנה read. The Radak: variant texts arose during the first exile ; when the Anshei Knesset HaGedolah restored the Torah they found small differences and kept both , one written , one read . The Rabbi's conclusion: \" To say every single letter today is entirely the same as Moshe received: I think that's difficult , and I don't know that the Rambam meant it . We see chaser and maleh , keri and ketiv , Tannaim with different texts. Of course we believe in Torah min HaShamayim , the whole idea. But the vav in Yaakov? I can't imagine that's part of this principle. \" Part 8: Point 2 Begins: Did Moshe Write All of It? Bava Batra 15a: the last eight verses , which describe Moshe's death . How could Moshe write them? View One opinion Yehoshua wrote them , not Moshe The other Moshe wrote them, with tears (To be continued next week.) Summary Principle 7: Moshe is father of all prophets before and after : angelic , no barrier, pure intellect , no intermediary . For the Rambam, not even Mashiach will equal him ( Albo disagrees). Prophecy's essence is connection to Hashem , not foretelling the future. Four differences (Bamidbar 12): no intermediary (no riddles, no aspaklaria ); awake, by day ; no fear , as friend to friend; at will . Why the emphasis: a rebuttal of Christian and Muslim claims that the Torah was superseded or changed . קם = past, עוד = future is the Rambam's reading . The Arizal/Tanya claim about Rashbi: \" the Rambam would call it heresy .\" Principle 8, four points: our Torah is Moshe's ; wholly divine ; all equally holy (hence the Rambam against standing for the Ten Commandments); the Oral Torah from Sinai . \"The same Torah\" means the words , not te'amim , tagin , sections or script. Even so, chaser/maleh , Rabbi Meir's Torah and keri/ketiv make letter-perfect identity \"difficult,\" and probably not the Rambam's intent . The last eight verses : Yehoshua , or Moshe in tears . Coming Up Next week: continuing Principle 8 : was every word written by Moshe from Hashem? , then equal holiness and the Oral Torah , and on to Principle 9 . And \" keep Robert's question in mind .\"","McmeLl0OwRU":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 6 (prophecy), with the opening of Principle 7 (Moshe's prophecy) Shiur: Part 12 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 6 January 2026 (the first shiur of the new term, the week after Asarah b'Tevet) These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Recap: Where the Thirteen Stand The first section of the Introduction was about Olam HaBa . The Rambam (in his mid-twenties ) then sets out the fundamentals a person needs for a portion in Olam HaBa . Last term we did the first five : # Principle 1 Hashem exists , is omnipotent and involved in the world 2 Yichud : His oneness, in the refined sense 3 אין לו גוף ואינו דמות הגוף : no body. The only principle with no source in Torah, Tanach or Chazal 4 Hashem precedes all ; the correct view is creation from nothing , not an eternal world 5 Serve nothing and no one besides Hashem: avodah zarah Three categories (Rav Yosef Albo) Principles Theme 1–5 Hashem Himself : existence, unity, incorporeality, creation, sole worship 6–9 The relationship between Hashem and humanity : prophecy (6), Moshe's unique prophecy (7), Torah (8, 9), revelation and instruction 10–13 Reward and punishment , in the broad sense Tonight begins the second category. Part 1: The Principle Itself היסוד הששי: הנבואה. והוא שידע האדם שזה מין האדם ימצאו בהם בעלי טבעים ומדות מעולות מאד ושלמות גדולה, ונפשותיהם נכונות עד שיקבלו צורת השכל. אחר כן ידבק אותו השכל האנושי בשכל הפועל, ונאצל ממנו עליו אצילות נכבד. ואלה הם הנביאים, וזו היא הנבואה, וזה ענינה. Translation: - Within the human race there are people of exceptional nature and character and great perfection , whose souls are so prepared that they fully realise their intellect . - That human intellect then cleaves to the Active Intellect . - From it, a great atzilut flows down upon them. (\"How would we translate atzilut ? A great flow . A great divesting down .\") - These are the prophets. This is prophecy. וביאור יסוד זה על בוריו ארוך מאד, ואין כוונתנו להביא מופת על כל יסוד מהם… לפי שזהו כלל החכמה כולה, אבל הזכרתה דרך סיפור בלבד. ומקראי התורה מעידים על נבואת נביאים הרבה. To explain this fully would take very long. My purpose isn't to prove each principle, since this is a whole science . I mention it only briefly . The Torah's verses attest to many prophets . What the principle is, and isn't \" The sixth principle is not that the words of the Nevi'im are true. That belongs to principles 8 and 9 , that the Torah is true and divine. The point here is that there is a concept of prophecy : Hashem speaks to perfected humans. \" Formulation Says Accurate? Ani Ma'amin (late 1400s/early 1500s, author unclear): אני מאמין באמונה שלמה שכל דברי נביאים אמת The prophets' words are true \" That's not the point of the principle. That's not correct. \" Yigdal: שפע נבואתו נתנו אל אנשי סגולתו ותפארתו Hashem gave His flow of prophecy to His chosen, special people \" Much more precise. \" Why it's a principle in its own right Hashem is involved in the world. The Ramban, end of Parashat Bo : the whole purpose of Mitzrayim and the ten plagues . Pharaoh believed in a divine power , but not one that communicates with and cares about humanity . Hence כי אני ה׳ בקרב הארץ : I am Hashem in the midst of the land. \" God didn't create the world and leave us. He gave us a Torah (later principles) and also constant instruction , at least until the destruction of the Beit HaMikdash .\" Part 2: Dreams, Ruach HaKodesh and Prophecy People often confuse prophecy with dreams and with Ruach HaKodesh . Their only common ground: each reveals information from a lofty spiritual source. (This three-level framework is the Ramchal's; see the caveat below.) 1. Dreams: \"one-sixtieth of prophecy\" (Gemara) Why we wash our hands in the morning: Reason Source Our hands moved and touched parts of the body overnight; wash before prayer The Rambam ( al derech hapeshat ) Like the Kohanim washing before the Temple service: preparing to serve Hashem The Rashba At night soul and body part ; the soul goes heavenward to recharge . The vacuum draws tumah , so we wash the extremities The Zohar (hence אלהי נשמה שנתת בי טהורה : \" You've given me back my soul\". \"That's not the simple meaning.\") The Ben Ish Chai: really we should wash the whole body daily; Chazal limited it to the hands, the body's \"tips\" (and \"really the toes too: a different discussion\"). The Ramchal on dreams: in the body the soul is suppressed . Freed at night, it meets spiritual entities , which may tell it about the future , and that is transmitted down : a dream is a fuzzy transmission. (The Rambam: \"To the best of my knowledge he doesn't discuss how dreams work.\") But not every heavenly force is good: malachei chabalah convey false, fabricated messages . Hence the contradiction: Zechariah: חלומות השוא ידברו : dreams speak falsehood. Yet the end of Bereishit is all dreams: Yosef's, Pharaoh's, everyone's. Answer: it depends on the source. Rule Why A dream follows its interpretation We can't know whether its source is true A repeated dream is taken seriously ויישן ויחלם שנית : why does the Torah stress that Pharaoh slept and dreamt again ? Interpret positively Even a true, bad dream can be changed by reframing it \" I remember one person who every week would call us together as a beit din to annul his dreams. Zichrono livrachah. Some people get very afraid of dreams.\" 2. Ruach HaKodesh : divine intuition Talmai's 72 Sages (from Asarah b'Tevet last week): each translating separately, they were all divinely inspired to make the same changes . \" That's not prophecy. They weren't having a prophetic experience. It was Ruach HaKodesh : divine intuition. \" The Ramchal, Derech Hashem : יקרה שיושפע בלב האדם שפע שיעמידהו על תוכן ענין מהענינים … a flow suddenly gives you understanding of something. Sometimes you won't notice it : אלא כמי שנופלת מחשבה בלבבו : like a thought just fell into your heart. \" A brilliant idea in Yiddish is an einfall : it fell from above . You're confused, and suddenly it all falls into place , and you feel divinely inspired . I think humans experience that. But it's completely different from prophecy.\" 3. Prophecy: an experience Dreams Ruach HaKodesh Prophecy When Asleep Awake Awake (stripped of the senses) What Information, true or false Intuition , a thought that \"falls in\" A Godly experience Clarity Fuzzy You may not even notice Total clarity Essence A message An insight Becoming a different person \" It's a big mistake to think prophecy is Hashem giving you extra information. That's a possible side effect . The essence is a Godly experience. \" Proof: the Gemara (Megillah) implies at least 1.2 million prophets (below). \" How many books of prophets do we have? We can count them on our fingers. We don't have their messages, because the message isn't the essence. \" And it doesn't endure: \" Prophecy for half an hour today doesn't mean tomorrow. You may have it once in your life .\" Hence the Bnei HaNevi'im : \" like medical school or law school, there was literally a school for prophets , where they practised receiving prophecy .\" The Rambam, Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah 7:1: בעת שתנוח עליו הרוח תתערב נפשו במעלת המלאכים הנקראים אישים, ויהפך לאיש אחר, ויבין בדעתו שאינו כמו שהיה אלא שנתעלה על מעלת שאר בני אדם החכמים, כמו שנאמר בשאול: והתנבית עמם ונהפכת לאיש אחר. When the spirit rests on him, his soul joins the level of the angels called ishim ; he becomes a different person , and knows he is not as he was : as said to Shaul : you will prophesy with them and become another man . The Ramchal, Derech Hashem : higher than both is prophecy: שיגיע האדם ויתקשר בבורא יתברך שמו, ויתדבק בו דביקות ממש in a way he feels the cleaving , with no doubt. והנה עיקר הנבואה הוא השג הדבקות והקשר הזה עודו בחיים : its essence is attaining this cleaving and connection while still alive . What is it like? \" I've got no idea, and it's very hard to describe. The best analogy: you're led blindfolded into a room. For hours you grope around, feeling this and that, trying to work out where you are. Then the blindfold comes off and you see everything. Everything falls into place. That's nevuah . \" The Rambam (Moreh): comparing prophecy to philosophy is like trying to pour all the water in the world into one jug . \"I could spend hours describing this room to you with your eyes closed, the colours, the books, the furniture; it can't compare to opening your eyes .\" \"All the questions, the philosophy, the investigation we go through in life trying to understand Torah and God: suddenly your eyes are opened . Not to everything , it's limited. But in whatever you're shown, you have a whole different understanding of what life is about. \" Part 3: Who Can Become a Prophet? (Moreh 2:32) The Moreh has three sections: God, prophecy, reward and punishment, \"really the thirteen principles in their three categories.\" Section two is mostly about prophecy. Among those who believe in prophecy there are three views: View 1: the masses ( da'at hamon ) שהאל יתעלה בוחר מי שירצה מבני אדם… ומשלחו Hashem simply chooses whom He wants. \" You wake up one morning and Hashem says: you're a prophet.\" אין לדעתם הפרש בין היות האיש ההוא חכם או סכל, זקן או נער : wisdom, age, experience make no difference . (He must be a good, decent person : \"Hashem isn't giving prophecy to the wicked.\") The Rambam: the view of the ignorant. It is also Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi's view in the Kuzari . View 2: the philosophers (incl. Aristotle, \"who believed in prophecy\") הנבואה היא שלמות מסוימת בטבע האדם : prophecy is a natural perfection , reached only after great training . The person perfect in intellect and character , with a developed imaginative faculty : הכרח שיתנבא : he must prophesy. View 3: the Torah's view (the Rambam's) היא כדעת הפילוסופית בדיוק, חוץ מדבר אחד : exactly the philosophers' view, except one thing : one who is fit and prepared may still not prophesy , לפי החפץ האלהי : according to the Divine will. (He then works through the verses that seem to say otherwise.) Requirements Then? \"The miracle is…\" 1. The masses / Kuzari A good person Hashem chooses …that you get prophecy 2. Philosophers Character + intellect + imagination Automatic No miracle 3. Torah / Rambam Character + intellect + imagination Still Hashem's choice …that you don't get it \" Views one and three are miktzeh el katzeh : from one end to the other. \" For the Rambam, prophecy doesn't just fall on a person ; you constantly train and perfect yourself . The most consistent thread in all the Rambam's writing: the purpose of humanity is perfecting the intellect and the middot . Mitzvot train the character ; study of Torah and the sciences refines the intellect . Question (class): View 1 seems to equate prophecy with getting a message, which we said isn't prophecy. \"Not necessarily. Hashem may choose you just to give you the experience : you now understand what other humans don't , without telling anyone. Sometimes you'll be sent with a message. The dispute isn't about what prophecy is for; it's about who gets it. \" Part 4: The Three Ingredients Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah 7:1: אין הנבואה חלה אלא על חכם גדול בחכמה, גבור במדותיו, ולא יהא יצרו מתגבר עליו בדבר בעולם, אלא הוא מתגבר בדעתו על יצרו תמיד, בעל דעה רחבה נכונה עד מאד. Moreh 2:36: prophecy is reached only through השלמות בחכמות העיוניות (the theoretical sciences ), perfecting the middot to their best possible state, together with the perfection of the imaginative faculty ( koach hamedameh ) by nature. Ingredient What it means The Rabbi 1. Intellect Torah inside out , a huge talmid chacham , and the sciences of the world ( not just Torah : \"otherwise he'd say Torah\") \" To understand God, study what He's given us : the Torah, and the world He made . From His handiwork you understand how He operates.\" Basic sciences , \"not technology\" 2. Character Never ruled by the yetzer : no anger, no lust of the eyes, no pull of financial gain \" We all have dips. Even such a person fluctuates , which is why sometimes he has prophecy and sometimes not \" 3. Imagination ( ba'al de'ah rechavah ) The ability to think broadly : you see a boat on a river and a bird flying , and imagine a boat flying \" I've never understood why this is essential, but the Rambam reiterates it in many places. Whether that means reading lots of sci-fi , I'm not sure he means that.\" A caveat from the Rabbi: \" I've probably shoehorned the Ramchal into the Rambam. Here the Rambam seems to equate Ruach HaKodesh with nevuah . The dreams / Ruach HaKodesh / prophecy distinction was straight from the Ramchal . I'm not sure the Rambam agrees. I need to look into that properly.\" The 1.2 million prophets Megillah 14a: הרבה נביאים עמדו להם לישראל, כפלים כיוצאי מצרים : double those who left Egypt . 600,000 × 2 = 1.2 million . \" Really? 1.2 million people reached the perfection the Rambam describes? Perhaps. Perhaps we don't know them. I think it's difficult ; the Rambam may not take it literally. But it does show prophecy isn't bearing a message : we don't have 1.2 million books.\" Part 5: Must a Prophet Be Jewish? The Kuzari: only a Jew, only in Israel Five levels: inanimate, plant, animal, human , which all the Rishonim accept, and a fifth: the Jew . The Jew is inherently different. So prophecy is a gift, only for a Jew , and only in Eretz Yisrael . Not even a convert : \"a full-fledged Jew for everything, but an inferior kind of soul \" for this purpose. Question (class): How does the Kuzari explain Bilaam? The Gemara: Hashem gave the nations prophets so they couldn't complain you had prophets and we didn't . The Kuzari would need a special exception . \" I can't see him saying it here. I'll check and post it on the group .\" The Rambam: any human \" I don't know anywhere the Rambam says the Jew is inherently different, and I don't believe he would. \" Prophecy isn't a gift : reach the perfection and you receive it, Jew and non-Jew equally . He always speaks of min ha'enoshi , the human race. But: Jews are more aligned to perfection , because more mitzvot means more refinement . \"More susceptible, not exclusive .\" The difficulty: Har Sinai At Matan Torah , the whole people heard Hashem ; the Midrash says they fainted , which many read as prophecy . How did a nation of slaves suddenly reach that perfection? \" According to the Rambam it wasn't full-blown prophecy; it was a kind of philosophical revelation. Obviously Har Sinai fits better with the Kuzari's view .\" The prophetic experience itself The Rambam and the Ramchal: the prophet is stripped of his physical senses ; it feels as if the body is turned inside out ; he can't do anything else and must lie flat . Part 6: Is There Prophecy Today? The Gemara: with the death of Chaggai, Zechariah and Malachi , early in the Second Temple, the spirit of prophecy departed . For the Kuzari, simple: we're in exile ; Hashem no longer gives it. For the Rambam , if someone reaches the three levels, why not? Moreh 2:36: the imaginative faculty can't be complete if a person isn't happy. - Yaakov had no prophecy for 22 years , mourning Yosef . - Music was essential to inducing prophecy: והיה כנגן המנגן ותהי עליו יד ה׳ (Elisha). The Rabbi's connection: the Gemara (Gittin; Sotah) forbids music after the Churban . By the strict letter ( Gemara, Rambam, Shulchan Aruch ) live music is forbidden all year , except perhaps at a wedding. \" Nobody keeps this halachah , we go to concerts; why is a different discussion . But I think there's a link: music was an ingredient of prophecy , bringing happiness .\" \" My understanding of the Rambam: not impossible today, but very, very hard. How can we be in exile, in servitude to the nations, without the Beit HaMikdash, and be fully happy? \" Part 7: A First Look at Principle 7 היסוד השביעי: נבואת משה רבנו עליו השלום. והוא שנאמין כי הוא אביהם של כל הנביאים אשר היו לפניו ואשר קמו אחריו, כולם הם תחתיו במעלה, והוא היה נבחר מכל מין האדם, אשר השיג מאמתתו יתברך יותר מכל מה שהשיגו ושישיגו שום אדם שנמצא או שימצא. Moshe is the father of all prophets before and after ; all are beneath him . He grasped more of Hashem's truth than anyone who ever was or will be . וכי הוא עליו השלום הגיע התעלותו מן האנושית אל המעלה המלאכותית ונכלל במעלת המלאכים, ולא נשאר מסך שלא קרעו… ולא מנעו ממנו מונע גופני, ולא נתערב לו שום חסרון בין רב בין מעט, ונתבטלו ממנו כוחות הדמיונות והחושים… ונשאר שכל בלבד. ועל הענין הזה נאמר עליו שהוא מדבר עם השם יתברך בלא אמצעות המלאכים. Angelic level He rose from the human to the level of the angels No veil left He tore through every barrier to understanding God No deficiency Nothing physical held him back; a perfect human Pure intellect Imagination, senses and desires nullified : only intellect remained No intermediary He spoke with Hashem directly , unlike other prophets, through angels \"I wanted to explain this…\" ורצוני היה לבאר זה הענין הנפלא, ולפתוח המנעול הנקוט בתורה, ולפרש ענין פה אל פה… but it would need many premises and parables : the angels and their levels (\"he hasn't written Mishneh Torah yet\"), the soul and its powers , the forms the prophets described, and even Shiur Komah . Shiur Komah is a Kabbalistic book . \" He wrote this, and later erased it : later in life he wrote that the book was a fabrication . In the latest Rambams you won't find it. \" ולא יספיק בענין זה לבדו אפילו אם יקוצר בתכלית הקיצור מאה דפים : even a hundred pages, as brief as possible, wouldn't suffice. So he leaves it for: Promised book What happened ספר הדרשות (the book of derashot ) Never written : as he said earlier, \" I'd need more meshalim to explain the meshalim \" ספר הנבואה (\"which I'm busy writing\") We don't have it. \" I'm convinced he incorporated it into the second section of the Moreh. \" A book explaining the principles Again, the Moreh : three sections matching the three categories Next week: the Rambam's four differences between Moshe and all other prophets . A shocking text to leave with The Arizal , quoted by the Ba'al HaTanya (the first of the seven Chabad Rebbes, ~250 years ago): השגת משה רבינו עליו השלום לא היתה בבחינת פנימיות חכמה עילאה : Moshe's grasp did not reach the inner dimension of the highest sefirot ( Chochmah, Keter ); למעלה מנצח הוד יסוד מלכות לא היתה לו שום השגה בפנימיות : above Netzach, Hod, Yesod, Malchut , only external understanding . The Tanya asks: ולכאורה יפלא, הרי נאמר ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה, ואיך השיג האריז״ל יותר ממנו? Answers: there's a great difference between the grasp of חכמי האמת , the Rashbi and the Arizal ( wisdom and knowledge ), and Moshe's , which was prophecy : seeing , as in וראית את אחורי ופני לא יראו . \" On its face: Kabbalistic knowledge and prophetic knowledge are two disciplines. Moshe was a prophet, not a Kabbalist. The theological consequences are far-reaching. I think the Rambam would view this as completely heretical. I find it shocking. We have to be very careful with these things. \" Question at the End Who were the prophets before Moshe, and how did they learn without books? Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov certainly: \"Hashem spoke to Avraham\" is prophecy. \" Knowledge doesn't just come from books. Avraham gazed at the world and understood it.\" And Chazal speak of some learning even before the Torah . Avraham kept the Torah before it was given : \"some take it literally. The simple understanding: he attuned himself to what the Torah would demand a thousand years later , because the Torah is the guide to perfecting middot and intellect , and he reached that himself.\" Summary Principle 6 opens the second category (6–9) : Hashem relates to humanity . It is not \"the prophets' words are true\" (that's 8–9 ; the Ani Ma'amin is imprecise, Yigdal precise). It establishes Hashem's involvement in the world ( Ramban : the plagues answered Pharaoh ). Dreams (true or false, follow the interpretation ), Ruach HaKodesh ( intuition , an einfall ) and prophecy ( a clear, transforming experience of cleaving to Hashem ), per the Ramchal . The Rabbi is unsure the Rambam draws the same line . Prophecy is an experience, not information , and doesn't endure ( Bnei HaNevi'im , 1.2 million prophets ). Who prophesies: the masses / Kuzari (Hashem chooses), philosophers (automatic), the Torah / Rambam ( prepared, then Hashem's will ). The miracle is not getting it. Three ingredients: intellect (Torah and the sciences), character , imagination . Kuzari: Jews only, in Israel. Rambam: any human , though mitzvot help. Har Sinai fits the Kuzari better. No prophecy today: for the Rambam, without joy the imagination can't be complete : exile, no Temple, no music . Principle 7: Moshe above all prophets , pure intellect, no intermediary ; the promised books became the Moreh ; the Arizal/Tanya claim is \" shocking .\" Coming Up Next week: finish Principle 7 , the four differences between Moshe and every other prophet , then begin Principle 8: Torah min HaShamayim , \"which has lots to discuss.\"","HcqDpnDoR6Q":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 4 ( Kadmut ) and Principle 5 (worship of Hashem alone) Shiur: Part 11 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 16 December 2025 (third night of Chanukah; the last shiur of the term) These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Recap Principles 1–3: a First Cause ; one , in the refined sense; no body , no physical attributes or emotions, the only principle with no explicit source in Torah or Chazal. Tonight, 4 and 5 , completing the five about Hashem . (Remember: the Rambam wrote this in his twenties; the Moreh came decades later.) Part 1: Principle 4: Two Texts The common text היסוד הרביעי: הקדמות. והוא שנאמין כי זה האחד האמור הוא קדמון בהחלט, וכל נמצא זולתו הוא בלתי קדמון בערכו אליו. והראיות על זה בכתבי הקודש רבות. והיסוד הרביעי הזה מורה עליו מה שנאמר: מעונה אלהי קדם. He alone is absolutely prior; everything else , however ancient, is not , relative to Him. מענה אלהי קדם : from the Torah's last parashah, \" which we say on Simchat Torah .\" On this text, Principle 4 is only: Hashem precedes all else. Rav Shilat's text The same, then an addition : ודע כי היסוד היותר גדול שבתורת משה רבנו הוא היות העולם מחודש, המציאו הקב״ה ובראו אחר ההעדר הגמור. וזה שתראני סובב סביב ענין קדמות העולם על דעת הפילוסופים, הוא כדי שיהיה המופת על מציאותו יתעלה מוחלט, כמו שבארתי במורה. The greatest foundation of Moshe's Torah is that the world is newly made: Hashem brought it into being after complete nothingness. When I seem to entertain the philosophers' eternity of the world , it's only to prove His existence absolutely, as I explained in the Moreh . \"As I explained in the Moreh\" : written decades later . Rav Shilat says the Rambam added it when revising ; the Oxford manuscripts show his marginal additions to the Commentary. The difference: \"Hashem precedes everything\" vs \"Hashem created from nothing.\" \"You may think it's the same. It's not so straightforward. \" Three views of creation (Moreh 2:13) View The universe Matter The Rambam Torat Moshe Ex nihilo : only God, then the world from absolute nothing Has a beginning None before \"The view of all who believe in the Torah of Moshe\" Aristotle God and universe both eternal . Creation from nothing is absurd ; no miracles; nothing changes No beginning or end Eternal Rejects entirely Plato God made the world from eternal formless matter ( hyle , היולי ), since even God can't do the impossible , and something from nothing is impossible Has a beginning Coexists eternally with God , under His control Sympathetic (below) Aristotle \"was very much the scientific view until the Big Bang theory . We now know he was completely wrong , and the Rambam called him out in the 12th century .\" (The Moreh lists eight proofs of Aristotle's; \"we don't need to go through them.\") Rishonim who held like Plato Ibn Ezra on בראשית ברא אלהים : most commentators say ברא = something from nothing (e.g. Korach: ואם בריאה יברא ה׳ ). But ויברא… את האדם , from earth ( adamah )! So ברא means לגזור ולשום גבול נגזר, והמשכיל יבין : to shape, to set boundaries . Hashem formed the world from pre-existing matter. The Ralbag held the same. The Kuzari: himself ex nihilo , but ואם יצטרך בעל תורה להאמין ולהודות בהיולי קדמון ועולמות רבים קודם העולם הזה, לא היה בזה פגם באמונתו, כי העולם הזה חדש מזמן ידוע מתחלת האדם . If a believer feels he must accept eternal matter, even prior worlds, his faith is unblemished : what matters is that this world began . The Ramban (opening of Bereishit): yesh me'ayin is a principle of faith , possible for an omnipotent God; deny it and you're a heretic . The mainstream of the Rishonim. What did the Rambam hold? Moreh 2:25: - Aristotle: if his proofs were compelling, I'd reinterpret the Torah. They aren't. And his view uproots miracles, and so the whole Torah . - Plato: דעת זו אינה הורסת יסודי התורה : this view doesn't destroy the Torah's foundations . It allows miracles ; the verses can be read according to it ; some even support it better . But nothing compels us , so we read the verses simply: creation from nothing. \" The Rambam held ex nihilo, like the Ramban and most Rishonim, no question. But I read him like the Kuzari : Plato's view is compatible with the Torah . Beseder , you may. \" So which text? Common text: Principle 4 = Hashem precedes everything , which all agree is fundamental. Ibn Ezra is within the faith. Rav Shilat's text: ex nihilo is part of the principle , so Ibn Ezra would be a kofer . \"I find Rav Shilat's text difficult \": the greatest foundation of Moshe's Torah is creation from nothing , when in the Moreh he's sympathetic to Plato? Hilchot Teshuvah 3 (\" Rav Shilat doesn't reference this \"): the five kinds of min : no God; two gods; a body; והאומר שאינו לבדו ראשון וצור לכל : \"not alone First and Rock of all\" : just first , matching the common text . \" Interesting that he doesn't emphasise ex nihilo here. \" What matters most There is a beginning : now accepted by science; the world isn't eternal as Aristotle thought. Hashem precedes everything. Possibly also ex nihilo : certainly the correct and majority view, and the Rambam's own . Why the Torah begins with Bereishit. Talmai (Ptolemy) had 72 Sages translate the Torah separately, and miraculously they all made the same changes in several places, lest it be misread. One: they reordered בראשית ברא אלהים to אלהים ברא בראשית*, lest readers think \"Bereishit\" is a god who created Elohim . (The Gemara: darkness descended that day; \" so many misconceptions come from not reading the original* .\") \" So why does the Torah open with Bereishit ? Because there is a beginning, and so things can change. Believe like Aristotle and nothing changes: no miracles, no control. A beginning means Hashem can change the world, and so can we: the world, and ourselves. \" Ani Ma'amin: שהבורא… הוא ראשון והוא אחרון . \" Last isn't part of this principle\" (eternity belongs to Principle 1). Yigdal: קדמון לכל דבר אשר נברא, ראשון ואין ראשית לראשיתו : nothing precedes His being first. Neither addresses pre-existent matter. Robert: if there's a beginning, must there be an end? Or does matter continue forever? \" I need to get back to you on that. My understanding is it's possible the world is eternal , but I need to check the Moreh. I don't think the two are necessarily dependent. \" Part 2: Principle 5 היסוד החמישי: שהוא יתברך הוא הראוי לעבדו ולגדלו ולהודיע גדולתו ולעשות מצותיו, ושלא יעשו כזה למי שהוא תחתיו במציאות, מן המלאכים והכוכבים והגלגלים והיסודות ומה שהורכב מהם, לפי שכולם מוטבעים בפעולותיהם, אין להם משפט ולא בחירה אלא לו לבדו יתברך. וכן אין ראוי לעבדם כדי שיהיו אמצעים לקרבם אליו, אלא אליו לבדו יכוונו המחשבות ויניחו כל מה שזולתו. וזה היסוד החמישי הוא שמזהיר מעבודה זרה, ורוב התורה מזהרת עליו. Three elements # 1 Serve Hashem : worship, magnify, make His greatness known, keep His mitzvot 2 Serve nothing below Him : angels, stars, spheres (which the Rambam, and Rav Saadia, held have awareness ), the elements, or what's made from them (idols). They act by nature, without judgement or choice 3 Not even as intermediaries to bring us closer to Him. Direct your thoughts to Him alone \"Most of the Torah warns against it\" : from the Golden Calf through the prophets and Baal . Says Missing Ani Ma'amin: שהבורא… לו לבדו ראוי להתפלל, ואין לזולתו ראוי להתפלל Pray only to Him Intermediaries (perhaps implied) Yigdal: הנו אדון עולם לכל נוצר, יורה גדלתו ומלכותו He's Master of all creation \" The vaguest stanza of Yigdal. Where does it say don't serve anyone else ? You have to add: He's Master of all, therefore serve Him only \" Element 1: why we serve \" Hashem doesn't need our service. We don't pray to change His mind . We serve because it's best for us : a perfect God needs no worship. \" Element 3: the Golden Calf The Ramban: after Sinai they believed : they'd seen and heard Hashem. They wanted something tangible. Why Moshe broke the Tablets (\"I read this somewhere; I can't remember where\"): not anger . He saw they needed something physical : give them the Tablets and they'll worship the Tablets. \" This replays in every generation : always something new to connect to Hashem through. Because He's so hard to relate to, we want something tangible: strings, blue things on our bodies, everyone has their own. All distractions from focusing on God directly , and distractions are always alluring.\" Graves \" It's incredible how widespread this has become in the frum world. Every few years, a new grave where you'll see a miracle. The newest, in the last ten years: Reb Shayele of Kerestir. Hardly anyone had heard of him; now ordinary people fly to Romania: go to him and you'll have **parnassah . He wrote no sefarim ; we have none of his. He was a great rebbe who fed the poor , so if you go, you'll have food .\" Is visiting graves permitted? Yes, there's a source. Calev went to pray at the Avot in Me'arat HaMachpelah on the spies' mission. A tzaddik 's resting place has significance , and prayer may be more accepted there. But nothing is intrinsic. It inspires us to connect to Hashem: praying by the Avot, he thought of Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov , and was moved to act like them . \"Perhaps in that person's merit your prayer is more accepted. Perhaps. \" \" But Meron and such places , if you've been you know: people pushing each other out of the way to get one metre closer , to touch the tziyun , as if prayer works better nearer. For me, that's avodah zarah . A violation of this principle.\" Enosh's mistake Hilchot Avodah Zarah 1: in Enosh 's day people reasoned: Hashem made the stars and spheres to run the world and honoured them, so He wants us to honour them: as you'd show respect to the guards at Buckingham Palace out of honour for the king. They built shrines , offered sacrifices : avodat kochavim . Ch. 2: עיקר הצווי בעבודה זרה שלא לעבוד אחד מכל הברואים, לא מלאך ולא גלגל ולא כוכב… ואף על פי שהעובד יודע שה׳ הוא האלהים והוא עובד הנברא הזה על דרך שעבד אנוש. Even knowing Hashem is God, serving a creature as Enosh did, as an intermediary, is forbidden. Summary Principle 4: Hashem is absolutely prior to everything. Rav Shilat's text (a later addition referencing the Moreh) makes ex nihilo \"the greatest foundation.\" Three views: ex nihilo (Torat Moshe); Aristotle's eternal universe (rejected: no miracles; disproved by the Big Bang ); Plato's eternal matter ( Ibn Ezra , Ralbag ; the Kuzari tolerates it; the Ramban calls its holders heretics). The Rambam held ex nihilo but found Plato compatible with the Torah . On the common text (and Hilchot Teshuvah's \"First\" ), Ibn Ezra stays within the faith . Bereishit : there's a beginning, so change is possible , for Hashem and for us (and Talmai's 72 Sages ). Principle 5: serve Hashem alone , for our sake; nothing beneath Him, not even as an intermediary (Enosh, the Golden Calf, the broken Tablets ). Graves inspire ; nothing is intrinsic . Pushing to touch the tziyun is avodah zarah . Coming Up After the break, the week of 5 January 2026: Tuesday nights resume with Principles 6 and 7, prophecy (the start of the next four, on revelation ). Monday nights finish Menachem Meshiv Nefesh and begin a new sefer ; Thursday halachah resumes. (No Thursday shiur this week: the Rabbi has a public lighting.) \"Chanukah sameach: a beautiful and illuminating Chanukah, good news for Am Yisrael, and a refreshing break.\"","A7OzALV7rtw":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 3, Shelilat HaGashmiyut Shiur: Part 10 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 9 December 2025 (the week before Chanukah) These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. The Text היסוד השלישי: שלילת הגשמות ממנו. וזה, שנאמין כי האחד הזה שזכרנו אינו גוף ולא כח בגוף, ולא ישיגוהו מאורעות הגופים, כמו התנועה והמנוחה והמשכן, לא מצד עצמותו ולא במקרה. ולכן שללו ממנו חכמים זכרונם לברכה החבור והפירוד, ואמרו: אין למעלה לא ישיבה ולא עמידה, לא עורף ולא עיפוי, כלומר לא פירוד ולא חבור… ואמר הנביא: ואל מי תדמיוני ואשוה, יאמר קדוש. ואילו היה גוף, היה דומה לגופים. וכל מה שבא בכתבי הקודש מתארים אותו בתארי הגופות, כמו הליכה ועמידה וישיבה ודבור, הכל דרך השאלה. וכן אמרו: דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם. וכבר דברו חכמים בזה הענין הרבה. והיסוד השלישי הזה מורה עליו מה שנאמר: כי לא ראיתם כל תמונה. Phrase Meaning אינו גוף ולא כח בגוף No body, and no bodily force מאורעות הגופים Movement, rest, dwelling : not inherently, not incidentally אין למעלה לא ישיבה ולא עמידה, לא עורף ולא עיפוי No sitting or standing, no division ( oref) and no joining (ipui)*. Ipui , from *ועפו בכתף פלשתים (Yeshayahu 11:14), joined shoulder to shoulder ואל מי תדמיוני ואשוה If He had a body, He'd resemble bodies הכל דרך השאלה; דברה תורה כלשון בני אדם Every bodily description is borrowed language כי לא ראיתם כל תמונה (Va'etchanan) At Sinai you saw no image , because there is none \"The Sages spoke about this a lot\"? \" Nowhere in the Talmud does it say this. He must mean philosophers . \" Ani Ma'amin captures it exactly: שהבורא… אינו גוף, ולא ישיגוהו משיגי הגוף, ואין לו שום דמיון כלל . Yigdal: אין לו דמות הגוף ואינו גוף, לא נערוך אליו קדושתו . Part 1: It Follows From Principles 1 and 2 Perfection means no limitation ; unity means no limitation . Anything physical has a measure . \"However big you imagine God, a huge throne in the sky, a huge body and a long beard , it still has a measurement .\" Yesodei HaTorah 1: אילו היה היוצר גוף וגויה, היה לו קץ ותכלית, שאי אפשר להיות גוף שאין לו קץ, וכל שיש לגופו קץ ותכלית יש לכחו קץ וסוף. ואלהינו ברוך שמו, הואיל וכחו אין לו קץ ואינו פוסק… אין כחו כח הגוף. והואיל ואינו גוף, לא יארעו לו מאורעות הגופות. Abarbanel asks: so why list it separately ? \" The simple answer: because so many people believed God has some form. Logically it follows; he felt it had to be said explicitly. \" Part 2: Why It Matters, and How to Live With Imagery Rabbi Sacks , zichrono livrachah , asked by an atheist why did God create atheists? , answered: to keep us in check. \" When faith is challenged, we're forced to define it , and to make it more intellectual and rational .\" This principle forces a mature understanding : \" we should be proud to say we don't believe in a God on a throne in the sky with a beard, waving a wand. \" \"We think in images\" \" It's absolutely fine to speak of God physically, as long as we know it's a symbol, not a representation. We're human; we think in images. \" Algebra. \"Working out wallpaper for a house with the builder, you talk about 2x, 3z, 4y as if they had their own identity. Fine, as long as at some point you translate back into actual wallpaper. \" Even the most rational believer uses imagery. Examples: Father, King ; יושב בשמים ישחק ; וירד ה׳ לראות את העיר ואת המגדל (Noach, a few weeks ago); דע מה למעלה ממך (Avot); עמוד מכסא דין ושב בכסא רחמים . Why \"in heaven\"? \" Hashem is nowhere and everywhere. In heaven only , He'd be enclosed: limited .\" Rav Bloch ( Shiurei Da'at ): psychologically we venerate what's high. So we lift our eyes in the Amidah, אשא עיני אל ההרים . \" All fine, all imagery. Just know it's imagery.\" \"If I knew Him, I would be Him\" Rav Yosef Albo ( Ikkarim 2:30) : a sage was asked whether he knew God's essence: אילו ידעתיו הייתיו . Only He grasps His essence. \" What He is, we don't know. That He is, we do.\" We can speak of how He acts in the world, not of what He is . Part 3: The Hardest Principle \" The hardest principle to accept, in my opinion. Not because I doubt it. Of course I believe it , and philosophically it makes complete sense. The problem is calling its deniers heretics with no portion in Olam HaBa. \" How severe the Rambam is Hilchot Teshuvah 3: ואלו שאין להם חלק לעולם הבא… המינים : one who denies God; one who believes in two ; והאומר שיש שם רבון אחד, אבל שהוא גוף ובעל תמונה . The Moreh (1:36) goes further: One who believes God has a body is without doubt worse than an idolater ; he provokes and angers God far more . Defend him as simple, ignorant, raised that way? The idolater too worshipped only from ignorance and upbringing , yet we make no allowance for him. The elephant (Moreh 1:60) You've never seen or heard of an elephant . Someone you trust says one exists. Then he describes it: one leg, three wings, lives in the sea, a transparent body, a human face, speaks like a man, sometimes flies, sometimes swims. Do you say you believe in elephants, just refine your picture ? Or you don't believe in elephants at all ? \" What he describes has nothing to do with elephants. \" So too : one who believes in a God with form doesn't believe in God. \" Like praying to a ruler, or to a cupboard. However much you pray, that's not God. \" Problem 1: the Torah reads as if God has a body \" An unartificial reading of the entire Torah is a physical God. \" - נעשה אדם בצלמנו כדמותנו - ויראו את אלהי ישראל, ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת הספיר (Mishpatim) - וראית את אחרי ופני לא יראו And the Rambam's proof-verses can be answered: you saw no image may mean He didn't reveal it ; to whom will you compare Me may mean a form unlike any other . \" I'm 100% certain the Rambam is right and these are metaphorical. But why penalise the innocent believer? \" Problem 2: no source in Chazal either All twelve other principles have a source, in the Torah or at least the Talmud (Mashiach and resurrection in the Mishnah ). This one has none. The Talmud even describes God bodily: Rabbi Yishmael saw Hashem wearing tefillin (Berachot). The first to say it explicitly: Rav Saadia Gaon (900s), in Emunot veDeot . But Targum Onkelos (the convert, ~200s CE) consistently softens every physical description: \" He clearly didn't believe in a physical God, 100%. \" He just didn't say it. The answer for the innocent: the A-level student \" I'm very sorry. You were taught wrong. But you don't believe in God. For the Rambam, Olam HaBa is intellectual and moral perfection: you can't enjoy an Olam HaBa you've completely misunderstood. \" \" A student applies to study maths at university with no A-level. He brings a doctor's note: two years in bed with cancer . They'll say: we're so sorry, we wish we could help, but without A-level maths you don't have the concepts. **\" The Ra'avad: \"greater and better than him\" למה קרא לזה מין? וכמה גדולים וטובים ממנו הלכו בזו המחשבה, לפי מה שראו במקראות. Why call him a heretic? Many greater and better than he held this view , from what they saw in scripture. (He agrees God has no body.) \"Greater than him \", the Rambam? \"The poskim were very troubled .\" - The Kesef Mishneh (the Beit Yosef): it can't have been said; a different text . - Rabbi Vidal of Tolosa (Maggid Mishneh): ממנו = \" from among us ,\" not \"than him.\" The Radbaz: an honest mistake isn't heresy: Hillel (in the Talmud) held there'd be no Mashiach , yet isn't called a kofer . שאין כפירתו… אלא מפני שחושב שמה שנראה בעיניו אמת, והוא אנוס ופטור. Rishonim who held God has form Rabbi Moshe Taku (Tosafist, 13th century), \"quite unconventional\": the Torah is literal : Hashem has a throne and sits on it . How can He know everything from one place? That's His greatness. - He also denied Hashem fills all existence (\" the song: Hashem is here, Hashem is there, Hashem is truly everywhere \"): not in impure places. \"An immature understanding,\" against Rav Saadia's mainstream view. - His proof (Sanhedrin 46b): twin brothers , one a king , one a robber . The robber hanged looks like the king, so the hanged are buried the same day. Man is in God's image, so God has a form. \" Easily disprovable, completely wrong. But the simple reading is on his side. \" Rashi? \"Some academics argue Rashi believed in a physical God, e.g. את ידי — ממש , My literal hand . See the Maskil LeDavid . I don't know enough about it. \" The Rambam knew : in the Letter on Resurrection he writes of would-be sages, even sitting dayanim, who believe God is physical . \" And he still calls them heretics. \" Where to land? The mainstream: the Rambam is right that God has no body , but one who believes otherwise because of simple upbringing isn't punished : Sefer HaIkkarim , the Ra'avad , Rabbeinu Bachya . The challot in the ark. \"In Tzfat , a simple man left challot in the Aron Kodesh each Friday, a gift for Hashem . Each Shabbat morning they were gone: Baruch Hashem, He ate them! (The shamash threw them out.) One Friday the rabbi caught him: How foolish! He stopped. The Arizal came to that rabbi: since the Temple was destroyed, Hashem's greatest pleasure was that man's challot. A classic hyperbolic Arizal story. The point: simplicity isn't penalised. \" \" I'm not sure which view to take. Intellectually, I think the Rambam is right: without that understanding you can't have Olam HaBa. Emotionally, it's very difficult : people who served Hashem their whole lives, who brought korbanot thinking He eats them…\" Part 4: Two Final Points Teaching children Moreh 1:35: incorporeality must be taught to everyone , children, women, the simplest , just as we teach that Hashem is one . \"We can tell kids Hashem sits in heaven judging us on Rosh Hashanah. Every so often, remind them: we don't mean it literally. \" Sefer Chassidim (Rabbi Yehudah HaChassid, 12th century): teaching children that Hashem is in heaven, point upwards . Berachot 48a: Abaye and Rava as boys, asked by Rabbah to Whom do we bless? where is He? : Rava pointed to the ceiling; Abaye went outside and pointed to the heavens. Both will become rabbis. Why would the Torah mislead? Moreh 1:46 (\" quite shocking \"): the masses had to be brought to God progressively : first a corporeal God, an improvement on many gods ; only later incorporeality . \" Essentially the Torah tells you something not true, to lead you to something more developed. There are problems with that; look it up yourself.\" Summary Principle 3: Hashem has no body, no bodily force , none of a body's states; all bodily language is borrowed . It follows from perfection and unity , but was listed because so many believed otherwise . Imagery is fine as symbol (algebra; \"in heaven\" because we venerate height ), translated back now and then. We know that He is, not what . The hardest principle: for the Rambam, a corporealist is worse than an idolater : the elephant with one leg and three wings. The problems: the Torah reads physically , and no source in Chazal (only Onkelos implicitly, then Rav Saadia explicitly). Answers: the student without A-levels ; but the Ra'avad , Radbaz and Ikkarim won't call the innocent heretics. Rabbi Moshe Taku held God has form; perhaps Rashi . The Tzfat challot : simplicity not penalised. \" Intellectually the Rambam; emotionally, difficult. \" Teach children God has no body (Abaye and Rava pointing up). The Torah's anthropomorphism is a staged pedagogy (Moreh 1:46). Coming Up Next Tuesday, the final shiur of the term (at 8, after candle-lighting; no Monday or Thursday shiur for Chanukah ): Principles 4 and 5 .","s3MTS7ZrlEw":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 2, Yichud Hashem Shiur: Part 9 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, early December 2025 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. The Text היסוד השני: יחוד השם יתברך, כלומר שנאמין שזה שהוא סיבת הכל אחד. ואיננו כאחד הזוג, ולא כאחד המין, ולא כאיש האחד שנחלק לאחדים רבים, ולא כגוף הפשוט האחד במנין שמקבל החלוק לאין תכלית. אבל השם יתברך אחד באחדות שאין כמותה אחדות. וזה היסוד השני, מורה עליו מה שנאמר: שמע ישראל ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד. \"When we say Shema every morning and evening, we think: God is one, not two. That's an element, but not all of it. It's a far subtler idea: a unity without parallel. \" Even one speck of dust divides: into halves, atoms, protons and neutrons. Not Hashem. Two parts tonight: (1) what \"one\" excludes; (2) what \"a unity unlike any unity\" means. Part 1: Five Things \"One\" Excludes 1. Polytheism: the enemy of Tanach The first principle is never argued in Tanach; this one fills it. The Golden Calf ; the cycle of the prophets: settle, serve idols , suffer, prophets warn. Pharaoh: מי ה׳ אשר אשמע בקלו? The Rishonim: which god is this? We have many: what does yours control? לא יהיה לך אלהים אחרים על פני. \" Thankfully few struggle with this today.\" 2. Dualism: the enemy of the Sages Two gods : Zoroastrian light and darkness. \"A convenient answer to evil\": if God is omnipotent (Principle 1), why evil? Two gods! Some early Christian scholars even taught that the God of the Old Testament is darkness, of the New, light . The Sages built the answer into the prayers , at the very moments day and night change: Arvit אשר בדברו מעריב ערבים… בורא יום ולילה, גולל אור מפני חשך Shacharit (the main service begins here) יוצר אור ובורא חשך, עושה שלום ובורא את הכל That's why Shacharit is ideally at netz , sunrise, and Arvit at nightfall : so as day and night turn over, you don't think there's a god of each . האומר מודים מודים משתקין אותו : a chazan who repeats Modim is silenced, lest he seem to thank two powers . The Gemara (Sanhedrin 39a): A Magian told Ameimar : from the waist up belongs to Hormiz, from the waist down to Ahormiz. Ameimar: then why does Ahormiz let Hormiz pass water through his territory? \"A cute story with a deep idea : an omnipotent God must control everything. Two gods means neither has full power.\" The subtle dualism we all fall into The Ramchal devoted much of his philosophy, and a whole book, Da'at Tevunot , to this. \"Deep down, we're all subtle dualists. Every time we get angry, every time we sin.\" A true monotheist knows nothing happens outside Hashem's will : so (a) when things go wrong, it all comes from Him ; (b) temptation is an illusion , since nothing outside His will can benefit me . Every sin says: I'll gain more from this than from Hashem's will : a denial of His oneness. Robert: isn't that denying God altogether, \"zero\" rather than \"two\"? \"You could see it either way, both bad . אין עוד מלבדו : there's no benefit outside His will. But Tanach's enemy was many or two , never zero .\" The original sin. והייתם כאלהים יודעי טוב ורע : the snake promised benefit from what Hashem forbade . Refusing would have affirmed His unity ; they denied it. All history rectifies this. When Israel truly recognises Yichud Hashem : the end of days . Why cover our eyes for Shema? The simple reason: focus . The Ramchal's: in this world we don't see His unity; there seem to be other forces, evil and temptation. I cover my eyes and say: Hashem Echad. I believe what I can't see. \"Hence mezuzot at every door, tefillin** every morning.\" Angels' names on mezuzot. The Rambam: whoever writes them inside a mezuzah has no portion in Olam HaBa. \" The whole mezuzah is Yichud Hashem . You take the very thing that declares there's nothing besides Him and write angels on it! If you must, on the back , which is why we have Shaddai there today. The Rambam's had nothing. \" 3. The Trinity \"Most Christians deny they're polytheists: one God with three parts. For us that's heresy. \" Halachic consequences: - The Letter on Martyrdom (learned last year): one forced to embrace Islam need not die, since Islam believes in one God . Christianity is arguably avodah zarah , so one would have to die rather than adopt it. - One may enter a mosque , even pray there (Rav Ovadia permits), but not even enter a functioning church . (It also contradicts Principle 3, God has no body: next week.) - Some Rishonim: for gentiles the Trinity is not avodah zarah : for them it counts as belief in one God. \"That has halachic ramifications.\" 4. Deism God created, transcends the world, and left it . \" Probably more popular today : since science now accepts a beginning , atheism can't explain the first elements, so: a creator who then walked away. \" Denies Principles 1 and 2 : Yichud Hashem means there's no reality outside Him. If the world exists, He is sustaining it, every moment : the imagined universe that vanishes when attention slips. Question: \"the mouth of Bilaam's donkey was created at twilight of the sixth day\": isn't everything set up front, God watching it unfold? \" Absolutely not. Whether all miracles were built into nature in the six days (the Rambam ) or are new (the Ramchal : a machloket ), maintenance is constant.\" 5. Pantheism God is nature; nature is God : \"really a subtle form of atheism .\" Deism puts God outside the world; pantheism identifies Him with it. Spinoza , a member of the Amsterdam community, was excommunicated on 27 July 1656 by the Ma'amad , \" with the rabbis' consent \" (it's the board that excommunicates), in the sharpest terms: cursed by day, cursed by night… London's own controversy. In 1703 , Chacham David Nieto of Bevis Marks (author of Mateh Dan ) preached that God and nature are one . Members of the board, \" more knowledgeable than the rabbi , of course,\" accused him of Spinozism ; Joshua Zarphati rallied people against \" the heretic rabbi, the pantheist in our midst .\" It raged over a year . Nieto didn't step down : I meant God's force runs through all nature. In 1705 the parnassim of Sha'ar HaShamayim asked the Chacham Tzvi (rabbi of Hamburg, briefly rabbi in London ). His ruling (on Sefaria): we must be grateful to the great chacham David Nieto, who warned the people against the philosophers who say nature acts by itself and nothing else exists*. Yishar ko'ach . \" *That killed the controversy. \" טבע = nature , and also to sink , as in טבעו בים סוף . \" It's easy to sink into nature : to think the world just runs by itself. Hashem is behind nature, not nature itself. \" טבע = 86 = אלהים in gematria . A sixth? Panentheism \" Briefly, 30 seconds. There's a term for a strong Chabad belief: God is in everything : everyone has a piece of God in them ; ultimately the world is an illusion . That's a major problem I have with Chabad. I think the Rambam would disagree, though it's not explicit here . A discussion for another time.\" Part 2: \"A Unity Unlike Any Unity\" The Mishneh Torah (Yesodei HaTorah 1): אלוה זה אחד הוא, ואינו שנים ולא יותר על שנים… אלא יחוד שאין יחוד אחר כמותו בעולם. Ch. 2 (on His knowledge): הקב״ה מכיר אמתו ויודע אותה כמו שהיא, ואינו יודע בדעה שהיא חוץ ממנו כמו שאנו יודעים, שאין אנו ודעתנו אחד. אבל הבורא יתברך, הוא ודעתו וחייו אחד מכל צד ומכל פינה ובכל דרכי יחוד… נמצאת אומר: הוא היודע, והוא הידוע, והוא הדעה עצמה, הכל אחד. ודבר זה אין כח בפה לאמרו, ולא באזן לשמעו. He is the knower, the known and the knowledge itself, all one. The mouth can't say it; the ear can't hear it. Reuven and his attributes. \" Reuven is clever, handsome, kind : there's Reuven , who happens to have these. That's not unified. God isn't God plus wisdom, kindness, compassion. It's all one. The Ramchal calls it פשיטות, simplicity : no parts, one essence. Very hard for us to grasp, because everything we know is composite .\" Yigdal: אחד ואין יחיד כיחודו, נעלם וגם אין סוף לאחדותו : hidden from us, endless. Ani Ma'amin: ואין יחידות כמוהו בשום פנים . Then what about the Thirteen Attributes? ה׳ ה׳ אל רחום וחנון ארך אפים ורב חסד ואמת : said \"over and over\" on Yom Kippur and all year. That's God plus compassion, plus truth. A plurality. Negative theology The Rambam: God has no positive attributes. Every attribute in the Torah tells us what God is not , because negations imply no plurality . The Torah says It means Strong / able ( יכול ) Not weak Compassionate Not hard-hearted Eternal Not caused Wise / knowing ( יודע ) Not ignorant Willing ( רוצה ) Not indifferent Moreh 1:58: דע שתאור האל בשלילות הוא התאור הנכון : describing God by negation is the correct description. Positive attributes imply composition and deficiency ; negations guide the mind without them. \" It changes your whole perspective on the Torah. On Yom Kippur, think El Rachum veChanun , that's fine. But theologically, His unity means complete simplicity. \" Why the Ramchal puts everything on Yichud Hashem We can't grasp any positive attribute : that would mean grasping God Himself . We can negate. And negating means: no other force. So the only thing we can truly understand of God is that He is one, and the purpose of life is to reveal Yichud Hashem . Pesachim 50a: ביום ההוא יהיה ה׳ אחד ושמו אחד (said daily in Aleinu ). Isn't He one now? Not like this world is the world to come. Here, for good news we say הטוב והמטיב ; for bad, ברוך דיין האמת : I don't see how it's good; Hashem is the true judge. In the world to come it will all be Hatov veHameitiv . \" Yichud Hashem means believing, philosophically if not practically, that even the bad is good, since it all comes from one source. One day we'll see it. Now we cover our eyes. \" A caution about the Sefirot The Rivash (Rav Yitzchak bar Sheshet, 14th century, \"quoted in halachah everywhere\") wrote of a great rabbi who prayed one prayer to one sefirah, another to another, each blessing of the Amidah aimed at a sefirah. To us, this is very strange; it looks like dualism.* A philosopher mocked: the Christians believe in three, the Kabbalists in ten.*** In Zaragoza the Rivash asked his friend Don Yosef ibn Shoshan , a great Talmudist, Kabbalist and tzaddik . God forbid, we pray only to the One. As with a king: for justice you go to the judge, for money to the treasury. \" Like one prime minister with a Foreign Office, Home Office, Treasury.\" The Rivash: very nice, but better to pray without all of this **. Never imagine parts of Hashem. \"As it was explained to me years ago: one sunlight through different media comes out different colours . That's the sefirot : the same light of God through different media. \" Summary \"Hashem is one\" excludes: polytheism (Tanach's enemy; who is Hashem? ); dualism (the Sages', answered in יוצר אור ובורא חשך ; Modim twice; Hormiz and Ahormiz ), including the subtle dualism of every sin (Ramchal): the original sin , eyes covered at Shema , no angels in mezuzot ; the Trinity (Islam vs Christianity; mosque vs church); deism (He sustains every moment); pantheism ( Spinoza ; Nieto vindicated by the Chacham Tzvi ; טבע = אלהים ). (And panentheism , a Chabad view the Rabbi rejects; for another time.) 2. \"A unity unlike any unity\" : knower, known and knowledge are one : simplicity, no parts . 3. So no positive attributes : every attribute is a negation (Moreh 1:58). The Thirteen Attributes say what He is not . 4. The only thing we can grasp is His oneness : the Ramchal 's key to everything. In the world to come, all is Hatov veHameitiv . 5. Sefirot are one light through different media , never parts of God (the Rivash ). Coming Up Principle 3: Hashem has no body. \"The next principles will be easier and quicker. The first two were the big ones. \"","_YmL0Uhm7oo":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: the letter to Rav Pinchas ben Meshullam HaDayan of Alexandria (Hebrew original, Shilat edition), Sections 3–5 and conclusion Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 1 April 2025. The last class on the Rambam's letters before Pesach These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap The fifth letter of the series (after Ovadia , astrology , Ibn Jabbar and Kiddush Hashem ): to Rav Pinchas , who came from Provence to become dayan of Alexandria . The Rambam was instrumental in his appointment , \"but they didn't see eye to eye on many things.\" Part Content 1 Be confident in halachah; rule by the truth, not by what people want to hear. Pinchas gave in to people demanding washing after an emission before Torah and prayer. Not required for Torah, and for prayer only a custom , \"and I myself am particular about this custom.\" \"I'm not angry, but I did take offence\" 2 The Mishneh Torah: no names (a chibbur , not a perush ; names already in the introduction; names would make laws look like one man's opinion); people err (\"I can't walk around with my book\"); Talmud study ( \"I teach Talmud a lot\" ); sources (all from Chazal; the story of the forgotten source , and his agmat nefesh that he never wrote them down) 3 Specific questions : tonight 4 Pinchas's troubles at home: tonight 5 A technical question on kiddushin : tonight Part 3: The Specific Questions a) \"Did you say in public that I know nothing?\" Pinchas, quoted verbatim: You said in public about so-and-so, the dayan of Alexandria , that he knows nothing and is not proficient in Talmud. Why would you say such a thing? And I asked you many questions, and you never answered. (And in a later letter:) Please teach me everything I asked. Listen carefully to my answer. I never said anything of the sort, because I don't lie , and it isn't true: you do know. So what did he say? \"He's admitting something .\" To understand it, some background. Background: two kinds of property a wife brings into a marriage נכסי מלוג , nichsei melog נכסי צאן ברזל , nichsei tzon barzel (\"iron sheep\") Ownership Stays hers Stays in her name Income (e.g. rent) Goes to the husband Goes to the husband Rise or fall in value Hers. A house worth £100k at the wedding and £200k at a divorce five years later: the gain is hers; a loss is too The husband is responsible. He must return the full value , even if it burned down or lost value For the husband Benefit, no responsibility Benefit and responsibility (Cash generally goes in the nedunya , the dowry; this is about property .) The case of the gold jewellery In a dispute over a piece of gold jewellery , Pinchas had ruled that it was tzon barzel : the husband is responsible for it. Then the husband, the son-in-law of Abu al-Faraj , came back: I don't want responsibility. I want it reclassified as melog **. The Rambam heard that Pinchas ruled he could , and could force it on the wife: \"the best of both worlds\" for the husband, all the benefit and none of the responsibility. I told Abu al-Faraj, the father-in-law: I don't believe Pinchas would rule like that. If he did, bring me proof. Then came a letter signed by two responsible witnesses : Pinchas had ruled publicly that any husband may convert tzon barzel into melog . מיד תמהתי : I was astonished at once. That isn't the halachah. What comes in under one law stays under that law. If they both agree , that's different, but the husband can't force it on the wife. \"There's no discussion about this. He's wrong. \" With the Rambam were another dayan and three or four others ; he showed them the letter, and everyone was astonished . What the Rambam actually said Don't be surprised: Pinchas comes from Provence, from France. All the Jews in Christian countries , even their great rabbis, aren't experts in practical law. Islamic lands (Egypt) Christian lands (Provence, France) Jewish courts Proper, permanent batei din Not allowed; makeshift batei din Monetary and marital cases Daily : \"bread and butter\" Rare; \"not on their fingertips\" Result Answer readily Take a long time , must search and analyse, and can err His analogy: \"If the Temple were rebuilt today and you came running to me with a practical question about a sacrifice , I couldn't answer straight away. I'd have to search, think, analyse, because it isn't something we do regularly.\" The Gemara's point: you can master all the books, but you can't compare even a sage who knows the whole Torah to someone who has faced practical questions . \"Any rabbi will tell you they remember best what they've taught and what they've been asked .\" Practical questions force you to think things through. \"God testifies that this is exactly what happened. I'm not adding or subtracting.\" So the rumour evolved from \"he comes from a country without regular courts, so he struggled and got it wrong\" into \"the Rambam says he knows nothing.\" Was it lashon hara ? The Rambam doesn't apologise . Why wasn't it lashon hara ? Saying a ruling is wrong isn't lashon hara . If someone says Rabbi X told me this is the halachah and you know it isn't, saying that rabbi is wrong is giving the correct ruling . Adding \"he's from France, where they have no batei din\" depends on tone . Said derogatorily (\" these Ashkenazim, they know nothing \"), it might be. Said to explain , understand their culture; they lack the experience we dayanim in Fustat have , it isn't. \"He's not saying it to demean Pinchas, just to explain .\" b) \"You never answer my questions\" I swear I don't know of any question you asked that I didn't answer , except one, about marriage , which came while I was ill . I'll answer it at the end of this letter . The rest: What Pinchas asked The Rambam's response One question, over and over (its content isn't stated) \"You harassed me with this question.\" I gave my answer. Do what you want. Stop asking A question about a get I answered with proofs ; you came back: I still don't see why you said it's kosher. I kept quiet Anything else Perhaps lost in the post . Ask again My way, always: when I debate with someone and see he won't budge or consider the other side, I fall silent and leave him to his view. \" An important principle in life. If you're trying to teach someone, you give your opinion, and they persist, leave them and move on. Don't waste your time.\" The Rabbi's own pre-Pesach example: \"A week ago someone asked me: can you buy Coca-Cola? I said yes. Full stop. Last night he texted: I asked another rabbi and he was very surprised at your ruling. Can you explain your reasoning? I didn't answer. Every year I get this. You have to know when to move on. \" c) The widow and the three signatures A widow was disputing a property (a house or courtyard) with her children . The ruling: she needn't take an oath , because she's in possession , so the children can't claim it. The Rambam answered and had two other dayanim sign with him. Background: תשובה , responsum פסק , ruling What A question sent from afar ; you give your opinion The parties appear before you Who signs The rabbi alone All three dayanim This was a responsum from Alexandria to Fustat, so Pinchas asked: why three signatures? You're right; normally I'd sign alone. But I was told that if only I signed, you'd tell the secular judge : this rabbi says that, I say otherwise; who's right? and the case wouldn't come out correctly. With three rabbis , your view would be the minority . So I forwent my honour and signed with the others to make sure the ruling stood. \"Don't accuse her.\" Pinchas had suggested the widow wasn't afraid to swear , so of course she'd claim what she claimed. How dare you say that about a Jew? How can a sage like you say it? Only the wicked aren't afraid to take an oath. Don't accuse this woman of being one of them. d) The get sent by agent Background 1: a get by agent. - A get can be handed over directly or via a shaliach , \"from one end of the earth to the other.\" \"We do this nowadays all the time; we send gittin to Israel.\" - She is divorced only when the get reaches her hand. Until then anything can cancel it : the agent resigns , dies , falls ill , loses it, or the husband retracts , without even telling the agent. - \"A terrible thing to do,\" so nowadays the husband takes a full oath not to retract the agency. Background 2: conditions in a get . (Theoretical: \"this is avoided nowadays for many reasons.\") \"על מנת\", \"on condition that\" Other wording Takes effect Now. Once she fulfils the condition (say, delivering a parcel to someone), she is divorced retroactively from today Only once the condition is fulfilled Can she remarry meanwhile? Yes No Formal requirements None : very easy Strict: double the condition ( tenai kaful ), state the positive before the negative , etc. The case: the same son-in-law of Abu al-Faraj came to the Rambam: Abu al-Faraj treated me badly; he made me divorce his daughter, though she never asked for it. While talking with him, the Rambam realised the get was being sent by agent . So, cleverly, he wrote to Abu al-Faraj : has your daughter received the get yet? Not yet , came the answer, with the text of the get and the agency. \"Don't delay. Make sure she receives it quickly.\" Two criticisms of Pinchas: 1. You dawdled. A get by agent can be annulled at any moment : the agent could die , be killed , drown at sea , be lost . \" What use is a dayan who doesn't think proactively and tell her to go and receive her get ?\" 2. You mixed the formulas. You used al menat and doubled the condition with positive before negative. Unnecessary : al menat needs none of that. \"It doesn't actually invalidate anything, but it's not appropriate for someone like you to write mixed messages. \" \"Chacham gadol.\" The Rambam calls Pinchas \" a great sage \" four or five times in the letter. \"I don't know if he literally means it, or is saying how can a great person like you keep making these mistakes? Maybe you're not as great as people say.\" Part 4: Pinchas's Troubles at Home \"Much shorter; we've done the main bits.\" Pinchas \" seems to be suffering a lot .\" Trouble The Rambam's response Avraham goes around Alexandria saying \"the Rambam is right and Pinchas is always wrong\" God knows I scolded him, by word and in writing. \"He thinks he's helping me. I told him clearly: I don't want your help. Don't speak badly about Pinchas.\" He made him swear to stop Abu al-Faraj says Pinchas ruled for the son-in-law because they're friendly : they eat together , and Pinchas got benefits from him (\"not exactly bribes, but close\") I wrote to Abu al-Faraj: that's not why. He made a mistake, and even the Supreme Court can err, as the Torah says. \" I pacified him. \" \"You said in public that I have an angry disposition\" \"Yes, I said it.\" (Below) Rumours Pinchas wants to return to \"Rome\" , to Christian Europe (Shilat's note) Don't. Don't leave where you're established Abu Zikri causing trouble Don't worry; he's powerless \"Yes, I said you're angry\", \"the best bit of the whole letter\" \"Everywhere else he defends himself robustly. Here it seems like checkmate : Pinchas says you called me an angry person in public , and the Rambam says I won't lie. I said it. \" How he gets out of it: The Gemara: when a talmid chacham gets angry, it's the Torah that heats him : הלא כה דברי כאש , \"are not My words like fire?\" I was praising you in the Gemara's words: your anger is the anger of Torah. \"Fascinating.\" \"It is difficult, I agree.\" Make peace with Rabbi Daniel The context: Pinchas's feud with Rabbi Daniel (Part 1: Daniel had ruled leniently on washing, and Pinchas opposed him). That's what upsets me. Why don't you get on with Rabbi Daniel? He's a great person, God-fearing , and he's bringing people to do good, in Fustat and in Alexandria . Be friends; study together. If you let him teach my Mishneh Torah to others ( under your leadership, of course ), you could ignite so many people with the depths of Torah. I wish it would happen. Make up with each other. \"Don't go back to Europe\" \"We don't have Pinchas's letters, but from everything the Rambam quotes, he seems to be going through a lot : complaining about this person and that person, the Rambam, the ruling, the people who came to him.\" Don't leave your place under pressure. (One line, about going \"to places with armies,\" is unclear.) Was \"Avraham\" the Rambam's son? The Rabbi's own idea (\"I just realised this now; no one says this , I have no proof\"): - When the Rambam names people he usually adds \"Mar\" or \"Abu\" ; here it's just \"Avraham\" . - Pinchas expects the Rambam to control Avraham, and the Rambam can order him to stop and make him swear . - The Rambam's only son was Avraham ( Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam ; he also had a daughter). So perhaps Avraham was the Rambam's son , zealously defending his father. Part 5: Is Kiddushin by Money Biblical or Rabbinic? \"I don't know if you could spend a whole shiur on it, but briefly.\" Pinchas's unanswered question, the one that came while the Rambam was ill. Background: three ways to betroth Method Source Most opinions The Rambam (Mishneh Torah) כסף , money Something of financial value A gezeirah shavah , an extrapolation by the Sages Biblical Rabbinic שטר , document A betrothal document (not the ketubah ) Also an extrapolation from a verse Biblical Biblical ביאה , relations With intent to betroth Explicit : ובעלה Biblical Biblical The Rambam's answer 1. Go and read my Sefer HaMitzvot . Written in Arabic (we now have it in Hebrew); Sa'adia in Alexandria has a copy . It sets out 14 principles for which commandments count among the 613 . Learn those 14 principles and you'll see that everyone who counted the mitzvot before me got it wrong. Background on counting: the Gemara says there are 613 , but go through the Torah and you can find many more . So which count? Many tried, including Rabbi Abba Kira (7th–8th century) and Shlomo ibn Gabirol . The Azharot read at Minchah on Shavuot are poems counting the 613, \"and they include things the Rambam says are completely wrong .\" (The Rabbi gave a Zoom shiur on this two or three years ago.) 2. The principle (Sefer HaMitzvot): of the 13 hermeneutic rules (said every morning at the start of Shacharit), what the Sages derive through them does not automatically become de'oraita . It counts as biblical only where the Sages say explicitly that it is . So: - Relations : explicit in the verse, so biblical . - Money : derived by gezeirah shavah , with no explicit statement, so rabbinic . - Document : also derived. \"You're right; by my principle it should be rabbinic.\" But the Gemara clearly treats it as de'oraita , so he counted it biblical. \"This is a whole shiur on its own: what de'oraita and de'rabbanan mean here.\" Did he retract? Rabbeinu Avraham , the Rambam's son, says his father retracted and held that all three are biblical, as almost everyone does. \" It's not clear he did. Much ink has been spilt over this.\" The Close My chieftain, don't worry about answering everything I've written. But equally, don't expect me to answer everything you write. I'm very busy and very weak . I haven't the time or strength even to read the letters I receive, let alone answer them. If you have practical questions, send them. Otherwise I have no time, because of my constant weakness, and because I need to learn a little myself. Dated the 35th day of the Omer . Summary of the Whole Letter Part 1 Washing after an emission : rule by the halachah, not the crowd; washing before prayer is only a custom 2 The purpose of the Mishneh Torah : a chibbur like the Mishnah; no names , no sources , all from Chazal; study Talmud too 3 \"He knows nothing\"? Never said it. Pinchas got tzon barzel vs melog wrong, explained by his coming from lands without Jewish courts , said to explain, not demean . Unanswered questions: \"you harassed me,\" and when someone won't budge, move on . Three signatures to make the widow's ruling stand; don't accuse her of a false oath. The get by agent : act fast , and don't mix the condition formulas 4 Pinchas's troubles : the Rambam rebuked Avraham and pacified Abu al-Faraj ; \"angry\" meant the fire of Torah ; make peace with Rabbi Daniel ; don't go back to Europe 5 Kiddushin by money is rabbinic because derived law isn't automatically biblical ( Sefer HaMitzvot ); document is biblical because the Gemara treats it so . His son says he retracted Close \"Don't expect me to answer everything. I'm busy and weak, and I need to learn a little myself.\" Coming Up This completes five letters , learned January to March : Ovadia, astrology, Ibn Jabbar, Kiddush Hashem and Rav Pinchas. A new series of the Rambam's letters after Pesach , including the letter to Ibn Tibbon , which seems to say the opposite about Talmud study. The class closed with a discussion of the format going forward.","yfYaNnT8DBA":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: the letter to Rav Pinchas ben Meshullam HaDayan of Alexandria (Hebrew original, Shilat edition), Section 2 Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 25 March 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap The series so far: the letters to Ovadia the convert in Jerusalem, to the sages of Montpellier on astrology, to Ibn Jabbar in Iraq, and the letter on martyrdom . Now the fifth: to Rav Pinchas HaDayan , who moved from Provence to become dayan of Alexandria , and whose relationship with the Rambam was strained . Written c. 1185–1195 . The letter has five parts : Part Content When 1 \"I was ill, not ignoring you.\" But I was angry that you caved in to the masses and preached the \"severity\" of washing after an emission, a custom , when the law permits Last week 2 The purpose of the Mishneh Torah : \"the main part of the letter\" Tonight 3–5 Shorter, more technical sections Next week Background: What the Mishneh Torah Was \"His magnum opus is certainly the Mishneh Torah. There was no one like him to do anything like it before him, and no one has done anything like it since. \" The problem it solved The Talmud is a discussion . Many things come up that aren't relevant to the point, and the laws of any one topic are scattered across Shas . The Rif came closest, but it isn't the same Rav Yitzchak Alfasi (the Rif ), from North Africa, a century or more before the Rambam, abridged the Talmud , keeping only the halachic material. But it still follows the Talmud's order : Rif Mishneh Torah Method Goes through the Talmud tractate by tractate, keeping the halachah Reorganises all of halachah by topic To learn all the laws of Shabbat Tractate Shabbat in the Rif contains material not about Shabbat, and misses Shabbat laws found elsewhere in Shas One section : Hilchot Shabbat Sources The Babylonian Talmud Bavli, Yerushalmi, Sifra, Sifrei, Tosefta, baraitot : all of rabbinic literature Shape An abridged Talmud Fourteen books of clear rulings Controversy His books were later burnt , \"but that was more because people didn't like the Rambam's philosophy in other works.\" Here, by contrast, Pinchas criticises the Mishneh Torah itself . Pinchas's Four Criticisms The Rambam quotes Pinchas: your Mishneh Torah is wonderful, but only for those who already know Talmud and the names of the Sages. # Criticism 1 You don't name the Sages. The Mishnah and Gemara are full of Rabbi Yehudah says, Rabbi Meir says ; you never say who said what 2 People misread your book and err in halachah 3 You don't make clear that Talmud study is essential. People think the Mishneh Torah is enough. You should preach that they mustn't neglect the Talmud 4 You give no sources. How do I know you're right? I find laws whose source I can't locate Behind criticism 3: the Rambam's own introduction says that one who learns Torah sheBichtav and then his book will know the whole Oral Law, which is why it's called Mishneh Torah , the \"second Torah.\" \"Some of these criticisms recur over and over throughout the generations .\" As recently as a generation ago, Chabad , for whom daily study of the Mishneh Torah is central, was criticised by Rav Shach for the same reason: it isn't enough to study the Rambam; study the Talmud. \"So the Rambam addresses it in black and white here, and that's why this is such a crucial letter.\" The opening rebuke I have to tell you off . I know deep down how you feel about me , even if you didn't say it: you don't give weight to who I am and what I've written. Answer to Criticism 3: \"I Never Said Don't Study Talmud\" Hashem is my witness : around where I live, only two or three people are studying parts of my Mishneh Torah. Most of my students I've taught the whole Rif. Whoever asked me to learn Talmud with them, I learned whatever they wanted. \"Imagine asking the Rambam, I want to learn Talmud with you , and he says yes, sure .\" Did I ever ask, or think, that every earlier book is no longer relevant and only mine should be learned? I wrote at the start of the Mishneh Torah that it's for those who don't have the time , or the capacity to work through the Talmud. For them I made a kitzur , the plain halachah. But I never, chas veshalom , said don't study Talmud. A tension to note In another letter , to his translator Ibn Tibbon (to be learned in the summer), the Rambam does seem to suggest that people waste their time on the Talmud's dialectic: \"I'm not saying these words lightly.\" \"We will try to understand that when we learn that letter.\" Here , though, he is unequivocal: people must study Talmud, and that's what he teaches. Answer to Criticisms 1 and 2: No Names, and Misreadings a) \"I listed them all at the start\" The introduction to the Mishneh Torah traces the whole chain of tradition , generation by generation. \"We've never done this together; we will, with Hashem's help.\" I listed all the names there in order. Why repeat them? b) \"No one has done what I did since Rabbeinu HaKadosh\" There were many books before me that abridged halachah, in Hebrew and in Arabic . But no one since Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi , who wrote the Mishnah, has done what I did: condense all of halachah into clear statements. On misreadings: \"When you write a book, I can't walk around with it explaining it to everyone. You write it, you give it out, people make mistakes. It's their problem. \" c) Chibbur versus perush : the heart of the answer Anyone who writes a book, in Torah or any other discipline, Jewish author or not, takes one of two approaches. חיבור , chibbur : composition פירוש , perush : commentary What it is Your own composition, to the point Explains, dissects and analyses an earlier text Contents Only the correct points : no questions, no answers, no proofs. \"The dry facts\" The correct view and the contradictory ones, questions on each , the answers , and the proofs that this view is right and that one wrong Model The Mishnah : Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi gathered the views and said this is the halachah The Talmud : the Amoraim commenting on the Mishnah. (Likewise Rashi on Shas) The Mishneh Torah ✔ \"I wanted it in the way of the Mishnah \" ✘ You didn't pay attention to what I wrote. You don't understand the difference between Mishnah and Gemara. That's why you keep writing that you find laws in my book whose source you don't know. He softens it with a title ( \"my chieftain\" ), \"I'm respecting you, giving you nice titles, but you haven't understood .\" d) \"But the Mishnah does quote names!\" The obvious challenge: the Mishnah often says this Rabbi says, that Rabbi says. The Rambam explains why , \"an important principle when learning Mishnah\": Most mishnayot have no names at all. A סתם משנה , an anonymous Mishnah, is authoritative : that is the halachah. Even named statements don't trace the chain. Rabbi Meir got his teaching from his teacher, and he from his; the Mishnah never lists the chain back. Rabbi sometimes merged two Sages' views he thought correct into one anonymous mishnah , without saying where each came from. He named Sages only where he was unsure of the halachah and communities followed different views. Naming them showed that each practice had authority (Rabbi Meir's, Rabbi Shimon's), so those following it shouldn't be castigated . The proof: Mishnah Eduyot, chapter 1. Why record Shammai , or one Sage against the majority , if the law doesn't follow them? So that a beit din or community following that minority view has an authority to rely on . e) Why that doesn't apply now In the Mishnah's time, some communities did this and some did that . Once the Talmud was sealed , there is one Jewish approach ; the vast majority of the vast majority practise it. There are no longer two camps. f) Names invite people to reject the law If I wrote this law comes from Rabbi X , people would say \"that's his opinion; I don't have to follow it,\" and they'd be בועט , kick against it. All Israel accepted the Talmud , and so its Sanhedrin-level authority. I want every halachah to appear to the one studying it as the halachah , stam, divrei hakol. Not \"he thinks this, he says that.\" This is the law. Follow the law. Full stop. Summary of his answer on names # Answer 1 All the names are in the introduction 2 I wrote a chibbur , a composition like the Mishnah, not a perush like the Talmud 3 The Mishnah named Sages only where communities followed differing valid views ; since the Talmud, all Israel follows one halachah 4 Names would let people dismiss the law as one man's opinion \"You criticised me because you misunderstand my purpose . If you'd taken a bit more time to understand, you wouldn't have this problem.\" Answer to Criticism 4: \"Where Are Your Sources?\" \"Perhaps the most amazing part of the letter.\" Pinchas had implied the Rambam wrote things from his own mind and passed them off as established law. I never did any such thing. Open your ear and your wisdom and pay attention. The Rambam's own key to his book How it's written Where it comes from Stated plainly , no qualification Certainly from the Sages : Bavli, Yerushalmi, Sifra, Sifrei, Mishnah, Tosefta , the halachic works of Chazal \"The Geonim ruled…\" From the Geonim (c. 650–1000 CE; \"like we learned on Monday nights\") \"It seems to me…\" / \"From this I deduce…\" My own conclusion , labelled as such So what's your problem? Why should I write the sources? Look in the Talmud. What Pinchas should have said What you should have said is that you sometimes can't remember where the source is . That happens to rabbis. Only great rabbis like you (the barb and the compliment together) can appreciate how much toil I put into this book . \"Not like the Rif. I swear.\" Most people think my work is like the Rif's : that I abridged, removed the questions and answers, and gave the conclusion. That's not what I did, I swear. The scale: 14 books, each of roughly 10 sections , each of roughly 10 chapters , and a single chapter is sometimes drawn from ten places in rabbinic literature. \"To write a chapter with the entire framework of Chazal in mind, and select this goes here, that goes there . Who can do that? That's incredible.\" I didn't follow the order of the Talmud. I gathered everything together so that it would no longer be scattered , so you can find it in one place . That was my whole purpose. It's impossible for anyone in the world to remember the entire Talmud constantly. When I had it all in my head (Bavli, Yerushalmi, Sifra, Sifrei), I put it together , sometimes from ten different places for one chapter. The story of the dayan and the law of murder A certain dayan came to me (older, apparently: he speaks down to him) holding the Book of Damages , open at the laws of murder . He showed me a law: \"What's your source?\" Where the Rambam looked Result Makkot and Sanhedrin , where laws of murder belong Went through all of both . Not there The Yerushalmi Not there. \"I was very shocked, confused\" Gittin : \"it must come up incidentally in the laws of divorce\" Not there either. \" I was really astonished at myself. Where did I get this from?\" \"Please give me a moment.\" The dayan left, and immediately I remembered Sent someone to bring him back , and showed him: it came incidentally , in a tractate dealing with marriage . The dayan was astonished, and left \"I always get scared\" Whenever people ask me \"what's your source?\", I get scared and upset. Sometimes I answer at once: look in this tractate. Sometimes I don't know until I search for it. If even I can't remember, what will others do? How do I console myself? I remind myself that that wasn't the purpose. My purpose was the dry laws, all the scattered laws together in one place. The book he planned and never wrote With Hashem's help, I plan another book giving the sources for every law , though it will be very cumbersome . The plan: - A law in Hilchot Shabbat sourced in Tractate Shabbat gets no citation : \"157 pages of Shabbat; go and find it yourself .\" - A Shabbat law sourced elsewhere in Shas gets a citation: this halachah stems from this tractate, that page. - A separate book , because putting sources in the Mishneh Torah would ruin its purpose : it is a chibbur , not a perush . I repeat: everything in the Mishneh Torah comes from Bavli, Yerushalmi, Tosefta, Sifra or Sifrei. It's hard to find because the Talmud's rules are scattered. Full stop. The Rabbi noted in passing: the Rambam calls the Pesach laws Hilchot Chametz uMatzah , while the Shulchan Aruch calls them Hilchot Pesach , \"interesting in itself.\" Under the plan, a law there drawn from Pesachim would go unsourced; one drawn from elsewhere would be cited. The Irony: The Most-Commented Book of the Rishonim Did the Rambam write that book? No. But dozens did it for him : Commentary Kesef Mishneh Rav Yosef Karo (author of the Beit Yosef) Maggid Mishneh Rabbeinu Manoach Lechem Mishneh …and many more Each trying to find the sources \"I'm almost sure this is correct. Yes, I say this with confidence: there is no book of the Rishonim , maybe Rashi aside, with as much commentary on it as the Mishneh Torah. \" The Rif and the Rashba have a few; but the Ra'avad was already writing on the Rambam right away, \"and then on and on.\" \" This is the funny thing. The Rambam made a book where all you need is the Torah and my book . Now you learn his book, and around it are tens and hundreds of commentaries. \" Why? Because he never wrote the source book . Much of the debate over the Rambam is what his source was , because what he means depends on where he took it from . One commentator says it's from here, so he means this; another says from there, so he means that, and you get a machloket over the Rambam's meaning. Had he written the sources, much of that would evaporate . One of the Rabbi's teachers: \"If the Rambam had known what they would do to his book, he wouldn't have written it.\" \"The whole purpose was a clear this is it, open it, that's it .\" Takeaways Learn the Mishneh Torah, in order If you've never studied it in order, try to find the time and complete it : once a week, once a day, one or two halachot . Especially for someone without much time for halachah or Talmud. \"If you know the Mishneh Torah inside out, you basically know the whole Torah.\" A test of authority Since all of Chazal is condensed into those 14 books, anything you never came across in the Mishneh Torah is not from Chazal . Someone else said it, and it doesn't carry the same authority . Kitniyot : there's no mention at all in the Rambam . It came in around the 1300s , after him. So \" it can't have the same tokef , the same power and authority. The only thing that binds us is the Torah and the Sages of the last Sanhedrin : anything in the Bavli and Yerushalmi binds us. Other things are a notch down .\" (The recording was paused for part of this discussion.) Summary Pinchas's four criticisms: no names , people err , it discourages Talmud study, no sources . Talmud: \"Hashem is my witness\": only two or three around him study the Mishneh Torah; he teaches the Rif and Talmud to the rest. The book is a kitzur for those without the time , never a replacement. (Contrast the letter to Ibn Tibbon , to come.) Names: listed in the introduction ; the book is a chibbur like the Mishnah , not a perush like the Talmud. The Mishnah named Sages only where communities followed different valid views (Eduyot 1). After the Talmud, one halachah for all , and names would invite people to dismiss the law as one man's view. Misreadings: an author can't walk around explaining his book . No one since Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi attempted this. Sources: plain statement = Chazal; \"the Geonim\" = Geonim; \"it seems to me\" = my own. Chapters drawn from ten places ; no one can hold the whole Talmud in mind . The story of the law of murder : even the Rambam sometimes couldn't find his own source , until he did. The unwritten source book : planned, never written; so the Kesef Mishneh, Maggid Mishneh, Lechem Mishneh and countless others did it, making it the most-commented work of the Rishonim . Practical: learn the Mishneh Torah in order ; what isn't in it isn't Chazal (e.g. kitniyot ). Coming Up Next week, 8–9, the final shiur of the season: Sections 3–5. Pinchas complains the Rambam said publicly that \"the dayan of Alexandria knows nothing and doesn't know Talmud,\" and never answered his questions. Shorter, more technical sections; then five letters complete, January to March , with a new series after Pesach.","Hu2gVSkmC3c":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: the letter to Rav Pinchas ben Meshullam HaDayan of Alexandria (Hebrew original, Shilat edition), opening section Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 18 March 2025 (second part of the evening, after finishing the Letter on Martyrdom) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Where We Are in the Series # Letter Theme 1 To Ovadia the Convert (three letters) Inspiration and halachic standing of a convert 2 To the rabbis of France (Montpellier) Astrology 3 To Ibn Jabbar of Baghdad Questions raised against the Rambam; encouragement to study Torah 4 Ma'amar Kiddush Hashem Martyrdom, finished tonight 5 To Rav Pinchas HaDayan Begun tonight Plan: this letter takes us to Pesach: tonight, next week, and a final session in a couple of weeks. After Pesach, a new set of letters. Introduction: Who Was Rav Pinchas HaDayan? Pinchas son of Meshullam , originally from Provence . At some point he migrated to Egypt , where he became a dayan in Alexandria . The letter suggests the Rambam was involved in his appointment and had some overarching influence over him. A go-between to French Jewry. Recall the end of the letter on astrology : the Rambam said he answered tersely, and only because someone sent an emissary who wouldn't leave until he wrote to the rabbis of Montpellier. That someone was Pinchas . Dates: we don't know when he came to Egypt, but by 1191 he was a dayan in Alexandria, and by 1202 he had died. The letter reads as written early in his tenure . Rav Shilat concludes it was written between 1185 and 1194 , in the last fifteen years of the twelfth century. The Rambam died in 1204 , so this is the peak of his career , towards the end of his life. A strained relationship The Rambam had an excellent relationship with the rabbis of Montpellier , and with another dayan in Egypt who had also come from abroad (generally identified as Rabbi Anatoli ). With Pinchas, the relationship was \"strained, to put it lightly.\" \"We're going to see some internal rabbinic politics , but also how the Rambam responded.\" What kind of letter Written in Hebrew , because Pinchas, from France, didn't read Arabic . Very personal. \"We get to see the Rambam's middot . We also get to see his harshness , and the Rambam can be harsh. He didn't suffer fools.\" He stands his ground very strongly when he wants to. He recognises his accomplishments : \"not arrogance,\" but in a few places he does say that what he has done, no one did before him . Contents: advice on personal relationships and on public leadership , but above all a clear insight into why the Rambam wrote the Mishneh Torah . \"That is the essence of this letter.\" Less famous than the letters on martyrdom or to Yemen; not on Sefaria. The only copy the Rabbi found was Shilat's edition on Bar-Ilan , shared on screen. Part 1: \"I Was Ill, Not Ignoring You\" The Rambam opens with honorifics: the great and holy Pinchas, may God protect him, the great judge, the tower, the wonderful sage. Pinchas had written repeatedly: why have you stopped answering me? What have people told you I said about you? The only reason I haven't answered your many letters is that I've been ill , not because of things I've heard you said about me. Don't believe what's passed along I know that anything passed from one person to another gets changed and added to , all the more when people have a tendency to speak badly and spread things. So: - You may have heard I stopped answering because of what you said. Don't accept it. - I don't accept what I've heard about you, because I know how stories grow. - And even if I heard it with my own ears , or knew it a hundred percent: people embarrass me, reject me, curse me, and I don't care. I forgive. \"Should I uproot what I planted?\" Pinchas had begged: please don't throw me away, keep in touch, I feel you're ignoring me. God forbid that I should uproot what I planted and smash what I built. \"This is why it seems the Rambam had some hand in bringing him to Egypt: you're a product of mine. I'm not going to leave you. \" I wouldn't do that to an ordinary student, let alone a sage like you. Part 2: Forgiving Is Not the Same as Not Minding \"You can forgive someone and not hold a grudge , and still be upset about how they behaved .\" So the Rambam goes on: if people curse and insult me, I move on. But I was angry at what you did, and I want to tell you so. ** How Pinchas found out The other dayan (probably Rabbi Anatoli) had told Pinchas that the Rambam was very angry about one of his letters. The Rambam doesn't deny it . \"What the Rambam doesn't address is why that dayan went and told Pinchas what the Rambam had said about him. That I find troubling. Maybe there was a constructive purpose ( to'elet ); we don't know the circumstances. But it's something to point out.\" Part 3: The Dispute: Washing Before Prayer The Rambam now quotes Pinchas's earlier letter verbatim (\"your exact letter, letter by letter\"). Pinchas was in Alexandria , the Rambam in Cairo . Pinchas had written: the people rose against me and said we no longer accept you; you permit what should be forbidden. Our tradition from those before us is that one who has had a seminal emission ( ba'al keri ) may not study Torah or pray until he washes, and you let people pray and learn without washing. The halachic background Stage What applied The Torah A ba'al keri must immerse before entering the Temple . Nothing about Torah study or prayer Ezra's enactment A ba'al keri may not study Torah until he immerses; later courts extended it to prayer Abolished The public couldn't sustain it , so the decree lapsed . The Rambam understands it lapsed for everything , study and prayer Later custom In Arab lands , a widespread custom ( minhag ) not to pray until one washes the whole body in water. Not a mikveh , just washing The Rambam codifies this in the Mishneh Torah (Hilchot Tefillah, ch. 4), read in class: All the ritually impure need only wash their hands ; even if able to immerse, immersion is not required for prayer . Ezra decreed that a ba'al keri may not study Torah until he immerses… This decree was not accepted by the majority of the community, who could not observe it, so it was abolished. But a widespread custom in Shin'ar and the West is that a ba'al keri does not pray until he washes his whole body … and for a sick person it doesn't apply. So by strict law ( me'ikar hadin ): a ba'al keri may come to shul without a mikveh and without even showering , read from the Torah and pray. Washing before prayer is only a custom of certain places. Part 4: \"Even Hillel Would Have Been Angry\" I think even Hillel would have got angry at your letter. The story (Shabbat 31a) Two men bet 400 zuz on whether Hillel could be made angry. One went to him on Erev Shabbat while Hillel was bathing for Shabbat , with absurd questions: why do the Babylonians have round heads? Why do Africans have wide feet? Each time Hillel came out, answered patiently, and went back. Finally the man said: are you Hillel, the great Nasi? May there not be many like you in Israel! Why? Because of you I've lost 400 zuz. Hillel: \"Better you lose 400 zuz, and another 400, than Hillel lose his temper.\"** Hillel is the exemplar of patience , and the Rambam says even he would have been provoked. Three things that angered the Rambam 1. You inflated a handful of ignorant people into \"the whole community.\" You wrote that everyone rose against you, old and young . I was told it was a few ignorant people who said this to you in shul. You made it a big thing. You gave them importance, made it sound as if great rabbis were challenging you, and then you asked me about it. 2. You gave in to them. The rest of the letter implied Pinchas followed them: from now on, no Torah study or prayer in shul without washing after an emission. 3. You preached about its \"severity.\" They asked you to explain how serious this is, and you gave a sermon on its severity. What severity? We're talking about a custom! \"I love these words of the Rambam. I identify with them so many times, when you hear people giving sermons, pilpulim and derashot about how important something is, and it's a minhag . People are going crazy about it.\" \"They laugh at us\" It's a custom in certain Arab places . In Italy, France and Provence , where you come from, as you know, they never washed . When people from the West come East and see we pray only after bathing, they laugh at us and say you're copying the Arabs , since the Arabs are particular about washing. What you should have said When intellectually deficient people come and challenge you, scold them . Say the truth, and don't hide from it because there's a crowd. The answer: אין דברי תורה מקבלין טומאה : words of Torah do not contract impurity. A ba'al keri may pray and study. Washing before prayer is a custom that took hold in the Arab countries. Where they wash, keep your custom; where they don't, don't change. Rabbi Daniel It appears a Rabbi Daniel (unidentified) had been telling everyone it was permitted , and Pinchas, \"for some reason, didn't get on with Rabbi Daniel .\" So when people complained about Rabbi Daniel, Pinchas sided with them: he's wrong; everyone must wash first. Part 5: \"How Does He Know Whether I Wash?\" \"This is unbelievable.\" Pinchas reported that someone claimed the Rambam himself doesn't wash after an emission. The Rambam: this intellectually impoverished person is lying. I have never failed to wash, except when I was ill . Why would I change my custom? \"He's almost making fun of Pinchas: how does this man know whether I go and wash? Why are you telling me what he said?\" And: I don't want to talk about this any more. The bottom line of the section I didn't stop answering because I was angry, because I forgive . But I was angry, and you need to know why: you wrote something that would have angered even Hillel. You gave in to the crowd, to ignorance and to the mob. Summary Rav Pinchas ben Meshullam , from Provence, became dayan of Alexandria , apparently through the Rambam; he was the go-between who got the Rambam to answer the rabbis of Montpellier. The letter dates from 1185–1194 . The relationship was strained . This Hebrew letter shows the Rambam's warmth, his harshness , and above all why he wrote the Mishneh Torah . \"I was ill, not ignoring you.\" Stories grow as they're passed on; don't believe them . Even if true: I forgive. I won't uproot what I planted. But forgiving isn't the same as not minding : the Rambam tells Pinchas plainly he was angry . (Why the other dayan passed that on is left unexplained, \"troubling.\") The halachah: Ezra's decree requiring a ba'al keri to immerse before Torah and prayer was abolished . Torah does not contract impurity. Washing before prayer is only a custom of Arab lands, unknown in Europe. Pinchas's failures: he turned a few ignorant people into \"the whole community\" , gave in to them , and preached the \"severity\" of a custom . \"Even Hillel would have been angry.\" Coming Up Next week, same time (8–9): the main point of the letter. Pinchas criticises the Mishneh Torah , published only a few years earlier, and the Rambam lashes out . \"It's going to get personal.\" Along the way, the clearest window into why the Rambam wrote the Mishneh Torah .","Isos0apG4Ls":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: Iggeret HaShmad / Ma'amar Kiddush Hashem , conclusion (Sections 4–5) Shiur: Eighth class in the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 18 March 2025 (first part of the evening, before the letter to Rav Pinchas HaDayan) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap The question: may a Jew acknowledge Muhammad to the one coercing him, to save his life? Another rabbi said those who did were wicked deniers of God; the Rambam demolished that, then answered in five parts: § Content 1 When must one die? Three cardinal sins: always. Other sins: if the coercer wants his own benefit, never; if he wants to make the Jew sin, during shmad , always (even one-on-one), otherwise only before ten Jews 2 Chillul Hashem (two kinds for everyone, two for leaders) and kiddush Hashem , with their punishment and reward 3 One killed for Hashem: an incomparable reward. One who should have died and didn't : wrong, but no punishment in this world By Section 1, it would seem that during shmad one must die rather than acknowledge Muhammad, even privately. Section 4 explains why not. Section 4: Why This Persecution Is Different Every persecution in the Sages' time was about an action , doing or not doing: don't learn Torah, don't circumcise, have forbidden relations. This one asks us only to say something. You can keep the whole Torah in private; nobody requires you to break Shabbat. They want only that we acknowledge their prophet, and they know perfectly well that those who say it don't mean it. The ruling One who gives his life rather than acknowledge Muhammad has done well : he has sanctified Hashem's name, and his reward is great. But if he asks me \"should I die or acknowledge?\", I say: acknowledge, and live. But don't linger. Don't walk about the streets where they'll ask you again. Stay home; if you must do business, do it quietly. Keep a low profile so you're not tested again. When the Sages said \"die rather than transgress,\" they never meant speech , only doing what is forbidden or not doing what is commanded. We've never encountered a persecution like this, demanding only words. Keep every mitzvah you still can You must still try to keep all the mitzvot . Don't say \"I've already done something forbidden, so…\" If you were forced to break Shabbat , don't then think you may carry too. Yeravam ben Nevat , who led all Israel to idolatry, will be punished in the World to Come for the golden calves, and for not making eruv tavshilin : the Sages' standard example of the most minor mitzvah. (Avraham, by contrast, kept the whole Torah, \"even eruv tavshilin\" .) Two sins at once: if someone steals his friend's wine and pours it as a libation to an idol , he's executed and doesn't repay the wine: קים ליה בדרבה מיניה , the greater penalty absorbs the lesser. That's only in this world. In the next, he answers for both . \"We often have this tendency to see things black and white: I'm not so observant with this, fine. No. Every detail is taken into account. Not like the other rabbi, who said if you acknowledged Muhammad there's no point going to shul any more.\" Leave, even without your family My advice to all: go to a place where you can practise freely , without being made to acknowledge a false prophet, even if it means leaving family and property . The wise know that family, spouse, children and possessions are beautiful, but none of it is eternal the way doing Hashem's will is. As the Rambam himself did. Choose the better place. Even between two Jewish cities, live in the more observant. A person should live only where there are ten righteous people : Hashem told Avraham He'd spare Sedom for ten . Environment shapes you. David: כי גרשוני היום מהסתפח בנחלת ה' לאמר לך עבוד אלהים אחרים : \"they drove me from Hashem's heritage, saying go serve other gods .\" The Gemara: living outside the Land among idolaters is like serving idols. הלא משנאיך ה' אשנא : \"I hate those who hate You.\" Avraham left his birthplace and family, Ur Kasdim . Advice, or obligation? Situation Leaving is Only acknowledging the prophet (this shmad ) Advice : get out as soon as you can Being forced to actually transgress Obligatory : \"go now. Travel night and day\" until you find a place to practise freely. The world is big אח לא פדה יפדה איש : \"a man cannot redeem his brother\" (Tehillim 49). Don't use family as an excuse : \"my children are too young to travel at night in the mountains.\" \"When you care about something, you travel even at the hardest times, even with little children.\" If you could have left and didn't, coercion becomes almost your own fault . \"But Mashiach is coming\" Those who say \"we'll wait here, Mashiach is coming any day\" are wrong. There's no fixed time. The Rambam is forceful about this elsewhere too: he opposes anyone who says Mashiach is coming today, tomorrow, or in ten years. Our duty is to keep the Torah , and if we merit Mashiach, wonderful. \"When Mashiach comes, life continues as usual\" ; the main difference is sovereignty. It's no excuse to stay somewhere you'll have to give up your religion. The Mishneh Torah is harsher Codifying this later in the Mishneh Torah (Yesodei HaTorah, Chapter 5), the Rambam is more forceful : one who is coerced but could have fled and didn't , \"I've got my nice house here, my friends, my family,\" and is then told \"bow to Jesus or die\" and complies: הרי הוא ככלב שב על קיאו : he is like a dog returning to its vomit , considered a deliberate idolater, and loses his share in the World to Come. Perhaps the young Rambam of the letter was giving hope and consolation ; codifying, he took the harder line. Shulchan Aruch, Yoreh De'ah 157 follows it: failing to die when required is wrong but carries no known consequence, unless you could have left and chose to stay. Section 5: If You Stay Anyway If you can leave and don't, you're doing wrong, close to desecrating Hashem's name , and will be punished. And yet every mitzvah you do there is enormously valuable, with extra reward , because you do it where you're persecuted for doing it. \"Again the Rambam doesn't take a black-or-white approach.\" But don't stay in order to earn more reward; keep thinking how to get out. Don't push them away Even those regularly desecrating Shabbat there: don't reject them . Draw them to the mitzvot; receive them warmly . לא יבוזו לגנב כי יגנוב : \"don't despise the thief when he steals\" (Mishlei). The Sages: when sinners come to \"steal\" a mitzvah , coming to shul to pray or do something good, don't embarrass them; bring them close. The close Since our exile we've never been free of persecution. We grow up in pain from birth. We know persecutions end. May this one pass, and may Yirmeyahu's words be fulfilled: Israel's sins will be sought and not found . \"How appropriate to end the letter on that note. We've always been through difficult times, and we are going through difficult times. But we know we will be victorious.\" A Question to Think About The Rambam says one who died rather than acknowledge Muhammad did well and sanctified Hashem's name. But elsewhere he holds that one who dies when not required to has done wrong . Here he rules you need not die, so how is dying praiseworthy? \"There seems to be a grey area. There is a technical answer,\" for those who stay after the recording. Summary of the Whole Letter First half: the Rambam castigates a rabbi who wrote that anyone acknowledging Muhammad forfeited everything, and that there's no point in mitzvot any more. Section 1: when one must give one's life. Section 2: chillul Hashem and kiddush Hashem. Section 3: the martyr's exalted reward; one who gave in did wrong, but life goes on. Section 4: this persecution demands only words , so acknowledge rather than die , keep every mitzvah you can, and leave . If forced to sin , leaving is obligatory ; family and \"Mashiach is coming\" are no excuse. The Mishneh Torah later makes staying when one could leave far graver. Section 5: staying is wrong, but every mitzvah done under persecution is precious, and those who sin there must be embraced, not rejected . Coming Up Next, the same evening: the Rambam's letter to Rav Pinchas HaDayan of Alexandria, \"a very halachic letter,\" and the first between the Rambam and a colleague .","pP1JLfttkf0":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: Iggeret HaShmad / Ma'amar Kiddush Hashem , continued (Sections 1–3) Shiur: Seventh class in the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 11 March 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap: Section 1, When Must One Die? The first half of the letter tore apart the rabbi who called anyone acknowledging Muhammad wicked. Then the Rambam began his own five-section answer: Three cardinal sins Other sins: coercer wants his own benefit Other sins: coercer wants you to sin , no shmad Other sins: coercer wants you to sin , during shmad Private Die Transgress Transgress Die Public (ten Jews) Die Transgress Die Die \"Mow my garden on Shabbat, I've got guests,\" even before a thousand Jews in a time of persecution: mow it . Ten Jews: men or women ; children and the victim himself are debated. The Rambam seems to require it only for active transgression; that's debated. Section 2: What Is Chillul Hashem? Four kinds: two for every Jew , two for sages and leaders . For every Jew 1. Sinning out of indifference. Not for pleasure or gain, and not even to rebel, but because I just don't care . Example: you're offered a kosher and a non-kosher meal, identical . If the non-kosher is tastier and you take it, that's a sin, but \"understandable.\" If they're the same and you take the non-kosher because it makes no difference to you , because Hashem's word means nothing to you, that's chillul Hashem. In front of ten Jews: public chillul Hashem. In front of even one other Jew: a lesser, \" hidden \" ( beseter ) chillul Hashem. Alone, just you and God: a sin, but not called chillul Hashem. 2. Inviting suspicion. Not sinning, but acting so that people suspect you of sin. והייתם נקיים מה' ומישראל (Matot): be clean in the eyes of Hashem and of Israel . That's why the Sages made so many enactments: mar'it ayin and chashad . The classic case: don't hang wet laundry on Shabbat , though you washed it on Friday, because people will think you washed it today. (Whether mar'it ayin and chashad are one concept or two is a discussion for another time.) The Gemara: when people say of someone, \"May God forgive him\" : he may have done nothing wrong, but he has a bad name ; people are embarrassed to be his friend. \"Better to be a fool in people's eyes than in Hashem's,\" the Sages say, and when you're doing right and not compromising yourself , don't worry what people think. But opening yourself to suspicion is different. For sages and leaders The Rambam says חכם ; the Rabbi reads it as anyone in a public or communal position : אדם חשוב שאני . 3. Permitted, but beneath your stature. Rav (Yoma 86a): \"For someone like me, chillul Hashem is buying meat and not paying immediately.\" For anyone else, \"pay me tomorrow\" is fine. For a sage, it isn't. Similar examples in the Gemara: walking four cubits without Torah or tefillin. \"You have to practise what you preach. More is expected of you.\" 4. A demeanour people don't want to be around. Not a specific act, just general conduct : business dealings without integrity, no smile , no warmth, not the finest character. \"Fine for any of us; we're all working on ourselves. But not in that position.\" People say: \"He studies Torah, and look how he behaves!\" (\"If I had more time I'd explain exactly how to conduct business and speak to people.\") He later did, in Hilchot Deot , which the class studied, more proof that this letter came before the Mishneh Torah. The Sephardi ideal: \"Some see a chacham as an ivory tower : he sits in his study, looks down on the people and issues rulings. That was never the Sephardi approach. The Sephardi chacham lives with the people. He's part of them.\" People should be happy in his presence. The Opposite: Kiddush Hashem קידוש השם הפך חילול השם Chillul Hashem Kiddush Hashem 1. Sinning out of indifference Doing a mitzvah purely because Hashem commanded it , not for honour, wealth, recognition or even reward in Olam HaBa, before others 2. Inviting suspicion Conduct that makes people praise you: ישראל אשר בך אתפאר . \"Wow, that's a good Jew.\" Not acting in order to be praised, but as the result of how you live 3. A leader doing what's beneath him A great person refraining even from permitted things unbefitting him 4. A leader people avoid A leader whose presence people enjoy \"Hashem wanted to give Israel merit\" The Mishnah (end of Makkot): רצה הקדוש ברוך הוא לזכות את ישראל, לפיכך הרבה להם תורה ומצוות . The Rambam explains: with so many mitzvot, at least one will surely be done purely , for its own sake, and that alone earns Olam HaBa. \"It's very hard to get to the bottom of why we do what we do… When we stop to think, we might not be pleasantly surprised: social pressure, livelihood, honour. לב יודע מרת נפשו : everyone knows his own flaws.\" The Severity of Chillul Hashem Four ways it stands apart: 1 אחד שוגג ואחד מזיד : inadvertent is like deliberate . (In Pirkei Avot, learned a few months ago, the Rambam says deliberate is of course worse, but both count as chillul Hashem.) \"I didn't realise people would suspect me.\" \"I didn't realise it was beneath my stature.\" But you did it 2 No credit : אין מקיפין בחילול השם . Hashem isn't a shopkeeper who waits for payment; the consequence is immediate 3 Done privately, punished publicly 4 Hardest to atone : neither teshuvah, Yom Kippur nor suffering suffices; only death : אם יכופר העון הזה לכם עד תמותון As Hilchot Teshuvah taught: ordinary negative mitzvot need teshuvah and Yom Kippur; karet and death-penalty sins add suffering; chillul Hashem needs all those and death . But all this is only when the act was willing . \"Inadvertent\" still means you chose the act without thinking of its consequences. Under compulsion is something else , which comes next. And just as chillul Hashem is grave, kiddush Hashem is a great mitzvah, incumbent on every Jew : ונקדשתי בתוך בני ישראל, אני ה' מקדשכם, המוציא אתכם מארץ מצרים להיות לכם לאלהים . Questions Mark: if I commit a chillul Hashem, realise, and then make a kiddush Hashem, does that offset it? No. \"There's no offset.\" The consequence must still be paid; the kiddush Hashem earns its own reward. And \"the reality is that someone like me has far more likely committed chillul Hashem than you have. The more communal a person, the more they have to be whiter than white.\" \"Sometimes great rabbis come out with statements which shock you. Even if you can interpret them, in your position you can't say something like that. \" Doron: didn't we say chillul Hashem doesn't take a \"bribe of mitzvot\"? That's the Midrash on אשר לא ישא פנים ולא יקח שוחד (Ekev), not this letter, but \"precisely the point.\" There's no offsetting . Mark's logic is more tempting here because kiddush Hashem is the direct opposite of chillul Hashem, unlike breaking Shabbat and giving charity, which are unrelated. Still, no : like ברוך שאמר , who takes no bribe . Section 3: One Who Died, and One Who Gave In One who gave his life Whenever the Sages required martyrdom (the three sins; public coercion to sin; coercion to sin during shmad ) and he gave his life, he sanctified Hashem's name ; before ten Jews, publicly . The Ten Martyrs , Chanah and her seven sons , all who died for Hashem. אספו לי חסידי, כורתי בריתי עלי זבח : \"Gather to Me My pious ones, who made My covenant with sacrifice.\" השבעתי אתכם בנות ירושלים בצבאות או באילות השדה : in the Sages' reading, I adjure you even in the generation of shmad ; בצבאות , those who did My will and became My host; באילות השדה , whose blood was spilled like the blood of the deer on the altar. One who merits this opportunity (the Rambam calls it an opportunity ), even if he committed terrible sins , has a share in Olam HaBa , and no one can stand in his place : הרוגי מלכות אין כל בריה יכולה לעמוד במחיצתן , even simple people, like the martyrs of Lod . One who should have died but didn't If he should have given his life and didn't , and served idols, committed adultery, or even murdered (\"shoot that Jew or I'll kill you\": \"we never have such a conflict,\" but suppose it), he has done wrong and desecrated Hashem's name under duress . But he receives none of the seven punishments : the four court executions, karet, lashes, or death at the hands of Heaven. Why? Hashem never punishes coercion. Example (Shabbat) Consequence מזיד , deliberate Breaks Shabbat knowingly, with witnesses and warning Stoning שוגג , inadvertent Mows the lawn or drives , not knowing it's Shabbat or that it's forbidden. A willed act with missing knowledge A korban (in Temple times) אונס / מתעסק , compelled or unintended Leans back and switches the light on by accident; or at gunpoint Nothing . Not even a korban ולנערה לא תעשה דבר : \"to the girl [who was assaulted] do nothing.\" So with every sin under duress: אונס רחמנא פטריה , the Torah exempts the coerced. So, astonishingly: someone who murdered under duress, publicly, with witnesses, is not called wicked, and is not disqualified as a witness . \"That's a riddle! How can it be?\" Because he didn't willingly desecrate Hashem's name. And the lesser sins? If even a death-penalty sin done under duress carries no punishment, all the more so a sin that carries only lashes (e.g. shaving with a razor). What has he actually transgressed? A lav , a simple negative commandment, by failing to sanctify Hashem's name. \"People think you get killed because you were meant to give up your life. No. \" The Midrash on ונתתי אני את פני באיש ההוא , \"I will set My face against that man \": \"that man,\" not one acting under duress. (The recording ends here, mid-section.) Summary Chillul Hashem for everyone: (1) sinning out of indifference , publicly before ten or \"hidden\" before one; (2) acting so people suspect you ( mar'it ayin , chashad : no wet laundry on Shabbat). For sages and leaders: (3) doing what's permitted but beneath your stature (Rav buying on credit); (4) a demeanour people avoid . The Sephardi chacham lives among the people, not in an ivory tower. Kiddush Hashem is the mirror: mitzvot purely for Hashem; a life that makes people say \"what a good Jew\"; leaders above reproach and pleasant to be with. Chillul Hashem is uniquely severe : even inadvertent, no credit, private becomes public, only death atones. No offsetting , not even by kiddush Hashem. One who dies for Hashem has an exalted share in Olam HaBa, whatever his sins. One who should have died but gave in (even to murder) receives no punishment , isn't called wicked, and remains a valid witness . He has transgressed only a lav . Coming Up Next week: the rest of Section 3; Section 4 : how this persecution differs from others, and the Rambam's answer about acknowledging Muhammad; Section 5 : how to live under shmad .","mcFMlHdBX2I":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: Iggeret HaShmad / Ma'amar Kiddush Hashem , continued Shiur: Sixth class in the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 4 March 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Last week: the question (should someone forced to declare Muhammad a prophet do so, or die?), the other rabbi's harsh answer (such people are heretics, gentiles, wicked, and even their prayers are sinful), and the Rambam's demolition of it: the distinction between duress and willingness ; the duty to weigh words; Moshe, Eliyahu, Yeshayahu and the angel punished for speaking ill of Israel; Rabbi Meir and Rabbi Eliezer who outwardly complied; Babylon and the Greek period. Part 1: The Last Criticisms Irrelevant points! Why is he discussing Mecca, or how many people Muhammad killed? \"Why am I so harsh?\" Hashem is my witness: had he spoken even worse of us , I'd have accepted it and said, \"we have sinned,\" and judged him favourably, assumed he meant well… Some academics use this line to argue the Rambam himself had once outwardly renounced his faith . \"There's no proof at all, but it's interesting.\" More likely he speaks collectively , for the people he's defending. What he can't stay silent about: the rabbi wrote that one who acknowledged Muhammad and then prays , even at home, sins and earns no reward. Whatever is written in a book, true or not, spreads. That's why there are so many false opinions among people: once something is in a book, it's very hard to believe it untrue, and it becomes accepted. I feared this letter would reach the simple and they'd think: I said I believe in Muhammad to save my life, so my prayers are worthless; why pray, why do any mitzvah? The wicked rewarded for tiny mitzvot The sin The small deed The reward Achav Had Navot killed for his vineyard ( הרצחת וגם ירשת? ) Humbled himself and, the Sages say, fasted for two and a half hours The punishment was deferred past his lifetime Eglon , king of Moab Oppressed Israel for 18 years When Ehud said דבר אלהים לי אליך , \"a word of God for you,\" he stood up from his throne in respect (just before Ehud stabbed him) Ruth , his daughter in tradition, became the ancestor of David , and of Mashiach Nebuchadnezzar Destroyed the Temple and killed multitudes As scribe to Merodach Baladan , he saw a letter to Chizkiyahu greeting the king before his God , and ran four steps after the messenger to reverse the order Forty years of sovereignty (Sanhedrin 96a) Esav On the day of the lentil stew, five sins : murder, adultery with a betrothed girl, denying resurrection, denying God, despising the birthright Asked Yitzchak how to tithe salt , to impress his father, out of honour for him His dynasty endures until Mashiach , who comes only once Esav's reward for honouring Yitzchak is paid If Hashem rewards such people for one tiny mitzvah , how much more a Jew who acknowledged Muhammad under duress and keeps doing mitzvot at home ? No \"bribe of mitzvot,\" in either direction People imagine Rosh Hashanah as scales : more mitzvot than sins and you win. No. Every mitzvah is rewarded and every sin has its consequence: לא ישא פנים ולא יקח שוחד , Hashem takes no bribe of mitzvot . You can't say \"knock this aveirah off against my mitzvot.\" So one wrong act, even if it was wrong, can't cancel the reward of every mitzvah after it. Part 2: The Rambam's Answer: Five Sections When I saw this letter, which made my eyes sick , I girded myself to gather spices from the early books and compound a remedy to heal this eye-sickness. He sets out five sections: § Topic 1 When must one give one's life? 2 Chillul Hashem : what it is, and its consequences 3 The status of one killed for Hashem, and of one who should have died but didn't 4 How this persecution differs from earlier ones, and the answer about Muhammad 5 How to live if one must remain under the shmad Section 1: When Must You Give Your Life? The three cardinal sins Idolatry, forbidden relations, murder : יהרג ואל יעבור , die rather than transgress, always : - in any period , persecution or not; - in public or private , even one-on-one (\"commit adultery with this woman or I'll kill you,\" with no one else ever knowing); - whatever the coercer's motive : to make you sin, or for his own benefit (\"kill my enemy for me so I don't have to\"). \"Everyone agrees to this.\" Every other mitzvah: a flowchart Step 1: why is he forcing you? - For his own benefit → transgress and live , even in public and even in a time of persecution . - Proof: Esther. How could she go to Achashverosh , publicly, in a time of decree? (1) She was passive , קרקע עולם , \"like the ground,\" which removes the cardinal-sin problem. (2) He acted for his own pleasure , not to make her sin. - To make you abandon your religion → Step 2: is it a time of shmad ? - Yes → give your life , even in private , even for a minor mitzvah (e.g. \"I want to see you, a Jew, wear sha'atnez \"). - No → Step 3: in front of ten Jews? - In private (fewer than ten) → transgress . - In public (ten) → give your life . His own benefit To make you sin, no shmad To make you sin, during shmad Three cardinal sins Die Die Die Other sins, private Transgress Transgress Die Other sins, public (10) Transgress Die Die Example: a gentile in front of your whole community on Shabbat, even in a time of persecution: \"My garden must be mowed today, guests are coming!\" He wants his lawn mowed , so mow it. Note: the letter cites neither the Mishneh Torah nor the Moreh , because he hadn't written them yet. This is the very start of his career. He codifies all this later. \"People think you only have to give up your life for the three cardinal sins. True, but there are many more times you have to.\" Who are \"the ten\"? Jews : men or women; ten Jewish women count. Children of educable age: a dispute. Does the victim count himself? He may die (so don't count him), or he may live (so do). The Minchat Chinuch (mitzvah 296, Kiddush Hashem ) discusses it. \"Who likes to go into these kinds of questions?\" Source for ten : a gezeirah shavah from בתוך בני ישראל (\"I shall be sanctified among the children of Israel\"), via תוך in Korach and עדה , \"congregation,\" with the spies : עד מתי לעדה הרעה הזאת , ten men. Was the Holocaust a \"time of shmad \"? The Rabbi asked: if a Nazi said \"shave your beard with a razor or I'll kill you,\" for no benefit of his own, just to make a Jew sin, is that a time of shmad ? It was persecution of extermination , not mainly religious. - Doron: the Purim decree was extermination, and it's still considered a time of shmad . \" 100% correct. Thank you.\" So the Holocaust would be too, and one would have to die rather than shave, even one-on-one. \"Wow.\" - Robert: does it all apply if the coercer is a Jew ? Yes, the same halachot. \"Passive\" isn't the same The commentators note the Rambam requires martyrdom only for an active transgression . \" If we see you wearing tefillin, we'll kill you \" asks only that you refrain , which is passive, so you needn't die. ( Brit milah is different; there's a discussion.) Why does a time of shmad make it stricter? Robert asked. The source is the Gemara in Sanhedrin ; no explicit rationale, \"but it's self-explanatory.\" In a time of national persecution, when the nations as a whole are trying to bring us down, that's when we must stand stronger , even unto death. Chanah and her seven sons A participant asked whether Chanah was right to let her young sons die. From all accounts the children chose themselves (as in the Kinot of Tisha B'Av). The conversos? \"In those times, yes, they should have given their lives.\" But they didn't, and they're still Jews : \"That's the mistake people make. They think if you were meant to die and didn't, you're out. The Rambam says: big mistake. \" That's Section 3 . Section 2 (beginning): Chillul Hashem Two kinds apply to everyone , and two more to sages and leaders . For everyone A sin with no benefit, done because you \"don't care\" : not desire, not gain. Swearing falsely (\"I didn't eat today\" when you did): pure disregard. Done in public (ten Jews), it is chillul Hashem . A modern example: someone leaving observance who sins to make a point . The opposite, kiddush Hashem : doing a mitzvah for no reason but that Hashem commanded it , publicly, is the greatest sanctification. Actions that look like sin , though they aren't: where the onlooker will clearly conclude you're sinning. This is the root of mar'it ayin . For sages and public figures: two further kinds, next week. Summary The Rambam is harsh because the rabbi's letter was written and spreading , and would make the simple think their mitzvot are worthless. Achav, Eglon, Nebuchadnezzar, Esav : the wicked were rewarded for tiny deeds. No bribe of mitzvot : every act counts separately, so a coerced declaration can't wipe out one's prayers. When to die: Three cardinal sins : always. Other sins : if the coercer wants his own benefit , transgress. If he wants you to sin : in a time of shmad , die even in private; otherwise, die only in public (ten Jews) . Only for active transgressions. Ten = Jewish adults (men or women); children and the victim himself are debated (Minchat Chinuch 296). A decree of extermination (Purim, and so the Holocaust) counts as a time of shmad . Chillul Hashem for everyone: sinning out of sheer disregard, publicly; and conduct that looks like sin ( mar'it ayin ). Coming Up Next week: Chillul Hashem for sages and leaders ; what happens to one killed for Hashem, and to one who should have died but didn't (Section 3); how the Rambam's persecution was different , and his answer about Muhammad (Section 4); and how to live under shmad (Section 5). Then the next letter. \"Surprisingly, for once we're ahead of schedule.\"","kYEutkwYOpg":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: Iggeret HaShmad / Ma'amar Kiddush Hashem (written in Arabic in the Rambam's twenties; read in Hebrew translation) Shiur: Fifth class in the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 25 February 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction So far the letters have gone to Israel (Ovadia), France (astrology) and Iraq (Ibn Jabbar), \"so you see how the Rambam is spread out.\" This, the longest so far , will take two or three sessions. \"This letter is a complete and absolute treasure.\" Two names The Rambam gave it no title. Two 14th-century Spanish Rishonim name it differently: Name Rivash (Rabbi Yitzchak bar Sheshet) אגרת השמד : the letter on shmad , decrees forcing Jews to abandon their religion Tashbetz (Rabbi Shimon ben Tzemach Duran, Spain and later Algeria) מאמר קידוש השם : the essay on sanctifying Hashem's name When was it written? Probably the Rambam's first known letter , written under 30 , and the Rabbi thinks in his early twenties , while he was himself living through the persecution (the Almohad shmad , which affected most Jewish communities of the time). His years of wandering (approximate dates, per Rav Shilat): Year Age 1138 0 Born in Córdoba ~1160 ~22 To Fez, Morocco , because of persecution ~1161 23 Begins the Commentary on the Mishnah ~1165 ~27 A few months in Israel ; \"it didn't work out\" 1166 Egypt 1168 30 Completes the Commentary in Egypt In the Letter to Yemen : how could I be exact in halacha, exiled from city to city, kingdom to kingdom? At the end of the Commentary on the Mishnah : Hashem decreed exile for him \"from one end of the earth to the other,\" and he wrote parts of it on board ship . Egypt had no such decree, so the letter predates it. \"To write with this confidence, clarity, precision, wisdom, and sarcasm at such an age is astonishing.\" This sharpness we don't see later in his life. Language: almost certainly written in Arabic . The Hebrew we have is less precise than the Rambam's usual perfect Hebrew, \"the kind of Hebrew we find in his European counterparts, the Tosafists.\" The translator wasn't the best. The question Someone forced to declare that Muhammad is a true prophet : should he say it to save his life , though it may lead to his children assimilating among the Muslims and to abandoning the mitzvot ? Or should he die rather than say it? He had asked another rabbi (identity unknown), didn't like the answer, and sent it to the Rambam. \"Remarkably, the majority of this letter is devoted to dismantling the previous rabbi's answer\" ; the Rambam's own ruling comes at the end. Part 1: \"A Weak, Tasteless Answer\" שאל איש מאנשי דורנו לאיש מאנשי החכמה לפי דבריו : someone of our generation asked a man who, by his own account , is wise… … someone who never experienced the persecution (may Hashem annul it). Had he lived it, he'd never have answered as he did. ויען הנשאל על זה דבר חלוש אין לו טעם : he gave a weak answer with no flavour , rambling, which would mislead even people lacking sense . (The original comparison is to women. The Rabbi notes it reflects the Rambam's time, when women were far less educated than men.) Why the Rambam won't quote it all I'd quote the bulk of it, long and weak as it is, but Hashem has given us the gift of speech : מי שם פה לאדם . So a person should be more sparing with his words than with his money. Don't say much with little content. Kohelet: כי בא החלום ברוב עניין, וקול כסיל ברוב דברים : a fool is known by many words. Iyov's friends: הרוב דברים לא ייענה? , \"will a multitude of words be answered?\"; איוב לא בדעת ידבר . \"We're very careful how we spend money. We should be at least as careful with our words.\" Part 2: The Rabbi's Errors The rabbi said The Rambam's reply 1 Acknowledging Muhammad is denying God : כל המודה בעבודה זרה כאילו כפר בכל התורה כולה Can't he tell the difference between acknowledging idolatry willingly , like Yeravam and his followers, and saying someone is a prophet out of fear of the sword ? 2 Whoever says it is a gentile , even if he keeps the whole Torah in public and in private Is there no difference between one who doesn't keep Shabbat because of the sword and one who doesn't by choice ? 3 If such a man enters their house of prayer (even saying nothing) and then prays in a synagogue , his prayer is a sin (Yirmeyahu: שתיים רעות עשה עמי , serving idols one day and coming to the Temple the next) Yirmeyahu condemned willing idolaters. This man sits in a mosque under duress and then goes to shul. You call his prayer a sin? 4 He's a rasha , invalid as a witness Calling him wicked is the core outrage 5 Even the Christians agree one should die rather than acknowledge Muhammad \" I was astonished. Have we no Torah, that we need proofs from Christians ? And if the nations offer their children to their gods, should we?\" \" Woe that such a question must be asked, and woe to one who answers so.\" \"I said I wouldn't judge him till I'd heard all his words\" : משיב דבר בטרם ישמע, אוולת היא לו . Perhaps, as Shlomo said, טוב אחרית דבר מראשיתו , the end would be better than the beginning. It wasn't. \"The beginning of his words is folly, and the end is worse.\" Part 3: Prepare Before You Speak, and More Before You Write Never speak in public until you've gone over your words four times. The Midrash learns it from אז ראה ויספרה, הכינה וגם חקרה : four expressions of preparation before Hashem \"spoke\" the Torah. \"If only everyone who gets up to speak would prepare four times. Maybe once before would be good! \" And what you put in writing , to be copied and passed around, you should check a thousand times , if that were possible. People give the written word more credence (as we keep seeing in these letters). This rabbi wrote once , sure of himself, and disseminated it unfinished : \"he has spread darkness.\" Part 4: Speaking Ill of Israel: Four Who Were Punished \"The fundamental mistake underlying everything he wrote\": condemning Jewish communities. 1. Moshe: \"They won't believe me\" In Egypt, Israel was at its lowest: - Only the tribe of Levi circumcised. When the korban Pesach required milah, Moshe circumcised, Yehoshua performed peri'ah and Aharon metzitzah , until the blood of milah mixed with the blood of the Pesach : בדמייך חיי . - They were steeped in forbidden relationships (a man marrying two sisters, and more), \"the 49th level of impurity.\" Yet when Moshe said והן לא יאמינו לי , \"they won't believe me,\" Hashem rebuked him: הן מאמינים בני מאמינים , \"they are believers, children of believers. You will not believe in the end\" (at the rock). And he was punished at once : his hand turned white with tzara'at . The Gemara: החושד בכשרים לוקה בגופו . 2. Eliyahu: \"Israel has forsaken Your covenant\" All of Israel but 7,000 served Ba'al. Eliyahu said קנא קנאתי… כי עזבו בריתך בני ישראל : \"surely he was right?\" The Midrash (Chazit, on Shir HaShirim) : Is it your covenant? They've torn down Your altars. Are they your altars? They've killed Your prophets. And you're still alive. Look at the nations: they build great temples to idols; why come to accuse My people? 3. Yeshayahu: \"A people of impure lips\" In Yeshayahu's time they had mezuzot on their doors and idols inside , murder, אכול ושתו כי מחר נמות : \"eat and drink, for tomorrow we die.\" Yet when he said בתוך עם טמא שפתיים אנכי יושב , \"I dwell among a people of impure lips,\" the seraph touched his mouth with a burning coal . The Rabbi adds the tradition that his sin was fully atoned only when Menasheh killed him. 4. The angel against Yehoshua the Kohen Gadol When the Satan accused Yehoshua (whose sons had married improper wives), Hashem said יגער ה' בך השטן : \"Hashem rebuke you, Satan!\" If the pillars of the world (Moshe, Eliyahu, Yeshayahu, the ministering angels) were punished for a small thing , perhaps justified, about people who sinned willingly (idolatry, murder, adultery), how dare this man call whole communities sinners, wicked, gentiles, unfit witnesses and deniers of God, people who did nothing willingly, only under persecution? Does he not know that Hashem never abandons those who sin under duress? When Yitzchak smelled Yaakov's clothes ( וירח את ריח בגדיו ), the Sages read בגדיו as בוגדיו , his traitors : even our betrayers smell sweet to Hashem. \"The Rambam hasn't yet said whether it's right to acknowledge Muhammad. He's talking after the fact : if you did it, Hashem won't abandon you.\" Part 5: The Sages Who Pretended Rabbi Meir Caught in a persecution: \"You're Meir!\" \"No, I'm not Jewish.\" \"Then eat this\": pork . He got away by pretending to eat without eating (Avodah Zarah 18b). \"You might think: eating pork isn't one of the three cardinal sins, so he didn't need to die. It's not so simple ; in that situation, halachically, he'd have had to give his life.\" By this rabbi's logic, Rabbi Meir is a gentile. He pretended to be a non-Jew in public and was a Jew in private, exactly what the rabbi said makes you a gentile. Rabbi Eliezer Arrested for minut (heresy), worse than idolatry: a Sefer Torah written by an idolater is merely unusable, but one written by a min must be burned . The governor: How can a man as wise as you waste his time on this religion? Rabbi Eliezer answered: נאמן עלי הדיין , \" The Judge is trustworthy to me\" (Avodah Zarah 16b). The governor took it as you are right; Rabbi Eliezer meant Hashem . \" Dimissus , you're released.\" He let the official believe he agreed with heresy , worse than idolatry, while his heart was with Hashem. By this rabbi's logic, Rabbi Eliezer is invalid. What's being demanded now In this persecution no one asks us to serve an idol , to bow to Jesus or an image. They only want us to say we believe Muhammad is a true prophet, and they know we don't believe it. Nebuchadnezzar's Babylon In Babylon all but Chananyah, Mishael and Azaryah bowed to the image, \"even the sages.\" No one ever called them wicked, and Hashem never counted it as idolatry, because they were forced. The Midrash on Esther: they served idols only outwardly, so I acted toward them only outwardly . Haman's decree looked like destruction, but inwardly Hashem had a plan. So by this rabbi's logic, he is criticising Hashem . The Greeks One of the Greek decrees: Jews could not lock their doors , so they couldn't secretly keep mitzvot. Publicly they acted like Greeks; secretly, when they could, they kept the mitzvot. Hashem never condemned them; the Sages called them righteous and instituted prayers for them. \"I didn't know this, actually.\" Summary The letter ( Iggeret HaShmad / Ma'amar Kiddush Hashem ), probably the Rambam's first, written in his twenties under persecution . The question: declare Muhammad a prophet to live, or die? Most of the letter dismantles another rabbi's answer , which branded those who complied heretics, gentiles, wicked, unfit witnesses , forbade their prayers, and cited Christians as proof. Duress vs. willingness : the rabbi failed to make the basic distinction. Be careful with words : prepare speech four times, writing far more. Speaking ill of Israel cost Moshe (tzara'at), Eliyahu , Yeshayahu (the coal) and the accuser of Yehoshua , though those they spoke of sinned willingly . Rabbi Meir, Rabbi Eliezer , Babylon, the Greek period: Jews who outwardly complied under duress were never condemned; Hashem never abandons them . Coming Up Next week: the last criticisms, then the Rambam's own answer: five categories of martyrdom , and what to do in practice.","qWfj4sIpJHc":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: the letter to Rabbi Yosef ibn Jabbar of Baghdad (c. 1195; written in Arabic, read in Hebrew translation) Shiur: Fourth class in the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 11 February 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Ibn Jabbar's letter itself has not survived; we know its questions from the reply. Introduction So far: the three letters to Ovadia the convert , and the two-part Letter on Astrology . The longer essays ( Martyrdom , Resurrection ) were next, but first some shorter letters \"to get a taste\": tonight Ibn Jabbar , next week the letter to a dayan . Who was Ibn Jabbar? Yosef ibn Jabbar (\"son of Avi al-Khayr\") of Baghdad : by his own description a simple Jew , who could barely read Hebrew and studied the Rambam through his Arabic Commentary on the Mishnah . Baghdad was home to the Exilarchate and Geonic establishment , which didn't like the Rambam's writings, \"and the Rambam didn't like them at all either\": he criticised them for using communal funds for their lifestyle, and argued with them over halacha. Ibn Jabbar defended the Rambam publicly and wrote for clarification of what people were saying. c. 1195 , near the end of the Rambam's life, when he was extremely busy . Last week's letter ended: \"I only wrote because the messenger stood over me.\" Yet he took the time for this simple man, clearly admiring his simplicity and idealism . The letter mixes halacha and principles of faith , and ends in an unusually personal vein. (The screen-share failed this week; the Rabbi sent the text as photos to the Tuesday-night WhatsApp group after calling on \"Doron, the engineer.\") Part 1: \"You Are Not an Am HaAretz\" You call yourself ignorant, but I see from your letter that you are knowledgeable, and that you work hard to understand our Commentary on the Mishnah . You don't yet understand the Mishneh Torah , written in Hebrew. Do not belittle yourself or call yourself an am ha'aretz . You are our student and beloved to us. Study in any language Whoever studies, even one verse , and understands it, does well. It makes no difference whether you learn in Hebrew, Aramaic or Arabic (today, English or French). What matters is that you understand . If even Kriat Shema may be said in any language, \"all the more so\" Torah study. \"Don't feel it's not the real thing.\" Never give up One who doesn't study at all desecrates God's name ; one who studied once but doesn't keep learning neglects the mitzvah of Talmud Torah , equal to all the mitzvot. Don't despair of reaching perfection. Many great sages, Rabbi Akiva for certain, started very late. Learn enough Hebrew Learn enough Hebrew to read the Mishneh Torah. It's easy: master one book of the fourteen and you'll understand the rest. The Rambam believed the Mishneh Torah was accessible to anyone with basic Hebrew . I won't translate it into Arabic : its beauty would be lost. In fact I'm having my Commentary on the Mishnah and Sefer HaMitzvot , written in Arabic, translated into Hebrew. (Elsewhere he says he regrets writing them in Arabic.) May Hashem grant you the success of both worlds . Why both? As Hilchot Teshuvah taught: Hashem gives this world so that you can build your share in the next. Part 2: Resurrection, Again \"This issue of the Rambam supposedly not believing in a physical resurrection really bogged him down for a lot of his career.\" What you heard in Baghdad, that I deny resurrection, is slander . Whoever says it is either cunning (they know I didn't mean it and want to defame me) or a fool who confuses Olam HaBa with Techiyat HaMetim. As learned in Hilchot Teshuvah: for many Rishonim they're the same , resurrection ushers in a new, physical world to come. For the Rambam they're separate : Olam HaBa is spiritual and exists now ; resurrection is physical. His full Essay on Resurrection will reach you. \"There is a major problem with the Rambam's conception of resurrection in the whole scheme of things. I don't have an adequate answer. We'll see it when we learn the Essay on Resurrection.\" Part 3: Milah Comes from Moshe, Not Avraham The charge: the Rambam wrote that all mitzvot, even those given before Sinai, bind us through Moshe . So we circumcise because Moshe commanded it , not because of Avraham. The Baghdad rabbis objected: the word ברית appears 13 times in the milah passage in Lech Lecha (the Rambam himself lists them in the Mishneh Torah), and the Gemara speaks of 13 covenants made over milah. The Rambam: \"I'm 100% right.\" גיד הנשה : the Gemara (Chullin) says it was forbidden at Sinai but written in its place (Vayishlach) where the story happened. Its authority is Moshe's. Ask these blind people who compete with the sighted: the verses in Lech Lecha about the covenant: who wrote them? Did Avraham say them and Moshe copy them, \"the way plagiarists do\"? Or did Hashem tell Moshe ? Obviously the latter, and whoever denies it denies that the whole Torah was given to Moshe . How would we even know what Hashem told Avraham, except through Moshe? Those who don't see this have spent their lives on secondary matters, not principles . Everything in the Torah, including what preceded his prophecy (the Noahide laws , Avraham's milah ), we keep only because Moshe commanded it at Sinai. Why it matters The Rambam writes elsewhere that one prophet's words can, in principle, be superseded by a greater later prophet . Only Moshe can never be superseded, since one of the 13 Principles is that he was the greatest prophet . If a mitzvah's authority came from Avraham, someone could claim a later, greater prophet had overridden it. Every word of the Torah, stories included, comes from Moshe at Sinai. Part 4: Sailing on Shabbat The charge: the Rambam permitted travelling by ship on Shabbat , if you boarded before Shabbat . \"Yes, I said it,\" and the Rosh Yeshivah of Baghdad (Shmuel ben Eli) \"never understood my position.\" He refers Ibn Jabbar to two earlier letters on it. Techum Shabbat Outside a city you may go only 2,000 amot (roughly 1,000m) The Rambam No techum above 10 tefachim (about 80cm). If there are 10 tefachim of water between the hull and the seabed, the ship may sail on The Gemara's doubt The Gemara is unsure whether techum applies above 10 tefachim. The Rambam: that doubt concerns land , which could be a public domain. The sea is at most a karmelit , a lesser domain, where there is no doubt If unsure of the depth Techum is rabbinic , so ספק דרבנן לקולא : be lenient. Only if you know it's shallower may you not go beyond 2,000 amot This became the accepted ruling of later poskim. A principle about strictness If the Baghdad rabbis know it's permitted but chose to be strict and made it the city's custom, you must keep it there . A custom accepted knowingly has the force of a communal vow (that's where minhag comes from). But if they thought it was forbidden , it's a mistaken custom and needn't be kept, and you should teach them they're wrong. Part 5: Niddah: Strictness vs. Karaism The Torah says things a niddah touches become impure, and ורחץ במים , \"he shall wash in water.\" The Karaites read that as a wash in the evening, a huge leniency (no mikveh), with a huge stringency: a niddah may not touch food, clothes or things in the house. Some Rabbinite communities adopted the stringency too: wives told not to cook or touch things. (\"I suppose some women would like that: half the month they don't have to do anything in the kitchen!\") The Rambam: - Distinguish assur/mutar from tamei/tahor . What is forbidden is relations and affection during niddah and the seven clean days. Ritual impurity of objects doesn't apply today. - If someone wants to avoid her touching things as an extra stringency , fine. - But in Egypt those communities often didn't keep the clean days or use the mikveh , relying on a wash, so the Rambam placed a cherem on those who don't go to the mikveh. But one who keeps that stringency thinking the Torah requires it has left the House of the Rabbis and become an unbeliever in the Oral Torah : a Karaite. \"You can be as strict as you want in life, as long as you recognise it's a chumra .\" The Mars bar A true story: a student at a London school was eating a Mars bar, not chalav Yisrael . His teacher said: \"Why are you eating chazir?\" The boy concluded they were the same, and in later life ate treif : \"for him it was the same thing.\" Torah prohibitions, rabbinic ones, and stringencies are different levels , and must be taught as such. Part 6: Shorter Rulings Topic The Rambam Verses embroidered on tzitzit / tallit Forbidden. A tallit has no inherent holiness (it isn't tashmish kedushah like tefillin or a Sefer Torah; strictly it may be thrown away, and you may even enter a bathroom in it, though we take it off). So verses with Hashem's name would be disgraced. Cut them out and put them in genizah Accepting a voluntary fast Accept it the afternoon before , at the end of Minchah (at Elokai Netzor ). The Mishneh Torah first had it inside the Amidah (in Shema Koleinu ); he found in the Yerushalmi that it belongs after , and the current text is corrected. (The Chida wrote a prayer to insert in Shema Koleinu ; the Rambam prefers no interruption.) The six public fasts need no acceptance Extra aravot in the arba minim Don't add them. Adding hadassim for beauty is fine (mentioned in the Talmud; in Hilchot Lulav he even praises it) A ba'al keri on Yom Kippur night Ezra's decree requiring immersion before prayer or study was annulled . So don't go to the mikveh on Yom Kippur (washing is forbidden). Some Baghdad sages permitted it, like washing off dirt, and the Ben Ish Chai ( Rav Pe'alim ) allows it if it disturbs one's concentration; we don't follow that Kedushah praying alone The Mishneh Torah says skip the Kadosh kadosh in the blessings before Shema when alone. Here he retracts : that's not our own sanctification (as in נקדישך in the Amidah, which needs a minyan) but a description of what the angels say, so say it . He never fixed the Mishneh Torah, but his son Rabbeinu Avraham confirmed it was his final view Olam HaBa: don't worry about the details You asked what the World to Come is. I've written what can be said. My advice: don't delve too deeply. Even great scholars struggle to grasp a soul without a body, and some deny it. All the more so a beginner like you. It won't harm your faith if, for now, you think the people of Olam HaBa have bodies, even that they eat, drink and have children in the upper heavens or Gan Eden, as many say. People believe far more foolish things without harming their core faith. \"This is remarkable,\" for two reasons: 1. In Hilchot Teshuvah he called that belief the view of \"the foolish Arabs.\" 2. Believing God is corporeal is heresy (the 13 Principles). The distinction: principles vs. their details. That there is an Olam HaBa is a principle . That it is purely spiritual is a detail : important to know one day, but if it's too hard now, a physical picture won't destroy you. Believing Hashem has a body does destroy you: that is a principle. That's why we don't teach children the Rambam's spiritual Olam HaBa. \"I can't tell you how many times people have asked me: will I see my wife again? my mother, my father? Nobody knows. Anyone who tells you they know doesn't.\" But if it helps someone believe in Olam HaBa, \"it's not the end of the world.\" Part 7: \"Leave Them Alone\" Everything you wrote of your love for me will bring you great reward, because it is love for the sake of Heaven , drawn from my words of Torah, which are Hashem's. We must love Hashem with all our heart, and that includes His Torah and those who teach it. I hear, true or not, that someone speaks ill of me , seeking to raise himself through my disgrace , going beyond proper criticism of my book. (He doesn't name him, \"but I don't think it's hard to guess.\") And I hear that you have stood up against them a little. Don't do it. We forgive whoever does it out of foolishness , and also whoever does it for some benefit . It doesn't harm me, and it benefits him , so stopping him would be the trait of Sedom ( zeh neheneh vezeh lo chaser ). He gains: when he finds fault with someone well known, especially in religious matters, he looks wise and knowledgeable, and his honour rises in people's eyes. And it benefits me too, in this world and the next. - The next: whoever hears himself insulted and doesn't respond is rewarded. - This world: כל מי שמבקש להעלים מה שגילה השם, אמנם מוסיף לו גילוי : whoever tries to suppress what Hashem has revealed only reveals it more . \"If only people nowadays understood this.\" Every year another book ban : \"we've read this book and it's heresy.\" The result? Sales go up. People are curious. Don't bring trouble on yourself. I need no man's help. Let everyone do as he wishes. \"How magnanimous. He knows he's right, and doesn't care what people are saying.\" Summary Don't call yourself ignorant. Torah in any language you understand; never give up ; late starters like Rabbi Akiva became great. Learn enough Hebrew for the Mishneh Torah. He refused to translate it into Arabic. He affirms physical resurrection ; critics are cunning or confuse it with Olam HaBa. Every mitzvah binds us through Moshe , even milah and the Noahide laws. Moshe alone can never be superseded. Sailing on Shabbat is permitted above 10 tefachim. A knowingly stricter custom binds like a vow; a mistaken one doesn't. Niddah : stringency is fine; thinking it's Torah law is Karaism. The Mars bar. No verses on tzitzit ; accept a fast after Minchah; no extra aravot; no mikveh on Yom Kippur; say Kedushah of Yotzer alone (his final view). Principles vs. details : a beginner may picture Olam HaBa physically for now; never God. Don't defend me. Critics gain honour, I gain reward, and suppression only spreads the book. Coming Up Next week, 8pm: the Rambam's letter to a dayan , in one session; then the longer essays: Kiddush Hashem (martyrdom) and Resurrection .","Rm5M2RrEXB0":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: the Letter to the Sages of Montpellier on Astrology (c. 1194–95), second half Shiur: Third class in the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 4 February 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap of Part 1 The letter (1194 or 1195), the Rambam's first to Provence, in Hebrew , to the sages of Montpellier and Rabbi Yonatan HaKohen of Lunel , a Tosafist and staunch defender of the Rambam. Three sources of knowledge : reason, the senses, tradition (as Rav Saadiah said 200 years earlier). Uncritical acceptance of whatever is written is \"a great illness.\" Belief in astrology cost us our sovereignty and the Temple . \"Don't reject astrology because there are no proofs for it; reject it because there are proofs against it.\" He read every book on paganism and found reasons for mitzvot (sha'atnez, meat and milk, pe'ot, the razor). Astronomy is \"an exceptional science\"; astrology is a pseudo-science. The Mishneh Torah says it all in one halacha (Avodah Zarah 11:16): ודברים האלו כולן דברי שקר וכזב הן… וכל המאמין בדברים האלו וכיוצא בהן, ומחשב בליבו שהן אמת ודבר חכמה אבל התורה מנעה אותן, אינן אלא מן הסכלים ומחסרי הדעת… אבל בעלי החכמה ותמימי הדעת ידעו בראיות ברורות שכל אלו הדברים… אינם דברי חכמה אלא תוהו והבל These are all lies… Whoever believes in them and thinks they're true and wise, only the Torah forbade them , is among the fools. The wise know by clear proofs that they are not wisdom but emptiness. (Elsewhere he adds \"and women and children, whose minds are not complete,\" reflecting a time when women were far less educated than men.) Part 1: Three Views of Creation Everyone in the Rambam's day, philosophers included, believed in a Creator , \"basically the case until relatively recently.\" The debate was how the universe came about : View Whose 1 The world is eternal , never began and never ends, co-existing with God Aristotle (\"the majority\") 2 God created the world, but from eternal unformed matter ( hylē , chomer ) that always existed alongside Him; even God can't make something from nothing. (In the Moreh : one day it will return to that matter) Plato ; the Ibn Ezra on Bereishit seems to hold it 3 God created everything from absolutely nothing The prophets and our tradition (a minority of philosophers, the Moreh says) \"Thousands of books\" argue each view; the Moreh says the debate is at least 3,000 years old , so 4,000 by now. The Monday class saw Rav Saadiah's thirteen views of creation and his refutation of Plato a year ago. He doesn't name Aristotle or Plato here. The sages of Provence didn't have Golden-Age Spain's exposure to philosophy, so naming them \"wouldn't be appreciated.\" The Rambam here vs. in the Moreh Here he's unequivocal: you must believe the third view; deny it and you've denied a fundamental. He wrote a whole book in Arabic (the Moreh ) proving God's existence, explaining anthropomorphic verses as metaphor, dismantling the proofs for eternity , and answering the question \"how can something come from nothing?\" Note the wording: he dismantled the case for eternity and answered the questions . He doesn't claim to have proved creation from nothing. In the Moreh he says explicitly that he can't prove it. A theme of the letters: the Rambam writes for his recipient. In the Moreh (II:25) he rejects Aristotle outright but is far more sympathetic to Plato . He concludes for creation from nothing, but doesn't treat Plato's view as heresy, and some Rishonim held it. That same chapter famously says: if Aristotle had been proven , \"I'd have no problem reinterpreting 'In the beginning God created' allegorically,\" as he does with the hundreds of anthropomorphic verses. \"That's not my problem. His proofs aren't good enough.\" \"We will see this over and over again , especially in the Letter to Yemen.\" Part 2: What All Three Agree On Intermediaries: the \"trickle-down\" All three views agree God acts through intermediaries : the spheres and stars, through which His energy (shefa) flows down to create and sustain plants, animals and minerals. The Rambam accepts this : \"it does not conflict with our tradition; the Sages believed it too,\" as he shows in the Moreh . In the Moreh : ten levels; each of nine spheres has an associated intelligence , and the tenth, the Active Intellect , governs the lowest realm, our world. מלאכים aren't \"angels.\" That translation came from our exposure to Christianity. מלאך means messenger, emissary, agent : וישלח יעקב מלאכים . In the Rambam they are incorporeal intelligences through which the flow passes. A participant asked: what about Gavriel, Michael and Raphael, who came to Avraham? (The Rabbi noted that Daniel, also suggested, is not an angel at all.) The names exist to give an identity to the different intermediaries: like one sunlight seen through different coloured glasses. Free will All three also agree a person's actions are in his own hands : serve Hashem and study, or follow the wicked into adultery and theft. Nothing pulls him either way. That's why the Torah commands at all. (The Rambam refers to his Arabic writings, the Eight Chapters .) Where we part company: what happens to us All three philosophical views agree what happens to a person is chance : neither God nor the stars decide it. To them, there's no difference between a lion eating a man and a cat eating a mouse; a man falling off a roof and a stone breaking a branch; armies fighting and dogs fighting over a carcass . All chance. We reject that. There is justice. Hashem watches every human action. ואם תלכו עמי קרי… והלכתי עמכם בחמת קרי : \"If you walk with Me by chance …, I will walk with you in the fury of chance.\" If you say, when bad things happen, \"it's just random,\" I'll leave you to randomness. Suffering comes only through sin. Two important points For the Rambam, providence covers humans only (and what affects them). The lions and cheetahs on National Geographic really are random. His complaint about the philosophers is that they lump humans in with nature . The taxi analogy: a man with 100 identical taxis retiring to keep only 10 sells the first 90 that come to hand. He cares that ten remain (general providence over a species), not which ten. Ownerless sheep are like that. A farmer's 100 sheep, by contrast, come under providence because he does . Treat life as random, and it becomes random. Providence is withdrawn and you're left to exist like an animal: you may prosper as a lion prospers, but you're left to chance. Part 3: Reuven the Tanner and Shimon the Spice-Merchant Reuven is a poor tanner whose children died; Shimon a wealthy perfumer with lovely children. Three explanations: Why? Can it change? The philosophers Chance Yes, randomly The astrologers (\"whom you've been hearing in France\") The stars decree it Not while the stars say so Our faith Hashem's will , which responds to their actions At any moment. If Shimon sins he'll become poor; if Reuven mends his ways, all will be good Both alternatives fail logically (astrology has been disproved) and religiously : if life were random or star-fixed, what would the Torah's laws be for? \"But I don't see it that way\" The Rambam covers this in one sentence: when someone improves and things don't change, there is a backlog of sin still to be paid, or (he adds) suffering to earn a greater share in the World to Come . \"We can't always understand how Hashem works.\" A subtlety: two weeks ago on Monday the class saw Rav Saadiah teach that suffering can increase one's share in Olam HaBa. The Rambam doesn't hold that ; in the Moreh he follows אין יסורין בלא עוון , no suffering without sin. So the editor, Rav Shilat , says that here the Rambam is citing others' view , not his own. \"Difficult, I accept, but consistent.\" The point is simply: there is a reason. Part 4: What About the Talmud's Astrology? The question from last week. The Gemara (Shabbat 156) devotes half a page to it: born on Sunday , all one thing or another; Monday , angry; Tuesday , wealthy and promiscuous; Wednesday , wise; Thursday , kind; then by planet: Mars sheds blood, and so on. I know you'll find in the Talmud and Midrashim statements of individual sages that seem to say the stars determine a person's fate. Don't let it trouble you. It isn't right to abandon a law that is logical and proven , wash your hands of it, and rely on a single sage's statement… \"Where did we see almost these exact words? Two weeks ago\" : the letter to Ovadia, about the Gemara that marriages are decreed forty days before birth. Don't abandon the roots for one or two statements. Three explanations 1 The matter was hidden from him : that sage was mistaken ממנו can mean from him or from us . Some, uncomfortable, read it as \"hidden from us .\" \"It shows how, because of personal feeling, people will reinterpret things.\" The Rabbi reads it as the sage himself missing something 2 He meant something deeper , hinted, metaphorical 3 He was responding to a situation E.g. Rava : \"life, children and livelihood depend on mazal , not merit.\" He saw two equally pious sages, Rabbah and Rav Chisda , one with a hard life, one a good one Like zugot: the Gemara warns against doing things in pairs . \"Nobody does this any more,\" though some do with eggs, and the S&P calls only an odd number of aliyot when adding extras (an eighth means calling a ninth too, then 11, 13, 15). Rav Moshe Feinstein wrote that such warnings apply to those who believe in them . Similarly, some say ayin hara has power only if you let it; the Rambam, the Rabbi thinks, didn't believe in it at all. \"Sometimes we do that as rabbis too\": people say things we don't believe, and \"we don't want to shake their faith, so we give it a religious touch.\" If we interpret Torah verses metaphorically, all the more so a few Rabbinic statements. ואל ישליך אדם דעתו אחריו, כי העיניים לפנים הם ולא לאחור : don't throw your intelligence behind you; your eyes are in front, not behind. Part 5: A False Mashiach, and the Signature They'd heard of a false Mashiach in Isfahan. No, says the Rambam: you mixed it up. It was in Yemen , about 22 years earlier : a man claiming to be the Mashiach's herald, gathering Jews and Arabs, changing the prayers, said to work miracles. His students asked him how to relate to it. I wrote that he means well but is a fool , that it was all falsehood, and that I feared for the Jews when the government found out. A year later he was caught. The Arab king asked for proof. \" Cut off my head and I'll live. \" \"Fine. If you're right, I'll convert.\" He was killed. תהא מיתתו כפרה לו ולכל ישראל. The Jews were fined in many places, and there are still fools there who say he'll rise again . (A \"second Jesus.\" Much more on this in the Letter to Yemen.) The close: Uproot the thorns and poisonous weeds ; plant in their place the tree of the knowledge of good and evil ; eat its fruit and enjoy it… May Hashem let us and you be satisfied from His goodness forever. Written under great pressure, on the 11th of Tishrei , in Egypt. \"The day after Yom Kippur! He didn't spend it recovering.\" And: \"Forgive my terseness; I'm so busy working for the gentiles.\" A later letter describes his schedule: busy at the Sultan's palace from Shabbat to Shabbat, studying only on Shabbat. He only replied because his friend Rav Pinchas sent a messenger with orders not to leave until the Rambam wrote . Part 6: Others Disagree Most Rishonim disagreed with the Rambam : the Ramban , Sefer HaChinuch , Meiri , Ralbag held astrology is a real science. - The Ramban: don't seek out astrologers, but if you hear something from one, don't ignore it ; don't rely on a miracle: take care and increase prayer . - The Vilna Gaon famously writes sharply against the Rambam: so many Talmudic passages speak of mazal and magic, but \"philosophy led him astray,\" and he explained the Talmud metaphorically, uprooting its simple meaning. The Gaon says there are deeper meanings, but Kabbalistic, not philosophical. - With the Rambam: Ibn Pakuda and others before him likewise held astrology to be futile. Robert asked: astrologers say your circumstances are set, but surely not your reactions ? Reward and punishment could still work. The Ran and others who accept astrology say it gives only a tendency (\"born under Mars, you tend toward blood; you can control it, but it's harder\"). The Rambam himself says something similar about character in the Eight Chapters : one can be born with a tendency. But to attribute that to the stars \"is too much: too much destiny.\" A participant suggested the Rambam might allow it as a variable, and the Rabbi agreed that is the majority view of the Sages , though not the Rambam's. \"The Rambam was an outlier on this point. Each person needs to make their own choices whom they follow.\" Part 7: So What Does \"Mazal Tov\" Mean? \"Good luck,\" everyone says. But we just spent two weeks saying luck is nonsense! The Rambam would never have said it. \"I really can't believe he would have.\" Read מזל literally as flow , from נוזל : may your actions bring down a good flow **. That fits the \"trickle-down\" he does accept. Most people just mean congratulations . The Shulchan Aruch forbids consulting astrologers and superstitions (Yoreh De'ah 179), then in the next halacha records the custom not to begin things on Mondays and Wednesdays and to marry when the moon is waxing . Isn't that superstition? No: it's a סימן , a sign. That's why Sephardim traditionally say בסימן טוב , or בסימנא טבא (as at the end of the Torah reading cycle). It doesn't mean good luck; it means starting on the right foot. The Meiri on the Rosh Hashanah foods: don't be stupid and think eating a date or an apple brings you a good year. A siman sets your frame of mind . The practical takeaway: if you accept the Rambam, wish people \"b'siman tov\" , or if you say \"mazal tov,\" mean a good flow . \"It would be interesting to research when 'mazal tov' came into use. It can't be very long ago.\" Summary Creation : three views (Aristotle, Plato, creation from nothing). The Rambam insists here on the third, more sharply than in the Moreh , because he writes for his audience . All agree God works through intermediaries (spheres, intelligences; mal'achim are messengers) and that humans have free will . But philosophers say what happens to us is chance , astrologers say it is fixed by the stars . Judaism says it is just , responsive to our deeds, and can change at any moment. Treat life as random and it will be. Providence in the Rambam covers humans, not animals as individuals. Talmudic astrology : the sage erred, spoke in hints, or answered a situation. Your eyes are in front, not behind. Most Rishonim (Ramban, Chinuch, Meiri, Ralbag, later the Vilna Gaon) disagree; the Rambam is an outlier. Say \"b'siman tov.\" Coming Up Next week: the Letter on Martyrdom ( Iggeret HaShmad ).","bS86imePTC4":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: the Letter to the Sages of Montpellier on Astrology (written in Hebrew, c. 1194–95) Shiur: Second class in the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 28 January 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Last week: an introduction to the letters, and the three letters to Ovadia the convert : the convert's liturgy, free will, and Islam (plus encouragement to the convert). Background to the Letter Title The Letter to the Sages of Montpellier , or על גזירת הכוכבים , on astrology Date 1194 or 1195 , about ten years before the Rambam's death Asked by Rabbi Yonatan HaKohen of Lunel (born 1135) and his colleagues Language Hebrew , so we have \"the Rambam's own words, unfiltered\" Place Some editions say Marseille; Rav Yitzchak Shilat , today the main authority on the letters, proves it was Montpellier Rabbi Yonatan HaKohen of Lunel A prominent Tosafist , born in Lunel in 1135, who moved later in life to Montpellier. A contemporary of the Ra'avad , the Rambam's great critic, yet he revered the Rambam and wrote a work defending him from the Ra'avad's critiques. The first known letter from the Rambam to Provence. He had corresponded with Yemen long before; a follow-up letter came a couple of years later. At his request the Rambam sent the Moreh Nevuchim (in Shmuel ibn Tibbon 's Hebrew translation), which spread through Provence in the late 1190s. Lunel: the moon The Rambam opens with a verse from Shir HaShirim: מי זאת הנשקפה כמו שחר, יפה כלבנה, ברה כחמה Who is this that looks forth like the dawn, beautiful as the moon , clear as the sun? Lunel , lunar , moon : \"You from Lunel are beautiful as the moon.\" A letter into a storm Provence had both staunch supporters and staunch opponents of the Rambam. The two great controversies came after his death: 1. 1232: in Montpellier itself, Rabbi Shlomo of Montpellier , also a Tosafist, led a ban on the Rambam's philosophical works, and Rabbeinu Yonah led the burning of his books. He later admitted he had been wrong, and many say he wrote Sha'arei Teshuvah as his repentance. 2. Late 1200s: a second controversy, involving the Rashba , with the Meiri among the Rambam's great defenders. \"Ultimately, history clearly showed that the Rambam was the winner.\" Three things in this letter would have delighted his supporters and infuriated his opponents : 1. How to relate to Greek and philosophical wisdom . 2. His willingness to dismiss certain statements of the Sages that contradict reason. 3. His great appreciation for non-Jewish philosophers . Part 1: \"You Haven't Received My Mishneh Torah\" He begins with warm greetings ( \"my chiefs and my teachers\" ) and praises the question as testimony to \"the purity of their hearts and the clarity of their minds.\" The question has many branches, but it all comes back to one principle: what is our view of astrology? It must be that you haven't yet received my book , which explains all the laws of the Torah. I already answered this in Hilchot Avodah Zarah , in Sefer HaMada . It has already spread east (Iraq) and west (Morocco, Spain, Yemen), and recently to Sicily ; it will reach you soon. There is evidence the Mishneh Torah had reached Provence in the early 1190s. Perhaps the Rambam is subtly chastising them for not paying it more attention. Still, he answers. (The Rambam \"had different personalities\": as a philosopher his magnum opus is the Moreh ; as a halachist , the way the yeshivah world sees him, it's the Mishneh Torah .) Part 2: The Three Sources of Knowledge דעו רבותי, שאין ראוי לאדם להאמין אלא אחד משלושה דברים Know, my teachers, that a person should only believe one of three kinds of things: # Source Examples 1 Clear proof of reason Arithmetic, geometry, the calculation of the seasons : one right answer, \"like a maths exam\" 2 The five senses This is red, this is black; sweet or bitter; hot or cold; a clear or an echoing voice; a good or bad smell 3 Tradition from the prophets or the righteous Rav Saadiah Gaon says the same in the introduction to Emunot VeDeot (learned over a year ago), with a fourth category, inference , which the Rambam would fold into reason. Audit your beliefs Anyone who believes something outside these three, הפתי יאמין לכל דבר : \"the fool believes everything.\" The Rambam's challenge: sit down one day, list your firm beliefs, and categorise each one. Example: Australia. Some of us have been there (the senses). Most of us believe it exists through tradition : decent people with no reason to lie. Or reason : goods arrive from there. If a belief fits none of the three, you may wonder , but you can't hold it as truth. \"One of the worst illnesses\" The fools have written thousands of books on astrology… Many people are great in years but not in wisdom ; they wasted their lives on these books, thought these trivialities were wisdom, and imagined themselves wise. The mistake of most of the world, except a very few: they think whatever is written in a book is true , and all the more so if the book is old. \"We hear this all the time: it's written, it's in the book. So what?\" (\"He's probably being hyperbolic about the thousands of books.\") Why we lost the Temple וזה הוא שאבד מלכותנו והחריב מקדשנו : this is what destroyed our kingdom and our Temple and brought us to this exile. Our ancestors studied the books of the astrologers (rooted in idolatry), thought them great wisdom, and did not train for war or conquest , thinking astrology would protect them. That's why the prophets called them fools, \"and indeed they were fools.\" \"This is incredible.\" We usually say the Temple fell because of idolatry, the three cardinal sins, baseless hatred. \"All true. But ultimately it comes down to a lack of self-determination .\" If life is run by the stars, why train an army? \"Warfare doesn't matter; everything is decreed by which constellation you're under.\" Part 3: \"I Read Every Book on Idolatry in Arabic\" I have investigated these matters a great deal… I don't think there is a single book translated into Arabic on idolatry that I haven't read and fully understood. \"How on earth did the Rambam find the time, alongside everything he wrote, all the Torah he knew, and his medical career? This is why he's one of the greatest people of all time.\" The halachic problem In the Mishneh Torah (Avodah Zarah 2:2) the Rambam himself writes: the idolaters wrote many books on their worship, and Hashem commanded us not to read them at all , not even to think about them. How could he read every one? Answer Source He did it for the perplexed : to refute their supposed proofs he had to know them ( Moreh Nevuchim , the \"guide for the nevuchim \"; נבוכים הם בארץ , Pharaoh's word for the Israelites in next week's parasha, Beshalach, the source of the Yiddish nebech ) Rivash , responsum 45 (a participant's suggestion too: to protect the Jewish people) The prohibition is reading them out of interest ; studying them to know what idolatry is, and to stay away from it, is permitted Meiri , probably the Rambam's own view What he learned: the reasons for the mitzvot From them I came to understand the reasons for many mitzvot that people think have no reason, and I wrote a whole book on this in Arabic. That is Part III of the Moreh , which goes through the mitzvot and explains them. Chukim aren't reasonless The common view: there are three categories of mitzvot: Category Description משפטים , judgements Basic decency: we'd have legislated them ourselves עדות / mitzvot shim'iyot (Rav Saadiah's term) We wouldn't have thought of them (Shabbat, Pesach) but their reason is apparent once given חוקים , \"statutes\" \"No reason\" : the archetype is parah adumah , of which Shlomo said \"I said I would be wise, but it is far from me\" Some go further and think a reasonless mitzvah shows the Torah's greatness. The Rambam: absolutely not. Every mitzvah has a reason; the more we understand it, the better. A chok is just a mitzvah whose reason isn't immediately apparent . Ask 99 out of 100 Jews why we don't wear sha'atnez , or mix meat and milk , and they'll say \"it's a chok.\" But the Rishonim all try to give reasons, while conceding they're uncertain. The Rambam's reasons often relate to idolatry , from what he read: Mitzvah The Rambam (Moreh) Not cutting the pe'ot ; not shaving the beard with a razor That was how pagan priests groomed themselves Sha'atnez Pagan priests wore wool and linen together in their service Meat and milk Pagans offered meat and milk together to their gods The whole Torah, he says, is geared to take us away from idolatry toward the unity of Hashem . Part 4: Astrology Is Nonsense; Astronomy Is Wisdom All their claims (that this will happen, that a birth under this star brings that) are all foolishness. I have strong proofs that cannot be refuted against astrology's principles. (Unfortunately he doesn't bring them here.) Who practised astrology? The Chaldeans, Canaanites and Egyptians . Who rejected it? The Greek philosophers , masters of all the sciences, who mocked it, and the sages of Persia . \"Don't think we reject it because they do. We have our own concrete proofs.\" A question to think about What about the Gemara (Shabbat 156) : one born under Mars ( Ma'adim ) will shed blood, so let him become a shochet or mohel , \"spill blood in a kosher way\"? The Rambam addresses such statements directly at the end of the letter, next week. Olivia: didn't Avraham read the stars? \"Firstly, I'm unaware of what you're talking about. It could be I'm laughing at myself, because I'm saying 'I've read it in a book, it must be true!'\" But see the next paragraph, which may be what's meant: Astronomy The wisdom of the stars (the forms of the spheres, their number, measure and courses, north, south, east and west) is true and beautiful. The sages of Greece, Persia and India wrote many books on it. From it you can work out eclipses , why the moon waxes and wanes and when it will be seen, why days are long or short , why two stars rise together but don't set together, why the day is 13 hours in one place and 15, 16 or 20 in another, why day and night are equal in some places, and why the day lasts six months and the night six months in others. He clearly knew about the far north. This is \"the calculation of the seasons,\" a great wisdom that displays our intellect, the expertise of the tribe of Yissachar ( יודעי בינה לעתים ). He knew it extremely well. Hilchot Kiddush HaChodesh, the astronomical chapters on sanctifying the new moon, is \"for me probably the most difficult bit in the Mishneh Torah. I've never understood it, and I don't know if I'll ever understand it.\" So: don't equate astrology with astronomy. If Avraham \"read the stars,\" it means astronomy. Sandy: the Midrash on Lech Lecha ויוצא אותו החוצה… הבט נא השמימה וספור הכוכבים : Hashem took Avram \"outside.\" The Midrash (in Rashi): outside the stars , above their influence; until then he couldn't have children because of his mazal . Isn't that proof of astrology? \"This is actually a much easier Midrash. I'll answer it now.\" Hashem was telling him: come out of thinking you depend on that . It's nonsense anyway. The Midrash needn't mean he was actually under its influence. The explicit Gemaras are harder, and the Rambam deals with them next week. Summary The letter was the Rambam's first to Provence , from the Montpellier sages led by Rabbi Yonatan HaKohen of Lunel , written in Hebrew around 1194–95, into a region that would later ban and burn his books. Believe only what is proven by reason , perceived by the senses , or received from prophets and the righteous . Everything else is \"the fool believes everything.\" \"It's written in a book\" is no proof. The worst human illness. Reliance on astrology instead of self-determination cost us our kingdom and Temple . The Rambam read every Arabic book on idolatry (permitted, per the Rivash and Meiri, to refute it and to know what to avoid), and found the reasons for mitzvot like pe'ot, sha'atnez and meat and milk. Every mitzvah has a reason; a chok 's is just not immediately apparent. Astrology is nonsense; astronomy is true wisdom. Coming Up Next week (4 February): the rest of the letter: three views of creation (Aristotle, Plato, the Torah) and their bearing on providence and reward and punishment; how Hashem acts through intermediaries (the spheres; what mazal really means, the \"trickling\" of energy from Hashem); free will ; and how to deal with Talmudic statements that seem to endorse astrology .","8n6VS_ap_zc":"Source: Igrot HaRambam: the three responsa to Ovadia the Righteous Convert (written in Hebrew) Shiur: First class of the series on the Rambam's letters, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 21 January 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction: Why Study the Rambam's Letters? The Tuesday-night shiurim \"over the past few years\" have focused on the Rambam: Hilchot Deot, Hilchot Teshuvah, Yesodei HaTorah, the 13 Principles, the Shemonah Perakim . This new series, \"a good part of the year, if not the whole year,\" is his letters . The Rambam in brief Life Born 1138 in Spain; a few years in Morocco; most of his life in Egypt ; died 1204 Day job Doctor; medical works and works on logic Commentary on the Mishnah His first work, written aged roughly 20–23 Mishneh Torah 14 books codifying the entire Torah and Talmud, \"the only one to have done what he did, before or since\" Sefer HaMitzvot Enumerating the 613 commandments Moreh Nevuchim His main philosophical work Responsa and letters What the letters add His books are systematic ; we learn little of his personal life from them. The letters are freer and more personal. For example, everyone knows of his son Rabbi Avraham ; only from the letters do we learn he also had a daughter, who died . Most letters were written in Arabic (the Rambam wrote that he expresses himself best in it), and we read them in Hebrew translation. The plan: shorter letters on specific topics first ( Ovadia, Astrology, Martyrdom ), later the big ones ( Resurrection, Yemen ). Who Was Ovadia? A convert from Islam , living in Jerusalem . He wrote to the Rambam in Hebrew , probably to express himself in his new language and identity, and the Rambam replied in Hebrew . So we have the Rambam's own words. The letters are undated, but they reference the Mishneh Torah , completed in 1180 , so they fall between 1180 and 1204 . They were cited by the Rashba and the Radbaz , and taken on as halacha . The first one settled a long-running debate. Letter Topic 1 The liturgy a convert should use 2 Free will , even in marriage 3 Whether Islam is idolatry, and how Jews must treat a convert The Rambam's tone throughout is striking: though there's no evidence Ovadia was a scholar, he calls him \"the wise, the understanding\" and praises and encourages him in all three letters. And he signs himself משה בן מימון , \"of those exiled from Jerusalem,\" that is, living outside the Land. Letter 1: \"The God of Our Fathers\"? The question Praying alone, or leading as chazan , should a convert say: - אלהינו ואלהי אבותינו , \"our God and God of our fathers\" (the Amidah) - אשר קדשנו במצוותיו וציוונו , \"who sanctified us… and commanded us\" - אשר בחר בנו מכל העמים , \"who chose us from all the nations\" (the blessing before the Torah reading) - שהנחלת לאבותינו ארץ חמדה , \"who gave our fathers a desirable land\" (Birkat HaMazon) - שהוצאתנו מארץ מצרים , \"who took us out of Egypt\"; שעשה ניסים לאבותינו , \"who did miracles for our fathers\" How can I say these? I chose voluntarily; Hashem didn't \"command\" me. Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov aren't my biological ancestors. The answer: say everything \"You should pray as every other Jew prays , whether alone or as chazan.\" Avraham is the father of all who follow him. He was the first to teach the unity of God; thousands came under his and Sarah's wings (\"Avraham looked after the men and Sarah the women\"). Hashem commanded him: למען אשר יצוה את בניו ואת ביתו אחריו ושמרו דרך ה' , \"teach your children and household after you.\" So his legacy endures: Everyone who converts, to the end of time, is a student of Avraham , of his household. Avraham is the ancestor of his biological descendants and the spiritual ancestor of all who practise what he preached. So \"God of our fathers\" is true, and so is \"the land You gave our fathers\" , since the covenant of the land was made with Avraham. What about \"who took us out of Egypt\"? Spiritual ancestry doesn't stretch that far. \"If you want,\" you may change the wording; but it's totally fine to say what everyone says: \"Once you come under the wings of Hashem, you are part of us . Everyone else says it; you say it with them.\" The Rambam is unequivocal : there is no difference between us and you. Don't say \"who chose Israel\" and the like; say \"who chose us … gave us the Torah.\" As the Torah says, חוקה אחת לכם ולגר , תורה אחת ומשפט אחד יהיה לכם ולגר הגר אתכם : one law for the native and the convert. Our ancestors were idolaters too \"Our own biological ancestors\" served idols in Egypt. The Midrash says the sea was reluctant to split, since \"these are idol worshippers and those are idol worshippers,\" until Moshe saved them. So why should you think less of yourself? \"We trace our lineage to Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov; you trace yours to Him who spoke and the world came to be.\" As Yeshayahu says: זה יאמר לה' אני, וזה יקרא בשם יעקב : one says \"I am Hashem's,\" another calls himself by the name of Yaakov. Only then, the source \"How you cannot divorce a posek's outlook from the way he rules\": the Rambam gives his own reasoning first and brings the source at the end . Mishnah Bikkurim 1:4 : a convert brings bikkurim but doesn't recite the declaration, because he can't say \"the land Hashem swore to our fathers\"; and praying alone he says \"God of the fathers of Israel.\" The Yerushalmi : Rabbi Yehudah disagrees: he does recite, because Avraham is called אב המון גוים , \"father of a multitude of nations.\" The Rambam rules like the Yerushalmi. That is rare. The Bavli is the authoritative Talmud, and this is an unattributed ( stam ) Mishnah, which carries great weight. The Rambam preferred the less authoritative Yerushalmi because it fitted his logic : he didn't want the convert to be made to pray differently from everyone else. \"Of course, if there were only the Mishnah, he'd have to accept it. But there were two sources.\" On starting points, the Rabbi said: some poskim start from forbidden until proven permitted ; others from permitted until proven forbidden . \"What I received from my teachers: unless you have a source that something is forbidden, it's not forbidden.\" The debate the Rambam settled Rabbeinu Tam (Rashi's grandson, France) forbade a convert to be chazan (he'd have to say \"God of our fathers\" ) or to lead zimmun . The real zimmun is that the leader recites the whole Birkat HaMazon for everyone, including \"the land You gave our fathers.\" After the Rambam's responsum, the Ramban, Rashba, Rosh and others followed him . The Shulchan Aruch (Orach Chaim 53) records that some disqualified a convert as chazan because he can't say \"God of our fathers,\" and that their view was rejected . That's also why a convert is called בן אברהם / בת אברהם (or בת שרה ). The Ohr HaChaim gives a different, Kabbalistic reason: converts' souls were at Sinai ; they were lost and have come back. \"Clearly not the Rambam's approach.\" Letter 2: Is Your Spouse Decreed from Heaven? Ovadia's teacher had told him that everything is from Heaven, including whom you marry. The Gemara (Sotah 2a): forty days before conception a heavenly voice proclaims, \"the daughter of so-and-so to so-and-so.\" Today people say \"it's bashert.\" The Rambam utterly rejects it. You're right, and we have full free will; that's why there is reward and punishment. - הכל בידי שמים חוץ מיראת שמים : nature is in Heaven's hands, everything except fear of Heaven. - He refers Ovadia to the Eight Chapters (studied together a year ago) and the Mishneh Torah (Hilchot Teshuvah, studied just now). \"Spiritual suicide\" Anyone who leaves the fundamentals and goes looking for one Midrash, one aggadah, or one statement of an early Gaon, and uses it to say \"it can't be true,\" is committing philosophical suicide. \"Key lines to remember for life.\" The method is the reverse: take the principles of the Torah, then fit the Midrashim around them. \"What people do is the opposite: the Midrash says this, so I'll build my whole world-view on this one Midrash and fit everything else in.\" The proof from war The Torah (Shofetim) exempts a man who has betrothed a woman and not yet married her from going to war, lest he die and another marry her . If whom you marry is decreed, why worry? The verse itself shows it isn't. Letter 3: Is Islam Idolatry? And How to Treat a Convert Ovadia's teacher had told him Muslims are idolaters ; when Ovadia disagreed, the teacher humiliated him and called him a fool . Islam is not avodah zarah אלו הישמעאלים אינם עובדי עבודה זרה כלל… והם מייחדים לאל יתעלה ייחוד כראוי, ייחוד שאין בו דופי These Ishmaelites are not idolaters at all … they affirm God's unity properly, a unity without flaw. \"They accuse us of saying God has a son\" , a false charge against some Jews. \"Because they make false claims about us, are we going to make false claims about them? We don't lie. \" Praying where idols once stood? Even if an idol were hidden in their sanctuary, their hearts are directed to God . The Gemara says someone who bows thinking he is bowing to Hashem, unaware of an idol before him, has done nothing wrong. \"Their other mistakes and stupidities I can't put in writing, for fear this letter will be picked up and I'll be informed upon.\" One of them we saw in Hilchot Teshuvah: the sensual paradise . Pagan origins. Historically they had three deities and rites (named in the letter as Pe'or, Markulis and Kemosh), which they acknowledge: exposure, throwing stones , not wearing sewn garments. They still do these things. But not for those purposes. Some say they throw stones to confuse the Satan ; their scholars say it is to reject the idol . \"Whatever it is, it's just their practice. It's become tradition.\" Their hearts are to God. Rabbeinu Tam said something similar about Christians The tractate Avodah Zarah forbids trade with idolaters three days before their festivals. How, then, could Jews in Christian Europe trade at Christmas? Rabbeinu Tam : they do these things as ancestral custom, not real belief . \"Unfortunately you could say that about many religions nowadays, and if we're honest and frank, our own as well.\" Halachic upshot The Gemara and Shulchan Aruch forbid entering a church , as a place of avodah zarah. Based on this letter, entering a mosque is permitted : they are pure monotheists. \"They have many other problems, like the Rambam says, but this is not one of them.\" \"Was he drunk?\" Your teacher who embarrassed you and called you a fool committed a terrible sin ; I hope by mistake. He must ask your forgiveness (even a teacher who embarrasses his student must), fast, and cry out to Hashem , and perhaps he'll be forgiven. Was he drunk , that he forgot the 36 places the Torah commands us concerning the convert? ולא תונו : verbal abuse of a convert is a Torah prohibition. Even if he had been right, he should have explained gently. \"All the more so when you're right and he's talking nonsense.\" He was so busy worrying about whether Muslims are idolaters that he forgot the much bigger thing. Parents we must honour and fear ; prophets we must heed ; it's possible to do those without loving them. The convert we must love : ואהבתם את הגר , just as ואהבת את ה' אלהיך . \"It brought tears to my eyes\" A man who left his father and his birthplace and a nation on the rise , understood, and joined this nation, despised today, subjugated to all the nations ; who recognised that ours is the religion of truth and righteousness, that other faiths have taken from it, added, subtracted, changed, lied, broken foundations, distorted; who pursued God, came under His wings, became a student of Moshe , and sought to be like an angel and cast this world from his heart; you call such a man a fool? God forbid! You are not a fool. You are wise and understanding, walking a straight path, a student of Avraham, who left his family to follow God. He who blessed Avraham will bless you, in this world and the next. May you yourself become a teacher and a posek , and may all of Israel's consolations be yours. \"What really comes out in this letter is the love the Rambam had for Ovadia.\" Summary Letter Ruling and teaching 1. Liturgy A convert prays exactly like every Jew : \"God of our fathers\", \"who chose us \", \"the land You gave our fathers\", even \"who took us out of Egypt\". Avraham is his father ; \"you trace your lineage to Him who spoke and the world came to be.\" The Rambam chose the Yerushalmi over a stam Mishnah to reach it, and settled the debate against Rabbeinu Tam (Shulchan Aruch OC 53) 2. Free will Marriage is not predetermined. Never build a world-view on one Midrash against the fundamentals. Proof: the betrothed man exempt from war 3. Islam and converts Muslims are pure monotheists , not idolaters, despite pagan-origin rites (so a mosque may be entered). The teacher who shamed Ovadia sinned gravely; the convert must be loved Coming Up 28 January and 4 February: the Rambam's Letter on Astrology , a longer letter, in two parts.","DWEJKHo3RzE":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapters: 9 and 10 (complete: the end of Hilchot Teshuvah) Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 7 January 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction: Finishing Hilchot Teshuvah \"Somehow it has evolved that we've been spending Tuesday nights for the last couple of years on the Rambam.\" Tonight finishes Hilchot Teshuvah. After a week's break, from 21 January a new series will go systematically through the letters of the Rambam : the famous ones (on resurrection, the Letter to Yemen , the essay on martyrdom ) and lesser-known ones (to a convert, to a dayan , to a simple Jew). In his books the Rambam wrote within a set framework; \"in his letters he was able to be more expressive and write in a more personal way.\" The three parts of Hilchot Teshuvah Part Chapters Topic 1 1 to mid-3 What teshuvah is, when and how 2 3–4 Identity: tzaddik, beinoni, rasha; what precludes Olam HaBa; the five categories of impediments 3 5–10 Free will (5–6); the power of teshuvah (7); Olam HaBa and its relationship to Techiyat HaMetim, Gehinnom, Gan Eden and Mashiach (8); this world's rewards (9); serving from love (10) Chapter 9: Primary and Secondary Rewards The question Chapter 8 established that the true reward is spiritual and beyond us: the most we taste in this world is 1/60th of Olam HaBa, which is Shabbat (Berachot 57b). The Sages use 1/60 because it is batel : \"It's there, but it's not really there,\" like a sixtieth of non-kosher food in a kosher pot. \"The problem is, our Torah doesn't really seem to say that.\" The Torah constantly promises physical results: the second paragraph of the Shema (love Hashem and I will give rain; turn away and I will stop the rain), and the blessings and curses in Bechukotai and Ki Tavo . (Why the righteous seem to suffer and the wicked prosper is \"a whole different question. We're not addressing that tonight.\") מאחר שנודע שמתן שכרן של מצוות… היא חיי העולם הבא… והנקמה שנוקמין מן הרשעים… היא הכרת… מהו זה שכתוב בכל התורה כולה: אם תשמעו יגיע לכם כך ואם לא תשמעו יקרה אתכם כך, וכל אותן הדברים בעולם הזה, כגון שובע ורעב, ומלחמה ושלום, ומלכות ושפלות, וישיבת הארץ וגלות, והצלחת מעשה והפסדו… Since the reward of the mitzvot is the life of the World to Come, and the punishment of the wicked is karet, what is all this in the Torah, \"if you listen, you will get this; if not, that will happen,\" all about this world: plenty and famine, war and peace, sovereignty and subjugation, living in the land and exile, success and failure…? The answer: all true, but secondary כל אותן הדברים אמת היו ויהיו… ואף על פי כן אין אותן הטובות הן סוף מתן שכרן של מצוות All these things are true; they were and will be… Nevertheless those benefits are not the ultimate reward of the mitzvot. הקדוש ברוך הוא נתן לנו תורה זו, עץ חיים היא, וכל העושה כל הכתוב בה ויודעו דעה גמורה נכונה זוכה בה לחיי העולם הבא, ולפי גודל מעשיו וגודל חכמתו הוא זוכה Hashem gave us this Torah, a tree of life; whoever does all that is written in it and knows it with complete, correct knowledge merits the World to Come, according to the greatness of his deeds and the greatness of his wisdom. Notice the pair, which runs all through Chapters 9 and 10: doing and knowing. \"It's deeds and intellect, you'll see this throughout.\" Your portion depends on your moral identity and your intellectual identity. What the physical promises are for שאם נעשה אותה בשמחה ובטובת נפש… שיסיר ממנו כל הדברים המונעים אותנו מלעשותה, כגון חולי ומלחמה ורעב… וישפיע לנו כל הטובות המחזיקות את ידינו לעשות התורה, כגון שובע ושלום ורבוי כסף וזהב, כדי שלא נעסוק כל ימינו בדברים שהגוף צריך להן, אלא נשב פנויים ללמוד בחכמה ולעשות המצווה If we keep it with joy… He will remove what prevents us , such as illness, war and famine, and provide what supports us , such as plenty, peace and wealth, so that we don't spend all our days on the body's needs but are free to study wisdom and do mitzvot. \"People spend their whole life making a living but don't actually live their life.\" And the reverse: if we abandon Torah for הבלי הזמן (as וישמן ישורון ויבעט , \"Yeshurun grew fat and kicked\"), Hashem removes the good that let us rebel and brings the impediments: illness, debts, pressure. This is not the blocking of teshuvah from Chapter 6; \"it's just harder.\" כי אין מעשה וחשבון ודעת וחכמה בשאול : there is no deed, reckoning, knowledge or wisdom in the grave. \"One of the most beautiful verses.\" The work can only be done here. Summary of Chapter 9: the Torah's promised blessings are secondary rewards that free us to build the moral and intellectual identity that is our primary reward, Olam HaBa. The curses are the opposite: obstacles. Question: why does the Torah emphasise the secondary? In his Essay on Resurrection the Rambam adds: because that's what motivates people . Most of us, day to day, think about what's good for me now , this week, this year. \"How often do we stop to think about our real identity and our real portion of Olam HaBa? Which is why this is so good that we're doing this, for yourselves and for myself.\" The Rabbi's analogy: salary and bonuses. - The salary is Olam HaBa, the main reward. - A valued employee also gets bonuses : a company car, a phone, private health insurance (\"you know you have to wait months or years for an operation on the NHS\"). Not because they are the reward, but because they make you more effective, and they motivate you now . Mark: \"But the wicked prosper\" Mark: won't people see wicked people with health and wealth, and be put off? \"A fair point.\" The Rabbi's answer: what Chapter 9 describes is the system for the completely righteous . The Gemara (Berachot) says people with some negatives suffer in this world to cleanse them for Olam HaBa , a second system running alongside, with \"millions of variables in the calculation.\" And \"blessing\" doesn't mean being a multi-millionaire: Rashi on Bechukotai says you eat a little and are satisfied, enough for peace of mind to focus on what matters. Zenut means hedonism The Rambam's phrase is שנעסוק בהבלי הזמן, ובאכילה ושתייה וזנות . Zenut is usually translated as promiscuity. But in Sefer HaMitzvot , on ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם ואחרי עיניכם , the Rambam says \"after your eyes\" means zenut , and defines it as the pursuit of physical, carnal pleasure in general. A participant: \"hedonism.\" \"Very good.\" - זנות shares a root with זן/מזון , to feed and nourish. Hence מזונות , sustaining food (the five grains: wheat, barley, spelt, rye, oats). - Rachav הזונה (Yehoshua 2): some Midrashim say she was not a harlot but an innkeeper , one who feeds people. - Nourishing the physical self, in whatever form: that is the life the Rambam warns against. The Mishnah \"whose fruits we eat in this world\" The Mishnah said every morning ( אלו דברים שאדם אוכל פירותיהן בעולם הזה ) implies some mitzvot are rewarded in this world. Either the Rambam follows the Gemara's view that שכר מצוה בהאי עלמא ליכא (there is no reward in this world), or he would read the Mishnah as greater secondary reward for those mitzvot. Halacha 2: Why we long for Mashiach ומפני זה נתאוו כל ישראל, נביאיהם וחכמיהם, לימות המשיח, כדי שינוחו ממלכויות שאינן מניחות להן לעסוק בתורה ובמצוות כהוגן This is why all Israel, its prophets and sages, longed for the days of Mashiach: to be free of the kingdoms that don't let them study Torah and keep mitzvot properly. \"So that we're not going to have the UN and all the other nations trying to take away our focus.\" In that era: - Knowledge and wisdom will abound , כי מלאה הארץ דעה את ה' ; war will no longer be taught; \"I will remove the heart of stone.\" - The king from David's line will be wiser than Shlomo and a great prophet, close to Moshe , though not like Moshe. All nations will come to learn from him. - The world continues as normal ; the core is sovereignty and peace, so we can sit in our land and study. - It is for humanity , not just Jews: the nations will return to the true faith. Some read this as conversion; the main understanding is that they will live by the seven Noachide laws . - \"Silk garments growing on trees,\" the origin of \"money grows on trees\": you won't need to go out to work for food, clothing and shelter. \"People think Mashiach, that's it, it's all over. No, absolutely not. Mashiach is just that we're able to focus on developing our moral and intellectual identities.\" Free will remains in the days of Mashiach (discussed two or three years ago in Da'at Tevunot ), otherwise development would be meaningless. But it is \"a higher level of free will: not between good and bad, but between good and excellent .\" Wiser than Shlomo? The verse says Hashem made Shlomo wiser than all before and after him. In the Moreh Nevuchim the Rambam explains that means in his own generation , not for all time. Chapter 10: Serving Hashem from Love \"One of the most loving chapters… the most heart-charging chapter in the Rambam.\" It closes Sefer HaMada , the first of the Mishneh Torah's 14 books (Yesodei HaTorah, Deot, Talmud Torah, Avodah Zarah, Teshuvah), and leads into the second book, Sefer Ahavah , the Book of Love , on the mitzvot of daily devotion. \"It bridges the gap.\" Halacha 1: Not for reward, not from fear אל יאמר אדם: הריני עושה מצוות התורה ועוסק בחכמתה כדי שאקבל כל הברכות הכתובות בה או כדי שאזכה לחיי העולם הבא, ואפרוש מן העבירות… כדי שאנצל מן הקללות… או כדי שלא אכרת מחיי העולם הבא. אין ראוי לעבוד את ה' על הדרך הזה, שהעובד על דרך זה הוא עובד מיראה, ואינה מעלת הנביאים ולא מעלת החכמים Let no one say: I keep the mitzvot and study for the blessings (Chapter 9) or for Olam HaBa (Chapter 8); I avoid sin to escape the curses or karet. That is not the proper way to serve Hashem: it is serving from fear , not the level of the prophets or the wise. ואין עובדין את ה' על דרך זה אלא עמי הארץ והנשים והקטנים, שמחנכין אותן לעבוד מיראה, עד שתרבה דעתן ויעבדו מאהבה. It is not wrong; \"it's good to serve Hashem from fear.\" It is where everyone begins. But \"this is a very aspirational chapter.\" Yirat haRomemut : a third level? Some Rishonim (e.g. Rabbeinu Yonah ) speak of three levels: Level Meaning 1. Fear of punishment The bottom level 2. Love Serving simply because you love Him, no ulterior motive 3. יראת הרוממות , awe of His exaltedness A fear that grows from the love The Rabbi's analogy for the third: being in the presence of the late Queen . You admire her, and in her presence you are afraid, not of being thrown out of the palace (that's level 1), but from awe at who she is. The Rambam never uses the term. The closest is in Yesodei HaTorah 2:2 : contemplating Hashem's works, a person comes to love and longing, and immediately recoils in fear , knowing he is a tiny creature. So perhaps he agreed with it. Halacha 2: Doing the truth because it is true העובד מאהבה עוסק בתורה ובמצוות והולך בנתיבות החכמה, לא מפני דבר בעולם, ולא מפני יראת הרעה, ולא כדי לירש הטובה, אלא עושה האמת מפני שהוא אמת, וסוף הטובה לבוא בכלל. ומעלה זו היא מעלה גדולה עד מאד, ואין כל חכם זוכה לה, והיא מעלת אברהם אבינו שקראו הקדוש ברוך הוא אוהבו One who serves from love… does the truth because it is the truth , and good comes in the end anyway. This is a very great level, which not every sage attains: the level of Avraham, whom Hashem called \"My beloved.\" Even spiritual reward (\"I want a great portion in Olam HaBa\") is not the highest motive. But: \"just because you can't do it doesn't mean you shouldn't do the mitzvot. Absolutely not.\" A story: the etrog with no reward \"I'm not a great fan of Chassidic stories, but this one I like.\" (Told of the Baal Shem Tov , or the Vilna Gaon .) In Eastern Europe a community would order one set of arba minim months in advance and share it. One year the town got none. A wealthy man in the next town agreed to lend his on one condition: he would receive the reward for the mitzvah. The townspeople hesitated to tell the rebbe, who was overjoyed: \"Now, for the first time in my life, I can do a mitzvah knowing I'll get no reward.\" \"We trick ourselves… Subconsciously, we know deep down there's a reward.\" The Rabbi doesn't believe the story happened, since he doesn't believe a mitzvah's reward can be transferred, \"but it's a nice idea.\" Isn't Rabbi Akiva a better example? He was waiting for the specific mitzvah of kiddush Hashem , which can only be fulfilled under that pressure. \"A similar idea,\" but more specific. Halacha 3: Lovesick וכיצד היא האהבה הראויה? הוא שיאהב את ה' אהבה גדולה יתירה עזה מאד, עד שתהא נפשו קשורה באהבת ה', ונמצא שוגה בה תמיד, כאלו חולה חולי האהבה, שאין דעתו פנויה מאהבת אותה אשה, והוא שוגה בה תמיד, בין בשבתו בין בקומו, בין בשעה שהוא אוכל ושותה. יתר מזה תהיה אהבת ה' בלב אוהביו… והוא ששלמה אמר דרך משל: כי חולת אהבה אני. וכל שיר השירים משל הוא לעניין זה What is proper love? To love Hashem so intensely that one's soul is bound to it, constantly absorbed in it, like someone lovesick who cannot stop thinking of a woman, sitting, standing, eating, drinking. Even more than that should the love of Hashem be in the hearts of those who love Him… As Shlomo said, \"I am lovesick.\" All of Shir HaShirim is a parable for this. \"All these movies with passionate romantic love stories\" exist, in a sense, so we can understand what love of Hashem means. \"If anyone's ever felt like that about Hashem, I'll bow down to such a person.\" Halacha 4: Taught only to select students, so why publicly? אמרו חכמים הראשונים: שמא תאמר הריני למד תורה בשביל שאהיה עשיר, בשביל שאקרא רבי, בשביל שאקבל שכר לעולם הבא? תלמוד לומר: לאהבה את ה'… במצותיו חפץ מאד, ולא בשכר מצותיו The great Sages taught this privately, to select students . Why? Because of Antigonos of Socho , who constantly taught \"don't be like servants who serve for reward.\" His students Tzadok and Baitos concluded there was no reward and no Olam HaBa, and founded the Sadducees and Boethusians. So how can the Rambam teach it publicly? Because he has already laid the foundation in Chapters 8 and 9 : there is an Olam HaBa. \"I will never tell you this by itself, but I can tell you this as the next step.\" Halacha 5: Lishmah and shelo lishmah כל העוסק בתורה כדי לקבל שכר או כדי שלא תגיעהו פורענות, הרי זה עוסק שלא לשמה. וכל העוסק בה לא ליראה ולא לקבל שכר, אלא מפני אהבת אדון כל הארץ שציוה בה, הרי זה עוסק בה לשמה. ואמרו חכמים: לעולם יעסוק אדם בתורה ואפילו שלא לשמה, שמתוך שלא לשמה בא לשמה Lishmah: because Hashem commanded it, not for Olam HaBa, a title, exams, semichah , becoming a rav or a dayan. Still study shelo lishmah : it leads to lishmah. Extended to mitzvot: the Gemara says it of Torah study; the Rabbi is \"almost sure\" that Rav Kafich's edition of the Rambam (\"one of the most precise editions… the one I like to study the Rambam from\") reads בתורה ובמצוות . Teaching children (Rambam, Commentary on the Mishnah, Sanhedrin): learn this chapter and I'll give you a nut (\"that was the sweets in those days\"; the Gemara keeps children awake on Seder night with nuts); later, nice clothes and shoes; then money; then status , a title and a community. Then, hopefully, the person matures and studies \"because this is the right thing to do.\" Rewards and punishments are not the ultimate, \"but it's better than nothing.\" Which shelo lishmah is bad? The Gemara also says one who studies shelo lishmah \"would be better not created.\" Tosafot distinguish: - Shelo lishmah for honour or reward: still worthwhile. - Studying לקנתר , to taunt or catch people out (\"I'm going to look it up and come back to you to dispute your ruling\"): corrosive . Better not to study. The Rabbi thinks the Rambam would agree. Where the Rambam would not agree with Tosafot: Tosafot (citing \"I give this coin to tzedakah so that my son will live, and he is a complete tzaddik\" ) say doing a mitzvah for a benefit is fine, and the problem is only if you'll regret it when the benefit doesn't come. The Rambam rejects this outright. He demands a very pure lishmah . Such a person is perhaps a tzaddik but not a chassid : \"fine, but not the highest level.\" Halacha 6: Love comes through knowledge ואינו אוהב הקדוש ברוך הוא אלא בדעת שידעהו, ועל פי הדעה תהיה האהבה, אם מעט מעט ואם הרבה הרבה. לפיכך צריך האדם ליחד עצמו להבין ולהשכיל בחכמות ותבונות המודיעים לו את קונו One loves Hashem only through knowing Him; love is proportional to knowledge , little or much. So a person must devote himself to understanding the wisdom and disciplines that make his Creator known to him. Sefer HaMitzvot, positive mitzvah 3 : one comes to love Hashem through studying His Torah and His world . Moreh Nevuchim : the natural sciences are prerequisites for love of Hashem and for prophecy. \"When we understand deep concepts in science, it's like: wow! Look at the wisdom in the world.\" How do you get to know an author or philosopher? You study their works. What did Hashem leave us? The book and the world. \"By studying the book, the Torah, and by studying the world, a person comes to understand and to love Hashem.\" \"What a beautiful way to complete Hilchot Teshuvah.\" Summary Chapter 9: the Torah's physical blessings and curses are true but secondary . They remove or add obstacles to building our deeds and knowledge , which earn Olam HaBa. Like a company's bonuses, they motivate us now. Zenut in the Rambam = hedonism , the life of physical nourishment alone. Mashiach = this world at peace and sovereign, free to pursue wisdom, for all humanity, with free will choosing \"between good and excellent.\" Chapter 10: serving for reward or from fear is legitimate but basic. The ideal is \"doing the truth because it is the truth,\" the level of Avraham, a love like being lovesick . It was taught privately because of Antigonos' students ; the Rambam can teach it after establishing Olam HaBa. Shelo lishmah leads to lishmah. Start with \"nuts.\" The Rambam's lishmah is purer than Tosafot's. Love grows from knowledge : of the Torah and of the world. Coming Up No shiur next week. From 21 January 2025: the new Tuesday-night series on the letters of the Rambam , about a year's worth. \"It's been a great journey. We started in Elul, in September, together, and we have now finished it.\"","YIkVOlDDLvU":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 8, Halachot 5–8 (end of the chapter), and the Rambam's whole framework of the afterlife Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 17 December 2024 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. The Sofer.ai audio of this class is the only recording found. Recap: Chapter 8, Halachot 1–4 Why here? Teshuvah naturally leads to Olam HaBa; and before Chapters 9–10 (no reward in this world, serve from love), the Rambam wants it clear that the real reward is Olam HaBa. Halacha 1: the greatest reward is Olam HaBa, \"long\" and \"good\"; the greatest consequence is karet , being cut off. Halacha 2: Olam HaBa has nothing physical : no eating, drinking, sleeping, sitting, pleasure or pain. \"A state of pure existence.\" The crowns of the righteous are their intellect : how far they refined their understanding of Hashem, Torah and mitzvot. The bigger the crown, the closer the seat. The Ra'avad's objection rested on treating Techiyat HaMetim as the gateway to Olam HaBa; for the Rambam they are separate. Halacha 3: the \"soul\" means intellect , not the animal life-force. Halacha 4: Tanach's many names ( הר ה', ארץ החיים, חצרות ה' …) all mean Olam HaBa. Part 1: Karet's Names (Halacha 5) הנקמה שאין נקמה גדולה ממנה, שתכרת הנפש ולא תזכה לאותן החיים… וזהו האבדון שקורין אותו הנביאים דרך משל: באר שחת, ואבדון, ותפתה, ועלוקה The greatest retribution is that the soul is cut off and does not merit that life… This is the destruction the prophets call, metaphorically, \"the pit of destruction,\" Avaddon , Tofteh , Aluka . Just as Olam HaBa has many names, so does karet : \"there is nothing worse than this.\" As explained before, for the Rambam karet is not only ceasing to exist; a residue of identity remains that knows what it is missing. People who read him as saying the wicked simply vanish thought he was wrong; this was one of the reasons his books were burned in the 13th century. \"As we proved last time, that clearly was not the Rambam's understanding.\" Part 2: Don't Imagine a Physical Paradise (Halacha 6) שמא תהיה קלה בעיניך טובה זו, ותדמה שאין שכר המצוות ושלמות האדם… אלא שיהיה אוכל ושותה מאכלות טובות, ובועל צורות נאות, ולובש בגדי שש ורקמה, ושוכן באהלי שן, ומשתמש בכלי כסף וזהב… כמו שמדמין אלו הערביים הטפשים האווילים השטופים בזמה. אבל החכמים ובעלי דעה יודעים שכל הדברים האלו דברי הבאי והבל… ואינן טובה גדולה אצלנו בעולם הזה אלא מפני שאנו בעלי גוף וגויה Perhaps this good seems slight to you, and you imagine the reward of mitzvot is to eat and drink fine food, enjoy beautiful partners, wear fine linen, live in ivory palaces and use gold and silver… as the foolish Arabs, steeped in promiscuity, imagine. But the wise know these are empty words… they seem great to us in this world only because we have bodies. The Rambam \"doesn't mince his words.\" Whatever one thinks of the political correctness, he is echoing the Gemara in Kiddushin : ten measures of promiscuity came down to the world, and nine went to the Arabs. (Who exactly \"the Arabs\" are there \"is a different discussion.\") He is referring to certain conceptions of a sensual paradise. Why do we value money, homes, food and partners? Because we live in bodies, which need security and pleasure. Remove the body, and they are irrelevant. \"This is why the concept of there being no body is so fundamental.\" Two proofs that physical pleasure isn't the ultimate good The diet (Rambam, Commentary on the Mishnah, Sanhedrin): at a wedding with the best caterer, a person on a diet still says \"no thank you.\" \"Even in the physical world we see that these pleasures are not the ultimate good.\" The Rabbi: \"I really liked that when I saw that.\" The blind man and colour : you could spend years describing red versus orange to someone who has never seen, and make no progress. So too, we can't describe the pleasure of Olam HaBa while we live in bodies. Part 3: Beyond Description (Halacha 7) מה רב טובך אשר צפנת ליראיך : How great is Your goodness, which You have hidden for those who fear You. David HaMelech longed for it : לולא האמנתי לראות בטוב ה' בארץ חיים , \"had I not believed I would see the goodness of Hashem in the land of the living…\" \"If only I could get a feeling in this world of what Olam HaBa is.\" It isn't possible. The prophets only described the days of Mashiach. \"The wolf will lie with the lamb, whatever that means,\" Israel's sovereignty: all physical, all this world. Olam HaBa: עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך , \"no eye has seen it, except God.\" \"Whoever tries to tell you what's going to happen and how you're going to feel there is talking nonsense.\" We can know general truths : it is the greatest good and it is everlasting. Just as with knowledge of Hashem, our physical limits stop us there. Part 4: The Most Important Halacha: Olam HaBa Exists Now (Halacha 8) \"It looks like an innocent halacha, but it's not.\" The Rabbi asked the class to translate עולם הבא . Olivia: \"the world to come,\" a world beyond ours. Which implies it isn't here yet. זה שקראו אותו חכמים העולם הבא, לא מפני שאינו מצוי עתה, וזה העולם אובד, ואחר כך יבוא אותו העולם. אין הדבר כן, אלא הרי הוא מצוי ועומד, שנאמר אשר צפנת… ולא קראוהו העולם הבא אלא מפני שאותן החיים באין לו לאדם אחר חיי העולם הזה The Sages called it \"the World to Come\" not because it doesn't exist now , and this world will be destroyed and then that world will come. That is not so. It exists and stands now , as it says \"which You have hidden away…\" It is called \"to come\" only because that life comes to a person after this life in body and soul. Why this matters \"The central point of this chapter is how Olam HaBa impacts on Olam HaZeh .\" If Olam HaBa is a separate future world, built for me later according to my mitzvot and aveirot, it is disconnected from my actions. The Rambam's view: Olam HaBa emerges from one's activities in this world. It exists concurrently . \"Through the identity we create every moment of life, the way we behave, we are creating or destroying our Olam HaBa .\" You must pass through this world first, because this is where the work is done, but what you arrive at is what you built. A participant: isn't it like crossing a bridge, with Olam HaBa waiting on the other side? \"That's true partially… but I want to say even more: you are creating what's going to be at the end of the bridge. \" (Asked about Gan Eden: \"Let's finish getting Olam HaBa clear and then we'll come to Gan Eden.\") The Ra'avad: what about 6,000 years? The Ra'avad objects: the Gemara says the world will exist for 6,000 years and then be destroyed (we are in 5785, \"so we have 215 years yet\"). Doesn't the Rambam deny this? Yes, he does. In the Moreh Nevuchim he says it can't be taken literally: after the destruction there are supposedly \"1,000 years of void,\" but time itself is created , so how do you measure a thousand years without a world? And it is a single opinion, not the accepted view. \"I suppose if we live the next 250 years we'd know… maybe my great-grandchildren.\" Part 5: Two Frameworks for the Afterlife The other view: Rav Saadiah Gaon, the Ramban, the Ramchal Stage Rav Saadiah / Ramban / Ramchal 1 We live and die 2 This world is destroyed 3 Those who merit it are resurrected in their bodies 4 They live forever, in their bodies, in a new world : that is Olam HaBa (The Monday class will reach this in Rav Saadiah's Essay 9.) The Ramchal ( Derech Hashem , Da'at Tevunot , which some of the class learned): the bodies and world are physical but highly refined , like Adam before the sin . Da'at Tevunot discusses what that body will be like, and stages of elevation (6,000 to 7,000, 7,000 to 8,000 years, and on). Bluntly: the body worked in this world, so the body must also receive its reward. In this view resurrection makes perfect sense : you need a body for Olam HaBa. The Rambam's puzzle For the Rambam, Olam HaBa is pure intellect, existing now. So why would Hashem take someone out of Olam HaBa to return to a body? \"It's mind-boggling what the Rambam does with Techiyat HaMetim.\" That is why he was accused, in his life and after, of denying resurrection. The Letter on Resurrection The plan: after finishing Chapters 9–10 in early January, spend about half a year on the Rambam's letters , including the long Essay on Resurrection . Some points from it: - Again: everyone wants to know about resurrection (what clothes, how tall?) and no one asks about Olam HaBa. - \"How can they accuse me?\" He listed Techiyat HaMetim in the 13 Principles , and in Hilchot Teshuvah 3 among those with no share in Olam HaBa is one who denies resurrection . - He heard that students in Damascus were quoting him as denying it. He ignored one person, but when many said it, he wrote to set the record straight. - Anyone who says I called resurrection a metaphor is lying. Show me where. The resurrected will eat, drink, marry, have children, and die after a long life , in a life like the days of Mashiach. But did he really mean it? \"So much ink has been spilled on this, a lot in academic circles.\" A common view is that the Rambam wrote the letter under pressure , with his books being banned, but really understood resurrection as a metaphor for the life to come. Two pieces of evidence the Rabbi finds strong: The cemetery blessing (seen after 30 days): the Gemara (Berachot 58b) has אשר יצר אתכם בדין… והמית אתכם בדין, ועתיד להקים אתכם בדין , \"who created you in judgement… caused you to die in judgement, and will raise you in judgement.\" A \"really subtle\" proof, credited to a Chief Rabbi (Haham) of the Spanish & Portuguese community about a century ago : compare the Rambam's codified version (Hilchot Berachot), which adds words not in the Gemara. On the Rabbi's reading, the Rambam's version ties the \"raising\" to the life of the World to Come , because for the Rambam the blessing should be about Olam HaBa, not resurrection. The 13 Principles include reward and punishment, Mashiach, and Techiyat HaMetim, but no principle called \"Olam HaBa\" , though it is the centre of his whole philosophy. That makes most sense if Techiyat HaMetim is Olam HaBa in his mind. \"It certainly seems, in my opinion, that the Rambam understood Techiyat HaMetim to be actually not a physical resurrection, but your identity continuing in Olam HaBa .\" But he wrote a whole essay saying otherwise, so the question stays open. Part 6: Where Mashiach Fits The Rambam is explicit elsewhere (Hilchot Melachim): Mashiach is in this physical world. - Israel fully sovereign : \"no more UN resolutions against Israel,\" no one on its borders trying to destroy it. - \"The wolf will dwell with the lamb\" : peace among the nations. - Midrashim about loaves and silk garments growing on trees are metaphors : you won't need to worry about the next meal, suit, rent or mortgage payment, so you can focus on developing your intellect . - Life goes on as usual: eating, drinking, marriage, everything bodily. - The Beit HaMikdash is rebuilt and Eliyahu HaNavi heralds the era. \"Of course we want Mashiach, because that's the time we can dedicate ourselves to Hashem and to developing our identity. But in the grand scheme of things it's a small thing in comparison to Olam HaBa.\" Not in the Rambam No gilgul (reincarnation). Not resurrection: the idea of the soul returning in another body. He never mentions it; \"most of the Rishonim held like him.\" No kaf hakela . The Rambam's Framework in Summary Element The Rambam Olam HaZeh Where you create your Olam HaBa, through the identity you build Olam HaBa Exists now ; non-physical; intellect and consciousness; the ultimate good and everlasting; indescribable. You enter after death Karet Cut off from Olam HaBa, with a residue aware of the loss Yemot HaMashiach A future era in this physical world: sovereignty, Beit HaMikdash, Eliyahu, freedom to pursue wisdom Techiyat HaMetim Officially a physical resurrection to a Mashiach-like life (Letter on Resurrection); possibly, in his deeper view, identity continuing in Olam HaBa 6,000 years Not literal; the world continues Gilgul / kaf hakela Not part of his thought Versus Rav Saadiah / Ramban / Ramchal: this world ends; the worthy are resurrected in refined bodies; Olam HaBa is that new, elevated, physical world. Everyone agrees on one thing: what Olam HaBa will actually be like is unfathomable. Coming Up Winter break. Resume in early January with Chapters 9 and 10 , the final chapters: more on reward, and serving Hashem from love . From late January: a new series on the Rambam's letters (Teiman, Astrology, Resurrection and others), \"a good half a year or so.\" Gan Eden, and how it fits, is still to come.","SGnjdMRSk6c":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 8, Halachot 1–4 Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 3 December 2024 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction: Why Olam HaBa Here? Why does the Rambam explain the World to Come inside Hilchot Teshuvah ? Two reasons: Teshuvah is returning to Hashem , and the end of that return is Olam HaBa. To prepare for Chapter 10. There the Rambam teaches that one must serve Hashem from love, not from fear or hope of reward. Pirkei Avot tells how Antigonos taught \"don't be like servants who serve for reward,\" and his students Tzadok and Baitos concluded there is no reward and no World to Come, and founded the Sadducees and Boethusians. So before Chapter 10, the Rambam first establishes that Olam HaBa exists and what it is (Chapters 8–9). Part 1: Karet, Revisited (Halacha 1) הטובה הצפונה לצדיקים היא חיי העולם הבא… ופרעון הרשעים הוא שלא יזכו לחיים אלו, אלא יכרתו וימותו… וזהו הכרת הכתוב בתורה… מפי השמועה למדו: הכרת בעולם הזה, תכרת לעולם הבא. כלומר, אותה הנפש שפירשה מן הגוף בעולם הזה אינה זוכה לחיי העולם הבא, אלא גם מן העולם הבא נכרתת Recap of last week: karet means having no future identity. But as Chapter 3 shows, some element remains that is aware of what it is missing. Karet has no physical side Look at the last line. The Gemara says \"cut off in this world, cut off in the next.\" Tosafot and others read \"cut off in this world\" as early death (at 50, or 60, per various opinions); some say karet is both. The Rambam turns it around : \"cut off in this world\" means the soul's separation from the body at death . Ordinary souls then go on to the next world; this one does not. For the Rambam, karet is purely spiritual. It has no bearing on this physical world at all. A famous \"machloket\" rethought: milah at 13 If a father did not circumcise his son and Beit Din didn't either, at 13 years and one day the son must arrange his own brit milah . Milah and the korban Pesach are the only two positive commandments whose omission carries karet. The Rambam writes the person only becomes liable to karet when he dies uncircumcised . The Ra'avad says he is immediately liable. This is \"famously\" presented in the yeshivah world as a dispute: karet at death versus karet at once. The Rabbi: \"they've all missed the point.\" Why would the Rambam delay the liability? He doesn't: - He agrees you are liable immediately (and teshuvah, getting circumcised, removes it). - But since karet has no manifestation in this world , it can only take effect after death. - The Ra'avad, like Tosafot, thinks karet can show itself in this life (early or unusual death), so for him the liability bites now. Karet vs. mitah bidei shamayim Karet מיתה בידי שמים , death at the hands of Heaven Example Not doing milah; korban Pesach A non-kohen eating terumah Severity Eternal Less severe After death No identity, no Olam HaBa Death atones , and you keep your identity for the World to Come, though you may undergo some (even severe) punishment This world? No Possibly an early or unusual death, but not only that: the Rambam says a suicide is liable to mitah bidei shamayim , which cannot mean early death (Rambam, Commentary on the Mishnah, Sanhedrin.) Part 2: What Olam HaBa Is Not, and Is (Halacha 2) The Rambam begins with what it is not : העולם הבא אין בו גוף וגויה, אלא נפשות הצדיקים בלבד, בלא גוף, כמלאכי השרת. הואיל ואין בו גוויות, אין בו לא אכילה ולא שתייה… ולא יארע בו דבר מן הדברים שמארעין לגופות בעולם הזה, כגון ישיבה ועמידה ושינה ומיתה ועצב ושחוק וכיוצא בהן The World to Come has no body or physical form, only the souls of the righteous without a body, like the ministering angels. Since there are no bodies, there is no eating or drinking… nor anything that happens to bodies: sitting, standing, sleeping, death, sadness, laughter and the like. Then the Gemara (Berachot 17a): העולם הבא אין בו לא אכילה ולא שתייה ולא תשמיש, אלא צדיקים יושבים ועטרותיהם בראשיהם ונהנים מזיו השכינה In the World to Come there is no eating, drinking or marital relations; rather the righteous sit with their crowns on their heads and enjoy the splendour of the Shechinah. \"Rav used to say this often\" The Gemara introduces it with מרגלא בפומיה דרב : it was \"a regular saying in Rav's mouth.\" He had to keep repeating it because people hold corporeal ideas of the World to Come. \"This is the tragedy of Jewish education nowadays.\" As the Rambam himself writes, children are taught with physical rewards (sweets, money, reputation), and that's appropriate for children. But people leave cheder , stop learning, and are \"left with a childish conception… and therefore they think Judaism is a joke.\" And if you hold onto those childish ideas, \"it's ridiculous to believe in something like that.\" Three phrases, decoded וזה שאמרו צדיקים יושבים, דרך חידה אמרו… כלומר הצדיקים מצויים שם בלא עמל ובלא יגיעה Phrase The Rambam Meaning צדיקים יושבים , \"the righteous sit \" A riddle : they exist there without toil or effort No chairs, no bodies. They simply are , tranquil, in the present עטרותיהם בראשיהם , \"crowns on their heads\" דעת שידעו, שבגללה זכו לחיי העולם הבא, מצויה עמהן, והיא העטרה שלהן : the knowledge they acquired, through which they merited Olam HaBa, stays with them; that is their crown \"One of the most important lines to remember as Jews\" נהנים מזיו השכינה , \"enjoy the splendour of the Shechinah\" שיודעים ומשיגים מאמיתת הקדוש ברוך הוא מה שאינם יודעים והם בגוף האפל השפל : they know and grasp something of the truth of Hashem that they cannot while in the dark, lowly body Understanding Godliness, according to the size of your crown Crowns are metaphors בעטרה שעטרה לו אמו (Shir HaShirim): nowhere does Tanach say Bat Sheva made Shlomo a crown; it means the wisdom she taught him. ושמחת עולם על ראשם , \"everlasting joy upon their heads\": joy is not an object you can put on a head. Why knowledge? Money can be lost; it is not intrinsic. Knowledge is intrinsic. It stays with you, not just in this world but forever, and you can keep thinking about it and developing it. How big is your crown? It depends on how much you learned and understood in your 120 years. And the Shechinah's splendour is enjoyed in proportion to the crown . \"That's it. This is Olam HaBa.\" You exist, in tranquillity; your wisdom is your crown; and through it you understand Hashem as far as you are able, for eternity. The stadium The Rabbi's analogy: Olam HaBa is a stadium, and Hashem, as it were, is the pitch. - A huge crown is a box-club season ticket , right at the front: you see and understand as much as a human can. - A small crown is a seat right at the back, \"but at least you're there, watching the game.\" - Karet is the person never admitted , who still somehow knows there's an epic game going on that he's missing. Mezuzah: not an amulet \"This is one of the things that really bother me.\" People say \"put a mezuzah on every door, it will protect you.\" Chazal do say a mezuzah brings protection, but we don't do the mitzvah for protection , and someone who treats it that way turns mitzvot into personal benefits. The Rambam ( Hilchot Mezuzah 6:13 ): going in and out, you encounter the unity of Hashem, it wakes you up and you remember: שאין שם דבר העומד לעולם ולעולמי עולמים אלא ידיעת צור העולם There is nothing that endures forever and ever except the knowledge of the Rock of the world. \"Remember this line for life.\" Questions from the class Dementia (from the chat): if a person built a big crown and then forgot it? Dementia is a physical ailment. What you learned and forgot \"is still part of your identity… you can't recall it, but it's there.\" The harder question: someone born with a low IQ may have a very happy life but cannot grasp anything deep about Hashem. In the Rambam's terms he has a portion according to what he understood, but \"I can't imagine it's going to be a big one.\" \"It's not fair, and life isn't fair.\" The anthropomorphist: the Rabbi knows religious people who daven three times a day yet picture Hashem sitting in heaven surrounded by the righteous. \"According to the Rambam such a person is nothing… Belief in an anthropomorphic God is not a belief in God.\" Joey Sharma: shouldn't a person be preparing for Olam HaBa all his life, the way we prepare all week for Shabbat? \"Of course… all these small things create a beautiful identity.\" But if the foundations are wrong, \"it's like a person who spends his whole life building a magnificent palace, a brick a day, a mitzvah a day, but the foundations are completely corroded. That palace is not going to last.\" Part 3: The Ra'avad's Objection, and Techiyat HaMetim The Ra'avad is sharp: \"The words of this man seem to me close to one who says there is no resurrection for bodies , only for souls.\" Chazal speak of the righteous rising in their clothes , and of people asking not to be buried in white or black clothes because of the resurrection. The answer (offered by a participant, and confirmed): Techiyat HaMetim is not Olam HaBa. Ra'avad (and many Rishonim) The Rambam Olam HaBa and Techiyat HaMetim The same thing: you are resurrected into the World to Come, so it is physical Two different things Techiyat HaMetim Physical : the dead return to this world. It is \"one minor aspect\" Olam HaBa Physical (bodies) Purely spiritual: souls and intellect The Rambam was accused throughout his life of denying resurrection, partly because of this halacha. He answered in his Letter on Resurrection (the class plans to study it in 2025): he never said resurrection isn't physical; people misread him because they conflate it with Olam HaBa . Elsewhere he observes, with some irony, that hardly anyone asks about Olam HaBa. Instead they ask: will the dead rise naked or clothed? in their burial shrouds? will they still be rich or poor? \"Pointless\" questions about a small part of the picture. The Ramchal (whose Derech Hashem some of the class learned) also speaks of a body in the World to Come, but clarifies he means a highly elevated form, like Adam before the sin , not corporeal flesh. Perhaps the Rishonim who spoke of bodies meant something like that. The Rambam, later in this chapter, mocks the crudely physical paradise of other faiths (the \"72 virgins\"): why restrain yourself in this world to indulge in the next? \"Who is right? I'll leave it to you. We're learning the Rambam.\" More questions Without bodies, how will we recognise each other? \"Who said you need to? There are far greater things than chatting with your friends.\" Joey: will the resurrected return aged 10, or 90? \"I actually don't know… it makes no difference.\" Exactly the kind of question the Rambam called beside the point. Robert: if resurrection happens once, what about people born afterwards? Does humanity end? The Rambam holds resurrection comes once , and there are strong grounds to say he holds this world continues (contrary to the Gemara's 6,000 years). \"Let's park it… we will come to all of that.\" \"Gathered to his people\" ( ויאסף אל עמיו ): a meeting of like minds who understand Hashem. \"Even in our physical bodies people are beginning to talk about telepathy.\" Part 4: Which \"Soul\"? (Halacha 3) כל נפש האמורה בעניין זה אינה הנשמה הצריכה לגוף, אלא צורת הנפש שהיא הדעה שהשיגה מהבורא כפי כוחה Every \"soul\" in this discussion is not the life-force the body needs, but the form of the soul : the knowledge it grasped of the Creator, according to its capacity. Not this nefesh This nefesh The life-force that makes us \"tick\" (blood, digestion, sensation), shared with animals. A body just after death is the same body, minus this Da'at : consciousness and intellect, the capacity to grasp abstract truths. Unique to humans; what lasts forever (Yesodei HaTorah, Chapter 4) Because this life has no death (death belongs to bodies), it is called צרור החיים , the bond of life : והיתה נפש אדוני צרורה בצרור החיים , said by Avigail to David. Hence our prayer \"may his soul be bound up in the bond of life.\" The Rabbi added in passing that whether things done on behalf of the deceased are effective is \"something to consider,\" and the Rambam's view would probably be negative, \"but that's not for now.\" Part 5: Its Many Names (Halacha 4) לטובה זו קראו הנביאים דרך משל בשמות הרבה: הר ה', ומקום קדשו, ודרך הקודש, וחצרות ה', ונועם ה', ואהל ה', והיכל ה', ובית ה', ושער ה'. והחכמים קראו לטובה זו המזומנת לצדיקים דרך משל סעודה, ובכל מקום קורין אותה העולם הבא \"There's no mountain in heaven.\" When you read מי יעלה בהר ה' in Tehillim, think Olam HaBa . The Sages call it a feast ( se'udah ): \"prepare yourself in the corridor so you may enter the banquet hall.\" What name is missing? The Rabbi asked, and someone got it: Gan Eden. For the Rambam (and almost everyone), Gan Eden is not Olam HaBa . It is something different, and in the grand scheme much smaller. (It isn't a \"VIP section\" either; \"Gan Eden is a different time.\") The same applies to Mashiach and the rebuilding of the Beit HaMikdash: separate pieces that will be fitted into the framework. \"Kol Yisrael yesh lahem chelek la-Olam HaBa\" means Olam HaBa in exactly this sense: everyone has some seat in the stadium. Summary Karet is purely spiritual; it can only take effect after death. The Rambam/Ra'avad \"dispute\" over milah at 13 is about manifestation , not liability. Mitah bidei shamayim is different: death atones, identity survives. Olam HaBa has no bodies. \"The righteous sit\" = exist without toil; \"crowns\" = the knowledge you acquired; \"enjoy the splendour of the Shechinah\" = grasping Hashem as you cannot in a body. Your crown is what you understood and turned into who you are. \"There is nothing that endures forever except the knowledge of the Rock of the world\" (Hilchot Mezuzah). Techiyat HaMetim ≠ Olam HaBa. Resurrection is physical and comparatively minor; Olam HaBa is spiritual. The Ra'avad conflated them. The nefesh that lives on is da'at , not the animal life-force. All the Tanach's names (Har Hashem, Ohel Hashem…) refer to Olam HaBa; Gan Eden is something else. Wisdom must come with action: כל שחכמתו מרובה ממעשיו אין חכמתו מתקיימת . That's why mitzvot matter too. Coming Up No shiur next week. In two weeks: the rest of Chapter 8, then fitting Gan Eden, Mashiach, Techiyat HaMetim and the rest into the Rambam's framework.","0kQuaQ1IQxo":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapters: 7 (complete) and the opening of Chapter 8 Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 26 November 2024 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction Chapter 7 is about the love of teshuvah , its beauty and power; the Rabbi went through it relatively quickly. The real focus was Chapter 8 , \"one of the most important chapters in the whole of the Mishneh Torah,\" where the Rambam lays out what Olam HaBa is. People hear Olam HaBa, Gan Eden, Gehinnom, Mashiach, Techiyat HaMetim, gilgulim , even kaf hakela , \"different snippets of the words of the Chachamim,\" but \"the vast majority of Jewish people do not have a full framework of how all of these things tie in together.\" The Rambam provides one. Where we are Chapters Topic 1–2 Teshuvah and viduy 3 A person's identity in relation to teshuvah 4 The five categories of impediments 5–6 Free will, and the verses that seem to deny it 7 The love of teshuvah 8 Olam HaBa Chapter 7 Halacha 1: Repent, confess, and \"shake off\" sin הואיל ורשות כל אדם נתונה לו כמו שביארנו, ישתדל אדם לעשות תשובה ולהתודות בפיו מחטאיו ולנער כפיו מחטאיו, כדי שימות והוא בעל תשובה ויזכה לחיי העולם הבא Since every person has free will, as we explained, a person should strive to repent, confess his sins verbally and shake his hands free of his sins, so that he dies a ba'al teshuvah and merits the life of the World to Come. A review of Chapters 1–2: - Teshuvah is personal : leaving the sin in past, present and future, inside oneself. - Viduy is relational : having fixed myself, I restore the relationship with Hashem, so it must be spoken . Like todah (thanks), from the same root: \"you can't thank someone in your mind.\" - ולנער כפיו , \"shake off\": the Rabbi thinks this stresses a firm, complete teshuvah, \"not a wishy-washy teshuvah.\" - כדי שימות , \"so that he dies [a ba'al teshuvah]\": teshuvah fixes, but it cannot substitute for personal growth. \"People think: I've done teshuvah, I've done everything I need to do. It doesn't stop there.\" Halacha 2: Don't wait לעולם יראה אדם עצמו כאילו הוא נוטה למות… לפיכך ישוב מחטאיו מיד, ולא יאמר כשאזקין אשוב, שמא ימות טרם שיזקין. הוא ששלמה אמר בחכמתו: בכל עת יהיו בגדיך לבנים A person should always see himself as close to death… so he should repent immediately and not say \"when I'm old I'll repent,\" lest he die first. As Shlomo said: \"At all times let your garments be white.\" This paraphrases Pirkei Avot : שוב יום אחד לפני מיתתך , \"repent one day before your death.\" The Gemara (Shabbat 153a): who knows the day of his death? So every day is that day. \"This isn't a morbid halacha… you never know how long you have.\" It applies to people too: \"If you have forgiveness to ask from someone, don't leave it till next week… rectify it now.\" Halacha 3: Teshuvah for character, not only actions אל תאמר שאין תשובה אלא מעבירות שיש בהן מעשה… אלא כשם שצריך אדם לשוב מאלו, כך הוא צריך לחפש בדעות רעות שיש לו ולשוב מן הכעס ומן האיבה ומן הקנאה ומן ההתול ומרדיפת הממון והכבוד ומרדיפת המאכלות וכיוצא בהן… ואלו העוונות קשים מאותן שיש בהן מעשה Don't say teshuvah is only for sins of action… one must also examine one's bad traits and repent of anger, hatred, jealousy, mockery, the pursuit of money and honour, and the pursuit of food … and these are harder than sins of action. An action is an action: \"I stole, I return it, I ask forgiveness.\" It doesn't define who you are. A trait is ingrained and habitual. As Yeshayahu says, יעזוב רשע דרכו : the wicked should abandon his way , his habits and character, not just his deeds. The Rabbi's question Anger, hatred and jealousy are forbidden or frowned upon in the sources. But where does the Torah forbid pursuing money (legally), honour, or good restaurants every night? Nowhere. So why must one do teshuvah for them? Answer: teshuvah is not only about fixing transgressions. It is a realignment of a person's values. Notice the Rambam does not say viduy here: there is nothing to confess, no prohibition broken. \"Olam HaBa isn't just about doing the mitzvot and staying away from the aveirot. It's about a whole identity.\" You can tick every box and still not be a ben Olam HaBa in the full sense, someone able to grow in understanding of Hashem in the World to Come, a place of intellectual and spiritual pleasure. This is not otherworldly: \"it's important to enjoy this world… within the right parameters,\" not living \"from one day to the next\" to amass wealth, honour or the next meal. A participant asked: is this like the Rambam on Talmud Torah, that you can't acquire the crown of Torah while focused on business? \"Precisely.\" The Rambam reads the Talmud's crowns (the righteous sit in Olam HaBa \"with crowns on their heads\") as intellectual refinement in understanding the Divine. You can do everything right and still not win the keter Torah . The Ramban on קדושים תהיו says the same: a person can be נבל ברשות התורה , a scoundrel with the Torah's permission , eating only kosher yet living for gluttony. \"We have to get out of this concept that life is just about doing this and not doing that.\" The same applies to prophecy : arrogance, anger and brazenness are not explicitly forbidden, yet they block spiritual growth. Halacha 4: Where ba'alei teshuvah stand אל ידמה אדם בעל תשובה שהוא מרוחק ממעלת הצדיקים… אין הדבר כן, אלא אהוב ונחמד הוא לפני הבורא כאילו לא חטא מעולם, ולא עוד אלא ששכרו הרבה, שהרי טעם טעם החטא ופירש ממנו וכבש יצרו. אמרו חכמים: מקום שבעלי תשובה עומדין, אין צדיקים גמורין יכולין לעמוד בו A ba'al teshuvah should not imagine he is far from the level of the righteous… he is beloved as if he never sinned; moreover his reward is great, for he tasted sin and left it and conquered his inclination. The Sages said: in the place where ba'alei teshuvah stand, the completely righteous cannot stand. Why follow Rabbi Abahu over his teacher? In Berachot 34b , Rabbi Yochanan holds the lifelong righteous are higher; Rabbi Abahu holds ba'alei teshuvah are higher. Rabbi Yochanan was Rabbi Abahu's teacher, and normally we follow the teacher. Three explanations: 1. In aggadah , unlike halacha, the usual rules of decision don't bind. 2. The Zohar sides with Rabbi Abahu, and the Rambam supposedly knew it. \"Clearly a misunderstanding of the Rambam's whole approach.\" 3. The simplest , from the Geonim and Rav Kafich , who checked it in 84 places in Shas: when the Gemara says ופליגא , \"and he disagrees with,\" the earlier statement is not the halacha. Here the Gemara says Rabbi Abahu's view disagrees , so the Rambam follows Rabbi Abahu. What \"the place\" means The Gemara and the Rambam both say מקום , a place . The Rambam, who usually rephrases, quotes it verbatim. From Shemonah Perakim, Chapter 6 : is it greater to want to sin and hold back, or not to want to at all? Type of mitzvah Higher level Rational mitzvot (don't murder, honour parents) Not wanting it at all. \"I wish I could kill him, but Hashem said no\" is not virtue; it is a defect. (Aristotle) Revealed mitzvot ( mitzvot shim'iyot , a term from Rav Saadiah), e.g. kashrut Wanting it and restraining yourself . \"I remember when I used to eat it, it's so delicious, but what can I do, Hashem told me not to\" beats \"pork disgusts me\" The ba'al teshuvah knows exactly what he is missing, so his restraint is greater. But it is only a place : \"People think: I've done teshuvah, so I'm on a greater level than a tzaddik. No. You're in a place where you may be able to get to a greater level, but you're not yet there. Teshuvah only does sur me-ra , it takes away the bad. You have a reservation.\" Analogy: a pupil hospitalised through Years 10 and 11 misses GCSE Maths. Nobody blames him, but in Year 12 the teacher says, \"I'm really sorry for what you've been through, but you can't do A-Level Maths without GCSE Maths.\" Teshuvah wipes the slate clean; it doesn't supply the growth you missed. Halacha 5: Redemption through teshuvah כל הנביאים כולן צוו על התשובה, ואין ישראל נגאלין אלא בתשובה, וכבר הבטיחה תורה שסוף ישראל לעשות תשובה בסוף גלותן ומיד הן נגאלין All the prophets commanded teshuvah; Israel will only be redeemed through teshuvah; and the Torah has already promised that Israel will repent at the end of their exile and be redeemed at once. The source is Parashat Nitzavim : ושבת עד ה' אלהיך… ושב ה' אלהיך את שבותך (\"you will return to Hashem… and Hashem will return your captivity\"), and ובא לציון גואל ולשבי פשע ביעקב . The Rambam The Ramban ושבת עד ה' אלהיך A promise , a description of what will happen A commandment to repent; one of the 613 As discussed in the first shiur in Elul, for the Rambam the only mitzvah is viduy ; teshuvah itself is its prerequisite, not a separate commandment. Halachot 6–7: From hated to beloved גדולה תשובה שמקרבת את האדם לשכינה… אמש היה זה שנאוי לפני המקום, משוקץ ומרוחק ותועבה, והיום הוא אהוב ונחמד, קרוב וידיד Great is teshuvah, which brings a person close to the Shechinah… Yesterday he was hated before God, detestable, distant, abominable; today he is beloved and desirable, near, a yedid . אמש , \"yesterday,\" in Rabbinic Hebrew means previously . Real teshuvah does not happen in a day. ידיד : a commentator (perhaps the Malbim ) reads it as יד־יד , \"hand-hand,\" two people holding hands, like close friends or husband and wife. Yechonyah (Coniah) , the penultimate king before the destruction of the First Temple: Yirmeyahu says \"write this man childless… even if Coniah were a signet ring on My right hand , I would tear him off.\" Later Chaggai says of his descendant Zerubavel, ושמתיך כחותם , \"I will make you as a signet.\" The Midrash: in Babylonian prison, when his wife came to him to continue the Davidic line, he discovered she was niddah and held back. Hashem said: in Jerusalem you didn't keep these laws, in exile you do. The same imagery that pushed him away brings him back. Halacha 7: separated, then attached. Hashem is everywhere, so what does \"separated\" mean? Loss of hashgachah , Divine providence: \"as interested in you as in the number of animals out in the desert.\" He cries out and is not answered; his mitzvot are rejected ( מי ביקש זאת מידכם , \"who asked you to bring these?\"). After teshuvah: מודבק בשכינה, צועק ונענה מיד , cleaving to the Shechinah, answered immediately, his mitzvot accepted with pleasure. Halacha 8: The ba'al teshuvah's humility, and our duty דרך בעלי תשובה להיות שפלים וענוים ביותר. אם חרפו אותן הכסילים במעשיהם הראשונים… שומעין ושמחים ויודעים שזו זכות להם… ועון גדול הוא לומר לבעל תשובה זכור מעשיך הראשונים Ba'alei teshuvah are very humble. If fools taunt them about their past… they listen and rejoice, knowing it is a merit… And it is a great sin to say to a ba'al teshuvah, \"remember your former deeds.\" \"I remember you when you were a schoolboy, what you used to do…\" The genuine ba'al teshuvah accepts it as part of his tikkun ; the embarrassment is part of regret. The Gemara: הנעלבין ואינן עולבין, שומעין חרפתן ואינן משיבין , those insulted who do not insult back. Two misused sayings \"An insult is an auspicious time for prayer.\" Some hear an insult and think, \"now I can daven for anything!\" That is \"manipulation of what the Chachamim say.\" The Gemara means someone who genuinely isn't hurt because he has let go, not someone seething and biding his time. \"Pray for your friend and you are answered first.\" Today there are WhatsApp groups where people with the same problem swap names. \"How are they both going to be answered first?\" It means genuinely feeling another's pain as your own, \"a really, really hard level,\" not a mechanical trick where you are really still praying for yourself. Bringing up someone's past is ona'at devarim It is forbidden to embarrass a ba'al teshuvah about his past. Likewise asking a convert \"what was it like eating…?\" is insensitive. It may not technically be a sin (it was before he was Jewish), but it is wrong and probably falls under verbal abuse . Chapter 7 in summary Halacha Point 1–2 Repent and confess now ; every day could be the last 3 Teshuvah covers character and values, even things not forbidden 4 Ba'alei teshuvah have a place above the righteous, a potential to be realised through good deeds 5 Redemption comes through teshuvah; the Torah promises it (Rambam) 6–7 Teshuvah changes identity: from distant and hated to beloved, answered and close 8 Ba'alei teshuvah bear insults humbly; we must never remind them of their past Chapter 8, Halacha 1: What Olam HaBa Is A Spanish & Portuguese aside The Rambam calls the reward הטובה הצפונה לצדיקים , \"the good hidden away for the righteous,\" from מה רב טובך אשר צפנת ליראיך . In the Spanish & Portuguese hashkavah (memorial prayer, said for men), the usual opening is טוב שם משמן טוב . But for a person of special communal standing, a warden or shamash or someone who served the community, the verse מה רב טובך אשר צפנת ליראיך is added before it. The definition הטובה הצפונה לצדיקים היא חיי העולם הבא, והיא החיים שאין מות עמהן והטובה שאין עמה רעה. הוא שכתוב בתורה למען ייטב לך והארכת ימים: מפי השמועה למדו, למען ייטב לך לעולם שכולו טוב, והארכת ימים לעולם שכולו ארוך The good hidden for the righteous is the life of the World to Come: life with no death and good with no evil. As the Torah says \"that it may be good for you and you may prolong your days,\" which tradition explains: \"good\" in a world that is entirely good, \"long\" in a world that is entirely long. Two features: only good, and everlasting. The reward of the righteous is to merit this pleasure. The wicked: pera'on , not punishment ופרעון הרשעים הוא שלא יזכו לחיים אלו, אלא יכרתו וימותו… נכרת ברשעו ואבד כבהמה. וזהו הכרת הכתוב בתורה: הכרת תכרת הנפש ההיא, מפי השמועה למדו: הכרת בעולם הזה, תכרת לעולם הבא The pera'on of the wicked is that they do not merit this life but are cut off and die… cut off in his wickedness and perishing like an animal . This is the karet of the Torah: \"cut off in this world, cut off from the World to Come.\" פרעון means payment , the consequence or return, \"not punishment.\" Halacha 5 says the same: הנקמה שאין נקמה גדולה ממנה שתכרת הנפש ולא תזכה לאותן החיים , the greatest retribution is that the soul is cut off. Why is Olam HaBa in Hilchot Teshuvah? Why not in Yesodei HaTorah ? Because the whole purpose of teshuvah is to bring a person close to Hashem, which is Olam HaBa. Having described how beloved teshuvah is (Chapter 7), it's natural to explain where it leads. The question of karet If the worst consequence is simply ceasing to exist, a person could live destructively for 120 years and simply stop. The Ramban was troubled by this; it isn't the traditional picture of the wicked suffering. But Chapter 3 of Hilchot Teshuvah says of those with no share in the World to Come: נכרתים ואובדין ונדונין על גודל רשען וחטאתם לעולם ולעולמי עולמים , cut off, lost, and judged… forever . The Rabbi's understanding, which he thinks the Ramchal shared: the wicked person ceases to exist, but an element of consciousness remains, aware of what it is missing, and that is eternal. What it is like, \"I've got no idea\"; the Rambam himself says later in Chapter 8 that we can speak only in general terms. Why would a benevolent God do that? Gehinnom, which refines a person for a limited time so they can enjoy Olam HaBa, is purposeful. But why leave a remnant aware of its loss, if it has no future anyway? \"A benevolent God… you don't do it, you miss out, finished.\" Suggestions from the class: - \"FOMO,\" a participant said: fear of missing out is one of the great anxieties of today's teenagers, and is itself the punishment. - \"So you understand you did wrong.\" The Rabbi: when I punish my child, it's so he does better in the future . Here there is no future. - \"The soul could come back in another body.\" \" Absolutely not. Let's make that very clear.\" The Rambam did not believe in gilgul (reincarnation). It is not mentioned anywhere in Tanach or the Rabbinic literature, and Rav Saadiah Gaon (in Emunot VeDeot ) calls believers in reincarnation fools , and those who think souls return as animals worse still. (Asked about other sources, the Rabbi agreed that \"not everything in this world is the Rambam,\" but Tuesday nights are the Rambam's methodology.) - Doron: it is less that God actively punishes and more that the identity you built persists. \"What you've carved out of yourself… a purely negative identity… now will persist.\" Your actions don't stop at death; ending in nothingness would be a clemency the person hasn't earned. The Rabbi: \"I want to think about that.\" - The Rabbi's addition: one of the Rishonim writes that without fear of punishment there would be no incentive . If the worst outcome were \"I enjoy 120 years and then nothing,\" why bother? The Rabbi noted this is \"quite different\" from Doron's point. Provisional conclusion: for the Rambam the greatest punishment is that you cease to exist , but for that to be a punishment, some part of you must be aware of it. Summary Chapter 7 is the love of teshuvah: do it now; do it for character and values, not just deeds; the ba'al teshuvah has a place above the righteous, to be earned through growth; redemption depends on it; it turns distance into closeness; and we must never shame anyone for their past. Chapter 8:1: Olam HaBa is life without death and good without evil . The wicked are \"paid\" with karet , being cut off. Chapter 3 suggests some awareness of loss remains, eternally. Why, and whether that is the identity a person built, remains open. No reincarnation in the Rambam's (or Rav Saadiah's) thought. Coming Up Next week: the rest of Chapter 8, what Olam HaBa actually is in the Rambam's mind, and how it fits with Gan Eden, Gehinnom, Techiyat HaMetim, Mashiach and mitah bidei shamayim .","M0rhQbliFWU":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 6 (complete) Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 19 November 2024 (week of Parashat Chayei Sarah) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction: Free Will, Part Two Chapter 5 established that we have free will. Chapter 6 deals with the verses that seem to contradict it . By coincidence, or perhaps not (\"I would like to believe that it was Hashem who did this\"), the Monday night class had just reached Rav Saadiah Gaon's treatment of free will in Emunot VeDeot , Essay 4. So this shiur repeatedly contrasts the Rambam with Rav Saadiah, who quotes the very same verses about 200 years earlier. הרבה פסוקים יש בתורה ובדברי נביאים שהם כמו סותרים עיקר זה, ונכשלים בהן רוב בני אדם There are many verses in the Torah and the Prophets that seem to contradict this principle, and most people stumble over them. People conclude from them that God decides whether a person does good or evil. The Rambam says: \"I will lay down a great principle\" through which all of them can be understood. Part 1: How Consequences Work (Halacha 1) Every action has consequences, and Hashem knows how each person will receive them. But the Torah gives basic guidelines. Three kinds of consequence in this world Through Includes בגופו , the body Physical suffering, and perhaps mental suffering too בממונו , possessions Financial loss בבניו הקטנים , young children Children below bar/bat mitzvah age are, halachically, like a person's \"possessions,\" since he is fully responsible for them (the Torah even lets a father marry off a minor daughter). Once they reach adulthood ( ish ), they bear their own consequences And when Some sins are punished… in this world only in the World to Come only (Gehinnom, karet : Chapter 8) in both Aside: \"Baruch she-petarani\" At many bar mitzvahs, after the boy's aliyah, the father says ברוך שפטרני מעונשו של זה , \"Blessed is He who has released me from this one's punishment.\" - The Rabbi said it is not found in the Talmud. It was introduced by Rishonim, mainly in Europe, on the idea that one is responsible for those under one's influence. The Rema codifies it, and Ashkenazi poskim debate whether to say it with Hashem's name. It is not in the Shulchan Aruch. - It was never a Sephardi custom . The Spanish & Portuguese siddur , \"always the authentic tradition from the last 400–500 years,\" does not contain it; some Sephardim adopted it only recently. - And according to this halacha, it is backwards. The Rambam says children suffer for their parents' sins, not the other way round. If anything, the child should thank Hashem that he is no longer liable for his father's sins. Part 2: Teshuvah as a Shield (Halacha 2) במה דברים אמורים? בשלא עשה תשובה. אבל אם עשה תשובה, התשובה תריס בפני הפורענות. וכשם שאדם חוטא לדעתו ומרצונו, כך הוא עושה תשובה לדעתו ומרצונו When does this apply? When he has not repented. But if he repented, teshuvah is a shield against punishment. And just as a person sins knowingly and willingly, so he repents knowingly and willingly. Why a \"shield\"? Recall Chapter 1: teshuvah alone does not always complete the atonement. Some sins need teshuvah plus Yom Kippur; some need teshuvah, Yom Kippur and suffering or death. So if someone ate non-kosher food and repented, full atonement waits for Yom Kippur, but in the meantime his teshuvah shields him from further consequences. Part 3: The Worst Punishment: Teshuvah Withheld (Halacha 3) ואפשר שיחטא אדם חטא גדול או חטאים רבים, עד שיתן הדין לפני דיין האמת שיהיה הפרעון מזה החוטא על חטאים אלו שעשה ברצונו ומדעתו, שמונעין ממנו התשובה, ואין מניחין לו רשות לשוב מרשעו, כדי שימות ויאבד בחטאו A person may commit a great sin, or many sins, until the True Judge decrees that his punishment for the sins he did willingly is that teshuvah is withheld from him , and he is not given leave to return from his wickedness, so that he dies and is lost in his sin. Something new Chapter 4's 24 impediments made teshuvah hard, even \"almost impossible,\" but Halacha 4:6 insisted that nothing actually prevents it . Here is a completely new idea: for some, teshuvah is actively blocked . \"That perhaps is the greatest punishment that there is.\" The verses Yeshayahu 6:10 : השמן לב העם הזה ואזניו הכבד ועיניו השע… ולבבו יבין ושב ורפא לו : \"Make the heart of this people fat, their ears heavy, shut their eyes… lest they understand with their heart, return, and be healed .\" In the Rambam's reading, they have reached such an abyss that Hashem says: I do not want them to return. Divrei HaYamim II 36:16 : they mocked prophet after prophet עד עלות חמת ה' בעמו עד לאין מרפא , \"until Hashem's anger rose against His people, without healing \": the healing of teshuvah was taken away. Pharaoh ולפיכך כתוב בתורה ואני אחזק את לב פרעה, לפי שחטא מעצמו תחילה והרע לישראל הגרים בארצו… נתן הדין למנוע התשובה ממנו עד שנפרע ממנו Therefore the Torah says \"I will harden Pharaoh's heart\": because he sinned first of his own accord and did evil to Israel, who lived in his land… justice decreed that teshuvah be withheld from him until he was punished. All the Rishonim wrestle with this: if Hashem hardened his heart, where was Pharaoh's free will, and why was he punished? The Rambam's reading: - In plagues 1–5 the Torah says Pharaoh hardened his own heart . - Only in plagues 6–10 does it say Hashem hardened his heart . - The second is the consequence of the first. Hashem did not make Pharaoh sin ; He made him not reconsider , so he would receive what he deserved. In the Shemonah Perakim (Chapter 8) the Rambam adds that just as Hashem can take away a person's sight or the movement of his hands (Yeravam's hand froze in the air; the men around Lot's house were struck blind , in last week's parasha), He can take away a person's appetite and desire for teshuvah . The source is the Midrash Shemot Rabbah : Hashem blocked teshuvah from Pharaoh after giving him five chances, and Pharaoh's heart became \"heavy as liver.\" Three approaches to \"I will harden Pharaoh's heart\" Rav Saadiah Gaon The Ramban The Rambam Meaning Physical strength: Hashem kept Pharaoh alive and well through the plagues, so they could run their course and Israel leave in splendour After five clear plagues, Pharaoh no longer had a free choice; he would have released them out of compulsion. Hashem restored the balance (50/50) so that his choice stayed free Hashem took away his will to repent , as a punishment for freely chosen sins Free will in plagues 6–10? Untouched; the verse is not about it Preserved, by the hardening Gone , yet he is still responsible Difficulty The verse says Pharaoh's heart (\"I haven't seen anyone else who gives this interpretation\") Why send Moshe again? (below) The key: addiction How can Pharaoh be responsible for plagues 6–10 if he no longer had free will? The Rabbi's answer: think of addiction. Imagine someone so addicted to nicotine that, theoretically, on Shabbat he truly cannot refrain from lighting a cigarette. \"God may say to him: you really had no choice… but you will still be culpable. You put yourself in that situation. \" One bad choice leads to the next until the way out is closed, and the person is responsible for all of it. \"Oh what a tangled web we weave…\" The Rabbi likes the proverb \"Oh what a tangled web we weave, when first we practise to deceive\" because it explains a verse in Parashat Kedoshim: לא תכחשו ולא תשקרו . As a commentator (possibly Rabbi David Pardo ) explains: - לא תכחשו : don't deny . The first lie is a free choice. - ולא תשקרו : don't then weave a web of lies to cover it. By then you may feel \"I have no choice, or I'll be caught.\" Perhaps so. But you put yourself there. The same with yichud : the Torah forbids secluding oneself with a forbidden woman. If, having chosen to be there, a person says in the moment \"I couldn't control myself,\" perhaps true, but \"you can't turn around later and say it's not my fault.\" Question: then why keep sending Moshe? If Hashem had blocked Pharaoh's teshuvah, what was the point of plagues 6–10? (For Rav Saadiah and the Ramban, Pharaoh was still free, so there is no problem.) Answer: to show the world that when Hashem withholds teshuvah, the person cannot return. By the seventh plague even Pharaoh's servants said, \"Can't you see this is the hand of God?\" Any sane king would have relented. The Rabbi compared today: 16 million Jews in a world of 8.1 billion, and people still imagine the Jews dominate the world. \"It's difficult for people to get past their biases… But there comes a stage where you see things over and over again.\" Pharaoh's refusal, time after time, demonstrated that he no longer had the choice. \"So the Rambam turns the whole story on its head. Everyone is asking what happened to free will… The Rambam is saying: actually, he didn't have free will , and that was just.\" Sichon, the Canaanites, and Eliyahu The Rambam applies the same principle to: - Sichon , whose heart Hashem hardened so he would not let Israel pass. Difficulty: the Torah records no earlier sin of Sichon's against Israel. Either the Rambam had a source, or it is his own inference. - The Canaanites : had they repented and surrendered they would have been spared; Hashem \"strengthened their hearts\" to go to war because of their sins. - Israel in the days of Eliyahu . Eliyahu says on Har HaCarmel: ואתה הסבת את לבם אחורנית , \"You have turned their hearts backwards.\" Rav Saadiah The Rambam ואתה הסבת את לבם אחורנית So troubled by it he adds a heh , reading it as a question: by allowing this miracle (the fire from heaven), You will turn their backward hearts back to You Plain meaning: You closed teshuvah from the most wicked of them השמן לב העם הזה (Yeshayahu) Entirely physical : weaken them, take away their faculties, so they lose in war Withholding teshuvah The Rambam's conclusion, and a difficulty נמצאת אומר שלא גזר האל על פרעה להרע לישראל, ולא על סיחון לחטוא בארצו… אלא כולם חטאו מעצמן, וכולם נתחייבו למנוע מהן התשובה So God did not decree that Pharaoh harm Israel, nor that Sichon sin in his land… They all sinned of their own accord, and all became liable to have teshuvah withheld. The Rabbi raised a difficulty: by removing the desire to repent, isn't Hashem indirectly causing Pharaoh to keep harming Israel? A participant suggested it is \"just the direct aspect\" the Rambam denies. The Rabbi agreed: not directly , and that is the best plain reading. Indirectly, the effect is the same. Part 4: What We Pray For (Halacha 4) ולפיכך שואלין הצדיקים והנביאים בתפלתן מאת ה' לעזרם על האמת, כמו שאמר דוד: הורני ה' דרכך Therefore the righteous and the prophets ask Hashem in their prayers to help them to the truth, as David said: \"Teach me, Hashem, Your way.\" If we have free will, what does \"teach me Your way\" mean? It means: don't block me from returning. After the sin with Bat Sheva, David prays ורוח נדיבה תסמכני , \"support me with a generous spirit\": despite my sin, don't take away my chance. \"Of course, it's my choice at the end. But I'm asking you: don't block me out.\" Part 5: \"He Guides Sinners on the Way\" (Halacha 5) טוב וישר ה', על כן יורה חטאים בדרך (Tehillim 25:8) If we have free will, how does Hashem \"guide\" sinners? 1. Through messengers : He sends the Torah and the prophets to urge us toward good. \"Ultimately, it's up to us.\" 2. Through how He made us : \"Again the same idea, addiction.\" When you choose good, you come to want more good. One good choice leads to the next ; that is the \"help\" given to whoever comes to purify himself. The mirror image of Pharaoh. Part 6: The Egyptians and the Covenant (Halacha 5, end) The question At the Brit Bein HaBetarim (in Lech Lecha, two weeks earlier) Hashem told Avraham ועבדום וענו אותם , \"they will enslave them and afflict them.\" So the Egyptians were decreed to do it. Why punish them? And at the end of the Torah, Moshe says \"I know that after my death you will act corruptly\" and serve idols. Where is free will? The Rambam's answer Hashem stated what will happen in general, not who will do it. As in the Shemonah Perakim: when the Torah says a certain sinner is stoned, it doesn't mean anyone must commit that sin. As כי לא יחדל אביון מקרב הארץ , \"the poor will never cease from the land,\" doesn't decree that any particular person must be poor. Each individual Egyptian could have chosen not to harm Israel. Hashem never told any particular Egyptian he must. A follow-up question \"Imagine Egypt had a million people, and each one chose not to harm Israel. Theoretically possible. What would happen to the covenant?\" Unlike \"there will always be poor people,\" here Hashem promised a specific outcome. The Rabbi's answer (with Mark ): the nature of human society is that you never have a society where everyone is perfect. Every individual Egyptian was free, but Hashem was telling Avraham how the world actually runs. \"I think that is the reading of the Rambam.\" The Ra'avad: \"childish words\" The Ra'avad is scathing here, calling the Rambam's words, in effect, childish . He asks exactly that question: if every Egyptian chooses good, what happens to the decree? A further answer: they did more than decreed The other approach the Rabbi cited: the Egyptians were meant to subjugate Israel; that was the covenant. They were punished for the excess : the cruelty and the glee. \"Did you have to do it with that intensity?\" The Rabbi compared the Stanford prison experiment (1970s): volunteers role-playing as guards began, within about two weeks, to enjoy abusing the \"prisoners.\" \"Okay, this is your experiment, you have to do it. But now I am taking enjoyment in it, I am doing more than I need to.\" That is what the Egyptians were punished for. Summary Consequences come through body, possessions or young children; in this world, the next, or both (Halacha 1). The bar mitzvah blessing Baruch she-petarani is a later Ashkenazi custom, absent from the Spanish & Portuguese siddur, and arguably backwards. Teshuvah is a shield while full atonement is pending (Halacha 2). The gravest punishment is that Hashem withholds teshuvah (Halacha 3). Pharaoh hardened his own heart for five plagues; then Hashem hardened it. Like an addict, he lost the choice, and was still responsible because he put himself there. Rav Saadiah (physical strength), the Ramban (restoring the balance) and the Rambam (withdrawing teshuvah) read the verses very differently. \"Teach me Your way\" means \"don't shut me out.\" \"He guides sinners\" means the Torah and prophets, and the momentum of good choices (Halachot 4–5). The Egyptians: the decree was general, every individual was free. The Ra'avad objects; another answer is that they were punished for exceeding the decree. Coming Up Chapter 7 next week: how teshuvah brings us close to Hashem, leading into Olam HaBa . Because teshuvah is about our identity and the reality we create for ourselves, the Rambam moves on to the World to Come, karet and Gehinnom: \"the full theological picture of what the afterlife consists of.\"","ftYY0Mhj0fQ":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 5 (complete, Halachot 1–5) Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 12 November 2024 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction: The Chapter in One Sentence Recap of Chapters 1–4 Chapter Topic 1 The definition of teshuvah and its relationship with viduy 2 Teshuvah gemurah ; the best times for teshuvah; how to do viduy; asking others' forgiveness; whether sins should be publicised 3 Tzaddik, rasha, beinoni ; those with no share in the World to Come 4 The 24 impediments to teshuvah, in five categories (almost impossible; doors locked; no complete teshuvah; presumed not to repent; hard to stop), yet teshuvah always remains possible \"We must know that we have free will, but we cannot know how we have free will\" That, the Rabbi said, is Chapter 5 in one sentence. Elements of it had come up in earlier shiurim (especially the Shemonah Perakim earlier in the year), but this chapter puts the whole picture together systematically. Why is this in a book of halacha? The Mishneh Torah is a book of halacha, not philosophy. The Rambam's philosophy is in the Moreh Nevuchim and in his commentary on the Mishnah (the eighth of the Eight Chapters). Yet he explains the mechanics of free will here because, as he repeats throughout the chapter, without free will, everything he has said about teshuvah makes no sense. If we are not free, we cannot be obliged to repent. Part 1: Every Person Is Free (Halacha 1) רשות כל אדם נתונה לו: אם רצה להטות עצמו לדרך טובה ולהיות צדיק, הרשות בידו; ואם רצה להטות עצמו לדרך רעה ולהיות רשע, הרשות בידו Every person is granted licence: if he wishes to incline himself to the good path and be righteous, the choice is his; if he wishes to incline himself to the evil path and be wicked, the choice is his. \"Behold, man has become like one of us\": two readings The proof-text is Bereishit 3:22: הן האדם היה כאחד ממנו לדעת טוב ורע. Reading Translation Meaning Rashi \"Man has become like one of us \" He is unique in the lower world as I am unique in the upper world. Others read: he is like the angels, knowing good and evil The Rambam , following Targum Onkelos ( \"ha adam havah yechidai be'alma minei\" ) \"Man has become one, unique , of himself , in knowing good and evil\" Man is the one species with nothing like it, able by his own mind to know good and evil and do whatever he wishes כלומר, הן מין זה של אדם היה יחיד בעולם ואין מין שני דומה לו בזה הענין, שיהא הוא מעצמו בדעתו ובמחשבתו יודע הטוב והרע ועושה כל מה שהוא חפץ, ואין מי שיעכב בידו That is: this human species is unique in the world, and no other species resembles it in this: that by his own understanding and thought he knows good and evil and does whatever he desires, and no one can hold him back. The Rabbi noted that he would have called Rashi's reading \"the simple one\" only because that is how he learned the verse as a child. Onkelos, roughly 2,000 years old and from the time of the Tannaim, reads it exactly as the Rambam does. Animals have no choice between good and bad; man alone does . That is why \"lest he stretch out his hand and take also from the Tree of Life\" follows: he might choose that too, so he is banished from Gan Eden. The halachic point: once it is established that a person is free, he is obliged to do teshuvah. Part 2: Nothing Decrees What You Will Become (Halacha 2) אל יעבור במחשבתך דבר זה שאומרים טפשי אומות העולם ורוב גולמי בני ישראל, שהקדוש ברוך הוא גוזר על האדם מתחילת ברייתו להיות צדיק או רשע. אין הדבר כן, אלא כל אדם ראוי לו להיות צדיק כמשה רבינו או רשע כירבעם, או חכם או סכל, או רחמן או אכזרי, או כילי או שוע, וכן שאר כל הדעות Do not let pass through your mind what the fools among the nations and most of the undeveloped among Israel say, that the Holy One, blessed be He, decrees from a person's birth whether he will be righteous or wicked. It is not so. Every person can be as righteous as Moshe Rabbeinu or as wicked as Yeravam; wise or foolish, merciful or cruel, miserly or generous; and so with all the other traits. No one forces him, decrees for him, or pulls him to either path. Yirmeyahu, read as a statement מפי עליון לא תצא הרעות והטוב (Eichah 3:38) Simply read, this is a rhetorical question : \"Do not both evil and good come from the mouth of the Most High?\" The Rambam reads it as a statement : from God does not come whether you will be good or bad. Hence the next verses: מה יתאונן אדם חי , \"what does a living man complain of?\"; נחפשה דרכינו ונחקורה ונשובה עד ה' , \"let us search our ways and return to Hashem.\" Because we are fully responsible, we must do teshuvah. Question 1: the angel at conception (Niddah 16b) The Hagahot Maimoniyot (student of the Maharam of Rothenburg, about 700 years ago) and others are astonished. The Gemara says an angel takes the drop at conception and asks: גבור או חלש, חכם או טיפש , strong or weak, wise or foolish? That sounds like exactly the determinism the Rambam rejects. \"All the commentators struggle… they give very difficult and really non-convincing answers.\" But there is a simple answer , which Rav Kafich also gives, and which the Rambam himself gives in the Shemonah Perakim (Chapter 8) and the Moreh : People are born with a propensity, a nature, a tendency. What you do with it is your free will. Born with… Free will decides… A high IQ Whether you ever develop it. If not, you will not end up wise A low IQ Whether to put in the (much greater) effort to develop it, \"perhaps even [becoming] a wise person\" A kind or a harder nature Whether you work on it or leave it undeveloped \"We do not deny that people are born with certain natures. Some people are born kinder than others. That's certainly true. The question is, do you develop that? … That's your choice.\" The Rabbi's comment on why the question arises: \"This is the problem if you learn parts of the Rambam without learning the rest of his works.\" Question 2: \"No prophet arose like Moshe\" One of the 13 Principles of Faith is that there will never be a prophet like Moshe ( ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה ). So how can the Rambam say anyone can be צדיק כמשה רבינו ? The answer again comes from the Shemonah Perakim: Chapter 2: the soul has several faculties. The Torah's commands are aimed at the faculty of will ( ratzon ), which is then expressed in the faculty of action . Chapter 7: prophecy requires two perfections: Intellectual perfection , a threshold: you have it or you don't. Moral perfection of the character traits. You need most of it for the lowest level of prophecy; how much you have determines the prophet's level. Moshe was unique in reaching 100% moral perfection, and in that it reached not only his actions but his inner will and character itself. So: Anyone can be as righteous as Moshe in deeds. Our actions can be as complete as his. What is reserved for Moshe alone is that 100% perfection becoming his intrinsic character . \"We do things all the time. The question is, do those actions translate into our intrinsic character or not?\" Part 3: The Pillar of Torah and Mitzvot (Halacha 3) ודבר זה עיקר גדול הוא, והוא עמוד התורה והמצוה This is a great principle; it is the pillar of the Torah and the commandments. The Rambam cites ראה נתתי לפניך היום את החיים ואת הטוב (\"See, I have set before you today life and good…\") and מי יתן והיה לבבם זה להם , \"If only their heart would be like this,\" which shows that Hashem does not force anyone ; He hopes we will choose well. Note the limits: the Rambam speaks of מעשי בני האדם , human actions . How wealthy, healthy, beautiful or plain a person is created is Hashem's decision. What we do is entirely ours. Rabbi Sacks: the fourteenth principle It was Rabbi Sacks' yahrzeit that week, and the Rabbi shared a \"beautiful idea\" in his memory. The Rambam loved the number 14 : - The Mishneh Torah is the יד החזקה , yad = 14, in 14 books . - His introduction to Sefer HaMitzvot lays down 14 principles for counting the 613 commandments. Yet there are only 13 principles of faith, and here he calls free will עיקר גדול , \"a great principle.\" Why isn't it the fourteenth? The usual answer: it is included in the principle of reward and punishment , which is impossible without free will. But when the Rambam explains reward and punishment, he never mentions free will. \"So it's still a bit difficult.\" Rabbi Sacks' answer: \"The 13 principles of faith are our faith in God. The fourteenth is God's faith in us\" : that we are free. He liked to quote Isaac Bashevis Singer : \"We must have free will; we have no choice.\" Life without free will The Rabbi: \"I just do not understand how people get out of bed and get on with their life\" if they believe we are purely programmed by genes, upbringing and society. - Morally , it would mean no one should be punished . At most we would remove \"biologically troublesome\" people from society. - Existentially , \"life becomes absolutely pointless.\" Part 4: Without Free Will, the Torah Collapses (Halacha 4) אילו האל היה גוזר על האדם להיות צדיק או רשע, או אילו היה שם דבר שמושך את האדם בעיקר תולדתו לדרך מן הדרכים… כמו שבודים מלבם טפשי הוברי שמים, היאך היה מצוה לנו על ידי הנביאים עשה כך ואל תעשה כך?… ובאי זה משפט או מדה נפרע מן הרשע או משלם שכר לצדיק? השופט כל הארץ לא יעשה משפט? If God decreed that a person be righteous or wicked, or if something drew a person from his very birth to one of the paths… as the foolish astrologers invent, how could He command us through the prophets \"do this, don't do that\"?… And by what justice would He punish the wicked or reward the righteous? \"Shall the Judge of all the earth not do justice?\" Avraham's challenge over Sedom applies: it is not justice to punish people who are not free. No commandments, no reward and punishment, \"the whole Torah would go.\" Question 3: doesn't this contradict \"propensity\"? In Halacha 2 we accepted that a person is born with a propensity. Here the Rambam seems to deny even that: nothing \"draws a person from his very birth.\" And the Gemara in Shabbat 156 says one born under Mars ( Ma'adim ) will spill blood, as a murderer, or as a shochet or mohel . So astrology seems to give at least a tendency. Answer: the Rambam is disagreeing with that Gemara , and the key word is astrologers . - The Rambam held that while the Talmud is binding, he had licence to depart from an individual statement where other Sages disagreed, and he says so explicitly. The Gemara itself says אין מזל לישראל , Israel is not bound by the stars. - In his Letter on Astrology (the class plans to study the Rambam's letters after finishing Hilchot Teshuvah, from January), he writes, in translation: \"I know that you may search and find sayings of some individual sages in the Talmud and Midrashim whose words appear to maintain that at the moment of a man's birth the stars will cause such and such to happen to him. Do not regard this as a difficulty… It is not proper to abandon matters of reason that have already been verified by proofs and depend on the words of a single one of the sages, from whom possibly the matter was hidden… A man should never cast his reason behind him, for the eyes are set in front, not in back.\" The famous Midrash on Lech Lecha ( ויוצא אותו החוצה : Hashem took Avram \"outside,\" above the stars, out of the influence of his mazal , so that he could have children) is another such individual statement. In the Letter to Yemen he tells them to wash these beliefs out of their minds \"just like you take your dirty garments to the dry cleaner.\" So, in summary: 1. The stars and constellations have no influence at all on a person's nature or decisions. 2. A person has full free will . 3. It is still true that a person is born with a propensity , not from the stars but because Hashem creates him with a certain nature , which he can work on and change. Question 4: how can a person act against God's will? שמא תאמר: והלא הקדוש ברוך הוא עושה כל מה שהוא חפץ… דע שהכל כחפצו נעשה The Rambam's answer: just as Hashem willed that fire rises and water falls and the planets keep their orbits, He willed that man be free. \"There is a force of nature called free will. Just like gravity is reality, friction is reality.\" Our freedom to act against His commands is His will. \"Rejoice, young man, in your youth\" A man in shul used to tease the Rabbi, home from yeshivah, with Kohelet: שמח בחור בילדותך , \"Rejoice, young man, in your youth\"; take a break, take it easy. But the verse continues: ודע כי על כל אלה יביאך האלהים במשפט , \"but know that for all these God will bring you to judgement.\" Shlomo is speaking rhetorically: do as you wish, but you will answer for it. Part 5: The Big Question: Foreknowledge (Halacha 5) The question We believe Hashem is perfect; therefore He knows everything: past, present and future . If He knows what I will choose, how am I free? שמא תאמר: והלא הקדוש ברוך הוא יודע כל מה שיהיה… דע שתשובת שאלה זו ארוכה מארץ מדה ורחבה מני ים Perhaps you will say: doesn't the Holy One, blessed be He, know everything that will be?… Know that the answer to this question is \"longer than the earth and broader than the sea.\" The Islamic philosophers wrestled with it, as does Rav Saadiah Gaon in Emunot VeDeot (the Monday class). There are four main approaches: Rav Saadiah , Ralbag (Gersonides), Rabbi Chasdai Crescas , and the Rambam . The others accept the question and try to limit one side or the other. The Rambam's answer, the Rabbi believes, is the best: there is no question. Step 1: God and His knowledge are one From Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah, Chapter 2 : the unity of God means not only that there is one God but that all His \"attributes\" are one with His essence : absolute simplicity, no multiplicity. A human being God There is the person and his wisdom, his kindness, his truthfulness. These can be lost or changed. Multiplicity. His knowledge, compassion and so on are not separate \"traits\"; they are His essence. Simplicity. אין הקדוש ברוך הוא יודע בדעה שהיא חוץ ממנו כבני אדם שהם ודעתם שנים, אלא הוא יתעלה שמו ודעתו אחד The Holy One, blessed be He, does not know with a knowledge external to Himself, like people, who are two, themselves and their knowledge. He, may His name be exalted, and His knowledge are one. What about the 13 Attributes of Mercy? They describe how we perceive Hashem's actions. The Rabbi's analogy: sunlight is one , but ten people on a beach wearing red, green and blue glasses each see a different light. \"So too… it is manifested to us in different ways… But God has no attributes.\" Step 2: we cannot know His essence, so we cannot know His knowledge וכשם שאין כח באדם להשיג ולמצוא אמתת הבורא, שנאמר כי לא יראני האדם וחי, כך אין כח באדם להשיג ולמצוא דעתו של בורא. הוא שהנביא אמר: כי לא מחשבותי מחשבותיכם ולא דרכיכם דרכי Just as man cannot grasp the true nature of the Creator, as it says \"no man can see Me and live,\" so man cannot grasp the Creator's knowledge. As the prophet said: \"My thoughts are not your thoughts, nor your ways My ways.\" Why this is not a cop-out \"He's not giving a cop-out answer… He's saying: of course they go together. … You're asking a question on God's knowledge… it's a different kind of knowledge.\" We are bound by time. Our knowledge is built on past, present and future, so \"knowing the future\" sounds like fixing it. Hashem's knowledge is above time. It is not \"fore\"-knowledge at all. So the contradiction exists only from the human point of view. \"The question doesn't start.\" ואנו יודעים בלא ספק שמעשה האדם ביד האדם And we know without doubt that a person's actions are in his own hands. How free will and divine knowledge coexist we cannot know; that they do, we know. Therefore a person is responsible for his actions, and the whole Torah makes sense. From the class: Mark suggested it depends whose perspective you look from, and that there are things beyond our control. The Rabbi agreed: from the human perspective there is a question; from God's there isn't. The Ra'avad's objection לא נהג המחבר הזה מנהג החכמים, שאין אדם מתחיל בדבר ואינו יודע להשלימו This author did not act in the manner of scholars, for no one begins something he does not know how to finish. The Ra'avad (Rabbi Avraham ben David): the Rambam raised a huge question and answered \"just believe.\" Better not to ask it at all than to plant doubts. He offers an answer of his own, similar to Rav Saadiah's: watching someone deliberate for sixty seconds about whether to get out of bed for shul, and knowing the outcome, doesn't force it. Then he concedes his own answer is not really a good one. The Rabbi's comments: 1. The Ra'avad did not understand the Rambam. Had he, there would be no question: the Rambam is not saying \"just believe\" but giving a philosophical answer, the same one he gives in the Shemonah Perakim and the Moreh . 2. Keeping quiet about hard questions? \"Surely part of faith is grappling with these things and discussing them.\" 3. It's fascinating that the Ra'avad worries the question will cause doubts, and then admits he has no good answer to it himself. Summary Every person is free to be righteous or wicked (Halacha 1); man is unique (Onkelos) in knowing good and evil. Nothing decrees who you will be (Halacha 2). We are born with propensities (the angel in Niddah), but what we do with them is our choice. Anyone can match Moshe in deeds ; what is unique to Moshe is perfection reaching his inner character. Free will is the pillar of Torah and mitzvot (Halacha 3). Rabbi Sacks: the 13 principles are our faith in God; the 14th is God's faith in us . Without free will there could be no commandments and no justice (Halacha 4). Astrology has no influence at all ; the Rambam openly disagrees with individual Talmudic statements suggesting otherwise. Our freedom is Hashem's will, like the laws of nature. Foreknowledge (Halacha 5): God and His knowledge are one; we cannot grasp His essence, so we cannot grasp His knowledge, which is above time . The contradiction exists only from our side. The Ra'avad's objection misreads the Rambam. We must know that we have free will. We cannot know how. Coming Up Chapter 6 next week. The full survey of approaches to free will and foreknowledge (Rav Saadiah, Ralbag, Crescas) will come in the Monday night Emunot VeDeot class. From January: the Rambam's letters, including the Letter on Astrology and the Letter to Yemen.","Y1EHEnaVhps":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 4 (complete, Halachot 1–6) Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 5 November 2024 (week of Parashat Lech Lecha) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Introduction: Teshuvah After Yom Kippur The Rabbi opened with a belated Chodesh Tov and a reminder that teshuvah is not a Yom Kippur-only project: \"teshuvah is something which is the whole year round.\" The series began in Elul; with Yom Kippur almost a month behind us, the aim now is to finish Hilchot Teshuvah by the end of December. Chapter 4 is \"relatively straightforward\" compared with what came before. Recap of Chapters 1–3 Chapter What we learned 1 The definition of teshuvah and its relationship with viduy (confession). Teshuvah is the prerequisite to viduy. 2 Teshuvah gemurah , complete repentance; the times especially suited to teshuvah; how viduy is done properly; asking forgiveness from other people ( bein adam lechavero ). 3 Tzaddik, beinoni, rasha (righteous, average, wicked) and a person's standing on Yom Kippur; and, in its second half, the categories of people who, in the Rambam's view, have no share in the World to Come. Part 1: Where Does the Number 24 Come From? The chapter opens: עשרים וארבעה דברים מעכבין את התשובה Twenty-four things hold back teshuvah. The Rabbi pointed out that although \"everything the Rambam says is sourced in the Talmud,\" the number 24 appears nowhere in the Talmud. The Rambam is quoting the Rif , Rabbi Yitzchak Alfasi of Algeria and North Africa, who lived about 150 years before him and listed the 24 at the end of his work on Masechet Yoma. The Rambam was actually asked where the Rif got the number from. In his reply he wrote that the number is not mentioned anywhere , not in the Tosefta, the Sifra or the Sifrei. Perhaps the Rif gathered them from another author, he could not say. But most of the 24 are mentioned, scattered across the Mishnah and Talmud, and the few that are not are derived from the ones that are. So the Rif simply lists the 24; the Rambam gives reasons for each one, explains it, and arranges them. The Five Categories The Rambam sorts the 24 into five categories, descending in severity : four items in the first category and five in each of the other four (4 + 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 24). Each of the first five halachot covers one category; the sixth halacha is the conclusion. Category Halacha The Rambam's description Meaning 1 (4 items) 4:1 אין הקב״ה מספיק בידו לעשות תשובה Almost impossible: Hashem withholds the ability to repent 2 (5 items) 4:2 נועלים דרכי התשובה The doors of teshuvah are locked . A locked door can still be broken down 3 (5 items) 4:3 אי אפשר לו שישוב בתשובה גמורה You can do teshuvah, but not complete teshuvah 4 (5 items) 4:4 אין חזקתו לשוב The presumption is you won't repent, though you can 5 (5 items) 4:5 קשה לחזור מהן Hard to separate from, once you're used to them Conclusion 4:6 Nothing finally prevents teshuvah The key point before starting: \"Despite everything we're going to learn, it's hard, it's impossible, the doors are locked, etc. Still, the option of teshuvah is always available… teshuvah always works in all circumstances, if done properly.\" Part 2: Category 1 – \"Hashem Does Not Give Him the Ability\" (Halacha 1) ארבעה מהן עון גדול, והעושה אחד מהן אין הקדוש ברוך הוא מספיק בידו לעשות תשובה לפי גודל חטאו Four of them are a great sin; whoever does one of them, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not grant him the means to repent, in proportion to the greatness of his sin . \"In proportion to the greatness of his sin\" That last phrase means there are levels within each category, and even within each sin. The worse the version of the sin, the harder the teshuvah. What does \"Hashem does not grant him the ability\" mean? The Rabbi referred back to the class's study of the Shemonah Perakim (the Rambam's Eight Chapters, his introduction to Pirkei Avot) earlier in 2024. In Chapter 8 the Rambam deals with free will and asks how the Torah can say Hashem hardened Pharaoh's heart , or Sichon's, apparently removing their free will. His answer: sometimes a person has sinned so much that Hashem takes away the ability to repent, because He wants the person to bear the consequences. Teshuvah rectifies everything; for these sins, Hashem withholds it. Normally, the Gemara tells us, one who comes to purify himself is helped. Here it is the reverse: Hashem not only does not assist, He actively makes repentance difficult. The opportunity still exists (Halacha 6), but it becomes \"almost impossible for us to want or to be able to.\" The four sins המחטיא את הרבים : causing the public to sin, which includes preventing the public from doing a mitzvah. Clearly causing others to sin is worse than preventing them from a mitzvah: levels again. המטה את חבירו מדרך טובה לרעה, כגון מסית ומדיח : leading another person from the good path to the bad. A mesit entices a few people to idolatry, a madiach a single person. This is the only case in the Torah where causing someone else to sin carries the death penalty. הרואה בנו יוצא לתרבות רעה ואינו מוחה בידו : seeing one's child going astray and not objecting. Since the child is under your authority, had you objected he would have stopped, so it is as if you caused him to sin. The same applies wherever you have authority and the ability to rebuke. האומר אחטא ואשוב : one who says \"I will sin and then repent,\" or \"I will sin and Yom Kippur will atone.\" Why the first three? Your actions are still \"out there\" The Gemara in Yoma explains: if someone who caused the public to sin could easily repent, he would earn his share in the World to Come while those he led astray would not. That would be unjust. The Meiri (13th-century Provence, a student of a student of the Rambam) adds an analogy: it is like a thief who wants to repent but has not returned the stolen object. As long as the people you influenced are still sinning, the effects of your actions are still out there. Question: why was Yeravam offered teshuvah? The Gemara in Sanhedrin says Hashem appeared to Yeravam ben Nevat and offered him the chance to repent, though he was the classic choteh u'machti et harabim . He set up idols along the pilgrimage route and told people they need not go to Jerusalem; they could offer their sacrifices there. Answer: the whole reason the door closes is the ongoing influence . A king has the greatest influence of all, so he alone had the power to undo what he had done and bring all those people back. Precisely because of that, Hashem gave him the opportunity. He did not take it. Rule of thumb: someone who caused others to sin is normally prevented from teshuvah, unless he still exerts great influence , in which case he is more likely to be given the chance, because he can repair the damage. Gechazi: preventing others from learning The Rabbi told the story from Melachim (also the haftarah of Parashat Metzora): - Na'aman , the Syrian commander, had tzara'at . The prophet Elisha healed him and refused payment (just as, in that week's parasha, Avram refuses the spoils from the king of Sedom). - Elisha's student Gechazi ran after Na'aman and took the money; Na'aman gave him double. - Elisha told him that since he took Na'aman's money, he would take Na'aman's illness too. - After they fell out, the Gemara says Gechazi tried to prevent students from going to Elisha's classes : the classic case of preventing the public from a mitzvah, here the mitzvah of Torah study. A parallel in Rav Saadiah Gaon On Mondays the class learns Emunot VeDeot . In the fifth essay (to be learned early next year), Rav Saadiah writes that all sins can be forgiven except three, and the first is one who leads the people astray with wrong advice, intentions or rulings. Even if he repents himself, he cannot rectify what he has done. Category 1, item 3: the duty to object, and the duty to keep quiet Educating children. The Gemara in Kiddushin gives two opinions on the key age for guiding one's child: 16 to 22, or 18 to 24. The Rabbi wondered whether that still holds: \"I think once kids have reached the age of 16 nowadays, I think they're in their own domain.\" Anyone who can object and does not is held responsible. The Gemara tells of a sage whose wife did something wrong; because he could have objected and didn't, he was held culpable. But there is also a mitzvah to stay silent. Just as it is a mitzvah to say something that will be heard, it is a mitzvah not to say something that won't be. Rebuke needs great wisdom and sensitivity. \"Every one of us has a position of influence,\" at work, in the family, in the community, whether we are conscious of it or not. When our words would have been accepted and we stay silent, we are liable, and the effects of that silence continue long after we regret it. \"Rebuke your amit \": first be a friend The verse says הוכח תוכיח את עמיתך , \"you shall surely rebuke your amit ,\" your friend. The Rabbi, citing one of the Acharonim: \"Some people think: whenever I see another Jewish person doing something wrong, I have a duty to tell them off. That's absolutely incorrect. Before you can tell someone off, you have to be their friend.\" Only within an existing relationship does rebuke make sense, and each person has to judge when to speak and when to keep quiet. Item 4: when teshuvah itself is the cause of the sin The Mishnah in Yoma: האומר אחטא ואשוב, אחטא ואשוב, אין מספיקין בידו לעשות תשובה; אחטא ויום הכפורים מכפר, אין יום הכפורים מכפר. Saying \"we have Yom Kippur at the end of the year\" is not how it works. The Rambam had a difficulty with this Mishnah, because he holds that teshuvah is always possible. In his commentary he explains that Yom Kippur's atonement is not just the fast; it requires preparation and real teshuvah. Such a person will find that Hashem makes it hard for him to go through the proper process. Why is this in Category 1? Because here teshuvah itself is what causes the sin : \"I sin because I say teshuvah will atone.\" So Hashem withholds teshuvah, the very thing being abused. Part 3: Category 2 – The Doors Are Locked (Halacha 2) חמשה דברים הנועלים דרכי התשובה בפני עושיהן Five things lock the paths of teshuvah before those who do them. It is possible, \"but you're going to have to break down the doors.\" The common thread: each one cuts you off from the people and sources that would guide you back. הפורש מן הציבור : one who separates himself from the community. There are times when the whole community is aroused to teshuvah together; if you are not part of it, you never share that moment. (In Chapter 3, before Yom Kippur, the class saw that \"one who separates from the community\" is also among those with no share in the World to Come, and discussed whether that means someone who keeps the mitzvot but takes no part in communal life, or someone who disassociates himself from being Jewish altogether.) החולק על דברי חכמים : one who disparages the words of the Sages. If you do not listen to them, you will not know how to do teshuvah. המלעיג על המצוות : one who scoffs at the mitzvot (\"this mitzvah isn't for me, it's nonsensical\"). Since increasing good deeds is one of the primary ways of doing teshuvah (Chapter 2), someone who sees no value in mitzvot has no way back. המבזה רבותיו : one who disgraces his own teachers. They will no longer take an interest in him or teach him. The uncensored text of the Rambam gives two examples: Gechazi , whom Elisha pushed away, and Jesus , who in the Talmud's account accused his teacher of gazing at a woman, was excommunicated, lost the relationship with his teacher, \"and the rest is history.\" The Rif, whom the Rambam is quoting, adds one who disrespects his parents here as well. השונא את התוכחות : one who hates rebuke. If you never accept feedback or criticism and \"never find imperfection in yourself,\" you will never change. The ideal communal guide Here the Rambam digresses: Moshe constantly rebuked Israel; Yeshayahu said \"the ox knows its owner\" yet Israel does not know its God; Hashem told the prophets קרא בגרון אל תחשוך , \"cry out with your throat, don't hold back.\" Therefore: לפיכך צריך להעמיד בכל קהל וקהל מישראל חכם גדול וזקן וירא שמים מנעוריו ואהוב להם, שיהא מוכיח לרבים ומחזירן בתשובה Therefore every community in Israel must appoint a great sage, elderly, God-fearing from his youth , and beloved to them , who will rebuke the public and bring them back in teshuvah. Qualification Why חכם גדול , a great sage He must be very wise זקן , elderly Experience ירא שמים , God-fearing \"Who are you to tell other people off when you don't do so yourself?\" מנעוריו , from his youth The Gemara: if you only became good in old age, people will ask \"where were you 20 years ago?\" אהוב להם , beloved to them Perhaps the most crucial. \"There's no point trying to tell people off when you don't have that love.\" The lesson: \"When you want to exert influence upon other people, the first thing we have to do is create a relationship of love.\" Part 4: Category 3 – Teshuvah, but Not Complete Teshuvah (Halacha 3) חמשה דברים העושה אותן אי אפשר לו שישוב בתשובה גמורה These are interpersonal sins where you no longer know whom you wronged , so you cannot ask forgiveness or make restitution. The Rabbi stressed: these are not the worst sins. \"Who's worse? Of course the person who steals.\" But the thief knows whom he stole from; these people do not. המקלל את הרבים : cursing a whole group. Insult one person and you can apologise; insult an entire sect, and whom do you ask? החולק עם גנב : sharing with a thief, buying from him, profiting from him. The thief himself is not on the list: he knows whom to repay. You, who encouraged him, do not. The Gemara's solution is to give the money to communal needs, so it diffuses through the community and, hopefully, reaches the person it was taken from. המוצא אבידה ואינו מכריז עליה : finding a lost object and not announcing it. The Torah (Parashat Ki Tetzei) commands returning and announcing lost property. Years later, how will you find the owner? האוכל שוד עניים, יתומים ואלמנות : taking the collateral (or property) of the poor, orphans and widows. It is forbidden from anyone, but an ordinary person has a fixed address; these people often do not, so you cannot find them to return it. המקבל שוחד להטות דין : a judge who takes a bribe. The Beit Yosef explains: \"once you take a bribe, it perverts wisdom.\" You can never again see the case objectively, so even years later you cannot know what the right ruling would have been. And you caused the briber to sin too. The Rabbi suggested that perhaps the way forward would be to ask someone else to judge the matter afresh, and act on that. Part 5: Category 4 – You Can, but Probably Won't (Halacha 4) חמשה דברים העושה אותן אין חזקתו לשוב עליהן, לפי שהם דברים קלים בעיני רוב בני אדם, ונמצא חוטא והוא מדמה שאין זה חטא Five things: whoever does them is not presumed to repent, because they are light in most people's eyes, so he sins and imagines it is no sin. \"It's fascinating to see the examples.\" These are everyday things that feel trivial. האוכל מסעודה שאינה מספקת לבעליה : eating from a meal that is not enough for its owner. The Gemara calls this אבק גזל , \"the dust of theft.\" You knock on a poor person's door, they are embarrassed and say \"come in and eat,\" and you can see there is barely enough. You were invited, but they did not really mean it. Today: pushing people into something beyond their means that they agree to because they feel they have no choice. המשתמש בעבוטו של עני : using a poor person's collateral. Holding collateral is fine, using it is forbidden. With an expensive watch you would know you were doing wrong; with \"an old axe,\" you would never think so. המסתכל בעריות : gazing at forbidden women. \"Did I touch her? No, I just looked.\" But gazing is the root of further sin. The Rambam counts ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם ואחרי עיניכם , \"do not stray after your heart and your eyes,\" from the Shema as one of the 613 mitzvot. Ordinary interaction and looking at someone's face is fine; gazing is forbidden. \"Eyes and the heart… that causes all sins.\" המתכבד בקלון חבירו : gaining honour from a colleague's disgrace. \"He's a great guy, very learned. I'm more learned.\" He is not present, you did not technically speak badly of him, but elevating yourself by comparison is still a sin. The Rabbi's example: a job interview. החושד בכשרים : suspecting innocent people. Suspicion feels like \"just a thought,\" but it is wrong in itself. And a person who thinks everyone is doing wrong ends up doing wrong himself: \"How many times do people tell me, 'everyone does it'?\" This is one reason for the mitzvah to judge others favourably. Part 6: Category 5 – Hard to Break the Habit (Halacha 5) חמשה דברים העושה אותן קשה לחזור מהן, לפיכך צריך אדם להזהר מהן שמא ידבק בהן Five things are hard to return from; therefore a person must beware of them lest he become attached to them. Here you know you are doing wrong , unlike Category 4, but the habit is hard to break. \"These are all bad character traits.\" Trait The Rabbi's explanation 1 רכילות ( rechilut ) In the Rambam's terminology: peddling information from person to person (\"Did you hear how much he made? Did you hear what she did?\"), not necessarily bad things 2 לשון הרע Speaking derogatorily about people 3 בעל חמה A person given to temper 4 בעל מחשבה רעה Always thinking negatively; extreme pessimism 5 המתחבר לרשע Close friendship with the wicked. \"I'm in that gang, that chevrah .\" Shlomo HaMelech: ורעה כסילים ירוע , a friend of fools becomes foolish The Rambam notes he has already explained in Hilchot Deot how to keep away from these traits. Part 7: The Conclusion – Nothing Stands in the Way (Halacha 6) כל אלו הדברים וכיוצא בהן אף על פי שמעכבין את התשובה אין מונעין אותה, אלא אם עשה אדם תשובה מהן, הרי זה בעל תשובה ויש לו חלק לעולם הבא All these and similar things, although they hold back teshuvah, do not prevent it. If a person repents of them, he is a ba'al teshuvah and has a share in the World to Come. Summary Category Severity The items 1. Almost impossible Hashem withholds the ability Causing the public to sin (or preventing a mitzvah); leading an individual astray ( mesit/madiach ); not objecting to one's child; \"I'll sin and repent\" / \"Yom Kippur will atone\" 2. The doors are locked Cut off from guidance Separating from the community; disparaging the Sages; scoffing at mitzvot; disgracing one's teachers (and, per the Rif, parents); hating rebuke 3. Not complete teshuvah Can't find whom you wronged Cursing a group; sharing with a thief; not announcing a lost object; taking from the poor, orphans and widows; a judge taking a bribe 4. Presumed not to repent Don't realise it's a sin \"Dust of theft\" at a meagre meal; using a poor man's collateral; gazing; honour at another's expense; suspecting the innocent 5. Hard to leave Habits Rechilut; lashon hara; temper; negative thinking; bad company Halacha 6 None of these finally prevents teshuvah Themes of the evening: 1. The 24 come from the Rif; the Rambam supplies the reasons and the order. 2. The worst impediments are about influence : your actions keep working on others after you regret them. 3. Rebuke only works from friendship and love , and sometimes the mitzvah is to stay silent. 4. Many \"small\" sins are dangerous precisely because we don't notice them. 5. Teshuvah is always possible. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 5 , on free will , \"a very deep and philosophical chapter,\" since an essential element of teshuvah is that we are free to choose. The class will look at the Rambam's approach and others', with the focus on the question: do we really have free will?","bLg5bvRSC5c":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 3, Halachot 5–14 (end of the chapter) Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 8 October 2024 (Aseret Yemei Teshuvah 5785) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Chapter Theme 1 What teshuvah is, in the Rambam's understanding 2 Viduy: getting confession right, \"very relevant for Yom Kippur\" 3 (last week, just before Rosh Hashanah) Identity : Rosh Hashanah judges our value system , not individual actions Chapter 3 has two sections: Halachot 1–5 , the \"weighing,\" and Halachot 6–14 , those with no share in the World to Come. Part 1: The First and Second Sins Aren't Counted (Halacha 5) בשעה ששוקלין עוונות אדם עם זכויותיו, אין מחשבין עליו עוון שחטא בו תחילה ולא שני, אלא משלישי ואילך. אם נמצאו עוונותיו משלישי ואילך מרובין על זכויותיו, הרי אותן שתי עוונות מצטרפין ודנין אותו על הכל. ואם נמצאו זכויותיו כנגד עוונותיו אשר מעוון שלישי ואילך, מעבירין כל עוונותיו ראשון ראשון, שהשלישי נחשב ראשון, שכבר נמחלו השניים, וכן הרביעי הרי הוא ראשון, שכבר נמחל השלישי, וכן עד סופן When a person's sins are weighed against his merits, the first and second time he committed a sin are not counted , only from the third onward. If, from the third onward, his sins outweigh his merits, the first two join in and he is judged on everything. But if his merits balance his sins from the third onward, all his sins are passed over one by one : the third counts as the first, since the first two were already forgiven; the fourth is the first, since the third was forgiven; and so on to the end. \"Ma'avir rishon rishon\" We say it again and again in Selichot: אל מלך יושב על כסא רחמים… מוחל עוונות עמו, מעביר ראשון ראשון . \"I wonder how many people actually know what it means.\" He passes them over, first, first. Now you do. The maths Your overall standing 1st and 2nd time 3rd time onward Merits ≥ sins (tzaddik or beinoni) Not counted Each repeat \"becomes the first\" and is wiped: מעביר ראשון ראשון Sins > merits (rasha) Not counted, until… …from the 3rd, all of them , including the first two, are weighed What's going on? \"This is from the Gemara.\" And it is good news for perfectionists who give up after one slip: humans are fallible. Sins come from fragmentation. If you live an integrated life, aligned with your core, a sin is a break in that: \"I was angry, I was tempted, I was weak.\" If you're good on the whole, each sin, even repeated, is a fragment , not who you are. But if your sins outweigh your merits, this is your identity , so from the third time it counts. Not a free pass Every individual sin still has consequences. \"Are we going to say to someone who broke Shabbat once, with witnesses and warning, 'it's the first time, you're exempt'? No, of course not.\" Yom Kippur deals with individual sins. The weighing in this chapter is about identity , and there, if your values are aligned with Hashem's will, repeated lapses don't define you. Question: isn't \"generally a good person\" subjective? Hashem decides. And \"good\" doesn't mean having a nice heart; it means your value system is aligned with the will of Hashem : tzaddik or beinoni, as opposed to rasha. Community: three, not two במה דברים אמורים? ביחיד, שנאמר: הן כל אלה יפעל אל פעמים שלוש עם גבר. אבל הציבור תולין להם עוון ראשון שני ושלישי, שנאמר: על שלושה פשעי ישראל ועל ארבעה לא אשיבנו This is for an individual (\"twice, three times with a man,\" Iyov). For a community , the first, second and third are held over (\"for three transgressions of Israel, and for four I will not turn it back,\" Amos, the haftarah of Vayeshev), and counting begins from the fourth. The beinoni and tefillin בינונים, אם היה בכלל מחצה עוונות שלהן שלא הניח תפילין מעולם, דנין אותו כפי חטאו Normally Hashem tips the beinoni toward kindness ( mateh kelapei chesed ) and the sins are passed over. Exception: if among his sins is that he never once put on tefillin , he is judged for everything. The Gemara: קרקפתא דלא מנח תפילין , \"a head that never wore tefillin.\" \"To Chabad's credit, putting tefillin on people randomly is very valuable. Even if a person doesn't wear tefillin his entire life, if he's worn it once , he leaves this category.\" Even the wicked have a share וכן כל הרשעים שעוונותיהן מרובין, דנין אותן כפי חטאיהם, ויש להם חלק לעולם הבא, שכל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא… שנאמר: ועמך כולם צדיקים, לעולם יירשו ארץ, ארץ זו משל, כלומר ארץ החיים, והוא העולם הבא. וכן חסידי אומות העולם יש להם חלק לעולם הבא Every Jew has a share, smaller and different for the wicked, but a share. \"The land\" is a metaphor for the land of the living , Olam HaBa. And the righteous of the nations have a share too. Part 2: Why the Chapter Turns to \"No Share in Olam HaBa\" (Halacha 6) Many commentators are troubled: Halachot 1–5 are all about the weighing; Halacha 6 suddenly lists those with no share in Olam HaBa. The simple link: having said even the wicked have a share, he lists the exceptions. The deeper link: the whole chapter is about identity , and identity is fully realised in Olam HaBa , where everything you built becomes your relationship with Hashem. Some identities are \"so rotten to the core\" that there is nothing left. ואלו הן שאין להן חלק לעולם הבא, אלא נכרתים ואובדין ונדונין על גודל רשען וחטאתם לעולם ולעולמי עולמים These have no share in the World to Come; they are cut off , they perish , and they are judged for the magnitude of their wickedness forever and ever. The rotten apple Purification comes through suffering in this world, or Gehinnom, \"12 months maximum.\" Think of an apple with bruises : cut out each bad spot and there's still a core. The more bruises, the more cutting. But an apple rotten to the core ? Cutting would remove everything, so you throw the whole apple away. That is karet : your identity ceases to exist. Then why \"judged forever\"? Someone asked, back when the class learned Derech Hashem : \"I live a hundred years of debauchery, die, and cease to exist. How would I know I'm missing anything? I got a hundred years of enjoyment!\" The Rambam , the Ramchal and Rav Kafich all say: though the identity ceases, an element remains that perceives what is missing, eternally. Otherwise you'd have a system where you could do as you please with no worry. \"That's scary.\" Part 3: The 24 with No Share (Halachot 6–13) המינים, והאפיקורוסין, והכופרים בתורה, והכופרים בתחיית המתים ובביאת הגואל, המורדים, ומחטיאי הרבים, והפורשים מדרכי ציבור, והעושה עבירות ביד רמה בפרהסיא כיהויקים, והמוסרים, ומטילי אימה על הציבור שלא לשם שמים, ושופכי דמים, ובעלי לשון הרע, והמושך ערלתו Some headings contain several sub-types, making 24 in all. Five minim חמשה הן הנקראים מינים # Belief Principle of faith denied 1 There is no God , no one runs the world God's existence 2 There is one Ruler, but with a body or form Incorporeality 3 There are two or more gods Unity 4 God was not first and Former of all Creation 5 Serving an intermediary (a star etc.) between oneself and God Worship of God alone These are the first five of the 13 Principles . האומר : \" one who says. \" Rav Kafich : says means declares , a motto, like הוא היה אומר in Pirkei Avot, \"he used to say.\" Someone who struggles with doubts isn't included; \"that happens, and that person needs to work on it.\" Sources. Each has a Gemara source: אמר נבל בליבו אין אלהים (\"the fool said in his heart, there is no God\"); Adam was created alone שלא יאמרו שתי רשויות , \"so the minim wouldn't say there are two powers.\" Except corporeality, for which there is no Gemara source. The Monday class spent chapters of Emunot VeDeot Essay 2 proving that anthropomorphic verses are metaphors. But the Ra'avad objects: why call this person a min? Many greater and better than him read the verses literally. This is the Rambam's own innovation. His logic: a God with a body isn't God. \"A God who sits on a throne in heaven with a long white beard… you may say you believe in God, but you don't.\" You can't have a relationship with such a thing. \"It's not your fault?\" Rav Chaim of Brisk : נעבעך אן אפיקורס איז אויך אן אפיקורס , \"a poor unfortunate heretic is still a heretic.\" Through no fault of his own, his understanding is too warped for a relationship with Hashem. #4 \"first and Former of all\" : by adding וצור לכל , the Rambam rejects not only Aristotle (an eternal world) but Plato : a pre-existing hylē , unformed matter, from which God made the world. Some Rishonim considered that acceptable; the Rambam says Hashem created everything , matter included. Why min ? Rav Moshe Shapira : min means species , category. With such beliefs you are \"a different category of human\"; you have lost your identity as Israel. \"When we come to Yom Kippur, it's not just about our actions. It's also about our outlook . In fact it's probably more about our outlook than our actions.\" Three apikorsim # Belief 6 There is no prophecy at all: no knowledge reaches people from God 7 Denying the unique prophecy of Moshe 8 God doesn't know what people do Moshe's uniqueness was a point the Rambam \"really, really scored home,\" more than Rav Saadiah did, because in his time Christianity and Islam each claimed a newer revelation. אפיקורוס has two meanings: Here: Epicurus , the Greek philosopher who denied any connection between God and humans. (The Rambam argues with him in the Moreh .) Elsewhere, from הפקר : someone who has thrown off every rein. That's how someone who denigrates the Sages is called an apikores . Three koferim baTorah # Denial 9 That the Torah is from God, even one verse, even one word : \"Moshe made it up himself\" 10 Its interpretation , the Oral Torah, and its transmitters (like Tzadok and Baitos ) 11 Saying God replaced the Torah with another, even if He gave it (as the Christians and Muslims claim) Standing for the Ten Commandments: the Rambam ruled against it in a responsum. It implies those words matter more than the rest, as if only they were from God. Every word is from God. The two parts of the Oral Torah: (1) what was passed down from Moshe; (2) what was left to the Sages to interpret . Once the Sanhedrin decided by majority, it binds. Hence the Mishnah's story of a sage who rejected the majority and whose coffin was stoned (the Rabbi recalled it as R. Eliezer ben Hyrcanus; in the Mishnah, Eduyot, it is Elazar ben Chanoch). Two meshumadim # Type 12 For one sin : habitually doing one particular sin, e.g. always wearing sha'atnez or always cutting the pe'ot , to rebel and anger God 13 For the whole Torah : converting to another religion, e.g. in times of persecution: \"why be with the persecuted Jews?\" Why sha'atnez and pe'ot? \"How much benefit are you getting?\" The only reason is \"I don't want to do the will of God.\" Today: someone who grew up never knowing these were forbidden \"is a whole different discussion.\" Mark: what about conversos forced under the Inquisition ? \"You won't be considered a meshumad.\" Converting to Islam under duress? \"Of course it's the wrong thing to do,\" but many say you needn't give your life for it, since it isn't actual idolatry. Robert: someone who believes in no God at all, \"we live and then we're eaten by worms\"? Not a meshumad (who changes faith), but a min . The rest # Category Explanation 14 מחטיאי הרבים , causing the public to sin In big things ( Yeravam's golden calves; Tzadok and Baitos ) or small , even a positive mitzvah; forcing people ( Menasheh ); leading them astray ( Yeshu ). Rav Kafich found no Gemara source for \"small things\" and suggests the Rambam is codifying Rav Saadiah Gaon ( Emunot VeDeot , Essay 5) 15 הפורש מדרכי ציבור , separating from the community \"Even without committing sins\": not sharing their troubles, not fasting their fasts, \"living like one of the nations.\" Difficulty: a source, and a contradiction with what the Rambam writes elsewhere (there, leaving and sinning). Read as: giving up all connection to Jewish identity 16 העושה עבירות ביד רמה בפרהסיא כיהויקים , sinning brazenly and in public King Yehoyakim tattooed himself, wore sha'atnez, killed men and violated their wives, openly 17–18 המוסרים , two kinds of informers Handing a Jew's person to non-Jews to be killed or beaten, or his money to be taken 19 מטילי אימה על הציבור שלא לשם שמים A public figure (warden, official) who rules by fear for his own honour, not for Heaven 20 שופכי דמים Murderers 21 בעלי לשון הרע Habitual slanderers 22 המושך ערלתו Surgically reversing one's circumcision 23–24 הכופרים בתחיית המתים ובביאת הגואל Denying resurrection, or the coming of the Redeemer (Mashiach) Robert: \"cultural Jews\" Today many people don't keep Shabbat but are very involved in the community. \"Of course you won't fall under this category.\" If you share in the community's troubles and joys, you are not poresh . A person who cares for his fellow Jews but believes \"it's all Darwin\" isn't poresh or an apostate either, \"just a regular min.\" Mesirah today \"This is why people are very reluctant to ever tell the police about another Jew.\" Many responsa explain that the classic mesirah doesn't apply in a democratic society under the rule of law, where Jews and non-Jews are, in theory, judged equally. The Sages' severity was about handing a Jew to a system where he'd be treated differently, to be killed, beaten or robbed. Part 4: Lesser Sins Done Habitually (Halacha 14) ויש עבירות קלות מאלו, ואף על פי כן אמרו חכמים שהרגיל בהן אין לו חלק לעולם הבא… המכנה שם לחבירו, והקורא לחבירו בכינויו, והמלבין פני חבירו ברבים, והמתכבד בקלון חבירו, והמבזה תלמידי חכמים, והמבזה רבותיו, והמבזה את המועדות, והמחלל את הקדשים The Gemara says, e.g., המלבין פני חבירו ברבים אין לו חלק לעולם הבא : one who shames another publicly has no share. Why isn't it among the 24? The Rambam's own reading: the 24 are identities , philosophies, or blatant and constant public conduct. These are lesser sins; once is a mistake (\"you do teshuvah, fine\"). It's the habitual offender who loses his share. Sin Note Giving someone a nickname , or calling him by one Without his permission; even if you didn't invent it Shaming someone in public Gaining honour from another's disgrace People with low self-esteem can feel better seeing others brought down. \"It happens here and there.\" Regularly is the problem Disparaging Torah scholars , or one's teachers Disgracing the festivals Rabbeinu Menachem HaMeiri : not dressing or eating differently; letting them pass unmarked Profaning kodashim Part 5: Nothing Stands Before Teshuvah במה דברים אמורים שכל אחד מאלו אין לו חלק לעולם הבא? כשמת בלא תשובה. אבל אם שב מרשעו ומת והוא בעל תשובה, הרי זה מבני העולם הבא, שאין לך דבר שעומד בפני התשובה. אפילו כפר בעיקר כל ימיו ובאחרונה שב, יש לו חלק לעולם הבא, שנאמר: שלום שלום לרחוק ולקרוב… כל הרשעים והמומרים וכיוצא בהן שחזרו בתשובה, בין בגלוי בין במטמוניות, מקבלין אותן All this applies only if he died without teshuvah . If he repented, he has a share, for nothing stands before teshuvah . Even if he denied the fundamentals all his life and repented at the end: \"Peace, peace to the far and to the near.\" All who return, openly or in secret , are accepted. Even someone who has come back privately but not yet publicly: \"we take you on and we encourage you.\" Aldous Huxley , an atheist writer of the 20th century, wrote late in life that he and others had wanted there to be no Creator, because a Creator would bring moral consequences, and he later changed his mind. \"If at the end of his life he recognised Hashem, it's a different story. A person can always do teshuvah.\" Summary The first and second occurrences of a sin are never weighed. If your merits hold, every repeat \"becomes the first\" and is passed over: מעביר ראשון ראשון . Sins are fragmentation, not identity, unless they are your identity. Individual sins still carry consequences; this weighing is about who you are . A community gets three, not two. Tefillin , even once, matters enormously. Every Jew has a share in Olam HaBa, and so do the righteous of the nations . The 24 without a share are identities rotten to the core: 5 minim, 3 apikorsim, 3 koferim baTorah, 2 meshumadim , and the rest. Karet is the apple thrown away, though something remains that knows its loss. Corporeality as heresy is the Rambam's own innovation: a God with a body isn't God. Lesser sins done habitually (nicknames, public shaming, disgracing the festivals…) also forfeit Olam HaBa. Nothing stands before teshuvah. \"These are the big things we need to be thinking about before we do teshuvah, not just 'I did this, I did that.'\" Coming Up \"I was over-ambitious, I have to admit,\" hoping to finish all ten chapters by Yom Kippur. \"We've got another 7 chapters to do. The rest are not as hard,\" but there's a lot to talk about. Next: Chapter 4 , the 24 impediments to teshuvah.","zPa-i4gwyO8":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 3, Halachot 1–4 Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 1 October 2024 (Erev Rosh Hashanah 5785) These notes are based on an automatic (Whisper) transcript of the recording. Introduction \"Of course, our minds are occupied with what's happening in our land.\" The Rabbi opened with תמיד עיני ה' אלהיך בה, מרשית השנה ועד אחרית שנה : \"the eyes of Hashem your God are always upon it, from the beginning of the year to the end of the year.\" The Gemara applies this verse to Rosh Hashanah, and on Erev Rosh Hashanah we pray it will be fulfilled for the Land of Israel. Recap: Chapters 1–2: teshuvah is not itself a mitzvah; when one returns, viduy is the mitzvah. The definition of teshuvah and of teshuvah gemurah ; the precise form of viduy; tzedakah as the \"horizontal\" balance to a \"vertical\" teshuvah. Chapter 3 has 15 halachot . \"This is a very difficult chapter, well, the first part of it.\" The plan: read the first three halachot together, raise all the questions, and put them together into one approach , \"and really prepare ourselves for tomorrow night when Rosh Hashanah commences.\" Part 1: The Text (Halachot 1–3) Halacha 1: Weighing merits and sins כל אחד ואחד מבני האדם יש לו זכויות ועוונות. מי שזכויותיו יתרות על עוונותיו, צדיק; ומי שעוונותיו יתרות על זכויותיו, רשע; מחצה למחצה, בינוני. וכן המדינה… וכן כל העולם כולו Every person has merits and sins. If merits exceed sins, he is a tzaddik ; if sins exceed merits, a rasha ; half and half, a beinoni . So too a country… and so too the whole world. First question: how many people are exactly 50–50? Halacha 2: The consequences, and a scale of magnitude אדם שעוונותיו מרובין על זכויותיו, מיד הוא מת ברשעו… וכן מדינה שעוונותיה מרובין, מיד היא אובדת: זעקת סדום ועמורה כי רבה… וכן כל העולם כולו… מיד הן נשחתין: וירא ה' כי רבה רעת האדם A person whose sins outweigh his merits dies immediately in his wickedness… a country whose sins outweigh its merits is immediately destroyed (Sedom)… the whole world, immediately destroyed (the Flood). ושיקול זה אינו לפי מניין הזכויות והעוונות אלא לפי גודלם. יש זכות שהיא כנגד כמה עוונות… ויש עוון שהוא כנגד כמה זכויות… ואין שוקלין אלא בדעתו של אל דעות The weighing is not by number but by magnitude . One merit can outweigh many sins… one sin can outweigh many merits… and only the God of knowledge can weigh them. Aviyah son of Yeravam : Yeravam was a terrible king who made the people sin and blocked the roads to the Temple; his family took part. But when appointed over a section of road, Aviyah let the pilgrims through , and was spared: יען נמצא בו דבר טוב . וחוטא אחד יאבד טובה הרבה : one sin can destroy much good. Philippe: who could remember everything you did all year? \"Only Hashem.\" No human, \"not even the greatest computer,\" could weigh the act together with a person's upbringing, context and motive. \"This should shake you\" \"I'm perplexed nobody's bothered!\" The Rambam says whoever has more sins than merits dies immediately ; cities full of debauchery are destroyed . That doesn't seem to be reality. Olivia: \"And there are so many people who are good and die early.\" \"Precisely.\" Halacha 3: Regretting mitzvot, and the annual judgement כל מי שנתחרט על המצוות שעשה ותהה על הזכויות, ואמר בליבו: ומה הועלתי בעשייתן? הלוואי לא עשיתי אותן! הרי זה איבד את כולן ולא הזכירו לו זכות בעולם Whoever regrets the mitzvot he did and says \"what did I gain? I wish I hadn't done them!\" has lost them all . וכשם ששוקלין זכויות אדם ועוונותיו בשעת מיתתו, כך בכל שנה ושנה שוקלין… ביום טוב של ראש השנה. מי שנמצא צדיק נחתם לחיים, ומי שנמצא רשע נחתם למיתה, והבינוני תולין אותו עד יום הכפורים: אם עשה תשובה נחתם לחיים, ואם לאו נחתם למיתה Just as merits and sins are weighed at death, so every Rosh Hashanah . The tzaddik is sealed for life, the rasha for death, and the beinoni is held until Yom Kippur . Part 2: The Questions \"So much ink has been spilt on these three halachot.\" Structure. Halacha 1 is the weighing, 2 the consequences, the end of 3 the annual judgement. What is the regret clause doing in the middle? Reality. The Ra'avad : the Rambam takes the Gemara's \"sealed for death\" as immediate death. That isn't so; we see wicked people who don't die. The Ra'avad reads it as dying earlier than one's allotted years, not this year. (The Rabbi: the Rambam \"obviously foresaw the question,\" as with free will and foreknowledge, where the Ra'avad said he never answered and \"he did, in a much deeper way.\" And Olivia's question remains even on the Ra'avad's reading.) Regretting a mitzvah. Suppose someone hears shofar at 11am on Rosh Hashanah, comes home at 1pm to find he missed an important delivery, and says, \"I should never have gone, what a waste.\" If he has lost the mitzvah, then the day-long obligation should return and he should have to hear shofar again . Same with Shema at night. No one has ever ruled that. Then what's special about teshuvah? The Ramchal ( Mesillat Yesharim ) calls teshuvah חסד גמור , a pure kindness. But if regret cancels both sins and mitzvot, it's just a symmetrical rule about regret: nothing special. Weighing at all? אשר לא ישא פנים ולא יקח שוחד (Ekev): Hashem takes no bribe. The Midrash, cited by Rashi: not even a bribe of mitzvot. Every sin must be accounted for individually, however many mitzvot you have; and every mitzvah rewarded however many sins. \"Who cares how much it weighs? It just makes no sense.\" Part 3: The Answer: Identity, Not Actions \"All of this ties back to the fundamental concept we've touched on over the last few weeks… Teshuvah, Rosh Hashanah, is about a person's identity. \" What regret takes away When you regret a mitzvah, the act stays done . You fulfilled the obligation and will be rewarded; no need to hear shofar again. What you lose is the value of that mitzvah in your character: When you do a mitzvah and rejoice in it, aside from the act, your value system improves : your identity becomes more positive. Regret it, and that part of your identity is neutralised. Why teshuvah is a pure kindness Regretting a mitzvah Regretting a sin (teshuvah) Value in your identity Lost Lost (you no longer value the sin) The act itself Stays , and is rewarded Erased : kaparah , wiped clean That's the chesed gamur : an action you did can somehow be undone. What's on the scale Individual actions are never balanced out. \"Even if you have a billion mitzvot and one aveirah, that aveirah has to have its consequences. There's no bribe of mitzvot.\" That is Yom Kippur's business: rectifying individual actions. Rosh Hashanah weighs something else: who you are. \"Rosh Hashanah is not about what we do. It's about who we are : the weight we give to these things.\" Usually identity is defined by what you do: \"what you eat is who you are; what you do is who you are.\" But memory and value shape identity too. You may have done many mitzvot this year and look back thinking \"it wasn't worth getting up early for Selichot all Elul, it wiped me out.\" The mitzvot stand; but they don't count on the scale of identity . Mary: how do we know Hashem is calculating everything? \"Is He writing down every single thing all year? It's a very long year.\" \"Especially a leap year, that's true!\" The Torah and the Sages tell us, and \"My ways are not your ways\" : as a child the Rabbi wondered how Hashem could know what was happening in Australia and England at once. A human question about a different order of being. Part 4: \"Sealed for Life\": Living Olam HaBa Now The Rishonim struggled with \"the wicked don't die.\" - The Kesef Mishneh (Rabbi Yosef Karo): you can't judge; the person you think is wicked isn't. \"Very difficult to accept,\" since some people are wicked by any standard. - Tosafot : \"sealed for life\" refers to the life of the World to Come , and the Lechem Mishneh reads the Rambam the same way. But then why judge it every year? Your Olam HaBa changes year to year; just assess it at death. The approach the Rabbi follows, from Rav Moshe Shapira: חיי העולם הבא here means the life of the World to Come in this world : living with Hashem's closeness this coming year. The light of Hashem's countenance The Ramchal (Derech Hashem): all our states in life depend on whether Hashem shines His countenance on us or hides it. The more it shines, the more a person truly enjoys life; to come close and bask in that light is the whole purpose. The Rabbi's image: the pillar of light at the start of Mr Bean . The light is always there; will you step into it? The Nefesh HaChaim (Rabbi Chaim of Volozhin): each mitzvah envelops a person in a kind of Divine ambience, which is why one mitzvah leads to another . Shabbat is 1/60 of Olam HaBa because it is when we reconnect with Hashem. Analogy: spend time with the person you most admire, \"perhaps when the Queen was alive,\" lunch, dinner, breakfast. As humans we'd eventually want to move on; but imagine that first feeling staying, even increasing. How much of that will you have this year? That is what's decided. The three categories, re-read On the scale of identity \"Sealed for…\" Tzaddik What you value most about your year is the good: \"I may have done terrible things, but that shiur, that tefillah, the person I helped, that's the highlight.\" One merit can outweigh many sins. You will still face consequences for individual sins Life : you will feel Hashem's presence in your day-to-day life Rasha Even with many mitzvot, what you love looking back on is \"that lunchtime hour gossiping with my colleagues\" Death : you'll live, and even do mitzvot, but without the illumination. \"How many religious people do mitzvot with no life in them, and how many seemingly irreligious people do a few mitzvot with all their heart?\" Beinoni Not a statistical 50–50 (maybe one person in 8 billion), but a conflicted value system : happy with the mitzvot, but enjoying the aveirot too. \"The vast majority of us\" Held until Yom Kippur : sort out your values in the Ten Days \"That's just, you read that, you laugh out loud. It's not true.\" The Rambam would never write that the wicked literally drop dead. \"You die\" means you're not living with Hashem. \"In your value system, you're dead.\" There are two judgements : we also say מי יעשיר ומי יעני , who will be rich and who poor. The physical judgement is real. But \"life\" in these halachot is the spiritual one. \"A sweet year\" Why sweet ? The Gemara says if Gan Eden is in Eretz Yisrael, its entrance is Beit She'an , and Rashi notes its fruit is the sweetest in the land. \"Sweet\" is the language of Gan Eden: a year in which you'll experience the light . \"If you've had those feelings… you won't trade any pleasure in this world for them.\" Kabbalah speaks of days of darkness and days of light; some years we have more. \"Life\" vs \"good life\": the Ramchal on the Amidah The Ramchal , in an essay on Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur, notes that in the Ten Days' Amidah: - The first two blessings add זכרנו לחיים and מי כמוך… זוכר יצוריו לחיים : just \"life.\" - The last two add וכתוב לחיים טובים and בספר חיים ברכה ושלום ופרנסה טובה : \" good life,\" a life of blessing and livelihood . Why? And the famous question: how could the Geonim add requests to the first three blessings, which are meant to be praise only? Answer: life is life. \"You don't say goodness is good.\" In the first blessings (Avot), whose purpose is to make us ask when will my deeds reach those of Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov? , \"life\" means real life : closeness to Hashem, needing no adjective. At the end, we pray for our physical life, which does need qualifying: good life, blessed, with livelihood. Part 5: Questions Adam: we're also meant to enjoy this world, even its physical pleasures, which are neither mitzvah nor aveirah. How does that fit the identity picture? Nothing is wrong with enjoying the world as part of a holistic life (the Shemonah Perakim ). But when \"your life's mission is to go to this next steak, this new kosher restaurant with steak on the grill\" (some of the new restaurant ads, which \"certain sects within Judaism are quite excited about\"), \" that's your value system \", and the Ramban calls such a person despicable within the Torah's permission. Adam suggested that nothing is truly neutral; how you engage with anything leads toward a tzaddik's or a rasha's mindset. The Rabbi: some book says no action is neutral (\"a very extreme view, but there's some truth to it\"), and the Rambam says something like it in the Shemonah Perakim, Chapter 3 . Engaging with the world healthily as part of a wider system is not a \"mitzvah,\" but it's right; or one can simply eat breakfast without thought. Someone was told: after Yom Kippur you may repeat a sin, just reduce it by 10% each year. \"That's incorrect.\" You can't enter the year planning the same sins, 10% less . \"A sin is a sin, full stop.\" If at year's end you find you've improved by 10%, that is an achievement to applaud. Part 6: The Shofar's Hint (Halacha 4) אף על פי שתקיעת שופר בראש השנה גזירת הכתוב, רמז יש בו, כלומר: עורו ישנים משנתכם, ונרדמים הקיצו מתרדמתכם, וחפשו במעשיכם וחזרו בתשובה… אלו השוכחים את האמת בהבלי הזמן, ושוגים כל שנתם בהבל וריק… הביטו לנפשותיכם והטיבו דרכיכם ומעלליכם Though shofar is a decree of Scripture, it hints: \"Wake up, sleepers, from your sleep; slumberers, rouse yourselves!\" Examine your deeds and return… you who forget the truth in the vanities of the time and waste your whole year… look to your souls. לפיכך צריך כל אדם שיראה עצמו כל השנה כולה כאילו חציו זכאי וחציו חייב, וכן כל העולם חציו זכאי וחציו חייב… עשה מצוה אחת, הרי הכריע את עצמו ואת כל העולם כולו לכף זכות Therefore every person should see himself all year as half meritorious and half guilty , and the whole world likewise. One mitzvah tips himself and the whole world to merit. In the identity reading: see your value system as poised at half and half. One or two actions can change who you are, and the world. That is why we increase mitzvot and Selichot in the Ten Days. The Rabbi noted the Rambam did Selichot only between Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur, not the whole of Elul as Sephardim do, nor from the week before Rosh Hashanah as Ashkenazim do. Summary Merits and sins are weighed by magnitude , not number, and only Hashem can do it. The problems: wicked people don't drop dead; regretting a mitzvah can't mean redoing it; teshuvah would be nothing special; and Hashem takes no \"bribe of mitzvot.\" The answer: Rosh Hashanah weighs identity , the value you place on what you did, not the acts themselves. Individual acts each have their own consequences. Regret of a mitzvah removes its value from your identity, but the act stands. Teshuvah for a sin removes both: a pure kindness. \"Sealed for life\" = living Olam HaBa in this world this year, in the light of Hashem's countenance (Rav Moshe Shapira, the Ramchal, the Nefesh HaChaim). Beinoni = a conflicted value system. \"Life\" in the Amidah needs no adjective; \"good life\" is the physical. The shofar's call : wake up, and see yourself and the world balanced on a knife-edge. \"Think, when Rosh Hashanah comes in less than 24 hours, about who we are, our value system . That's what the shofar awakens us to: those moments when we feel this is the truth , and I keep on falling, but that's not who I am .\" Shanah tovah : \"a sweet year, with Hashem's presence in our lives… may we see Israel saved from its enemies, and the revelation of Hashem's presence over all humanity.\" Coming Up Next Tuesday, 8pm , during the Ten Days: the rest of Chapter 3, in preparation for Yom Kippur.","uVUaRwZeKxc":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 2 (complete) Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 17 September 2024 (Elul 5784) These notes are based on an automatic (Whisper) transcript of the recording. Recap of Chapter 1 For the Rambam, teshuvah itself is not one of the 613 (at least during the year): you can't be commanded to return to what you were already commanded to do. When you choose to return, viduy is the mitzvah. Teshuvah = leaving the sin in past, present and future (regret, stopping, resolve). It is internal . Viduy is relational , spoken toward Hashem or another person, and must be specific : subject, verb, object. The scapegoat, and what Yom Kippur atones for. \"It's very hard, and we're going to see tonight how hard teshuvah is. But it's possible. If it weren't, it wouldn't be expected of us.\" And what is often called teshuvah \"isn't necessarily what teshuvah is.\" Part 1: Why Start with Complete Teshuvah? (Halacha 1) Normally halacha gives the basic requirement first, then the extra. On Seder night you must recline for the matzah and wine; reclining through the whole meal is praiseworthy . Here the Rambam reverses it: Halacha 1 is complete teshuvah, Halacha 2 is basic teshuvah. איזו היא תשובה גמורה? זה שבא לידו דבר שעבר בו, ואפשר בידו לעשותו, ופירש ולא עשה מפני התשובה, לא מיראה ולא מכשלון כח. כיצד? הרי שבא על אשה בעבירה, ולאחר זמן נתייחד עמה, והוא עומד באהבתו בה ובכח גופו, ובמדינה שעבר בה, ופירש ולא עבר: זהו בעל תשובה גמורה What is complete teshuvah? When the same opportunity comes, he is able to do it, and he refrains because of teshuvah, not from fear and not from weakness. For example: he sinned with a woman, and later is alone with her, still attracted to her, with the same physical strength, in the same place where he sinned, and he refrains. That is a ba'al teshuvah gemurah . \"In the same place\" : the Rambam often stresses environment . If you now live somewhere you'd be more likely to be caught, refraining isn't complete teshuvah. זכור את בוראיך בימי בחורותיך , \"remember your Creator in the days of your youth.\" \"How many times do we hear people say near the end of their lives, 'it wasn't worth it, what I did when I was younger'? Great, you've worked it out at 70. What you're meant to do is work it out at 20, in the prime of a person's urges and desires.\" Repenting in old age, when you no longer can , \"though not the finest teshuvah, still helps; he is a ba'al teshuvah.\" Even on the last day of life. Don't recreate the test \"This is obvious, but it's important to say it.\" The Rambam is not suggesting you seclude yourself with the same woman to prove yourself. We are told never to walk into a test ( nisayon ). His point: your reality has changed , so that if you were put back in exactly the same situation, Hashem could testify you wouldn't sin. \"You're just not that person.\" A new identity, not one resolution \"People say before Rosh Hashanah: take on one thing. That's lovely, and there are sources for it. But that's very far from what we're talking about. We're talking about a whole change of identity. \" Why Rosh Hashanah comes before Yom Kippur Why judge before atoning? Because Rosh Hashanah is for creating the vision , who I want to be . That's why we don't confess details on Rosh Hashanah (\"you get lost in the specifics\"), and why the Zohar discourages praying for personal things. The details come in the Ten Days and on Yom Kippur. Sealed, and sealed again Kabbalistic sources speak of further \"seals\": Yom Kippur is the outer seal, Hoshana Rabbah the inner; the Zohar says even on Simchat Torah it can change; Chassidim extended it to Zot Chanukah . The Rabbi once found in Nitei Gavriel a Chassidic rebbe saying the real seal is on Purim (from the king's signet in the Megillah). \"This makes a mockery.\" His understanding , from the Gemara's debate whether the world was created in Tishrei or Nisan (it was conceived in one and born in the other): on Rosh Hashanah we conceive an identity ; we may tweak it on Yom Kippur and after; by Nisan we should see its fruit. That's why there is teshuvah on Erev Rosh Chodesh and at the end of every week: \"The only way I have found to be effective for teshuvah: on Rosh Hashanah set the goals, the ambitions, the identity of who you want to be. Then set aside times through the year to look back: am I in line? Are there fruits from that identity?\" Write it down. Elazar ben Durdaya: why did he die? The Gemara (Avodah Zarah 17a): Elazar ben Durdaya spent his life with every prostitute in the world. Once, about to sin, he realised it was wrong. He begged the mountains, the sun and the moon to plead for him, then realised it depends on me alone , put his head between his knees, wept until he died , and was declared a ba'al teshuvah with a share in Olam HaBa: יש קונה עולמו בשעה אחת . Why would teshuvah kill someone? The Rabbi, drawing on Rabbi Matis Weinberg : - Every decision we make is reversible . Death is the one thing that isn't, which is why we fear it most. - Complete teshuvah is letting that option die . The possibility of returning to the sin becomes as unthinkable as speaking to someone you're sitting shiva for. - Elazar ben Durdaya's whole identity was that life. When he let it die completely, he died with it . \"The reason New Year's resolutions don't last is because we're not willing to let our previous mindset die. \" So why start with complete teshuvah? Because teshuvah isn't a mitzvah with levels; it's a reality , a change of identity. The Rambam first shows you what that reality is (Halacha 1), then the lower version (Halacha 2). Once that reality exists, there is a mitzvah of viduy. Part 2: Basic Teshuvah, Two Ways (Halacha 2) ומה היא התשובה? הוא שיעזוב החוטא חטאו, ויסירו ממחשבתו, ויגמור בליבו שלא יעשהו עוד… וכן יתנחם על שעבר… ויעיד עליו יודע תעלומות שלא ישוב לזה החטא לעולם… וצריך להתוודות בשפתיו ולומר עניינות אלו שגמר בליבו Teshuvah: the sinner leaves his sin, removes it from his thoughts, resolves never to repeat it… regrets the past… until He who knows all secrets can testify that he will never return to this sin… and he must confess with his lips what he has resolved in his heart. Rav Soloveitchik: regret first, or resolve first? In Chapter 1 the order is regret, then resolve ( והרי ניחמתי… ולעולם איני חוזר ). In Chapter 2 it is leaving, then resolve, then regret . \"In typical Rav Soloveitchik fashion,\" there's no contradiction: two kinds of teshuvah. Emotional teshuvah (Ch. 1 order) Intellectual teshuvah (Ch. 2 order) Starts with Regret: feeling revolted by what you did A rational decision that this lifestyle is harmful Then \"I never want to feel like this again,\" so resolve Willpower overrides the emotion; regret follows once you're living differently Example: drinking You wake up sick and disgusted, empty the fridge, and resolve never again You love it and never get hangovers, but hear in a class what it does to your liver, decide to stop, and only later look back and say \"it wasn't worth it\" This also answers last week's question: \"what if you can't feel regret yet?\" Start with the intellect. Both are fine ; the goal is the same, and it's a process. Why must viduy be spoken ? If I've done the whole process in my heart, why say it? - A participant: speaking actualises it. \"Until you have to say 'I did X,' you don't feel it on a real level.\" - Mark: like the Amidah , which must at least be mouthed audibly; speaking creates the connection. - The Rabbi: both, and also teshuvah with Hashem mirrors teshuvah with people . Between people, saying sorry in your mind doesn't work. Without viduy there is no kaparah . A tzaddik with sins: Minchat Chinuch The Gemara (Kiddushin): a man betroths a woman \"on condition that I am a tzaddik .\" Even if he is known as a terrible person, the betrothal may be valid and she may need a get : perhaps he thought teshuvah in his heart. The Minchat Chinuch asks: doesn't teshuvah require spoken viduy? Answer: teshuvah is identity , viduy brings kaparah . \"It's possible, maybe surprising to some, for a person to be a tzaddik full of sins without having kaparah .\" He has become a new person; the slate is not yet wiped. Halacha 3: Tovel veSheretz beYado One who confesses without resolving to leave is like immersing in a mikveh holding a sheretz . (There's a halachic discussion whether you are pure under the water and impure on emerging, or never pure at all.) Yom Kippur is the mikveh : walk out still holding the sin, and you're back where you were. Specific confession Being specific makes it real. \"I'm sorry\" is not the same as \"I'm sorry for embarrassing you in public.\" That's why we say the general חטאנו , then the detailed על חטא , and Rabbeinu Nissim's viduy goes into still more detail. Part 3: The Paths of Teshuvah (Halacha 4) מדרכי התשובה: להיות השב צועק תמיד לפני השם בבכי ובתחנונים, ועושה צדקה כפי כחו, ומתרחק הרבה מן הדבר שחטא בו, ומשנה שמו, כלומר אני אחר ואיני אותו האיש שעשה אותן המעשים, ומשנה מעשיו כולן לטובה ולדרך ישרה, וגולה ממקומו, שגלות מכפרת עון, מפני שגורמת לו להיכנע ולהיות עניו ושפל רוח Until now it has been \"a very intellectual process.\" Now the emotional side. All five come from the Gemara (Rosh Hashanah 16b). The Lechem Mishneh : each one alone can tear up a decree; all together is complete. Path The Rabbi's explanation Crying out to Hashem Teshuvah is emotionally hard; a relationship with Hashem gives hope and keeps you from drowning in guilt Tzedakah Teshuvah can become narcissistic , all \"me, my identity, my relationship with Hashem\": vertical. Tzedakah restores the horizontal . A participant: we define ourselves by our money and job; giving it away says that isn't who I am . \"Lovely, I really like that.\" Distancing from the sin Put fences in place Changing one's name אני אחר , \"I am someone else\": a new identity Changing one's deeds Of course Exile Leaving the environment that fed the old identity; it brings humility Part 4: Public or Private? (Halacha 5) ושבח גדול לשב שיתוודה ברבים ויודיע פשעיו להם… אבל בעבירות שבין אדם למקום, אינו צריך לפרסם עצמו, ועזות פנים היא לו אם גילם The Gemara reconciles לא יצליח מכסה פשעיו (\"he who covers his sins will not prosper\") with אשרי… כסוי חטאה (\"happy is he whose sin is covered\"): 1. Between people: publicise; between you and Hashem: keep private. (The Rambam's choice.) 2. Sins already publicly known: confess publicly; private sins: privately. The Ra'avad asks why the Rambam ignores the second answer. The Lechem Mishneh : read the Rambam's wording carefully and he includes it; publicising private sins against Hashem causes chillul Hashem (\"if he does it, so can I\"). The Rabbi's application: if you wronged someone in public, apologise in public. \"Very hard, I know. But that really is what's suggested.\" Part 5: The Ten Days and Yom Kippur (Halachot 6–8) אף על פי שהתשובה והצעקה יפה לעולם, בעשרה הימים שבין ראש השנה ויום הכפורים היא יפה ביותר ומתקבלת היא מיד… במה דברים אמורים? ביחיד. אבל צבור, כל זמן שעושים תשובה וצועקין בלב שלם, הם נענין Why should the time matter, if teshuvah is teshuvah? Psychologically, everyone is doing it together , so it is more heartfelt and genuine. A community has that power all year. יום הכפורים… הוא קץ מחילה וסליחה לישראל Note: mechilah and selichah , not kaparah . As Chapter 1 showed, Yom Kippur doesn't fully wipe the slate for the gravest sins. לפיכך חייבים הכל לעשות תשובה ולהתוודות ביום הכפורים The first time the Rambam calls teshuvah an obligation . No contradiction: Yom Kippur's mitzvah is to be atoned ( לפני ה' תטהרו ), which is only possible through teshuvah and viduy. So on Yom Kippur teshuvah becomes required. Viduy before the meal: \"in case he chokes\" ומצוות וידוי יום הכפורים שיתחיל מערב היום קודם שיאכל, שמא יחנק בסעודה \"One of the strangest halachot.\" Nobody says keep Shabbat from Friday lunchtime in case you're run over on the way home . Why pre-empt? Because teshuvah isn't a time-bound mitzvah like sukkah; it's a reality, a mindset , and it can happen any time. So \"just in case, do it now.\" That's also why we confess at every Yom Kippur prayer, in the middle of the Amidah: repeating it reinforces I'm a different person. The core of viduy is אבל אנחנו חטאנו . Confessing last year's sins again עבירות שהתוודה עליהן ביום הכפורים זה, חוזר ומתוודה עליהן ביום הכפורים אחר… שנאמר: וחטאתי נגדי תמיד \"Modern psychology wouldn't be happy with this.\" Not every day, but once a year , reflect on where you came from, as a precaution against slipping back. Part 6: Between People (Halachot 9–11) אין התשובה ולא יום הכפורים מכפרין אלא על עבירות שבין אדם למקום… אבל עבירות שבין אדם לחבירו… אינו נמחל לו לעולם עד שיתן לחבירו מה שהוא חייב לו וירצהו. אף על פי שהחזיר לו ממון… צריך לרצותו ולשאול ממנו שימחול לו. אפילו לא הקניט את חבירו אלא בדברים, צריך לפייסו Paying back the money isn't enough; you must repair the relationship , even for words alone ( ona'at devarim ). Three rows of three לא רצה חבירו למחול לו, מביא לו שורה של שלשה בני אדם מריעיו… לא נתרצה להן, מביא לו שנייה ושלישית. לא רצה, מניחו והולך לו, וזה שלא מחל הוא החוטא. ואם היה רבו, הולך ובא אפילו אלף פעמים Spot the difference with the Shulchan Aruch, the Rabbi asked: - A participant noticed the Shulchan Aruch omits that the one who refuses is the sinner , focusing on the one asking. \"True.\" - The halachic difference: the Rambam has you first ask alone , then bring three groups of three. The Shulchan Aruch goes straight to the groups. The Bi'ur Halachah says the Rambam is right; the first attempt should be one-to-one. - Total: nine people plus you = ten , which explains Halacha 11: if the person has died , you bring ten people to his grave and say \"I sinned against Hashem and against this man.\" - \"Has anyone ever seen anyone do this?\" Halacha 10: Don't be cruel אסור לאדם להיות אכזרי ולא יתפייס… ובשעה שמבקש ממנו החוטא למחול, מוחל בלב שלם ובנפש חפצה… שזה הוא דרכם של זרע ישראל \"One of the hardest halachot to keep.\" Daniel (chat): does forgiving mean rebuilding the relationship? No. Forgiving means no longer harbouring ill will . \"Are you required to go and be friends again? Of course not… you choose who your friends are.\" Are there things you needn't forgive? The Rema (from the Maharil ): if someone defamed you, you need not forgive, because it can't be repaired ; once a reputation is ruined, it spreads. The Rabbi suggests a principle: irreparable harms. \"Someone murders a person's child, chas veshalom . Are you going to tell me I'm a sinner if I can't rid my heart of hatred? The Torah wasn't given to angels. \" Try your best, but be realistic. Robert: must we forgive non-Jews? (He mentioned people who have done things to Israel this past year.) \"Take it away and think about it.\" Halacha 10 frames it as who we are (\"not the way of the seed of Israel to harbour hatred\"), so it's about you as much as them, which might suggest it's universal. And forgiveness is only owed to someone who genuinely asks: through the whole process, three rows of three. The call an hour before Yom Kippur \"There are certain people who call you an hour before Yom Kippur: 'Are you mochel me for anything?'\" They had all Elul and the Ten Days; now they want to feel good going in. \"You don't want to sound harsh on Erev Yom Kippur, so you often end up lying : 'yeah, I'm mochel.' You have no duty to forgive that.\" The Ben Ish Chai says to ask parents for mechilah: with people close to you, a general \"if I did anything, forgive me\" is genuine and right. But where something specific went wrong, a vague request isn't mechilah. \"If we were brave we'd say: I'm not willing to forgive you now. Let's speak after Yom Kippur.\" Avram and Lot: sometimes, separate A participant: when Avram's and Lot's shepherds quarrelled, Avram said הפרד נא מעלי , \"please separate from me.\" Sometimes the right teshuvah is to not continue a relationship that would turn toxic. \"Fantastic. Precisely what Avraham says: let's be far-away friends rather than close friends who keep falling out.\" Boundaries are legitimate. The same participant offered another reading of Elazar ben Durdaya: perhaps Providence took him before he could slip back. The Rabbi: \"Difficult, because the Gemara uses this as the showcase of what teshuvah is. Something to think about; I'm not convinced.\" Summary Complete teshuvah : same person, same place, same desire, same strength, and you don't. Not by testing yourself, but by becoming someone else . Let the old self die : Elazar ben Durdaya. New Year's resolutions fail because we won't let the old mindset die. Rosh Hashanah = vision ; review it through the year (Erev Shabbat, Erev Rosh Chodesh); expect fruit by Nisan. Two routes (Rav Soloveitchik): emotional (regret leads) or intellectual (resolve leads). Viduy must be spoken and specific. Without it, identity without kaparah (Minchat Chinuch). Paths of teshuvah : crying out, tzedakah, distance, a new \"name,\" new deeds, exile. Public sins against people: confess publicly. Private sins against Hashem: privately. Yom Kippur makes teshuvah obligatory ; confess before the last meal, and again next year. Between people : pay, appease, ask (alone, then three rows of three). Forgive wholeheartedly, which doesn't oblige you to rebuild the relationship; and some harms may be beyond what a human can be expected to forgive. \"As much as you benefit from the shiur, I benefit from giving it… I hope we're able together to go into Yom Kippur with this perspective.\" Coming Up Next week is the shloshim of Rabbi Eliyahu (as transcribed), so the shiur will be rescheduled. Then Chapter 3 .","rw4kYL_nz0Q":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah (The Rambam's Laws of Repentance) Chapter: 1 (with the definition of teshuvah from Chapter 2) Shiur: Given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 10 September 2024 (Elul 5784) These notes are based on an automatic (Whisper) transcript of the recording. Introduction: A Full Month of Elul Welcome back after the summer. This year the calendar gives us a full month of Elul to think about teshuvah (\"normally we get thrown straight from August into Rosh Hashanah\"), and \"there is nothing better than learning the ten chapters of the Rambam\" to prepare. The plan: Chapter 1 this week, Chapter 2 next week, then selected parts of the other eight. \"The core of Hilchot Teshuvah is in the first two chapters.\" Where Hilchot Teshuvah sits The Mishneh Torah has 14 books . The first is Sefer HaMada , the Book of Knowledge, which the Rambam places first because it contains the fundamentals every person needs before learning the rest of Torah : Yesodei HaTorah (Foundations of the Torah) Deot (character traits), which the class has studied Talmud Torah Avodah Zarah Teshuvah You might expect teshuvah to come after learning all of halacha, for when you stray. But the Rambam treats it as part of the foundations : without it a person cannot fully understand the rest of the Torah. Two puzzles The definition comes late. Chapter 2 opens איזוהי תשובה גמורה , \"what is complete teshuvah?\", and in 2:2 ומה היא התשובה , \"what is teshuvah?\" Chapter 1 never defines it; it deals with viduy and the scapegoat . Why would \"the laws of teshuvah\" not start with what teshuvah is? \"Laws\" of teshuvah at all? The Rambam was the first to create a body of law called Hilchot Teshuvah . The Mishnah and Talmud have tractates for Rosh Hashanah, Sukkah, Shabbat, but none for teshuvah, and the Shulchan Aruch has none either. Teshuvah seems personal and emotional, \"between me and Hashem.\" You can legislate a shofar or the size of a sukkah. How do you legislate a change of heart? Part 1: The Mitzvah Is Viduy (Halacha 1) כל מצוות שבתורה, בין עשה בין לא תעשה, אם עבר אדם על אחת מהן, בין בזדון בין בשגגה, כשיעשה תשובה וישוב מחטאו, חייב להתוודות לפני האל ברוך הוא, שנאמר: איש או אשה כי יעשו… והתוודו את חטאתם אשר עשו (במדבר ה). זה וידוי דברים, ווידוי זה מצוות עשה For every mitzvah of the Torah, positive or negative, if a person transgressed it, intentionally or not, when he does teshuvah and returns from his sin, he is obliged to confess before God, as it says (Naso): \"a man or woman who commits any sin… shall confess their sin.\" This is verbal confession, and this confession is a positive commandment. \"Repentance\" is not quite the word תשובה comes from שב / לשוב , to return . Picture a path: you veer off, and the longer you walk the wrong way, the harder the way back. Teshuvah is getting back onto the path. How to confess אנא השם, חטאתי עויתי פשעתי לפניך, ועשיתי כך וכך, והרי ניחמתי ובושתי במעשי, ולעולם איני חוזר לדבר זה. וזהו עיקרו של וידוי. וכל המרבה להתוודות ומאריך בעניין זה, הרי זה משובח \"Please, Hashem, I have sinned, transgressed and rebelled before You, and I did such-and-such; I regret it and am ashamed of my deeds, and I will never return to this.\" This is the essence of viduy. The more one confesses and elaborates, the more praiseworthy. Word Kind of sin חטאתי Unintentional עויתי Intentional פשעתי Intentional, as an act of rebellion against Hashem, not just out of desire Nothing else atones without teshuvah and viduy Even… …does not atone until Bringing a korban ( chatat, asham ), deliberate or accidental עד שיעשו תשובה ויתוודו Receiving lashes , or even execution by Beit Din the person repents and confesses Paying full compensation for injuring someone or damaging their property he confesses and resolves never to do it again Notice the repetition: every time, \"until he does teshuvah and confesses.\" So these are two distinct things : teshuvah , defined in Chapter 2, and viduy , defined in Chapter 1. And the Rambam begins with viduy, as though it is the main thing. Part 2: Is Teshuvah a Mitzvah? In Sefer HaMitzvot , the Rambam does not count teshuvah among the 613. Ramban, Sefer Mitzvot Gadol and others The Rambam Source ושבת עד ה' אלהיך… והשבות אל לבבך (Nitzavim, read just before Rosh Hashanah) Reads those verses as a promise of what will happen Is teshuvah one of the 613? Yes: once you leave the path, a separate command says come back No: the original command already says stay on the path The mitzvah Teshuvah Viduy , which comes after teshuvah \"You may be thinking: teshuvah isn't a mitzvah? Teshuvah is one of the greatest things! 'Where ba'alei teshuvah stand, even the completely righteous cannot stand'!\" The road-sign analogy. The sign says stay in lane . If you drift out, you don't need a new sign telling you to get back in. The first instruction still applies; every moment out of lane, you are breaking it. The Ramban's view is that Hashem, knowing we are frail, gave 612 rules and a 613th: when you do drift, come straight back. The Rambam's view is that \"come back\" is built into every mitzvah already. The consequence: if a person does teshuvah without viduy , \"that's fantastic,\" but strictly speaking he hasn't performed a specific mitzvah. Part 3: What Teshuvah Is (from Chapter 2:2) ומה היא התשובה? הוא שיעזוב החוטא חטאו, ויסירו ממחשבתו, ויגמור בליבו שלא יעשהו עוד… וכן יתנחם על שעבר What is teshuvah? That the sinner abandon his sin, remove it from his thoughts, resolve in his heart never to do it again… and regret what he did. Three ingredients that are really one , עזיבת החטא , leaving the sin, in three tenses: Tense Element Meaning Present Stopping Stop the act, and stop thinking about it Past Regret ( charatah ) \"If I could go back in time, I would never have done this\" Future Resolve ( kabbalah ) A firm commitment never to do it again Why regret is part of \"leaving\" Memory makes us who we are . (This is why dementia is such a terrible illness: we live in the present based on our past.) If you stop now but still look back fondly (\"that was good fun\"), it's still part of your identity ; you haven't really returned. Sincere regret says: this is not who I am. Why resolve is part of it \"I really regret it… but next week I may end up doing it again.\" Without real commitment, you haven't left. \"That's the reality of human nature.\" Then why Yom Kippur every year? If teshuvah means never again , why not do it once? 1. Different years, different failings. 2. The dry-cleaner: you have a suit cleaned knowing it will get dirty again, and nobody says \"why bother?\" If you never cleaned it, eventually the cleaner would say it's beyond saving. And you learn from each stain: if the baby got food on it, next time you use a napkin. Next time it will be different dirt, a trip in the dust. \"Fine. Again, I learn from that.\" A worked example: lashon hara The Rabbi cited a survey of American Jewish communities on character traits: 91% rated chesed excellent, while lashon hara came out worst, with over 60% feeling the community fell short. \"Something we all need to work on.\" On Yom Kippur you decide: no more. But if you still savour those dinners laughing about someone, you haven't left it. If you regret it but know that around the table with friends next month you'll do it again, you haven't left it. And note: the sin was already forbidden by the commandment don't speak lashon hara . There's no new commandment to stop. Leaving it is just keeping the original mitzvah. What the Torah adds is viduy . Part 4: Why Viduy, and Why \"Laws\" of Teshuvah Teshuvah is personal; viduy is relational Teshuvah, stopping, regretting and resolving, is \"all in your own mind… all within yourself.\" But how do you repair the past? You can't turn back the clock. The employee analogy: someone slacks for months, nearly gets fired, then returns to doing the job properly. \"Am I now going to tell you you're doing an amazing job? You're doing what you were meant to be doing all along.\" The wasted months remain. If you could, you'd pay back the salary; restitution is best. Between people: someone who has been hurting another stops, regrets it, resolves never again, but \"you can't just go back to that person and pretend everything's fine.\" If it was money, pay it back. Often you can't. So you confess and ask forgiveness. Viduy is the interpersonal act : toward another person in bein adam lachavero , toward Hashem in bein adam laMakom . It says: I recognise what I did; I want to restore our relationship. This answers the second puzzle. The \"deeply personal thing\" is teshuvah, and there are no laws for that. Viduy is interpersonal , and that you have to get right. That's what the laws are for. Viduy and todah : the same root The Rabbi saw this idea in a commentator: להתוודות (to confess) and תודה (thanks) share a root. Relationships need balance . When one side keeps giving, you restore it by reciprocating, or failing that, by saying thank you . When one side has wronged the other, you restore it by acknowledging : confession. Why is revenge sweet? Because it also restores balance: you put me down, I put you down. But it spirals. The better way is forgiveness (very hard) or an apology, an acknowledgement , which \"goes a long way.\" \"Mistakes were made\" \"If only public figures would pay attention to this.\" A real apology has a subject, a verb and an object : I did this to you . Not \"mistakes were made\": by whom? to whom? what? That's why viduy is specific: ועשיתי כך וכך , \"and I did such-and-such.\" Questions from the class Does teshuvah need to be spoken? No, it's internal and can be done at any time. That is the point of Elul and the Ten Days : by Yom Kippur the teshuvah should already be done. \"If you go through Yom Kippur without doing teshuvah, it doesn't work.\" Should people accept others' apologies? The Rambam discusses it in Chapter 2: generally yes, with some exceptions. Can I regret upsetting Hashem but still enjoy the sin itself? \"An excellent question.\" No. The whole Torah is about our relationship with Hashem; if I still revel in the memory of gossiping, it's still part of me. \"It's very difficult. But that's the avodah of teshuvah. That's why we have 40 days.\" Why say viduy eleven times on Yom Kippur? Technically, if the teshuvah is done, 30 seconds of viduy would do. We repeat it because saying the words, sin by sin, awakens the teshuvah: \"is this applicable to me?\" \"The viduy is to awaken the teshuvah within us.\" The tovel veSheretz beYado כל המתוודה בדברים ולא גמר בליבו לעזוב, הרי זה דומה לטובל ושרץ בידו (2:3) One who confesses with words but hasn't resolved to leave the sin is like someone immersing in a mikveh while holding a sheretz (an impure creature). \"One of the most beautiful analogies.\" Yom Kippur is the mikveh. You can say every viduy in the machzor, but if you come out still holding the sin, you come out impure again. Yeshayahu's rebuke The haftarah of Shabbat Chazon before Tisha B'Av: why are you bringing me all these sacrifices , then oppressing the widow and orphan the next day? Today's equivalent: writing a big cheque to charity with no introspection. \"A good deed, but it doesn't repair things. That's the lazy way out.\" Part 5: The Scapegoat (Halachot 2–3) שעיר המשתלח, לפי שהוא כפרה על כל ישראל, כהן גדול מתוודה עליו על לשון כל ישראל The English word \"scapegoat\" was coined by William Tyndale in 1530 , translating the Torah. It was psychological : watching your sins \"placed\" on the goat and sent away, it's not me any more, it's not my identity. All part of teshuvah. The Kohen Gadol confessed on behalf of everyone, for every kind of sin. Without teshuvah , would the goat atone? Chachamim : not at all. Rebbi : yes, even for severe sins. The Rambam takes a middle path : only for less severe sins (those without death penalty or karet). The Beit Yosef (Kesef Mishneh) leaves this as a question; others (Lechem Mishneh) offer answers. The Rabbi's suggestion: the process itself had some intrinsic effect. בזמן הזה שאין בית המקדש קיים ואין לנו מזבח כפרה, אין שם אלא תשובה. התשובה מכפרת על כל העבירות Today, with no Temple and no altar, there is only teshuvah, and teshuvah atones for all sins. Even a lifetime of sin: the Gemara (Avodah Zarah 17a) tells of Elazar ben Durdaya , who spent his life travelling the world pursuing women, realised his life had no meaning, did complete teshuvah, was fully forgiven, and was granted a share in the World to Come. Part 6: Four Levels of Atonement (Halacha 4) Sin What atones Neglecting a positive mitzvah (tefillin, shofar, sukkah, tzitzit, prayer) Teshuvah alone, immediately. \"As soon as you decide 'I'm coming back,' done. You don't need Yom Kippur.\" A negative mitzvah without death penalty or karet (lashes) Teshuvah, completed by Yom Kippur A sin carrying karet or a death penalty (e.g. public chillul Shabbat) Teshuvah and Yom Kippur, plus suffering ( yissurin ) during the year Chillul Hashem All of the above, and only death completes it Mechilah, selichah, kaparah On Yom Kippur we ask for מחילה, סליחה וכפרה . Throughout this chapter the Rambam uses כפרה , the highest: - לכפר means to wipe clean , a clean slate. Yaakov before meeting Esav: אכפרה פניו במנחה , \"I will clear [his anger] with the gift.\" - מחילה is partial: forgiven, but not wiped clean. - So for the gravest sins, Yom Kippur gives mechilah but not full kaparah until the suffering completes it. Summary Hilchot Teshuvah is foundational : the last section of Sefer HaMada, the \"knowledge\" every Jew needs first. For the Rambam, the mitzvah is viduy, not teshuvah. Teshuvah is returning to what you were always obliged to do; the original commands still apply. Teshuvah = leaving the sin in the present (stopping), past (regret) and future (resolve). Teshuvah is internal; viduy restores the relationship. That's why it has laws , why it must be spoken and specific (\"I did X\"), and why it shares a root with todah . Viduy without teshuvah is immersing with a sheretz in your hand. Today, teshuvah atones for everything , with four levels of how atonement is completed. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 2 : teshuvah gemurah , complete teshuvah, and more on what teshuvah and viduy involve.","8ZQnkKmpQAk":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Deot (Rambam's Laws of Ethics) Chapter: 2, Halacha 7, and Chapter 3 (complete) Shiur: Part 4 of the seven-part series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom and Facebook, Tuesday 17 November 2020 Dedication: Le'ilui nishmat Yaakov ben Avraham HaKohen These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Silence and Identity Last week was about silence. For the Rambam and the Hachamim, silence is a virtue. People who are comfortable with silence are usually comfortable with their own identity, and people who are not comfortable with their identity will usually not find silence attractive. In practice: Don't speak just for the sake of speaking. Let people finish before responding. Teach with brevity and rich content. Be transparent: not \"one with the mouth and one with the heart,\" but תוכו כברו , inside like outside. Tonight finishes Chapter 2, continuing the idea of being comfortable in one's own identity. Then the shiur moves to Chapter 3, a new topic: how we engage with the world around us. Part 1: Neither Jester nor Mourner (Chapter 2, Halacha 7) לא יהא אדם בעל שחוק ומהתל, ולא עצב ואונן, אלא שמח A person should not be a jester and a mocker, nor sad and mournful, but happy. The ba'al sechok , the cynic. This is the person who finds a joke in everything. A depressed person isolates himself from the world because he is so focused inward. The cynic isolates himself in the opposite way: one way to isolate yourself from the world is to laugh at it. As the Rabbi put it, one cynical laugh can destroy years of building. You can learn from someone for years, and one cynical remark about the person or the teaching undoes it. Too much laughter and light-headedness lead to promiscuity. Laughter has its place, in the right amounts. The goal is to greet everyone with a cheerful face. The sour face is a pit in the public domain The Talmud discusses at length the damages for digging a pit in a public place. One of the rabbis said that someone who walks around in public with a miserable face is a pit in the public domain. There is no difference between a physical pit people fall into and an emotional one: everyone who sees you becomes sour as well. That is also damage, just of a different kind. Outward actions shape the inner self \"This is easier said than done. Sometimes it's difficult to be happy.\" But at the very least, put on a happy face outside. In the previous halacha ( genevat da'at ) Here (working on yourself) Putting on a show Forbidden. Don't pretend to want someone for Shabbat when you know they're away Encouraged. Smile even when you don't feel it Why It deceives other people Outer actions arouse the inner self. You avoid making others sour, and in time it changes you too Simcha is the natural state (Hilchot Lulav 8:15) At the end of Hilchot Shofar, Sukkah and Lulav, the Rambam writes: השמחה שישמח אדם בעשיית המצוה ובאהבת האל שצוה בהן עבודה גדולה היא, וכל המונע עצמו משמחה זו ראוי ליפרע ממנו The joy a person feels in doing a mitzvah, and in love of God who commanded it, is a great service. Whoever withholds himself from this joy deserves to be punished. The source is David and Michal . Michal, a princess and daughter of a king, watched her husband King David dancing wildly before the Ark and told him he had behaved like one of the common people. David answered that he would lower his honour even further, because the real honour is to be joyful before God. The Rambam says that anyone who stands on his own dignity in such moments \"is a sinner and a fool.\" There is no personal honour before the King. The Rabbi pointed to the wording המונע עצמו : one who withholds himself. For the Rambam, happiness is the natural default of a human being. To be unhappy, something must be withholding it from you. \"If you just be , and stop being extreme on either side, a person's natural state is simcha.\" The Rabbi acknowledged that many people today struggle with mental health, and this is an aspiration rather than something simple. Money: neither panic nor laziness ולא יהיה בעל נפש רחבה ונבהל לממון, ולא עצל ובטל ממלאכה, אלא בעל עין טובה וממעט בעסק ועוסק בתורה, ואותו מעט שהוא חלקו ישמח בו He should not be grasping and frantic for money, nor lazy and idle from work, but generous of eye: limiting business, occupied with Torah, and happy with the little that is his portion. The Chafetz Chaim's shop. About a hundred years ago in Radin, the Chafetz Chaim (Rabbi Yisrael Meir HaKohen), true to the rabbinic tradition, had a job. He opened his shop after Shacharit and closed it as soon as he had enough for the day's needs. The Rabbi guessed that the whole town made sure to shop there early so he could get back to learning. This is not practical today, and the Rabbi was not suggesting copying it. The point is the aim: work and earn a parnasah , but don't make your goal amassing wealth so that your great-grandchildren are financially secure. \"Your children and grandchildren can worry about themselves.\" There comes a point to step back and dedicate yourself to more important things. Part 2: Jealousy, Lust and Honour Take a Person Out of the World ולא יהא בעל קטטה, ולא בעל קנאה ולא בעל תאוה ולא רודף אחר הכבוד. כבר אמרו חכמים הקנאה והתאוה והכבוד מוציאין את האדם מן העולם He should not be quarrelsome, nor jealous, nor lustful, nor a pursuer of honour. The Sages said: jealousy, lust and honour take a person out of the world. Don't be confrontational. If a comment can be read two ways, don't take it the wrong way. Jealousy ( kin'ah ) We are only jealous of those we share a trait with. A doctor envies another doctor, not an accountant; a lawyer envies a lawyer. (\"And rabbis, of course, don't get jealous.\") The dog test. You may envy your neighbour for owning a beautiful dog, but nobody envies the dog . If someone said he would love to have the dog's existence, we would say he has an identity crisis. Even if animals arguably have a simpler, better life, it has no relevance to me, because I'm a human. The ideal: another person's life should be as irrelevant to my identity as the dog's. \"That's another identity. What I have is what I need.\" Jealousy comes from not recognising your own identity. Kohelet 4:4: כי היא קנאת איש מרעהו . Shlomo HaMelech saw all the toil and economies of the world, and it all comes from one person's envy of another. קנאת סופרים תרבה חכמה : rivalry among scholars increases wisdom. (\"That rabbi wrote a book, I'll write a book.\") Such drives are useful for human progress, but they are primal drives. Live by your own yardstick, not someone else's. This is how the halacha continues last week's: silence is easy only for those content with their identity, and so is freedom from jealousy. Lust ( ta'avah ) People who always want more are never happy with what they have, because they are looking at the world around them. When you think \"I need that,\" ask whether your identity really needs it. The word חלק means a portion , a part of something. Hashem gave you a role in this world: \"Do you think I would give you this role without giving you everything you need to fulfil it?\" Honour ( kavod ) Needing kavod means looking to others to make yourself valuable. If you approve of your own identity, you don't need their approval. Drive What it really reveals קנאה – jealousy You don't recognise your own identity תאוה – lust You aren't happy with your own chelek כבוד – honour You need others to give you your standing All three come down to the same thing: not being content with who you are. That is why they \"cripple\" a life. And again, \"these things take a lifetime to change.\" The rule of the middle כללו של דבר ילך במדת בינונית שבכל דעה ודעה עד שיהיו כל דעותיו מכוונות באמצעית… פלס מעגל רגלך וכל דרכיך יכונו The general rule: walk in the middle measure of every trait until all of them are aligned to the centre… \"Balance the path of your feet, and all your ways will be established\" (Mishlei 4:26). From the class: someone asked whether you should respond when accused of something untrue. The Rabbi said the Rambam deals with this explicitly in Chapter 7, the last chapter. Part 3: Withdrawing from the World Is a Sin (Chapter 3, Halacha 1) Having heard how destructive jealousy and lust are, a person might decide to run to the other extreme: שמא יאמר אדם… אפרוש מהן ביותר ואתרחק לצד האחרון, עד שלא יאכל בשר ולא ישתה יין ולא ישא אשה ולא ישב בדירה נאה ולא ילבש מלבוש נאה אלא השק והצמר הקשה… גם זה דרך רעה היא ואסור לילך בה Perhaps a person will say: I will withdraw to the other extreme. I won't eat meat, drink wine, marry, live in a nice home or wear nice clothes, only sackcloth and coarse wool… This too is a bad path, and it is forbidden to walk in it. Notice the wording. The Rambam doesn't say \"bad clothes\"; he says not nice clothes. Even deciding to dress merely ordinarily as a religious statement is wrong. It is fine to wear nice clothes, live in a nice house and eat good food. This is halacha. As the Rabbi said in the first shiur, these are halachot as binding as any ritual mitzvah. Same Rambam, same assur and mutar . One who goes this way is called a sinner. Of the Nazir it says וכפר עליו מאשר חטא על הנפש , he must atone for sinning against his soul (Bamidbar 6:11). If he needs atonement for abstaining from wine alone, how much more so someone who abstains from everything. \"You will need atonement for what you thought was your atonement.\" דייך מה שאסרה תורה : isn't it enough what the Torah forbade, that you forbid yourself more? Don't pile on vows and oaths to be extra religious. Constant fasting is not the right path. אל תהי צדיק הרבה : \"Do not be overly righteous… why should you destroy yourself?\" (Kohelet 7:16). Why does the Rambam mention Christian priests? In the original, uncensored text, the Rambam compares these ascetics to Christian priests . Why mention them? One person in the class suggested it's because they don't marry. The Rabbi offered a broader historical reading: Rambam (12th century) Ashkenazi Rishonim (12th–14th centuries) Where Spain and the Islamic world Germany, France, England Surrounding culture Muslim Christian Ideal of piety Engagement with the world, for the right purpose Strong ascetic tendencies, e.g. Rabbi Yehudah HaChasid's Sefer Chasidim and the Rokeach (with its long lists of fasts for past sins: \"you might get nightmares reading it\") The Rabbi said he was \"almost sure\" this is a direct critique of contemporaries who promoted self-affliction as the path to atonement. Those ideas grew up under the surrounding Christian culture. \"We often look at our Torah through the society around us without realising it.\" He compared it to an earlier shiur on reward and punishment, which we tend to picture as coins piling up in a bank account. Self-affliction is not how you get kaparah . Enjoying the world is not a concession People often assume Hachamim should live in modest houses and wear scruffy clothes. \"For the Rambam, this is absolutely wrong.\" That week's parashah, Toldot, shows the opposite: Yitzchak hands over leadership and wants to bless with a full heart, so he asks for a feast: two young goats, a banquet rather than a light supper. The spiritual berachah comes from a physical act of eating. Yaakov gives his beloved son not a Shas or a shelf of books, but a beautiful coloured coat. \"He's a 17-year-old. Let him enjoy himself.\" Yerushalmi (end of Kiddushin): a person will have to give an account for every fruit he saw, could have eaten, and didn't. One of the Sages would save money to buy new fruits for this reason. Fasting is for someone who has done a terrible sin and has no other way to relate to it. \"The real good thing is wisdom: learn more, understand more, connect.\" Part 4: Engage, but for the Right Purpose (Chapter 3, Halachot 2–3) \"If you've listened until now, don't leave now, or you'll get the wrong impression.\" Halacha 2 has to go with Halacha 1: צריך האדם שיכוון לבו וכל מעשיו כולם לידע את השם ברוך הוא בלבד A person must direct his heart and all his actions solely to knowing God. Activity The wrong aim The right aim Business A bigger number in the bank account to smile at Providing for food, the mortgage and the family Eating Whatever is sweet and gives momentary pleasure (\"like a dog or a donkey\") What is healthy, even if bitter. The Rabbi remembered cod liver oil every night as a child Marriage and children Help in the business, or chutra lemachei (a staff in old age) Children who may become wise and great in Israel, out of service of God The gym Muscles so people look at you A healthy body to pray, learn and do chesed Health A long life to spend more time at the cinema Strength to serve Hashem The Rambam said 800 years ago what dieticians tell us now. He also gives humoral advice (someone with a \"hot temperament\" should avoid meat and honey). The Rabbi said he didn't know how true that is medically today. And he was sure that if the Rambam were alive today he would tell us to exercise at least weekly. \"So that the soul is clear to know God\" שיהיה גופו שלם וחזק כדי שתהיה נפשו ישרה לדעת את ה', שאי אפשר שיבין וישכיל בחכמות והוא רעב וחולה או אחד מאיבריו כואב His body should be whole and strong, so that his soul is fit to know God; for one cannot understand and grasp wisdom while hungry, ill or in pain. If you're starving, you can't think deeply. If you're worried about paying bills at the end of the month, you can't give the text your full focus. That is why you need to earn, eat and look after yourself: to have a free mind. The Avot were very wealthy. Avraham was כבד מאד (\"very heavy\") with cattle, silver and gold. Yitzchak reaped מאה שערים , a hundredfold. Yaakov too. They were blessed with wealth so they could serve Hashem free of financial worry. עניות מעבירה את האדם על דעתו ועל דעת קונו : poverty turns a person from his own mind and the mind of his Creator (Eruvin 41b). Even sleep is service of God נמצא המהלך בדרך זו כל ימיו עובד את ה' תמיד, אפילו בשעה שנושא ונותן ואפילו בשעה שבועל… ואפילו בשעה שהוא ישן Whoever lives this way serves Hashem constantly, even while doing business, even during relations… even while asleep. וכל מעשיך יהיו לשם שמים : let all your deeds be for the sake of Heaven (Avot 2:12). בכל דרכיך דעהו והוא יישר ארחתיך : in all your ways know Him, and He will straighten your paths (Mishlei 3:6). The Shulchan Aruch (Orach Chaim 231) codifies this. Eating, drinking, walking, sitting, sleeping, relations and conversation should all be for the service of the Creator. Even if one is hungry or thirsty, eating purely for pleasure is not praiseworthy. The practical test. If you really ate to live, you would never drink a can of Coca-Cola. (\"Maybe I'm exaggerating a bit. A can here and there isn't that bad.\") Eating late at night when you know you've had enough is the opposite. Next week the Rambam will say that overeating is one of the greatest causes of illness. A realistic kavanah \"I don't expect anyone to go home and think at their next meal, 'I'm having this only to serve God.'\" A simple thought is enough: I'm eating because I need to live, and without it I can't pray, learn or help others. I'm going to sleep so I can serve God tomorrow. I'm earning to look after my family, as God commanded me. Two people can do exactly the same things all day with two different motives, and they are two totally different lives. Questions from the Class What about people who lived with only a bed and a candle? If you don't have money, you make do. But the Rambam surely lived in a decent home. His brother David supported him for years and drowned at sea. The Rambam wrote in a letter that for years afterwards he could not get over it and was constantly weeping. \"This is the same Rambam who tells us not to be depressed. This is the aspiration.\" People who lived extremely simply probably feared that caring about clothes or \"the tiles in my toilet\" would pull them away from serving Hashem. Some people can't hold the balance, so they go to an extreme. As the Ra'avad said, it is easier to fast a whole day than to stop halfway through a delicious banquet. What about Avot 6:4, \"bread with salt… sleep on the ground\"? (Daniel) The Mishnah means you must be ready to live that way for Torah, if necessary. It is not the aspiration. \"The Rambam absolutely does not hold that the less you enjoy this world, the more you get in the next.\" Isn't poverty the norm in Jewish history? (Ronnie) The Rambam doesn't exclude the poor. It is simply harder to focus on wisdom with financial burdens on your head, as the Gemara already says. Doesn't tradition tell us to blend in and not attract the evil eye? (Tommy) \"100%.\" Yaakov told his sons to go down to Egypt for food even though they had it, so as not to stand out. Don't be ostentatious with fur coats and gold chains. A nice, decent house, car, food and drink doesn't contradict this. The naval bi-reshut ha-Torah . (Flora) Ramban at the start of Kedoshim describes a \"scoundrel with the Torah's permission,\" who keeps the letter of the law while indulging endlessly. The Rabbi agreed this is the same idea: pursuing pleasure for its own sake, even permitted pleasure, is wrong. It all comes back to balance. All the recordings were on Facebook, and the link was posted in the chat. No Facebook account is needed to listen. Summary End of Chapter 2: neither cynic nor mourner. Smile, because outward actions shape the inner self. Simcha is the natural state; whoever withholds it from himself is to blame. Money: neither frantic nor lazy. Be happy with your chelek . Jealousy, lust and honour take a person out of the world, because each shows discomfort with one's own identity. Chapter 3: withdrawing from the world through self-denial, constant fasting or deliberately plain living is forbidden , a sin requiring atonement. The Rabbi read the \"Christian priests\" reference as a critique of ascetic piety absorbed from the surrounding culture. Engage fully, for the right purpose: a healthy body and a free mind so the soul can know Hashem. Then even business, eating and sleep become service of God. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 4, Rambam's Diet. \"If you listen to everything I tell you in this chapter, you will never become ill. Free medical advice from a rabbi!\" Tuesday 1 December 2020: a conversation with Rabbi Dr Joshua Berman on archaeology and Torah.","xjQCWhGr9h0":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Deot (Rambam's Laws of Ethics) Chapter: 2, Halachot 4–6 Shiur: Part 3 of the seven-part series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom and Facebook, Tuesday 10 November 2020 Dedication: Le'ilui nishmat Rabbi Lord Jonathan Sacks zt\"l, who passed away that week These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Part 1: A Tribute to Rabbi Lord Jonathan Sacks The Rabbi opened the shiur with about fifteen minutes on the loss of the former Chief Rabbi, three days after his passing. \"I still feel numb every time I talk about the fact that Rabbi Sacks is no longer with us.\" For the past seven or eight years, he said, Rabbi Sacks had felt like his personal Rabbi, even without a close relationship. Only that week did he realise how many other people felt the same way. Two meetings The first meeting. The Rabbi was a 13-year-old boy reading the Torah in shul, only a few times after his Bar Mitzvah. As he read, he was touching the parchment with the yad, which one should not do. Rabbi Sacks was called up for an aliyah and gently lifted his hand off the Sefer Torah, twice in the same aliyah. Only afterwards did the shul's Rabbi tell him who that person was. They spoke at the Kiddush, and that was the first time he met him. The last meeting. This was in September 2019, at the funeral of Mrs Estelle Levy, Rabbi Levy's wife. The Rabbi went over to tell Rabbi Sacks how much his books, and his weekly Covenant & Conversation , had transformed his thinking and the way he delivers his own lectures and sermons. Rabbi Sacks told him three or four times in the conversation how happy he was to hear it. Driving home, the Rabbi reflected that \"this world-class philosopher, this world-class theologian, is actually encouraged by little me.\" He drew two lessons: Humility. Rabbi Sacks appreciated every single person who valued his work. Words of encouragement go far. \"Don't think that the great and the lofty are not human. They also need inspiration and encouragement.\" \"From failure to failure\" Rabbi Sacks was honest about the difficult times in his life, including depression. The Rabbi retold a story Rabbi Sacks used to tell. A rabbi in America looked over his CV and asked what he had ever failed at. Rabbi Sacks answered, \"I have failed at everything.\" Then he quoted his favourite definition of success, from Winston Churchill: going from failure to failure without a loss of enthusiasm. We only ever see people's successes, the Rabbi said. What made Rabbi Sacks great was that he persevered, and lived with the knowledge that we can lift ourselves up when we fall and that Hashem forgives us. The Great Partnership: mah and lamah The Rabbi first came across Covenant & Conversation about eight years earlier. He was stunned by how relevant it made the Torah, and how the sacred and the secular sat together. For him at the time, they had seemed incompatible. He recommended The Great Partnership as the book to start with, and quoted its famous line: science tells you what, religion tells you why. Science Torah Hebrew word מה ( mah ) – what למה ( lamah ) – why What it does Breaks the world down into its components Gives the framework, context and meaning Alone it is… \"always too little\" ( ve-nachnu mah ) the meaning of reality There is no contradiction between Hashem's works (the physical world) and Hashem's words (the Torah). They come hand in hand. The Rabbi said this \"changed my whole hashkafah in life.\" The prophetic voice The Rabbi admitted that whenever a sermon he had written did not feel inspiring or relevant enough, he turned to Rabbi Sacks' archive. Searching a topic gives \"ten essays with different ideas\" that always gave the dvar Torah an extra boost. He quoted Prince Charles calling Rabbi Sacks \"a prophetic voice.\" This is exactly what the Nevi'im were: people who took the Torah and made it relevant to the society around them. Torah cannot stay in an ivory tower. The takeaway: seek out the Hachamim while they are here \"Everyone is now listening to his lectures. Why now? Why not when he was alive?\" Rabbi Sacks' books were trending on Amazon that week. That is human nature, the Rabbi said: we only appreciate things once they are gone. His lesson was to seek out the great Hachamim and Tsaddikim of this generation, spend time with them and learn from them while they are alive. Don't push it off to another time. He announced a plan for 2021, after this Rambam series: a series on Rabbi Sacks' books, one book each week. (That series later ran as the conversations with Rabbi Joseph Dweck on The Great Partnership and Not in God's Name .) The shiur was dedicated le'ilui nishmato. Part 2: Recap – Getting to the Middle Path Week 1: the path of the Hacham is to be equidistant from the extremes , with every character trait in moderation. Week 2: there are two cases where one should go to an extreme: To correct a trait that has gone off-kilter. Bend the paper the other way. Someone who overspends should be extremely thrifty for a while. Someone with a sour face should walk around \"jolly and merry and dancing\" for a while. Then return to the middle. Anger and humility. Stay as far from anger as possible. If authority must be shown, show it outwardly while staying calm inside. Humility can never be too much. It does not mean being a pushover. It means recognising that everything is from Hashem and we are His conduits. The Rabbi noted that in preparing this shiur, \"I really saw Rabbi Sacks in the Rambam,\" and would return to him during the class. Part 3: Silence Is a Virtue (Halacha 4) לעולם ירבה אדם בשתיקה ולא ידבר אלא או בדבר חכמה או בדברים שהוא צריך להם לחיי גופו A person should always be excessively silent, and speak only words of wisdom or what he needs for his physical life. Rav never spoke idle talk. It was said of Rav, the student of Rabbeinu HaKadosh (Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi, who compiled the Mishnah in the 3rd century), that he never engaged in idle talk ( שיחה בטלה ) his whole life. The Rambam adds a dose of realism: this is how most people spend their talk. \"Haki balash.\" Seeing Arabic speakers on the call, including his mother, the Rabbi offered the Arabic expression for sichah beteilah : empty talk. Someone in the class supplied the English, \"shooting the breeze.\" Even about necessary physical matters, don't speak at length. כל המרבה דברים מביא חטא – whoever talks too much brings sin. לא מצאתי לגוף טוב אלא שתיקה – I found nothing better for the body than silence (Avot 1:17). Few words, rich content וכן יהיו דבריו מעטים וענייניהם מרובים… ולעולם ישנה אדם לתלמידיו דרך קצרה His words should be few and their content rich… and one should always teach students in a concise way. Teach in principles that people can take home from a shiur. The Rabbi quoted Einstein: everything should be made as simple as possible, but no simpler. This was another of Rabbi Sacks' gifts. He could take the most philosophical ideas and bring them down, while the deep truths remained. Many words with little content is foolishness. \"The voice of a fool comes with many words\" (Kohelet 5:2). Part 4: Don't Be Quick to Answer (Halacha 5) סייג לחכמה שתיקה, לפיכך לא ימהר להשיב ולא ירבה לדבר The fence around wisdom is silence; therefore one should not be hasty to answer, nor speak too much. Two readings of \"before he hears\" The Rabbi brought Mishlei 18:13 and asked the class to read the Hebrew, not the English translation: משיב דבר בטרם ישמע אולת היא לו וכלמה One who answers before he hears, it is folly and shame for him. The word ישמע can mean to understand or to hear . For example, Yosef's brothers did not know that Yosef was שומע (understanding), because there was an interpreter between them. He obviously heard them; the word there means understood . Reading Meaning Lesson Before he understands (the usual translation) Don't answer before you have processed the question Take time to analyse and think it through Before he hears (Rav Saadia Gaon) Don't answer before the other person has finished speaking Don't cut people off. Listen and show respect for what they are saying Rav Saadia Gaon's reading describes the conversations where you can tell the other person is just waiting to make their own point: \"If you let me finish the sentence, you'll understand. There'll be an answer to your question.\" ומלמד לתלמידיו בנחת ובישוב בלא צעקה ובלא אריכות לשון… דברי חכמים בנחת נשמעים He teaches his students calmly and with composure, without shouting and without verbosity… The words of the wise are heard when spoken calmly (Kohelet 9:17). Benachat here means both calmly and to the point . Part 5: Two Side Points Does this apply to women? The Rabbi raised two Gemarot: Kiddushin 49b: \"Ten measures of chatter came down to the world, and women took nine.\" Berachot 48b: the young women who gave Shaul long, roundabout directions to Shmuel. One explanation in the Gemara is that \"women are chatterers.\" The Gemara also gives other reasons, for example that they wanted to gaze at Shaul's beauty. He stressed that these must be read in context and not in a misogynistic way. Two thousand years ago, women were not educated and did not work. Commerce and business were handled by their husbands, so conversation was inevitably sichah . \"The world has changed today,\" and he would argue this no longer applies to the same extent. The Rambam's five categories of speech In his commentary on the Mishnah (Avot 1:17), the Rambam explains that one of the signs of the wise is few words; one of the signs of the fool is many words. He divides all speech into five categories: # Category What it covers The Rabbi's examples 1 מצווה – commanded Torah, its study, shiurim – 2 אסור – forbidden False testimony, lashon hara, cursing, embarrassing people – 3 נמאס – repugnant (not forbidden, but no benefit) \"What happened to so-and-so, what are King X's customs, how did Z get rich\" \"Vos iz neias?\" ; hours spent on that week's Trump–Biden news and tweets 4 נאהב – beloved Praise of virtue and good people, ethical ( mussar ) works Speaking well of others, positive talk 5 מותר – permitted Business, commerce, everyday needs – The Rabbi laid these out as a spectrum. Mitzvah speech is at one extreme and forbidden speech at the other. The repugnant sits just next to the forbidden, the beloved next to the mitzvah, and the permitted is in the middle. Part 6: Why Is Silence in This Chapter? \"Awkward Silences\" The puzzle is that all of this seems to be a new topic. Why isn't it a new chapter, and how does it continue last week's teaching on the middle path? The Rabbi brought an idea he heard from Rabbi Joseph Dweck . The simple link. The previous halacha ended with the pious who are insulted but do not respond and stay happy. So the next halacha continues: keep quiet when you don't need to speak. The deeper link. Hilchot Deot is about our da'at , and how we interact with the people around us. The main way we interact is speech. Until now the Rambam has taught how to get to the middle. Now he teaches how to stay there. Think of learning to ride a bike. At first you wobble right and left, then you find your balance. The hard part is getting there, but once you're there, how do you stay there without being dragged off-kilter by foreign elements? The Rambam's answer is don't talk. The default of being is silence. The Rabbi titled the shiur \"Awkward Silences.\" Silence is uncomfortable for most of us. Radio can't have dead air. If he stopped the lecture for half a minute, people would start asking whether everything was OK. Alone it's even worse: how often do we sit without music, radio, a book or a phone, and just be ? Radio is \"chewing gum for the brain.\" Real friends can be silent together. With a spouse or a very close friend, you can walk in the park or sit over lunch in silence with no awkwardness. Small talk exists to fill the time with people we don't fully express ourselves to. Why that matters. The tsaddik of the previous halacha hears insults, does not respond, and is still happy inside . \"I know who I am. You don't define me by what you say. I am who I am, with or without you.\" Someone comfortable with silence is comfortable with their own identity. Speech creates, so it is not the default. \"Death and life are in the hand of the tongue\" (Mishlei 18:21). Speech is the power to create, but we are not always in creative mode. Real speech needs silence around it, before and after, to be processed. A deep idea dropped into constant banter will not be heard. Shimon ben Rabban Gamliel (Avot 1:17): כל ימי גדלתי בין החכמים ולא מצאתי לגוף טוב אלא שתיקה – \"All my days I grew up among the Hachamim, and I found nothing better for the body than silence.\" The whole series is about how to become a Hacham. The Hacham is happy within his own skin and does not need to fill the air. \"How are you?\" \"How are you?\" means nothing. If someone actually told you how they were, you'd think something was wrong. A more meaningful question, for someone you haven't seen all week, is: How has your week been? Any highlights? That is real speech that needs a real answer. It is דיבור נאהב , beloved speech, as opposed to filler that only gets us past the awkwardness of silence. Summary of the idea: The default of being is silence, because we create when we speak. Silence doesn't disturb our existence; it allows our being to be . We only struggle with silence when we struggle with ourselves. Be a human being , not a human doing . From the class Flora pointed out that Esther and Binyamin were rewarded for their silence. She also mentioned a Gemara that whoever is silent becomes a master of halacha, because when you listen, you internalise. The Rabbi added the Gemara that a sign of good lineage ( meyuchas ) is being a שתקן , one who is silent and listens. That too was a trait of Rabbi Sacks: \"He was an introvert in essence,\" and spoke when it was important to. Part 7: Smooth Talk and Stealing Minds (Halacha 6) אסור לאדם לנהוג בדברי חלקות ופיתוי, ולא יהיה אחד בפה ואחד בלב, אלא תוכו כברו One may not use smooth and seductive speech, nor be one thing in the mouth and another in the heart; rather his inside must be like his outside. חלק / smooth. Hebrew and English share the image. Chalak meat (Yiddish glatt ) has smooth lungs, and a glatte sevara is smooth logic. In English we call it \"smooth talk.\" Yosef's brothers. They \"could not speak to him peaceably.\" The Hachamim find the silver lining: at least they weren't two-faced. Everyone knew where they stood. Genevat Da'at: stealing someone's mind It is forbidden to mislead people, even a non-Jew. This is not outright lying. It means creating a false impression. The Rambam's case How the Rabbi explained it Selling non-kosher meat as if it were shechted A non-Jew asks for meat. You hand him kosher meat and slip in some neveilah you need to get rid of. You haven't said a false word, but you've stolen his mind. A shoe from a dead animal sold as one from a slaughtered animal Leather from a slaughtered animal was evidently better quality, perhaps because you knew the animal was healthy. Pressing a friend to eat with you when you know he won't You invite him for Shabbat lunch on Monday, knowing he flies to Israel on Thursday. You give the impression you want his company when you don't. Offering gifts you know won't be accepted – Opening barrels \"in his honour\" that you needed to open anyway The Rabbi's version: you need to open twenty whiskies for a tasting in two weeks. You open them early in front of your guest, the Blue Label and the Glenfiddich, \"all for you, lichvodcha ,\" when really you only need to sell them. ואפילו מלה אחת של פיתוי ושל גניבת דעת אסור, אלא שפת אמת ורוח נכון ולב טהור מכל עמל והוות Even a single word of seduction or deception is forbidden. Rather, a truthful lip, a steadfast spirit and a heart pure of all trickery. Summary Rabbi Sacks: humility, honesty about failure (\"from failure to failure without a loss of enthusiasm\"), Torah and science as lamah and mah , and the lesson to seek out the Hachamim while they are alive. Silence is a virtue and the safeguard of wisdom. Speak only words of wisdom or what is needed, and teach with few words and rich content. Don't answer before you hear. Process the question, and don't cut people off (Rav Saadia Gaon). Five categories of speech: commanded, forbidden, repugnant, beloved and permitted. Why silence follows the middle path: the Rambam first teaches how to reach equilibrium, then how to stay there. Silence is the default state of being. Awkward silences point to a problem in our relationship with ourselves. No smooth talk and no genevat da'at : your inside must match your outside, even towards a non-Jew, even by one word. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 3, on enjoying this world and partaking of it in the right way. Tuesday 1 December 2020: a conversation with Rabbi Dr Joshua Berman on his book Ani Maamin , about the Torah in the context of archaeology and history. Thanks to Kenneth Pratt, who gave the Rabbi the book.","boESLdqXQFk":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Deot (Rambam's Laws of Ethics) Chapter: End of Chapter 6 (Halachot 8–10) and Chapter 7 (complete) Shiur: Part 7, the final part of the series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom and Facebook, Tuesday 19 January 2021 Dedication: Le'ilui nishmat Mrs Elizabeth Swift, Ettel Basha bat Batsheva, who passed away and was buried that day. Long life to her daughter, Alison. These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Introduction \"Tonight we discuss a very important topic, one which I think there's a bit of confusion about\": lashon hara and rechilut . We've heard these terms a lot, but don't necessarily know exactly what they mean. Recap of last week (Chapter 6): - Society has a strong influence on us, so choose the right friends and influences. - Don't keep hatred in your heart; speak it out. That was a progressive idea for the Rambam's time. - The primary mitzvah of rebuke is not about telling people off for religious transgressions. It is about interpersonal relationships : when something goes wrong with someone, talk to them and try to reconcile. Part 1: Finishing Chapter 6 The only mitzvah with a built-in warning (Halacha 8) המוכיח את חבירו תחלה לא ידבר לו קשות עד שיכלימנו, שנאמר ולא תשא עליו חטא One who rebukes his friend should not, at first, speak harshly to him so as to embarrass him, as it says: \"and do not bear sin because of him.\" Straight after \"do not hate your brother in your heart… surely rebuke your fellow,\" the verse adds ולא תשא עליו חטא , \"and do not bear a sin\" (Vayikra 19:17). The Sages explain: you might think you should rebuke him until his face changes colour. So the verse says not to bear a sin. From here we learn that it is forbidden to embarrass a Jew, and all the more so in public. It is such a great sin that the Hachamim say one who embarrasses another in public has no share in the World to Come. \"To the best of my knowledge, this is the only mitzvah in the Torah where the Torah gives you a mitzvah and warns you: while doing this mitzvah, be careful you don't commit a sin.\" Rebuke privately and without condescension. Only then does the other person feel it comes from love. If you do it in public, it is most likely coming from anger. Orphans and widows (Halacha 10) Speak to orphans and widows gently, softly and with honour. Favouring the orphan. The Rambam usually stresses treating people equally; a teacher shouldn't favour pupils. Here he says the opposite: a teacher should treat an orphan differently from the rest of the class, with extra time, patience and mercy. God is the orphan's father, so if we don't treat the orphan properly, we aren't treating HaKadosh Baruch Hu properly. Who is an orphan? Someone who still needs an adult, i.e. who is not yet independent. There is no age limit . An 80-year-old who was very close to a parent may be \"an orphan\" for a while as they regain their bearings. Today that usually lasts until marriage, when someone has a partner to share their life with. From the class: rebuke over WhatsApp Flora described carefully writing a message to a WhatsApp group about something that troubled her. She softened it several times and checked it with six or eight people, but people were still upset. Was it wrong? The Rabbi said he couldn't comment on the specific case and offered to discuss it privately. The general principle: Texting and messaging lose context. Face to face, you see whether someone is smiling, frowning, disappointed or happy. A blank message has none of that and is easily misread, \"which is why emojis can sometimes be very good.\" \"When you're unsure about something, it's never good to write about it. It's best to speak it out,\" one-to-one, in private, in person. Part 2: The Peddler (Chapter 7, Halacha 1) המרגל בחבירו עובר בלא תעשה, שנאמר לא תלך רכיל בעמיך One who \"goes about\" ( meragel ) concerning his fellow transgresses a negative commandment: \"Do not go as a peddler among your people.\" מרגל comes from רגל , foot . The gossip goes around on his feet, from person to person: \"Did you know what happened? What did he do? What did she do?\" In Yiddish, \"vos iz neias?\" , which \"normally means asking for trouble.\" It ignores people's privacy. There are no lashes, because it's done by speech, not action, but it is a great sin, and it causes many deaths. That is why the next words in the verse are ולא תעמוד על דם רעך , \"do not stand by your fellow's blood\" (Vayikra 19:16). Isn't that an exaggeration? Remember Doeg the Edomite. David fled from Shaul to Nov, the city of the kohanim. Doeg saw him there and reported back to Shaul, who destroyed the whole city of priests for helping David. Part 3: Three Kinds of Bad Speech (Halacha 2) Term What it is True or false? רכילות – rechilut , peddling Carrying information from person to person: \"So-and-so said this; I heard this about so-and-so\" Can be true לשון הרע – lashon hara Speaking about someone's flaws True מוציא שם רע – motzi shem ra Defamation False The Rabbi asked the class to put up their hands if they thought rechilut was the worst. Most people assume it is. The Rambam says otherwise: אי זהו רכיל? זה שטוען דברים והולך מזה לזה… אף על פי שהוא אמת הרי זה מחריב את העולם. יש עון גדול מזה עד מאד, והוא בכלל לאו זה, והוא לשון הרע Who is a peddler? One who carries words and goes from one person to another… even if it is true, he destroys the world. There is a far greater sin than this, included in the same prohibition: lashon hara. Rechilut is about concealed things. If something is already public knowledge, passing it on isn't rechilut . \"That's why the Hachamim never liked newspapers,\" the Rabbi added. The puzzle The Rambam describes the two in almost identical words: Rechilut: \"so-and-so said this, I heard this about so-and-so.\" Lashon hara: \"so-and-so did this, his fathers were like this, I heard this about him.\" So (1) what's the difference, (2) why is lashon hara so much worse, and (3) why does the Rambam quote a verse only for lashon hara? One person in the class suggested that rechilut is one-to-one while lashon hara is told to a group. \"A nice suggestion, but the Rambam doesn't say that.\" The answer: walking vs. sitting Rechilut Lashon hara The Rambam's verb הולך מזה לזה – goes from one to another יושב ואומר – sits and says What the speaker does Passes on information as it came Works on the information: edits, cuts and frames it to show the person in the worst light The image A messenger An editor at an editing desk Consequence Destroys relationships, \"destroys the world,\" a serious sin Loses one's share in the World to Come על זה אמר הכתוב יכרת ה' כל שפתי חלקות לשון מדברת גדלות About this the verse says: \"May Hashem cut off all smooth lips, the tongue that speaks boastfully\" (Tehillim 12:4). חלק = smooth. The ba'al lashon hara is a smooth talker. Think of a used-car salesman: he doesn't lie, but he presents only the positives and conveniently leaves out the flaws. Lashon hara does the same in reverse. Every word is true, but the picture has been manufactured to be negative. \"Without getting too political,\" that's the real problem with much of the media . Many news outlets won't lie to you. They present real information, but edit it and put it in a manufactured context, so you lose your ability to judge. The root: a bad eye There are lovely initiatives where people commit not to speak lashon hara for an hour or two a day. \"Fantastic. But when you don't know the root of a problem, you can't fix it.\" What about the other hours? The root is an ayin hara, a bad eye. Someone who sees everything negatively and pessimistically speaks with a bad tongue. That's why it's called lashon* hara***. The spies. Was anything they said untrue? The fruit was huge, there were giants: all true. They were like a media outlet, the only source of information, since no one else had been there. Instead of reporting to Moshe and Aharon, they went straight to the people and filtered out every positive. They were even speaking about land and fruit, not people. The problem was how they perceived it. The cure is to change how we see. דברי פי חכם חן : the words of the wise are gracious (Kohelet 10:12). ולשון חכמים מרפא : the tongue of the wise heals (Mishlei 12:18). That is why Hilchot Talmud Torah comes straight after Hilchot Deot. Torah gives a wholesome picture of reality. A good eye ( ayin tovah ) naturally produces a good tongue ( lashon tov ). From the class: venting at work Someone asked: if you always feel hard done by a certain person at work, and you need to talk to someone about it, is that lashon hara? The Rabbi answered that the first step is always to speak to the person directly : without embarrassing them, genuinely, with the aim of reconciliation. If you've done that and it still doesn't work, then speaking to others for a constructive purpose is permitted. \"I'm not saying that every time someone does something to you, you can go and tell others.\" In his Commentary on Pirkei Avot, the Rambam wrote that he explained this at length because \"people are in great blindness about it, and it is a great sin that is always with people.\" \"Things haven't changed 800 years on.\" Part 4: Why Lashon Hara Is Like Denying God (Halacha 3) שלש עבירות נפרעין מן האדם בעולם הזה ואין לו חלק לעולם הבא: עבודה זרה, וגילוי עריות, ושפיכות דמים, ולשון הרע כנגד כולם For three sins a person is punished in this world and has no share in the World to Come: idolatry, forbidden relations and murder, and lashon hara is equal to all of them. כל המספר בלשון הרע כאילו כפר בעיקר, שנאמר אשר אמרו ללשננו נגביר שפתינו אתנו מי אדון לנו Whoever regularly speaks lashon hara is as if he denied God, as it says: \"Those who said: with our tongues we shall prevail; our lips are with us, who is master over us?\" (Tehillim 12:5). Why heresy? It fits the explanation above. The ba'al lashon hara sees the world negatively and cannot see the unity of creation, so he cannot see the unity of God. \"Our lips are with us\" : I control reality with my words, and there is no external truth I need to respect. That attitude can lead to denying HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Kashrut for the mouth Lashon hara is a negative commandment like eating non-kosher food, and according to the Rambam even worse. Yet look how careful we are with food: checking ingredients, worrying whether there's a sixtieth of something treif . As one Hacham said: if only we were as careful about what comes out of our mouths as about what goes in. \"We need the same hechsher on our mouths for what goes out as for what comes in.\" Three are harmed שלשה לשון הרע הורגת: האומרו, והמקבלו, וזה שאומר עליו, והמקבלו יותר מן האומרו Lashon hara kills three: the one who says it, the one who accepts it, and the one it's about, and the one who accepts it more than the one who says it. Who Why they are harmed The speaker He lives detached from reality, a very negative life The listener who accepts it He has given up the ability to filter and judge for himself, and takes on the speaker's negative mindset. \"I don't have a fully satisfactory explanation for why he's worse,\" but we have a responsibility to protect ourselves from it The subject It often leads to very negative consequences for him Part 5: The \"Dust\" of Lashon Hara (Halacha 4) יש דברים שהן אבק לשון הרע There are things that are \"the dust of lashon hara.\" Example Why it's avak \"If only X were always like he is now!\" Nothing negative was said, but it clearly implies he used to be difficult \"Don't start me on that person. I don't want to say what happened.\" \"Unfortunately quite common.\" You've said nothing, and everything Excessively praising someone in front of people you know dislike him It provokes them to speak against him. מברך רעהו בקול גדול בבקר השכים קללה תחשב לו , \"blessing your friend loudly early in the morning will be counted as a curse\" (Mishlei 27:14). Picture going around the street with a microphone at five in the morning praising him: everyone woken up will curse him Lashon hara said as a joke , lightheartedly rather than from hatred כמתלהלה הירה זקים חצים ומות… ואמר הלא משחק אני , like someone shooting arrows who kills a person and says \"I was only playing\" (Mishlei 26:18–19) Saying something \"innocently,\" as if you didn't know it was negative – This fits the definition: real lashon hara is deliberate, manipulative editing . Joking or careless remarks aren't \"the real thing,\" but they're still a sin. \"We get told as children what lashon hara is, but we're not told this distinction.\" Miriam and Aharon spoke about Moshe behind his back. In the Rambam's terms, the Rabbi suggested, that was probably avak lashon hara , not real lashon hara. It makes no difference whether it's said in front of the person or not. It's about the way you see the world, not just the person's embarrassment. Part 6: Harmful Information, and \"Before Three\" (Halacha 5) Speech that causes damage is also lashon hara , even if it isn't negative. The Chafetz Chaim cites this as the source for not revealing a secret. For example, someone is about to open a business and wants it kept quiet for a month. If you spread it, a competitor might open first and cause him a loss. ואם נאמרו דברים אלו בפני שלשה, כבר נשמע הדבר ונודע… אין בו משום לשון הרע, והוא שלא יתכוין להעביר הקול ולגלותו יותר If these things were said before three people, the matter is already known… repeating it is not lashon hara, provided he does not intend to spread it further. As Benjamin Franklin put it: \"Three may keep a secret, if two of them are dead.\" Why the condition? If you intend to spread it, it may no longer be lashon hara, but it is still rechilut : going around peddling information. The Chafetz Chaim adds that even when it's technically permitted, \"I don't advise it.\" It's generally not good to talk about people. Example: assume, for argument's sake, that saying someone got divorced is lashon hara (some say it isn't). Once it's known before three, mentioning it in passing is technically permitted. Going around saying \"Did you hear?\" to make a story of it is rechilut. Newspapers: what's in the paper generally won't be lashon hara, but repeating it to damage the person further would be rechilut at the very least. Don't sit with them (Halacha 6) כל אלו בעלי לשון הרע הן, ואסור לדור בשכונתן, וכל שכן לישב עמהם ולשמוע דבריהם All of these are speakers of lashon hara; it is forbidden to live in their neighbourhood, and certainly to sit with them and listen. Don't go to lunch with someone you know always speaks lashon hara. Even if he doesn't intend to, it will happen. The decree on our ancestors in the desert, forty years of wandering, was sealed only because of lashon hara , the report of the spies. \"If the only thing we take from this shiur is not to be one of those people who always has a juicy story to tell, it will have been worth it. That's a very sad kind of person to be.\" From the class: how rechilut becomes lashon hara Becky asked whether rechilut turns into lashon hara as a story passes along and grows. The Rabbi's example: \"I saw him going into McDonald's.\" That is pure rechilut , just passing on what you saw. The next person says: \"He told me he saw him eating there.\" That person has edited the information. That is lashon hara . Someone who just repeats \"I heard from X that he saw him go in\" is continuing the chain of rechilut. The first person may have caused the next to speak lashon hara, which could be avak lashon hara , but he didn't commit the terrible sin himself. Part 7: Revenge and Bearing a Grudge (Halachot 7–8) לא תקם ולא תטר את בני עמך… אני ה' Do not take revenge and do not bear a grudge against the members of your people… I am Hashem (Vayikra 19:18). Example (from the Rambam) What's wrong נקימה – revenge Your friend refused to lend you his axe. Next day he asks for your sickle, and you refuse: \"You didn't lend me yours.\" Acting on the grudge נטירה – bearing a grudge You lend it, but say: \"Here, I'm not like you, who wouldn't lend me.\" Keeping it in your heart, even without acting The Rambam is stringent. Most Rishonim understood the prohibition of revenge to apply only to financial matters , since that is how the Gemara always discusses it. On their view, insulting someone who insulted you isn't the Torah's prohibition of revenge. The Rambam rejects this: it applies to anything that happens to you. \"If you're a clever person, you recognise it's not worth it.\" Why forbid the grudge too? As long as you keep it, you may come to take revenge. So the Torah says to wipe it from your heart. In the Rambam's words, this is the right attitude, through which society and people's dealings with one another can continue to exist. Yosef: the monotheist who couldn't hate After Yaakov died, the brothers feared Yosef would finally take revenge. They told him their father had instructed, before he died, that Yosef should forgive them. The Rabbi noted that the brothers lied here: from this we learn that one may say something untrue when afraid for one's life. \"It's strange that Yaakov would have told the brothers to tell Yosef that. Maybe Yosef knew they were lying.\" Yosef's response was magnanimous: \"Am I in place of God? You intended evil against me, but God intended it for good\" (Bereishit 50:19–20). A person who lives with yichud Hashem , the unity of God, cannot harbour hatred. We say שמע ישראל ה' אלהינו ה' אחד several times every day because \"deep down, we are all dualists.\" We half-believe there are other powers in the world that shape our lives. When something bad happens, there are two possible responses: \"Who did this to me? Let me go and sort him out.\" \"This has happened. It's part of Hashem's will and plan.\" Yosef was a pure monotheist . He firmly believed there was no other power in the world, so he had nothing to avenge. \"Easier said than done, no question about it.\" Closing the Series \"Exactly on time for nine o'clock, the end of Chapter 7 and the end of Hilchot Deot.\" The series began after Sukkot. \"Go over it again. Read the text again, listen again. I believe these seven chapters can change a person's perspective on life. It's not just about knowing what we've learned; it's about implementing it. The more you learn it, the more it becomes part of us.\" Summary Rebuke privately and gently. It's the only mitzvah with a built-in warning not to sin while doing it. Embarrassing someone in public forfeits one's share in the World to Come. Orphans get special treatment, even in the classroom, and the status has no fixed age. Rechilut = walking information around. Lashon hara = sitting and editing true information to show someone in the worst light, which is far worse. Motzi shem ra = lies. The root of lashon hara is a bad eye. Fix the perception, through Torah, and the tongue follows. Lashon hara equals the three cardinal sins and is like denying God, because it rejects any reality outside one's own words. The listener who accepts it is harmed most. The dust of lashon hara: insinuation, provocative praise, joking and feigned innocence. Before three it's public, but spreading it further is still rechilut . Revenge and grudges are forbidden in all matters (the Rambam, against most Rishonim). Yosef shows that true belief in yichud Hashem leaves no room for hatred. Coming Up Next Tuesday: a break. 2 and 9 February 2021, 8:00pm: in conversation with Rabbi Joseph Dweck on Rabbi Sacks' The Great Partnership (science and Torah) and Not in God's Name .","N5RLTlN1sw4":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Deot (Rambam's Laws of Ethics) Chapter: 6, Halachot 1–7 Shiur: Part 6 of the seven-part series (\"our penultimate chapter\"), given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom and Facebook, Tuesday 12 January 2021 These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. The recording stops partway through Halacha 7, so the end of the shiur is not included. Introduction: From the Self to Society The first five chapters were all about the individual: Chapter Topic 1–2 Carving out the middle path for yourself; avoiding extremes 3 Enjoying the world around us, for the right purpose 4 The Rambam's diet 5 How to become a wise person (the traits of the Hacham, covered the previous week) 6 How we relate to other people and to society, including people we don't get along with 7 Next week: the laws of slander and gossip, lashon hara and rechilut Announcements: - In a couple of weeks, on 2 and 9 February, the Rabbi would be in conversation with Rabbi Joseph Dweck on two of Rabbi Sacks' books, The Great Partnership and Not in God's Name . Details would go on the WhatsApp group. - A new daily halacha WhatsApp group had started, learning the laws of kibbud av va'em . People could ask in the chat to join. Part 1: You Become Your Company (Halacha 1) דרך ברייתו של אדם להיות נמשך בדעותיו ובמעשיו אחר רעיו וחביריו ונוהג כמנהג אנשי מדינתו It is human nature to be drawn, in one's views and actions, after one's friends and companions, and to follow the customs of the people of one's country. לפיכך צריך אדם להתחבר לצדיקים ולישב אצל החכמים תמיד כדי שילמוד ממעשיהם, ויתרחק מן הרשעים ההולכים בחשך Therefore a person must connect himself with the righteous and sit constantly with the wise, to learn from their deeds, and distance himself from the wicked who walk in darkness. Two different verbs Verb Meaning צדיקים – the righteous להתחבר , to connect Have good friends who are good people חכמים – the wise לישב אצל , to sit beside You can't just be friends with them. You have to sit with them, learn from them and go to them The tsaddik is righteous but not necessarily learned. The Hacham is \"the complete picture,\" the one who has the wisdom, so you have to sit with him. הולך את חכמים יחכם ורועה כסילים ירוע : whoever walks with the wise becomes wise; whoever keeps company with fools will suffer (Mishlei 13:20). אשרי האיש אשר לא הלך בעצת רשעים : David HaMelech opens Tehillim with this \"sociological truth.\" Praiseworthy is the one who did not walk in the counsel of the wicked, stand in the way of sinners, or sit in the seat of scoffers. Who you spend time with defines who you are. The Torah doesn't deny your nature The Torah could simply have said, \"If you're among such people, don't copy them.\" Instead it recognises that it's not possible to be among them without being influenced. We are social beings, influenced by people's actions and their ideas. (Even in a year of lockdowns, we're still influenced by the media and our daily interactions.) The Midrash's analogy: spend enough time in a perfumery and you come out smelling nice; spend it in a tannery and you come out smelling bad. Two security guards. Two people take the same course and qualify together. One spends 30 years guarding a high-security prison; the other spends 30 years in an art museum. \"Without a shadow of doubt, at 50 they will be very different people,\" because of what each heard and saw every day. The Stanford Prison Experiment. Volunteers playing guards and prisoners were so quickly influenced by their roles, the guards acting sadistically, that the planned two-week experiment was called off after six days. When we do make time to examine our lives, we look at what we've done right and wrong. We rarely examine our friends and communities. The worst outcome is that we stop thinking altogether and just follow the people around us. \"The power of the aggregate can be very strong.\" \"As in our times\": what if everywhere is bad? ואם היו כל המדינות שהוא יודען ושומע שמועתן נוהגים בדרך לא טובה כמו זמנינו… ישב לבדו יחידי… ואם היו רעים וחטאים שאין מניחים אותו… יצא למערות ולחוחים ולמדברות If all the countries he knows of behave badly, as in our times … he should sit alone… and if they won't let him be unless he joins them, he should go out to the caves, the thickets and the deserts. If your friends are bad, find other company. If the whole town is bad, move to a better town. If everywhere is like that, or you can't travel (because of war or illness), become a loner: ישב בדד וידום , \"let him sit alone and be silent\" (Eichah 3:28). If even that isn't allowed, go to the desert. מי יתנני במדבר מלון אורחים , \"If only I were in the desert, in a travellers' lodge\" (Yirmiyahu 9:1). \"Pretty harsh words,\" clearly hyperbolic, but striking: 850 years ago the Rambam said that wherever he looked, there was a problem. So what did the Rambam do? He lived a fairly secluded life. He never took a public position. He wasn't a communal rabbi or head of a Beth Din. He earned his living as a doctor, and the rest of the time he studied and wrote. We are social beings and need to mix with people. The lesson is: never underestimate the impact of those around you on your life and your children's lives. Part 2: Cleave to the Hachamim (Halacha 2) מצות עשה להדבק בחכמים ותלמידיהם כדי ללמוד ממעשיהם, כענין שנאמר ובו תדבק It is a positive commandment to cleave to the wise and their students, in order to learn from their deeds, as it says: \"and to Him you shall cleave.\" Why say it twice? Halacha 1 already told us to keep good company. This is a pattern in the Rambam: First the moral and psychological truth. This is how human beings work. Then the religious duty. And by the way, it's also one of the 613 mitzvot, like tefillin. He did the same at the start of the series. He laid out the psychology of the middle path, and only then said it is a mitzvah of the Torah to walk in the ways of Hashem. For the Rambam, the Torah isn't a document superimposed on life. It is a blueprint for life , flowing from the reality of the world, that lets you live in sync with your deepest nature. Who is the Hacham? The Rabbi asked the class this question. The answer is Chapter 5 , last week's shiur. \"That's why everything in the Rambam is in order.\" The whole of the last chapter defines the Hacham; this one says to cleave to people who meet those criteria. They are very hard to find today, so you have to really seek out a real Hacham. לפיכך צריך אדם להשתדל שישא בת תלמיד חכם… ולאכול ולשתות עם תלמידי חכמים, ולעשות פרקמטיא לתלמיד חכם, ולהתחבר להן בכל מיני חבור… והוי מתאבק בעפר רגליהם ושותה בצמא את דבריהם One should try to marry the daughter of a Torah scholar… to eat and drink with scholars, do business on their behalf, and connect with them in every way… \"Sit in the dust of their feet and drink their words thirstily\" (Avot 1:4). For the Rambam this isn't about helping the Hacham. It's for your own benefit. Through marriage, meals and business you'll be in the right company, and as Halacha 1 taught, you'll come to behave the same way. Spot the difference The source is the Gemara (Ketubot 111b): The Gemara lists… Does the Rambam include it? Marrying one's daughter to a Torah scholar Yes Doing business on behalf of Torah scholars Yes Benefiting Torah scholars from one's property No – deliberately omitted The Gemara says that whoever does these is considered as if he cleaves to the Shechinah. The class first guessed eating and drinking or learning, but those are in the Rambam. What's missing is supporting the scholar with money. This is consistent with the Rambam's strong view that a Hacham may not benefit materially from Torah (Hilchot Talmud Torah 3:10): Whoever decides to study Torah without working, and to live from charity, desecrates God's name, dishonours the Torah, extinguishes the light of faith, brings evil on himself and forfeits his life in the World to Come… Any Torah not accompanied by work will in the end be negated and lead to sin, and such a person will end up stealing from others. \"Extremely sharp words.\" The Rambam couldn't include giving money to the Hacham as a requirement, because he held that the Hacham must be self-sufficient. From the class: rabbis' salaries Flora asked about the professionalisation of the rabbinate, which she placed around Rabbi Shimon ben Tzemach Duran (the Tashbetz, late 14th to early 15th century). She noted that the Rambam is drawing on Pirkei Avot 4 (\"don't make the Torah a crown to magnify yourself, or a spade to dig with\"), and that the Tashbetz clearly disagreed with him. The Rabbi agreed that the Tashbetz disagreed. Do rabbis take salaries? Yes, for several reasons: The Noda BiYehudah (Rabbi Yechezkel Landau, about 250 years ago): a rabbi may be paid שכר בטלה , compensation for the income he gives up by serving the community instead of working in another profession. More simply, the role has changed. The Rambam is criticising people who only sit and study, serve the community in no way, and rely on communal funds. Today a rabbi's role includes pastoral work, emotional support and much more. \"Sometimes they end up being psychotherapists.\" Even the Rambam would agree it's fine to charge for that. What's problematic is charging specifically for Torah, for a shiur. Part 3: Love Every Jew (Halacha 3) מצוה על כל אדם לאהוב את כל אחד ואחד מישראל כגופו, שנאמר ואהבת לרעך כמוך. לפיכך צריך לספר בשבחו ולחוס על ממונו כאשר הוא חס על ממון עצמו ורוצה בכבוד עצמו It is a mitzvah for every person to love every single Jew as himself, as it says: \"love your fellow as yourself.\" Therefore one must speak in his praise and care for his money as he cares for his own, and for his honour as he wants his own honour. Even the wicked? From the class Flora asked whether a commentator says you don't have to love someone wicked. The Rabbi answered from the Rambam's 13 Principles of Faith , which the class had studied earlier: A person who believes in all these principles enters the category of Israel, and it is a mitzvah to love him and have mercy on him… even if he commits sins out of desire and the overpowering of his base nature. He is punished according to his sins, but he still has a share in the World to Come and is counted among the sinners of Israel. But if one of these principles is compromised, he leaves the category of Israel. A rasha who believes is still part of Am Yisrael. He has broken Shabbat or done this and that, but he counts for a minyan, his shechitah is valid, and there is a mitzvah to love him. Someone who denies the principles of faith is no longer included, and there is no longer a duty to love him. The Rabbi warned against misreading this Rambam as \"some kind of liberal\" statement that \"you love everyone, it doesn't matter.\" The duty is to love people who are not attacking our beliefs. Edwin asked: what if someone does something bad to you, do you still love him? The Rabbi said the chapter answers that shortly (see Part 5). What about non-Jews? Why is the mitzvah focused on fellow Jews? Because love needs a focus. \"You can't just say 'love every single person.' We are in a community; we are one people.\" The obligation and the imperative point to fellow Jews, but that doesn't exclude loving non-Jews. Compare other verses that say רעך (\"your fellow\"), such as don't covet your fellow's wife and don't bear false witness against your fellow . Nobody thinks this permits coveting a non-Jew's wife or bearing false witness against a non-Jew. The verse tells us where the obligation is focused; it doesn't exclude other forms of love. You can't command a feeling, so act it into being Why does the Rambam explain love through speaking well of people and caring for their money ? Because you can't simply switch on an emotion. \"I can't tell myself, 'I'm going to love every Jew now.' Life isn't that simple.\" The electricity example. When you leave your house, you switch off the lights and heating because you don't want to waste money. If someone lends you their house, or you're in a communal building, you do the same, because you care about their money as much as your own. When you live with this awareness, speaking positively about people and caring for their property like your own, it generates real love and care inside you. It takes work. Part 4: Loving the Convert (Halacha 4) אהבת הגר שבא ונכנס תחת כנפי השכינה שתי מצות עשה: אחת מפני שהוא בכלל ריעים, ואחת מפני שהוא גר, והתורה אמרה ואהבתם את הגר Loving a convert who has come under the wings of the Shechinah involves two positive mitzvot: one because he is included among \"your fellows,\" and one because he is a convert, and the Torah said: \"you shall love the convert.\" Once someone converts, they are a fully fledged Jew , with an additional mitzvah on top. Just as we are commanded to love God ( ואהבת את ה' אלהיך ), we are commanded to love the convert. Hashem Himself loves the convert ( ואוהב גר , Devarim 10:18). The Rambam's letter to Ovadiah the convert. The Rabbi attributed it to the Iggeret HaShmad / Kiddush Hashem (it is usually known as a separate letter to Ovadiah). The Rambam wrote that Jews trace their lineage to Avraham, whereas you trace your lineage to God, and are therefore more dear to Him. Converts have \"joined the club, and they're fully part of the club.\" We must love them at the very least as much as any other Jew. It's a double imperative. Part 5: Hatred in the Heart (Halacha 5) כל השונא אדם מישראל בלבו עובר בלא תעשה, שנאמר לא תשנא את אחיך בלבבך… ולא הזהירה תורה אלא על שנאה שבלב, אבל המכה את חבירו והמחרפו, אף על פי שאינו רשאי, אינו עובר משום לא תשנא Whoever hates a Jew in his heart transgresses a negative commandment: \"Do not hate your brother in your heart.\"… The Torah warned only about hatred in the heart . One who strikes or curses his fellow, though forbidden to do so, does not transgress \"do not hate.\" Same severity as eating non-kosher food. Both are negative commandments. The only difference is that there are no lashes here, because lashes apply only to a prohibition involving an action, and this is in the heart. \"There's a deep psychological point here.\" The Rambam puts it even more clearly in Sefer HaMitzvot : when you tell your friend \"I'm upset with you,\" you do not transgress this prohibition. You might transgress other things, but not this one. Hatred stored in the heart is a very great sin, worse than many others. \"Listen to that last line and remember it for life.\" If you want to hate him, go and tell him. That is better than walking around smiling while inside you think I hate this guy. A modern idea from an ancient source Today we're used to \"talk it over,\" but that's quite modern. \"I don't know about Ashkenazim, but in Sephardi circles, the older generations of parents and grandparents didn't like to talk. People liked to brush things under the carpet.\" Sitting down over a tea to sort out differences is a new thing, yet the Torah said hundreds of years ago that the greatest problem is guarding hatred in your heart. We also know today that it has terrible consequences for physical and mental health. Part 6: The Real Mitzvah of Rebuke (Halacha 6) כשיחטא איש לאיש לא ישטמנו וישתוק… אלא מצוה עליו להודיעו ולומר לו: למה עשית לי כך וכך? ולמה חטאת לי בדבר פלוני? שנאמר הוכח תוכיח את עמיתך When someone wrongs another, he should not hate him and keep silent… rather, it is a mitzvah to tell him: \"Why did you do this to me? Why did you wrong me in this matter?\", as it says: \"You shall surely rebuke your fellow.\" What not to do: Avshalom and Amnon. After Amnon violated his half-sister Tamar, Avshalom was furious and said nothing to him at all, \"neither bad nor good, because Avshalom hated Amnon\" (Shmuel II 13:22). Two years later he had him killed. \"It only dawned on me today\" The Rabbi shared an insight he had while preparing the shiur. The usual idea of tochachah What the verse is mainly about You see someone desecrating Shabbat, or talking in shul. If you're in a position to influence him, staying silent is tacit approval, so you must correct him. Look at the context: \"Do not hate your brother in your heart; you shall surely rebuke your fellow\" (Vayikra 19:17). A real mitzvah, but secondary , derived from this verse. The primary mitzvah is interpersonal. Someone wronged you; don't store it, go and talk to them. Someone spoke badly about you or embarrassed you in public. You can ignore them for the next two years and get upset every time you see them. Or you can go to them, constructively and without shouting, and say: \"I want to tell you something. I'm upset about what you did to me.\" Then not only have you removed the hatred from your heart, you've fulfilled a positive mitzvah. \"That's the beauty of the Rambam: he tells you things that you then realise are so obvious.\" Back to Edwin's question. When someone wrongs us, if we do nothing we violate several commandments: we no longer love him, and worse, we hate him. What we need to do is try to have a conversation with him. Part 7: Rebuke Only Your Friend , and from Love (Halacha 7, beginning) הרואה חבירו שחטא או שהלך בדרך לא טובה, מצוה להחזירו למוטב ולהודיעו שהוא חוטא על עצמו במעשיו הרעים One who sees his friend sinning or going on a bad path has a mitzvah to bring him back to the good and let him know he is harming himself through his bad deeds. Read every word. Until now the Rambam spoke of ish le-ish , one person and another. Whenever he speaks about rebuke, he says חבירו , his friend. Don't try to rebuke people who aren't your friends. It won't work. You can't expect someone to listen without a close relationship. Why rebuke so often backfires: the person doesn't feel it comes from love. \"If it comes from a place of love, people like to hear rebuke. Any normal person wants to grow: tell me more, please.\" Not like this: people who go around telling strangers off thinking they're doing kiruv , or who stop someone in the middle of the street to force a mitzvah on them. \"That's not the way we operate.\" You can't walk into a shul you've attended for a day or two and tell people their tefillin are on wrong or they talk too much. They'll inevitably reply: \"Who are you? I've known you for a few hours and you're telling me off already?\" Work within your own circle, your friends, community and the people you work with. It must come from חברות , friendship and love. Shouting at someone isn't coming from love either. המוכיח את חבירו, בין בדברים שבינו לבינו בין בדברים שבינו לבין המקום, צריך להוכיחו בינו לבין עצמו… One who rebukes his friend, whether about matters between them or between him and God, must rebuke him privately… The recording ends here. Summary We become our company. Choose friends among the righteous and sit with the wise. The Torah doesn't pretend we can resist influence. Cleaving to the Hachamim is a positive mitzvah. The Hacham is defined in Chapter 5. The Rambam deliberately omits supporting scholars financially, consistent with his view that Torah must not be a livelihood. Love every Jew who holds the principles of faith, even a sinner. Love grows from actions: speaking well of people and guarding their property and honour. The convert is owed a double love. Hatred in the heart is a negative commandment, and \"worse than many other sins.\" Saying \"I'm upset with you\" is better than silent resentment. The primary mitzvah of tochachah is to talk to the person who wronged you, constructively. Correcting others' religious conduct is derived from it, and works only between friends and from love. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 7, the final chapter: the laws of slander and gossip, lashon hara and rechilut . 2 and 9 February 2021: in conversation with Rabbi Joseph Dweck on Rabbi Sacks' The Great Partnership and Not in God's Name .","EVdqd4ypHa8":"Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Deot (Rambam's Laws of Ethics) Chapter: 4 (complete) Shiur: Part 5 of the seven-part series, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom and Facebook, Tuesday 24 November 2020 Dedications: Le'ilui nishmat Davida bat Chaim Alexander, Barbara Feldman's mother, and le'ilui nishmat Chana bat Kamil These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap Last week (Chapter 3): a person must not cut himself off from the world or keep fasting. Eat well, live a regular life, and do it for the right motives. Eating, drinking and sleeping for the right purpose are all part of avodat Hashem. This week, the Rambam explains how to live a healthy life: what to eat and drink, your schedule, when and how to sleep. \"This is a very rare and intriguing chapter. We don't get this from other rabbis.\" Part 1: Introduction – Five Points Before We Begin הואיל והיות הגוף בריא ושלם מדרכי השם הוא, שהרי אי אפשר שיבין או ידע דבר מידיעת הבורא והוא חולה, לפיכך צריך להרחיק אדם עצמו מדברים המאבדין את הגוף ולהנהיג עצמו בדברים המברין והמחלימים Since keeping the body healthy and whole is among the ways of God, because one cannot understand or know anything of the Creator while ill, a person must distance himself from what destroys the body and accustom himself to what heals and strengthens it. 1. This is halacha, not advice As the Rabbi said in the first shiur, the Rambam is paskening here. It is no different from how to wear tefillin or tzitzit. \"You can't eat unhealthy food and say, 'I'm a good Jew, I'm just not being good to my body.'\" Health isn't merely a physical necessity; it's an indispensable part of Torah. According to the Rambam, it is assur to be unhealthy. If a person is able to lose weight and keeps overeating, he is violating a prohibition like any other. 2. A guarantee At the end of the chapter, the Rambam, \"one of the greatest rabbis and one of the greatest doctors we've ever had,\" guarantees that whoever follows it will not fall ill. 3. Holistic and preventative The Rambam doesn't tell you what to do if you have a particular illness. He gives a whole schedule, from waking to sleeping, to prevent illness. If you are already ill, he says, go to a doctor. 4. Why so much detail? Why not simply say \"look after your health and see your local doctor\"? The Rabbi compared the end of Hilchot Kiddush HaChodesh , one of the hardest sections of the Mishneh Torah, with its chapters of astronomical calculation. There the Rambam explains that he set it all out so that people of understanding would lack nothing, and would not need to go searching for it in other books. The same applies here. Medical knowledge belongs in the book of halacha, all in one place. 5. Where does it come from, and does it still apply? Question The Rabbi's answer Is this from the Gemara or from medicine? Mostly from the medicine of his time. Commentators try to source every line in the Talmud, but many statements have no source, and some contradict the Gemara. How can the Rambam contradict the Gemara? The Kesef Mishneh (Rabbi Yosef Karo) asks this about the laws of bloodletting. He answers that the Gemara spoke of a different climate, country and time. The Rambam is telling us what is correct for his day. Is it a problem that he learned from non-Jews (his medical teacher was Galen )? No. In Kiddush HaChodesh the Rambam says the astronomy came from Greek books, but since it is proven, \"we don't rely on the authority of the person who said it, but on the proofs.\" Accept the truth from whoever says it. What about statements that are medically wrong today? That doesn't contradict the eternity of halacha. (a) The Rambam drew on the medicine of his time, and medicine develops. \"Had the Rambam been around today, he would have crossed out and added to Chapter 4.\" (b) Halacha already rules that the Gemara's medical remedies may not be relied on today, whether because nature has changed or knowledge has. Relying on them and failing would bring the Sages into disrepute. \"The vast majority of what he says is still true today, and it's remarkable that someone 800 years ago wrote this with such clarity.\" Part 2: Eating and Drinking Eat only when hungry לעולם לא יאכל אדם אלא כשהוא רעב ולא ישתה אלא כשהוא צמא, ואל ישהה נקביו אפילו רגע אחד Never eat unless hungry, nor drink unless thirsty, and never hold back from relieving yourself, even for a moment. We eat to live; we don't live to eat. Don't eat because you're bored, anxious, depressed or have nothing else to do. Drinking only when thirsty. The Rabbi noted that, as far as he knows, today it is well established that you should drink even when not thirsty, to avoid dehydration. This is one place where medicine has moved on. Someone in the class made the same point, and he agreed. Never delay going to the toilet. Always err on the side of caution. The Rambam develops this later. Stop at three-quarters לא יאכל אדם עד שתתמלא כריסו אלא יפחות כמו רביע משבעתו Don't eat until your stomach is full; stop about a quarter short of being satisfied. It takes sensitivity to know when you've reached three-quarters, but the point is: never eat until completely full. Water with meals Drink only a little water during the meal, diluted with wine if needed. Drink more once the food begins to digest. Today too, the Rabbi said, the advice is to drink before or after a meal rather than during it. Exercise before eating: the morning routine כללו של דבר יגע גופו ויתיגע בכל בוקר עד שיתחיל גופו לחום, וישקוט מעט עד שתתיישב נפשו, ויאכל The rule: exert and tire the body every morning until it begins to warm, rest a little until you settle, and then eat. \"I'm not putting words in the Rambam's mouth. He tells you clearly.\" The ideal day: Wake up early. Exercise until your body is warm. This is what \"afflict your body\" means: the process hurts, but you're happy afterwards. Have a hot shower. Rest until your breath settles. Then eat breakfast. Don't sit down to a main meal straight after three hours of driving, because your body isn't warm enough to digest properly. How to eat Sit in one place, leaning slightly to the left. Don't eat while walking, or while riding (today, driving). No exercise after eating. \"Those who have supper and then go for a swim: for the Rambam, a very bad idea.\" The Rabbi said his father always said the same. Exercise in the morning, never after supper, and not late at night on a treadmill when the body is tired. \"Better not to exercise at all than to exercise after eating.\" Part 3: Sleep היום והלילה ארבע ועשרים שעות, דיו לאדם שישן שלישן שהוא שמנה שעות Day and night are 24 hours; it is enough for a person to sleep a third of them, eight hours, ending shortly before sunrise. \"Enough.\" The word דיו implies a maximum: you don't need more than eight hours. The Ben Ish Chai says between six and eight hours: not more than eight, like the Rambam, and not less than six, which is unhealthy. Doesn't the Rambam also say to give the nights to Torah? In Hilchot Talmud Torah 3:13 he writes that a person acquires most of his wisdom at night, and should not waste his nights on sleep, food or talk. There is no contradiction. That refers to the waking hours of the night that people usually waste. With only a candle it was harder to learn, so the night needed protecting. A personal story. At Gateshead Yeshiva, the young, enthusiastic Rabbi Kada planned to cut his sleep to learn more. The Rosh Yeshiva, Rabbi Gurwicz, told him: \"You're not cleverer than the Rambam. The Rambam told you eight hours. Sleep eight hours.\" Don't try to be clever by cutting sleep. It will catch up with you. How to lie Instruction The Rambam's reason Note Not on your back or front, but on your side – The Gemara gives a reason of tseniut Left side at the start of the night, right side at the end Digestion: food is still being digested early in the night Kabbalah says the same for a completely different reason: the start of the night is din , the end is rachamim Don't go to sleep soon after eating The food needs to digest \"People who eat far more than they need at a dinner and go to bed half an hour later are asking for trouble.\" Part 4: What to Eat, and in What Order Laxative foods before the meal: grapes, figs, mulberries, pears, melons, cucumbers and the like. Eat them first, and the main meal a little later. \"This is very true today.\" Binding foods after the meal: pomegranates, quinces, apples, a type of pear. Light before heavy: poultry before meat, eggs before poultry, small animals before large. Don't put pressure on the digestive system all at once. Seasons: in summer, cooling, lightly spiced food, with vinegar to prevent dehydration. In winter, warming food such as hot soup, and more spice (\"apparently mustard is good\"). Eat less in summer than in winter. Summer intake should be about two-thirds of winter's, on top of stopping at three-quarters full. Unripe fruit should be avoided, and that is still true today. Honey and wine are bad for children and good for the elderly. Foods the Rambam warns against Category Examples The Rabbi's comment Very harmful – never eat Large, old, salted fish; old salted cheese; mushrooms; old salted meat; wine fresh from the press; any food with a bad smell The Rambam dislikes old food. Salt was the preservative of the day. \"I'd imagine he wouldn't like six-month-old freezer food either. Pot Noodles and processed food in a box for months would be poison for the Rambam, and we know today they are.\" Harmful – rarely Large fish, milk more than 24 hours old, lentils, refined white bread, cabbage, onion, garlic, mustard, radish \"White bread isn't good.\" Not great Water fowl, bread kneaded with oil, and so on \"I don't know how true all of this is today.\" Part 5: The Great Principle – Loose Bowels ודבר זה כלל גדול ברפואה: כל זמן שהרעי נמנע או יוצא בקושי, חלאים רעים באים This is a great principle in medicine: whenever the bowels are blocked or move with difficulty, serious illnesses come. Always keep the bowels loose, and if anything, err towards that extreme. Regular constipation is a sign that illness is on its way. The remedy for constipation: Young people: in the morning, salted vegetables or legumes with olive oil, or the broth of spinach or beets with oil and salt, without bread , which blocks you. Olive oil is a well-known laxative, even today. Older people: every morning, hot water with honey (essentially a tea), then don't eat for about four hours. The Rabbi said he does something similar himself: only teas for a good few hours in the morning. \"It definitely works for me.\" From the class: Is breakfast forbidden? No. The four-hour morning fast is only for someone suffering from constipation, for a few days until things are loose again. What applies to everyone is not eating until after you've exercised and warmed up. The three principles כל זמן שאדם מתעמל ויגע הרבה, ואינו שבע, ומעיו רכין, אין חולי בא עליו… ואפילו אוכל מאכלות רעים As long as a person exercises and exerts himself a great deal, does not eat to satiety, and his bowels are soft, no illness will come upon him… even if he eats bad foods. Do these three… …and you'll be healthy even with bad food Neglect them… …and you'll suffer even with good food 1. Exercise and exertion Sitting comfortably, no exercise Pains and weakness 2. Never eat to the full Overeating, even healthy food \"Like poison,\" the main cause of illness 3. Loose bowels Regular constipation Illness is coming ואכילה גסה לגוף כל אדם כמו סם המות, והוא עיקר לכל החלאים Overeating is like poison to the body, and it is the root of all illness. שומר פיו ולשונו שומר מצרות נפשו (Mishlei 21:23): Whoever guards his mouth and tongue guards his soul from troubles. Plainly this means not speaking lashon hara, which comes in a later chapter. The Rambam reads it here as guarding your mouth from eating when you don't need to. Part 6: Bathing, Bloodletting and Relations The weekly bathhouse יכנס אדם למרחץ משבעה ימים לשבעה ימים A person should go to the bathhouse once every seven days. The bathhouse wasn't jumping in the shower. It was a three- or four-hour affair. That is why the Gemara forbids going to the bathhouse before praying Mincha: you could easily miss the time. Don't go straight after eating, or when hungry. First perspire: exert yourself and sweat out \"all the bad stuff.\" Then wash with hot, then lukewarm, then cold water. Don't stay too long. Relieve yourself before and after. Dress fully before going outside, even in summer. They had a room for this outside the bathhouse. \"Especially now in the winter.\" Afterwards, rest. Don't go straight to work or exercise. A short sleep is great, and then eat. Don't drink water in the bathhouse. \"Basically this is today's sauna, followed by a cold shower. I don't know who's got the time for all of this, and with his schedule I don't know if the Rambam did either. Maybe once a week.\" Bloodletting and relations Bloodletting , very common medicine in those days, should not be done too often. Relations: the life force should be used sparingly. אל תתן לנשים חילך (\"Don't give your strength to women,\" Mishlei 31:3). The Rambam cites doctors who held that one in a thousand dies of other illnesses and 999 of excessive relations. \"This isn't true today, but it's interesting to see his perspective.\" Part 7: The Guarantee כל המנהיג עצמו בדרכים אלו שהורינו אני ערב לו שאינו בא לידי חולי כל ימיו עד שיזקין הרבה וימות, ואינו צריך לרופא Whoever conducts himself in the ways we have taught, I guarantee that he will not fall ill all his days until he grows very old and dies, and he will not need a doctor. The Rambam is not one of those rabbis who guarantees success for saying something on a particular day at a particular time. Nowhere does he do that. This guarantee is different because for him it is science , at least for his time. The exceptions he lists: a body that was unhealthy from birth; someone who kept bad habits from childhood; or a plague or famine. \"There'll be some sort of pandemic, then I can't guarantee anything,\" the Rabbi noted, speaking in November 2020. \"If you wake up at 80 and want to take the Rambam up on his promise, that's a bit late. But the more we do this, the better.\" Everything here is for a healthy person. Someone already ill must see a doctor and follow their own programme. Never live without a doctor A Torah scholar may not live in a town that lacks ten things. Among them are a doctor , a bathhouse, a toilet, a synagogue and a teacher of children. \"This shows the importance of the medical profession: the NHS, the key workers.\" Summary: The Rambam's Three Main Principles Exercise. Every morning if you can, 20 minutes to half an hour, then shower, relax and eat. Never eat until you're absolutely full. Overeating is the root of most illness. Keep the bowels loose. Also: sleep six to eight hours, on your side; eat light foods before heavy ones; avoid old, preserved and processed food. \"Don't think you can be a religious Jew and not keep these things. This is an essential part of avodat Hashem. When we exercise, we can think that we're doing it for HaKadosh Baruch Hu, because you can't live a life of serving Him with aches across your body.\" From the class Flora: The Rambam wrote a famous letter (to Rabbi Shmuel ibn Tibbon) saying he had no time to eat or sleep. Did he keep these rules? The Rabbi answered that this was towards the end of his life, when he was employed as physician to the court. Earlier he had a much more relaxed life and wrote most of his works then. \"I'm sure he kept up his exercise, 100%. The main thing is not to overeat.\" Coming Up Next Tuesday, 1 December, 8:00pm: a conversation with Rabbi Dr Joshua Berman , \"one of the leading biblical scholars of this generation.\" Not to be missed. The following Tuesday: a shiur on Chanukah. The series then pauses for Chanukah and the winter, and resumes early in the new year with Chapters 5–7. Recordings are on Facebook (no account needed). Shiur dedications are available; please contact the Rabbi.","oFdsxbPJ7mc":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (Rambam), Chelek Aleph — Rabbi Chaim Makbili's Hebrew edition / Sefaria Chapters: I:21 — עבר (to pass), Part 3 (conclusion); I:22 — בא (to come) Topic: The Rambam's fourth interpretation of ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא — adding the word קול , the voice of Hashem passed , which makes ה' ה' a doubled call; the Rambam's summary: \"choose whichever you want\" — so long as you never think God physically passed; Rav Makbili's ranking of the four, from the most intellectual to the most physical; reading the whole passage of Ki Tisa four ways; the four questions answered , with the full comparison chart; the טעמים ; why not to count the Thirteen Attributes on your fingers ; עבירה and המעביר על מדותיו ; and Chapter 22 : the four meanings of בא , and what it means for Hashem to \"come\" Schedule note: The Moreh continues every Tuesday, 8:00–9:00pm. Chapter 21 is now complete; Chapter 22 was learned in the last 15 minutes. Next week: Chapters 23, 24 and (b'ezrat Hashem) 25. Recap: Where We Are Chapter 21 explains the word עבר , in order to explain ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא (Shemot 34:6) — and with it the whole dialogue between Moshe and HaKadosh Baruch Hu in Parashat Ki Tisa . The five meanings of עבר # Meaning Example 1 To physically pass something; movement across space והוא עבר לפניהם 2 To proclaim — a voice or sound spreading, reverberating ויעבירו קול במחנה 3 A prophetic vision passing through a person's imagination ולפיד אש אשר עבר בין הגזרים 4 To overreach — to go beyond the boundaries וכגבר עברו יין 5 To divert — to skip one thing and move to another והוא ירה החצי להעברו The three interpretations so far # Whose reading עבר means What happened 1 The Rambam's own, preferred 5 — diversion Hashem diverted Moshe from God's own face (His essence) to His back ; ויקרא ה' — and Hashem called the Thirteen Attributes, which are the back of God: the way He operates in this world 2 Targum Onkelos, simple reading (2A) 1 — physical passing Onkelos adds a word: a created, physical manifestation — the Shechinah, the kavod — passed Moshe's face; then Hashem called. (This is why people bow their heads at ויעבר — as if the glory is passing over us.) 3 Targum Onkelos, deeper reading (2B) 3 — prophetic vision The glory of Hashem passed in Moshe's mind , through a נבואה ; and Hashem called the attributes in that prophecy So far we have used עבר 5, עבר 1 and עבר 3. Now comes the fourth and final interpretation — which uses עבר 2 . The key to it: the Targum (and Rav Saadia Gaon, as we saw) is content to add words into a verse , especially in the context of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. The Rambam says: my preferred explanation is the first one — עבר as diversion, which needs no added word. But if you want to use the Targum's principle, I'll use it too — and add a different word. Part One — Interpretation 4: \"The Voice of Hashem Passed\" The Rambam's text ומוכרח לפרש באופן של השמטת הנסמך כדרכו של אונקלוס תמיד, שלעתים מפרש שהמלה המושמטת היא כבוד — יקרא, ולעתים שכינה, ולעתים מימרא, כל מקום כפי ענינו — If we are going to follow Onkelos's interpretive method — supplying a missing word, which he does constantly, sometimes יקרא (glory), sometimes שכינה , sometimes מימרא (word), each according to its context — הרי שאנחנו גם כן נפרש, שהנסמך המושמט כאן הוא קול. — then we too will supply a missing word here: not שכינה or כבוד , but קול , voice . ויעבר קול ה' על פניו ויקרא — וכך יש לקרוא. This is how you read the verse: the voice of Hashem passed in the presence of Moshe, and it called — the voice called. וכבר ביארנו את ההשאלה בלשון של העברה ביחס לקול: ויעבירו קול במחנה. לפי זה, הקול הוא אשר קרא. This is meaning 2 of עבר : a proclamation, a reverberation — just as Moshe \"passed a voice\" through the camp, stop bringing donations for the Mishkan . On this reading, the voice of Hashem reverberated over the presence of Moshe — על פניו — and the voice is what called. The reading of the verse — and the double ה' (The Rabbi skipped paragraph 12 to read paragraph 13 first, then came back.) הפירוש לפי הקריאה הזו יהיה אם כן כך: ועבר קול מלפני ה' בנוכחותו של משה, וקרא: ה' ה'. A voice from before Hashem passed — reverberated — in Moshe's presence, and called out: \"ה', ה', אל רחום וחנון...\" וכפילות ה' תהא עבור הקריאה, משום שהוטלה בו קריאה — כמו משה משה, אברהם אברהם. וגם זה פירוש נאה מאוד. This changes how the verse divides. Until now — in interpretations 1, 2 and 3 — ויקרא always went with the next word: ויקרא ה' , and Hashem called . That is why we said last week that ה' ה' does not go together: the first ה' is the one calling, and what He called begins with the second. But now the verse reads ויעבר קול ה' על פניו, ויקרא — and the voice called — so the subject of ויקרא is the voice, and ה' ה' is what it called: a doubled expression , the way one calls out a name twice — משה משה (Shemot 3:4), אברהם אברהם (Bereishit 22:11). \"And this, too, is a very fine interpretation.\" What this shows us: the Rambam understands all the other interpretations without a double Hashem. Only on reading 4 is there a double ה' ה'. And this is the reading reflected in how we say it in shul — on Yom Kippur, and whenever we say the Thirteen Attributes: ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא — ה' ה'... , as if the doubled Name is part of the attributes. (More on this below.) Paragraph 12: a voice that speaks, a voice that calls אל ירחק בעיניך ייחוס הקריאה לקול. Don't think it strange that I attribute calling to a voice — כי במילים האלה עצמן סופר על דברי ה' עם משה: וישמע את הקול מדבר אליו. (Bamidbar 7:89 — the end of Parashat Naso) — the Torah itself describes Hashem's speech with Moshe this way: Moshe heard the voice speaking to him. Just as speech is attributed to the voice, so calling can be attributed to the voice. In Naso the voice speaks ; here in Ki Tisa the voice calls . And another example: קול אמר קרא, ואמר מה אקרא (Yeshayahu 40:6) — a voice says, \"Call!\" and he said, \"What shall I call?\" Again, a voice in the context of calling. A side point for those who love dikduk: מִדַּבֵּר וישמע את הקול מדבר אליו — a very interesting, very peculiar, very rare word. What it looks like An ordinary מְדַבֵּר , a פיעל — speaking What it really is מִדַּבֵּר = מִתְדַּבֵּר , a התפעל — speaking to itself The grammar The ת of the hitpael falls away, and the דגש in the second letter, the ד , replaces the missing ת The meaning (Rashi) God speaks to Himself. He doesn't speak to humans — you listen in This is what the Rambam talks about at length in many places: prophecy is tuning in . There is a beam — a prophecy, a calling, a speaking, a light — coming down constantly, and it is, as it were, talking to itself. It is up to you to come into that beam , to come into that prophecy, to tune in. That is the meaning of מִדַּבֵּר . Part Two — The Rambam Concludes: \"Choose Whichever You Want\" Don't be disturbed אל יהא מגונה בעיניך קיומם של פירושים רבים לעניין הקשה הזה. Calm down. Don't be disturbed that I have now given you four ways to read a single verse in the Torah, each using a different meaning of עבר . Perhaps you'll think, what does this verse mean? Stop confusing me! — No, it shouldn't disturb you. There is nothing wrong with that. ואתה בחר אחת מהסברות. The Rambam gives us permission: whenever you read these verses — every morning in Shacharit, at Mincha, when we read the Torah, on Yom Kippur — you choose one of the four. The four options, in the Rambam's words 1. The Rambam's own reading (עבר 5) אם שיהיה מעמד הגדול זה כולו במראה נבואה בלא ספק, וכל השאיפה של משה — השגה שכלית, שביקש ושנמנעה ממנו, ומה שהשיג — הכל שכלי, בלי שום חוש, כמשפט שבתחילה. The entire great event is a prophetic vision; Moshe's whole desire was a deep intellectual understanding of God — to see His face; that was withheld, but he did attain God's back. Completely intellectual — no physical sense at all. A complete intellectual journey. 2. Onkelos, simple reading (עבר 1) או שיחד עם זאת הייתה השגת חוש הראייה על דבר נברא, שבראייתו מושגת שלמות השגה שכלית — כמשפט אונקלוס. Or, together with that, there was a perception by sight of a created thing : Hashem created a physical manifestation, it passed Moshe, and that is what Moshe saw — a corporeal, physical, created manifestation of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. 3. Onkelos, deeper reading (עבר 3) אלא אם השגת הראייה הזאת הייתה גם היא במראה נבואה, כפי שנאמר אצל אברהם: והנה תנור עשן ולפיד אש אשר עבר בין הגזרים האלה. Or this \"seeing\" was itself in a prophetic vision — like the smoke and fire passing between the pieces at the Brit Bein HaBetarim, which (as we saw) happened in Avraham's mind. The Shechinah passed in Moshe's imagination. 4. The Rambam using Onkelos's principle (עבר 2) או שיחד עם זאת הייתה השגת חוש שמיעה גם כן, וזה הקול אשר עבר על פניו. Or there was a perception by hearing : a created voice reverberated in Moshe's presence, and that voice called ה' ה' . The one thing that matters בחר איזה דעה שתחפוץ — כי כל המטרה היא שלא תחשוב שעבר כפשוטו... כי ה' יתעלה ויתרומם אינו גוף, ולא שייך בו תנועה. Choose whichever you want — \"I don't care.\" The whole purpose is that you should not read ויעבר as meaning 1 applied to God Himself — God actually passing — because Hashem has no body, and movement cannot apply to Him. That is impossible. The Rabbi: if there is one thing we take from this chapter, it is the Rambam's conclusion — there is no physical passing and no physical face, because God has no corporeality. Rav Makbili: the four are a ladder You could say the Rambam simply lists the four in the order he explained them . But Rav Makbili points out something beautiful: the order is a choice offered by level. Level Reading Sense Why it sits here Highest 1 — The Rambam's own Intellect only The most non-corporeal: a complete dialogue in Moshe's mind about trying to understand God. The best of the four — clearly the Rambam's preference Lowest 2 — Onkelos, simple Sight of a created glory If the first is too hard for you, I'll bring you \"all the way back down\": the most fleshed-out, most corporeal reading — an actual, created glory of God passing Moshe. \"I don't want to say it's the worst — but it's the most physical one.\" In between 3 — Onkelos, prophetic Prophetic vision You've gone beyond understanding things physically, but you're not yet ready for reading 1: the Shechinah passing in Moshe's mind In between 4 — The voice Hearing Also for one who has moved beyond the corporeal: a voice is very subtle , much more subtle than sight — it's not so physical \"How beautiful!\" — Extreme one is reading 1; extreme two is reading 2; and 3 and 4 are for the person in between. Part Three — Reading Ki Tisa Four Ways The Rabbi then went through the verses quickly according to all four interpretations . (\"Follow with me.\") The common opening: ויאמר: הראני נא את כבדך — Moshe asks, \"Show me Your glory, Your face.\" Hashem says: you can't see My face; no human can. הנה מקום אתי, ונצבת על הצור — if you want intellectual standing, מקום , constantly think about God, the origin of all. The passage, reading by reading Verse 1 — Rambam (diversion) 2 — Onkelos simple (physical) 3 — Onkelos deeper (prophecy) 4 — Rambam / voice (proclamation) והיה בעבר כבדי (33:22) When I Myself ( כבודי = God Himself) divert you from My face to My back When My created, physical, manifested glory passes you When My glory passes in your mind — when you experience the prophetic vision When My voice passes you ושמתיך בנקרת הצור, ושכתי כפי עליך עד עברי I will give you intellectual protection throughout the diversion, so you don't get intellectually burnt — die or go mad, like the four who entered the Pardes A physical guarding: not literally God's palm, but I'll protect you from the created light — it's very scary, even though it isn't God Himself I will guard you so you don't get burnt while experiencing the prophetic vision It's a scary voice, a strong voice — God's voice — so I'll cover you with My metaphorical palm והסרתי את כפי, וראית את אחרי Then you will see My back — My attributes, the way I act You'll see the back of the glory — \"My back,\" as it were — but never My face, God Himself — — וירד ה' בענן, ויתיצב עמו שם (34:5) Hashem rests His presence on the \"thickness\" of the human Moshe ( ענן — see Chapter 22), in permanent dialogue — — — ויעבר ה' על פניו (34:6) God diverted Moshe from God's face to God's back God's created glory passed Moshe's presence The Shechinah passed in Moshe's mind , in prophecy The voice of Hashem reverberated in Moshe's presence ויקרא ויקרא ה' — and Hashem called : ויקרא ה' — Hashem called, through the physical manifestation: ויקרא ה' — the prophecy called: ויקרא — and the voice called : What was called \"ה' אל רחום וחנון, ארך אפים ורב חסד ואמת...\" — the Thirteen Attributes, the back of God \"ה' אל רחום וחנון...\" \"ה' אל רחום וחנון...\" \"ה', ה', אל רחום וחנון...\" — a doubled call The Rabbi's note on verse 23 (thinking aloud): on the Rambam's reading there is a small difficulty — the verse says I will remove My palm, and then you will see My back. If the palm is intellectual protection, why remove it before seeing the back? He answered that וראית את אחרי may mean and you will have seen My back — the diversion is over. (Doron then offered a better answer — see Part Five.) Part Four — The Four Questions, Answered When we began this chapter (at the end of the shiur two weeks ago), the Rabbi asked four questions. Now we can answer them all. The comparison chart The Rabbi asked: \"Doron, if we can get an AI chart of all of these four meanings, with the נפקא מינות — in terms of Hashem, in terms of His face, in terms of עבר , in terms of who called, in terms of the passing of בעבור כבודי — I think that would be amazing… so we've got it visually in front of us.\" 1 — The Rambam's own reading 2 — Onkelos, simple (2A) 3 — Onkelos, deeper (2B) 4 — The Rambam, using Onkelos's principle Word added to the verse? None שכינה / יקרא — the glory שכינה / יקרא — the glory קול — the voice Meaning of עבר 5 — diversion 1 — physical passing 3 — prophetic vision 2 — proclamation / reverberation What \"passed\"? Nothing passed: Hashem diverted Moshe from His face to His back A created, physical glory of God The Shechinah in Moshe's mind A created voice Whose face (פניו)? God's face Moshe's — in his presence Moshe's — in his presence Moshe's — in his presence Who called (ויקרא)? Hashem — ויקרא ה' Hashem — ויקרא ה' Hashem — ויקרא ה' The voice — ויקרא ה' ה' — doubled? No — the first ה' is the caller No No Yes — a doubled call, like משה משה בעבר כבדי כבודי = God Himself; בעבר = when I divert you When My created glory physically passes When My glory passes in your mind When My voice passes ושכתי כפי עליך Intellectual protection during the diversion Physical protection from the created light Protection in the mind during the vision Protection from a strong, awesome voice Sense involved Intellect alone — no physical sense Sight Prophetic imagination Hearing Rav Makbili's ladder Highest — most non-corporeal Lowest — most physical Middle Middle — a voice is subtle Bowing at ויעבר Nothing to bow for (last week) Makes sense — the glory passes before you — — Fits the טעמים? Not so well Not so well Not so well Yes — the pause comes after ויקרא First ה' one of the 13 attributes? No No No No — the doubling is \"for beauty,\" as it were, not an attribute Question 1 — What does עבר mean? It depends on the reading: Rambam (1) — meaning 5 , diversion; Targum simple (2) — meaning 1 , physical passing; Targum deeper (3) — meaning 3 , prophetic imagination; Rambam with Targum's principle (4) — meaning 2 , reverberation and proclamation. Question 2 — Whose face? On reading 1 , the Rambam's preferred, פניו is God's face . On readings 2, 3 and 4 — both readings of the Targum and the Rambam's fourth — פניו is Moshe's face, i.e. his presence . A question from the class: why can't Moshe see God's face? Because God has no face. \"God's face\" means the very deep and intimate understanding of God , which is impossible for a human to comprehend. I can look at another human being, study their face, look into their eyes, and come to know them — that is not possible with HaKadosh Baruch Hu. \"What about looking at Him from far?\" — There is nothing to look at, because there is nothing physical to look at. That's the whole point. \"Even like a storm, or like lightning?\" — No. God is far more encompassing than that. Whatever you try to limit God into is limited — even if it is the biggest thing in the world, it still has a framework; it is still physical. Question 3 — How do we read ויקרא ה' ה'? Readings How the verse divides 1, 2 and 3 ויעבר ה' על פניו — ויקרא ה': \"ה' אל רחום וחנון...\" — and Hashem called : \"Hashem, merciful God...\" 4 ויעבר ה' על פניו, ויקרא: \"ה' ה' אל רחום וחנון...\" — and [the voice] called : \"Hashem, Hashem...\" The טעמים As Doron pointed out last week, the cantillation fits reading 4 much better: ויקרא carries a זרקא , and ה' ה' follows with a סגולתא — there is a pause after ויקרא . On readings 1, 2 and 3 you would expect the verse to run ויקרא ה' together. And Doron sent the Rabbi a source during the week: Ibn Ezra already asks this — because Rav Saadia Gaon also read it ויקרא ה' , and Hashem called , and Ibn Ezra asks: if so, the טעמים should reflect it. The Rabbi's assessment: this is a registered, very early question on readings 1, 2 and 3. It's not a huge question , because the טעמים are very, very late, so he isn't too worried. But it is important to recognize that the טעמים are not so \"glatt\" — not so clean — according to readings 1, 2 and 3. Question 4 — The Thirteen Attributes: don't count them on your fingers A halachic point the Rabbi wanted to bring out. How we sing it in shul — whether עדות המזרח or S&P, everyone does the same: ויקרא בשם ה', שנאמר: ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא, ה', ה', אל רחום וחנון, ארך אפים, ורב חסד ואמת, נוצר חסד לאלפים, נושא עון ופשע וחטאה, ונקה. Approach Source Is the first ה' an attribute? Practice Count on your fingers The Ben Ish Chai , based on the Arizal Yes — the first ה' is attribute 1, the second ה' is attribute 2 Count each attribute on a finger as you say it Don't count Chacham Ovadia Yosef It is a big מחלוקת ראשונים how to count them Just say them, with in mind \"whatever the 13 are, the 13 are\" — don't start thinking \"1, 2, 3, 4…\" The Rambam and Onkelos This chapter No — on all four readings. On 1–3 the first ה' is the one calling ; on 4 the doubling is not really part of the attributes The attributes start from the second ה' The Rabbi used to count them on his fingers — and stopped , when he came across this. Chacham Ovadia's position is totally logical: the Arizal held that way, fine — but look at the Rishonim, and all the different ways of reading the verse we've now seen. His main point: we have seen clearly that the Rambam does not count the first ה' as an attribute of mercy — and neither does Onkelos. So if you count on your fingers starting from the first ה' , you are going against the Rambam and against Onkelos. If you're convinced the Arizal and the Ben Ish Chai are right, fine, do that — \"then you probably wouldn't be coming to these shiurim!\" Otherwise: just have in mind that whatever the 13 attributes are, they are the 13 attributes. Part Five — Loose Ends: עבירה, המעביר על מדותיו, and Doron's Question עבירה — overreach Doron raised this last time: is עבירה meaning 4 (overreaching) or 5 (diverting)? Rabbi Sacks — beautifully, in his usual beautiful way — says עבירה is meaning 4: an overreach. A sin is, essentially, going beyond what we are meant to be doing — beyond the boundaries, beyond where we are meant to be in life. So meaning 4 finally finds its use. We used meanings 5, 1, 3 and 2 in the four interpretations — never 4 . Now we see that 4 is the meaning behind the concept of sin. כל המעביר על מדותיו מעבירין לו על כל פשעיו The famous statement of the Chachamim (Rosh Hashanah 17a): whoever \"passes over\" his middot, all his sins are \"passed over.\" When people insult you and you are not strict in judgment — when you overlook what others do to you — God overlooks what you do wrong to Him. Which meaning of עבר is מעביר? Certainly not physical passing (1), not proclamation (2), not a prophetic vision (3). It's 4 or 5. Meaning 4 — overreach Meaning 5 — diversion המעביר על מדותיו Your middot are telling you to take retribution, to stand up, to be angry, to take revenge — and you pass that over ; you overreach it. I'm going to ignore those middot You divert from your middot — from being strict in judgment — like the Rambam interpreted ויעבר מעבירין לו על כל פשעיו Your sins, which are meant to be connected to you — we overreach them from you ; we say they're not part of you We divert from you all your sins The Rabbi His first thought: \"it must be four, really\" \"Probably better is number five\" — and that is certainly the best reading of the second half The lesson: what we're learning gives us utensils to understand many statements. All the verbs we have learned over these 21 chapters can be applied to other statements of the Chachamim. Try to remember and apply what we've learned. Doron's question: \"I will remove My palm\" Doron , on the Rambam's first reading: the Rabbi had said there was a problem with ושכתי כפי... והסרתי את כפי . The difficulty: if כפי is intellectual protection during the diversion, why does the verse say I will remove My protection, and then you shall see My back ? The Rabbi's first answer Doron's reading ושכתי כפי עליך עד עברי I will protect you until I finish the diversion The protection is needed until the diversion is complete — during it, he hasn't yet fully seen the back והסרתי את כפי Once the diversion finishes, I remove the intellectual protection Once I've finished the diversion and given you the כלים not to fall into trying to penetrate My face, the protection is no longer needed וראית את אחרי And you will have seen My back — past tense Now you can focus on the back freely — think about the Thirteen Attributes freely. אחורי isn't something seen once in the past: it's going forward, throughout your whole life's experience — the way I conduct Myself Verdict \"Both make sense\" \"Very nice, beautiful… probably yours is actually better.\" The danger is in the diversion itself: taking someone away from trying to penetrate God's face, towards His back. Once that is done, there is no more danger in contemplating the back. Summary of Chapter 21 Over three shiurim (the end of the one two weeks ago, last week and this week), we went through the five meanings of עבר and applied them to four interpretations of ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא and the whole passage — with ramifications for how to read the verses, how to divide them, the טעמים, bowing, and counting the attributes. The main thing: do not think God passed Moshe. That is impossible, because God has no body and God has no face. Part Six — Chapter 22: בא, \"To Come\" A much easier, more straightforward, very short chapter. The Rambam continues his theme throughout these chapters: multi-meaning verbs that the Torah uses of Hashem. Why בא, and why now? Explanation The connection to Chapter 21 Rav Makbili In the same chapter of the Torah as ויעבר ה' על פניו (Shemot 34), about 20–30 verses later, it says ובבא משה לפני ה' לדבר אתו (34:34) — when Moshe came before Hashem to speak with Him . So having discussed one multi-meaning verb from that chapter, the Rambam discusses another: בא . The Rabbi's own suggestion See the end of the chapter: the Rambam himself adds a word here. In Chapter 21 he accepted Onkelos's method of adding words; now he shows that he does it too . The four meanings of בא הביאה בלשון עברית means four things — \"really three,\" since the fourth is an extension of the third. # Meaning Example Physical? Onkelos (Rav Kapach) 1 Coming to a place or a person בא אחיך במרמה (Bereishit 27:35) — Yaakov literally came, from one place to another Yes — physical, geographical אתא ( he came ) 2 Coming into an enclosed place ויבא יוסף הביתה (Bereishit 43:26); כי תבאו אל הארץ — into an enclosed geographical place Yes — physical, geographical על ( he entered ) 3 The \"coming\" of something entirely non-corporeal — i.e. it happens כי יבא דבריך וכבדנוך (Shoftim 13:17); מאשר יבאו עליך (Yeshayahu 47:13) No — 4 Extended even to a privation — the \"happening\" of something that isn't really there ויבא רע... ויבא אפל (Iyov 30:26) No — Meaning 1 — coming to נקבע לביאת בעל חיים, כלומר לגישתו למקום מסוים או לפרט אחר: בא אחיך במרמה. Any creature coming to somewhere. (We'll read it in Parashat Toldot in a couple of weeks.) Your brother came — he literally came. Meaning 2 — coming into עוד נקבע לכניסת בעל חיים למקום מסוים. Don't mix 1 and 2: 1 is just coming to a place; 2 is coming into somewhere — Yosef came into the house; Bnei Yisrael will come into the land. Rav Kapach's observation: whenever the Torah means meaning 1, Targum uses אתא (alef-tav-alef); whenever it means coming into an enclosed place, meaning 2, Targum uses על (ayin-lamed). \"All these years I never realized this when I read the Targum — but now, beautiful. Now I'm looking out for this all the time.\" Meaning 3 — something non-corporeal \"comes\": it happens הושאל לחלותו של דבר שאינו גשמי כלל. Manoach and his wife ask the מלאך ה' : tell us your name, so that when your words come — when we have the baby boy, Shimshon — we may honour you. Words don't come. They don't come to a place or into a place. It means: when something non-corporeal is manifested in a happening . Yeshayahu mocks those who go to the astrologers, the stars, the avodah zarah: יעמדו נא ויושיעך הברי שמים, החזים בכוכבים, מודיעם לחדשים מאשר יבאו עליך — let them tell you what will come upon you . Nothing is coming onto you geographically: it's a happening. Meaning 4 — even a privation עד כדי כך שהושאל להעדר מסוים: ויבא רע, ויבא אפל. The Rambam holds that there is no such thing as bad, and no such thing as darkness — we'll discuss this much later in the Moreh. Bad, darkness and evil are all privations ( העדר ), all lackings — not creations. Yet the verse says bad came, darkness came — it happened — even though these things never really \"come,\" since they are only a privation. בא can be extended even that far. There are even halachic ramifications. A famous piece of Rav Elchanan Wasserman : is darkness a creation, or a privation? When it says ויהי ערב ויהי בקר , does ערב mean a creation of evening and darkness — or simply a privation, a lack of light, a lack of day? The Rambam holds it's the latter: not a creation, but a privation. When בא is used of Hashem: always meaning 3 בהתאם להשאלה זו — שהוראתה שהתרחש דבר מה בכלל — הושאל גם לגבי תוארו יתרומם: אם לחלות הגעת דברו, או לחלות השראת שכינתו. Whenever the Torah says Hashem \"comes,\" it can't mean 1 or 2 — He has no physicality — and it can't mean 4. It must be meaning 3 , and it means one of two things: Hashem \"coming\" means… Example Explanation The resting of His Shechinah הנה אנכי בא אליך בעב הענן (Shemot 19:9) — at Har Sinai, Parashat Yitro I am resting My Shechinah on you in the thickness of a cloud . Rav Makbili: בעב הענן = in the thickness and cloudiness of human nature — even though a human is made of חומר , of thickness and corporeality, I will still rest My presence amongst you and upon you The resting of His Shechinah השער הזה סגור יהיה, לא יפתח, ואיש לא יבא בו, כי ה' אלהי ישראל בא בו (Yechezkel 44:2) The gate of the Third Temple will be closed until Hashem \"comes in it\" — until He decides to rest His Shechinah there The fulfillment of His word ובא ה' אלהי כל קדשים עמך (Zechariah 14:5) חלות דברו — כלומר, קיום הבטחותיו שהבטיח ביד נביאיו. The promises He made through His prophets will come — will happen The Rambam adds a word On Zechariah, the Rambam writes: כאלו אמר: ובא דבר ה' אלהי על ידי כל קדשים עמך — as if the verse said, the word of Hashem my God will come through all the holy ones with you — i.e., through the prophets amongst Yisrael. His words will be fulfilled; His prophecies will be accomplished. Notice: the Rambam has to add words here. It's not so clean; he struggles a bit. And that, perhaps, is the connection between this chapter and Chapter 21. In Chapter 21 the Rambam had his preferred reading of ויעבר , which needed no added word — but he said of Onkelos's method, \"I'll accept that.\" Here he shows that he isn't, in principle, against the method of Rav Saadia and Targum Onkelos of adding words: he does it himself. \"Even according to my own interpretation, I agree that sometimes I do need to add in words.\" And ביאה? What about ביאה in the sense of intercourse? The Torah itself uses בא this way — ובא אליה . The Rabbi: that must be meaning 2 — a physical coming into : geographical, physical, but into . Summary of Chapter 22 Meaning Physical? Used of Hashem? 1 — coming to a place Yes Never 2 — coming into a place (and ביאה) Yes Never 3 — a non-corporeal happening No Always — the resting of His Shechinah , or the fulfillment of His word through the prophets 4 — even a privation ( ויבא רע , ויבא אפל ) No No Whenever we talk about בא in the context of HaKadosh Baruch Hu, it means number three: non-corporeal. \"A very straightforward chapter.\" Summary Chapter 21 complete: Interpretation 4: ויעבר קול ה' על פניו, ויקרא — the Rambam uses Onkelos's principle of adding a word, but adds קול : a created voice reverberated in Moshe's presence ( עבר 2 ), and the voice called \"ה' ה'\" — the only reading with a doubled ה' , like משה משה . A voice can call, just as וישמע את הקול מדבר אליו . The Rambam's conclusion: בחר איזה דעה שתחפוץ — choose any of the four — but never think God Himself physically passed , because He has no body and no movement. Rav Makbili's ladder: reading 1 is the highest (intellect alone); 2 the lowest (sight of a created glory); 3 (prophecy) and 4 (hearing — subtle) in between. The four questions: עבר = 5 / 1 / 3 / 2; פניו = God's face only on reading 1, Moshe's on 2–4; ויקרא ה' on 1–3, ויקרא: ה' ה' on 4 (which the טעמים favour — Ibn Ezra's question on Rav Saadia); and no reading counts the first ה' as an attribute — so, with Chacham Ovadia, don't count them on your fingers . עבירה = overreach (meaning 4, Rabbi Sacks); המעביר על מדותיו = 4 or, probably better, 5; and Doron's reading of והסרתי את כפי : the danger is in the diversion — afterwards you see the back freely, all your life. Chapter 22: בא = (1) coming to; (2) coming into (Onkelos: אתא vs על ); (3) a non-corporeal happening; (4) even a privation. Of Hashem it always means 3 — His Shechinah resting, or His word fulfilled — and the Rambam himself adds a word to explain ובא ה' אלהי כל קדשים עמך . Coming Up Next week: three short chapters — 23 , 24 and, b'ezrat Hashem, 25 . Chapter 23: the opposite of coming — יציאה , going out . Chapter 24: another word for going — הלוך / הליכה . Chapter 25: שכינה . During the week: reflect on it and think about it. These are very deep things. The shiur is an hour, but the Rabbi prepares many hours for each one — so it's important to go over it and review. And next time you say ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא , choose your reading. B'Ezrat Hashem","2Y6Fe2jh3ug":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim, Siman 321 (סימן שכ\"א) Seifim covered: 2–6 (pickling), 7, 9 and 12 (tochen). Seif 1 is held back to be learned with the rest of tochen. Topic: The rabbinic prohibition of pickling: salt-water solutions, salting many pieces together, and salting in order to pickle. Rashi (דומה לעיבוד) versus the Rambam (דומה לבישול). Practical questions: salads, sugar on fruit, vinegar, and putting half-pickled food back in the brine. Then the opening principles of מלאכת טוחן : the back of an instrument, אין טוחן אחר טוחן , cutting דק דק , and אין טוחן אלא בגידולי קרקע . Schedule note: This was the first Hilchot Shabbat shiur after the chagim, on Thursday, 6:15–7:15pm. Where We Are The break. Hilchot Shabbat paused for about three months: before Tisha B'Av we learned the Rambam's Mishneh Torah on Hilchot Ta'aniyot and Tisha B'Av ; in Elul/Tishrei we learned the Rambam on Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur and Sukkot . We have learned these sections in the Shulchan Aruch before, but never in the Rambam. \"I think that was very meaningful.\" The project. We have been learning through the Shulchan Aruch on Shabbat for over two and a half, almost three years : Simanim 242–301: the positive aspects of Shabbat; Simanim 301–320: the melachot. Siman 320 was completed at the end of the summer term. Major melachot covered so far: hotza'ah (carrying) and muktzeh ; building and demolishing ; trapping, hunting and killing ; tying and untying ; making a tent ; cooking (bishul); borer (selecting); squeezing (sechita), among others. What's left. The simanim run to 344 , and the melachot really conclude around 340 . Most of the coming simanim are short, so Siman 321 is the last major one. Overview of Siman 321 Seifim Topic 1 Tochen. Saved for the end of the tochen section. 2–6 Pickling (rabbinic: similar to tanning or to cooking) 7–12 Tochen : grinding, crushing, mashing, cutting fine 13 A separate matter 14–end Lash : kneading Three major topics in this siman: Lash (kneading). One of the most complicated melachot to understand. \"So many people don't understand the principles of lash, and therefore make mistakes.\" It is only three seifim in the Shulchan Aruch and highly complicated, but once you understand the principles and go back to the Gemara, \"it all makes total sense.\" We will learn it towards the end of the siman. Tochen. Crushing, mashing, grinding and cutting. Pickling. This is where we start tonight. Part One — Pickling (כבישה): Seifim 2–6 Introduction: The Prohibitions The Gemara gives two explicit prohibitions : Making a salt-water solution (מי מלח). Salting many pieces together of vegetables that are normally pickled , such as cucumbers, onions and cabbage. Think of חמוצים , the cans and tubs of pickled vegetables. A third prohibition (below) follows from these: salting even one piece in order to pickle it. Why? A מחלוקת ראשונים It is definitely a rabbinic prohibition. The question is what the Chachamim modelled it on. Rashi and most Rishonim: דומה לעיבוד, similar to tanning Me'abed (tanning) is one of the 39 melachot: The first 11 melachot are the process of making the dyes: the land, ploughing, through to kneading and baking. The next set deal with animals: trapping, slaughtering, skinning (מפשיט) and tanning (מעבד) . One of the roofs of the Mishkan was made of hides. Salting is an essential part of the tanning process. The Rabbi read up on this online before the shiur. Salt both begins the tanning and makes the hide durable for storage. The Gemara says explicitly אין עיבוד באוכלין : there is no tanning with food, so there is no Torah melacha here. Even so, salt completely changes the texture of the food (think of a cucumber becoming a pickle), just as salting a hide changes its texture and makes it durable. So the Chachamim forbade pickling rabbinically , as a corollary of tanning. The Rambam: דומה לבישול, similar to cooking The Rambam was presumably not happy linking hides with food. Which melacha would you tie it to instead? Robert: \"Isn't it a way of making food edible? So you might say it's some sort of cooking.\" The Rabbi: \"Exactly, perfect, very good.\" The Rambam says salting makes food edible and is דומה לבישול , similar to cooking. Of course it isn't actual bishul: there is no fire, and as we learned in Siman 318 , fire is essential to bishul. But by changing the texture through salt, you are cooking in a sense, on a rabbinic level. The Rabbi finds this the more straightforward explanation . Mark: \"I read somewhere that salt gives off heat, so from that point of view that's where the cooking occurs.\" The Rabbi: \"Interesting, I never thought of it like that.\" He had understood the Rambam as comparing the change in texture (uncooked → cooked; unpickled → pickled). The heat may also be part of it. \"Maybe it's a combination.\" Follow-up later in the shiur: a participant looked it up. Dissolving table salt (sodium chloride) in water is not exothermic . Other salts (calcium chloride, magnesium chloride) do give off heat, but we don't put those in food. Himalayan salt is still sodium chloride, and kosher/coarse salt is just a coarser grain. Perhaps older sources didn't know the science, or perhaps the heat comes from salt reacting with the proteins in a hide during tanning. That part of the tanning process does involve some heat, which may be where the idea came from. The Shulchan Aruch brings both Seif 2 gives Rashi's reason (משום דמי לעיבוד), and Seif 3 gives the Rambam's (מפני שהוא כמבשל). We have seen before that the Shulchan Aruch sometimes combines reasons when it finds both convincing. There are few, if any, clear practical differences between them. Robert's question: a decree on a decree? Robert: I can understand why you can't pickle. But why can't you add salt to water? Pickling itself is only rabbinic, so banning the salt water looks like a second derabbanan on top of the first. The Rabbi: It isn't a gezeirah l'gezeirah . This is הכל גזירה אחת : it was all one enactment , made in one go. When the Chachamim banned pickling, they banned the things that make up pickling , such as salt-water solutions, together with it. Seif 2 — Making salt water אין עושים מי מלח הרבה ביחד לתת לתוך הכבשים משום דמי לעיבוד. אבל יכול לעשות ממנו מעט לתת לתוך התבשיל. אם נתן לתוכו שני שלישי מלח, אסור לעשות ממנו אפילו מעט. Translation: One may not make a large amount of salt water to put into pickles, because it resembles tanning. One may make a small amount to put into a dish. If two-thirds of it is salt, even a small amount is forbidden. Solution Ruling Two-thirds salt (extremely salty) Forbidden in any quantity. Something that salty must be part of pickling. Less than two-thirds, large quantity Forbidden . A large amount suggests pickling, even if you only dip and eat. Less than two-thirds, small quantity Permitted . It is part of eating (צורך אכילה). What is \"a little\" and what is \"a lot\"? The logic: a small amount is clearly for dipping and eating , while a large amount suggests you're coming to pickle . The Shulchan Aruch HaRav (the first Lubavitcher Rebbe, about 250 years ago) wrote that he doesn't know the difference between מעט and הרבה. Others have tried to give measurements. The Rabbi: \"The answer I take with all of this is very simple: common sense.\" It depends on the family, the context, the se'udah and what's going on. \"You can't define these things.\" (The same issue comes back in tochen tonight.) Practical case: salt water for karpas at the Seder \"We Syrians use lemon water,\" but Ashkenazim , and some Sefardim , use salt water . A Seder salt water won't be two-thirds salt, so that isn't the issue. But if you have 50 guests and need a large amount for bowls across the table, that is a large quantity . That's why the Rema writes in Hilchot Pesach that the salt water should be made beforehand . Seif 3 — Salting many pieces together אסור למלוח חתיכות צנון ד' או ה' ביחד, מפני שהוא ככובש כבשים, והכובש אסור מפני שהוא כמבשל. אלא מטבל כל אחת לבדה ואוכלה. אבל ביצים מותר למלחן. You may not salt four or five pieces together of radish (or cucumber, cabbage, onion, or anything normally pickled). Done together, it looks like the start of pickling , even if you intend to eat it immediately. Two or three pieces may be salted and eaten. Here the Shulchan Aruch gives the Rambam's reason, כמבשל . אלא מטבל כל אחת לבדה ואוכלה: dip each piece individually, either into an existing solution or into one made under the conditions of Seif 2. The poskim: the Shulchan Aruch's \"dip\" is just אורחא דמילתא , the normal way of saying it. According to the Shulchan Aruch it is equally fine to salt two or three pieces . אבל ביצים מותר למלחן: eggs are just the Gemara's example. Anything not normally pickled may be salted. The Ashkenazi minhag (Magen Avraham) Ashkenazim recorded a minhag never to salt vegetables on Shabbat at all . If you want salt on a vegetable, dip the vegetable into the salt , so the question never arises. A parallel: the Magen Avraham also records the Ashkenazi minhag not to bathe even in cold water on Shabbat (because of squeezing, heating and other concerns). That is one of the reasons Ashkenazim don't swim on Shabbat. Me'ikar hadin , for Sefardim , swimming in a swimming pool on Shabbat is permitted under certain conditions. Not the sea. \"Unless you know those conditions, don't do it.\" For Sefardim: you may dip your vegetables into salt or a salt-water solution, or salt two or three pieces. The third prohibition: salting in order to pickle Even one piece, if you're salting it to pickle it, is forbidden. This applies even on Friday night for Shabbat afternoon. The Rabbi's own example: you finish a tub of chamutzim , and a strong solution is left at the bottom. During the week he cuts up carrots or cabbage and drops them in. After a day or two they're pickled again, not as strong as the original but pickled. You can't do this on Shabbat, even with a single piece , because that is pickling. Summary: the three rabbinic prohibitions # Prohibition What is permitted 1 Making salt water Only a small amount, less than two-thirds salt 2 Salting many pieces together (4–5+) of normally-pickled vegetables Salt 2–3 pieces, or dip each piece (Ashkenazim: dip only) 3 Salting even one piece in order to pickle it Not permitted All three are rabbinic , rooted in either me'abed (Rashi) or bishul (Rambam). Practical Questions 1. Salting a salad Suppose the salad is cucumbers, carrots and cabbage , all commonly pickled. Based on what we've learned, you can't put salt on that by itself . The halacha: the salt must come together with oil or lemon . Some say to put the oil in first . What's clear: if you dress the salad in one go (salt, then a second later oil, then lemon), that is fine. \"I don't do a lot in the kitchen, but I do know how to spice a salad: salt, oil and lemon.\" Salt alone , for a salty salad, is a problem. If you salted it, went out to a friend, and came back to add the oil and lemon, that is asur . Which vegetables count? Red tomatoes aren't pickled. Chacham Ovadia writes specifically about green tomatoes , which are pickled: be careful not to salt them. A normal mixed salad (red tomatoes, cucumbers, carrots, lettuce) combines pickled and unpickled items. לעניות דעתי , you may put the salt on by itself . You're salting a salad as a whole , and that is not דומה לכובש כבשים . \"The whole salad is clearly a salad.\" Some disagree, because part of the salad is commonly pickled. If the salad is primarily commonly-pickled vegetables , put the salt on together with oil or lemon , not salt alone. 2. Marie: sugar on fruit Marie: Instead of salting, may you sweeten fruit on Shabbat, putting sugar on it and leaving it to absorb? The Rabbi: \"That's so funny you ask that.\" Chacham Ovadia was asked exactly this, about putting sugar on strawberries . Permitted: It is not bishul : fruit isn't cooked , vegetables are. We find the same principle in Hilchot Bishul. It is not ibud : fruit isn't \"tanned\". Caveat: it must be for Shabbat. You may do it Friday night for Shabbat afternoon , but not Shabbat afternoon to eat on Sunday , since that is preparing for after Shabbat . For Shabbat itself, it is permitted \"100%, according to all opinions.\" 3. Robert: other pickling agents, such as vinegar Robert: What about pickling with things other than salt, like vinegar or lemon? If the reason is tanning, you would never use vinegar on a hide. The Rabbi: the Chachamim made a comprehensive ban on pickling , whatever does the pickling. Tanning is only Rashi's account of why. The Mishnah Berurah (ס\"ק ח') states explicitly that vinegar is included. So putting garlic into vinegar to pickle it is the same problem. Lemon juice alone doesn't pickle. Salting is the main part of the pickling process, and that was the Chachamim's concern. So squeezing lemon onto food isn't comparable. 4. Chacham Ovadia: אין כובש אחר כובש? The case: you pickled vegetables during the week and serve them on Shabbat. At the end of the meal you realise some are only half pickled . They should have stayed in the solution longer. Can you put them back in? (Chacham Ovadia also asks whether something already pickled in salt may go into vinegar, or vice versa.) If pickling is like… Then… Bishul (Rambam) In bishul there is a מחלוקת ראשונים , as we saw in Siman 318 : the Rosh holds that once food is מאכל בן דרוסאי (half-cooked, barely edible), further cooking is not Torah bishul. With bishul we're machmir because it's a safek de'oraita . Pickling is derabbanan , so we could say אין כובש אחר כובש and put it back. Ibud (Rashi) Who says there's an אין עיבוד אחר עיבוד? Who says half-tanned leather may be finished? Chacham Ovadia's conclusion: uncertain, so better not to put it back. Seif 4 — Dipping individually, then gathering יש מי שכתב שמותר לטבול כמה חתיכות צנון אחת אחת לבדה ולהניחם יחד לפניהם כדי לאוכלם זו אחר זו. This comes from the Terumat HaDeshen . Dip one piece , put it down; dip the second, put it next to it; then the third, fourth and fifth. Then eat them. Even though the end result is 4–5 salted pieces in front of you, this is permitted. Why: the Chachamim forbade the act of salting (מליחה) , and you're dipping . The Mishnah Berurah discusses whether the pieces may touch, and says they should be kept separate . Seif 5 — Salting cooked meat or eggs to keep them אסור למלוח בשר מבושל או ביצה מבושלת להניחה. You may not salt cooked meat or a cooked egg להניחה , to preserve it for later . Preserving falls under the same prohibition. What does להניחה mean? Some poskim say even a later meal on the same day . The Rabbi's understanding of the Shulchan Aruch: it means for later on , i.e. for a long time. That is what makes it pickling or preserving. Robert: Surely \"for later\" must mean later in the same day. For a later day it's already forbidden as hachana , preparing for another day. The Rabbi: The hachana prohibition is a very minor rabbinic prohibition , not comparable to the others, so it isn't what this seif is about. Robert: I didn't know there were different levels of rabbinic prohibitions. Isn't the punishment the same, e.g. lashes (מכת מרדות)? The Rabbi: \"Of course there are different levels.\" How can you compare a מלאכה שאינה צריכה לגופה to hachana ? Incomparable. Makat mardut isn't for every rabbinic matter, and you definitely wouldn't get it for hachana . The poskim weigh one derabbanan against another when one must be violated, considering how close each is to the de'oraita, among other factors. \"That's a whole shiur for another time.\" Seif 6 — Beans and lentils אין למלוח ביחד הרבה פולין ועדשים. This is another example: you may not salt many beans or lentils together , because these are commonly pickled. Summary of Pickling (Seifim 2–6) The prohibition is rabbinic. Rashi says it resembles tanning ; the Rambam says it resembles cooking . The Shulchan Aruch brings both . Two Gemara prohibitions: making salt water (small amounts under two-thirds salt are permitted); salting many pieces together (2–3 pieces or dipping is permitted). A third prohibition: salting even one piece in order to pickle it. Practical rulings: Salads: salt with oil or lemon . A mixed salad may be salted alone, according to the Rabbi. Sugar on fruit is permitted for Shabbat. Vinegar is included in the ban. Half-pickled food: better not to put it back in the brine. Dipping pieces one by one and then gathering them is permitted . You may not salt cooked meat or eggs to preserve them . Part Two — מלאכת טוחן (Grinding / Crushing): Seifim 7–12 Tochen runs from Seif 7 to Seif 12 inclusive, plus Seif 1 , which we'll come back to at the end. We made a start tonight with about 20 minutes left. What is Tochen? Tochen is the ninth melacha. It is still within the first 11 , the process of making the dyes . We ploughed, harvested and threshed, then selected the good seeds and removed the bad ( borer ) and sifted. Now the seeds must become a paste: 9. Tochen : crush the seeds; 10. Lash : knead them (later in this siman); 11. Bishul / Ofeh : cook or bake them (already learned). Three forms of tochen are definitely forbidden from the Torah: grinding , e.g. salt; crushing , e.g. pepper; mashing , e.g. a banana. Principle 1 — A שינוי גדול: the back of an instrument The normal rule: a melacha done with a shinui (an unusual way) is rabbinically forbidden , though not de'oraita. The classic example is writing with your left hand. The exception: there are three places in the melachot of Shabbat where the Chachamim permitted a Torah melacha done with a שינוי גדול , a very great deviation. The Rabbi remembers two offhand; he needs to think about the third. Two of them are in this siman: tochen (now) and lash (later). The term שינוי גדול is the language of the Acharonim . It isn't found in the Gemara, and the Rabbi doesn't know if it's in the Rishonim. \"This is at least my understanding. There may be another.\" What counts as a שינוי גדול for tochen: crushing with the back of an instrument , meaning the handle of a knife, fork or spoon, rather than the working end. This is so unusual that the Chachamim permitted it, because it is needed for eating . Normal grinding instruments: a mortar; a cheese grater ; vegetable/carrot graters . All of these are forbidden for tochen. Most graters, certainly specialised vegetable graters, are muktzeh. Exception: a cheese grater is not muktzeh. We'll learn why later. Practical: you want to make banana purée for a baby or mash an avocado . Mashing normally would be forbidden from the Torah , with some nuance we'll discuss. But you may mash it with the back of a fork or knife and eat it. Seif 7 — Pepper מותר לדוך פלפלין אפילו הרבה ביחד, והוא שידוכם בקתא של סכין ובקערה. You may crush peppers, even many together , using the handle of a knife , onto a plate . \"Onto a plate\": there are instruments you grind with and instruments you grind into (a mortar bowl, a grater's base). Neither specialised kind may be used. A normal plate is fine. Side point: crushing pepper inside a cloth (the Maharshal) The case: you put a lot of pepper in a cloth, wrap it up, and crush it with the back of a knife. Why not? The Maharshal (about 500 years ago) first said no , because of מוליד ריח , creating a new smell in an item. We'll learn this properly later. You may spray perfume or deodorant on your body on Shabbat. You may not spray perfume on your clothes , or a lavender spray on your pillow before bed. The fabric takes on a new smell, which the Chachamim forbade as making something new. Here, the cloth would absorb the pepper's smell. The Maharshal later retracted and permitted it. There are several possible reasons: פסיק רישא בדרבנן מותר: the smell is an inevitable side-effect (psik reisha), and the prohibition it touches (מוליד ריח) is only rabbinic. Following the Maggid Mishneh 's line we've seen before: the result is so far removed from the act that it isn't even a psik reisha. \"If you got that, you got that. If not, don't worry about it.\" Bottom line: you may crush pepper in a cloth if you're doing it to crush , not to scent the cloth. If you intend to give the cloth the smell, it is forbidden. Seif 7 (continued) — Salt אין כותשין מלח במדוך של עץ, אבל מרסקו ביד של סכין או בעץ הפרור, ואינו חושש. This is the same principle, with salt instead of pepper. You may not crush salt with a specialised wooden (or stone) pestle . You may crush it with the handle of a knife or a spoon, \"and there's no concern.\" Principle 2 — אין טוחן אחר טוחן: no grinding after grinding Rema: ודוקא מלח הגס, אבל מלח שהיה דק מתחלה ונתבשל ונעשה פתיתין, מותר לחותכן בסכין כמו שחותך הפת. The ban applies only to originally coarse salt , salt that has never been processed . Salt that was fine to begin with and then cooked into flakes may be cut with a knife, just like cutting bread . The principle: something that was already ground may be ground, crushed or mashed again . Bread: flour was ground from wheat and then reconstituted into bread or a cracker. So you may crumble bread into tiny, tiny pieces . These first two principles are unanimous and accepted . Salt today: Most salt today has already been processed , so it's already been ground. \"Someone can check this.\" Very coarse, natural salts would still be a problem. Himalayan salt grinders: These are forbidden : at least rabbinically , since you're using a specialised instrument . Whether it's de'oraita depends on a principle still to come: whether you're grinding for immediate consumption . The grinder itself is muktzeh. You can't even touch or move it on Shabbat. Lumps in ordinary salt (e.g. water got in): you may crush them . The salt was already ground, so אין טוחן אחר טוחן . Seif 12 — Cutting very fine: the fourth form of tochen The Rabbi jumped ahead to Seif 12 before Seif 9, because Seif 9 only makes sense after it. המחתך הירק דק דק חייב משום טוחן. We had three forms so far: grinding, crushing and mashing. Some Rishonim (the Rabbi thinks the Rashba ) understood the Gemara to mean that cutting into very small pieces is also tochen, a fourth form . So even without grating, crushing or mashing, cutting a vegetable into minuscule pieces makes you חייב משום טוחן . Practical: you can't make a salad cut into tiny pieces . \"I remember Rabbi Falk in Gateshead telling us you can't make an Israeli salad on Shabbat.\" The Rabbi isn't sure: an Israeli salad (finely diced tomatoes and cucumbers) is quite small. Is it דק דק? What is דק דק? The Rishonim already write: שיעור דקותו לא נודע לנו , \"we do not know the measure of its fineness.\" So the Beit Yosef writes: don't cut too small , since that might be the size meant. What's clear: regular small pieces are fine . The prohibition is about very small pieces. \"Whatever each person thinks is very small.\" But you definitely cannot cut a carrot into very, very small pieces . Seif 9 — Cutting cooked meat finely מותר לחתוך בשר מבושל או צלי דק דק בסכין. You may cut cooked or roasted meat (and cooked fish) into extremely small pieces with a knife. We just said דק דק is tochen, so why is this permitted? Two factors combine: (a) אין טוחן אלא בגידולי קרקע. Tochen applies only to things that grow from the ground . Cheese doesn't grow from the ground, so you may crush or grate cheese on Shabbat. (Mushrooms were a \"bad example\" : they are subject to the same machloket.) What about meat, chicken and eggs ? This is a מחלוקת ראשונים . Some Rishonim say that since animals feed from the ground , they and their products count as גידולי קרקע . That would include eggs, because chickens feed from the ground. (b) Tochen applies only to inedible food. There was a major opinion, though not the halacha , based on the Gemara's case of סילקא . What exactly סילקא is is unclear, just as with the Rosh Hashanah simanim. On this view, tochen means making something edible that wasn't, e.g. something too large to eat. If a food is already edible (a carrot, for example), crushing or cutting it small adds nothing. We don't hold this way. Together: cooked meat is already edible (b), and it doesn't grow from the ground (a), even though some Rishonim count animals as ground-produce. The combination permits it. Practical: Food Ruling Cooked meat / chicken / fish May be cut very fine , mashed or puréed , e.g. for a baby Cooked eggs May be mashed in the normal way , with no need for the back of a knife: they are cooked (edible) and come from an animal, not directly from the ground Cheese Permitted outright: it doesn't grow from the ground, so no combination is needed Egg + mayonnaise A separate question : that is lash (kneading), later in Siman 321 Summary of Tochen So Far Three principles of tochen learned tonight: # Principle Practical result 1 A שינוי גדול (the back or handle of an instrument) is permitted for any grinding Mash banana or avocado, crush pepper or salt, with the back of a fork or knife, onto a plate 2 אין טוחן אחר טוחן Bread may be crumbled; processed salt and salt lumps may be crushed 3 אין טוחן אלא בגידולי קרקע Cheese is permitted. Cooked meat, chicken, fish and eggs may be mashed or cut fine, by combination with the \"already edible\" view. Also: cutting vegetables דק דק is tochen (Seif 12). Regular small pieces are fine; \"very small\" is unknown, so don't go too fine. Coming Up More tochen: further important principles and practical examples ; grinding for immediate consumption (relevant to whether a salt grinder is de'oraita); why a cheese grater isn't muktzeh ; the remaining seifim and Seif 1 . Then lash (kneading): \"one of the most complicated melachot\" , Seifim 14–end, including the second שינוי גדול heter. At the end, the Rabbi asked everyone to stay on so he could discuss options for learning in the coming year . B'Ezrat Hashem","a9ynezTcd0w":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (Rambam), Chelek Aleph — Rabbi Chaim Makbili's Hebrew edition / Sefaria Chapter: I:21 — עבר (to pass), Part 2 Topic: The five meanings of עבר ; the Rambam's preferred reading of ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא — Hashem diverting Moshe from His \"face\" (His essence) to His \"back\" (His ways); why the Thirteen Attributes are not thirteen parts of God; what this means for bowing and for how we chant the verse; the danger of reaching too far, and ושכתי כפי עליך ; and Onkelos : the Shechinah passing in Moshe's presence, read either as a created light or as a prophetic vision Schedule note: The Moreh continues every Tuesday, 8:00–9:00pm (tonight ran to 9:05 to finish Onkelos). Chapter 21 will be finished next week, perhaps with the short Chapter 22 as well. Recap Chapter 20: רם and נשא , high and exalted, can mean physical height or exalted status. Used of Hashem they never mean He is physically above or located in the heavens; they express His greatness, authority and power . We went through Yeshayahu 6:1 , six words of which we have now explained, to see how learning the Moreh changes our reading of Tanach. Chapter 21, introduction (last week): Shemot 33 — Moshe asks to see Hashem's face; Hashem says you can see My back , and I will put My palm upon you and protect you ; the words מקום and נצב ; Moshe carves the luchot; וירד ה' בענן ויתיצב עמו שם ויקרא בשם ה' — Hashem rests His presence permanently with Moshe; and then ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא: ה' ה'... The four questions: What does it mean that Hashem passed ? Whose face — Moshe's or God's? How do we read it — ויקרא ה': ה' אל רחום ( Hashem called : \"Hashem, merciful God…\"), or ויקרא: ה' ה' אל רחום ( He called : \"Hashem, Hashem…\")? What are the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy ? The Road Map for Chapter 21 Five meanings of עבר , and then four interpretations of ויעבר ה' על פניו , each using one of those meanings: # Interpretation 1 The Rambam's own, preferred reading 2 Targum Onkelos , understood one way (2A) 3 Targum Onkelos , understood another way (2B) 4 The Rambam's own reading using the Targum's principle — a completely different interpretation (next week) Each one changes how we read the parts of the verse — and really the whole narrative and the whole chapter. Once we have them all, we will read the story again under each, and make a chart comparing all four. Part One — The Five Meanings of עבר Before reading: the Rabbi asked the class for meanings. To pass. — Yes, on the simple level. The past (עבר), as opposed to the future. — That is the same as meaning one: the past is what has passed . ויעבר אברם בארץ (this week's parashah) — Avram passed through the land. — Still meaning one. Meaning 1 — Physical movement across space משמעותו הראשונה היא כמשמעות \"עבור\" בערבית, והיא תזוזת גוף במרחב; ודוגמתו הראשונה העברת בעל חיים על פני מרחק מסוים. A body — human or animal — moving across a space, from one place to another: והוא עבר לפניהם (Bereishit 33:3) — Yaakov went ahead of his family and bowed to Esav. עבר לפני העם (Shemot 17:5) — Hashem tells Moshe, before he strikes the rock: walk in front of the people. Why not ויעבר אברם בארץ? The Rabbi doesn't know. The commentators spend a long time on why the Rambam quotes this pasuk rather than that one, and in this order rather than that. It is part of the depth of the Rambam, but to do it for every verse we would be here forever; it is beyond the scope of the shiur. Meaning 2 — The spreading of sound אחר כך הושאל להתפשטות הקולות באוויר. ויעברו קול במחנה לאמר: איש ואשה אל יעשו עוד מלאכה לתרומת הקדש, ויכלא העם מהביא (Shemot 36:6) — stop bringing for the Mishkan. \"They passed a voice through the camp\" means a proclamation : a public announcement. כי לא טובה השמעה אשר אנכי שמע מעברים עם ה' (Shmuel I 2:24) — Eli to his sons, whose corruption we know: the rumours the people of Hashem are passing about you are not good. Not walking anywhere: talking , reverberating. Meaning 3 — The light and Shechinah prophets see in a vision This week's parashah, Lech Lecha. אחר כך הושאל להשראת האור והשכינה שרואים הנביאים במראה הנבואה, כמו שנאמר: והנה תנור עשן ולפיד אש אשר עבר בין הגזרים האלה (Bereishit 15:17). וזה היה במראה הנבואה, משום שבפתיחת הסיפור נאמר: ותרדמה נפלה על אברם, והנה אימה חשכה גדלה נפלת עליו. According to the Rambam, the Brit Bein HaBetarim never physically happened. There were no animals, no pieces, no fire and no smoke: it all happened in Avraham's mind. His proof: a deep sleep fell upon Avram . The Rabbi adds that the episode opens היה דבר ה' אל אברם במחזה — it almost says so explicitly; it's no great chiddush. Why is עבר used for prophecy? Because in prophecy, Hashem's communication passes through a person's mind : it begins at a certain moment and ends at a certain moment. The radio analogy (in the Rambam, the Ramchal, everywhere): prophecy is like tuning a radio. You tune in, but it is very rare to hold the right signal constantly. The Shechinah passes through, and then it goes. So what are the smoke and fire passing between the pieces? Probably the firmness and durability of the covenant (the commentator explains this here; the Rabbi thinks it is straightforward) — perhaps its warmth too. Whatever they signify precisely, the whole thing is happening in Avraham's mind. ולפי השאלה הזו נאמר: ועברתי בארץ מצרים (Shemot 12:12) On the night of Pesach, at midnight, people would suddenly experience something extraordinary — and a few moments later it would stop. It passes through them. Meaning 4 — Overreaching: going beyond the limit והושאל גם למי שעושה מעשה כל שהוא והפריז בו ועבר את גבולו, כמו שנאמר: הייתי כאיש שכור, וכגבר עברו יין (Yirmiyahu 23:9). Yirmiyahu says: I was so zealous for You, Hashem, fighting the false prophets, that I was like a drunk man — like a man whom wine has \"passed over\" . A little wine is good; drink too much and it has passed the limit, and you're drunk. I overreached; perhaps I went too far. This is the only meaning not used in the chapter. If it's the one you forget, that's fine. We need 1, 2, 3 and 5. Meaning 5 — Skipping one target and aiming at another: diversion והושאל גם למי שדילג על מטרה כלשהי וכיוון מטרה אחרת, או תכלית אחרת. You can pass across something (like Yaakov passing before his family) — or pass over it: skip it. I'm going 1, 2, 3, and then I jump over 4 and land on 5. I have diverted. You create a diversion — perhaps a misapprehension. והוא ירה החצי להעברו (Shmuel I 20:36) Haftarat Machar Chodesh, which we read a week ago. David doesn't know where he stands with Shaul; if he comes, Shaul may kill him. So Yonatan arranges a code: the way he shoots the arrows will tell David whether to come back or to run. Other Rishonim read להעברו as meaning 4: he shot the arrow further than expected — overreaching — as the signal to flee. The Rambam does not. He reads it as meaning 5: there was a lad with Yonatan, who must not catch on to the secret code. Yonatan shot the arrow so as to divert the lad's attention — to give him a misapprehension of what was really going on between David and Yonatan. The five meanings — summary # Meaning Example Used in Ch. 21? 1 Physical movement across space והוא עבר לפניהם Yes 2 Proclaiming; sound and rumour spreading ויעברו קול במחנה Yes 3 A prophetic experience passing through the mind ולפיד אש אשר עבר בין הגזרים Yes 4 Overreaching a limit וכגבר עברו יין No 5 Skipping, diverting, creating a misapprehension והוא ירה החצי להעברו Yes Doron's question: what does עבירה mean? Doron: when we say someone does an עבירה , is that meaning 4 — going beyond what you were meant to do — or meaning 5 — diverting from the path? The Rabbi: \"Wow, excellent. I actually never put it together.\" The Rabbi thought עבירה as a noun for sin is probably rabbinic , not biblical. If so, it can't mean physical passing (1), proclaiming (2) or prophecy (3) — so it must be 4 (overreaching) or 5 (diverting) . But Doron pointed out that the verb is used in Devarim , for breaking the covenant: לעבר בריתו — וילך ויעבד אלהים אחרים וישתחו להם The Rabbi: of course — לא עברתי ממצותיך ולא שכחתי (Devarim 26:13). I didn't \"pass\" Your mitzvot — not physical, not prophetic, not proclaiming. So it must be 4 or 5. And why didn't the Rambam discuss it? Doron: it's almost as if the brit is a physical thing that you cross over — perhaps from the Brit Bein HaBetarim. The Rabbi: but that covenant isn't physical. Very interesting — he wrote it down to come back to at the end of the chapter , together with several other words he wants to think about. The Rabbi to those listening on YouTube: it's lovely when people come in person so we can have a dialogue — but if you are listening and have questions, and more importantly thoughts to add, like this one , contact him through YouTube or by phone. This is a conversation to keep going. Part Two — Interpretation 1: The Rambam's Preferred Reading The class's guesses Which of the five meanings will the Rambam use for ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא ? Yehuda: meaning 3 — a prophetic experience. Maor: meaning 5 — by elimination (1 is corporeal; 2 doesn't fit), and because perhaps Moshe went too far in trying to comprehend. The Rabbi: that reasoning is really meaning 4 (overreaching) — but 5 is right, though not for that reason. Doron: 3 feels the most natural. The Rambam לדעתי, דברי \"ויעבר ה' על פניו\" הם לפי השאלה הזאת — the fifth meaning. וכינוי הגוף ב\"פניו\" חוזר עליו — \"his face\" refers to Hashem . Question The Rambam's answer 1. What is עבר? Meaning 5 — diversion 2. Whose face? Hashem's 3. How to read it? ויקרא ה' — and Hashem called : \"ה', אל רחום וחנון...\" 4. The Thirteen Attributes? Hashem's \"back\" : the ways He is manifested in the world Put together: Moshe asked, show me Your face. Hashem said, לא תוכל לראות את פני , you can't see My face — וראית את אחרי , you will see My back. How did that happen? ויעבר ה' על פניו — Hashem diverted Moshe from trying to understand Hashem's face. ויקרא ה' — and Hashem called out the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy. The Thirteen Attributes are Hashem's back. Why are they His \"back\"? Because they are the ways Hashem is manifested in this world. As we will learn when we reach ייחוד ה' : there are no thirteen parts of God. There is only one God. \"Thirteen attributes\" means thirteen ways in which His presence is manifested in the world — and understanding Hashem through the way He acts in the world is seeing His back. You won't understand My essence; you can't. I divert you from My face, and give you instead the way I act in this world. \"This changes everything.\" What this means for how we say it We say this verse constantly. At Selichot each day, Mondays and Thursdays, 26 times on Yom Kippur , twice daily in Shacharit and Mincha (for Sephardim) — a good 500 times between Rosh Chodesh Elul and Yom Kippur. What are people thinking when they scream ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא? Or the chazanim who go on and on? Whose face? What passing? Bowing. Most people take פניו as Moshe's face, which is why people bow down at ויעבר ה' על פניו — Hashem is passing before your face, so out of kavod you bow. On the Rambam's reading, there is nothing to bow for : the verse means something completely different — Hashem diverted Moshe from God's face. The chant. The way it is sung (especially in the Spanish & Portuguese tradition, but everyone does it) — ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא, ה' ה'... — is wrong on this reading. It is ויקרא ה' — Hashem called — and what He called begins ה' אל רחום וחנון : My Thirteen Attributes, which are My back. (More on this at the end of the chapter.) The Chachamim's support: Hashem wrapped Himself וכך פירשו חכמים שפניו... וגם כי אמרו זאת באגדות שאין במקומן — הרי יש בכך חיזוק לדעתנו. The Rambam knows most people read פניו as Moshe's face, so he brings a proof that the Chachamim read it as God's — even if they said it in an Aggadic context: \"ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא\" — אמר רבי יוחנן: אלמלא מקרא כתוב, אי אפשר לאומרו. מלמד שנתעטף הקדוש ברוך הוא כשליח צבור, והראה לו למשה סדר תפלה. (Rosh Hashanah 17b) (This is one of the reasons Ashkenazim wear a tallit for Selichot.) What has a tallit to do with it? When you wrap a tallit, you pass it around yourself — it covers the body. So if the Chachamim said Hashem wrapped Himself, they must have read על פניו as Hashem's own \"face\": He passed the tallit around Himself. At least one Aggadic reading takes פניו as God's face. The Rambam explains וביאור הדבר לדעתי, במה שנראה לי: שמשה עליו השלום ביקש השגה מסוימת — והיא שכינה אותה \"פנים\" בדבריו \"ופני לא יראו\" — והובטח לו השגה פחותה ממה שביקש, והיא שכינה \"ראיית אחור\": \"וראית את אחרי\". וכבר עמדנו על זה במשנה תורה. אם כן, הוא אומר כאן שהשם יתעלה הסתיר ממנו את ההשגה המכונה \"פנים\", ועבר הלאה לעניין אחר — כלומר ידיעת המעשים המיוחסים אליו יתעלה, שהם נחשבים עניינים רבים. Moshe asked for a very high intellectual attainment — \"the face\". He was promised something lower — \"the back\". Hashem withheld the \"face\", and passed on to another matter : knowledge of the actions attributed to Him , which people think of as many different things. That is the back of God. The same idea in Mishneh Torah Right at the beginning, in Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah (1:10) , after establishing that God has no body: מהו זה שביקש משה רבינו להשיג כשאמר: הראני נא את כבודך? ביקש לידע אמתת המצאו של הקדוש ברוך הוא, עד שיהא ידוע בלבו כמו ידיעת אחד מן האנשים שראה פניו ונחקקה צורתו בלבו... The Rabbi's illustration: we're talking to each other now; when I close the computer, I'll still have the image of your face in my head. Moshe wanted to know God like that — the essence of His existence. ...והשיבו הקדוש ברוך הוא שאין כח בדעת האדם החי, שהוא מחובר מגוף ונפש, להשיג אמתת דבר זה. ... ועל דבר זה אמר הכתוב: וראית את אחרי ופני לא יראו. No living human, made of body and soul, can grasp this. (Mishneh Torah doesn't interpret ופני לא יראו in quite the same words, but it is the same idea.) Why \"covered by My palm\": the danger of going too far בדברי \"הסתיר ממנו\", כוונתי לומר שהשגה זו נסתרת ונמנעת מטבעה. \"Hashem withheld it\" doesn't mean it would have been possible otherwise. It is by nature impossible for a human being. וכל אדם שלם, כאשר שכלו נקשר בדבר שטבעו להשיגו, וישאף להשגה נוספת מעבר לכך — עלולה השגתו להשתבש, או שימות, כפי שאבאר בפרק הבא — אלא אם ילווה אליו סיוע אלוהי, כמו שנאמר: ושכתי כפי עליך עד עברי. When a person reaches for spiritual, metaphysical, philosophical attainment beyond what his mind can grasp , he can go mad, or die — unless Hashem helps him : ושכתי כפי עליך עד עברי , I will cover you with My palm until I have passed. The four who entered the Pardes (Chagigah 14b): Ben Azzai died; Ben Zoma went mad; Elisha ben Avuya became a heretic (to an extent, also a kind of madness); Rabbi Akiva went in correctly and came out in peace. When you try to reach this level, be careful not to go too far — and sometimes you need to pray for Hashem's help so that you don't. \"Jerusalem syndrome\": there are more people who have lost their way roaming Jerusalem, proportionally — anyone who has learned in yeshiva there has seen it. Why? Because Jerusalem is where we come close to Hashem, and people can go too far spiritually and philosophically. (The Rabbi stressed: this is still meaning 5, not meaning 4.) The whole narrative on Interpretation 1 הראני נא את כבדך — Show me Your glory, Your face. לא תוכל לראות את פני, כי לא יראני האדם וחי — No human can see My face and live. הנה מקום אתי, ונצבת על הצור — There is an intellectual standing with Me: stand constantly on the Rock, the origin of the universe. והיה בעבר כבדי — When I (here כבודי means God Himself, as the Rambam will say in a later chapter) divert you from My face to My back — ושמתיך בנקרת הצור, ושכתי כפי עליך עד עברי — I will protect you intellectually while I divert you. Even seeing just My back, you could go mad; I will protect you so that you don't, from the intellectual experience you are about to have. והסרתי את כפי, וראית את אחרי — then I will remove My palm, and you will see My back: the way I act. And then it happens: וירד ה' בענן, ויתיצב עמו שם — Hashem rests His presence with Moshe, permanently. ויעבר ה' על פניו — Hashem diverts Moshe from His face. ויקרא ה' — Hashem calls out His attributes, His back: \"ה' אל רחום וחנון, ארך אפים ורב חסד ואמת, נצר חסד לאלפים, נשא עון ופשע וחטאה ונקה.\" \"You can just jump out of excitement when you learn this.\" A question from the class: what does it mean that God Himself is \"passing\" in בעבר כבודי ? The Rabbi: בעבר means when I divert — כבודי is Hashem doing the diverting, not the destination. When I, God, divert you, I will place My palm upon you until I finish taking you from My face to My back. Part of the confusion is the chapter break (chapter divisions are a Christian concept): the end of chapter 33 tells Moshe what will happen; chapter 34 is it happening. Part Three — Interpretations 2 and 3: Targum Onkelos The Rambam accepts Onkelos's interpretation — he calls it good. Onkelos's method והנה התרגום נהג כמנהגו בדברים כאלו: דהיינו שכל דבר שאנו מוצאים אותו מיוחס לה' ויש בו הגשמה או ספק הגשמה — הוא מפרש אותו בצורת שם ונסמך, כך שכינוי הגוף יהיה לדבר שהוא נסמך לשם... Wherever a verse attributes to Hashem something corporeal — or even possibly corporeal — Onkelos adds a word , so that the physical action belongs to a created manifestation , not to God. (Rav Saadia does exactly the same, as we learned in Emunot VeDeot; interestingly, the Rambam doesn't mention him here.) Verse Onkelos והנה ה' נצב עליו (Yaakov's ladder) — how can Hashem stand ? He has no body יקרא דה' מעתד עלוהי — the glory of Hashem stood upon it יצף ה' ביני ובינך (Lavan and Yaakov's pact — Hashem will watch between us; does He have eyes?) יסך מימרא דה' — the word of Hashem will watch (hashgachah) כך נהג אונקלוס עליו השלום... וכן עשה לגבי מה שנאמר \"ויעבר ה' על פניו\": ואעבר ה' שכינתיה על אפוהי וקרא. Hashem passed His Shechinah before him, and called. אם כן, לדעתו הדבר שעבר הוא נברא, בלי ספק; והוא פירש את כינוי הגוף ב\"פניו\" שמוסב על משה רבינו. Interpretation 2 (2A): a created light passing in Moshe's presence Question Onkelos (2A) 1. What is עבר? Meaning 1 — physical passing — of a created manifestation (the Shechinah, the glory), not of God 2. Whose face? Moshe's 3. How to read it? ויקרא ה' — Hashem called the attributes And on this reading, bowing makes sense. The Shechinah, a created light, passes before Moshe; we replicate it, and bow as it passes our face. \"על פניו\" means \"in his presence\" ולפי זה, פירוש \"על פניו\" — בנוכחותו, כמו שנאמר: ותעבר המנחה על פניו. (Bereishit 32:22) This is very important. On the Rambam's reading, פניו is a literal \"face\" — Hashem's face, as opposed to His back. On Onkelos's reading, על פניו doesn't mean Moshe's literal face, but his presence . The proof: Yaakov's gifts to Esav (we'll read it in Vayishlach in a few weeks) passed על פניו — not over his face, but in front of him, in his presence . Interpretation 3 (2B): a prophetic vision But why does the Rambam choose this verse , of all the verses about gifts passing? Look at the end of it : ותעבר המנחה על פניו, והוא לן בלילה ההוא במחנה. He was sleeping. So Onkelos can also be read with meaning 3 : the Shechinah passed \"before him\" — in a prophetic vision. Not a created, physical light at all: it happened in Moshe's mind. The Rambam doesn't say this here, but he says it explicitly at the end of the chapter , and that is why he brings this verse. Question Onkelos (2B) 1. What is עבר? Meaning 3 — a prophetic experience passing through Moshe's mind 2. Whose face? Moshe's — \"in his presence\" 3. How to read it? ויקרא ה' — Hashem called Support for Onkelos from the verse itself ואישוש לפירוש אונקלוס ניתן למצוא בדברי הכתוב: \"והיה בעבר כבדי\" — הרי שם נאמר במפורש שמה שיעבור הוא דבר מיוחס לו יתעלה, לא עצמותו... ועל הכבוד הזה נאמר \"עד עברי\" ו\"ויעבר על פניו\". The verse does say \"when My glory passes\" — which implies that what passes is something of God , His kavod , not God Himself. The Rambam will say (in a chapter in the 60s) that he can understand כבודי as God Himself — which is how Interpretation 1 reads it — but the simple meaning of כבודי is \"the glory of\" , which supports Onkelos. Summary Five meanings of עבר: (1) physical movement across space; (2) proclaiming — sound and rumour spreading; (3) a prophetic experience passing through the mind; (4) overreaching a limit (not used in this chapter); (5) skipping one target for another — diversion . Three interpretations of ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא so far: Meaning of עבר Whose face What passes / what it means 1. The Rambam (preferred) 5 — diversion Hashem's Hashem diverted Moshe from His face (essence) to His back (His ways in the world); ויקרא ה' — Hashem called the Thirteen Attributes, which are that \"back\" 2. Onkelos (2A) 1 — physical passing Moshe's (\"in his presence\") A created Shechinah, a light, passed before Moshe; then Hashem called 3. Onkelos (2B) 3 — prophetic experience Moshe's (\"in his presence\") The Shechinah passed in Moshe's mind , in a prophetic vision ( והוא לן בלילה ההוא ) The Thirteen Attributes are not thirteen parts of God — there is only one God — but thirteen ways He is manifested in the world. No human can know His essence (Yesodei HaTorah 1:10); reaching beyond what the mind can grasp leads to madness or death without Hashem's help ( ושכתי כפי עליך ). And on the Rambam's reading, bowing at ויעבר and chanting \"ויקרא, ה' ה'\" don't fit the verse. Coming Up Next week: a quick review of these three; Interpretation 4 — the Rambam's own reading using the Targum's principle of adding a word, but completely different; the Rambam's conclusions on all four; a comparison chart ; and back to עבירה and the other words the Rabbi has noted ( לא עברתי ממצותיך , לעבר בריתו ). Chapter 21 should be finished in about 45 minutes, and perhaps the short Chapter 22 in the last 10–15. During the week: reflect on this, read over the notes. We say this verse so often in tefillah that there are plenty of opportunities to think about it. If you're convinced by the Rambam and don't want to bow at ויעבר — that's fine. The point is to think about these three. B'Ezrat Hashem","KpEWXoLPY4g":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam) — Rav Dana's Hebrew edition; Feldheim edition for the Torah commentary extracts Chapter: Review of Chapters 1–9; Perek Yud (10) — Perishut , abstinence (Part 1) Topic: A review of the sefer so far; two comments from Rabbeinu Avraham's commentary on Bereishit ( טוב מאד and ויפח ); body and soul, and why the material world is a barrier; how the pursuit of wealth swallows a life, whether it is hoarded or spent; \"the unlearned grow more foolish with age\" ; the soul after death and כף הקלע ; and Elisha's question to Gechazi, העת לקחת? Schedule note: Rosh Chodesh Cheshvan 5787. We began this sefer in January–February, so we are nearly a year in; the plan is to finish by Pesach . This week the shiur was at 6:15pm; from next week it returns to 6:00–7:00pm for the winter unless otherwise notified. Chapter 10 should take about four shiurim ; then Chapters 11, 12 and 13, and the sefer is complete. A New Weekly Feature — Rabbeinu Avraham on the Parashah Rabbeinu Avraham wrote a commentary on the Torah — probably on all five books, but only Bereishit and Shemot survive. It is not well known: it was found only in the last hundred years or so , and the Hebrew edition of Bereishit and Shemot was brought out by Solomon David Sassoon (father-in-law of Rabbi Avraham Gabbai of Ohel David in London). The plan: through the weeks of Bereishit and Shemot (until Pesach), spend about five minutes each week , at the start or end of the shiur, on an idea from his commentary. The Feldheim edition of HaMaspik includes selected extracts ( likkutim ) at the back, which will serve as the baseline. Flicking through, the Rabbi noticed that many of the ideas tie in closely with HaMaspik — and that Rabbeinu Avraham quotes not only his father but often his grandfather, Rav Maimon . Part One — Review of Chapters 1–9 Why review now: there has been a long break (we learned until the end of July, had one shiur in early September, then the chagim). And perishut is a very lofty concept — beyond many of us, the Rabbi included. Learned without the framework of the previous nine chapters it would be out of place. Even with it, it is an aspiration worth learning, and Rabbeinu Avraham's understanding of perishut seems, at least on the surface, to run starkly against what his father believed and wrote . We will go into that in detail. Chapter 1 — Two paths: tzaddik and chasid Among those who serve Hashem there are two paths. The tzaddik fulfils everything required of him. The chasid goes beyond. This book is written for the chasid. But there is no point trying to be a chasid if you are not yet a tzaddik. No point being machmir in one area while being negligent in another. Very often, people who are extremely strict in one specific area are unbalanced : being laser-focused on one thing to the exclusion of everything else usually means they have an issue, often psychological, with that particular topic. If someone is like that in everything, wonderful. Chapter 2 — The traits are interrelated Work on one trait and it affects the others. We will see this tonight: perishut ties in with histapkut (Chapter 9). Chapter 3 — Taharah: purity of action, four levels Level Why a person serves Hashem 1 (lowest) Social pressure. At least he does it. 2 To make a good impression on people — and also because he cares about Hashem 3 For Hashem, but out of fear or for reward 4 (highest) Because it is simply right and the best life : Hashem commanded me; if God commanded it, it must be the best thing to do. We should aspire to the fourth level. Chapter 4 — Rachmanut: compassion Compassion matters, but it is not always right ; sometimes a person must use its opposite, achzariyut . When in doubt, veer towards compassion. The Rambam says the same in Shemonah Perakim, chapter 4 : although one should be balanced, when in doubt veer towards the better side. Chapter 5 — Nedivut: generosity Definition: giving when there is no need to . Towards others Towards oneself (one's own spending) נדיבות generosity ↔ קמצנות miserliness בזבוז extravagance ↔ חסכון constant saving Be generous to the appropriate people ; it is a mistake to be generous to everyone (as the Gemara says, mercy to the wrong people can be very wrong). Generosity can be with four things , all learned from Avraham Avinu : (1) money , (2) intellect / wisdom , (3) faith / religion , (4) influence / power . And it strengthens bitachon — another example of how the middot interrelate. Chapter 6 — Arichut apayim: calmness, slowness to anger Not apathy — apathy is not good. It means being in control of one's emotions . It is fine, sometimes correct, to show displeasure — calmly . It is no good to harbour anger and not express it, nor to feel anger and express it; work on showing displeasure without anger. Getting angry is like getting drunk. Most people get angry at big things and not small ones; to acquire this trait, don't get angry even at small things . How to train: Remember the consequences. The Rabbi tells every couple before their wedding: once something is said in anger, it can't come back — like toothpaste out of the tube, a boomerang, כחצים ביד גבור — an arrow once released. Anger and arrogance are linked : often one gets angry because one is arrogant. The Torah's stories : those who were not calm (Moshe and the water) and those who were (Yosef forgiving his brothers). The mitzvot that train patience : the cities of refuge; rebuking someone (express displeasure rather than harbouring it). In religious matters one sometimes needs to show anger without feeling it, as the prophets did. And when in doubt whether to show displeasure — refrain. The Rabbi's own experience: recently he was 95% certain it was right to show displeasure. With a 5% doubt, he went ahead — and in retrospect it was wrong. He remembered Rabbeinu Avraham: unless you are 100% certain, better not to. Chapter 7 — Anavah: humility Internal humility (genuinely felt) and external humility (how you walk, dress, speak). Towards Hashem and towards people. The story: someone says ונפשי כעפר לכל תהיה in the Amidah with great kavanah — my soul shall be like dust — then finishes and goes off shouting at people. My soul is dust before Hashem, but not before people? No: humility is כלפי הקדוש ברוך הוא and towards other people. Very important: humility means recognising your true standing and not exaggerating it. It does not mean negating your own talents, abilities and capacity. That is lowliness , which is wrong and leads to depression. We live in a world where everyone exaggerates — on the CV, everywhere — to get anywhere. Anavah means living with who you are. How to train: External humility — dress, behaviour, speech. The Torah's stories — those who lacked humility: Nadav and Avihu, Shaul, Avshalom, Doeg, Yoav and others; and the twelve mitzvot that instil anavah (converts, bikkurim, standing for the elderly, monuments and more). Analyse what people become arrogant about: purely physical things (eating, drinking) — animals have those too; semi-physical things (money, power, influence) — they breed hatred, strife and competition, as with kings; spiritual things (prophecy, wisdom) — they can be lost so easily (as the Moreh teaches, and as this chapter will mention). Religious arrogance is also very bad. Humility doesn't mean degrading yourself: wear clean, simple clothes, not dirty ones. A public figure sometimes has to act without outward humility — but must stay humble inside. Chapter 8 — Bitachon (the longest chapter) Relying on Hashem , Creator of all, to whose will everything is subservient. Not the bitachon of prophets (who divest themselves of natural means), and not the view of those who deny providence — but the bitachon for the vast majority of us: Hashem oversees all the natural intermediaries He set in the world. There are laws of science and physics; we believe in them and learn them, as Aristotle did — and we recognise that Hashem oversees them. So do your hishtadlut, but don't go overboard . Ten categories (five, each in physical and spiritual matters): avoiding seriously harmful situations; avoiding minor harms; attaining what is essential ; attaining what is important but not essential ; attaining what is not important . He also addressed verses that seem to contradict bitachon. Four levels of bitachon: Level Description 1 Trusts Hashem in his heart — but is also worried 2 Trusts and does things to strengthen the trust, such as prayer — we should all aim for at least this 3 Complete trust and confidence : he knows it will happen 4 (highest) Doesn't even \"place\" his trust , because whatever happens is good from Hashem Chapter 9 — Histapkut: contentment Contentment prevents jealousy and the pursuit of unimportant things. Train for it by aspiring only to what is necessary . The Torah's stories teach its importance. Part Two — Rabbeinu Avraham on Bereishit 1. Why \"טוב מאד\"? וירא אלהים את כל אשר עשה, והנה טוב מאד; ויהי ערב ויהי בקר יום הששי. (Bereishit 1:31) The question: אחר שאמר השם יתעלה בפרטי הבריאה \"כי טוב\", \"כי טוב\" — each day already said ki tov . Why repeat טוב at the end, and add מאד ? His answer: מפני מציאות האדם המשיג ענייני העולם — it is human nature to try to understand the world around us. Some things we can see the benefit of immediately: the sun, the moon, the heavens and the earth; most species of animals and plants. Some are beyond us: וקצתם נשגב מן האדם אופן הכוונה במציאותם והתועלת בהם. (The Rabbi: why do we need millions upon millions of stars and galaxies? To show Hashem's glory, perhaps — but it is hard to say.) Some even seem better uncreated: ולא עוד, אלא אפשר שיעלה על הדעת במקצתם שמוטב שלא נבראו — כמציאות העקרב והנחש... והארי והדב... והתמסח — the scorpion, the snake, the lion, the bear, the crocodile . ואין ראוי לאדם שיסמוך בטוב בריאתו יתעלה על הבנתו ועל מה שיגיע אליו שכלו, אלא ידע כי לשכלו ולדעתו גבול אשר יגיע אליו. We cannot rely on our own intellect to decide what was worth creating. Our understanding has a limit. But because God is perfect, everything He creates is \"very good\" — whether we understand it or not. That is what the verse says. אילו ידעתיו הייתיו — \"If I knew Him, I would be Him.\" Often attributed to Rav Saadia; the Rabbi thinks it is actually Rav Yosef Albo, in the Ikkarim . David HaMelech says the same: מה רבו מעשיך ה', כלם בחכמה עשית (Tehillim 104 — Rosh Chodesh) — Do I always understand them? No. But I know they are all with wisdom . פליאה דעת ממני, נשגבה לא אוכל לה (Tehillim 139) — some knowledge is too lofty for me. והראוי לאדם להסתכל במה שנסתפקו הקדמונים בתועלתו ושנעלם מהם — look at things whose benefit earlier generations doubted and which we now understand. The Rabbi's example: the appendix — as far as he knows, we still don't know what it is for; that's why it's called an appendix . But many things in the body and in nature were once a mystery and are now understood. Just as then, so now: every part of nature has a purpose. It is טוב מאד. Simple ideas — but beautiful. 2. Why \"ויפח\" — He breathed ? ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים, ויהי האדם לנפש חיה. (Bereishit 2:7) (This verse opens Chapter 10 tonight.) As we learn in the Moreh: everything is chomer (matter) and tzurah (form). Hashem made Adam עפר מן האדמה — that is the chomer — and gave him a tzurah : intellect, consciousness, the mind, what we call the neshamah . Why describe the giving of the form as blowing ? כדי לקרב אל הבנתנו יושר השכל ודקות אותה צורה שכלית, שהרוחות וכדומה נראה יותר זך מן הגשמים האחרים שאנו מכירים. To convey how subtle the form is . Of all our senses, the most subtle is breath and smell . Hence the Chachamim's play on כל הנשמה תהלל יה — read it as כל הנשימה : for every single breath we should praise Hashem. The soul and the breath go together. By contrast, the Rambam famously writes that touch is the most shameful of the senses . Smell is the sense most associated with the neshamah — so the Torah uses ויפח for implanting the tzurah. Part Three — Chapter 10: Perishut (Introduction) What perishut is — and the problem לפרוש means to abstain. The chapter's purpose: try to abstain from what is unnecessary. This is clearly chasidut . By the Torah itself — depending on how one understands קדושים תהיו — a person could arguably eat all the meat he wants, marry many wives and so on, all within the Torah's permission, and still be a tzaddik who has kept the whole Torah. Rabbeinu Avraham is urging us to go beyond that: to be a chasid, and to abstain. The problem: those who learned the Shemonah Perakim with the Rabbi (about two and a half to three years ago) know that the Rambam wrote against this . We cannot examine that until we have seen what Rabbeinu Avraham says. Keep it in mind; we will come back to it. You may feel you're not there yet. There is still value in learning this, as we will see. And the opening of the chapter is really an introduction — almost a continuation of Chapter 9 — on understanding, and being content with, the physical world. The material world is a barrier העולם החומרי הוא מחיצה גדולה החוצצת בין הבריאה לבין בוראו. The physical world is a great partition between creation and its Creator . כי האדם מורכב מחומר יסודי, כמו שאמרה התורה: וייצר ה' אלהים את האדם עפר מן האדמה; והיתה אצילות הנשמה מעולם העליון — והוא שהתכוון אליו הכתוב באמרו: בצלם אלהים, בדמותו. Man is made of elemental matter — dust from the ground — and of a soul from the upper world : the צלם אלהים , which is the intellect , exactly as his father explains in Moreh I:1 . וכן אמר הכתוב על הקשר והצירוף של שניהם: ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים. — the joining of the two: He breathed the tzurah into the chomer. ויהי האדם לנפש חיה — man became a living being only once the neshamah was breathed in. We are truly called alive only when we have the tzurah. (Compare Rav Saadia, Emunot VeDeot, Essay 6 , on the soul: you cannot have a body without a soul, nor a soul without a body.) והחומר בעצמו אין לו שום פעולה או תועלת, אלא כל כוחות האדם פועלים באמצעות הצורה. לפיכך אין המת רואה ואינו שומע ואינו טועם, אף על פי שכל איבריו קיימים — because הכוחות הרוחניים שקשורים באיברים נפסקו מן הגוף . A person who has just died has every limb intact — and can do nothing, because the tzurah has gone. וכן הקשר בין האדם לבין קונו קיים באמצעות הצורה, שבעזרתה יבין וישיג מה שישיג מגדולת בוראו, ויהיה קרוב אליו. Our relationship with Hashem exists through the tzurah , by which we understand what we can of His greatness. ובעלות הצורה הזאת אל מקורה — שם היא תהנה ותתענג עונג רב. ובירידתה אל מצולות השכבה התחתונה, שהיא החומר — תחשך תקוותנו מכך, וייבצר מאיתנו האושר הגדול שהשם ייעד לנו, שנאמר: באורך נראה אור. The more a person develops the tzurah, the more he will relate to Hashem when it returns to its source. The more he is sucked into physicality, the more the tzurah is covered and the harder the relationship becomes. (We see this at length in the Rambam, and in the Ramchal's Derech Hashem and Da'at Tevunot .) How the pursuit of wealth swallows a life ולכן מי שלהוט אחר הבלי העולם הזה ותאב לקנייניו — כגון הון, רכוש וכבוד — ומשתוקק לתענוגיו — כגון אכילה, שתייה, משגל וכיוצא בהם — הרי זה מוציא זמנו לריק... וגורם לנשמתו להתפשט מטה עמהם. The target that never arrives \"Once I've made X, I'll relax.\" People do this all the time. But על פי רוב אין לה שיעור — usually there is no real limit, כמו שאמר החכם: אוהב כסף לא ישבע כסף . Even with a fixed target: suppose you calculate it all — enough for you, your wife and children until you're 120, homes for your grandchildren and great-grandchildren, inflation… you'd be talking £100 million plus . Fine — once I reach that, I'll relax. ...שיסע במדבר או בים במשך שנים... ונמצא מבזבז את מרבית חייו כדי לנסות להשיג אותה. He travels the deserts and seas for years. Nothing has changed: the Rabbi has noticed that the wealthier people are, the more they are away on business — half the time they're not even here. Buying and selling, the stocks… most of a life spent trying to reach it. ימיו — עייפות ויגיעה , as David says: יצא אדם לפעלו ולעבדתו עדי ערב . ולילותיו — שינה עמוקה כדומם, מרוב עייפות , as it says: מתוקה שנת העבד . And in his few free hours? הרי טרוד במחשבות שמא טעה בדברים שהתעסק בהם בעבר, או בדברים שיתעסק בהם בעתיד. The Rabbi knows an extremely wealthy elderly man who wakes two or three times a night — goes to the toilet, checks the stocks , goes back to sleep if he can. Even his dreams: אין מראין לו לאדם אלא מהרהורי לבו , as Daniel said to Nevuchadnezzar: אנת מלכא רעיונך על משכבך סלקו. כמו שאמר שלמה: כי כל ימיו מכאבים וכעס ענינו, גם בלילה לא שכב לבו. וכך ישאר על פי רוב עד יום מותו, ולא ישיג תאוותו. — most people, and this is 100% true, never reach the target. And if he does reach it — two options Option 1 — hoard it. או שישמור את הונו ויצפינהו במשמרות הראויות, כדרך הקמצנים , of whom Shlomo said: יש רעה אשר ראיתי... איש אשר יתן לו האלהים עשר... ולא ישליטנו האלהים לאכל ממנו, כי איש נכרי יאכלנו — זה הבל וחלי רע הוא. \"I worked so hard for it — how can I spend it?\" How often do we see it: people make millions upon millions, hoard it or give very little, then die — and half goes on lawsuits , the other half to charities that abuse the money . He never enjoyed it. וחושב תמיד כיצד יסתיר את הונו וישמרהו מבעלי גנבות. And he is constantly worried about losing it: if you are worth billions, from one night to the next you can double, or lose half . Imagine losing half your wealth overnight! And when he is forced to spend something, he grieves without end — and then must refill it. Option 2 — spend it on pleasures. שיוציאנו בתאוותיו ותענוגיו... Yachts, beautiful homes scattered around the world, feasts, banquets. But: דרוש זמן רב ומחשבה רבה כדי לקבוע את הדברים הללו. \"I have to sort out my property here, and my property there…\" — the bills for each property, the insurance on each car . The Rabbi has heard this over and over, secretaries notwithstanding. ולמשל, אם יתעורר לבנות לו בית גדול ונאה — יחפוץ שיהא תואר הבית ומלאכתו מעשיהם של מומחים... — the finest kitchen, the finest wallpaper, the research… ואם עומד הוא לערוך משתה מעדנים... יחפוץ להקדיש יום תמים, אם לא למעלה מזה, להכנת המשתה. A whole day to prepare a banquet, he says. Nowadays, months. The Rabbi has seen couples delay their wedding by six months or a year — \"because we need to prepare the wedding\" — for one day that lasts a few hours, after which everyone has a stomach ache. ועל כל זאת, אם תדלדלו בבזבוז הוצאות עד שההון שהשיגו כלה — יחלו למלאות החסר... וכך יאבדו חייהם לריק ביגון ואנחה. Spend it all, then spend the rest of life refilling it. The Rabbi: these are simple points — but the beauty of the Ba'al HaMechaber is that the simplicity itself reminds us of their truth. \"The unlearned grow more foolish with age\" וכל כמה שיאריכו הימים — תחלש התקשרות הנפש למקורה. The longer such a person lives, the weaker the soul's bond to its source. And with this Rabbeinu Avraham gives a new explanation of the Chachamim: זקני עמי הארץ, כל זמן שמזקינין — טפשות מתוספת בהם , as it says: מסיר שפה לנאמנים, וטעם זקנים יקח (Iyov 12:20). ולכן רואה אתה זקניהם, כשהם תשושים וקרובה מיתתם... מרבים הבלים ושיחה בטלה: \"כעת עשינו, וכעת חרשנו, וכעת ראינו, וכעת הפסדנו, וכעת הרווחנו\" — והוא מבוקי סריקי , as it says: הקץ לדברי רוח? (Iyov 16:3) People who did not live fulfilling lives talk more and more nonsense as they age. Why? Because the longer the neshamah lives in the darkness of a purely physical identity, the more detached it becomes from its source. The Rabbi's application: if I spend my 120 years working on both — looking after my body, my health, my exercise, and my spiritual identity — wonderful. But if I spend my whole life on my guf alone, the longer my neshamah is in my body, the more detached it becomes from its makor — and the \"more foolish\" I become. \"Very interesting. I never thought of it like that.\" After death: the soul and כף הקלע עד שיבוא עליהם המוות, וינתק הקשר בין גוף לנפש, ותפול הנפש בענויים קשים. ויפסקו ממנה תענוגות העולם הזה שהשתוקקה להם, ויהא לה מכאוב כמכאובו של הסומא בהעדר עונג הראיה שאבד לו. Notice this line carefully: the pain of a blind man who once could see — he knows the pleasures of colour and sight, and no longer has them. Your soul will feel the same, because its whole identity was its physical identity , and that is gone. ( אך בשרו עליו יכאב ונפשו עליו תאבל — Iyov 14:22.) That is (a). יתר על כן, לא תהא לה גישה לדברים שלא נתעסקה בהם בחייה בעולם הזה, מן השלמויות שהם יסוד והקדמה לשלמות הנצחית. (b): it will have no access to the spiritual perfections , because it never worked on that part of itself — and it will be in constant pain. ותיחשב עם אלה שנגזר עליהם יסורי הנפש ומבוכתה, כמו שנאמר: ואת נפש איביך יקלענה בתוך כף הקלע. Kaf hakela — Kabbalah? כף הקלע is a phrase much used in Kabbalah (the Rabbi wasn't sure whether in the Zohar or the Arizal): the soul of a rasha is slung from one end of the universe to the other , perpetually. Some would say: here's a proof that the Rambam and Rabbeinu Avraham secretly knew Kabbalah, though they never mention a word of it! So let's look at the verse, the Gemara and the Rishonim. The verse Avigayil is calming David after Naval disgraced him and David wants revenge — as so often, it is the women who calm us down : ויקם אדם לרדפך ולבקש את נפשך, והיתה נפש אדני צרורה בצרור החיים את ה' אלהיך, ואת נפש איביך יקלענה בתוך כף הקלע. (Shmuel I 25:29) Don't take revenge — your soul is bound up with Hashem, and He will deal with your enemy. Rashi: כף הקלע is simply the wide piece of leather in the middle of a sling, in which the stone is placed. The Gemara (Shabbat 152b) תניא, רבי אליעזר אומר: נשמתן של צדיקים גנוזות תחת כסא הכבוד, שנאמר: והיתה נפש אדני צרורה בצרור החיים — (hence תהי נשמתו צרורה בצרור החיים on so many gravestones) . ושל רשעים זוממות והולכות, ומלאך אחד עומד בסוף העולם ומלאך אחד עומד בסוף העולם, ומקלעין נשמתן זה לזה, שנאמר: ואת נפש איביך יקלענה בתוך כף הקלע. Avigayil and David were not speaking of the World to Come, but the Gemara is doresh it — and from this developed the Kabbalistic concept of כף הקלע : the wicked in perpetual torment, thrown from end to end. Ralbag \"והיתה נפש אדני צרורה בצרור החיים\" — עולם הבא... \"ואת נפש איביך יקלענה בתוך כף הקלע\" — רוצה לומר: שלא יהיה לה השארות וקיום. No survival and no existence. As we have learned in the Rambam many times (Hilchot Teshuvah and elsewhere), karet — the greatest punishment — is that the soul ceases to exist . You sling a stone, and it is gone. This is how the Rabbi would understand the verse in the Rambam's framework. The Rabbi's open question Whom is Rabbeinu Avraham describing? Reading Implication (a) Someone who has received karet Then we have a clear proof that Rabbeinu Avraham — and most likely the Rambam, since such a major point would come from his father — held that even after karet, some element of identity remains intact, enough to feel that it has been cut off : the pain of no access to the spiritual, and of losing the physical pleasures it loved. The Rabbi thinks (from inferences, not a full proof) that the Ramchal held this; Rabbi Daniel Rowlands believes the Ramchal definitely did. (b) Someone in Olam Haba, at a very low level He has some connection, but a weak one, because he keeps thinking of the physical and isn't tuned for the spiritual. Like getting into a top university — and then sitting there thinking: was it worth it? I'm out of place here. The Rabbi isn't sure. He considers this a key passage for understanding what the Rambam and Rabbeinu Avraham held happens after karet — and there is very little commentary on Rabbeinu Avraham. Ralbag fits the view that you simply cease to exist; Rabbeinu Avraham seems to say there is perpetual pain. Elisha and Gechazi: \"Is there a time to take?\" ובכן, מי ששקוע בהבלי העולם הזה — הרי הוא מוסת ומפותה, ומעלים ומפסיד מעיקרו של ענייננו. Engross your life in the mundane, and that becomes your identity. Therefore Elisha said to Gechazi: The story: Elisha healed Naaman of tzara'at; Naaman wanted to pay; Elisha refused. Gechazi saw, ran after Naaman, and took the money. Elisha found out: העת לקחת את הכסף, ולקחת בגדים וזיתים וכרמים וצאן ובקר ועבדים ושפחות? (Melachim II 5:26) ...כדי לרכוש זיתים וכרמים ועבדים ושפחות — hiding it from me isn't the main problem; you wanted the money to buy more physical things . ותמוה הדבר שתהיה תלמיד נביא ותעסוק בכך. הסבור אתה שזמננו הקצר, שהוא כמידת חיינו בעולם הזה, נועד כדי לרכוש קניינים גשמיים כאלה ולהתענג בהם כהמון העם? וכי זה מנהגם של נביאים וחסידים, מנעוריהם עד שנזדקנו, להשיג תענוגות אלה ולבלות בהן שנותיהם? The simple meaning of העת לקחת , the Rabbi thinks, is \"Is this the appropriate time to take?\" — it's not the moment to take from Naaman. Rabbeinu Avraham reads it differently: \"Is there any time to take?\" For a prophet or a prophet's student, there is no time in this world for this. Our time is short. Summary The review: Chapter 1 — tzaddik before chasid; 2 — the traits interconnect; 3 — four levels of purity of action, aiming for \"it is simply right\" ; 4 — compassion, and when in doubt veer to it; 5 — generosity, to the right people, with money, wisdom, faith and influence; 6 — calmness: show displeasure without anger, and when in doubt refrain; 7 — humility as accurate self-knowledge, towards Hashem and people; 8 — bitachon in Hashem's oversight of nature, in ten categories and four levels; 9 — contentment. Rabbeinu Avraham on Bereishit: טוב מאד — everything Hashem made is very good even where we can't see why; our understanding has limits. ויפח — breath, the subtlest sense, conveys the subtlety of the soul. Chapter 10 (introduction): perishut — abstaining from the unnecessary — is chasidut beyond the Torah's requirement, and seems to conflict with the Rambam (to be examined). Man is chomer and tzurah; the body is alive and functions only through the tzurah, and our bond with Hashem runs through it. The material world is a barrier : wealth has no natural endpoint; it consumes days, nights, spare hours and dreams; if attained it is either hoarded in anxiety or spent in endless management and then refilled. A life lived only in the body weakens the soul's bond with its source — Rabbeinu Avraham's new reading of the unlearned grow more foolish with age . After death such a soul suffers the loss of the pleasures it lived for and has no access to the spiritual — כף הקלע , which the Gemara reads as being slung from end to end and Ralbag as ceasing to exist, leaving open whether Rabbeinu Avraham means conscious pain after karet or a low place in Olam Haba. And Elisha's העת לקחת? means: there is no time in a short life for this. Coming Up Next week, 6:00–7:00pm: the definition of perishut and its benefits — and how all this ties into perishut. We will return to the apparent conflict with the Rambam's Shemonah Perakim . A short idea from Rabbeinu Avraham on the parashah each week through Bereishit and Shemot. Chapter 10 should take about four shiurim , then Chapters 11–13 — finishing by Pesach . B'Ezrat Hashem","6SqFU46amtc":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Mishneh Torah (Rambam), Sefer Zemanim — Hilchot Shofar (three chapters only) Perek: 2 (with a note on Perek 3) Topic: Who is obligated in shofar; that the mitzvah is to hear , not to blow; who can be motzi whom; kavanah in mitzvot; and why the shofar is not blown when Rosh Hashanah falls on Shabbat Schedule note: First Thursday halachah shiur back, just before Erev Rosh Hashanah . We have been learning Hilchot Shabbat for the last few years and are around siman 320 — that now pauses for a few weeks while we learn the yamim tovim from the Rambam : Rosh Hashanah tonight, Hilchot Yom Kippur next week , and Sukkot the week after. In past years these halachot were learned from the Shulchan Aruch; the Rambam is what's wanted this year. Why Perek 2: Perek 1 is important but largely technical and less practical — a shofar up a tree, what the shofar may be made from, a shofar belonging to a non-Jew, a cracked or broken shofar. Perek 2, which deals with hearing and blowing the shofar and who is obligated , is the practical chapter. Halachah 1 — Who Is Obligated הכל חייבין לשמוע קול שופר: כהנים, לויים, וישראלים, וגרים, ועבדים משוחררים. אבל נשים ועבדים וקטנים פטורין. ומי שחציו עבד וחציו בן חורין, וטומטום ואנדרוגינוס — חייבין. The central point: the mitzvah is to hear , not to blow The Torah says יום תרועה / זכרון תרועה — a day of blowing . And yet the Chachamim interpreted the mitzvah not as the blowing but as the hearing — and this is how the Rambam codifies it, and all subsequent poskim after him. The obligation is לשמוע. Of course you must blow in order for there to be something to hear; but the mitzvah itself is the hearing. Three practical ramifications: The berachah. We do not say litkoa ; we say אשר קדשנו במצוותיו וציוונו לשמוע קול שופר . Blowing into a pit. If the shofar is blown in a pit, the listener has not fulfilled the mitzvah — he heard only the echo of the blast, not the sound of the shofar. You performed an act of blowing; he did not hear a shofar. No preference to do it yourself. Elsewhere in halachah there is a concept that it is better to perform a mitzvah personally than through an agent. Here that does not apply at all. Even if you know how to blow, there is no preference to blow for yourself at home — because the mitzvah is not blowing. Having heard it, you have fulfilled exactly the same mitzvah, at exactly the same level, as the person blowing. (This carries a further and sharper implication in Halachah 3.) The categories Women — exempt, as with all mitzvot aseh shehazman gerama . There is a mitzvah/value in their hearing it even though they are not obligated, but per the Rambam a woman does not recite the berachah , since it is something she is exempt from. (An old debate.) חציו עבד וחציו בן חורין — how a person comes to be in that state is a discussion for another time; but he is obligated, because the half of him that is free must hear the shofar . טומטום — one whose genitalia are covered, so we do not know whether male or female → obligated out of doubt . אנדרוגינוס — one with both male and female genitalia → obligated out of doubt . Halachah 2 — Who Can Be Motzi Whom כל מי שאינו חייב בדבר, אינו מוציא את החייב ידי חובתו. לפיכך אישה או קטן שתקעו בשופר — השומע מהן לא יצא. אנדרוגינוס מוציא את מינו ואינו מוציא את שאינו מינו. טומטום אינו מוציא לא את מינו ולא את שאינו מינו, שהטומטום שנקרע אפשר שיימצא זכר אפשר שיימצא נקבה. An important distinction: \"not obligated\" vs \"already fulfilled\" Someone who heard all hundred blasts in shul is no longer obligated today — may he go and blow for a man in hospital? Of course he may. When the Rambam writes כל מי שאינו חייב בדבר , he means someone who is not a bar chiyuva — not within the category of the obligated at all. A man who has already discharged his obligation today is still, in essence, an obligated person: tomorrow is the second day, and next year he will be obligated again. Contrast a woman , who will never be obligated, and a child , who is not obligated until he is no longer a child — a different person, halachically. A woman may certainly blow for other women — nothing precludes it, since they share the same level of obligation. A woman need not hear the shofar from a man. Androginos — may be motzi only another androginos . Why? Because the Gemara (in one view) treats the androginos as a distinct category of person — a beriah bifnei atzmah . (The Rabbi's contemporary comparison: much as one hears today of male, female, and non-binary — here too there is male, female, and a third category.) Two androginosim share one and the same status, so one can discharge the other's obligation. Tumtum — may be motzi no one at all, not even another tumtum . Because a tumtum is not a category but a state of ignorance : were each of them opened up, one might turn out male and the other female — and a female cannot be motzi a male. Can a tumtum blow for himself? Obviously yes: if he is male, a male has blown and heard; if female, she was never obligated in the first place. Halachah 3 — The Half-Slave, and the Proof of the Whole Principle וכן מי שחציו עבד וחציו בן חורין — אינו מוציא אפילו את עצמו, שאין צד עבדות שבו מוציא צד חירות שבו. אלא כיצד ייצא ידי חובתו? שישמע מבן חורין שיתקע לו. He cannot discharge his own obligation, because the slave-side of him — which is not obligated, like a woman — cannot produce a shofar-sound that discharges the free side of him. The four levels Level Who Can be motzi others? Can be motzi himself? 1 A man Yes — anyone Yes 2 Androginos Only another androginos Yes 3 Tumtum No one Yes 4 חציו עבד וחציו בן חורין No one No — must hear from a free man The Rabbi's question — and the answer that proves the chapter's premise How does hearing from someone else help him? Every part of him is half-slave; half his ear is hearing through a slave's receptacle! The answer returns to Halachah 1: the mitzvah is to hear, not to blow. The blower is not performing the mitzvah — he is causing it to be performed by producing a sound to be heard. So when the half-slave blows for himself, the sound emerging from the instrument comes from someone only half obligated , and hearing such a sound cannot discharge the obligation. But when he hears from a fully obligated man, the sound is fully valid — and whatever part of him is obligated has now heard it and is discharged. This is the clearest demonstration in the chapter that the essence of the mitzvah is לשמוע קול שופר. Homiletically (a whole derashah could be built on this): the mitzvah is not to blow but to hear — and shomea in Hebrew means to receive , to let something sink in; it also carries the sense of to understand . The mitzvah is to understand the sound of the shofar. Halachah 4 — Kavanah המתעסק בתקיעת שופר להתלמד — לא יצא ידי חובתו, וכן השומע מן המתעסק לא יצא. Someone merely practising has not fulfilled the mitzvah, and neither has one who hears from him — even though the correct sounds were produced — because he never made a conscious decision to perform the mitzvah. Shitat HaRambam: מצוות צריכות כוונה The Rambam evidently holds — here and in what follows — that mitzvot require intention , at least mitzvot de'oraita . This is a major debate, but this chapter is a clear indication of his position. The famous contradiction — matzah אכל מצה בלא כוונה, שאנסוהו עכו\"ם — יצא ידי חובתו (Hilchot Chametz U'Matzah) If mitzvot require kavanah, how can force-feeding discharge the mitzvah? Answer 1 — the Maggid Mishneh (which the Rabbi finds difficult): he was forced, but he knew it was Pesach and he knew this was matzah — so there was kavanah even while being coerced. The Shulchan Aruch follows this in Hilchot Pesach: אכל מצה בלא כוונה, כגון שאנסוהו לסטים — יצא , provided he knows it is Pesach, knows it is matzah, and knows he is obligated. If he thought it was an ordinary day, or did not realize it was matzah, he has not fulfilled it. Answer 2 — Rabbeinu Nissim (the Ran), and the Kesef Mishneh — the Rabbi's preferred approach. To understand it, an important principle: Excursus: meizid / shogeg / mitasek Using Shabbat as the model: Category Definition Consequence Meizid I knowingly and deliberately transgress Full liability Shogeg Inadvertent — either I did not know it is forbidden, or I did not realize it is Shabbat Korban Mitasek I did not even know what action I was performing — leaning back onto a light switch and switching it on; uprooting grass while walking Patur — no korban at all But mitasek is not always exempt. In forbidden foods and arayot , a mitasek still requires kapparah — because he benefited ; the substance was ingested, the act was consummated. (Someone who does not know the fat before him is forbidden fat, or knows the fat but not that it is forbidden, is shogeg and brings a korban. Someone who has it poured down his throat without knowing what it is, or eats it while dreaming, is mitasek — and still requires kapparah, because his body ingested it. Likewise one who is pushed into forbidden relations.) And so with matzah Even holding מצוות צריכות כוונה , says the Ran: with shofar , hearing requires intention to hear, and there is nothing more to the act. But with matzah , the act of ingestion is itself sufficient — just as with forbidden fats and forbidden relations, where the substance becoming part of your body carries consequence without kavanah. So the Rambam's ruling on matzah is not a contradiction; matzah is the exception, for either of these reasons — he knew it was Pesach and matzah, or, better, because ingestion suffices. Mutual kavanah — blower and listener נתכוון שומע לצאת ידי חובתו ולא נתכוון התוקע להוציאו, או שנתכוון התוקע להוציאו ולא נתכוון השומע לצאת — לא יצא ידי חובתו, עד שיתכוונו שניהם. Both must intend — the listener to fulfil, the blower to discharge him. A practical note — the Magen Avraham, 400 years ago: the very fact that you have come to shul on Rosh Hashanah , and come for the shofar , and are standing for the berachah, is itself sufficient kavanah. You do not have to positively think \"I am now fulfilling the mitzvah.\" The modern practice of someone announcing \"everyone have in mind to be yotzei\" is, in the Rabbi's words, very nice — but superfluous. Halachah 5 — Blowing for Whoever Hears מי שתקע ונתכוון להוציא כל השומע תקיעתו, ושמע השומע ונתכוון לצאת ידי חובתו — אף על פי שאין התוקע מתכוון לזה פלוני ששמע תקיעתו ואינו יודעו — יצא, שהרי נתכוונו שניהם. לפיכך מי שהיה מהלך בדרך או יושב בתוך ביתו ושמע תקיעות משליח ציבור — יצא אם נתכוון לצאת, שהרי שליח ציבור מתכוון להוציא את הרבים ידי חובתן. Even though the blower has no idea this particular person exists or is listening — his blanket intention \"for anyone who hears me,\" plus the listener's intention to fulfil, is enough. The Rambam writes elsewhere that one appointed to perform a mitzvah on behalf of others carries a chazakah of intending it for anyone: unless he thinks or states otherwise, the presumption is that a person blowing shofar is blowing on behalf of all who hear. Practically: Someone ill at home who lives close enough to the shul to hear the blasts can fulfil the mitzvah from his bed — the fact that the blower has no idea there is a person next door is irrelevant. (The Rabbi has known someone for whom this was actually relevant.) Someone walking past a shul, who had not intended to attend that day, who stops, hears, and has it in mind — has fulfilled the mitzvah. So far: Halachot 1–3 dealt with categories of people; Halachot 4–5 with intention. The chapter now turns to Shabbat. Halachah 6 — Why We Do Not Blow When Rosh Hashanah Falls on Shabbat Directly relevant this year, since the first day of Rosh Hashanah falls on Shabbat. יום טוב של ראש השנה שחל להיות בשבת — אין תוקעין בשופר בכל מקום. אף על פי שהתקיעה משום שבות ומן הדין היה שיתקעו, יבוא עשה של תורה וידחה שבות דבריהם. First, what the reason is not There is a rabbinic prohibition on playing or sounding any musical instrument on Shabbat — גזירה שמא יתקן כלי שיר — and a shofar is a musical instrument. But that is not the reason we don't blow. Were that the only obstacle, we would say the mitzvat aseh from the Torah overrides the rabbinic shevut . The actual reason גזירה שמא ייטלנו בידו ויוליכנו למי שיתקע לו, ויעבירנו ארבע אמות ברשות הרבים, או יוציאנו מרשות לרשות — וזה סקילה. שהכול חייבין בתקיעה ואין הכול בקיאין לתקוע. The decree is against carrying — that a person will pick up the shofar and take it to someone who can blow for him, carrying four amot in a reshut harabim or transferring between domains, which is a capital-level Shabbat violation . And this is a live risk precisely because everyone is obligated but not everyone knows how to blow . The commentators' question — the shofar up the tree In Perek 1 (and explicitly in the Mishnah, with no dispute) the Rambam rules: it is the first day of Rosh Hashanah on a weekday, and your only shofar is up a tree . May you climb up and get it? You may not — even though blowing is a mitzvah de'oraita and climbing a tree on yom tov is only derabbanan. (You may engage a non-Jew — שבות דשבות במקום מצווה — but assume there is none and no other shofar.) So we do not always say the aseh overrides a derabbanan. Why then invoke that principle here? The answer: the principle applies where there was no alternative . With the shofar in the tree there was a possibility, at least theoretically — you could have retrieved it before yom tov. But when Rosh Hashanah coincides with Shabbat there is nothing whatsoever that could have been done ; on that reasoning the aseh certainly would override the shevut on musical instruments — hence the need for the separate decree about carrying. Rav Kappach's observation — the Rambam departs from the Gemara The Gemara gives the reason as: you will carry the shofar to a baki , an expert, to teach you how to blow . The Rambam instead writes that you will carry it to someone to blow for you . Why the change? Perhaps because he felt it unlikely that a person would actually learn to blow on the day — and he wanted the more common scenario: you know someone who can blow, he doesn't happen to have a shofar with him, so you carry yours to him. The Rabbi's reflections on the severity of carrying Consider what this halachah means: the entire Jewish world forgoes hearing the shofar because of the concern that a tiny fraction of the population might carry — and this in an era when many communities have an eruv . It is a striking demonstration of the seriousness of Shabbat, and of hotza'ah in particular. Tosafot at the beginning of Masechet Shabbat already notes the oddity that the masechet opens with the laws of carrying and the four domains — when carrying is the penultimate melachah, the thirteenth in order. Why open there? Because people have always treated carrying as though it were not really forbidden. You know people — as does everyone — who consider themselves fully shomer Shabbat, who would never use electricity, drive, or cook, but who carry a handkerchief or their keys in their pocket, thinking: what's wrong with that? That attitude existed then too — and precisely because of it, a person might well pick up the shofar and carry it. The Ran's striking suggestion Rabbeinu Nissim offers a theoretical point (which others dispute). Really, he says, the Chachamim would never have cancelled everyone's mitzvah for the sake of the few who might err inadvertently. But in their time nobody knew for certain which of the two days was the real Rosh Hashanah — so blowing on any given day was only a safek mitzvah de'oraita , and for a mere doubt, yes, it is worth forgoing to prevent carrying. He asks on himself: then why did they not blow in Yerushalayim and its surroundings, where the true day was known? Answer: they did not want a split practice — some places blowing, others not. And today, when we know exactly when Rosh Hashanah is? The halachah is long established and is not changed — but it is an interesting thing to sit with. Halachah 7 — Children Practising (Based on the Mishnah in Rosh Hashanah, perek 4.) התינוקות שלא הגיעו לחינוך — אין מעכבין אותן מלתקוע בשבת של יום טוב של ראש השנה שחל להיות בו... ומותר לגדול שיתעסק עמהן כדי ללמדן ביום טוב, בין קטן שהגיע לחינוך בין קטן שלא הגיע — שהתקיעה משום שבות. Children below the age of chinuch are not stopped from blowing — certainly on Rosh Hashanah, but even on an ordinary Shabbat . If you see a five- or six-year-old walking around blowing a shofar, do not stop him : it is a good thing that he learns and practises. (The Rabbi's own almost-four-year-old goes round blowing his toy shofar constantly — and even with a real shofar it would be fine.) And an adult may even help train them — but note carefully what that means: verbal guidance only. \"That was a good sound — can you do it again? That one should be a bit shorter.\" You may not take the shofar in your own hand and blow it to demonstrate. All of this is permitted because the prohibition on blowing is only a shevut . The practical corollary — blowing \"for fun\" Since blowing is really forbidden as a musical instrument and is only permitted for the sake of the mitzvah , many poskim rule that once the quota of blasts (100/101) is complete, one may not blow again. Someone comes over and says \"I want to see if I can blow\" — on Rosh Hashanah falling on a weekday, or any day — you should not give it to him : the mitzvah is done, and the rabbinic prohibition on musical instruments is back in force. Exception: blowing for women is fine — there is no obligation but there is still a mitzvah in their hearing it, and that suffices to override the shevut. A rejected opinion held that once blowing is permitted at all on the day, one may blow as much as one likes — 200, 300, 400 blasts, all part of the mitzvah. That view was not accepted. This was the answer to the poll the Rabbi posted in the daily halachah group that morning: no, you may not — unless you are doing it for your wife. Halachah 8 — Where the Decree Did Not Reach: Temple Times כשגזרו שלא לתקוע בשבת, לא גזרו אלא במקום שאין בו בית דין. אבל בזמן שבית המקדש קיים והיה בית דין הגדול בירושלים — היו הכול תוקעין בירושלים כל זמן שבית דין יושבין. Two conditions together: the Beit HaMikdash standing and the Sanhedrin sitting in Jerusalem (recall that there were periods when the Sanhedrin sat elsewhere — Yavneh, Usha). And not only the people of Jerusalem : אלא כל עיר שהיא בתוך תחום ירושלים — within the techum (2,000 amot), והיא רואה את ירושלים — and can see Jerusalem — לא שהיא בתוך הנחל , not sunk in a valley, והיא שומעת קול ירושלים — and is near enough to hear it — ולא שהיא בראש ההר , not perched high on a mountain, looking down but out of earshot, ויכולה לבוא לירושלים — and can walk in — לא שיש נהר מפסיק ביניהם , with no river cutting them off. אנשי אותה העיר תוקעין... אבל בשאר עיירות אין תוקעין. Why? Because of the eimah — the awe of the Sanhedrin sitting there. And note: this was not restricted to blowing before the beit din. Every small shul fifteen hundred amot outside Jerusalem blew , because the presence of the Sanhedrin radiated outward and no one would come to carry. A note on \"Yerushalayim\": as we have discussed in the past, this means Jerusalem proper — roughly the Old City, up to Sha'ar Yafo and Sha'ar Shechem. Not Har Nof, not Sorotzkin, not the modern neighbourhoods. Halachah 9 — Nowadays: Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai's Takanah ובזמן הזה שחרב בית המקדש — Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai instituted that כל מקום שיש בו בית דין קבוע — tokin. The Rambam's conditions, stacked: בית דין קבוע — a fixed, permanent, established beit din. Not three men who assemble ad hoc (of the sort one sees today with certain conversion batei din). והוא שיהיו בארץ ישראל — in Eretz Yisrael. With proper semichah — dayanim genuinely ordained in the unbroken chain. We do not have this today. (There were attempts to re-establish semichah — famously in the 1500s — which we have discussed; in practice it does not exist today.) ואין תוקעין בשבת אלא בבית דין שקידשו את החודש — and specifically a beit din with the standing to sanctify the new moon . Semichah alone is not enough; אבל שאר בתי דינין אין תוקעין בהן אלא אם כן היו סמוכין . ואין תוקעין אלא בפני בית דין בלבד, כל זמן שיושבין — and only in the presence of the beit din , while they sit. אפילו ננערו לעמוד — תוקעין בפניהם ; even if they have risen to their feet it is fine. אבל חוץ לבית דין אין תוקעין. ולמה תוקעין בפני בית דין? מפני שבית דין זריזין הן, ולא יבואו התוקעין להעבירו ארבע אמות בפניהם, שהרי בית דין עומדין עליהם. Note the contrast with Halachah 8: in Temple times the Sanhedrin's presence covered the entire city and its surroundings , whatever the local shul. Post-churban, the permission is confined to the beit din's own presence — three dayanim, but samuch dayanim, and only where they are. The machloket View Requirement Consequence Rashi (one extreme) A beit din of 71 — the Sanhedrin Effectively never applies Rambam Three , but with genuine semichah and the standing to sanctify the month, and only in their presence Does not apply today, for lack of semichah Rif (other extreme) Three , even without full semichah And it is well recorded that Rabbi Yitzchak Alfasi himself blew the shofar in his beit din on Shabbat Rosh Hashanah There have been attempts through the ages to revive the practice in cities with batei din, but the accepted practice has been as the Rambam writes — and since we have no semichah today, it does not apply. A participant asked: what about a beit din of 23? Yes, all the more so — if three suffices, twenty-three certainly does. But the same characteristics are required : semichah, and the standing to sanctify the month. Which is precisely the problem today: take twenty-three of today's dayanim, or even seventy-one, and it means nothing — because each one individually is \"zero\" , lacking semichah. And even then it would only work in the presence of the dayanim — you would have to go to the beit din itself. Halachah 10 — The Two Days of Golus בזמן הזה שאנו עושין שני ימים בגלות — כדרך שאנו עושין בראשון כך עושין בשני. ואם חל יום ראשון להיות בשבת ואין תוקעין במקום בית דין — הרי אלו תוקעין ביום שני בלבד. Exactly our situation this year: day one falls on Shabbat, so we blow only on the second day . One difference the Rambam does not write here but held elsewhere: that shehecheyanu is not said on the second day . This year, however, we will say shehecheyanu — since the shofar is first heard on day two. Perek 3 — For Your Own Learning Chapter 3 covers how many blasts are required and how each blast is to be sounded . It contains some unique positions of the Rambam and is beyond the scope of this evening — learn it over Rosh Hashanah . Summary The Torah speaks of teruah , but Chazal — and the Rambam after them — define the mitzvah as hearing the shofar: hence the berachah לשמוע קול שופר , hence a blast heard as an echo from a pit does not count, hence there is no virtue in blowing for oneself, and hence the half-slave cannot discharge even his own obligation but must hear from a free man. Only a bar chiyuva can be motzi others — so a woman or child cannot blow for a man (though a man who has already heard may blow for others, and a woman for women); an androginos only for another androginos; a tumtum for no one but himself. Kavanah is required on both sides, blower and listener — though the Magen Avraham holds that coming to shul for the shofar is itself sufficient intent — and a blanket intention \"for whoever hears\" covers the sick man at home and the passer-by in the street. Finally, when Rosh Hashanah falls on Shabbat , the shofar is silenced not because it is a musical instrument (the aseh would override that shevut) but out of the fear that someone will carry it to a person who can blow — a decree whose scope shows the gravity of hotza'ah, and which was lifted only in Jerusalem in Temple times and, post-churban, in the presence of a properly ordained beit din. This year, day one being Shabbat, we blow only on the second day. Coming Up Next week: a chapter from the Rambam's Hilchot Yom Kippur , in anticipation of Yom Kippur. The week after: a chapter on Sukkot . Then back to Hilchot Shabbat , siman 320 onwards. Perek 3 of Hilchot Shofar — the number and form of the blasts — to be learned privately over Rosh Hashanah. B'Ezrat Hashem","mLfgudcuAVg":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam) — Rav Dana's Hebrew edition Chapter: Perek Tet (9) — Histapkut , contentment / self-sufficiency Topic: The meaning and root of histapkut ; how one trains for it; its relationship to bitachon (Ch 8) and perishut (Ch 10); the Torah's own testimony to the trait; the spiritual damage caused by its absence; and the practice of the prophets in receiving gifts Schedule note: First shiur back after the summer break, a few days before Rosh Hashanah. Normally each new term opens with a summary of everything learned so far — but the calendar makes that impractical now: next Monday is Tzom Gedaliah (no shiur, because of Minchah/Maariv), then Yom Kippur , then Chol HaMoed Sukkot , then Asru Chag . So Chapter 9 — short and self-contained — is being learned in full tonight, and HaMaspik then breaks until after Sukkot . On return: a full recap of Chapters 1–9 , then Chapter 10 — Perishut (abstinence) , and the sefer should be finished comfortably before Pesach , probably earlier. New feature from after Sukkot: aside from HaMaspik, Rabbeinu Avraham also wrote a commentary on the Torah — he wrote on all five books as far as we know, but only Bereishit and Shemot survive. Each week the last 5–10 minutes of the shiur will be given to a selected piece of his commentary on the weekly parashah, which will carry us through Bereishit and Shemot from after Sukkot until Pesach. (Shiur time may shift slightly in October until the clocks change — watch the group.) Recap — Where We Are Chapter Trait Earlier chapters The path of Hashem: purity of action, humility, and the other middot covered in the first part of the sefer 8 Bitachon — reliance and trust in Hashem. A long chapter with many parts, completed at the end of the summer term 9 (tonight) Histapkut — contentment 10 (next) Perishut — abstinence Part One — What Does Histapkut Mean? The Root ס־פ־ק The tav in histapkut is only the hitpa'el (reflexive) form; the root is ספק — samech, peh, kuf. The Rabbi raised a genuine open question here: what does this root actually mean in Tanach? He researched it and could not resolve it, and invites anyone who has seen an answer to share it. There are three distinct connotations , and he could not find the link between them: 1. To clap / to strike ויחר אף בלק אל בלעם ויספק את כפיו After Balak's three failed attempts to have Bilam curse, he clapped his hands . (A participant noted that in Arabic the root likewise means to clap — the Rabbi: \"exactly, very good.\") Note: פוסחים על שתי הסעפים is a different root entirely — that is a se'if, not a safek. 2. To provide / to supply what suffices Lesapek — to provide for someone: סיפק לו את צרכיו , סיפק להם את המן . Common in rabbinic literature. Why does providing come from this root? Because when you provide for someone, you give them what is sufficient for them . This is the sense we are using tonight: to suffice, to be enough . 3. Doubt — safek . The Rabbi's sense is that this is the latest of the three usages. Ordinarily, when a Hebrew root carries several connotations, thinking properly will reveal a connection between them. Here the Rabbi could not find one — clapping, sufficing, and doubt. Worth thinking about. Flora's question: in modern Hebrew we say lo hispakti — \"I didn't manage to.\" Is that the same word? Yes — the same idea: I was not sufficient, I did not have enough. Sufficient and content are the same word. (And no, it is not from doubt — that is the separate third connotation.) So histapkut is a reflexive noun from the second sense: I am self-sufficient, I am self-content, I have everything I need. Part Two — The Definition (Rabbeinu Avraham's Opening) הסתפקות מן המידות התרומיות Contentment is among the exalted character traits. ופירושה: שיהא אדם שמח בחלקו מקנייני עולם הזה, ולא יהא בהול ורודף להוסיף עליהם A person is happy with his portion of this world's possessions, and is not frantically chasing after more and more. והיא מעידה על העדר חמדנות ומיעוט החיבה לתענוגי העולם הזה, המביאה את האדם לידי עבירות ועוולות ופוגעת בהשגת השלמות The trait testifies to the absence of covetousness and to only a slight attachment to the pleasures of this world — an attachment which otherwise leads a person into sins and injustices and damages the pursuit of perfection ( shelemut ). והלא יודע אתה כמה הדגישה התורה את איסור החמדנות והתאווה במקרא עשרת הדיברות Look how strongly the Torah stressed the prohibition of covetousness and desire in the Ten Commandments themselves — lo tachmod . ואין דבר המביא לידי חמדנות ותאווה כמו אי הסתפקות — וזללנות ורעבתנות וחמדת עולם הזה וקנאה And nothing brings a person to covetousness and desire like the absence of contentment — gluttony, ravenousness, craving for this world, and jealousy. In short: when you have this trait, you desire less overall, and in particular you stop desiring other people's property and possessions . The Core of the Trait ועיקר הסתפקות הוא שלא תהא הנפש תלויה בתענוגות העולם, ולא תשאף אלא לדבר שהוא בגדר ההכרח The essence: the soul is not dependent on the world's pleasures, and aspires only to what falls within the category of necessity . I aspire to a home to live in. I aspire to food and drink, and not to worry where the next meal comes from. I aspire to provide for my children's needs. That is what contentment aspires to. How You Train For It וההכשרה המביאה לידי הסתפקות: הכשרת הנפש והרגלתה להסתפק בדברים שהם בהכרח ולהתרחק מן המותרות The training that produces contentment is habituating the soul to suffice with the necessary and to keep away from luxuries. The Rabbi's illustration: there is nothing wrong with a treat. It is Shabbat or Yom Tov, you want a nice ice cream, you want to order a McFlurry — enjoy, no problem at all. But during an ordinary week, when a person trains himself to say \"I don't need this. There is nothing special about today that requires this ice cream — so I won't have it\" — that is the training. Why take more when I already have what I need? (This develops much further in Chapter 10, perishut.) וממעט בתקוות השווא ולא ישתדל בהם And it reduces vain hopes and the effort spent on them — freeing a person for productive and spiritual development. There Is No Satiety in Physical Things וכבר אמר שלמה המלך: אוהב כסף לא ישבע כסף One who loves money is never sated by money. This is simply the nature of physical things : having something makes you want more of it. There is no such thing as being complete in the material. (The Rambam treats this in the Moreh as well — and we will meet it in Chapter 17 , next week's Moreh chapter.) והרי זו דוגמה לשאר דברים — this is only an example ; it is not specifically about money. If you love ice cream you will never be satisfied by it either. Yes, you may eat so much that you feel ill and want no more — but a few hours later, tomorrow, you want more. Whatever it is in life, you will always want more. והוא דבר שאין לו קצבה — it has no limit, כמו שנאמר: לא תשבע עין לראות ולא תמלא אזן משמע . אמר אבא מורי — Rabbeinu Avraham quotes his father, the Rambam (in the Moreh, section three): If you feel you must have silver vessels — remember there are finer gold ones. And if you aspire to gold — think of those who have crystal . וכל בר דעת יעריך אפוא את מידת ההסתפקות, כי היא עושרו האמיתי Any sensible person will therefore value contentment — because it is his true wealth . Which brings to mind exactly what everyone is thinking of — the Mishnah in Pirkei Avot: איזהו עשיר? השמח בחלקו That is true ashirut . And this is the Rabbi's point about material things: you may be temporarily satisfied, but the reality is you will want more. Think of the well-known studies of lottery winners — within a very short time, weeks or at best months, they return to their original baseline of happiness. Yes, life may be more convenient and the financial worries lighter — but in terms of happiness , nothing has moved. Q&A — Olivia: Wanting Something for a Child Olivia asked: if a person is content for himself but wishes something for his child — for example, that the child should get married — is that a failure of contentment? It isn't a material thing; for a man it is a mitzvah from the Torah, and for a woman too there is a mitzvah. The Rabbi's answer — and he noted it revealed the progression of the chapters beautifully: Forget the child for a moment; take yourself. I am not married and I want to get married. That is not a lack of histapkut — because I know it is the right thing in life for me. The Torah says clearly לא טוב היות האדם לבדו ; I know what the Chachamim say, I know the halachah, I know the mitzvah. You cannot say \"I'll be content and stay unmarried\" — that is wrong. Like every character trait, contentment applied in the wrong place is a fault, not a virtue. So how do the two chapters divide? Domain Which trait applies Material matters — money, food, a house, possessions Chapter 9 — Histapkut. I have what Hashem has given me; I am content. Non-material, obligatory matters — mitzvot, Torah values, marriage Want it, desire it, pursue it. That is not a lack of contentment. And if you do not have it yet — Chapter 8, Bitachon. It will come at the right time. So: I am not married and I am not content about it — should I be depressed? No: bitachon. That is exactly how Chapter 8 flows into Chapter 9. Part Three — Histapkut and Perishut (the Chapter 10 Connection) וההסתפקות היא יסוד ושורש למידת הפרישות Contentment is the foundation and root of abstinence — that a person minimizes even from the permitted (as the whole next chapter explains, including the intent behind sigufim ). ומבחינה ידועה נובעת הסתפקות מן הפרישות And from a certain perspective the arrow runs the other way : contentment flows from abstinence — כי מסתפק הוא מי שאינו מצטער ממה שלא השיג מצרכיו, ושמח בחלקו הניתן לו , since the content person is the one who is not pained by what he did not attain and is happy with the portion given him. נמצא אפוא שהפורש מענייני העולם, מחייבת פרישותו למידת ההסתפקות — וכל בעל פרישות אפוא בעל הסתפקות. אבל לא כל בעל הסתפקות הוא בעל פרישות. The rule: everyone who has reached perishut necessarily has histapkut — you could not truly abstain without being content. But not everyone who is content has reached perishut. A worked example: Baruch Hashem, I have a good home, a good family, enough money — I have what I need, and I also partake of things beyond the strictly necessary. You have the means, you enjoy it, there is nothing wrong with it. You are Chapter 9, but you are not yet Chapter 10. Flora's exchange: she raised Rashi on קדושים תהיו — that holiness means abstaining min ha'arayot — and asked whether holiness and contentment connect: part of feeling holy is feeling whole, feeling that you need nothing else. The Rabbi was not convinced of the link to histapkut specifically; and the Ramban's famous נבל ברשות התורה on that same verse belongs squarely to Chapter 10 — perishut , not to this chapter. Part Four — Histapkut Is One of the Torah's Own Aims וההסתפקות היא אחת ממטרות התורה Rabbeinu Avraham brings three proofs: 1. לא תחמד ולא תתאווה בית רעך — the Torah's own prohibition on covetousness (Ten Commandments). 2. The qualifications of judges. When Yitro advises Moshe on the system of judges he lists seven qualities, among them שונאי בצע — haters of unjust gain. Rabbeinu Avraham (in his commentary on the Torah) explains this to mean not merely that you hate money, but that you have no desire for it at all . That is a very high level — complete contentment — and the Torah makes it a qualification for the bench . 3. Ben Sorer U'Moreh. Consider carefully what the verses say and what Chazal transmitted orally about the rebellious son — a boy stoned, with his own father and mother handing him over and commanded not to pity him . What did he do that was so terrible? הוא חטא התאווה, כמו שכתוב: זולל וסבא — וכן תרגם המתרגם: זליל בבישרא וסבי חמרא — he devours meat and guzzles wine. ודברים אלו נועדו לבטא זולל וסובא בעצמו, כמו שנאמר: אל תהי בסבאי יין בזללי בשר למו Even though the case of ben sorer u'moreh also involves theft of property and breaking away from the mussar of the words of God and of halachah , its essence is gluttony and drunkenness , as the Gemara in Sanhedrin has it. The Rabbi's explanation: the Gemara stacks up so many conditions — he must have stolen from his parents' money, used it to buy meat and wine, consumed them within a very short window, his parents must not be disabled, and (per one opinion) must have identical voices — that the case becomes practically impossible; one opinion holds it never happened and never will . That is not the point. The point is the value the parashah teaches : the root is zolel v'sovei — an indulgent person, never content, always wanting more. Part Five — The Damage Done by Its Absence הוה זהיר אפוא שלא תדבק נפשך בתענוגי העולם... על כן תסתפק בהכרחי ותרגיל עצמך לשבוע בהכרחי, כי תענוגות הללו הן מכשול גדול לדרכך בפרישות ודע, כי אי ההסתפקות היא הסיבה היותר חזקה להיכשל בעבירות חמורות ולדחות מצוות עשה גדולות Know that a lack of contentment is the single most powerful cause of falling into serious sins and of pushing aside great positive commandments. This is quite a statement. Why is it so? Step 1 — Most people never attain what they aspire to כי מי שמטפח תקוות גדולות בענייני העולם הזה — לא ישיג על פי רוב את תאוותו, ולא יצלח בידו הרבה ממה שהוא מתאווה לו \"I want to make X amount, I want that kind of house, that kind of car.\" It is a fact of life that the majority do not attain the material goals they set out with, כמו שאמרו חכמינו: אוהב כסף לא ישבע כסף . Step 2 — The first aveirah: bitul Torah ומתוך כך מוציא רוב עיתותיו ועונותיו להשיג את תקוותיהם — he spends most of his hours and years chasing it. עבירה הראשונה שעובר בזה היא ביטול מצווה שהיא עיקר כל המצוות — the first transgression is neglect of the mitzvah that is the root of them all : talmud Torah . שהרי אמרו תנאים: כך היא דרכה של תורה — פת במלח תאכל, ומים במשורה תשתה, ועל הארץ תישן, וחיי צער תחיה, ובתורה אתה עמל The Rabbi's crucial qualification: a person must work to earn and provide, and if the job genuinely requires those hours, that is fine — that is the will of Hashem . The problem is the hours worked beyond what parnasah requires, purely to have the most beautiful house in the community, the most money, the best car. Those extra hours are bitul Torah. And this explains the Mishnah: it is not a glorification of poverty but a statement of what acquiring Torah requires — if you constantly want more and better, you will not have the time to acquire Torah at all. Step 3 — And if honest means don't get you there ואם לא השיג התקוות הללו בכשר — כגון שיסע בים וביבשה וממדינה למדינה לקנות סחורה בזול — אז יקנה... מגזל ואבק גזל ושבועת שוא במשא ומתן First the endless travel — by sea and land, country to country, buying cheap and selling. (The Rabbi's observation: the wealthier a person is, the more he seems to be on planes. ) And when even that fails to reach the target: theft, the dust of theft, and false oaths in business. Step 4 — And if you do attain it ואם השיג התקוות הללו — ישתעבד בתענוגותיהם ויכנס עוד בתפלות עסקיו של עולם Now you are enslaved to the pleasures themselves . I have the money — now I must use it: parties to throw, the finest materials for the finest house. And so you still cannot learn Torah or do the mitzvot, because you are consumed with spending what you made. And that assumes you reach it in time to enjoy it. Most who do reach their aspirations reach them too late — the dream home is finally built, and they are already pensioners who can barely walk through it. Step 5 — Mitzvot hollowed out הן ביציאת חוץ מן המצווה... הן בביטול תוכה — either you drop the mitzvah entirely, or you empty it of its content : כגון מי שקורא קריאת שמע או מתפלל שחרית במהירות עצומה — Shema or Shacharit raced through; מתפלל ערבית מתוך שינה — Arvit prayed half-asleep, מתוך כוח המשא ורוב העבודה כל היום , from the sheer weight of the day's business; ויברך בחטיפה ברכת המוציא, שכיוון שגמר ברכתו השיג תאוותו — HaMotzi snatched, because the moment the berachah ends he has what he wanted; ויחטוף ברכת המזון בעוד לבו ועיניו נרדמים מחמת כרסו המטופחת — Birkat HaMazon grabbed while his heart and eyes are dozing from his stuffed stomach. On HaMotzi: the berachah is only ten words — ברוך אתה ה' אלוקינו מלך העולם המוציא לחם מן הארץ — which is why we place ten fingers , both hands, on the bread as we say it. Not only on Shabbat — any time you have bread, even a weekday sandwich. This is also why we say עיני כל אליך ישברו ואתה נותן להם את אכלם בעתו — likewise ten words. The Rabbi's story: after a long service — Shabbat, Rosh Chodesh — he was making kiddush slowly, waiting for people to come in and hear it. Someone was so hungry and thirsty that as the Rabbi was still saying ברוך אתה ה' אלוקינו מלך העולם , he had already drunk his grape juice. That is exactly the phenomenon Rabbeinu Avraham describes. עוד נשוב בעזרת השם לעניין הפרישות — more on this in Chapter 10. Part Six — The Prophets and the Receiving of Gifts איני סבור שיש צורך לבאר לך דבר הסתפקותם ופרישותם של נביאים, ושל פרושי האומות הקודמות מחסידים ואנשי מעשה, עד שהגיעו לתלמידיהם It hardly needs saying that the prophets — and the ascetics of earlier nations, the chasidim and anshei ma'aseh, down to their disciples — possessed contentment and abstinence. But here is the point worth your attention: אבל עליך לתת דעתך, כי שלילת גאוותם וכן פרישותם והסתפקותם גרמו להם שיקבלו מה שניתן להם מן המתנות דרך של מקבלי נדבה Their absence of arrogance , together with their perishut and histapkut, is precisely what allowed them to receive gifts as recipients of a nedavah. Three reasons: וכל זאת מפני חוסר גאוותם — to accept a gift at all, you must not be arrogant. ומפני ידיעתם כי הניתן להם בא מאיתו יתעלה, והנותן הוא רק מתווך — they recognized that what was given came from Hashem , and the giver is merely an intermediary . This was their parnasah, sent by Hashem. וגם מפני שקבלת המתנות תעניק לנותן עונג רב ושפע טובה וברכה — because receiving gives the giver great pleasure , and an abundance of good and blessing. The halachic parallel — adam chashuv: to effect kiddushin a man must give the woman something of value — hence the ring. But if a woman gives a gift to a genuinely important person and he accepts it from her, he can betroth her through that acceptance — because the pleasure she receives from his accepting her gift is itself the value. That is the halachah of adam chashuv . You see from it that there is real benefit conferred by allowing someone to give to you. Sometimes the cruellest thing is to refuse: someone truly wants to express gratitude — \"please accept this\" — and the refusal denies them that. The Practice Was Standard — Three Proofs 1. Shaul and his attendant, going to Shmuel והנה נלך, ומה נביא לאיש? כי הלחם אזל מכלינו ותשורה אין להביא לאיש האלוקים — מה אתנו? And the attendant answers: הנה נמצא בידי רבע שקל כסף, ונתתי לאיש האלוקים והגיד לנו את דרכנו . מכאן ראיה כי כך היו מביאים מנחה לאנשי האלוקים — standard practice: you come to the prophet for guidance, you bring a gift. 2. Yeravam sending his wife to Achiyah HaShiloni Yeravam's son was ill. Yeravam — a sinner, whether embarrassed or too proud — sends his wife, telling her to disguise herself and to take: ולקחת בידך עשרה לחם ונקדים ובקבק דבש ובאת אליו, הוא יגיד לך מה יהיה לנער Bread, cakes and honey — the gift accompanies the request. 3. The man from Baal Shalishah, to Elisha ואיש בא מבעל שלשה ויבא לאיש האלוקים לחם בכורים עשרים לחם שערים וכרמל בצקלנו, ויאמר: תן לעם ויאכלו From this verse the Gemara in Ketubot derives that bringing a gift to a talmid chacham is like bringing bikurim . (Rabbeinu Avraham notes that this is one of the practices the Sufis — פרושי האסלאם — copied from us: their leaders too would accept gifts from the people.) The Two Caveats Caveat 1 — take only the little, return the much אבל עליך לדעת שהנביאים היו מקבלים רק את המועט ומחזירים את המרובה Caveat 2 — only from someone it is fitting to receive from ולא היו מקבלים אלא ממי שהוא מוכשר וראוי לקבל דבר שבא ממנו The proof for both: the very same Elisha who accepted the twenty barley loaves returned Naaman's ten kikarim of silver and six thousand gold pieces after his tzara'at was cured, and refused the forty camel-loads sent by Ben Hadad . And this explains the disguise: Yeravam told his wife to dress as someone else בדאגתו כי לו ידע הנביא שהיא אשתו, לא היה עולה על דעתו שיקבל ממנה — he knew that had Achiyah known whose wife she was, he would never have accepted from her and would not have given the guidance. ולא יצדק המקבל מתנות מן הפרוצים וכדומיהם באמתלא שצריך להם, ושהוא אוכל מתנות השם — והבן את הדבר כראוי One cannot justify accepting from the unworthy on the excuse that \"I need it\" or \"I am eating from Hashem's gifts.\" Understand this properly. The Rabbi's aside: people like to say that donations come in all currencies — but the reality is that you accept from the appropriate people. And the Rambam's related ruling: the Torah commands ולדבקה בו — to cleave to Hashem. The Gemara asks how this is possible and answers: cleave to the chachamim — marry your daughter to a talmid chacham's son, and do business (perakmatia) with them — help invest with them. From this the Rambam rules (in his commentary to Pirkei Avot , which we learned together a few years ago) that it is a mitzvah to do business with a chacham . Outright gifts, by contrast, are only from the right person and only in the necessary amount. And this fits Rabbeinu Avraham exactly: since accepting a gift confers a benefit on the giver, it makes sense with someone from whom it is fitting to receive — but why confer that benefit on a rasha? Summary — Chapter 9 Histapkut is contentment: being happy with one's portion and not frantically chasing more. One trains for it by aspiring only to what is necessary and by recognizing that in material things there is always more — silver, then gold, then crystal — so that אוהב כסף לא ישבע כסף and איזהו עשיר? השמח בחלקו . It sits between two chapters: material wants belong to histapkut, while obligatory, mitzvah matters (marriage and the like) should be desired, with bitachon (Ch 8) carrying you until they come. Anyone who has attained perishut (Ch 10) necessarily has histapkut, though not the reverse. The Torah itself values the trait — from לא תחמד , from שונאי בצע as a qualification for judges, and from ben sorer u'moreh , whose essence is zolel v'sovei . Its absence is the strongest cause of serious sin and of neglected mitzvot : most never attain their aspirations, and meanwhile lose Torah to the extra hours, slide toward gezel and false oaths, and — if they do attain — become enslaved to spending it, with mitzvot raced through or hollowed out. Finally, the prophets received gifts, out of humility, out of recognition that the giver is only Hashem's intermediary, and because receiving is itself a kindness to the giver — but only the little, returning the much, and only from those from whom it is fitting to receive . Coming Up No shiur next Monday (Tzom Gedaliah), nor through Yom Kippur, Chol HaMoed Sukkot and Asru Chag. HaMaspik resumes after Sukkot . On return: a full recap of Chapters 1–9 , then Chapter 10 — Perishut (abstinence) , where sigufim , the Ramban's נבל ברשות התורה , and the further discussion of the prophets' abstinence all belong. Only a few chapters remain after that; the sefer should be completed before Pesach . New weekly feature after Sukkot: the last 5–10 minutes on a selected piece of Rabbeinu Avraham's commentary on the parashah (Bereishit and Shemot), running through the winter until Pesach. Shiur time may vary slightly in October until the clocks change — details on the group. B'Ezrat Hashem","eXAWkMjrAiU":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (Rambam), Chelek Aleph — Rabbi Chaim Makbili's Hebrew edition / Sefaria Chapter: I:16 — the term tzur (צור), preceded by a full review of the Introductions and Chapters 1–15 Date: Tuesday 1 September 2026 · 19 Elul 5786 — first shiur of the new term, a week and a half before Rosh Hashanah (very early this year) Topic: Where we stand in the Moreh; the four meanings of tzur ; tzur as origin/source — of Avraham and Sarah, and of God; the completed reading of ויאמר ה' הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור Schedule note: The series began after Pesach; the summer term covered the Introductions and Chapters 1–15 — just under a quarter of Section 1 (which has some 79 chapters). This term runs through to the end of December , with breaks for Sukkot and Chol HaMoed — though the chagim fall nicely on the weekend this year, so momentum should hold. No shiur next week ; the next Moreh shiur is Tuesday 4 Tishrei , the day after Tzom Gedaliah, in Aseret Yemei Teshuvah. Monday night HaMaspik continues; Thursday to be decided. Chapter 17 was intended for tonight but was not reached — it will open the learning in the new year, 5787. Part One — Review of the Introductions The Rambam himself instructed us to review constantly, so before opening new material we run the full framework again. (Thank you to those who took part in the summer revision questions — the aim was precisely to encourage chazarah.) The Letter to Rabbi Yosef The Moreh opens with the Rambam's letter to his student Rabbi Yosef , of whom he was very fond and who had left him. His stated purpose: to write essays explaining multi-meaning terms in Tanach that are open to different interpretations — many of which we have now seen across the first 15 chapters — and to clarify veiled matters in the Torah. Who the Book Is For The next introduction states that the book is only for one educated in both Torah and philosophy — someone caught between the verses of Tanach and tradition on the one hand and philosophy on the other, who is constantly nevoch , perplexed, unwilling to neglect either his intellect or his tradition, and seeking a way to hold them together. (The Rabbi's aside: by that standard he himself, giving the shiur, does not qualify — yet the Rambam also says many people can benefit from the book, which is why we learn it together.) Why It Cannot Be Exhaustive The Rambam will not explain everything exhaustively — it isn't possible, and moreover: Ma'aseh Merkavah = the study of philosophy and theology Ma'aseh Bereishit = the study of the building blocks and first principles of science and existence The Chachamim already ruled that these may not be expanded upon in a public setting, so the Rambam gives only rashei perakim — chapter headings. (This is the subject of Chapter 17, coming next.) The more one develops one's character and intellect, the more zohar — shine — one draws from the book. The Order of Learning Science → theology → God. This is why the Torah begins with Bereishit : the first principles of existence come first, because you must understand the natural world in order to understand HaKadosh Baruch Hu. A beautiful juxtaposition — on Simchat Torah (only weeks away) we move from the death of Moshe , greatest of all humans and greatest of prophets, straight back to Bereishit : you need first principles to understand HaKadosh Baruch Hu and to reach the levels of prophecy. Meshalim and Final Advice The Rambam distinguishes kinds of allegory : some meshalim where every word carries significance, and others where only the general concept is intended. His concluding advice: read the book many times, compare the chapters , and judge him favourably where something appears surprising — don't jump on him. The Seven Reasons for Contradictions His final introduction gives seven reasons why contradictions appear in any book. The two that matter for us are reasons five and seven — including cases where the Rambam has deliberately contradicted himself . We will meet examples. Part Two — Review of Chapters 1–15 Ch Term / Subject Core teaching 1 Tzelem u-demut God is incorporeal (the philosophical proof comes much later). Tzelem Elokim is not physical form but tzurah — the intellect. In that respect the human is most domeh to God. To'ar is the word for purely physical form (ויהי יוסף יפה תואר ויפה מראה). 2 Before and after the sin Adam already had the intellect — no one gets rewarded with more intellect for sinning. What changed: perception shifted from emet ve-sheker , objective intellectual truth, to tov ve-ra , subjective judgements shaped by culture, emotion and desire. The sin was a turn toward the sensual and the subjective — appearance over intellectual truth — and middah ke-neged middah , reality became blurred. The snake's והייתם כאלוקים ידעי טוב ורע has nothing to do with God : Elokim = judges , who must make arbitrary determinations between good and bad. 3 Tavnit u-temunah Tavnit is always physical — never used of God in Tanach (תבנית המשכן ואת תבנית כל כליו). Temunah can be physical, but can also mean the true essence of a thing — and with God it always carries that latter meaning. 4 Ra'ah · Hibbit · Chazah Three verbs of seeing: ra'ah = to look; hibbit = to gaze, to stare; chazah = to uncover, to penetrate to the depths. All three can be physical — but with HaKadosh Baruch Hu they always denote intellectual sight. 5 Caution The burning bush, with four readings: (1) God was literally in the bush — heretical ; (2) a created Kevod Hashem , a physical manifestation — acceptable (Rav Se'adyah) but not the Rambam's view; (3) entirely a prophetic vision; (4) Moshe held back — the whole episode is a metaphor for caution on entering the divine realm. Go slowly, gradually, refining yourself through logic and intellectual study — unlike those who rush. (Hence the Rabbi's own approach: better to go slowly and review than to cover more ground.) 6 Ish ve-ishah Borrowed terms beyond male and female: ish = that which attaches itself; ishah = the receptacle that receives. All of existence reduces to attacher and attached — philosophically tzurah and chomer . Essential for Chapter 17 — go back over Chapter 6. 7 Yalad Continues Chapter 6: the offspring of ish ve-ishah . To give birth physically, but also to produce novel thoughts, views and teachings in another. For 130 years Adam could not \"birth\" properly in tzelem Elokim because of the sin, producing incomplete humans — which is all the Chachamim mean by shedim . Hence a rav is said to be molid his talmid. 8 Makom Geographical place; or status/rank (ממלא מקומו); or a conceptual, intellectual position — as in English, \"I'm in a bad place.\" Hence ברוך כבוד ה' ממקומו — and הנה מקום אתי , needed for tonight. 9 Kisei A physical chair; or something majestic (shamayim, the Mikdash); or God Himself — as in כי יד על כס יה in Beshalach. 10 Alah ve-yarad Literal ascent/descent; levels of holiness; or the directing of attention . With people: up toward the lofty and holy, down to the mundane — the prophet ascends, then divests himself of prophecy to come down and teach. With God it is always attention: yarad = resting the Shechinah and imparting prophecy (or punishing); alah = the prophecy has concluded. 11 Yashav To sit → stability, permanence, continuity . With Hashem: completely unchanging. Hashem does not sit in heaven any more than anywhere else — He is everywhere and nowhere — but the verses locate His \"sitting\" in heaven because in Aristotle's and the Rambam's cosmology the heavens are the most enduring and unchanging thing there is. 12 Kam Physically rising, or establishing something — but with Hashem always executing judgment or fulfilling a promise (תקום תרחם ציון: either judgment on Tzion's enemies or fulfilment of the promise to rebuild her). 13 Amidah Physical standing; stopping; and third — permanence . With Hashem always the third: continuance and permanence. 14 Adam The first human; humanity as a whole; or the ordinary person in contradistinction to the ish , the elevated one. We are all Adam , all belonging to the material — but we have the capacity to become ish . 15 Nitzav Like yashav , in the sense of permanence and stability . With God it means His eternal, unchanging nature — and with it the Rambam re-read the whole episode of Yaakov's ladder : והנה מלאכי אלהים עלים וירדים בו והנה ה' נצב עליו. The shape of the fifteen chapters: a great deal of time on verbs of posture — yashav , kam , amidah , nitzav , alah , yarad — alongside types of human being — ish , ishah , adam — and the foundational concepts. Chapter 16 now completes the unit of makom , nitzav and tzur — it all comes together — and Chapter 17 begins something entirely different . Part Three — Chapter 16: צור (Tzur) The Verse Behind the Chapter After the sin of the golden calf, Moshe says: הראני נא את כבדך — Show me Your glory. Hashem answers that no person can see His face and live, and then: ויאמר ה' הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור \"There is a place with Me, and you shall stand on the rock.\" Two of the three key words we already have: מקום (Chapter 8) — a conceptual place . You asked to see My glory; I cannot give you everything, I cannot show My face — but there is a conceptual place , an intellectual ascent, that I can give you. ונצבת (Chapter 15) — this cannot be physical standing. Even though the verse speaks of a human being, he is being addressed in relation to Hashem — and He is commanding him to stand, so it must mean something more. It means permanence : be permanently al hatzur . What remains is the last piece of the jigsaw: what is the tzur? And alongside the definition, the Rambam will draw a mussar lesson about how we ought to live. The Four Meanings of Tzur צור מונח רב משמעותי — tzur is a multi-meaning term. 1. A boulder — a great mountainous rock שם ההר — as in והכית בצור ויצאו ממנו מים ושתה העם The English editions render this as \"mountain,\" but the Rabbi's own reading is that it means something more like a boulder — a very large rock. Why does the Rambam take it beyond an ordinary rock? Likely because of הנני עמד לפניך שם על הצור בחרב — the tzur is associated with Chorev , with the mountain, so he understands it as a piece of the mountain. It would be wrong to call a big rock in the street a tzur — it would have to be boulder-like. 2. A hard, sharp stone — flint הוא שם האבן הקשה כחלמיש — as in עשה לך חרבות צרים ושוב מל את בני ישראל שנית Hashem tells Yehoshua to circumcise Bnei Yisrael again — with charvot tzurim , sharp flint knives. Obviously you cannot circumcise with a boulder; here tzur must be flintstone . 3. A quarry — the rock-face stones are hewn from והוא שם המכרה שכורים ממנו אבני מחצב — as in הביטו אל צור חצבתם The verse in Yeshayahu (51): שמעו אלי רדפי צדק מבקשי ה'... הביטו אל צור חצבתם ואל מקבת בור נקרתם \"Listen to Me, you who pursue justice... look to the rock you were hewn from , and to the pit you were quarried out of.\" A great quarry from which stone is cut — that is a tzur . Note that all three meanings so far are entirely physical and material. 4. (Borrowed) The origin and source of anything אחר כך הושאל מונח זה מן המשמעות האחרונה למשמעות של מוצא ושורש From the third meaning — the quarry — the term was borrowed to mean the origin, source and root of a thing. And this is proved from the continuation of that very verse in Yeshayahu: הביטו אל אברהם אביכם ואל שרה תחוללכם כי אחד קראתיו ואברכהו וארבהו כאילו פירש שהצור שחצבתם ממנו הוא אברהם אביכם — as if to say explicitly: the quarry you were hewn from is Avraham your father (and Sarah your mother). ועל כן לכו בעקבותיו והנהגו על פי דתו והדמו למידותיו — therefore walk in his footsteps, follow his way, and make yourselves resemble him in his character traits — כי טבע המכרה חייב להימצא במה שנחצב ממנו , because the nature of the quarry must be found in whatever is hewn from it. (In English: a chip off the block — the chip should be true to the block.) Note carefully: this fourth meaning is still not about God. At this stage it is purely the idea of going back to the origins of something. Habitu el Tzur — the Spiritual Quarry So הביטו אל צור חצבתם means: look at where you came from — Avraham and Sarah — and be like them. Clearly this is not a genetic point but a matter of character and path . The Rambam's letter to Rabbi Ovadiah the Ger We learned this letter together just under a year ago (in the winter term). Ovadiah the convert asked: may I say אלוקינו ואלוקי אבותינו — \"our God and the God of our fathers\" — when Avraham is not my father? The Rambam replied: he is not your genetic father, but he is your spiritual father , and that is what \"father\" means here — so yes, you say it. This became the halacha , even though it is far from obvious: it appears the Yerushalmi held this way and the Bavli did not, and the Rambam seems to follow the Yerushalmi. His whole point: the true father is the one whose character you take on . The end of Hilchot Avadim — the Rambam's own application מותר לעבוד בעבד כנעני בפרך — one is permitted, by strict law, to work a Canaanite slave harshly. ואף על פי שהדין כך, מדת חסידות ודרכי חכמה שיהיה אדם רחמן ורודף צדק ולא יכביד עולו על עבדו ולא יצר לו Even though the din permits it, that is not how good people behave — feed him first, don't shout at him, don't be harsh with him. And note who is saying this : the same Rambam who counts לעולם בהם תעבדו among the 613 mitzvot — a mitzvah not to free an eved Kena'ani (a machloket Rishonim, but assume the Rambam's view). And still: ואין האכזריות והעזות מצויה אלא בעכו\"ם עובדי עבודה זרה. אבל זרעו של אברהם אבינו, והם ישראל שהשפיע להם הקדוש ברוך הוא טובת התורה וציוום בחוקים ומשפטים צדיקים — רחמנים הם על הכל Cruelty is found among idolaters; but the seed of Avraham — Israel, upon whom Hashem bestowed the goodness of the Torah — are merciful to all . Not merely genetic descent: you are the spiritual children of Avraham , and that is how Avraham behaves. That is הביטו אל צור חצבתם in practice. Hashem as Tzur ולפי המשמעות האחרונה הזאת נקרא השם יתעלה צור Only in this fourth sense — origin, source, quarry — is Hashem called tzur : כי הוא הראשית והסיבה הפועלת לכל מה שזולתו He is the beginning and the active cause of everything besides Himself. Note the word הפועלת — active , ongoing. It is a fundamental principle of our belief that Hashem is constantly engaged with this world and constantly sustaining it, giving existence at every moment. The Tzur Verses (Timely: Ha'azinu , where the word appears repeatedly, is read on Shabbat Shuvah, the Shabbat after Rosh Hashanah — next week's parashah being Nitzavim-Vayeilech.) הצור תמים פעלו כי כל דרכיו משפט Every rabbi has this verse on his lips too often — it is said at Tzidduk HaDin , at every funeral, every stone-setting, every shivah. Why this verse when someone dies? Because at exactly the moment when a person is confused and cannot see Hashem in his life, we affirm: You are the Tzur, the source of all existence — even of the things that are not good. Yes, it is painful, and we cannot fathom why it happened; but it came from You, the origin of everything. That itself is the nechamah. צור ילדך תשי ותשכח אל מחללך You grew forgetful of the Rock that bore you — you forgot your origin , the One who gave birth to you. Like parents who gave you everything and are cast aside: they are your origins, you came into this world through them, and you discard them. אם לא כי צורם מכרם וה' הסגירם How can enemies pursue and defeat us with so much smaller a force? It must be that their Rock — their origin — handed them over . אין קדוש כה' כי אין בלתך ואין צור כאלהינו Chanah, after the birth of Shmuel. Why does she reach for the word tzur ? Because she had all but given up — and now declares: You are the source of all existence; it all came from You in the end. The Gemara in Berachot's wordplay: read not ein tzur but אין צייר כאלוקינו — there is no artist like our God. And what is an artist? The source of the painting. Put paint in front of you or me and we make a mess; the artist is the origin — he can do whatever he wishes, he gives the painting its very nature by how he joins the colours. Tzur and tzayar sit together beautifully: as Hashem is the Rock, the origin of all, so the artist is the origin of any painting. Perhaps that is exactly why the Chachamim made the play here. בטחו בה' עדי עד כי ביה ה' צור עולמים Trust in Hashem forever, for in Him is the Rock of worlds . Why should you have bitachon? Because He is the origin of everything — so what is there to worry about? Those at the Monday night HaMaspik shiurim will recall that we finished the levels of bitachon at the end of last term in July , and that Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam identifies this very pasuk as the highest level — where you are entirely confident that everything comes from Hashem. Now we see why it is the highest: because you recognise that everything originates in Him — He is the Tzur, the quarry . The Verse Completed — ונצבת על הצור הישען והתמד על בחינת היותו של השם יתעלה ראשית כל מציאות Lean upon, and be constant in, the contemplation that Hashem is the origin of all existence — for this is the path by which you reach Him. One verse; three chapters; a completely transformed reading: Word Chapter Meaning מקום 8 A conceptual, intellectual place/rank — not a location ונצבת 15 Permanence — constantly, firmly הצור 16 The origin and source of all existence ונצבת על הצור therefore means: contemplate the Source of all existence firmly and constantly. And when you do that — הנה מקום אתי — you will reach that place. Look how it answers Moshe. He asked הראני נא את כבדך . Hashem replies, in effect: yes, I am willing to show you My glory — but you must do your part. I cannot show you Myself; I can show you My creation, and through it you can come to understand Me. So stand permanently on the rock — be constantly turned toward the origins of everything — and by that you will reach and grasp My glory. The Same Idea in Mishneh Torah This is precisely Hilchot Yesodei HaTorah 2:2 , after Chapter 1's treatment of God's unity and incorporeality. How do we fulfil the mitzvah to love Hashem? בשעה שיתבונן האדם במעשיו וברואיו הנפלאים הגדולים ויראה מהן חכמתו שאין לה ערך ולא קץ — מיד הוא אוהב ומשבח ומפאר ומתאווה תאווה גדולה לידע השם הגדול When you contemplate His works and creations and see in them wisdom without measure or limit, love follows immediately . And therefore, says the Rambam, I will now explain to you the first principles of science — so that you can understand Hashem. Yesodei HaTorah 2:2 is the fulfilment of ונצבת על הצור. So when we read Parshat Ki Tisa, the verse now reads: there is a makom — a status, a place of intellectual comprehension of Me — and the way you attain it is ונצבת על הצור , by constantly standing on, contemplating and developing your grasp of the Tzur : the quarry, the origin, the beginning of all, the Painter and Artist of everything, HaKadosh Baruch Hu Himself. Do that, and you will find the makom . Stunning. Closing Points Ma'oz Tzur Yeshuati Now think about the Chanukah piyut: מעוז צור ישועתי, לך נאה לשבח . Why address HaKadosh Baruch Hu specifically as Ma'oz and Tzur ? Because the piyut runs through every persecution — Greeks, Romans, Persians, Haman — and declares: through all of it, You are unchanging; more than that, You are the stronghold and the Rock, the origin of all . We recognise that even the terrible things trace back to You. The Chapter's Double Achievement Removing incorporeality — where the verse uses tzur of Hashem it means nothing physical whatsoever; and it completes the reading of makom and nitzav . A moral teaching — the intellectual apprehension of Hashem on the one hand, and the ethical character of Avraham and Sarah on the other. We have a duty toward both . A Final, Deliberately Pointed Note Return to הביטו אל צור חצבתם . Could one not read the \"quarry you were hewn from\" as God Himself , given that tzur elsewhere does refer to Hashem (הצור תמים פעלו)? Notice that the Rambam pointedly does not use Yeshayahu 51 among his God-verses. The straightforward answer is that the next verse says explicitly Avraham and Sarah . But there is more to it. This kind of reading is commonly heard today and the Rabbi considers it outright heretical. His own experience: his daughter came home from school in Year 1 with a vocabulary list — adam = man, and neshamah translated as \"a piece of Hashem inside us.\" What does a Year 1 child understand by \"soul\"? He phoned the school immediately: what do you mean, a piece of Hashem?! This idea — that there is a little piece of Hashem within us — is heard a great deal in Chabad , and it is very likely precisely why the Rambam avoided that verse : so that no one should read הביטו אל צור חצבתם as though we were hewn out of God . We are not. There is no piece of Hashem within us. There is no such concept in the Rambam's thought. We should not think in these terms, should not teach our children these things, and when we hear them we should call them out. Perhaps the Ba'al HaTanya held that way — but it is not our derech. Summary — Chapter 16 Tzur has four meanings: a boulder (והכית בצור), a flint (חרבות צרים), a quarry (הביטו אל צור חצבתם), and — borrowed from the third — the origin and source of a thing. In that fourth sense the verse in Yeshayahu identifies the quarry as Avraham and Sarah : look to your spiritual origin and take on their character, for the nature of the quarry must be found in what is hewn from it. In that same fourth sense alone is Hashem called Tzur — הראשית והסיבה הפועלת לכל מה שזולתו , the beginning and the constantly active cause of all that is besides Him. Hence the Ha'azinu verses, Chanah's אין צור כאלוקינו (and the Gemara's אין צייר), and the highest level of bitachon, כי ביה ה' צור עולמים. And hence the completed verse: הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור — the intellectual makom is attained by constant contemplation of the Source of all existence , exactly as the Rambam prescribes in Yesodei HaTorah 2:2. The chapter both strips away corporeality and delivers a dual mussar : intellectual apprehension of God, and the ethical character of Avraham. Coming Up Chapter 17 — not reached tonight. Here the Rambam goes completely off-track again: a very short chapter (two or three paragraphs) on the concealment of esoteric teachings , after which he returns to terms. Why he does this, and what he is teaching, next time. Chapter 6 (ish ve-ishah) is essential background — please review it , because Chapter 17 cannot be understood without it. No shiur next week. Next Moreh shiur Tuesday 4 Tishrei , the day after Tzom Gedaliah, during Aseret Yemei Teshuvah — b'ezrat Hashem beginning Chapter 17 in the new year 5787 . Monday night HaMaspik continues; Thursday to be confirmed. תזכו לשנים רבות וטובות — the Rabbi's berachah to all. B'Ezrat Hashem","LThvDHCa3wE":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (Rambam), Chelek Aleph — Rabbi Chaim Makbili's Hebrew edition / Sefaria Chapters: I:14 — the concept of Adam (אדם); I:15 — the term nitzav (נצב) Topic: The three meanings of Adam ; the mussar of rising from Adam to Ish ; nitzav as firm standing vs. permanence; the true meaning of Yaakov's ladder; ונצבת על הצור Schedule note: This was the final Moreh shiur of the term — break until Elul , b'ezrat Hashem. The class was announced as Chapters 14, 15 and 16, but Chapter 16 ( tzur — צור) was not reached and will open next term (Chapter 17 then begins a new theme). The Rabbi closed with a passionate plea: do chazarah of Chapters 1–15 over the summer — using the note summaries, the transcripts, the recordings, or simply re-reading the Moreh — so that next term we can go straight in. Recap — Where We Are in the Moreh Chapter Term / Theme 1–2 Tzelem Elokim — the image of God 3 Tavnit and temunah 4 Ra'ah , habit , chazah — verbs of seeing 5 Caution in approaching HaKadosh Baruch Hu 6 Ish ve'ishah — man and woman 7 Yalad 8 Makom — place = rank, status 9 Kisei 10 Alah ve'yarad — ascent/descent, including of the mind 11 Yashav — to sit → permanence and continuity ; why Hashem \"sits in heaven\" (the heavens are permanent); the mabul 12 Kam — four meanings: physically rising; establishing/fulfilling; rising up against; Hashem executing judgment 13 Amidah — three meanings: physical standing; stopping; permanence . With Hashem it is always the third meaning — e.g. ואתה פה עמד עמדי, Moshe's constant prophetic experience Chapters 11–13 (last week) were all \"standing\" words. Chapter 15 ( nitzav ) continues that thread — but first, in Chapter 14, the Rambam digresses to discuss the word Adam . Why he digresses is itself the key to the chapter (see below). Do not confuse Chapter 14 with Chapter 6. Chapter 6 dealt with ish ve'ishah (man and woman) in the creation narrative. Here we return to the creation narrative again — but now to discuss what Adam means, which is something different. Chapter 14 — Adam (אדם) A very short chapter — but as we've seen repeatedly, the shortest chapters are often the deepest . The Three Meanings of Adam רב משמעויות אדם — the word Adam has several meanings. Meaning 1 — The primordial human Adam is the name of the first human , and it is a derived name (שם גזור) — derived from adamah (אדמה), the ground, as the pesukim state explicitly: וייצר ה' אלהים את האדם עפר מן האדמה ויפח באפיו נשמת חיים Hashem formed the human — dust from the ground — and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life. The Torah says explicitly that he was taken from the ground — hence the name Adam from adamah . Incidental aside (Rav Kook): Chava, the primordial female, was not taken from the ground — she was taken from Adam . Rav Kook explains that this is why women are generally less earthly and material than men: men are taken directly from the ground, whereas women are taken a level beyond — from the man who is from the ground. Generalizing, this is why women are typically more repulsed by very \"earthly\" things (blood, a creature in the bathroom) than men are. There is much to discuss here; the Rabbi noted it only in passing. Meaning 2 — The human race והוא שם המין האנושי — Adam is also the name of the human species as a whole: לא ידון רוחי באדם לעלם בשגם הוא בשר \"My spirit will not abide in the human forever, for he too is flesh\" — because humans are composed of material matter, they must have a limited timespan (the 120 years is the general maximum span, even though some individuals lived longer). מי יודע רוח בני האדם העלה היא למעלה ורוח הבהמה הירדת היא למטה לארץ (Kohelet) \"Who knows the spirit of humans — whether it rises upward, or whether, like the animal's, it descends down to the earth.\" We discussed this verse at length with Rav Se'adyah Gaon: one reading is that no one knows whether a given human will rise or go down like an animal. ומותר האדם מן הבהמה אין (Kohelet; said daily in Shacharit) \"The advantage of the human over the animal is naught\" — (different texts read אין or a slight advantage). All three verses clearly use Adam for the human race , not a specific individual. The deeper observation — \"Ha-Adam\" throughout Bereishit On a deeper level, perhaps some — or even all — of the mentions of Adam in the creation narrative refer not to a specific individual but to the human species. Once you notice this, you can't un-notice it: Go through Parashat Bereishit and count how many times it says אדם without the definite article. Only twice. Everywhere else it is הָאָדָם — the human: ויגרש את האדם · הן האדם היה כאחד ממנו · ויקרא האדם שם אשתו חוה The first time the Torah calls him simply \"Adam\" as a name is only in chapter 4, verse 25 : וידע אדם עוד את אשתו ותלד בן ותקרא את שמו שת — when the primordial human has relations with his wife and fathers another human being. Until then, through nearly four chapters, he is always ha-Adam . This opens the discussion of whether the \"Adam\" of the opening chapters is one human — or the human species. The Rambam does say plainly (meaning 1) that Adam is the primordial human's name, but the Torah's consistent ha-Adam shows something deeper is going on. Most people who read Bereishit have never picked this up — and it changes the whole reading of the parashah. Jack's observation: all of the Rambam's proof-texts for the second meaning (the human race) themselves carry the definite article — ba-adam , bnei ha-adam , ha-adam . The Rabbi: \"Very good, excellent point\" — it confirms exactly the pattern above. Meaning 3 — The masses, in contradistinction to the ish והוא שם להמון — Adam is a name for the multitudes , כלומר לפשוטי העם — the simple people — להוציאם מהיחידים שבהם , as distinct from the special individuals among them. As it says in Tehillim 49: שמעו זאת כל העמים... גם בני אדם גם בני איש יחד עשיר ואביון If Adam and ish meant the same thing, why repeat? Because they are two different things : ish means a distinctive, important person; Adam means the general masses. Compare Rashi at the start of Parashat Shelach — שלח לך אנשים : כל אנשים שבמקרא לשון חשיבות — anashim in Tanach denotes importance. So the pasuk is saying: rich or poor, whether you are an Adam — a simple person — or an ish — a distinguished person — everyone pay attention to this psalm. The Two Verses Explained by Meaning 3 ובמשמעות השלישית הזאת — with this third meaning, the Rambam explains two verses that have agitated commentators. 1. בני האלהים ובנות האדם (end of Parashat Bereishit) ויראו בני האלהים את בנות האדם כי טבת הנה ויקחו להם נשים מכל אשר בחרו What — angels marrying humans?! The Rambam — like many Rishonim (Onkelos, one interpretation in Rashi, Ramban) — understands that בני האלהים has nothing to do with God . Elohim in Tanach can mean a judge or any important person . So: the noblemen saw the daughters of the Adam — the simple girls of the masses — that they were good, ויקחו להם נשים מכל אשר בחרו, and took advantage of them , taking women from wherever they chose. Nobility exploiting the common girls — the third meaning of Adam exactly. Rabbi Makbili adds a beautiful alternative reading: the bnei ha-Elohim — those created b'tzelem Elokim , with great potential — \"saw the bnot ha-adam \": they saw the material world, they saw adamah , and pursued adamah . Either way, it turns on the Adam/ish distinction. 2. אכן כאדם תמותון (Tehillim 82) אני אמרתי אלהים אתם ובני עליון כלכם. אכן כאדם תמותון וכאחד השרים תפלו \"I thought (says Asaf) that you were Elohim \" — important people (proven from the parallel ובני עליון, exalted ones) — \" but in fact you will die like an Adam \" — like a simple person. I thought you were important; in reality you are only simple people. Related aside: in that same psalm (Tehillim 49 — which by old Sephardi minhag is said in the beit avel , and you'll see why): ואדם ביקר בל ילין נמשל כבהמות נדמו The Adam is compared to an animal — again the Adam/ish distinction: the psalm addresses everyone, גם בני אדם גם בני איש, but the one who remains an Adam ends up like an animal. The Mussar of the Chapter — Rise from Adam to Ish This short chapter is one of the Moreh's mussar chapters . The Moreh is not only about stripping corporeality from our conception of God; it also teaches how to live. The Rambam is telling us: our duty is to rise to ish and not to remain our entire lives as Adam . Look at the verses he chose: ומותר האדם מן הבהמה אין — if you remain an Adam , you are essentially an animal. ואדם ביקר בל ילין נמשל כבהמות נדמו — stay rooted in earth, in materialism and physicality — and you are compared to the beasts. אכן כאדם תמותון — you will die as an Adam , and all that is left of you is an Adam. Only the Adam dies. A person who rises from Adam — from materialism — becomes an ish , becomes bnei Elohim , and essentially does not die : only the body, the earthly material nature, dies. The ish — the intellect, the neshamah — continues forever. That is what Olam Ha-Ba is: the continuation of the ish . Our adamah dies; we continue living as ishim . The Mishnah in Pirkei Avot — a new layer ובמקום שאין אנשים, השתדל להיות איש \"In a place where there is no ish — strive to be an ish .\" There are always Adam — wherever you go there is Adam . Where there is no ish , you must become the ish : set the example, rise from being adamah . The opening of the Moreh itself Right at the beginning of the Moreh — immediately after the opening letter to his student — the Rambam quoted three verses, the first of which we now understand far more deeply: אליכם אישים אקרא וקולי אל בני אדם \"To you, ishim , I call; my voice is to the bnei adam .\" Everyone will read this book — all bnei Adam will see it — but the Adam will understand it as an Adam understands. The Rambam is calling to the ishim . To read this book properly, we have to be an ish . Why Does the Rambam Digress Here? Chapters 12–13 dealt with kimah and amidah ; Chapter 15 deals with nitzav — all \"standing\" words. Why plant Adam in the middle? Answer 1 (Shem Tov, among the commentaries): Chapter 13 ended with Moshe — ואתה פה עמד עמדי, Moshe's constant prophetic standing with Hashem. Having discussed Moshe's constant nevuah, the Rambam turns to Adam and ish — because Moshe is the quintessential ish , the paradigm of one who rose up from being an Adam . Answer 2 (Rav Makbili) — the Rabbi's preferred reading: based on the pasuk in Yechezkel: ויאמר אלי בן אדם עמד על רגליך ואדבר אתך \"Ben Adam — stand on your feet and I will speak with you.\" Now the sequence flows beautifully: Chapter 13 — amidah , permanence; Chapter 14 — permanently get up from being an Adam . Stand on your feet; rise from Adam and go up to ish . The Rabbi: \"I really think that's the true interpretation of what's going on here.\" Chapter 14 — Summary The word Adam has three meanings: (1) the primordial human, named from adamah ; (2) humanity as a whole — and note the Torah's consistent ha-Adam through Bereishit; (3) ordinary people, the masses, in contrast to the elevated ish . The Rambam uses this distinction to teach that while every person begins as an Adam bound to the material world, the goal of human life is to become an ish through intellectual and spiritual perfection. Only the Adam dies — אכן כאדם תמותון; the ish , the intellect, the soul, continues forever. That is Olam Ha-Ba. Chapter 15 — Nitzav (נצב) Having discussed Adam , the Rambam returns to verbs — and nitzav follows beautifully from Chapters 12 ( kimah , arising) and 13 ( amidah , standing): another form of standing. Rav Makbili adds a further connection: it flows from the verse at the beginning of Iyov — a Tisha B'Av reading: ויהי היום ויבאו בני האלהים להתיצב על ה' ויבוא גם השטן בתוכם — the bnei ha-Elohim (which we just discussed in Chapter 14!) came להתיצב before Hashem (see below on what that standing means). A Grammar Point First Nitzav or yitzav — sometimes the root appears with a nun (נצב), sometimes with a yud (יצב): אף שאלו שני שרשים שונים עם שמות במלה אחת... כידוע לך בכל נטיותיהם Even though these are two different roots , they mean the same thing in all their forms. Interesting — the Rabbi noted he had always assumed it was one verb whose nun or yud drops, but the Rambam clearly treats them as two distinct three-letter roots with identical meaning. Meaning 1 — Standing Firm, Erect, Strong לעתים משמעותו עמידה והתייצבות — sometimes it means standing. But not ordinary standing — we've already had kimah and amidah . This is an erect, firm, assertive standing. Three verses: ותתצב אחתו מרחק לדעה מה יעשה לו Moshe has been placed in the basket among the reeds; his sister — Miriam, about six years old — stands from afar. Vatetatzav here means peering — standing craned, looking over intently to know what would happen to him. יתיצבו מלכי ארץ ורוזנים נוסדו יחד על ה' ועל משיחו (Opening of Tehillim 2) The kings of the earth stand against Hashem — they stand firmly, erect, arrogantly. Yityatzev is always a very strong standing. ודתן ואבירם יצאו נצבים פתח אהליהם ונשיהם ובניהם וטפם The classic case (which Rashi picks up): in the Korach episode, Moshe goes to make peace, and Datan and Aviram come out nitzavim — strong, firm, defiant, at the entrance of their tents. Aside — why a gravestone is a matzeivah : this is why the monument we place is called a מצבה (and Yaakov built a matzeivah ) — not a ma'amadah . Because nitzav means to stand tall, erect and firm . And the Iyov verse: ויבאו בני האלהים להתיצב על ה' — they came to stand firmly against Hashem, to question Him (they proceed to question Him about Iyov). Meaning 1 exactly: standing strong and firm against something. Meaning 2 — Permanence and Stability ולעתים משמעותו קבעות ותמידות — just as amidah can mean permanence, so can nitzav . The example — a verse said many times on Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur: לעולם ה' דברך נצב בשמים \"Forever, Hashem, Your word is nitzav in the heavens\" — כלומר קבוע וקיים, fixed and enduring . Revision question (from last week): why does the verse locate the permanence of Hashem's word in the heavens ? Because — as we learned in Chapter 11 — Aristotle and the Rambam viewed the heavens as permanent : the celestial bodies endure, unlike everything on earth, which is subject to generation, corruption and death. Your words are stable and unshakeable like the heavens are stable and unshakeable . Aside — the Rashash's 12 recitations: the custom of saying this verse 12 times when taking out the Sifrei Torah on Yom Kippur (and, as far as the Rabbi recalls, Rosh Hashanah) comes from the Rashash — Rabbi Shalom Sharabi, the mekubal of ~250 years ago. His kavanah is entirely different from the Rambam's peshat: davar (as opposed to amar ) denotes hard speech — the harsh edicts — so we pray 12 times: may Your hard edicts stay nitzav in heaven and not descend to us (with 12 different kavanot). Note: Spanish & Portuguese do not say this verse this way at all (perhaps once before the shofar). With God, it is always Meaning 2 כל אימת שהמונח הזה נאמר על הבורא — הוא במשמעות הזאת : whenever nitzav is said of God, it means permanence — never physical erect standing. Yaakov's Ladder — the Rambam's Re-reading The verse that underscores this: ויחלם והנה סלם מצב ארצה וראשו מגיע השמימה והנה מלאכי אלהים עלים וירדים בו. והנה ה' נצב עליו והנה ה' נצב עליו — Hashem was \"standing\" on the ladder? Hashem has no body to stand with. Rather: קבוע וקיים עליו — He is permanently, constantly over it. The context: Yaakov is on the run — fleeing Esav, on his way to Lavan, afraid. Hashem tells him: I am at the top. Everything is in My control. I am permanent and constant, watching over all of existence. The deeper meaning: the ladder stands with one end on the ground and its top reaching heaven, ובו מטפס ועולה כל העולה — all who ascend climb it, rung by rung, until they reach what is at its top. And Hashem is on top of the ladder but not part of it — so too in the nimshal: He is above all of existence but not part of existence. Whoever climbs the ladder correctly will encounter the Divine — He is always there. (Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam discusses this \"encounter\" in HaMaspik — we will reach it later this year, b'ezrat Hashem.) מלאכי אלהים — Prophets, Not Angels מלאכי אלהים = הנביאים. Full stop. Ask 99% of people about Jacob's ladder and they'll describe winged angels going up and down. The Rambam changes our entire understanding : it is not angels — it is prophets , people striving to ascend and encounter the Divine. Proof that malach can mean a human: וישלח מלאך ויצאנו ממצרים והנה אנחנו בקדש (Parashat Chukat) \"He sent a malach and took us out of Egypt\" — clearly Moshe Rabbeinu . ויעל מלאך ה' מן הגלגל אל הבכים (Shoftim) The malach went up from Gilgal to Bochim and spoke to the people — this cannot be a winged angel; it must be flesh and blood. The midrashim say it was Pinchas . This makes total sense linguistically: מלאך comes from הלך , to go — a malach is simply a messenger . Angels are called malachim because they are God's messengers; a human messenger is equally a malach . (The Rambam deliberately did not quote וישלח יעקב מלאכים לפניו אל עשו — because there the meaning is arguable, and he didn't want to enter the discussion; he chose unambiguous proofs.) The Gemara in Moed Katan now makes sense: אם דומה הרב למלאך ה' צבאות — יבקשו תורה מפיהו, ואם לאו — אל יבקשו תורה מפיהו (On the verse in Malachi: כי שפתי כהן ישמרו דעת ותורה יבקשו מפיהו כי מלאך ה' צבאות הוא.) Just as a rav must choose his talmid carefully (the Gemara in Makkot — lest he end up in exile with him), a talmid must choose his rav carefully: only seek Torah from a rav who is like a malach Hashem — a person of spiritual stature. And the Rambam's reading is not novel — the Midrash (Bereishit Rabbah) already says on Yaakov's ladder: ולמדנו לנביאים שנקראים מלאכים... והנה מלאכי אלהים — זה משה ואהרן The malachei Elokim of the ladder are not angels but prophets — Moshe and Aharon (a different interpretation from the Rambam's, but the same fundamental move). עלים וירדים — Why Ascent Before Descent כמה מדויקים דבריו: עולים ויורדים — how precise the verse is: ascending before descending. Why? Because after the ascent and the attainment of a level on the ladder of encountering the Divine, באה הירידה — the descent comes — להנהיג את אנשי הארץ , to lead and teach the people of the land . Recall Chapter 10 (two weeks ago): aliyah and yeridah can be physical — but can also mean the movement of the mind , ascent and descent of the intellect. That is the meaning here: the prophets ascend in thought to whatever level they attain — and then bring it back down to the people . You cannot be a prophet your whole life and simply stay there; you must come down to the people. Reading the Vision Whole (and a link back to Chapter 14) והנה סלם מצב ארצה — a ladder planted in the earth. The Rabbi noted, thinking aloud: how beautifully this fits the previous chapter — the bottom of the ladder is in adamah , where the people who remain Adam stay. וראשו מגיע השמימה — its top reaches heavenward. והנה מלאכי אלהים עלים וירדים בו — the noble ones, the prophets, the ishim (the bnei Elohim of Chapter 14!) ascend to encounter the Divine — and descend to teach. והנה ה' נצב עליו — and Hashem is constantly, permanently there on top . ונצבת על הצור — Standing on the Rock ובמשמעות הזאת — with meaning 2 we can also understand the episode of Moshe (touched on last week under amidah ). Moshe asks: ויאמר הראני נא את כבדך (The Rambam will treat these verses fully in later chapters.) And Hashem answers: ויאמר ה' הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור Look how everything comes together — the Rambam here relies on two other chapters: הנה מקום אתי — makom , as we learned in Chapter 8 , means rank, status . \"There is a high rank with Me.\" ונצבת על הצור — tzur , as the Rambam will explain in Chapter 16 (next term), means the Creator — the source and origin of all existence . So: you asked to see My glory, you want this makom , this rank? ונצבת על הצור — you shall be permanently on the tzur : constantly, stably contemplating the Source of all existence. Why must the Rambam read it this way? If nitzavta meant literal standing, then standing \"with God\" on the rock would imply a spatial context for Hakadosh Baruch Hu — impossible. Therefore it must mean permanence and stability: there is a makom , a status, in which you maintain a constant perspective on the tzur . And the Rambam closes the chapter: והנה כבר התבאר לך שמשמעות ניצב ועמד אחת היא בענין זה Nitzav and amad are one and the same in this sense — as it says: הנני עמד לפניך שם על הצור בחרב — \"I stand before you there on the rock at Chorev.\" Omed (Chapter 13, meaning 3) and nitzav (Chapter 15, meaning 2) both mean permanence : I am permanently with you, just as you are with Me — a constant, mutual relationship. You stand permanently contemplating the tzur; הנני עמד לפניך — I am constantly with you upon it. Chapter 15 — Summary Nitzav has two meanings: (1) to stand firmly, erect and tall (Miriam peering; the kings of Tehillim 2; Datan and Aviram; the bnei ha-Elohim in Iyov; hence matzeivah ); (2) permanence and stability (לעולם ה' דברך נצב בשמים — permanent like the heavens). In relation to Hakadosh Baruch Hu it always means the second : the eternal, unchanging nature of God, never physical posture. Hence Yaakov's ladder is a vision of prophets — not winged angels — ascending to attain knowledge of God and descending to teach the people, while Hashem remains permanently above all of existence, on top of the ladder but not part of it . And hence ונצבת על הצור: be permanently fixed in contemplation of the Source of all existence — with Hashem's reciprocal הנני עמד לפניך שם. Q&A Mark: Did Moshe never descend? Or did he have to descend to speak to the people? — The Rabbi: an excellent question, and one he had thought about himself. There seems to be a contradiction: on the one hand ואתה פה עמד עמדי, ונצבת על הצור, הנני עמד לפניך — Moshe is told to be constantly with Hashem (which is why Moshe separated from his wife — not something we generally emulate; we believe in being part of the world, but Moshe's utterly unique constant relationship demanded it). On the other hand, Moshe led the people — the ladder itself teaches עולים ויורדים , descending to teach. The answer (tentatively — perhaps it becomes clearer later in the Moreh): one must distinguish two elements within Moshe — an intellectual Moshe in constant thought, permanently על הצור, contemplating the Almighty without interruption, while simultaneously communicating those words to the people. How is such a level possible? \"I don't know how\" — but that is what one must say to resolve the contradiction. A participant (end of shiur), supporting this answer: In Shemot 34, after 40 days and nights with Hashem, ויהי ברדת משה — Moshe comes down and puts the masveh (veil/mask) over his face . The Rabbi, delighted: this fits beautifully as an answer to Mark's question — Moshe remains with Hashem (עמד עמדי), but to connect with the people he must \"come down\" in some sense, and even then requires the masveh just to speak with them. There is indeed \"some kind of coming down\" — but the redet is not a full departure from the encounter. Pinchas: Does the concept of the hitpashtut of Moshe Rabbeinu b'chol dor vador (an extension/presence of Moshe in every generation — perhaps from the Zohar?) tie in with ונצבת על הצור — a sort of permanent representation \"with\" Hashem? — The Rabbi: first we must define the concept. If it means there is someone in every generation carrying Moshe's mandate, the Rambam would likely not accept it — for the Rambam, there is nothing like Moshe . The Rabbi always understood it to mean Moshe's influence persists in every generation, not that there is a Moshe-figure in every generation. But if one takes the first reading, one could perhaps interpret ונצבת על הצור as \"there will be an element of you permanently on the tzur, in every generation.\" Perhaps. Interesting — but the source and meaning need checking (the Rabbi doubts it is actually the Zohar). (Jack's observation on the definite article in the Chapter 14 proof-texts is recorded above in Chapter 14.) Coming Up — Next Term (Elul) Chapter 16 — Tzur (צור): not reached this evening. In one word for now: tzur means the Source of all existence — which is what unlocks ויאמר ה' הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור. A whole chapter awaits; it completes the theme. Chapter 17 then begins an entirely new theme in the Moreh. Summer chazarah — the Rabbi's personal request: in many years of giving shiurim he has never asked this before. To take this journey seriously, please review Chapters 1–15 over the break — via the note summaries (Baruch Hashem for Dan's summaries!), the transcripts, the recordings, or reading the Moreh itself — so that in Elul we go straight in. B'Ezrat Hashem","7Xs2DqLA4V4":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem — Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam (Rav Dana's Hebrew edition) Chapter: 8 — Bitachon and Reliance (conclusion) Topic: The highest levels of bitachon — Level 3 (certainty) and Level 4 (hafkadah, total entrustment) — bitachon in the final redemption, and a summary of the entire chapter Schedule note: The final shiur before the summer break — completing Chapter 8 after some seven or eight weeks in it, the longest chapter of the sefer. We restart b'ezrat Hashem toward the end of August (Rosh Hashanah is very early this year, so timing to be confirmed). Recap — The Levels So Far Not bitachon at all (from last week): (a) one who has given up hope — despair; (b) one who puts his trust in another person or thing instead of Hashem. Those are simply lack of bitachon. Among those who do trust Hashem, there are four levels — and Rabbi Avraham will say this evening that he is not entirely sure all four fall under the word \"bitachon\"; the word itself shifts meaning by context. Level Description 1 Trusts Hashem — but with doubts: \"do I deserve it? Perhaps Hashem won't give it to me\" 2 Trusts (perhaps still with doubts) — and also turns to Hashem : prays, seeks Him, wants the relationship Rabbi Avraham: the vast majority of us are obligated in at least level 1 or 2 — that is the bare minimum; below that is not bitachon at all. Part 1: Level 3 — Certainty Without Doubt (חוסה בה') 1.1 The definition המדרגה השלישית היא מעלת מי שבטוח בהשגת משאלותיו מאתו יתעלה, מאמין בזאת בוודאות בלי ספקות One who trusts that his request will be fulfilled — with certainty, without any doubts . No \"perhaps, perhaps not\": I trust in Hashem and I know it will happen as I trust. וזה נקרא חוסה בה', מאמין בו, בוטח בו — this is \"taking refuge\" in Hashem: completely under His wing, and certain. The models: Avraham: והאמין בה' ויחשבה לו צדקה — when promised children and the Land, he trusted fully, and Hashem accounted it worthy. Our ancestors in Egypt: ויאמן העם וישמעו כי פקד ה' — the very first time Moshe and Aharon spoke to them, the people believed fully. The counter-example — the generation of the midbar: ודור המדבר יצא מן הכלל מפני שחסרו מעלה זו — as it says, בדבר הזה אינכם מאמינים בה' אלהיכם ההולך לפניכם בדרך ; and Assaf in Tehillim: לא האמינו לדברו ולא חכו לעצתו — not only did they not trust, they would not even take the advice (they still didn't listen to the two spies). 1.2 Who can reach it? Only through nevuah or Ruach HaKodesh מעלת הבטחון זו לא ישיגו רוב בני אדם Most people cannot attain it — because how can you be certain Hashem will grant what you seek? (And remember: bitachon is not always about what I want — it is about what I need in order to do Hashem's work. Even when you are convinced something is best for your avodah, you cannot be certain it will happen.) Certainty is possible only through prophecy or Ruach HaKodesh — or by being told by someone who has experienced them: Avraham could be certain — כשבישרו ה' על דבר הבנים — because he was told in prophetic revelation. The generation leaving Egypt — ויאמן העם — note: the people did not hear from Hashem directly; they were told by the messenger of Hashem (Moshe) through nevuah — and trusting a verified navi is itself obligatory (below). On this level David said: ויושיעם כי חסו בו ; בך חסיתי ; ביי חסיתי איך תאמרו לנפשי... 1.3 Important: the word \"bitachon\" is context-dependent ולפעמים יבוא ביטוי בטיחה in different senses — it can denote levels 1, 2 or 3 depending on context, since all three are bitachon, albeit different levels. Sometimes \"boteach\" describes one who trusts with doubts; sometimes the seeker; sometimes total certainty. (The Rabbi asked mid-shiur for someone to check how often the shoresh בטח appears across Tanach — the answer came back: around 120 times, roughly half in Tehillim, matching his estimate of ~100+ with 50–60 in Tehillim. His summer suggestion: whenever you read a bitachon pasuk, ask which level it is describing in context.) 1.4 The prophets' praise — and the restriction This is the loftiest level of bitachon (level 4 may not be \"bitachon\" at all — below), and the Nevi'im praise it greatly: והבוטח בה' חסד יסובבנו · בוטח בה' אשריו · ה' צבאות אשרי אדם בוטח בך But — unlike levels 1 and 2, which bind every shomer Torah in all his ways: ואילו מעלה זו לא תיכון אלא במי שהוא חף מן העוונות ונקי מפשעים והגיע לשלמות וטעם מהשגחתו יתעלה Level 3 befits only one clean of sin who has reached perfection and tasted Hashem's hashgacha. David captures both tiers in one verse: ויבטחו בך יודעי שמך כי לא עזבת דורשיך ה' — \"those who know Your name \" (prophecy/Ruach HaKodesh — level 3) will trust in You; \"You do not forsake those who seek You \" — the seekers (level 2). And the complete, faithful seeking is the prerequisite — the necessary preface — to reaching level 3. 1.5 Chanina ben Dosa — the fluency test The famous Mishnah in Berachot: R' Chanina ben Dosa would pray for the sick and know the outcome. They asked how: אם שגורה תפלתי בפי — יודע אני שהוא מקובל; ואם לאו — יודע אני שהוא מטורף \"If my prayer is fluent in my mouth, I know it is accepted; if not, I know it is rejected.\" (This is the same R' Chanina who lived למעלה מן הטבע — the vinegar-that-burned-like-oil story, and the snake story we saw earlier in the chapter.) At times he had exactly this level of certainty. And crucially — it is not arrogance: אין זה מפאת בטחונם מצדקתם וזכותם, אלא מפאת בטחונם בחסדו יתעלה — כמו שאמר דוד: ואני בחסדך בטחתי יגל לבי בישועתך The certainty flows not from \"I am so righteous that Hashem must answer me,\" but from closeness — from trust in His chesed : I know this is what is best, what is needed for Am Yisrael and the world, and therefore He will grant it. 1.6 Being told by a navi — a Torah obligation of certainty כל דבר שבא ממנו יתעלה על ידי נביא נאמן — חייב כל מאמין להיות בטוח בהשגתו ללא שום ספק Even without your own prophetic experience: once a prophet has been verified (recall the machloket Rishonim on verification — Saadia leaning on miracles; the Rambam on the navi's qualities plus predictions that come true), you are obligated to accept his word with full certainty. This is why the anger went out against Bnei Yisrael at Masa u-Meriva: they had seen what they had seen — כי עין בעין נראה אתה ה' ועננך עומד עליהם — and had been told by the navi that Hashem would lead them through the desert; yet they cast doubts and tested the hashgacha — היש ה' בקרבנו אם אין . Hence — in this week's parsha, Va'etchanan : לא תנסו את ה' אלהיכם כאשר ניסיתם במסה Do not test Hashem as you tested Him at Masa — i.e., once a navi's prophecy is verified, you must believe and hold it true. As the Rambam codifies at the end of Yesodei HaTorah (ch. 10) : a navi may be verified directly or by another navi's testimony; and a navi known as such — אסור לחשב אחריו ולומר שמא נבואתו אינה אמת... ואסור לנסותו יותר מדאי... אלא מאחר שנודע שזה נביא — יאמינו וידעו שה' בקרבם, ולא יבחנו ולא ינסו אותו It is a prohibition — one of the 613 — to second-guess or keep testing a verified navi. Nowadays: we have no nevuah (as the Gemara says, and as the Rambam discusses in the Moreh), so this route to level 3 is largely inapplicable — חייבים אנחנו כל שומעי התורה to hold at least levels 1 and 2; level 3 binds whenever it genuinely comes to us from Hashem . 1.7 בטחו בה' עדי עד — two readings בטחו בה' עדי עד כי בי-ה ה' צור עולמים Rabbi Avraham: the verse depends on which level \"bitachon\" means here. If level 3 (commanded through prophecy): adei ad = trust completely, absolutely . If levels 1–2 : complete certainty is impossible (you don't know it will happen) — so adei ad = trust in Hashem in all matters, for everything . Support from the verse's ending: כי בי-ה ה' צור עולמים — Hashem is the Rock of the worlds (and what \"Tzur\" means is exactly one of tomorrow evening's Moreh chapters — this very verse comes up there!) — שהשם יתעלה מייסד כל העולמות — He founds and sustains all; everything one could lean on — wealth, property, standing — traces back to Him; therefore lean on Him in everything, ואסור להישען בזולתו שהוא פעול ולא פועל, מסובב ולא סיבה — never on what is caused rather than the Cause, subject to change, suffering and death: אל תבטחו בנדיבים בבן אדם שאין לו תשועה... תצא רוחו ישוב לאדמתו... אשרי שאל יעקב בעזרו שברו על ה' אלהיו . 1.8 Bitachon and the final redemption — which level? Timely for this week — shavua shechal bo, our minds on Tisha B'Av, the Beit HaMikdash, the Sanhedrin, the geulah. Work it through the levels: Level 1 — trust it will come? Certainly. Giving up hope on the geulah is a failure of emunah in the fundamentals. Level 2 — seek it, pray for it, want it, try to bring it? Certainly — three times daily in the Amidah. Level 3 — full confidence it will happen in my lifetime? No. מוטל עלינו כל שומרי תורת משה רבנו... שנאמין בגאולה הנכספת We are obligated to believe in the awaited redemption — Hashem told us in His sefer: ושב ה' אלהיך את שבותך ורחמך... ושב וקבצך מכל העמים אשר הפיצך ה' אלהיך שמה (aside: Menasseh Ben Israel — founder of the Spanish & Portuguese community here in England, 1656 — took \"from all the peoples\" so literally that he held Mashiach could not come while England banned Jews, which drove his campaign for readmission; probably not the pshat, but a wonderful historical note) ; and in Haazinu: כי ידין ה' עמו ועל עבדיו יתנחם... ; and the Nevi'im are full of it beyond counting. And note the model of the Avot: Hashem told Avraham at the Brit Bein HaBetarim — וגם את הגוי אשר יעבדו דן אנכי ואחרי כן יצאו ברכוש גדול — but we never find Him telling Yaakov or Yosef directly. Yet Yaakov : הנה אנכי מת והיה אלהים עמכם והשיב אתכם אל ארץ אבותיכם ; and Yosef : פקד יפקד אלהים אתכם והעליתם את עצמותי מזה אתכם . They believed the prophecy though not told explicitly — all the more so us. But as to one's own lifetime: אך אם יבטח כל אחד מאתנו כי יקום הדבר ויהיה בחייו כפי דרגת הבטחון השלישית — אין זה בטחוננו כפי המעלה השלישית. כי אפשר שיקום הדבר בזכותו ורחמיו יתעלה, ואפשר שלא יקום מפני חטאינו. ולפיכך נאמר: אשרי המחכה ויגיע — כי לא כל המחכה יגיע It may come in our days — it could be today, b'ezrat Hashem, it could be Thursday, it could be a hundred or two hundred years — we cannot be certain we will see it; \"fortunate is the one who waits and arrives , for not everyone who waits arrives.\" We hold levels 1 and 2, we pray, and we hope to be among the fortunate. 1.9 Ruach HaKodesh — level 3 without full prophecy ומלבד זאת אולי תהיה לאחד מאתנו a divinely-granted intuition: מגודל חסידותו וחסדו יתבונן בו כח הרגשה זו, ומתוך כח הרגשה זו יבטח בדבר ההוא כפי המעלה השלישית — אף על פי שלא באה לו ההתגלות הנבואית ממש A person of great piety may be given a powerful sense — strong divine intuition — and on its strength trust with certainty, though no actual prophecy occurred. Hillel HaZaken (Gemara Berachot): מעשה בהלל הזקן שהיה בא בדרך ושמע קול צווחה בעיר, אמר: מובטח אני שאין זו בתוך ביתי. ועליו הכתוב אומר: משמועה רעה לא יירא נכון לבו בטוח בה' Hearing screams in the city, he said \"I am certain this is not from my house.\" Not prophecy — intuition at the level of Ruach HaKodesh; and that too sits within level 3. Part 2: Level 4 — הפקדה: Total Entrustment (perhaps beyond bitachon) 2.1 The definition המדרגה הרביעית היא מעלת ההפקדה. ופירושה — שיפקיד האדם את ענייניו או אחד מהם בידי השם יתעלה ויסיר מעליו כל צפייה ותשוקה, עד שאם לא ישיג דבר שהשתוקק עליו — יסתפק... ונמצא מסתפק במה שגוזר ועושה השם יתעלה A person deposits his affairs — all of them, or even one of them — into Hashem's hands and removes all expectation and desire . Whether or not he attains anything makes no difference to him; he is content with whatever Hashem decrees and does. The contrast with level 3: level 3 is still about something — \"I have full confidence I will receive X\" (even the navi: full confidence Hashem will grant him life to speak His word). Level 4 says: whatever happens to me — I have no want; it is all in Hashem's hands; there is nothing left to seek. 2.2 Is it even bitachon? זאת המעלה, אם אפשר למנותה ממעלות הבטחון — היא העליונה שבכולן; ואם אין למנותה ביניהן — הרי היא עליונה וקדושה למעלה מכולן If it counts among the levels of bitachon — it is the highest; if not — it is a holy level above them all. Why the doubt? Because bitachon, as defined all these weeks, is trust in Hashem for something you want . Here there is no want. It may simply be a different — higher — category. 2.3 The sources — and the Rabbi's honest difficulties (a) Beshalach — התיצבו וראו את ישועת ה': trapped against the sea, Moshe says \"stand and see the salvation of Hashem…\" Rabbi Avraham reads a hint to hafkadah here. The Rabbi's difficulty: yeshuah implies waiting to be saved — which is precisely a want; on level 4, whether I live or die is equally accepted, so \"salvation\" seems to cut against entrustment. The footnote suggests the weight is on התיצבו — stand firm , whatever happens. And a lovely connection: tomorrow evening's Moreh includes the word נצב — which can denote firmness/permanence — so perhaps: stand firm, permanently — and whatever comes is the yeshuat Hashem , because you have wholly entrusted yourself. (The Rabbi: \"I find it difficult; if anyone reads it another way, I'd be happy to hear.\") (b) The anger over the water in the midbar — ויש בזה רמז לחיוב הבטחון כפי מעלה זאת — Rabbi Avraham calls it a hint that they were expected to reach entrustment. (The Rabbi: note the tension — a few lines earlier the same episode illustrated the failure of level 3 ; how he derives an expectation of hafkadah here is not clear. אלו הם הרמזים — they are, as he says, hints.) (c) Explicit — Eliezer eved Avraham: הנה אנכי נצב על עין המים ובנות אנשי העיר יוצאות לשאוב... היא האשה אשר הוכיח ה' לבן אדוני (That word נצב again!) Eliezer sets the test and hands the outcome entirely to Hashem — כלומר הפקיד את הדבר בידי השם יתעלה : whatever Hashem chooses and desires as beneficial — \"that is what I have intended and decided.\" Not up to me anymore. (d) David — השלך על ה' יהבך והוא יכלכלך: cast your burden on Hashem — מסירת העניין בידו יתעלה — and He will sustain you as His good wisdom decrees. (The Rabbi again playing honest devil's advocate: v'hu yechalkelecha — \"He will sustain you\" — names a want, sustenance; arguable.) (e) The strongest textual case — גול על ה' דרכך ובטח עליו והוא יעשה: roll your way onto Hashem, trust in Him, and He will do . Rabbi Avraham himself spells out the two readings of והוא יעשה : He will do His will ( כפי מה שעלה רצון מלפניו ) — a pure level-4 verse: heap everything on Him; whatever He does is good. He will do your will — then it belongs with levels 1–3. And he offers a beautiful composite reading: if you cannot reach the hafkadah of גול על ה' דרכך — then at least hold fast to the bitachon of ובטח עליו והוא יעשה . Compare ויבטחו בך יודעי שמך / כי לא עזבת דורשיך — one verse spanning distinct levels. 2.4 The levels are contiguous — and we move between them כי כל מדרגות הבטחון ראויות לשבח... ואפשר שההדמות ביניהן לפי שהן רצופות. כי בעל הבטחון יש לו בעת... המעלה הראשונה שקראתיה תקוה, ויש לו... מעלת ההפקדה The levels shade into one another, and one person moves among them: sometimes in tikvah (trusting while fearing), sometimes — in certain matters — in complete entrustment. The Rabbi's sharp observation: we usually arrive at level 4 only when there is no alternative — \"there is nothing more any human can do; I hand it all to Hashem.\" Rabbi Avraham is teaching us to come to hafkadah even when there is an alternative . 2.5 The explicit exemplars David (Shmuel, as he flees Yerushalayim): אם אמצא חן בעיני ה' והשיבני והראני אתו ואת נוהו; ואם כה יאמר לא חפצתי בך — הנני, יעשה לי כאשר טוב בעיניו \"If I find favour — He will bring me back and show me [the Aron] and His abode; and if He says 'I do not want you' — here I am; let Him do to me as is good in His eyes.\" Brings me back — great; doesn't — also great. Explicit hafkadah. Chananya, Mishael and Azarya (Daniel), before the furnace: הן איתי אלהנא די אנחנא פלחין יכיל לשיזבותנא... והן לא — ידיע להוא לך מלכא די לאלהיך לא איתנא פלחין \"Our God whom we serve can save us — and if not , know, O king, that we will not serve your gods.\" We do not know whether we will be saved; either way, whatever happens is right — and we serve Him regardless. 2.6 Rabbi Avraham's closing words on the chapter ועכשיו איני מטיל ספק שכבר נתתי לפרק זה ביאור מספיק... וה' יתן לנו — הוא אשר מאתו נבקש עזר — בתיקון מצפוננו הפנימיות והחיצוניות כפי שנאמר בחיבור זה \"I have now given this chapter sufficient explanation.\" And the fitting end: after seven or eight weeks of intellectual study, the work is implementation — and that is sought from Hashem Himself: may He grant us help in repairing our inner and outer conscience as set out in this work. Part 3: Summary of the Whole of Chapter 8 Part 1 — The definition. Bitachon is complete reliance on Hashem, Creator of all, to whom everything in the universe is subservient. Living with it produces betichah — security and serenity. One does not place trust in the intermediaries Hashem created — medicine, wealthy patrons, any person or means. Part 2 — The three TYPES (not levels) of bitachon. The bitachon of the prophets , who divested themselves of natural means by Hashem's instruction (Eliyahu and the ravens). Denial of Hashem's providence — Aristotle's world running on self-sufficient natural law — including the hidden deniers who outwardly affirm bitachon but deep down don't. (The Rabbi's challenge: examine ourselves honestly — do we say \"I trust in Hashem\" and then chase this person, that connection, that doctor, without the framework we've learned?) The correct bitachon of the majority: Hashem oversees all the regular intermediaries He set in the world — real causes with real effects — and can change their regular operation whenever He wills. We engage the natural world (unlike the prophets, who had explicit instruction not to need it); we neither go overboard in pursuit of goals nor become lazy relying on miracles (the chachamim had jobs; relying on miracles is arrogance). Part 3 — Cause and effect. No contradiction between bitachon and natural causality. Not the Mutakallimun , who abolished cause and effect to \"protect\" providence (against experience — and mocked by scientists); not Aristotle , whose causes run immutably once created. The correct view: real cause and effect, under Hashem's constant oversight, changeable at His will. Part 4 — The five categories of employing bitachon (× physical/spiritual = ten): Avoiding serious harm (mortal illness, a storm at sea): employ every natural means, pray (Tehillim 91 — יושב בסתר עליון — the Rabbi's recommended psalm for strengthening trust), and remember the outcome is Hashem's; when nothing remains but bitachon, sometimes one is saved and sometimes not — that is His hashgacha. (Including the discussion of enemies.) Minor harms: Rabbi Avraham's chidush — do not employ bitachon (the wasp bothering you); other Rishonim disagree — trust Hashem even in the smallest things. Essentials (basic food, clothing, shelter): trust and do everything reasonably possible — with the two questions (why is bitachon needed if Hashem feeds all creatures? and is trust natural — עיני כל אליך ישברו — or praiseworthy?) and their answers. Important non-essentials: for yourself, essentials suffice; for wife, children and dependents — bitachon even for the non-essential; and for some (arguably all of us nowadays) the non-essential is effectively essential. Luxuries: wrong to employ bitachon — \"make me win the lottery\" — who said you may ask for that? Part 5 — The four levels (tonight): (1) trust with doubt; (2) trust plus seeking and prayer; (3) full certainty — only via nevuah, a verified navi, or Ruach HaKodesh; (4) hafkadah — entrustment: whatever Hashem does is good. For the redemption : levels 1 and 2 — always; level 3 — impossible regarding one's own lifetime ( אשרי המחכה ויגיע ). Coming Up Tomorrow (Tuesday) — the final Moreh shiur of the season: three chapters including Nitzav and Tzur — both of which surfaced tonight (Eliezer's אנכי נצב; בטחו בה'... כי בי-ה ה' צור עולמים is quoted by the Rambam there). Then the summer break — restarting b'ezrat Hashem toward the end of August (Rosh Hashanah is early this year). B'Ezrat Hashem","iBFuiL5ZNwk":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Ta'aniyot — Rambam Perek: 5 (the final chapter of Hilchot Ta'aniyot) Topic: The fixed fasts of all Israel — their purpose (teshuvah, not mourning), the four fasts, Ta'anit Esther, the Nine Days and the week of Tisha B'Av, the halachot of Tisha B'Av itself, and the post-churban decrees Schedule note: Final Thursday-evening shiur until Elul, in preparation for Tisha B'Av. No shiur next Thursday (Tisha B'Av itself); the last shiurim of the season are Monday and Tuesday next week. Recap — Hilchot Ta'aniyot So Far The Rambam has five chapters on Ta'aniyot: Perek Content 1 The yesod : a Torah mitzvah — not to fast, but to cry out to Hashem with trumpets/shofar in times of distress and do teshuvah, and not to write troubles off as coincidence and chance. The chachamim added fasts; when fasts are established, who fasts, private fasts and how to accept them, conduct while fasting 2 The communal troubles over which the chachamim of the generation sound the alarm — plagues, wild animals, locusts, wars, persecutions, collapsing buildings; the affected city fasts and cries out, surrounding communities pray 3 The rainfall fasts specifically — escalating in severity: individuals/chachamim first, then communal series 4 The special order of tefillot at the most severe stage — seven fasts, the Torah taken out to the street, sackcloth, even Ne'ilah prayed. (Largely not practised nowadays, certainly outside Eretz Yisrael) Perek 5 turns to fasts binding on all of Am Yisrael , everywhere — not tied to a specific afflicted community. Halacha 1 — The Purpose: Teshuvah, Not Commemoration יש שם ימים שכל ישראל מתענים בהם מפני הצרות שאירעו בהן, כדי לעורר הלבבות ולפתוח דרכי התשובה. ויהיה זה זכרון למעשינו הרעים ומעשה אבותינו שהיה כמעשינו עתה, עד שגרם להם ולנו אותן הצרות. שבזכרון דברים אלו נשוב להיטיב, שנאמר: והתודו את עונם ואת עון אבותם במעלם אשר מעלו בי There are days on which all Israel fast because of the troubles that occurred on them — in order to awaken the hearts and open the paths of teshuvah ; a reminder of our own evil deeds and of the deeds of our ancestors which were like our deeds now , until they caused them and us these troubles; through remembering, we return to the good. Two questions the Rabbi raised Why this verse? In Hilchot Teshuvah, the Rambam derives the Torah mitzvah of viduy from Bamidbar: איש או אשה כי יעשו מכל חטאת האדם... והתודו את חטאתם אשר עשו . Here he quotes a different verse — from Vayikra: they shall confess their sins and the sins of their fathers . Why switch? Why the emphasis \"the deeds of our ancestors which were like our deeds now \"? The answer (Rav Kappach; the Rabbi heard it years ago from Rav Asher Weiss, and it is based on the Chasam Sofer — and it is really the simple pshat of the Rambam) The essence of all these fasts is teshuvah — not mourning, not commemoration. (Yom Kippur is explicitly so in the Torah — כי ביום הזה יכפר עליכם לטהר אתכם — and Ta'anit Esther is a different creature; we mean the four fasts: 17 Tammuz, Tisha B'Av, Tzom Gedalya, 10 Tevet.) The Rambam holds we do not institute fasts to remember sad things and mourn the past. We are not a religion of commemorating catastrophes. Good things — yes: Hashem took us out of Egypt, we make a festival; carried us forty years in the midbar — a festival; gave us the Torah — a festival. But commemorative days for bad events per se have no point. The foundation of these days is teshuvah. Where did the Rambam get this? From the famous statement — which 99% of people misquote. People say: כל דור שלא נבנה בית המקדש בימיו — כאילו נחרב בימיו (\"as if it was destroyed in its days\"). That is not what it says. The Talmud Yerushalmi (beginning of Yoma) says: כל דור שאינו נבנה בימיו — מעלין עליו כאילו הוא החריבו \"Any generation in which it is not rebuilt — it is accounted as if that generation itself destroyed it .\" Not the passive nechrav (nif'al — \"it was destroyed,\" sad, something that happened to us, to be mourned generation after generation) but the active, causative hu hechrivo (hif'il) — we destroyed it . (The Yerushalmi there also notes: the first Temple fell for the three cardinal sins; the second generation — שהיו יגעים בתורה וזהירים במצוות — toiled in Torah and mitzvot, אלא שהיו אוהבים את הממון ושונאים אלו את אלו שנאת חנם — but they loved money and hated one another baselessly. We always hear about sinat chinam; we rarely hear the first half — the love and pursuit of money that corrupted them.) This explains everything. The fasts exist so that we do not continue the path of our ancestors. And that is why the Rambam swaps the viduy verse: not the ordinary confession of one's own sin (Bamidbar), but והתודו את עונם ואת עון אבותם — confess your sins and your fathers' sins. The same question arises in the daily viduy: אבל אנחנו ואבותינו חטאנו — why drag our parents into our confession? We are not responsible for their sins. We are responsible for continuing their path — for not breaking the cycle. That is the confession: yes, we sinned, and our ancestors sinned, and we continued it — and now we stop. Practical framing for this period: every year the questions pour in — what may I do in the three weeks, the nine days, the week of Tisha B'Av, until when meat, and so on. All fine — but too often we drown in the restriction-details and lose the overarching picture. The Rambam opens the whole chapter with the foundation: the entire purpose of these times is לפתוח דרכי התשובה — so that it will not be said of our generation, כאילו הוא החריבו . In one halacha, the Rambam changes the perspective of the whole period. Halachot 2–3 — The Four Fasts and What Happened on Them Third of Tishrei — Tzom Gedalya יום שלישי בתשרי, שבו נהרג גדליה בן אחיקם ונכבת גחלת ישראל הנשארה וסיבב להתם גלותן Gedaliah ben Achikam was killed, \"the remaining ember of Israel was extinguished, and it caused their exile to be completed.\" The background (from the Nevi'im): After the churban, Nevuchadnezzar exiled most of Am Yisrael but permitted a small remnant in the south of Yehudah under Gedaliah as governor. Yishmael ben Netanya — jealous of Gedaliah (he felt Gedaliah was not of Davidic lineage, and resented subjugation to Nevuchadnezzar), and under the influence of Ba'alis king of Amon — murdered him. Gedaliah had been warned of the plot and refused to accept the report — the Gemara learns from here that sometimes one must accept (and speak) lashon hara for protection — and he was killed. (Whether he was killed on Rosh Hashanah with the fast postponed, or actually on the 3rd — the Rambam clearly holds he was killed on the third of Tishrei.) Why do we fast for one man's death? (\"If I died, would Gedaliah fast for me ?\" as someone once asked the Rabbi.) We are not fasting for Gedaliah . The consequence of his assassination was that the terrified remnant fled to Egypt — sealing the completeness of the first churban and the exile of Am Yisrael. That finale is what the fast marks. Tenth of Tevet שבו סמך נבוכדנאצר הרשע מלך בבל על ירושלים והביאה במצור ובמצוק Nevuchadnezzar laid siege to Yerushalayim. The timeline: the siege began on 10 Tevet — two and a half years before the churban; on 9 Tammuz the walls of the first Temple's Yerushalayim were breached; a month later, on 9 Av, the Temple itself was destroyed. We fast over the origins : when contemplating a tragedy, one looks not only at the event but at where it began — with the siege, despite all Yirmiyahu's warnings. Megillat Ta'anit adds (compiled by Chizkiya ben Garon's household, in the era of Hillel and Shammai; its content is quoted in the Gemara) two further events of 10 Tevet and the days before it: the Torah was translated into Greek — into another language for the first time; and Ezra HaSofer died . Ezra in many ways replicated Moshe's role: he carried the Mesorah, stopped the intermarriage, reinstated what had been forgotten (the pesukim say they had not built Sukkot in so long they had forgotten it). The Rambam brings only the siege — the main reason — but be aware of the others. Seventeenth of Tammuz — five things happened י\"ז בתמוז — חמשה דברים אירעו בו: נשתברו הלוחות; ובטל התמיד מבית ראשון; והובקעה העיר בחורבן שני; ושרף אפוסטמוס הרשע את התורה; והעמיד צלם בהיכל The Luchot were broken — by Moshe. The Korban Tamid ceased in the first Temple — under siege there were no longer animals for the daily offering. The city was breached in the second churban (the Romans). Note: in the first churban the walls were breached on 9 Tammuz (as above). The Gemara explains the chachamim did not institute two fasts eight days apart — too much — and fixed the 17th, because the second churban is the more recent one, the one we are still inside. Apostomus (a Roman general) burned the Torah publicly. And placed a tzelem in the Heichal. The Yerushalmi has two readings: הועמד — it was set up, i.e. by Menashe (worse — a Jewish king!); or העמיד — Apostomus himself set it up. The Rambam follows the second reading. Are these three fasts strictly obligatory today? The Gemara in Rosh Hashanah (Rav Papa) grades them: in times of persecution — obligatory; in times of shalom — they become days of joy; in between — רצו מתענין, רצו אין מתענין — voluntary. Some Rishonim (e.g. the Rashba ) take this at face value: aside from Tisha B'Av, these fasts are strictly mandatory only when there is trouble (and one could well argue Am Yisrael is at war now ), and mere minhag in stable times. The Rambam brings none of this. He clearly understands \"when there is shalom\" = when the Temple stands . Temple — no fasts; no Temple — obligatory. (Hence the interesting question: did they fast during the second Temple era? The Rambam writes elsewhere that they chose to, per that Gemara.) And so everyone rules today — Shulchan Aruch included: these fasts are a chiyuv , not a custom contingent on quiet times, until the Temple is rebuilt. Halacha 3 — Tisha B'Av: five things happened תשעה באב — חמשה דברים אירעו בו נגזר על אבותינו במדבר שלא יכנסו לארץ — the night the spies returned, the people wailed, and it was decreed the generation would not enter the Land. 2–3. The first and second Temples were destroyed (or began to be destroyed) on 9 Av. The great city was captured — ובת תור שמה. The Rambam's text of the Mishnah reads Bet Tor (today called Beitar): tens of thousands of Israel were there, והיה להם מלך גדול ודימו כל ישראל וגדולי החכמים שהוא המלך המשיח — they had a great king (Bar Kochva), and all Israel and the greatest of the chachamim thought he was the Melech HaMashiach — ונפל ביד הרומיים ונהרגו כולם, והיתה צרה גדולה כמו חורבן המקדש . Our tradition places this 52 years after the churban (dates given range c. 122–130 CE). Only ~70 years had separated the first and second Temples — so hope of imminent rebuilding was live and burning; Bar Kochva led the revival, and Rome crushed it, killing thousands upon thousands to extinguish the hope (the Gemara — not to be taken literally, there is something deeper — says the blood flowed 40 mil to the sea). The catastrophe is tied to the Temple: the hope of rebuilding died with Beitar. ובו ביום המוכן לפורענות חרש טורנוסרופוס הרשע את ההיכל וסביביו — on that day, designated for calamity, Turnus Rufus ploughed the Heichal and its surroundings — לקיים מה שנאמר: ציון שדה תחרש — the culmination: Zion ploughed like a field. And again: we mark these not to wallow in the past, but so that we do not continue the deeds of our ancestors. Halacha 4–5 — The Four Fasts in the Navi; Ta'anit Esther; Shabbat Collisions The four fasts are explicit in the Kabbalah (the Nevi'im) צום הרביעי וצום החמישי וצום השביעי וצום העשירי יהיה... לששון ולשמחה (Zechariah) צום הרביעי — 17 Tammuz (the fourth month: Nisan, Iyar, Sivan, Tammuz) צום החמישי — Tisha B'Av (the fifth month) צום השביעי — Tzom Gedalya (the seventh month — Tishrei) צום העשירי — 10 Tevet (the tenth month) The thirteenth of Adar — Ta'anit Esther ונהגו כל ישראל בזמנים אלו להתענות, ובי\"ג באדר — זכר לתענית שהתענו בימי המן, שנאמר: דברי הצומות וזעקתם Note the language: נהגו — a custom. And note the reason: not to remember something sad , but to remember that our ancestors fasted — the fasts Esther initiated: לך כנוס את כל היהודים... וצומו עלי... שלושת ימים . It appears nowhere in the Gemara; the custom arose in the Geonic period (sometime around the 7th–9th centuries). Hence it is by far the most lenient fast — the Rama goes so far as to say even minor eye-pain exempts one. Participant question (Robert): so Ta'anit Esther carries no teshuvah-pressure like the others? Answer: correct — it is not a sad day and not a teshuvah day; it is purely commemorative of the fasting itself (which is why it's called Ta'anit Esther ). Follow-up: but we say the fast-day Tachanunim/Selichot on it, which doesn't quite fit? Answer: those Selichot are very late additions (roughly the 14th–15th centuries or later) — once composed for fast days they were extended to Ta'anit Esther too, since it is structurally a fast; conceptually it is definitely not the same. When fasts collide with Shabbat 13 Adar on Shabbat (Purim on Sunday): we fast earlier — on Thursday . We don't push it after Purim, because it isn't tied to a sad date needing observance — it commemorates the fasting itself; and we don't fast Friday out of kevod Shabbat (a fast in its proper time may be completed on Friday, but a fast that is being moved anyway is not placed on Friday). The four fasts on Shabbat — pushed to Sunday (their nature is sad, teshuvah-awakening — not for Shabbat). If a fast falls on Friday , one fasts on Friday — in our fixed calendar only Asara B'Tevet can fall on Friday. On all these fasts ובכל הצומות האלו אין מתריעין ולא מתפללין בהן תפלת נעילה, אבל קורין בתורה שחרית ומנחה ויחל משה. ובכולן אוכלין ושותין בלילה — חוץ מתשעה באב No trumpet-alarm and no Ne'ilah (unlike the severe rain-fasts of Perek 4); Torah reading of ויחל משה (the Thirteen Attributes) at Shacharit and Mincha; and on all of them one may eat and drink through the night until alot hashachar — except Tisha B'Av . Halacha 6 — משנכנס אב: the Nine Days and the Week of Tisha B'Av משנכנס אב ממעטין בשמחה. ושבת שחל תשעה באב להיות בתוכה — אסור לספר ולכבס וללבוש כלי מגוהץ... עד שיעבור התענית, ואפילו לכבס ולהניח לאחר התענית אסור First observation: the Rambam has no \"Three Weeks\" There is no concept of three weeks in the Rambam — the notion of a restricted three-week period is quite late. Mi-dina of the Gemara, haircuts and weddings are fully permitted in that span (the Ashkenazi restrictions are minhag). The only \"three weeks\" item the Rambam recognises anywhere is the minhag of the special Haftarot read in this period. (Music is no proof — the Rambam forbids instrumental music all year round , as we'll see below; and Shehecheyanu-avoidance is a much later idea.) Second observation: where are the Nine-Days restrictions? The commentators ask: the Talmud explicitly restricts from Rosh Chodesh Av — e.g. one who already has children may not marry; no building for luxury (a swimming pool in the nine days). Why does the Rambam list none of it here? Answer: he already codified it — in Perek 3, Halacha 8 , regarding the escalating rain-fasts: reduce business dealings , luxury building and planting , and marriages (for those who have fulfilled peru u'revu). The Rambam understands ממעטין בשמחה as a general rubric that incorporates those same reductions: from Rosh Chodesh Av (i.e. from yesterday) one reduces non-essential business (where you can afford the hit), luxury construction, and weddings where one already has children. The week of Tisha B'Av — the real Gemara-level restrictions This year is a proper shavua shechal bo — Tisha B'Av falls on Thursday (the last two years it fell on Sunday, so we effectively had none). From Motzaei Shabbat: No haircuts — including, apparently, shaving. No laundering , and no wearing laundered/pressed clothes — even simple linen garments — until the fast passes; even laundering now to set aside for after the fast is forbidden . Practical prep: any clothes you plan to wear next week should be worn some hours this week so they are no longer \"fresh\" (the Kaf HaChayim records doing exactly this) — e.g. one shirt to shul Shabbat morning, switch to another Shabbat afternoon; a few hours suffices; use common sense. This is about outer clothes — suits, shirts, dresses — not underwear , which is changed daily regardless. Note the Rambam's repeated עד שיעבור התענית — until the fast passes : immediately after Tisha B'Av everything is permitted (the Sefardi position, and the Rambam and Shulchan Aruch read this way) — unlike the Ashkenazi practice of extending into the 10th — even though, ironically, the Temple was still burning on the 10th (it was set alight on the afternoon of the 9th); the chachamim fixed the boundary at the fast's end. Especially this year, when the fast ends into Erev Shabbat: laundry and haircuts Thursday night are permitted. Meat this week וכבר נהגו ישראל שלא לאכול בשר בשבת זו ולא יכנסו למרחץ עד שיעבור התענית. ויש מקומות שנהגו לבטל השחיטה מראש חדש ועד התענית Israel have the custom not to eat meat during this week and not to enter the bathhouse until the fast passes; some places shut the shechita from Rosh Chodesh. Note: (a) it is explicitly a minhag — mi-dina of the Gemara meat is forbidden only at the seudah hamafseket; (b) the Rambam writes נהגו ישראל as if universal — apparently unaware of the Temani minhag of eating meat right up to the seudah hamafseket. The Shulchan Aruch records three customs: no meat all three weeks; from Rosh Chodesh; or the week of Tisha B'Av only. The general accepted Sefardi custom today (per the Ben Ish Chai): meat on Rosh Chodesh itself, not after — though it remains community-dependent (many Moroccan communities eat meat through this week until Motzaei Shabbat). Halachot 7–8 — Erev Tisha B'Av and the Seudah Hamafseket תשעה באב לילו כיומו לכל דבר, ואין אוכלין אלא מבעוד יום, ובין השמשות שלו ספק כיום הכפורים. ולא יאכל בשר ולא ישתה יין בסעודה המפסקת; אבל שותה הוא יין מגתו שיש לו שלשה ימים או פחות, ואוכל בשר מליח שיש לו שלשה ימים או יתר. ולא יאכל שני תבשילין The night is like the day in all respects; eating only while still day; its bein hashemashot is a safek like Yom Kippur's. Seudah hamafseket: no meat, no wine — but : wine from the press three days old or less is permitted, and salted meat three days old or more is permitted. Why these strange thresholds? Because it is not a din of mourning — it is zecher l'Mikdash. Two proofs it isn't mourning: (a) mourners are permitted meat during shiva; (b) the (later) minhag extends the meat/wine restriction into the 10th of Av — which fits remembrance of the Temple, not aveilut. The logic: sacrificial meat could be eaten at most שני ימים ולילה אחד (two days and one night — e.g. Shelamim, Kodashim Kalim, as we say in Shacharit) — so meat three days old is no longer \"sacrifice-able\"; and wine less than three days from the press is still tosses , unfit for libation. What we deny ourselves is specifically what could be offered — remembering the korbanot and nesachim we have lost. (By this din even chicken would be permitted at the seudah hamafseket — but the minhag today is no meat, chicken or wine at all.) One cooked dish, not two — unless it is by nature a single dish: the Syrian minhag is mejadra (rice and lentils) for the seudah hamafseket — two cooked components, but eaten together, one name, one dish. All this applies only when the mafseket is eaten after chatzot on a weekday and is genuinely the final meal. Earlier in the day (Wednesday morning this year) — fish, meat, wine, as much as you want, mi-dina. And Erev Tisha B'Av on Shabbat : eat all you need, אפילו כסעודת שלמה בשעתו — even at seudah shlishit. Halacha 9 — the Rambam's personal reflection (not a din) זו היא מדת כל העם שאינן יכולין לסבול יתר מדאי. אבל חסידים הראשונים כך היתה מדתן: ערב תשעה באב היו מביאין לו לאדם לבדו פת חרבה במלח ושורה במים, ויושב בין תנור וכירים, ואוכלה ושותה עליה קיתון של מים — בדאגה ובשממון ובבכיה, כמי שמתו מוטל לפניו. וכזה ראוי לחכמים לעשות, או קרוב מזה. ומימינו לא אכלנו ערב תשעה באב תבשיל, אפילו תבשיל של עדשים, אלא אם כן היה בשבת Everything above is the din all must keep — this is midat kol ha'am , who cannot bear more. But the chasidim harishonim : on Erev Tisha B'Av a person would eat alone — dry bread with salt, soaked in water to make it edible — sitting between the oven and the stove (the dirty corner of the kitchen), with a small jug of water, in worry, desolation and weeping, like one whose dead lies before him . \"And thus it is fitting for chachamim to do, or close to it. And in all our days we never ate a cooked dish on Erev Tisha B'Av — even a dish of lentils — unless it fell on Shabbat. \" (The Rabbi's note: he had always assumed \"we never ate a cooked dish on Erev Tisha B'Av\" means at the seudah hamafseket — but re-reading it, the Rambam may mean the entire Erev Tisha B'Av. Worth checking what Rav Kappach says.) Practical: this year the mafseket is Wednesday afternoon — keep it genuinely simple: bread, one plain dish or none; fruit and vegetables are fine (not \"cooked dishes\"). Halachot 10–11 — Who Fasts; the Afflictions; Work Pregnant and nursing women Exempt from the other fasts — but must fast on Tisha B'Av . Historical note: Rav Sadka Hussein — sent (with many chachamim) from Aleppo, the home of Torah, to Baghdad after the 1743 cholera killed many of Baghdad's rabbis — instituted there that pregnant women should not fast, against the Gemara, the Rambam and the Shulchan Aruch: the heat was unbearable and women were miscarrying. The halacha remains that a pregnant woman does fast — the default is to begin the fast; if she genuinely cannot continue (very relevant if this highly unusual English 30° heat persists next week, in homes without air conditioning), she breaks it. The afflictions No washing at all — hot or cold, even a finger in water; no anointing (oil, massages); no leather shoes; no marital relations. Work on Tisha B'Av Depends on local custom — but talmidei chachamim do not work; and even where custom permits work, אינו רואה סימן ברכה לעולם — one sees no blessing from it. Practical: if self-employed and able, don't work the entire day; if employed with limited leave already consumed by the chagim — go, but expect no benefit from that day's work. If you have the choice — refrain. Halachot 12–13 — Greeting, Learning, Tefillin Greeting תלמידי חכמים אין נותנין שלום זה לזה בתשעה באב, אלא יושבים דוים ונאנחים כאבלים. ואם נתן להם עם הארץ שלום — מחזירים לו בשפה רפה וכובד ראש The Rambam: talmidei chachamim don't greet one another; they sit grieving and sighing like mourners; if an am ha'aretz greets them, they answer faintly (so as not to appear to snub him). The Shulchan Aruch — and the universal minhag — extends this to everyone . The machloket turns on the Tosefta (3:14): אין שאלת שלום לחברים בתשעה באב — the Shulchan Aruch and other Rishonim read chaverim as \"fellows\" generally; the Rambam reads chaver in its frequent Gemara sense — a talmid chacham — so the specific prohibition is among chachamim. Learning Torah ואסור לקרות בתשעה באב בתורה או בנביאים או בכתובים ובמשנה ובהלכות ובגמרא ובהגדות... וקורא הוא באיוב ובקינות ובדברים הרעים שבירמיהו. ותינוקות של בית רבן בטלין בו Torah gladdens the heart — forbidden across the board; permitted: Iyov , Kinot , and the harsh passages of Yirmiyahu . Schoolchildren are idle that day. Tefillin — three positions ומקצת החכמים נוהגין שלא להניח בו תפילין של ראש Background: the Gemara rules an aveil on the first day (the day of death and burial) does not don tefillin, from Yechezkel: פארך חבוש עליך — your pe'er (splendour), bound upon you — removed from the mourner. Maharam of Rothenburg (to the Rabbi's knowledge among the first): on Tisha B'Av no tefillin at all , no beracha — the day is like מתו מוטל לפניו . The Rosh: you must don tefillin — with a powerful kal vachomer: an aveil is forbidden work all seven days yet dons tefillin from day two; on Tisha B'Av abstaining from work is mere custom — all the more so tefillin! The Rambam records a middle minhag: some chachamim omit only the shel rosh — since the verse says pe'er on the head — donning the shel yad with the beracha להניח תפילין. (The commentaries note the Rambam himself wore full tefillin; the Rabbi knows of no one who keeps the shel-rosh-only practice today.) Today (Shulchan Aruch, and the general Sefardi and Ashkenazi minhag): tallit and tefillin are worn at Mincha rather than Shacharit — a compromise across the shitot. Halachot 14–19 — The Post-Churban Decrees; the End of the Fasts Quickly through the final halachot — the takanot instituted after the churban: Unfinished corner: when building or refurbishing a house, leave a portion incomplete. Jewelry: a woman never wears her complete set of jewelry at once. The chuppah: ash is placed on the groom's head (Gemara Gittin; so rule the Rambam and Shulchan Aruch) — many Sefardim do exactly this today (the Rabbi did at his own wedding); Ashkenazim transmuted it into breaking a glass . Music: וכן גזרו שלא לנגן בכלי שיר — not to play, nor even to hear , musical instruments — the entire year , except at a wedding/seudat mitzvah. Not an Omer or Three-Weeks rule — all year, mi-dina of the Gemara, Rambam and Shulchan Aruch. Why the world is lenient today is a genuinely difficult question; one could argue recorded music (phone, CD) differs from live instruments — but sitting at a live orchestral concert is, by the din, assur. Food for thought. Kriah on seeing Yerushalayim: one tears the outer garment — still practised today. Halacha 19 — the destiny of these days כל הצומות האלו עתידים ליבטל לימות המשיח. ולא עוד אלא שהם עתידים להיות ימים טובים וימי ששון ושמחה, שנאמר: כה אמר ה' צבאות — צום הרביעי וצום החמישי וצום השביעי וצום העשירי יהיה לבית יהודה לששון ולשמחה ולמועדים טובים, והאמת והשלום אהבו All these fasts are destined to be annulled in the days of Mashiach — and more: to become festivals, days of joy and gladness (Zechariah). (Historical footnote: this is why, when Shabtai Tzvi proclaimed himself Mashiach, his followers held banquets on Tisha B'Av and said Ya'aleh V'Yavo in Birkat Hamazon — \"Mashiach has come; the fast is a festival.\" May the genuine article come — perhaps even this year, b'ezrat Hashem, Amen.) And with that, Hilchot Ta'aniyot is complete. Summary The yesod of Perek 5: the fixed fasts of all Israel exist לעורר הלבבות ולפתוח דרכי התשובה — not to commemorate tragedy. From the Yerushalmi's כאילו הוא החריבו (active — we destroyed it), the Rambam learns the fasts target the continuation of our ancestors' ways — hence his choice of the verse confessing עונם ועון אבותם : break the cycle. The four fasts (explicit in Zechariah): 3 Tishrei (Gedalya — the extinguished ember; exile sealed), 10 Tevet (the siege — the origins), 17 Tammuz (five events; the Rambam reads העמיד — Apostomus set up the tzelem), 9 Av (five events; Bet Tor/Beitar and Bar Kochva whom even the gedolei chachamim took for Mashiach; Turnusrufus ploughing Zion). The Rambam reads the Gemara's \"shalom\" as \"the Temple standing\" — so today all four are obligatory, not custom. Ta'anit Esther: a Geonic-era minhag commemorating the fasting of Esther's days — hence lenient, and moved earlier (to Thursday) when 13 Adar falls on Shabbat. The period: no \"Three Weeks\" in the Rambam; ממעטין בשמחה from Rosh Chodesh = reduced business, luxury building, and weddings (per 3:8). The real Gemara restrictions are the week of Tisha B'Av : no haircuts/shaving, no laundering, no fresh clothes (prepare by pre-wearing) — and everything permitted immediately after the fast. Seudah hamafseket: no meat/wine as zecher l'Mikdash (hence the three-day thresholds — what is unfit for the mizbe'ach is permitted); one cooked dish (mejadra counts as one); the chasidim harishonim's dry bread between the oven and stove, כמי שמתו מוטל לפניו . Tisha B'Av: night like day; the five innuyim; pregnant women fast (begin, at least); work sees no blessing; talmidei chachamim don't greet (Rambam) — everyone (Shulchan Aruch); only Iyov, Kinot and the harsh Yirmiyahu; tefillin at Mincha today (Maharam vs Rosh; the Rambam's shel-rosh minhag). The end of the story: all these fasts will become yamim tovim — ימי ששון ושמחה. Coming Up No shiur next Thursday — Tisha B'Av itself. The final shiurim before the summer break are Monday (HaMaspik — completing the levels of bitachon and the chapter summary) and Tuesday (Moreh — Chapters 14–16), and then we break until Elul. B'Ezrat Hashem","9Dunl0ztkSE":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam; Hebrew text: Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern translation Section: I, Chapters 11, 12, 13 Topic: Yashav (sitting), Kimah (rising) and Amidah (standing) — stripping corporeality from the Torah's language about Hashem Schedule note: Penultimate Moreh shiur before the break — three chapters this week and three next week (Ch. 14–16), moving slightly faster than usual but still in depth. Recap — Section I So Far Chapters Term(s) 1–2 Tzelem Elokim; the sin of Adam 3 Tavnit and Temunah 4 Ra'ah, Hibbit, Chazah — three verbs of seeing 5 Caution when ascending to the Divine 6–7 Ish and Isha; Yalad — giving birth, for man and woman 8 Makom 9 Kisse — chair, throne 10 Alah and Yarad — literal ascent/descent; higher or lower status ; or directing one's attention (up = more important, down = less). Applied to God: \"descent\" = resting His Shechinah / imparting prophecy; \"ascent\" = prophecy ending, or punishing humans This evening: Yashav (Ch. 11), Kimah (Ch. 12), Amad (Ch. 13). Chapter 11 — Yashav (Sitting) 11.1 Two meanings Meaning 1 — physical sitting (the original usage): מונח זה נקבע בתחילה בלשוננו לישיבה פיזית — the term was first fixed in our language for physical sitting As with Eli HaKohen sitting in the Temple when Chana came. Meaning 2 — borrowed: any stable, permanent, unchanging state: וכיון שהאדם היושב הוא קבוע ויציב... הושאל המונח לכל מצב יציב וקבוע שאינו משתנה Since a sitting person is settled and stable in the most complete way, the term was borrowed for any permanent, stable, continuous situation. This is why yashav also means to dwell — וישב יעקב בארץ מגורי אביו בארץ כנען — sitting and dwelling are two different acts, but the same idea of permanence and stability. So Zechariah promises Jerusalem וישבה תחתיה — it will be settled, permanent; and in Hallel: מושיבי עקרת הבית אם הבנים שמחה — Hashem \"seats\" the barren woman, i.e. מעניק לה קביעות ויציבות — establishes and settles her as a joyful mother of children. 11.2 Applied to Hashem: permanence amid turmoil אתה ה' לעולם תשב כסאך לדור ודור (Eicha — which we read in a couple of weeks) Despite all the tragedy, turmoil and chaos of the churban, You Hashem sit forever — You are permanent, continuous, stable; Your throne ( kisse — see Ch. 9) endures generation to generation. Likewise: אליך נשאתי את עיני היושבי בשמים — I am going through difficulty, but I lift my eyes to the One who \"sits in heaven\" — the continuous, stable One. יושב בשמים ישחק, אדני ילעג למו — the resha'im scheme, but the One who sits in heaven laughs — the Permanent One sees through their plans, which will not come to fruition. 11.3 What \"He does not change\" means — three kinds of no-change הקיים לעד והיציב שלא משתנה באף פנים מצד שינוי No intrinsic change (שינוי עצמות): a person matures, becomes more merciful, kinder — none of this applies to Hashem. Even when we ask Hashem to \"move from the throne of din to the throne of rachamim,\" chas v'shalom to think we are asking God to change ; we are asking that His current manifestation toward us be one of rachamim and chesed rather than din. (The Rambam will discuss this at length.) No extrinsic change (אין לו מצב מחוץ עצמותו שישתנה בו): I step into the sun, I become hot and sweat — change caused by external factors, a mikreh (recall this concept from our Se'adyah learning: accident/occurrence). No external happening changes Hashem. No relational change (וגם יחסו לזולתו לא משתנה): someone smiles at you, you become happy; someone growls, you become upset. כיוון שאין יחס בינו לבין זולתו שישתנה ביחסו — verses saying Hashem \"becomes happy\" or \"sad\" clearly do not mean change. ובזאת נשלם — in this sense God is perfect : היותו לא משתנה כלל בשום אופן , as Malachi says: כי אני ה' לא שניתי — ואתם בני ישראל לא כליתם \"I Hashem have not changed — and you, Bnei Yisrael, will not perish.\" Just as He is continuous, so the nation is continuous: individuals go through turmoil, but as a nation — beli shinuy klal . במשמעות זו נאמרה ישיבה כאשר היא תיזכר לגביו יתעלה — whenever yeshiva is said of Hashem, it means this: continuity, stability. The purpose, as throughout these chapters, is removing corporeality. 11.4 Why specifically \"sitting in heaven\"? וייחס לשמים דווקא לרוב מקומות... משום שהשמים הם אלה שאין בהם שינוי ולא שוני — כלומר שפרטיהם אינם משתנים כמו שמשתנים פרטי הדברים המתהווים והנפסדים בארץ The Rambam follows the Aristotelian picture: the heavens and celestial bodies are made of different, permanent material — enduring and unchanging — unlike things on earth, which are open to generation and destruction. On earth, Aristotle held, the species endure (the human species continues), while the individuals within them are born and die. The Nevi'im therefore depict Hashem \"sitting in heaven\" to convey His permanence, in contradistinction to the changing world below. Rabbi Twersky's note: today we know this cosmology isn't true — the heavens change just as earthly things do. But that isn't the point: to the naked eye the celestial bodies appear the most enduring things we have, and in that sense the verse anchors God's permanence to them. 11.5 \"Sitting\" even relative to the earth — the species, not the individuals וכן כאשר יוחס יתעלה... למיני הנמצאות המתהווים והנפסדים — נאמר עליו גם כן יושב, כי מציאות המינים הללו מתמדת, סדורה וקבועה — כמציאות הפרטים בשמים Hashem can even be compared (as far as a shared name allows) to the species of earthly things, whose existence is permanent and ordered like the individual bodies of heaven. Hence Yeshaya: היושב על חוג הארץ — \"He who sits upon the circuit of the earth\" — הקיים לעד על טיב כיפת הארץ... כלומר סיבובה, ברמיזה לדברים המתהווים באופן מחזורי Not the earth's physical/gravitational rotation (Pines translates \"rotation,\" but they didn't know the earth rotates) — rather the continuous cycle of generation, regeneration and death. Individuals are born and perish; the cycle, and the species, endure — and God \"sits\" over that enduring circuit. Participant question (Mark): but species do go extinct — so they're not enduring? Answer: (a) This is Aristotle's conceptual framework, which the Rambam is working within; the concept of relative endurance is the point, not its scientific accuracy today (we now know there's no material difference between celestial bodies and earthly things at all). (b) Looking more broadly, even where a specific kind became extinct, the larger family continues — the point is the perpetual circuit of things coming into the world. 11.6 ה' למבול ישב — the Friday-night verse ה' למבול ישב וישב ה' מלך לעולם What can \"Hashem sat at the flood\" mean? כלומר שגם כאשר מצבי הארץ משתנים ונהרסים — אצלו יתעלה היחס שווה — even amid the flood's total destruction, Hashem endures; and moreover, יחסו לכלל הדבר המתהווה וכלה הוא יחס אחד יציב וקבוע — כיחסו למיני הדברים המתהווים ולא לפרטיהם — His relationship is with the categories of created things, and that relationship is stable even while individuals perish. The created order and its governance endure through the chaos. בכלל: כל לשון ישיבה שתמצא מיוחס להשם — read it this way everywhere (including, implicitly, the \"sitting on the throne\" of Yechezkel's vision — throne in Ch. 9, sitting here; the picture is assembling). 11.7 Rav Kook's beautiful application — Acher and Metatron (Chagiga) Angels stand — this is behind the minhag (brought in Shulchan Aruch: יש שנוהגים כל היום והלילה ) to stand on Yom Kippur, resembling the angels. Why do angels stand and never sit? By this chapter it makes perfect sense: only Hashem \"sits\" — only He is true permanence; even angels, in the domain of God, are transient and unstable relative to Him. Now the famous Gemara — four entered the pardes (the orchard = intellectual apprehension of God), and Acher (Elisha ben Avuya) קיצץ בנטיעות — cut the plants, speculated incorrectly. What exactly did he do wrong? חזא מיטטרון דאיתיהיבא ליה רשותא למיתב, למכתב זכוותא דישראל. אמר: גמירא דלמעלה לא הוי לא ישיבה ולא תחרות... שמא חס ושלום שתי רשויות הן He saw Metatron — who had been given permission to sit — sitting and writing the merits of Israel, and said: we have a tradition there is no sitting above — perhaps there are two authorities! And he became a heretic. On our chapter this is exquisite: Acher saw in an angel's sitting a claim of permanence — and permanence belongs to God alone; hence \"two reshuyot.\" And note what Metatron was writing: the merits of Am Yisrael. Recall the Malachi verse the Rambam quoted — אני ה' לא שניתי ואתם בני ישראל לא כליתם . The greatest permanence in existence aside from God is the continuity, the immutability, of Am Yisrael. Perhaps that is the pshat in why Metatron sat for this task : the sitting denotes the permanence of the nation whose merits he records. Acher could not absorb that — and he cut the plants. Aside: this is also why we speak of יושב בדין — a judge sits in judgment: judgment requires the calm, settled steadiness that sitting embodies, not merely the physical posture. 11.8 Chapter 11 summary Yashav has two meanings: literal sitting, and figurative stability/permanence/continuity. With Hakadosh Baruch Hu it always means the second: no intrinsic, extrinsic or relational change. Hence \"sitting in heaven\" (the heavens symbolise permanence against the changing world below) and \"sitting at the flood\" (individuals perish; His governance and the order of creation endure). Purpose: removing any corporeal \"sitting\" from God. Chapter 12 — Kimah (Rising) 12.1 Four meanings קימה מונח רב־משמעי Meaning 1 — physical rising , the opposite of sitting ( שכנגד הישיבה ): וכל עבדי המלך... לא קם ולא זע ממנו, וימלא המן על מרדכי חמה Mordechai neither rose ( lo kam ) nor even stirred ( lo za ) — not even the small hidur , the slight lift of recognition when someone passes; certainly not full standing. Meaning 2 — establishment / fulfilment of a matter (קיום הדבר ונכונותו): יקם ה' את דברו — Elkanah to Chana: Hashem must fulfil His word. (Context: Chana had been blessed with Shmuel, who by Hashem's word through Eli was to be dedicated to the Mikdash. Elkanah set out for aliyah la'regel; Chana said she must stay until the child is weaned; Elkanah agreed — stay, \"only may Hashem fulfil His word.\") ויקם שדה עפרון... לאברהם למקנה — the field \"arose\" to Avraham: no standing up — it became legally established as his. וקם הבית אשר בעיר... לצמיתת לקנה אתו לדרתיו (Behar) — a house in a walled city not repurchased within the year becomes permanently, legally established to the buyer. וקם... ממלכת ישראל — the kingdom will be established . Meaning 3 — resolving to act; rising up against, rebelling: וכיוון שהמחליט לעשות דבר כלשהו יוצא אל מעשה ידי קימה... נאמר על כל מי שמתקומם כנגד אדם שהוא קם One who resolves to act gets up to do it; hence \"rising against\" someone: כי הקים בני את עבדי עלי — Shaul: my son (Yonatan) has \"raised\" David against me — not stood him up, not established him: made him rebel , take action against me. Meaning 4 — the extension of 3, borrowed for God: executing decreed punishment: המשמעות הזו הושאלה לביצוע צו השם באנשים שנגזר עליהם עונש לעונשן וקמתי על בית ירבעם — I will \"rise\" against the house of Yerovam = I will take action, punish. וקם על בית מרעים — He will rise against the house of evildoers. (The Rabbi suggested you can equally frame these as 3a and 3b: rising-up-against in the human context, and in the God context = rising to execute judgment.) 12.2 With Hashem: always meaning 2 or 4 — never physical במשמעות הזו נאמר כל לשון קימה על השם יתעלה עתה אקום יאמר ה' — \"Now I will arise\" = אקיים בעת... את דבריי והבטחותיי ואיומיי — now I fulfil what I have said: promises, or warnings and punishments. אתה תקום תרחם ציון כי עת לחננה — You will \"arise\" and have mercy on Zion = תקיים מה שהבטחת להתרחם עליה — You will fulfil Your promise of mercy. And the Rambam adds: וייתכן שדבריו אלה שהזכיר \"עתה אקום\" — במשמעות זו — perhaps those very verses can instead be read by meaning 4: now I will arise — עתה ארומם עתה אנשא — now I punish; and אתה תקום תרחם ציון — You will rise against her enemies and take them down. Either way — 2 or 4; never 1: לא שיש לו יתעלה קימה וישיבה... אמרו עליהם השלום: אין למעלה לא ישיבה ולא עמידה There is no sitting or standing Above (the Gemara; and amidah is sometimes said in place of kum — the terms interchange). 12.3 Shem Tov: why is punishment called \"rising\"? Shem Tov (15th-century Spanish commentary on the Moreh): the natural order of the world is good — recall the previous idea: sitting is the stable, natural state; standing is the change. When Hashem \"stands,\" He is — so to speak — breaking the stable natural goodness of the world; hence rising = the departure from the norm that punishment represents. 12.4 The Rabbi's question: why didn't the Rambam bring קומה ה'? Is there not a perfect Torah verse for meaning 4 — Hashem arising to execute judgment on the wicked? From Beha'alotcha, said by Ashkenazim when opening the Aron: ויהי בנסע הארן ויאמר משה: קומה ה' ויפצו איביך וינסו משנאיך מפניך Ibn Ezra, immediately bothered by it, comments: כדרך בני אדם המדברים — metaphoric: as humans rise to fight, \"rise, Hashem, and fight our enemies.\" So why did the Rambam not bring this superb source? Suggested answer: perhaps the Rambam understood קומה ה' there as referring to the cloud — the physical Ananei HaKavod that literally rose and moved when the camp set out to travel and fight. If the verse describes a genuinely physical ascent (of the cloud), it cannot serve as proof for the non-physical meaning — so it is unhelpful for his purpose, and he leaves it out. 12.5 Aside: the order of the Rambam's proof-verses Note the Rambam jumps from Shmuel to Bereishit to Vayikra — seemingly out of order. As we have seen over the years (and as the Abarbanel and the commentaries discuss at length on this chapter), there is always a deliberate order in the Moreh's citations; it is beyond the scope of the shiur, but be aware nothing here is haphazard. 12.6 Chapter 12 summary Kam has four meanings: (1) physically rising; (2) establishing/fulfilling (legal title, promises); (3) rising up against someone (humans); (4) — the borrowed extension to God — rising to execute decreed judgment. With Hashem it is always 2 or 4, never physical: continuing the rejection of corporeality. Chapter 13 — Amidah (Standing) 13.1 Three meanings — and a deep fourth application עמידה מונח רב־משמעי Meaning 1 — physical standing (קימה ועמידה): ויוסף בן שלשים שנה בעמדו לפני פרעה — Yosef, from prison, now stands before Pharaoh. אם יעמד משה ושמואל לפני — Hashem to Yirmiyahu: even if Moshe and Shmuel stood (from their graves) in prayer before Me, I would not forgive. והוא עמד עליהם תחת העץ ויאכלו (Vayera) — Avraham standing over the three angels as they eat. Why is Vayera brought last, out of order? Because for the Rambam the entire episode of the angels occurred in a vision — no one ever physically stood over angels on a piece of earth. It is therefore the weakest proof of physical standing, and he deliberately places it last. (A good example of the deliberate ordering we noted in Ch. 12 — though we won't chase this every time.) Meaning 2 — cessation, stopping (הימנעות והפסקה): Why? Because when you stop walking — you stand. כי עמדו לא ענו עוד (Iyov) — Elihu began speaking only when the friends had stopped answering. ותעמד מלדת (Vayetzei) — Leah, after Yehuda, stopped giving birth. Meaning 3 — permanence, endurance, posterity (קביעות וקיום): ונתתם בכלי חרש למען יעמדו ימים רבים (Yirmiyahu) — place the deeds in an earthenware vessel so they will last many days. אם את הדבר הזה תעשה... ויכלת עמד (Yitro) — delegate to ministers of thousands, hundreds, fifties and tens, \"and you will be able to stand \" — not physically; you will be able to continue , sustainably, without burning out. עמד טעמו בו — Moav's \"taste has stood\": it has lasted , undisturbed, until now (and now Hashem will change that). צדקתו עמדת לעד — the tzaddik's righteousness stands forever — קבועה וקיימת , enduring for posterity. 13.2 With Hashem: always meaning 3 כל עמידה שנאמרה לגבי השם יתעלה — המשמעות הזאת אחרונה Never 1 (no physicality), never 2 (no stopping) — always permanence and continuity. Three verses to analyse: (a) Zechariah — ועמדו רגליו ביום ההוא על הר הזיתים — \"His legs will stand that day on Har HaZeitim.\" ותהיינה סיבותיו קבועות — His consequences will be fixed and enduring. The Rambam will explain in I:28 that regel , certainly in the context of God, means consequence and impact — your legs extend out from you; they are your reach into the world. So the verse means: in the messianic age, God's impacts — the recognition of His presence and providence — will be constant, enduring, visible day in and day out. דבר זה עוד יתבאר (Ch. 28). (b) ואתה פה עמד עמדי (Va'etchanan) — Hashem to Moshe: \"You, stand here with Me.\" Probably 99% of us have always read this as physical standing — and what would even be wrong with Moshe standing? The problem: if it means literal standing with Me , it attributes spatial context to Hakadosh Baruch Hu — a \"place\" beside God to stand in. Therefore the Rambam reads meaning 3: you will be continuously with Me — a permanent, uninterrupted prophetic bond. This is exactly the Rambam in Mishneh Torah on the uniqueness of Moshe's prophecy (one of the distinctions we discussed last year): כל הנביאים אין מתנבאים בכל עת שירצו — משה רבינו אינו כן, אלא בכל זמן שיחפוץ רוח הקודש לובשתו... שהרי הוא מכוון ומזומן ועומד... ובזה הבטיחו האל, שנאמר: לך אמר להם שובו לכם לאהליכם — ואתה פה עמד עמדי All other prophets prophesy only when Hashem wills; Moshe — whenever he wished, because he is perpetually prepared, directed, standing ready . At Har Sinai the others were sent home — שובו לכם לאהליכם — but Moshe: stand here with Me — remain in continuous prophetic dialogue. הא למדת שכל הנביאים... חוזרים לאהליהם — ומשה רבינו לא חזר לאהלו הראשון, לפיכך פירש מן האשה לעולם — which is why Moshe separated from his wife permanently: the dialogue never stopped. (c) אנכי עמד בין ה' וביניכם בעת ההוא להגיד לכם את דבר ה' — Moshe: \"I stood between Hashem and you at that time.\" Again, physical standing \"between\" would attribute spatial context to God. Meaning 3: I am the continuous conduit — the permanent intermediary passing on the word of Hashem. 13.3 Makbili's deeper point: Moshe as the permanent conduit between God and humanity Rabbi Makbili suggests the Rambam is teaching something deeper in verse (c) — Moshe is the standing , permanent channel between God and humanity, with three implications: The world cannot function without Torat Moshe — the continuous conduit is constitutive, not historical. All prophets receive their koach ha'nevuah through Moshe — as the Rambam writes elsewhere. Even a non-Jew's Torah is Torat Moshe. The famous (and famously hard) Rambam: the Gemara says a non-Jew earns a portion in the World to Come by keeping the seven Noahide laws — on which basis most of humanity would qualify. But the Rambam adds, of his own accord ( svara d'nafshei , as the Beit Yosef notes): כל המקבל שבע מצות ונזהר לעשותן הרי זה מחסידי אומות העולם ויש לו חלק לעולם הבא — והוא שיקבל אותן ויעשה אותן מפני שצוה בהן הקדוש ברוך הוא בתורה, והודיענו על ידי משה רבינו שבני נח מקודם נצטוו בהן. אבל אם עשאן מפני הכרע הדעת — אין זה גר תושב ואינו מחסידי אומות העולם, אלא מחכמיהם Only one who keeps the sheva mitzvot because Hashem commanded them in the Torah through Moshe is of the chasidei umot ha'olam with a chelek in Olam Haba; one who keeps them from reason alone is of their chachamim — wise, but not pious. Where the Rambam derived this is a vast discussion — but on Makbili's reading it flows from our verse: all morality and connection to God passes through the standing conduit — אנכי עמד בין ה' וביניכם . 13.4 Chapter 13 summary Amad means (1) physical standing, (2) stopping, or (3) permanence/endurance. In relation to Hashem it always signifies (3): His enduring actions and consequences (Zechariah — the messianic age); and of Moshe \"standing\" with/before God — the constant, never-interrupted prophetic relationship and his permanent role as intermediary of Am Yisrael. Summary of the Evening Ch. 11 — Yashav: to sit; borrowed for stability, permanence, continuity. With God — always the second: no intrinsic, extrinsic or relational change ( אני ה' לא שניתי ). \"Sitting in heaven\" borrows the (Aristotelian) permanence of the celestial realm; \"sitting on the circuit of the earth\" and \"at the flood\" — His enduring relationship with the species and order of creation even as individuals perish. Rav Kook's read of Acher and the seated Metatron: sitting = permanence, which belongs to God (and, through the merits Metatron records, to the immutable Am Yisrael) — misread by Acher as a second authority. Ch. 12 — Kam: (1) rising (Mordechai); (2) establishing/fulfilling (Efron's field; the walled city; יקם ה' את דברו); (3) rising against (Yonatan \"raising\" David); (4) with God — executing decreed judgment (בית ירבעם). With Hashem: always 2 or 4. Shem Tov: punishment is \"standing\" because it departs from the world's stable natural goodness. Why no קומה ה' ויפצו אויביך? Likely because there it is the cloud physically rising — useless as proof. Ch. 13 — Amad: (1) standing (Yosef; the vision of Vayera deliberately last as the weakest proof); (2) stopping (Elihu; Leah); (3) enduring (Yitro's ויכלת עמד; צדקתו עומדת לעד). With God — always 3: His consequences ( raglav = impacts, see I:28) endure in the messianic age; ואתה פה עמד עמדי — Moshe's continuous, ever-ready prophecy (hence separating from his wife); אנכי עמד בין ה' וביניכם — Moshe the permanent conduit, through whom all prophecy, all Torah, and (per the famous Rambam on chasidei umot ha'olam) even the Noahide path must pass. Coming Up Next week — the final Moreh shiur before the break: three more chapters. Chapter 14 — Adam (huge chapter, extremely short — the short ones are the big ones): what does Adam mean, now that we know Ish and Isha ? Plus Nitzav (standing — yet another kind) and Tzur (rock). Then we break. Summer avodah from the Rabbi: over the summer, when reading pesukim — on Shabbat, when davening alone, when there's more time to think — look out for the terms we've learned these past months, and apply the Rambam's meanings in context. B'Ezrat Hashem","lGHkx0k0YHw":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: 1 · Chapters 10 & 11 Topic: The Terms ירד & עלה (Descending & Ascending) and ישיבה (Sitting) in Torah Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Where We Are Chapters Terms Treated 1–4 Tzelem/Demut · Temunah/Tavnit · the verbs of seeing 5 Caution approaching the divine (the burning bush; the nobles) 6–7 Ish/Isha · Yalad (Ma'aseh Bereshit) 8–9 Makom (place) · Kisei (throne) 10–11 (tonight) Yarad/Alah (down & up) · Yeshiva (sitting) The project continues: terms that seem to ascribe physicality or position to Hashem — reread correctly. PART 1 — Chapter 10: עלה and ירד (Ascending and Descending) Rambam's Opening Note — Why These Terms Are Here כבר הקדמנו שאנחנו מזכירים בפרקי הספר הזה כל מונח שיש בו שיתוף — we treat only multi-meaning (homonymous) terms that risk being misunderstood about God. (He is not writing a dictionary — only defusing the dangerous words.) The Two Literal Meanings Term Literal Meaning ירד (yarad) Moving from a higher place to a lower place — physical descent עלה (alah) Moving from a lower place to a higher place — physical ascent The Borrowed Meaning — Status and Rank Just as with makom (Ch. 8), the terms were borrowed for greatness and status : הושאלו שני מונחים אלו לגדולה ולכבוד When a person's standing declines — we say he \"descended\" (ירידה); when his standing rises — he \"ascended\" (עלייה). The Torah's Examples Verse Usage הגר בקרבך יעלה עליך מעלה מעלה, ואתה תרד מטה מטה (the Tochacha) The stranger will rise higher and higher over you, and you will descend lower and lower — status , not stairs ירוד ירדנו (Yosef's brothers) \"We have surely declined\" Modern Hebrew Preserves This עולה לארץ / עלייה — one who moves to Eretz Yisrael \"ascends\" (a rise in spiritual status) יורד — one who leaves Israel \"descends\" עלייה לתורה — being called up to the Torah (an honor — status) (Even in English: \"moving up in the world,\" \"a step down.\") Applied to Hashem — Three Distinct Usages Usage 1 — Prophecy: The Prophet \"Ascends,\" the Shefa \"Descends\" When the term relates to prophecy and apprehension : ירידה — the descent of the divine overflow (shefa) onto the prophet עלייה — the prophet's ascent of the mind toward apprehension Examples: וירד ה' על הר סיני — \"Hashem descended on Har Sinai\" = the prophetic revelation descended; not God moving down עלה אלי ההרה — \"ascend to Me on the mountain\" = rise in apprehension (though Moshe also physically climbed — remember the rule: the literal stays unless there's reason to remove it ; the physical climb is real, and the deeper meaning rides on it) וירד ה' לראות את העיר ואת המגדל (Bavel) — Hashem \"descended to see\" the city and tower: His knowledge engaged the lowly deeds of men , not spatial motion Usage 2 — Punishment: \"Descent\" Before Striking the Low When Hashem is described as \"descending\" toward punishing a people: ירידה here = the turning of the divine attention downward toward those who have made themselves low through their deeds (Bavel; Sedom — ארדה נא ואראה \"I will descend and see\"). (And the deeper lesson of ארדה נא ואראה — see below: judges must not rule on hearsay.) Usage 3 — The Shechinah's \"Descent\" to a Place ירידת השכינה to a mountain or the Mishkan — the settling of the divine presence/glory upon a location: a manifestation , not movement of God (who has no body and no place — Ch. 8). The Beautiful Derash — \"I Will Descend and See\" (Sedom) ארדה נא ואראה: הכצעקתה הבאה אלי עשו — כלה; ואם לא — אדעה Does Hashem need to \"go down and check\"?! Obviously not — He knows all. The Midrash (quoted by Rashi): לימד לדיינים שלא יפסקו דיני נפשות אלא בראייה He taught judges never to rule in capital matters except by direct examination — not by report, not by rumor. If the Omniscient One \"descends to see\" before judging Sedom — a human judge certainly may not condemn on hearsay. (A model of Hashem performing pedagogy through the Torah's very phrasing.) PART 2 — Chapter 11: ישיבה (Sitting) The Literal Meaning תחילת קביעתו של המונח הזה בלשוננו היא ישיבת האדם על המקום Sitting — a person settling on a place. The Borrowed Meaning — Stability, Permanence, Unchangingness הושאל מונח זה לכל מצב יציב קבוע שאינו משתנה Borrowed for any stable, fixed, unchanging state. Why? Think about what sitting is : standing is effortful and temporary; sitting is settled. A person who sits has come to rest — stability. The Example — Yerushalayim ורבתי עם... ישבה בדד (Eicha — appropriate as we approach Tisha B'Av) And the promise: וירושלים עוד תשב על מקומה — \"Yerushalayim will yet sit in her place\" = will endure, established and unchanging. Applied to Hashem — The Unchanging One אתה ה' לעולם תשב (Eicha 5) — \"You, Hashem, sit forever\" ישב בשמים (Tehillim) — \"He who sits in the heavens\" \"Sitting\" applied to Hashem = His absolute permanence and unchangeability — לא מצד עצמו ולא מצד יחסו לזולתו: He does not change in Himself — no aging, no development, no mood-shifts He does not change in relation to anything else — His relationship to the cosmos is constant Combined with kisei (Ch. 9): \"Hashem sits on the throne\" = the Unchanging One in His majesty — not a seated figure on furniture. The Rambam's Closing Cross-Reference The full meaning of \"sitting\" as applied to God will deepen later (when treating God's knowledge and unity). For now: whenever Tanakh says Hashem \"sits\" — read permanence, stability, immutability. The Verse That Ties It All Together ישב בשמים ישחק (Tehillim 2) — \"He who sits in the heavens laughs\" The nations rage and conspire ( למה רגשו גוים ) — and He who sits — the Stable, Unchanging One, unthreatened and unmoved — \"laughs.\" Their plots change nothing in Him. The metaphor of sitting carries the entire theology: God's unshakeable constancy over against human turbulence. Tonight's Summary Chapter 10 — Yarad & Alah Literal: physical descent/ascent → Borrowed: decline/rise in status (the Tochacha; aliyah to Israel; aliyah laTorah) Applied to God: (1) prophecy — the shefa \"descends,\" the prophet's mind \"ascends\" (Sinai); (2) judgment — attention turned to those made low by their deeds (Bavel, Sedom); (3) the Shechinah's settling on a place ארדה נא ואראה → judges must see, not rely on hearsay The literal meaning remains where nothing forces its removal (Moshe really climbed) Chapter 11 — Yeshiva Literal: sitting → Borrowed: any stable, fixed, unchanging state (ישבה בדד; עוד תשב על מקומה) Applied to God: אתה ה' לעולם תשב — He is unchanging in Himself and in His relations ישב בשמים ישחק — the Unmoved One over the raging nations Coming Up Next Week Chapter 12 ( קימה — standing/rising) and onward — continuing the lexicon of motion and posture applied to the divine. B'Ezrat Hashem","S787gnOqNf4":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 8 (ביטחון — Bitachon and Reliance) — Part 5 This Week: Miracles — Can Anything Harm the Person of True Bitachon? · The Copper Snake Reinterpreted · Never Rely on Miracles · Spiritual Mortal Danger (Shmad) · Category 2 — Minor Harms (the Machloket with Rabbeinu Yonah) · Category 3 Begun — Basic Necessities · Two Great Questions Recap Bitachon defined : Hashem oversees all the regular intermediaries He set in the world and can change them at any time — so no going overboard in pursuit of wealth/health, no laziness, no arrogant reliance on miracles Bitachon does not contradict cause and effect — science is real, and Hashem oversees it The five categories of use : (1) avoiding extremely harmful situations, (2) avoiding minor harms, (3) attaining the essential, (4) attaining the important-but-not-essential, (5) attaining luxuries — each in physical and spiritual form Prerequisite : no employing bitachon without doing Hashem's will Category 1 (begun last week) : in serious danger — trust, Tehillim (91, 18), prayer, and every natural means PART 1 — Completing Category One: Miracles The Principle — Nothing Can Harm the Person of True Bitachon ודע לך לדעת שאם גדלה צדקתו של אדם וטובים מעשיו, ובטחונו בשם יתעלה בכל ענייניו נכון ונאות... עד שזרח אורו עליו וחש בקרבתו אליו ובגודל השגחתו עליו — אז אין שליטה עליו לגורמים המזיקים If a person's righteousness is great, his conduct good, his reliance on Hashem complete — living in love of Hashem, feeling His closeness and His hashgacha constantly — then the harmful forces have no power over him. The Verses Verse Meaning כי תעבור במים אתך אני, ובנהרות לא ישטפוך (Yeshayahu) Crossing water and rivers — you will not drown כי תלך במו אש לא תכוה, ולהבה לא תבער בך Walking through fire — it will not singe or consume you על שחל ופתן תדרוך, תרמוס כפיר ותנין You will tread on lions and vipers (Caveat coming below: this is theoretical — never put yourself in danger.) The Copper Snake — A Stunning Reinterpretation (Parshat Chukat) The Standard Reading (how we've always read it) The people complain → Hashem sends the snakes → many die → they do teshuvah and beg Moshe to pray → Moshe makes the copper snake on the banner → the plague stops , and anyone already bitten looks at it and is healed. The Avi Ezer's Reading — The Snakes NEVER Stopped והוא יתעלה לא הסיר את הנחשים — והיו נושכים אותם כשם שנשכום קודם Hashem did NOT remove the snakes. They continued biting the people after the teshuvah exactly as before! Look at the verse itself: והיה אם נשך הנחש את איש — והביט אל נחש הנחושת וחי \"And it would be, if the snake BIT a man — he would look at the copper snake and LIVE.\" The verse describes an ongoing arrangement : the biting continues; only the dying stops. Why? כדי שידעו שפעולתם של נחשים שפועלים בהם היא רק עבור הסרת השגחתו מעליהם So that they would know that the snakes' power over them existed ONLY because His providence had been removed. Once trust in Hashem was restored — the same bites, from the same snakes, simply could not kill. (Rabbi Kada: In all my life I never read the passage this way — and searching the commentators, I found no one else who says this — yet it appears to be the most straightforward reading of the verses. The snakebite doesn't determine death; the will of Hashem does.) No Mystical Power in the Copper Snake ואין סיבת הדבר איזה כוח מסתורי שבנחש, כמו שחשבו כופרים וסכלים מישראל There was no mystical power in the copper snake — as heretics and fools imagined. The proof: generations later people began worshipping it — וכתת נחש הנחושת אשר עשה משה, כי עד הימים ההם היו בני ישראל מקטרים לו, ויקרא לו נחושתן Chizkiyahu smashed it (they had named it \"Nechushtan\" and were offering to it) — קיצץ נחש הנחושת והודו לו — and the Sages praised him for it. The same applies to talismans and amulets — the Rambam, and his son here following him, held there is no value whatsoever in amulets, strings, and charms. Completely false. So What WAS the Copper Snake For? — The Mishnah וכי נחש ממית או נחש מחיה? אלא: כל זמן שישראל מסתכלין כלפי מעלה ומשעבדין את לבם לאביהם שבשמים — היו מתרפאים, ואם לאו — היו נימוקים \"Does a snake kill or does a snake give life? Rather — whenever Israel looked upward and subjugated their hearts to their Father in Heaven, they were healed; if not, they wasted away.\" The copper snake was an external directing device — not an intermediary (that would be avodah zarah!) — a tool to turn their attention and trust to Hakadosh Baruch Hu. When they looked and directed their hearts to Hashem — they lived. The Gemara — Chanina ben Dosa and the Chavarbar תנו רבנן: מעשה במקום אחד שהיה בו חברבר והיה מזיק את הבריות... A venomous snake was killing people in a certain place. They told Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa . He said: \"Show me its hole.\" He placed his heel over the mouth of the hole — the snake came out, bit him — and the snake died. He slung it over his shoulder, carried it to the Beit Midrash and said: ראו בני — אין חברבר ממית, אלא החטא ממית \"See, my sons — it is not the snake that kills; it is SIN that kills.\" At that moment they said: אוי לו לאדם שפגע בו חברבר, ואוי לו לחברבר שפגע בו רבי חנינא בן דוסא \"Woe to the man who meets a chavarbar — and woe to the chavarbar that meets Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa!\" Many similar stories fill the Talmud. One who lacks understanding denies them; one of firm religiosity, straight principles, and complete apprehension will understand and believe them. The Crucial Caveat — NEVER Rely on Miracles והנסים והנפלאות הללו — אין אדם יכול להשליך חייו עליהם אם לא הורה לו עליהם [ה'] This is all theory, not practice . Even a prophet — all the more so we — is forbidden to put himself in danger without absolute necessity: Figure The Evidence Shmuel Told to anoint David: איך אלך? ושמע שאול והרגני — \"How can I go? Shaul will hear and kill me!\" Moshe (master of the prophets) ויברח משה מפני פרעה — fled Pharaoh; and וינס משה מפניו — fled from his own staff-turned-snake Eliyahu Fled for his life (וירץ... וימלט את נפשו) The Torah and Chazal ולא תשים דמים בביתך — build the parapet; do not bring bloodshed into your house Chazal prohibited many things on grounds of danger: מים מגולים (water left exposed overnight — lest a snake left venom) and the nibbled melon (halachically, most poskim today permit exposed water indoors — hygiene aside — but the principle stands: the Sages built precautions so no one relies on miracles) לעולם אל יעמוד אדם במקום סכנה ויאמר עושין לי נס — שמא אין עושין לו נס, ואם עושין לו נס — מנכין לו מזכיותיו Never stand in danger saying \"a miracle will save me\" — perhaps it won't; and if it does, it is deducted from your merits The Practical Ruling אין צורך וגם אין רשות לאדם השומר את התורה כראוי להפליג בים... או לצאת במדבריות שהם בחזקת סכנה... כדי להרבות סחורה והון One has neither need nor permission to sail dangerous seas or cross dangerous deserts merely to amass more wealth (contemporary application: traveling to a dangerous country for a business deal — not correct) . The only justification for such journeys: fleeing an enemy or oppression. Don't Be Fooled by the Survivors ולא ישלה אדם את עצמו — \"but many people take these risks and survive!\" If they are not righteous — Hashem is paying out their reward in this world (a terrible bargain) If they are righteous — this is exactly Chazal's warning: the miracle is deducted from their merits The Spiritual Parallel — Mortal Danger to One's Religion (Shmad) כשם שעלול אדם לסבול נזק בענייניו הגשמיים ולבוא לידי סכנת נפשות — כך עלול הוא לסבול נזק דתי ולבוא לידי סכנת הדת, כגון סכנת השמד Just as the body can face mortal danger, the soul can face religious mortal danger — forced conversion, persecution (which the Rambam himself lived through) . If it happens, it is a test and decree from Hashem : Trust in Him and cleave to Him with all the heart — and pray, like David: אל תט לבי לדבר רע (\"do not incline my heart to an evil thing\"), אל תשליכני מלפניך Make every effort to FLEE — like the Tannaim and men of deed who fled the Greek persecutions and hid in caves Or, where halacha requires — give up one's life al Kiddush Hashem , like Chananyah, Mishael, and Azaryah, Daniel, and the Asarah Harugei Malchut (whom we read about on Tisha B'Av) — according to the rules of יהרג ואל יעבור / יעבור ואל יהרג (as the Rambam laid out in Iggeret HaShmad, which we learned over a year ago) End of Category One — in both its physical and spiritual forms. PART 2 — Category Two: Minor Harms (הרחקת נזק בלתי חמור) The Surprising Ruling — Don't Employ Bitachon for Trivial Annoyances ועכשיו בנוגע לחלק השני — הרחקת נזק בלתי חמור, כגון פשפשים ופרעושים טורדים, או עגמת נפש בעניין פעוט... אין זה מן הראוי שיפעיל האדם את ביטחונו להרחקת הפגעים הפעוטים הללו — אלא אם כן נמנה הוא עם גדולי החסידים והנביאים Annoying insects, fleas, minor distress, someone insulting you — it is NOT appropriate to deploy bitachon for such trivial harms — unless one is among the greatest of the pious and the prophets. The Exceptions — Those Who Lived Supernaturally Figure The Story Chanina ben Dosa Caught in the rain: כל העולם כולו בנחת — וחנינא בצער? (\"the whole world is at ease and Chanina suffers?\") — and the rain stopped (a normal person may not pray against rain that benefits everyone — let alone for so trivial a bother) Elisha The youths taunted him on the way to Beit El — he prayed — ותצאנה שתים דובים... ותבקענה מהם ארבעים ושני ילדים (the bears mauled 42 of them) וכל חסיד, וכל שכן סתם אדם כשר, אם יפעילו ביטחונם באלו — הרי זה אומץ לב שאין הדעת סובלתו For any ordinary pious person to deploy bitachon this way is an audacity the mind cannot tolerate. The Logic (from Prerequisite 2, last week) Bitachon's highest use is for Hashem's will , not merely our own comfort. For essentials — fine. But to enlist trust-in-God for one's own trivial comfort? Only one whose every fiber serves Hashem — for whom even a mosquito genuinely impedes avodat Hashem — may do so. May One Still PRAY About Small Things? Yes. One may pray to Hashem about anything (the mosquito keeping you up at night). The Avi Ezer's point is narrower: do not deploy BITACHON — the confident reliance \"Hashem, I trust You WILL remove this\" — for trivial personal matters. Prayer, yes; confident reliance as a claim, no. The Machloket — Rabbeinu Yonah Says the OPPOSITE Rabbeinu Yonah on Mishlei 3:6 — בכל דרכיך דעהו (\"In all your ways know Him\"): בכל פועל שתבקש לעשות זכור את ה' יתברך וקווה אליו שיצליחך... כי יש מי שבוטח בה' יתברך בכלל אך לא ישיב עניין הביטחון אל לבו בפרטים — על כן אמר בכל דרכיך דעהו: בכל פרטי מעשיך Some trust Hashem for big things (setting sail on a business venture) but forget Him in small things (will the weather hold on my trip?) — because it seems too minor to bother. Therefore the verse says: know Him in ALL your ways — in matters great and SMALL. The Machloket Crystallized Avi Ezer (Rabbi Avraham) Rabbeinu Yonah Bitachon for minor personal matters Inappropriate — don't make God the solver of your trivial issues (unless you are of the greatest chassidim) Required — בכל דרכיך דעהו: trust Him even for the fly bothering you (One might try to resolve it — Rabbeinu Yonah speaks of prayer, not bitachon? — but examining his words inside, he speaks explicitly of bitachon. It is a genuine machloket Rishonim.) When Minor Becomes Major ושוב אפשר שהנזק הבלתי חמור הזה... ייהפך לחמור If the \"minor\" harm compounds — e.g., a sensitive, hot-tempered person whose sleep is stolen night after night by the insects until fever and illness result — it reverts to Category One (serious harm), with all its rules: full bitachon, prayer, and every natural means. Minor Harms in SPIRITUAL Matters — The Rule Reverses וכן הנזק הפעוט בענייני הדת — צריך להתחשב בו שמא יארע נזק גדול... כי רגלים לדבר שכמה מענייני הדת... מביאה עשייתם לידי תוצאות גדולות ועל הזנחתם לתוצאות גדולות For spiritual matters, even MINOR breaches demand bitachon — because small spiritual lapses compound into great consequences : הוי זהיר במצוה קלה כבמצוה חמורה, שאין אתה יודע מתן שכרן של מצוות — Be careful with a \"light\" mitzvah as with a weighty one שכר מצוה מצוה, ושכר עבירה עבירה — one mitzvah draws another; one sin draws another Therefore: חייב כל אדם להשתדל להתאמץ להתפלל לשם יתעלה, לבקש ממנו רחמים, לבטוח בו שיצילנו מכל נזק דתי Pray and trust Hashem to save you even from small spiritual failures — like David: חקרני אל ודע לבבי, בחנני ודע שרעפי, וראה אם דרך עוצב בי (\"Search me, God... see if there is any offensive way in me\"). The Example — Being Pressured to Tell a Lie In the grand scheme it's \"minor\" (not Shabbat desecration, not conversion). By the physical-matters logic, bitachon would be inappropriate. But in spiritual matters — deploy bitachon even here. Why? Because one small lie breeds bigger ones — \"Oh what a tangled web we weave, when first we practise to deceive\" (Sir Walter Scott, 1808). Small spiritual things grow. End of Category Two. PART 3 — Category Three Begun: Bitachon for Basic Necessities ואשר לחלק השלישי — דהיינו השגת תועלת הכרחית, כגון מזונות ומלבושים הכרחיים וכיוצא בזה For essential needs — basic food, basic clothing, basic shelter — employing bitachon is absolutely appropriate. The model is Yaakov Avinu : והוא שהצטמצם בהכרחי לבד — לחם לאכול ובגד ללבוש — ויבטח בו יתעלה שיתן לו זה Yaakov confined his request to the essentials only — לחם לאכול ובגד ללבוש — and trusted Hashem to grant it. And: work a normal job as Yaakov shepherded Lavan's flock — with the inner recognition that parnasah comes from Hashem. Rabbeinu Bachya (on Yaakov's request) זאת שאלת הצדיקים מאת השם: לא ישאלו מותרות, רק דבר הכרחי בלבד This is the request of the righteous: they do not ask for luxuries — only what is essential. The Supporting Verses — Hashem Feeds All Verse Point נותן לחם לכל בשר He gives bread to all flesh כולם אליך ישברון לתת אכלם בעתו All hope to You to give their food in its time עיני כל אליך ישברו ואתה נותן להם את אכלם בעתו (said before HaMotzi) The eyes of all look to You נותן לבהמה לחמה, לבני עורב אשר יקראו He feeds the animals, even the young ravens זן מקרני ראמים ועד ביצי כינים (Chazal) He sustains from the horns of the wild ox to the eggs of lice (\"With the baby comes the bread.\") The Two Great Questions (Left for Next Time) שתי שאלות חיוניות שאפשר שיתעוררו אצל בעל הביטחון Question 1 — If Hashem Feeds Even the Lice, Why Bitachon? Why Work? The verses just quoted say Hashem grants sustenance even to creatures without da'at (the eggs of lice!). If so — מה תועלת אפוא בביטחון בעניין הפרנסה? הלא בין אם יבטח האדם ובין לא יבטח, בין ישתדל במלאכה ובין לא ישתדל — מכל מקום תבוא הפרנסה! Whether you trust or don't trust, work or don't work — the sustenance comes anyway? Then what advantage has the botei'ach over the non-botei'ach — and why work at all? Question 2 — Is Bitachon Natural or Praiseworthy? (It Can't Be Both) The verse says עיני כל אליך ישברו — everyone's eyes look hopefully to You — implying trust in Hashem is natural and universal . But then: Why do other verses praise the one who trusts ( אשרי הגבר אשר שם ה' מבטחו )? And condemn the one who doesn't ( הנה הגבר לא ישים אלהים מעוזו )? If bitachon is universal — praise is meaningless. If only some have it — the verse claiming all do seems contradicted. (And empirically — not everyone does trust in Hashem.) The answers — next shiur. Summary Category One Completed — Miracles The person of complete bitachon cannot be harmed (fire, water, snakes) — proven by the copper snake , radically reread: the snakes never stopped biting; the deaths stopped — teaching that the snake doesn't kill, the removal of hashgacha kills (אין חברבר ממית אלא החטא ממית — Chanina ben Dosa) The copper snake had no mystical power (Chizkiyahu smashed it; amulets are worthless) — it was a device to direct hearts to Heaven But never rely on miracles : Shmuel, Moshe, and Eliyahu all fled danger; Chazal built safety fences; a miracle deducts from one's merits ; no permission to court danger for wealth Spiritual Category One = shmad : trust, pray, flee (the caves) — or, where halacha demands, Kiddush Hashem Category Two — Minor Harms Physical : do NOT deploy bitachon for trivial annoyances (insects, insults) — unless you are a Chanina ben Dosa or Elisha. (Prayer is fine; confident reliance is not.) — Against Rabbeinu Yonah , who requires bitachon בכל דרכיך, even in the small If minor harm compounds into serious harm → back to Category One Spiritual : the rule REVERSES — deploy bitachon even for the smallest spiritual danger, because small breaches compound (שכר עבירה עבירה) Category Three Begun — Essentials For basic food/clothing/shelter: bitachon is right and proper — the model of Yaakov (לחם לאכול ובגד ללבוש), working while knowing parnasah is from Hashem Two questions pending : (1) if Hashem feeds even the lice, why bitachon and why work? (2) is bitachon natural (עיני כל) or praiseworthy (אשרי הגבר) — it can't be both? Schedule Note To finish the bitachon section before the Tisha B'Av break (two Mondays remain): proposal to hold Thursday's shiur as HaMaspik (in place of Hilchot Shabbat) if five people can attend — then the two Mondays — completing the topic before the break. (Tomorrow: Moreh as usual.) B'Ezrat Hashem","NZVdiXInXh4":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Mishneh Torah · Sefer Zemanim · Hilchot Ta'aniyot Perek: 1 (of 5) — The Fundamental Concepts of a Ta'anit Occasion: Learned on Shiv'ah Asar B'Tammuz itself Central Theme: A fast is not about the fast — it is about crying out to Hashem and returning to Him (teshuvah). This theme opens the chapter, runs throughout, and closes it. The Structure of Hilchot Ta'aniyot Perek Content 1 (tonight) The mitzvah of a ta'anit — where the concept comes from, and its fundamentals 2–4 Details and specific scenarios 5 Tisha B'Av, Shiv'ah Asar B'Tammuz, and the fixed fasts The Rambam's Introduction (Minyan HaMitzvot) מצות עשה אחת — והיא לצעוק לפני ה' בכל עת צרה גדולה שלא תבוא על הציבור One positive Torah commandment governs these five chapters — and it is not \"to fast.\" It is to cry out before Hashem at any time of great communal trouble. The fast is built around the crying out — not the other way around. Halacha 1 — The Mitzvat Aseh: Pray and Sound the Trumpets מצות עשה מן התורה לזעוק ולהריע בחצוצרות על כל צרה שתבוא על הציבור, שנאמר: על הצר הצורר אתכם והרעותם בחצוצרות. כלומר: כל דבר שיצר לכם — כגון בצורת ודבר וארבה וכיוצא בהן — זעקו עליהן והריעו. What Does זעקה (Ze'akah) Mean? (Rav Kapach) NOT shouting. Nowhere in all of Tanach is there a concept of shouting in prayer — the one exception being Eliyahu mocking the prophets of Ba'al (קראו בקול גדול — \"cry aloud to your god!\"). (The one possible Talmudic source for loud prayer: the Gemara in Shabbat on answering אמן יהא שמיה רבא בכל כוחו — Rashi: with all one's concentration ; Tosafot: with a loud voice .) Ze'akah = intense, focused prayer — to cry out inwardly with strength, not to raise the volume. Where Does the Rambam Get \"Prayer\" from the Verse? The verse (Beha'alotecha) says only: וכי תבואו מלחמה בארצכם על הצר הצורר אתכם — והרעותם בחצוצרות, ונזכרתם לפני ה' אלהיכם ונושעתם מאויביכם. Rav Kapach: the Rambam understands והרעותם as prayer with the mouth — and the trumpet as the external symbol that arouses the prayer , as in Amos: אם יתקע שופר בעיר ועם לא יחרדו — the blast startles people toward teshuvah. So the mitzvah is: pray, accompanied by trumpet blasts. (The blowing itself: a teru'ah, with a teki'ah before and after — as we know from elsewhere that every teru'ah is sandwiched by teki'ot: תקיעה-תרועה-תקיעה.) \"The Aggressor\" Is Not Only War Though the verse says על הצר הצורר (\"the enemy who oppresses\"), the Rambam reads it as any aggressor — human or natural : famine (בצורת), pestilence (דבר), locusts (ארבה), and the like. Any great communal trouble (על הציבור) — not a private one — triggers the Torah obligation. Trumpets or Shofar? The primary mitzvah is with trumpets (chatzotzrot) ; without trumpets, a shofar may be used (developed in Halacha 4). Why Don't We Do This Today? — The Magen Avraham's Astonishment Magen Avraham (~400 years ago): ואני תמה — למה אין אנו נוהגים לתקוע בעת צרה? \"I am astonished — why do we not practice blowing in times of trouble? Even without a ta'anit, there is a Torah mitzvah to blow!\" He leaves the question unanswered. The Mishnah Berurah's Three Answers (from the Pri Megadim) # Answer Assessment Today 1 The verse says בארצכם — the mitzvah applies only in Eretz Yisrael We have Israel today 2 Even in Israel — only when the land is under our sovereignty Israel is sovereign today 3 Only when the decree threatens the majority of Am Yisrael Difficult — the Rambam says על הציבור ( the community ), not \"the majority of Israel\"; this answer doesn't fit his words The Contemporary Application (e.g., the Iranian missile attacks) Take even the strictest reading: In Israel? ✓ Sovereign? ✓ Affecting the majority of Am Yisrael? — When 150 missiles are launched at Israel, everyone in Israel is endangered and Jews worldwide are in genuine distress (a tzara can be a mental tzara, as the Rambam will show later in the chapter) — that is a tzara for the majority of the Jewish people. Rav Mordechai Eliyahu zt\"l held this mitzvah is d'Oraita today and practiced it — assembling people at the Kotel in times of national crisis and blowing trumpets and shofarot. (In Israel this remains common; blows are sounded and people gather to pray.) There is nothing to lose — and a Torah mitzvah to gain. We should not hunt for justifications to avoid it. The Key Practical Point (Rav Kapach) Both here and in Sefer HaMitzvot, the essence of the mitzvah is the TEFILLAH — the trumpets accompany it. Therefore even without trumpets , when a tzara faces Am Yisrael, there is arguably a Torah obligation on each of us — in Israel or outside it — to pray intently (ze'akah) to Hakadosh Baruch Hu. What Kind of Prayer? (Discussion) Any genuine reaching out to Hashem — including free-form prayer in English (\"please help us\"). Options discussed: Tefillat Nedavah is appropriate — permitted whenever there is something new (לחדש בה דבר), and this new tzara is exactly that The Rambam's general view (Hilchot Tefillah): Chazal fixed the Amidah because people struggle to express themselves — so using the Sages' words has value Chassidic thought (esp. Rav Nachman): the highest prayer flows from one's own heart A hybrid works well : the fixed text with personal insertions in the appropriate places, articulating your own fears and requests Halacha 2 — Why: This Is a Path of Teshuvah ודבר זה מדרכי התשובה הוא, שבזמן שתבוא צרה ויזעקו עליה ויריעו — ידעו הכל שבגלל מעשיהם הרעים הורע להן... וזה הוא שיגרום להסיר הצרה מעליהם. When trouble comes and we cry out and blow — everyone comes to know that it is because of our deeds (ככתוב: עונותיכם הטו — \"your sins have turned away the rains\") — and this itself is what removes the trouble. Like the shofar of Rosh Hashanah (Hilchot Teshuvah): עורו ישנים משנתכם — \"Awake, sleepers, from your slumber!\" The blast arouses teshuvah. And as the Rambam writes there — famine and the like are not arbitrary \"punishments\" but consequences of abandoning His will; recognizing the source is itself the repair. Halacha 3 — The Opposite Path: \"It's Just Random\" Is Cruelty אבל אם לא יזעקו ולא יריעו, אלא יאמרו: דבר זה ממנהג העולם אירע לנו, וצרה זו נקרה נקרית — הרי זו דרך אכזריות, וגורמת להם להידבק במעשיהם הרעים, ותוסיף הצרה צרות אחרות. If instead we say \"this is just the way of the world; it happened by chance\" — three consequences: דרך אכזריות — it is a path of CRUELTY. Why \"cruelty\"? When one sees Hashem's hand behind events, even painful events have a guiding hand — distressing, but not cruel. Remove Hashem from the equation and the world becomes a genuinely cruel, random place. (\"The world is a cruel place\" — yes, if nothing and no one guides it.) It causes us to cling to our bad deeds — no teshuvah. ותוסיף הצרה צרות אחרות — more troubles will follow (like the escalating stages of tzara'at: first the house, then the clothes, then the body — Hashem raises the volume until we hear, or ceases to intervene). The Torah Source והלכתם עמי בקרי — והלכתי גם אני עמכם בחמת קרי The Rambam reads קרי as randomness/chance : If you treat My providence as chance, I will intensify that very \"chance\" upon you — so that you return and recognize there is a reason. Halacha 4 — Now the Fast: A Rabbinic Mitzvah ומדברי סופרים: להתענות על כל צרה שתבוא על הציבור עד שירוחמו מן השמים. ובימי התעניות האלו זועקין בתפילות ומתחננים ומריעים בחצוצרות בלבד. ואם היו במקדש — מריעים בחצוצרות ובשופר. השופר מקצר והחצוצרות מאריכות, שמצות היום בחצוצרות. ואין תוקעין בחצוצרות ושופר כאחד אלא במקדש, שנאמר: בחצוצרות וקול שופר הריעו לפני המלך ה'. Fasting itself is מדברי סופרים — rabbinic (rooted in the Nevi'im): on any communal tzara, fast until Heaven has mercy The source in Nevi'im (Divrei HaYamim II 20): when Moav and Ammon massed against Yehudah — ויתן יהושפט את פניו לדרוש לה' ויקרא צום על כל יהודה — Yehoshafat proclaimed a fast on all of Yehudah During these fasts: intense prayer (זועקין), supplication, and trumpet blasts Trumpets + shofar together — only in the Mikdash (בחצוצרות וקול שופר הריעו לפני המלך ה' — only \"before the King\"); there, the shofar blows short and the trumpets long, for the mitzvah of the day is with trumpets . Outside the Mikdash: trumpets; without trumpets — shofar. The crucial ordering: The Rambam opens Hilchot Ta'aniyot with prayer and trumpets — and only then introduces the fast, immediately adding that during the fast we pray, plead, and blow. A ta'anit without tefillah and ze'akah — without turning to Hashem — is pointless. (This is why learning Torah and praying with focus on the fast day itself — as we do in this very shiur, before Mincha — is exactly the purpose of the day.) Halacha 5 — Scheduling: Monday–Thursday–Monday תעניות אלו שגוזרין על הציבור מפני הצרות אינן יום אחר יום... ואין גוזרין בתחילה תענית בחמישי שלא להפקיע השערים; וכן הסדר הזה: שני וחמישי ושני — עד שירוחמו. Communal fasts are not decreed on consecutive days (too taxing, even with eating at night) Never begin on a Thursday — shopkeepers, seeing the rabbis fasting Thursday, would assume a food shortage before Shabbat and raise prices (inflation); beginning Monday makes clear it's a fast-cycle, not scarcity The order: Monday, Thursday, Monday — days associated with extra mercy (Moshe ascended and descended on Monday/Thursday) — repeated until Heaven has mercy (Open question in the mefarshim: after the first Mon-Thu-Mon cycle, may one continue directly with the next Thursday, or must each cycle restart on a Monday?) Halacha 6 — No Decreed Fasts on Shabbat and Yom Tov (With Exceptions) אין גוזרין תענית על הציבור לא בשבתות ולא בימים טובים, וכן אין תוקעין בהן לא בחצוצרות ולא בשופר, ולא זועקין ומתחננין בהן בתפילה — אלא אם כן היתה עיר שהקיפוה גוים או נהר, או ספינה המיטרפת בים, ואפילו יחיד הנרדף מפני גוים ומפני ליסטים ומפני רוח רעה — מתענין עליהם בשבת, וזועקין ומתחננין עליהם בתפילה. אבל אין תוקעין, אלא אם כן תקעו לקבץ העם לעזור אותם ולהצילם. Normally: no communal fasts, no blowing, no crying-out prayers on Shabbat or Yom Tov (a non-urgent tzara — e.g., drought — is scheduled for after Shabbat) EXCEPT for immediate, life-threatening danger: a city besieged by enemies or floodwaters, a ship foundering at sea — even for ONE individual pursued by enemies, bandits, or ruach ra'ah (a severe mental affliction/depression) — (Rav Kapach establishes the correct girsa: מתענין עליהם בשבת ) — one may fast and cry out in prayer even on Shabbat Blowing on Shabbat: only to assemble the people to actually help and rescue The Practical Halacha for \"Mi Sheberach\" on Shabbat This is the source that we may pray for the ill on Shabbat only if they are critically ill (גוסס / נוטה למות). Hence when saying the refuah prayer on Shabbat for the non-critical, we add: שבת היא מלזעוק ורפואה קרובה לבוא — \"it is Shabbat, when we do not cry out — and healing is soon to come\" — declaring that this is not a ze'akah. Halacha 7 — No Fasts Initially Decreed on Semi-Festive Days וכן אין גוזרין תענית בתחילה בראשי חדשים, ולא בחנוכה ופורים, ולא בחולו של מועד. ואם התחילו להתענות על הצרה אפילו יום אחד ופגע בהן יום מאלו — משלימין אותו התענית. No fasts initially decreed for Rosh Chodesh, Chanukah, Purim, or Chol HaMoed But if the fast-cycle already began (even one day) and one of these days falls within it — the fast is completed on that day (e.g., began Monday, and Thursday is Chol HaMoed — fast on it) Halacha 8 — Who Is Exempt; Eating at Night תעניות אלו שמתענין על הצרות — אין מתענין בהן לא עוברות ולא מניקות ולא קטנים. ומותרין לאכול בלילה אף על פי שמתענין למחר, חוץ מתעניות המטר כמו שיתבאר. וכל תענית שאוכלין בה בלילה, בין ציבור בין יחיד, הרי זה אוכל ושותה עד שיעלה עמוד השחר — והוא שלא ישן; אבל אם ישן — אינו חוזר ואוכל. Exempt: pregnant women, nursing women, and children Why state the obvious about children? The Magen Avraham's chiddush : the Rambam may mean even those over bar mitzvah who are not yet fully mature (he suggests perhaps to 18!) are exempt from these decreed fasts Eating at night is permitted (these fasts are daytime-only — except the rain-fast series, to be explained later) One may eat and drink until amud hashachar (dawn) — provided one has not gone to sleep. Once asleep, one has \"accepted the day\" and may not eat again upon waking The Tnai (stipulation): the Rambam omits it, but other Rishonim and the Shulchan Aruch record it — if one stipulates before sleeping (\"my sleep is not an acceptance of the fast; I intend to eat if I wake\"), one may eat/drink upon waking before dawn. This is our practice (especially useful for winter fasts like Asarah B'Tevet) Halacha 9 — The Individual's Fast for His Own Tzara כשם שהציבור מתענים על צרתן, כך היחיד מתענה על צרתו. כיצד? הרי שהיה לו חולה, או תועה במדבר, או אסור בבית האסורין — יש לו להתענות עליו ולבקש רחמים בתפילתו, ואומר עננו וכו' בכל תפילה שמתפלל. ולא יתענה לא בשבתות ולא במועדות, ולא בראשי חדשים, ולא בחנוכה ופורים. Just as the community fasts for its trouble, an individual fasts for his own : a sick relative, someone lost in the desert, someone imprisoned One fasts on their behalf (they themselves cannot — the ill person is weak, the desert traveler must not fast) — a striking case where my fast benefits another (my prayer for them is more readily accepted) Precedent: David HaMelech fasting for the ill child of Batsheva Say עננו in every tefillah — this is the source of the Sephardi practice of saying Anenu in Shacharit as well (following the Rambam), unlike Ashkenazim who postpone it to Mincha (an Ashkenazi Rishon reasoned: perhaps you'll break the fast in the afternoon and your morning Anenu will retroactively be false — a reasoning that is difficult; see Halacha 13 below: at worst it becomes a ta'anit sha'ot, for which Anenu is said) No such private fasts on Shabbat, Yom Tov, Rosh Chodesh, Chanukah, or Purim (The Gemara adds another device: one who cannot fast now may vow — \"I will fast once he recovers\" — and this too has power) Halacha 10 — A Fast Must Be Accepted in Advance כל תענית שלא קיבלה עליו היחיד מבעוד יום — אינה תענית. כיצד מקבלה? כשיתפלל תפילת מנחה אומר אחר התפילה: הריני בתענית למחר, וגומר בלבו להתענות למחר. A private fast not accepted before sunset the previous day is no fast at all How to accept: at Mincha, after the Amidah (in Elokai Netzor), say: הריני בתענית למחר — \"Behold, I will fast tomorrow\" — and resolve in the heart Clearly a verbal declaration l'chatchila (the poskim discuss purely mental acceptance) The Chida prefers saying it in Shome'a Tefillah ; the Rambam — at the end of the Amidah; and technically any time that day before sunset works , even outside of prayer Eating that night (after acceptance) is fine Multi-day acceptance: one who accepts 3–4 consecutive fast-days in a single acceptance — it covers all of them, even though he eats each night; no need to re-accept daily The Gemara's Image כל תענית שלא קיבלה מבעוד יום — לאו שמה תענית. ואם התענה? אמר רבה בר שילא: דמי למפוחא דמליא זיקא An unaccepted \"fast\" is like a bellows full of air — a container of wind, nothing. The Ta'anit Sha'ot Reconciliation But doesn't a ta'anit sha'ot (a fast of hours — Halacha 13) have value without prior-day acceptance? Resolution: if you intend a full ta'anit without having accepted it — it's nothing (you attempted something that doesn't exist). If you consciously intend a ta'anit sha'ot from this point on — that works, as its own category. Halachot 11–12 — Ta'anit Chalom (The Dream Fast) הרואה חלום רע צריך להתענות למחר, כדי שישוב ויעור במעשיו ויחפש בהן ויחזור בתשובה. ומתענה ואפילו בשבת... והמתענה בשבת מתענה יום אחר מפני שביטל עונג שבת. A bad dream → fast the very next day (no prior acceptance possible or needed — you dreamt at night!) The theme again: the purpose is כדי שישוב ויעור במעשיו — to awaken, search one's deeds, and return in teshuvah. Hashem sent the dream to rouse you One fasts for a dream even on Shabbat — but must then fast another day to atone for having annulled oneg Shabbat (the famous paradox; an open question raised: would one say Anenu in Musaf on such a Shabbat?) The contemporary reflection: fasting today is far harder for our constitutions and lifestyles, and most of us fail to use the day properly. One with the strength, whom fasting draws closer to Hashem — certainly should. But for most, the essence is ישוב ויעור במעשיו — the reflection, the self-searching, the return. A fast spent on films and games — מה הועילו חכמים בתקנתן? (As one authority put it: better they eat and do teshuvah than fast without it.) Halacha 13 — Ta'anit Sha'ot (A Fast of Hours) מתענה אדם שעות, והוא: שלא יאכל כלום שאר היום. כיצד? הרי שהיה טרוד בחפציו ועסקיו ולא אכל עד חצות או עד תשע שעות, ונמלך להתענות אותן שעות שנשארו — הרי זה מתענה אותן שעות ומתפלל בהן עננו, שהרי קיבל עליו התענית קודם שעות התענית. וכן אם אכל ושתה ואחר כך התחיל להתענות שאר היום — הרי זה תענית שעות. One may fast part of a day : e.g., busy until midday without having eaten, and deciding — \"I'm not hungry; I'll fast the rest of the day\" → accept it now and fast until nightfall Anenu IS said in the tefillot of a ta'anit sha'ot (the acceptance preceded the fasting hours) Works in both directions: didn't eat all morning → complete the day; or ate in the morning → fast from now to nightfall (This further undermines the concern about morning Anenu being retroactive falsehood: at the very least the day becomes a ta'anit sha'ot, for which Anenu is valid.) Halacha 14 — How to Conduct Oneself on Any Fast כל השרוי בתענית — בין שהיה מתענה על צרתו או על חלומו, בין שהיה מתענה עם הציבור על צרתם — הרי זה לא ינהוג עידונין בעצמו, ולא יקל ראשו, ולא יהיה שמח וטוב לב — אלא דואג ואונן, כענין שנאמר: מה יתאונן אדם חי, גבר על חטאיו. On any fast — private or communal: No indulgences (עידונין) No frivolity (קלות ראש) Not joyful and merry — rather דואג ואונן : concerned and reflective — \"Why should a living man complain? Let each man [reflect] on his sins\" The application to our day: sleeping to get through the fast — understandable. But passing the fast watching films and playing games — what did the Sages accomplish with their enactment? Use the time: learn, reflect, pray with focus. (Hence a shiur on the fast itself, going straight into Mincha with these ideas in mind.) Tasting ומותר לטעום את התבשיל... ובלבד שלא יבלע — אלא טועם ופולט One may taste food (e.g., checking a dish) provided one does not swallow — taste and expel (no beracha, since it is not eating). Forgetting שכח ואכל — משלים תעניתו One who forgot and ate — continues and completes the fast (true today as well, and for a ta'anit yachid too). Halachot 15–16 — Completing the Fast; The Traveler The Individual Completes Even If the Crisis Passes Fasting for a sick person or a court case — even if the person recovers or you are vindicated before midday — complete your fast. Traveling Between Cities ההולך ממקום שמתענין למקום שאין מתענין — הרי זה מתענה עמהן. ממקום שאין מתענין למקום שמתענין — הרי זה מתענה עמהן [ומשלים]. From a fasting city to a non-fasting city → complete your fast Into a fasting city → fast with them If you forgot and ate — don't eat in public (שלא יתראה בפני בני אדם), and no indulgence The Community — Different Rule If the community's trouble is relieved before chatzot (midday) → they need not complete ; they eat, and say Hallel and praise Hashem. After chatzot → most of the day has passed in the fast → complete it. Halacha 17 — The Fast Day Itself: The Beit Din's Program בכל יום תענית שגוזרין על הציבור מפני הצרות — בית דין והזקנים יושבין בבית הכנסת ובודקים על מעשי אנשי העיר מאחר תפילת שחרית עד חצות היום, ומסירין המכשולות של עבירות, ומזהירין ודורשין וחוקרין על בעלי חמס ועבירות ומפרישין אותן, ובעלי זרוע משפילין אותן... ומחצי היום ולערב — רביע היום קוראין בברכות וקללות... ומפטירין בנביא מענין הצרה... ורביע האחרון מתפללים מנחה, ומתחננים וזועקים ומתוודים כפי כוחם. The structure of a decreed fast day: Portion of Day Activity Shacharit → chatzot Beit Din and elders sit in the synagogue examining the deeds of the townspeople : removing stumbling blocks to sin, warning and exhorting, investigating the violent and corrupt and separating them, humbling the strong-armed Third quarter Reading the Blessings and the Curses (במוסר ה' בני אל תמאס — \"do not despise Hashem's discipline\"), and a haftarah relating to the tzara Final quarter Mincha — supplication, ze'akah, and viduy (confession) , each according to his strength The Spanish & Portuguese Mincha on a Ta'anit fits the Rambam beautifully — beginning as on Yom Kippur (שמע קולי), Anenu before Mincha, Torah reading, Amidah, and then the full Selichot (אנשי אמונה אבדו... תמנו מרעות) — about an hour, and well worth it. (A minhag not seen elsewhere.) The Chapter's Master Theme (Opening and Closing) A ta'anit is not about not-eating. The Torah mitzvah is ze'akah — crying out to Hashem (with trumpets as the arousal). The rabbinic fast is a vehicle: its days are days of prayer, pleading, self-examination, removing stumbling blocks, and viduy. Treating a tzara as random chance (קרי) is cruelty — it strips the world of its guiding hand, entrenches us in our deeds, and invites further trouble. Recognizing Hashem's voice within events — ידעו הכל שבגלל מעשיהם הרעים הורע להן — is itself what removes the tzara. Practically today: when great trouble faces Am Yisrael, there is a strong case for a live Torah obligation to pray intensely (and, per Rav Mordechai Eliyahu, to sound trumpets/shofarot in Israel). And on our fixed fasts — the day is for teshuvah, learning, and focused tefillah, not for passing time. Coming Up Next week: either back to Hilchot Shabbat , or Perek 5 — the fixed fasts (Tisha B'Av and Shiv'ah Asar B'Tammuz) — to be decided. B'Ezrat Hashem","hkQaPPry5Jo":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: 1 · Chapters 8 & 9 Topic: The Terms מקום (Makom / Place) and כסא (Kisei / Throne) in Tanakh Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Where We Are in the Moreh Chapters Theme 1–4 Incorporeality — verses implying corporeality (tzelem, temunah, seeing God) 5 Caution approaching the divine (Moshe at the burning bush) 6–7 Ma'aseh Bereshit — Ish/Isha (form & matter); Ben (birthing & the power of a teacher) 8–9 (tonight) Back to incorporeality — terms that seem to imply God has physicality: Makom (place) and Kisei (throne) PART 1 — Chapter 8: The Term מקום (Makom / Place) The Two Meanings of Makom Meaning 1 — Physical Space (the literal meaning) ועיקר קריאת מונח זה היא למקום מיוחד או כללי The primary meaning is a specific or general physical space. Meyuchad (specific) : \"He's sitting in this house, on this chair, in this room\" Klali (general) : \"He's in this neighborhood / this city\" Either way — the literal makom is the physical space something occupies. Meaning 2 — Status / Rank (a borrowed, conceptual sense) ולאחר מכן הורחבה משמעותו... והפך למונח לדרגת האדם ומעלתו It was broadened to denote a person's status and level — his standing in a particular subject. \"פלוני במקום פלוני בנושא פלוני\" — X is at place X in subject X (e.g., \"which place are you up to in the Gemara?\" — a conceptual place, not geographical) The Gemara: ממלא מקום אבותיו — one who \"fills the place of his fathers\" — i.e., follows them בחכמה וביראה (in wisdom and fear), not a geographical spot The Mishnah: ועדיין המחלוקת במקומה עומדת — \"the debate still stands in its place\" — i.e., in its status (במעמדה), not a physical location This conceptual usage exists in other languages too: in English, \"I'm not in a good place today\" — not a geographical statement, but about one's mental/emotional status . The same borrowing happens in Hebrew. Applying It — \"Baruch Kevod Hashem MiMekomo\" ברוך כבוד ה' ממקומו (Yechezkel) — said four times a day (before Shema, in Kedusha, in Uva Letzion, and at Mincha) This does NOT mean \"blessed is Hashem from the [physical] place where He is\" — Hashem is not in a specific place (that would imply limitation/corporeality). כלומר לפי דרגתו וגודל חלקו במציאות It means: according to His level and supreme standing in existence — His conceptual \"place,\" which is incomparable / sui generis (אין שווה לו ולא דומה לו). Word by word: Baruch (blessed) · Kevod Hashem (God's glory — either God Himself, or those who recognize His glory) · mimkomo (from His \"place\" = His unique conceptual standing in all of reality). The whole point of the chapter: so you don't fall into thinking Hashem occupies a specific place — which would imply corporeality and limitation. His \"place\" is a unique conceptual standing, because nothing is comparable to Him. A Crucial Aside — This Book Is NOT a Lexicon דע שכל מונח שאנו מבארים לך בחיבור זה... אין מטרתנו בכך להאיר את מה שנזכר בו בלבד, אלא אנחנו פותחים בכך שער Rambam's purpose isn't merely to define words. Each word's explanation is a key (מפתח) — opening gates to understand all the places that term appears across Tanakh and Chazal, for our philosophical purpose , not for linguistic interest. ואתה תתבונן בספרי הנבואה... ותבין כל מונח רב משמעי במשמעות המתאימה לו Once you learn this book, go back and study Tanakh again — now reading each multi-meaning term in its proper context. Our words are a key to this work and others. Applying It Again — \"Hinei Makom Iti\" (Ki Tisa) After the golden calf, Moshe asks הראני נא את כבודך (\"show me Your glory\"). Hashem responds: הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור — \"Behold, there is a place with Me, and you shall stand on the rock\" דרגת עיון והתבוננות השכל — this means a level of intellectual contemplation — not a physical eye/place. זאת בנוסף למקום בהר... שבו הייתה התבודדות — this is in addition to the simple meaning that there was a physical spot on the mountain. The deeper meaning: Makom here = a rank . Hashem tells Moshe: \"You asked to see My glory; I cannot give you the whole, but I will raise you to a new intellectual place (conceptual understanding of Me) you've never reached before.\" A Key Methodological Principle (will recur often) Rambam does NOT remove the literal meaning unless there is a need to do so. Here the verse still literally means a physical place on the mountain — and also carries the deeper meaning of an intellectual ascent. Rashi on This Verse הנה מקום אתי — peshat : \"there is a place prepared for your need where you'll stand and behold what you behold\" (sheltered so you won't be harmed). Midrash : המקום אתי ואינו אומר אני במקום — Hashem says \"the place is with Me ,\" not \"I am in a place\" — because הקדוש ברוך הוא מקומו של עולם ואין עולמו מקומו — God is the \"place\" of the world; the world is not His place. The Gemara (Berachot 7a) At this moment, Moshe also asked Hashem to explain צדיק ורע לו, רשע וטוב לו — why the righteous suffer and the wicked prosper. He didn't receive a full answer — but it shows this was an intellectual encounter and an opening for philosophical inquiry, confirming makom here is a conceptual place. Developing the Concept — Makom in Other Places 1. \"Ayeh Mekom Kevodo\" (Keter / Musaf) כבודו מלא עולם, ומשרתיו שואלים: איה מקום כבודו להעריצו \"His glory fills the world, and His ministers ask: where is the place of His glory, to revere Him? \" Not \"where geographically is God?\" — rather: where is the intellectual space to grasp Him? How can any creation — even angels — comprehend God's glory and presence? The drawn-out \"Ayeh\" (Sephardi / Arizal custom): the old Iraqi and Syrian custom of extending ayeh (sometimes for a full minute — the highlight of Shabbat morning). The likely reason: as we say ayeh , we are straining to attain an intellectual conception of Hakadosh Baruch Hu — where, how, can we reach this? The Arizal on the Yamim Nora'im: there are four times we say ayeh (Rosh Hashanah Day 1 Musaf, Day 2 Musaf, Yom Kippur Musaf, Ne'ilah). At each, pray for one of three things only: (1) Ruach Hakodesh/prophecy, (2) parnasah, (3) righteous children. (One custom: choose differently for the first three; the fourth — what you choose — defines what you most want in life.) A beautiful link: as we conceptualize Hashem ( ayeh ), we draw near to prophecy, to becoming good role models (→ righteous children), and to recognizing all comes from Hashem (→ parnasah). 2. \"HaMakom\" as a Name of Hashem ברוך המקום ברוך הוא (Pesach Haggadah); המקום ינחם אתכם בתוך שאר אבלי ציון וירושלים (Ashkenazi consolation) Why is Hashem called \"HaMakom\"? Simply: because He is the \"place\" of the entire universe — His presence sustains all existence (as Rashi said). (Per Rambam's chapter, one could add: because His standing is sui generis — incomparable — He is \"the Place.\") The Gematria (attributed to the Vilna Gaon): the square of the Name יקוק = 186 (10² + 5² + 6² + 5² = 100 + 25 + 36 + 25 = 186). And המקום = 40 + 100 + 6 + 40 = 186 (with the kollel ). The two are equal. Why this matters (via the Ramchal, Da'at Tevunot) — why Ashkenazim say \"HaMakom yenachem\" at a shiva: יקוק is Hashem's Name of mercy — but its mercy is not always recognized in life (we see much that seems to contradict it). The square represents the Name fully manifested / drawn out . When does a person most question Hashem's mercy? When losing a loved one (when middat hadin is most apparent). So we bless them: HaMakom yenachem — may the Name of mercy (יקוק) be fully squared, manifested, and felt in your life — that you not only know theoretically that יקוק is mercy, but actually experience it, and so be comforted. 3. Makom in the Akeidah Four times in a short span: וילך אל המקום אשר אמר לו האלהים · וירא את המקום מרחוק · ויבאו אל המקום · ויקרא אברהם שם המקום ההוא ה' יראה Abarbanel (500 years ago) cites those who argued — based on this very Rambam — that because makom can mean a conceptual place and is stressed four times, the Akeidah never physically happened (only in a vision). Abarbanel demolishes this. The Akeidah physically happened — two real people, Avraham and Yitzchak, saddled a donkey and traveled. As Rambam himself taught (on hinei makom iti ), one removes the literal meaning only when there is a necessity — a logical or scientific problem. Here there is none. The emphasis on makom teaches that through this process they ascended to new intellectual levels — it's both a physical place and a conceptual ascent. 4. The Makom of Gan Eden (to be developed later) Was Hashem placing Adam in Gan Eden a physical/geographical space — or a heightened intellectual space to understand God, from which he was banished after the sin? (Raised here as something to think about; to be revisited later in the book.) Chapter 8 Summary Makom means (1) a specific/general physical space , or (2) a rank/conceptual standing . Hence: Baruch kevod Hashem mimekomo = according to His incomparable conceptual standing; hinei makom iti = an intellectual ascent (while still literally a spot on the mountain). Applied further: ayeh mekom kevodo (where is the intellectual space to grasp Him), HaMakom as a Name (He is the place of the world), the Akeidah (physical and an ascent), and Gan Eden (to come). PART 2 — Chapter 9: The Term כסא (Kisei / Throne) Meaning 1 — A Chair / Throne (the literal meaning) קביעותו העיקרית בלשון היא שמו של הכיסא The primary meaning is a seat . Crucial linguistic point: In Hebrew there is no separate word for \"chair\" vs. \"throne\" — kisei is both. Why? Because a chair was once a luxury — ordinary people sat on the ground; only the great had seats. (Modern Hebrew now needs both because everyone has chairs.) וכיון שעל הכיסא היו יושבים רק בעלי הרוממות והגדולה כמו המלכים... והכיסא נעשה לדבר המורה גדולתו של מי שראוי לו Since only the eminent (kings, dignitaries) sat on a seat, the seat became an indicator of the importance of the one who sits on it. Meaning 2 — Something of Importance/Majesty (borrowed sense) Because the seat denotes importance, kisei is borrowed for important things : The Temple Is Called \"Kisei\" נקרא המקדש כיסא... כיון שהוא מורה על גדולתו של מי שהתגלה בו והשרה את אורו The Mikdash is called a throne because it indicates the greatness of the One whose holiness rests there. כסא כבוד מרום מראשון מקום מקדשנו (Yirmiyahu) — \"Throne of glory, exalted from the beginning, the place of our Temple.\" The Heavens Are Called \"Kisei\" נקרא השמים כיסא... משום שהם מורים... גדולתו של מי שיצרם ומנהיגם The heavens (the entire celestial order — galaxies, celestial bodies) are called a throne because, to one who understands them, they display the greatness of their Creator. השמים כסאי והארץ הדום רגלי (Yeshayahu — Haftarah of Shabbat Rosh Chodesh) — \"The heavens are My throne, the earth My footstool.\" I.e., they indicate My existence (מציאותי), My greatness, and My power to sustain them — just as a throne indicates the importance of the one fit for it. Rambam highly valued astronomy and astrophysics (the paths and composition of celestial bodies) as genuine chochmah through which one apprehends God — while rejecting astrology (the claim that celestial bodies influence humans), as he makes clear in his letters. A supporting proof (Rabbi Kada — surprised Rambam didn't cite it): Pharaoh to Yosef — רק הכסא אגדל ממך (\"only by the throne will I be greater than you,\" Miketz). This is not a bigger physical chair — it means \"only in importance will I exceed you.\" Kisei = importance, not merely a physical seat. The Principle Stated לא שיש גוף שהאלוה שוכן עליו... כי עוד יתבאר לך שהוא יתעלה אינו גוף NOT that there is a body upon which God sits — for He has no body; so how could He have a place (Ch. 8) or a throne? Rather, any place Hashem glorified and singled out with His light — like the Mikdash or the heavens — is called kisei . This runs against the Midrash (Bereishit Rabbah 1:4) that the Kisei HaKavod was one of the things preceding creation — which Rambam must either reinterpret or not accept. A Stylistic Observation (Rabbi Kada) — How Rambam Teaches Most people give a drasha by: quote the verse → raise a question → give the answer → \"from here we learn.\" Rambam consistently does the opposite. He first lays the foundations (\"what does makom / kisei mean generally?\"), and only then brings the verse — so you are led naturally to the conclusion rather than starting \"on the back foot\" with a problem. A more elegant mode of delivery: build the lesson, then discover it in the Torah. Meaning 3 — Kisei as God Himself (the verse in Beshalach) After Amalek: ויבן משה מזבח ויקרא שמו ה' נסי, ויאמר: כי יד על כס יה , מלחמה לה' בעמלק מדור דור \"...and he said: for there is a hand upon the throne (kes) of God — Hashem has war with Amalek in every generation.\" Why neither Meaning 1 nor 2 fits: It can't be a physical throne (God has no body); and \"something important\" doesn't fit the sentence either. So what does kes Yah mean here? מה שהלשון הרחיב בו... הרי הוא תיאור גדולתו ורוממותו... שאין ראוי לדמיין שום דבר מחוץ לעצמותו ולא נברא מנבראיו It is a description of God's own greatness — and one must not imagine anything outside His essence. כך שיתעלה נמצא בלי כסא ונמצא עם כסא — זו כפירה בלי ספק To imagine God sometimes with a throne and sometimes without — is outright heresy. כבר נאמר במפורש: אתה ה' לעולם תשב, כסאך לדור ודור (Eicha) — \"You, Hashem, dwell forever; Your throne is for all generations\" — מה שמורה שהוא דבר שלא נדמהו ממנו — showing the throne is not distinct from God. ולפיכך הכוונה בכסא... תפארתו וגדולתו שאינו חוץ מעצמותו Therefore \"kisei\" here means God's own splendor and greatness — not something outside His essence. The Underlying Principle — The Unity of God God's absolute unity forbids speaking of an attribute as separate from Him. With a human we say \"X is wise, handsome, generous\" — three things X has and could lose. This cannot apply to God: His unity demands everything is included in the One. So one cannot speak of \"the throne of Hashem\" as though there were Hashem and His throne — even conceptually — because that would imply sometimes a throne, sometimes not . Rejected on philosophical grounds. So the Verse Means: Moshe swears by raising his hand — kisei Yah = Hashem Himself . Raising the hand always signals an oath (cf. Eliezer: וישם העבד את ידו תחת ירך אברהם... וישבע ). So כי יד על כס יה = Moshe lifts his hand in an oath upon Hashem Himself. (This is unlike Onkelos, who reads it differently — for another time.) Chapter 9 Summary — The Three Meanings of Kisei A physical seat/throne (literal) Something majestic/important — the Mikdash, the heavens (which display God's greatness; cf. rak hakisei egdal mimcha ) God Himself (in kes Yah ) — because the unity of God forbids treating \"His throne\" as anything distinct from His essence A doorway to later study: this prepares us to understand the visions of Yechezkel and Yeshayahu \"seeing Hashem on an elevated throne\" — i.e., a deep intellectual apprehension of God's majesty, not a literal seated figure. Tonight's Summary Chapter 8 — Makom Two meanings: physical space (specific/general) · conceptual rank/status Baruch kevod Hashem mimekomo = His incomparable conceptual standing; hinei makom iti = an intellectual ascent (while still literally a place — remove peshat only when necessary ) The book is a key, not a lexicon — apply each term across Tanakh Developed: ayeh mekom kevodo · HaMakom as a Name (gematria 186; the consolation formula) · the Akeidah (Abarbanel: physical and an ascent) · Gan Eden (later) Chapter 9 — Kisei Three meanings: a seat/throne · majesty/importance (Mikdash, heavens) · God Himself ( kes Yah ) Rooted in the unity of God — no attribute or \"throne\" is separate from His essence Rambam's elegant method: lay foundations first, then reveal the verse Coming Up Next Week Chapters 10 (and possibly 11): the terms for standing and sitting, ascending and descending — what these motions mean when applied to the divine. B'Ezrat Hashem","0sfz6U-SGv8":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 8 (ביטחון — Bitachon and Reliance) — Part 4 This Week: The Transition from Theory to Practice · The Ten Categories of Bitachon · Two Prerequisites · Category One — Bitachon in Serious (Life-Threatening) Danger Recap from Parts 1–3 The Definition Bitachon = complete reliance on Hakadosh Baruch Hu, the Creator of all, to whose will everything is subservient. Living this brings security and serenity ( betach ). We use the intermediaries Hashem created — but place no reliance on them. The Three Categories of Bitachon Category Who Verdict 1 Prophets/exceptional individuals who divested from natural means by divine instruction Not the norm 2 Deniers of providence (Aristotle) / outward affirmers who inwardly trust intermediaries Wrong 3 The Yirei Hashem — recognize Hashem oversees all intermediaries and can change them anytime The goal Established Last Time Category 3 done properly → no need to go overboard in hishtadlut — but also never laziness (forbidden) Cause and effect is real, not an illusion (against the Mutakallimun) — but it's open to change at any moment by Hashem's will The Turning Point — From Theory to Practice ולאחר שהבינות ותפסת את כל הדברים האלה, דע כי הביטחון נחלק לכמה וכמה חלקים Now that you've grasped all this [theory], know that bitachon divides into many parts — regarding what one trusts Hashem for , and the outcome sought. Until now: theoretical bitachon. Now: practical scenarios — when and how bitachon is actually employed. A Caveat on the Difficulty אך הדברים הללו קשים לביאור, וידיעת סודם בשלמות... נשגבת מבינתנו Understanding precisely how to apply bitachon in every scenario is beyond us — even the prophets couldn't fully grasp God's ways. Asaf said ואחשבה לדעת זאת, עמל הוא בעיני (\"when I sought to understand this, it was wearying\"); Hashem told Moshe וחנותי את אשר אחון (\"I show favor to whom I show favor\"). So Rabbi Avraham offers general categories and direction , not an exhaustive system. The Structure — Five (× Two = Ten) Categories The Two Master Axes Axis 1 — Purpose: Bitachon is used either להרחקת הנזקים — to avoid harm/danger , OR להשגת התועלת — to attain a benefit/goal Axis 2 — Degree of necessity of the goal/harm: הכרחית — essential/critical בלתי הכרחית אך כמעט הכרחית — non-essential but nearly so (important) בלתי הכרחית לחלוטין — completely non-essential (a luxury) The Five Categories (הביטחון יפיק כוח עשרה דברים) # Category Physical Example Spiritual Example 1 Avoiding serious harm (הרחקת נזק חמור) Drowning at sea; life-threatening illness Pressure to convert / abandon faith 2 Avoiding non-serious but real harm A non-life-threatening but distressing illness A spiritual discomfort, not critical 3 Attaining an essential benefit (תועלת הכרחית) Basic bread and water to survive — 4 Attaining an important but non-essential benefit General nutrition (fruit, etc.) — 5 Attaining a luxury (non-essential, non-important) A pleasant extra — Each of the five × spiritual or physical = TEN total. E.g., \"avoiding serious harm\" can be a physical serious harm or a spiritual serious harm. Two Prerequisites Before the Categories Prerequisite 1 — You Cannot Use Bitachon Without Doing Hashem's Will המבוא לביטחון הוא השמיעה בקול השם יתעלה ודבקות בתורתו והישמר מפני מרידה The gateway to bitachon is obeying Hashem, cleaving to His Torah, and guarding against rebellion. There is no point claiming bitachon — however sincere — while not observing the mitzvot. Doing Hashem's will is the alef-bet prerequisite for trusting in Him and gaining serenity of mind. The Supporting Verses (Tehillim) Verse Point בטח בה' ועשה טוב Trust in Hashem — and do good (first) התענג על ה' ויתן לך משאלות לבך Delight in Hashem, then He grants your heart's desires גול על ה' דרכך ובטח עליו והוא יעשה Cast your way on Hashem — but do the mitzvot קוה אל ה' ושמור דרכו Hope in Hashem — and guard His way ותשועת צדיקים מה'... ויושיעם כי חסו בו He saves them because they took refuge in Him (and did His will) Yirmiyahu's Castigation אל תבטחו לכם על דברי השקר לאמר: היכל ה' היכל ה' היכל ה' המה Don't trust the falsehood of \"the Temple, the Temple, the Temple\" — i.e., I'll do the three pilgrimages (Pesach, Shavuot, Sukkot) and bring sacrifices, then do as I please all year. It doesn't work that way: הגנוב, רצוח, ונאוף... ובאתם ועמדתם לפני? Can you steal, murder, commit adultery, swear falsely, serve idols — then come stand before Me [in the Temple]? Only כי אם היטיב תיטיבו את דרכיכם — if you genuinely improve your ways — will Hashem dwell with you. This is the obvious-but-essential reminder: there's no practical bitachon without fulfilling Hashem's will. Prerequisite 2 — Spiritual Bitachon Is Always More \"Employable\" Than Physical כל ביטחון שתכליתו דתית הרי הוא נכון ושלם מן הביטחון שתכליתו גשמית Bitachon for a spiritual purpose is more valid and complete than for a physical purpose — even bitachon for a non-essential spiritual matter outranks bitachon for an essential physical matter. The Logic It makes more sense to employ bitachon to fulfill Hashem's will than to employ it for Hashem to fulfill your will. Physical bitachon is for me — my body, myself. Spiritual bitachon is for the will of Hashem — so even a non-critical spiritual aim trumps a critical physical one. The Analogy — Two People of Bitachon Person What They Trust For Purpose Verdict Person 1 Basic survival — bread, clothing, shelter Purely mundane — not to die of hunger/cold, not to beg charity. Not in order to serve Hashem or learn Torah Valid, but lower Person 2 Wealth and authority (non-essential) Spiritual — to give tzedakah, sustain the needy and Torah-scholars (like Ovadiah , who hid and fed 100 prophets from Izevel); to use power to restrain the wicked, protect the oppressed, establish justice More valid and complete (יותר נכון ויותר שלם) (We don't judge Person 1 — someone who can't make ends meet, simply trusting Hashem for food, is in a hard place. But it's not the same level.) (Person 2's intent must be genuine — \"you can fool yourself, but you can't fool Hashem.\") The Highest Form — Essential AND Spiritual: Yaakov's Bitachon אם יהיה אלהים עמדי ושמרני בדרך הזה... ונתן לי לחם לאכל ובגד ללבש... ושבתי בשלום אל בית אבי Yaakov asked for food, clothing, and safe return — all essential — and his purpose was והיה ה' לי לאלהים ( \"that Hashem be my God\" ): so he could be free to serve Hashem undistracted by material struggle. Essential + spiritual = the most praiseworthy bitachon of all. The Practical Takeaway — Reframing Changes Everything A sick person praying \"Hashem, heal me so I won't suffer pain\" → bitachon for a mundane matter. The same person praying \"Hashem, heal me so I can keep going to shul, helping the poor, serving You\" → transformed into spiritual bitachon. The shift in focus changes the entire quality of the bitachon. This is why focus matters when employing it. CATEGORY ONE — Bitachon in Serious (Life-Threatening) Danger הראשון בהם הוא הרחקת נזק חמור The first is averting serious harm. What Counts as \"Serious Harm\" A life-threatening illness (חולאים הקשים) Sudden dangers (סכנות מקריות) — accident, attack War / civil war; falling into quicksand (טיט היון); storms, thunder (סופות רעמים); earthquakes (רעידת אדמה) — natural forces beyond one's control These are critical, death-involved, uncontrollable situations, for the sake of survival (a mundane purpose). The Method — How to Employ Bitachon Here Step 1 — Turn Your Heart to Hashem שיתלה האדם את לבו בשם יתעלה, ויפתח בו שירחיק ממנו את הנזק ויצילו מן הפגעים — כי כל הסיבות כפופות לחפצו ורצונו Cast your heart on Hashem, trusting He will remove the danger and save you — for all causes are subject to His will. Step 2 — Recall Hashem's Promises Through Tehillim Tehillim 91 (יושב בסתר עליון) — the \"song against perils\": (Said Shabbat morning, Motzaei Shabbat, and at a levaya — to protect against the spiritual dangers of the soul's ascent.) Opening (the structure of trust): אמר לה' מחסי ומצודתי, אלהי אבטח בו — \"I say to Hashem: my refuge and fortress, my God in whom I trust\" Middle : כי אתה ה' מחסי, עליון שמת מעונך — \"You, Hashem, are my refuge\" End (the reason ): כי בי חשק ואפלטהו, אשגבהו כי ידע שמי — \"Because he desired Me I will rescue him... because he knows My name\" — i.e., a relationship, a brit with Hashem Tehillim 18 (David's song when saved from Shaul): Opening (his love and trust): ארחמך ה' חזקי, ה' סלעי ומצודתי ומפלטי, צורי אחסה בו Middle ( why he was saved — his righteousness): כי שמרתי דרכי ה'... כל משפטיו לנגדי... ואהי תמים עמו — Hashem saved him because he kept Hashem's ways How Tehillim actually works: NOT a mystical/magical effect of reciting the words — but to remind ourselves who truly governs the world and to reset our priorities. When anxious, open 91, read it (even in English), understand it. (Anxiety often stems from a lack of bitachon; bitachon can help overcome it — though this is not to say every anxiety sufferer lacks bitachon, nor a substitute for treating genuine medical conditions.) Step 3 — Pray (Tefillah) צריך הבוטח לחזק בטחונו על ידי תפילה ובקשה ותחינה Verse Point מהולל אקרא ה' ומן אויבי אִוָּשֵׁעַ \"I call to Hashem... and am saved from my enemies\" בצר לי אקרא ה'... ישמע מהיכלו קולי \"In my distress I call to Hashem... He hears my voice\" כי מי גוי גדול אשר לו אלהים קרובים אליו What nation has God so near, whenever we call — we are privileged to speak to Hashem כי תבא מלחמה... והרעתם בחצצרות ונזכרתם... ונושעתם At war, blow the trumpets, be remembered before Hashem, and be saved Step 4 — STILL Employ Every Natural Means מכל מקום ברי כי לשם הרחקת הפגעים הרעים, יש להשתמש באמצעים שתיקן השם יתעלה להרחקתם Nonetheless — to avert harm, one must use the means Hashem established for averting it. A sick person must find the best doctor (within reason), follow the best diet (within reason — not at the expense of Shabbat meals, etc.) — together with the trust and prayer above. The Torah's Own Proofs — Trust AND Act Even though Hashem is the ultimate cause, the Torah itself commands natural action: Hashem's Role (verse) Yet the Torah Commands אני אמית ואחיה — \"I bring death and life\" ועשית מעקה לגגך — build a parapet so no one falls (Ki Teitzei) מחצתי ואני ארפא — \"I wound and I heal\" ורפא ירפא — a doctor shall heal (the license to practice medicine) The Kohen anointed for war says אל ירך לבבכם... אל תיראו — trust Hashem, say Shema Yet ופקדו שרי צבאות בראש העם — appoint generals; ובנית מצור — build siege-works (employ war tactics) כי לא בחרבם ירשו ארץ — \"not by their sword did they inherit the land\" (Bnei Korach) Yet ויכהו ישראל לפי חרב — Israel struck Sichon by the sword The resolution: \"Not by their sword\" means they didn't rely on the sword alone — they relied on Hashem's providence ( כי ימינך וזרועך ואור פניך, כי רציתם ) while wielding it. Build balconies, use doctors, raise armies, wield swords — but know the outcome is from Hashem. The Snakebite / Cancer Paradigm אם קצצך עקרב או הכישך נחש — if a scorpion stings or a snake bites you (or, bar minan , a sudden cancer diagnosis): לא רק תסרוט את מקום הנשיכה ותגום תריאק — don't only lance the wound and take the antidote (do that, certainly!) אלא עליך לבטוח במי שבידו נפש כל חי — but also trust in the One in whose hand is all life ובתוך כדי שתיית הצרי ולקיחת התרופה — תבטח בה' While taking the medicine / undergoing the procedure — trust in Hashem that healing comes from Him. This does not diminish appreciation for the doctors and medicines — on the contrary, it should increase our recognition of how wondrously Hashem created these means. The Yehi Ratzon Over Medicine (Gemara Berachot, codified in Shulchan Aruch) יהי רצון מלפניך ה' אלהי שיהא עסק זה לי לרפואה \"May it be Your will, Hashem, that this engagement be for my healing.\" (Rabbi Kada: rarely seen practiced today — seemingly forgotten.) It connects the physical act to the recognition that healing comes from Hashem. When There Are No Means Left — Pure Bitachon אם תקרה למקום שלא תמצא בו דבר מרפא — if you reach a place with no remedy (bitten in the desert with no antidote; ill with no hospital): אז לא נשאר עליך אלא ביטחון בלבד — then only bitachon remains. The Sailor in the Storm מי שהפליג ללב ים, וזעף עליו הים, ונתקו פרשיו, ואבדה עצת מנהיגי הספינה וקברניטיה — לא נשאר אלא ביטחון בלבד The sails are torn, the sailors and captain are at a loss (a \"Titanic\" scenario) — only bitachon remains. Hashem knows the magnitude of the danger and, in His mercy, can save where all hope is lost — as He saved Chananyah, Mishael, and Azaryah from the furnace, and Daniel from the lions' den, biskhut godel tzidkatam (in the merit of their great righteousness): אלהי שלח מלאכה וסגר פום אריותא . A sobering note: Rabbi Avraham's own uncle, Rabbi David (the Rambam's brother), died at sea — and the Rambam grieved deeply for years. So bitachon does not always \"work\" as hoped. Why? — the next paragraph. Why the Righteous Sometimes Are Not Saved ויש עוונות מסתירים ומעכבים חסדו של אלהים — there are sins that conceal and block God's kindness; or, טעם כמוס — a hidden reason beyond human grasp. So ויבא הדבר שהוא ירא ממנו — the very thing one feared (and trusted Hashem to prevent) sometimes happens. The Examples The Ten Harugei Malchut (the ten martyrs, read on Tisha B'Av) — killed by wicked Romans Uriah killed by Yehoyakim; Zechariah killed by Yoash (whose own father had saved Yoash); other prophets killed by wicked Israelites אין סיבת הדבר קוצר יד ההשגחה — it is not because Hashem is unable to save — אלא סיבות נסתרות בחכמתו — but hidden reasons in His wisdom, judgments beyond human comprehension. The Gemara Story — Lulianus and Pappos (Turianus/Trajan) The wicked Roman ruler Turianus seized two righteous Jews (Lulianus and Pappos) and taunted: \"If you are the people of Chananyah, Mishael, and Azaryah — let your God save you from my hand as He saved them from Nebuchadnezzar!\" They answered: חנניה מישאל ועזריה צדיקים גמורים היו, ונבוכדנצר מלך הגון היה וראוי לעשות נס על ידו — ואנו נתחייבנו הריגה למקום \"They were perfectly righteous and Nebuchadnezzar was a worthy king for a miracle. But WE have been found liable for death by Heaven. If you don't kill us, Heaven has many other agents. Hashem has merely handed us to YOUR hand — to exact our blood from you.\" Two Versions of the Ending Our Gemara : He killed them — but then officials came from Rome and killed Turianus (his brain was crushed). The Be'er Avraham's text : reads that they were saved in their merit (שבזכותן ניצולו) — evidently he had a different girsa. The depth here: their knowledge of providence , their acceptance of Heaven's judgment , and a kiddush Hashem they were not even commanded to make. A Note on Enemies (Quick Halachic Aside) When enemies seek your life: Enemy When Not Imminently Pursuing Reasoning Non-Jewish enemy sworn to kill you Strike first if you have the ability (כמו בימי דוד) — combine bitachon with military action (Applied: Israel's neighbors sworn to its destruction — 100% permitted to act first) Jewish enemy not actively pursuing Do NOT attack — trust Hashem, escape elsewhere Like David , who twice caught Shaul and spared him (When someone breaks in actively threatening your life — בא במחתרת — you may kill even a Jewish pursuer to save yourself. The above is about non-imminent enemies.) The Sage's Answer (Tehillim 37) Asked whether to get informers/slanderers killed: דום לה' והתחולל לו — read homiletically: be silent before Hashem, and He will make them into חללים (corpses); השכם והערב עליהם בבית המדרש והם כלין מאליהם — pray morning and evening, and they will perish on their own. Spiritual Enemies — Different Rules For those who endanger the faith — machtiei harabim (those who cause the masses to sin), mosrim (informers), apikorsim (heretics) publicly desecrating Hashem's name — דינם כדין הגויים ; whoever can eliminate them is praiseworthy (in eras when this applied). If you lack the ability (e.g., in exile, under hostile government): only bitachon remains — pray, and tolerate the situation (יסבול את מצב הדחוק). The Chazon Ish : nowadays we don't act against heretics — that was for eras when Hashem's providence was more revealed. Rav Kook : today the only way to draw them back is with love. The Beautiful Gemara — Bruria and Rabbi Meir Rabbi Meir was tormented by biryonei (lawless bandits) in his neighborhood and prayed for them to die. His wife Bruria challenged him: מי כתיב \"יתמו חוטאים\"? \"חטאים\" כתיב! — Does it say \"may sinners be consumed\"? It says \"sins\"! Look at the verse's end: ורשעים עוד אינם — \"and the wicked are no more\" — because they will have repented. אלא בעי רחמי עלייהו דלהדרי בתיובתא — pray for them to do teshuvah (so the wicked are \"no more\"). Rabbi Meir prayed for their repentance — and they repented. A model of true yirat shamayim. Summary The Framework Bitachon now moves from theory to practice — but precise application in all scenarios is beyond full human grasp; we work in general categories Five categories (avoid serious harm / avoid lesser harm / attain essential / attain important / attain luxury) × physical or spiritual = TEN Two Prerequisites No bitachon without doing Hashem's will (against Yirmiyahu's \"Heichal Hashem\" falsehood) Spiritual bitachon always outranks physical — even non-essential spiritual over essential physical; Yaakov's (essential + spiritual) is the highest; reframing a request from mundane to spiritual transforms its quality Category One — Serious Danger Turn your heart to Hashem (all causes are subject to His will) Recall His promises through Tehillim 91 and 18 — to reset priorities , not as magic Pray (tefillah, tachanun) Use every natural means — best doctor, diet, tactics — the Torah itself commands parapets, doctors, armies, swords, while trusting Hashem The Yehi Ratzon over medicine connects the act to Hashem When no means remain — pure bitachon (Chananyah/Mishael/Azaryah; Daniel) — though it doesn't always \"work\" (the Rambam's brother at sea; the martyrs), for hidden reasons beyond human grasp (Lulianus & Pappos) Enemies : strike first against a non-Jewish enemy if able; spare a non-pursuing Jewish enemy; for spiritual enemies, act only where able and where they can't be returned — otherwise pray (Bruria: pray for the sins to end, not the sinners) Coming Up Next Week When may one rely on miracles (the final aspect of Category One)? Spiritual serious danger (Category One, spiritual side) Then Category Two — bitachon for important-but-non-critical matters (Schedule: Rambam tomorrow evening — thrones; Hilchot Shabbat Thursday — new chapter. Aiming to finish Perek 8 before Tisha B'Av.) B'Ezrat Hashem","jaMQ4dJNFww":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Shulchan Aruch · Orach Chaim Siman: 320 (ש\"כ) Topic: מלאכת דש — Threshing & Squeezing (Completion) · מלאכת צובע — Dyeing Tonight's Seifim: 18 (the flax stopper · psik reisha d'lo nicha lei) · 19 (saffron — no dyeing in food) · 20 (colored fruit on a handkerchief) Plus: Squeezing hair · baby wipes · toothbrushes · contemporary Tzovei'a questions (sunbathing · toilet disinfectants) Recap — The Structure of Siman 320 Part Seifim Content 1 א–ח Mefarek/Sechita: squeezing fruit, lemons, pickles 2 ט–יד Crushing ice and snow 3 טו–יז Sechita of Melaben vs. Dash — towels, cotton, sponges 4 (tonight) יח The flax stopper — completing Dash/Melaben (tonight) יט–כ Melechet Tzovei'a (dyeing) The Two Sechitot (Established) Melacha What You Want Dash (Mefarek) The liquid being squeezed out (squeeze fruit/cloth to USE the liquid) Melaben The item — squeeze to REMOVE the liquid from a garment you want clean Seif 18 — The Flax Stopper (חבית שפקקה בפקק של פשתן) The Text חבית שפקקה בפקק של פשתן לסתום נקב שבדפנה שמוציאים בו היין, יש מי שמתיר (להסירו) ואף על פי שאי אפשר שלא יסחוט — והוא שלא יהיה תחתיו כלי, דכיון שאינו נהנה בסחיטה זו הוי פסיק רישא דלא ניחא ליה ומותר. וחלקו עליו ואמרו: אף על גב דלא ניחא ליה, כיון דפסיק רישא הוא — אסור. The Case A wine barrel sealed with a flax/cotton stopper in a hole in its wall. The stopper is saturated with wine . Removing it (and reinserting it — per Mishna Berura) inevitably squeezes wine out of it. The Fundamental Machloket — Psik Reisha D'Lo Nicha Lei Opinion Ruling Reasoning The Aruch (early Rishon, 1000+ years ago) Permitted (if no vessel underneath, so wine goes to waste) A psik reisha in which you have no benefit (לא ניחא ליה) is permitted — even for a d'Oraita melacha — because Shabbat requires מלאכת מחשבת (purposeful, beneficial work) Tosafot / majority Forbidden Even with no benefit, an inevitable consequence (psik reisha) is assur (at least d'Rabbanan) The condition \"no vessel underneath\" (שלא יהיה תחתיו כלי) is critical. With a vessel collecting the wine, it's נחא ליה (beneficial) — real Dash, potentially d'Oraita. The whole discussion is only about wine going to waste on the floor. The Classic Illustration Washing your hands in a friend's garden: it's Zoreia (planting/watering) from the Torah, but you get no benefit — you just want clean hands, while inevitably watering the garden. Aruch: permitted (lo nicha lei, even d'Oraita). Shulchan Aruch/majority: forbidden (still assur, though not d'Oraita). Three Principles Learned from This Seif Principle 1 — There Is NO Libun With Other Liquids (אין ליבון בשאר משקין) Since the wine here is red wine , why isn't pulling the stopper a problem of Melaben (cleansing the stopper of liquid)? Because the Shulchan Aruch rules there is no Libun with liquids other than water. Squeezing wine out of a cloth doesn't \"launder\" it — it's still stained and smelly. The Rambam held there IS libun with other liquids; the Shulchan Aruch rules against the Rambam. (Proof: if there were libun with wine, it wouldn't matter whether the wine goes to waste — pulling the stopper would inherently be Melaben. The fact that the Shulchan Aruch debates only the Dash/waste angle shows he holds no libun with wine.) Principle 2 — Dash That Goes to Waste Is Still Dash D'Rabbanan Even though the wine here (a) has no libun and (b) goes to waste, the Shulchan Aruch brings the main opinion that it's still forbidden — because Dash performed where the liquid goes to waste is still assur d'Rabbanan. Principle 3 — Psik Reisha D'Lo Nicha Lei Is Assur (Not Like the Aruch) The Shulchan Aruch rules mainly like the second opinion: a psik reisha is forbidden even when you have no benefit — not like the Aruch who permits it. (Not d'Oraita, but still assur.) The Biur Halacha's Clarification (Rabbeinu Tam's Framework) Two kinds of sechita: Water from a garment → Melaben (the water goes to waste; not Dash) Other liquids (wine) → the reverse: no Melaben (no cleansing) — the issue is only Dash , and only when you need the squeezed liquid So with the wine barrel, the issue is only Dash ; and since the wine goes to waste, it falls outside Dash for those who hold so. The entire seif is written only according to the poskim who hold no libun with other liquids. According to those who equate all liquids with water (especially wine), pulling the stopper would be forbidden from the Torah (Melaben). The People's Lenient Practice (והעולם נוהגין היתר) People are very lax — some even pull the stopper and collect the wine in a vessel underneath (which no one permits). The only justification (לימוד זכות): The stopper has a handle (בית אחיזה) projecting out, so the hand doesn't touch the saturated part — like a sponge with a handle (Seif 17). But this is a weak argument (יש בה גמגום), because unlike a sponge (where you only wipe with the surface), here you're pulling the stopper out and it squeezes. Therefore: at minimum, ensure there is no vessel underneath to collect the wine. White Wine (Side Point) The Taz (Rav David Segal) holds the Shulchan Aruch's leniency is only for red wine ; white wine has the status of water → Melaben would apply. This is questionable (white wine still smells of wine — is squeezing it really \"laundering\"?). Psik Reisha D'Lo Nicha Lei Everywhere ELSE in the Torah (Mishna Berura 53) אבל חיובא ליכא בפסיק רישא דלא ניחא ליה לכולי עלמא — everyone agrees there's no Torah liability on Shabbat אבל... שאר איסורי תורה — דעת הרא\"ש דלא ניחא ליה לכולי עלמא אסור, ואיסורו מן התורה The whole leniency (or near-leniency) of psik reisha d'lo nicha lei is unique to Shabbat — because Shabbat requires melechet machshevet (purposeful work). Everywhere else in the Torah, a psik reisha d'lo nicha lei is forbidden — and forbidden from the Torah. (Example: pouring wine on the Mizbe'ach inevitably extinguishes some of the fire — violating אש תמיד תוקד; even lo nicha lei, it's assur d'Oraita, because there's no melechet machshevet requirement outside Shabbat.) Additional Dash Topics Squeezing Hair Water is not fully absorbed into hair — so squeezing hair isn't true Dash. But it's assur d'Rabbanan because the water amasses in the hair, resembling Dash. (Universally agreed.) Drying Hair After a Shower/Mikvah Majority of poskim : Don't rub/squeeze the hair dry — pat (dab) only with the towel Ben Ish Chai (surprisingly lenient here) : Permitted to rub with a large towel — It's sechita k'le'achar yad (an unusual manner of squeezing) Squeezing hair is only d'Rabbanan The water goes to waste (no Dash) Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach also lenient: it's two d'Rabbanans (waste + hair), and the water is immediately absorbed into the towel (not visible/usable) → permitted Practical: Majority say pat only; there are opinions to rely on for rubbing with a towel. Baby Wipes (Know the Metziut!) Both Rav Moshe Feinstein and Rav Shlomo Zalman originally permitted baby wipes — but a student reports Rav Shlomo Zalman retracted at the end of his life. Why the change? Modern wipes (Pampers, Huggies) hold far more liquid than the wipes of 40–50 years ago. Squeeze one today and significant liquid comes out. (Rabbi Kada recalls Rav Falk z\"l demonstrating this 20+ years ago in Gateshead.) The ruling: Squeezing a baby wipe → assur d'Oraita (real Dash/Mefarek — you use the extracted liquid) Using the surface moisture only (gently gliding, not pressing) → permitted Toothbrushes Densely packed bristles resemble hair → potential Dash concern. Some poskim : forbidden (hence Ashkenazi \"Shabbat toothbrushes\" with widely-spaced bristles → definitely permitted) Chacham Ben Tzion Abba Shaul & others : permitted — it has a handle (like the sponge with a handle), so it's not psik reisha The halacha : There's ample basis to use a toothbrush with water on Shabbat . (Toothpaste is more debatable — see the earlier shiur.) PART 2 — Melechet Tzovei'a (Dyeing) Background — Tzovei'a in the Mishkan Dyeing was done in the Mishkan (the coverings and the rams' hides were dyed). Tzovei'a vs. Writing Writing (Kotev) : putting text/marks on an underlying substance (paper) Tzovei'a (Dyeing) : enhancing/coloring an existing thing — \"coloring in\" Deepening an existing color is also Tzovei'a d'Oraita. This is why shoe polish on Shabbat is forbidden from the Torah (among several melachot) — even though the shoe is already black, rubbing in more makes it darker/shinier = Tzovei'a. Likewise makeup/lipstick (intensifying the lip color) is Tzovei'a d'Rabbanan, even though it fades by the next day (אינו מתקיים → d'Rabbanan, not d'Oraita). Seif 19 — Saffron in Food (No Dyeing in Food) ליתן כרכום בתבשיל מותר, ואין לחוש משום צובע, דאין צביעה באוכלין One may put saffron in a cooked dish; no concern of dyeing, because there is no dyeing with foods. The Universal Principle: אין צביעה באוכלין Everyone agrees: there is no dyeing prohibition with foods. Two reasons (Rav Ben Tzion Abba Shaul): It's not the derech (normal way) to dye food — so even when it happens, Chazal didn't forbid it Food is meant to be eaten — all food eventually rots, so dyeing food can never be מתקיים (permanent) ; since permanence is impossible, Chazal didn't forbid it Application: Dipping bread into wine (reddening the bread) → permitted — ein tzevia ba'ochalin. Is There Dyeing With LIQUIDS? (יש צביעה במשקין?) The marquee case: adding red wine to white wine to color it. Opinion Ruling Rambam & most Rishonim / Shulchan Aruch No dyeing with liquids — permitted The Yere'im (Rabbi Eliezer of Metz) There IS dyeing with liquids (יש צביעה במשקין) — because the essence of Tzovei'a is making paint , which is itself a liquid The Halacha וכן מותר ליתן יין אדום בתוך יין לבן אף שמתאדם — one may add red wine to white wine even though it reddens (Mishna Berura) Chacham Ovadia : Permitted outright — even Mishna Berura holds mikar hadin it's permitted Even the stringent view permits it with a small shinui : since paint is normally made by putting water first then adding dye, reverse the order — put a little red wine in the cup first , then pour the white wine. Everyone permits this. Ben Ish Chai (machmir) : Requires the shinui — always add the color-base first, then the liquid (e.g., making Ribena/squash: water first, then concentrate → reverse it) Intentionally Coloring Food or Drink ואפילו אם מכוון לכתחילה לעשות מראה... גם כן מסתבר דאין להחמיר (Mishna Berura) Examples: Pesach : adding red wine to white wine to fulfill the inyan of red wine at the Seder (recalling the blood) A child's yogurt : adding food coloring to make it purple so the baby will eat it Opinion Ruling Mishna Berura / Biur Halacha / Chacham Ovadia / majority Permitted even when intentionally coloring (food OR drink) Nishmat Adam / Chayei Adam / Kitzur (Gantzfried) Wanted to forbid when done intentionally Bottom line: permitted even if your intent is to make it colorful. Where Coloring IS Forbidden ומכל מקום אין רשאי לעשות מראה ביין ודבש שיקנו ממנו — וכל שכן שלא לשים סממנים בצלוחית מים להעמיד בחלון נגד השמש, דחייב חטאת Commercial purpose (coloring wine/honey to sell it) → forbidden Coloring a NON-edible liquid (e.g., dye drops in a decorative bowl of water for display) → forbidden, possibly d'Oraita (chayav chatat) Key clarification: Ein tzevia b'mashkin applies only to liquids you will drink . Water for display/decoration is not \"mashkin\" in this sense. Likewise children's craft pasta that's dyed and dried to last → that's not edible-in-practice → forbidden . Seif 20 — Colored Fruit and the Handkerchief יש מי שאומר שאוכל תותים או שאר פירות צבועין צריך להזהר שלא יגע בידיו הצבועות בבגדיו או במפה משום צובע. אבל אם צובע פיתו במשקה הפירות — לית לן בה, דאין צביעה באוכלין. The Case Eating strawberries / beetroot / mango → your hands are stained red/purple/yellow. One should be careful not to wipe the colored hands on clothing or a napkin (because of Tzovei'a). (Dipping bread in the fruit juice is fine — ein tzevia ba'ochalin.) Why This Should Actually Be Permitted — Several Layers Layer 1 — Psik Reisha D'Lo Nicha Lei B'Issur D'Rabbanan Drying your hands inevitably colors the cloth → psik reisha You have no benefit — you want clean hands, you're dirtying the cloth → lo nicha lei The coloring is not permanent → d'Rabbanan Psik reisha d'lo nicha lei on a d'Rabbanan = permitted Layer 2 — \"צריך להזהר\" Means Caution, Not Prohibition (Chacham Ovadia / Chida) The Shulchan Aruch wrote tzarich lizaher (\"one should be careful\") — which, per the Chida (Machzik Beracha) , means a commendable caution, NOT a prohibition. Mikar hadin it's permitted; he recorded it only because the Yere'im said it. Layer 3 — The Radbaz: צביעה דרך לכלוך (Coloring in a Dirty Manner) Rabbi David ben Zimra : This isn't Tzovei'a at all. The essence of Tzovei'a is precise, enhancing, artful coloring — done to beautify . Here you're dirtying a cloth while cleaning your hands, with no harmony or precision (a bit of red here, purple there). Coloring in a dirty manner is simply not the melacha of Tzovei'a. → Permitted. Red vs. White Handkerchief Posek View Magen Avraham A red handkerchief is worse — you lose the Radbaz's \"dirtying\" sevara (you're not dirtying a red cloth) Eliya Rabba A red handkerchief is better — red-on-red isn't really coloring anything The Halacha Mishna Berura : Rely on the Radbaz where it's difficult (eating outside, no tap/tissues nearby); but mikar hadin be strict with a white handkerchief Chacham Ovadia : Mikar hadin permitted — it's psik reisha d'lo nicha lei (only a zehirut) AND tzevia derech lichluch (Radbaz's strong sevara). המחמיר תבוא עליו ברכה , but it's fully permitted to wipe colored hands on a white (or red) handkerchief Disposable Tissues — Permitted by All (Shulchan Aruch HaRav) Even if one is strict, the prohibition would only apply to a reusable cloth (handkerchief, tablecloth). Disposable tissues are permitted by everyone. Proof (Ba'al HaTanya): A woman's bedikah cloth — checking for blood inevitably \"colors\" the cloth. How is this permitted? Because the bedikah cloth is disposable — no Tzovei'a concern. So anything disposable is fine. Practical hierarchy: Tissues (best) → handkerchief → water → (last resort) wipe on arm/leg. PART 3 — Contemporary Tzovei'a Questions Question 1 — Sunbathing / Tanning on Shabbat The case: Intentionally sitting in the sun on Shabbat afternoon to get a tan (not merely incidentally while reading). The Minchat Yitzchak (Rabbi Yitzchak Weiss, Manchester Av Beit Din, ~60 yrs ago) — FORBIDS Five reasons, each difficult: # Argument The Counter 1 Refuah (medicine) on Shabbat But דרך בריאים — things normal healthy people do are permitted; everyone does this 2 Tzovei'a (dyeing your skin) See the grama response below 3 Causing oneself pain Valid — if you suffer in the sun for the tan, that's against oneg Shabbat → wrong 4 Hashash of using creams (memareiach) Just be careful not to spread creams in a forbidden way 5 Uvda d'chol (weekday activity) Vague catch-all Chacham Ovadia (and Rav Shlomo Zalman) — PERMIT On the Tzovei'a argument: it's grama (indirect) — coloring normally happens immediately when you paint; here it takes hours. → permitted Rav Shlomo Zalman: you're completely passive — just sitting back while it happens by itself; how can that be a melacha? (Counter-thought: just as planting (Zoreia) is not called grama even though the seed sprouts days later — because that's the nature of the melacha — one could argue tanning's slowness is also the nature of Tzovei'a (vat-dyeing took time). The response: vat-dyeing was one form, but the tzura of the melacha is immediate coloring.) Ruling: Mikar hadin permitted to sunbathe/tan on Shabbat — provided it's enjoyable (not painful) and creams aren't applied in a forbidden manner. (Moot for those whose skin doesn't tan.) Question 2 — Toilet Disinfectants / Colored Hygiene Blocks In the Cistern (back tank) → PERMITTED by all Flushing sends water out; new water enters and gets colored by the block. It's a psik reisha (you flush to remove waste; coloring is inevitable) AND it's psik reisha b'grama — the coloring happens gradually (seconds/half a minute to collect) Psik reisha b'grama is permitted by all → no problem leaving a cistern disinfectant in on Shabbat In the Bowl itself → MORE QUESTIONABLE (homework for next week) Flushing colors the water in the bowl. Can't use ein tzevia b'mashkin (this isn't a drinkable liquid). So why might it be permitted (or forbidden)? Rabbi Kada left this as an open question to think through using the principles learned — and what to do if one already left it in (may one still flush?). To be resolved at the start of next week's shiur, then on to Melechet Tochein. Summary of Tonight's Halachot Seif 18 — The Flax Stopper The Aruch : psik reisha d'lo nicha lei is permitted (even d'Oraita); Shulchan Aruch/majority: forbidden Three principles: (1) no Libun with liquids other than water; (2) Dash that goes to waste is still d'Rabbanan; (3) psik reisha d'lo nicha lei is assur Psik reisha d'lo nicha lei is lenient only on Shabbat (melechet machshevet); everywhere else it's assur d'Oraita Squeezing hair (pat, don't rub — though there's room to be lenient); baby wipes (surface only, never squeeze); toothbrush with water (permitted) Seifim 19–20 — Tzovei'a Topic Ruling Saffron / dyeing food Permitted — אין צביעה באוכלין (universal) Red wine into white wine (liquid) Permitted (Shulchan Aruch); even stringent view permits with reversed order; Ben Ish Chai requires the shinui Intentionally coloring food/drink Permitted (majority) — Pesach wine, child's yogurt Commercial / non-edible liquid coloring Forbidden (possibly d'Oraita) Colored hands on a handkerchief Mikar hadin permitted (psik reisha d'lo nicha lei + Radbaz's tzevia derech lichluch); disposable tissues permitted by all Sunbathing/tanning Permitted (grama; passive) — if enjoyable, creams used properly Toilet disinfectant in cistern Permitted (psik reisha b'grama) Toilet disinfectant in bowl Open question — next week Coming Up Next Week Resolving the toilet-bowl disinfectant question (and what to do if left in) Beginning Melechet Tochein (grinding) and subsequent melachot B'Ezrat Hashem — Siman 320 Complete","4f1rq8NbsAc":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: 1 · Chapter 6 (completed) & Chapter 7 Topic: Ish & Isha — Is God Male or Female? · Yalad and the Power of a Teacher · Shedim Reinterpreted Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Recap — The Arc of Section 1 So Far Chapters Theme 1–2 Tzelem Elokim; Adam before and after the sin (emet/sheker → tov/ra) 3 Temunah vs. Tavnit — images of God 4 The verbs of seeing God — sight vs. insight 5 Caution in ascending to the divine; the burning bush (four readings); Moshe vs. the nobles 6 (begun last week) Ish & Isha — terms borrowed for male/female creatures, then for anything that attaches/receives. Ish = Tzura (form); Isha = Chomer (matter). All existence is the union of form and matter Recap of Chapter 6's Core (Last Week) Ish/Isha primarily = human male/female; borrowed for animals (מכל הבהמה... איש ואשתו = simply male and female); then isha borrowed for anything designated to attach/receive (the Mishkan curtains: חוברות אשה אל אחותה) Ish = Tzura (form, the affector/mashpia); Isha = Chomer (matter, the affected/nishpa) Per Yesodei HaTorah: nothing exists as form-alone or matter-alone — all existence is their union לזאת יקרא אשה כי מאיש לוקחה — the woman as receptacle/manifester of the man Implications: equal but different (egalitarianism-as-sameness is un-Torah); marriage as completion (לבשר אחד; one without a spouse lacks shalom = shleimut , completeness) PART 1 — Completing Chapter 6: Is God Male or Female? The Verse — \"Hashem Ish Milchamah\" ה' איש מלחמה, ה' שמו \"Hashem is a man of war, Hashem is His name\" (Shirat HaYam) Obviously not a physical man. So what does it mean that Hashem is a \"man of war\"? Why God Is Usually Referred to in the Masculine Hakadosh Baruch Hu is primarily the משפיע (affector/influencer/giver) , rather than the affected. That is why Hashem is normally referred to in the masculine — He is the mashpia, the affector. What About the Feminine References to God? We Sefardim say נקדישך ונעריצך (feminine: \"we will sanctify You\"); many liturgical references are feminine. Does this mean Hashem is affected by us? Absolutely not. Anything that is affected is incomplete — which would contradict God being shalem (complete and unchanging). When we refer to Hashem in the feminine, we are NOT referring to God Himself, but to a manifestation — the Shechinah , etc. God Himself is never feminine, because He cannot be affected. In essence, God is neither masculine nor feminine — but insofar as we assign Him a gender, it is masculine, because He is primarily the affector. The Kli Yakar (Rabbi Shlomo Laniado, ~500 years ago) (Brought via Rabbi Daniel Korobkin's Moreh shiurim.) On the last verse of Bereishit Chapter 1 — and the question: Why do we begin Friday-night Kiddush with \"Yom HaShishi\" before \"Vayechulu\"? The Hidden Name The phrase יום הששי ויכלו השמים — the final letters spell: יום הששׁ י ו יכלו ה שמים → י-ה-ו-ה (the four-letter Name) וכפי הפשט חתם על מעשה בראשית בשם של י-ה המשותף בין איש לאשה \"He sealed the creation with the Name Y-H, which is shared between ish and isha.\" The Yud and the Hei The Gemara (Bava Batra): everything Hashem created is male or female (זכר ונקבה בראם). Hence Hebrew — unlike English — assigns gender to everything, even inanimate objects. Letter Gender In the words Names of God Yud (י) Masculine — the affector אִישׁ (Aleph-Yud-Shin) carries the Yud Masculine Names begin with Aleph (the beginning/affector): Adon-ai, Elok-im, Kel Hei (ה) Feminine — the affected אִשָּׁה (Aleph-Shin-Hei) carries the Hei Feminine Names (Shechinah, Shakkai, Shabbat) relate to the end of the alphabet (the Shin/affected) (This is Rabbi Tatz's framework: Yud = masculine, Hei = feminine.) The Kli Yakar's Point כי כל זכר דמיון למשפיע, וכל נקבה דמיון למושפע \"Every male resembles the affector; every female resembles the affected.\" Everything in the universe contains an affecting side and an affected side. In the creation of a child: the man gives the zera (the tzura — the conceptual/theoretical), and the woman receives it and over nine months develops the chomer (the matter). הקדוש ברוך הוא המשפיע הראשון... אבל הוא יתברך אינו מושפע מזולתו \"Hashem is the first affector... but He Himself is never affected by another.\" The Kli Yakar holds God is innately masculine — always referred to as \"He\" — because He is the eternal mashpia, never affected. (Feminine references = manifestations like the Shechinah, not God Himself.) The master point: the universe is binary — male or female, affector or affected. There is no non-binary. This is why creation is \"sealed\" with Y-H: existence is complete only when it is both male and female. (The Vav-Hei of the Name, the Kli Yakar adds, relate to the future/Olam Haba — beyond our scope here.) Closing Chapter 6 — Ach and Achot Too הרי התבאר לך שגם אחות ואח הם רב-משמעיים על דרך ההשאלה כמו איש ואשה Brother and sister are likewise multi-meaning, borrowed terms. Achot — from the Mishkan curtains (חוברות אשה אל אחותה — \"each to her sister\") Ach — likely from the Keruvim: ופניהם איש אל אחיו (\"their faces, each man to his brother\") — denoting their character as mashpiim (affectors), facing one another Just as ish/isha are metaphorical and multi-meaning, so are ach/achot . Why Is This Chapter Here? (The Placement Question) The first five chapters dealt with incorporeality . Chapter 6 seems to digress. Why? The Narboni's Answer It continues the incorporeality theme because of ה' איש מלחמה — Rambam wants to clarify that \"man of war\" doesn't mean God is a male, but that He is the mashpia (affector) — a borrowed term, not an anthropomorphism. The Simpler/Preferred Answer Rambam told us in his introduction he would also explain Ma'aseh Bereshit (the workings of creation/physics). Just as Chapters 1–2 covered both incorporeality and the creation narrative (Adam's sin), ish and isha are central to the creation narrative — what marriage is, the binary structure of existence. This is Ma'aseh Bereshit: understanding the physics of the world Hashem created. Chapter 6 Summary Ish/Isha are borrowed for male/female creatures and, crucially, for anything that attaches — ish the affector, isha the receptacle. All existence reduces to ish/isha = tzura/chomer. From this: the bond of marriage, the binary universe, why God is usually masculine (the affector), and that ach/achot are likewise metaphorical terms. PART 2 — Chapter 7: Yalad, the Power of a Teacher, and Shedim This chapter continues from Chapter 6: a man and woman unite — what is the product? And it ties directly into Rambam's halachic works. The Five Meanings of יָלַד (Yalad — \"to give birth\") # Meaning Proof Text 1 Literal birth — offspring of man and woman וילדו לו בנים (Ki Tetzei) 2 Creation of natural phenomena בטרם הרים יולדו — \"before the mountains were born\" (Tehillim) 3 The earth sprouting (rain causing growth) הגשם... הולידה והצמיחה — rain \"gave birth to\" the earth (Yeshayahu) 4 New events occurring כי לא תדע מה ילד יום — \"you don't know what the day will birth\" (Mishlei) 5 Birthing new ideas / views — being taught יחבל און... וילד שקר — conceiving and \"birthing\" falsehood (Tehillim); ובילדי נכרים יספיקו — adopting the ideas of the nations (Yeshayahu, per Yonatan ben Uziel) The Power of a Teacher — Meaning 5 in Depth מי שלימד אדם דבר מה והקנה לו דעה, הוא כאילו הוליד את האיש ההוא One who teaches a person and imparts knowledge to him — it is as if he gave birth to that person. This is why the disciples of prophets are called בני הנביאים (the \"sons of the prophets\") — not biological sons, but students. The Gemara (Sanhedrin 19b) כל המלמד את בן חברו תורה, מעלה עליו הכתוב כאילו ילדו One who teaches another's child Torah — Scripture credits him as if he birthed the child. Proof: Bamidbar opens ואלה תולדות אהרן ומשה (\"these are the offspring of Aharon and Moshe\") — yet then lists only the sons of Aharon (Nadav, Avihu, Elazar, Itamar). Why are they called Moshe's offspring too? אהרן ילד, ומשה לימד — לפיכך נקראו על שמו Aharon birthed them; Moshe taught them — therefore they bear his name too. Illuminating the Verse in Toldot ואלה תולדות יצחק בן אברהם — אברהם הוליד את יצחק The apparent redundancy is resolved: Yitzchak was Avraham's son both physically and intellectually — Avraham birthed him and taught him his Torah and worldview. The Halachic Payoff — Saving Your Teacher Before Your Parent The Mishnah (end of Bava Metzia Ch. 2): if your parent and your rav are both drowning and you can save only one — you save your rav first. The Qualification (Gemara) This applies only to your rabo muvhak — your primary teacher from whom you learned the majority of your Torah and intellect. (Rare today: in earlier times one teacher took a student from alef-bet all the way up. Now we pass through many teachers, so a true rabo muvhak is uncommon.) Why This Makes Sense (via the Rambam) A rabo muvhak gave birth to you — just as your parent did. Your parent birthed you into a life of 120 years; your teacher birthed you into Olam Haba — infinite time. So the teacher's \"birthing\" has the longer-lasting effect. The Further Halacha If your father also taught you Torah (even not the majority), you save him first — because he birthed you both physically and (partly) spiritually . Only when the father taught little, and the rabo muvhak gave you the majority of your Torah, do you save the rav first. This is also the foundation of the premium on kavod for teachers and elders (מפני שיבה תקום והדרת פני זקן; אפילו לימדו אות אחת חייב לכבדו) — they are, in a sense, one's parents. The Cryptic Cross-Reference — \"When We Discuss Ben \" Rambam says he'll explain more \"when we discuss the term ben (son)\" — but he never does (Rav Kapach notes this). Rav Makbili's Beautiful Suggestion Notice: for literal birth (meaning 1), Rambam cited a late verse (Ki Tetzei: וילדו לו בנים) — skipping the obvious earlier one in the creation narrative itself: בעצב תלדי בנים \"In pain you shall bear children\" (Bereishit 3, the consequence to Chava) Why skip it? Deliberately — so that we connect the dots ourselves: be'etzev teldi banim is not only a curse but a reality — that with hard work (etzev), one can \"birth\" ideas and concepts in those who come after, and they become one's banim . Through toil, a person births many \"children\" far beyond biological offspring — by teaching. The Climax — Adam, Shet, and the Meaning of Shedim The Verse ויחי אדם שלושים ומאת שנה, ויולד בדמותו כצלמו, ויקרא את שמו שת Adam lived 130 years, and birthed in his likeness and image, and called him Shet. Rambam's Reading For Adam's first 130 years (after the sin), his children did not attain the true human form — they lacked the full tzelem and demut (the intellectual form, per Chapters 1–2). Only Shet — whom Adam taught and gave understanding — attained human completeness. Hence specifically of Shet: birthed in his likeness and image. Adam needed 130 years to do teshuvah and develop himself enough to transmit tzelem Elokim properly to a descendant Shet = from yashat/shitah — foundation ; he is the next foundation of the world after Adam and Chava, because the world is founded on those with correct intellect (Rav Nebenzahl) (Rabbi Korobkin suggests this could even engage the evolution question — the pre-Shet \"incomplete humans\" as a separate matter; a discussion for elsewhere.) The Final Paragraph — Worse Than an Animal כל מי שלא השיג אותה הצורה... אינו אדם, אלא בעל חיים בעל תבנית אדם ותארו One who has not attained that [intellectual] form is not a human — but an animal in the figure (tavnit) of a human. And in fact worse than an animal : כי כוח המחשבה ושיקול הדעת... משתמש בהם לצורך תחבולות הגורמות לרעות ולנזקים Because the intellect meant for perfection is used instead for schemes that cause harm — a capacity no animal has. והרי הוא כדבר הדומה לאדם המחקה אותו He is like something resembling a human, imitating one — not truly human. This explains human criminality and immorality, which we don't find among animals: humans can turn their intellect to destructive ends. The Gemara (Eruvin 18b) — Reinterpreted כל אותן השנים שהיה אדם הראשון בנידוי, הוליד רוחין ושדין ולילין All the years Adam was \"excommunicated,\" he birthed spirits, demons, and lilin. The Gemara clarifies: during 130 years Adam separated from his wife (a chasid doing teshuvah) — so the \"births\" were from nocturnal emissions. Rambam's Figurative Reading — What a Shed Actually Is Rambam reads this entire Gemara as a metaphor (against the Kabbalistic literal reading) : A shed (demon) is not a spiritual entity coming to get you. It is a sub-human — a person with the physical figure of a human but undeveloped or corrupted intellect. For 130 years Adam birthed sub-humans — creatures with eyes, mouth, arms, legs, but without realized tzelem Elokim . That is what \"ruchin, shedin, lilin\" means. Only at 130, after true teshuvah, did he birth Shet — bidmuto ketzalmo . The Narboni's Sharp Point Many religious people spend their lives on segulot and incantations to ward off imperceptible shedim — and thereby turn their attention away from the real shedim : the human beings around us with corrupt characters and bad influence. Those are the demons to avoid. The Sweep of Supporting Sources — Rambam's Consistent Reinterpretation The Metzora Sits Shiva for Himself (Rav David Fohrman) The leper is cast out, grows hair, tears clothes, conveys tumah almost like a corpse. Why? When a person dies, the family mourns a vacuum. The metzora mourns himself — still physically alive and human in body, but having lost his tzelem Elokim , become animal-like. He sits shiva for himself. \"Cursed Is He Who Lies With an Animal\" (Pesachim) ארור שוכב עם כל בהמה — said of one who marries the daughter of an am ha'aretz Not a mere ignoramus — but one who misused their intellect and became sub-human (no tzelem u'demut ). Hence the comparison to an animal. Min = A New Species (Rav Moshe Shapiro) We call heretics minim — species — because such a person is a new kind of creature : not human (no tzelem u'demut), yet not a four-legged beast. A human figure that is, in truth, an animal — a new min . Capital Punishment Is Not \"Murder\" Once Beit Din issues a guilty verdict deserving capital punishment, the person is no longer classified with the status of a human — so executing him is not Lo Tirtzach ; it is not killing a human. Rambam on Bava Kamma (Commentary on the Mishnah) On שור של ישראל שנגח שור של נכרי — פטור (a Jew's ox that gores a non-Jew's ox is exempt), Rambam (in his 20s) preempts the moral concern: מי שאין בו שלמות המידות האנושיות... אינו בכלל האדם על האמת One lacking human moral perfection is not truly included in \"human.\" Here Yisrael = one who developed character and bears tzelem u'demut ; nochri = one who did not — a sub-human . The exemption is because it's not goring between two full humans. (Maor's question — is this like Ir Nidachat? Yes — and there one must say that living among a majority so corrupt, one is inevitably influenced, so the whole city bears a degree of that sub-humanity.) Rambam's Pattern Across His Writings — Shedim Are Not Literal Source The Gemara Rambam's Reading Makkot Hatra'ah valid even על ידי עד שד (\"from a demon\") Not a demon — being warned without seeing the warner (e.g., hearing a voice from the other side of a building); a human then testifies to it. (Tosafot, by contrast, takes \"shed\" literally and constructs an okimta.) Shabbat (Bameh Madlikin) Extinguishing a candle מפני רוח רעה Ruach ra'ah = melancholy/depression (he writes \"melancholy\" in the Commentary) — the person needs to sleep and can't with the light on Gittin/Yevamot (agunah) Testimony of a death valid only if you saw the speaker or his shadow ( בבואה דבבואה ) — lest it be a shed Rambam omits this qualification entirely when codifying — because he didn't accept the concept of shedim Snakebite on Shabbat One may recite an incantation (lachash) on a wound Rambam: it doesn't work — permitted only psychologically , to prevent the sufferer's distress Rambam consistently reinterprets shedim, incantations, and amulets figuratively or psychologically. The Vilna Gaon's Sharp Objection The Gra was famously upset with the Rambam here. On Rambam's statement that כשפים, שמות, לחשים, שדים, וקמיעות — הכל הוא שקר (\"witchcraft, names, incantations, demons, and amulets are all false\"): כבר הכו על קדקדו, שמצינו הרבה מעשה בגמרא [בכשפים]... ופילוסופיה הטתו... לפרש הגמרא בדרך הליצה ולעקרם מפשטם The Gra: it was his philosophical leanings that bent him to explain these Gemarot metaphorically and uproot them from their plain meaning. וחס ושלום, איני מאמין בהם ולא מהם ולא מהמונם — אלא כל הדברים כפשטם, ויש להם פנימיות \"God forbid — I do not [side] with them; rather all these things are literal, and they have an inner meaning\" — not the philosophers' meaning. So the Gra holds shedim, amulets, and incantations are literal (with a deeper layer); Rambam reads them figuratively. (Some suggest Rambam had a different girsa — but the pattern is clearly deliberate.) Tonight's Summary Chapter 6 (Completed) — Is God Male or Female? Ish milchamah — God is \"masculine\" only in the sense of being the mashpia (affector) ; feminine references point to manifestations (Shechinah), never God Himself, who cannot be affected Kli Yakar : Kiddush's Yom HaShishi Vayechulu encodes the Name Y-H-W-H; Yud = masculine, Hei = feminine ; the universe is binary — sealed as \"male and female He created them\" Ach/Achot are likewise metaphorical (Keruvim; Mishkan curtains) Placement: this is Ma'aseh Bereshit — the physics of creation Chapter 7 — Yalad, Teachers, and Shedim Yalad = (1) birth, (2) creation of nature, (3) earth sprouting, (4) new events, (5) birthing ideas — teaching To teach someone is to give birth to them (בני הנביאים; Aharon birthed, Moshe taught; אברהם הוליד את יצחק) — grounding the halacha to save your rabo muvhak before your parent (he birthed you into infinite time) Be'etzev teldi banim (cryptic) — with toil, one births \"children\" through teaching Adam & Shet : for 130 years Adam birthed sub-humans; only after teshuvah did he birth Shet bidmuto ketzalmo — the new foundation A shed is a sub-human — a person in human figure lacking realized tzelem Elokim ; can be worse than an animal by misusing intellect The real demons are corrupt people around us (Narboni) — not imperceptible spirits Supporting reinterpretations: the metzora mourning himself; min = a new species; capital punishment not \"murder\"; the Bava Kamma ox; and across Makkot, Shabbat, Gittin, snakebite — Rambam reads shedim/incantations figuratively , against which the Vilna Gaon strongly objected Coming Up Next Continuing the Moreh — the next chapters of Section 1. (Chodesh Tov — Rosh Chodesh Tammuz.) B'Ezrat Hashem","Ud89T0FERbY":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 8 (ביטחון — Bitachon and Reliance) — Part 3 This Week: Cause and Effect (Sibah u'Mesovav) · The Three Groups of People · Hashgacha Pratit vs. Hashgacha Klalit · The Avot as the Model Recap from Parts 1 & 2 The Definition of Bitachon Complete reliance on Hakadosh Baruch Hu — He is Creator of all, and everything is subservient to His will. Living this way brings security and serenity ( betach ). It does NOT mean abandoning the mediums Hashem created — there IS cause and effect — but that cause and effect never excludes Hashem overseeing the entire system. The Three Categories of Bitachon (from Parts 1-2) Category Who Verdict 1 Prophets who lived off miracles by divine instruction Forbidden for anyone not so instructed 2 Deniers of providence (Aristotle) / those who affirm outwardly but trust intermediaries inwardly Wrong 3 Those who recognize Hashem oversees all the regular intermediaries — and can change them anytime The correct path Key Principle Established Living with Category 3 bitachon means never going overboard in pursuit of wealth or health — but this is NOT laziness. The Sages themselves worked; relying on miracles without authorization is arrogance. Framing — Tonight's Two Targets This section takes aim at two errors about cause and effect: Target 1 — The Mutakallimun (and those who deny causation) The Mutakallimun were Islamic philosophers (the school of Kalam), some of whom — in order to \"preserve\" God's total control — denied cause and effect entirely. On this view, causation is an illusion : You think clouds cause rain? Illusion. You think wind makes the leaf fall? It would have fallen anyway. All apparent causation is really only the direct will of Hashem at each moment. Rabbi Avraham says this is clearly wrong — it contradicts what we see and feel, and it causes religious people to be mocked by scientists and philosophers. (Apparently some Jews in his time held this; it's not found among the medieval Rishonim.) Target 2 — Those Who Believe Science Is Set in Stone Those who do believe in cause and effect and science (like we do) — but believe the laws of nature are fixed and unchangeable. The correct view: Believe in cause and effect, study science, be enlightened by it — while recognizing that Hashem oversees all of it and can change it at any time. PART 1 — The Chain of Cause and Effect (Sibah u'Mesovav) וכן אעיר תשומת לבך על מה שצריך להשיגו לפני מעלת הביטחון, כדי שביטחונך יהיה אמיתי ולא דמיוני I draw your attention to what must be grasped before the level of bitachon — so that your trust will be real and not imaginary. Many people think they have bitachon, but it's a nonsensical bitachon — because they don't believe in cause and effect. כי עליך לדעת שהשם יתעלה... ברא את העולם בתקנה על פי סיבה ומסובב, וקבע לכל תכלית כמה הקדמות Hashem created the world ordered by cause and effect, and set for every end-result a number of preliminaries. The Mashal — The Chain of Sustenance Rabbi Avraham traces the full causal chain of how we are fed: Link The Cause Human/animal life depends on nourishment (food) Nourishment comes from animals or vegetation Even animals we eat are sustained from the ground (vegetation) Vegetation grows from the earth + air + water + the sun's heat Water for the plants comes from rain and dew (condensed moisture) Rain comes from vapor rising and forming clouds — ואד יעלה מן הארץ The rising vapor is driven by the winds , by Hashem's will — רוח סערה עושה דברו So all sustenance traces back through a long natural chain: food → ground → rain → clouds → vapor → wind. The Reconciliation — Nature AND God's Will ירידת המטר באה איפוא מן הטבע כמו שאמרה התורה Rain comes through nature — and yet the Torah says it depends on Hashem: Direction Verse Rain given יפתח ה' לך את אוצרו הטוב — \"Hashem will open His good treasury\" Rain in its time ונתתי מטר ארצכם בעתו — \"I will give the rain of your land in its season\" Rain withheld ועצר את השמים ולא יהיה מטר — \"He will shut the heavens and there will be no rain\" The Resolution וכל הגורמים הטבעיים סופם לחזור אל עילת העילות All natural causes ultimately trace back to the Cause of all Causes — for to Him all actions return (ולו נתכנו עלילות). There IS cause and effect, and there IS God's will. They are reconciled because the entire causal system is itself Hashem's, traceable back to Him at every link. PART 2 — The Three Groups of People בני אדם לחלוקתם הם בשלוש כתות People divide into three groups. Group 1 — Those Who Ignore/Deny Cause and Effect This group lumps together several types: 1a — The Simply Uneducated (נבערים מדעת) אינם חושבים על סיבה ומסובב, אוכלים כמאכל הבהמה They don't think about cause and effect at all. They eat like animals — nourished only if food is in front of them , like monkeys or small children whose minds haven't matured. They can't reason \"if I plant this, I'll get that.\" About them: ומותר האדם מן הבהמה אין — \"man's advantage over the beast is nothing\" (Kohelet). 1b — The \"Religious\" Who Bury Their Heads (added later in the section) People who willfully avoid studying cause and effect, science, nature — because they fear that studying it (like the philosophers) will lead to heresy. They think faith means not thinking too much, and they consider themselves elite and pious for it. They derive all their understanding of God only through emotion/feeling or tradition (\"my father did this, my mother did this\") — never through contemplation. 1c — Those Who Deny Causation on \"Religious\" Grounds (the Mutakallimun type) Those who studied, understand causation, but deny it as a matter of \"true faith\" — claiming that believing in secondary causes detracts from God's providence. They say all causation is illusory. כי כפירת הפילוסופים היא מן המחקרים — \"the philosophers' heresy comes from their investigations\" — so (they reason) avoid investigation entirely. The Problem With All of Group 1 ובאו לידי כפירה בדברים שהשכל מעיד על נכונותם, ואפילו החושים מרגישים בהם They end up denying things the intellect attests to and even the senses perceive. They become a laughingstock (לשחוק... ולעג) before people of understanding. ואם חושב כן אחד מחכמי ישראל וחסידיהם — הרי הוא... חילול ה' ממש If a Torah scholar or pious person thinks this way — it is, in my opinion and that of anyone with a straight mind, an actual desecration of Hashem's name — as it says: \"These are the people of Hashem, yet they went out of His land\" (Yechezkel). (Rabbi Kada's example: dinosaur fossils. You can (a) be wholly uneducated about them, (b) know they exist but willfully refuse to engage \"lest it contradict Hashem,\" or (c) acknowledge them but offer some implausible explanation. All three fall into Group 1 — denying what the eye plainly sees. Generally, denying what is blatantly evident in the scientific world is a chillul Hashem.) Group 2 — The Enlightened Philosophers Who Stop Short הם בעלי דעת ובינה ומחשבה והתבוננות, שעסקו בחכמות והבינו את סיבותיהם וגורמיהם של כל דבר Wise, intellectual, scientifically educated people who understand the cause and effect of everything. Some of them even reached the Cause of all Causes — God — and grounded their faith on the link between cause, effect, and Creator. They are \"wise men and scientists who keep the Torah,\" outwardly. Their Fatal Flaw אלא שגם הללו קצרה דעתם מלהבין את האמת בשלמותה Their intellect fell short of the complete truth. They concluded: השם יתעלה אינו משנה שום דבר טבעי, ואינו מחולל שום סיבה מתוך מסובב Hashem changes nothing in nature, and brings about no cause outside the natural order. Once nature was created, it runs purely by fixed law. From this they came to: כפירה בידיעתו יתעלה, והכחשת ההשגחה הפרטית Denial of God's knowledge of particulars, and denial of individual providence — for what is there left for God to oversee if everything runs by fixed law? This is the view of the Greek philosophers (elaborated in the Moreh). About them: משיב חכמים אחור ודעתם יסכל — \"He turns the wise backward and makes their knowledge foolish.\" Group 3 — The Truly Religious Who Understand the Secret of Torah הם שומרי התורה המבינים את סוד התורה Before defining them, Rabbi Avraham clarifies whom he is NOT talking about: not the foolish among Torah-keepers (the Group 1b/1c types — the Amei Ha'aretz, \"לא ידעו ולא יבינו\" , those warned against being כסוס כפרד אין הבין — \"like a horse or mule without understanding\"). What Group 3 Actually Is מתבוננים אל הסיבות המשניות ומסיקים מהם מסקנות חוקי הטבע, כדרך שעשתה הקבוצה השנייה ... ואינם נופלים מהם They contemplate the secondary causes and derive the laws of nature — exactly like Group 2 (the enlightened scientists) — and are in no way inferior to them. They know everything the scientists know; they can hold their own in conversation and debate with philosophers and scientists. The Crucial Addition והשם הבינם על ידי תורתו את אשר נשגב מבינת החכמים והפילוסופים Hashem, through His Torah, granted them understanding of what is beyond the grasp of the philosophers — namely, that God does know particulars, does watch over the human condition, exercises individual providence (Hashgacha Pratit) over each person as befits him, and general providence (Hashgacha Klalit) over each species, as His wisdom and will determine. PART 3 — Two Kinds of Hashgacha (Providence) Rabbi Avraham alludes to two crucial lines (a major theme in the Moreh, developed further by the Ramchal in Derech Hashem): והשגחתו הפרטית על כל אחד ואחד כפי שראוי לפניו יתעלה ובהשגחתו על כל סוג מסוגי הטבע כפי מה שקבעו חכמתו ורצונו Type Applies To Nature Hashgacha Pratit (individual) Humans Detailed providence over each individual — but commensurate with the person's level , relationship, and covenant with Hashem Hashgacha Klalit (general) Species / animals Providence over the species as a whole, not the individual creature The Black Cab Analogy (Rabbi Kada) Imagine someone who owns 100 black cabs and, after five years, wants to downsize to 10. Suppose all 100 are identical — same mileage, same wear, same scratches. He randomly picks 90 to sell and keeps 10. He doesn't care about any individual cab — only that he has 10 functioning cabs to continue his business. That's Hashgacha Klalit : Hashem ensures (say) a certain number of sheep exist in the world, without attending to the individual pain, pleasure, or fate of each particular sheep. Hashgacha Pratit — detailed, individual providence — is reserved for human beings . And even among humans, the degree of detailed providence is commensurate with each person's level and relationship with Hashem. (A theme to be developed elsewhere.) PART 4 — How Group 3 Lives: Nature Runs by God's Will אך הוא יתעלה הנה הפועל והמסבב את הסיבות הללו But it is He who acts and brings about these causes. וברצונו מתנהלות הן בדרכיהן הטבעיות — and by His will they run in their natural ways וברצונו חורגות הן מדרכיהן הטבעיות — and by His will they deviate from their natural ways The Fire/Oil Illustration Normally fire burns with oil/wax, not vinegar — that's the law of nature. But that law operates only because Hashem wills it. Every time you light a candle, it works because Hashem wills it; and Hashem can, at any time, decree that the usual fuel won't ignite. When Moshe asked (in Beha'alotecha) how so much food could be provided in the desert, Hashem answered: היד ה' תקצר? עתה תראה היקרך דברי אם לא \"Is Hashem's hand short? Now you will see whether My word comes true.\" ולכן כולם תלויים בעילה הראשונה, עילת כל העילות — therefore everything depends on the First Cause, the Cause of all Causes. ולכן נקרא ה' צור העולמים — therefore Hashem is called the Rock of the worlds (the bedrock behind all that happens). The Constant Discipline ולפיכך מחזיקים בחזקה בעיקרון זה, ומשווים אותו נגד עיניהם, ואינם סוטים ממנו עקב הסתכלות מדעית They hold firmly to this principle and keep it before their eyes — and do not deviate from it because of scientific study (e.g., the philosophy of Aristotle and his colleagues), nor because of physical observation (e.g., studying the movements and phenomena of the heavens). They study deeply — and still trace it all back to Hakadosh Baruch Hu. PART 5 — The Avot as the Model ובכל הדברים האלו עושים הם כמעשי אבות In all these things they act as the Avot did. The Avot had complete bitachon — and yet engaged fully in natural means and stratagems , tracing everything back to Hashem: Patriarch The Natural Means / Stratagem The Recognition Avraham Devised a plan to save himself (told Sarah to say \"he is my brother\" before Paroh/Avimelech) — \"אמרי לי אחי הוא\" Yet relied on Hashem, not the scheme — and was rewarded with great wealth; a miracle struck Paroh (וינגע ה' את פרעה) Yitzchak Worked the land, shepherded, moved his dwelling because of the famine (did NOT sit passively saying \"it's all illusion\") Traced success to Hashem — rewarded with מאה שערים (a hundredfold) Yaakov Devised the elaborate scheme with the rods to obtain his share of the flock Yet ascribed it entirely to Hashem — ויצל אלהים את מקנה אביכם ויתן לי (\"God took your father's flock and gave it to me\") אך בכל מעשיהם לא סמכו על חשבונותיהם ויגיעתם, אלא רק על השם יתעלה In all their actions they did not rely on their calculations and toil — but only on Hashem. Therefore divine providence accompanied them in their natural endeavors, and at times open miracles beyond nature occurred — all in the merit of their true beliefs and their bitachon, not because of their knowledge and calculations. The Torah's Summary of This Worldview כי לא על הלחם לבדו יחיה האדם, כי על כל מוצא פי ה' יחיה האדם \"Man does not live by bread alone, but by all that issues from the mouth of Hashem\" We need bread; we must understand how it comes to be — and we know it comes ultimately by Hashem's decree. כי היא חכמתכם ובינתכם לעיני העמים \"For it is your wisdom and understanding in the eyes of the nations\" — the nations will see Israel's intellect and wisdom, in people who also recognize everything comes from Hashem. PART 6 — The Decisive Response to the Scientist The final paragraph — the perfect encapsulation of Group 3: ונמנים הם על אלו שאם יאמר להם הפילוסוף — they are among those who, if the philosopher says to them : \"Natural phenomena — the sprouting of plants, the birth of horses — have natural causes,\" they answer: בזאת צדקת — \"In this, you are correct\" (I fully agree with everything in your class) אך מוטעית סברתך שהסיבות הללו קבועות בלי תמורה — \"But you err in thinking these causes are fixed without change\" The natural order deviates from its course according to the will of the One who created nature and placed its power within these causes. The Three Proofs from Tanakh Natural Law When Hashem Suspended It Fire burns — He created fire and gave it the power to burn He nullified that power for Chananya, Mishael, and Azarya in the furnace — and strengthened it again afterward, so it burned the informers thrown in with them. (Same fire — but whether it burns depends on Hashem's will.) Humans need bread to live Yet Bnei Yisrael were sustained by the מן for forty years וכן משגיח השם יתעלה על מי שבוטח בו, עד כדי כך שמשנה לו סדרי בראשית Hashem watches over the one who trusts in Him — to the point of changing the very orders of creation for him — for the prophets and the pious, when they subdued their nature before Him. Summary The Core Teaching True bitachon requires understanding cause and effect — otherwise it is imaginary bitachon. The world runs by a real causal chain (food → ground → rain → clouds → vapor → wind), but the entire system traces back to the Cause of all Causes. The Three Groups Deny/ignore causation — the uneducated, the head-in-the-sand \"pious,\" and those who call causation an illusion. They deny what intellect and senses attest → a chillul Hashem when done by a Torah scholar. Enlightened philosophers who stop short — know all the science, even reach God, but conclude nature is fixed and unchangeable → deny individual providence. The truly religious (correct path) — know everything Group 2 knows, can debate the scientists as equals, but trace it all back to Hashem , who can change nature at any moment. Two Kinds of Providence Hashgacha Pratit — individual, for humans, commensurate with one's level Hashgacha Klalit — general, for species (the black-cab analogy) The Model The Avot engaged fully in natural means and clever stratagems (Avraham's plan, Yitzchak's farming, Yaakov's rods) — yet relied only on Hashem, and merited both natural success and open miracles. Man does not live by bread alone. Coming Up Next: Five (really ten) uses of Bitachon in different spheres of life — the practical application: in different scenarios (avoiding harm, pursuing benefit, etc.), what is the correct response for a person of true bitachon? (Schedule note: Rabbi Kada away next week Monday–Wednesday — no shiur Monday/Tuesday next week; this resumes in two weeks.) B'Ezrat Hashem","CC3IzIpPD0k":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: 1 · Chapter 5 (completed) & Chapter 6 (begun) Topic: Caution When Approaching the Divine · The Burning Bush Reinterpreted · Ish & Isha — Gender in Torah Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Recap of the Journey So Far Chapter Content Introductions How to learn the book, meshalim, the seven contradictions 1 Tzelem & Demut — the image of God = the intellectual faculty 2 Adam's sin — from objective truth (emet/sheker) to subjective good/bad (tov/ra) 3 Temunah vs. Tavnit — temunah with the divine = intellectual essence 4 The three verbs of seeing (ra'ah, habit, chazah) — sight vs. insight; with God, always insight 5 (begun last week) The introduction: like Aristotle, approach metaphysics with caution — requiring (1) training in logic and science, (2) refined character PART 1 — Chapter 5: The Burning Bush Reinterpreted The Scene (Shemot 3) Moshe is shepherding Yitro's flock. He sees: והנה הסנה בוער באש והסנה איננו אוכל The bush burning with fire — but the bush was not consumed Moshe says: \"Let me turn aside and see this great sight — why the bush is not burnt.\" Then: וירא ה' כי סר לראות — Hashem saw that he turned to look Hashem calls \"Moshe, Moshe,\" tells him to remove his shoes, and declares: אנכי אלהי אביך, אלהי אברהם אלהי יצחק ואלהי יעקב And then the critical verse: ויסתר משה פניו כי ירא מהביט אל האלהים And Moshe hid his face, for he was afraid to gaze at God (This continues directly from Chapter 4, where we learned the verb הביט.) The Four Levels of Interpreting This Verse Level 1 — The Crude/Heretical Reading ❌ Moshe physically sees God — a physical, corporeal God — and turns his face away in fear. Absolutely rejected. As established throughout these chapters, God has no corporeality. This reading is heretical. Level 2 — Saadia Gaon's Reading (Acceptable but Incorrect) From Emunot VeDe'ot (Essay 2, Chapter 12) : Hashem created a special light (the Kavod , the Shechinah ) which prophets see when Tanakh describes them \"seeing God\": וכאשר יראהו אחד מהם אומר: ראיתי כבוד ה' On this reading, Moshe saw a physical light — a created manifestation of God's presence — and was afraid to gaze at it. Rambam's verdict (end of the chapter): ואם ירצה אחד מהמתרשלים... להבין שראיית ה' היא ראיית אורות נבראים, מלאכים או אחרים — אין בזה נזק \"If one of those who do not reach the full level wishes to understand 'seeing God' as seeing created lights or angels — there is no harm in this.\" Not harmful, not heretical — but not the correct interpretation. (Rav Kapach identifies this passage as Rambam's implicit reference to Saadia Gaon.) Level 3 — The Correct Simple Reading (Rambam's Peshat) The entire episode — the bush, the fire, the voice, the dialogue — occurred in a prophetic vision, in Moshe's mind . Moshe intellectually perceives the entire experience, hears Hashem declaring Himself, and is afraid. The \"hiding of the face\" is his reaction within the prophetic vision. Level 4 — The Metaphorical Reading (The Point of This Chapter) על ענין זה נאמר: ויסתר משה פניו כי ירא מהביט אל האלהים \"Moshe was afraid to gaze at God\" = Moshe proceeded slowly, gradually, cautiously when entering the divine realm. This isn't about the bush at all — it's about Moshe's entire approach to metaphysics, theology, Ma'aseh Merkavah. At the start of his prophetic journey (age 80, with trepidation — very different from the Moshe of Bamidbar who challenges Hashem in dialogue), Moshe deliberately held back. He treaded with caution. Fascinating: Rambam is drawing a loose comparison between Aristotle (who opened his metaphysics with a plea for caution) and Moshe (whose hiding of the face WAS that caution). Moshe's Reward for Caution והשם יתעלה שיבחו על הדבר הזה... והאצילה מנעמו וטובו מה שהגיע לו בסוף — ותמונת ה' יביט Hashem praised him for this — and as a reward, later in his career Moshe attained the clearest possible apprehension of God — u'temunat Hashem yabit (as explained in Chapter 3: the highest intellectual understanding available to any human). The Gemara (Berachot 7a) בשכר שלוש זכה לשלוש In the merit of three things, he merited three things: In the merit of... He merited... ויסתר משה פניו — hiding his face קלסתר פנים — the radiant glow of his face כי ירא — being afraid וייראו מגשת אליו — the people's awe of him (Ki Tisa) מהביט — restraining his gaze ותמונת ה' יביט — gazing at the temunah of Hashem Caution at the beginning purchased clarity at the end. The Contrast — The Atzilei Bnei Yisrael (End of Mishpatim) The Verses ויראו את אלהי ישראל, ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת הספיר וכעצם השמים לטוהר They saw the God of Israel — and under His feet was like a sapphire brickwork, like the essence of heaven in purity ואל אצילי בני ישראל לא שלח ידו, ויחזו את האלהים ויאכלו וישתו Against the nobles of Bnei Yisrael He did not send His hand — and they beheld God, and ate and drank (Linguistic note: אצילי from אצל — \"to extend/set aside,\" as in Esav's cry הלא אצלת לי ברכה — \"have you not reserved a blessing for me?\" The Atzilim are those \"extended\" prominence — the nobles: the seventy elders plus Nadav and Avihu.) Rambam's Reading ואילו אצילי בני ישראל — התפרצו ושילחו מחשבותיהם , הם השיגו — אך השגה שאינה שלמה The nobles were impetuous — they \"burst forth\" and let their thoughts loose. They DID attain a prophetic experience — but an incomplete, flawed one. The Proof from the Verse Itself לא נאמר \"ויראו את אלהי ישראל\" בלבד — אלא \"ויראו... ותחת רגליו \" The Torah doesn't simply say \"they saw the God of Israel.\" It adds \"and under His feet\" — crude, anthropomorphic content. Why? כי מטרת הדברים אינה אלא להטיל ביקורת על ראייתם , לא לתאר כיצד ראו The verse's purpose is to CRITICIZE their vision, not describe it. The anthropomorphic content (\"His feet\"!) signals that their apprehension contained hagshamah — some corporeality, some flawed conception of God. (What exactly was the flaw? Rambam doesn't specify. Perhaps ascribing corporeality; others suggest they wrongly concluded there was pre-existent matter (hyle) alongside God — Plato's view, which Rambam rejects in Section 2. Either way: an error born of haste.) The Consequence כי מחויב בכך שהתפרצו לפני שהיו שלמים — הם התחייבו כליה Because they burst in before they were complete — they deserved destruction. Moshe prayed for them and the punishment was deferred: The seventy elders — died in the fire at Tav'erah (Beha'alotecha) Nadav and Avihu — burnt in the Ohel Moed (Shemini) Why Specifically Fire? — Two Interpretations Rav Makbili's Interpretation A contrast with Moshe: Moshe was cautious before the fire of the bush — they were not cautious — so they were destroyed by fire . Rabbi Kada's Suggested Interpretation (the more beautiful one) The nature of fire is impulsiveness. Fire is one of the most important things in life — warmth, light, energy. But when does fire become dangerous? When it moves too fast — when it spreads impulsively, without care for its surroundings. This is why Chazal compare anger to fire. Controlled displeasure has a function (as the Rambam and Rabbi Avraham teach in Ma'amar Al HaChassidut — a person shouldn't be a doormat); the destructive form is fire-anger — impetuous, consuming, careless. The nobles ascended to Hashem like fire — too fast, impulsively, without gradual care. Mida k'neged mida: they were consumed by the very quality of their ascent. All of them — the seventy at Tav'erah, Nadav and Avihu at the Ohel Moed — died by fire. The Midrash Confirms (Beha'alotecha) The Midrash asks: Why does Moshe complain (in Beha'alotecha) that he has no one to help him? He already had seventy elders from Mishpatim! Answer: Those elders had died — שאף הם הקלו ראשם בעלותם להר סיני לשכינה — ונשרפו They had acted with levity when ascending at Sinai toward the Shechinah — and were burnt. That's why Moshe needed seventy NEW elders. Nadav and Avihu — The Same Mistake Twice Why did Nadav and Avihu die earlier (Shemini)? The same flaw : they offered אש זרה אשר לא צוה — a fire that was not commanded. Whatever the precise sin (the interpretations are many), the common denominator: impulsiveness, disrespecting boundaries, moving too fast in spiritual matters. Abrabanel's observation (noted by Rambam): they died in the Ohel Moed — the very place of divine inspiration. The location highlights the lesson: divine closeness is good; approaching it too fast, too casually, kills. A Window into Elisha ben Avuya With this framework, we can see how Rambam would read the Gemara of the four who entered the Pardes . Elisha ben Avuya became Acher — a catastrophic spiritual breakdown. Rambam would interpret: he went too fast in the matters of the divine. The Kal VaChomer for Us ואם כך היה לגביהם — כל שכן לגבינו אנחנו הנחותים, ומי שלמטה מאתנו If this happened to the nobles of Israel — all the more so for us who are far lower (Rambam speaking of himself!), and those lower still (us, a thousandfold). The Required Preparation שראוי לו שיכוין עצמו ויתעסק בהשלמת הלימודים המכינים , ורכישת ההקדמות המטהרות את ההשגה מטומאתה — שהיא הטעיות — ואז ייגש להתבונן בכבוד הקודש האלוהי Complete the preparatory studies (logic, science — as per last week) Acquire the prerequisites that purify apprehension from its impurity — which is ERRORS Only then approach contemplation of the divine The Verse Reinterpreted — The Kohanim at Sinai וגם הכהנים הנגשים אל ה' יתקדשו, פן יפרוץ בהם ה' \"The Kohanim who approach Hashem must sanctify themselves, lest Hashem burst forth against them\" Simple reading: don't cross the physical boundary around Har Sinai. Deeper reading: The Kohanim — metaphorically, those who seek to experience the divine most intensely — must sanctify and prepare themselves. Don't cross the boundary too fast, too casually, too impulsively. Shlomo's Warning שמור רגליך כאשר תלך אל בית האלהים \"Guard your feet when you go to the house of God\" (Kohelet) The Gemara reads this practically. The deeper meaning per Rambam: guard your steps — tread slowly, cautiously, when approaching God. The Vilna Gaon's Parallel — The Ramp of the Mizbeach ולא תעלה במעלות על מזבחי \"Do not ascend My altar by steps\" — a ramp was required instead. The Gra explains: steps are jumps — fast, discontinuous leaps. A ramp is slow, gradual, continuous ascent. The altar's architecture itself teaches gradualism in approaching Hashem. The Stunning Reframe — Impurity = Intellectual Error Rambam said the prerequisites \"purify apprehension from its impurity — which is errors .\" This is a profound redefinition: tumah (impurity) = mistaken conceptions. The Source — End of Hilchot Mikvaot (Mishneh Torah) דבר ברור וגלוי שהטומאות והטהרות גזירות הכתוב הן ... ואף על פי כן רמז יש בדבר : כשם שהמכוון לבו לטהר — כיון שטבל טהור, ואף על פי שלא נתחדש בגופו דבר — כך המכוון לבו לטהר נפשו מטומאות הנפשות, שהן מחשבות האון ודעות הרעות ... The laws of purity/impurity are decrees of Scripture — yet there is a hint in them. Just as one who focuses his heart to purify becomes pure upon immersion (though nothing changed in his body), so one who focuses his heart to purify his soul from the impurities of the soul — which are wicked thoughts and bad notions ... Impurity = wrong thoughts, bad conceptions, intellectual error. Mikvah without intention is just jumping in water; the purification is in the turning of the mind. The Sharp Contemporary Application The masses experience \"God\" most intensely at... death. Shiva customs, covering mirrors, candle timing — suddenly everyone is meticulous. The rest of the year — indifference. (And unfortunately some rabbis cultivate exactly this.) That is precisely ascending to Hashem through tumah — through death, through error, through unprepared impulse — rather than through life, preparation, and clarity. The Final Paragraph — \"They Ate and Drank\" ואומר שגם מעשיהם התבלבלו בשל כך... ולכן נאמר: ויחזו את האלהים ויאכלו וישתו Because of their intellectual error (born of haste), the nobles' conduct also became confused — they tilted toward corporeal things. Hence the verse stresses: \"they beheld God — and ate and drank.\" Not literal gluttony at the moment of vision — the point is that their conception of God was materialized , and so their engagement with the divine was entangled with the physical. Mida K'neged Mida (As Always in Rambam) There is no external \"punishment\" in Torah — punishment is the built-in consequence of the act. They rushed the ascent → their apprehension came out corporeal → their experience of God was an eating-and-drinking one. The Contrast with Moshe — Perfect Symmetry On the mountain forty days and forty nights: לחם לא אכל ומים לא שתה Bread he did not eat, and water he did not drink The Nobles Moshe Rushed in, unprepared ויסתר משה פניו — held back, cautious Vision contaminated with corporeality (\"under His feet\") The purest apprehension (ותמונת ה' יביט) \"Saw God\" while eating and drinking Neither ate nor drank Consumed by fire Face radiant with light Chapter 5 Summary The verse \"Moshe hid his face, for he was afraid to gaze at God\" means — at the deepest level — that Moshe entered the divine realm slowly, gradually, carefully. The nobles, by contrast, had a vision — but a flawed, partially corporeal one, because they ascended without preparation. Their impulsiveness was repaid in kind: death by fire, the element of impulse. If that befell the nobles of Israel — all the more must we prepare: logic, science, refined character — purifying our apprehension from impurity, which is intellectual error — before approaching metaphysics and theology. PART 2 — Chapter 6: Ish & Isha (Begun) The Shortest Chapter — Five Lines After five chapters on incorporeality, a digression. The whole chapter: איש ואשה — מונחים שנקבעו בתחילה לגבר ואשה. לאחר מכן הושאלו לכל זכר ונקבה משאר בעלי חיים... לאחר מכן הושאל המונח \"אשה\" לכל דבר המוכן ומיועד להתחבר לדבר אחר ... הרי התבאר לך שגם אחות ואח הם רב-משמעיים על דרך ההשאלה כמו איש ואשה Ish/Isha originally = human male and female Borrowed for animal male/female: מכל הבהמה הטהורה תקח לך שבעה שבעה איש ואשתו (Noach) — \"each man and his wife\" = simply male and female Then isha borrowed further: for anything designated to join/attach to something else — proof: חמש היריעות תהיינה חוברות אשה אל אחותה — the Mishkan curtains attached \"each woman to her sister\" Conclusion: ach and achot (brother/sister) are likewise multi-meaning terms Side Point — The Gemara in Sanhedrin On \"ish v'ishto\" by Noach's animals, the Gemara asks: אישות לבהמה מי אית לה? (Do animals have marriage?) and answers: those that had not \"sinned\" (been promiscuous with other species). The Gemara reads \"man and wife\" as moral fidelity. Rambam reads it far more simply: a borrowed term meaning male and female — no marital union implied. The Crucial Line — What This Chapter Is Really About לכל דבר המוכן ומיועד להתחבר לדבר אחר \"...for anything prepared and designated to join with something else\" (Per Rav Kapach — and it's clear — this is the key sentence. The Narboni reads the chapter as teaching that masculine language about God isn't literal; but Rambam never mentions God here, so that's unlikely to be the point.) Ish & Isha = Tzura & Chomer (Form & Matter) The creation narrative represents all existence. Everything in the universe reduces to the Aristotelian pair: Ish Isha Concept Tzura — form Chomer — matter Mode Potential, conceptual, theoretical, vision Substance, receptacle, manifestation Function That which affects That which is affected and actualizes The Mishneh Torah Source (Yesodei HaTorah) לעולם אין אתה רואה גולם בלא צורה, או צורה בלא גולם You will never see matter without form, or form without matter The chair analogy: the wood/legs = chomer; the conception \"chair\" = tzura. Form alone is a dream; matter alone is a lump. Only together do they constitute an existing thing. Why Hebrew Is Gendered Everything in Hebrew is masculine or feminine — because everything in existence is binary: tzura and chomer, affecting and affected. There is no non-binary in the structure of reality as Torah presents it. (Rav Makbili suggests this underlies the Midrash that Kayin and Hevel were each born with twin sisters — creation as male-female pairing perpetuated into the second generation.) The Verse in Bereishit Illuminated In Bereishit Chapter 1: זכר ונקבה ברא אותם — created as zachar and nekevah (biological male/female). Only after Chava is taken from Adam does the Torah switch terms: לזאת יקרא אשה כי מאיש לוקחה זאת \"This one shall be called ISHA, for from ISH she was taken\" Isha is the receptacle/manifester of Ish — chomer actualizing tzura. The terminology itself encodes the form-matter relationship: the woman is designated to attach, to receive, to manifest in the world. The Torah Implications (Rabbi Kada's Development) 1. On Egalitarianism The expectation that men and women be identical — in roles, careers, expectations — is alien to Torah thought and against reality. Different does not mean unequal (that's a separate discussion) — but men and women are constitutionally different from the very structure of creation. The world is beginning to rediscover this. 2. On Marriage — Why It Completes a Person If ish/isha = tzura/chomer, marriage is fully intelligible: form without matter is nothing; matter without form is nothing. Only the combination exists. על כן יעזב איש את אביו ואת אמו ודבק באשתו והיו לבשר אחד \"Therefore a man leaves his father and mother and cleaves to his wife — and they become ONE FLESH\" One flesh = one complete existing entity. Hence: לא טוב היות האדם לבדו, אעשה לו עזר כנגדו \"It is not good for the human to be alone\" Without a spouse, neither can fully manifest. And the Gemara in Yevamot (the opening halacha of Even HaEzer): one without a spouse lacks many things — among them shalom . Shalom is from shalem — completeness, full identity, manifested wholeness. 3. On the Torah's Position on Homosexuality (A Deeper Layer) Beyond the standard reasons (the act itself; no children — as most Rishonim write): a same-sex relationship pairs tzura with tzura or chomer with chomer — two forms or two substances — which cannot complete each other. The person believes they have a life partner, but in the deep structure, neither can actualize the other. (Conjecture requiring further thought: perhaps this is why the Torah is more severe with male homosexuality — tzura-with-tzura may be a deeper incompletion than chomer-with-chomer.) 4. The Nuance — Both Within Each Person Man is primarily tzura; woman is primarily chomer. But every man has chomer within, and every woman has tzura within. There are moments when a man is the affected and a woman the affecting. This matters for understanding human emotion — no one is a single essence. Coming Up Next Week Completing Chapter 6 : The beautiful Kli Yakar on how we relate to Hakadosh Baruch Hu — is God male? female? neither? both? Chapter 7 : Shedim — demons. What do demons mean in Rambam's reading, and the meaning of \"Adam fathered in his likeness and image\" Tonight's Summary Chapter 5 — Caution When Approaching the Divine Four levels of \"Moshe hid his face\": (1) heretical-corporeal ❌, (2) Saadia's created light — acceptable, not correct, (3) prophetic vision in the mind — the peshat, (4) metaphorical: Moshe entered the divine slowly and cautiously His reward (Berachot 7a): the radiant face, the people's awe, ותמונת ה' יביט The nobles rushed in → flawed, partially corporeal vision (\"under His feet\"; \"ate and drank\") → destroyed by fire — the element of impulsiveness (mida k'neged mida) Kal vachomer for us: prepare with logic, science, character — purifying apprehension from impurity, which Rambam redefines as intellectual error (end of Hilchot Mikvaot) שמור רגליך / the Mizbeach ramp (Gra) / the Kohanim at Sinai — all teach: ascend gradually Chapter 6 — Ish & Isha (Begun) Ish/Isha → borrowed for animals → isha = anything designated to attach to another Ish = Tzura (form); Isha = Chomer (matter) — the binary structure of all existence לזאת יקרא אשה כי מאיש לוקחה — the terminology encodes form-and-matter Implications: marriage as completion (לבשר אחד; shalom = shleimut); the Torah's view of gender difference; each person contains both B'Ezrat Hashem","RIY2IDehgtk":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 8 (ביטחון — Bitachon and Reliance) — Part 2 This Week: Category 2 (The Bitachon of the Heretics) · Category 3 (The Correct Bitachon Expected of Us) · Balancing Effort and Trust Recap from Part 1 The Definition of Bitachon Complete reliance on Hakadosh Baruch Hu — He is the Creator of all, and everything in the universe is subservient to His will. Living with this brings security and serenity. We may use the mediums Hashem created (medicine, doctors, work) — but we do not place our reliance on them. The Three Categories Framework Category Who Status 1 The Prophets — divested from natural means, relied on miracles by divine instruction (prophecy or Ruach HaKodesh) The highest level — forbidden for anyone not instructed 2 Tonight — the heretics The wrong bitachon 3 Tonight — what's expected of us The correct bitachon Key Point from Part 1 A person without prophecy or Ruach HaKodesh is forbidden to rely on miracles — that's brazenness (chutzpah) leading to chillul Hashem. PART 1 — Category 2: The Bitachon of the Heretics (ביטחון של הכופרים) החלק השני הוא הפוכו של החלק הראשון — בטחונם של הכופרים והנלווים עליהם באמצעים הקרובים החיצוניים והרגילים, בעוד שבפנימיותם אינם בוטחים בהשם יתעלה The second category is the opposite of the first — the trust of the deniers and those like them, in the proximate, external, regular intermediaries — while inwardly they do not trust in Hashem. Important Framing We are NOT discussing outright atheists — that's a failure of Emunah , a different discussion entirely. Bitachon is the level after Emunah. Category 2 describes people who believe in God but place their actual trust in intermediaries. The Telltale Signs כגון מי שבוטח בעניין פרנסתו רק על מאמציו, ובשעת מחלתו רק ברופאו וברפואותיו Livelihood : \"How much money I make depends entirely on how hard I work\" Illness : All trust placed in the doctor and the medicine (You might think — of course income depends on effort! We'll refine this in Category 3. The error is the word ONLY — when you believe it depends completely and solely on the effort/doctor/medicine.) Category 2 Has Two Forms Form 2A — Open Denial (כפירה גלויה): The Greek Philosophers כדרך שמצינו בפילוסופי יוון... שחכמתם נעוצה בדברים שבטבע ובאמצעים רגילים, וכופרים בהשגחתו הפרטית של השם יתעלה על מעשי בני אדם The position of Aristotle and his followers: Yes, there is a God — a Prime Mover ( Matzui Rishon ), an ultimate existence behind the universe. But once He created it, the universe runs purely by the laws of nature. Effort in → money out. Medicine in → health out. There is no hashgachah pratit (individual providence) over human affairs — so there is nothing to rely on God for . Form 2B — Hidden Denial (כפירה נסתרת): The Subtle Trap כגון מי שתראהו בוטח בהשם יתעלה מן השפה ולחוץ The person who trusts Hashem from the lips outward. He says all the right things: ויאמר שהשם מפרנס ומכלכל, מחיה וממית, מוחץ ומרפא \"Hashem provides and sustains, gives life and death, wounds and heals\" — Baruch Hashem! B'ezrat Hashem! But deep in his heart (בסתר לבו): His parnassah → really depends on his own acquisitions and schemes In illness → really depends only on the doctors In war → really depends only on strength and strategy This is the subtle form of denial that we ourselves can fall into. What Tanakh Says About Category 2 Source Verse Message Tehillim אין אלהים כל מזמותיו David calls such people wicked Tehillim שנאתי השומרים הבלי שוא, ואני אל ה' בטחתי \"I hate those who rely on vain follies — I trust in Hashem\" Devarim (Eikev) השמר לך פן תשכח את ה' אלהיך... ואמרת בלבבך כחי ועצם ידי עשה לי את החיל הזה The Torah's warning: when you prosper in the Land, don't say \"my own strength made me this wealth\" Devarim וזכרת את ה' אלהיך כי הוא הנותן לך כח לעשות חיל \"Remember Hashem — HE gives you the strength to make wealth\" Tehillim אל תבטחו בנדיבים, בבן אדם שאין לו תשועה \"Do not trust in nobles, in a human who has no salvation\" Yeshayahu חדלו לכם מן האדם אשר נשמה באפו, כי במה נחשב הוא \"Cease relying on man, whose breath is in his nostrils\" Tehillim הנה הגבר לא ישים אלהים מעוזו, ויבטח ברב עשרו The man who doesn't make God his stronghold, and trusts his wealth Tehillim חיל כי ינוב אל תשיתו לב \"When wealth increases, do not set your heart on it\" Iyov אם שמתי זהב כסלי, ולכתם אמרתי מבטחי Iyov's self-defense: \"Did I make gold my security? I did not!\" Yirmiyahu ארור הגבר אשר יבטח באדם ושם בשר זרועו, ומן ה' יסור לבו \"Cursed is the man who trusts in man... AND turns his heart from Hashem\" The Rambam's Crucial Reading of \"Arur HaGever\" (Quoted by His Son) Rabbi Avraham quotes his father (the Rambam) on the verse \"Cursed is the man who trusts in man... and from Hashem turns his heart\" : כי לולא המילים \"ומן ה' יסור לבו\" — היו כלולים בכלל זה רוב בני אדם, ואפילו כולם If not for the words \"and turns his heart from Hashem\" — nearly EVERY person would fall under this curse! Because in normal life: הבן בוטח על אביו — a child relies on a parent והאשה על בעלה — a wife relies on her husband (for livelihood, in the historical context) והשותף על שותפו — a business partner relies on his partner The Resolution ואילו הקללה נוגעת אך ורק במי שמצמצם בטחונו בבני אדם — ולא במי שבוטח בפנימיותו שהשם יתעלה, ורק נעזר בבני אדם ומשתמש באמצעים The curse applies ONLY to one who confines his trust to humans alone — not to one who inwardly trusts Hashem and merely makes use of people and means. A child trusting a parent → fine — knowing Hashem is the ultimate source A sick person hiring the best doctor → fine — knowing healing is ultimately from Hashem It is the exclusivity of the trust in the intermediary that constitutes the curse The Diagnostic Test — How to Know If You're in Category 2B Rabbi Kada's sharpening of the distinction: Scenario: Someone is seriously ill. They search relentlessly for the best doctor, willing to turn everything upside down. — That's fine. That's Category 3. But now: Despite everything, the best doctor is unavailable — can't come, can't be paid, whatever. The person thinks: \"Oh no — NOW I have less chance of getting better.\" That thought reveals Category 2B. You said you trusted Hashem — but your sense of security was actually pinned to the doctor. If you truly trusted Hashem, then having done everything in your power, you'd hand the rest to Him with equanimity. PART 2 — Category 3: The Correct Bitachon (What's Expected of Us) והסוג השלישי הרי הוא הבטחון המוטל על כל שומרי התורה The third category is the bitachon incumbent upon all who keep the Torah. The Definition והוא שיהא נטוע באמונתו של אדם וברור בלבו, שהעילות הטבעיות... מסורים ביד השגחה אלוקית פרטית לגבי כל אחד ואחד, בכל זמן וכל מצב It must be planted in a person's faith and clear in his heart that ALL the natural causes are in the hands of Hashem's individual providence — for each person, at every time, in every situation. By Hashem's will → the natural causes run their regular, pre-set course (the overwhelming majority of the time) By Hashem's will → occasionally, rarely, they deviate from their regular course The Health Mashal (Analogy) The Facts of Nature (as known then) Good bread, lamb, and wine → produce good blood, health, and strength Beans, lentils, spoiled meat, old wine → damage the blood; habitual consumption → illness Bloodletting when veins are full → restores health Laxatives when the body has excess → cleanse it Cooling medicines for fever; warming ones for chills → restore health (Today's equivalents: vitamin C for colds, the right medicine for the right illness, a good diet, etc. — these are facts of medical science.) The Critical Addition כל הגורמים הטבעיים הללו כפופים לרצונו יתעלה ALL these natural causes are subservient to His will. ועל פי פקודת רצונו פועלים הם על רוב בני אדם... ובפקודת רצונו פועלים הם לפעמים בדרך הפוכה ממה שהיה מצופה By His will they work in the vast majority of cases (99.9%). And by His will they sometimes work in the opposite way from what was expected. The Two Directions If Hashem has decreed someone be punished with illness: אפילו יאכל מאכלים משופרא דשופרא — הרי הם מחוללים בו דם נשחת... אפילו אם יקח סמי מרפא לא יועילו לו Even the finest foods will generate bad blood Even the best medicines won't help — \"He cannot heal you, nor cure you of your wound\" (Hoshea) If Hashem has decreed someone be healthy: אפילו יאכל מאכלים נפסדים בהיותו אנוס לכך — לא ישחיתו דמו Even bad food (eaten under duress — when there's no choice) won't make him ill As the verse says: כל המחלה אשר שמתי במצרים לא אשים עליך, כי אני ה' רופאך — \"I will not place upon you the diseases of Egypt, for I am Hashem your healer\" And: והסיר ה' ממך כל חולי — \"Hashem will remove from you all illness\" Note the qualifier — בהיותו אנוס לכך (under duress): You cannot eat junk daily and say \"I trust in Hashem\" — that's demanding a miracle (Category 1 behavior without authorization). The promise applies to extenuating circumstances. Even Brief Illness וגם אם נפל למשכב... יתרפא בלא תרופה, ובתרופה פשוטה ובדבר פשוט If Hashem decreed a brief illness as a reminder — the person will recover easily, even without medicine or with a simple one. As Chazal expound: \"I will not place illness upon you — and if I do, אני ה' רופאך, I am your Healer.\" The Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa Illustration The Gemara tells: Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa came home one Friday and his wife had no oil for the Shabbat candles. He said over the water (the Gemara's version: vinegar): \"The One who told oil to burn can tell water to burn\" — and it lit. The Lesson It is not the oil that produces the flame — it is the will of Hashem that oil produces flame. Oil is the natural medium we are commanded to operate through. But when a person of that level has exhausted every natural possibility, the underlying truth shows itself: nature is nothing but Hashem's standing will. (Obviously — we don't do this. The point is the principle, not the practice.) The Parnassah Mashal The Facts of Agriculture (as known then) מדרך הטבע הוא שהתבואה הנזרעת... תיתן שבעה שערים או לכל היותר עשרה שערים Normal planting yields sevenfold, at most tenfold return. The Two Directions One cursed (who transgressed): יזרע ארדב ויאסוף מחציתה בלבד He plants a measure (ardab) and harvests half of what he planted — as it says: זרע רב תוציא השדה ומעט תאסוף — \"You will sow much and gather little\" One blessed: יזרע ארדב ויאסוף עשרים He plants one measure and harvests twentyfold — \"ברכת ה' היא תעשיר\" — \"The blessing of Hashem enriches\" The Ultimate Example — Yitzchak and Me'ah She'arim ויזרע יצחק בארץ ההיא וימצא בשנה ההיא מאה שערים, ויברכהו ה' \"Yitzchak planted in that land and found in that year a HUNDREDFOLD — and Hashem blessed him\" When the norm is 7-10x, Yitzchak received 100x — ten times the maximum. (This is where the name of the Yerushalayim neighborhood \"Me'ah She'arim\" comes from — a hundredfold of blessing.) The same applies to children, flocks and herds, trade, seafaring — all dependent on Hashem's will. The Practical Conduct of Category 3 חייב אפוא שומר הדת, בשעה שהוא מקדיש את חילו לגורמים טבעיים... שבטחונו על השם יתעלה ולא לבו תלוי בהפקת התועלת When engaging the natural means (for health or livelihood) — your trust must be on Hashem, not your heart hanging on the means themselves. The Chazal Institution — The Yehi Ratzon Before Medicine הנכנס להקיז דם אומר: יהי רצון מלפניך ה' אלהי שיהא עסק זה לי לרפואה One entering bloodletting (= medical treatment) says: \"May it be Your will, Hashem my God, that this engagement be for my healing.\" Modern application: When taking a pill, say the Yehi Ratzon. It's not merely a prayer — it's a recalibration : reminding yourself that whether the medicine works depends on Hashem, even as you faithfully use the means He created. ההולך למוד את גורנו אומר: יהי רצון שתשלח ברכה במעשה ידינו Checking your threshing floor (= modern: going to a job interview, launching a venture): \"May it be Your will to send blessing in the work of my hands.\" You prepared everything you could — and you hand the outcome to Hashem. ומתוך הפרטים האלו תקיש לדומיהם — extrapolate from these examples to everything else. The Constant Reminder וצריך שיהיה דבר זה חוזר במחשבתו של אדם בשעת המעשה וקבוע בלבו, כדי שלא יתעלם ממנו This must recur in a person's thoughts at the time of the action and be fixed in his heart — so it doesn't slip away. It is very easy to slide into Category 2B — saying \"B'ezrat Hashem\" while actually pinning your security on this doctor, that medicine, this deal. The remedy is active, repeated reminding at the moment of engagement. The Anti-Extremism Consequence ואז לא ילהט יותר מדי אחרי התחבולות והסיבות When you live with this attitude, you won't chase the means obsessively or disproportionately. Health Example You're ill; you found and hired a very good doctor. There exists a marginally better one — but reaching him means selling your house, extreme upheaval, for an extra couple of percent. That's too far. You've genuinely done what's expected; the disproportionate extra is a Category 2 behavior (acting as if it ALL hangs on the human means). Parnassah Example Yes — work hard (this is about to be emphasized strongly). But you are not expected to work 6 AM to 10 PM. What about Torah? What about tefillah? Balance. Do your normal, honest effort — then step back. כמו שאמרו חז\"ל: הוי ממעט בעסק ועסוק בתורה — Minimize business and engage in Torah Fear Also Returns to the Source פלטה נפשי מרשע חרבך — \"Save my soul from the wicked — from YOUR sword\" (David) Even the wicked man's sword is ultimately Hashem's sword — it can only harm if Hashem removes His protection. So even fear is redirected to its true address. Kohelet's Summary of Natural Limits שבתי וראה תחת השמש: כי לא לקלים המרוץ, ולא לגיבורים המלחמה, וגם לא לחכמים לחם, וגם לא לנבונים עושר, וגם לא ליודעים חן — כי עת ופגע יקרה את כולם \"The race is not to the swift, nor the battle to the strong, nor bread to the wise, nor riches to the intelligent, nor favor to the knowledgeable — for time and chance happen to them all.\" Statistically, the strong win battles. But not always — because the outcome is Hashem's. The Chiddush — Hashem Provides for Those Who Trust Rabbi Avraham now reveals something beyond mere theology — a practical promise : The Verse (Tehillim 127) אם ה' לא יבנה בית — שוא עמלו בוניו בו; אם ה' לא ישמר עיר — שוא שקד שומר \"If Hashem does not build a house — its builders toil in vain; if Hashem does not guard a city — the watchman watches in vain.\" (Modern application: without Hashem's will, the best Iron Dome and the best military cannot protect.) The Key Verse שוא לכם משכימי קום מאחרי שבת אוכלי לחם העצבים — כן יתן לידידו שנה \"In vain do you rise early, stay up late, eat the bread of anxiety — for He gives to His beloved ones sleep.\" Rabbi Avraham's Interpretation הוי המתחכמים להשגת משאלותיכם הגשמיות, וקמים באשמורת הלילה... לשוא כל שקידתכם אם לא יצטרף אליה עזר משמים. ואילו הייתם מאלה האהובים עליו יתעלה — בזכות בטחונכם בו היתה מספקת לכם יגיעה מועטת, ויכולתם לישון בשלוה Woe to those who wake before dawn and return home late at night, eating their bread in stress — all your diligence is in vain without Heaven's help. But were you among His beloved — in the merit of your bitachon, MINIMAL effort would suffice, and you could sleep in tranquility. כי באמת נכון הדבר שהשם יתעלה מספק לאהובים עליו את כל צורכיהם... בלי יגיעה וטורח It is truly so: Hashem supplies all the needs of those beloved to Him — without excessive toil. בשלום יחדיו אשכבה ואישן, כי אתה ה' לבדד לבטח תושיבני \"In peace I lie down and sleep — for You alone, Hashem, settle me in security\" PART 3 — The Critical Counterbalance: This Is NOT a License for Laziness ואין כוונת הפסוקים... כי מפני בטחונו רשאי שומר התורה לשבת בחיבוק ידיים Do not conclude from these verses (e.g., השלך על ה' יהבך והוא יכלכלך — \"Cast your burden on Hashem and He will sustain you\") that one may sit with folded hands and wait for food to fall from heaven like the man, or be delivered by ravens like Eliyahu. כי זו מדרגה שלא כל אחד יכול להשיגה That is a level not everyone can attain — it's Category 1, requiring prophecy/Ruach HaKodesh. The Proof — The Greatest Sages All Worked Figure Occupation Hillel Wood-chopper Karna Water-carrier Rabbi Yehoshua Coal merchant Abba Chilkiya Day-laborer plowman — the same Abba Chilkiya whose prayer brought rain in a drought! Even the Avot and Prophets Worked Figure Occupation Source Noach Worked the ground ויחל נח איש האדמה ויטע כרם (Onkelos: גבר פלח בארעא) Avraham Shepherd/cattle רועי מקנה אברם Yitzchak Farmer ויזרע יצחק בארץ ההיא Yaakov Shepherd (20 years for Lavan) מתי אעשה גם אנכי לביתי — \"When shall I provide for my own house?\" Moshe Rabbeinu — the master of prophets Shepherd for Yitro Chazal's Praise of Honest Work יגיע כפיך כי תאכל — אשריך וטוב לך \"When you eat the toil of your hands — fortunate are you and good is yours\" The Gemara (Berachot): גדול הנהנה מיגיעו יותר מירא שמים Greater is one who benefits from his own labor than one who fears Heaven! For the God-fearer it says only \"Fortunate is the man who fears Hashem\" — but for the one who eats his own toil: אשריך בעולם הזה, וטוב לך לעולם הבא — fortunate in THIS world, and good in the NEXT world. Don't Confuse the Lazy Man with the Man of Bitachon הישמר פן יתחלף לך בין העצל הראוי לגנאי ובין בעל ביטחון הראוי לשבח Beware of confusing the lazy person (deserving condemnation) with the person of bitachon (deserving praise). Shlomo HaMelech in Mishlei: Verse Message אוגר בקיץ בן משכיל, נרדם בקציר בן מביש The wise son gathers in summer; the shameful one sleeps through harvest אל תאהב שנה פן תורש; פקח עיניך שבע לחם \"Don't love sleep lest you become poor; open your eyes and be satisfied with bread\" עובד אדמתו ישבע לחם, ומרדף ריקים ישבע ריש \"One who works his land is satisfied with bread; one who chases empty things is satisfied with poverty\" \"One who chases empty things\" (מרדף ריקים) = those who imagine themselves worthy of miracles, or hold a vain, idle \"bitachon.\" The Chillul Hashem Warning — Painfully Relevant Today ואם יחשוב כל אדם כי ראוי הוא לחסד אלוה כפי אליהו ואלישע — יהיה ללעג ולקלס One who fancies himself worthy of Eliyahu-and-Elisha-level providence will be mocked. About such people: דור טהור בעיניו ומצואתו לא רוחץ — \"a generation pure in its own eyes, yet unwashed of its filth.\" The Mechanism of the Chillul Hashem כי הרשעים רואים את שפל מצבם... והם נזקקים לנדבותיהם The non-religious see those who claim devotion to God dependent on the donations and charity of the very people whose lifestyle they reject. What conclusion does the benefactor draw? — \"Apparently it really IS all up to me and my effort.\" This doubles down their materialism and desecrates Hashem's name. (Rabbi Kada: This is before even discussing those who twist and lie to systems to live off benefits — which is plainly wrong. Even legitimately relying on donations from those you consider to be living wrongly, when avoidable, sends this corrosive message. ודי למבין.) But Also — Don't Become Addicted to Work ואין דברי שלמה בשבח העבודה... מחייבים התמכרות למעשה Shlomo praised work — but never said \"toil to become rich\": אל תיגע להעשיר — \"Do not toil to become rich\" טוב מעט ביראת ה' מאוצר רב ומהומה בו — \"Better a little with fear of Hashem than great treasure with turmoil\" The Balanced Formula שיעבוד האדם למחייתו באמצעים רגילים בלי הפרזה יתירה ... ויקדיש רוב עתותיו לתורה ולמצוותיה Work for your livelihood through normal means — without excess Recognize deep down the Torah principle of bitachon Dedicate the majority of your free time to Torah and mitzvot Pirkei Avot's Framework יפה תלמוד תורה עם דרך ארץ, שיגיעת שניהם משכחת עוון. וכל תורה שאין עמה מלאכה — סופה בטלה וגוררת עוון Torah study with worldly occupation is beautiful — the toil of both banishes sin. Torah without work ends in nullity and drags sin behind it. אם אין קמח אין תורה; אם אין תורה אין קמח No flour, no Torah; no Torah, no flour. The Rambam's Daily Schedule (for his era) 24 hours: 8 hours sleep (the Rambam writes this!) ~4 hours for prayer and necessities Of the remaining 12: 3 hours work, 9 hours study The 9 study-hours: 3 Torah (Tanakh), 3 Talmud, 3 intellectual inference and wisdom (Times differ — modern employment doesn't map onto this. The principle stands: work what's needed; devote free time to Torah.) The Closing Warnings — Avoiding Both Extremes השמר פן תשקע ראשך ורובך בסיבות עבודת הפרנסה — פן תהיה עם אותם הכופרים Don't sink head-and-shoulders into the pursuit of livelihood — or you slide into Category 2 (\"it all depends on my work\"), about whom it says: הבוטחים על חילם וברוב עשרם יתהללו. ולא תנהג בבורות ותחזיק עזות פנים לסמוך על הביטחון בלבד בלי השקעה בעבודה And don't act with ignorant brazenness, relying on bitachon alone without working — that's Category 1, requiring sanctification and levels you have not attained. The Conclusion — The Formula for True Bitachon ראה את הביטחון בהשם יתעלה כמחייב אותך לסמוך עליו תוך כדי עיסוק באומנות רגילה, והצטמצם במלאכה פשוטה שהיא טורדך מעבודתו יתעלה כדי חייך — אזי תהיה בעל ביטחון אמיתי See bitachon as obligating you to rely on Hashem WHILE engaged in a normal occupation. Confine yourself to straightforward work sufficient for your needs (so you never become dependent on others) — then you will be a possessor of TRUE bitachon. And from there one can rise higher — if Hashem so wills in His kindness — toward sustenance with minimal toil: ברוך הגבר אשר יבטח בה', והיה ה' מבטחו \"Blessed is the man who trusts in Hashem — and Hashem will be his security\" The Future Promise וכבר הבטיח השם יתעלה לישראל כי לעתיד יופיע להם השגחתו... ויבטל בטחונם בגורם אחר In the Messianic future, Hashem's providence will be manifest and all reliance on intermediaries will fall away — everyone will reach Category 1: והיה שארית יעקב... כטל מאת ה', כרביבים עלי עשב, אשר לא יקוה לאיש ולא ייחל לבני אדם \"The remnant of Yaakov will be like dew from Hashem, like raindrops on grass — which hopes not in man and waits not for humans\" Just as rain depends on no human hand — so will our trust be purely in Hashem. Summary Category 2 — The Wrong Bitachon (Heretics) 2A — Open : The Greek philosophers — God exists but the world runs purely by nature; no individual providence 2B — Hidden : Saying \"B'ezrat Hashem\" outwardly while inwardly pinning security on effort, doctors, medicine, wealth The curse of ארור הגבר applies only when trust in humans replaces Hashem (ומן ה' יסור לבו) — using people and means while trusting Hashem is fine The test : when the best means become unavailable after full effort — do you panic (2B) or hand it to Hashem with equanimity (3)? Category 3 — The Correct Bitachon (For All Torah-Keepers) All natural causes are subservient to Hashem's individual providence, for every person, at every moment Normally nature runs its course; Hashem can redirect it at any time (in both directions — for blessing or its opposite) Engage the means fully — medicine, doctors, honest work — while your trust rests on Hashem Say the Yehi Ratzon when taking medicine or beginning a venture — the recalibration matters Don't go overboard in pursuit of health or wealth — disproportionate chasing reveals misplaced trust The chiddush : Hashem supplies the needs of those who truly trust Him — with reduced toil (\"He gives to His beloved sleep\") Not laziness : Hillel chopped wood; the Avot herded sheep; Moshe shepherded for Yitro. Relying on miracles without authorization = mockery + chillul Hashem The formula : normal work without excess + majority of free time in Torah + heart fixed on Hashem = true bitachon Coming Up Next Week Continuing Chapter 8 — further development of the laws and attitudes of Bitachon. (Schedule note: Moreh tomorrow (Tuesday) 8:00pm; no shiur this Thursday; regular schedule resumes next week — Monday, Tuesday, Thursday.) B'Ezrat Hashem","94aUWe7Dc9o":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Shulchan Aruch · Orach Chaim Siman: 320 (ש\"כ) Topic: מלאכת דש — Threshing & Squeezing (Continued) Tonight's Seifim: 10 (breaking ice) · 11 (washing hands with snow) · 12 (salt & soap) · 13 (walking on snow) · 14 (urinating in snow) · 15 (towel over water) · 16 (wedging cotton in a jug) · 17 (sponges) · Beginning 18 (the flax stopper & psik reisha d'lo nicha lei) Plus: Freezing water on Shabbat · The full discussion of soap · Introduction to Melaben's relationship with Dash Recap — The Structure of Siman 320 Part Seifim Content 1 א–ח Mefarek/Sechita: three categories of fruit, lemons, pickles, squeezing onto solids 2 ט–יד Crushing ice and snow — the rabbinic extensions 3 טו–יח Sechita of Melaben vs. Sechita of Dash — towels, sponges, stoppers (End) יט–כ Melechet Tzovei'a (dyeing) — to be learned separately Recap of Part 2 So Far (Last Week) The Gemara: אין מרסקין לא את השלג ולא את הברד, אבל נותן הוא לתוך הכוס ואינו חושש — one may not crush snow or hail on Shabbat, but may put it into a cup. Three reasons in the Rishonim: Rishon Reason Nature of Problem Rambam דומה לדש — similar to Dash The action of producing liquid from solid Rashi Similar to מכה בפטיש — creating something new The action Ba'al HaTerumah (Rabbeinu Baruch of Mainz) Nolad — a new entity exists that didn't exist when Shabbat began The result The Shulchan Aruch clearly holds like Rambam (he placed these halachot in the Dash chapter) — and we'll see tonight he also incorporates Rashi's reasoning. Practical results established last week: Leaving ice to melt on its own : Sefardim permitted (Rashi/Rambam — no action); Ashkenazim should not in the first instance (Ba'al HaTerumah/Rema — the result is the problem) Putting ice cubes into a drink : Permitted by all (the transformation is never discernible — immediately mixed in) Crushing the ice once it's in the drink : Ba'al HaTerumah permits (result is absorbed); Rashi/Rambam — machloket; Shulchan Aruch rules strictly → Sefardim should not crush ice cubes in their Coke; Ashkenazim have room to be lenient Seif 10 — Breaking Ice to Reach the Water מותר לשבר הקרח כדי ליטול מים מתחתיו It is permitted to break ice in order to take the water beneath it. The Case A frozen pool/mikveh/body of water on a cold morning — you may break the ice to reach the water underneath (e.g., for tevillah). The Gemara in Berachot says Benayahu ben Yehoyada broke the ice to immerse in a mikveh — and there are many Chassidic stories of the same. The Question We just learned you may NOT crush ice! Why is breaking ice permitted? The Answer — Intention Look back at Seif 9: the prohibition is crushing כדי שיזובו מימיו — in order that its water should flow . The entire rabbinic prohibition (under all three reasons) is when your purpose is to CREATE liquid. Here, you're breaking the ice to access the water beneath — not to convert the ice into water. Some liquid will incidentally result, but that's not your intention. Permitted. The Magen Avraham's Concern (Rejected) The Magen Avraham suggested that breaking ice attached to the ground (a river) might be a problem of stira (destruction). All the Poskim disagreed — water has no binyan u'stira. Chacham Ovadia rules outright: permitted. Important Application for Ashkenazim Based on this seif, the Ashkenazi Poskim qualify their stringency about leaving ice out to melt: Problem : Leaving ice out for an extended period with the intention that it melt into liquid NOT a problem : Taking ice cubes out of the freezer before serving so they soften slightly and can be extracted — some melting happens mimela (automatically), but you're not doing it כדי שיזובו מימיו The Ashkenazi stringency only applies to intentional transformation. Incidental melting in the normal course of use is permitted for everyone. Seif 11 — Washing Hands with Snow/Ice Water in Winter צריך ליזהר בחורף שלא יטול ידיו במים שיש בהם שלג או ברד, ואם יטול — יזהר שלא ידחקם בין ידיו שלא יהיה מרסק In winter, one should be careful not to wash one's hands with water containing snow or ice. If one does wash — be careful not to press them between the hands, so as not to crush. The Halacha Better not to wash hands with water containing snow/ice If you do — don't rub/press your hands together, because you'd be crushing the ice into liquid (and there you WANT the extra liquid to wash with) Critical Inference Notice: the ice here is already mixed with liquid water — and still the Shulchan Aruch says don't crush it. This proves the Shulchan Aruch holds you cannot crush solids even when they're sitting in liquid. This confirms what we said on Seif 9: Sefardim should not crush ice cubes in their drink on Shabbat. (Side note from Rabbi Kada: whether netilat yadayim itself works with snow is a separate question — likely problematic since it's not \"mayim\" — to be checked separately.) Side Topic — Freezing Water on Shabbat The Question: It's a hot summer Shabbat afternoon, the ice cubes have run out. May I put water in the freezer for tonight? (If it's for after Shabbat → forbidden as hachana. The question is for use later the same Shabbat.) The Analysis Opinion Ruling Why Rambam / Rashi (Sefardim) Permitted — no issue at all The entire prohibition is an action of crushing (similar to Dash / Makeh B'Patish). Putting liquid in a freezer involves no action of crushing — it freezes on its own Ba'al HaTerumah (Ashkenazi concern) Question arises If the problem is creating something new even passively — isn't freezing also transforming liquid → solid? The Ashkenazi Resolution — Majority Lenient A minority of Ashkenazi poskim forbid. But the majority are lenient — including the Shevet HaLevi (Rav Wosner) and Orchot Shabbat . Two reasons given: The Ba'al HaTerumah's chiddush was specifically solid → liquid — creating water , which is more useful than the solid. Going the other way (liquid → solid) was never part of the prohibition. The prohibition only covers things subject to crushing (risuk) — a liquid cannot be \"crushed\" into a solid, so the gezeira never applied. Bottom Line Everyone may freeze water into ice cubes on Shabbat (for use that same Shabbat). Seif 12 — Salt (and the Gateway to Soap) יש ליזהר שלא ישפשף ידיו במלח One should be careful not to rub one's hands with salt. The Halacha Don't rub salt between your hands — you'd be dissolving/transforming the solid salt particles into liquid , just like crushing ice into water. Working It Through the Three Reasons Opinion Does the salt prohibition make sense? Rashi (Makeh B'Patish — creating something new) ✓ Yes — transforming salt to liquid creates something new Ba'al HaTerumah (Nolad — the result) ✓ Yes — a new liquid now exists Rambam (Domeh l'Dash) ✗ No issue! Dash requires something omed l'mashkin — something with liquid-potential like fruit, or even snow/ice. Salt is not omed l'mashkeh — there's no resemblance to Dash The Mishna Berura writes this explicitly: according to the Rambam, salt and borit (soap) and other things not designated for liquid would be permitted l'chatchila . The Shulchan Aruch's Dual Position Interesting observation: the Shulchan Aruch: Placed these halachot in the Dash chapter → holds like Rambam But ALSO forbids rubbing salt → that's only explicable like Rashi Conclusion: the Shulchan Aruch incorporates both reasonings (Rambam AND Rashi) — but certainly not the Ba'al HaTerumah. The Big Application — Soap on Shabbat (Bar of Soap) The Rema's Ruling (Siman 326:10) ואסור לרחוץ ידיו במלח, וכל שכן בבורית שקורין סאבו\"ן, דהוי נולד It is forbidden to wash one's hands with salt, and all the more so with borit called \"sabon\" (soap) — for it is nolad. Just as you can't transform ice → liquid or salt → liquid, you cannot rub a bar of soap into liquid on Shabbat. This is how the Ben Ish Chai and many Sefardi poskim rule — forbidden. The Ben Ish Chai explicitly rejects the lenient view: even though while washing, the soap mixes with water droplets already on your hands (and we've learned that transformation INTO existing liquid is permitted) — he still forbids, and says this is the minhag. The Lenient View — Pachad Yitzchak & Ginat Veradim The Pachad Yitzchak distinguishes: אבל הבורית מצד עצמו לא יהפך למים, אלא על ידי המים עולה קצף על ידיו, ואין דמיון לבורית עם השלג והברד Snow/ice/salt → directly transform into liquid water Soap → does NOT become water. It becomes ketzef — FOAM — via the water Foam is not a liquid → no comparison to snow and ice → no prohibition The Second Issue Everyone Asks About — Memachek (Smoothing) Most people think the reason soap is forbidden on Shabbat is Memachek (smoothing — like smoothing wax over a barrel opening). But notice: the Rema never mentions Memachek! He only raises nolad. Chacham Ben Tzion Abba Shaul and Chacham Ovadia derive from this a very important principle: Memareach/Memachek is only forbidden when you are smoothing something in order to make it smooth — creating a smooth layer you want (like wax sealing a barrel). Applied to soap: The lather smoothed on your hands? You have zero interest in it — it's washed off seconds later. Not Memachek at all. The smoothing of the bar itself? You have no intention for that — it's a פסיק רישא דלא ניחא ליה (inevitable but of no benefit to you) on what is at worst a d'Rabbanan — permitted . (BUT: if you deliberately rub the bar to make it nice and smooth — which people sometimes do — that is forbidden according to everyone.) The Final Ruling on Soap Issue Analysis Nolad / transforming solid → liquid It becomes foam, not liquid → machloket on a d'Rabbanan → safek d'Rabbanan l'kula Memachek No interest in the smoothness → psik reisha d'lo nicha lei on a d'Rabbanan → permitted Chacham Ovadia: Mikar hadin, one may use a bar of soap on Shabbat — but ba'al nefesh yachmir (a scrupulous person should be stringent). Practical guidance: If you see people using bar soap on Shabbat — they have what to rely on; no need to correct them If you have liquid soap available — use it and avoid the whole question Seif 13 — Walking on Snow דורס שלג ברגליו ואינו חושש One may tread on snow with his feet and need not be concerned. The Halacha You may walk on snow on Shabbat (to shul, to a friend) — even though your steps crush it. Two Reasons in the Acharonim Posek Reason Vilna Gaon אינו מתכוון — you have no intention to crush Taz (Rabbi David Segal) אי אפשר ליזהר — it's impossible to avoid; there's no alternative (you can't stay trapped at home all Shabbat) Nafka Mina 1 — Half the Pavement Is Clear Part of the pavement has snow, part is clear: Taz : You must walk on the clear side — the whole heter is that there's no alternative Vilna Gaon : Walk wherever is easiest — you're not intending to crush either way Ben Ish Chai rules strictly (like the Taz) : if there's an alternative route, take it Nafka Mina 2 — Enjoying the Crunch The road is fully snowed; you must walk through it. But you enjoy crunching snow (we all know that feeling — wellies on, stomping the snow) and deliberately walk harder to crush it: Taz : No problem — there's no alternative route anyway Vilna Gaon : Problem — you're now mitkaven (intending) to crush Agreed by Both Going out to jump on snow in order to crush it (teenagers/adults for fun) — forbidden according to both reasons: you're either mitkaven (Gra) or have an alternative (Taz — you don't need to be doing this at all). (Rabbi Kada: I wouldn't stop young children doing this — no need for chinuch on something like this.) Walking on Ice Same status as snow — permitted where needed. (Though probably inadvisable for safety reasons!) Seif 14 — Urinating in Snow מהר\"ם מרוטנבורג מתיר להטיל מי רגלים בשלג, והרא\"ש נזהר The Maharam of Rothenburg permits urinating in snow; the Rosh was careful (not to). A Teacher-Student Machloket The Rosh (Rabbeinu Asher) was the student of the Maharam MiRotenburg. (Historical note: the Maharam spent years imprisoned; the Rosh fled the German persecutions to Toledo.) The Positions Maharam : Permitted — same logic as walking on snow (no intention to crush / no alternative) Rosh : Be careful not to — here you're directly causing the solid to change to liquid (more like crushing an ice cube in liquid than like incidentally stepping on snow) The Halacha If there's any need — one may be lenient (it's d'Rabbanan, plus kavod haberiyot) If there's truly nowhere else to go — everyone agrees it's permitted Only one doing so in order to melt the snow would have a problem (an unusual case) Seif 15 — A Towel Over the Water Bowl (Sechita of MELABEN) Introduction — The Two Sechitot This is the key conceptual distinction for Part 3 of the siman. There are two melachot of squeezing, and your intention determines which one applies: Melacha What You Want Example Dash (Mefarek) You want the liquid being squeezed out Squeezing grapes for juice, olives for oil Melaben (laundering) You want the garment — squeezing to get rid of the liquid Wringing out a soaked cloth Melaben Has Three Torah-Level Components שריה (Sheriyah) — soaking the garment שפשוף (Shifshuf) — scrubbing/rubbing it סחיטה (Sechita) — wringing it out (an essential part of laundering — removing the dirty water) Both melachot are called \"sechita\" — which is confusing. Squeezing to USE the liquid = Dash. Squeezing to REMOVE the liquid from the item you want = Melaben. This distinction recurs throughout Hilchot Shabbat. The Halacha of Seif 15 אסור לפרוס סודר על פי החבית וליתן על גביו כלי ששואבים בו, שמא יבוא לידי סחיטה. אבל בגד העשוי לפרוס עליו — מותר, שאינו חושש עליו לסוחטו The Case Many people keep a netilat yadayim set by their bed (bowl + cup of water — so as not to walk daled amot in the morning without washing). The Ben Ish Chai and other poskim also note you shouldn't leave water uncovered (mayim megulim) — so people cover the cup with a towel. The Ruling A regular cloth/towel/garment spread over a water vessel → FORBIDDEN — out of concern it will dip into the water and you'll come to squeeze it out (sechita of Melaben — you want the towel dry, not the liquid) A cloth designated for this use (your regular netilat yadayim hand towel) → PERMITTED — since its nature is to be used with water and be wet, you won't care to squeeze it Practical Your regular hand towel from the netilat yadayim set → fine to use as a cover on Shabbat Friday night and your hand towels are in the wash, so you grab a random shirt or fresh towel from the closet → forbidden to use as the cover Seif 16 — Wedging Wet Cotton into a Jug Opening (Sechita of DASH) אסור להדק מוכין בפי פך שיש בו משקין, משום סחיטה It is forbidden to wedge soft material (cotton/wadding) into the mouth of a flask containing liquid, because of sechita. The Case A jug stoppered with soft material (cotton wadding). The wadding is wet (it's been sitting in the jug opening). Pressing it back in = psik reisha — you will inevitably squeeze liquid out of it into the jug . Which Sechita Is This? DASH — because the squeezed drops go INTO the jug, adding to your liquid. It's nicha lei (beneficial to you) — even a few drops add to your wine/liquid. You're extracting liquid that you keep. (Note the confusion: Seif 15's sechita was Melaben; Seif 16's sechita is Dash. Same word, different melachot.) Even Designated Material Unlike Seif 15 (where a designated cloth was fine), here even designated wadding is a problem — because the issue isn't \"you might come to squeeze\" (a concern); it's that wedging it inevitably squeezes liquid into the jug right now. Seif 17 — Sponges ספוג — אין מקנחין בו אלא אם כן יש בו בית אחיזה, גזרה שמא יסחוט A sponge — one may not wipe with it unless it has a handle... Correction on the Language The Shulchan Aruch says \"gezera shema yischot\" but the Gemara's reasoning is mishum sechita — it's not a gezera (precaution), it's psik reisha : holding a wet handle-less sponge and wiping a table inevitably squeezes liquid out. Which Sechita? DASH — as you wipe, you squeeze liquid out of the sponge and use that liquid to clean the surface. You want the extracted liquid → Dash. The Halacha Sponge Status Wet sponge, no handle Forbidden — psik reisha of squeezing Wet sponge with a handle (בית אחיזה) Permitted — no longer inevitable; you use the surface moisture only (still be careful not to squeeze) Beginning Seif 18 — The Flax Stopper & Psik Reisha D'Lo Nicha Lei This is the major final seif of the Dash/Melaben section. Tonight we lay the groundwork; we'll finish next shiur. Introduction Point 1 — Is There Libun (Laundering) with Liquids Other Than Water? (Based on the sugya in Ketubot daf 5-6 and the famous Tosafot there.) The Question: Melaben means squeezing out to clean. Does it apply when the absorbed liquid is NOT water — e.g., a towel drenched in red wine or Coca-Cola ? Opinion Ruling Logic Rambam (and others) YES — יש ליבון בשאר משקין — squeezing out wine IS Melaben (d'Oraita) Even though the stain and smell remain, you've certainly improved the garment Rabbeinu Tam (and many Rishonim) NO — אין ליבון בשאר משקין Laundering means cleaning . Squeeze out water → garment is clean. Squeeze out wine → still dirty. That's not laundering Key for the psak: the Shulchan Aruch rules NOT like the Rambam — there is NO libun with other liquids. (Squeezing wine out of a cloth is not Melaben.) Introduction Point 2 — Dash V'Holech L'Ibud (Squeezing Where the Liquid Goes to Waste) Setup: Clearly, squeezing an expensive wine OUT of a towel INTO a cup (to save the wine) = Dash from the Torah — you want the extracted liquid. The Question: What if I squeeze the wine out of the towel and it goes to waste (down the drain)? No Melaben issue (per the Shulchan Aruch's ruling — wine isn't libun). Is the squeezing itself a Dash problem? Opinion Ruling Logic Rabbeinu Tam Assur d'Rabbanan It's Dash performed as a מלאכה שאינה צריכה לגופה (extraction not for the sake of using the extract) Other Rishonim Muttar Squeezing-to-waste simply ISN'T Dash. The essence of Dash-sechita is wanting what comes out . Squeezing to discard falls under the category of Melaben — and if the liquid has no libun (wine), it's permitted entirely The Seif Itself (Begun) חבית שפקקה בפקק של פשתן שמוצין בה היין, יש מי שמתיר (להסירו) אף על פי שאי אפשר שלא יסחוט A barrel stoppered with a flax stopper through which wine is drawn — there is one who permits (removing/reinserting it) even though it is impossible not to squeeze. (Textual note: the word להסירו was added by the Mishna Berura's explanation and copied into modern editions — it's not in the original Shulchan Aruch text. The meaning: to remove and reinsert the stopper.) The Famous Opinion — The Ba'al HaAruch This is the celebrated position of the Aruch (one of the early Rishonim): פסיק רישא דלא ניחא ליה — מותר A psik reisha (inevitable consequence) in which you have no interest — is PERMITTED , even where the melacha would be d'Oraita. Defining \"Lo Nicha Lei\" Precisely Critical clarification: Lo nicha lei does NOT mean \"it's bad for me.\" It means \"I have no interest / no benefit in it.\" Proof from Tosafot: the Gemara's case of watering בארעא דחבריה (a friend's field) — the water benefits the friend's land, nothing bad happens — yet it's still called psik reisha d'lo nicha lei because I have no interest in it. Indifference qualifies as lo nicha lei . To Be Continued Next Shiur Completing Seif 18: how the machloket about psik reisha d'lo nicha lei plays out The Mishna Berura's treatment and halacha l'ma'aseh applications Then: Melechet Tzovei'a (dyeing) — the final two seifim of the siman (Note: no shiur next Thursday; regular shiurim otherwise — Monday HaMaspik, Tuesday Moreh.) Summary Table of Tonight's Halachot Seif Topic Bottom Line 10 Breaking ice to reach water Permitted — intention is access, not liquid creation 11 Washing hands with snow/ice water Avoid; if you do — don't rub hands together — Freezing water into ice cubes Permitted for everyone (for that Shabbat's use) 12 Rubbing salt Forbidden (Shulchan Aruch incorporates Rashi's reason) — Bar of soap Mikar hadin permitted (foam ≠ liquid; no Memachek interest); ba'al nefesh yachmir; liquid soap avoids the question 13 Walking on snow Permitted ; don't deliberately stomp to crush; Ben Ish Chai: take a clear path if one exists 14 Urinating in snow Maharam permits, Rosh careful; lenient if any need 15 Towel over water bowl Designated towel — permitted; random garment — forbidden (Melaben concern) 16 Wet cotton wedged in jug Forbidden — psik reisha of Dash (drops squeezed into your liquid) 17 Wet sponge Forbidden without a handle; permitted with one 18 Flax stopper (begun) The Aruch: psik reisha d'lo nicha lei is permitted — to be continued B'Ezrat Hashem","sPi5imIEJjo":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: 1 · Chapters 3 & 4 (with introduction to Chapter 5) Topic: Temunah & Tavnit · The Three Verbs of Seeing — Sight and Insight Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Recap of the Journey So Far Stage Content Introductions How to learn the book, purpose, meshalim, seven contradictions Chapter 1 Tzelem & Demut — the image and likeness of God = the intellectual faculty; humans are the most similar to God Chapter 2 Adam did not gain intellect from the Etz HaDa'at — he already had it. Before the sin: objective perception ( emet/sheker ). After: subjective perception ( tov/ra ) shaped by desire and culture. The snake's offer was moral sovereignty A Note on Rambam's Method These chapters are foundational. Rambam writes in cryptic form — מגלה טפח ומכסה טפחיים — revealing a handbreadth and concealing two. He reveals something, then conceals even more. We'll see examples of this especially in Chapters 5-7. PART 1 — Chapter 3: Temunah vs. Tavnit The Problem Rambam Is Solving Rambam continues to insist Hashem is incorporeal — not yet on philosophical grounds (that comes later), but purely on textual/linguistic grounds. The Troubling Verse (Bamidbar / Beha'alotecha) After Aharon and Miriam spoke about Moshe, Hashem reprimands them: פה אל פה אדבר בו ומראה ולא בחידות, ותמונת ה' יביט \"Mouth to mouth I speak with him... and he gazes at the form (temunah) of Hashem\" This seems blatantly corporeal — Moshe \"gazes at the form of God.\" Why We Can't Just Call It \"Intellectual\" The problem is the parallel verse in Va'etchanan (the revelation at Sinai): כי לא ראיתם כל תמונה ביום דבר ה' אליכם בחורב מתוך האש \"You saw no form on the day Hashem spoke to you at Chorev from the fire\" So far fine. But it continues: פן תשחיתון ועשיתם פסל תמונת כל סמל, תבנית זכר או נקבה, תבנית כל בהמה... תבנית כל ציפור כנף... תבנית כל דגה \"...lest you make an idol, the form (temunah) of any symbol, the form (tavnit) of male or female, the form of any animal, bird, fish...\" Here temunah and tavnit are used interchangeably, both describing clearly physical things — animals, birds, fish, sculpted images. So temunah looks unavoidably physical. The challenge: If temunah is physical, then u'temunat Hashem yabit implies a physical God. Rambam's Solution — They Are NOT Synonyms חושבים שמשמעות תמונה ותבנית בלשון עברית אחת היא, ואין הדבר כן \"People think temunah and tavnit mean the same thing — this is not so.\" Tavnit — Always Physical משום שתבנית הוא מונח הגזור מן הבנה \"Tavnit derives from the root בנה (bana) — to build.\" It means structure, form — square, round, triangular, etc. Always physical. Examples (all physical): Source Phrase Terumah תבנית המשכן — the form of the Mishkan and its vessels Terumah בתבניתם אשר אתה מראה בהר — the form shown at the mountain Va'etchanan תבנית כל ציפור כנף — the form of birds Yechezkel תבנית יד — the form of a hand (in his prophetic vision) Yechezkel תבנית האולם — the form of the hall (Beit HaMikdash) The Conclusion on Tavnit ולכן אין העברית מחילה כלל את הביטויים האלו על תיאורים הקשורים באלוה \"Therefore Hebrew never applies tavnit to descriptions connected with God.\" Tavnit always means something physical → it is never used in conjunction with the divine in Tanakh. Temunah — Three Meanings ואילו תמונה הוא מונח מסופק בעל שלוש משמעויות \"Temunah is a polysemous word with three meanings.\" Meaning 1 — Physical Form (Like Tavnit) The simple sensory image of something — a chair, a table — objectively there. ועשיתם פסל תמונת כל — \"You shall make an idol, the form of anything\" כי לא ראיתם כל תמונה — \"You saw no form\" (at Sinai) This is the interchangeable-with-tavnit usage from Va'etchanan. Meaning 2 — Imagined Physical Form Something physical, but existing in the imagination , not objectively touchable. From Iyov (Elifaz consoling Iyov, recounting a night vision): בשעיפים מחזיונות לילה בנפול תרדמה על אנשים... יעמוד ולא אכיר מראהו, תמונה לנגד עיני \"In the disquieting visions of the night... it stood, but I could not recognize its appearance — a form (temunah) before my eyes\" Elifaz saw a mysterious spirit with a form in his dream — physical, but imaginative, not objective. Meaning 3 — Pure Intellectual Essence Something purely incorporeal — the true essence apprehended by the intellect. ולפי המובן השלישי הזה נאמר אצלו יתעלה תמונה \"In this third sense, temunah is used regarding Hashem.\" ותמונת ה' יביט — מובנו: שאת אמיתת השם ישיג \"'He gazes at the temunah of Hashem' means: he apprehends the truth of God.\" What Does the Third Meaning Actually Mean? From Rambam's own Yesodei HaTorah: כלומר שאין שם משל אלא רואה הדבר על בוריו, בלא חידה ובלא משל \"There are no allegories — he sees the matter clearly, without riddle or parable.\" Moshe — unlike all other prophets — was able to attain the clearest intellectual understanding of Hashem, without riddles, without constraints, without fear. That's what u'temunat Hashem yabit means. Two Major Questions on Chapter 3 Question 1 — Where Is Tavnit Used With Hashem Outside Tanakh? Rambam says tavnit is never used with the divine in Tanakh . But there's a famous case in Rabbinic literature . The Answer: Yishtabach — and more directly, the Sheva Berachot : ברוך אתה ה'... אשר יצר את האדם בצלמו, בצלם דמות תבניתו \"...Who created man in His image, in the image of the likeness of His form (tavnito)\" This is a major problem. If tavnit always means physical (as Rambam insisted), and the beracha says man was created b'tzelem demut tavnito — does that make tzelem and demut physical too? But we established in Chapters 1-2 that tzelem and demut are intellectual! The Resolutions Option A — Split the beracha: Tzelem and demut = intellectual; tavnit = physical. Highly difficult. Option B — Abrabanel (the answer we must take): לשון תנ\"ך לחוד, לשון חכמים לחוד \"The language of Tanakh is its own; the language of the Sages is its own.\" In Tanakh, tavnit is never used with God. But in the language of Chazal, tavnit can connote something spiritual/immaterial. Rambam himself codifies this exact beracha (from the Gemara in Ketubot) — so we can't claim he had a different text. We must say Chazal used tavnit differently than Tanakh does. (Rabbi Kada acknowledged this is uncomfortable, but it's what we have to say. A student noted this seems to push against Rambam — surely Chazal understood Tanakh's terms precisely. Possible responses: Chazal relied on the words appearing alongside tzelem and demut; or possibly there wasn't a sharp temunah/tavnit distinction for them. It remains a genuine difficulty.) Question 2 — The Contradiction in the Verses About Seeing God This is a contradiction even on a corporeal reading: Beha'alotecha: ותמונת ה' יביט — Moshe gazes at the form of Hashem Ki Tisa: Moshe asks to see Hashem, and Hashem replies ופני לא יראו — \"My face shall not be seen\" (you may see My \"back,\" not My \"front\") How can Moshe both gaze at the temunah of Hashem AND be told you cannot see My face ? The Resolution \"You cannot see My face\" = an allegory: אמיתת מציאותו כפי שהיא אינה ניתנת להשגה — the true essence of God as it is cannot be apprehended. Ramchal (Da'at Tevunot) elaborates: you can only \"see My back\" — never fully attain understanding of God Rabbi Yehuda HaLevi (Kuzari): אילו ידעתיו הייתיו — \"If I knew Him, I would be Him\" — to fully comprehend God would be to equate yourself with God, which is impossible So u'temunat Hashem yabit means: Moshe grasped the highest level any human can grasp of the divine — not the complete essence (the \"face\"), but the maximum a human can attain (the \"back\"). Hashem tells Aharon and Miriam: Moshe has attained this level; you have not — therefore do not speak ill of him. Chapter 3 Summary Word Meaning(s) Used with God? Tavnit Physical form ONLY (from בנה, to build) Never in Tanakh Temunah (1) Physical form, (2) Imagined physical form, (3) Pure intellectual essence Yes — in sense (3) When the Torah says Moshe saw the temunah of Hashem, it means the highest intellectual apprehension possible for a human — not a physical image, and not the complete comprehension of God's essence (which is impossible). PART 2 — Chapter 4: The Three Verbs of Seeing (Sight and Insight) Why This Chapter Follows Because Rambam quoted u'temunat Hashem yabit in Chapter 3, he now examines the verb יביט (yabit — \"gazes\") and the other verbs of seeing used regarding Hashem. The Three Verbs דע ששלושת הביטויים ראה, הבט, וחזה משמשים במובן של ראיית העין, ושלושתם הושאלו להשגת השכל The three verbs — רָאָה (ra'ah), הִבִּיט (habit), חָזָה (chazah) — all denote physical eye-sight, AND all three are borrowed for intellectual apprehension. Sight (with the eyes) vs. Insight (with the mind/brain). Verb 1 — רָאָה (Ra'ah) — to see Physical sight וירא והנה באר בשדה — \"He saw a well in the field\" (Yaakov — physical seeing) Intellectual insight ולבי ראה הרבה חכמה ודעת — \"My heart saw much wisdom and knowledge\" (Shlomo) How can a heart see wisdom? This must be insight , not sight. With the Divine = Insight Phrase Meaning ראיתי את ה' — \"I saw Hashem on the throne\" Intellectual insight וירא אליו ה' באלוני ממרא — Hashem appeared to Avraham Insight/vision וירא אלהים כי טוב — \"God saw it was good\" God recognized it was good הראני נא את כבודך — \"Show me Your glory\" Give me deeper insight ויראו את אלהי ישראל — \"They saw the God of Israel\" An insight of God Why Eyes Can't Apply to God משום שהעיניים אינן משיגות אלא גוף, וגם זה רק מכיוון אחד, ורק חלק מן המקרים שלו \"The eyes apprehend only a body — and only from one angle, only some of its aspects.\" When I see something, I see only its front, only one layer, only certain features (its color, etc.). God has no body and no aspects to see partially. So \"God saw\" must mean intellectual apprehension. Saadia Gaon's Remarkable Opposing View (Laid out here, to be returned to in Chapter 5) In Emunot VeDe'ot (Essay 2) , Saadia Gaon — the first authority to insist so sharply that any corporeality of God is heretical — nonetheless holds: When prophets \"see\" Hashem, it is a PHYSICAL seeing with their eyes — but not of God Himself. Rather: יש לבורא אור בראהו, והראהו לנביאים להיות ראיה להם על כל דברי הנבואה \"The Creator has a light He created, and showed it to the prophets as a sign for all the prophecy they would hear.\" When a prophet says ראיתי כבוד ה' ( \"I saw the glory of Hashem\" ) → he saw this created light. Makes sense. When it says ראיתי את ה' ( \"I saw Hashem\" ) → this is a hester (an ellipsis); it means I saw the Kevod Hashem — the created light. Saadia's proof: In Mishpatim, ויראו את אלהי ישראל ( \"they saw the God of Israel\" ) is followed by ומראה כבוד ה' כאש אוכלת ( \"the appearance of the glory of Hashem like a consuming fire\" ) — showing that \"seeing God\" really means seeing the glory of God. On \"you may see My back, not My front\": Even this created light is overwhelming — other prophets collapse when they see it. Moshe could withstand it but only partially; the full light/image cannot be attained. Rambam Rejects This Rambam holds ra'ah with the divine = insight, NOT sight. He will indirectly reference and reject Saadia's view in Chapter 5. Verb 2 — הִבִּיט (Habit) — to gaze Physical sight אל תביט אחריך — \"Do not look behind you\" (fleeing Sedom; Lot's wife did look) ונבט לארץ והנה חושך — \"We gaze at the earth and behold darkness\" (Yeshayahu) Intellectual insight לא הביט אוון ביעקב — \"He did not gaze at iniquity in Yaakov\" You don't see iniquity with the eyes — only with the mind. (Machloket: Rashi/Ramban say this refers to Hashem; Onkelos/Saadia/Rambam say it refers to Bilam. Either way, the \"gazing\" is mental, not physical.) The Famous Case — והביטו אחרי משה והביטו אחרי משה עד בואו האהל \"And they would gaze after Moshe until he entered the tent\" Chazal say this means insightful gazing — the people pondered Moshe's actions and words. There are two Midrashic interpretations: The positive reading: They would watch Moshe with admiration, trying to apprehend his greatness, his wisdom, his level of prophecy. The painful reading (Yalkut Shimoni): Some spoke badly of Moshe — \"Look how large/wealthy he is! He eats from the Jews, drinks from the Jews — everything he has is from our donations. He ran the Mishkan project; of course he pocketed money!\" When Moshe heard this, he insisted on giving a full public accounting — which is why we have אלה פקודי המשכן (a complete reckoning of every donation). A powerful lesson on transparency for leaders — even Moshe Rabbeinu, the greatest of prophets, was subject to such talk. With the Divine = Insight Phrase Meaning הבט נא השמימה — \"Gaze now at the heavens\" (Avraham, in prophetic vision) Think heavenward, contemplate the universe כי ירא מהביט אל האלהים — \"He was afraid to gaze at God\" (burning bush) Afraid to contemplate God too deeply ותמונת ה' יביט Moshe's intellectual apprehension והבט אל עמל לא תוכל — \"You cannot look upon wrongdoing\" (Chavakuk) You cannot perceive wrong and stay silent Verb 3 — חָזָה (Chazah) — to perceive Physical sight ותחז בציון עינינו — \"Our eyes will see Zion('s destruction)\" (Micha — physical) Intellectual insight אשר חזה ישעיהו על ירושלים — \"What Yeshayahu envisioned concerning Jerusalem\" היה דבר ה' אל אברהם במחזה — \"The word of Hashem came to Avraham in a vision\" With the Divine ויחזו את האלהים — \"They perceived God\" → intellectual insight Why These Three Verbs? (The Order) Rambam explains the gradation, and why he presents them in this order: Verb Quality רָאָה (ra'ah) Simple, sudden seeing — without focus הִבִּיט (habit) To gaze, to stare — more focused חָזָה (chazah) Deep, penetrative sight — piercing This is why chazah is most often used for intellectual visions — it connotes a piercing, penetrating perception. (Think of eyes that \"pierce\" you.) The order makes sense: Even though Rambam came off Chapter 3 having discussed yabit (habit), he starts with ra'ah because it's the most basic form of seeing, then habit (focused gazing), then chazah (penetrative sight). Chapter 4 Summary All three verbs — ra'ah, habit, chazah — can mean physical sight. Tanakh also uses all three for intellectual insight. Whenever they are used in conjunction with the divine, they denote intellectual seeing (insight), not physical seeing (sight). Sight = eyes. Insight = mind. With God, always insight. PART 3 — Beginning Chapter 5: The Introduction (Aristotle & Caution) Why Rambam Digresses Here Because Chapter 4 brought verses about people \"seeing\" God — Moshe afraid to gaze at the burning bush , yet the elders gazed at God — Rambam now digresses to explain: Why was Moshe afraid while the elders were not? What does this reveal? This will be developed next week. But first, an introduction. Rambam's Admiration for Aristotle This is the first place we see Rambam's great admiration for Aristotle (ראש הפילוסופים — \"the chief of the philosophers\"). כאשר החל ראש הפילוסופים לחקור ולהביא ראיות לדברים נסתרים מאד, התנצל \"When the chief philosopher began to investigate and bring proofs for very hidden matters, he gave an apology/introduction.\" Aristotle explained: a reader should not ascribe to him brazenness (chutzpah) or arrogance for theorizing about such esoteric matters (they had no laboratories or evidence — only theory). Rather, it reflects his diligence and zeal to attain true beliefs as far as a human can. Rambam on Aristotle (from his letters) Aristotle reached the peak of human intellect — short only of what can be attained through prophecy (implying he was close to the level of prophecy) A person should spend most of his philosophical study on Aristotle, not other philosophers Yet Rambam is unafraid to disagree with Aristotle on major matters throughout the Moreh The Context of Aristotle's Caution Aristotle was discussing the motion of the celestial bodies (a geocentric model, spheres moving around the earth, the outermost closest to the Matzui Rishon — the Prime Mover). He noted a puzzle (more motion observed in closer bodies than further ones) — based on the science of his time, now known to be wrong, but the principle stands: approach elusive matters with awe. Rambam's Parallel Introduction — Tread With Caution שראוי לאדם שלא יתפרץ לדבר הגדול והנכבד הזה מבלי שיאמן את עצמו במדעים ובדעות, ויתקן את מידותיו היטב \"A person should not burst into this great and exalted matter without first training himself in the sciences and beliefs, and rectifying his character thoroughly.\" The Prerequisites for Studying Theology/Metaphysics 1. Scientific and Logical Training Master the sciences and know how logic works. This is why Rambam's first work (age ~17) was Sefer HaHigayon — a book on logic — written before his commentary on the Mishnah. Before you can write anything on Torah, you must understand how logic works. 2. Rectification of Character ויתקן את מידותיו היטב, ויאמית תאוותיו ודמיונותיו \"Rectify his character thoroughly, and put an end to his desires and fancies.\" The Profound Principle on Character and Truth (from Section 2) Rambam writes something powerful (shared here though it comes later): כי כשהאדם מוצא את עצמו... נוטה לתאוות והנאות, או מעדיף רוגז וכעס... הוא יטעה לעולם ויכשל בכל מקום שילך, כי יבקש דעות שיסייעו לו במה שטבעו נוטה אליו \"When a person finds himself inclined toward desires and pleasures, or toward anger — whether inherited or self-trained — he will always err and stumble wherever he goes, because he will seek out theories that support what his nature inclines toward.\" A person with corrupted character cannot reach intellectual truth — because they will be intellectually dishonest to comply with their desires. The Aldous Huxley Illustration The 20th-century British author Aldous Huxley, a lifelong near-atheist, admitted near the end of his life that he wanted there to be no God — to free himself (and his contemporaries) sexually and otherwise. This is precisely Rambam's point: unrectified character distorts intellectual conclusions. A person bends their philosophy to fit their desires. The Method אל לו לעצור בדעה ראשונה שתעלה בדעתו... אלא יבוש ויעצור ויעמוד עד שתתקדם בדרכה \"He should not stop at the first thought that arises... but be patient, cautious, and advance step by step, with modesty.\" Don't grab the first artificial thought. Be patient. Advance in order. Be humble. Where This Is Going (Next Week) The whole point of this introduction: it explains the difference between Moshe and the elders. Moshe was afraid to gaze at the burning bush — \"I'm not ready for this yet\" (proper caution) The elders gazed at God too early — without proper preparation Rambam will develop this crucial theme next week in Chapters 5-7, which he describes as really, really important. Tonight's Summary Chapter 3 — Temunah vs. Tavnit Tavnit = physical form only (from בנה); never used with God in Tanakh Temunah = three meanings: (1) physical, (2) imagined physical, (3) pure intellectual essence U'temunat Hashem yabit = Moshe's highest intellectual apprehension of God — not the complete essence (you cannot see the \"face\") The Sheva Berachot uses tavnit with God → resolved by Abrabanel: lashon Tanakh and lashon Chachamim differ Chapter 4 — The Three Verbs of Seeing Ra'ah, Habit, Chazah all mean physical sight AND intellectual insight With the divine → always insight , never sight Saadia Gaon disagrees (prophets physically see a created light) — Rambam rejects this Gradation: ra'ah (simple) → habit (focused) → chazah (penetrating) Chapter 5 (Begun) — The Introduction Like Aristotle, approach metaphysics with caution Prerequisites: (1) scientific and logical training, (2) rectified character Corrupted character distorts intellectual truth — you seek theories that justify your desires This sets up the contrast between Moshe (cautious) and the elders (premature) Coming Up Next Week Chapter 5 (continued) — Moshe vs. the elders; who may gaze and when Chapter 6 — Further fundamental concepts These chapters (5, 6, 7) are among the most important in this section of the Moreh B'Ezrat Hashem","d3N7A9pF1JA":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Shulchan Aruch, Orach Chaim Siman: 320 (שכ) Topic: מלאכת דש — Threshing & Squeezing (third shiur on this siman) Tonight's seifim: 4 (clarifications), 5 (boser), 6 (lemons), 7 (kvashim u'shlakot), 8 (fish), 9 (snow & ice) Recap of the Last Two Shiurim Foundational Concepts Established Melacha of Dash (in the Mishkan) — extracting wheat kernels from their husks. Toledah of Mifarek / Sechita — extracting liquid from a solid (e.g., olive oil, wine). Three categories of fruit: Category Examples Status 1 — Zeitim v'Anavim Olives, grapes Forbidden d'Oraita 2 — Tutim u'Rimonim Berries, pomegranates, apples, oranges (commonly squeezed) Forbidden d'Rabbanan 3 Fruits not normally squeezed Permitted to squeeze Gezeirat Mashkin Shezavu — liquid that flows on its own from Cat. 1 or Cat. 2 fruit is rabbinically forbidden (gezeira lest one come to squeeze). Squeezing in the mouth (derech achilah) — permitted. Pre-crushed olives/grapes — liquid that continues to ooze afterwards is permitted (covered last shiur, seifim 2-3). The Foundation We Ended On (Seif 4 + Seif 7) סוחט אדם אשכול ענבים לתוך קדרה אבל לא לתוך קערה \"A person may squeeze a cluster of grapes into a pot [containing food], but not into a [empty] bowl\" The Gemara's principle: משקה הבא לאוכל כאוכל דמי \"Liquid that comes [into] food has the status of food\" Because the liquid is going directly onto a solid, we treat it as solid-from-solid (not a Dash violation). Rabbeinu Chananel disagreed — held it's forbidden even onto food. Everyone else (Rambam, Rosh, etc.) disagreed with him. Lechatchila one is machmir for olives and grapes only (d'Oraita), but mei'ikar hadin even those are permitted. PART 1 — Five Clarifications on Squeezing Onto Food Several critical clarifications must be made on the meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel principle. Clarification 1 — Even Fruits Set Aside for Liquid Even if the fruit was set aside to be squeezed for juice ( omeid l'mashkeh ) — bought before Shabbat with the intention to make juice after Shabbat — you may still squeeze it directly onto a solid on Shabbat. The principle applies regardless of the owner's broader intention with the fruit. Clarification 2 — Must Be DIRECTLY Onto the Solid The whole heter is only when squeezing goes directly from the fruit onto the food at the moment of extraction . What Is NOT Permitted Squeeze an orange into an empty cup Then pour from the cup onto a salad (even seconds later) Even though your intention from the start was to put it onto the salad Everyone agrees this is forbidden. The Reason At the moment of extraction , the liquid is emerging into an empty space. That makes the action liquid-from-solid — exactly what Dash forbids. What you intend to do with the liquid afterwards doesn't change the status of the extraction itself. D'Oraita or D'Rabbanan? For olives/grapes (Cat. 1): debated in Acharonim Radbaz held: since intention is for food, it's not d'Oraita Either way: assur — don't do it. Clarification 3 — The Sugar Cube at the Bottom Trick Chacham Ovadia warns against a common misunderstanding. People hear the heter of meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel and think: \"I'll put a sugar cube at the bottom of my cup and squeeze 4-5 oranges over it — now I have orange juice and it's permissible because I squeezed onto a solid!\" This Does NOT Work The heter has specific conditions. The liquid emerging onto the solid must be: Nivla — absorbed into the solid, OR Metakken — improving or enhancing the solid Common-Sense Test Action Permitted? Reason Squeeze lemon onto fish ✅ Enhances the fish Squeeze olives onto salad ✅ Adds to the salad Squeeze a few drops onto a sugar cube for flavor ✅ Improving the sugar Squeeze 4 oranges onto one sugar cube to make juice ❌ Not the heter — you're just squeezing into a cup Use common sense. The amount must be reasonable — what the solid can absorb or be enhanced by. Clarification 4 — Must Leave Edible Food Inside (Shulchan Aruch Harav / Ba'al HaTanya) You cannot squeeze so thoroughly that nothing edible remains in the fruit. The Logic The whole heter is that you're treating the squeezed liquid as a solid coming from a solid — both edible. If the fruit you're squeezing has nothing edible left: You're now extracting an \"edible\" (the liquid) from an inedible (the empty peel) That's the original Dash — extracting edible from inedible encasement The classic case from earlier hilchot: extracting peas from inedible pods is forbidden Practical Implication When squeezing a lemon, orange, or any fruit onto food on Shabbat, don't squeeze it bone-dry . Leave some pulp/flesh inside the peel. Clarification 5 — Squeezing Onto Sugar to Then Add to Tea What if I want to put lemon in my tea, but want to do it via this two-step approach? Squeeze the lemon onto a sugar cube in a separate cup Immediately pour the lemon-soaked sugar into the tea Mishna Berura's Ruling: Permitted ✅ The Mishna Berura rules clearly that this is permitted, even though your full intention from the start is to pour it into a liquid. Why At the moment of extraction , the liquid is going onto a solid (the sugar cube). That's the operative moment for Dash. What happens afterwards (mixing with tea) is not the issue. The Chazon Ish's Dissent The Chazon Ish brought proofs to argue that since your intention is to put it into a liquid, the lemon juice retains the status of liquid — and therefore this should be forbidden. The Halacha Most Poskim disagree with the Chazon Ish. The Aruch HaShulchan rules permitted The mainstream halacha follows the Mishna Berura Even if your intention is to immediately pour it into a liquid afterwards — at the time of extraction, it's onto a solid, and that is permitted. Bottom Line on Squeezing Onto Food You may squeeze any fruit directly onto food on Shabbat — salads, fish, sugar — and this is k'din (according to halacha) even with olives and grapes mei'ikar hadin. Those who want to be machmir like Rabbeinu Chananel only need to be careful with olives and grapes (d'Oraita). PART 2 — Seif 7: Kvashim u'Shlakot (Pickles & Cooked Vegetables) Definitions Kvashim — pickled items (cucumbers, pickles in brine) Shlakot — vegetables cooked with liquid (carrots in chicken soup) The Rule Purpose of Squeezing Status To improve the pickle/vegetable (get rid of excess soggy liquid) ✅ Permitted To use/drink the liquid that comes out ❌ Forbidden Rabbeinu Chananel? Here we are not machmir for Rabbeinu Chananel because the issue is only d'Rabbanan. Practical Applications Open pickle jar on Shabbat → pickles are too soggy → squeeze excess brine off ✅ Carrots in your chicken soup are too soggy → squeeze them off ✅ Saving the brine to drink as pickle juice → ❌ (though hard to imagine wanting to) PART 3 — Seif 8: Squeezing Fish הסוחט דג לצירו דינו כסוחט כבשים ושלוקות \"One who squeezes a fish for its liquid has the same law as one who squeezes pickles and cooked vegetables\" The same rule applies: Purpose Status To improve the fish (get rid of excess oil/liquid) ✅ Permitted To use the fish-liquid for something else ❌ Forbidden Even though lots of liquid may come out — as long as the purpose is to make the fish more palatable, you may squeeze. PART 4 — Seif 6: Lemons — The Shortest Seif, Most Debated Halacha מותר לסחוט לימונים \"It is permitted to squeeze lemons\" This three-word seif is one of the most heavily debated halachot in the entire Shulchan Aruch. In the Beit Din, when testing dayanim on Shabbat halachot, this question of lemons consistently triggers an internal argument among the dayanim. The Dayanim deliberately stay away from this question. The Source — Rabbeinu Asher (the Rosh) The Rosh (Rabbeinu Asher), 700-800 years ago, wrote clearly that it is mutar to squeeze lemons on Shabbat . His Reasoning Lemons are Category 3 (fruits not commonly squeezed for their juice). Category 1: olives and grapes (d'Oraita) Category 2: berries, pomegranates, apples, oranges (commonly squeezed for juice you drink alone) Category 3: fruits not normally squeezed for standalone juice — and lemons fit here 500 years ago (and arguably today), people did not squeeze lemons to drink lemon juice alone. Lemon juice was always used with something else — water, sugar, food. The Beit Yosef's Question Rabbi Yosef Karo, living in Egypt while writing the Shulchan Aruch, observed something striking: \"In Egypt, I see everyone squeezes lemon — they squeeze lemons onto water with sugar inside to make lemonade. They put sugar in their cups, then squeeze the lemons in. And לא ראינו מי שפקפק בדבר — I have never seen anyone who questioned this practice on Shabbat.\" The Apparent Contradiction The Rosh said lemons are permitted because people don't squeeze them for juice . But the Beit Yosef is seeing the opposite — everyone IS squeezing them, in significant quantities, to make lemonade. Important Note: It's Not the Meshakeh Ha'ba El Ha'ochel Heter You might think: maybe the Beit Yosef thought this works because you're squeezing onto sugar (a solid). But this can't be the explanation, because: A reasonable amount onto a sugar cube — yes, that would be meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel But filling up cups of lemonade — that's far more than a sugar cube can absorb or be enhanced by So why are the people in Egypt doing it freely on Shabbat? The Beit Yosef gives two answers . The Beit Yosef's Answer 1 — Lemons Are Never Drunk Alone The Chachamim only gave a status of liquid (Category 2) to fruits whose juice is valued as a standalone drink . Fruit Drink Alone? Category Pomegranate ✅ People drink pomegranate juice Cat. 2 Apple, orange ✅ People drink apple/orange juice Cat. 2 Strawberries, berries ✅ Berry juice Cat. 2 Lemon ❌ No one drinks lemon juice alone Cat. 3 The whole reason the Chachamim gave a status of liquid to Category 2 fruits is because people machshiv (give importance to) the juice as a drink in its own right. Since no one drinks lemon juice alone — it's always added to something — the Chachamim never gave it the status of liquid. It permanently stays Category 3. The Beit Yosef's Answer 2 — They're Squeezing It Into Something Else A subtly different reason: because they're squeezing the lemon directly into water with sugar (not alone), this shows the lemon juice doesn't have a standalone status. The heter, according to this reason, is specific to the act : you're squeezing into something else (the sugar water). The Critical Nafka Mina Between the Two Answers Can I squeeze many lemons on Shabbat afternoon into a jug alone — nothing else — and then add water and sugar later in the day? (Example: hot summer day, 60 kids coming over, want to make lemonade in advance.) According to Answer 1 (Beit Yosef) ✅ Permitted. Lemons are never drunk alone — their status as Category 3 doesn't depend on what you mix them with at the moment. They are permanently Category 3. According to Answer 2 (Beit Yosef) ❌ Forbidden. The heter only works when squeezing directly into something else. Squeezing alone into a jug — even with intent to add water/sugar later — doesn't get the heter. What Does the Shulchan Aruch Actually Rule? He writes simply: \"מותר לסחוט לימונים\" — without specifying which reason. What's Clearly Permitted (Both Reasons Agree) Squeezing lemons directly into tea — Permitted according to both Beit Yosef answers. This is the clear simple reading of the Shulchan Aruch. What's Debated Squeezing lemons into an empty cup with intent to add water/sugar later — permitted per Answer 1, forbidden per Answer 2. The Mishna Berura's Stringent Position The Mishna Berura wrote that times have changed : בזמן הזה שידוע שממלאים חבית למאות לשתות עם פאנץ' באיזה מקומות, צריך עיון גדול אם מותר לסחטן בשבת לתוך משקה, דאפשר דדמי לתותים ורימונים דנעשו משקין בפני עצמן Nowadays it's known that they fill barrels of lemon juice in some places to drink as a kind of punch — it requires great consideration whether it is permitted to squeeze them on Shabbat into liquid, because perhaps it now resembles berries and pomegranates which are made into standalone drinks. His Ruling ועל כן צריך להיזהר שיסחט מקודם על הסוכר בפני עצמו דהוה כמשקה הבא לאוכל Therefore one must be careful to squeeze first onto sugar by itself — using the meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel heter (with a reasonable amount onto the sugar). Why the Mishna Berura Diverges from Both Beit Yosef Answers Answer 1 would still permit (lemons still not drunk alone) Answer 2 would forbid (since the derech is now to make standalone lemon juice) The Mishna Berura takes Answer 2 — that the current practice determines the status. Since now there's a lemon juice industry, Answer 2 fails, and the heter falls. This is why Ashkenazim do NOT squeeze lemons directly into tea on Shabbat. They follow the Mishna Berura. The Ben Ish Chai's Position — Also Machmir The Ben Ish Chai is machmir, ruling like the Mishna Berura that lemons should be squeezed onto a solid first. His Beautiful Mnemonic Based on Yaakov's vision of the ladder: סולם מוצב ארצה \"A ladder set on the ground\" The word סולם is rashei teivot: ס = ס וכר (Sukar — sugar) ל = ל ימון (Limon — lemon) מ = מ ים (Mayim — water) The Practical Lemonade Method (Following the Sulam Order) Sugar first in the bowl/cup Lemon squeezed onto the sugar Water added last Never: put the water first and then squeeze lemons in. Chacham Ovadia's Lenient Position Chacham Ovadia rules like Answer 1 of the Beit Yosef : Lemons are never drunk alone (still true even today — commercial \"lemon juice\" is for cooking/mixing, not standalone drinking) Therefore lemons remain Category 3 The first reason of the Beit Yosef is logical and applies even now One may squeeze lemons even into an empty cup on Shabbat and add water/sugar later One may squeeze lemons directly into tea Chacham Ben Tzion Abba Shaul also ruled leniently. Practical Sephardi Ruling The simple reading of the Shulchan Aruch supports the lenient position Since it's a Rabbinic safek, one can be lenient One may squeeze lemons directly into tea One may even squeeze into an empty cup and use later (though the sulam method is also fine) Mark's Question — Pour Lemon Into Water, or Water Into Lemon? If you have sugar water and squeezed lemon separately, is there a difference between which way you pour them together? According to Answer 1 (Beit Yosef) No difference. Lemons are permanently not classified as liquid because they're never drunk alone. Direction of pouring is irrelevant. According to Answer 2 (Beit Yosef) Yes, there IS a difference. The heter is specifically that you're squeezing the lemon into something else. So you'd need to squeeze the lemon into water (not water into the lemon). Bottom Line Since this is a safek d'Rabbanan and the Beit Yosef explicitly testifies \"I have never seen anyone who questioned\" the practice, one can be lenient — particularly per Answer 1. Chacham Ovadia's Important Caveat — Kalei HaBishul When squeezing lemon into tea, do not squeeze into a kli rishon (the original kettle from the fire). Squeeze into a kli sheni (a cup that has been poured from the kettle) — because lemons are kalei habishul (easily cooked), and bishul could be an issue in a kli rishon. The Borer Concern There could also be a borer issue when squeezing lemons: You're separating the desirable juice from the undesirable pulp/seeds The Resolution Apply the principle of guf echad (one body) — something coming naturally from one body is not Borer This heter works lechatchila only samuch l'seudah (close to consumption) Practical Implication Squeezing lemons should be done close to when the juice will be used — not hours in advance. (This is one reason many people squeeze lemons before Shabbat.) PART 5 — Seif 5: Unripe Grapes (Boser) יש מי שאומר דהוא הדין לבוסר שמותר לסחוט אותו לתוך האוכל, ורבינו תם אוסר בבוסר שאינו ראוי לאכילה The Machloket Rishonim Rabbeinu Yosef: Just as you may squeeze ripe grapes onto food ( meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel ), you may also squeeze unripe grapes ( boser ) onto food. Rabbeinu Tam: Forbidden. The heter only works when the fruit is itself edible. Unripe grapes are inedible. Rabbeinu Tam's Reasoning — Two Explanations in the Acharonim Explanation 1 — Borer You're separating something edible (the juice) from something inedible (the unripe pulp). This is borer. (If this is the reason, samuch l'seudah would be permitted.) Explanation 2 — Dash (the main reason) The heter of meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel depends on the squeezing being from edible to edible. Since boser is inedible: You're extracting an \"edible\" (the juice) from an \"inedible\" (the unripe pulp/peel) That's the classic Dash — like the example earlier about edible food in an inedible pod This is exactly the same logic as Clarification 4 above (Shulchan Aruch Harav's point about leaving edible fruit inside). How the Shulchan Aruch Rules He brings the first opinion as \"yesh mi she'omer\" (anonymous \"some say\") and then brings Rabbeinu Tam by name . Sha'ar HaTziyun's Rule (Mishna Berura) \"When the Shulchan Aruch brings the first opinion anonymously and then brings a Rishon by name as the second opinion — follow the named opinion.\" Therefore, halacha follows Rabbeinu Tam: unripe grapes (boser) may NOT be squeezed onto food on Shabbat. Chacham Ovadia Rules the Same Way Chacham Ovadia agrees mei'ikar hadin that Shulchan Aruch follows Rabbeinu Tam. Chacham Ovadia's Three-Fold Leniency for Immediate Use However, Chacham Ovadia permits squeezing boser onto food samuch l'seudah (immediately for use). His reasoning combines three considerations: Reason 1 There is still Rabbeinu Yosef as a senior Rishon who permits — we don't follow him, but his opinion is part of the safek. Reason 2 Maybe Rabbeinu Tam's reason is Borer (Explanation 1) — and we know samuch l'seudah is permitted for Borer. Reason 3 (Most Innovative) Even if Rabbeinu Tam's reason is Dash (Explanation 2): Most Rishonim hold that the heter of samuch l'seudah applies only to Borer , not Dash Why? Dash is a more \"industrial\" melacha — done en masse in the fields, threshing wheat. Borer is more personal. But the Rashba held that just as samuch l'seudah is permitted for Borer, it's also permitted for Dash Combining all three svarot together — Chacham Ovadia rules that boser may be squeezed onto food on Shabbat only if for immediate use . Practical Ruling Squeezing boser onto a salad samuch l'seudah → ✅ Permitted (Chacham Ovadia) Squeezing boser hours in advance → ❌ Forbidden PART 6 — Wrapping Up Part 1 of Siman 320 This completes the Toledah of Mefarek/Sechita — the d'Oraita and d'Rabbanan laws of squeezing fruit on Shabbat. Summary of Squeezing (Seifim 1-8) Three categories of fruit Gezeirat mashkin shezavu Squeezing in the mouth (derech achilah) Pre-crushed olives/grapes The principle of meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel The five clarifications on this principle Kvashim u'shlakot Fish Lemons — the famous debate Unripe grapes (boser) PART 7 — Beginning Part 2 of Siman 320: Snow and Ice (Seif 9) We now turn to the rabbinic prohibitions related to Dash. This second section runs from Seif 9 through Seif 14 , then concludes with material related to Melaben and a critical principle at the end of the chapter. The Gemara (Shabbat 51) אין מרסקין לא את השלג ולא את הברד בשבת בשביל שיזובו מימיהן. אבל נותן הוא לתוך הכוס או לתוך הקערה ואינו חושש One may not crush snow or hail on Shabbat to extract liquid from them. But one may place them into a cup or bowl, and there is no concern. Why Would Crushing Snow Be Forbidden? You have snow, you bring it inside, you crush it to make ice water. Why is this rabbinically forbidden? There are three explanations in the Rishonim — all agreeing this is d'Rabbanan , but differing on the underlying reason. Reason 1 — Rashi: Similar to Makeh BePatish משום דקא מוליד בשבת ודמי למלאכה שבורא המים האלו Because you are creating something — and this resembles the melacha that brings these waters into being. You're creating something new — solid becoming liquid. This is similar to makeh bepatish (the final blow that completes a creation). Clearly only rabbinic — you're not literally creating something out of nothing, just changing form. But the appearance of creating something new makes it forbidden d'Rabbanan. Reason 2 — Rambam: Similar to Dash The Rambam places this halacha in his discussion of Dash. His reasoning: When you squeeze fruit, you extract liquid from a solid. Here too, you're extracting liquid from a solid (snow → water). Rabbinically forbidden because of its similarity to the Toledah of Sechita under Dash. The fact that the Shulchan Aruch places this halacha within Siman 320 (Dash) strongly suggests he follows the Rambam's understanding. Reason 3 — Rabbeinu Baruch of Mainz (Ba'al HaTerumah): Nolad A third, minority opinion (which we saw briefly in Hilchot Bishul). The Ba'al HaTerumah held it's a form of nolad (newly-created on Shabbat) — like an apple that fell from a tree on Shabbat is muktzeh. Something genuinely new is being created — a kind of muktzeh issue. Critical Implication of the Third Opinion Even allowing solid → liquid to happen on its own would be a problem, because the prohibition focuses on the creation of the new, not the action. The Three Reasons Compared Reason Author Focus Allows passive melting? Makeh bePatish Rashi The crushing action creating something new ✅ Yes Dash Rambam Extracting liquid from solid ✅ Yes Nolad Ba'al HaTerumah The creation itself ❌ No Practical Question 1: Can I Take Ice Cubes Out to Melt on Their Own? You have a tray of ice cubes from before Shabbat. Can you take them out and leave them on the counter to melt on their own? Per Rashi and Rambam ✅ Permitted. The Gemara forbade crushing (an action). Passive melting is not crushing — and the Shulchan Aruch (following these reasons) clearly permits. Per Ba'al HaTerumah ❌ Forbidden. Even melting on its own creates something new. What Does the Shulchan Aruch Rule? וכן אם הניחם בחמה או כנגד המדורה והושרו — מותרין One may leave snow/ice in the sun or near a heater and they may melt on their own — permitted. The Shulchan Aruch clearly does NOT follow the Ba'al HaTerumah. The Rema's Position In Hilchot Bishul (Siman 318) , the Rema discussed the case of an empanada (meat pastry with congealed fat) being placed near the fire. The fat melts and becomes gravy. The Rema cited that yesh machmirim (some are stringent) following the Ba'al HaTerumah: ונהגו להחמיר ובמקום צורך יש להקל The custom is to be stringent, but in case of need one may be lenient. Practical Ruling Sephardim: Follow Shulchan Aruch — ✅ may take ice cubes out and leave them to melt Ashkenazim: Lechatchila avoid; b'di'avad / in case of need (extreme heat, baby needs milk, frozen wine for Shabbat, etc.) — permitted Practical Question 2: Can I Put Ice Cubes Into a Drink? This is universally permitted — agreed by all three reasons. Why All Three Agree Reason Why putting in liquid is permitted Rashi (makeh bepatish) You can't see the new water being created — it's hidden in the existing liquid Rambam (dash) The new \"liquid\" immediately merges with existing liquid — not standalone extraction Ba'al HaTerumah (nolad) The new liquid never has independent existence The Shulchan Aruch (Seif 9) השלג והברד אין מרסקין אותם — דהיינו לשברם חתיכות דקות כדי שיזובו מימיהן. אבל נותן לתוך הכוס של יין או מים, והוא נימוח מאליו ואינו חושש Snow and hail may not be crushed (broken into thin pieces to extract their liquid). But one may place them into a cup of wine or water, and they melt on their own, without concern. Practical Question 3: Can I Crush Ice Cubes Already in My Drink? You go to a function, they give you a drink with three-quarters ice cubes and only a quarter of actual drink. You want to crush the ice with your spoon to speed up the melting. May you? According to the Ba'al HaTerumah ✅ Permitted — the prohibition is on creating something new with independent existence; here it's immediately mixed in. According to Rashi/Rambam — A Machloket Acharonim One side (more stringent): The Gemara forbade crushing — that's an action The Gemara permitted only passive melting in liquid Therefore active crushing remains forbidden Other side (Magen Avraham, Mishna Berura mei'ikar hadin): The whole prohibition was about seeing the new water created When in liquid, you don't see anything new being created Therefore even active crushing is permitted Shulchan Aruch's Wording נותנו לתוך הכוס והוא נימוח מאליו ואינו חושש \"One may place it in the cup AND IT MELTS ON ITS OWN, and there is no concern.\" The phrase \"melts on its own\" (נימוח מאליו) suggests the heter is only for passive melting, not active crushing. Halacha Mishna Berura: Mei'ikar hadin permitted, but cites the strict view Ben Ish Chai: Forbidden — because the simple reading of Shulchan Aruch (Maran) implies forbidden Chacham Ovadia: Forbidden — follows Shulchan Aruch's apparent stringency Practical Bottom Line Sephardim: Do NOT crush ice cubes in your drink with a spoon or fork on Shabbat Ashkenazim: Mei'ikar hadin permitted (Mishna Berura), but better to refrain The Ben Ish Chai's Key Distinction — Sugar Is Different סוכר שקורין קנדי שמתקנים באירופא... מותר לרסקו בתוך המים למתקו בהם Sugar (called \"candy\" — from the Arabic kand , which is where the English word \"candy\" comes from) that is made in Europe — it is permitted to crush it in water to sweeten the water. The Logic — A Major Distinction יש חילוק גדול בין סוכר ובין שלג וברד There is a great distinction between sugar and snow/hail. Substance Nature of \"crushing in liquid\" Ice cubes Transformation — solid water becomes liquid water (change of form) Sugar cubes Dissolution — sugar disperses in water but remains sugar The Proof for Sugar Evaporate the water — and the sugar will return at the bottom. The sugar is simply dispersing into the water, not changing its essential nature. So even Maran (Shulchan Aruch), who would forbid crushing ice in water, would agree that sugar cubes may be crushed in tea. Practical Application ✅ You may use your spoon to dissolve a sugar cube in your tea on Shabbat (Sephardim and Ashkenazim alike). ❌ You may not use your spoon to crush ice cubes in your soda (Sephardim per Ben Ish Chai and Chacham Ovadia). A Final Important Note All of these prohibitions only apply when one is crushing or melting to make liquid (or a significant quantity of liquid). A few drops of incidental melting is not a concern. Complete Summary Table Squeezing Fruit (Seifim 1-8) Action Status Squeeze fruit directly onto food (meshakeh ha'ba el ha'ochel) ✅ Permitted (be machmir with olives/grapes) Squeeze fruit into empty cup, even with intent to add to food ❌ Forbidden Squeeze fruit so dry that nothing edible remains ❌ Forbidden Squeeze a few drops onto a small solid like sugar (reasonable amount) ✅ Permitted Squeeze multiple oranges onto one sugar cube to \"make juice\" ❌ Not the heter Squeeze fruit onto sugar, then pour into liquid ✅ Permitted (Mishna Berura) Squeeze kvashim/shlakot to discard excess liquid ✅ Permitted Squeeze kvashim/shlakot to use the liquid ❌ Forbidden Squeeze fish for the fish itself (excess liquid) ✅ Permitted Squeeze lemons via the sulam method (sugar → lemon → water) ✅ Universally permitted Squeeze lemons directly into tea ✅ Sephardim per Shulchan Aruch / ❌ Ashkenazim per Mishna Berura Squeeze lemons into empty cup for later use ✅ Chacham Ovadia / debated Squeeze lemons into kli rishon (the kettle itself) ❌ Forbidden (kalei habishul) Squeeze boser (unripe grapes) for immediate use samuch l'seudah ✅ Per Chacham Ovadia Squeeze boser in advance ❌ Forbidden Snow & Ice (Seif 9) Action Sephardim Ashkenazim Take ice cubes out of freezer to melt on their own ✅ Permitted Lechatchila avoid; b'di'avad permitted Put ice cubes in a drink ✅ Permitted ✅ Permitted Actively crush ice cubes in a drink with spoon ❌ Don't do it Mei'ikar hadin permitted, better not Crush sugar cube in tea (Ben Ish Chai's distinction) ✅ Permitted ✅ Permitted Crush snow/hail to extract water (active crushing) ❌ Forbidden ❌ Forbidden Coming Up Next Week Walking on snow on Shabbat The major and important discussion of washing hands with a bar of soap on Shabbat Shabbat Shalom","_KAHIjSrwsk":"Detailed Halachic Notes from Shiur Siman 494 - Hilchot Shavuot Overview This chapter appears after the completion of Hilchot Omer Contains three seifim (sections) covering the laws of Shavuot Located in Shulchan Aruch Siman 494 Seif 1 - Basic Laws of Shavuot Date and Timing When : Shavuot occurs on the 50th day after counting the Omer (49 days) This year : The 49th night falls on Motzaei Shabbat, making Shavuot begin Sunday night Prayer Service Structure Format : Same basic service structure as Pesach Key Difference : Instead of saying \"זמן חרותנו\" (time of our freedom), we say \"זמן מתן תורתנו\" (time of the giving of our Torah) Hallel : Complete Hallel is recited Torah Reading : Two Torah scrolls are taken out Torah Reading Details First Sefer Torah : Five aliyot from \"בחדש השלישי\" until the end of the section (Parshat Yitro - Ten Commandments) Maftir : Read from second Torah - \"יום הביכורים\" from Parshat Pinchas (discussing Shavuot sacrifices) Haftarah : From Yechezkel's vision of the Divine chariot (Merkavah), ending with \"ותשאני רוח\" Reason for Haftarah : Parallels the revelation at Mount Sinai, as Hashem revealed Himself both at Sinai and in Yechezkel's prophetic vision Critical Halachic Discussion: Timing of Maariv and Kiddush The Controversy Mishnah Berurah's Position : Wait until nightfall (tzet hakochavim) for both Maariv and Kiddush on Shavuot night Historical Development Original Sources : No mention in Gemara or Rishonim about waiting until nightfall for Shavuot First Mention : Rav Horowitz from Poland (450-500 years ago) first suggested this practice Later Development : The Taz (Turei Zahav) extended this to include Maariv as well The Reasoning (Rav Horowitz) Problem : Making Kiddush before nightfall ends the 49th day of Omer prematurely Source : \"שבע שבתות תמימות תהיינה\" - Seven complete weeks they shall be Logic : Early Kiddush detracts from the \"תמימות\" (completeness) of the 49th day Fundamental Halachic Issues with This Position 1. Contradicts Torah Mitzvah of Tosefet Yom Tov Torah Requirement : \"להוסיף מחול על הקודש\" - Add from weekday to holy time Application : We add time before Shabbat (minimum 2 minutes, typically 15-40 minutes) Yom Tov : Same mitzvah applies (either Torah or Rabbinic obligation) Logical Conclusion : Should bring in Shavuot early, not late 2. Lack of Early Sources Missing from Rishonim : No mention in any early authoritative sources Selective Application : Rishonim only mention waiting until nightfall for Pesach and Sukkot (due to Torah obligations of matzah and sukkah) Inconsistency : No mention in Shulchan Aruch for Shavuot specifically Sephardic Response and Position 18th Century - Chacham Laniado (Syria) Testimony : \"We have never seen anyone doing this\" Rationale : Why delay learning on Shavuot night? 19th Century - Rav Chaim Palachi (Izmir, Turkey) Position : \"This is not our custom, we don't do this\" Ruling : Permissible to bring in Shavuot early Contemporary Ruling - Chacham Ovadiah Compromise Position : If praying Maariv and making Kiddush after shkiah (sunset), certainly acceptable Reasoning : We are not stringent on this new din that requires waiting until full nightfall Maximum Stringency : If wanting to be careful, wait until sunset Practical Applications Ashkenazi Practice Standard : Wait until after 10:00 PM for Maariv Kiddush : Often not until 10:45-11:00 PM Result : Very late Yom Tov meal Sephardic Practice Northwest London Standard : Mincha/Maariv, then home after shkiah (~9:00 PM) for Kiddush Alternative : Regular early times (7:15-7:30 PM) with early Kiddush Principle : \"להוסיף מחול על הקודש\" - Adding to the holiness When Early Times Are Recommended Young children who want to participate in Yom Tov meal Family considerations (spouse, elderly relatives) When late timing compromises \"עונג ושמחה\" (joy and pleasure of Yom Tov) Practical challenges with very late meat meals Bottom Line Ruling Sephardim : Never accepted this stringency; early Kiddush is acceptable Ashkenazim : If late timing causes difficulty or detracts from Yom Tov joy, early times are permissible Universal Principle : Yom Tov must maintain its essential elements of joy and celebration The Custom of Staying Awake (Tikkun Leil Shavuot) Source in Mishnah Berurah Zohar : Early chassidim would stay awake all night studying Torah Arizal : Anyone who doesn't sleep at all and studies Torah is guaranteed to complete his year without harm Halachic Context No Early Sources : Not mentioned in Gemara, Rishonim, or Shulchan Aruch Only Mandatory All-Night : Shulchan Aruch only requires staying awake on Pesach night (\"עד שתחטפנו שינה\" - until sleep overtakes you) Historical Development : Practice spread from Zohar through Arizal to become nearly universal Practical Considerations Potential Problems : Sleeping until 5:00 PM the next day Missing proper Shacharit (praying while falling asleep) Ruining the entire next day of Yom Tov Sleeping through the second evening Alternative Approach : Have normal Yom Tov meal Sleep normal hours Wake up refreshed for proper Shacharit Spend the day productively learning Torah Underlying Reason : Rectification for Bnei Yisrael oversleeping before receiving the Torah Seif 2 - Second Day of Shavuot Torah Reading Parshah : \"כל הבכור\" until end of section (end of Parshat Re'eh - discusses festivals) Maftir : Same as first day (from Parshat Pinchas) Haftarah : From Chavakuk - \"והשם בהיכל קדשו... הופיע מהר פארן\" Reason for Haftarah Selection Reference : \"הר פארן\" refers to Har Sinai Biblical Source : Beginning of V'zot Haberachah - \"הופיע מהר פארן ואתה מרבבות קודש\" Theme : Hashem's appearance from various places, culminating at Har Sinai Seif 3 - Post-Shavuot Observances Prohibition of Fasting Rule : \"אסור להתענות במוצאי חג השבועות\" - Forbidden to fast on the day after Shavuot Reasons for Enhanced Status of Isru Chag Shavuot 1. Temple Service Consideration When Shavuot fell on Shabbat : Individual sacrifices (שלמי חגיגה, עולת ראיה) were offered the next day Difference from other holidays : Pesach/Sukkot: Seven days available for makeup sacrifices Shavuot: Only one day, so next day becomes sanctified for this purpose 2. Alternative Dating Theory Opinion : Torah was actually given on the seventh of Sivan Implication : What we call \"Isru Chag\" was actually the main day of Shavuot Result : Highest level of kedushah among all Isru Chag days Tachanun Practices Universal Rule \"ואין אומרים תחנון מתחילת ראש חודש עד ח' בו\" - No Tachanun from Rosh Chodesh until after Isru Chag Reasoning : Rosh Chodesh : No Tachanun Second of Sivan : Moshe receives instructions for three-day separation Three days of preparation : Sanctified time Shavuot : No Tachanun Isru Chag : Extended sanctity Different Customs Ashkenazi Practice : Resume Tachanun day after Isru Chag (8th of Sivan) Sephardic and Chassidic Practice : Full seven days from beginning of Shavuot This year's calculation : Shavuot begins Sunday night Day 1: Monday, Day 2: Tuesday... Day 7: Sunday Resume Tachanun: Monday morning (8th day) Outside Israel (Chutz La'aretz) : Kaf HaChaim : Count from second day of Yom Tov Result : Additional day without Tachanun (resume Tuesday) Customs and Practices Spreading Grass and Plants Practice : \"ונוהגים לשטוח עשבים בשבועות בבית הכנסת ובבתים\" Source and Development : Origin : Maharil in Germany (14th century, 600-700 years ago) Original : Only in synagogue Rema's Addition : Extended to homes Spread : Originally Ashkenazi, now accepted by Sephardim Reason : Biblical Source : \"הר סיני גם הצאן והבקר אל ירעו\" - sheep and cattle shouldn't graze Inference : There was grass around Har Sinai Commemoration : Spread grass and small plants to remember Sinai Trees in Synagogue - Controversial Practice Development of Custom Basis : Mishnah in Rosh Hashanah - \"בשבועות נידונים על פירות האילן\" (On Shavuot we are judged regarding fruit of trees) Reasoning : Pesach : Judgment on produce (wheat/omer) Shavuot : Judgment on tree fruits (harvested from Shavuot to Sukkot) Sukkot : Judgment on water for winter; all fruits gathered Practice : Put up trees to remember this judgment Vilna Gaon's Opposition Prohibition : Violates \"ובחקותיהם לא תלכו\" - don't follow ways of gentiles Reference : Christmas trees Ruling : Forbidden to put trees in synagogue Mainstream Halachic Response Two Interpretations of \"ובחקותיהם לא תלכו\" : Strict View (Vilna Gaon) : Any imitation of gentile practices forbidden Problem : Would prohibit medical gowns, seatbelts, safety regulations Example : Rav Moshe Feinstein's question about doctors' attire Mainstream View : Only forbidden if: Practice lacks clear, apparent reason Origins are known to be from pagan sources Permitted : Practices with logical, non-pagan reasons Application to Trees : Argument for Permission : Origins purely for Shavuot remembrance, no pagan connection Counter-argument : Unknown historical development; possible unconscious influence Practical Result : Most authorities permit, but controversy remains Dairy Foods Custom Rema's Ruling \"ונוהגין בכל מקום לאכול מחלי חלב ביום ראשון של שבועות\" - Customary everywhere to eat dairy foods on first day of Shavuot Historical Development Time Period : 1300s-1400s in Europe Original Practice : Dairy foods (not entire meals) Rema's Explanation Parallel to Pesach : Pesach : Two commemorations - Korban Pesach (afikoman) and Chagigah (egg) Shavuot : Remember \"שתי הלחם\" (two loaves) - unique Shavuot sacrifice Mechanism : Begin with dairy dish using challah Challah becomes dairy and cannot be reused with meat Bring fresh bread for meat course Result: Two loaves of bread (שתי הלחם) Important Clarifications What the Custom Is : Original : \"מאכלי חלב\" - dairy foods Not : Complete dairy meals Purpose : Symbolic remembrance, not meal replacement What the Custom Is Not : Incorrect : Substituting entire Yom Tov meal with dairy Halachic Problem : Goes against Yom Tov requirement for meat/chicken Exception : Personal dietary preferences or health considerations Proper Practice : Yom Tov Standard : Meat and chicken meals Accommodation : Dairy acceptable if person uncomfortable with meat Principle : Individual's simcha takes precedence over custom Summary Points for Review Key Halachic Principles Established Tosefet Yom Tov : Torah obligation to add time to holy days Simchat Yom Tov : Joy and celebration must be preserved Minhag vs. Halachah : Distinguish between customs and obligations Historical Development : Understanding origins prevents over-stringency Individual Needs : Personal circumstances can override general customs Practical Takeaways Timing : Sephardim may make early Kiddush; Ashkenazim have flexibility when needed Learning : Stay awake only if it enhances rather than detracts from Yom Tov Customs : Grass/plants universally accepted; trees controversial; dairy foods supplementary Post-Shavuot : No fasting on Isru Chag; varying Tachanun customs Questions for Further Study How do we balance historical customs with practical needs? What constitutes \"ובחקותיהם לא תלכו\" in contemporary contexts? How should individual simcha be weighed against communal minhagim? Next Topic: Beginning Hilchot Muktzeh","00zVTTtGsYE":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: Completing Chapter 1 · Beginning Chapter 2 Topic: Tzelem & Demut · The Creation Narrative Reexamined Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Why This Shiur Matters We are approaching Shavuot 5786 — the festival whose essence is Torah. Tonight Rambam will give us a whole new understanding of Bereishit that we read every year since childhood. Dayan Lopian z\"l once said: \"The older I get, the less I understand Bereishit.\" This evening's mission: finish Chapter 1 (the meaning of tzelem and demut ), then begin Chapter 2 — where Rambam takes apart and reconstructs the entire story of Gan Eden. Recap from Last Week Section 1 Structure (76 Chapters) Chapters 1-49: Refuting any physical conception of Hashem; explaining anthropomorphic language Chapters 50-69: Negative theology — God can only be described in negations Chapters 70-76: Against the Mutakallimun (Islamic theological philosophers) The Opening of Chapter 1 The verse נעשה אדם בצלמנו כדמותנו led people to corporealism They reasoned: we can't deny the pesukim, so God must have some physical form Even the \"refined\" version (bigger than human, more luminous, not flesh and blood) is still corporealism — and the Rambam will reject it equally Shiur Komah — that strange text with measurements of God's body — Rambam explicitly rejected in Teshuvah 117 (\"a great mitzvah to erase this book\") Corporealism doesn't just contradict Hashem's nature — it contradicts His unity The Vocabulary Foundation — Three Words Word Meaning Toar Physical form / bodily features Tzelem Essence — the defining nature that makes something what it is Demut Similarity / resemblance (not identity) What We Established About Toar Yosef: יפה תואר ויפה מראה — beautiful in form and appearance Shaul asking the medium about Shmuel: מה תארו — \"what is his form?\" Gideon: כתאר בני המלך — like the bearing of royalty Crafting: יתארהו בשרד — forming with marker and compass המונח הזה אינו חל על אלוקות חלילה וחס Toar is NEVER applied to Hashem in Tanakh. Because toar means physical form, and Hashem has no physical form, the Torah's vocabulary itself respects His incorporeality. PART 1 — Completing Chapter 1: Tzelem and Demut Defining Tzelem ואילו צלם חל על הצורה הטבעית, כלומר על העניין המגדיר את עצמותו של דבר ועושה אותו למה שהוא, והוא מהותו האמיתית באשר הוא מצוי זה Tzelem describes the natural form of a thing — the matter that defines its essence and makes it what it is, its true essence as that which exists. In other words: tzelem = essence , not body. What Is the Tzelem of a Human? ועניין זה הצלם אצל האדם הוא מה שבאמצעותו תתבצע ההשגה האנושית The tzelem of a human is what enables human apprehension — intellectual capacity. What makes a human a human? Not the shape of the body (we share that with mammals). It is the rational faculty — the capacity for intellect, abstraction, reason. בשל אותה השגה שכלית נאמר עליו בצלם אלוהים ברא אותו That is what the verse means when it says man was created b'tzelem Elokim : Hashem has intellect, and humans share — at the deepest level — this same essential property of intellect. (Of course Hashem's intellect and human intellect are radically different. We will see how the word demut handles this.) Proof 1 (Clear): Tehillim — צלמם תבזה David HaMelech in Tehillim: כחלום מהקיץ, ה' בעיר צלמם תבזה \"Like a dream upon awakening, when Hashem arises, He will despise their tzelem\" The Question When Hashem despises wicked people, does He despise their face, eyes, and body? Of course not. He despises their essence — their wicked character. The Proof כי ביזיון שייך לנפש שהוא צורת המין, לא לתבנית האיברים ומתארם Contempt is directed at the soul (the form of the species), not at the bodily structure. When the verse says Hashem will despise their tzelem , tzelem must mean their inner essence , not their physical form. This is a clean, decisive proof for Rambam's definition. Proof 2 (Apparent Counter-Example): Idols Are Called Tzelamim In Tanakh, idols are called tzelamim . Surely this proves tzelem means physical form? Rambam's Answer וכך אני אומר שהפסלים נקראו צלמים משום שהמבוקש מהם היה העניין שיחסו להם, לא לתבניתם ומתארם Idols are called tzelamim because of the concept attributed to them — not because of their physical structure. The Key Insight (Chapter 36 Preview) Even the greatest idolater never believed in the essential quality of the wood or metal itself. The idolater wasn't worshipping the wood. He was worshipping what the idol represented — the supernatural connection it embodied, the transcendence it pointed to. The wood was a vehicle for a concept. So tzelem still means essence/concept , even in the idolatry context — the essence being supernatural connection. Proof 3 (Hardest Counter-Example): Tzalmei Techoreichem The Philistines, plagued for seven months after capturing the Aron, were told to return it with a guilt offering: חמשה טחורי זהב — five golden hemorrhoids. The next verse says: ועשיתם צלמי טחוריכם \"And you shall make tzalmei techoreichem (images/forms of your hemorrhoids)\" The simple reading: they made literal physical images of hemorrhoids. This seems to give tzelem a purely physical meaning. Rambam's Answer עניין הרחקת פגע הטחורים, לא לתבנית הטחורים The verse refers to the concept of repudiating the plague of hemorrhoids — not the physical shape of hemorrhoids. Two Possible Readings of Rambam's Position Reading 1 Reading 2 They did NOT make literal hemorrhoid forms; they made something whose function was to dispel the plague. Tzelem = the concept. They DID make physical hemorrhoid icons, but the tzelem refers to what those icons concept-fully represent — the plague itself, which they sought to dispel. If You Insist on Physical Meaning Rambam offers a fallback: ואם יכריח הוא שצלמי טחוריכם וצלמים נקראים ככה לשם התבניות והמצרים, יהיה צלם מונח רב משמעות ומשותף If you insist that here tzelem means physical form — fine. Then tzelem is a multi-meaning word: sometimes essence (Tehillim), sometimes physical form (these cases). But in Bereishit: ויהיה המכוון בדבריו נעשה אדם בצלמנו: הוא ההשגה שכלית, לא התבנית המצרית The intent in \"let us make man in our tzelem\" is intellectual apprehension — NOT physical form , since God has no physical form to share with us. The Rambam wins either way: Tzelem always means essence (preferred reading), OR Tzelem is multi-meaning, but specifically in Bereishit it means intellect Defining Demut ואלו דמות הוא מונח הנגזר מדמה — וגם הוא דמות בניין עניין Demut derives from domeh (similar) — denoting similarity in concept, not identity. This is a crucial distinction: demut means resembling , not being identical to . Proofs for Demut as Conceptual Similarity Proof 1 — דמיתי לקאת מדבר David in Tehillim: \"I am like (damiti) the pelican of the desert\" לא באו לומר שהוא דומה בכנפיה ובראשה, אלא שיגונו דומה ליגונה He doesn't mean he has wings and a beak like a pelican. He means his pain and loneliness resemble the pelican's loneliness in the desert. (Saadia Gaon explicitly explains the pelican as the lonely desert bird.) Proof 2 — כל עץ בגן אלוהים לא דמה אליו ביפיו Yechezkel comparing Ashur to the trees of the Garden: \"No tree in the garden of God was like it in its beauty\" Comparing a country to trees obviously isn't a physical similarity — it's a similarity in beauty as a concept . Proof 3 — חמת למו כדמות חמת נחש \"Their venom is like the venom of a snake\" David isn't saying Hashem will literally bring snakes to bite the wicked. The concept of snake venom — dangerous, deadly — is what is being compared. Proof 4 — דמיונו כאריה יכסוף לטרוף \"His image is like a lion eager to tear\" The wicked are like predatory lions — not in physical form, but in concept (waiting to attack the innocent). Proof 5 — דמות הכסא (Yechezkel's vision) \"An image of a throne\" דמות בעניין הרוממות והנכבדות, לא בריבועו וגובהו ורוחב רגליו כמו שחושבים הפתאים Similar to a throne in its elevation and majesty — not in its square shape, height, and leg-width \"as the foolish think.\" Yechezkel saw an elevated, majestic presence — not a literal piece of furniture. Proof 6 — דמות החיות (the angels) \"An image of the living beings\" Above the angels was something resembling a throne — meaning elevated and majestic, not a literal throne. Now Putting It Together — נעשה אדם בצלמנו כדמותנו The Verse Means וכיון שהאדם מתייחד בענין מופלא מאוד שבו, שאינו קיים בדבר מן הנמצאים תחת גלגל הירח, והיא ההשגה השכלית שאינה משתמשת לא בחוש ולא בגופים Since humans are uniquely distinguished by something extraordinary — found in no other creature under the heavens — namely intellectual apprehension that uses neither senses nor bodily organs... (Why \"under the heavens\"? Because Rambam holds — like many medieval Rishonim — that celestial bodies possess their own intellect. The phrase delimits the comparison to terrestrial creatures.) The Similarity to God דמתה אותה להשגת האלוה This non-bodily intellect resembles God's intellect — which also uses no physical organ. But — Similar, Not Identical אף שאין זו דמות באמת אלא במבט הראשון בלבד It is not a true resemblance, only at the first glance — because, as Yeshayahu says: לא מחשבותי מחשבותיכם — \"My thoughts are not your thoughts\" (Rambam uses this verse extensively to explain how Hashem's foreknowledge is compatible with human free will — His knowing is categorically different from ours.) Why \"Resemblance\" Then? Even though our intellect is radically different from Hashem's, it is the closest resemblance possible. Of all created things, the human's intellectual faculty is what most approximates God's nature — therefore the verse uses demut . Chapter 1 — Concluding Statement ובשל אותו עניין, כלומר השכל הדבוק בו, נאמר לאדם שהוא בצלם אלהים ובדמותו, לא שהאל יתעלה הוא גוף Because of this — the intellect that adheres to the human — the verse says man is in God's image and likeness — NOT that God Himself has a body. The Full Vocabulary Now Resolved Word Meaning Applied to Humans Toar Physical form Used (Yosef, etc.); never used of God Tzelem Essence The intellectual faculty Demut Resemblance (not identity) We resemble God in having intellect, though our intellect is categorically lower Jonathan's Beautiful Observation in the Shiur \"It seems Rambam is dematerializing avodah zarah while corporealists are materializing God — moving in opposite directions but with the same misconception about essence and form.\" The Rambam confirms this is correct. The idolater materializes the divine; the corporealist materializes God. Both are reading tzelem as physical when it really means essence. PART 2 — Beginning Chapter 2: The Creation Narrative Reexamined We now leave the simple vocabulary discussion and enter what Rambam calls a digression — but it is the most profound digression in the early chapters of the Moreh. The discussion of tzelem raises a question about Bereishit that changes how we read the entire creation narrative . We will not finish this chapter tonight. There is enormous depth here. We will spend most of next week's shiur on it. The Setup — Three Meanings of \"Elohim\" Before stating the question, Rambam needs us to know that Elohim is a polysemous (multi-meaning) word in Hebrew with three distinct meanings: Meaning 1 — God The most common usage. Meaning 2 — Angels (Malachim) כי ה' אלוקיכם הוא אלהי האלהים — \"For Hashem your God is the God of gods\" (Eikev) The Torah doesn't condone other gods. So \" Elohei ha'Elohim \" must mean: the God of the angels. Here Elohim = angels. Meaning 3 — Judges or Important People עד האלהים יבא דבר שניהם — \"The case shall come before the Elohim\" (Mishpatim) Here it clearly refers to human judges — those of standing and authority. Onkelos on והייתם כאלוהים יודעי טוב ורע In the seduction by the serpent: ביום אכלכם ממנו ונפקחו עיניכם והייתם כאלהים יודעי טוב ורע \"You will be like Elohim, knowing good and evil\" Onkelos translates this as: ותהון כרברבין חכימין בין טב לביש \"You will be like rabbis/great ones — wise (or discerning) between good and bad.\" Onkelos chooses Meaning 3 — important/prominent people. NOT \"like gods\" (a common but wrong translation). This is critical. If you don't take Onkelos's interpretation seriously, the rest of Rambam's argument falls apart. The \"Astonishing Question\" — Posed by a Learned Person The Rambam describes the question as: קושיא מופלאה (Arabic: ghariba — strange/wondrous/deep) A question worthy of careful examination. The Question Stated Reading the simple peshat of Bereishit: Chapter 1 (creation): Man is created b'tzelem Elokim — which we just learned means with intellect. Chapter 3 (the sin): The serpent says — \"on the day you eat, you will be like Elohim, knowing good and evil.\" And the verse says after they ate, their eyes were opened and they realized they were naked. The peshat seems to imply: the human's faculty for distinguishing good and evil came only AFTER the sin. The Contradiction with Chapter 1 If the human had full intellect (tzelem Elokim) in Chapter 1, why does the snake offer \"knowledge\" as if it's something new in Chapter 3? The Even Sharper Question The intellect to distinguish good and evil is what makes a human a human — the very essence we just established. But here: שהעונש על החטא הזה מתן שלמות שלא היתה לו The \"punishment\" for the sin is the granting of a perfection the human didn't have before! This is bewildering. Why would Hashem reward a sin with the granting of the human's defining essence? The Rambam compares this absurdity to: \"It's like saying: a person sinned and was rewarded by being turned into a star in the heavens\" — a reference to Greek mythology (likely Orion the hunter), which the Rambam dismisses as nonsense. Rambam's Rebuke Before Answering אתה מעיין בירורים ראשונים ומחשבות מזדמנות... כמו שעובר על אחד מספרי ההיסטוריה או אחד מן השירים \"You investigate based on your first superficial thoughts. You want to understand a book that has guided generations as if you're reading it in your spare time between meals and entertainment — like a history book or a poem.\" The Rambam is unimpressed with the questioner despite earlier calling him \"learned.\" The question is real, but the questioner approaches Torah carelessly. \"Pay attention to my answer — it is not what you think on first glance.\" Rambam's Answer — Premise 1: Man Had Full Intellect in Chapter 1 שהשכל שהשם השפיע על האדם והוא שלמותו האחרונה הוא שקיבל האדם לפני חטאו The intellect that Hashem bestowed upon man — which is his ultimate perfection — was received before his sin. This intellect: Was complete from creation Was what made commanding him possible (you don't command beings without intellect) Is referenced when the verse says he was created b'tzelem Elokim ולגבי השכל נאמר על אדם שבצלמנו כדמותנו נעשה Because of this intellect, the verse says he was created in our tzelem (essence) — which we just learned means intellect. Note Against Mark's Suggestion in the Shiur Mark suggested: maybe man was given a lower level of intellect in Chapter 1 and a higher level after eating from the tree. Rambam explicitly rejects this. The human had full intellect from the start — not partial. The change wasn't quantitative. So what DID change? Rambam's Answer — Premise 2: There Are TWO Different Domains of Knowledge This is the bomb. Pay close attention: ובשכל מבחינים בין האמת לבין השקר, וזה לאדם אופן מושלם Through intellect we distinguish between truth and falsehood — and in this man was perfectly equipped from the start. BUT: הגנאי והנאה הוא מן המפורסמות, לא מן המוסכלות Good (na'eh) and bad (mguneh) are not in the domain of intellect — they belong to the realm of social consensus (mefursamot), not to intellectual truth. The Crucial Distinction Rambam Is Making Domain Type of Knowledge Examples Muskalot (intellectual truths) Objective — true or false \"The sky is blue.\" \"World War II ended in 1945.\" \"Two plus two equals four.\" \"The earth orbits the sun.\" Mefursamot (social consensus) Subjective — good or bad \"Nudity is shameful.\" \"Honor your elders.\" Most norms of ethical behavior in contexts where it isn't basic murder/theft. משום שאין אומרים השמים כדוריים נאה, והארץ שטוחה מגונה — אלא אומרים אמת או שקר We don't say \"the sky is spherical is good\" or \"the earth is flat is bad\" — we say \"true\" or \"false.\" Because these are facts. וכן בלשוננו יאמר על הנכון ועל הכזב אמת ושקר, ועל המגונה טוב ורע, ובשכל מכיר האדם את האמת והשקר, וכך הוא בכל הדברים השכליים Even in our language: for facts we say true/false; for moral/aesthetic things we say good/bad. Intellect handles truth and falsehood — and works the same way for all intellectual matters. The Implication — The Bombshell Here is the framework Rambam is setting up: Before the Sin The human had full intellect — which works in the domain of truth and falsehood . In Gan Eden, the human saw the world purely objectively: True / False Real / Unreal Existent / Non-existent Good and bad simply weren't categories that defined his experience. Everything in Gan Eden was tov me'od — there was no domain of \"good and bad\" to navigate. The intellect didn't need it. After the Sin The human gained access to a new domain — the domain of mefursamot, social/subjective good and bad . This is not an upgrade of intellect. It's the acquisition of a different kind of knowing — a more subjective, blurry, conventional kind of knowing. The Question About Nudity (To Be Developed Next Week) Before the sin: nudity is just a fact — true (we are naked). The human had no embarrassment because embarrassment is a mefursamot concept (\"nakedness is bad\") that requires the social-good-and-bad faculty. After the sin: the human acquires the faculty of mefursamot and immediately realizes \"nakedness is bad\" — and is ashamed. This is why the verse says \"and their eyes were opened, and they knew they were naked.\" They didn't gain new vision. They didn't gain new intellect. They gained the faculty of subjective moral/aesthetic judgment that the original intellect did not include. Jonathan's Important Observation (To Be Developed) \"It seems like Rambam is talking about the difference between an 'is' and an 'ought' proposition — descriptive statements vs. normative ones.\" Exactly right. This is precisely the distinction: Is propositions (descriptive): handled by intellect → truth/falsehood Ought propositions (normative): handled by mefursamot → good/bad In modern philosophical terms, the Rambam is making a fact/value distinction — and saying the original human inhabited the world of facts (the world of is ), and only after the sin began to inhabit the world of values (the world of ought ). The Rambam, following Aristotle, holds that ethics belong to the realm of norms (subjective social agreement) — not to the realm of objective intellectual truth. This is a profoundly modern philosophical position embedded in his reading of Bereishit. Why the \"Punishment\" Was Actually a Loss The questioner thought the sin was rewarded with intellect. The Rambam is reversing the entire reading: Before: Pure intellectual existence — engaging with reality as is / is not After: Forced into the realm of should / shouldn't , conventional social norms, embarrassment, shame, the subjective The \"punishment\" wasn't a gain — it was a descent from a higher mode of being (pure intellectual engagement with truth) to a lower mode of being (entanglement with social good-and-bad). The \"opening of the eyes\" was a regression in this sense — not an elevation. Where We Stop — Setup for Next Week We have laid out: The completed definitions of toar, tzelem, and demut The question on Bereishit The vocabulary needed (three meanings of Elohim; Onkelos on \"like Elohim\") The opening of the answer — that the human had full intellect from the start The bombshell — there are TWO domains of knowledge (muskalot and mefursamot) Coming Up Next Week Full development of the answer: How did the sin specifically cause the shift from muskalot to mefursamot? What was the serpent actually offering? The clothing, the shame, the nakedness — all reread through this framework The deeper meaning of \"their eyes were opened\" What the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil really represents This will likely take the entire next shiur After Chapter 2 we will return to the vocabulary project — explaining many more multi-meaning Hebrew terms in Tanakh. Tonight's Summary Chapter 1 (Complete): Toar = physical form (never used of God) Tzelem = essence (used of God's likeness in man = intellect) Demut = resemblance, not identity (we resemble God only at first glance — our intellects are categorically different) Chapter 2 (Begun): The Bereishit question is real and profound The answer involves recognizing two distinct domains of knowledge: Muskalot — intellect — true/false — objective Mefursamot — social consensus — good/bad — subjective Man had FULL intellect before the sin The sin caused not an intellectual upgrade but a transition from pure intellectual existence to a more subjective, norm-bound existence The \"opening of the eyes\" is a descent , not an ascent Chag Sameach · Have a wonderful Shavuot","IcEB9-MapEY":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 7 (ענוה — Humility) — Part 5 (concluding the chapter) Edition: Feldheim This week: Limits of humility · Humility between people · When leaders must suspend external humility · Praise of the humble Recap: The Full Chapter So Far Definition (Part 1) Humility = recognizing one's true status without exaggeration in either direction Internal and external humility; humility before Hashem and before people Humility ≠ self-debasement — this will become critical tonight The Three Methods of Training Practical/experiential — company, exterior conduct, possessions Through the Torah — stories (Tanakh figures who lived or failed humility) + mitzvot (12+ specific mitzvot designed to instill anavah) Intellectual / philosophical analysis (Parts 3 & 4) The Intellectual Analysis (Parts 3 & 4) Physical things — identical to animals; their excess causes illness Material/positional things — generate strife; kings die violently and prematurely Spiritual/intellectual things — fragile (lost through hunger, fatigue); the higher you reach, the more you see how much is above you Cosmic comparison — if you compare downward, you must compare upward too (celestial bodies, angels, Hashem) Religious arrogance — most dangerous form; the Rambam's \"teshuva for having nothing to do teshuva for\" Critical caveat: the analysis is a counterweight to pride, NOT a tool for self-loathing Last Week's Final Section Humility before Hashem — universal obligation (all creatures with intellect) The five bowings in Amidah (don't miss the fifth — at the three steps back) The King had to be bowed throughout the entire Amidah (Shlomo as the model) Tonight's Roadmap The limits of humility — distinguishing anavah from shiflut (lowliness) Humility between people — practical conduct Leaders: when external humility must be suspended Praise of the humble — concluding the chapter PART 1 — The Limits of Humility: Anavah vs. Shiflut Rabbi Avraham makes a critical distinction before going further. External humility is real and obligatory — but it must not degrade into self-debasement. והוא שעליך להבדיל בין ענווה חיצונית המחויבת ובין השפלות והבזיון שהם מדות נתעבות You must distinguish between obligatory external humility and lowliness/degradation, which are abominable traits. The Principle on Clothing על אף שהענווה החיצונית מחייבת ללבוש בגדים גסים, אין מחובת הענו ללבוש בגדים מטונפים ומגואלים External humility requires simple clothes — NOT dirty or stained clothes. The psychological/spiritual logic is real: A person who presents respectfully on the outside maintains internal respect A person who walks in disrespectful, unkempt clothing damages their own inner sense of self The exterior reflects — and reinforces — the interior The Story of Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach A father came to visit his son at Yeshiva. The son wasn't in his room. The father saw the room — clean, tidy, neat — left a note, and went home. When asked why he didn't go to the Beit Midrash to see his son, he answered: \"I went to his bedroom. I saw everything clean and tidy. I know he's doing well and learning well. I can go home now.\" The clean, ordered exterior tells the truth about the inner life. The Talmid Chacham and Stained Clothes The Gemara learns from a verse in Mishlei: כל משנאי אהבו מות — אל תקרא משנאי אלא משניאי \"All who hate Me love death\" — do not read 'hate Me' but 'cause others to hate Me'.\" A Talmid Chacham with stained clothes who functions as a public role model causes others to hate Torah — because he reflects badly on what he represents. The Gemara goes so far as to say he is chayav mitah . The Standard for Clothing and Home אלא שיהיה לבוש תלמיד חכם לבן ונקי The Talmid Chacham's clothing should be clean — Rabbi Avraham adds the word lavan (white). What Does Lavan Mean Here? Interpretation Source Meluban — cleansed, freshly laundered The commentators on Rabbi Avraham Literally white — invoking purity Rabbi Avraham's great-grandson Rabbeinu David Either way, the core point is clean and presentable , not extravagant. The Full Standard Even simple cotton clothes (thin in summer, thick in winter — no need for silk or expensive materials) Must not be stained with oil or grease Shoes must not be covered in dirt and mud They should protect the feet from filth — i.e., be functional and clean The home, even without marble floors, must be swept and washed Rugs, even of simple reed material, must not be filthy מכאן אפשר ללמוד לכל שאר הדברים כי הצמצום וחוסר התפנוקים מחזקים את הענווה. ואילו הלכלוך והטינופת אינם אלא זלזול וטומאה Extrapolate to everything: minimalism and lack of luxury strengthen humility. But filth and dirt are mere disrespect and impurity. The Practical Principle Anavah Shiflut (Wrong) Simple clothes Dirty clothes Modest home Filthy home Functional shoes Mud-caked shoes Plain rugs Stained rugs Recognized one's place Debased oneself PART 2 — Humility Between People (Practical Conduct) Now Rabbi Avraham turns to how humility is practiced in interactions with other human beings. Conduct 1 — Pleasantness and Greeting שלא יתגאה בן אדם ולא יראהו סר וזעף אלא רך הליכות... הוי מקבל את כל האדם בסבר פנים יפות Don't appear haughty, angry, or sullen. Be gentle in manner. Greet everyone with a pleasant face. Two Specific Practices 1. Always be the first to greet מימי לא הקדמני אדם לשלום אפילו גוי The Chacham (likely Rav in Berachot) said: \"No one ever greeted me first — even a non-Jew.\" The point: don't wait for others to acknowledge you. Acknowledge them first. The wait-to-be-greeted mentality is the mentality of arrogance. 2. Respond fully when greeted It's not enough to say \"shalom\" back. \"V'yachzir lahem shalom\" means returning the greeting with continuation — not just a curt \"hi\" but engaging the conversation. The Boaz Model When Boaz came to his harvesters in the field: ה' עמכם — \"Hashem be with you\" ויענו לו: יברכך ה' — \"And they answered: Hashem bless you\" A complete two-way greeting — Boaz greeted first, they responded fully. This is the model. (Some Sefardim follow this when called to the Torah: \"Hashem imachem\" and the community responds \"Yevarechecha Hashem\" .) Conduct 2 — Don't Seek Prominent Seats בשעת נוכחותו בעצרותיהם... לא יהא להוט אחר מקומות הגבוהים Whether at a mitzvah gathering or a social one — don't pursue the prominent seats. Shlomo's Verse כי טוב אמור לך עלה הנה מהשפילך לפני נדיב אשר ראו עיניך \"Better to be told 'come up here' than to be lowered before the prince whom your eyes have seen.\" The painful experience the verse warns about: you go to the front, you can't find your seat there, you sit somewhere reserved — and you're publicly told to move back. Far better to take a back seat and, if your importance warrants it, be invited forward. The Logic This is not about thinking lowly of yourself It's about knowing your place — and trusting that if you have underestimated yourself, the natural movement of events will bring you forward The chase for prominence reveals insecurity, not value Conduct 3 — Tolerating Difficult People ועליו לנהוג באדיבות עם הבריות כמידת יכולתו Act with adivut (calmness/grace/tact) toward people as much as you can. When Someone Is Coarse or Insulting Rabbi Avraham's reframe is striking: עליו להעביר על מידותיו ולהצטער על אותו אדם Pass over his own dignity — and feel sorry for that person. When someone speaks roughly to you, the response is not anger, not retaliation, not grudge — but pity for the person who must speak that way . The Five Negative Mitzvot at Stake Don't let the yetzer hara drive you to: כדי שלא יעבור על חמישה לא תעשה שבפניהם Mitzvah 1 לא תקום — Don't take revenge 2 לא תטור — Don't bear a grudge 3 לא תשנא את אחיך בלבבך — Don't hate your brother in your heart 4 ואהבת לרעך כמוך — (as a positive companion) Love your neighbor as yourself 5 (Various related prohibitions on hostile speech) The Critical Boundary — Don't Become Anyone's Servant This is where the chapter's anti-shiflut theme returns: אין צריך אף לא נאה אף אסור שיאדם בעל אופי קל דעת... מן השקט עם עם הארץ ונותן לו קדימה ומשרתו תמיד בלי צורך דתי או חומרי It is not necessary, not appropriate, and even forbidden to act in a foolish manner — to be one who, for example: Kisses the hand of an ordinary, ignorant person Always gives precedence to common folk Constantly serves them with no religious or practical reason שאין ענווה אלא ביזיון והשפלה מראים החיסרון דעת ושכל This is not humility — it is degradation and lowliness, revealing lack of intelligence and discernment, not good character or religious devotion. The Distinction Legitimate (Anavah) Illegitimate (Shiflut) Hakarat hatov to someone who helped you Subservience to ordinary people for no reason Honoring a Chacham to learn from him Kissing the hand of the ammei ha'aretz Serving a parent, mentor, or rabbi Being everyone's servant Respecting a person's authentic position Performance of self-degradation Why It's Forbidden The people one might falsely \"honor\" in this way — when they are described: מרבים שיחה בטבע האכילה והשתייה וכיוצא באלה These are people who waste time on food and drink and trivialities. Honoring them with submission is not anavah — it endorses what is corrupt. פן תטעה ותחשוב על אחד מהדברים שהזהרנו עליהם שהיא ענווה דגולה ובאמת אינו אלא השפלת המידות ופסול דתי ורגש מגונה Beware of confusing what you think is humility with what is actually a corruption of character. PART 3 — Leaders: When External Humility Must Be Suspended This is the heart of the chapter's practical sophistication. For most people, external humility is constant. For leaders, the situation is different. אבל מי שעליו מוטלת האחריות להנהגת אחרים הרי הוא מאחד משני סוגים If responsibility for leading others is on you, you are one of two kinds: Type 1 — Leader of a Spiritual Group A leader of a select group on a spiritual journey: Less common today, more common in Rabbi Avraham's time Examples: Chasidic groups, schools of prophets The Rule for Type 1 חובה עליהם להחזיק בענווה החיצונית והפנימית Such a leader must maintain both internal AND external humility. Why? Because the leader is the model. The students see how he walks the path. If he abandons external humility, the lesson is broken. The Examples Elisha went with the Bnei HaNeviim to cut wood. He didn't say \"I'm the prophet, you do the work.\" He worked alongside them. Shmuel stood with the schools of the prophets — present in their training, not aloof. The lesson: spiritual leadership requires walking among the disciples. Type 2 — Public Leader of Diverse Masses A leader of many different kinds of people — with varying aspirations and views: King of Israel Rosh Galut (Exilarch) Rosh Yeshiva or chief rabbi Shoftim (judges) and the officers of the community Anyone whose role is to enforce, judge, guide, and command The Rule for Type 2 מחייבהו הצורך המפורש בתורה... שום תשים עליך מלך — שתהא אימתו עליך The Torah commands: \"You shall surely place a king over you\" — and the Gemara extends this to mean \"that his awe should be upon you.\" For the leader's authority to function, the people must have reverence/awe for him. This requires the leader to present externally with the bearing of authority , not with the demonstrative humility of a Type 1 leader. וזוהי סטייה מן הענווה החיצונית This is a departure from external humility. The Critical Caveat אבל סטייה נובעת מנחיצות, ורק בתנאי שתשמר הענווה הפנימית It is a departure required by necessity — and only on the condition that internal humility is preserved. The Torah's warning to the king: לבלתי רום לבבו מאחיו — \"that his heart not be lifted above his brothers.\" Even with all the external pomp, the internal must remain humble. David HaMelech and Michal When David danced before the Aron and was rebuked by Michal: David: \"I will be even more lowly than this and humble in my own eyes\" (internal humility preserved) David: \"And with the maidservants you mentioned, I will be honored by them\" Why the Second Statement? If David is truly humble, why does he care about the maidservants' opinion? Answer (Rabbi Avraham): Because David is the king. The maidservants — and through them, the community — must respect the king for his authority to function. So David is not seeking personal glory; he is preserving the legitimacy of his role by ensuring the community treats him with the honor his position requires. ועם זאת אין צריך אף לא נאה ולא יאדם בעל אופי קל דעת External honor for a leader is required — but internal arrogance is forbidden. Practical Halacha — The King's Conduct Public Behavior כיוון שנתמנה אדם פרנס לציבור אסור לעשות מלאכה בפני שלושה Once a person is appointed a public leader, he may not perform menial work in front of three people. If only 1-2 people are present → fine If 3+ are present → forbidden (it undermines the authority needed for the role) Practical examples for a public figure: Don't fix the toilet when guests are at home Don't repair the tap when others see you Don't perform menial labor publicly Honoring Chachamim The Rambam in Hilchot Melachim: It is a mitzvah for everyone to honor the king The king must honor Chachamim — when the Sanhedrin enters, he stands BUT only in private. In a public setting, the king does NOT stand for the head of the Sanhedrin. Why? Because in public, the king's authority must be preserved. If people see the king standing for the Rosh Sanhedrin, the king's authority erodes. Yehoshafat's Model When Yehoshafat King of Judah was alone with a Talmid Chacham: He would rise from his throne Embrace and kiss the Chacham Call him \"Rabbi and my master\" But in public: He addressed the same Chacham by name Maintained his royal bearing Did not display the same demonstrative humility This is the model: private humility, public authority. How to Preserve Internal Humility Despite External Authority This is the most dangerous balance — and Rabbi Avraham gives a practical regimen: ולכן המחפש דרך ה' צריך שירחיק מן השררה והשלטון Therefore, one seeking the path of Hashem should avoid positions of leadership unless they are necessary for the benefit of others. The Mishna in Pirkei Avot: שנא את הרבנות — \"hate positions of authority.\" Because the tightrope is so difficult. If You Must Lead — The Anti-Arrogance Regimen ישמור לבו וגאוותו שכבר נתנו לו סממנים על ידי תפילות ותחנונים, וקימה בלילות וריבוי כריעות והשתחויות Practice Purpose Extra prayer and supplication Counterweight against internal pride Waking at night to pray Pre-dawn humility before dawn's public role Many bowings and prostrations Physical reminder of lowliness before Hashem Studying the prophets' humility Internalizing the model Reviewing the intellectual analysis of last week Re-grounding the philosophical case Spending time with God-fearing people Subduing oneself in their presence Hachna'ah chitzonit (external submission) before them in private Feeling the proper hierarchy David's Practice חצות לילה אקום להודות לך — \"At midnight I rise to praise You\" חבר אני לכל אשר יראוך — \"I am a companion to all who fear You\" בחנני ה' ונסני, צרפה כליותי ולבי — \"Test me, Hashem, examine my innermost being\" David HaMelech's regimen: night-time prayer, association with the God-fearing, willingness to be examined — all to preserve internal humility while bearing the external weight of kingship. A Modern Application — Parents and Children A practical example raised in the shiur: Should children call their parents by their first names? Approach Assessment Traditional (Abba/Imma, Mommy/Daddy) Maintains the eimah and proper hierarchical relationship Children call parents by first names Generally inappropriate — even in loving relationships, removes the necessary respect for parental authority Cultural variation (some American families) Halachically debatable — may be implied consent (parent is mochel al kevodo ), but the structural concern remains The Halachic Source Honoring parents (and rabbis) is a mitzvah The standard is not to call them by first name unless they consent A rav or parent can be mochel al kevodo (forgo their honor) Implied cultural consent might cover the family that's used to it But the underlying principle: authority requires respect — even within love. A parent who loses their authority loses their effective role. The Broader Principle Beyond Religious Leadership The principle applies to any position of legitimate authority : Parent → child Teacher → student Manager/CEO → employees Rabbi → community Judge → litigants In each case: External : bearing of authority required for the role to function Internal : constant humility, prayer, self-examination In private : deference to those who deserve it (a CEO to his board, a rabbi to his rebbeim) The Risk Curve Position Internal Risk Following Easy to be humble Leading a small group Moderate risk Leading the masses Maximum risk The king Highest risk — therefore the most intensive antidote (the king's Sefer Torah, total prostration in prayer, etc.) This is consistent with what we saw earlier in the chapter — those at risk of the worst arrogance are given the most intensive humility-training mitzvot. PART 4 — Praise of the Humble The chapter concludes with biblical praise for the humble. What the Humble Receive 1. Hashgachah (Divine Providence) השגחת השכינה שורה עליהם The Shechinah dwells upon the humble. They are protected and watched over. 2. Emotional Stability בזכות חירותם מגאוה אינם מתפעלים, ואינם מתרגזים משלטונם של עושי עול They are not easily moved or shaken emotionally They don't quickly become upset by the behavior of the wicked Arrogant people are emotionally fragile — easily disturbed; humble people have steadiness David: > אל תבואני רגל גאוה ויד רשעים אל תנדני \"Let not the foot of pride come upon me, and let not the hand of the wicked move me\" The connection: if I'm not arrogant, I cannot be moved by the wicked. Arrogance is what makes us vulnerable to others' provocations. 3. Their Prayers Are Heard תאות ענוים שמעת ה', תכין לבם תקשיב אזנך \"You hear the desire of the humble, Hashem — You direct their heart, You incline Your ear\" Because the prayers of the humble come from a pure heart, Hashem accepts them and fulfills their needs. 4. They Are First to See Salvation יפאר ענוים בישועה \"He glorifies the humble with salvation\" בונה ירושלים ה' נדחי ישראל יכנס, הרופא לשבורי לב ומחבש לעצבותם \"Hashem builds Jerusalem; gathers the dispersed of Israel; heals the broken-hearted and binds up their sorrows\" Yeshayahu speaks specifically of bringing good news of salvation to the humble : > רוח ה' אלהים עלי... לבשר ענוים לחבוש לנשברי לב 5. Hashem Dwells Among Them מרום וקדוש אשכון, ואת דכא ושפל רוח להחיות רוח שפלים ולהחיות לב נדכאים \"Though I dwell on high and in holiness, I also dwell with the contrite and humble of spirit — to revive the spirit of the lowly and the heart of the contrite\" The exalted Hashem chooses to dwell precisely with the humble. 6. Humility Is More Valued Than Sacrifices David's stunning verse: כי לא תחפוץ זבח ואתנה, עולה לא תרצה. זבחי אלהים רוח נשברה, לב נשבר ונדכה אלהים לא תבזה \"You do not desire sacrifice, that I should give it; You do not want a burnt offering. The sacrifices of God are a broken spirit; a broken and contrite heart, O God, You will not despise.\" A humble spirit is the best korban — greater than all the burnt offerings. The Final Verse — Zephaniah בקשו את ה' כל ענוי הארץ, אשר משפטו פעלו, בקשו צדק, בקשו ענוה. אולי תסתרו ביום אף ה' \"Seek Hashem, all you humble of the earth, who fulfill His justice. Seek righteousness, seek humility. Perhaps you will be sheltered on the day of Hashem's anger.\" Three Inferences from This Verse The seekers of Hashem are primarily the humble — \"Seek Hashem, all you humble of the earth\" The humble are the ones who fulfill the mitzvot — \"who fulfill His justice\" Seek both righteousness AND humility specifically — even when you are already humble, the work continues. Humility is a constant journey. אף שכבר תארם קודם ענוי הארץ, ציווה עליהם להתמיד בדרך הענווה הזאת ולבקש להוסיף בשבחה ולא להסתפק במה שכבר השיג ממנה Even after being called \"humble of the earth,\" they are still commanded to seek humility further. There is no arriving — only deeper seeking. This is the perfect closing principle: humility is never a destination. The moment you think you've arrived, you haven't. Closing Words והוא יתעלה יעזור למי שחפץ ללכת בדרך הענווה הזאת ויעלהו ממדרגה למדרגה עליו... ידרך ענוים במשפט וילמד ענוים דרכו Hashem will help one who wishes to walk this path of humility and elevate him from level to level. He guides the humble in justice and teaches the humble His way. Summary of the Entire Chapter (Perek Zayin Complete) What Humility Is Recognition of one's true status without exaggeration Internal and external Before Hashem and before people NEVER self-debasement Three Methods of Training Practical — company, possessions, conduct Through Torah — stories + 12+ mitzvot Intellectual — philosophical analysis of each domain The Limits and Subtleties Anavah ≠ shiflut — simple clothes yes, filthy clothes no Honor those who deserve it (parents, rabbis, those who helped) — don't kiss every ignorant person's hand Leaders suspend external humility for the sake of the role — but never lose internal humility The higher the position, the greater the risk, the more intensive the anti-arrogance regimen Specific Practices for Most People Greet first; respond fully Don't seek prominent seats Tolerate difficult people without retaliation Wear clean simple clothes; keep a clean simple home The Rewards Hashgachah Emotional stability Prayers heard First to see salvation Hashem dwells among them More valued than sacrifices The path of constant growth — never a destination Coming Up Next After Shavuot: We begin Chapter 8 — Bitachon (Trust in Hashem) The longest chapter in HaMaspik We will work toward defining what genuine bitachon is, what it requires, and how it relates to other virtues A critically important chapter B'Ezrat Hashem","YcjDAxM8YYE":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Topic: מלאכת דש — Threshing and Squeezing Siman: שכ (320) Seifim covered tonight: Seif 1, 2, 3, beginning of Seif 4 (and Seif 7 introduced) Source text: Shulchan Aruch with Rema, Mishna Berura, and Bi'ur Halacha Where We Are in Hilchot Shabbat So far in Hilchot Shabbat we have covered: Hotza'ah (carrying — Siman 301) Muktzeh Boneh and Soter (building and destroying) Tzad and Shochet (trapping, killing) Kosher and Matir (tying, untying) Bishul (cooking — Siman 318) Borer, Meraked, and Zoreh (selecting, sieving, winnowing — Siman 319, just completed) Now we begin Siman 320 — Dash (threshing), with all its derivatives — primarily the laws of squeezing. What is Melechet Dash? The Av Melacha Dash in the Mishkan is the fifth melacha. To make the dye for the Mishkan's roof: Plant Plow Harvest Gather the stalks Dash — thresh the seeds from the stalks Zoreh — winnow with wind Borer — select good from bad Tochen — grind Meraked — sieve Knead → Bake (as dye) The essence of Dash: extracting something edible from an inedible encasement. The Toledot (Derivatives) Toledah Action Status Extracting kernels from stalks The Av itself d'Oraita Mifarek (extracting) Milking a human or animal d'Oraita Toledah Squeezing liquids from fruit Extracting juice from olives/grapes especially d'Oraita Toledah Key Principles Only Gidulei Karka (D'Oraita) אין דישה אלא בגידולי קרקע Dash applies only to things that grow from the ground . Mid'Oraita, it doesn't apply to non-plant matter. However: Milking mammals is still d'Oraita per most Rishonim, because animals (and humans) get their nutrients from the ground — so they are considered gidulei karka in an extended sense Squeezing liquid from a woolen garment to use the liquid = d'Oraita Dash (wool is gidulei karka) Squeezing liquid from a synthetic fiber = NOT d'Oraita Dash (synthetic isn't gidulei karka) The Overlap with Melaben (Laundering) This siman will also touch on Melaben (laundering) because an essential aspect of laundering clothes is the squeezing out of liquid . Action Dash applies Melaben applies Squeezing fruit ✅ ❌ Squeezing a wet garment to USE the liquid ✅ (d'Oraita if wool) ✅ Squeezing a wet garment NOT for the liquid ❌ (not Dash) ✅ (still Melaben because squeezing is part of laundering) When discussing garments, both melachot must be considered. The Three Categories of Fruit (Foundational) The Gemara establishes three categories of fruit for the law of squeezing: Category 1 — Olives and Grapes (Zetim v'Anavim) Status: Forbidden d'Oraita to squeeze. Why is this a Toledah of Dash? You had a liquid contained within a solid encasement You extract the liquid, leaving the solid behind Just like Dash extracts wheat from stalks, this extracts juice from a solid fruit Why specifically these two? The Torah recognizes their liquids as mashkin (formal liquids) They have their own status as liquids — special brachot, special halachot Olive oil and wine are formal mashkin Therefore extracting them = liquid from solid = Dash Category 2 — Strawberries, Pomegranates, and Commonly Squeezed Fruits Status: Forbidden mid'Rabbanan to squeeze. Why only d'Rabbanan? The Gemara identifies tutim v'rimonim (strawberries, pomegranates) as the prototypes Rashi's reason: lav orchaihu behachi — it's not the common practice (in those days) to squeeze them, so what comes out is not formally a \"mashke\" Treated as food from food (ochel from ochel) at the Torah level — therefore no d'Oraita Dash But Chachamim forbade as a precaution against confusion with Category 1 The Chayei Adam's Challenging Position (200-300 years ago) The Chayei Adam argued that nowadays — when oranges, apples, and many fruits are commonly squeezed — those fruits should be d'Oraita. Almost all poskim rejected this. The Torah's category of \"mashke\" is fixed: only olive oil and wine. Modern fruits remain at d'Rabbanan level no matter how common their juice has become. Practical Today (Category 2 includes): Strawberries, pomegranates Apples, oranges, grapefruits, pears Cranberries, lychees Any commonly-squeezed-for-juice fruit Category 3 — Fruits Not Commonly Squeezed Status: Permitted to squeeze on Shabbat. Examples discussed: Figs — fibrous and fleshy, ~80% water but high sugar content; not suited to juice; better for pastes, jams, dried (develot). The Mishna's reference to develot (dried figs) suggests the lack of juice tradition is ancient. Dates — also generally not juiced; processed into silan/honey instead Possibly melon/watermelon — though even this has become more common today; status debatable Quick Reference Table Category Examples Squeezing Status Liquid Oozed Out On Its Own (Mashkin Shezavu) 1 — Zetim v'Anavim Olives, grapes d'Oraita forbidden Forbidden regardless of intent (mid'Rabbanan + Muktzeh) 2 — Tutim v'Rimonim Strawberries, pomegranates, apples, oranges, etc. d'Rabbanan forbidden Forbidden ONLY if designated for squeezing 3 — Other fruits Figs, dates, etc. Permitted Permitted Gezeirat Mashkin Shezavu (Liquids That Flowed Out) A critical secondary principle: what about liquids that ooze out of the fruit on their own, without any active squeezing? The Setup A non-Jew squeezed olives on Shabbat (no instruction from you), or Very ripe grapes/olives gushed liquid on their own The liquid is sitting there — you didn't do anything The Gezeira The Chachamim were worried: if we permit such liquid, you might come to actively squeeze the remaining olives/grapes. So they forbade. Category Designated to Eat Designated to Squeeze 1 (olives, grapes) ❌ Forbidden ❌ Forbidden 2 (tutim, etc.) ✅ Permitted ❌ Forbidden 3 ✅ Permitted ✅ Permitted (not subject to gezeira) The Muktzeh Dimension The liquid that flowed out from Category 1 grapes/olives is also muktzeh — a double prohibition (can't drink it AND can't move it). Why Even a Gezeira on Category 2? This is a gezeira ligzeira (decree upon a decree), since squeezing Category 2 is itself only d'Rabbanan. The Chachamim still extended it — the limitation being that the designation must be for liquid. Important Limitation Surface moisture — a tiny amount of moisture on the surface is insignificant and not subject to this gezeira. The Shulchan Aruch Seifim Seif 1 — The Three Categories Codified זיתים וענבים אסור לסחטן Olives and grapes — forbidden to squeeze. ואם יצאו מעצמם אסורים אפילו לא עמדו לאכילה If liquid flows of its own accord — still forbidden, even if you only intended to eat the fruit (mid'Rabbanan, gezeirat mashkin shezavu). ותותים ורימונים אסור לסחטן Strawberries and pomegranates — forbidden to squeeze (mid'Rabbanan). ואם יצאו מעצמן: אם עומדים לאכילה מותר, אם עומדים למשקין אסור If liquid flows of its own accord: Designated to eat → permitted Designated to drink → forbidden ושאר כל הפירות מותר לסחטן All other fruit — permitted to squeeze. The Rema — Local Customs and Category 2 ובמקום שנהגו לסחוט איזה פירות לשתות ממימיו מחמת צמא או תענוג דינו כתותים ורמונים Where a particular fruit IS commonly squeezed for pleasure or refreshment, it takes Category 2 status in that place. Example given by Rav Falk many years ago: Carrots in Israel. (Practically complicated by tochen issues, but theoretically the principle applies — what's \"commonly squeezed\" varies by region.) The Mishna Berura's Modification The Mishna Berura disagreed with the Rema's strict locality reading. He held: If a fruit is not squeezed somewhere because of cultural/taste preference → Category 3 there If a fruit is not squeezed somewhere because they don't have enough OR it's too expensive → still Category 2 universally (because if they had access, they would squeeze it) Mishna Berura's example: Pears. Pears in some European cities ~100 years ago were squeezed; in others not — only because of supply. The Mishna Berura held pears were already Category 2 everywhere , and time has proven him right — pear juice is now widespread. He also discussed apples, agreeing they are firmly Category 2. Medicinal Squeezing Doesn't Count אבל אם נהגו לסחטן לרפואה לבד אין לחוש If a fruit is squeezed only for medicinal purposes, it doesn't establish Category 2 status — only squeezing for pleasure/refreshment counts. Squeezing Into the Mouth — Derech Achila A critical practical question: can I extract juice from a fruit by sucking it? Universal Agreement: In the Mouth = Permitted If you put a fruit in your mouth and squeeze with your teeth, extracting the juice in your mouth → everyone agrees this is permitted, even for olives and grapes. This is derech achila — the way of eating. The Disputed Middle Case: Sucking with Lips Outside the Mouth וכל זה דווקא לסחוט אסור אבל מותר למצוץ בפיו מן הפירות המשקים שבהן You hold a grape (or any fruit) at your lips and suck the juice out without putting the whole fruit in your mouth. Permitted? Position Source Ruling Permitted Most Rishonim It's like derech achila — lips are an extension of the mouth; or the action is so different from normal squeezing that it qualifies as a major shinui Forbidden Some Rishonim (Agudah and others) Until the fruit is fully in the mouth it isn't derech achila — and you're still extracting liquid externally Why Not Like Breastfeeding? One might compare this to a mother squeezing milk into a child's mouth (which IS forbidden as we'll see later). But the cases are different: A child sucking is the normal way the child drinks That is derech yenika Adults sucking fruit with lips is not the normal way of consuming Halacha L'Maaseh Category Sucking with Lips Outside the Mouth Category 1 (olives, grapes) Should be machmir — don't do it Category 2 and 3 (oranges, lemons, etc.) Permitted So if you have an ulcer and want to suck a lemon for relief → permitted. If you want to suck a grape with your lips → be machmir. If you put the grape fully in your mouth and squeeze with teeth → always permitted. Lollipops A separate question entirely. No extraction issue: It's a single solid being dissolved in the mouth No liquid is being extracted from a solid Permitted Seif 2 — Already-Crushed Olives and Grapes; Wine in a Barrel Already-Crushed Fruits זיתים וענבים שנתרסקו מערב שבת משקים היוצאים מהם מותרים Olives and grapes that were fully crushed before Shabbat — the liquid that continues to ooze out on Shabbat is permitted. Why Different from Seif 1? Seif 1: Whole, intact olives/grapes — there's still a real risk you might squeeze further. So the Chachamim forbade the oozed liquid. Seif 2: Already fully crushed before Shabbat. Not much liquid remains, and even if you squeezed, it would not reach d'Oraita levels. The Chachamim did not extend the gezeira here. Grapes Above a Wine Barrel ואפילו אם לא נתרסקו כל צרכן מערב שבת, אם יש יין בגיגית שהענבים בתוכה אף על פי שהענבים מתבקעים בשבת בגיגית מותר לשתותו Even if the grapes weren't fully crushed — if they sit in a barrel of wine and ooze liquid that immediately mixes into the existing wine , you may drink the wine. The principle: What oozes out is immediately batel (nullified) in the existing wine of the barrel. The drops that come out never have an independent existence as forbidden liquid — they enter the wine instantaneously. Why Doesn't דבר שיש לו מתירין Block This? Normally, a thing that will become permitted (דבר שיש לו מתירין — like Chametz after Pesach) does NOT nullify even in 1000 parts. So why does the oozing wine nullify here, given that after Shabbat it would be permitted anyway? Answer: דבר שיש לו מתירין applies only when the forbidden substance had a moment of independent existence before mixing. Here, the drops never had that moment — they emerged directly into the wine, instantly nullified. Practical Implication If the wine were flowing through a slope and only later reaching the barrel, the drops would have a moment of independent existence and become forbidden. Only direct, immediate mixing creates the heter. Seif 3 — Wine from Grape Skins and Seeds (Temed) חרצנים וזגים שנתן עליהם מים לעשות תמד מותר בשבת לשתותן Grape skins and seeds that were soaked in water to extract their wine-flavor — permitted to drink on Shabbat. Why Permitted? Same logic as Seif 2 — the wine flavor immediately mixes with the water No moment of independent forbidden existence אפילו נתן בהם מים ויצאו מעצמם גם כן מותר לשתותן Even if more wine flows out on its own from the soaked skins — still permitted to drink. Since only the skins and seeds remain (the main wine was already extracted), anything that flows out is permitted. Seif 4 (Beginning) — Squeezing Onto Food This is the major practical principle of the whole chapter. We'll cover the foundation tonight and complete it next week. The Gemara's Foundational Statement (Shabbat) אמר רבי זירא אמר רב חייא בר אשי אמר רב סוחט אדם אשכול של ענבים לתוך הקדרה אבל לא לתוך הקערה Rav: One may squeeze a cluster of grapes into a kederah , but not into a kearah . Vessel Contents Status Kederah Pot with food in it ✅ Permitted to squeeze into Kearah Empty bowl (no food) ❌ Forbidden The Underlying Principle משקה הבא אל האוכל כאוכל דמי \"A liquid that comes into food has the status of food.\" The full logic: Dash forbids extracting a liquid from a solid When you squeeze grapes onto a salad, the liquid emerges and immediately enters the food The purpose is to add flavor to the salad — the liquid is integral to the food Halachically, that liquid is treated as food , not as a liquid Therefore the action is food from food , not liquid from food Food from food = no Dash What Squeezing into an Empty Bowl Looks Like The liquid emerges as a distinct, independent liquid → liquid from solid → full Dash → forbidden. Kvashim u'Shlakot (Pickles and Cooked Vegetables) — A Separate Discussion A second case the Gemara raises: squeezing pickles or cooked vegetables (which absorbed external liquid) . Posek When squeezing for the vegetable itself (to discard the liquid) When squeezing for the liquid (to drink/use it) Rav Permitted Forbidden d'Rabbanan Shmuel Permitted (whether pickled or cooked) Forbidden d'Rabbanan Rabbi Yochanan Permitted Forbidden d'Oraita (chayav chatat) The d'Oraita position is difficult to explain — there's much discussion in the Rishonim about why this would be Torah-level since the absorbed liquid isn't even native to the vegetable. Rabbeinu Chananel's Challenging Position Rabbeinu Chananel made two radical claims : Claim 1 — Halacha Follows Rabbi Yochanan Generally הלכה כרבי יוחנן (when Rabbi Yochanan argues with Rav or Shmuel individually). Claim 2 — Rabbi Yochanan Disagrees with the Earlier Halacha Too Rabbeinu Chananel held Rabbi Yochanan is disputing not just kvashim but also the original principle (משקה הבא אל האוכל כאוכל דמי). So according to him, you cannot squeeze grapes onto a salad either. Universal Rejection All other Rishonim disagreed with both claims: On Halacha: Rav AND Shmuel both rule leniently → majority rules. The general rule of הלכה כרבי יוחנן applies when he disputes one Amora, not two. On Scope: Even if you posken like Rabbi Yochanan for kvashim, he's only disputing the kvashim case — not the broader principle of squeezing into food. Practical Halacha (Tonight's Practical Conclusion) The Shulchan Aruch in Seif 4: מותר לסחוט אשכול ענבים תוך קדרה שיש בה תבשיל כדי לתקן האוכל, דהוי ליה משקה הבא לאוכל וכאוכל דמי, אבל אם אין בתבשיל אסור You may squeeze a cluster of grapes into a pot that contains food, to enhance the food — because the liquid going onto food has the status of food. But into an empty bowl is forbidden. The Shulchan Aruch then brings Rabbeinu Chananel's stricter view as a secondary opinion: ולרבינו חננאל כל שהוא צריך למימיהן חייב חטאת, אפילו סחט לקדרה שיש בה אוכל, ולדבריו סוחט אשכול ענבים נמי אסור The Bottom Line Action Sefardi and Ashkenazi Mainstream Rabbeinu Chananel Squeezing grapes onto a salad ✅ Permitted ❌ Forbidden d'Oraita Squeezing olives onto food ✅ Permitted ❌ Forbidden d'Oraita Squeezing lemon onto fish ✅ Permitted (Not d'Oraita category) המחמיר תבוא עליו ברכה — \"One who is stringent is blessed.\" For Category 1 (olives and grapes specifically), since Rabbeinu Chananel's position is at the d'Oraita level, one should preferably be machmir and not squeeze them directly onto food . For other fruits (Category 2 and 3), squeezing directly onto food is fully permitted with no concern. Practical Summary Can I Squeeze on Shabbat? Action Status Squeeze grapes/olives in an empty bowl ❌ d'Oraita Squeeze strawberries/oranges/apples in an empty bowl ❌ d'Rabbanan Squeeze figs/dates in an empty bowl ✅ Permitted Squeeze grapes/olives directly onto food ✅ Permitted (but lechatchila be machmir) Squeeze lemon/orange onto salad or fish ✅ Permitted Squeeze with teeth in the mouth (any fruit) ✅ Permitted (derech achila) Squeeze with lips outside the mouth (Cat. 1) ❌ Be machmir Squeeze with lips outside the mouth (Cat. 2, 3) ✅ Permitted Drink wine that flowed naturally from grapes on Shabbat (uncrushed) ❌ Mashkin shezavu Drink liquid from pre-crushed grapes that continued oozing ✅ Permitted Coming Up Next Week Seif 4 continued — more details on squeezing into food vs. squeezing into something that then goes into food (the \"two-step\" question) Seif 5 — additional details on this principle Seif 6 — Lemons (the shortest seif in Shulchan Aruch) מותר לסחוט לימונים — \"It is permitted to squeeze lemons\" Why lemons are different from all other fruits The major Acharonim's discussion of the lemon question over the past few centuries There is no shiur next week. We'll resume after that. Shabbat Shalom","enFARIS7WrE":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: Concluding the Introduction · Beginning Section 1, Chapter 1 Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Tonight's Roadmap This evening is a major hinge point. We are: Finishing the seven reasons for contradictions in books (reasons 4–7) Hearing the Rambam's examples of where each reason appears Reviewing the entire introduction from start to finish Beginning Section 1, Chapter 1 — the first words of the Moreh proper, on Tzelem u'Demut PART 1 — The Seven Reasons for Contradictions in Books Recap of Reasons 1–3 (Last Week) # Cause Example 1 Compilation without attribution The Mishna — multiple rabbis' views, no attribution preserved 2 The author changed his mind Rambam's own Peirush HaMishnayot — manuscripts show his later corrections 3 One or both statements are allegorical \"Hashem is a man of war\" vs. \"God is not a man\" — one is metaphor Reason 4 — A Hidden Caveat or Qualification שיש תנאי שאינו מפורש במקומו... אחד מהם אינו מבואר במקומו Two statements appear contradictory because one or both have an unstated condition that distinguishes them. Example A — Answering a Fool אל תען כסיל כאיולתו — don't answer a fool ענה כסיל כאיולתו — answer a fool The Gemara's resolution: בדברי תורה — when it concerns Torah, you must answer (can't let foolish ideas about Torah pass) במילי דעלמא — when it concerns mundane matters, ignore Example B — Marriage ומוצא אני מר ממות את האשה — I find a woman more bitter than death (Kohelet) מצא אשה מצא טוב — who finds a wife finds good (Mishlei) The Gemara (Yevamot) asks the contradiction. Resolution: it depends what kind of wife. The bitter verse refers to a bad spouse; the positive verse refers to a good spouse. The caveat is implicit. Reason 5 — Pedagogical Imprecision (The Teacher's Necessity) כלומר אם עניין מסוים עמוק וקשה לתפיסה שיש צורך להזכירו או לקחתו כהנחה לביאור עניין שקל לתופסו A deep concept must be touched on to explain a simpler concept. But the student isn't ready for the depth. So the teacher introduces the deep concept superficially and imprecisely to enable the immediate lesson. Later, when the deeper concept is the actual topic, the teacher returns and gives the full, precise account. The two presentations contradict each other — but the contradiction is intentional pedagogy, not error. Why the Rambam Will Use This Method This is one of the two reasons the Rambam openly states he will employ in the Moreh. The reader who notices the contradiction is meant to understand: \"When he said it the first time, he was simplifying for the sake of the immediate point. Here is the precise version.\" Reason 6 — Hidden Contradictions in Conclusions היות הסתירה סמויה ולא מתבארת אלא אחרי הנחות רבות The two statements themselves are fully consistent . But each, when combined with additional true premises and taken to its logical conclusion, yields contradictory conclusions . This is the only one of the seven that is a mistake — the author didn't notice that two of his consistent statements, when developed, would conflict. דברים כאלה הם חומקים מעיניהם של החכמים מחברי החיבורים Even great authors miss this. It's a human limitation, not a deliberate technique. Important Distinction The Rambam is NOT discussing bad authors who openly contradict themselves. He's discussing good authors whose statements are individually consistent but whose downstream logical implications collide. Reason 7 — Deliberate Contradiction to Conceal Depth כורח הדיון בדברים עמוקים ביותר שראוי להסתיר חלק מעניינם ולגלות חלק מהם The deepest and most fascinating reason. When discussing profound matters , the author intentionally contradicts himself to: Hide the full truth from those not ready Reveal partial truth to those who are Arouse the discerning reader's attention to dig deeper Avoid pronouncing a definitive view on something the author wants the reader to work out themselves The Technique ראוי שההמון לא ירגיש כלל במקום הסתירה שביניהם The contradictions must be framed so the unsophisticated reader doesn't notice them. Only the profound reader, examining carefully, will discover the inconsistency and understand: this is intentional — the author is signaling depth here. Famous Examples in the Moreh Topic The Apparent Contradiction Eternity of the universe In some places he accepts it; in others he rejects it. Is he hiding his real view? The burning bush Was it a physical bush that burned without being consumed (a real physical event)? Or was the entire scene a vision in Moshe's mind? The Rambam contradicts himself across the book. Will's Question — Important Clarification: Is the Rambam fudging when he leaves these issues open? The Rambam is not evading. He is deliberately leaving things unresolved because he wants the profound reader to investigate, examine, and arrive at understanding themselves. Direct statement of his view would be less effective than carefully placed contradictions that guide the reader's exploration. There's also a real possibility that, on some questions, the Rambam did not have a single fixed view and the contradictions reflect a genuinely open question for him. The Seven Causes — Comprehensive Summary # Cause Nature 1 Compilation of multiple authors Reasonable — different sources 2 Retraction over time Reasonable — change of mind 3 One or both statements allegorical Reasonable — figurative speech 4 Hidden caveat or condition Reasonable — context-dependent 5 Pedagogical imprecision for teaching Intentional technique 6 Hidden contradictions in downstream conclusions Mistake — the only one 7 Deliberate concealment of depth Intentional technique PART 2 — Where Each Reason Appears The Mishna — Reason 1 אשר לסתירות הנמצאות במשנה ובברייתות הריהן לפי סיבה ראשונה The Mishna's contradictions are due to reason 1 — different rabbis' views compiled together. Classic Gemara formulation: קשיא רישא אסיפא — תשובה: רישא רבי פלוני, סיפא רבי פלוני \"The beginning contradicts the end — answer: the beginning follows Rabbi X, the end follows Rabbi Y.\" Even Rebbi Yehuda HaNasi himself appears to contradict himself when he sometimes followed one Tanna and elsewhere followed another: ראה רבי דבריו של פלוני... וראה דבריו של פלוני... סתם מתניתין מני רבי פלוני The Talmud — Reasons 1 and 2 ואילו הסתירות או החילופים הנמצאים בתלמוד הריהם לפי הסיבה הראשונה והשנייה Reason 1 in the Talmud סבר לה כרבי פלוני בכך וכך, וסבר לה כרבי פלוני בכך וכך \"He held like Rabbi X in this matter, and like Rabbi Y in that matter.\" תרי אמוראי אליבא דרבי פלוני \"Two Amoraim citing the same rabbi differently\" — the original teacher didn't contradict himself, but two of his students remember different versions. Reason 2 in the Talmud הדר ביה רב מהיא — \"Rav retracted from this\" The Gemara explicitly identifies retractions. The Gemara then asks which version is final : מהדורא קמא דרב אשי אמר לן כך וכך, מהדורא בתרא אמר לן כך וכך \"In Rav Ashi's first edition he taught us this; in his second edition he taught us that.\" Tanakh — Reasons 3 and 4 (Mostly) אשר לסתירות... בכל ספרי הנבואה הריהם מחמת הסיבה השביעית והחמישית Correction: Rabbi Avraham clarifies during the shiur — most contradictions in Tanakh are due to reasons 3 and 4, not 5 and 7. The Rambam emphasizes that this is one of the main reasons he wrote this entire introduction. The seemingly contradictory verses in Tanakh nearly always dissolve when: One verse is recognized as allegory (reason 3), or A hidden caveat is identified (reason 4) Classic Pattern — \"Three Verses\" כתוב אחד אומר כך, וכתוב אחד אומר כך — יבוא הכתוב השלישי ויכריע ביניהם Two verses contradict; a third verse resolves the contradiction — by identifying that one is non-literal, or by supplying the missing condition. Example — Shlomo's Apparent Contradictions on Death The Gemara in Shabbat 30 has Rabbi Tanchum of Noy challenge Shlomo: שלמה לא דייך שדבריך סותרים את דברי אביך אלא שדבריך סותרים זה את זה \"Shlomo, it's not enough that your words contradict your father's — they contradict themselves!\" David: \"The dead cannot praise God\" Shlomo (Kohelet): ושבח אני את המתים שכבר מתו — \"I praise the dead who have already died\" Shlomo (Kohelet): כי לכלב חי הוא טוב מן האריה המת — \"a living dog is better than a dead lion\" The Gemara resolves these through caveats — different contexts speak to different aspects of death. Theological Contradictions Are the Rambam's Real Target רוב עסקם בדברי הנבואה הוא בהקשר של דינים ומוסר... ואילו מטרתנו אינה אלא להעיר הפסוקים שבחיצוניותם סותרים בדברי אמונות The Chazal mostly resolve verse-contradictions on legal and ethical matters. The Rambam's task is to resolve them in matters of theology and belief. That's the Moreh's project. Does Tanakh Use Reason 7? אשר לשאלה אם יש בספרי הנביאים סתירות מחמת הסיבה השביעית זהו נושא עיון וחקירה וראוי שלא תקפוץ למסקנה בכך Whether Tanakh intentionally uses contradictions to conceal depth (reason 7) — the Rambam says this is a matter requiring careful investigation. He doesn't commit to an answer here. Don't jump to conclusions. Philosophical Books — Reason 5 אשר לחילופים בספרי הפילוסופים אנשי האמת בהם הם מחמת הסיבה החמישית Philosophical works inevitably contain reason-5 contradictions because they must introduce basics first and depth later. The Rambam acknowledges this is a universal feature of teaching philosophy. Midrash and Agadot — Reasons 6 and 7 כיוצא בזה יש במדרשות ואגדות סתירות גדולות מחמת איזה סיבה Midrash often has reason 6 (logical-conclusion contradictions): a Midrash uses one parable to make one point, another parable elsewhere makes a different point, and the conclusions don't fit. Hence the Gemara's saying: אין מקשין באגדה — \"We don't raise contradictions on aggadah\" The Midrash is using each parable to teach its local lesson; the global coherence isn't the point. ויש בהם גם סתירות מחמת הסיבה השביעית Midrash also uses reason 7 — deliberate contradictions to conceal depth. The Moreh Itself — Reasons 5 and 7 ONLY The Rambam's brave self-declaration: ואילו החילופים שימצאו בחיבור הזה הם מחמת הסיבות החמישית והשביעית Any contradiction you find in the Moreh is either: Reason 5 — pedagogical imprecision (deep concept introduced lightly first, fully later), or Reason 7 — deliberate concealment of depth (signaling that the careful reader should investigate further) Crucially, NOT reason 6 — the Rambam does not claim to have made downstream-logical mistakes. He stakes the consistency of his work on this. דע זאת ועמוד על כך והתבונן בכך תמיד כדי שלא תבוא מחמת המבוכה \"Know this, hold it firmly, and remain attentive to it always — so that you do not become confused.\" PART 3 — Comprehensive Recap of the Entire Introduction Before beginning Chapter 1, the Rambam's introduction can be summarized as: Why and For Whom Written for Rabbi Yosef , his student Purpose: to explain multi-meaning terms and imagery in Tanakh Audience: those trained in both Torah and philosophy who are perplexed by the apparent tension What It Will and Will Not Do Will not be exhaustive — Ma'aseh Bereishit (physics) and Ma'aseh Merkavah (theology/metaphysics) can only be discussed in rashei perakim (chapter headings) The lightning analogy — even understanding comes in flashes; conveying it to others is even harder Even Bereishit (the simplest beginning) is multi-layered Order of Study Science → Philosophy/Theology → Understanding Hashem This is why Torah begins with Bereishit — the natural world must be understood before the divine. Why Meshalim The candle and the diamond — worthless candle illuminates priceless gems Golden apples in silver settings — surface has value, depths have greater value Multi-layered meaning protects the deep truths while allowing access at every level The Two Types of Mashal Type A — every word matters (Yaakov's ladder, 7 elements) Type B — general concept matters (the seductress in Mishlei 7, one unified warning) The reader must learn which type they're facing Advice on Reading Read carefully, every term and chapter sequence intentional Oath 1 — don't teach the Rambam's chiddushim to those unready Oath 2 — give the benefit of the doubt, even forced interpretations are better than unjust accusations The book benefits the beginner (somewhat), the wise-and-perplexed (greatly), the confused-and-self-confident (harms) Mission statement: better to help one elevated person than comfort ten thousand ignoramuses Seven Reasons for Contradictions (Just Completed) And the Moreh contains only reasons 5 and 7. PART 4 — Beginning Section 1 Structure of the Moreh The Moreh has three sections . There are some shiurim that skip directly to Section 3 (the most practical and accessible). Rabbi Kada is teaching in order — Sections 1, then 2, then 3 — because real understanding requires the full sequence. Section 1 — Overview (76 Chapters) The Rambam opens Section 1 with a verse from Yeshayahu: פתחו שערים ויבא גוי צדיק שמר אמנים \"Open the gates and let the righteous nation that keeps faithfulness enter.\" Two notes from the opening verse: \"Open the gates\" — the book gives tools to unlock difficulties, especially in Hebrew language \"Righteous nation that keeps faithfulness\" — only for those righteous in character AND belief; not for the doubtful or non-observant Section 1 as \"Negative Theology\" Section 1 is largely about what God is NOT. Before we can understand what God is, we must clear away what He is not. Chapters Focus 1–49 Incorporeality — refuting any physical conception of God; explaining the anthropomorphic language of Tanakh 50–69 (approx.) Negative theology proper — the attributes of God can only be understood negatively 70–76 (approx.) Against the Mutakallimun — critique of the Islamic theological philosophers The Core Message of Section 1 (In One Sentence) Do not accept things just because you've been told them or read them in a text. The Rambam will spend 76 chapters elaborating this — the Torah verses describing God's \"hand\" or \"eye\" cannot mean what their surface suggests. Tradition alone is not enough; you must penetrate to the meaning. Negative Theology — Preview When we say in the 13 Attributes of Mercy: אל רחום וחנון ארך אפים — \"merciful, gracious, slow to anger\" These are NOT describing Hashem. Per the Rambam: \"Hashem is merciful\" really means \"Hashem is not not-merciful\" The more positive descriptions we apply to Hashem, paradoxically, the less we may understand Him All true descriptions of God must be negations (This is counterintuitive — we will develop it in coming weeks.) What Section 1 Will Also Address Abuse of the Names of Hashem (kameot/amulets, charms) The Mutakallimun — Islamic philosophical school PART 5 — Section 1, Chapter 1: Tzelem u'Demut The Verse and the Problem נעשה אדם בצלמנו כדמותנו \"Let us make man in our image, in our likeness.\" This verse is the source of all anthropomorphic problems — the strongest scriptural-seeming evidence that God has a physical form. What People Mistakenly Think אנשים חשבו שצלם בעברית מורה על תבניתו ומתארו של דבר... דבר זה הביא להגשמה גמורה People thought tzelem means physical form/structure . Combined with \"in our image,\" this led them to complete corporealism — believing God has a body, face, hand. The Self-Justifying Logic וסברו שאם יטו את האמונה הזאת יכחישו את הכתוב \"They thought that if they rejected this belief, they would be denying the verse — and even denying God's existence.\" Their logic: \"We can't deny the pesukim, so we must believe God has a body.\" The \"Refined\" Version Some softened the view: yes God has a body, but: Bigger than human More illuminated, shining Not made of flesh and blood זוהי שיא רוממותו של האל לפי סברתם \"This was the peak of God's exalted nature, according to their view.\" This is not as crude as the picture of \"an old man with a white beard sitting on a throne\" — but it's still corporeal. Rambam will reject it equally. Important Precedent — Ibn Daud This view was articulated even before the Rambam. Ibn Daud (author of Emunah Ramah , early 1200s, just before the Rambam) wrote that some Christians had a purer conception of God than many of the Jews he encountered — because so many Jews insisted on corporealism on the strength of literal pesukim readings. The Shiur Komah Problem There is a Hebrew text called Shiur Komah that gives precise measurements of God's body — neck, head, beard. For example: \"The length of His neck is 13,000 myriads and 800 parasangs...\" \"The circumference of His head is 300,000...\" Who Wrote It? Authorship is debated . Some Geonim quote it approvingly. In Kabbalah tradition, the book is accepted and treated as containing profound mystical meaning. The Rambam's Position In Teshuvot HaRambam (Teshuvah 117), Rambam writes: לא חשבתי מעולם שהוא מחיבורי החכמים. וחלילה להם שזה יהיה מהם. ואינו אלא חיבור אחד הדרשנים היוונים בטולו. וסוף דבר למחוק זה הספר ולכרות זכרו היא מצווה רבה \"I never thought this was a work of the Chachamim. God forbid it should be from them. It is the work of one of the Greek-style drashanim, and it is null. In short — to erase this book and cut off its memory is a great mitzvah.\" The Rambam categorically rejects Shiur Komah. The Sefardi tradition follows the Rambam on this. Why Corporealism Denies the Unity of God מה שראוי לומר בהפרכת הגשמת האל ובאישוש אחדותו האמיתית שאינה תקפה אלא בביטול הגשמות Corporealism doesn't just contradict Hashem's nature — it contradicts the unity of Hashem. The Argument Anything physical, by definition, has boundaries and measurements Even if you imagine an infinite-expanding blob constantly growing — at any given moment, it has measurements (those measurements are changing, but they exist) Anything with measurements has limitations Limitations are incompatible with the absolute unity of God No matter how grand and magnificent you make a physical conception of God, it remains limited — and therefore not the true unity of Hashem. (This will be developed extensively over the next many chapters.) To Define Tzelem and Demut, First We Need to Define \"Toar\" The Rambam's method: before explaining tzelem (which describes the spiritual/intellectual image), he first introduces a contrasting word that does describe physical form — toar . Toar = Physical Form / Features הצורה המפורסמת בקרב ההמון... המונח המיוחד בעברית הוא תואר Throughout Tanakh, toar is the word for physical form, structure, appearance . Examples Yosef ויהי יוסף יפה תואר ויפה מראה \"Yosef was beautiful of form and beautiful of appearance\" The verse introducing Potiphar's wife's seduction of Yosef. Toar and mareh are both physical descriptors: Toar — specific features (face, eyes, nose) Mareh — overall appearance, the whole aura (Rashi and Ibn Ezra discuss the precise difference; for our purposes both are physical.) Shaul's Question About Shmuel's Spirit When Shaul HaMelech, distraught and unable to receive prophecy after Shmuel's death, consults the medium at Ein Dor: מה תארו? ותאמר איש זקן עלה והוא עוטה מעיל \"What is his toar? She said, an elderly man, wrapped in a cloak.\" Toar = physical description of how the figure appeared. Gideon כתאר בני המלך \"Like the toar of the king's sons\" — Gideon was perceived to have the physical bearing of royalty. Crafting / Forming Objects יתארהו בשרד ובמחוגה יתארהו \"He forms it with a marker, he shapes it with a compass.\" Forming a physical object — the word again describes physical dimension . The Key Statement המונח הזה אינו חל על האלוה יתעלה כלל חלילה וחס This term ( toar ) is NEVER applied to Hashem in Tanakh. Because toar means physical form, and Hashem has no physical form, the Torah never uses it of Him. The Rambam has just made his first philological argument: the very vocabulary of Tanakh respects God's incorporeality. What This Sets Up By contrast, when we encounter tzelem and demut applied to humans being created \"in God's image,\" we know: The Torah is using different words than the ones it uses for physical form Therefore, tzelem u'demut CANNOT mean physical form It must mean something else — the Rambam will tell us what next week Where We Stop We have finished the entire introduction and made the opening move of Chapter 1: Established that toar is the word for physical form in Tanakh Noted that toar is never used of Hashem Setting up the positive definition of tzelem and demut for next week Coming Up Next Quick review of what tzelem u'demut is NOT The Rambam's positive interpretation: what tzelem and demut actually mean — and how the verse \"let us make man in our image\" should be understood without any corporeal implication This launches the 49-chapter project of dismantling anthropomorphism systematically B'Ezrat Hashem","qbmFzhFWkMM":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 7 (ענוה — Humility) — Part 4 Edition: Feldheim This week: Concluding the intellectual analysis, religious arrogance, and humility before Hashem Recap: Chapter 7 So Far Definition (Part 1) Humility = recognizing one's true status, not exaggerating it in either direction Internal and external humility; humility before Hashem and before people Humility ≠ self-debasement The Three Methods of Training Personal/experiential — surrounding oneself with humble people, basic practical training (Part 1) Through the Torah — stories and mitzvot (Parts 2 & 3) Intellectual / philosophical analysis (Parts 3 & 4) Last Week (Part 3) — Method 3 Begins Three categories analyzed: Purely physical things (food, drink, indulgence) — identical to animals; the very things that sustain us also destroy us Material with metaphysical edge (wealth, power, authority) — generate strife, jealousy, premature death (especially among kings) Spiritual things — Layer 1 (intellect, prophecy) — can be lost through the smallest physical disturbance (hunger, sadness, fatigue) Example: Yaakov lost prophecy for 22 years because of his grief over Yosef Example: Even Moshe's level was limited because כי לא יראני האדם וחי This Evening's Roadmap Spiritual arrogance — Layer 2: Why the wise become more humble, not more arrogant Cosmic comparison: The Rambam's view of celestial bodies and angels — and what this means for human pride Religious arrogance: The most dangerous form Humility before Hashem: The fifth bowing in Amidah and the example of David Closing principle: Internal humility always; external humility sometimes PART 1 — Why the Wise Are the Most Humble The Pattern Among the Prophets and Sages זו היא סיבת ענוותם של הנביאים The prophets and intellectuals stayed humble because they were passionately seeking perfection — and they realized that the physical material (chomer) is the great obstacle to reaching it. The higher the level a person reaches, the more they realize: How easily it can be lost How fragile the connection to the heights is How material conditions can derail spiritual attainment in an instant Conclusion: Those who reached the highest peaks became more humble, not less — because of their meditation on their own shortcomings, not their achievements. David's Insight הן בעוון חוללתי ובחטא יחמתני אמי David HaMelech describes the human condition: from our very conception we carry a tendency toward selfishness and materialism. Every person, says Rabbi Avraham, is \"stained from his seed\" — born with the pull of materialism inherent in our biological origin. The Concealed Wisdom הן אמת חפצת בטוחות ובסתום חכמה תודיעני Rabbi Avraham quotes Rabbi Avraham HeChasid (likely brother of R' Yehuda HeChasid) on this verse: בטוחות (covered things): Perfection is always coated and covered over by the material ובסתום חכמה תודיעני: Whatever wisdom a person attains is hidden, fragmentary — only hints and signs, never explicit — because of the screen of material existence David's response: ולבי חלל בקרבי — my heart is hollow within me. This profound recognition led to his deep humility. The Paradox of Wisdom Producing Humility ולכן החכמים התקיפים בדעתם עוסקים בלשמה מביאה אותם חכמתם לענווה ולא לגאווה The wise come to humility through their wisdom — not arrogance. How? Two-step movement: They see their wisdom is substantial relative to others But they recognize it is trivial compared to what lies beyond The more you study, the more you realize how much more there is. Beginners think they know it all. True experts know how vast the unknown is. Story — Rabbi Akiva and the Brazen Critic A man asked Rabbi Akiva a halachic question. Rabbi Akiva didn't know the answer. The man retorted brazenly: אתה עקיבא בן יוסף ששמך הולך מסוף העולם ועד סופו? אשריך שזכית לשם ועדיין לא הגעת לרועה בקר! \"You are Rabbi Akiva whose name extends from one end of the world to the other? You're fortunate to have such a reputation — but you haven't even reached the level of a shepherd!\" How would most great rabbis respond to such an insult? Rabbi Akiva's response: אפילו לרועה בקר לא הגעתי \"In truth, I haven't even reached the wisdom of a shepherd.\" He didn't defend his honor. He recognized how much more there was to know — and so deflection became humility, not anger. The deeper insight: Real wisdom dissolves the impulse toward arrogance, because the wise person sees the vastness of what remains unknown. PART 2 — The Cosmic Hierarchy This section reflects the medieval Rambam-and-Rishonim worldview that celestial bodies possess intellect (sentience) — a higher form of spiritual existence than humans, but lower than angels. (Note: The Rambam states this explicitly in Mishneh Torah, Yesodei HaTorah — celestial bodies have souls, knowledge, intellect, and praise Hashem according to their level.) The Hierarchy of Perfection Level Entity Description Highest Hashem Unattainable perfection Malachim (angels) Highest created beings Pure form, no material Celestial bodies Spheres, stars Higher than humans, lower than angels Humans — Mixed chomer and tzura Animals — Pure chomer, no tzura The Chain of Spiritual Attainment The celestial bodies attain their perfection through the angels The prophets attain their perfection through the angels and celestial bodies Olam HaBa is intellectual existence alongside the angels The implications: every level of human spiritual achievement is mediated through these higher levels. Therefore, comparison to them dissolves any sense of human superiority. The Principle: Refinement of Matter → Spiritual Flourishing כל אדם שנזדככה גשמיותו ונצטמקה חומריותו ונתעלה הוייתו תתגלה רוחניותו ויגיע לשלמות המגומרת The more a person refines their material side, the more their spiritual side can shine. This is a major theme echoed throughout the Rishonim (Ramban, Ramchal, Rabbeinu Bachye, Nefesh HaChaim). Conversely: כל מי שגסה גשמיותו ונתעכרה נפשותיו דלות ואין להם מספר The coarser the materiality, the more difficult to reach spiritual heights. The Critical Insight: You Must Compare BOTH Ways This is the key argument of this section. A person might say: \"True, compared to angels and celestial bodies I am nothing. But compared to other humans — to those who eat, drink, and indulge crudely — I am elevated.\" Rabbi Avraham's response: If you're going to compare, you must compare in both directions. Comparing Downward (Allowed but Incomplete) Compared to the \"Sudanese, the Berbers\" (Rabbi Avraham's geographic/cultural references for very primitive societies) Compared to people consumed by gluttony, drink, and illicit physical pursuits True, you're higher Comparing Upward (The Necessary Counterweight) חייב לערוך את שלמותו... מול שלמות הגלגלים המתגלה דרך חומרם וצייתנותם ועבודתם You must also compare yourself to: The celestial bodies — completely unaffected by hunger, fatigue, distraction Their constant praise (השמים מספרים כבוד אל, וצבא השמים לך משתחוים) Then to the angels — pure form with no material interference at all Daniel's Response in Prophecy When Daniel encountered the angels in his prophetic visions, what did he say? איך יוכל עבד אדוני זה לדבר עם אדוני זה ואני מעתה לא יעמוד בי כוח ונשמה לא נשארה בי \"How can this servant of my lord speak with my lord? I have no strength left, no breath remains in me.\" The Rambam (Yesodei HaTorah) makes the same point: meditation on the celestial bodies and angels produces love and awe of Hashem, and the person sees himself as a vessel \"full of embarrassment and shame, empty and lacking.\" Comparison to Hashem Himself — Total Dissolution If you try to compare to Hashem: Avraham: אנכי עפר ואפר (I am dust and ash) Iyov: הן קלותי מה אשיבך (I am insignificant — how can I respond to You?) Iyov: והחכמה מאין תמצא — wisdom is unattainable in the land of the living The Final Liturgical Echo In Ne'ilah, at the peak spiritual moment of the year, we say: הלא כל הגיבורים כאין לפניך ואנשי השם כלא היו וחכמים כבלי מדע ונבונים כבלי השכל \"All the mighty are as nothing before You, men of renown as if they never were, the wise as if without knowledge, the discerning as if without understanding.\" The peak spiritual moment is precisely the moment we declare our nothingness — because the higher we rise, the clearer the comparison becomes. Yirmiyahu's Verse — Resolved אל יתהלל חכם בחכמתו... כי אם בזאת יתהלל המתהלל השכל וידוע אותי \"Let not the wise glory in their wisdom... only in this should one glory: in understanding and knowing Me.\" The Question Doesn't this verse permit a kind of arrogance — namely, pride in knowing God? The Resolution No. The Rambam (in the Moreh) explains: \"Glory\" here doesn't mean \"be arrogant\" It means be joyful, fulfilled, satisfied that you have attained the only thing worth attaining Furthermore: anyone who truly attains understanding of Hashem is automatically humbled , not made arrogant The verse's logic: if you really reach this attainment, it produces humility — so the verse cannot be a source for arrogance. The very thing it permits glory in produces its opposite. Closing Verse — Hashem's Concern for the World כי אני ה' עושה חסד משפט וצדקה בארץ Despite His incomprehensible loftiness, Hashem concerns Himself with this earth — which is as a single point compared to the upper world. David: מה אנוש כי תזכרנו ובן אדם כי תפקדנו — what is man that You should remember him? The fact that Hashem cares about us, despite our insignificance, should fill us with extreme humility (anavah kitzonit). PART 3 — Religious Arrogance (CRITICAL SECTION) This is the most practical and dangerous section of the chapter. כשם שיש חכמים הבאים לכלל טעות ומתגאים בחכמתם... כך יש צדיקים הבאים לכלל טעות ומתגאים בדקדוק מצוותיהם Just as the wise can fall into intellectual arrogance, the righteous can fall into religious arrogance — pride in their observance, their charity, their piety. This is dangerous precisely because: It feels justified (\"look how much I do for Hashem!\") It comes wrapped in religious language It produces contempt for others who appear less observant The Counter-Argument — Three Steps Step 1: The Sheer Scope of Obligation We are obligated to keep all 613 mitzvot — positive and negative. The Torah repeatedly emphasizes this: Source Phrase Devarim מי יתן והיה לבבם זה להם ליראה אותי ולשמור את כל מצוותי כל הימים Eikev בכל הדרך אשר ציוה ה' אלוקיכם אתכם תלכו Multiple ועשיתם את כל מצוותי No picking and choosing. And consider the level of detail in each mitzvah. The more you study, the more you realize how many sub-obligations each mitzvah contains. Step 2: Even Keeping 612 Falls Short Imagine someone keeps every single mitzvah except one — once in their entire life. Are they \"perfect\"? גם אז יהיה לוקה בחסר בשלמות הדת No — they have failed in completeness of the religion, because they violated some of what Hashem commanded. The Principle: No Cancellation in Torah The Midrash on אשר לא ישא פנים ולא יקח שוחד (\"Who shows no favor and takes no bribe\") is interpreted as שוחד מצוות : You cannot \"bribe\" Hashem with mitzvot Doing 612 mitzvot doesn't cancel out 1 sin Each act has its own consequence Step 3: Even a Single Imperceptible Sin Even if someone hypothetically kept all 613 — could you honestly say you've never once transgressed: לא תקום ולא תטור את בני עמך ואהבת לרעך כמוך Never carried a grudge in your heart? Never bore even the feeling of vengeance? Never failed in loving your fellow? Even David HaMelech said: כי עווני אגיד אדאג מחטאתי \"I will confess my iniquity, I worry about my sin.\" And David was testified by Hashem himself to be utterly righteous (with one famous exception). Aside: Did David Sin with Batsheva? כל מי שאומר דוד חטא אינו אלא טועה The Gemara's defense of David: Apparent Sin Gemara's Resolution Sending Uriah HaChiti to die Uriah was guilty of treason and chayav mitah anyway. The only flaw was not convening the Sanhedrin. Being with Batsheva (married woman) כל היוצא למלחמת בית דוד כותב גט כריתות לאשתו — all soldiers in David's army wrote conditional get to their wives before war. She was therefore divorced. This is offered here as testimony to David's overall righteousness, not as a complete defense of the episode. Shlomo's Conclusion כי אדם אין צדיק בארץ אשר יעשה טוב ולא יחטא \"There is no righteous person on earth who does only good and does not sin.\" (Note: Rav Saadia Gaon interpreted this verse differently — that there is no one incapable of sinning. He held it is possible to live without sin. Rabbi Avraham follows the simple reading: no one actually lives without sin. ) The Mishna's converging witness: יצר לב האדם רע מנעוריו — the inclination of man's heart is evil from youth. The Levels of Religious Observance Rabbi Avraham distinguishes two categories of observant people: Category 1 — External Religion (Datiut Chitzonit) Keeps the mitzvot externally Doesn't understand the inner meaning Doesn't have spiritual fervor connecting the action to its purpose If you are in this category — what are you arrogant about? The external is incomplete without the internal. You haven't yet reached the higher level of practice. Category 2 — Internal Religion (Datiut Penimit) Profound spiritual fervor and meaning Deep connection between action and devotion Internalized commitment If you are in this category — be even more cautious. הקדוש ברוך הוא מדקדק עם סביביו אפילו כחוט השערה \"Hashem is exacting with those close to Him, even by a hair's breadth.\" The closer to Hashem, the more precise the standard. Failures that would be unnoticed in others become consequential at higher levels. Example — Moshe and Aharon At Mei Merivah, the sin was so subtle we can barely identify it from our position. But on their level: יען לא האמנתם בי להקדישני \"Because you did not believe in Me to sanctify Me.\" And they were punished — not allowed to enter the Land. The higher the position, the higher the standard. The Beautiful Story — The Rambam and the Self-Satisfied Mitpalel אמרו לפניו כי אחד משומרי דת אמר בליל יום הכיפורים איני יודע על איזה עוון אחזור בתשובה A pious person on Yom Kippur night said: \"I don't know what sin to repent for this year.\" The Rambam heard this and replied: מסכן שכמותו... לא ידע שעליו לדעת לחזור בתשובה על החושב הוא שאין לו עוון לחזור עליו בתשובה \"How unfortunate. He needs to do teshuva for the very thought that he has nothing to do teshuva for.\" The act of believing you are sin-free is itself the sin requiring teshuva. The moment you think you've arrived, you've failed. CRITICAL CAVEAT — Don't Misread This Rabbi Avraham now stops and clarifies, because this section could easily damage a healthy reader: אין כוונתו שתראה עצמך פחות צדיק ממה שאתה באמת \"I do not mean for you to see yourself as less righteous than you actually are.\" What This Section Is NOT Saying ❌ Walk around feeling terrible about yourself all the time ❌ Discount the good you've genuinely done ❌ Treat yourself as a rasha (cf. the Mishna in Avot: אל תהי רשע בפני עצמך ) ❌ Despair of your spiritual development What This Section IS Saying אלא שתחשוב כי הדרגה שאתה נמצא בה נמוכה מאוד לעומת מה שהיה עליך באמת Only at the moment of arrogance — when you feel \"I am better than him, I am better than her\" — pull out this analysis. When You Feel Then Apply Gratitude for mitzvot you've performed Be happy, \"Baruch Hashem I was zocheh\" Awareness of growth Recognize the genuine progress Pride / superiority over others Remember failings, the scope of obligation, the levels above you Rav Nachman of Breslov's Parallel אל תהי רשע בפני עצמך Rav Nachman elaborated this extensively: do not treat yourself as wicked, because despair is itself spiritually destructive. Self-deflation that leads to giving up is worse than the arrogance it tries to counter. The whole analysis is a counterweight to arrogance, not a tool for self-loathing. PART 4 — Humility Before Hashem הענוה לפניו יתעלה חובת כל יצור שיש בו דעה Humility before Hashem is the obligation of every intelligent creature. Universal Humility Before Hashem Entity Their Humility Celestial bodies & angels וצבא השמים לך משתחוים Avraham ואנכי עפר ואפר David מי אנכי? מי חיי? משפחת אבי? Iyov הן קלותי מה אשיבך Moshe מי אנכי כי אלך אל פרעה? / לא איש דברים אנכי / לא אוכל אנכי לבדי Every great figure in Tanakh expressed deep, almost reflexive, humility before Hashem. The Halachic Expression — Bowing in Amidah Halacha mandates five bowings during prayer. Many people know the first four but miss the fifth: # Where What 1 Beginning of Amidah Baruch Atah Hashem (start of first bracha) 2 End of first bracha מגן אברהם 3 Beginning of Modim מודים אנחנו לך 4 End of Modim הטוב שמך ולך נאה להודות 5 Stepping back at the end Complete prostration as you step three steps back The fifth one is widely missed. Many people just step back without bowing. The Halacha (Rambam, Shulchan Aruch) is clear: the three-step retreat should be done from a position of bowing. The King's Posture in Prayer ומלך ישראל היה כורע בכל התפלה מקרא ראשון עד סוף כל התפלה The King of Israel had to remain bowed throughout the entire Amidah — from the first verse to the last. Proof: Shlomo HaMelech ויהי ככלות שלמה להתפלל קם מלפני מזבח אדוני מכרע על ברכיו \"When Shlomo finished praying, he rose from kneeling before the altar.\" The verse implies he rose at the end — meaning he had been kneeling the entire prayer. Why More Humility for the King? Leadership by example — he demonstrates to the people what humility before Hashem looks like Position requires it — his power and authority make him most at risk of arrogance, so he is given the most intensive antidote (Note the structural parallel with the king's Sefer Torah obligation from last week — the king receives extra anti-arrogance measures because his position carries the most risk.) David's Anticipation ושחקתי לפני ה'... ונקלותי עוד מזאת והייתי שפל בעיני \"I will dance before Hashem... I will be lowlier than this and humble in my own eyes.\" David anticipated and embraced this — even relishing it. The greater his position, the more eagerly he sought lowliness. Closing Principle — Internal Always, External Sometimes הענווה הפנימית שהזכרנו שם צריכה להתקיים בלב האדם בכל רגע ורגע Internal humility must be present at every moment, in every situation, without exception. Nothing — not success, not position, not honors — should change it. Like David: ולבי חלל בקרבי / לא גבה לבי ולא רמו עיני ולא הלכתי בגדולות ובנפלאות ממני אכן הענוה החיצונית... קרה פעמים שזה הצריך לסטות מצד הקו External humility, however, is not absolute. Sometimes the situation requires acting without external humility. We will see when next week. Summary of This Evening's Shiur Three Major Conclusions from the Intellectual Analysis Wisdom produces humility — the wise see how much more there is, like Rabbi Akiva refusing to defend his honor Cosmic comparison dissolves pride — celestial bodies, angels, Hashem; if you compare, compare both ways Religious arrogance is the most dangerous form — the scope of obligation, the impossibility of perfection, the Rambam's \"teshuva on having nothing to do teshuva for\" The Critical Boundary The analysis is a counterweight to pride, not a tool for self-loathing When grateful for growth: celebrate it When feeling superior: pull out the analysis אל תהי רשע בפני עצמך — never despair of yourself The Two Layers of Humility Internal humility: mandatory always, in every situation External humility: sometimes (to be developed next week) Humility Before Hashem Universally obligatory for all intelligent beings The five bowings in Amidah (don't miss the fifth) The king's total prostration as the ultimate model Coming Up Next Concluding Chapter 7 (before Shavuot): Limitations of humility — the difference between humility and excessive self-negation Humility with other people — practical character traits and behavior When humility is the wrong response — situations requiring assertiveness The reward of humility — praise and consequences After Shavuot, we begin Chapter 8 — the longest chapter in the sefer. B'Ezrat Hashem","yqwB6HLuXp0":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Topic: מלאכת בורר — Selecting and Sorting (completing the siman) Siman: שיט (319) Seifim covered tonight: Seif 6, Seif 7 (Zoreh), Seif 9-16 (Meraked and related) Source text: Shulchan Aruch with Rema, Mishna Berura, and Bi'ur Halacha Recap: What We've Covered in Borer So Far The Three Conditions for Permitted Selection Le'altar / Samuch L'Seudah — for immediate use B'yad — using the hand (or extension of hand) Ochel from Pesoleth — taking the wanted from the unwanted Major Principles Established Two edible foods — the unwanted is treated as pesoleth (Rama) Borer = selecting AND sorting Definition of a mixture (ta'arovet) — required for borer to apply Yom Tov vs. Shabbat — on Yom Tov, take the easier path (less tircha); on Shabbat, always take the ochel from the pesoleth Last Three Shiurim's Major Chiddushim Le'altar in seconds (derech achila) — machloket Maharit Abulafia vs. Maharitatz; halacha not relied upon as sole heter Ein Borer B'Lach — no borer when a solid is mixed with a liquid (Sefardi and many Ashkenazi poskim) Take the entire small mixture (fly + spoonful of soup) — universally permitted Guf Echad — when something is naturally one composition (bones in fish, seeds in watermelon, peel on cucumber, stem on tomato), removing the unwanted is tikun ochel , not borer No psik reisha with borer — a natural action whose inevitable consequence happens to involve separation is permitted (salt shaker with rice, removing a tea bag) Tonight's Roadmap We're moving beyond borer proper into the related selecting melachot: Seif 6 — extracting beans from inedible pods (technically Dash, brought here for context) Seif 7 — Zoreh (winnowing): the rules of using wind/air for separation Seif 9-10 — Meraked (sieving/straining): liquids and the three categories of clarity Seif 11-13 — additional rules around cloths, baskets, and improvised strainers Seif 14 — pouring out unwanted liquid from a mixture (very practical) Seif 15-16 — the egg through mustard, drinking water with worms, and churning butter Brief Introduction — The Sequence of Selecting Melachot in the Mishkan Understanding Zoreh and Meraked requires seeing where they sit in the workflow that produced the Mishkan dyes: Step Melacha Action 5 Dash (threshing) Extracting seeds from pods 6 Zoreh (winnowing) Throwing the mixture in the air → wind blows the chaff away 7 Borer (selecting) Hand-selecting good seeds from remaining pebbles/debris 8 Tochen (grinding) Crushing the seeds into powder 9 Meraked (sieving) Sieving the powder to separate fine from coarse Then the powder was made into paste and used as dye for the Mishkan's roof. All four selecting melachot (Dash, Zoreh, Borer, Meraked) appear in this sequence. Seif 6 — Extracting Beans from Pods (Technically Dash) This seif is really about Dash (the 5th melacha — extracting from an encasement), but the Shulchan Aruch brings it here because it's said in the same Gemara as Seif 7. What is Dash? Taking edible food from an inedible encasement — for example, taking grain or beans from their inedible pods. Important Limitation Edible pods (e.g., sugar snap peas, mangetout): No issue of Dash, because the whole thing is edible. There's no \"encasement\" of inedible material. Inedible pods only: Dash applies — taking out the edible seeds from the inedible pod. The Three Levels of Action Method Status Opening the pod normally and extracting seeds Forbidden d'Oraita Rubbing pods between hands to release seeds Forbidden mi'derabbanan Pressing with the tips of the fingers in small quantity Permitted (with shinui) The Halacha (Shulchan Aruch) אין מוללין מלילות... אלא מולל בשינוי בראשי אצבעותיו You may extract with the tips of the fingertips, but only a small quantity — so it shouldn't appear like weekday activity. Read carefully: the small quantity is on top of the shinui, not the heter. The shinui itself permits it; the small quantity ensures it doesn't look like a weekday work. Rema — The Stringent Position וטוב להחמיר... ולכן אסור לפרק אגוזים בלוזים או אגוזים גדולים מתוך קליפתן הירוקה The Rambam and Rif understood the underlying Gemara (Beitzah) as applying to both Shabbat and Yom Tov . Other Rishonim understood it as applying only to Yom Tov . The Rema is machmir like the second view. Practical: Sefardim: May press with fingertips in small quantity (following Shulchan Aruch) Ashkenazim: Should be stringent and not extract from green/inedible shells at all PART 2 — Zoreh (Winnowing) What is Zoreh? The melacha of Zoreh is using wind, an artificial air current, or even one's breath to separate impurities from a mixture. Examples Throwing a mixture in the air so the wind separates chaff from seeds Using a fan to blow away lighter particles Blowing on something to separate (e.g., blowing shells off peanuts) — this can be d'Oraita Critical Differences from Borer Borer Zoreh Heter of samuch l'seudah Yes (with all 3 conditions) NO Why no leniency? — It's a \"sophisticated\" melacha — using wind has no everyday eating-related dispensation Therefore: Even immediately before eating, you cannot blow shells off peanuts. That would potentially be issur d'Oraita . Why Wind Counts as a Direct Act (Not Grama) Generally in halacha, wind is considered indirect (grama). So why isn't every Zoreh a grama (which would be permitted on Shabbat)? Answer: This is the derech of the melacha . The original melacha was performed with wind. Therefore, wind is the direct act here, not the indirect cause. Zoreh Requires a Mixture Just like borer, Zoreh only applies when there is a ta'arovet . If there's no mixture, there's no Zoreh. The Peculiar Rema at the End of the Siman הרוקק ברוח בשבת והרוח מפזר הרוק חייב משום זורה If a person spits on Shabbat and the wind disperses the spit, the Rema (quoting the Yerushalmi) says he is chayav for Zoreh . Difficulty Spit appears to be one substance, not a mixture. So how does Zoreh apply? Resolutions Some Acharonim suggest: spit is actually a combination of liquid + phlegm + other elements, so there is a mixture being separated by the wind Most Acharonim reject the Rema's ruling here as not l'halacha The Ben Ish Chai is one notable exception who does accept it L'halacha: Zoreh only applies to genuine mixtures Practical Outflow — Aerosols and Sprays on Shabbat Based on these principles: It's one substance (no mixture) Many hold Zoreh applies only to gidulei karka (things that grow from the ground) The combination of these reasons → deodorants, hairsprays, and other aerosols are permitted on Shabbat Seif 7 — Practical Halachot of Zoreh Setup You already extracted seeds from pods before Shabbat (no Dash issue), but the seeds are mixed with chaff. What's Forbidden לא ינפה בכנון ותמחוי Method Status Use a sieve or utensil Forbidden (borer with kli) Throw up and down with two hands Forbidden Throw from one hand to the other Should be avoided (Rambam in Peirush HaMishnayot, Mishna Berura, poskim) Blowing on it Forbidden d'Oraita (Zoreh) What's Permitted אלא מנפה בידה אחת בכל כחה You may forcefully throw up and down with one hand only. Practical Application — Peanuts in Shells You have a bunch of shelled peanuts mixed with empty shells: ❌ Don't throw with two hands ❌ Don't toss between hands ❌ Don't blow on them ✅ Take a handful in one hand, toss it forcefully up and down. Whatever shells fall away, fine. Repeat with one hand if needed. The principle: avoid using wind/air currents as the primary mechanism of separation. PART 3 — Meraked (Sieving / Straining) What is Meraked? The word meraked comes from \"to dance\" — referring to the bouncing/shaking motion of items in a sieve. The classical Meraked is shaking items in a sieve so the fine passes through and the coarse remains. What About a Strainer (Meshamerret)? The Mishna says a wine strainer (meshamerret) has the same prohibition. But why? Two views in the Rishonim: Position Rationale Rashi A strainer is a type of sieve → forbidden as Meraked Rambam A strainer doesn't shake items together; it pours one through the other → forbidden as Borer with a kli Either way, using a strainer on Shabbat in certain circumstances is forbidden d'Oraita. Seif 9 — The Major Chiddush: Pouring Water Through Existing Sediments משמרת אפילו תלויה מערב שבת אסור ליתן בה שמרים You cannot put fresh wine with sediment through a pre-set-up strainer. BUT — The Chiddush אבל אם נתן בה שמרים מערב שבת מותר ליתן עליהם מים If sediments were already in the strainer from before Shabbat, you may pour fresh water through them — even though: The water mixes with the sediments Then strains out, picking up the wine flavor The strainer performs a separation Why Is This Permitted? The principle: borer is judged by the moment of action, not the consequences. At the moment you poured the water in: The water itself was clear — no mixture Only after pouring does it mix with the sediments and then get strained Since at the moment of your action there was no mixture , no borer occurs. The strainer's subsequent activity is not your action. Practical Application — Tea Leaves You used the tea leaves before Shabbat. Used tea leaves sit in the teapot. On Shabbat: ✅ Pour fresh hot water in to extract additional flavor ✅ Pour out the resulting tea The water enters as clear water (no mixture); it leaves having picked up flavor through the tea leaves This is muttar because at the time of pouring, there was no mixture being separated. Seif 10 — The Three Categories of Liquid Clarity This is the foundational structure for liquid filtration on Shabbat. Two introductory points first: Introduction 1 — Three Categories Category Description Status 1 — Achurim Legamrei Completely cloudy/impure; the vast majority would not drink without filtering D'Oraita to filter 2 — Achurim Ketzat Slightly cloudy; drinkable, but most prefer to filter D'Rabbanan to filter (with strainer); permitted with cloth 3 — Tzilulim Basically clear; just want extra refinement Permitted to filter Introduction 2 — Melaben (Laundering) The melacha of Melaben (washing/laundering) is relevant here. There's a debate among Rishonim whether soaking a clean cloth is also Melaben (laundering by additional cleansing). Bear this in mind for the cloth rules. The Halacha (Shulchan Aruch) יין או מים שהם צלולים מותר לסננם במשמרת אף על פי שיש בהם קסמים דקים Category 3 (Clear liquid): May use a strainer (meshamerret) even if there are tiny twigs or impurities, since it's already drinkable. אבל בסודרים אסור משום ליבון Cloth filter on clear water: Forbidden because of Melaben (laundering the cloth) — or alternatively because of gezeirah shema yischot (you might come to squeeze the cloth). ויין ושאר משקין מותר Wine or other (non-water) liquids in a cloth: Permitted, because there's no Melaben concern (laundering only applies to water). ואם הם עכורים בין מים בין יין אסור לסננם Category 1 (Completely impure): Forbidden d'Oraita to filter, whether water or wine. The Middle Category (Achurim Ketzat) — Implied The Shulchan Aruch leaves out Category 2 explicitly, but it follows from the framework: ❌ Strainer: forbidden (would be borer with a kli at d'rabbanan level) ✅ Cloth: permitted (two derabbanans don't combine to forbid) The Three Categories — Comprehensive Summary Category Strainer Cloth Category 1 (achurim legamrei) ❌ D'Oraita ❌ Category 2 (achurim ketzat) ❌ D'Rabbanan ✅ Category 3 (tzilulim) ✅ ❌ Water (Melaben), ✅ Wine/other The Rambam's Stricter View The Rambam was machmir on all three: Strainer — never permitted, even for clear liquids (weekday activity) Cloth — only permitted for fully clear, never for any cloudiness Exception — fresh wine still fermenting (the sediment is part of how it's drunk) Halacha: All poskim — both Sefardi and Ashkenazi — follow the first opinion (Shulchan Aruch) , not the Rambam here. Practical Applications of the Three Categories Filter Jugs (Brita and Similar) Setting Category Permitted? England (drinkable tap water, filtering for refinement) Category 3 ✅ Permitted Israel (when most don't drink straight from tap) Category 2 ❌ Forbidden Personal preference only (you alone don't drink tap; family does) Discussion (batla da'ato) Be machmir Tap Strainers Pre-installed before Shabbat (no boneh issue) Same logic: water type determines the category In England (Category 3) → permitted to use Orange Juice Lots of pulp/pips, undrinkable for most → Category 1 → strainer forbidden d'Oraita Some pulp, drinkable but most prefer clear → Category 2 → strainer forbidden d'rabbanan; cloth permitted Basically clear, just refining → Category 3 → strainer permitted Seif 11 — Cloth Strainer Without an Indentation כשמסננין יין בסודר צריך להזהר שלא יעשה גומא לקבל בו משום שינוי When using a cloth as a permitted strainer (per Seif 10), don't form an indentation in the cloth that would catch the liquid. Keep it flat. Why? This would be the typical weekday way of using a cloth-strainer. Maintaining shinui keeps it within the heter. Seif 12 — The Egyptian Basket (Kefifa Mitzrit) כל מקום שמותר לסנן יין בסודר מותר לסננו בכפיפה מצרית A kefifa mitzrit (Egyptian-style basket) follows the same rules as a cloth — wherever cloth is permitted, the basket is too. ויש ליזהר בכפיפה שלא יגביהה משפת הכלי טפח Caveat: Don't lift the basket more than a tefach (~handsbreadth) above the receiving vessel. Two reasons given: Shinui (it should look different from weekday) Possible Ohel (forming a roof-like covering) Seif 13 — Don't Build a Strainer at the Mouth of a Vessel כלי שמערים בו יין מהחבית לא יתן בפיו קשים וקסמים בחזקה שהם מסננים כעין משמרת If using a vessel to draw wine from a barrel, do not firmly pack twigs or material at the mouth of the vessel that would create an improvised strainer. That counts as making/using a strainer. Seif 14 — Pouring Out Unwanted Liquid (HUGELY PRACTICAL) מותר לערות בנחת מכלי לחבירו You may gently pour from one vessel to another , even if there's sediment at the bottom. ובלבד שיזהר כשיפסק הקילוח ומתחילים לראות ניצוצות קטנות הנוטפות באחרונה מתוך הפסולת יפסיק BUT — when the steady stream stops and only small drops start coming out from among the sediment, stop pouring and leave those final drops with the sediment. If you continue, the drops being separated from the sediment is borer . Two Critical Principles Here Principle 1 — A Liquid Mixture Is Local, Not Whole We've seen this before with the fly in soup. The liquid is only considered \"mixed\" with the sediment at the bottom where the sediment is , not throughout the entire vessel. So pouring off the top — which has no sediment touching it — is not selecting from a mixture. Principle 2 — The Thing Being Poured Out Is Considered \"Taken\" This is the key chiddush. When you pour out something, what's being poured is what's being \"taken\" — even though physically it leaves your hand. The Chayei Adam disagreed and held the opposite (what stays in your hand is \"taken\"). But all other poskim follow the main view: poured = taken. Practical Implications Tuna Can Oil ✅ Pouring off the top layer of oil in a steady stream → no problem (you're \"taking\" the oil; it wasn't mixed at that level) ❌ Once it slows to drops mixing with the tuna → stop . Continued pouring is borer. If you don't want the oil at all: Pour off what comes off in a steady stream; for the rest, use a fork to remove the tuna and discard the remaining oil another way. Peaches in Syrup If you want to drink the syrup: ✅ permitted (poured = taken; you're taking what you want) If you don't want the syrup, only the peaches: ❌ Problem. The syrup and peaches are fully mixed throughout. Pouring off the syrup is taking the unwanted from the wanted (borer pesoleth from ochel). Solution: Take peaches out with a fork. Seif 15 — Egg Through Mustard מסננת שנתן בה חרדל לסננו מותר ליתן בה ביצה אף על פי שהחלמון יורד למטה עם החרדל והחלבון נשאר למעלה A strainer with mustard already in it from before Shabbat. You may put a raw egg through it: The yolk passes through and colors the mustard yellow The albumin (white) stays in the strainer This appears to be a textbook borer with a kli. Why is it permitted? Two Approaches in the Acharonim Approach 1 — An Egg Is One Unit Yolk and albumin together = one substance. There's no mixture, hence no borer. Implication: You could even separate yolk from white when you actually want the yolk (like for cooking) — permitted any time Approach 2 — Two Distinct Substances, But No Borer Anyway A cooked egg is universally one unit (everyone agrees) A raw egg has more separation between yolk and white, so it might be considered two substances Then why is it permitted? Because of the Pri Megadim's position: borer is select-and-take. Here you want neither the yolk nor the albumin (you only want to color the mustard). Since you're not selecting either as \"the wanted,\" it's just sorting, not selecting. Practical Difference Between the Approaches Approach 1: Can always separate yolk/white, regardless of intent Approach 2: Only permitted in this specific case where neither is \"wanted\"; if you wanted the yolk for itself, it would be borer Seif 16 — Drinking Water with Worms Through a Cloth מים שיש בהם תולעים מותר לשתותם על ידי מפה בשבת If your water has insects/worms in it (Category 1 — undrinkable as is), you may drink it through a cloth placed over your mouth. Why Is This Permitted? דלא שייך בורר ומשמר אלא במתקן העניין קודם אכילה ושתייה אבל אם בשעת שתייה מעכב הפסולת שלא יכנס לתוך פיו אין זה מלאכה Borer/Meraked applies only to preparation before eating/drinking. When the act of drinking itself separates the worms (the cloth holds them back as you drink), this is derech achila/shtiyah — the way of consuming, not preparation. This is parallel to (but stronger than) what we learned about taking bones out in your mouth. As a universally accepted derech achila — not a discussion like the salmon-second-before-eating case from last week — drinking through a cloth is fully permitted. Seif (end) — Churning Butter (Mechavetz) המחבץ תולדת בורר הוא Churning butter from milk (Mechavetz) is a toledah of borer (Rambam). Therefore: אף על פי שנותנים שומשמין ואגוזים לדבש לא יחבצם בידו Even though sesame seeds and nuts are added to honey, don't churn them with your hand on Shabbat. Comprehensive Summary of the Whole Siman 319 Borer (Seifim 1-5, 8) — Already Covered Three conditions: l'altar, b'yad, ochel from pesoleth Guf echad permits removing bones, seeds, peels from natural compositions Ein borer b'lach (Sefardi position) No psik reisha with borer Take the entire small mixture (fly + spoonful) Dash (Seif 6) — Tonight Inedible pods: pressing with fingertips in small quantity = permitted with shinui (Sefardi) Rema is machmir for green nut shells Zoreh (Seif 7) — Tonight No samuch l'seudah dispensation Forbidden: blowing, two-handed throwing, hand-to-hand Permitted: forceful one-handed up-and-down throw Aerosols permitted (one substance + gidulei karka considerations) Meraked (Seifim 9-16) — Tonight Three categories of clarity: d'Oraita / d'Rabbanan / permitted Seif 9 chiddush: judging borer by moment of action (clear water poured through existing sediments) Seif 10: Strainers and cloths by category Seif 14 chiddush: the thing poured out is \"taken\"; stop when stream becomes drops Seif 15: Egg through mustard (machloket why) Seif 16: Cloth over the mouth while drinking (derech shtiyah) End: Mechavetz (churning) is a toledah of borer Coming Up Next Next week is our final shiur before Shavuot. Either: Begin Siman 320 — Hilchot Dash (threshing) and related selecting/processing melachot, or Shavuot-related shiur Decision next week. Shabbat Shalom","9zqiH_iP8iM":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: The Rambam's Introduction (continuing toward conclusion) Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Recap: The Rambam's Introduction So Far Shiur 1 (Pre-Pesach) — Background Biography of the Rambam and the historical context of the Moreh Shiur 2 — Why Rambam Wrote the Book Letter to his student Rabbi Yosef Purpose: to explain veiled imagery, multi-meaning terms, and metaphors in Tanakh Audience: those educated in both Torah and philosophy who are perplexed Why he can't explain everything exhaustively (Ma'aseh Bereishit and Ma'aseh Merkavah belong to the realm of rashei perakim only) The lightning analogy — most prophets have flashes; the most we can hope for is occasional shine Shiur 3 — Why Bereishit and the Importance of Meshalim Bereishit is natural science — the first rung of the ladder up to metaphysics → understanding Hashem → proper observance of mitzvot Even Bereishit is multi-layered (simple meaning for children, deeper meanings for the developed) The abandoned projects on prophecy and on derashot Two beautiful meshalim: The candle and the diamond — a worthless candle can light up priceless treasures Golden apples in silver settings — the surface has value, but the depth is greater (silver eyelets that allow the gold beneath to peek through) This Evening's Shiur We are completing the introduction this evening (with possibly some spillover into next week). Three major sections: Two types of meshalim in Tanakh — when every word matters vs. when only the general idea matters The Rambam's advice to the reader — including his two oaths and his most powerful personal statement The first four (of seven) reasons books contain contradictions — laying the groundwork for understanding apparent contradictions in the Moreh itself PART 1 — The Two Types of Meshalim דע שיש שתי דרכים במשלי הנבואה The Rambam tells us this is crucial to understand. There are two types of meshalim in Tanakh: Type A — Every Word Matters Each individual word in the mashal carries its own meaning and contributes a specific element to the deeper truth. Type B — The General Concept Matters The mashal is verbose. Many words are present, but not every word carries its own deeper meaning. The extra words serve one of two purposes: To make the mashal more attractive and complete as a story To deliberately conceal the deeper meaning — paradoxically, more words can hide more effectively Counter-intuitively, sometimes more verbiage is used to obscure , not to clarify. The deeper truth is buried inside an elaborate narrative. Example of Type A — Yaakov's Ladder והנה סולם מצב ארצה וראשו מגיע השמימה והנה מלאכי אלהים עלים וירדים בו והנה ה' נצב עליו The Rambam identifies seven distinct elements , each of which adds meaning to the nimshal: # Element Word(s) 1 The ladder itself סולם 2 Set on the earth מצב ארצה 3 Reaching to heaven ראשו מגיע השמימה 4 Angels מלאכי אלהים 5 Going up עלים 6 Going down יורדים 7 Hashem standing above ה' נצב עליו One Reading of the Seven (Following the Ramchal and Others) The Rambam doesn't spell out what the seven correspond to, but the meforshim suggest: All reality is composed of chomer and tzura (matter and form, physical and spiritual) The ladder is the bridge connecting the two realms Artza (earth) = the chomer, the physical, the material Hashamayma (heavens) = the spiritual realm Why \"up\"? Hashem is no more \"up\" than down. But humans naturally give prominence to elevated things — a tall person commands more presence — so when conveying spiritual elevation, the language of \"up\" becomes natural (אשא עיני אל ההרים) Malachei Elohim = the word malach comes from לך (to go) — these are messengers, agents of change, reactions and effects Olim (going up) = our human actions emanating change upward — every mitzvah, every aveirah, sends consequences up. This is a major theme in the Ramchal and Nefesh HaChaim. Yordim (going down) = celestial forces emanating change downward into our physical world Hashem nitzav alav = Hashem oversees and supervises the entire system Each word is a discrete piece of metaphysical instruction. Every word counts. Layers of the Same Mashal Notice how this same mashal works at multiple depths: Children's level: Yaakov dreamed of a ladder with angels going up and down Rashi's level: the angels of chutz la'aretz were leaving and the angels of Eretz Yisrael were arriving as Yaakov crossed the border Rambam's / philosophical level: the seven-element map of how chomer and tzura interact through human action and divine emanation This is exactly what the Rambam established in earlier shiurim — multi-level meaning. Example of Type B — The Seductress in Mishlei (Chapter 7) כי בחלון ביתי בעד אשנבי נשקפתי ואראה בפתאים אבינה בבנים נער חסר לב... A long, vivid, deliberately verbose passage: The narrator looks out his window and sees a young, foolish boy walking through the streets at twilight A woman approaches him, dressed as a harlot, \"rebellious\" and never at home — sometimes in the squares, sometimes lurking at corners She grabs him, kisses him, speaks brazenly: \"Today I have brought sacrifices and fulfilled my vows...\" She invites him: \"My bed is decked with Egyptian linens, perfumed with myrrh, aloes, and cinnamon — let us drink our fill of love until morning...\" Her closing argument: \"My husband is not at home — he has gone on a long journey, taking a purse of money. He won't return until the full moon.\" The young man is seduced and led to ruin The Single Point of the Whole Mashal העולה מקצת הדברים האלו היא האזהרה מפני המשכות אחרי תענוגות הגוף ותאוותיו Don't be drawn after physical desires. That's the entire point. One unified message — an extended warning against following the seductive pull of materialism. Why an Eshet Ish AND a Zonah? Why does Shlomo describe the seductress as both a harlot AND a married woman ? Surely one would suffice? The answer goes to the heart of chomer and tzura: Chomer (matter) is traditionally represented by the woman — she receives form, holds it, develops it Tzura (form) is represented by the man — he provides the formative principle Example: conception. The man contributes the formative tzura in moments. The woman holds, develops, and gives physical reality to that form over nine months. Without her chomer, the tzura is just theoretical. So: the seductress as eshet ish = matter that already belongs to another form. When you chase physical desire (the eshet ish-zonah), you are taking matter that is bound to a different form, claiming it for yourself improperly. It's not just any indulgence — it's appropriation of matter that has another rightful relationship. Eshet Chayil at the End of Mishlei The end of the book reverses this image — the Eshet Chayil , the woman of valor, is matter rightly aligned with its proper form. She develops her home and her husband. This is the redemptive counter-image to the seductress of chapter 7. The Single Lesson כלומר האדם לא ימשך אחר בהמיותו בלבד Don't follow your purely animal nature. Why? Because your physical/material aspect is identical to that of any animal. If you live only by physical desires, there is no differentiation between you and a beast. The Rambam's Critical Warning Now that we know this is a Type B mashal, don't ask why the verses say: \"Sacrifices today, vows fulfilled\" — why specifically that? \"Egyptian linens\" — why Egyptian and not Persian? \"My husband is far away with a money purse\" — why mention the money? These are part of the literary scaffolding — designed to depict adulterous behavior vividly. They're not coded with deeper meaning. שקצת המצבים שהזכיר הם מצב ממין מצב הנואפים Some of the details are simply how adulterers actually behave and speak — narrative realism, not encrypted instruction. The Rambam's Sharpest Critique of Bad Methodology אך המבולבלים שמוחם הזדהם בהשקפות מוטעות... הם יסלדו מפרקים רבים ממנו If you press for hidden meaning where none was intended, two errors result : Error 1 — You Lose the Main Point Investigating \"why Egyptian linens?\" instead of \"what is this mashal warning against?\" → you miss the entire purpose. Error 2 — You Manufacture Meaning That Was Never There או שיחייב אותך לפרש דברים שאין להם פירוש ושלא נולדו לפירוש You force interpretations onto material that was never written to bear them. ומחייבת זאת תביא אותך אל כגון ההזיה העצומה הזאת שרוב כיתות העולם בזמננו הזו אותם The Rambam sees this happening in his own time — and it's eerily applicable to ours: Someone has a nice idea Finds a pasuk that almost fits Stuffs the idea into the verse — through gematria, through wordplay, through forced reading Declares: \"This is what the Torah meant!\" The Rambam's verdict: this is מגלה פנים בתורה שלא כהלכה — explaining Torah as it was never meant to be explained This is a genuine chiddush of the Rambam: not every word in every mashal is loaded with meaning. Some words are dressing. The skill of the reader is knowing which is which. How to Read Meshalim Correctly אלא לעולם תהא כוונתך ברוב המשלים לדעת הכללות שהם כיוונו להם For most meshalim, focus on the general meaning — what is this trying to teach overall? For some, the Rambam says he won't even explicitly identify the nimshal: משום שכאשר תדע שזה משל יתברר לך מיד לאיזה דבר הוא משל Once you know it's a mashal, you can usually figure out the point yourself. Just identifying something as a mashal is, the Rambam says, like removing a screen between vision and the visible — it's enough. Summary of Part 1 Two Types of Meshalim Type A — every word matters Yaakov's ladder (7 distinct elements) Type B — general idea matters The seductress in Mishlei 7 (one unified warning against materialism) The reader's job: identify which type you're dealing with, and don't manufacture meaning where none was intended. PART 2 — The Rambam's Advice to the Reader Advice 1 — Read the Whole Book Carefully and Compare Chapters אם תרצה להשיג את כל מה שכללתי בו... השווה פרקיו זה עם זה Don't just understand the general idea of any single chapter. Pay attention to: Every term the Rambam uses Every phrase — even if it seems incidental to the chapter's main topic The order of chapters — even if you think a topic belongs elsewhere, the Rambam placed it where he did for a reason Constant cross-reference — comparing chapters reveals layers a single reading misses שבחיבור הזה הדברים לא באו כפי שנזדמן אלא בדקדוק גדול ובדיוק רב The book is constructed with extreme precision. Nothing is accidental. אל תקרא אותו אחר דמיונך ושיבושך Don't read it through your preconceived notions. That damages both the author and the reader. Advice 2 — The Two Oaths משביע אני בשם יתעלה כל מי שקרא חיבור זה The Rambam imposes two oaths on every reader. Oath 1 — Don't Teach What's Genuinely Original Here לא יפרש ממני אפילו מילה אחת ולא יבאר לאחרים ממני אלא מה שהוא מבואר ומפורש בדברי מי שקדם לי Only repeat to others what is already explicit in established Torah and earlier Chachamim. Don't repeat the Rambam's own chiddushim to people who aren't ready. Oath 2 — Don't Rush to Accuse ולא יחפוץ לטעון נגדי Don't be quick to attack. The Rambam writes: Maybe what you understood from my words is not what I meant You will harm me when I was trying to help you That would be repaying good with evil If you find something that helps you — say a hearty thanks to Hashem. If you find nothing useful — treat it as if the book hadn't been written. Move on. If you find something that seems wrong or problematic — give the benefit of the doubt and look for an alternative reading, even a forced one . יפרשנו שלא כפשוטו אפילו בפירוש דחוק ביותר וידינו לכף זכות This obligation is greater for a Talmid Chacham than for the average person: Average person: if it's 50/50 whether they did wrong, you're obligated to give benefit of the doubt Talmid Chacham: even if 90% of the evidence is against them, you should still give benefit of the doubt The Problem with Oath 1 Oath 1 is genuinely difficult — taken literally, it would mean we shouldn't be having this shiur. So how do we approach it? Possible Resolutions (Rav Kook and others discuss this) The Rambam himself softens it later — he is going to say in this very introduction that עת לעשות לה' הפרו תורתך — sometimes the urgent need overrides ordinary protocols of esoteric concealment Everyone before us has interpreted the Moreh — Abarbanel, Shem Tov, Crescas, and many others wrote commentaries. Either they all violated the oath, or the oath has a more nuanced reading. Halachically uncertain whether you can publish a book and then bind readers by oath retroactively The deeper point of Oath 1 — the Rambam is making a strong rhetorical statement: don't misuse this book. Don't bring it to people for whom emunah peshutah is healthier. There are people who are better served by simple faith than by the kinds of questions and answers in the Moreh. The Practical Outflow For some people, simple faith is genuinely better Bereishit at the simple level satisfies their need For perplexed people, the Moreh's depth is essential Match the audience to the level Advice 3 — Who Will Benefit From This Book The Rambam identifies three categories of reader: Beginners יודע אני שכל מתחיל מבני אדם שלא ישיג דבר מן העיון יפסיד Even beginners will gain something — though not everything. (Note: this is striking, because the Rambam emphasized earlier that the book is for those already trained in both philosophy and Torah. Yet he acknowledges that even simple readers will pick up some benefit.) The Wise and Perplexed השלם מבני אדם מאמין בתורה נבוך כמו שציינתי יפיק תועלת בכל פרקיו For those who are knowledgeable in Torah AND philosophically trained AND perplexed by the apparent tension — every chapter will be useful, and the experience will be deeply joyful. The Confused but Self-Confident אך המבולבלים שמוחם הזדהם בהשקפות מוטעות This is the most damning category — those whose minds are polluted with mistaken views, who think they are wise and philosophical but actually know nothing of philosophy or science. Better that they not touch this book. They will recoil from many chapters They won't understand the methodology And worst — if they probe deep enough, they'll discover their own shallowness, which they will resent The Rambam's Most Powerful Personal Statement אני האיש כשנלחץ הדבר וצר המקום ולא מצאתי תחבולה ללמד האמת המוכחת אלא בדרך שתאות לאיש מעלה אחד ותאות להקניט אלפים בורים — אעדיף לאומרה לאותו אחד... \"I am the man who, when faced with an unavoidable need to teach a demonstrated truth, and finding no way to do so except in a manner that satisfies one elevated person while displeasing ten thousand ignoramuses — I will say it for that one elevated person, and I will not pay heed to the criticisms of the multitude.\" ואשאף לחלץ אותו איש מעלה בודד ממה שנקלע אליו ואנחהו ממבוכתו עד שיגיע שם אל השלמות ויירגע \"I will strive to extract that one elevated person from his confusion, to lead him from his perplexity until he arrives at completeness and finds rest.\" This is the mission statement of the entire Moreh in one paragraph: better to truly help one perplexed person than to comfortably please ten thousand who don't actually want truth. If only our leaders in every walk of life would think this way — what a different world we would live in. Summary of Part 2 — The Advice # Advice 1 Read carefully — every term, every phrase, every chapter sequence is intentional 2 (Oath 1) Don't pass on the Rambam's own chiddushim to those not ready 2 (Oath 2) Don't rush to attack — give benefit of the doubt, find alternative readings if needed 3 The book benefits beginners (some), the wise-and-perplexed (greatly), but harms the confused-and-arrogant 4 The mission: rescue one perplexed person rather than comfort ten thousand confused ones PART 3 — The Seven Reasons for Contradictions in Books Why This Final Introduction? The Rambam knows readers will encounter what appear to be contradictions in the Moreh. Rather than let readers conclude he was sloppy or self-contradictory, he prepares them in advance: Books in general can contain contradictions for seven distinct reasons He will list all seven Then he will tell us which reasons apply to the Moreh (it turns out, only #5 and #7) So if you find an apparent contradiction in the Moreh, you'll know to interpret it through reasons 5 or 7 — not the others. Reason 1 — Compilation Without Attribution שמחבר אסף אמירות של אנשים בעלי דעות שונות והשמיט שם האומר The author collected statements from different people with conflicting views — but didn't say who said what. Result: the book contains contradictions because page 5 cites Rabbi A and page 65 cites Rabbi B, but neither attribution is preserved. Classic example: the Mishna. We know each statement reflects a different opinion, but to a casual reader unfamiliar with the rabbis' positions, the contradictions seem unaccountable. One of the rabbanim used to say: keeping this principle in mind when learning Shulchan Aruch resolves a great many of your questions. Reason 2 — The Author Changed His Mind כאשר בעל הספר הזה היתה דעה מסוימת ולאחר מכן חזר בו The author held one view when he wrote one section, and a different view when he wrote a later section. He never went back and revised the earlier material. The Rambam himself did this: The Cambridge Genizah preserves manuscripts of the Peirush HaMishnayot that he wrote in his early 20s We can see his own marginal corrections from later in life But not every author has time to retract — and many contradictions in any author's body of work come down to early-career vs. late-career thought Reason 3 — One Statement Has a Deeper Meaning כאשר לא כל הדברים האלו כחיצוניותם אלא חלקם כחיצוניותם וחלקם משל שיש לו תוך Two statements appear to contradict, but one (or both) has a deeper meaning. They only seem contradictory at the surface level. Example A — Seeing Hashem Moshe: לא יראני האדם וחי — no human can see Me and live (Shemot) Yeshayahu: ואראה את ה' יושב על כסא רם ונשא — I saw Hashem sitting on a throne, high and exalted (Yeshayah 6) At surface level: direct contradiction. Resolution: clearly one or both is not literal — Yeshayahu's \"vision\" is a prophetic experience, not physical sight. Example B — Hashem as a Man? Beshalach: ה' איש מלחמה ה' שמו — Hashem is a man of war Balak: לא איש אל ויכזב — God is not a man Resolution: \"Man\" in Beshalach is metaphorical (a warrior); the literal sense is denied in Balak. One is mashal, the other is straight statement. Reason 4 — A Hidden Caveat / Qualification שיש תנאי שאינו מפורש במקומו Two statements look contradictory because there's an unstated condition or context that distinguishes them. Example — Answering a Fool The Gemara cites two verses from Shlomo: אל תען כסיל כאיולתו — do not answer a fool according to his folly ענה כסיל כאיולתו — answer a fool according to his folly Resolution from the Gemara (Shabbat): הא בדברי תורה — when it concerns Torah matters, answer (you can't let foolish ideas about Torah pass) הא במילי דעלמא — when it concerns mundane matters, ignore The contradiction dissolves once the unstated condition is supplied. Reasons 5, 6, 7 — Coming Next Week These are more complex and require further development. We'll cover them next time, then see the Rambam's examples for each, and finally see his identification of which reasons apply to the Moreh itself (only #5 and #7). Where We Stand We have completed: The two types of meshalim (Part 1) The advice to the reader (Part 2) Reasons 1–4 for contradictions in books (Part 3, partial) We have remaining: Reasons 5, 6, 7 Examples of each The Rambam's identification of which apply to the Moreh And then — finally — the actual Chapter 1 of the Moreh itself Coming Up Next Next class (first half): Reasons 5, 6, 7 for contradictions, examples, and the Rambam's classification of the Moreh's contradictions Following that: Chapter 1 of the Moreh itself — the actual discussion of specific terms in Tanakh begins. We start unpacking the multi-meaning words and metaphors that this entire introduction has been preparing us to read. B'Ezrat Hashem","RSVrOo-cS2s":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 7 (ענוה — Humility) — Part 3 Edition: Feldheim This week: The 12 mitzvot designed to instill humility + the beginning of philosophical analysis (Method 3) Recap: Chapter 7 So Far The Definition (Part 1) Humility = recognizing one's true status, not exaggerating it (up or down) Two dimensions: internal and external Two relationships: vis-à-vis Hakadosh Baruch Hu and vis-à-vis other people Humility ≠ self-debasement Three Methods of Training Toward Humility Personal/experiential — reflecting on what causes arrogance, surrounding oneself with humble people (covered Part 1) Through the Torah — two sub-methods: 2a. Stories in Tanakh of humble vs. arrogant figures (covered Parts 1 & 2) 2b. Mitzvot in the Torah designed to cultivate humility (this week) Philosophical analysis (begins this week) Last Week's Tour of Tanakh Korach, Datan & Aviram, Nadav & Avihu, Shaul, Doeg, Achitofel, Avshalom, Yoav, Adoniyah, Rechavam, Yeravam, Uziyahu, Chizkiyahu — every figure shows that greatness offers no protection from the fall of arrogance. Opening Discussion: What Mitzvot Train Us in Humility? Before reading Rabbi Avraham's list, the class brainstormed possibilities. Several excellent suggestions came up — none of which Rabbi Avraham himself lists, but all valid: Tzedakah — recognizing that everything comes from Hashem Kibbud Av Va'Em — they brought you into the world (more accurately hakarat hatov) Mahatzit HaShekel — equal contribution rich and poor (though primarily for the census) Removing the deshen — the kohen performing menial-seeming tasks Lo tikom v'lo titor (no revenge, no grudges) — recognizing Hashem is the arbiter, not you (Rambam mentions this elsewhere in Sefer HaMitzvot) Rabbi Avraham's important caveat: a single mitzvah can carry multiple training purposes . He brought \"loving the ger\" earlier (Chapter 4) under the heading of compassion . Here he brings it again under humility . Same mitzvah, multiple functions. The 12 Mitzvot Designed to Instill Humility Mitzvah 1 — Loving the Ger (Convert/Stranger) כי גרים הייתם בארץ מצרים The Torah commands love and kindness toward the ger The given reason: you were once strangers in Egypt Even the prohibition not to despise an Egyptian ger — though Egyptians enslaved us — applies, because you were once a stranger in his land The training: remember the lowliness of being an outsider , and never let your heart become haughty when you are the insider Mitzvah 2 — Remembering Egyptian Bondage וזכרת כי עבד היית במצרים Beyond the basic reason (to keep the Torah out of gratitude), this remembrance directly cultivates humility Rabbi Sacks z\"l's framing: psychology shows the abused often become abusers. The Torah commands us repeatedly: remember Egypt — and do not repeat it. Don't perpetuate what you suffered. Mitzvah 3 — Land Cannot Be Sold in Perpetuity (This Week's Parsha — Behar) כי גרים ותושבים אתם עמדי Land returns to its ancestral owner in the Yovel Buy in the second year of the cycle → 48 years; buy in year 40 → 9 years; either way, the land returns The reason: you are guests on My land Counters the arrogance of wealth: even your property is not permanently yours. You don't have carte blanche just because you have means. Mitzvah 4 — Bikurim and the Bikurim Declaration ולקחת מראשית כל פרי האדמה... The first fruits, brought in a basket, given personally to the Kohen The Mishna's striking detail: even King Agrippas carried the basket on his shoulder all the way to the Azarah The verse confirms this: ולקח הכהן הטנא מידך — the Kohen takes the basket from your hand The humility encoded: whoever you are — even the king — you must come yourself, subdue yourself before the Kohen, and acknowledge dependence on Hashem Mitzvah 5 — The Bikurim Declaration & Bowing The text of the declaration is entirely about bondage in Egypt, redemption, and gratitude for the land The Torah commands bowing: והשתחוית לפני ה' אלוקיך Why specifically when bringing first fruits? Because of the warning in Eikev: פן תאכל ושבעת ובתים טובים תבנה... ורם לבבך ושכחת את ה' אלהיך The natural human pattern: poverty makes us turn to Hashem; abundance makes us forget Him Therefore, specifically at the moment of abundance (first fruits, full harvest), the Torah inserts an act of bowing and gratitude. The training is precisely targeted at the moment of greatest temptation toward arrogance. Mitzvah 6 — General Confession (Vidui) והתודו את חטאתם אשר עשו Confession of sin is a Torah mitzvah — both the Yom Kippur vidui and the daily/private confession Rabbi Avraham cites: והתודו את עונם... אז יכנע לבבם הערל ואז ירצו את עונם — then their blocked heart will be subdued, and then their iniquity will be atoned The mechanism: the act of confession is itself an act of self-subjugation. It directly produces hachna'ah. Mitzvah 7 — The Banishment of the Impure from the Camp וישלחו מן המחנה כל צרוע וכל זב וכל טמא לנפש Different levels of impurity, different distances of removal from the camp Most striking case: the metzora (one with tzaraat) Clothes torn, head uncovered, mouth covered, called \"tamei tamei\" Banished from all three camps: בדד ישב מחוץ למחנה מושבו Treated like a mourner Why the Metzora Is Treated Like a Mourner A beautiful idea (not from Rambam himself): What distinguishes a human from an animal is koach hadibbur (power of speech) — ויהי האדם לנפש חיה → רוח ממללא When a person corrupts speech (lashon hara), they have, in a sense, \"died\" as a human — losing their distinguishing identity They mourn for their lost humanity until they are healed The whole experience is one prolonged training in humility Mitzvah 8 — Standing for Elders and Honoring Sages מפני שיבה תקום והדרת פני זקן Direct cultivation of derech eretz, hachna'ah, and humility The act of standing for someone older or wiser physically expresses an inward acknowledgment Mitzvah 9 — No Yeast or Honey on the Mizbe'ach כי כל שאור וכל דבש לא תקטירו ממנו אשה לה' A more drush-style explanation: yeast rises, honey froths These represent expansion beyond one's true measure — the very symbol of arrogance The Mizbe'ach exists, per the Ramban, so we contemplate that this should be us — the sacrifice substitutes for the self Therefore, anything that symbolizes self-inflation is banned from the altar Mitzvah 10 — The Prohibition of Matzevah לא תקים לך מצבה אשר שנא ה' אלוהיך A pillar to be worshipped — physically elevating an object Rabbi Avraham reads this as part of the same theme: the rejection of raising up in any form Mitzvah 11 — The Prohibition of Even Maskit An even maskit (debated among Rishonim — Rambam: a stone placed before an idol for prostration; others: a stone floor) Rabbi Avraham's reading: it is fundamentally an act of elevating oneself (above the natural ground) for the purpose of worship The contrast verse: ממעמקים קראתיך ה' — from the depths I called You — true prayer comes from lowliness, not elevation Practical Halachic Outflow The Gemara derives from this: one must not pray standing on an elevated place (Shulchan Aruch, OC 90): המתפלל לא יעמוד גבי מיטה ולא גבי כיסא ולא גבי ספסל ולא גבי מקום גבוה Exceptions: the chazan on the bimah (representing the tzibbur), or someone ill The principle: prayer arises from the depths Mitzvah 12 — The King's Sefer Torah והיה כשבתו על כיסא ממלכתו... וכתב לו את משנה התורה The king must write his own personal Sefer Torah and read from it constantly Two stated reasons: To learn yirat Hashem and observe the mitzvot לבלתי רום לבבו מאחיו — that his heart not be lifted above his brothers The king is most at risk of arrogance because of his power → therefore he is given the most intensive antidote : constant Torah study designed specifically to humble him The reward: למען יאריך ימים — long kingship for him and his descendants The contrast: every king we saw last week who fell — fell because they did not internalize this Closing Verse ידרך ענוים במשפט וילמד ענוים דרכו Hashem leads the humble on His path The path of humility = the path of Hashem. The 12 mitzvot above (and many others) are not random — they are the structured curriculum by which Hashem trains us to walk His path. Quick-Reference Table — The 12 Mitzvot # Mitzvah Mechanism 1 Love the ger Remember being the outsider 2 Remember Egyptian bondage Don't repeat the abuse you suffered 3 Land returns in Yovel You are a guest, even on your own land 4 Bikurim (bringing) Even the king carries the basket 5 Bikurim declaration & bowing Targets the moment of abundance 6 Vidui Direct self-subjugation 7 Banishment of the impure (esp. metzora) Imposed isolation forces self-reflection 8 Standing for elders Daily physical expression of submission 9 No yeast or honey on Mizbe'ach Reject symbols of self-inflation 10 No matzevah Reject objects of elevation 11 No even maskit Reject elevation in worship 12 King's Sefer Torah Most-at-risk receives most intensive antidote Method 3: Philosophical / Intellectual Analysis The third method of training begins now. Instead of stories or mitzvot, this method works through reasoning: what could a person possibly be arrogant about, and why does close analysis dissolve each potential basis for that arrogance? The structure: Rabbi Avraham splits potential bases for arrogance into three categories , with each one analytically dismantled. Purely physical things (food, drink, sex, health) Material things with a metaphysical edge (wealth, power, authority) Genuinely spiritual things (intellect, character, even prophecy) The Foundational Premise — Chomer and Tzura Every human is composed of: Chomer — matter (the physical, corporal) Tzura — form (the spiritual, the meaning-seeking element) As Rabbi Sacks framed it: humans are \"the mammals who look for meaning in life.\" We are mammals AND we are searching beings. All our characteristics emanate from one or the other. The analysis below works through what each domain can — and cannot — offer as legitimate grounds for pride. Category 1 — Purely Physical Things Why There Is Nothing to Boast About In the physical realm, there is no difference between a human and the lowest mammal We share with all animals: eating, drinking, reproducing, sleeping, falling ill, recovering Rabbi Avraham (echoing the Rambam in The Guide III:8): the sense of touch is the most embarrassing of the senses , because it is the most shared with animals Why would you take pride in something animals do exactly the same way? What About Health? A person says: \"I am healthy and physically perfect.\" Reality check: physical health is exquisitely fragile A change of climate A bit too much or too little food A minor change → everything turns We see this constantly. People who are healthy one moment are gravely ill the next. What is there to be proud of in something so easily lost? What About Indulgence? A person says: \"I have all the food I want, all the drink, every desire fulfilled.\" Counter-argument: the very same desires you think you have mastered are the source of the greatest suffering Examples Gluttony → Rambam in Hilchot De'ot: 99 of 100 illnesses come from overeating. The very thing that gives life takes it Indigestion and revulsion → the pasuk: כל אכל תתעב נפשם — when one is ill, all food becomes repulsive Excessive intercourse → the medicine of those days held this leads to weakness and disease (and the underlying point — that overindulgence in any drive damages the body — remains valid) The Galen Principle כי גידולו ריבויו וחיותו של היצור חי הם עילת התמעטותו ניבונו ומיתתו The Greek physician Galen and others: the very things that produce growth and life are also the causes of decline and death. Extremity is destruction. Conclusion of Category 1 How can a person take pride in a state that he himself is causing to be undone ? Pride in physical things is pride in something that, by its nature, contains the seeds of its own dismantling. Category 2 — Material Things with a Metaphysical Edge These are things that depend on the physical but are not purely physical — they are enabled by matter but exist as goods in a different sense : wealth, power, authority, ambition, position. What People Take Pride In Wealth, beautiful clothes, beautiful homes, power, rulership, leadership, the satisfaction of ambition David captured this attitude: ויבטח ברוב עשרו יעוז בהוותו — he trusted in his great wealth, was emboldened by his power Why This Too Is Hollow 1. These Things Generate Strife Money and power → jealousy, hatred, enemies, rivals, anxiety The constant fear, vigilance, weight of responsibility, competition Source Teaching Pirkei Avot מרבה נכסים מרבה דאגה — more property, more worry Kohelet עושר שמור לבעליו לרעתו — wealth kept for its owner to his detriment Avot הוי קבל וקיים — be obscure and you'll endure (Rashi: ofel — the more obscure, the more permanent) Mishlei טוב פת חרבה ושלוה בה מבית מלא זבחי ריב — better dry bread with serenity than a house full of feasting and quarrels 2. The Lived Reality How often do we see: Wealth as the cause of family rifts? Inheritance fights only happen in families where there is enough to fight over. A small inheritance generates no court cases — the more there is, the worse the fighting. People injured or killed because of their money? Wealth as a magnet for trouble? The Test Case — Kings התבונן על מצב המלכים שהם יותר נכבדים מבני אדם ויותר עשירים... Kings have: The most honor The most wealth The most fulfillment of physical desire The most arrogance But they also have: The most need for security and protection The greatest internal suffering — anxiety has no limit at their level Constant entanglement in wars And their endings: most kings do not die natural deaths — killed in war, assassinated openly with the sword, killed secretly through cunning, or destroyed early through illness brought on by their own indulgence in food, drink, and excess Conclusion of Category 2 How can a person take pride in things that: Are temporal — gone in a moment Generate suffering proportional to their amount Lead to violent and premature death in their fullest expression? Important Caveat Rabbi Avraham is not saying don't make money or be ambitious. That would be against Torah hashkafa. The point is the difference between comfortable provision and amassment-as-identity . The arrogance and false security in wealth is the target — not honest pursuit of livelihood. The Outlier — David HaMelech David is the rare example of a king who had everything yet did not fall: Trait Source Inner humility ולבי חלל בקרבי Hung around righteous people חבר אני לכל אשר יראוך Despised the arrogant גבה עינים ורחב לבב אתו לא אוכל Praised the humble Mizmor 15 — מי יגור באהלך... Talked of the reward of the humble וענוים ירשו ארץ והתענגו על רב שלום Two readings of נבזה בעיניו נמאס in Mizmor 15: Rashi & others: those whom Hashem rejects (arrogant) → the humble person rejects them Rabbeinu Bechaye: in his own eyes he feels lowly — recognizes his own deficiencies David is the proof that one can hold power and wealth without arrogance — but it requires extraordinary inner work. Category 3 — Spiritual / Intellectual Excellence This is the most dangerous category — and the one most likely to trip up the person who has worked hard on themselves. A person says: \"Fine, I accept I'm not better than anyone in physical things, and money/power are vanities. But I am genuinely wiser. I have worked harder on my character. Surely that is grounds for pride? \" Rabbi Avraham's answer is in two layers. Layer 1 — Compared to Real Perfection, You Are Nothing Whatever level you have reached — even the highest — is כאין וכאפס (as nothing and naught) compared to true perfection. זאת מפני המסך של החומר האנושי המעכב בעד השגת השלמות The very fact of being human, embodied in chomer, places a screen between you and true perfection. Your highest is asymptotic to a level you can never reach. Layer 2 — Even the Highest Spiritual Achievement Is Lost to Tiny Physical Disturbances This is the powerful argument. Even prophecy — the highest perfection a human can reach — is undone by: Hunger Thirst Fatigue Sadness Anger Other physical states How can you take pride in something so dependent on physical conditions you don't fully control? Examples Cited Yaakov Avinu During the 22 years separated from Yosef , prophecy left him because of his sadness It returned only when he saw the wagons and his spirit revived: ותחי רוח יעקב אביהם — Targum: the spirit of prophecy returned to Yaakov our father Yaakov During the 20 Years with Lavan A novel suggestion of Rabbi Avraham (he doesn't seem to have a clear precedent): no prophecy is recorded during this entire period Why? Two reasons: Physical exhaustion — היתי ביום אכלני חרב וקרח בלילה ותדד שנתי מעיני (heat by day, frost by night, no sleep) Constant mental engagement with Lavan's deception — והחלפת את משכרתי עשרת מנים (you changed my wages ten times) Both physical strain and material distraction blocked prophetic capacity for two decades Yitzchak's Blessing Yitzchak insisted on tasty food and (in Yaakov's case) wine before he could bless Why? Because the soul, when channeling spiritual blessing, requires physical balance as its substrate The physical body must be in a calibrated state for spiritual transmission to occur Elisha Explicit verse: קחו לי מנגן — bring me a musician Music was used to actively summon prophetic states The Schools of Bnei HaNevi'im Schools for prophets — they trained in meditation, music, harp, drum, flute Prophecy was cultivated through deliberate physical and emotional preparation This proves the dependency: even the highest perfection requires careful physical setup Application to Intellect (Not Just Prophecy) The Chachamim said: אין השכינה שורה לא מתוך עצבות ולא מתוך עצלות The Shechina does not rest amid sadness or laziness The Gemara extends this directly: אמר רב וכן לדבר הלכה And so too for matters of halacha You cannot reach depth in Torah when: You are sad You are hungry You are physically uncomfortable You are fatigued The same dependency on physical conditions that limits prophecy limits intellectual penetration. The Final Move — Moshe Rabbeinu The strongest example. Moshe was the greatest prophet who ever lived. What stopped him from reaching even higher? כי לא יראני האדם וחי Hashem says: while you are human, you cannot fully attain Me. The very fact of being embodied placed a ceiling. And what does the Torah say about Moshe? ענו מאד מכל האדם אשר על פני האדמה The most humble human ever to live. The Paradox at the Heart of This The greater you become, the more humble you become — because: You see how high you've reached AND You see how easily it can be lost AND You see the infinite gap between your high and the true peak This is why the most arrogant people are usually those with the least to be arrogant about. The truly accomplished see the ceiling above them, the gap between achievement and perfection, and the fragility of their position. The unaccomplished see only the people below them. Conclusion התבוננות אמיתית מחייבת שלא להתגאות בשום עניין מענייני העולם הזה True analytical reflection requires us to take pride in nothing in this world — neither the purely physical, nor the materially-enabled, nor even the genuinely spiritual. The Throughline of This Shiur We've now covered Method 2b (mitzvot) and the opening of Method 3 (philosophical analysis): The mitzvot train humility through structured action: love of the stranger, Yovel, bikurim, vidui, banishment, standing for elders, the Mizbe'ach, the king's Sefer Torah The philosophical analysis dissolves each potential basis for arrogance: Physical things → identical to animals, fragile, self-undermining Material/positional things → temporal, strife-generating, lethal at their height Spiritual/intellectual things → dependent on physical conditions, asymptotic to true perfection, yielding paradoxically MORE humility the higher one rises The deepest insight: Moshe was the most humble because he was the greatest. The closer you come to the truth, the more visible the gap becomes. Coming Up Next Continued philosophical analysis — Rabbi Avraham continues this theme, then discusses celestial bodies and their (Rambam's view) intellectual nature Religious arrogance — the special case of pride in religious accomplishment Humility vis-à-vis other humans and vis-à-vis Hakadosh Baruch Hu — the two relationships in their fullness This will likely take another shiur and a half before we conclude Chapter 7 B'Ezrat Hashem","V8Zq8xnXAWM":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Topic: מלאכת בורר — Selecting and Sorting Siman: שיט (319) Seifim covered tonight: Seif 4 (main focus), Seif 5, Seif 8 Source text: Shulchan Aruch with Bi'ur Halacha and Mishna Berura Recap: What We've Covered in Borer So Far The Three Selecting Melachot Borer — selecting by hand Meraked — sifting through a sieve Zoreh — winnowing with wind The Three Conditions for Permitted Selection on Shabbat Le'altar / Samuch L'Seudah — for immediate use (a few minutes, very close proximity) B'yad — using your hand (cutlery is considered an extended hand; some Poskim permit even a peeler) Ochel from Pesoleth — taking the wanted from the unwanted, never the reverse Key Principles Established Previously Two edible foods: Borer applies even when both items are edible if you only want one now (Rama, accepted halacha) Borer = selecting AND sorting (even sorting for later use, where you take neither now) Definition of a mixture (ta'arovet) — size, color, type all factor in Gidulei Karka: Borer applies to all things, not only items that grow from the ground Removing top items to reach lower items — permissible Silverware on Shabbat — permissible (combination of four reasons) Seif 4 — The Core Halacha The Text of the Shulchan Aruch הבורר פסולת מתוך אוכל אפילו בידו אחת חייב Selecting waste from edible food — even with one hand alone — you are liable. Practical Application Bowl of shelled pistachios with some shells left in by the person who shelled them A classical mixture of ochel (pistachios) and pesoleth (shells) Even with one hand, even using a hand only, even for immediate use — you are chayav for taking out the shells The third condition is absolute: you must take the food from the waste, not the waste from the food The Question of Two Edible Items Pistachios + peanuts, only want pistachios now Shulchan Aruch and Rambam are equivocal Halacha: the unwanted is treated as a kind of \"pesoleth\" — you must take what you want, not remove what you don't want The Rama's Addition ואפילו האוכל מרובה ויש יותר טורח בברירת האוכל אפילו הכי לא יברור הפסולת אפילו כדי לאכול לאלתר Why the Rama Emphasizes This Even if there is more food than waste and it would be far more bothersome to remove the food Even if removing 10 shells from a bowl of nuts is much easier than picking out all the nuts Still forbidden on Shabbat to remove the pesoleth The Contrast with Yom Tov Shabbat Yom Tov Default rule Always take ochel from pesoleth Reduce tircha (bother) More food than waste Take out all the food Take out the waste (less bothersome) Practical No tircha consideration You should follow the easier path Important nuance from Robert's question: On Yom Tov, removing the easier path is not just permitted but the proper way — you should be ma'amit tircha (reducing bother), not just permitted to do so. The Bi'ur Halacha — Two Major Questions Question 1: Selection at the Moment of Eating (Derech Achila) The principle: derech achila (the way of eating) is what allows the three conditions of selection in the first place. Universally agreed: Selecting in the mouth is fine. Fish or chicken with bones in your mouth → take out the bone in your mouth → permitted (it's during the act of eating) The question: What about taking out the waste literally a second before eating ? Not \"le'altar\" in the few-minutes sense Not the standard immediate proximity we've been discussing The actual moment of bringing the food to your mouth — you pluck out the bone The Machloket of the Acharonim Position Posek Reasoning Permitted Maharit Abulafia (R' Yitzchak Abulafia, Tzfat, 1600s) This is derech achila — an extension of selection in the mouth Forbidden Maharitatz (R' Yom Tov Tzahalon, Tzfat, 1600s) Only in the mouth itself counts; before is still borer The Birkei Yosef (Chida, ~250 years ago) brings this dispute. The Bi'ur Halacha later argues this is actually a Rishonim machloket — Ramban with the lenient view, Rosh with the stricter view. Halacha L'Maaseh Both Sefardi and Ashkenazi poskim: generally do not rely on this alone It can be used as an additional reason for leniency (snif l'heter) when combined with other factors Don't take out waste from the good even just before eating, based purely on this principle Question 2: A Fly in the Soup — Three Solutions Setup: a fly fell into your soup or tea. You don't want it. Can you take it out? Solution 1 — Maharitatz (relying on derech achila) Just before drinking, take out the fly Rejected — we don't take this approach as a primary heter Solution 2 — Ein Borer B'Lach (the major Sephardi principle) There is no borer when a solid is mixed with a liquid. Logic: Borer requires a ta'arovet (mixture). When a solid sits in liquid and is clearly discernible , it is not considered mixed with the entire body of liquid Maharitatz cites the pasuk: וגם את הצרעה ישלח — also, He will send away the wasp (Eikev). You may \"send away\" the fly. Limitation: Only works for one, two, or three discernible solids. Twenty croutons in soup would be enmeshed and considered a mixture. Posek Position on Ein Borer B'Lach Maharitatz Permitted Taz (R' David Segal, ~400 years ago) Forbidden — still a mixture Chacham Ovadia Permitted Ben Tzion Abba Shaul Permitted All Sefardi poskim Permitted Some Ashkenazi poskim Permitted (others follow Taz) Solution 3 — Take the Fly with Surrounding Liquid (Universal) Everyone agrees this is permitted. Use a spoon and lift the fly out together with some surrounding soup Logic: The mixture is not the fly with the entire bowl — it is the fly with a small area of soup around it By removing the entire mixture (fly + immediate surrounding liquid), you are not taking pesoleth from ochel; you are removing the whole ta'arovet Works only when there are few separate items — not many enmeshed items Robert's Question: Oil Floating on Soup Removing the oil with the top layer of soup → certainly fine (entire mixture removed) Removing the oil alone using \"ein borer b'lach\" logic → probably not — the principle applies to solid + liquid, not two liquids, even if they don't physically blend Rabbi Kada to confirm and follow up Summary Table — Solutions for Fly in Soup Approach Status When Applicable Take it out alone (derech achila reason) Not relied upon — Take it out alone (ein borer b'lach) Permitted for Sefardim, machloket for Ashkenazim One/few discernible solids in liquid Take it out with surrounding liquid Universally permitted Few separate items in liquid The Major Principle — Bones in Fish & Chicken (Guf Echad) The Problem Big fish at a sheva brachot, salmon with bones, chicken with bones, watermelon with seeds People routinely remove the spine of the fish before serving All the rules above seem to forbid this The Ma'amar Mordechai's Stringent Position R' Mordechai Karmi (Turkey, ~300 years ago): Removing the spine of a fish to serve guests = boreir pesoleth from ochel = chayav (Torah liability) Even taking out a single bone before eating = forbidden (he holds like Maharitatz, not Abulafia) His allowance: Take out a bone and suck it — by deriving hana'ah from it, you make it ochel Otherwise, leave the bones in and let each person take their own fish from the bones The Bi'ur Halacha's Difficulty He cannot understand why the world is not careful about this Especially when food is being prepared in the kitchen 5–10 minutes before eating, even there bones are routinely removed \" אמרתי לחפש להם זכות \" — I sought to find a merit for the practice The Bi'ur Halacha's Major Heter — Guf Echad והוא משום דעצמות והבשר הם חיבור אחד Bones and flesh are one composition. The fish with its bones, the chicken with its bones — this is how they are made They were not man-mixed ; they are a single naturally-formed unit He cites the Magen Avraham in the name of the Maharshal: the same logic for nuts and shells — \"מין אחד הוא\" (it is one entity) Therefore, removing the bones is תיקון אוכל בעלמא — merely the rectification/preparation of food Not an act of borer at all — borer applies to mixtures , and a guf echad is not a mixture The Mishna Berura's Position Crucially , two seifim later (in Seif 6's Mishna Berura), he brings this not just as a justification of the practice but as a fully accepted opinion: דדבר שגדל עם הקליפה ומתקנו כך אוכל הוא ולא שייך בורר בזה This is why even some Ashkenazi poskim adopt this principle. Sefardim (Ben Ish Chai, Rav Ovadia, Rav Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul) all rule that this is a valid heter. The Critical Distinction Permitted (Guf Echad) Forbidden (Man-Made Mixture) Removing bones from intact fish/chicken After the meal, sorting separated bones from chicken pieces Removing watermelon seeds from a slice Mixed bones with mixed chicken pieces on a serving plate Pulling stem off a tomato A bowl of separated nuts and shells Peeling a cucumber — The moment items are separated and remixed , it becomes a regular mixture and the regular borer rules apply. Five Heterim for Removing Bones from Fish/Chicken A practical summary of all the options: Take the fish from the bone (rather than the bone from the fish) — always permitted in close proximity Take out the bone and suck it — turning it into ochel through hana'ah (Ma'amar Mordechai) Edible/soft bones — already considered ochel themselves Take out bones immediately as you eat — Maharit Abulafia / potentially Ramban (snif l'heter only) Guf Echad — this is one composition, not a mixture; removing bones is tikun ochel and not borer at all (Bi'ur Halacha, accepted Sephardi practice) Practical Range of Guf Echad Applications Bones from fish and chicken Seeds from watermelon Stems from tomatoes Peel from cucumbers Any natural composition where the unwanted is part of the same naturally-grown unit as the wanted As long as you are doing it samuch l'se'udah (close to the meal), it is tikun ochel be'alma and permitted. Seif 5 — The Turmosin Beans (The Reverse Case) The Text of the Shulchan Aruch הבורר תורמוסין מתוך פסולת שלהם חייב Selecting turmosin (lupine beans) from their shells — chayav. This appears to be the opposite of everything we have learned: he removes beans (the food) from the shells and is liable. Why? The Rambam's Explanation מפני שהפסולת שלהם ממתקת אותם כששלקו אותן עמהם ונמצא כבורר פסולת מתוך אוכל The unique feature of turmosin beans: They must be cooked seven times, with the water changed each time, to become edible The beans themselves are intensely bitter During the cooking process, the shells have some sweetness while the beans are still bitter Relatively speaking, at this stage the shells are more \"ochel\" and the beans are more \"pesoleth\" Therefore taking the bitter beans from the sweeter shells is borer pesoleth from ochel → chayav The Concept of \"Relative Ochel and Pesoleth\" The whole framework of borer is relative to the moment, not absolute: Whatever is wanted now = ochel Whatever is unwanted now = pesoleth This even overrides what something will become later in a process If you wanted the beans (e.g., to use as game pieces), removing them would be permitted as ochel from pesoleth. The chiyuv exists specifically when the goal is to ultimately consume the shells. (Note: Rashi gives three other explanations of this Mishna, but they are difficult and not the practical halacha.) Seif 8 — Borer with a Utensil (and Psik Reishei) The Text אין שורין את הכרשינין — you may not soak certain seeds in water to separate seeds from chaff (the water functioning as a kli for separation) ולא שפין אותם ביד — and you may not rub them by hand to remove the chaff (k'borer) The Critical Allowance אבל נותנן בכברה אף על פי שנופל הפסולת דרך נקבי הכברה You may place them in a sieve-like trough — even though the chaff inevitably falls through the holes. The Question This is a classic case of psik reishei (inevitable consequence) for borer. The chaff will fall through automatically. Why is this not forbidden? The Ran's Answer The Ran reads the Mishna narrowly — that the chaff doesn't always fall through. The Major Principle (Most Rishonim and the Halacha) When you are performing a natural, normal action — not the melacha itself — the inevitable consequence does not make it forbidden. This is the principle of: The Magid Mishneh on heating water (where dirt separates as a side effect) Walking past a motion-sensor light at night Any case where the act being performed is fundamentally something else, and the borer/melacha is incidental The animal's natural feeding utensil happens to have holes. You are doing an act of feeding the animals , not an act of borer , even though selection happens automatically. Practical Applications Salt Shaker with Rice Grains People put rice in salt shakers to keep the salt loose When you shake out salt, the rice stays inside Permitted — you are doing an act of salting your food , not an act of borer The selection happens automatically through the natural action Tea Bag Removal You made your tea (subject to all the bishul rules learned previously) You want to remove the tea bag Permitted to lift it out — you are performing an act of removal/extraction Inevitably a few drops will come out — this is incidental, not the goal What is Forbidden — Squeezing Drops Out Lifting the tea bag out and holding it over the cup to drain it Letting it dangle to extract the liquid back into the cup Forbidden, at least mid'rabbanan — here you are actively using the tea bag as a separation tool The act is no longer \"extraction\" but separation of liquid from solid The Fundamental Principle When the act you are performing is fundamentally a different action — feeding, salting, extracting — and the borer is automatic and incidental, the act is permitted even if the separation is inevitable. This is distinct from cases where the apparatus itself is being used as a tool of separation (water as a kli for soaking, a hand specifically rubbing to remove chaff, a tea bag deliberately positioned to drain). Comprehensive Summary Seif 4 — Core Rules Cannot take pesoleth from ochel, even with one hand Even when more food than waste, even when more bothersome — Shabbat unlike Yom Tov Even just before eating (derech achila) — not relied upon as sole heter Solid in liquid (ein borer b'lach) — permitted for Sefardim Take the entire small mixture (fly + spoonful) — universally permitted Guf Echad — removing bones, seeds, stems, peels from naturally-formed compositions is tikun ochel, not borer Seif 5 — Turmosin (The Exception That Proves the Rule) Borer is relative to current circumstances Even removing what will eventually be the food can be borer if it is currently the pesoleth (the bitter beans during cooking) Seif 8 — Inevitable Selection in Natural Acts Acts whose primary purpose is not borer are permitted even if selection is inevitable Sieve trough for animals, salt shaker with rice, tea bag removal Forbidden to actively use the apparatus to separate (draining the tea bag) Coming Up Next Seifim 6 and 7 — Zoreh (winnowing): the laws of separating with wind Seif 9 onwards — moving from Borer into Meraked (sieving) Different levels of sieving for water, wine, and other liquids The de'oraita / de'rabbanan distinction in using sieves and water as separation tools Shabbat Shalom","fEwB4p3H4CA":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: The Rambam's Introduction (continued) Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Recap: What We've Covered So Far Shiur 1 (Pre-Pesach): General Introduction Rambam's biography and the context in which the Moreh was written Overview of the work — three parts, spanning years of study Shiur 2: The Rambam's Introduction Part 1 Why Rambam wrote the book: Originally individual essays sent to his student Rabbi Yosef, who had to leave. Rambam felt bereft and wanted to pass on these ideas in writing. The purpose of the book: To explain terms in Tanakh that are multi-meaning, metaphorical, ambiguous, or veiled — giving the educated reader tools to understand what the nevi'im intended. Who the book is for: A person educated in Torah AND philosophy, of good character, who is perplexed by the tension between philosophical truths and the plain meaning of Torah verses. The two extremes to avoid: (1) Neglecting your intellect and philosophical understanding, which makes you constantly upset. (2) Neglecting the Torah and foundations of faith. The Moreh tries to balance and reconcile the two. The caveat: Rambam cannot explain everything exhaustively — these topics touch on Ma'aseh Bereishit (natural science / physics) and Ma'aseh Merkavah (theology / metaphysics), which the Chachamim say you only teach in headlines (rashei perakim). The lightning analogy: Prophetic experiences are like flashes of lightning in a dark night. Moshe had constant illumination. Most prophets had flashes — sometimes more, sometimes less. The best we can hope for in our generation (without prophecy) is a faint shine, like the glint of a blade. The more you develop character and intellect, the more shine you receive. This Evening's Shiur Rambam continues his introduction, explaining three major ideas: Why deep truths cannot be conveyed directly — even by those who have attained them Why the Torah begins with Bereishit — a fascinating alternative to Rashi's famous answer The nature and importance of meshalim (parables/analogies) — with several beautiful Midrashim Part 1: Why Deep Truths Cannot Be Fully Expressed Even the one who attains truth struggles to convey it דע שמי שירצה אחד מן השלמים לפי דרגת שלמותו להביע דבר מה שבין מאותם הסודות... לא יוכל להבהיר בשלמות כסדר אפילו את המידה שהשיג A person who has attained deep understanding — whether through prophecy, illumination, or intellect — cannot fully express what they've understood , whether verbally or in writing. This is not a failure of effort. It is the nature of these truths. The flickering quality of understanding Even when teaching others, a person will find that sometimes they can explain more fully, sometimes less, and sometimes not at all. Understanding is like tuning a radio — even prophets had to \"tune in.\" Sometimes they needed external aids (music, meditation) to access prophetic states. Chaim Vital wrote in his Sefer HaChezyonot that for two years (ages 23–25) he could not study Torah at all. Illumination comes and goes — across years, months, days, even hours. Therefore, what you understood on day one you may not be able to express on day two. The necessity of meshalim לכן כאשר כיוון כל חכם אלוהי רבני... לא דיבר בהם בשמם המפורשות Because these truths resist direct expression, any rav or chacham who attains deep truth is almost forced to convey it through analogy and metaphor. Think of a sunset, a mountaintop experience, the feeling of being in love — how can you put these into words? You can only approximate through comparison and imagery. This is why the nevi'im, the Torah, and the Chachamim all use meshalim. It's not a literary choice — it's a necessity imposed by the nature of the subject matter. Part 2: Why the Torah Begins with Bereishit The Rambam's answer vs. Rashi's answer Rashi's famous question and answer (from Rabbi Yitzchak): Why does the Torah begin with Bereishit instead of the first mitzvah? Answer: So the nations cannot accuse Israel of stealing the land — כוח מעשיו הגיד לעמו לתת להם נחלת גויים. God created the world and gave each part to whom He chose. Rambam gives a completely different answer: הלא תראה שכאשר רצה השם יתברך להשלימנו ולתקן מצבי חברתנו במצוות המעשיות... The logic runs as follows: Hashem gave us the Torah and mitzvot for our good — to make us the best people we can be. But mitzvot cannot be performed properly without understanding who we are doing them for. You can't just perform mitzvot like a robot — shake the lulav, sit in the sukkah, blow the shofar — without understanding why, what, and who for. Understanding God requires the study of theology/metaphysics (חכמת האלוהות). But metaphysics can only be understood after the study of physics/natural science (חכמת הטבע) — physics borders on and precedes metaphysics in the order of learning. Therefore the Torah begins with Bereishit — which is natural science — because that is the first rung on the ladder. The progression: Physics → Metaphysics → Understanding God → Performing mitzvot with full recognition of Hakadosh Baruch Hu. Bereishit contains deep secrets ומשום גודל העניין ונכבדותו... סתם לך הכתוב בראשית ברא אלוהים Bereishit is the study of science — Rambam says this unambiguously. But it contains within it deep secrets that Hakadosh Baruch Hu couched in very simple terms. A child of four or five reads בראשית ברא אלוהים את השמים ואת הארץ — in the beginning God created heaven and earth — and understands the simple meaning. But clearly there is something far deeper. The simple reading is for children and simple people. There are multiple layers. The Chachamim in the Midrash say: להגיד כוח מעשה בראשית לבשר ודם אי אפשר — it's not possible to tell flesh and blood the full power of creation. Shlomo's observation אמרתי אחכמה והיא רחוקה ממני Creation happened a very long time ago — רחוק מה שהיה — and is profoundly deep — ועמוק עמוק מי ימצאנו Multi-layered language is deliberate בכל זה נעשה שימוש במונחים רב משמעיים כדי שההמון יבינו אותם כפי כוח הבנתם The Torah deliberately uses terms and phrases that are multi-layered — so the masses understand them simply, and the more developed person plumbs the deeper meaning. This is what the Chachamim mean by שבעים פנים לתורה — the Torah deliberately allows multiple interpretations so each person can access it at their level. Yom Echad, Yom Sheni, Yom Shlishi — for the simple person, day one, day two, day three. But clearly there is something far deeper. Part 3: Rambam's Abandoned Projects The book on prophecy and the book of correlation In his Peirush HaMishnayot (Sanhedrin), as a young man, Rambam announced he would later write: A book explaining the concepts of prophecy A \"book of correlation\" (ספר ההתאמה) — explaining all the difficult drashot of Chazal whose surface meaning seems far from truth Why he abandoned them לא היה טוב בעינינו מה שהתחלנו לבאר בדרך הזאת He was stuck either way (מנא נפשך): Option A: Fully expose the deeper meaning of the drashot → but this violates the principle that these truths are deliberately cloaked. The Chachamim and nevi'im intentionally used meshalim to veil them. Option B: Explain the mashal by giving another mashal → but what's the point of replacing the Chachamim's mashal with his own? Either way he couldn't win — so he abandoned the project. He settled for explaining the basics of faith in the Mishneh Torah instead. Part 4: Three Types of People and How They Read Drashot Type 1: The Ignoramus (בור) Reads statements of the Chachamim that seem to defy reality — e.g., the stories in Bava Batra about Rabbah bar bar Chanah seeing a fish the size of an island, geese dripping with oil, etc. Accepts everything literally because they don't understand natural science — the impossible seems possible to them. This includes people who know all the halachot and are called great rabbanim, but will accept the most impossible things as literally true because they don't want to think the Chachamim were wrong. Rambam's position: the impossible does not happen. You cannot accept ridiculous ideas at face value. Type 2: The intellectual who rejects Reads the same statements and says: \"This is ridiculous. The person who said this is foolish.\" This is bad — but Rambam makes a remarkable statement: ואין בכך הרס יסודות האמונה — this does NOT destroy the foundations of faith. Why? Because at least this person isn't ascribing impossible things to the Chachamim. He may be wrong to think badly of them, but he's not corrupting his understanding of reality. Type 3: The ideal — recognizing deeper meaning או שיניח שיש להן תוך Reads the statements and recognizes: there must be a deeper meaning here. Whether or not he understands what the deeper meaning is, he at least knows the surface reading is not the full picture. This is the correct approach — and the one Rambam writes the Moreh for. Part 5: The Importance of Meshalim — Three Beautiful Midrashim Midrash 1: The Well and the Rope למה היו דברי תורה דומים עד שלא עמד שלמה? לבאר שהיו מימיה עמוקים וצוננים ולא היה אדם יכול לשתות מהם Until Shlomo, the words of Torah were like a well of deep, cold water — nobody could drink from it. What did a clever person do? He connected one strand to another, made a rope, and drew out the water. כך היה שלמה ממשל למשל — Shlomo wove mashal to mashal, word to word, until the Torah could be accessed. That's why the book is called Mishlei — it's the collection of ropes for drawing water from the Torah's depths. What was too deep to access? Not the laws of Sukkah or Choshen Mishpat — the deeper things, matters of metaphysics and theology. Midrash 2: The Candle and the Diamond זה שאבד סלע או מרגלית בתוך ביתו. עד שהוא מדליק פתילה באיסר מוצאת המרגלית A person lost a precious diamond somewhere in his dark house. He lights a cheap candle — worth a few pennies — and finds the diamond worth thousands. כך המשל הזה אינו כלום — the mashal itself looks like nothing, looks cheap, looks superficial. But with it, you find the diamonds of Torah. The Rambam draws out the layers: The diamond = the deep truths of Torah ( דברי תורה הם מרגלית ) The dark house = our state of not understanding The cheap candle = the mashal — seemingly worthless, but the key to finding everything The diamond is there in the house, you own it — but without the candle, it's as if you've lost it entirely The Pasuk: Golden Apples in Silver Settings תפוחי זהב במשכיות כסף דבר דבור על אופניו Shlomo HaMelech in Mishlei — golden apples in silver settings is a word spoken properly. Rambam's reading: Maskiyot = settings with tiny holes (eyelets) — like silverwork with minute openings From a distance, you see silver and think it's a silver apple But if you look closely through the tiny holes — you see gold inside The application: The outer meaning (the simple reading) should be good — like silver. It has value. The inner meaning should be better — like gold exceeding silver. The outer meaning should contain enough hints — like the eyelets — to show the perceptive reader that there is something deeper within. The nevi'im's meshalim work exactly this way: the surface is useful (ethical wisdom, social guidance), but the depths contain אמונות אמת אמיתית — genuine truths of faith. Part 6: Two Types of Meshalim in Tanakh Rambam introduces a crucial distinction for studying his book and all of Tanakh: Type 1: Every word matters Some meshalim in Tanakh are constructed so that every single word in the mashal has a deeper meaning. Next week's example: Yaakov's ladder — every element (the ladder, the angels, ascending, descending, Hashem standing above) carries specific meaning. Type 2: The general concept matters Other meshalim are very long and verbose, but not every word adds meaning to the deeper idea. Extra words serve to: Make the mashal more attractive and complete as a story Deliberately deepen the concealment — paradoxically, more words can hide the meaning more effectively Next week's example: The seductive woman in Mishlei — a long mashal about adultery, but the point is the general concept, not every individual word. This is an important warning for studying the Moreh: Don't over-analyze every word in every mashal. Some meshalim are Type 1 (every word matters), some are Type 2 (general concept matters). Knowing which is which is part of the skill. Summary of This Evening Deep truths cannot be conveyed directly — even those who attain them struggle to express them. The nature of these truths forces the use of meshalim. The Torah begins with Bereishit because it is natural science — the first rung on the ladder from physics → metaphysics → understanding God → proper performance of mitzvot. This is a fundamentally different answer from Rashi. Bereishit is multi-layered — simple meaning for simple people, deeper meaning for developed minds. שבעים פנים לתורה. Rambam abandoned his earlier projects (book on prophecy, book of correlation) because he couldn't resolve the dilemma of either exposing too much or just replacing one mashal with another. Three types of readers: the ignoramus who accepts the impossible literally; the intellectual who rejects and thinks badly of the Chachamim (bad, but not a destruction of faith); and the ideal reader who recognizes deeper meaning. Meshalim are essential, not optional — three Midrashim illustrate this: the well and the rope, the candle and the diamond, golden apples in silver settings. Two types of meshalim — every word matters vs. general concept matters. We will see examples of each next week. Coming Up Next Two examples of the two types of meshalim: Yaakov's ladder (every word matters) and the seductive woman in Mishlei (general concept) Rambam's advice on how to study the Moreh Seven reasons for apparent contradictions in the Moreh — which concludes the introduction Then we begin Part One, Chapter One — the actual discussion of specific terms in Tanakh B'Ezrat Hashem","51LkNgjxDmU":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 7 (ענוה — Humility) — Part 2 Edition: Feldheim Theme: Training Method 2 (continued) — what happens when humility is missing Recap: Chapter 7 Part 1 Chapter 1: Two paths — the Tzaddik (basics) and the Chasid (beyond) Chapter 2: All middot are interrelated Chapter 3: Purity of motive — four levels Chapter 4: Compassion — first of the 13 Attributes. When in doubt → mercy Chapter 5: Generosity — four kinds: money, influence, intellect, faith Chapter 6: Calmness — intellect over emotion. Anger + arrogance linked; patience + humility linked. Sometimes anger is appropriate for spiritual matters. Chapter 7 Part 1: Humility defined — internal vs. external, vis-à-vis God and humans. Humility = recognizing yourself exactly as you are, not exaggerating up or down. NOT self-debasement. Three methods of training: Personal/experiential — reflecting on past arrogance, surrounding oneself with humble people Through the Torah — stories of humble vs. arrogant figures + mitzvot designed to cultivate humility Philosophical analysis — to come in a future class Last class covered: Positive examples (Avraham — \"I am dust and ash\"; Yaakov; Elisha) + the negative example of Nadav and Avihu — root failure was not taking counsel from Moshe despite their lofty status. This Week's Theme This class continues the second method of training — but now turning to the warning side: people in Tanakh who lost humility and what it cost them. Two crucial framings throughout: These figures were not simple troublemakers. Rabbi Avraham emphasizes again and again that they were genuinely great, prominent, even prophetic people. The whole point is that even greatness offers no protection. These stories are not just history — they function as mirrors. Rabbi Avraham wants us to see ourselves in them. In the Torah: Korach, Datan & Aviram Don't underestimate who they were. Rabbi Avraham insists we correct a common misconception. Korach was not a simple man with an inflated ego. He was a great person — and Datan and Aviram, though not on Korach's level, were also significant figures. Evidence for Korach's Greatness The 250 men who joined him are described as נשיאי עדה קריאי מועד אנשי שם — princes of the community, men of name. Prominent leaders accepted his argument. When Moshe proposed the test of the firepans, Korach accepted without hesitation. A simple challenger would not be entertained by Moshe — the test shows Moshe took the threat seriously. ויפול על פניו — Moshe fell on his face. Rashi: exhaustion at yet another machloket. Rabbi Avraham: genuine fear, because the threat was real. Moshe had to pray that Hashem not accept their offering. If they were ordinary sinners, no such prayer would be needed. Even after they perished, Hashem honored their firepans by incorporating them into the Mishkan: כי הקריבו לפני ה' ויקדשו The people complained: אתם המיתם את עם ה' — they mourned Korach as a prominent figure. Datan and Aviram — A Surprising Reading We're accustomed (from Rashi) to seeing Datan and Aviram as perennial troublemakers. Rabbi Avraham reads them differently. The verse calls them אנשים — and in Tanakh, אנשים often denotes prominent people (as in שלח לך אנשים, the meraglim). They were people of standing. Why called resha'im? Relative to Moshe and Korach, they were on a lower spiritual level — average rather than wicked They were corrupting themselves by joining the rebellion The Religious Veneer — Critical Point Korach's rebellion had a religious face: רב לכם כי כל העדה כלם קדשים ובתוכם ה' ומדוע תתנשאו על קהל ה' Moshe himself said: כי משאת נפשם היתה כהונה — they wanted priesthood, a holy ambition. Even arrogance dressed as religious zeal is still arrogance. Datan and Aviram had a different flavour — pure jealousy and resentment of Moshe's authority (המעט כי העליתנו מארץ מצרים כי תשתרר). Less religiously framed, more about kavod — but the same root. In the Nevi'im: Shaul HaMelech Rabbi Avraham (and the Rambam) hold that Shaul was not only a king but also a prophet — בחיר השם, chosen by Hashem. Three failures, all rooted in arrogance and pursuit of human approval: Failure 1 — The War with Amalek Commanded to wipe out Amalek completely. He didn't — spared Agag and the choice animals. Why? פחדו מבני אדם פן ישביתו את שלטונו הגשמי — he feared the people would undermine his sovereignty. His confession: יראתי את העם ואשמע בקולם — I feared the people and listened to them. Even after confessing, he asked Shmuel: כבדני נא נגד זקני עמי — please honor me before the elders. Even at the moment of repentance, his concern was still public perception. Failure 2 — Jealousy of David After David killed Goliath, the women sang הכה שאול באלפיו ודוד ברבבותיו From that day: ויהי שאול עוין את דוד — the unusual word עוין means he analyzed David negatively, constantly looking to find fault. Root: I am the king, I must be on top, everyone must recognize this. Pure ga'avah. Failure 3 — The Massacre at Nov Doeg told Shaul that the priests of Nov had given David bread. Shaul ordered the entire city slaughtered — men, women, children, animals. The bitter irony: What Shaul failed to do to Amalek, he did to a city of priests. Connection to this Shabbat's Pirkei Avot: הקנאה התאוה והכבוד מוציאין את האדם מן העולם — jealousy, desire, and honor remove a person from the world. Doeg HaAdomi Doeg was no ignoramus. He was a Talmid Chacham. The verse describes him as נעצר לפני ה' — constantly serving Hashem in the Mishkan. His failure: wanting to find favor in Shaul's eyes. He reported on the priests of Nov. When Shaul's own servants refused to carry out the slaughter, Doeg himself struck down the Kohen Gadol. Rambam in Peirush HaMishnayot cites Doeg as the example of אל תתודע לרשות — do not become overly familiar with the authorities. Three punishments (from Tehillim, derived in Sanhedrin): אין לו חלק לעולם הבא — no portion in the world to come His Torah teachings would not be cited (יחתך) His descendants would not become Talmidei Chachamim (ושרשך מארץ חיים) A Talmid Chacham who lost everything — Olam HaBa, his learning legacy, his line — all because he chased the king's favor. Achitofel David's counselor, one of the great minds of his generation. Chazal describe him as enormously wise. His failure: wanting to find favor in Avshalom's eyes during the rebellion. He calculated Avshalom would win and switched allegiance. He gave Avshalom two pieces of advice. The military advice was sound — Avshalom didn't take it. At that moment Achitofel realized Avshalom would fail and David would return. Why he killed himself (Radak): He knew David would have him executed for rebellion (mored b'malchut) An executed rebel's property was forfeit to the king By killing himself first, he saved his estate for his children Result: Hanged himself, lost his portion in the world to come (Mishna in Sanhedrin), died disgracefully — all to chase favor with a son who would never sit on the throne. Avshalom Surprisingly elevated terms — Chazal say Avshalom was a permanent Nazir (אבשלום נזיר עולם). Even David himself was deceived when Avshalom asked permission to go to Hevron to fulfill a vow: כי נדר נדר עבדך... ועבדתי את ה' Of course this was a ruse — Avshalom was going to Hevron to consolidate the rebellion. But David, who knew his son well, was fooled — which shows Avshalom was not a transparent villain. He had real religious credentials. Downfall: תאוות השררה והתנשאות — desire for power and elevation. Adoniyah ben Haggit Another son of David, attempted to claim the throne ahead of Shlomo. Attracted serious figures — Yoav and Evyatar HaKohen joined him. This is the famous opening of Sefer Melachim — source of the royal anointing ceremony. Prominent enough to attract great supporters — yet his hunger for power, despite David's clear designation of Shlomo, brought his downfall. Yoav ben Tzeruyah David's general — a great military leader. Establishing Yoav's Real Greatness Before the war with Ammon: חזק ונתחזק בעד עמנו ובעד ערי אלוהינו והשם יעשה הטוב בעיניו — a man of bitachon. Listened to the wise woman of Avel who pleaded not to destroy the city for one rebel (Sheva ben Bichri). One Midrash identifies the wise woman as Serach bat Asher. Protested David's census: למה יבקש זאת אדוני למה יהיה אשמה לישראל Yet Yoav Came to Murder Twice Avner ben Ner — After Avner made peace with David, Yoav killed him. Reasons: revenge for his brother Asael's death + suspicion Avner would replace him. Amasa ben Yeter — After David replaced Yoav with Amasa as general, Yoav killed him. Both times by deception — calling them over to \"talk,\" then striking. The verse in Mishpatim: וכי יזיד איש על רעהו להרגו בערמה מעם מזבחי תקחנו למות Rabbi Avraham reads this as a hint to Yoav — who indeed fled to the altar and was taken from it to be executed by Benayahu on Shlomo's orders. We do not find this happening to anyone else. Shlomo's revealing words: השב פגע בשני אנשים צדיקים וטובים ממנו — the phrase ממנו (\"better than him\") implies Yoav himself was a righteous man — just less so than Avner and Amasa. Rechavam ben Shlomo The son of Shlomo — the beginning of the unraveling of the kingdom. People came requesting relief from heavy burdens. Elders advised compromise. The young men advised: קטני עבה ממתני אבי — my little finger is thicker than my father's loins. His refusal caused the rebellion of Yeravam and the splitting of Am Yisrael — the eventual exile of the Aseret HaShevatim. The pattern played out twice: Later, when his reign was reconsolidated and Hashem strengthened him, he again forgot Hakadosh Baruch Hu. Shishak of Egypt was sent against him. Only when Rechavam and his princes humbled themselves did Hashem relent: ובהכנעו שב ממנו אף ה' Arrogance summons punishment; humility relieves it. Yeravam ben Nevat Yeravam, whose name has become synonymous with heresy, did not start that way. A great man, chosen by Hashem through the prophet Achiyah HaShiloni. Chazal say he and Achiyah were the great Chachamim of the generation — his colleagues did not approach his level. Hashem promised him: ואותך אקח ומלכת בכל אשר תאוה נפשך... והייתי עמך What went wrong: Once given the kingship, ga'avah took hold. Worried that if his subjects went up to Yerushalayim for the regalim, they'd be drawn back to the southern kingdom. Rather than trust Hashem, he built the golden calves at Beit El and Dan, instituted alternative festivals, led the nation into avodah zarah. The verse traces his thought process: ויאמר ירבעם בלבו עתה תשוב הממלכה לבית דוד — pure political fear, pure jealousy. His memory is recorded as a curse. All from ga'avah. Uziyahu HaMelech Uziyahu (Azariah) ben Amatzyahu was a good king — the verse explicitly testifies to his righteousness. His failure was not avodah zarah. It was something subtler: וכחזקתו גבה לבו עד להשחית Entered the Heichal to offer ketoret — the privilege of the Kohanim alone. Azariah HaKohen and 80 priests confronted him. He refused to back down. Parallel to Korach: This too was religiously-framed arrogance — he wanted to serve Hashem with the ketoret. But his sense that \"I am the king, this should be mine too\" was the same root. Result: Struck with tzaraat on the spot, lost his kingship, his son Yotam took the throne while still young. Chizkiyahu HaMelech — The Most Striking Case Rabbi Avraham saves the most surprising case for last. Chizkiyahu was an exceptional king — removed avodah zarah, restored Torah, profoundly righteous. Yet ga'avah attached even to him. The unique element: Chizkiyahu's arrogance did not lead him to commit any specific sin. No murder, no avodah zarah, no rebellion. ולא נזכר שהגאווה הזאת הביאתו לידי מרד נגד ה' אלא על עצם הגאווה בא הקצף The wrath came on the arrogance itself. Chizkiyahu was deathly ill, prayed, was healed with a sign. ולא כגמול עליו השיב חזקיהו כי גבה לבו — he did not respond with the gratitude expected, because his heart became elevated. When he humbled himself again, the wrath was held back. The chilling lesson: For the truly close servants of Hashem, ga'avah itself — even without external sin — is enough to summon divine displeasure. Arrogance is itself a form of rebellion (חטא ומרד), as the verse says: תועבת ה' כל גבה לב — every arrogant heart is an abomination to Hashem. Statements of the Chachamim The Rambam on Pirkei Avot מאוד מאוד הוי שפל רוח שתקוות אנוש רימה The Rambam stresses the doubled מאוד מאוד. Why? Because remembering this single truth — that the body returns to dust — is enough to evoke true humility in any thinking person. The root of sin is rarely ignorance. We know what is right. The problem is that we forget. We live on autopilot. Simply learning these matters, speaking them out loud, thinking about them — that itself is the avodah. The act of study is the act of remembering. The Threefold Teaching: Hashem's Greatness and Humility The Gemara at the end of Megillah: כל מקום שאתה מוצא גבורתו של הקדוש ברוך הוא שם אתה מוצא ענוותנותו Wherever you find the strength of Hakadosh Baruch Hu, there you find His humility. Mentioned three times: Torah (Eikev): האל הגדול הגיבור והנורא → followed immediately by עושה משפט יתום ואלמנה ואוהב גר Nevi'im (Yeshayah): רם ונשא שוכן עד וקדוש שמו → followed by ואת דכא ושפל רוח Ketuvim (Tehillim — the one we read on Shavuot): סולו לרוכב בערבות → followed by אבי יתומים ודיין אלמנות Hashem's strength is inseparable from His care for the lowly. Other Statements David in Tehillim: וענוותך תרבני — Your humility makes me great Mishna in Avot: תלמידיו של אברהם אבינו עין טובה ונפש שפלה ורוח נמוכה — the students of Avraham have a good eye, a low soul, and a humble spirit. (We will discuss the difference between נפש שפלה and רוח נמוכה next time.) Gemara in Pesachim: אוי לה לרבנות שמקברת את בעליה — woe to leadership, for it buries those who hold it. Yeshayahu HaNavi outlived four kings (Uziyah, Yotam, Achaz, Chizkiyahu) Michah also outlived four kings Yosef HaTzadik lived only 110 years — fewer than his brothers — because of his leadership role (Berachot) Too much authority is dangerous. The crown wears down its bearer. The Throughline Across every figure in this shiur, the same pattern repeats with variations: Religiously masked arrogance: Korach, Uziyahu Straightforward pride: Yeravam, Avshalom Fear of losing position: Shaul, Yoav Chasing favor with authority: Doeg, Achitofel Heart simply elevated for a moment: Chizkiyahu The remedy in every case is הכנעה — bringing oneself low. Where it was applied (Rechavam after Shishak; Chizkiyahu in his moment of recognition), the wrath was held back. The reason this chapter comes through stories rather than philosophy is precisely the Rambam's point: we don't fall because we don't know. We fall because we forget. The stories are designed to be remembered. Coming Up Next Next class: Continuation of Chapter 7 — the mitzvot in the Torah designed to cultivate humility. The standout example is the king, who is given an extraordinary number of mitzvot precisely to keep his ga'avah in check. After that: The third method of training — philosophical analysis. B'Ezrat Hashem","0tMPvhbrEAA":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Siman: 319 (Borer — Selecting and Sorting on Shabbat) Seifim Covered: Seif Gimmel (in depth) + completing the utensil discussion from Seif Aleph Topics: Hand vs. utensil, forks/knives/peelers, ochel from ochel, selecting vs. sorting, defining a mixture, mitchloket on gidulei karka, silverware on Shabbat Recap: Seifim Aleph & Bet (Previous Class) Three melachot of selection: Zoreh (winnowing — wind), Borer (all other selection), Meraked (sieving) Three conditions of Borer (all three required): Hand, not utensil Ochel from psolet (wanted from unwanted) Le'altar (immediate use) Le'altar = for the upcoming meal. Not about minutes — about mindset. Birkat HaMazon rule (Chacham Ovadia): Don't do borer before Birkat HaMazon for dessert after — benching ends the meal. Borer must apply to a mixture — no mixture, no borer. If you violate borer: be-mezid → food assur (can be rectified by putting back and re-selecting, per Chacham Ovadia); be-shogeg → permitted. Completing the Utensil Discussion The Three Levels of Utensils (From Seif Aleph) Utensil Type Example Status Specialized sieve (נפה וכברה) Fine sieve, strainer Chayav d'oraita Non-specialized utensil (קנון ותמחוי) Funnel, cone-shaped filter Assur mi-derabbanan Hand (ביד) Your own hand Mutar Cutlery: Forks, Knives, Spoons All poskim agree: יד ארוכתא — \"An extension of your hand\" Forks, knives, spoons = yad arichta (extension of your hand) Permitted to use for hygiene or convenience — no one disagrees with this MACHLOKET: What If the Utensil Actually Helps in the Selection? Example: Congealed fat on top of soup → using a spoon to separate the fat from the soup. Position 1 — Mishnah Berurah + Rav Moshe Feinstein: When cutlery helps in the act of selection → assur mi-derabbanan Reasoning: The reason Chachamim forbade kanon and tamchui was because they help with selection. A fork/spoon/knife that helps in selection should similarly be assur. The \"yad arichta\" heter only applies when it's purely an extension of the hand — not when it's performing a selection function. Position 2 — Chacham Ovadia (and others): Even when cutlery helps in the act of selection → mutar Reasoning: If the Gemara meant any utensil that helps in selection is assur, why specify kanon and tamchui? Kanon and tamchui are utensils sometimes/commonly used for selection (e.g., selecting wheat) — that's why they're specifically assur Forks, knives, spoons are not commonly used for selection — they're cutlery for eating Therefore: no din of borer with cutlery at all — even when helping selection Peelers — Chacham Ovadia's Extension Chacham Ovadia's logic: A peeler is not a specialized selection utensil — it's essentially a special kind of knife Meikar ha-din → permitted, even on Shabbat But: המחמיר תבוא עליו ברכה — preferable to avoid if possible Contemporary poskim: Many contemporary poskim forbid using a peeler on Shabbat Rabbi Kada's position: Those who use peelers have what to rely on — shouldn't object to them, but even Chacham Ovadia says better not to if you don't have to Seif ג (Gimmel): Ochel from Psolet When Both Are Food The Shulchan Aruch's Text (Without the Rema) היו לפניו שני מיני אוכלין מעורבים, בורר אחד מאחד ומניח לאכול מיד Two kinds of foods (both edible) mixed together Select one from the other, leave, eat immediately This is verbatim from the Rambam What the Shulchan Aruch DOESN'T Say Just says \"select one from the other\" — doesn't specify which direction Could imply you're allowed to take either one out (wanted OR unwanted) Major question: When both items are good foods, does ochel/psolet still apply? The Rema's Addition — The Game-Changer בורר אחד מאחד ומניח השני לאכול מיד Rema adds the word השני (\"the second one\") Now it's clear: you take out the wanted, leave the unwanted behind The Rema is establishing that even with two edible foods, you must take what you want, not what you don't want The Underlying Machloket Position 1 — Kiryat Sefer, Maggid Mishneh, some Rishonim and Acharonim: When both are edible foods, the rule of \"ochel from psolet\" does NOT apply You have a mixture (so time + utensil conditions apply) But you can take either direction — wanted from unwanted OR unwanted from wanted Position 2 — Halacha (Mishnah Berurah, all poskim Ashkenaz and Sefard): Even with two edible foods, you must take the wanted from the unwanted The unwanted is psolet relative to you at this moment L'halacha: We follow Position 2 — always take the wanted from the unwanted. But know there are substantial poskim who disagree. The Lesson About Shulchan Aruch vs. Rema The Rema can substantively change the meaning of the Shulchan Aruch If Rambam/Shulchan Aruch held it's mutar → the Rema completely reversed it Important to read the Shulchan Aruch first without the Rema to see what it actually says Major Question: Selecting vs. Sorting The Pri Megadim's Question (300 Years Ago) Scenario: 12:00 PM Shabbat Mixture of pistachios and peanuts — both edible I want pistachios at 5:00 PM My wife wants peanuts at 5:00 PM We'll sit down together at 5:00 to eat them Can I separate them at 12:00? The Question: I don't want either NOW I don't NOT want either — I want them both, just not now Is this borer or not? Does Borer Mean to SELECT or to SORT? If borer = sorting → assur I'm sorting/distinguishing at 12:00 for 5:00 — not immediate use If borer = selecting → mutar I haven't selected anything because I want both I've just separated them for later use No act of \"choosing one over the other\" The Resolution Pri Megadim: Borer means SELECTING. Therefore permitted to sort for later. Biur Halacha: Borer means SORTING. Therefore even sorting for later is assur. L'halacha: Follow the Biur Halacha — borer IS sorting. Sorting for later is assur. Important nafka mina: This comes up with silverware (see below). Defining a Mixture (Ta'arovet) The Rema's Rulings שני מיני דגים מקרי שני מיני אוכלין Two kinds of fish (e.g., salmon + haddock) = two different foods = a mixture Borer applies — must select immediately, with hand אף על פי שהחתיכות גדולות וכל אחת ניכרת בפני עצמה Even if the pieces are large and each one is identifiable → still a mixture Still requires the laws of borer אבל כל שהוא מין אחד... לא מקרי ברירה One species (e.g., all salmon, big and small pieces) = NOT a mixture Can divide big from small freely — it's \"division,\" not \"selection\" Example: giving big pieces to adults, small pieces to children → fine at any time The Mishnah Berurah's Extensions Considered a mixture (same species): Burnt vs. unburnt pieces of the same food Raw vs. cooked pieces of the same food בשר צלי ומבושל — roasted meat vs. boiled meat Apples: sour vs. sweet → borer applies Different meats/poultry mixed together (big issue at big seudot) Grey Areas — Judge for Yourself These are unclear — depends on whether YOU consider it a mixture: Scenario Mixture? Pink Lady apples vs. Gala apples Unclear Red apples vs. green apples Unclear Chicken thighs vs. chicken legs (same cooking) Unclear Rabbi Kada's guidance: If it looks like a mixture to you → follow laws of borer If it doesn't → don't need to Safek d'oraita l'chumra — when in doubt, be strict Essentially subjective: do YOU see this as a mixture or not? Clearly NOT a Mixture Items clearly separated and discernible (not piled on each other) Silverware in separate compartments of a drawer Fruits clearly laid out in distinct sections Does Borer Apply to Non-Gidulei Karka? The Principle Some melachot only apply to gidulei karka (things that grow from the ground): Dash (threshing) — אין דש אלא בגידולי קרקע Me'amer (gathering) Applies to: vegetables, fruits, grains, and also animals/poultry (because they eat from the ground — loosely gidulei karka) Does NOT apply to fish (fish are not gidulei karka) The Machloket for Borer Position 1 — Terumat HaDeshen, Mordechai, most Rishonim: Borer applies to everything , not just gidulei karka Includes fish, utensils, clothing, anything mixed Position 2 — Rabbi Akiva Eiger, others: Just like dash only applies to gidulei karka → borer too Would NOT apply to fish, clothes, utensils The Rambam: Equivocal — says gidulei karka only for dash, but doesn't specify for borer. Could infer it applies to everything. L'halacha: Borer applies to EVERYTHING. Fish bones, clothing, utensils — all subject to borer. Practical Applications Clothes in a Dark Drawer (Friday Night) Drawer with mixed socks, low light You look, investigate, pick out the pair you want Since borer applies to everything → this is borer Can't take out for the next morning Clothes Clearly Visible Drawer lit up, blue/red/yellow/black socks clearly separate and visible Not a mixture → no problem Removing Items on Top to Reach Below Scenario: 5 dresses on pegs, the one you want is #4 Take off #1, #2, #3 to reach #4 Is this taking psolet from ochel? Resolution via Biur Halacha: Biur Halacha asked a technical question: why is taking psolet from ochel chayav if it seems like melacha she'eina tzricha legufa? Answer (Chacham Ben Tzion Abba Shaul): That IS the melacha — same as zore'a (planting takes months to yield, but that's the derekh of the melacha) From the Biur Halacha's discussion: when you're removing something on top to reach what's underneath , that's called act of removing , not borer Therefore: removing dresses #1, #2, #3 to reach #4 = mutar Multiple Coats on a Peg (Shul Cloakroom) Five coats on one peg, yours is in the middle Take off the ones above to reach yours Mutar — same principle, act of removing, not selecting Silverware on Shabbat — The Big Question The Scenario Friday night — hosted 20 guests End of meal → 60+ pieces of mixed cutlery (forks, knives, spoons, teaspoons) Did the washing up → all in a big pile, washed Shabbat morning lunch → 20 more guests Can I lay the table Friday night for Shabbat morning? The Kaf HaChayim's Strict Position Don't do this — it's a clear act of borer on a mixture for non-immediate use Easy workaround: When washing up, pile them as you wash: Take a fork → wash it → put on fork pile Take a knife → wash it → put on knife pile Your \"immediate use\" is washing it — not setting the table Once washed, they're sorted by the washing act itself Chacham Ovadia's Lenient Position — Four Combined Sfeikot Point 1: Machloket whether borer applies to non-gidulei karka at all Though halacha takes on it does → still a machloket Point 2: Pri Megadim's svara — borer is selecting, not sorting Setting the table is sorting, not selecting (I don't want any one in particular) I want them all equally for later Point 3: Ochel from ochel (two wanted things) Cutlery is all wanted — there's no \"unwanted\" cutlery Magen Avraham and Ben Ish Chai hold that ochel from ochel for the next meal is mutar even not le'altar: מסעודה לסעודה אחר שאכל סעודה מכין סעודה אחרת אף שהיא עדיין זמן מופלג Though l'halacha we don't take this on Point 4: Each piece is individual and visible — arguably not a true \"mixture\" Chacham Ovadia's Conclusion All four sfeikot together → permitted to lay the table Friday night for Shabbat morning from a washed mixture of cutlery But the Kaf HaChayim's workaround (sorting during washing) is simpler and avoids the question entirely The Biur Halacha's Technical Question (Lomdus) The Question Borer of psolet from ochel is chayav de'oraita But isn't this melacha she'eina tzricha legufa ? (I don't want the psolet — I'm just moving it out) Chacham Ben Tzion Abba Shaul's Answer That IS the melacha — this is its very definition Parallel to zore'a: planting now for a harvest in 3 months — isn't that grama (indirect)? Answer: that's the derekh of the melacha — plant now, harvest later = the definition of zore'a So too borer: removing unwanted IS the melacha The Yeshu'ot Ya'akov's Attempted Answer The melacha is really \"making the wanted food better to eat\" (fixing the ochel), not removing the psolet Nafka mina: When you have two wanted foods (both edible) and remove one, you're NOT improving the other → would be melacha she'eina tzricha legufa Biur Halacha uses this to establish: removing something on top to reach below = not borer Seif ג Final Rulings (Rema + Shulchan Aruch) The Rema's Additional Halacha ואפילו היו שני מינים ובורר משניהם ביחד הגדולות מתוך קטנות או איפכא שרי הואיל ואינו בורר מין אחד מתוך חברו Permitted to take mixed items together: Mixture of pistachios and peanuts At 12:00 for guests at 5:00 Take a big spoon — scoop out pistachios AND peanuts together Move half to another bowl Not selecting/sorting by type → just dividing Fine to do for later use The Shulchan Aruch's Closing Line ואם בירר והניח לאחר זמן אפילו לבו ביום כגון שבירר שחרית לאכול בין הערבים חייב Selecting in the morning to eat in the afternoon → chayav de'oraita Even two edible foods, even same day L'halacha: we do NOT follow the Ben Ish Chai/Magen Avraham leniency of \"meal-to-meal\" being considered le'altar Key Takeaways Cutlery (fork, knife, spoon) = extension of hand. Chacham Ovadia: mutar even if helping selection. Rav Moshe: assur if helping selection. Peelers — Chacham Ovadia meikar ha-din permits; many contemporary poskim forbid. Ochel from ochel — halacha: must take wanted from unwanted even when both are edible. Rema added the word \"הַשֵּׁנִי\" to establish this. Borer means sorting, not just selecting — sorting two wanted things for later is also assur. Mixture defined: Different species, or same species with different qualities (cooked/raw, burnt/unburnt, sweet/sour). Grey areas → subjective, lean strict. Borer applies to everything , not just gidulei karka. Includes clothes, utensils, fish bones. Removing items on top to reach what's below = act of removal, not borer. Mutar. Silverware on Shabbat: Easy solution — sort during washing. Chacham Ovadia: combined sfeikot permit sorting washed cutlery for next day's meal. Preview: Next Class (Seif Daled) Bones of fish and meat — is there borer with parts of one unit (derech bri'ato)? Food mixed into liquid (solids in liquids, fly in soup) Borer with one unit of food Working through remaining seifim of Siman 319 Shabbat Shalom","yMLqA-bTwv8":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: מורה נבוכים / Moreh Nebuchim — The Guide for the Perplexed Author: רבינו משה בן מימון — Rambam (Maimonides) Section: Introductory Letter to Rabbi Yosef + Beginning of the Introduction Text: Sefaria (modern Hebrew translation from Arabic, likely Prof. Schwartz) Preliminary Notes Which text are we using? Sefaria has a modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic — not the Rambam's original words, but a translation. Rav Kapach's translation with footnotes is the primary study text for preparation. Important: The Moreh is not really a \"book\" — Rambam never calls it that. He wrote essays/chapters and sent them to his student. They were later compiled into what we call the Moreh. The headlines (rashei perakim) on Sefaria are NOT from the Rambam — they're modern editorial summaries. Same for bracketed text. The Introductory Letter: Why Rambam Wrote This Opening — בשם ה' אל עולם Rambam opens with the pasuk from the end of Parashat Vayera: ויטע אשל בבאר שבע ויקרא שם בשם ה' אל עולם Avraham = the father of monotheism, proclaiming the unity of Hashem to the world Rambam is saying: I am continuing this mission — proclaiming the yichud Hashem The Student: Rabbi Yosef ben Rabbi Yehuda Rambam addresses his student Rabbi Yosef ben Yehuda ibn Shimon We know: he was a member of the Beit Din in Egypt for a period, died in 1226 He came from far away to study under the Rambam in Egypt The Progression — How Rambam Assessed His Student Before meeting in person: Rabbi Yosef sent letters, poetry, and prose from Alexandria Rambam was impressed but cautious: שמא תשוקתו גדולה מהשגתו — \"Perhaps his desire is greater than his reach\" After meeting: Rambam guided him in astronomy → saw he already had a basis Sent him to study more mathematics (prerequisite for astronomy) Then more astronomy Then logic Only THEN: felt it appropriate to reveal the secrets of the Nevi'im The Hierarchy of Study There's a clear order the Rambam insists on: Mathematics (foundation) Astronomy (builds on maths) Logic (the discipline of reasoning) Secrets of Torah / Theology (only after all the above) Imagine today: Going to a rav to study theology and he says \"First, go study some more maths and science. Then come back to me.\" That's what the Rambam did. The Mutakallimun (Kalam Philosophers) Rabbi Yosef asked Rambam to explain the Mutakallimun — Islamic philosophers Kalam (كلام) = \"words\" in Arabic (the school of Islamic philosophy) They tried to reconcile Greek philosophy with Islamic theology (8th–9th centuries onward) Saadia Gaon relied heavily on the Mutakallimun in Emunot VeDeot Rambam generally didn't like them — felt they were too apologetic Rav Kapach thinks when Rambam says \"you learned from others,\" he's referring to Saadia Gaon The Catalyst — Why He Actually Wrote It וכאשר גזר האל את פרידתנו... עוררוני הפגישות הללו When Rabbi Yosef had to leave → Rambam felt a lack The time they spent studying together had aroused something in him He had thought about writing these things before but never got round to it Now that his student was gone → he felt compelled to continue teaching through writing הניע אותי חסרונך לחבר את החיבור הזה \"Your absence motivated me to compose this work\" Wrote in chapters/essays → sent them one by one to Rabbi Yosef The Teacher-Student Dynamic The Gemara: יותר ממה שהעגל רוצה לינוק הפרה רוצה להניק — \"More than the calf wants to suck, the cow wants to nurse\" Pirkei Avot: מתלמידי יותר מכולם — \"From my students I learned the most\" Teaching satisfies the teacher — when the student left, the Rambam felt a void → that void became the Moreh The Rambam's Introduction — Purpose of the Moreh Three Opening Verses Tehillim: הודיעני דרך זו אלך כי אליך נשאתי נפשי — \"Show me the path, Hashem — I look to You for guidance\" Mishlei: אליכם אישים אקרא וקולי אל בני אדם — \"To you men (ishim) I call, and my voice to humans (bnei adam)\" Mishlei: הט אזנך ושמע דברי חכמים ולבך תשית לדעתי — \"Listen to the sages, but put your heart to MY knowledge\" Why these three? Verse 1: General — guide me on the path Verse 2 (Efodi's explanation): The Moreh has multiple levels of meaning אישים = the elect, the wise → will understand deeper levels בני אדם = regular people → will understand the surface level Same text, different depths of understanding Verse 3 (Rav Kapach): Rambam will quote many philosophers and sages throughout Listen to them all — BUT: ולבך תשית לדעתי — at the end of the day, follow MY view Purpose 1: Explaining Terms in Tanach מטרתו הראשונה של חיבור זה היא ביאור משמעויות של מונחים המופיעים בספרי הנבואה Three categories of terms that cause confusion: Category 1: Multi-meaning terms (מונחים רב-משמעיים) One Hebrew word with multiple meanings in different contexts Example: חי (chai) — can mean \"live,\" \"raw,\" or when applied to Hashem (חי ה') → neither alive nor dead, something beyond Example: קרן (keren) — can mean \"horn\" or \"ray\" (as in קרן עור פניו) Simple people understand only ONE meaning → miss the deeper layers Category 2: Borrowed/metaphorical terms (מושאלים) Words used metaphorically — people take them literally Classic example: עיני ה' (the eyes of Hashem) — doesn't mean literal eyes with pupils → means hashgachah (divine oversight/attention) This is the core of anti-anthropomorphism — a major theme of the entire Moreh Rambam will later write (end of Part 1): believing in a corporeal God is worse than serving avodah zarah During his lifetime, many people — even rabbis — believed God had some kind of physicality Category 3: Ambiguous terms (מסופקים) Sometimes borrowed, sometimes multi-meaning — unclear which Context determines the correct understanding Who Is This Book For? אין מטרתו של חיבור זה להסביר את כולם להמון NOT for: The masses / simple people Beginners in study Even great Talmidei Chachamim who only know the legal aspects of Torah (halacha, Gemara, dayanut) but nothing beyond Written FOR a person who has ALL of the following: Religious — observant, committed to Torah Clear belief in the truth and veracity of Torah Completed their character — good middot, wholesome personality Studied philosophy — understands philosophical terminology and concepts Has intellectual curiosity — naturally drawn to understand more But is PERPLEXED — reads verses in Tanach that seem to contradict what they've studied philosophically The Perplexed Person's Dilemma Such a person faces two terrible options: Option A: Follow their intellect → throw away their Torah beliefs → think they've abandoned the foundations of faith Option B: Stick with their simple Torah understanding → suppress their intellectual curiosity → constantly troubled, walking a tightrope ויהיה שרוי תמיד בכאב לב ובמבוכה גדולה Either way → constant pain and great confusion The Moreh is written to rescue this person from the dilemma Purpose 2: Explaining Veiled Imagery in Tanach ביאור משלים חבויים מאוד הכתובים בספרי נביאים Some passages in Tanach use imagery/analogy/parable — but it's not obvious that they're imagery Simple people read them at face value and think there's nothing deeper Even wise people who read them at surface level → become confused When the deeper meaning is explained → the confusion is resolved This is why it's called \"The Guide for the Perplexed\" What This Book Will NOT Do אין אני אומר שחיבור זה פותר כל קשייה Won't solve ALL your difficulties — but will solve most of them and the hardest ones Won't treat any topic exhaustively — even in person that would be impossible, all the more so in writing Won't explain every image/parable to its deepest level Why Not Exhaustive? Two Reasons Reason 1: These are matters of Ma'aseh Bereishit (physics/cosmology) and Ma'aseh Merkavah (theology/metaphysics) The Gemara says: don't teach Ma'aseh Bereishit to two people, don't teach Ma'aseh Merkavah even to one — unless he's wise, and even then only rashei perakim (headlines/rubrics) Therefore: אל תבקש ממני יותר מראשי פרקים — \"Don't expect more than general guidelines\" Reason 2: Deliberately not systematic אפילו התחלות אלו אינן מסודרות... אלא מפוזרות ומעורבות Rambam purposefully scattered and mixed the ideas throughout the book He wants the truth to shine through but also to disappear again He doesn't want to go against Hashem's intent: סוד ה' ליראיו — the secrets are for those who fear Hashem The Bombshell About Bereishit דע שגם בדברים הטבעיים אין ללמוד בפירוש Even matters of physics — the Torah doesn't state them plainly Even the Chachamim spoke about them in riddles and parables Implication: The first chapter of Bereishit = Ma'aseh Bereishit = cosmology/physics Rambam is saying: Bereishit is not to be understood at face value — it's analogy, it's imagery for something deeper Physics is a prerequisite for theology → both have secrets → both are expressed through imagery in Torah The Lightning Analogy — Levels of Understanding לעתים האמת מציצה לנו כך שאנו חושבים אותה ליום ושוב... לחזור ולהיבלע לחושך Humans grope in darkness in matters of physics and metaphysics Sometimes we get a streak of light → then darkness returns The Spectrum Level Description Example Constant lightning — night becomes day The highest prophet Moshe Rabbeinu — ואתה פה עמוד עמדי / קרן עור פניו Regular flashes throughout the night The regular prophets Yeshayahu, Yechezkel, etc. One flash in entire lifetime Minimal prophetic experience Eldad & Medad — ויתנבאו ולא יספו (prophesied once, never again) No lightning — but occasional sparks from rubbing stones No prophecy, but intellectual illumination Most of us at best — flashes that come and go, כלהט החרב המתהפכת Complete darkness — groping in the night No light at all, ever The masses — לא ידעו ולא יבינו בחשכה יתהלכו Key Points About the Analogy Prophecy is not constant — even prophets don't always experience it They \"tune in\": והיה כנגן המנגן — they would use music to enter the right state Some prophets experienced more visions than others (did Yechezkel have more than Yeshayahu? We don't know) For us without prophecy: the best we can hope for is occasional intellectual shining — how much depends on our own development Rambam's key message: The more you develop your intellect AND your character → the more light you'll receive from this book Yom Ha'atzmaut Thought Magdil vs. Migdol in Birkat HaMazon Weekdays: מגדיל ישועות מלכו (from Tehillim) — active, Hif'il → you are CAUSING salvation, bringing it about Shabbat: מגדול ישועות מלכו (from Shmuel) — completed tower → everything is whole, the redemption is complete Weekdays = ששת ימים תעשה — we work, we build, we bring about yeshuah. We are in magdil mode. Shabbat = כאילו כל מלאכתך עשויה — we exist, we BE, everything feels complete. We are in migdol mode. The prayer: that Eretz Yisrael will go from magdil (building, working) to migdol (the completed tower) Rav Kook — Why Rabbi Abba Kissed the Stones Gemara (Ketubot): Rabbi Abba would kiss the stones and dust of Eretz Yisrael when arriving Rav Kook: why specifically stones? Not the earth? If he kissed the earth → you'd think it's because of mitzvot ha-teluyot ba'aretz (agricultural mitzvot) Stones have no growth, no agricultural mitzvot → he kissed them for the intrinsic kedushah of the land itself Rav Reines — Ben Tzion The Chida: if a woman's children are dying in infancy → name the next child Ben Tzion for protection Rav Reines' metaphorical interpretation: \"Children dying\" = people dying spiritually, losing faith, losing connection to Torah \"Call them Ben Tzion\" = give them a love of Tzion, a love of Israel Because if they love Israel → subconsciously they believe in Torah (the promised land = Torah's promise) Eretz Yisrael can be the anchor that brings people back Key Takeaways The Moreh was written because Rambam's student left → the void of not teaching drove him to write. Prerequisites for understanding the Moreh: maths → astronomy → logic → then theology. Purpose 1: Explain multi-meaning, metaphorical, and ambiguous terms in Tanach. Purpose 2: Explain veiled imagery in Tanach that isn't obviously metaphorical. Written for: Religious, philosophically educated people who are perplexed between Torah and philosophy. Won't be exhaustive — only rashei perakim (headlines). Deliberately scattered, not systematic. Bereishit is not to be read at face value — it's Ma'aseh Bereishit expressed through imagery. Lightning analogy: Different people receive different amounts of intellectual/prophetic light — from Moshe's constant illumination to complete darkness. Our level depends on our own development. Next Class Continuing the Rambam's Introduction — his advice on how to study this book, and the second part of the introduction Yom Ha'atzmaut Sameach — Chag Sameach","FABfRVE9Mc0":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 7 (ענוה — Humility) — Part 1 Edition: Feldheim Recap: Chapters 1–6 Chapter 1: Two paths — the Tzaddik (basics) and the Chasid (beyond) Chapter 2: All middot are interrelated Chapter 3: Purity of motive — four levels Chapter 4: Compassion — first of the 13 Attributes. When in doubt → mercy Chapter 5: Generosity — four kinds: money, influence, intellect, faith Chapter 6: Calmness — intellect over emotion. Anger + arrogance linked; patience + humility linked. Sometimes anger is appropriate for spiritual matters. Key connection: Rabbi Avraham taught in Chapter 6 that calmness and humility are interrelated, anger and arrogance are interrelated. This is why Chapter 7 (humility) follows Chapter 6 (calmness) — the less prone to anger, the more humble you are. Note on structure: The chapters get longer as the book progresses — Rabbi Avraham seems to move from easier middot to harder ones. Chapter 7 is the longest chapter so far (expected 3–4 classes). Part 1: How Bad Arrogance Is / How Beautiful Humility Is Arrogance — Despised by God הגאוה מאוסה לפניו יתעלה Arrogance is despicable before Hashem Shlomo HaMelech: תועבת ה' כל גבה לב — it's an abomination to God Mishlei lists seven kinds of people Hashem hates — the very FIRST is arrogant people (רום עינים) Then: murderers, liars, plotters, those who rush to evil, false witnesses, those who cause fights Arrogance leads to denying God: Parashat Ekev: ורם לבבך ושכחת את ה' אלהיך — when everything goes well (nice houses, good food), you become haughty → you forget Hashem Humility — One of the Most Beautiful Traits ענוה היא מן המעולות שבמדות הטרומיות Humility = the prerequisite for completeness as a human being Shlomo: ולפני כבוד ענוה — before honour comes humility \"Kavod\" here doesn't mean honour/aggrandizement — that would be the opposite of humility Kavod = completion, wholeness of a human being (as in: וכבוד והדר תעטרהו — Tehillim about God crowning humanity with grandeur) The Zohar also calls the neshamah \"kavod\" Meaning: before you can be completed as a human → you need humility Humility brings the fear of God: עקב ענוה יראת ה' — on the heel of humility comes yir'at Hashem Part 2: The Definition of Humility Four Kinds of Humility מתחלקת לכמה וכמה סוגים Dimension Two Sides Inner vs. Outer Inner humility (in your heart) vs. outer humility (behaviour, dress, conduct) Vis-à-vis God vs. Humans Humble before Hashem vs. humble before other people Inner and Outer Must Be Symmetrical Some people act humble externally but aren't humble internally It's not enough to feel humble inside — your external actions must reflect it too David HaMelech's testimony (Tehillim): לא גבה לבי — \"My heart wasn't arrogant\" = inner humility ולא רמו עיני — \"My eyes didn't act haughty\" = outer humility אם לא שויתי ודוממתי נפשי — \"I kept silent\" = outer humility ולבי חלל בקרבי — \"My heart is hollow within me\" = inner humility The Rambam in Hilchot Melachim: Just as the Torah tells us to honour the king, so too the king is commanded to be humble: ולבי חלל בקרבי — same verse Rabbi Avraham quotes (he gets this from his father) The Rabbi Yisrael Salanter Story Saw someone praying the Amidah with great kavvanah In Elokai Netzor: ונפשי כעפר לכל תהיה — \"let my soul be like dust to everyone\" Thought: this must be a very humble person A few moments later → saw the same person arguing arrogantly with someone Asked him about it → the man said: \"Vis-à-vis Hashem I'm humble, but vis-à-vis other people I'm greater\" The lesson: It's not enough to be humble before Hashem if you're arrogant before people Vis-à-vis God AND Humans Before God: Avraham: ואנכי עפר ואפר — \"I am but dust and ash\" David: ונקלותי עוד מזאת — when dancing with the Torah, Michal criticized him → he said \"I'll be even more degraded than this\" Before other humans: David after killing Goliath → Shaul wants to marry him to Michal → David says: הנקלה בעיניכם התחתן במלך? ואנכי איש רש ונקלה — \"Who am I? I'm a simple person\" David to Shaul (who's chasing him): אחרי מי אתה רודף? אחרי כלב מת, אחרי פרעוש אחד — \"Why chase me? I'm just a flea\" Part 3: What Humility Actually Means — The Surprising Definition Arrogance Defined שהכח השכלי באדם רואה עצמו נעלה מכפי שהוא Arrogance = a person thinking about themselves more than they really are You think you deserve more than you deserve You think you're cleverer, more beautiful, calmer than you really are Rabbi Avraham equates arrogance with self-love (loving yourself excessively) Humility Defined — The Corollary ומכאן מובנת הגדרת הענוה... היא הפוכה של גאוה First statement: Humility = seeing yourself as less than you are (the opposite extreme of arrogance) But then he clarifies: אם יראה אדם עצמו פחות ממצבו בעולם או כפי שהוא באמת בלא הוסף ובלי טעות If a person sees himself as less than he really is, OR as he really is without exaggeration The True Definition Seeing yourself less than you are = a temporary measure to counterbalance arrogance (like the Rambam's method: bend to the opposite extreme for a period) The real, stable definition of humility: See yourself exactly as you are — without adding, without exaggerating Recognize your talents — don't downplay them Recognize your deficiencies — don't hide them Don't think more of yourself than you are Don't think less of yourself than you are Later in the chapter, Rabbi Avraham writes explicitly: a person shouldn't think badly of himself What Humility Is NOT אך נאירה צימת לבך על ההבדל בין מי שמזגו מחייב הענוה הגבולה או ההתנברות והשפלות Humility ≠ cowardice (מורך) Humility ≠ feeling low about yourself (נברות ושפלות) Humility ≠ debasing/embarrassing yourself (ביזיון) אדרבה ענוה פירושה שלא תינתן לגאוה ועם זאת לא תהיה שפל Humility means: don't become arrogant, but also don't become lowly The Gemara in Sotah — Can a Humble Person Say \"I'm Humble\"? End of Masechet Sotah: when someone died, the Mishnah says \"humility ceased\" One of the rabbis said: \"That's not true — I'm still around\" This shows: asserting that you're humble doesn't mean you're not humble If you've worked on yourself and genuinely recognize it → that's fine But: Going around telling everyone \"I'm a tzaddik\" = obviously not humility (what's the purpose?) The key: it's about internal honesty, not external proclamation Part 4: Nature vs. Training Born With a Tendency Like all middot: some people are born with a propensity toward humility, others toward arrogance The Rambam (Eight Chapters + Hilchot De'ot): every person has free will, but humans are born with different tendencies This doesn't excuse anyone — if you're born with more arrogance, you have a harder job, but you still must work on it The Two Extremes Reaching the highest humility: Born with propensity toward humility PLUS chinuch musari (ethical training) PLUS hitkashrut datit (spiritual/Torah training) PLUS histaklut mada'it (intellectual meditation) → Can reach the highest level of humility Reaching the worst arrogance: Born with propensity toward arrogance PLUS habits of this world (competition, bossing people around) PLUS lack of spiritual training PLUS ignorance (never studying, never reflecting) → Can reach the worst level of arrogance Most people: Somewhere in between — contradictions, sometimes working on it, sometimes not The Foolish Person vs. David HaMelech Am ha'aretz shoteh: buys fancy clothes, walks with shtolz (arrogant bearing) → Shlomo says: גם בדרך כשסכל הולך לבו חסר — \"When a fool walks, you can tell he's a fool\" Yeshayahu: בנות ציון הלכו נטויות גרון — daughters of Zion walked with necks stretched high, eyes raised Contrast with David: מי אנכי ומי חיי משפחת אבי — \"Who am I?\" Part 5: Training for Humility — Method 1: Working on Oneself External Actions That Build Inner Humility Stay away from: שררה והתנשאות — domineering, asserting authority over others קנאה ומדון — jealousy and competition התהדרות במלבושים ובמרכבה — showing off with clothes and vehicles (horses then, cars now) ישיבה בארמונות — living in the most extravagant accommodation חברים בעלי גאוה — associating with arrogant people (company is crucial) Actively pursue: הכנעה לחכמים ולשמשם — act with humility before those greater than you, serve the wise Example: Elisha poured water on Eliyahu's hands — willing to serve his master The Mishnah: הוי מתאבק בעפר רגליהם — \"Bask in the dust of the Chachamim\" סלוח למי שהרע לו — try to forgive those who wrong you סבול שמיעת החרפה — learn to bear hearing insults without reacting הצטמצם במלבושיו — dress simply, as needed — no more Example: Eliyahu wore only a hairy cloak and a belt דירה פשוטה — live simply Example: Elisha was given a small attic room The Nevi'im were content with simplicity: \"Let's take a beam and make a little place to sit\" התחבר עם ענוים — associate with humble people Example: When Shaul was with Doeg (bad company) → killed the Kohanim of Nov in anger When Shaul was with Shmuel (good company) → he was able to humble himself, threw off his royal garments, and prophesied The Principle ואלו דברים משותפים לפרישות ולענווה ולשאר סגולות המידות הטובות Working on humility also brings you to other good traits — especially abstinence Ultimately brings you toward encountering Hashem Part 6: Training Method 2 — Stories from Torah Positive Examples — Humble People Person What They Said/Did Source Avraham ואנכי עפר ואפר — \"I am dust and ash\" Bereishit 18 Yaakov קטנתי מכל החסדים — \"I don't deserve all the kindness\" Bereishit 32 Moshe מי אנכי כי אלך אל פרעה — \"Who am I to go to Pharaoh?\" (while speaking to Hashem!) Shemot 3 Moshe ונחנו מה — \"Who are we?\" Shemot 16 Moshe המקנא אתה לי — \"Don't be zealous for me\" (re: Eldad & Medad) Bamidbar 11 Moshe The Torah testifies: עניו מאד מכל האדם — the most humble of ALL humans Bamidbar 12:3 Shaul הלא בן ימיני אנכי מקטני שבטי ישראל — \"I'm from the smallest tribe\" Shmuel I 9 David ואנכי איש רש ונקלה — \"I'm a simple person\" Shmuel I 18 David אחרי כלב מת, אחרי פרעוש אחד — \"I'm just a flea\" Shmuel I 24 David צעיר אנכי ונבזה — \"I'm young and embarrassed\" Tehillim 119 Elisha Went with his students to cut wood — willing to do manual labour alongside them Melachim II 6 Hashem מרום וקדוש אשכון ואת דכא ושפל רוח — \"I dwell with the holy AND the humble\" Yeshayahu 57:15 Negative Example — Nadav and Avihu The problem with the Midrash: Chazal say one reason Nadav and Avihu died was because of their arrogance Also: הורו הלכה בפני רבם — \"they gave a halachic ruling in front of their teacher\" Rabbi Avraham says: DON'T take this at face value Nadav and Avihu were prophets and anointed with the shemen hamishchah David said: אל תגעו במשיחי ובנביאי אל תרעו — don't criticize prophets It would be a great sin to call them genuinely arrogant people Rabbi Avraham's interpretation: They weren't arrogant in any normal sense Their sin: they acted with courage and initiative WITHOUT consulting Moshe first They offered an unauthorized fire → without asking Moshe אומץ לב ללא נטילת רשות — courage without taking counsel For people on THEIR level → that tiny lack of humility = a serious deficiency \"They ruled halacha before their teacher\" doesn't mean an actual question was asked It means: they decided the answer themselves without bothering to consult They were so confident → they didn't think to ask Even the shocking Midrash — \"when will Moshe and Aharon die so we can lead?\" — same idea Not literally wishing them dead Acting as if they could make decisions independently → behaving like leaders before their time Not humble enough to recognize: there is a Moshe here — go ask him Key Takeaways Arrogance = thinking more of yourself than you are. Humility = seeing yourself as you truly are. Humility ≠ self-debasement. Don't confuse humility with cowardice, depression, or thinking badly of yourself. If you're good at something → acknowledge it. Downplaying genuine strengths is NOT humility. Humility must be both inner AND outer, vis-à-vis God AND humans. Company matters enormously. Associate with humble people; distance from arrogant ones. Work on the external (dress, behaviour, speech) → it will eventually affect the internal. Everyone is born with tendencies — some toward humility, some toward arrogance. Either way, you must work on it. Nadav and Avihu — their \"arrogance\" was not consulting Moshe. On their level, that was a fatal deficiency. Lesson: always take counsel, even when you're confident. Preview: Next Class More stories of arrogant people (Korach, Datan) Mitzvot in the Torah designed to develop humility (many surprising ones) Intellectual meditation on humility B'Ezrat Hashem","fiHy6iHBg0Q":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Siman: 319 (Borer — Selecting and Sorting on Shabbat) Seifim: 1, 2, 3, 4 (the core principles) Topics: The Three Conditions of Borer, Definition of Immediate Use, Utensils, Ochel from Psolet Background: Three Melachot of Selection Borer doesn't exist in isolation — it's one of three melachot that all involve selection/sorting. Understanding the context: The Process in the Mishkan The first 11 melachot relate to preparing dyes for the roof of the Mishkan The sequence: Plow → Plant → Harvest → Gather → Thresh (Dash) → then three kinds of selection: Melacha # Name What It Is How 6 Zoreh (Winnowing) Separating pods from seeds Using wind/air force 7 Borer (Selecting) Removing chaff/unwanted material from seeds By hand or utensil 9 Meraked (Sieving) Ensuring everything is ground properly Using a sieve After these: Tochen (grinding, melacha 8) → then sieving (melacha 9) → kneading → baking The Differences Between the Three Zoreh — Very specific: selection through wind/air force only Practical example today: blowing chaff off peanuts = de'oraita (using breath as air force to separate wanted from unwanted) Not much else to discuss — very narrow melacha Meraked — Very specific: selection using a sieve Word comes from לרקוד (to dance) — you move the sieve up and down Will be covered later in this siman Borer — The broad category: ALL other kinds of selection This is the main melacha we discuss — most practical, most commonly violated The Three Conditions of Borer Source: The Gemara (Shabbat) תנו רבנן היו לפניו מיני אוכלין בורר ואוכל בורר ומניח ולא יבור ואם בורר חייב חטאת The Baraita says three things: select, then don't select, then if you did select you're chayav The Gemara asks: what does this mean? Five answers given — first two rejected, three accepted All three accepted answers are required together — the Rishonim agree on this The Three Answers (= Three Conditions) 1. Rav Yosef — Type of Utensil: Hand = permitted Non-specialized utensil (funnel, dish) = forbidden rabbinically (patur) Specialized utensil (sieve) = forbidden de'oraita (chayav) 2. Rav Hamnuna — Direction of Selection: Selecting the wanted from the unwanted (ochel from psolet) = permitted Selecting the unwanted from the wanted (psolet from ochel) = forbidden de'oraita 3. Abaye — Time Frame: Selecting for immediate use (le'altar) = permitted Selecting for later use = forbidden de'oraita (as if selecting for storage — לאוצר) Why All Three Together = Permitted (Derech Achilah) In principle, ALL acts of selection should be forbidden — because in the Mishkan they selected But when all three conditions are met together → it's considered derech achilah (the normal way of eating) Anything that's part of the act of eating is permitted Analogy: squeezing grapes is forbidden on Shabbat → but chewing grapes in your mouth (which squeezes out juice) is fine because it's derech achilah Same principle applies across all food-related melachot The Prerequisite: There Must Be a Mixture (Ta'arovet) Borer only applies when there is an existing mixture If there's no mixture → no borer, even if you're \"sorting\" Example — No mixture: 10 identical pink lady apples, all in excellent condition Taking 5 for now and leaving 5 for later = fine — there's no mixture here What constitutes a mixture? (To be discussed in detail later) Different types of nuts in one bowl = mixture Different coloured apples? Different varieties? Salmon: fried vs. roasted vs. coated — one food or a mixture of types? Salmon with bones — is that a mixture? Or is it derech bri'ato (the way it was created)? Seif א (Aleph): Utensils — Three Levels The Shulchan Aruch's Ruling הבורר אוכל מתוך פסולת... או שהיו לפניו שני מיני אוכלין ובירר מין ממין אחר Important first point: Borer applies even when BOTH items are perfectly good food Example: bowl of pistachios and peanuts — both are ochel (edible, good) But right now I only want pistachios → the peanuts are \"psolet\" (unwanted) relative to me Ochel/psolet = wanted/unwanted — NOT necessarily good/bad The Three Levels of Utensils Utensil Type Example Status Specialized sieve (נפה וכברה) Fine sieve, coarse sieve Chayav — de'oraita Non-specialized utensil (קנון ותמחוי) Funnel, dish, cone Patur — forbidden rabbinically Hand (ביד) Your hand Mutar — permitted Why the sieve is de'oraita: Using a specialized selection utensil = derech berirah (industrial/professional level). No longer considered part of eating — it's an act of melacha. Why a non-specialized utensil is derabanan: It's not the professional way to select, but it's still using a tool — so Chachamim forbade it Why the hand is permitted: Combined with the other two conditions → it's just part of eating Question from the shiur — What about cutlery? To be discussed in detail next class — cutlery is not a specialized selection utensil. Seif ב (Bet): Immediate Use — The Time Frame The Shulchan Aruch's Ruling הבורר אוכל מתוך הפסולת בידו להניחו אפילו לבו ביום נעשה כבורר לאוצר וחייב Even with your hand, even ochel from psolet → if you leave it for later in the day = chayav de'oraita It's as if you're selecting for storage (לאוצר) The Rema: What Does \"Immediate\" Mean? וכל מה שבורר לצורך אותה סעודה שמסב בה מיד מיקרי לאלתר Anything for the upcoming meal you're about to sit down to = le'altar (immediate use) Even if it's for the second course, third course, or dessert Don't need to stop mid-meal and go prepare each course separately The norm: when you prepare a meal, you prepare ALL courses at once — that's derech achilah Key Ruling: You Can Select for Others Too ואפילו אחרים אוכלים עמו שרי You can do borer for other people — not just yourself One person can prepare and select for the whole table The Mishnah Berurah: even for someone you won't be eating with — as long as for THEM it's wanted, with your hand, for their immediate use The Rema's Practical Example ולכן מותר לברור הירק שקורין סאלאטין מן העלים המעופשים Therefore: permitted to select good lettuce leaves from bad/wilted ones when making a salad for the upcoming meal Detailed Analysis: What Does Le'altar (Immediate) Mean? Point 1: If You Didn't End Up Eating It All — Fine Made a big salad with many acts of selection → only ate half → leaving rest for seudah shlishit Permitted — because מלאכת מחשבת אסרה תורה — the Torah follows your intention at the time of the action At the time of selecting → your intention was for the upcoming meal → that's what matters Point 2: What If You Change Your Mind Immediately After? Selected Brazilian nuts for the meal → finished selecting → then decided \"too many calories, I don't want them\" Pri Megadim: Not correct to do this (doesn't explain why) Sha'ar HaTziyun (Mishnah Berurah): Disagrees — \"I don't understand his reasoning, he needs to prove this\" Chacham Ovadia: Agrees with the Mishnah Berurah — no problem Ben Ish Chai: Rules it's forbidden (like the Pri Megadim) Halacha: Permitted — at the time of the act, the intention was for immediate use. That's what counts. Caveat: If in the back of your mind you always intended to leave it for later — that's genuinely assur. You can't trick Hashem. Point 3: Preparing a Full Plate for Guests (Ben Ish Chai) Making a big platter for guests → you know they won't finish it all Permitted — the \"use\" is impressing your guests with a full table That IS the immediate use — the presentation itself Even if you're doubtful whether they'll eat any of it at all → still fine The point is: I'm putting it out NOW for you to see NOW Point 4: How Long Before the Meal? The Major Question Three opinions: Posek How Long Before the Meal? Ben Ish Chai Up to 1 hour before (understood \"sha'ah\" in the Mordechai literally) Rav Ben Tzion Abba Shaul Up to half an hour (from \"samuch le-Minchah\" = half hour) Chacham Ovadia A few minutes — literally just before Rabbi Kada's position: It's not about minutes — it's about mindset and engagement Am I NOW doing actions for THIS meal? If I have 50 guests → preparation takes 2 hours → that's all le'altar because I'm actively engaged the entire time If I have 1 person → takes 2 minutes → that's le'altar The frame: I am currently, actively, imminently preparing to eat Point 5: Chacham Ovadia on Birkat HaMazon Family does Birkat HaMazon in the middle of the meal → then wants dessert after Someone goes to prepare dessert during Birkat HaMazon Chacham Ovadia says: don't do this Birkat HaMazon = ending a meal → you're NOT about to imminently eat Even though dessert is 5 minutes away after benching Wait until after Birkat HaMazon → then do your selecting → then eat dessert This underscores: it's about mindset, not minutes Point 6: Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach — Sometimes Hours Before Is Fine Example: vegetables that need to soak in oil and lemon for 2 hours for optimal taste I want to eat at 12:00 → this needs to start soaking at 10:00 → I need to select and prepare at 9:55 Permitted — because the ONLY way to eat this at 12:00 is to prepare it at 10:00 The preparation at 10:00 IS part of eating at 12:00 → it's derech achilah Same logic: anything that's a necessary preliminary step for the meal = le'altar Point 7: Borer for Animals You can do borer for your animals as well — same conditions apply Bi'ur Halacha discusses this — quite obvious but worth noting Seif ג (Gimmel): Repetition of Principles Repeats in different forms the first two conditions (utensil + time frame) No new major chiddushim beyond Seifim 1–2 Seif ד (Dalet): Psolet from Ochel — The Third Condition The Shulchan Aruch's Ruling הבורר פסולת מתוך אוכל אפילו בידו אחת חייב Selecting the unwanted from the wanted — even with your hand, even with ONE hand — chayav de'oraita Why This Way Is Forbidden The normal act of borer (in the Mishkan): you have good material in front of you → you remove what's bad That's the industrial/professional way to select When you do the OPPOSITE (take the good out from the bad) → you're doing it in a non-professional way → it's part of eating Question from the shiur: \"Aren't these two sides of the same coin?\" Answer: The norm of borer is taking away what's unwanted from a pile of what's wanted Normally the majority = the ochel (wanted) Taking away psolet from ochel = the way a professional would do it Taking ochel from psolet = the way an eater would do it That's the conceptual distinction — even though the result is the same If You Violated Borer — Status of the Food Intentional (Be-Mezid) Standard rule of ma'aseh Shabbat: forbidden for you forever , for others until motzei Shabbat Ben Ish Chai: Rules this way — the separated food has the standard issur Can You \"Undo\" the Borer by Putting It Back? If you put it back (mix it all together again) → then re-select properly: Chacham Ovadia: Permitted — because you've removed all benefit from the original melacha Similar to: if you washed a shirt on Shabbat intentionally → can wear it again after Shabbat if you dirty it first Does putting it back count as teshuvah for the act? Ben Ish Chai: No — you go after your machshavah at the time of the act. You did the melacha. Putting it back later doesn't retroactively undo the sin. Nice gesture, but the melacha was done Unintentional (Be-Shogeg) Already learned: be-shogeg → permitted even on Shabbat itself Most cases of borer violations on Shabbat are be-shogeg Important Questions to Be Discussed in Coming Classes What counts as a \"hand\"? — cutlery, peelers, forks, spoons Borer with non-food items? — socks in a drawer, books on a shelf Solids mixed with liquids — a fly in soup One body = a mixture? — bones in chicken, stem on a tomato (derech bri'ato) How to define a \"mixture\" — different colours? different varieties? different preparations? Good from bad vs. bad from good — what if the majority is the psolet? Key Practical Summary Borer = selecting from a mixture. No mixture → no borer. Three conditions must ALL be met: hand (not a sieve), ochel from psolet, and le'altar (immediate use). Le'altar = for the upcoming meal. All courses of that meal count. Preparing for others is fine. It's about mindset, not minutes. Am I actively, imminently preparing to eat? Don't prepare dessert during Birkat HaMazon — wait until after. If food needs soaking/preparation time → selecting earlier is fine because it's a necessary step. Ochel and psolet = wanted and unwanted — not necessarily \"good\" and \"bad.\" Two good foods can be ochel/psolet relative to each other. Many people stumble with borer (Mishnah Berurah) — it's a melacha that requires careful understanding. Preview: Next Shiur Detailed laws of utensils — cutlery, peelers, strainers Ochel from psolet — defining \"wanted\" and \"unwanted\" in detail What if the majority is the unwanted? Non-food borer Solids and liquids Shabbat Shalom — Chodesh Iyar Tov","cfWq9kt6vs4":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 6 (אריכות אפים — Slow to Anger / Calmness) — Conclusion Edition: Feldheim Recap: Chapters 1–6 (Part 1) Chapter 1: Two paths — the Tzaddik (basics) and the Chasid (beyond). No point being a Chasid without the basics. Chapter 2: All middot are interrelated — working on one affects others. Chapter 3: Purity of motive — four levels, from social pressure (lowest) to serving because Hashem commanded (highest). Chapter 4: Rachamanut (compassion) — first of the 13 Attributes. Important, but sometimes cruelty is necessary. When in doubt → choose mercy. Chapter 5: Nedivut (generosity) — giving with no obligation. Four kinds: money, influence, intellect, faith. Avraham modeled all four. Chapter 6 (Part 1): Calmness ≠ apathy. Intellect must control emotion. Anger = drunkenness. Anger + arrogance go together; patience + humility go together. Training Method 1: stories from Torah (negative examples — Kayin, Eisav, the brothers, Moshe at Mei Merivah, Shaul, David). Training Method 2: Torah's mitzvot designed to prevent anger. Training Through Stories: Positive Examples of Calmness 1. Reuven — Calming the Brothers The brothers wanted to kill Yosef Reuven intervened: \"Throw him into this pit instead\" Reuven's plan: calm them down first, then bring Yosef home Result: Yosef wasn't killed — only sold Reuven used calmness to de-escalate 2. Yosef — Forgiving His Brothers The first place in Torah where someone forgives After Yaakov dies → the brothers are afraid → they make up a story that Yaakov told them to ask for forgiveness Yosef says: don't worry, it was all from Hashem He could have used his power and anger → but he recognized the yichud Hashem (everything comes from God) He was willing to overlook what they did 3. Shaul — Forgiving Those Who Disrespected Him ויאמר שאול לא יומת איש ביום הזה כי היום עשה ה' תשועה בישראל People of Yavesh Gilad wanted to surrender to Nachash king of Ammon Hashem made a miracle and they were saved Some people demanded execution: \"They were willing to surrender!\" Shaul said: no one will be killed today — Hashem has given us victory Willing to forgo personal honour 4. David — Not Killing Shaul Shaul tried to kill David multiple times David had the opportunity to kill Shaul — didn't do it Just cut his coat as a hint: \"I could have killed you but didn't\" Shaul's response: צדיק אתה ממני — \"You are more righteous than I\" 5. David — Not Punishing Shim'i ben Gera Shim'i ben Gera publicly cursed and denigrated David HaMelech David said: הניחו לו ויקלל — \"Leave him, let him curse\" Later, when Shim'i came to apologize at the Jordan → David forgave him and swore not to kill him But: At the end of his life, David commanded Shlomo to execute Shim'i Why the contradiction? This was not personal revenge — it was halachic obligation A Talmid Chacham who is publicly disgraced — the Gemara debates whether he can forgive But a king, nasi, or leader — the Torah does NOT allow them to forgo their honour David felt the timing wasn't right during his lifetime → left it to Shlomo Proof: David writes in Tehillim that he didn't take personal revenge → the command to Shlomo was din, not nekama The Chachamim on Calmness לעולם יהא אדם ענוותן כהלל ואל יהא קפדן כשמאי Always be humble like Hillel, don't be strict like Shammai עלובין ואינן עולבין שומעין חרפתן ואינן משיבין Those who sanctify Hashem's name: hear their disgrace and do not respond עלובין = they don't even feel it inside — such a high level that it doesn't affect them Hashem loves such people: ואוהביו כצאת השמש בגבורתו לעולם יהא אדם רך כקנה ואל יהא קשה כארז Be soft like a reed, not hard like a cedar That's why we write a Sefer Torah with a quill made from a reed (kaneh) — it's soft, flexible, not stubborn כל המעביר על מידותיו מעבירין לו על כל פשעיו A person who overlooks the wrongdoing of others → Hashem overlooks their wrongdoing \"Who does He forgive? The one who forgives others\" Calmness Is on the Path to Prophecy אריכות אפיים היא מתכונות הנבואה A person who is truly calm is on the way to reaching a high level of prophecy It is also one of the attributes of Hashem Himself Training Through Mitzvot: Commandments Designed to Prevent Anger 1. Arei Miklat — Cities of Refuge פן ירדוף גואל הדם אחרי הרוצח כי יחם לבבו Purpose: let everyone calm down The relative of the accidentally killed person may be so angry they can't control themselves Torah says: go to the city of refuge, a safe space — let emotions settle The Rambam in Moreh Nevuchim says the same: arei miklat = a designated place to allow people to cool down Stays there until the Kohen Gadol dies → only then can he come out 2. Mitzvat Tochachah — Rebuking Your Fellow הוכח תוכיח את עמיתך ולא תשא עליו חטא Two understandings of this pasuk: a) The Gemara's reading: When you rebuke someone, don't do it publicly (that would be bearing a sin — embarrassing them). Take them aside privately. b) The second reading (Rishonim + Rambam in Hilchot De'ot): Tell someone when they've wronged you — don't harbor the upset within yourself. ולא תשא עליו חטא = don't carry the sin/wrongdoing inside you Many people harbor displeasure within themselves → the Torah says: speak it out Rambam: מצוה עליו להודיעו — \"It is a mitzvah to tell him: why did you do this to me?\" When you speak it out → you release the negativity from within yourself 3. Lo Tikom V'Lo Titor — Don't Take Revenge, Don't Harbor The Torah prohibits revenge because revenge = exercising emotional anger Working on calmness → you won't come to revenge Netira (harboring) also prohibited — even if you don't act, the simmering is destructive 4. Don't Curse a Judge, Leader, or Deaf Person Aside from the terrible act of cursing → these prohibitions help a person not lose control of emotions The Rambam in Sefer HaMitzvot: the prohibition stems from the Torah wanting us to control our emotions When a person curses → they've lost control 5. Don't Hit Anyone We learn from Ki Tetzei: it's forbidden to hit someone beyond what's required Whether another person or a parent (even worse punishment for parents) Rabbi Avraham: the reason = you're losing control of your emotions, getting angry, not being calm 6. Don't Blaspheme A person who curses Hashem → stems from lack of emotional control 7. Helping Your Enemy's Donkey כי תראה חמור שונאך רובץ תחת משאו... עזוב תעזוב עמו Simple meaning: \"You must help him\" (the verb can mean \"to help\") Targum Onkelos reads differently: עזוב = leave — leave the anger within you, leave the negativity in your heart, and go help him The Gemara learns: if you have two people to help and can only help one — prefer the person you don't get along with → to work on removing animosity When Anger IS Appropriate — Spiritual Matters The Principle אף שתכונה הכרחית היא בעניינים התלויים ברצונותינו... אין הדבר כן בעניין הדת When it comes to personal matters → always be calm When it comes to spiritual matters → sometimes you must show anger and displeasure כל תלמיד חכם שאינו נוקם ונוטר כנחש אינו תלמיד חכם Any Talmid Chacham who doesn't take revenge like a snake = not a Talmid Chacham Resolution: לא קשיא — הא במילי דעלמא הא במילי דשמיא Personal matters → don't take revenge Spiritual matters → a person must stand up Moshe Rabbeinu — The Model When Moshe did NOT get angry (personal matters): The leaders said \"Hashem should punish you\" after conditions worsened in Egypt → Moshe didn't get angry, went back to Hashem and said: \"Help them, they're right\" Eldad and Medad prophesied outside the plan → Yehoshua ran to Moshe upset → Moshe said: המקנא אתה לי ומי יתן כל עם ה' נביאים — \"If only everyone had such enthusiasm\" Miriam spoke derogatorily about Moshe → she was punished with tzara'at → Moshe still prayed for her: אל נא רפא נא לה When Moshe DID get angry (spiritual matters): The Golden Calf → ויחר אף משה וישלך מידיו את הלוחות — threw the tablets The sons of Elazar and Itamar didn't eat the chatat properly → Moshe got angry (even though they were later proven right) The war against Midian → soldiers kept the girls alive against Hashem's command → Moshe got angry The pattern: Personal → forgo. Spiritual → stand up. Other Examples of Righteous Anger Pinchas — killed Zimri and Cozbi for public immorality → rewarded with priesthood Shmuel — cursed Shaul for not killing Agag/Amalek as commanded → executed Agag himself Shaul — decreed death on Yonatan for eating during the fast (spiritual matter, even his own son) David — ordered the death of the man who claimed to kill Shaul (even though David benefited) and Rechab and Baanah who killed Ish-Boshet Shlomo — killed his brother Adoniyahu for rebelling against the monarchy Eliyahu — killed 450 false prophets of Baal → קנא קנאתי לה' — \"I was zealous for Hashem\" Elisha — spoke harshly to King Yehoram for being an idol worshipper Note on Eliyahu and Pinchas: Some Midrashim say they're the same person. Rabbi Avraham says either way the point stands — if the same person, it's another example of Pinchas' zealotry; if different, you have two examples. The Chachamim on Righteous Anger Shammai — was very strict in religious matters. Chachamim say don't be like Shammai — but you see he stood up for what was right Rav Kahana — killed an informer who was going to endanger Jewish lives by reporting to the authorities. Rav told him: flee to Bavel The Gemara says: האי צורבא מרבנן דרתח אורייתא הוא דקמרתח ליה — \"When a Chacham seems angry, it's the Torah burning within him\" The Mishnah: הוי עז כנמר... וגבור כארי לעשות רצון אביך שבשמים — sometimes you need to be bold like a leopard and strong like a lion Critical Qualifications 1. Not Everyone Should Be Telling People Off מי שרחוק מלכפות חובות התורה על זולתו... אסור לצאת מגדר סבלנות If you don't know how to do it properly → stay out of it If it's not your responsibility → stay out of it If you don't have a relationship with the person → stay out of it The Chatam Sofer on הוכח תוכיח את עמיתך: \"amitecha\" = your friend. You can't rebuke someone unless you have a relationship first. Create the relationship → only then can you influence. 2. Even When Appropriate — Show Anger, Don't BE Angry The Rambam in Mishneh Torah and Moreh Nevuchim: when you need to discipline or rebuke → show yourself as angry, but remain fully in control internally Your intellect still runs the show You're acting angry — not being angry 3. On Balance — Calmness Should Far Outweigh Anger אפילו בענייני הדת לא ימהר למקום להעניש אלא לאחר בירור אורך רוח וחקירה ודרישה Even in spiritual matters → don't rush to punish Investigate thoroughly first Even with avodah zarah (the worst sin): ודרשת וחקרת ושאלת היטב — investigate, interrogate, ask well Hashem's attributes: more are about mercy than judgment → so should ours be A person who gets angry at everything → it's not genuine A person who never gets angry → also not good True character: doesn't get angry about personal matters, but stands up for spiritual matters Does Hashem Get Angry? The Answer: No — It's a Borrowed Expression כי לא תחול בו מידת הכעס ולא מידה אחרת Hashem is above feelings — any mature understanding of God doesn't allow for God to \"get angry\" Whenever Torah says ויחר אף ה' → it means Hashem is about to punish The Radak (Rabbi David Kimchi) on the pasuk: \"They think they're making Me angry — but they're only punishing themselves\" Proof: הניחה לי ויחר אפי בהם After the Golden Calf, Hashem says to Moshe: \"Allow Me to get angry and destroy them\" If Hashem were truly angry → He wouldn't ask permission When you're angry, you don't say \"please allow me to get angry\" — you just get angry Therefore: הניחה לי ויחר אפי = הניחה לי ואכלה אותם = \"Allow Me to punish them\" Proof from Devarim (Parshat Eikev): When Moshe retells the story, he says הרף ממני ואשמידם — \"Leave Me alone and I will destroy them\" — no mention of anger at all This confirms: \"anger\" was never the point — punishment was The Principle דיברה תורה כלשון בני אדם — The Torah speaks in human language Every time Torah uses \"anger\" about Hashem → it means punishment/retribution The Rambam explains this at length in the Moreh Nevuchim and Mishneh Torah Key Takeaways — Full Chapter 6 Summary Calmness ≠ apathy. It means having feelings but being in control of them. Training Method 1: Meditate on stories — both negative (anger → destruction) and positive (calmness → reward). Training Method 2: Think about the mitzvot designed to prevent anger — arei miklat, tochachah, lo tikom, not cursing, not hitting, helping enemies. Personal matters → always be calm. Spiritual matters → sometimes you must stand up. The Moshe model: Never angry for himself. Always angry for Hashem's honour. Not everyone should rebuke. Only if you have the ability, responsibility, AND relationship. Even when showing anger → remain in full control. Show anger, don't be angry. On balance → calmness should far outweigh anger. Investigate thoroughly before acting. Hashem doesn't \"get angry\" — it's a borrowed expression meaning punishment. We should aspire to the same: when we \"get angry\" for spiritual matters, it's really about standing up for what's right. Next Chapter Chapter 7: ענוה — Humility (the longest chapter yet — expected 3–4 classes)","luw1nahsHiA":"Siman 489 (Continued) Se'if 7 — Forgetting to Count at Night: Counting During the Day Shulchan Aruch: If you forgot to count the entire night — count during the day without a bracha . Background: The Machloket Opinion Ruling Reasoning Majority of Rishonim (including Rambam) Count during the day with a bracha Temimot means count at the beginning of the night le-chatchila. But just as counting at the end of the night (before dawn) is valid with a bracha, so too during the day. Ba'al Halachot Gedolot (Bahag) — R. Shimon Kayara, ~8th century Cannot count with a bracha during the day Temimot (completeness) means counting must be within the nighttime boundary. Dawn to dawn is the outer limit. Once day comes, the temimot is lost for that night. Halacha (Shulchan Aruch): Count during the day without a bracha — accommodating the Bahag's stringency. This is the accepted practice. Subsequent nights: Continue counting with a bracha from the following night onward. Even according to the Bahag, counting during the day preserves the continuity — you haven't missed a full 24-hour period. Origin of counting at Shacharit: The Sephardi minhag of counting the Omer during Shacharit (morning prayers) developed from this — as a safety net for those who forgot to count at night. According to the majority of Rishonim, counting during the day is fully valid. Se'if 8 — Missing an Entire Day: The Major Machloket Shulchan Aruch: If you forgot an entire day (didn't count at night and didn't count the following day) — whether it was the first day or any other day — continue counting subsequent nights without a bracha . But if you're doubtful whether you missed a day, continue counting with a bracha . The Core Debate: One Mitzvah or 49 Separate Mitzvot? This is a debate going back to the Geonim (pre-Rishonim era): Opinion View Consequence of Missing a Day Bahag (Ba'al Halachot Gedolot) The 49 days form one unit. Temimot demands completeness across all 49. Missing a full day breaks the temimot. Cannot continue with a bracha. Rav Hai Gaon Each night is a separate mitzvah . Missing one night doesn't affect subsequent nights. Continue with a bracha. Rav Hai Gaon's two answers to \"What about temimot?\": You can \"complete\" the temimot by counting the missed day retroactively the next night — e.g., say \"Tonight is 32\" and then add \"and yesterday was 31.\" (Not accepted in practice.) Temimot simply teaches that you should count at the beginning of the night — it doesn't mean missing one day invalidates the entire count. The Final Halacha Scenario Ruling Definitely missed an entire day Continue counting every night — but without a bracha (because of the Bahag). Do NOT stop counting! Doubtful whether you missed Continue counting with a bracha — because of sfek sfeka (double doubt). The Sfek Sfeka (Double Doubt) Explained When you're doubtful whether you counted: Doubt 1: Did I count yesterday or not? Perhaps I did. Doubt 2: Even if I didn't count — perhaps the halacha follows Rav Hai Gaon that each night is a separate mitzvah and I can continue with a bracha anyway. Two independent doubts = sfek sfeka = may be lenient even with a potentially de'oraita matter. This principle extends: Any doubt about the count (e.g., you only counted days but not weeks) is sufficient to allow continued counting with a bracha. Only a definite missed 24-hour period triggers counting without a bracha. As learned last week: Even counting from after Plag HaMincha (before sunset) is sufficient to avoid \"missing a day\" — allowing continued counting with a bracha on subsequent nights. Practical Applications of Se'if 8 1. Knowing in Advance You'll Miss a Night Question: If I know I have surgery scheduled and will be unconscious for a full day during the Omer — should I even begin counting with a bracha on night one? The Chida's opinion: Don't begin counting with a bracha if you know you'll miss a day. Majority of Poskim (accepted halacha): Count with a bracha from night one. When the missed day comes, stop saying the bracha from then on. Why? Even according to the Bahag, there is a mitzvah each night. The bracha is said each night (not once at the beginning for all 49). This proves each night has independent value. The Bahag's point is only that once you miss a day, the temimot aspect is lost and you can't say a bracha — but there's still a mitzvah to count. 2. A Bar Mitzvah Boy Scenario: A boy counted every night of the Omer as chinuch (educational obligation). He becomes Bar Mitzvah mid-Omer. Can he continue counting with a bracha? Opinion Ruling Reasoning Chacham Ovadia Count without a bracha The pre-Bar Mitzvah countings were a lower-level obligation (chinuch). Not the same as full adult obligation. Lacks temimot. Kaf HaChaim, Rav Ben Tzion Abba Shaul, many Ashkenazi Poskim (majority) Count with a bracha Multiple reasons (see below) Reasons to allow the Bar Mitzvah boy to continue with a bracha: Factual completeness: He has physically counted every night. Even if the obligation was different, the count itself is complete. Not coming from nothing: Unlike a convert (see below), the child had a genuine obligation — chinuch. It's a real obligation, just a different category. Neder from age 12: From age 12, a child's vows have halachic validity. By counting every night, he's accepted it upon himself like a neder, creating a chiyuv even before Bar Mitzvah. Sfek sfeka: Even without all the above — there's still the fundamental doubt whether each night is a separate mitzvah (Rav Hai Gaon). Combined with the other reasons, there's plenty to rely on. 3. A Convert (Ger) Scenario: A person converts mid-Omer. Even if they counted every night before conversion — can they continue with a bracha? Halacha: Count without a bracha for the rest of that year's Omer. Why is this different from a Bar Mitzvah boy? Before conversion, the person had no obligation whatsoever. Not even chinuch. The pre-conversion counting was meaningless halachically — there was no mitzvah being performed at all. Therefore there's no temimot and no basis for a bracha. 4. An Onen (Before Burial of a Close Relative) Scenario: A close relative (one of the seven relatives) dies. The person is an onen — exempt from all positive mitzvot. If burial is delayed beyond 24 hours, the person will miss an entire day of counting. Halacha: The onen should count without a bracha , even though an onen normally cannot perform mitzvot. Why is this permitted? The reason for onen exemption: To ensure full focus on honoring the deceased and arranging burial. Counting the Omer takes literally 10 seconds — it won't detract from this focus. (Unlike the full range of brachot and prayers, which would.) Minority opinion: Some hold an onen is only exempt from mitzvot, not forbidden . If he chooses to, he can. We can factor this in. Special concern: If he misses a full day, he'll lose the bracha for the rest of the Omer. This added consideration justifies allowing the brief counting. What to say: Just the counting itself. No Leshem Yichud, no HaRachaman, no LaMenatzeach, no Ana BeKoach — just the bare count. \"Today is X days to the Omer, which is Y weeks and Z days.\" Se'if 9 — Shabbat, Yom Tov, and Motzaei Shabbat Shulchan Aruch: Situation When to Count Leil Shabbat / Leil Yom Tov Count after Kiddush in the synagogue (because we want to bring in Shabbat/Yom Tov as early as possible — Kiddush first) Motzaei Shabbat / Motzaei Yom Tov Count before Havdalah (because we want to draw out Shabbat/Yom Tov as long as possible — delay Havdalah) When last day of Pesach falls on Motzaei Shabbat (as this year) Count before the combined Kiddush/Havdalah — even though Kiddush is being said, the Havdalah element means we delay it to extend Shabbat Se'if 10 — The Prohibition of Chadash (New Produce) Shulchan Aruch: It is forbidden to eat chadash (new produce) even nowadays — whether bread, parched grain, or any preparation of the five grains — until the beginning of the 18th of Nissan (outside Israel) or the 17th of Nissan (in Israel). What Is Chadash? The Torah (Vayikra 23:14): \"Ve-lechem ve-kali ve-charmel lo tochlu ad etzem ha-yom ha-zeh, ad havi'achem et korban Elokeichem\" — You shall not eat bread, parched grain, or fresh grain until the day you bring the Omer sacrifice. The concept: Before partaking of the primary sustenance (the five grains) each year, we must recognize it comes from Hashem by first bringing the Korban Omer. How it works: When Did It Take Root? Status Before the 16th of Nissan (before Pesach) Permitted once the Omer is brought / once the date passes After the 16th of Nissan (after Pesach) Forbidden (chadash) until the following Pesach passes Applies only to: The five grains (wheat, barley, spelt, oats, rye). Does not apply to fruit, vegetables, rice, etc. Nowadays Without the Omer Sacrifice Location When Chadash Becomes Permitted Eretz Yisrael Beginning of the night of 17th Nissan (3rd night of Pesach) Outside Israel Beginning of the night of 18th Nissan (4th night of Pesach) — because of two-day Yom Tov Why later without the Temple? When the Omer was brought on the morning of the 16th, produce was permitted immediately that afternoon. Without the sacrifice, we must wait until the entire day passes. Does Chadash Apply Outside Israel? The key phrase: \"B'chol moshvoteichem\" — \"in all your dwelling places.\" Interpretation Meaning Who Holds This Everywhere — Israel and abroad Chadash applies internationally Rambam, Rif, Rosh, Ran (vast majority of Rishonim) Only after settling the Land Chadash applies only in Eretz Yisrael Rabbeinu Baruch of Magentza (Ba'al HaTerumah) — minority Why Were Many Ashkenazim Historically Lenient? The problem: In Europe (especially Poland), the agricultural cycle was different from the Middle East. \"Spring wheat\" was planted after Pesach, took root after Pesach, and was harvested months later — creating a widespread chadash problem. The Bach (R. Yoel Sirkis, ~500 years ago) justified the lenient practice with two arguments: B'chol moshvoteichem means only in Israel (following the minority opinion of Ba'al HaTerumah). Perhaps chadash only applies to produce of a Jew — not a non-Jew's produce. The Rama's sfek sfeka: Doubt 1: Is this produce from this year or stored from last year? Doubt 2: Even if from this year, perhaps it took root before Pesach? The problem with this sfek sfeka: Both \"doubts\" are really one question — is this produce chadash or yashan? You haven't created two genuinely independent doubts. A valid sfek sfeka requires two separate, independent doubts (like the Omer example: Did I count? + Is each night a separate mitzvah?). The Acharonim say this sfek sfeka is very difficult to accept. The Mishnah Berurah's Conclusion The Mishnah Berurah writes: \"Rov ha-olam einam chosheshim klal be-issur chadash\" — most people aren't careful about chadash at all. He acknowledges the leniencies exist but says: one should not rely on them . According to the Rambam, Rif, and Rosh, this is an issur de'oraita . Those who are lenient have what to rely on — but one should be particular. Important principle (from the Rav): Distinguish between two types of leniencies: Interpretation-based: \"My reading of the law is lenient\" — legitimate. Justification-based: \"Everyone's already doing it, so we need to find a heter\" — problematic. The chadash leniencies fall into the second category — justifications after the fact, not independent legal interpretations. Practical Guidance for Today Time of Year Concern Level After Pesach through summer No concern. Any flour/grains on shelves now certainly took root before Pesach. Beginning of winter (Oct–Dec onwards) Increasing concern. New-harvest grains may enter the market. Check kashrut notifications. For Sephardim Must be particularly strict — no basis for the Ashkenazi leniencies. Tip: If you want to avoid all concern — stock up on flour and grain products earlier in the year. Pay attention to kashrut authority notifications in winter months about which products may have chadash issues. Historical note: Dayan Berkovits z\"l of the Federation (London) was among the first kashrut authorities (late 1980s/early 1990s) to insist that all licensees and restaurants use only yashan produce. This awareness has grown significantly in the last 20–30 years. Siman 493 — Customs of the Omer Period Se'if 1 — No Weddings During the Omer Shulchan Aruch: It is customary not to marry between Pesach and Shavuot, until Lag la-Omer (the 33rd day), because the students of Rabbi Akiva died during this time. Important notes: Engagement (erusin/kiddushin) and engagement parties are permitted. If someone went ahead and married — they are not punished. (It is after all a minhag, not mentioned in the Talmud. Recorded from the late Geonim / early Rishonim. Not mentioned in the Rambam.) Tradition When Can You Marry? Sephardim (Shulchan Aruch) From the 34th day (Lag la-Omer itself is still forbidden — they died on the 33rd) Ashkenazim (Rama) From Lag la-Omer (the 33rd day) onward Se'if 2 — Haircuts and Shaving Shulchan Aruch: It is customary not to have haircuts until Lag la-Omer. Sephardi halacha: Must wait until the 34th day in the morning . Cannot have a haircut during the 33rd (Lag la-Omer) itself. Exception: If Lag la-Omer falls on a Friday — haircuts are permitted on Friday morning in honor of Shabbat (kevod Shabbat). This Year's Application Lag la-Omer falls on Thursday night / Friday. Therefore: Haircuts and shaving: Permitted from Friday morning — for both Sephardim and Ashkenazim — because of kevod Shabbat. Weddings: Also permitted on Friday for the same reason. Summary of Key Practical Rulings Topic Ruling Forgot to count at night Count during the day without a bracha. Continue subsequent nights with a bracha. Missed an entire day Continue counting every night without a bracha. Do NOT stop counting. Doubtful whether you missed Continue counting with a bracha (sfek sfeka). Know you'll miss a day in advance Still begin counting with a bracha. When you miss, stop bracha from then on. Bar Mitzvah boy Majority: continue with a bracha (if counted every night before). Chacham Ovadia: without a bracha. Convert (ger) Count without a bracha for the rest of that year. Onen Count without a bracha (bare counting only — no additions). Shabbat/Yom Tov Count after Kiddush. Motzaei Shabbat: count before Havdalah. Chadash Forbidden de'oraita even nowadays, even outside Israel (majority opinion). Be particular in winter months. Sephardim especially must be strict. Weddings during Omer Sephardim: from 34th day. Ashkenazim: from Lag la-Omer (33rd). Haircuts this year Permitted from Friday morning of Lag la-Omer (falls on Friday — kevod Shabbat).","MTnL8q9yaWM":"Recap of Class 1 Topic Key Point The Omer sacrifice Night of 16th Nisan: harvest barley → grind → morning: Kohen waves an Omer measure → takes kometz → burns on Mizbeach. From then, new produce permitted. De'oraita or de-rabbanan? Rambam: de'oraita even nowadays. Rosh/Ran (majority): de-rabbanan (counting linked to sacrifice). Shulchan Aruch: de-rabbanan. Why count upward? Each day has intrinsic value — building toward Shavuot, not passively waiting. Why no Shehecheyanu? (1) Sadness — only a remembrance without the Temple. (2) Hechsher mitzvah — Shehecheyanu on Shavuot covers it. La-Omer vs. Ba-Omer Sephardim: la-Omer (from the Omer sacrifice). Ashkenazim: ba-Omer (in the Omer period). Neither is me'akev. When to count Best: nightfall. Valid: from sunset (de-rabbanan, so bein ha-shmashot is fine). After Arvit, unless Arvit is very late. Women Exempt (time-bound). Sephardim: count without bracha. Ashkenazim: divided. Must understand the count Unique to Omer — if you don't understand the Hebrew numbers, count in English. Why no double counting Unclear counting = not a counting at all. Calendrical doubt only observed when it doesn't impair another mitzvah. Siman 489 (Continued) Se'ifim 2–3 — The Earliest Time to Count: A Detailed Analysis These se'ifim form one connected sugya about the earliest permissible time for counting the Omer. Se'if 2 — The Cloudy Day Case (The Rashba) Shulchan Aruch (quoting the Rashba): If it was a cloudy day and people mistakenly counted the Omer thinking it was already nightfall — they must recount (chozrim li-spor) when it actually gets dark. Additionally: \"U-medakdekim einam sofrim ad tzeit ha-kochavim, ve-chein ra'ui la'asot\" — meticulous people don't count until nightfall, and so is the fitting practice. Understanding the Rashba's Case Three time windows in the evening: Window Status Before Plag HaMincha (~1.25 halachic hours before sunset) Too early for any nighttime mitzvah — counting here is meaningless for everyone After Plag HaMincha, before sunset Disputed — see below After sunset, before nightfall (bein ha-shmashot) Valid le-chatchila (Omer is de-rabbanan → safek de-rabbanan le-kula) After nightfall (tzeit ha-kochavim) Best practice — satisfies even the Rambam (de'oraita) What exactly happened in the Rashba's case? They thought it was nightfall, but it was actually only after Plag HaMincha, before sunset . We know this because: If it were before Plag HaMincha → the Rashba wouldn't need to say anything; it's obviously meaningless. If it were after sunset → the Shulchan Aruch holds you can count from sunset (de-rabbanan); there'd be no need to recount. The Rashba also says: don't repeat Arvit (because Arvit after Plag is valid), but repeat the Omer. This only makes sense if the mistake was counting after Plag but before sunset. Conclusion from Se'if 2: Counting after Plag HaMincha but before sunset → must recount when it gets dark. Counting after sunset → valid (me'ikar ha-din). Counting after nightfall → best practice (medakdekim). Se'if 3 — Counting with the Community \"Mibe'od Yom\" Shulchan Aruch: If you are praying with the community \"mibe'od yom\" (while it is still day) — count with them without a bracha . If you remember later at night, say the bracha and recount. Rama adds: Even if you answered amen to the community's bracha on the Omer — if you intended not to fulfill the mitzvah (da'ato shelo latzeit), you should repeat with a bracha at night. The Major Dispute: What Does \"Mibe'od Yom\" Mean? Two readings among the Acharonim, with major practical consequences: Reading 1 — The Vilna Gaon, Taz, Pri Megadim, Magen Avraham: \"Mibe'od yom\" = from sunset (not its usual meaning). They are forced to this reading because: if it meant from Plag HaMincha, there'd be no value in counting at all (since Se'if 2 shows counting before sunset is worthless). So the Shulchan Aruch must be talking about counting from sunset — a valid but non-ideal time. What comes out: Counting from Plag HaMincha is absolutely nothing . Count from sunset le-chatchila (valid), best from nightfall. If in a community counting from sunset, count without bracha and stipulate: \"This only counts if I forget later.\" Reading 2 — Maamar Mordechai, Eliyahu Rabbah, other Acharonim: \"Mibe'od yom\" = its normal meaning = from Plag HaMincha . The Shulchan Aruch is addressing communities in the northern hemisphere that adopted counting from Plag HaMincha because nightfall was so late people would forget. Although not ideal, there is some value to counting after Plag — a minority opinion supports it. What comes out: Counting from Plag is extremely be-di'avad but not worthless. You shouldn't do it with a bracha, but it has some value as a fallback. The Major Nafka Mina (Practical Difference) Scenario Reading 1 (Vilna Gaon) Reading 2 (Maamar Mordechai) Counted after Plag, before sunset, then forgot to recount at night Counting was worthless. Missed a full day. Cannot continue with bracha next night. Counting has some value. Can continue with bracha next night. Se'if 2 — recount on cloudy day with bracha? Yes — with bracha. What you counted from Plag was nothing. No — without bracha. There's safek whether the Plag counting had value. Final Halacha (Kaf HaChaim, Rav Ovadia) Rule Details Le-chatchila Do not count before sunset with a bracha. If you counted from Plag with a bracha Do not repeat the bracha later (safek berachot le-hakel). But do repeat the counting without a bracha after sunset/nightfall. If you counted from Plag and forgot to recount all day You can continue counting subsequent nights with a bracha — relying on the opinion that Plag counting has some value. In a community counting from Plag Count with them without a bracha. Stipulate: \"This only counts if I forget later.\" If you remember at night — count with bracha. Practical Summary of Earliest Times Time Status Before Plag HaMincha Meaningless. Not a counting at all. After Plag HaMincha, before sunset Extremely be-di'avad. No bracha. Only as emergency fallback. After sunset (bein ha-shmashot) Valid le-chatchila . Me'ikar ha-din this is fine. After nightfall (tzeit ha-kochavim) Best practice — satisfies even the Rambam. Se'if 4 — Being Asked \"What Night Is It?\" Shulchan Aruch: If someone asks you during bein ha-shmashot (twilight), \"What night of the Omer is it tonight?\" — you should answer: \"Yesterday was X.\" Why? Because if you say \"Tonight is 26\" — you have effectively counted, and you can no longer recount with a bracha. However — before bein ha-shmashot (before sunset): You can freely say what tonight's count will be. Since it's not yet the time of counting, it means nothing halachically. Practical Workaround If asked after sunset and you haven't yet counted, you can say just the number without \"tonight\" or \"hayom\": ✅ \"26\" — just a number, not a counting. ❌ \"Tonight is 26\" or \"Hayom 26 yom\" — this is a counting. Why does saying \"26\" work? A counting requires a statement that tonight/today is X days. A bare number in response to a question, without \"tonight\" or \"hayom,\" doesn't constitute a formal count — even if you say \"la-Omer\" or \"ba-Omer\" after the number. Se'if 4 (Rama) — Eating Before Counting Rama: Once the time to count has arrived, it is forbidden to eat until you have counted. Three scenarios: Scenario Ruling Began eating before the time arrived (e.g., started Shabbat meal before sunset) ✅ Continue eating. Finish the meal, say Birkat HaMazon, then count. No need to interrupt. The time arrived and you haven't begun eating yet ❌ Do not begin. Count first, then eat. Began eating after the time arrived (shouldn't have, but did) Stop eating. Count. Then continue eating. What Counts as \"The Time\"? Opinion \"The Time\" Is... Mishnah Berurah Nightfall. So you can begin eating between sunset and nightfall. Chacham Ovadia Sunset. Even from sunset you cannot begin eating. Additional stringency (Mishnah Berurah): Even half an hour before the time of counting, you shouldn't begin a meal — similar to bedikat chametz. In practice, this means about half an hour before nightfall (which usually falls around sunset). Friday Night Application (Most Practically Relevant) This is when people forget the Omer most — especially in London/England. Typical scenario: Pray Mincha-Arvit at 7:00 PM. Get home around 8:00. Sunset is 8:34. You start Shalom Aleichem, Eshet Chayil, blessings for children, Kiddush — all before sunset. Then begin the meal. The halacha: Since you began eating before sunset (before the time), you can continue your meal through nightfall without interrupting. Count after Birkat HaMazon. Note: \"Eating\" means sitting down with bread (a formal meal). Kiddush, Shalom Aleichem, the blessings — these are not \"eating.\" You can do all of these even after the time arrives, then count, then wash and begin the meal. The Friday Night Eitzah (Practical Advice) If you frequently forget to count on Friday nights (no phone reminder): After Arvit in synagogue on Friday evening (still before sunset), say to yourself: \"I am now counting the Omer for tonight — without a bracha — and this counting should only be valid if I forget to count later tonight.\" If you remember later → count with a bracha (the earlier counting was stipulated as nothing). If you forget → at least you counted from after Plag HaMincha, and you can continue counting with a bracha on subsequent nights (per the second opinion). Se'if 5 — Not Knowing What Night It Is Shulchan Aruch: If you don't know what night it is, you can say the bracha intending to hear the count from your friend and repeat after him. You listen, he counts, you repeat — yatza (fulfilled the mitzvah). However — le-chatchila you should not do this. Two reasons: Reason Source Hefsek (interruption): There will be a pause between your bracha and your counting (while you wait to hear your friend) General concern Must know the count during the bracha (stronger reason): When saying the bracha, you should already know what you're blessing for — not just \"Omer\" generically, but which specific night The Taz Practical takeaway: Before saying the bracha, make sure you know what night it is. If you're unsure — ask first (\"Yesterday was...?\"), then count. Se'if 6 — Wrong Night in Mind During the Bracha Shulchan Aruch: Two scenarios: Scenario A: Wrong intention → correct counting You began the bracha thinking tonight is 4 (wrong). By the time you finished and actually counted, you realized it's 5 and counted 5. Ruling: Yatza — you've fulfilled the mitzvah. No need to repeat. At the end of the day, you counted correctly. Scenario B: Correct intention → wrong counting You began the bracha correctly thinking tonight is 4 (correct). But when you actually counted, you mistakenly said 5. Shulchan Aruch says: Eino chozer u-mevarech — you don't need to repeat the bracha. Almost all Acharonim disagree with this ruling. How can you not repeat? You counted the wrong night! Of course you need to recount with a bracha. The Taz's Reading (Resolving the Difficulty) The Taz reinterprets Scenario B. He says the Shulchan Aruch means: You began the bracha thinking tonight is 4 (correct). You finished the bracha still thinking correctly (tonight is 4). But then you miscounted and said 5. Since during the bracha itself your intention was correct throughout, the bracha stands. Just recount the correct number without a new bracha. On the Taz's reading: If the entire bracha from beginning to end was said with the wrong night in mind → the bracha is wasted and must be repeated. What Comes Out for Practice During the Bracha At the Count Must Repeat Bracha? Part of bracha had wrong night, part had right night Counted correctly ❌ No — bracha valid Part of bracha had wrong night, part had right night Counted wrong Recount correctly, no new bracha needed Entire bracha had wrong night Counted correctly ❌ No — the correct counting saves it (majority opinion) Entire bracha had wrong night Counted wrong ✅ Yes — must repeat bracha and recount (Taz) Le-chatchila: Always have the correct night in mind when saying the bracha. If unsure — better to have no specific intention than the wrong intention. Summary of Key Practical Rulings Topic Ruling Earliest time Sunset le-chatchila. Nightfall is best (medakdekim). Plag HaMincha only as extreme fallback without bracha. If asked \"what night?\" After sunset: say \"yesterday was X\" or just the number without \"tonight.\" Before sunset: say freely. Eating before counting Don't begin eating once the time arrives. If already eating from before: continue, count after Birkat HaMazon. Friday nights Start meal before sunset → finish → count after bentching. Safety net: count without bracha after Arvit with a tenai. Don't know what night Ask first, then count. Le-chatchila: know the night before the bracha. Wrong night in mind If counted correctly at the end: fine. If counted wrong: must recount. Whole bracha with wrong intention: problematic (Taz).","JqpIYyJGgO4":"Introduction — What Is the Omer? The Sacrifice of the Omer On the night after Pesach (the 16th of Nisan) — in Temple times there was only one day of Pesach — the following procedure took place: Night of the 16th: The barley was harvested (cut) in a public ceremony. This was done emphatically and repeated three times to make clear that \"mimacharat ha-Shabbat\" means the night after Pesach — not Saturday night, as the Tzedukim (Sadducees) claimed. This is the origin of the declaration some communities recite on the first night of counting. During the night: The barley was ground into flour. Morning of the 16th (first day of Chol HaMoed): The ground barley was brought to the Kohen. The Kohen performed tenufah (waving) of the Omer, then took a kometz (three fingers' worth) and burned it on the altar. From that moment: It became permitted to eat from the new produce (chadash) of that year. Before the Omer sacrifice, there is a Torah prohibition against eating new harvest grain. This prohibition still applies today (Se'if 10 discusses this). Why Is It Called \"Omer\"? The Torah says: \"ve-ha'Omer asirit ha-eifah hu\" (Beshalach) — an Omer is a measurement, one-tenth of an eifah. The amount of barley waved and sacrificed was an Omer, and the name stuck. The Mitzvah of Counting The Torah (Emor): \"U-sefartem lachem mimacharat ha-Shabbat, mi-yom havi'achem et omer ha-tenufah, sheva shabbatot temimot tiheyenah\" — Count from the day you bring the Omer offering, seven complete weeks. So on the night of the 16th — the same night the barley was harvested — we begin counting. This continues for 49 nights until Shavuot. The Major Machloket: Is Counting the Omer De'oraita or De-rabbanan Nowadays? Since we no longer have the Beit HaMikdash and therefore no Omer sacrifice, is the counting still a Torah obligation? Opinion Ruling Reasoning The Rosh and Ran (majority of Rishonim) De-rabbanan nowadays The Torah says \"u-sefartem lachem mi-yom havi'achem et omer ha-tenufah\" — count from when you bring the Omer. Counting and sacrifice are intrinsically linked . No sacrifice = no Torah-level counting. The Rambam (minority, but it's the Rambam) De'oraita even nowadays The Torah is telling you when to count, not linking the two mitzvot. They begin on the same night but are independent. No sacrifice doesn't affect the counting obligation. The Shulchan Aruch (Se'if 2): Rules me'ikar ha-din like the majority — Sefirat HaOmer is de-rabbanan nowadays. Why Does This Matter? The biggest practical difference is timing : If De'oraita (Rambam) If De-rabbanan (Rosh/Ran) Must count only after nightfall (tzeit ha-kochavim) — just like all Torah mitzvot done at night Can count already from sunset (bein ha-shmashot) — safek de-rabbanan le-kula Other differences arise throughout the Shulchan Aruch (e.g., what happens if you miss a night). Why Do We Count Upwards? The Sefer HaChinuch: Counting the Omer is preparation for Shavuot — just like someone counting down to an exciting event. The obvious question: If we're excited about Shavuot, shouldn't we count downwards? \"49 days to go, 48 days to go...\" The answer: Counting downward means the days in between have no intrinsic value — you're just letting time pass. Counting upward (1, 2, 3...) shows that each day has its own value. You're not waiting for Shavuot passively — you're actively building toward it, making each day count. Analogy: An employer says: \"Work for 100 days and earn £100,000 — £1,000 per day, but you have to show up.\" The employee comes home each night and says: \"One day done — £1,000 earned. Two days — £2,000.\" He counts upward because each day represents real, earned value. Counting \"99 days left\" would miss the point — you have to do the work. In Kabbalistic literature: This period between Pesach and Shavuot is the greatest time of the year — even greater than Elul — for building one's spiritual identity. Why No Shehecheyanu? Two reasons given: Reason 1 (based on the Rosh — de-rabbanan): Since the counting today is only a zecher (remembrance) of the Temple-era mitzvah, there is an element of sadness — we don't have the full mitzvah. This is also the origin of saying \"HaRachaman hu yachzir avodat Beit HaMikdash li-mekomah\" after counting — praying for the return of the Temple so the mitzvah can be fulfilled mi-de'oraita. Reason 2: The counting is a hechsher mitzvah — a preparation for Shavuot. The Shehecheyanu we say on Shavuot night covers both the Chag and the completion of the 49-day count. Important: The mitzvah itself is only the bracha and the counting. Not the Leshem Yichud before. Not the LaMenatzeach after. Just the bracha and the count. \"La-Omer\" vs. \"Ba-Omer\" We say \"la-Omer\" (to/from the Omer) — not \"ba-Omer\" (in the Omer). Why? We're counting from when the Omer sacrifice was brought. We're not counting within a period called \"the Omer.\" The correct way: \"Hayom achad asar yom la-Omer \" — 11 days from the Omer. This is why the correct Sephardi term is Lag la-Omer (the 33rd from the Omer), not \"Lag ba-Omer.\" The Ashkenazi text \"ba-Omer\" treats the Omer as a period of time. The Sephardi text \"la-Omer\" preserves the original meaning — counting from the Omer sacrifice. Siman 489 – Seder Tefillat Leil Sheni Shel Pesach u-Sefirat HaOmer Contains 10 Se'ifim Se'if 1 – When to Count Shulchan Aruch: On the second night of Pesach, after Arvit, we begin counting the Omer. After Arvit — Ashkenazi vs. Sephardi Practice Ashkenazim Sephardim Count immediately after the Amidah, before Aleinu LeShabeach Count after the full Arvit service Ashkenazi reasoning: Arvit technically ends at the Amidah. Aleinu is an addition. Counting immediately shows the urgency of doing the mitzvah at the beginning of the night. Some held: You should count even before Arvit — because the mitzvah of counting at the start of the night might override the usual rule of tadir ve-she'eino tadir, tadir kodem (regular mitzvot come first). Practical Ruling Scenario What to Do Praying Arvit at or near nightfall Pray Arvit first, then count Praying Arvit significantly later (10:00, 10:30, 11:00 PM) Count the Omer at the beginning of the night, pray Arvit later Community praying after sunset but before nightfall (common in England) Count with the community after sunset — this is valid Why count at the beginning of the night? The Torah says \"sheva shabbatot temimot tiheyenah\" — seven complete weeks. The Gemara derives: counting must be at night (for completeness). The Rishonim extended this: it should be at the beginning of the night — as tamim (complete/wholesome) as possible. Is counting with a minyan better than counting on time? No. No one holds that counting together with a minyan trumps counting at the proper time. The tiny hidur of counting with a community does not override the importance of counting at the beginning of the night. Community Practice In England, where nightfall in this season can be 9:00–9:30 PM, the practical guidance: In a community setting: Count after sunset (valid le-chatchila for a de-rabbanan mitzvah). People are there, they'll remember, it's worthwhile. If counting individually and not concerned about forgetting: Try to wait until nightfall. Women and Sefirat HaOmer Status: Sefirat HaOmer is a mitzvat aseh she-ha-zeman geramah (time-bound positive commandment) — because it must be done at night. Therefore, women are exempt . Can women count with a bracha? Ashkenazi ruling Sephardi ruling Generally yes — women can take on a mitzvah and say the bracha (same as lulav, shofar, sukkah) Generally no — how can you say \"ve-tzivanu\" (and commanded us) when you weren't commanded? The Chida notes: Many Sephardi communities (e.g., Iraq) did have women say the bracha on mitzvot like lulav. But the general Sephardi halacha follows the Shulchan Aruch — no bracha. Magen Avraham — even for Ashkenazim, two concerns: Women may not understand the count: There's a unique din in Sefirat HaOmer — unlike other mitzvot, you must understand what you're counting . With Shema, saying it in Hebrew fulfills the mitzvah even without comprehension. With Omer, comprehension is essential — because the essence of counting is knowing what you're counting. Women may forget to count consistently: If you know you won't count every night throughout the seven weeks, there's an opinion you shouldn't start with a bracha at all. In practice: Group Practice Ashkenazi women Most count. Some with a bracha, some without. Sephardi women Count without a bracha (general practice). Ben Ish Chai Women shouldn't count at all (Kabbalistic reasons) — but this is not the accepted practice. Why We Don't Count Twice Each Night (The Double-Counting Question) Outside of Israel we keep two days Chag because of calendrical doubt. Logically, each night of the Omer we should count twice — once assuming Pesach was the first night, once assuming it was the second night. On Shavuot night we should still be counting (49th day if Pesach was the second night). Why don't we? A count that isn't clear and precise isn't a count at all . If I say \"tonight is 11 days — or maybe 10 days,\" I haven't counted anything. The essence of counting requires certainty. Double-counting would undermine the entire mitzvah. Principle: We only observe the calendrical doubt (two days Chag) when it doesn't impose on any other mitzvah. When it would impair another mitzvah — like making the counting meaningless — we don't do it. This is also why you must understand what you're saying: If you count in Hebrew but don't know what \"achad asar\" means, you haven't counted. Count in English if needed: \"Today is 11 days of the Omer, which is one week and four days.\" With the bracha. Perfectly valid. Se'if 2 – Counting Before Nightfall / On a Cloudy Day Shulchan Aruch: If it was a cloudy day (no clocks in those times), and a person counted thinking it was already night — but it was actually still before sunset — he must repeat the counting when it gets dark. \"U-medakdekim einam sofrim ad tzeit ha-kochavim\" — people who are precise don't count until nightfall. \"Ve-chein na'og la'asot\" — and so is the correct practice. Understanding the Three Time Windows Time Status for Counting Before Plag HaMincha (~1.25 halachic hours before sunset) Counting is meaningless — too early for any nighttime mitzvah After Plag HaMincha, before sunset Some communities counted here (out of concern people would forget). Valid only with specific caveats (see below). After sunset, before nightfall (bein ha-shmashot) Valid le-chatchila — me'ikar ha-din, since Omer is de-rabbanan After nightfall (tzeit ha-kochavim) The best practice — fulfills even the Rambam's de'oraita standard What the Shulchan Aruch is describing: A case where someone counted after Plag HaMincha but before sunset — thinking it was already dark (cloudy day). He must repeat. Communities That Counted After Plag HaMincha Some communities in the northern hemisphere counted after Plag HaMincha because nightfall was very late and they feared people would forget. If you find yourself in such a community: Count without a bracha with them. Have in mind explicitly: \"I don't intend to fulfill the mitzvah with this counting\" (ein ani mitkaven latzeit). Later, when night actually falls — count again with a bracha . General principle: You can say the Omer count at any time without it \"counting\" — if you explicitly have in mind that you're not fulfilling the mitzvah. This is because kavana (intention) is required for fulfillment. What Medakdekim Means The Shulchan Aruch says \"medakdekim\" (precise people) wait until nightfall. This is not saying that counting after sunset is be-di'avad. Rather: Me'ikar ha-din: Counting from sunset is fully valid le-chatchila (since it's de-rabbanan). Medakdekim: To accommodate the Rambam who holds it's de'oraita, precise people wait until nightfall. Both are le-chatchila — one is just more precise than the other. Summary of Key Points Topic Key Ruling De'oraita or de-rabbanan? De-rabbanan nowadays (Shulchan Aruch). Rambam: de'oraita. When to count After Arvit. If Arvit is very late — count at beginning of the night first. Earliest valid time After sunset (me'ikar ha-din). Medakdekim: after nightfall. Before Plag: meaningless. Community practice Count after sunset together — valid, practical. Individual practice Try to wait until nightfall if possible. Women Exempt. Sephardim: count without bracha. Ashkenazim: divided — some with bracha, some without. Language Must understand what you're counting. If you don't understand Hebrew numbers: count in English. La-Omer vs. Ba-Omer Sephardim: la-Omer (from the Omer). Ashkenazim: ba-Omer (in the Omer). Double counting Not done — would undermine the clarity required for counting. Why count upward? Each day has intrinsic value. You're building, not just waiting. Why no Shehecheyanu? (1) Nowadays it's only a remembrance, with sadness. (2) Shehecheyanu on Shavuot covers it.","CEs78Ga8lrA":"With Rabbi Daniel Kada · Special Shiur How the Rambam would have conducted his Seder — Mishneh Torah Commentary primarily based on Rav Yosef Kapach z\"l Chapter 7 — The Mitzvot of the Night Halacha 1 — The Biblical Obligation to Tell the Story Rambam: It is a mitzvat aseh (positive Torah commandment) to speak about all the miracles and wonders Hashem did for our ancestors in Egypt, on the night of the 15th of Nisan. Source: \"Zachor et ha-yom ha-zeh asher yatzata mi-Mitzrayim\" (Shemot 13:3) — Remember the day you left Egypt. The Rambam draws a parallel to \"Zachor et yom ha-Shabbat\" — just as there's a mitzvah to verbally proclaim Shabbat (Kiddush), so too there's a mitzvah to verbally proclaim the Exodus. How do we know it's specifically the night of the 15th? \"Ve-higadeta le-vincha ba-yom ha-hu\" — tell your children on this day. The Gemara explains: \"bi-she'ah she-yesh matzah u-maror munachim lefanecha\" — when matzah and maror are placed before you. Since the mitzvah of eating matzah and maror is on the night of the 15th, so too the mitzvah of telling the story is on the night of the 15th. Note on \"bincha\": When the Torah says \"ben\" without \"bat\" next to it, it means all children — sons and daughters. \"Ben\" only means exclusively male when contrasted with \"bat.\" Even without children: You still must speak about the Exodus. Even great scholars: Are obligated. \"Ve-chol ha-ma'arich bi-devarim eilu... harei zeh meshubach\" — the more you elaborate, the more praiseworthy. Key Points Derived The mitzvah lasts the entire night (Rambam's view). Just as all mitzvot of the night can be done until dawn, so too discussing the Exodus is a mitzvah the whole night — until sleep overcomes you (\"ad she-techtefenu sheinah\"). This is actually the only place in the Shulchan Aruch where staying up all night is mentioned — on Pesach, not Shavuot. Practical tip: Go to bed with a Pesach book — Meam Loez, Haggadah commentaries — so you fall asleep with Pesach on your mind. There's still a mitzvah even in those final moments. Note: The Shulchan Aruch rules (following other Rishonim) that we should try to complete the main mitzvot before chatzot (midnight). But the mitzvah of discussing the Exodus continues all night per the Rambam. Two lessons from the Shabbat parallel: Even if alone, you must say it aloud — just as you say Kiddush even alone on Shabbat, you must verbalize the Haggadah even if alone (relevant during COVID-era sedarim). Women are equally obligated — just as women are obligated in Kiddush (because they're obligated in the negative commandments of Shabbat, connecting positive and negative), so too women are obligated in Haggadah (because they're obligated in the negative commandments of Pesach — don't eat chametz, etc.). Important: It is a mistake for women to spend days preparing and then be too exhausted to participate in the Seder. They have an equal Torah obligation to men. Halacha 2 — Teaching According to the Child's Level Rambam: There is a mitzvah to inform your children even if they don't ask. The Torah says \"ve-higadeta\" (you shall tell) — not \"ve-heshivota\" (you shall answer). A father teaches according to the child's level of understanding. For a young or simple child: \"My child, we were all once slaves, like this servant, in Egypt. And on this night Hashem redeemed us to freedom.\" Simple, visual, concrete. For an older, wiser child: Elaborate on the details — the miracles, Moshe Rabbeinu, the plagues, the specifics. Rav Kapach's observation: The Rambam characteristically gives visual, tangible examples. Notice he says \"like this servant\" — pointing to a real person. Don't just speak — act it out, show it, make it real. Give practical, visual demonstrations so children can live the experience, not just hear about it. Halacha 3 — Making the Night Different (Arousing Questions) Rambam: You must do things differently on this night to arouse the children's curiosity, so they ask \"Mah Nishtanah?\" Three methods from the Gemara: Method What the Rambam Says Modern Practice Give treats Nuts (in those days a big treat) Sweets, prizes, games — prepare in advance Remove the table Each person had an individual table; you'd move it away before eating We remove the Seder plate (since we share one large table) \"Chotfin\" — snatching matzah Pass the matzah around, snatch it from person to person Syrian/Iraqi custom: pass matzah on shoulders, ask each other \"Where are you coming from? Where are you going?\" in Arabic Note on \"Chotfin\": Some Rishonim interpret this as going through the Seder quickly so children don't fall asleep. The Rambam does not interpret it this way — he understands it as physically passing/snatching the matzah to create curiosity. The Afikoman \"stealing\" game: Based on the Rambam's word \"Hotfin\" (snatch). The simple reading: snatch it away to preserve it for the end. The game of stealing and ransoming is not the Sephardi minhag and is educationally questionable. If no children are present: Situation Who Asks? No children Your spouse asks No spouse Colleagues ask each other Even if all are great scholars Still ask each other Completely alone Ask yourself Why ask yourself if you know the answer? Asking questions is the greatest demonstration of intellectual freedom . In Egypt, Paroh suppressed our ability to think (\"Tichbad aleihem ha-avodah\"). Asking — even without needing an answer — reenacts our mental liberation. Halacha 4 — Begin with Shame, End with Praise Rambam: The telling must begin with the negative (genut) and conclude with the positive (shevach). Two dimensions: Spiritual: Our ancestors were once idol worshippers (Mitechilah) → Hashem brought us to the truth, separated us from the nations, and brought us near to His yichud (oneness/unity). Physical: We were slaves to Paroh (Avadim Hayinu) → Hashem performed miracles and liberated us. The Rambam's order: It appears the Rambam had Mitechilah first (spiritual liberation), then Avadim Hayinu (physical liberation). Our Haggadot today have the opposite order. The Yichud Hashem: Notice the Rambam concludes with \"ve-kirvanu le-yichudo\" — He brought us near to His unity. The oneness of God is central throughout the Rambam's thought (Moreh Nevuchim, laws of converts, etc.). On Pesach night too, we should discuss and reflect on Yichud Hashem. \"Ve-chol ha-mosif u-ma'arich... harei zeh meshubach\" — the more you elaborate, the better. Halacha 5 — The Three Essential Statements Rambam: Anyone who did not say these three things on the night of the 15th has not fulfilled their obligation: Item Reason Pesach Because Hashem passed over (pasach) the houses of our ancestors in Egypt Maror Because the Egyptians embittered the lives of our ancestors Matzah Because we were redeemed — the dough didn't have time to rise These, together with Avadim Hayinu and Mitechilah, constitute the Haggadah. These are the absolute essentials that every person — men and women — must say. Halacha 6 — Experiencing the Exodus Yourself Rambam: \"Be-chol dor va-dor chayav adam le-harot et atzmo ke-ilu hu be-atzmo yatza me-shibud Mitzrayim\" — every generation, a person must show themselves as if they personally left Egypt. Important textual distinction: Text Meaning Lirot et atzmo (Ashkenazi Haggadah) To see/feel yourself leaving — very demanding Leharot et atzmo (Rambam / Shulchan Aruch) To show/demonstrate yourself leaving — causative, reenact it The Rambam's reading is more achievable: you don't necessarily have to feel it (though that's ideal), but at minimum you reenact and demonstrate it — through leaning, putting matzah on your back, cushions, the whole Seder structure. There is value in the reenactment itself, even if you don't achieve the emotional experience. Halacha 7 — Leaning and Four Cups Rambam: Because we reenact leaving Egypt, we must eat and drink while reclining, in the manner of freedom. Both men and women are obligated to drink four cups. Women's obligation in four cups: Even though it's a positive time-bound rabbinic mitzvah (from which women are normally exempt), women are obligated because \"af hen hayu be-oto ha-nes\" — they too were part of the miracle. Children: The Rambam rules children are not obligated in the four cups (unlike the Shulchan Aruch which rules they are). The Gemara debates this; the Rambam follows the opinion that wine isn't appropriate for children's chinuch. Chacham Ovadia: If a family can't afford grape juice for children, rely on the Rambam — give children treats instead. Amount per cup: Occasion Minimum Amount Regular Friday night Kiddush Melo lugmav (cheekful) ≈ 44 ml Each of the four cups on Pesach Revi'it ≈ 86 ml Even the poorest person must be provided with four cups. Halacha 8 — Who Must Lean Rambam: Even the poorest person must lean. It's the manner of freedom. Women: The Rambam says women don't need to lean (not customary in front of their husbands) — unless she is a \"prominent woman\" (ishah chashuvah). Modern ruling: Knesset HaGedolah (R. Benveniste, ~400 years ago) and subsequent Poskim established that all our women are considered chashuvot . Therefore Sephardim rule: both men and women lean. Ashkenazim: Women don't lean (because nowadays nobody truly reclines when eating — following the Ravyah). Student before teacher: Must ask permission to recline. A child before a father has implied consent. How to lean: To the left only. Leaning to the right, backward, or forward does not count. When leaning is essential: Eating the ke-zayit of matzah and drinking the four cups. The rest of the meal: lean as much as comfortable — praiseworthy but not strictly required. Halacha 9 — The Four Cups: Two Elements Rambam: There are two separate components to the four cups: Drinking four cups at the correct points in the Seder Experiencing freedom through the drinking Scenario Four Cups Fulfilled? Freedom Fulfilled? Drank four cups undiluted (too strong to enjoy) ✅ Yes ❌ No Drank four cups of good wine but all in one go ❌ No ✅ Yes Practical takeaway: The wine should be enjoyable (if you don't like wine, use grape juice — though the Rambam himself held grape juice is not ideal). And drink each cup at its proper place in the Seder. Be-di'avad: A cheekful suffices. Le-chatchila: drink a full revi'it. Halacha 10 — Bracha on Each Cup (Rambam vs. Our Practice) Rambam: Say Borei Peri HaGafen on each of the four cups. Why? Not because of hesech ha-da'at (interruption). Rather, the Rambam holds each cup is an independent mitzvah — the Chachamim established four separate mitzvot. Therefore each one gets its own birkat ha-mitzvah. Cup Rambam (& Ashkenazim) Shulchan Aruch / Sephardim 1st — Kiddush Borei Peri HaGafen ✅ ✅ 2nd — Haggadah Borei Peri HaGafen ✅ ❌ (covered by 1st) 3rd — Birkat HaMazon Borei Peri HaGafen ✅ ✅ (new bracha after bentching) 4th — Hallel Borei Peri HaGafen ✅ ❌ (covered by 3rd) Why Sephardim don't follow the Rambam here: The Rosh (Rabbeinu Asher) was doubtful whether each cup is a separate mitzvah or one combined mitzvah. Safek brachot le-hakel — when in doubt about blessings, we're lenient. Drinking between cups: Permitted throughout the Seder — except between the 3rd and 4th cup (concern about intoxication preventing completion of Hallel). Tea, water, and other beverages are fine during the Haggadah. Halacha 11 — Charoset Rambam: Eating charoset is a mitzvah mi-divrei sofrim (rabbinic), to remember the mortar (tit) in Egypt. The Rambam's recipe: Dates, figs, or raisins → crush them → add vinegar (chometz) → add spices. In Perush HaMishnayot he adds hyssop. \"Like straw in cement.\" No bracha on charoset. Why? It's tafel (secondary) — always dipped into with matzah or maror. Rav Kapach's novel ruling: If stranded on Seder night with only charoset (no matzah, no maror), one should say \"asher kideshanu be-mitzvotav al achilat charoset.\" The bracha is only omitted because it's normally secondary — but if it's all you have, it stands on its own. Halacha 12 — Maror Is Rabbinic Nowadays Rambam: Eating maror is not a Torah obligation on its own. The Torah links it to the Korban Pesach: \"Al matzot u-merorim yochluhu.\" Without the Korban Pesach, maror is only rabbinic. Five species qualify as maror: Chazeret (romaine lettuce), endives, horseradish, date ivy, wormwood. Chazeret (lettuce) is best: The Gemara gives two reasons — (1) it has a touch of sweetness (like Egypt, sweet at first then bitter), (2) the Aramaic \"chasa\" means compassion. Ashkenazim use horseradish — lettuce wasn't available in Europe. Rules: Can eat roots. Cannot cook it (must retain the bitter taste). A combination totaling a ke-zayit works. Chapter 8 — The Practical Order of the Seder Halacha 1 — Kiddush Pour a cup for each person. Say Borei Peri HaGafen, Kiddush, and Shehecheyanu. Drink. The word \"mozgin\" (they pour) — some derive from this the custom that others pour for you. Halacha 2 — Urchatz (With a Bracha!) Rambam: After Kiddush, say the bracha \"Al Netilat Yadayim\" and wash hands. Why with a bracha? Because you're about to dip a vegetable into liquid, and the halacha (Rambam, Rif) requires netilat yadayim with a bracha whenever dipping food into one of the seven liquids. Why don't we say a bracha today? Tosafot was uncertain whether this applies nowadays. The Shulchan Aruch rules to wash without a bracha (compromise). Ashkenazim stopped washing for dipping entirely during the year — so they explain Urchatz as \"to arouse the children's curiosity.\" But the original reason is clear: it's the halacha of washing before dipping. Halacha 2 (continued) — Karpas: Major Differences from Today Rambam says: Say Borei Pri Ha-Adamah Take any vegetable Dip it in charoset (not salt water!) Eat a ke-zayit (not less) Everyone eats a ke-zayit Why charoset and not salt water? Saadia Gaon also said this. The Rambam likely viewed karpas as aristocratic hors d'oeuvres (not just to arouse questions), and charoset fits that role. Why a ke-zayit? If karpas is substantive (hors d'oeuvres for freedom), you need a proper amount. The bracha issue: The Rambam held you eat a ke-zayit of karpas, do NOT say Borei Nefashot, and the Borei Pri Ha-Adamah carries through to cover the maror later. Birkat HaMazon at the end covers the ke-zayit of karpas. Why do we eat less than a ke-zayit today? Later Rishonim (Tosafot) were concerned: if you eat a ke-zayit, you should say Borei Nefashot, which closes the bracha, creating a problem for maror. Solution: eat less than a ke-zayit → no Borei Nefashot needed → Borei Pri Ha-Adamah carries through to maror. That became our practice. Halacha 2 (continued) — No Yachatz! The Rambam had no Yachatz. He went straight from karpas to removing the Seder plate and beginning the Haggadah. The fifteen simanim (Kadesh, Urchatz, Karpas, Yachatz...) are not from the Rambam. Halacha 3 — Maggid and Mah Nishtanah Remove the Seder plate. Pour the second cup. The child asks. The leader says Mah Nishtanah. The Rambam's Mah Nishtanah had FIVE parts (not four): On all other nights we dip once, tonight twice On all other nights we eat chametz and matzah, tonight only matzah On all other nights we eat roasted and boiled, tonight only roasted (the Korban Pesach) On all other nights we eat other vegetables, tonight bitter herbs On all other nights we eat sitting and reclining, tonight only reclining Nowadays: We omit #3 since we don't have the Korban Pesach. So we have four parts. Note: It's one question with multiple parts — not \"four questions.\" Then say the Haggadah: begin with genut (shame), end with shevach (praise), read Arami Oved Avi, etc. Halacha 4 — Pesach, Matzah, Maror + Hallel Return the Seder plate. Say: Pesach: Lift and explain — Hashem passed over Maror: Lift and explain — they embittered our lives Matzah: Lift and explain — we were redeemed The Rambam's order: Pesach → Maror → Matzah (ending with the positive — freedom). Our Haggadot today: Pesach → Matzah → Maror. Nowadays: Say \"Pesach she-hayu avoteinu ochlin\" (which our ancestors ate) — not \"Pesach zeh\" (this Pesach), since we don't have the Korban. Conclude with \"Lefichach\" and Hallel. Say the bracha Asher Ge'alanu. Say Borei Peri HaGafen on the second cup (per Rambam) and drink. Halacha 5 — Rachtzah Wash hands a second time with a bracha (Al Netilat Yadayim) — because you've had hesech ha-da'at during the long Haggadah reading. Halacha 5 (continued) — Motzi Matzah: Only TWO Matzot The Rambam had only two matzot (not three): Take two matzot Break one of them Place the broken pieces inside the complete matzah Say HaMotzi Lechem Min Ha-Aretz Say Al Achilat Matzah Why only two? \"Lechem Oni\" — the bread of affliction is broken bread. The Rambam held you don't need lechem mishneh (two complete matzot) on Pesach — because the Torah emphasizes poverty/affliction, not double portions. Rav Kapach: This applies to ALL seven days of Pesach according to the Rambam — even Shabbat Chol HaMoed. Always one complete and one broken matzah. Why do we have three matzot today? Other Rishonim disagreed — you still need lechem mishneh (two complete matzot). So: break one earlier (Yachatz), have three total at HaMotzi, drop the bottom one, and say Al Achilat Matzah on the remaining two (one complete, one broken). That's how Yachatz was created. The Rambam clearly had soft matzah — otherwise the folding/wrapping he describes wouldn't work. Halacha 5 (continued) — Korech and the Meal In Temple times: Wrap matzah + maror together, dip in charoset, say brachot on eating matzot u-merorim and on the Korban Pesach and Chagigah. Nowadays: Say HaMotzi + Al Achilat Matzah → dip matzah in charoset and eat Say Al Achilat Maror → dip maror in charoset and eat Wrap matzah + maror together (Korech) → dip in charoset → eat without bracha (zecher la-Mikdash) Notable: The Rambam says to dip the matzah in charoset (not just the maror). This is debated — you need to taste the matzah — but that's what the Rambam writes. Halacha 6 — Afikoman and Conclusion Eat the meal. Then eat a ke-zayit of matzah as Afikoman (remembering the Korban Pesach). Don't eat anything afterwards — maintain the taste of matzah. Wash hands (Mayim Acharonim). Say Birkat HaMazon. Drink the third cup. Say Hallel on the fourth cup. Say Yehalelucha. Say Borei Peri HaGafen. Drink. Don't drink anything else the rest of the night except water. Halacha 7 — The Fifth Cup Rambam: \"Ve-yesh lo limzog kos chamishi\" — you should pour a fifth cup and say Hallel HaGadol (\"Hodu la-Hashem ki tov\") on it. This fifth cup is not obligatory like the four cups, but it is commendable. Some Sephardi communities historically observed this. It is discussed in the Gemara. What the Rambam Did NOT Have Element Status Yachatz ❌ Did not exist — went straight from karpas to Haggadah Three matzot ❌ Only two matzot Fifteen simanim ❌ Not the Rambam's framework Salt water for karpas ❌ Used charoset Nirtzah / songs ❌ Not mentioned Shir HaShirim ❌ Not mentioned (though it is our custom) Egg on the Seder plate ❌ Two pieces of meat instead Summary: The Rambam's Seder vs. Our Practice Element Rambam Our Practice Today Urchatz With bracha (Al Netilat Yadayim) Without bracha Karpas Dip in charoset, eat a ke-zayit Dip in salt water, eat less than ke-zayit Yachatz Doesn't exist Break middle of three matzot Matzot Two (one complete, one broken) Three Mah Nishtanah Five parts (including roasted meat) Four parts Order of Haggadah Mitechilah first, then Avadim Hayinu Avadim Hayinu first Pesach/Maror/Matzah order Pesach → Maror → Matzah Pesach → Matzah → Maror Bracha on each cup Borei Peri HaGafen on all four Only on 1st and 3rd (Sephardim) Seder plate Two pieces of meat Meat (zero'a) and egg (beitzah) Matzah dipped in charoset Yes Not standard practice Children and four cups Not obligated Obligated (Shulchan Aruch) Fifth cup Recommended (Hallel HaGadol) Not standard (some Sephardi communities) Soft matzah Clearly yes Varies by community","8Q7iKFwHRaw":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapters: End of Perek 5 (נדיבות — Generosity) & Beginning of Perek 6 (אריכות אפים — Slow to Anger / Calmness) Edition: Feldheim Recap: Chapters 1–5 Chapter 1: Two paths — the Tzaddik (fulfills everything required) and the Chasid (goes beyond). The sefer focuses on the Chasid's path. Chapter 2: All middot are interrelated — working on one affects others. Chapter 3: Purity of motive — four levels, from serving for social pressure (lowest) to serving because Hashem commanded (highest). Chapter 4: Rachamanut (compassion) — first of the 13 Attributes of Mercy. Very important, but sometimes cruelty is necessary. When in doubt → choose mercy. Chapter 5 (first part): Nedivut (generosity) — giving when there's no obligation. Four kinds: money, influence, intellect, faith. Avraham Avinu modeled all four. Chapter 5 (Conclusion): The Knock-On Effects of Generosity Generosity Trains Satisfaction and Trust in Hashem ומנדיבון הנפלא של אברהם אבינו... למדים אנחנו על גדולת טבעו הנדיב From Avraham's generosity we learn about his generous nature But also: a byproduct of generosity = being satisfied with what you have, not putting too much emphasis on transient things Avraham's example: Torah tells us: אברם כבד מאד במקנה בכסף ובזהב — very wealthy Yet after winning the war against the kings: הרימותי ידי אל ה' אל עליון... אם מחוט ועד שרוך נעל... ולא תאמר אני העשרתי את אברם \"I don't want anything. I don't need your money.\" Despite being wealthy → not greedy, not constantly wanting more Generosity trains this: when you give freely, you learn to be content Each Kind of Generosity Has a Specific Knock-On Effect Rabbi Avraham goes through all four: 1. Generosity with Money → Trust in Hashem (Bitachon) The fact you're willing to give ma'aser, tzedakah, 10–20% → shows trust that Hashem will provide Also reminds us: don't focus on transient things — money comes and goes The opposite (kamtzanut with money): Causes lack of trust in Hashem and over-focus on material things 2. Generosity with Influence → Trust Hashem Will Maintain Your Power Sharing your connections and influence → shows trust that your ability won't diminish You won't become less powerful by helping others Rabbi Sacks' principle: Power = zero-sum game. Influence = the opposite — the more you share, the more you have. The opposite: Hoarding influence → shows lack of trust, belief that everything is in human hands alone 3. Generosity with Intellect → Belief That Wisdom Grows by Sharing Teaching others → shows you trust your wisdom won't diminish הרבה חכמה למדתי מחברי יותר מרבותי ומתלמידי יותר מכולם \"I learned much from my colleagues, more from my teachers, but the most from my students\" True wisdom (חכמה נכונה) = wisdom you can teach and express to others Shlomo HaMelech: חכמות בחוץ תרנה — true wisdom sings in the streets, proclaims itself publicly The Brisker Rav: \"If you can't explain it to your chavruta, you don't understand it\" The opposite: Hoarding knowledge → shows you don't truly value it and don't trust it will endure 4. Generosity with Faith → Shows You Love Your Beliefs If you truly love something → you want to share it If you truly believe → you want others to experience it too Dovid HaMelech: לכו בנים שמעו לי יראת ה' אלמדכם — \"Come, children, I will teach you the fear of Hashem\" The opposite: Sitting in your corner, becoming uber-religious but not sharing → shows lack of excitement about your faith, lack of genuine love for it The Rambam in Hilchot Tzedakah (Chapter 10) לעולם אין אדם מעני מן הצדקה ואין דבר רע ולא היזק נגלל בשביל הצדקה You will never lose out from charity No harm or damage ever comes from generosity והיה מעשה הצדקה שלום — the result of charity is peace Chapter 6: אריכות אפים — Slow to Anger / Calmness (Part 1) Definition אריכות אפים היא ביטוי להעדר החמה והכעס כמעט כליל Arichut Apayim = an almost complete absence of anger He says \"almost\" — because sometimes anger is necessary (he'll address this later) One of the 13 Attributes of Mercy: אל רחום וחנון ארך אפים Nature vs. Training Some people have a natural tendency toward calmness — balanced temperament, serene character → easier for them to acquire this trait Others have a natural tendency toward hot-temperedness — חמוצי מזג, easily angered → much harder for them In their science/medicine: believed to depend on the balance of the four humors in the body Either way → training is essential If you're naturally prone to anger → you need more training, but that's life Hashem created us with inequalities — some find it harder than others Why We Can Never Judge Others אל תדין את חברך עד שתגיע למקומו — Don't judge your fellow until you reach his place (Pirkei Avot) A person who gets angry once may be worse than someone who gets angry five times The five-times person may have stopped himself 45 other times — because it's much harder for him The once-angry person may find it easy — so why did he even get angry that one time? Only Hashem can truly judge character and temperament The Core Principle: Intellect Must Trump Emotion אריכות האפיים המשובחת היא שליטת כוח השכל על כוח היצר The essence of calmness = intellect in control over emotion Melech (King) = מלך = מוח (brain) → לב (heart) → כבד (action) (Rav Moshe Shapiro) A person who's truly in control: brain directs heart directs action Not the reverse — when emotions direct the brain, mistakes follow Anger = Loss of Intellect אם חכם הוא חכמתו מסתלקת ממנו The Gemara says: even the greatest chacham, when angry, loses his wisdom This is NOT a supernatural punishment — it's a natural consequence When emotion takes full control → intellect is simply no longer functioning You can't reason with an angry person, just like you can't reason with a drunk person Two Important Qualifications 1. Calmness ≠ Apathy ולא מתוך קרירות מזג בעלמא או רפיון היצרים או מורך הלב Being calm doesn't mean being weak, submissive, or apathetic It doesn't mean everyone runs over you Sometimes you NEED to show displeasure, be firm, be stern 2. Consequences Are Important — But Not from Anger מי שמעניש כפי חובתו לא מתוך רתחה ולא מתוך התרגשות הרי הוא ממדרגת חסידות גדולה קרוב לנבואה A person who punishes when necessary — not from anger, not from emotion — is at an extremely high level of piety, close to prophecy This is NOT revenge (nekama) — it's called עונש צדק (just consequences) The Rambam in Hilchot De'ot: If you need to educate/reprimand → show yourself as angry (pretend), but be fully in control internally. Your intellect remains in charge — you're acting angry, not being angry. Anger Causes Revenge and Harboring הכעס המסית לנקמה... ומוציא משטמה בנפש פנימה Anger causes two things: Nekama (revenge) — if you have power → you act on it immediately Netira (harboring) — if you lack power → it simmers inside, bubbling, festering The Torah juxtaposes them: לא תקום ולא תטור — don't take revenge AND don't harbor Both are destructive Harboring leaves an imprint within a person — studies show links between living with anger/resentment and health problems (including cancer) Anger and Drunkenness הכעס מעביר את האדם מדעתו ממש כשיכור The Chachamim compared anger to drunkenness: לא תרתח ולא תחטא, לא תרוה ולא תחטא \"Don't get angry and you won't sin; don't get drunk and you won't sin\" The parallel: both cause complete loss of intellect An angry person = a drunk person: can't rationalize, can't reason with them Look at an angry person — they look crazy, like they've lost control Anger and Arrogance Go Hand-in-Hand דרך הסבלנות ודרך הענווה שותפות הן כשם שדרכי הכעס והגאווה שותפות הן Paired Together Paired Together Patience + Humility Anger + Arrogance What causes anger? Often arrogance: \"I'm going to show you! You can't do this to ME! You've belittled ME!\" What causes patience? Humility: \"It's not about me\" The Gemara: לעולם יהא אדם ענוותן כהלל ואל יהא קפדן כשמאי — Be humble like Hillel, don't be strict like Shammai What does humility have to do with strictness? Because they're linked — if you're humble, you won't be quick to anger Levels of Anger — Where Do You Fall? אדם בעל מזג ממוצע אינו רוגז על קלות ואינו עומד בחמורות Normal person: Gets angry at big things, doesn't get angry at small things Not yet called \"patient\" — but not called \"angry\" either Most of us fall here Closer to angry: Gets angry at small things too → he's on the wrong side Closer to calm: Doesn't get angry even at serious provocations → he's on the right path Many levels and variations in between Mesilat Yesharim (Ramchal, end of Chapter 11): Goes through different levels of anger — from the highest level (like serving avodah zarah, would destroy the world if he could) down through various gradations. What Does \"Calmness\" Actually Mean? כעס המגונה הוא אפילו כשכועס אדם על מה שראוי לכעוס עליו Bad anger isn't just getting angry for no reason — only fools do that Bad anger = getting angry even when it's REASONABLE to be angry True calmness = not getting angry even when you have every right to be The Hillel story (Gemara Shabbat): Two people made a bet: can you get Hillel angry? One went to Hillel on Erev Shabbat while he was in the bathhouse Asked ridiculous questions: \"Why are Babylonians' heads round?\" etc. Hillel had every right to be angry — but he wasn't. He answered calmly. The man lost his bet This is the model of true calmness Training for Calmness — Two Methods Method 1: Habit (Mussar / Training) שירגיל האדם את נפשו לאריכות אפיים בדברים מרגיזים Practical steps: a) When about to get angry → let intellect rein: ולא ימהר חימה כמו שציווה שלמה אל תבהל ברוחך לכעוס Don't rush to anger — slow down Count to ten, take a breath — whatever method works This is already discussed in Rabbi Avraham's time — \"anger management\" is not modern b) Remember the consequences of past anger: יזכור את התפרצות רוגזו בימי חייו ואשר גרם לו Think about times you DID get angry What were the consequences? Broken relationships — some can never be fixed Words that left the mouth that can never be returned כחצי גיבור שנונים — Like arrows: once they leave, you can't take them back Marriages destroyed by a single comment Friendships ended by a moment of rage Think about this BEFORE you get angry next time c) Remember: whatever gain you think anger will bring → you'll lose in the end: עתיד שכרו לצאת בהפסד ייסורים \"I'll teach this person a lesson!\" → In the short term you feel vindicated In the long term → you lose. Always. Shlomo: איש חמה יגרה מדון וארך אפים ישקיט ריב — Angry people are always fighting; calm people have peace d) The fundamental realization: You cannot change other people — only yourself The older you get, the more you realize this Trying to \"teach someone a lesson\" through anger almost never works Far easier to change how YOU react than to change others e) The 40-day principle: One of the Chasidic masters: train something for 40 days → it becomes part of your character The Omer period beginning in a week → ideal time to start working on this Even if we can't fully change → just pausing to think before getting angry = progress Method 2: Torah — Two Sub-Categories שניים הם סוגי התקשרות על ידי התורה לאריכות אפיים a) Stories in Torah and Nevi'im — examples of anger and its consequences, and calmness and its rewards (covered in this shiur — see below) b) Mitzvot of the Torah — specific commandments designed to train patience and prevent anger (to be covered next shiur) Negative Examples from Torah: Anger and Its Consequences 1. Kayin and Hevel Kayin got angry → killed his brother גדול עוני מנשוא — \"My sin is too great\" Punished with exile; his descendants also punished (Tuval Kayin killed by Lemech's accident) Root cause: anger and jealousy 2. Eisav and Yaakov Eisav's hatred → decided to kill Yaakov ויאמר עשו בלבו יקרבו ימי אבל אבי ואהרגה את יעקב אחי — \"When my father dies, I'll kill Yaakov\" Rivka said: wait עד אשר תשוב חמת אחיך — until your brother's anger cools down Years later (beginning of Vayishlach) → Yaakov still worried about Eisav's anger אכפרה פניו — \"I'll calm his face\" (Onkelos: \"I'll calm his anger\") Anger's long reach: decades of broken brotherhood 3. The Brothers and Yosef Their hatred → decided to kill him Root cause: anger over his dreams Onkelos: וימררוהו ורבו — they were bitter and wanted revenge No doubt they regretted when they saw Yaakov's devastation But: what's done is done — regret didn't help Consequences: they ended up going down to Egypt → slavery Yaakov's words about Shimon and Levi: ארור אפם כי עז — \"Cursed is their anger, for it is fierce\" 4. Moshe at Mei Merivah The Rambam's understanding (followed by Rabbi Avraham): the sin was NOT hitting the rock The sin was getting angry — when he called Bnei Yisrael שמעו נא המורים — \"Listen, you rebellious ones!\" He lost control of his emotions (on his level, as it were) Consequence: Could not enter Eretz Yisrael There are 20+ explanations of Moshe's sin — but the Rambam's: it was the anger 5. Shaul and the Kohanim of Nov Shaul thought the Kohanim in Nov were supporting his enemies Out of anger → destroyed Nov, the city of Kohanim Despite being God's chosen king 6. David and Naval Naval insulted David → David was about to kill him out of anger Avigayil stopped him: \"This will be bad for you\" David responded: ברוכה את אשר כליתיני היום הזה מבוא בדמים — \"Blessed are you for stopping me from bloodshed\" He recognized his anger almost led to a terrible sin 7. David and Mephibosheth Ziva told David that Mephibosheth was against him (a lie) David accepted it out of anger → didn't accept Mephibosheth's excuse later Punished David: יצאה בת קול — \"Rechav'am and Yerav'am will divide the kingdom\" The division of the kingdom traced back to this moment of anger 8. Asa King of Yehuda Despite being a righteous king → got angry with the prophet Hanani ויכעס אסא אל הרואה — he was angry at the seer Imprisoned him → led to bloodshed 9. Yoash King of Yehuda Got angry → killed Zechariah ben Yehoyada the Kohen Despite Yehoyada having been kind to Yoash Anger caused him to forget all the chesed done for him The Chachamim's Statement: Anger = Avodah Zarah הקורע בגדיו בחמתו ומפזר מעותיו בחמתו יהיה בעיניך כעובד עבודה זרה Tearing clothes in anger, throwing things around in anger = like serving avodah zarah Today the yetzer tells you: do this. Tomorrow: go serve avodah zarah. And: לא יהיה בך אל זר — \"You shall not have a foreign god within you\" What foreign god is within you? → זה יצר הרע — the yetzer hara The specific meaning: that force of emotion where you lose all intellectual control = the \"foreign god\" within Key Takeaways Generosity has knock-on effects: Generosity with money → bitachon. With influence → trust Hashem maintains your power. With intellect → wisdom grows by sharing. With faith → shows you truly love it. Calmness = intellect in control over emotion. Not apathy, not weakness. Anger = loss of intellect — like drunkenness. The Gemara's \"wisdom departs\" is a natural consequence, not a punishment. Anger and arrogance go hand-in-hand. Patience and humility go hand-in-hand. True calmness = not getting angry even when you have every right to be (the Hillel model). Consequences are important — but deliver them calmly, with intellect in control. That's close to prophecy. You can't change other people — only yourself. Anger aimed at \"teaching someone a lesson\" almost never works. Training Method 1 (Habit): Remember past consequences, slow down, let intellect rein. Training Method 2 (Torah): Learn from the stories — every major act of destruction in Tanakh traces back to anger. Anger = like avodah zarah — the \"foreign god within you\" is the yetzer hara of uncontrolled emotion. Preview: Next Shiur (After Pesach) Positive examples from Torah — people who were calm and gained from it Mitzvot of the Torah designed to train patience When calmness is NOT appropriate What to do when in doubt The concept of \"anger\" attributed to God Chag Pesach Kasher v'Sameach","QJ1ePh74eZU":"With Rabbi Daniel Kada From Kaddesh to Nirtzah — Everything You Need to Know The Seder Plate The Three Matzot Why three? Not because of Kohen, Levi, Yisrael (that's a nice addition but not the real reason). The real reason: We have two matzot for lechem mishneh (double bread — as on every Shabbat and Yom Tov) plus one extra matzah for Yachatz (breaking). That's it. The Zero'a (Shank Bone) Represents the Korban Pesach (Paschal lamb). We use a shank bone because the pasuk says Hashem took us out \"bi-zero'a netuyah\" (with an outstretched arm). In essence, any piece of meat would work — the shank bone is just symbolic of this pasuk. The Beitzah (Egg) Represents the Korban Chagigah (festive sacrifice brought on every Chag — on Pesach we had both the Chagigah and the Paschal lamb). We use an egg because we can't use a second piece of meat (already used for zero'a), and the egg carries a tinge of avelut (mourning) — reminding us we don't have the Beit HaMikdash. The roundness of the egg is always associated with mourning. Calendar connection: Tish'ah Be-Av always falls on the same day of the week as the first night of Pesach. This year: Friday night / Shabbat (postponed to Sunday). Karpas Any vegetable. Sephardim: typically parsley or celery. Ashkenazim: often potato (access issue in Europe historically). Any vegetable suffices. Maror and Chazeret Sephardim: lettuce for both. Ashkenazim: lettuce for one, horseradish for the other. Charoset Reminds us of the mortar and servitude in Egypt. This Year's Timing: First and Second Nights First Night (Friday Night) Le-chatchila: Begin at nightfall (~8:50 PM in London). If necessary (young children, difficulty waiting): May begin from sunset (~8:00 PM), provided Maggid does not begin before nightfall . Why? The only mitzvot that are biblically mandated at night are eating matzah and relating the Exodus (sippur yetzi'at Mitzrayim). All other Seder mitzvot (leaning, four cups, maror, etc.) are rabbinic — and rabbinic mitzvot may be performed from sunset if necessary. Second Night (Motzaei Shabbat) Must begin at nightfall (~8:52 PM). Cannot begin early because Kiddush on the second night includes Havdalah with a candle — which cannot be done before Shabbat ends. However: From sunset (~8:00 PM), you may set the table — lay out cutlery, crockery, Seder plate — without doing any melachah (no heating food, no carrying fire, etc.). Why is this permitted? The principle: issur de-rabbanan bein ha-shmashot le-tzorech mitzvah mutar — a rabbinic prohibition (preparing from Shabbat to Yom Tov) during twilight for the purpose of a mitzvah is permitted. Ashkenazim: The Mishnah Berurah is doubtful whether this applies from Shabbat into Motzaei Shabbat (as opposed to Friday into Shabbat). Sephardim definitely hold it applies in both directions. Kaddesh — The First Cup The Four Cups Why four? Either because of the four expressions of redemption in Parashat Va'era, or because of the four exiles we have survived. Wine vs. Grape Juice Grape juice has the same halachic status as wine. There is a preference for non-mevushal wine — but if you're not particular about this during the rest of the year (regular Friday night Kiddush), there's no reason to be particular about it specifically on Pesach. Red Wine The wine/grape juice should be red — representing the blood (dam) and the richness/nobility of the color. Everyone Must Drink Unlike regular Shabbat/Yom Tov Kiddush (where one person can drink for everyone), on Pesach night every person must drink their own cup. One person cannot experience freedom (herut) on behalf of another. How Much to Drink When Amount Required Regular Friday night Kiddush Melo lugmav (cheekful) = ~44 ml Each of the four cups on Pesach A revi'it = ~86–90 ml Reference: A standard small plastic cup is ~170–180 ml. A revi'it is about half of that. Important: It is written that it's good to drink the majority of the cup, but strictly the revi'it suffices. Don't use tiny cups (less than 86 ml). And don't use huge cups and only sip from them — drink the full revi'it. Drink in one or two gulps — don't sip slowly over time. Brachot on the Four Cups (Sephardim vs. Ashkenazim) Cup Sephardim Ashkenazim 1st cup Borei Pri HaGafen ✅ (have in mind the 2nd cup too) Borei Pri HaGafen ✅ 2nd cup No bracha Borei Pri HaGafen ✅ 3rd cup Borei Pri HaGafen ✅ (have in mind the 4th cup too) Borei Pri HaGafen ✅ 4th cup No bracha Borei Pri HaGafen ✅ Shehecheyanu When saying Shehecheyanu in Kiddush, have in mind all the mitzvot of the night : eating matzah, eating maror, relating the Exodus — not just the Chag itself. Other Details Nice custom: one person pours for his neighbor (not strictly required). No need to wash the cup between cups. Can pour the next cup directly into leftover wine/juice. This is what Chacham Ovadia himself did. (According to Kabbalah — yes, wash inside and outside. According to halacha — not necessary.) Children and the Four Cups The Gemara teaches that children (even those who've reached the age of chinuch) are not required to drink the four cups. Of course we give them cups — it's part of the excitement. But Chacham Ovadia makes an important point: if money is tight, spend on treats and entertainment for the children rather than on four cups of grape juice for each child. The essence of the night is \"Ve-higadeta le-vincha\" — telling your children. Engaging them is more important than their drinking. Prepare in advance: Treats, rewards, games, activities to keep children engaged and participating throughout the evening. Urchatz — Washing Hands Sephardi reason: This is the year-round halacha (Shulchan Aruch). Anytime one dips a vegetable into one of the seven liquids (including water), one must wash hands without a bracha. We do this every time, not just on Pesach. Ashkenazi reason: To arouse the curiosity of children (since Ashkenazim don't hold of the year-round obligation to wash for dipping). Between Urchatz and Karpas: One may speak — as long as it's relevant and there's no complete break between washing and eating. Karpas — Dipping the Vegetable The Main Reason To remind us of the tears of Am Yisrael in Egypt. We dip the vegetable in salt water (or lemon water) and ingest it — internalizing the tears and suffering. A Secondary Reason This is the way of nobility — having hors d'oeuvres before the main meal, opening the appetite. Leaning for Karpas? Reason Lean? Tears / servitude (main reason) ❌ No — not a moment of freedom Nobility / hors d'oeuvres (secondary) ✅ Yes In practice: We do not lean for Karpas (following the main reason). Some take two pieces — one without leaning, one with — to cover both opinions. The Bracha and How Much to Eat Say Borei Pri Ha-Adamah . This bracha is intended to last all the way through until Maror (covering the lettuce eaten then). Therefore: Eat less than a ke-zayit (less than ~18 grams) — a small taste. If you eat a ke-zayit or more, technically you'd need an after-bracha, which would \"close\" the Borei Pri Ha-Adamah and it wouldn't cover the Maror. If you accidentally ate a lot: Still don't say an after-bracha. The main point of Karpas is the dipping — not the eating. Take a small piece, dip it, eat it. If you don't have parsley or celery: Any vegetable works. Don't stress about finding specific vegetables. Eating during Maggid: Strictly speaking, if necessary, it is permissible to eat during Maggid. Yachatz — Breaking the Middle Matzah Why break it? We're about to say \"Ha Lachma Anya\" — this is the bread of affliction — and we say the Haggadah upon broken bread . Why the middle and not the top? We keep the top matzah complete for the bracha of HaMotzi later. This way we have both: a complete matzah for the bracha and a broken matzah for the Haggadah. The pieces: The bigger piece is set aside for Afikoman . The smaller piece goes back under the top matzah. Why set aside Afikoman now? So we don't forget and eat all the matzah during the meal, leaving nothing for the required matzah at the end. Syrian/Iraqi custom: Break the matzah into the shape of a Vav and a Dalet. The \"Stealing\" of the Afikoman Based on the Rambam's word \"Hotfin\" (snatch). The simple reading: snatch it away to ensure it's preserved until the end. Some turned this into a game of stealing and ransom — but this is not the Sephardi minhag and is questionable educationally (teaching children to steal mitzvot and hold them for ransom). Syrian/Iraqi custom: Each person, from oldest to youngest, puts the matzah on their shoulder and asks/answers questions: \"Where are you coming from? Where are you going? What do you have?\" — done in Arabic traditionally. Maggid — Telling the Story What Makes This Night Different from Every Other Night? We remember the Exodus every night (in the Shema). So what's special about Pesach night? Two key differences: Question and answer format. During the year, we simply state we left Egypt. On Pesach, it must be done through questions and answers — demonstrating mental/intellectual freedom . Paroh's ultimate goal was to suppress our ability to think independently (\"Tichbad aleihem ha-avodah\"). Asking questions is the greatest expression of intellectual freedom. Experiential. \"Be-chol dor va-dor chayav adam lir'ot et atzmo\" — you must feel as if you yourself left Egypt. It's not historical recollection — it's personal experience. Even a person alone asks Mah Nishtanah — because the act of questioning itself is the demonstration of freedom, regardless of whether anyone answers. Maggid Is NOT a Prayer There is no value in reading the Haggadah without understanding. Unlike prayer (where standing before the Shechinah has intrinsic value even without comprehension), Maggid is a discussion. The Haggadah is a springboard — not a set text to be read word for word. No bracha on Maggid: Because the mitzvah has no defined endpoint — the entire night is available for discussing the Exodus. What Language? The main sections should be in Hebrew. Everything else can and should be said in English (or your vernacular) if that enables understanding and discussion. The Essential Sections of Maggid If time is limited, these are the absolute minimum: Section Why Essential Mah Nishtanah The questions — core of the night's format Avadim Hayinu The answer — we were slaves, now we're free Rabban Gamliel Hayah Omer (Pesach, Matzah, Maror) Halachic requirement — must mention these three things Hallel and brachot at the end (Halleluyah + Asher Ge'alanu) Prayer and praise — in Hebrew If there's more time: Add Mitechilah (our history) and Be-chol Dor va-Dor. Maggid doesn't end at Maggid. The entire evening — during the meal, after — is open for discussion about the Exodus. Rachtzah — Washing for Matzah Wash hands with a bracha — exactly as for bread throughout the year. Nothing special. Motzi Matzah — Eating the Matzah The Procedure Take all three matzot in hand. Say HaMotzi Lechem Min Ha-Aretz on the two complete matzot (top + bottom) — fulfilling lechem mishneh. Put down the bottom matzah. Hold top + broken middle. Say Al Achilat Matzah . Eat a ke-zayit from the top matzah and a ke-zayit from the middle (broken) matzah. Why two ke-zeitim? Debate whether Al Achilat Matzah applies to the complete matzah or the broken one. We eat from both to cover both opinions. This only applies to the person leading the Seder. Everyone else needs only one ke-zayit . How Much Is a Ke-zayit? Standard Amount Recommended 27 grams Sufficient (room to be lenient) 18 grams Two ke-zeitim (for the leader) 18 + 18 = 36 grams suffices Timeframe: Eat the ke-zayit within 4.5 to 9 minutes (preferably under 5 minutes). Leaning Must lean while eating the matzah. Not Speaking Critical: From the bracha of HaMotzi and Al Achilat Matzah until the end of Korech — do not speak about unrelated matters. This bracha covers the matzah in Korech too. Speaking about immediate needs (pass the salt, is this enough matzah, etc.) is fine. Unrelated conversation should be avoided. Note: Between Rachtzah (washing) and HaMotzi — strictly speaking one may speak (same as during the year). But from HaMotzi onward through Korech — that's where silence matters most. The Biblical Mitzvah Eating matzah is the second biblical mitzvah of the night (after sippur yetzi'at Mitzrayim). The absolute bare minimum for the night: Kiddush + briefly telling the story to your child + eating a ke-zayit of matzah. Maror — The Bitter Herb Why Maror? To remember the bitterness of slavery in Egypt. Important: Maror today is only a rabbinic obligation. The Torah links maror to the Korban Pesach — without the Korban Pesach, maror is not biblically required. We eat it rabbinically to remember. How Much? One ke-zayit (~18–27 grams). Dipping in Charoset The halacha uses the word \"lishko'a\" — submerge the maror into the charoset (to remember the mortar and hard labor). But then — shake it off. You must retain the taste of bitterness. If you leave the charoset on, you lose the maror taste entirely. Common mistake: People submerge and eat with all the charoset. That's actually the halacha for Korech (see below) — not for Maror itself. Leaning? Do not lean for Maror. It represents bitterness and suffering — not freedom. Korech — The Sandwich Why? Hillel held that matzah and maror must be eaten together. The halacha doesn't follow Hillel — but we do Korech as a zecher (remembrance) of his opinion. Since we've already fulfilled the mitzvot of matzah and maror separately, Korech is an extra — not strictly obligatory. Charoset in Korech Here — unlike for Maror — you may submerge the maror in charoset and do not need to shake it off . Since the mitzvot are already fulfilled, enjoy it. This is where the common confusion comes from: People think you keep the charoset on for Maror. No — for Maror, shake it off. For Korech, enjoy it. Shulchan Orech — The Meal Enjoy the meal. The Egg Different customs about when to eat the hard-boiled egg: beginning of the meal, end of the meal, some even before Maggid. It makes no difference. Follow your family's custom. If you don't have one, eat it during the meal. Syrian custom: Eat the egg at the end of the meal, before Birkat HaMazon. Can you eat from the Seder plate? Halachically yes — the purpose of the Seder plate was originally to eat from. The Arizal (Kabbalistically) said not to. But halachically, taking the egg from the plate is fine. Tzafun — Afikoman Why Another Piece of Matzah? This is the third matzah eating of the night. It is rabbinically required to remember the Korban Pesach — which would have been eaten at the end of the meal. One ke-zayit suffices. Deadline: Before Midnight The Korban Pesach was eaten before midnight → Afikoman should be eaten before midnight. This year: Midnight = 1:01 AM. If you want to spend hours discussing Egypt — do it after Afikoman, not before. Leaning Must lean for Afikoman. No Eating After Afikoman To maintain the taste of matzah in your mouth until morning — as an expression of love for the mitzvah. Permitted after Afikoman: Tea, coffee, water. Not permitted: Food, dessert. Serve dessert before Afikoman. Practical tip: Have your meal → dessert → Afikoman → Birkat HaMazon. Barech — Birkat HaMazon Don't forget: Birkat HaMazon is a mitzvah from the Torah — as important as eating matzah. Give it proper attention. The Third Cup Say Borei Pri HaGafen (this bracha covers the 4th cup too — Sephardim). Hallel — Praise Pour the fourth cup. Say Hallel. Say it together in unison — sing it, not rush through it individually. This is prayer and praise to Hashem (unlike Maggid which is storytelling/discussion). Drink the fourth cup. Nirtzah — Songs Why End with Songs Like Echad Mi Yodea? A psychological/spiritual idea: When your mind is truly focused on something, you connect everything to it. The Chachamim told us that techelet (blue thread on tzitzit) reminds us of the sea, which reminds us of heaven — because when you live with awareness of Hashem, everything traces back to Him. Echad Mi Yodea demonstrates: after an evening immersed in the Exodus, throw out any number — 1 through 13 — and we make a Jewish connection to it. This shows the evening has penetrated our consciousness. Every number, every concept, connects back to our faith and our story. Summary: The Seder at a Glance Step Key Points Kaddesh Everyone drinks ~86 ml. Lean. Red wine/grape juice. Sephardim: bracha on 1st and 3rd cups only. Shehecheyanu covers all mitzvot of the night. Urchatz Wash without bracha. Year-round halacha for Sephardim (dipping vegetable in liquid). Karpas Small amount (< ke-zayit). Dip in salt water. Don't lean. Bracha covers until Maror. Yachatz Break middle matzah. Bigger piece = Afikoman. Smaller piece back under top matzah. Maggid Question and answer. Understand, don't just read. Use English if needed. Minimum: Mah Nishtanah, Avadim Hayinu, Rabban Gamliel, Hallel. Must be after nightfall. Rachtzah Wash with bracha (like regular bread). Motzi Matzah Leader: two ke-zeitim (top + middle). Everyone else: one ke-zayit (18–27g). Lean. Don't speak until after Korech. Eat within ~5 minutes. Maror One ke-zayit. Dip in charoset then shake off. Don't lean. Korech Sandwich. May keep charoset on. Lean. Shulchan Orech Enjoy the meal. Egg: follow your custom, doesn't matter when. Tzafun Afikoman: one ke-zayit. Before midnight (1:01 AM this year). Lean. No food after. Barech Birkat HaMazon — Torah obligation. Give it attention. Hallel Say together in unison. Pour 4th cup. Bracha on 3rd cup covers 4th (Sephardim). Nirtzah Songs. Every number connects to Judaism — proof the evening penetrated our hearts. The Absolute Bare Minimum If time, energy, or circumstances are severely limited: Kiddush (first cup) Tell the story — even briefly, even one paragraph, even in English — to your child Eat a ke-zayit of matzah These are the biblical core of the night.","VkjEevk2ulg":"With Rabbi Daniel Kada Foundations: Torah Sources for Kashering Source 1 — Vayikra 6:21 (Parashat Tzav): The Korban Chatat The Torah discusses the sin offering which had to be consumed by dawn the next morning. It states: An earthenware utensil in which the sacrifice was cooked must be broken — it cannot be kashered. A copper (metal) utensil need only be purged and rinsed with water . Two principles derived: Earthenware vessels can never be kashered. The Torah testifies: \"he'idah Torah al klei cheres she-einam yotz'im mi-yedei dofyan le-olam\" — they can never be rectified. Metal vessels can be kashered through purging with water. Source 2 — Bamidbar 31 (Parashat Matot): The Midianite Booty After the war with Midian, Elazar ben Aharon instructed the people on how to kasher captured utensils: Whatever was used with fire → pass through fire Whatever was used with hot water → purge in hot water Whatever was used cold → just wash Rashi's famous formulation: \"Ke-vol'o kach polto\" — the way it absorbs is the way it must be expelled. Is Kashering a Mitzvah? Opinion Ruling Tosafot Yes — a mitzvah, comparable to tevilat kelim Rambam and majority of Rishonim No — it is not one of the 613 mitzvot. It is merely a preparation, removing something that prevents you from using the utensil. Practical consequence: No bracha is recited on kashering utensils. This is comparable to salting meat to remove blood or removing forbidden fats — necessary processes, but not intrinsic mitzvot. Contrast with tevilat kelim: Immersing a utensil bought from a non-Jew is a positive transformation (from non-Jewish domain to Jewish domain) — that is why it has a bracha and is one of the 613 mitzvot. The Two Methods of Kashering 1. Libun (Burning / Red Hot) Definition: Heating the utensil in intense fire until sparks fly out from it, or in modern terms, until it becomes red hot . What it does: Completely destroys the absorbed flavors — they no longer exist. Essentially makes the utensil like new. When required: When the utensil was used to cook dry food (without liquid). Modern reality: Almost no utensils today can withstand becoming red hot. Therefore, anything requiring libun generally cannot be kashered — you put it away and buy new. One exception: Inside an oven (self-clean function or extended high heat). Note: Ashkenazim distinguish between libun chamur (severe) and libun kal (lighter). For Sephardim, we discuss libun as one category. 2. Hag'alah (Immersion in Boiling Water) Definition: Immersing the utensil in a pot of boiling water. What it does: Removes the absorbed flavors and transfers them into the boiling water. The flavors still exist (unlike libun which destroys them). When required: When the utensil was used to cook food with liquid . This is the method used for the vast majority of kitchen utensils. Why Doesn't the Flavor Get Re-Absorbed? Two explanations given: The boiling water is assumed to be 60 times the volume of the expelled flavor — so the flavor is nullified. Since the boiling water is causing the flavor to exit, it lacks the ability to re-absorb it into the walls. Key Principles Noten Ta'am Li-Fgam (Spoiled Flavor) From the Torah's perspective, any flavor absorbed in a utensil for 24 hours or more is considered rotten/spoiled (pagum). It would not add any benefit to food cooked in it. Therefore, from the Torah, a clean pot unused for 24 hours may be used to cook kosher food — even if it previously cooked completely non-kosher food. The Chachamim forbade this le-chatchila — concerned a person might use it within 24 hours. Be-di'avad: If you accidentally cooked in a chametz pot that was clean and unused for 24+ hours, even on Pesach itself — the food is permitted. Situation Ruling Le-chatchila Do not cook in a chametz pot, even after 24 hours, without kashering Be-di'avad (accidentally used it after 24 hours) Food is permitted — even on Pesach (Sephardim) Ashkenazim are stringent and say noten ta'am li-fgam does not apply on Pesach. Sephardim hold that regular year-round rules apply, and therefore noten ta'am li-fgam is mutar even on Pesach. Kli Rishon, Irui, and Kli Sheni Term Definition Example Kli Rishon (primary utensil) The pot in which food was cooked on the fire Pot on the stove Irui Kli Rishon (pouring from primary) The stream poured from a kli rishon Pouring from a kettle. Can cook only \"k'dei klipah\" — the outer layer. Kli Sheni (secondary utensil) The vessel receiving the poured liquid Bowl, cup, plate that food is poured/served into Rov Tashmish (Majority Use) — SEPHARDIM ONLY This is a critical principle, often unknown: You kasher according to the majority use of the utensil — not its occasional use. Scenario Required Kashering Pot usually used with liquid, occasionally used dry Hag'alah (not libun) — follow the majority Plate/cutlery usually used with cold, occasionally used with hot Just wash — follow the majority Cup usually used with cold drinks, once or twice used with hot Just wash Ashkenazim do NOT hold this. If a utensil was used even once with chametz in a manner requiring hag'alah/libun, they require the full process. Why does this work? After 24 hours, retained flavor is only problematic mi-de-rabbanan. The Chachamim only imposed this concern for regular/majority usage — not for occasional, irregular use. Heitera Bala vs. Issura Bala (Permissible vs. Prohibited Absorption) Type When the Flavor Was Absorbed Kashering Required Heitera bala The food was permissible at the time of absorption (e.g., chametz during the year — permitted to eat) Even dry use → only hag'alah needed (Rabbeinu Tam) Issura bala The food was prohibited at the time of absorption (e.g., non-kosher meat) Dry use → libun required Machloket on chametz: Is chametz absorbed during the year considered heitera bala (it was permissible when you cooked it) or issura bala (because it's forbidden on Pesach)? Opinion Chametz Is... Consequence Rabbeinu Tam Heitera bala — permissible absorption Even dry chametz use → hag'alah suffices Rif / Shulchan Aruch Issura bala — treated as prohibited absorption Dry chametz use → libun required The Shulchan Aruch follows the stricter view — but Rabbeinu Tam's opinion is used as a factor in lenient rulings (safek sefeika). Practical Process of Hag'alah Before Kashering The utensil should not be used for 24 hours before kashering (le-chatchila). If you did kasher within 24 hours — it's still acceptable be-di'avad. Clean thoroughly — scrub away all dirt and rust. If heavily rusted, don't kasher it. Good practice: use chemicals when cleaning (ensures noten ta'am li-fgam even if within 24 hours). The Process Take a large pot and boil water in it (this pot can be either chametz or Pesach — either is fine). Place the utensil into the boiling water. Does not need to be done in one go (unlike tevilat kelim). You can dip half, then turn it around and dip the other half. Can leave utensils at the bottom — no requirement to remove immediately. Good minhag: Wash with cold water immediately upon removal (to prevent re-absorption while still hot). Not essential — if you didn't, it's fine. Timing All hag'alah should be completed by the morning of Erev Pesach (the fifth hour of the day). If done after this time, additional complex laws apply. Practical Guide: Utensil by Utensil Metal Plates & Bowls (Kli Sheni) Plates and bowls into which food is poured from a pot are kli sheni. Kashering: Pour boiling water into/onto them (irui kli rishon). Strictly speaking (Chacham Ovadia): Kli sheni does not absorb or expel — me'ikar ha-din all plates could be used for Pesach just by washing. However, as a chumra for Pesach, we pour boiling water on them. Be-di'avad: If you accidentally served hot Pesach food onto metal chametz plates — the food is permitted (Sephardim only, not Ashkenazim). Cutlery (Normally Kli Sheni) Almost all cutlery is used as kli sheni (eating from plates, not cooking on fire). Kashering: Pour boiling water from a kettle over all the cutlery (place in a bowl, pour over). This is sufficient. Exception: Knives or utensils used directly on the fire (stirring pots on the stove) — these are kli rishon and need full hag'alah (dipping in boiling water). Cutlery used only with cold food during the year: Just wash. No kashering needed. Even if used once or twice with hot — follow rov tashmish (Sephardim). Cups Used Only for Cold Drinks Just wash and use for Pesach. Cold cannot absorb. Pots and Pans Used with Liquid Hag'alah — dip in boiling water. Standard process. Pans Greased with Minimal Oil (Just to Prevent Burning) Machloket: Some say this counts as \"dry\" (needs libun → can't kasher). Others say the oil counts as liquid (hag'alah suffices). Chacham Ovadia: Lenient — hag'alah is sufficient. Based on a safek sefeika combining the oil question with Rabbeinu Tam's heitera bala opinion. Non-Stick Pans The entire purpose is cooking without liquid (dry). This requires libun, which will destroy the non-stick coating. Put them away for Pesach. Cannot be kashered. Earthenware / Porcelain / Glazed China Used With... Can It Be Kashered? Hot food during the year ❌ Cannot be kashered. Must be put away and locked/cordoned off. Cannot even use for cold on Pesach (gezeirah — might come to use with heat). Only cold food during the year ✅ Immerse in cold water for three 24-hour periods → can then be used on Pesach even for hot food. Chacham Ovadia rules glazed chinaware and porcelain have the same status as earthenware. Glass Glass does not absorb and does not expel. Just wash and use for Pesach — even if used with hot chametz during the year. This is not a leniency — it is the din of the Shulchan Aruch. Includes: Drinking glasses, glass bowls, glass serving dishes. Pyrex: Chacham Ovadia holds Pyrex has the same status as glass — just wash, can be used to cook in the oven on Pesach. Ashkenazim: Do not hold this regarding glass. However, combined with rov tashmish and the safek whether glass absorbs, there is often a sfek sfeka to rely on. Plastics Chacham Ovadia holds plastics can be kashered the same way as metals — following the same rules (hag'alah if used with liquid, etc.). Note: the process may damage the plastic. Microwave Chacham Ovadia: Be machmir — treat it as if it absorbs. Process: Clean thoroughly Wait 24 hours Place a bowl of water inside Run on highest setting for a few minutes until steam fills the interior Good to go Dishwasher Sephardim: Can be used for Pesach. Process: Clean all trays and grooves thoroughly — ensure no chametz remains Run a full cycle on the highest setting with detergent Good to go — no separate Pesach dishwasher needed Rationale: The chemicals and heat during regular cycles make all absorbed flavors completely mutar. (Chacham Ovadia holds the same principle year-round — a dishwasher can even be used for meat and milk separately.) Oven Process: Clean thoroughly (use oven cleaner/chemicals) If it has self-clean → run the self-clean cycle. Done. If no self-clean → clean well, then run on high for about an hour. Good to go. Oven racks: Self-clean may damage them. If you don't want to risk it → buy new racks. Racks cannot be kashered with hag'alah (they're used dry/with direct heat). Stovetop / Hob (Gas Burners) Process: Clean thoroughly — burners and all parts Turn on all burners on high for approximately one hour Good to go Glass Induction Hob It's glass. Just wash it. Glass doesn't absorb. Sinks Pour boiling water directly onto every part of the sink. Critical: Do not pour onto one spot and let it trickle — each part must receive direct irui (pouring). Go in circles around the basin, then up the walls. Chacham Ovadia notes this is itself a chumra — it's unlikely the sink ever absorbed at yad soledet bo temperature. But we do it. Alternative: If you prefer, just cover the sink. But kashering properly means no covering is needed. Countertops If used with hot items during the year: Pour boiling water directly over every part (same as sinks — don't just pour in one spot and let it trickle). If only used with cold items: Just clean. No kashering needed. If you always use trivets for hot pots: Arguably the countertop was only used cold — just cleaning may suffice (based on rov tashmish). Alternative: Cover with foil instead of kashering. You do NOT need to do both. Either kasher OR cover. Both is unnecessary. Hot Plate (Plata / Shabbat Plate) Process: Clean thoroughly (use chemicals) Wait 24 hours Turn on high for about an hour Good to go — no foil covering needed if kashered properly Fire safety note: Israeli fire services have warned against covering hot plates with foil — heat reflects downward and can reach dangerous temperatures (200°C+). If you must keep food warm, place pans in disposable foil containers on the hot plate instead. Urn / Water Heater If only used for boiling water (tea, coffee): Clean and use for Pesach. No chametz contact. If the lid was used to warm challot etc.: Kasher the lid with hag'alah (dip in boiling water). The body of the urn is fine. Soda Machine All cold. Just clean and use. Barbecue Spit / Grill Grates Used dry with direct fire → requires libun (red hot). Cannot practically be done today. Cannot be kashered for Pesach. Buy new or put away. Kiddush Cup (Silver) If only used for cold grape juice/wine during the year → just wash. No kashering needed (both Sephardim and Ashkenazim agree). Summary Table Utensil Method Notes Metal pots (used with liquid) Hag'alah Wait 24 hours, dip in boiling water Metal plates/bowls Irui (pour boiling water) Kli sheni — be-di'avad just washing is fine Cutlery (eating) Irui (pour boiling water) Almost always kli sheni Cutlery (used on fire) Hag'alah Kli rishon — dip in boiling water Pans with minimal oil Hag'alah Chacham Ovadia lenient — safek sefeika Non-stick pans Cannot kasher Needs libun → put away Earthenware / china (hot use) Cannot kasher Lock away for Pesach Earthenware (cold use only) 3 × 24 hours cold water Then usable even for hot on Pesach Glass / Pyrex Just wash Does not absorb (Sephardim) Plastic Same as metal May damage the plastic Microwave Steam with water on high Clean, wait 24 hours, bowl of water, few minutes Dishwasher Clean + run hot cycle No separate Pesach dishwasher needed Oven Self-clean or high heat ~1 hour Racks may need replacing Stovetop / hob High heat ~1 hour Clean burners first Glass induction hob Just wash Glass doesn't absorb Sink Direct irui everywhere Don't let water trickle — pour directly Countertops Direct irui or cover Either kasher OR cover, not both Hot plate Clean + high heat ~1 hour No foil needed if kashered Urn Clean (kasher lid if used with challah) Body fine if only used for water Soda machine Just clean All cold BBQ spit/grill Cannot kasher Needs libun → put away Kiddush cup (cold use) Just wash No kashering needed","XbXUKD0Fdoo":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Siman: 318 (Bishul — Cooking on Shabbat) Seifim: 17, 18, 19, 11, 12 Topics: Melting Congealed Solids (Nolad), Stirring Pots on the Fire (Hagasah), Adding Spices to Roasted Meat, Mixing Hot & Cold Water Recap: What We've Covered in Siman 318 Seifim 1–2: Ma'aseh Shabbat; cooking for a choleh Seif 3: Sun, toldot hachamah; microwave; dud shemesh Seif 4: Major machlokot — ma'achal Ben Drosai, bishul achar bishul b'lach, kli sheni, irui kli rishon, kalei habishul, kli shlishi Seif 5: Bishul achar afiyah — toast, coffee, tea Seifim 6–10, 13–15: Yad soledet bo defined, stacking pots, food on an urn, salt, irui kli rishon, placing near fire, davar gush Seif י\"ז (Tet Zayin): Melting Congealed Solids — Nolad Background: Why Can't You Crush Snow on Shabbat? The Gemara states clearly: אין מרסקין לא את השלג ולא את הברד Forbidden to crush snow or hail with your hands But: אבל נותנו לתוך הכוס ואינו חושש — you CAN put a piece of snow/ice into a cup of water and let it melt on its own Think of this as: ice cubes. You cannot crush an ice cube with your hand. But you can drop it into your drink. Three Reasons in the Rishonim Why Crushing Snow Is Forbidden All agree it's an issur derabanan. The question is: which rabbinic prohibition? 1. Rashi — Makeh B'Patish / Molid / Tikkun Mana You're creating something new — before there was snow, now you've created water Similar to makeh b'patish (final hammer blow) on a rabbinic level Putting in a cup of water = gets mixed in immediately → doesn't look like creating something new → permitted 2. Many Rishonim (seemingly the Rambam too) — Dash (Threshing) Dash = extracting liquid from something in an outer casing Crushing snow isn't technically dash — there's no outer casing But it's similar (domeh) — you may come to squeeze fruit (grapes etc.) where you ARE extracting from an outer layer Chachamim forbade crushing snow as a gezeirah Putting in a cup = results aren't clearly visible → permitted 3. Sefer HaTerumah (Rabbeinu Baruch MiMagenza) — Nolad (Muktzeh) On a rabbinic level, something NEW is being created It's a kind of muktzeh — a new entity comes into existence Chachamim forbade it — but not when it automatically mixes into something else The Critical Practical Difference Between the Opinions According to Rashi and the Dash opinion: The problem is the active action — crushing, squeezing If ice melts by itself on the table → no problem at all You haven't done any action similar to a melacha According to Rabbeinu Baruch (Nolad): The problem is the new substance being created — solid becoming liquid Even if it happens by itself → still a problem! Because something new (a visible liquid) is being formed Consequence for Ashkenazim (who are machmir like Rabbeinu Baruch): Cannot leave ice cubes out on the table to melt on their own CAN put ice cubes into a drink (the nolad mixes in immediately → no visible new entity) CAN make ice cubes on Shabbat (liquid → solid is NOT the same issue — nolad is specifically solid → liquid) For Sefardim (who follow the Shulchan Aruch / first two opinions): CAN leave ice cubes out to melt on their own — no problem The only prohibition is actively crushing with your hands Can You Crush Ice in Your Mouth? Yes — permitted for everyone In your mouth it's not visible — the results go straight in Same logic as putting ice in a cup — the new liquid isn't a visible separate entity The Shulchan Aruch's Ruling — The Pashtida/Inpanada מותר ליתן אינפנאדה כנגד האש במקום שיד סולדת בו ואף על פי שהשומן שבה שנקרש חוזר ונימוח A pashtida = pastry with meat inside, very oily food You may place it near the fire where it reaches yad soledet bo Even though the congealed oil melts and becomes a layer of liquid → permitted Reason: you haven't actively done anything — the melting happens mi'meila (by itself) The Shulchan Aruch clearly does NOT follow Rabbeinu Baruch's nolad opinion The Rema's Addition וכל שכן קדרה שיש בו רוטב שנקרש All the more so: a pot with congealed sauce/gravy → when the fat melts and mixes back into the food → permitted Because it mixes in immediately (even Rabbeinu Baruch would agree when it mixes in) But: ויש מחמירין — some are strict (= Rabbeinu Baruch / nolad) ונהגו להחמיר... מיהו במקום צורך יש לסמוך על סברא ראשונה Ashkenazi minhag: be strict in the first instance But if there's a need → may rely on the main (lenient) opinion The Mishnah Berurah's Clarification Even for the machmirim — the problem is only with a substantial amount of oil/liquid If just a small amount of fat comes out (as with any good piece of meat on a hotplate) → fine for everyone The issue is only when so much oil melts that it forms its own visible, recognizable layer on top Mishnah Berurah's Additional Leniency for Ashkenazim Putting the pashtida on the hotplate before the hotplate turns on (via timer) → permitted Similar to Chacham Ovadia's ruling about reheating liquids via gramma Since you didn't do the action — the heat came on by itself afterwards Two Important Practical Takeaways for Sefardim No issue with melting congealed substances mi'meila — leave ice cubes out, reheat oily foods freely Congealed liquid = currently a solid — therefore even though it will become liquid when heated, bishul achar bishul b'lach does NOT apply at the moment of placement. You can take congealed soup/sauce from the fridge and reheat it because right now it's a solid. Asymmetry: Solid → Liquid vs. Liquid → Solid Question from the shiur: If you can't melt ice (solid → liquid), can you freeze water (liquid → solid)? Making ice cubes on Shabbat: Permitted — even for Ashkenazim Rabbeinu Baruch's nolad concern is specifically solid → liquid (a new flowing substance is \"born\") Liquid → solid = just a formation/hardening — not the same kind of \"new creation\" Vast majority of Ashkenazi poskim permit making ice cubes For Sefardim: certainly permitted (we don't hold of nolad at all in this context) Seif י\"ח (Yud Chet): Stirring Pots on the Fire — הגסה (Hagasah) The Gemara's Statement The Gemara in Beitzah says multiple people can be chayav for one act of bishul: One person puts the pot on → chayav (because of libun — tempering a new pot on the fire) One person puts food in → chayav One person stirs → chayav mishum bishul Stirring = an issur de'oraita of bishul (when the food is not fully cooked) Why Is Stirring Bishul? By stirring food on the fire, you are memaher bishulo — speeding up the cooking process This constitutes an act of bishul Practical Note About Urns From the same logic: ensure your urn is fully boiled before Shabbat If you open the urn tap when the water hasn't fully boiled → you're causing mixing/movement inside → speeding up the cooking process Not d'oraita (it's grama — indirect) — but still problematic The Major Machloket: Stirring FULLY COOKED Food Most Rishonim (Rashba, Ramban, Ran): Stirring is only an issue when food is NOT fully cooked Once fully cooked (nitbashel kol tzorko) → אין בישול אחר בישול → stirring is fine The Kol Bo (and possibly the Rambam): Even fully cooked food → there is an issue with stirring on the fire The Rambam's language (Perek 3, Halacha 11): אסור להכניס מגרפה לקדרה בשבת והיא על האש... מפני שמגיס בה וזה מצרכי בישול הוא The Rambam doesn't differentiate between fully cooked and not The Kol Bo learns from this: stirring is always forbidden on the fire The Mishnah Berurah's Difficulty ולא ידעתי הדבר — \"I don't understand this\" We have אין בישול אחר בישול — if fully cooked, what's the problem? If cholent becomes crispier overnight on the hotplate (מצטמק ויפה לו) → that's mutar So why would stirring be different? Three Reasons Given by Acharonim Why Stirring Fully Cooked Food Is Problematic 1. Igros Moshe (Rav Moshe Feinstein) — Unexposed Portions The dish as a whole = nitbashel kol tzorko (fully cooked, fully edible) But within it, there are always bits not fully cooked By stirring → you expose those bits to direct heat → they become fully cooked That's a new act of bishul on those specific portions 2. Chazon Ish — New Dimension True: אין בישול אחר בישול — reheating and making crispier is fine But stirring on the fire adds a qualitatively new dimension to the food It's not just \"more heat\" — it's a different kind of transformation (May need to ask a chef to fully explain this) 3. Gezeirah (Derabanan Reason) Don't stir fully cooked food → so you won't come to stir food that's NOT fully cooked Since stirring not-fully-cooked food = clear issur de'oraita → Chachamim made a fence Stirring vs. Taking Out with a Ladle The Ran deduces from the Gemara a distinction: Action Status Reasoning Stirring (circular motion on the fire) De'oraita (if not fully cooked) Memaher bishulo — speeding cooking Taking out with a ladle (half-circular motion) Derabanan Similar to stirring but less intense The Shulchan Aruch's Ruling האלפס והקדרה שהעבירן רותחין מעל גבי האש Even if you took the pot off the fire (kli rishon, still yad soledet bo): If NOT fully cooked: Cannot stir → issur de'oraita (whether on or off the fire, as long as yad soledet bo) Cannot take out with a ladle → issur derabanan (similar to stirring) If fully cooked: Can take out with a ladle — whether on or off the fire (Beis Yosef clarifies: even on the fire) Stirring on the fire → the Shulchan Aruch doesn't explicitly rule, but poskim are machmir The Rema's Chumra ולכתחילה יש להיזהר אף בקדירה בכל עניין Be careful even off the fire, even just to take out The Mishnah Berurah says: No one actually takes this on. It's too strict. But: one should be machmir not to stir when fully cooked (on the fire) To take out → everyone is lenient Practical Summary: Stirring & Taking Out For Sefardim (Chacham Ovadia / Beis Yosef): Scenario Stir? Take out with ladle? Not fully cooked, on fire Asur de'oraita Asur derabanan Not fully cooked, off fire (yad soledet bo) Asur de'oraita Asur derabanan Fully cooked, on fire Should not (machmir — Kol Bo) Mutar Fully cooked, off fire (yad soledet bo) Mutar Mutar Fully cooked, off fire, cooled below yad soledet bo Mutar Mutar For Ashkenazim (Mishnah Berurah): Scenario Stir? Take out with ladle? Not fully cooked (on or off fire, yad soledet bo) Asur de'oraita Asur derabanan Fully cooked, on fire Should not Mutar Fully cooked, off fire (still yad soledet bo) Should not (machmir) Mutar Fully cooked, off fire, cooled below yad soledet bo Mutar Mutar Rav Moshe Feinstein's Leniency for Hotplates Rav Moshe suggests: the entire issue of stirring fully cooked food may only apply on an actual fire/flame A hotplate (plata) may not have the same intensity → the \"new dimension\" doesn't apply Rabbi Kada noted: Chacham Ovadia doesn't discuss this Practical recommendation: still don't stir on the hotplate even if fully cooked — to be safe Seif י\"ט (Yud Tet): Adding Spices to Roasting Meat אסור לטוח שמן ושום על הצלי בעודו כנגד המדורה Cannot put oil or garlic on roasted meat while it's still near the fire Even if the meat was fully roasted before Shabbat (אפילו נצלה מבעוד יום) Reason: the garlic and oil themselves will become cooked Straightforward halacha — the meat may be done, but the additions are not Seifim י\"א–י\"ב (Yud Alef — Yud Bet): Mixing Hot & Cold Water Background These seifim are extremely complex in the Rishonim (8+ opinions, 3 ways to understand the Rambam) Rabbi Kada noted: spent approximately two weeks on this in kollel Not highly practical in everyday life — covering the main takeaway Seif Yud Alef: The Ambati (Large Bathtub) The Rambam's words (quoted by the Shulchan Aruch): אמבטי שהיא מלאה מים חמין אין נותנים לתוכה מים צונן שהרי מחממין הרבה A large bathtub full of very hot water → cannot add cold water Because: the hot water is so abundant that the cold water will automatically be heated to yad soledet bo אבל נותן הוא ממים חמין שבזה האמבטי לתוך אמבטי אחר של צונן But: you CAN pour from the hot bath into another bath of cold water Because: you're only pouring a small amount of hot into a large amount of cold → the result becomes lukewarm, not yad soledet bo The Rema adds (Tosfot/Rosh): אף על פי שהוא כלי שני Even though the ambati is a kli sheni — because it's such a huge volume and so hot, it has cooking ability The Rambam may not hold of this chiddush (unclear if he considers it kli rishon or kli sheni — major debate) Seif Yud Bet: Pouring from a Vessel After Removing Hot Water מיחם שפינה ממנו מים חמים מותר ליתן לתוכו מים צונן מרובים כדי להפשירן A vessel where you removed the hot water and some hot water remains → you CAN add a large amount of cold water to make it lukewarm ומותר לצוק מים חמים לתוך מים צונן או צונן לתוך חמים והוא שלא בכלי ראשון You may mix hot into cold or cold into hot — not in a kli rishon (because a kli rishon can heat it too much) ומים מרובים כל כך שאי אפשר שיתבשלו... אפילו בכלי ראשון שרי אלא כשלא על האש If the cold water is so abundant that it cannot reach yad soledet bo → permitted even in a kli rishon (off the fire) Why Doesn't Irui Apply When Mixing Hot & Cold Liquids? We learned irui kli rishon is forbidden — so why can you pour hot water into cold? Answer: with liquids , the particles mix immediately The cold water absorbs and neutralizes the heat instantly As long as the resulting mixture won't reach yad soledet bo → permitted Main Practical Takeaway You may pour cold water into hot water (or vice versa) as long as the result won't reach yad soledet bo Not in a kli rishon that's on the fire — never put anything on the fire In a kli rishon off the fire → only if the cold water is so abundant it can't reach yad soledet bo In a kli sheni → permitted freely (כלי שני אינו מבשל) Completion of Melechet Bishul — Siman 318 This shiur concludes the study of Siman 318 — Melechet Bishul. The siman contains 19 seifim covering: The consequences of cooking on Shabbat (ma'aseh Shabbat) Sun cooking, toldot hachamah, and practical applications Bishul achar bishul — solids, liquids, and the Rema's position Kli rishon, kli sheni, kli shlishi — the Ashkenazi/Sefardi divide Kalei habishul and irui kli rishon Bishul achar afiyah — toast, coffee, tea Yad soledet bo — the 45°C–71°C range Placing food on urns, near fire, stacking pots Salt, meat, and specific exceptions Nolad — melting congealed substances Hagasah — stirring pots on the fire Mixing hot and cold water Key Practical Summary for This Shiur Melting congealed food (Sefardim): No issue — congealed sauce from the fridge can be reheated freely (currently a solid, so bishul achar bishul b'lach doesn't apply) Melting congealed food (Ashkenazim): If a substantial layer of liquid forms → machmir in the first instance. If small amount → fine. Putting on hotplate before it turns on → always fine. Ice cubes: Sefardim can leave out to melt. Ashkenazim should put in drinks, not leave to melt independently. Both can make ice cubes on Shabbat. Stirring: Never stir a pot on the fire on Shabbat, even if fully cooked. Take the pot off first (Sefardim: then you can stir if fully cooked). Taking out with a ladle: If fully cooked → permitted even on the fire (Sefardim). Ashkenazim agree for taking out but not stirring. Don't add raw spices/garlic to meat near the fire — even if the meat is fully cooked, the additions will cook. Mixing hot & cold water: Permitted as long as the result can't reach yad soledet bo. Not in a kli rishon on the fire. Chodesh Tov — Rosh Chodesh Nisan Chag Pesach Kasher v'Sameach","B34G4lyNAL4":"With Rabbi Daniel Kada Dedicated le-ilui nishmat Avraham ben Moshe ve-Flora Chaya Marokim (Albert Mani z\"l) — a student of Chacham Ovadia Yosef Part 1: Cleaning & Searching the House The Torah Prohibition: Bal Yera'eh u-Val Yimatzei The Torah (Parashat Bo) states: \"Ve-lo yera'eh lecha chametz\" — it is forbidden to own chametz on Pesach. This constitutes two separate negative prohibitions (lo ta'aseh): Bal yera'eh — it shall not be seen (i.e., owned) by you Bal yimatzei — it shall not be found (i.e., owned) by you Critical distinction: The prohibition is on owning chametz, not on seeing it. If chametz is in your home but doesn't belong to you, there is no Torah prohibition. Bitul (Nullification) — The Torah-Level Solution From the Torah's perspective, a person can simply nullify ownership of their chametz (bitul) and that is sufficient. Even if the chametz physically remains in the house — as long as you have genuinely abandoned ownership, there is no Torah violation. This is done by declaring: \"Kol chamira de-ika bi-reshuti... le-batil ve-lehevei hefker ke-afra de-ar'a\" — all chametz in my possession is nullified and ownerless like the dust of the earth. The test of sincerity: If someone walks into your home and takes all your chametz, you cannot be upset or object — because it doesn't belong to you anymore. Why the Chachamim Required More Than Bitul The Chachamim instituted that bitul alone is not sufficient. You must also physically dispose of chametz. Two reasons: Reason Explanation Insincere nullification If you have hundreds of pounds of chametz, the Chachamim were concerned you won't genuinely abandon ownership. You'll say the words but not mean them. Temptation Even if the nullification is sincere, having chametz around you creates a risk — you might forget it's Pesach and eat it. The Major Question: What Must You Dispose Of? This is where the misconception arises. When the Chachamim said \"dispose of chametz,\" what exactly did they mean? Opinion What You Must Search For Reasoning Minority opinion Everything — including crumbs Even crumbs could tempt a person Majority opinion Only a ke-zayit (substantial piece) A person won't fail to sincerely nullify crumbs, and won't be tempted by crumbs A ke-zayit = approximately 17–27 grams. Not crumbs. A small piece of bread, something visible and substantial. The Halacha (Chacham Ovadia and Many Poskim) Since this is a rabbinic mitzvah (the Torah only requires bitul) and the logic supports the second opinion: When searching for chametz, you are looking for PIECES of chametz — not crumbs. This alone should save hours of Pesach preparation. The One Exception: Places Used With Food on Pesach Location What to Search For Kitchen (where you cook/bake) Crumbs — because food is prepared here and crumbs may mix into Pesach food Dining table (where you eat) Crumbs — same reason, food contact Everywhere else in the house Only pieces of chametz (ke-zayit or more) Why crumbs matter in the kitchen/table: Not because of the prohibition of owning chametz (which only applies to a ke-zayit), but because of the prohibition of eating chametz — crumbs near food preparation could get mixed into your Pesach food. Where Do You Need to Check? General rule (Shulchan Aruch): You only need to check places where chametz is brought in during the year. Place Need to Check? Notes Kitchen ✅ Yes — thoroughly (crumbs too) Food preparation area Dining room ✅ Yes — thoroughly (crumbs too) Eating area Bedrooms where you eat ✅ Yes — pieces only Only if food is brought in during the year Bedrooms where you never eat ❌ No No chametz brought in = no need to check Bathroom cupboards ❌ No Not a place where food is brought Shed ❌ No (usually) Unless you bring food there Garden ✅ Yes — quick check for pieces If you eat/picnic there. Takes 5 minutes. Car ✅ Yes Commonly a place where people eat Wine storeroom ❌ No Shulchan Aruch's own example The Exception: Young Children in the House If there are toddlers/young children (below the age of responsibility): You must check everywhere — bathrooms, toilets, every room — because the Gemara says children are like mice: they carry chametz everywhere. However: Only check where the children can reach. If you have a high cupboard they can't access and you never put food there — no need to check it. Specific Common Questions Pockets: Do you need to check your pockets? If you never put unwrapped food in your pockets → No need to check. If you regularly put food in your pockets → Yes, check for crumbs (because you might put food in the pocket on Pesach and it'll mix with chametz crumbs). Adults generally don't need to. Children's pockets — yes, check them. Books: Do you need to check books for crumbs? If the books will not be used while eating on Pesach → No need to check. Crumbs in books are nullified. If the books will be used while eating (e.g., Birkat HaMazon booklets) → Yes, check them. Or better: use different copies for Pesach. Garden: Do you need to do bedikat chametz in the garden? Yes, if you eat/bring food there. Walk around, check for pieces. Takes 5 minutes. If the last time anyone ate in the garden was many months ago (e.g., last summer in England), the food would have been consumed by animals or become inedible — no need to check. Bedikat Chametz — The Night-Time Search The Chachamim instituted a formal search for chametz on the night before Pesach . Why at night? People are busy during the day A flame/candle provides focused light for checking behind furniture, in corners, etc. When to do it: Best: from nightfall (~8:45 PM in London area) Acceptable: from sunset (~8:00 PM) — since it's a mitzvah de-rabbanan, bein ha-shmashot is fine Especially if young children want to participate — can start from sunset If you missed the night: When You Remember What to Do Next morning (Erev Pesach) Do bedikat chametz during the day During Pesach / Chol HaMoed Still do bedikat chametz — even on the 8th day After Pesach is over Still do bedikat chametz — because chametz she-avar alav ha-Pesach is asur be-hana'ah (chametz owned over Pesach is permanently forbidden) Places already checked: Any room thoroughly cleaned before bedikat chametz night — where no chametz has entered since — does not need to be checked again on the night of bedikat chametz. Bi'ur Chametz — Disposing of Chametz Tradition Method Sephardim (me'ikar ha-din) Any form of disposal: crush it, throw it in a public bin, pour chemicals on it to make it inedible. Burning is not required. Ashkenazim (minhag) Burn the chametz The Bracha: Al Bi'ur Chametz (Not Bedikat Chametz) The blessing recited before the search is \"al bi'ur chametz\" (on the disposal of chametz) — not \"al bedikat chametz\" (on the searching). Why? Because searching is the beginning of a process that concludes with disposal the next morning. The main purpose of searching is to dispose. The Chachamim made the bracha on the end goal, not the preliminary step. The Minhag of Placing 10 Pieces Origin: The Arizal instituted placing specifically 10 pieces of chametz around the house before the search. The Ra'avad's criticism: \"Minhag nashim hu\" — a custom of the unlearned. The mitzvah isn't to find chametz; it's to search and then dispose. If you search and find nothing — you've still fulfilled the mitzvah. The Chacham Tzvi would mock this practice. Nevertheless — it is our minhag and we do it. But two critical points: Don't turn bedikat chametz into a treasure hunt for 10 pieces. The purpose is to search the house for chametz. Finding the 10 pieces is incidental — not the goal. Use pieces smaller than a ke-zayit. If you lose one of the 10 pieces, you don't need to turn the house upside down — because as we learned, you don't need to search for less than a ke-zayit. Just do bitul and you're fine. Part 2: Hechsherim — What Needs a Pesach Certification? Two Foundational Principles Principle 1 — Chametz on Pesach is not nullified (be-mashehu): Unlike regular kashrus where 1-in-60 nullifies a forbidden substance, on Pesach itself even a drop of chametz that falls into food makes the entire food forbidden. However: This stringency only applies on Pesach itself . Before Pesach, regular nullification (1-in-60) works. The Sephardi position: Chametz that was nullified before Pesach does not reawaken on Pesach (ein chozer ve-ne'or). This is a critical distinction from the Ashkenazi position. Position Before Pesach nullification On Pesach itself Sephardim Works. Stays nullified even on Pesach. A drop of chametz prohibits everything. Ashkenazim The nullification \"reawakens\" on Pesach (chozer ve-ne'or). A drop of chametz prohibits everything. Principle 2 — Achzukei issura lo machzikinan: We do not assume something went wrong unless we see it go wrong. The Shulchan Aruch gives the example of meat, cheese, and fish set aside for Pesach — even if no one was careful to guard them from chametz — the food is permitted, because we don't assume chametz fell in. What Does NOT Need a Pesach Hechsher (For Sephardim) Based on these two principles, anything with pure, natural ingredients bought before Pesach does not need a Pesach hechsher: Item Hechsher Needed? Notes Water ❌ No Salt ❌ No Sugar ❌ No Pure tea (no additives) ❌ No Pure coffee / instant coffee ❌ No Non-flavored Pure oil ❌ No 100% fruit juice ❌ No Must be pure, no additives Dried fruit ❌ No Chacham Ovadia explicitly permits Pure spices (cinnamon, saffron strands, etc.) ❌ No 100% pure Why buy before Pesach? If bought on Pesach (e.g., Chol HaMoed), there's a possibility the product was manufactured on Pesach itself — and nullification doesn't work on Pesach. Buying before Pesach ensures any incidental chametz contact was nullified before Pesach. After Pesach: Products bought after Pesach are also fine — because the moment Pesach ends, nullification kicks in retroactively. The nullification does not \"reawaken\" the following Pesach. Why do these items have Pesach hechsherim in stores? Because the kashrus market is primarily run according to Ashkenazi standards, where chozer ve-ne'or applies. For Ashkenazim, these items do need certification. The balance: Chacham Ovadia emphasizes — if you can afford to be strict, wonderful. But recognize it is a chumra , not a halachic requirement for Sephardim. And being stringent in one area often leads to leniency elsewhere (chumra ati lidei kula). Tablets, Medications & Vitamins The Rambam's principle: Anything with no satisfaction to the palate (she-ein bo hana'at ha-cheich) does not fall under the prohibition of chametz. The standard: Food is forbidden on Pesach only if it is ra'ui la-achilat kelev (fit for a dog to consume). Anything inedible is not prohibited. Item Hechsher Needed? Reasoning Regular tablets/pills (no flavor) ❌ No No taste, not ra'ui la-achilat kelev. Even if it contains chametz — you're not giving importance to the chametz element (no achshavei). Medications for illness (even mild) ❌ No Even if it contains chametz. A person who is unwell may take any medication. Very slight headache (Chacham Ovadia) ⚠️ Preferably avoid if you know it has chametz But plenty to rely on if you take it Vitamins (Rav Moshe Feinstein) ❌ No No taste → permitted even though taken for health, not illness Flavored medications / syrups (e.g., Calpol for children) ✅ Yes — check ingredients Flavor is intentionally added → hana'ah la-cheich → needs checking Cosmetics & Toothpaste Item Hechsher Needed? Reasoning Lipstick ❌ No Not being consumed. Not ra'ui la-achilat kelev. Sichah ke-achilah (anointing = eating) — we don't hold of this. Creams, perfumes, cosmetics ❌ No Not ingested, not edible Toothpaste ⚠️ Some say yes Has intentional flavor, goes in mouth. Some Poskim recommend a hechsher. Others argue you have no interest in the chametz element. Understandable to be strict here. Existing cosmetics from during the year: No need to buy new ones. Look at the lipstick — if there's visible chametz on it, put it away. If not, use it (or wipe off one layer). Part 3: Kitniyot Kitniyot Is NOT Chametz Chametz = only the five grains: wheat, barley, spelt, oats, rye. Nothing else can be chametz. Kitniyot (legumes, rice, beans, corn, etc.) is a minhag — a custom that evolved among European Ashkenazim in the 13th century. Reasons given include: grains of wheat were commonly mixed with legumes, leading to confusion. Chacham Ben Tzion Abba Shaul's observation: People are extremely particular about avoiding even a derivative of kitniyot — yet the food most statistically likely to contain chametz on Pesach is matzah itself (timing errors during baking, water contact, going over 18 minutes). A person who eats nothing but matzah all Pesach to avoid kitniyot may actually be increasing their chametz exposure. Practical Implications Ashkenazim eating at a Sephardi home: An Ashkenazi who doesn't eat kitniyot may eat at a Sephardi home where kitniyot is served. Food cooked in a pot that had kitniyot 24 hours earlier is absolutely fine for Ashkenazim — because kitniyot is not real chametz. The ta'am (absorbed flavor) of kitniyot in the pot is not a problem. An Ashkenazi woman married to a Sephardi man: She may eat kitniyot. She does not need hatarat nedarim according to Chacham Ovadia (though this is a broader discussion). Summary Topic Key Takeaway Searching the house Look for pieces of chametz (ke-zayit+), not crumbs. Crumbs only matter in kitchen/dining area. Where to check Only places where chametz is brought during the year. Not bathrooms, not sheds, not high cupboards. Children Check everywhere they can reach — they carry chametz like mice. Garden, car Quick check for pieces. Books, pockets Only if used with food on Pesach / if you put food in pockets. Bedikat chametz Night before Pesach. Already-cleaned rooms don't need rechecking. Bi'ur chametz Sephardim: any disposal method. Ashkenazim: burning. 10 pieces Use small pieces (< ke-zayit). Don't make the search about finding them. Hechsherim Pure natural products bought before Pesach: no hechsher needed (Sephardim). Medications Tablets without flavor: fine. Flavored syrups: check. Vitamins: fine. Cosmetics No hechsher needed. Toothpaste: some recommend checking. Kitniyot Not chametz. Ashkenazim can eat at Sephardi homes freely.","mSwuIE9gets":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444:1 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; Erev Pesach on Shabbat (intro) Class 6 444:2–5, 445 Erev Pesach on Shabbat (continued): bitul/bi'ur schedule; disposing on Shabbat; bi'ur chametz methods Class 7 446–447:1–2 Finding chametz during Pesach; muktze on Yom Tov; kashrut principles; be-mashehu rule Class 8 447:4, 5, 10 Chozer ve-ne'or (reawakening); practical implications for hechsherim; noten ta'am li-fgam on Pesach Class 9 448 Chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach; mechirat chametz — origins, kinyanim, and practical details Class 10 451:1–4, 6 Hag'alat Kelim Part 1: Torah sources, libun vs hag'alah, rov tashmisho, earthenware, knives, bowls Class 11 451:5, 7–26 Hag'alat Kelim Part 2: Pans, Teflon, glass, sinks, covering surfaces, cold absorption Class 12 471–472 The Seder night: preparing when Erev Pesach falls on Shabbat, reclining (hasabah), the four cups, matzah on Erev Pesach, women's obligations, engaging children Siman 471 – Erev Pesach in the Afternoon Eating on Erev Pesach — What's Permitted and When Matzah on Erev Pesach Shulchan Aruch: It is forbidden to eat matzah on Erev Pesach. From when? Three opinions in the Rishonim: Opinion Prohibition Begins Who Holds Minority opinion All 24 hours before (from the previous night) A minority view Rambam (Shulchan Aruch follows) From the morning of Erev Pesach Middle position The Rosh From midday (chatzot) of Erev Pesach Lenient position This year (Erev Pesach on Shabbat): On Friday night , a person is permitted to eat regular matzah for the Shabbat meals. The prohibition only begins on Shabbat morning. So you can use matzah for lechem mishneh at the Friday night seudah. Matzah Ashirah: Permitted on Erev Pesach all the way until the 10th hour (two hours before the end of the day — approximately 5:00 PM in England). From the 10th hour onward: Don't eat a substantial amount of anything — so you eat the matzah at the Seder with appetite (le-te'avon). A bit of fruit or vegetables is fine. People who get full easily should eat very little. The Shulchan Aruch says you shouldn't even have wine from this time. Children and Matzah on Erev Pesach Rama: Young children may be given matzah to eat on Erev Pesach — since otherwise they'd have nothing substantial to eat (no bread, no matzah for adults). This Year: Preparing the Seder Table on Shabbat The problem: Erev Pesach falls on Shabbat. You cannot prepare from Shabbat for after Shabbat (hachana). Nightfall in London is approximately 8:40 PM. If you only begin setting the table at 8:40, it could take 20–30 minutes with a large gathering — delaying the Seder significantly. The Solution: Ben HaShmashot (Twilight) Principle: It is permissible to do something forbidden rabbinically during ben ha-shmashot (from sunset to nightfall) if it is for a mitzvah purpose (le-tzorech mitzvah). Preparation from Shabbat to Yom Tov is forbidden rabbinically (not de'oraita). Ben ha-shmashot in London this year: from approximately 7:52 PM (sunset) to 8:40 PM (nightfall). If there is a genuine tzorech mitzvah — e.g., many guests, young children who can't stay up, need to begin the Seder on time for proper participation — you may begin setting the table from 7:52 PM. What counts as tzorech mitzvah? Each person must determine this for themselves. Large families, many guests, young children — certainly qualifies. Even a small gathering where timely participation would be enhanced — arguably qualifies. Critical Restrictions — What You CANNOT Do During This Window Even during ben ha-shmashot, you can only do things that are forbidden because of hachana (preparation). You cannot move items that are intrinsically muktzeh : Item Status on Shabbat Can You Prepare It from 7:52? Cutlery, plates, cups Not muktzeh ✅ Yes Seder plate Not muktzeh ✅ Yes Pouring wine into cups Not muktzeh ✅ Yes Havdalah candle Muktzeh (fire) ❌ No — wait until nightfall Matzah Shmura (designated for the Seder) Muktzeh (set aside for a mitzvah — no permissible use on Shabbat) ❌ No — wait until nightfall Regular matzah Not muktzeh (can feed to children) ✅ Yes Why is Matzah Shmura muktzeh? Regular matzah has a use on Shabbat (feeding children on Erev Pesach). But the expensive matzah shmura designated specifically for the Seder has no permissible use on Shabbat — adults can't eat it (Erev Pesach), and you wouldn't feed it to children. Therefore it's muktzeh. Exception: If a person uses matzah shmura for the entire Pesach (all seven days), then all their matzah is the same — arguably not muktzeh on Shabbat. A Note on Regular Matzah and Matzah Shmura Regular Rakuzen machine matzah is essentially matzah shmura (watched from the time of grinding). The person who runs Rakuzen confirmed: they say \"le-shem matzat mitzvah\" on all their matzah, knowing many people rely on it. The expensive hand-baked matzah shmura has two advantages: (1) hand-baked (vs. machine), and (2) the wheat is guarded from harvesting (not just from grinding). Me'ikar ha-din: matzah shmura from the time of grinding suffices. Regular matzah is fine for the mitzvah. Siman 472 – Setting Up the Seder Beginning the Seder — Don't Delay Shulchan Aruch: Set your table in advance. When you return from the synagogue, begin immediately. Don't delay. Why? So the children won't fall asleep and will participate fully. The mitzvah is to keep them engaged. Kiddush: Should only be said after nightfall (approximately 8:40 PM in London). Why not start early, as on a regular Shabbat? On Shabbat, we add from the weekday to Shabbat (tosefet Shabbat) — so Kiddush can be early. On Pesach night: matzah must be eaten at night (de'oraita). The Haggadah must be recited at the same time as the matzah obligation. The four cups are also a nighttime obligation. Therefore, Kiddush (the first of the four cups) should ideally be after nightfall. However — leniencies if it's late: Chacham Ovadia and many Poskim: In the northern hemisphere where nightfall is very late, you may begin Kiddush from sunset — as long as you don't begin Ma Nishtana before nightfall. Extreme circumstances (care homes, elderly, very young children): You can do even more of the Haggadah before nightfall, leaving at least one section and the matzah eating for after nightfall. Havdalah This Year Since it's Motzei Shabbat into Yom Tov, Havdalah is incorporated into Kiddush (yaknahaz — yayin, Kiddush, ner, havdalah, zeman). The Ner (candle) issue: You need a flame for Boreh Me'orei HaEsh. You cannot use electricity for Havdalah (unlike Hadlakat Nerot Shabbat). It must be a naked flame. If starting before Shabbat ends, you'd need an existing flame — not one that's been lit. Practical advice: Unless there's a genuine need, start the Seder after nightfall to avoid all these complications. Hasabah (Reclining) — Detailed Laws The Basic Requirement Shulchan Aruch: A person should be in a mode of reclining the entire night — not just at the specific mitzvah moments (matzah, four cups). In practice, we focus on the key moments, but the spirit is comfort and freedom throughout. How to Recline Position Valid? Leaning backwards ❌ Not considered aristocratic reclining Leaning forward / face down ❌ Looks worried, not free Leaning to the right ❌ Dangerous (food can get trapped); also impractical (right hand needed for eating) Leaning to the left ✅ Correct — even for left-handed people Why left even for left-handed people? The danger of food getting trapped (leaning right) is more serious than the eating convenience — so everyone leans left. Practical reality (the Ravia's position): The Ravia (R. Eliezer Ben Yoel HaLevi, 12th century Germany) argued: the entire concept of reclining was only relevant when people ate on couches (Roman-era culture). Nowadays, leaning on a chair is uncomfortable and doesn't represent freedom at all. No Sephardi Rishon took this position. The Rambam codified all the reclining laws fully — because in Eastern cultures, people continued eating on low tables and couches for centuries. The fossilization problem: Without a Sanhedrin, we cannot formally change established halachot, even when the underlying reason no longer applies. If a Sanhedrin existed today, they would likely modernize this — perhaps to comfortable seating on couches around a low table. Practical advice from the Rav: The most important thing is to be comfortable and feel genuine freedom. Get a good cushion, sit back, relax. If you have the space and the couches — eating on couches around a low coffee table is actually the ideal fulfillment of hasabah. If You Forgot to Recline Shulchan Aruch: If you did any mitzvah requiring reclining without reclining — you must repeat it. This can even mean going back through the entire Seder (since the four cups must be in order with the Haggadah in between — shelo ka-seder). In practice: Many Poskim say be-di'avad you can rely on the Ravia (reclining isn't necessary nowadays) and don't need to repeat. As long as you were comfortable and felt good throughout — that's sufficient. Who Must Recline? The famous reversal: Shulchan Aruch (based on the Mishnah): Women don't need to recline — unless they are \"prominent\" (ishah chashuva). Rama: All our women nowadays are considered prominent (chashuva). However, because of the Ravia (who says nobody needs to recline nowadays), the Ashkenazi custom is that women don't recline — relying on the Ravia specifically for women. Sephardi development: The Knesset HaGedolah (Benvenisti) and others eventually established that Sephardi women are also considered chashuva. And since Sephardim never adopted the Ravia's opinion, the result is: Community Men Recline? Women Recline? Sephardim ✅ Yes ✅ Yes Ashkenazim ✅ Yes ❌ No (relying on the Ravia for women) This is the exact opposite of what a plain reading of the Shulchan Aruch and Rama would suggest. Reclining in the Presence of Your Rav A son by his father: does not need to ask permission to recline. A student in the presence of his Rav: must ask permission . It is disrespectful to recline in front of your Rav without permission. If the Rav doesn't grant permission — the student simply doesn't recline. That's not me'akev (it doesn't invalidate the mitzvah in that case). The student should stay and not leave. The Four Cups (Arba Kosot) — How Much to Drink The Amounts Measurement Volume When Required Revi'it (full measure) ~86 ml Le-chatchila — drink this for each of the four cups Melo Lugmav (cheekful) ~44 ml (varies by person) Be-di'avad — if you really can't drink a full revi'it Comparison to regular Kiddush: On Friday night, you only need melo lugmav (44 ml) le-chatchila. On Pesach night, you need a full revi'it (86 ml) — because the four cups are about experiencing herut (freedom), not just fulfilling Kiddush. The Rambam's additional opinion: You should drink the majority of your cup , regardless of its size (not just a revi'it). This is why some people use small cups — so they can drink the whole thing. Kaf HaChaim: You should drink the entire cup — the whole thing. Even if the cup holds more than a revi'it. Practical reference: A standard small plastic cup ≈ 170 ml. A revi'it (86 ml) is about half of that. For regular Shabbat Kiddush, about a third suffices. Time Frame for Drinking You should drink each cup in one go — not sip it slowly. Standard Time Frame Le-chatchila A few gulps — kedei shiyur revi'it Be-di'avad Within kedei achilat pras (~4.5 minutes) Finding It Difficult The Rashba: Even if wine is difficult for you — you must still push yourself to drink the four cups. The Chachamim established this mitzvah and it must be fulfilled. If wine causes genuine medical problems (migraines, allergies): consult a rabbinical authority for alternatives. Red Wine vs. White Wine Shulchan Aruch: It is preferable to use red wine for the four cups. Two reasons: Commemorates the Midrash about the Egyptians bathing in the blood of the Israelites. Red wine was traditionally considered more prestigious/important. Exceptions: If you prefer white wine — white is fine. If there are blood libels in the community — the Mishnah Berurah says use white wine to avoid accusations. Mevushal (Pasteurized) Wine and Grape Juice Shulchan Aruch: You can fulfill the mitzvah of the four cups with mevushal (pasteurized) wine and with grape juice. The Rambam's strict opinion: Yayin mevushal no longer has the status of wine — you cannot fulfill Kiddush (or the four cups) with it. The Shulchan Aruch brings this opinion in Hilchot Kiddush (Siman 272) as well. The Rav's important point on consistency: Many people insist on non-mevushal wine specifically for Pesach night, citing the Rambam — but use mevushal wine every other Friday night. This is inconsistent. If You Follow... Then... The Rambam (non-mevushal required) Be consistent: non-mevushal every Shabbat, not just Pesach The main halacha (mevushal is fine) Mevushal works for Pesach just as it works every Shabbat There's an argument that regular Shabbat Kiddush has a stronger de'oraita basis than the four cups (which are entirely de-rabbanan) — so if anything, Friday night should be stricter. Women's Obligation Shulchan Aruch: Women are equally obligated in all mitzvot of the Seder night — matzah, maror, four cups, Haggadah, everything. Why? They were part of the miracle of the Exodus. The Midrash says: \"We were redeemed from Egypt because of the righteous women.\" Engaging Children Give children their own cups if they're old enough to understand. This is a mitzvah but not obligatory (not me'akev). The Chachamim never established a formal mitzvah of chinuch for wine, since children don't enjoy it — and chinuch requires the child to benefit from and understand the mitzvah. Chacham Ovadia writes: If it's difficult financially or practically — children can drink from their parents' cups. No need for separate cups for every child. Have treats ready for the children — nuts, sweets, rewards — so they stay alert and engaged throughout the evening. Ma Nishtana — Don't Stop at the Question A common mistake: children sing Ma Nishtana beautifully and then disengage. But Ma Nishtana is just the question — the answer is Avadim Hayinu and everything that follows. The Syrian minhag: Between Ma Nishtana and Avadim Hayinu, the ba'al ha-bayit announces: \"Hada jawab\" (Arabic: \"This is the answer\") — making the point explicitly that we've asked the question and now comes the response. If you had only one minute for the entire Seder, it would be: Ma Nishtana → Avadim Hayinu → Pesach, Matzah, Maror. Summary of Key Practical Points for This Year Topic Key Takeaway Matzah on Erev Pesach Forbidden from the morning (Shulchan Aruch). Friday night this year: matzah is permitted. Preparing the table Cannot prepare from Shabbat for after Shabbat. From sunset (~7:52 PM London): permitted for tzorech mitzvah. Cannot move muktzeh items (Havdalah candle, Matzah Shmura). Starting the Seder Ideally after nightfall (~8:40 PM London). Can start Kiddush from sunset if late/needed. Don't start Maggid before nightfall. Reclining Lean to the left on something comfortable. Sephardim: men and women. Ashkenazim: men only. Be-di'avad if forgot: can rely on the Ravia. Four cups Le-chatchila: 86 ml each. Be-di'avad: 44 ml (melo lugmav). Drink in a few gulps, not sipping. Wine type Red preferred. Mevushal is fine. Be consistent with what you do on Shabbat. Women Fully obligated in all Seder mitzvot. Children Own cups if able — but not obligatory. Have treats ready. Engage them beyond Ma Nishtana into the answers.","8jsikqba6BU":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444:1 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; Erev Pesach on Shabbat (intro) Class 6 444:2–5, 445 Erev Pesach on Shabbat (continued): bitul/bi'ur schedule; disposing on Shabbat; bi'ur chametz methods Class 7 446–447:1–2 Finding chametz during Pesach; muktze on Yom Tov; kashrut principles; be-mashehu rule Class 8 447:4, 5, 10 Chozer ve-ne'or (reawakening); practical implications for hechsherim; noten ta'am li-fgam on Pesach Class 9 448 Chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach; mechirat chametz — origins, kinyanim, and practical details Class 10 451:1–4, 6 Hag'alat Kelim Part 1: Torah sources, libun vs hag'alah, rov tashmisho, earthenware, knives, bowls Class 11 451:5, 7–14, 20, 22–26 Hag'alat Kelim Part 2: Kli rishon/sheni details, pans and Teflon, handles and lids, covering surfaces, cold absorption, glass Siman 451 (Continued) Se'if 5 – Ke-Volo Kach Polto: Matching the Kashering to the Absorption Shulchan Aruch: The way a utensil absorbed chametz determines how it must be kashered. How It Was Used How to Kasher Kli Rishon (cooked on fire, or off fire but still boiling — yad soledet bo) Kasher in a Kli Rishon — boiling water on/from the fire Irui Kli Rishon (hot liquid poured onto it from a pot) Kasher by pouring from a Kli Rishon (irui) Kli Sheni (received food transferred from a Kli Rishon) Kasher in a Kli Sheni (or of course in a Kli Rishon) Examples: A ladle/spoon used to stir a pot on the fire → absorbed as Kli Rishon → must be kashered in a Kli Rishon. A pot that was on the fire → to kasher, place it inside a bigger pot of boiling water on the fire. A bowl that received soup poured from the pot → absorbed as irui Kli Rishon → kasher by pouring boiling water over it. Does Kli Sheni Absorb at All? Background: We hold (especially Sephardim) that Kli Sheni does not cook (ein Kli Sheni mevashel) — with very few exceptions (kalei ha-bishul: heavily salted fish, pure tea leaves). So why kasher something used only as a Kli Sheni? Answer: The Shulchan Aruch in Yoreh De'ah (Siman 105) rules that although Kli Sheni doesn't cook , there are opinions that it can absorb and expel flavor. Since this is a machloket Rishonim, we are machmir le-chatchila (in the first instance) and kasher Kli Sheni items as well. However: This is only le-chatchila. Be-di'avad — if you didn't kasher a Kli Sheni item and used it on Pesach — the food is fine. You can rely on the lenient opinions. Kli Shelishi — No Concern at All A third vessel (Kli Shelishi) — e.g., food served with a ladle (Kli Sheni) into a bowl (Kli Shelishi) — does not need kashering. Just wash it. Practical scenario: If you always serve soup using a ladle (not pouring directly from pot to bowl): Item Status Kashering Needed? The pot Kli Rishon ✅ Yes — hag'alah in Kli Rishon The ladle Kli Sheni (if plastic/non-conducting) ✅ Le-chatchila — in Kli Sheni or Rishon The bowl Kli Shelishi ❌ No — just wash Spoons used in the bowl Kli Shelishi ❌ No — just wash Note on metal ladles: Some Poskim say a metal ladle sitting in a hot pot takes on the status of Kli Rishon (because metal conducts). This is itself a machloket. Combined with the doubt about whether Kli Sheni absorbs at all → safek sefeika (double doubt) → one can be lenient. Kashering On vs. Off the Fire Shulchan Aruch: A Kli Rishon can be kashered in another Kli Rishon, even off the fire (as long as it's still boiling). Later Poskim (what we do in practice): If the utensil was used on the fire , it should ideally be kashered in water that is on the fire — not just boiling water that's been taken off. In practice at communal hag'alah events: The fire is kept on underneath the pot throughout. At home: keep the stove burner on while kashering pots. Kashering by Pouring (Irui) — Practical Rules for Sinks Since sinks absorb chametz through irui (hot water poured from pots hits the sink walls), they are kashered by irui — pouring boiling water over them. Two critical rules for effective irui: Rule 1: Direct, unbroken stream (nitzok) The boiling water must hit the surface in a direct, continuous stream — not splash, drip, or run down from another surface. If you pour onto the walls and it runs down to the basin — that does not kasher the basin. You must pour directly onto the basin. Practical order: Start with the base/basin of the sink (pour directly in circular motions), then work up the walls/sides . Rule 2: The surface must be dry Irui can only kasher a dry surface. If there is cold water pooled on the surface, the boiling water loses its ability to kasher upon contact with the cold water. This is another reason to start from the base first — if you start from the walls, water runs down and pools at the base, which you'd then need to dry before kashering the base. Se'if 7 – Ivory Utensils (Precious Materials) Shulchan Aruch: Spoons or utensils made from ivory (keren) — cannot be kashered. Why? Not because hag'alah doesn't work on ivory in principle, but because ivory is so precious and delicate that the owner won't do hag'alah properly — they won't heat the water high enough or dip it in fully, for fear of damaging it. This is a Rabbinic concern, not from the Gemara — the Rishonim added this safeguard. Modern application: Difficult to think of a parallel today. Any utensil so expensive you'd be afraid to boil properly? Possibly certain decorative items — but if you only use them cold, no kashering is needed anyway. Se'if 8 – Materials That Can Be Kashered Shulchan Aruch: The following materials can all be kashered normally (hag'alah or libun as appropriate): Wood Stone (including quartz) Metal Bone Practical relevance: Quartz sinks and countertops — these are stone and can be kashered. People building kitchens who want to use the same sink for Pesach: quartz is fine, kasher it normally. Se'if 10 – Baskets Used for Salting Meat: Ein Machzikin Issura Shulchan Aruch: Baskets used for salting meat — two opinions on whether they need kashering: Opinion Reasoning First opinion: Need hag'alah Perhaps chametz crumbs were mixed in with the meat during salting. Heavy salting can absorb and extract even cold. Second opinion (halacha): No kashering needed Ein machzikin issura — we don't assume chametz is lurking where there's no evidence of it. The Shulchan Aruch rules like the second opinion. If you know there was chametz, kasher it. If you don't know — don't assume it. This is the same principle from Siman 447:5 — ein machzikin issura. Combined with chozer ve-ne'or (Class 8), these are the two reasons why plain ingredients before Pesach don't need a hechsher. Plastic Modern question: What is the status of plastic? Opinion View Extreme opinion Plastic = like glass (zechuchit). For Ashkenazim who can't kasher glass → can't kasher plastic either. General consensus Plastic either doesn't absorb at all (most logical scientifically), or it can be kashered normally. In practice We kasher plastic. It works. The opinion that it can't be kashered is not followed. Se'if 11 – Frying Pans: The Rosh vs. the Rashba Shulchan Aruch: A pan used for frying with oil — can be kashered with hag'alah . If it's very long and doesn't fit in a pot → do hag'alah on the sides, libun in the middle section. The Core Machloket: Opinion Ruling Reasoning The Rosh (Shulchan Aruch follows) Hag'alah suffices for frying pans Even though oil sometimes burns and dries up, there's always some moisture (lachluchit) remaining. As long as you cook with oil/liquid, it's considered wet absorption → hag'alah. The Rashba (Rama follows) Needs libun Sometimes the oil dries completely, the food burns → those moments are dry absorption → needs libun. The Rama's compromise: Because of the combination of (a) Rabbeinu Tam — chametz is hetera bala anyway, plus (b) the Rosh's opinion that hag'alah suffices → the Rama allows libun kal (a lighter form of libun = putting it in the oven at high temperature for about 30 minutes). For Sephardim: Hag'alah suffices for regular frying pans. Full stop. Teflon / Non-Stick Pans The problem: The entire purpose of non-stick pans is to cook without liquid — minimal or no oil. This is genuinely dry cooking. Approach Reasoning Strict (recommended) Non-stick = truly dry → needs libun. Libun will likely destroy the pan. Better to put it away and buy a Pesach one. Those who do hag'alah Not necessarily wrong — because: (1) hetera bala (Rabbeinu Tam) in the background; (2) is it really 100% dry? Some oil is still used; (3) what counts as libun? Maybe oven suffices. Multiple leniencies combine. Practical advice: Non-stick Teflon pans — put them away for Pesach. This is one of the few things worth buying new. Oven Trays / Cake Tins Mishnah Berurah (65): Baking trays (tavnit) — used completely dry in the oven — need libun to kasher. Cannot be kashered with hag'alah. Libun at highest oven temperature may ruin them. Practical advice: Put oven trays away and use Pesach ones, or line with foil. Oil Temperature Question Interesting question raised: Oil fries at much higher temperatures than 100°C (boiling water). Shouldn't we need hotter water to extract what went in through hot oil? Answer: None of the Poskim ever raised this concern. In practice, we use boiling water for hag'alah regardless. The question deserves investigation scientifically, but halachically, hag'alah with boiling water has always been accepted for oil-based absorption. Se'if 12 – Handles of Utensils Shulchan Aruch: You must also kasher the handles of utensils — not just the cooking surface. Why? Metal handles: Metal conducts heat — forbidden flavor travels through the pot walls into the handle. Non-metal handles (wood, etc.): The Mishnah Berurah asks: why? Perhaps hot chametz touches the handle during washing, or your chametz-covered hands grip it while cooking. Some concern of absorption. The Rama says: If you didn't kasher the handles — be-di'avad, the pot and its contents are still fine. His reasoning: The same heat that travels from the pot to the handle during cooking also travels during kashering — so the handle gets kashered automatically along with the pot. In practice: We do kasher handles (le-chatchila). But if you missed them — no need to re-do. Se'if 14 – Lids Shulchan Aruch: Lids of pots must be kashered. Why? The lid receives evaporation and steam (zei'ah) from the food cooking below. This steam carries chametz flavor and absorbs into the lid. If you placed an un-kashered lid on a Pesach pot on Pesach: Timing Consequence On Pesach itself All contents of the pot are forbidden — because on Pesach, even a drop of chametz is be-mashehu (no bitul) On Erev Pesach after chatzot Fine — if there's 60 times more food than the absorbed flavor (likely). Everyone agrees 1-in-60 works from chatzot until Pesach itself. Lid not used for 24 hours Sephardim: noten ta'am li-fgam — be-di'avad, the food is permitted. Se'if 20 – The Origin of Covering Surfaces Shulchan Aruch: Tables and storage boxes/cupboards where food is kept all year round — we pour hot water (irui) onto them to kasher, because sometimes soup spills from a pot onto the table. Key insight: If you never bring pots to the table (only bowls — which are Kli Sheni), there is no need to kasher the table at all. A spill from a Kli Sheni onto the table is not a concern. Where Does \"Covering\" Come From? Magen Avraham: Even after kashering the table, put a tablecloth on it — perhaps some chametz remains. The Rama and Eliyahu Rabbah disagree: For flat tables — not necessary. Just kasher it. Eliyahu Rabbah (cited in Mishnah Berurah): For cupboards, shelves, fridge, freezer — where food sits directly on surfaces and it's very hard to clean every trace — cover them . This is the origin of the entire practice of covering surfaces for Pesach. Modern Application Surface What's Needed Dining table If pots come to the table: kasher with irui (or cover). If only bowls/plates: just wash. Kitchen counters Kasher with irui (hot spills from Kli Rishon likely during the year). Or cover. Fridge / freezer shelves If you place unwrapped food directly on them: cover the shelves. If all food is wrapped/packaged : no need to cover — just wash well. Cupboard shelves Same as fridge: cover if unwrapped food sits on them; wash if everything is packaged. Important: Nowadays we have extensive plastic packaging. The old concern was about unwrapped food sitting directly on surfaces. If your food is packaged, much of the covering is unnecessary. Se'if 22 – Cold Chametz and the Two Exceptions Shulchan Aruch: All utensils — even earthenware — that were used only with cold chametz throughout the year → can be used on Pesach for even hot food. Just wash them. Two exceptions where cold chametz still creates a problem: Exception 1: Beit Se'or (Place of Extended Contact) If chametz dough sat on a surface for 24 consecutive hours (me'et le'et) — it absorbs even though it's cold. This is the principle of kavush ke-mevushal — prolonged soaking/contact is equivalent to cooking. Example: A kneading board where dough sits overnight. Exception 2: Beit Charoset (Sharp Ingredients) If chametz was used together with sharp ingredients (davar charif) — garlic, onion, ginger — even cold, even without 24 hours. Sharp foods have the ability to cause absorption and extraction even when cold. Example: Cutting onions and bread together with a knife. Even with these exceptions: The utensil can be kashered (hag'alah for non-earthenware). Even without kashering — it can be used for cold food on a one-off (temporary) basis. Earthenware with these issues: cannot be kashered → don't use on Pesach. Practical example: A knife used to cut onions on bread (cold, but sharp ingredient + chametz together) → needs hag'alah. A knife used to cut bread alone (cold, no sharp) → just wash it. Se'if 23 – Glazed Earthenware (China) Shulchan Aruch: Earthenware vessels covered with a glass glaze — have the status of earthenware (cannot be kashered). Why? Even though glass doesn't absorb, when the glass glaze is fired together with the earthenware during manufacturing, it becomes porous along with the clay. The glaze doesn't provide independent protection. Critical distinction — glass on ovens/hobs: Scenario Status Kashering? Glazed china (glass fused with clay during manufacturing) = Earthenware ❌ Cannot be kashered Glass cooktop / induction hob (separate glass panel on top) = Glass ✅ Just clean well with soap — no kashering needed Oven door glass (separate glass panel) = Glass ✅ Just clean — no kashering needed This is a very common misconception. People think they need to kasher their glass induction hob or oven glass. You don't — it's glass, it's a separate item, not glazed earthenware. Just clean it thoroughly. Se'if 25 – Drinking Cups and Mugs Shulchan Aruch: All drinking vessels — whether glass, wood, metal, or even earthenware — can be used on Pesach just by washing . No kashering needed. Even if during the year, hot chametz was occasionally placed in them (e.g., a child poured hot soup with croutons into a cup). Why? Because the rov tashmisho (majority use) of cups and drinking vessels is with cold liquids. We follow the majority. Modern application — mugs: Usage Pattern Kashering Needed? Used for coffee/tea throughout the year; once or twice had hot soup with chametz ❌ No — just wash. Rov tashmisho is cold/non-chametz drinks. Regularly used for porridge, hot chametz soups, etc. ✅ Yes — needs hag'alah (rov tashmisho is hot chametz) Used only for cold water and cold drinks ❌ No — just wash The Rav's emphasis: This saves significant money and effort. People buy new cups and glasses for Pesach unnecessarily. If you understand rov tashmisho, most drinking vessels in your home need nothing more than a good wash. Se'if 26 – Glass (זכוכית) Famous machloket: Opinion Ruling Reasoning Shulchan Aruch (Sephardim) Glass does not absorb at all. Even used with hot chametz all year → just wash and use on Pesach. Glass is non-porous — it doesn't take in flavors. Rama (Ashkenazim) Glass is like earthenware — so porous it can't even be kashered with hag'alah. Cannot be used on Pesach if used with chametz. Glass is a form of sand/earth — similar to clay. For Sephardim: Glass is the ultimate solution. A set of glass dishes, cups, and cookware can be used for meat, milk, and Pesach — year-round. Just wash between uses. No kashering, no separate sets needed. Practical tip from the Rav: For people who struggle with the expense of multiple sets of dishes — invest in glass. One set works for everything: meat, dairy, and Pesach. This is fully halachically valid for Sephardim. Summary of Key Practical Takeaways Item What's Needed for Pesach Pots used on fire Hag'alah in Kli Rishon (keep fire on underneath) Ladles/spoons (stirring pots) Hag'alah in Kli Rishon Bowls (served with ladle) Kli Shelishi — just wash Cutlery (used in bowls) Kli Shelishi — just wash (le-chatchila: hag'alah) Frying pans (with oil) Sephardim: hag'alah. Ashkenazim: libun kal (oven, high temp, 30 min) Non-stick / Teflon pans Put away — buy Pesach ones Oven trays / cake tins Need libun — usually easier to put away or line with foil Sinks Irui — pour boiling water directly; start from base, then walls; surface must be dry Kitchen counters Irui (pour boiling water) or cover Tables If pots come to table: irui or cover. If only bowls: just wash. Fridge / cupboard shelves Cover if unwrapped food sits on them. If packaged: just wash. Drinking cups / glasses Just wash — rov tashmisho is cold Mugs Usually just wash — unless regularly used with hot chametz Glass (Sephardim) Just wash — glass doesn't absorb Glass (Ashkenazim) Cannot be used — like earthenware Glazed china Cannot be kashered — = earthenware Glass cooktop / induction hob Just clean well with soap — it's glass, not glazed china Handles Le-chatchila: kasher them. Be-di'avad if missed: fine. Lids Must kasher — absorbs steam Plastic Can be kashered — hag'alah works Quartz sinks/counters = Stone — can be kashered normally","qz_I2P9EJQ0":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444:1 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; Erev Pesach on Shabbat (intro) Class 6 444:2–5, 445 Erev Pesach on Shabbat (continued): bitul/bi'ur schedule; disposing on Shabbat; bi'ur chametz methods Class 7 446–447:1–2 Finding chametz during Pesach; muktze on Yom Tov; kashrut principles; be-mashehu rule Class 8 447:4, 5, 10 Chozer ve-ne'or (reawakening); practical implications for hechsherim; noten ta'am li-fgam on Pesach Class 9 448 Chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach; mechirat chametz — origins, kinyanim, and practical details; feeding animals Class 10 451:1–4, 6 Hag'alat Kelim Part 1: Torah sources, principles of kashering, libun vs hag'alah, rov tashmisho, earthenware, knives, bowls and cutlery Siman 451 – The Laws of Kashering Utensils (דיני הגעלת כלים) Contains 27 Se'ifim — This class covers the foundational principles and Se'ifim 1–4, 6 Foundational Principles Before diving into the Shulchan Aruch, several core principles must be understood. These underpin every halacha in this siman. Principle 1: The Two Torah Sources for Hag'alat Kelim Source 1 — Parshat Tzav (Vayikra 6:21) — The Korban Chatat וכלי חרש אשר תבשל בו ישבר ואם בנחשת בשלה ומורק ושוטף במים Earthenware vessel used to cook the chatat offering → must be broken (cannot be kashered). Copper/metal vessel → can be purged (morak) and rinsed (shutaf) in water. The Gemara (Avodah Zarah) derives: העידה תורה על כלי חרס שאין להם תקנה לצאת מידי דופיין — \"The Torah testified that earthenware vessels have no remedy to remove their absorbed flavors.\" Two lessons from this pasuk: Earthenware that absorbed forbidden flavor through cooking can never be purged — it's too absorbent. Metal utensils that absorbed forbidden flavor can be purged through water (hag'alah). Source 2 — Parshat Matot (Bamidbar 31:22–23) — The War with Midian After the war with Midian, Elazar HaKohen instructed Bnei Yisrael on how to kasher the captured utensils: כל דבר אשר יבא באש תעבירו באש וטהר... וכל אשר לא יבא באש תעבירו במים If the utensil was used with fire (i.e., forbidden food was cooked in it with fire) → pass it through fire (libun). If it was not used with fire → pass it through water (hag'alah). Rashi's principle: כבולעו כך פולטו — \"The way it absorbed is the way it expels.\" This is the master rule of all kashering. Note: The pasuk also requires tevilat kelim (immersion in a mikveh) for utensils acquired from a non-Jew — but that is a completely separate din from hag'alat kelim. You cannot kasher a utensil by taking it to the mikveh. These are two different processes. Principle 2: Is There a Mitzvah to Kasher Utensils? Opinion Is Hag'alat Kelim a Mitzvah? Reasoning Tosafot ✅ Yes — just like tevilat kelim Both are Torah-derived processes for making utensils usable Rambam ❌ No — not listed in the 613 mitzvot Hag'alah merely removes an obstacle (forbidden flavor). Tevilat kelim is a proactive transformation — changing the status of a utensil for Jewish use. Practical consequence: No bracha is recited on hag'alat kelim — because even if it's a mitzvah, the consensus treats it as a preparatory process (hechsher mitzvah), not a standalone mitzvah. Principle 3: Libun vs. Hag'alah — The Two Methods of Kashering Feature Libun (ליבון) Hag'alah (הגעלה) Method Burn in intense fire until sparks fly / red hot Immerse in boiling water What it does Destroys the absorbed flavor entirely Removes the flavor from the walls into the water Temperature Extremely high (~250°C+, red hot) Boiling (100°C, yad soledet bo ~45°C minimum) When required Utensil absorbed flavor dry, with direct fire Utensil absorbed flavor with water/liquid Immersion N/A — fire-based Can be done in halves (unlike tevilat kelim, which requires full immersion at once) Libun Chamur vs. Libun Kal Type Definition Who Holds Libun Chamur (stringent) Sparks fly from it / becomes red hot Shulchan Aruch, Rambam — one standard of libun Libun Kal (lenient) Hot enough that straw placed on the outside would burn Some Rishonim; Rama permits for doubtful cases For Sephardim: Where libun is required, it means libun chamur — full intensity. There's no halfway. For Ashkenazim (Rama): Libun chamur is the default, but libun kal can be used when there's doubt whether the utensil needs libun or merely hag'alah. Why Doesn't the Flavor Reabsorb During Hag'alah? Since hag'alah merely removes flavors into the water (unlike libun which destroys them), why don't the flavors go back into the pot? Two answers in the Rishonim: The boiling water that causes the flavor to exit cannot simultaneously cause it to reabsorb — the process only works in one direction at a time. The water is assumed to be 60 times the volume of the absorbed flavor — so the flavor becomes nullified (batel be-shishim) upon exiting the walls. Practical consequence of this machloket: We advise rinsing the utensil in cold water immediately after hag'alah — to ensure the flavor doesn't reabsorb (satisfying the first reason). Principle 4: Rov Tashmisho (רוב תשמישו) — Majority of Its Use This is a critical principle that simplifies Pesach preparation enormously. Rule: We follow the majority of how a utensil was used during the year — not the occasional exception. Majority Use Occasional Use What's Required With water/liquid (needs hag'alah) Sometimes dry on fire (would need libun) Hag'alah suffices With cold food Sometimes with hot food Washing suffices — no kashering needed Why so lenient? Because the entire requirement to kasher after 24 hours is only de-rabbanan (since after 24 hours, the flavor is noten ta'am li-fgam — degraded). Since it's a rabbinic decree, the Chachamim only applied it when the majority of use creates the concern. Practical example: A pot used 100 times during the year — 97 times with cold chametz food, 3 times with hot chametz food. You only need to wash it thoroughly — no hag'alah needed, because rov tashmisho was cold. Principle 5: Issura Bala vs. Hetera Bala (אסורא בלע / התירא בלע) Core question: Was the absorbed food forbidden or permissible at the time of absorption? The Gemara's undisputed rule: If the food was permissible when absorbed (hetera bala), then even dry absorption only needs hag'alah — not libun. Example everyone agrees on: Sacrificial meat cooked on a spit — permissible when cooked, only becomes forbidden later (when the time limit passes). Even though it's dry/fire → hag'alah suffices because it was hetera bala. The machloket: Is chametz hetera bala or issura bala? Opinion Chametz is... Reasoning Consequence Rabbeinu Tam (Tosafot) Hetera bala — absorbed permissibly During the year when you cooked with chametz, it was completely permitted You never need libun for Pesach. Hag'alah always suffices — even for dry/fire use. The Rif Issura bala — absorbed as if forbidden Chametz is inherently something that becomes forbidden (unlike sacrifices, which are always permitted when fresh). Had it been Pesach, it would be forbidden. Dry/fire use → needs libun Shulchan Aruch: Follows the Rif — chametz is issura bala. Dry/fire use requires libun. However — critical caveat (Mishnah Berurah 451:28): Rabbeinu Tam is still a significant opinion. Whenever there is another reason to be lenient (e.g., rov tashmisho, doubt about usage), we combine Rabbeinu Tam's opinion and treat chametz as hetera bala. This combination appears many times throughout this siman. Principle 6: Kli Rishon, Irui Kli Rishon, and Kli Sheni Level Definition Degree of Absorption Kli Rishon (on fire) Pot cooked directly on the stove/fire Complete absorption through entire walls Kli Rishon (off fire, still boiling) Same pot, removed from fire but still roteach (boiling) Same — complete absorption Irui Kli Rishon Pouring from a kli rishon onto/into another vessel Absorbs k'dei klipah (a peel's depth) only Kli Sheni A vessel that received food/liquid from a kli rishon On Shabbat: does not cook. On Pesach: slightly stricter (see Se'if 6) The Shulchan Aruch — Se'if by Se'if Se'if 1 — Earthenware (כלי חרס) and China Shulchan Aruch: Earthenware pots used with chametz during the year: Clean them well — ensure no visible chametz remains (mamashut / substance). Put them away for after Pesach — store in a discreet place where you won't accidentally use them. After Pesach — you may use them freely. The absorbed chametz in the walls is chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach, but the Chachamim's penalty only applies to tangible chametz (mamash), not to flavor absorbed in walls . Why is this not obvious? There was an opinion in the Gemara that the absorbed chametz flavor in the walls should also be treated as chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach (and the pots could never be used again). Shmuel disagreed: the gezeirah only applies to tangible substance, not to absorbed flavor. We follow Shmuel. Storage recommendations (from the Rishonim): Source Recommendation Gemara / Shulchan Aruch Put away in a discreet place you won't frequent The Rosh Put it somewhere you won't forget and use it accidentally The Tur (son of the Rosh) Lock it away and put the key somewhere separate Nowadays Sellotape / labels on the cabinets — serves as a reminder (equivalent of \"locking\") Can earthenware be kashered at all? Method Does It Work? Hag'alah (boiling water) ❌ No — earthenware is too absorbent Pouring boiling water into it ❌ No — irui kli rishon still absorbs k'dei klipah Libun (fire) in a regular oven ❌ No — insufficient; risk of not heating evenly Reconstituting in a kiln (כבשן) ✅ Yes — putting it back in the kiln where new earthenware is fired essentially recreates the vessel What About Kli Sheni? The Shulchan Aruch in Se'if 1 only mentions cooking and pouring (irui) as problematic — he does not mention kli sheni here. In Se'if 5 (and 6), he does say kli sheni should ideally be kashered. The Poskim resolve this: Since earthenware cannot be kashered anyway, and kli sheni is itself a chumra — you can use earthenware/china on Pesach that was only used as a kli sheni during the year (just wash it well). Ashkenazim disagree and would not permit this. Even many Sephardim would say don't do this in the first instance — but strictly speaking, there are grounds for leniency. Practical example: China bowls used on the dinner table where food was poured into them (irui kli rishon → the bowl is kli sheni). Strictly speaking for Sephardim: wash well and usable on Pesach. In practice: most people have separate Pesach china. Se'if 2 — [Specific utensil type — skipped as not practically relevant] Se'if 3 — Knives (סכינים) Shulchan Aruch: Knives — kasher with hag'alah in a kli rishon. That's sufficient. But wait — shouldn't knives need libun? If you used a knife on a barbecue (dry, with direct fire), that should be issura bala requiring libun! Why hag'alah suffices: The Mishnah Berurah explains this is a combination of two leniencies: Rabbeinu Tam — chametz is hetera bala, so you never need libun for Pesach anyway. Rov tashmisho — knives are rarely used dry on the fire. Their majority use is with water, with food, on the table, cutting, etc. These two reasons combined allow hag'alah for knives. Exception: A knife used exclusively or primarily for barbecue (dry, on fire) — that specific knife would need libun . What about knives used only with cold food? If a knife is only used to cut bread, cheese, vegetables — always cold — then me'ikar ha-din (strictly speaking) it doesn't need kashering at all. Just wash it thoroughly. Rov tashmisho be-tzonen (majority use is cold) = washing suffices. Removing Rust Before Hag'alah Shulchan Aruch (continuation): Before doing hag'alah on knives (or any utensil), you must: Remove all rust — the pasuk says אך את הזהב (\"only the gold\") — the word אך (ach) is a limitation: hag'alah must act on the metal itself , not on rust covering it. If there are chips, grooves, or holes where rust cannot be cleaned → hag'alah doesn't work there; those spots need libun . In practice: if you have very rusty utensils that can't be cleaned — you cannot kasher them. Se'if 4 — Things Used on Naked Fire (Spits, Grills) Shulchan Aruch: Utensils used directly on fire — such as spits (shefudim) and grates/grills (askalot) — require libun . This follows the Rif — chametz is issura bala. If it were like Rabbeinu Tam (hetera bala), libun would never be needed for Pesach. Libun means: Sparks fly from it / it becomes red hot (~250°C+). The Mishnah Berurah (451:28) adds the critical caveat: ומכל מקום אין ללמוד מדין זה למקומות אחרים — \"Don't extrapolate from this to all other cases.\" When there are additional reasons to be lenient , we combine Rabbeinu Tam's opinion and permit hag'alah instead of libun. The default follows the Rif (libun for dry/fire), but it's not applied rigidly in every scenario. Se'if 6 — Bowls, Cutlery, and Rov Tashmisho in Practice Shulchan Aruch: כל כלי הולכים בו אחר רוב תשמישו — We follow the majority of a utensil's use. Example — Bowls (ke'arot): Bowls on the table: their majority use is receiving food poured from a kli rishon → that makes them irui kli rishon (the pouring is irui; the bowl itself becomes kli sheni). Even though sometimes the bowl is placed directly on the fire → since the majority is irui, kasher by irui (pouring boiling water over it). No need for full hag'alah in a kli rishon. The Rama's Stringencies (Ashkenazi Practice) The Rama disagrees with the Shulchan Aruch's leniency on rov tashmisho in three escalating ways: Rama's Position What It Means Everything as kli rishon Don't rely on rov tashmisho — kasher everything with full hag'alah in a kli rishon, even if majority use was irui or kli sheni Even if \"perhaps\" used as kli rishon Even spoons and cutlery — if there's any possibility it was once stuck into a pot on the fire → kasher as kli rishon Even cold-use vessels Drinking cups and vessels used primarily with cold — if there's any chance it was once used with hot chametz → kasher with hag'alah This is why Ashkenazim kasher virtually everything — cutlery, cups, bowls — even when Sephardim would say it's unnecessary. Practical Example: The Kiddush Cup Used throughout the year for cold grape juice/wine. Me'ikar ha-din (Sephardi): no kashering needed — rov tashmisho is cold. Just wash it. The Rama: perhaps during the year someone drank hot coffee in it, or it was washed in hot water with chametz dishes → kasher it, or use a new one for Pesach. Strictly speaking: There is absolutely no need for a separate Pesach Kiddush cup. Using Cold Food in Non-Kosher/Chametz Utensils Rama (from Hilchot Kashrut, applied here): Any utensil that needs kashering — you cannot use it even with cold food on a regular basis. Why? The absorbed flavors can only be expelled with heat — cold food doesn't extract them. So technically, cold use is fine. But the Chachamim forbade it as a regular practice because you might forget and use it with hot food. However — one-off use (be-derech arai) is permitted: Scenario Permitted? Eating cold cereal and milk in a non-kosher hotel bowl (possibly used with bacon) ✅ Yes — one-off, cold, the bowl is clean Keeping chametz plates in regular use throughout Pesach for cold food ❌ No — regular/fixed use Emergency on Pesach: no clean dish available, using a clean chametz plate for cold food once ✅ Yes — one-off emergency Summary of Key Principles Principle Rule Earthenware Cannot be kashered (break it or put it away for after Pesach) Metal Can be kashered — libun (dry/fire) or hag'alah (liquid) Ke-volo kach polto The way it absorbed is the way it's expelled Libun Sparks / red hot (~250°C+). Destroys the absorbed flavor. Hag'alah Immersed in boiling water. Removes flavor into the water. Rinse immediately after. Rov Tashmisho Follow the majority use — not the occasional exception Hetera Bala / Issura Bala Rabbeinu Tam: chametz = hetera bala (never need libun). Rif/Shulchan Aruch: chametz = issura bala. But Rabbeinu Tam is combined with other leniencies when available. Ben Yomo (24 hours) After 24 hours, absorbed flavor is degraded (noten ta'am li-fgam). All kashering after 24 hours is de-rabbanan. Cold use Technically fine (no extraction), but forbidden as regular practice. One-off = permitted. Kli Rishon → Irui → Kli Sheni Decreasing levels of absorption and severity. Kasher at the level it absorbed.","YKQHtKwQt0w":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; Erev Pesach on Shabbat Class 6 446–447:1–2 Finding chametz during Pesach; muktze on Yom Tov; introduction to kashrut principles; be-mashehu rule Class 7 447:4, 5, 10 Chozer ve-ne'or (reawakening); practical implications for hechsherim; noten ta'am li-fgam on Pesach Class 8 448 Chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach; mechirat chametz — origins, kinyanim, and practical details; feeding animals chametz Siman 448 – Chametz After Pesach (דין חמץ שעבר עליו הפסח) Contains 7 Se'ifim Note: The Shulchan Aruch does not have a dedicated section on Mechirat Chametz (selling chametz). The laws of selling chametz appear within this siman, as a natural extension of the discussion on chametz left over after Pesach. Se'if 1 – A Non-Jew's Chametz After Pesach Shulchan Aruch: Chametz that belonged to a non-Jew throughout Pesach is permitted after Pesach — even to eat. Why? No prohibition was violated. The non-Jew was not subject to any restriction on owning chametz. Note: There was an opinion in the Gemara that even a non-Jew's chametz would be prohibited after Pesach, but the halacha rejects this. Se'if 2 – Receiving a Gift of Chametz from a Non-Jew on Pesach Shulchan Aruch: If a non-Jew brings a Jewish person a gift of chametz during Pesach (even on the 8th day, which is de-rabbanan): Do not accept it — accepting creates ownership, which violates Bal Yera'eh / Bal Yimatzei. Do not imply willingness to accept — e.g., saying \"leave it in my house, I'll have it after Pesach\" constitutes implied consent, and your chatzer (property) would acquire it on your behalf. It is good (though not strictly required) to explicitly state: \"I do not want to take ownership of this.\" Why the Emphasis on the 8th Day? The Beit Yosef explains: this halacha applies throughout Pesach, but the chiddush is that even on the 8th day (which is only de-rabbanan outside of Israel), you still cannot accept chametz. The Chatzer Koneh Principle Normally, your property (chatzer) automatically acquires ownerless items or gifts placed within it — this is called chatzer koneh . However, nothing can be acquired against your will . If you don't want your chatzer to acquire the chametz, it doesn't. The Rosh says: You don't even need to explicitly state that you don't want your chatzer to acquire it. The default assumption (stam) is that during Pesach, a person would not want their property to acquire chametz. It's implicit. However: If you do something that implies consent (e.g., putting it in a cupboard, saying \"I'll have it after Pesach\"), then you've given authority to your chatzer to acquire — and that's a problem. Practical Scenario: Chametz Delivered Through Your Letterbox If a company delivers chametz through your letterbox on Pesach (e.g., promotional Weetabix), by default your chatzer doesn't acquire it (you wouldn't want chametz during Pesach). Don't pick it up and put it in a cupboard — that implies consent. Do: Leave it where it is. Ideally, say explicitly \"I don't want my chatzer to acquire this.\" Cover it with a utensil (as we learned in Siman 446). After Pesach, you can acquire it. If the Non-Jew's Chametz Is in Your House (Not Yours) If chametz is in your house but doesn't belong to you (non-Jew left it, or you explicitly rejected ownership): You still need to build a mechitzah (partition) of at least 10 tefachim (approx. 80cm) or at minimum cover it — so you don't forget and come to eat it (as learned in Siman 440). Se'if 3 – The Core Din: Chametz She'avar Alav Ha-Pesach Shulchan Aruch: Chametz that a Jewish person owned over Pesach — asur be-hana'ah (forbidden to benefit from) after Pesach. This is even if the person: Circumstance Still Forbidden? Shogeg (forgetful — forgot to get rid of it) ✅ Yes — forbidden Ones (completely helpless — no way to do anything about it) ✅ Yes — forbidden Why So Severe? This is a kenas (penalty) instituted by the Chachamim. The Chachamim observed that people were lax about getting rid of chametz before Pesach. When Chazal impose very strict penalties, it typically indicates they saw widespread laxity. Parallel: The Chachamim similarly banned bathing in hot water on Shabbat (even if heated before Shabbat) because people were lying about when they heated the water. Even Ones? Normally, ones carries zero culpability — you had no choice, no way out (e.g., you were in prison, on a voyage, an onein dealing with a funeral). Shogeg (forgetfulness) has some culpability — you didn't check carefully enough. Even so , the Chachamim applied the penalty to both — because people would claim \"I was ones\" to circumvent the rule. The blanket rule: Any chametz owned by a Jew over Pesach is permanently forbidden — no exceptions for intent or circumstances. What Must You Do? The chametz must be destroyed — pour it down the drain, throw it away, etc. It is forbidden for any Jewish person — not just the owner. You cannot sell it, eat it, or benefit from it in any way. One Leniency: Safek (Doubt) If there is a genuine doubt whether the chametz was owned by a Jew over Pesach → one may be lenient. Why? Because this entire prohibition is de-rabbanan, and safek de-rabbanan le-kula (in cases of rabbinic doubt, we are lenient). Practical Ramifications of Chametz She'avar Alav Ha-Pesach Buying from Jewish shops after Pesach: You must ensure the shop either has a hechsher (the entire shop is under a kashrut authority that handles mechirat chametz) or that the owner reliably did mechirat chametz. Not all Jewish shops in areas like Golders Green and Hendon operate under a hechsher. Some operate independently — you are relying on the owner's personal mechirat chametz. If the owner did not sell properly or at all — it is forbidden for any Jew to buy from that shop's chametz stock. Eating at a non-religious Jewish friend's home after Pesach: If they serve pasta, bread, or other non-perishable chametz immediately after Pesach — there's a concern it was owned over Pesach. If it's clearly something that could have been bought fresh the morning after Pesach — it may be fine. If it's a genuine safek — you can be lenient (safek de-rabbanan le-kula). If it's highly likely or certain that the chametz was owned over Pesach — asur. No way around it, however uncomfortable. Se'if 3 (Continued) – The Solution: Selling or Gifting to a Non-Jew Before Pesach Shulchan Aruch: If, before Pesach, you sold or gave as a gift your chametz to a non-Jew — the din of chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach does not apply. After Pesach, the chametz is fully permitted. Conditions: Outright gift or complete sale — must be genuine with no conditions attached. Even if the Jew and non-Jew are friends and the Jew knows the non-Jew will keep it and return it after Pesach — it's still valid. Even if sold for a small amount — valid (mechira gemura be-davar mu'at). No tanai (conditions) — cannot say \"I'm giving it on condition you return it\" (see below on Matana Al Menat Lehachzir). The Laws of Mechirat Chametz (Selling Chametz) — Detailed Discussion Origins and History The Talmud does not explicitly discuss mechirat chametz as we practice it today. The Tosefta (Pesachim) describes someone at sea who cannot do bi'ur chametz — he can do a kinyan with a non-Jew to transfer ownership, even remotely. This is the only original source. The Rambam codifies this — notably, in the context of someone who is unable to destroy their chametz (on a voyage, etc.). The Sdeh Hemed (approx. 250 years ago) writes that the widespread practice of institutional mechirat chametz began with Ashkenazi shopkeepers in Europe who had large quantities of chametz stock and couldn't afford to destroy it all. Originally, individuals would sell directly to their non-Jewish neighbour — a simple, personal transaction. The institutional community-wide sale through a rabbi is a later development. Chacham Ovadia wrote at length defending the current practice, saying there is plenty to rely upon. Ideal Practice Everyone would agree: the ideal is to finish your chametz before Pesach, not to sell it. Plan ahead — use up pastas, flour, bread, etc., in the weeks before Pesach. Mechirat chametz should be a backup for items you genuinely cannot consume or dispose of (e.g., expensive whiskey). Taking the Chametz Out of the House Shulchan Aruch writes: You sell or give it to a non-Jew outside your house (mi-chutz la-bayit) before Pesach. Two readings of this requirement: Reading Explanation Practical Consequence Strict reading (many Acharonim, Pri Chadash) You must physically remove the chametz from your house for it to feel like a genuine sale. If it stays in your house, the concern is: (a) it doesn't feel like a real sale; (b) you might come to eat it. You'd need the non-Jew to come and take the chametz out — which we don't do nowadays. Lenient reading (other Acharonim) \"Mi-chutz la-bayit\" doesn't mean literally take it out — it means do a proper kinyan (of which meshicha / physical transfer is one common method). Other kinyanim work even if chametz stays in your house. The chametz can remain in your home as long as a valid kinyan was performed. The solution that satisfies both readings: Together with selling the chametz, you also rent/lease the area/space where the chametz is stored to the non-Jew (makneh the area space). This means: The space no longer belongs to you — it belongs to the non-Jew. The non-Jew has authority and permission to enter and take from that place. Even according to the strict reading, it's no longer considered \"your house\" (beitcha) — the concern is alleviated. This is what we do in practice today — the mechirat chametz document includes renting/leasing the storage area together with selling the chametz. How Does the Kinyan (Acquisition) Work? Background: Kinyan Between Jews The Gemara records a machloket: Opinion De'oraita Kinyan De-rabbanan Kinyan Rabbi Yochanan Kesef (money) — the Torah creates transactions through payment Meshicha (physical taking/pulling) — Chachamim instituted this as a precaution Reish Lakish Meshicha (physical taking) — derived from \"ve-kano mi-yad amitecha\" (Vayikra 25) Kesef — also works The Problem with a Non-Jew The pasuk says \"ve-kano mi-yad amitecha \" — \"acquire from your fellow [Jew].\" The Gemara derives: whatever kinyan works for a Jew from the Torah does NOT work for a non-Jew from the Torah. If you follow... De'oraita for Jew Therefore for Non-Jew... Rabbi Yochanan (Rambam follows) Kesef works de'oraita Kesef does NOT work with a non-Jew; Meshicha does Reish Lakish (Rashi follows) Meshicha works de'oraita Meshicha does NOT work with a non-Jew; Kesef does Practical halacha: Because of this fundamental machloket, we do both kesef (money) AND meshicha — to satisfy all opinions. Additional Kinyanim Used Today Since we lack proper meshicha (we don't physically hand over all the chametz), multiple additional kinyanim are incorporated: Kinyan Description Status Kesef (money) The non-Jew pays a first instalment toward the value of the chametz Satisfies Rabbi Yochanan Meshicha / Hagba'ah (physical transfer) Ideally, the non-Jew takes the chametz. In practice, achieved by renting the storage space. Satisfies Reish Lakish Kinyan Chalipin (exchange) The rabbi lifts an object belonging to the non-Jew (e.g., a pen) — a symbolic exchange De-rabbanan kinyan; source in Megillat Rut Kinyan Chatzer (property acquisition) The non-Jew's rented space acquires the chametz on his behalf De-rabbanan kinyan Metaltelin Agav Karka Movable goods (chametz) sold together with a piece of land De-rabbanan kinyan — powerful because land kinyan is more straightforward Handshake / contract Accepted commercial practice in the country — valid based on dina de-malkhuta dina The Mishnah Berurah validates kinyanim based on accepted societal commercial norms In practice today: The mechirat chametz document incorporates all of these kinyanim together — kesef, chalipin, chatzer, agav karka, contract, handshake — to ensure the sale is valid according to every opinion. It Must Be a Genuine Sale If the non-Jew decided to keep all the chametz and not sell it back — that's his right. The Jew cannot sue him in non-Jewish courts. If you did sue — it would make a mockery of the entire institution. Rav Moshe Feinstein was asked about a non-religious shopkeeper who did mechirat chametz but told others \"I don't really believe in this.\" Rav Moshe ruled: no problem — the person signed, gave authority to the rabbi, and the kinyan was performed. Internal thoughts don't invalidate a legal transaction. Only if an action demonstrated insincerity would it be a problem. Pricing The Shulchan Aruch writes you can sell \"even for something small\" (davar mu'at). Some poskim were concerned that selling a huge amount of chametz for very little money implies the sale isn't genuine. Counter-argument: Because of the urgency to avoid transgression, a person is willing to sell cheaply. In practice today: The sale is for the full estimated value . The non-Jew pays a first instalment (e.g., £50–100). The remainder is a debt (zekufah alav be-milveh). After Pesach, the Jew buys it back — cancelling the debt. Specifying the Chametz The Biur Halacha and Chacham Ovadia emphasise: you should specify where your chametz is on the sale form. Good: \"In the kitchen, third cabinet above the sink; garage shelf, bottom row.\" Not good: \"All the chametz in my house.\" (Too vague — weakens the sale.) Matana Al Menat Lehachzir Does NOT Work Matana al menat lehachzir = a gift given on the condition that it be returned. Normally in halacha, this is a valid gift (shemah matana — it has the legal status of a gift). On Pesach, it does NOT work. Why? Because if the condition isn't fulfilled (the non-Jew doesn't return it), the gift is retroactively void — meaning you owned chametz the entire time. Therefore: no conditions (tanai) of any kind may be attached to the sale or gift of chametz. It must be an outright, unconditional transfer. Se'if 5 – Even with Bitul, the Penalty Applies Shulchan Aruch: Even if you did bitul chametz (said Kol Chamira), but chametz was found in your house after Pesach — it is still forbidden (chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach). Also: Simply declaring it hefker (ownerless) doesn't help either — you didn't physically remove or sell it. The Chachamim's penalty applies regardless of bitul or hefker — only a genuine sale or gift to a non-Jew exempts you. Se'if 6 – Feeding Chametz to Animals on Pesach Shulchan Aruch: It is forbidden to feed chametz to any animal on Pesach — even other people's animals, even stray animals. Why can't you feed a stray animal? The Mishnah Berurah explains: there is hana'ah (benefit/satisfaction) in the act of feeding an animal. Every human has an innate instinct to care for animals — feeding a stray cat or dog gives you a sense of satisfaction. That emotional benefit constitutes hana'ah from chametz. Also: Even hefker chametz that you find on the street — you cannot pick it up to feed animals, because by picking it up, you would acquire it (kinyan). If a non-Jew asks you to feed his animal on Pesach with chametz — you may not. Se'if 7 – Pet Food on Pesach Shulchan Aruch / Poskim: You cannot feed your pet chametz-based food on Pesach. Pet food containing chametz grains belongs to you — you bought it for the dog/cat. It's fitting for animal consumption — so it has the halachic status of chametz. You are violating Bal Yera'eh / Bal Yimatzei — owning chametz on Pesach. This is potentially an isur de'oraita . It doesn't matter that you're not eating it — the prohibition is on owning it. Solutions Option Details Buy kosher-for-Pesach pet food Available nowadays from kosher suppliers Sell the pet food as part of mechirat chametz Include it in your sale to the non-Jew Sell the animal to the non-Jew over Pesach Then the non-Jew can feed it whatever he wants — even if you know he'll use chametz. After Pesach, buy it back. Give the animal to a non-Jew to care for But you cannot tell the non-Jew to feed it chametz. If you don't specify, and the non-Jew feeds it chametz on his own — that's fine. Summary of Key Halachot Se'if Key Takeaways 448:1 Non-Jew's chametz after Pesach: fully permitted, even to eat. No prohibition was violated. 448:2 Don't accept chametz gifts from a non-Jew on Pesach. Don't imply consent. Chatzer koneh doesn't apply when you wouldn't want it. Good practice: say explicitly \"I don't want ownership.\" Cover any chametz left in your house. 448:3 Chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach: asur be-hana'ah — even if shogeg or ones. A kenas by the Chachamim. Safek: lenient (de-rabbanan). Must verify Jewish shops did mechirat chametz. 448:3 (mechira) Sale or gift to a non-Jew before Pesach exempts from the penalty. Must be genuine — no tanai. The Shulchan Aruch says \"outside the house\" — resolved by renting the storage space to the non-Jew. 448 (history) Mechirat chametz as we know it: not in the Talmud (only Tosefta, for someone at sea). Began with Ashkenazi shopkeepers ~400 years ago. Ideal: finish chametz before Pesach. 448 (kinyanim) Machloket Rishonim: kesef or meshicha for non-Jew. We do both, plus chalipin, chatzer, agav karka, handshake/contract. Modern mechirat chametz documents include all kinyanim. 448 (matana) Matana al menat lehachzir doesn't work for chametz — if condition fails, ownership was never transferred. Must be outright gift or sale with no conditions. 448:5 Even with bitul or hefker — if chametz remained in your house, the penalty applies. Only sale/gift to a non-Jew works. 448:6 Can't feed chametz to any animal on Pesach — even strays. Emotional satisfaction = hana'ah. Can't pick up hefker chametz to feed animals. 448:7 Pet food with chametz: isur de'oraita of owning chametz. Buy Pesach pet food, sell the food, or sell the animal to a non-Jew over Pesach.","n_7NRDrAjT0":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Siman: 318 (Bishul — Cooking on Shabbat) Seifim: 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 13, 14, 15 Topics: Yad Soledet Bo Defined, Stacking Pots & Hatmanah, Placing Food on an Urn, Salt & Kli Rishon, Irui Kli Rishon, Why Kli Sheni Doesn't Cook, Placing Near Fire, Davar Gush Recap: Where We Are in Siman 318 Seifim 1–2: Ma'aseh Shabbat — consequences of desecrating Shabbat; cooking for a choleh Seif 3: Cooking in the sun / toldot hachamah; microwave; dud shemesh Seif 4: Major seif — bishul from ma'achal Ben Drosai; bishul achar bishul b'lach; Rema's position; kli sheni; irui kli rishon; kalei habishul; kli shlishi Seif 5: Bishul achar afiyah — cooking after baking; toast, coffee, tea bags, tea leaves Yad Soledet Bo — The Threshold of Bishul (Full Treatment) The single most important measurement in all of Hilchot Bishul Every question about whether something constitutes bishul on Shabbat depends on this threshold Definition in the Gemara — Two Formulations יד סולדת בו — your hand is scalded by it (adult) כריסו של תינוק נכוית — the stomach of a baby is burnt by it (more tender) Note: scalding (adult) vs. burning (baby) — different severity, same temperature Both describe the same threshold from different perspectives The Ben Ish Chai's Practical Test Problem: you can't use a thermometer on Shabbat — how do you assess? Ben Ish Chai's guideline (accepted by Chacham Ovadia and many poskim): Can eat/drink it freely → NOT yad soledet bo Would refrain because too hot → IS yad soledet bo Example: boiling tea you need to blow on and wait = still yad soledet bo Once you can sip freely and comfortably = below yad soledet bo The Temperature in Degrees Posek Temperature Chacham Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul 40°C Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach 45°C Chacham Ovadia Yosef 45°C (quotes 40°C but seems to take on 45°C) Rav Moshe Feinstein (maximum) 71°C Accepted minimum: 45°C Accepted maximum: 71°C True yad soledet bo is somewhere in that range Why 40°C Is Difficult to Accept People sit comfortably in mikvah water at 43°C (some mikva'ot in London display temperature) If people relax in 43°C water, hard to say 40°C is \"scalding\" Rav Falk's response (heard in shiur in Gateshead): our bodies have changed due to modern heating and constant hot water exposure — resistance to heat has increased. What was scalding at 40°C may no longer feel scalding to us, but halachically remains yad soledet bo Interesting idea, but majority of poskim take on 45°C Proof from Gemara That ~40°C Is Not Yad Soledet Bo Gemara in Chullin: חם בית השחיטה צונן — warmth at the slaughter site (body temperature, ~37–40°C) is halachically \"cold\" (צונן) Has ramifications for kashrut (kashering knives) Demonstrates that 40°C and below = not yad soledet bo The Critical Two-Direction Application The 45°C–71°C range creates a zone of uncertainty. Direction matters: Direction 1 — For stringency (putting something cold into hot liquid): Use the minimum — 45°C From 45°C upward, assume it may be yad soledet bo → capable of bishul Liquid at 50°C? → Cannot put uncooked items into it Direction 2 — For leniency (is my liquid still \"hot enough\" to reheat?): Use the maximum — 71°C Must be at least 71°C to be certain it's still yad soledet bo Liquid dropped to 60°C? → Cannot be sure it's above yad soledet bo → cannot reheat Liquid at 80°C? → Certainly still yad soledet bo → can reheat Quick reference: Scenario Ruling Soup cooled to 55°C — can I reheat? No — may be below yad soledet bo (could be as high as 71°C) Soup still at 80°C — can I reheat? Yes — certainly still yad soledet bo Liquid at 40°C — any concern? No — below minimum threshold by all opinions Seif ו (Vav): Placing a Hot Pot on Top of a Covered Pot The Scenario One pot already insulated/covered with a towel (hatmanah) Another pot with hot liquid (still yad soledet bo) you want to keep warm Can you place the second pot under the same covering, on top of the first? The Halacha — Permitted כלי שיש בו דבר חם שהיד סולדת בו מותר להניחו בשבת על גבי קדרה הטמונה כדי שישמור חמו ולא יצטנן A vessel with hot liquid (yad soledet bo) may be placed on top of a covered pot to retain its heat Hatmanah Background Generally: cannot fully wrap/insulate food on Shabbat, even with materials that don't add heat Key principle: If something is already covered, you may add to it Since the first pot is already insulated → adding the second pot under the existing insulation = permitted Also mentioned: can seal pot opening with dough if no lid (dough must be kneaded before Shabbat) — less relevant today The Restriction — Cooled Liquids אבל אין מניחים כלי שיש בו דבר שאינו חם כל כך... If the liquid in the top pot is no longer yad soledet bo → cannot place on top of a hot pot where it could be heated back to yad soledet bo Reason: yesh bishul achar bishul b'lach — cooled liquid is subject to further bishul Exception: If the bottom pot can only warm it slightly (not to yad soledet bo) → permitted Seif ז (Zayin): Even If the Bottom Pot Is on the Fire The Question We've established stacking pots is fine But what if the bottom pot is on the fire? Isn't placing the top pot essentially \"putting food on the fire\" on Shabbat? Two Opinions First opinion (halacha follows): Even if the bottom pot is on the fire → permitted The intervening pot = clear separation (hefsek) Better than garuf v'katum: Garuf v'katum = covered flame — a thin separation Whole pot in between = obvious, unmistakable separation Clear heker (indication) → won't come to cook or stoke Second opinion: Having bottom pot on fire = same as partial cover Cannot place food on a partial cover for the first time on Shabbat Shulchan Aruch rules like the first opinion. Seif ח (Chet): Same Principle for Solids Same two opinions applied to dry, fully cooked solids Shulchan Aruch again rules like the first opinion: permitted Caveat: If food has any liquid (מרק/רוטב) that is not yad soledet bo but could reach it from the heat below → forbidden according to all opinions Practical Application: Placing Food on an Urn Urn = water on a fire (the heating element) The urn itself = \"bottom pot on the fire\" Placing food on top of the urn = exactly this scenario Rulings: What You're Placing On the Urn? Dry, fully cooked solids Yes — always fine, even if reaches yad soledet bo Liquid still yad soledet bo (piping hot) Yes — not subject to further bishul Liquid cooled below yad soledet bo Only if urn surface cannot bring it to yad soledet bo Fully cold liquid (from fridge) Only if urn surface cannot bring it to yad soledet bo Plastic urn lid (standard white UK urn): Rabbi Kada's assessment: cold liquids on plastic lid likely cannot reach yad soledet bo Therefore: permitted to place cold liquids to warm them Metal urn lid: May conduct enough heat → cold liquids could reach yad soledet bo Must ensure: dry solid, or still yad soledet bo, or confirm surface isn't hot enough Additional point: Even if the urn element were considered a \"naked flame\" (most poskim say it isn't), you could still put food on top — the urn acts as the intervening pot (hefsek), exactly as in these seifim. Seif ט (Tet): Kli Rishon Cooks Even Off the Fire כלי ראשון אפילו לאחר שהעבירו מעל האש מבשל כל זמן שהיד סולדת בו Kli rishon = the original pot food was cooked in Retains cooking ability even off the fire, as long as yad soledet bo Cannot put spices or anything uncooked into it Practical picture: pot of soup off the hotplate, lid closed → stays boiling hot 30+ minutes → full kli rishon status the whole time Important Point Many People Don't Realize Kli rishon on the fire → cooks everything Kli rishon off the fire → does NOT cook everything The Gemara states clearly: meat of an ox (בשר שור) — very tough meat — cannot be cooked in a kli rishon off the fire The pot doesn't have enough sustained heat without the fire to cook such tough meat Everyone agrees on this The Two Versions About Salt Two lishnas (versions) of Rav Nachman's statement: First version (lishna kamma): Salt = the opposite extreme from ox meat Salt is kalei habishul — so easy to cook, it can cook in conditions weaker than a normal kli rishon Cannot put salt even where you'd normally be lenient Second version (lishna batra): Salt is like ox meat — extremely hard to cook Just as ox meat can go in a kli rishon off the fire → salt can too Rule of psak: When Gemara has two versions → halacha follows lishna batra (second version) Shulchan Aruch's Ruling אבל מלח מותר ליתן לתוכו כיון שהעבירו מעל האש דצריכא מלחא בישולא כבשרא דתורא Rambam, Rif, Rosh all follow the second version Salt may be placed in a kli rishon off the fire — needs as much cooking as ox meat Rabbi Kada tried to find scientific info on whether salt is easy or hard to cook — didn't find much The Rema's Position (Ashkenazim) Tosfot preferred the first version (salt = kalei habishul) The Rema brings this: ויש אוסרים ליתן מלח אפילו בכלי שני... והמחמיר תבוא עליו ברכה \"One who is strict will be blessed\" = main halacha permits it, but good to be machmir Summary: Salt on Shabbat Community Kli Rishon on Fire Kli Rishon off Fire Kli Sheni Sefardim Forbidden Permitted Permitted Ashkenazim (main halacha) Forbidden Permitted Permitted Ashkenazim (machmir) Forbidden Permitted Praiseworthy to avoid Be'dieved: Salt Put on the Fire ואם עבר ונתן מלח אפילו בכלי ראשון אפילו הוא על האש מותר המאכל דהמלח בטל If you transgressed and put salt even in a kli rishon on the fire → food is still permitted Reason: salt is batel (nullified) — less than 1/60th of the food The Difficulty: Avid L'ta'ama Major principle in kashrut: דבר העביד לטעמא לא בטל — something added for taste is never nullified, even less than 1/60th Salt = the quintessential example of something added for taste! So how can the Rema say it's batel? Resolution (acharonim): Must be talking about food already partially salted before Shabbat Principle of zeh v'zeh gorem (combined causes): original salt from before Shabbat + new Shabbat salt together produce the taste Forbidden element (Shabbat salt) is not the sole cause → bitul applies If food was NOT salted before Shabbat: Salt = avid l'ta'ama → NOT batel Food subject to full ma'aseh Shabbat rules (Seif Aleph): Intentional (meizid): violator may never eat it; others may eat after Shabbat Unintentional (shogeg): no one eats on Shabbat; everyone eats after Shabbat Other Opinion on Meat Some say: although ox meat can't be cooked in kli rishon off fire, the moisture/liquid inside it can be cooked → therefore forbidden Not followed — main halacha permits placing hard meat in kli rishon off the fire Seif י (Yud): Irui Kli Rishon — Pouring from a Kli Rishon אסור ליתן תבלין בקערה ולערות עליהם מכלי ראשון Forbidden to put anything in a bowl and pour onto it from a kli rishon Follows Rabbeinu Tam (over Rashbam): irui kli rishon has cooking ability Key details: Irui cooks kdei klipa — one layer/peel. Less powerful than a full kli rishon but still constitutes bishul For Shabbat: Any cooking = forbidden de'oraita, so kdei klipa makes no practical difference For kashrut: Matters — irui only affects the outer layer (transfer of taste between meat/milk etc.) For Pesach: Same — pouring hot chametz liquid onto a surface affects only kdei klipa Seif י\"ג (Yud Gimmel): Putting Cold Water in a Kli Sheni מותר ליתן קיתון של מים בכלי שני שיש בו מים חמים אבל בכלי ראשון אסור Cold water (even in a bottle) → permitted in a kli sheni Cold water → forbidden in a kli rishon (even off the fire) Why the Gemara Specifies a Bottle (Kiton) Could have just said \"you can put cold water in a kli sheni\" — why mention a bottle? Chiddush is on the kli rishon side: You might think the bottle insulates and the water inside can't cook Gemara teaches: no — even water in a bottle, in a kli rishon off the fire, can be heated to yad soledet bo → forbidden In a kli sheni — with or without bottle — fine, because כלי שני אינו מבשל Why Doesn't Kli Sheni Cook? (Tosfot's Explanation) Fundamental question: water in a kli sheni can still be 80–90°C Pour from urn into cup → temperature drops maybe 1–3°C → still boiling hot A child's stomach would certainly be burnt → so why doesn't it cook? Tosfot's answer: Cooking involves not just the liquid's temperature but also the walls of the pot Fire heats both the liquid AND the walls → walls contribute additional cooking power from all sides Once transferred to kli sheni → new walls were never heated by fire Cold walls being warmed by the liquid ≠ fire-heated walls Liquid is hot but lacks the reinforcement of fire-heated walls → no full cooking ability Scientifically: Difficult to explain — temperature difference between kli rishon and kli sheni is minimal. But this is the accepted halachic reasoning and the basis for the entire kli rishon/sheni/shlishi framework. Seif י\"ד (Yud Daled): Placing Food Near the Fire מותר ליתן קיתון של מים כנגד האש להפיג צינתן ובלבד שיתנם רחוק... שאינו יכול להתחמם... עד שתהא יד סולדת בו You may place food/liquid near a fire to warm it Condition: far enough that it cannot reach yad soledet bo even if left indefinitely Background: Why \"Near\" Is Different from \"On\" Rabbinically: can't put food on the fire on Shabbat (even fully cooked) Reason 1: may come to stoke coals (מחתה בגחלים) Reason 2: looks like cooking (מחזי כמבשל) Near the fire (not on it) → doesn't trigger these concerns Doesn't look like cooking Won't come to stoke No rabbinic prohibition But: May still be a Torah prohibition of bishul if food can reach yad soledet bo from radiated heat The Critical Rule: Even for a Short Time אבל אסור לקרבו אל האש למקום שיכול להתחמם שתהא יד סולדת בו ואפילו להניח בו שעה קטנה Cannot place anything where it could reach yad soledet bo Even if you intend to remove it before it gets that hot Even \"a short time\" to just take the chill off = forbidden Reason: concerned you'll forget — get distracted, answer the door, start a conversation → food reaches yad soledet bo Practical examples: Scenario Ruling Next to a radiator → warms to 30°C, cannot reach 45°C Mutar Next to a roaring fireplace → could reach yad soledet bo in 30 min Asur — even if you plan to remove in 5 min On a windowsill in direct sun → gets warm but not yad soledet bo Mutar (also: cooking in sun itself is permitted per Seif Gimmel) The Rema: Fruit and Raw Edible Foods הוא הדין בפירות ושאר דברים הנאכלים כמות שהן חיים Even fruit and items edible raw are subject to bishul Cooking improves them (apple → baked apple) Cannot place where they could reach yad soledet bo Sha'ar HaTziyun: cooking already-edible food is most likely de'oraita (cooking transforms and improves = meaningful bishul) Towels on Radiators (Sha'ar HaTziyun) Dry towel on a radiator: Permitted Even if it could get very hot Reason: if it gets too hot → gets ruined/burnt → you won't forget to remove it Natural built-in reminder Wet towel on a radiator: Forbidden Water in the towel = subject to bishul Also: drying a wet garment on a radiator may involve melabein (laundering) — heat helps remove absorbed substances Seif ט\"ו (Tet Vav): Dry, Fully Cooked Solids Near the Fire דבר שנתבשל כל צרכו והוא יבש... מותר להניחו כנגד המדורה אפילו במקום שהיד סולדת בו Fully cooked dry solid → may be placed near the fire even where it can reach yad soledet bo Two reasons this is permitted: No bishul issue: אין בישול אחר בישול for solids — already fully cooked No shehiyah issue: Next to the fire, not on it — shehiyah doesn't apply ואפילו נצטנן כבר Even if already cooled down completely → dry solids are not subject to further bishul Can take cold schnitzel and place right next to a roaring fireplace → perfectly fine Practical application: Holiday house with a fireplace on Shabbat — place fully cooked dry chicken, schnitzel, kugel etc. next to the fire freely. But NOT cooled soup, gravy, or dishes with significant liquid. The Rema on Liquids Near the Fire ונהגו להקל בזה אם לא נצטנן לגמרי Reiterates his position from Seif Dalet Ashkenazim: if liquid is still lukewarm (poshrim, ~27–30°C) → may place near fire If fully cold → may not (even next to the fire — liquid could reach yad soledet bo) A Question from the Shiur: Food on the Fire Before Yad Soledet Bo Robert's question: Put uncooked food in a kli rishon on the fire, water only 25–30°C, realize mistake and take off — have you committed an aveirah? For Solids Not an issur de'oraita — provided food hasn't reached ma'achal Ben Drosai (minimum edibility: ~half-cooked or one-third cooked) Once it reaches ma'achal Ben Drosai → definitely violated Torah prohibition If it hasn't: Have violated a rabbinic prohibition — never allowed to leave something where it can reach yad soledet bo What to do: Take off immediately, before ma'achal Ben Drosai → at least rectify de'oraita For Liquids Major discussion: what is ma'achal Ben Drosai for liquids? Is it half of yad soledet bo? One-third? Some poskim: concept of ma'achal Ben Drosai doesn't apply to liquids at all — only solid food Rabbi Kada noted he hasn't reviewed this recently but there is significant debate Davar Gush — The Maharshal's Chiddush (Mishnah Berurah, Seif Tet) The Concept (Not Found in the Rishonim) Liquids: When transferred between vessels → temperature genuinely decreases Particles move and disperse, transferring heat to new vessel's walls Kli sheni is genuinely cooler → the transfer makes a real difference Solids (davar gush): A solid mass (boiling hot potato, chunk of meat, kugel) retains heat regardless of vessel Heat trapped inside the solid → doesn't dissipate by moving to a new plate A boiling hot potato on your plate = still effectively a kli rishon Full cooking ability, regardless of second plate, third plate, tenth plate Practical Implications Community Position Practical Result Ashkenazim (Mishnah Berurah) Machmir like Maharshal Cannot put salt, butter, spices on a hot potato until it cools below yad soledet bo — regardless of how many plates Sefardim (Chacham Ovadia) Do NOT accept davar gush Hot solid on second plate = kli sheni; third plate = kli shlishi. Regular rules apply. Rabbi Kada's observation from yeshiva: \"I remember in yeshiva they wouldn't put anything on potatoes. You had to wait until it wasn't hot anymore.\" — Ashkenazi practice following the Maharshal. Key Practical Summary Yad soledet bo: 45°C–71°C. On Shabbat: if you can't eat/drink it freely → yad soledet bo Stacking pots: May place hot pot on top of another (even one on fire) and cover together — intervening pot = hefsek, better than garuf v'katum Food on an urn: Dry cooked solids → always fine. Hot liquids (yad soledet bo) → fine. Cold liquids → only if urn surface can't bring to yad soledet bo Kli rishon off fire: Still cooks — but cannot cook ox meat or salt (Shulchan Aruch) Salt: Sefardim → kli rishon off fire freely. Ashkenazim → main halacha permits, praiseworthy to avoid even kli sheni Irui kli rishon: Forbidden de'oraita (Rabbeinu Tam). Never pour from urn directly onto uncooked items Kli sheni doesn't cook: Walls not heated by fire (Tosfot) Near the fire: Only where it cannot reach yad soledet bo. Even momentary placement in a hotter spot = forbidden Dry cooked solids near fire: Always permitted — even fully cooled, even if can reach yad soledet bo Davar gush: Ashkenazim → hot solids retain kli rishon status. Sefardim → do not accept Preview: Next Shiur Seifim י\"א–י\"ב: Tata'ah gavar / ila'ah gavar — hot meets cold, which dominates? Seif י\"ז: Congealed liquids melting on Shabbat Seif י\"ח: Stirring pots on Shabbat (הגסה) — important practical point many aren't aware of Shabbat Shalom","cuTECxA06BI":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; Erev Pesach on Shabbat Class 6 446–447:1–2 Finding chametz during Pesach; muktze on Yom Tov; introduction to kashrut principles; be-mashehu rule Class 7 447:4, 5, 10 Chozer ve-ne'or (reawakening); practical implications for hechsherim; noten ta'am li-fgam on Pesach Siman 447 (Continued) – Mixtures of Chametz on Pesach Recap of Key Principles from Last Week Be-mashehu on Pesach: During the year, lach be-lach (liquid mixtures) are nullified at 1 in 60. On Pesach, even 1 in 1,000 is not nullified (be-mashehu) — this is de-rabbanan (Rabbeinu Tam disagrees entirely). Two reasons for this chumra: (a) Severity of chametz (karet). (b) Davar she-yesh lo matirin — chametz will become permitted after Pesach, so don't rely on nullification now. Two hot solids touching: Kedei netilah / kedei klipah still applies on Pesach — no change from the rest of the year. Be-mashehu only applies where you'd normally need 1 in 60. Timing: Be-mashehu only applies to mixtures that occurred during Pesach itself . Mixtures from before Pesach (even from chatzot on Erev Pesach) follow the regular 1 in 60 rule. Se'if 4 – Does Nullified Chametz \"Reawaken\" on Pesach? (חוזר וניעור) This is one of the most consequential halachot for everyday Pesach observance. The scenario: Chametz got mixed into non-chametz before Pesach , and there was 60 times more non-chametz — so it was properly nullified according to regular kashrut rules. Now Pesach arrives. Does the nullified chametz \"come alive again\" (chozer ve-ne'or) and become prohibited? Two Opinions in the Shulchan Aruch Opinion Position Who Holds It Ramification Stam (default opinion) It does NOT reawaken. Once nullified before Pesach, it stays nullified throughout Pesach. You may eat the food on Pesach. Rosh, Rashba, and others More lenient — food nullified before Pesach is permitted on Pesach Yesh Cholkim (some disagree) It DOES reawaken. When Pesach begins, the nullified chametz becomes active again and prohibits the mixture. Understood to be the Rambam (as interpreted by the Ravad HaMagid — not explicit in the Rambam) More strict — even food nullified before Pesach cannot be eaten on Pesach If It Reawakens — What Was the Point of Nullification Before Pesach? The second opinion still acknowledges that nullification before Pesach has real effects: Erev Pesach afternoon (from chatzot until nightfall): Even according to the yesh cholkim, the food is permitted during this window — it was nullified, and it only reawakens when Pesach itself begins at nightfall. Ta'am (taste) vs. Mamashut (substance): Even the yesh cholkim agree that if only taste remains (no physical particles of chametz), the taste does not reawaken. Only when there is mamashut — actual physical substance/particles of chametz in the mixture — does it reawaken. Practical example of the distinction: Bread fell into a large pot of soup before Pesach. You strained the soup and removed all pieces. Only taste remains. → Everyone agrees the soup is permitted on Pesach. Ta'am doesn't reawaken. Bread fell into soup before Pesach. You did not strain it. Tiny particles of bread remain dissolved in the soup. → First opinion: permitted (doesn't reawaken). Second opinion: prohibited on Pesach (mamashut reawakens). Does Reawakening Require Heat? Even among those who hold it reawakens, there is a further machloket: View Position Some Acharonim Reawakening only occurs when heat is applied on Pesach — it doesn't happen automatically. If you don't cook the food on Pesach, it doesn't reawaken even according to the yesh cholkim. Mishnah Berurah Seems to be machmir (strict) — reawakening may happen even without heat Important to note: We are stacking multiple layers of chumra here: The entire be-mashehu rule on Pesach is de-rabbanan (Rabbeinu Tam disagrees entirely) Within this de-rabbanan, there's a machloket whether it reawakens Even among those who say it reawakens, there's a machloket whether heat is required All of this assumes chametz was actually present in the first place The Halacha Shulchan Aruch (Sephardim): Follows the stam — it does not reawaken. Once nullified before Pesach, the food is permitted on Pesach. This is the opinion of Chacham Ovadia Yosef. Rama (Ashkenazim): Makes a compromise : Type of Mixture Rama's Ruling Lach be-lach (pure liquid with liquid — completely intermingled, no solid particles) Lenient — follows first opinion. Does not reawaken. Yavesh be-yavesh or solid in liquid (any element of solid involved — e.g., bread fell into wine) Strict — follows second opinion. Does reawaken on Pesach. Practical consequence: Since most real-world mixtures involve some element of solid (even tiny particles), the Rama is strict in most cases. Only pure liquid-to-liquid mixtures (e.g., two liquids poured together) get the leniency. Massive Practical Implications: Do You Need a Hechsher on Natural Products? Understanding Se'if 4 has enormous practical ramifications for what foods require a Pesach hechsher. The Logic (Following the Shulchan Aruch / Sephardi Psak) Take the example of olive oil (or salt, sugar, coffee, tea, yoghurt, rice cakes, crisps, milk, etc.): Check the ingredients — if the label says 100% olive oil, that's what you're getting. We live in a country with strict manufacturing regulations and allergy/allergen disclosure requirements. What's on the label is what's in the product. Theoretical concern: Perhaps in the production facility, a non-Jewish worker's bread crumb fell in, or some cross-contamination occurred. Even if that happened (which is highly unlikely in modern clinical manufacturing conditions) — it would have been nullified before Pesach (well over 1 in 60). Concern: When you heat the oil on Pesach, it reawakens. Shulchan Aruch: It does not reawaken → permitted. Even for the yesh cholkim: You don't have to assume chametz was even present (see Se'if 5 below). The Ruling For Sephardim (following Shulchan Aruch and Chacham Ovadia): All natural products whose ingredients are clearly listed and contain no chametz grains (wheat, barley, oats, rye, spelt) do not need a Pesach hechsher , provided you buy them before Pesach. This includes: olive oil, vegetable oil, salt, sugar, coffee, tea, yoghurt, rice cakes, crisps, milk, and similar products. If you want to be machmir and follow the Rama / Ben Ish Chai / yesh cholkim — that is praiseworthy. But it should not be imposed on the community, especially given the significant price differences for Pesach-certified products. Should You Buy Before Pesach? Timing Recommendation Before Pesach (ideal) Buy natural products before Pesach and store/freeze them. This covers all opinions — even the yesh cholkim agree nullification before Pesach is effective for ta'am. On Chol HaMoed There is a concern that the product was manufactured on 1st/2nd day Chag (during Pesach) and arrived at the supermarket on Chol HaMoed — in which case nullification before Pesach wouldn't apply. However, based on Se'if 5 (below), there are strong grounds to say this is also permitted since you don't have to assume chametz was present. Ashkenazi Guests If you are Sephardi, hosting Ashkenazi guests, and bought products without a Pesach hechsher: Based on Se'if 4 alone, there could be a problem (the Rama holds it reawakens in most cases). Based on Se'if 5 (below), there are strong grounds for leniency even for Ashkenazim. Best practice: At minimum, disclose to your guests what you're following. There are grounds for leniency, but this is a question that requires further discussion among the poskim. Se'if 5 – Salting Before Pesach; The Principle of \"Don't Assume Chametz Is Present\" Shulchan Aruch: If you had dried meat, chicken, fish, or cheese that was salted before Pesach, and you weren't careful about the salt (didn't check that the salt was free of chametz), and you salted it in a chametz utensil — you may eat these foods on Pesach. Why Is It Permitted? Three Reasons Reason 1 — Salting doesn't extract from utensils: Melichah (salting) does not have the ability to extract bliot (absorbed tastes) from the walls of utensils. This is an important principle in all of kashrut — only heat (cooking, boiling, frying) extracts bliot. Salting does not. Therefore, salting in a chametz utensil is not a concern. Reason 2 — Stam kelim einam ben yoman: The default assumption about any utensil is that it hasn't been used in the last 24 hours (stam kelim einam ben yoman). After 24 hours, any absorbed taste becomes pagum (degraded) — noten ta'am li-fgam — which is permitted (see Se'if 10 below). Reason 3 — The crucial principle: אין מחזיקין איסורא / We do not presume prohibition: The Shulchan Aruch states: We do not assume that chametz is present. Don't presume things are problematic or prohibited without evidence. Some people approach Pesach thinking chametz is \"lurking in every corner, waiting to catch you.\" The halacha says: the default presumption is that things are not chametz. Unless you actually saw chametz, or there's a real, specific reason to suspect it — you don't have to worry. This applies even to the salt used — you don't have to assume there was chametz in the salt. What Does \"Salting\" Mean in This Context? Salting in kashrut means salting to the extent that the food is inedible because of the salt (heavy salting for preservation, then washing off). This is not regular seasoning or light salting. Exception: Salting on Pesach Itself in Chametz Utensils Shulchan Aruch: If you salted food on Pesach itself in chametz utensils and then soaked it in water in those utensils — yesh lehachmir (one should try to be stringent and not eat it). Note: \"Yesh lehachmir\" (one should be stringent) is not the same as \"yesh cholkim\" (some disagree). It means: ideally be strict, but bedi'avad it would be permitted. The Rama's Position Rama: The custom in Ashkenazi lands is to be strict in the first instance and not eat foods salted with salt that wasn't verified chametz-free. However, even the Rama says: if you washed the meat three times thoroughly (soaked, rinsed, removed all traces of salt), you may eat it. Why is the Rama strict? He holds it reawakens (chozer ve-ne'or) — see Se'if 4. He doesn't fully rely on the presumption that chametz isn't present. However: The Rama was discussing salt that was \"lying around\" — loose salt in an open kitchen, hundreds of years ago, where chametz could plausibly be mixed in. In modern manufacturing conditions — sterile factories, single-product production lines, allergy regulations, cross-contamination controls — even the Rama might agree the concern is negligible. Se'if 10 – Noten Ta'am Li-Fgam on Pesach (נותן טעם לפגם) This is another fundamental machloket between the Shulchan Aruch and Rama with far-reaching practical consequences. Background: Noten Ta'am Li-Fgam During the Year When food taste has been absorbed in the walls of a utensil for more than 24 hours , the taste becomes pagum (degraded/spoiled). If you then cook in that utensil, the degraded taste that transfers into the food is called noten ta'am li-fgam — \"giving a spoiled taste.\" Year-round halacha: Lechatchila (ab initio) — don't cook in a non-kosher pot. Bedi'avad (post facto) — the food is completely permitted . The spoiled taste doesn't prohibit. From the Torah: Even within 24 hours, noten ta'am li-fgam is permitted. The Chachamim forbade within 24 hours as a precaution. The Question on Pesach Since we found that the Chachamim imposed a special stringency on Pesach (be-mashehu — even a drop prohibits), should we extend this stringency to noten ta'am li-fgam as well? Should we say that on Pesach, even degraded taste prohibits? The Machloket Opinion Ruling Reasoning Shulchan Aruch Noten ta'am li-fgam is permitted on Pesach — same as the rest of the year We don't extend chumrot beyond what the Chachamim explicitly stated. What we found (be-mashehu), we found. We don't add to it. Rama Noten ta'am li-fgam is prohibited on Pesach Since we found the Chachamim were strict in one area, we should extend the stringency to other areas as well. This was the custom in Ashkenaz. The Rama adds: Even a \"double\" leniency — be-mashehu + noten ta'am li-fgam combined — is still asur according to the Ashkenazi custom. Practical Scenario You accidentally cooked Pesach food in a chametz pot that was clean and hadn't been used for more than 24 hours: According to... Is the food permitted? Shulchan Aruch (Sephardim) ✅ Yes — noten ta'am li-fgam, same leniency as the rest of the year Rama (Ashkenazim) ❌ No — on Pesach, even degraded taste prohibits Important: This is one of the major reasons Ashkenazi Pesach practice is significantly more stringent than Sephardi practice. The Shulchan Aruch refuses to extend chumrot beyond what the Gemara explicitly stated; the Rama extends them based on the spirit of the Pesach stringency. Summary of Key Halachot Se'if Key Takeaways 447:4 (chozer ve-ne'or) Chametz nullified before Pesach (1 in 60): Shulchan Aruch (stam) = does NOT reawaken on Pesach. Yesh cholkim = does reawaken. Halacha for Sephardim: does not reawaken (Chacham Ovadia). Rama compromises: lach be-lach = lenient; solid involved = strict. 447:4 (ta'am vs mamashut) Even the yesh cholkim agree: if only taste remains (no physical particles — e.g., you strained the soup), it does NOT reawaken. Only mamashut (substance) reawakens. 447:4 (heat) Even among those who say it reawakens: machloket whether heat is needed to trigger reawakening. Some Acharonim: no heat = no reawakening. 447:4 (practical) For Sephardim: natural products (oil, salt, sugar, tea, coffee, yoghurt, rice cakes, milk) do NOT need a Pesach hechsher if ingredients are clearly listed and chametz-free. Buy before Pesach ideally. Strong grounds to be lenient even on Chol HaMoed. 447:5 (salting) Food salted before Pesach with unchecked salt, in chametz utensils: permitted. Three reasons: melichah doesn't extract from utensils; stam kelim einam ben yoman; ein machzikin issura (don't assume chametz is present). 447:5 (don't assume) Critical principle: we do NOT presume chametz is lurking. Default is things are kosher. Don't assume chametz contamination in modern manufacturing without specific evidence. 447:5 (Rama) Ashkenazi custom: strict about unchecked salt. But was discussing loose salt in pre-modern conditions — in modern factories, even the Rama might agree it's fine. 447:10 (noten ta'am li-fgam) Cooked in a chametz pot not used in 24+ hours: Shulchan Aruch = permitted on Pesach (don't extend chumrot). Rama = prohibited (extend the Pesach stringency). Major practical difference between Sephardi and Ashkenazi Pesach. Why Sephardi Pesach Is \"Easier\" — Summary of the Fundamental Differences Issue Shulchan Aruch (Sephardim) Rama (Ashkenazim) Be-mashehu (even a drop) Applies on Pesach itself only Applies on Pesach itself only Chozer ve-ne'or (reawakening) Does not reawaken Reawakens in most cases (except pure liquid-liquid) Ein machzikin issura (don't assume chametz) Applies fully — don't assume contamination More cautious — custom to be strict Noten ta'am li-fgam Permitted on Pesach (same as year-round) Prohibited on Pesach Need for Pesach hechsher on natural products Generally not needed if ingredients are clear Generally needed as a stringency","AW-D-GegxaM":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; Erev Pesach on Shabbat Class 6 446–447 Finding chametz during Pesach; muktze on Yom Tov; non-Jew's chametz in your domain; introduction to kashrut principles; mixtures of chametz on Pesach (be-mashehu) Note: Siman 445 (methods of bi'ur chametz — burning, breaking down, scattering, throwing into the sea) was covered briefly at the end of the previous class. Siman 442 (ta'arovet chametz) still to be revisited. Siman 446 – Finding Chametz During Pesach (מי שמוצא חמץ בתוך המועד) Contains 4 Se'ifim Se'if 1 – Finding Chametz on Chol HaMoed vs. Yom Tov Shulchan Aruch: If you find chametz in your possession during Pesach: On Chol HaMoed Immediately take it out and destroy it. Do not delay — the concern is you may forget about it or come to eat it. Methods of destruction (for Sephardim): burn it, flush it down the toilet, throw it into the sea — any of the three forms we learned. Ashkenazim require specifically burning. On Yom Tov (1st and 7th Day) You may not move it — it is muktze mahamat gufo (the highest class of muktze: it has no permitted use whatsoever — you can't eat it, you can't benefit from it). Cover it with a utensil until after the Chag, then destroy it. You cannot burn it in place either, even if it happens to be near a fire — because burning on Yom Tov is only permitted le-tzorech ochel nefesh (for food preparation purposes), and burning chametz for destruction is not ochel nefesh. The Major Question: What If You Didn't Do Bitul Before Pesach? Scenario Status What To Do on 1st/7th Day Did bitul before Pesach Mid'oraita: you don't own the chametz (bitul removes ownership). Mid'rabbanan: still must destroy it. Cover it over. Wait until after the Chag to destroy. Do not violate muktze. Did NOT do bitul before Pesach Isur de'oraita — every moment it is in your house, you are violating Bal Yera'eh / Bal Yimatzei (owning chametz on Pesach). You cannot do bitul now — it's too late. Shulchan Aruch / Rambam: Still cover it over and wait. Do not violate muktze. Even though there's an isur de'oraita of owning, it is a passive violation (shev ve'al ta'aseh), not an active one. Better to passively violate a de'oraita than to actively violate the de-rabbanan of muktze. Some Rishonim disagree and hold that if you didn't do bitul, you should violate muktze and carry it out to stop transgressing Bal Yera'eh. However, the Shulchan Aruch follows the Rambam that even in this case, one covers it over. Best Option: Ask a Non-Jew You may explicitly ask a non-Jew to come, take the chametz, and destroy it. Why is this permitted? Asking a non-Jew to do melachah is normally asur de-rabbanan (shvut). But here it is shvut de-shvut le-tzorech mitzvah — a double rabbinic leniency for the sake of a mitzvah — which is permitted. What About Second Day Yom Tov (2nd and 8th Day — Outside Israel)? Shulchan Aruch: Yesh mi she'omer — some Rishonim hold that second day Yom Tov has the status of Chol HaMoed for this matter. Mishnah Berurah's ruling (consensus of poskim): Scenario on 2nd/8th Day What To Do Did bitul before Pesach Cover it over — no isur de'oraita of ownership, so no reason to violate muktze Did NOT do bitul before Pesach Take it out and destroy it — better to violate muktze (de-rabbanan) on the second day of Yom Tov (also de-rabbanan) — a double de-rabbanan — rather than continue violating the isur de'oraita of Bal Yera'eh Rationale: On the first day (de'oraita Yom Tov), the Rambam/Shulchan Aruch say don't actively violate even a de-rabbanan against a passive de'oraita. But on the second day (de-rabbanan Yom Tov), combining two de-rabbanans (muktze + second day) against a de'oraita tips the balance. Se'if 3 (Discussed in class) – A Non-Jew's Chametz That Entered Your Property Shulchan Aruch: If you live near a non-Jew and his chametz rolled or was pushed into your domain on Pesach (e.g., wind blew bread onto your balcony, a child pushed it over): You are not violating any isur de'oraita — it doesn't belong to you. However, mid'rabbanan, the Chachamim don't want chametz in your house — you might come to eat it. Situation What To Do Chol HaMoed Do not lift it up — unlike your own chametz which you lift to destroy, here you're not destroying it (it belongs to the non-Jew). Lifting creates a concern you may eat it. Instead, prod it away with a stick back into the non-Jew's domain. Shabbat / Yom Tov Cannot even prod it (tiltul min ha-tzad issues — see below). Cover it over with a utensil. After the Chag, prod it back. Muktze Subtlety: Tiltul Min Ha-Tzad vs. Tiltul Be-Shinui Type Definition Example Permitted on Shabbat/YT? Tiltul min ha-tzad (indirect movement) Using another object to move the muktze item Prodding chametz with a stick Generally asur — this is why you can't prod it on Shabbat/YT Tiltul be-shinui (movement in an unusual manner) Moving the item yourself but in a strange/unusual way Pushing it with your elbow Generally mutar — permitted when there's a need So theoretically, you could push the non-Jew's chametz away with your elbow on Shabbat/Yom Tov. But you should not lift it, even with a shinui, because of the concern you may come to eat it. The simplest option remains: cover it over. What About Hefker Chametz? (Ownerless Chametz — e.g., a Fox Brought Bread into Your Garden) A chatzer (courtyard) acquires hefker items automatically — chatzer koneh — so this chametz may technically belong to you. The Kol Chamira formula you said before Pesach does not cover chametz that arrived after Pesach began. However: You are an ones (completely helpless / couldn't have prevented it), and the Mishnah Berurah says in such a case, you just cover it over. You didn't do anything wrong — you didn't forget, didn't leave it. Preventive measure: Before Pesach, one can declare: \"I explicitly do not want my chatzer to acquire any chametz on my behalf.\" This removes the automatic acquisition. Se'if 4 (Discussed in class) – Doubt: Is This Matzah or Bread? Shulchan Aruch: If you find food in your house on Pesach and have a genuine doubt — is it matzah or bread? You may assume it is matzah and eat it. Why? The presumption (chazakah) is that it came from the most recent time — i.e., after you cleaned for Pesach. Since you did bedikat chametz, whatever you find now is presumed to be Pesach food. If it is moldy — and it couldn't have become that moldy since the start of Pesach — you must throw it out (it's clearly from before Pesach). Applicability today: This would apply to soft/thick matzah (Sephardi-style) that could be confused with pita or laffa. With modern cracker matzah, confusion with bread is essentially impossible. However, a scenario could arise with matzah marked \"not suitable for Pesach use\" (factory matzah made year-round) mixed up with kosher-for-Pesach matzah. Rabbeinu Hananel's understanding: This halacha only means you don't have to destroy it after Pesach (it won't have the status of chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach). But you should not eat it during Pesach itself. Biur Halacha: Better not to rely on this leniency in practice. Wait until after Pesach to eat it. Siman 446 – Summary Finding Chol HaMoed 1st/7th Day (YT de'oraita) 2nd/8th Day (YT de-rabbanan) Your chametz — did bitul Take out & destroy immediately Cover over; destroy after Chag Cover over; destroy after Chag Your chametz — did NOT do bitul Take out & destroy immediately Cover over (Shulchan Aruch/Rambam — even here, don't violate muktze). Best: ask a non-Jew. Take out & destroy — violate muktze (double de-rabbanan vs. de'oraita) Non-Jew's chametz Prod away with a stick; don't lift it Cover over Cover over Siman 447 – Mixtures of Chametz on Pesach (תערובות חמץ בפסח) Beginning of a large chapter — introduction and Se'ifim 1–2 covered Essential Background: Three Principles of Kashrut (Year-Round) Before learning the Pesach-specific halachot, one must understand the basic kashrut principles that apply throughout the year. These form the foundation for understanding what changes on Pesach. Principle 1: Lach Be-Lach (Liquid with Liquid — לח בלח) Definition: Two liquids mixed together, or a solid that fell into a liquid. Examples: Non-kosher sauce mixed with kosher sauce; non-kosher wine poured into kosher wine; a piece of kosher meat falling into non-kosher sauce. The rule: Because liquids fully intermingle, the taste (ta'am) transfers completely. Therefore: Sub-type De'oraita De-rabbanan Min be-she'eino mino (different flavours) Need 1 in 60 — until 60 times more kosher than non-kosher, the taste is still present Need 1 in 60 Min be-mino (same flavour) Majority suffices (rov) — since flavours are identical, there's no distinguishable taste Still need 1 in 60 (rabbinic stringency) Note on tasting: Theoretically, if a non-Jew who is an expert taster confirmed there's no non-kosher taste, one could rely on less than 1 in 60. In practice, we don't do this — the Chachamim established fixed thresholds: under 60 = definitely taste present; over 60 = definitely no taste. Principle 2: Two Hot Solids Touching (יבש ביבש — חם בחם) Definition: Two solid pieces of food touching each other — one kosher, one non-kosher — with heat involved. The rule: Since they're solid, taste doesn't fully transfer like with liquids. The transfer is limited to the surface area of contact. Scenario How Much to Remove from the Kosher Piece Both hot (45°C+ / yad soledet bo) Kedei netilah — cut away a thumb-breadth from where they touched One hot, one cold (regardless of which) Kedei klipah — peel away a thin sliver from the point of contact Both cold Just wash it — no heat means no meaningful transfer Exception — fatty/oily (shaman): If either piece is very fatty, oily, or has bubbling liquids, the fat can transfer taste across the entire piece. In that case, the leniency of kedei netilah/klipah doesn't apply — you need 1 in 60 (like Principle 1). Principle 3: Mixture Without Taste Transfer (תערובת בלי טעם) Definition: Kosher and non-kosher foods got mixed together in the same container/bag, but they haven't touched, cooked together, or transmitted taste to each other. You simply don't know which is which. Examples: Pieces of kosher and non-kosher meat mixed in the same bag (not touching, both cold). Sub-type De'oraita De-rabbanan Min be-mino (same type/flavour) Majority suffices — can eat all pieces (each individually) Can eat all pieces, but cannot cook them together Min be-she'eino mino (different flavours) Majority suffices Need 1 in 60 — gezeirah: you might come to cook them together, which would trigger Principle 1 How These Principles Change on Pesach The Key Difference: Be-Mashehu (במשהו) — Even One Drop Prohibits Shulchan Aruch (Se'if 1): On Pesach, a mixture of chametz — whether min be-mino or min be-she'eino mino — is forbidden be-mashehu (with even the tiniest amount). Even one drop of chametz liquid in a thousand parts of kosher food renders the entire mixture forbidden. This applies to Principle 1 (lach be-lach) situations — where during the year you would need 1 in 60, on Pesach even 1 in 1,000 is forbidden. Two Reasons Given by the Rishonim Reason Explanation Severity of chametz Chametz on Pesach carries karet — far more severe than standard non-kosher food (which carries only malkut). The Chachamim therefore applied a stricter standard. Davar she-yesh lo matirin Chametz is something that will become permitted after Pesach. Since you can simply wait and eat it later, the Chachamim said: don't rely on nullification now when the prohibition is temporary. Important: This be-mashehu rule is de-rabbanan . Mid'oraita, even on Pesach, 1 in 60 would suffice. Minority Opinion: Rabbeinu Tam and the Sheiltot Rabbeinu Tam, the Sheiltot (one of the Geonim), and Abba HaGaon held that even on Pesach, standard year-round rules apply — 1 in 60 suffices. The Shulchan Aruch does not follow this, but the Mishnah Berurah notes that in extremely pressing circumstances (e.g., very large financial loss, major communal event), there is something to rely on in the background, bearing in mind the entire be-mashehu rule is de-rabbanan. Se'if 1 – The Halacha of Be-Mashehu Shulchan Aruch: A mixture on Pesach — whether min be-mino or min be-she'eino mino — is forbidden be-mashehu (even with one drop). It is forbidden even to have benefit from (asur be-hana'ah). Rama adds: Some poskim suggested you could sell the mixture to a non-Jew, deducting the value of the chametz portion (so you don't benefit from the isur). But the Rama rules: we do not do this. The entire mixture must be destroyed. However, the utensils used do not need to be kashered — they can be used for future Pesach. What Principle 2 (Hot Solids Touching) on Pesach? Shulchan Aruch: The be-mashehu rule only applies where you would normally need 1 in 60 during the year (i.e., Principle 1 situations). Where you would only need kedei netilah or kedei klipah (Principle 2 — two hot/warm solids touching without fatty liquid), those same leniencies still apply on Pesach. Example: A hot piece of bread touched a hot piece of matzah on Pesach — cut away a thumb-breadth (kedei netilah) from the matzah, and the rest is fine. No be-mashehu here, because there was never a 1 in 60 issue to begin with. Exception: If the solids are fatty/oily (shaman), then you're back to needing 1 in 60 — which on Pesach means be-mashehu. Se'if 2 – When Did the Mixture Occur? Shulchan Aruch: The be-mashehu rule only applies to mixtures that occurred during the seven days of Pesach itself (or eight days outside Israel). If chametz became mixed in before Pesach (even from chatzot onwards on Erev Pesach when eating is already forbidden), the year-round rule of 1 in 60 applies — not be-mashehu. The special stringency of be-mashehu is limited to the seven/eight days of Pesach proper. Introduction to Noten Ta'am Li-Fgam (נותן טעם לפגם) Principle: If a non-kosher taste has been absorbed in a utensil for more than 24 hours, it becomes \"pagum\" (degraded/spoiled). A degraded taste that transfers to food is called noten ta'am li-fgam — \"it gives a spoiled taste.\" Year-round halacha: Bedi'avad, if you accidentally cooked in a non-kosher pot that hadn't been used for 24+ hours, the food is permitted . The absorbed taste is considered spoiled and doesn't prohibit. On Pesach: Opinion Ruling on Noten Ta'am Li-Fgam Shulchan Aruch Same leniency applies on Pesach — noten ta'am li-fgam is permitted bedi'avad, just like during the year Rama More stringent — does not apply this leniency on Pesach itself. However, agrees it applies from six hours on Erev Pesach (when chametz is already forbidden rabbinically but Pesach hasn't yet begun) Practical implication: If you accidentally cooked Pesach food in a chametz pot that hadn't been used for 24+ hours — according to Sephardim (Shulchan Aruch), the food is permitted bedi'avad. Summary of Key Halachot Siman:Se'if Key Takeaways 446:1 Find chametz on Chol HaMoed: take out and destroy immediately. On Yom Tov (1st/7th): cover it (muktze mahamat gufo). Even if no bitul was done, Shulchan Aruch says cover — don't violate muktze against a passive de'oraita. Best option: ask a non-Jew (shvut de-shvut le-tzorech mitzvah). 446:1 (2nd/8th day) If bitul was done: cover. If bitul was NOT done: take out and destroy (violate muktze de-rabbanan on de-rabbanan Yom Tov to avoid isur de'oraita). 446:3 Non-Jew's chametz in your domain: no isur de'oraita. Chol HaMoed: prod with stick. Shabbat/YT: cover over. Tiltul min ha-tzad (indirect prodding) is asur; tiltul be-shinui (elbow) is mutar. 446:3 (hefker) Ownerless chametz (e.g., fox brought bread): chatzer koneh applies — may technically be yours. You're an ones; just cover it. Preventive: declare before Pesach that chatzer should not acquire chametz. 446:4 Doubt if food is matzah or bread: may assume matzah (chazakah — came from most recent time). If moldy: throw out. Biur Halacha: better not to eat on Pesach — wait until after. 447:1 On Pesach, mixtures are forbidden be-mashehu (even one drop) — unlike 1 in 60 during the year. This is de-rabbanan. Applies to lach be-lach situations. Asur even be-hana'ah. Utensils don't need kashering. 447:1 (solids) Kedei netilah / kedei klipah still apply on Pesach for hot solids touching — be-mashehu only overrides where you'd normally need 1 in 60. 447:1 (Rabbeinu Tam) Minority opinion: standard 1 in 60 applies even on Pesach. Not followed, but available as background leniency in extreme circumstances. 447:2 Be-mashehu only applies to mixtures that occurred during Pesach itself . Mixtures from before Pesach: standard 1 in 60 applies. 447 (noten ta'am li-fgam) Degraded taste (24+ hours in utensil): Shulchan Aruch permits bedi'avad on Pesach. Rama is strict on Pesach itself but lenient from 6 hours on Erev Pesach.","aVOpCLqZsXw":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444:1 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; Erev Pesach on Shabbat (intro) Class 5b 444:2–5, 445 Erev Pesach on Shabbat (continued): bitul and bi'ur schedule; disposing of chametz on Shabbat; washing dishes; travelling; the laws of bi'ur chametz Siman 444 (Continued) – When Erev Pesach Falls on Shabbat Recap of Se'if 1 (from last class) Bedikat Chametz on Thursday night. Leave food for two Shabbat meals. Seudah Shlishit: cannot use chametz (too late) or matzah (Erev Pesach). Best option: matzah mevushelet (boiled) or matzah metugenet (fried). Alternatively: matzah ashirah (Sephardim), or meat/fish/fruit. Seudah Shlishit must be eaten before the 10th hour (two hours before end of day) — so you eat the matzah at the Seder with appetite (le-te'avon). Se'if 2 – Bitul and Bi'ur Schedule When Erev Pesach Falls on Shabbat The Key Distinction: Bitul vs. Bi'ur Term Meaning Type of Action Bitul Nullification — verbal/mental declaration giving up ownership of chametz Declaration (Kol Chamira) Bi'ur Disposal/destruction — physically getting rid of chametz Physical act (burning, flushing, crumbling, etc.) The Schedule Thursday Night: Bedikat Chametz (with bracha). Bitul — say Kol Chamira (night version: \"which I don't know about\" — because you still want the chametz you know about for Shabbat). Friday (any time — preferably before chatzot): Bi'ur — dispose of all chametz you won't need for Shabbat. No bitul on Friday — there's no purpose. You already did bitul Thursday night, and you still need to keep chametz for Shabbat. The Shulchan Aruch recommends doing bi'ur at the normal time (one hour before chatzot) — not because it's required, but so that in other years you maintain the habit of disposing at the correct time. If you forgot or were delayed — you can do bi'ur any time on Friday , even late afternoon. This is because the prohibition of owning chametz doesn't begin until Shabbat morning (end of the 5th hour). Shabbat Morning (after finishing your meal, before the deadline): Bi'ur — dispose of any remaining chametz (see methods below). Bitul — say Kol Chamira again (day version: \"which I know about and which I don't know about\" — now you're giving up everything). Summary Table When Bitul? Bi'ur? Thursday night (after Bedikah) ✅ Yes — Kol Chamira (night version) ❌ Not yet Friday (any time, preferably before chatzot) ❌ No purpose ✅ Yes — dispose of all chametz not needed for Shabbat Shabbat morning (after meal, before 5th hour deadline) ✅ Yes — Kol Chamira (day version) ✅ Yes — dispose of remaining chametz Se'if 3 – Don't Cook Sticky Foods Before This Shabbat Shulchan Aruch: Don't cook foods that stick to the pot (e.g., porridge, dough-based dishes) for this Shabbat. Why? Because you'll need to wash/scrub the pots on Shabbat to remove chametz residue — and washing dishes on Shabbat raises a concern of hachanah (preparation for after Shabbat). The Rama adds: If you did cook such foods and there is stuck chametz, you may wash on Shabbat — because in this case, it's not preparation; it's removing chametz before the prohibition kicks in. Modern Application: Washing Dishes on Shabbat This halacha depends entirely on your normal weekday practice: Your Normal Practice Washing Dishes on Shabbat? You always wash dishes immediately / keep a clean kitchen during the week ✅ Permitted on Shabbat — it's part of your normal oneg Shabbat (enjoyment), not hachanah You let dishes stack up during the week and wash every few days ❌ Not permitted on Shabbat — washing would be hachanah (saving time for the week) Same principle applies to loading a dishwasher on Shabbat: If you normally load it immediately during the week (you don't like dirty dishes on the counter), you may do so on Shabbat. If you normally wait and batch-load, you may not. Se'if 4 – What to Do with Remaining Chametz on Shabbat Morning After eating: Shake off crumbs onto the floor — that's sufficient. The Mishnah Berurah endorses this: Crumbs on the floor will be trampled upon during Pesach and are not a concern. You don't need to panic about every crumb. A word of musar from the Rav: People should not get anxious, shout at family members, or create anger over crumbs of chametz. This violates prohibitions of ona'at devarim (hurtful speech) and causes unnecessary stress — all over crumbs that the halacha itself says are not a problem. Be calm. Shake crumbs onto the floor; they'll be trampled. Remaining chametz dishes: Put them away where you store your chametz dishes for Pesach. If You Have Leftover Bread — Options for Bi'ur on Shabbat Since you can't burn, carry to the sea, or crumble into the wind on Shabbat, here are the options in order of priority: Option 1: Give to a non-Jew You can give a non-Jew small amounts that he could eat now (one meal's worth). You cannot give large amounts that he'd need to carry home — that's implicitly instructing him to carry in reshut ha-rabim (a Torah prohibition), which you cannot ask a non-Jew to do even for a mitzvah. If he decides on his own to take small amounts home — that's fine. If really stuck: Many poskim hold our regular streets are not reshut ha-rabim de'oraita (only a karmelit / de-rabbanan). In that case, you could ask the non-Jew to carry even more — it's a rabbinic prohibition being done for a mitzvah purpose (shvut de-shvut le-tzorech mitzvah). Option 2: Gift the entire chametz to a non-Jew (if too much to carry) If you have a large amount (e.g., a whole garage of chametz you forgot about), give it all to the non-Jew as a complete gift with a proper kinyan. Kinyan options: he lifts it up; you hand over the key to the room. Giving gifts on Shabbat is normally not permitted — but here it's for a mitzvah, so it's allowed. The gift must be genuine — he can keep it if he wants. You can't make it conditional. Selling on Shabbat: Some poskim permitted selling (since it's to avoid a prohibition, not \"business\"). However, we don't follow this — the main halacha is you must give it as a gift, not sell. Option 2b: Add to your mechirat chametz cupboard If you've done mechirat chametz and designated a specific cupboard/room — you can theoretically add remaining chametz to it on Shabbat morning. Why? The sale was executed on Friday, and can take effect (chal) on Shabbat. There's nothing wrong with a pre-arranged sale taking effect on Shabbat. Option 3: No non-Jew available Do bitul (which you can do on Shabbat — it's a verbal declaration). Cover the chametz with a utensil (kli). Dispose of it after the Chag. Option 4: Modern methods Flush down the toilet — the Mishnah Berurah endorses this. Pour strong chemicals/detergent on it to make it completely inedible (even for a dog). Then place it in your bin — it's no longer halachically chametz. Option 5: If you have an eruv Take the chametz outside, crush it into small pieces, and leave it in a public area (not your property). The wind/elements will disperse it — this constitutes bi'ur. You cannot just leave a whole challah on the street — that's not an act of destruction. You must crush/crumble it first. Se'if 4 (Continued) – Do Bitul Even If No Chametz Remains Shulchan Aruch: Even if you consumed all your chametz perfectly — ate everything Friday night and Shabbat morning with nothing remaining — you still say Kol Chamira at the correct time on Shabbat morning. Why? Just in case there is still chametz from Thursday night until now that you forgot about or didn't notice in your house. The bitul covers any unknown chametz. Se'if 5 – Travelling for a Mitzvah and Remembering Chametz at Home Scenario: You're on your way to a brit milah or engagement party on Erev Pesach, and you suddenly remember you left chametz at home. Situation What To Do Enough time to go back, dispose, and still do the mitzvah Go back and do bi'ur, then go to the mitzvah Not enough time — doing bi'ur means missing the mitzvah Just do bitul (in your heart/verbally) and continue to the mitzvah Critical caveat: Bitul only works when the chametz still belongs to you — i.e., before the end of the 5th hour (one hour before chatzot, when ownership is rabbinically revoked). From chatzot onwards, the chametz certainly doesn't belong to you (de'oraita), and you cannot do bitul. Timing Can You Do Bitul? Before end of 5th hour (before rabbinical cutoff) ✅ Yes — bitul works; skip bi'ur for the mitzvah After chatzot (Torah cutoff) ❌ No — chametz no longer belongs to you; you must go back and do bi'ur Siman 445 – The Laws of Bi'ur Chametz (דין ביעור חמץ) Se'if 1 – Methods of Disposal: The Machloket Famous machloket in the Mishnah (Pesachim): Opinion Method of Bi'ur Chachamim (majority) Any form of destruction: burning, crumbling and scattering to the wind, throwing into the sea Rabbi Yehuda Must burn — compared to notar (leftover sacrifice that must be burnt) Shulchan Aruch: Follows the Chachamim — any form of destruction suffices. Three valid methods: Sorfo — burn it Crumble and scatter — break into small pieces and throw into the wind/air Throw into the sea — or a river, lake, etc. If the bread is stale/hard , break it into smaller pieces first before throwing into the sea — so the water can break it down properly. Rama (Ashkenazim): The minhag is to burn (lesorfo) — following Rabbi Yehuda's opinion. This is why Ashkenazim always try to burn their chametz. For Sephardim: Burning is not required. Flushing down the toilet, pouring chemicals to make it inedible, crushing and scattering — all valid. The Ben Ish Chai does recommend burning even for Sephardim as a nice minhag, but it's not obligatory. The Rama adds: It's a good custom to burn during the day (not just at night) — paralleling how leftover sacrifices (notar) were burnt during the day in Temple times. However, if there's concern that a mouse will take the chametz overnight, you may burn at night. COVID-era relevance: During COVID, Ashkenazi authorities in London ruled you could flush chametz down the toilet instead of going out to burn — confirming that even for Ashkenazim, the fundamental halacha allows other forms of disposal. Se'if 1 (Continued) – Leave Something for Bi'ur Mishnah Berurah and Acharonim: If you gave all your chametz to a non-Jew before the prohibition — that's fine, but leave at least a kezayit to perform the mitzvah of bi'ur chametz yourself. Why? Giving chametz to a non-Jew is not an \"act of destruction\" — it's a gift. The mitzvah of tashbitu requires an actual act of disposal/destruction. So always keep a small amount for personal bi'ur. What About Eating as Bi'ur? The Minchat Chinuch held that eating chametz is a form of bi'ur (you're destroying it by consuming it). He even suggested: from 30 days before Pesach, every time you eat chametz, have in mind you're fulfilling the mitzvah of bi'ur chametz. The Rav's view: This is a stretch — eating for breakfast isn't really \"disposing\" of chametz. The mitzvah of bi'ur implies an act of destruction, not consumption. Practical advice: Don't rely solely on eating. Always leave aside at least a kezayit of chametz for proper bi'ur. Se'if 2 – Coals from Burning Chametz When Was the Chametz Burnt? Status of the Coals Before the time of prohibition Coals are permitted — you can use them, benefit from them After the time of prohibition Coals are forbidden (asur be-hana'ah) — you cannot make a fire with them, since they originated from chametz that was already asur be-hana'ah Se'if (from Mishnah Berurah) – A Non-Jewish Maid Living in Your Home Scenario: A non-Jewish live-in maid eats from your food as part of the employment agreement. Can you give her chametz before Pesach, knowing she'll eat it during Pesach (reducing what you'd need to provide her)? Mishnah Berurah: This is permitted — as long as you don't explicitly say \"this is your food for Pesach\" (which would be benefiting from chametz on Pesach). If you simply give her food and she happens to eat it during Pesach, that's fine. She can still come to your fridge for Pesach food if she wants — you can't refuse her based on having given her chametz. Summary of Key Halachot Siman:Se'if Key Takeaways 444:2 (schedule) Thursday night: Bedikat Chametz + Bitul. Friday: Bi'ur (preferably before chatzot, but any time is fine). No bitul on Friday. Shabbat morning: Bi'ur + Bitul at the correct time. 444:3 (dishes) Don't cook sticky chametz foods for this Shabbat (washing concern). Modern application: if you normally keep a clean kitchen, you may wash dishes on Shabbat. 444:4 (disposal on Shabbat) Crumbs: shake onto floor. Remaining bread: give to non-Jew (small amounts); gift with kinyan (large amounts); flush toilet; pour chemicals; add to mechirat chametz cupboard; or cover with kli + bitul. 444:4 (bitul) Even if all chametz consumed — still say Kol Chamira on Shabbat morning as a precaution. 444:5 (travelling) Travelling for mitzvah + remembered chametz at home: if time, go back. If not, bitul suffices — but only before the 5th hour cutoff. After chatzot: bitul doesn't work, must do bi'ur. 445:1 (methods) Bi'ur chametz: burn, crumble + scatter, or throw in sea. Ashkenazim: minhag to burn. Sephardim: any method valid. Always leave a kezayit for personal bi'ur. 445:1 (eating) Minchat Chinuch: eating = bi'ur. Most poskim disagree. Don't rely solely on eating. 445:2 (coals) Coals from chametz burnt before prohibition: permitted. After prohibition: forbidden (asur be-hana'ah).","rnVFTnRoEmI":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapters: Perek 3 (טהרת המעשים — Purity of Action) & Perek 4 (רחמנות — Mercy/Compassion) Edition: Feldheim Chapter 3: טהרת המעשים — Purity of Action Recap: Chapters 1–2 Chapter 1: Two paths of Avodat Hashem — Derech Ha'am (the common path of fulfilling mitzvot) and Derech HaYachid/Chasid (the deeper path of those who seek closeness to Hashem). The purpose of this sefer is the path of the Chasid. Chapter 2: All middot are interrelated — working on one naturally affects others, and vice versa. The Two Extremes of Motivation Chapter 3 presents two polar extremes of why a person performs mitzvot, and then two intermediate levels between them. Level 4 (Lowest): Mitzvot Shelo Lishmah — For Other People The worst level: a person performs mitzvot only because of societal pressure . He doesn't want to be caught out, doesn't want to show face. If he were certain there would be no social consequences, he would abandon the mitzvot entirely and commit aveirot freely. Rabbi Avraham says this is so obvious he shouldn't even need to mention it. HaKadosh Baruch Hu looks into the heart — He doesn't want people just going through the motions for external appearances. Nevertheless: Even this level is better than doing nothing at all. As Chazal say: מתוך שלא לשמה בא לשמה — From doing mitzvot for the wrong motives, a person may eventually come to do them for the right motives. Level 1 (Highest): Tahara Gemura — Complete Purity / Serving from Love המדרגה טהורה גמורה — The completely pure level This person performs mitzvot solely because HaKadosh Baruch Hu commanded them , with no regard for what other people think. Two scenarios that don't affect this person: If people think he is righteous but deep down he feels he isn't — he is filled with fear and trepidation before Hashem, because he knows the truth regardless of external perception. If people think he is NOT righteous but deep down he knows he is doing everything for Hashem — he pays no attention to their judgment. ולכן יש מהם שאמרו יהי חלקי עם מי שחשדין אותו ואין בו One of the Amoraim said: \"Let my portion be with those who are suspected but have nothing [wrong] in them.\" Important Qualification: והייתם נקיים מה' ומישראל This does NOT mean a person should deliberately act in a way that arouses suspicion. The Yerushalmi Shekalim teaches — based on Moshe Rabbeinu giving a full accounting in Parshat Pekudei (because people questioned his wealth from the פסולת of the Luchot) — that just as a person must appear correct before Hashem, he must also appear correct before other people. The distinction: If you are acting correctly and people still suspect you — ignore them. If your behaviour is actually causing suspicion — you must address it. Dovid HaMelech's Confidence בחנני ה' ונסני צרפה כליותי ולבי — Examine me, Hashem, test me, refine my kidneys and my heart (Tehillim 26:2) חקרני אל ודע לבבי בחנני ודע שרעפי — Search me, God, and know my heart; test me and know my thoughts (Tehillim 139:23) Dovid was comfortable inviting Hashem to examine his innermost being because he felt his motives were completely pure. The Essence of This Level שעושה מעשיו רק כדי לקיים מצותו שהוא חובת הציווי A person does everything only to fulfill Hashem's command — because it is an obligation. As the pasuk says: וזכרת כי עבד היית במצרים ושמרת ועשית את החוקים האלה — \"Remember you were a servant in Egypt, and you shall keep these laws.\" Rabbi Avraham interprets: You were once a servant to Par'oh; now you are a servant of HaKadosh Baruch Hu — therefore you keep the mitzvot. Three things this person does NOT do mitzvot for: Not for reward — even though Hashem will certainly reward him. Not for fear of punishment — even though Hashem certainly punishes. Even in a hypothetical where Hashem would punish for keeping mitzvot — he would still keep them. Even if told there would be no punishment for violating — he would still not violate. This is called עובד מאהבה — serving from love. The Rambam's Parallel (Hilchot Teshuva, Chapter 10) אל יאמר אדם הריני עושה מצוות התורה כדי שאקבל כל הברכות... כדי שאזכה לחיי העולם הבא Don't do mitzvot for blessings or even for Olam Haba. Critical point from the Rambam: Even serving Hashem in order to receive Olam Haba is classified as עובד מיראה (serving from fear), not love. This is because wanting reward and fearing punishment are ultimately the same thing in Rambam's framework — punishment IS the lack of reward (loss of Olam Haba). העובד מאהבה... עושה האמת מפני שהוא אמת — Does the truth because it is the truth. ומעלה זו היא מעלה גדולה מאד ואין כל חכם זוכה לה — This is an exceedingly great level, and not every sage merits it. Level 2: Serving from Fear (עובד מיראה) A person who keeps mitzvot because of fear of Hashem's punishment or desire for Hashem's reward — but NOT because of any human element. No societal pressure involved — it's all directed toward God But it's still self-serving: if you were guaranteed no punishment, you wouldn't keep the mitzvot This includes: fear of losing Olam Haba AND desire to gain Olam Haba As Rabbeinu Bachya ibn Paquda writes in Chovot HaLevavot: as long as a person serves for something — whether fear or reward — ultimately he is serving himself. Level 3: Dual Motivation — For Hashem AND for People A person who performs mitzvot both for Hashem and for social recognition. He gets dual satisfaction — he fulfils the command AND enjoys the prestige. Example: Learning Torah both to understand what Hashem requires AND to be known as a talmid chacham. Praying Shacharit both because it's obligatory AND because he wants people to see him praying. לא תעשם עטרה להתגדל בהם ולא קרדום לחפור בהם — Don't make the Torah a crown to glorify yourself or a spade to dig with (Pirkei Avot) Summary: The Four Levels Level Motivation Category Level 1 (Highest) Only because Hashem commanded — no reward, no fear עובד מאהבה Level 2 Fear of Hashem's punishment / desire for Hashem's reward עובד מיראה Level 3 For Hashem AND for social recognition Mixed — shelo lishmah element Level 4 (Lowest) Only for societal pressure — would abandon if no consequences Shelo lishmah Practical Reflection Rabbi Avraham acknowledges this is a very high level — even great Chachamim struggle to reach Level 1. The purpose of studying this is to intellectually grasp the goal and aspire toward it. Questions to ask ourselves: Why do I keep Shabbat? Why do I put on tefillin? Why do I learn Torah? Is it because everyone else does it (Level 4)? For recognition (Level 3)? For reward/fear (Level 2)? Or because Hashem commanded me and this is the best way to live (Level 1)? Different mitzvot may come from different levels within us — and the goal is to gradually move upward. Chapter 4: רחמנות — Mercy / Compassion The Meaning of Rachamanut The word רחמנות comes from the three-letter root רחם (rechem) — a womb. Two explanations: The maternal bond: The greatest example of mercy and compassion in humanity is the love of a mother for her child. As Yeshayahu says (49:15): התשכח אשה עולה מרחם בן בטנה — \"Can a nursing woman forget, from having mercy on her child?\" The pasuk uses מרחם — the same root — connecting womb to mercy. Seeing people as developing: A rechem/womb represents growth and development. We naturally have patience and compassion for people who are growing (e.g., a baby crying on a plane). Rachamanut means seeing others as developing, still in their \"womb\" of growth — and therefore extending compassion. Rachamanut as a Foundation of Torah הרחמנות היא מן המידות הטרומיות ומטרות התורה — Compassion is one of the lofty character traits and one of the purposes of the Torah. Rabbi Avraham identifies this as the first specific middah he discusses (likely because it is the first of the 13 Attributes of Mercy: אל רחום וחנון). והיא התכונה הראשונה מבין התכונות המיוחדות לישראל — It is the first of the special character traits of Israel. Torah Mitzvot That Train Compassion The Torah gave us many mitzvot specifically to soften indifference and develop mercy: Tzedakah — supporting the poor and needy Returning collateral (השבת עבוט) — if you take a poor person's garment as collateral, you must return it at night so he can sleep. The Torah even gives the reason: כי היא כסותו לבדה היא שמלתו לעורו במה ישכב — \"It is his only covering, his garment for his skin — what shall he sleep in?\" לא תאמץ את לבבך — \"Do not harden your heart\" (in the context of giving charity) The Prohibition of Achzariyut (Cruelty/Indifference) The word אכזריות comes from the root זר (zar) — a stranger/foreigner. Cruelty at its root is indifference — treating someone as a stranger, as if their suffering has nothing to do with you. Key insight: You don't have to be actively cruel to be guilty of achzariyut. Simply not caring about someone's plight is achzariyut. Tzedakah Requires Empathy — Not Just Money (Rambam, Hilchot Matanot Aniyim) The Rambam rules: כל הנותן צדקה לעני בסבר פנים רעות ופניו כבושות בקרקע אפילו נתן לו אלף זהובים איבד זכותו והפסידה If you give charity with an unfriendly face, looking down — even if you give a thousand gold coins — you have lost your merit. אלא נותנו בסבר פנים יפות ובשמחה ומתאונן עמו על צרתו Rather, give with a pleasant face, with joy, and empathize with his suffering . Source: Iyov 30:25 — אם לא בכיתי לקשה יום עגמה נפשי לאביון — \"Did I not weep for the one whose day was hard? Was my soul not grieved for the poor?\" Sympathy vs. Empathy: Sympathy: Understanding someone's suffering from the outside — \"I can see why you're sad.\" Empathy: Actually feeling their pain from within — sharing in their suffering. The Rambam's halacha requires empathy , not just sympathy. Part of the mitzvah of tzedakah is attempting to feel the other person's pain. When Mercy Is Wrong — Misplaced Compassion Rabbi Avraham warns: rachamanut does not mean being soft-hearted and impressionable in every situation. True mercy requires wisdom — knowing when to be compassionate and when to be firm. The Torah commands firmness in specific cases: A murderer — מעם מזבחי תקחנו למות — even from the altar, take him to die. Even if he seeks refuge with Hashem Himself, do not show mercy. (Rambam in Moreh Nevuchim explains: the mizbeach represents seeking refuge with the highest authority — even that doesn't help.) A meisit (one who entices to idolatry) — לא תחמול ולא תכסה עליו — do not have pity, even if it is your own son. A ben sorer u'moreh (rebellious son) — parents must bring him to Beit Din. Examples from the Prophets: Mercy and Firmness Together Moshe Rabbeinu — within two or three pesukim: Kills the Egyptian who was beating a Jew (firmness) Saves the daughters of Yitro and waters their flock (compassion) The same person. Because mercy must be applied correctly. Dovid HaMelech: Compassion for the poor man's lamb (the parable of Natan HaNavi) Harshness toward the Ammonim (killing with saws and iron implements) Shmuel HaNavi: Killed Agag (וישסף שמואל את אגג לפני ה') HaKadosh Baruch Hu Himself Even Hashem, who is רחום וחנון ארך אפים ורב חסד — also: פוקד עון אבות על בנים — remembers sins כי ה' אלוהיך אש אוכלה הוא אל קנא — Hashem is a consuming fire, a jealous God Moshe, who heard the 13 Attributes of Mercy, still declared Hashem is \"a consuming fire\" Mercy and justice coexist — and knowing when to apply each is the mark of true wisdom. The Gemara's Principle כל מי שיש בו מידת הרחמנות הקדוש ברוך הוא מרחם עליו — Whoever has the trait of mercy, HaKadosh Baruch Hu has mercy on him. Source: ונתן לך רחמים ורחמך והרבך — \"He will give you mercy and have mercy on you and multiply you.\" But also: אין מרחמים אלא על מי שיש בו דעת — Only have mercy on one who has understanding. This will be elaborated on in the next shiur — the idea that mercy must be applied with discernment, not indiscriminately. Key Takeaways Purity of motive has four levels — from doing mitzvot only for people (lowest) to doing them solely because Hashem commanded (highest). Even serving Hashem for Olam Haba is classified as serving from fear, not love (Rambam and Rabbi Avraham agree on this). The goal is to intellectually understand Level 1 and aspire toward it, even if we can't fully reach it. Rachamanut (mercy) is the first specific middah discussed — rooted in the maternal bond (rechem) and the recognition that people are developing. True tzedakah requires empathy, not just money — feel the person's pain. Achzariyut (cruelty) at its root is indifference — treating others as strangers. Mercy must be applied wisely — there are times the Torah demands firmness, even harshness. Misplaced compassion is itself a form of wrong. When in doubt — be merciful. (To be elaborated next time.) Preview: Next Shiur Completing the discussion of rachmanut — mercy when in doubt The meaning of \"only have mercy on one who has understanding\" Chapter 5: נדיבות — Generosity","hwEtx1XVNnk":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–432 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol; Bedikat Chametz timing; the bracha Class 2 432–433 Why \"al biur\" not \"al bedikat\"; candle vs torch; where to check; high/low places; hole in walls; synagogues Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — bitul night and day; chametz timeline; appointing agents; missed bedikah; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kezayit threshold; kavua principle; non-Jew depositing chametz Class 5 443–444 Chametz on Erev Pesach after six hours; calculating the times; smelling chametz; deposited chametz; bartering; Erev Pesach on Shabbat Note: Siman 442 (ta'arovet chametz / mixtures) was skipped for now and will be revisited later. Siman 443 – Chametz on Erev Pesach After Six Hours (דין חמץ בערב פסח לאחר שש שעות) Contains 3 Se'ifim Background: Three Torah Obligations Regarding Chametz Before learning the se'ifim, it is essential to understand the three distinct Torah obligations regarding chametz: # Obligation When It Applies Source Consequence 1 Eating chametz on Pesach itself From nightfall of 15 Nisan בל תאכל חמץ Karet (spiritual excision) 2 Eating and benefiting from chametz from chatzot on Erev Pesach From halachic midday of 14 Nisan לא תאכל עליו חמץ Malkut (lashes) — a negative prohibition (lav) 3 Positive mitzvah to destroy chametz (tashbitu) Before chatzot on 14 Nisan תשביתו שאור מבתיכם Failure to fulfil a positive commandment Eating and Benefit from Chatzot (De'oraita) The Torah says that from the time the Korban Pesach could be sacrificed (chatzot on Erev Pesach), one may not eat chametz — \"לא תאכל עליו חמץ.\" The majority of Rishonim, and the Shulchan Aruch, hold that this prohibition applies even nowadays when there is no Korban Pesach. The Gemara explains that when the Torah says \"Lo Tochal\" (do not eat), this generally includes a prohibition of hana'ah (benefit) as well. Therefore, from chatzot and onwards on Erev Pesach, it is a Torah prohibition to both eat and benefit from chametz, carrying the punishment of malkut (lashes). There are some Rishonim who hold the prohibition is only de-rabbanan, but either way it is forbidden. The Shulchan Aruch follows the majority view that it is de'oraita. Owning Chametz After Chatzot on Erev Pesach Owning chametz after chatzot on Erev Pesach does not violate a negative prohibition. The Torah says \"seven days you shall not have chametz in your possessions\" — the prohibition of ownership applies only during the seven days of Pesach itself . However, if one still owns chametz after chatzot, one loses the opportunity to fulfil the positive mitzvah of tashbitu (destroying chametz at the correct time). Summary: After chatzot on Erev Pesach — eating and benefit = Torah prohibition (lav); owning = missing a positive mitzvah but not violating a lav. Se'if 1 – The Timeline of Prohibitions on Erev Pesach Shulchan Aruch: Chametz from six hours (chatzot) onwards is forbidden to benefit from. The Chachamim forbade eating from two hours before chatzot (the beginning of the 5th hour). Although eating is forbidden during the 5th hour, one may still sell it, benefit from it, give it to animals, sell to a non-Jew, or give it to a non-Jew. When giving to animals, one must ensure they finish everything and nothing remains. From the beginning of the 6th hour (one hour before chatzot), they forbade benefit from it as well. The Complete Timeline Time Period Eating Benefit (selling, feeding animals, etc.) Owning Source Before the 5th hour (until 4 halachic hours into the day) ✅ Permitted ✅ Permitted ✅ Permitted — 5th hour (2 hours before chatzot) ❌ Forbidden (rabbinic) ✅ Permitted — can sell, feed animals, give to non-Jew ✅ Permitted Chachamim's gezeirah for eating 6th hour (1 hour before chatzot) ❌ Forbidden ❌ Forbidden (rabbinic) — cannot sell, feed animals, etc. ✅ Still permitted (but should destroy — tashbitu) Chachamim's gezeirah for benefit From chatzot (halachic midday) ❌ Forbidden (Torah — lav/malkut) ❌ Forbidden (Torah — lav/malkut) No lav, but failure to fulfil tashbitu De'oraita: לא תאכל עליו חמץ From nightfall of 15 Nisan (Pesach itself) ❌ Forbidden (Torah — karet ) ❌ Forbidden (Torah) ❌ Forbidden (Torah — bal yera'eh / bal yimatzei) Most severe level Why Did the Chachamim Add Extra Hours? In the days without clocks, people relied on the sun to tell the time. On a cloudy day, a person could easily miscalculate when chatzot is. Therefore: For eating: The Chachamim added two hours before chatzot — more severe caution, since eating could lead to karet if it continued into Pesach. For benefit: The Chachamim added one hour before chatzot — less severe, since benefit alone doesn't carry karet. Selling Chametz After the Deadline If you did sell chametz after the prohibited time (which you should not do — it's asur): The transaction is valid (chal). The chametz would not have the status of chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach — you could use it after Pesach. But this should not be done deliberately. Is a Gift Considered Hana'ah (Benefit)? Yes — giving chametz as a gift is a form of benefit, because you build a relationship and there may be a mutual benefit later on. This is clear from the halachot of basar be-chalav (meat and milk): meat cooked with milk is asur be-hana'ah, and that includes even giving it as a gift. However: Leaving chametz as hefker (ownerless, on the street for anyone to take) — this is not considered benefit and is permitted. There is no relationship being built when you simply abandon something. Practical example: With stam yeinam (non-kosher wine) — Sephardim hold it is asur be-hana'ah, so you can't sell it. Rather than pour it down the drain, simply leave it out on the street and let someone else take it. The Rema: Calculating the Hours — Sha'ot Zemaniyot vs. Fixed Hours General rule: We use sha'ot zemaniyot (seasonal/halachic hours) — dividing the daylight period into 12 equal parts. The Terumat HaDeshen's Opinion The Terumat HaDeshen (a major Ashkenazi posek, ~600 years ago) held a novel position based on the Gemara. The Gemara asks: why did the Chachamim add specifically two hours? It answers: because that's the maximum a person can get confused. The Terumat HaDeshen infers from this that \"two hours\" means two regular 60-minute hours — fixed, regardless of season. The same applies to the one hour for benefit. Method Calculation When It's a Chumra When It's a Kula Sha'ot zemaniyot (main halacha) Divide daylight into 12; count back from chatzot Before the equinox (shorter days = shorter hours = earlier deadline) After the equinox (longer days = longer hours = later deadline) Terumat HaDeshen (fixed hours) Simply subtract 120 minutes from chatzot for eating; 60 minutes for benefit After the equinox (21 March) — comes out earlier than seasonal hours Before the equinox — comes out later than seasonal hours Practical Example (London, April) Chatzot is approximately 12:15 PM. Terumat HaDeshen: Latest time to eat = 10:15 AM. Latest time to sell = 11:15 AM. Sha'ot zemaniyot: Latest time to eat ≈ 10:35–10:40 AM (because seasonal hours are slightly longer than 60 minutes after the equinox). This year, Pesach falls after the equinox (April), so the Terumat HaDeshen is a chumra — his times come out earlier. Halacha Everyone agrees the main halacha is sha'ot zemaniyot. However, the Terumat HaDeshen can be relied upon bedi'avad and bishat hadchak (in pressing circumstances) when it results in a leniency — e.g., if you're running late selling chametz, this may give you an extra 10–20 minutes. Furthermore, the further north you go, the greater the difference between the two calculations, even in April. Note on Mechirat Chametz: You can actually do mechirat chametz yourself if you know how — you can sell chametz to your next-door non-Jewish neighbour directly if you know the proper procedure. From When Do You Calculate the Hours? Opinion Calculation Result Dawn (alot hashachar) to nightfall (tzet hakochavim) — stringent Longer day = each seasonal hour slightly shorter = earlier deadline This is what we follow in the first instance Sunrise (netz) to sunset (shkiyah) — lenient Shorter day = each seasonal hour slightly longer = later deadline United Synagogue calendar uses this; can be relied upon bedi'avad The difference can be 20–30 minutes — significant for families with children who need to eat. In the first instance, calculate from dawn to nightfall (the more stringent opinion). Bedi'avad, one can rely on sunrise to sunset. Note: Since this relates to a safek de'oraita (the Torah prohibition begins at chatzot, and the rabbinic buffer protects against violating it), we are stringent in the first instance. The Federation calendar uses the more stringent opinion, which is why you may find discrepancies between different community calendars. Smelling Chametz on Pesach Question: Can you intentionally walk past a bakery on Pesach and smell the bread? Background: The prohibition of benefit from chametz applies to all chametz — not just your own. The Torah says chametz shouldn't be eaten, not \"your chametz.\" There is an opinion in the Rema (Siman 216) that one says a bracha on the smell of fresh bread — implying smell is something substantial. Biur Halacha adds: unlike treif food (which you'd never eat, so smelling is fine), chametz is food that is normally kosher during the year — you don't have the same repulsion for it, and smelling it may tempt you to eat it. Halacha (consensus of poskim, including Sephardi poskim): Scenario Permitted? Intentionally walking past a bakery to smell the bread ❌ Forbidden — potentially asur de'oraita Intentionally breathing in deeply while passing ❌ Forbidden Walking past normally and a waft reaches you unintentionally ✅ Permitted — no need to block your nose Taking a deliberate detour to pass the bakery for the smell ❌ Forbidden Distinction from non-kosher food: There is no prohibition to smell treif food generally (e.g., walking past McDonald's is fine). But you cannot smell something that is asur be-hana'ah , like basar be-chalav (meat cooked with milk) — or chametz during Pesach. Se'if 2 – A Jew's Chametz Deposited with You Shulchan Aruch: If a Jewish person left chametz in your care and didn't return: If you can communicate: Contact them and ask what to do. If you cannot communicate: Wait until the 5th hour (one hour left to sell). If no one has come, you may sell it — even though you weren't given permission to sell. Why? This is meshiv aveidah (returning lost property) — in one hour, this chametz will be permanently worthless (asur be-hana'ah forever). By selling it, you are saving the depositor's property. If selling isn't possible or time is too tight, you can sell even earlier. If you could not sell it: You must destroy it by the end of the 5th hour, even though it's not yours. Why destroy someone else's property? Two reasons: (a) The mitzvah of tashbitu from the Torah applies. (b) If the depositor didn't do bitul, he will be violating a Torah prohibition of owning chametz on Pesach. You have a duty to save him from transgression. Liability for Not Selling If you had the opportunity to sell the chametz and were lazy or negligent, and then had to destroy it — some poskim hold you bear financial responsibility for the loss (as part of your duty of care as a custodian). This is a minority opinion and considered a weak argument, but an interesting side discussion in Choshen Mishpat. Se'if 3 – Bartering Chametz After the Prohibited Time Shulchan Aruch: If someone used chametz to barter/buy something after the prohibited time (which is asur — a violation): The item received in exchange is permitted — the prohibition does not transfer to the bartered item. Some authorities say: for the person who did it, it's forbidden as a penalty (kenas). But the main halacha is that the item itself is permitted. Siman 444 – When Erev Pesach Falls on Shabbat (ערב פסח שחל בשבת) Partially covered — continuation next week Se'if 1 – Bedikat Chametz on Thursday Night Shulchan Aruch: When the 14th of Nisan falls on Shabbat: Bedikat Chametz is done on Thursday night (the night of the 13th) — the Chachamim instituted it on this night. All the regular halachot of Bedikat Chametz apply — bracha, candle, searching properly, etc. All chametz must be destroyed before Shabbat (i.e., on Friday). The Shulchan Aruch says \"before Shabbat\" — even late Friday afternoon is fine, since the prohibition of owning chametz only begins on Shabbat after the 5th hour. You leave aside chametz for two meals only — Friday night and Shabbat morning. What If You Missed Thursday Night? As we learned previously, if you didn't do Bedikat Chametz on the night of the 13th, you would do it during the day on the 13th (i.e., Erev Shabbat / Friday). If you missed that too, you could theoretically do Bedikat Chametz on Shabbat itself. Why Only Two Meals? Friday night: Mitzvah of Seudat Shabbat — use chametz (or see alternatives below). Shabbat morning: Mitzvah of Seudat Shabbat — use chametz (or alternatives). Seudah Shlishit: Cannot use chametz — it's already after the prohibited time on Shabbat afternoon. Cannot use regular matzah — prohibited on Erev Pesach. Must use matzah ashirah (Sephardim) or alternatives (Ashkenazim). The Prohibition of Eating Matzah on Erev Pesach Gemara: כל האוכל מצה בערב פסח כבועל ארוסתו בבית חמיו — \"One who eats matzah on Erev Pesach is like one who has relations with his fiancée in his father-in-law's house\" (i.e., before the wedding). This is an issur de-rabbanan — you must come to the Seder with appetite and excitement for the matzah. Important: The halakha is that there is no prohibition from a month before, or from Rosh Chodesh Nisan — those are customs only. The actual prohibition is specifically on Erev Pesach. The specific expression used — fiancée, not just any prohibition — has halachic ramifications (see matzah mevushelet section below). Scope of the Prohibition: When Does \"Erev Pesach\" Begin? Opinion Scope Who Follows Most poskim The entire day of Erev Pesach (from the morning of 14 Nisan) Majority Magen Avraham Only during the daytime — the night before (Friday night) is not yet \"Erev Pesach\" Chabad The Magen Avraham's novel leniency means that one could eat regular matzah on Friday night, and then only switch to chametz or alternatives for Shabbat morning. What Is Matzah Ashirah (מצה עשירה)? Definition: Matzah baked with fruit juice instead of water. Property Detail Ingredients Flour + 100% pure fruit juice (no water at all) Chametz status Not chametz — flour only becomes chametz when mixed with water Matzah status Not valid matzah for the Seder — matzah must be made from something that could become chametz Rising Permitted to rise beyond 18 minutes (since fruit juice doesn't cause chametz) Why called \"rich\" It's bigger/broader because of the extra rising time — just as rice can rise because it's not chametz, so flour with pure fruit juice can rise Important: Matzah ashirah packages clearly state it cannot be used for Seder night, as one does not fulfil the mitzvah of eating matzah with it. The Ashkenazi Problem Rama: The Ashkenazi custom is not to eat matzah ashirah on Pesach at all. Concern: The fruit juice may contain some water — especially modern concentrates from supermarkets, which often contain added water. If any water is present, the mixture would become full chametz. Consequence for Seudah Shlishit: Ashkenazim cannot use chametz (too late), matzah (Erev Pesach prohibition), or matzah ashirah. Their options are limited to: meat, fish, fruit, or cakes made from matzah meal. They can rely on the four opinions for fulfilling Seudah Shlishit that we learned previously (Siman 289): bread, mezonot, meat and fish, or even fruit. The Bracha Problem with Matzah Ashirah Matzah ashirah has the status of pat haba'ah be-kisnin (similar to cake/pastry) — normally mezonot, not hamotzi. Can you fulfil Seudat Shabbat (which requires lechem/bread) with something that's mezonot? Answer: Some poskim argue that if you eat a significant amount (shiur kvi'at seudah), it becomes hamotzi. Others argue that since there's nothing else available, matzah ashirah is the \"bread of the hour\" — similar to how cracker matzah becomes hamotzi on Pesach itself because it's the only bread available. But this is debatable. Note on pat haba'ah be-kisnin: One interpretation is that it refers to something that is \"koses\" — crunchy, requiring chewing and crushing. This is why cracker matzah (which is crunchy) is mezonot during the year according to many opinions, while soft hand-made matzah (like the kind made by Dan David in Hendon, or widely available in Israel) is hamotzi year-round because it has the texture of real bread. The Best Practical Solution: Matzah Mevushelet (Boiled/Fried Matzah) Chacham Ovadia Yosef strongly advocated this approach. The Aruch HaShulchan (Baruch Epstein) writes this for Ashkenazim as well. The Principle The Gemara's prohibition of matzah on Erev Pesach applies to matzah you would use to fulfil the mitzvah at the Seder . The specific expression — \"like one who has relations with his fiancée\" — implies: you can't wait a few hours for something you'll be having tonight. If you can't fulfil the mitzvah with that matzah in a few hours' time, then it's not part of the prohibition. Halacha: If matzah is baked and then boiled or fried , bishul (cooking) overrides afiyah (baking) — it loses its status as \"baked matzah\" and cannot fulfil the mitzvah of eating matzah at the Seder. Therefore, it is not prohibited on Erev Pesach. Source of the principle: This is the same halacha that proves bishul achar afiyah (cooking after baking) changes the status of food — which is relevant in Hilchot Shabbat regarding the question of ein bishul achar bishul. If you bake matzah and then boil it in soup, it is no longer \"baked matzah\" — proof that bishul overrides afiyah. This means that on Pesach night itself, if you put baked matzah into soup (during Shulchan Orech, later in the Seder), you have not fulfilled the mitzvah of matzah with the soup-soaked piece — everyone agrees on this. How to Prepare Before Shabbat (Friday): Take regular matzah. Boil it in soup/water or fry it (e.g., dip in egg and fry) — ensure it is properly cooked/fried so it has the halachic status of being cooked. Keep it intact — if it crumbles into small pieces, there are issues with lechem mishneh and its bread status. Use this for all three Shabbat meals — Friday night, Shabbat morning, and Seudah Shlishit. Advantages Advantage Detail Works for all three meals No need for chametz at all — eliminates the entire problem of destroying chametz on Shabbat Use Pesach dishes from Friday night Everything is already Pesach-ready; no last-minute switching No chametz destruction issues on Shabbat No flushing bread down toilets; no carrying without an eruv Both Sephardim and Ashkenazim can rely on this Aruch HaShulchan writes this for Ashkenazim; Chacham Ovadia for Sephardim No bracha issues It's still bread-like enough for hamotzi if kept intact Note on Ashkenazi gebrochts: Sephardim do not hold of gebrochts (concern about matzah + water becoming chametz). The matzah mevushelet approach does not pose a gebrochts concern since the matzah is being prepared before Pesach — there's no issue of gebrochts before Pesach begins. The Problems with Using Real Chametz on Shabbat If you do use chametz for Friday night and Shabbat morning, you face significant practical difficulties: Destroying chametz on Shabbat: How do you get rid of the remaining crumbs and bread? You can't burn it, and you can't carry it outside without an eruv. Flushing down the toilet: One of the few available options, but uncomfortable — especially with large families and lots of children where you can't precisely measure how much bread is needed. Switching dishes: You'd have to use non-Pesach dishes for chametz meals and then switch to Pesach dishes mid-Shabbat. This is why the matzah mevushelet approach is recommended — it avoids all of these complications. Seudah Shlishit Timing Seudah Shlishit on Erev Pesach must be eaten before the 10th hour of the day (i.e., two halachic hours before the end of the day). Reason: You need to come to the Seder hungry and with appetite for the matzah. The Kabbalists are particular about praying Mincha before Seudah Shlishit. On this Shabbat, that is nearly impossible — Shacharit and Musaf take up the morning, and one needs to eat and dispose of chametz by ~10:30 AM. The poskim say it is fine to have Seudah Shlishit before Mincha in this case. Early Shacharit This Shabbat The standard Shabbat morning service in many communities finishes at 11:30 or 12:00 — far too late this year. One needs to finish eating chametz and destroy any remaining chametz by the end of the 5th hour (approximately 10:30 AM in London). Therefore, communities must schedule early Shacharit and Musaf so that people can get home, have their Shabbat morning meal, and destroy remaining chametz in time. Even if using matzah mevushelet (no chametz at all), early services are still advisable for the community's sake, since not everyone will have prepared this way. Practical Schedule: Erev Pesach on Shabbat (This Year — 5785 / 2025) Time Activity Thursday night Bedikat Chametz (with bracha, candle — all regular halachot) Friday morning/afternoon Destroy/burn remaining chametz (that you won't need for Shabbat) Friday Prepare matzah mevushelet (boiled/fried matzah) for all Shabbat meals. Set out Pesach dishes. Friday night Seudat Shabbat with matzah mevushelet (or chametz, if preferred) + Pesach food Shabbat morning Early Shacharit + Musaf — must finish in time to eat and dispose of chametz before the deadline (~10:30 AM). If using matzah mevushelet: no chametz issues. By end of 5th hour (~10:30 AM) All chametz must be eaten or destroyed. Do Bitul Chametz (daytime formula). Shabbat afternoon (before 10th hour) Seudah Shlishit — matzah mevushelet, matzah ashirah (Sephardim), or meat/fish/fruit Shabbat afternoon Mincha Motzei Shabbat Pesach begins — Seder night Summary of Key Halachot Siman:Se'if Key Takeaways 443:1 Three Torah obligations: eating on Pesach (karet), eating/benefit after chatzot on Erev Pesach (malkut), and tashbitu (positive mitzvah to destroy). Chachamim added 2 hours for eating, 1 hour for benefit. Owning chametz after chatzot on Erev Pesach is not a lav — only during the 7 days. 443:1 (times) Use sha'ot zemaniyot calculated from dawn to nightfall (stringent). Terumat HaDeshen (fixed 60-minute hours) can be relied upon bedi'avad. United Synagogue calendar uses the more lenient netz-to-shkiyah; Federation calendar uses the stringent opinion. Difference can be 20–30 minutes. 443:1 (smell) Intentionally smelling chametz on Pesach is forbidden — potentially de'oraita. Unintentional wafts are fine. Distinguished from smelling treif food (which is permitted). 443:1 (sale after deadline) Sale after deadline is asur but valid (chal). Chametz would not be classified as chametz she'avar alav ha-Pesach. Giving a gift = hana'ah. Leaving as hefker ≠ hana'ah. 443:2 Jewish person's chametz deposited with you: contact them if possible. If not, sell it in the 5th hour (meshiv aveidah). If you can't sell, destroy it — to save the depositor from violating a Torah prohibition. 443:3 Bartering chametz after the prohibited time: the item received is permitted (prohibition doesn't transfer). Some say a kenas applies to the person who did it. 444:1 Erev Pesach on Shabbat: Bedikah on Thursday night. Destroy chametz on Friday. Leave food for two meals only. Seudah Shlishit cannot be chametz or matzah. 444 (matzah on E\"P) Matzah is forbidden on Erev Pesach (de-rabbanan). Magen Avraham: only during the day, not the preceding night. Most poskim: entire day. 444 (matzah ashirah) Flour + 100% fruit juice. Not chametz, not matzah. Ashkenazim don't eat it on Pesach (concern about water content). Bracha issues (pat haba'ah be-kisnin / mezonot). 444 (matzah mevushelet) Best solution: boiled or fried matzah. Bishul overrides afiyah — loses matzah status, so not prohibited on Erev Pesach. Can be used for all three Shabbat meals. Recommended for both Sephardim and Ashkenazim. 444 (seudah shlishit) Must eat before the 10th hour. OK to have before Mincha on this Shabbat. Early Shacharit required this year.","gPcYcyPxaX0":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–430 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol Class 2 431–433 Time for Bedikat Chametz; the bracha; where to check; candle; various scenarios Class 3 434–437 After Bedikah — Bitul night and day; missed Bedikah; travelling before Pesach; renting a house Class 4 438–440 Mouse/child with chametz; kavua principle; kezayit threshold; non-Jew depositing chametz; living with a non-Jew; non-Jewish worker on Pesach Siman 438 – A Mouse or Child Entering a Checked Room with Chametz (עכבר שנכנס למקום בדוק וככר בפיו) Contains 2 Se'ifim Se'if 1 – Mouse vs. Child Entering with Bread Case 1: A Mouse Shulchan Aruch: A house has been checked. A mouse is seen running in with a piece of bread in its mouth. You follow and find crumbs — even crumbs that together equal the full amount of the original piece. Ruling: You cannot assume the crumbs are from that bread. You must search the entire house again . Reason (Gemara): It is not the nature of a mouse to create crumbs — mice eat, they don't scatter. Therefore, the crumbs you found must have been pre-existing (missed during the original Bedikah), and the mouse's bread is still somewhere in the house. Case 2: A Child Shulchan Aruch: Same scenario, but a child entered with bread instead of a mouse. Ruling: If you find crumbs — you do not need to re-search. Reason: It is the nature of a child to create crumbs while eating. Therefore, we can presume the crumbs are from the bread the child brought in, and the child ate the rest. What If There Are No Crumbs (Child)? You must search that room again . There is a vadai (certainty) that chametz entered the room. You cannot say \"maybe the child ate it\" — אין ספק מוציא מידי ודאי — a doubt cannot override a certainty. Mishnah Berurah: Only the room the child entered needs re-checking (not the whole house) — since bedikat chametz is de-rabbanan and bitul has been done, we can be lenient. What If the Crumbs Don't Equal the Full Piece? One opinion (brought by Shulchan Aruch): If the crumbs don't add up to the original piece, you must re-check. Main halacha (which we follow): Even if the crumbs are only a partial amount, we assume the child ate some and crumbed the rest. No need to re-check. Age of the \"Child\" This entire discussion refers to a child without da'at — too young to communicate meaningfully (roughly ages 1–3). Once a child can respond and explain (\"I ate it\" / \"I threw it in the bin\"), the child is believed , however young they are. If the child can communicate — just ask them. Problem solved. Se'if 2 – A Kezayit of Chametz in an Unreachable Place Shulchan Aruch: A kezayit of chametz is found on top of a high beam (e.g., thrown up during a simcha). Even though it's hard to reach, you are obligated to get a ladder and retrieve it . Reason: During Pesach, it may fall, and you might come to eat it or benefit from it. Chametz in a pit: You do not need to retrieve it — it won't come out on its own. Bitul in your mind suffices. Two Critical Deductions 1. No obligation to search improbable places The halacha says you must retrieve chametz on the beam because you know it's there . There is no obligation to climb a ladder and search the top of a chandelier \"just in case.\" You only need to retrieve chametz from hard-to-reach places when you know or have reasonable grounds to believe it is there. 2. The threshold is a kezayit The Shulchan Aruch specifies kezayit — implying that less than a kezayit does not trigger this obligation. Mishnah Berurah: Brings two opinions: Shulchan Aruch's view: Bedikat chametz obligation applies only to a kezayit or more. Other poskim: Even less than a kezayit requires searching. Mishnah Berurah's leaning (in Sha'ar HaTziyun): Favours the lenient view — the obligation was only for a kezayit. What Is a Kezayit? Commonly cited as 27 grams — but this is a stringent modern estimate. Likely the true kezayit is much smaller — general consensus of poskim places it between 15–20 grams . Some opinions say as low as 3 grams. Cannot rely on the 27g figure to be lenient (i.e., you can't say \"it's less than 27g so I don't need to search\"). Key practical takeaway: You are searching for substantial pieces of chametz, not tiny crumbs. There is no obligation to hunt for crumbs — the Shulchan Aruch does not require that. What If It's Dangerous or Impossible to Retrieve? Scenario Obligation Dangerous to climb (shaky ladder, unsafe height) No obligation — pikuach nefesh; rely on bitul No ladder available, found out hours before Pesach Rely on bitul — no other option Can hire a builder/professional to retrieve it Must do so — it's a chiyuv de-rabbanan like any other; spend money to fulfil it Physically impossible to access Rely on bitul Siman 439 – Chametz and Matzah Confusion; the Principle of Kavua (דין מי שבדק ולא מצא משפט ככרות שנתחלפו) Selected Se'ifim — key Talmudic principles Se'if 1 – Nine Piles of Matzah and One of Chametz (The Kavua Principle) Shulchan Aruch (from the Gemara): Nine piles of matzah and one pile of chametz. A mouse takes something — we don't know from which pile. He runs into a checked house. Question: Since there's a 9:1 majority of matzah, can we follow the majority (rov) and assume the mouse took matzah — and therefore no need to re-check? Answer: No. You must do bedikat chametz again. The Talmudic Principle: Kavua (קבוע) Kol kavua ke-mechtzah al mechtzah dami — Anything that is stationary/fixed (kavua) at the time the doubt arises is treated as 50-50 , regardless of the actual ratio. Scenario Principle Result Doubt arises at the stationary source (e.g., mouse takes from the fixed piles) Kavua — treated as 50-50 Cannot follow majority; must re-check Doubt arises after separation from the source (e.g., a piece was already pushed away, then the mouse took it) Kol de-parish me-ruba parish — what separates is assumed to come from the majority Can follow majority; no need to re-check The Classic Gemara Example (Ketubot / Chulin) Nine kosher butcher shops and one non-kosher , in a row. Meat found outside the shops (already separated): Kosher — because kol de-parish me-ruba parish. Person walked into one of the shops and bought meat (doubt at the stationary source): Treated as 50-50 — the meat is a safek, despite the 9:1 ratio. Application to Our Case If someone pushed one piece away from the nine-and-one piles before the mouse came, and the mouse then took that separated piece → Fine — follow the majority; no need to re-check. If the mouse took directly from the stationary piles → Kavua — must re-check. Note: This principle has enormous ramifications throughout halacha — lineage (yuchsin), kashrus, and many other areas. Se'if 4 – Chametz Found in a Different Location Shulchan Aruch: If you left chametz in one specific place, and then find chametz in a different place : You must search the room again . Reason: The chametz found elsewhere may be a different piece entirely. The original chametz you left is unaccounted for — it may have been moved by a mouse or child. Exception: If you can identify that it is the same chametz (you recognise it), then it's fine. Siman 440 – A Non-Jew Who Deposits Chametz in a Jewish Person's Home (דין חמץ של נכרי שהופקד אצל ישראל) Contains 4 Se'ifim Background: The Torah's Two Pesukim The Gemara identifies an apparent contradiction between two pesukim: \"לא יראה לך\" — \"It shall not be seen to you \" → Implies only your chametz is problematic; a non-Jew's chametz in your house should be fine. \"לא ימצא\" — \"It shall not be found \" → Implies any chametz is a problem, even if it's not yours. Resolution (Gemara): It depends on whether you have accepted responsibility (achrayut) for the chametz. If you have responsibility, you have an association (connection) to it — it's as if it is partly \"yours\" for this purpose. Se'if 1 – Levels of Responsibility Shulchan Aruch — Two Opinions: Opinion 1 (Stam / Rambam — Main Halacha): Only if you accepted responsibility for theft and loss (gneva va-aveida) — a higher level of liability — are you obligated to remove the chametz. If you only accepted responsibility for negligence (peshia) — a lower level — you are not obligated. Opinion 2 (Rosh): Even responsibility for negligence alone creates sufficient association — you must remove it. Halacha: We follow the first opinion (stam of the Shulchan Aruch). Only a higher level of responsibility (theft and loss) creates the obligation. If No Responsibility Was Accepted at All The chametz may remain in your house — no problem whatsoever . You have no association to it; it is entirely the non-Jew's. But you must still make a partition (see Se'if 2) so you don't accidentally eat it. Special Case: A Non-Jew You're Afraid Of Shulchan Aruch: Even if you didn't officially accept responsibility, if the non-Jew is someone powerful/threatening who would force you to pay if the chametz is damaged, there is an association — because you effectively care about it. Some disagree with this stringency. What If You Must Get Rid of It? First option: Return it to the non-Jew (put it back in his house/give it back). If you can't return it: You must destroy it . Mishnah Berurah: There is a possibility of selling it — though some say this is difficult since it's not truly \"yours.\" But if no other option exists, you could transfer it to someone else. Se'if 2 – No Responsibility Accepted: The Mechitzah Requirement Shulchan Aruch: If a non-Jew deposited chametz in your house and you accepted no responsibility , the chametz may stay. However, you must create a mechitzah (partition) of 10 tefachim (~80 cm) as a reminder not to eat it. Key Details About the Mechitzah Point Detail Height 10 tefachim (~80 cm) Type A proper partition — not just covering with a vessel (kli) Why not just cover it? Covering (kofeh kli) is only for chametz found during Chag itself when you can't build a mechitzah Before Pesach You can and must build a proper mechitzah During Chag (non-Jew deposited chametz on Pesach itself) Cover it with a kli; on Chol HaMoed, build a proper mechitzah Modern Application: Sale of Chametz When we sell our chametz, the wall of the cupboard acts as the mechitzah. We also place a sign as an additional reminder. If sold chametz were lying around the house (not in a cupboard), you would need to erect a mechitzah around it. Biur Halacha: Does the Mechitzah Apply to All Chametz? Strong argument: The mechitzah is only needed for ready-to-eat chametz (bread, baked goods) — items you might impulsively eat. Raw ingredients (flour, yeast) don't need a mechitzah — by the time you'd prepare them, you'd remember it's Pesach. In practice: Make the mechitzah for everything anyway. Interesting Point: Pat Akum (Non-Jewish Bread) There is a halacha to prefer bread baked by a Jew (pat Yisrael) over non-Jewish bread (pat akum). Question: If someone is strict about pat Yisrael all year, can they skip the mechitzah — since they'd never eat the non-Jew's bread anyway? Answer: No. Since me'ikar hadin one is permitted to eat pat akum (in certain conditions), the mechitzah is still required. Rama: Non-Jew Deposits Against Your Will If a non-Jew puts chametz in your house without asking — same rules apply. Before Pesach: Make a mechitzah. During Chag: Cover it; build mechitzah on Chol HaMoed. Se'if 3 – A Non-Jewish Worker Entering Your House with Chametz Shulchan Aruch: A non-Jewish person enters your house carrying his own chametz (e.g., a plumber/builder brings his sandwiches). The Rules Aspect Ruling Can he bring chametz into your house? Yes — you don't have to ask him to leave it outside Can you see his chametz? Yes — \"lo yera'eh\" means chametz belonging to you shouldn't be seen; seeing someone else's chametz is fine Does he need a mechitzah? No — he's holding/carrying it; it's his food with him. Mechitzah is only for deposited/left chametz Can he eat on your table? Yes — as long as you are not eating at the same time and you clean the table afterwards Can you eat together at the same table? No — forbidden, even with a separation/sign between you Why Can't You Eat Together? (Meat-and-Milk Comparison) General halacha (basar be-chalav): Two Jews eating at the same table — one dairy, one meat — must create a siman (sign/recognition) between them, but may eat together. A Jew eating with a non-Jew eating non-kosher food — fine without any siman , because there's no temptation to swap (it's not kosher). On Pesach — stricter: The prohibition of chametz on Pesach is be-mashehu — even the tiniest crumb is forbidden (unlike other prohibitions that require a minimum amount). Therefore, even eating at the same table with proper separation is forbidden — a crumb could mix into your food. This applies even to a non-Jew's chametz, which you would never otherwise eat. Practical Summary for Non-Jewish Workers He may enter with his chametz. He may eat in your house — on your table, at a different time from you. You must clean the table thoroughly afterwards. He may not eat at the same table at the same time as you. No mechitzah required for chametz he carries (as opposed to chametz he leaves/deposits). Se'if 4 – A Jew Depositing Chametz with Another Jew Shulchan Aruch: If a Jewish person deposited chametz with another Jewish person, even if the depositee accepted responsibility — both Jews can be liable for the prohibition. The depositor: It's his chametz. The depositee: He accepted responsibility, creating an association. Unless they sell it, destroy it, or otherwise deal with it — both are in violation. Summary of Key Practical Points Siman Key Takeaways 438:1 Mouse with bread + crumbs found = must re-search (mice don't crumb). Child with bread + crumbs found = fine (children crumb). No crumbs from child = must search that room. Child with da'at = just ask them. 438:2 Kezayit on a high beam = must retrieve (ladder). In a pit = bitul suffices. No obligation to search improbable places unless you know chametz is there. Bedikat chametz obligation is for a kezayit — not crumbs. 439:1 Kavua principle: doubt at stationary source = 50-50 regardless of majority. Doubt after separation = follow majority (kol de-parish). 439:4 Chametz found in a different place than where you left it = must re-search (may be a different piece). 440:1 Non-Jew deposits chametz: if you accepted responsibility for theft/loss = must remove. Negligence only = no obligation (main halacha). No responsibility = fine, but need mechitzah. 440:2 No responsibility accepted: chametz may stay but must have a 10-tefachim mechitzah. Modern application: cupboard wall + sign when selling chametz. 440:3 Non-Jewish worker may bring chametz into your house and eat on your table (not at the same time as you). Clean up after. No mechitzah needed for carried chametz. 440:4 Jew depositing chametz with another Jew: both potentially liable.","EZkyhJWnAJg":"Recap of Previous Classes Class Simanim Topics Covered Class 1 429–430 30 days before Pesach; month of Nisan; Shabbat HaGadol Class 2 431–433 Correct time for Bedikat Chametz; the bracha; appointing agents; where to check and various scenarios Class 3 434–437 After the Bedikah; missed Bedikah; travelling before Pesach; renting/letting a house before Pesach Siman 434 – Laws After Bedikat Chametz (דינים הנוהגים תכף אחר הבדיקה) Contains 4 Se'ifim Se'if 1 – Securing the Remaining Chametz Shulchan Aruch: אחר הבדיקה יהא נזהר בחמץ שמשייר להצניעו כדי שלא יצטרך בדיקה אחרת — After completing the Bedikah, one must be careful with the chametz that remains (for eating that night and the next morning) and place it in a secure location , so that one does not end up needing to do Bedikat Chametz all over again. What Constitutes \"Secure\"? Not merely a place where you know where it is — it must be inaccessible to children and vermin (mice, rats, etc.). Cover it, wrap it, hang it up, or place it in a sealed cupboard. The Mishnah Berurah laments the common practice of people who check meticulously but then leave the remaining chametz lying around in an open bag — this is incorrect. When Do You Need to Re-check? The obligation to redo Bedikat Chametz depends on two conditions being met simultaneously: Condition Result Left in a secure place + chametz is missing the next morning No need to re-check — assume someone took it; the secure location rules out vermin Left in an insecure place + you didn't pay attention to how much chametz you left No need to re-check — since you don't know anything is actually missing Left in an insecure place + you know for a fact there is less chametz than you left + no explanation for where it went Must do full Bedikat Chametz again — even during the day of Erev Pesach Why Can't We Assume the Mouse Ate It? There is a vaddai (certainty) that a piece of chametz existed and is now missing. There is only a safek (doubt) whether the mouse ate it — perhaps it merely moved it elsewhere in the house. Principle: אין ספק מוציא מידי ודאי — a doubt cannot override a certainty. Therefore, the chametz must be searched for again. Connection to the Ten Pieces This is an additional reason why the ten pieces left out for Bedikat Chametz should each be less than a kezayit . If a piece goes missing and it was less than a kezayit, these halachot do not apply — there is no obligation to re-check. If the pieces were a kezayit or more, a missing piece triggers the full obligation to search again. Se'if 2 – Bitul (Nullification) After Bedikat Chametz at Night Shulchan Aruch: Immediately after completing Bedikat Chametz at night, one must perform Bitul (nullification) by declaring: \"Any chametz (חמירא) in my possession which I have not seen and have not removed — let it be annulled and become like the dust of the earth.\" Why Do We Need Both Bedikah and Bitul? Min HaTorah: Either Bitul alone or Bedikah alone would suffice. The Chachamim instituted both as a double safeguard: The Bedikah physically removes chametz. The Bitul covers anything the Bedikah may have missed. Why Did the Chachamim Institute Bitul Specifically at Night? Ideally, Bitul would make more sense in the morning (to cover the intervening hours). However, the Chachamim were concerned a person would forget if it were left to the morning. Therefore, they instituted it together with the Bedikah — immediately after searching, you nullify, so you won't forget. Critical: The Night-Time Wording Is Different from the Daytime At night, one says ONLY: \"Any chametz in my possession which I have not seen and have not removed — let it be annulled…\" One does NOT say at night: \"which I have seen or not seen, which I have removed or not removed\" (that is the morning formula). Why the difference? At night, you are intentionally keeping chametz to eat that evening and the next morning. If you were to use the morning formula at night, you would be nullifying all your chametz — including what you plan to eat. This creates a serious problem: The next morning, when you dispose of the chametz, it no longer belongs to you (you already nullified it). You cannot fulfil the mitzvah of תשביתו שאור מבתיכם (expelling chametz from your house) on chametz that isn't yours. Common mistake: People accidentally say the morning formula at night — this is wrong and undermines the morning mitzvah. The Bitul Must Be in a Language You Understand Rama: The Bitul must be said in any language the person understands . The original Aramaic formula (חמירא) was enacted because Aramaic was the vernacular — the Chachamim wanted everyone to understand what they were saying. If you don't understand Aramaic: Say it in English, Hebrew, or whatever language you understand. If you said it without understanding: The Bitul is invalid — you cannot relinquish ownership of something without understanding that you are doing so. Mishnah Berurah: Many people recite it thinking it is some kind of prayer or supplication — this does not work at all. Bedi'avad: If you understand the general concept of what you are doing (i.e., you were told by the Rav that this statement relinquishes ownership), even without understanding the specific words, it works after the fact. If Bitul is invalid but Bedikah was done: You are still fine mi-d'oraita (the Bedikah suffices biblically). But if Bedikah was also not done properly, this is a serious problem — you would go through Pesach actually owning chametz. Best practice: Say the Bitul in Aramaic (as the Chachamim instituted), and then repeat it in English (or your vernacular language). The Word \"Chamira\" (חמירא) The Aramaic word חמירא is unique in that it encompasses both chametz (leavened product) and se'or (the leavening/rising agent itself). When translating into other languages (including Hebrew), one must specify both — e.g., \"any leaven or rising agent.\" These are two distinct Torah prohibitions: (a) actual chametz, and (b) substances that cause leavening. Full details in a later siman. Se'if 3 – Bitul During the Daytime (Morning) Shulchan Aruch: The Bitul during the daytime uses the expanded formula: \"Any chametz in my possession — whether I have seen it or not seen it, whether I have removed it or not removed it — let it be annulled…\" Night vs. Day Formula Comparison Night-Time Bitul Daytime Bitul Wording \"Which I have not seen and not removed\" \"Whether I have seen it or not , removed it or not \" Scope Only chametz you missed during Bedikah All chametz — including what you previously kept Status Obligatory (Chachamim's institution) Recommended (good practice; instituted by Rishonim) If forgotten Serious problem Not the end of the world Timing of the Morning Bitul Must be done while the chametz still belongs to you — i.e., before one hour before halachic midday (chatzot) . After one hour before chatzot, the Chachamim revoke your ownership (מפקיע), so Bitul is no longer possible. Critical: Dispose First, Then Do Bitul Do not do Bitul first thing in the morning \"just in case you forget.\" The correct order: First dispose/burn the chametz → then do Bitul . Reason: You must dispose of chametz while it still belongs to you to fulfil the mitzvah of תשביתו. If you do Bitul first, the chametz no longer belongs to you, and the disposal doesn't fulfil the mitzvah. The Timeline of Chametz Prohibitions Time Eating Owning Source Until 2 halachic hours before chatzot Permitted Permitted — From 2 hours before chatzot Forbidden (rabbinic) Permitted Chachamim added extra caution for eating From 1 hour before chatzot Forbidden Forbidden (rabbinic) Chachamim's deadline for ownership From chatzot (halachic midday) Forbidden (Torah — lav) Forbidden (Torah — lav) Negative prohibition; lashes From nightfall of 15 Nisan (Pesach) Forbidden (Torah — karet) Forbidden (Torah — karet) Most severe; spiritual excision Why the stagger? The Chachamim were more concerned about eating (potentially more severe) than owning, so they added an extra hour's buffer for eating. If one accidentally ate or owned chametz only in the rabbinic window (between chatzot and the Chachamim's earlier deadlines), the transgression is only de-rabbanan . Se'if 4 – Appointing an Agent for Bitul Shulchan Aruch: An agent (shaliach) can perform Bitul on your behalf. How the Agent Says It The agent does not say \"any chametz in my possession .\" The agent says: \"Any chametz belonging to Mr. X / of [person's name] — let it be annulled…\" Do not say \"chametz in his house\" — he may have chametz elsewhere. Does Appointing an Agent for Bedikah Automatically Include Bitul? Scenario Does agency extend to Bitul? Owner is in the city / at home No — we assume you did not extend agency to Bitul Owner has left town Yes — we assume agency extends to Bitul by implication If the Owner Didn't Appoint Anyone for Bitul He should do Bitul wherever he is (even in another city/country). If he doesn't, his wife should do it . The Problem with the Wife Doing Bitul Question: How does the wife know whether her husband did Bitul or not? (Historically, before telephones.) Answer: There is a chazakah (presumption) that a person will do it. Nonetheless, it is still good (tov) for the wife to do it as well. From Talmudic law, a wife's property belongs to her husband — so the question arises whether she can nullify chametz that technically belongs to him. We assume he has given her implied permission to take ownership for the purpose of Bitul. Nowadays: Simply call and ask. If he says he can't do it, he gives permission, and she does it. In the first instance: The husband/owner should always do it himself, not rely on his wife. Why Can't We Use the Principle of Zachin (Acting for Someone's Benefit)? Zachin le-adam she-lo be-fanav = You can do something for someone's benefit without their knowledge. Question: Bitul is a merit (zechut) — why can't anyone just do it for him? Answer: Zachin only works when it is purely beneficial . Bitul involves taking away ownership of something — that is a chovah (detriment). Since there is an element of loss, Zachin does not apply. Implication: A Rav cannot do Bitul on behalf of his entire congregation — you cannot unilaterally strip people of their property, even for a mitzvah. Siman 435 – One Who Did Not Check on the Night of the 14th (דין מי שלא בדק בליל י\"ד) Contains 1 Se'if Se'if 1 – The Ongoing Obligation of Bedikat Chametz Shulchan Aruch: The obligation of Bedikat Chametz never expires . If missed at the ideal time, it carries forward: When Missed What to Do Bracha? Didn't check on the night of the 14th Check during the day of the 14th (Erev Pesach), as early as possible Yes Didn't check the entire day of the 14th Check during Pesach itself (Chol HaMoed) Yes Didn't check during Pesach Check after Pesach No Checking During Erev Pesach (Day of the 14th) Should be done as soon as possible — ideally before the hour preceding chatzot. Even if that time has passed, you are still obligated to check throughout the entire day. The Tannaic Debate — Checking After the Prohibition Kicks In Rabbi Yehudah: Once you can no longer eat chametz, do not search — concern that you might find something appealing and be tempted to eat it. Chachamim (accepted halacha): Since the entire purpose of your search is to expel chametz, we are not concerned that you will eat what you find while actively trying to remove it. Checking During Pesach Itself If you did Bitul before Pesach: Check during Chol HaMoed (not on Yom Tov itself). Say a bracha. If you find chametz on Pesach, you cannot burn it — cover it until you can properly dispose of it. If you did NOT do Bitul before Pesach: You must check even on Yom Tov/Chag itself . When you find chametz, cover it and nullify your ownership of it immediately. Checking After Pesach — No Bracha If you didn't check before or during Pesach, you must check after Pesach . No bracha is said on this post-Pesach check. Reason: After Pesach, there is no Torah prohibition against having chametz in your house. The purpose is to identify and separate chametz she-avar alav ha-Pesach — chametz that remained in Jewish ownership over Pesach — which is permanently forbidden for any benefit (eating, selling, etc.). You must distinguish between: Old chametz (from before Pesach) — forbidden forever New chametz (purchased after Pesach) — perfectly fine Chametz She-Avar Alav HaPesach (חמץ שעבר עליו הפסח) If chametz remained in Jewish ownership throughout Pesach (not sold, not nullified), the Chachamim decreed it is forbidden permanently — not just for eating, but for any benefit . This is a penalty (kenas) imposed by the Chachamim. Practical ramification: Eating at the homes of non-observant Jews immediately after Pesach raises questions — they may not have sold their chametz, meaning their whiskey, bread, etc. from before Pesach would be forbidden. This is a major topic discussed at length in a later siman. Siman 436 – One Who Travels Before Pesach (דין מי שהולך ממקומו קודם הפסח) Contains multiple Se'ifim — largely impractical nowadays Core Principle from the Gemara Shulchan Aruch: If a person is travelling before Pesach and leaving no one at home, and has not appointed an agent: Scenario Obligation Leaving within 30 days of Pesach + no one at home + no agent Must do Bedikat Chametz the night before departing Leaving before 30 days of Pesach No need to check before leaving Leaving before 30 days but intends to return before Pesach Must check before leaving (concern you'll return too late) Why This Is Largely Impractical Today The Shulchan Aruch notes this is situation-dependent , and nowadays it is almost impossible to find a case where these halachot apply, because: Most people leave someone at home (spouse, children at age of chinuch) — if so, none of this applies; the person at home checks. Modern communication — even a single person travelling can phone someone and appoint an agent. Agency is easy to arrange — historically, there was even a practice of paying someone to do Bedikat Chametz on your behalf. Bottom line: If you're travelling, either leave someone at home to check, or appoint an agent by phone. In the rare case where neither is possible, the Gemara's original din applies. Siman 437 – Renting or Letting a House Before Pesach (דין המשכיר בית לחבירו) Contains multiple Se'ifim Se'if 1 – Who Is Obligated to Check: Landlord or Tenant? Shulchan Aruch: The obligation depends on who has the keys at the onset of the night of the 14th. Scenario Who checks? Tenant has completed the rental contract but does not yet have the keys on the night of the 14th Landlord Tenant has received the keys before the night of the 14th (even moments before) Tenant — even if he hasn't physically moved in yet Tenant moves in after the night of the 14th but had keys before Tenant Modern Keyless Entry For houses without physical keys (smart locks, screen access, etc.), we go by when the rental contract takes effect rather than physical key handover. Se'if 2 – Renting on the 14th: Can You Assume It's Been Checked? If you know the landlord is not religious or won't check → the obligation falls upon you (the tenant). You cannot live in an unchecked house. If you can communicate with the landlord → ask him whether he checked. Don't rely on a chazakah (presumption) when you can simply find out. If you cannot communicate with the landlord → you may assume it has been checked (based on chazakah), but you must still do Bitul yourself. Staying in someone else's house (not renting): If they have given you access and relinquished ownership of contents for Pesach → you would need to check. If they have merely allowed you to stay → assume they've handled their own Bedikat Chametz and sale of chametz. Best practice: Ask whether chametz has been dealt with. Se'if 3 – Finding Chametz in a Supposedly Checked Rental Shulchan Aruch: If a landlord told the tenant the house was checked, and the tenant moves in and finds chametz : The tenant must check the house himself (he now has an obligation). However, he cannot void the rental contract (mekach ta'ut / transaction under false pretences). Reason: The landlord has not caused the tenant financial loss — only imposed a mitzvah upon him. Causing someone to need to do a mitzvah is not grounds for voiding a transaction. Se'if (final) – Who Can Perform Bedikat Chametz A woman may perform Bedikat Chametz. A child who has reached the age of chinuch may perform Bedikat Chametz. These are considered reliable for the search. Summary of Key Practical Points Siman Key Takeaways 434:1 Secure remaining chametz after Bedikah; if insecure + chametz missing with no explanation → re-check. Leave pieces less than a kezayit. 434:2 Do Bitul immediately after Bedikah at night. Must understand what you're saying — use your own language. Night formula differs from morning. 434:3 Morning Bitul is recommended (not strictly obligatory). Dispose of chametz first , then do Bitul. Must be done before 1 hour before chatzot. 434:4 An agent can do Bitul for you. Wife can do it if husband is away. Zachin doesn't apply (involves loss of ownership). 435:1 Bedikat Chametz obligation never expires — carries through Erev Pesach, Pesach itself, and even after Pesach (without bracha). Chametz she-avar alav ha-Pesach is forbidden forever. 436 Travelling before Pesach: check before leaving if within 30 days + no one at home + no agent. Largely impractical today due to communication. 437:1 Landlord vs. tenant obligation: determined by who has the keys on the night of the 14th. 437:2 If renting on the 14th: ask if it's been checked; if you can't ask, assume yes but do Bitul. 437:3 Finding chametz in a \"checked\" rental: must check, but cannot void the lease.","7XPbKYQxzuA":"Recap of Previous Class Siman Topics Covered 429 Learning Hilchot Pesach 30 days before; month of Nisan; fasting; eulogies; stone settings; ma'ot chittin 430 Shabbat HaGadol — why it's called that; customs 431 Bedikat Chametz — rabbinic mitzvah; Torah only requires bitul; two reasons Chachamim required bedikah (sincerity concern + temptation concern); beginning of the night; precedence over Arvit; candle; restrictions before bedikah Siman 432 – The Bracha on Bedikat Chametz (דין ברכת בדיקת חמץ) Contains 2 Se'ifim Se'if 1 – The Bracha and Its Wording Shulchan Aruch: קודם שיתחיל לבדוק יברך אשר קדשנו במצותיו וצונו על ביעור חמץ — Before beginning the search, one recites the bracha: \"…Who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us concerning the destruction of chametz (על ביעור חמץ).\" Why \"Al Biur Chametz\" and Not \"Al Bedikat Chametz\"? We are about to search , not destroy — so why mention destruction? Answer: The entire purpose of the bedikah is the biur (destruction) the next morning. The search is merely a hechsher (preliminary/preparation) for the real mitzvah. The Chachamim did not want to establish a bracha on something that is only preparatory — even though bedikat chametz is a mitzvah de-rabbanan in its own right. The bracha covers both the bedikah being done now and the destruction the next morning — it is a continuing bracha. When saying the bracha: Have in mind both the search you are doing now and the destruction the following morning. Why Not \"Al Bitul Chametz\"? Bitul (nullification) happens in the mind — it is a conscious mental decision. You do not say a bracha on something that occurs purely in a person's thoughts. \"Al\" (על) vs. \"Le-\" (ל) We say \"al biur\" — not \"leva'er chametz.\" \"Le-\" = \"to do it now\" — but we are not destroying now; that happens tomorrow morning. \"Al\" = \"concerning\" — the destruction is going to happen, and we are beginning the process tonight by searching. Bedi'avad: If you said \"leva'er chametz,\" you are yotzei, but one should be particular to say \"al biur.\" The Meaning of \"Biur\" — Sephardi vs. Ashkenazi Machloket in the Gemara: Rabbi Yehuda vs. Chachamim. Chachamim (main halacha, accepted by all): Biur = destroy by any means — break into pieces, throw into the sea, crumble, etc. Rabbi Yehuda: Biur = specifically burn . Sephardim follow the Chachamim: biur means to destroy/get rid of chametz by any method. Ashkenazim are machmir and prefer burning, but agree that me'ikar hadin any destruction suffices. This year (5785): Since Erev Pesach falls on Shabbat, you cannot burn chametz on Shabbat. You can burn on Friday morning, but the remaining chametz on Shabbat must be destroyed by other means — relying on the main opinion of the Chachamim. No Separate Bracha the Next Morning Since the bracha said at night covers the biur the next morning, no new bracha is said when destroying the chametz. If, however, you did the entire bedikah at night without saying any bracha at all , then the next morning before the biur you do say \"al biur chametz.\" Speaking During Bedikat Chametz Shulchan Aruch: ויזהר שלא ידבר בין הברכה לתחילת הבדיקה — One must not speak between the bracha and beginning the search. Rules on Speech Timing Permitted? Details Between bracha and beginning to search No — any unrelated speech is a hefsek Would require repeating the bracha (bracha le-vatalah) Speech related to the bedikah (e.g., \"Where should I begin?\" / \"Where is the candle?\") Yes — this is part of the mitzvah Not a hefsek After beginning the search (at least one area checked) Me'ikar hadin permitted — the bracha has \"landed\" on something But Maran says it is tov (good practice) not to speak unnecessary matters until the entire search is complete, to maintain focus Urgent matters (e.g., phone call) Permitted me'ikar hadin But better to avoid if possible Speech about the bedikah throughout Always permitted \"Has this cupboard been checked?\" / \"Is this chametz?\" — all part of the mitzvah Se'if 2 – One Bracha for Multiple Properties Shulchan Aruch: בברכה אחת יכול לבדוק כמה בתים — With one bracha, a person can check multiple properties. The Principle The mitzvah of bedikat chametz is to check everywhere in your possession . The bracha covers this entire obligation — even if you have to travel between properties. Travelling between homes is not a hefsek , because it's all one continuous mitzvah. Example: Check your home, then drive to your shop or synagogue — all under one bracha. Speaking Between Properties If you spoke mundane matters while travelling between properties — you do not need to repeat the bracha , as long as you already began searching in the first location. If properties are very far apart (e.g., different countries — theoretical): The poskim advise having in mind that the bracha applies only to the current property , then saying a new bracha at the distant property. If properties are nearby (same neighbourhood): One bracha covers all. Family Members Splitting Up Shulchan Aruch: The ba'al habayit (homeowner) can gather his family, say the bracha, and then each person goes to search a different property or a different part of the house. The family members hear the bracha and are yotzei through it. They act as the ba'al habayit's agents (shluchim) — since it is his property, they search on his behalf. This only works when the properties belong to the ba'al habayit. If children own their own homes, they say their own bracha in their own home. Potential Problem — Sending an Agent Mid-Search If the ba'al habayit began searching alone, said the bracha himself, and then mid-search decides to send his son to continue in another property: The ba'al habayit cannot say the bracha again (already said it). The son cannot say the bracha (it's not his obligation — only as agent). The son didn't hear the original bracha. Solution (bedi'avad): The son continues without a bracha — it's still one continuous mitzvah. Best practice: If you plan to split up, gather everyone before the bracha so they all hear it. Who Can Perform Bedikat Chametz? Person Can they search? Notes Adult man Yes Primary obligation Adult woman Yes Me'ikar hadin, fully capable. If living alone — obligated to say bracha and search herself. If married — husband as ba'al habayit says bracha, but she can search as agent Child (bar/bat mitzvah age) Yes Preferred over younger children Child (below bar/bat mitzvah, age of chinuch) Yes, me'ikar hadin Because bedikat chametz is de-rabbanan, a child is believed. Better if over bar mitzvah. Must be reliable and understand what they're doing Comparison to Tevilat Kelim De'oraita obligations (e.g., immersing metal utensils): A child under bar/bat mitzvah is not believed — an adult must be present or do it. De-rabbanan obligations (e.g., immersing glass utensils; bedikat chametz): A child is believed . The Minhag of Leaving Pieces of Chametz (Rama) Rama: נוהגים להניח פתיתי חמץ במקום שימצאם הבודק — It is customary to leave pieces of chametz around the house for the searcher to find, כדי שלא יהיה ברכתו לבטלה — so the bracha won't be in vain (in case no chametz is found). Key Points The pieces are placed where the searcher will find them — not hidden in impossible locations. This is not a treasure hunt. Me'ikar hadin, this is unnecessary: The bracha is on the searching , not on finding. Even if nothing is found, the bracha is valid. The Rama himself clarifies: ומיהו אם לא נתן לא עכב — \"If you didn't do this minhag, it's absolutely fine.\" Historical Reception Ra'avad: Mocked this minhag — called it \"מנהג נשים\" (a custom of the uneducated/ignorant). Chacham Tzvi: Called it ridiculous and pointless. The poskim generally did not find great significance in it. Modern justification: It motivates the searcher to look properly — especially relevant nowadays when the house has already been cleaned for weeks and the bedikah night can feel like going through the motions. The Arizal — Ten Pieces There is no halachic concept of ten pieces — this is entirely from Kabbalah. The Arizal established that one should leave ten pieces , and Sephardim generally follow this custom. Critical: Size of the Pieces Each piece must be less than a kezayit (currently accepted as ~27 grams, though historically likely much less). Why? If a piece goes missing (child hid it and can't remember where): Less than a kezayit: No problem — just do bitul and move on. A kezayit or more: Major problem — you have definite chametz somewhere in the house and must search until it's found. Practical advice: Make the pieces very small. Have children write down the exact location of each piece before beginning. Do not play the \"hiding game\" without a written record. Siman 433 – Further Laws of Bedikat Chametz (דיני בדיקת חמץ) Contains 11 Se'ifim Overarching Principle You only need to check in places where it is possible that chametz was brought in during the year. If you are certain chametz never entered a place, it does not need to be checked. Se'if 1 – Checking with a Candle; Prior Searches Shulchan Aruch: הבדיקה צריך שתהא לאור הנר ולא לאור הלבנה — The search must be done by candlelight, not moonlight. Prior Searches Are Valid (Mishnah Berurah) If you searched a room before the night of the 14th, and you are certain no chametz has entered since , you do not need to re-check that room. This is a crucial practical point: bedikat chametz on the night of the 14th only applies to places not yet properly searched . If the entire house has been searched in advance except the kitchen (where chametz is still being used), me'ikar hadin you only need to do bedikat chametz in the kitchen . The One Exception: Young Children If there are young children in the house who were not under constant watch, you cannot rely on prior searches . You must re-check anywhere the child could have reached — even rooms previously cleaned and declared chametz-free. A couple without children (or with only older, reliable children) who searched the whole house in advance will have a very quick bedikat chametz. Checking During the Day If you missed the night of the 14th, you must check the next morning — before eating breakfast , as soon as possible after Shacharit. Use a candle even during the day (not just sunlight), to access nooks and crannies. Exception: A room with abundant natural light (large windows, skylight) — you can rely on sunlight during the day. A bracha is still said when checking during the day. Se'if 2 – Type of Candle Shulchan Aruch: אין בודקין לאור האבוקה אלא לאור הנר — Do not search with a large flame (avukah/torch), only a single candle. Reasons A large flame is dangerous — you won't check properly for fear of burning the house. Too big to insert into nooks and crannies. Specifications Type Permitted? Single wax candle Yes — the ideal Fatty/greasy candle (tallow, oil) No — will spill; person won't check corners properly Two-wicked candle (e.g., Havdalah candle) No — considered an avukah Torch / phone flashlight Yes — the poskim accept this (see below) Practical Modern Consideration Walking around modern homes with carpet, sofas, and wood surfaces with an open flame can be impractical and even dangerous (the very concern the Chachamim had). The Rav's personal practice: Use a candle to fulfil the concept the Chachamim established, but supplement with a phone torch when checking underneath sofas, behind furniture, and in tight spaces where a flame would be dangerous. Se'if 3 – The Core Principle: Only Check Where Chametz May Have Been Brought Shulchan Aruch: בודק כל המקומות שיש לחוש שמא הכניסו בהם חמץ — Check any place where there is reason to suspect chametz was brought in. Certainty Level Need to Check? Definite chametz is brought there regularly Yes Occasional — not regularly, but sometimes Yes Doubtful — possibly but not certain Yes Certain no chametz was ever brought there No Shulchan Aruch: כל חדרי הבית ועליות צריכים בדיקה — All rooms and upper floors need checking, because sometimes a person walks through the house with bread in hand. Exception: Storehouses, sheds, and storage areas where you would never bring food during a meal — these do not need bedikah. Practical Applications Area Need to check? Reason Kitchen Yes Primary chametz area Dining room Yes Regular eating Bedrooms (if you sometimes eat there) Yes Walking through with bread Bedrooms (strict no-food policy, no kids) No Chametz never enters Bathroom / toilet No (unless young children) No one eats there Garden shed No Never bring food there Top of high bedroom cupboards No Food would never reach there Loft/attic (slanted, storage only) No You wouldn't eat there Loft/attic (flat, walkable) Yes (unless no-food policy) Could bring food there Se'if 4 – Protruding Beams and High/Low Places Shulchan Aruch: Protruding beams from walls that are not too high and not too low need checking. But: Very high (beyond arm's reach) — no bedikah needed — chametz wouldn't reach there. Very low (less than 3 tefachim / ~24 cm from ground) — no bedikah needed — you wouldn't store chametz there. Exception (Mishnah Berurah): With young children, the low areas do need checking — children place chametz everywhere. Se'if 5 – Slanted Roofs / Loft Spaces Shulchan Aruch: A slanted attic roof or tower where there is no usable space — does not need bedikah . You would never spend time eating there; you only enter briefly to retrieve items. If the loft is flat and walkable — it would need bedikah (unless there's a no-food policy). Se'if 6 – Animal Areas and Courtyards Shulchan Aruch: Cattle shed: No bedikah needed — even if chametz was given to animals, the animals would have eaten it. Chicken coop: No bedikah needed — same reason. Middle of the courtyard / garden: No bedikah needed — birds and ravens would eat any chametz. The Garden — Important Qualification The above applies only to safek chametz (doubt whether chametz is there from earlier in the year). We assume birds/animals ate it. Vadai chametz (definite chametz — e.g., you ate in the garden on the night of bedikat chametz itself) — you must check that area of the garden. Chametz in a public domain (reshut harabim): Even if you definitely left chametz there on the night of bedikah — no need to check , because in a public space with many people and birds, we assume it has been consumed. Se'if 7 – Hole Between Two Jewish Neighbours Shulchan Aruch: If there is a hole connecting two Jewish properties: Each person checks as far as their hand can reach into the hole. If there's a space in the middle neither can reach — just do bitul in your heart ; that's sufficient. Hole Between a Jew and a Non-Jew Shulchan Aruch: No bedikah needed at all in this hole. Reason (Gemara): שמא יאמר כשפים הוא עושה לי — The non-Jew may think the Jew is performing sorcery (putting a candle through a hole late at night) and may harm him. The Principle of Shluchei Mitzvah Einan Nizokin Question: Aren't those engaged in a mitzvah protected from harm? Answer: שאני דשכיח היזיקא — When danger is common , we do not rely on this principle. The Gemara means that persecution of Jews by non-Jews was common , and the slightest provocation could trigger violence. Therefore, the Chachamim exempted this entirely. If you know for certain there is chametz there: Check during the daytime the next morning (less suspicious) and remove it. Practical Lesson Do not perform bedikat chametz in a way that arouses suspicion or creates danger. Be sensitive to non-Jewish neighbours and surroundings. Use common sense regarding health and safety. Se'if 8 – A Collapsed Wall with Chametz Underneath Shulchan Aruch: A wall where chametz was stored in its holes has collapsed into a heap of stones: Height of Heap Known Chametz? Need to Check? ≥ 3 tefachim (~24 cm) Yes or No No — just do bitul. A dog cannot access it at that height. < 3 tefachim Safek (unsure if chametz is there) No — due to danger of scorpions/creatures in the heap < 3 tefachim Vadai (definitely chametz there) Yes — must search with a spade, but only for chametz (not personal items) Why Not Check the Low Heap (Safek)? A dog can smell and access chametz up to 3 tefachim — so there's a concern it would drag chametz out during Pesach. But dangerous creatures (scorpions) inhabit these heaps. Question: You're doing a mitzvah — shluchei mitzvah einan nizokin should protect you! Answer: The Chachamim were concerned that after finishing searching for chametz , you'll continue rummaging for personal belongings (lost items). At that point, you're no longer engaged in the mitzvah, and you lose the protection. Important Gemara Principle — Doing a Mitzvah with Personal Benefit The Gemara distinguishes: Scenario Protected by shluchei mitzvah? While searching for chametz , you also look for personal items Yes — like giving charity while hoping for personal reward (האומר סלע זו לצדקה בשביל שיחיה בני — הרי זה צדיק גמור) After finishing the chametz search, you continue looking for personal items No — the mitzvah is over; you're now acting for yourself only Therefore, the Chachamim said: Don't check at all (in the safek case), because inevitably you'll continue searching for personal things after the mitzvah ends. Se'if 9 – Wine Cellar with Stacked Barrels Shulchan Aruch: A wine cellar with rows of barrels/bottles stacked high and wide: Only need to check the top two rows (the highest and the one beneath it). Only need to check what is directly in front of you when entering — not the full width/depth of the cellar. Reason: That's where you would realistically reach and where chametz could have been left when entering with food. This only applies if you sometimes enter the cellar with food; if you have a policy of no food in the wine cellar — no bedikah needed at all. Se'if 10 – Synagogues and Study Halls Shulchan Aruch: בתי כנסיות ובתי מדרשות צריכים בדיקה — Synagogues and study halls need bedikat chametz. Question: It's forbidden to eat in a synagogue — why check? Answer: מפני שהתינוקות מכניסים בהם חמץ — Because children bring chametz into these places (sweets, snacks given to children during services). A beit midrash (study hall) — there are circumstances where eating is permitted there, so checking is more straightforward. Mishnah Berurah: People don't always do this — it is the rabbi's and gabbai's responsibility to ensure the synagogue is checked. Se'if 11 – Cleaning vs. Searching Shulchan Aruch: If a person cleaned his room on the 13th of Nisan, even with intent to clean for Pesach, and was careful not to bring chametz back in — he still must do bedikat chametz on the night of the 14th . Cleaning ≠ Searching. A sweep or tidy-up is not bedikat chametz. Bedikat chametz requires actively searching in nooks, crannies, holes, behind and under furniture — not just making the house look neat. Practical implication: When cleaning before Pesach, make sure you are also searching — checking behind drawers, in corners, under beds. Otherwise, you'll have to redo everything on the night of the 14th. Rama — Preparation and Pockets Rama: Prepare before searching: כל אדם צריך לכבד חדריו קודם הבדיקה — Clean and organise rooms before the bedikah night so the search is easier and more efficient. Check pockets: הכיסים או בתי יד של בגדים שנותנים בהם לפעמים חמץ צריכים בדיקה — Pockets of clothing where chametz is sometimes placed need to be checked. If you never put food in pockets — no need to check (not common for adults). Children's pockets — definitely need checking. Method: Sufficient to shake out the pockets thoroughly. Summary of Key Practical Points Siman Key Takeaways 432:1 Bracha is \"al biur chametz\" — covers both the search (tonight) and destruction (tomorrow). Sephardim: biur = destroy by any means. No new bracha in the morning unless you forgot entirely at night. 432:1 (speech) No speech between bracha and beginning search. After beginning — permitted me'ikar hadin but best to stay focused. Bedikah-related speech always fine. 432:2 One bracha covers multiple properties. Family can split up after hearing one bracha. Best to gather everyone before the bracha if planning to split. 432 (who can check) Women and children (age of chinuch) can check — it's de-rabbanan. Better if over bar/bat mitzvah. 432 (Rama — pieces) Minhag to leave pieces; Arizal says 10. Me'ikar hadin unnecessary. Must be less than a kezayit. Write down locations. 433:1 Check by candlelight. Prior searches valid if no chametz entered since. Exception: young children. 433:2 Single wax candle — not large flames, fatty candles, or two-wicked. Supplement with torch for safety. 433:3 Only check where chametz may have been brought. Strict no-food policy = no bedikah needed in that area. 433:4–5 Very high/low places and slanted lofts — no bedikah (unless children access them). 433:6 Animal areas and gardens — no bedikah for safek; vadai chametz from night of bedikah must be checked. Public domain — no need even for vadai. 433:7 Hole between Jewish neighbours — each checks as far as they can reach. Between Jew and non-Jew — no bedikah (danger). 433:8 Collapsed wall — depends on height and certainty. Shluchei mitzvah protection doesn't apply when you'll also search for personal items. 433:9 Wine cellar — only top two rows and what's in front of you. 433:10 Synagogues need bedikah because of children. Rabbi/gabbai's responsibility. 433:11 Cleaning ≠ searching. Must actively search, not just tidy. Check pockets of clothing. Prepare rooms before bedikah night.","FxLlVtOIMfw":"Series Introduction New series for 2025 on Hilchot Pesach , following previous learning of Hilchot Shabbat, Sukkot, and Chanukah. Hilchot Pesach comprises 61 simanim in the Shulchan Aruch — significantly more complex and extensive than Chanukah (~15 simanim). This year (5785/2025): Erev Pesach falls on Shabbat (Pesach begins Motzei Shabbat), adding unique complexities addressed in dedicated simanim later. Focus of this series: The preparatory halachot from the beginning — Bedikat Chametz, Biur Chametz, Hagalat Kelim, baking of Matzot — rather than the Seder itself. Primary source: Shulchan Aruch with Sephardi psak, supplemented by Rama, Mishnah Berurah, and Acharonim. Siman 429 – Laws of the Month of Nisan (שאין נופלין על פניהם בכל חודש ניסן) Contains 2 Se'ifim Se'if 1 – Learning Hilchot Pesach 30 Days Before Shulchan Aruch: שואלין בהלכות הפסח קודם לפסח שלושים יום — We ask and discuss the halachot of Pesach beginning 30 days before the Chag. Source — Two Gemarot and an Apparent Contradiction Gemara (Pesachim 6a): שואלין ודורשין בענין החג שלושים יום קודם — \"We inquire and expound regarding the Chag 30 days before.\" Most Rishonim understand this as an obligation to teach, study, and discuss the halachot of Pesach from 30 days prior. Gemara (Megillah): Moshe Rabbeinu instituted that Bnei Yisrael should study the laws of each Chag on the Chag itself — Hilchot Pesach on Pesach, Hilchot Sukkot on Sukkot. The Contradiction — Rabbenu Nisim's Resolution Question: Is the obligation 30 days before, or on the Chag itself? Rabbenu Nisim's Chiddush: The \"30 days before\" is a din in Talmud Torah — it means that when a rabbi has many questions to answer, from 30 days before Pesach he must prioritise Pesach-related questions over others. However, the actual obligation to teach and study the halachot is only on the Chag itself, per Moshe's institution. Majority View — Obligation from 30 Days Before Most Rishonim reject Rabbenu Nisim and hold there is a genuine obligation to learn Hilchot Pesach from 30 days before. They resolve the contradiction in two ways: Moshe's institution was the original; the 30-day rule was a later development expanding it. Beit Yosef: 30 days before = learning about preparations for the Chag. Moshe's institution = learning the halachot of the Chag day itself (Yom Tov). Two different aspects. Practical Halacha The Shulchan Aruch rules in accordance with the majority: we begin studying Hilchot Pesach 30 days before . Every individual has an obligation to review and learn Hilchot Pesach annually — set aside time each day from 30 days before. The 30 days before Pesach begins on Purim — this is why there is a minhag to share a Pesach-related halacha or Torah idea already on Purim itself. Rambam lists these as two distinct dinim : (a) learning on the Chag itself, and (b) preparing 30 days before. Does This Apply to Other Chagim? Opinion Scope Some Poskim Applies to all Chagim — 30 days before Sukkot, Shavuot, etc. Other Poskim Specifically Pesach only, because its halachot are so complex and vast Vilna Gaon (compromise) Before Pesach = obligatory . Before Shavuot/Sukkot = a good thing / nice custom , but not the same level of obligation Shulchan Aruch's implication: This is a din unique to Pesach, since he records it specifically in Hilchot Pesach. Nevertheless, on all three Chagim , one should prioritise learning the halachot relevant to that Chag on the Chag day itself. Mishnah Berurah — Focus on Practical Halachot The Mishnah Berurah criticises the practice found in some communities (especially very Charedi ones) where the rabbi gives a highly complex, theoretical pilpul on Shabbat HaGadol that is not practically relevant . He insists the focus must be on relevant, practical halachot of the Chag. This has always been the Derech of the Sephardim as well. Se'if 2 (Rama) – Kimcha DePischa: Charity Before Pesach Rama: The minhag is to purchase wheat to distribute to poor people in advance of Pesach. Anyone who has been living in the community for 12 months must contribute to the communal fund for the poor. Key Points Rama uses the word \"minhag\" (custom) , but the Acharonim point out this appears to be a genuine obligation — it is recorded in the Talmud Yerushalmi as obligatory. Resolution: The obligation is to give tzedakah for Pesach. The minhag aspect is the specific practice of going and buying actual food/wheat rather than just giving money. Why specifically Pesach? Pesach is recognised as the most expensive of the Chagim — we don't find a parallel halacha before Sukkot or Shavuot. The Name \"Kimcha DePischa\" (קמחא דפסחא) Literally: \"the flour of Pesach.\" Development of the practice: Originally: buy wheat for the poor (Rama) Then: give flour Current practice: give matzot themselves (Mishnah Berurah) Any form of tzedakah given for Pesach fulfils this obligation. Who Is Obligated? Someone who has lived in the community for 12 months . Someone who has moved to the community with intent to live there permanently — even if less than 12 months. A person visiting just for Pesach — lesser obligation, though people should still support them. Priorities in Giving There is an obligation to focus first on the poor within your own town/community . Giving to organisations and Torah institutions worldwide is commendable, but it needs to start at home , especially before Pesach. Se'if 2 (continued) – Practices During the Month of Nisan No Tachanun the Entire Month Reasons for the festive character of Nisan: First 12 days: In the time of the Mishkan, the inauguration (Chanukat HaMishkan) took place — each Nasi brought his sacrifice daily, making each day a Yom Tov. 8 days of Pesach follow. Since the majority of the month (20 out of 30 days) is already festive, we extend it to the entire month. Midrash: The inauguration of the future Beit HaMikdash will take place at the end of Nisan, after Pesach. Specific Practices Omitted No Tachanun the entire month of Nisan. No Tzidkatecha at Shabbat Mincha — because Tzidkatecha is a form of Tzidduk HaDin (acceptance of divine judgement / remembrance of the dead) for three Tzaddikim who died on Shabbat (Moshe, David, and possibly Shlomo). Inappropriate during the festive month. No public eulogies — in olden days, eulogies were intensely emotional events with crying and chest-beating; this is forbidden during Nisan. No public fasts announced during Nisan. Fasting in Nisan — Detailed Rules Situation Shulchan Aruch Rama Private fixed fast (e.g., every Mon/Thu) Permitted — may continue in Nisan Forbidden — no fasting at all, even private, even for parents' yahrzeit Public fast Forbidden Forbidden Ta'anit Chalom (bad dream) Permitted — one may even fast on Shabbat for this Permitted Compensation fast after fasting on Shabbat Not required in Nisan — fasting in Nisan is less problematic than on Shabbat, so no compensatory fast needed Same Bechorot on Erev Pesach Discussed in a later siman — Stone Settings (Matzeivot) It is forbidden to schedule a stone setting (unveiling) during the month of Nisan. This is not widely known. A funeral must take place (with limited eulogy), but a stone setting — which involves eulogies and sadness — should not be fixed for a date during Nisan. Fasting on a Wedding Day in Nisan Some allow it as a private fast — this is the general Ashkenazi practice. Sephardi position: The minhag of the Sephardim is that it is actually wrong (not correct) for a Chatan to fast on his wedding day — the wedding day is a personal Yom Tov, making fasting inappropriate. Exception: If the Chatan is getting married at night, he may fast during the day and eat before the Chuppah. If getting married during the day — Sephardim do not fast. No Tzidduk HaDin at Funerals The Rama states: we refrain from Tzidduk HaDin at funerals during the entire month of Nisan. Mizmor LeTodah on Erev Pesach We do not say Mizmor LeTodah on Erev Pesach — because the Todah offering included bread (chametz), and since chametz could not be offered on Erev Pesach, we omit the psalm. Note: This is a minhag. Isru Chag (איסרו חג) Rama: It is customary to eat well on the day after each of the three festivals. This is called Isru Chag . Source: Gemara in Sukkot, expounding the pasuk from Hallel: אִסְרוּ חַג בַּעֲבֹתִים עַד קַרְנוֹת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ. Simple meaning: \"Bind the festival offering with ropes to the horns of the altar.\" Gemara's drash: אִסְרוּ חַג — \"tie yourself to the festival\" / stay attached to its spirit. בַּעֲבֹתִים — with \"fat\" animals (עבות = thick/fat), i.e., eat well. עַד קַרְנוֹת הַמִּזְבֵּחַ — as if one offered a sacrifice. Concept: After the spiritual high of the Chag, we take something mundane (a meal) and infuse it with the light of the Chag, carrying it into daily life. One should not fast on Isru Chag — not after Pesach, Shavuot, or Sukkot. Siman 430 – Shabbat HaGadol (שבת הגדול) Contains 1 Se'if Se'if 1 – Why It Is Called Shabbat HaGadol Shulchan Aruch: The Shabbat preceding Pesach is called Shabbat HaGadol because of the miracle (נס) that occurred on it. The Miracle When Bnei Yisrael left Mitzrayim on 15 Nisan (a Thursday) , the 10th of Nisan was a Shabbat . The Torah commands (Parashat Bo): בֶּעָשֹׂר לַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה — on the 10th of Nisan, each family was to take a sheep and prepare it for the Korban Pesach. The sheep was the deity of the Egyptians . The Jews took these sheep and tied them to their doorposts openly. The great miracle: Despite the Egyptians' fury, they could not harm the Jews. The dread of Hashem was already upon them. The Chachamim chose to commemorate this miracle on the Shabbat (not on the 10th of the month), even though Shabbat doesn't always fall on the 10th. Alternative Reason Some Acharonim say it is called \"HaGadol\" because the rabbi gives the longest (gadol) derashah of the year on this Shabbat. The Greeting of Shabbat HaGadol The Chida writes that the minhag of the Medakdikim (meticulous ones) of Yerushalayim is to greet each other with \"Shabbat HaGadol Shalom\" rather than the usual \"Shabbat Shalom U'Mevorach.\" This is the only special Shabbat (besides Shabbat Shuvah) where the greeting changes — despite other named Shabbatot existing (Shabbat Chazon, Shabbat Nachamu, etc.). When Erev Pesach Falls on Shabbat (This Year — 5785) Shabbat HaGadol itself is Erev Pesach — that Shabbat carries the title \"Shabbat HaGadol.\" However, the great derashah is given on the preceding Shabbat — because on Erev Pesach/Shabbat itself, it would be too late to teach people how to kasher utensils, etc. Everything else — including the Ashkenazi minhag of reading the Haggadah (see below) — remains on Erev Pesach/Shabbat HaGadol itself. Rama – Reading Part of the Haggadah on Shabbat HaGadol Ashkenazi minhag: Read part of the Haggadah on Shabbat HaGadol — from \"Avadim Hayinu\" until \"Lechaper Al Kol Avonoteinu.\" Reason: To commemorate the miracle of the 10th of Nisan. Vilna Gaon's reservation: He did not follow this minhag, since the Haggadah itself states the mitzvah of discussing the Exodus is specifically on Pesach night , not before. Result: Not all Ashkenazi communities follow this minhag, though many do. Siman 431 – The Time of Bedikat Chametz (בדיקת חמץ) Multiple Se'ifim discussed Se'if 1 – When and How to Check Shulchan Aruch: At the beginning of the night of the 14th (of Nisan), one checks for chametz by the light of a candle, in all the cracks and crevices of the house, in all places where it is customary (derech) to bring chametz . The Timing The mitzvah is to perform Bedikat Chametz as early as possible at the beginning of the night . This is more than the general principle of zerizin makdimin (the diligent perform mitzvot early). There is a specific din that the Chachamim instituted Bedikat Chametz to be at the very beginning of the night . This Year (5785): The 14th falls on Friday night (Shabbat), so Bedikat Chametz cannot be performed then. It is therefore brought forward to Thursday night. Why Bedikat Chametz Exists — Torah vs. Rabbinic Obligation Min HaTorah (Biblical level): A person can fulfil the obligation by simply nullifying (bitul) ownership of all chametz — sincerely declaring in one's heart that he relinquishes all property rights over any chametz he owns. The Torah prohibits: בַּל יֵרָאֶה וּבַל יִמָּצֵא — chametz should not \"be seen\" or \"be found\" that belongs to you . Once ownership is relinquished, even chametz physically present in your home is not a Torah violation. Seeing chametz that isn't yours (e.g., walking past a bakery on Pesach) — no prohibition at all. The Chachamim instituted Bedikat Chametz for two reasons: Sincerity concern: People may not genuinely relinquish ownership. The test: if your chametz were stolen over Pesach, would you truly feel no loss? If you'd claim insurance, the bitul wasn't sincere. Temptation concern: Even with sincere bitul, if chametz is physically present, a person may forget it's Pesach and come to eat, drink, or benefit from it. Therefore: The Chachamim required physical searching and removal of chametz — not merely verbal nullification. Note on Mechirat Chametz (selling chametz): The concept of selling chametz is a much later development . These halachot assume no sale — the obligation is to physically remove and destroy. Why Specifically at Night? Two reasons: Practical: Nighttime is when people are home from work and available. Functional: The contrast of darkness with candlelight allows one to see into corners and crevices more effectively than daylight alone. Using a Torch / Phone Flashlight The Poskim accept that a torch or phone flashlight works as \"or ha'ner\" (the light of the candle) for Bedikat Chametz. It has the same — if not better — illumination. Switching Off the Lights? Syrian minhag: Switch off electric lights and search by candlelight only. Justification: The contrast of darkness with the candle flame may help reveal chametz in corners (per Mishnah Berurah's reasoning for nighttime). Practical ruling: This is a nice minhag but not obligatory . If searching is more effective with lights on, that is perfectly fine. The Rav personally keeps lights on. What Needs to Be Checked? The rule: Only places where it is derech (customary) to bring chametz need to be checked. In Talmudic and Rishonic times, there was no concept of weeks-long Pesach cleaning . The entire search was done on the night of the 14th, taking several hours. Modern reality: Bigger homes, more appliances, more complexity — so people clean in advance. If you cleaned in advance: Areas checked before Pesach do not need rechecking on the night of the 14th, provided you are certain no chametz has entered since. \"Certain\" means: you know that no one has gone into that room/area since it was checked. Exception — Young Children: The Gemara compares a child to a mouse — they carry chametz from place to place. Any area within a child's reach must be rechecked on the night of Bedikat Chametz, even if previously cleaned. This includes: ground level, low drawers, anywhere a child could drop food while walking around. No need to check: Chandeliers, toilets, tops of bedroom cupboards, attics — anywhere chametz is never brought or stored . If there is a strict household policy of no food in certain areas (e.g., upstairs bedrooms), those areas don't require checking. Exception: With young children, you must check everywhere they can reach — even toilets. Restrictions Before Bedikat Chametz — Not Beginning Activities General principle (found in many areas of halacha): When the time for a mitzvah arrives, one may not begin activities that could cause one to forget. Shulchan Aruch: From the time of Bedikat Chametz (nightfall, or half an hour before for eating), one may not begin a new meal, activity, or Torah study . If one began before the time arrived — one may continue (began b'heter). Mishnah Berurah — Stricter View: There is a special din in Bedikat Chametz beyond the regular rule. Since the Chachamim's takanah was specifically for the beginning of the night , even having something small to eat is problematic once the time arrives. One should begin Bedikat Chametz immediately . Rama — Even Stricter: Even if one began eating or learning before the time — one must stop when the time for Bedikah arrives. This is unique to Bedikat Chametz — in all other areas of halacha, if you began before the time b'heter, you may continue. This underscores the special emphasis on beginning Bedikat Chametz right at the start of the night. Arvit vs. Bedikat Chametz — Which Comes First? Halacha (Sephardi psak): Arvit first, then Bedikat Chametz. Question raised: With Chanukah, we learned that candle-lighting comes before Arvit because the mitzvah is specifically at the beginning of the night. Why not the same here? Two answers: The \"beginning of the night\" aspect of Bedikat Chametz is debatable — the Shulchan Aruch mentions it but also says one need not stop a prior activity (unlike Chanukah where you do stop). Bedikat Chametz takes hours , so there is a real concern that if done first, one will forget or be too tired for Arvit. Chanukah candle-lighting takes one minute — no such concern. Beginning from Sunset (Shkiyah) In England (and other northern locations), nightfall (tzet hakochavim) can be late — around 8:15–8:30 PM in mid-April. There is room to begin Bedikat Chametz from shkiyah (sunset) , provided the search continues into actual nightfall. This leniency is especially relevant for families with young children who want to participate. Siman 432 – The Bracha on Bedikat Chametz (ברכת בדיקת חמץ) Multiple Se'ifim discussed Se'if 1 – The Wording of the Bracha: \"Al Biur Chametz\" Shulchan Aruch: Before beginning Bedikat Chametz, one recites the bracha: \"…אשר קדשנו במצוותיו וציוונו על בִּעוּר חמץ\" — \"…Who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us concerning the destruction of chametz .\" Why \"Biur\" and Not \"Bedikat\"? The question: We are about to search for chametz (bedikah), not destroy it (biur). Why say \"al biur chametz\"? Answer: Bedikat Chametz is only a means to an end . The bedikah has no intrinsic purpose — it exists solely to enable the biur (destruction) the next morning. If one searches the entire house meticulously but never destroys the chametz found — the whole exercise was pointless. The Chachamim instituted the search so that one can then properly destroy. Therefore, the bracha is formulated on the end purpose (biur) , not the intermediate step (bedikah). The Word \"Al\" (על) vs. \"Le-\" (ל) We say \"al biur\" (על ביעור) — \"concerning\" the destruction — not \"le-va'er\" (לבער) — \"to destroy.\" \"Le-\" would imply: \"I am about to do this right now.\" \"Al\" implies: \"concerning this matter\" — it is going to happen (tomorrow), and the bedikah tonight is part of that process. The Meaning of \"Biur\" Sephardi understanding: Biur means destruction / getting rid of — by any means (burning, crumbling, flushing, etc.). Ashkenazi understanding: Biur means specifically burning — which is why Ashkenazim are particular to burn their chametz. Unique Aspect — Bracha Now, Fulfilment Later Question raised in class: This seems unprecedented — the bracha is said at night, but the biur doesn't happen until ~12 hours later the next morning. Resolution: The bracha is technically going on the bedikah itself — that is the mitzvah being performed now. We simply use the word \"biur\" because the bedikah is a means toward the biur. There is no hefsek (interruption), because the mitzvah act (searching) begins immediately after the bracha. A Person Without a Home Discussion: Does a homeless person perform Bedikat Chametz? On his pockets? The takanah of the Chachamim was for homes and dwellings — not pockets. While we do check pockets as well (agav / incidentally), there was no formal institution for checking pockets. This remains a matter of debate among the Poskim. Se'if 2 – Forgetting the Bracha Shulchan Aruch: If one forgot to say the bracha before beginning, one may say it at any point during the search, as long as one has not yet finished — because searching the entire home is one continuous mitzvah. Example: If you've searched half the house and then remember — say the bracha and continue searching the remaining half. Se'if 3 – Speaking During Bedikat Chametz Shulchan Aruch: From the bracha until beginning to search: One must not speak at all (no hefsek between bracha and start of the mitzvah). After beginning the search: One should ideally not speak until completing the entire search, in order to maintain focus and attention. In practice: Once at least one room/area has been searched, the bracha has \"landed\" on something. If necessary, one may speak — but it is better not to. What Speech Is Permitted? Anything related to the search: \"Has this cupboard been checked?\" / \"What is this ingredient?\" / \"Check behind there\" — all permitted; it is part of the bedikah. Unrelated speech (phone calls, casual conversation): Should be avoided. If urgent, one may take a phone call — but try to minimise. Summary of Key Topics Covered Siman Topic 429:1 Obligation to study Hilchot Pesach 30 days before; begins on Purim; Rambam's two distinct dinim 429:2 (Rama) Kimcha DePischa — obligation to give tzedakah/food before Pesach; who is obligated (12-month resident) 429:2 (cont.) Month of Nisan: no Tachanun, no eulogies, fasting rules, stone settings, wedding-day fasting (Sephardi view), Isru Chag 430:1 Shabbat HaGadol — the miracle of 10 Nisan; the derashah; greeting \"Shabbat HaGadol Shalom\"; this year's special case 430 (Rama) Ashkenazi minhag to read Haggadah on Shabbat HaGadol; Vilna Gaon's reservation 431:1 Bedikat Chametz — timing (beginning of the night); Torah vs. rabbinic obligation; bitul vs. physical search; why at night; candle/torch/lights; what to check; children; restrictions before bedikah; Arvit vs. Bedikah 432:1 Bracha of \"Al Biur Chametz\" — why not \"Bedikat\"; biur = means vs. end; forgetting the bracha; speaking during the search","53HQvYcoi2A":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Siman: 318 (Bishul — Cooking on Shabbat) Seif: ד (Dalet) — Continued Topics: Bishul Achar Bishul B'Lach (continued), The Rema's Position, Sefardi/Ashkenazi Interactions, Hotplates on Timers, Kli Sheni, Irui Kli Rishon, and Kalei HaBishul Recap: Where We Are in Siman 318 Seifim 1–2: Ma'aseh Shabbat — the penalty/consequences of cooking on Shabbat (intentional and unintentional), who may benefit and when. Written in the context of bishul but applies to all melachot. Seif 3: Toldot Ha'Or vs. Toldot HaChamah — the paradox that cooking directly in the sun is permitted, but cooking with something heated by the sun is forbidden. Applications: dud shemesh, microwave. Seif 4 (Part 1 — previous week): Machloket #1: Cooking from Ma'achal Ben Drosai to fully cooked — Rambam (asur de'oraita) vs. Rosh (mutar). Shulchan Aruch rules like Rambam. Machloket #2: Bishul Achar Bishul B'Lach — Rambam (ein bishul achar bishul even for liquids) vs. Rosh (yesh bishul achar bishul for liquids). Shulchan Aruch rules like the Rosh. Defining solid vs. liquid for mixed dishes (Chacham Ovadia: go by the nature/name of the dish). Bishul Achar Bishul B'Lach — Continued The Rema's Position (Ashkenazi Ruling) The Rema does not comment in Seif Dalet itself, but addresses this in Seif ט\"ו (15) : דכל שאינו נתנו על גבי האש או הכירה ממש רק סמוך לו אפילו נצטנן מותר Some say that if a liquid has cooled down, you may reheat it because there is no bishul achar bishul for liquids. ונהגו להקל בזה אם לא נצטנן לגמרי The Rema rules: The minhag of Ashkenazim is to be lenient — you may reheat a liquid, but only if it has not fully cooled down. Analysis: Whose Opinion Is This? The Rema's position does not precisely follow either side: Opinion Ruling Rambam Even if fully cold, you may reheat — ein bishul achar bishul for liquids Rosh / Shulchan Aruch If cooled down below yad soledet bo (45°C), it is subject to further bishul — asur Rema Compromise — you may reheat if it hasn't fully cooled down (still lukewarm / poshrim) What Does \"Not Fully Cooled Down\" Mean? For Sefardim: \"Cooled down\" = below yad soledet bo (45°C). Below 45°C, a liquid certainly cannot be put back on the hotplate. For the Rema: \"Not fully cooled down\" = poshrim (lukewarm) — approximately 27–30°C. The key test: you can still sense that this was once hot. The Chazon Ish's Explanation of the Rema The Chazon Ish explains that the Rema is fundamentally ruling like the Rambam — that a cooled liquid is not subject to further bishul. However: With a solid that has cooled, you can see it has been cooked (its form has changed), so no one will suspect you of cooking for the first time. With a liquid that has fully cooled, an observer might think you are heating it for the first time. Therefore, the Rema adds a derabanan-level restriction: only reheat if the liquid is still lukewarm, so it's evident it was previously heated. Implication: If an Ashkenazi reheated a completely cold liquid, it would not be an issur de'oraita — only a violation of this derabanan concern. Practical Summary: Sefardi vs. Ashkenazi Scenario Sefardi (Shulchan Aruch) Ashkenazi (Rema) Liquid still above 45°C (yad soledet bo) See discussion below Mutar to reheat Liquid lukewarm (27–30°C, poshrim) Asur — below yad soledet bo Mutar — still \"not fully cooled\" Liquid fully cold Asur Asur (derabanan) Can a Sefardi Ask an Ashkenazi to Reheat a Liquid? The Question If Sefardim and Ashkenazim are eating together and the soup has cooled, can the Sefardi ask his Ashkenazi friend to put it on the hotplate (since for the Ashkenazi, if it's still lukewarm, it's permitted)? The Comparison to Early Shabbat Acceptance One might compare this to the well-known halacha about early Shabbat acceptance: If you accepted Shabbat early (e.g., 7:30 PM in summer) but it's still permissible for others (Shabbat doesn't start until 9:15 PM), you may ask someone who hasn't accepted Shabbat to do melachah for you. Reason: You had the potential to do the melachah at that time — you voluntarily gave it up by accepting Shabbat early. Why It's Different Here The Tzitz Eliezer and Chacham Ovadia both rule: A Sefardi may NOT ask an Ashkenazi to reheat liquid. With early Shabbat: you had the potentiality to do the melachah — you chose to accept Shabbat early. With bishul achar bishul b'lach: the Sefardi never has the potential to reheat the liquid. The Shulchan Aruch's ruling means it is always forbidden for a Sefardi on Shabbat. Therefore, you cannot ask someone else to do what you could never do yourself. What Is Permitted If the Ashkenazi of his own accord heats it up for himself, the Sefardi may partake. If the Ashkenazi heated it and the Sefardi benefits without any communication, hint, or implication — it is permitted. But asking, hinting, or implying that the Ashkenazi should reheat it — asur. Hotplates on Timers — Chacham Ovadia's Ruling The Problem If yesh bishul achar bishul b'lach, a Sefardi cannot take soup from the fridge and put it on a hotplate on Shabbat morning. The Solution: Place Before the Hotplate Turns On Chacham Ovadia rules: You may take the soup out of the fridge and place it on the hotplate before the hotplate turns on (via a timer). When the timer activates, it heats the soup. Why This Is Permitted Multiple factors combine: Gramma (indirect causation): The heating is not immediate — it happens when the timer activates later. This is an indirect cause. Gramma is a machloket Rishonim: Most Rishonim hold gramma is mutar outright. At worst, gramma is derabanan. Combined with the machloket on bishul achar bishul b'lach: Since according to the Rambam there is no bishul achar bishul for liquids, and gramma at worst is derabanan, we can combine the leniencies. Gramma b'makom mitzvah / hefsed: Gramma in the context of need is universally permitted. Important: This leniency does NOT apply to completely uncooked food — only to liquids that were previously cooked. Practical Application If the hotplate timer is set to turn on at 11:00 AM — place your liquid dishes on the hotplate at 10:00 AM before going to tefillah. When it turns on, it heats them permissibly. Asking a Non-Jew to Reheat Liquids If the hotplate is already on and you need to heat liquids: You may ask a non-Jew to place the liquid on the hotplate. Normally, asking a non-Jew to do melachah on Shabbat is forbidden (amira l'akum). However, the Chida writes: when the prohibition itself is subject to a machloket (here, the Rambam permits it), you may rely on the lenient opinion to ask a non-Jew. Since according to the Rambam reheating liquids is permitted, you may ask a non-Jew explicitly to heat the liquid, even though we (Sefardim) don't follow that ruling ourselves. The Biur Halacha: Pouring from an Urn in Two Stages The Scenario You're pouring hot water from an urn (kli rishon) into a cup. Halfway through, you stop (someone calls you). You come back 10 seconds later. Can you continue pouring into the same cup? The Problem (Pri Megadim, cited in Biur Halacha) The water you poured into the cup is now kli sheni water. כלי שני אינו מבשל — kli sheni cannot cook, meaning this water has \"lost\" its cooked status in a certain sense. When you now pour more water from the urn (kli rishon) into the cup, you are pouring kli rishon water onto kli sheni water. According to the Shulchan Aruch (yesh bishul achar bishul b'lach), this is problematic — you are \"reheating\" the kli sheni water. The Ruling According to the Pri Megadim and the Biur Halacha: You should not do this. When pouring from an urn into a cup, pour in one continuous stream — do not stop and resume. Support from Rabbeinu Yona (Beit Yosef, Siman 253) Rabbeinu Yona addresses a related case: Can you take hot water from the urn and pour it into your cholent/hamin that is drying out? Ruling (Chacham Ovadia follows this): You may not. When the water leaves the urn and enters a cup/vessel, it becomes kli sheni. Pouring kli sheni water back into the hamin (kli rishon on the fire) means you are putting \"uncookable\" water into a cooking vessel — this is bishul achar bishul b'lach. Practical Implications Pouring from an urn: Pour in one continuous stream into the cup. Do not stop and resume. Adding water to cholent/hamin: Cannot pour water from the urn via a cup into the hamin (kli sheni water into kli rishon). There may be theoretical workarounds like a direct stream from the urn tap into the hamin, but there are practical issues including kashrus concerns of ze'ah (steam making the urn meaty). A Drop of Water in a Cup If a cup has been washed and has a few tiny drops of cold water at the bottom, you may still pour hot water into it. Multiple leniencies apply: melachah she'einah tzrichah l'gufah (you have no interest in \"cooking\" those drops), the amount is negligible, and tata'ah gavar considerations (discussed later). Even Ashkenazi poskim (Shevet HaLevi / Rav Wosner) permit this. Irui Kli Rishon — Pouring from a Kli Rishon The Major Machloket: Rabbeinu Tam vs. Rashbam This machloket appears in 4–5 places throughout Shas (Tosfot in Shabbat, Kodashim, Pesachim, etc.): Position Held By Ruling Irui kli rishon is like a kli rishon — it cooks Rabbeinu Tam Pouring from urn onto raw tea leaves = issur de'oraita Irui kli rishon is like a kli sheni — it does not cook Rashbam Pouring from urn onto raw tea leaves = mutar Halacha follows Rabbeinu Tam: Irui kli rishon is forbidden. This is the ruling in Shabbat, in Kashrut (Yoreh De'ah), and in Hilchot Pesach. Kli Sheni — The Major Ashkenazi/Sefardi Divide The Mishna (Shabbat 145b / discussed on 39a) כל שבא בחמין מלפני שבת שורין אותו בחמין בשבת — Anything heated before Shabbat may be soaked in hot water on Shabbat. וכל שלא בא בחמין מלפני שבת מדיחין אותו בחמין בשבת — Anything NOT heated before Shabbat may be rinsed with hot water on Shabbat. חוץ מן המליח הישן וקוליס האיספנין שהדחתן זו היא גמר מלאכתן — Except for old salted items and small salted fish (sardines), for which rinsing alone completes their cooking. Three Ways to Learn This Mishna 1. According to Rashbam (We do NOT follow this) \"You can rinse\" = irui from a kli rishon (since Rashbam holds irui kli rishon doesn't cook). \"Except the fish\" = you can't even do irui kli rishon on these. Kli sheni never cooks — not even for delicate items. 2. According to Rabbeinu Tam — First Answer of Tosfot (Ri) Since irui kli rishon does cook, \"you can rinse\" must mean from a kli sheni . Why say \"rinse\" (pour from kli sheni) and not \"soak\" (put in kli sheni)? Because you cannot put things in a kli sheni — forbidden derabanan because מחזי כמבשל (it looks like cooking). Major chiddush: Although כלי שני אינו מבשל de'oraita, it is forbidden derabanan because it appears like cooking. 3. According to Rabbeinu Tam — Second Answer of Tosfot \"You can rinse\" means from a kli sheni, AND you could also soak in a kli sheni. The Mishna says \"rinse\" only to set up the exception about the fish (chiddush of the seifa). No issue of mechzi k'mevashel — kli sheni is fully permitted. The Halachic Ruling Ashkenazim (Mishnah Berurah) Follow the first answer of Tosfot : Kli rishon: Cooks — forbidden de'oraita Irui kli rishon: Cooks — forbidden de'oraita (Rabbeinu Tam) Kli sheni: Does not cook de'oraita, but forbidden derabanan (מחזי כמבשל) Practical result: Cannot put tea leaves, a tea bag, or most items into a kli sheni. Salt, sugar, and certain spices may be permitted. Sefardim (Chacham Ovadia) Follow the second answer of Tosfot : Kli rishon: Cooks — forbidden de'oraita Irui kli rishon: Cooks — forbidden de'oraita (Rabbeinu Tam) Kli sheni: Does NOT cook and no issue of mechzi k'mevashel — fully permitted The concept of mechzi k'mevashel is not found in the Gemara; it is a chiddush of Tosfot that we do not accept Additionally, even if it existed, it would only be derabanan — safek derabanan l'kula Practical result: May pour water from the urn into a cup and then put a tea bag into it. (Cannot pour directly from the urn onto the tea bag — that's irui kli rishon.) Kalei HaBishul — Things Easily Cooked The Concept The Mishna's exception — old salted items and small salted fish — introduces kalei habishul : certain extremely delicate/sensitive items that can be cooked even in a kli sheni. The Rambam's Expansion לאו דוקא אלו דהוא הדין לכל כיוצא בזה דבר דק ורך ביותר Not only the specific items mentioned. Anything extremely soft, thin, and sensitive to heat is classified as kalei habishul. Examples mentioned by poskim: Raw eggs, very thin salted fish (sardines), items salted for over a year, possibly tea leaves. Rules for Kalei HaBishul Even according to those who permit kli sheni generally: Cannot be put in a kli sheni Cannot have irui from a kli sheni poured onto them CAN be put in a kli shlishi (third vessel) — see below Kli Shlishi — The Debate Position Held By Ruling No distinction between kli sheni and kli shlishi — forbidden even in kli shlishi as long as yad soledet bo Chazon Ish Asur in kli shlishi Kli shlishi is permitted even for kalei habishul Rav Moshe Feinstein Mutar in kli shlishi Halacha: Virtually everyone — both Sefardim and Ashkenazim — follows Rav Moshe Feinstein . Kli shlishi is permitted for everything, including kalei habishul. The Additional Ashkenazi Stringency Beyond mechzi k'mevashel, Ashkenazi poskim add: since we don't know exactly which items qualify as kalei habishul, assume everything might be (except items the Gemara explicitly says are hard to cook, such as raw meat and cinnamon). Combined with the Chazon Ish's view, this would create problems even in a kli shlishi — but in practice, most Ashkenazim follow Rav Moshe that kli shlishi resolves the issue. Shulchan Aruch Text — Seif Dalet (Full) תבשיל שנתבשל כל צרכו יש בו משום בישול אם נצטנן — A fully cooked dish is subject to bishul if it has cooled down. ואם לא נתבשל כל צרכו אפילו נתבשל כמאכל בן דרוסאי שייך בו בישול אפילו בעודו רותח — If not fully cooked, even if ma'achal Ben Drosai, bishul applies even while still hot. והני מילי שיש בישול אחר בישול בתבשיל שיש בו מרק — This applies to dishes with liquid. אבל דבר שנתבשל כבר ויבש מותר ליתנו בחמין בשבת — But a dry, fully cooked item may be placed in hot water on Shabbat. ואם הוא דבר יבש שלא נתבשל מערב שבת אין שורין אותו בחמין בשבת — If dry and NOT cooked before Shabbat, you may not soak it in hot water. אבל מדיחין אותו בחמין בשבת — But you may rinse it with hot water [from a kli sheni]. חוץ מן המליח הישן ומן הדג שנקרא קוליס האיספנין שהן צריכים בישול מעט — Except for old salted items and sardine-type fish, which need only minimal cooking [kalei habishul]. Rema's addition: דוקא מצטמק ויפה לו — Bishul achar bishul b'lach only applies if further heating improves it. Summary Table: Kli Rishon, Irui, Kli Sheni, Kli Shlishi Level Status Sefardi Ashkenazi Kli Rishon (on the fire) Cooks — asur de'oraita Asur Asur Irui Kli Rishon (pouring from kli rishon) Cooks — asur de'oraita (Rabbeinu Tam) Asur Asur Kli Sheni (cup filled from urn) Does not cook de'oraita Mutar (no mechzi k'mevashel) Asur derabanan (mechzi k'mevashel) Irui Kli Sheni (pouring from kli sheni) Less potent than kli sheni Mutar Mutar (for non-kalei habishul) Kli Sheni for kalei habishul Can cook delicate items Asur Asur Kli Shlishi (even kalei habishul) Permitted Mutar Mutar (Rav Moshe; Chazon Ish disagrees) Key Practical Takeaways Sefardim: Cannot reheat a liquid that has cooled below 45°C. Ashkenazim: Can reheat if still lukewarm (~27–30°C). A Sefardi may not ask an Ashkenazi to reheat liquid — but may benefit if the Ashkenazi did so independently. Hotplate on a timer: A Sefardi may place cold liquid dishes on the hotplate before it turns on (gramma combined with machloket). Asking a non-Jew: If the hotplate is already on, a Sefardi may ask a non-Jew to place liquid on it. Pouring from an urn: Pour in one continuous stream . Do not stop and resume. Adding water to cholent: Cannot pour water from the urn via a cup into the hamin. Kli sheni: Sefardim may put a tea bag into a kli sheni. Ashkenazim may not. Kalei habishul: Cannot be placed in a kli sheni. Can be placed in a kli shlishi (virtually all poskim). Preview: Upcoming Topics Seif ה (Heh): Is there bishul after baking/roasting? (יש בישול אחר אפייה?) — implications for tea, coffee, and instant products. Detailed laws of tea and coffee preparation on Shabbat. Further details on irui kli rishon (Seif Yud). Purim Sameach and Shabbat Shalom","lTLQSKpCkYY":"Sefer: Ha-Maspik L'Ovdei Hashem by Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam Scope: Completion of Perek Aleph, Perek Bet (full), and beginning of Perek Gimel Background & Recap The sefer has 13 chapters , some much longer than others. The first three are relatively short and serve as introductory material. Previous shiurim covered a biography of Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam and began Chapter 1. Chapter 1 (Perek Aleph) – Completion Recap: Two Paths of Serving Hashem Rabbi Avraham establishes two fundamental paths of divine service: Derech Ha'Am HaGluyah (The Revealed Path): The basic, universal path — observance of mitzvot. This is straightforward and doesn't have as many gradations. Either you observe or you don't (though there is some range in how you perform mitzvot). The Intimate / Lofty Path (Derech HaSegula): Going beyond the letter of the law. This is the subject of the entire sefer. Within this path, there are many levels and gradations . The analogy given previously: three people all keeping Shabbat with extra intention, yet the gap between their levels is vast. Key Point: You Cannot Skip Steps Core Principle: Before pursuing the intimate, lofty path, one must first master the basics. The Vilna Gaon's Interpretation of the Pasuk: \"V'lo ta'aleh b'ma'alot al mizbechi\" (Shemot 20:23) — the altar had to be a ramp, not steps. The Vilna Gaon explains: Don't \"jump up steps\" — don't skip rungs on the ladder of spiritual growth. It must be like a ramp: gradual, steady ascent. Personal Anecdote — The Siddur HaRashash: The teacher recalled encountering someone who prayed from the Siddur HaRashash , a kabbalistic siddur where each word of the Amidah fills an entire page of meditations (each Amidah taking about an hour). He was deeply impressed — until he saw that same person flagrantly violating basic mitzvot. This illustrated the core problem: engaging in lofty spiritual practices while neglecting fundamentals is hollow. The Text Inside Hebrew: \"V'lifnei shenatchil bazot… ki hashkafah nechonah v'haseder hana'ot mechayevim linkoт techilah b'derech hagluyah.\" Translation/Explanation: Before discussing the intimate path, we must state: a proper outlook requires one to first take the basic, revealed path. Key Passage: \"Ki da'ut gasah hi\" — It is a brazen, terrible mistake — \"sheyeha ploni muchzak chasid b'einav u'v'einei zulato\" — for a person to consider himself a Chasid (or be considered one by others) just because he: Doesn't marry (delays marriage) Fasts excessively Abstains from food Wears uncomfortable woolen garments …while simultaneously transgressing positive and negative commandments . Terminology Reminder In Rabbi Avraham's lexicon: Tzadik = one who keeps the mitzvot properly Chasid = one who goes beyond the exact requirements of the mitzvot \"V'lo Am Ha'aretz Chasid\" Rabbi Avraham quotes Chazal: An ignorant person (Am Ha'aretz) cannot be a Chasid. An Am Ha'aretz will inevitably violate mitzvot due to ignorance. If one claims ignorance as an excuse, this itself proves a violation of Talmud Torah (the obligation to study), which is the most important mitzvah. Why Learn This If We Haven't Perfected the Basics? The teacher raised the honest question: shouldn't we focus on fixing our basic observance before studying these lofty topics? Answer: At minimum, we gain aspirations . If you aspire high, you reach reasonably high. If you aspire to mediocrity, you fall below even that. Learning about the lofty path sets the bar we aim for. Clarification on \"Not Marrying\" Rabbi Avraham does not promote celibacy. He himself married and had two sons (David and Ovadia). His son clarified in a footnote: the custom was for some to delay marriage to focus on Torah study, a concept found in the Gemara. Another interpretation: they would marry but spend time away studying, returning home only for Shabbat (sleeping in the Beit Midrash during the week). Connection to Hilchot Shabbat: The concept of a \"temporary knot\" (up to 7 days) is derived from this practice — Talmidei Chachamim would only untie their shoes upon returning home Friday afternoon. Critical caveat: Any such arrangement must be done with the spouse's consent and happiness . Neglecting spousal obligations while pursuing \"chassidut\" defeats the purpose. Rabbeinu Bahya's Mashal (from Chovot HaLevavot) Rabbi Avraham quotes Rabbeinu Bahya ibn Pakuda: Extra prayers are like a gift (matanah). Obligatory prayers are like paying a debt (pir'on chov). A creditor will not accept a gift from someone who hasn't paid their debt. Practical application: Men are obligated to pray three times a day; women one or two. The Gemara says you can pray additional Amidot on weekdays (adding something new each time). But there's no point in extra prayers if you don't pray Shacharit, Mincha, and Arvit at their proper times. Analogy: If you owe someone £100 and bring them a silver dish worth £150, they'll say: \"That's lovely — but give me my £100 first.\" A New Interpretation: \"V'lo Yikach Shochad\" (Devarim 10:17) The problem: What does it mean that \"Hashem doesn't take a bribe\"? How would one bribe God? Existing approaches: Rashi: You can't appease God with money. Ramban & Sforno: Shochad mitzvot — you can't \"bribe\" God by doing many mitzvot to cancel out sins. Each mitzvah is rewarded; each sin must be dealt with separately. No \"netting.\" Rabbi Avraham's novel interpretation: V'lo yikach shochad means Hashem doesn't accept \"gifts\" (extra spiritual practices) when you haven't fulfilled the basic obligations. The Prophet Yeshayahu's Rebuke Rabbi Avraham cites Yeshayahu (Chapter 58): \"Call out loudly… declare to My people their transgressions… They seek Me daily, they desire to know My ways, like a nation that performed righteousness… they ask Me for righteous judgments, they desire closeness to God.\" The people want to be close to God and seek Him — but they haven't addressed their basic sins. What's the point of seeking closeness while violating fundamentals? The Psychology of Shortcuts Why do people skip the basics? Keeping all the mitzvot daily is hard, unglamorous work . Extra fasting, learning Kabbalah, extra prayers give a spiritual \"high\" — they feel good and dramatic. It's the \"easy path\" — a shortcut to feeling spiritual without doing the hard daily grind. But there are no shortcuts in Torah. The Meshech Chochmah makes a similar point regarding Har Sinai. Student's comment (Simon Cohen): This is exactly the pattern — someone replaces the difficult daily commitment (e.g., praying three times a day) with a dramatic one-off (e.g., a big fast). Rabbi Avraham says: no, the basics come first, even though they're less exciting. Key distinction: There's a difference between things that make you feel good individually vs. your actual obligations . Extreme Examples Rabbi Avraham drives the point home with stark examples: Ten years of fasting can be spiritually nullified by one act of eating stolen food . Years of wearing uncomfortable clothes (as abstinence) can be lost by wearing a four-cornered garment without tzitzit . Years of living as a hermit in the mountains can be lost by living in a house without a mezuzah . Important caveat: This doesn't mean the mitzvot literally \"cancel out\" — what you've done is done. But the spiritual growth and closeness to Hashem that the extra practices were building can be undone by neglecting basics. If it happened by mistake ( b'shogeg or b'ones ), one can ask for forgiveness. Story of Abba Chilkiya Abba Chilkiya was the grandson of Choni HaMa'agel (who famously drew a circle and demanded rain). He and his wife had reached such a level that their joint prayers for rain were answered. When he returned from a journey, his wife came out adorned and attractive . The Chachamim asked why. He explained: so that he wouldn't even be tempted to look at another woman . Not that anyone suspected him — but out of fear of the Yetzer Hara . Even the greatest can stumble, and one stumble could destroy years of spiritual growth. Lesson: The Chachamim went to extraordinary lengths to protect against basic sins, because all their \"extra\" spiritual achievements depended on maintaining the foundation. The Rambam Connection (Doron's Insight) A student (Doron) connected this to the Rambam's broader philosophy: The Rambam teaches that the purpose of mitzvot is personal development — character (through mitzvot) and intellect (through Torah). If someone does \"extra\" spiritual practices but then violates basics, it reveals they were never truly at that level . They hadn't actually developed; they just experienced a temporary high. It's like a drug-induced high — you feel elevated, but it's not who you actually are. Real growth is internal and permanent. This is why Rabbi Avraham says \"yitkapchu lecha\" (it will be lost): It's not that mitzvot cancel out — it's that you were never really there. The fast wasn't real growth if the basics aren't in place. Chapter 1 Conclusion Rabbi Avraham's closing statement: \"If you aspire to the level of the select few, to walk the intimate path with Hashem — the ways of the Chassidim, the ways of the prophets — you must first begin on the basic revealed path and fulfill it sincerely and completely. Do not neglect anything you are obligated in. Only then may you begin the unique path.\" Summary of Chapter 1: Two paths: Basic (Tzadik) and Intimate (Chasid). The Chasid path has many nuances and levels. Do not attempt to be a Chasid before being a Tzadik. At minimum, we can aspire — aiming high brings us higher than aiming low. Go slowly up the ramp. No shortcuts. Chapter 2 (Perek Bet) – Interconnection of Middot This is a very short, introductory chapter. Core Teaching: Good Middot Are Interconnected \"Da ki hamidot hatromiyot keruchot zo b'zo\" — All good character traits are interrelated, interconnected, and spring from one another . Training in one midah serves as preparation and strengthening for another. Examples of Interconnected Traits Trait A Trait B Connection Compassion ( Rachmanut ) Generosity ( Nedivut ) The more generous you are, the easier it is to be merciful — and vice versa. A stingy person struggles with mercy. Compassion ( Rachmanut ) Patience / Slow to Anger ( Orech Apayim ) A calm person can be merciful; a merciful person is generally slow to anger. A cruel person gets angry quickly. Patience ( Orech Apayim ) Humility ( Anavah ) The more humble, the calmer; the calmer, the more humble. Abstinence ( Prishut ) Contentment with Little ( Histapkut B'mu'at ) These naturally go together. Fighting the Yetzer Hara Trust in Hashem ( Bitachon ) Interrelated — trust strengthens one's ability to resist temptation. Practical observation: You find generous people are merciful, calm people are humble, those content with little can abstain from excess — all consistently. The Reason: A Unified Purpose \"Sibat hadavar hi ki kol derachim halalu tachilitam achat\" — The reason all middot are connected is that they all serve one ultimate purpose : becoming the best version of oneself in order to have a relationship with Hakadosh Baruch Hu. The Mishnah: Lev Tov (A Good Heart) Rabbi Avraham cites the famous Mishnah in Avot: Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai asked his five students: What is the best character trait? Various answers were given (a good eye, etc.) Rabbi Elazar ben Arach said: Lev Tov — a good heart. Rabbi Yochanan ben Zakkai agreed: \"I prefer his words, for included in his words are all of yours.\" A \"good heart\" is the umbrella trait — it encompasses everything. All middot development ultimately aims at making you a genuinely good person. Connection to the Omer Lev (לב) = 32, Tov (טוב) = 17 → Total = 49 — the gematria matches the 49 days of the Omer. The purpose of the Omer period is to work on middot, one each day — character development through Pirkei Avot and focused self-improvement. Purim is a week away; Pesach five weeks after that; the Omer begins in five weeks. Practical Takeaway The teacher urged: don't save middot-work for Elul alone. Think about it weekly, even daily: What am I doing this week to develop patience ? What am I doing to develop compassion or generosity ? What one thing can I do this week to develop humility ? Just being conscious of these things makes a difference. Derech vs. Pegiyah (Path vs. Encounter) Rabbi Avraham distinguishes between two stages: Derech (Path): The training phase — working on middot during our entire lives. This is where we are. Pegiyah (Encounter): The ultimate goal — devekut (cleaving) to Hashem. The moment of true spiritual communion. Some middot relate directly to God's attributes (compassion, generosity, patience — we can speak of God as merciful, generous, slow to anger). Working on these is both derech (path) and approaching pegiyah (encounter), because we are emulating God's ways. Other middot (e.g., abstinence, asceticism) don't relate to God's attributes — they are only derech , tools on the path. The mitzvah of Devekut: \"U'vo tidbak\" — Cleave to God (Devarim 10:20). The Chachamim ask: how can the physical cleave to the spiritual? Answer: by emulating His ways ( Imitatio Dei ). The more similar we become to God in character, the closer the relationship — just as in human life, we bond with those similar to us. Chapter 2 Conclusion \"V'atah korei, achar she'heivanta v'hiskalta… shit libcha v'da'atcha v'shkod al haperakim haba'im\" — Now that you understand these first two chapters, pay close attention to the chapters ahead, about each of the elevated middot, which is the purpose of the entire Torah (as the Rambam says: the purpose of Torah is character improvement). Summary of Chapter 2: Good middot are interconnected because they all serve a single purpose — drawing close to Hashem. Working on one strengthens others. The ultimate aim is devekut. The entire Torah's purpose is character development. Chapter 3 (Perek Gimel) – Taharat HaMa'asim (Purity of Actions) Translation & Meaning Literal: \"Purity of Actions\" Better translation: Sincerity and precision in one's motives Core idea (rooted in the Rambam): Serving Hashem for the sake of serving Hashem — without ulterior motive. The Framework: Categories of Observant Jews \"Da ki shomrei haTorah nechlakkim b'yachas ma'aseihem b'kiyum hamitzvot l'kamah kitot\" — Torah-observant people can be divided into groups based on their motives for keeping mitzvot. There are two extremes and a middle . (Five levels total emerge.) Level 1 (Lowest): Chanufah — Insincerity / Hypocrisy Definition of Chanufah: Rashi: Acting wickedly. Ibn Ezra: Doing bad secretly ( asot ra b'seter ). Ramban: Doing the opposite of what appears to the eye ( asot hefech hanireh la'einayim ). In the Gemara generally: Insincerity, hypocrisy — acting one way while internally being another. Rabbi Avraham's usage here: Doing mitzvot solely for the sake of other people's perception . Examples given: Keeps Shabbat and avoids chametz on Pesach — but only because he fears being caught and punished. If he could be sure no one would see, he wouldn't keep Shabbat. Studies Torah — only so people honor him and consider him knowledgeable, or for financial benefit. Prays extensively and prostrates — so people think he's pious. Wears tzitzit constantly and puts on tefillin — so people consider him a Chasid. All of these: the person's motivation is entirely about other humans, not about Hashem. Historical Note: Tzitzit Wearing Through the Ages An interesting observation emerges from comparing sources across 300 years: Rambam (c. 1200): \"Even though a person is not obligated to buy a four-cornered garment and wear tzitzit, it is not fitting for a Chasid to exempt himself from this mitzvah.\" In the Rambam's time, regular Jews did not routinely wear tzitzit. Only the pious ( chasidim ) did. Shulchan Aruch (c. 1500s): \"It is correct for every person to be careful to wear a tallit katan all day.\" No longer just for chasidim — it had become the standard for all observant Jews. This shows how the custom evolved from an act of piety to a baseline expectation. On Tefillin: \"Wears tefillin a lot\" likely refers to wearing tefillin all day (not just during prayer), which was the practice of the especially pious. Chazal's Terminology: Yuhara (Pretentiousness) The Chachamim called this behavior yuhara — arrogance and pretentiousness in mitzvah observance. It appears in the Al Cheit on Yom Kippur: \"Al cheit shechatanu lefanecha b'yuhara.\" The Chachamim were so concerned about yuhara that they restricted certain optional practices to prevent it. Example 1 — The Chatan and Shema: Halacha: A groom is exempt from Shema on his wedding night. One opinion: Even if he wants to say Shema, he shouldn't — chashinan l'yuhara — we worry he'll develop pretentious thoughts (\"Look how pious I am, even on my wedding night!\"). Example 2 — Working on Tisha B'Av: In places where the custom is to work on Tisha B'Av, choosing not to work is considered yuhara. Lishma vs. Shelo Lishma Rabbi Avraham classifies this behavior as \"oseh Torato shelo lishma\" — doing Torah for the wrong motives. The Midrash on Tehillim 111:10: \"Sechel tov l'chol oseihem\" — \"Good understanding for all who do them\" — interpreted as: for those who do them lishma (for their own sake). Those who do them shelo lishma: \"It would have been better had they not been created.\" The Apparent Contradiction in the Gemara: One statement: \"Always learn Torah, even shelo lishma.\" Another statement: \"One who learns shelo lishma — better he wasn't created.\" Resolution (some Rishonim): There's a spectrum within shelo lishma: Learning for personal motives (honor, money) — not ideal, but still learn. Learning in order to argue against and undermine others ( l'kanter ) — this is truly destructive. Rabbi Avraham's stricter position: Even basic shelo lishma (for personal honor/gain) yields no spiritual benefit from the mitzvah. Still, Better Than Nothing \"U'v'chol zot, he'edif ha'oseh mitzvot b'ofen zeh al she'eino oseh klum\" — Despite everything, doing mitzvot shelo lishma is still better than doing nothing at all , in the hope that one will eventually progress to a higher level. The Harsh Truth As long as people in this category do mitzvot only l'mar'it ayin (for appearance), they are like \"empty people\" ( anashim reikim ) before Hashem — because God judges the heart, not the external appearance. \"Ki ha'adam yir'eh la'einayim, v'Hashem yir'eh la'leivav\" (Shmuel I 16:7) — Humans see the exterior; Hashem sees the heart. Classification This group is called by Chazal the \"Kat Chanefim\" (the sect of hypocrites) — one of the four groups that do not receive the Divine Presence (Sanhedrin 103a). As it says: \"Ki lo lefanav chanef yavo\" (Iyov 13:16) — a hypocrite cannot come before God. Where This Fits This is Level 1 of 5 — the lowest extreme. Next time: the opposite extreme (completely altruistic, pure lishma) and the levels in between. Discussion Points from the Shiur On Picking and Choosing (Will's Comment) People often say \"I'm spiritual but not religious\" and take bits from various systems. This is essentially choosing the easy, feel-good parts while avoiding the hard foundational work of any single system. True spiritual development requires discipline and training, not just pleasant feelings. Rabbi Yisrael Salanter's Teachings It is harder to correct one character trait than to learn the entire Talmud. The Shochet's Story: A shochet wanted to quit because he feared he may have fed people non-kosher food. He planned to go into business instead. Rabbi Salanter responded: \"And you're not scared of sinning in business dealings with other people?\" The lesson: we tend to be vigilant about dramatic religious concerns while neglecting the everyday ethical basics — exactly what Rabbi Avraham warns against. Key Terms Hebrew Translation Context Derech Ha'Am HaGluyah The Revealed Path Basic mitzvah observance Derech HaSegula / HaYachid The Intimate/Unique Path Going beyond the law; subject of this book Tzadik Righteous person One who keeps mitzvot properly Chasid Pious person One who goes beyond the mitzvot Am Ha'aretz Ignorant person Cannot be a Chasid Yuhara Pretentiousness / Arrogance Doing mitzvot to appear pious Chanufah Insincerity / Hypocrisy Keeping mitzvot only for social reasons Lishma For its own sake Proper motivation for mitzvot Shelo Lishma Not for its own sake Improper motivation Pegiyah Encounter The ultimate spiritual communion with Hashem Devekut Cleaving/Clinging Attaching oneself to God through emulating His ways Lev Tov Good Heart The all-encompassing character trait (gematria = 49) Derech Path The journey/training phase of spiritual development Looking Ahead No shiur next week (Purim). Next session will continue Chapter 3: the remaining four levels of motivation in mitzvah observance, from the worst (chanufah, covered today) to the best (completely lishma / altruistic).","rN_ClGILq-o":"Overview of Siman 318 Seifim 1–2 (covered previously): General rules regarding the status of a melachah performed on Shabbat — whether one may benefit from the result, including cases of pikuach nefesh. These are written in the context of bishul but apply to all melachot. Seifim 3–19 : Bishul proper — the specific laws of cooking on Shabbat. Seif ג (Gimmel): Toldot Ha'Or, Cooking in the Sun, and Toldot HaChamah Source Gemara (Shabbat 38b–39a) Mishna (38b): אין נותנין ביצה בצד המיחם בשביל שתתגלגל — You may not place a raw egg next to a hot kettle/urn so that it cooks. ולא יטמיננה בחול ובאבק דרכים בשביל שתצלה — You may not bury the egg in sand or road dust so that it roasts. Gemara: איבעיא להו: גלגל מאי? — If one did place the egg next to the urn, what is the din? Answer: חייב חטאת — It is a Torah-level prohibition (chayav chatat). Rav Nachman's Framework Rav Nachman establishes the following framework: Cooking in the sun itself (בחמה) — Everyone agrees this is mutar (permitted). Cooking with fire (באור) — This is the core melachah of bishul; clearly asur min haTorah . Toldot ha'or (תולדות האור) — Cooking by something heated by fire (e.g., a pot removed from the fire = כלי ראשון). Everyone agrees this is asur min haTorah , exactly like fire itself. There is no distinction between an av melachah and a toldah regarding chiyuv chatat. Toldot hachamah (תולדות החמה) — Cooking by something heated by the sun (e.g., a cloth or rooftop heated by the sun). This is the machloket : Chachamim : Asur — a gezeirah lest one confuse it with toldot ha'or. Rabbi Yose : Mutar — since the sun itself is mutar, its derivatives should also be mutar. Halachah follows the Chachamim : Toldot hachamah is asur miderabanan . The Paradox You may cook directly in the sun, but you may not cook in something heated by the sun. The reason: an observer seeing food cooked by a sun-heated surface might think it was cooked by fire (toldot ha'or), leading to confusion. Why Is the Sun Permitted? Although the sun is literally a ball of fire, it does not have the halachic status of \"fire\" (אור). In the Mishkan, the melachah of bishul was performed with regular fire, not solar heat. The sun is a fundamentally different category. Therefore, cooking directly in sunlight is not included in the melachah at all. Shulchan Aruch — Seif Gimmel כשם שאסור לבשל באור כך אסור לבשל בתולדות האור — Just as it is forbidden to cook with fire, so too it is forbidden to cook with something heated by fire (toldot ha'or / kli rishon). Examples given: Placing a raw egg next to a hot pot (כגון ליתן ביצה בצד קדרה) Breaking an egg onto a cloth heated by fire (לשברה על סודר שחם באור) These are all asur min haTorah. ואפילו בתולדות חמה כגון בסודר שחם בחמה — Even toldot hachamah (e.g., a cloth heated by the sun) is asur miderabanan , as a gezeirah because of toldot ha'or. וכן אסור להטמינה בחול או באבק דרכים שחמו מכח חמה — Similarly, burying an egg in sand or road dust heated by the sun is asur. אבל בחמה עצמה כגון ליתן ביצה בחמה או ליתן מים בחמה כדי שיחמו — מותר — But placing an egg or water directly in the sun to heat up is mutar . Yad Soledet Bo — The Threshold of \"Hot\" The halachic definition of heat sufficient for cooking: יד סולדת בו — hot enough to scald a hand. Practical threshold : From approximately 45°C (113°F) and above, one must treat it as potentially capable of cooking, and it is forbidden. For leniencies, some poskim require higher temperatures (70–80°C). Further details discussed later in the siman. Application: A pot removed from the fire that has cooled below 45°C is no longer considered yad soledet bo, and placing food near it is not an issue of bishul. Note on Burying an Egg in Sand (Hatmanah) Burying an egg in hot sand is forbidden even from erev Shabbat , as it may involve the prohibition of hatmanah (insulation) — specifically, insulation בדבר המוסיף הבל (in something that adds heat). To be discussed further in hilchot hatmanah. Practical Application #1: Dud Shemesh (Solar Boiler) A dud shemesh is a water heater that uses solar energy (not electricity or gas) to heat water, common in Israel. The Question: If the gas element is off before Shabbat and only the sun is heating the water, may one open the hot water tap on Shabbat? When hot water exits, cold water is drawn into the tank and is then heated by the existing sun-heated water. This cold water is being heated by toldot hachamah — which is asur miderabanan. The Halachah — Mutar (according to Sefardi poskim: Chacham Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul, Chacham Ovadia Yosef, Chacham Mordechai Eliyahu; and many Ashkenazi poskim as well). Reasons it is permitted (cumulative): Pesik resha b'derabanan — Opening the tap is intended to use hot water; drawing in cold water is an unintended but inevitable consequence. Since toldot hachamah is only derabanan, this is a pesik resha on a derabanan. Halachah: Pesik resha b'derabanan is mutar when it is d'lo nicha lei (you don't want the result). Melachah she'einah tzrichah l'gufah — You don't need or want the new water to reach yad soledet bo; you just want warm water, not scalding hot. Therefore, the \"cooking\" of the new water is a melachah she'einah tzrichah l'gufah. Grama (indirect causation) — The cold water doesn't boil immediately upon opening the tap; it trickles in and heats gradually. This is an indirect, delayed process. Pesik resha b'grama is mutar. D'lo nicha lei — Chacham Ovadia argues you may not even want or need additional hot water, making this pesik resha d'lo nicha lei (though this depends on family size and usage). Hot into cold / cold into hot — Not all opinions hold that cold water entering hot water constitutes bishul. (This will be discussed in detail in Seif 12.) Tzitz Eliezer's additional argument — Rav Eliezer Waldenberg argues that the incoming cold water is primarily heated directly by the sun (through the copper pipes), not merely by the pre-heated water, making it chamah itself (mutar). Important caveats regarding dud shemesh usage: A person may not take a full-body hot bath on Shabbat, even from water heated before Shabbat (gezeirah of the Chachamim). This applies to mikveh discussions as well. According to most opinions, one may not wash hands, feet, or face with water heated on Shabbat itself (even by the sun). Therefore, the primary permitted use of the dud shemesh on Shabbat is for washing dishes, hot water bottles , etc. — not for personal bathing (per Chacham Ben-Tzion; Chacham Ovadia likely agrees). Using an electrical boiler on Shabbat is asur for various reasons (discussed separately). Practical Application #2: Cooking in a Microwave on Shabbat The Question: A microwave uses electromagnetic waves, not fire. What is its halachic status? Rashi's comment on the Gemara (בחמה דכולי עלמא לא פליגי דשרי): דאין דרך בישול בכך — The reason cooking in the sun is permitted is because \"it is not the normal way of cooking.\" Two interpretations: Interpretation Held By Implication for Microwave The sun is mutar because it's not the normal way of cooking. Any normal cooking method, even without fire, would be asur de'oraita. Rav Moshe Feinstein Microwave cooking = asur de'oraita (since microwaves are a common, normal way to cook) Rashi is just explaining why the sun wasn't included in the gezeirah despite being actual fire. The real point is: no fire = no de'oraita. Shemirat Shabbat KeHilchatah; Rav Ovadia Yosef Microwave cooking = asur miderabanan only (no fire involved) Practical difference (Rav Ovadia): If one must cook for a choleh (ill person) on Shabbat and has a choice between a fire/stove and a microwave, it is preferable to use the microwave (since it may only be derabanan). Bottom line: Cooking in a microwave on Shabbat is certainly asur — both for electricity and for bishul — but whether the bishul component is de'oraita or miderabanan is debated. Practical Application #3: Food Cooked in the Sun — Can It Be Placed on a Hotplate? The Question (raised by the Pri Megadim): If one cooked an egg fully in the sun (which is mutar), may one then place it on a hotplate (heated by fire) to cook it further? Arguments: In favor (mutar): The principle of אין בישול אחר בישול (no cooking after cooking) should apply — the egg is already fully cooked. The fact that it was cooked in the sun doesn't make it \"uncooked.\" Against (asur): Perhaps since the sun isn't halachically \"fire,\" the cooking done by the sun doesn't count, and placing it on fire would be a first act of \"real\" cooking. Halachah: The poskim rule it is mutar . Cooking in the sun is genuinely called \"cooking\" (bishul) in reality — it just isn't the type of fire the Torah prohibited. Since the food is actually fully cooked (mevushal kol tzorcho), placing it on the hotplate involves no further bishul. The food must be fully cooked (not just partially) before placing it on the fire. Seif ד (Daled): Bishul Achar Bishul — Cooking After Cooking This is one of the largest and most important seifim in all of Hilchot Bishul. Multiple major machlokot Rishonim are addressed. Machloket #1: Cooking from Half-Cooked to Fully Cooked The Question: If food was cooked to the level of ma'achal Ben Drosai (partially cooked, edible in extenuating circumstances) before Shabbat but not fully cooked, may one continue cooking it on Shabbat (e.g., place it on a hotplate)? What Is Ma'achal Ben Drosai? Ben Drosai was a highwayman who ate his food only partially cooked because he was always on the road. Ma'achal Ben Drosai = food that is edible in a pinch, but not the comfortable or normal way of eating. How much cooking is that? Rashi / Shulchan Aruch (Siman 254): Half cooked (חצי בישולו). Other Rishonim: One-third cooked (שליש בישולו) — used leniently for derabanan matters like shehiyah and bishul akum. Everyone agrees: Cooking food from raw to ma'achal Ben Drosai on Shabbat is chayav chatat / chayav sekilah — an issur de'oraita. Three Opinions Among the Rishonim Opinion Held By Ruling Mutar — Once food is edible (ma'achal Ben Drosai), further cooking is not \"bishul.\" Rabbeinu Asher (Rosh), Ramban, Rabbeinu Yonah, Ran, Meiri Ein bo bishul — permitted Asur miderabanan — It's not Torah-level bishul but rabbinically prohibited. Rashba Middle position Asur min haTorah — Further cooking from half to fully cooked is a Torah violation. Rambam (inferred by Beis Yosef) Chayav from the Torah Rambam's language: מבשל על האור דבר שמבושל כל צרכו או דבר שאין בו בישול כלל — פטור The Beis Yosef infers: Only if it's fully cooked (מבושל כל צרכו) is there no chiyuv. Implication: if it's not fully cooked and you cook it further, you are chayav. Shulchan Aruch's Ruling אם לא נתבשל כל צרכו, אפילו נתבשל כמאכל בן דרוסאי — שייך בו בישול If food has not been fully cooked — even if it has reached ma'achal Ben Drosai — it is still subject to bishul. The Shulchan Aruch rules like the Rambam . Mishnah Berurah (s.k. 27): This is bishul min haTorah . והוא הדין יותר עד שיגמור בישולו שייך בו בישול מן התורה Sha'ar HaTziyun: The Gra also rules like the Rambam — it is de'oraita. Even if the food is still hot — e.g., one heated it to half-cooked moments before Shabbat and it's still warm — placing it on the hotplate on Shabbat is still asur. Be'dieved (After the Fact) Mishnah Berurah (s.k. 27): If a Jew did take half-cooked food and finished cooking it on Shabbat, the food is not asur to eat. Since this is a machloket Rishonim (many major Rishonim permit it), be'dieved one may rely on the lenient opinions. What About Placing Half-Cooked Food Before Shabbat? Placing half-cooked (or even raw) food on a hotplate (without a dial) before Shabbat is a question of shehiyah , not bishul — since no melachah is being done on Shabbat itself. Chacham Ovadia's ruling: A hotplate without a temperature dial has no issue of shehiyah, so one may place even raw food on it before Shabbat. Machloket #2: Bishul Achar Bishul with Liquids — אין בישול אחר בישול The Principle: אין בישול אחר בישול — There is no cooking after cooking. If food was fully cooked before Shabbat, reheating it on Shabbat does not constitute bishul. The Question: Does this principle apply equally to solids and liquids? The Machloket Opinion Held By Ruling No distinction — אין בישול אחר בישול applies to both solids and liquids, even if fully cooled. Rambam, Rashba, Ran Reheating cold liquid = mutar Distinction — אין בישול אחר בישול applies only to solids. Liquids that have cooled are subject to further bishul. Rosh (inferred from Rashi), Rabbeinu Yonah, Tur Reheating cold liquid = asur min haTorah Source for the Rosh's Distinction Rashi, in two places, implies that reheating cooled liquid is bishul: Regarding hatmanah : Rashi says the reason for the gezeirah is שמא ימצא קדרתו צוננת ויבא לבשל — \"lest he find his pot cold and come to cook it.\" The Rosh asks: if it's already cooked, why is there a concern of \"cooking\"? It must be that reheating cooled liquid is indeed bishul. Regarding stirring : Rashi again implies further bishul applies. The Sevara (Logic) Behind the Distinction Solids: Heat application does two things: (1) changes food from inedible to edible (primary effect), and (2) makes it hot (secondary effect). Once cooled, the primary change (inedible → edible) remains. Therefore, reheating is not \"cooking.\" Liquids: Water/liquid is drinkable before heating. The primary reason for heating liquid is to make it hot. Once cooled, that primary purpose is lost. Therefore, reheating is a new act of \"cooking.\" Shulchan Aruch's Ruling תבשיל שנתבשל כל צרכו — יש בו משום בישול אם נצטנן — A fully cooked dish is subject to bishul if it has cooled down. והני מילי... בתבשיל שיש בו מרק — This applies to a dish that has liquid. אבל דבר שנתבשל כבר והוא יבש — מותר לשרותו בחמין בשבת — But a dry, fully cooked item may be soaked in hot water on Shabbat. The Shulchan Aruch rules like the Rosh: Liquids that have cooled are subject to further bishul; solids are not. Biur Halachah notes: The Rambam, Rashba, and Ran all disagree and hold אין בישול אחר בישול even for liquids. The Gra does not decisively rule between the positions. Summary of the Shulchan Aruch's Rulings Across Both Machlokos Issue Shulchan Aruch Rules Like Ruling Cooking from half-cooked to fully cooked Rambam (against the Rosh) Asur — bishul min haTorah Bishul achar bishul with cooled liquids Rosh (against the Rambam) Asur — yesh bishul achar bishul for liquids In both cases, the Shulchan Aruch rules strictly (l'chumra). Be'dieved for Liquids Same principle as above: If someone reheated a cooled liquid on Shabbat (e.g., placed cooled water back on a hotplate), the water is mutar to drink , since this is a machloket Rishonim and be'dieved one may rely on the lenient view. What Counts as \"Liquid\" vs. \"Solid\"? This is a critical practical question, as many dishes are a combination. Clear cases: Liquid: Water, soup (even with solid pieces — it's fundamentally a liquid dish). Solid: Schnitzel, dry chicken, etc. The grey area: Fish with sauce, chicken with gravy, cholent with some liquid, etc. The Machloket Opinion Held By Standard Go by majority — if the majority is solid, treat it as solid. Based on Rabbeinu Yerucham: תבשיל שיש בו רוב רוטב (a dish with a majority of liquid). Chacham Ovadia Yosef A dish that is fundamentally \"a solid dish\" with some sauce = solid = mutar to reheat Any liquid makes it subject to bishul achar bishul for the liquid component. He claims the correct reading in Rabbeinu Yerucham is שיש בו רוטב (not רוב), matching the Shulchan Aruch's language: תבשיל שיש בו מרק. Chacham Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul Even a small amount of liquid = asur Practical guideline (the Rav's formulation): Ask: \"What is this dish called? Is it a solid dish or a liquid dish?\" Fish with a bit of teriyaki sauce = a solid dish (mutar). Soup with chunks of chicken = a liquid dish (asur). Congealed Liquids If the liquid/sauce has congealed in the fridge (become solid due to cold), it is currently a solid and may be placed on the hotplate. This is true even according to Chacham Ben-Tzion, since at the moment of placement it is physically solid. Note on Mitztamek V'Yafeh Lo (Improving Already-Cooked Food) Question raised in class: If something is fully cooked, isn't there a concern that further heating \"improves\" it, making it asur? Answer: Two separate concepts must be distinguished: Bishul (the melachah): Once food is mevushal kol tzorcho (fully cooked), there is no further bishul even if the food improves with more heat. This is the principle of אין בישול אחר בישול for solids. Shehiyah (leaving food on the fire): There is an opinion among the Rishonim that even fully cooked food, if it is mitztamek v'yafeh lo (it improves by remaining on the fire), has a shehiyah concern (that one might stoke the coals). This requires a blech / covered fire (geruf v'katum) — but this is a shehiyah issue, not a bishul issue. Bottom line: For the melachah of bishul itself, once fully cooked, further cooking is permitted (for solids). Shehiyah, hazarah, and hatmanah are separate discussions with their own rules. Preview of Next Week Topics still to be covered in Seif Daled: The Rama's view (Ashkenazi position) — a compromise between the Rambam and Rosh regarding liquids. Pouring from a kli rishon , rules of kli sheni , kli shlishi . Kalei ha-bishul — things that are easily cooked. Pouring from a kli sheni. Cross-community questions (Ashkenazi asking Sefardi to reheat, etc.). Key Halachic Summary Table Scenario Status Source Cooking with fire on Shabbat Asur min haTorah Av melachah Cooking with toldot ha'or (kli rishon off the fire) Asur min haTorah Toldah — same as fire Cooking directly in the sun Mutar Not the \"fire\" of the Mishkan Cooking with toldot hachamah (object heated by sun) Asur miderabanan Gezeirah — lest one confuse with toldot ha'or Using a dud shemesh (drawing in cold water) Mutar (Sefardi poskim) Multiple combined leniencies Cooking in a microwave Asur; de'oraita or miderabanan debated Machloket Rav Moshe vs. Rav Ovadia Sun-cooked food placed on hotplate Mutar (if fully cooked) Ein bishul achar bishul applies Half-cooked food placed on hotplate on Shabbat Asur min haTorah Shulchan Aruch like Rambam Fully-cooked solid, cooled, reheated on Shabbat Mutar Ein bishul achar bishul (all agree for solids) Fully-cooked liquid, cooled, reheated on Shabbat Asur (Shulchan Aruch like Rosh) Yesh bishul achar bishul for liquids Mixed dish (solid with some liquid) Depends on majority/nature of dish Machloket Chacham Ovadia vs. Chacham Ben-Tzion Congealed sauce/liquid from fridge Mutar to place on hotplate Currently solid","oJrV3fMKgLI":"Class Notes — Rosh Chodesh Adar תשפ״ו Sefer: Hamaspik l'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek Aleph — \"The Path of the Torah Which Has No End\" Edition: Feldheim Context Full work = 2,500+ pages, four major sections; we are studying part of section two 13 chapters total — each covers a different character trait and how to work on it First three sections (which we don't have) = day-to-day halacha, regular behaviour → Derech Ha'am This remaining section = going beyond the basics → Derech HaYachid / Chasidut The Two Paths: Overview דרך התורה המתמשכת והולכת בלי גבול — the path of Torah has no end Divides into two distinct paths: Derech Ha'am (דרך העם) — the common path Derech HaYachid (דרך היחיד) — the unique/individual path Path 1: Derech Ha'am — The Common Path = fulfilling the 248 positive commandments + refraining from 365 negative commandments Each person according to their specific obligations Obligations vary by category: Gender: Milah, Sukkah, Lulav — males only (women may do voluntarily) Kehunah: Temple service, dietary rules, marriage restrictions — Kohanim only Time/place: Mikdash laws — not applicable in Galut Role: \"לא תשא פני דל ולא תהדר פני גדול\" — applies to dayanim specifically Requirements for walking this path correctly: Must understand what Hashem commanded to do and not do Must consider how each mitzvah relates to you personally Must accept the obligation and act on it Must study — without knowledge, you can't succeed Source: Avot — אין בור ירא חטא (an ignorant person cannot be God-fearing) Titles earned on this path: Tzaddik — righteous Tam — wholesome Yashar — person of integrity Sar Mera — turns from evil Kasher (Rabbinic term) — appropriate/fitting Word \"kasher\" not in Torah or Nevi'im — only Ketuvim (Kohelet, Esther) Esther to Achashverosh: \"וכשר הדבר\" = it is appropriate HaRambam's three levels of conduct: Mishpat (justice) — enforceable in Beit Din Tzedakah (appropriateness) — the right thing to do, not enforceable but obligatory E.g. returning a poor person's night garment used as collateral NB: English \"charity\" is imprecise — implies no obligation; Torah's tzedakah = moral duty Chesed (kindness) — going beyond what is appropriate Doing everything commanded = tzaddik But not yet a chasid — that's the next level Failure to keep this path: Called: rasha, poshea, averayan, moreh Label matches severity of failing = debasing yourself — not satisfying your nefesh sichliyit (intellectual soul) Path 2: Derech HaYachid — The Unique Path = understanding the goals and reasons of mitzvot (תכלית המצוות וסודותיהן) = learning from the ways of prophets and pious people Title earned: Kadosh (holy), Chasid (pious) Rabbi Avraham's three-tier hierarchy: Tzaddik → Chasid → Navi Importance of Ta'amei HaMitzvot (reasons for mitzvot): Comes directly from HaRambam: End of Hilchot Me'ilah: \"It is correct to try to understand the laws and their reasons\" End of Hilchot Temurah: even chukim deserve contemplation אף על פי שכל חוקי התורה גזירות הם, ראוי להתבונן בהם On chukim: HaRambam disagrees that chukim have no reason — every mitzvah has a reason; a chok is simply harder to understand Temurah example: can't swap a dedicated animal even for a better one ירדה תורה לסוף מחשבת האדם — Torah understands human rationalisation around money Key insight: understanding the reason → understanding the direction → knowing how to take the mitzvah further → Chasidut Key characteristics of Derech HaYachid: Not everyone can reach it — \"זוהי מדרגה שלא כל שומר תורה מגיע אליה\" Not explicit in Torah — \"לא נזכר חיובה במפורש\" No human punishment for not reaching it — \"לא יהיה נענש בידי אדם\" Examples: Tzaddik vs. Chasid Shabbat Tzaddik: keeps Shabbat, avoids 39 melachot, Kiddush, meals ✓ Chasid: additionally contemplates the meaning — creation, God as primal existence, renewal of the world Tzitzit Tzaddik: wears tzitzit ✓ Chasid: uses tzitzit as a tool to remember Hashem constantly → \"והייתם קדושים לאלהיכם\" Kashrut Tzaddik: eats kosher, avoids treif ✓ Chasid: understands the reason (Rambam, Moreh Nevuchim — not overindulging in food) → restrains even within permitted foods Arayot Tzaddik: refrains from forbidden relationships ✓ Chasid: understands the principle (minimise indulgence) → exercises additional restraint in permitted relationships too Levels: Derech Ha'am vs. Derech HaYachid In Derech Ha'am — minimal variation: Mostly binary: keep Shabbat or don't; eat kosher or don't Differences mainly quantitative: Number of prohibitions (eating bugs = 4-6 issurim vs. basar b'chalav = 1) Frequency (wore garment without tzitzit once vs. every day) Exception within Derech Ha'am: Some mitzvot have inherent gradation even at the common level: Yirat Hashem — fear of punishment (lower) vs. fear of His exaltedness (higher) Ahavat Hashem — love because He's good to me vs. unconditional deep love Avodat HaLev — service of the heart In Derech HaYachid — vast variation: רחבים תחומיה עד מאד ורחוקים זה מזה כרחוק מזרח ממערב Even in one mitzvah , even one time — enormous differences in depth The Mashal: Three Chasidim Keeping One Shabbat Chasid 1 — General Contemplation Keeps Shabbat fully (baseline) + Thinks generally about Shabbat's purpose Reflects: heavens and earth created in six days; nothing before Hashem; God = primal existence Chasid 2 — Detailed Contemplation Everything of Chasid 1 + Delves into specifics — universe from earth's centre to celestial spheres Reflects on wisdom of the Creator through details of creation Goes through each day of Bereishit = Ma'aseh Bereishit (Yesodei HaTorah) — science, astronomy, natural world as path to God's wisdom Chasid 3 — Total Absorption / Ecstatic Encounter Everything of Chasid 2 + Reaches true inner holiness (kedushah penimit amitit) Filled with joy; shining countenance Fully appreciates the bond (zikah) between himself and God Understands Shabbat's purpose: ביני ובין בני ישראל אות היא לעולם Refrains from eating/drinking — not ascetic choice, but genuinely absorbed Does not hear sounds; does not register what he sees כמו חלב ודשן תשבע נפשי — soul satisfied like richest foods Parallel: HaRambam, end of Hilchot De'ot — lovesick for Hashem, כי חולת אהבה אני How can not eating on Shabbat be permitted? Gemara Pesachim: Mar brei d'Ravina fasted every day except Shavuot, Purim, Erev Yom Kippur Rabbeinu David: not a formal fast — passion for Torah so great he forgot to eat until night Even on Shabbat: Shabbat requires oneg — and this was his oneg Shulchan Aruch siman 288: if fasting is your enjoyment, you may fast on Shabbat Shabbat ≠ obligation to eat; Shabbat = obligation to experience delight Analogy: absorbed in a book/film → forget you're hungry → not conscious refusal, thought simply doesn't arise Key Term: Pegiah (פגיעה) — Encounter Arabic original: wasul → Hebrew: pegiah = to encounter (cf. ויפגע במקום) Chasidut is experiential — not just performative Greater Chasidut = greater pegiah = deeper encounter with God All character traits (perishut, anavah, etc.) have many levels of pegiah Humility of Moshe ≠ humility of other tzaddikim; perishut of Eliyahu ≠ perishut of other nevi'im Q&A Points Is excessive chesed a problem? In human relationships — yes: גומל חסדים טובים — implies there is also bad chesed \"Gemilut chasadim\" from \"gamal\" (camel) — best chesed = making someone independent Torah calls sibling incest \"chesed\" (Acharei Mot) — natural love crossing into destructive territory With HaKadosh Baruch Hu — possibly: if chasidut comes at expense of other obligations; needs further thought Are we commanded to go beyond? ועשית הטוב והישר — Chachamim derive lifnim mishurat hadin from this Falls under tzedakah (appropriateness), not chasidut We don't appear to be explicitly commanded to be chasidim Chasidut = voluntary spiritual aspiration Gadol metzuveh ve'oseh? Applies to derech ha'am Chasidut = realm of the mitnadeiv (volunteer) beyond obligation Chapter 1 Conclusion Chasidut can only be pursued when basics (Derech Ha'am) are firmly in place Cannot be a chasid without first being a tzaddik Summary Table Derech Ha'am Derech HaYachid Involves Fulfilling all commandments Understanding reasons + going beyond Applies to Every Jew Unique individuals aspiring to closeness Title Tzaddik, Kasher, Yashar Kadosh, Chasid Variation Minimal (quantitative) Vast — \"east to west\" Source Explicit in Torah Inner meaning, not explicit Punishment Yes No human punishment Nature Revealed (gluyah) Inner/hidden (penimit) Next week: Finish Chapter 1 + begin Chapter 2, be'ezrat Hashem.","sbwq8A_WxPk":"General Overview of the Siman Siman 318 deals with דיני המבשל בשבת — the laws of cooking on Shabbat — and contains 19 se'ifim. It is one of the largest and most discussed simanim in Hilchot Shabbat. The Gemara plays on the letters שי\"ח (shin-yud-chet), noting that שיח means \"discussion\" — because there is so much to discuss. Bishul vs. Ofeh — Background The 11th melachah in the Mishnah is listed as אופה (baking), not בישול (cooking). However, in the Mishkan, when making dyes for the coverings, they cooked with liquid — which is bishul, not ofeh. The Gemara asks: why does the Tanna call it Ofeh? Answer: סידורא דפת נקט — the Mishnah follows the order of bread-making, and the first 11 melachot trace that process. So the Tanna uses \"Ofeh\" to maintain that sequence. In practice, we refer to this melachah as Bishul , which is why the Shulchan Aruch and Rama use that term. Avot and Toladot of Bishul Avot (primary categories, all equally prohibited d'Oraita): בישול — cooking (with liquid) אפייה — baking (dry heat) טיגון — frying צלייה — roasting Toladot (secondary categories): Melting substances Heating something until it becomes red-hot (To be discussed further with the Rambam in Seif Gimmel) Seif Aleph (א): Ma'aseh Shabbat — Benefiting from Melachah Done on Shabbat Why Is This Here? Seif Aleph does not directly discuss bishul. It addresses a broader principle: can one benefit from the product of a Shabbat violation? The Shulchan Aruch places it here because the Gemara discusses this principle in the context of bishul — but it applies to virtually all melachot. The Three Talmudic Opinions The Gemara (appearing in several places in Shas) records three Tannaitic opinions: 1. Rabbi Meir המבשל בשבת — בשוגג יאכל, במזיד לא יאכל Beshogeg (unintentional): Everyone may eat the food, even on Shabbat itself. Bemeizid (intentional): No one may eat it on Shabbat, but everyone may eat it immediately after Shabbat (Motza'ei Shabbat). 2. Rabbi Yehuda (Middle Opinion) בשוגג יאכל במוצאי שבת, במזיד לא יאכל עולמית Beshogeg : No one may eat the food on Shabbat. Everyone may eat it immediately after Shabbat. Bemeizid : The violator himself may never eat it (אסור לו לעולם). Everyone else may eat it after Shabbat. 3. Rabbi Yochanan HaSandlar (Strictest — Not Followed) Beshogeg : Only others may eat after Shabbat; the person himself may never eat it. Bemeizid : No one may ever benefit from it. Summary Table Scenario Rabbi Meir Rabbi Yehuda R. Yochanan HaSandlar Shogeg — on Shabbat Everyone may eat No one may eat No one may eat Shogeg — after Shabbat Everyone may eat Everyone may eat Others only; he — never Meizid — on Shabbat No one may eat No one may eat No one may eat Meizid — after Shabbat Everyone may eat Others may eat; he — never No one — ever The Halachic Ruling General principle: in a מחלוקת ר' מאיר ור' יהודה, הלכה כרבי יהודה . The Rif , Rambam , and most likely the Rosh all rule like Rabbi Yehuda. Tosafot and the Vilna Gaon (GR\"A) rule like Rabbi Meir, based on a story in the Gemara where Rav paskened like Rabbi Meir. The Rif addresses this, saying Rav did not follow the general principle (klal), but we do. The Shulchan Aruch's Ruling (Following Rabbi Yehuda) המבשל בשבת או שעשה אחת משאר מלאכות — במזיד: אסור לו לעולם, ולאחרים מותר למוצאי שבת מיד. בשוגג: אסור בו ביום גם לאחרים, ולערב מותר גם לו מיד. Bemeizid : Forbidden to the violator forever. Permitted to everyone else immediately after Shabbat. Beshogeg : Forbidden to everyone on Shabbat. Permitted to everyone (including the violator) immediately after Shabbat. Mishnah Berurah and the Ashkenazi Position The Mishnah Berurah notes that the GR\"A follows Tosafot (i.e., Rabbi Meir). Ruling : במקום הצורך (in a case of need), one may rely on the GR\"A and Tosafot — meaning beshogeg, one could eat even on Shabbat itself. Practical example : You are invited to someone's home for Shabbat lunch and discover the food was cooked on Shabbat beshogeg. According to the Shulchan Aruch: forbidden until Motza'ei Shabbat. According to the Mishnah Berurah: bemakom hatzorech (e.g., to avoid embarrassment), one could rely on Rabbi Meir. Rav Ovadia Yosef's Position (Sefardi Ruling) We do not rely on the Mishnah Berurah's leniency. The Shulchan Aruch brings only one opinion (Rabbi Yehuda) — there is no second opinion to rely on bemakom hatzorech. One cannot rely on שיטות דחויות (rejected opinions). Even bemakom hatzorech, the food is forbidden on Shabbat if cooked beshogeg. Defining Shogeg, Meizid, and Mitasek Three categories of intent on Shabbat: Category Definition Example Meizid (Intentional) Knows it's Shabbat, knows the action is forbidden, does it anyway Deliberately turns on oven on Shabbat Shogeg (Unintentional) Conscious action, but either doesn't know it's Shabbat OR doesn't know the action is forbidden Drives to shul thinking it's Sunday; cooks not knowing it's forbidden Mitasek (Non-deliberate) Performs the action without any conscious intent Leans against wall and accidentally flips light switch Key distinctions: Shogeg requires a conscious, willful action — the error is only in knowledge (of the day or the prohibition). Mitasek is not considered a ma'aseh (act) at all — no korban chatat would be brought in the time of the Mishkan. Chacham Ovadia's ruling on asking a Rav : If someone asked a competent Rav who mistakenly permitted an action — that is shogeg. But if someone knowingly asked an incompetent Rav (who they know doesn't know halachah properly), and that Rav permitted it — that is meizid , because the person bears responsibility for asking the wrong authority. Nature of the Prohibition (Geder HaIssur) D'Oraita or D'Rabbanan? D'Rabbanan. Although one opinion in the Gemara learns it from a pasuk, all agree this is only an אסמכתא (textual support, not actual derivation). What Kind of D'Rabbanan? It is a קנס (penalty) — Chazal penalize the violator to prevent people from benefiting from forbidden acts. Why penalize shogeg too? שוגג אטו מזיד — shogeg acts as a fence to prevent meizid. If shogeg were permitted, people might claim shogeg while acting bemeizid. Rav Moshe Feinstein: It Is an Issur Hana'ah (Prohibition of Benefit) Rav Moshe understands the prohibition as specifically an issur of benefiting from the product of the violation. Practical implication — מצוות לאו ליהנות ניתנו (mitzvot are not given for the purpose of personal enjoyment): Mikvah case : If someone accidentally turned on the mikvah heater on Shabbat, a woman who needs to immerse may do so — because immersion is a mitzvah, not primarily for hana'ah. Sukkah case : If relatives built their sukkah on Shabbat (even beshogeg), you may sit in it — because sitting in a sukkah is a mitzvah, and mitzvot are not considered hana'ah in this context. The Scope of Issur Hana'ah The prohibition is against directly benefiting from the product of the melachah — e.g., eating food that was cooked. It is not a blanket issur hana'ah like chametz. One may: Sell the item Give it to others (for whom it is permitted) The Gemara states explicitly: היא ולא דמיה — \"it, but not its monetary value.\" You can benefit financially, just not from the direct product of the violation. D'Rabbanan Violations If the Shabbat violation itself was only d'Rabbanan (e.g., cooking through toldot hachama — heat of the sun): Beshogeg : Everyone may benefit from it even on Shabbat (Rabbi Yehuda effectively agrees with Rabbi Meir for d'Rabbanan violations). Bemeizid : Forbidden for everyone until after Shabbat. Application to Other Melachot The Rambam codifies this principle regarding אחת מהמלאכות (any of the melachot) — not specifically bishul. The Gemara used bishul as an example, but the principle applies broadly. The Chayei Adam's Distinction: Intrinsic Change vs. No Change The Chayei Adam introduces an important leniency: דוקא בדבר שנעשה מעשה בגופו של דבר שישתנה מכמות שהיה — כמבשל. אבל המוציא מרשות לרשות שלא נשתנה הדבר מכמות שהיה... Melachot that intrinsically change the object (cooking, building, laundering, tying, planting, etc.): The full penalty of ma'aseh Shabbat applies. Melachot that do NOT change the object (e.g., hotza'ah — carrying): The penalty is more lenient: Beshogeg : Everyone may benefit even on Shabbat. Bemeizid : Everyone must wait until after Shabbat. Effectively follows Rabbi Meir even for d'Oraita. Example (Chacham Ovadia) : Sweets carried to a shul on Shabbat without an eruv — if done beshogeg, one may eat them on Shabbat, since carrying does not change the nature of the sweets. Possible extension to borer (selecting) : The lecturer suggests that borer (separating/sorting) also does not intrinsically change the substance, and the Chayei Adam's leniency may logically apply — though he notes he has not seen this explicitly in the sources. Chayei Adam's Distinction — Summary The Chayei Adam's ruling applies on the level of Rabbi Meir even for d'Oraita violations, meaning: Beshogeg : Everyone may benefit even on Shabbat. Bemeizid : Everyone (including the violator) must wait until after Shabbat. This is specifically for melachot where there is no intrinsic change to the object (e.g., carrying, and potentially selecting/borer). For melachot that do change the object (cooking, building, laundering, etc.), the standard halachah of Rabbi Yehuda applies. Planting Exception If someone plants on Shabbat, the seed cannot be benefited from ever, because you wouldn't benefit from it on Shabbat anyway — the penalty remains permanently. Laundering Example Beshogeg : Cannot wear the laundered garment until after Shabbat; then everyone may. Bemeizid : The violator can never wear the garment again (אסור לו לעולם); everyone else may wear it after Shabbat. Ben Ish Chai's question : Can one dirty the garment and re-launder it to remove the original issur? He contradicts himself — in Rav Pe'alim he says no; in the Ben Ish Chai itself he says yes. Chacham Ovadia relies on this leniency (combined with the factor of Rabbi Meir's opinion). The Emphasis on מיד (Immediately) The Shulchan Aruch emphasizes מיד (immediately) twice — both for bemeizid (others after Shabbat) and beshogeg (everyone after Shabbat). Purpose : To exclude the concept of בכדי שיעשו (\"the time it would take to perform the action\"). With a non-Jew who performs a prohibited melachah: sometimes one must wait after Shabbat for the amount of time the action would have taken (e.g., if a non-Jew cooked food that took one hour, you'd wait one hour after Shabbat). With a Jew : There is no such requirement. The reason: אין אדם חוטא ולא לו — we are not concerned that Jews will routinely commission other Jews to violate Shabbat on their behalf, so the extra fence of בכדי שיעשו was not applied. \"Others\" Includes the Intended Beneficiary Even if someone cooked specifically for you on Shabbat bemeizid (and you didn't ask them to), you are still considered אחרים (others) — not the violator. You may eat that food after Shabbat. Example : A non-religious parent cooks for you on Shabbat despite being told not to — you may eat the food after Shabbat. Only B'Akrai (Occasional) — Chacham Ovadia's Limitation This leniency (that \"others\" may eat after Shabbat) applies only when it happens occasionally (בעקראי) , not on a regular basis. Example : A kosher restaurant in Israel owned by a secular Jew who cooks on Shabbat afternoon for Motza'ei Shabbat customers — if it happens once, others may eat. If it is a weekly occurrence , the food is forbidden (beyond the separate issue of lifnei iver). A parent who cooks every Shabbat for their child cannot be relied upon — the food is forbidden every time. A one-off occurrence is permissible after Shabbat. Can the Pot Be Used After Meizid Cooking? Mishnah Berurah : The pot cannot be used by the violator, because the forbidden taste has been absorbed (בלוע) into the pot walls. However, after 24 hours, the absorbed taste becomes נותן טעם לפגם (imparts a bad flavor), and the pot may be used. Rav Ovadia Yosef : Rules that the pot is 100% fine to use. He argues that even with bishul akum (food cooked by a non-Jew), where the Shulchan Aruch requires hag'alah (kashering), it is a machloket Rishonim — and here, the prohibition applies only to one person, making it even more lenient. Safek (Doubt) — Leniency וכל שיש ספק פלוגתא... אין לאסור בדיעבד, דכל האיסור הזה דרבנן שקנסו, וספיקא דרבנן לקולא. Whenever there is a machloket among the Rishonim about whether a particular action constitutes a violation, and the action was done — bediavad, the penalty does not apply . Since the entire prohibition of ma'aseh Shabbat is d'Rabbanan, the rule of ספיקא דרבנן לקולא applies. Example 1 — Reheating liquids : The Shulchan Aruch holds reheating cooled liquids is assur d'Oraita, but the Rambam permits it. Bediavad, if someone reheated a cooled liquid on Shabbat (even bemeizid), the food may be eaten. Example 2 — Ma'achal Ben Drusai : If food was already half-cooked (edible) before Shabbat and further cooked on Shabbat, there is a machloket Rishonim about whether this is an issur d'Oraita. Bediavad, the food may be eaten. Electricity in Israel Many people in Israel avoid using standard electricity on Shabbat because Jewish workers operate the power plants bemeizid. They use private generators instead. Rav Ovadia Yosef permits standard electricity, based on the argument (also stated by Rav Mordechai Eliyahu) that electricity must be continuously produced for pikuach nefesh — hospitals, critically ill patients, etc. Since it cannot be determined which electricity serves whom, the production is permissible, and everyone may benefit. Seif Bet (ב): Melachah Done Permissibly for a Choleh (Ill Person) Shechitah (Slaughtering) for a Choleh השוחט בשבת לחולה — בין שחלה מאתמול בין שחלה היום — מותר הבריא לאכול ממנו חי בשבת. If an ill person (pikuach nefesh) needs meat on Shabbat, one may slaughter an animal for him. Since you must slaughter the entire animal (you cannot slaughter half), there is leftover meat. Healthy people may eat the leftover meat raw on Shabbat — because the slaughter was done be'heter (permissibly). Raw meat only — you cannot cook it (that would be a separate melachah not done for the choleh). You also cannot salt the meat on Shabbat, as salting is a d'Rabbanan of מעבד (tanning). You must wash off any surface blood. Cooking or Other Melachot for a Choleh ומבשל או שעשה שאר מלאכה לחולה — אסור בשבת לבריא ולחולה שאין בו סכנה. If someone cooked for a choleh on Shabbat (permissibly, for pikuach nefesh) and there are leftovers: Healthy people may NOT eat the cooked food on Shabbat. It is permitted after Shabbat. Reason : דחיישינן שמא ירבה בשבילו — we are concerned that the cook may add extra food for the healthy person while already cooking for the choleh. This concern may be d'Oraita. Why the Difference Between Shechitah and Bishul? Factor Shechitah Bishul Can you \"increase\" for others? No — you slaughter one whole animal Yes — easy to add more food to the pot Leftovers permitted on Shabbat? Yes (raw only) No Concern of מרבה Does not apply Applies — hence the prohibition Even a Non-Jew Cooking for the Choleh ואפילו בשל עכו\"ם אסור בשבת Even if a non-Jew cooked for the ill person, healthy people still cannot eat the cooked food on Shabbat. Fruits Cut from a Tree for a Choleh אם קצץ מן הפירות מן המחובר לחולה בשבת — אפילו היה חולה מבעוד יום — אסור לבריא בשבת משום דגדל והולך בשבת ויש בו משום מוקצה. If fruits were plucked from a tree on Shabbat for a choleh: Even though only one fruit was taken (no concern of מרבה, similar to shechitah) — it is still forbidden for healthy people on Shabbat. Reason : The fruit is muktzah because it continues to grow on Shabbat. The additional growth that occurred on Shabbat is considered נולד (newly created), making the entire fruit muktzah. There is a discussion between the Magen Avraham and the GR\"A about why this growth isn't batel (nullified), but the halachah stands that it is forbidden. Ben Ish Chai: Choosing Between Animals for Shechitah If the shochet has a choice between a large chicken and a small chicken, and the choleh only needs the small one: Ben Ish Chai : It is wrong to slaughter the large one (to minimize the melachah). Hamabit : Disagrees — shechitah is shechitah regardless of size. There is no concept of מרבה in the act of cutting a larger vs. smaller neck. Key Principles Summary Ma'aseh Shabbat — benefiting from a Shabbat violation — is an issur d'Rabbanan (a penalty/קנס). Halachah follows Rabbi Yehuda : Beshogeg — forbidden on Shabbat, permitted for everyone after. Bemeizid — forbidden forever for the violator; others permitted after Shabbat. Ashkenazi leniency (Mishnah Berurah): Bemakom hatzorech, one may rely on Rabbi Meir (beshogeg = permitted even on Shabbat). Sefardi psak (Rav Ovadia): No reliance on this. D'Rabbanan violations : Beshogeg = permitted immediately for everyone. Bemeizid = wait until after Shabbat. Chayei Adam : Melachot that don't change the substance (carrying, possibly borer) — more lenient; effectively follows Rabbi Meir. Mitzvot la'av leihonot nitnu : If the benefit is for a mitzvah (mikvah, sukkah), the penalty doesn't apply. Safek/machloket : If there is a dispute about whether the action is a violation, bediavad the penalty does not apply (ספיקא דרבנן לקולא). מיד — no בכדי שיעשו : Unlike with a non-Jew, there is no waiting period after Shabbat beyond Shabbat itself. Choleh : Leftover cooked food from cooking for a choleh is forbidden on Shabbat (concern of מרבה); raw meat from shechitah is permitted.","64QvD-4k9U0":"Series Overview New Monday night series — follows three completed sefarim over ~6–7 years: Derech Hashem (Ramchal) Da'at Tevunot (Ramchal) Emunot VeDe'ot (Rav Sa'adia Gaon) — the first systematic theology of Torah; studied by the Rambam who elaborated on many of Sa'adia's ideas Parallel learning: Tuesday nights approaching the end of Rambam's Hakdama to Perek Chelek, after which the shiur will begin Moreh Nevuchim — so both sefarim will run in parallel. Background: The Rambam Born: Nissan, 1135 (main view) or 1138, in Cordoba Died: Tevet, December 1204, in Fustat (Old Cairo) Lived approximately 69 years To the best of our knowledge, his only child was Rabbi Avraham (born when Rambam was ~51 years old) Why the Name \"Avraham\"? From a letter Rambam wrote to Rabbi Yosef ben Yehuda (the dedicatee of the Moreh Nevuchim): \"My son Avraham — Hashem gave him grace and blessing from the blessing of the one he is called after\" — i.e., Avraham Avinu . Rambam describes his son as: Extremely humble (ענו ושפל שבאנשים) Of great character and traits (טוב מידותיו) Possessing a fine intellect and good nature (בעל שכל דק וטבע נאה) Destined for fame: \"יהיה לו בעזרת השם שם בגדולים בלא ספק\" Dating the letter: Likely written in 1201 (Rabbi Avraham aged ~15), a few years before Rambam's death in 1204. The Pattern of Names Across Three Generations Generation Father Son Named Reason Rabbi Maimon — Moshe Great reverence for Moshe Rabbeinu; Maimon added Tehillim 90 (תפילה למשה) daily before Baruch She'amar Rambam (Moshe) — Avraham After Moshe, Avraham Avinu was Rambam's most revered figure; couldn't name him Moshe (his own name) Rabbi Avraham — David & Ovadya David — after Rambam's brother who drowned at sea; Ovadya (עבד ה׳) — reflecting the theme of his book HaMaspik Le'Ovdei Hashem Rabbi Avraham himself writes (in his commentary on Bereishit, on ותמות דבורה מינקת רבקה) that naming someone after an important person gives them some positive change to their character and identity. Biography of Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Key Dates Born: Sivan (June) 1186, Fustat, Egypt Died: 1237, aged 51 Father died when he was only 18–19 years old Torah Learning Learned most of his Torah — both halacha and machshava — directly from his father There were things Rambam told him privately that he would not record in writing Example: On the pasuk \"עין תחת עין\" — Rabbi Avraham says he received something from his father but cannot disclose it Rambam himself in Moreh Nevuchim III:41 writes: \"I have my opinion about this law which I'll only express verbally\" (עם זאת יש לי דעה גם לגבי הלכה זו שתשמע על פה) Public Life & Communal Role Became Nagid (head of the Jewish community / representative to the government) at age 19 The position of Nagid continued in his family for ~150 years through to his great-great-grandson Rabbi Avraham Maimoni Doctor like his father; manager of a hospital in Cairo Traveled regularly between Fustat (Old Cairo) and New Cairo for hospital duties Invoked Shir HaShirim: \"כרמי שלי לא נטרתי\" — lamenting he couldn't attend to his own development due to communal responsibilities Fame & Recognition At age 25 (1211), a group of Baalei Tosafot from France on their way to Eretz Yisrael stopped in Fustat specifically to meet him Titles given to him: \"Young in years but great in wisdom\" — Rabbi Yehuda Al-Harizi חסיד — Maharam Alshakar (15th century Egypt) נשיא אלוקים בתוכנו — \"The Prince of God is in our midst\" שכינה שרויה בנינו — \"The Shechina dwells among us through him\" ראש כל החסידים — \"Head of all the pious\" Rabbi Avraham's Works Book Subject HaMaspik Le'Ovdei Hashem Comprehensive guide to serving Hashem (the sefer we are studying) Commentary on Bereishit & Shemot Torah commentary (unclear if he completed beyond these) Maaseh Nissim Refutation of Daniel HaBavli's 47 challenges to the Mishneh Torah Birkat Avraham Refutation of Daniel HaBavli's 13 challenges to the Moreh Nevuchim Milchamot Hashem Defence of the Rambam against the broader controversies (especially after the burning of the Moreh in Montpellier) Teshuvot / Responsa Various halachic responses Commentary on Rambam's Pirush HaMishnayot Written at a young age Note on Daniel HaBavli: Despite being urged to place him in cherem, Rabbi Avraham refused, saying it would appear to be a conflict of interest. Rabbi Avraham's Unique Approach Personal & Experiential vs. Intellectual Rambam emphasised the intellectual understanding of Hashem Rabbi Avraham emphasised a personal, experiential encounter with Hashem These complement each other — as Rambam himself writes (Moreh III:51): after intellectual understanding of God, the goal is constant focus and standing before Hashem — \"וזהו עבודת השם המיוחדת\" The Question of Abstinence (Perishut) Rabbi Avraham adopted practices of chasidut/perishut including: Waking at night for prayer/meditation Extensive bowing and prostration during prayer Wearing uncomfortable clothing (e.g., coarse wool) Various forms of self-denial The apparent contradiction with Rambam: In Shemoneh Perakim (Ch. 4), Rambam explicitly criticises such practices: \"כסילים חסידים עשו אלה הפעולות... יענו את גופותם בכל מיני ענוי\" — Foolish pious people afflict their bodies... these are bad things. Resolution: Rambam himself acknowledges (in the same chapter) that certain Chassidim did these things as a form of healing — temporary measures to correct imbalance, like bitter medicine for illness. Rabbi Avraham may have seen his generation as needing this corrective. Relationship with Sufism Rabbi Avraham openly acknowledged learning from Sufi practices of abstinence and purity His principle: He only adopted practices for which he could find evidence that the Nevi'im or Chassidim HaRishonim had engaged in them This was not \"influence\" but reawakening lost Jewish practices that Muslims had originally received from us What he rejected: e.g., the Sufi practice of repeating God's name many times — he found no Jewish source for this He received criticism for some of these innovations (e.g., additional bowings in prayer) His Father-in-Law's Influence Married the daughter of Rabbi Chananel ben Shmuel HaDayan , himself a Dayan in Fustat and known as a Chassid — possibly where Rabbi Avraham developed his orientation toward chasidut The Book: HaMaspik Le'Ovdei Hashem Title Arabic: כפאיה אלעאבדין — Kifayat Al-Abidin Hebrew (by Rabbi Avraham himself): אומספיק לעובדי השם — with the \"ו\" prefix suggesting \"a sufficient guide\" rather than \"the definitive guide\" Feldheim English title: \"The Guide to Serving God\" — but this loses the word \"maspik\" (sufficient/enough) Better translation: \"A Sufficient Guide to Serving God\" Concept: Just as Rambam wrote the Mishneh Torah as the sufficient guide for halacha , Rabbi Avraham wrote this as the sufficient guide for avodat Hashem in practice. Structure of the Complete Work The full HaMaspik consisted of 4 parts with 10 sections total: Part Content Status Part 1 Torah study, Shabbat, Festivals Mostly lost Part 2 Incorporeality of God, Prayer, Bowing, Tefillin, Mezuzah, Vows, Speech Mostly lost Part 3 Interpersonal relationships, Financial law, Marriage, Charity Mostly lost Part 4 Derech HaChasidut — The Way of the Pious What we have and will be studying The first three parts were practical halacha interweaved with musar — a very practical guide (like modern \"practical laws of Shabbat\" etc.), in contrast to his father's Mishneh Torah which codified the entire Torah including non-applicable laws. Completion & Spread Completed: 1230 (a few years before his death in 1237) Spread to: Egypt, Syria, Lebanon, Safed, Algeria, and even France (Rabbi Aharon of Lunel mentions it) Quoted by: Maharam Alshakar, the Radbaz (Rabbi David ben Zimra), and others Translation History 1927: First English translation by Samuel Rosenblatt (from Arabic to English — same translator as Emunot VeDe'ot) 1965: First Hebrew translation by Rabbi Solomon Sassoon (grandson of Flora Sassoon), produced in honour of the wedding of his niece Mozelle to Rabbi Abraham Gabbay (later rabbi of Ohel David, Golders Green) The 1965 Hebrew text is the basis of the Feldheim edition used today Practical Notes The sefer is not available on Sefaria Available editions: Feldheim — green (Hebrew), purple (English) Estimated study duration: approximately one year Key Themes to Watch For The relationship between intellectual understanding and experiential service of God Rabbi Avraham's concept of chasidut and what it means in his context The tension/resolution between abstinence and Rambam's golden mean Insights into the Rambam himself through his son's writings The interplay between Jewish tradition and external practices (Sufi influence) Primary source for this introduction: Article by David Yosef (not the Chief Rabbi), supplemented by the Feldheim introduction.","-OU4IbTNzt8":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 13 of 14 Topic: Medicine on Shabbat (the five levels of illness) and Mastering Muktseh (the seven categories) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 12 March 2019 Schedule note: Break the following week (Purim); final shiur 26 March. Recap Chazal restricted medicine on Shabbat because remedies once required grinding herbs. Pikuach nefesh is different — a mitzvah overriding Shabbat, better done by a Jew than delegated to a non-Jew. Part A — The Five Levels of Illness Level Description What is permitted 1. Meichush be'alma — mild discomfort Slight headache, sore throat, chapped hands — you carry on as normal No medicine at all (no paracetamol; no rubbing oil into chapped hands) 2. Choleh be'ever echad — pain in one limb Earache, heartburn, a headache you can still work through A Jew may not medicate; you may ask a non-Jew to do something rabbinic (e.g. remove a mostly-detached painful nail; apply Vaseline) 3. Choleh kol gufo — whole-body illness Flu; or one-limb pain so severe you can't function (bad migraine, bedridden toothache or stomach ache) — you wouldn't go to work Medicine permitted ; a non-Jew may do anything , even Torah-level, asked outright; a Jew may do rabbinic acts (preferably with a shinui) — and, per Chacham Ovadia, even a Torah-level act with a shinui if no non-Jew is available 4. Sakanat ever — risk to a limb e.g. a severed finger Today treated as pikuach nefesh — everything permitted 5. Pikuach nefesh Any danger to life Everything permitted — an obligation Paradoxically, a slight ailment (Level 1) is treated more strictly than a worse one. Level 3 examples A non-Jewish helper may make you tea, or go to the chemist for medicine. Alone and bedridden with flu late on Friday night, needing a hot bath: try to find a non-Jew; failing that, turn the hot tap on with an elbow or teeth (a shinui). (Q: may you close it afterwards? — a good question; the \"drive home\" leniency is for pikuach nefesh, and may not extend here.) Lying with a migraine and the light on: ask a non-Jew; otherwise switch it off with an elbow. An LED is only rabbinic anyway; even a filament bulb may be switched off with a shinui in this situation. Frail elderly people and young children (up to about 6–7, when they no longer need their parents for small things) automatically have Level 3 status. Q (elderly with ongoing skin needs) — not a blanket rule; it depends on the actual need. Leniencies for Levels 1–2 Derech bri'im — what healthy people normally do isn't \"medicine\": prune juice for constipation, bananas for diarrhoea, a shot of whisky or vodka for a mild toothache (but not gargling — that's medicinal). A course of treatment begun before Shabbat (antibiotics, a regular hand cream) may be continued — the decree targets someone who discovers a problem on Shabbat and anxiously improvises. Exercise and massage Exercising or a massage in order to sweat (perspiration was a recognised therapy) is forbidden. Exercise or massage for enjoyment or routine (e.g. a strict fitness programme) — strictly permitted, though hardly in the spirit of Shabbat (and you'll want a shower afterwards — cold only). Creams and oils — memare'ach Smoothing is forbidden (hides were smoothed in the Mishkan) only where a smooth layer remains — like wax smeared to seal a barrel. Runny oil — no smoothing issue at all. Cream — rub in a little at a time until fully absorbed, leaving no layer (rabbinic if a layer remains). Thick ointment (Vaseline) spread as a layer — forbidden. Separately, medicinal use must be permitted: sunscreen before sun exposure is prevention — fine (rubbed in); after-sun cream for a mild burn (Level 1–2) — not. Thermometers Measuring is forbidden on Shabbat (linked to writing — one usually records measurements). For a Level 3 patient — permitted; Level 2 — ask a non-Jew. If there is any chance of danger, take the temperature. Part B — Muktseh What it means Muktseh (from katzeh , \"set aside\") — something not designated for use on Shabbat. A rabbinic decree against moving it — not against touching it. A heavy object (a car) may be touched; a light one that would move when touched may not. Four reasons Ra'avad: limits carrying, as a fence around the prohibition of carrying. Rambam (a): a general fence — pens lead to writing, scissors to cutting, scales to measuring. Rambam (b): to keep Shabbat from becoming mundane — just as we walk and speak differently on Shabbat (no business or politics), we handle different things. Rambam (c): so that those who don't work during the week (people of leisure) also feel Shabbat is different. The seven categories Category Examples May it be moved? 1. Kli shemelachto le'issur — a tool whose main use is forbidden Hammer, pen, sieve, empty pot or frying pan, a fan Yes — for a permitted use ( letzorech gufo : hammer to crack a nut, empty pan to hold apples) or for its place ( letzorech mekomo : a pen on your bed). Not to protect it. 2. Muktseh machmat chesron kis — valuable forbidden-use items A mohel's or shochet's knife; computer, washing machine; probably an expensive smartphone No — not even for its place or use 3. Muktseh machmat gufo — not a utensil, no use Stones, sand, money; raw flour, raw potatoes, raw meat or fish No (unless designated before Shabbat, e.g. sand set aside for children to play with) 4. Muktseh machmat issur Fruit that fell from a tree on Shabbat No — lest one pick more 5. Muktseh machmat mitzvah A lulav on Shabbat of Sukkot (also hadassim) No — but an etrog may be moved and smelled 6. Nolad — came into being on Shabbat An egg laid on Shabbat; milk a non-Jew milked on Shabbat No 7. Basis — a base for muktseh A table holding muktseh at the start of Shabbat No Notes on the categories The status is fixed when Shabbat begins. A pot with food in it isn't muktseh and stays permitted after it's emptied; a pan empty at the start of Shabbat is muktseh all Shabbat. Once lifted permissibly, you may carry it on and put it away — you needn't drop it immediately. Garden tools left out in the rain: you may not bring them in to protect them. You may, however, genuinely decide to use the garden space (e.g. for a game), or use a hammer to crack a coconut, and move them in the process. A fan may be repositioned (as long as there's no risk of unplugging it), or turned away if the draught bothers you. A phone left ringing as an alarm: an old basic phone is a kli shemelachto le'issur — move it out of the room for quiet. An expensive smartphone is likely chesron kis . Value is judged objectively, not by sentimental attachment, and may change over time. Paintings and picture frames: Ashkenazim treat valuables never moved (e.g. paintings) as muktseh. For Sephardim, chesron kis applies only to items whose use is forbidden — so a painting, a framed picture or a vase may be moved (not electrical ones). Two ways to move any muktseh Tiltul min hatzad — moving it indirectly via something else (lift the sheet and shake the camera off your bed) — only for the sake of a permitted thing, not to protect the muktseh item. Tiltul begufo — with your body in an unusual way: kick it, blow it, push it with an elbow — permitted even to protect it (e.g. kick a banknote under the bed). Q: Someone left an iPhone on your place in shul? — Move it with your elbows, or tilt the table it sits on (min hatzad, for your space). Asking a non-Jew — only where there's a mitzvah need (e.g. nowhere else to sit). Asking a non-Jew to do a Torah act with a shinui, or a non-Jew to ask another non-Jew — big questions for the final shiur. Summary Five levels: 1 — no medicine; 2 — a non-Jew for rabbinic acts; 3 — medicine permitted, a non-Jew for anything, a Jew with a shinui; 4–5 — everything. Derech bri'im foods and courses begun before Shabbat are permitted. Exercise only for enjoyment. Rub creams in fully; no thick layers. Thermometers only for Level 3 (or via a non-Jew at Level 2). Muktseh is about moving, not touching. Tools with forbidden uses may be moved for a permitted use or their place; valuables, stones, raw food, nolad and bases may not — except indirectly for permitted needs, or with your body. Coming Up Final shiur — 26 March: Children and babies on Shabbat, including childbirth; and asking a non-Jew on Shabbat. Details of a new series after Pesach to follow — leave your details to stay updated. B'Ezrat Hashem","-bBluo9875k":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, conclusion of the discussion of Olam HaBa, and his commentary on the rest of the Mishnah Shiur: Part 6 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 11 November 2025 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Where We Are Over recent weeks: Mashiach, techiyat hametim, Gan Eden and Gehinom in the Rambam's understanding, against Rav Saadia (Monday nights) and the Ramban . All secondary. Tonight: the primary, Olam HaBa , then perhaps a start on the 13 Principles. \" This coming paragraph is extremely important. It summarises the Rambam's whole philosophy: of Judaism, of life, of Olam HaBa, of reward and punishment. This paragraph is everything, really. \" Part 1: The Paragraph והתכלית הוא העולם הבא, ולמענו ההשתדלות. The goal is Olam HaBa, and for it is all the effort. Olam HaBa, for the Rambam: consciousness of Hashem; souls; understanding; a person's identity. ולפיכך זה החכם המחזיק בידיעת האמת עיין בתכלית האחרון והניח מה שזולתו, ואמר: כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא. So Rabbi Yehudah HaNasi , \"the sage who held fast to the truth,\" looked to the final goal and set the rest aside: the Mishnah says Olam HaBa , not Mashiach, not resurrection. But don't serve for it ועם היותו התכלית המבוקש, אינו ראוי למי שרוצה לעבוד מאהבה שיעבוד להשיג העולם הבא. Though it's the goal sought, one who wants to serve from love shouldn't serve in order to attain Olam HaBa. How, then? אבל יש לעבוד על הדרך שאומר: כשיאמין שיש שם חכמה, והיא התורה, שהגיעה לנביאים מאת הבורא יתעלה, שהודיע בה המדות הטובות, והן המצות, והמדות הרעות, והן העבירות, ראוי לו מצד שהוא אדם ישר במזג שיעשה הטובות ויסור מן הרעות. There is objective wisdom: the Torah , revealed to Moshe and all the prophets . In it Hashem made known the good traits, which are the mitzvot , and the bad traits, which are the sins .\" Look how the Rambam calls the mitzvot and sins: middot tovot and middot ra'ot . That's all they are. \" Every mitzvah aims to develop either our moral character or our intellect. He says it in many places. So a balanced, upright human being will simply do the good and avoid the bad . \" Not because there's Olam HaBa afterwards. Because this is the best way to create the best version of myself. \" וכשיעשה זה, ישלם בו ענין האנושי, ויהיה נבדל מן הבהמה. Doing so, he completes his humanity and is distinguished from the animal. \"The key line\" וכשיהיה אדם שלם הוא — מטבע האדם השלם אשר אין לו מונע, שתחיה נפשו ותתקיים בקיום הידוע לה. וזהו העולם הבא. When a person is complete, it's the very nature of the complete person, with nothing holding him back, that his soul lives on and endures through what it has come to know. And that is Olam HaBa. \" Amazing. Olam HaBa is spending eternity in what we worked on and developed here. What am I familiar with? My middot , my intellect, Hashem. That's what I'll spend eternity in.\" \" I really don't like the word reward , but we have no better. There is reward in Olam HaBa. But it's not what we serve for. It's the direct result of how we live. The more developed your character and intellect, the more you're attuned and accustomed to what Olam HaBa is : basking in Hashem's presence . Hashem is shalem , perfection; the more perfect you make yourself, the more similar to Him. \" \"Don't be like a horse or a mule\" אל תהיו כסוס כפרד אין הבין, במתג ורסן עדיו לבלום (Tehillim 32) כמו שמונע את הבהמות דבר מבחוץ כהמתג והרסן : animals are restrained from outside , by bit and bridle. \"Even the best-behaved horse , never wandering onto others' property or hurting anyone, behaves only because its owner muzzled it . David says: don't be like that. Don't keep mitzvot because if I sin I'll burn, if I do a mitzvah I'll be rewarded .\" אבל יהיה המונע אותו מתוכו, מעצמו , רצוני לומר צורתו האנושית, כשתהיה שלמה, היא תמנענו מאותם הדברים שמונעים ממנו השלמות, והן הנקראות מדות רעות. What holds you back must come from within : your perfected human form will restrain you from what blocks your completeness. זה מה שנתבאר לי מכלל דבריהם בענין הנכבד והנעלה הזה. \" He doesn't often talk like that. This is the most elevated and important thing I'm sharing with you. \" Why it matters so much \" I'm being repetitive because it's so important. Many religious people serve from fear : if I don't keep Shabbat or kashrut, if I eat on Yom Kippur, I'll be punished. Or for reward . That's good, better than nothing, but not the ideal. The ideal is an intellectual recognition that the Torah's way is the best way to live on planet earth. Full stop. \" \" If you live with this, you have a completely different Judaism, a completely different relationship with Hashem and with the Torah. Mitzvot become exciting , not impediments. Some religious people see sins as things I wish I could do but can't . It should be: this is best for me, so why would I endanger becoming the best person I can be? It's worth reviewing this paragraph again and again.\" Discussion Robert: once we reach Olam HaBa the clock stops; we can only bask. \" Only if it's something you appreciate , something you're aligned with .\" Robert: some mitzvot obviously refine me; others don't. How does sha'atnez make me a better person, beyond obedience? \" Remember: every mitzvah refines either character or intellect. \" For the Rambam (Moreh) no mitzvah lacks a reason . A chok isn't a law without a reason (as Rashi , for example, holds) but one whose reason is harder to see : \" both the Rambam and the Ramban are very clear. \" Sha'atnez , the Rambam says, was worn by pagan priests in their idol worship. Refraining from it awakens awareness of Hashem's unity : intellect and character together . \"And parah adumah ? I don't even understand why tumah is bad.\" \" How it works, we don't know. Shlomo didn't. But revelation tells us tumah creates a barrier between us and purity, and that the heifer purifies, and purification makes me better. The Rambam doesn't say you must understand why these eight sheratzim defile. Within revelation, every mitzvah leads back to character or intellect. Lulav , for example: we're praying for rain , recognising that all sustenance comes from Hashem .\" \" Happy to continue offline. \" Richard: this isn't one of the 13 Principles. Shouldn't it be printed with them? \" It's beyond a principle. It's the basis of everything. One of the most important things we've ever learned together. The problem is we fall into routine and forget , which is why we keep reminding ourselves.\" Part 2: The Book He Never Wrote And in that work I'll gather all the derashot in the Talmud and explain them: which are literal , which parables , which were dreams (e.g. Rabbi Yishmael seeing Hashem wearing tefillin in Berachot: \" a dream, with a deeper message \"), with many principles of faith . He never wrote it. In the Moreh he explains why: flesh out the derashot fully and you expose what the Sages meant to keep partly hidden ; substitute other parables , and \" why would my parable be better than the Sages'? \" So he abandoned it. \" Such a shame. It would have been incredible. \" He was in his early twenties when he wrote this introduction: \"this deep concept, in his early 20s.\" \"I wasn't philosophically precise\" : deliberately, to lay out a most important principle simply , \" so everyone can understand it .\" Part 3: The Rest of the Mishnah ואלו שאין להם חלק לעולם הבא: האומר אין תחיית המתים מן התורה, ואין תורה מן השמים, ואפיקורוס. Apikoros ומלת אפיקורוס היא ארמית, וענינה מי שמפקיר ומבזה את התורה או לומדיה : from הפקר , treating the Torah or its students as worthless . So anyone who doesn't believe in the foundations of the Torah is called an apikoros , as is one who disgraces Sages, any Torah scholar, or his own teacher. Disgracing a Sage isn't heresy in itself , but it's called apikorsut for its consequence : the Sages carry the tradition ; despise them, and you'll stop keeping and come to deny the Torah . The real etymology: \" The Rambam knew perfectly well it comes from Epicurus . \" He quotes Epicurus in the Moreh as a heretic, and Rav Yosef Albo says so explicitly. \"The Sages probably knew too, and played on the word . He's following the Gemara's derashah . \" Reading \"external books\" Rabbi Akiva: אף הקורא בספרים החיצונים. The Rambam: books of those in error : heresy . Hilchot Avodah Zarah: don't read idolaters' books or even dwell on their views, because human understanding is limited , and people are easily swayed, \"especially by something printed in a book.\" You'll start to doubt Hashem's unity, whether the Torah is true , whether revelation happened . ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם. But rabbis read them. \" Rabbi Sacks , zichrono livrachah (his yahrzeit is this week), often quoted heretical books. The Rambam quotes heretical views throughout the Moreh. The Meiri , and everyone agrees: you may read them in order to know how to answer them. Not just for fun or curiosity. \" The God Delusion. \"A student once read Richard Dawkins' book; he said it shattered his faith . I hadn't read it, so I did , and we had a long conversation. That's the kind of reason. \" Ben Sira The Gemara adds Ben Sira : by the Rambam's account, \"nonsense\" about physiognomy , reading faces and palms , a waste of time . Likewise Arabic chronicles , genealogies of kings, song books : no wisdom and no bodily benefit, just lost time. (Physiognomy, the class found: \"once considered a science, now widely regarded as a pseudoscience. The Rambam already knew.\") Who was Ben Sira? A midrash (cited by Maharam Mintz ) says he was Yirmeyahu's son , conceived when a woman bathed after Yirmeyahu in a bathhouse; Sira = Yirmeyahu in gematria . \"Scientifically impossible, but it has halachic ramifications .\" The Chelkat Mechokek and Beit Shmuel (Even HaEzer) ask, citing it: has such a father fulfilled pru u'revu , and is the child his? The poskim learn from it about artificial insemination. Robert: isn't that learning halachah from aggadah? \" When there's nowhere else to learn from, most hold you may. \" Why lose Olam HaBa for nonsense books? (Notably, Hilchot Teshuvah ch. 3 omits this.) 1. A cause of a cause : nonsense leads to reading anything, including heresy . 2. The Yerushalmi: Ben Sira forbidden, Homer permitted , \" like reading a letter .\" Why? Ben Sira is written in Tanach's style , so readers mistake it for scripture . \" No one reading Homer thinks they're reading Tanach. \" Healing with verses הלוחש על המכה… כל המחלה אשר שמתי במצרים לא אשים עליך כי אני ה׳ רפאך : with spitting , a pagan practice, a disgrace to Hashem . The Mishneh Torah (Hilchot Avodah Zarah) goes further, \" most people are shocked when they read it, because so many people do this \": One who recites a verse over a wound , or over a child so he won't be frightened , or puts a Sefer Torah or tefillin by an infant so he'll sleep : not only are they diviners and charmers, they're among those who deny the Torah , for they make the words of Torah a cure for the body, when they are only a cure for the soul : והיו חיים לנפשך . But a healthy person may recite verses and Tehillim so that the merit of reciting protects him . \"Like the tiny Tehillim or Raziel HaMalach under a child's pillow. The Torah is to study and learn from , not for financial or health gain.\" Pronouncing the Name Abba Shaul: ההוגה את השם באותיותיו : saying the four-letter Name as written, in one go. ( Rashi : the 42-letter Name.) \"There's a sect of Christians named after it (' something's Witnesses '). Never say that word. \" And more, from elsewhere המלבין פני חברו ברבים, המכנה שם לחברו, המתכבד בקלון חברו : shaming someone publicly , calling people nicknames , building yourself up on another's disgrace . Why lose Olam HaBa for \"light\" sins? Only someone of a deficient soul would do these, lacking the completeness Olam HaBa requires . \" A siman, not a sibah , as we'd say in yeshivah: a sign, not a cause. \" Hilchot Teshuvah: it means habitually . \" Embarrass someone once , it's wrong, but you won't lose Olam HaBa.\" Summary The paragraph that holds everything: the goal is Olam HaBa , but don't serve for it . Believe the Torah is revealed wisdom showing the good traits (mitzvot) and bad (sins) , and do good because it's right . Then you become complete , and a complete soul endures in what it knows: that is Olam HaBa. Not like a horse with a bit : the restraint must come from within , from our perfected humanity . Fear and reward: \" better than nothing, not the ideal. \" Every mitzvah develops character or intellect : even sha'atnez (against idolatry) and parah adumah (within revelation). The derashot book was abandoned : full exposure would betray the Sages' concealment. The Mishnah's list: the apikoros (a play on hefker ; really Epicurus ); heretical books (allowed to answer them : Dawkins ); Ben Sira , mistakable for scripture (unlike Homer ); verses as medicine (even tefillin by a baby's pillow , per the Mishneh Torah); the Name ; and habitual shaming, nicknaming, self-aggrandising : signs, not causes . Coming Up ומה שצריך שנזכיר בכאן… עיקרי דתנו ויסודותיה, והם שלשה עשר יסודות. Next week: the concept of principles of faith , and Principle 1 . \"Very short in the Rambam, but there's so much to discuss.\"","5HfakYPTMy0":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: the introduction to the 13 Principles, and the start of the First Principle Shiur: Part 7 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 18 November 2025 (the week of Chayei Sarah) These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Where We Are On כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא , the Rambam explained Olam HaBa and its \"corollary\" forms (Gan Eden, Gehinom, Mashiach, resurrection): Olam HaBa is the essence of everything we do. Now: ומה שצריך שנזכיר בכאן, והוא הראוי מכל המקומות, שעיקרי דתנו ויסודותיה שלשה עשר יסודות. Here is the most fitting place : the roots ( ikkarim ) of our religion and its foundations are thirteen. Since the Mishnah discusses who has no portion , this is where to set out the foundations . \"He's in his twenties, and he's the first to do this.\" Part 1: The First Jewish Creed \"Judaism has a dogma of dogmalessness\" : a line attributed to Moses Mendelssohn , and argued by some Jewish academics: Judaism is about practice , not belief. \" That is completely wrong, in my opinion, and definitely in the Rambam's. \" There are beliefs every Jew must hold, scattered across Torah and Chazal. Rav Saadia (Monday nights) says we must believe in Mashiach, in resurrection , but made no list . The Rambam (12th century) is the first to collate a creed. \"That part, everyone can agree is authentic. The emphasis is uniquely his\": intellect in serving Hashem; mitzvot must be accompanied by understanding . Belief over practice: the convert The Gemara (Yevamot 47a) The Rambam (Issurei Bi'ah 14:1–2) Why convert? Don't you see Israel is afflicted, oppressed, harried? If he says I know, and I'm unworthy , accept him immediately The same , almost word for word Teach him some lighter and some weightier mitzvot ; leket, shichechah, pe'ah, ma'aser ani ; the punishment ( until now forbidden fat and Shabbat carried no karet or stoning; now they do ) and reward ומודיעין אותו עיקרי הדת, שהוא יחוד השם ואיסור עבודה זרה, ומאריכין בדבר הזה. Teach him the foundations of the faith, Hashem's unity and the prohibition of idolatry, and elaborate at length . Then some mitzvot, ואין מאריכין בדבר זה : and don't elaborate \" He's turned the Gemara on its head , and added something he made up himself . How can he? For the Rambam it was obvious : you can't accept a convert who doesn't know the fundamentals of belief .\" (Halachot 3 and 4, on Olam HaBa, are from the Gemara, \"and we still say them at the end of a conversion today.\") Part 2: The Thirteen at a Glance # Principle God 1 Hashem exists 2 Hashem is one 3 Hashem is incorporeal : no body, no physicality 4 Creation from nothing (what that means isn't simple) 5 Worship Hashem alone : no avodah zarah Revelation 6 Prophecy 7 Moshe is the greatest prophet 8 Torah from heaven 9 The Torah won't change Reward and punishment 10 Hashem knows our actions 11 He rewards good and punishes evil 12 Mashiach will come 13 Resurrection Part 3: It Took Time to Be Accepted Today everyone says 13 . \"It wasn't so simple.\" Who How many The Rambam 13 Rav Yosef Albo ( Sefer HaIkkarim , Spain, 1400s) 3 : God, revelation, reward and punishment . If there's a Creator, He'd reveal His will; if He did, there must be consequences Rav Chasdai Crescas A different formulation (as the Radbaz puts it, really one ) The Radbaz (Rav David ben Zimra, Egypt, 1500s, Responsa 344) None שאלת ממני על עיקרי הדת, על איזה חשבון אני מסכים : the Rambam's 13, Crescas's one, or Albo's three? אין דעתי מסכמת לשום דבר מתורתנו התמימה שהוא עיקר, לפי שכולה עיקר נאמרה מפי הגבורה. I don't agree that any part of our Torah is an ikkar, because all of it is. כל האומר כל התורה כולה מן השמים חוץ מפסוק אחד הוא כופר, so every mitzvah is a root and cornerstone. ומי יכניס ראשו בין ההרים הגדולים? Who can rank a light mitzvah against a severe one? He follows Abarbanel: every detail of the Torah is a root; deny any, and you're a kofer . \" The Radbaz has a very strong point. So what are the 13 above everything else?\" Part 4: Then Why Principles at All? Three Answers 1. Rav Yosef Albo: true vs foundational The whole Torah is emet. Some things are also ikkarim . The difference is focus : you must believe everything, but some things need more attention . 2. Here, ignorance is no excuse Principle 3: Hashem has no body. The Rambam: believe He does, and you're a heretic with no portion , even if that's how you were naively raised ( Hashem sits on a throne in heaven ). The Ra'avad: of course He has no body (Rav Saadia devotes an essay to it), but greater men than you believed He did (some French Tosafists ). Call them heretics for a mistake? The famous answer (usually said in Yiddish): misken , I'm sorry for you, but if you \"believe in God\" and to you \"God\" means a pencil, you don't believe in God. \"Saying the words doesn't make you a believer. A physical God, however big, isn't God. I'm sorry that's how you were taught, but you have no understanding of Godliness. \" Elsewhere, an honest mistake costs nothing : a naive, even \"Islamic\" picture of Gan Eden and Gehinom is just an error. These 13 you must know, with no excuse. A nebach apikoros is also an apikoros . Mark: wasn't the Rambam reacting to Islam and Christianity around him? \"Interesting: all 13 have sources in Torah or Chazal except the third. Nowhere do they say Hashem has no body. It's his own reasoning . Rav Saadia was equally adamant.\" Perhaps a reaction : \"Now I think of it, in one of his letters he writes that he formulated the 13 because he saw great scholars, who knew all of Shas, with babyish beliefs . I think he meant exactly this.\" 3. They define who is \"in Klal Yisrael\" At the end of the 13: וכאשר יאמין האדם אלה היסודות כולם ונתבררה אמונתו בהם, הוא נכנס בכלל ישראל, ומצוה לאהבו ולחמול עליו… ואפילו עשה כל העבירות מחמת תאותו ותגבורת יצרו הרע… וכשיפקפק אדם ביסוד מאלו היסודות, הרי זה יצא מן הכלל וכפר בעיקר, ונקרא מין ואפיקורוס וקוצץ בנטיעות, ומצוה לשנאתו ולאבדו. Believe them all, and you enter Klal Yisrael : we must love and care for you, even if you've committed every sin out of desire . Doubt one, and you've left the community : a min , an apikoros . \"Enter Klal Yisrael\"? Wasn't he Jewish already? Still valid מומר, קידושיו קידושין : a heretic's kiddushin is fully binding (Shulchan Aruch); his get works. A gentile's kiddushin needs no get; a heretic's does. Jewish lineage suffices Requires shem Yisrael Shechitah : an apikoros 's shechitah isn't kosher. The mitzvot to help and care for a fellow Jew Part 5: Two Later Versions Form Origin יגדל Poetic Uncertain; some say Rabbi Daniel ben Yehudah of Rome , early 1400s. Certainly by the late 1400s. Sung Friday night. \" The Arizal didn't like it , not entirely clear why, so many communities don't sing it after Arvit; the old Sephardi custom always did \" אני מאמין באמונה שלמה Prose Uncertain. Earliest print: a 1502 Haggadah , at Nirtzah . It was in the Livorno Sephardi siddurim; less common in Sephardi siddurim today \"We'll compare each principle against them. They're not always precise to the Rambam. \" Part 6: What \"Emunah\" Really Means ונתבררה אמונתו בהם : his belief has become clear to him. That's the Rambam's emunah sheleimah . The Christian distortion. \"Many concepts in English, and even in Judaism, have been severely affected by Christian philosophy : I've said it of reward and punishment , and it's true of belief .\" The Oxford Dictionary: faith: strong belief… based on spiritual conviction rather than proof . An early Christian writer (the line is usually attributed to Tertullian): I believe because it is absurd. \" The more absurd, the more you need faith.**\" \"That is absolutely not what emunah means. The complete opposite.\" - אמן is the root: ותקעתיו יתד במקום נאמן, a peg in a firm place; ומצאת את לבבו נאמן לפניך, Avraham's heart steadfast; ויהי אומן את הדסה, to raise and train. - \"There's a creature in Australia I've never seen: I trust you, I believe you. That's not emunah.\" - The Ramchal: כל איש מישראל צריך שיאמין וידע: believe and know . Emunah is tantamount to knowledge. - So באמונה שלמה isn't strictly necessary; אני מאמין already means it. Rav Moshe Feinstein (Igrot Moshe) : may religious parents eat at a non-observant child's home? A child isn't believed as a witness (that's trusting testimony). But if the parents know with certainty the child respects them and wouldn't deceive them, it's barur to them : they know . \" The difference between believing something and knowing it. That's real emunah , and why it's a lifelong project , and why the Rambam counts it among the 613 .\" Haran in the furnace וימת הרן על פני תרח אביו באור כשדים. The simple meaning: Haran died in his father's lifetime . Targum Yonatan ben Uziel: Haran watched Avraham thrown into the furnace , undecided: If he comes out, I'll follow his God; if not, I won't. Avraham emerged unscathed, Haran jumped in, and died. \"One of my rabbis said: his problem was living with doubt : an agnostic's wager.\" Pascal's wager (believe, since 120 years' inconvenience beats eternal hell) fails, \"not least because it works for every religion. You can't live your life with constant doubts. Make decisions, have convictions, even if it's the wrong one. \" Why send to Ur Kasdim for a wife? Avraham and Sarah's kiruv in Canaan brought thousands and tens of thousands . Yet Yaakov's family goes down to Egypt seventy souls . Where did they go? The Ran ( Derashot ): easy come, easy go : people inspired by one shiur leave after one contrary book. In Ur Kasdim they had such conviction they threw Avraham into a fire. \"Avraham wanted a girl with conviction , even in the wrong faith . That's what we mean by emunah . \" Part 7: The First Principle היסוד הראשון: להאמין מציאות הבורא יתברך, והוא שיש שם נמצא שלם בכל דרכי המציאות, והוא עילת מציאות הנמצאים כלם, בו קיום מציאותם וממנו קיומם. ואל יעלה על הלב העדר מציאותו, כי בהעדר מציאותו נתבטלה מציאות כל הנמצאים ולא נשאר נמצא שיתקיים מציאותו. ואם נעלה על לבנו העדר כל הנמצאים זולתו, לא תתבטל מציאות השם יתברך ולא תגרע… כי הוא מסתפק במציאותו ודי לו בעצמו, ואין צורך במציאות זולתו. וכל מה שזולתו מן המלאכים וגופי הגלגלים ומה שבתוכם ומה שמתחתיהם, הכל צריכים למציאותם אליו. וזה היסוד הראשון, מורה עליו הדבור אנכי ה׳ אלהיך. \" People say the First Principle is just believe in God . There's much more. He has to define what God means, because a distorted understanding isn't belief in God. \" Five elements # Element Words 1 There is a Creator (Mishneh Torah: מצוי ראשון , the Primal Being) מציאות הבורא 2 He is perfect. \" An imperfect God is an oxymoron. A God who can't stop the bad in the world isn't God\" שלם בכל דרכי המציאות 3 He is self-sufficient , needing nothing , including His creations (really 2b) מסתפק במציאותו ודי לו בעצמו 4 Everything else exists only through Him, continuously בו קיום מציאותם וממנו קיומם 5 His existence is necessary : without Him nothing would exist; without everything else, He'd be undiminished אל יעלה על הלב העדר מציאותו The Mishneh Torah opens with exactly this , a halachic book: יסוד היסודות ועמוד החכמות, לידע שיש שם מצוי ראשון, והוא ממציא כל נמצא. \" You can't learn halachah until you know this properly. \" That's ה׳ אלהים אמת : everything else needn't exist; He must. Against Yigdal and Ani Ma'amin Has Lacks יגדל אלהים חי וישתבח, נמצא ואין עת אל מציאותו A God who exists , beyond time ; perhaps חי = continually sustaining Perfection , and most of the rest אני מאמין… שהבורא יתברך שמו הוא בורא ומנהיג לכל הברואים, והוא לבדו עשה ועושה ויעשה לכל המעשים Creator , and continuous sustainer Perfection \"They're summaries. The real thing is the Rambam. \" Summary The Rambam is the first to formulate a creed. \" A dogma of dogmalessness \" is wrong. His emphasis on belief : converts are taught the foundations at length, mitzvot briefly , his own addition to the Gemara. 13 in three groups: God (1–5), revelation (6–9), reward and punishment (10–13). Not immediately accepted: Albo 3, Crescas fewer, the Radbaz none ( the whole Torah is an ikkar ). Why principles? (a) Albo : all true, some need focus ; (b) no excuse of ignorance : a God who's \"a pencil\" isn't God (Ra'avad vs Rambam on corporeality, the one principle without a source ); (c) they define entry into Klal Yisrael : kiddushin still works, shechitah doesn't. Yigdal and Ani Ma'amin : later, not always precise. Emunah isn't \"belief without proof\" : from amen , firmness. Believe and know (Ramchal; Rav Moshe on the non-observant child). Conviction : Haran's wager; Avraham's wife from Ur Kasdim . Principle 1 : five elements, Creator, perfect, self-sufficient, sustaining all, necessary : the opening of the Mishneh Torah . Coming Up Next week: each of the five elements in depth, with the Rishonim's arguments for God's existence (\"nowhere in Tanach or Chazal was it ever doubted; only the Rishonim began to argue it\"), then Principle 2 .","7w7RMatc5lQ":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, continued: where this-worldly reward, Gan Eden and Gehinom fit Shiur: Part 3 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 16 September 2025 (the week of Ki Tavo, before Rosh Hashanah) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Five views of the afterlife: Gan Eden , Mashiach , techiyat hametim , this world , all combined . All miss the real thing: Olam HaBa . Three readings of aggadah : literal ; literal, so the Sages were wrong (which angered the Rambam); and metaphorical , the true view, held by very few. Read the Sages physically, and you'll imagine a physical reward. Olam HaBa : עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך . Wholly spiritual, so a physical body can't comprehend it : the fish and heat , the blind man and colours . The Sages used graphic images so the wise could draw out something of the truth. A correction, mid-sentence. \"And that's what our focus is on… let me rephrase. The Rambam wouldn't be happy if I said that.\" We don't serve Hashem for Olam HaBa. We are עושי האמת מפני שהוא אמת : we keep the mitzvot because they're best for our character, identity, consciousness and intellect . Olam HaBa is what results. Olam HaBa = spiritual identity, cleaving to Hashem : grasping Godliness. The more refined your intellect and character, the closer the connection and the greater the bliss. The Queen. \"If Queen Elizabeth , zichronah , announced she'd spend time with people according to how refined and 'royal' they'd become, the more you'd learned royal etiquette and refined yourself, the closer you'd get . Swap in whoever you'd most love to meet. That's Olam HaBa. \" Tonight: where the other \"rewards,\" with sources in Torah and Chazal, fit around it. Part 1: Reward and Punishment in This World The Torah's blessings and curses ( \"didn't we just read them on Shabbat?\" ): היעודים הטובים והנקמות הרעות הכתובות בתורה, ענינם… כי הוא אומר לך: אם תעשה המצות האלה, אסייע לך על עשייתם והשלמות בהם, ואסיר מעליך המעיקים והמונעים כלם. If you keep these mitzvot, I'll help you keep them and perfect yourself in them, and remove every obstacle. לפי שהאדם אי אפשר לו לעשות המצות כשהוא חולה ורעב וצמא, ולא בשעת מלחמה. No one can keep mitzvot while ill, hungry, thirsty or at war. So the Torah promises those will go, so that they can complete their knowledge and merit Olam HaBa. Maslow's hierarchy. \"You can't think about culture or friendships before food and shelter. You can't think about Olam HaBa, about who you want to be, while you're fighting to put bread on the table or going from one illness to the next.\" And the curses: abandon the Torah, and those very conditions are withheld , until you can't keep the mitzvot. A new reading of Ki Tavo תחת אשר לא עבדת את ה׳ אלהיך בשמחה ובטוב לבב מרב כל (Devarim 28:47) Bang in the middle of the curses. The Rishonim ask: is the punishment for not serving , or for not serving joyfully ? Reading The common reading You served, but without joy , so you were punished The Rambam's You didn't serve Hashem when you had joy, a settled heart and plenty : no illness, no money worries, every ability to serve . Not serving while fighting to survive is one thing; with everything and still not serving , Hashem says: the conditions I gave you don't help you, so I'll take them away אם עשית קצת מצות מאהבה והשתדלות, אעזרך לעשות כולם… ואם תעזוב דבר מהם דרך בזיון, אביא לך מונעים שימנעוך מעשותם כולם. \"The reward of a mitzvah is a mitzvah\" שכר מצוה מצוה ושכר עבירה עבירה (Avot 4:2) For the Rambam, who holds mitzvot aren't done for reward , this is the simplest reading: the reward of a mitzvah is the ability to do more (Hashem grants livelihood, health, peace of mind); the consequence of a sin is losing that ability: under-development . \" Isn't this beautiful? According to the Rambam there's no reward and punishment written in the Torah at all. \" Rabbi Akiva: שכר מצוה בהאי עלמא ליכא : no reward in this world. \"Seemingly preposterous, when the Torah says do this, get that over and over. The Rambam: correct, because those aren't rewards. \" בדרך שאדם רוצה לילך מוליכין אותו : Hashem leads you down the path you choose. כל המקיים את התורה מעוני, סופו לקיימה מעושר; וכל המבטל את התורה מעושר, סופו לבטלה מעוני (Avot 4:9). Wealth in every sense: money, health, peace of mind. Why doesn't the Torah mention Olam HaBa? (Every Rishon asks; Monday night's topic.) Because the Torah doesn't talk about reward at all. \"Don't lose your life\" Rabbi Levi Yitzchak of Berditchev saw a man run out of shul mid-prayer: I have to get to work, to make a living. While you're running to make a living, be careful you don't lose your life. \"We're so busy with day-to-day things that we can't think about our mission in life . This season forces us to. \" The paradox of progress. \"We used to wash clothes in the sea ; now we throw them in a washing machine . We should have so much more time to develop ourselves. But more time means more distractions. Without that free time there'd be no Hollywood . The question is what we do with it. Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur force us to ask. \" A question: doesn't this make tzaddik vera lo worse? If good deeds lead Hashem to remove obstacles, why do the righteous struggle? \" I hear the question, and I've never thought of it that way. You're right: the Rambam's approach does make it bigger , because you can't push it into some future calculation. Theodicy is the biggest question we've always had.\" We never really know what's going on in another person. The Rambam himself wrote in a letter that he suffered depression for a couple of years after his brother died. Rav Chaim Vital (the Arizal's transcriber, the source of most Kabbalah we have) wrote that for two formative years (around 23 to 25) he couldn't learn , and wanted to study chemistry instead. אין צדיק בארץ אשר לא יחטא ; וסביביו נשערה מאד : Hashem may be stricter with those close to Him. \"Perhaps it's relative: it would have been harder.\" \" That's always the way out, I suppose. \" Part 2: Gan Eden A show of hands: Gan Eden is after death? Or in this world? \"As a kid I always thought it was some beautiful place in heaven, after we die . Some Rishonim say so. The Rambam doesn't. He holds Gan Eden is a physical place on this earth. \" וגן עדן הוא מקום דשן, מבחר הארץ, ורבים בו הנהרות והאילנות העושים פירות, יגלה אותו השם לבני אדם לעתיד לבוא, ויורה אותם הדרך ללכת בו, ויתענגו בו. ואפשר שימצאו בו צמחים נפלאים מאד, גדולי התועלת, ערבים מאד, מלבד הידועים אצלנו. וכל זה אינו נמנע ולא רחוק, אבל קרוב להיות. A fertile place, the choicest on earth , full of rivers and fruit trees, which Hashem will reveal in the future and show us the way to it . Perhaps it holds wondrous plants , beneficial and sweet, beyond those we know. Not impossible or far-fetched , all the more so since it's written in the Torah. \"In the future\" = Mashiach's days or the resurrection; not revealed now . \"Show us the way\" : because Adam was expelled : ויגרש את האדם, וישכן מקדם לגן עדן את הכרבים ואת להט החרב המתהפכת לשמר את דרך עץ החיים . \" It seems a physical place , guarded by an ever-turning flaming sword. That's the simple reading , and the Rambam's.\" \"But Google Maps…\" \"I think we've mapped every metre of the planet.\" (Don: a quick search says 97–99%.) Caves; the ocean is less mapped than outer space. \"It is slightly problematic , I'll be honest.\" The Ramban ( Sha'ar HaGemul ): also physical, below the equator . He'd heard reports from India of sightings of the ever-turning flame . \"It's in Iran!\" \"Where in Iran?\" \"I read it. Persian people told me.\" \" Let's go and see it. \" \"After we conquer Iran?\" \"Maybe that's why we don't know yet.\" The Ben Ish Chai's difficulty: the Gemara says the world is 1/60 of the Garden , the Garden 1/60 of Eden. So if Gan Eden is on earth, it must be vast but take up no space , like the Ark and Keruvim that took up no room in the Holy of Holies. \"I don't think the Rambam would accept that; he doesn't like impossibilities.\" The key point: it's very physical : beautiful waters, drinks, \"wine pouring out.\" So group 1 isn't wrong , but it isn't the ultimate. It's one stage. Part 3: Gehinom Three or four lines , against pages on Mashiach and resurrection. ואמנם גיהנם הוא שם לצער ולעונש שישיג לרשעים, ולא פרשו בתלמוד תואר זה העונש. A name for the pain and punishment of the wicked. The Talmud never described its nature. Some say Proof The sun will draw near and burn them הנה היום בא בער כתנור (Malachi) A strange heat will arise in their bodies and burn them רוחכם אש תאכלכם External heat or internal heat. That's it. The Shelah points out: in the entire Mishneh Torah there is no real mention of Gehinom , only in passing, quoting a Gemara that a careless judge may go there. Gan Eden appears only indirectly, in the Sheva Berachot : שמח תשמח… כשמחך יצירך בגן עדן מקדם . They are not principles of faith. Deny resurrection, Mashiach or eternity , and you're a heretic. Deny Gan Eden or Gehinom : you contradict the Sages, but it's minor in the grand scheme. This is crucial. The Ramban's reading of the Rambam Some accused the Rambam, so brief on Gehinom, of not believing in it . The Ramban: not true. \"I think his reading is correct.\" First, recall: the ultimate punishment for the Rambam is karet , the soul ceases to exist . (Class: \"to go to hell?\" \"No.\" \"Karet.\" \"Good.\") The Ramban's questions: Justice. One man kept the whole Torah but once , knowingly, ate on Yom Kippur and never repented; another ate every Yom Kippur. Both: karet. Fair? The Mishnah's list. Many sins carry karet : so what's special about the heretic , singled out as having no portion ? (This one can be answered: there are levels of karet.) His answer, in the Rambam's name: before karet comes a process : Gehinom , up to twelve months , proportionate to the severity. The worst (a kofer ) gets karet and intense Gehinom. Kaddish. \"That's why people say Kaddish twelve months : the Gemara says Gehinom lasts at most twelve. People make a massive thing of it, every prayer. In the grand scheme, it's not a major thing. \" So: Nature Duration Principle of faith? Gan Eden Physical pleasure Temporary No Gehinom Physical burning , proportionate to the sin Temporary No Olam HaBa The soul's eternal intellectual bliss Forever Yes Karet The soul ceases Final Other Rishonim: \"waiting rooms\" The Rambam The Ramban, Ramchal and others Olam HaBa Soul only : pure intellect, consciousness Body and soul together , in a future, renewed world When The moment you die : \"the world after this one\" In the future Gan Eden / Gehinom Physical places on earth , in the future Spiritual waiting rooms where souls are refined (after 120 years in the body) until that world comes Why the difference No gap to fill: so they can't be waiting rooms Open questions Who enjoys the Rambam's Gan Eden: the body or the soul? The body's in the grave! \" Excellent question. \" A participant: maybe they're spiritual places in this world, invisible now, physical later. \" Like the Ben Ish Chai. But for the Rambam it's physical : one day we'll sit, eat and drink there.\" So how do the dead wicked receive a physical Gehinom? The Rambam says resurrection is only for the righteous. Will the wicked be revived to be punished? \" I don't think the Rambam believes that. I need to think about it and check. I don't have an answer now. \" Summary Olam HaBa is the core , but it's the result of doing truth, not the motive. This-worldly blessings and curses aren't reward or punishment: Hashem removes, or imposes, the obstacles to serving Him. Ki Tavo: you didn't serve when you had everything . שכר מצוה מצוה. So the Torah promises no reward at all. (It does sharpen tzaddik vera lo .) Gan Eden is a physical place on earth , to be revealed in the future. Real, but one stage , not the ultimate. Gehinom : a name for punishment , some burning, undefined. Per the Ramban's reading, a temporary , proportionate stage before karet . Neither is a principle of faith ; the Mishneh Torah hardly mentions them. Resurrection and Mashiach are . Other Rishonim: Gan Eden and Gehinom are spiritual waiting rooms before a body-and-soul Olam HaBa. The Rambam's Olam HaBa begins at death. Coming Up Techiyat hametim, \"a foundation of Moshe Rabbeinu's Torah,\" and Mashiach at length; then: \"the ultimate is Olam HaBa,\" and the 13 Principles . The schedule: Rosh Hashanah next Tuesday, then Erev Yom Kippur, Sukkot and Simchat Torah, so probably no Tuesday night shiur until the week after Simchat Torah (perhaps a Tuesday-morning one; watch the group). A blessing: \"May we have all the blessings promised (happiness, livelihood, health) so that, with simchah, rov kol and tuv levav , we can come to Hashem, work on ourselves, and become the best versions of ourselves. \"","AtBwy5-rB0E":"Dayan Daniel Kada · Thursday Night Halacha · Shiur 7 of 7, the final shiur Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 682, 683, 684 and 677 This shiur was dedicated לעילוי נשמת יצחק בן אלכסנדר, and the Rabbi wished Maor a long life. The Whole Course at a Glance Siman Topic 670 What may not be done on Hanukah 671 Where to place the menorah 672 When to light 673 Fuels 674 Lighting from one light to another 675 The lighting makes the mitzvah, not the placing 676 The order of the berachot and the direction of lighting; the berachah on seeing 678 Shabbat candles take precedence over Hanukah lights when money is short 679 Friday: light Hanukah first 680 Don't place it where the door will blow it out on Shabbat 681 Havdalah: in shul, menorah first; at home, the Sephardi practice is Havdalah first Tonight: 682, 683 and 684, then 677 , travelling and being a guest on Hanukah, \"and we will complete Hilchot Hanukah this evening.\" Siman 682: Al HaNissim Seif 1: Where it is said, and what if you forget כל שמונת ימי חנוכה אומרים על הנסים בברכת המזון בברכת הארץ, ובתפלה בברכת מודים. ואם לא אמר, אין מחזירין אותו. Why in those places? Hanukah is all about הלל והודאה , praise and thanksgiving . So Al HaNissim goes where we give thanks: - In the Amidah, in מודים (\"We thank You\"). - In Birkat HaMazon, in נודה לך (\"We thank You\"). Contrast the festivals and Rosh Ḥodesh, whose additions ( רצה והחליצנו , יעלה ויבא ) go into the third berachah, רחם . Why not repeat if forgotten? The Gemara itself says one may include it, but doesn't have to. It does not have the status of יעלה ויבא on the festivals and Rosh Ḥodesh. Why not in Al HaMiḥyah? In על המחיה we add a mention of the festivals (the three regalim and Rosh HaShanah) and of Rosh Ḥodesh , but nothing for Hanukah or Purim . Why? A simple answer fails: \"Only biblical days get an addition.\" But Rosh Ḥodesh is mentioned ( וזכרנו לטובה ביום ראש החדש הזה ) and is rabbinic in its commemoration, though one could argue its concept is biblical. The Rishonim note that the Yerushalmi implies we should add it, and some Aḥaronim, including R. Ḥayim Palagi , did include Hanukah and Purim in Al HaMiḥyah, \"believe it or not.\" The Rabbi knows of no community that does so today. Three explanations: 1. Tosafot: frequency. Al HaMiḥyah is said less often than the Amidah and Birkat HaMazon, so no addition was instituted. But that doesn't explain Rosh Ḥodesh and the festivals. 2. Structure. Follow the text: - על המחיה ועל הכלכלה ועל תנובת השדה : sustenance (= Birkat HaMazon's first berachah). - ועל ארץ חמדה טובה ורחבה... : the land (= the second berachah, נודה לך... על ארץ ). - רחם נא... : mercy (= the third berachah). There is no explicit \"we thank You\" in Al HaMiḥyah. Additions normally go after רחם , before ובנה ירושלים , which suits רצה and יעלה ויבא . Al HaNissim belongs in a thanksgiving passage, and there is none, so inserting it would be an interruption. 3. The Brisker Rav, the Rabbi's favourite: - Al HaMiḥyah's real name is ברכה מעין שלש , a single berachah summarising Birkat HaMazon. Really four berachot, since כי אתה טוב ומטיב reflects the fourth, but Birkat HaMazon's core is the first three, up to בונה ירושלים אמן . - A little-known law: if you forgot יעלה ויבא in Birkat HaMazon and remember after בונה ירושלים אמן but before starting the fourth berachah , you insert a special berachah : ברוך אתה ה'... שנתן ראשי חדשים לעמו ישראל לזכרון (or ...ימים טובים... ). (If you remember before saying Hashem's name in בונה ירושלים, go back to רחם . If you have begun the fourth berachah, even just \"ברוך,\" it's too late.) Many siddurim omit it because it is so rarely needed. - So Rosh Ḥodesh and the festivals can generate a berachah of their own within Birkat HaMazon, which is why the summary berachah mentions them. - Al HaNissim never generates a berachah of its own. Forget it and that's it. So it has no place in the summary. \"על הנסים\" or \"ועל הנסים\"? Ashkenazim say ועל הנסים , as does the S&P siddur (and, the Rabbi understands, Ḥacham Ovadia), with the vav showing that it continues the preceding thanksgiving. Most Sephardi siddurim say על הנסים . \"So, Ashkenazim do it [too], so that's what I do.\" When you are obliged to repeat Birkat HaMazon The general principle: where there is an obligation to eat a se'udah , forgetting to mention the day means repeating . Where there is no obligation, you don't (with some exceptions). Meal Forgot רצה? Friday night Everyone agrees: repeat Shabbat lunch Debated; depends on the timing of the meal Se'udah shelishit Don't repeat . Some kabbalists, and the Ben Ish Ḥai, say to go back Shabbat Ḥanukah: you said רצה but forgot Al HaNissim. Repeat? No. The Friday-night meal obligation is because of Shabbat, not Hanukah , and you mentioned Shabbat. (If you forgot רצה , of course you repeat, saying both.) Before Hashem's name, go back ומיהו אם נזכר באותה ברכה, כל זמן שלא הזכיר את השם, אפילו נזכר בין \"אתה\" ל\"ה'\", חוזר. If you remember before saying Hashem's name in that berachah (even between ברוך אתה and ה' ), go back to נודה לך or מודים and include it. After the name, you don't go back. The Rema: a substitute in הרחמן יש אומרים : if you forgot Al HaNissim in Birkat HaMazon, then at the הרחמן section add: הרחמן הוא יעשה לנו נסים ונפלאות כשם שעשה לאבותינו בימים ההם בזמן הזה. בימי מתתיהו... The poskim write that the same applies to the Amidah : in אלקי נצור , add יהי רצון... שתעשה לנו נסים... בימי מתתיהו... Why there? - Birkat HaMazon formally ends with the fourth berachah: ולעולם מכל טוב אל יחסרנו . The הרחמן requests are an addition. After thanking Hashem for food it is a favourable moment ( עת רצון ) to ask, as Yitzḥak wished to bless Esav once he had eaten and was satisfied. Speaking at that point is not an interruption. - The Amidah formally ends at המברך את עמו ישראל בשלום : we answer אמן to mark the end of a series of berachot. אלקי נצור comes after. May we ask for miracles? The Gemara's principle: one may not ask for a miracle. It is chutzpah to ask the Holy One to change סדרי מעשה בראשית , the order of creation. So how can we say הרחמן הוא יעשה לנו נסים ? Some Aḥaronim: the prohibition is for an individual . Including yourself among Am Yisrael , \"make us miracles,\" is permitted. A more Ḥasidic idea: Hanukah is a time of miracles , and its aura allows one to ask. The Ben Ish Ḥai writes that women would make vows in the merit of Hanukah. The spiral staircase: \"Jewish festivals are not just historical commemorations, they are reliving the events.\" Picture a spiral staircase : each year we go higher, but we return to the same point in the circle. That is why the month of Av is meant to be a difficult time: we are not just remembering, we are reliving it. Likewise we relive Hanukah, a time of nissim and nifla'ot , so it is fitting to ask for miracles. Seif 2: Musaf גם במוסף של שבת ושל ראש חודש צריך להזכיר של חנוכה, אף על פי שאין מוסף בחנוכה. Al HaNissim is included in Musaf of Shabbat and Rosh Ḥodesh, although Hanukah has no Musaf of its own. Seif 3: Don't end with a request אין אומרים \"כשם שעשית לאבותינו כך תעשה לנו נסים\", אלא מסיים \"ועשית עמהם נסים ונפלאות...\" The Beit Yosef notes a variant text that ended Al HaNissim with \"as You did miracles for our fathers, so do miracles for us.\" The Shulḥan Arukh rules not to say it . Why? A suggested reason: we don't ask for things in the last three berachot of the Amidah. The Beit Yosef rejects that: during the Ten Days of Repentance we add requests there, because the restriction applies to personal requests, while communal needs may be asked even in the first and last three. His real reason: it contradicts the berachah we are in. Modim is הודאה , thanksgiving, which is always about the past . \"Sometimes people thank you for something and then ask you for something straight away afterwards, and then you recognise that the thanksgiving was never actually to say thanks. The thanks was just an introduction to be able to ask for the next thing.\" Hoda'ah is על לשעבר , for the past, and so Al HaNissim ends with thanks alone. Siman 683: Hallel כל שמונת ימי חנוכה גומרין את ההלל. All eight days of Hanukah we say the complete Hallel. Why full Hallel every day of a rabbinic festival, when Pesaḥ has full Hallel only at the start? The Gemara: each day of Hanukah was a new miracle . The oil lasted another day, every day. Festival Full Hallel Why Pesaḥ First day(s) only One miracle, the Exodus. (The seventh day brought the splitting of the sea, a new miracle, but we don't say full Hallel \"when the Egyptians were drowning\") Sukkot Every day A different sacrifice was offered each day Hanukah Every day A new dimension of the miracle each day Women and Hallel on Hanukah (The Rabbi has discussed this at length in the Daily Halacha series.) The Rishonim discuss whether women say Hallel on Hanukah. Ruling Sephardim (Shulḥan Arukh) Women should not say a berachah on Hallel. It is good practice for them to say the full Hallel all eight days, without a berachah Ashkenazim Not obligated, but may say it, even with a berachah Sephardi communities with their own custom The Spanish & Portuguese , many in Morocco , and, surprisingly, even in Iraq , women did bless on time-bound mitzvot (arba minim, shofar), against the Shulḥan Arukh. If that is your family's custom, continue it, including Hallel on Hanukah Absent a known custom, Sephardim follow the Shulḥan Arukh: no berachah for women on time-bound positive mitzvot. The Rema: throughout Hanukah we omit Taḥanun , Seliḥot , Tzidduk HaDin , and צדקתך at Shabbat Minḥah. Siman 684: The Torah Reading Seif 1: The Nesi'im קורין בקרבנות הנשיאים שבפרשת נשא, שלשה בכל יום. Each day we read from Parashat Naso : the offerings of the princes at the dedication of the Mishkan. Three are called up. Why the Nesi'im? Back to the four aspects of Hanukah from the very first shiur: 1. The military victory : Hallel and Al HaNissim ( הלל והודאה ). 2. The oil : the menorah, and oily foods (a much later custom). 3. Yehudit , daughter of Yoḥanan Kohen Gadol, who fed a Greek ruler dairy, put him to sleep and killed him, which began the victory: dairy foods (a very late custom). 4. The Mishkan : it was ready on 25 Kislev , but Hashem said, \"Let's leave it till Nisan, the month the world was created,\" and it was inaugurated on Rosh Ḥodesh Nisan. Kislev was disappointed, so Hashem said, \" I'll repay you, and give you Hanukah later on. \" That fits Hanukah's core meaning, re-inauguration : on recapturing the Temple, the Ḥashmonaim had to purify it, clean it out and restart the service. So we read the Nesi'im's inauguration of the very first Mishkan , about 3,000 years ago. The number of aliyot reveals the holiness The Ramḥal (Derech Hashem): Aliyot Day Why 7 Shabbat The holiest 6 Yom Kippur No work, like Shabbat, but a lesser punishment for violation 5 Festivals and Rosh HaShanah Work for food is permitted 4 Rosh Ḥodesh , Ḥol HaMoed 3 Hanukah, Purim \"People make a whole great thing out of Hanukah and Purim, but actually the holiness of Rosh Ḥodesh is seemingly greater.\" Hanukah comes once a year, so we get excited; Rosh Ḥodesh comes every month, \"so you don't have the same enthusiasm for it. But really, Rosh Ḥodesh is a very special time.\" Beginning with Birkat Kohanim ומתחילין בברכת כהנים. The Nesi'im begin at ויהי ביום כלות משה להקים את המשכן , but on the first day Sephardim begin a few verses earlier, with Birkat Kohanim . Why? The victory came about through Kohanim : Mattityahu , Yoḥanan Kohen Gadol . The Kohanim served in the Temple and seemed the least able to fight, yet they led the victory. ויש אומרים שמתחילין \"ויהי ביום כלות משה\", וכן אנו נוהגין (the Rema). Ashkenazim begin with the Nesi'im. How the aliyot are split: - Sephardim: the Kohen and Levi divide the day's prince , and the Yisrael re-reads that day's prince in full. And so every day. - Ashkenazim: the third aliyah reads the next day's prince. - The eighth day: from the eighth prince to the end of the section . Seif 2: Shabbat Ḥanukah Two Sifrei Torah: the weekly parashah, and the day's prince. Haftarah: רני ושמחי (Zechariah), with his vision of a golden menorah . On a second Shabbat Ḥanukah (when the first and eighth days are both Shabbat), the Haftarah is from Melachim : Shlomo and the lamps of the Temple. Seif 3: Three Sifrei Torah When Shabbat, Rosh Ḥodesh and Hanukah coincide: three Sifrei Torah . The only times in the year (besides Simḥat Torah) are Shabbat Rosh Ḥodesh Ṭevet during Hanukah , and Shabbat Rosh Ḥodesh coinciding with Shekalim or HaḤodesh . Siman 677: The Guest and the Traveller Seif 1: Contributing to the host's oil אכסנאי שאין מדליקין עליו בתוך ביתו, צריך ליתן פרוטה לבעל הבית להשתתף עמו בשמן. A guest for whom no one is lighting at home must give the host a perutah to become a partner in the oil. Who is a \"guest\"? The key is eating AND sleeping there. Situation Status What to do You live, sleep and eat with the host for all eight days Part of the household Nothing. Sephardim light one menorah per household, and the host's lighting covers you Just one night , e.g. Shabbat with family Not part of the household (near-universal view) Light in your own home Friday afternoon, and again on Saturday night when you're back Several nights (two, three, four) Unclear Best practice: contribute to the host's oil Sleeping there but eating your own food (renting a room, or yeshivah students in the past who boarded only for meals or beds) Not part of the household Light yourself , or contribute to the host's oil A common case: a young couple stays with the in-laws for Shabbat Ḥanukah , three nights. Sleeping and eating there, but not for all eight days, so they should contribute. Instead of paying, the Mishnah Berurah allows the host to give you a share, but it must be a formal acquisition of a portion of the oil. Only if no one lights for you at home All this applies only if no one is lighting for you at home. - A yeshivah student boarding in a house is covered by his parents at home. - A business traveller staying with a friend, out for business meals all day, is covered by his wife lighting at home . Your own entrance ואם יש לו פתח פתוח לעצמו, צריך להדליק בפתחו : someone with their own entrance (a self-contained studio beside the house) must light there , even if they eat at the host's table, because of חשד (suspicion). The Rema: nowadays, when we light indoors , suspicion doesn't apply, so you light where you eat , and that is the custom. Mark asked: can't they just light in their own room? The Rabbi: of course, if they have a place to light (ideally a window facing the street), they may light there with a berachah , provided no one lights for them at home. The contribution is for someone who wants to be included in the household's lighting. Time differences and \"opting out\" Two questions, which combine: 1. Time differences. Parents in the UK, student in Israel: two hours ahead. At 5 pm in Israel it is 3 pm in London, but when the parents light at 5 pm it is 7 pm in Israel. Fine. But America and Israel: seven hours . By the time the parents light, the student is asleep. Can you really rely on that? 2. Opting out. Can a traveller or yeshivah student say, \" I don't want to be included in my household's lighting,\" so as to light and bless independently? Opting out The Rema Yes , you can The Beit Yosef (in the Mishnah Berurah) No. Your parents support you, pay your flights and expenses, or you live with your spouse. \"You can't say 'I'm not part of that.' Of course you are\" Ḥacham Ovadia's synthesis (ספק ספקא): neither factor alone is enough. But with both together , a significant time difference (not one or two hours; five or seven, \"use your common sense\") and an explicit intention to exclude yourself, you may light your own menorah with the berachot. Even for Australia, the basic premise is that you are part of the household; only the combination allows your own berachah. (You may always light without a berachah.) The whole household travels: the hotel A couple with no children left at home: the household moves with them . Staying with people for all eight days, they are part of that household. For part of Hanukah, they contribute or receive a formal gift of the oil. But a hotel that allows lighting neither in the room nor in the lobby? What can they do? A participant's story: on a flight to Australia , losing a day, they lit in the airline lounge . An air hostess came and told them to put it out; they said they couldn't, and either she left it or blew it out herself. They had been told by a rabbi that a relative in the States or in London could light on their behalf, keeping them in mind . The Rabbi: \"I'm not entirely clear how that works.\" You must be part of the household. It is נר איש וביתו . You can't simply have someone keep you in mind. Unless you genuinely support that household and its food, \"that's a different question.\" So if you truly cannot light: fall back on ברכת הרואה , the berachah on seeing. Walk down a Jewish street and, seeing a menorah, say שעשה נסים (and שהחיינו on the first night). Not להדליק . Seif 2: A child קטן שהגיע לחינוך צריך להדליק. A child at home is covered by the parents. Even Ashkenazim , where each person lights, customarily do so only from bar/bat mitzvah . Seif 3: Somewhere with no Jews יש אומרים : even if your home lights for you, in a place with no Jews you light with berachot. \"This is a very troublesome halacha, because it contradicts what we've learned.\" There are answers; the Rabbi left it. The Rema adds: even among Jews, someone who wants to be stringent may light and bless. As explained above, the Mishnah Berurah brings the Beit Yosef 's disagreement, and we don't do this. Seif 4: Leftover oil after the eighth night הנותר ביום השמיני מן השמן הצריך לשיעור הדלקה, עושה לו מדורה ושורפו בפני עצמו. - A bottle of olive oil you hadn't put into the menorah is just oil. Use it however you like. - Leftover oil in the menorah on nights one to seven can be used the next night . - On the eighth night: oil set aside for the required half hour , left over because the light went out early, is הוקצה למצותו , set aside for the mitzvah. It may not be used for anything else and must be burned on its own . - Oil beyond the half hour (you filled it for two hours and it burned for one) is ordinary. Do what you like with it. Summary Al HaNissim goes in the thanksgiving berachot (Modim; Nodeh Lecha), because Hanukah is הלל והודאה . Forgot it? Don't repeat , but go back if Hashem's name has not yet been said. Substitute in הרחמן or אלקי נצור . Not in Al HaMiḥyah: the Brisker Rav : it is a summary berachah, and Al HaNissim, unlike יעלה ויבא , never generates a berachah of its own. Shabbat Ḥanukah: said רצה but forgot Al HaNissim? Don't repeat : the meal obligation is Shabbat's. Asking for miracles is normally chutzpah. It is permitted as a community , and in a season of miracles we relive each year ( the spiral staircase ). But don't end Al HaNissim with a request : thanksgiving is for the past . Full Hallel all eight days: a new miracle every day . Sephardi women: Hallel without a berachah , unless their community's custom is to bless. Torah reading: the Nesi'im (re-inauguration). Three aliyot , fewer than Rosh Ḥodesh (the Ramḥal's scale). Sephardim open with Birkat Kohanim , for the Kohanim who led the victory. Shabbat: two Sifrei Torah, רני ושמחי ; with Rosh Ḥodesh, three. Guests: living, sleeping and eating there for all eight days means you are covered. One night: light at home. In between: contribute to the oil . All only if no one lights for you at home . Opting out of your household's lighting: only with both a large time difference and explicit intent (Ḥacham Ovadia). A hotel where you cannot light: you cannot be covered by relatives' homes. Use the berachah on seeing . Leftover oil from the eighth night's required half hour must be burned on its own . Conclusion חזק וברוך to everyone. \"This is the end of Hilchot Hanukah. I'm delighted we've been able to get through it all.\" The course resumed in 2025 , with a WhatsApp poll on the topic: Hilchot Pesaḥ (a significant part, perhaps half, \"if we really work hard\") or all of Hilchot Purim , in time for Purim. Ḥanukah Same'aḥ.","BusVeHxZD3g":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed) — Rambam Section: 1 · Chapter 2 (deep dive) Topic: The Creation Narrative Reexamined Text: Sefaria edition (Rabbi Chaim Makbili's modern Hebrew translation from the Arabic) Recap from Last Week Chapter 1 — Completed Toar = physical form; never applied to Hashem Tzelem = essence; what makes something what it is Demut = similar (resemblance, not identity) The verse נעשה אדם בצלמנו כדמותנו means man was created with intellectual capacity ( tzelem ) that resembles ( demut ) Hashem's intellect — though categorically lower Three Meanings of \"Elohim\" # Meaning Example 1 God Most common usage 2 Angels אלהי האלהים — God of angels 3 Judges, prominent people, lords עד האלהים יבוא דבר שניהם — Mishpatim The Question Posed If man was created with full intellect (tzelem Elokim) in Chapter 1, why does the snake in Chapter 3 offer \"knowledge of good and evil\" as if it's something new? And why would the \"punishment\" for the sin be the granting of this intellect — the very thing that defines a human? Tonight's Mission We are now going to dissect Rambam's answer in detail — work through it line by line, develop its implications, and watch as it transforms our entire reading of Bereishit. PART 1 — Premise One: Adam Had FULL Intellect Before the Sin והשכל שהשם השפיע על האדם והוא שלמותו האחרונה הוא שקיבל האדם לפני חטאו The intellect that Hashem bestowed upon man — his ultimate perfection — was received before his sin. What This Establishes The intellect humans received in Chapter 1 (b'tzelem Elokim) is complete — not partial This intellect is the basis on which Hashem could command him \"You cannot give commandments to animals or those without intellect\" — the very existence of the command (don't eat from the tree) proves the human had the rational capacity to receive and understand it ולגבי השכל נאמר לאדם שהוא בצלמו ובדמותו, ובגללו דיבר אליו השם וציווה אותו Because of the intellect, he was created in His tzelem and His demut — and because of it, Hashem spoke to him and commanded him. PART 2 — Premise Two: TWO Domains of Knowledge This is the radical insight of the chapter. There are two completely different domains of \"knowing\": ובשכל מבחינים בין האמת לבין השקר. וזה היה לאדם באופן המושלם. אך המגונה והנאה הוא מפורסמות לא ממושכלות With intellect one distinguishes between truth and falsehood — and this Adam had completely. But ugly/beautiful (good/bad) belongs to mefursamot — social consensus — NOT to intellectual understanding. The Critical Distinction Domain Type of Knowledge Hebrew Term Examples Muskalot Objective intellectual truth אמת ושקר (true/false) \"The sky is blue.\" \"2+2=4.\" \"World War II ended in 1945.\" Mathematical equations. Scientific facts. Mefursamot Subjective social consensus טוב ורע (good/bad) \"Nudity is shameful.\" \"Honor your elders.\" Most norms of behavior. Aesthetic judgments. Why This Distinction Matters משום שאין אומרים השמים כדורים נאה, והארץ שטח מגונה — אלא אמת ושקר We don't say \"the sky being spherical is beautiful \" or \"the earth being flat is ugly \" — we say true or false . Hebrew itself encodes this: For facts → we use אמת ושקר (true/false) For aesthetic/moral matters → we use טוב ורע (good/bad) Intellect Only Handles Truth/Falsehood ובשכל מכיר האדם את האמת מן השקר וכן הוא בכל הדברים המושכלים The intellect distinguishes truth from falsehood — and this is how it works for all intellectual matters. Intellect gives you objectivity. It doesn't (and can't) give you subjective social-good-and-bad. That's a different faculty entirely. PART 3 — The Before/After Picture Before the Sin וכשהיה במצבו המושלם עם תכונותיו הטבעיות המושכלות שבעבורם נאמר עליו ותחסרהו מעט מאלהים When man was complete with his natural intellectual capacities — to the extent that David in Tehillim calls him almost a mini-god ( ותחסרהו מעט מאלהים — \"You have made him a little less than the divine\") because of his resemblance in intellect to Hashem... What Adam Could NOT Do Before the Sin לא היה לו כוח כלל העוסק במפורסמות, ולא היה יכול להשיגם He had no capacity whatsoever to engage with mefursamot — and could not perceive them. The Nudity Example עד שאפילו הגנאי המפורסם ביותר — גילוי הערוה — לא היה גנאי בבחינתו, ולא השיג גנותו Even the most-agreed-upon \"ugly\" thing — uncovered nakedness — was not \"ugly\" in his perception. He had no way to perceive its ugliness. Why? Because \"ugly\" is a social/aesthetic category, not an intellectual one. Nudity is just a fact — true (we are uncovered) or false (we are covered). Whether it's \"ugly\" or \"shameful\" is a mefursamot judgment that Adam's intellect simply did not produce. Adam's mind didn't operate in those categories at all. The Sin Itself — Not Just Eating אך כאשר חטא ונטה אחר תאוותיו ודמיונותיו והנאותיו החושים הגופניים But when he sinned — and turned after his desires, his imaginations, and his bodily sensual pleasures... The Quote from the Verse כי טוב העץ למאכל וכי תאוה הוא לעיניים \"The tree was good for eating and desirable to the eyes\" This is the language of sensual perception — eyes, taste, desire. He veered from the intellect (which sees emet/sheker) toward the senses (which judge tov/ra). What \"Cheit\" Actually Means The word חטא does NOT mean \"sin\" in the archaic English sense. The shoresh means to miss, to veer off the target. The Tanakh uses this root to describe shooting: velo yachati — \"he would shoot and never miss\" Cheit = veering off direction Teshuvah = returning to direction You're driving on the motorway, you veered off course — cheit . You're coming back on track — teshuvah . Two of the most important words of the Yamim Noraim, both mistranslated. The Sin Was the Turning, Not the Eating The actual sin was the veering of attention — toward the sensual, the imaginary, the desirable-to-the-eyes. The eating was merely the manifestation of this internal turning. The Punishment — Measure for Measure נענש בכך שנשלמה ממנו אותה השגה שכלית He was punished by having that intellectual perception diminished from him. The Logic — Mida K'neged Mida The Sin The Consequence Veered after physical/sensual perception Now perceives the world through good/bad (the realm of the senses/society) Abandoned objective truth-seeking Becomes immersed in subjective social judgments Turned the face toward the desirable The face is now fixed in that direction (will develop below) ולכן לא ציית לציווי שבשל שכלו נצטווה בו Therefore he did not obey the commandment which he had received because of his intellect. The Acquisition — A New Faculty ונקנתה לו השגת המפורסמות He acquired the perception of mefursamot — the faculty to engage with social good/bad. ושקע בראיית דברים כגון הם And he became submerged in seeing things this way. The word שקע is striking — he didn't just gain this perception, he sank into it. It became the dominant mode of his engagement with reality. The Realization ואז התוודע לו גודל מה שאיבד והופשט ממנו ולאיזה מצב הגיע Then he realized what he had lost, what had been stripped from him, and to what state he had descended. The realization came after the sin, not before. He didn't know what he was giving up until it was gone. PART 4 — Understanding the Snake's Temptation If eating from the tree caused a loss of intellectual clarity and an immersion in subjectivity — why would Adam want to do it? What was attractive about the snake's offer? The Verse Restated והייתם כאלהים יודעי טוב ורע \"You will be like Elohim, knowing good and evil\" The Three Meanings of Elohim Become Critical Here If Elohim meant God here, the verse would imply: \"You will become like God.\" But that doesn't fit — they were already in God's tzelem with full intellect. But Elohim has three meanings. Onkelos understood it as Meaning #3 — important/prominent people, lords, judges : ותהון כרברבין — \"You will be like great ones\" The Snake's Real Offer Not divinity. Sovereignty. The snake's promise: \"Yes, you'll lose some intellectual clarity. The world will become more subjective and blurrier. BUT — you will become the arbiters. You will be the judges of good and bad. You will gain moral autonomy.\" Why This Was Tempting When everything is intellectual (emet/sheker), you submit to objective truth. The truth simply is . But when everything is moral (tov/ra) and the criteria are mefursamot — social consensus — you get to decide. You become the lord of values. You become elohim in the third sense — like a judge who interprets and arbitrates. Adam wanted moral sovereignty. He traded objective submission for subjective autonomy. The trade was: Loses: clarity, certainty, the perception of objective truth Gains: moral sovereignty, arbiter status, autonomy in determining values A Mapili's Observation on the Word \"Etz\" The word עץ (tree) shares the same root as עצה (counsel, advice). What do judges and lords do? They administer etzah — counsel and judgment. They are sources of advisement . When the woman saw the tree ( עץ ), it represented the etzah (counsel) — the ability to give moral advice, to be morally sovereign. The \"tree\" wasn't just botanical; the language hints that what she desired was the judicial position the eating would confer. This is suggestive — not definitive. The Moreh will later raise the question of whether the entire narrative is metaphorical anyway. PART 5 — \"Their Eyes Were Opened\" — A New Perception The verse: ותפקחנה עיני שניהם וידעו כי ערומים הם \"And the eyes of both of them were opened, and they knew they were naked\" The Crucial Question Why does it say their eyes were \"opened\" (פקחנה) and not that they \"saw\" (יראו)? לא נאמר ותפקחנה עיני שניהם ויראו, משום שמה שראו לפני כן הוא אשר ראו אחרי כן It doesn't say \"their eyes were opened and they saw\" — because what they saw before was what they saw after. The same physical eyes, the same objects. Nothing Was Removed from Their Eyes לא היה כיסוי שעל עיניו והוסר There was no veil on their eyes that was removed. What Did Change? אלא נוצר לו מצב אחר שבו הוא רואה כמגונה מה שלא היה כמגונה קודם לכן A new condition was created in which he now perceives as \"ugly\" what was not \"ugly\" before. The change was not in what they could see, but in how they perceived what they saw. The same nakedness, but now classified through tov/ra rather than just emet/sheker . Pikoach — A Word of Perception, Not Vision ודע שאותה מילה — כלומר פיקח — משמשת רק במשמעות של גילוי הבחנה, לא של התחדשות של החושים Know that this word — pikoach — is always used in the sense of revelation of perception , not of new sensory creation. Hagar in the Desert ויפקח אלהים את עיניה ותרא באר מים \"God opened her eyes, and she saw a well of water\" The water was there the whole time. Hashem didn't miraculously create water. She didn't have the perception to see it because she was exhausted, despairing. The \"opening\" was an opening of her perceptual capacity, not of her physical eyes. Yeshayahu — The Future Redemption אז תפקחנה עיני עורים \"Then the eyes of the blind shall be opened\" Same eyes, same eyesight. But after the redemption, we will perceive the hand of Hashem in a way we don't now. Now we live blindly to His presence — then we will see what was always there. Why a \"Pikeach\" Means a Wise Person The Talmud uses pikeach as the opposite of a shoteh (fool). Why does \"one whose eyes are open\" mean \"wise\"? Because pikoach doesn't mean to see physically — it means to perceive truly. A wise person isn't one with better eyesight; they're one who perceives what others miss. The Linguistic Parallel: Hear vs. Listen English distinguishes: Hear — sound waves entering the ear (passive, mechanical) Listen — actually attending to and understanding the sound (active, perceptual) Hebrew uses shema for both — but the meaning is \"listen, perceive\": Shema Yisrael — not \"Hear, Israel\" but \" Listen , perceive, focus, Israel\" The verse: פקוח אזניים ולא ישמע \"Ears are open, but he does not listen\" The sound waves are entering. But there's no perception. Same with: עיניים להם ולא יראו \"They have eyes but they do not see\" These verses use the language of perception (not mechanical vision) to describe spiritual blindness. PART 6 — \"You Changed His Face\" — The Punishment Made Personal The verse from Iyov speaking of this very event: משנה פניו ותשלחהו \"You change his face, and you send him away\" The Question According to Rambam, nothing physically changed in the human. His eyes are the same. His face would be the same. So what does \"you change his face\" mean? Rambam's Explanation כאשר שינה את מגמת פניו — גורש When he changed the direction of his face — he was banished. Why \"Panim\" Means Both Face and Direction שכן פנים הוא מלשון המונח הגזור מפנה. משום שהאדם מכוון אל פניו לדבר שהוא מכוון אליו את רצונו The word panim (face) derives from panah (to turn). Because a person turns their face toward what they want. Your face shows where your desire is directed. Panah = to turn. Panim = the place that turns. The face is the visible manifestation of where the soul is oriented. The Sin Redefined כאשר שינה את מגמת פניו, וכיוון אל עבר דבר שהיה עליו ציווי קודם לכן שלא יכוון אליו — גורש מגן עדן When he changed the direction of his face — turning his attention toward something he had been commanded not to direct his attention toward — he was banished from Gan Eden. The Sin Was the Direction Itself Notice what this is saying: The sin was not primarily the eating. The eating was the manifestation. The sin was the turning — the change of panim , the new orientation toward the sensual, the imaginary, the desirable. The eating was the natural consequence of having already turned. The Punishment Is the Sin וזהו העונש המוטעם מבחינה עניינית לחטא This is the punishment that fits the sin in its essence — measure for measure. You turned your direction. Therefore your direction is now fixed in this new place. You sank into sensual perception. Therefore you will now live submerged in the world of sensual perception. The punishment for veering after the physical is to be subjected to a life governed by physical/social judgments. PART 7 — The Consequences in Practice The Rambam now traces how the sin manifests in everyday human existence: שמשום שהותר לו לאכול מהדברים הטובים ולהיות בשלוה... Before the sin: he was permitted to eat from the good things and to be in tranquility. וכאשר נהיה חמדן והולך אחרי הנאותיו ודמיונותיו, ואחר מה שנעשה לו לאכול — נמנע ממנו הכל When he became a person of desire, following his pleasures and imaginings, and after the food he had been forbidden — everything was withheld from him. The New Conditions of Human Life והוכרח לאכול את המאכל הגרוע ביותר, שלפני כן לא היה לו כמזון He was forced to eat the most degraded food — which previously was not food for him. (Animal food, basic vegetation — what he hadn't eaten before.) וגם זאת אחרי המאמץ And even this only with great effort. The verses: וקוץ ודרדר תצמיח לך \"It will grow thorns and thistles for you\" בזעת אפך תאכל לחם \"By the sweat of your brow you will eat bread\" וישלחהו ה' אלהים מגן עדן לעבוד את האדמה אשר לוקח משם \"And Hashem God sent him from Gan Eden to till the ground from which he was taken\" The Animal-Likeness והשוותו לבהמות במזונו וברוב מצביו He became equated to animals in his food and most of his conditions. ואכלת את עשב השדה \"And you shall eat the vegetation of the field\" The Verse from Tehillim אדם ביקר בל ילין נמשל כבהמות נדמו \"A human who does not maintain his honor — he is likened to the beasts that perish\" A human who doesn't possess yakar (intrinsic dignity, self-grandeur) — who acts according to the lower impulses — becomes like an animal. And that's exactly what happened to Adam through the sin. The Final Word ישתבח בעל החפץ — תכליתו וחכמתו אינם ניתנים להשגה Blessed be He who wills — His purpose and wisdom are not fully attainable. The Rambam is signaling: we've gone as far as the intellect can carry us in this question. The deeper why of the whole scheme is beyond us. PART 8 — Three Important Questions on the Chapter Question 1 (Jonathan): Where Is \"Subjectivity\" in the Text? \"Where in the Rambam's text do we find the idea of subjective morality?\" Answer In the line: המגונה והנאה הוא מפורסמות לא ממושכלות \"Good/bad (the ugly and the beautiful) belongs to mefursamot — not to muskalot .\" Mefursamot literally means \"things accepted by social consensus\" (from פרסום — publicity/widespread acceptance) Muskalot means \"things known through intellectual reasoning\" This is precisely Aristotle's distinction — the Rambam is importing Aristotelian ethics: general moral judgments are opinions held by general consensus , not deductions of pure reason. This does NOT mean Torah ethics are subjective. It means the category of \"good and bad\" — as humans naturally apply it — operates by consensus rather than logical proof. Question 2 (Mark): Are Basic Mitzvot Like \"Do Not Murder\" Also Subjective? Answer This is unclear in the Rambam, but here is the resolution: The Rambam (in Shmoneh Perakim) and Saadia Gaon distinguish: Mitzvot Sichliyot (rational mitzvot) — including murder, adultery, theft — these are so clear they fall under muskalot , not mefursamot Mitzvot Shimiyot (received mitzvot) — those that require revelation to know So the foundational moral truths — don't murder, don't commit adultery, don't steal — are objectively true even within Rambam's framework. They fall under muskalot . What Rambam is discussing in the Bereishit narrative is the broader category of moral judgment — including aesthetic and social norms — that came into being with the sin. Question 3 (Mark): Why Bring \"Judges\" Into It at All? \"Why do we need 'judges' for good and bad? Can't intellect also adjudicate?\" Answer You don't need judges for mathematics. No one needs a judge to decide if 2+2=4 No judicial review of a chemistry equation Math exams are graded objectively — true or false But you need judges for law, ethics, politics, narrative. Legal essays, political opinions, ethical dilemmas — these go to judicial review That's the entire judicial system — interpreting and applying values This is the domain of Elohim (Meaning 3) — important arbiters The snake's offer is precisely this: you will become like judges. You will be the ones who interpret and decide values. Sovereignty over the moral order. Question 4 (Jonathan): Wasn't Adam Enlarged by Gaining Moral Choice? \"Before the sin, Adam had clarity but maybe less choice and responsibility. After the sin, he has moral sensibility, the ability to choose. Isn't that an enlargement?\" Two Responses 1. The Rambam's Position The Rambam does not seem to view this as enlargement — he views it as yerida , a descent. The whole framing is measure for measure punishment . Adam realized what he lost, and what he gained was the consequence of his sin, not a positive achievement. 2. Rav Dessler's Different View (alluded to in the shiur) Rav Dessler (Michtav Me'Eliyahu) argues something close to what Jonathan suggested — that Adam sinned in order to gain greater moral responsibility, and this could be understood as a kind of Kiddush Hashem . Rabbi Kada noted he doesn't fully understand this position and doesn't think it accords with Rambam. 3. The Ramchal's Important Point The single command (don't eat from the tree) is reflective of all mitzvot — it's not really one isolated choice. Ramchal explains the Tree of Knowledge represents Yichud Hashem (the unity of Hashem) — and all mitzvot and aveirot are about Yichud Hashem. So Adam did have meaningful choice before the sin; the single command represented the totality of moral choice. This argues against the \"more responsibility now\" reading. PART 9 — Was There Bad Before the Sin? (Free Will Question) A serious objection to the chapter: \"If 'good and bad' only came into being through the sin, how could Adam have free will before the sin? Free will requires choosing between good and bad. So either bad existed before the sin (contradicting the chapter), or Adam didn't really have free will (but then how could Hashem command him?).\" The Nefesh HaChayim's Resolution Rav Chaim Volozhin in Nefesh HaChayim addresses this directly: הנמצא, כי קודם החטא לא היה כולל רק מכוחות הקדושה לבד, לא מכוחות הרע. אבל אחר החטא נכללו ונתערבו בו גם כוחות הטומאה והרע Before the sin, man was composed only of forces of holiness — no forces of evil within him. After the sin, the forces of evil were incorporated and mixed into him. The Critical Move Free will existed before the sin — but the \"bad\" was external , not internal. אמנם, לא שהיה ענין בחירתו מחמת שכוחות הרע היו כלולים בתוכו, כי הוא היה אדם ישר לגמרי His free will did NOT come from evil being mixed within him — he was completely upright. וכוחות הרע היו עומדים לצד ובענין בפני עצמו חוץ ממנו The forces of evil stood off to the side, as a separate matter outside of him. The Snake Was External והיה בעל בחירה להכנס לכוחות הרע חס ושלום He had free will to enter into the forces of evil, just as a human has the choice to jump into a fire. כמו שהאדם הוא בעל בחירה להכנס לתוך האש Like a human has free will to jump into fire — the fire is external, but you have free will to expose yourself to it. לכן כשירצה השטן להחטיאו, הוצרך הנחש מבחוץ לבוא ולפתותו Therefore when the satanic force wanted to cause him to sin, the snake had to come from outside and entice him. Compare to Today לא כמו שעתה שהיצר מפתה את האדם בתוך האדם עצמו, ונדמה לאדם שהוא עצמו הוא המושך את עצמו Not like today, when the yetzer ha'ra tempts a person from within the person themselves — and it appears to the person that he himself is pulling himself toward the temptation. The Sharp Distinction Before the Sin After the Sin Free will between holiness (internal) and evil (external) Free will between competing internal voices Bad was like a fire — you knew not to jump in, but you could Bad whispers from inside your own consciousness Snake had to come from outside Yetzer Hara is inside Adam knew bad was destructive We confuse bad for good because it sounds like our own voice You wouldn't choose suicide because you know it's destructive We constantly choose against our own welfare because the temptation sounds like us Before the Sin: Bad Like Suicide A human today knows that suicide is destructive. Even if available, you don't do it because you know it's wrong. Adam viewed all evil this way before the sin. It was a real option, but a self-evidently destructive one. The free will was real, but the choice was clear. The Snake's Achievement The snake's job was not to create evil but to make evil look attractive . The tree was good to look at, desirable to the eyes — the snake made the destructive thing look beautiful. Adam, exercising free will, chose to ingest the external evil — and from that point, evil became internal. The cake outside you — everyone tells you it's poisonous, you know it's poisonous, but it looks delicious. You ingest it. Now the poison is inside you. That's the sin of Adam — he ingested the evil, and now it's part of every human being. Summary of Chapter 2 The Main Argument Adam had full intellect from creation — b'tzelem Elokim means with the rational faculty of objective truth-seeking. There are two domains of knowledge: Muskalot — intellect — true/false — objective Mefursamot — social consensus — good/bad — subjective Before the sin, Adam operated only in muskalot. Nakedness was not \"ugly\" because \"ugly\" is a mefursamot category that didn't exist for him. The sin was the turning of attention toward the sensual, imaginative, desirable. The eating was the manifestation; the sin was the directional change. The punishment is measure for measure — he turned his face toward the senses, so his face is now fixed in that direction. He is now submerged in the world of mefursamot . The snake's offer was sovereignty, not divinity — \"You will be like Elohim\" = \"You will be like judges/lords\" — arbiters of moral truth. Moral autonomy in exchange for intellectual clarity. \"Their eyes were opened\" is a perception change, not a vision change. Same eyes, same objects — new categorical framework. \"You changed his face\" — panim derives from panah (to turn). The face is the direction of the soul's desire. Adam changed his orientation; the punishment fixed him in the new orientation. Result: Humans are now subjected to the world of subjective evaluations. They must work for food, struggle for clarity, live under norms — animal-like in much of their existence. Free will existed before the sin (per Nefesh HaChayim) — but evil was external, not internal. The snake came from outside. After the sin, evil is internal, and we mistake our yetzer for our own voice. What This Chapter Changes About Bereishit Conventional Reading Rambam's Reading Adam was unintelligent until he ate Adam had full intellect before The sin gave him new knowledge The sin diminished his intellectual clarity \"Their eyes were opened\" = new vision Same eyes; new perceptual category Becoming \"like God\" = becoming divine Becoming \"like Elohim\" = becoming lord-judges Punishment = expulsion + hardship Punishment = the sin itself (mida k'neged mida) The eating was the sin The turning of attention was the sin Good and evil are objectively created categories Good and evil (mefursamot) only emerge after the sin Coming Up Next Week Chapter 3 — Continuing to explain anthropomorphic terms in Tanakh Chapter 4 — Same project Possibly Chapter 5 — Depending on time The Moreh now returns to its main vocabulary-explanation project, having completed this remarkable digression into the heart of Bereishit. B'Ezrat Hashem","DzuVTq67ebA":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 4 of 14 Topic: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 2 — She'hiyah, Hazara and Hatmana (hotplates, blechs, urns, crockpots) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 13 November 2018 Recap of Bishul Bishul — transforming food with the power of fire , as in the Mishkan. Cooking directly in the sun is permitted; cooking in something heated by the sun is rabbinically forbidden (an onlooker assumes it came off the fire). A kli rishon — even off the fire — still cooks while above 45°C. In a kli sheni there is strictly no Bishul, apart from a few tender foods (eggs, perhaps tea). No problem of bishul after afiyah or vice versa — croutons may go into soup (for Ashkenazim too if a ladle was used). Reheating: solids may be reheated ( ein bishul achar bishul be'davar yavesh ) — their main purpose, being edible, remains. Liquids, once cooled below yad soledet bo (45°C) , may not be reheated. Part A — Completing Bishul A solid dish with some liquid Salmon cooked before Shabbat in a sauce, taken from the fridge: solid or liquid? Chacham Ovadia Yosef : follow how the dish is called . We call it \"salmon\" (with some sauce for taste), not \"sauce with salmon\" — so it is a solid and may be reheated. But a very watery cholent or a stew that is more liquid than solid counts as a liquid and may not be reheated. Asking a non-Jew to reheat a liquid Normally what is forbidden to us may not be asked of a non-Jew. But many great authorities — including the Rambam — hold there is no Bishul in reheating an already-cooked liquid. Although we don't follow them, on the strength of their view one may directly ask a non-Jew (e.g. an au pair) to reheat soup that was fully cooked (boiled) before Shabbat and has since cooled — no need even to hint. Eating in a home where Bishul isn't kept For many melachot there is a penalty ( knas ) : one may not benefit on Shabbat from something done in a forbidden way — even if done in error, and even by a guest who did nothing — so as not to encourage, even implicitly, the breaking of Shabbat. Food cooked (or finished cooking) on Shabbat may not be eaten on Shabbat ; after Shabbat it may be eaten (if done unintentionally). If you know or strongly suspect your host does not keep the laws of Bishul — that is a serious problem; if already invited, consult a rabbinic authority. This applies to Bishul , which changes the food's essence. Laws like Borer (selecting) don't change the food itself — so eating where the host may not know the details of Borer is fine. Part B — She'hiyah: Leaving Food on the Fire into Shabbat The principle — and the rabbinic exception Anything set going before Shabbat may continue into Shabbat: a garden sprinkler switched on at 3:45 may water all Shabbat; timers may switch lights on and off. By the same logic, a raw piece of meat put on at 3:30 to cook through Shabbat should be fine from the Torah — and it is. But Chazal knew human nature — especially our love of Shabbat food. You come home from shul at 5, guests arrive at 5:30, and the meat needs two more hours… You may be tempted to turn up the flame — two Torah prohibitions at once: Bishul — speeding up cooking ( megis ). Mav'ir — kindling: raising a flame is like lighting one. In Talmudic times this meant stoking the coals. So Chazal forbade leaving food on the fire into Shabbat unless it has reached a certain stage. How cooked must it be? A major debate in the Gemara and poskim: One view: fully cooked . The other: ma'achal ben Derusai — edible in a pinch, roughly half-cooked . (Ben Derusai was a highwayman always on the move, who ate his food half-cooked.) Once half-cooked, there is no fear you will tamper to finish it. The halacha follows the second view: once food is half-cooked before Shabbat, it may be left on even an uncovered flame to finish cooking overnight or until morning. Winter warning: in summer, food put on at 7 is ready long before a 9 pm Shabbat. In midwinter, with Shabbat at 3:35–4:00, food put on at 3 for a 5:30 meal may not be half-cooked when Shabbat begins — then it may not be left as is. The blech Chazal permitted less-than-half-cooked food if the coals were covered — a clear sign that you've taken your mind off the fire. That is the origin of the blech : a sheet of metal covering the flame as a reminder not to touch the fire. If food is already half-cooked (and you won't return food during Shabbat), you don't need a blech. Covering the dials: today one raises the heat with the knobs, not the coals, so some cover the dials too (some blechs bend over them). Strictly, not necessary — the sign belongs on the place of the fire. Hotplates: modern hotplates in England have no dial — a fixed temperature you cannot change. So food — even less than half-cooked — may be left to cook on a hotplate from before Shabbat. (Old hotplates with dials would be a problem.) Gas or electric makes no difference. The urn The same rule applies to liquids . Fill the urn early enough — at least half an hour before Shabbat (more for a big urn) — so the water reaches at least 45°C (\"half-cooked\") by Shabbat. Better still, fully boiled : if you take water out while it is still heating (say at 75°C), the smaller remaining volume will heat faster — you've indirectly sped up the boiling. If only at 45°C at Shabbat, don't draw from it until it has fully boiled. Stuck with no blech or hotplate Make a makeshift blech from several folded layers of aluminium foil (one sheet is not substantial enough). Question from the floor: no foil — can you put an empty pot on the flame and the food pot inside or on top of it? — Yes , excellent: it serves as the sign, as long as there is a gap. If nothing is available and Shabbat is minutes away — seek rabbinic guidance. Some argue the decree doesn't apply to a steady modern flame : Chazal feared stoking coals that die down; a gas flame doesn't fade. We don't follow this, but it is a significant opinion for someone stranded. Part C — Hazara: Returning Food to the Fire on Shabbat From the Torah, putting a fully cooked schnitzel into a pot on a lit flame on Shabbat is no problem — solids aren't re-cooked and no flame is being adjusted. Chazal forbade it for two reasons: Mechzei ke'mevashel — it looks just like weekday cooking (fridge → pot → fire). You may be tempted to turn up the flame to reheat it faster. This applies even to food that was on the fire when Shabbat began but was taken off and has cooled. Returning food to a blech — two conditions The flame is covered (blech, foil) — a sign against tampering, and not the normal way of cooking. Continuity — the food was taken off intending to return it and not put down somewhere that breaks the connection (e.g. the fridge or the floor). Off the blech, onto the table, serve, back on — fine. Into the fridge and back — a new act, forbidden even with a blech. Hotplates — a different category Neither concern applies to a dial-less hotplate: you can't raise the temperature, and it doesn't look like cooking — it's even called a \"plata shel Shabbat\" . So with a hotplate there is no Hazara problem at all : A solid (or a mostly-solid dish like salmon in sauce) may be taken straight from the fridge and put on the hotplate — directly on the plate too. Challah may be warmed on the hotplate (but not placed fresh on a blech). Liquids: the issue isn't Hazara but Bishul — a cooled liquid may not be reheated. Soup taken off to serve and returned while still above 45°C is fine. Reheating cold soup yourself — the timer method Ask a non-Jew to put the cold (pre-cooked) soup on the hotplate — or even a blech (not a naked flame). Or: if the hotplate is on a timer and currently off , you may place the pre-cooked, cooled soup on it; when the timer switches on, it heats by itself (Chacham Ovadia — explicit). E.g. put it on at 8:30 before shul for an 11 o'clock lunch. Only for food already cooked — never raw food. Q: If the hotplate went off at 11 and comes back on at 12, may you put the soup on while it's still warm? — Only if the soup is below 45°C ; above that, it would be cooking. Part D — Hatmana: Insulating Food Wrapping to retain heat Without a hotplate, people used to boil the food just before Shabbat and wrap the pot in towels — big pots stay hot for four or five hours (a common practice in Israel). Chazal forbade fully wrapping food on Shabbat itself , even with something that only retains heat ( davar she'eino mosif hevel ) — because one naturally heats food up before wrapping it, and so may come to cook or kindle. Wrapping before Shabbat with something that only retains heat — permitted . Only full wrapping is forbidden. Leaving one side open, or a tea towel just on top , is fine. Wrapping in something that adds heat ( davar ha'mosif hevel ) — e.g. burying a pot in coals — is forbidden even before Shabbat . Crockpots A crockpot's element surrounds the pot — seemingly hatmana in something that adds heat, forbidden even from before Shabbat; some do not allow crockpots for this reason. But the halacha is that we may use crockpots . Among the reasons: a pot's own lid is not considered wrapping (otherwise one could never put a lid on), and the crockpot has only that single lid — but one should not put anything further on top of it. To satisfy all views, place two balls of aluminium foil between the pot and the heating element, creating a gap. Thermos flasks and hot-water bottles Both are permitted — including filling them from the urn on Shabbat. Hatmana applies only to a kli rishon ; once water is poured into a flask or bottle it is a kli sheni, with far less concern that one will heat it first. Likewise, hot water poured into a pan may be wrapped. Summary A mostly- solid dish (salmon in sauce) may be reheated; a watery stew may not. A non-Jew may be asked to reheat cooled soup. Food cooked on Shabbat may not be eaten on Shabbat — beware hosts who don't keep Bishul (not an issue for Borer). She'hiyah: food left on from before Shabbat must be at least half-cooked — or on a blech or dial-less hotplate . Fill the urn early; ideally fully boiled. Hazara: returning food to a blech needs a covered flame and continuity (not via the fridge). On a hotplate any cooked solid may go on, even from the fridge; cooled liquids only via a non-Jew or a hotplate on a timer that is off. Hatmana: don't fully wrap a kli rishon on Shabbat; before Shabbat is fine with heat-retaining wraps. Crockpots permitted (ideally with foil balls); thermos flasks and hot-water bottles permitted. Coming Up Shiur 5 — 20 November: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 3 — Threshing and Selecting Dash — peeling and squeezing fruit and vegetables: orange juice, lemon on salad or in tea Ice cubes, freezing water, using soap; milking Borer — selecting: bones in fish and chicken, setting the table on Friday night for Shabbat lunch B'Ezrat Hashem","E_eUSze13TU":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapters: End of Perek 4 (רחמנות — Compassion) & Perek 5 (נדיבות — Generosity) Edition: Feldheim Recap: Chapters 1–4 So Far Chapter 1: Two paths of Avodat Hashem — the simple path of the righteous (fulfilling everything required) and the path of the Chasid (going beyond what's necessary). The sefer focuses on the path of the Chasid. Chapter 2: All middot are interconnected — working on one naturally affects others. Chapter 3: Purity of motive — four levels: Level 1 (highest): Serving Hashem because He commanded — no reward, no fear Level 2: Serving from fear of punishment / desire for reward (עובד מיראה) Level 3: Dual motivation — for Hashem AND for social recognition Level 4 (lowest): Only for societal pressure Chapter 4 (first part): Rachamanut = one of the most important middot in Torah First of the 13 Attributes of Mercy: אל רחום וחנון Many mitzvot given to develop compassion within us Achzariyut (cruelty) at its root = indifference (from זר — stranger) Tzedakah requires empathy, not just money (Rambam) Sometimes mercy is wrong — the Torah commands firmness in specific cases (murderer, meisit, ben sorer u'moreh) Examples from Moshe, Dovid, Shmuel — mercy and firmness in the same person Chapter 4 (Conclusion): When in Doubt — Be Merciful The Core Principle סכנת הרחמים על מי שאינו ראוי להם פחותה מסכנת האכזריות אל מי שראוי לרחמים The danger of being merciful to someone who doesn't deserve it is less than the danger of being cruel to someone who deserves mercy Therefore: when in doubt, choose mercy over cruelty This parallels the Rambam in the Eight Chapters (Ch. 4): when you can't stay in the golden mean, better to veer toward the merciful extreme than the cruel one The Chachamim's Statement כל מי שאין בו דעת אין מרחמין עליו A person who is a fool / doesn't appreciate / can't recognize / would be harmed by receiving mercy → wrong to be merciful Example: Sinwar ימ\"ש — treated for cancer in an Israeli hospital, then orchestrated October 7th Not commenting on the diplomatic decisions behind it But: this illustrates misplaced mercy — rachamanut used in the wrong way Investigate Before Being Cruel ודרשת וחקרת ושאלת היטב Even with the greatest sin (avoda zara) — Torah says: investigate, interrogate, ask thoroughly Ed zomem (false witness to be executed) — must search and investigate carefully The point: yes, the Torah commands cruelty sometimes — but you must be 100% certain before acting harshly In doubt → don't do it Charity When in Doubt — Give אמר פרנסוני אין בודקין אותו If someone asks for food → give immediately, don't question This is halacha in the Shulchan Aruch and the Gemara Caveat: This applies specifically to food — basic necessities Food = essential, basic mercy Source: על אשר לא קדמו אתכם בלחם ובמים — Ammon and Moav condemned for not offering bread and water For monetary charity generally → verification is appropriate (e.g., Va'ad HaTzedakah certificates in England) But for food → no questions, give straight away Don't Be Quick to Punish וכן צריך שלא למהר ולהעניש כמו שנסתבר ממידותיו יתעלה ארך אפים Be patient — don't rush to punish Hashem Himself is described as ארך אפים — slow to anger Don't Delay Charity — The Story of Nachum Ish Gamzu Nachum Ish Gamzu — famous Tanna, righteous, miracle worker Yet: blind, body covered in boils and illness His students asked: why did this happen to you? The story (Gemara Taanit): Traveling on his donkey A poor man came and said: פרנסני — feed me Nachum said: fine, but follow me a bit further down the road, I'll unload and give you plenty The poor man was too tired and hungry — couldn't wait — collapsed and died Nachum knew he had sinned by not hastening to feed him immediately The sin was so great in his eyes → he asked for suffering in this world (yissurim) to preserve his portion in Olam Haba The illnesses came upon him as a result Lesson: When you have a mitzvah of charity or compassion → do it immediately, don't delay God's Mercy Is 500 Times Greater Than His Judgment מרובה מידת רחמים ממידת הדין אחת לחמש מאות The calculation: Mercy: נוצר חסד לאלפים — \"guards kindness for thousands\" Onkelos translates: אלפי דרין — thousands of generations מיעוט רבים שנים — minimum of \"thousands\" (plural) = 2,000 generations Judgment: פוקד עון אבות על בנים על שלשים ועל רבעים — exacts justice for 4 generations 2,000 ÷ 4 = 500 God's mercy exceeds His judgment by a ratio of 500:1 The Rambam in Moreh Nevuchim — Same Principle Sometimes a person needs to show anger, portray judgment, be particular Examples: destroying nations so they don't corrupt you — necessary harshness But: ראוי שיהיו פעולות הרחמים... מרובות הרבה מפעולות העונש A person's acts of mercy should be far more than acts of punishment Because the 13 Attributes of Mercy are almost all mercy — only one involves judgment Practical Application — Relationships Whether parent-child, employer-employee, spousal relationship: Sometimes you need to criticize, offer negative feedback, reprimand, punish But if at the end of the week/month you see more instances of punishment than praise → something is wrong Breakdown in relationship will follow The ratio must favor mercy over cruelty Chapter 5: נדיבות — Generosity The Meaning of Nedivut From the root נדב — as in כל נדיב לבו (Terumah, Vayakhel, Pekudei) At least seven Hebrew words for giving: nedavah, mattanah, minchah, shai, doron, berachah, and more Nedavah is the highest level — always associated with the heart (נדיב לב) Generosity from complete volition — no compulsion, purely from the heart This is why Hashem told Moshe about the Mishkan: only people who are nediv libo Result: so much was given that Moshe had to tell people to stop — perhaps the only time in history Definition הנדיבות הוא ביטוי להענקת רוב טובה למי שאינו חייב לו המעניק Generosity = giving to someone when there is no obligation to do so Of your own volition, you decide to give כי לתת לפועל שכרו ולפרוע לבעל חובו את חובו אינו נדיבות אלא יושר Paying your employee, repaying a loan = not generosity, just justice (yosher) Hospitality, gifts, charity = true nedivut Generosity as a Divine Attribute כי הוא יתעלה הגדיר כתכונותיו את מה שרואים ממעשיו הטובים Hashem describes Himself as חנון ורב חסד — generous and abundant in kindness Important philosophical point: When we speak of God's 13 Attributes, we don't mean God literally \"has\" 13 separate attributes God is peshitut — simplicity, indivisible These are descriptions of how we perceive God's actions from our perspective Rabbi Avraham follows his father's (the Rambam's) philosophy on this exactly ובריאת עולמו היא עצמה מעשה חסד ונדבה The creation of the universe itself = an act of generosity As Dovid HaMelech says: אמרתי עולם חסד יבנה — the world was built with kindness God created the world le-heitiv — to bestow goodness Four Key Definitions: Generosity, Miserliness, Extravagance, Thriftiness Rabbi Avraham distinguishes two separate symmetries: Symmetry 1 — Vis-à-vis others: Term Hebrew Direction Meaning Generosity נדיבות Toward others Giving to others from your own volition Miserliness קמצנות Toward others Refusing to give to others Symmetry 2 — Vis-à-vis yourself: Term Hebrew Direction Meaning Extravagance בזבוז Toward yourself Spending lavishly on yourself — best house, clothes, holidays Thriftiness חסכון Toward yourself Not spending on luxuries for yourself — saving Key insight: You can be a mevazbez (extravagant on yourself) and still be a kamtzan (miser toward others). Having the nicest house and car doesn't make you generous — generosity is measured by what you give to others . אדרבה אם מצמצם בהוצאותיו ומטיב עם זולתו נחשבת נדיבות The greatest generosity: thrifty on yourself, generous with others A difficulty with this framework: The Gemara (Ketubot) says: המבזבז אל יבזבז יותר מחומש — \"one who is mevazbez should not give more than one-fifth\" This uses the word בזבוז in the context of giving tzedakah to others According to Rabbi Avraham's definitions, bisbuz = spending on yourself — not on others Rabbi Kada noted: this is a genuine difficulty. The Gemara seems to use mevazbez for generous giving to others, not just self-spending He doesn't have a resolution but noted the question Generosity Must Be Directed Correctly והנדיבות הנזכרת... פירושה שאדם נדיב ומטיב במה שיש לו לאנשים הראויים לעת ולמקום Generosity doesn't mean giving everything to everyone at any time Give to the right people, at the right time, in the right measure Example from God Himself: God gave intellect to humans — not to animals. Not everything is appropriate for everyone. God gave us incredible variety of food — fruits, vegetables, colors, flavors. Animals don't have the same variety. God gave the Torah to Am Yisrael — not to every nation God gave prophecy only to those worthy of it The principle: גומל חסדים טובים — there is chesed tov (good kindness) and chesed ra (bad kindness) Water = most important thing in life. Give too much → you'll kill someone. Kindness = same. Give too much, to the wrong person, at the wrong time → you'll cause harm. We want chasadim tovim — good generosity directed properly. Don't Hoard ושלא יטמין האדם מה שחננו אלוהים כדי להניח לאחרים Don't hoard money just to leave it to the next generation Source: יצבור ולא ידע מי אוספם — \"He gathers and gathers and doesn't know who will collect it\" (Tehillim) How many cases of parents who saved their whole lives → children squabble over the will → lawyers get the money Shlomo HaMelech: sometimes Hashem punishes someone by giving them money, money, money — and in the end it goes to someone else The Order of Giving Start with yourself and your family — ושמחת בכל הטוב... אתה וביתך Then include the poor, the stranger — ואתה והלוי והגר אשר בקרבך Don't just throw lavish parties for wealthy friends — host those who actually need it Hospitality vs. entertainment: Hospitality = caring for those in need. Entertainment = enjoying company of those who don't need you. Four Kinds of Generosity והנדיבות המעוללת אינה רק בממון... אלא גם השפעתו חכמתו ואמונתו Generosity is not just about money. Rabbi Avraham identifies four categories: 1. Generosity with Money (נדיבות בממון) Share your assets with those who need it Don't just spend on luxuries for yourself and your household Spend on necessities, family first, then extend outward The Torah gave many mitzvot to train this: Pe'ah (corner of the field) Peret (fallen grapes) Olelot (unformed grape clusters) Shichechah (forgotten produce) Ma'aser ani (tithe for the poor) Tzedakah (charity generally) Halva'ah (lending) Matnot Kehunah (gifts to Kohanim) Ma'aser Levi Ha'anakah to an Eved Ivri (bonus upon release) Shmitah (land rest every 7 years) Shemitat kesafim (loan release every 7 years) Yovel (jubilee every 50 years) All designed to develop generosity within us Ha'anakah — The Eved Ivri Bonus: He served you for six years. You paid for him when you bought him. That's it, finished. Yet the Torah says: give him extra when he leaves — a \"package\" People say \"that's their job, why thank them?\" — Torah teaches the opposite This is the earliest version of an employment separation package 2. Generosity with Influence (נדיבות בכוח השפעה) If Hashem blessed you with connections, relationships, contacts → share them with those who need them Source: the Navi says: היש לדבר לך אל המלך או אל שר הצבא — \"Can you speak to the king for me?\" Common problem in the charity world: \"I know him, I can't share his number, that's my contact\" Rabbi Sacks' point: Power is a zero-sum game; influence is the opposite If you see your connections as power → you won't share (sharing diminishes your power) If you see them as influence → sharing increases your ability to influence The more you share, the more you influence, the better it is 3. Generosity with Intellect (נדיבות בחכמה) Teach wisdom to those who seek it and are worthy Source: ושננתם לבניך — teach your children. Chazal: אלו תלמידיך — \"your children\" = your students Source: ראה למדתי אתכם — \"See, I have taught you.\" Gemara: מה אני למדתי בחנם אף אתם למדו בחנם — just as I taught for free, you teach for free Applies to Torah, but certainly to all fields The beauty of a community: each person specialized in something (lawyer, doctor, rabbi, engineer) → a vibrant community shares these talents with one another 4. Generosity with Faith (נדיבות באמונה) One of the earliest references to the concept of kiruv (bringing people closer) Bring those who are far from faith closer ישיב לב פושעים בתוכחות נבונות — return the hearts of sinners through wise reproof Source: הוכח תוכיח את עמיתך — reprove your fellow — but in the correct way, responsibly, beneficially The Miser Is Compared to a Heretic מי שנפשו שרויה במידת הקמצנות כינתה התורה בשם כופר The Torah calls a miser by the term \"kofer\" (heretic) Rabbi Avraham's proof — juxtaposition of the same phrase \"bnei beliya'al\": Ir HaNidachat (city that committed avoda zara): יצאו אנשים בני בליעל — heretics Refusing to lend before Shmita: השמר לך פן יהיה דבר עם לבבך בליעל — same term The Torah uses \"beliya'al\" (without a yoke / heretic) for both idol worship AND refusing to lend Rabbi Avraham deduces: they are comparable — avoda zara and miserliness Rabbi Kada's note: this appears to be Rabbi Avraham's own idea — no earlier source identified Avraham Avinu — The Model of All Four Kinds of Generosity ועליך להתבונן בתולדות אבינו האציל אברהם אשר יסד מנהג הנדיבות הידוע גם היום Avraham established the practice of generosity still known today All laws of hachnasat orchim (hospitality) trace back to him The acronym אש\"ל (Eshel): ויטע אשל בבאר שבע — He planted an eshel in Be'er Sheva א = אכילה — eating ש = שתייה — drinking ל = לוויה — accompanying On Levaya (accompanying guests): Halacha in Shulchan Aruch: walk a guest out at least arba amot (approx. 2 meters) Eating and drinking nourish physically; levaya nourishes emotionally The Maharal (Prague) on Egla Arufa: the elders declare \"we didn't spill the blood\" = we made sure to accompany the traveler. If he wasn't accompanied, he lacked the emotional strength to face his journey. That's why they'd be responsible. Avraham's Generosity with Intellect (חכמה) Taught philosophy and monotheism to masses of people ואברהם זקן בא בימים — the Chazal read: זקן = זה קנה חכמה — he acquired and taught wisdom ואת הנפש אשר עשו בחרן — \"the souls they made in Charan\" — Onkelos: the souls they brought to Torah כי ידעתיו למען אשר יצוה — \"I chose him because he will teach others\" Avraham's Generosity with Faith (דת) Beyond philosophical teaching — he preached simple faith in God Wherever he went, he built a mizbeach and called out in the name of Hashem After feeding guests, they'd say \"let me thank you\" → he'd say \"no, thank the One who gave us the food\" Rambam in Mishneh Torah (Avodah Zarah): Avraham traveled city to city teaching → gathered thousands upon thousands (אלפים ורבבות) Avraham's Generosity with Influence (השפעה) וירק את חניכיו ילידי ביתו — when Lot was captured, Avraham mobilized his entire army He had power and influence → he used it for others, didn't say \"I don't want to endanger my people for my nephew\" Avraham's Generosity with Money (ממון) The famous hospitality scene with the angels: Said: \"Take a bit of water, a bit of bread\" (said little) Actually prepared: the finest flour, three se'ah (large amount), butter, milk, and the choicest meat צדיקים אומרים מעט ועושים הרבה — the righteous say little and do much Then: והוא עומד עליהם תחת העץ ויאכלו — he stood over them while they ate Not just food → personal presence, spending time The highest form of hospitality Lot Followed Avraham's Path Lot learned generosity from his uncle Avraham When the angels came to Sodom → he rushed to invite them, prepared a feast Even offered his daughters to protect his guests (going to excess) Note on \"vision\": Rabbi Avraham refers to Lot's story as a חזיון (vision) Following his father the Rambam's philosophy: all encounters with angels in Torah = prophecy/vision, not literal HaRamban (Nachmanides) famously disagreed One of Ramban's questions: how did Lot merit prophecy? But Rabbi Avraham clearly follows his father on this point Key Takeaways When in doubt — be merciful. The danger of misplaced mercy < the danger of misplaced cruelty. Don't delay charity. The story of Nachum Ish Gamzu — immediate action saves lives. God's mercy exceeds judgment 500:1 — our ratio should favor mercy too. Generosity ≠ giving everything to everyone. Direct it to the right people, at the right time, in the right measure. Four kinds of generosity: money, influence, intellect, and faith. All are important. Extravagance on yourself + miserliness toward others = still a kamtzan. True generosity = thrifty on yourself, generous with others. The miser is compared to a kofer — the Torah uses the same term (beliya'al) for both. Avraham Avinu modeled all four kinds — hospitality, teaching philosophy, preaching faith, using influence for others. Preview: Next Shiur Completing Chapter 5 (nedivut) Chapter 6: כעס ונוחות — Anger and Calmness","GZPCZGBGF6E":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Mishneh Torah (Rambam), Sefer Zemanim — Hilchot Shofar, Sukkah V'Lulav (Sefaria text) Perek: 6 — who is obligated in sukkah, and how the mitzvah is performed Topic: Women, children and the age of chinuch; Rashi vs Tosafot on who carries the obligation of chinuch; the ill and the mitzta'er ; the mourner and the chatan; shluchei mitzvah and travellers (including Chol HaMoed outings); תשבו כעין תדורו : living in the sukkah, not just eating in it; achilat arai and what counts as a meal; the first-night kezayit; learning, davening and rain Schedule note: Erev Sukkot 5787, shortened shiur ( 7:15–8:00pm ). The third in this year's series on the chagim from the Rambam (previous years were from the Shulchan Aruch): Hilchot Shofar Perek 2, then Hilchot Shevitat Asor Perek 1, and now Sukkah. The shiur ended early because the Zoom account's 40-minute limit cut in. All shiurim resume after Sukkot. The Structure of the Section The Rambam's Hilchot Shofar, Sukkah V'Lulav has eight chapters : Perakim Subject 1–3 Shofar / Rosh Hashanah (we learned Perek 2 before Rosh Hashanah) 4 Building the sukkah 5 Sechach 6 Who is obligated, and how to dwell in the sukkah (tonight) 7–8 The arba'ah minim End of 8 (~Halachah 12 onward) Simchah , the mitzvah of rejoicing on Sukkot Perek 6 is long; we got through as much as possible. Halachah 1: Who Is Obligated נשים ועבדים וקטנים פטורין מן הסוכה. Women, servants and children are exempt. For women the reason is simple: it is a מצות עשה שהזמן גרמא , a time-bound positive mitzvah (seven days a year). טומטום ואנדרוגינוס חייבין מספק. Anyone whose sex is in doubt, either because the genitalia are covered ( tumtum ) or because both are present ( androginos ), is obligated out of doubt , since he may be male. וכן מי שחציו עבד וחציו בן חורין — חייב. The half of him that is free is obligated. The age of chinuch קטן שאינו צריך לאמו, שהוא כבן חמש כבן שש, חייב בסוכה מדברי סופרים, כדי לחנכו במצות. A child who no longer needs his mother, around five or six, is obligated rabbinically , to train him in mitzvot. Where does \"five or six\" come from? The Mishnah/Gemara does not give an age. It says קטן שאינו צריך לאמו , and the Gemara defines that in two ways: he can go to the toilet by himself , or he does not wake at night calling \"Imma, Imma, Imma!\" endlessly. A three- or four-year-old still asks a parent to come and help. The Rambam presumably reckoned that the Gemara's two examples come out at around five or six (a Gemara in Eruvin also mentions ages of this kind). So there is no fixed age of chinuch. It depends on the child: how mature, how needy. A very bright, mature child might be ready at four; another at seven or eight. Who carries the obligation of chinuch? View The rabbinic obligation falls on… Rashi (the standard explanation) The father. The child is a child and is exempt; the father has a rabbinic obligation to educate him. Tosafot The child himself. The Chachamim placed a rabbinic obligation on the child from about five or six. The parents should encourage, but it is incumbent on him. Tosafot's proof: the Gemara in Berachot. A child who ate a kebeitzah of bread (the biblical measure) can say birkat hamazon on behalf of an adult who ate only a kezayit . According to Rashi the child has no obligation at all, so how can he discharge an adult? According to Tosafot it works: an adult who ate only a kezayit is obligated in birkat hamazon only rabbinically , and the child also has a rabbinic obligation, so a derabbanan discharges a derabbanan . Rashi needs to explain that Gemara. What does the Rambam hold? The Rabbi is not sure; it can be read both ways. He writes חייב בסוכה , \" he is obligated\", about the child himself, not the father, which is a small proof for Tosafot. But he opened the halachah with קטנים פטורין ; perhaps that meant biblically exempt. There is room to discuss it. Tosafot's view is the greater chiddush of the two. Why this matters: it is a fundamental question running through all of hilchot chinuch. Whenever we speak of a father's obligation of chinuch (and, a separate question, a mother's), is the peshat that the parent has a rabbinic obligation to educate, or that the Chachamim placed a rabbinic obligation on the child so he gets used to the mitzvah? The two readings have numerous ramifications . Either way, young children should be encouraged to sit in the sukkah. Halachah 2: The Ill and the Mitzta'er חולים ומשמשיהן פטורין מן הסוכה. ולא חולה שיש בו סכנה, אלא אפילו חש בראשו, אפילו חש בעיניו. The ill and those looking after them are exempt, and not only someone dangerously ill: even a headache, even if his eyes are bothering him. The underlying principle: תשבו כעין תדורו The Gemara says it clearly. The pasuk says בסוכות תשבו שבעת ימים , and תשבו כעין תדורו : dwell as you live . When you feel slightly unwell at home, you make yourself as comfortable as possible: you lie down, take an extra cushion, take some tablets. Sitting in the sukkah is fine for a healthy person, but when you are unwell your own bed is far more comfortable. So a choleh is exempt, because staying in the sukkah would not be dwelling as you live . This is the most fundamental principle in the whole mitzvah of sitting in the sukkah , and it keeps coming back. The Rabbi's own example: he could feel a small cold coming on, his throat tickling. He would not rely on that for the first night , but for the rest of Sukkot a genuine cold is enough to exempt a person from sleeping, or even eating , in the sukkah. The helpers: obviously (the Rambam doesn't say it, the poskim do) they are exempt only while they are helping . While someone is bringing the ill person food he may eat where he needs to; as soon as he finishes, he goes back to the sukkah. The mitzta'er מצטער פטור מן הסוכה — הוא ולא משמשיו. ואיזהו מצטער? זה שאינו יכול לישון בסוכה מפני הרוח, או מפני הזבובים והפרעושים וכיוצא בהם, או מפני הריח. One who is distressed by sitting in the sukkah is exempt: he, but not those serving him. What is a mitzta'er ? Someone who cannot sleep in the sukkah because of the wind , flies, fleas and the like, or a bad smell . Point 1: same principle. תשבו כעין תדורו. If one room at home had a draught you couldn't fix, you'd move to another room. So too here: if it is very windy or very cold, if there are lots of flies, if there's a bad smell, you may leave. (Rain is a separate matter with its own rule; see the last halachah.) Point 2: note the examples. They are all about sleeping . \"He cannot sleep in the sukkah because of the wind…\" The Rambam gives no example of eating . From this the poskim deduce that a greater degree of distress is needed to leave the sukkah for eating than for sleeping. How much greater is a matter of common sense. It makes sense: sleep is delicate and lasts a long time, so a lower level of discomfort is enough. For eating, put your coat on; you're sitting up, not lying down. Point 3: the distress must be external and not of your own making. Wind is not in your control; flies, largely not; a smell, not. But if you put up your sukkah in a place with a perpetual external problem, you cannot claim mitzta'er . Some say such a sukkah is pasul altogether . The Rabbi's case: a sukkah in an infested garden. He once had a situation with mice and rats in the garden. You might say: I can't sleep there at night, but I can eat there, because while people are walking around and eating they don't come in. There is a strong צד to say that you do not even fulfil the mitzvah of eating there , because the sukkah stands somewhere intrinsically unfit to live in for seven days (you would never sleep there). So it lacks the name of a sukkah, even for eating. So the distinction is: a smell that arrives , for example someone spills something foul next to your sukkah on the second day, makes you a mitzta'er and you may go in. But if from the outset there is a perpetual factor stopping you living there, even if it only affects sleeping, you may not fulfil the mitzvah even when you eat there. Build your sukkah somewhere it is actually possible to live. Some things are beyond our control (Hashem sends a strong wind, someone starts building next door), and those are exactly what mitzta'er is for. Halachah 3: The Mourner and the Chatan האבל חייב בסוכה. A mourner is obligated. This is an avel , after the burial of a father, mother, son, daughter, brother, sister or spouse, as opposed to an onen (before burial), who like all mitzvot is exempt. Why state it? The Gemara asks: isn't an avel in pain? Why is he not a mitzta'er ? The answer carries a psychological insight: mitzta'er is defined by external causes : cold, smell, infestation, the rats that suddenly started coming on the third night. Distress that is internal, within the person, is not mitzta'er. So the avel is obligated. חתן וכל השושבינין וכל בני החופה פטורין מן הסוכה כל שבעת ימי המשתה. The chatan , all his shoshvinin and all the bnei hachuppah are exempt all seven days of the celebration . The chatan is engaged in a mitzvah, rejoicing with his new wife, and cannot spend quality time with her in a cramped, uncomfortable sukkah. The case: someone who married between Yom Kippur and Sukkot , so the sheva brachot run into Sukkot. It would be permitted מעיקר הדין to hold a sheva brachot lunch on the first day of Sukkot outside the sukkah , since the chatan and those rejoicing with him are exempt. (The Rabbi was unsure about the first and second nights , and thought probably not.) His friends may join, but only while they are making him happy ; after that they are obligated again, while the chatan remains exempt. (The Rabbi doesn't know anyone who married in that window; getting guests there between Yom Kippur and Sukkot would be hard work. But in principle.) A digression: the Rambam's view of the chuppah This fits the Rambam's position in Hilchot Ishut/Kiddushin : one is fully married only on bringing the wife into one's home. Why then does the chuppah make the marriage? Because in those days the chuppah was the quasi-home . They would build a canopy, and the couple would spend the next seven days there, with the party there too. It was like bringing her home for the week, and it usually wasn't big enough to hold everyone. That is why the chatan and his company are exempt : sheva brachot could not be held in a sukkah, there wasn't room. So there is strong room to say that if you couldn't fit into a sukkah, you could hold the first-day sheva brachot lunch in a regular room or hall. Halachah 4: Shluchei Mitzvah, Travellers and Guards שלוחי מצוה פטורין מן הסוכה, בין ביום ובין בלילה. Someone on his way to perform a mitzvah (taking money to redeem a captive, delivering tzedakah, visiting his rav, travelling far to learn Torah) is exempt by day and by night . Now compare everyone else. They are exempt only for their specific period : Person Exempt Obligated הולכי דרכים ביום : travelling by day (e.g. to inspect one's property elsewhere) By day By night: find a sukkah and sleep in it הולכי דרכים בלילה : travelling by night By night By day שומרי העיר ביום : city guard on a day shift By day By night שומרי העיר בלילה : city guard on a night shift By night By day שומרי גנות ופרדסים : guards of gardens and orchards Day and night — Why are orchard guards exempt entirely? The Gemara: שמא יעשה השומר סוכה, ידע גנב שיש שומר במקום קבוע, ויבוא ויגנוב ממקום אחר. If the guard builds a sukkah, the thief will know where he is stationed and come round the other side. Don't give away your position. Why is the shaliach mitzvah exempt even at night? The Gemara tells of Amoraim who, on their way to a mitzvah, did not bother to sleep in a sukkah. Tosafot: the exemption at night applies only where finding a sukkah would detract from the mitzvah : it would take longer, you wouldn't rest properly, you'd arrive tired. But if you reach a city with public sukkot available, why should you be exempt? The Rambam (and Rashi seems to lean this way): simply exempt. If you are on your way to a mitzvah, the entire journey, even while resting, is עוסק במצוה. Though you're asleep now, the whole journey is one great mitzvah. So even if there is a sukkah nearby, you need not trouble yourself to find it. The ordinary traveller is exempt only while travelling . Chol HaMoed outings A common question: you leave at 9:00am on a Chol HaMoed outing and come back at 8:00pm. Can you have lunch out? Are you a holech derachim by day? Rav Ovadia Yosef and Rav Moshe Feinstein both held no : holchei derachim means travelling towards a purpose , not leisure . By contrast: if you take a train from London to Manchester because you need to, for example a property has just been vacated and you must go and inspect it, you are a holech derachim during that time and may eat on the train , sandwiches or even a full meal. Halachah 5: How the Mitzvah Is Performed כיצד היא מצות ישיבת סוכה? שיאכל וישתה וידור בסוכה כל שבעת הימים, בין ביום ובין בלילה, כדרך שהוא דר בביתו בשאר ימות השנה. Eat, drink and dwell , all seven days, day and night, the way he lives in his house the rest of the year. What does dar mean? To live. And living means everything : your phone calls, your emails, relaxing, reading, playing games. The forecast for London: 25° tomorrow, 23° on Shabbat. There is absolutely no excuse to be at home reading the newspaper. Read it in the sukkah. At night it may be cold, maybe. But during the day, if you're home, be in the sukkah. This is your home , not just the place you have your meals. How the sukkah became a dining room The Rabbi has given a whole shiur on this elsewhere. The idea that the sukkah is chiefly a place to eat and drink comes from Tosafot , from Ashkenaz : they lived in northern Europe, where it was too cold . So the mitzvah was in effect reframed : the primary dwelling became eating and drinking. The shitah has many ramifications, and one of them is a minhag the Rabbi calls a shame: we say the berachah of leisheiv basukkah only when we eat. The Rambam would throw this out completely , in the Rabbi's reading. Why not make a berachah every time you enter the sukkah to dwell ? You've come home from Shacharit, you don't want to eat, you want to sit and read, study, bring the laptop in and work. You are doing a mitzvah. Tosafot said no berachah because the main mitzvah is eating and drinking, and they said that because of the climate . And it has affected us psychologically : we think of the sukkah as where we eat. That is wrong. Anyone who has had Sukkot in Israel knows proper Sukkot: seven days in the sukkah, sleeping, eating, drinking, doing everything there. Here it is harder, but when the weather is good, try to do everything in the sukkah. The source: the seven days of the miluim The Gemara derives day and night from a gezeirah shavah, שבעת ימים / שבעת ימים , from the seven days of inauguration , when the kohanim had to remain at the Ohel Moed day and night (end of Parashat Tzav, stated there explicitly). So too the sukkah: day and night. וכל שבעת הימים עושה אדם את ביתו עראי וסוכתו קבע, שנאמר: שבעת ימים. All seven days the house becomes temporary and the sukkah permanent , your main abode. Honouring the sukkah כיצד? כלים הנאים... מעלה אותן לסוכה. Bring your nice things into the sukkah: nice placemats, silverware. Don't use plastic. Allowance for real difficulty: if you live on the fourth floor of a block of flats and would have to carry everything down four flights each time, that is mitzta'er , and plastic is fine. No excuse the other way: many people now have the luxury of a sukkah built into the house . Unless it's very cold, bring the nicest utensils. וכלי שתיה, כגון אשישות וכוסות, בסוכה. Drinking vessels, your nice cups, in the sukkah where feasible. אבל כלי אכילה, כגון קדרות וקערות, חוץ לסוכה. But pots stay outside: don't bring in the big pot of chicken soup. It isn't respectful. What are קערות? Tosafot: another kind of pot , large cooking vessels. The Rambam's simple reading: plates. Then the halachah is: once you have finished, don't leave the dirty plates in the sukkah. Within reason: you needn't run in the moment you swallow. But you can't finish, bentch, go off to relax or visit someone and clear up hours later. It is not respectful to the sukkah. והמנורה בסוכה. ואם היתה סוכה קטנה, מניחה חוץ לסוכה. The lamp or candles belong inside, unless the sukkah is small and there is a real fire hazard; then leave them outside. Halachah 6: Eating and Sleeping Outside the Sukkah אוכלים ושותים וישנים בסוכה כל שבעה, בין ביום ובין בלילה. ואסור לאכול סעודה חוץ לסוכה כל שבעה, אלא אם כן אכל אכילת עראי: כביצה או פחות, או יתר מעט. One may not eat a meal outside the sukkah all seven days, except temporary eating : a kebeitzah, or less, or a little more. A point about Shabbat: some poskim held that on Shabbat , because of the mitzvah of eating on Shabbat, even a small amount becomes a fixed meal. The Rambam does not seem to hold that , because he says כל שבעה , which includes Shabbat and Yom Tov: arai is arai even then. (It ties in with other issues too.) Note the wording: \"a kebeitzah, or less, or a little more.\" Rather loose. Hold that thought. ואין ישנים חוץ לסוכה אפילו שינת עראי. No sleeping outside, not even a nap. ומותר לשתות מים ולאכול פירות חוץ לסוכה. Water and fruit, as much as you like. Halachah 7: The First Night, and What the Rest of the Week Requires אכילה בליל יום טוב הראשון בסוכה חובה. אפילו אכל כזית פת, יצא ידי חובתו. מכאן ואילך רשות: רצה לאכול סעודה, יאכל בסוכה; רצה אינו אוכל כל שבעה אלא פירות או קליות חוץ לסוכה, אוכל. The first night (and in chutz la'aretz, the second night too) is obligatory ; after that it is voluntary. If you want a meal, eat it in the sukkah; if you want to live all seven days on fruit and roasted grains outside, you may. What achilat arai really means Notice: in both halachot the Rambam's examples of what may be eaten outside are water, fruit, nuts/grains . In his commentary to the Mishnah , the Rambam defines achilat arai : eating when you are not sitting down to a meal . You're working, something is nagging at you, it isn't mealtime yet, so you go to the cupboard and have an apple (or, if you're unhealthy, a piece of cake): להשקיט רעבונו , to quieten your hunger a little. (In those days the proper practice was two meals: breakfast, and a late-afternoon/early-evening meal. Between them you might grow hungry; that snack is arai.) So arai means something eaten temporarily until the main meal. Nowadays halachah measures quantities. But: The Rabbi's view, לעניות דעתי: if you sit down to meat, chicken, rice, potatoes, beans and say \"this is achilat arai, I haven't eaten a kebeitzah of bread, 60 grams,\" is that what the Rambam meant? He doesn't think so. He knows the poskim will say it's permitted. But on the basis of the Rambam's commentary to the Mishnah and his text here, it seems clear that a full meal needs a sukkah, even with no bread. Snacking through the day (fruit in the morning, nuts in the afternoon, fruit again in the evening) is not a meal. A full meal is not arai. And even with arai : the Rambam's limit is a kebeitzah, or a little more , not three pieces of cake. Today we say ~56 grams (he suspects the real figure is considerably less). 60 grams of cake belongs in the sukkah. And the whole business of \"I'll just have mezonot\" becomes ridiculous: you end up never sitting in the sukkah for anything , with knock-on effects on the berachah too. Accept the spirit here, and the Rabbi thinks the halachah as well. ומי שיחמיר על עצמו ולא ישתה חוץ לסוכה אפילו מים, הרי זה משובח. One who is strict and won't drink even water outside the sukkah is praiseworthy . Note that the Rambam hardly ever records \"praiseworthy\" practices . Later poskim do it constantly, but the Rambam does so only where the Mishnah itself does. Here it does (the Rabbi recalled Rabbi Tarfon or Rabbi Tzadok having even his water brought up to the sukkah). This is a good chumra, 100%, indisputable. The first-night kezayit Authority Minimum on the first night Rambam , Shulchan Aruch A kezayit of bread is sufficient Rabbeinu Nissim (Ran) A kebeitzah (two kezeitim) Mishnah Berurah You should eat a kebeitzah ; many recommend it The source: a gezeirah shavah, חמשה עשר / חמשה עשר (\"the fifteenth\", used both of Pesach and of Sukkot). Just as on the first night of Pesach matzah is obligatory and the rest of the week voluntary, so on the first night of Sukkot a kezayit in the sukkah is obligatory and the rest is voluntary. Practically, to satisfy all opinions: eat a kebeitzah , within about five or six minutes (a kezayit for sure), and eat it plain : plain challah, no salads, hummus, tahini or spreads until you have finished the kezayit (or the kebeitzah). The reasoning: the matzah must be plain, so this should be too. That is debatable; but if you want to cover every view, eat it plain first and then add whatever you like. (Halachah 8 was skipped in the shiur.) Halachah 9: Learning and Davening in the Sukkah כל שבעת הימים קורא בתוך הסוכה. וכשמבין ומדקדק במה שיקרא, יקרא חוץ לסוכה, כדי שתהא דעתו מיושבת עליו. All seven days, one reads (learns) in the sukkah . But when one is studying in depth , analysing and concentrating, one may study outside the sukkah, so that one's mind is settled. Small things can distract serious learning. The Magen Avraham adds another reason: it can be a real bother to carry all your books . When researching something you may have ten or twenty books open at once, and you don't know where you'll end up or which books you'll need. Running back and forth to the shelf each time is a burden, depending on how far the sukkah is. So you may learn at home. מתפלל — Davening may be inside or outside the sukkah. Halachah 10: Rain ירדו גשמים — הרי זה נכנס לתוך הבית. מאימתי מותר לפנות? משירדו לתוך הסוכה טפות, שאם יפלו לתוך התבשיל יפסל. When it rains you may go in, but only once enough rain falls that it would spoil a dish , even a simple one. A single drop does not count , nor a few splatters: people feel one drop and say \"let's go in\" . It must be enough to ruin the food. And if it stops: if you went in because of rain and it then stops, you may finish the meal indoors ; you need not go back out mid-meal. The same applies to sleeping. Summary Women, servants and young children are exempt from sukkah (time-bound mitzvah), the tumtum and androginos obligated out of doubt, and a child who no longer needs his mother, roughly five or six but really depending on the child , is obligated rabbinically for chinuch , with Rashi placing that obligation on the father and Tosafot on the child himself (proved from the child who discharges an adult's rabbinic birkat hamazon). The ill, down to a headache, and their helpers while helping are exempt, as is the mitzta'er , all flowing from תשבו כעין תדורו ; the Rambam's sleeping examples imply a higher bar for leaving to eat , and the distress must be external and not self-inflicted , so a sukkah built somewhere perpetually unliveable may not work even for eating. The avel is obligated because inner grief is not mitzta'er; the chatan and his company are exempt through the seven days, which the Rambam's view of the chuppah as the couple's quasi-home explains. The shaliach mitzvah is exempt day and night (for the Rambam, the whole journey is a mitzvah; Tosafot limits it), the ordinary traveller only while travelling, and leisure outings don't count . The mitzvah itself is to eat, drink and live in the sukkah as in one's home: reading, working, relaxing. The dining-room sukkah is an Ashkenazi adaptation to a cold climate that the Rambam would reject. Bring nice things in, keep pots and dirty plates out; arai means a snack between meals, not a breadless full meal ; the first night requires a kezayit (ideally a kebeitzah, eaten plain); in-depth learning may be done indoors; and rain means going in only when it would spoil the food . Coming Up Over Sukkot: learn the end of Perek 8 , from about Halachah 11–12 to the end : three short halachot on simchah , how really to appreciate the happiness of these eight days, the happiest time of the year . All shiurim resume after Sukkot : Monday HaMaspik (recap of Ch 1–9, then Ch 10, perishut), Tuesday Moreh (Ch 18–20), Thursday back to Hilchot Shabbat , siman 320 onward. B'Ezrat Hashem. Chag Sameach, תזכו לשנים רבות וטובות.","IE5HNq288P4":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 1 (Creation), Chapter 3: the first of the rival theories (eternal matter, creation from God Himself, the combination of the two, and dualism) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 6 May 2024, on Zoom (the first shiur after Pesach) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: The Three Premises Since the start of the year the class had learned Rav Saadia's Introduction and begun Essay 1, on creation . Before Pesach Rav Saadia had established three premises: Chapter Premise 1 The world is finite: there was a beginning , against those who say it is eternal 2 That beginning was caused by something external , not by the universe itself 2 The external Creator made it from nothing \"That's his theory, and his belief, which he believes is the Torah's belief, and the opinion of the vast majority of the Rishonim.\" (Who that Creator is, God, \"we haven't talked about yet. That's going to be Essay 2.\") Chapter 3: twelve rival theories Now that I have established these three roots by reasoning, and they are verified by the words of our prophets, I will bring twelve other opinions. I will explain each, bring their proofs, refute them, and where I can, bring verses against them as well. As agreed before Pesach, the class would not go through the whole long chapter in detail. The Rabbi would give background to each theory, read parts inside, and aim for five or six tonight. The labels for the schools (Upanishads, Sankhya, Mani) are the Rabbi's identifications. Theory 1: Plato: Creation from Eternal Matter The Creator made all things from eternal, very fine matter , and from it He fashioned this complex universe. Why they held it: \"How can a creator create from nothingness? Surely a creator has to create from something.\" To them creation from nothing seemed absurd, even for God. So they posited very fine matter, as eternal as Hashem , from which He made the world. Rav Saadia describes in detail how they thought God then shaped it, \"quite complex; I'm not going to go into all of it.\" Four refutations # Refutation The point 1 You have never seen eternal matter either Your whole reason was that we have never seen something made from nothing. But we have never seen this eternal matter either. Positing it is as unreasonable as what you were running from 2 Matter without form? Hot and cold, moist and dry, appearance, taste, boundaries, more and less, place and time : all these belong to physical, finite matter and came after creation. So the eternal matter had none of them . How can matter exist with no form at all? \"A direct replica of Aristotle's argument against Plato : you can't have matter without form\" 3 From formless to formed? How could something with no length, width or depth become something with them, and the four elements of fire, air, water and earth ? \"If you say God can do that, take what has no form and give it form, then He can also create something from nothing . So why are you running away?\" 4 Why the stages? On Plato's account God had to go through a whole complex series of creations to produce the universe. If He can create, why does He need the process? And if He can only work in stages, He is limited Rav Saadia also brings two verses that people used as proofs for Plato's view, and rejects both readings. \"Stay away from this falsehood.\" Theory 2: Creation from God Himself The Rabbi identified this with the Upanishads , the Hindu teachings from around 600 BCE , \"a good 2,500 years ago.\" (A participant on Zoom asked him to spell it: U-P-A-N-I-S-H-A-D-S.) The Creator made the world from Himself. (An aside on language: \"I normally try to avoid using pronouns for HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Not for modern-day reasons, but because Hashem has no pronouns. He isn't a he or a she. But for simplicity's sake, we'll use He.\") Their reasoning: They accept a Creator . They reject creation from nothing . They reject Plato : there was no eternal matter. So there was nothing but God , and He must have made the world out of Himself . \"Have mercy on such people,\" says Rav Saadia. \"They are more foolish than the previous ones.\" After restating the general arguments of Chapters 1 and 2, he gives five new ones . The class tries first The Rabbi asked for logical objections before reading on. A participant suggested that God would then be incomplete , having taken part of Himself out. \"You could argue that.\" Flora raised tzimtzum : \"Hashem contracted Himself to make room for the world.\" \"Excellent. I was going to come on to that at the end.\" Rav Saadia's five arguments 1. The limitless cannot make limits out of itself. It is absurd that something with no form, no characteristics, no location, no time, no properties could turn itself into something with all of them. \"Only someone very stupid can think something like this.\" But can't God do anything? \" God can't do literally anything. \" Can God create a stone so heavy He cannot lift it? If He can, He cannot lift it; if He cannot, He cannot create it. Either way, a deficiency? \"This is purely an issue of semantics . We associate the word can't with something negative. That's not true. Not every can't is a deficiency. I'm so healthy, I can't catch the flu. That's not a deficiency in me; that's something positive.\" Because Hashem is so complete and can lift anything, He cannot create something He cannot lift , and that is no more a deficiency \"than saying God can't catch the flu.\" So too: in Rav Saadia's purely rationalist mind, the limitless essence of the Creator cannot make from itself a world of limitations. 2. Why would anyone limit himself? If you had all power and everything you wanted, why would you turn part of yourself into physical things that hunger and thirst and suffer every deficiency? 3. Why put part of Himself in pain? Before creation everything is perfect. Why send part of Himself into suffering? Either it was a punishment for something that part did, or it was for no reason, \"which also doesn't make sense.\" 4. Who chose which part? If God is one, who decided which part would go? Was one part better than another? Did it go out of fear, or hope? \"That's all ridiculous.\" 5. Why not rescue it? Once the Creator saw that part of Him was suffering in the physical world, surely He would want to redeem it . \"Essentially, Saadia's argument is that it's irrational to assume that the Creator can make from Himself, who has no limitations and no form, something with form and limitations.\" Tzimtzum and Rav Saadia The Rabbi returned to Flora's point. Historical context first. Rav Saadia wrote in the tenth century, \"long before Kabbalah has any public appearance.\" He showed no knowledge of it. The Zohar came to public light only in the 1300s (attributed to Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai a thousand years earlier), and the doctrine of tzimtzum comes only from the Arizal , about 500 years ago. The doctrine. Before creation there was only God, permeating all existence. (The Hachamim warned against dwelling on \"before\" creation, since it is so hard for finite minds within time to think about the infinite.) So the Arizal asked: how was there room for a universe? His answer: tzimtzum , from letzamtzem , to make smaller, to withdraw. Hashem withdrew , and the whole universe, planets and galaxies, exists within that withdrawal. Theory 2 (refuted by Rav Saadia) The Arizal's tzimtzum Claim God made the world out of Himself God withdrew to leave a \"safe space\" The world is made from God's own essence Nothing : the Arizal believes in creation from nothing Why Nothing else to make it from If the world were within God, everything would be perfect ; there could be no failures or evil \" Chas veshalom to say that God could take from Himself and make the universe from Himself. No one would accept that in Jewish thinking. That, as Saadia argued, is philosophically ridiculous.\" Would Rav Saadia accept tzimtzum ? \"I highly doubt it.\" Some of his arguments would refute it too. He would say instead that just as God created something from nothing, He was able to create a place for the universe to exist, not by withdrawing Himself . \"All I'm pointing out is that the Arizal's theory is similar, although different .\" God in pain? One of Rav Saadia's arguments was: why would the Creator put part of Himself under suffering? The Rabbi recalled Chazal's reading of עמו אנכי בצרה (Tehillim 91:15), \"I am with him in distress,\" from which they learn that the Shechinah goes into exile with us, as it were. \"Saadia rationally makes very strong arguments. I'm just saying there are elements in other Jewish thinkers which could answer some of these points\": the idea that, for humans to go through their lives, Hashem puts Himself, as it were, under some pain too. Theory 3: The Combination \"Very short and very simple.\" The Rabbi identified it with the Indian Sankhya school: The Creator made the world both from eternal matter (like Plato) and from Himself. Rav Saadia does not rehash anything: all the arguments against the first two theories apply to this one. Rejected. Theory 4: Dualism: Two Creators \"I'm going to spend some time on this, to the end of the shiur, because the Hachamim of the Talmud were very much aware of this , and they sought in many different ways to prevent us from believing it.\" Background This is Gnostic dualism , associated with the Iranian teacher Mani , from whom Manichaeism takes its name. It was extremely prevalent in the centuries before Rav Saadia and spread from Iran to other lands. There are two creators: a creator of good and a creator of evil. Why was it attractive? Because of the hardest question in theology : \"If God is perfect, how can there be bad in this world? This is probably the most difficult philosophical issue for theologians, whoever they may be: Jewish, Christian, Muslim. How can a perfect and good God create suffering and evil? \" There are solutions, and Rav Saadia will have his own, \"but the much easier way out of all of this is to say: very simple. There are two creators.\" Elements of this appear in some strands of early Christianity: a god of light and a god of darkness , with the god of darkness giving the covenant to Israel and the god of light the \"new covenant.\" \"By the way, this is a very black-and-white approach to life. You can't accept that things are grey , that things have a mixture of good and bad in them. So you say there's a good creator and all good comes from him, and a bad creator and all bad comes from him.\" How Chazal fought it Where What Why Mishnah, Berachot A chazan who says \" modim, modim \" twice is silenced and removed He seems to be thanking two powers The morning blessing Shacharit's main section opens יוצר אור ובורא חשך We have just passed from darkness to light. \"So the night was the time of the god of darkness and now it's the god of light?\" No: the same God creates both The evening blessing Arvit opens אשר בדברו מעריב ערבים , He rolls away light before darkness As the light fades, we might think the god of darkness is returning to power. No: the same God The Talmud also records many stories of people who challenged the Hachamim with this belief. Flora pointed to the verse behind the blessing, יוצר אור ובורא חשך עשה שלום ובורא רע (Yeshayahu 45:7): He forms light and creates darkness, makes peace and creates evil . The Rabbi agreed that the wording of the blessing softens the verse, since it would be too difficult for people in the morning prayer, \"because of the polemic.\" Another participant offered a thought: God has to create bad as well as good, because if everything were good, nobody would know it is good. \"You mean as a contrast. Lovely, thank you. There is an idea like that: you can only appreciate light from the darkness.\" Rav Saadia's text They are more foolish than all who came before them. They could not accept that one agent can make two opposite kinds of things . They saw that everything in the world is a mixture of good and bad , beneficial and harmful, and concluded the good comes from the good god and the bad from the bad. Part of the theory, as Rav Saadia reports it: the good god is unbounded in five directions (above, east, west, south and north) but bounded below , where the evil god's realm begins; the evil god is unbounded below and in the four directions but bounded above , where the good god is. The two mixed together and made the universe . Why? Some said the good god wanted to heal the evil one, a kind of peace treaty; others that the evil god felt left out, wanted some of the good, and hijacked it. \"As you can see, it sounds ridiculous. But this was a very prevalent and common belief.\" The refutations Rav Saadia gives many arguments (the Rabbi counted about fifteen) and did not read them all. The main points: One person, two natures. A single human being can be angry one moment and appeased and forgiving the next. We don't say he is two different personalities. One thing can contain different aspects. \"Not that he's suggesting God is bad. What he's saying is: just because you find good and bad in the world doesn't mean there must be two creators. \" Why would good associate with evil? If you are good, you do not want to mix with evil. If it had no choice, it is not all-powerful. And if it was to make the bad good, it failed: the world is still a mixture. Is darkness a creation at all? Then something very interesting, \"and here amongst the Hachamim we have different opinions\": Rav Saadia argues that darkness is not a creation in its own right. Darkness as a beri'ah (a creation) Darkness as a he'der (an absence) Meaning of \"Hashem created night\" Hashem created a positive thing called darkness When light withdraws, we call that absence darkness Consequence Leaves room to imagine a god of light and a god of darkness Dualism has no ground : there is nothing for a second god to make Verses like יוצר אור ובורא חשך Literal Hashem creates the circumstances in which darkness occurs, through the withdrawal of light Rav Saadia goes into detail to prove the second view. But \"it's not so clear that everyone agrees,\" and there are practical halachic differences . Very briefly: Rabbeinu Tam's opinion that nightfall is 72 minutes after sunset , much later than the view we follow, which allows certain things much later in the evening. Some have argued that this rests on his understanding that night is a creation in itself , coming about in stages. \"I don't want to go into the details. In short, I'm just pointing out that the theory that darkness is a withdrawal is not something everyone agrees to.\" Summary The three premises of Chapters 1 and 2: a beginning; an external cause; creation from nothing. Chapter 3 now tests twelve rival theories . Plato (eternal matter): we have never seen such matter either; matter cannot exist without form ; if God can give form to the formless, He can create from nothing; and why the elaborate stages? Creation from God Himself (which the Rabbi identified with the Upanishads): the limitless cannot make limits of itself ( the stone God cannot lift is semantics, like \"I can't catch the flu\"); why would God limit Himself, suffer, choose a part, or leave it unredeemed? The Arizal's tzimtzum is similar but different : withdrawal and creation from nothing, not creation out of God. Rav Saadia would probably still reject it. The combination (Sankhya): refuted by the same arguments. Dualism (Mani): two gods of good and evil, the easy way out of the problem of evil. Chazal fought it in the Mishnah ( modim modim ) and in our blessings ( yotzer or uvorei choshech ). Rav Saadia: one person can have two natures; why would good join evil? And darkness is only an absence , a point on which the Hachamim differ ( Rabbeinu Tam 's 72 minutes). Coming Up Next week: the remaining theories, \"much quicker, because some of them are quite bizarre,\" aiming to finish the chapter so the class can move on to the next essay. \"We've already established scientifically nowadays that there was a creation, so we shouldn't spend too long on this.\"","IqVrJp7o_0w":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (Rambam), Chelek Aleph — Rabbi Chaim Makbili's Hebrew edition / Sefaria Chapter: I:17 — the need to keep esoteric teachings hidden , and החומר, הצורה וההעדר (matter, form and privation) Date: First shiur of 5787, in the days after Rosh Hashanah — תזכו לשנים רבות נעימות וטובות Topic: Why even scientific matters must be taught in metaphor; the three principles of all generated and perishing things; why privation drives all change; how Hashem is the tzur above the tzurah ; and why the Rambam must ration what he teaches Schedule note: Chapter 18 was planned for this evening but was not reached — Chapter 17 needed the full hour. Next shiur: Chapters 18 (karav/nigash — drawing near), 19 (maleh — filling) and 20 (ram — exalted) , all three of which are relatively easy and should fit in one hour. That shiur will either be next Tuesday 8:00pm or after Sukkot — to be confirmed on the group. No shiur on Chol HaMoed ; we resume after Sukkot regardless. The Rabbi's request: this is a very important chapter and not an easy one. Those present and those listening to the recording should review it — go over the notes, listen again — before we move on. Recap — Chapter 16: Tzur We reviewed the whole of Section 1 last time and then learned Chapter 16 , which is directly relevant here. Tzur can mean a boulder, a sharp flint, or a quarry — but the purpose of the chapter is the figurative meaning: the source from which something originates . Hence Yeshayahu's הביטו אל צור חצבתם — look to where you come from, Avraham and Sarah, and copy their ways. Theologically: when tzur is used of HaKadosh Baruch Hu it always means the ultimate source and cause of all existence , never a physical rock. And so ונצבת על הצור — combining nitzav (permanence, from the earlier chapters) with tzur — means: firmly and constantly contemplate Hashem as the source of all being , and thereby attain a higher knowledge of Him. Where Chapter 17 Sits Chapter 16 was tzur . Chapter 18 is karav and nigash — drawing near. Chapter 19 is maleh , Chapter 20 is ram — more of the terms we have been working through all along. Chapter 17 does something completely different : it discusses the importance of keeping esoteric teachings hidden from the masses . It looks random and out of place. It is not — but we will read the chapter first, and only then return to the question of the sequence. The chapter is not complex; it simply requires laying out a few Aristotelian concepts. The Chapter — Three Paragraphs Paragraph One — Even Science Must Be Veiled אל תחשוב שרק מדעי האלוהות נמנעו מן ההמון, אלא גם רוב מדעי הטבע Do not think that only the divine sciences are withheld from the masses — most of natural science is too. וכבר אמרתי לך דברי חז\"ל: ולא במעשה בראשית בשניים As we learned in the Introduction, the Mishnah in Chagigah teaches that even Ma'aseh Bereishit — the deep scientific matters, the building blocks of existence — may not be taught even to two people at once, but only one to one. ודבר זה אינו רק בדת שלנו, אלא גם בדת הפילוסופים And this is not only our religion's position — the philosophers held the same : certain concepts must be kept from the masses. חכמי האומות בימי קדם היו מסבירים את הדיונים על הראשית ומדברים עליהם בחידות The sages of the nations in ancient times explained discussions of first principles in riddles . ואפלטון ומי שקדם לו היו מכנים את החומר נקבה ואת הצורה זכר Plato and his predecessors called matter female and form male — metaphors, not plain statement. Reminder: chomer and tzurah Everything in this universe, in Aristotelian and Platonic thought, is composed of chomer (matter) and tzurah (form). Take a chair: it is made of matter, but what makes it a chair is that it has the form of a chair. Matter plus form together produce the composite object. Everything physical is a composite of the two. Why male and female? Because the form acts upon the matter , as the male acts upon the female. Think of the conception of a child: a tiny drop from the man, processed over nine months by the woman, produces a child. The man's contribution is the acting, forming principle — the tzurah ; the woman is the substance that receives and develops it — the chomer . (We went into this at length in Chapter 6 with ish and ishah, and these themes recur throughout the book.) The Rambam's point in this paragraph Over the last many chapters we have been explaining terms whose plain sense is not their meaning — kisei is a throne but means majesty ; shamayim is heaven but means permanence and endurance . Why does the Tanach speak this way instead of saying it outright? Because lofty ideas revealed outright are dangerous — injurious to someone not ready for them. And what the Rambam adds here is that this is true not only of theology but of science : even the philosophers, when speaking of the building blocks of existence, wrapped them in metaphor. A parenthesis on Plato. This is the first time the Rambam names Plato in the Moreh. He alluded to Aristotle earlier, in Chapter 5 — the caution required in approaching the burning bush — without naming him, and he will cite Aristotle many times throughout the book. Plato appears far less. He will be discussed at length in Section 2 , where we treat the origin of all existence — creation ex nihilo versus creation from pre-existing matter. (Plato was, of course, Aristotle's teacher.) Paragraph Two — The Three Principles This is the key paragraph, and the one that needs explaining. יודע אתה שראשיות הנמצאים ההווים ונפסדים שלוש: החומר, והצורה, וההעדר המיוחד המחובר עמו תמיד The principles of all things that come into being and perish are three: matter, form, and the particular privation that is always joined to matter . Everything material has a beginning and an end — even if that span is thousands or millions of years. Until now we have spoken of two principles, chomer and tzurah . Now a third: הֶעְדֵּר — deficiency, lack, absence; in the English editions, privation . לולי חיבורו של ההעדר אליו — לא היה מקבל צורה Were privation not joined to matter, matter would never receive a form at all. ומהבחינה הזאת ההעדר נמנה עם הראשיות And in that respect privation is counted among the principles . עם קבלת הצורה יבטל אותו ההעדר — כלומר העדר הצורה שקיבל החומר — ויתחבר לו העדר אחר. וכן לעולם, כפי שידוע במדעי הטבע. When the form is received, that privation is annulled — and a new privation attaches in its place. And so on forever , as is known in natural science. The Aristotelian background Principle 1: things that are perfect do not change. This is why God does not change — He is perfect. (This is also, incidentally, the root of Aristotle's conclusion that the universe is eternal and uncreated: for there to be an act of creation there must be an imperfection, a deficiency. We will treat the Rambam's arguments against the eternity of the universe at length in Section 2 — mentioned here only as a hakdamah. Aristotle was wrong, as we now know from the Big Bang; but until the 1920s–60s his was the most widely held view, because as a general rule the laws of the universe do not change: things reproduce and perish, but the rules of physics hold constant, and in that sense they are \"perfect.\") Principle 2: everything is a composite of chomer and tzurah. There is no pure matter and no pure form in this world. The inference: if perfect things do not change, and all there were was matter and form, nothing would ever change — matter and form would simply sit there. So there must be a third ingredient instigating every change — and that ingredient must be a lack , a deficiency, a privation. Because it is precisely the absence of perfection that produces change. So: when matter \"senses\" a he'eder , it calls upon itself a further form to attach to it, transforming it into something else and annulling that deficiency. Four illustrations 1. The unmusical man (Aristotle's kind of example). Take a person who has never encountered music — cannot play violin, trumpet, shofar, trombone. He goes to a teacher and after some years can play the violin. Element In the analogy Chomer (matter) The human being — the unmusical man Tzurah (form) Musicality — the concept of violin-playing; nothing material about it He'eder (privation) \"I don't know how to play the violin\" He passes a wedding, sees someone playing, and feels the lack — and that lack is what drives him to the teacher . Without it he would simply have remained as he was. Then he masters the violin, grade nine, the lot — the deficiency is filled. And the next day he goes to another wedding, hears a trumpet, and a new deficiency kicks in: I can play violin, but I can't play trumpet. וכן לעולם. 2. The bronze. A chunk of bronze is already matter with some (haphazard) form. The deficiency in its being a mere chunk is what drives it to be made into a statue . And then: lovely — but it could be made finer still. 3. The Rambam's own analogy (which he gives later in the Moreh, Section 3): the adulterous woman. A single woman seeks marriage — there is a form, the form of a couple, a unit. Married, she is matter joined to form. But the promiscuous woman says I want to taste something else — and finds a second man; the deficiency satisfied, a third is now wanted. And so on. (The reason the analogy runs this way round — woman as the seeker — is that chomer is the female principle, and it is matter that bears the privation and reaches for new form.) The Rabbi's aside (his own, offered briefly and left for us to develop): this explains a great deal of what one sees in the world today. When everything is possible and everything has been tried, one must reach for ever more obscure things — because there is always another absence demanding to be filled. 4. A participant's example — the acorn. An acorn has both form and substance. Planted, it has a deficiency: it needs to become a tree. It becomes a sapling — still not a full oak, so a further deficiency, and it grows and grows. The Rabbi: a fantastic example; it fits perfectly. (And ultimately it dies — why things die is a separate question, but without deficiency it would simply persist unchanged forever, which it cannot.) Paragraph Three — All The More So For Us ואם אלה שאין להם נזק בהעברה השתמשו במונחים מושאלים ונקטו דימויים וטרחו בלימוד — כל שכן אנחנו מקבלי התורה, שמוטלת החובה עלינו לא לומר בפירוש דבר שקשה להמון להבינו, או שיבינו את אמיתת העניין באופן שונה ממה שצריך ללמד. דע זאת גם כן. If they — whose subject matter carries no great danger in transmission — nonetheless used borrowed terms, resorted to similes, and laboured over how to teach, how much more so we who received the Torah . We are obliged not to state plainly what the masses will find hard, or what they will understand differently from how it ought to be taught. Know this too. The Two Questions Read plainly, the chapter's purpose is Paragraphs 1 and 3: esoteric matters must be kept hidden, in science as in Torah. Which leaves two problems: Why is this chapter here at all , between tzur (16) and karav/nigash (18)? Why is Paragraph Two here? Paragraphs 1 and 3 make the point perfectly well on their own. Why introduce the whole business of privation? The class's suggestions (all taken as partial): Doron — perhaps it is really a preface to the entire project of explaining terms, and so belongs at Chapter 1 or 2; and Paragraph 2 may simply be an example of a lofty idea that must be handled carefully — after all, it took us half an hour. Another participant — Paragraph 2 is \"the motivation of matter,\" an illustration of why such things need veiling. The Rabbi: both are acceptable as far as they go, but incomplete — there is much more going on here. A further exchange: one participant suggested tzurah be understood as \"a creative force.\" Corrected: tzurah is form , nothing more. Another raised the difficulty of reconciling any of this with current scientific knowledge. The Rabbi: whether this science is true today is not the point of the chapter ; this is what the Rambam, following Aristotle and Plato, believed, and our task is to understand what he is doing with it. Answer to Question One — Hashem Is the Tzur of the Tzurah (The Rabbi: \"I'm surprised nobody picked up on it — I've been banging on about tzurah , and Chapter 16 was about tzur .\" He credits this to Korobkin , whom he has quoted before, though he cannot recall Korobkin's own source.) צור — the source and origin of all existence (Chapter 16). צורה — form. Now notice a grammatical oddity. In Plato's, Aristotle's and the Rambam's thought, form is the male principle — it acts upon matter. Yet tzurah is a feminine noun. Any noun ending in heh is feminine — with exactly one exception in biblical Hebrew: לילה (hence laylah tov , not laylah tovah ). Why should form be a feminine noun? Because something acts even upon form. Who is the ultimate male, the ultimate actor? HaKadosh Baruch Hu. He is the tzur of the tzurah — above form, above matter, above privation. Relative to Him, even form is feminine , in that it receives its instruction from Him. And that is the continuation from Chapter 16. This is the difference between studying science and studying science with HaKadosh Baruch Hu : yes, there is chomer , there is tzurah , there is he'eder — but above all of it is Hashem, directing it, causing form to combine with matter and privation and so producing all that exists. Having established in Chapter 16 that Hashem is the origin of all being, the Rambam now shows how that origin stands over the physical structure of the world . Answer to Question Two — Privation Applied to Human Beings Paragraph Two is here because the Rambam is describing us . First, a refinement of Chapter 6. Generally the male is tzurah and the female chomer — but a human being can be either, depending on context . The Rabbi teaching the class is tzurah , and everyone listening — men included — is chomer ; later this evening or tomorrow the roles reverse. A woman teaching a room of boys is the tzurah and they are the chomer . It shifts constantly. Either way, the point stands: human beings, like matter, are constantly hungry to fill a deficiency. Materially The natural state of a human being is to want more . I have money, I want more money; power, more power; food, more food. This is the meaning of Shlomo HaMelech's אוהב כסף לא ישבע כסף — and, as Rabbeinu Avraham makes explicit in HaMaspik Chapter 9 (which we learn on Monday nights, this coming week, on histapkut — contentment), this is not specifically about money; money is an example of all material matter. Map it: I am the chomer ; the intellectual construct of \"being rich\" — the holidays, the freedom, whatever your mental image is — is the tzurah ; and the he'eder attaches to me and says \"more.\" My target: £X by the end of the year. I make it. The deficiency is annulled — and instantly a new one kicks in. I wanted this food; I've had it, now the next; this wine, then the next. There is no end. (This, incidentally, is precisely why histapkut is such a hard character trait .) Intellectually — and this is the Rambam's real move Beyond material things, the same structure governs intellect and identity . The Rambam writes explicitly later in the Moreh that a person naturally wants to develop himself intellectually . Nobody wants to stagnate; we all have a drive to move forward. And when a person does not respond to that he'eder — that is what the Rambam calls ra , bad. He says it specifically about the failure of intellectual development : a person who does not recognize that the privation is acting upon him, and ignores it, is bad . Therefore — the Rambam's own teaching method This is why Paragraph Two sits between Paragraphs One and Three. The Rambam is saying: I must be careful how much I give you, because the more I feed you, the hungrier you become. The thirst analogy. You are the chomer ; water is the tzurah ; thirst is the he'eder . Fill it — but fill it too fast and a person can die from over-drinking. The fast-day analogy. Think of last night, after the fast. We are all famished — and what do most of us do? Overeat , and then lie awake with stomach ache. That is exactly the danger in teaching. \"I am giving you intellect and information throughout this book,\" says the Rambam in effect. \"I must give it to you in a way that respects these three ingredients — in order, slowly, esoterically — or I will drown you.\" The he'eder has to come in sequence : fill one, then the next, then the next. Lay the whole table out at once and the student is harmed. Summary — Chapter 17 The Rambam teaches that profound theological and scientific ideas must sometimes be conveyed through metaphor , because revealing them directly to one who is not ready is harmful — a point he makes at the very start of the chapter. He then sets out that physical change occurs when matter acquires form in response to a deficiency , and that once that deficiency is filled another immediately emerges — endlessly. He applies this to human beings, who are driven to seek more both materially and intellectually . And therefore: \"I must be careful how much knowledge I reveal, and to whom\" — because overfeeding an appetite that by its nature can never be satisfied is dangerous. The structure of the chapter is the argument: Paragraph One establishes the practice, Paragraph Two supplies the reason it is necessary, Paragraph Three drives it home kal vachomer for those who received the Torah. And the placement after Chapter 16 is no accident: Hashem is the tzur of the tzurah — the source standing above matter, form and privation alike. Q&A Mark: Without privation, could there be free will? — The Rabbi: it likely does tie in. With only chomer and tzurah we would be angelic — what we are at birth is what we would remain. And it is not only free will that would go: there would be no growth at all — our bodies would not develop, we would be born a baby and stay a baby. (An angel simply is what it is; there is no growing.) Jonathan: Is the Rambam effectively offering a synthesis — absorbing the Aristotelian structure of form and substance and superimposing a divine edifice upon it, reconciling the scientific knowledge of his day with divine involvement? With the ultimate tzurah as the divine intellect continually inspiring form to act upon matter — so that existence is not a stable situation but an inherently unstable one, divinely programmed with a drive toward improvement ? — The Rabbi: 100%, exactly correct. And it ties directly to what those at the Monday night shiurim learned in HaMaspik Chapter 8 (bitachon) , just before the summer break: Rabbeinu Avraham on the importance of learning science and then placing Hashem on top of it — the whole edifice, and then Hashem above it. That is precisely what is happening here. A participant: There seems to be an echo of the burning bush in this chapter — Hashem being approached with caution, and this whole area likewise being approached with caution. — The Rabbi: correct, it links very well with Chapter 5. Philip raised the question of teshuvah in relation to all this — deferred, since the discussion at that point was still confined to matter rather than human beings. (The human application above is the beginning of the answer; the Rabbi noted separately that a person working on himself against his natural state is a different matter — \"that's teshuvah, let's leave that.\") Coming Up Chapters 18, 19 and 20 — karav and nigash (drawing near), maleh (filling), ram (exalted). All relatively easy; all three should fit in one hour. That shiur will be either next Tuesday 8:00pm or after Sukkot — watch the group. No shiur on Chol HaMoed. Review Chapter 17 before then. B'Ezrat Hashem","JD2CViA_ycU":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nebuchim (The Guide for the Perplexed), Section 1 Chapters: I:23 — יצא (to go out) and שב (to return); I:24 — הלך (to go) Shiur: Tuesday 3 November 2026, 8:00pm, on Zoom Schedule note: The Rabbi hoped to cover Chapters 23, 24 and 25 tonight. Chapters 23 and 24 were completed; Chapter 25 ( שכן , to dwell) moves to next week. These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are Last week we completed Chapter 21 , the word עבר (to pass), which took a couple of weeks. It covered the whole passage in פרשת כי תשא where we say the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy. We saw five meanings of the word עבר across the Torah and four ways of reading the verses, and we worked through the passage according to each one. We then learned Chapter 22 , the word בא (to come), which has four meanings: # Meaning of בא Physical? 1 Physical movement towards something Yes 2 Physical movement into something, into a defined place Yes 3 Something occurring, something being fulfilled No 4 Even the \"coming\" of a privation, a negative, such as darkness or evil No When בא is used of HaKadosh Baruch Hu it can never mean physical movement. It means either His Shechinah becoming manifest, or His words and promises being fulfilled. Tonight: three verbs The plan for the evening: Chapter Verb Meaning 23 יצא (and its opposite שב ) To go out (and to return) 24 הלך To go 25 שכן To dwell (next week) \"These are three relatively short, important chapters, as they all are of course.\" Part One — Chapter 23: יצא, \"To Go Out\" Why this chapter now On the simplest level the continuation is obvious. Chapter 22 discussed בא , coming, so Chapter 23 discusses its opposite, יציאה , going out. The Rambam opens: פרק כ\"ג: היציאה כנגד הביאה Going out corresponds to coming. יציאה is the corollary of ביאה ; the two are symmetrical. (The Rabbi later suggested a second reason for the placement, in Part Two.) The two meanings of יצא Meaning 1: a physical body leaving one place for another. משמש ליציאת גוף ממקום שהיה נח בו למקום אחר, בין אם הגוף ההוא בעל חיים ובין אם לאו בעל חיים It is used for a body going out of the place where it was resting to another place, whether that body is a living creature or not. A corporeal entity was in one place, resting there, and it went out to a new place. It makes no difference whether it is animate or inanimate. Living beings: הם יצאו את העיר לא הרחיקו (Bereishit 44:4). This is the end of פרשת מקץ . The brothers, the שבטים , had just left Yosef's home, and Yosef commanded that they be pursued. \"They, that's human beings, left. They were in one place, in Yosef's home, and they moved and they left. They went out to a new place.\" Non-living things: כי תצא אש (Shemot 22:5), in פרשת משפטים . If fire goes out and consumes a stack of grain, you have to pay. \"The fire is going out from place A into place B, although the fire is not a בעל חיים , it's not a living organism.\" \"That's the first meaning. That is pretty straightforward and clear.\" Meaning 2: the appearance of something that is not a body. והושאל להופעת דבר שאינו גוף It was borrowed for the appearance of something that is not a body. The Rambam's examples: הדבר יצא מפי המלך ופני המן חפו (Esther 7:8). As soon as Achashverosh suspected Haman of acting inappropriately with Esther, \"the matter came out of the king's mouth,\" and Haman's face was covered. \"What does it mean, the word came out of the mouth of Achashverosh? It doesn't mean literally something came out. It's not talking about the sound waves.\" The matter came out in a metaphorical sense. כי יצא דבר המלכה על כל הנשים להבזות בעליהן בעיניהן (Esther 1:17). Memuchan, Achashverosh's adviser, warns that when word of what the queen did gets out, women will belittle their husbands. \"There are no words travelling from one place to another place like the brothers travelled, or even like the fire travels. What it means is that the matter will spread.\" The Rambam: כלומר התפשטות הדבר , the spreading of the matter, its publicity. כי מציון תצא תורה ודבר ה' מירושלים (Yeshayahu 2:3). \"What, Torah's going to walk out? It's going to creep out? No, it's going to be publicised from Tziyon.\" השמש יצא על הארץ ולוט בא צוערה (Bereishit 19:23), from last week's parasha. Lot reached Tzoar just as the sun had come out. The Rambam: כלומר הופעת האור , the appearance of the light. The sun is not literally coming out; the light is spreading. Summary of the two meanings # Meaning of יצא Examples Physical? 1 A body leaving one place for another, animate or inanimate הם יצאו את העיר ; כי תצא אש Yes 2 The appearance or spreading of something non-corporeal הדבר יצא מפי המלך ; כי יצא דבר המלכה ; כי מציון תצא תורה ; השמש יצא על הארץ No Part Two — \"Behold, Hashem Is Coming Out of His Place\" The rule וכל לשון יציאה שנאמרה בו יתעלה Whenever the word \"going out\" is said of Him, may He be exalted, it must be the second meaning, never the first. The verse הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו (Michah 1:3) — Behold, Hashem is coming out of His place. The Rambam explains: יופיע דברו המוסתר כעת מאיתנו His word, which is now hidden from us, will appear. כלומר, יצירת מה שלא יצא להוויה That is, the bringing into being of what had not yet come into being. כי כל היווצרות על ידו יתעלה מיוחסת לדבריו For everything brought into being by Him is attributed to His word, as it says, בדבר ה' שמים נעשו וברוח פיו כל צבאם (Tehillim 33:6), by the word of Hashem the heavens were made. בדמיון לפעולות היוצאות מן המלכים, שהכלי שלהם לביצוע רצונם הוא הדיבור This is like the actions that come from kings, whose instrument for carrying out their will is speech. והוא יתעלה אינו נזקק לכלי לפעול באמצעותו, אלא פעולתו היא רצונו בלבד, וגם אין דיבור כלל כמו שנתבאר But He, may He be exalted, needs no instrument through which to act; His action is His will alone. And there is no speech at all, as has been explained. Adding a word \"You have to add in a word. This is important.\" The verse הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו is to be read as: הנה דבר ה' יוצא ממקומו Behold, the word of Hashem is coming out of His place. Why the \"word\" of Hashem means the will of Hashem Kings and powerful, authoritative people get their will done through speech, through their commands. So when the Torah wants to describe Hashem wanting something done, it speaks of His word. \"Not that there is any word with HaKadosh Baruch Hu. That's impossible.\" \"And not that Hashem needs the words.\" \"Whatever God's רצון is, we're not discussing that now. When Hashem's רצון wants to be done, it talks about His word.\" \"So the word of Hashem is the will of Hashem. You understand that?\" What \"His place\" means We learned in Chapter 8 of the Moreh that מקום , used of Hashem, means His special status, His exalted standing. Before the event, the will of Hashem was מוסתר , hidden, concealed, still \"in its place\", in its special status with the will of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. When Hashem wants it to be manifested, it comes out. \"The word of Hashem comes out and is manifested from its original מקום , its original special status. It comes out from there and is manifested, is publicised.\" So יוצא , used of Hashem, means: becoming readily apparent, manifested, publicised. A second reason for the chapter's placement The Rabbi suggested another reason why the Rambam brings this chapter now. In Chapter 21 the Rambam conceded to Onkelos that sometimes words must be added to a verse; Onkelos added שכינה / יקרא , and the Rambam himself added קול . In Chapter 22 the Rambam added a word too. הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו read as הנה דבר ה' יוצא ממקומו is another example of the same principle: \"sometimes verses have to have words added to them.\" Summary of the argument Step The verse הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו Read it as הנה דבר ה' יוצא ממקומו — the word of Hashem is coming out \"Word\" means The will of Hashem (as kings act through their words) \"His place\" means His special, exalted status, where the will was concealed (Chapter 8) \"Coming out\" means Becoming manifest, apparent, publicised (meaning 2) Part Three — שב: The Other Opposite of יצא Two opposites The final paragraph of the chapter is \"very important. It's an introduction to something which we'll learn much later, but it starts making us think.\" יצא has two opposites: Pair Verse Source יצא / בא — going out and coming in לא אוכל עוד לצאת ולבוא — I can no longer go out and come in Moshe at 120 (Devarim 31:2) יצא / שב — going out and returning ויצא יצוא ושוב עד יבשת המים מעל הארץ — it went out, going out and returning, until the waters dried up Noach's raven (Bereishit 8:7) The Rambam on שב וכיוון שיציאה הושאלה להופעת פעולה מפעולותיו, כמו שביארנו בנאמר ה' יוצא ממקומו Since \"going out\" was borrowed for the appearance of one of His actions, as we explained regarding \"Hashem is coming out of His place,\" הושאלה גם שיבה להסתלקות אותה פעולה בעת הימנע רצון האלוהי \"returning\" was also borrowed for the withdrawal of that action when the divine will is withheld. יציאה means Hashem's will becoming more manifested. שיבה means it going back, returning to its original place, no longer manifested. \"I will go and return to My place\" וכן נאמר: אלכה ואשובה אל מקומי (Hoshea 5:15) \"How beautiful!\" (The Rabbi set aside אלכה until Chapter 24.) המשמעות היא הסתלקות מאתנו של השכינה שהייתה בינינו The meaning is the withdrawal from us of the Shechinah that had been among us. \"I will return to My place\" means: I will return to being aloof from you, to My special status. \" הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו — Hashem is coming out from His aloofness. What was concealed is becoming readily manifested, public and apparent. No, no, no. When you misbehave, אשובה אל מקומי , I will go back to my aloofness, I will go back to my special status, I will leave you.\" What the withdrawal means: the loss of hashgacha המובילה להעדר השגחה עלינו which leads to the absence of providence over us. As it says in the curses: ועזבתים והסתרתי פני מהם והיה לאכול ומצאוהו רעות רבות וצרות (Devarim 31:17) I will forsake them and hide My face from them, and they will be devoured, and many evils and troubles will find them. \"You turn your face from Me, I will turn My face from you. That means I'm not being משגיח .\" The biggest curse: randomness משום שאם תעדר השגחה, יופקר, ויעשה מטרה לכל מה שיוכל לקרות For if providence is absent, a person is abandoned and becomes a target for anything that may happen. והטוב והרע שיבוא עליו יהיה כפי המקרה and the good and the bad that come upon him will be by chance. כמה קשה איום זה How terrible a threat this is! The Rabbi highlighted two points. 1. Without hashgacha, life is not necessarily bad. It is random. \"Notice, when Hashem leaves His hashgacha from you, it doesn't mean you're going to have a bad life. It could be you'll have a good life. But it's random. Good or bad which happens to you is completely coincidental. It could be you'll have everything good, but it's luck. The good which you have can change any moment due to randomness.\" 2. It is not a punishment. It is measure for measure. \"This isn't a punishment. It's simply מידה כנגד מידה ,\" something the Rambam touched on in the very first chapter. \"You turn your face from Hashem, you turn your interest from Hashem, Hashem turns His face from you. Simple as that.\" \"Until they seek My face\" The verse in Hoshea continues: אלך ואשובה אל מקומי עד אשר יאשמו ובקשו פני — until they acknowledge their guilt and seek My face. \"It's so beautiful, Rambam, it's so precise. I am going back to my aloofness because you're not interested in me. But if בקשו פני , if you want my face, if you want my hashgacha, if you want to be involved with me, I'll come back. It's as simple as that.\" The Rambam will discuss hashgacha in great detail later in the Moreh. \"This is not the place to go into the Rambam's שיטה of hashgacha, that's a whole discussion in itself. But in short, the Rambam holds, very simply: if you seek Hashem, you get Hashem. If you don't, you're left to randomness.\" Hashgacha is commensurate with a person's own desire for a relationship with HaKadosh Baruch Hu: \"The more a person wants there to be hashgacha, the more there will be hashgacha.\" Why sometimes בא and sometimes שב? Pair When it is used Example יציאה / ביאה Leaving A to arrive at B Moshe: \"I can't go out of the desert and come into Kena'an\" — לצאת ולבוא יציאה / שיבה Leaving A for B, then coming back to A The raven goes out of the ark into the sky and comes back to the ark — יצוא ושוב (On לא אוכל עוד לצאת ולבוא there are many explanations; the Rabbi used the simplest: \"I can't go out from the desert and I can't come into Yisrael.\") Applied to HaKadosh Baruch Hu, \"it makes beautiful sense\": \"Naturally, I am aloof. I'm HaKadosh Baruch Hu. I'm giving you a favour. I'm giving you some of My hashgacha, some of My light, some of My presence. I'm coming out, making Myself accessible and manifested to you. You don't want that? I will return to My place. I'm coming out from A to B, making Myself accessible to human beings. You don't want that? I will go back to מקומי , to being exalted and aloof from human beings.\" יצא of Hashem שב of Hashem Verse הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו אלכה ואשובה אל מקומי Meaning His will becomes manifest, accessible His will returns to concealment; His Shechinah withdraws For us Hashgacha Absence of hashgacha: randomness When — When we turn our face from Him Reversed when — ובקשו פני — when we seek Him Part Four — \"And Avraham Returned to His Place\" The Rabbi ended the chapter with a verse from last week's parasha, Vayera. After Avraham's long negotiation with HaKadosh Baruch Hu over Sedom, \"forwards and backwards, having a dialogue, he thinks he's got a point\", the parasha ends: וילך ה' כאשר כלה לדבר אל אברהם ואברהם שב למקומו (Bereishit 18:33) Hashem went when He had finished speaking to Avraham, and Avraham returned to his place. \"What does that mean? He goes back to his place, his place of what? Of being a human being who can't argue with God.\" \"Until now, Avraham came out: the big negotiator, the challenger of HaKadosh Baruch Hu. Incredible. He tries. Unsuccessful. ואברהם שב למקומו . Avraham returns back to his place. What's your place? You're a human being. Go back to your place.\" We have the same idiom in English: to put someone in their place. The Rabbi noted that this is his own reading, not one the Rambam gives: \"That was a thought. I was reading Vayera and thought of that.\" From the class Doron pointed out that this explains why both שב and מקומו must be read this way. Avraham was talking with Hashem, possibly in a vision. \"What on earth does it mean, ואברהם שב למקומו ? It was a dream. Where did he go that he needs to come back from?\" The Rabbi agreed: \"Exactly. It was a dream. So he returned back to his status. All these years we've been reading this verse. What does this mean, Avraham returned to his place? Now it makes absolute, perfect sense based on what we've learned in Rambam.\" A second participant asked whether מקום must mean special status, or simply one's status relative to another. The Rabbi checked Chapter 8 with the class: the Rambam brings both usages, as in פלוני במקום פלוני . \"In the context of HaKadosh Baruch Hu it means special status. In the context of others, it just means your status. Correct, very good.\" So Avraham goes back to his status relative to Hashem, the lower status of \"a regular human being, not arguing with the divine.\" \"Avraham was willing to challenge the divine, חלילה לך . That was special, that was a coming out, that was unusual. But now he comes back to his place, comes back to being a human being, and recognising you can't challenge HaKadosh Baruch Hu.\" Summary of Chapter 23 יציאה (going out) is the counterpart of ביאה (coming), Chapter 22. It has two meanings: (1) a body, living or not, leaving one place for another; (2) the appearance and spreading of something non-corporeal. Used of Hashem it is always the second meaning. הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו = הנה דבר ה' יוצא ממקומו : His word, which is His will, comes out of its concealment in His exalted status and becomes manifest. שב (returning) is a second opposite of יצא . Used of Hashem, אלכה ואשובה אל מקומי means the withdrawal of His Shechinah and hashgacha. Without hashgacha a person is left to randomness. Good or bad comes by chance. This is not a punishment but מידה כנגד מידה , and it is reversed the moment we seek Him: ובקשו פני . יצא / בא is used for going from A to B; יצא / שב for going from A to B and back to A. The Rabbi's application: ואברהם שב למקומו — Avraham returns to his status as a human being after challenging the divine. \"Think about it. Go over this. There's a lot more which can be said about it. Whenever we see the words יצא or שב or בא , think about why that specific word has been used, especially when it's in the context of HaKadosh Baruch Hu.\" Part Five — Chapter 24: הלך, \"To Go\" Why this chapter now \"Now that makes total sense.\" We have had בא , coming (22), and יצא , going out (23), and Chapter 23 quoted אלכה ואשובה . Having explained אשובה , the Rambam now explains אלכה : what does \"I will go\" mean? גם הליכה היא מכלל המונחים שנקבעו לתנועה מסוימת מתנועת בעלי חיים \"Going\" too is one of the terms coined for a particular movement of living creatures. The four meanings of הלך The Rabbi took three now and the fourth at the end of the chapter, as the Rambam does. Meaning 1: a living creature moving from place to place. ויעקב הלך לדרכו ויפגעו בו מלאכי אלהים (Bereishit 32:2), which we will read in a couple of weeks. \"Human beings moving, going from one place to another place.\" The Rambam adds וזה נפוץ , this is very common. Meaning 2: the spreading and extending of bodies subtler than those of living creatures. מונח זה הושאל להתפשטות והימתחות של גופים שהם דקים יותר מגופי בעלי חיים והמים היו הלוך וחסור עד החדש העשירי (Bereishit 8:5). The waters \"went and decreased\" for a few months. \"Where are they going? They're going in the sense they are decreasing. They are moving.\" וה' נתן קלת וברד ותהלך אש ארצה (Shemot 9:23), in פרשת וארא , the plague of hail: fire went down to the earth. \"Those are both corporeal things, although one is more subtle than the other.\" Meaning 3: the spreading of something, even something not corporeal at all. לאחר מכן הושאל להתפשטות דבר כל שהוא והפצתו, גם אם אינו גשמי כלל קולה כנחש ילך (Yirmiyahu 46:22). Yirmiyahu prophesies that the Egyptians will suffer so greatly that their voice will go like a snake. \"Just like a snake is heard everywhere — you can be very far and you hear the hissing of the snake and you get scared — so too the Egyptians will be in so much pain and distress that their voice will be heard everywhere.\" וישמעו את קול ה' אלהים מתהלך בגן לרוח היום (Bereishit 3:8). The Rambam: הקול הוא שנאמר שהיה מתהלך , it is the voice that is said to be walking. \"It's not that they heard God in the garden. That's a wrong reading of the verse. They heard the voice of Hashem going in the garden.\" Sound waves. The Rabbi noted that today we might say a voice \"goes\" in the sense that sound waves travel. \"They didn't know that then. Sound waves are not apparent to the naked eye.\" The Rambam, without the concept of sound waves, treats a voice \"going\" as something non-physical spreading. Meaning 4: walking in upright ways (see Part Eight). # Meaning of הלך Examples Physical? 1 Movement of a living creature from place to place ויעקב הלך לדרכו Yes 2 Spreading of subtler bodies (water, fire) והמים היו הלוך וחסור ; ותהלך אש ארצה Yes, though subtle 3 Spreading of something non-corporeal קולה כנחש ילך ; קול ה' אלהים מתהלך בגן No 4 Walking in upright, moral ways והלכת בדרכיו No — and said of people, not of God Part Six — הלך Said of Hashem The rule לפי השאלה זו, כל לשון הליכה שנאמרה בה' יתעלה According to this borrowing, every use of \"going\" said of Hashem, may He be exalted, is the third meaning, something non-corporeal. It means one of two things: אם להתפשטות הדבר, אם לסילוק השגחה either the spreading of a matter, or the removal of providence. Why \"going\" can mean removing hashgacha Spreading is easy to understand: when something goes, it spreads. But why should going mean the removal of hashgacha? שבעל חיים דומה לכך — הפנייה לאחור מדבר מה, דבר שבעל חיים עושה בהליכה Because a living creature, when it walks, turns its back on something. \"When you walk in a certain direction, you turn your back to the other direction. If I walk here now, I'm turning my back to you.\" So הלוך can mean \"I'm removing My hashgacha. I'm turning, I'm going from you.\" The Rambam ties this to the language of the hidden face: כשם שכינה את הסרת ההשגחה הסרת פנים — just as Scripture calls the removal of providence \"the hiding of the face\": ואנכי הסתר אסתיר פני ביום ההוא על כל הרעה אשר עשה כי פנה אל אלהים אחרים (Devarim 31:18) I will surely hide My face on that day, because he turned to other gods. \"Because you turned your face.\" Just as it is called turning the face, כך כינה אותה הליכה , so too it is called going: שיש משמעות שפניה לאחור מדבר מה , which means turning away from something. ועל זה נאמר: אלך ואשובה אל מקומי \"I will go and I will return\": the same thing twice The Rabbi noted (citing a commentator) that, according to Chapters 23 and 24 together, אלך ואשובה אל מקומי turns out to be a repetition. \"Not a difficulty, but something we should be aware of.\" Word Chapter Meaning used of Hashem אלך — I will go 24 I will turn away — like a creature that walks off and turns its back — and remove My hashgacha ואשובה אל מקומי — and I will return to My place 23 I will return to My aloofness, My exaltedness, and remove My hashgacha \"It's two words, but saying exactly the same thing.\" The verse means: \"I will doubly leave My hashgacha, and go back to My exaltedness and to My aloofness.\" Part Seven — \"Hashem's Anger Burned Against Them, and He Went\" Both meanings at once ויחר אף ה' בם וילך (Bamidbar 12:9) The anger of Hashem burned against them, and He went. This is the story of Aharon, Miriam and Moshe. Miriam and Aharon spoke negatively about Moshe. Hashem came and reprimanded them: \"Why did you speak about My servant Moshe? בכל ביתי נאמן הוא .\" Then: \"the anger of Hashem burned against them, and He went.\" The next verse says Miriam was stricken with tzara'at. (\"Leave חרון אף aside. We're not discussing anger now.\") הרי בהם נמצאו שתי משמעות גם יחד Here both meanings are found together. Meaning In this verse סילוק השגחה, המכונה פנייה לאחור — removal of providence, called turning away Hashem \"went\": He turned away and took His hashgacha from them התפשטות הדבר והתגלותו — the spreading of the matter and its appearance שחרון האף הלך והתפשט על שניהם, ולכך הפכה מרים מצורעת כשלג — the anger went out and spread over the two of them, and so Miriam became leprous as snow \"There's something deep here. Let's work this through slowly.\" Hashem went: He took His hashgacha away from them. And the anger of Hashem spread, diffused, upon them, \"which caused consequences, one of them being that Miriam had leprosy.\" The question \"If we've been listening to what we've been learning this evening about hashgacha, what question should we have on this?\" We learned in Chapter 23 that the absence of hashgacha means randomness, not punishment. So if Hashem was removing His hashgacha, why is there a directed punishment of tzara'at? The Rabbi's answer \"What the Rambam is saying here, I think, is that הליכה means the removal of hashgacha. And as a result of the lack of hashgacha, the randomness spreads and causes the leprosy. Because Hashem takes His hashgacha, this causes the effect in the humans.\" Why Miriam and not Aharon? \"Have you ever thought, in this whole story, why is it that והנה מרים מצורעת כשלג ? Why specifically did Miriam get leprosy and not Aharon?\" Explanation Reasoning The simple answer The verse begins ותדבר מרים ואהרן במשה . Miriam spoke first; she initiated, so it happened to her The Rabbi's reading of the Rambam Hashem \"went,\" leaving His hashgacha; the lack of hashgacha means negative things and randomness spread; \"randomly Miriam gets tzara'at and not Aharon\" The Kuzari (II:62), by contrast All tzara'at, and physical ailments in general, are a directed punishment \"I don't understand that for the Rambam, because for the Rambam there's no directed punishment. It's the opposite. It's the lack of hashgacha which causes bad things to happen to you.\" The anger \"bore fruit, bore consequences, it diffused. The anger diffused to make Miriam have tzara'at.\" The Rabbi invited the class to offer a different interpretation if anyone had one. Part Eight — The Fourth Meaning: Walking in His Ways וכן הושאל לשון הליכה להליכה בהנהגות נעלות, בלי תואר גוף כלל \"Going\" was also borrowed for walking in exalted ways of conduct, with no bodily sense at all. This meaning has nothing to do with God's actions. It is about a human being walking with morality, \"which is why he puts it right at the end.\" Examples: והלכת בדרכיו (Devarim 28:9) — you shall walk in His ways אחרי ה' אלהיכם תלכו ואותו תיראו (Devarim 13:5) — after Hashem your God you shall walk לכו ונלכה באור ה' (Yeshayahu 2:5) — come, let us walk in the light of Hashem Rav Makbili: why the chapter ends here Rav Makbili \"beautifully points out\" why the Rambam adds this at the end: this kind of behaviour causes the hashgacha of Hashem to rest upon us. \"So there won't be הליכה , there won't be שיבה of the previous chapter. If we walk in this path, לכו ונלכה באור ה' , והלכת בדרכיו , then you won't have Hashem's הליכה , you won't have His שיבה .\" When we walk in His ways, He does not walk away from us. Summary of Chapter 24 הלך has four meanings: (1) a creature moving from place to place; (2) the spreading of subtler bodies like water and fire; (3) the spreading, the diffusion, of something non-corporeal, such as a voice; (4) walking in upright, moral ways. Used of Hashem it is always the third meaning, and it means either the spreading of something, or turning away, the removal of hashgacha, just as a creature that walks off turns its back. אלך ואשובה אל מקומי therefore says the same thing twice: \"I will turn away, and I will return to My aloofness.\" ויחר אף ה' בם וילך has both meanings at once: Hashem removed His hashgacha, and His anger spread over them. In the Rabbi's reading, Miriam's tzara'at came about through the randomness that follows the withdrawal of hashgacha, rather than as a directed punishment, in contrast to the Kuzari. The fourth meaning, walking in Hashem's ways, is what brings His hashgacha upon us, so that He does not \"go\" or \"return\". Summary: Three Verbs Used of Hashem Verb Chapter Opposite Used of Hashem means Key verse בא — to come 22 יצא His Shechinah resting; His word being fulfilled הנה אנכי בא אליך בעב הענן יצא — to go out 23 בא ; שב His word, i.e. His will, becoming manifest, coming out of concealment הנה ה' יוצא ממקומו שב — to return 23 יצא His will returning to concealment; the withdrawal of His Shechinah and hashgacha אלכה ואשובה אל מקומי הלך — to go 24 — Either the spreading of something, or turning away and removing hashgacha (or both) אלך ואשובה אל מקומי ; ויחר אף ה' בם וילך The thread running through both chapters: Hashem \"comes out\" to us when He makes His will and His hashgacha manifest, and \"goes\" and \"returns to His place\" when we turn our faces from Him. Without His hashgacha, life is left to chance. It may even be good, but it is random. And the way back is in our hands: ובקשו פני , seek His face, and והלכת בדרכיו , walk in His ways. Coming Up B'Ezrat Hashem, next Tuesday at 8:00pm we will learn Chapter 25 , שכן , to dwell, the root of שכינה . After that the Rambam takes a break of about three chapters from discussing verbs and turns to more general topics.","LBsdXFKV_98":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 1 of 14 Topic: Introduction and Preparation for Shabbat — Friday Afternoon & Candle Lighting Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 16 October 2018 Halachic basis: Primarily the rulings of Chacham Ovadia Yosef zt\"l, noting Ashkenazi practice and the Ben Ish Chai / Kabbalistic view where they differ. Opening The evening opened with words of welcome from Barbara, chair of the shul team, who noted that in the shul's roughly fifteen-year history there had never been a series of fourteen shiurim on one topic. The shiurim were to be recorded so that anyone missing a session could catch up. The Rabbi thanked the rabbis of the synagogue, Dr. Kahen — described as a driving force behind the initiative — the Tiferet Eyal committee, and everyone attending. Coming out every week to learn Hilchot Shabbat is, he said, itself praiseworthy — Chazak u'Baruch . Part A — Why Shabbat Is So Central \"Shomer Shabbat\" — why this mitzvah defines us We ask whether someone is Shomer Shabbat — we do not ask the same about any of the other mitzvot. Shabbat is the one observance by which a person's religious commitment is commonly measured. Why? Zachor and Shamor The Torah records the Ten Commandments twice — in Parashat Yitro (Shemot) and Parashat Va'etchanan (Devarim). The two versions are almost identical, but Chazal immediately noted one major difference: זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת לְקַדְּשׁוֹ (Shemot 20:8) — Remember the Shabbat day to sanctify it. שָׁמוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת לְקַדְּשׁוֹ (Devarim 5:12) — Guard the Shabbat day to sanctify it. Chazal teach: זכור ושמור בדבור אחד נאמרו — Zachor and Shamor were said in a single utterance. Something beyond human comprehension — but its message is that the two are equally important : Zachor — remembering Shamor — guarding Character The positive dimension The restraining dimension Expressions Honouring Shabbat, good food, fine clothes, shul, time with family, appreciating Hashem and Torah Refraining from melacha — the precise details of what one may not do Many Jews are Zochrei Shabbat — they make Kiddush, have the meals, come to shul — without being Shomrei Shabbat . A Shomer Shabbat both marks the day positively and keeps its details. Neither half is enough alone: one cannot only enjoy the day without its laws, nor only keep the laws without appreciating the beauty of the day. The painting analogy. Standing before a beautiful painting, if you come too close and fixate on each brushstroke, you lose the beauty of the whole. Yet without every brushstroke there is no painting. Zachor is the transcendent beauty of the whole; Shamor is the detail. We need both. The email analogy. One wrong dot or hyphen in an email address and the message goes to Australia. The detail matters — but it serves the whole. Three reasons Shabbat is so fundamental Creation — Hashem created the world in six days and rested on the seventh. By working six days and resting on the seventh, we affirm that the universe has a Creator. (Stated explicitly in the Torah.) Yetziat Mitzrayim — Every Kiddush mentions זֵכֶר לִיצִיאַת מִצְרָיִם . In Egypt, despite the back-breaking labour, Pharaoh allowed a rest on the seventh day (as cited from the Moreh Nebuchim). Remembering Egypt affirms not just a Creator, but a God actively involved in the world — Divine Providence, as the miracles of the Exodus demonstrated. Being, not doing — Wherever the Torah commands Shabbat it prefaces: שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים תַּעֲבֹד — six days shall you work. The contrast is deliberate: six days of doing , a seventh day of simply being . \"We are human beings, not human doings.\" The first question people ask is \"What do you do?\" — Shabbat reminds us that our identity is not our occupation. The Rosh Hashanah analogy — Malchiyot, Zichronot, Shofarot The Rabbi recalled his Rosh Hashanah drasha on the three sections of Musaf, using the image of driving a car: Malchiyot — the clear windscreen : our vision ahead. Zichronot — the mirrors : the vision must rest on our past, our mesorah . Shofarot — the horn, traffic lights and \"tiredness kills, take a break\" signs : stopping to reassess whether we are on track. Yom Kippur was a month ago — what remains of it? Shabbat is a weekly \"mini Yom Kippur\" : a regular stop for reflection on the past week and building toward the next. And Shabbat lets us live in the present — not anxious about the week behind or the week ahead. Shabbat as the centre of the week (Maharal) We usually see Shabbat as the end of the week. The Kabbalists and the Maharal of Prague (16th century) see it as the centre . A three-dimensional object has six sides, but those six planks are meaningless until there is a centre around which they are built — an invisible centre that gives the structure its meaning. So too the six weekdays revolve around Shabbat: from Tuesday night we prepare toward Shabbat; from Sunday we descend from it. Shabbat gives meaning to the whole week. Why learn Shabbat in such detail — the three mitzvot of Marah Before Matan Torah, at Marah , Hashem gave Israel a few mitzvot to prepare them, including Parah Adumah , Dinim (civil law) and Shabbat (Sanhedrin 56b). The Maharal explains these represent three dimensions of Torah learning: Mitzvah Dimension Parah Adumah Philosophical depth — a mitzvah even Shlomo HaMelech could not fathom Dinim Analytical rigour — dissecting and adjudicating Shabbat Breadth — so many details that one must keep learning and learning Fourteen shiurim will not make anyone a master of Hilchot Shabbat — it is a lifetime's study. The goal is to give musagim (concepts): to know where there is an issue , so that one knows to ask. Rabbi Yonatan Eybeschutz (approx. 300 years ago): it is almost impossible to be Shomer Shabbat without regularly learning Hilchot Shabbat. Approach of the course: Rulings will follow mainly Chacham Ovadia Yosef zt\"l (whose yahrzeit had just passed), noting Ashkenazi practice and, for those who follow it, the Ben Ish Chai / Kabbalistic view where it differs. Part B — Friday Afternoon 1. Working on Friday afternoon The halacha: The Shulchan Aruch (OC 251) rules that from Mincha Ketana — roughly 2½ hours before sunset — one should stop working. One who continues אינו רואה סימן ברכה — sees no sign of blessing from that work. Chazal wanted us to prepare for Shabbat, not jump straight into it. Taken literally, that would mean leaving the office 3½ hours before Shabbat. In practice, four reasons make this halacha rarely applicable: Only arduous, recognisable melacha was included — plumbing, weaving, writing, field work. Buying and selling ( perakmatya ) is permitted until close to Shabbat — hence shops may stay open (though not so late that Jewish staff or customers end up travelling into Shabbat; 20–30 minutes before is generally fine). Only one's own work — an employee may keep working until close to Shabbat. Work for Shabbat itself is permitted — an electrician fixing something for Shabbat, dry-cleaning a garment or a haircut for Shabbat. Financial loss — if the work cannot wait (a client would be lost), it is permitted. Where it does apply: a self-employed sofer writing a Sefer Torah due in six months — real melacha, self-employed, not for Shabbat, no loss. Such a person should stop about 2½ hours before Shabbat each Friday. In all cases: don't just \"land\" into Shabbat — arrive with preparation and the right frame of mind. 2. Travelling on Friday The halacha (OC 249): based on the Gemara, one should not set out on a journey of more than roughly three parsaot (about 3½ hours) on Erev Shabbat. The reason is commonly misunderstood. The concern is not getting stranded — it is food : arriving somewhere just before Shabbat with nothing prepared, and so failing to enjoy Shabbat properly. If the destination knows you are coming and will provide food, you may travel up until Shabbat — e.g. a businessman flying home whose wife expects him. Where it could still apply: yeshiva students in Israel who would board a bus and arrive unannounced somewhere (say Jerusalem to Tzfat), relying on finding hospitality. The Magen Avraham (some 400 years ago) already writes that people no longer hesitate over this, since hosts always cook extra. Caution: never cut it so fine that you are tempted to drive \"another ten minutes\" into Shabbat. If caught, one would have to stop on the hard shoulder for 25 hours. Leave ample time. Question from the floor: a participant asked about relying on facilities at the destination (e.g. a place next door being open for food). The Rabbi confirmed: the same principle — provided food will be available, travel is fine. 3. A large meal on Friday The halacha (OC 249): Chazal forbade sitting down to a meal one is not accustomed to during the week — the entire Friday — to preserve one's appetite and keep Shabbat distinct. Applies only to a meal with bread (washing) . Fine food without a bread meal is permitted the whole day. For someone who eats out regularly during the week, a restaurant meal is not special — permitted. A seudat mitzvah — e.g. a brit milah — is permitted the whole day, even if lavish. From about three hours before sunset , especially in winter, one should avoid eating so much that the Shabbat meal is spoiled. (The Rabbi added his own concern — not upsetting a spouse who has worked hard on the Shabbat food!) Part C — Lighting Shabbat Candles (Hadlakat Nerot) Status and reward Some hold lighting candles is rooted in the Torah or the Nevi'im; at the very least it is a rabbinic mitzvah expressing honour for Shabbat. The Gemara (Shabbat 23b) promises: הָרָגִיל בְּנֵר הָוְיָין לֵיהּ בָּנִים תַּלְמִידֵי חֲכָמִים — One who is careful with lighting will have children who are Torah scholars. Candles bring light into the world, as Torah does — כִּי נֵר מִצְוָה וְתוֹרָה אוֹר (Mishlei 6:23). Hence women's custom to daven for their family after lighting — an auspicious time. It is not only a women's mitzvah The mitzvah is equally incumbent on men and women . Women take precedence by tradition (traditionally being at home), but if a man sees his wife is delayed — a minute or two before Shabbat and she is still getting ready — he should light himself rather than let Shabbat be desecrated. Two reasons Oneg Shabbat — enjoying Shabbat: food that can be seen is enjoyed more. Kavod Shabbat — honour of Shabbat: a lit home, so no one stumbles in the dark. One candle is sufficient. The old custom is two (corresponding to Zachor and Shamor). If only one is at hand, don't delay looking for a second. Two separate obligations There is (1) a personal obligation and (2) an obligation that each home lived in on Shabbat be lit . Usually one lighting covers both. But: Husband away, wife at home: her lighting covers the home and his personal duty — but where he is staying must also be lit , so he lights there. Wife away, husband at home: he lights at home; she lights where she is. Eating out on Friday night, sleeping at home A common question for young couples eating at their parents: Best: light at home at the proper time, ensuring the candles are still burning when you return , so you benefit from them. (Lighting and immediately leaving gives no benefit — the bracha would be in vain.) This is not always practical — some are uneasy leaving candles alight, and leaving mid-afternoon is too early to light. (If you leave after nightfall, having benefited during the first part of Shabbat, that also suffices.) Alternatively: light where you are eating — arriving before Shabbat — preferably in another room (e.g. the kitchen). For Sephardim , once the room is already lit by the hosts, a second lighting there cannot be made with a bracha , since it adds nothing. Forgot to light? If it is still Bein HaShemashot (twilight — roughly from sunset until nightfall, e.g. 6:00–6:50 pm that week), one may explicitly ask a non-Jew to switch on the lights — the principle being that during twilight one may ask a non-Jew for something needed for Shabbat or for a mitzvah. After nightfall it is too late. The fine ( knas ): a woman who forgot must add an extra candle every week for life. (Note: large numbers of candles in many homes are a custom of one per child — not penalties!) Today, since we have electricity , the poskim rule the fine generally does not apply — the reason for it was a Shabbat spent in darkness. It applies only if she forgot the candles and the house was left completely dark. The bracha — before or after lighting? The Rabbi polled the room: most light first, then say the bracha. Shulchan Aruch: the bracha comes first — as with every mitzvah ( over la'asiyatan ), e.g. lulav: say the bracha before taking the four species. Rema (Ashkenazim): light first, cover the eyes , say the bracha, then look — because there is an opinion that the bracha constitutes accepting Shabbat , after which one could no longer light. Covering the eyes means one has not yet benefited , so the bracha is still quasi-before the mitzvah. Many Sephardim have long done the same — an old custom recorded by the Chida , not a recent London borrowing. Practical ruling: keep your family tradition (follow your mother's custom). With no established custom (e.g. a ba'al teshuva or convert), say the bracha first, then light . Doing melacha after lighting You light, then realise you forgot to switch off a light: Ashkenazim: lighting is accepting Shabbat — no more melacha, unless one stipulates ( tenai ) at lighting that one does not intend to accept Shabbat yet. Sephardim: strictly, lighting (even with the bracha) is not accepting Shabbat — melacha is permitted until Shabbat. Best for all: make the stipulation — especially when lighting before driving to a host. Summary Zachor and Shamor are equally essential: the beauty of Shabbat and its details — the painting and its brushstrokes. Shabbat affirms Creation , Divine Providence (the Exodus) and our identity as human beings, not human doings ; it is the centre that gives the week meaning (Maharal). Friday work: in practice almost always permitted until close to Shabbat (simple work, employees, work for Shabbat, financial loss). Friday travel: permitted as long as you arrive with time to spare and food is expected. Friday meals: no unaccustomed bread meal all day — except a seudat mitzvah. Candles: an equal obligation on men and women; two obligations (person and home); options when eating out; the fine largely inapplicable today with electricity; keep your custom on the bracha, otherwise bracha first; for all, stipulate if you intend to continue working after lighting. Coming Up Shiur 2 — 23 October: Wine, Beer, Whisky and Spices Kiddush — its importance, how much to drink, Kiddush on challah The Shabbat meals — their significance; dieting on Shabbat Havdala — its various aspects B'Ezrat Hashem","LvmyaUWIfiw":"Dayan Daniel Kada · Thursday Night Halacha · Shiur 3 of 7 Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 671:7–8 and 672:1 Review and Tonight's Plan Shiur 1 (Siman 670): what is not allowed on Hanukah, and the historical aspects of the festival. Shiur 2 (Siman 671): where to place the menorah, its height (minimum and maximum), and what to do if you live above twenty amot. Tonight, in the order of the Shulḥan Arukh: 1. Lighting in the synagogue (671:7, second half). 2. A house with two entrances to the public domain (671:8). 3. The earliest and correct time to light (672:1). 4. The latest time to light, which moved to next week. Part One: The Synagogue Menorah (671:7) The Text ובבית הכנסת מניחו בכותל דרומי (the Rema corrects: בדרום , in the southern part), כמו שהיה המנורה בדרום, ומדליקין ממזרח למערב. ומדליקין ומברכין בבית הכנסת משום פרסומי ניסא. In the synagogue it is placed in the south, as the Menorah stood in the south of the Temple, and one lights from east to west. And one lights and says the berachah in the synagogue, because of publicising the miracle. Picture your synagogue: face the east, toward the Ark. The menorah stands on the south side, and the lights are lit from east to west , from the Ark end toward the back, as in the Beit HaMikdash. (Which direction to light at home comes later.) The Bi'ur Halacha's question: why does the Shulḥan Arukh first tell us where to place it, and only then that we light and bless? Surely the main law should come first. Answer: this seif is about placement . Having described placement in the home (opposite the mezuzah, the height and so on), it contrasts the synagogue, where the doorway is irrelevant and the menorah simply goes in the south. Only then does it add that we light and bless there. The Rema's Additions Lighting in the synagogue does not fulfil your own obligation. The ḥazzan or rabbi who lights, or whoever is honoured, must light again at home . The custom is to light between Minḥah and Arvit. On Friday , some light before Minḥah , out of concern that after Minḥah it may already be Shabbat. During the week , if the congregation is in a hurry (\"apparently even in those days people were in a rush to pray\"), the ḥazzan may light the first light and start Arvit while the shamash finishes lighting the rest . The Origins of the Custom The Gemara speaks only of the household's menorah, lit by the head of the household. Nowhere in the Talmud, nor among the Geonim, is a synagogue menorah mentioned. Source Who / where / when Notes Sefer Ha'Ittur R. Yitzḥak ben Abba Mari, Marseille , late 12th century The first mention known to the Rabbi, describing it as an established custom The Me'iri Provence, a few years later Perhaps the first among Sephardim to mention it The Rif and the Rambam No mention at all. \"We should be very clear on that\" So the custom takes hold in the late 1100s and 1200s , about 800 years ago. Five Reasons for the Custom The Rabbi found five different reasons in the Rishonim. Remember them, because they have practical ramifications. # Reason Source 1 פרסומי ניסא : to publicise the miracle further, as an elaboration of the home mitzvah. The main reason, given by most Rishonim The Me'iri, the Ittur and others 2 Persecution : there was a time when Jews were afraid to light even at home, even on the table, lest it identify them as Jews, and many did not light at all. At the very least, one menorah was lit in the synagogue The Rivash (R. Yitzḥak bar Sheshet), Barcelona, 14th century 3 מקדש מעט : the synagogue is a small Temple, and the Temple had a Menorah Sefer HaMikdash , 13th century 4 The rabbi or ḥazzan lived in the synagogue. He lit for his family there, which served the whole congregation, and the custom grew from that The Shibbolei HaLeket , Rome 5 For guests and travellers with nowhere proper to light The Beit Yosef himself A Unique Feature of Hanukah Connected to reason 2: later we will learn that someone unable to light can say a berachah on seeing a menorah . The Rabbi could think of no other mitzvah where, if you cannot perform it, you bless on seeing someone else perform it. This exists only on Hanukah, because of pirsumei nisa . A Berachah on a Custom? The custom is to light, which is fine. But the Shulḥan Arukh rules that we say a berachah . The Mishnah Berurah (Seif Katan 44): just as we bless on Hallel of Rosh Ḥodesh , which is only a custom, so we bless on this custom. The Problem for Sephardim The Gemara obliges Hallel on Pesaḥ, Sukkot and other days. Rosh Ḥodesh is not one of them. Hallel on Rosh Ḥodesh is a minhag . The Rambam's principle: you never say a berachah on a custom. You cannot say אשר קדשנו במצוותיו וצונו , \"who commanded us,\" about something Hashem did not command. The Shulḥan Arukh follows the Rambam, and the Middle Eastern Sephardim (Syrians, Iraqis and others) do not bless on Hallel of Rosh Ḥodesh . Ashkenazim do bless (as the Mishnah Berurah says), as did many Moroccan communities and the Spanish & Portuguese . A personal aside: when the Rabbi joined the S&P some eleven or twelve years earlier, he faced exactly this. His own custom is not to bless on Rosh Ḥodesh Hallel, yet the S&P custom is to bless, and he often leads Hallel. The Ḥida has a responsum on a Sephardi leading Hallel in an Ashkenazi synagogue, and rules that he may, for various reasons. It is a classic Ḥida responsum: although Sephardi himself, the Ḥida travelled the world and spent time in many Ashkenazi communities. \"A rabbi's responsa very often reflect the social milieu he was in.\" So the question stands: if Sephardim do not bless on Rosh Ḥodesh Hallel, how can the Shulḥan Arukh rule that we bless on the synagogue menorah, which is clearly a custom? The Classic Minhag Without a Berachah: The Aravah The Rabbi asked the class: what is the classic once-a-year minhag on which we pointedly do not say a berachah? The aravah on Hoshana Rabbah. The Gemara's entire description is four words: חביט חביט ולא בריך , he beat it, he beat it, and did not bless . The Gemara concludes that taking the aravah by itself is a minhag , an ancient one, and therefore no berachah . \"People don't know what they're saying when they're banging this aravah on the floor.\" This is the Rambam's source for no berachah on a minhag. Three Answers 1. The Rivash (about 600 years ago): distinguish small customs from great customs . The aravah is a small thing; publicising the miracle is a great and important custom, which justifies a berachah, just as Hallel on Rosh Ḥodesh is a great custom worth blessing on. But that works only for those who bless on Rosh Ḥodesh Hallel. It does not help Sephardim. 2. The Vilna Gaon (also discussed by the Ḥacham Tzvi and many commentators): there is another time in the year when the congregation publicly blesses on a custom. The Rabbi asked the class when. Hallel on Seder night in the synagogue , said with a berachah at the end of Arvit on both nights of Pesaḥ (not the Hallel in the Haggadah, which is an enactment of the Sages in the Mishnah). There is a mention in the Yerushalmi, but essentially it is a custom , and Sephardim bless on it. Why? Because it publicises the miracle. Pirsumei nisa is so prominent that it justifies a berachah even on a custom. There are other cases too, such as a woman immersing out of custom only in the first weeks after childbirth, where the poskim debate whether to bless. But the general principle remains: anything not Talmudic, a later development, gets no berachah. Hanukah is the exception, because of pirsumei nisa. 3. Ḥacham Ovadia: we refuse a berachah on a minhag when the minhag is an entirely new thing . Beating the aravah, or Hallel on Rosh Ḥodesh, is new; you cannot say וצונו . But Hanukah lighting is already a mitzvah : Hashem commands you to light at home tonight. Extending it to the synagogue is an embellishment and continuation of a real commandment, so וצונו fits. Even so, the Rabbi is \"pretty confident\" that the Rambam would say not to bless here. Why Not \"ספק ברכות להקל\"? Usually, when there is doubt about a berachah, we are lenient and do not say it , even in many places where the Shulḥan Arukh rules to bless. So why not here? Because when an established custom follows one side of a dispute, we bless. We rely on this every morning: הנותן ליעף כח , \"who gives strength to the weary,\" appears nowhere in the two Talmudic discussions of the morning blessings. The Shulḥan Arukh itself rules that this custom is incorrect and that one should not say the berachah. Yet almost all Sephardim say it, because the Arizal endorsed it , and once it is an established practice, safek berachot lehakel does not apply. A participant's correction: \"Not everybody.\" The S&P siddur omits it. The Rabbi had checked the night before: \"It's not there.\" It is the only siddur he has found that leaves it out, following the Shulḥan Arukh. (He wondered what the Italian rite does.) Applied to the synagogue menorah: every synagogue today lights, so it is an established practice among Israel, and we bless. Public Menorahs: Weddings, Dinners, Shopping Centres Everyone has lit at home, but at a wedding, bar mitzvah or dinner on Hanukah someone suggests lighting a menorah for the atmosphere. May one say a berachah? A participant suggested: no, because the custom was established only for the synagogue. Test it against the five reasons: Reason Applies in a hotel or public space? 1. Pirsumei nisa Arguably yes 2. We can't light at home No: we already light at home and in the synagogue 3. Mikdash me'at No: it is not a synagogue 4. The rabbi lives there No 5. Guests have nowhere to light No Only pirsumei nisa remains. So the majority of poskim rule: no berachah when lighting in a public place. Ḥacham Ovadia takes a more lenient line: יש להם על מה שיסמוכו , those who bless have something to rely on, because pirsumei nisa is unique to Hanukah, and מצוות נר חנוכה חביבה היא עד מאוד . \"For reasons I don't fully understand,\" the Rabbi added. Ḥacham Ovadia adds that it is best to hold Arvit there , which gives the place something of a synagogue's status. \"Think about the incredible work which Chabad do,\" spreading menorahs to the most far-flung places. Every year another London neighbourhood gets one; the previous year Westfield shopping centre had a huge menorah on its top floor for the first time. \"This is incredible, beautiful. But the reality is, according to the vast majority of poskim, it's not correct to say a berachah on these menorahs,\" since it is neither your home nor a synagogue, and blessing in the synagogue is already enough. That said, the Rabbi has seen Ḥacham Yitzḥak Yosef light at a Hanukah dinner and say the berachah. \"People do do this nowadays... but we should be aware it's very shaky, the foundations.\" Practical Laws of the Synagogue Menorah A minyan is required The berachah is said because of pirsum ha-nes , which requires ten people . Without a minyan, it is not lit with a berachah. Who counts? Unanimously, women , and children of ḥinuch age . Pirsum ha-nes is modelled on קידוש השם : - Normally one does not give up one's life except for the three cardinal sins. But if someone demands, in front of ten , that you transgress specifically to make you renounce your faith (e.g. \"eat this non-kosher food in front of your community, to show you Jews\"), you must give up your life. - For that \"ten,\" women certainly count , and possibly children of ḥinuch age. Men and women are equal there, and men and women are equally obligated in Hanukah lighting. - So nine men and one woman are enough to light with a berachah (for the menorah, not for Arvit). Friday afternoon: lighting before the minyan arrives A very common case: Shabbat is about to come in, only five people are present, and latecomers will make ten within minutes. May you light now? Some poskim say you must wait. But a commentary on the page brings quite convincing proofs that you may light, provided you are confident that ten people will have benefited from the menorah by the end of the service. Ḥazon Ovadia follows this, and \"we often have to rely on this on Friday nights on Hanukah.\" How long must it burn? At home the lights must burn thirty minutes (details to come). In the synagogue: - Arvit takes ten or fifteen minutes at most; then everyone goes home to light. - Leaving a flame burning in an empty building is a safety hazard , so most synagogues extinguish it after Arvit . - That is fine. The thirty minutes belongs to the home , so passers-by see it. In the synagogue the public lighting itself is the mitzvah , so even a few minutes is enough. Lighting again at home, and Sheheḥeyanu The one who lit in the synagogue must light again at home, even if home alone , and says the berachot again. But on the first night : At home... Berachot Lighting for a wife or children All three , including Sheheḥeyanu , for them Alone The first two only. No Sheheḥeyanu Why? Sheheḥeyanu is on the festival and the mitzvah of lighting. Some argue the home lighting deserves its own Sheheḥeyanu, but the general understanding is that the synagogue lighting, although a custom rather than an obligation, was still the mitzvah of lighting the menorah with a berachah, so its Sheheḥeyanu counts. When the first night falls on Friday: everyone lights at home before coming to synagogue and has already said Sheheḥeyanu, so the synagogue lighting omits Sheheḥeyanu . The timing on Friday, and the \"contradiction\" problem The Rema writes that some light before Minḥah, but his own custom was between Minḥah and Arvit . A newer idea (no mention of it until about 300–400 years ago; the Rabbi believes the Pri Megadim is first): on Friday of Hanukah, pray Minḥah before lighting . Otherwise you have lit for the coming night and then pray the previous day's Minḥah, which is תרתי דסתרי , a self-contradiction. - The Ben Ish Ḥai was sharp about this, and R. Mordechai Eliyahu went so far as to say it is better to pray Minḥah alone at home than with a minyan after lighting. - The Rabbi: \"That's a very extreme shitah, and the halacha, we don't hold that at all.\" - In any case, the whole discussion concerns individuals . For the congregation , lighting between Minḥah and Arvit, or before Minḥah, is entirely acceptable, and that is the custom in most places. The English winter makes this concrete. With Shabbat coming in at about 3:30 in December, a synagogue would need to start at 3:10 to pray Minḥah, light and then begin Kabbalat Shabbat. Robert pointed out that there may not be enough time at all: you would need to light at home early enough, reach the synagogue, pray Minḥah and still light before Shabbat. The Rabbi agreed: \"That's a really good point.\" In more equatorial places the hours are less extreme, but \"you just need a couple of guys not to show up on time and it's all going to fall apart.\" In England, with roughly a half-hour window, it is almost impossible. Sharing the lighting: Sephardi and Ashkenazi customs The Rema allowed the shamash to finish lighting so Arvit can start. The Mishnah Berurah permits it because of טירחא דציבורא , the burden on the congregation, though normally the one who starts lighting should finish. The Sephardi custom is different: in many families everyone of ḥinuch age, women and children included, takes a turn lighting the one family menorah. That developed precisely because there is only one menorah. Ashkenazim, each with their own menorah, never had that situation, so for them, \"you lit your menorah, you finish it.\" For Sephardim, sharing the mitzvah is normal, provided those lighting are Jewish and children old enough to understand what is happening. Part Two: A House With Two Entrances (671:8) בית שיש לו שני פתחים משני רוחות, צריך להדליק בשתיהן מפני החשד. אבל אם שניהם ברוח אחת, די לו באחד. A house with two entrances on two sides must light at both, because of suspicion. If both are on the same side, one is enough. The concern (חשד): people passing on the other side will assume you did not light. Most houses do not face two public domains: the back is a garden. But on a very busy corner it can happen. The Rema: a berachah is said only on the first lighting. And nowadays, when we light indoors where no one sees, the concern does not apply . Today: if you light indoors, there is nothing to discuss. But if you light at a window facing a public domain , and the house has another window facing another busy public domain , then really you should light two menorahs : the berachah on the main one, the second only because of suspicion. Here a child's menorah could be used. Even though Sephardim do not normally light two menorahs, here it would be appropriate. \"It's not really practical\": such houses are rare, and it applies only to lighting that can be seen from outside. Part Three: The Earliest Time to Light (672:1) אין מדליקין נר חנוכה קודם שתשקע החמה, אלא עם סוף שקיעתה, לא מאחרים ולא מקדימים. One does not light before sunset, but with the end of sunset: not later and not earlier. The seif continues: if you are very busy and will not be able to light later, you may light from פלג המנחה , provided there is enough oil to last until half an hour after nightfall . \"Of course we rely on that every Friday of Hanukah,\" since we cannot light at sunset once Shabbat has begun. Which other circumstances allow this comes next week. What does \"end of sunset\" mean? The Gemara says משתשקע החמה , \"from when the sun sets.\" Does that mean: - when the sun begins to set , what we call שקיעה (that evening about 4:03 ), or - when the sun has fully set , nightfall (that evening about 4:35–4:40 )? The Rambam doesn't settle it: he writes עם שקיעתה , \"with its setting.\" Opinion Rishonim Practice When the sun has set = nightfall Rabbeinu Tam, the Rosh, the Mordechai, the Tur Sephardim : at nightfall When the sun begins to set = sunset The Rashba, the Ran Many Ashkenazim , notably the Vilna Gaon : right after sunset The Shulḥan Arukh's one-word addition The Shulḥan Arukh's line is an exact quotation from the Rambam, except for one word: סוף . The Rambam wrote \"with its setting\"; the Shulḥan Arukh wrote \"with the end of its setting,\" and so Sephardim light at nightfall. Those who follow the Gra light a minute or two after sunset (\"4:04, 4:05\"). Robert asked: if the Shulḥan Arukh meant nightfall, why not just say so? The Rabbi: because it is preserving the words of the Rambam and of the Gemara . Robert's second question: do the Gra's followers then light before Arvit? Yes. Those who light at sunset (many in Israel and in Jerusalem do) pray Arvit later. How Arvit interacts with lighting for those who light at nightfall comes next week. And a third: for those who light at sunset, must the oil last half an hour after sunset? Strictly by the Gaon, yes, half an hour after sunset. But in practice they add more oil and keep it burning until half an hour after nightfall, to be yotzei according to all . The Bi'ur Halacha's proof about the Rambam The main Hanukah sugya is in the second chapter of Shabbat, במה מדליקין (said on Friday nights), because it discusses the wicks and oils for Shabbat, and Hanukah comes up in that context. There the Gemara says לא מקדימין ולא מאחרין , which most read as referring to Shabbat candles, but the Rambam applies it to Hanukah too. The Bi'ur Halacha: the warning \"not earlier and not later\" makes perfect sense if the time is sunset . A moment earlier is daytime, and lighting by day is pointless; too late, and people are no longer passing. But if the time is nightfall , why the emphasis? A few minutes before nightfall it is already בין השמשות , already dark. So the Rambam must have meant sunset. Important: Don't Wait for the Calendar's \"Shabbat Ends\" Time People often reason: we follow the Shulḥan Arukh, so we light at nightfall, and nightfall is the time Shabbat goes out on the calendar. That's wrong, for several reasons: Published times include תוספת שבת , the extra time added to Shabbat. Even apart from that , there are many ways to calculate nightfall, and the calendars adopt one of the strictest , because Shabbat is d'oraita. In essence there is probably a good five to ten minutes, or more, during which it is already nightfall. (\"I say this carefully, and this should never be misused.\") The Shulḥan Arukh says \"with the end of sunset.\" You don't literally have to wait for full nightfall. Some opinions count from sunset , so it is better to err slightly earlier than later. The worked example (that evening) Time Event 4:03 Sunset. Followers of the Gra light from 4:04–4:05 ≈ 4:35 Recommended lighting time for Sephardim ≈ 4:40 Actual nightfall 4:54 Calendar time for the end of Shabbat (with tosefet and strict reckoning) Lighting at about 4:35 is still within half an hour of sunset , so you satisfy those who count from sunset, while it is already סוף שקיעה , the end of sunset. \"Better to bring it slightly basically just as nightfall... not wait until it's properly nightfall.\" Tools: the MyZmanim website shows the different calculations. The same applies to fast days: the times published for London are strict. For rabbinic matters one may rely on 13.5 degrees , which brings most rabbinic fasts out 10–15 minutes earlier . For Hanukah, too, it is good to rely on that and light within the half hour rather than delaying. Summary The synagogue menorah stands in the south , lit east to west , like the Temple Menorah, between Minḥah and Arvit. It does not fulfil your home obligation. It is a custom , unknown in the Talmud, Geonim, Rif or Rambam; first recorded in Sefer Ha'Ittur (Marseille, c. 1200) . Five reasons: pirsumei nisa (the main one), persecution (Rivash), mikdash me'at (Sefer HaMikdash), the rabbi's home (Shibbolei HaLeket), travellers (Beit Yosef). A berachah on a custom is normally ruled out (the Rambam; the aravah). It is justified here because of pirsumei nisa : the Rivash's \"great custom,\" the Gra's parallel of Seder-night Hallel in shul , and Ḥacham Ovadia's \" extension of a real mitzvah .\" An established custom overrides safek berachot lehakel (compare הנותן ליעף כח ). Public menorahs (weddings, dinners, shopping centres): the majority of poskim say no berachah . Ḥacham Ovadia is lenient, preferably with Arvit held there. Ten are needed for the berachah; women and children of ḥinuch age count . On Friday you may light relying on ten arriving. It may be put out after Arvit . First night: after lighting in shul, at home alone omit Sheheḥeyanu ; for family, say all three. When the first night is a Friday, the synagogue omits Sheheḥeyanu. Friday timing: for the congregation, between Minḥah and Arvit (or before Minḥah) is fine. The \"pray Minḥah first\" view is extreme and not the halacha. Two entrances on two public sides: light at both because of suspicion, but not relevant when lighting indoors. The time to light: Sephardim light at the end of sunset (the Shulḥan Arukh's added word סוף ); followers of the Gra light at sunset. Don't wait for the calendar's Shabbat-ends time. Light around nightfall, slightly early, within half an hour of sunset. Coming Up Next week: 672:2 and 673 . The latest time to light; what to do if the time has passed; whether spouses should wait for each other; and which oils and wicks may be used.","MxZQlTt56Uw":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (Rambam), Chelek Aleph — Rabbi Chaim Makbili's Hebrew edition / Sefaria Chapters: I:18 — the three terms for drawing near: קרב, נגע, נגש ; I:19 — the term מלא (to fill) Topic: Spatial versus intellectual nearness; why God can be neither near nor far in space; the verses we say every day (קרוב ה' לכל קוראיו) and what they really mean; God \"touching\"; why the Rambam leaves out דבק ; the three meanings of מלא ; מלא כל הארץ כבודו ; and why our prayers are better received at the Kotel Schedule note: First Moreh shiur after Tishrei. The Tuesday Moreh shiur now continues every week through the winter , until further notice. No Thursday shiur this week ; the other shiurim resume next week. Chapter 20 was not reached and opens next week. Recap — Chapter 17 Last time we reviewed Chapters 1–16 and then learned Chapter 17 , where the Rambam continued the theme of צור (the source of all existence). He taught that profound theological and scientific matters must sometimes be expressed through metaphor , because revealing them openly can be harmful. Using Aristotle, he set out the three building blocks of all existence: חומר, צורה and העדר (matter, form and privation). By the nature of matter, when one deficiency is filled another emerges. He applied this to people: we are naturally driven to seek more, materially, intellectually and spiritually. So the Rambam must be careful not to reveal too much at once and \"drown\" us. Chapter 17 was a digression , prompted by צור in Chapter 16. In Chapter 18 the Rambam returns to explaining verbs , and continues doing so for the next few chapters. Chapter 18 — קרב, נגע, נגש The Three Verbs Verb Plain meaning קרב To draw near נגע To touch נגש To approach, draw close שלושת המונחים הללו, כלומר קריבה, נגיעה, נגישה, נאמרים לעתים במשמעות של מגע וקירבה במקום, ולעתים במשמעות של התקשרות הידיעה בדבר הנודע, והרי זה כאילו דומה לקירבת גוף אל גוף. All three verbs carry two meanings : Spatial — physical nearness or touch. Intellectual — the mind coming close to, or \"touching\", something it knows. It is like physical nearness, but nothing moves in space. What Is the Difference Between Them? (Abarbanel) Abarbanel ranks them by degree of closeness: Degree Verb Meaning 1 קרב I was far, and I draw near 2 נגש I draw even nearer 3 נגע I touch: the closest of all Then why does the Rambam list them as קרב, נגע, נגש, rather than in ascending order? Abarbanel's answer: קרב and נגע are used of anything , human or not, animate or inanimate. נגש is used only of a person approaching. Because they are different kinds of verb, the Rambam groups them this way. (Keep this in mind; it matters later in the chapter.) Meaning 1 — Spatial Nearness הנה המשמעות הראשונה של קריבה, שהיא קריבה במקום: קרב: ויהי כאשר קרב אל המחנה וירא את העגל ומחלת (Ki Tisa) Moshe has been away for forty days; he comes near the camp, sees them dancing and breaks the luchot. ופרעה הקריב Pharaoh, after pursuing Bnei Yisrael for three days, draws near. נגע — מגע גוף בגוף , body touching body: ותגע לרגליו The mysterious episode at the start of Shemot: Moshe is in some danger, Tzipporah circumcises their son and makes the blood touch his feet. Whether these are Moshe's feet or the child's is a machloket Rishonim ; either way, a physical touch. ויגע על פי (Yeshayahu) This is a prophecy, and the Rambam holds that all prophecy comes in a vision or dream , as we will learn. But within the vision there was a physical touch. It did not materially happen, but in the vision it is spatial touching. נגש — התקדמות אדם אחד אל אדם אחר , one person approaching another: ויגש אליו יהודה ויאמר בי אדני The opening of Vayigash: Yehudah comes very close to Yosef to plead for Binyamin. Meaning 2 — Intellectual Nearness והמשמעות השנייה של שלושת המונחים הללו היא קישור בידיעה, מגע של השגה, לא מגע של מקום. Contact of apprehension, not contact of place. The Rambam begins this time with נגע (the Rabbi isn't sure why): נגע: כי נגע אל השמים משפטה (Yirmiyahu, of Bavel) Bavel has not touched the sky. It means their conduct has come right up before Me ; it must be dealt with. קרב: והדבר אשר יקשה מכם תקריבון אלי ושמעתיו (Devarim, Moshe retelling the court system Yitro advised) The hardest cases come to Moshe. This does not mean physically carrying the case papers to him. It means כאילו נאמר: תודיעוני , inform me : bring it to my attention so I can consider it. הרי ששימש בהוראת ידיעת דבר מדבר. נגש: ויגש אברהם ויאמר (Vayera, Sedom) Avraham did not physically move while speaking to Hashem. היה זה בהתגלות ובמראית הנבואה — he drew near prophetically , developing himself intellectually to speak with Hashem. And at the end of the episode, ואברהם שב למקומו : the prophecy ended. (We will see this verse again.) יען כי נגש העם הזה בפיו ובשפתיו כבדוני, ולבו רחק ממני (Yeshayahu) Yeshayahu's famous complaint: the people draw near with their lips , but their hearts are far. When we pray, we do not come physically near to Hashem; we try to draw near intellectually. These people do it only with their lips. With God — Only the Second Meaning אם כן, כל לשון קריבה או נגישה שתמצא בספרי הנבואה בין השם יתברך לבין הנבראים — כולם מהמשמעות האחרונה הזו. כי השם יתברך אינו גוף. (As we will prove philosophically later.) ולכן הוא יתעלה אינו נוגע בדבר ולא מתקרב לדבר, ושום דבר אינו מתקרב אליו ואינו נוגע בו יתעלה. כי כשאין גשמיות אין מקום, ומתבטלים כל קירבה או מגע או ריחוק או קשירות או היפרדות או השקה או סמיכות. Where there is no body there is no place. So every word for nearness, contact, distance, conjunction, separation, touching or proximity means nothing in space when used between God and His creatures. Whenever these verbs describe us and Hashem, they mean intellectual positioning: coming closer, or moving further away, in understanding. Why the Rambam leaves out נגיעה here (Abarbanel) Notice his wording: כל לשון קריבה או נגישה . He omits נגיעה , though he uses it elsewhere in the chapter. Abarbanel: because you will never find נגע used of a human being towards Hashem. (You may find it the other way round; see below.) נגע is the closest degree, and the Torah says כי לא תוכל לראות את פני : there is a limit to how close we can come to Hashem, even intellectually. Even in prophecy there is no complete union. So the word for full contact is never used of man reaching God. This is why, for example, Yaakov's ladder says: והנה סלם מצב ארצה וראשו מגיע השמימה Its top reaches heaven , not God. As we have seen twice already, the ladder is a metaphor for prophetic ascent : the climb reaches heaven intellectually, but never \"touches\" Hashem. Verses You May Have Been Misreading All Your Life ואל יסבור שמה שנאמר: קרוב ה' לכל קוראיו... או: קרבת אלהים יחפצון... או: ואני קרבת אלהים לי טוב — יגרום לך ספק או בלבול. כי כל אלה קרבת ידיעה, כלומר השגה של ידע, לא קרבת מקום. 1. קרוב ה' לכל קוראיו, לכל אשר יקראהו באמת We say this in Ashrei three times a day . Does it mean Hashem moves closer to us? Of course not. This is the theme of השגחה (providence), which the Rambam develops in Chapter 21, throughout Section 2 and especially later in the book. The degree of Hashem's providence over a person matches how far that person places themselves under it . The Rabbi's analogy: a beam of light. Think of the opening of the Mr Bean films, where he falls into a beam of light. When you step into the beam, you are in the light. Seek Hashem in truth and you put yourself under the beam: you feel the השגחה. No spatial movement is involved. So tomorrow morning in Ashrei: קרוב ה' means that if we put ourselves in Hashem's light, we come under His השגחה. 2. קרבת אלהים יחפצון Read the verse in context (Yeshayahu 58): ואותי יום יום ידרשון, ודעת דרכי יחפצון, כגוי אשר צדקה עשה ומשפט אלהיו לא עזב, ישאלוני משפטי צדק, קרבת אלהים יחפצון. The whole verse is intellectual: seek Me out, desire to know My ways, inquire into My just laws — and then, they will desire nearness to God . The nearness comes from understanding His Torah, His world and His universe. 3. ואני קרבת אלהים לי טוב The full verse (Tehillim 73): ואני קרבת אלהים לי טוב, שתי באדנ-י ה' מחסי, לספר כל מלאכותיך. What does David mean by nearness to God? The rest of the verse says: making Hashem my refuge (bitachon) and recounting all His works (the world He has made). Similar verses כי מי גוי גדול אשר לו אלהים קרבים אליו, כה' אלהינו בכל קראנו אליו (Va'etchanan) We are fortunate to have Hashem \"near\" us. The end of the verse explains: whenever we call to Him. It means we can have a relationship with Him, not that He comes physically closer. קרב אתה ושמע את כל אשר יאמר ה' אלהינו (said to Moshe before the Aseret HaDibrot) Draw near = prepare yourself intellectually to receive. ונגש משה לבדו אל ה', והם לא יגשו (Yitro / Mishpatim) Moshe alone may apprehend Hashem as far as a human being can ; the elders and the people cannot. The Rambam's Leeway — Rav Saadia's Approach As in Chapter 5 (the burning bush), the Rambam offers a fallback for those who find this hard: אלא אם תרצה להבין שמה שאמר על משה \"ונגש\" — שהכוונה היא שהוא יתקרב אל המקום בהר אשר נברא בו האור, כלומר כבוד ה' — יכול אתה. If you prefer to say that Moshe approached the place on the mountain where a created light appeared — a physical manifestation called כבוד ה' , created by God but not God Himself — you may. This is how Rav Saadia Gaon often reads such verses. It is not heresy, but it is not correct. אבל החזק בעיקר: שאין הבדל בין אם האדם במרכז הארץ או על גבי הגלגל התשיעי, לו היה אפשר — אינו רחוק מן השם פה ולא קרוב שם. אלא הקרבה אליו יתעלה היא בהשגתו, והריחוק ממנו הוא באי ידיעתו. Hold on to the main principle. Whether you are at the centre of the earth or on the ninth sphere (the highest heaven in their cosmology), even if that were possible, you are no nearer to Hashem there and no further from Him here. Nearness to Him is knowing Him ; distance from Him is not knowing Him . ומן הפינה הזאת יש הבדלים גדולים מאוד בין בני אדם בין הקרוב והרחוק. In that sense, people differ enormously in how near or far they are. The Rambam will explain the levels of apprehension later. Rav Kappach brings a verse that captures it perfectly: אם אסק שמים שם אתה, ואציעה שאול הנך (Tehillim 139) Whether I climb to the heavens or lie down in the grave, You are there. We are no nearer and no further either way. When God \"Touches\" So far: humans drawing near to Hashem. What about verses where Hashem touches something ? ה' הט שמיך ותרד, גע בהרים ויעשנו (Tehillim 144) Hashem, bend Your heavens and come down; touch the mountains and they will smoke. הכוונה היא: הבא ציווייך עליהם. Bring Your decree upon them, as it says of Iyov: וגע אל עצמו — כלומר שלח בו מכותיך. Iyov first: Iyov's family and possessions have been struck, and the Satan says, but You have not touched his own flesh : וגע אל עצמו . Meaning: bring Your decree, Your affliction, upon Iyov himself. So too גע בהרים ויעשנו : Hashem need only decide, need only will it, and the mountains smoke. \"Touching\", for God, is the coming of His decree upon something. No physical contact. An aside on the Satan: do not imagine a creature called the Satan. There is no Satan as a being in the Rambam's philosophy , and it makes no sense. Later in the Moreh the Rambam explains that שטן derives from שטה/סטה , to turn aside: a complete distancing, a turning away from Hashem. הבן זאת — Apply It Elsewhere וכן יש לבחון בכל מקום כפי עניינו את הנגיעה וכל מה שנגזר ממנה: לעתים הכוונה מגע גוף בגוף, ולעתים התקשרות ידיעה והשגת דבר מה, כאילו מי שמשיג דבר שהיה רחוק ממנו — התקרב אליו. הבן זאת. Judge each occurrence by its context. Why add \"הבן זאת\", understand this? The Efodi suggests the Rambam is pointing to two verses he wants us to read this way: 1. Har Sinai (Yitro): השמרו לכם עלות בהר ונגע בקצהו; כל הנגע בהר מות יומת The simple reading is physical. But it can also mean: whoever \"touches\" the mountain — whoever tries to go too high in apprehending Hashem at the revelation — will die , will be unable to bear it. 2. The Etz HaDaat (this week's parashah, Bereishit): ומפרי העץ אשר בתוך הגן אמר אלהים לא תאכלו ממנו ולא תגעו בו פן תמתון The simple story: don't touch it, or you will die. Or: don't come too close intellectually ; don't try to grasp too much knowledge too fast. Do that and you will go mad. The four who entered the Pardes (to be discussed in detail later in the Moreh): only Rabbi Akiva came out whole. The others went too far intellectually: one died, one went mad, and one lost his faith. That is \"touching and dying\": coming too close, intellectually. The Fourth Verb — Why Doesn't the Rambam Mention דבק? The Rabbi's question: can you think of a fourth verb that means both physical and intellectual closeness and is used of Hashem in the Torah — one you would have expected the Rambam to bring? It's in Sefer Devarim, and we say it many times on Yom Kippur: ואתם הדבקים בה' אלהיכם, חיים כלכם היום And in this week's parashah : ודבק באשתו והיו לבשר אחד Hashem creates Chava and says the man shall cleave to his wife and they become one flesh, through intimacy or through the child they create together. At least a physical attachment. And ואתם הדבקים בה' says you are stuck to Hashem, glued to Him. If anything, דבק is the strongest word of all (in modern Hebrew it's what glue does). So why does the Rambam never discuss it? Suggestions from the class: it is purely intellectual, so it couldn't be used of a human and Hashem; or it refers to the heart, which is separate from the intellect. The Rabbi: but then how would you explain you are stuck to Hashem ? The answer: the Rambam expects you to know the Gemara before you learn the Moreh. He deals with what is not already explicit in the Talmud, and the Gemara already asks this question (Ketubot 111b): \"ואתם הדבקים בה' אלהיכם\" — וכי אפשר לדבוקי בשכינה? והלא כבר נאמר: כי ה' אלהיך אש אכלה הוא! אלא: כל המשיא בתו לתלמיד חכם, והעושה פרקמטיא לתלמידי חכמים, והמהנה תלמידי חכמים מנכסיו — מעלה עליו הכתוב כאילו מדבק בשכינה. כיוצא בדבר אתה אומר: \"לאהבה את ה' אלהיך... ולדבקה בו\" — וכי אפשר לאדם לידבק בשכינה? אלא... (the same answer) How can one cleave to the Shechinah, which is a consuming fire? Rather: marry your daughter to a talmid chacham, do business with talmidei chachamim (help them earn a living), let them benefit from your property — and Scripture counts it as if you cleaved to the Shechinah. But why didn't the Gemara simply say it means intellectual cleaving? Because the verse says חיים כלכם היום — all of you. Not every Jew experiences that intimate intellectual closeness to Hashem. So the Gemara could not read it as intellectual positioning. It had to refer it to the manifestation of Hashem in this world — the closest thing we have: the chachamim , and before them the kohanim (hence the gifts to the kohanim), who represent Hashem in the world as far as anything can. All of you cleave to Hashem through your attachment to Moshe and the chachamim across the generations. This matches what the Rambam taught in this chapter about כבוד ה' : a created manifestation that one can approach. That is why דבק needed no chapter of its own. Chapter 18 — Summary The three verbs קרב, נגש and נגע can mean physically approaching, coming near or touching, or intellectual and spiritual closeness . Whenever they are used of Hashem, only the second meaning applies: closeness through prophecy, prayer, trust (bitachon), seeking refuge in Him — never physical proximity, because God has no body and therefore no place. נגע, full contact, is never used of a human reaching Hashem , because no one can see His face. The everyday verses — קרוב ה' לכל קוראיו , קרבת אלהים יחפצון , ואני קרבת אלהים לי טוב — all speak of nearness through knowledge, providence and trust. If that is too hard, you may read them with Rav Saadia as approaching a created glory, כבוד ה' ; that is acceptable but not the true meaning. When Hashem \"touches\" something, it means His decree coming upon it. And דבק is absent because the Gemara already explained it: cleaving to the chachamim, Hashem's representatives in the world. Chapter 19 — מלא (To Fill) Why Here? Makbili suggests the link is Yeshayahu 6 , the vision of Hashem on a high and exalted throne, ושוליו מלאים את ההיכל — His train filled the Heichal. In that same chapter: ויגע על פי — one of the seraphim touched Yeshayahu's mouth (a seraph, not Hashem, just as Abarbanel said). Chapter 18 dealt with נגיעה in a spiritual context, so Chapter 19 turns to מלא from the same vision, and Chapter 20 will take רם ונשא , also from that vision. The Three Meanings of מלא מלא — מונח רב משמעי. 1. One body filling another דוברי השפה משתמשים בו לגבי גוף הנמצא בתוך גוף וממלא אותו. ותמלא כדה ותעל (Chayei Sarah) — Rivkah fills her pitcher with water: two physical things, one filling the other. מלא העמר לאחד — an omer-full of manna for each person. A note on quotation: Makbili points out that this exact phrase is not in the Torah . The verses read מלא העמר ממנו and, a few verses later, שני העמר לאחד . The Rambam has merged them from memory . Rav Kappach notes many times that the Rambam quoted pesukim from memory — he did not always have a Chumash in front of him — so a word or letter is sometimes slightly different. We'll see another example next chapter ( מתוך העפר where the verse says מן העפר ). 2. The completion of a period of time ומשתמשים בו במובן של תום פרק זמן מסוים. וימלאו ימיה ללדת, והנה תומם בבטנה — Rivkah's days were \"full\": her nine months were complete. וימלאו לו ארבעים יום, כי כן ימלאו ימי החנטים — the forty days of Yaakov's embalming were completed. Nothing physical fills anything. Completing a span of time is filling it — in time rather than in space. 3. Perfection — attaining the height of excellence ומשתמשים בו במובן של שלמות במעלה והגעת תכליתה. ונפתלי שבע רצון ומלא ברכת ה' (read a few days ago on Simchat Torah) — Naftali is full of Hashem's blessing: not space, not time, but excellence . מלא אתם חכמת לב לעשות כל מלאכת חרש וחשב — Hashem filled Betzalel, Ohaliav and the other artisans with wisdom. וימלא את החכמה ואת התבונה ואת הדעת — Chiram, the craftsman of the Beit HaMikdash: intellectual filling, and a perfection. Meaning Sense Example 1 Body within body (space) ותמלא כדה 2 Completion of time וימלאו ימיה ללדת 3 Perfection and excellence ומלא ברכת ה' מלא כל הארץ כבודו ובמשמעות הזה נאמר: מלא כל הארץ כבודו — פירוש הדברים: כל הארץ מעידה על שלמותו, כלומר מורה עליה. קדוש קדוש קדוש ה' צבאות, מלא כל הארץ כבודו — said several times a day. (We have not yet defined כבוד ; for now take it simply as His glory.) It does not mean filling space (meaning 1), nor completing time (meaning 2). It means meaning 3 : the whole earth testifies to His perfection. This is why the Rambam writes in so many places that studying the universe brings a person to understand Hashem. The universe is not perfect in every part — physical things come into being and decay — but as a whole , its harmony, its symphony and its rhythm display Hashem's wisdom and perfection. A person who is \"full\" has all the qualities, and the universe is full of His. וכבוד ה' מלא את המשכן כיוצא בזה נאמר: וכבוד ה' מלא את המשכן (Pekudei) Once the Mishkan and all its vessels were complete, the glory of Hashem \"filled\" it. Not physically (He has no body), not in time (no time is involved), but meaning 3 : through the building of the Mishkan, Hashem's glory was made whole and manifest . Seeing the Mishkan functioning, the kohanim, the avodah, one could experience the divine there more than anywhere else . Why Are Our Prayers Better Received at the Kotel? This means there is no intrinsic holiness to a place. The Rabbi's question: why is the Kotel a place where our prayers are more readily accepted? \"Because the Beit HaMikdash was there, so Hashem is closer there\"? Based on Chapters 18 and 19 we should all be shouting: that cannot be. Hashem is everywhere; on the ninth sphere or anywhere on earth, He is equally near. The real reason: the Kotel is a place where כבוד ה' was manifest : וכבוד ה' מלא את המשכן . When the Mishkan and the Beit HaMikdash stood, one saw Hashem's excellence there — the beauty, the order, the people, the avodah. Because of that history, one experiences the divine more there. Being there, a person rises spiritually and comes closer to Hashem intellectually , comes more fully under His השגחה — and then his prayers are naturally more accepted. It is wrong to think: \"I'm at the Kotel now, so the same prayers I say everywhere automatically count for more.\" Your prayers are the same prayers. They are more accepted only if you tune in : because I am at the Kotel I experience Hashem more, I come under His השגחה more — and everything else flows from that. The Rambam's Conclusion — and His Leeway Again וכל לשון מלאה שתמצא מיוחסת להשם יתברך — היא מהמשמעות הזאת, לא שיש גוף שממלא מקום. Whenever מלא is used of Hashem, it means perfection and excellence — never, God forbid, a body filling a space. אלא אם תרצה להבין את כבוד ה' כאור הנברא, המכונה כבוד, והוא שמלא את המשכן — אין נזק בכך. Again the Rambam allows the fallback: if you are not ready, read כבוד ה' as a created light — a physical manifestation, not Hashem Himself — that filled the Mishkan, as Rav Saadia Gaon does. No harm; not heresy. But it is not the true meaning, and you should be working towards understanding that Hashem's excellence and perfection filled the Mishkan. What is unacceptable — \"off the charts\" — is thinking Hashem Himself filled the Mishkan. And for us: a person becomes \"full\" by contemplating Hashem through the universe, its structure, and the structure of things like the Mishkan. Chapter 19 — Summary מלא has three meanings: (1) filling something in space (water in a pitcher), (2) completing a period of time, and (3) perfection and excellence . Used of Hashem, it never means that He physically occupies space. מלא כל הארץ כבודו means the universe displays His wisdom and perfection, so that through it we come to know Him; וכבוד ה' מלא את המשכן means the Mishkan was a powerful setting in which the divine could be experienced and recognised — which is also why prayer is better received at the Kotel, not because Hashem is more there, but because we can be more there. Rav Saadia's reading (a created glory filling the Mishkan) is permitted but is not the main meaning. Coming Up Next week: Chapter 20 — the two terms רם and נשא (exalted, lifted up). A short chapter, about 15 minutes. Then a start on Chapter 21 — עבר (to pass: ויעבר ה' על פניו ). A huge and fundamental chapter , at least two shiurim, maybe more. There is a small chance the Rabbi cannot give next week's shiur; assume it is on at the normal time unless told otherwise. No Thursday shiur this week ; all shiurim resume next week. B'Ezrat Hashem","OUdMyzfcpjo":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 8 (ביטחון — Bitachon and Reliance) — Part 1 Edition: Feldheim This week: Defining Bitachon · The First Category (the prophets) · Too much and too little Bitachon Why This Chapter Matters Chapter 8 is the longest chapter in the entire sefer. There are 13 chapters in HaMaspik, and after Chapter 8 the chapters become much shorter. The length itself signals how important this topic was to Rabbi Avraham. This chapter will take us through to at least the end of the summer term. Where We've Been in HaMaspik Chapter Topic 1 The difference between a Chassid and a Tzaddik 2-4 Generosity, compassion, calmness 5-7 Continuing character traits 7 Humility (just completed) 8 Bitachon Defining Our Terms: Emunah vs. Bitachon Emunah Root: א-מ-נ (alef-mem-nun) → amen Meaning: firm, loyal, trustworthy Examples throughout Tanakh: ומצאת את לבבו נאמן לפניך — \"You found Avraham's heart faithful before You\" (we say this every morning in Shacharit) ותקעתיו יתד במקום נאמן — \"I will fix him as a peg in a firm place\" (Yeshayahu) ויהי אומן את הדסה — \"He was the guardian of Hadassah\" (Esther) — when you adopt or raise a child, there's a kind of loyalty and firmness Important: Translating emunah as \"faith\" is misleading. \"Faith\" connotes blind acceptance. Emunah means firmness in our beliefs about Hashem and Torah — based on understanding, not blindness. Bitachon Root: ב-ט-ח (bet-tet-chet) Meaning: secure, safe, confident — stronger than firmness The classic verse from the end of the Torah: וישכון ישראל בטח בדד עין יעקב \"Yisrael will dwell securely, alone, like Yaakov's spring\" (V'zot HaBerachah) In modern Hebrew, betach is everyday usage: \"Can I rely on you for this?\" — \"Betach!\" (Of course!) = 100%, no worries. Bitachon = confidence, security, very strong emunah The Relationship Bitachon is the next level up from Emunah. Without emunah, no bitachon is possible Emunah is the intellectual worked-out truth Bitachon is living that truth — applying it day-to-day You can have emunah without bitachon. You cannot have bitachon without emunah. PART 1 — The Foundational Definition Bitachon as a Foundation of Torah הביטחון בהשם יתעלה ויתברך שמו הוא אחד המידות המעולות ואפילו יסוד מיסודות התורה Bitachon is one of the most exalted character traits and a foundation of the foundations of Torah. Nobody ever disputed the centrality of bitachon It's discussed across the entire Torah and prophets The word/concept is on everyone's lips — both the common people and the elite A Crucial Caveat from the Rambam (Father) Rabbi Avraham echoes his father's warning from the Moreh (end of Section 1): Faith is not merely what is uttered with the lips, but what is apprehended by the soul. If you content yourself with giving utterance to words without apprehending them or believing in them, especially with the true God, you have a very easy task — many ignorant people profess articles of faith without connecting any idea with them. It's easy to say \"B'ezrat Hashem\" many times a day. But what does it mean? Bitachon must be planted in the heart (נטוע בלב) — not just spoken from the lips. This is hard. And working on this is the entire work of Chapter 8. Bitachon Throughout Torah The Foundational Verse כי על כל מוצא פי ה' יחיה האדם \"Man lives by everything that comes from the mouth of Hashem\" (Eikev) The Mitzvot That Embody Bitachon The seventh year (Shemittah) and the fiftieth year (Yovel) are built on bitachon: No planting, no harvesting, no claiming produce This is only possible if you have bitachon The Only Question in the Torah וכי תאמרו מה נאכל בשנה השביעית? הן לא נזרע ולא נאסוף את תבואתנו This is the only place in the Torah where the Torah itself paraphrases a question — \"What shall we eat in the seventh year, when we cannot plant and cannot gather our crops?\" Hashem's answer: וצויתי את ברכתי לכם בשנה הששית ועשת את התבואה לשלש השנים \"I will command My blessing for you in the sixth year — it will produce enough for three years\" The whole sabbatical system requires bitachon. It cannot work without it. The Core Definition of Bitachon ועיקר הביטחון הוא חיזוק האמונה כי השם יתעלה הוא הבורא והמפרנס, המחיה והממית, המחלה והמרפא, המעשיר והמרושש Bitachon is the strengthening of emunah that Hashem is: The Creator and Sustainer Who gives life and death Who brings illness and health Who brings wealth and poverty וכל מקרי העולם הכלליים והפרטיים חוזרים אליו... מלבד המשמעת והמרי All events in the universe — general and individual — trace back to Hashem. The only exception is human free will (obedience and rebellion). What This Means Bitachon is not mere emunah: Emunah = intellectual recognition of the truth Bitachon = living with that truth applied to every aspect of life You can intellectually know everything traces back to Hashem and still not have bitachon. Bitachon is when this knowledge becomes the actual frame of how you experience life. Sources Throughout Tanakh The chapter cites a series of verses establishing the principle: Source Verse Hashem to Moshe at the burning bush מי שם פה לאדם או מי ישום אלם או חרש או פקח או עור — \"Who gives a person speech, sight, hearing?\" Devarim וזכרת את ה' אלוקיך כי הוא הנותן לך כח לעשות חיל — \"Remember that Hashem gives you the power to make wealth\" Ha'azinu ראו עתה כי אני אני הוא... אני אמית ואחיה — \"See now that I am He... I give death and life\" Yaakov האלוקים הרועה אותי מעודי — \"God who has shepherded me my whole life\" Chanah ה' ממית ומחיה... ה' מוריש ומעשיר משפיל אף מרומם — \"Hashem causes death and life, impoverishes and enriches, lowers and elevates\" Elifaz (to Iyov) כי הוא יכאיב ויחבש, ימחץ וידיו תרפינה — \"He wounds and binds, He strikes and His hands heal\" Asaf כי אלוקים שופט, זה ישפיל וזה ירים — \"God is the judge; He lowers one and raises another\" Saadia Gaon's Insight on \"Death Before Life\" In אני אמית ואחיה , why does death precede life? Saadia Gaon (covered in our earlier learning) explained: to tell you there is life after death. The order itself communicates resurrection. What True Bitachon Looks Like in Practice ואמיתת הביטחון היא שיאמין הבוטח ויתן ביטחונו ואמונתו בכל העניינים בהשם יתעלה ולא בשום אמצעי מן האמצעים הרגילים True bitachon means placing one's trust in Hashem for all matters — not in any of the regular intermediaries. Example 1 — Medicine You have a headache. You take Paracetamol. You feel better. עליו לדעת בלבו ומצפונו בוודאות שהתרופה לא הועילה לו אלא מאת הבורא יתעלה Bitachon means knowing with certainty that the medicine helped you only because Hashem willed it. This is NOT Negating Medicine This will be developed more later, but already Rabbi Avraham makes clear: The medicine itself, the science, the pharmacists — these are real They were discovered through human wisdom and effort They work 99.99% of the time But they work because Hashem wills nature to continue running At any moment, the same medicine could be made not to work Bitachon does NOT mean refusing medicine or rejecting human discovery. It means recognizing that nature's reliability is itself dependent on Hashem. The Verse: מחצתי ואני ארפא \"I have wounded, and I will heal\" — Hashem is in charge of both directions. Example 2 — Danger והנהגה מזיקה לא תזיק אלא ברצון הבורא Something normally dangerous will only cause harm if Hashem wills it. Again — this doesn't mean to act recklessly. We don't put ourselves into dangerous situations. The point is the inverse: even regular, safe situations contain risk that Hashem allows or prevents. Example 3 — Business You bought low and sold high. You made a profit. יודע הוא במצפונו המוחלט כי לא יבואנו רווח מן הפעולות הללו אלא בחסד השם יתעלה You know absolutely that this profit came only by Hashem's kindness. You were sharp, clever, business-savvy — but ultimately the profits came from Hashem's will. As Onkelos translates כי הוא הנותן לך כח לעשות חיל : \"He has given you the wisdom and shrewdness to acquire wealth.\" The reverse is also true: if you lost money, suddenly your shares dropped, you lost thousands — that too traces back to Hashem. זרע רב תוציא השדה ומעט תאסוף כי יחסלנו הארבה \"You will sow much seed and gather little — for the locust will consume it\" PART 2 — The Three Categories of Bitachon Rabbi Avraham now introduces a framework that will structure the entire chapter: Category Whose Bitachon What It Means 1 The Prophets Reliance on miracles 2 The Philosophers (Aristotle) Belief in God but no real bitachon — nature is fixed 3 The Correct Category for Us Nature exists, intermediaries are real — but all subservient to Hashem and changeable at any moment Critical Clarification Up Front Category 1 — reliance on miracles — is ONLY for those who have been instructed to rely. This is the easy thing to misunderstand. Rabbi Avraham is NOT telling ordinary people to rely on miracles. He is describing what the prophets did — and only when they had been told to. For example: Hashem tells Avraham to go and offer Yitzchak. It would be easy to say \"perhaps I misunderstood, perhaps Hashem didn't mean it.\" Avraham's bitachon was responding with full faith. והאמין בה' ויחשבה לו צדקה \"Avraham believed in Hashem, and it was counted to him as righteousness\" Even when told to do something extraordinary, carrying through with faith is itself an act of bitachon. Category 1: The Bitachon of the Prophets The Definition והוא התערטלות מכל האמצעים הרגילים והנטייה לסמוך על ניסים הסותרים את חוקי הטבע Divesting oneself of all regular intermediaries, and leaning on miracles that contradict the laws of nature. Three Classic Examples Yonatan (Shaul's son) vs. the Plishtim He went into the Philistine camp with only his arms-bearer: אין להשם מעצור להושיע ברב או במעט \"Nothing prevents Hashem from saving by many or by few\" David vs. Goliat He approached Goliat bekela u'bechalukei avanim — with a sling and smooth stones. He took off the armor, helmet, and regular weapons of war. The verse says: \"ki lo nissiti\" — the simple meaning is \"I am not used to them\" (he had no practice wearing armor). But Yonatan ben Uziel translates midrashically: \"hari lo bahon mit'aved nissa\" — \"a miracle cannot happen through them.\" David removed the regular weapons specifically because he was relying on a miracle. Eliyahu HaNavi — Sustenance from Heaven First, by the stream: Hashem promised water from the stream and food from ravens. Eliyahu went, relied, and was sustained. Then, when the stream dried up: Hashem sent him to the widow in Tzarfat. The flour and oil miraculously did not run out. How Did They Reach This Level? The Rambam (in the Moreh, Section 2) describes 11 levels of prophecy: The lowest level is called Ruach HaKodesh — a strong feeling that Hashem is with you, that this is the right thing to do, but without a clear verbal communication ביטחון ישר ושלם אשר כזה יכול להיגרם ברוח הקודש... או על ידי הבטחת השם יתברך לנביא בדרך התגלות This kind of bitachon happens in ONE of two ways: Direct prophecy — Hashem explicitly tells them (e.g., Eliyahu being instructed where to go) Ruach HaKodesh — a prophetic intuition, a deep feeling of divine direction (Yonatan and David didn't receive direct words, but they had this inner certainty) Even with Prophecy, Bitachon Is Still a Merit אמונת הנביא וביטחונו בחזון המתגלה לו אינם חוץ מהצדק ואינם נקראים ביטחון Even though they've been told this, it's still meritorious — they could have doubted, could have hesitated, could have said \"maybe I misunderstood the prophecy.\" Avraham receiving Hashem's promise of children and the Land — Avraham believing it counted as tzedakah (righteousness). The believing is itself the act. How Rare Is This Level? מדרגה עליונה זו בעניין הביטחון יקרת המציאות היא. לא כל הנביאים יכולים לצפות להשגתה. וגם הנביא שהשיגה לזמן מה, אינו מצפה להשגתה בכל עניין This level is extremely rare. Not all prophets reach it. And even a prophet who reached it doesn't have access to it all the time. The Critical Principle Even prophets are only on this level when they receive explicit divine instruction. When they don't have prophecy or Ruach HaKodesh, they must use normal human plans — and they are not blamed for doing so. Yaakov Avinu — The Pattern of Prophets His Bitachon Level Yaakov clearly reached the highest level of bitachon: He left all his father's wealth (Avraham and Yitzchak were rich) ויצא מבאר שבע וילך חרנה במקלו — \"He left Beer Sheva and went to Charan with only his staff\" Holech ragli — he traveled by foot Lo behema lirkov aleha — no animal to ride Lo chaver imo — no friend with him V'lo cherev lehagen al atzmo — no sword to defend himself He slept on the ground with stones for a pillow כי במקלי עברתי את הירדן הזה \"For with my staff I crossed this Jordan\" (when later recounting it) And Yet — He Was Afraid Fleeing from Lavan 22 years later, Yaakov is worried about Lavan and flees. When Lavan catches up: כי יראתי כי אמרתי פן תגזול את בנותיך ואת בני \"I was afraid, for I said: perhaps you would take away your daughters and my sons by force\" Approaching Esav ויירא יעקב מאוד וייצר לו \"And Yaakov was very afraid and distressed\" He devised an elaborate plan — gifts, animals, divided camps. The Question (Raised by Mark in the Shiur) Did Yaakov lose his bitachon when Yosef was missing for 22 years and he lost his prophecy? Rabbi Kada's nuance: This is a different question. Yaakov lost prophecy not because of a lack of bitachon, but because of sadness . When you're deeply sad, prophecy doesn't come. The Rambam himself wrote that he was sad to the point of near-depression for years after his brother Rabbi Avraham's uncle died at sea . Sadness itself is an obstacle to prophecy. The point Rabbi Avraham is making here is different: even a high-level person like Yaakov did not rely on his bitachon all the time — only when he had explicit divine reassurance. More Examples — Shmuel and Eliyahu Shmuel and Shaul After Hashem rejected Shaul as king (over Amalek), Hashem instructed Shmuel to go anoint David. Shmuel's response: איך אלך ושמע שאול והרגני? \"How can I go? Shaul will hear and kill me.\" This is a great prophet expressing genuine fear. He's not punished for it. Hashem gives him a workaround: עגלת בקר תקח בידך ואמרת לזבוח לה' באתי \"Take a heifer in your hand and say: I have come to offer a sacrifice\" Shmuel uses subterfuge with Hashem's explicit approval. Eliyahu and Izavel Eliyahu had reached extraordinary heights: Could rebuke King Achav: \"I have not troubled Israel — you and your father's house have\" Confronted all of Israel at Mount Carmel: \"How long will you straddle two opinions?\" The people couldn't even respond He slaughtered 450 prophets of Baal And then: וירא ויקם וילך אל נפשו \"He was afraid, got up, and fled for his life\" — from the threats of Izavel. How could the same Eliyahu who confronted Achav and killed 450 prophets be afraid of one woman? Because at the moment of Carmel, Eliyahu was acting bidvar Hashem — \"according to Your word, I have done all these things\" (and indeed the text says exactly this). When the explicit divine instruction was absent, he reverted to ordinary human responses, including fear. Note on the Tension with the Shmoneh Perakim The Rambam in his Shmoneh Perakim (Chapter 7) seems to imply that fear for Yaakov and Shmuel was a problem. Rabbi Avraham here takes a different position — there's no fault when the prophet has not been prophesied a specific reassurance. There are ways to reconcile, but the simple reading of Rabbi Avraham is that the prophets' fear in unprompted situations was not a flaw. Defining Ruach HaKodesh More Precisely Rabbi Avraham now clarifies what Ruach HaKodesh actually is: בהרגשה נבואית ועוז רוח אלוהי A prophetic feeling and a divine strength of spirit. Examples from Tanakh: ותהי על יפתח רוח ה' — \"The spirit of Hashem was upon Yiftach\" ותצלח עליו רוח ה' — \"The spirit of Hashem rushed upon him\" (Shimshon) This is what Yonatan and David had when they relied on miracles in battle. It's not direct verbal communication — it's an inner certainty, an intuition, that this is Hashem's will and they will succeed. David and Achish — Bitachon Through Intuition The story: David, fleeing Shaul, comes to King Achish of Gat. Achish's servants identify David as the famous warrior. David is terrified — they will surely kill him. What does David do? וישנה את טעמו בעיניהם ויתהלל בידם ויתו על דלתות השער ויורד רירו אל זקנו He pretended to be insane — scribbled on the doors, let saliva run down his beard. Achish dismissed him: \"This man is crazy — what use is he?\" Where did this idea come from? Hashem gave him this intuition (eitzah). The Principle או ישיא ה' לעבדו עצה שתגן בפחדיו Hashem will whisper to His servant a plan that will protect him in his fears. When you don't have direct prophecy, sometimes you get divine intuition pointing to the right path forward. The Critical Conclusion of Category 1 הוי אומר: כל התולה תקוותו בנסים בלבד לא הלא התאמה, ללא התגלות או ללא הרגשה אלוהית אמתית להם שהם, הרי הוא מתאווה למה שאינו ראוי לו וחוטא בחוצפה המביאה לידי חילול השם — אין ספק שיבוא על עונשו Anyone who hangs their hope on miracles alone — without preparation, without prophecy, without genuine divine inspiration — is wanting something inappropriate for them, sinning with the brazenness that leads to chillul Hashem. There is no doubt they will be punished. Why This Is So Severe The person who says \"I rely on Hashem!\" and behaves recklessly creates a double risk: Their plans fail Everyone sees that \"the religious person trusted Hashem and failed\" — creating chillul Hashem This is why excessive bitachon (when not justified) is described as chutzpah — brazenness. PART 3 — Too Much Bitachon vs. Too Little This is one of the most striking sections of the chapter. Both deficiencies are punishable. דומה הוא למי שביטחונו לוקה בחסר Excessive bitachon (when not warranted) is comparable to deficient bitachon. Example of Deficient Bitachon — Dor HaMidbar The generation of the desert was given everything : The exodus from Egypt with miracles and wonders The pillar of cloud by day The pillar of fire by night Manna for food Water from the rock The Aron going before them, finding their resting places A cloud above protecting from heat The Shechinah dwelling among them Moshe Rabbeinu as a direct intermediary — \"Stand, and let me hear what Hashem will command\" This is the highest level of miraculous reality in recorded history. Their Failure — The Spies When Moshe brought them to Eretz Kena'an: Hashem had explicitly told them: \"See, Hashem has given you the land. Go up, possess it, do not fear or be discouraged\" They asked to send spies ( nishlecha anashim lefaneinu ) Moshe relented ( vayitav b'einai hadavar ) Hashem said: shelach lecha — \"You want this? Send them, on your responsibility\" The spies returned with frightening reports. The people cried. Moshe insisted: \"Hashem who goes before you will fight for you\" . They didn't believe. The Verse That Captures It ובדבר הזה אינכם מאמינים בה' אלוקיכם ההולך לפניכם \"In this matter you do not believe in Hashem your God who goes before you\" Result: 40 years in the desert. The entire generation could not enter the land. Hashem's words: עד אנה ינאצוני העם הזה ועד אנה לא יאמינו בי, בכל האותות אשר עשיתי בקרבו? \"How long will this nation provoke Me? How long will they not believe in Me, with all the signs I have done among them?\" Why Was This So Severe? Because with everything they had been given , the expected level of bitachon was extraordinarily high. To fall below it was a catastrophic failure. The Psalm That Captures This — Tehillim 78 (Asaf) משכיל לאסף — האזינה עמי תורתי, הטו אזניכם לאמרי פי \"Listen, My people, to My Torah; incline your ears to the words of My mouth\" The mizmor's purpose, says Rabbi Avraham: וכוונת כל המזמור הזה הוא לחזק את האדם ולהמריצו על הביטחון The entire mizmor is to strengthen and arouse a person to bitachon. The mizmor describes their failure as: דור סורר ומורה, דור לא הכין לבו ולא נאמנה את אל רוחו \"A rebellious and stubborn generation, a generation whose heart was not steadfast, whose spirit was not faithful to God\" והבן את הדרך של המזמור הזה כי התורה סובבת על הביטחון, ועל ידי הביטחון נשמרת התורה Understand this mizmor: the Torah is centered on bitachon, and through bitachon the Torah is kept. A person without bitachon is described as sorer u'moreh — rebellious and stubborn — meredbi'emunato — rebelling against his very faith. Example of Excessive Bitachon — The Ma'apilim Now, the very same parashah (Shelach Lecha) contains the opposite mistake. After Hashem decreed 40 years in the desert, some Jews said: \"Wait! We were wrong. Now we'll go up and fight as we should have done.\" They went up to fight the next morning. Why This Was Wrong אבל זה נאמר לגבי מי שמזניח כל חלקי ביטחון, ולא לגבי מי שבוטח ביטחון בנביאים ודומה להם They had just lost the hashgachah. The conditions for the high-level bitachon were no longer present. They had been told they would not enter the land — that this generation would die in the desert. To now rely on miracles was to claim a category they no longer had access to. The Torah's harsh verdict: ותזידו ותעלו ההרה \"You acted brazenly and went up the mountain\" The word zadon (brazenness) is the same word Rabbi Avraham used to describe relying on miracles without authorization. The Beautiful Symmetry In the same parashah, the same story , you see both deficiencies: Before the decree: They should have had high bitachon (they had all the signs). They didn't. They were punished with 40 years. After the decree: They tried to suddenly have high bitachon. They didn't have authorization. They were punished with defeat. Both are failures. Both are deviations from where you are actually supposed to be. The Request for a King — Another Example of Failure In the time of Shmuel HaNavi, Bnei Yisrael requested a king to fight their enemies. Hashem to Shmuel: כי לא אותך מאסו, כי אותי מאסו ממלוך עליהם \"It is not you they have rejected — it is Me they have rejected from ruling over them\" Shmuel's rebuke included a sign: rain in the summer (which is destructive in Israel) — to show Hashem's displeasure. The people acknowledged: כי יספנו על כל חטאתינו רעה לשאול לנו מלך \"We have added to our sins the evil of asking for a king\" Why Was This a Failure of Bitachon? Because Hashem was their King . Asking for a human king was a tacit declaration that they didn't trust Hashem's direct kingship over them. It was deficient bitachon — relying on human structures of power instead of divine protection. Final Principles for This Section ובכן, אלה שהיתה עליהם תמיד השגחת ה' ותוכחת עבדיו הנביאים ועושה להם ניסים — חייבים שביטחונם בה' יתעלה יהיה שלם ומוגמר, וירידתם מזה נחשבת לחטא Those upon whom Hashem's hashgachah constantly rests, who experience prophecy and miracles — they MUST have complete bitachon. Falling from that is a sin. ומאידך גיסא, מי שמעז פניו וסומך על ביטחון כזה למרות חוסר הכשרתו ופחיתות סגולותיו — הרי הוא מבקש מה שאינו ראוי לו, במקום להשיג את קרבת ה' יתעלה ישיג ההפך הגמור On the other hand, one who brazenly relies on this kind of bitachon despite not being trained or qualified — seeks what isn't appropriate for them, and instead of achieving closeness to Hashem, achieves the opposite entirely. The Talmudic Maxim לא כל הרוצה ליטול את השם יטול \"Not everyone who wants to take 'the Name' may take it\" You cannot self-declare into a higher spiritual category. Bitachon at the prophetic level is reserved for those who have actually earned that station. Summary What Bitachon Means Complete reliance on Hashem Recognition that He is Creator of all Everything in the universe (except free will) is subservient to His will Living with security and serenity NOT rejecting natural means and intermediaries — Hashem created them Bitachon means knowing those intermediaries are themselves dependent on Hashem's ongoing will The Three Categories (Framework) The Prophets — reliance on miracles (covered tonight) The Philosophers — belief in God but no real bitachon (coming up) The Correct Category for Us — nature exists, intermediaries are real, but all subservient to Hashem (coming up) Category 1: The Prophets' Bitachon Divesting from regular means Relying on miracles ONLY by divine instruction — either direct prophecy or Ruach HaKodesh Examples: Yonatan, David, Eliyahu, Avraham Even prophets cannot rely on this all the time Yaakov, Shmuel, Eliyahu all experienced fear when divine reassurance wasn't present — and they were not blamed for it Both Failure Modes Are Real Too little bitachon when the situation calls for it → severe sin (dor hamidbar) Too much bitachon when unauthorized → brazenness, chillul Hashem (ma'apilim) The art is being in the right category for who you actually are, in the situation you're actually in. Coming Up Next Week We continue with Categories 2 and 3 : Category 2 — The Philosophers' Position: They believe in God but believe nature is fixed and unchangeable. Their \"bitachon\" is therefore very limited. Category 3 — The Correct Category for Us: Recognizing nature as real and using intermediaries normally, while knowing they are all subservient to Hashem and can change at any moment. This is the bitachon expected of us. B'Ezrat Hashem","PP6K7dYz45w":"Dayan Daniel Kada · Thursday Night Halacha · Shiur 2 of 7 Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 671:3–7 Review of Shiur 1 What is forbidden on Hanukah from the strict law: fasting and eulogies . Women also have the custom of not doing burdensome work in the first half hour after lighting. The four aspects of Hanukah and how they are commemorated: 1. The military victory : Hallel and the tefillot of Hanukah. 2. The oil : the menorah. 3. The miracle of Yehudit , daughter of Yoḥanan: dairy foods. 4. The inauguration of the altar : the Torah reading of the Nesi'im. The altar was meant to be inaugurated on 25 Kislev, and Hashem gave Kislev \"another Hanukah.\" Siman 671:1–2: the mitzvah is so important that even a poor person must obtain at least one light per night. Sephardim light one menorah per household so it is clear which night it is. Ashkenazim (Rema) have each person light, adding a light each night. Children who come home with their own menorah may light it, but in a different part of the house . A question at the start: may Ashkenazim place everyone's menorahs right next to each other? The Rabbi: even for Ashkenazim they should not be right next to each other; each should be in its own place, \"to make it very clear\" which night it is. Tonight's focus is \"very, very practical\": where you light, how high , and what to do if you live in a flat . Seif 3: One Candle With Two Wicks נר שיש לו שני פיות, עולה לו בשביל שנים. A lamp with two spouts counts as two lights. The Mishnah Berurah clarifies: this is not a Havdalah candle. A Havdalah candle has several wicks but makes one big flame , and everyone agrees that is a single light. We are speaking about a candle with two wicks on either side, producing two distinct and separate flames . For what purpose does it count as two? The halacha accepts it for both : Purpose Example Mehadrin : adding a light each night On the second night, one candle with two distinct wicks counts as the night's two lights Two separate obligations under one roof Two people living together who are completely financially independent count as two households, each obliged to light. One lights the left wick, the other the right. (What counts as a \"household\" comes in later chapters.) \"I don't know how practical that is,\" the Rabbi added, \"but that's the halacha.\" Seif 4: A Bowl of Oil With Many Wicks, and the Problem of a Bonfire Reminder: you don't need a menorah. Even for the very highest level of the mitzvah, a row of eight wax candles is enough. The case: it is the eighth night and I have no menorah, so I fill a bowl with oil and set eight wicks around its rim. Does that work? Setup Status Covered with a vessel, restricting the air Each wick counts as a separate light Open to the air, the flames merging Does not even count as one light Why not even one? Because it becomes a מדורה , a bonfire. The mitzvah is a נר , a flame. \"If I light a bonfire on Hanukah, can I make a berachah? Does that constitute the mitzvah? No.\" A bonfire does not fulfil the mitzvah at all. In practice: one bowl with many wicks works as long as the wicks are separate and you can clearly see eight distinct flames . If they join up, it does not work. Why menorahs are made in a straight line \"You'll never find a circular menorah. Why can't you do a circular menorah? Eight branches, nice and round...?\" Because of this halacha it became customary not to arrange the lights in a circle, so there is never even the perception of one large fire. A straight row keeps the flames clearly distinct. The Rema: separate lamps and wax candles Separate lamps (flames, not electric) are fine, since each is distinct. With wax candles , take care that they do not melt into each other . If they stick together they become one big flame, the problem of a bonfire. Summary of Seif 3–4: one candle can serve as two lights if it has two distinct flames; a bowl with many wicks works if the flames stay separate; whatever the fuel, the flames must be distinct, which is why menorahs are not round. The Big Question: Where Do You Put the Menorah? Three questions to answer: 1. Where? Inside or outside, the window, the front door, the table? 2. How high? What is the minimum and the maximum? 3. Which side of the doorway? \"This halacha, as you can imagine, has changed a lot over the centuries, and we'll see why.\" Seif 5: The Location נר חנוכה מניחו על פתח הבית הסמוך לרשות הרבים מבחוץ. ואם יש חצר לפני הבית, מניחו על פתח החצר. Place the Hanukah light at the entrance of the house that adjoins the public domain, on the outside. If there is a courtyard in front of the house, place it at the entrance of the courtyard. The ideal, from the Gemara Picture an English house with a front door and a front garden: - Not inside the front of your house, but outside it . - If you have a front garden , at the front of the front garden , by the street. A question: won't it blow out in the open? The Rabbi: first, this is not England. But even in Israel it can be extinguished, and we'll discuss that. A flat with no door to the street If you live on the second or third floor and have no door opening to the public domain, place it in the window that faces the public domain . \"This is where the window comes from.\" A time of danger ובשעת הסכנה מניחו על שלחנו, ודיו. In a time of danger, he places it on his table, and that is sufficient. This is already in the Gemara. \"Persecution, antisemitism is nothing new.\" When identifying yourself as Jewish could have consequences, lighting on the table inside the house is enough. Where Does the Shamash Come From? The Rabbi put the question to the class: why does every menorah have a shamash, eight plus one? \"You use it to light the other candles.\" True, practically. \"So we can benefit from the light.\" Also true. But why must every menorah be built that way? The source is right here. When lighting on the table in a time of danger, in an age without electricity, a candle on the table is the most ordinary thing in the world. So you need a היכר , a sign that this is not a regular candle. You light an extra candle for use, so it is clear the others are for the mitzvah. A fireplace (as Richard supplied the word) can serve as that extra light, unless you are the sort of household that uses the fire only for warmth and lights separate candles for light. Then it does not count, and you still need the extra light. But that need exists only for the table. When lighting at the window or the doorway it is obvious the lights are for the mitzvah. Nonetheless, the Me'iri , some 800 years ago, wrote that the custom of everyone, including his teachers, was always to light an extra candle, even with no intention of using it. That practice spread so it can never look as if you are lighting for light. That is the shamash. What This Halacha Teaches About פרסומי ניסא First principle: bedi'eved, the mitzvah works anywhere in the house. We see this from the time-of-danger rule. Why, if the whole mitzvah is publicising the miracle? Because pirsumei nisa includes your own household, and even yourself . No distinction is drawn between a large family, a couple, or someone who lives alone. Even home alone, lighting on the table, you light and say the berachah : the publicity is for everyone, and for yourself. Who Actually Lights Outside Today? Then, says the Rabbi, if you are able to do what the Shulḥan Arukh says, you should : light at the entrance of your home or the front of your front garden. \"Have you ever seen anyone in England do this?\" A class member mentioned one house with a large gas menorah in the garden, but that is more like the big public menorahs. Another participant: in Miami people do light outside their door, and in blocks of flats by the elevators. In Israel , many people light at the front of their house, or at the entrance to their block of flats, often on a little stand. Why most of us light inside The Rema (end of the seif) quotes the Maharam of Rothenburg : nowadays we all light inside , because lighting outside is a danger. The Maharam knew religious persecution firsthand: he was imprisoned for seven years and died in prison in Germany. Today: - Contemporary poskim discuss whether, where there is no danger at all, one should go back to lighting outside. - With the surge in antisemitism, people (perhaps even in Miami that year) are understandably concerned about openly identifying as Jewish. Outside Israel, the Rabbi suggested, the next safest place may well be the UAE . - Some poskim say that after so many centuries of danger, generation after generation, lighting inside has become the norm , which is why even in places with no danger (even in Israel) some are not particular. - Most poskim say that when you can, you should light outside. In England it is a combination of factors: certainly some fear, but also wind and rain make it very difficult to light with real fuel outdoors. \"If you're living in a place where it's practical, both from the perspective of the non-Jews around us and from the weather perspective, then really one should light outside the house or outside the front garden.\" Seif 6: The Height מצוה להניחו למעלה משלשה טפחים, ומצוה להניחו למטה מעשרה טפחים, ואם הניחו למעלה מעשרים אמה לא יצא. The minimum: above three tefaḥim A tefaḥ is a handbreadth, about the width of a fist. The Rabbi uses 8 cm , so three tefaḥim ≈ 24 cm . This is not in the Gemara; the Rishonim write it. The logic is the principle of לבוד : anything within three tefaḥim of the ground is treated as part of the ground , so you have given the light no height at all. Two qualifications: 1. The measurement is to the flame , not the menorah's base. A decent-sized menorah standing on the floor usually has its flames above 24 cm, which is fine. 2. Even if the flame is below three tefaḥim, bedi'eved it works , because pirsumei nisa applies even to yourself. \"What's wrong with lighting the menorah on the floor? ...The only reason you don't want to is pirsumei nisa,\" and that includes yourself. The preference: below ten tefaḥim (≈ 80 cm) Note the language: it is a mitzvah , commendable, to place it below ten tefaḥim . Why? Above ten it looks like an ordinary candle lit for use. Between three and ten it is high enough to be seen but low enough that no one would light a candle there for practical use , so it is recognisable as a mitzvah light. The background to this ruling: - In the Gemara, a statement that מצוה להניחו למטה מעשרה is brought, but its proof is rejected. - The Rif and the Rambam therefore do not hold of it at all: above three tefaḥim, full stop. - The Rosh still holds it is a mitzvah. - Normally, with the Rif and Rambam against the Rosh, the Shulḥan Arukh follows the majority. But here the Beit Yosef reasons: what is there to lose? The Rif and Rambam do not say you shouldn't place it below ten; they simply see no mitzvah. Since the Rosh sees a mitzvah, place it there. - The Beit Yosef concludes: וכן נוהגים המדקדקים , that is what the meticulous do , conceding that most people did not. The Mishnah Berurah (Seif Katan 27): some argued that since we now light indoors, the ten-tefaḥim preference no longer matters. That is incorrect ; the meticulous keep it even indoors. But when you light in a window , use the window even if it is higher than ten tefaḥim from the floor. And in the synagogue we are not particular: there we place it as high as we can, because synagogue lighting is a different kind of lighting (next week). The maximum: twenty amot Above twenty amot (measured to the flame , from the ground) the mitzvah is not fulfilled at all. (In the previous shiur's notes this was roughly 9.6 m.) הדלקה עושה מצוה: The Lighting Makes the Mitzvah ואם הניחו למעלה מעשרים אמה לא יצא, אפילו אם הורידו אחר כך. If you lit it above twenty amot and someone pointed out it was too high, moving it lower afterwards does not help . You have not done the mitzvah, and must light again with the berachah. At the moment you light and say the berachah, the menorah must be in a valid position. The reverse is also true, and people often get this wrong: \"People think once you've lit the menorah you have to leave it there. That's wrong.\" If you lit in a valid place, you may move it (during the week; of course not on Shabbat), from one part of the window to another, for example. Example to come later: if the head of the household is ill in bed, one posek suggested bringing the menorah to him to light and then moving it to the window. Don't do that. Lighting at a bedside or table is a significant bedi'eved, relying on the time-of-danger leniency. Moving it to the window afterwards does not improve the mitzvah, because the lighting is the mitzvah . A question: what if I light on the table and then move it near the door because it's more convenient? The Rabbi: it would work only \"very bedi'eved,\" because at the moment you lit, you were relying on the time-of-danger leniency. Another question: is the window really better than the front door? The Rabbi: \"It doesn't work like that.\" The halacha is fixed ( לא פלוג ): by the window or by the entrance. The Practical Hierarchy Rank Where Height 1. Ideal Outside , at the front of the house or the front of the front garden Above 3, below 10 tefaḥim (24–80 cm), on the side opposite the mezuzah 2. Next best (common practice in England) The window facing the street Wherever the window is, even if above 10 tefaḥim 3. Next Inside the front door , opposite the mezuzah Try for above 3, below 10 tefaḥim 4. Last resort The table Only when there is no other option Personal note: lighting by the door in Israel When the Rabbi lived in Israel, with no option to light outside and no window facing the street, the family lit inside the front door , opposite the mezuzah, on a little stand within ten tefaḥim. In the second year, with a baby crawling around, a flame at 30–50 cm was not safe, \"so then you have to use your common sense, put it higher.\" The ten-tefaḥim rule is a commendable mitzvah, not an absolute requirement. Practical questions Frosted glass: one participant had clear glass in the living-room window and frosted glass in the window next to the front door . Which is better? The Rabbi: the frosted window by the front door , since you can still see something through it and it is by the entrance, \"although that's speculative.\" The risk is the door opening and the wind blowing out the candle. Maor asked: can I just put it on the dining table? The Rabbi: only in a time of danger . You cannot decide from the outset to put it there; it is the worst-case scenario. Someone ill and living alone , cared for by a non-Jewish carer and unable to reach the front door: the menorah may be brought to the bedside. That is the best that person can do, and pirsumei nisa includes yourself. Seif 7: Which Side of the Doorway? מצוה להניחו בטפח הסמוך לפתח משמאל, כדי שתהא מזוזה מימין ונר חנוכה משמאל. ואם אין מזוזה בפתח, מניחו מימין. Place it in the tefaḥ nearest the left-hand doorpost (as you enter), so that the mezuzah is on the right and the Hanukah light on the left . This comes from the Gemara itself. No mezuzah in that doorway: place it on the right , since the right is generally the preferred side. Lighting indoors by the front door : likewise opposite where the mezuzah is. By a window the question does not arise. Surrounded by mitzvot The Geonim add: the one lighting wears his tallit , so he is completely surrounded by mitzvot : mezuzah on the right, Hanukah light on the left, tzitzit around him. Ḥacham Ovadia's question: we light at night , and tzitzit is a daytime mitzvah ( וראיתם אותו , \"you shall see them\"). How can the Geonim speak of tzitzit at night? The answer lies in a dispute between the Rosh and the Rambam about כסות יום בלילה , a daytime garment worn at night: - The Rosh: a garment designated for daytime remains obligated at night. - So the Geonim must hold like the Rosh. Practical illustration: if one corner of your tzitzit became invalid in the evening, you might think, \"It's night, I'll fix it tomorrow.\" Wrong , according to the Rosh: it is a day garment, still obligated at night, and you would be wearing a four-cornered garment with only three valid corners. In practice we are stringent both ways. Hiddur and the tallit This ties back to last week's theme of beauty . When the Gemara teaches הידור מצוה from זה אלי ואנוהו (\"This is my God and I will beautify Him\"), the example it gives is טלית נאה, ציצית נאה , a beautiful tallit and beautiful tzitzit. \"There's a whole thing coming here with beauty, and what beauty really means.\" The Rabbi has a full shiur on this, and hopes to return to it nearer Hanukah. The Rema: today we light inside ומיהו בזמן הזה שכולנו מדליקים בפנים... But nowadays, when we all light inside , we need not be so particular about the tefaḥ nearest the doorway, though it is still good practice. For households with several menorahs (Ashkenazim): spread them out (\"one here, one there\"), but not where you would normally have lights for use, such as by the kitchen counter where you cut vegetables or cook. There it looks as if you lit for light. Flats Above Twenty Amot The Mishnah Berurah (Sha'ar HaTziyun): if your flat is higher than twenty amot , you should not light by the window. - Lighting at the window is aimed at the street outside , and that is above twenty amot , which does not work. - Lighting inside your front door measures the twenty amot from your own floor , so it is valid. \"Some say\" that if there are high-rises opposite you, people across the way at your level can see your window, so it is fine. The Rabbi is not convinced: pirsumei nisa is for everyone walking down the street . \"Who are you lighting for? For the people opposite you? Light for the people in your house. Light in the entrance of your house, where more people in your house will see it.\" Summary: Putting It All Together Seif 3: a candle with two distinct flames counts as two, both for mehadrin and for two separate households. Seif 4: a bowl with many wicks counts only if the flames stay separate . Merged flames are a bonfire and do not fulfil even the basic mitzvah. This is why menorahs are straight, not round . Keep wax candles from melting together. Seif 5: the Gemara's ideal is outside , at the front of the house or front garden; a flat without a street door uses the window to the street ; in danger, the table . The shamash originates in the need for a sign when lighting on the table. The Me'iri records that the custom became to always light an extra candle. Pirsumei nisa includes yourself. That is why the mitzvah works bedi'eved anywhere in the house, even for someone alone. Today most of us light inside, as the Maharam of Rothenburg recorded, because of persecution and, in England, the weather. Most poskim say light outside where you safely can . Height: the flame above 3 tefaḥim (~24 cm) (bedi'eved even lower is fine), preferably below 10 (~80 cm) (the Rosh / Beit Yosef, \"the meticulous\"), and never above 20 amot . הדלקה עושה מצוה: it must be in a valid place when you light . Moving an invalid menorah afterwards does not help; moving a valid one is permitted. The hierarchy: outside, then the street-facing window (even above 10 tefaḥim), then inside the front door opposite the mezuzah (preferably 3–10 tefaḥim), then the table only in danger. Seif 7: mezuzah on the right, menorah on the left , surrounded by mitzvot. No mezuzah: the right side. Above twenty amot: light inside your front door , not at the window. Coming Up Next week: the synagogue menorah . Its origins, the reasons for the custom, its placement and timing, and whether a berachah may be said on a custom.","QFOS6MvKRsM":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek: Principle 1 in depth; the start of Principle 2 Shiur: Part 8 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 25 November 2025 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Recap The Mishnah: כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא . Who has a portion? Those who believe the 13 Principles. Last week: - The Rambam's emphasis on belief (converts, Yevamot) - Other counts: three, one, none (the Radbaz) - Why principles matter : ignorance is no excuse Tonight: Principle 1 dissected , and the Rishonim's arguments. היסוד הראשון: להאמין מציאות הבורא יתברך… שיש שם נמצא שלם בכל דרכי המציאות, והוא עילת מציאות הנמצאים כלם… ואם נעלה על לבנו העדר כל הנמצאים זולתו, לא תתבטל מציאות השם יתברך ולא תגרע… וזה היסוד הראשון, מורה עליו הדבור אנכי ה׳ אלהיך. Part 1: The Five Elements 1. There is a Creator \" Straightforward. Whether creation was from nothing or from pre-existent matter as old as God (as Ibn Ezra and others held) is Principle 4 .\" 2. He is perfect Harold Kushner's When Bad Things Happen to Good People : the leading Conservative rabbi argued God wants to stop evil but can't . \" According to the Rambam, and everyone, that's a misunderstanding of God. His essence is perfection , which means omnipotence .\" Can God do the impossible? (Discussed with Rav Saadia on Monday nights.) Can He make a rock He can't lift? No, and that's no imperfection. \" It's semantics : we hear can't and think weakness . Hashem can't catch flu : He's not physical. Rav Saadia's example: He can't make a square a triangle. \" The Rambam (Moreh): we don't call a man weak because he can't lift 100,000 weights . Nor is God incapable because He can't make Himself corporeal , create His equal , or make a square whose diagonal equals its side . The dissent: Rebbe Nachman of Breslov : the philosophers say God can't make a triangle a square; I say He can, for His ways are hidden. \" Rebbe Nachman hated philosophers and doctors: both of which the Rambam was. A minority view, not logically sound .\" 3. He depends on nothing A consequence of perfection. אדון עולם אשר מלך בטרם כל יציר נברא : He reigned before anything was formed. \"Hashem doesn't need our prayers.\" \"Contrary to Christian theology . We sometimes talk as if Hashem is waiting for our tefillot. Prayer is transformative, for our own sake : a meditation for our intellectual and moral development.\" \"If I didn't know Hebrew and read the English of the siddur , through a Western, Christian lens, I wouldn't want to pray: holy, holy, holy, we worship You… as if God needs us. It's quite babyish .\" 4. Everything depends on Him, continuously Not a God who created and left (deism). \" Even if you believe in a perfect, independent Creator who then left the world, that isn't Hashem. \" המחדש בטובו בכל יום תמיד מעשה בראשית. The imagined universe. \"Create a beautiful universe in your mind . It exists only while you're thinking of it . Someone tells you some news, your attention slips, it's gone .\" So Hashem actively sustains every element, every moment . \"No carpenter who makes something and walks away.\" 5. His existence is necessary From the Mishneh Torah : וה׳ אלהים אמת : \" Two plus two is four , not merely logically: it must be. That's emet . \" Yigdal and Ani Ma'amin lack perfection ; Ani Ma'amin has continuous involvement ( עשה ועושה ויעשה ). Part 2: Arguments for a Creator \"It's not my expertise; I'll share what's in the Rishonim.\" Three caveats: No proof will be 100%. If it were, there'd be no free will. We seek beyond reasonable doubt . Unconscious bias.Aldous Huxley , the prominent British writer, admitted that he and his friends wanted the world to have no meaning: meaninglessness freed them from morality , above all sexual morality. The Gemara (Sanhedrin 63b): יודעים היו ישראל בעבודה זרה שאין בה ממש, ולא עבדו עבודה זרה אלא להתיר להם עריות בפרהסיא. They knew idols were nothing; they worshipped them to permit forbidden relations openly. Rav Elchanan Wasserman on ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם ואחרי עיניכם : לבבכם = heresy; עיניכם = promiscuity (Berachot). Why in one verse? We stray after the heart so that we can stray after the eyes. Tanach never tries to prove God. \"It's a given throughout Tanach and Talmud. Only the Rishonim began offering proofs.\" Argument 1: the cosmological argument The most common : Rav Saadia , the Rambam (Moreh) , Rav Yosef Albo , Rabbeinu Bachya ibn Pakuda ( Chovot HaLevavot ) . Simply: the computer, the table: made by a technician, a carpenter; who made the carpenter? His parents; theirs… A dependent thing can't be the final cause , so trace back to Hashem . The standard refutation (Kant): the argument assumes everything needs a cause , then concludes with something uncaused . \" Why posit God at the end? Posit an infinitely old universe. \" \" Fair. But at least that's now 50/50 : agnostic, not atheist .\" The Rambam's deeper version (Moreh) Step 1 Everything in the universe is physical and finite : bounded, however vast 2 The physical and finite must have a cause and can't be infinitely old 3 So we must posit something non-physical and infinite : the מצוי ראשון What \"infinite\" really means: \" 33.333… : each step is finite .\" A blob that doubles every second would, in an hour, fill the universe; in a thousand years be billions of universes . Still, at any moment you could measure it. \" Indefinitely extendable, not infinite. Bigness isn't infinity.\" Anything measurable is finite. Only zero has no edges, and zero is negation . Infinity is like zero, but existent : no space, no time, no shape . So the universe, being physical, can be very, very old, but not infinitely. Once there was no space, no time, nothing. We need something above space, time and boundaries , which the physical depends on: that's the infinite God. Not huge : beyond all boundaries. Against Aristotle. \" Until the 1960s , many believed like Aristotle that the universe is infinitely old. Now we know it had a beginning. The Rambam was proven right. \" He revered Aristotle, even implying something like prophecy , yet argued here. \" If I thought Aristotle were right philosophically, I'd reinterpret בראשית ברא אלהים. That's not why I disagree. I think he's philosophically wrong. **\" Robert's challenge: matter is never destroyed, only changed into energy. Infinite forward in time. Then why not backward? \" The Rambam also writes there's no reason the world must end. I hear that. Let me think about it. None of these are foolproof.\" Argument 2: design (teleological) The Rambam and Chovot HaLevavot : the order of the universe, the sun at just the right distance , can't be accident. Rabbeinu Bachya: you find a page of fresh ink . A dog knocked over the quill : a 0.001% chance. Logic says someone wrote it. The Rambam (Yesodei HaTorah): love of Hashem comes from contemplating how beautiful and precise the world is. Avraham (the Midrash): a burning palace: מי בעל הבירה? Who owns this palace? Hashem appeared and told him. Objection: evil and suffering. \" That needs an explanation, a different discussion. It doesn't negate the design we clearly see elsewhere.\" Evolution: \" Absolutely acceptable , provided you posit a Creator behind the process .\" Other arguments Tradition (the Kuzari ): the chain back to Sinai; the Torah couldn't have been falsified. The ontological argument (Christian; \"I haven't seen it in the Rishonim\"): a dragon combines things we know: a horse, birds' wings, fire. But nothing in experience resembles God. How could humans conceive Him if He didn't exist? Fulfilled prophecy. \" The State of Israel is one of the strongest proofs. A miracle in itself. \" \"You don't need to prove love\" Rav Soloveitchik, quoting Kierkegaard: a bride and groom in each other's arms don't need to prove their love. \" Some things you just feel. \" \"A philosopher argued nothing exists except in your imagination ; the only certainty is that you yourself think . You can doubt even me. But I know you're here. So too with Hashem. We work through the arguments, but the deepest emunah is when you feel His embrace. \" \"When I was younger I spent a lot of time on these questions; they bothered me a lot . Some people have doubts, and that's acceptable. But living with Torah and mitzvot, many come to just feel Hashem's presence. That's the deepest emunah . \" How He created \" Nothing precludes a frum Jew believing Hashem created through a Big Bang, or through evolution, or both. Principle 1 says there's a Creator of the first matter .\" Rebbe Nachman ( Likutei Moharan II:52) , in substance: it's fitting that there be objections to God's existence , because He is so exalted above our minds . \"I don't fully understand it, but: never push people away. If they struggle, help them. \" A coming debate. \"In January I'll debate a neighbour, a professor of science and avowed atheist , publicly, with the community. He'll know more science than me. But some things you feel, and rationally it's beyond reasonable doubt : where did first matter come from? Where did consciousness begin? \" Part 3: Principle 2 Begins היסוד השני: יחוד השם יתברך, כלומר שנאמין שזה שהוא סיבת הכל אחד. ואיננו כאחד הזוג, ולא כאחד המין, ולא כאיש אחד שהוא נחלק לאחדים רבים, ולא כגוף הפשוט האחד במנין שמקבל החלוק לאין תכלית. אבל השם יתברך אחד באחדות שאין כמותה אחדות. וזה היסוד השני, מורה עליו מה שנאמר: שמע ישראל ה׳ אלהינו ה׳ אחד. Note: not simply \"God is one,\" but the Cause of everything is one , and not \"one\" in any ordinary sense : Not one as… Because One pair A pair of socks splits into two socks One species \" The human race \" contains many One person A person has many parts One simple body Even it is endlessly divisible אחד באחדות שאין כמותה אחדות : one with a unity unlike any other unity. What that is, he doesn't say here; in the Mishneh Torah he writes it's almost impossible to explain . Yigdal: אחד ואין יחיד כיחודו, נעלם וגם אין סוף לאחדותו A precise encapsulation : no unity like His, hidden, without end Ani Ma'amin: שהבורא… הוא יחיד ואין יחידות כמוהו בשום פנים, והוא לבדו אלהינו, היה הוה ויהיה Same, plus the only God Summary Principle 1 has five elements: a Creator ; perfect (omnipotent, but can't do the impossible , no imperfection; contra Rebbe Nachman); depending on nothing ( Hashem doesn't need our prayers ); sustaining everything continuously (no deism: the imagined universe ); necessary ( emet ). Arguments: never 100% (free will); beware unconscious bias (Huxley; idolatry to permit immorality ; heart before eyes ). Tanach never tries. Cosmological (Saadia, Rambam, Albo, Chovot HaLevavot), refined by the Rambam: the finite can't be infinitely old , so the infinite is beyond space and time. Proven right against Aristotle. (Robert: matter seems endless forward ; \"let me think.\") Design (ink on paper; מי בעל הבירה ); evil is a separate question; evolution is fine with a Creator. Tradition, ontological, prophecy (Israel). The deepest emunah is felt , like love (Soloveitchik/Kierkegaard). Never push away those who struggle (Rebbe Nachman). Principle 2 begins: one with a unity like no other : not a pair, species, person or body. Coming Up Next week, Principle 2 in full: monotheism vs polytheism, dualism and deism in the Rishonim, and what the Rambam means by a unity beyond all unity . (Thursday: the chazarah shiur.)","SyYC7y80wFk":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 2 of 14 Topic: Wine, Beer, Whisky and Spices — Kiddush, the Shabbat Meals and Havdala Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 23 October 2018 Schedule note: No shiur the following week (half-term); the series resumed on 6 November. Recap Last week we introduced Shabbat's two elements — Zachor (the positive: honouring and enjoying the day) and Shamor (refraining from melacha) — and the importance of making Shabbat distinct from the six other days. The primary way we fulfil Zachor is Kiddush . Part A — Kiddush A biblical mitzvah, said over wine Reciting the text of Kiddush on Friday night fulfils the Torah mitzvah of זָכוֹר אֶת יוֹם הַשַּׁבָּת לְקַדְּשׁוֹ . The act of remembering is the text itself . Chazal added that it be said over a cup of wine , for two reasons: To mark the night as different — we eat a meal every night; beginning this one with Kiddush over wine sets Friday night apart. Zechirah is linked to wine — נַזְכִּירָה דֹדֶיךָ מִיַּיִן (Shir HaShirim 1:4), \"we will recall your love more than wine.\" Make Kiddush promptly The Shulchan Aruch rules that on coming home one should hasten to make Kiddush — we are to remember Shabbat as soon as it begins. Very relevant once the clocks go back: in midwinter Shabbat comes in around 3:30 pm and people may be home from shul at 4:30 yet wait until 6:30 or 7 to make Kiddush. If one genuinely cannot eat yet, waiting is fine — but where possible, make Kiddush early. Women are equally obligated Normally women are exempt from positive, time-bound mitzvot — and Kiddush is both. Yet because זכור ושמור בדבור אחד נאמרו , whoever is bound by the prohibitions of Shabbat (Shamor) is equally bound by its positive mitzvot (Zachor). So a woman's obligation in Kiddush is identical to a man's — and therefore: A husband who would rather not drink wine or grape juice that night may hear Kiddush from his wife without any problem. Kol isha: Chacham Ovadia Yosef rules that chanting the Kiddush is not singing — just as a woman may read the Megillah to its cantillation. How much must be drunk? The minimum is מְלֹא לֻגְמָיו — a cheekful , roughly 40–45 ml (about a quarter of a standard 200 ml plastic cup). A common, awkward case: you are a guest, and the host takes only a sip. Do you need your own Kiddush? The Shulchan Aruch cites the view that it suffices if anyone at the table — man or woman — drinks a cheekful. Ideally the one making Kiddush drinks it; if not, try to drink a cheekful yourself (if you won't seem greedy!). In real difficulty only: a view that it suffices if a cheekful is drunk between everyone combined . If nobody drinks the required amount, the Kiddush is void and must be said again. Listeners don't have to drink A common misconception. One who hears Kiddush and answers Amen has fulfilled the obligation and may eat — strictly, there is no need to taste the wine. Tasting is mitzvah min hamuvchar (praiseworthy), hence the custom to share it. At a crowded kiddush where you cannot reach the grape juice, you need not stand waiting — you may eat. Distributing the wine — kos pagum Wine already used for a mitzvah and drunk from is \"blemished\" ( pagum ) once transferred elsewhere, so pouring from the Kiddush cup into everyone's glasses after drinking is not correct. Three equally good options: Pass the cup around — everyone drinks from the same cup (simplest, least hygienic). It remains fit while in the same cup. Rectify the cup — after drinking, add a significant amount of fresh wine to the cup, then pour out to others. Pour first — pour into others' cups before drinking yourself. This is part of the mitzvah, so it is no interruption ( hefsek ) between bracha and drinking. Also fine: each guest has a pre-poured glass before Kiddush — you need not drink from the Kiddush cup itself, at home or in shul. Wine or grape juice? Red or white? Any wine may be used if fresh and without a bad smell. The Rambam disqualifies wine that has been processed — sweetened or yayin mevushal (pasteurised). Most Rishonim, and the Shulchan Aruch, permit it — but out of respect for the Rambam, if equally enjoyable, prefer non-mevushal wine . If the wine available is mevushal anyway , wine and grape juice are equally good . Red is preferred over white — some opinions require it to look like wine as well as taste like it. White is acceptable if there is no red. No wine or grape juice? The Rabbi asked the room — and Adrian gave the right answer: it depends when. When What to use Why Friday night Kiddush on the challah : wash, then say the full Kiddush with Hamotzi in place of Hagafen The long bracha Mekadesh HaShabbat makes it clearly distinct from any other meal Shabbat morning Chamar medina — the alcoholic drink of the country (in England: whisky, beer) Morning Kiddush is just Borei Pri HaGafen ; over bread it would look like an ordinary weekday meal Shabbat morning, nothing at all Bread Kiddush belongs with a meal Havdala Wine, or chamar medina — never bread Havdala is not tied to a meal We hold chamar medina must be alcoholic — not tea or Coca-Cola (some Ashkenazim are more lenient). Question: For Kiddush on challah on Friday night, do you wash first? — Yes , wash, then say Kiddush over the challah. Part B — The Shabbat Meals (Se'udot Shabbat) Why three meals? Chazal obligated three meals so that we actually enjoy Shabbat . Like booking an appointment with yourself or your family — without putting it in the diary, the break never happens; even on Shabbat one can get carried away and forget to enjoy it. The obligation can only be understood in that context: Chazal want us to be me'aneg et haShabbat . With bread All three meals should include bread — even a lavish gourmet meal without bread does not fulfil the institution. Two reasons: The source of three meals is the manna , which the Torah calls lechem . A meal with bread has kevi'ut — formality and importance: you wash, sit down, and gather. When you're not obliged to eat Because the meals exist for enjoyment, one who will not enjoy them need not force himself: A medical condition (e.g. cannot tolerate gluten or wheat). Someone who knows he will feel bloated and uncomfortable . Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach : even one merely uneasy that eating will harm his weight or health is not obligated. But if one can eat a kezayit of bread (just under 30 g) without discomfort, one should. Diets A diet for health — a strong case for exemption if the meals would undermine it. The Shulchan Aruch speaks of someone who fasts every day: he need not eat on Shabbat, as it would disrupt his routine. A diet only to be thinner , not for health — it is very questionable whether one may skip the meals for this. Kiddush bimkom se'udah — Kiddush must lead into a meal From וְקָרָאתָ לַשַּׁבָּת עֹנֶג (Yeshayahu 58:13), Chazal learn that where Kiddush is \"called\", there the meal must be. They should be closely joined : Kiddush at 5:30 with the meal at 7:30 is a problem — better to make Kiddush at 7 and eat straight away. Shul kiddush on Shabbat morning: do you need Kiddush again at home an hour later? You fulfil bimkom se'udah at the shul kiddush if you have: a revi'it of wine (just under 90 ml), or mezonot cake or crackers — a kezayit (just under 30 g). Just a sip of grape juice and then sweets is not enough — that Kiddush is void, and you have effectively eaten before Kiddush. Preferably don't do this; but if you see others doing so, there is no need to rebuke them. Questions from the floor Does the revi'it have to be wine, or any alcoholic drink? — It has to be wine , beyond the Kiddush amount. Can you drink it slowly? — It must be drunk within a short time (roughly 4–9 minutes; under 10). Drawn out, it loses significance. Francis: if you won't have a significant amount of food at the shul kiddush, should you partake? — Better not to ; a couple of pieces of cake or a little wine suffices. Does Shabbat morning need an extra cup? — No. May one drink before Kiddush on Shabbat morning? — Preferably not; but one who is very thirsty may drink water (e.g. before Musaf). Part C — Havdala What Havdala really is We think of Havdala as four brachot — in essence it is two : Borei Pri HaGafen and Hamavdil bein kodesh lechol . The Rambam holds that just as there is a Torah duty to remember Shabbat as it enters, there is an equal duty to remember it as it leaves — Havdala is as important as Kiddush. People careful with Kiddush should be equally careful with Havdala. Besamim and fire — not the essence Besamim — to revive the spirit as Shabbat departs; some feel downcast when Shabbat leaves. Fire — Adam HaRishon first made fire on Motzei Shabbat by striking two stones (Pesachim 54a); we commemorate it. Neither distinguishes Shabbat from the week, so: don't delay Havdala for them, and you are not obliged to go out and buy spices or a candle (\"you don't have to go to Tesco\"). If they are at hand, include them. Travelling straight after Shabbat Walking to a bus stop at the end of Shabbat is permitted (it is not preparation for after Shabbat), provided one carries nothing muktseh. But may one make Havdala before Shabbat ends? The Shulchan Aruch permits Havdala — without fire — from Plag HaMincha (about 1¼ hours before sunset). The poskim advise against it, for fear one will then forget it is still Shabbat. So: if you can make Havdala the next day (Sunday), wait. Only if you won't be able to make Havdala until Monday or Tuesday (e.g. a 20-hour flight) may you make it early — from Plag, without fire , and preferably without besamim. Going to sleep early in summer In midsummer Shabbat ends around 10:35 pm (Plag around 8 pm). A tired parent or a teenager wants to sleep at 9: Sephardim may make up Havdala on Sunday only (the \"Shabbat as the centre of the week\" idea from last week). The question of whether early Havdala (from Plag) or Sunday is better is debated; many argue that if one goes straight to sleep afterwards, making it early is better — especially for Sephardim. Women are equally obligated in Havdala — if not on Motzei Shabbat, then at least on Sunday morning. The same for children and teenagers. Dvar Torah — Parashat Vayera: Pursuing Chesed The shiur fell on 15 Cheshvan, exactly a month after Sukkot. One who built a sukkah but was rained out for all seven days rightly feels sorry — he wished to do a mitzvah when it was due. But a month later, no one sits wishing he could be in a sukkah, or eat matzah: there is no merit in yearning for a mitzvah out of its time. Why, then, does Avraham sit at the door of his tent on a scorching day — made hot precisely so that no travellers would trouble him — longing for guests when there is no one in need? Rav Moshe Feinstein explains that chesed is different from every other mitzvah. Other mitzvot we do when the opportunity arises. Chesed must be pursued — a person who never does kindness simply because no one around seems to need it is lacking. צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף — pursue righteousness. Hashem created the world only to do chesed , and we are to walk in His ways; so we must seek out opportunities to help, even when none present themselves. Summary Kiddush is a Torah mitzvah, said over wine and as early as possible; women are equally obligated and may make Kiddush for men. A cheekful (~45 ml) must be drunk — ideally by the one making Kiddush, otherwise by anyone; if no one does, repeat it. Avoid kos pagum : pass the cup, top it up, or pour before drinking. Mevushal wine and grape juice are equal; prefer non-mevushal wine if enjoyed; red over white . No wine: challah on Friday night; chamar medina on Shabbat morning; Havdala never on bread . The three meals are for enjoyment — with bread; no obligation where they cause real discomfort. Kiddush bimkom se'udah: follow Kiddush with a meal, a revi'it of wine or a kezayit of cake. Havdala = Hagafen + Hamavdil; besamim and fire are extras. Make it up by Sunday (Sephardim); early Havdala only in real need. Coming Up Shiur 3 — 6 November: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 1 — Bishul (cooking) Reheating food, how to serve food, stirring food on the fire Cooking in the sun ( toldot chamah ) What really is a kli sheni — and how to make a cup of tea The Rabbi also invited everyone to a kiddush on Shabbat Parashat Chayei Sarah (3 November) to celebrate the birth of his baby daughter. B'Ezrat Hashem","X4srKyFukW0":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 10 of 14 Topic: Tzad (Trapping), Shochet (Killing), Tofer and Kore'a (Sewing and Tearing) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 5 February 2019 Dedication: For the refuah shelemah of a member of the community, as announced in the recording. Schedule note: The course is a week behind; the final Q&A session will be used to complete the material. Remaining shiurim: 26 Feb, 5 Mar, 12 Mar, 26 Mar. Recap Last time: tying and untying, cleaning clothes, and (the week before) dyeing. Tonight: trapping, killing, and sewing/tearing. Part A — Tzad (Trapping) Background and two key principles The Mishkan's coverings included rams' skins and the skins of the tachash (now extinct) — animals that had to be trapped. The kind of creature: creatures commonly trapped for use (deer, birds such as parrots, fish, dogs) — trapping them is a Torah prohibition. Creatures trapped only to be rid of them (mice, rats, flies, mosquitoes) — only rabbinic . The enclosure: it is Torah-level only if the creature is confined where you can take hold of it easily, in a single lunge . A bird in a cage — trapped; a bird brought into a living room — only rabbinic (you would still have to chase it). A deer in a living room — easily caught in a moment or two, so trapped. Mousetraps Best: set the trap before Shabbat — it may catch mice on Shabbat. Setting it on Shabbat right in front of a mouse that goes straight in — Torah prohibition. Setting it where a mouse may enter later — only rabbinic . Since mice aren't trapped for use (rabbinic) and the trapping isn't immediate (rabbinic), it is twice removed — and with real discomfort (including emotional distress at mice in the house), Chacham Ovadia permits setting the trap yourself , not just via a non-Jew. Rats , especially with children around, are dangerous — certainly permitted. Poison traps set before Shabbat are fine. Closing a window on a butterfly A butterfly flew in; it turns cold and you want to shut the window. Trapping it is a pesik reisha — but in a room it is only rabbinic (too big to catch in one go), and you are acting because of discomfort , not to trap it. Pesik reisha bidrabanan bimkom tza'ar — permitted. Pets and domesticated animals A domesticated animal that accepts its owner's authority is already considered \"trapped\" — anyone may confine it. (Animals are generally muktseh — don't lift them; you may nudge them along, gently — see the Muktseh shiur.) A parrot or a newly bought dog that doesn't yet submit to its owner is not domesticated: if you open the cage, closing it re-traps it. Feed through the bars, or block the opening with your body. Leaving the house past such a dog: don't open the door wide and close it; slip out with your body blocking the gap. Trapping a person? A seriously discussed question — locking a child in a room, or a Jewish prison guard in Israel recapturing an escapee on Shabbat: The Tzitz Eliezer (Rav Eliezer Waldenberg): young children are under their parents' authority — like domesticated. Humans aren't creatures \"commonly trapped\", so at most rabbinic. (A dangerous criminal is a different matter entirely.) Deeper: whenever the Torah says \"take\" a person ( kach et Aharon …), Rashi comments קָחֵהוּ בִּדְבָרִים — take him with words . The Maharal explains: a person's essence is his mind. You can confine his body, but a prisoner in London can be thinking of Australia — his mind is free. So some argue there is no \"trapping\" of human beings at all. Wasps and mosquitoes Trapping them is only rabbinic. If one is on your skin or about to sting , Chazal waived the prohibition — you may trap it (e.g. under a cup). If it is just buzzing around the room, no. Killing it is not permitted — unless there is danger (an allergy; arguably near a baby). Part B — Shochet (Killing) In the Mishkan the trapped animals were slaughtered for their hides. Killing any creature is forbidden. Fly spray Spraying directly at insects — forbidden. Spraying around the room with the window open — permitted: they have an escape route, so their death is not inevitable. (\"The aim of philosophy,\" said Wittgenstein, \"is to show the fly the way out of the fly-bottle.\") Spraying a sealed room — forbidden. Insects in the toilet or the sink Flushing a toilet with insects in it — permitted by the poskim, for several reasons: Melacha she'einah tzrichah legufah — you aren't killing them to use them (only rabbinic). Death isn't immediate — they may drown later in the pipes, so it is indirect. Kavod habriyot — human dignity overrides rabbinic prohibitions. A sink with spiders: no human-dignity factor here, but if the tap water lands beside them (not directly on them) and they are carried off later, it is indirect and permitted. Don't aim at them, and don't fill the basin until they inevitably drown. Checking vegetables for insects Ideally check before Shabbat (mint, lettuce). On Shabbat: If it is not certain there are insects — permitted: a safek pesik reisha (doubtful inevitability). If it is visibly infested — forbidden; washing will inevitably kill them. Produce that is almost always infested (e.g. raspberries) — practically cannot be checked on Shabbat. Drawing blood Blood is the life-force ( כִּי הַדָּם הוּא הַנֶּפֶשׁ ), so drawing blood for use is a Torah prohibition: A blood test that can wait a few days — forbidden on Shabbat. Pinching hard enough to bring blood to the surface — forbidden, even with permission. Brushing teeth Without toothpaste: squeezing water from the bristles is not an issue. Bleeding gums: if you don't usually bleed, it isn't inevitable — permitted. If you almost always bleed, it is a pesik reisha — don't brush on Shabbat. No special Shabbat toothbrush needed; you may rinse the brush afterwards (it's for cleanliness, not preparing for the next day). Toothpaste: most poskim forbid it — memare'ach (smoothing). Chacham Ovadia permits it: smoothing is forbidden only when you want the smooth result (e.g. sealing a barrel with wax), not toothpaste that is immediately rinsed away. Prefer mouthwash or liquid toothpaste ; if genuinely stuck and uncomfortable, there is what to rely on. Floss — the same as brushing: only if you don't usually bleed. Part C — Tofer and Kore'a (Sewing and Tearing) The Mishkan's cloth panels were sewn together. Tofer = joining two items together — sewing, gluing paper, sellotaping , stapling . Kore'a = separating them. Pulling a loose stitch tight to close a gap in a tablecloth or shirt is re-sewing — forbidden. Buttons, safety pins, zips, Velcro Buttons — designed to open and close, so not sewing (like closing a door isn't building). Safety pins — likewise permitted, even for an extended period. Zips — permitted, e.g. zipping a hood onto a coat, even if left on for weeks. Velcro — more debated: avoid fastening it on Shabbat for more than seven days (e.g. new Velcro shoes fastened once and then slipped on and off). New socks joined by the manufacturer — may be cut apart with a knife; the join was never meant to be permanent. Opening post Business correspondence — muktseh; don't open it. Personal, non-business letters — may be opened by tearing the envelope destructively (not making a neat \"receptacle\"). Unsealing the flap neatly is hard to justify (the Ra'avad and others debate it). Toilet paper Tearing toilet paper along its perforation is mechatech (cutting to size). Pre-tear it before Shabbat. If you forgot: ask a non-Jew; if none is available, tear it with a shinui (e.g. between the elbows, or with the weaker hand) — permitted because of kavod habriyot . Don't deliberately aim for the perforation; if it happens to tear there, fine. Food packaging and bottles — to be discussed. Summary Tzad: Torah-level only for commonly-trapped creatures in a confined space. Mousetraps — before Shabbat, or on Shabbat if not trapping immediately. Closing a window on a butterfly is fine; domesticated pets may be confined; wasps on you may be trapped but not killed. Shochet: fly spray around an open-windowed room; flushing toilets fine; tap water beside, not on, insects; check vegetables only if not definitely infested; no drawing blood. Brushing teeth without toothpaste is fine (unless you usually bleed); toothpaste per Chacham Ovadia, but prefer mouthwash or liquid toothpaste. Tofer/Kore'a: no gluing, taping, stapling or tightening stitches. Buttons, safety pins and zips fine; avoid long-term Velcro. Tear personal envelopes open destructively; pre-tear toilet paper. Coming Up Shiur 11 — 26 February: Writing and building — cutting through cakes with writing on them, puzzles, CCTV, Lego, opening cans and bottles, umbrellas — and swimming on Shabbat. B'Ezrat Hashem","XAEODNiQZdI":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Moreh Nevuchim (Rambam), Chelek Aleph — Rabbi Chaim Makbili's Hebrew edition / Sefaria Chapters: I:20 — the terms רם and נשא ; I:21 — עבר (Hashem \"passing\"), introduction only Topic: Why the Rambam splits רם and נשא into separate halves of the chapter; being exalted versus being lifted up ; why נשא can also mean to forgive and to count; רם ונשא in davening and in Kaddish; a first glimpse of negative theology; reading Yeshayahu's vision with everything we have learned; and the groundwork for ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא : the story, the revision, and four questions to hold on to Schedule note: The Moreh continues every Tuesday through the winter. Chapter 21 is a major chapter and will take several shiurim; tonight covered only its introduction. The Rabbi asks everyone to listen to this whole shiur before next week , because next week goes straight into Chapter 21 and cannot be followed without this introduction. Recap — Chapters 18 and 19 Chapter 18: the three verbs קרב, נגש, נגע can mean physically approaching or touching, but can also describe intellectual or spiritual closeness . Used of Hashem, they always mean drawing closer to Him through understanding, prophecy, tefillah, or seeking refuge in Him; never physical proximity. Chapter 19: מלא can mean a physical filling or the completion of a period of time, but used of Hashem it always means perfection . מלא כל הארץ כבודו means the universe displays excellence, perfection and wisdom, through which we come to know Hashem. וכבוד ה' מלא את המשכן means the Mishkan was a setting in which one could experience the divine and see Hashem's glory. Chapter 20 — רם and נשא Why These Two Words, and Why Now? This continues the verse we keep returning to, the opening of Yeshayahu 6 : בשנת מות המלך עזיהו, ואראה את אדנ-י ישב על כסא רם ונשא, ושוליו מלאים את ההיכל. Chapter 19 took מלא from this verse. Chapter 20 now takes רם and נשא . Literally: elevated and lifted up . Part 1 — רם רם הוא מונח רב משמעי: משמעות רום המקום — physical elevation; ומשמעות רום המעלה, כלומר הגדולה והכבוד והעוז — elevation in status : greatness, honour, strength. Meaning 1 — physical height ותרם התבה מעל הארץ (this week's parashah, Noach) The ark was lifted above the earth by the floodwaters. A physical elevation. Meaning 2 — elevation of status הרימותי בחור מעם (Tehillim) \"I raised up a young man from the people\": David HaMelech. Hashem did not physically lift him; He made him important and made him king. יען אשר הרימותיך מתוך העפר (of Yeravam) and אשר הרימותיך מתוך העם (of Ba'asha) \"I raised you up\": not from a lower place to a higher one, but in status. A reminder: the verse actually says מן העפר , not מתוך העפר . As we said last week, the Rambam quotes verses from memory, so small variations creep in. רם used of Hashem וכל ביטוי של הרמה שנאמר על ה' — המשמעות השנייה זו. Whenever רם is used of Hashem, it must mean the second meaning. For example: רומה על השמים אלהים, על כל הארץ כבודך (Tehillim) \"Be exalted above the heavens, O God.\" It cannot be physical: as we learned last week, there is no difference in closeness to Hashem whether one is on earth or on the ninth sphere. David is asking Hashem to bring justice upon the wicked who have troubled him, so that people see that He is there. Through exacting justice, He will be seen to be in charge of the universe — that is the \"exaltation\". Part 2 — נשא Why does the Rambam split the chapter in two? Notice something. Until now, whenever the Rambam discussed several related verbs in one chapter, he grouped them together : Chapter 4: ראה, חזה, הביט — three kinds of seeing, but all seeing, treated together. Chapter 18: קרב, נגע, נגש — slightly different, but all physical or intellectual proximity, treated together. Here he divides the chapter: first רם, then נשא. Why? Because there is a fundamental difference between them: רם נשא Part of speech Adjective Verb Meaning Is high, elevated, exalted To lift up : to move something from low to high Source of the height Intrinsic — רם בעצמו From someone else — נשא על ידי אחרים The Malbim says this explicitly (see below). And every verse the Rambam brings for נשא is a verse of lifting up . This is why review matters. You can only see what the Rambam is doing here if you remember how he handled Chapters 4 and 18. Everything ties into everything else. Those listening on YouTube: go over the shiurim. The two meanings of נשא משמעות של רום המקום — physical lifting, from lower to higher; ומשמעות של רום המעלה והגדולה והתרוממות המעלה — raising in status: taking someone low and bringing him up. Meaning 1 — physical: וישאו את שברם על חמריהם (Mikeitz) The brothers lifted their grain from the ground onto their donkeys. Meaning 2 — status: ותנשא מלכתו (Balak) Bilam's curse-turned-blessing: there will be times when Israel's kingdom is low, in exile, far down — but it will always be lifted up again. Not physically: its status rises, from low to high. וינטלם וינשאם כל ימי עולם (Yeshayahu) Hashem will always take us up and raise our status. ומדוע תתנשאו על קהל ה'? (Korach) Korach's complaint: כי כל העדה כלם קדשים ובתוכם ה' — we are all on the same level, all holy. So why do you lift yourselves up above everyone? Why נשא means so many things The Rabbi has long wondered how one root, נ־ש־א , can mean so many different things: Meaning Example To lift up ותנשא מלכתו To forgive נשא עון ופשע וחטאה ונקה (said again and again on Yom Kippur; Chapter 21) To count נשא את ראש בני גרשון (Naso) Perhaps also to appoint (the Rabbi couldn't recall an example offhand) His suggestion: the core of נשא is taking something from low to high . When you count or appoint someone, you lift them up conceptually: you give them significance. When you forgive someone, they were low in your books, and you raise them up in your mind. So all the meanings are one meaning: moving from low to high — unlike רם, which is already high. נשא used of Hashem וכל לשון נשיאה המיוחסת לו יתעלה — פירושו כזה. Whenever נשא is used of Hashem, it must mean the second meaning, status. הנשא שופט הארץ, השב גמול על גאים (Tehillim; said every morning) Does it mean \"God, raise Yourself from low to high in space\"? No. At the moment it looks as though there is no justice, as though God has abandoned the world. Judge the earth, bring justice to the wicked — and so You will be raised up , conceptually, from low to high in status. כי כה אמר רם ונשא — Why Both Together? כי כה אמר רם ונשא, שכן עד וקדוש שמו: מרום וקדוש אשכון, ואת דכא ושפל רוח (Yeshayahu 57) רוממות וגדולה ועוז, לא עילוי מקום. Status, not place. But if רם and נשא are fundamentally different, why does the verse put them together? Notice: whenever the two appear together, רם always comes first. In Shacharit, just before the Amidah: על זאת שבחו אהובים ורוממו לאל, ונתנו ידידים זמירות שירות ותשבחות, ברכות והודאות, למלך אל חי וקים, רם ונשא, גדול ונורא... The meaning: Hashem is רם , intrinsically exalted. And He is also נשא : He becomes lifted up , at least in our minds , through our worship and service of Him. Obviously Hashem does not need our prayers. But when we pray, we lift Him up in our own understanding; asking someone for something is an acknowledgement of them. And in Kaddish: יתברך וישתבח ויתפאר ויתרומם ויתנשא ויתהדר... ויתרומם — You are already up. ויתנשא — our job is to \"carry You up\", from low to high. Hashem needs no one to carry Him; but conceptually, in our minds, He is both: רם in Himself and נשא through us. The Rabbi's illustration: \"Some are born great, and some have greatness thrust upon them.\" רם is born to greatness. נשא has greatness thrust upon it — lifted up by others. Hashem's true essence is beyond anything we can understand, but in our minds, at least, we can lift Him up. A Paragraph on Negative Theology (Developed Much Later) The Rambam closes the chapter with one paragraph that he will develop at length at the end of Section 1 : שמא יקשו עליך דברי — רוממות, מעלה, גדולה ועוז — ותאמר: כיצד זה נותן אתה משמעויות רבות למשמעות אחת? The question: I have defined רם and נשא using several words: exaltedness, elevation, greatness, strength . How can one word have four definitions? עוד יבואר לך שהשם יתעלה, אצל המשיגים השלמים, אין לו תארים, ושכל התארים המרובים הללו האמורים עליו... כולם חוזרים למשמעות אחת, ומשמעות זו היא עצמותו, ולא שום דבר חוץ מזה. אך המטרה בפרק הזה אינה אלא שאין פירושם [של רם ונשא] מקום אלא מעלה. The answer: those who truly understand Hashem know that He has no attributes . All the many descriptions used of Him come back to one thing: His essence , and nothing outside it. I will explain this later. My only aim in this chapter is to show that רם and נשא mean status, not place. An example of the Introduction's fifth reason This is a case of what the Rambam told us in the Introduction : of the seven reasons an author may appear to contradict himself, one (the Rabbi thought the fifth) is that he teaches something not fully precise in order to make a point, and clarifies it later . The Rambam's purpose in these first fifty-odd chapters is to remove corporeality from the problematic terms. So for now he uses rich language (רוממות, גדולה, עוז) to drive the point home, knowing he will refine it later. He cannot give everything in one chapter. Negative theology in a sentence Later we will learn that no word truly describes Hashem , because the finite human mind can never describe the infinite. However many words we use — גדול, גבור, נורא, רחום וחנון, ארך אפים — they do not describe Him. When we say אל רחום וחנון , what we can actually grasp is that He is not not merciful . We can understand negatives, because negatives are limits, and limits are within our grasp. Positive attributes of God are unlimited and undefined , and a finite mind, however clever, cannot hold them. Don't worry if that isn't fully clear yet; we will go into it in great detail later. For now: don't be troubled that I am using many words for one verb. They all come back to one thing, His essence, His unity. Chapter 20 — Summary Continuing from מלא in Chapter 19, the Rambam explains רם and נשא . Both can mean physical height or elevation of status , and used of Hashem both always mean status, never place. But the Rambam splits the chapter in two , unlike his earlier groupings, because רם is an adjective — intrinsically high — while נשא is a verb — lifted from low to high by someone else ; hence נשא can also mean to forgive and to count. Hashem is רם in Himself and נשא in our minds through our worship: hence רם ונשא , ויתרומם ויתנשא , always with רם first. And the many words the Rambam uses to define these terms will later be shown to refer back to one thing, Hashem's essence , which no attribute can describe. Revision — Reading Yeshayahu 6:1 with Twenty Chapters Behind Us Before starting Chapter 21, the Rabbi stopped to read the verse we have been circling, to show why review matters: בשנת מות המלך עזיהו, ואראה את אדנ-י ישב על כסא רם ונשא, ושוליו מלאים את ההיכל. How many of its words have we now studied in the Moreh? Avi: six. The Rabbi: \"Very good.\" # Word Chapter Meaning with Hashem 1 ואראה 4 Intellectual seeing, a prophetic perception 2 ישב 11 Permanence, stability, constancy 3 כסא 9 Majesty; His involvement and השגחה 4 רם 20 Intrinsically exalted 5 ונשא 20 Lifted up — in the prophet's mind 6 מלאים 19 Perfection The Malbim on this verse goes through it exactly this way, drawing on the Moreh: פעל ראיה לה' מורה ראית השכל , as in Kohelet: ולבי ראה הרבה חכמה ודעת — the heart doesn't see; it perceives. (Moreh, Chapter 4.) ופעל ישב נאמר עליו — הוא שלא ישתנה; הישיבה מורה קביעות והנחה על עניין אחד. (Chapter 11.) והכסא מורה על הנהגה והשגחה, ונקרא השמים כסא ה' — the heavens are His throne because through them one sees His השגחה in the world. (Chapter 9.) רם ונשא — רוצה לומר: רם בעצמו, ונשא על ידי אחרים, שהם המשיגים שהוא נעלה על כל עילוי ורוממות. (Chapter 20 — exactly the distinction made in this shiur.) And ושוליו מלאים את ההיכל (the Malbim doesn't say it, but it follows from Chapter 19): we haven't yet defined שוליו or היכל , but the Heichal was filled with perfection , the third meaning of מלא. Before the Moreh , we read the verse like simpletons: Uzziyahu died; I saw God sitting on a raised-up chair, and the hem of His robe was filling the Temple. Now it reads: I had an intellectual vision of Hashem — enduring, permanent; on a throne — in majesty, with His involvement and השגחה; intrinsically exalted, and through this He was lifted up in my mind; and I saw the Heichal filled with perfection. One verse, six words, twenty chapters — and the whole verse changes. Chapter 21 — עבר: Introduction (Part 1) Chapter 21 deals with the verb עבר , to pass. It is not a long chapter, but there is a great deal to discuss, and to appreciate it we need to revise and go back to the Torah. It's a shame we didn't learn it before Yom Kippur, because it is about the verse we said 26 times on Yom Kippur , and throughout the forty days of Selichot, and on Mondays and Thursdays (and, for Sephardim, every day): ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא: ה' ה' אל רחום וחנון... The Story (Shemot 33–34) After the sin of the golden calf , Moshe secures forgiveness. Then: ויאמר: הראני נא את כבדך. (33:18) \"Show me Your glory.\" We haven't yet defined כבוד , but we know ראה with Hashem means intellectual seeing. So Moshe is asking to grasp something of Hashem intellectually. ויאמר: לא תוכל לראות את פני, כי לא יראני האדם וחי. (33:20) From Hashem's reply we learn that \"show me Your glory\" amounted to \"show me Your face\" — intellectually. What that means, we don't know yet. ויאמר ה': הנה מקום אתי, ונצבת על הצור. (33:21) Revision: in this eight-word verse, three words we have already explained: Word Chapter Meaning מקום 8 An intellectual position or standing, not a geographical place ונצבת 15 Permanence: stand constantly, firmly הצור 16 The origin and source of all existence So: You asked to see My face — that I cannot give you. But there is an intellectual standing with Me that I can give you: stand constantly on the Rock — continually contemplate the origins of everything, the universe, and God. Nothing new so far. והיה בעבר כבדי, ושמתיך בנקרת הצור, ושכתי כפי עליך עד עברי. והסרתי את כפי, וראית את אחרי, ופני לא יראו. (33:22–23) Here is the new word: When My glory passes , I will put you in the cleft of the rock , and cover you with My palm until I have passed . Then I will take away My palm, and you will see My back , but My face will not be seen. What does \"passing\" mean with Hashem? What is the cleft, the palm, the back? That is the subject of Chapter 21 , and by its end these verses will make sense. Then Moshe carves the second luchot: וישכם משה בבקר ויעל אל הר סיני כאשר צוה ה' אתו, ויקח בידו שני לחת אבנים. (34:4) וירד ה' בענן, ויתיצב עמו שם, ויקרא בשם ה'. (34:5) Revision again — two words we know: וירד (Chapter 10): not a physical descent, but Hashem resting His Shechinah . ויתיצב (Chapters 13/15): continuity, permanence . So Hashem rested His presence with Moshe, and it was a permanent presence; and he called in the name of Hashem. ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא: ה' ה' אל רחום וחנון, ארך אפים ורב חסד ואמת... (34:6) What does it mean that Hashem \"passed\"? Four Questions to Hold On To As we learn Chapter 21, keep these four questions in mind. We will return to all four at the end of the chapter. 1. What does עבר mean when used of Hashem? — in ויעבר ה' , and earlier in והיה בעבר כבדי . 2. Whose face? — ויעבר ה' על פניו : is it Moshe's face, or Hashem's own \"face\"? It could be either. (If it is Hashem's own face, we will have to understand what that means.) 3. Where does the colon go? Ask 99% of davening Jews and they will read: ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא: ה' ה' אל רחום וחנון... Hashem passed before his face and called: \"Hashem, Hashem, merciful and gracious God…\" But it can also be read — and we will see this is the Rambam's preferred reading : ויעבר ה' על פניו, ויקרא ה': ה' אל רחום וחנון... Hashem passed before his face, and Hashem called : \"Hashem, merciful and gracious God…\" — the colon comes after the first ה' . 4. How do we count the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy? This has halachic implications: The Ben Ish Chai writes that when you say the Thirteen Attributes, you should count them as you go: ה' (one), ה' (two), אל (three)… Chacham Ovadia says don't , because you would be entering a major machloket Rishonim about how to count them. Is the first ה' one of the thirteen? Is the second? What Comes Next Before answering any of this, the Rambam gives five meanings of the word עבר . Four of the five are needed to understand the chapter, so they must be understood clearly; that should take the first twenty minutes or so of next week's shiur. Then come three readings of the verse — which are really four — each using a different meaning of עבר: the Rambam's preferred reading first , then others he accepts but does not prefer. In each reading, the answers to the four questions change. (The Rabbi expects a chart to help lay it out.) Summary of the Shiur Chapter 20: רם and נשא both mean physical height or elevation of status, and of Hashem always status. The Rambam separates them because רם is intrinsic height and נשא is being lifted up by another ; hence נשא also means to forgive and to count, and hence Hashem is רם in Himself and נשא in our minds through our worship. The many words used to define them all point back to Hashem's essence , a theme the Rambam will develop as negative theology . Revision: Yeshayahu 6:1 now reads, word by word, through six chapters of the Moreh. Chapter 21 (introduction): Moshe's request הראני נא את כבדך , Hashem's answer הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור , the cleft, the palm, the back, וירד ה' בענן ויתיצב עמו שם , and ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא — with four questions: what is עבר , whose face , where the colon goes , and how to count the Thirteen Attributes . Coming Up Next week: Chapter 21 — the five meanings of עבר , then the three (really four) readings of ויעבר ה' על פניו ויקרא , and the answers to the four questions. Essential preparation: listen to this whole shiur before next week. The Rabbi will also post a reminder on the group. \"I've been giving shiurim for many, many years — this is essential.\" Keep reviewing the earlier chapters; everything ties together. (The recording ends as Mark begins a question about Chapter 19.) B'Ezrat Hashem","XvarMjmuZUk":"Dayan Daniel Kada · Thursday Night Halacha · Shiur 6 of 7 Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 676, 678, 679, 680 and 681 (677 held over for the final shiur) Where We Are Siman Topic 670 What is forbidden on Hanukah: eulogies, fasts, work 671 Positioning the menorah; lighting in the synagogue 672 The correct time 673 Fuels 674 Lighting from one light to the next 675 The lighting is the mitzvah, not the placing Tonight: as much as possible of the remaining chapters , with the rest on the following Monday, \"just in time to come to Hanukah, knowing all of the halachot of Hanukah together.\" Siman 676: The Order of the Berachot and the Lighting Seif 1: The three berachot On the first night: להדליק נר (של) חנוכה , שעשה נסים לאבותינו , שהחיינו . If Sheheḥeyanu was missed on the first night, say it on the second, third, even the eighth night . Recap and a new point: \"נר חנוכה\" vs. \"נר של חנוכה\" The original text (Gemara, Rishonim, Rambam) is להדליק נר של חנוכה . That is what Ashkenazim and the Spanish & Portuguese say, and what the original Sephardim said. Most Sephardi siddurim today say להדליק נר חנוכה , because of the Arizal : the initials ל-נ-ח spell נחל , a stream, which draws down a stream of shefa (Divine abundance). The Ḥida's question: if so, why does everyone, including the women lighting Shabbat candles, say להדליק נר של שבת ? The answer: Shabbat has many aspects : the candles, the food, the dress. So \"the light of Shabbat\" is one aspect among many. Hanukah's whole essence is the light. נר and חנוכה are synonymous, so נר חנוכה fits. Middle Eastern Sephardim say נר חנוכה , but the original Sephardi custom was נר של חנוכה . \"Those should follow the minhag, whatever it is.\" \"בזמן הזה\" or \"ובזמן הזה\"? Ashkenazim and almost all Sephardim: בימים ההם בזמן הזה : miracles for our ancestors in those days , thousands of years ago, at this season , on 25 Kislev. Spanish & Portuguese and some older texts: בימים ההם ובזמן הזה , \"in those days and at this time,\" which can mean that we pray and hope for miracles now as well . Follow your custom. Absent one, the general practice is בזמן הזה . Forgot Sheheḥeyanu all eight nights: can you say it on the eighth day? On the eighth morning someone realises they never said Sheheḥeyanu. Too late? What is Sheheḥeyanu on: the festival, or the mitzvah of lighting? - On the festivals , the Gemara is clear: Sheheḥeyanu is on the day itself . On Yom Kippur we say it in shul, not over a candle. On Yom Tov, if there was no Kiddush, you'd still say it. On Sukkot, if you forgot until Ḥol HaMoed, you say it then and there, walking down the street . - Some poskim applied this to Hanukah: the berachah is on the special time, merely attached to the lighting, so you could say it on the eighth day. - We do not hold this. A ḥag has kedushah : it is from the Torah, a regel , with tosefet (adding to its sanctity). Hanukah is not a ḥag. It is a holy time in our calendar, but the Sheheḥeyanu is on the new mitzvah , which comes only once a year. The main halacha: no Sheheḥeyanu on the eighth day without lighting, though one who did say it \"would have what to rely on.\" \"Ḥag Ḥanukah Same'aḥ\"? An aside \"What really bothers me: people who wish each other 'Ḥag Ḥanukah Same'aḥ'... It's a complete misuse of the word ḥag. It's not a ḥag.\" A ḥag is one of the three regalim: Pesaḥ, Shavuot, Sukkot. Even Rosh HaShanah is imprecise: it is not a ḥag, nor a time of simḥah. Say \"a good Yom Tov,\" \"Shabbat Shalom,\" or anything else. When the Rabbi calls Hanukah a \"festival,\" he means it \"in a very loose sense: a special time in our calendar.\" Seif 2: Finish all the berachot, then light On the following nights you say two berachot. Say all the berachot before beginning to light. Why does this need saying? Because there is an opinion that you should start lighting immediately after the first berachah , \"to light,\" and say שעשה נסים while lighting. Its logic: שעשה נסים is not intrinsic to the act of lighting. It relates to seeing the lights (we will see that someone who only sees a menorah says it). The custom of Beit El and the mekubalim , brought by the Kaf HaḤayim and the Ḥida , and followed by many Sephardim, was to start lighting straight after the first berachah. The Rabbi: \"Unless you are a kabbalist... it seems better halachically to wait to complete the berachot.\" While saying שעשה נסים and שהחיינו your mind should be focused, not \"mucking around with the wicks.\" The main halacha for both Ashkenazim and Sephardim: finish the berachot, then light. Forgot the berachah until after lighting the first light? Fourth night: you lit the first light and realise you never said להדליק . Normally, once a mitzvah is done, it's too late for its berachah. A class member: the first light is the night's mitzvah, and it's done. The others are only hiddur. The Rabbi: but hiddur is still part of it. You may still say the berachah , because as long as you are engaged in the mitzvah , even its extra parts, you can bless. Parallels: - Arba minim: you're told to hold the etrog upside down for the berachah, but people forget and pick everything up together. The mitzvah is technically done, yet you still bless , on the waving and the circuits, all part of one big mitzvah. - Tefillin: the mitzvah is tying the shel yad. You should bless first, but if you tied it and then blessed, it's fine; the remaining straps are still part of the mitzvah. An aside on tefillin: the Shulḥan Arukh rules explicitly that you tie the shel yad, immediately put on the shel rosh without interruption, and only then wind the seven straps, which are not intrinsic to the mitzvah. \"In practice, I don't know any community which does this. Even the Spanish and Portuguese.\" The Arizal said to wind the seven straps first, and that spread. Seif 3: Birkat HaRo'eh, the berachah on seeing Someone who has not lit and has no one lighting for them , and sees a Hanukah menorah, says שעשה נסים (and on the first night שהחיינו ), but not להדליק , since they are not lighting. Who qualifies? The Rambam The Rashba, the Ran (the halacha) The condition Simply not lighting yourself that night Not lighting AND no one lighting for your home Example A Sephardi yeshivah student whose parents light at home A traveller in a hotel that forbids lighting, single, with no household lighting for them On yeshivah students: a Sephardi student financially supported by his parents (they pay for the yeshivah, food and plane tickets) is part of their household and does not light . \"When I was in yeshivah, I didn't light a menorah. I relied on my parents.\" By the Rambam , seeing Ashkenazi friends light, he would say the berachah of seeing. The Rashba and Ran disagree : if your home is lit for you, it is as if you have lit. The Beit Yosef writes that the Rambam reads the Gemara more precisely, but rules like the Rashba and Ran . So the situation is rare: you would need to have no way to light and no household lighting for you . Continuing the case: that traveller said שעשה נסים and שהחיינו on seeing a menorah on the first night. On the second night , back home, he lights his own and does not repeat שהחיינו . Earlier we concluded you cannot say Sheheḥeyanu on the day itself without lighting, but in association with seeing the lights you can , and he has. The husband who comes home at 1 a.m. on the first night The first night is a big work event; he gets home at midnight or 1 a.m. As discussed, if he'll be home after 8 or 9 pm, his wife or children light at the proper time and he fulfils the mitzvah through them. On the second night he comes home in time and lights himself for the first time . Does he say Sheheḥeyanu? Ruling Mishnah Berurah (Seif Katan 16) No. His wife did it on his behalf the night before Ḥacham Ovadia Yes , on the basis of a triple doubt (ספק ספקא) Ḥacham Ovadia's reasoning: 1. Some poskim hold that since he did not actively perform the mitzvah, he says Sheheḥeyanu the next night. 2. A further opinion supports saying it. (The Rabbi was not entirely clear on this point.) 3. A general principle worth knowing: ספק ברכות להקל does not really apply to Sheheḥeyanu. It is a berachah of personal thanksgiving and happiness , so we are more lenient in saying it. Ruling (Ḥacham Ovadia): say Sheheḥeyanu on the second night in this case. Seif 4: הנרות הללו After lighting , we say הנרות הללו אנו מדליקין על התשועות ועל הנסים ועל הנפלאות... The Gemara does not mention it. It comes from Massechet Soferim (8th–9th century), \"not part of the Talmud... a much later addition,\" printed at the end of one of the tractates. Two texts exist. The Arizal required exactly 36 words , corresponding to the 36 lights of Hanukah (excluding shamashim), and changed the text accordingly. The old Spanish & Portuguese siddur and many old Sephardi siddurim ( Syria, Iraq ) have the earlier text. The Syrian tune: \"When I was younger and used to be particular about these things,\" the Rabbi would try to fit the Arizal's 36 words into the special Syrian melody , but it never worked. \"Then I realised, actually, it's the wrong thing to do. You follow the minhag, and just say what your father said , even if it's not in line with the Arizal.\" A participant noted that their siddur cites the Maharam of Rothenburg as the source of the alternative version. The Rabbi: that is the Ashkenazi version (with הנרות הללו itself, more than 36 words). The point is that before the Arizal there were various texts, and the Arizal specified 36. Timing matters: say הנרות הללו only after the first light has been lit. \"What I sometimes see, and you should be careful with: as soon as people finish the berachot they start saying הנרות הללו. That's wrong.\" The berachah must take effect on at least one light first. הנרות הללו is a lovely post-Talmudic text, not part of the berachot, and it would be an interruption between the berachah and lighting the first light. Seif 5: Which direction? Picture your menorah. On the first night you light the light on the right . Each night you add a light to its left . Sephardim (Shulḥan Arukh), all Sephardim The Vilna Gaon (some Ashkenazim) Start with The new light , the leftmost of the night The same first light every night , the one nearest the doorway Direction Left to right Right to left Reasoning 1) Every turn should be toward the right . 2) Bless on the new light : a new night, a new miracle Why bless on a hiddur light rather than the main one? And the berachah should be on the light nearest the doorway ( סמוך לפתח ), the more important mitzvah. Moving right is a principle, but a greater principle is to bless on the main light Kenneth confirmed that some Ashkenazim always begin with the same light each night. Siman 678: When Money Is Short נר ביתו (נר של שבת) קודם לנר חנוכה, משום שלום בית. The Shabbat light takes precedence over the Hanukah light, because of peace in the home. If you cannot afford, or cannot find, fuel for both, buy Shabbat candles. Why not one candle for both? (A participant suggested אין עושין מצוות חבילות חבילות, not bundling mitzvot together. True, \"but much simpler.\") Their purposes are opposite. The Shabbat light is for benefit : you enjoy it and eat by it, which was its whole purpose before electricity. The Hanukah light may not be used . Obviously: if you can afford one Shabbat candle and one Hanukah light, or two Shabbat candles, light one of each. Two Shabbat candles are nice, but one fulfils the mitzvah . The Mishnah Berurah's puzzle (work it out yourself) You have one Shabbat candle and one Hanukah light, and money for one more . It's the second or third night. A second Shabbat candle, or an extra Hanukah light? Mark: the extra Hanukah light. The Shabbat candle already provides shalom bayit, and the extra Hanukah light is hiddur. Exactly. The hiddur of Hanukah is in the Gemara ; nowhere does it say to light two Shabbat candles . That is a much later custom. Paradoxically: one Shabbat candle comes before one Hanukah light, but after that, Hanukah lights come before a second Shabbat candle. \"But Shabbat is more frequent (tadir)!\" Tadir decides only which to do first when you do both. Here you are doing both mitzvot. A good point raised: even if you can afford only a second light on the eighth night, there is still value in lighting extra lights, even short of the full number. Shabbat candles or Kiddush wine? (from Hilchot Shabbat, two years earlier) Maria suggested Kiddush , since it is d'oraita. The Rabbi: \"Your logic is sound, but don't forget: you can make Kiddush on bread .\" So buy Shabbat candles and make Kiddush on the ḥallah. Hanukah or Kiddush wine? יקנה לנר חנוכה משום פרסומי ניסא. Having secured Shabbat candles, spend the rest on Hanukah lights , not wine; make Kiddush on bread. The Rema: Hanukah lights also come before Havdalah wine . The full hierarchy: 1. Shabbat candle 2. Hanukah light 3. Wine for Kiddush 4. Wine for Havdalah \"Even though of all these, Kiddush is the most important , Kiddush has an alternative .\" The Mishnah Berurah's caveat: all this assumes you have bread. With no bread for Kiddush, spend the money on grape juice. Siman 679: Friday Afternoon: Which Candles First? בערב שבת מדליקין נר חנוכה תחלה, ואחר כך נר שבת. On Friday, light the Hanukah lights first, then the Shabbat candles. \"Everyone does this, but it's actually not simple at all.\" Behind it: the Ba'al Halachot Gedolot (Behag, about 1,200 years ago), the source of the custom for women to light Shabbat candles and then say the berachah: - Shabbat begins at sunset, or from pelag haminḥah whenever a person explicitly accepts it ; after that, melachah is forbidden (at least rabbinically, possibly biblically; even then only a negative prohibition, not the full Shabbat penalty). - The Behag: lighting Shabbat candles is itself an acceptance of Shabbat. So light Hanukah first , or you would have accepted Shabbat and could no longer light. Position Ashkenazim Follow the Behag : lighting Shabbat candles accepts Shabbat Sephardim (Shulḥan Arukh) Do not. Lighting Shabbat candles does not necessarily accept Shabbat, especially if one has in mind not to The Rashba Rejects the Behag, and says Shabbat candles first , because they are more frequent (tadir) So why does the Shulḥan Arukh rule Hanukah first? The Ba'ḥ explains with a principle worth remembering: When there is a dispute in halacha, even if you don't hold like that opinion, if it's easy to satisfy it and it makes no significant difference, do it. Why enter into a dispute? The Rashba's tadir argument is counterbalanced ( many disagreed with him, since there are considerations on both sides, such as pirsumei nisa), which leaves the Behag. We don't follow the Behag, but what's wrong with lighting Hanukah first? If you lit Shabbat candles first by mistake (Shabbat has not yet begun): Ruling Sephardim , men or women Light the Hanukah lights afterwards. Absolutely fine Ashkenazim , a man May still light Ashkenazim , a woman Should not light (her husband, or someone else, can). If she is alone with no one to light for her, she does not light Enough fuel, and Minḥah The berachah is said even in daylight, since it is from pelag haminḥah . The fuel must last until half an hour after nightfall , about an hour and a half , which is a problem with ordinary small candles. At least one light should last that long. The Mishnah Berurah (Seif Katan 2): \"some Aḥaronim\" say to pray Minḥah early , then light Hanukah and Shabbat candles, then go to shul for Kabbalat Shabbat. The first to say this was the Pri Megadim, about 300 years ago. In the Rishonim's time the prevalent custom was to light at home, come to shul, light there and pray Minḥah. The Pri Megadim's concern: praying the previous day's Minḥah after lighting for the following night looks like a contradiction. Some went so far as to say it is better to pray Minḥah alone than after lighting. The Rabbi: very hard to accept. Minyan is a far older concept, and this idea is \"300 years old at most.\" It is a good thing to pray early when you can (that year, with Boxing Day on the Thursday , perhaps many would be off work on Friday), but in a normal year it is fine to light at home and then pray Minḥah. It isn't really a contradiction: \"I'm lighting it now in preparation for the night which is coming in half an hour's time,\" only because I can't light on Shabbat. Siman 680: Protect the Lights on Friday Night צריך ליזהר... שיניח דבר מפסיק בין הנרות לפתח : put something between the lights and the door, so that opening and closing it won't inevitably blow them out. This applies on weekdays too (don't leave a menorah where it will certainly go out), but on Shabbat it is more severe, since extinguishing is a Shabbat prohibition. It concerns a פסיק רישא , where extinguishing is inevitable or very likely. An unexpected gust when you open the door carries no responsibility from either the Hanukah or the Shabbat perspective. \"Use your common sense.\" The old door-holder: some used to set the light in a holder on the door itself . On Shabbat that is impossible: each time the door moves, the oil moves toward or away from the flame, raising muktzeh and extinguishing or kindling issues. (There is an opinion based on פסיק רישא דלא ניחא ליה , but \"it's not really relevant.\") Siman 681: Motza'ei Shabbat: Havdalah or Menorah First? אין מבדילין בנר חנוכה, שאין נהנים לאורו. One may not use the Hanukah light for Havdalah, since one may not benefit from it. Why? (from Hilchot Havdalah, learned about a year before) בורא מאורי האש is a berachah of praise , commemorating the benefit of fire given to Adam HaRishon . You may only say it when you are benefiting from the light . The Hanukah light may not be used, so it can't serve for Havdalah. In the synagogue: menorah first Light the menorah, then Havdalah. Normally we try not to do melachah until real Havdalah (even after אתה חוננתנו ), and Havdalah is more frequent. But because of pirsumei nisa , and because after Havdalah everyone leaves, the custom is to light first . This is the universal custom in synagogues . At home: both are acceptable For Havdalah first For the menorah first Tadir : Havdalah is more frequent Pirsumei nisa : \"a beautiful mitzvah, a special mitzvah\" Better not to do melachah before Havdalah At home it isn't public \"There's no correct answer here.\" דעביד כמר עביד, ודעביד כמר עביד : whichever you follow is fine. The general Sephardi practice , in the Rabbi's experience: Havdalah first at home, then the menorah . Summary 676:1: three berachot. The original text is נר של חנוכה . The Arizal's נר חנוכה spells נחל , and differs from נר של שבת because Hanukah's whole essence is the light. בזמן הזה (most) vs. ובזמן הזה (S&P). No Sheheḥeyanu on the eighth day without lighting: it is on the mitzvah , and Hanukah is not a ḥag . (Please don't say \"Ḥag Ḥanukah Same'aḥ.\") 676:2: finish the berachot, then light (not the kabbalistic custom). Forgot the berachah until after the first light? You can still say it while engaged in the mitzvah (compare arba minim and tefillin). 676:3: the berachah on seeing applies only to someone with no way to light and no household lighting for them (Rashba, Ran, Beit Yosef). A husband whose family lit for him on the first night says Sheheḥeyanu on the second (Ḥacham Ovadia's triple doubt; Sheheḥeyanu is personal joy). 676:4: הנרות הללו is from Massechet Soferim . Say it only after the first light is lit . Follow your family's text, 36 words (Arizal) or not. 676:5: Sephardim begin with the new light and go left to right . The Gra: always begin with the light nearest the door. 678: Shabbat candle → Hanukah light → Kiddush wine → Havdalah wine. After one Shabbat candle and one Hanukah light, extra Hanukah lights beat a second Shabbat candle . 679: Hanukah lights before Shabbat candles , to avoid the Behag's dispute \"when it's easy.\" Lit Shabbat candles first? Sephardim: still light Hanukah. Ashkenazi women: not. Pray Minḥah early if possible, but lighting before Minḥah is fine. 680: keep the lights from being inevitably blown out by the door on Shabbat. 681: no Havdalah on the Hanukah light . In shul: menorah, then Havdalah. At home: either, and Sephardim usually Havdalah first . Coming Up The final shiur, on Monday evening, 6:30–7:30 (not the usual 6:00): 682 (Al HaNissim), 683 (Hallel) and 684 (the Torah reading), each short, followed by 677 , the big chapter on guests and travellers over Hanukah . A vote: for the new year (starting in January), Hilchot Purim , which can be completed in two months, or Hilchot Pesaḥ , which is huge and could not be finished in three months, \"but it's Pesaḥ.\"","Yoxu_YrjBek":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek (Commentary on the Mishnah, Sanhedrin ch. 10), opening Shiur: Part 1 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 2 September 2025 These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. The Fourth and Final Introduction Introduction Focus When To Avot ( Shemonah Perakim ) A person's moral and ethical growth , the soul Last year To the Mishnah Legal : the Oral Torah, its transmission, the Sages' authority, the Mishnah's structure The past few months To the Mishneh Torah Legal : transmission; the authority of the Sanhedrin and the Gemara Short To Perek Helek The philosophical and theological underpinnings of the Torah , and the source of the 13 Principles Now Perek Helek is the final or penultimate chapter of Sanhedrin (the penultimate in the Rambam's version), named for its opening: who has a חלק , a portion, in the World to Come. Since the Mishnah raises who has and hasn't a portion, the Rambam takes the opening to discuss what Olam HaBa is , and the actions and beliefs that include or exclude a person, along with Mashiach, techiyat hametim, Gan Eden and Gehinom . The first Jewish creed Before the Rambam, Sages told us what Jews believe ( Rav Saadia's Emunot veDeot , studied on Monday nights). But no one formulated a creed : these you must believe to be part of the Jewish people. The Rambam was the very first , and \" much has been written , Torah and academic, on whether it responded to the cultures around him, to Christianity.\" It \" stuck \": - יגדל , sung on Friday night in many communities, condenses the 13 Principles. - אני מאמין באמונה שלמה , said after Shacharit (Ashkenazi siddurim and some Sephardi). - Today, denying one makes you \"a kofer .\" The halachic stakes The Rambam writes (we'll see it inside): one who rejects any of the 13 is not part of Israel. - Shechitah: kosher shechitah must be done by a Yisrael . One who rejects the 13 \" isn't a Yisrael \", so his meat isn't kosher. \" Unbelievable. \" - Minyan: seemingly he can't count either. \"We don't interrogate people. But if someone publicly renounced a principle, it's a real halachic issue.\" The Mishnah כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא, שנאמר: ועמך כלם צדיקים, לעולם יירשו ארץ, נצר מטעי, מעשה ידי להתפאר. ירשו ארץ = ארץ החיים , the metaphorical land above ( לולא האמנתי לראות בטוב ה׳ בארץ חיים ). נצר מטעי : the planting of Gan Eden : ויטע ה׳ אלהים גן בעדן . The default: every Jew has a portion , commensurate with his deeds, even one executed for Shabbat-breaking, adultery or murder. (Today, with no executions, an unrepented capital sin is \"a different question.\") The exceptions האומר אין תחיית המתים Some texts add מן התורה (denying it's hinted in the Torah). The Rambam's text lacks it : you must believe in resurrection, not necessarily that it's in the Torah אין תורה מן השמים What that means is itself huge. \"Those who know British Jewish history will know the greatest rabbinic scandal of the century: the Louis Jacobs affair in the '50s and '60s, over exactly this\" אפיקורוס Two explanations: a follower of Epicurus 's heretical philosophy; or from הפקר , treating the Torah and its Sages as ownerless , free to denigrate Rabbi Akiva: הקורא בספרים החיצונים Reading forbidden books הלוחש על המכה : כל המחלה אשר שמתי במצרים Using verses as medicine : \"the Rambam was very sharp on this\" Abba Shaul: ההוגה את השם באותיותיו Pronouncing the Divine Name as written , the four letters, instead of Ad-nai ראיתי לדבר כאן עיקרים רבים באמונה, גדולים ונכבדים עד מאד. \"Here is the right place to discuss many great principles of faith.\" Five Mistaken Views of Reward He begins with the wrong answers . Even people of Torah disagree widely on the good that comes from keeping the mitzvot and the harm from transgressing : …ונשתבשו בה הסברות שבוש גדול, עד שכמעט לא תמצא בשום פנים אדם שנתבאר לו הדבר הזה. There's such confusion that you'll hardly find anyone clear on it. \"I think not much has changed. Ask the average Jew on the street: you'll get all kinds of answers, but rarely a clear one. There are so many statements (in the Torah, the Sages, different shiurim) that seem to contradict, and no one puts it all together. \" Ultimate reward Ultimate punishment Support claimed 1. Gan Eden A place where you eat and drink without toil : houses of precious stones, beds spread with silk, rivers of wine and perfumed oils Gehinom : a place burning with fire where bodies are burnt, with all kinds of suffering \"Most\" rabbinic statements and verses , read plainly 2. The days of Mashiach Everyone like angels , living forever , growing in stature ; Mashiach himself lives forever; the earth yields woven garments and baked bread Not being there Some statements 3. Techiyat hametim Living again after death, back with your family : eating, drinking, never dying Not rising with them Statements and verses 4. This world Rest and worldly pleasure : fertile land, wealth, many children, health, peace and security , a Jewish king over our enemies The opposite: \" as we are today, in exile \" The Torah's own blessings and curses : \"you don't need to look further than Ki Tavo \" 5. All of the above (most people) Mashiach comes, the dead rise, they enter Gan Eden, eat, drink and are healthy forever Missing it all On Gehinom: \"You've heard the stories. Read Rav Chaim Vital 's Sefer HaChezyonot , his diary of life with the Arizal : very graphic descriptions.\" \"Gan\" and \"Eden\" are two places The Rambam (in Rav Shilat's and Rav Kafih's text) writes גן ועדן , which the printed abbreviation hides. Many Gemaras treat them as two : - Berachot: עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך : Rabbi Levi: wine preserved in its grapes since the six days of creation ; Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani: that's Eden, which no eye has seen . But Adam was there? He was in the Gan . ונהר יצא מעדן להשקות את הגן : Gan is one thing, Eden another. Eden is reserved for the righteous. - Another Gemara: Egypt is 1/60 of Kush, the world 1/60 of the Garden, the Garden 1/60 of Eden, Eden 1/60 of Gehinom. \"What that means, I can't tell you. But they're two places .\" The common denominator Class answer: they're all physical. \" Excellent. \" Wine, a long-lived king, family, bread on trees: all physical . What People Should Ask, and What They Do Ask אבל זה הענין הנפלא ורעיון העולם הבא, מעט תמצא בשום פנים שיעלה על לבו. Hardly anyone even asks where Olam HaBa fits : is it the goal, or one of the five? Which is the goal, and which the means to it? What everyone asks instead: Will the dead rise naked or clothed? In the shrouds they were buried in, or in their fine clothes ( \"so I can still show off my Prada shoes\" )? When Mashiach comes, will there be rich and poor , strong and weak? A dig at Rav Saadia (unnamed): his essay on resurrection asks exactly these, ten questions ( what clothes? will I recognise my loved ones? ). The Rambam: you can discuss them, but that's not the essence. In fairness, the Gemara (Sanhedrin 90b) records Queen Cleopatra asking Rabbi Meir whether the dead rise clothed. He said clothed . The Rambam would say he answered because she asked . \"And what age will we be resurrected at? If it's one, the clothes won't fit!\" \" I'll worry about it when it happens. The Rambam writes elsewhere that no one knows these answers, about Mashiach or resurrection. When it comes, you'll see. \" The Parable of the Child He knows he's going against the masses, so first, an analogy. A young child is brought to a teacher to learn Torah. It's the greatest good for him, but being young and immature, he doesn't see its value. So the teacher, more mature, must motivate him with things he loves . Age Incentive \"To him, the goal is…\" Young child Nuts, figs, honey : \"today, sweets \" The sweet. Learning is toil endured for it Older (8 or 9) Nice shoes, fine clothes The clothes Teenager A dinar or two : \" £10 for this chapter\" The money Young adult \"Learn so you'll be a leader, a dayan, and people will honour you and stand for you\" Honour , praise, admiration \" Look at that gadol, how people honour him; learn properly and you'll be like him. We hear this all the time. The Rambam says 'unfortunately'; I'm not sure it's unfortunate. At that age, it's probably what you need to tell yeshivah students. \" וכל זה מגונה. All of this is unworthy , but necessary , because people take time to mature. The Sages call it שלא לשמה , not for its own sake, and warned: אל תעשם עטרה להתגדל בהם ולא קרדום לחפור בהם. The true goal: אין תכלית לימוד החכמה אלא לדעת אותה, ואין תכלית האמת אלא שתדע שהוא אמת. והתורה אמת, ותכלית ידיעתה לעשותה. The purpose of wisdom is to know it; the purpose of truth is to know that it's true. The Torah is truth, and the purpose of knowing it is to do it. \"What do I get?\" A mature person mustn't ask what reward will I get for these mitzvot? That's the child asking what will you give me if I read? The question itself shows immaturity , so we answer him according to his level : ענה כסיל כאולתו . The implication, though he doesn't say it outright: all those Rabbinic statements about wine, bread on trees, family reunions and fire are the sweets, clothes, money and honour . Tell people there's no reward, just learn , and they'll reject the Torah . Antigonos אל תהיו כעבדים המשמשין את הרב על מנת לקבל פרס, אלא… שלא על מנת לקבל פרס. He meant: believe the truth because it's the truth. That's serving from love. במצותיו חפץ מאד : Rabbi Elazar: in His commandments, not in their reward. The Sifrei: I'll learn to be rich, to be called Rabbi, to earn Olam HaBa. לאהבה את ה׳ : do everything only from love , not even for Olam HaBa . The four species. (Told of the Ba'al Shem Tov, or the Vilna Gaon.) Before Sukkot no arba minim could be found; one man in a nearby town had a set. \"In Poland you import them; Sephardim, among the date palms, didn't have this problem.\" The rabbi sent a messenger to pay any sum. The man replied: not for money, only for the reward of the mitzvah. The messenger returned crestfallen. The rabbi was thrilled : Run back! Finally I can test myself, doing a mitzvah for nothing at all. \"You can always psychoanalyse the subconscious. But that's the ideal.**\" Why the Sages allowed the incentives That's the level of Avraham Avinu, who served from love. But the Sages knew it's very hard ; few reach it, and slowly. A person does nothing except for a benefit, or to avoid a loss. \" We're not naturally altruistic. We can be, with training.\" Imagine telling your children, your congregation: do mitzvot for nothing. ** So the masses were permitted to keep mitzvot hoping for reward and avoid sin fearing punishment , to strengthen them until they reach completeness , as with the child. Antigonos was criticised : not for the idea, but for preaching it constantly . His students Tzadok and Baitos concluded there's no reward at all , and broke away. And the masses lose nothing by serving from fear and hope: it builds strength and habit , from which knowledge of the truth grows, and they come to serve from love . לעולם יעסוק אדם בתורה ובמצות אף שלא לשמן, שמתוך שלא לשמן בא לשמן. Summary Perek Helek is the Rambam's philosophical introduction, and the birth of the first Jewish creed , with halachic teeth ( shechitah, minyan ). The Mishnah: every Jew has a portion, even the executed, except those who deny resurrection or Torah from heaven, the apikoros , readers of forbidden books, verse-healers, and pronouncers of the Name. Five mistaken views of the ultimate reward: Gan Eden (wine; Gehinom's fire), Mashiach's days (bread on trees), resurrection (family), this world (Ki Tavo), all combined . Their common thread: they're all physical. People ask about shrouds vs Prada shoes , not where Olam HaBa fits . Gan and Eden are two places. The parable of the child: sweets → clothes → money → honour , all she-lo lishmah , necessary but not the goal . Truth is learned because it's true. Those physical descriptions are answers to the immature, at their level . Serve from love (Antigonos, Avraham), but don't preach it to all ; mitoch she-lo lishmah ba lishmah . Coming Up One more introduction: the misconception that every Rabbinic statement must be taken literally . Then what Olam HaBa really is , and how Mashiach, resurrection and Gan Eden fit around it.","Z1f-Rjunobw":"Dayan Daniel Kada · Thursday Night Halacha · Shiur 4 of 7 Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 672 and 673 Context and Review It was Shabbat Mevarchim Kislev , with Rosh Ḥodesh on the Sunday, so Hanukah was less than a month away. The aim was to cover Siman 672 and 673 in one shiur, in order to finish all of Hilchot Hanukah before the festival. Last week: the history of the synagogue menorah, its origins and reasons, how one can make a berachah on a minhag, how long it must burn in shul, who counts toward the ten, the extension to public places, the house with two entrances, and the start of the timing laws. Part One: The Earliest Time (672:1), Summarised אין מדליקין נר חנוכה קודם שתשקע החמה, אלא עם סוף שקיעתה, לא מאחרים ולא מקדימים. The Gemara: משתשקע החמה . The Rambam: עם שקיעתה . The Shulḥan Arukh quotes the Rambam but understands \"with sunset\" as \"with the end of sunset\" : nightfall , when the sun has finished going down. Rabbeinu Tam and others: nightfall. The Rashbam , perhaps the Rosh , and a possible reading of the Rambam (as the Bi'ur Halacha argues): the beginning of sunset , lighting right after the sun sets. The Vilna Gaon follows the sunset view, which is why some Ashkenazim light at sunset. The Sephardi custom has always followed the Shulḥan Arukh: at nightfall . As last week: nightfall does not mean waiting for the calendar's Shabbat-ends time. You have a good 10, 15, even 20 minutes before that time to light. Arvit or the Menorah First? If you... Then Follow the Gra and light at sunset Pray Minḥah before sunset, light at sunset, go back to shul later for Arvit Pray Minḥah before sunset and Arvit straight after , as many Sephardim do Pray Arvit, go home, and it's time to light Are particular all year to pray Arvit at nightfall (the Sephardim \"were never really particular about this\") Both mitzvot fall due at the same moment. Which comes first? The Rabbi asked the class for arguments. Mark: תדיר ושאינו תדיר, תדיר קודם : the more frequent mitzvah comes first, so Arvit . A second argument for Arvit: Arvit contains Shema , a Torah mitzvah. However beautiful and beloved Hanukah is, it is rabbinic. Do the d'oraita first. Indeed, at any later time in the evening (coming in from work at 7 or 8), you should pray Arvit first, for both reasons. But the Bi'ur Halacha proves, and this is how we rule, that at nightfall you light the menorah first. Why? - It is not clear what the Rambam holds: sunset or nightfall. We follow the Shulḥan Arukh's nightfall reading. - If you wait for Arvit at nightfall and then come home, 10, 15 or 20 minutes later, you are definitely outside the half hour after sunset , and according to the Gra, the Rashba and the other Rishonim you have missed the proper time. - If you light just as it becomes nightfall , you are still within half an hour of sunset , even in the UK where sunsets are long. You satisfy every opinion. - The principles of tadir and d'oraita first apply when you can do both properly. Here, delaying risks losing the mitzvah according to some Rishonim, so the menorah goes first. Ruling: at nightfall, if you haven't yet prayed Arvit, light first, then pray . Later in the evening, pray Arvit first . Lighting From Pelag HaMinḥah ויש מי שאומר שמפלג המנחה ולמעלה יכול להדליק, ובלבד שיתן בה שמן שתדלק עד שתכלה רגל מן השוק. A principle for learning the Shulḥan Arukh: \"יש מי שאומר\" When the Shulḥan Arukh writes \"there is one who says\" without dissent, it does not mean he disagrees . Many poskim, the Ḥida very clearly among them, explain: he uses this phrase for a ruling found in one or two books with no source in the Gemara , written by a Rishon whom nobody contested. He holds of it, but presents it as that author's view. Here the source is a late Rishon of the Aboab family, a near-contemporary of the Shulḥan Arukh, and this is what we follow . The logic: every Friday of Hanukah we must light from pelag haminḥah, since at sunset it is already Shabbat. So pelag haminḥah is already, to some extent, the start of the next day, and in extenuating circumstances you may light from then, with a berachah, on any night of Hanukah , provided there is enough oil to last until half an hour after nightfall . This is established practice. What counts as extenuating? Each person has to judge, but for example: - An evening flight: you leave for the airport just before sunset and won't land until the next morning. Light at pelag haminḥah. - Working the entire evening and not getting home until very late. (Whether pelag haminḥah or a very late lighting is better is discussed below.) Part Two: The Latest Time (672:2) שכח או הזיד ולא הדליק עם שקיעת החמה, מדליק והולך עד שתכלה רגל מן השוק, שהוא כמו חצי שעה... ואם עבר זה הזמן ולא הדליק, מדליק והולך כל הלילה. ואם עבר כל הלילה ולא הדליק, אין לו תשלומין. Three levels Level When Status 1 At the proper time : nightfall (Sephardim) or sunset (some Ashkenazim) Ideal 2 עד שתכלה רגל מן השוק : while people are still walking home, about half an hour Still lechatḥilah 3 The rest of the night, until dawn Bedi'eved , but valid Why half an hour? In Talmudic times, when it got dark you stopped work and walked home, which took twenty minutes or half an hour. That is when the streets were busy. If a whole night is missed, there is no make-up (אין לו תשלומין). Don't light an extra light the next night, and don't try to \"light for last night.\" Carry on with the same count as everyone else. \"It's gone. No more opportunity.\" The Rema: \"nowadays we light indoors\" הגה: ויש אומרים דבזמן הזה... אין לחוש... Some say that since we light indoors out of fear, the timing no longer matters. This was very widespread in the Rishonim , which underlines how common it was not to light outdoors: - Tosafot write that the time limit no longer applies, because we light indoors. - The Me'iri writes that his beit midrash did not interrupt learning : they learned all day and lit late at night, since it was indoors anyway. \"Nowadays, I think everywhere across the world... kollelim, yeshivot all wrap up early, four o'clock, whatever time it is,\" so people can get home to light at the right time. But today we usually light by a window , so there is pirsumei nisa, and the Shulḥan Arukh's original law returns: be particular to light within the half-hour window. Even the Rema himself writes that even indoors it is good to keep to the correct time. Lighting very late: must someone be awake? The Magen Avraham (in the Mishnah Berurah): \"you may light all night\" with a berachah only if members of the household are awake . If everyone is asleep, light without a berachah. The Mishnah Berurah even suggests that if you come home very late before dawn and find everyone asleep, wake some of the family so you can say the berachah. The obvious question, which the class raised: didn't we learn that pirsumei nisa applies even to yourself ? Someone living alone lights with a berachah, as does someone lighting on a table in time of danger, or in a place with no other Jews at all. Robert added that we light indoors where no one sees. The Mishnah Berurah is sceptical : in the Sha'ar HaTziyun he brings those who disagree and says one may follow either opinion. The Ben Ish Ḥai does follow the Magen Avraham and requires someone to be present. Ḥacham Ovadia and many others (the Knesset HaGedolah , R. Ḥayim Benveniste; the Petaḥ HaDvir ) say this is not needed . Pirsumei nisa is a mitzvah even individually. Our ruling: you may light all night, with a berachah, even alone . If you come home at one in the morning, single, to an empty house, you still light with a berachah. It is always nicer to gather the family, but it is not required. The Brisker Rav's idea: has \"רגל מן השוק\" moved? An idea in the name of the Brisker Rav : the Sages set \"until people stop walking in the market\" at half an hour because that was the reality then. Today residential streets are busy at 5, 6 or 7 pm; on a main road perhaps 9 or 10 pm. So the oil should last as long as people are still walking around . Everyone rejected this: the Sages fixed half an hour , and once they set a measure, it stays. \"No one really takes this to halacha. It's a very interesting idea.\" And yet... ...in a different respect the poskim do rely on it , somewhat contradictorily: many write that because reality has changed and the streets are still busy, level 2 (lechatḥilah) now extends as long as people are still out. Coming home at 5 or 6 pm and lighting by the window is still lechatḥilah , not bedi'eved. Coming home at 10 pm is certainly bedi'eved. \"On one hand we say the time has extended; on the other, we say you don't need oil for that long. Which is why I'm not convinced.\" Remember: if you light indoors on the table , where no one sees, this whole discussion hardly arises. It concerns people lighting at windows and front doors , where there is publicity. Questions From the Class Should we wait for the family to come home? Robert: today, in effect, we light for the household . If I'm home at 4 but the five or six children only get home at 6, isn't it better to wait, for more pirsumei nisa? A participant countered: the people passing in the street outnumber your household, however big. \"But are they all Jews?\" came the reply. The Rabbi: \"I can hear that argument,\" with a big family, and if no one is going to be home earlier. R. Shlomo Zalman Auerbach's question: should the wife wait? A wife is equally obligated in Hanukah. Usually the husband says the berachot, by the general rule that when both are present and able, the man performs it. (The Even HaEzer is sharp about a capable man relying on his wife, as with Hallel.) But: The wife and children are home at 4. The husband won't be back until 10. Should they wait? Consider the reverse: if the wife is out, the husband doesn't wait . Strictly, he lights at the right time (\"it may not be great for the shalom bayit at home\"). So: If he'll be home... Then While people are still out and about They wait for him and light together as a family Later (nine o'clock; on quiet streets even eight) The wife lights at the correct time Likewise adult children who are part of the household (dependent, eating at home and supported by their parents) may light for it. A practical case: a couple flying to Rome for an anniversary ask their teenage children , boy or girl, to light at home in London. \"Absolutely fine.\" Their household is in London, and it is being lit there. \"I hope what I've done is given you the halachic principles. Each person has to weigh up the circumstances.\" How Long Must It Burn? And a Safety Warning There is no need for more oil than the time requires. If you put in more, you may extinguish it after the time has passed. \"Unfortunately the reality is we've heard of many Hanukah tragedies.\" A few years ago there was a serious Hanukah fire in the USA. People leave the menorah burning, go out to Hanukah parties, and come home to a fire. There is no mitzvah in leaving the menorah on after half an hour. If you are going out, stay with it for a while, then extinguish it . Some might argue that, on the \"extended time\" view, there would be value in keeping it burning, but \"we don't really take this on.\" It's lovely to watch the lights burn for hours, but the mitzvah is done after half an hour. The Mishnah Berurah: extra oil to make it burn longer is no added mitzvah . Hiddur is at the moment of the mitzvah: - Tall wax candles are a beautification (with wax, a handsome long candle is hiddur). - A huge amount of oil so it lasts longer is not hiddur mitzvah. Hiddur means beautifying the mitzvah at the time of the mitzvah , not making it last longer than required. After the time has passed , you may also benefit from the light , which is rarely relevant today with electricity. Candles that won't last half an hour The Bi'ur Halacha: if you have so little oil, or such small candles, that they cannot last half an hour , they do not fulfil the mitzvah. When the Rabbi used to light in care homes , the small coloured \"Hanukah candles,\" essentially birthday candles, were gone in twenty minutes or less. If you doubt that your candles will last half an hour, light without a berachah. Don't sit down to a meal Once the time to light has arrived, don't sit down to a proper meal (washing for bread) before lighting; a small snack is fine. Ḥazon Ovadia: if the family is waiting for the one who will light, say at 6, they need not go hungry. They have no mitzvah yet; they are waiting for someone else to perform it. Part Three: Fuels (673) כל השמנים והפתילות כשרים לנר חנוכה, ואף על פי שאין השמנים נמשכים אחר הפתילה, ואין האור נתלה יפה באותן פתילות. All oils and all wicks are valid for the Hanukah light, even if the oil doesn't draw well up the wick and the flame doesn't hold well on it. Why the contrast with Shabbat? Every Friday night we say במה מדליקין , with its many rules about which oils and wicks may be used for Shabbat candles: none with a bad smell (you'll leave the room), and none that flicker or burn poorly. That is because the Shabbat light is for oneg Shabbat : it must create an atmosphere in which you want to stay. Hanukah is about publicity. As long as there is a wick and a flame, that is what we need. This is why we don't say במה מדליקין on Shabbat Ḥanukah. On Friday night of Hanukah, its restrictions might be misunderstood as applying to the menorah, so it is skipped \"to make it clear.\" The custom of skipping it when Friday night is Yom Tov began with Hanukah and spread from there. The best fuel Fuel Status Olive oil (שמן זית) Best. Burns brightest and is a special mitzvah, as in the Beit HaMikdash Other oils with a good flame Good Wax candles (the Rema: the custom in Europe) Acceptable, since wax burns brightly. The Rema mentions it because people couldn't afford that much oil every night Everyone agrees olive oil is better. For bulk lighting (care homes, hotels) wax is simpler, \"but really at home we should all be making an effort to be lighting with olive oil.\" Wax versus a weaker oil? If the oil available burns less brightly than a wax candle, which is better? - The Maharal (cited in the Mishnah Berurah): oil is always better . We are publicising a miracle of oil , and the Temple used oil. - Others: the brighter flame is better publicity. Bottom line: use olive oil if you can afford it , then other oils, then wax. Fuels forbidden for Shabbat are fine for Hanukah, even on Friday Even on Friday afternoon, fuels forbidden for Shabbat candles may be used for the menorah, since you may not use the menorah's light anyway, and you will light separate candles for oneg Shabbat. A participant: surely the bad-smelling ones are still a problem, since the reason for the Shabbat rule is that you'll leave the room? The Rabbi: no. A bad-smelling Shabbat candle fails to fulfil the Shabbat-candle mitzvah . If you have proper Shabbat candles and a smelly menorah, both mitzvot are fulfilled; the smell is \"an external issue.\" No Benefit From the Hanukah Lights Why is it forbidden? 1. Pirsumei nisa: to show clearly that the lights are only for the miracle. 2. In the Beit HaMikdash , the Menorah was not used for other purposes either. How strict? Not even counting money ; not even reading a Torah book . (Some permit study, but in practice the law is almost irrelevant today.) The shamash, again Because of this law, the shamash became customary, already about 700 years ago : if you need light, you are using the shamash's. Ashkenazim Sephardim The shamash's role Lit first and used to light the others Primarily the extra light , lit at the end In common Placed higher or apart, so no one thinks it is one of the Hanukah lights and you can see which night it is \"Menorah\" or \"Ḥanukiyah\"? What we light is not the Menorah of the Beit HaMikdash. That had seven lamps: a central stem with three branches each side. Ours has nine : eight plus the shamash. We call it a \"menorah\" in a borrowed sense, which is probably why the word ḥanukiyah arose. Why it hardly matters today: almost every menorah has a shamash, and we have electricity. Only if your house were pitch dark, with no shamash and no other light, would these laws apply in practice. Mixed Oil (the Rema) Background: setting oil aside for Hanukah does not sanctify it: הזמנה לאו מילתא היא , designation alone means nothing. You may change your mind and cook with it, just as material set aside for a tallit bag may be used to mend a coat. Leftover oil may of course be used the following night. The specific case: you lit, the flame went out before half an hour , so the remaining oil was dedicated to Hanukah and may be used only for Hanukah lights, and then it got mixed with ordinary oil . The Rema: it is not nullified, even one in a thousand , because it is a דבר שבמנין , a \"counted\" item (the lights are numbered by the nights). The Aḥaronim (in the Mishnah Berurah) disagree: normal nullification applies, one in two . In this unusual scenario, if there is twice as much ordinary oil, it may be used. הדלקה עושה מצוה: If It Goes Out הדלקה עושה מצוה. לפיכך אם כבתה קודם שעבר זמנה, אינו זקוק לה. The lighting performs the mitzvah. So if it went out before its time had passed, he need not relight it. An accident: you lit properly, and five minutes later a child knocked it over. You're done. You lit, you publicised it for five minutes. Even on Friday afternoon: if it goes out a minute after lighting, you need not relight. You may relight to be stringent , and the Mishnah Berurah cites Aḥaronim who say it is good, especially on Friday. But no berachah . The condition: at the moment of lighting, it must have been able to last half an hour . - A miscalculation (too little oil) is your mistake: you have not fulfilled the mitzvah. - Predictable wind , lighting somewhere it would probably blow out, doesn't help even if it lasted 25 minutes: you would need to light again, possibly even with a berachah. In practice, no berachah , because it is hard to be certain whether it could have lasted. In theory, if you knew for certain that it couldn't, you would relight with a berachah. - An unexpected freak gust: you have fulfilled the mitzvah. The same principle , from Siman 671: don't light at the bedside and then move it to the window. At the moment of lighting, it must be in the proper place. Lighting in a valid place and then moving it (from one windowsill to another) is fine. Reusing wicks (673:4) You need not use new wicks every night . Reusing them is not disrespectful to the mitzvah. Replace them when they can no longer be used. Summary The time to light: Sephardim at nightfall , the end of sunset (the Shulḥan Arukh); followers of the Gra at sunset. Light early in that window. Arvit vs. menorah: at nightfall light first , to stay within half an hour of sunset and satisfy all opinions (Bi'ur Halacha). Later in the evening, pray Arvit first (tadir; Shema is d'oraita). Pelag haminḥah: in genuine need (a flight, working late), light from pelag with a berachah and enough oil to last until half an hour after nightfall. \"יש מי שאומר\" means the Shulḥan Arukh accepts the view. Three levels: the proper time, then the half-hour window (lechatḥilah, possibly extended while streets are busy), then all night until dawn (bedi'eved). No make-up for a missed night. Alone or late: light with a berachah even if everyone is asleep or you live alone (Ḥacham Ovadia), against the Magen Avraham and the Ben Ish Ḥai. Waiting for a spouse (R. Shlomo Zalman Auerbach): wait if they will be home while people are still out; otherwise the one at home lights. Household members, including adult dependent children, can light for the household. Half an hour is enough. You may extinguish afterwards: don't leave a menorah burning when you go out. Candles that can't last half an hour don't count. Fuel: all oils and wicks are valid, unlike Shabbat. Hence no במה מדליקין on Shabbat Ḥanukah . Olive oil is best , then other oils, then wax. No benefit from the lights, hence the shamash (lit last by Sephardim, first by Ashkenazim). Our \"menorah\" is really a ḥanukiyah . הדלקה עושה מצוה: if it goes out by accident, you're done. If it was never able to last, relight. Wicks may be reused. Coming Up No shiur the following Thursday (the Rabbi was travelling). Classes resumed on Thursday 12 December , possibly with extra sessions to finish before Hanukah, starting with Siman 674 : lighting one Hanukah light from another.","ZLbAbEv-Yec":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 8 of 14 — the start of Part 2 of the course Topic: Gozez (Cutting) and Sove'a (Colouring) — hair, nails, make-up, sunbathing Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 15 January 2019 Schedule note: Melaben (cleaning), planned for tonight, was moved to the next shiur. Recap Part 1 covered preparing for Shabbat, the kitchen melachot (cooking, threshing, selecting, grinding, kneading) and ploughing, planting and harvesting. Part 2 opens with cutting and colouring. Part A — Gozez (Cutting) Definition In the Mishkan, the lowest roof covering was woven from four-ply thread — three strands of wool and one of linen — and the wool came from shearing sheep . Its derivatives are very common: cutting hair , nails , peeling skin around the nails, removing a wart . Gozez = removing any appendage of the body. (Cutting a limb is not Gozez — that is the body itself.) Combing and brushing hair Combing isn't cutting — but it is a pesik reisha : an inevitable result is forbidden even if unintended (the Gemara's case: cutting off a chicken's head to give a child a toy — you can't claim you didn't intend to kill it). A brush with strong, thick bristles will inevitably pull out at least one hair — and even one hair is forbidden. So: A hard, thick-bristled brush — forbidden (it shouldn't even be used on Shabbat). A brush with soft bristles on a spongy base — the bristles bend under pressure, so pulling hair is not inevitable; a hair that does come out is unintended and permitted. Even so, brush moderately — to look neat and respectable, not a vigorous, thorough brushing. Ashkenazi custom: a special brush reserved for Shabbat. Sephardim need not — the weekday brush is fine if soft. Plaiting hair; wigs Plaiting hair is rabbinically forbidden as a form of building : the Torah says Hashem \"built\" Chava, and the Gemara says He braided her hair before bringing her to Adam. Undoing a plait is likewise forbidden (demolishing). Plaiting a wig is not building (the hair is detached) but is rabbinically forbidden as weaving ( oreg ). Combing a wig is permitted — the hair is already detached — unless the hair is badly matted (a Makeh Be'Patish issue). Nails Cutting nails is Gozez. A woman due at the mikveh on Friday night who forgot to cut her nails: the halacha is that she may ask a non-Jew to cut them. This rests on a recurring principle — shevut deshevut bimkom mitzvah : something twice removed from a Torah prohibition is permitted for a mitzvah. Asking a non-Jew is itself only rabbinic. Cutting nails one doesn't intend to use is, according to many, only rabbinic — melacha she'einah tzrichah legufah (in the Mishkan the wool was cut to be used). A non-Jew may likewise comb her hair for the mikveh: a pesik reisha through a non-Jew is permitted. (Similarly, a non-Jew may open a fridge whose light will inevitably go on.) Q: Can she cut them herself with her teeth or hands? — The ruling is to ask a non-Jew. A partly detached nail Permitted to remove when all three conditions are met: It is painful or bothering you considerably. The majority of it is already detached (no longer \"attached\" in Torah terms). You remove it with your hand or teeth (a shinui) — not a nail clipper. Alternatively, ask a non-Jew to use clippers. Q: what if someone normally bites their nails? — We follow how most people do things, and most cut their nails with clippers. Skin on the lips: be very careful not to pull it off with your teeth — that may be the normal way to do it, and the Mishnah Berurah suggests it could even be a Torah prohibition. Part B — Sove'a (Colouring and Dyeing) Definition The threads of the Mishkan's covering were dyed blue, purple and scarlet — so dyeing is a Torah melacha. It includes enhancing an existing colour , not only changing it: repainting a white wall white, or polishing black shoes (one reason shoe polish is forbidden). Creating a picture is not dyeing but writing (Kotev). Food and drink — ein tzevi'ah be'ochlin Combining foods that happen to change colour — fine by all. Even mixing foods to create a colour — e.g. jam into a toddler's white yoghurt to make it pink and appetising — is permitted : there is no dyeing with food, which is transient (and food wasn't dyed in the Mishkan). Accepted by Sephardim and most Ashkenazim. Only for eating or drinking . Colouring food for business display, or colouring water as an ornament — forbidden by the Torah. Food colouring may be used (Q from the floor) — it is colouring food. Some (Ashkenazi stringency) reverse the usual order — e.g. cordial/syrup first, then water. Good to do, but strictly it is permitted. Blood and stains on cloth Wrapping a bleeding finger in a towel, or wiping red strawberry or pomegranate juice off your hands onto a towel, inevitably \"dyes\" it — but it is a pesik reisha of a destructive act you get no benefit from ( pesik reisha delo nicha lei in a rabbinic matter), so it is permitted . The poskim add: if a tap is nearby, rinse first. A cut finger isn't an emergency — but there's no need to delay dressing a wound. Make-up Make-up on the skin is not permanent, so it is rabbinic — but all regular make-up and lipstick are forbidden. Regular make-up is oil- or cream-based and adheres to the skin. Pure powder — no oil or cream — merely sits on the surface and is very transient. Most poskim — Chacham Ovadia, Rav Moshe Feinstein — permit it (\"Shabbat make-up\"); Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach was not happy with it. Apply it only to a clean face , not over cream-based make-up from before Shabbat (which would rub the powder in). Removing make-up is permitted. Sunbathing to tan Not a walk on a hot day — but deliberately sitting on a balcony for hours to tan. The room was split. The point: you are not doing anything; the sun acts on you — gerama (indirect). A handful of poskim forbid it: dyeing is inherently a gradual process (like leaving cloth in a dye vat), just as cooking in an oven isn't \"indirect\". The majority (Chacham Ovadia among them) permit sunbathing even with intent to tan. But not if it becomes uncomfortable — sitting in discomfort just to show off a tan contradicts oneg Shabbat . And consider the spirit of Shabbat: half an hour reading in the sun, yes; a whole afternoon soaking up rays, no. For vitamin D: what ordinary people do for enjoyment isn't forbidden as \"medicine\" (like prune juice for mild constipation), so it is permitted even for health. Toilet colouring blocks A block in the cistern — permitted by all: the new water is coloured only as it refills, an inevitable but indirect result ( pesik reisha be'gerama ) — like pouring water onto concrete that trickles onto grass. A block in the bowl — flushing sends water straight through it. Debated: some call flushing indirect (you only lift a valve and gravity does the rest), or say the colour is too transient. Remove it before Shabbat if you can; if you find one at someone's home, there is plenty to rely on to use the toilet. Q: forming shapes (with food) — a Kotev (writing) question, to be discussed later. Summary Gozez: no cutting hair, nails or skin. Only a soft, sponge-based brush, used moderately. No plaiting hair or wigs; combing a wig is fine. For the mikveh, a non-Jew may cut a woman's nails and comb her hair. A painful, mostly-detached nail may be removed by hand or teeth. Don't bite skin off your lips. Sove'a: no dyeing or enhancing colour (shoe polish). No dyeing with food and drink — colouring for eating is fine. Wiping blood or juice on a towel is permitted. Regular make-up forbidden; pure powder on a clean face permitted by most. Sunbathing permitted in moderation and comfort. Cistern toilet blocks are fine; remove in-bowl ones before Shabbat if possible. Coming Up Shiur 9 — 22 January: Melaben (cleaning) — stains on a suit, laundering, cleaning dust off clothes Knots — tying shoelaces, tying up the dustbin Animals — trapping and hunting; pets B'Ezrat Hashem","_HbnLTzb5iQ":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 1 (Creation), end of Chapter 1 (the fourth proof, from time) and Chapter 2 (the world could not create itself; against creation from pre-existing matter) Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 15 April 2024, on Zoom (the last shiur before Pesach) These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Where We Are In the introduction to the essay Rav Saadia warned that we cannot perceive creation with our senses, so we must rely on reason, as every other theory must too. Chapter 1 then opened with his thesis: Hashem created the world from nothing , from בראשית ברא אלהים , since the word bara is used only of God and signifies creation from nothing. Then came four rational proofs that the universe had a beginning . Three were done last week: # Proof In short 1 Finitude Heaven and earth are finite, so their force is finite, so they began 2 Design / composition Everything is beautifully put together, so there is a Maker 3 Accidents ( mikrim ) Everything has happenings occurring to it and is inseparable from them, so it is itself a happening 4 Time Tonight Tonight's three steps The argument builds in stages, \"getting more and more\": There was a beginning (finishing Chapter 1 with the proof from time). The beginning did not happen by itself : it was caused by something external (Chapter 2). It was not made from pre-existing matter , against Plato (Chapter 2). Part 1: Proof Four: Time \"I won't exaggerate. I spent many hours trying to understand it, and literally broke my head over it. It's a very complicated, very difficult one. We'll try our best to explain it, although it may not necessarily be convincing for yourself. At least I'm not convinced by it. \" The text Time has three parts: past, present and future . The present is smaller than any instant, but for the sake of argument I take the present as a point , as one takes a point in a line. Now if a person tried in his thought to ascend from this point upwards (that is, back into the past), he could not , if time were infinite. What is infinite, thought cannot traverse. The Rabbi illustrated: \"It is now 6:08 on 15 April 2024. Let's mark that. Imagine the present freezes. Now go back a minute, and another, and back and back and back. You can't do that. It's impossible for our finite minds to traverse an infinity backwards.\" This goes back to Aristotle: the infinite is that which cannot be traversed. \"So far so good.\" Then the key step: For the same reason, generation could not have traversed the infinite down to reach us. And if generation did not reach us, we would not have been generated ; we and all that exists would not exist. Since I find that I exist, I know that generation has passed through time until it reached me. If time were not finite, generation could not have traversed it. And the same holds for the future as for the past. He brings verses for time having a far end, such as אנוש יביט מרחוק (Iyov 36:25), man looks from afar: there is a \"far\" to look to, so time is finite. Making sense of it: the Kalam The classic commentaries did not help: \"they basically said they couldn't understand it themselves.\" What finally helped was an old academic paper (from about the 1940s) on the Kalam arguments for creation in the Rishonim. The Kalam were the Arabic theologians whose methods were used by Rav Saadia, by the Rambam , and by Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi in the Kuzari. The paper divides the argument into two parts: Part Content The preamble The infinite is that which cannot be traversed , and so has no first, no beginning . Rav Saadia speaks of traversing in thought because time, for Aristotle (whom he follows), exists in the mind: without a soul there would be no time The argument proper If anything's coming into being depended on completing an infinite chain of prior generations , nothing could ever come into being And the key line: If there is no first, there is no cause at all; and if there is no cause, there can be no effect. The Rabbi's explanation \"What series is he talking about? The series of generated human beings. We are generated by our parents, who were generated by theirs, and so on. When I say generation, I don't mean the noun, dor . I mean the verb: being generated.\" If the universe is eternal, the chain of generation goes back infinitely . An infinite series has no beginning : \"it's an inherent paradox to talk about the beginning of infinity.\" No first means no cause. No cause means no effect. But we exist : Rav Saadia in the tenth century, and we in 2024. So the chain had a beginning. \"It's very hard to wrap your head around. But that's the best I think we can do with it.\" Zeno's challenge and Rav Saadia's reply Rav Saadia then quotes \"one of the deniers.\" The Rabbi identified the objection with Zeno , the Greek philosopher of the fifth century BCE, who disputed this very premise: we traverse the infinite every time we walk. \"We can all walk a mile. Now cut the mile in half: half a mile. A quarter, an eighth, a sixteenth... Surely you could divide that into infinity, into the smallest atom, half of that atom, half of that. The division of the mile is infinite, and yet you traverse it. \" So the premise of the fourth proof, that the infinite cannot be traversed, seems destroyed. Discussion. Mark suggested that walking a mile covers only a small section and \"doesn't take you to the beginning.\" The Rabbi agreed that time is different, \"but remember, we're using time as an example of the general concept, so if we destroy the premise, the whole thing falls.\" Another participant tried the reverse: if you keep halving, you never reach the end of the mile. \" I travelled a mile today. Fact. So the proposition that anything infinite cannot be travelled is proven wrong.\" Rav Saadia mentions two answers others gave, neither of which he accepts: Answer Idea The indivisible atom Division does not go on forever: there is a smallest thing that cannot be divided further. (The Rabbi asked whether anyone with a background in physics could say if that is still held today. \"My granddaughter, but not me,\" said one participant. \"Well, ask her for next week.\") The \"leap\" You do not actually traverse the infinite parts; you jump over them His own answer: The infinite division of a mile is only in the mind. In theory I can halve it again and again; in practice you quickly reach a point too fine to divide. But time actually passed , in reality. You cannot use something theoretical to refute something real. \"A nice response. But if you got that, great. It's not crucial at all.\" Further proofs, and Hiwi al-Balkhi Rav Saadia says he has more proofs , some in his commentary on the Torah (which we no longer have) and some in his refutation of Hiwi al-Balkhi . The Rabbi read about Hiwi from Wikipedia: A biblical critic of the late ninth century , born in what is now Afghanistan . It is unclear whether he was a Jew or a member of a Christian sect; some say he belonged to the Bukharan Jewish community. He wrote 200 objections to the divine origin of the Torah . His views spread so widely that schoolmasters taught children from textbooks based on his criticisms . Rav Saadia had those books prohibited and answered Hiwi in a book of refutation. Both books are lost. Rav Saadia closes Chapter 1 by saying that when we see the refutations of the other theories in Chapter 3 , our own position will be strengthened further. Part 2: Chapter 2: Could the World Have Created Itself? \"When it became clear to me that the world had a beginning, I asked: did things create themselves, or did something external create them? \" \"Clearly for us nowadays we would think this is absurd, the thought of something creating itself. But let's just see his logic.\" The Rabbi said it is actually relevant to the Big Bang : Believing in the Big Bang is fine, and it may even corroborate the Torah's account (with the hint in the Ramban ). \"The problem is the Big Bang when you don't posit anyone who created the primordial soup.\" Where did it come from? \"They'll tell you it was just there, it created itself.\" That, says Rav Saadia, is unacceptable, for three reasons. The three proofs # Argument The Rabbi's explanation 1 Stronger after than before If something was strong enough, while it did not yet exist , to make itself, then once it exists it should be even stronger and able to make more of itself. But earth cannot make more earth. If it cannot do it from the stronger position, it could not have done it from the weaker one 2 When? Before it existed, it was nothing, and nothing cannot act . After it existed, it no longer needed creating. There is no third time besides these, \"and the present is too tiny to be sufficient.\" \"If this non-existent thing decided to create itself, then it wasn't non-existent.\" 3 The power to create implies the power not to Whatever can create itself must also be able not to. When would it have that ability? If after it exists, it already exists; if before, it is nothing. You would have existence and non-existence together , \"completely wrong and nonsensical.\" Similar to the second, \"but slightly different\" \"I'm going through this quickly because it's straightforward, and I think most of us see the clear and inherent logic.\" The verse: the prophet's rebuke of Pharaoh , who said לי יאורי ואני עשיתני (Yechezkel 29:3), \"My river is mine, and I made myself.\" This is the rebuke of anyone who thinks a thing can make itself. End of section two: four proofs that the world had a beginning; three proofs that it could not have made itself. \"So therefore we must posit a creator.\" Rav Saadia has not yet said who that Creator is. That is Essay 2. Part 3: Chapter 2 Continued: Against Pre-existing Matter The final option is Plato's : there is a Creator, things cannot create themselves, but He made the world from matter that existed eternally . \"The universe as we know it had a beginning, millions or billions of years ago. But when God created it, He created it from tiny matter which existed for eternity.\" \"To be honest, I've read about this in many different places. I've never really understood it.\" Rav Saadia's arguments, as the Rabbi presented them: Argument one: what \"creation\" means The very word creation means bringing something about where there was nothing . To speak of creation from something is to say there was no real beginning. \"Logically, it makes more sense: when I say creation, creation means from nothing.\" Argument two: two equal arguments? Rav Saadia anticipates a clever objection: You proved a Creator from what you see : everything in the world was made by someone. \"If I see a clock, I assume it came from parts and was put together by someone. If I look at my clothes, someone wove them. If I look at my glasses, someone put them together.\" No action without an actor. But by the same senses, everything you see was made from something . You have never seen anything made from nothing! \"So why am I favouring one argument over the other?\" Shouldn't the universe likewise come from pre-existing matter? Rav Saadia's reply: whether the world is from nothing is the very thesis under investigation . You cannot bring as proof, in the middle of that investigation, one of the two sides of the question itself . \"He tells you off. It's not the way you think.\" \"This just shows us how to use logic.\" The Rabbi added that the Rambam wrote a treatise on logic (\"not easy reading\"), and Rav Saadia is doing the same here: teaching how to frame an argument correctly. Argument three: who decided which is which? If the matter was eternal, then it and the Creator are equally eternal : \"there's no hierarchy between God and matter.\" So why would the matter obey ? Why should one be the maker and the other the made? \"There would have to be a third thing that assigned this one to be the Creator and that one to be the created. That's nonsense. We only find a Creator and the created. We didn't find a third thing.\" Rav Saadia brings a few more arguments against Plato, which the class would finish quickly next time. A Question for the Class Chapter 3 is long. It examines twelve other theories of how the world came to be, \"some very ancient and quite difficult to understand.\" Should the class go through them all, or skip them, given that science now tells us the world had a beginning ? \"The only value I see is completeness, getting the whole picture, and logic : seeing how Saadia thinks. There is value. But I'm not sure it's worth spending a month or so.\" Mark suggested studying a selection, perhaps three of them , to see the logic. \"It's a good idea. Thank you, Mark.\" Over Pesach the Rabbi would analyse which are most relevant or most logical and choose some, inviting those listening to the recording to send their views too. Summary Proof four, from time: the infinite cannot be traversed; an infinite chain of generations has no first, so no cause, so no effect ; we exist, so the chain began. The Rabbi found it the hardest proof and is not convinced by it, making sense of it only through a paper on the Kalam . Zeno's objection: we cross infinitely divisible distances every day. Rav Saadia's answer: that infinity exists only in thought , while time actually passed. Others answered with the indivisible atom or the leap . More proofs were in his lost commentary on the Torah and in his lost refutation of Hiwi al-Balkhi , the critic whose 200 objections were being taught to children. Chapter 2: nothing creates itself. Three proofs: it would be stronger afterwards; there is no time at which it could have acted; the power to create implies the power not to. Pharaoh's ואני עשיתני . This is the problem with a Big Bang without a Creator. Against Plato's eternal matter: creation means from nothing ; you cannot prove a disputed thesis from one side of the dispute ; and two equally eternal things leave no reason for one to rule the other . Coming Up Pesach break: no shiur for the next two Mondays. The class resumes in three weeks , finishing Chapter 2 briefly and moving to a selection of the twelve theories in Chapter 3 .","afdP9Fny-8o":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 7 of 14 Topic: Out and About — Choresh (Ploughing), Zore'a (Planting) and Kotser (Harvesting) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 18 December 2018 (after Chanukah) Schedule note: Last shiur of Part 1; a winter break followed, resuming 15 January. Recap We have covered the kitchen melachot — cooking, selecting, threshing, grinding and kneading. Tonight we move outdoors, to three melachot normally done outside the house. ( Me'amer , gathering, was not reached.) Part A — Choresh (Ploughing) Derivatives of ploughing We rarely plough a field, but many derivatives ( toldot ) are equally forbidden by the Torah. Ploughing in the Mishkan prepared the land for planting the dye-plants; so any act that improves the soil for planting is Choresh: Fertiliser, manure, compost. Weeding (among other issues). Levelling the ground ( ashvuyei gumot ) — filling in a hollow or flattening a bump outdoors. Making a furrow or a hole in the earth deep enough to plant in — even without planting. Walking sticks, buggies, heels and football studs A walking stick pokes holes with every step; a buggy cuts furrows in soft ground; high heels and studs make holes. Since a pesik reisha (inevitable result) is normally forbidden even if unintended, why are these permitted? Melacha she'einah tzrichah legufah — the holes aren't made for planting, the Mishkan purpose, so it is only rabbinic. (Likewise squeezing a pickle just to get the juice out of it, not to use the juice.) Shinui — no one makes holes with a buggy or a walking stick; an unusual manner is only rabbinic (like writing with one's weaker hand). Arguably destructive ( mekalkel ) — you don't want the holes. A pesik reisha involving a doubly-rabbinic act is permitted. So one may push a buggy through a park, wear heels or studs, and use a walking stick — provided one does not intend the holes (e.g. to plant in them later), which would be forbidden (rabbinically, being a shinui). Games needing a level surface Because levelling is Choresh, Chazal forbade games that need a level surface on earth — originally rolling nuts, today marbles — lest one fill in a dip mid-game. They even forbade it indoors when most houses had earth floors; today, with tiled floors, such games are fine indoors , and on concrete. Golf — forbidden on Shabbat (needs a level surface on earth). Tennis — usually on hard courts, so not this problem. Football — a casual game doesn't really need a perfectly level pitch, so this isn't the issue. Is a ball muktseh? And football on Shabbat The Shulchan Aruch rules a ball is muktseh — in those days balls (made from things like an animal's stomach) had no recognised use. Today, when balls are manufactured for play, many poskim say it is hard to call a ball muktseh — designating something for use removes muktseh (as a stone designated as a doorstop). Some remain hesitant. Children may certainly play with a ball. Even so, getting changed and playing a competitive game is hardly in the spirit of Shabbat . A casual kick-about for someone who needs an outlet is acceptable; but Shabbat holds so much more to do than play football. Sweeping Sweeping inside the house, even with a broom, is permitted; sweeping an earth area outside is not (levelling). Questions from the floor Putting garden furniture on soft earth? — Permitted. A \"hole\" is made by poking in and pulling out in one act; placing a chair and later lifting it is a different act. Removing a stick or stone from the ground? — You are taking something out, not actively making a hole. Part B — Zore'a (Planting) Any act that assists growth Not only planting seeds: anything that assists growth is forbidden by the Torah — including watering . A sprinkler or hose set before Shabbat, or on a timer, may run on Shabbat. Don't tamper with a multi-valve sprinkler system: closing one valve sends more water to the others — at least an indirect Zore'a. Washing hands or pouring water on grass Washing hands (e.g. netilat yadayim at a garden lunch) over grass, or tipping yesterday's rain off a garden chair onto the lawn — even in someone else's garden with no interest in it — is a pesik reisha and forbidden . This applies to water and water-based drinks (juice, Coke); not wine or chemicals, which don't benefit growth. Indirect is permitted: washing your hands on concrete from which the water later trickles onto the grass — unintended and indirect ( gerama ). The same for flushing toilets or drains that run into the garden. If you intend to water the grass, it is forbidden. Flowers in a vase — clearing up the confusion Cut flowers are detached , so water cannot make them grow — there is no Torah prohibition . Flowers not yet fully opened (e.g. half-opened roses): a rabbinic prohibition on putting them in water — it looks like planting. This applies even if the vase was filled before Shabbat, and even to put back a flower that fell out. Fully opened flowers: Sephardim may fill a vase from the tap and put them in on Shabbat. Flowers are not muktseh — vases and flowers may be moved, and flowers taken out. A lulav (or anything muktseh) may not have water added on Shabbat. Flower pots: watering a pot that stands on earth with drainage holes is a Torah prohibition; indoors, a rabbinic one — do not water plant pots on Shabbat. Moving them may be permitted in some circumstances — ask. Questions from the floor Plant food that comes with a bouquet? — Fine if the flowers are fully open; into a plant pot it would be forbidden by the Torah. Removing dead flowers from the vase? — Large, clearly distinct flowers are not a \"mixture\", so it is not Borer. Flowers that open in daylight? — Moving them to a sunny windowsill so that they open is a problem (because of the light). Part C — Kotser (Harvesting) Definition Kotser = detaching something from its source of growth. Once detached, cutting it further is not Kotser — e.g. breaking a cut myrtle (hadas) leaf to release its scent, or snapping the stems of a bouquet by hand (scissors are muktseh anyway). Flower pots: bringing a perforated pot standing on soil indoors detaches it from its source of nutrients — a Torah prohibition; taking an indoor pot outside onto earth is planting. Cress sold growing in its punnet — cutting it on Shabbat is real harvesting , very possibly a Torah prohibition. Cut it before Shabbat and keep it in the fridge. Weeds or plants growing in a non-earth spot where they took root — still their place of growth; detaching them is forbidden. Walking through grass is fine; running through long grass should be avoided (it may inevitably uproot). Touching and smelling plants Attached plant Touch? Smell? Soft stem, inedible (e.g. grass) Yes Yes Hard stem (e.g. lavender, rosemary, cinnamon tree) No — one may snap it Yes, without touching Edible, even soft (e.g. strawberry plant) No — one may pick it No — enjoying the smell may tempt you to pick and eat it When smelling fragrant plants without holding them, most hold one should still say the bracha on the scent. Using trees Chazal were very strict about trees. One may not climb a tree on Shabbat — remarkably, one who climbed it deliberately and then repented must stay up until Shabbat ends (one who climbed by mistake may come down). Using a tree also includes: Leaning on it to the extent that you would fall if it were removed (merely touching it is fine). Prodding a ball out of a tree or bush. Hanging a coat on a branch — or even on a peg directly fixed to the tree . Something twice removed is permitted: A hammock tied directly to the tree — forbidden; hung from pegs or hooks fixed to the tree — permitted. A ladder leaning directly on a tree — forbidden; on a peg attached to the tree — permitted (assuming the ladder is designated for a permitted use, else it is muktseh). Summary Choresh: anything that improves soil for planting — fertilising, weeding, levelling, making holes. Walking sticks, buggies, heels and studs are permitted (no intent). No marbles or golf on earth; indoor tiled floors are fine. Balls are arguably not muktseh today; competitive football isn't in the spirit of Shabbat. Zore'a: anything that assists growth. Don't pour water or wash hands onto grass; indirectly via concrete is fine. Fully opened cut flowers may be put in water; unopened ones may not. Don't water plant pots. Kotser: detaching from the source of growth — beware plant pots and cress. Don't touch hard-stemmed or edible plants; smell inedible ones freely. Don't climb or lean on trees; a hammock only via pegs. Coming Up Part 2 of the course resumes on 15 January: Cutting, Cleaning and Colouring — haircuts and nails, cleaning and washing clothes, make-up on Shabbat. Later: Muktseh, healing on Shabbat, and asking a non-Jew. Updates on the Tiferet Eyal website. B'Ezrat Hashem","ch0JBHIEP0Q":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, continued: the three approaches to aggadah, and what Olam HaBa is Shiur: Part 2 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 9 September 2025 (before Rosh Hashanah) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Five mistaken views of the ultimate reward: Gan Eden (and Gehinom); Mashiach's utopia (food growing on trees); resurrection (eat, drink, be merry); this world , as in Bechukotai ( if you keep My mitzvot… rain, land ); and all combined . Their shared traits: crude and physical , and all well supported , from explicit verses (the fourth) to many Rabbinic statements (the first). What no one discusses: Olam HaBa , and how everything else fits around it. Instead: will I rise clothed? The parable: the less mature, the cruder the reward: sweets → clothes → money → fame . All she-lo lishmah . The ideal: במצותיו חפץ מאד , loving the mitzvot, not their reward. We do the truth because it's true. Why doesn't the Torah mention reward in Olam HaBa? (A question every Rishon discusses; Monday night's class started on it yesterday.) For the Rambam it's simple: the Torah promises no reward, physical or spiritual, because reward isn't why you do mitzvot. The blessings and curses in this week's parashah are aids to serving Hashem, or obstacles to it. Three Ways to Read the Sages' Aggadah Before Olam HaBa, one more introduction, because every mistaken view cites aggadah . Group 1: everything literal (the majority) הם מאמינים אותם כפשוטם, ואין סוברים בהן פירוש נסתר בשום פנים, והנמנעות כולם אצלם מחויבות המציאות. \"Most of what I've seen, read and heard.\" They take every aggadah literally , with no hidden meaning , so impossibilities become necessities . פשט comes from להפשיט , to strip , as in the Shabbat labour mafshit , skinning an animal. \"Strip it down, and that's all there is .\" The Moreh's three categories: impossible (the sun setting in the east), possible (rain next week), necessary (the sun rising in the east). Group 1 turns the impossible into the necessary , \"because the Sages said it.\" Why? They never developed, and no teacher ever woke them up to ask perhaps there's something deeper? Tell their beliefs to ordinary people, let alone the wise , and they'd be astonished anyone could believe them. ויש להצטער על סכלותם, לפי שהם מכבדים ומנשאים החכמים כפי דעתם, והם משפילים אותם בתכלית השפלות. \"It's sad.\" They think they're honouring the Sages by believing every word, and they're degrading them utterly , making them look like fools. \" How many times do we see this? \" וחי השם, כי הכת הזאת מאבדים הדרת התורה ומאפילים זהרה. By God's life: this group destroys the Torah's glory and darkens its light. The Torah promises the nations will say רק עם חכם ונבון הגוי הגדול הזה ; these make them say \"a foolish, silly little nation.\" Who's to blame? The preachers ורוב מה שעושים זה הדרשנים , who explain to the masses what they don't understand. מי יתן החרש תחרישון (Iyov): if only they'd keep quiet ! Or at least say: we don't understand what the Sages meant here. \"Preaching the aggadot of Berachot and Perek Helek word for word\" : e.g. \"Hashem wears tefillin\" (Berachot), or the strange dream interpretations, without even saying there's a deeper meaning I may not understand . Rav Shilat's insight: it's the same problem as the five views. Read the Sages stripped to the surface , and the reward is just the surface : wine flowing in Gan Eden, and nothing beyond. The Ben Ish Chai's proof The Ben Ish Chai (\"a great kabbalist, with interesting shitot \") brings, in the name of the Rambam's son , a proof from Eruvin 63a : A student ruled in front of his teacher , Rabbi Eliezer, who said to his wife: I'll be surprised if he lives out the year. Are you a prophet? No, but I've been taught: one who rules before his teacher is liable to death. He died. Rabbah bar bar Chanah, citing Rabbi Yochanan: his name was Yehudah ben Guria . Why name him and his father? שלא תאמר משל היה : so you won't say it was a parable. The Ben Ish Chai: a great and powerful proof that the Talmud's stories are generally parable . If even this unmiraculous story needed names to show it really happened, how much more the wondrous ones, like Rabbah bar bar Chanah's tales in Bava Batra. \" This shuts the mouths of those who mock our Sages. \" Who agreed with the Rambam? Ibn Ezra (introduction to the Torah), the Kuzari , the Meiri . Some Tosafists took aggadah literally. Rav Yaakov Emden's criticism: \" I heard that in the Spanish Inquisition the simple Jews gave their lives, while the Rambam-ist philosophers converted\": don't mock the simple. \"Whether it's historically true, I don't know. Two approaches : some Sages were more sympathetic; the Rambam wasn't. \" Rav Chaim Brisker: \"A nebach apikoros is also an apikoros .\" I'm sorry you're a fool, but it's still your problem. Group 2: literal, therefore ridiculous They too read only the surface, and conclude the Sages were fools. They mock, considering themselves wiser : often those who style themselves physicians and astrologers , \" wise in their own eyes, sharp, philosophers. \" והם סכלים יותר מן הכת הראשונה… והיא כת ארורה. More foolish than the first group, a cursed group : they attack great men of proven wisdom. If they understood how wisdom must be written for the masses, they'd see the Sages were wise. \"Group 1 he pities. Group 2 makes him angry. \" Group 3: there's always something deeper והם, חי השם, מעטים עד מאד, עד שאין ראוי לקרוא אותם כת, אלא כמו שיאמר לשמש מין. So few, \"by God's life,\" they barely count as a group : as one wouldn't speak of \"the species of sun.\" They've seen, in a few scattered statements, how profound the Sages were, and so understand: - the Sages don't speak nonsense ; - their words have a revealed side and a hidden side ; - every impossible statement is riddle and parable , the way of the great sages . Proofs: - Shlomo opens Mishlei: להבין משל ומליצה, דברי חכמים וחידתם . A chidah means the hidden, not the plain: אחודה נא לכם חידה (Shimshon). - Shir HaShirim : \"a very romantic, erotic love story,\" yet a parable for something much deeper. - The Sages themselves turn verses into parables (Berachot 18b): - Benayahu ben Yehoyada הכה את שני אריאל מואב : \"smote two lions\"? The two Temples. - ירד והכה את הארי בתוך הבור : \"struck the lion in the pit\"? He mastered Vayikra , which sits \"in the middle of\" the Torah . - David's מי ישקני מים מבור בית לחם : a pressing halachic question . - Iyov : some say משל היה . Yechezkel's dead : some say משל היה . \" If the Sages read verses as parables, why fear reading the Sages' words as parables? \" \"Leave now\" ואם אתה המעיין מאחת משתי הכתות הראשונות, אל תשגיח בדברי. If you're in group 1 or 2, stop reading. It won't help you; you'll hate it. Light food. One used to bad, heavy food can't enjoy light, wholesome food. \"Once you live healthily you look at people eating rubbish: how do they eat that? And they look at you: how can you not enjoy Coke, this pizza? \" Like Israel and the manna, הלחם הקלקל : degraded, or light, so easy to digest . \"They want heavy meat and fish; they can't appreciate the delicacy .\" But if you're group 3: when a statement seems far-fetched, stop, think, know it's a riddle and parable , and go to bed troubled by it , searching for its sense. Then read my book, and it will shine for you. Then why is the Rambam so hard on group 1? The Sages wrote simply on purpose , for children and the masses (Introduction to the Mishnah). Not everyone can be wise; in Hilchot Teshuvah we said not everyone reaches the highest levels ( Todd ). So why fault the simple? The answer: \" It's fine to be a simpleton and read Hashem wears tefillin or Batya's arm grew long and take it at face value, as long as you know there's a deeper meaning you don't yet understand. For now I take it simply; one day I'll grow. Then you're group 3. \" \" Group 1 is when that's your shitah : when you insist the surface is all, and get angry when someone explains it otherwise: Chazal said X, how dare you! How can it be a grievance that I explain the Sages in a way that brings them more glory? That's the problem.\" (So the Tosafists who held it literally? \" That's the group. \") Olam HaBa The blind can't perceive colours , the deaf can't perceive sounds , a eunuch can't perceive physical desire. So bodies can't perceive the pleasures of the soul. (Correct editions say תאוות הביאה , not the cruder word: \" the Rambam would never use that word. \") And just as fish don't know the element of fire , since they exist in its opposite, water , so in this physical world we can't know the delight of the spiritual world. Why add the fish? Rav Shilat: a blind man lives among colours; the fish's whole environment is the opposite of fire. Olam HaBa is diametrically opposed to our entire physical existence. All we know is physical pleasure : food, drink, intimacy. Anything else we don't recognise at first thought, only after great investigation . \"This bothered me\": earlier, can't grasp it; here, can, after great study . \" Levels. After great analysis, as we're doing, we grasp it conceptually . Never really.\" The difference Physical pleasures Spiritual pleasures Inferior, and they end Constant and eternal No relation, no comparison at all The angels, stars and spheres (the Rambam believed they have awareness ; Rav Saadia didn't, and Rav Saadia was seemingly right ) have no bodily pleasure , yet constant delight in knowing the Creator . So we, purified and elevated after death , won't want bodily pleasures: Like a king. Like a great king who wouldn't want to give up his kingdom to go back to playing ball with the children , as he did before he reigned, back when he couldn't tell the two apart. \" How did I ever behave like that? You know those moments.\" A taste, even here Even in this world, we choose non-physical goods over physical: We sacrifice physical pleasure for… Honour and status : we toil for wealth and titles largely for recognition , \" not tangible \" Revenge : people give up sleep and money to get even, \"unfortunately we see a lot\" Avoiding disgrace : many shun the greatest bodily temptation for fear of shame A good name \" Look how feeble we are. In a few weeks, Yom Kippur : have an easy fast! 24 hours without food is so hard. And yet we give up physical cravings for non-tangible things.\" How much more so in the spiritual world, where our souls know the Creator as the higher beings do: a delight that can't be divided, described or compared . מה רב טובך אשר צפנת ליראיך (Tehillim 31). I can't tell you what it is. \"Their crowns on their heads\" העולם הבא אין בו לא אכילה ולא שתיה ולא רחיצה ולא סיכה ולא תשמיש המטה, אלא צדיקים יושבים ועטרותיהם בראשיהם ונהנים מזיו השכינה (Berachot 17a). Phrase The Rambam עטרותיהם בראשיהם השארות הנפש בקיום המושכל : the soul endures through what it has understood . \" The true soul is a person's intellectual identity \": the knower and the known are one , as the philosophers proved נהנים מזיו השכינה Those souls delight in what they grasp of the truth of the Creator , as the Chayot HaKodesh do The goal, and the ultimate loss הנה כי הטובה והתכלית האחרון הוא להגיע לחברה העליונה הזאת : to reach this exalted society , the soul enduring endlessly , sustained by the Creator who is the cause of its existence. The greatest good, beyond comparison : how can the eternal compare to what ends ? למען ייטב לך והארכת ימים : \"in the world that is wholly long.\" והרעה השלמה והנקמה הגדולה היא שתכרת הנפש ותאבד : the soul is cut off and lost. That's karet : הכרת תכרת : cut off in this world, cut off in the next . Versus והיתה נפש אדני צרורה בצרור החיים . One who chose bodily pleasure and despised the truth \"remains mere matter, cut off .\" כל הנביאים כלם לא נתנבאו אלא לימות המשיח, אבל העולם הבא עין לא ראתה אלהים זולתך. The prophets spoke only of Mashiach's days; Olam HaBa, no eye has seen. \"We'll never fully comprehend it till the day we die . When we get there, we'll experience it. Just as the unhealthy can't comprehend health .\" Before the Yamim Noraim \"If only we could spend a few moments on Rosh Hashanah and Yom Kippur thinking about this, rather than only the book of life, Hashem give me this and that . I say it every year because I'm saying it to myself. It's fine to pray for a good year, but let that not be the focus. \" The Ramchal (as far as I remember): זכרנו לחיים and מי כמוך… זוכר יצוריו ברחמים לחיים : plain life . Then וכתוב לחיים טובים and בספר חיים, ברכה ושלום . \" Once life needs an adjective, it's physical. The first two ask for life itself : a life with Hashem, a taste of Olam HaBa now. Who will I be this year? Will I grow, be closer to Hashem, more perfect morally and intellectually? \" A participant: \"This makes it attainable. Otherwise it's scales in the sky, begging, and no lasting change ; Yom Kippur ends, the magic's done .\" \" Exactly. So people make it physical: take on one kabbalah . One man told me his Yom Kippur kabbalah was saying Birkat HaMazon from the book . Lovely, but look at the big picture. \" \"It's more comfortable to escape the truth.\" \" Correct , rather than address the core issues .\" Summary The Torah promises no reward : mitzvot aren't done for one. Three readings of aggadah: literal (the majority, \"honouring\" the Sages while degrading them, blamed on preachers ); literal, therefore foolish (a \" cursed \" group, more foolish than the first); always deeper (so few they're like \"the species of sun\"). The Ben Ish Chai's proof (Yehudah ben Guria). Parables in Tanach (Shir HaShirim, Benayahu's lions). 3. It's fine to be simple if you know there's more . The fault is insisting there isn't. 4. Olam HaBa can't be perceived by the physical : the blind and colours, the fish and fire . We sense its reality only in our willingness to trade the physical for honour or a good name. 5. \"Crowns on their heads\" = the soul's intellectual identity , enduring; \"the radiance of the Shechinah\" = knowing Hashem . Eternal and incomparable. Karet = the soul cut off . 6. Before Rosh Hashanah: pray for חיים , not just חיים טובים . Coming Up Next week: how Gan Eden, Gehinom, Mashiach and techiyat hametim fit around Olam HaBa , and then the 13 Principles begin. (No halachah shiur this Wednesday or Thursday.)","d9Zhz8VfvWQ":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 9 of 14 Topic: Melaben (Cleaning and Laundering) and Kosher/Matir (Tying and Untying) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 22 January 2019 (a snowy night) Schedule note: The course was now about a lecture behind; trapping, slaughtering, sewing and tearing moved to the next shiur (5 February). Recap Last week: Gozez — cutting hair and nails, combing — and Sove'a — no dyeing with food and drink, make-up, sunbathing, toilet colouring blocks. Part A — Melaben (Cleaning) Definition In the Mishkan, sheared wool had to be cleaned before it could be spun. Melaben is cleaning the fabric itself — removing something absorbed into the material. Removing something sitting on the surface (e.g. feathers all over your clothes) is not cleaning — permitted. Three stages — each a separate Torah prohibition Soaking in water — begins releasing the dirt. Scrubbing — dislodges it. Squeezing out the water. Even if two stages happened before Shabbat or by themselves, the third is still forbidden. So: don't run the tap onto a dirty shirt in the sink; don't put water on a stain; if rain fell on a stain, don't scrub it; don't wring out a wet garment. Soaking a clean garment Some Rishonim hold that soaking even a clean garment adds cleanliness. Halacha: soaking a clean garment is permitted. A clean towel may be soaked to cool a headache. A clean towel may be used to wipe up spilt water — but not a dirty (soiled) one. Don't saturate it so much that lifting it inevitably squeezes water out — use another towel; a soaked towel is also a concern because one might come to wring it out. Paper towels/tissues may be soaked completely and thrown away — but don't squeeze them while lifting. Coloured spills As discussed last week, a bleeding finger may be wrapped in a white towel (better to rinse under the tap first, but strictly permitted). For red juice or wine spills, prefer paper tissues to a reusable towel or handkerchief — disposables raise no colouring question. With no alternative, a towel may be used. Removing a stain without water Dried mud on clothes: is scraping it off Tochen (grinding)? One opinion says no — earth was already \"ground\" ( ein tochen achar tochen , like crumbling a biscuit) — but we don't rely on that. Rather: Removing what is on top of the fabric with a fingernail — permitted by all. The stain remaining in the fabric — preferably ask a non-Jew to scrape it with a nail. If none is available and you need to look presentable in public, there is room to scrape it yourself. Never with water — and you may not ask a non-Jew to use water (a Torah prohibition), except in extreme circumstances. Dry mud on shoes may likewise be scraped off — wet mud is a different matter. Dust on clothes A hat, jacket or dress that fell on a dusty floor: for Sephardim, dust sits on the surface, so one may shake or brush it off — even with a clothes brush. Since one view (followed by Ashkenazim) considers it a Torah prohibition, preferably do it with a shinui , e.g. the left hand. Brushing a hat is fine. Spreading out wet clothes Chazal forbade spreading out wet clothes to dry — because of mar'it ayin (onlookers may think you washed them on Shabbat) — and mar'it ayin applies even in private ( afilu bechadrei chadarim asur ). So no spreading on a banister, airer or washing line, even indoors. Clipping a garment with one peg so it just hangs down is not \"spreading\" — permitted (a peg is a kli shemelachto le'issur , usable for a permitted purpose). Two pegs spreading it out is a problem. Hanging a wet coat in its usual place , e.g. on a coat hook or hanger — fine. A jacket over the back of a chair, as normal — fine; spreading a dress over a chair — not. Wet socks — you can't spread them out to dry; keep them on, or borrow dry ones. Wet items on a radiator — a Torah prohibition Placing something wet next to a heat source is forbidden by the Torah: Bishul (heating the water) and Melaben (heat-drying is part of laundering). A heated towel rail may keep a dry towel warm — but don't dry your hands on it and hang it back, and never put a wet tea towel on a radiator. Q: A hat dropped in the dirt and blown about by the wind? — You may brush it off; that is not a \"growing\" (Zore'a) issue. Part B — Kosher and Matir (Tying and Untying) Background In the Mishkan, the techelet dye came from the chilazon (whether we have it today is debated — hence some wear tzitzit with blue threads); catching it required nets , made by knotting cords. Spools of thread were also knotted end to end. Two views of what a Torah-level knot is All agree a Torah-level knot must be permanent ( kayama ). The debate is whether it must also be a professional/craftsman's knot (like the Mishnah's sailors' and camel-drivers' knots): View Torah prohibition Rabbinic prohibition Practical rule Rif, Rambam, Shulchan Aruch — Sephardim Permanent and professional Either one alone An ordinary knot meant to be untied within 7 days is permitted; a professional knot is forbidden even for an hour Rashi, Rosh, Rema — Ashkenazim Permanent, even if not professional Any knot meant to last a while A knot must be meant to be untied within 24 hours Shoelaces Sephardim may tie shoelaces intending to untie them within a week — e.g. on Shavuot night, staying up for more than 24 hours. Those who tie shoes once and then slip them on and off for weeks must never tie that first knot on Shabbat. Double knots The Rema treats a double knot as a craftsman's knot (Ashkenazim avoid it even for an hour). The Chida (Rav Chaim Yosef David Azulai), who travelled widely, records the custom in all Sephardi lands: a double knot is not professional. So Sephardim may tie a double knot to be untied within seven days — shoelaces, a woman's headscarf, the bands of a Sefer Torah (the Spanish and Portuguese custom). Even a Yom Tov Sefer Torah, which may not be opened again for months, may be tied with a double knot — a special case with its own reasoning. A bow is not a knot A bow ( aniva ) slips undone with a pull — not a knot at all, and may be tied even for the long term. A tie (necktie) is also an aniva and may be tied on Shabbat even if kept tied for weeks. (Shoelaces are a single knot plus a bow.) Knots that are normally permanent Intending to untie within a week doesn't help for knots that are by nature permanent : Tzitzit that have come undone — may not be re-tied, even \"temporarily\". Even tightening a loose tzitzit knot is making a knot — possibly a Torah prohibition. A broken eruv cord — may not be re-tied by a Jew; here one may ask a non-Jew. Dustbin bags Permanence counts even if you get no benefit from it: a double knot on a bin bag will stay tied for good. Use a simple single knot (one twist of the two ends), which has no permanence at all. Q: two single knots, one on each pair of corners? — each is just a single knot: fine. Beware the overhand knot: a knot tied within one strand locks on itself — it has the status of a double knot (only if untied within seven days). Untying The same rules apply in reverse: one may untie any knot that is not professional and was not made to last more than seven days. Q: Taking the bins out on Shabbat? — Raises carrying and muktseh questions; possible in certain circumstances — to be discussed in the Muktseh shiur. Summary Melaben = cleaning the fabric itself. Soaking, scrubbing and wringing are each forbidden. Clean towels may be wetted; prefer paper for coloured spills. Surface dirt may be removed; a dry stain preferably by a non-Jew with a fingernail — never with water. Dust may be shaken or brushed off (ideally with a shinui). Don't spread wet clothes out to dry, even privately; hanging from one peg or in the normal place is fine. Nothing wet on a radiator or heated rail. Knots (Sephardim): any non-professional knot — single or double — to be untied within 7 days; bows and neckties always fine. Never re-tie or tighten tzitzit; bin bags with a simple single knot. Coming Up Break next week. Shiur 10 — 5 February: Trapping — pets, birds flying in, mousetraps; putting a baby in a cot; returning an escaped prisoner Slaughtering — flies in the house, infested vegetables, fly spray Tearing — envelopes and packaging B'Ezrat Hashem","dBrduh1DVJ4":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 12 of 14 Topic: Makeh Be'Patish (the Final Hammer Blow); Mav'ir and Mechabeh (Fire) — swimming and electricity; Medicine on Shabbat, Part 1 — Pikuach Nefesh Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 5 March 2019 Schedule note: Asking a non-Jew moved to the final shiur; Muktseh next week. Recap Last week: writing and erasing (puzzles, cakes, CCTV), building and demolishing (Lego, cans and bottles, umbrellas). Part A — Makeh Be'Patish (The Final Hammer Blow) Completing an object The last blow that gives a built object its finished form. Any finishing touch is forbidden: Opening the stitched-shut pockets of a new suit. Cutting open the uncut pages of a new book. Threading shoelaces into new shoes for the first time (re-inserting laces that came out is fine). Stuffing new feathers into a pillow (putting back feathers that came out is fine). Inflating a new ball for the first time (re-inflating a deflated ball, even with a pump, is fine). Balloons — no, as you would come to tie them. Breaking off a splinter from a detached branch to use as a toothpick — you've made a tool. Repairing Repairs are also Makeh Be'Patish — e.g. bending back a bent piece of cutlery, oiling a creaking door (WD-40). Q: why may laces and feathers be put back, but repairs not? — Repair is forbidden where it takes skill and effort ; simply putting something back where it belongs takes none. Q: Tying a new necktie? — Fine; the tie is already a finished item. Toveling a utensil on Shabbat Some say immersing a new food utensil in the mikveh \"completes\" it. Others: the utensil itself is finished — the need for tevilah is external, a spiritual status. The Shulchan Aruch permits toveling on Shabbat (where carrying is permitted). One who wishes to be stringent should do it before Shabbat; if forgotten, give the utensil to a non-Jew as a gift and borrow it back — a non-Jew's utensil needs no tevilah (this works indefinitely). Three rabbinic extensions Folding clothes on their creases — in Chazal's time it improved the garment. Fold a tallit against its creases (not on them) — don't leave it lying around, which is disrespectful. Many have a custom to fold on the creases, since folding today is no professional act; the Shulchan Aruch does say not to, so only with an established custom. No difference whether the tallit is worn only on Shabbat. Spraying perfume on clothes — molid rei'ach , creating a new scent in the garment. On the body or hair is fine. Musical instruments — forbidden, lest one come to repair them. This underlay the 19th-century Reform–Orthodox controversy over a non-Jew playing an organ in shul (argued as \"shevut deshevut for a mitzvah\"); the Chatam Sofer and others opposed it strongly. The same applies to non-Jewish bands at simchas on Shabbat. Dancing and clapping in rhythm: part of the music decree. The Rema permits (today we don't know how to repair instruments) — hence Ashkenazi practice. The Shulchan Aruch forbids — for Sephardim: no dancing (lifting the feet, as at a wedding) or clapping to a song. Clapping to wake someone or to quieten shul is fine; walking in a circle isn't dancing. On Simchat Torah dancing is permitted. If someone won't accept a well-meant rebuke on a rabbinic matter, the Rema says better to stay silent ( mutav sheyihyu shogegin ). Part B — Mav'ir and Mechabeh (Kindling and Extinguishing) Fire was used throughout the Mishkan (e.g. smelting gold). Driving a car — the engine combusts — is a Torah prohibition. Swimming on Shabbat Open water (sea, lake) — swimming (both feet off the ground) is forbidden by all : Chazal feared one would make a float or raft. Wading in the sea with your feet on the ground is fine — even up to the neck (not in clothes not made for water). An enclosed pool — five possible issues: Squeezing hair — just don't; wrap a towel round it. Wringing the swimsuit — a swimsuit is made to get wet, so wetting it is fine; just don't wring it (or use spin-dryers). Drying with a towel — towels are made to get wet; fine. Carrying — at an outdoor pool in a public area, dry yourself fully before walking around, or you carry the water. Hot water — the main issue: Chazal forbade bathing in hot water even if heated before Shabbat (people were heating water on Shabbat and claiming otherwise). Hot = above lukewarm; Chacham Ovadia: about 37°C and below is lukewarm and permitted — only if heated before Shabbat . Water being heated on Shabbat (as in many hotels) is forbidden even if lukewarm — check first. Strict halacha: swimming in an enclosed, lukewarm pool heated before Shabbat is permitted, with those precautions. Whether it suits the spirit of Shabbat is another question — for a child on holiday, reasonable; an adult every week, not ideal (though better than sleeping all afternoon). We don't invent new decrees: \"if you start forbidding what is permitted, people end up doing what is really forbidden.\" Q: Natural hot springs (e.g. Tiberias, Ein Gedi)? — The Shulchan Aruch discusses the springs of Tiberias: permitted. Q: Wet sand? — Walking on it is an unwanted pesik reisha , fine; playing with wet sand is Lash , forbidden. Hot mikvaot Many mikvaot are hotter than 37°C (the men's mikveh in Golders Green about 43°C). The Korban Netanel (17th century) argues Chazal forbade bathing , not immersion — a novel view Sephardim prefer not to rely on. So men should use only a lukewarm mikveh on Shabbat morning, and women should preferably immerse on Friday night before nightfall (during bein hashemashot). Electricity Chazon Ish: completing a circuit is Boneh — a major novelty, rejected by many (e.g. Rav Shmuel Wosner). Mav'ir: scientifically, a closed circuit isn't fire. But an incandescent (filament) bulb glows red-hot — Torah-level Mav'ir. Makeh Be'Patish: completing a functional circuit — some forbid even LEDs on this basis. Chacham Ovadia: electricity (apart from glowing filaments) is rabbinic — very important for when a non-Jew may be asked, and for dimming an LED. Q: Ashkenazim who ask a non-Jew to switch on a light? — Two separate issues: instructing a non-Jew and benefiting from his work. Even for Sephardim, one may not explicitly ask for a light where there is no existing light; details in the final shiur. Part C — Medicine on Shabbat, Part 1 Why medicine is restricted Historically medicines were herbal and had to be ground . People anxious about their health ( adam bahul al gufo ) might grind herbs on Shabbat, so Chazal made a blanket decree against medicine for minor ailments. Today almost all medicine is ready-made and few know how to compound remedies — the Chayei Adam argues the decree no longer applies. We do not follow him, but his view is an extra factor for leniency in some cases. Five levels of illness — next week. Pikuach nefesh — danger to life Where there is any possibility of danger to life, one is obligated to break Shabbat. Rav Chaim of Brisk , famously strict, told a congregant who felt faint on Yom Kippur to eat. Challenged on his \"leniency\", he replied: I'm not lenient — I'm stringent about the mitzvah to guard life. Breaking Shabbat for pikuach nefesh is a stringency, not a leniency. Applies to any physical danger, for Jews and non-Jews alike. Mental health too: a young child locked in a toilet, crying and panicking — one may break even Torah prohibitions (call the fire brigade, break the door), since the trauma can last a lifetime. A rabbi who hasn't taught his community this is responsible if someone hesitates and harm results. With a shinui? The Rema: if there is no rush, do it with a deviation (e.g. dial with the weaker hand). The Shulchan Aruch — which we follow: no need for a shinui ; and never where it endangers others (e.g. driving). (Childbirth is different — final shiur.) Jew, not non-Jew: even if a non-Jew is present, it is better done by a Jew — it is taken more seriously, and shows how important pikuach nefesh is. The quicker, the greater the reward. Maggid Mishneh: for a patient in danger, Shabbat is overridden even for needs not directly medical — e.g. switching on music he asks for — since a stronger spirit helps the fight against illness. Postscript — a sukkah roof in the rain Pulling a cover over the sukkah when it rains on Shabbat creates a tent unless it was already open at least a tefach before Shabbat. Summary Makeh Be'Patish: no completing objects (new pockets, pages, laces, balls) or repairs; toveling permitted (or gift it to a non-Jew); fold a tallit against its creases; no perfume on clothes; no instruments; Sephardim don't dance or clap to music. Swimming: never in open water; an enclosed lukewarm pool heated before Shabbat is strictly permitted (don't squeeze hair or swimsuit, dry before walking outdoors) — consider the spirit of Shabbat. Avoid hot mikvaot on Shabbat. Electricity: per Chacham Ovadia rabbinic, except glowing filaments. Pikuach nefesh: an obligation, including mental health; no shinui needed; better done by a Jew. Coming Up Shiur 13 — 12 March: Completing medicine on Shabbat (the five levels of illness), then Muktseh . Final shiur: children, babies and childbirth, and asking a non-Jew. B'Ezrat Hashem","dZ2EDdSVhns":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 11 of 14 Topic: Kotev and Mochek (Writing and Erasing); Boneh and Soter (Building and Demolishing) — puzzles, CCTV, Lego, umbrellas, buggy hoods, cans and bottles Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 26 February 2019 Schedule note: Makeh Be'Patish and Mav'ir/Mechabeh (fire, swimming, electricity) were not reached and moved to the next shiur. Part A — Kotev and Mochek (Writing and Erasing) In the Mishkan The Mishkan's beams were numbered so each could return to exactly the same place every time it was re-erected — because of ma'alin bakodesh ve'ein moridin : a beam that stood nearer the Kodesh HaKodashim may not be moved to a less holy position (as worn-out tefillin straps may not become a belt). Mistakes were erased — hence Mochek. What counts as writing Drawing a picture is Kotev — writing means creating any message, in words or images. (Colouring it in afterwards is Sove'a.) Torah-level only when written in the usual manner and permanently . The weaker hand — only rabbinic. This has major implications for doctors who must work on Shabbat — a subject in its own right, no ruling given here. Temporary writing (in sand, on a misted window, in ashes, with liquid on a table) — rabbinic. Tracing letters in the air or with a dry finger on a table — permitted; too transient to forbid. Puzzles and bringing letters together Doesn't completing a jigsaw \"draw\" a picture? Similar case: a word printed across the edge of a book's pages — closing the book \"writes\" it, opening it \"erases\" it. For Sephardim and Ashkenazim alike, this is permitted : writing is creating a letter, not bringing existing parts together. Likewise, putting together a torn note to read it. Jigsaw puzzles are permitted — even interlocking ones (some are stricter about those, but the logic doesn't distinguish). Avoid making a puzzle you intend to keep assembled for months. Shabbat is meant for more spiritual things than a whole afternoon of puzzles — fine for children. Q: Fridge magnets forming words? — Permitted. CCTV Typing letters on a computer is, almost unanimously, Torah-level writing. A camera image is different: before the shutter it is like a mirror — transient; once captured, it is stored as electronic code , not visible text — which many argue is not writing at all. Walking into a place with CCTV (shul, hospital, the Kotel security) — permitted by all : even if it were writing, it is an unwanted pesik reisha at most rabbinic. Wanting to be seen — e.g. knocking at your own door so the family inside can see you on the screen — depends on whether stored images count as writing. Practice: before Shabbat, disconnect it, or keep recording for security but cover the screen inside so no one uses it to see who is at the door. If forgotten, there is room to be lenient. A camera that only displays and doesn't record — like a mirror; no issue. Cutting through writing — cakes and packets Some say avoid cutting through letters on a birthday cake or packet. The halacha: no need to be careful , for several reasons: Erasing is Torah-level only to write in its place — at worst this is rabbinic. An unwanted pesik reisha in a rabbinic matter is permitted. It is done in an unusual, destructive manner. Some Ashkenazim cut between the letters; Sephardim need not. Q: Footprints in snow, or shoes with lettering on the sole? — Transient and unintended: permitted. Part B — Boneh and Soter (Building and Demolishing) Lessons from the Mishkan Permanent doesn't mean forever. The Mishkan was built knowing it would be dismantled at some unknown time — it might stand two days or nineteen years (as at Kadesh). Building \"for now, indefinitely\" is permanent. Demolishing is Torah-level only when done in order to rebuild . Breaking down a door just to retrieve something is rabbinic (a person locked in — that may be pikuach nefesh; next shiur). Forbidden building includes pitching a tent, digging a pit, screwing things in, and levelling a floor in a dwelling (earth floors, a front path) — Boneh, just as levelling a field is Choresh. Lego, sandcastles, plasticine Lego is permitted. Like screwing a lid onto a thermos flask, something made to be constantly put together and taken apart is not \"building\". (Chacham Ovadia) Sandcastles — rabbinically forbidden: sand can be used for real, permanent building (like writing in sand). Plasticine — not a building problem but smoothing ( memare'ach ) — avoid. Ohel — making a tent or roof A permanent tent is a Torah prohibition; even a temporary roof that shelters you is rabbinically forbidden — e.g. throwing a sheet over four existing poles to make shade. But the Gemara permits opening a folding chair or bed ( teraskal ), even though it creates a \"tent\" underneath — the tent already exists, just folded. (Laying a mattress across bare bed-frame planks, though, creates a new roof.) Umbrellas Is opening an umbrella like opening a folding chair? The difference: a folding bed or chair isn't opened for shelter ; an umbrella is. Halacha: opening a pre-existing \"tent\" for shade or protection is rabbinically forbidden — so no opening umbrellas on Shabbat. An umbrella opened before Shabbat, used where there's an eruv — hard to forbid in principle, but it should still be avoided (people would think it was opened on Shabbat; and it is uvda d'chol , a weekday activity). Buggy hoods and parasols Opening a buggy hood from fully closed creates shade — forbidden. Once a roof of a tefach (about 8 cm) exists, extending it further is permitted. So before Shabbat, leave the hood open at least a tefach ; on Shabbat open and close it, never closing it completely. Opening it halfway from closed is still making a tent. You may ask a non-Jew to open it (some permit it outright). Sitting under a garden parasol that was already open — fine. Opening one on Shabbat — like an umbrella (ask a non-Jew). Fixed-open structures — no issue. A hood on your head isn't a tent — no tefach of space. Opening cans, bottles and packets One of the biggest subjects in Hilchot Shabbat. Six potential issues — building (creating a new vessel), demolishing, makeh be'patish , tearing, cutting to size, erasing. For Sephardim almost everything is permitted: Building a vessel? Not if you will throw it away; a container used only until the contents are finished is not a \"kli\". If you intend to reuse the container — forbidden. Demolishing / tearing? The Gemara permits breaking open packaging (e.g. around dates) to get to food ; so too tearing open a milk carton. Cutting to size (pull-tab cans, perforations) — Chacham Ovadia: you don't care about the exact shape of the opening, so it is not mechatech . Likewise tissue boxes (better to open before Shabbat). Item On Shabbat Tins (pickles etc.), unless you plan to reuse the tin Permitted Drink pouches pierced with a straw Permitted Ring-pull cans (Coke) Permitted Wine corks Permitted Screw-cap bottles whose seal ring breaks off (e.g. grape juice) Strictly permitted (Chacham Ovadia: the cap existed as a cap before the ring was attached) — but good to open before Shabbat; if a non-Jew is available, ask them Q: May an Ashkenazi who is strict ask a Sephardi to open a bottle? — Yes. Where the issue is a modern, unresolved debate (Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, Rav Moshe Feinstein — not a clear Rema/Shulchan Aruch split), one strict for himself may ask another who follows the lenient view. (In yeshiva, \"they always came to the Sephardim to open the bottles!\") Where the rulings are clear and divided — e.g. reheating lukewarm soup, permitted to Ashkenazim but not Sephardim — one may not ask across traditions. Tearing connected tissues apart — at the final shiur. Summary Kotev: drawing is writing; temporary or weaker-hand writing is rabbinic; in the air is fine. Puzzles, book-edge words, fridge magnets permitted. CCTV: walking past is fine; to be seen at your door, cover the screen. Cutting through writing on cakes and packets is permitted. Boneh: permanent means \"for now\"; demolishing only Torah-level to rebuild. Lego permitted; sandcastles and plasticine not. Ohel: folding chairs fine; no opening umbrellas; buggy hoods — leave open a tefach before Shabbat. Cans, bottles, packets may be opened (unless reusing the container); grape-juice screw caps ideally before Shabbat. Coming Up Shiur 12 — 5 March: Medicine and non-Jews — taking medicine on Shabbat; asking a non-Jew. Also, if time allows, the remaining material: Makeh Be'Patish (folding a tallit, perfume, clapping and dancing), and Mav'ir/Mechabeh (fire, electricity, swimming). Then Muktseh, and children and babies on Shabbat. B'Ezrat Hashem","fBesEg3dcTA":"Dayan Daniel Kada · Thursday Night Halacha · Shiur 5 of 7 Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 674, 675 and the opening of 676 Where We Are Siman Topic 670 What is forbidden on Hanukah: eulogies, funerals, fasting 671 Where to place the menorah (inside, outside, where outside), and the synagogue menorah 672 When: the precise time to light 673 What: the correct oils and wicks \"So we've got the time, we've got the location, we've got the material.\" Now come further laws about using the lights, lighting one from another, and how exactly the mitzvah is done. Siman 674: Lighting One Hanukah Light From Another מותר להדליק מנר לנר... It is permitted to light from one light to another... Rav versus Shmuel Rav: forbidden to light one Hanukah light from another. Shmuel: permitted. The general rule: in the hundreds of disputes between Rav and Shmuel across Shas, monetary law follows Shmuel (\"Shmuel was the sharp one with financial things\") and issur (prohibitions) follows Rav. This dispute concerns a prohibition, so we would expect to follow Rav. But the Gemara says this is one of only three places in Shas where the halacha follows Shmuel against Rav on an issur. Rav's two reasons ביזוי מצוה, disrespect to the mitzvah: using one mitzvah light to kindle another is not honourable. אכחושי מצוה, weakening the mitzvah: while you draw the flame to light the second, the first weakens for a few moments. Rabbi Sacks' insight Rabbi Jonathan Sacks, of blessed memory, used to explain why Shmuel wins here: Yes, while you light another, the first flame may weaken for a moment. That happens when you have to \"light other people up\": sometimes you have to be weakened for a few moments. But the ultimate good is that there are now two flames, and that is far more powerful than the momentary loss. Directly, or through an intermediary? Within Shmuel's view there is a further question: View Ruling The default (סתם) opinion Permitted only directly, flame to wick. Through an intermediary such as a match there is a ביזוי and it is not permitted \"Some say\" (יש אומרים) Even through an intermediary is permitted The rule for the Shulḥan Arukh: when it gives a סתם followed by יש אומרים, the halacha follows the סתם. So it is permitted, but only directly. Two scenarios 1. One menorah. Second night, one match left. You light the first light and the match goes out. You may not dip the spent match into the first flame and carry it over. You may bring the two wicks together, or lift the second light to the first, light it, and put it back. 2. Two households under one roof. Two separate, financially independent households living in one house, common in the past in large shared homes, each must light its own menorah (Siman 677), because the mitzvah is נר איש וביתו. One may light from the other's menorah, but directly: bring your candle to the other menorah, light it, and take it back. - Some are uneasy, because (as 675 will teach) it is better not to move a menorah at all. One posek permits it because it is brief and both are obligated. Others say the menorahs should be brought together. The main halacha: permitted, directly. Even for the extra (hiddur) lights The second and following lights on one menorah are only mehadrin, not obligatory, yet everyone agrees you may light them from the first. This underscores the earlier theme: the Sages saw something unique in the Hanukah menorah, enough to allow even the hiddur lights to be lit from the obligatory one. But not when there is no mitzvah If a light went out after being lit properly, there is no mitzvah to relight it (הדלקה עושה מצוה, as we learned). So you may not relight it from another Hanukah light, even directly. A first lighting of an extra light is at least a hiddur; relighting one that went out is not even that. That is why we have the shamash. It is not part of the menorah, so once it is lit you may use it freely to light anything. This whole discussion applies only without a shamash. The Rema: the custom not to light even directly The Rema, quoting Tosafot: although we follow Shmuel, it is a good custom not to light one from another even directly, since the essential mitzvah is a single light. Three qualifications: 1. The Shach: even by the Rema, the stringency applies only to one's own menorah. Lighting another household's obligatory menorah from yours is a real mitzvah, and Tosafot and the Rema agree it is permitted (directly). 2. The custom concerns lighting from the first (obligatory) light. You may light the third and fourth from the second, since those are hiddur lights. 3. It applies only during the first half hour. After the half hour there is no more mitzvah (which is why you may extinguish the lights then), so everyone agrees you may light from it, even through an intermediary. 674:2: Hanukah to Shabbat or synagogue lights? יש מי שאומר that one may light from a Hanukah light to the synagogue lights or the Shabbat candles. The Rema adds: even a candle for studying a shiur in the evening. The poskim reject this, because, as learned in 673, one may not benefit from the Hanukah lights even for holy purposes. So one should not light Shabbat candles from the menorah. However, in a genuine emergency (Shabbat is about to begin and this is the only flame), there is room to rely on it, directly. Siman 675: Lighting Makes the Mitzvah, Not Placing הדלקה עושה מצוה, ולא הנחה עושה מצוה. What this permits If placing made the mitzvah (one view in the Gemara), the menorah would have to be put down for the sake of the mitzvah, like sechach: if a non-Jew laid the sechach, you lift it and put it back down. We do not hold that. Case Ruling A menorah that stayed in the same spot all year since last Hanukah Fine. No need to move it and put it back Your non-Jewish cleaner sets the menorah on the windowsill Absolutely fine A Shabbat lamp still burning on Saturday night Extinguish it and relight it; no need to lift and replace it What it still demands ומכל מקום צריך שידליקנה במקום הנחתה. שאם הדליקה בפנים והוציאה לחוץ, לא יצא, שהרואה אומר לצרכו הוא מדליקה. Nonetheless one must light it where it will stand. If he lit it inside and took it outside, he has not fulfilled the mitzvah, because an onlooker says he lit it for his own use. So placing still matters in two ways: 1. Light it where it is meant to stand. Lighting it in an improper place and then moving it to the proper one does not work. 2. You may not simply hold it (below). The reverse: proper place, then moved What if you lit it outside, the ideal place, and after ten minutes, cold, you bring it in to enjoy it? It was lit in the correct place, so the main view seems to permit it. The Mishnah Berurah: preferably don't move the menorah at all within the half hour. If it was lit properly and must be moved, there is room for it. What is definitely wrong: the father is ill in bed; his wife brings the menorah to his bedside, he says the berachah and lights it there, and then it is carried down to the window. That's wrong. The bedside is not the place of obligation, and the lighting is the mitzvah. Better: the wife, or the children over bar/bat mitzvah, light at the window. Questions \"What if my window faces a wall with no view?\" Then light at another window that does have a view. \"Which house does not have a window?\" \"Like a council flat in Brixton?\" \"They have a view. They're not living in prison.\" If the flat is very high (above the street sight line discussed earlier, so a passer-by wouldn't look up and see it), light inside the front door, for yourself and the family, rather than at the window. Otherwise, light at any window with a view. \"I lit in a hotel room under the TV, and it burned the TV.\" \"You should have lit by the entrance of the door rather than by the TV.\" \"I didn't know.\" \"So now you know for next time.\" You can't just hold it A hotel scenario: the hotel forbids lighting for health and safety. Since the basic mitzvah is one light, you'll hold a single candle by the window for half an hour. No. The Sages said that someone holding a candle looks as if they are using it for light, which is what one normally holds a candle for. It must be set down. Enough fuel at the moment of lighting The time of lighting makes the mitzvah, so at that moment there must be fuel for the full half hour. Lighting with too little oil and topping it up a minute later does not work. On Friday afternoon you need oil to last until half an hour after nightfall, about an hour and a half. The small Hanukah candles sold in packs are a problem even for half an hour, and certainly fail on Friday. You cannot add fuel once Shabbat begins, and from the Hanukah perspective it wouldn't help anyway. Again: the Shulḥan Arukh writes this as יש מי שאומר, \"there is one who says.\" That does not mean he disagrees. He found it in one source and brings it in that form. Everyone agrees with this ruling. 675: Women and Children Women אשה מדלקת נר חנוכה, שאף היא חייבת בה. A woman may light, since she too is obligated. The Shulḥan Arukh means more than a woman living alone: a woman may light on behalf of the household, including her husband. Why obligated, when women are exempt from positive time-bound mitzvot? Hanukah is positive and time-bound, but: 1. The decree was upon them too: brides were taken to the ruler before their weddings. 2. The salvation came through a woman: Yehudit, daughter of Yoḥanan Kohen Gadol, who killed the ruler. So men and women are equally obligated. Then why does the husband usually light? The general principle: when husband and wife are both obligated, both present and both able, the בעל הבית should perform it. The Gemara says it is improper otherwise. When the wife should light: - He is away: on a business trip for a day or more, she lights at home, and his obligation is fulfilled through his household. - She knows the berachot and he doesn't (\"she's more religious than him\"). He should learn them, but if he can't, she lights. - He is ill and can't come down. - He is coming home very late: she lights at the proper time, rather than waiting (as discussed under 672). Children Although women and children are often grouped together, a child does not have the same obligation, even one who has reached חינוך. A child's obligation is one of education, not the real obligation. The main halacha in the Shulḥan Arukh: if a child lights the menorah, the mitzvah has not been done, even if an adult placed it. Placing is irrelevant; the lighting makes the mitzvah. \"Some say\" a child who has reached ḥinuch may light. But for Megillah the Shulḥan Arukh clearly rules that a child cannot read for others, and the laws are comparable. So lechatḥilah, the father or mother lights. Who lights which light? (third night, father, mother, a ten-year-old) Light Who The berachot and the first (obligatory) light The father: the mother can, but when both are present he does The second light The mother may, \"and I believe should be encouraged to participate\" Further lights Children who have reached ḥinuch: see below The shamash Children under ḥinuch (four or five years old) The extra lights by children of ḥinuch age: - The Ben Ish Ḥai: no. Each light is a hiddur and a mitzvah, and a child doesn't perform the mitzvah. - The Syrian custom, which goes back a long time: once the father has lit the night's first light, he hands the remaining lights to each member of the household who has reached ḥinuch, boys and girls. \"I remember clearly growing up in my house... my father would light the first candle, and then the boys and the girls would light the other ones.\" The Rabbi calls it \"a beautiful minhag to encourage everyone to participate in the mitzvah,\" and there is \"ample room\" for it, though some poskim want every light except the shamash lit by someone over bar mitzvah. Children under ḥinuch should not light any of the night's lights: although they are only hiddur, the child has no understanding, and the lighting makes the mitzvah. In the Rabbi's home, the four- or five-year-old lights the shamash, which has no significance. Ashkenazim (Rema): each person lights their own menorah, and a child who reaches ḥinuch lights. The general Ashkenazi custom today, as the Rabbi understands it, is that children light their own menorah from bar/bat mitzvah. \"For us anyway we light one menorah.\" What is \"the age of ḥinuch\"? There is no fixed age. It means the age at which a child understands what is being done: sometimes five, sometimes six, sometimes more. \"Sometimes you get these child prodigies,\" and some children are seven or eight before they are ready. It depends on the child. A Ḥeresh and a Blind Person A ḥeresh (deaf and mute) has the halachic status of someone whose intellect is not considered developed, is not obligated, and cannot light on others' behalf. A blind person is a different story. Throughout Shas, a blind person is obligated in every mitzvah possible for them: Megillah, shofar and so on. (A ḥeresh need not hear the Megillah or shofar.) May a blind person light, and say the berachah? Best practice: if he lives with family, his wife or a child over bar mitzvah lights and he listens and fulfils the mitzvah. If alone, with someone to guide his hand: - The Mishnah Berurah cites one posek: he lights with assistance, without a berachah, since he cannot see the lights. - The vast majority disagree. Compare the morning berachah פוקח עורים and, before Shema, יוצר המאורות, a whole berachah about luminaries a blind person cannot see. He says them, because the light benefits others, and others help him. Likewise, Hanukah is pirsumei nisa for others. Even though it also applies to oneself, there is definitely an element of publicising the miracle outside. So a blind person may light and say all the berachot. Strictly speaking, if a man who has lit for his family for many years becomes blind, ḥas veshalom, and wants to keep his mitzvah, his wife holds his hand, and he says the berachot and lights. Mark asked: isn't this like a blind person being called to the Torah? The Rabbi: the final halacha does allow a blind person an aliyah with the berachot, though the Shulḥan Arukh originally did not. It changed because originally each person read his own portion; now the ḥazzan reads for everyone, \"so it's slightly different there.\" Another participant: isn't it depressing for a blind man to light something he can't see? The Rabbi: \"We can't assume to know how blind people feel... To bring light to the world, even if you can't see the light yourself. We do many things in life where we don't see the light in it for ourselves, but we see the light is for other people, and that's worthwhile.\" A Convert A ger may say all the berachot, including שעשה ניסים לאבותינו, \"who performed miracles for our fathers.\" Just as the Rambam wrote in his famous letter to Ovadyah the convert: a convert says אלקינו ואלקי אבותינו, אלקי אברהם, because Avraham Avinu is the spiritual father of every convert. Once part of the community, they are part of its spiritual ancestry. Siman 676 (Opening): The Berachot The Rabbi pressed on a few minutes more, keen to finish \"another five chapters, very, very small ones\" before Hanukah. המדליק בלילה הראשון מברך שלש ברכות: להדליק נר חנוכה, ושעשה נסים, ושהחיינו. ואם לא בירך זמן בלילה ראשון, מברך בלילה שני או כשיזכר. On the first night one says three berachot: to light the Hanukah light, who performed miracles, and Sheheḥeyanu. If he did not say Sheheḥeyanu on the first night, he says it on the second night, or whenever he remembers. The first berachah: \"נר חנוכה\" or \"נר של חנוכה\"? Text Where להדליק נר של חנוכה The Gemara, the Rambam, all the original sources. All Ashkenazi siddurim. The original Sephardi text, still preserved by the Spanish & Portuguese להדליק נר חנוכה Middle Eastern Sephardim, who changed their text because of the Arizal \"Always look back at S&P, because S&P had the original Sephardi text.\" The Arizal's reasoning: the initials of להדליק נר חנוכה are ל-נ-ח, which rearranged spell נחל, \"a stream.\" Saying it this way draws down a stream of blessing. On Shabbat we still say נר של שבת, since there is no such acronym there. The Ben Ish Ḥai was enthusiastic: don't say \"של,\" or you lose the meaning of the stream. His לשם יחוד prayer before lighting mentions the נחל. \"If that's your minhag, fine. But we should know the main text is להדליק נר של חנוכה, and that was the text in the Gemara, the Rambam and the Rishonim.\" The second berachah: \"בזמן הזה\" or \"ובזמן הזה\"? שעשה נסים לאבותינו בימים ההם... S&P siddur: בימים ההם ובזמן הזה, \"in those days and in this time,\" with a vav . Kenneth reported the Ashkenazi text: בזמן הזה without the vav , \"in those days, at this season.\" That is also how (Middle Eastern) Sephardim say it. \"And the meaning is actually very different\": with the vav , the berachah speaks of miracles in our own time as well. The recording ends at this point, and the discussion continues next week. Summary 674: the halacha follows Shmuel, one of three places in Shas where he beats Rav on an issur: you may light one Hanukah light from another. Rabbi Sacks: a moment's weakening is worth two flames. Directly only, not through a match (the סתם). The Rema: it's a good custom not to light from the first light even directly, but you may for another household, from the hiddur lights, and after half an hour. No relighting a light that went out from another Hanukah light (no mitzvah remains). The shamash solves everything. Not to Shabbat candles from the menorah, except in a real emergency, and directly. 675: הדלקה עושה מצוה. A non-Jew may set out the menorah. But light it where it will stand: don't light at a bedside and carry it to the window, don't hold it, and have enough oil at the moment of lighting (about 1½ hours on Friday). Women are equally obligated (the decree, and Yehudit) and may light for the household, though when both are present and able, the husband lights. Children, even at ḥinuch, do not light the obligatory light. The Syrian custom: after the father lights the first, children of ḥinuch age light the rest. Small children light the shamash. A blind person may light with help and say all the berachot (the majority, like יוצר המאורות). A ḥeresh may not light for others. A convert says שעשה נסים לאבותינו, as the Rambam wrote to Ovadyah the convert. 676: three berachot on the first night; a forgotten Sheheḥeyanu can be said on a later night. The original text is נר של חנוכה (the Arizal: נר חנוכה, for נחל). בזמן הזה vs. ובזמן הזה (S&P). Coming Up Next shiur: the rest of 676, then 677–681. The order of the berachot, which way to light, הנרות הללו, priorities when money is short, Friday and Motza'ei Shabbat, and guests and travellers.","fJhbJzySIgk":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: מורה נבוכים / Moreh Nebuchim — The Guide for the Perplexed Author: רבינו משה בן מימון — Rambam (Maimonides) This Class: Background, biography, context, translations, controversies, and structure of the Moreh Context: Why Now? Over the past 4–5 years, Tuesday evenings have covered many aspects of the Rambam's writings: Sefer Madda, Hilchot De'ot, Hilchot Talmud Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah Letters: Iggeret HaShmad, letter on resurrection, letter to Rabbi Ovadyah the convert Introductions: to Mishneh Torah, to Pirkei Avot (Eight Chapters), to Perek Chelek (Sanhedrin) All of these trace back to one source: the Moreh Nevuchim The Moreh is where Rambam's philosophy and theology ultimately originates It's a daunting book — but it's time to deal with \"the real stuff\" Expected timeline: 2–3 years, going through systematically chapter by chapter Biography of the Rambam Birth and Early Years Born 14th of Nisan , either 1135 or 1138 (evidence now leans toward 1138) Born in Cordoba, Spain Father: Rabbi Maimon ben Yosef — a Dayan in Spain Rabbi Maimon was a student of the Ri Migash (Rabbi Yosef ibn Migash, 1077–1141, Seville) The Ri Migash was a student of the Rif (Rabbi Yitzchak Alfasi, 1013–1103, Algeria/Fez) Almost unbroken chain: Rif → Ri Migash → Rabbi Maimon → Rambam Rambam writes: \"In very few places I disagreed with the Rif, because normally the Rif is always correct\" Also exposed to philosophy from a young age — through the rise of the Islamic school of the Mutakallimun Mutakallimun = from the Arabic word \"kalam\" (the word / literalism) Developed in the 8th century → Greek works (Aristotle, medical texts) translated into Arabic Both Islamic and Jewish thinkers benefited from this Leaving Cordoba (1148) Around age 10 (or 13) — family leaves Cordoba due to the Almohad persecution (Islamic Crusaders) Sojourned around the Iberian Peninsula for 12 years (1148–1160) Spent time in Seville — met a student of Ibn Bajjah (expert astronomer, quoted in the Moreh) Developed interests in astronomy and other disciplines during this period Fez (1160–1165) Family moves to Fez for five years — father still alive Big question: why move to Fez where the Almohads were in control? Unclear whether he could live a full Jewish life there Wrote the famous Iggeret HaShmad (Essay on Forced Conversions) — in his early 20s Written with confidence and sarcasm Some suggest a hint that he himself lived a crypto life — Rav Shilat completely rejects this At age 23: begins writing his Commentary on the Mishnah (first major rabbinic work) Completes it seven years later in Egypt at age 30 Already gaining a high reputation in his 20s Israel (1165) In May 1165 — arrives in Akko with his father and brother David Stormy voyage — Rambam promised: Two days of fasting for the whole family One day of personal meditation and seclusion Travels from Akko → Yerushalayim → Hevron Spends approximately one year in Israel Then leaves for Egypt (reasons unclear — perhaps financial, perhaps too difficult) Question from the shiur: How can he live in Egypt? Torah says don't go back there! The Radbaz has a teshuvah with 3–4 answers on this Egypt — Fustat (1166 onwards) Settles in Fustat (old Cairo) — approximately 4,000 Jews there at the time Spends the rest of his life here Brother David — the businessman of the family Traveled to India, Singapore, and other Far Eastern locations for trade 1177: Shipwreck — David is lost at sea Rambam mourns deeply: \"If not for the Torah which is my delight, and for the scientific matters which let me forget my sorrow — I would have perished in my affliction\" (יגעתי בעוני) Some suggest David had been financing Rambam → after his death, Rambam turned to practicing medicine Rabbi Avraham (Rambam's son) names his elder son David — most likely after this uncle Major Works Written in Egypt Mishneh Torah — halakhic magnum opus, written in Hebrew (took 10 years) Written to be like a \"replica of the Mishnah\" — just as Rabbi Yehuda HaNasi wrote the Mishnah because Torah was being forgotten Sefer HaMitzvot — enumeration of 613 mitzvot, written in Arabic Many had tried before him — Rambam says they all got it wrong Includes 14 rules for how to properly enumerate the mitzvot Suggestion: read through Sefer HaMitzvot over the two days of Shavuot Commentary on the Mishnah — written in Arabic , translated by others into Hebrew Medical works — 10 books on medicine Most of Rambam's works are in Arabic — the Mishneh Torah is the notable exception (in Hebrew) Court Physician Eventually becomes physician at the court of Saladin (Muslim ruler) Personal physician to Saladin's son Al-Afdal Had to commute from Fustat to the other side of Cairo daily Writes in a letter: \"I barely have time to study — only on Shabbat\" By the time he comes home in the evening → people waiting at his door for medical consultations Death Dies 20th of Tevet, 1204 Buried in Teveria (Tiberias) The Moreh Nevuchim — Background When Was It Written? Began: 1185 — completed: 1191 (5–6 years) Rambam dies in 1204 → written in the last ~20 years of his life If born 1138 → he begins writing it around age 47 Some ideas intended from much earlier: In his Commentary on Mishnah Sanhedrin (age ~23–30): mentions intending to write a book on prophecy → never writes a separate book → incorporates it into the Moreh Also mentions intending to write a book explaining the drashot of the Chachamim → decides against it (\"if I explain too clearly it's not good; if I use more allegories, what's the point?\") Who Was It Written For? Primarily for his student: Yosef ben Yehuda ibn Shimon Had been studying under Rambam in Fustat Moved to Aleppo → Rambam decided to write this for him But also intended for a wider audience: Clear indications throughout that he wants philosophically trained people to read it In some places explicitly writes that certain ideas should reach the masses Example: the danger of believing in God's physicality → \"teach this to the children, teach this to the women\" Purpose Navigate the conflict between Aristotelian philosophy and Torah tradition For the person who is both well-versed in science/philosophy AND in Torah Deals with: God's essence, unity, incorporeality, knowledge, revelation, prophecy, evil, mitzvot, providence, prayer The Original Name Rambam writes in Arabic — does NOT call it \"Moreh Nevuchim\" Original Arabic name: דלאלת אלחאירין — Dalalat al-Ha'irin Meaning: \"Direction for those who are confused / lacking direction / misled / trapped\" Translations Shmuel ibn Tibbon (1165–1232) Translates from Arabic to Hebrew Completed the translation in 1204 — the year of Rambam's death Rambam writes to him in 1199 : don't translate word for word \"Good translators don't get stuck on word-for-word translation\" Similar approach to Aryeh Kaplan (translator of Derech Hashem, the Living Torah Chumash, etc.) Despite this advice, some say ibn Tibbon was too literal Ibn Tibbon is the son of Yehuda ibn Tibbon — who translated: Emunot VeDeot (Saadia Gaon) from Arabic to Hebrew (the \"Amar Yehuda\" at the start of each essay) Chovot HaLevavot (Rabbeinu Bachya ibn Pakuda) from Arabic to Hebrew The ibn Tibbon dynasty made these Arabic works part of Hebrew literature — incredible contribution The Name \"Moreh Nevuchim\" Shmuel ibn Tibbon coins the Hebrew name — not the Rambam The word נבוכים appears twice in Tanach: Shemot 14:3 — Pharaoh says about Bnei Yisrael: כי נבוכים הם בארץ — \"they are trapped/confused in the land\" Esther 3:15 — והעיר שושן נבוכה — \"the city of Shushan was confused\" In both cases : the confusion leads to the ultimate redemption (Yam Suf / Purim) Perhaps ibn Tibbon's message: this guide will lead the perplexed from confusion to clarity, just as nevuchim led to geulah Yehuda Alcharizi (Toledo) Second translation — only 9 years after ibn Tibbon Felt ibn Tibbon was too literal → his translation was more free Rabbi Avraham (Rambam's son) preferred ibn Tibbon's translation Makes sense: Rambam and ibn Tibbon had been communicating directly Ibn Tibbon's version became the accepted one Latin Translation (1224) Translated into Latin — now accessible to Christians (Dominicans, monks) This directly leads to controversy (see below) Modern Translations Rabbi Yosef Kapach — great Yemenite leader, translated from Arabic to Hebrew Dr. Michael Schwartz — Israeli professor, more accessible modern Hebrew from Arabic Friedlander — translated from Arabic directly to English (red book: \"The Guide for the Perplexed\") Same Friedlander who translated Emunot VeDeot from Arabic to English Controversies Around the Moreh During Rambam's Lifetime Not much controversy about the Moreh specifically during his life He completes it in 1191, dies in 1204 — doesn't have time to spread widely He DID have controversy about resurrection (techiyat hametim) → wrote Ma'amar Techiyat HaMetim Controversy #1 — After His Death (Early 1200s) Already in Egypt: Daniel HaBavli challenges both the Mishneh Torah and the Moreh Rabbi Avraham (Rambam's son) writes Milchamot Hashem (\"Wars of Hashem\") — defending his father Issues included: Rambam's conception of angels His views on prophecy and miracles His statement that he would reinterpret Bereishit if Aristotle had proven the universe was eternal Controversy spreads to Europe — France, Provence The Burning of 1232 (Montpellier) Agitators showed selections from the Latin translation to the Dominicans Selections were deemed heretical Public burning of the Moreh ordered in Montpellier, 1232 A few years later: burning of the Talmud — whether connected, only Hashem knows The Jews would never have done this alone — it was under Dominican oversight Controversy #2 — 1305 Indirectly about the Rambam — not a direct attack on the Moreh Dispute between the Rashba and Abba Mari Issue: people were misunderstanding the Moreh → Rashba proposed a ban on studying philosophy under age 25 Many disagreed → a major fight Not the Rambam's fault — but the Moreh was indirectly implicated Later Critics Rav Yaakov Emden (1700s): wrote that the Moreh cannot have been written by the Rambam \"The person who wrote the Mishneh Torah cannot be the same person who wrote this — it must be someone else\" Too much \"heresy\" within it Still, even with critics in every generation → Rambam won Approximately two-thirds of the Shulchan Aruch follows the Rambam's rulings In philosophy: he is the most eminent Jewish philosopher Structure of the Moreh Introduction Written to his student Yosef ben Yehuda ibn Shimon To be studied after Pesach Part One (70+ chapters) Main theme: God — What God Is and Isn't Strong, elaborate rejection of anthropomorphism All literal physical descriptions of God in Tanach are to be reinterpreted This is the source for everything we've learned elsewhere (Madda, Sanhedrin, etc.) Negative theology — you cannot describe what God IS, only what God is NOT How can you talk about God being \"compassionate\" if He is infinite and indivisible? When we say Hashem is רחום → we mean He is \"not not rachum\" Unity of God — philosophical elaboration Importance of intellect in human perfection and understanding God Loosely corresponds to the first five of the 13 Principles (God exists, is one, has no body, is first, no idol worship) Though the fourth principle (God is first) is dealt with in Part Two Part Two (50+ chapters) Main theme: The Universe and God's Relationship with It Is the world eternal? — the major question Aristotle's dominant view: the universe is eternal (no beginning) Now vindicated by the Big Bang (Hubble 1929, confirmed in the 1960s) — but Rambam had to grapple with this head-on Rambam rejects Aristotle's view — not because the Torah says so, but because he believes it wasn't philosophically sound Aristotelian cosmology — the spheres having intelligence (complex topic) Conflict between Bereishit and Aristotelian/Platonic views of creation Prophecy — nature, levels, and mechanics of prophetic experience Miracles — what they are and how they operate Angels — conceived as forces of nature, not winged beings Part Three (50+ chapters) Main theme: Evil, Ethics, and Mitzvot Why is there evil? — categorized into three types Hashgachah Pratit (Divine Providence) — what it means, how it operates Reasons for the Mitzvot (Ta'amei HaMitzvot) Underlying theme in many mitzvot: weaning us off Avodah Zarah (idol worship) Prayer, ethics, and the purpose of human existence Very Loosely — The Three Parts Map To: Part Focus Question Part One God Himself What is God? What is God not? Part Two God and the Universe Did God create the world? How does He reveal Himself? Part Three God and Humanity Why do we do what we do? Why is there evil? Key Predecessors Referenced by the Rambam Author Work Approximate Dates Rav Saadia Gaon Emunot VeDeot Early 1000s Rabbeinu Bachya ibn Pakuda Chovot HaLevavot Late 1000s / early 1100s (d. 1141) Rabbi Avraham ibn Daud (Ra'avad) Emunah Ramah Mid-1100s Note: this is NOT the Ra'avad who wrote glosses on the Rambam — different person Rambam has read all of these, disagrees with some, and references them throughout Key Takeaways The Moreh is the source of Rambam's philosophy — everything in Mishneh Torah, Commentary on the Mishnah, and the letters traces back here. Written in Arabic (Dalalat al-Ha'irin) — the Hebrew name \"Moreh Nevuchim\" is from the translator ibn Tibbon, not from Rambam. Written for his student Yosef ben Yehuda — but intended for a wider audience. Purpose: navigate the conflict between Aristotelian philosophy and Torah tradition. Three parts: God Himself → God and the Universe → God and Humanity. Controversial from shortly after Rambam's death — but ultimately Rambam won . A journey of 2–3 years ahead — brick by brick, chapter by chapter. Next Class (After Pesach) The Rambam's own Introduction to the Moreh — his letter to his student before Part One begins. Chag Pesach Kasher v'Sameach","i9eV0L8eF7E":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem — Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam (Rav Dana's Hebrew edition) Chapter: 8 — Bitachon and Reliance Topic: Employing bitachon for non-essential matters — Category 4 (important but non-essential) and Category 5 (luxuries) — and the beginning of the final section: the four levels of bitachon Schedule note: This is the penultimate HaMaspik shiur until Elul. Next Monday b'ezrat Hashem we complete the levels of bitachon and summarise the whole chapter before the summer break. Shiurim continue as usual this week: Tuesday evening the Moreh, Thursday evening Halacha. Recap — Where We Are in the Chapter We are nearing the end of the longest chapter Rabbi Avraham wrote in this sefer. So far: Section Content Definition Bitachon defined; the different types of bitachon and the three attitudes toward hashgacha Framework Five main categories of employing bitachon (× physical and spiritual) Category 1 Avoiding seriously harmful situations — in mundane and spiritual matters — correct to employ bitachon Category 2 Avoiding minor harms — Rabbi Avraham holds it is not correct to employ bitachon; we saw the machloket Rishonim (vs. Rabbeinu Yonah on בכל דרכיך דעהו) Category 3 Obtaining essential matters — certainly correct to employ bitachon for basic material needs, as Yaakov Avinu did The two questions on Category 3 (from previous weeks): Q1: Many verses state that Hakadosh Baruch Hu grants basic parnassah even to animals — so what is the practical need for bitachon? Answer: True for animals; but humans have free will, and our obedience or disobedience can broaden or cut off our basic parnassah. Q2: Verses like עיני כל אליך ישברו imply that trusting in Hashem is natural for every creature — how does that fit verses that praise bitachon (אשרי אדם בוטח בך)? Two answers: (a) kol means rov — the majority either voluntarily choose bitachon or are compelled to it in difficult scenarios; (b) kol means literally all — everyone trusts in someone or something, and everything traces back to Hashem, so all are indirectly trusting in Him; the verses of praise speak of those who consciously and voluntarily choose bitachon. Jobs and bitachon: Some occupations make bitachon easier (income open to chance/luck — e.g. self-employment), some harder (income fixed and dependent on other people — e.g. the personal assistant of a wealthy employer). But in every job, including total dependence on one employer, a person remains obligated to have bitachon — it is simply harder work. Part 1: Category 4 — Important but Non-Essential Matters 1.1 The principle: yourself vs. your family Rabbi Avraham's position, summarised before learning it inside: For yourself — strictly, a person should employ bitachon only for essential matters, not for non-essentials, even important ones. For your family — you can and should employ bitachon even for matters that are non-essential but important. What is \"extra\" for you is considered essential for your wife and children. ומה שקרוב אל הנחיצות הוא כל הדרוש לאדם לפרנסת אשתו וטפו Things needed for the support of your spouse and children may not be essential for you personally, but they are considered essential for them — and therefore it is correct, appropriate and required to employ bitachon for them. ואילו ככל שהרחיב אדם את בני משק ביתו כגון עבדים ושפחות... וכל שמתרבה הרי הוא קרוב לנחוץ But the more a person expands his household with things that are not essential, the more he borders on Category 5 — unnecessary luxuries. 1.2 Where is the line? It depends on the person התחום שבין שני הקצוות גדול מאוד ומשתנה לפי מצבו השונה של היחיד The territory between the two extremes (essential ↔ luxury) is vast, and it shifts according to each individual's situation. כי מלך ישראל זקוק לדברים ששום נזיר מתבודד אינו זקוק להם A king of Israel needs things no secluded hermit needs. Hence the provisions of David and Shlomo — their armies, horses and so on — were close to necessary despite their quantity. But if one of the servants of the righteous took an extra servant he could manage without, that acquisition would be for luxury. 1.3 The Shunamite woman — the model of self-limitation When Elisha, grateful for her hospitality, offered: היש לדבר לך אל המלך או אל שר הצבא — \"Shall I speak on your behalf to the king or the army commander?\" — she answered: בתוך עמי אנכי ישבת — \"I dwell among my own people.\" She limited herself to what was necessary; she needed no powerful political connections. And note her consistency: when travelling, though a donkey was saddled and walking with her to spare her the exertion, she told her attendant not to stop for her to ride unless she said so — she would not use even what was prepared, unless genuinely needed. A very high level — and it depends on each person. 1.4 Not everyone can live on dry bread — temperament and upbringing ודע... שלא כל אדם יכול לסיבת מזגו והרגלו להסתפק באכילת פת חרבה ולבישת מלבוש גס Not every person, by temperament ( mezeg ) and habit/upbringing ( hergel ), is able to satisfy himself with plain bread and coarse clothing. אלא יש מי שמוכרח מחמת מזגו ומצבו לפת את פיתו בבשר וללבוש בגדים בינוניים ולדור בבית בינוני — כמעט כשם שאחרים צריכים פת חרבה ובגדי כותנה וצריף דל For some people, bread with meat, average clothing and an average home are as necessary as dry bread and a poor hut are for others. What is technically non-essential can be, for that person, an essential. ויבוא עליו היודע צרכיו של כל אדם — Only Hakadosh Baruch Hu knows, deep down, what each person truly needs, והוא היודע אם פלוני מצמצם ומבקש ממנו רק את הדרוש לו או דברים מותרות — and He knows whether we are asking for what we require or for luxury. 1.5 The short Amida — \"give each person according to their needs\" This is why Chazal instituted the abbreviated Amida (Gemara Berachot; still halachically applicable today in specific pressed circumstances): צרכי עמך ישראל מרובין ודעתם קצרה — the needs of Your people are many, but their capacity to articulate them is short — יהי רצון מלפניך ה' אלוהינו שתתן לכל אחד ואחד כדי פרנסתו ולכל גויה וגויה די מחסורה, והטוב בעיניך עשה Give each individual according to his parnassah , each body what it lacks — and do what is good in Your eyes. The tefillah itself acknowledges that \"need\" is defined per person, and Hashem is the one who knows the true measure. 1.6 Rabbi Kada's application: nowadays The Rabbi argued that in the society and civilisation we live in today, even things beyond the bare basics have become, for us, close to essential: a decent home, a decent car, decent clothes. Given the world we live in and the upbringing we have had, it is reasonable today to ask Hashem for these, to employ bitachon for these — even though strictly speaking they are non-essential. One might argue: \"No — spend your time learning Torah, satisfy yourself with the most basic home, everyone sleeping in one room; you have shelter, clothes and bread.\" Rabbi Avraham's framework says otherwise: we may work for these things, pray for these things, and have bitachon for these things — ובלבד שיתלה לבו במבטח השם יתעלה ובצד השתדלותו — provided the heart hangs its trust on Hashem alongside the hishtadlut. 1.7 Yaakov Avinu — the proof case, in two phases Phase 1 — alone: Fleeing from Esav, before he had wives or children, Yaakov asked only for essentials: ונתן לי לחם לאכול ובגד ללבוש — bread to eat and clothing to wear. Bare basics. Phase 2 — as head of a household: With wives and children, he sought a broader, better parnassah: מתי אעשה גם אנכי לביתי — \"When shall I also provide for my own house?\" (his words to Lavan: I must stop working for you and build for my family — more than the basics.) And Hashem helped him because he trusted: והשם יתעלה סייעו בזכות שבטח בו — even though he toiled shepherding Lavan's flocks, and even though Lavan schemed to defraud him ( ותחלף את משכרתי עשרת מונים — \"you changed my wages ten times\"), and even though Yaakov engaged in the whole strategy of the rods (המקלות) in Parashat Vayetzei so the flocks would bear according to his wage-terms — with all that, he acknowledged that everything he attained came from Hashem's help, not from his own devices: ויצל אלוהים את מקנה אביכם ויתן לי — \"G-d has taken your father's livestock and given it to me.\" And Rachel and Leah acknowledged the same: כי כל העושר אשר הציל אלוהים מאבינו — לנו הוא ולבנינו — all this wealth was destined from Hashem. The conclusion Rabbi Avraham draws: even when engaged in obtaining things beyond bare necessity, חייב אדם להישען על השם יתעלה ועל סיבותיו — a person must lean on Hashem and His causes; the occupation of Torah-keepers must remain bound up with bitachon even in our own days — שהביטחון קודם לעיסוק והלב תלוי... בעילת העילות ובסיבת הסיבות — the bitachon precedes the work, and the heart stays suspended on the Cause of all causes. 1.8 The Rabbi's important aside: do not impose your chumrot on your family Even if you personally are on a level of chasidut/piety and wish to hold yourself to the strict standard (essentials only), that is lovely — for yourself . You cannot impose your stringencies on people who have not chosen them, especially your spouse and children . This is the general Torah approach to chumrot: take on whatever you want for yourself; do not impose it on others. For your dependents, anything beyond the bare necessities is treated as essential — you must work for it and employ bitachon for it. Part 2: Category 5 — Luxuries 2.1 The principle: do not ask, do not lift your eyes ולא ישאל ולא ישא עיניו לשום דבר שאינו בגדר ההכרח או בקרבתו A person should not ask — not even pray — and should not employ bitachon for anything outside the domain of the necessary or what borders on it. ואם השיג את מבוקשו יסתפק בו ולא יצטרך לשאר דברים And once he has obtained what he needs — be satisfied; stop asking. 2.2 The examples (and the modern translations) Food: Granted, today we cannot be expected to eat bare bread and salt daily; nice evening meals, bread, fruit, vegetables — appropriate. But: ואינו חייב להשתמש במיני בשרים מיוחדים להתקין מהם מטעמים כטעמי המלך — even if you have the financial ability, you should not be asking Hashem for the finest steaks every night like the delicacies of a king. Note: This is not a discussion of perishut (abstinence) — that is a separate topic coming later in the sefer. If Hashem blessed you with means and you choose to enjoy them, that is fine from this perspective . The point is narrower: it is inappropriate to ask for, and place bitachon on, pure luxury. Clothing: וניטל ללבוש מעיל צמר בחורף ובגד פשתן בקיץ — a wool coat in winter, linen in summer: understandable; today one needs to dress respectably and presentably. But not: פרוות סנאים מרוקמת משי — squirrel fur embroidered with silk — או פשתן דקה או בגד שרב אלכסנדרי בקיץ — fine linen or an Alexandrian sharav garment. (A participant suggested \"sharav\" here may mean an elaborate, very expensive scarf.) Designer everything — don't ask for it. Transport: ואינו חייב לרכב על פרד יקר או סוס אציל חבוש אוכף מפואר ומלא אבני חן ועטור רסן תפארת — no need for a costly mule or a noble horse with a magnificent gem-studded saddle and a glorious bridle — i.e., in our terms, the most beautiful luxury car. A decent, presentable car — understandable. Family: Clothing your wife and children respectably, even somewhat generously — understandable (that's Category 4). But: ואינו חייב לקשט אשתו בעדי עדיים ולהלבישה משי וכתונת פסים... גם כן לבנותיו — not to adorn them in finery, silk and striped robes. Household help: If you need a servant to serve you and your children — fine. ואינו חייב להשכיר כמה וכמה עבדים... אם אפשר בלעדיהם — but not many servants when you can manage without them. 2.3 Why luxury-bitachon fails — and whose fault it is כמו שאמר שלמה: אשר עשה האלקים את האדם ישר והמה בקשו חשבונות רבים Hashem made man straight — but people seek out many calculations (justifications). The person who ישליך יהבו על השגת המותרות — casts his hope on obtaining these luxuries — then ישתומם על שאין ביטחונו מוצא פרי — is astonished that his bitachon bears no fruit. \"I prayed every day to win the lottery and buy a house on Billionaire's Row — and I had true bitachon!\" It is not going to happen; why should you have the audacity to ask for such a thing? The Rambam in the Moreh says similarly: the desire for luxuries has no end ( ein ladavar sof ). Necessities can be budgeted — electricity, gas, tax, food: there is a limit. Luxuries have no limit: if I attain silver cutlery I will want gold; if gold, then crystal. וכן כל בור שמחשבתו נפסדת — every fool of corrupted thinking אינו חדל לסבול הצער — never stops suffering the pain that he hasn't attained what others have. Why does he have the best car and house and I don't? People even put themselves in real danger — בהפלגה בים ועבודה בשדה — perilous sea voyages, punishing labour — to get the extra thing; then complain against Hashem, curse their luck (\"It's not fair — I work as hard, I'm observant, I trust as much!\") — עד כדי כך הגיע הדמיון... עד שחשבו הבורא לחוסר יכולת — imagination goes so far that they attribute it to inability on the Creator's part: \"I prayed, I had bitachon — Hashem must be unable .\" Life does not work like that. They blame Hashem when really they should blame themselves. 2.4 The categories are person-relative: Hillel and dei machsoro The Gemara in Ketubot: a wealthy man was accustomed to travel with servants running before him; he lost his money. Hillel held that the appropriate tzedaka for him was not merely food and clothing but servants to run before him — because that is די מחסורו — \"sufficient for his lack.\" Having grown up with it his whole life, for him it may be Category 3 — a bare necessity. But someone who did not grow up that way, who puts himself into dependence on such things — it is wrong to ask for them, wrong to have bitachon for them, and wrong to blame Hashem when they don't come. \"You can ask Hashem for anything\"? We're told as children: ask Hashem for anything. No — ask Hashem for what's necessary , what you need , what's important in life. The exception — l'shem Shamayim: If you genuinely mean it — \"I want to open a Torah centre, a new school, a new kind of institution, because the education available isn't right\" — for that it is appropriate to ask Hashem for the funds and the ability. As we said at the beginning of the chapter: for things for Hashem , you may always ask. 2.5 Objection: והתענג על ה' ויתן לך משאלות לבך שמא תאמר — doesn't David say (Tehillim 37): \"Delight in Hashem and He will give you the requests of your heart\"? Doesn't hit'aneg (delight/oneg) imply the beyond-necessary? Answer — read the whole Psalm: לא נתכוון דוד לבעל תאווה שיתמכר לתענוגות — David is not addressing the pleasure-seeker chasing luxuries. The Psalm opens : אל תתחר במרעים אל תקנא בעושי עולה — do not compete with evildoers, do not envy the workers of iniquity — Don't be jealous of the wicked's success. Why? It is all transient: כי כחציר מהרה ימלו וכירק דשא יבולון — like grass they swiftly wither, like green herbage they fade. Rather (v. 3): בטח בה' ועשה טוב שכן ארץ ורעה אמונה — trust in Hashem, do good, dwell permanently , shepherd faithfulness — play the long game. Then comes \"delight in Hashem.\" Clearly, one who is not envying evildoers but trusting Hashem and nurturing his emunah לא יבקש לו מותרות בחיים — is not going to be requesting life's luxuries; ומשאלות לבבו... בגדר ההכרח — the \"requests of his heart\" are within the domain of the necessary. And look to the end of the passage: גול על ה' דרכך ובטח עליו והוא יעשה. והוציא כאור צדקך ומשפטך כצהרים — tzidkecha is what is moderately, rightly yours; mishpatecha is the entitlement a person deserves — perhaps slightly more than the bare measure. Trust in Hashem and He gives you what you need, and maybe a bit beyond. Two deeper readings of \"delight\": והתענג על ה' means delight in Hashem — spiritual closeness, not physical things. ויתן לך משאלות לבך may refer to השלמות האמיתית — the true perfection — the request of David himself: אחת שאלתי מאת ה' אותה אבקש, שבתי בבית ה' כל ימי חיי, לחזות בנועם ה' ולבקר בהיכלו . And one who genuinely aspires to that closeness receives it — מאין ספק כי השם יתעלה נותן לשואפי השלמות מבוקשם , as Chazal teach: בא לטהר מסייעין אותו , and on והתקדשתם והייתם קדושים: אדם מקדש עצמו מעט — מקדשין אותו הרבה; מלמטה — מקדשין אותו מלמעלה . King Shlomo's dream is the proof: Hashem said שאל מה אתן לך . Shlomo asked for a heart that hears — wisdom. Hashem answered: יען אשר שאלת את הדבר הזה ולא שאלת לך עושר... הנה עשיתי כדבריך — because you asked for this and not for wealth, I give you the wisdom — and also what you did not ask for . When you don't ask for these things, Hashem may well give them to you. 2.6 A kal vachomer from the king The Torah warns even the king of Israel — one of the 613 mitzvot — against excess: לא ירבה לו סוסים... ולא ירבה לו נשים... וכסף וזהב לא ירבה לו מאד Chazal set the limits (18 wives; a corresponding limit on horses). And a king needs horses for his armies, and his court is part of his royal role. אם במלך... נאמר כך — קל וחומר לשאר בני אדם : if the Torah says this of a king who genuinely requires provision, all the more so for the rest of us. 2.7 Objection: but the Avot were wealthy! שמא תאמר — doesn't the Torah say explicitly that the Avot amassed great wealth? Avraham: ואברהם כבד מאד במקנה בכסף ובזהב — very heavy in livestock, silver and gold. Yitzchak: whatever he touched returned a hundredfold (invest £1, receive £100). Yaakov: enriched through the episode with Lavan — כי כל העושר אשר הציל אלוהים מאבינו לנו הוא ולילדינו . Rabbi Avraham's answer — two parts: They needed it for their public role: האבות היו זקוקים לכך מפני שייסדו את האומה והיו עושים נפשות — they founded the nation and were \"making souls,\" drawing people close to Hashem: את הנפש אשר עשו בחרן by Avraham; and by Yaakov — ויאמר יעקב לאחיו לקטו אבנים (at the covenant with Lavan). Yaakov had no brothers with him — Esav was not there — so echav must mean his companions/students: אחיו בדת, שמושכם אל הטוב ומחזקם . Students turn to their teachers not only for Torah but also for material assistance; and — like it or not — wealth carries influence. Their wealth served the mission. They never asked for it: יתר על כן, האבות לא ביקשו להם אלא די הצורך — the Torah itself testifies: Yaakov asked only לחם לאכול ובגד ללבוש . Rather, השם יתעלה הגדיל חסדו עמהם והשפיע עליהם מרוב טובו אשר לא ביקשוה ולא כיוונו לה — Hashem, from His abundant kindness, in the merit of their dedication and their bitachon, poured on them what they had not requested — exactly as with Shlomo: וגם אשר לא שאלת נתתי לך, גם עושר גם כבוד . And this was David's own prayer: הט לבי אל עדותיך ואל אל בצע — turn my heart to Your testimonies, not to profit — and worldly success came to him unbidden. 2.8 Closing the five categories ק\"ץ מאמרנו: נאמר כי אין צורך לשאוף עליהם — in short: one need not, and should not, aspire to luxuries; bitachon placed on such things is misplaced. The Torah-keeper who aspires to this elevated level שלא ישים את מבטחו ואת עיניו בהם — should not set his trust or his eyes upon them. And the promise: it may well be that Hashem repays such a person beyond his requests — יכול להיות שהשם יתעלה ישלם לו כגמולו... ויתרה עליו גמולים שאינם הכרחיים מצד חסדו וטובו — as the Torah promises abundance, and as the navi says: והריקותי לכם ברכה עד בלי די . And Chazal expound: מאי עד בלי די? עד שיבלו שפתותיכם מלומר די — blessing until your lips wear out from saying \"enough!\" (The Rabbi: until you're saying — I have nowhere to put all these clothes, my driveway is too small for all these cars…) Summary of the five categories: Category Employ bitachon? 1. Avoiding serious harm (physical & spiritual) Yes — with all natural means 2. Avoiding minor harms No (Rabbi Avraham; machloket Rishonim) — though for small spiritual matters, yes 3. Essentials (food, clothing, shelter) Absolutely — ask and trust 4. Important non-essentials For your dependents — yes, obligatory; for yourself — today, arguably yes, given our society and upbringing 5. Luxuries Never — do not ask, do not trust for them, and do not be surprised when Hashem does not listen Part 3: The Final Section — The Levels of Bitachon (begun) The remaining section of the chapter (to be completed next week): the four levels of bitachon , in ascending order. 3.1 Bitachon lives in the heart וכל זאת עליך לדעת כי הביטוי \"בטחון\" כולל הרבה מדרגות, אף על פי שמובנה האמיתי של המילה מחייב שתהיה רק מדרגה אחת נקראת בטחון — colloquially we call all four levels \"bitachon,\" though strictly only one (the third) is the true meaning. And lack of bitachon also has degrees. ביאור הדבר: בטחון מסור ללב — bitachon is entrusted to the heart. Seven weeks we have spent learning this chapter — the point is וידעת היום והשבות אל לבבך : knowledge must be brought down into the heart and into practical life. The verses consistently attach bitachon to the heart: בו בטח לבי ונעזרתי ; נכון לבו בטוח בה' . 3.2 Two things that are NOT bitachon Despair. כל אימת שאין לב אדם בוטח בשם להשגת משאלתו — a person who has given up hope of attaining his need (\"I've lost my money and there's no hope from Hashem or anyone\") — אין אותו איש נקרא בוטח בשם . This is corrupt thinking, turning away from the Creator's hashgacha. Against it: קוה אל ה' חזק ויאמץ לבך וקוה אל ה' ; יחל ישראל אל ה' מעתה ועד עולם ; וקוה אל אלוהיך תמיד — never give up hope; so long as you are doing your best, Hashem never abandons you. Trusting in flesh and blood instead of Hashem. One who seeks his parnassah from a human being and rests his trust on him alone — \"I trust only my employer\" — without any mention of Hashem's help or seeing the human as merely His messenger: ארור הגבר אשר יבטח באדם ושם בשר זרעו ומן ה' יסור לבו — cursed. Both of these are מדרגות של חוסר ביטחון — degrees of the absence of bitachon. 3.3 The four levels — levels 1 and 2 tonight והנה מדרגות הביטחון הרי הן ארבע במספר Level 1 — המיחל לחסד ה' (hoping for His kindness, while fearing one's sins): שאדם מקווה ומצפה להשגת משאלתו מהשם יתעלה ברוב רחמיו, אף על פי שהוא חושש ודואג... שמא עוונותיו מונעים ממנו את הרחמים A person genuinely trusts in his heart that everything comes from Hashem — but simultaneously worries it won't happen for him : not from lack of trust in Hashem, but \"I don't deserve it; too much has gone wrong; my aveirot hold it back.\" This is a level of bitachon — a good level, the lowest — called המיחל לחסד ה', המחכה ומקוה לרחמיו , a ba'al yirah v'tikvah . David's verses encourage precisely this person: חזקו ויאמץ לבבכם כל המיחלים לה' — you're already there in trust; have more hope, more courage (and yes, stop sinning where relevant — but don't assume it always holds you back). And Hashem cherishes this person: רוצה ה' את יראיו, את המיחלים לחסדו — those who fear His punishment yet wait for His kindness even when they feel they have no merit. Level 2 — trust plus active seeking (inner and spoken request): מי שעל תקותו ותוחלתו מוסיף הבקשה לה' יתעלה — ביקוש פנימי שבלב וחיצוני שבלשון בתפילה Everything in Level 1, but the trust is also lived and expressed : constant inner request and verbal tefillah — \"Hashem, I know it all comes from You; this is the correct mish'alot libi , what I genuinely feel is necessary or important; please give it to me.\" As the Torah says explicitly (Va'etchanan): ובקשתם משם את ה' אלקיך ומצאת כי תדרשנו בכל לבבך ובכל נפשך . And many verses of David: דרשו ה' ועזו בקשו פניו תמיד ; לך אמר לבי בקשו פני, את פניך ה' אבקש ; אליך ה' אקרא ואל ה' אתחנן ; ודורשי ה' לא יחסרו כל טוב ; בקשו את ה' כל ענוי הארץ . We should all be aiming for levels one and two. Level 3 — begun (to be developed next week): certainty — חוסה בה': מדרגה שלישית היא מעלת מי שבטוח בהשגת משאלתו מאת השם יתעלה ומאמין בזאת בוודאות בלי ספק Not only trusting and praying, but absolute confidence, without doubt , that it will happen — the one who takes refuge in Hashem ( חוסה בה' ). Rabbi Avraham holds this is the true meaning of the word bitachon — a very high level. The Rabbi Yisrael Salanter lottery story: A man told the Rabbi: \"I've entered the lottery for £50,000 every week for years with full bitachon in Hashem — and never won.\" The Rabbi offered: \"Let me give you £5,000 right now.\" — \"Yes, absolutely!\" — \"Then I'm not giving you a penny. If you had full bitachon that you need — and will receive — fifty thousand, why on earth would you take five?\" That is Level 3: full certainty. (And the Rabbi added a second answer from tonight's learning: why are you employing bitachon for a lottery win at all? It's beyond the necessary — Category 5.) Level 4 — an extremely high level, almost impossible, perhaps beyond bitachon itself — will be presented next week, followed by a summary of the entire chapter. Summary Category 4 (important non-essentials): For your dependents, the beyond-basic is essential — employ bitachon and work for it, as Yaakov did ( מתי אעשה גם אנכי לביתי ), while attributing all success to Hashem ( ויצל אלוהים את מקנה אביכם ). For yourself, the strict standard is basics — but need is person-relative ( מזג והרגל ; king vs. hermit; Hillel and dei machsoro ), and in today's world the Rabbi argued most of us may legitimately ask for the decent home, car and clothes our society renders near-essential. Never impose your personal stringencies on your family. Category 5 (luxuries): Do not ask, do not lift your eyes, do not place bitachon — designer clothes, the jewelled saddle (the luxury car), the staff of many servants. Luxury has no end (silver → gold → crystal); the one who trusts for it ends up blaming Hashem, even deeming Him \"unable.\" Exception: genuine l'shem Shamayim dreams (building Torah institutions). והתענג על ה' read in context (Tehillim 37) promises spiritual delight and what is rightly yours — not luxuries; Shlomo received wealth precisely because he didn't ask. The Avot's wealth served their public mission and came unrequested, from Hashem's chesed. Ask only for the basics — and Hashem may bless עד בלי די . Levels of bitachon (begun): Not-bitachon = despair, or trust in flesh and blood. Level 1: heartfelt trust while fearing one's sins disqualify him — Hashem loves this person ( רוצה ה' את יראיו את המיחלים לחסדו ). Level 2: adds constant inner and verbal seeking ( ובקשתם משם ). Level 3 (the true \"bitachon,\" begun): certainty without doubt — חוסה בה' — the Salanter lottery test. Level 4 next week. Coming Up Next Monday — the final HaMaspik shiur before the break: completing Level 3 (including bitachon in the geulah / end of days), Level 4, and a summary of the whole of Chapter 8, before resuming b'ezrat Hashem in Elul. This week as usual: Tuesday — Moreh Nebuchim; Thursday — Halacha. B'Ezrat Hashem","mNPbdKlac50":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Rabbeinu Avraham ben HaRambam); Rabbeinu Avraham's commentary on the Torah for the weekly parashah Chapter: Perek Yud (10) — Perishut , abstinence (Part 2: definition) Topic: Four ideas from Rabbeinu Avraham on Parashat Vayera (Sarah and Yishmael's מצחק ; Hagar's fear and שמא יגרום החטא ; the peshat of באשר הוא שם ; בעת ההיא versus אחרי הדברים האלה ); why perishut matters — the prophets' careers as shepherds and farmers, the simplicity of their lives, and the Torah's proofs from cherem , Yericho, Achan and Hoshea; the roadmap for the rest of the chapter; and the definition : perishut is in the heart — with Avraham, Yitzchak and Yaakov as wealthy men who were nonetheless perushim . Schedule note: The clocks have changed in the UK. Throughout the winter, b'ezrat Hashem: Monday 6:00pm — HaMaspik ; Tuesday 8:00pm — Moreh Nebuchim ; Thursday 6:00pm — Halacha (Hilchot Shabbat) . (One participant had seen 6:15 — that was last week's time, before the clocks changed. From now on it is 6:00.) The Weekly Feature — Rabbeinu Avraham on the Parashah As announced last week, each week we look at something small from Rabbeinu Avraham's commentary on the Torah , which survives only on Bereishit and Shemot . Last week we saw his commentary on Bereishit; this week, Parashat Vayera . This week the Rabbi shared four ideas — \"quite a lot\" — before returning to the sefer. Part One — Rabbeinu Avraham on Parashat Vayera 1. What did Sarah see? — \"מצחק\" (Bereishit 21:9) ותרא שרה את בן הגר המצרית אשר ילדה לאברהם מצחק. Sarah sees the son of Hagar — Yishmael, the older brother — מצחק . The Torah is deliberately playing on the word: Yitzchak is named for laughter, and Yishmael is metzachek . But there are two kinds of שחוק . What was the problem? ראתה אותו מצחק ומנבל את פיו ומתנהג כדרך הנערים. Sarah saw him fooling around as youngsters do — and using his mouth for inappropriate speech . Playing around is understandable from the word מצחק ; where Rabbeinu Avraham gets מנבל את פיו , coarse language, the Rabbi doesn't know — but for some reason he adds it. Why is that a problem? It is מפריע מדרך השלמות — it prevents a person from reaching perfection, as Shlomo HaMelech said in Kohelet: לשחוק אמרתי מהולל... טוב כעס משחוק. When I was younger I thought it was good to laugh around; when I was older I realised that, although anger is bad, sometimes it is better than mucking around. It shows the dangers of שחוק used in the wrong way . וחששה שמא יצחק נעתק אליו ולשחוק, בהתחברו עם ישמעאל, ויבלה זמנו שעה בלא עסק בשלמות המיוחדת לו. Sarah was afraid Yitzchak would copy Yishmael — remember, Yishmael is older — and, instead of using his time to work on the perfection unique to him , would spend it playing around. וזה הוא הראוי שיעמוד בסגולתו... ושרה מטפלת בזה, לפי ששניהם נוכחים בבית אצלה בשעת המנוחה. Yitzchak's task is to work on his own perfection. There was a strong relationship between Sarah and Yitzchak — understandably, he is her biological child — and they spent a great deal of time together, resting and eating together. Very interesting: although both are Avraham's children, Rabbeinu Avraham suggests that Sarah took personal responsibility for Yitzchak's spiritual development . When she saw it threatened by Yishmael's behaviour, she felt it was time to drive Yishmael out. Two new points from Rabbeinu Avraham: (1) Yishmael was speaking wrongly , not just playing; (2) Sarah saw herself as responsible for guiding Yitzchak towards perfection. 2. Why did Hagar fear Yishmael would die? (21:16) Yishmael and Hagar have been sent away with limited food and water, and run out. Hagar distances herself: כי אמרה אל אראה במות הילד — \"I don't want to see my child die.\" The question: In last week's parashah, Lech Lecha , the malach told Hagar that Yishmael would grow up and become a nation — ידו בכל — prominent and influential. So what is she worried about? How will he become a nation if he dies now? A strong question. אין זה ספק מצדה בהבטחתו יתעלה שהבטיח לה, והוא יהיה פרא אדם ידו בכל ויד כל בו, אלא על דרך יראת אברהם ויעקב עליהם השלום... כשסבר שמא יגרום החטא. The easy answer would be that Hagar doubted — she lacked proper emunah. Rabbeinu Avraham is not comfortable with that. He is convinced Hagar did trust the message. Rather — as the Rambam says (we learned it together in Shemonah Perakim , and it appears in many other sefarim) — שמא יגרום החטא : even when one has been promised something, such promises are often conditional . If a person misbehaves, he can lose them. So she had full faith in the promise — and at the same time feared that sin (Yishmael's, her own, or both) could stop it. A question from Mark: The obvious parallel is the Akedah. Avraham was promised Yitzchak would become a great nation — and then told to slaughter him. The Rabbi: Someone (he couldn't recall who) says that this was part of the test itself: you've been told clearly that you and your son will become a great nation — will you now doubt that prophecy? Avraham didn't doubt, and went ahead. Whatever one answers for Avraham and Yitzchak, answer the same for Hagar and Yishmael. Fair point. 3. \"באשר הוא שם\" — the simple meaning (21:17) וישמע אלהים את קול הנער, ויקרא מלאך אלהים אל הגר מן השמים... אל תיראי כי שמע אלהים אל קול הנער באשר הוא שם. The well-known interpretation (Chazal — the one we discuss on Rosh Hashanah): where he is now — although Yishmael will later do bad things, at this moment his identity is good, and he is judged as he is now. But, says Rabbeinu Avraham, that is not the simple reading: פשטי דקרא, שהביט הקדוש ברוך הוא יתעלה במצבו וצערו שהוא בו, במקום שבו עזבתו אמו — תחת אחד השיחים. Geographically , not spiritually: Hashem heard the child crying where he physically was — abandoned, alone, under one of the bushes. \"I always read the verse as 'where he is now'. That's why I wanted to share it with you.\" 4. \"ויהי בעת ההיא\" vs \"אחרי הדברים האלה\" — a principle for the whole Torah After Yishmael grows up in the wilderness, the Torah says ויהי בעת ההיא — and it was at that time — and moves to the covenant between Avraham and Avimelech. What does at that time mean? ויהי בעת ההיא — מורה על הזמן שלא הוגדר במקרא, שהוא כינוי החוזר על העת, ואותה העת לא הוגדרה; ואפשר שתהיה העת מורה על הזמן של הסיפור שקדם זכרונו, או... על הזמן של הסיפור שיבוא אחר כך. Phrase Meaning ויהי בעת ההיא — \"at that time\" Vague. It may refer to the story before it, or to the story after it. We simply don't know. אחרי הדברים האלה — \"after these things\" Defined. It always refers to the time of the story itself. וזה פירוש שאני מעתיק מן אבא מרי — this I have from my father (the Rambam). The Rabbi has not seen this anywhere in the Rambam's own writings; this may be its only source. The proof — Devarim, Parashat Eikev: ובני ישראל נסעו מבארות בני יעקן מוסרה, שם מת אהרן ויקבר שם, ויכהן אלעזר בנו תחתיו... בעת ההיא הבדיל ה' את שבט הלוי. Aharon's death is in the 40th year after leaving Egypt. Straight afterwards: בעת ההיא Hashem separated the tribe of Levi — which happened in the 2nd year (Korach / the inauguration of the Leviim). So בעת ההיא is open-ended. In Vayera, ויהי בעת ההיא could refer back to the whole narrative of Yishmael in the desert, or forward to the covenant with Avimelech — but not necessarily to either. Part Two — Recap of Chapter 10 So Far Last week: a review of the entire book so far — \"if you haven't listened to it, I'd advise listening to get an overview of what we've learned\" — and the start of Chapter 10. We didn't yet go into abstinence properly; we began with the idea of not being too attached to this world . A human being is חומר (matter) and צורה (form / consciousness). Neither is non-functional, but someone who pursues his material identity weakens his tzurah and will not be able to enjoy Olam HaBa properly. We discussed Rabbeinu Avraham's view of Olam HaBa — \"a very interesting discussion.\" Before defining perishut, Rabbeinu Avraham first explains how important it is. Part Three — Why Perishut Matters הפרישות אפוא מן המידות התרומיות המוליכות לאהבת ה'. Perishut is one of the lofty character traits that lead to the love of Hashem. כי חסיד הפרוש — אין לבו טרוד בדאגות עולמיות, ויש לו פנאי להגות במה שיכונן כוונתו, ועיתותיו חופשיות מן העייפות והיגע. The parush is not preoccupied with worldly worries , has time to contemplate what matters, and is not constantly worn out trying to make money — מפני שהוא משתמש בהשגת מה שמספיק, מה שדרוש בהכרח בעולם הזה — כגון לחם לאכול ובגד ללבוש. — because he seeks only what is necessary , and uses the rest of his time in the search for meaning, spirituality and personal development. ואם יסתפק אדם בדרוש ובהכרחי — לא תקשה עליו השגתם. ולכן ראינו את בקשת יעקב אבינו: לחם לאכול ובגד ללבוש. Most people don't struggle to obtain the basics. This is why Yaakov , asking Hashem for help, asked only for bread to eat and clothes to wear — a point Rabbeinu Avraham made in Chapter 8 (Bitachon) : it is right to have bitachon for the basics, but not for extravagance . Focus on the basics and you will have time for what is more important than your chomer . The careers of the prophets and the righteous והנביאים והחסידים נטו לבחור במחייתם במרעית הצאן ועבודת האדמה. The prophets and the righteous chose work that took little time , leaving the rest for meditation, concentration and growth — shepherds and farmers . כי הרועה ינהג צאנו שעה אחת ביום לרעות הדשא, וישב יפקח עליהם עין, ובשאר שעה יפקח בלבו לשלמות — בפרט אם מקום המרעה במדבר ובערבה ובמקומות הרחוקים מן הישוב, כמו שנוסיף לגלות אזניך בפרק הבדידות. A shepherd leads the flock for an hour, then simply watches over it — and while watching, is free to think. And the pastures are usually in solitude , far from settlement. (More on this in the coming chapter on hitbodedut .) ועובד אדמה מתייגע רק בעת הזרע והקציר, ואילו בשאר עונות השנה ישבות בלבדו. A farmer works hard only at sowing and harvest — the start of winter, the start of summer. The rest of the year he is free. ולכן היה אלישע איכר, וכן אבא חלקיה ועוד כיוצא בהם. Elisha was a ploughman ; Abba Chilkiya likewise. He doesn't bother listing the shepherds — we know Moshe Rabbeinu and David HaMelech were shepherds. Two reasons: such work (1) gives you free time , and (2) keeps you away from crowds . So Rabbeinu Avraham's advice when choosing a career: choose work that leaves you time to develop, and in which you deal with as few people as possible. (A rabbi certainly satisfies the first — \"well, okay, I shouldn't say that; it depends on your community\" — but certainly not the second!) Class discussion: what is the modern equivalent? Suggestion Discussion Trading stocks — alone, potentially The Rabbi knows many traders: in the office at six, not a second free all day. That is the pursuit of more mammon — money makes more money, and more desire. (A participant: \"My son trades bonds and futures — he said 'Mum, I'll be burnt out by 26', and he's still at it.\") A teacher of Torah You learn while you work — but teachers today work extremely hard, with all the extra work after hours. A shopkeeper Employ someone for some days and work three hours a day yourself — as we know of the Chafetz Chaim (whose shop famously supported him). A chazan \"That's true, actually\" — sing here and there, and if it's a full salary you don't have to deal with people much. It is very hard today, unless you are self-employed. The simple lives of the prophets ושמואל הרמתי הייתה מתנתו רבע שקל כסף; ואחיה השילוני — בקבוק דבש ועשרה לחם ונקודים; אליהו — כסותו אזור עור ואדרת שער; אלישע — סעודתו למאה איש בני נביאים עשרים לחם שעורים וכרמל בצקלונו. Shmuel accepted a quarter of a shekel from those who visited him. Achiya HaShiloni — a flask of honey and some bread. Eliyahu — a leather belt and a hairy cloak. Elisha — for himself and a hundred students , twenty barley loaves. ודירתם — בית מגורם בנוי מחומר פשוט, כמו שנאמר: נלכה נא עד הירדן ונקחה משם איש קורה אחת ונעשה לנו שם מקום לשבת שם. The bnei nevi'im would go to the Jordan, each take a single beam, and build a little place to live — happy with a little roof over their heads . ואולם היה מקרה רע, שהאחד מהם דאג על אבדת גרזנו, שלא היה לו כסף לרוכשו, ולכן שאלו. One of them lost an axe — and couldn't afford another; he had borrowed it. ואבא חלקיה היה חורש שכיר יום, גלימתו שאולה. Abba Chilkiya was a hired day-labourer , in a borrowed cloak. הנה כי כן יתחבר לך כי הפרישות היא מהמידות... כמחייב השכל הישר. From all this you see that perishut is an important trait, as straightforward reason requires. Proofs from the Torah (1) Mitzvot that train us not to over-indulge ולכן ציוותה התורה... — the Torah gave many mitzvot for this purpose, as the Rambam explains in the Moreh : mitzvot given so that we don't become too focused on things. The Rabbi adds: Rabbeinu Avraham doesn't say this about kashrut , but Rabbi Shimshon Raphael Hirsch (or a contemporary) does — the general idea of kashrut is to restrain : removing the blood, shechitah, permitted and forbidden animals — mitzvot that train us not to fall into indulgence. (2) Cherem after the war with the Canaanite (Parashat Chukat) אם נתן תתן את העם הזה בידי, והחרמתי את עריהם... וישמע ה' בקול ישראל ויתן את הכנעני, ויחרם אתהם ואת עריהם. Bnei Yisrael vowed: if You give us victory, we will consecrate their cities — and not take the spoils. Hashem listened; they won, and consecrated it. This is a machloket rishonim: Reading of חרם Who To consecrate — as in the charamim of Parashat Bechukotai Rabbeinu Avraham and Rashi To destroy — ענינו לכלות ולהחרים הערים מהם , as in החרם תחרים אותם (the seven nations) and the cherem of Achan Ralbag Why one word for both? The Rabbi: when you consecrate something, you destroy your personal connection to it . \"I think that's pashut — that's the connection between the words.\" So the proof is good, but not 100% — one could read it like the Ralbag. An objection Rabbeinu Avraham raises himself: ואם יאמר, כי דבר זה אירע מפני שהיו אסורים אז ליכנס לארץ, כפי תום ארבעים שנה. Perhaps they consecrated it only because this was the 40th year: the Canaanites came from the Land, Bnei Yisrael were not yet allowed to enter it — and perhaps not to benefit from it either. His answer — Yericho and Achan: נזכיר עוד ציווי... בענין יריחו ששם הושם חרם, ואת העונש הנורא שנענש עכן... מפני שמעל בחרם. Yericho too was made cherem . (The commentators cite the Radak : we never actually find that Hashem commanded it — either Hashem told Yehoshua and it isn't written, or it was Yehoshua's own decision. Either way, it is a proof that consecration and perishut are good.) Achan — his clan was caught, then he himself, and he was put to death for taking from the spoils. וזה ראיה נצחת ומוראת למי שניתן לו רוח בבטנו... — this should suffice for any thinking person to recognise that the Torah recognises, and demands, perishut . (3) Hoshea — indulgence alongside idolatry כאהבת ה' את בני ישראל, והם פנים אל אלהים אחרים ואהבי אשישי ענבים. (Hoshea 3:1) Hashem loves Bnei Yisrael — but instead of reciprocating, they turn to other gods — and to fine wine . דימה הכתוב את אהבת תענוגות העולם הזה למראה כפני עבודה זרה... וזו ראיה חותכת, כי הפרישות מענייני העולם הזה — התקרבות אל אהבת האל, והתעמקות בהתחברות אליו. The verse compares the love of worldly pleasure to idolatry. A decisive proof : the more you love this world, the further you are from Hashem — and perishut brings you close. So far: last week, the importance of not focusing too much on materialism; this week, that perishut is an important trait , with proofs from consecration and elsewhere in the Torah. Lovely. But what is it? Part Four — The Roadmap for the Rest of Chapter 10 וכיון שבארנו נחיצות הפרישות — הבה נבאר לך את מהותה, ודרכי הכושר להשגתה, והחזקה בהגתה, ואחר כך את פרותיה ותוצאותיה, ואחר כך ניישב קושיות הבאות בסדר השגתה. # Stage When 1 Its definition ( מהותה ) This week 2 How to train towards it ( דרכי הכושר להשגתה ) Next week 3 How to know you've achieved it ( החזקה בהגתה ) Coming weeks 4 Its fruits and consequences ( פרותיה ותוצאותיה ) Coming weeks 5 Resolving stories in Tanach that seem to contradict it Around Parashat Toldot — a lengthy discussion of Yitzchak's blessings , which will fall perfectly in two or three weeks, at the end of the chapter Part Five — The Definition: Perishut Is in the Heart ונאמר כי עיקר הפרישות הוא שתהא יוצאת מן הלב — שהלב פרוש מאהבת העולם הזה, ותהיינה עיניו שקועות בתורה ובעבודת השם, אחד עמו, מאס בכל שאר הדברים מלבדו. \"This is the most important line\" — of the shiur, and of the whole chapter. A person is where his mind is (perhaps the Baal Shem Tov). Rabbeinu Avraham is not asking you to fast, abstain from wine, abstain from this and that — while thinking about it all the time . That is not perishut. \"You've missed the boat.\" Perishut is in the heart. How to get there is very hard — that's next week — but first, understand the definition. Like humility We've discussed this before — even in HaMaspik itself. Humility doesn't mean thinking low of yourself. \"I've got talent, I'm good at this — Hashem bless you then.\" There's an English saying along the lines of: humility is not thinking less of yourself, it's thinking of yourself less. Anavah is not walking around all day thinking \"I'm so low, I'm trying to be humble\" — that defeats the point. Stop focusing on yourself. So too perishut isn't spending the whole day thinking, \"I didn't eat today, I'm so holy, I wish I could have that chocolate but I'm abstaining for Hashem\" — while sitting in the shiur thinking about the chocolate. That doesn't help. Perishut means: \"I have no interest in these things — because my focus is on something else.\" Two ways to lose interest — the diet analogy Level What it looks like 1 — Trained restraint You've dieted long enough that when you see unhealthy food you have no desire for it. But it comes from \"theoretically, if it had no calories, I'd love it.\" 2 — Absorbed elsewhere (true perishut) You lost weight not by dieting, but because you're so busy with another project — work, family, whatever — that you don't have time to think about those foods. Perishut means a person is so focused on the pursuit of his relationship with Hakadosh Baruch Hu, and the development of his spiritual identity and intellect, that he has no time to think about these things. כי עיקר הפרישות הוא שתהיה יוצאת מן הלב — רוצה לומר, שהלב פרוש מן העולם הזה, ושקוע באהבתו יתעלה ובעבודתו ושמחתו... שאינו מתאוה. Wealth is no contradiction וככה נראה שפרוש זה — ילבש בגדי משי, ויאכל מעדנים, ויתענג בתענוגים... אלא אם באו לו נכסים אלו בלא כוונה מצדו, מירושה וכדומה, או בתמצות מועטת להשגת דמי החיים. A parush may wear silk, eat delicacies and live in a beautiful home — if these came to him without his pursuing them : an inheritance, a gift, or ordinary modest effort. \"I have it now, I enjoy it — but it's not on my mind.\" It is not about what I have; it is about what is on my mind. You can live well, dress well, eat well and sleep well and still be a parush . Yitzchak: ויזרע יצחק בארץ ההיא, וימצא בשנה ההיא מאה שערים, ויברכהו ה'. He did ordinary farming and received a hundredfold — without chasing extra money. Yaakov: despite Lavan, ויפרץ האיש מאד מאד, ויהי לו צאן רבות ושפחות ועבדים וגמלים וחמרים. He became extremely wealthy. Avraham: ואברם כבד מאד במקנה בכסף ובזהב (read last Shabbat) — extremely wealthy, and still a parush . How do we know Avraham was a parush ? (1) One wife The strongest drive in men, Rabbeinu Avraham says, is women — and a wealthy man could have married many. והתרחק מן הדבר הזה — לא לקח אשה אחרת על אשתו, בלעדי הגר, וגם זאת ברשות שרה, ורק כדי להביא זרע. Rabbeinu Avraham insists in several places in his Torah commentary that the ideal is one wife . The Torah permits more; the Chachamim advised not more than four , for the women's emotional sake. But the ideal: Adam and Chava — one and one. Yitzchak and Rivka — one and one. Yaakov wanted only Rachel . Lavan tricked him into Leah; he then married Rachel, whom he really wanted; when Rachel couldn't have children he took another — and ended with four. That wasn't his plan. Avraham — only Sarah; Hagar only at Sarah's request, for children. (2) Sarah's beauty was never the focal point התבונן ביופיה של שרה... עד שאפילו בימי זקנתה חשקוה אנשי הארץ — Avimelech took her to his palace at ninety . (Why he cites Avimelech rather than Pharaoh the Rabbi wasn't sure — perhaps because there her age is explicit.) וחז\"ל אמרו כי לא התבונן אברהם אבינו ביפיה והדרה עד שבא למצרים, וזה סיבת מאמרו: הנה נא ידעתי כי אשה יפת מראה את. The famous midrash — \"now I know\" — doesn't mean Avraham had never looked at his wife. Of course he saw her and appreciated her beauty. It simply was never the focal point of their relationship. Now they were going to Mitzrayim — עגלה יפהפיה מצרים — a society where beauty is the focus (it ties into Chanukah, as discussed in the past). So now they had to be careful. Being a parush doesn't mean marrying someone unattractive or living in poverty — it means it isn't your focus. (3) Keturah — not Hagar ומה שלקח אברהם אבינו את קטורה אחר מות שרה... — ויסף אברהם ויקח אשה ושמה קטורה (next week's parashah, Chayei Sarah). The Midrash (cited by Rashi ) says Keturah was Hagar — then there's no question; he had no wife and took her back. But Rabbeinu Avraham holds she was a third woman. So why would a parush marry again? לא היה זה אלא על פי רוח הקודש, או ציווי שאינו כתוב בתורה, ותכליתו לפרסם המשך הנס שנולד יצחק באחרית הימים... By Divine instruction — to publicise the miracle that even beyond 140 he could still father children. How does he know Avraham was over 140? The Rabbi could see 137 (Sarah died at 127; Avraham was ten years older) but not 140 — until Donna pointed out: Avraham was 100 when Yitzchak was born, and Yitzchak was 40 when he married Rivka (start of Toldot). So Avraham was 140 at Yitzchak's wedding , and Keturah came after. \"Excellent — clearly. Pashut.\" (4) Refusing the spoils of Sedom אם מחוט ועד שרוך נעל, ואם אקח מכל אשר לך, ולא תאמר אני העשרתי את אברם. (Lech Lecha) After defeating the four kings, Avraham refused even a shoelace from the king of Sedom. He didn't care. Who is not a parush ולכן אין לומר על כל עני שרואים אותו לבוש קרעים ואוכל חתיכות פת חרבה שהוא פרוש... Not every poor person living simply is a parush — he has no choice. אפילו מי שסיגל עצמו בדרכי פשיטות — לבישת בגדים גסים ואכילת מאכלים תפלים כגון פת יבשה, ויתבודד במדברות ובחדרים וכיוצא בהם — אלא על תנאי שלבו מרוחק מן העולם הזה, ולא ירצה בהם ולא ישתוקק לשום אחד אשר בעולם... Even someone who has trained himself in coarse clothes, plain food and solitude is not a parush — unless his heart is detached from this world. אמור מעתה: מי שמרוחק בלבו מאהבת העולם הזה — הוא הפרוש האמתי. ומי שמשתוקק אליהם, ולבו בוער באהבתם, ומצטער על חסרונם — איננו שום פרוש. וזה דבר שראוי לך לתת דעתך עליו. In conclusion: if in your heart you don't care about these things — you are the true parush . If you long for them, even while not living with them — you are not a parush at all . Something we can all work on The most important thing to take from this shiur — and from the whole chapter — is that perishut is in the heart . How a person lives is not the main point; it is about the attention we give these things. Fasting and wearing simple clothes may be hard to relate to in our generation — \"at least for me myself\" — but developing the heart is something every one of us can work on . The honest test: if our desire for material things is stronger than our desire for spiritual growth, that's a problem. Do I get more excited when I've understood something new in the Rambam, in the Torah — or when I've been given a new cheque? Summary Rabbeinu Avraham on Vayera: Sarah saw Yishmael fooling around and speaking coarsely , and, feeling personally responsible for Yitzchak's perfection , sent him away. Hagar didn't doubt the promise — she feared שמא יגרום החטא , since promises can be conditional (and the Akedah, as part of Avraham's test, raises the same question). באשר הוא שם in its simple meaning is where he physically lay , under the bush. And בעת ההיא is vague (before or after), whereas אחרי הדברים האלה is definite — proven from Devarim, where at that time follows the 40th year but refers to the 2nd. The importance of perishut: it frees the heart from worry and the time from toil; the prophets chose to be shepherds and farmers for free time and solitude, and lived extremely simply. The Torah's mitzvot train restraint; cherem (consecration, per Rabbeinu Avraham and Rashi; destruction, per Ralbag), Yericho and Achan show the Torah values it; and Hoshea compares love of pleasure to idolatry. The definition: perishut is in the heart — not in fasting while longing, but in being so absorbed in serving Hashem that one has no interest in the rest. Like humility — thinking of yourself less, not less of yourself. Wealth that comes unsought is no contradiction: Yitzchak, Yaakov and Avraham were wealthy perushim . Avraham's single marriage, Sarah's beauty never being his focus, Keturah by Divine instruction after 140, and his refusal of Sedom's spoils all show it. A poor man, or even a trained ascetic whose heart still longs, is not a parush. Coming Up Next week, Monday 6:00pm: how to attain perishut — two levels, and the means of training towards it. \"That'll be the whole shiur next week.\" Then: how to know you've achieved it; its fruits; and the contradictions in Tanach — with Yitzchak's blessings around Parashat Toldot . A short idea from Rabbeinu Avraham on the parashah each week through Bereishit and Shemot. Tuesday 8:00pm — Moreh Nebuchim (\"a fascinating shiur\" — listen to last week's first if you're following). Thursday 6:00pm — Halacha. B'Ezrat Hashem","nCSbLk1opq8":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, continued: techiyat hametim and the days of Mashiach Shiur: Part 5 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 28 October 2025 These notes are based on the transcript of the recording. Recap The final part of the secondary forms of reward and punishment. People focus too much on this-worldly reward, Gan Eden, Gehinom, Mashiach and resurrection . The real thing is Olam HaBa. Last week: Gan Eden and Gehinom , for the Rambam physical places on earth, sometime during resurrection and the Messianic era . Tonight: resurrection and Mashiach, contrasted with other Rishonim. Then back to Olam HaBa. Part 1: Techiyat Hametim תחיית המתים הוא יסוד מיסודות משה רבנו עליו השלום, ואין דת ולא דבקות בדת היהודית למי שלא יאמין בזה. אבל היא לצדיקים. Resurrection is one of the foundations of Moshe Rabbeinu's Torah. One who doesn't believe it has no religion and no attachment to the Jewish faith. But it is for the righteous. Contrast: \"I emphasised last week: Gan Eden and Gehinom are not among the 13 Principles. In the grand scheme they're minor . I know that may sound shocking, but it's the truth. \" Resurrection is a foundation. Bereishit Rabbah: גבורת גשמים לצדיקים ולרשעים, ותחיית המתים לצדיקים בלבד. Rain falls on everyone, wicked and righteous; resurrection is for the righteous alone. ואיך יחיו הרשעים והם מתים אפילו בחייהם? How could the wicked live again, when they're dead even in life? רשעים בחייהם קרויים מתים, צדיקים במיתתן קרויים חיים. ודע כי האדם יש לו למות בהכרח ויתפרד וישוב למה שהורכב ממנו. And know that a person must die and disintegrate, returning to what he was made of , even after resurrection. Point 1: resurrection ends The Rambam is quite unique here. Nothing material or physical can endure forever, and resurrection is of the body . So however long it lasts (\"a thousand, two thousand years,\" a figure he'll give for Mashiach), it's finite . The Ramban (and Ramchal, many Rishonim) The Rambam Resurrection Body and soul rise into Olam HaBa , a new world , and live forever A finite period on earth; the resurrected die again His reply to the Rambam \"Hashem can do miracles. He can make us live forever, change our bodies' nature\" Philosophically, what's created must end ; dust returns to dust (Rav Saadia: resurrection also ends, but \"let's leave him aside.\") Point 2: then why is it a principle of faith? If resurrection means living forever, it's obviously foundational. If it's a temporary period after which you die again, why is it one of the 13? The Rambam asks this himself, in the Moreh: because it's the greatest miracle there can be. The sea split, but a dead person living again is \" the quintessential miracle .\" Not believing it means not believing in miracles, which means not believing in Hashem's hashgachah . \"It's a symptom of how you understand God operating in the world.\" (The prophets' revived child: \"it's not entirely clear he'd actually died rather than been unconscious. Leave that aside.\") Point 3: why leave Olam HaBa to come back? The Rambam's timeline: die today, begin Olam HaBa immediately : \"no Gan Eden, no Gehinom, and for sure no gilgulim .\" Mashiach and resurrection are in the future . And he's not tied to 6,000 years : \" Rav Saadia is: the world lasts 6,000 years, so Mashiach must come in the next 214 years (it's 5786 ). The Rambam isn't. The world continues, people are born and die, and everyone from the last 5,786 years is already in Olam HaBa .\" The famous question: why bring someone down from Olam HaBa , the ultimate bliss of the radiance of the Shechinah , to live a thousand years on earth? \" However utopian, it's incomparable. \" (For the Ramban, resurrection is Olam HaBa; for Rav Saadia, Olam HaBa hasn't begun yet. Only the Rambam has this problem. ) Rav Shilat's answer , \" I think you're almost forced to say it \": there will be bechirah , free will, for the resurrected , and the Rambam is explicit that free will continues in Mashiach's days. A new opportunity to develop one's character and intellect: that's worth coming down for. \"How there's still free will after you've tasted Olam HaBa , I can't answer. God can do that. \" A weaker alternative: resurrection exists to demonstrate that Hashem performs the greatest miracles. \"I find that difficult.\" Point 4: who are \"the righteous\"? Reading Everyone not cut off Anyone with a portion: most of the Jewish people, \"maybe humanity\" Only the very righteous \" The crème de la crème \": if it's a second shot , given after you've seen the bliss, perhaps it's reserved for the elite Either way, the cut-off don't rise. But sinners with a portion do , for Gehinom 's purging (last week): Gehinom is on earth, at the resurrection. \" Otherwise, who's Gehinom for? \" Questions Why not use Hilchot Teshuvah's definition of tzaddik (merits outweigh sins)? \"Because coming back from Olam HaBa for a second chance is a huge chiddush , and I'm trying to limit it. And in the prophets, resurrection sometimes just contrasts righteous and wicked. Hilchot Teshuvah's rasha can still have a portion. I don't think he has that specific definition in mind here. It's possible either way. \" \"It's scary: you earned Olam HaBa, now you're sent back for a thousand years, and could lose it!\" (Rav Dessler asks this in Michtav MeEliyahu.) First, a parallel: gilgul . \"I don't believe in gilgul , as the Rambam didn't.\" If you're reincarnated to fix your sins and come back worse , a third time, a fourth? Rav Chaim Vital (in the Arizal's name): if you were 70% good, 30% bad , you return with only the 30% , spread across you, so you can only gain . \"Completely incompatible with the Rambam's framework.\" (Rav Chashin of Mir, one of its greatest scholars and a Breslover, told the Rabbi that his tradition is: if you're offered a gilgul in Heaven, decline.) The Rambam's answer (as the Nefesh HaChaim says of pre-sin Eden): Mashiach's era is free will between good and better . Everyone knows Hashem; His presence is revealed, not hidden . \" Doing bad will be very difficult. The question is how far you're willing to exert yourself: to go from good to great. You can't go backwards.\" The class: so the real gain is now, choosing good against bad; then it's extra on top. \" That fits very nicely: this life is the main thing. \" On doubting even with clarity: \"As a kid I thought: what's the big test of the Akeidah? If Hashem told me, I'd do it too. It's not so simple. We always find a way out : maybe He didn't really mean it. Even after seeing Olam HaBa, we could find a way to doubt.\" Part 2: The Days of Mashiach אמנם ימות המשיח הוא זמן שתשוב המלכות לישראל, ויחזרו לארץ ישראל, ויהיה אותו המלך גדול מאד, ובית מלכותו בציון, ויגדל שמו… יותר מן המלך שלמה, וישלימו עמו כל האומות, ויעבדוהו כל הארצות לצדקו הגדול ולפלאות שיהיו על ידו, וכל מי שיקום עליו ימסרהו השם בידו. Sovereignty Returns to Israel ; all Jews return to the Land The king Very great , his seat in Zion , renowned beyond Shlomo The nations Make peace with him, drawn by his righteousness and wonders His enemies Hashem delivers them into his hand מלך המשיח = the anointed king , since kings were anointed with oil. \"As we saw a couple of years ago with King Charles , anointed with holy oil from Jerusalem .\" \" A completely sovereign Israel. No terrorist attacks, nobody at the UN vetoing Israel or severing diplomatic ties . Everyone wants ties with Israel , everyone wants this king to visit.\" Nature doesn't change ולא ישתנה במציאות דבר ממה שהוא עתה, אלא שהמלכות תחזור לישראל. אין בין העולם הזה לימות המשיח אלא שעבוד מלכויות בלבד. Nothing changes in nature. There will still be rich and poor, strong and weak . One difference: livelihood will be very easy : בעמל מעט… יגיע לתועלת גדולה . \" This is where the world is heading. People see AI and robots as a threat to work, and from that angle rightly so. In the Rambam's framework it makes total sense: we're coming to a time when humans won't have to work. Just switch on the machine.\" \"Cakes and garments from the trees\" עתידה ארץ ישראל שתוציא גלוסקאות וכלי מילת (Shabbat 30b): loaves and fine woollen garments . Not literal. It's like saying of someone who comes home to everything cooked: he found baked bread and a cooked dish . \" Money growing on trees : you don't mean it literally; it's that easy.\" Proof: ובני נכר אכריכם וכרמיכם (Yeshayahu): there will still be farmers and vinedressers . Rabban Gamliel and the mushrooms. His student objected: אין כל חדש תחת השמש ! Rabban Gamliel answered with mushrooms and truffles , which spring up overnight. The Rambam: that wasn't a real answer. The student had taken it literally, so he answered a fool according to his folly . Mishlei 26:4–5 : אל תען כסיל כאולתו / ענה כסיל כאולתו : ignore a foolish question about worldly matters; answer one about Torah, at his level. The real point of Mashiach והתועלת הגדולה שתהיה באותם הימים, שננוח משעבוד מלכויות, שמונע אותנו מלעשות המצות כולן, ותרבה החכמה… כי מלאה הארץ דעה את ה׳ כמים לים מכסים, ותשבתנה המלחמות, כמו שנאמר: לא ישא גוי אל גוי חרב. Freedom from subjugation that stops us keeping all the mitzvot ; wisdom will increase; wars will cease. The reservists. \"A good friend in Israel told me the batei midrash have been less than half full for the last two years . Why? People are out fighting. How can you develop your intellect while fighting wars? There won't be wars. \" ונזכה בו לחיי העולם הבא. \" Look at these words. \" In Mashiach's days we can improve our portion in Olam HaBa . What will I do without work? \" You'll have time to study. That's the greatest joy: time to sit undisturbed and develop your intellect. \" The king will die ומשיח ימות, וימלוך בנו תחתיו ובן בנו. A dynasty . לא יכהה ולא ירוץ עד ישים בארץ משפט : until he establishes justice; then he'll die. His reign will be long , and people will live longer , since worries and sorrows will be gone . \" Same as today, people living longer , but more extreme.\" ואין לתמוה שתתקיים מלכותו אלפים שנה : it could last two thousand years. קבוץ הטוב קשה פירודו. Why the prophets longed for it ואין אנו מתאוים ומקוים לימות המשיח לרוב התבואות והעושר, ולא שנרכב על הסוסים ולא שנשתה היין בכלי שיר, כמו שחושבים מבולבלי הדעת. Not for abundant crops and wealth , not to ride horses (\" the best cars \") or drink wine with music (\" the best wine \"), \"as the confused think.\" The prophets and the pious longed for it for the gathering of the righteous , good conduct and wisdom , the king's justice, wisdom and closeness to God , and keeping all the Torah's mitzvot without laziness or compulsion . \" How many mitzvot can't we keep now? No Temple, no kohanim functioning. \" ולא ילמדו עוד איש את רעהו… דעו את ה׳, כי כולם ידעו אותי למקטנם ועד גדולם ; ונתתי את תורתי בקרבם ועל לבם אכתבנה ; והסרתי את לב האבן מבשרכם . ובאלה העניינים ישיגו העולם הבא השגה חזקה. Through these, Olam HaBa is attained powerfully. Key Points on Mashiach Mashiach is in this world : everyone agrees. It excludes those who think Mashiach's days are Olam HaBa . The king has military and spiritual roles : he wins wars and makes treaties, and teaches Torah . (The Rabbi remarked that today there's essentially one group of Jews who both fight and teach Torah.) It's a process (the Moreh; Gog and Magog ). There may be battles, even potential messiahs who die . A false messiah is one who comes to change the Torah. Shabbetai Tzvi is the classic case. Bar Kochba wasn't false : he tried and lost , but didn't change the Torah, and Rabbi Akiva supported him. Jesus (or his students) changed the Torah , so the Rambam calls him a false messiah. Not supernatural. That's why Hilchot Melachim u'Milchamot holds the laws of Mashiach. They're not in Sefer HaMada with resurrection and Torah min hashamayim : \" Mashiach is a halachic time. \" The Rambam's siddur: after ובא לציון (\" if we prayed Shacharit by the Rambam's siddur, I think many more people would come \"): …שנחיה לשמור חקיך בעולם הזה ולימות המשיח, כדי שנזכה ונירש טובה וברכה לחיי העולם הבא. May we live to keep Your laws in this world and in the days of Mashiach, so that we merit the life of Olam HaBa. Will the world end? He's writing a thousand years ago that Mashiach might last two thousand years , impossible if the world ends at 6,000. The Moreh addresses שיתא אלפי שני הוו עלמא וחד חרוב (Sanhedrin): 1. It's one Sage's view. 2. \"A thousand years destroyed\" : if the world were gone, how is there time? Time is part of creation. So charuv means change , not the end. 3. It may hint at something deeper. The world likely continues forever. The Rambam's Whole Timeline We die Immediately into Olam HaBa , each by his portion Someday (maybe today) The Messianic era , with wars , then Mashiach . Those in Olam HaBa see it. Gan Eden, Gehinom and resurrection happen within it Its end The resurrected die again and return to Olam HaBa; those who lived through it die and (hopefully) go there And then The earth continues : 10,000, 20,000, 40,000 years. People are born, live and die, and go to Olam HaBa: a continuous cycle Not all agree there's bechirah in Mashiach's days : e.g. Shabbat 151b: הן ימים אשר תאמר אין לי בהם חפץ — אלו ימות המשיח, שאין בהם לא זכות ולא חובה . \" No one can reconcile every statement of the Sages. Everyone tries their best. \" A last question: who recognises Mashiach? If it's a process, and humans must acknowledge the criteria (the old problem with false messiahs), who decides? \" Excellent question. The criteria are very specific (in the Mishneh Torah): military, Torah, and more. He'll need the majority of Israel to accept him. Would the Sanhedrin be reconstituted first? I don't know. If there were a Sanhedrin, it would be simple.\" \" We could have a Sanhedrin even now. We just need the majority of Klal Yisrael to agree who the Sages would be. They tried 500 years ago , and couldn't agree. It would solve so many of our issues , and be a good step towards Mashiach .\" Summary Resurrection is a foundation : not believing it means not believing in miracles and hashgachah . It's for the righteous ; the cut-off don't rise , though sinners with a portion do, for Gehinom. For the Rambam alone, resurrection ends : the body must die again . (The Ramban: forever, by miracle.) Why leave Olam HaBa? A second chance at growth , free will between good and better (Rav Shilat). This life is the main thing. Mashiach : sovereignty, peace, ingathering , a king greater than Shlomo, in a world whose nature doesn't change . Easy livelihood (AI and robots point the way); \"cakes on trees\" = money growing on trees . Its purpose: freedom to learn and keep every mitzvah , and so a stronger Olam HaBa . Not for wealth or wine. Mashiach dies ; a dynasty; perhaps 2,000 years . It's a process ; a false messiah changes the Torah . Halachic, not supernatural (Hilchot Melachim). The world likely continues forever. Coming Up Next week: the paragraph where \"the Rambam's whole philosophy is summarised\" (Olam HaBa again), and then the 13 Principles of Faith .","nitgexpO-YM":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: Mishneh Torah (Rambam), Sefer Zemanim — Hilchot Shevitat Asor (three chapters only) Perek: 1 — all seven halachot Topic: Why the Rambam does not call these Hilchot Yom Kippur ; the four mitzvot of the day; the three places Yom Kippur appears in the Torah; melachah on Yom Kippur and how it compares to Shabbat; the Or Sameach on \"the tenth\" versus \"Yom Kippur\" ; kinuv yerek ; affliction as eating and drinking; whether the other four innuyim are biblical; tosefet — and why, according to the Rambam, there is no such thing as tosefet Shabbat ; and the women who ate until dark Schedule note: Second week of the yamim tovim series. We have been learning Hilchot Shabbat for the last few years and are around siman 320 ; that is paused for a few weeks while we take the chagim from the Rambam rather than from the Shulchan Aruch as in previous years. Last week was Hilchot Shofar ; tonight Yom Kippur; next week Sukkot . Chapters 2 and 3 were not reached — learn them over Yom Kippur (see Coming Up). Introduction — Why It Is Not Called \"Hilchot Yom Kippur\" Worth pausing on. In the Talmud the masechta is called Yoma — \"the day.\" The Rambam writes in Hebrew, so he would not call it Yoma; you would therefore expect Hilchot Yom Kippur . He calls it neither. He calls it הלכות שביתת עשור — the laws of resting on the tenth . Compare his other titles: Hilchot Rosh Hashanah , Hilchot Sukkah V'Lulav , Hilchot Chametz U'Matzah (why Pesach is titled that way is its own discussion). Why not Yom Kippur? Because the kaparah — the atonement aspect of the day — he has already dealt with exhaustively in Hilchot Teshuvah , which we have learned together in full, in great length and depth, a few years ago. Here he is not dealing with the atonement of the day at all. He is dealing with the day itself : resting on the tenth and fasting on the tenth. Hence the title. The Four Mitzvot (from the Rambam's own introduction to the section) He tells us in the introduction — it is not in the text of the chapter — that there are four mitzvot in this section: two positive and two negative , which are really two mitzvot , each with a positive and a negative: # Mitzvah Form 1 Rest from work Positive — to rest 2 Do not do work Negative 3 Afflict yourself Positive 4 Do not fail to afflict yourself (i.e. do not eat or drink) Negative That is the whole agenda of the three chapters — and precisely why the section is titled the laws of resting on the tenth . The Three Places Yom Kippur Appears in the Torah Before opening the Rambam it is crucial to see the verses. Yom Kippur is discussed in only three places in the entire Torah — twice in Vayikra, once in Bamidbar : Place Parashah When we read it Vayikra 16 Acharei Mot The morning of Yom Kippur Vayikra 23 Emor First day of Pesach; first two days of Sukkot Bamidbar 29 Pinchas (the korbanot) Maftir of Yom Kippur morning 1. Acharei Mot (Vayikra 16) After the whole procedure of the goats: והיתה לכם לחקת עולם: בחדש השביעי בעשור לחדש תענו את נפשתיכם וכל מלאכה לא תעשו The seventh month (Tishrei), the tenth day — afflict your souls (positive #1) and do not do any work (negative). כי ביום הזה יכפר עליכם לטהר אתכם מכל חטאתיכם לפני ה' תטהרו The kaparah verse — the Rambam will not discuss this here ; it belongs to Hilchot Teshuvah. שבת שבתון היא לכם ועניתם את נפשתיכם שבת שבתון is the positive mitzvah to rest from work; ועניתם is afflict yourselves a second time . והיתה זאת לכם לחקת עולם לכפר על בני ישראל מכל חטאתם אחת בשנה — again kaparah, again Hilchot Teshuvah. Tally from Acharei Mot: two positives to afflict, one positive to rest, one negative not to work. 2. Emor (Vayikra 23) אך בעשור לחדש השביעי הזה יום הכפרים הוא — kaparah, not our subject. ועניתם את נפשתיכם — afflict yourselves, a third time — וכל מלאכה לא תעשו . כי כל הנפש אשר לא תענה בעצם היום הזה ונכרתה מעמיה — here is the negative : one who does not afflict himself receives karet . And likewise one who does work on this day receives karet. שבת שבתון הוא לכם ועניתם את נפשתיכם — the positive to rest, and a fourth afflict yourselves . בתשעה לחדש בערב מערב עד ערב תשבתו שבתכם — when : from the ninth in the evening, evening to evening (this is the whole basis of tosefet — see Halachah 6). 3. Pinchas (Bamidbar 29) Again the tenth of the seventh month — ועניתם את נפשתיכם (a fifth time) — וכל מלאכה לא תעשו . The grand tally: five times the Torah says positively \"afflict your souls.\" Remember this — it is the key to Halachah 5. Halachah 1 — The Positive and Negative of Melachah Note the order: the Rambam begins with work, not affliction , because work is the more familiar category — as on Shabbat and yom tov. מצות עשה לשבות ממלאכה בעשור לחדש השביעי, שנאמר: שבת שבתון היא לכם. A positive mitzvah to rest from all melachah on the tenth of the seventh month. וכל העושה בו מלאכה ביטל מצות עשה ועבר על לא תעשה, שנאמר: ובעשור לחדש השביעי הזה... כל מלאכה לא תעשו. One who works has lost the positive mitzvah and violated a negative . ומה הוא חייב על עשיית מלאכה ביום זה? אם עשה ברצונו בזדון — חייב כרת. עשה בשגגה — חייב קרבן חטאת קבועה. Willingly and intentionally: karet . Inadvertently (he did not know it was Yom Kippur, or knew but did not know this was forbidden): in Temple times, a fixed sin offering — as opposed to the sliding-scale offering. Two comments 1. Why does the Rambam quote Pinchas? When citing the negative he goes all the way to Bamidbar , even though Vayikra says it explicitly too — וכל מלאכה לא תעשו בעצם היום הזה. And in Sefer HaMitzvot he quotes Vayikra . It is peculiar that at the opening of the section he reached for the later verse in Parashat Pinchas. The Rabbi could not find anyone who discusses this and has no settled answer — possibly there is an explanation; incidental, but worth noting. 2. Driving on Shabbat is more severe than driving on Yom Kippur. It always strikes the Rabbi as peculiar how many people who consider themselves traditionally shomer Shabbat drive on Shabbat but will not drive on Yom Kippur — \"on Yom Kippur we walk.\" The reality is the reverse in severity. Someone driving a regular (non-electric) car has almost certainly violated מבעיר , combustion, one of the 39 melachot — an issur d'oraita ; done willingly and consciously, he is חייב סקילה , which carries karet with it and is more severe still. Yom Kippur carries karet, not the death penalty. So it is lovely that people do not drive to synagogue on Yom Kippur — but if you are going to break Yom Kippur, why break Shabbat? There is an answer, and it comes in the next halachah. Halachah 2 — Everything Is the Same as Shabbat כל מלאכה שחייבין על זדונה בשבת סקילה — חייבין על זדונה בעשור כרת. וכל שחייבין עליה קרבן חטאת בשבת — חייבין עליה קרבן חטאת ביום הכפורים. וכל דבר שאסור לעשותו בשבת אף על פי שאינו מלאכה — אסור לעשותו ביום הכפורים. (For example the techum : rabbinically 2,000 amot, biblically 24,000 — walking the forbidden biblical amount on Shabbat is an issur d'oraita though not sekilah. Anything forbidden on Shabbat even though it is not one of the 39 melachot is equally forbidden on Yom Kippur.) ואם עשה — מכין אותו מכת מרדות, כשם שמכין אותו על השבת. וכל שאסור לטלטלו בשבת — אסור לטלטלו ביום הכפורים. (Muktzeh on Shabbat is muktzeh on Yom Kippur.) וכל שאסור לאומרו או לעשותו לכתחלה בשבת — אסור ביום הכפורים. (Everything forbidden to say on Shabbat: speaking to a non-Jew, discussing business, walking towards something forbidden, running — all of ממצוא חפצך ודבר דבר .) כללו של דבר: אין בין שבת ליום הכפורים בעניינים אלו, אלא שזדון מלאכת שבת בסקילה וזדון יום הכפורים בכרת. The only difference is the punishment: Shabbat, sekilah; Yom Kippur, karet. (One tiny exception in what you may do follows in Halachah 3 — and it does not apply nowadays.) The Or Sameach — look carefully at the wording Count the comparisons: six times the Rambam sets Shabbat against Yom Kippur — כל מלאכה / וכל שחייבין / וכל דבר / וכל שאסור / וכל שאסור / כללו של דבר. Now notice: in comparisons two through six he calls the day \"Yom HaKippurim.\" But in the first — the one about the punishment for willful violation — he writes חייבין על זדונה בעשור כרת : on the tenth . Why switch? Either call it \"the tenth\" throughout or \"Yom Kippur\" throughout. It is an apparent and basic question. The Or Sameach — Rabbi Meir Simcha HaKohen of Dvinsk, about a hundred years ago — points to something beautiful: If you violate Yom Kippur willingly, intentionally, בזדון — it is no longer called Yom Kippur for you. It is \"the tenth.\" If I broke it by mistake, or transgressed something rabbinic, or muktzeh, or spoke improperly — it is still Yom Kippur for me : it is still the day of atonement, and I still receive atonement for the whole past year, as in Hilchot Teshuvah. But if I consciously and willingly break Yom Kippur, it is not Yom Kippur for me at all — it is merely עשור , the tenth day of the seventh month. And with this we can answer the question from Halachah 1. There is perhaps some justification for that widespread (and still wrong) practice: yes, breaking Shabbat is more severe in punishment — sekilah rather than karet — but if I intentionally violate Yom Kippur , then the entire day goes out of the window; it is no longer Yom Kippur for me, and what would be the point of the whole synagogue? Better, of course, to do no melachah on either. But that is a beautiful explanation of the instinct. Halachah 3 — The One Leniency (Which We Do Not Practise) Straight from the Talmud — the only place Yom Kippur differs from Shabbat in what you may do. It does not apply nowadays, but here it is. מותר לקנב את הירק ביום הכפורים מן המנחה ולמעלה. From Minchah time onward one may do kinuv to vegetables. What is kinuv ? — שיסיר את עליהן המעופשות ויקצץ השאר, כדי שיהיה מתוקן לאכול לערב : remove the withered leaves of a lettuce or cabbage head and cut up the rest, so it is ready to eat in the evening. וכן מפצעין באגוזין ומפרכין ברימונים מן המנחה ולמעלה, מפני עגמת נפש. Likewise cracking nuts and opening pomegranates — מפני עגמת נפש , so a person should not be distressed. יום הכפורים שחל להיות בשבת — אסור בקניבת ירק ובפציעת אגוזים ובפריכת רימונים כל היום. If Yom Kippur falls on Shabbat it is ordinary Shabbat and none of these leniencies apply . (This year Yom Kippur falls on a weekday, so they would apply mi-dina .) וכבר נהגו העם בשנער ובמערב שלא יעשו אחד מכל אלו ביום הצום, אלא הרי הוא כשבת לכל דבריו. The custom in Babylonia and in the west is not to do any of these, and to treat Yom Kippur exactly like Shabbat in every respect — as we learned in Halachot 1 and 2. The Gemara itself already records that this became the custom , so although the leniency is the strict law, the Rambam rules this way, the Shulchan Aruch rules this way, and nobody does this today . What was it, and why was it permitted? עגמת נפש — if you could not prepare, you would spend the day distressed and preoccupied: what am I going to eat tonight? So you may start preparing salads and fruit for afterwards. But how is removing the withered leaves not borer ? The Chachamim cannot permit a d'oraita just so a person can prepare his post-fast meal. Note first that other Rishonim read kinuv differently — Tosafot take it to mean only washing the vegetables, not removing anything, precisely because of the borer concern. The Rambam understands it as removing the bad leaves. So how is it not borer? There is a large discussion in the Acharonim: Answer Reasoning Maggid Mishneh It is not פסולת גמור — the withered leaves are not genuinely waste; they are still edible. Recall the principle from our learning of borer. Others It is all מין אחד — one single species. Rav Kappach (and others) — the best of the three, in the Rabbi's view It is clearly identifiable. As we learned a few months ago, borer applies only to a genuine mixture — peanuts, pistachios and cashews mixed together and not ascertainable without moving things. Here you have a lettuce head with the withered leaves on the outside and the good ones in the middle: that is not a mixture at all . And the nuts? Here the issue is דש , threshing — extracting the nut from its shell. Cracking to eat immediately is one thing; cracking to leave for later is another. So the poskim point out it must be that you crush them without actually extracting them — they crack and mix together, and you pick them out and eat them straight after the fast. Halachah 4 — The Positive and Negative of Affliction That completes the melachah half of the introduction's four mitzvot. The remaining mitzvah — the positive and negative of affliction — runs from here to the end of the chapter and through Chapters 2 and 3. מצות עשה אחרת יש ביום הכפורים, והוא לשבות בו מאכילה ושתיה, שנאמר: תענו את נפשתיכם. Notice what he does not say: not washing, not shoes, not relations, not anointing. Only eating and drinking. מפי השמועה למדו: איזהו עינוי שהוא לנפש? זה הצום. וכל הצם בו קיים מצות עשה. From the oral tradition: the affliction of the soul is fasting . One who fasts fulfils the positive mitzvah. Why does \"afflict your soul\" mean not eating and drinking? The Rambam already wrote in his commentary to the Mishnah : דבר שהנפש תלויה בו — זו אכילה ושתיה — the thing upon which life itself depends is eating and drinking. The Gemara's alternative (Yoma): perhaps תענו את נפשתיכם means you should sit in the sun until you are boiling hot and sunburnt? The Gemara rejects it: affliction of the nefesh means something the soul is dependent upon — without which you cannot live. וכל האוכל ושותה בו ביטל מצות עשה ועבר על לא תעשה, שנאמר: כי כל הנפש אשר לא תענה בעצם היום הזה ונכרתה מעמיה. מאחר שהעניש הכתוב כרת למי שלא נתענה, למדנו שמוזהרין בו על אכילה ושתיה. Since the Torah imposes karet on one who is not afflicted, we learn that eating and drinking are prohibited . וכל האוכל או שותה בו בשוגג — חייב בקרבן חטאת קבועה. So: eating or drinking loses a positive mitzvah and violates a negative carrying karet ; inadvertently, a fixed sin offering. Halachah 5 — The Other Four Innuyim: Biblical or Rabbinic? A major discussion. Read it carefully. וכן למדנו מפי השמועה שאסור לרחוץ בו, או לסוך, או לנעול את הסנדל, או לבעול. From the oral tradition: washing, anointing, wearing (leather) shoes, and marital relations are forbidden. ומצוה לשבות מכל אלו כשם שהוא שובת מאכילה ושתיה, שנאמר: שבת שבתון — שבת מן האכילה, שבתון מן אלו. A double rest: shabbat from eating and drinking, shabbaton from these. ואין חייבין כרת או קרבן אלא על אכילה ושתיה. Karet (intentionally) and a sin offering (inadvertently) apply only to eating and drinking — everyone agrees, because the Torah wrote תענו in connection with eating. אבל אם רחץ או סך או נעל או בעל — מכין אותו מכת מרדות. The tension מפי השמועה implies something biblical (not of the same severity as eating and drinking, but biblical) — and he quotes a verse . Yet מכת מרדות always means rabbinic lashes . So what has a person done if he showered, or applied creams, or engaged in relations, or wore leather shoes on Yom Kippur? Why these five things at all? This is why we went through the verses at the start of the shiur. Five times the Torah says positively afflict your souls : ועניתם and תענו (twice in Acharei Mot), twice in Emor, once in Pinchas. The Chachamim played on the fivefold repetition of the one mitzvah: there must be something more to add on — hence washing, anointing, shoes and relations alongside eating and drinking. The positions Authority Holds Rosh, Tur and many other Rishonim All four are rabbinic ; the verses cited in the Gemara (שבת שבתון, and the repetitions of תענו) are the peg. Hence rabbinic lashes, and no biblical prohibition in showering on Yom Kippur. Rabbeinu Nissim on the Rambam Understands the Rambam to hold d'oraita . Radbaz and others A whole machloket as to what the Rambam holds. The Rabbi's own reading Rabbinic , to be consistent with his other writings (below). Two strong indications that the Rambam holds rabbinic: His commentary to the Mishnah (Yoma) — written in his twenties — says it outright: הם רמזים ואסמכתות, ועיקר שאינם מקבלה, ולכן אינו חייב עליהם כרת אבל מכת מרדות. He calls them an asmachta , which is clearly rabbinic. At least when he wrote that, he definitely held the other four are rabbinic. Sefer HaMitzvot, mitzvah 196 — the prohibition against not afflicting oneself on Yom Kippur — discusses only eating and drinking , with not a word about the other four. Since Sefer HaMitzvot deals only with biblical mitzvot, the implication is clear. What remains difficult is מפי השמועה . One could say he is merely quoting the Gemara's verses as an asmachta, not real proof-texts — but the phrase itself is hard, and that is why the tension in the Rambam persists. Robert asked: if it is rabbinic, how do we deal with the fact that he invokes an oral tradition , which would suggest halachah l'Moshe mi-Sinai ? — The Rabbi: exactly right, those are the problematic words. And if you hold it is d'oraita, what will you do with מכת מרדות? Both questions are valid; you are left in a quandary either way. You could say מפי השמועה means rabbinic traditions — but that is very strange, because when the Rambam uses מפי השמועה he almost always means the Chachamim interpreting a biblical verse . Hence the tension. What is clear regardless: eating and drinking are d'oraita with karet ; for the other four there is no karet and no sin offering for shogeg — whether one has violated a negative biblical prohibition or a rabbinic enactment is open to dispute. Halachah 6 — Night and Day, and Tosefet כשם ששבות ממלאכה בו בין ביום ובין בלילה, כך שבות לעינוי בין ביום ובין בלילה. Just as melachah is forbidden both day and night — the word yom notwithstanding — so too the afflictions apply at night. You cannot go home at night and eat, drink or do the other innuyim. Straightforward; the Gemara learns it clearly. וצריך להוסיף מחול על הקודש בכניסתו וביציאתו, שנאמר: ועניתם את נפשתיכם בתשעה לחדש בערב. The source The verse in Emor reads: שבת שבתון הוא לכם ועניתם את נפשתיכם בתשעה לחדש בערב, מערב עד ערב תשבתו שבתכם. The Gemara asks: Yom Kippur is on the tenth — the Torah has said so repeatedly! So \"the ninth in the evening\" must mean late afternoon of the ninth, just before the tenth: you must add on . And מערב עד ערב — you must add on at the end as well. This verse and this Gemara are the entire source of the concept of adding on before and after. But notice exactly what the Rambam writes כלומר: התחל לצום ולהתענות מערב תשעה סמוך לעשירי. וכן ביציאתו — שוהה בעינויו מעט מליל אחד עשר סמוך לעשירי, שנאמר: מערב עד ערב תשבתו שבתכם. Begin to fast from the evening of the ninth; and at the end, remain in your affliction a little into the night of the eleventh. The adding on and the extending are only with regard to fasting. Why? Because he reads the verse that way: \"the ninth in the evening\" attaches to the last phrase — ועניתם את נפשתיכם , afflict yourselves, i.e. eating and drinking — not to שבת שבתון , the resting from work. The yesod — according to the Rambam there is no tosefet Shabbat There is a halachah in Shulchan Aruch called תוספת שבת — there is even a sefer called Tosefet Shabbat , by a great commentator on Hilchot Shabbat. According to the Rambam there is absolutely no such concept , not on Yom Kippur and not on any Shabbat or yom tov of the year. The only thing the Gemara derived is adding on to Yom Kippur, for eating and drinking . Work it through practically. Say the addition is two minutes. Sunset this Sunday night is roughly 7:03–7:04 (the fast in our calendars comes in at 6:49 ). Then: From the Torah, a positive mitzvah, I must stop eating by about 7:02 . But I may do melachah at 7:03 — I could light a match and light the candles. And on every Shabbat of the year , I may not work from sunset, but a minute before sunset I may , if I know exactly when sunset is — because there is no tosefet Shabbat . Other Rishonim held otherwise: when the Gemara derives the addition from שבת שבתון הוא לכם ועניתם... בתשעה לחדש בערב , \"the ninth\" goes on both — on the affliction and on the resting; and because it says שבת שבתון , a double Shabbat, it applies to every Yom Kippur and every Shabbat . That is how the Rishonim rule and how the Shulchan Aruch rules — there is a whole discussion there about tosefet , adding on to Shabbat. So the Shulchan Aruch does not rule like the Rambam: צריך להוסיף מחול על הקודש, ותוספת זו אין לה שיעור — and likewise in Hilchot Shabbat. How long is the addition? There is no shiur — the Shulchan Aruch says so explicitly. The poskim give a minimum of 2 minutes ; the practice ranges from 2 to 15 minutes, and in Israel (Jerusalem) even 40 minutes, for a different reason. Our calendars in London use 15 minutes — hence the fast coming in at 6:49 with sunset around 7:04. Everything we do nowadays — bringing Shabbat in early, taking it out late — takes the strictest calculations of nightfall (there are many ways to calculate it) and adds minutes on top , every single Motzaei Shabbat, because of tosefet Shabbat . According to the Rambam, no such concept exists. A participant asked: surely the addition would apply rabbinically to the other afflictions too — shoes, washing — since the word appears five times? — The Rabbi: on a rabbinic level, of course it would; you should rest the extra minutes from shoes and everything else as well. The Rambam's point, and the contrast being drawn here, is with melachah , which on the other view applies everywhere, all year round. Halachah 7 — The Women Who Ate Until Dark נשים שאוכלות ושותות עד שחשכה, והן אינן יודעות שתוספת מחול על הקודש מצות עשה — אין מחין בידן, שלא יבואו לעשות בזדון. שהרי אי אפשר שיהא שוטר בבית כל אחד ואחד להזהיר נשיו. והנח להן שיהו שוגגין ולא יהיו מזידין. וכן כל כיוצא בזה. Women who eat and drink until dark , not knowing that adding on from the mundane to the holy is a positive mitzvah — we do not object to them , lest they come to do it intentionally. Because you cannot post a policeman in every single house to warn a man's wives. (Remember the setting: a man with several wives, not all living in one house; you are keeping the fast in one house, and there are wives in others. There is no WhatsApp, no fixed printed calendar times — they look at the sky and continue eating.) Leave them: better they act inadvertently than deliberately. And so with anything similar. Problem one — the Gemara in Beitzah The Gemara in Beitzah states very clearly that the principle better they transgress inadvertently than deliberately does not apply to biblical matters . But this is biblical — we just derived the mitzvah of adding on from a verse! The Maggid Mishneh explains: yes, it is biblical — but when the Gemara says \"biblical\" it means explicitly written in the Torah . Do not eat on Yom Kippur is explicit, so you must tell them, even if they will not listen. Here it is a din derived — from the Torah, but not explicit — and in such a case we say better they violate it unintentionally than intentionally. The Rema (Orach Chaim 608) writes exactly this: ודוקא שאינו מפורש בתורה, אבל אם מפורש בתורה — מוחין בידם , even though it is d'oraita either way. Problem two — the bigger issue: what does עד שתחשך mean? The plain reading is until nightfall . Which means these women are eating and drinking right through sunset! Take Sunday night: sunset around 7:04, so with a two-minute minimum they should stop at 7:02. If they carry on for, say, 13 minutes past sunset — they are in a ספק דאורייתא : if after sunset is already nightfall, then it is already Yom Kippur and they are eating on Yom Kippur . Missing the mitzvah of tosefet is one thing; this is quite another. What is going on? The Mishnah Berurah answers that עד שתחשך here means until sunset , not nightfall. The Rambam clearly held it means until nightfall. Why could he? Because the Rambam holds that ספק דאורייתא לחומרא is only a din derabbanan. The Rambam's position on safek d'oraita This is a major machloket Rishonim — it originates, bizarrely enough, in the whole discussion of mamzer . Is the rule that a doubt in a biblical prohibition must be treated strictly a Torah requirement , or a rabbinic one? The Rambam held — unlike others — that it is only miderabbanan . From the Torah itself, a doubt about a biblical prohibition may be treated leniently . And therefore: when those women eat and drink after sunset but before nightfall, I do not have to object . They are missing the mitzvah of adding on, granted; and potentially it is already night, potentially already Yom Kippur — but bein hashmashot is ספק יום ספק לילה , a genuine doubt, and a doubt in a biblical matter may biblically be treated leniently; only rabbinically must we be strict. Fascinating. Summary The Rambam titles this section הלכות שביתת עשור — not Hilchot Yom Kippur — because the kaparah of the day belongs to Hilchot Teshuvah; here he treats the four mitzvot of resting and afflicting. Working on Yom Kippur forfeits a positive mitzvah and violates a negative carrying karet (a fixed chatat for shogeg), and in every other respect — biblical non-melachot, rabbinic prohibitions, muktzeh, forbidden speech — Yom Kippur is identical to Shabbat , the sole difference being sekilah versus karet. The Or Sameach explains the Rambam's switch to \"the tenth\" in that very clause: violate the day deliberately and it is no longer Yom Kippur for you at all — which is perhaps why people instinctively guard Yom Kippur even while (wrongly) treating Shabbat lightly, though driving on Shabbat is the graver offence. The one halachic leniency — kinuv yerek , cracking nuts and pomegranates from Minchah onwards מפני עגמת נפש — was already abandoned by custom, and its permissibility turns on why removing withered leaves is not borer (Rav Kappach: it is not a mixture at all, being clearly identifiable). The affliction is defined by the oral tradition as fasting — דבר שהנפש תלויה בו — carrying karet; the other four innuyim carry only makkat mardut , and whether the Rambam holds them biblical or rabbinic is a live tension (his commentary to the Mishnah and Sefer HaMitzvot 196 point firmly to rabbinic, against the words מפי השמועה ). The afflictions apply night and day , and tosefet is derived from בתשעה לחדש בערב — which the Rambam attaches only to the affliction , yielding the striking yesod that according to him there is no such thing as tosefet Shabbat , against the Shulchan Aruch. And finally, the women eating until dark are not to be rebuked — better shogeg than meizid for a derived (not explicit) biblical law, and, for the Rambam, because ספק דאורייתא לחומרא is itself only rabbinic . Coming Up Chapters 2 and 3 were not reached — learn them over Yom Kippur , inyana d'yoma : Chapter 2 — how much eating and drinking one is liable for, and the laws of the ill . Chapter 3 — the other restrictions: washing, anointing, shoes and relations , and one who has an emission on Yom Kippur night going to the mikveh (a fascinating discussion nowadays). Next week: a chapter on Sukkot from the Rambam — to be confirmed on the group whether the Thursday shiur runs. Then back to Hilchot Shabbat , siman 320 onwards. B'Ezrat Hashem — גמר חתימה טובה","oZXiQRakuAU":"Source: The Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, continued: Gan Eden and Gehinom in depth Shiur: Part 4 of the series on the Rambam's Introduction to Perek Helek, given by Dayan Daniel Kada on Zoom, Tuesday 21 October 2025 (Rosh Chodesh Cheshvan, after the festivals; the week of Bereishit) These notes are based on the Sofer.ai transcript of the recording. Recap Five views of the ultimate reward and punishment: Gan Eden/Gehinom , Mashiach's days , resurrection , this world , all combined . All physical. The real thing is Olam HaBa. The parable: sweets, then money, then clothes, then fame, until we learn because learning is right . The ideal is service without ulterior motive. Three readings of aggadah: literal : \"sad, wrong, foolish\"; \"the Sages didn't understand\" : \" very angry \"; deep messages masked in simple words for the masses : the Rambam's own. Olam HaBa : עין לא ראתה : indescribable, as sight is to the blind or heat to a fish. \" He himself doesn't fully understand it. \" Its essence: נהנים מזיו השכינה , relationship with Hashem . Don't serve for Olam HaBa , though it is the ultimate. (\"I don't like the words reward and punishment ; for simplicity, we'll use them.\") This-worldly blessings and curses (Ki Tavo, Bechukotai, Shema) are consequences, not rewards : בדרך שאדם רוצה לילך מוליכין אותו ; המקיים את התורה מעוני סופו לקיימה מעושר . Hashem removes distractions , or allows them. Tonight: Gan Eden and Gehinom again, \"because I picked up some new points over the weeks without shiurim, and tried as much as possible to make order of this.\" Part 1: Gan Eden וגן עדן הוא מקום דשן ושמן מבחר הארץ… יגלה אותו השם לבני אדם לעתיד לבוא, ויורה אותם הדרך ללכת אליו. \"Think of a beautiful oasis .\" Streams and trees, perhaps wondrous plants and medicines beyond ours: \" really utopia .\" Hashem must show the way , since, as we read last Shabbat in Bereishit , He banished Adam and Chavah and guarded the entrance. ונהר יצא מעדן להשקות את הגן ומשם יפרד והיה לארבעה ראשים. Three views of Gan Eden Rav Shilat points to Berachot 28b : Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai , dying, wept: יש לפני שני דרכים, אחת של גן עדן ואחת של גיהנם, ואיני יודע באיזו מוליכים אותי, ולא אבכה? Rav Saadia Gaon (Monday night, \"it just worked out\") The Ramban The Rambam Gan Eden is… Synonymous with Olam HaBa A spiritual place: a waiting room for souls until resurrection, when body and soul are reunited in a new world after the 6,000 years A physical place on earth , separate from Olam HaBa Physical? Yes , since his Olam HaBa is physical No Yes Accepted? \" No one really took it on \" The mainstream (Rambam and Ramban): Gan Eden and Gehinom are distinct from Olam HaBa , but the Sages use them as shorthand for it , since they're what people can relate to. Gan Eden is in the Torah; Gehinom is from גיא בן הנם , the valley where people were burned as sacrifices. So Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai's \"two paths\" means Olam HaBa or karet . Then why does the Rambam insist on a physical Gan Eden? Because the Torah describes one : a garden, rivers, plants. \" It's wrong to claim the Rambam held all of Bereishit to be one big metaphor. \" (The snake is debated.) So he wouldn't strip away the simple meaning entirely. Where is it, and has it been found? \" Looking at it again , I don't think the Rambam necessarily says it's here now .\" יגלה may mean Hashem will bring it back . Perhaps when humanity was banished, the place itself was withdrawn from the earth, to be re-exposed when its time comes. One opinion in Eruvin : only the entrance to Gan Eden is on earth. \" Both readings are possible. \" When? The Ramban ( Sha'ar HaGemul ): during Mashiach's days or the resurrection , \"the most logical way to understand it,\" and for the Rambam the two come together to some extent. Not now, and not Olam HaBa , which is souls and Hashem alone , with no earth left . Why have it at all? \"The only explanation I have: everything must revert to its original state before this physical universe ends.\" האדם, not אדם. \"Once you notice it you can't forget it: Bereishit almost never says Adam , always ha'adam , humankind .\" Not that there was no person called Adam: \" the one time it says Adam is when he dies .\" But humanity was banished from Eden, so humanity must return . Part 2: Gehinom ואמנם גיהנם הוא שם לצער ולעונש שישיג לרשעים, ולא פירשו בתלמוד תואר זה העונש. Some say Proof The sun draws near and burns them הנה היום בא בער כתנור (\"exactly the verse Rav Saadia quoted last night\") A strange heat arises inside their bodies , a fever רוחכם אש תאכלכם Full stop. The Sages use Gehinom as shorthand for karet , yet for the Rambam there's still something physical : heat , fittingly for a name from the valley of fire . \"They are judged according to their sins\" The Mishneh Torah (Hilchot Teshuvah): וכן כל הרשעים שעונותיהן מרובים, דנין אותן כפי חטאיהם, ויש להן חלק לעולם הבא. A complete rasha has no portion : karet . But this is a sinner who does have a portion. What does \"judged according to their sins\" mean? Reading 1. Their place (Class: \"the stadium analogy.\") A seat further back : a less intense bliss. \" That was always my understanding, and I believe the main one. \" 2. Gehinom Sins must be purged , and punishment purges. It fits twelve months : a karet-bound rasha needs no Gehinom, he's simply cut off \"Aren't we reading the colloquial Gehinom into the text?\" \" I hear that , which is why I think the first reading is simpler. But then what's Gehinom for ? The complete rasha is cut off; the partial one sits further back. Why burn him? I'm asking myself. I don't know. \" Daniel: the wicked rise too ורבים מישני אדמת עפר יקיצו, אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם (Daniel 12:2) The Rambam's Letter on Resurrection calls this the clearest source for resurrection. \"Some to everlasting life, some to shame.\" So, answering Maurice's question from last time, the wicked will be resurrected too, which suggests (the Rabbi's \"own conjecture\") that Gan Eden and Gehinom begin at the resurrection , on earth. Two kinds of resha'im : Fate The ultimate rasha , אין להם חלק (very few) Karet : they die, the soul is cut off. They don't rise חוטאי ישראל : sinners who are still Israel , with a portion They rise, and are purged in Gehinom , then continue The Ramban's reading Even those with karet pass through Gehinom , because otherwise how do you distinguish one who ate chametz on Pesach or on Yom Kippur once from one who did it for 120 years ? (\" Chametz on Yom Kippur is karet too!\") So Gehinom's intensity matches the count. The Rabbi: \"I find that difficult. The Rambam would answer: even the cut-off have some consciousness that they're cut off. A hundred karets = a deeper awareness of what's lost. \" A class objection: why need levels? We don't execute someone ten times for ten murders. Death is the ultimate. \" I don't agree with the proof : every court execution comes with karet as well. But the idea, that karet is simply the ultimate, I hear very strongly. \" \"It feels like a lot is intentionally left out.\" \" Correct. People like to know exactly. But these aren't the ikkarim . \" Not principles of faith Gan Eden and Gehinom are not ikkarei emunah . Contrast what comes next: techiyat hametim is יסוד מיסודות משה רבנו , deny it and you have no portion ; Mashiach , likewise. Gan Eden appears in the Mishneh Torah only in the Sheva Berachot ( כשמחך יצירך בגן עדן מקדם ); Gehinom only once. \" Whatever they are, don't spend too long on them. In the grand scheme, they're very minor.\" Questions Q: In the Rambam's view, what happens to a sinner with a portion between death and resurrection? \" He begins his Olam HaBa , maybe further back, but with an impediment : he can't fully experience it until the purging , which comes at the resurrection.\" Why make him wait? \" I don't know. \" ילכו מחיל אל חיל (Tehillim 84, said at Minchah): the Gemara: צדיקים אין להם מנוחה לא בעולם הזה ולא בעולם הבא : there's growth in Olam HaBa . The Hashkavah . \"That's why I've never liked the Spanish and Portuguese Hashkavah : מנוחה נכונה בישיבה עליונה , true rest in the academy on high . What rest? The righteous have no rest! \" The Arizal used a simpler text ( טוב שם משמן טוב… שם תהא מנוחתו ), though for a different reason: he disliked the hyperbolic terms. Q: Doesn't Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai's \"two paths\" fit the Ramban well, Gan Eden and Gehinom right after death? \"Only on Rav Saadia's view. Otherwise it's proof the Sages used Gan Eden and Gehinom as shorthand for Olam HaBa : what else can you tell people about the ultimate punishment? I know the story of Gan Eden; I know the fires. \" Richard: those with karet, do they enter Gehinom? \" I have a proof: they do not. They don't rise for Gehinom. Only those with a portion do. \" \"כל ישראל יש להם חלק לעולם הבא\" \"People love quoting it. That's half the Mishnah. In the prayer book it stops there; the source continues: ואלו שאין להם חלק , and a whole list.\" The Timeline The Rambam Rav Saadia The Ramban At death The complete rasha : karet . Everyone else begins Olam HaBa , by their spiritual level (Unexplained) A spiritual waiting room : Gan Eden or Gehinom In the future, on earth Mashiach and resurrection (order unstated). The righteous rise and enjoy Gan Eden ; sinners with a portion rise and pass through Gehinom 's physical heat. The cut-off stay cut off A new world = Olam HaBa = Gan Eden/Gehinom, body and soul , with heat or light Resurrection of body and soul into Olam HaBa , ending Gan Eden and Gehinom Finally That period ends, the world ends , and only souls exist eternally with Hashem Summary Gan Eden : three views : Rav Saadia (= Olam HaBa), the Ramban (spiritual waiting room), the Rambam ( a physical place on earth , revealed or returned in the future, during Mashiach/resurrection , because humanity, ha'adam , must return to Eden ). The Sages use Gan Eden and Gehinom as shorthand for Olam HaBa and karet, but the Rambam won't strip away the Torah's physical Eden. Gehinom : burning , from the sun or within. \" Judged according to their sins \": a further seat , or Gehinom's purging . Daniel 12:2 : the wicked rise too: sinners with a portion for Gehinom; the cut-off never rise . (The Ramban: Gehinom for karet too, by count; the Rabbi prefers degrees of awareness .) Neither is a principle of faith. Resurrection and Mashiach are . Coming Up Next week: techiyat hametim and Mashiach , then a complete overview of everything .","ok7Ef5mHUSw":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Sefer: HaMaspik L'Ovdei Hashem (Kifayat al-Abidin) — Rabbi Avraham ben HaRambam Chapter: Perek 8 (ביטחון — Bitachon and Reliance) — Part 6 This Week: The Two Questions on Bitachon and Their Answers · Compelled vs. Voluntary Bitachon · Everything Traces Back to Hashem (the Suckling Analogy) · Which Jobs Make Bitachon Easier or Harder (Schedule: this Thursday shiur replaces Hilchot Shabbat to finish the bitachon chapter before the summer break — HaMaspik continues Monday, finishing the chapter the following Monday. Next Thursday: the Rambam's understanding of Tisha B'Av.) Recap Category 1 (serious danger): Tehillim, trust, prayer, every natural means. If a person truly lives with Hakadosh Baruch Hu, no danger can harm him (the snakes in Chukat after the teshuvah) — but never put yourself in danger: you may lack the merit, and even if saved, it deducts from your merits. In mortal spiritual danger, pray — and sometimes one must give up his life. Category 2 (minor harms): do not employ bitachon (prayer is fine) unless one is of the greatest people — though Rabbeinu Yonah in Mishlei disagrees (a machloket Rishonim). In spiritual matters even small things can become very big, so bitachon is appropriate even for small spiritual dangers. Category 3 begun (achieving essential matters — basic bread, clothes, shelter): employ bitachon, pray, and request the basics, like Yaakov Avinu. The five categories: (1) avoiding extremely harmful situations, (2) avoiding less harmful situations, (3) achieving the essential, (4) achieving the important-but-non-essential, (5) the non-important and non-essential. Tonight: the two questions Rabbi Avraham raises in the middle of Category 3 — and his answers. QUESTION 1 — If Hashem Feeds Even the Animals, Why Bitachon? The Question Many verses state Hashem grants basic parnasah even to animals — זן מקרני ראמים עד ביצי כינים (from the horns of wild oxen to the eggs of lice). If Hashem sustains all creatures anyway, what practical need is there for humans to have bitachon? The Answer — Free Will Changes Everything אף על פי שפרנסת הבריות מובטחת... הנה לפעמים גורם העוון לידי הכרתת הפרנסה Even though parnasah is guaranteed to creatures by nature — sin can cause a person's parnasah to be cut off. The default position is indeed that Hashem feeds everyone. But humans have bechirah — free will. Our obedience or disobedience changes the parnasah in three directions: Direction 1 — Aveirot Can Remove Parnasah Entirely The Torah's curses (Bechukotai etc.) explicitly warn of famine when we don't do Hashem's will Direction 2 — Or Parnasah Comes, But Only Through Great Toil Adam after the sin: בזעת אפך תאכל לחם... וקוץ ודרדר תצמיח לך — you'll have bread, but by the sweat of your brow, with thorns and thistles Kayin : כי תעבוד את האדמה לא תוסף תת כוחה לך — the ground will no longer yield its strength to you אוכלי לחם העצבים — you'll eat \"the bread of sadness\": parnasah delivered with difficulty and turmoil King Asa of Yehudah : he turned to Ben-Hadad, king of Aram, for help against Baasha king of Israel — and the navi rebuked him: בהישענך על מלך ארם ולא על ה' אלהיך — because you leaned on the king of Aram and not on Hashem your God. When you relied on Hashem, He delivered the vast armies of the Kushim and Luvim into your hand; כי ה' עיניו משוטטות בכל הארץ להתחזק עם לבבם שלם אליו — Hashem's eyes rove the whole earth to strengthen those whose heart is complete with Him. נסכלת על זאת — כי מעתה יש עמך מלחמות : you acted foolishly; from now on you will have wars. (Not total removal — but everything becomes a fight.) Direction 3 — Obedience Brings Broad, Easy Parnasah וכנגד זה מביאה צייתות לסדר השם הרחבת הפרנסה וריבויה Parshat Ekev : והיה עקב תשמעון... וברך פרי בטנך ופרי אדמתך דגנך ותירושך ויצהרך — Hashem blesses land, grain, wine, oil, cattle The botei'ach is by definition obedient (bitachon presupposes and is obedience) — so Hashem rewards his bitachon with easy parnasah : כן יתן לידידו שנא — He gives to His beloved in their sleep (as explained a few weeks ago — you make your parnasah and sleep easily) ודורשי ה' לא יחסרו כל טוב — those who seek Hashem lack no good The Contrast Verses (from Birkat Hamazon and Shabbat mornings) כפירים רשו ורעבו, ודורשי ה' לא יחסרו כל טוב What does \"young lions went hungry\" mean? Metzudat David : כפירים = the wealthy and the great — a metaphor. The wealthy went hungry because they forgot Hashem and their bitachon ; while the seekers of Hashem lack nothing. Chana : שבעים בלחם נשכרו — those who were sated with bread ended up hiring themselves out (they lost their trust) Doeg (who maligned David to Shaul — initially a righteous talmid chacham): גם אל יתצך לנצח... וישחקו עליו — God will tear him down forever, and the righteous will see and laugh: הנה הגבר לא ישים אלהים מעוזו, ויבטח ברוב עשרו — behold the man who did not make God his stronghold, but trusted in his great wealth The Explicit Mishnah (Kiddushin 82) — Which the Avi Ezer Surprisingly Doesn't Bring רבי שמעון בן אלעזר אומר: ראית מימיך חיה ועוף שיש להם אומנות? והן מתפרנסין שלא בצער. והלא לא נבראו אלא לשמשני, ואני נבראתי לשמש את קוני — אינו דין שאתפרנס שלא בצער? אלא שהרעותי את מעשי וקיפחתי את פרנסתי Have you ever seen a beast or bird with a trade? Yet they are sustained without anguish. They were created to serve me, and I was created to serve my Maker — surely I should be sustained without anguish! But I ruined my deeds — and forfeited my parnasah. The kal vachomer is exactly Rabbi Avraham's answer : animals have no free will, so their sustenance flows automatically; humans chose badly, and so parnasah became hard. Summary of Question 1 Hashem gives parnasah to all — but because of our free will, obedience improves and broadens our parnasah, while disobedience removes it or delivers it only through turmoil and hard work. That is the practical need for bitachon. QUESTION 2 — Is Bitachon Natural to Everyone, or Rare and Praiseworthy? The Question Verses imply trust in Hashem is natural and universal — most classically (said before eating bread, in Ashrei): עיני כל אליך ישברו ואתה נותן להם את אכלם בעתו — the eyes of ALL look hopefully to You But other verses praise the one who trusts — אשרי אדם בוטח בך — and condemn the one who doesn't — הנה הגבר לא ישים אלהים מעוזו . If everyone naturally has bitachon, why is it praised or its absence cursed? The verses seem to contradict. Rabbi Avraham gives two answers. ANSWER 1 — \"Kol\" Means Majority; Bitachon Can Be Compelled or Voluntary Two introductions: כל כוונתו רוב — \"all\" in Tanakh often means the majority הביטחון יכול להיות הכרחי או רצוני — bitachon can be compelled or voluntary (and voluntary bitachon can itself be general or specific — below) Compelled Bitachon — \"No Atheists in a Foxhole\" When life is good — wealth, health, everything — a person forgets Hashem. (The Mesillat Yesharim discusses this: poverty is a great test of trust — but the Ramchal suggests the test of wealth is the greater one.) Then the person loses the wealth or the health — and now he turns to Hashem: בעלי עושר וכבוד... אם יתרוששו ויאבדו את עושרם וכבודם ישובו לביטחון When the wealthy and honored lose their wealth and status — they return to bitachon. Not from free good will, but compelled by circumstances — their transient walking-stick (עושר, כבוד, בריאות) was knocked away. Compelled Bitachon Even Among Idolaters ודבר זה מצוי תדיר אפילו אצל עובדי עבודה זרה The Rambam's principle (opening of Hilchot Avodah Zarah): idolatry didn't begin with people believing idols have intrinsic power — it began with intermediaries : people who couldn't face approaching Hashem directly, so they put something in between. (This is why Moshe broke the Luchot on 17 Tammuz — and why even today religious people cling to amulets, red strings, blue charms: an avoidance of direct communication with Hashem, placing trust in an intermediary. That, essentially, was the golden calf.) Most ovdei avodah zarah serve their idols in order to come closer to the true Savior : כי ביטחונם בפסיליהם... הוא אמצעי בינם ובין השם המושיע . Proof 1 — the sailors of Yonah: In the storm, ויזעקו איש אל אלהיו — each cried to his own god. But when Yonah told them עברי אנכי ואת ה' אלהי השמים אני ירא — וייראו האנשים יראה גדולה — they feared Hashem , and prayed to Him: אנה ה' אל נא נאבדה בנפש האיש הזה . A moment ago they were praying to idols — and they trace right back to Hakadosh Baruch Hu. Proof 2 — Mesha, King of Moav: Under Israel's oppression he offered his firstborn son to his god — and there was קצף גדול על ישראל (Israel was blamed for having driven him to it). The horror doesn't contradict the fact that כוונתו היתה לשם... בצרתו — in his distress, his intent traced toward Heaven. Hence Yirmiyahu : מי לא יראך מלך הגוים כי לך יאתה — who does not fear You, King of the nations — even idolaters ultimately trace back to You. And David : מבטח כל קצוי ארץ וים רחוקים — You are the trust of all the ends of the earth. Voluntary Bitachon — The Choice of Yichidei Segulah והביטחון הרצוני הוא ביטחונם של יחידי סגולה מיחדים את השם Voluntary bitachon is chosen — not squeezed out by desperation: דבקים בתורה, מאמינים באמת, וכל עניינם שהכל מכל מהשם יתברך — they place their reliance on Hashem even while they have wealth, health, strength, and status. Bitachon and Yichud Hashem: note the phrase מיחדים את השם — true bitachon is an expression of the unity of God. One who truly lives knowing that nothing can harm or help him apart from Hashem is living שמע ישראל ה' אלהינו ה' אחד . Bitachon strengthens yichud Hashem, and yichud Hashem strengthens bitachon. Two Levels Within Voluntary Bitachon Level Description הביטחון הכללי — general The bitachon of ordinary people \"in the thoughts of their hearts\": we all say and feel we trust Hashem — though it may not manifest in our day-to-day activities. (Even Sancheriv, despite his heresy, said בלא ה' עליתי על המקום הזה — he recognized his campaign came from Hashem.) הביטחון הפרטי — specific/rare The bitachon of the special shomrei Torah who live it every moment : in every business dealing, every negotiation, their trust is in Hashem and not in their efforts — בטח אל ה' בכל לבך ואל בינתך אל תשען... בכל דרכיך דעהו — training every thought toward Hashem (The avodah: never be compelled into bitachon — choose it voluntarily; and then work to raise the general into the specific, so it manifests in everything we do.) The Resolution (Answer 1) הפסוקים שבהם נאמר כי כל אחד בוטח בהשם — כוונתם לרוב בני אדם, והביטחון מהסוג המוכרח; והפסוקים האמורים על נדירותו של הביטחון — כגון אשרי אדם בוטח בך — כוונתם לביטחון הרצוני המובחר \"עיני כל אליך ישברו\" = the majority of people do, at the end of the day, turn to Hashem — mostly compelled (when the going gets hard and there's no alternative) \"אשרי אדם בוטח בך\" = praise for the one who voluntarily chose bitachon — that is the rare and praiseworthy kind ANSWER 2 — Kol Really Means ALL: Everyone Trusts in Something, and Everything Traces Back to Hashem (Rabbi Avraham himself loves this answer — he closes it with \"this is one of the beautiful and correct interpretations.\") כי כל הגורמים חוזרים אליו יתעלה... נמצא הבוטח באותו גורם סומך בטחונו עליו יתעלה — בין אם הבין זאת... או שלא הבין Everyone trusts in someone or something — and that someone or something traces back to Hashem . So in effect, even without realizing it, everyone indirectly trusts in Hashem. The Three Examples You trust in... But... Verse Your wealth Hashem produced it — and guards it ה' מוריש ומעשיר, משפיל אף מרומם ; and for the oleh laregel: ולא יחמוד איש את ארצך (Hashem guards your property while you're away) Your strength / status Hashem gave it וזכרת את ה' אלהיך כי הוא הנותן לך כח לעשות חיל Another person (your employer of thirty years) Hashem created him, gives him life, and gives him his ability כי יוצר הכל הוא ; אשר בידו נפש כל חי ; והעושר והכבוד מלפניך הבוטח בגורמים האלו... הרי הוא סומך בטחונו עליו יתעלה — בין אם הבין... בין לא ידעו וכפר בו Whether you understand it, don't understand it, or even deny it — the theoretical truth is you are trusting in Hashem. The Suckling Analogy (דומה לנער קטן) One who doesn't grasp this is like a baby at his mother's breast : The infant — all he knows is his mother's milk; as far as he's concerned, his mother is the source of everything The child (~5) — matures a little: mother provides the food, but from father's money The teenager — father's money comes from his employer The mature adult — the employer, and everything, traces back to Hashem : וכן הלאה עד שיבין ויתפוס שהכל מאת השם יתברך David HaMelech said it in his maturity about his own infancy: כי אתה גוחי מבטן, מבטיחי על שדי אמי; עליך השלכתי מרחם, מבטן אמי אלי אתה You drew me from the womb; You were my security ON MY MOTHER'S BREASTS — upon You I was cast from the womb. As a suckling he couldn't picture it; grown, he recognized the truth: it was Hashem all along. Extending It to the Animals — The Verses Finally Explained Now those puzzling pesukim make sense. When do ravens or lions \"call to Hashem\"? נותן לבהמה לחמה, לבני עורב אשר יקראו — He gives the beast its bread, the young ravens that call הכפירים שואגים לטרף — ולבקש מאל אכלם — the lions roar for prey, seeking their food from God ידע שור קונהו וחמור אבוס בעליו — the ox knows its owner, the donkey its master's trough The animal relies on whoever tends and feeds it (the sheep trusts the farmer) — but the one it relies on depends on Hashem : הגורם שבו בוטחים הם תלוי בך, השם יתברך — והם לא יודעים זאת . So כולם אליך ישברון is literal: ALL — humans and animals — trust in Hashem, most without knowing it. הבן את הדבר והשיגהו בשכלך, כי הוא מהפירושים היפים והנכונים — \"Understand this and grasp it with your mind, for it is one of the beautiful and correct interpretations.\" The Resolution (Answer 2) \"עיני כל אליך ישברו\" = literally everyone (and everything): all trust something , and that something traces back to Hashem — indirect, unknowing trust The praise-verses = those who trust Hashem directly and knowingly — cutting out the intermediary and placing trust straight from themselves to Hakadosh Baruch Hu CLOSING SECTION — Which Jobs Make Bitachon Easier or Harder One final teaching on Category 3. Whenever working to provide the basics, one is obligated in complete bitachon: חובה מוטלת על הבוטח בהשם יתברך שבהשגת הצרכים האלו... ביטחון שלם בהשם — trust in Hashem and not in the job you're doing to get these things. But the job itself affects how easy bitachon is: Three Kinds of Livelihood Type Examples Bitachon High-chance occupations דייג בים או צייד בשדה — fisherman, hunter; raising animals, farming, seafaring Strengthens/eases bitachon — the hashgacha is out in the open; success is visibly chance-dependent. Yaakov (posing as Esav, asked how he returned so fast): כי הקרה ה' אלהיך לפני — \"Hashem made it happen before me\" Human-dependent occupations מי שתלוי פרנסתו במלך — one whose livelihood hangs on a king (or a single patron/employer) Weakens/hardens bitachon — everything depends on finding favor in one person's eyes. Hence David: אשרי הגבר אשר שם ה' מבטחו ולא פנה אל רהבים — happy is he who trusts Hashem and did not turn to the powerful In between ההתעסקות במסחר — general commerce, buying and selling The middle path between the two Contemporary Application (Rabbi Kada) Modern \"hunters\" : careers of near-total chance — an actor, a footballer — very hard to make it; those paths demand enormous bitachon The other extreme : a personal assistant to an extremely wealthy man — everything guaranteed through one human — bitachon is much harder Self-employed vs. employed : the self-employed person, whose finances shift day to day with clients and luck, generally needs (and more easily develops) more bitachon than the salaried employee with a fixed, predictable income The Crucial Caveats — Trends, Not Rules אין כוונתי לומר שכל המתפרנס באמצעות בני אדם או הון אי אפשר שיהיה לו ביטחון... ובכל זאת יש לפעמים ביטחון מלא גם במצב כזה Working for a king does not preclude bitachon — it's just not easy ; reaching it there is a very high level: Ovadiah rose to the highest bitachon while serving in the court of Achav David HaMelech , with all his wealth, still said: כי ממך הכל ומידך נתנו לך — \"everything is from You, and from Your hand we have given You\"; העושר והכבוד מלפניך And Iyov : אם שמתי זהב כסלי ולכתם אמרתי מבטחי — \"if I made gold my confidence...\" — not to trust one's own fortune while possessing it is a great level Conversely, not every hunter or fisherman reaches the bitachon of Yaakov and Yitzchak — some leave it all to randomness and chance. The point: in the former it's easier, in the latter harder — be cognizant of which kind of livelihood you're in. End of Category Three. Summary Question 1 — Hashem Feeds Even the Animals; Why Bitachon? Because of free will : obedience broadens and eases parnasah (Ekev; כן יתן לידידו שנא); disobedience removes it (the curses; kefirim rashu; Chana; Doeg) or delivers it only through toil and war (Adam; Kayin; King Asa). The Mishnah in Kiddushin says it explicitly: אלא שהרעותי את מעשי וקיפחתי את פרנסתי . Question 2 — Natural or Praiseworthy? Two Answers Kol = majority; bitachon is compelled or voluntary. Most people reach bitachon compelled (no atheists in a foxhole — the ruined wealthy; even idolaters, whose service is an intermediary tracing back to Hashem: Yonah's sailors, Mesha of Moav). The praise-verses speak of the voluntary botei'ach — who chooses trust while he still has everything (and within the voluntary: the general feeling most of us have vs. the rare, specific bitachon lived out in every deal and every step — בכל דרכיך דעהו). Kol = literally all. Everyone (humans and animals) trusts in something — wealth, strength, an employer, an owner — and every one of those traces back to Hashem: indirect, unknowing trust (the suckling analogy: mother → father → employer → Hashem; כי אתה גוחי מבטן). The praise is for trusting Him directly . Jobs and Bitachon High-chance livelihoods (hunter, fisherman — today: self-employment, acting, sport) ease bitachon; human-dependent livelihoods (serving a king — today: the fixed salary, the single patron) harden it; commerce is in between. But the trend can always be broken: Ovadiah in Achav's court, David in his wealth. Coming Up Monday: Category 4 — bitachon for important-but-non-essential matters (nice clothes, comfort). Preview of the principle: for yourself , the essential standard is basic food, clothing, shelter — לא הרחבה ומותרות — but this stringency applies only to one without a wife and family; for those dependent on you , bitachon beyond the basics is appropriate. And Category 5 (luxuries — \"Hashem will make me a billionaire for my fancy cars\") — certainly inappropriate, just like the mosquito (unless the aim is genuinely l'shem Shamayim, e.g., to build cities of Torah). The following Monday: the different levels of bitachon in Hakadosh Baruch Hu — completing the chapter before the break. Next Thursday: the Rambam's understanding of Tisha B'Av. B'Ezrat Hashem","qEHV4GJetig":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 3 of 14 Topic: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 1 — Bishul (Cooking) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 6 November 2018 Schedule note: Four consecutive weekly shiurim from this week. Recap The first two shiurim covered the positive side of Shabbat — Zachor : preparing for Shabbat, candles, Kiddush, the meals and Havdala. From tonight we turn to Shamor , the prohibited melachot — starting with one of the biggest of all: Bishul . Part A — Where the Melachot Come From The Torah mostly says only לֹא תַעֲשֶׂה כָל מְלָאכָה — do no melacha . Only two specific acts are named: carrying and lighting a fire . But \"work\" means different things to different people. Chazal learnt the definition from the Mishkan : Hashem commanded its building and then — six days you shall work, but on Shabbat rest. Whatever activities built the Mishkan are the blueprint of forbidden melacha — the 39 melachot . Some are tied to processes no longer common today; others, like Bishul, are very practical. Bishul in the Mishkan: the lowest of the Mishkan's roof coverings was dyed; the dye was made by boiling a paste. So the essence of Bishul is changing a substance using fire — not only food: heating metal until red-hot and soft is also Bishul. Tonight focuses on the kitchen. Part B — The Basic Rules of Bishul Yad soledet bo — the critical temperature The Gemara defines the temperature of cooking as יָד סוֹלֶדֶת בּוֹ — hot enough that the hand recoils . The consensus of the poskim: 45°C . Food placed near a fire where it can never reach 45°C , however long it stays, is fine — even if it gets quite warm. Once it can reach 45°C or above, it is Bishul and forbidden. Aside on kashrut: washing-up water you can hold your hands in without gloves is usually below yad soledet bo — so mix-ups in the sink are rarely a kashrut problem. Toldot ha'esh — you don't need a flame A common misconception is that Bishul requires fire. Anything heated by fire — a derivative of fire ( toldot ha'esh ) — can also cook, as long as it is at least yad soledet bo: A pot of soup taken off the fire is still piping hot. Placing an egg against its side to cook is forbidden by the Torah — an explicit Mishnah. Putting a raw onion into that pot before serving — forbidden by the Torah. An oven switched off before Shabbat but still hot — food may not be put in to cook. The vessel heated directly on the fire is called a kli rishon (first vessel) and cooks just like the fire itself while above 45°C. Microwaves: no fire, but they cook. Leaving aside the electrical issues, poskim debate whether it is a biblical or rabbinic prohibition — either way, we do not use a microwave on Shabbat. Cooking in the sun — chamah and toldot chamah The Rabbi asked the room: may you crack an egg into a frying pan and leave it in the summer sun on Shabbat? Yes — cooking in the sun is permitted. Bishul means cooking with fire; whatever physics says, the sun is not \"fire\" in the halachic sense. The paradox: cooking in something heated by the sun ( toldot chamah ) — e.g. cracking an egg on a hot car, or on a frying pan that has already been baking in the sun — is forbidden rabbinically , because of mar'it ayin : an onlooker would assume the hot pan came off the fire. Take a cold pan out of the kitchen, crack the egg in the sun — permitted ; the sun is doing the cooking. A pan already hot from the sun — forbidden ; the hot pan is doing the cooking. Chacham Ovadia Yosef : using a magnifying glass to concentrate the sun's rays on water is permitted — it is still the sun's rays doing the cooking, even well above 45°C. Q: Is melting a solid into a liquid (or the reverse) a problem? — Not a Bishul problem. The solar water heater ( dud shemesh ) Very common in Israel. When you open the hot tap, cold water flows into the tank and is heated by the hot water already there — a toldot chamah , seemingly forbidden. Yet the halacha (Chacham Ovadia and other poskim) is that a purely manual dud shemesh may be used on Shabbat: It is gerama — an indirect result — of something only rabbinically forbidden. It is a pesik reisha one does not intend. If opening the tap triggers electric heating, it is forbidden. (The Rabbi's family used one in Israel on Shabbat.) Part C — Kli Rishon and Kli Sheni Pour soup from the pot ( kli rishon ) into a bowl and the bowl is a kli sheni — twice removed from the fire. The Gemara is explicit: כְּלִי שֵׁנִי אֵינוֹ מְבַשֵּׁל — a kli sheni does not cook , even above 45°C. The walls of the pot that stood on the fire are part of the cooking process; the bowl's walls never were. Why then do some people pour into a third vessel? Mechzei ke'mevashel — it looks like cooking to an onlooker (Tosafot's reading of the Gemara). The Mishnah lists some very tender foods that cook even in a kli sheni — e.g. salted herring . Since we no longer know exactly which foods are like this, a blanket stringency applies. Practice Kli sheni Ashkenazim Do not put raw food in a kli sheni (for both reasons); use a kli shlishi Sephardim Most foods may be put in a kli sheni; avoid only the known tender foods — salted fish such as herring, egg, and according to some lemon Making tea The urn is a kli rishon ; a cup filled from it is a kli sheni . Ashkenazim: pour from the urn into a cup, then into a second cup ( kli shlishi ), and only then add the tea bag or leaves. Sephardim (strict halacha — Chacham Ovadia): one may put a tea bag into the first cup (kli sheni). Never pour from the urn directly onto a tea bag or leaves already in the cup — that is iruy kli rishon , pouring from a kli rishon, which has a status close to the kli rishon itself. Since tea leaves are tender, it is praiseworthy to use a kli shlishi — or to pour the hot water from the first cup onto the tea bag in a second cup. Those who don't need not be objected to. Tea essence (already-processed concentrate) — fine even from the urn. Instant coffee, milk Instant coffee (e.g. Nescafé) — already processed, so you may pour directly from the urn onto the granules. Milk into a kli sheni — fine for Sephardim; it is pasteurised, so many Ashkenazim also permit it. Q: Is there a dyeing problem when milk colours the coffee? — No; there is no tzove'a with food and drink. Q: Must the cup be dry? — If the drops in it are water that was already boiled (e.g. your second coffee), certainly fine. If just rinsed with cold water, many say it is fine (only a few drops); some prefer to dry it. Davar gush — hot solids Some argue the kli sheni leniency applies only to liquids ; a hot solid (a boiled potato, a kugel) keeps its heat however many times it is moved, and could still cook spices or salt sprinkled on it. Hence many Ashkenazim avoid raw spices on hot kugel even on the third or fourth plate. Sephardim do not hold this stringency — a solid moved onto a plate is a kli sheni like a liquid. Part D — Ein Bishul Achar Bishul אֵין בִּשּׁוּל אַחַר בִּשּׁוּל — once something is fully cooked , it cannot be cooked further. Bishul (cooking) vs Afiyah (baking) Bishul — changing a substance with fire and water (a pot of soup). Afiyah — changing it with fire without water (baking challah, roasting). Is cooking something that was baked (or baking something that was cooked) a new melacha? The Shulchan Aruch brings both views; the main view is the second — it is permitted : once fully processed in one way, the other way is not a new Bishul. Chacham Ovadia notes it is good to be stringent where easy. Putting challah into a pot of soup off the fire — permitted for Sephardim. Taking cooked meat out of soup and putting it on a hotplate (dry heat) — permitted. Ashkenazim are stringent: a kli shlishi, since already-cooked food is even more tender. Croutons in soup The croutons are baked; soup is bishul — so for Sephardim, croutons may go into a bowl of soup (kli sheni). For Ashkenazim, if the soup was served with a ladle , the ladle is itself a vessel, so the bowl is already a kli shlishi — permitted for them too. Only when pouring directly from the pot would they need a further bowl. Turkish (black) coffee The grounds are roasted (dry heat), so strictly one may pour from the urn directly. In practice Chacham Ovadia advises against it — not every grain is fully roasted, so one may be cooking something for the first time. Preferably use a kli sheni. Barbecued meat; toast Barbecued meat (dry heat) may be put into a pot of soup that is off the fire. Toast: baked challah may be put directly on a (permitted) hotplate or on top of the urn — even if it darkens. It is already fully baked. (The status of hotplates comes next week.) Part E — Reheating, and Speeding Up Cooking Solids vs liquids Solids (e.g. a schnitzel), once fully cooked, may be reheated even after they have cooled — the main purpose of cooking a solid is to make it edible, and it remains so. Liquids , once cooled, may not be reheated to above yad soledet bo — the main purpose of heating a liquid is that it be hot, so once cold it is subject to Bishul again. So: soup taken off the hotplate to serve guests, once it has cooled below 45°C, may not go back on. Here the Ashkenazim are more lenient: as long as the liquid is still warm (even 20–25°C), they may return it. For Sephardim, once below yad soledet bo it may not be reheated. What counts as \"fully cooked\"? Food is fully cooked when it is edible as it is — \"if I wanted to eat it now, I would.\" A cholent that is edible but will get tastier overnight ( mitztamek ve'yafeh lo ) is already fully cooked. Bones: if the dish is cooked but the bones are not yet soft, returning it to the fire would finish cooking the bones. The poskim say it depends: if you eat the bones, it is forbidden; if you don't, it is permitted. Megis — speeding up cooking Accelerating cooking — e.g. turning up the flame under half-cooked food — is Bishul from the Torah . Practical applications: Lifting the lid: the cholent went on an hour before Shabbat; two hours in, you lift the lid and see it needs another half hour. You may not put the lid back — that concentrates the heat and speeds up cooking. Stirring food that is not fully cooked — forbidden, even after taking it off the fire, since stirring speeds up cooking. Stirring on the fire — forbidden even if fully cooked (it looks like cooking, and adds a qualitative change). Ladling is \"a mini-stir\": from food not fully cooked, don't even take out a portion. Some forbid ladling even fully cooked food while it is on the fire. Best practice: take the pot off the fire, serve, then return it (to a permitted hotplate or blech). Left for next week: eating in a home where these laws are not kept — may one eat the food? Dvar Torah — Parashat Toldot: The Cost of Not Communicating Rivka suffers in pregnancy and goes to seek counsel — not from her husband Yitzchak or her father-in-law Avraham, the greatest people of the age, but from Shem , son of Noach. Shem tells her she carries twins: one righteous, one wicked. Later, when Yitzchak plans to bless Esav, Rivka does not speak to him — she has Yaakov disguise himself instead. The consequences: Esav's hatred, Yaakov's flight for 22 years, and Rivka never seeing her beloved son again. The Netziv (Rav Naftali Tzvi Yehuda Berlin, 19th-century Lithuania) explains — \"if he hadn't said it, I wouldn't dare\" — that Yitzchak and Rivka's relationship differed from that of Avraham and Sarah or Yaakov and Rachel. From their first meeting — Rivka saw Yitzchak in prayer and fell from her camel — she was in awe of him, and never able to open up to him. So she went to Shem rather than to Yitzchak; and having done so, could not later tell him of the prophecy. Hence the whole drama of the blessings. The lesson for our own lives: the importance of communication and dialogue . Had they been able to talk, things would have turned out very differently. Summary Bishul = changing a substance with fire; the threshold is yad soledet bo, 45°C . A kli rishon (and anything heated by fire) cooks like fire itself — no flame needed. Sun : permitted; something heated by the sun : rabbinically forbidden. A manual dud shemesh is permitted. Kli sheni does not cook — Sephardim may use it for most foods (not herring, egg, lemon); Ashkenazim use a kli shlishi. Tea: never pour from the urn onto the tea bag; Sephardim may add it to the first cup, a second cup is preferable. Instant coffee: directly from the urn. Ein bishul achar bishul ; cooking after baking and vice versa permitted for Sephardim (croutons, toast, challah in soup). Cooled liquids may not be reheated (Sephardim: below 45°C); solids may. Don't stir , re-cover or ladle food that is not fully cooked; take pots off the fire to serve. Coming Up Shiur 4 — 13 November: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 2 She'hiyah — leaving food on the fire from before Shabbat Hazara — returning food to the fire on Shabbat Hatmana — insulating food, e.g. wrapping in a towel Hotplates and blechs B'Ezrat Hashem","s65SxyoMkTE":"Dayan Daniel Kada · Thursday Night Halacha · Shiur 1 of 7 Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 670 (and the opening of 671) Introduction: Why Learn Hilchot Hanukah Now The Thursday night course began in Elul with some of the laws of Sukkah. With roughly two months to go until Hanukah (which that year began on the night of 25 December), the Rabbi set out to cover the entire Hilchot Hanukah before the festival arrived. The laws of Hanukah in the Shulḥan Arukh run from Siman 670 (תר״ע) to Siman 684 (תרפ״ד) , fourteen chapters, some of them very short. Over six or seven weeks it is achievable to begin and conclude all of them, so that when Hanukah comes \"we will be able to appreciate the festival, a special time in our calendar, more.\" The roadmap: what you may and may not do on Hanukah; how to light the menorah and where to place it; the correct time to light; which fuels to use; moving the menorah; the blessings; moving home or being a guest over Hanukah; and the Hanukah candles on Friday afternoon and Shabbat. The Rabbi posted the Sefaria link in the Zoom chat for those following along. Part One: Siman 670 — What Is and Is Not Done on Hanukah The Text (Seif 1) בכ״ה בכסלו מתחילין שמונת ימי חנוכה, ואסורים בהספד ותענית, אבל מותרים בעשיית מלאכה, ונוהגות הנשים שלא לעשות מלאכה בעוד שהנרות דולקות, ויש מי שאומר שאין להקל להן. On the 25th of Kislev the eight days of Hanukah begin. Eulogies and fasting are forbidden, but work is permitted. Women have the custom not to work while the candles are burning, and there is an opinion that one may not be lenient with them about it. The Historical Background (Mishnah Berurah, Seif Katan 1) The Rabbi read the Mishnah Berurah inside, because it sets out what the Greeks actually did: They prevented us from observing our faith and from studying Torah. They took our money and property. They took control of our girls. The persecution \"wasn't just a religious one.\" It also involved violence against young women. They profaned the Temple. This lasted for a period of time and caused great distress. Afterwards we had a measure of autonomy for roughly another two hundred years, until the Roman destruction in 70 CE. A point that is less well known: only a few years after the great military victory, we lost control of the Temple again. \"Nonetheless, we're still celebrating the beauty and the value of this miracle.\" The victory came on 25 Kislev . When the Ḥashmonaim entered the Temple, they found only one sealed flask of oil, which lasted for eight days, and the Ḥachamim established eight days to commemorate it. What Does \"Hanukah\" Mean? Reading Meaning Status חנו כ״ה \"They rested on the 25th\": the fighting stopped and they were victorious on 25 Kislev A nice play on words (Mishnah Berurah) חנוכה = inauguration Like זאת חנוכת המזבח , \"this is the inauguration of the altar.\" After years under Greek control the Temple had lost its sanctity, so it was re-inaugurated The simple meaning This is why it is so common to inaugurate a synagogue or install a rabbi on Hanukah. The Rabbi mentioned that his own synagogue, Holland Park, was inaugurated on Hanukah, and they try to mark its anniversary then, though holiday time doesn't always allow it. The Four Aspects of Hanukah Through the shiur the Rabbi \"kept count of the miracles.\" By the end there are four distinct aspects of Hanukah, and every practice of the festival maps onto one of them. Aspect 1: The Military Victory \"People forget this aspect. You ask nine out of ten people what Hanukah is, they'll tell you, oh, the menorah, the oil lasted for eight days... That's true, but that's not the main miracle. The main miracle of Hanukah is the victory at war. \" We were persecuted for years under the Greek-Syrian emperor Antiochus. The Ḥashmonaim were a smaller, seemingly less trained army, and yet miraculously overcame them. Aspect 2: The Miracle of the Oil On entering the Temple they found one flask, enough for one day, which lasted for eight. Aspect 3: The Miracle of Yehudit This explains the custom of eating dairy foods on Hanukah , which is less well known than the custom of oily foods. According to the account the Rabbi gave, a royal edict required every betrothed girl to spend the night before her wedding with the ruler. Yehudit, the daughter of Yoḥanan Kohen Gadol , was due to be married just before the battle began. She prepared a \"romantic evening,\" fed him dairy foods (milk and cheese) that made him drowsy, and when he closed his eyes she killed him and escaped . This was the beginning of the revolt. Aspect 4: The Re-inauguration of the Altar The Rema (Seif 2, below) gives this as a reason for festive meals: those were the days on which the altar was inaugurated . The Mishnah Berurah brings a Midrash: The original Mishkan was completed on 25 Kislev , but Hashem waited until Rosh Ḥodesh Nisan to inaugurate it. Kislev \"complained\": if the work was finished in my month, it should be inaugurated in my month. Hashem answered: you are right, and I will give you another chance at inauguration. Hence Hanukah, the re-inauguration of the Beit HaMikdash in Kislev. Every Hanukah Practice Maps to an Aspect Practice When Commemorates Full Hallel all eight days (unlike Pesaḥ's half Hallel, and no Hallel on Purim) Morning 1: Military victory. The essence of Hanukah is הלל והודאה , praise and thanksgiving Al HaNissim in Amidah and Birkat HaMazon All day 1: Military victory. The whole paragraph is about \"the mighty in the hands of the weak, the impure in the hands of the pure\"; the lighting is mentioned only at the end, with no explicit mention of the miracle of the oil Lighting the menorah Evening 2: The oil Latkes and oily foods Any time 2: The oil Dairy foods Any time 3: Yehudit Torah reading of the Nesi'im (the twelve princes' offerings at the dedication of the altar) Morning 4: The inauguration of the altar Gifts to teachers; emphasis on Torah study Throughout Religious freedom: the persecution Hanukah ended was a spiritual one On the custom (especially strong among Ḥasidim) of children bringing gifts to their teachers: \"The whole of Hanukah is about Torah... It's all well and good to have religious freedom. If you don't practise your religious freedom, what's the point of having those freedoms? If we're not studying, then we're not enjoying our religious freedoms.\" Eulogies (הספד) on Hanukah The Shulḥan Arukh forbids hesped on Hanukah. This often becomes a real question when there is a funeral during Hanukah and a child or spouse wishes to speak. What the Ḥachamim meant by hesped was very different from a modern eulogy. In the sugyot that discuss it: - Women were employed to bang their chests, shout and shriek . - It was a whole procession: someone would call out and the people would repeat after them, \"like a whole cry.\" Modern application: - A talk designed to make people cry, a deeply emotional and moving lament, should be avoided on Hanukah. - But most eulogies today, \"at least in England,\" are biographical: my father was born here in 1928, he worked in this, he had a remarkable life. That is absolutely fine. - \"If you're giving a talk and you happen to shed some tears whilst you're doing so, I don't think that qualifies as being a hesped.\" The Exception (Seif 3): A Ḥacham You may deliver a full hesped for a Ḥacham when the coffin is present (at the funeral). This does not extend to the shiv'ah house. Mourning continues on Hanukah. Unlike Shabbat and Yom Tov, Hanukah gives no respite from aveilut: - The days of Hanukah count toward the shiv'ah, and shiv'ah continues as usual. - Hallel is said in the shiv'ah house, though the mourner should not lead it. - Tzidduk HaDin (the affirmation of Divine justice said at a funeral) is not said on Hanukah: it expresses a very stark, \"raw\" acceptance of judgment, which does not fit days on which we perceive Hashem's mercy. It is likewise omitted on Rosh Ḥodesh. Fasting on Hanukah Situation Ruling A full fast (no eating or drinking ) Forbidden during the eight days A \"diet fast\" (no food during the day, but drinking) Fine. It is not a halachic fast Yahrzeit of a parent (normally one tries to fast) Not observed on Hanukah A bad dream (ta'anit ḥalom) Permitted , but you must make up for it with another fast after Hanukah, exactly like the law of fasting for a dream on Shabbat If someone had a bad dream on each of the eight nights and fasted every day, they would owe eight further fasts after Hanukah: sixteen fasts in all. Work: The Women's Custom (Minhag Nashim) Strictly (מעיקר הדין) work is permitted on Hanukah. But the Tur already quotes an older custom that women do not work while the candles are burning . How long? Everyone agrees this does not mean as long as the oil lasts. Even with a candle that burns for three or four hours, the custom covers only the first half hour after lighting . What counts as \"work\"? Not the 39 melachot of Shabbat. Here \"work\" means טירחא יתירה , burdensome, effortful tasks: Avoided in the first half hour Not included Laundry (in those days a major task; arguably pressing a washing machine button is not the same) Writing Sewing and knitting Light tasks Cooking and baking (Ḥacham Ovadia includes these) Borer and other Shabbat categories, which are irrelevant here Possibly washing up One Rishon held that, since it is a firm and established custom, one may not be lenient. Ḥacham Ovadia also rules that women should preferably refrain from burdensome work for half an hour after lighting. Why women specifically? Because the revolt began through Yehudit . The custom was established specifically for women. The Mishnah Berurah notes some places where men were stringent too, but the Rabbi said, \"I don't know anyone who does this nowadays.\" An extreme custom that was rejected There was apparently a custom for women to refrain from household work for all eight days and nights . The poskim decried it: \"What's a woman going to do for eight days and eight nights if she can't do household chores? It's not good for her,\" and idleness leads to sin. The halacha is that the custom covers only the first half hour and only burdensome work . Seif 2: Festive Meals on Hanukah ריבוי הסעודות שמרבים בהם הם סעודות הרשות, שלא קבעום למשתה ושמחה. The many festive meals people hold are voluntary meals, since the days were not established for feasting and joy. This goes back to the Maharam of Rothenburg , about 800 years ago. People were already holding many festive meals on Hanukah, and he wanted to clarify that there is nothing wrong with them, but they are not a mitzvah . The Rema disagrees: ויש אומרים שיש קצת מצוה ברבוי הסעודות . Some say there is a bit of a mitzvah in them, because those were the days of the inauguration of the altar . Note that the Rema's reason is the inauguration (Aspect 4), not simply \"because it is Hanukah.\" The practical synthesis: everyone agrees that any gathering with songs, praise of Hashem and words of Torah is a mitzvah, on any weekday of the year. So have the parties, but infuse them with Torah and holiness . The Rema adds: there is a custom to eat cheese on Hanukah , because the miracle came about through milk that Yehudit fed the enemy. Why a feast on Purim but not on Hanukah? On Purim the se'udah is one of the four central mitzvot. Why not on Hanukah? The Mishnah Berurah already raises this from earlier Aḥaronim. The Rabbi's answer: Purim and Hanukah are the two prototypes of persecution in Jewish history. Purim Hanukah Nature of the threat Physical. You are Jewish, so you must die, regardless of your beliefs. No offer of conversion: \"one people, scattered, their laws are strange.\" Like the Holocaust, where one Jewish grandparent was enough Spiritual. Give up your faith, accept Hellenism, abandon your \"barbaric\" laws and you may live among us The salvation Of the body Of the soul The celebration Physical: eat meat, drink wine, a mitzvah to become intoxicated: \"the body should enjoy itself\" Spiritual: הלל והודאה , praise and thanksgiving Obligatory se'udah? Yes No A side note from the Rabbi: the word \"barbarian\" comes from the Greeks. Anyone who did not share their philosophy was a barbaros . The Bi'ur Halacha on Hanukah Parties The Bi'ur Halacha (on Seif 2), quoting the Maharshal : - The joy of the feast should be mixed with the joy of Torah , and you should not turn away from your regular path. If you have regular shiurim, don't give them up for a Hanukah party. - \"In our great sins,\" instead of singing and praising Hashem, people sit at these parties and play cards . \"If you care about your soul, stay away from this.\" \"Unfortunately, nothing changes.\" The Rabbi recalled an organisation that ran a Hanukah casino night a few years earlier, which he raised as \"completely incorrect\" and contradictory to the festival. An aside: Nittel Nacht Some Ḥasidim believe one should not learn Torah or do mitzvot on the night of 25 December, for fear of giving strength to forces of evil. The Rabbi has given a shiur on why this is wrong. In yeshivah, where about a third of the students were Ḥasidim, a third of the 300-strong beit midrash would empty at nightfall on that date (an ordinary learning day) to play football and cards. That year, 25 December fell on the first night of Hanukah , \"so they have to light the menorah. They can't get out of that.\" Part Two: Siman 671 — The Mitzvah of Lighting (Opening) \"חביבה היא עד מאוד\" צריך להיזהר מאוד בהדלקת נרות חנוכה. One must be very careful with the lighting of the Hanukah candles. Even a poor person supported by charity must, if the community has overlooked his needs, borrow or sell his clothing ( שואל או מוכר כסותו ) to buy oil. This is a unique law . It appears only for mitzvot of פרסומי ניסא , publicising the miracle. The one parallel is the four cups at the Seder , which a poor person must also secure. Normally the Shulḥan Arukh simply states the law. Here it says \"one must be very careful,\" echoing the Rambam, who writes, unusually for him, in flowery language: מצות נר חנוכה מצוה חביבה היא עד מאוד , the mitzvah of the Hanukah light is an exceedingly beloved mitzvah . The Rabbi noted this is the Rambam's own expression, not taken from the Gemara. The Rabbi has given a separate shiur on the unique beauty of Hanukah , which explains why the Rambam wrote this way specifically here. The Gemara: הרגיל בנר הויין ליה בנים תלמידי חכמים . One who is particular with the light (of Shabbat and Hanukah) will have children who are Torah scholars. The light of mitzvah connects to ותורה אור : Torah is light, and when we are careful with the Shabbat and Hanukah lights, we infuse our children with love and knowledge of Torah. This is why some are particular to light olive oil even on Friday nights. Practical limit (Magen Avraham): the poor person need only secure enough fuel for one light each night . One should not take charity to light olive oil for eight nights. (Olive oil \"is expensive nowadays, as we know.\") Seif 2: How Many Lights? Three Levels The Gemara (Shabbat 21b) sets out three levels: Level Name Practice 1 נר איש וביתו (the basic mitzvah, fully performed) One light per household per night 2 מהדרין , those who beautify One light per person in the household per night 3 מהדרין מן המהדרין , the most beautiful Add a light each night : one, two, three... up to eight The Dispute: Does Level 3 Build on Level 1 or Level 2? Rambam Tosafot Level 3 means Level 2 plus adding a light each night: every person lights the night's number Level 1 plus adding a light each night: only one menorah for the household Reasoning The highest level includes everything below it The whole point of adding a light each night is to show which night it is , the continuity of the miracle. If everyone lights, you can no longer tell Consequence Each person lights It is wrong to light a second menorah in the home The Shulḥan Arukh rules like Tosafot: ואפילו אם רבים בני הבית לא ידליקו יותר , even if there are many in the household, they should not light more. The Rema rules like the Rambam: ויש אומרים דכל אחד מבני הבית ידליק, וכן המנהג פשוט , each member of the household lights, and that is the widespread custom, but each person's lights must be given a separate place so the number of the night remains clear. \"We see this: menorahs next to each other. They should not be next to each other.\" Current Practice Sephardim: one menorah per household (Shulḥan Arukh / Tosafot). Ashkenazim: each person lights (Rema / Rambam), in separate places. Two Burning Questions on the Shulḥan Arukh Question 1: Why would the Shulḥan Arukh follow Tosafot against the Rambam? - The Shulḥan Arukh chose three pillars: the Rif , the Rambam and the Rosh . Just over two-thirds of the Shulḥan Arukh follows the Rambam. Following the Rosh against the Rambam happens occasionally; following Tosafot against the Rambam is very rare. - The Rif here is like the Rambam. - Answer: the Rambam himself writes that the prevalent custom in his own country was like Tosafot . Although he ruled otherwise, he did not try to change it. The Shulḥan Arukh followed that established custom. Question 2: Why doesn't the Shulḥan Arukh state the basic law at all? - The basic halacha is one light per household per night. The Shulḥan Arukh's first duty is to state the law and then add the beautification. Yet it opens straight with the mehadrin min ha-mehadrin practice. - Answer: on Hanukah, hiddur becomes the obligation . With every other mitzvah, hiddur is a topping: a tallit is the mitzvah and a beautiful tallit is hiddur; an undecorated sukkah fulfils the mitzvah perfectly. Hanukah is different. \"Hanukah without hiddur isn't a Hanukah.\" The full explanation is a larger shiur, \"a very deep concept\" that \"will change your Hanukah forever.\" Practical note: you can still fulfil the basic law. When travelling, a couple of candles is fine. You Don't Need a Menorah \"There's no halacha you need a menorah. I want to make this clear.\" You can line up eight candles or tea lights on a windowsill, making sure they don't fall. The menorah itself, the very symbol of Hanukah, comes from the mehadrin min ha-mehadrin , the beautifiers of the beautifiers. \"That's also incredible to think about.\" Questions from the Class On a \"Hanukah bush\" (as some do in America): \"That's a completely forbidden thing to do, and a desecration of the mitzvah,\" all the more so in a year when Hanukah begins on 25 December. Robert asked: for those who hold that several people light, doesn't Tosafot's concern assume everyone lights together? The Rabbi: in those days there was no lighting \"at 7 o'clock.\" You lit at nightfall, within the half hour when people were still in the streets, so everything happened within a few minutes of each other: \"essentially all the same time.\" On children's menorahs: Should Sephardim have one menorah for the adults and one for the children? The Rabbi: according to the Shulḥan Arukh we do not. When his own children come home from school wanting to use their menorahs, he lets them light on the other side of the house , but tells them each year: \"I'm letting you, but you recognise it's not the right thing... this is not our minhag.\" On lighting both indoors and outdoors: to be discussed in the coming chapter on where to place the menorah. Summary Hilchot Hanukah spans Shulḥan Arukh OḤ 670–684 . The aim is to finish all of it before Hanukah. \"Hanukah\" most simply means inauguration . \"They rested on the 25th\" is a play on words. Four aspects: the military victory (the main miracle), the oil, Yehudit, and the re-inauguration of the altar. Every Hanukah practice maps onto one of them. Hesped is forbidden. That means emotional lamentation designed to make people cry, not a biographical eulogy. A Ḥacham may be eulogised with the coffin present. Mourning continues. Tzidduk HaDin is not said. Fasting is forbidden, except a dream fast, which must be made up afterwards. No yahrzeit fast. A diet \"fast\" with drinking is fine. Work is permitted. Women customarily avoid burdensome work for the first half hour after lighting, in honour of Yehudit. Festive meals are voluntary (Shulḥan Arukh). The Rema says there is some mitzvah because of the inauguration. Fill them with song, praise and Torah, not cards. Purim celebrates a physical rescue with physical joy. Hanukah celebrates a spiritual rescue with הלל והודאה . Siman 671: even a poor person must sell his clothing to light, as with the four cups. The Rambam calls this mitzvah חביבה עד מאוד . Three levels of lighting. Sephardim light one menorah per household (Tosafot / Shulḥan Arukh). Ashkenazim light one per person in separate places (Rambam / Rema). On Hanukah, hiddur itself is the expectation . Coming Up Next week: Siman 671 continued . Where to place the menorah (doorway, window, inside), its height, the synagogue menorah, and related laws.","uPKy52hscIU":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 6 of 14 Topic: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 4 — Tochen (Grinding), Lash (Kneading) and Me'abed (Pickling) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 27 November 2018 Note: The shiur was dedicated l'ilui nishmat of two departed souls, as announced at the start of the recording. Recap We have covered Bishul, leaving food on the fire (She'hiyah), Borer and Dash. Tonight, the remaining two big kitchen melachot — Tochen and Lash — and a brief word on Me'abed . Part A — Tochen (Grinding) Definition In the Mishkan, the seeds for the dye were ground before being made into a paste. Tochen is not only \"grinding\" with a machine: it is making something big into very small pieces — grinding, mashing (avocado, banana), crushing, mincing, chopping very finely. How small is \"small\"? The poskim give no exact measure: as long as the pieces are not extremely fine, it is fine. A finely chopped Israeli salad is probably on the border but still acceptable. Tochen applies to non-food too: shaving wood, crushing a clod of earth — relevant, for example, to dried mud on clothes. Does Tochen apply to food that is already edible? In the Mishkan the seeds were inedible. Some Rishonim hold Tochen applies only when grinding makes something edible (e.g. raw beetroot, which the Gemara discusses). The final halacha: Tochen applies even to edible food — so one may not grind or finely chop vegetables as a rule. Grinding for immediate use The Gemara explicitly permits Borer for immediate use, but says nothing about Tochen. The Rashba (13th century) argues the same applies: grinding with your hands for immediate eating is no different from grinding with your teeth — there is no melacha in the mouth; it is the way of eating. Halacha: edible produce may be mashed or finely chopped for immediate use — for the upcoming meal, even if it lasts hours — combining both lenient views. E.g. mashing avocado for the meal about to start. Never with a specialised instrument Even when Tochen is permitted, use only ordinary cutlery — fork, spoon, knife; a chopping board is fine. A grater, mincer, chopper or potato masher is forbidden rabbinically as uvda d'chol (a weekday activity) — such tools are even muktseh . Electrical machines, of course, are forbidden. Egg slicer: permitted — it slices rather than grinds, so it is not a grinding tool and not muktseh. Q (peelers): a vegetable peeler is a Borer question rather than Tochen (see Shiur 5). Food that does not grow from the ground Meat, fish, chicken and eggs don't grow from the ground. Combining two disputes (edible food; non-plant food), Tochen does not apply to cooked meat, fish or chicken, or to eggs — even not for immediate use. Mashing eggs on Friday night for Shabbat morning is fine — still without a specialised tool. Ein tochen achar tochen Something already ground and re-formed — bread, cake, biscuits made from flour — may be crumbled again, even for later. The handle of the knife Doing a melacha in an unusual manner ( shinui ) normally reduces it to a rabbinic prohibition. But the Gemara treats mashing with the back or handle of a knife or fork ( kat ha'sakin ) as so unusual that it is permitted outright — for any food, even not for immediate use. Practical examples Case Ruling Mash avocado for the meal about to start Permitted — edible, immediate use Crush raw (uncooked) beetroot for now Better not — inedible raw Mash avocado on Friday night for the next morning Only with the handle of a knife/fork Mash a cooked piece of fish, now or later Permitted — not from the ground and already edible Chop salad extremely finely Only for immediate use Machines that make noise — hashma'at kol Generally, what is set going before Shabbat may run during Shabbat (a garden hose). But some forbid letting a noisy machine — classically a mill — run on Shabbat, since people may think it was started on Shabbat. Sephardim: no such prohibition. A washing machine on a timer may run on Shabbat — perhaps not in the spirit of the day, but permitted if tucked away and not disturbing. Ashkenazim are stringent on noise. An alarm clock is permitted by all — everyone knows it is set in advance. Some forbid a television even switched on before Shabbat on this basis. Q: a radio on a timer? — Strictly speaking, yes (for Sephardim). Part B — Lash (Kneading) Definition After grinding, the seeds were mixed with water into a paste. Lash = binding small particles into a unified mass using a liquid (water, oil, vinegar). The classic case is making dough. Making a sandwich is not Lash — no particles are bound together. Two stages — and the key dispute Putting the ingredients together (which begins the mingling). The act of kneading/mixing. View What is forbidden Shulchan Aruch, Rif, Rambam — Sephardim Only the act of kneading/mixing Ashkenazim Both — even putting the ingredients together Two kinds of mixture Belila avah — a thick, dense mass that won't pour, or moves only very slowly (dough): forbidden by the Torah . Belila rakah — semi-loose, pours slowly: forbidden rabbinically . (Something truly runny isn't Lash at all.) The permitted method — criss-cross strokes The Gemara permits mixing with a big shinui : criss-cross strokes — one direction, then the perpendicular one, lifting the spoon out between strokes — instead of the normal circular motion. For Sephardim: since only kneading is the problem, one may combine the ingredients on Shabbat and mix with criss-cross strokes — even a thick mixture . For Ashkenazim: the shinui helps only the kneading, not combining. So a thick mixture can be made on Shabbat only if the ingredients were combined before Shabbat. A semi-loose mixture (only rabbinic) is permitted with a minor change — e.g. reversing the order of the ingredients (oil first, then egg). Ashkenazim rely on the Sephardi view in special cases, e.g. for a child or an invalid. When it binds by itself — beware The leniency of combining ingredients assumes the mass forms only through your mixing. Where the ingredients bind on their own as soon as they touch, it is forbidden even for Sephardim. Example: Weetabix with a little milk — the milk is absorbed and binds the particles by itself. Pour in a large amount of milk so it stays runny, then mix with criss-cross strokes. Thinning is fine Adding liquid to loosen a mixture is the opposite of Lash and generally permitted — e.g. adding water to ready-made tehina that is already edible. From the Rabbi's handout of practical examples (The Rabbi distributed a sheet of common questions he had compiled; thanks to Michael for printing it.) Tehina from ground sesame and water — forbidden: it binds by itself. Ready-made tehina, edible — may add water to loosen it. Ready-made tehina too thick to eat — adding water makes it bind into an edible paste: use criss-cross strokes. Aubergine salad with oil — Sephardim: criss-cross strokes; for a looser version, oil first. Egg and onion with oil or mayonnaise; liver with oil; tuna with mayonnaise — usually thick. For Sephardim: combine and mix with criss-cross strokes. Weetabix / porridge — plenty of milk so it is runny, then criss-cross strokes. Jelly may be made on Shabbat, if there are no other issues. Part C — Me'abed (Pickling) Pickling is forbidden on Shabbat — e.g. putting a cucumber into a pickle jar. Reasons given: it resembles cooking , or tanning (changing the texture, like processing leather). Be careful with salting vegetables such as cucumbers and leaving them to stand — salt just before eating, or add oil , which neutralises the salt's effect. Summary Tochen = making something into very small pieces, food or not. Permitted: edible produce for immediate use; cooked meat/fish/chicken and eggs at any time; already-ground foods (bread, cake); anything with the handle of a knife or fork. Never with a grater, masher or mincer; an egg slicer is fine. Noisy machines on timers (washing machine) — permitted for Sephardim; Ashkenazim are stringent. Lash = binding particles with liquid. Sephardim may combine ingredients and mix with criss-cross strokes ; Ashkenazim need ingredients combined before Shabbat for thick mixtures. Beware things that bind by themselves (Weetabix, tehina from sesame). Me'abed — no pickling; don't leave salted cucumbers standing. Coming Up Break for Chanukah; the series resumes on 18 December . Shiur 7 — 18 December: Out and About — Horesh, Zore'a, Kotser Ploughing — walking with football studs or high heels, making holes in the ground, playing marbles or football Planting — putting flowers in a vase on Shabbat Harvesting — climbing trees, relaxing in a hammock attached to a tree One more shiur before the winter break, resuming in January. B'Ezrat Hashem","uT6_4sxjodk":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 5 of 14 Topic: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 3 — Dash (Threshing) and Borer (Selecting) Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 20 November 2018 Recap So far: an introduction to Shabbat and Erev Shabbat; Kiddush, the meals and Havdala; and two weeks on Bishul — cooking, and what may be left on or returned to the fire. Tonight, two more major melachot: Dash (threshing) and Borer (selecting). Part A — Dash (Threshing) The definition In the Mishkan, seeds were extracted from their pods to make the dye for the lowest roof covering. So Dash = extracting something from an inedible casing — or extracting something that was inaccessible before. Opening an edible pod (e.g. green sugar-snap style pods) and taking out the edible beans — permitted : both are food. Extracting kernels of wheat from the husk — the classic Dash, forbidden . 1. Squeezing fruit and vegetables Squeezing a liquid out of a fruit extracts something previously inaccessible — a form of Dash. Fruit Status Grapes and olives Forbidden by the Torah — the only truly significant drinks (compare the price of grape juice and olive oil with apple juice) Fruit and vegetables commonly squeezed for juice (orange, apple, pear, pineapple, tomato) Forbidden rabbinically — similar to grapes and olives Produce rarely squeezed (e.g. melon) Permitted The Rabbi recalled once being offered melon juice in the home of someone on a special diet — it exists, but is not common. Carrot juice involves other issues (grinding) and its status is debatable. Squeezing between the lips Inside the mouth , while eating — no melacha at all; that is the normal way of eating. Squeezing by hand into the mouth — forbidden. Sucking juice out between the lips (not while eating) — debated. Halacha: avoid with grapes and olives (a Torah prohibition is at stake); permitted with other fruit (strawberry, orange segment). Squeezing directly onto food Cutting a fruit into pieces is never Dash. Juice squeezed directly onto solid food (e.g. orange onto a fruit salad) never takes on an identity as a liquid — it goes from solid to solid — so it is permitted for any fruit . But squeezing into a cup and then pouring onto the salad is not ; it must go straight onto the food. Lemons The Shulchan Aruch, in one of its shortest rulings: מותר לסחוט לימונ\"ש — one may squeeze lemons. Even into an empty cup. Lemon juice is not drunk on its own — it is always added to something — so it is not comparable to grapes and olives. By hand only, not with a squeezer or machine. One may squeeze lemon straight into tea — preferably into a kli shlishi , as lemon may be a \"tender\" food for Bishul purposes; strictly a kli sheni is fine. Peeling fruit Isn't peeling an orange extracting the fruit from an inedible case? No — peeling is permitted . The action is on the peel, removing it to expose the fruit, rather than extracting the fruit from its case (among other reasons). 2. Milking — animals, and expressing milk Milking an animal is Dash from the Torah . But tza'ar ba'alei chayim — preventing animal suffering — is also a Torah mitzvah. If an animal is in pain from not being milked: Ask a non-Jew to milk it — even directly; instructing a non-Jew is only rabbinic. If none is available, a Jew may milk it to waste : a melacha with no constructive purpose ( mekalkel ) is only rabbinically forbidden — permitted to relieve the animal. Use an electric milking machine on a timer : attach it while off; when it switches on, it is only gerama (indirect) — and the milk may be kept. If the machine is already running, let the first drops (directly caused by attaching it) go to waste; the rest is gerama. A nursing mother in discomfort is in exactly the same position: she may express to waste; if the baby needs the milk, use a timer-operated pump as above. Without one, seek rabbinic guidance. Q (milk from Mehadrin dairies milked electrically on Shabbat): no reason it may not be used. If a non-Jew milks the cows while a Jew watches, it is still chalav Yisrael . 3. Crushing ice, snow and hail The Gemara forbids crushing ice, snow or hail on Shabbat. Reasons: it resembles Dash (turning a solid into liquid — rabbinic, since nothing is extracted from a case); the Rambam sees it as Makeh Be'Patish / \"creating\" new water. Either way: Only the act of crushing is forbidden. Ice cubes in a drink, or a tray of ice left on the table to melt — permitted . Don't crush ice cubes against the side of the glass with a spoon. Making ice (liquid → solid) is fine — but only for use on that Shabbat, not for Motzei Shabbat or the following week. Automatic electric ice-makers raise electrical issues (to be discussed separately). Using a bar of soap Two concerns: turning a solid into a liquid, and smoothing ( memare'ach ) the bar. Chacham Ovadia: dissolving by rubbing isn't creating a liquid; smoothing here is an unintended, purely cosmetic effect (a rabbinic pesik reisha delo nicha lei ). So Sephardim may use a bar of soap — but where liquid soap is available, use it. (Ashkenazim have additional issues.) Part B — Borer (Selecting) The definition Borer = selecting an item out of a mixture. (A lesson in itself: you may walk for hours on Shabbat, but organising and categorising — an industrial process — is forbidden.) Selection becomes permitted — a form of eating rather than an industrial act — when three conditions are met: Take the good from the bad — \"bad\" just means what you don't want now. From a bowl of mixed nuts, take out the almonds you want; don't remove the peanuts and walnuts you don't. By hand — a fork or spoon counts as an extension of the hand. A specialist tool does not: peeling a potato with a knife is fine; with a peeler , avoid. For immediate use — within about half an hour, or for the upcoming meal . Preparing dessert half an hour before sitting down is fine, even if eaten three hours later. Preparing it at 9 am before going to shul for a lunch hours later — avoid. No mixture, no Borer The most important principle. A bowl of identical apples may be divided — that is division, not selection . Hence: Bones from fish or chicken — a piece of salmon is one entity, not \"salmon mixed with bones\" (Mishnah Berurah). You may remove the bones, even for later. But once plates are cleared and leftover fish and bones are piled together, that is a new mixture. Chicken skin — same idea; but loose skins mixed back together become a mixture. Watermelon seeds may be removed (even the Ben Ish Chai is lenient). Pomegranate — opening it and taking out seeds is neither Dash (you're opening the outer case) nor Borer (one entity). Once seeds and white pith are loose in a bowl, it is a mixture. Tinned tuna in oil — a mixture: take the tuna out; don't drain the oil (unless you want the oil). A platter of different fish balls (cod, salmon…) — a mixture. Large items clearly visible on a table (apples and oranges) — not a mixture. Sorting cutlery After Friday night dinner, 20 knives, forks and spoons are mixed together. Sorting them isn't \"good from bad\" — you want them all, just later. Some see organising as Borer; others require selection. Combined with further reasons to be lenient (a view that \"bad\" not truly bad is only an issue beyond the upcoming meal; and that big, clearly distinguishable utensils may not be a mixture at all), Chacham Ovadia permits washing up on Friday night and sorting the cutlery to set the table for Shabbat morning — for the next meal only, not for Se'udah Shlishit. If some of it (e.g. special guest cutlery) won't be used in the morning, it becomes good-from-bad and is forbidden. Q: Does tircha (effort) matter? — That concept applies to Borer on Yom Tov, not Shabbat. Solid and liquid A carrot sitting in chicken soup is not \"mixed\" with it — a solid and a liquid are clearly distinct. So one may remove an unwanted carrot from soup ( ein Borer be'ochlin u'mashkin in this sense). Two liquids together would be a mixture. Only an active act of selection The Mishnah: carrying a trough that incidentally sifts the food is permitted — Borer requires a deliberate act of selection, and pesik reisha doesn't apply here. So a salt shaker with rice inside may be used; the sieve holding back the rice isn't your act of selecting. Borer applies to non-food items too: toys, and playing cards — similar cards (same number and colour, different suit) may form a mixture; think games through before playing. Questions from the floor Searching through a pile of tallitot in shul for a good one — permitted: you are randomly picking up and putting aside to reach what you want, not performing an act of selection. Likewise lifting coats off a hook to reach yours. Sorting books — books with visible titles are not a mixture; identical unmarked books would be. Summary Dash = extracting from an inedible casing. Squeezing grapes and olives — Torah; commonly juiced fruit — rabbinic. Squeezing directly onto food is permitted for any fruit; lemons may be squeezed even into a cup. Peeling is fine. Milking / expressing: non-Jew; to waste; or a timer-operated machine. Don't crush ice or snow; letting it melt or making ice for Shabbat is fine. Bar soap permitted for Sephardim; liquid soap preferable. Borer needs a mixture; permitted with good-from-bad, by hand, for immediate use. Bones, skin, watermelon seeds — not a mixture. Cutlery may be sorted on Friday night for the morning meal. Coming Up Shiur 6 — 27 November: The Shabbat Kitchen, Part 4 — Grinding, Kneading and Pickling Mashing eggs, liver, cheese, avocado Making tehina; pickling food on Shabbat After that, a break for Chanukah; the series resumes on 18 December. B'Ezrat Hashem","wQG9ZdLvqjM":"Class Notes — Dayan Daniel Kada Series: Laws of Shabbat — a 14-part course Shiur: 14 of 14 — the final shiur Topic: Childbirth, Babies and Children on Shabbat; Amirah Le'Akum — Instructing a Non-Jew; Closing Words and Q&A Recorded: Tiferet Eyal, Hendon — 26 March 2019 Recap Last time: the five levels of illness, and Muktseh. Tonight: children and babies, then asking a non-Jew, then questions and plans for the coming year. Part A — Childbirth on Shabbat When is a woman \"in labour\"? A woman in childbirth is pikuach nefesh . The Gemara gives three signs she is definitely a yoledet : she can no longer walk unaided; she begins bleeding ; she sits on the birthing stool . But one must be somewhere safe by that point — so add the time needed to get to hospital. General guidance: once contractions are regular, about every 15–20 minutes , she has the status of a patient and Shabbat may be broken for anything she needs. (Waters breaking usually indicates that time has come, unless you live next door to the hospital.) Her psychological needs are paramount Just as a child locked in a room may be released even by breaking Torah prohibitions, a woman in labour's peace of mind is part of a safe delivery: Her husband may travel with her if she wants him there, even though he gives no medical care. If she wants her mother , her mother may be called and may drive to the hospital. Getting home afterwards The companion may return home — otherwise people would hesitate to accompany her in the first place. Halacha: only via rabbinic means — e.g. a non-Jewish taxi , not driving oneself (more so if children are at home alone). Doctors and Hatzalah may even drive back themselves (partly because they may be needed again). With a shinui Unlike other pikuach nefesh, childbirth is a normal occurrence (the Rambam: one in a thousand died in his day). So where it causes no delay, do everything with a shinui — e.g. lift the receiver and dial with the weaker hand. In a real emergency, act as fast as possible. Q: Taxi or drive yourself? — A non-Jewish taxi involves only rabbinic issues (phoning, paying), so prefer it; drive yourself if a taxi would be slow (e.g. Christmas Day). Ninth-month preparations From about four to five weeks before the due date, prepare so as to minimise desecration of Shabbat: a packed bag ; lights left on where needed; exact taxi fare by the door, the cab pre-arranged; the urn on ; the phone left on . Staying near the hospital for Shabbat in the ninth month is a good idea, but not required (some poskim do require it where possible). After birth Period Status Days 1–3 (first 72 hours) Shabbat is broken for her needs even if she says she doesn't need it, if others judge she does (e.g. turning on the heating — honestly for her) Days 4–7 Whatever she asks for is done, even if others think it unnecessary; if she says she doesn't need it, listen to her Days 8–30 A choleh she'ein bo sakana (Level 3): a non-Jew may do anything for her; she may do rabbinic acts with a shinui (unless complications keep her in danger) Miscarriage after 40 days of pregnancy gives her the status of a yoledet — Shabbat may be broken for her needs; stillbirth certainly so. Part B — Babies on Shabbat Baby wipes Wiping squeezes liquid out of the wipe — sechita , which many forbid. Chacham Ovadia permits wipes if you don't squeeze — wipe gently, taking only surface moisture. Modern wipes can be very wet — take care. (Alternatives: pour water and dry with tissue; wash the baby.) Warming a bottle Stand the bottle in hot water poured into a bowl (a kli sheni) — fine. You may even pour from the urn directly onto the bottle : iruy kli rishon only cooks the outer surface — the plastic, not the milk inside. Make sure the outside is dry (no cold water drops on it). Fully submerging it in the hot water is fine — hatmana applies only to a kli rishon. Nappy cream A baby has the status of a choleh , so medicine is permitted. The only issue is smoothing: oil is fine; even thick cream (which Ashkenazim avoid) is permitted for Sephardim in small amounts rubbed in so that no layer remains. Cold Kol cholim etzel tzinah — everyone counts as \"ill\" when it comes to cold. When it is truly cold (the Shulchan Aruch warns against those who rely on this when it's only a little cold), one may instruct a non-Jew outright to switch on the heating — since a very cold person will probably fall ill. With a baby or elderly person in the home, the bar is much lower. Q: with no non-Jew available, may a Jew do it? — The Rabbi found no source permitting it unless one is actually ill (then, with a shinui). Nappies Opening a nappy's tabs is fine — they're made to be undone (not permanent sewing). But when disposing of it, don't seal it closed with the tabs — that's permanent; just put it in a bag. Bathing a baby Adults may not bathe in hot water even if heated before Shabbat (as discussed with swimming); a baby may be bathed in hot water, provided no new heating is caused (e.g. not opening a hot tap that draws cold water into a heating tank). Water from the urn should be put in a bowl first (a kli sheni) before adding cold water. Part C — Amirah Le'Akum: Instructing a Non-Jew Rabbinic, with a hint in the Torah The Torah says לֹא תֵעָשֶׂה מְלָאכָה — \"work shall not be done \" (passive), hinting that work done for you is included; but the prohibition is rabbinic. Three reasons: Rashi: he acts as your agent ( shaliach ). Yeshayahu (58:13, recited at Shabbat morning Kiddush) — מִמְּצוֹא חֶפְצְךָ וְדַבֵּר דָּבָר : your speech on Shabbat should not be like weekday speech — not business, not football, not giving orders. Rambam: otherwise there would be no Shabbat left — shops open with non-Jews running them — and people would end up doing things themselves. Two millennia ago much of what we avoid was not technically forbidden by the Torah — handling money, trading. People simply felt the spirit of Shabbat . As loopholes appeared, Chazal's decrees — Muktseh and Amirah Le'Akum chief among them — preserved that spirit. We are grateful for them. Two separate issues: instructing, and benefiting Hinting avoids the problem of instructing — but not of benefiting from work a non-Jew does for you . A completely dark room: you may not even hint to a non-Jew to switch on the light; if he does it on his own for you, you may not benefit (you should stop him if you can). If there is already some light (a streetlamp, the corridor light), hinting for more light is fine — the added benefit is minor. Switching off a light, or turning down the heating — you only benefit indirectly; you may hint. Turning on air-conditioning — a direct benefit; not unless it's unbearable. A non-Jewish flatmate who switches on a light for himself — you may benefit. Q: asking before Shabbat? — Solves the speech issue only; the other two reasons still forbid it. Further principles Pesik reisha through a non-Jew is permitted: ask him to open the fridge and bring you a drink, even though the light will come on. Ask for the task, not the method: asking a cleaner to wash up is fine even if she uses hot water or the dishwasher (she could use cold water); asking a hotel porter to bring something up even if he uses the lift. Shevut deshevut / rabbinic acts may be asked of a non-Jew for one of three reasons: a mitzvah , significant pain or discomfort , or great financial loss . Hotel electronic key cards — opening the door is a rabbinic matter; ask a member of staff where truly needed (e.g. to get into your room to sleep), not for trivial reasons. Lifts — depends on how each lift works (whether your weight affects the motor); prefer the stairs. A Shabbat lift is fine, certainly for the elderly. Communal needs (e.g. a broken eruv ) — a non-Jew may even be asked to do a Torah-level act (Ba'al HaItur, cited by the Rema). This leniency is much abused; it doesn't extend to a private Shabbat lunch. For a meal, a non-Jew may be asked to do a rabbinic act (e.g. carry through a karmelit) for what is genuinely needed (the Mishnah Berurah). Closing Words A long winter and a wonderful series — a real zechut to learn these halachot together. Shabbat comes every week; we grow used to it and forget its intricacies, so a refresher every few years is vital. But Shabbat cannot be reduced to mutar and assur . As in the very first shiur: a beautiful painting is made of fine details — without them there is no painting — yet fixate on one brushstroke and you lose the whole. The spirit of Shabbat — its feeling, enjoyment, holiness and aura — must be felt as well as kept. Fourteen lectures only open the door; there is so much more to learn. If everyone now knows where the questions are, the series has done its job. Coming series After Pesach (May–July): a short series of 7–8 shiurim on Kashrut , Tuesday nights. After Sukkot, for a full year: the Rambam's Thirteen Principles of Faith — each principle, the disagreements about it, and what it means for our lives. Leave your details with Barbara for weekly reminders. Questions from the Floor May a non-Jewish helper do laundry on Shabbat that you won't use on Shabbat? — You may not instruct it, even before Shabbat; it depends on how she is employed (a job-based arrangement differs from paid-by-the-hour) — a complicated topic. A door sensor with nobody around? — If you must get through, it's a pesik reisha one doesn't intend; discuss specifics. Owning a business that trades on Shabbat? — With a non-Jewish partner there are arrangements (a contract that you take no Shabbat profits), but they are very complex — consult a rabbi. Passing a baby during niddah ? — Under about six months, put the baby down for the other parent to pick up; once older and moving on its own, it may go from one to the other. Summary Labour: regular contractions (about every 15–20 min) — pikuach nefesh; meet her emotional needs; use a shinui where it causes no delay; prepare in the ninth month; companions may return via a non-Jewish taxi. Days 1–3, 4–7, 8–30 rules; miscarriage after 40 days. Babies: wipes gently without squeezing; warm bottles in or under urn water; nappy cream rubbed in; heating via a non-Jew (low threshold for babies); don't seal used nappies; babies may be bathed in pre-heated water. Non-Jews: no instructing and no direct benefit; hinting helps only where benefit is minor; pesik reisha via a non-Jew is fine; ask for tasks, not methods; rabbinic acts for a mitzvah, pain or financial loss. Above all, the spirit of Shabbat . Chazak u'Baruch — B'Ezrat Hashem","yc4p24A7sqI":"Class Notes — Rabbi Daniel Kada Siman: 318 (Bishul — Cooking on Shabbat) Seifim: End of ד (Dalet) + ה (Heh) Topics: Bishul Achar Afiyah (Cooking After Baking), The Ladle Question, Toast, Turkish Coffee, Nescafé, Tea Bags & Tea Leaves Recap: What We Have Covered in Siman 318 So Far Seifim 1–2: Ma'aseh Shabbat — penalties for cooking on Shabbat (intentional/unintentional), benefiting from the result. Cooking for a choleh. Seif 3: Toldot Ha'Or, cooking in the sun, toldot hachamah. Applications: dud shemesh, microwave. Seif 4 — Six major machlokot covered across three shiurim: Cooking from ma'achal Ben Drosai to fully cooked — Rambam (asur de'oraita) vs. Rosh (mutar). Shulchan Aruch rules like Rambam: asur. Bishul achar bishul b'lach (liquids) — Rambam (ein bishul achar bishul even for liquids) vs. Rosh (yesh bishul). Shulchan Aruch rules like the Rosh: asur. Plus: Rema's position, Sefardi/Ashkenazi interactions, hotplates on timers, asking a non-Jew. Irui kli rishon — Rabbeinu Tam (cooks, asur de'oraita) vs. Rashbam (doesn't cook, mutar). Shulchan Aruch rules like Rabbeinu Tam: asur. Kli sheni — Mechzi k'mevashel (first answer of Tosfot: asur derabanan) vs. no issue (second answer). Ashkenazim: asur. Sefardim: mutar. Kalei habishul — delicate items that cook even in a kli sheni. Cannot put in kli sheni; can put in kli shlishi (Rav Moshe vs. Chazon Ish). This shiur: Bishul achar afiyah / afiyah achar bishul (cooking after baking and vice versa). Seif ה (Heh): Bishul Achar Afiyah — Cooking After Baking Background: The Categories of Heat Transformation Category Hebrew Method Example Bishul בישול Cooking in liquid (water or oil) Boiling soup, deep frying Afiyah אפייה Baking (dry heat, enclosed) Challah in an oven Tzli צלייה Roasting (dry heat, open flame) Barbecue, Korban Pesach Afiyah and tzli are halachically identical — both are \"dry heat.\" The distinction matters because the principle of אין בישול אחר בישול (no cooking after cooking) clearly applies within the same category. The question is: does it apply across categories? The Machloket Rishonim Rabbi Eliezer mi-Metz (Sefer Yereim) — There IS Bishul Achar Afiyah Position: Although אין בישול אחר בישול, there is bishul after afiyah and afiyah after bishul. If something is baked, you cannot cook it on Shabbat, and vice versa. The act of cooking something that was previously baked creates a ta'am chadash (new dimension/transformation) — it is not merely \"further processing\" but a fundamentally different act. Two proofs from Pesach: Proof 1 — Matzah: Halacha states אין יוצאים במצה מבושלת — you do not fulfill the mitzvah with boiled matzah. Matzah must be baked, and if you then cook it (soak it in hot water), you no longer fulfill the mitzvah. This shows that cooking after baking changes the essential status of the food — it is no longer \"baked bread\" but \"cooked bread.\" Proof 2 — Korban Pesach: The Torah states כי אם צלי אש — the Korban Pesach must be roasted only. The Gemara (Pesachim) rules: צלאו ואחר כך בשלו חייב — if you roasted it and then cooked it, you have violated the prohibition. This shows that cooking after roasting is a meaningful new act. The Ra'avyah (Avi Ezri) — There is NO Bishul Achar Afiyah Position: Both proofs are refutable. אין בישול אחר בישול applies across all categories — once food is fully processed by heat (in any form), it is not subject to further bishul. Refutation of Proof 1: The Gemara says ta'am matzah ba'inan — you need to taste the matzah. Boiling the matzah doesn't create a \"new cooking\"; it simply destroys the taste of baked matzah. You cannot fulfill the mitzvah because you no longer taste baked bread, not because a new act of \"cooking\" occurred. Refutation of Proof 2: The Torah specifically commands כי אם צלי אש — \"only roasted.\" This is a specific prohibition unique to the Korban Pesach. You cannot generalise from this to all foods on Shabbat. The Beit Yosef's comment: He writes that the question on the Yereim is a \"kushya chazaka\" (strong challenge), indicating he finds the Ra'avyah's refutation compelling. Shulchan Aruch's Ruling (Seif Heh) יש מי שאומר שדבר שנאפה או נצלה אם בשלו אחר כך במשקה יש בו משום בשול ואסור ליתן פת אפילו בכלי שני \"There is one who says that something baked or roasted, if you then cook it in liquid, it is subject to bishul, and you may not put bread even in a kli sheni.\" ויש מתירין \"And there are those (plural) who permit.\" Key textual observation (Chacham Ovadia): The first opinion is introduced as יש מי שאומר (singular — \"one who says\"), while the second is ויש מתירין (plural — \"those who permit\"). This indicates the permissive view is more mainstream. Why Can't You Put Bread Even in a Kli Sheni? (Two Reasons for Ashkenazim) If you hold bishul achar afiyah, the Yereim adds: bread cannot be placed even in a kli sheni because: Mechzi k'mevashel — It looks like cooking (the Tosfot reason from the Mishna, applicable to all items in a kli sheni). We don't know what kalei habishul is — Since we cannot define precisely which items are \"easily cooked,\" we must suspect everything might be kalei habishul and therefore cookable even in a kli sheni. An alternative reading (Rav Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul): Perhaps because it was already baked, the kli sheni paradoxically can cook it — the prior baking makes it more susceptible to further transformation by liquid heat. The Rema's Ruling (Ashkenazi) ונהגו להיזהר לכתחילה שלא ליתן פת אפילו בכלי שני כל זמן שהיד סולדת בו The Ashkenazi minhag: Be strict — do not put bread even in a kli sheni as long as it is yad soledet bo. The Rema also brings three tiers: First opinion (Yereim): Asur even in kli sheni. ויש מתירים בכלי שני — Some permit in a kli sheni (not worried about kalei habishul for everything). ויש מקילים אפילו בכלי ראשון — The Ra'avyah: permitted even in a kli rishon (no bishul achar afiyah at all). Psak for Ashkenazim: Be strict — do not put bread in kli sheni. The Ladle Solution (Mishnah Berurah) The Mishnah Berurah (s.k. 45) addresses those who pour soup over bread: לכל הפחות ישאב בכף מן הקדרה כדי שתהא ככלי שלישי Solution: Use a ladle. The ladle becomes kli sheni; the bowl then becomes kli shlishi . In a kli shlishi, even Ashkenazim agree you may put bread. Apparent contradiction in Mishnah Berurah: In s.k. 87, he writes that a ladle can retain the status of the vessel it serves from (i.e., remain kli rishon). Resolution: Here, with multiple doubts (machloket Rishonim about bishul achar afiyah + already in a kli sheni context), he is lenient regarding the ladle. For completely uncooked items, he would not rely on the ladle downgrading the level. Status of the Ladle for Sefardim (Chacham Ovadia) Chacham Ovadia rules that a ladle generally does transfer down a level (kli rishon → kli sheni), with one caveat: if the ladle is left in the pot long enough to become very hot itself, it may retain the pot's status. For a normal quick scoop, it transfers down. Material matters: Metal conducts heat faster than plastic, so a metal ladle left in the pot is more likely to retain kli rishon status. Be'dieved The Mishnah Berurah rules: בדיעבד אפילו בכלי ראשון אין לאסור משום בישול — After the fact, even if placed in a kli rishon, the food is not forbidden. This consistent principle applies: whenever there is a machloket Rishonim and the violation is disputed, the food is permitted be'dieved. The Contemporary Sefardi Machloket Rav Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul — Be Strict In his introduction to Or LeTzion, he argues: The general rule יש ויש הלכה כבתרא (when Shulchan Aruch brings two opinions, follow the second) does not apply to issurei de'oraita (Torah-level prohibitions). When the potential violation is de'oraita, one must follow the stricter opinion even if it's the first one cited. Therefore: Sefardim should be strict — yesh bishul achar afiyah. Chacham Ovadia Yosef — Be Lenient Chacham Ovadia disagrees, based on multiple arguments: יש ויש הלכה כבתרא — The second opinion (matirin) is the halacha. Why bring the first opinion? The Chida explains: mishum kevodo — out of respect for a significant opinion, even though we don't follow it. Singular vs. plural: יש מי שאומר (one person says it's asur) vs. ויש מתירין (many permit) — the permissive view is more widely held. Proof from later in the siman: The Shulchan Aruch permits placing fully cooked food next to a hotplate (which constitutes \"baking/roasting after cooking\" — afiyah achar bishul), showing he does not hold bishul achar afiyah. Conclusion: Chacham Ovadia rules there is no bishul achar afiyah and no afiyah achar bishul. Once food is fully processed by any form of heat, it is not subject to further bishul. Practical Applications Toast on Shabbat Question: Can you place bread on a hotplate to make toast? Analysis: Bread is baked (afiyah). Toasting it further is afiyah achar afiyah — baking after baking. Even the Yereim who forbids bishul achar afiyah agrees that afiyah achar afiyah is permitted (same category). Rav Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul's objection: Although it's technically the same category, toasting creates a ta'am chadash (new taste/texture) and should be forbidden. He extends the logic of bishul achar afiyah to include any transformation that produces a new dimension. Ben Ish Chai's objection: Makeh b'patish — completing bread into toast is a \"final hammer blow.\" However, virtually all poskim reject this: there is no makeh b'patish with food. Chacham Ovadia's ruling: Mutar. We don't hold bishul achar afiyah, and extending it to afiyah achar afiyah with ta'am chadash is an unjustified chiddush. You may make toast on Shabbat by placing bread on the hotplate. Turkish Coffee The issue: Turkish coffee beans are roasted (tzli/kalui — dry heat). Pouring hot water on them is bishul achar tzli. Approach Ruling Rav Ben-Tzion Abba Shaul Cannot pour from urn (irui kli rishon) onto Turkish coffee. Put water in cup first (kli sheni), then add coffee. Also concerned it may not be fully roasted. Chacham Ovadia Me'ikar hadin mutar — no bishul achar tzli. But tavo alav bracha (praiseworthy) to use kli sheni because perhaps the beans aren't fully roasted. Practical recommendation for Sefardim: Pour water from urn into cup (kli sheni), then add Turkish coffee. Nescafé / Instant Coffee / Cocoa These products have been fully cooked during manufacturing (processed with liquid/heat). Chacham Ovadia rules clearly: You may pour directly from the urn onto Nescafé or cocoa in a cup. This is irui kli rishon onto something already fully cooked — אין בישול אחר בישול. Those who are machmir: Use a kli sheni (pour water into cup first, then add the instant coffee) — out of concern that it may not be fully processed, or to avoid confusion with other coffees. Ashkenazim: Use kli shlishi for Turkish coffee. For Nescafé, there is more room for leniency but many still use kli sheni or shlishi. Tea — The Major Discussion The Mishnah Berurah's Lengthy Treatment The Mishnah Berurah writes an extended discussion warning about the severity of improperly making tea on Shabbat: תה בשבת פשוט בפוסקים דיש בו משום בישול ובמזיד יש בו איסור סקילה ובשוגג חייב חטאת Tea involves real bishul — intentional violation carries sekilah; unintentional, a chatat. What everyone agrees is forbidden: Pouring from the urn directly onto tea leaves (irui kli rishon — asur de'oraita for both Sefardim and Ashkenazim) What the Mishnah Berurah says is also forbidden: Putting tea leaves into a kli sheni — because of mechzi k'mevashel AND because tea leaves may be kalei habishul The Mishnah Berurah's two solutions: Pre-cook the tea leaves: Pour boiling water onto the leaves before Shabbat, mix them thoroughly. They are now \"cooked\" and can be further heated on Shabbat (אין בישול אחר בישול). Strain the essence before Shabbat. Make a tea essence (recommended): Prepare concentrated tea before Shabbat. On Shabbat: pour water from the urn into a cup (kli sheni), then add the pre-made essence. Water is explicitly permitted in a kli sheni (the Gemara states this directly). Note on dyeing (tzvi'ah): Adding tea essence to water colours the water. The Mishnah Berurah and Chacham Ovadia agree: there is no prohibition of dyeing with foods. The Ben Ish Chai is machmir and says to add the \"dye\" (essence) first and then the liquid (reversing the normal dyeing process), but this is not followed. Chacham Ovadia's Ruling on Tea Tea leaves / mint (na'ana) leaves: Me'ikar hadin: may be placed in a kli sheni. HaMachmir tavo alav bracha: to be stringent, use a kli shlishi (third vessel) because they may be kalei habishul. Tea bags: Mutar to put in a kli sheni — even if kli sheni could theoretically cook kalei habishul, it cannot cook more than irui kli rishon, which only cooks kdei klipa (one layer). The bag itself absorbs that layer of \"cooking,\" and the tea inside the bag is protected. The Mishnah Berurah's entire discussion was about tea leaves , not tea bags (which didn't exist in his time). Ashkenazim with tea bags: Many are machmir to use kli shlishi — because they suspect everything is kalei habishul and kli sheni can cook it. Summary Table: Practical Rulings Item Sefardi (Chacham Ovadia) Ashkenazi (Mishnah Berurah) Toast (bread on hotplate) Mutar Mutar (afiyah achar afiyah) Challah in soup Mutar (no bishul achar afiyah) Asur in kli sheni; use ladle → kli shlishi Croutons in soup Mutar Very likely mutar (deep-fried = bishul; use ladle) Matzah in soup (Pesach/Shabbat) Mutar Asur in kli sheni; use ladle → kli shlishi Nescafé / cocoa Mutar even with irui kli rishon Kli sheni or shlishi recommended Turkish coffee Kli sheni recommended Kli shlishi Tea bags Kli sheni — mutar Kli shlishi Tea leaves / na'ana Kli sheni me'ikar hadin; kli shlishi recommended Kli shlishi, or pre-made essence Key Halachic Principles from This Shiur Bishul achar afiyah / afiyah achar bishul: Machloket Rishonim. Ashkenazim are machmir. Chacham Ovadia: mutar — no bishul across categories. Afiyah achar afiyah / bishul achar bishul: Everyone agrees — permitted (same category, no new dimension). Exception: Rav Ben-Tzion's ta'am chadash argument (rejected by Chacham Ovadia). Afiyah achar tzli / tzli achar afiyah: No issue — these are the same halachic category (dry heat). The ladle: Transfers down one level (kli rishon → kli sheni) according to most poskim. Solves many problems for Ashkenazim. Be'dieved: Whenever bishul achar afiyah is in doubt and the food was placed in hot liquid, it is permitted after the fact. Tea bags vs. tea leaves: Tea bags have an additional layer of protection (the bag itself absorbs kdei klipa), making them more lenient than loose leaves. Preview: Next Week Seifim ו–ח (Vav, Zayin, Chet): Further laws of bishul including placement near fire, hazarah (returning food to the fire), and related topics. Shabbat Shalom","zrQWOszQEp4":"Sefer: Emunot VeDeot (The Book of Beliefs and Opinions) by Rav Saadia Gaon Essay/Chapters: Essay 1 (Creation), Chapter 4: the questions people ask about creation (questions 3 to 10), completing Essay 1 Shiur: Part of Dayan Daniel Kada's Monday series on Emunot VeDeot, Monday 20 May 2024, on Zoom These notes are based on an automatic transcript of the recording. Recap: Essay 1 So Far Essay 1 has been about the creation of the universe : not yet who created it (that is Essay 2, \"Hashem, next time\"), and not yet what Hashem wants from us (the later essays). Chapter What Rav Saadia established 1-2 Three premises: there was a beginning ; it came from something external ; and it was from nothing 3 Twelve rival theories of how the world came to be, each refuted, \"some of them nowadays logically we consider nonsensical,\" studied over the past few weeks 4 Now that we know there is a Creator, ten questions people ask The first two questions (last week) # Question Rav Saadia's answer 1 דור הלך ודור בא והארץ לעולם עמדת (Kohelet 1:4): a generation goes and a generation comes, and the earth stands forever . Doesn't that imply the earth is eternal? Everything on earth is constantly renewed, and so was the earth : it too came into being. The verse means that relative to the humans, animals and plants on it, the earth is relatively constant 2 How can anyone create something from nothing? If you knew, you would be the Creator yourself. This is precisely one of the differences between us and God: He can create from nothing and we cannot, so our faculties cannot grasp how Tonight: the remaining eight, on space, time, and the reason for creation . Question 3: What Was There Before, in Space? A person thinks about the place where the earth now is, and asks: what was in that space before the earth was created? \"Remember, all of these questions are based on the fact that Saadia has now proven there's a beginning. If you hold the world is eternal, there's nothing to ask: the space was always occupied.\" The class tried: \" A void .\" Another: \" Godliness filled that space.\" Rav Saadia goes further: there is no question at all . If you ask this, you have not understood what place is. You imagine place as something underneath that holds things . But then you would have to ask what holds that place, and what holds that , without end. Place is where two physical bodies meet. When there are no physical things, the concept of place does not apply. HaMakom : \"He is the place of the world\" \"This is the depth of what the Hachamim say.\" Why do we call Hashem המקום, HaMakom , the Place , as in ברוך המקום ברוך הוא in the Haggadah? הוא מקומו של עולם ואין עולמו מקומו He is the place of the world; the world is not His place. \"Our brains don't always understand this, but let's repeat it. We think of the world as God's place. That's the wrong way round. He is the makom of the world. \" When there is nothing physical, there is only godliness, and no \"place\" to ask about. The gematria of Makom The Rabbi recalled something taught long ago, in the series on Yichud Hashem: Letters Value The Name י ה ו ה 10 + 5 + 6 + 5 = 26 מקום מ ק ו ם 40 + 100 + 6 + 40 = 186 The Name, each letter squared 10² + 5² + 6² + 5² 100 + 25 + 36 + 25 = 186 Why we comfort mourners with HaMakom . We say המקום ינחם אתכם , may HaMakom comfort you. When a loved one dies, \"that's the time when it's most difficult to recognise Yichud Hashem . Such a bad thing has happened.\" So we pray that the Name will be squared : that Hashem's presence will be fully manifested and expressed in the world . \"You now only see something bad. But when Hashem's name is squared and His presence is manifest in the world, that will console you, because then you'll see the presence of Hashem in the world.\" Question 4: What Was There Before, in Time? Whenever the world was created (Rav Saadia will say about 4,000 years before his day; we would say over 5,000, or 15 billion years, \"whatever it may be\"), how was there time, with no sun and no moon? The class answered well: (a) nothing was measuring time without sun or moon; and (b) \" time is relative , it's not absolute\"; \" time is a creation , when the world is created.\" Rav Saadia: you ask this only if you think time is something external to the universe , constantly running on and on, with the world inside it. That is not how time works. When there are no created things, there is no time. \"This is an important principle which he's going to reiterate later on as well: time is relative to the things that are in it. \" So the question what was Hashem doing before He created the world? does not begin. \"It's very difficult for us to think in these terms. Our lives are dominated by time. Yes, within the physical realm. But once we leave the physical realm, there is no time.\" So far: without creation there is no specific place (question 3) and no specific time (question 4). Question 5: Is This All the Creator Could Make? A person looks around and asks: is this all the wisdom of the Creator? Just the earth and the sky? The Rabbi set the question in its time: in the 900s \"all you have really is the earth and the heavens,\" and not even all of the earth. \"It doesn't know about America, it doesn't know about Australia. All you have is Asia, Africa and a bit of Europe.\" Rav Saadia's answer: Hashem created as much as we are able to grasp , enough for us to recognise His wisdom. He would not create what we could not take in. \"I think that fits in very beautifully. As our scientific understanding grows, so does our understanding of how vast the universe is .\" Thousands, probably millions, of planets; our one planet in one galaxy, the Milky Way, among millions of others. \"Nowadays we'd say there's no such question. On the contrary: this is incredible, the wisdom of Hashem.\" Question 6: Did He Create All of It? Is there any part of the universe that Hashem did not create? No. When we say Hashem created the universe, we do not mean He created some of it. He created all of it, entirely. Question 7: Is the World Only 4,693 Years Old? How can you accept that the world is only 4,693 years old ? That number dates the writing: by the reckoning of the time (Adam created on day six), 4,693 years from creation puts Rav Saadia writing this book in 933 CE . His answer: whenever the beginning was, someone would be living at a given distance from it. \"Imagine we were created a hundred years after the universe . Would we ask, how can the world only be a hundred years old, so therefore it can't be the beginning? No, of course not.\" There is no benchmark . If you would not ask it after 100 years, why ask it after 4,693? \"There was a beginning. Whether it's 4,000, whether it's 100, whether it's 6,000 or 16 million years, doesn't really make a difference.\" The modern question A participant noted that Rav Saadia had no carbon dating , no scientists saying a bone is fifteen million years old. \"Yes. There's a whole different question which Saadia of course never dreamt of\": how do we reconcile the Torah's account with a universe that seems to be millions or billions of years old? The Rabbi had given a class on this six or seven years earlier and summarised some approaches: Approach Idea The Flood After the mabul of Noach things changed completely, including radioactive dating Long days The six days were much longer. \"Remember, we said time is a creation ,\" together with the sun and moon. How do you measure hours on days one and two? Adam as the completed human The Rabbi's preferred explanation (below) Adam: \"the pinnacle of humanity, the completed article.\" When the Torah says that on day six Hashem created man, it means the completed man as we know him , the final Homo sapiens . As science tells us, that man had many ancestors, \"the Neanderthals and all those that came before him,\" which over millions of years became the sophisticated adam . Then Hashem breathed into him the nishmat chayim : \"Until now you were a kind of mammal developing. Now we're going to give you the ability to choose between good and bad .\" That is why Bereshit chapter 2 stresses that only when the neshamah was blown into him did ויהי האדם לנפש חיה happen. \"That's the human whom the Torah is directed to. The Torah is not directed to the incomplete humans before that.\" Is there a contradiction with keeping Shabbat every seven days? Someone once asked the Rabbi: if the days were millions of years, Shabbat should come every few million years. \"No. At the end of the day the Torah still calls it a period of evening and morning, yom echad . So we keep that pattern every seventh day.\" But clearly it was not 24-hour days \"because there's no sun.\" It was a process, \"and if you want to include evolution in that, so be it. I don't find a problem with that, provided you see HaKadosh Baruch Hu as the progenitor of everything and the mind behind it all , completing with the pinnacle of creation.\" Discussion. One participant said they had always given that kind of answer, but when they asked Dr Gerald Schroeder (author of a well-known book on the subject), he told them it was \"absolutely wrong\" and should not be taught. They offered another: that Hashem created and destroyed many worlds before this one. The Rabbi found that it \"doesn't sit comfortably with science\" either: \"What did He destroy? If artifacts of the previous worlds remain, how much did He destroy?\" He added that he personally does not find the question difficult: \"I really see the creation narrative fitting in very beautifully with the development of humanity.\" The participant replied that it is very hard to convince a non-believer. \"To convince people is something else. We always need to understand things in our own terms before we can explain them to others. There's no proof of it. It's a way to reconcile.\" Question 8: Was Not Creating an Act? \"I'm not fully clear on this one. I'll do my best to explain it.\" When a person has the ability to do something and abstains , we consider that abstention a kind of action : \"I've made a conscious decision not to do it.\" Hashem left things uncreated until He created them. So was He actively abstaining before creation? Rav Saadia's answer, as the Rabbi understood it: again, you are applying human terms to God . For humans, every choice has an opposite: if you don't want something you are against it; if you don't hate, you love. With Hashem we do not say that because He did not create, He wanted not to create. Questions 9 and 10: Why Create? And Why Then? \"Now he comes to the big question.\" Rav Saadia throws it in right at the end of the whole essay : why did Hashem create the universe at all? \"In the Torah and the Talmud there is never any discussion of why Hashem created the world. It's fascinating when you think about it. Saadia is the very first Jewish philosopher to ask this question in writing , only about a thousand years ago.\" Three answers # Rav Saadia's answer The Rabbi's comment 1 Without a purpose. But humans never act without a purpose; someone who does is wasting his time! That is because humans act for goals and ends. Godliness is elevated above this. In short: He created for a reason we do not know . \"Not very convincing,\" at first sight 2 To show His great wisdom \"I don't really like it. It means you have this Creator who wants to show off His universe.\" To whom, if nobody yet existed? Perhaps only if we are meant to learn from His characteristics and develop 3 For the good of His creatures , so that they can serve Him and be rewarded , and be guided by Him: אני ה' אלהיך מלמדך להועיל מדריכך בדרך תלך (Yeshayahu 48:17), I am Hashem your God who teaches you for your benefit, who guides you in the way you should go \"The one we understand well\" \"The answers contradict each other,\" the Rabbi noted. \"You can't say He created it for no reason and then give a reason.\" So these are three distinct options , and the third is the one people can most readily hold on to. The Rambam: no question to ask The class guessed the Rambam would choose the third answer. In fact he goes with the first . The Rabbi read from the Moreh Nevuchim (3:13) : \"Intelligent persons are perplexed when they inquire into the purpose of the Creation...\" An agent acting with intention has a purpose, and what is produced with intention has passed from non-existence into existence. But a being whose existence is absolute, who never was and never will be without existence, needs no agent , and \"what is the purpose?\" can only be asked of what is the product of an agent. \"We ask why did he do this, why did she do that, what's the motive , all the time. Because we think in those terms, we get stuck.\" For both Rav Saadia's first answer and the Rambam: there is no question. \"You're only asking because you're a human, and therefore an agent, and you expect things to have purposes. That's not the way things work with God.\" It is hard for us to accept, \"because our minds always think of cause and effect, purpose and goal,\" but both Rav Saadia and the Rambam are content with it, \"and therefore we have to be willing to think about this answer.\" Discussion: belief, science and the polemic One participant argued that, in today's world, every schoolchild could challenge the science behind these arguments: \"You have to work with belief.\" There was nothing; God was there; He created. \"If you don't believe, all the explanations are challengeable.\" Another participant asked: wasn't Emunot VeDeot a polemic against those who claimed Islam had superseded Judaism, and how was it received? \"It definitely was,\" the Rabbi said, though not only against Islam: the twelve theories included Plato and Hindu schools. How the outside world received this particular answer: \"I actually don't know.\" A participant recalled the midrash that Hashem looked into the Torah as a blueprint and created the world. \"It says that clearly,\" the Rabbi agreed, \"100 percent. But that's about how, not about why He created it.\" The Ramchal: the depth of the third answer The way the question is usually presented today, the Rabbi said, is the Ramchal's , which develops Rav Saadia's third answer (as those who studied Derech Hashem together will remember): God is good, and the nature of the good is to bestow good. He wants to bestow perfect good, which is Himself . The best way is for human beings to choose good themselves , and so become good , which is the closest you can get to God, and enjoy His goodness. A participant pushed back: what is \"good\"? With technology (he cited the book Deep Utopia ) we may soon have no hunger or want, and then what would charity or helping others mean? And how many people are truly working on themselves? The Rabbi answered that good is two things : character , working on yourself (\"you don't get angry\"), as well as acts of goodness. \"That's what we're doing with the Sefirat HaOmer every day.\" And: \"I still think humans deep down want to work on themselves and become better people.\" Question 10: why at that moment? Whichever the reason, why did He create when He did, and not earlier? Answer There is no time with God \"To ask why 15 billion years ago and not 16, or 100, or 200 billion, there's no such question, because there is no concept of time\" A free agent chooses That is how one who chooses acts: He chose then , and you cannot ask why someone with free will chose when he did A participant objected that Rav Saadia compares God to humans when it suits him . \"That's a very good question. I agree with you. To be honest, I don't like that answer at all. I find it very, very difficult to describe Hashem as a ba'al bechirah , one who chooses. I think the first answer answers it all : there's no time, so you can't ask the question.\" Essay 1 Complete נשלם המאמר הראשון. The position of Rav Saadia, \"basically accepted by all Jewish philosophers and authorities\": Hashem created the world from nothing. He refuted the alternatives and answered the questions. \"Most of the things here we already knew and believe as Jewish people. In Essay 2 and Essay 3 there are more novel things. Here we've been establishing that there is a Creator and a creation.\" Summary Question 1: the earth stands forever means only relatively constant. Question 2: how He creates from nothing is beyond us; if we knew, we would be the Creator. Question 3, space: place is where bodies meet; before creation there is no place. הוא מקומו של עולם ואין עולמו מקומו ; מקום = 186 = the Name squared , and why we comfort mourners with HaMakom . Question 4, time: time is a creation, relative to what is in it. No \"before.\" Questions 5-6: Hashem made as much as we can grasp (and science keeps showing us more); and He made all of it. Question 7, 4,693 years (933 CE): a beginning is a beginning, whenever it was. The Rabbi on old-universe questions : Adam as the completed human who receives the nishmat chayim and free choice. Question 8: not creating was not an \"act\" of refusing: human categories do not apply. Question 9, why create? No purpose we can ask about (Rav Saadia's first answer, and the Rambam, Moreh 3:13 ); to show His wisdom ; for the good of His creatures , developed by the Ramchal : the good wants to bestow good. Question 10, why then? No time with God; and a chooser chooses. The Rabbi prefers the first. Coming Up No shiur next week (the Rabbi is officiating at a wedding on the bank holiday). The class resumes in two weeks with Essay 2 , on the unity of God . Chazak u'varuch."};